window.KB_ENRICHMENT = {"version":"gold114","diseases":144,"enriched":140,"referenceDiseases":861,"totalSearchable":1001,"byId":{"ACUTE_BRONCHITIS":{"name":"Acute Bronchitis","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e printed pp.21, 252-253, 272-273, 1024-1025, 1537, 1595","clinicalPearls":["Defined as a dry or productive cough lasting <3 weeks, occurring without clinical or radiographic signs of pneumonia; it is most common during winter (p.1537).","Sputum purulence does not distinguish viral from bacterial cause; in acute bronchitis the cough is predominantly viral, whereas bacteria play a larger role in chronic bronchitis (p.1537).","Among acute respiratory infections, only acute otitis media, sinusitis, streptococcal pharyngitis, and pneumonia warrant antibiotics; acute bronchitis is not on that list (p.252).","Acute bronchitis and sinusitis are common at all stages of HIV infection, with more severe disease at lower CD4+ counts (p.1595).","An individual episode of acute bronchitis does not typically cause lasting reductions in pulmonary function (p.1537 context; long-term lung-function data p. respiratory infections section)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute bronchitis is predominantly a viral infection of the lower airways below the vocal cords, caused by common respiratory viruses; the inflammatory airway response produces cough that may be dry or productive, without parenchymal consolidation. Bacterial involvement is uncommon, in contrast to chronic bronchitis where bacteria contribute more prominently (p.1537).","additionalDifferentials":["Community-acquired pneumonia (distinguished by a new infiltrate on chest radiography and focal exam findings) (p.1024).","Acute exacerbation of chronic bronchitis / COPD (p.1024).","Heart failure (p.1024).","Pulmonary embolism (p.1024).","Influenza and nonspecific upper respiratory infection, which overlap clinically with lower-tract infection (p.252)."],"infectionMimics":["Pneumonia (cough plus sputum, dyspnea, chest pain, fever, and a new infiltrate point to pneumonia rather than bronchitis) (p.1537, p.1024).","Influenza (overlapping cough and systemic features; can be complicated by secondary bacterial infection) (p.1537).","Pertussis / subacute postinfectious tracheobronchitis (a frequent cause of cough persisting 3-8 weeks) (p.272)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute coronary syndrome / myocardial infarction presenting as cough and dyspnea, illustrated by a fatal case in which exertional dyspnea and chest heaviness were prematurely labeled acute bronchitis (p.21).","Cough-variant asthma (p.272).","Gastroesophageal reflux (p.272).","Rhinosinusitis with postnasal drainage (p.272).","ACE-inhibitor-induced cough (p.272).","Inhalation of noxious chemicals or smoke, and aspiration, as causes of acute cough (p.272)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiography should be obtained whenever pneumonia is suspected, to confirm the absence of an infiltrate that defines acute bronchitis (p.1537).","Specific viral diagnosis is generally unnecessary but, when needed, RT-PCR/multiplex respiratory panels are the most sensitive tests; results must be interpreted cautiously because viral genome can persist for weeks after a resolved infection (p.1537)."],"redFlags":["Dyspnea, especially exertional dyspnea or chest heaviness/pain, which should trigger consideration of cardiac or thromboembolic disease rather than acute bronchitis (p.21).","Cough accompanied by sputum, fever, chest pain, or focal chest signs suggesting pneumonia, prompting chest radiography (p.1537).","Hemoptysis or weight loss (raising concern for malignancy or other serious pulmonary disease) (p.272).","Cough persisting beyond a few weeks, warranting reassessment and chest radiography for serious causes (sarcoidosis, lymphoma, lung cancer, tuberculosis) (p.272-273)."],"prognosis":"Acute bronchitis is a self-limited illness; the cough usually resolves within about 3 weeks, and antibiotics neither speed resolution nor prevent complications in most acute respiratory infections. A dry, irritative postbronchitic cough may linger for more than 2 months, particularly in winter, and a residual subacute cough of 3-8 weeks is common after tracheobronchitis (p.252, p.272-273).","pitfalls":["Prescribing antibiotics for acute bronchitis is inappropriate stewardship; roughly half of ambulatory antibiotic prescriptions for acute respiratory infections are unnecessary, and antibiotics cause adverse effects, C. difficile infection, microbiome disruption, and resistance (p.252).","Treating purulent sputum as evidence of bacterial infection is a mistake, since purulence does not reliably indicate bacterial etiology in acute bronchitis (p.1537).","Premature closure: relying on a rapid 'URI/bronchitis' protocol can cause clinicians to miss life-threatening conditions such as myocardial infarction; a patient who does not look 'sick' may be wrongly assumed to have a benign illness (p.21).","Not eliciting a full dyspnea history (exertional onset, chest heaviness, relief with rest) can lead to a dangerous misdiagnosis of acute bronchitis (p.21)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.21","Harrison 22e p.252","Harrison 22e p.253","Harrison 22e p.272","Harrison 22e p.273","Harrison 22e p.1024","Harrison 22e p.1537","Harrison 22e p.1595"],"crossLinks":["CAP","URTI","COPD_EXACERBATION"]},"AMOEBIC_LIVER_ABSCESS":{"name":"Amoebic Liver Abscess","system":"Hepatobiliary / Tropical Fever","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1798-1801 (Ch. 230, Amebiasis)","clinicalPearls":["Most patients are febrile with right-upper-quadrant pain that may be dull or pleuritic and may radiate to the shoulder; point tenderness over the liver and a right-sided pleural effusion are common, whereas jaundice is rare (p.1799).","Although the colon is the initial site of infection, fewer than one-third of patients with an amebic abscess have active diarrhea at presentation (p.1799).","Strongly age- and sex-dependent: men aged 30-60 are infected at a rate roughly 10-12 times that of women in the same age group; testosterone-modulated NK-T-cell interferon-gamma signaling is implicated experimentally (p.1798).","Young patients tend to present acutely with prominent symptoms of under 10 days' duration, while older patients from endemic areas more often follow a subacute course over months with weight loss and hepatomegaly; only about one-third of those with chronic presentations are febrile (p.1799).","Of travelers who develop an amebic liver abscess after leaving an endemic area, 95% do so within 5 months (p.1799).","Amebic liver abscesses are always preceded by intestinal colonization, which may be entirely asymptomatic (p.1799).","On imaging, more than 80% of patients symptomatic for over 10 days have a single right-lobe abscess, but about 50% of those symptomatic for under 10 days have multiple abscesses (p.1800).","The classic necrotic 'anchovy paste' contents are variable in color and bacteriologically sterile, composed of granular debris with few or no cells; amebae, when present, sit near the abscess capsule rather than in the aspirable necrotic center (p.1799).","Liver enzymes are usually normal or only minimally elevated even with large abscesses; alkaline phosphatase is the most commonly elevated value, while aminotransferase elevation suggests acute disease or a complication (p.1800).","Invasive amebiasis does not elicit eosinophilia (p.1800)."],"pathophysiology":"Entamoeba histolytica trophozoites invade colonic mucosal venules and travel via the portal venous system to the liver; the organism is resistant to complement-mediated lysis, which is critical to its survival in the bloodstream. An early acute infiltrate (predominantly neutrophils) is followed by lysis of those neutrophils on contact with amebae, and the released neutrophil toxins are thought to contribute to hepatocyte necrosis. The liver parenchyma is progressively replaced by necrotic material surrounded by a thin rim of congested liver tissue, producing the classically described (but variably colored) 'anchovy paste' that is sterile and largely acellular, with amebae found mainly at the capsule. Genetically, a leptin-receptor substitution (Q223R) increases the risk of amebic liver abscess in adults (p.1798-1799).","additionalDifferentials":["Pyogenic liver abscess (the most important imaging-based distinction once a hepatic abscess is identified) (p.1801)","Malaria (p.1801)","Typhoid/enteric fever (p.1801)","Pulmonary disease (p.1801)","Gallbladder disease (p.1801)","Any febrile illness with few localizing signs / fever of unknown origin, since 10-15% present with fever alone (p.1799, p.1801)"],"infectionMimics":["Pyogenic liver abscess (p.1801)","Malaria (p.1801)","Typhoid (enteric) fever (p.1801)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Gallbladder disease (p.1801)","Pulmonary disease presenting with right-sided pleural/pleuritic findings (p.1801)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum alkaline phosphatase and aminotransferases (enzymes often normal/minimally elevated; raised aminotransferases suggest acute disease or complication) (p.1800)","Full blood count for leukocytosis >10,000/uL, present in about three-quarters of patients, especially with acute or complicated disease (p.1800)","Enzyme immunoassay for the Gal/GalNAc lectin antigen, usable on stool and on liver-abscess aspirates (p.1800)","Multiplex PCR for E. histolytica DNA (near 100% sensitivity/specificity) (p.1800)","Loop-mediated isothermal amplification (LAMP) on abscess pus as a low-cost molecular option (p.1800)","Diagnostic aspirate sent for Gram stain and bacterial culture when distinguishing amebic from pyogenic abscess (p.1801)"],"redFlags":["Pleuropulmonary involvement (reported in 20-30%, the most frequent complication): contiguous spread or frank rupture into the pleural space, the latter requiring drainage (p.1799).","Large abscess (>10 cm) in the superior right lobe, which may rupture into the pleural space (p.1800).","Left-lobe abscess, which may rupture into the pericardium, the complication carrying the gravest prognosis and able to occur even during medical therapy (p.1799-1800).","Rupture into the peritoneum, presenting as an indolent leak or as an acute abdomen (p.1799).","Hepatobronchial fistula causing cough productive of large amounts of necrotic material (p.1799).","Lack of clinical response within 3-5 days or threat of imminent rupture (p.1801)."],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and treatment, mortality from uncomplicated amebic liver abscess is under 1%, and over 90% of patients respond dramatically with reduced pain and fever within 72 hours; recurrence is rare. Abscesses resolve slowly and may even enlarge despite clinical improvement, so follow-up ultrasonography can be misleading; complete resolution within 6 months is expected in about two-thirds of patients, while roughly 10% have persistent imaging abnormalities for up to a year. A hepatobronchial fistula carries a good prognosis, whereas pericardial rupture (usually from left-lobe abscesses) carries the gravest prognosis (p.1799-1801).","pitfalls":["Expecting concurrent dysentery: fewer than one-third of patients have active diarrhea, so a negative stool examination or absent diarrhea does not exclude the diagnosis (p.1799).","Trophozoites are rarely recovered from liver aspirates because they reside in the abscess capsule, not in the necrotic center that is actually aspirated (p.1799).","Up to 10% of patients with acute amebic liver abscess have negative serology early; titers usually become positive within a week (p.1799).","Microscopy cannot distinguish E. histolytica cysts from non-pathogenic E. dispar, E. moshkovskii, or E. bangladeshi; species-specific diagnosis on microscopy requires seeing trophozoites that have ingested erythrocytes (p.1799).","In endemic areas serology may stay positive for years (indirect hemagglutination up to ~10 years), complicating interpretation (p.1799).","Follow-up imaging may be confusing because abscesses resolve slowly and can transiently enlarge despite a good clinical response (p.1800)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1798","Harrison 22e p.1799","Harrison 22e p.1800","Harrison 22e p.1801"],"crossLinks":["LIVER_ABSCESS","MALARIA","ENTERIC_FEVER"]},"ASPIRATION_PNEUMONIA":{"name":"Aspiration Pneumonia","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1066-1067, 1072, 1077, 1190, 1418","clinicalPearls":["Aspiration pneumonia is best viewed as a point on a continuum that also includes community-acquired and hospital-acquired pneumonia, defined by a phenotype with macroaspiration risk factors and involvement of characteristic gravity-dependent lung locations (p.1066).","Aspiration pneumonia is estimated to account for roughly 5-15% of community-acquired pneumonia cases; reliable figures for the hospital-acquired setting are not available (p.1066).","The historical view that aspiration pneumonia is caused mainly by anaerobes has shifted: when acquired in the community or hospital, the likely pathogens mirror those of CAP or HAP, with anaerobes still relevant mainly in poor dentition, lung abscess, necrotizing pneumonia, or empyema (p.1067).","Anaerobic aspiration infections tend to follow an indolent course, a clinical clue that helps separate them from more acutely presenting conditions such as chemical pneumonitis or pneumococcal pneumonia (p.1418).","Sputum in bacterial aspiration pneumonia is characteristically NOT malodorous unless the process has been ongoing for at least about a week (p.1418).","Expectorated sputum is unreliable for anaerobic culture because of inevitable contamination by normal oral microbiota; reliable anaerobic specimens historically required transtracheal or transthoracic aspiration, techniques rarely used now (p.1418).","In CAP overall, no specific etiology is identified in more than half of cases despite careful history, examination, and imaging (p.1067)."],"pathophysiology":"Macroaspiration of oropharyngeal or gastric contents delivers a high microbial burden directly into the lower airways; lung involvement reflects this rather than invasion of a sterile space, since the lungs normally harbor a microbiota. Mechanical defenses (nasal hairs/turbinates, branching airways, mucociliary clearance, gag and cough reflexes) reduce but do not fully block bacterial access to the lower respiratory tract. Bacterial aspiration pneumonia develops over several days to weeks (not hours) through a combination of increased bacterial burden, increased virulence of aspirated organisms, and potential airway damage from aspirated gastric fluid. This contrasts with chemical pneumonitis (Mendelson syndrome), where aspirated acidic gastric juice causes rapid, direct destruction of the alveolar lining with fluid transudation into the alveolar space within hours (p.1066, p.1418).","additionalDifferentials":["Foreign-body/food aspiration causing major-airway obstruction with atelectasis and nonspecific inflammation, managed by removal rather than antibiotics (p.1418)","Chemical (acid) pneumonitis, e.g., Mendelson syndrome from regurgitated gastric contents (p.1418)","Pneumococcal pneumonia, distinguished by its more acute presentation (p.1418)","Lung abscess (subacute anaerobic infection) as a related/overlapping entity (p.1072, p.1418)"],"infectionMimics":["Pneumococcal pneumonia presenting more acutely than indolent anaerobic aspiration infection (p.1418)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chemical pneumonitis (Mendelson syndrome) from aspiration of acidic gastric juices, with rapid alveolar injury and pulmonary edema, often without fever and frequently needing no antibiotics (p.1418)","Foreign-body/food aspiration with airway obstruction and atelectasis (p.1418)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiography looking for consolidation in dependent lung segments (basilar lower-lobe segments if aspiration occurred upright; posterior segment of the upper lobe or superior segment of the lower lobe if supine) (p.1418)","Gram stain of sputum, which characteristically shows a mixed bacterial population with many polymorphonuclear leukocytes (p.1418)","Bronchoscopic protected-brush specimens or bronchoalveolar lavage, which may recover anaerobes from the lower tract with less oropharyngeal contamination (role still being defined) (p.1418)","Assessment of dentition/oral hygiene, given the association of severe dental disease with aspiration pneumonia (p.1418)"],"redFlags":["Gross hemoptysis suggesting necrotizing pneumonia (p.1067)","Progression to respiratory failure; in chemical pneumonitis a chest x-ray can evolve from normal to a complete bilateral whiteout within 8-24 hours (p.1067, p.1418)","Septic shock and organ failure in severe pneumonia (p.1067)","Weight loss and anemia indicating a more chronic process (p.1418)","Development of lung abscess or complicated/empyematous pleural effusion (pleural fluid pH <7.2, glucose <2.2 mmol/L, or LDH >1000 U/L, or organisms seen/cultured indicates need for drainage) (p.1072)"],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish chemical pneumonitis (Mendelson syndrome) from bacterial aspiration pneumonia: chemical pneumonitis is a non-infectious acid injury that usually resolves with supportive care and does NOT warrant antibiotics unless bacterial superinfection develops (p.1418).","Routinely covering anaerobes for all aspiration pneumonia: anaerobic coverage is unnecessary unless dentition is poor or there is lung abscess or necrotizing pneumonia (p.1070).","Expecting an acute, foul-smelling presentation: bacterial aspiration pneumonia evolves over days to weeks and sputum is usually not malodorous until the process has lasted about a week (p.1418).","Relying on expectorated sputum for anaerobic culture, which is invalid because of oral-flora contamination (p.1418)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1066","Harrison 22e p.1067","Harrison 22e p.1070","Harrison 22e p.1072","Harrison 22e p.1077","Harrison 22e p.1190","Harrison 22e p.1418"],"crossLinks":["LUNG_ABSCESS","CAP","HAP"]},"BRAIN_ABSCESS":{"name":"Brain Abscess","system":"Central Nervous System","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1178-1181","clinicalPearls":["The classic triad of headache, fever, and a focal neurologic deficit is present in fewer than half of cases, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis (p.1178).","Headache is the single most common symptom (more than 75% of patients), typically a constant, dull ache that becomes progressively more severe and refractory to treatment (p.1178).","Fever is present in only about half of patients at diagnosis; its absence should not be used to rule out a brain abscess (p.1178).","Most patients present a relatively long time after symptom onset (commonly around 11-12 days), and a brain abscess usually behaves like an expanding mass lesion rather than an obvious infection (p.1178).","Meningismus is generally absent unless the abscess has ruptured into the ventricle or infection has reached the subarachnoid space (p.1178).","Diffusion-weighted MRI helps distinguish abscess from tumor: an abscess shows restricted diffusion (high DWI signal, low ADC) within the cavity, whereas necrotic tumors typically do not (p.1178).","Lesion location predicts the source and the deficit: otogenic abscesses favor the temporal lobe and cerebellum, while sinus and dental abscesses favor the frontal lobes (p.1179).","New-onset partial seizures are the most common manifestation of neurocysticercosis, an important focal CNS lesion in patients from Latin America (p.1181)."],"pathophysiology":"Intact brain parenchyma is relatively resistant to infection; bacterial invasion generally requires preexisting or concomitant ischemia, necrosis, or hypoxemia in brain tissue. Abscesses arise by direct contiguous spread (sinusitis, otitis media, mastoiditis, dental infection), by inoculation after penetrating trauma or neurosurgery, or by hematogenous seeding from a distant focus; about a quarter are cryptogenic with no identified source. Hematogenous abscesses are often multiple, frequently lie at the gray-white junction in the middle cerebral artery territory, and tend to be poorly encapsulated. Once established, the lesion evolves through stages: early cerebritis (days 1-3, perivascular inflammation around a core of coagulative necrosis with marked edema), late cerebritis (days 4-9, expanding necrotic center with macrophage/fibroblast infiltrate and a developing thin capsule), early capsule formation (days 10-13, capsule better formed on the cortical than ventricular side, correlating with the ring-enhancing appearance on imaging), and late capsule formation (day 14 onward, dense collagenous capsule, regressing edema, and surrounding gliosis with reactive astrocytes that may underlie later seizures). In right-to-left cardiac or pulmonary shunts, bacteria bypass the pulmonary capillary filter while shunt-related hypoxemia and polycythemia create ischemic nidi favoring abscess formation (p.1178-1179).","additionalDifferentials":["Subdural empyema","Bacterial meningitis","Viral meningoencephalitis","Superior sagittal sinus thrombosis","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis","Primary or metastatic brain tumor (the major consideration when fever is absent)","Cerebral infarction or hematoma (can resemble abscess on CT/MRI)","Neurocysticercosis","Tuberculoma (a major cause of focal CNS mass lesions in India and East Asia)"],"infectionMimics":["Subdural empyema","Bacterial meningitis","Viral meningoencephalitis","Cerebral toxoplasmosis (reactivation, mainly in immunocompromised/HIV hosts)","Neurocysticercosis","Tuberculoma","Nocardial, Aspergillus, Candida, or Cryptococcus CNS lesions in immunocompromised hosts"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Primary or metastatic brain tumor","Cerebral infarction","Intracerebral hematoma","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis","Superior sagittal sinus thrombosis"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI is preferred over CT, being superior for detecting early (cerebritis-stage) lesions and posterior fossa abscesses (p.1178).","Diffusion-weighted imaging with apparent diffusion coefficient mapping to separate abscess (restricted diffusion) from tumor (p.1178).","Gram stain plus aerobic, anaerobic, mycobacterial, and fungal cultures of abscess material obtained by CT-guided stereotactic needle aspiration (p.1179).","Inflammatory markers: peripheral leukocytosis in about half, elevated ESR in about 60%, and elevated C-reactive protein in about 80% of patients (p.1179).","Serum IgG antibody to Toxoplasma gondii when imaging suggests toxoplasmosis (p.1181).","Enzyme-linked immunotransfer blot as a confirmatory test for neurocysticercosis, plus funduscopic examination in suspected cases (p.1181)."],"redFlags":["Lumbar puncture should NOT be performed when a focal intracranial infection such as abscess or empyema is known or suspected, because CSF analysis adds nothing and LP increases the risk of herniation (p.1179).","Signs of raised intracranial pressure (papilledema, nausea/vomiting, drowsiness or confusion) can dominate the presentation, particularly with cerebellar abscesses (p.1178).","Rupture of an abscess into the ventricle (heralded by new meningismus) is an ominous development (p.1178).","High risk (about 35%) of focal or generalized seizures (p.1180).","Glucocorticoids and prior treatment can alter the CT/MRI appearance of the capsule, potentially masking or modifying the imaging picture (p.1178)."],"prognosis":"With modern neuroimaging, stereotactic aspiration, and improved antibiotics, mortality has fallen to typically less than 15% in recent series. However, significant sequelae - including seizures, persisting weakness, aphasia, or mental impairment - occur in at least 20% of survivors (p.1180).","pitfalls":["Relying on the classic triad: headache, fever, and focal deficit coexist in fewer than half of patients (p.1178).","Excluding the diagnosis because the patient is afebrile - fever is absent in roughly half of cases (p.1178).","Performing a lumbar puncture in a patient with a suspected focal intracranial infection, risking herniation without diagnostic benefit (p.1179).","Misreading a ring-enhancing lesion as tumor when diffusion-weighted imaging would point to abscess (p.1178).","Forgetting that glucocorticoid therapy can alter the imaging appearance of the abscess capsule (p.1178).","Overlooking cerebral toxoplasmosis, which characteristically reactivates and causes ring-enhancing lesions in HIV-infected and other immunocompromised patients (p.1181).","Dismissing a cryptogenic abscess as having no source - many are in fact attributable to occult dental infection (p.1179)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison describes a staged histopathologic/radiologic evolution rather than a clinical severity score: early cerebritis (days 1-3), late cerebritis (days 4-9), early capsule formation (days 10-13, when ring enhancement appears), and late capsule formation (day 14 and beyond) (p.1178).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1178","Harrison 22e p.1179","Harrison 22e p.1180","Harrison 22e p.1181"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS","SINUSITIS","brain_tumour","IE"]},"BRONCHIECTASIS_EXACERBATION":{"name":"Bronchiectasis Exacerbation","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"p.2242-2244","clinicalPearls":["Bronchiectasis is irreversible airway dilation, classically categorised as cylindrical/tubular (most common), varicose, or cystic; it may be focal or diffuse (Harrison 22e p.2242).","An acute exacerbation is defined primarily by a change in sputum character - increased volume and increased purulence - rather than by classic infection signs; fever and new infiltrates may be absent (Harrison 22e p.2243).","The usual clinical picture between exacerbations is a persistent productive cough with thick, tenacious sputum, often with crackles, wheeze, and sometimes digital clubbing (Harrison 22e p.2243).","Pseudomonas aeruginosa preferentially colonises damaged airways and evades host defences; its colonisation marks more severe disease and worse outcomes (Harrison 22e p.2242-2243, p.2244).","The pattern of lung-field involvement on imaging hints at the underlying cause: upper-zone in CF and post-radiation fibrosis, lower-zone in chronic aspiration/end-stage fibrosis/immunodeficiency, midlung in NTM (MAC) and dyskinetic cilia syndrome, and central-airway in ABPA (Harrison 22e p.2242-2243).","MAC-related bronchiectasis classically affects nonsmoking women over 50 years of age (Harrison 22e p.2242).","Up to 25-50% of patients referred for bronchiectasis have idiopathic disease after workup (Harrison 22e p.2242)."],"pathophysiology":"The dominant mechanism of infectious bronchiectasis is the 'vicious cycle hypothesis': susceptibility to infection plus impaired mucociliary clearance permits microbial colonisation of the bronchial tree; the persistent microbial presence incites chronic inflammation that damages the airway wall, which further impairs secretion and microbial clearance, perpetuating the infectious-inflammatory cycle. Some organisms, notably Pseudomonas aeruginosa, are especially adept at colonising damaged airways and evading host defences. Impaired mucociliary clearance may be inherited (e.g., cystic fibrosis, dyskinetic cilia syndrome), and a single severe infection such as Bordetella pertussis or Mycoplasma pneumoniae can cause enough airway damage to seed the cycle. Pathologically there is small-airway wall inflammation with larger-airway wall destruction and dilation, and loss of elastin, smooth muscle, and cartilage; inflammatory cells release proteases, reactive oxygen species, and proinflammatory cytokines that damage larger airways while ongoing small-airway inflammation produces airflow obstruction. Antiproteases such as alpha-1 antitrypsin help neutralise neutrophil elastase, and bronchiectasis/emphysema occur in alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. Molecular endotypes with distinct inflammatory and microbiome signatures may correlate with exacerbation risk. Noninfectious mechanisms include immune-mediated bronchial wall damage (e.g., Sjogren's syndrome, rheumatoid arthritis) and traction from parenchymal fibrosis. (Harrison 22e p.2242-2243)","additionalDifferentials":["COPD - airflow obstruction on pulmonary function testing overlaps with bronchiectasis at presentation (Harrison 22e p.2243)","Asthma - may coexist; ABPA should be considered in refractory asthma (Harrison 22e p.2242-2243)","Cystic fibrosis - should be excluded early in any diffuse bronchiectasis workup (Harrison 22e p.2243)","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (central-airway-predominant) (Harrison 22e p.2242-2243)"],"infectionMimics":["Nontuberculous mycobacterial (especially MAC) airway infection - organisms may be colonisers rather than true pathogens, complicating interpretation of positive cultures (Harrison 22e p.2243)","Active or reactivated tuberculosis (in endemic areas, a cause and mimic of recurrent airway infection) (Harrison 22e p.2242)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Traction bronchiectasis from underlying lung fibrosis (e.g., idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, post-radiation fibrosis) - dilated airways from parenchymal distortion rather than active infection (Harrison 22e p.2242-2243)","Autoimmune/rheumatologic airway disease (rheumatoid arthritis, Sjogren's syndrome, inflammatory bowel disease) (Harrison 22e p.2242)","Recurrent aspiration (e.g., from esophageal motility disorders such as scleroderma) (Harrison 22e p.2242)"],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution chest CT - imaging modality of choice; more specific than chest radiograph; look for airway dilation as 'tram tracks' or the 'signet-ring sign' (airway diameter at least 1.5x the adjacent vessel), lack of bronchial tapering, bronchial wall thickening, and inspissated secretions in a 'tree-in-bud' pattern (Harrison 22e p.2243)","Bronchoscopy - almost always required in focal bronchiectasis to exclude airway obstruction by mass or foreign body; consider with BAL in diffuse disease if no pathogen is identified (Harrison 22e p.2243)","Pulmonary function testing - functional assessment, typically showing mild-to-moderate airflow obstruction (Harrison 22e p.2243)","Etiologic workup for diffuse disease: sputum stains/cultures for bacteria, acid-fast bacilli, and fungi; immunoglobulin levels and CBC with differential; HIV testing; sweat chloride and genetic testing for CF; alpha-1 antitrypsin level; nasal/respiratory brush or biopsy for ciliary dyskinesia; autoimmune serologies; ABPA workup (Aspergillus skin test/precipitins, serum IgE, eosinophils); swallowing/neuromuscular assessment (Harrison 22e p.2242)"],"redFlags":["Hemoptysis - bronchiectatic airways are dilated, inflamed, and highly vascular (supplied by the high-pressure bronchial circulation), so exacerbations can produce massive, life-threatening hemoptysis (Harrison 22e p.2243-2244, p.1408 hemoptysis chapter)","Massive hemoptysis - may require intubation to stabilise the patient, protection of the nonbleeding lung, and bronchial artery embolisation or surgery (Harrison 22e p.2244)","Emergence of antibiotic-resistant organisms after recurrent infections and repeated antibiotic courses (Harrison 22e p.2244)"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary widely with the underlying etiology and comorbidities, and are influenced by exacerbation frequency and, in infectious disease, the specific pathogens involved - Pseudomonas aeruginosa colonisation is associated with worse outcomes. Lung-function decline in non-CF bronchiectasis can resemble that of COPD, with FEV1 falling roughly 50-55 mL/year versus 20-30 mL/year in healthy controls. (Harrison 22e p.2244)","pitfalls":["Expecting classic infection signs: an exacerbation may lack fever or new infiltrates, so reliance on these can miss it - the defining change is sputum volume and purulence (Harrison 22e p.2243).","Chest radiographs lack sensitivity for bronchiectasis; a normal or unremarkable film does not exclude it, and CT is needed for confirmation (Harrison 22e p.2243).","Positive NTM cultures may reflect colonisation rather than true infection; consensus diagnostic criteria (e.g., two positive sputum cultures, or one positive BAL, plus symptoms and radiographic findings) should be applied before attributing disease (Harrison 22e p.2243).","Focal bronchiectasis should prompt bronchoscopy to exclude an obstructing mass or foreign body rather than being attributed to infection alone (Harrison 22e p.2243)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2242","Harrison 22e p.2243","Harrison 22e p.2244"],"crossLinks":["COPD_EXACERBATION","PULMONARY_TB","CAP"]},"CAP":{"name":"Community Acquired Pneumonia (non-severe)","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1066-1072","clinicalPearls":["Typical and atypical pathogens cannot be reliably separated on clinical or radiographic grounds because both can produce overlapping syndromes; atypicals (Mycoplasma, Chlamydia, Legionella) are not seen on Gram stain or grown on standard media and are intrinsically resistant to beta-lactams (Harrison 22e p.1067).","In more than half of CAP cases no specific pathogen is identified despite history, examination, and imaging; in one large emergency-department series an etiology was established in under 8% (Harrison 22e p.1068).","Physical examination has limited diagnostic accuracy in pneumonia (sensitivity ~58%, specificity ~67%), so a compatible clinical picture plus a new infiltrate on chest film remains the diagnostic standard (Harrison 22e p.1068).","In the elderly the presentation may be subtle, manifesting as new or worsening confusion or decompensation of a chronic illness with few classic respiratory findings (Harrison 22e p.1068).","Gross hemoptysis raises concern for necrotizing pneumonia, such as that caused by community-associated MRSA (Harrison 22e p.1067).","Up to about 20% of patients have gastrointestinal symptoms (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea) in addition to constitutional and pulmonary findings (Harrison 22e p.1067-1068).","Aspiration pneumonia sits on a continuum with CAP/HAP and accounts for roughly 5-15% of CAP cases; modern organisms mirror the usual CAP/HAP flora, with anaerobes mainly relevant in poor dentition, lung abscess, necrotizing pneumonia, or empyema (Harrison 22e p.1066-1067).","Epidemiologic exposures can flag specific organisms (e.g. birds for Chlamydia psittaci/Histoplasma, recent hotel or cruise stay for Legionella, local influenza activity for influenza/pneumococcus/S. aureus) (Harrison 22e p.1067).","CAP can be complicated by cardiovascular events (heart failure, arrhythmia, myocardial infarction, stroke), especially in the elderly with pneumococcal or influenza pneumonia, attributed to increased myocardial load and inflammation-driven plaque destabilization, and these events may occur acutely or up to a year later (Harrison 22e p.1072)."],"pathophysiology":"Pneumonia results from proliferation of microbial pathogens in the alveoli together with the host inflammatory response. Organisms reach the lower respiratory tract chiefly by microaspiration of oropharyngeal flora, but also by inhalation and direct mucosal spread; mechanical defenses (nasal hairs/turbinates, branching airways, mucociliary clearance, gag and cough reflexes) are imperfect barriers. Rather than simple invasion of a sterile space, current models view pneumonia as an emergent disturbance of the lung microbiota driven by self-reinforcing positive-feedback loops: an inciting epithelial/endothelial injury (often a viral infection plus microaspiration) releases cytokines, chemokines, and catecholamines that can selectively favor growth of organisms such as S. pneumoniae and P. aeruginosa, amplifying inflammation. When innate and adaptive immunity fail to contain this, mediators (IL-6 and TNF cause fever; IL-8 and G-CSF recruit neutrophils) drive an alveolar-capillary leak producing hypoxemia and radiographic infiltrates. Bacterial toxins amplify inflammation, and some pathogens blunt hypoxic vasoconstriction, worsening hypoxemia; reduced compliance, increased secretions, raised respiratory drive, and intrapulmonary shunting cause dyspnea and, when severe, respiratory failure (Harrison 22e p.1066). Pathologically, classic (lobar pneumococcal) pneumonia passes through edema, red hepatization, gray hepatization, and resolution; a bronchopneumonia pattern predominates in nosocomial disease while a lobar pattern is more typical of bacterial CAP, and viral pneumonias are alveolar rather than interstitial processes (Harrison 22e p.1066).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute bronchitis (Harrison 22e p.1068)","Acute exacerbation of chronic bronchitis/COPD (Harrison 22e p.1068)","Tuberculosis (suggested by an upper-lobe cavitating lesion) (Harrison 22e p.1068)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Heart failure / pulmonary edema (Harrison 22e p.1068, p.1072)","Pulmonary embolism / pulmonary infarction (Harrison 22e p.1068, p.1072)","Lung carcinoma (consider with relapse or recurrence in the same segment, raising concern for underlying neoplasm) (Harrison 22e p.1072)","Radiation pneumonitis (Harrison 22e p.1072)","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (Harrison 22e p.1072)","Connective tissue disease involving the lungs (Harrison 22e p.1072)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Sputum Gram stain and culture (recommended chiefly for hospitalized patients, particularly severe cases or those at risk for MRSA or P. aeruginosa; an adequate specimen needs >25 neutrophils and <10 squamous epithelial cells per low-power field) (Harrison 22e p.1068)","Blood cultures (reserved for high-risk patients such as neutropenia, asplenia, complement deficiency, chronic liver disease, or severe CAP, and those at risk for MRSA/P. aeruginosa; yield only ~5-14% in hospitalized CAP) (Harrison 22e p.1068)","Pneumococcal and Legionella urinary antigen tests (reserved for severe cases or relevant epidemiology; Legionella test detects only serogroup 1) (Harrison 22e p.1068)","Multiplex respiratory PCR / nasopharyngeal swab PCR (standard for viral diagnosis including influenza and coronaviruses; can also detect Legionella, M. pneumoniae, C. pneumoniae, mycobacteria) (Harrison 22e p.1068)","Procalcitonin and C-reactive protein (adjuncts for stewardship only; PCT is not accurate enough to diagnose bacterial CAP or to withhold initial antibiotics, and CRP is even less sensitive) (Harrison 22e p.1068-1069)","CT chest (for suspected loculated effusion, cavitary disease, or postobstructive pneumonia from tumor or foreign body) (Harrison 22e p.1068)","Diagnostic pleural fluid sampling for a significant effusion (Harrison 22e p.1072)","Follow-up chest radiograph ~4-6 weeks after hospitalization (not routinely needed in well, nonsmoking outpatients whose symptoms resolve in 5-7 days) (Harrison 22e p.1072)"],"redFlags":["Septic shock requiring vasopressors or respiratory failure requiring invasive mechanical ventilation (major severe-CAP criteria mandating direct ICU admission) (Harrison 22e p.1069)","Room-air oxygen saturation <92% (Harrison 22e p.1069)","Confusion or disorientation, including new or worsening confusion in the elderly (Harrison 22e p.1068-1069)","Respiratory rate >=30 breaths/min (Harrison 22e p.1069)","Multilobar infiltrates (Harrison 22e p.1069)","Hypotension requiring aggressive fluid resuscitation (Harrison 22e p.1069)","Gross hemoptysis suggesting necrotizing pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1067)","Failure to improve by ~day 3 of therapy, prompting reassessment for resistant or unexpected pathogen, sequestered focus (abscess/empyema), or a noninfectious mimic (Harrison 22e p.1072)"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on age, comorbidities, and site of care. Young patients without comorbidity generally recover fully in about 2 weeks, whereas older patients or those with comorbid illness may take several weeks longer. Overall mortality is under 5% for outpatients but ranges from roughly 12% to 40% for hospitalized patients depending on the patient category and the timeliness of appropriate antibiotics; in older patients the 1-year mortality after CAP exceeds the 30-day mortality. Fever and leukocytosis typically resolve in 2-4 days in otherwise healthy patients, while chest radiographic clearance is slowest (4-12 weeks). About 18% of hospitalized CAP patients are readmitted within a month (Harrison 22e p.1067, p.1072).","pitfalls":["Treating a macrolide as adequate empirical monotherapy where high-level pneumococcal macrolide resistance is prevalent risks treatment failure (Harrison 22e p.1069).","Relying on chest radiography or clinical features to determine etiology is unreliable; imaging often cannot distinguish CAP from other conditions and seldom yields a specific organism (Harrison 22e p.1068).","Using procalcitonin or CRP in isolation to decide on or withhold antibiotics is inappropriate, as neither is sufficiently accurate for diagnosing bacterial CAP (Harrison 22e p.1068-1069).","Neither PSI nor CURB-65 is designed to determine the need for ICU admission, so they should not be used for that purpose (Harrison 22e p.1069).","Delayed ICU transfer of a deteriorating ward patient is associated with higher mortality than initial ICU monitoring of equally ill patients (Harrison 22e p.1069).","Inability to expectorate sputum may reflect dehydration rather than absence of disease; rehydration can yield a productive sample and a more apparent infiltrate (Harrison 22e p.1068).","Pneumonia that relapses or recurs in the same lung segment should prompt evaluation for an underlying neoplasm or focal abnormality such as bronchiectasis (Harrison 22e p.1072)."],"severityClassification":"Two validated tools guide the site-of-care decision. The Pneumonia Severity Index (PSI) is a prognostic model using 20 variables (age, comorbidities, abnormal exam and laboratory findings) that stratifies patients into five classes with rising mortality (class 1 ~0.1%, class 2 ~0.6%, class 3 ~2.8%, class 4 ~8.2%, class 5 ~29.2%); classes 1-2 are generally suitable for outpatient care and class 3 for an observation unit. CURB-65 scores one point each for Confusion, Urea >7 mmol/L, Respiratory rate >=30/min, low Blood pressure (systolic <=90 or diastolic <=60 mmHg), and age >=65: a score of 0 (30-day mortality ~1.5%) suggests outpatient treatment, 1-2 generally warrants admission (unless driven only by age >=65), and >=3 (mortality ~22%) may need ICU care. PSI is better validated and more discriminating but harder to compute. Severe CAP (Harrison/IDSA-ATS criteria) is defined by either major criterion (invasive mechanical ventilation for respiratory failure, or septic shock requiring vasopressors), mandating ICU admission; presence of at least 3 of 9 minor criteria (RR >=30/min; PaO2/FiO2 <=250; multilobar infiltrates; confusion/disorientation; uremia with BUN >=20 mg/dL; leukopenia <4000/uL; thrombocytopenia <100,000/uL; hypothermia <36 degrees C; hypotension needing aggressive fluids) warrants ICU or high-level monitoring. Independent of these scores, inability to maintain oral intake, concerns about compliance (e.g. cognitive impairment, homelessness), or room-air O2 saturation <92% are indications for hospitalization (Harrison 22e p.1069).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1066","Harrison 22e p.1067","Harrison 22e p.1068","Harrison 22e p.1069","Harrison 22e p.1072"],"crossLinks":["SEVERE_CAP","HAP","ASPIRATION_PNEUMONIA","pleural_effusion","pe"]},"CA_UTI":{"name":"Catheter-Associated Urinary Tract Infection","system":"Genitourinary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1086-1095, 1147","clinicalPearls":["CAUTI is diagnosed only when localizing urinary and/or systemic symptoms (e.g., fever) are present together with pyuria and bacteriuria; bacteriuria and pyuria alone do not establish the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1090).","Because the catheter is a conduit for bacterial entry, bacteriuria is essentially inevitable with catheterization, so an abnormal urinalysis alone should not be the endpoint of evaluating a febrile catheterized patient (Harrison 22e p.1090).","The accepted bacteriuria threshold for CAUTI is at least 10^3 colony-forming units per mL of urine (Harrison 22e p.1090).","Typical UTI features (pain, urgency, dysuria, fever, peripheral leukocytosis) have lower predictive value in catheterized patients than in non-catheterized patients (Harrison 22e p.1090).","The catheter should be exchanged or, if feasible, removed during treatment to clear biofilm-associated organisms; ideally the exchange precedes urine culture collection so results reflect bladder rather than catheter-wall microbiology (Harrison 22e p.1094).","Very high pyuria levels are common with urinary stasis (e.g., end-stage renal disease, obstructed catheters) and the degree of pyuria should not influence the distinction between asymptomatic bacteriuria and true UTI in an asymptomatic patient (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Candiduria is common in catheterized patients, especially after antimicrobial therapy for CAUTI or inappropriate treatment of asymptomatic bacteriuria; removal of the catheter alone resolves candiduria in over a third of asymptomatic cases (Harrison 22e pp.1088, 1095).","Urine nitrite is detectable only with Enterobacterales and adequate accumulation, so it can be falsely negative with non-Enterobacterales pathogens (e.g., enterococci, Pseudomonas, Candida) that are prominent in CAUTI (Harrison 22e pp.1090-1091)."],"pathophysiology":"Urinary catheters and other foreign bodies (stones, stents, nephrostomy tubes) provide an inert surface for bacterial colonization and formation of a persistent biofilm, and catheterization dramatically increases bacterial entry into the urinary tract. The catheter serves as a conduit allowing organisms to ascend into the bladder, making bacteriuria nearly universal during catheterization. Biofilm on the catheter shelters organisms and acts as a nidus for reinfection, which is why catheter exchange or removal is central to clearing infection. The microbiology is more diverse than in uncomplicated cystitis; while E. coli remains predominant, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, enterococci, Staphylococcus aureus, and Candida are important additional pathogens (Harrison 22e pp.1087-1088, 1094).","additionalDifferentials":["Asymptomatic bacteriuria / catheter-associated asymptomatic bacteriuria (positive culture without localizing or systemic symptoms attributable to UTI)","Complicated UTI extending beyond the bladder (prostatitis, pyelonephritis, bacteremia)","Candiduria representing colonization rather than true infection in a catheterized patient","Other infectious or noninfectious source of fever or altered mentation in a catheterized patient, which must be systematically excluded before attributing symptoms to CAUTI"],"infectionMimics":["Asymptomatic bacteriuria with incidental pyuria","Candida colonization of the catheter/urine without invasive infection","Another concurrent infection (e.g., respiratory, intra-abdominal, bloodstream) causing the fever or systemic findings"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Delirium from non-infectious causes (e.g., metabolic, medication-related) in an older patient, mistaken for UTI-related encephalopathy","Pyuria due to urinary stasis from an obstructed catheter rather than active infection","Hematuria or local irritation from catheter trauma"],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine culture collected from a freshly placed catheter (after exchanging/removing the indwelling catheter) to reflect bladder rather than biofilm microbiology, with susceptibilities to guide therapy","Urine dipstick (nitrite, leukocyte esterase) and microscopic urinalysis, interpreted with awareness that pyuria and bacteriuria are expected in catheterized patients","Systematic workup to exclude alternative infectious and non-infectious causes of fever or altered mental status before settling on CAUTI"],"redFlags":["Fever, rigors, nausea/vomiting, or flank/costovertebral angle pain suggesting infection has extended beyond the bladder (pyelonephritis or bloodstream)","Signs of sepsis in a catheterized patient with systemic symptoms","Isolation of S. aureus from urine, which should prompt consideration of concomitant bacteremia","Persistent fever and/or bacteremia despite appropriate therapy, suggesting renal/perinephric abscess or obstruction requiring drainage"],"prognosis":"Asymptomatic bacteriuria is common in catheterized and elderly patients but does not itself increase risk of infection or death, which argues against treating it. In the absence of anatomic abnormalities (e.g., reflux, obstruction), recurrent urinary infection does not lead to chronic pyelonephritis or renal failure; however, with underlying renal abnormalities such as obstructing stones, infection can accelerate renal parenchymal damage. A randomized trial found more relapses with 3 versus 14 days of therapy, supporting longer treatment than for uncomplicated cystitis (Harrison 22e pp.1094-1095).","pitfalls":["Treating catheter-associated asymptomatic bacteriuria: apart from pregnancy (and pre-urologic-procedure screening), urine culture and treatment are not indicated for asymptomatic catheterized patients, and treating it does not reduce subsequent infection (Harrison 22e pp.1147, 1095).","Attributing nonspecific symptoms such as confusion in an elderly catheterized patient to bacteriuria without excluding other time-sensitive causes of delirium, leading to premature diagnostic closure (Harrison 22e pp.1089, 1147).","Improper treatment of asymptomatic bacteriuria followed by repeat 'test-of-cure' cultures, driving a vicious cycle of escalating antimicrobial use and resistance (Harrison 22e p.1147).","Sending urine cultures in catheterized patients lacking signs/symptoms of UTI (poor diagnostic stewardship), which promotes overdiagnosis and overtreatment (Harrison 22e pp.1089, 1147).","Sampling urine through the existing biofilm-laden catheter rather than from a newly placed catheter, yielding culture results that reflect the catheter wall rather than the bladder (Harrison 22e p.1094).","Routinely using antimicrobial (silver- or nitrofurazone-impregnated) catheters to prevent CAUTI, which has not shown significant clinical benefit (Harrison 22e p.1094)."],"severityClassification":"CAUTI falls within the complicated UTI category, defined by infection in the setting of intermittent or indwelling urinary catheterization. Severity ranges from localized symptomatic infection to infection extending beyond the bladder (pyelonephritis, bacteremia) signaled by fever, rigors, flank pain, and systemic toxicity (Harrison 22e pp.1086-1087, 1089).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1086","Harrison 22e p.1087","Harrison 22e p.1088","Harrison 22e p.1089","Harrison 22e p.1090","Harrison 22e p.1091","Harrison 22e p.1094","Harrison 22e p.1095","Harrison 22e p.1147"],"crossLinks":["COMPLICATED_UTI","PYELONEPHRITIS","CYSTITIS","PROSTATITIS"]},"CELLULITIS":{"name":"Cellulitis","system":"Skin & Soft Tissue","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1095, 1007, 428, 436, 93","clinicalPearls":["Non-purulent cellulitis (no associated abscess, focal purulence, or drainage) is a rapidly spreading, diffuse process typically due to S. pyogenes, often accompanied by lymphangitis and fever; purulent cellulitis arising from a focal lesion (furuncle, carbuncle, abscess, surgical wound) is more likely staphylococcal (Harrison 22e p.1095).","Even with needle aspiration of the advancing edge or punch biopsy of involved tissue, cultures are positive in only about 20% of cases, suggesting low bacterial numbers and that much of the spreading erythema is driven by extracellular toxins and host inflammatory mediators (Harrison 22e p.1095).","When a draining wound or visible portal of entry is present, Gram stain and culture of that material can give a definitive microbiologic diagnosis, unlike cultures of intact cellulitic skin (Harrison 22e p.1095).","A careful exposure/epidemiologic history is diagnostically valuable because many less common causative organisms occupy characteristic ecological niches (e.g., cat/dog bite, freshwater laceration, fish/swine handling, aquarium water) (Harrison 22e p.1095).","Bilateral, symmetric lower-leg involvement favors a mimic such as stasis dermatitis, whereas true cellulitis is usually unilateral (Harrison 22e p.428)."],"pathophysiology":"Bacteria reach the dermis and subcutaneous soft tissue after disruption of the epidermal barrier by cracks, abrasions, cuts, burns, insect bites, surgical incisions, IV catheters, trauma, tinea/fungal infection, or pressure and venous-stasis ulcers. Organisms already colonizing the skin (notably S. aureus and group A streptococci) most readily exploit such breaks, though many exogenous bacteria can be introduced. Streptococcal disease characteristically spreads diffusely via lymphatics, producing lymphangitis, whereas staphylococcal disease spreads from a central focus such as an abscess or infected foreign body. Much of the expanding erythema reflects the action of bacterial extracellular toxins and host-derived inflammatory mediators rather than high bacterial burden. Progression to deeper structures with loss of vascular integrity (purple bullae, ecchymosis, cutaneous anesthesia) signals possible necrotizing infection (Harrison 22e p.1095, p.1007).","additionalDifferentials":["Erysipelas (S. pyogenes superficial dermal/lymphatic infection with sharply demarcated, raised, indurated borders and abrupt fiery-red swelling) (Harrison 22e p.1095)","Necrotizing fasciitis / deeper necrotizing soft-tissue infection (Harrison 22e p.1095)","Purulent cellulitis associated with a focal staphylococcal lesion such as a furuncle, carbuncle, or abscess (Harrison 22e p.1095)"],"infectionMimics":["Herpes zoster (dermatomal vesicular eruption may be mistaken for cellulitis early) (Harrison 22e p.93)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Stasis dermatitis, which when acutely inflamed with crusting and exudate is easily confused with cellulitis; symmetric bilateral leg involvement favors stasis dermatitis (Harrison 22e p.428, p.436)","Eczema (Harrison 22e p.93)","Lymphedema (Harrison 22e p.93)","Contact dermatitis (Harrison 22e p.428)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Gram stain and culture of drainage, open wound, or identifiable portal of entry when present, for definitive etiology (Harrison 22e p.1095)","Needle aspiration of the leading edge or punch biopsy of involved skin in selected cases, recognizing low (~20%) culture yield (Harrison 22e p.1095)"],"redFlags":["Purple/hemorrhagic bullae, ecchymosis, or cutaneous anesthesia suggesting loss of vascular integrity and possible necrotizing fasciitis or myonecrosis (Harrison 22e p.1095)","Pain or tenderness disproportionate to visible skin findings, or unexplained fever with deep soft-tissue pain even without overt skin inflammation (Harrison 22e p.1095)","Brawny edema, dark-red induration, skin friability with bluish/maroon/black discoloration, or systemic toxicity/shock indicating deep necrotizing infection (Harrison 22e p.1095)"],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Routine cultures of intact cellulitic skin are frequently negative (positive in only ~20% even with aspiration or biopsy), so negative cultures do NOT exclude cellulitis (Harrison 22e p.1095).","When patients labeled 'cellulitis' are reviewed by dermatology, roughly one-third have an alternative diagnosis (e.g., eczema, lymphedema), so an overcall risks unnecessary hospitalization and antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.93).","Bilateral symmetric lower-extremity erythema is more likely stasis dermatitis than cellulitis (Harrison 22e p.428).","Early necrotizing fasciitis may present with pain or unexplained fever as the only manifestation; do NOT rely on overt cutaneous inflammation to exclude it (Harrison 22e p.1095)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison distinguishes non-purulent cellulitis (diffuse, spreading, typically streptococcal, often with lymphangitis and fever) from purulent cellulitis (associated with a focal lesion such as furuncle, carbuncle, abscess, or surgical wound, typically staphylococcal, the FDA-preferred term for these). Loss of vascular integrity (purple bullae, ecchymosis, cutaneous anesthesia, systemic toxicity) marks progression toward necrotizing infection (Harrison 22e p.1095).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1095","Harrison 22e p.1007","Harrison 22e p.428","Harrison 22e p.436","Harrison 22e p.93"],"crossLinks":["ERYSIPELAS","NECROTIZING_FASCIITIS","DIABETIC_FOOT","dvt"]},"CHIKUNGUNYA":{"name":"Chikungunya","system":"Tropical Fever","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1060, p.1706, p.1712","clinicalPearls":["Chikungunya virus (CHIKV) is an arthritogenic alphavirus transmitted by Aedes (yellow-fever and Asian tiger) mosquitoes; after a 2-10 day incubation it causes abrupt high fever, sometimes with a saddleback pattern, plus severe arthralgia (Harrison 22e p.1712).","Migratory polyarthritis predominantly involves the small joints of the hands, wrists, ankles, and feet, though larger joints may also be affected; periarticular swelling and immobility are common, while frank effusions are less frequent (Harrison 22e p.1712).","A rash appears at onset or several days in, often coinciding with defervescence around day 2-3, is most intense over trunk and limbs, and may desquamate; children more often develop a bullous rather than maculopapular/petechial rash (Harrison 22e p.1712).","Although petechiae and epistaxis are occasionally seen, CHIKV should not be classified as a cause of viral hemorrhagic fever; older reports of 'chikungunya hemorrhagic fever' largely reflected concurrent dengue coinfection (Harrison 22e p.1712, p.1706).","A recovery period of weeks is common, and a substantial proportion of middle-aged to older patients go on to develop chronic, sometimes disabling arthritis or arthralgia syndromes typically involving the same joints (Harrison 22e p.1712).","Pregnant women are not at increased risk of severe disease, but there is a high risk of maternal-fetal transmission, especially intrapartum, which has occasionally led to fetal death (Harrison 22e p.1712).","A live-attenuated chikungunya vaccine was approved in the United States in 2023 for adults at increased exposure risk and is contraindicated in immunocompromised persons; about 12% of recipients developed chikungunya-like symptoms in trials (Harrison 22e p.1060)."],"pathophysiology":"CHIKV is an arthropod-borne alphavirus (family Togaviridae) spread by Aedes mosquitoes. It produces an acute febrile illness with prominent joint involvement; rheumatic manifestations span arthralgia with or without inflammatory signs (periarticular redness, swelling, immobility). Adults experience more severe and articular-predominant disease than children. Chronic musculoskeletal sequelae are thought to relate to host factors, with increased risk in patients who have preexisting osteoarthritis, severe acute disease, or the HLA-B27 subtype, and may be associated with cryoglobulins (Harrison 22e p.1712).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Dengue (co-circulating Aedes-borne flavivirus; may coinfect) (Harrison 22e p.1712, p.1060)","Zika virus infection (Aedes-borne; can also cause joint manifestations) (Harrison 22e p.1712, p.1060)","Other arthritogenic alphaviruses with clinically similar presentations: Ross River virus, Barmah Forest virus, Sindbis virus, o'nyong-nyong virus, Mayaro virus (Harrison 22e p.1712)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":[],"redFlags":["Cardiorespiratory, neurologic, renal, or ocular complications in severe acute disease (uncommon but reported, typically in hospitalized patients) (Harrison 22e p.1712)","Pregnancy near delivery, given high risk of intrapartum maternal-fetal transmission and reported fetal death (Harrison 22e p.1712)"],"prognosis":"Life-threatening illness is rare; most patients recover, though recovery may take weeks. A significant fraction of middle-aged to older patients develop chronic, potentially disabling arthritis/arthralgia. Deaths have been reported, typically in the setting of large outbreaks. Children generally have milder disease and are less frequently hospitalized (Harrison 22e p.1712, p.1060).","pitfalls":["Do not label CHIKV as a viral hemorrhagic fever despite occasional petechiae/epistaxis; apparent 'hemorrhagic' cases historically reflected concurrent dengue infection (Harrison 22e p.1712, p.1706).","Do not assume severe disease in pregnancy; the principal pregnancy concern is intrapartum vertical transmission rather than maternal severity (Harrison 22e p.1712).","Do not consider the live-attenuated vaccine safe in immunocompromised persons, in whom it is contraindicated (Harrison 22e p.1060)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1060","Harrison 22e p.1706","Harrison 22e p.1712"],"crossLinks":["DENGUE"]},"CHOLANGITIS":{"name":"Acute Cholangitis","system":"Hepatobiliary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"p.2489, p.2735","clinicalPearls":["The classic presentation is Charcot's triad: biliary pain, jaundice, and spiking fevers with rigors; it is present in roughly 70% of patients with ascending cholangitis (Harrison 22e p.2489, p.2735).","Addition of shock and confusion to Charcot's triad constitutes Reynolds's pentad, which signals high mortality and should trigger urgent biliary drainage (Harrison 22e p.2489).","Bile cultures are positive in approximately 75% of patients with acute cholangitis early in the symptomatic course, and blood cultures are frequently positive (Harrison 22e p.2735).","Most acute cholangitis is the nonsuppurative form, which often responds relatively quickly to supportive care and antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.2735).","Initial medical management (fluids and IV antibiotics) usually stabilizes the patient and buys a window of about 24 hours within which biliary drainage should be established, typically by ERCP (Harrison 22e p.2489).","The bile duct may not yet be dilated on early ultrasound during acute biliary obstruction, so a normal-caliber duct does not exclude cholangitis (Harrison 22e p.2489)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute cholangitis arises from infection within an obstructed biliary tree, most often due to choledocholithiasis (common duct stones complicate about 10-15% of cholelithiasis). At least partial obstruction to bile flow combined with bacterial colonization of bile produces inflammation; rising intrabiliary pressure in an obstructed system promotes translocation of organisms into the bloodstream, causing bacteremia and biliary sepsis. In suppurative cholangitis, pus accumulates under pressure within a completely obstructed duct, driving severe toxicity (Harrison 22e p.2489, p.2735).","additionalDifferentials":["Biliary colic / choledocholithiasis without infection","Acute cholecystitis","Gallstone pancreatitis","Obstructive jaundice from malignancy of the pancreatic head, bile ducts, or ampulla of Vater (often painless jaundice)","Primary sclerosing cholangitis with superimposed bacterial cholangitis"],"infectionMimics":["Acute cholecystitis","Hepatic (pyogenic) abscess","Septic shock from another occult source (e.g., pneumonia, pyelonephritis) presenting before the biliary source is evident"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Biliary colic from choledocholithiasis (pain without fever or rigors)","Painless obstructive jaundice from neoplastic biliary obstruction","IgG4-associated (sclerosing) cholangitis, which causes biliary stricturing and is glucocorticoid-responsive","Gallstone pancreatitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum alkaline phosphatase (almost always elevated in biliary obstruction; rise may precede clinical jaundice)","Serum aminotransferases (two- to tenfold elevation, especially with acute obstruction)","ERCP with endoscopic sphincterotomy as the preferred initial procedure for both definitive diagnosis and therapeutic drainage","EUS as a less invasive alternative for detecting duct stones when the pretest probability of a persistent stone is lower"],"redFlags":["Reynolds's pentad (Charcot's triad plus shock and confusion) indicating suppurative cholangitis and high mortality","Mental confusion, bacteremia, and septic shock signaling suppurative cholangitis with pus under pressure","Failure to respond to antibiotics, which in suppurative disease is associated with multiple hepatic abscesses","Delay in establishing biliary drainage beyond the ~24 h stabilization window, risking recrudescent sepsis"],"prognosis":"Nonsuppurative acute cholangitis generally responds well to antibiotics and supportive care. Suppurative acute cholangitis carries a far worse prognosis: response to antibiotics alone is poor and mortality approaches 100% unless prompt endoscopic or surgical relief of the obstruction with drainage of infected bile is achieved. Reynolds's pentad and undue delay in drainage both predict increased morbidity and mortality (Harrison 22e p.2489, p.2735).","pitfalls":["Relying on antibiotics alone in suppurative cholangitis; biliary decompression is required because antibiotic response is poor and mortality is very high without it.","Excluding cholangitis because the bile duct appears non-dilated on early ultrasound, which can occur in acute obstruction.","Awaiting the full Charcot's triad before acting, since not all patients display all three components.","Mistaking the relentless toxicity of suppurative disease for simple sepsis without recognizing the need for urgent source control."],"severityClassification":"Harrison distinguishes nonsuppurative acute cholangitis (the more common, milder form responsive to antibiotics) from suppurative acute cholangitis (pus under pressure in a completely obstructed duct, with severe toxicity, bacteremia, septic shock, and very high mortality without drainage). Clinically, Charcot's triad (pain, jaundice, fever/rigors) defines the typical syndrome, and progression to Reynolds's pentad (adding shock and confusion) marks the severe/suppurative end of the spectrum requiring urgent biliary decompression (Harrison 22e p.2489, p.2735).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2489","Harrison 22e p.2735"],"crossLinks":["CHOLECYSTITIS","biliary_colic","pancreatitis","SEPSIS"]},"CHOLECYSTITIS":{"name":"Acute Cholecystitis","system":"Hepatobiliary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Chap. 357 (Diseases of the Gallbladder and Bile Ducts), pp. 2730-2734; acute abdomen differential/imaging, Chap. 16, pp. 113-115","clinicalPearls":["Acute cholecystitis usually opens as an attack of biliary pain that progressively worsens and becomes more generalized in the right upper abdomen; pain may radiate to the interscapular area, right scapula, or shoulder (Harrison 22e p.2732).","Roughly 60-70% of patients recall prior self-limited biliary attacks before the index episode of acute cholecystitis (Harrison 22e p.2732).","Biliary pain that persists beyond about 5 hours should raise suspicion of acute cholecystitis rather than uncomplicated biliary colic (Harrison 22e p.2731).","Fever or rigors accompanying biliary pain generally signal a complication such as cholecystitis, pancreatitis, or cholangitis (Harrison 22e p.2731).","Murphy's sign: deep inspiration or cough during subcostal palpation of the RUQ produces increased pain with inspiratory arrest, a characteristic bedside finding (Harrison 22e p.2732).","An enlarged, tense gallbladder is palpable in only about 25-50% of cases, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2732).","Generalized peritoneal signs and abdominal rigidity are usually absent in uncomplicated acute cholecystitis; their presence should prompt concern for perforation (Harrison 22e p.2732).","About 75% of medically treated patients remit within 2-7 days, but roughly 25% develop a complication despite conservative management, and recurrence is common (about 25% within 1 year) (Harrison 22e p.2732).","In empyema or free perforation the picture may mimic cholangitis with high fever, severe RUQ pain, marked leukocytosis, and prostration (Harrison 22e p.2733).","A sudden transient relief of RUQ pain (gallbladder decompression) followed by generalized peritonitis suggests free perforation (Harrison 22e pp.2733-2734)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute cholecystitis is acute inflammation of the gallbladder wall that usually follows obstruction of the cystic duct by a stone. Three mechanisms drive the inflammatory response: mechanical inflammation from increased intraluminal pressure and distention producing mucosal/wall ischemia; chemical inflammation from release of lysolecithin (via phospholipase action on biliary lecithin) and other local tissue factors; and bacterial inflammation, which contributes in roughly 50-85% of cases. Chronic cholecystitis is almost always associated with gallstones and results from repeated bouts of subacute/acute inflammation or persistent mechanical irritation of the wall by stones (Harrison 22e pp.2732-2733).","additionalDifferentials":["Choledocholithiasis / common bile duct stone (suggested by elevated bilirubin and/or alkaline phosphatase with biliary pain) (Harrison 22e p.2731)","Peptic ulcer disease, especially perforated peptic ulcer (Harrison 22e p.113, p.155)","Gastritis / GERD (Harrison 22e p.113)","Hepatitis (Harrison 22e p.113)","Right lower-lobe pneumonia / pleurisy / subdiaphragmatic abscess (Harrison 22e p.113)","Mirizzi's syndrome (cystic-duct/gallbladder-neck stone compressing the CBD with obstruction and jaundice) (Harrison 22e p.2732)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute cholangitis (overlapping fever, RUQ pain, leukocytosis; empyema of the gallbladder may clinically resemble cholangitis) (Harrison 22e p.2733)","Hepatitis (Harrison 22e p.113)","Right-sided pneumonia / empyema / subdiaphragmatic abscess presenting as RUQ pain (Harrison 22e p.113)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute pancreatitis (HIDA scanning can help differentiate acute cholecystitis or biliary colic from acute pancreatitis; serum amylase may rise in cholecystitis as well) (Harrison 22e pp.114-115, p.155)","Uncomplicated biliary colic / symptomatic cholelithiasis (Harrison 22e p.2731)","Acalculous cholecystopathy (gallbladder dysmotility causing recurrent biliary-type RUQ pain without stones) (Harrison 22e p.2733)","Sphincter of Oddi dysfunction causing recurrent RUQ pain (Harrison 22e p.2733)","Myocardial infarction / pericarditis (epigastric/RUQ overlap in the acute abdomen differential) (Harrison 22e p.113)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound demonstrates calculi in about 90-95% of cases and shows signs of inflammation: gallbladder wall thickening, pericholecystic fluid, and bile-duct dilatation (Harrison 22e p.2732).","Radionuclide (HIDA/DISIDA) biliary scan can be confirmatory when bile-duct imaging is seen without gallbladder visualization, indicating cystic-duct obstruction; useful when ultrasound is equivocal (Harrison 22e p.2730, p.2732).","WBC count typically shows leukocytosis around 10,000-15,000/uL with a left shift (Harrison 22e p.2732).","Serum bilirubin is mildly elevated (<5 mg/dL [85.5 umol/L]) in fewer than half of patients; about one-fourth have modest aminotransferase elevations (usually <5-fold) (Harrison 22e p.2732).","CT or ultrasound showing a large, tense, static, stone-free gallbladder with poor emptying can support acalculous cholecystitis (Harrison 22e p.2733).","Plain abdominal film may reveal gas within the gallbladder lumen/wall or pericholecystic tissues in emphysematous cholecystitis, and pneumobilia in cholecystoenteric fistula/gallstone ileus (Harrison 22e p.2733)."],"redFlags":["Generalized peritoneal signs / abdominal rigidity (suggests perforation) (Harrison 22e p.2732)","High fever, severe RUQ pain, marked leukocytosis, and prostration resembling cholangitis (suggests empyema of the gallbladder, which carries high risk of gram-negative sepsis and perforation) (Harrison 22e p.2733)","Gas in the gallbladder lumen/wall on imaging (emphysematous cholecystitis; considerable morbidity/mortality) (Harrison 22e p.2733)","Transient relief of RUQ pain followed by generalized peritonitis (free perforation) (Harrison 22e pp.2733-2734)","Acute cholecystitis arising in the setting of severe critical illness (trauma, burns, prolonged fasting, major non-biliary surgery) suggesting acalculous disease with a higher complication rate (Harrison 22e p.2733)"],"prognosis":"Most medically treated patients (about 75%) achieve remission of acute symptoms within 2-7 days, but roughly 25% develop a complication requiring prompt surgery. Among those who remit, about 25% recur within 1 year and 60% within 6 years, so early surgery is preferred. Mortality for emergency cholecystectomy is about 1-3%, versus about 0.5% for early elective cholecystectomy in patients under 60, rising with age and comorbidity. Acalculous and emphysematous forms carry higher complication and mortality rates, and free perforation has a mortality of roughly 30% (Harrison 22e pp.2732-2734).","pitfalls":["Vague post-fatty-meal symptoms (epigastric fullness, dyspepsia, eructation, flatulence) are nonspecific and should not be misread as biliary pain (Harrison 22e p.2731).","Jaundice is unusual early in acute cholecystitis; when present it may reflect bile-duct involvement or a coexisting CBD stone rather than simple cholecystitis (Harrison 22e p.2732).","Leukocytosis alone should never be the single deciding factor for operation, and a normal WBC count does not exclude a surgical abdomen including perforation (Harrison 22e p.155).","Clinical features of acalculous cholecystitis are indistinguishable from calculous disease; diagnosis depends on recognizing the critically ill setting and using imaging (Harrison 22e p.2733).","Emphysematous cholecystitis presents essentially like nongaseous cholecystitis clinically and is identified by imaging, not by a distinct clinical picture; it favors elderly men and diabetics (Harrison 22e p.2733).","Acalculous cholecystitis can occur with unusual organisms (e.g., Leptospira, Salmonella, Vibrio cholerae) or systemic disease, so failure to find stones at surgery does not exclude cholecystitis (Harrison 22e p.2733)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2730","Harrison 22e p.2731","Harrison 22e p.2732","Harrison 22e p.2733","Harrison 22e p.2734","Harrison 22e p.113","Harrison 22e p.114","Harrison 22e p.155"],"crossLinks":["CHOLANGITIS","biliary_colic","pancreatitis"]},"CNS_TB":{"name":"CNS Tuberculosis (TB Meningitis)","system":"Central Nervous System / Tuberculosis","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1124, 1127, 1136, 1389; pharmacogenomics of adjunctive steroids p.1026","clinicalPearls":["Typically evolves over 1-2 weeks (subacute) - a longer course than typical bacterial meningitis; often begins as headache plus subtle mental changes after a prodrome of weeks of low-grade fever, malaise, anorexia and irritability (p.1389).","Because meningeal involvement is most pronounced at the base of the brain, cranial nerve palsies (ocular nerves in particular) are a frequent finding (p.1389).","Involvement of cerebral arteries at the skull base can produce focal ischemia/infarction (p.1389).","Untreated, the disease progresses toward coma with hydrocephalus and intracranial hypertension (p.1389).","In more than half of cases, evidence of old pulmonary lesions or a miliary pattern is found on chest radiograph (p.1389).","CNS TB accounts for roughly 5% of extrapulmonary TB cases; it is seen most often in young children but also in adults, especially those with HIV (p.1389).","The last tube of CSF collected at lumbar puncture is the best tube to send for an AFB smear; if a pellicle or cobweb-like clot forms on the fluid surface, AFB are best demonstrated in a smear of that clot/pellicle (p.1127).","Empiric anti-tuberculous therapy is often started on a high index of clinical suspicion without adequate laboratory support, given the poor smear yield and slow culture (p.1127).","A lymphocytic pleocytosis with low CSF glucose should prompt consideration of fungal or tuberculous meningitis, Listeria meningoencephalitis, or noninfectious causes such as sarcoid or neoplastic meningitis (p.1124)."],"pathophysiology":"Tuberculous meningitis in adults does not arise acutely from direct hematogenous seeding of the meninges. Rather, during hematogenous dissemination of primary infection, miliary (millet seed-sized) tubercles form in the brain parenchyma; these enlarge and usually caseate. Whether a caseous focus produces meningitis depends on its proximity to the subarachnoid space (SAS) and how quickly it becomes fibrous-encapsulated. Subependymal caseous foci discharge bacilli and mycobacterial antigens into the SAS; rupture of a subependymal tubercle into the subarachnoid space is a recognized route. The mycobacterial antigens trigger an intense inflammatory reaction producing a thick exudate that fills the basilar cisterns and encases the cranial nerves and the major blood vessels at the base of the brain - explaining the basal enhancement, cranial nerve palsies, arteritic infarcts and obstructive hydrocephalus seen clinically. Meningitis can also result from hematogenous spread of primary or postprimary pulmonary TB, and is a particular concern in very young children and immunocompromised persons (e.g., people living with HIV) (p.1127, 1389).","additionalDifferentials":["Fungal (subacute/chronic) meningitis, e.g. Cryptococcus neoformans, Histoplasma capsulatum, Coccidioides immitis (p.1124, 1127)","Listeria monocytogenes meningoencephalitis (p.1124)","Syphilitic (Treponema pallidum) meningitis (p.1127)","Neoplastic / carcinomatous meningitis (p.1124)","Neurosarcoidosis (p.1124)"],"infectionMimics":["Cryptococcal meningitis (lymphocytic CSF with low glucose) (p.1124, 1127)","Histoplasma and Coccidioides meningitis (p.1127)","Listeria meningoencephalitis (p.1124)","Neurosyphilis (p.1127)","Viral meningitis due to mumps, LCMV, HSV-2, VZV or enteroviruses, which can occasionally cause low CSF glucose and mimic the TB profile (p.1124)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Neoplastic / neoplastic (carcinomatous) meningitis (p.1124)","Neurosarcoidosis (p.1124)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CSF AFB smear taken from the last LP tube, or from any pellicle/cobweb clot - but smears are positive in only ~10-40% of adult cases (p.1127).","Mycobacterial CSF culture - gold standard, but takes 4-8 weeks and is positive in only ~50% to up to 80% of cases (p.1127, 1389).","CSF nucleic acid amplification testing - Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra or Xpert MTB/RIF; in the TB chapter the Xpert MTB/RIF assay has sensitivity up to 80% and is the preferred initial diagnostic option, with treatment started immediately on a positive result; a negative result does not exclude TB (p.1127, 1389).","CSF opening pressure measurement (characteristically elevated) (p.1127).","Repeat lumbar puncture to increase diagnostic yield, since AFB are infrequently seen on a single direct smear (p.1389).","CT/MRI brain looking specifically for hydrocephalus and abnormal enhancement of the basal cisterns or ependyma; contrast-enhanced ring lesions on CT/MRI suggest tuberculoma (biopsy needed to confirm) (p.1389)."],"redFlags":["Declining level of consciousness / progression toward coma (p.1389)","New cranial nerve palsies, especially ocular nerves, indicating basal meningeal involvement (p.1389)","Focal neurologic deficit suggesting arteritic cerebral infarction (p.1389)","Clinical or imaging evidence of hydrocephalus and raised intracranial pressure (p.1389)","Acute deterioration with severe headache, confusion, lethargy, altered sensorium and neck rigidity in a previously subacute presentation (p.1389)"],"prognosis":"If unrecognized, tuberculous meningitis is uniformly fatal. The disease responds to chemotherapy, but neurologic sequelae are documented in about 25% of treated cases - most often when the diagnosis was delayed. Adjunctive glucocorticoids may speed resolution of CSF abnormalities and raised CSF pressure and lower rates of death or severe disability and relapse; in one trial adjunctive dexamethasone improved survival in patients >14 years but did not reduce the frequency of neurologic sequelae. Among people living with HIV, a placebo-controlled study showed no survival or secondary-endpoint benefit from a 6-8 week tapering course of adjunctive dexamethasone (p.1389). Pharmacogenomic data suggest only a subset of patients benefit from steroids: those homozygous for the proinflammatory LTA4H allele were most helped, whereas those homozygous for the anti-inflammatory allele may be harmed (p.1026).","pitfalls":["Low CSF smear yield (AFB seen on direct smear in only ~10-40% of adult cases) and slow culture (4-8 weeks) mean diagnosis cannot wait for microbiologic confirmation - empiric anti-TB therapy is appropriately started on high clinical suspicion (p.1127).","Any one of the classic CSF parameters (cell count, protein, glucose) can be within the normal range, so a normal value does not exclude TB meningitis (p.1389).","A negative CSF Xpert MTB/RIF result does not exclude TB and requires further diagnostic workup (p.1389).","Early in the course CSF may show a neutrophil predominance rather than lymphocytes, which can mislead toward bacterial meningitis (p.1389).","Delayed recognition allows the subacute illness to evolve acutely (severe headache, confusion, altered sensorium, neck rigidity) and is the main driver of death and of the ~25% rate of neurologic sequelae (p.1389)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison describes a clinical continuum that maps to the British MRC staging used for TB meningitis: an early stage with headache and slight mental changes after a prodrome of low-grade fever/malaise/anorexia, evolving (if unrecognized) to severe headache, confusion, lethargy and altered sensorium with neck rigidity, and ultimately to coma with hydrocephalus and intracranial hypertension; outcome worsens markedly with more advanced stage at presentation/diagnostic delay (p.1389).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1124","Harrison 22e p.1127","Harrison 22e p.1136","Harrison 22e p.1389","Harrison 22e p.1026"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS","PULMONARY_TB","DISSEMINATED_TB"]},"COMPLICATED_UTI":{"name":"Complicated Urinary Tract Infection","system":"Genitourinary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1129-1138","clinicalPearls":["Harrison defines complicated UTI as infection extending beyond the bladder (e.g., prostatitis, pyelonephritis, bacteremia) or UTI occurring in the setting of intermittent or indwelling catheterization; this is a shift from the older approach that automatically labeled any UTI in men, diabetics, the immunocompromised, or those with anatomic abnormalities as complicated (p.1129-1130).","Recurrent UTI is not inherently complicated; individual episodes may be uncomplicated and managed as such (p.1130).","Fever, rigors, and unilateral back or flank pain are incompatible with infection confined to the bladder and should trigger evaluation for infection beyond the bladder (kidney, prostate, or bloodstream) (p.1132).","In men, UTI is uncommon outside the context of catheterization/instrumentation or obstructive uropathy from prostatic hypertrophy; a urine culture should be obtained in every case of male UTI (p.1132).","Men with febrile UTI, even at first episode, should undergo imaging (CT or ultrasound) if not done previously, specifically to assess for urinary tract abnormalities or obstruction (p.1132).","Predictors of a surgically correctable disorder in men with febrile UTI include urinary retention symptoms, early UTI recurrence, hematuria at follow-up, and voiding difficulties; men with none of these had normal urinary tracts on workup (p.1132).","Early recurrence within 2 weeks is regarded as relapse rather than reinfection and may signal a sequestered focus of ongoing infection that needs evaluation (p.1130).","Foreign bodies such as stones, stents, and catheters provide an inert surface for bacterial colonization and persistent biofilm formation, serving as a nidus for recurrent infection (p.1130-1131).","Initial evaluation of recurrent cystitis should include imaging to identify renal calculi or another nidus of infection and assessment for urinary retention (p.1138)."],"pathophysiology":"Most UTIs arise by ascent of bacteria from the urethra to the bladder, with further ascent up the ureter producing renal parenchymal infection; the hematogenous route accounts for under 2% of cases and typically involves virulent organisms such as S. aureus and Salmonella (isolation of either from urine should prompt consideration of concomitant bacteremia). Whether bladder entry leads to symptomatic infection depends on the interplay of host, pathogen, and environmental factors. Conditions causing urinary stasis or obstruction (vesicoureteral reflux, ureteral obstruction from prostatic hypertrophy, neurogenic bladder, urinary-diversion surgery) and foreign bodies that support biofilm all predispose to infection. Uropathogenic E. coli virulence factors include P fimbriae (mediating binding to renal epithelial receptors and important in pyelonephritis and bloodstream invasion) and type 1 pili (mediating mannose binding and initiating bladder infection), plus toxins, iron-acquisition systems, biofilm formation, and capsules. Candiduria is pathogenetically distinct in that the hematogenous route is more common, and in non-instrumented immunocompetent patients its presence implies either genital contamination or possible widespread visceral dissemination (p.1131).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute bacterial prostatitis (consider in men with UTI plus systemic symptoms and no other clear anatomic source) (p.1132)","Chronic bacterial prostatitis (consider with recurrent UTI in men, especially outside catheterization) (p.1132)","Pyelonephritis with or without urinary obstruction (p.1132-1133)","Chronic pelvic pain syndrome in men (not associated with bacteriuria, not improved by antibiotics) (p.1132)"],"infectionMimics":["Sexually transmitted infection / cervicitis (Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoeae) presenting with dysuria (p.1133)","Vaginitis (Candida albicans, Trichomonas vaginalis) (p.1133)","Herpetic urethritis (p.1133)","Urethritis from STI pathogens (gonococcal, chlamydial, Mycoplasma genitalium); urethral pain, pruritus, or discharge should prompt STI evaluation (p.1130, p.1133)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Asymptomatic bacteriuria misclassified as UTI, especially in older patients with isolated altered mentation; degree of pyuria should not drive the distinction (p.1132)","Interstitial cystitis (p.1133)","Noninfectious vaginal or vulvar irritation (p.1133)","Chronic pelvic pain syndrome in men (p.1132)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CT or ultrasound imaging in men with febrile UTI to evaluate for anatomic abnormality or obstruction (p.1132)","Blood cultures when complicated UTI, pyelonephritis, or systemic features are present (p.1134 diagnostic algorithm)","Urine dipstick (nitrite and leukocyte esterase) and microscopic urinalysis as point-of-care tools mainly useful for ruling out UTI when the clinical diagnosis is unclear (p.1133-1135)","Imaging to identify renal calculi or other nidus, plus assessment for urinary retention, in recurrent UTI (p.1138)","Serum creatinine monitoring (a rapid rise may be the first sign of bilateral papillary necrosis) (p.1133)"],"redFlags":["Fever, rigors, nausea/vomiting, and flank pain suggesting severe pyelonephritis or infection beyond the bladder (p.1133)","Continued fever and/or bacteremia despite antibacterial therapy, suggesting intraparenchymal renal abscess (p.1133)","Failure to respond clinically within 72 h, prompting evaluation for an uncontrolled source of infection (p.1137)","Gas in renal/perinephric tissues (emphysematous pyelonephritis), almost exclusive to poorly controlled diabetes (p.1133)","Acute papillary necrosis with sloughed papillae obstructing the ureter complicating pyelonephritis (p.1133)","Isolation of S. aureus or Salmonella from urine, raising concern for concomitant bacteremia / hematogenous seeding (p.1131)","Symptoms of urinary retention, early UTI recurrence, hematuria at follow-up, or voiding difficulty in men with febrile UTI (predict surgically correctable disease) (p.1132)"],"prognosis":"Most simple cystitis carries a low risk of serious complication, but tissue-invasive complicated disease (pyelonephritis and beyond) carries greater risk and patients are often systemically ill. Pyelonephritis may be complicated by acute papillary necrosis with obstruction (requiring relief of obstruction to preserve renal function), perinephric or renal cortical abscess (often requiring percutaneous drainage), emphysematous pyelonephritis (a severe form, almost exclusive to poorly controlled diabetes), and xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis (suppurative renal destruction associated with chronic obstruction, often by staghorn calculi, that can mimic renal malignancy and frequently requires nephrectomy). Bilateral papillary necrosis is rare but may present with a rapid rise in creatinine (p.1133, p.1137).","pitfalls":["Treating asymptomatic bacteriuria outside pregnancy or pre-urologic-procedure settings: it does not reduce future UTI and may even increase risk, possibly by promoting colonization with more virulent/resistant pathogens (p.1129, p.1136).","Ending the workup of a febrile catheterized patient at an abnormal urinalysis: bacteriuria and pyuria are essentially inevitable with catheterization, so other infectious and noninfectious causes must be systematically excluded before diagnosing CAUTI (p.1133-1134).","Premature diagnostic closure in elderly patients with isolated delirium; collateral history is needed and a reported history of recurrent UTIs may actually reflect persistent ASB (p.1132).","Relying on a negative urine dipstick to rule out bacteriuria in pregnancy; it is not sensitive enough and culture-based ASB screening is required (p.1135).","Using nitrofurantoin or fosfomycin as if they treat tissue-invasive disease; they do not achieve adequate renal tissue levels and should not be used for pyelonephritis, and nitrofurantoin is inactive against many Proteus, Providencia, Morganella, Pseudomonas, Serratia, and some Klebsiella/Enterobacter (knowledge of intrinsic resistance) (p.1136-1137).","Interpreting a mixed-flora urine culture as infection; it usually represents contamination except in long-term catheterization, chronic retention, or urinary-enteric/genital fistula (p.1135)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames the practical categorization around whether infection is confined to the bladder (uncomplicated cystitis) versus extends beyond it (complicated UTI: prostatitis, pyelonephritis, bacteremia) and whether catheter-associated (CAUTI). The key bedside questions are whether the patient is stable for outpatient management, whether a recalcitrant/recurrent source (e.g., obstructing calculi) must be identified, and whether the chosen agent must reach adequate blood and renal-tissue levels in addition to urine. Pyelonephritis itself ranges from mild (low-grade fever +/- back or costovertebral angle pain) to severe (high fever, rigors, nausea, vomiting, flank pain) (p.1130, p.1133).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1129","Harrison 22e p.1130","Harrison 22e p.1131","Harrison 22e p.1132","Harrison 22e p.1133","Harrison 22e p.1134","Harrison 22e p.1135","Harrison 22e p.1136","Harrison 22e p.1137","Harrison 22e p.1138"],"crossLinks":["CYSTITIS","PYELONEPHRITIS","CA_UTI"]},"COPD_EXACERBATION":{"name":"Acute COPD Exacerbation","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1305-1308, p.1534-1535, p.1756, p.2301-2302","clinicalPearls":["An exacerbation is defined clinically as an episodic acute worsening of respiratory symptoms (increased dyspnea, cough, and/or a change in sputum volume and character), usually evolving over fewer than 14 days; it may or may not be accompanied by fever, myalgias, or sore throat (p.2301).","COPD exacerbation is a clinical diagnosis; no single test confirms it, and measurement of pulmonary function (unlike in asthma) has not been shown to aid diagnosis or management of a COPD exacerbation (p.2301).","The strongest single predictor of future exacerbations is a history of a previous exacerbation, and the single greatest risk factor for hospitalization is a history of a previous hospitalization (p.2301).","The cardinal symptoms of a bacterial exacerbation are increased cough with increased sputum production, sputum purulence, and increased dyspnea relative to baseline; nontypeable H. influenzae exacerbations typically show only low-grade fever and no infiltrate on chest x-ray (p.1305-1307).","Exacerbation frequency rises as airflow obstruction worsens: patients with severe obstruction (FEV1 <50% predicted) average 1-3 episodes per year, though some severely obstructed patients are not frequent exacerbators (p.2301).","A target oxygen saturation of 88-92% is used; in patients with acute or chronic hypercarbia, supplemental O2 does not reduce minute ventilation but may modestly raise arterial PCO2 by altering ventilation-perfusion relationships - this should not deter giving the oxygen needed to correct hypoxemia (p.2302)."],"pathophysiology":"A variety of stimuli converge on a final common pathway of airway inflammation with increased respiratory symptoms. Viral respiratory infection, once thought less common than bacterial infection, is detected by PCR-based studies in over half of exacerbations; acquisition of a new bacterial strain is associated with increased near-term exacerbation risk. Non-infective contributors include air pollution, allergens, and environmental/Aspergillus sensitization, and in a significant minority no specific precipitant is identified (p.1756, p.2301).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Pneumonia - the most frequent abnormal finding on chest imaging in this setting; about 25% of x-rays obtained for moderate-to-severe exacerbations in severe COPD are abnormal (p.2301)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pulmonary embolism - should be actively considered because the incidence of PE is increased during COPD exacerbations (p.2301).","Congestive heart failure - a common alternative or contributing finding on imaging in patients presenting as an exacerbation (p.2301).","Pneumothorax - asymmetry on chest examination may indicate pneumothorax (or large-airway obstruction) mimicking an exacerbation (p.2301)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas in patients with advanced COPD, a history of hypercarbia, altered mental status (confusion, sleepiness), or significant distress; hypercarbia is defined as PCO2 >45 mmHg and carries treatment implications (p.2301).","Chest x-ray or chest CT in patients with severe underlying COPD, moderate-to-severe distress, or focal examination findings (p.2301).","C-reactive protein, as part of emerging expert-consensus objective criteria (combining dyspnea, vital signs, CRP, and arterial blood gases) to diagnose and stage exacerbation severity, mainly of use in the emergency department (p.2301-2302)."],"redFlags":["Respiratory acidosis with hypercarbia (PCO2 >45 mmHg, pH <=7.35) - an indication for noninvasive positive-pressure ventilation, which reduces mortality, need for intubation, complications, and length of stay (p.2302).","Severe respiratory distress, severe hypercarbia and/or acidosis despite noninvasive ventilation, markedly impaired mental status, respiratory arrest, or hemodynamic instability - indications for invasive mechanical ventilation via endotracheal tube (p.2302).","New or worsening hypoxemia, severe underlying COPD, or significant comorbidity such as heart failure - features suggesting the need for inpatient treatment (p.2301).","Inability to speak in complete sentences, accessory muscle use, perioral or peripheral cyanosis, tachypnea, tachycardia, altered mental status, or paradoxical abdominal wall motion on examination (p.2301)."],"prognosis":"Mortality for patients requiring mechanical ventilatory support during a COPD exacerbation is 17-49% for that hospitalization. After any hospitalization for COPD, about 20% are rehospitalized within 30 days and 45% within a year, and mortality is approximately 20% in the year after discharge. Because of the high mortality of invasive ventilation, advance directives (e.g., resuscitation preferences) should be discussed in the outpatient setting (p.2302).","pitfalls":["Withholding needed supplemental oxygen for fear of CO2 retention is an error; oxygen should be titrated to 88-92% saturation to correct hypoxemia despite a possible modest PCO2 rise (p.2302).","Contraindications to noninvasive positive-pressure ventilation that must be screened for include cardiovascular instability, impaired mental status, inability to cooperate, copious or unclearable secretions, and craniofacial abnormality or trauma preventing mask fit (p.2302).","M. catarrhalis is easily mistaken for commensal Neisseria species on respiratory culture and was historically overlooked as a pathogen, so culture interpretation requires care (p.1308)."],"severityClassification":"Assessment should grade both the severity of the acute exacerbation and the severity of preexisting COPD; the more severe either component, the greater the likelihood of needing hospital admission. An expert consensus has proposed more objective criteria to diagnose and stage exacerbation severity using dyspnea, vital signs, C-reactive protein, and arterial blood gases, although this schema is more useful in the emergency department than the outpatient setting (p.2301-2302).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1305","Harrison 22e p.1306","Harrison 22e p.1307","Harrison 22e p.1308","Harrison 22e p.1534","Harrison 22e p.1535","Harrison 22e p.1756","Harrison 22e p.2301","Harrison 22e p.2302"],"crossLinks":["copd_exac_ni","asthma_exac","pe","heart_failure","pneumothorax"]},"CYSTITIS":{"name":"Acute Uncomplicated Cystitis","system":"Genitourinary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chapter 140 (Urinary Tract Infections: Cystitis, Prostatitis, and Pyelonephritis), p.1085-1092","clinicalPearls":["Bacteria in the urine without related symptoms is asymptomatic bacteriuria (ASB), not UTI; ASB usually needs no treatment and the diagnosis of cystitis requires symptoms, not merely a positive culture or pyuria (p.1086-1087).","In an otherwise healthy non-pregnant woman with typical symptoms and no complicating factors, cystitis can be diagnosed and treated on history alone, with no dipstick or culture required (p.1089, p.1091).","Patient self-diagnosis of recurrent cystitis is highly accurate, which underlies the success of patient-initiated treatment for recurrent UTI (p.1090).","Fever, rigors, or unilateral back/flank pain are inconsistent with uncomplicated cystitis and should prompt evaluation for infection beyond the bladder (kidney, prostate, or bloodstream) (p.1089).","A dipstick negative for both nitrite and leukocyte esterase should prompt consideration of non-UTI causes for symptoms, but a small fraction can be falsely negative and a negative dipstick does not fully exclude UTI in a symptomatic woman (p.1089, p.1092).","Avoid sending urine cultures in patients lacking urinary or systemic symptoms (diagnostic stewardship) to reduce overdiagnosis of UTI and inappropriate treatment of ASB (p.1089).","In older patients presenting only with altered mentation and chronic cognitive deficits, distinguishing true UTI from incidental ASB is difficult; clinicians should look for sepsis, seek collateral history, and rule out other time-sensitive causes of delirium before attributing it to UTI (p.1089).","A reported history of recurrent UTIs in an elderly patient may actually reflect persistent ASB rather than true infection (p.1089)."],"pathophysiology":"Most cystitis arises by ascending infection: gut flora (typically E. coli) colonize the vaginal introitus and periurethral area, then ascend the urethra into the bladder. Introduction of bacteria into the bladder does not inevitably cause sustained symptomatic infection; normal voiding and innate bladder defenses often clear organisms (e.g., after intercourse). Outcome reflects the interplay of host, organism, and environmental factors. Anything that increases bacterial entry or retention raises risk. The shorter female urethra is considered the primary anatomic reason cystitis predominates in young women over young men. Uropathogenic E. coli express virulence factors including type 1 pili (fimbriae) that bind mannose on bladder uroepithelium and initiate bladder infection (P fimbriae are more relevant to pyelonephritis/bloodstream invasion); toxins, iron-acquisition systems, biofilm formation, and capsules also contribute. Disruption of the normal vaginal lactobacilli (e.g., by spermicide/nonoxynol-9, or estrogen loss after menopause) permits Gram-negative colonization and bacteriuria. Hematogenous spread accounts for under 2% of UTIs. Intracellular bacterial communities within bladder epithelium are demonstrated in models but their clinical significance in humans is unclear (p.1088).","additionalDifferentials":["Cervicitis (Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoeae) presenting with dysuria (p.1090)","Urethritis, including gonococcal/chlamydial and herpetic urethritis (p.1087, p.1090)","Sexually transmitted infection mistaken for UTI, especially with multiple partners or inconsistent condom use; vaginal discharge should prompt STI consideration (p.1090-1091)"],"infectionMimics":["Vaginitis due to Candida albicans or Trichomonas vaginalis causing dysuria (p.1090)","Sexually transmitted urethritis/cervicitis (gonococcal, chlamydial, Mycoplasma genitalium) (p.1087, p.1090)","Acute or chronic bacterial prostatitis in men presenting as cystitis-like symptoms (p.1089)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Interstitial cystitis / bladder pain syndrome (p.1090)","Noninfectious vaginal or vulvar irritation (p.1090)","Chronic pelvic pain syndrome in men (not associated with bacteriuria, not improved by antibiotics) (p.1089)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine dipstick for nitrite and leukocyte esterase as point-of-care testing, used mainly to help rule out UTI when the clinical diagnosis is unclear (p.1091)","Microscopic urinalysis (white and red cell counts); pyuria is present in nearly all cystitis and hematuria in about 30% (p.1092)","Urine culture when a resistant organism is suspected, in recurrent UTI, after prior treatment failure, or in all male UTI; a colony count threshold of >10^2 bacteria/mL is more sensitive than 10^5/mL for diagnosing cystitis in symptomatic women (p.1089, p.1092)","STI screening / pelvic examination in women with risk factors for STI or with vaginal discharge (p.1090-1091)"],"redFlags":["Fever, rigors, or unilateral back/costovertebral or flank pain suggesting pyelonephritis or other extension beyond the bladder (p.1089)","Perineal, pelvic, or prostatic pain in a man, suggesting prostatitis (p.1089, p.1091)","Recurrent cystitis in a man, warranting evaluation for a prostatic focus and for urinary retention (p.1089)","Vaginal discharge raising concern for STI rather than UTI (p.1090-1091)","Early recurrence within 2 weeks (relapse), suggesting a sequestered focus of ongoing infection (p.1087)"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated cystitis generally responds promptly and favorably to appropriately chosen modern antimicrobials. About 20-30% of women who have one UTI develop recurrent episodes, and recurrences may cluster after an initial infection; recurrence likelihood decreases as time since the last infection increases. Withholding effective antimicrobial therapy (e.g., treating with NSAIDs alone, or strict delayed therapy) prolongs discomfort and carries a small increased risk of progression to pyelonephritis (p.1087, p.1092).","pitfalls":["Treating ASB as if it were UTI; the degree of pyuria in an asymptomatic patient should not drive a UTI diagnosis, and very high pyuria is common with urinary stasis (e.g., end-stage renal disease, obstructed catheters) (p.1089).","Relying on nitrite: only Enterobacterales convert nitrate to nitrite, so it can be falsely negative with non-Enterobacterales organisms or with frequent voiding from high fluid intake, and can be positive in Enterobacterales ASB (p.1091-1092).","Treating an STI (especially C. trachomatis) as UTI because symptoms overlap; women at risk for UTI are often also at risk for STI (p.1090-1091).","Assuming a negative dipstick excludes infection; it is not sensitive enough to rule out bacteriuria in pregnancy, where culture-based ASB screening is required (p.1092).","The >10 WBC/HPF pyuria cutoff has poor specificity (about 36%) for UTI; a much higher count may correlate better with symptoms, though >10/HPF remains the accepted standard (p.1092).","Misinterpreting a mixed-flora culture as infection; mixed growth usually indicates contamination except with long-term catheterization, chronic retention, or a urinary-enteric/genital fistula (p.1092)."],"severityClassification":"Uncomplicated UTI is infection confined to the bladder in a woman or man without a urinary catheter; complicated UTI is infection extending beyond the bladder (prostatitis, pyelonephritis, bacteremia) or occurring with intermittent/indwelling catheterization (CAUTI). Recurrent UTI is not inherently complicated, since individual episodes may be uncomplicated and treated as such. This contrasts with the older approach that automatically classified UTI in men, diabetics, those with anatomic abnormalities, or the immunocompromised as complicated (p.1086-1087).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1085","Harrison 22e p.1086","Harrison 22e p.1087","Harrison 22e p.1088","Harrison 22e p.1089","Harrison 22e p.1090","Harrison 22e p.1091","Harrison 22e p.1092"],"crossLinks":["PYELONEPHRITIS","COMPLICATED_UTI","PROSTATITIS"]},"C_DIFF":{"name":"Clostridioides difficile Infection","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chapter 139, p.1082-1086 (with CT-imaging note p.640)","clinicalPearls":["CDI is the most commonly diagnosed hospital-acquired diarrhoeal illness and the leading health care-associated infection; it follows ingestion of spores that germinate and produce toxins once the normal colonic microbiota has been disrupted (Harrison 22e p.1082).","Almost any antibiotic can precipitate CDI, including vancomycin itself; clindamycin, ampicillin, cephalosporins (especially 2nd/3rd generation), and fluoroquinolones carry the highest risk, whereas penicillin/beta-lactamase-inhibitor combinations carry notably less (Harrison 22e p.1082).","Proton pump inhibitors are at most a modest risk factor; the data are weak and PPIs have not been clearly implicated in patients not already on antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.1082).","Asymptomatic faecal carriage rises with length of hospital stay (often >=20% after 2 weeks inpatient) versus only 1-3% in community residents; neonates and infants are very frequently colonised yet almost never develop disease, attributed to absent mucosal toxin receptors early in life (Harrison 22e p.1082-1083).","Stools in CDI are essentially never grossly bloody and have a characteristic odour, ranging from soft/unformed to watery or mucoid (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Recurrence after treatment occurs in roughly 15-30% of cases and may be either relapse with the same strain or reinfection with a new one, driven by ongoing disruption of the faecal microbiota by the treating antibiotic; a first recurrence raises the risk of a second (Harrison 22e p.1084-1085).","Serum IgG antibody to toxins A and B from prior colonisation/childhood exposure helps determine who develops symptomatic disease versus remains an asymptomatic carrier and protects against recurrence (Harrison 22e p.1083).","Spores persist on environmental surfaces for months and on the hands of staff; alcohol hand gels are NOT sporicidal, so soap-and-water hand washing is required during outbreaks (Harrison 22e p.1083, p.1086)."],"pathophysiology":"Ingested spores of toxigenic C. difficile survive gastric acid, germinate in the small bowel, and colonise the lower gut once antibiotic exposure has disrupted the protective microbiota. There they elaborate two large clostridial toxins: toxin A (an enterotoxin and potent neutrophil chemoattractant) and toxin B (a cytotoxin). Both glucosylate Rho-family GTP-binding proteins that regulate the actin cytoskeleton, causing loss of cell shape, adherence and tight junctions, with epithelial barrier breakdown, fluid leakage, diarrhoea and pseudomembrane formation. Toxin B appears to be the dominant virulence factor: disease occurs with toxin A-negative but not toxin B-negative strains. A third (binary) toxin, CDT, is present in all NAP1/BI/027 epidemic isolates but its pathogenic role is undefined. Disease typically requires at least three events: antibiotic exposure establishing susceptibility, acquisition of a toxigenic strain, and an inadequate host (anamnestic antibody) immune response. Notably, patients colonised by (often nontoxigenic) strains who have not previously had CDI actually have a DECREASED, not increased, risk of subsequent CDI (Harrison 22e p.1082-1084).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Only diarrhoeal (unformed) stool should be tested; testing of asymptomatic patients is not recommended except for epidemiologic study (Harrison 22e p.1084).","NAAT/PCR for the toxin gene is rapid and sensitive but may detect mere colonisation in a patient whose diarrhoea is due to another cause; toxin EIA is specific but insensitive (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Recommended multi-step algorithms: NAAT followed by toxin EIA on NAAT-positive results, or GDH (glutamate dehydrogenase common-antigen) plus toxin EIA with NAAT as arbiter when the two disagree; confirming actual toxin in stool is favoured to avoid over-diagnosing colonisation (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Empirical treatment is appropriate if stool testing is delayed (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Endoscopy (sigmoidoscopy/colonoscopy) is highly specific only if pseudomembranes are seen but is insensitive (pseudomembranes visible in only ~50% of culture/toxin-positive diarrhoea); a negative endoscopy does NOT exclude CDI. It is most useful as a rapid test in the seriously ill patient with an acute abdomen and no diarrhoea (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Abdominal CT in colitis shows bowel-wall thickening (significantly more prominent than in other colitides), wall oedema, mesenteric stranding and ascites (Harrison 22e p.640, p.1085)."],"redFlags":["Unexplained leukocytosis with WBC >=15,000/uL, especially when stool passage has ceased due to adynamic ileus (ileus seen on x-ray in ~20%) - a clue to unsuspected CDI and a marker of high risk for toxic megacolon and sepsis (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Marked leukocytosis >=20,000 WBCs/uL with an acute abdomen suggests fulminant CDI (Harrison 22e p.1085).","Fulminant disease may present WITHOUT diarrhoea, mimicking an acute surgical abdomen, with sepsis (hypotension, fever, tachycardia, leukocytosis), obstruction/ileus, colon-wall thickening and ascites - include CDI in the differential of acute abdomen, sepsis or toxic megacolon in anyone given antibiotics in the prior 2 months (Harrison 22e p.1085).","Toxic megacolon, colonic perforation, shock and the need for colectomy denote fulminant/complicated disease; a rising serum lactate (aim to operate before it reaches 5 mmol/L) signals the need for surgery (Harrison 22e p.1085-1086).","Even a single recurrence carries an ~11% risk of serious complications (shock, megacolon, perforation, colectomy or death within 30 days) (Harrison 22e p.1085)."],"prognosis":"Most patients recover, but recurrences are common (~15-30%). Attributable mortality, historically 0.6-3.5%, has reached ~6.9% in recent epidemic-strain outbreaks and rises progressively with increasing age (Harrison 22e p.1086).","pitfalls":["Do not perform a 'test of cure' after treatment: >50% of patients keep harbouring the organism and its toxin after diarrhoea resolves, and results do not predict recurrence; such results must not be used to restrict long-term-care or nursing-home placement (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Distinguish colonisation from infection - a positive NAAT/PCR in a patient with diarrhoea from another cause may simply reflect colonisation; treating asymptomatic carriers is not indicated (Harrison 22e p.1084).","In adynamic ileus the diagnosis is frequently overlooked because stool passage stops - look for unexplained leukocytosis (Harrison 22e p.1084).","A negative endoscopy does not rule out CDI, and pseudomembranes are absent in roughly half of confirmed cases (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Antiperistaltic agents and opiates may mask symptoms and possibly worsen disease and should generally be avoided (Harrison 22e p.1084)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison applies the IDSA/SHEA-aligned severity tiers as treatment-decision markers: SEVERE disease is indicated by leukocytosis >=15,000 WBCs/uL and/or a serum creatinine >=1.5 mg/dL; FULMINANT (severe complicated) disease is severe CDI plus hypotension/shock, ileus, or toxic megacolon, often with leukocytosis >=20,000/uL and a rising lactate. Mild-to-moderate disease lacks these features (Harrison 22e p.1084-1085).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1082","Harrison 22e p.1083","Harrison 22e p.1084","Harrison 22e p.1085","Harrison 22e p.1086","Harrison 22e p.640"],"crossLinks":["GASTROENTERITIS","DYSENTERY","ibd_flare"]},"DENGUE":{"name":"Dengue Fever","system":"Tropical Fever","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1711-1714, 1718, 1721-1722","clinicalPearls":["Symptomatic dengue evolves through three phases: febrile, critical (plasma leakage), and recovery; most patients never enter the critical phase, but the possibility must be actively considered in every case (p.1714).","The critical/plasma-leakage phase characteristically begins around the time fever subsides (defervescence, roughly days 3-5), so worsening at this transition - not at peak fever - signals deterioration (p.1721).","A 'saddle-back' fever pattern (recurrence of fever after defervescence and a brief remission) can occur in a subset of patients (p.1714).","Most primary DENV infections are subclinical, and only about 25% of the ~400 million annual infections cause clinical illness (p.1711).","Incubation is approximately 4-7 days (p.1714).","A positive tourniquet test (>=10 new petechiae per square inch on the upper arm after BP-cuff inflation for 5 min) reflects microvascular damage and is more often linked to severe dengue (p.1714).","Near defervescence a maculopapular rash typically starts on the trunk and spreads to the extremities and face; a separate transient macular rash may appear at illness onset (p.1714).","During recovery a patchy erythematous pruritic rash that desquamates may develop as extravascular fluid and effusions are reabsorbed (p.1722).","Early IgG appearing about 4 days after onset and rising rapidly suggests a secondary (rather than primary) infection, where IgG otherwise appears later and at much lower titers (p.1721)."],"pathophysiology":"Dengue is caused by the four flavivirus (orthoflavivirus) serotypes DENV-1 to DENV-4, transmitted mainly by Aedes aegypti (and the secondary vector Aedes albopictus), which bite during the day. After infection of one serotype, the cross-protection against a different (heterotypic) serotype is only transient and wanes within weeks to a few weeks of convalescence; subsequent heterotypic infection can produce classic dengue or, less often, severe dengue. The progression to severe disease centers on immune-mediated enhancement, in which enhancing, non-neutralizing antibodies are implicated, whereas neutralizing antibodies are associated with lower viremia on reinfection. The hallmark of severe dengue is a vascular-leak syndrome producing increased capillary permeability, intravascular volume depletion, hypoalbuminemia, and serosal (pleural, ascitic) effusions; recent evidence assigns the DENV NS1 protein a key role in this vascular leak. Host/viral risk modifiers include age (susceptibility to severe dengue drops markedly after 12 years), the specific sequence and timing of serotype infections (e.g., DENV-1 then DENV-2 tends to be more severe), and viral-variant heterogeneity (p.1711, 1721).","additionalDifferentials":["Zika virus disease (often misdiagnosed as dengue; low-grade fever, itchy maculopapular rash, arthralgia/myalgia, nonpurulent conjunctivitis)","Influenza","Other arboviral fevers presenting as fever-and-myalgia syndrome"],"infectionMimics":["Chikungunya","Zika virus infection","Yellow fever","Leptospirosis","Other viral hemorrhagic fevers"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antigen-detection ELISA or RT-PCR (nucleic acid amplification) during the acute phase, useful within roughly the first 5 days of illness (p.1714, 1721)","Combination point-of-care testing pairing NS1 antigen with IgM antibody assay, increasingly used clinically (p.1721)","Paired (acute and convalescent) serology during recovery (p.1714)","Serum AST (often modestly elevated without true hepatic synthetic dysfunction) (p.1714)","Tourniquet test as a bedside marker of microvascular fragility (p.1714)"],"redFlags":["Onset of restlessness or lethargy together with thrombocytopenia (<100,000/uL) and hemoconcentration 2-5 days into illness, typically at defervescence (p.1721)","Signs of plasma leakage: rising hematocrit/hemoconcentration, hypoalbuminemia, and serosal effusions (pleural effusion, ascites) (p.1721)","Circulatory compromise with narrowed pulse pressure, hepatomegaly, and cyanosis progressing to shock (p.1721)","Severe or spontaneous bleeding in the absence of a predisposing lesion (p.1721)","Severe organ dysfunction: altered mental status or seizures (CNS), arrhythmias (cardiac), acute kidney injury (renal), hepatic injury (p.1721)","Respiratory distress from plasma leakage (p.1721)"],"prognosis":"Most patients with uncomplicated dengue recover from the acute illness within 7-10 days, though convalescence may be prolonged and post-acute fatigue and depression are recognized sequelae. Among the millions of annual DENV infections, about 500,000 progress to severe dengue, with an overall case-fatality rate near 2.5%; with early recognition and good supportive care most patients with severe dengue respond well, and CFR at experienced tropical centers can be as low as ~1% (p.1714, 1721-1722).","pitfalls":["Anchoring on the febrile phase and missing the critical phase, which paradoxically begins as fever declines rather than at its peak (p.1721).","Serologic cross-reactivity with antigenically similar orthoflaviviruses (yellow fever, Zika, Japanese encephalitis virus) - including after vaccination - can confound serologic diagnosis (p.1721).","Relying on antigen/nucleic-acid tests outside their acute-phase window: after about day 5 diagnosis must shift to serology (p.1721).","Failing to distinguish secondary from primary infection: early, rapidly rising IgG points to secondary infection and higher severe-disease risk (p.1721)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison endorses the WHO pragmatic categories that replaced the older 'dengue fever / dengue hemorrhagic fever / dengue shock syndrome' terminology: dengue without warning signs, dengue with warning signs (which directs inpatient management), and severe dengue. Severe dengue is defined as confirmed or presumptive dengue plus at least one of: (1) plasma leakage severe enough to cause shock or respiratory distress, (2) severe bleeding, or (3) severe organ dysfunction. Every patient with presumptive dengue of any severity should be assessed up front for warning signs and for the criteria of severe dengue, with the provisional diagnosis of severe dengue made clinically (p.1704, 1721).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1711","Harrison 22e p.1712","Harrison 22e p.1713","Harrison 22e p.1714","Harrison 22e p.1718","Harrison 22e p.1721","Harrison 22e p.1722"],"crossLinks":["CHIKUNGUNYA","LEPTOSPIROSIS","MALARIA"]},"DEVICE_INFECTION":{"name":"Device-Related Infection (CIED / Vascular Catheter)","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1078, 1082-1085, 1200-1201","clinicalPearls":["CIED-related endocarditis (CIED-IE) involves either the device hardware itself or the endothelium at points of device contact, and occasionally coexists with concurrent valvular infection (Harrison 22e p.1078).","The timing of CIED-IE is split roughly into thirds: about one-third present within 3 months of implantation or manipulation, one-third between 3 and 12 months, and one-third more than a year afterward (Harrison 22e p.1078).","CIED infection can be obvious from a generator pocket infection (especially within 6 months of device manipulation) or cryptic, arising through bacteremic seeding without any visible pocket involvement (Harrison 22e p.1082).","Risk of device-associated endocarditis is reported as higher on implanted defibrillators and resynchronization devices than on permanent pacemakers (Harrison 22e p.1078).","In a patient with a CIED, an unexplained Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia (and persistent coagulase-negative staphylococcal bacteremia) without another source is likely to indicate CIED-IE or valvular IE and should be investigated and managed as such (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Relapse of bloodstream infection after a course of antimicrobial therapy in a CIED patient increases the likelihood that the device is the source and warrants treatment as CIED-IE (Harrison 22e p.1084).","S. aureus device-related infections tend to be acute with both local and systemic manifestations and to progress more rapidly than the indolent course seen in non-S. aureus staphylococcal infections; a pyogenic collection at the device site is relatively common (Harrison 22e p.1200-1201)."],"pathophysiology":"Cardiac implantable electronic devices (pacemakers, implantable defibrillators, cardiac resynchronization devices) and other prosthetic intravascular hardware provide an avascular foreign surface that organisms colonize and on which they persist. Infection localizes to the generator pocket and/or the intravascular leads, and may extend to the endothelium at points of lead contact, producing endocarditis (CIED-IE). Staphylococci dominate because skin flora is the principal portal; S. aureus and coagulase-negative staphylococci cause the majority of CIED-IE cases. Once organisms establish on the foreign material, antimicrobial therapy alone usually fails to eradicate them, because the retained device continues to act as a nidus for persistent or recurrent infection (Harrison 22e p.1078, p.1201).","additionalDifferentials":["Left-sided native-valve or prosthetic-valve endocarditis (may coexist with or be mistaken for CIED-IE) (Harrison 22e p.1078, p.1084)","Bloodstream infection from an extracardiac focus (e.g., gram-negative, streptococcal, or enterococcal bacteremia) not arising from the device (Harrison 22e p.1084)","Remote-site metastatic infection complicating bacteremic CIED infection, such as vertebral osteomyelitis (Harrison 22e p.1084)"],"infectionMimics":["Non-device bloodstream infection from a separate source presenting with fever and sepsis in a CIED patient (Harrison 22e p.1084)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transesophageal echocardiography (TEE): detects vegetations in >90% of patients with definite IE; an initial study may be falsely negative in a minority, so a negative TEE when IE is likely should be repeated in 7-10 days rather than used to exclude the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1083).","Three or more separate blood culture sets (two-bottle, ~10 mL per bottle) from different venipuncture sites; if negative at 48-72 h, obtain additional sets and consult the lab for optimal techniques (Harrison 22e p.1082-1083).","FDG-PET/CT: less sensitive than TEE for intracardiac pathology in CIED-IE but useful for assessing CIED pocket and lead infection and extracardiac/remote complications (Harrison 22e p.1083).","Aspiration of any pyogenic collection at the device/pocket site, combined with blood cultures, to establish the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1201).","Echocardiographic assessment is specifically indicated in monomicrobial S. aureus bacteremia when an intracardiac device is present (Harrison 22e p.1084)."],"redFlags":["S. aureus bacteremia in a patient with a CIED with no alternative source (Harrison 22e p.1084)","Persistent or relapsing bacteremia after antimicrobial therapy in a device patient (Harrison 22e p.1084)","Generator pocket inflammation/pyogenic collection at the device site (Harrison 22e p.1082, p.1201)","Lead vegetation >2 cm, which carries a risk of pulmonary embolism (Harrison 22e p.1085)"],"prognosis":"Removal of the infected CIED during the initial hospitalization is associated with increased 30-day and 1-year survival compared with antibiotic therapy and device retention. Attempted salvage of an infected device with antibiotics alone plus long-term suppressive therapy is usually unsuccessful and is reserved only for patients whose devices cannot be removed or who decline removal; left in place, the device remains a nidus for persistent or recurrent infection (Harrison 22e p.1084, p.1085, p.1201).","pitfalls":["Treating an infected CIED with antibiotics alone (salvage/suppression) usually fails and should not be the default approach (Harrison 22e p.1084).","Using a negative TEE to rule out CIED-IE when clinical suspicion is high, rather than repeating the study in 7-10 days (Harrison 22e p.1083).","In critically ill patients already on antibiotics for other indications, blood cultures may be negative and device-related endocarditis missed (Harrison 22e p.1200-1201).","Assuming every bloodstream infection in a CIED patient indicates the device: gram-negative, streptococcal, or enterococcal bacteremia from an extracardiac focus may not be device-related, though relapse afterward raises suspicion for CIED-IE (Harrison 22e p.1084)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison distinguishes generator pocket infection without bacteremia (more localized) from bacteremic CIED infection and CIED-IE (more severe, with risk of coincident left-sided valve infection or remote metastatic foci); complete hardware removal is required across the spectrum of established CIED-IE and pocket or intracardiac lead infection (Harrison 22e p.1084, p.1085).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1078","Harrison 22e p.1082","Harrison 22e p.1083","Harrison 22e p.1084","Harrison 22e p.1085","Harrison 22e p.1200","Harrison 22e p.1201"],"crossLinks":["IE","SEPSIS"]},"DIABETIC_FOOT":{"name":"Diabetic Foot Infection","system":"Skin & Soft Tissue","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.91-92, 1111-1112, 3271-3272","clinicalPearls":["A wound that probes to bone (rock-hard, gritty base without intervening soft tissue) strongly predicts underlying osteomyelitis; the probe-to-bone test has a positive predictive value of ~90% in high-pretest-probability populations (p.1112).","In a hospitalized diabetic patient with a chronic deep foot ulcer, the pretest probability of osteomyelitis is high (~65%), so a positive probe-to-bone test can often establish the diagnosis clinically without imaging (p.91, 1112).","A negative probe-to-bone test lowers the likelihood of osteomyelitis (likelihood ratio ~0.2) (p.92).","An infected ulcer is a clinical diagnosis; superficial swab cultures recover multiple organisms of uncertain significance and correlate poorly with bone biopsy (only ~24% concordance in one study) (p.1111-1112, 3271-3272).","Culture-pathogen concordance between deep samples and bone is best for S. aureus (40-50%) and poorest for coagulase-negative staphylococci, anaerobes, and gram-negative bacilli (p.1112).","About 34% of people with diabetes develop a foot ulcer in their lifetime; among those with an ulcer, ~5% per year progress to foot osteomyelitis (p.1111).","Gas gangrene-like findings may occur in a diabetic foot wound even in the absence of clostridial infection (p.3272)."],"pathophysiology":"Diabetic foot infection arises from the interaction of peripheral sensory, motor, and autonomic neuropathy with abnormal foot biomechanics, peripheral arterial disease, and impaired wound healing. Sensory neuropathy removes protective pain sensation, allowing unrecognized repetitive or major trauma; impaired proprioception causes abnormal weight-bearing and callus/ulcer formation. Motor and sensory neuropathy produce structural deformities (hammer/claw toe, prominent metatarsal heads, Charcot joint). Autonomic neuropathy causes anhidrosis with dry, fissured skin. Peripheral arterial disease and poor healing then allow minor skin breaks to enlarge and become infected. Foot osteomyelitis is almost always acquired by the exogenous (contiguous) route, progressing from skin/soft-tissue infection into bone, and can also follow deep wound infection after foot surgery (p.1111, 3271).","additionalDifferentials":["Charcot neuroarthropathy (neuropathic joint disease) — can mimic or coexist with foot osteomyelitis (p.1111, 3272)","Purely neuropathic ulcer without infection (p.3272)","Cellulitis without underlying ulceration (p.3272)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Charcot (neuropathic) arthropathy with bony deformity, which can radiographically and clinically resemble osteomyelitis (p.1111, 3272)","Callus or pressure changes from abnormal foot biomechanics (p.3271)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Probe-to-bone test with a blunt sterile instrument as a bedside clinical assessment (p.92, 1112, 3272)","Bone biopsy with culture (preferred basis for diagnosing/treating osteomyelitis given poor swab-to-bone correlation) (p.1112)","Cultures taken from the debrided ulcer base, purulent drainage, or wound aspiration rather than superficial swabs (p.3272)","MRI when pretest probability is lower or imaging needed (sensitivity 80-100%, specificity 80-90%) (p.1112)","Nuclear medicine studies (PET, CT/SPECT) or labeled white-cell scans as alternatives to MRI (p.3272)","Ankle-brachial index screening for peripheral arterial disease in diabetics >50 years with risk factors (p.3272)"],"redFlags":["Wound that probes to bone (high likelihood of osteomyelitis) (p.1112, 3272)","Chronic ulcer failing to respond to therapy (prompt plain radiographs to assess for osteomyelitis) (p.3272)","Underlying peripheral arterial insufficiency / ischemic limb (contributes to non-healing and amputation risk) (p.3272)","Charcot foot deformity complicated by chronic osteomyelitis (p.1111)"],"prognosis":"Diabetes is the leading cause of nontraumatic lower-extremity amputation. Diabetic foot osteomyelitis increases amputation risk; a foot ulcer (or subsequent ulceration) carries a 14-24% risk of eventual amputation. Adequate management of early-stage infection lowers the amputation rate, and wound debridement combined with antibiotics makes amputation unnecessary in roughly two-thirds of patients (p.1111-1112, 3271).","pitfalls":["Do NOT rely on superficial swab cultures to guide therapy — they grow contaminants/colonizers and correlate poorly with bone culture (p.1112, 3272).","Plain x-rays have limited sensitivity (30-90%) for diabetic foot osteomyelitis and are mainly useful for follow-up of confirmed cases, not for early diagnosis (p.1112).","A negative probe-to-bone test does not fully exclude osteomyelitis, especially when pretest probability is low — pursue MRI (p.1112).","Antiseptic agents should be avoided and topical antibiotics are of limited value in wound care (p.3272)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison references the IWGDF/IDSA 2023 guidelines for diagnosis and treatment of diabetes-related foot infections but does not reproduce the detailed IDSA/IWGDF grading scale in this text. Infections are described broadly as mild/non-limb-threatening versus more severe (p.1112, 3272).","references":["Harrison 22e p.91","Harrison 22e p.92","Harrison 22e p.1111","Harrison 22e p.1112","Harrison 22e p.3271","Harrison 22e p.3272"],"crossLinks":["CELLULITIS","NECROTIZING_FASCIITIS"]},"DISSEMINATED_TB":{"name":"Disseminated (Miliary) Tuberculosis","system":"Systemic / Tuberculosis","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1386, 1389-1390, 1393","clinicalPearls":["Presentation is notoriously nonspecific and protean, varying with the organs predominantly seeded; constitutional features (fever, night sweats, anorexia, weakness, weight loss) dominate in most patients (Harrison 22e p.1390).","Choroidal tubercles on fundoscopic eye examination are pathognomonic of miliary TB and are present in up to roughly 30% of cases, so dilated fundoscopy is a high-yield bedside step (Harrison 22e p.1390).","A high index of suspicion is essential; the diagnosis is easily missed and untreated miliary TB is lethal, whereas early treatment is curative (Harrison 22e p.1390).","Sputum-smear microscopy is negative in most cases, so a negative smear does not exclude the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1390).","The tuberculin skin test may be negative in up to half of patients, with reactivity sometimes returning during chemotherapy; a negative TST therefore cannot rule out disease (Harrison 22e p.1390).","In adults miliary TB may arise from either recent infection or reactivation of old disseminated foci, whereas in children it more often follows primary infection (Harrison 22e p.1390).","Granulomas are often found on liver or bone-marrow biopsy, and bronchoalveolar lavage with transbronchial biopsy improves bacteriologic confirmation (Harrison 22e p.1390)."],"pathophysiology":"Miliary/disseminated TB results from hematogenous spread of M. tuberculosis when an acquired immune response fails to contain infection. Bacilli carried to regional lymph nodes reach the central venous return and the systemic circulation, seeding multiple organs with innumerable small (1-2 mm) yellowish granulomas resembling millet seeds. Occult hematogenous dissemination commonly follows primary infection; in the very young and the immunocompromised (including people living with HIV), insufficient immunity allows progression to fatal miliary disease or tuberculous meningitis (Harrison 22e p.1386, 1390).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Disseminated fungal infection (a differential when miliary or interstitial pulmonary infiltrates and multi-organ involvement are present) (Harrison 22e p.1390)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundoscopic eye examination for choroidal tubercles (pathognomonic, up to ~30% of cases) (Harrison 22e p.1390)","Bronchoalveolar lavage and transbronchial biopsy for improved bacteriologic confirmation (Harrison 22e p.1390)","Full blood count for hematologic clues (anemia with leukopenia, lymphopenia, neutrophilic leukocytosis/leukemoid reactions, polycythemia; pancytopenia in nonreactive form; DIC reported) (Harrison 22e p.1390)","Liver function tests including alkaline phosphatase (elevated with hepatic involvement) (Harrison 22e p.1390)","Tuberculin skin test (may be falsely negative in up to half of cases) (Harrison 22e p.1390)","Xpert MTB/RIF as the initial test when TB meningitis is suspected; positive results prompt immediate treatment (Harrison 22e p.1393)"],"redFlags":["Meningismus, present in fewer than 10% of cases, signals possible concurrent tuberculous meningitis and warrants urgent CNS evaluation (Harrison 22e p.1390).","Pancytopenia with an acute septicemic course suggests nonreactive miliary TB, a rare and rapidly fatal form from massive dissemination (Harrison 22e p.1390).","Disseminated intravascular coagulation has been reported and indicates severe disease (Harrison 22e p.1390)."],"prognosis":"Unrecognized miliary TB is lethal, but with proper early treatment it is amenable to cure. Cryptic miliary TB (seen in the elderly) runs a chronic course with mild intermittent fever and anemia, ultimately progressing to meningeal involvement before death. The nonreactive (acute septicemic) form is rapidly fatal with multiple necrotic, nongranulomatous lesions at postmortem. Glucocorticoid therapy has not proved beneficial in miliary TB (Harrison 22e p.1390).","pitfalls":["Relying on sputum-smear microscopy or the tuberculin skin test to exclude the diagnosis, since both are frequently negative in miliary TB (Harrison 22e p.1390).","Expecting a typical chest radiograph: early in the course, and especially among people living with HIV, the CXR may show no abnormality; findings may instead be large or interstitial infiltrates or pleural effusion rather than the classic miliary reticulonodular pattern (Harrison 22e p.1390).","Overlooking cryptic miliary TB in elderly patients with only mild intermittent fever and anemia (Harrison 22e p.1390)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1386","Harrison 22e p.1390","Harrison 22e p.1393"],"crossLinks":["PUO","CNS_TB","PULMONARY_TB","malignancy_b"]},"DYSENTERY":{"name":"Dysentery (Invasive Bacterial Diarrhea)","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e: Ch 49 (Diarrhea and Constipation) pp.348-350; Ch 138 (Acute Infectious Diarrheal Diseases and Bacterial Food Poisoning) pp.1119-1124; Ch 166 (Diseases Caused by Gram-Negative Enteric Bacilli, E. coli pathotypes) pp.1331-1332; Ch 167/168 (Campylobacter) pp.1306,1348-1349; Ch 172 (Shigellosis) pp.1366-1368; Ch 230 (Amebiasis) pp.1753-1757","clinicalPearls":["Dysentery is bloody diarrhoea arising from mucosal invasion or cytotoxin injury to the colon, not from secretory/enterotoxin mechanisms; the triad of high fever, abdominal pain and grossly bloody stool points to invasive disease rather than toxin-mediated watery diarrhoea (p.306, p.1077).","The single most useful initial distinction is inflammatory vs noninflammatory diarrhoea: grossly bloody or mucoid stool, plus fecal leukocytes or a positive fecal lactoferrin/calprotectin, defines the inflammatory (dysenteric) category and a colonic/distal small-bowel locus (p.1079, p.1077).","Tenesmus (painful rectal spasm with a strong urge but little stool passage) signals proctitis and is characteristic of the distal-colonic involvement of shigellosis and amoebiasis (p.1078).","Bloody diarrhoea with a low-grade fever or no fever is a clinical clue to Shiga toxin-producing/enterohaemorrhagic E. coli (EHEC, e.g. O157:H7), which can be mistaken for noninfectious disease such as ischaemic or inflammatory bowel disease (p.1081, p.1288).","Unlike most watery diarrhoeal syndromes, dysenteric illness rarely presents with major dehydration; in shigellosis the stools are small-volume and dehydration is uncommon (p.1366).","Shigellosis classically evolves through four phases: incubation (usually 1-4 days), initial watery diarrhoea, then dysentery with frequent small bloody mucopurulent stools, tenesmus and cramps, followed by a postinfectious phase (p.1366).","On stool microscopy, abundant PMNs in every field favour bacterial enteroinvasive disease (especially Shigella), whereas erythrophagocytic trophozoites with few PMNs favour Entamoeba histolytica (p.1366).","Fewer than ~40% of patients with amoebic dysentery are febrile, in contrast to bacterial dysentery; nearly all have heme-positive stools, and amoebic colitis typically has a more gradual onset over 2-6 weeks (p.1754).","EHEC/STEC stools are commonly grossly bloody with abdominal pain and fecal leukocytes in ~70% of cases, yet fever is characteristically absent (p.1288).","An appendicitis-like presentation (right-sided pain, tenderness) should raise Yersinia enterocolitica, which invades the terminal ileum and proximal colon; Campylobacter and amoebic caecal involvement can similarly mimic appendicitis (p.306, p.1078)."],"pathophysiology":"Dysentery results from injury to the colonic (and distal small-bowel) mucosa by two overlapping mechanisms. (1) Bacterial invasion: Shigella and enteroinvasive E. coli invade mucosal epithelial cells, multiply intracellularly and spread cell-to-cell, producing acute colitis mainly of the distal colon and rectum with edematous, hemorrhagic, ulcerated mucosa. (2) Cytotoxin production: organisms such as Shigella dysenteriae type 1, Shiga toxin-producing/enterohaemorrhagic E. coli and Vibrio parahaemolyticus elaborate cytotoxins that destroy mucosal cells; Shiga toxins inhibit the 60S ribosomal subunit, shut off protein synthesis, and after systemic translocation injure renal and cerebral microvascular endothelium. The resulting mucosal destruction yields small-volume bloody, mucopurulent stools containing inflammatory cells. Salmonella invades the bowel mucosa and causes inflammatory diarrhoea but generally does not destroy enterocytes or produce the full dysentery syndrome. Entamoeba histolytica trophozoites adhere via the Gal/GalNAc lectin, degrade mucus and extracellular matrix with proteases, and kill epithelial and inflammatory cells by contact-dependent (apoptosis, trogocytosis, pore-forming peptides) and contact-independent cytotoxicity, producing characteristic flask-shaped colonic ulcers and bloody diarrhoea, and can invade the portal venous system to seed the liver (p.306, p.1076-1077, p.1366, p.1754-1755).","additionalDifferentials":["Shigella species (bacillary dysentery; prototypic invasive cause)","Shiga toxin-producing / enterohaemorrhagic E. coli (STEC/EHEC, e.g. O157:H7)","Enteroinvasive E. coli (EIEC; shigellosis-like dysentery, shares ancestry with Shigella)","Campylobacter jejuni (inflammatory diarrhoea, ~10% grossly bloody in adults)","Non-typhoidal Salmonella (inflammatory diarrhoea; full dysentery uncommon)","Yersinia enterocolitica (terminal ileitis/right-sided pain mimicking appendicitis)","Entamoeba histolytica (amoebic dysentery; subacute, travel/endemic exposure)","Vibrio parahaemolyticus (can cause dysentery; shellfish exposure)","Clostridioides difficile (cytotoxin-mediated colitis; antibiotic/healthcare exposure)","Klebsiella oxytoca (antibiotic-associated haemorrhagic colitis)"],"infectionMimics":["Clostridioides difficile colitis (consider with recent or current antibiotic use, hospitalisation/long-term care; can also be community-acquired without prior antibiotics)","Enteric fever (Salmonella Typhi, Yersinia enterocolitica) - penetrating organisms that can present with fever and abdominal pain; fecal mononuclear leukocytes rather than frank dysentery","Schistosomiasis and other invasive parasitic colitides in endemic exposure","Sexually transmitted proctocolitis (gonococcal, syphilitic, chlamydial/LGV) presenting with bloody mucoid rectal discharge and tenesmus"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inflammatory bowel disease - a first flare of ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease can be clinically indistinguishable from invasive bacterial dysentery, particularly in industrialised settings; travel history and stool studies help discriminate, and IBD should not be diagnosed until invasive infection (e.g. Campylobacter, amoebiasis) is excluded (p.1366, p.1306)","Ischaemic colitis - typically age >50, acute lower abdominal pain preceding watery then bloody diarrhoea, with inflammation of the sigmoid/left colon and rectal sparing (p.306)","Colonic diverticulitis (p.307)","Graft-versus-host disease of the gut (p.307)","Intussusception (can mimic afebrile bloody diarrhoea, especially in children) (p.1288)","Drug-induced colitis (e.g. NSAIDs and other medication-related diarrhoea) (p.306)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fecal leukocyte smear (methylene blue wet mount) - suggests inflammatory disease, though predictive value is debated (p.1078-1079)","Fecal lactoferrin (latex agglutination or ELISA) - more sensitive surrogate marker of fecal leukocytes than microscopy; fecal calprotectin similarly used (p.306, p.1079)","Specific/selective stool culture with isolate retention for Salmonella, Shigella and Campylobacter (MacConkey/Salmonella-Shigella/selenite media; Campylobacter on selective medium at 42C microaerophilic); sampling the bloody/mucopurulent portion or a rectal swab improves Shigella yield (p.1079-1080, p.1367)","Dedicated Shiga toxin / Shiga toxin-gene testing AND culture in ALL community-acquired diarrhoea (inflammatory or not), because bloody stool and fecal leukocytes are not reliably present with STEC/EHEC; O157 screened as sorbitol non-fermenters on sorbitol-MacConkey (p.1080, p.1288-1289)","Culture-independent multiplex nucleic acid amplification panels (e.g. detecting STEC, ETEC, EPEC, EAEC, EIEC) - faster and more sensitive than culture, but a positive result should be followed by attempted culture isolation for susceptibility testing and outbreak surveillance (p.1080-1081, p.1289)","Stool examination for ova and parasites with E. histolytica-specific rapid antigen detection (microscopy is less sensitive/specific); definitive amoebic diagnosis requires hematophagous trophozoites, as cysts cannot be distinguished from E. dispar/E. moshkovskii/E. bangladeshi; examine at least three fresh specimens (p.1080, p.1756)","Amoebic serology (ELISA) - positive in >90% of amoebic colitis/ameboma/liver abscess and useful before starting glucocorticoids when IBD is considered (p.1756)","CBC and peripheral smear (microangiopathic hemolytic anaemia, schizocytes, thrombocytopenia signalling HUS), renal function/creatinine, and LDH when STEC/EHEC or S. dysenteriae 1 is suspected (p.1288, p.1366)","Sigmoidoscopy/colonoscopy with biopsy - reserved for uncharacterised persistent diarrhoea or suspected IBD; endoscopic biopsy may not distinguish Campylobacter colitis from IBD, and is hazardous during fulminant amoebic colitis (perforation risk) (p.307, p.1306, p.1756)"],"redFlags":["Grossly bloody diarrhoea with low-grade or absent fever - suspect STEC/EHEC and the risk of haemolytic-uraemic syndrome (p.1081, p.1288)","Falling urine output, pallor, irritability, edema, or new neurological signs after several days of diarrhoea - developing HUS (microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia, acute renal failure) (p.1288, p.1366)","Abdominal distension and tenderness with loss of haustration / dilated transverse colon - toxic megacolon, a complication of severe Shigella (and severe amoebic) colitis (p.1366)","Signs of perforation or peritonitis (rebound, free air) (p.1366)","Altered consciousness, seizures or coma - seen especially in children under 5 with severe shigellosis and carries a poor prognosis (p.1366)","Severe dehydration (lethargy, feeble pulse, hypotension, shock) (p.1078)","High fever (>=38.5C), profuse diarrhoea, severe abdominal pain in patients over 50, or illness in the elderly (>=70) or immunocompromised - warrant prompt stool microbiological evaluation (p.306)","Rectal prolapse in a child with dysentery (a recognised complication of severe shigellosis) (p.1367)"],"prognosis":"Most invasive bacterial dysentery is self-limited: shigellosis usually resolves within about a week (faster with appropriate therapy), and STEC/EHEC illness typically lasts 5-10 days. Severe outcomes and death cluster in children under 5 (especially the malnourished) and the elderly. Adverse prognostic features in clinically severe shigellosis include nonbloody diarrhoea, moderate-to-severe dehydration, bacteraemia, absence of fever, abdominal tenderness, rectal prolapse, and altered consciousness. Haemolytic-uraemic syndrome occurs in roughly 15% of infected children under 10 with STEC/EHEC; with dialysis support its mortality is under 10%, but survivors often have persisting renal and neurological dysfunction; acute renal failure complicates 55-70% of HUS cases yet renal function recovers in most. Postinfectious sequelae include reactive arthritis (after Shigella flexneri, Salmonella, Campylobacter, Yersinia) and, after Campylobacter, Guillain-Barre syndrome; postinfectious irritable bowel syndrome may follow, particularly after Campylobacter (p.306, p.1288, p.1306, p.1366).","pitfalls":["Do NOT give antibiotics for suspected enterohaemorrhagic/Shiga toxin-producing E. coli (grossly bloody diarrhoea, often afebrile) - antibiotics induce Shiga toxin-producing phage replication, can increase toxin production roughly 20-fold, and raise the risk of HUS; this caution applies especially to children (p.1081, p.1289).","Avoid antimotility/antiperistaltic agents (e.g. loperamide, diphenoxylate-atropine) in febrile or bloody dysentery - they may prolong fever and shedding in Shigella, increase the risk of toxic megacolon, and have been implicated in HUS in EHEC-infected children (p.349, p.1077, p.1367).","Do not diagnose inflammatory bowel disease before excluding invasive infection - Campylobacter and amoebic colitis can closely mimic and even be histologically confused with ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease (p.1306, p.1755).","Exclude amoebiasis (stool studies plus serology) before giving glucocorticoids for presumed IBD - unsuspected amoebic colitis can become fulminant or develop toxic megacolon on steroids (p.1755-1756, p.1757).","A negative fecal leukocyte/lactoferrin test or absence of fever does NOT rule out invasive disease - STEC/EHEC frequently lacks reliable fecal leukocytes and fever, which is why both Shiga toxin testing and culture are recommended in all community-acquired diarrhoea (p.1080, p.1289).","Shigella is fragile and is easily lost in transport (temperature/pH changes); inoculate promptly or use a holding medium (Cary-Blair) and sample the bloody/mucopurulent stool portion or use a rectal swab to maximise yield (p.1367).","Microscopy cannot distinguish E. histolytica cysts from non-pathogenic E. dispar (and E. moshkovskii/E. bangladeshi); a microscopic species diagnosis of E. histolytica requires demonstrating trophozoites that have ingested erythrocytes (use antigen/molecular testing) (p.1080, p.1756).","Empirical antimotility therapy should be avoided more broadly with febrile dysentery; bismuth subsalicylate should be avoided in immunocompromised or renally impaired patients (bismuth encephalopathy risk) (p.349)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames severity primarily by the inflammatory vs noninflammatory distinction and by host/feature risk rather than a single formal dysentery score. Inflammatory (dysenteric) disease is defined by grossly bloody/mucoid stool with fecal leukocytes/lactoferrin and a colonic locus. Indications that mark an episode as severe and warranting evaluation/empirical therapy include profuse diarrhoea with dehydration, overtly bloody stools, fever >=38.5C (>=101F), duration >48 h without improvement, severe abdominal pain in patients over 50, and elderly (>=70) or immunocompromised hosts. Dehydration is graded clinically as mild (thirst, dry mouth, decreased urine output), moderate (orthostatic hypotension, skin tenting, sunken eyes/fontanelle), and severe (lethargy, obtundation, feeble pulse, hypotension, shock). For shigellosis, severe disease is signalled by features such as bacteraemia, altered consciousness/seizures, toxic megacolon, perforation, severe dehydration, and occurrence in malnourished young children or the elderly (p.306, p.1077-1078, p.1366).","references":["Harrison 22e p.306","Harrison 22e p.307","Harrison 22e p.349","Harrison 22e p.350","Harrison 22e p.1076","Harrison 22e p.1077","Harrison 22e p.1078","Harrison 22e p.1079","Harrison 22e p.1080","Harrison 22e p.1081","Harrison 22e p.1288","Harrison 22e p.1289","Harrison 22e p.1306","Harrison 22e p.1366","Harrison 22e p.1367","Harrison 22e p.1754","Harrison 22e p.1755","Harrison 22e p.1756","Harrison 22e p.1757"],"crossLinks":["GASTROENTERITIS","C_DIFF","ENTERIC_FEVER","AMOEBIC_LIVER_ABSCESS","ibd_flare"]},"ENCEPHALITIS":{"name":"Encephalitis","system":"Central Nervous System","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1111-1117 (Ch.142 Encephalitis); arboviral/Japanese encephalitis p.1670-1671; autoimmune/anti-NMDAR encephalitis p.746-747","clinicalPearls":["Encephalitis differs from meningitis by the presence of altered brain function itself - altered consciousness (confusion, behavioural change, lethargy to coma) and focal or diffuse neurologic signs - whereas uncomplicated meningitis spares brain parenchymal function (p.1111).","Seizures (focal or generalized) occur in many patients with encephalitis, and prophylactic anticonvulsant therapy may be considered given the high seizure frequency in severe cases (p.1111, p.1115).","Clinical features alone cannot reliably establish the specific viral cause; presentations overlap too much, so etiology rests on CSF PCR, serology, imaging and epidemiologic clues (p.1111).","Features pointing to inferomedial frontotemporal involvement - prominent olfactory or gustatory hallucinations, anosmia, bizarre behaviour or personality change, and memory disturbance - should specifically raise HSV encephalitis (p.1114).","HSV is the single most common cause of sporadic encephalitis and the chief treatable cause, so the central diagnostic task once non-viral causes are excluded is to distinguish HSV from other viruses (p.1112, p.1114).","A CSF pleocytosis (>5 cells/uL) occurs in >95% of immunocompetent patients with viral encephalitis; severely immunocompromised patients (HIV, glucocorticoids/immunosuppressants, chemotherapy, lymphoreticular malignancy) may fail to mount this response (p.1112).","CSF cell counts exceed 500/uL in only about 10% of cases; counts >1000/uL should prompt consideration of non-viral infection or other inflammatory processes (p.1112).","A low CSF glucose is distinctly unusual in viral encephalitis and should suggest bacterial, fungal, tuberculous, parasitic, leptospiral, syphilitic, sarcoid or neoplastic disease instead (p.1112).","Prominent brainstem involvement suggests flaviviruses (West Nile, St. Louis, Japanese encephalitis), HSV, enterovirus A71, rabies or Listeria; deep grey matter (basal ganglia/thalamus) involvement with movement disorders suggests flavivirus infection (p.1114).","Persisting CSF neutrophilia should prompt consideration of bacterial infection, leptospirosis, amebic infection, and non-infectious acute hemorrhagic leukoencephalitis (p.1112).","Empiric aciclovir for suspected HSV should be started immediately on clinical suspicion (especially with focal features) while awaiting diagnostic studies, and is more effective the earlier it is given (p.1115; principle only - no dose)."],"pathophysiology":"Encephalitis is inflammation of the brain parenchyma, usually viral (or from a primary autoimmune process). Neurotropic viruses tend to injure characteristic CNS regions, producing the clinical pattern. Associated meningeal inflammation (meningoencephalitis) is common, and spinal cord or nerve-root involvement may co-occur (encephalomyelitis, encephalomyeloradiculitis). HSV has a predilection for the inferomedial frontotemporal/limbic regions, producing the characteristic temporal-lobe imaging and EEG changes and a hemorrhagic/necrotizing process; punctate microhemorrhages explain CSF red cells in some cases. Flaviviruses such as West Nile and Japanese encephalitis virus preferentially involve deep grey structures (thalamus, basal ganglia, brainstem). VZV tends to produce a CNS vasculopathy with hemorrhagic and ischemic infarction rather than a true parenchymal encephalitis. Some viral encephalitides (notably HSV, and Japanese encephalitis) can trigger a subsequent antibody-mediated autoimmune encephalitis, e.g. anti-NMDA-receptor antibodies after HSV (p.1111-1114, p.746).","additionalDifferentials":["Rabies (encephalitic/furious form with hydrophobia and aerophobia, or paralytic form) - bat exposure is the key risk in the US and a bite history may be lacking","Cytomegalovirus, EBV, HHV-6 encephalitis (notably in immunocompromised / allogeneic stem-cell transplant recipients)","Enterovirus A71 / D68 with acute flaccid myelitis-like presentation","Nipah virus encephalitis (Paramyxoviridae; Southeast Asian outbreaks)","SARS-CoV-2 - true viral CNS encephalitis (rare) versus encephalopathy from systemic/hyperinflammatory illness"],"infectionMimics":["Mycobacteria (tuberculous meningitis/meningoencephalitis)","Fungi","Rickettsiae","Listeria monocytogenes (including rhombencephalitis)","Mycoplasma","Bartonella and other bacteria","Neurosyphilis","Naegleria fowleri primary amebic meningoencephalitis (neutrophilic CSF, low glucose, motile trophozoites in warm fresh CSF; warm freshwater exposure)","Acanthamoeba / Balamuthia mandrillaris granulomatous amebic encephalitis (can mimic acute viral encephalitis; near-100% mortality)","Baylisascaris procyonis (raccoon roundworm) encephalitis - usually children, dirt/raccoon-feces exposure, eosinophilia","Leptospirosis","Lyme disease and cat-scratch disease"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Autoimmune anti-NMDA-receptor encephalitis - young women/children, viral-like prodrome then psychiatric symptoms, insomnia, reduced verbal output, seizures, dyskinesias, autonomic instability and hypoventilation; MRI often normal; can follow HSV encephalitis (NMDAR antibodies in up to 25% of HSV recoveries) and may be initially mistaken for primary psychosis (p.746-747, p.1114)","Other antibody-mediated autoimmune encephalitides - LGI1/CASPR2 (anti-VGKC complex), AMPA receptor, GABA receptor, GAD65 (p.1114)","Paraneoplastic encephalitis with onconeural antibodies (anti-Hu, Yo, Ma2, amphiphysin, CRMP5/CV2)","Hashimoto's encephalopathy (anti-thyroglobulin / anti-thyroperoxidase antibodies)","Prion disease","Acute hemorrhagic leukoencephalitis","Metabolic/toxic encephalopathy (e.g. hyperammonemia, hepatic failure, intoxication)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Diffusion-weighted and FLAIR MRI sequences in addition to standard MRI - increase sensitivity for HSV temporal-lobe lesions (detect ~90% when added)","Repeat CSF HSV PCR if the initial test is obtained within the first ~72 h and clinical suspicion of HSV remains high (early PCR can be falsely negative)","CSF virus-specific IgM / intrathecal antibody synthesis (IgG index) - especially West Nile CSF IgM (diagnostic), and VZV CSF IgM as complementary to PCR; HSV CSF antibody testing useful when illness >1 week and PCR negative","Store frozen CSF (collect ~20 mL, freeze 5-10 mL) for later virus-specific PCR or metagenomic next-generation sequencing, as many neuroinvasive viruses are only transiently present in CSF","Metagenomic next-generation sequencing (mNGS) of CSF/brain for obscure or immunocompromised-host cases","Autoimmune/paraneoplastic antibody panel in serum and CSF (e.g. NMDAR, LGI1, CASPR2, AMPAR, GABA-R, GAD65; onconeural antibodies) when infectious workup is negative","Brain biopsy - reserved for patients with focal MRI abnormality, negative CSF PCR, no autoimmune serology, who deteriorate despite aciclovir and supportive care"],"redFlags":["Focal neurologic findings on examination, neuroimaging, or EEG in suspected encephalitis - strongly suggest HSV and mandate immediate empiric aciclovir while testing proceeds","Severely depressed or declining level of consciousness / coma (Glasgow Coma Scale ~6 at start of therapy predicts death or severe sequelae)","Seizures, particularly status or recurrent seizures","Signs of severely raised intracranial pressure (a relative contraindication to lumbar puncture)","Hypothalamic-pituitary dysfunction (temperature dysregulation, diabetes insipidus, SIADH)","Acute flaccid paralysis accompanying encephalitis (West Nile, EV-A71/D68)"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies widely by etiology and host factors. For HSV encephalitis treated with aciclovir, roughly 81% survive but only about half of survivors have no or minor sequelae; outcome is directly related to patient age and to the level of consciousness at the start of therapy - younger patients with good neurologic function do markedly better, while those comatose (GCS ~6) at treatment initiation die or survive with severe deficits. Eastern equine encephalitis carries very high sequelae rates (~80% of survivors severely impaired), whereas EBV, California (La Crosse) and Venezuelan equine encephalitis rarely cause severe sequelae. West Nile survivors frequently have prolonged cognitive impairment, weakness, tremor, myoclonus and parkinsonism, with recovery taking many months. Japanese encephalitis case-fatality in hospitalized patients is high (~20-30%) with common long-term neurologic disability; rabies is almost invariably fatal. (p.1115-1117, p.1671)","pitfalls":["Do not wait for CSF PCR confirmation before starting aciclovir in suspected HSV encephalitis - early treatment improves outcome, and PCR can be falsely negative in the first ~72 h (start empirically, repeat PCR if needed) (p.1112-1115).","A negative HSV CSF PCR reduces but does not exclude HSV encephalitis; interpret against pretest probability, timing, and prior antiviral use, and consider repeat testing or CSF antibody studies (p.1112-1113).","Worsening neurologic or psychiatric symptoms in the weeks after recovery from HSV encephalitis are often post-HSV anti-NMDA-receptor autoimmune encephalitis (needing immunotherapy), not aciclovir-resistant HSV - test for NMDAR antibodies (p.1114, p.1116).","Detecting NMDAR antibodies does not exclude HSV - they appear in up to 25% of HSV-encephalitis recoveries, so still test and treat for HSV when indicated (p.1114).","Negative CSF viral culture does not exclude HSV or EBV encephalitis (culture is insensitive; >95% of HSV cases have negative CSF culture) (p.1113).","A positive EBV CSF PCR may be non-specific, reflecting reactivation in lymphocytes entering the CNS during an unrelated process - corroborate before attributing encephalitis to EBV (p.1113).","Up to ~10% of PCR-proven HSV encephalitis cases have a normal MRI, and CT is normal in 20-35% - normal imaging does not exclude HSV (p.1113).","Discontinue aciclovir once HSV encephalitis is excluded (with possible exception of severe VZV or EBV encephalitis) (p.1115).","Serum HSV serology alone has no diagnostic value because of high background seroprevalence (p.1114)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1111-1117","Harrison 22e p.1670-1671","Harrison 22e p.746-747"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS","VIRAL_MENINGITIS","seizure_epilepsy"]},"ENTERIC_FEVER":{"name":"Enteric Fever (Typhoid)","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1358-1361 (Chapter 171, Salmonellosis / Enteric (Typhoid) Fever)","clinicalPearls":["Both 'enteric fever' and 'typhoid fever' refer to the same systemic illness caused by dissemination of S. Typhi or S. Paratyphi; the names are used interchangeably (p.1358).","The classic 'hallmarks' of fever plus abdominal pain are actually variable: fever is present at presentation in over three-quarters of cases, but abdominal pain is reported in only about 30-40% (p.1358).","Prolonged fever (roughly 38.8-40.5 C) is the most prominent symptom and can persist up to about 4 weeks if untreated (p.1359).","Mean incubation for S. Typhi is about 10-14 days (range 5-21), shortened by larger ingested inoculum (p.1359).","Relative bradycardia at the peak of high fever is described in under half of patients (p.1359).","Rose spots are faint, salmon-colored, blanching maculopapular lesions on the trunk/chest, appearing in about 30% near the end of the first week and fading in 2-5 days; they are hard to see in highly pigmented skin and Salmonella can be cultured from punch biopsies of them (p.1359-1360).","Headache (~80%) and coated/furred tongue are among the most common symptoms/signs; in a Nepal series S. Typhi and S. Paratyphi A infections were clinically indistinguishable even though Paratyphi A is generally thought milder and more GI-predominant (p.1359).","Diarrhea is somewhat more common than constipation in enteric fever, contrary to older teaching (p.1359).","Up to ~10% of treated patients have a mild relapse, typically 2-3 weeks after fever resolves, with the same strain and susceptibility profile (p.1360).","Chronic asymptomatic carriage (S. Typhi shed in stool or urine for over a year) develops in 2-5% and is more common in women, infants, and those with biliary disease; organisms persist by forming biofilms on gallstones (p.1360)."],"pathophysiology":"After ingestion (infectious dose ~200 to 10^6 CFU; lower gastric acidity or impaired intestinal integrity raises susceptibility), S. Typhi and S. Paratyphi traverse the small-bowel mucosa via M cells overlying Peyer's patches, using a type III secretion system to induce membrane ruffling and bacterium-mediated endocytosis. Crossing the epithelium, they are phagocytosed by macrophages but survive the intracellular environment by remodeling their surface (e.g., via the PhoP/PhoQ regulatory system) and by delivering effector proteins across the phagosome to remodel the Salmonella-containing vacuole for survival and replication. Carried within macrophages, the organisms disseminate via lymphatics and colonize reticuloendothelial tissue (liver, spleen, lymph nodes, bone marrow); the patient is largely asymptomatic during this incubation. Fever and abdominal pain emerge once a critical bacterial load triggers macrophage/epithelial cytokine release recognized by innate immune receptors; hepatosplenomegaly reflects mononuclear-cell recruitment and acquired cell-mediated immunity. Recruitment of mononuclear cells and lymphocytes to Peyer's patches over subsequent weeks causes marked enlargement and necrosis, the basis for later intestinal complications. Many S. Typhi and S. Paratyphi A strains also produce a toxin within host cells that is exported extracellularly and probably contributes to systemic and neuropsychiatric features. This contrasts with the mononuclear (rather than neutrophilic) bowel infiltrate that distinguishes enteric fever from nontyphoidal Salmonella gastroenteritis (p.1358-1359).","additionalDifferentials":["Malaria","Viral hepatitis","Bacterial enteritis","Dengue fever","Rickettsial infection","Leptospirosis","Amebic liver abscess","Acute HIV infection"],"infectionMimics":["Malaria","Dengue fever","Leptospirosis","Rickettsial infection","Viral hepatitis","Acute (primary) HIV infection","Amebic liver abscess","Bacterial enteritis / other Salmonella gastroenteritis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Buffy-coat (centrifuged blood) culture — speeds isolation because most S. Typhi reside in the mononuclear-cell/platelet fraction, though it does not raise overall sensitivity (p.1360).","Bone marrow culture — about 80% sensitive and, unlike blood culture, its yield is not reduced by up to 5 days of prior antibiotics (p.1360).","Culture of intestinal/duodenal secretions (noninvasive duodenal string test) — can be positive even when bone marrow culture is negative; combining blood, marrow and intestinal-secretion cultures gives a yield over 90% (p.1360).","Stool culture — often negative in the first week (negative in 60-70%) but may turn positive by the third week in untreated patients (p.1360).","Culture of rose-spot punch biopsies (p.1360).","Rapid immunodiagnostic serologic tests (e.g., Tubex, Typhidot detecting IgM/IgG to O and H antigens) — point-of-care, low cost, but only ~70-80% sensitive and ~80-90% specific, so insufficient to replace blood culture (p.1360).","Blood PCR for S. Typhi/S. Paratyphi — reported sensitivity ~40-100% depending on gene target (p.1360)."],"redFlags":["Gastrointestinal hemorrhage (~6%) — most often in the third and fourth weeks; life-threatening, needs fluid resuscitation and possible surgery (p.1360).","Intestinal (ileal) perforation (~1%) — from necrosis/ulceration of ileocecal Peyer's patches, typically weeks 3-4; requires urgent surgery and broadened cover for polymicrobial peritonitis (p.1360).","Neuropsychiatric deterioration ('muttering delirium' or 'coma vigil', picking at bedclothes/imaginary objects) (p.1360).","Neurologic complications — meningitis, Guillain-Barre syndrome, neuritis (p.1360).","Shock and obtundation, marking critically ill / severe typhoid (p.1361).","Disseminated intravascular coagulation, hemophagocytic syndrome, myocarditis/pericarditis/endocarditis, severe pneumonia, or hemolytic-uremic syndrome as uncommon severe complications (p.1360)."],"prognosis":"Overall case-fatality is about 2.5%, rising to ~4.5% in hospitalized patients and to 10-30% in untreated disease; prompt appropriate antibiotics prevent severe complications and cut mortality to under 1%. Complications occur in roughly 27% of hospitalized patients and correlate with longer symptom duration before admission, host factors, strain virulence/inoculum, and antibiotic choice. Chronic S. Typhi carriage carries an increased long-term risk of gallbladder cancer, especially in endemic regions like the Indian subcontinent (p.1360-1361).","pitfalls":["No single laboratory test other than a positive culture is diagnostic; the presentation is nonspecific, so a high index of suspicion is needed in any febrile returning traveler from an endemic region (notably the Indian subcontinent, Southeast Asia, or Africa) (p.1358, p.1360).","Blood culture sensitivity is only ~40-60% and falls further with low sample volume, prior antibiotics, or sampling in the first week, because very few organisms (<15/mL) circulate — a negative blood culture does not exclude enteric fever (p.1360).","Rapid serologic/immunodiagnostic tests are not accurate enough to replace blood culture for diagnosis (p.1360).","Leukopenia/neutropenia occur in only ~15-25%; counts can instead be normal or, especially in children or with perforation/secondary infection, show leukocytosis — so the WBC count is unreliable for ruling the diagnosis in or out (p.1360).","The textbook triad is fluid: abdominal pain is present in only ~30-40% and splenomegaly/hepatosplenomegaly are documented in a minority on exam, so their absence does not exclude the diagnosis (p.1358-1359).","Fluoroquinolones should no longer be used empirically in regions with high decreased-ciprofloxacin-susceptibility prevalence (Indian subcontinent, parts of Africa); travel from Pakistan raises concern for XDR/ceftriaxone-resistant strains (p.1361)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal grading scale; it distinguishes uncomplicated enteric fever (manageable at home with oral therapy) from disease warranting hospitalization (persistent vomiting, diarrhea, or abdominal distension) and from critically ill / severe typhoid characterized by shock and obtundation, plus complicated disease with GI hemorrhage, intestinal perforation, or other end-organ involvement (p.1360-1361).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1358","Harrison 22e p.1359","Harrison 22e p.1360","Harrison 22e p.1361"],"crossLinks":["GASTROENTERITIS","MALARIA","DENGUE","LEPTOSPIROSIS","DYSENTERY"]},"ERYSIPELAS":{"name":"Erysipelas","system":"Skin & Soft Tissue","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1051-1052, 1210-1211","clinicalPearls":["Abrupt onset of fiery-red swelling of the face or extremities, with well-defined indurated margins (notably along the nasolabial fold), rapid progression, and intense pain (Harrison 22e p.1052).","The sharply demarcated, raised border distinguishes erysipelas from cellulitis and reflects involvement limited to the superficial (upper) dermis and superficial lymphatics rather than the deeper subcutaneous tissue (Harrison 22e p.1051, p.1210).","A peau d'orange (orange-peel) texture of the skin is characteristic and is thought to reflect involvement of superficial lymphatics (Harrison 22e p.1210).","Lesion typically develops over a few hours and is accompanied by fever and chills; flaccid superficial blebs/bullae may appear on the second or third day (Harrison 22e p.1052, p.1210).","Classic distribution is the malar area of the face (often extending over the bridge of the nose to the contralateral malar region) or the lower extremities (Harrison 22e p.1210).","Infants and elderly adults are most commonly affected, and the severity of systemic toxicity varies (Harrison 22e p.1052).","Desquamation of the involved skin occurs roughly 5-10 days into the illness (Harrison 22e p.1052).","Classic cases with typical features are almost always due to beta-hemolytic streptococci, usually group A and occasionally group C or G (Harrison 22e p.1210)."],"pathophysiology":"Streptococcus pyogenes infecting the epidermis is translocated laterally to deeper structures via lymphatics, producing the characteristic rapid superficial spread of erysipelas. Subsequent engorgement or obstruction of lymphatics causes flaccid edema of the epidermis. The infection is confined to the superficial dermis and superficial lymphatics (in contrast to cellulitis, which involves deeper subcutaneous tissue), and extension to deeper soft tissues is rare (Harrison 22e p.1051-1052, p.1210).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Cellulitis (deeper dermal/subcutaneous involvement, with less sharply demarcated borders) (Harrison 22e p.1210)","Staphylococcus aureus cellulitis, which can produce a similar appearance, especially when the lesion is atypical, less intensely red, fades into surrounding skin, or arises around a wound or ulcer (Harrison 22e p.1210-1211)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":[],"redFlags":["Edema with purple bullae, ecchymosis, and cutaneous anesthesia suggests loss of vascular integrity and necessitates exploration of deeper structures for necrotizing fasciitis or myonecrosis (Harrison 22e p.1051).","Unexplained fever with disproportionate pain and tenderness in soft tissue, even without acute cutaneous inflammation, should raise suspicion for a deeper necrotizing process (Harrison 22e p.1051)."],"prognosis":"Generally responds well to therapy; with appropriate treatment, fever, pain, and the intense red color diminish, although swelling may continue to progress initially. Recurrence at the same site (sometimes years later) is not uncommon, particularly where normal lymphatic drainage has been disrupted (Harrison 22e p.1052, p.1210-1211).","pitfalls":["Atypical erysipelas may involve an unusual anatomic site, appear less intensely red, fade into surrounding skin, or cause only mild systemic illness, in which case a specific clinical diagnosis cannot reliably be made and broader empirical coverage (including S. aureus) is prudent (Harrison 22e p.1210-1211).","Extension to deeper soft tissues is rare in true erysipelas; if present, reconsider the diagnosis and exclude a deeper or necrotizing infection (Harrison 22e p.1052).","In most parts of the world, acute rheumatic fever is NOT a sequela of streptococcal skin infection (unlike pharyngitis), though post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis may follow either skin or throat infection (Harrison 22e p.1210)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1051","Harrison 22e p.1052","Harrison 22e p.1210","Harrison 22e p.1211"],"crossLinks":["CELLULITIS","NECROTIZING_FASCIITIS"]},"FEBRILE_NEUTROPENIA":{"name":"Febrile Neutropenia","system":"Systemic / Oncology","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.499-500 (Disorders of Granulocytes and Monocytes), p.617-618 (Principles of Cancer Treatment / myelosuppression), p.186 (ecthyma gangrenosum), p.154 (acute abdominal pain / typhlitis)","clinicalPearls":["Susceptibility to infection rises sharply once the neutrophil count drops below 1000/uL, and worsens markedly at lower thresholds (Harrison 22e p.499).","When the absolute neutrophil count falls below 500/uL, the host's ability to contain its own endogenous flora from sites such as the mouth and gut becomes impaired (Harrison 22e p.499).","Acute neutropenia from cancer chemotherapy carries a substantially higher infection risk than chronic neutropenia of months-to-years duration, which is often better tolerated (Harrison 22e p.499).","After conventional chemotherapy doses, the neutrophil nadir typically occurs about 6-14 days later, defining the window of highest infection risk (Harrison 22e p.617).","Certain agents (nitrosoureas, dacarbazine, temozolomide, procarbazine) can cause delayed marrow toxicity that may not appear until roughly 6 weeks after dosing (Harrison 22e p.617).","Ecthyma gangrenosum is a characteristic skin lesion of Pseudomonas bacteremia that occurs predominantly in neutropenic patients and is reported in up to roughly a quarter of Pseudomonas bacteremia cases (Harrison 22e p.186).","Even highly experienced clinicians may have no advantage over a junior physician in managing the neutropenic patient with fever and hypotension, underscoring the need for protocolized rapid response rather than reliance on intuition (Harrison 22e)."],"pathophysiology":"Most cytotoxic chemotherapeutic agents suppress bone marrow function, and polymorphonuclear neutrophils, with their very short circulating half-life of roughly 6-8 hours, are the cell line most rapidly and severely depleted. As the neutrophil count falls, the host loses the first-line cellular defense that normally contains commensal flora at mucosal surfaces; below an ANC of 500/uL control of endogenous mouth and gut organisms is impaired, and below 200/uL the local inflammatory response is essentially absent, so signs of infection are blunted. The combination of profound neutropenia and chemotherapy-induced mucosal barrier injury allows translocation and rapid dissemination of bacteria from the patient's own gut and oral flora. Neutropenia is most commonly iatrogenic, arising from cytotoxic or immunosuppressive therapy. (Harrison 22e p.499-500, p.617)","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Neutropenic enterocolitis (typhlitis) presenting as abdominal pain and fever in chemotherapy-induced marrow suppression (Harrison 22e p.154)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute graft-versus-host disease, which should be considered when a marrow-suppressed/transplant patient develops abdominal pain and fever (Harrison 22e p.154)","Drug fever and chemotherapy-related fever in the absence of demonstrable infection"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial absolute neutrophil count (band forms plus mature neutrophils) to track depth and duration of neutropenia (Harrison 22e p.499)","Skin examination for ecthyma gangrenosum suggesting Pseudomonas bacteremia (Harrison 22e p.186)","Cross-sectional abdominal imaging when neutropenic enterocolitis/typhlitis is suspected, with serial clinical examination for perforation (Harrison 22e p.154)"],"redFlags":["Hypotension or hemodynamic instability accompanying fever and neutropenia (Harrison 22e)","Ecthyma gangrenosum skin lesions, signaling probable Pseudomonas bacteremia (Harrison 22e p.186)","Abdominal pain and fever suggesting neutropenic enterocolitis (typhlitis) with risk of bowel perforation (Harrison 22e p.154)"],"prognosis":"Risk of serious infection is driven by both the depth and the expected duration of neutropenia; acute chemotherapy-induced neutropenia is more dangerous than chronic neutropenia, and the deepest risk corresponds to the post-chemotherapy nadir at roughly 6-14 days. Pseudomonas bacteremia in this setting is associated with rapidly progressive illness and characteristic ecthyma gangrenosum. (Harrison 22e p.499, p.617, p.186)","pitfalls":["Classic signs of infection and inflammation are blunted or absent in profound neutropenia (local inflammatory response is essentially lost below ANC 200/uL), so the usual physical findings of focal infection may be missing despite serious infection (Harrison 22e p.499).","The infecting organisms are frequently the patient's own endogenous mouth and gut flora rather than exogenous pathogens, so absence of an obvious external source does not exclude bacteremia (Harrison 22e p.499).","A single normal neutrophil count is insufficient; a falling count or failure to mount a neutrophil response to challenge is what defines risk and warrants attention (Harrison 22e p.499)."],"severityClassification":"Febrile neutropenia is defined by fever together with neutropenia (granulocytes below ~1500/uL, with clinically significant risk concentrated at ANC <500/uL and severe impairment of host defense at ANC <200/uL) (Harrison 22e p.499, p.617). Risk stratification for management intensity is performed with validated tools such as the MASCC risk index, which separates low-risk from high-risk febrile neutropenia (referenced in existing curated investigations; specific scoring not detailed in this Harrison excerpt).","references":["Harrison 22e p.499","Harrison 22e p.500","Harrison 22e p.617","Harrison 22e p.618","Harrison 22e p.186","Harrison 22e p.154"],"crossLinks":[]},"GASTROENTERITIS":{"name":"Acute Gastroenteritis","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch 49 (Diarrhea and Constipation, p.302-307); Ch 138 (Acute Infectious Diarrheal Diseases and Bacterial Food Poisoning, p.1076-1082); Ch 209 (Viral Gastroenteritis, p.1627-1630)","clinicalPearls":["More than 90% of acute diarrhea episodes are infectious, and most are mild and self-limited; vomiting, fever, and abdominal pain frequently accompany the illness (p.305).","The first decision point is to separate noninflammatory (watery, small-bowel, enterotoxin/viral) from inflammatory (dysenteric, colonic, invasion/cytotoxin) disease, since this dichotomy drives the need for stool studies and therapy (p.1078-1079).","Clinical timing offers etiologic clues: an abrupt illness with prominent vomiting and little or no fever within hours of eating points to a preformed toxin (e.g., S. aureus, B. cereus), whereas high fever with significant abdominal pain points to invasive or cytotoxin-producing organisms (p.305).","Vomiting can be the predominant or sole presenting feature of viral gastroenteritis, especially in children (p.1629).","Bloody diarrhea with only low-grade fever or no fever should specifically raise concern for Shiga toxin-producing/enterohemorrhagic E. coli (p.1079, p.1081).","Bulky pale/grey stools suggest a malabsorptive small-bowel process, while profuse 'rice-water' stools suggest cholera or a similar toxigenic secretory process (p.1078).","Reduced gastric acidity (achlorhydria, gastric surgery, or acid-suppressing drugs such as PPIs/H2 blockers) lowers the infectious barrier and increases susceptibility to Salmonella, Giardia, and other enteric pathogens (p.1077).","Immunocompromised hosts (cell-mediated defects/AIDS, hypogammaglobulinemia) get more severe, protracted, and sometimes invasive disease, and may harbor opportunistic pathogens not seen in immunocompetent patients (p.1080, p.305).","Tenesmus and frequent small-volume stools indicate a distal colonic/proctitis process (e.g., shigellosis, amebiasis) rather than a small-bowel secretory process (p.1078)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute infectious diarrhea results when an ingested pathogen overwhelms or bypasses host defenses (gastric acid, intestinal motility/peristalsis, mucus layer, secretory IgA, and the protective resident microbiota). Mechanistically there are two broad patterns. Noninflammatory disease arises in the proximal small bowel and is mediated by enterotoxins (e.g., cholera toxin, E. coli heat-labile [LT] and heat-stable [ST] toxins) or by viral injury; cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates a GTP-binding protein to persistently activate adenylate cyclase, raising cyclic AMP, increasing chloride secretion and decreasing sodium absorption, producing watery secretory diarrhea without mucosal destruction. Inflammatory disease affects the colon or distal small bowel through mucosal invasion (Shigella, enteroinvasive E. coli, Salmonella) or cytotoxins (S. dysenteriae type 1, Shiga toxin-producing E. coli, C. difficile), destroying epithelium and producing dysentery with fecal leukocytes. Viral pathogens (norovirus, rotavirus) injure the small-intestinal villous epithelium: rotavirus destroys mature absorptive enterocytes and the loss of brush-border enzymes leaves unmetabolized disaccharides, generating combined secretory and osmotic diarrhea, with the nonstructural protein NSP4 acting as an enterotoxin. Crucially, glucose-coupled sodium absorption in the small intestine remains intact even during toxin-driven secretion, which is the physiologic basis for oral rehydration therapy (p.1076-1077, p.1081, p.1629).","additionalDifferentials":["Enterohemorrhagic E. coli (O157:H7 / Shiga toxin-producing) - watery progressing to bloody, HUS risk","Enteroaggregative E. coli (emerging traveler's-diarrhea pathogen)","Cholera (Vibrio cholerae) - profuse rice-water secretory diarrhea","Preformed-toxin food poisoning (Staphylococcus aureus, Bacillus cereus, Clostridium perfringens)","Sapovirus, astrovirus, enteric adenovirus types 40/41 (viral, mainly young children)","Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, Giardia (esp. travelers, hikers, immunocompromised, persistent cases)"],"infectionMimics":["Enteric fever / typhoid (penetrating pathogens Salmonella Typhi, Yersinia enterocolitica with fever, relative bradycardia, splenomegaly)","Yersinia enterocolitica terminal ileitis mimicking acute appendicitis","Clostridioides difficile colitis (recent or current antibiotics; predominant nosocomial cause)","Acute viral hepatitis A and other systemic infections (legionellosis, listeriosis, toxic shock, malaria) in which diarrhea is one feature","SARS-CoV-2 - diarrhea can be an early symptom"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Medication side effects (antibiotics, NSAIDs, antidysrhythmics, antihypertensives, some antidepressants, chemotherapy, laxatives) - the most common noninfectious cause","Ischemic colitis (age >50, lower abdominal pain preceding watery then bloody diarrhea, spares rectum)","Colonic diverticulitis; partial bowel obstruction; graft-versus-host disease","Initial presentation or flare of inflammatory bowel disease (Crohn's, ulcerative colitis) with abrupt onset mimicking infection","Toxic ingestions (organophosphates, amanita/other mushrooms, arsenic) and seafood toxins (ciguatera, scombroid)","Food allergy including alpha-gal (delayed mammalian-meat allergy) which can cause systemic collapse and be mistaken for dehydration"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fecal leukocyte smear (methylene blue) or, more sensitively, fecal lactoferrin to distinguish inflammatory from noninflammatory disease","Culture-independent multiplex nucleic-acid amplification (PCR) stool panels - now first-line for severe/domestically acquired disease; faster and more sensitive than culture","Stool culture for Salmonella, Shigella, and Campylobacter in febrile inflammatory diarrhea acquired outside hospital","Sorbitol-MacConkey plating / Shiga-toxin or PCR testing when EHEC (E. coli O157:H7) is suspected (visibly bloody stool)","Specific antigen/microscopy for Giardia and Cryptosporidium, and antigen detection for E. histolytica, when suspected or in persistent diarrhea","Examination for ova and parasites if diarrhea persists beyond ~10 days","Nosocomial diarrhea workup should focus on C. difficile toxin testing; routine stool culture in the hospital is low-yield","Endoscopy/imaging reserved for persistent (>30 day) or suspected noninfectious disease; not indicated during the acute episode (risk of false-positive results)"],"redFlags":["Signs of severe dehydration: lethargy/obtundation, feeble pulse, hypotension, shock","Profuse diarrhea with dehydration","Overtly bloody stools","High fever (>=38.5 C / 101 F)","Diarrhea persisting >48 h without improvement (or >2 weeks suggesting a different chronic spectrum)","Severe abdominal pain in a patient aged >50 years","Elderly (>=70 years) or immunocompromised host","Recent antibiotic use (C. difficile concern)","Bloody diarrhea with low-grade or absent fever (EHEC / HUS warning)"],"prognosis":"Most acute infectious gastroenteritis is self-limited and resolves within days; viral norovirus/sapovirus illness typically lasts 1-3 days and rotavirus 3-7 days. Death is uncommon in healthy adults but occurs chiefly from dehydration in infants and debilitated elderly; globally acute diarrhea remains a leading cause of childhood death. Oral rehydration has dramatically reduced mortality (e.g., cholera case fatality from >50% untreated to <1%). Recognized post-infectious sequelae include reactive arthritis (after invasive Shigella/Salmonella/Campylobacter/Yersinia), Guillain-Barre syndrome (after Campylobacter), hemolytic-uremic syndrome (after Shiga toxin-producing bacteria), post-infectious IBS (notably after Campylobacter), and transient post-infectious lactase deficiency; recurrent childhood diarrhea in resource-poor settings causes environmental enteropathy with growth and developmental stunting (p.302, p.305, p.1077-1078, p.1081, p.1628-1629).","pitfalls":["Avoid antimotility agents (e.g., loperamide) and bismuth-based slowing in febrile or bloody/dysenteric diarrhea - they may prolong fever and pathogen shedding in Shigella and other invasive infections; in viral gastroenteritis antibiotics and antimotility agents are not indicated.","Do NOT give antibiotics when enterohemorrhagic (Shiga toxin-producing) E. coli is suspected, because antibiotics can increase toxin production and markedly raise the risk of hemolytic-uremic syndrome.","Opioid/antimotility therapy can increase the frequency of bacteremia in Salmonella gastroenteritis and prolonged illness in Shigella.","Most acute diarrhea needs no microbiologic workup; routine extensive stool testing of mild self-limited disease wastes resources.","Do not screen for or endoscope to exclude celiac/Crohn disease or IBD during an acute episode - the risk is low and false-positive results are common.","A positive multiplex PCR panel may detect multiple organisms or carriage and can be hard to interpret; lack of an isolate prevents susceptibility testing and outbreak typing, so culture confirmation is still advised after a positive bacterial PCR.","Empirical antibacterial coverage is warranted regardless of pathogen recovery in selected high-risk patients (immunocompromised, prosthetic/mechanical heart valves, recent vascular grafts, elderly)."],"severityClassification":"Severity is graded clinically by functional impact and dehydration rather than by a single score. Mild: activity unrestricted. Moderate: activities altered. Severe: incapacitated. Dehydration assessment is the key severity measure on examination - mild = thirst, dry mouth, reduced axillary sweat, decreased urine output, slight weight loss; moderate = orthostatic blood-pressure fall, skin tenting, sunken eyes (or sunken fontanelle in infants); severe = lethargy/obtundation, feeble pulse, hypotension, and shock. Frequency of stools forewarns impending dehydration. By duration, diarrhea is acute (<2 weeks), persistent (2-4 weeks), or chronic (>4 weeks) (p.302/304, p.307, p.1078).","references":["Harrison 22e p.302","Harrison 22e p.304","Harrison 22e p.305","Harrison 22e p.306","Harrison 22e p.307","Harrison 22e p.1076","Harrison 22e p.1077","Harrison 22e p.1078","Harrison 22e p.1079","Harrison 22e p.1080","Harrison 22e p.1081","Harrison 22e p.1627","Harrison 22e p.1628","Harrison 22e p.1629"],"crossLinks":["DYSENTERY","C_DIFF","ENTERIC_FEVER"]},"HAP":{"name":"Hospital Acquired Pneumonia","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.131 (Pneumonia), pp.1066, 1072-1076","clinicalPearls":["VAP is defined as pneumonia with onset 48 hours or more after the start of mechanical ventilation; research on HAP has centred on VAP, but the same principles extend to ventilated HAP and to non-ICU HAP (p.1072).","About 70% of HAP cases are acquired outside the ICU and 30% within it; roughly 35% of all HAP patients require mechanical ventilation, which makes ventilated HAP a distinct entity whose bacteriology and outcome resemble those of VAP (p.1072).","In nonintubated HAP an expectorated sputum sample is used for microbiologic diagnosis but is confounded by frequent colonisation with oral pathogens; deep lower respiratory tract samples in VAP/ventilated HAP are more reliable yet may still contain colonising organisms (p.1072).","The non-MDR 'core pathogens' of VAP closely mirror those of severe CAP and predominate when pneumonia develops in the first 5-7 days of admission; with risk factors (especially prior antibiotics) MDR pathogens become a concern even early (p.1072).","Most hospitals contend with P. aeruginosa and MRSA, but the specific MDR organisms are institution-specific and can differ even between critical-care units in the same hospital (p.1072).","Prior antibiotic exposure is by far the dominant risk factor for colonisation with pathogenic organisms; P. aeruginosa almost never infects patients with no prior antibiotic exposure (pp.1073, 1076).","Only about one-third of colonised intubated patients go on to develop VAP, indicating that the final step is overwhelming of host defences by a large bacterial inoculum rather than colonisation alone (p.1073).","Severely ill septic or trauma patients appear to enter a state of immunoparalysis several days after ICU admission, coinciding with the period of greatest VAP risk; hyperglycaemia and frequent transfusions adversely affect immune response (p.1073).","Serial changes in oxygenation may detect HAP/VAP earlier than other findings and can also serve to monitor response to therapy (p.1073).","Absence of bacteria on a Gram stain of an endotracheal aspirate makes pneumonia an unlikely cause of the fever or infiltrate; presence of an MDR pathogen can be excluded as a target if tracheal aspirate cultures (or sensitive molecular tests) are negative, enabling de-escalation (p.1074).","Anaerobes may be somewhat more common in nonintubated HAP than in VAP because of greater macroaspiration risk and lower lower-airway oxygen tension, but they usually contribute only to polymicrobial pneumonia and rarely need specific targeted therapy (p.1076).","A bronchopneumonia pattern is most common in nosocomial pneumonias, in contrast to the lobar pattern more typical of bacterial CAP; in VAP, respiratory bronchiolitis may precede a radiologically apparent infiltrate (p.1066).","The 'healthcare-associated pneumonia' category has been abandoned because it did not reliably predict resistant-organism infection and drove excessive broad-spectrum antibiotic use; resistance risk should instead be judged individually from comorbidities, recent hospitalisation, and recent antibiotic therapy (p.1066)."],"pathophysiology":"Three factors are central to VAP pathogenesis: colonisation of the oropharynx with pathogenic organisms, aspiration of those organisms into the lower respiratory tract, and compromise of host defences (p.1073). The endotracheal tube is the single most important risk factor: it bypasses normal anti-aspiration mechanisms, and although it blocks large-volume aspiration, microaspiration is worsened by secretions pooling above the cuff. The tube and suctioning damage tracheal mucosa and facilitate colonisation, and pathogenic bacteria form a protective glycocalyx biofilm on the tube surface that shields them from antibiotics and host defences; biofilm fragments can embolise to distal airways or be dislodged during suctioning to reinoculate the trachea (p.1073). Normal oropharyngeal flora is frequently replaced by pathogenic organisms under antibiotic selection pressure, cross-infection, severe systemic illness, and malnutrition (p.1073). Almost all intubated patients have transient colonisation, but progression to pneumonia reflects a large bacterial inoculum overwhelming host defences amid post-admission immunoparalysis (p.1073). More broadly, pneumonia is now viewed as an emergent disturbance of the lung microbiota driven by self-accelerating inflammatory feedback rather than simple invasion of a sterile space (p.1066).","additionalDifferentials":["Purulent tracheobronchitis (notably in chronic ventilator units, where it can impede weaning) (p.1073)"],"infectionMimics":["Antibiotic-associated diarrhoea (as an alternative source of fever/leukocytosis) (p.1074)","Central line-associated infection (p.1074)","Sinusitis (p.1074)","Urinary tract infection (p.1074)","Fungal or viral pneumonia (mimics requiring different antimicrobials than VAP) (p.1074)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Atypical pulmonary oedema (p.1074)","Pulmonary contusion (p.1074)","Alveolar haemorrhage (p.1074)","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (p.1074)","Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) (p.1074)","Pulmonary infarction / pulmonary embolism (p.1074)","Pancreatitis (as a non-infective source of fever/leukocytosis) (p.1074)","Drug fever (p.1074)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative culture of deep respiratory tract samples to separate colonisation from true infection; the more distal the sample, the more specific the result and the lower the colony-count threshold (e.g., endotracheal aspirate threshold ~10^6 cfu/mL vs protected specimen brush ~10^3 cfu/mL), at the cost of falling sensitivity for blindly collected distal samples (p.1074)","Gram stain of endotracheal aspirate (absence of bacteria argues against pneumonia) (p.1074)","Differential cell counts and staining for intracellular organisms on respiratory samples (p.1074)","Detection of local protein levels elevated in response to infection (p.1074)","Serial / repeat quantitative cultures to track microbiologic response and clarify treatment failure (p.1075)","Procalcitonin to help guide duration of therapy in conjunction with the initial clinical response (already curated; Harrison adds the duration-of-therapy context, p.1075)"],"redFlags":["New or worsening hypoxaemia / serial decline in oxygenation in a ventilated patient (may herald pneumonia before other signs) (p.1073)","Septic shock accompanying suspected VAP (influences empirical regimen breadth) (p.1075)","Failure to improve clinically by 48-72 h of appropriate therapy (improvement, when it occurs, is usually evident in this window) (p.1076)","Necrotising pneumonia (e.g., P. aeruginosa or S. aureus) causing significant pulmonary haemorrhage or empyema (p.1075)"],"prognosis":"Crude VAP mortality is reported as high as 50-70%, but the key measure is attributable mortality, since many VAP patients have underlying disease that would itself be fatal. Attributable mortality exceeded 25% in one matched-cohort study, whereas more recent estimates are much lower (5-10%); VAP complicating COVID-19 carries higher attributable mortality than other VAP. VAP in trauma patients shows no attributable mortality, likely because many were previously healthy. MDR pathogens carry significantly greater attributable mortality than non-MDR organisms, and some pathogens (e.g., S. maltophilia) chiefly mark a severely immunocompromised, high-risk host. Nonintubated non-ICU HAP has lower mortality and lower risk of antibiotic failure than VAP and ventilated HAP because of better host defences (pp.1076).","pitfalls":["Applying CAP-type clinical criteria to ventilated patients consistently overdiagnoses VAP, owing to frequent tracheal colonisation, multiple alternative causes of radiographic infiltrates, and many other sources of fever in the critically ill (pp.1073-1074).","No single set of criteria reliably diagnoses pneumonia in a ventilated patient; over-reliance on portable chest radiographs is limited by frequent pre-existing infiltrates and poor portable film quality (p.1073).","Effective antibiotic given in the 24-48 h before sampling can drive quantitative cultures below the diagnostic threshold and yield false-negative results; conversely, counts above threshold during therapy suggest the current antibiotics are ineffective (p.1074).","Sampling too early in the course, or after an effective host response has already reduced bacterial counts, can also give sub-threshold results; ideally sample as soon as pneumonia is suspected and before starting or changing antibiotics (p.1074).","Chest radiographs often worsen initially during treatment, making them less reliable than clinical criteria as indicators of response (p.1076).","Recurrent VAP with the same organism can occur because the endotracheal tube biofilm allows persistence and reintroduction; about half of recurrent Pseudomonas cases are actually due to a new strain (p.1075)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames empirical management around two risk axes rather than a discrete severity score: (1) presence or absence of risk factors for MDR pathogens (prior antibiotic therapy, prior hospitalisation, local antibiogram; for MRSA additionally known MRSA colonisation, chronic haemodialysis, or local MRSA pneumonia rate >10% or unknown), and (2) predicted risk of death, with a mortality risk of 15% or less considered low risk. The frequency of VAP also varies with duration of ventilation, with the highest hazard in the first 5 days, a plateau (~1%/day) after about 2 weeks, and a cumulative rate up to ~70% by 30 days of ventilation (pp.1073, 1075).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1066","Harrison 22e p.1072","Harrison 22e p.1073","Harrison 22e p.1074","Harrison 22e p.1075","Harrison 22e p.1076"],"crossLinks":["VAP","CAP","ASPIRATION_PNEUMONIA"]},"IE":{"name":"Infective Endocarditis","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1036-1048 (Chapter 133, Infective Endocarditis)","clinicalPearls":["IE spans a clinical continuum from acute to subacute, and the causative organism largely dictates the tempo: beta-hemolytic streptococci, S. aureus and pneumococci tend to produce an acute, rapidly destructive course, whereas viridans streptococci, enterococci, CoNS and HACEK organisms usually cause subacute disease (Bartonella, T. whipplei, C. burnetii and M. chimaera are exceptionally indolent).","Fever is present in 80-90% of cases and a heart murmur in 80-85%, but in subacute IE fever is typically low-grade (rarely above 39.4 C) and may be blunted altogether in elderly, severely debilitated, or renal-failure patients.","A new or worsening regurgitant murmur is heard in only 20-50% of cases; in acute IE on a previously normal valve a murmur may be absent at first but is ultimately detected in about 85%.","Classic peripheral stigmata (Janeway lesions, Osler nodes, Roth spots, subungual/splinter hemorrhages) appear in only about 2-15% of cases and have become infrequent with earlier diagnosis; their absence does not exclude IE.","Janeway lesions are nonsuppurative painless hemorrhagic macules of the palms/soles classically linked to prolonged subacute infection, whereas septic emboli (subungual hemorrhages, Osler nodes) mimicking these lesions are common in acute S. aureus IE.","Three independent predictors of embolization are S. aureus etiology, mobile vegetations larger than 10 mm, and involvement of the anterior mitral leaflet.","In people who inject drugs, 35-60% of IE is confined to the tricuspid valve and presents with fever but faint or absent murmur and no peripheral stigmata, often with septic pulmonary emboli (cough, pleuritic pain, nodular infiltrates).","S. aureus bacteremia carries an 8-14% prevalence of IE and high mortality, so echocardiography (high-quality TTE, preferably TEE) is recommended routinely; intracardiac device, emboli, 2 or more positive cultures, community acquisition, vertebral osteomyelitis or persistent bacteremia each raise IE risk and argue for early TEE.","About 12-17% of patients with E. faecalis bacteremia and about 7% with non-beta-hemolytic streptococcal bacteremia have IE, justifying echocardiographic screening when predictive features are present.","Up to half of arterial emboli precede the diagnosis of IE; embolic risk falls rapidly once effective antibiotics begin, so emboli occurring late during or after therapy do not by themselves indicate treatment failure.","In hemodynamically stable patients with suspected subacute IE, empirical antibiotics can reasonably be withheld initially to allow uncontaminated blood cultures, but patients with sepsis or deteriorating hemodynamics should be treated immediately after the first three culture sets are drawn.","Surgery is indicated (as a knowledge concept, distinct from antibiotic regimens) for optimal outcome when there is moderate-severe CHF/shock from valve dysfunction, paravalvular extension with abscess/fistula/heart block, persistent bacteremia without an extracardiac source despite 7-10 days of therapy, lack of effective antimicrobial therapy (fungal, Brucella, multidrug-resistant gram-negative), or an unstable partially dehisced prosthetic valve; it is strongly considered for large (>10 mm) hypermobile vegetations with prior embolus and valve dysfunction, very large (>30 mm) vegetations, S. aureus PVE with intracardiac complications, and relapse after optimal therapy. Removal of all hardware is recommended for established CIED-IE."],"pathophysiology":"The prototypic lesion is the vegetation - a mass of platelets, fibrin, microorganisms and scant inflammatory cells - usually on heart valves but also on the low-pressure side of a ventricular septal defect, on mural endocardium injured by aberrant blood jets or foreign bodies, or on intracardiac devices. Intact endothelium resists most bacteria; endothelial injury (e.g., at sites of high-velocity jet impact or on the low-pressure side of a structural lesion) permits direct infection by virulent organisms or formation of a sterile platelet-fibrin thrombus (nonbacterial thrombotic endocarditis, NBTE) that becomes a nidus for bacterial seeding during transient bacteremia. NBTE is most often associated with mitral regurgitation, aortic stenosis, aortic regurgitation, ventricular septal defects and complex congenital heart disease, and also arises from hypercoagulable states (marantic endocarditis of malignancy/chronic disease and the bland vegetations of SLE and antiphospholipid syndrome). Circulating organisms adhere via surface adhesins (MSCRAMMs) - fibronectin-binding proteins, S. aureus clumping factor, enterococcal Fss2/Ace/Ebp pili, streptococcal glucans/FimA; S. aureus can even invade intact endothelium via fibronectin-binding proteins and von Willebrand factor, allowing infection of previously normal valves. Adherent organisms proliferate into microcolonies, induce platelet deposition and a local procoagulant (tissue-factor) state, and become buried in fibrin; deep organisms are metabolically inactive and relatively antibiotic-resistant while surface organisms are continuously shed into the blood. Clinical manifestations arise from intracardiac structural damage, embolization of vegetation fragments causing remote infarction/infection, hematogenous seeding during bacteremia, and immune-complex deposition; constitutional symptoms reflect cytokine release.","additionalDifferentials":["Marantic (nonbacterial thrombotic) endocarditis associated with malignancy and chronic wasting disease","Atrial myxoma","Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome with bland valvular vegetations","Systemic lupus erythematosus with sterile (Libman-Sacks-type) vegetations"],"infectionMimics":["Culture-negative IE due to fastidious organisms (Coxiella burnetii, Bartonella spp., Tropheryma whipplei, Brucella, nutritionally variant Granulicatella/Gemella/Abiotrophia, Corynebacterium/Cutibacterium acnes, Mycobacterium chimaera)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Marantic endocarditis of malignancy","Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome","Atrial myxoma","SLE-associated sterile endocarditis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Transesophageal echocardiography (TEE) detects vegetations in over 90% of definite IE and is preferred over TTE, which misses vegetations under 2 mm and yields inadequate images in about 20% of patients; a negative TEE with high clinical suspicion should be repeated in 7-10 days.","Three-dimensional TEE to better define vegetations and perivalvular extension.","ECG-gated multislice cardiac CT angiography - superior to TEE for pseudoaneurysm/abscess and can assess coronary patency preoperatively; CTA findings count as a major imaging criterion.","18F-FDG-PET/CT - increases sensitivity in suspected prosthetic-valve IE (including TAVR-PVE), aortic graft, CIED pocket/lead and LVAD infection, and extracardiac foci; false positives within 3 months of valve surgery.","Molecular testing of excised/embolized vegetation: organism-specific PCR (C. burnetii, Bartonella, T. whipplei, C. acnes, M. hominis) and broad-range 16S (or 28S for fungi) rRNA PCR with sequencing; only moderately sensitive, so a negative result does not exclude IE.","Serologic/PCR testing for culture-negative pathogens: Coxiella burnetii (phase I IgG titer >=1:800) and Bartonella henselae/quintana (IgG titer >=1:800).","Brain MRI/MRA in any neurologic signs or symptoms (including unusual headache) to detect emboli, hemorrhage, or mycotic aneurysms.","Spine MRI for recalcitrant back pain or focal spinal tenderness to assess for vertebral osteomyelitis/spondylodiscitis and epidural abscess.","Routine adjunctive labs: CBC (anemia in 70-90%), ESR (elevated in 60-90%), CRP (elevated in over 90%), microscopic hematuria (30-50%), rheumatoid factor (~50%), creatinine and liver function tests."],"redFlags":["Congestive heart failure or pulmonary edema/cardiogenic shock from valve dysfunction or intracardiac fistula (develops in 30-40%) - a surgical emergency","New electrocardiographic conduction disturbance or new heart block, suggesting paravalvular/septal abscess extension","Cerebrovascular embolism (stroke or encephalopathy), which complicates 15-35% of cases","Intracranial hemorrhage from hemorrhagic infarct or ruptured mycotic aneurysm","Persistent fever or persistently positive blood cultures despite appropriate antibiotics, indicating uncontrolled infection","New or worsening regurgitant murmur or prosthetic valve dehiscence","Large (over 10 mm) mobile vegetation, particularly on the anterior mitral leaflet, with high embolic risk","Acute S. aureus or fungal IE, and any prosthetic-valve involvement"],"prognosis":"Overall survival in developed countries is about 80-85%, but varies markedly by subgroup. About 85-90% survive NVE caused by viridans streptococci, HACEK organisms, or synergy-susceptible enterococci. Non-injecting patients with S. aureus NVE have 55-70% survival; injecting patients have 85-90%, but 1-year mortality rises to 20-30% if substance-use disorder is not addressed. Prosthetic-valve IE arising within 2 months of surgery carries 40-50% mortality versus only 10-20% for late-onset PVE; elderly TAVR-PVE in-hospital mortality is 35-50%, rising to 60-75% at 1 year. Crude survival after successful treatment is roughly 80-90% at 1 year and 70-80% at 2 years. Death often reflects comorbidities and end-organ complications rather than antibiotic failure. Adverse outcomes are linked to older age, severe comorbidity/diabetes, delayed diagnosis, prosthetic or aortic valve involvement, virulent or resistant pathogens (S. aureus, P. aeruginosa, yeast), intracardiac and major neurologic complications, and health care-associated infection.","pitfalls":["Relying on a new/worsening murmur alone - it is a physical-exam minor criterion only; a major imaging criterion requires echocardiographic evidence of new valvular regurgitation, not auscultation.","Treating a negative TTE (or even a negative initial TEE) as ruling out IE; TTE misses vegetations under 2 mm and is poor for prosthetic valves, and initial TEE is falsely negative in 6-18% (especially TAVR-PVE) - repeat in 7-10 days when suspicion persists.","Forgetting that 5-15% of IE is blood-culture negative, often because of prior antibiotics; withholding empirical therapy when stable preserves diagnostic yield.","Interpreting persistent organism DNA detected in excised valves (in ~45% of patients who completed therapy) as antibiotic failure - relapse after surgery for active IE is actually uncommon.","Misattributing emboli that occur late during or after effective therapy to treatment failure.","Overlooking that prior antibiotics within 2 weeks blunt culture yield and that nontypical organisms require 3 or more positive sets (plus no extracardiac focus) to count as a major criterion.","Assuming antithrombotic therapy prevents systemic emboli in NVE - it does not."],"severityClassification":"Diagnosis uses the 2023 Duke-International Society for Cardiovascular Infectious Diseases (Duke-ISCVID) update of the modified Duke criteria (sensitivity 80-93%). MAJOR criteria: (1) Microbiologic - a typical IE organism in 2 or more separate blood culture sets (S. aureus, S. lugdunensis, E. faecalis, all streptococci except S. pneumoniae and S. pyogenes, Granulicatella/Abiotrophia/Gemella, HACEK group; and, when intracardiac prosthetic material is present, CoNS, C. striatum, C. jeikeium, S. marcescens, P. aeruginosa, C. acnes, nontuberculous mycobacteria and Candida), OR a nontypical organism in 3 or more sets; positive PCR/single culture/serology for C. burnetii, Bartonella spp. or T. whipplei. (2) Imaging - echocardiography or cardiac CT showing vegetation, perforation/leaflet aneurysm, abscess, pseudoaneurysm or fistula, OR new valvular regurgitation, OR new prosthetic dehiscence; OR abnormal FDG-PET/CT activity on a valve/graft/lead/prosthetic material. (3) Surgical - IE seen at direct surgical inspection. MINOR criteria: predisposing condition (prior IE, injection drug use, prosthetic valve, prior valve repair, congenital heart disease such as bicuspid aortic valve, CIED, more-than-mild regurgitation/stenosis, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy); fever over 38.0 C; vascular phenomena (arterial emboli, septic pulmonary infarcts, mycotic aneurysm, intracranial/conjunctival hemorrhage, Janeway lesions); immunologic phenomena (glomerulonephritis, Osler nodes, Roth spots, positive rheumatoid factor); microbiologic evidence not meeting a major criterion; and PET/CT activity within 3 months of prosthetic implantation. CLASSIFICATION: DEFINITE IE = pathologic confirmation OR 2 major, or 1 major + 3 minor, or 5 minor criteria. POSSIBLE IE = 1 major + 1 minor, or 3 minor. REJECTED = firm alternative diagnosis, or resolution with under 4 days of antibiotics, or no histologic evidence at surgery/autopsy after under 4 days of therapy. IE is also classified by tempo (acute vs subacute), site (native vs prosthetic valve, right-sided/tricuspid), cause, and predisposition (injection drug use, health care-associated).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1036","Harrison 22e p.1037","Harrison 22e p.1038","Harrison 22e p.1039","Harrison 22e p.1040","Harrison 22e p.1041","Harrison 22e p.1046","Harrison 22e p.1047","Harrison 22e p.1048"],"crossLinks":["DEVICE_INFECTION","SEPSIS","ischemic_stroke","glomerulonephritis","MENINGITIS"]},"LEPTOSPIROSIS":{"name":"Leptospirosis","system":"Tropical Fever","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1439-1444","clinicalPearls":["Classically biphasic: an acute leptospiremic phase (fever lasting ~3-10 days, organism culturable/PCR-detectable in blood) followed by an immune phase in which symptoms may resolve as antibodies appear and leptospires can be recovered from urine; the two phases are not always distinct, mild cases may lack the second phase, and severe disease can be monophasic and fulminant (p.1442).","Incubation is usually 1-2 weeks but ranges from 2 to 30 days (p.1442).","Conjunctival suffusion (redness without exudate) is a characteristic but non-pathognomonic sign; muscle pain is intense, especially in the calves, back, and abdomen (p.1442).","Headache is intense and frontal or retro-orbital, sometimes with photophobia, resembling dengue (p.1442).","Weil's syndrome denotes the severe triad of jaundice, acute kidney injury, and hemorrhage (p.1442-1443).","Severe pulmonary hemorrhage (with or without jaundice) is increasingly recognized as an important and widespread presentation; hemoptysis may not become apparent until the patient is intubated (p.1439, 1442).","Jaundice occurs in only ~5-10% of patients, can be deep with an orange skin cast, and is usually NOT associated with fulminant hepatic necrosis (p.1442).","Aseptic meningitis is more common in children than adults; altered mental status in severe disease may reflect leptospiral meningitis, which can occur in anicteric patients lacking other hallmarks (p.1442-1443).","Autoimmune-associated uveitis is a recognized, potentially chronic late sequela (p.1443).","Idea that specific serogroups cause distinct clinical syndromes has been refuted, although some serovars tend to cause more severe disease (p.1442).","Serology lacks sensitivity in the early acute phase (up to day 5) and cannot guide a timely treatment decision; PCR (notably real-time PCR) can confirm diagnosis during the first 5 days (p.1443).","MAT antibodies generally do not reach detectable levels until the second week of illness, and the antibody response may be blunted by early antibiotics (p.1443)."],"pathophysiology":"Leptospires enter through cuts, abraded skin, or mucous membranes (especially conjunctival and oral). The highly motile organisms proliferate, cross tissue barriers, and disseminate hematogenously to all organs (leptospiremic phase), evading innate immune mechanisms such as complement-mediated killing and phagocytosis; an exaggerated proinflammatory response correlates with mortality. With the immune phase, antibodies appear and leptospires clear from blood but persist in liver, lung, kidney, heart, and brain. Renal pathology shows acute tubular damage and interstitial nephritis, progressing to interstitial edema and acute tubular necrosis, with dysregulated nephron transporters causing impaired sodium absorption, potassium wasting, and polyuria. Liver histology shows focal (not widespread) hepatocellular necrosis, inflammation, bile canaliculus plugging, and hepatocyte apoptosis, with disruption of intercellular junctions and bile leakage. Petechiae and hemorrhages occur across many organs. Hemorrhage is associated with thrombocytopenia (likely platelet consumption); a consumptive coagulopathy with elevated coagulation-activation markers, reduced anticoagulant markers, and dysregulated fibrinolysis may progress to overt DIC. Endothelial cell activation (raised soluble E-selectin and von Willebrand factor) correlates with severity, and leptospires/leptospiral proteins disrupt endothelial barrier function, increasing permeability. The LPS has unusually low endotoxic potency, yet serovar-specific anti-LPS antibodies are central to protective humoral immunity (p.1441-1442).","additionalDifferentials":["Influenza and SARS-CoV-2 / other respiratory viral infections (when fever, headache, myalgia predominate)","Chikungunya","Ehrlichiosis / rickettsial diseases","Acute HIV infection","Hantavirus infection (hemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome or hantavirus cardiopulmonary syndrome)","Yellow fever and other viral hemorrhagic fevers (Rift Valley fever, filovirus infections, Lassa fever) when bleeding is present","Acute schistosomiasis"],"infectionMimics":["Dengue (and dengue hemorrhagic fever when bleeding present)","Malaria","Typhoid fever","Scrub typhus (rash may be misattributed)","Viral hepatitis","Acute HIV infection","Hantavirus infection","Yellow fever / viral hemorrhagic fevers"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PCR (notably real-time PCR) of blood/CSF/urine — confirms diagnosis with high accuracy during the first 5 days of illness, before serology becomes positive (p.1443)","Microscopic agglutination test (MAT) using live leptospiral strains — paired acute/convalescent sera seeking a fourfold rise; single high titer (1:200-1:800 depending on endemicity) supportive (p.1443)","IgM ELISA and commercial rapid lateral-flow / latex agglutination tests (useful where MAT/culture unavailable) (p.1443)","Blood/urine/CSF culture (special media; may take weeks to months) (p.1441, 1443)","C-reactive protein, procalcitonin, ESR (raised inflammatory markers) (p.1443)","Coagulation profile / DIC markers (D-dimer, antithrombin, protein C) in severe disease (p.1443)","Serum amylase (often elevated even without overt pancreatitis) (p.1443)","Serum electrolytes including magnesium (urinary magnesium loss is characteristic; hypokalemia, hyponatremia) (p.1443)","Urinalysis (leukocytes, erythrocytes, hyaline/granular casts, mild proteinuria) (p.1443)","Bilirubin, aminotransferases, alkaline phosphatase (bilirubin may be high; transaminase rises typically only moderate) (p.1443)","Chest radiograph / CT (patchy bilateral lower-lobe alveolar pattern from alveolar hemorrhage; pleura-based densities; ground-glass ARDS pattern) (p.1443)","ECG (nonspecific ST/T changes; repolarization abnormalities and arrhythmias are poor prognostic signs) (p.1443)","CSF examination when meningitis suspected (pleocytosis from a few to >1000 cells/uL, lymphocytic but may be PMN-predominant early; protein normal or slightly raised, glucose normal) (p.1443)","Co-testing for rickettsiae, dengue, and hantavirus given overlap and reported dual infections (p.1443)"],"redFlags":["Age >40 years","Altered mental status","Acute kidney injury / oliguria or anuria","Respiratory insufficiency, hemoptysis, or pulmonary hemorrhage","Hypotension / septic shock","Cardiac arrhythmias and repolarization abnormalities on ECG","Jaundice with bleeding (Weil's syndrome triad)","Thrombocytopenia (platelet count <=100 x10^9/L) — linked to bleeding and renal failure","Evidence of coagulation activation / DIC","Pregnancy (high fetal mortality)"],"prognosis":"Most patients recover, with renal and hepatic function typically returning to normal in survivors (hemodialysis can be lifesaving). Overall mean case-fatality is near 10% for severe cases; severe leptospirosis carries a case-fatality of 1-50%. Mortality is highest in the elderly and those with pulmonary hemorrhage or Weil's syndrome. Untreated leptospiral meningitis has reported mortality of ~13% versus ~2% with antibiotics. Pregnancy is associated with high fetal mortality. Post-leptospirosis sequelae include prolonged fatigue, myalgia, malaise, headache, depression-like symptoms persisting for years, and chronic autoimmune uveitis (p.1443-1444).","pitfalls":["Absence of a travel or freshwater-exposure history does not exclude the diagnosis (p.1443).","Absence of renal involvement does not rule out leptospirosis (p.1443).","Relying on serology in the first ~5 days risks a false-negative; antibodies are often undetectable until the second week and early antibiotics blunt the response (p.1443).","Cross-reactions are frequent in MAT, so the infecting serovar/serogroup cannot be definitively identified without isolating the organism (p.1443).","Leptospiral meningitis may present without jaundice or other classic severe-disease hallmarks, making it easy to miss (p.1443).","Jaundice can be profound yet usually does not signify fulminant hepatic necrosis, so hepatic transaminase rises are only moderate (p.1442-1443).","The transient rash may be misdiagnosed as scrub typhus or a viral exanthem (p.1442).","Dual infection (e.g., with dengue, rickettsiae, or hantavirus) can occur, so a positive alternative test does not exclude leptospirosis (p.1443)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison distinguishes mild leptospirosis (sudden-onset flu-like febrile illness with headache, myalgia, conjunctival suffusion; usually self-limiting over 7-10 days, low mortality) from severe leptospirosis, which is often rapidly progressive with multiorgan involvement. Severe disease classically presents as Weil's syndrome (triad of jaundice, acute kidney injury, and hemorrhage) and/or severe pulmonary hemorrhage syndrome, with a case-fatality of 1-50% (p.1442-1443).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1439","Harrison 22e p.1440","Harrison 22e p.1441","Harrison 22e p.1442","Harrison 22e p.1443","Harrison 22e p.1444"],"crossLinks":["DENGUE","MALARIA","SCRUB_TYPHUS","VIRAL_HEPATITIS","CHIKUNGUNYA"]},"LIVER_ABSCESS":{"name":"Liver Abscess","system":"Hepatobiliary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1117-1118 (pyogenic/intraabdominal abscesses); p.1289-1290 (hypervirulent K. pneumoniae); p.1755-1757 (amebic liver abscess)","clinicalPearls":["The liver is the visceral organ most prone to abscess formation; in one series of intraabdominal abscesses, hepatic abscesses accounted for roughly half of all visceral abscesses (p.1117).","Fever is the most common presenting feature; about half of patients with a liver abscess have no localizing signs (no hepatomegaly, right-upper-quadrant tenderness, or jaundice), so the liver may not be obviously implicated on examination (p.1117).","In the elderly, liver abscess may present solely as fever of unknown origin, so abdominal imaging (especially of the right upper quadrant) should form part of any FUO workup (p.1117).","An elevated serum alkaline phosphatase is the single most reliable laboratory abnormality, present in about 70% of patients; bilirubin and aminotransferases are often normal or only modestly raised (p.1117).","The microbiology of pyogenic liver abscess tracks with the source: biliary-tract origin yields enteric gram-negative aerobic bacilli and enterococci, pelvic/intraperitoneal origin yields a mixed aerobic-anaerobic flora (B. fragilis most common), and haematogenous seeding usually yields a single organism such as S. aureus or a Streptococcus milleri (anginosus)-group streptococcus (p.1117).","Anaerobes are not typically involved in liver abscesses arising from biliary infection unless prior surgery has occurred (p.1117).","Candida liver abscess characteristically follows fungaemia in patients receiving chemotherapy and often becomes clinically apparent as neutrophil counts recover after a period of neutropenia (p.1117)."],"pathophysiology":"Pyogenic liver abscesses arise either from haematogenous spread of bacteria or from local spread from contiguous intraperitoneal infection. The organism reaches the liver by several routes: ascending biliary infection (currently the most common associated source), portal venous seeding via suppurative thrombosis of the portal vein (pylephlebitis, classically from pelvic or other intra-abdominal infection), direct contiguous spread, or systemic bacteraemic (haematogenous) seeding. Historically, ruptured appendicitis was the leading source. Hypervirulent (hypermucoviscous) K. pneumoniae causes a distinct community-acquired monomicrobial syndrome in which the organism establishes a primary liver abscess and then disseminates metastatically; this contrasts with the polymicrobial, source-driven pattern of biliary or enteric abscesses. (p.1117, p.1289-1290)","additionalDifferentials":["Splenic abscess (different clinical setting and organism profile, most often associated with bacterial endocarditis; high index of suspicion needed as it is frequently fatal if untreated) (p.1118)","Hepatic actinomycosis (Actinomyces israelii can produce multiple hepatic abscesses, sometimes with a contiguous splenic lesion) (p.1290 region)","Candidal (fungal) liver abscess in immunocompromised/post-chemotherapy patients (p.1117)"],"infectionMimics":["Amebic liver abscess (Entamoeba histolytica) — a treatable alternative with overlapping right-upper-quadrant pain, fever, and point hepatic tenderness; jaundice is rare and fewer than one-third have active diarrhoea at presentation (p.1117, p.1756)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Imaging is the most reliable diagnostic modality: ultrasonography, CT, MRI, and indium-labelled-WBC or gallium scintigraphy; more than one study may be needed (p.1117).","Chest radiography may give a clue — a newly elevated right hemidiaphragm, right basilar infiltrate, or right pleural effusion (p.1117).","Diagnostic aspiration of abscess contents (with Gram stain and culture), together with blood cultures, ideally obtained before starting empirical antibiotics (p.1118).","Amebic serology is positive in >95% of amebic liver abscess cases and PCR can also be used; negative results help exclude amebiasis (p.1117). Note enzyme immunoassay for the Gal/GalNAc lectin and LAMP/PCR can also be applied directly to abscess aspirate/pus (p.1756-1757).","Supportive labs: leukocytosis (~77%), normochromic normocytic anaemia (~50%), hypoalbuminaemia (~33%); concomitant bacteraemia in one-third to one-half of patients (p.1117).","For suspected hypervirulent K. pneumoniae, the string test for hypermucoviscosity is suggestive but not optimally sensitive or specific; the most accurate identification uses the biomarkers iucA, iroB, peg-344, rmpA, and rmpA2 (p.1290)."],"redFlags":["Features suggesting hypervirulent K. pneumoniae with metastatic spread — concurrent endophthalmitis (threatening vision loss), meningitis/brain abscess, or septic pulmonary nodules — mandate urgent evaluation of distant sites (p.1289-1290).","Multiple sizeable abscesses, viscous contents prone to plugging a catheter, presence of yeast, communication with an untreated/obstructed biliary tree, or no clinical response to percutaneous drainage within 4-7 days predict failure of percutaneous drainage and favour surgical intervention (p.1118).","Mortality remains appreciable (around 15%) despite treatment (p.1118)."],"prognosis":"Mortality from pyogenic liver abscess averaged about 15% despite treatment in the data cited; outcome is poorer with delayed source control or factors predicting failed percutaneous drainage. For hypervirulent K. pneumoniae infection, survivors may suffer catastrophic sequelae from metastatic spread, including vision loss (endophthalmitis) and major neurologic deficits. (p.1118, p.1289)","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish pyogenic from amebic liver abscess: amebic serology is positive in >95% of amebic cases, amebic abscesses are classically a single right-lobe lesion, and most travellers with amebic abscess present within ~5 months of leaving an endemic area — so travel history, lesion location, and serology should be sought before committing to a purely bacterial diagnosis (p.1117, p.1755-1756).","Aspirating an amebic abscess for trophozoites is low-yield: trophozoites reside in the abscess capsule, not in the readily aspirated necrotic ('anchovy-paste') centre, so their absence on aspirate does not exclude amebiasis (p.1756).","Up to 10% of patients with acute amebic liver abscess have negative serology early on, usually seroconverting within a week — a single early negative test does not exclude the diagnosis (p.1757).","Assuming anaerobic coverage is always required: anaerobes are generally not involved in abscesses of biliary origin (unless prior surgery), whereas pelvic/intraperitoneal-source abscesses are typically polymicrobial with B. fragilis (p.1117).","Attributing a cryptogenic pyogenic liver abscess solely to the liver: because biliary, portal (pelvic/appendiceal), and other intra-abdominal infections are leading sources, the underlying source must be sought and addressed (p.1117).","Overlooking that ampicillin/amoxicillin exposure within the prior 30 days has been associated with increased risk of the hypervirulent K. pneumoniae liver abscess syndrome, presumably via selection for the causative strain (p.1117)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1117","Harrison 22e p.1118","Harrison 22e p.1289","Harrison 22e p.1290","Harrison 22e p.1755","Harrison 22e p.1756","Harrison 22e p.1757"],"crossLinks":["AMOEBIC_LIVER_ABSCESS","CHOLANGITIS","SEPSIS"]},"LUNG_ABSCESS":{"name":"Lung Abscess","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1034-1036","clinicalPearls":["Roughly 80% of lung abscesses are primary, arising from aspiration in the absence of an underlying pulmonary or systemic disease; the remaining ~20% are secondary to a predisposing process such as bronchial obstruction or immunocompromise (p.1034).","A foul-smelling (putrid) breath, sputum, or empyema is considered essentially diagnostic of an anaerobic lung abscess; abscesses with no isolated pathogen (about 40%) are termed nonspecific and anaerobes are presumed (p.1035).","Primary abscesses favor the dependent segments (posterior segments of upper lobes, superior segments of lower lobes), and the right lung is involved more often than the left because the right mainstem bronchus is less angulated (p.1035).","Anaerobic abscesses often present indolently with night sweats, fatigue, and anemia, whereas non-anaerobic causes such as S. aureus can produce a fulminant course with high fevers and rapid progression (p.1035-1036).","Edentulous patients are thought very unlikely to develop a lung abscess, since teeth (especially with gingivitis/periodontal disease) provide the nidus for the anaerobic/microaerophilic colonization that seeds the lung (p.1034).","Lemierre's syndrome (oropharyngeal infection, classically Fusobacterium necrophorum, with septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein) is a recognized embolic source of lung abscess (p.1034-1035).","Metronidazole alone is inadequate because it covers anaerobes but not the microaerophilic streptococci that are commonly part of the mixed flora of primary abscesses (p.1036)."],"pathophysiology":"A lung abscess is necrosis and cavitation of lung parenchyma after microbial infection, typically a single dominant cavity >2 cm. In primary disease, anaerobes and microaerophilic streptococci colonizing the gingival crevices are aspirated into a susceptible host who carries an overwhelming aspirated burden or cannot clear it. An initial pneumonitis (worsened by gastric-acid tissue damage) progresses over about 7-14 days to parenchymal necrosis and cavitation, with anaerobes driving more extensive synergistic tissue destruction in polymicrobial infection. Secondary abscesses follow the predisposing mechanism: post-obstructive (tumor/foreign body) impairment of secretion clearance, or impaired host defense in immunocompromise allowing a broad pathogen range. Abscesses may also arise hematogenously from septic emboli (e.g., tricuspid endocarditis, Lemierre's syndrome).","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary malignancy presenting as a cavitary lesion (p.1035)","Pulmonary infarction (p.1035)","Pulmonary sequestration (p.1035)","Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (p.1035)","Sarcoidosis (p.1035)","Vasculitis / autoimmune disease such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis (p.1035)","Infected lung cysts or bullae (fluid-containing) (p.1035)","Septic emboli, e.g., from tricuspid valve endocarditis (p.1035)","Pulmonary manifestations of inflammatory bowel disease or pyoderma gangrenosum (p.1035)"],"infectionMimics":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis (and M. avium, M. kansasii) cavitary disease (p.1034)","Endemic fungi: Coccidioides, Histoplasma capsulatum, Blastomyces (p.1034)","Parasitic causes of cavitation: Entamoeba histolytica, Paragonimus westermani, Strongyloides stercoralis (p.1034)","Nocardia, Aspergillus, Mucorales, Cryptococcus, Rhodococcus equi, Pneumocystis jirovecii in immunocompromised hosts (p.1034)","Empyema / pleural space infection (must be distinguished from a peripheral abscess as it may need urgent drainage) (p.1035)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cavitary lung cancer (p.1035)","Pulmonary infarction (p.1035)","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis and other vasculitides (p.1035)","Sarcoidosis (p.1035)","Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (p.1035)","Fluid-filled cysts or bullae (p.1035)","Pulmonary sequestration (p.1035)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CT chest, which defines cavitation earlier than plain film, can reveal an underlying cause such as malignancy, and helps separate a peripheral abscess from a pleural infection (p.1035)","Molecular methods such as 16S rRNA gene amplification for more specific pathogen identification (p.1035)","Serologic studies for opportunistic pathogens (viral/fungal) when abscess is secondary or empirical therapy fails (p.1035)","Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage or protected brush specimen (early if bronchial obstruction is suspected) (p.1035-1036)","CT-guided percutaneous needle aspiration (p.1035)","Induced sputum for tuberculosis in patients from TB-endemic areas or with TB risk factors such as HIV (p.1036)","Bronchoscopy with biopsy in patients with malignancy risk or atypical presentation (p.1036)"],"redFlags":["Life-threatening hemoptysis (p.1036)","Massive aspiration of abscess contents into the airway (p.1036)","Extension to the pleural space with empyema, which may require urgent drainage (p.1035-1036)","Failure to defervesce or radiographic progression despite appropriate therapy (10-20% of patients do not respond) (p.1036)","Abscess >6-8 cm in diameter, which is less likely to respond to antibiotics alone (p.1036)","Sepsis at presentation (poor prognostic factor) (p.1036)"],"prognosis":"Mortality for primary lung abscess can be as low as ~2%, but secondary abscesses carry a much higher mortality, reported as high as 75% in some series. Adverse prognostic factors include age >60, malignancy-associated abscess, presence of aerobic bacteria, sepsis at presentation, symptom duration >8 weeks, and abscess size >6 cm. Defervescence on appropriate therapy can take up to 7 days, and 10-20% of patients fail to respond. Larger cavities at presentation predispose to persistent pneumatoceles or bronchiectasis. Prevention centers on airway protection, oral hygiene, minimizing sedation, head-of-bed elevation in aspiration-prone patients, and pathogen prophylaxis in selected immunocompromised hosts (p.1036).","pitfalls":["Failure to exclude an obstructing malignancy: in older patients, those with malignancy risk factors, or those lacking clear aspiration risk, early bronchoscopy/biopsy and CT are indicated rather than assuming a primary anaerobic abscess (p.1035-1036).","Treating a presumed primary abscess that does not improve without re-evaluating for a secondary cause (obstruction, unusual pathogen) (p.1036).","Sputum culture may miss the diagnosis: infection is usually polymicrobial and routine cultures often fail to reflect anaerobes (p.1035).","Using metronidazole as monotherapy, which misses microaerophilic streptococci (p.1036).","Misclassifying a peripheral abscess as a pleural infection (empyema) or vice versa, since empyema may need urgent drainage (p.1035).","Underestimating risk in immunocompromised hosts, who are fragile, infected by a wide pathogen range, and warrant early culture-directed diagnostics (p.1035)."],"severityClassification":"Lung abscesses are classified as primary (~80%, aspiration-related, anaerobe-predominant, no underlying lung/systemic disease) versus secondary (underlying obstruction, systemic illness, or immunocompromise); and by chronicity as acute (<4-6 weeks) versus chronic (~40% of cases). Size >6-8 cm marks lesions less responsive to antibiotics alone (p.1034, p.1036).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1034","Harrison 22e p.1035","Harrison 22e p.1036"],"crossLinks":["ASPIRATION_PNEUMONIA","lung_cancer","PULMONARY_TB"]},"MALARIA":{"name":"Malaria","system":"Tropical Fever","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e ch.231, pp.1760-1768","clinicalPearls":["Malaria is not a clinical diagnosis: any patient in or from an endemic area with fever needs thick and thin blood smears (or a rapid test if reliable microscopy is unavailable) to confirm infection and identify the species (Harrison 22e p.1766).","Early symptoms are nonspecific (malaise, headache, fatigue, abdominal discomfort, myalgia, then fever) and resemble a minor viral illness, so a high index of suspicion is essential (Harrison 22e p.1763).","The classic regular febrile paroxysms (chills, rigors, fever spikes at fixed intervals) are now unusual and, when present at presentation, suggest P. vivax or P. ovale (often a relapse); prompt effective treatment prevents the synchronized fever pattern from developing (Harrison 22e p.1763).","Mild thrombocytopenia is usual in malaria; a normal platelet count should call the diagnosis into question (Harrison 22e pp.1765, 1768).","In falciparum malaria, peripheral parasitemia underestimates true parasite burden because mature parasites sequester in deep vascular beds and only younger ring forms circulate (Harrison 22e p.1762).","A PfHRP2-based rapid test can stay positive for weeks after treatment, which helps diagnose severe malaria in a patient who has already taken antimalarials and cleared peripheral parasites but is a drawback in high-transmission settings (Harrison 22e p.1767).","Persistent gametocytemia after a full treatment course does not indicate drug resistance or a need to re-treat, because mature P. falciparum gametocytes are unaffected by most antimalarials (Harrison 22e p.1767).","Any obtundation, delirium, abnormal behavior, or generalized seizure in falciparum malaria must be taken seriously as a possible herald of cerebral malaria (Harrison 22e pp.1763-1764).","Cerebral malaria is a diffuse symmetric encephalopathy without true meningism; focal neurologic signs are unusual and signs of meningeal irritation are absent (Harrison 22e p.1764).","Specific retinal changes (patchy retinal opacification/whitening, vessel decolorization, pale-centered hemorrhages) on funduscopy support a diagnosis of cerebral malaria (Harrison 22e pp.1764-1765).","In severe malaria the usual adrenergic warning signs of hypoglycemia (sweating, tachycardia, gooseflesh) are absent and its neurologic effects cannot be distinguished from the disease itself, so glucose must be checked actively (Harrison 22e p.1764).","Quinine can provoke hypoglycemia by stimulating pancreatic insulin secretion, compounding the hypoglycemia of severe malaria (Harrison 22e p.1764).","Noncardiogenic pulmonary edema may appear even after several days of effective antimalarial therapy and is readily precipitated by over-vigorous IV fluids; mortality exceeds 80% (Harrison 22e p.1764).","Acidotic ('respiratory distress') breathing in a child with severe anemia is usually due to metabolic acidosis, not 'anemic heart failure' as previously mislabeled (Harrison 22e pp.1765-1766).","Approximately 6% of children and <1% of adults with severe malaria have concomitant bacteremia (higher with >20% parasitemia in adults), and nontyphoidal Salmonella bacteremia is specifically associated with P. falciparum (Harrison 22e p.1765).","In high-transmission areas low-density parasitemia is a common incidental finding, so severe malaria is easily overdiagnosed and must be distinguished from sepsis with coincidental parasitemia (Harrison 22e pp.1765, 1767).","Sudden delayed hemolysis can follow artesunate treatment of hyperparasitemia in nonimmune patients, from synchronous loss of once-parasitized 'pitted' red cells (Harrison 22e p.1765)."],"pathophysiology":"Infection begins when a female Anopheles inoculates sporozoites, which reach the liver and undergo preerythrocytic schizogony, each sporozoite yielding 10,000 to >30,000 merozoites. Released merozoites invade red cells, becoming trophozoites that multiply roughly 6- to 20-fold each asexual cycle (every 48 h for P. falciparum/P. vivax/P. ovale, 72 h for P. malariae, 24 h for P. knowlesi); symptoms begin around densities of ~50 parasites/uL. In P. vivax and P. ovale a fraction of liver forms persist as dormant hypnozoites for weeks to a year or more, causing the relapses characteristic of these species (P. falciparum, P. malariae, and P. knowlesi do not relapse). Disease results from red-cell invasion and destruction plus the host inflammatory response: schizont rupture releases material that activates monocytes/macrophages and proinflammatory cytokines, producing fever. The central pathology of severe falciparum malaria is cytoadherence: ~12-15 h after invasion 'knobs' on the infected red cell extrude the variant adhesin PfEMP1, which binds endothelial receptors (ICAM-1 and endothelial protein C receptor in brain, VAR2CSA/chondroitin sulfate A in placenta, CD36 elsewhere). Cytoadherence, rosetting (adherence to uninfected red cells), and agglutination sequester mature parasites in vital organs, especially the brain, obstructing microcirculatory flow and metabolism while escaping splenic clearance. Uninfected red cells also become less deformable in severe disease. P. falciparum invades cells of all ages and can reach very high densities (P. knowlesi can also, owing to its 24-h cycle), whereas other species spare most red cells and rarely exceed ~2% parasitemia. The spleen augments clearance and 'pits' damaged ring forms; severe lactic acidosis arises from anaerobic glycolysis in sequestered beds, parasite lactate production, gut-derived acids, and failed hepatic/renal clearance, and hypoglycemia from impaired gluconeogenesis with increased glucose consumption. (Harrison 22e pp.1760-1765)","additionalDifferentials":["Acute viral illness / minor febrile viral syndrome (early nonspecific malaria mimics this) (Harrison 22e p.1763)","Bacterial meningitis (severe headache in malaria, but neck stiffness and photophobia are absent in malaria) (Harrison 22e p.1763)","Sepsis / bacteremia with incidental parasitemia, especially in high-transmission areas (Harrison 22e pp.1765, 1767)"],"infectionMimics":["Dengue fever (myalgia is prominent but usually more severe in dengue) (Harrison 22e p.1763)","Leptospirosis (muscle tenderness, which malaria lacks; petechial hemorrhages a feature) (Harrison 22e p.1763)","Typhus/rickettsial illness (tender muscles, unlike malaria) (Harrison 22e p.1763)","Viral hemorrhagic fevers (petechial hemorrhages, which are very rare in malaria) (Harrison 22e p.1763)","Nontyphoidal Salmonella and other bacteremia complicating or coinciding with malaria (Harrison 22e p.1765)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Diabetic ketoacidosis / ketoacidosis (acidotic 'respiratory distress' breathing overlaps with severe malaria acidosis; ketoacidosis can contribute in children) (Harrison 22e pp.1764-1766)","Hematologic malignancy / lymphoproliferative disorder (hyperreactive malarial splenomegaly with massive splenomegaly and pancytopenia can be mistaken for it) (Harrison 22e p.1766)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Thick blood film (concentrates parasites ~40-100x over thin film, increasing sensitivity; examine 100-200 fields before calling negative) (Harrison 22e pp.1766-1767)","Thin blood film for species identification and parasite quantification (parasitized cells per 1000 RBCs) (Harrison 22e pp.1766-1767)","Giemsa (pH 7.2) or other Romanowsky stain; acridine orange for rapid detection of low-level parasitemia (Harrison 22e p.1766)","Rapid diagnostic tests detecting PfHRP2, parasite LDH, or aldolase (note PfHRP2/3 gene-deletion false negatives and that RDTs do not quantify parasitemia) (Harrison 22e p.1767)","PCR / ultrasensitive PCR for species confirmation and detection of low-density/asymptomatic infection (reference setting; too sensitive for primary diagnosis in endemic areas) (Harrison 22e pp.1767-1768)","Plasma lactate and bicarbonate (best biochemical prognosticators in severe malaria) (Harrison 22e p.1764)","Coagulation studies (PT, aPTT, fibrinogen) and platelet count in severe disease (Harrison 22e pp.1764-1765, 1768)","Assessment of malaria pigment in neutrophils (>5% pigment-containing) and proportion of mature pigmented trophozoites/schizonts as severity/prognostic markers (Harrison 22e pp.1764, 1767)"],"redFlags":["Impaired consciousness, delirium, abnormal behavior, or unarousable coma (cerebral malaria) (Harrison 22e pp.1763-1764)","Generalized or repeated convulsions (>2 in 24 h), or subtle seizure activity such as tonic-clonic eye movements (Harrison 22e p.1764)","Acidotic deep ('respiratory distress') breathing, hyperlactatemia >=5 mmol/L, or acidemia (arterial pH <7.25) (Harrison 22e p.1764)","Hypoglycemia (plasma glucose <2.2 mmol/L, <40 mg/dL) (Harrison 22e p.1764)","Hypotension/shock with impaired perfusion (Harrison 22e p.1764)","Severe anemia (hematocrit <15% or Hb <50 g/L with high parasitemia; Hb <=3 g/dL on presentation carries increased mortality) (Harrison 22e pp.1764-1765)","Macroscopic hemoglobinuria / blackwater fever (black, brown, or red urine) (Harrison 22e pp.1764-1765)","Acute kidney injury (creatinine >265 umol/L, >3 mg/dL) or anuria (Harrison 22e pp.1764-1765)","Noncardiogenic pulmonary edema / ARDS or O2 saturation <92% with tachypnea (Harrison 22e p.1764)","Spontaneous bleeding / DIC (Harrison 22e p.1764)","Hyperparasitemia (>5% in nonimmune, >10% in any patient) or >100,000/uL (Harrison 22e pp.1763-1764)","Prostration / inability to sit or stand unaided (Harrison 22e p.1764)","Low core temperature <36.5C, marked agitation, jaundice (bilirubin >50 umol/L) with vital-organ dysfunction (Harrison 22e p.1764)"],"prognosis":"Promptly and appropriately treated uncomplicated falciparum malaria has a mortality of <0.1%, but risk rises steeply once vital-organ dysfunction occurs or parasitemia exceeds 2%. Cerebral malaria carries roughly 20% mortality in adults and 15% in children even with treatment; about 10% of surviving children (especially with hypoglycemia, severe anemia, repeated seizures, or deep coma) have residual neurologic deficits, most of which improve or resolve within 6 months, though persistent language and cognitive deficits, increased epilepsy risk, and reduced life expectancy occur in some. Noncardiogenic pulmonary edema in adults has >80% mortality. Plasma bicarbonate and lactate are the best biochemical prognosticators. Among complications, poor prognosis is indicated by >20% mature pigment-containing parasites or >5% of neutrophils containing pigment, and by hyperparasitemia. In severe acute renal failure that survives, urine flow returns in a median of 4 days and creatinine normalizes in a mean of 17 days; early dialysis improves survival. (Harrison 22e pp.1763-1765, 1767)","pitfalls":["Treating malaria as a clinical diagnosis; confirmation by smear or rapid test is required (Harrison 22e p.1766).","Accepting a normal platelet count without question, as mild thrombocytopenia is the rule in malaria (Harrison 22e pp.1765, 1768).","Relying on peripheral parasitemia to gauge true burden in falciparum malaria, since mature parasites are sequestered (Harrison 22e p.1762).","Overdiagnosing severe malaria in high-transmission areas where low-density parasitemia is a common incidental finding, missing concurrent sepsis (Harrison 22e pp.1765, 1767).","Over-vigorous IV fluid administration, which can precipitate noncardiogenic pulmonary edema (Harrison 22e p.1764).","Missing hypoglycemia because its usual autonomic signs are absent in severe disease and its neurologic effects mimic malaria itself (Harrison 22e p.1764).","Re-treating on the basis of persistent gametocytemia, which does not signify drug resistance after a full course (Harrison 22e p.1767).","False-negative RDTs from PfHRP2/3 gene-deleted parasites (notably in the Horn of Africa); RDTs also cannot quantify parasitemia (Harrison 22e p.1767).","Using serology to diagnose acute illness; serology has no role in acute diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1768).","Mistaking acidotic breathing in a severely anemic child for 'anemic congestive cardiac failure' (Harrison 22e pp.1765-1766)."],"severityClassification":"Severe falciparum malaria (WHO-based criteria, Harrison 22e Table 231-2, p.1764) is defined by one or more of: unarousable coma/cerebral malaria (no localizing response, coma >30 min after a generalized convulsion, Glasgow Coma Score <11 or Blantyre <3 in young children); acidemia/acidosis (arterial pH <7.25, base deficit >8 meq/L, or plasma bicarbonate <15 mmol/L; venous lactate >=5 mmol/L; deep labored 'respiratory distress' breathing); severe normochromic normocytic anemia (hematocrit <15% or Hb <50 g/L with parasite density >10,000/uL); renal failure (creatinine >265 umol/L / >3 mg/dL); noncardiogenic pulmonary edema/ARDS (or O2 sat <92% on room air with respiratory rate >30/min); hypoglycemia (glucose <2.2 mmol/L / <40 mg/dL); hypotension/shock (systolic BP <70 mmHg in children 1-5 y or <80 mmHg in adults with impaired perfusion); significant bleeding/DIC; convulsions (>2 generalized seizures in 24 h); hemoglobinuria (macroscopic black/brown/red urine, not due to oxidant drugs or G6PD deficiency); extreme weakness/prostration; hyperparasitemia (>5% in nonimmune, >10% in any patient); and jaundice (bilirubin >50 umol/L / >3 mg/dL) with parasitemia >100,000/uL or other vital-organ dysfunction. Note: criteria thresholds for renal failure are for adults; lower values define severe disease in children. (Harrison 22e p.1764)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1760","Harrison 22e p.1761","Harrison 22e p.1762","Harrison 22e p.1763","Harrison 22e p.1764","Harrison 22e p.1765","Harrison 22e p.1766","Harrison 22e p.1767","Harrison 22e p.1768"],"crossLinks":["MIXED_MALARIA","DENGUE","LEPTOSPIROSIS","SCRUB_TYPHUS","RICKETTSIAL_FEVER","SEPSIS","PUO"]},"MENINGITIS":{"name":"Acute Bacterial Meningitis","system":"Central Nervous System","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chapter 143 (Acute Meningitis), printed p.1118-1125; CSF comparison table 124-4, p.1005; coma/altered-mentation discussion p.[Ch.301]; SIADH causes table [Ch.49]","clinicalPearls":["The classic triad of fever, headache and nuchal rigidity is useful but each component occurs in over 80% of adult cases while the full triad is frequently incomplete, so its absence does not exclude bacterial meningitis (p.1119).","A reduced level of consciousness, ranging from lethargy to coma, is present in more than three-quarters of patients; nausea, vomiting and photophobia are also common (p.1119).","Nuchal rigidity is the characteristic sign of meningeal irritation (neck resists passive flexion), but Kernig's and Brudzinski's signs have uncertain sensitivity/specificity and may be absent in the very young, the elderly, the immunocompromised or those with depressed mental status (p.1119-1120).","Coexisting cervical spine disease in older patients can produce false-positive neck stiffness (p.1120).","Whenever bacterial meningitis is suspected, draw blood cultures immediately and start empirical antibiotics plus adjunctive dexamethasone without delay; if neuroimaging is needed before LP, give antibiotics after blood cultures rather than waiting (p.1120).","Antibiotics given a few hours before LP do not meaningfully change CSF WBC count or glucose and are unlikely to prevent Gram-stain visualization or PCR detection of bacterial nucleic acid, so a delayed LP after starting therapy still has diagnostic value (p.1120).","Dexamethasone should ideally be given about 20 minutes before, or at the latest with, the first antibiotic dose, because steroids blunt macrophage/microglial TNF-alpha production only if given before those cells are activated by endotoxin; benefit is unlikely if started more than 6 hours after antibiotics (p.1120, p.1123).","Petechial or purpuric skin lesions are an important clue to meningococcal infection and, if present, should be biopsied (Gram stain of the lesion may reveal the organism) (p.1119, p.1121).","Profound depression of consciousness (stupor/coma), seizures or focal neurologic signs point away from uncomplicated viral meningitis and suggest encephalitis or bacterial/parameningeal disease (p.1123)."],"pathophysiology":"Bacterial meningitis is an acute purulent infection of the subarachnoid space accompanied by a CNS inflammatory reaction that can cause decreased consciousness, seizures, raised intracranial pressure and stroke; meninges, subarachnoid space and brain parenchyma are often jointly involved (meningoencephalitis). The leading organisms (S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis) first colonize the nasopharynx, cross or breach the epithelium into the bloodstream, evade neutrophil phagocytosis and complement killing via their polysaccharide capsule, and reach the CSF through the choroid plexus or cerebral capillary endothelium. They multiply readily in CSF because normal CSF has few leukocytes and little complement/immunoglobulin, so opsonization and phagocytosis are poor. Much of the neurologic injury comes from the host inflammatory response rather than direct bacterial damage, so injury can progress even after antibiotics sterilize the CSF. Lysis of bacteria releases cell-wall components (gram-negative LPS; pneumococcal teichoic acid and peptidoglycan) that drive production of TNF-alpha, IL-1beta, chemokines and other mediators by microglia, astrocytes, monocytes, endothelium and CSF leukocytes. These cytokines increase blood-brain barrier permeability (vasogenic edema and protein leakage), the proteinaceous/leukocytic exudate obstructs CSF flow and resorption (obstructive and communicating hydrocephalus, interstitial edema), and neutrophil degranulation plus reactive oxygen/nitrogen species and excitatory amino acids produce cytotoxic edema and cell death (notably in the hippocampal dentate gyrus). Cerebral blood flow rises early then falls with loss of autoregulation; vasculitis and thrombosis of arteries, cortical veins and dural sinuses cause ischemia/infarction. The combined interstitial, vasogenic and cytotoxic edema raises ICP and can lead to coma and herniation (p.1118-1120).","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex virus encephalitis (headache, fever, altered consciousness, focal deficits, seizures; distinguished by CSF profile, MRI temporal-lobe changes and HSV CSF PCR) (p.1121)","Subacute meningitis presenting acutely: M. tuberculosis, Cryptococcus neoformans, Histoplasma, Coccidioides, Treponema pallidum (p.1122)","Mycoplasma pneumoniae CNS infection, which can mimic viral meningitis/encephalitis (p.1124)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral (aseptic) meningoencephalitis, especially enteroviral and HSV (p.1121)","Rocky Mountain spotted fever (Rickettsia rickettsii) with early maculopapular rash that can resemble meningococcemia (p.1121)","Ehrlichiosis/anaplasmosis (tick-borne) with fever, headache, confusion and sometimes rash (p.1121)","Focal suppurative CNS infections: subdural empyema, epidural empyema, brain abscess (consider when focal neurologic findings present) (p.1121)","Fungal, tuberculous, Listeria, Brucella, Coxiella, Leptospira meningitis (lymphocytic pleocytosis, hypoglycorrhachia may be absent early) (p.1124)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Subarachnoid hemorrhage (the major non-infectious consideration) (p.1121)","Medication-induced hypersensitivity (drug-induced) meningitis (p.1121)","Chemical meningitis from rupture of tumor contents into CSF (cystic glioma, craniopharyngioma, epidermoid/dermoid cyst) (p.1121)","Carcinomatous or lymphomatous (neoplastic) meningitis (p.1121)","Inflammatory disorders: sarcoidosis, systemic lupus erythematosus, Behcet's syndrome (p.1121)","Pituitary apoplexy (p.1122)","Uveomeningitic syndromes (Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada) (p.1122)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CSF opening pressure measurement: raised (>180 mmH2O) in over 90% of cases, >400 mmH2O in about 20% (p.1120)","CSF cell count and differential: PMN pleocytosis, >100 cells/uL in ~90% (p.1120)","CSF glucose and CSF/serum glucose ratio: low glucose (<40 mg/dL) and ratio <0.4 are highly suggestive (ratio also low in fungal/TB/carcinomatous meningitis) (p.1120)","CSF protein: elevated (>45 mg/dL) in ~90% (p.1120)","CSF Gram stain (organisms in >60%) and CSF culture (positive in >70-80%; required for susceptibility testing) (p.1120-1121)","CSF multiplex PCR (e.g., FilmArray ME panel) for rapid detection of S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, E. coli, L. monocytogenes, H. influenzae, group B strep; does not include S. aureus, coagulase-negative staphylococci or many gram-negatives and cannot replace culture (p.1120-1121)","MRI (preferred over CT) for cerebral edema/ischemia; gadolinium meningeal enhancement is non-specific and not diagnostic of meningitis (p.1121)","Skin lesion biopsy with Gram stain when petechiae/purpura are present (p.1121)","Serum procalcitonin and CSF protein as adjuncts to distinguish bacterial from aseptic meningitis (children) (p.1124)"],"redFlags":["Petechial or purpuric rash, which may herald fulminant meningococcal disease that can progress to death within hours of symptom onset (p.1119)","Signs of raised ICP: deteriorating consciousness, papilledema, dilated poorly reactive pupils, sixth-nerve palsy, decerebrate posturing, Cushing reflex (bradycardia, hypertension, irregular respirations) (p.1120)","Seizures at presentation or within the first 24 hours (associated with worse outcome) (p.1120, p.1123)","Coma or markedly decreased level of consciousness on admission (p.1123)","Indications to obtain CT/MRI before LP: recent head trauma, depressed/abnormal consciousness, papilledema, or focal neurologic deficit (do not delay antibiotics for imaging) (p.1120)"],"prognosis":"Case-fatality varies by organism: roughly 3-7% for H. influenzae, N. meningitidis or group B streptococcal meningitis, about 15% for Listeria, and around 20% for pneumococcal meningitis. Risk of death rises with decreased consciousness on admission, seizures within 24 h, signs of raised ICP, age (infancy or >50 years), comorbidities such as shock or need for mechanical ventilation, and delay in starting treatment. A markedly low CSF glucose (<40 mg/dL) and very high CSF protein (>300 mg/dL) predict worse outcomes in some series. About 25% of survivors have moderate-to-severe sequelae, commonly decreased intellectual function, memory impairment, seizures, sensorineural hearing loss with dizziness, and gait disturbance (p.1123).","pitfalls":["Relying on the complete classic triad or on Kernig's/Brudzinski's signs to rule out meningitis; these are insensitive, particularly in elderly, very young or immunocompromised patients (p.1119-1120).","Delaying antibiotics and dexamethasone to obtain neuroimaging or LP first; treatment should not wait when bacterial meningitis is suspected (p.1120).","Assuming a recent dose of antibiotics renders the LP useless; CSF chemistry, Gram stain and PCR usually remain informative for several hours (p.1120).","Interpreting gadolinium meningeal enhancement on MRI as diagnostic of meningitis; it occurs with any condition that increases blood-brain barrier permeability (p.1121).","Treating with dexamethasone in settings where it has not shown benefit: trials in low-income countries (late presentation, prior antibiotics, malnutrition, HIV, unproven diagnosis) failed to show efficacy, and patients with a negative CSF Gram stain and culture in those settings should not receive it (p.1123).","Forgetting that neurologic injury and complications can progress even after CSF sterilization because much of the damage is immune-mediated (p.1118).","Overlooking that a giving of IV glucose (e.g., D50) shortly before LP rarely alters CSF glucose unless several hours have elapsed, so a low CSF glucose remains meaningful (p.1120)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison 22e does not provide a formal graded severity score for acute bacterial meningitis; it frames the disease as a medical emergency (goal: antibiotics within 60 min of arrival) and lists adverse prognostic markers (decreased consciousness on admission, seizures within 24 h, signs of raised ICP, age extremes, comorbidities such as shock/ventilation, treatment delay, very low CSF glucose, very high CSF protein). For distinguishing bacterial from aseptic meningitis in children, the prospectively validated Bacterial Meningitis Score is cited: very low probability (NPV ~99.7%) when all of negative CSF Gram stain, CSF neutrophils <1000/uL, CSF protein <80 mg/dL, peripheral ANC <10,000/uL, and no seizures are met (p.1123-1124).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1118","Harrison 22e p.1119","Harrison 22e p.1120","Harrison 22e p.1121","Harrison 22e p.1122","Harrison 22e p.1123","Harrison 22e p.1124","Harrison 22e p.1005 (Table 124-4, CSF profiles)"],"crossLinks":["VIRAL_MENINGITIS","ENCEPHALITIS","BRAIN_ABSCESS","CNS_TB"]},"MIXED_MALARIA":{"name":"Mixed Malaria (P. falciparum + P. vivax)","system":"Tropical Fever","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chapter 231 (Malaria), printed pp. 1760-1768","clinicalPearls":["Mixed infections (most commonly P. falciparum together with P. vivax) are frequently under-detected by light microscopy and rapid antigen tests; PCR is the most sensitive method and is increasingly used specifically for speciation and genotyping in mixed infections (p.1767).","Because microscopy and RDTs may detect only the dominant species, a smear or RDT reported as single-species (especially P. falciparum) does not exclude a coexisting vivax/ovale component carrying dormant liver-stage hypnozoites.","P. vivax + P. falciparum is recognized as a distinct co-circulating pattern in endemic mapping, with roughly equal prevalence of the two species in Oceania (p.1761-1762).","Standard combination RDTs typically carry one P. falciparum-specific band plus a pan-malaria or P. vivax band; this design distinguishes falciparum from the 'less dangerous' malarias but is not optimized to flag concurrent dual infection (p.1766).","RDTs may miss low-level P. vivax (and P. ovale/P. malariae) parasitemia and PfHRP2-based tests can be falsely negative where parasites carry HRP2/3 gene deletions — both failure modes can mask one component of a mixed infection (p.1766).","The classic regularly-spaced febrile paroxysm at presentation suggests P. vivax or P. ovale (often relapse); its presence in a patient with confirmed falciparum should raise suspicion of a coexisting vivax/ovale component.","Apparent vivax/ovale relapses arising after treatment of a mixed infection reflect persistence of liver-stage hypnozoites, which are not cleared by standard blood-stage schizonticides."],"pathophysiology":"Sporozoites inoculated by anopheline mosquitoes reach the liver and undergo pre-erythrocytic schizogony; a single sporozoite yields thousands of merozoites that invade RBCs and multiply (every 48 h for P. falciparum and P. vivax). In P. vivax (and P. ovale) infection, a fraction of intrahepatic forms do not divide but persist as dormant hypnozoites for weeks to a year or more, later reactivating to cause relapses — a feature absent in P. falciparum. In a mixed P. falciparum + P. vivax infection, the falciparum component drives the risk of severe disease through cytoadherence (PfEMP1 binding endothelial receptors), rosetting, and agglutination, causing sequestration of mature parasites in vital organs; only ring forms circulate, so peripheral parasitemia underestimates true burden. P. vivax preferentially invades young reticulocytes and produces lower parasitemia (seldom >2%), while P. falciparum invades RBCs of all ages and can reach very high densities. The simultaneous presence of a vivax hypnozoite reservoir means blood-stage cure of both species does not prevent later vivax relapse (p.1760-1763).","additionalDifferentials":["Single-species P. falciparum malaria with an undetected coexisting vivax/ovale component (the central diagnostic trap of mixed infection)","P. knowlesi infection, which on RDT/microscopy may be misidentified as P. falciparum and can reach dangerously high densities (24-h cycle)","Recrudescent falciparum vs. true vivax/ovale relapse vs. reinfection as the cause of recurrent fever after treatment"],"infectionMimics":["Dengue (myalgia typically more severe than in malaria)","Leptospirosis (tender muscles, unlike malaria)","Typhus","Viral hemorrhagic fevers (petechiae/rash, which are very rare in malaria)","Bacterial sepsis with incidental parasitemia (concomitant bacteremia coexists with severe malaria and is hard to distinguish, especially in children)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thick and thin Giemsa-stained blood films examined immediately for parasite demonstration AND species identification; examine 100-200 fields before calling a thick film negative","PCR amplification for definitive speciation and genotyping when mixed infection is suspected (more sensitive than microscopy/RDT for detecting and distinguishing species)","Combination RDT carrying a P. falciparum-specific antigen plus a pan-malaria or P. vivax band","Quantification of parasitemia (RDTs do not quantify) and assessment of parasite maturity/pigment for prognostic stratification","G6PD status (knowledge basis for safe hypnozoite-directed therapy of the vivax component) — testing rationale only"],"redFlags":["Coma, obtundation, delirium, or abnormal behavior (cerebral malaria) — attributable to the falciparum component","Repeated or generalized convulsions (>2 in 24 h)","Respiratory distress / acidotic deep breathing (acidosis, lactate >=5 mmol/L)","Hypoglycemia (<2.2 mmol/L)","Shock / hypotension with impaired perfusion","Severe normocytic anemia (Hct <15%) and/or spontaneous bleeding / DIC","Hemoglobinuria (black/brown/red urine)","Acute kidney injury (creatinine >265 umol/L)","Hyperparasitemia (>2% indicates increased risk; >5-10% defines severe disease) — a falciparum-driven marker","Prostration / inability to sit or stand unaided"],"prognosis":"Promptly treated uncomplicated falciparum malaria has a mortality of <0.1%, but risk rises steeply once vital-organ dysfunction occurs or parasitemia exceeds ~2%. In a mixed infection the falciparum component dictates the severe-disease prognosis; cerebral malaria carries ~20% mortality in adults and ~15% in children even with treatment, and ~10% of child survivors have residual neurologic deficits (most improving within 6 months). Poor-prognosis features include hyperparasitemia (>100,000/uL), predominance of mature pigment-containing parasites (>20%), pigment in >5% of neutrophils, hypoglycemia, hyperlactatemia, acidemia, and coagulopathy. The vivax component does not usually drive mortality but, if its hypnozoite reservoir is not addressed, predisposes to later relapse and recurrent anemia (p.1763-1767).","pitfalls":["Treating an apparently single-species falciparum infection as if vivax were absent: the coexisting vivax hypnozoite reservoir is not cleared by blood-stage drugs and later causes relapse — manage mixed infection as falciparum AND provide hypnozoite-directed coverage for vivax.","Relying on microscopy or RDT alone to exclude a second species — both under-detect the minor component; PCR is needed to confirm/exclude mixed infection.","Misreading PfHRP2 RDT positivity that persists for weeks after a prior infection as evidence of the current species mix.","Mistaking persistent P. falciparum gametocytemia after treatment for treatment failure or active infection.","Attributing all post-treatment fever to falciparum recrudescence when it may be a true vivax/ovale relapse from the mixed-infection liver reservoir.","Misidentifying P. knowlesi as P. falciparum on microscopy/RDT, distorting the apparent species picture."],"severityClassification":"Severity is governed by the P. falciparum component per Harrison Table 231-2 (Manifestations of Severe Falciparum Malaria): major criteria include unarousable coma/cerebral malaria, acidosis (arterial pH <7.25 / lactate >=5 mmol/L), severe normocytic anemia (Hct <15%), renal failure (creatinine >265 umol/L), noncardiogenic pulmonary edema/ARDS, hypoglycemia (<2.2 mmol/L), hypotension/shock, bleeding/DIC, and repeated convulsions; other criteria include hemoglobinuria, prostration, and hyperparasitemia (>5% nonimmune, >10% any patient). Uncomplicated disease = patient can sit/stand unaided and swallow, with no vital-organ dysfunction and parasitemia <2% (p.1763-1764).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1760","Harrison 22e p.1761","Harrison 22e p.1762","Harrison 22e p.1763","Harrison 22e p.1764","Harrison 22e p.1766","Harrison 22e p.1767"],"crossLinks":["MALARIA"]},"NECROTIZING_FASCIITIS":{"name":"Necrotizing Fasciitis","system":"Skin & Soft Tissue","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1036-1037, p.1093-1097, p.1254-1255","clinicalPearls":["Pain out of proportion to physical findings is the classic early clue: physical signs may be minimal (only soft-tissue edema and mild erythema) while pain and fever are severe; this disparity helps distinguish NF from superficial cellulitis, where skin appearance is more abnormal but pain is only mild-to-moderate.","Early disease is frequently misdiagnosed (e.g., as thrombophlebitis) and patients may be sent home with analgesics, especially in the strep type that begins deep at a site of non-penetrating minor trauma (bruise, muscle strain) with no visible portal of entry.","Classic late signs evolve sequentially: swelling and brawny edema/tenderness, then dark-red (violaceous) induration, then bullae filled with blue/purple fluid, then friable bluish-maroon-to-black skin; thrombosis of dermal-papillae vessels is extensive by this stage.","Paradoxical decrease in pain or progression to anesthesia over the involved area is an ominous sign reflecting thrombosis of small vessels and infarction of cutaneous nerves.","GAS NF can be preceded by varicella, childbirth, or muscle strain, and most patients deny an antecedent streptococcal infection.","The inoculation site may be inapparent and remote from clinical involvement (e.g., minor hand trauma manifesting as infection over the shoulder or chest).","Myositis/myonecrosis coexists with NF in roughly 20-50% of cases and serum creatine phosphokinase (CPK) may be markedly elevated, signaling deeper muscle involvement."],"pathophysiology":"NF is infection of the superficial and/or deep fascia investing the muscles of an extremity or trunk, with extensive necrosis of subcutaneous tissue and fascia. Infection spreads rapidly along fascial planes and via venous channels and lymphatics, typically sparing the muscle itself in pure fasciitis. Two main microbiologic patterns: a mixed aerobic-anaerobic (type I) process beginning with a breach of a mucosal barrier (GI/GU tract), and a monomicrobial group A streptococcal (type II) process. In GAS disease, organisms reach deep fascia either by transient bacteremic seeding at a site of minor non-penetrating trauma (no portal) or by spread from cutaneous infection/penetrating trauma. Thrombosis of dermal vessels produces progressive skin necrosis; infarction of cutaneous nerves causes the characteristic late anesthesia. NF caused by Clostridium perfringens or extending into muscle (clostridial/streptococcal myonecrosis) carries very high systemic toxicity with hemolysis. PVL-producing MRSA strains have been reported as a cause.","additionalDifferentials":["Clostridial myonecrosis / gas gangrene (distinguished from clostridial NF by muscle biopsy)","Streptococcal necrotizing myositis (GAS infection extending into muscle)","Pyomyositis (usually S. aureus; localized, shock uncommon unless toxin-producing)","Synergistic nonclostridial anaerobic myonecrosis (necrotizing cutaneous myositis / synergistic necrotizing cellulitis) — a mixed aerobic-anaerobic variant","Cellulitis / erysipelas (superficial; skin more abnormal but pain mild-moderate)","Halophilic Vibrio vulnificus infection (can be as fulminant as NF; Gulf/Atlantic water exposure or raw seafood in cirrhosis)","Aeromonas soft-tissue infection (freshwater laceration exposure)"],"infectionMimics":["Aggressive cellulitis from Vibrio vulnificus or Aeromonas hydrophila","Ecthyma gangrenosum (Pseudomonas, neutropenic hosts)","Clostridial myonecrosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Thrombophlebitis (a common early misdiagnosis)","Muscle strain or contusion (the minor trauma at the deep nidus)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Soft-tissue plain radiography, CT, and MRI to assess depth of infection and detect tissue gas or a localized abscess — most useful when lesions progress rapidly or SIRS is present; note imaging may show only soft-tissue swelling and is NOT specific for fulminant GAS NF/myonecrosis, where gas is typically absent","Gram stain and aerobic/anaerobic culture of surgically excised tissue to identify GAS vs mixed flora vs MRSA vs Clostridium","Frozen-section histology of aspirate or punch biopsy (valuable in NF), though aspiration/biopsy is negative in ~80% of cases","Serum CPK (markedly elevated when myonecrosis coexists)","Lactate, d-dimer, coagulation, liver and renal function as markers of systemic toxicity/organ failure"],"redFlags":["Pain out of proportion to examination findings","Rapidly progressing lesion with signs of systemic inflammatory response / toxicity","Violaceous (blue/purple) bullae, skin sloughing, dusky/mottled erythema","Progression of tenderness to anesthesia over the lesion (cutaneous nerve infarction)","Crepitus or gas in deep tissue (suggests mixed/clostridial infection)","Renal impairment that may precede shock, plus hypotension/multiorgan failure","Urgent surgical exploration supersedes other diagnostic or therapeutic maneuvers — debridement is life-saving and must not be delayed"],"prognosis":"Mortality for NF is roughly 11-34% overall and rises substantially when associated with toxic shock syndrome. GAS/clostridial fascial-muscle infection historically carried 20-50% mortality with penicillin alone. Survivors of GAS NF often require repeated surgeries including amputation. Clostridial NF/myonecrosis is far more lethal: spontaneous (nontraumatic) myonecrosis mortality exceeds 65% (often C. septicum with underlying malignancy); trunk infection ~63% vs limb ~12% mortality. Any delay in surgery increases the risk of death; early aggressive surgery markedly improves outcomes.","pitfalls":["Early physical findings are minimal relative to pain/fever, leading to underestimation of severity and misdiagnosis as thrombophlebitis or simple cellulitis.","Absence of gas does NOT exclude NF: gas is usually absent when the cause is GAS or MRSA and is seen mainly with mixed/clostridial infection.","Normal or nonspecific imaging does NOT rule out NF — CT/MRI may show only soft-tissue swelling and are not specific for fulminant GAS NF/myonecrosis.","A negative aspirate or frozen-section biopsy does NOT exclude NF (negative in ~80% of cases).","Decreasing pain should not be reassuring — it can signal nerve infarction and disease progression rather than improvement.","Do not wait for imaging or cultures when NF is suspected with systemic toxicity; surgical exploration is both diagnostic and therapeutic."],"severityClassification":"Microbiologic/clinical typing: Type I = mixed aerobic-anaerobic, begins at a mucosal-barrier breach (GI/GU), common in diabetes, peripheral vascular disease, malignancy, surgery, or abdominal penetrating injury; Fournier gangrene is the perineal/scrotal form. Type II = monomicrobial group A streptococcal (GAS implicated in ~60% of NF cases), can occur in otherwise healthy hosts, two presentations (with vs without a defined portal of entry). PVL-producing MRSA is a recognized monomicrobial cause. NF qualifies as soft-tissue necrosis under the CDC streptococcal toxic shock syndrome case definition (hypotension plus >=2 of: renal impairment, coagulopathy, hepatic impairment, ARDS, generalized erythematous desquamating rash, soft-tissue necrosis/NF/myositis/gangrene). (LRINEC score not described in this source.)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1036","Harrison 22e p.1037","Harrison 22e p.1093","Harrison 22e p.1095","Harrison 22e p.1096","Harrison 22e p.1254","Harrison 22e p.1255"],"crossLinks":["CELLULITIS","ERYSIPELAS"]},"PHARYNGITIS":{"name":"Acute Pharyngitis","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"300-302","clinicalPearls":["Sore throat is not synonymous with pharyngitis: it may also arise from submandibular/retropharyngeal/peritonsillar space infections, thyroiditis, gastroesophageal reflux, tumors, or postnasal drainage (Harrison 22e p.300).","Pharyngeal erythema is most commonly seen with viral infection (common cold, influenza), not bacterial disease (Harrison 22e p.300).","Pharyngeal exudate can be mimicked by Candida (cottage-cheese appearance, scrapes off leaving a bleeding surface) or leukoplakia (cannot be scraped off) — do not equate exudate with bacterial pharyngitis (Harrison 22e p.300).","GAS is the only common cause of sore throat that warrants antibiotics; most non-GAS pharyngitis is viral and self-limited (Harrison 22e p.300).","About 10% of adults with sore throat have GAS; in children the prevalence may reach ~35%, peaking at ages 5-15 years, and is higher in winter and early spring (Harrison 22e p.300).","Risk of GAS pharyngitis is elevated in health care and child care workers, teachers, parents of young children, and contacts of known strep cases; clinicians should watch for local outbreaks in military and institutional settings (Harrison 22e p.300).","GAS carriage is common (up to ~20% of schoolchildren, ~5% of adolescents/young adults); rapid antigen tests and culture cannot distinguish true infection from carriage (Harrison 22e p.301).","In adults meeting zero or one Centor criterion, a positive GAS test is very likely a false positive and/or represents carriage; indiscriminate testing of adults with sore throat is discouraged (Harrison 22e p.301).","Lower the threshold to test/treat GAS in patients with a history of acute rheumatic fever, documented strep exposure in the past week, those in a community strep epidemic, and diabetic or immunocompromised patients (Harrison 22e p.301).","In children the Centor criteria are less specific, so GAS pharyngitis should be confirmed by testing; children with pharyngitis but no viral signs (conjunctivitis, runny nose, cough, hoarseness, nonexudative oral lesions) warrant testing (Harrison 22e p.301).","In developed countries the prevalence of rheumatic fever is extremely low, and antibiotic treatment does not prevent poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis (Harrison 22e p.300).","Full-blown infectious mononucleosis (EBV) appears in only about one-fourth of cases and is a triad of clinical, hematologic, and serologic findings; new EBV causes 1-6% of pharyngitis in young adults and is rare over age 40 (Harrison 22e p.301).","Heterophil antibody is detectable in only ~40% of mononucleosis patients in the first week but in 80-90% by the third week, so an early negative test does not exclude EBV (Harrison 22e p.302).","Gonococcal pharyngitis is more often asymptomatic than symptomatic; suspect it with persistent sore throat unresponsive to presumptive strep treatment and relevant sexual exposure (Harrison 22e p.302).","Diphtheria produces only mild pharyngitis beneath its characteristic grayish pseudomembrane (Harrison 22e p.302).","Acute HIV infection causes sore throat in roughly half of cases; consider it when sore throat accompanies headache, fever, myalgias, lymphadenopathy, anorexia, and rash (Harrison 22e p.302)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute pharyngitis (symptoms generally present for days) is most often caused by respiratory viruses; group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (GAS, Streptococcus pyogenes) are a frequent bacterial cause, with other contributors including Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Epstein-Barr virus, HIV, non-group-A streptococci (groups C and G), Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydia pneumoniae, and Haemophilus influenzae. Complications of GAS pharyngitis arise from both suppurative spread (e.g., peritonsillar abscess, sinusitis, other invasive GAS infection) and nonsuppurative immune-mediated sequelae (acute rheumatic fever, poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis, scarlet fever) (Harrison 22e p.300-301).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute infectious mononucleosis (EBV) — marked cervical adenopathy, exudative pharyngitis, palatine petechiae, gelatinous uvula, splenomegaly (Harrison 22e p.301-302)","Non-group-A streptococcal pharyngitis (group C and group G streptococci) (Harrison 22e p.302)","Gonococcal pharyngitis (Neisseria gonorrhoeae) (Harrison 22e p.302)","Mycoplasma pneumoniae pharyngitis (persistent sore throat with bronchopulmonary symptoms) (Harrison 22e p.300)","Chlamydia pneumoniae pharyngitis (Harrison 22e p.302)","Haemophilus influenzae pharyngitis (cough and otitis media) (Harrison 22e p.300)","Arcanobacterium haemolyticum (scarlatiniform rash) (Harrison 22e p.300)","Acute HIV infection (Harrison 22e p.302)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute epiglottitis/supraglottitis — sore throat with dysphagia, odynophagia, hoarseness, stridor or tachypnea, drooling; airway emergency (Harrison 22e p.302)","Peritonsillar abscess — fever, dysphagia, profound throat pain with drooling, trismus, 'hot potato voice', unilateral palate bulge and uvular deviation (Harrison 22e p.302)","Retropharyngeal abscess — post-URI in children with sore throat, dysphagia, deep neck pain, neck stiffness, trismus, drooling; severe cases with dyspnea/stridor (Harrison 22e p.302)","Submandibular abscess / Ludwig angina — swelling of the floor of the mouth displacing the tongue posteriorly, usually after dental infection or extraction (Harrison 22e p.302)","Lemierre syndrome — septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein with metastatic infection (Harrison 22e p.302)","Candida pharyngitis — cottage-cheese exudate that scrapes off leaving a bleeding surface (Harrison 22e p.300)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Gastroesophageal reflux / laryngopharyngeal reflux (Harrison 22e p.300)","Postnasal drainage (Harrison 22e p.300)","Head and neck tumors (Harrison 22e p.300)","Thyroiditis (Harrison 22e p.300)","Leukoplakia mimicking exudate (cannot be scraped off) (Harrison 22e p.300)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Rapid GAS antigen test: sensitivity ~80%, specificity ~95%; proper technique (swabbing tonsils/pharynx and any exudate or ulceration) is essential to sensitivity (Harrison 22e p.301)","Throat culture: single-swab sensitivity ~85-90%; can be falsely positive in carriers; not recommended for routine adult evaluation given 24-48 h delay (Harrison 22e p.301)","Heterophil antibody test for EBV mononucleosis (low early sensitivity, rises by week 3) (Harrison 22e p.302)","CBC for atypical lymphocytosis in mononucleosis (absolute lymphocytes >4000/uL or relative >50% with >10% atypical morphology) (Harrison 22e p.302)","CT imaging for suspected head and neck abscess (except obvious peritonsillar abscess) (Harrison 22e p.302)","Lateral neck radiograph in epiglottitis may show the 'thumb sign' (not required for diagnosis) (Harrison 22e p.302)"],"redFlags":["Shaking chills/rigors, neck swelling beyond lymphadenopathy, trouble swallowing, drooling, or symptoms persisting >5 days without improvement should prompt urgent re-evaluation (Harrison 22e p.301)","Stridor, dyspnea, hoarseness, or tripod/forward-leaning posture with drooling — suggests epiglottitis and impending airway obstruction (Harrison 22e p.302)","Non-GAS pharyngitis that is not resolving in a teenager/young adult with rigors, neck pain or swelling, or extrapharyngeal symptoms — suspect Lemierre syndrome (Harrison 22e p.302)","Trismus, 'hot potato voice', and unilateral palate bulge with uvular deviation — peritonsillar abscess (Harrison 22e p.302)","Grayish pharyngeal pseudomembrane in a traveler from an endemic area — suspect diphtheria (Harrison 22e p.300, 302)"],"prognosis":"Both streptococcal and nonstreptococcal pharyngitis should resolve within 3-5 days. Nonsuppurative complications are rare, and even streptococcal pharyngitis is self-limited in the vast majority of cases; outside the U.S., because complications are rare, some guidelines do not recommend routine GAS testing or antibiotic treatment of sore throat (Harrison 22e p.301).","pitfalls":["Prescribing antibiotics to the majority of adults with sore throat despite only ~10% having GAS — most cases are viral (Harrison 22e p.300).","Testing adults who meet zero or one Centor criterion: 40-60% of adults with pharyngitis fall here, and a positive test is highly likely to be false-positive or carriage (Harrison 22e p.301).","Relying on throat culture or rapid test to distinguish active infection from GAS carriage — neither can (Harrison 22e p.301).","Applying adult Centor criteria to children, in whom they are less specific (Harrison 22e p.301).","Mistaking Candida or leukoplakia for bacterial pharyngeal exudate (Harrison 22e p.300).","Attempting to examine or swab the posterior pharynx in suspected epiglottitis — this can provoke laryngospasm and should only be done in a controlled setting (Harrison 22e p.302).","Assuming antibiotic treatment of GAS prevents poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis — it does not (Harrison 22e p.300).","A negative early heterophil antibody test does not rule out EBV mononucleosis (Harrison 22e p.302)."],"severityClassification":"Centor criteria (4 findings, 1 point each): history of fever, absence of cough, tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy, and tonsillar exudate or swelling. For an adult pretest probability of ~10%, post-evaluation probability of GAS is ~2% at 0 points, ~3% at 1, ~8% at 2, ~19% at 3, and ~41% at 4 points (area under the curve 0.79). Conceptually: 0-1 criteria -> neither test nor treat; 2-3 -> rapid antigen test to guide management; 4 -> rapid test or empirical treatment. (Harrison 22e p.300-301).","references":["Harrison 22e p.300","Harrison 22e p.301","Harrison 22e p.302"],"crossLinks":["URTI","SINUSITIS","glomerulonephritis"]},"PROSTATITIS":{"name":"Acute Bacterial Prostatitis","system":"Genitourinary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1087, 1089, 1132, 1136, 1186","clinicalPearls":["Acute bacterial prostatitis (ABP) typically presents as a severe illness with dysuria, urinary frequency, and pain in the prostatic, pelvic, or perineal region, and may be accompanied by fever, rigors, and/or bladder outlet obstruction (Harrison 22e p.1089).","The prostate is involved in the majority of febrile UTI cases in men; ABP should be specifically suspected when a man presents with UTI plus systemic symptoms but no other clear anatomic source such as pyelonephritis (Harrison 22e p.1089, p.1136).","Bacteriuria documented on urine culture is what distinguishes the less common bacterial prostatitis syndromes (acute and chronic) from the far more common chronic pelvic pain syndrome, which is NOT associated with bacteriuria and is NOT improved by antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Men with febrile UTI may show an elevated serum prostate-specific antigen (PSA) and an enlarged prostate and seminal vesicles on ultrasound, supporting prostatic involvement; PSA is therefore not interpretable as a cancer marker during acute infection (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Prostatitis is largely unrepresented in UTI clinical trial data, so complicated-UTI practice guidelines cannot be assumed to apply to prostatitis; older literature suggests prolonged therapy with prostate-penetrating agents may be needed (Harrison 22e p.1087)."],"pathophysiology":"Prostatitis can complicate ascending cystitis or arise hematogenously, for example as a metastatic focus of Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia. Infectious prostatitis is almost always bacterial and is far less common than the noninfectious chronic pelvic pain syndrome. In general, most genitourinary infections in men ascend from the urethra to the bladder, with the prostate becoming involved as part of febrile UTI; less than 2% of UTIs are hematogenous, usually from virulent organisms such as S. aureus and Salmonella. Obstruction from prostatic hypertrophy after age 50 promotes urinary stasis and is a major reason UTI incidence in older men approaches that in women (Harrison 22e p.1087, p.1089, p.1131).","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic bacterial prostatitis (CBP) — presents insidiously as recurrent episodes of cystitis, sometimes with pelvic and perineal pain, usually without systemic signs of infection; should be suspected with recurrent UTI not in the context of catheterization (Harrison 22e p.1089, p.1136).","Pyelonephritis — another febrile genitourinary infection that must be excluded as the anatomic source before attributing systemic symptoms to ABP (Harrison 22e p.1136).","Acute cystitis in men — uncommon and warrants caution; urine culture should be obtained in all male UTI to help identify a prostatic focus (Harrison 22e p.1089)."],"infectionMimics":["Pyelonephritis — overlapping febrile UTI presentation; distinguished by flank/costovertebral angle pain and an upper-tract source (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Urethritis — urethral pain, pruritus, or discharge should prompt evaluation for sexually transmitted infections (Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Chlamydia trachomatis, Mycoplasma genitalium) rather than assuming prostatitis (Harrison 22e p.1087)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chronic pelvic pain syndrome (formerly chronic prostatitis) — a noninfectious disorder that is the far more common cause of prostatic-type symptoms, with NO bacteriuria and NO response to antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Benign prostatic hypertrophy with obstructive uropathy / urinary retention — can produce lower urinary tract symptoms and predispose to UTI (Harrison 22e p.1089, p.1131)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine culture in all male UTI — documenting bacteriuria differentiates bacterial prostatitis from chronic pelvic pain syndrome; dipstick and/or microscopy alone are not sufficient (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Cross-sectional imaging (CT or ultrasound) for men with febrile UTI, including the first episode, if not previously done, to evaluate for urinary tract abnormalities or obstruction (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Evaluation for urinary retention / prostatic focus in men with recurrent cystitis (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Urologic consultation referral if the diagnosis of UTI is unclear or UTI is recurrent (Harrison 22e p.1089)."],"redFlags":["Bladder outlet obstruction or urinary retention accompanying ABP (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Fever and rigors indicating a severe, systemic illness (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Identification of a prostatic abscess — should prompt urologic specialist consultation (Harrison 22e p.1136).","Symptoms predictive of surgically correctable disease in men with febrile UTI: urinary retention, early UTI recurrence, hematuria at follow-up, and voiding difficulties (Harrison 22e p.1089)."],"prognosis":"ABP is typically a severe acute illness but is amenable to therapy with prostate-penetrating antimicrobials. A prostatic abscess is a recognized complication requiring urologic intervention. Chronic bacterial prostatitis recurrences are not uncommon. Men with febrile UTI and none of the warning features (retention, early recurrence, hematuria, voiding difficulty) generally have normal upper and lower urinary tracts on urologic workup, implying a favorable structural prognosis (Harrison 22e p.1089, p.1136).","pitfalls":["Do not attribute prostatic-type symptoms to bacterial prostatitis without bacteriuria; absence of bacteriuria points to chronic pelvic pain syndrome, which will not respond to antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Do not interpret an elevated PSA during febrile male UTI as a cancer marker — acute prostatic infection itself raises PSA and prostate/seminal vesicle size on imaging (Harrison 22e p.1089).","Do not assume complicated-UTI guideline regimens and durations apply to prostatitis, since prostatitis is largely absent from UTI trial data and may require prolonged prostate-penetrating therapy (Harrison 22e p.1087).","Do not overlook a prostatic focus or urinary retention in men presenting with recurrent cystitis (Harrison 22e p.1089)."],"severityClassification":"Prostatitis is categorized as acute bacterial prostatitis (ABP) versus chronic bacterial prostatitis (CBP). ABP is a typically severe illness with systemic features (fever, rigors, possible bladder outlet obstruction); CBP presents insidiously as recurrent cystitis with pelvic/perineal pain and typically without systemic signs, often managed in the ambulatory setting. Both are distinct from noninfectious chronic pelvic pain syndrome (Harrison 22e p.1089, p.1136).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1087","Harrison 22e p.1089","Harrison 22e p.1131","Harrison 22e p.1132","Harrison 22e p.1136","Harrison 22e p.1186"],"crossLinks":["CYSTITIS","COMPLICATED_UTI","PYELONEPHRITIS"]},"PULMONARY_TB":{"name":"Pulmonary Tuberculosis","system":"Respiratory / Tuberculosis","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chapter 183 (Tuberculosis), pp.1380-1394","clinicalPearls":["Pulmonary TB is traditionally split into primary (soon after initial infection, often middle/lower lung zones, common in children) and postprimary/adult-type (reactivation or recent reinfection, apical-posterior upper lobes); molecular data show many adult cases in endemic areas reflect recent infection rather than old reactivation (p.1385).","Postprimary disease favours the apical and posterior segments of the upper lobes and the superior segments of the lower lobes, where higher local oxygen tension favours mycobacterial growth (p.1386).","Cough develops in up to ~90% of cases and is often nonproductive initially before becoming purulent; hemoptysis occurs in 20-30%, and constitutional symptoms (diurnal fever, night sweats, weight loss, anorexia, malaise) are typical but nonspecific (p.1386).","Absence of fever does not exclude TB; physical examination is frequently normal even with significant parenchymal disease, so reliance on exam findings can mislead (p.1386).","Xpert MTB/RIF is the WHO first-line diagnostic, simultaneously detecting M. tuberculosis and rifampin resistance in under 2 hours; overall pulmonary sensitivity ~85% (up to 98% in smear-positive, ~70% in smear-negative), specificity 98% (p.1391-1392).","A negative NAAT (Xpert/Ultra) does NOT exclude TB and requires further workup; conversely culture remains the gold standard for definitive diagnosis (p.1390-1393).","Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra adds sensitivity (notably in smear-negative/culture-positive cases and PLWH) but its 'trace calls' can detect nonviable bacilli; trace calls in recently treated patients may represent dead-bacillus DNA (p.1392).","At least one to two early-morning sputum specimens should be submitted for both AFB smear and mycobacterial culture; tissue for culture must NOT be placed in formaldehyde/preservative (p.1392).","In HIV with partially preserved immunity, pulmonary TB looks classic (upper-lobe infiltrates and cavitation); with CD4 <200/uL it shifts to a primary-like pattern with diffuse/subtle interstitial infiltrates, little or no cavitation, pleural effusion and intrathoracic adenopathy (p.1391).","Because HIV-associated TB cavitates less, such patients are often less infectious; PLWH with culture- and smear-positive disease may even have a normal chest radiograph (p.1382, p.1391-1393).","Urine lipoarabinomannan (LF-LAM) is a point-of-care adjunct for TB diagnosis specifically in PLWH with advanced disease or low CD4 counts, not a general test (p.1393).","MDR-TB is defined as resistance to at least isoniazid and rifampin; rifampin resistance (RR-TB) is used as a practical proxy for MDR; pre-XDR adds fluoroquinolone resistance, and XDR-TB adds resistance to a fluoroquinolone plus at least one additional group A drug (e.g., bedaquiline or linezolid) (p.1380-1382)."],"pathophysiology":"M. tuberculosis is an aerobic acid-fast bacillus whose mycolic-acid-rich cell wall confers acid fastness and low permeability (reducing antibiotic efficacy). Transmission is by airborne droplet nuclei (<5-10 um) from a person with infectious pulmonary or laryngeal TB; cavitary cases are most infectious (10^5-10^7 AFB/mL of sputum). Inhaled bacilli reaching the alveoli are phagocytosed by alveolar macrophages; the bacillus arrests phagosome maturation and phagosome-lysosome fusion, replicates, and (via the ESX-1/RD1 secretion system) ruptures the macrophage to infect newly recruited phagocytes. Around 2-4 weeks, cell-mediated (TH1/IFN-gamma-driven, CD4+ T-cell-dependent) macrophage activation and a delayed-type hypersensitivity tissue-damaging response develop; their balance determines disease form. Granulomas (tubercles) of activated macrophages, epithelioid and giant cells form, with central caseous (cheese-like) necrosis in which low oxygen and pH inhibit but do not kill bacilli. When the macrophage-activating response is weak, delayed hypersensitivity causes progressive caseous liquefaction, bronchial and vascular erosion, and cavity formation; bacilli multiply within cavities and are discharged into airways, driving transmission and bronchogenic spread of satellite lesions. Lymphohematogenous dissemination can seed distant organs (miliary disease, meningitis). The continuum from infection to disease is governed largely by host immune competence; only ~10% of immunocompetent infected persons progress to active disease over a lifetime, about half within ~18 months. (pp.1380-1385)","additionalDifferentials":["Bronchogenic carcinoma (lung cancer) - upper-lobe mass/cavity, hemoptysis, weight loss; patients with such radiographic findings often undergo bronchoscopy/biopsy to distinguish from TB (p.1393)","Nontuberculous mycobacterial pulmonary disease (e.g., M. avium complex, M. kansasii) - AFB-positive smear with limited CXR abnormality, favoured by absence of TB risk factors and presence of underlying chronic lung disease (p.1393)","Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis / aspergilloma colonising an old TB cavity - hemoptysis, weight loss, fatigue, often misdiagnosed as TB recurrence; Aspergillus IgG precipitins and a fungal ball within a cavity suggest the diagnosis (p.1390-1391)","Bacterial lung abscess / necrotising pneumonia (cavitary lesion with air-fluid level) (p.1386)"],"infectionMimics":["Nontuberculous mycobacteria (M. avium complex, M. kansasii) causing AFB-positive cavitary or nodular lung disease (p.1393)","Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis / aspergilloma in residual cavities mimicking TB relapse (p.1390-1391)","Bacterial lung abscess or necrotising pneumonia producing upper-lobe cavitation (p.1386)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Bronchogenic carcinoma presenting as a cavitary upper-lobe mass with hemoptysis and weight loss (p.1393)","Solitary pulmonary nodule - TB can present as one, overlapping with neoplastic causes (p.1393)","Sarcoidosis and other granulomatous diseases (caseating vs non-caseating granuloma distinction) (granuloma differential, p.1384)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra (next-generation NAAT with higher sensitivity in smear-negative and HIV-positive cases) (p.1392)","Truenat MTB / MTB Plus and Truenat MTB-Rif Dx - portable, battery-operated molecular tests for peripheral settings (p.1392)","Line probe assays (LPAs) for rapid detection of rifampin (rpoB) and isoniazid (katG, inhA) resistance, and second-line LPAs for fluoroquinolone/injectable resistance (p.1393)","Liquid culture (MGIT) as WHO reference standard; solid media (Lowenstein-Jensen, Middlebrook 7H10/7H11) require 4-8 weeks (p.1392)","Universal drug-susceptibility testing - at minimum rifampin DST on all initial isolates (p.1392-1393)","Targeted next-generation sequencing (tNGS) and whole-genome sequencing for comprehensive resistance profiling (p.1393)","Sputum induction with hypertonic saline when spontaneous sputum cannot be produced (p.1393)","Fiberoptic bronchoscopy with bronchial brushings, endobronchial/transbronchial biopsy, and bronchoalveolar lavage (submit for Xpert, culture, AFB smear) (p.1393)","Early-morning gastric lavage / induced sputum for children unable to expectorate (p.1393)","Urine lateral-flow lipoarabinomannan (LF-LAM) assay in advanced HIV (p.1393)","Chest CT to clarify equivocal plain-film findings (p.1393)","Tuberculin skin test (TST/PPD) and IFN-gamma release assays (IGRA: QuantiFERON-TB Gold/Plus, T-SPOT.TB) to identify TB infection - note both have low predictive value for who will progress and cannot distinguish infection from active disease (p.1393-1394)","M. tuberculosis antigen-based skin tests (TBSTs using ESAT-6/CFP-10) with accuracy similar to IGRA (p.1394)"],"redFlags":["Massive hemoptysis from erosion of a vessel in a cavity wall or rupture of a Rasmussen's aneurysm - potentially life-threatening (p.1386)","Extensive disease causing dyspnea progressing to acute respiratory distress syndrome (p.1386)","Features of hematogenous/miliary dissemination (miliary reticulonodular CXR, hepatosplenomegaly, choroidal tubercles) - high mortality if unrecognised (p.1389)","Meningismus, headache, altered sensorium or cranial nerve palsy suggesting tuberculous meningitis - uniformly fatal if untreated (p.1388-1389)","Advanced HIV with possible TB: NAAT-positive results warrant immediate treatment because diagnostic delay can be fatal (p.1391, p.1393)"],"prognosis":"Untreated, TB is often fatal: about one-third of patients die within 1 year of diagnosis, and historically ~70% (weighted mean) of smear-positive cases died within 10 years; paucibacillary smear-negative disease had a lower ~20% case-fatality at 5 years, and ~60% of 5-year survivors underwent spontaneous remission. With timely, proper drug-susceptible treatment the vast majority are cured. Improper therapy lowers mortality but generates chronic infectious, often drug-resistant cases. A substantial proportion of cured survivors suffer post-TB lung disease - chronic airflow impairment, dyspnea, residual cough, bronchiectasis, pulmonary hypertension, and predisposition to aspergilloma/chronic pulmonary aspergillosis in residual cavities. (pp.1383, 1386, 1390-1391)","pitfalls":["Treating on clinical/radiographic grounds alone: CXR has high sensitivity but poor specificity and no pattern (including a normal film) is pathognomonic in the HIV era - bacteriologic confirmation must always be pursued (p.1393).","Interpreting a negative Xpert/NAAT or negative smear as ruling out TB - smear microscopy sensitivity is only 40-60% and a negative molecular test still requires culture and further workup (p.1392-1393).","AFB microscopy cannot distinguish M. tuberculosis from nontuberculous mycobacteria, and urine/gastric specimens are prone to false negatives (low organism load) and false positives (commensal mycobacteria) (p.1392).","Relying on the chest exam: physical findings are of limited use and may be entirely normal in significant pulmonary TB (p.1386).","Missing diagnosis in atypical hosts (elderly nursing-home residents, teenagers, immunosuppressed/PLWH with lower-zone or noncavitary disease) (p.1391).","TST/IGRA pitfalls: false-negative TST in immunosuppression and overwhelming TB; false-positive from NTM or BCG; the TST 'boosting phenomenon' can mimic conversion; neither test distinguishes latent infection from active disease (p.1393-1394).","Putting tissue intended for culture into formaldehyde, which kills the organism and precludes culture (p.1392)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames severity primarily by disease form and host rather than a numeric score: primary vs postprimary (cavitary) pulmonary disease; pulmonary vs extrapulmonary vs disseminated (miliary) TB; and by drug-resistance category - drug-susceptible TB, rifampin-resistant (RR-TB), multidrug-resistant (MDR-TB: resistant to at least isoniazid and rifampin), pre-XDR-TB (MDR/RR plus fluoroquinolone resistance), and extensively drug-resistant (XDR-TB: MDR plus fluoroquinolone resistance and resistance to at least one additional group A drug such as bedaquiline or linezolid). Infectiousness grades with cavitation and sputum bacillary load (smear-positive cavitary > smear-negative/culture-positive > culture-negative/extrapulmonary, the last essentially noninfectious). (pp.1380-1386)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1380","Harrison 22e p.1381","Harrison 22e p.1382","Harrison 22e p.1383","Harrison 22e p.1384","Harrison 22e p.1385","Harrison 22e p.1386","Harrison 22e p.1387","Harrison 22e p.1388","Harrison 22e p.1389","Harrison 22e p.1390","Harrison 22e p.1391","Harrison 22e p.1392","Harrison 22e p.1393","Harrison 22e p.1394"],"crossLinks":["DISSEMINATED_TB","CNS_TB","lung_cancer","sarcoidosis"]},"PUO":{"name":"Pyrexia of Unknown Origin","system":"Systemic","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.22 (Mulders-Manders & Rovers), pp.149-154 (file pages 192-197)","clinicalPearls":["Current (post-Petersdorf) FUO definition requires all of: fever >=38.3C (101F) on at least two occasions, illness lasting at least 3 weeks, no known immunocompromised state, and a diagnosis still uncertain after thorough history, examination, and a defined set of obligatory investigations (p.149).","Inflammation of unknown origin (IUO) shares the FUO definition but lacks the temperature criterion (elevated CRP/ESR with normal temperature); its causes and workup are identical to FUO (p.149).","FUO usually reflects an ATYPICAL presentation of a COMMON disease rather than a rare disease; pattern recognition explains the higher diagnostic yield at expert centers (pp.150, 153).","The cornerstone of the workup is the search for potentially diagnostic clues (PDCs) - all localizing signs, symptoms, and abnormalities pointing toward a diagnosis - via repeated history, examination, and obligatory tests; further testing should be PDC-driven (pp.152-153).","Examination should pay special attention to the eyes, lymph nodes, temporal arteries, liver, spleen, prior surgical sites, the entire skin surface, and mucous membranes (p.152).","History should capture fever pattern (continuous vs recurrent) and duration, prior medical history, current/recent drugs, family and sexual history, country of origin, recent and remote travel, environmental/hobby exposures, and animal contacts (p.152).","Giant cell arteritis is notably prevalent among FUO patients, with rates up to 17% in the elderly; temporal artery biopsy is still recommended for patients >=55 years (p.153).","FUO with headache should prompt CSF microbiologic examination for herpes simplex virus (especially type 2), Cryptococcus neoformans/gattii, and M. tuberculosis (p.153).","In patients with recurrent/episodic FUO, ask the patient to return DURING a febrile episode; further tests should be done during an inflammatory episode since abnormalities may be absent between episodes (pp.152-153).","Geography shapes the differential: infections (especially TB) dominate non-Western cohorts, while noninfectious inflammatory diseases (NIIDs) are the most common cause in Western cohorts, accounting for up to one-third of cases (p.150).","Investigation of cryoglobulins is a useful low-cost screening test in FUO given the frequent absence of specific symptoms (p.153)."],"pathophysiology":"FUO is a syndrome, not a single disease - a final prolonged febrile illness in which intensive standard evaluation has failed to localize a cause. Etiologies cluster into four groups: infections, noninfectious inflammatory diseases (NIIDs: autoimmune, autoinflammatory, granulomatous, and vasculitides), malignancy, and miscellaneous, with a substantial undiagnosed fraction. The spectrum has shifted over time: intraabdominal abscesses, tumors, and infective endocarditis have declined as CT, ultrasound, blood culture, and echocardiography improved, while entities such as acute HIV and autoinflammatory syndromes are now recognized. NIIDs may precede their typical clinical or laboratory features by months, requiring prolonged observation. The 'FUO paradox' describes how, despite better imaging (CT/MRI/PET-CT), the undiagnosed proportion is now higher (>50% in some Western cohorts) because most febrile illnesses are diagnosed before 3 weeks elapse, leaving only the most difficult cases to meet FUO criteria (pp.149-150).","additionalDifferentials":["Infective endocarditis (including culture-negative endocarditis from fastidious organisms - nutritionally variant streptococci, HACEK group, Coxiella burnetii, Tropheryma whipplei, Bartonella) - one of the more common infectious causes (pp.150-151)","Extrapulmonary and miliary tuberculosis - up to half of infections in non-Western FUO are M. tuberculosis; miliary TB is especially hard to diagnose (pp.150, 153)","Diverticulitis (p.150)","Vertebral osteomyelitis / spondylodiscitis (pp.150-151)","Q fever (Coxiella burnetii) - consider with rural exposure, animal contact, valve disease, aortic aneurysm, or vascular prosthesis; test by immunofluorescence assay (pp.150-151)","Whipple disease (Tropheryma whipplei) - consider with unexplained CNS, GI, or joint symptoms; PCR on stool and blood early (p.150)","Intraabdominal / hepatic / renal / pelvic abscesses (p.151)","Endemic infections after relevant travel/residence: malaria, visceral leishmaniasis, histoplasmosis, coccidioidomycosis (p.150)","Acute HIV infection (p.150)","Adult-onset Still's disease (NIID, relatively common in FUO) (p.150)","Large-vessel vasculitis and giant cell arteritis / polymyalgia rheumatica (pp.150, 153)","Systemic lupus erythematosus (p.150)","Sarcoidosis (p.150)","Familial Mediterranean fever and other autoinflammatory syndromes (rare except in specific regions) (p.150)","Schnitzler's syndrome - urticaria, bone pain, monoclonal gammopathy (p.150)","VEXAS syndrome - adult-onset, middle-aged/elderly males, recurrent inflammation, chondritis, skin lesions, lung disease, venous thrombosis, arthritis, MDS; bone-marrow vacuoles in myeloid precursors; somatic UBA1 variants (p.150)","Malignant lymphoma - by far the most common neoplastic cause; fever may precede detectable lymphadenopathy (p.150)","Common solid tumors causing fever: breast, colon, hepatocellular, lung, pancreatic, renal cell carcinoma (p.151)"],"infectionMimics":["Culture-negative endocarditis - sterile cultures may simply reflect ongoing antibiotic therapy rather than a noninfectious cause (pp.151-152)","Marantic (nonbacterial thrombotic) endocarditis - sterile, paraneoplastic, especially with adenocarcinomas, mimics infective endocarditis (p.151)","Sterile endocarditis of SLE and antiphospholipid syndrome (p.151)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Drug-induced fever - virtually any drug, even after long-term use; common culprits include allopurinol, carbamazepine, lamotrigine, phenytoin, sulfasalazine, furosemide, and antimicrobials (sulfonamides, minocycline, vancomycin, beta-lactams, isoniazid); fever persisting >72h after stopping the drug makes it an unlikely cause (pp.150, 153)","DRESS / drug-induced hypersensitivity syndrome - often with eosinophilia and extensive lymphadenopathy (p.150)","Benign or exercise-induced hyperthermia - elevated temperature WITHOUT raised CRP/ESR or other inflammation; may be postinfectious (triggers similar to chronic fatigue syndrome: EBV, Q fever, COVID-19) (p.150)","Factitious fever - artificially induced (e.g., IV injection of contaminated water); suspect with access to medical equipment (p.150)","Fraudulent fever - patient is normothermic but manipulates the thermometer; simultaneous multi-site measurement (rectum/ear/mouth) and pulse-temperature dissociation are clues (p.150)","Central thermoregulatory disorders - brain tumor, stroke, encephalitis, hypothalamic dysfunction (p.151)","Peripheral thermoregulatory causes - hyperthyroidism, pheochromocytoma, heat stroke (p.151)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Obligatory baseline panel: ESR and CRP, platelet count, leukocyte count with differential, hemoglobin, electrolytes, creatinine, total protein, alkaline phosphatase, ALT, AST, LDH, creatine kinase, ferritin, antinuclear antibodies, rheumatoid factor, protein electrophoresis, urinalysis, three blood cultures, urine culture, chest x-ray, abdominal ultrasonography, and TST or IGRA (p.149)","Cryoglobulins - useful low-cost screening test (p.153)","Fundoscopy by an ophthalmologist - early, to exclude retinal vasculitis when PDCs are absent or misleading (p.153)","18F-FDG-PET/CT - established next step when PDC-guided tests are unrevealing; ~50% total diagnostic yield; best performed during active inflammation (elevated CRP/ESR or fever) and before glucocorticoids; outperforms gallium and labeled-leukocyte scintigraphy (pp.152-154)","Gallium-67 citrate or 111In/99mTc-labeled leukocyte scintigraphy - alternatives when PET/CT unavailable (lower yield; gallium localizes ~1/3, leukocyte ~1/5 of cases) (p.154)","Whole-body MRI - one study reported yield comparable to PET/CT (needs confirmation) (p.154)","Echocardiography - low yield without PDCs; reserve for high suspicion of endocarditis (p.153)","Liver biopsy (acid-fast smear, culture, PCR) - highest yield if miliary TB suspected; bone marrow / lymph node biopsies are alternatives (p.153)","Mycobacterial PCR alongside cultures - may shorten time to TB diagnosis (p.153)","Specialized blood cultures held long enough for fastidious organisms (e.g., HACEK); inform the lab and use special media for Histoplasma or Legionella; >3 blood cultures or >1 urine culture is useless without PDCs (p.153)","Q fever serology by immunofluorescence assay; T. whipplei PCR on stool/blood (pp.150-151)","Temporal artery biopsy in patients >=55 years for suspected giant cell arteritis (p.153)","CSF examination (HSV especially type 2, Cryptococcus, M. tuberculosis) when FUO is accompanied by headache (p.153)","Later-stage PDC-driven biopsies for pathology and/or microbiology (p.154)"],"redFlags":["Clinical deterioration without new PDCs - the trigger to escalate workup (rather than wait) and the situation where empirical therapy may be justified (pp.152-153)","Hemodynamic instability or neutropenia - good indications for empirical antibiotic therapy (p.155)","Granulomatous disease on liver or bone marrow biopsy - should prompt (re)consideration of miliary tuberculosis (p.153)","Early phase of evaluation carries the highest FUO-related mortality risk (deaths occurred during index admission in cohort data; intravascular lymphoma and pneumonia found at autopsy) (p.154)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends chiefly on the underlying diagnosis. When FUO remains unexplained after extensive evaluation, outlook is favorable: large cohorts report high rates of spontaneous fever resolution and mortality of 8% or less over several years of follow-up. The greatest mortality risk is during the early diagnostic phase. A normal 18F-FDG-PET/CT is associated with higher rates of spontaneous resolution and may help predict who will resolve. A symptom duration >12 months and a normal PET/CT both lower the chance of ever reaching a final diagnosis (pp.150, 154).","pitfalls":["Starting empirical therapeutic trials before a diagnosis - should be AVOIDED except when the patient is rapidly deteriorating, because most undiagnosed patients resolve spontaneously (p.154).","Continuing antibiotics/glucocorticoids during workup - they mask disease: cultures become unreliable and glucocorticoids shrink lymph nodes regardless of cause; stop them before further testing (p.152).","Early empirical glucocorticoids - can mask fever while infection or lymphoma spreads and reduce the chance of diagnosing a treatable malignancy; avoid until infection and lymphoma are sufficiently excluded (p.155).","Ordering more screening tests without PDCs - low yield; immunologic and microbiologic serologies without PDCs more often give false-positives; better to wait for new PDCs unless the patient deteriorates (pp.153-154).","Over-culturing - performing >3 blood cultures or >1 urine culture is useless absent PDCs (p.153).","Failing to recognize PDCs already present in available information is a frequent cause of diagnostic delay (p.154).","Interpreting a negative TST/IGRA as excluding TB - it does not; results may be indeterminate in miliary TB, malnutrition, immunosuppression, or with anti-IFN-gamma autoantibodies (p.153).","Misreading PET/CT - physiologic FDG uptake in brain, heart, bowel, kidneys, and bladder can obscure pathology (e.g., cardiac uptake masking endocarditis - mitigate with a pre-scan low-carbohydrate diet) (p.153).","Not excluding factitious/fraudulent fever and drug fever early, especially when inflammatory markers are normal (pp.150, 153)."],"severityClassification":"FUO categories (by host/setting), each requiring a distinct approach: (1) classic FUO - the immunocompetent adult per the formal definition; (2) nosocomial FUO; (3) neutropenic FUO; and (4) HIV-associated FUO. The contemporary Harrison definition explicitly EXCLUDES immunocompromised states, which need a different, more aggressive approach. Etiologies are grouped into four diagnostic categories - infections, noninfectious inflammatory diseases (NIIDs), malignancy, and miscellaneous - with a large 'no diagnosis' fraction (pp.149-150).","references":["Harrison 22e p.149","Harrison 22e p.150","Harrison 22e p.151","Harrison 22e p.152","Harrison 22e p.153","Harrison 22e p.154","Harrison 22e p.155"],"crossLinks":["DISSEMINATED_TB","IE","malignancy_b","vasculitis","sarcoidosis"]},"PYELONEPHRITIS":{"name":"Acute Pyelonephritis","system":"Genitourinary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1088-1094, 1133-1137; 1329; 2493","clinicalPearls":["Fever is the principal feature distinguishing pyelonephritis from simple cystitis; presence of fever, back/flank pain, rigors, nausea or vomiting signals infection that has extended beyond the bladder (Harrison 22e p.1086-1090, 1133).","Severity spans a wide spectrum: mild disease may present only with low-grade fever with or without costovertebral angle pain, whereas severe disease manifests with high fever, rigors, nausea, vomiting, and flank pain; onset is generally acute and may or may not be preceded by cystitis symptoms (Harrison 22e p.1090).","Pyelonephritis represents tissue-invasive disease and patients are often systemically ill, so the threat of progression to bacteremia and urosepsis is real (Harrison 22e p.1090, 1137, 1329).","In a woman presenting with at least one localizing urinary symptom (dysuria, frequency, hematuria, or back pain) and no complicating factors, the combined probability of acute cystitis or pyelonephritis is about 50% (Harrison 22e p.1133).","Men with febrile UTI should undergo upper-tract imaging (CT or ultrasound) if not done previously, to evaluate for urinary tract abnormality or obstruction, and elevated PSA with enlarged prostate/seminal vesicles suggests prostatic involvement (Harrison 22e p.1132).","Pregnant patients are at unusually high risk of developing pyelonephritis, which can adversely affect pregnancy outcome (preterm delivery, low birth weight); this underlies routine prenatal screening for asymptomatic bacteriuria (Harrison 22e p.1086, 1329)."],"pathophysiology":"Pyelonephritis usually develops as an ascending infection: uropathogens move from the urethra to the bladder and then continue up the ureter to the renal parenchyma, which is the route for most renal parenchymal infection. Its microbiology therefore mirrors that of acute cystitis. A minority arises hematogenously (<2% of UTIs overall), typically with relatively virulent organisms such as Staphylococcus aureus and Salmonella; isolation of either from urine should prompt consideration of concomitant bacteremia, and hematogenous seeding can produce focal renal abscesses. Anatomic factors causing urinary obstruction or retention (congenital anomalies, cystocele, pelvic-organ prolapse, neurogenic bladder, benign prostatic hypertrophy) impede the protective flushing action of micturition and predispose to renal involvement. A foreign body such as a stone or catheter provides an inert surface for colonization (Harrison 22e p.1088, 1130-1131).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute prostatitis in men presenting with febrile UTI (Harrison 22e p.1137)","Complicated UTI (fever in a patient with structural/functional urinary abnormality, immunocompromise, or instrumentation) (Harrison 22e p.1133-1134)","Sexually transmitted infection mimicking UTI, particularly Chlamydia trachomatis cervicitis/urethritis (Harrison 22e p.1133-1134)","Renal/ureteral calculus with obstruction (renal colic) presenting with flank pain and pyuria (Harrison 22e p.2493)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute prostatitis (Harrison 22e p.1137)","Cervicitis or urethritis due to Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoeae (Harrison 22e p.1133-1134)","Vaginitis (Candida albicans, Trichomonas vaginalis) or herpetic urethritis causing dysuria (Harrison 22e p.1133-1134)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Renal/ureteral stone with proximal obstruction, which may present exactly as acute pyelonephritis (Harrison 22e p.2493)","Interstitial cystitis or noninfectious vulvovaginal irritation causing dysuria (Harrison 22e p.1133-1134)","Sloughed renal papilla or blood clot causing ureteral obstruction without infection (Harrison 22e p.2493)","Musculoskeletal flank/back pain, herpes zoster, abdominal aortic aneurysm, duodenal ulcer (differential for flank pain) (Harrison 22e p.2493)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper-tract imaging (CT or renal ultrasound) to evaluate for obstruction, abscess, or anatomic abnormality, especially in men, in patients not responding within 72 hours, or when complicated infection is suspected (Harrison 22e p.1132, 1137)","Urine dipstick (nitrite, leukocyte esterase) and microscopic urinalysis as point-of-care tests; note nitrites detect only Enterobacterales and may be falsely negative with non-Enterobacterales organisms (Harrison 22e p.1134-1135)","Imaging for gas in renal/perinephric tissues when emphysematous pyelonephritis is suspected in a diabetic patient (Harrison 22e p.1090)"],"redFlags":["Acute papillary necrosis with sloughed papillae obstructing the ureter, producing obstructive uropathy that requires intervention to relieve obstruction and preserve renal function (Harrison 22e p.1090, 1137)","UTI in the setting of ureteral obstruction is a medical emergency requiring immediate restoration of drainage by ureteral stent or percutaneous nephrostomy (Harrison 22e p.2493)","Emphysematous pyelonephritis: gas production in renal and perinephric tissues, occurring almost exclusively in patients with poorly controlled diabetes; a particularly severe form (Harrison 22e p.1090)","Continued fever and/or bacteremia despite antibacterial therapy should raise suspicion for intraparenchymal, perinephric, or renal cortical abscess (Harrison 22e p.1090, 1137)","Failure of clinical response within 72 hours warrants evaluation for an uncontrolled source of infection (Harrison 22e p.1137)","Persistently elevated/increasing fever, flank pain, and rising neutrophil counts should prompt evaluation for intrarenal/perinephric abscess and/or obstruction (Harrison 22e p.1329)","Bilateral papillary necrosis may present first as a rapid rise in serum creatinine (Harrison 22e p.1090)"],"prognosis":"Most pyelonephritis treated effectively resolves, though fever may take 5-7 days to clear completely even with appropriate therapy. Renal parenchymal damage and loss of renal function are uncommon and occur primarily in association with urinary obstruction (preexisting, or, rarely, de novo in diabetic patients with papillary necrosis). Chronic bacterial pyelonephritis is rarely a cause of chronic renal failure because it seldom affects both kidneys. Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis arises from chronic obstruction (often staghorn calculus) plus chronic infection causing suppurative destruction of renal tissue and frequently requires nephrectomy (Harrison 22e p.1090, 1137, 1329, 2493).","pitfalls":["Treating presumed UTI when the true cause is an STI (e.g., C. trachomatis); vaginal discharge should prompt STI consideration since symptoms alone do not reliably distinguish them (Harrison 22e p.1133-1134).","Stopping the workup at an abnormal urinalysis in a catheterized or febrile patient: pyuria and bacteriuria are expected with catheterization and do not by themselves establish UTI as the cause of fever (Harrison 22e p.1133-1135).","Mistaking an obstructing stone with proximal infection for uncomplicated pyelonephritis and missing the need for emergent drainage (Harrison 22e p.2493).","Misattributing xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis to renal malignancy because its mass effect can mimic tumor (Harrison 22e p.1090).","Delayed/withheld antimicrobial therapy in true UTI carries a small increased risk of progression to pyelonephritis (Harrison 22e p.1136)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison describes a clinical spectrum rather than a formal score: mild pyelonephritis (low-grade fever, with or without lower back or costovertebral angle pain) versus severe pyelonephritis (high fever, rigors, nausea, vomiting, flank pain). Distinct severe/complicated entities include emphysematous pyelonephritis (gas-forming, almost exclusively in poorly controlled diabetes), xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis (chronic obstruction plus infection causing suppurative renal destruction), and pyelonephritis complicated by papillary necrosis with obstruction or by intrarenal/perinephric abscess. Acute pyelonephritis is one of the systemic UTI presentations that may be grouped as urosepsis or febrile UTI, with or without bacteremia (Harrison 22e p.1090, 1329).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1086","Harrison 22e p.1088","Harrison 22e p.1090","Harrison 22e p.1130","Harrison 22e p.1132","Harrison 22e p.1133","Harrison 22e p.1134","Harrison 22e p.1135","Harrison 22e p.1136","Harrison 22e p.1137","Harrison 22e p.1329","Harrison 22e p.2493"],"crossLinks":["COMPLICATED_UTI","CYSTITIS","renal_colic","SEPSIS"]},"RICKETTSIAL_FEVER":{"name":"Rickettsial Fever (Spotted Fever Group)","system":"Tropical Fever","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.137-141 (Ch.21 Fever and Rash); p.1454-1459 (Ch.192 Rickettsial Diseases); p.1488 (Ch.189 Leptospirosis differential)","clinicalPearls":["All acute rickettsial illnesses look alike for roughly the first 5 days: fever, headache, and myalgias, often with nausea, vomiting, or cough; the distinguishing features (rash, eschar, pneumonitis, meningoencephalitis) emerge only as the illness progresses (p.1454).","An inoculation eschar (tache noire) is a necrotic, often painless black-crusted lesion with a surrounding erythematous halo at the bite site; it can appear during the incubation period and is common in Mediterranean spotted fever (~50%), African tick-bite fever (~90%), and rickettsialpox (~90%), but is essentially absent in classic RMSF (<1%) and in epidemic/murine typhus (p.1455).","African tick-bite fever (R. africae) is the rickettsiosis most often imported into Europe and North America via travelers returning from sub-Saharan Africa; because Amblyomma ticks feed in groups, multiple eschars and regional lymphadenopathy are characteristic, and rash may be vesicular, sparse, or absent (p.1457-1458).","In RMSF the rash is centrifugal and evolving: erythematous macules begin on wrists and ankles, spread to extremities and trunk, and only later involve palms and soles; macules progress to non-blanching petechiae as vascular damage advances (p.1456).","Palm/sole involvement and the classic centripetal/centrifugal spread are late and inconsistent: in RMSF palms/soles are involved after day 5 in only ~43% and never in 18-64% of cases (p.1457).","The full classic triad of fever, rash, and recalled tick exposure is present in only ~3% of RMSF patients during the first 3 days, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis (p.1457).","Characteristic supportive labs across rickettsioses are thrombocytopenia, a normal or low (not elevated) WBC count, mildly elevated hepatic aminotransferases, and hyponatremia (p.1454-1456); hyponatremia in RMSF (~56%) reflects appropriate ADH secretion in a hypovolemic state (p.1457).","Spotted fever group serology cross-reacts among species, so a positive titer confirms a SFG rickettsiosis but generally cannot identify the specific organism (p.1458)."],"pathophysiology":"Rickettsiae and Orientia are obligately intracellular gram-negative coccobacilli that primarily infect vascular endothelial cells. After tick saliva inoculates organisms into the dermis (in RMSF after roughly 6 h of feeding), they spread lymphohematogenously and infect contiguous foci of endothelium throughout the body, producing a widespread infectious vasculitis with increased vascular permeability. The resulting edema, hypovolemia, and tissue ischemia (rather than occlusive thrombosis) drive the organ injury: prerenal azotemia, noncardiogenic pulmonary edema, and CNS dysfunction. Platelet consumption causes thrombocytopenia in about a third to half of RMSF cases, but frank DIC with hypofibrinogenemia is rare; the coagulation changes appear to be homeostatic responses to endothelial injury forming nonocclusive hemostatic plugs (p.1454, p.1456).","additionalDifferentials":["Human monocytotropic ehrlichiosis (Ehrlichia chaffeensis) and human granulocytotropic anaplasmosis (Anaplasma phagocytophilum) - tick-borne, often rashless, share thrombocytopenia/leukopenia/transaminitis (p.1455-1457)","Epidemic (louse-borne) typhus (R. prowazekii) and recrudescent Brill-Zinsser disease (p.1458)","Murine (endemic) typhus (R. typhi) (p.1458-1459)","Q fever (Coxiella burnetii) (p.1455)"],"infectionMimics":["Influenza and enteroviral infection (mimic early rashless RMSF) (p.1457)","Infectious mononucleosis (p.1457)","Viral hepatitis (p.1457)","Leptospirosis (p.1457; shares epidemiologic and clinical features, dual infection reported, p.1488)","Typhoid fever (epidemic typhus is frequently misdiagnosed as typhoid in tropical countries) (p.1457-1458)","Gram-negative or gram-positive bacterial sepsis / septic shock (p.1457)","Meningococcemia and disseminated gonococcal infection (p.1457)","Secondary syphilis (p.1457)","Measles (rubeola) and rubella (p.1457)","Dengue and hantavirus infection (share epidemiologic and clinical features) (p.1488)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Drug hypersensitivity reaction (p.1457)","Immune (idiopathic) thrombocytopenic purpura (p.1457)","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (p.1457)","Kawasaki syndrome (p.1457)","Immune complex vasculitis (p.1457)","Toxic shock syndrome (p.1457)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Indirect immunofluorescence assay (IFA) for SFG IgG - the most common confirmatory test; sensitivity 89-100% and specificity 99-100%, but a reactive titer (>=64) is not detectable until 7-10 days after onset and is usually negative at presentation (p.1458)","Immunohistologic examination or PCR of a 3-mm punch biopsy of a rash lesion - the only test useful during acute illness (immunohistology ~70% sensitive, 100% specific; PCR likely higher) (p.1458)","PCR of eschar biopsy or eschar swab, or skin biopsy, for SFG rickettsiae (p.1458)","Note: PCR of peripheral blood is NOT adequately sensitive because circulating organism quantities are low (p.1458)","Platelet count, WBC count, hepatic aminotransferases, serum sodium, and renal function as supportive clues (p.1454-1457)","CSF analysis if meningoencephalitis suspected (typically 10-100 cells/uL, mononuclear predominance, normal glucose, mildly elevated protein) (p.1456-1457)"],"redFlags":["Hypotension/septic-shock presentation and multiorgan failure (p.1454, p.1457)","Noncardiogenic pulmonary edema / ARDS requiring mechanical ventilation (RMSF: ARDS ~12%) (p.1456-1457)","Encephalitis - confusion, lethargy, stupor, delirium, ataxia, seizures, or coma (p.1456-1457)","Acute kidney injury from acute tubular necrosis in shock (p.1457)","Skin necrosis, peripheral gangrene, or purpura fulminans (severe MSF) (p.1454; p.1488 / Ch.21)","Gastrointestinal hemorrhage; severe anemia requiring transfusion (p.1457)","Fulminant RMSF (fatal within 5 days), especially in male patients with G6PD deficiency (p.1457)","Extremes of age (children <10 years, adults >70 years) and immunosuppression - higher case-fatality (p.1456-1457)"],"prognosis":"Outcome is strongly time-dependent: untreated RMSF carries mortality of roughly 20-25% (historic) with confirmed-case fatality around 9-10% where R. rickettsii is the sole agent, versus ~3-5% when treated early; delayed recognition and treatment markedly increase death. For unclear reasons RMSF case-fatality approaches 50% in Mexico and Brazil. Severe Mediterranean spotted fever has mortality near 50% without treatment but improves dramatically with even a single day of appropriate therapy. Untreated epidemic typhus historically killed up to 60% and still exceeds 10-15% in outbreaks. Many survivors of severe RMSF recover fully, but permanent neurologic deficits and gangrene requiring amputation can follow severe illness (p.1456-1457; p.1488 / Ch.21).","pitfalls":["Waiting for serology before treating: SFG serology is typically negative at presentation and only turns positive at 7-10 days, so therapy must be started empirically on clinical and epidemiologic suspicion and never delayed for a confirmatory titer (p.1454, p.1458).","Excluding the diagnosis because there is no rash or no recalled tick bite: ~10-15% of RMSF patients never develop a rash, rash is present in only ~14% on day 1 and ~49% by day 3, and only ~60% recall a tick bite (p.1455-1457).","Treating a positive early SFG titer as proof of current disease: in areas with prevalent low-virulence SFG species (e.g., R. amblyommatis), a positive titer in the first 5-6 days often reflects preexisting cross-reactive antibody, not the acute illness (p.1456, p.1458).","Misattributing abdominal pain/vomiting to enterocolitis (has led to unnecessary laparotomy) or CNS findings to primary meningoencephalitis (p.1457).","Using ineffective or harmful agents: beta-lactams, aminoglycosides, and erythromycin have no role in RMSF, and sulfa drugs are associated with worse outcomes than no treatment (p.1458).","Missing the eschar: it can be small, painless, and located in skinfolds/scalp and frequently goes unnoticed by patient and clinician, yet careful skin examination for an eschar is a key diagnostic clue (p.1454-1459)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal grading scale, but stratifies along a spectrum: (1) self-limited febrile illness that resolves without evolution; (2) classic disease with rash and/or eschar; and (3) severe/complicated disease with organ involvement - pneumonitis/ARDS, meningoencephalitis, acute kidney injury, hemorrhage/skin necrosis/gangrene, sepsis/septic shock, and (rarely) fulminant RMSF fatal within 5 days. Higher-risk groups include children <10 years, adults >70 years, the immunosuppressed, and (for fulminant RMSF) males with G6PD deficiency (p.1454-1457).","references":["Harrison 22e p.137","Harrison 22e p.138","Harrison 22e p.139","Harrison 22e p.141","Harrison 22e p.1454","Harrison 22e p.1455","Harrison 22e p.1456","Harrison 22e p.1457","Harrison 22e p.1458","Harrison 22e p.1459","Harrison 22e p.1488"],"crossLinks":["SCRUB_TYPHUS","DENGUE","LEPTOSPIROSIS","MALARIA","ENTERIC_FEVER"]},"SBP":{"name":"Spontaneous Bacterial Peritonitis","system":"Hepatobiliary / GI","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.329-330, 372-373, 1069-1072, 2715-2716","clinicalPearls":["An ascitic-fluid PMN (neutrophil) count of at least 250/microL is diagnostic of SBP, irrespective of whether cultures grow an organism (Harrison 22e p.329, 372, 1070).","Fever is the single most common manifestation, present in roughly 80% of cases; abdominal pain, acute onset, and peritoneal signs aid diagnosis but their absence does not rule SBP out (Harrison 22e p.1070).","Abdominal tenderness is found in only about 40% of patients and rebound tenderness is uncommon, so a benign abdomen does not exclude the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.329).","SBP can present with no abdominal symptoms at all, instead manifesting as new or worsening hepatic encephalopathy, altered mental status, jaundice, or acute kidney injury without another explanation (Harrison 22e p.329, 1070, 2715).","In any cirrhotic patient hospitalized with ascites who deteriorates or shows signs of infection, a diagnostic paracentesis is mandatory; tapping within 12 hours of admission lowers mortality through early SBP detection (Harrison 22e p.329, 2715).","Ascitic fluid should be injected directly into blood-culture bottles at the bedside to improve yield, and blood cultures drawn simultaneously, since the organism burden in the fluid is low and bacteremia frequently coexists (Harrison 22e p.329, 1070).","Culture samples should be obtained before antibiotics are started to maximize recovery of the organism (Harrison 22e p.1070).","SBP is almost always monomicrobial; a single isolate is the rule (Harrison 22e p.329, 1070).","Gram stain of ascitic fluid is frequently negative in SBP and a negative result should not deter treatment (Harrison 22e p.1070).","SBP virtually always arises on a background of preexisting ascites, which predates the infection (Harrison 22e p.1070).","A repeat paracentesis after 48 hours of therapy should confirm at least a 25% fall in the ascitic PMN count from baseline; failure to fall, or persistent ascitic leukocytosis after treatment, should prompt a search for an alternative or secondary diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.329, 1071)."],"pathophysiology":"SBP is spontaneous infection of ascitic fluid in the absence of an intraabdominal surgical source. The presumed mechanism is bacterial translocation: gut flora cross an edematous, leaky intestinal wall into mesenteric lymph nodes, producing bacteremia that seeds the ascites, which is a good growth medium. Susceptibility in cirrhosis reflects impaired hepatic filtration from a diseased liver and altered portal circulation, low ascitic complement levels (lower than in non-cirrhotic ascites), and diminished opsonic and phagocytic function of PMNs in advanced liver disease. Cirrhosis is also associated with gut dysbiosis (increased Enterobacteriaceae), small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth, reduced Paneth-cell defensins, decreased intestinal motility, reduced pancreatobiliary secretions, and portal-hypertensive enteropathy, all of which promote pathologic translocation. SBP occurs in up to ~30% of hospitalized cirrhotics with ascites but is an uncommon event overall, affecting 10% or fewer of cirrhotic patients. (Harrison 22e p.1070, 2715)","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary bacterial peritonitis from a perforated or inflamed viscus or intraabdominal abscess (Harrison 22e p.329, 1071)","Hepatic encephalopathy from other precipitants (GI bleed, volume depletion, hyponatremia, constipation) when SBP presents as altered mentation (Harrison 22e p.2716-2717)","Acute kidney injury / hepatorenal syndrome from non-infectious causes when SBP presents as renal deterioration (Harrison 22e p.329, 2716)"],"infectionMimics":["Secondary bacterial peritonitis (perforated viscus, ruptured appendix/diverticulum/ulcer, intraabdominal abscess) (Harrison 22e p.1071-1072)","Spontaneous bacterial empyema complicating hepatic hydrothorax (Harrison 22e p.330)","Tuberculous peritonitis (ascitic lymphocytosis; can complicate or mimic infected ascites) (Harrison 22e p.373)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Peritoneal carcinomatosis / malignant ascites (low-SAAG ascites; SBP is rare in malignant ascites) (Harrison 22e p.372-373)","Pancreatic ascites (high ascitic amylase, typically >1000) (Harrison 22e p.373)","Worsening uncomplicated tense ascites with increased girth but no infection (Harrison 22e p.329)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Ascitic fluid PMN/neutrophil count (>=250/microL is diagnostic) (Harrison 22e p.329, 1070)","Ascitic fluid inoculated into blood-culture bottles at the bedside (Harrison 22e p.329, 1070)","Gram stain of ascitic fluid (often negative; does not exclude SBP) (Harrison 22e p.1070)","Serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) and ascitic total protein to characterize the underlying ascites and assess risk (Harrison 22e p.372)","Ascitic glucose and LDH when secondary peritonitis is suspected (Harrison 22e p.373)","Contrast-enhanced abdominal CT to seek an intraabdominal source / exclude secondary peritonitis (Harrison 22e p.1070-1071)","Chest and abdominal radiography to exclude free air (perforation) when abdominal pain is present (Harrison 22e p.1070)","Repeat paracentesis at 48 hours to confirm >=25% fall in ascitic PMN count (Harrison 22e p.329)"],"redFlags":["New or worsening hepatic encephalopathy or altered mental status in a cirrhotic with ascites (Harrison 22e p.329, 1070, 2715)","Unexplained acute kidney injury, jaundice, or nonlocalizing malaise/fatigue in a susceptible patient (Harrison 22e p.1070)","Multiple organisms recovered from ascitic fluid, especially with anaerobes, suggesting secondary peritonitis requiring source control (Harrison 22e p.329, 1071)","Free air under the diaphragm on imaging, signaling perforation (Harrison 22e p.1070)","Concurrent GI/variceal bleeding, which markedly increases SBP frequency (Harrison 22e p.330, 2716)","Failure of the ascitic PMN count to fall, or persistence of WBCs after therapy, indicating treatment failure or an alternative diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.329, 1071)"],"prognosis":"SBP is a common and potentially lethal complication of cirrhotic ascites and carries significant morbidity and mortality, largely because it tends to occur in patients with the most advanced liver disease. In-hospital mortality is roughly 17-25%; one tertiary-center series reported ~23% mortality at 30 days and ~37% at 90 days. Recurrence is high, with up to ~70% of patients having another episode within a year. Patients with PBP usually respond to appropriate antibiotics within about 72 hours. The underlying prognosis of cirrhosis with ascites is itself poor, with under 50% surviving 2 years after ascites onset in some series, so SBP should prompt consideration of liver transplantation. (Harrison 22e p.1071, 2715-2716, 373)","pitfalls":["Diagnosis rests on the ascitic PMN count >=250/microL, not on a positive culture; ascitic cultures are often negative because the organism burden is low (Harrison 22e p.329, 1070).","Recovery of more than one organism (a mixed flora, especially with anaerobes) alongside an elevated PMN count points to SECONDARY peritonitis from a ruptured viscus or abscess, not SBP, and demands a search for a surgical source (Harrison 22e p.329, 1071).","Multiple organisms WITHOUT an elevated PMN count suggest bowel perforation by the paracentesis needle rather than true infection (Harrison 22e p.329).","Failing to perform a diagnostic paracentesis in a decompensated cirrhotic who deteriorates (encephalopathy, AKI, sepsis) misses SBP, since the abdomen may be benign and tenderness is present in only ~40% (Harrison 22e p.329, 2715).","A low ascitic total protein (<1.0-1.5 g/dL) reflects low opsonic activity and identifies patients at higher risk for SBP; this group warrants heightened suspicion (Harrison 22e p.330, 1071).","The 250/microL PMN threshold is specific to SBP and should not be applied to secondary peritonitis or to peritoneal-dialysis peritonitis, where lower leukocyte cutoffs are used (Harrison 22e p.1070, p.2486 context).","Gastric acid suppression (e.g., proton-pump inhibitors) has been associated with increased SBP risk in observational data (Harrison 22e p.1071)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal severity grading system specific to SBP. Risk stratification is framed around predictors of poor outcome and renal injury: higher SBP risk in patients with low ascitic-fluid total protein (<1.0-1.5 g/dL), impaired renal function (creatinine >=1.2 mg/dL, BUN >=25 mg/dL, or serum sodium <=130 mEq/L), and advanced liver failure (Child-Turcotte-Pugh score >=9, bilirubin >=3 mg/dL); these are used to identify candidates for prophylaxis. The greatest benefit of adjunctive albumin is seen in those presenting with serum creatinine >=1 mg/dL, BUN >=30 mg/dL, or total bilirubin >=5 mg/dL. (Harrison 22e p.1071)","references":["Harrison 22e p.329","Harrison 22e p.330","Harrison 22e p.372","Harrison 22e p.373","Harrison 22e p.1070","Harrison 22e p.1071","Harrison 22e p.1072","Harrison 22e p.2715","Harrison 22e p.2716"],"crossLinks":["decomp_cirrhosis","hepatic_enceph","variceal_bleed"]},"SCRUB_TYPHUS":{"name":"Scrub Typhus","system":"Tropical Fever","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1035, p.1455-1456, p.1460, p.3773","clinicalPearls":["The classic triad of an inoculation eschar at the chigger feeding site, regional lymphadenopathy, and a maculopapular rash is the textbook description, but these signs are seldom seen in indigenous (local endemic) patients (Harrison 22e p.1460).","Among returning travelers, fewer than 50% develop an eschar and fewer than 40% develop a rash; when a rash does appear it typically emerges on day 4-6 of illness (Harrison 22e p.1460).","Eschar formation occurs at the site of mite/chigger feeding and may develop during the incubation period; an eschar is a useful diagnostic clue that should prompt consideration of scrub typhus (Harrison 22e p.1455).","Onset after a 6-21 day incubation is characterized by fever, headache, myalgia, cough, and gastrointestinal symptoms; some patients recover spontaneously after a few days while others progress to severe disease (Harrison 22e p.1460).","Chigger bites may be asymptomatic, so the absence of a recalled bite does not exclude the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.3773).","Immunity to scrub typhus is short-lived: protection against the homologous strain wanes over 1-3 years, and the organism's marked antigenic diversity means cross-protective immunity can be lost in as little as 1 month, so reinfection is possible (Harrison 22e p.1460)."],"pathophysiology":"Scrub typhus is caused by Orientia tsutsugamushi, an obligate intracellular organism placed in a separate genus within the family Rickettsiaceae. It differs substantially from Rickettsia species both genetically and in cell-wall composition, notably lacking lipopolysaccharide. Disease results from infection of vascular endothelial cells with increased vascular permeability (a mechanism shared across the rickettsioses). The organism is maintained in nature by transovarial transmission within trombiculid mites; infected larval mites (chiggers, the only mite stage that feeds on a vertebrate host) inoculate the organism into the skin during feeding. Severe disease reflects widespread vascular injury, producing the encephalitis and interstitial pneumonia that characterize advanced cases. Closely related Orientia species (O. chuto, Candidatus O. chiloensis) cause scrub typhus-like illness in newly recognized foci (Harrison 22e p.1455-1456, p.1460, p.3773).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Other rickettsioses presenting with eschar, rash, fever and headache (e.g., Mediterranean spotted fever, African tick-bite fever, rickettsialpox, murine and epidemic typhus) (Harrison 22e p.1455-1456).","Atypical/community-acquired pneumonia, given the cough and dyspnea of pulmonary involvement (Harrison 22e p.1126).","Other undifferentiated tropical febrile illnesses with thrombocytopenia and transaminitis."],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serology is the mainstay of laboratory diagnosis: indirect fluorescent antibody, indirect immunoperoxidase, enzyme immunoassays, and rapid lateral-flow chromatographic tests (Harrison 22e p.1460).","PCR amplification of Orientia genes is effective when performed on eschar material, but is less sensitive on blood (Harrison 22e p.1460).","Platelet count: thrombocytopenia is a characteristic laboratory finding in scrub typhus (as in other rickettsioses) (Harrison 22e p.1455).","WBC count is typically normal or low (Harrison 22e p.1455).","Serum sodium: hyponatremia is a recognized associated finding across the rickettsioses (Harrison 22e p.1455).","Hepatic aminotransferases: mild to moderate elevation is commonly seen (Harrison 22e p.1455)."],"redFlags":["Altered mental status / encephalitis (meningoencephalitis is a feature of severe disease) (Harrison 22e p.1035, p.1455).","Interstitial pneumonia / pneumonitis with dyspnea or progression toward respiratory failure (Harrison 22e p.1035, p.1460).","Myocarditis (Harrison 22e p.1035).","Signs of disseminated intravascular coagulation or hemorrhage (Harrison 22e p.1035).","Renal failure (Harrison 22e p.1035).","Progressive hypotension and multiorgan failure (a severe-disease pattern shared by the rickettsioses) (Harrison 22e p.1455)."],"prognosis":"Illness ranges from mild and self-limiting to fatal. The case-fatality rate for untreated classic cases is about 6% and would likely be lower if all mild cases were captured; overall reported mortality varies widely by location (cited as 1% to 70%). Risk of death rises with increasing age and with the presence of myocarditis, delirium, pneumonitis, or signs of hemorrhage. Recognized in time, the disease responds well to appropriate antibiotic therapy (Harrison 22e p.1035, p.1460).","pitfalls":["Relying on the classic triad: eschar, rash, and lymphadenopathy are frequently absent (eschar <50% and rash <40% of travelers; signs seldom seen in indigenous patients), so a negative skin exam does not exclude scrub typhus (Harrison 22e p.1460).","Expecting a recalled bite: chigger bites may be asymptomatic and the mites are tiny and rarely noticed (Harrison 22e p.3773).","Assuming geographic exclusion: although classically eastern/southern Asia, northern Australia, and Pacific/Indian Ocean islands, emerging and reemerging foci (e.g., Chile, parts of South/Central America, Africa, Arabian peninsula via related Orientia species) challenge the traditional epidemiology (Harrison 22e p.1460, p.3773).","Note that PCR on blood is less sensitive than PCR on eschar material (Harrison 22e p.1460).","Some Thai isolates are poorly responsive to doxycycline or chloramphenicol (Harrison 22e p.1460)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal severity score; it contrasts mild/self-limiting illness with severe disease. Severe scrub typhus is marked by encephalitis/meningoencephalitis and interstitial pneumonia due to vascular injury, and may progress to myocarditis, DIC, renal failure, hypotension, and multiorgan failure (Harrison 22e p.1035, p.1455, p.1460).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1035","Harrison 22e p.1455","Harrison 22e p.1456","Harrison 22e p.1460","Harrison 22e p.3773"],"crossLinks":["RICKETTSIAL_FEVER","LEPTOSPIROSIS","DENGUE","MALARIA","ENTERIC_FEVER"]},"SEPSIS":{"name":"Sepsis","system":"Systemic","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"2310-2324 (Harrison 22e; principal sepsis chapter 315, p.2316-2324; shock chapter 314, p.2310-2316; ICU scoring chapter 311, p.2291-2292)","clinicalPearls":["Sepsis-3 (2016) reframes sepsis as life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection, not merely a systemic inflammatory state; the older SIRS-based criteria and the term 'severe sepsis' were discarded for poor specificity (p.2316).","Operationally, sepsis is identified by an acute rise of >=2 points in the SOFA score over baseline in a patient with suspected or confirmed infection (p.2320).","Septic shock is the subset of sepsis with persistent hypotension needing vasopressors to keep MAP >65 mmHg PLUS lactate >2 mmol/L despite adequate fluid resuscitation, reflecting circulatory, cellular, and metabolic derangement that markedly raises mortality (p.2310, 2320).","qSOFA (>=2 of: respiratory rate >=22/min, altered mentation/GCS <15, systolic BP <=100 mmHg) flags infected patients at high risk of poor outcome; it is more specific but less sensitive than SIRS and was NOT designed or endorsed as a standalone diagnostic/screening test for sepsis (p.2292, 2320).","There is NO gold-standard diagnostic test for sepsis; the diagnosis is a synthesis of epidemiologic, clinical, laboratory, imaging, and microbiologic data (p.2320).","Presentation is frequently nonspecific (fever, tachycardia, lethargy, myalgias) and may lack localizing features; the CNS, kidneys, and skin act as accessible 'windows' onto early organ hypoperfusion (altered mentation, oliguria, mottling/delayed capillary refill) (p.2320, 2312).","Hypotension or an isolated low BP reading does not by itself define sepsis/shock; interpret BP against the patient's baseline and look for evidence of organ hypoperfusion, since hypertensive patients can decompensate at 'normal' pressures (p.2312).","Absence of tachycardia does not exclude sepsis: nodal-blocking drugs or conduction disease can blunt the compensatory heart-rate response (p.2312).","Sepsis is the commonest cause of distributive (high-cardiac-output, low-SVR) shock and of high-output hypotension overall; warm extremities with a wide pulse pressure suggest this physiology (p.2310, 2293).","Lactate is central to risk-stratifying sepsis: elevation reflects tissue hypoperfusion/anaerobic metabolism, correlates with worse outcome, and serial measurements help track response to resuscitation (p.2313).","Most sepsis is community-onset (~88%); the urinary tract is the single most common primary site, followed by respiratory, intra-abdominal, and skin/soft-tissue sources (p.2316).","Hospital-onset sepsis tends to involve sicker, more comorbid patients with more intra-abdominal infection and bacteremia from Enterococcus, Candida, and Pseudomonas, and carries higher ICU admission and mortality (p.2317).","Sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy (reduced LVEF, dilated LV, RV impairment) affects up to half of septic-shock patients and can convert initial distributive shock into a mixed distributive-cardiogenic picture (p.2311, 2322)."],"pathophysiology":"Sepsis arises when an infection provokes a dysregulated, self-amplifying host response rather than a contained local one. Pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs, e.g., LPS, bacterial cell-wall components, microbial nucleic acids) and damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs, e.g., histones, HMGB1, S100 proteins, mitochondrial/double-stranded DNA, ATP released from injured cells) are sensed by pattern-recognition receptors (TLRs, CLRs, RAGE, RIG-I, NOD-like receptors) on innate immune and parenchymal cells. This triggers exuberant release of cytokines and chemokines and upregulation of vascular adhesion molecules. Myeloid cells (neutrophils, monocytes, M1 macrophages, dendritic cells) drive injury through phagocytosis, degranulation, reactive oxygen/nitrogen species, and neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs), while emergency granulopoiesis releases immature neutrophils (a poor-prognosis marker). Lymphoid responses are dysregulated, with lymphocyte apoptosis, exhaustion, and myeloid-derived suppressor cells producing a state of immunosuppression that predisposes survivors to secondary infection. The endothelium is activated and injured: glycocalyx breakdown, loss of VE-cadherin/tight junctions, and apoptosis cause capillary leak, interstitial edema, and a widened oxygen-diffusion distance. A prothrombotic shift (increased tissue factor, vWF, PAI-1; depleted antithrombin, tissue factor pathway inhibitor, and protein C; complement activation with C3a/C5a and the C5b-9 membrane attack complex) generates microvascular thrombi and, once clotting factors and platelets are consumed, bleeding. Excess nitric oxide drives systemic vasodilation. The net result is impaired oxygen delivery, uptake, and utilization; even when oxygen delivery is adequate, mitochondrial and metabolic dysfunction impairs ATP generation and ion homeostasis, producing multiorgan cellular injury and dysfunction (p.2317-2319).","additionalDifferentials":["Sepsis mimics, retrospectively found in ~25% of ICU 'sepsis' admissions; continuous reevaluation for an alternative diagnosis is imperative when infection is not confirmed (p.2320)","Distributive shock from non-septic causes (e.g., neurogenic shock from brain/spinal-cord injury disrupting vascular autonomic tone) (p.2311)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Noninfectious inflammatory febrile syndromes - connective tissue diseases and vasculitides (p.2320)","Heart failure and other noninfectious causes of pneumonitis or lung injury (the commonest mimic category in one ICU series was cardiovascular, e.g., heart failure, cardiac arrest) (p.2320)","Noninfectious abdominal syndromes - mesenteric ischemia and inflammatory bowel disease (p.2320)","Acute pancreatitis (a recognized noninfectious cause of distributive/high-output shock) (p.2293, 2311)","Adrenal insufficiency / adrenal crisis (distributive shock from cortisol/aldosterone deficiency, unmasked by stress of infection or surgery) (p.2293, 2311)","Severe burns (distributive shock with cellular hypoxia from reduced SVR) (p.2311)","Acute liver failure (high-output hypotension) (p.2293, 2311)","Anaphylaxis (IgE-mediated distributive shock with rapid plasma extravasation) (p.2311)","Thyrotoxicosis and large peripheral arteriovenous shunts (other causes of high-output hypotension) (p.2293)","Hypovolemia and autonomic dysfunction as noninfectious causes of hypotension (p.2320)","Pulmonary embolism / obstructive shock (acute chest pain, dyspnea, hypoxia in the immobile or malignancy patient) (p.2313)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum procalcitonin / point-of-care pathogen testing where available to support diagnosis (p.2320)","Repeat/serial lactate to gauge response to resuscitation, not just a single value (p.2313)","Arterial (or venous) blood gas for acid-base assessment; arterial sampling if hypoxemia present (p.2313)","Central venous oxygen saturation (ScvO2) as a surrogate of oxygen-delivery adequacy in refractory shock (p.2313)","Echocardiography / point-of-care ultrasound for sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy, volume responsiveness, and to exclude mimics (tamponade, PE, pulmonary edema, pneumothorax) (p.2293-2294, 2322)","EEG, brain imaging, or lumbar puncture if seizure, stroke, or meningoencephalitis is suspected in a septic patient with neurologic findings (p.2321-2322)","Coagulation profile / DIC and sepsis-induced-coagulopathy (SIC) markers - platelets, PT/INR, fibrinogen, fibrin split products (p.2323)","Abdominal ultrasound in the septic patient with elevated bilirubin to look for biliary obstruction, cholecystitis, or cholangitis (p.2323)"],"redFlags":["qSOFA >=2 (RR >=22, altered mentation, SBP <=100) in suspected infection - identifies the most acutely ill subset with higher mortality and ICU need (p.2292, 2320)","Persistent hypotension requiring vasopressors plus lactate >2 mmol/L after adequate fluids = septic shock, with mortality up to ~40% (p.2310, 2316)","New altered mental status / encephalopathy or new oliguria as early signs that compensatory mechanisms are exhausted (p.2312)","Skin mottling, prolonged capillary refill, cool clammy skin - hypoperfusion (p.2312, 2322)","Every hour of delay in appropriate antibiotics in bacterial septic shock raises mortality (estimated 7-8% per hour after shock recognition) - empiric antibiotics within 1 h of shock recognition (p.2320-2321)","An infection source needing urgent control (intra-abdominal abscess, bowel perforation, cholangitis, obstructed/infected urinary tract, necrotizing soft-tissue infection, infected indwelling catheter/endovascular infection) - source control as fast as possible (p.2321)","Failure to confirm infection should prompt active search for a mimic rather than indefinite empiric antibiotics (antibiotic stewardship: stop antibiotics when sepsis is excluded) (p.2315, 2320)"],"prognosis":"Overall in-hospital mortality is roughly 15% among hospitalized adults with sepsis, rising to up to ~40% with septic shock; globally an estimated ~11 million sepsis-related deaths occur yearly (about one in five deaths worldwide). Mortality climbs with age and is higher in men. Elevated SOFA scores and machine-learning-defined phenotypes track population-level outcome (e.g., alpha/beta/gamma/delta phenotypes with ~2%/5%/15%/32% mortality) but cannot reliably predict an individual patient's outcome. Survivorship carries a heavy long-term burden: physical (prolonged fatigue, muscle loss, weakness, reduced functional capacity), cognitive (cognitive decline, dementia), and psychological (depression, reduced quality of life) sequelae, plus increased risk of cardiovascular events, recurrent infection, and readmission. About half of survivors are rehospitalized within a year and roughly one in six die within the first year, with most post-sepsis deaths in the first 6 months and elevated death risk persisting up to ~2 years (p.2316-2317, 2324). No FDA-approved host-targeted/immunomodulatory therapy exists for sepsis (p.2324).","pitfalls":["Treating sepsis as a single pathogen rather than a syndrome - empiric therapy and workup must be driven by the most likely source (p.2316).","Relying on SIRS criteria or a single abnormal vital sign to rule sepsis in or out - poor specificity led to their abandonment in Sepsis-3 (p.2316).","Using qSOFA as a diagnostic test - it is a risk-stratification/screening prompt, not a diagnostic criterion (p.2292, 2320).","Being falsely reassured by a normal heart rate (beta-blockade/conduction disease) or by a low BP reading in a chronically hypotensive patient (p.2312).","Anchoring on 'sepsis' when infection is unconfirmed - up to a quarter of presumed-sepsis ICU cases are mimics; failure to reevaluate delays the correct diagnosis (p.2320).","Continuing broad-spectrum antibiotics once infection is excluded, undermining stewardship and risking C. difficile, allergy, and AKI (p.2315, 2321).","Over-resuscitation with fluid - excess volume is likely harmful; targeted, dynamically reassessed resuscitation is the goal (p.2294).","Attributing all altered mentation to sepsis-associated encephalopathy without excluding seizure, stroke, or CNS infection (p.2321-2322).","Missing sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy, which can turn distributive shock into mixed shock and change management (p.2311, 2322)."],"severityClassification":"Sepsis-3 (Singer et al., JAMA 2016) is the current framework: (1) Infection (suspected or confirmed) without organ dysfunction is not sepsis. (2) Sepsis = suspected/confirmed infection with an acute increase of >=2 points in the SOFA score from baseline (life-threatening organ dysfunction from a dysregulated host response). The SOFA score grades six systems 0-4: respiratory (PaO2/FiO2), coagulation (platelets), liver (bilirubin), cardiovascular (MAP / vasopressor requirement), CNS (Glasgow Coma Scale), and renal (creatinine / urine output). (3) Septic shock = sepsis with vasopressor requirement to maintain MAP >65 mmHg AND serum lactate >2 mmol/L despite adequate fluid resuscitation. qSOFA (>=2 of RR >=22/min, altered mentation, SBP <=100 mmHg) is a rapid bedside risk-stratification prompt for poor outcome in suspected infection, not a diagnostic criterion. The prior 1991/1992 SIRS-based definitions and the term 'severe sepsis' are obsolete under Sepsis-3 (p.2292, 2316, 2320).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2292","Harrison 22e p.2293","Harrison 22e p.2310","Harrison 22e p.2311","Harrison 22e p.2312","Harrison 22e p.2313","Harrison 22e p.2316","Harrison 22e p.2317","Harrison 22e p.2318","Harrison 22e p.2319","Harrison 22e p.2320","Harrison 22e p.2321","Harrison 22e p.2322","Harrison 22e p.2323","Harrison 22e p.2324"],"crossLinks":["SEPTIC_SHOCK","FEBRILE_NEUTROPENIA","adrenal_crisis","dic","anaphylaxis","cardiogenic_shock"]},"SEPTIC_SHOCK":{"name":"Septic Shock","system":"Systemic","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e: Ch.315 Sepsis and Septic Shock p.2316-2321; Ch.314 Approach to the Patient with Shock p.2353-2358","clinicalPearls":["Septic shock under Sepsis-3 is a subset of sepsis with profound circulatory, cellular, and metabolic derangements that carry a higher mortality than sepsis alone; sepsis itself is life-threatening organ dysfunction from a dysregulated host response to infection (p.2316).","Sepsis-3 abandoned the older SIRS criteria (used by Sepsis-1/2) because of poor specificity for distinguishing infection from other inflammatory states, and eliminated the term 'severe sepsis' (p.2316).","Sepsis is operationalized as an acute rise of at least 2 points in the SOFA score from baseline in a patient with suspected or confirmed infection (p.2320).","qSOFA (respiratory rate >=22/min, altered mentation with GCS <15, systolic BP <=100 mmHg) is a rapid bedside screen; a score >=2 is more specific but less sensitive than SIRS and flags patients at higher risk of death, ICU admission, and longer stay (p.2313, p.2320).","Sepsis presentation can be nonspecific (fever, tachycardia, lethargy, myalgias) with or without localizing findings; there is no single gold-standard diagnostic test, so the diagnosis integrates clinical, laboratory, microbiologic, and imaging data (p.2320).","Approximately 88% of sepsis cases are community onset (detected within 48 h of admission); hospital-onset sepsis (after 48 h) tends to involve more comorbidities, intraabdominal infection, and Enterococcus/Candida/Pseudomonas, with higher ICU admission and mortality (p.2316-2317).","Among culture-positive community-onset sepsis, the most common primary sites are urinary tract (~49%), respiratory tract (~33%), intraabdominal (~14%), and skin/soft tissue (~10%) (p.2316).","About 53% of US sepsis cases are bacterial culture positive with a roughly even gram-positive / gram-negative split; common gram-positives are S. aureus, Streptococcus, and Enterococcus, and common gram-negatives are E. coli, Klebsiella, and P. aeruginosa (p.2316)."],"pathophysiology":"Septic shock is the most common form of distributive shock, in which the primary hemodynamic derangement is a fall in systemic vascular resistance (vasoplegia) rather than a primary loss of preload or pump function. The vasodilatory profile is distinctive: SVR is reduced, cardiac output is typically increased as a compensatory response, and both central venous pressure (CVP) and pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) tend to be low (p.2353-2354). Arterial and venous vasodilation is mediated in part by excess nitric oxide; vasopressin deficiency may also contribute to refractory hypotension. The dysregulated host response begins when pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs, e.g. LPS, microbial nucleic acids) and damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs, e.g. histones, HMGB1, mitochondrial DNA, ATP) are sensed by pattern-recognition receptors (TLRs, CLRs, RAGE, RIG-I, NOD receptors) on neutrophils, monocytes/macrophages, and parenchymal cells, triggering proinflammatory cytokine/chemokine release and upregulation of endothelial adhesion molecules. Activated neutrophils release reactive oxygen species and neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs); endothelial activation/injury, glycocalyx degradation, and loss of VE-cadherin tight junctions cause capillary leak, interstitial edema, and increased oxygen-diffusion distance. A prothrombotic state arises from tissue-factor-driven coagulation, platelet/vWF microthrombi, complement activation (C3a, C5a, C5b-9 membrane attack complex), reduced anticoagulants (antithrombin, tissue factor pathway inhibitor, activated protein C), and impaired fibrinolysis (elevated PAI-1); consumption of clotting factors and platelets can then produce hemorrhage. The net effect is hypotension (from vasodilation, myocardial depression, and hypovolemia), microcirculatory dysfunction, and mitochondrial/metabolic failure, so that oxygen delivery, uptake, and utilization are all impaired and cellular injury occurs even when bulk oxygen delivery appears adequate (p.2317-2320). Sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy can add a cardiogenic component, producing mixed shock (p.2355).","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of distributive shock: pancreatitis, severe burns, liver failure, anaphylaxis, neurogenic shock from brain/spinal cord injury, and adrenal insufficiency/adrenal crisis (p.2353-2354)","Cardiogenic shock (myocardial infarction, myocarditis, arrhythmia, acute valvular failure) (p.2353-2354)","Hypovolemic shock (hemorrhagic from trauma or GI bleeding; nonhemorrhagic from vomiting, diarrhea, osmotic diuresis in DKA or diabetes insipidus, or skin loss) (p.2353-2354)","Obstructive shock (tension pneumothorax, cardiac tamponade, constrictive pericarditis, pulmonary embolism, air or fat embolism, aortic dissection) (p.2353-2354)","Mixed shock, where more than one mechanism coexists (e.g. distributive sepsis plus sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy) (p.2355)"],"infectionMimics":["Noninfectious pneumonitis or lung injury that mimics pneumonia/respiratory sepsis (p.2320)","Noninfectious abdominal syndromes such as mesenteric ischemia and inflammatory bowel disease, which can mimic an intraabdominal source (p.2320)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Noninfectious inflammatory febrile syndromes including connective tissue diseases and vasculitides (p.2320)","Heart failure and cardiac arrest (the most common cardiovascular mimics in ICU series, where ~25% of presumed septic patients were later judged to have a mimic) (p.2320)","Noninfectious chronic obstructive pulmonary disease exacerbation (common respiratory mimic) (p.2320)","Hypotension from noninfectious causes: hypovolemia, autonomic dysfunction, and adrenal insufficiency (p.2320)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Complete blood count with differential, basic metabolic panel, and liver function tests to assess end-organ dysfunction (p.2320)","Coagulation panel (PT/PTT/INR, platelets) to detect sepsis-induced coagulopathy/DIC (p.2320, p.2356)","Cultures of blood and other potentially infected sites (urine, sputum, wound) ideally before antimicrobials, plus point-of-care pathogen testing when available (p.2320)","Urinalysis and urine sediment to evaluate for pyuria/urinary source (p.2356, p.2320)","Cardiac enzymes/troponin to detect a primary or contributory cardiac process (p.2356)","Venous or arterial blood gas (arterial if hypoxemia present) to quantify acidosis (p.2356)","Focused imaging (chest x-ray, CT, or ultrasound) of suspected sites; point-of-care ultrasound (POCUS, e.g. RUSH/ACES protocols) helps classify undifferentiated and mixed shock (p.2356)","SOFA score components for severity (GCS, MAP/vasopressor use, PaO2/FiO2, bilirubin, creatinine, platelets) (p.2317)"],"redFlags":["Persistent hypotension requiring vasopressors to keep MAP >65 mmHg plus lactate >2 mmol/L despite adequate fluid resuscitation defines septic shock and marks the highest-mortality end of the spectrum (p.2320, p.2354)","Acute rise of >=2 SOFA points from baseline with suspected infection indicates new organ dysfunction (p.2320)","qSOFA >=2 (RR>=22, GCS<15, SBP<=100) signals high risk of death and ICU need (p.2313, p.2320)","Elevated and rising serial lactate, which correlates with worse outcome and reflects tissue hypoperfusion (p.2356)","Sepsis-associated encephalopathy (altered consciousness, cognition, or behavior) signaling CNS organ dysfunction (p.2319)","New oliguria / acute kidney injury, occurring in up to two-thirds of septic patients (p.2319)","Signs of coagulopathy/DIC with consumption of platelets and clotting factors and bleeding risk (p.2319)"],"prognosis":"Septic shock carries up to ~40% mortality, far higher than the ~15% overall mortality among US hospitalized adults with sepsis (p.2316). In emergency-department shock cohorts, septic and cardiogenic shock have the highest 90-day mortality (reported ~56% and ~52% respectively), and septic shock predominates among ICU shock (~62% of ICU shock patients in one multicenter series), whereas hypovolemic shock has lower mortality (p.2355). Mortality rises with age and is higher in men; comorbidities such as diabetes, obesity, neurologic/respiratory/cardiac disease, renal or hepatic insufficiency, cancer, and immunosuppression worsen outcomes (p.2317). SOFA scores predict mortality at the population level but not for individual patients; proposed clinical 'phenotypes' (alpha/beta/gamma/delta) carried roughly 2%, 5%, 15%, and 32% mortality (p.2317). Survivors face substantial long-term sequelae: physical (fatigue, muscle loss, weakness), cognitive and psychological (cognitive decline, dementia, depression), and increased risk of cardiovascular events, recurrent infection, and readmission. Roughly half of survivors are rehospitalized within 1 year and about one in six die within the first year, with most post-sepsis deaths in the first 6 months and elevated risk persisting up to 2 years (p.2321).","pitfalls":["Relying on SIRS criteria to define or exclude sepsis: SIRS lacks specificity, which is why Sepsis-3 replaced it (p.2316).","Treating shock as a single entity: distributive (septic) shock has low SVR with high cardiac output and low filling pressures, the opposite hemodynamic profile of cardiogenic and hypovolemic shock, so misclassification leads to wrong resuscitation (p.2353-2354).","Assuming a single shock type: sepsis frequently coexists with a cardiogenic component (sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy) or hypovolemia, producing mixed shock that POCUS can help dissect (p.2355).","A normal hemoglobin/hematocrit early in concurrent hemorrhage, or a normal WBC, does not exclude the relevant process; leukocytosis is suggestive but not diagnostic of infection (p.2356).","Anchoring on sepsis when infection is not confirmed: about 25% of presumed septic ICU patients had a mimic, so alternative diagnoses must be continually reconsidered (p.2320).","Failure to identify and control the source (e.g. infected catheter, abscess, endocarditis) undermines management even with appropriate antibiotics (p.2321)."],"severityClassification":"Sepsis-3 spectrum: (1) Sepsis = life-threatening organ dysfunction from a dysregulated host response to infection, operationalized as an acute increase of >=2 points in the SOFA score in a patient with suspected/confirmed infection. (2) Septic shock = a subset of sepsis with profound circulatory, cellular, and metabolic abnormalities and higher mortality, operationally defined as a septic patient who, despite adequate fluid resuscitation, requires vasopressors to maintain MAP >65 mmHg AND has serum lactate >2 mmol/L. qSOFA (RR>=22, GCS<15, SBP<=100; score >=2) is a rapid risk-stratification screen, not a diagnosis. The historical Sepsis-1/2 terms 'severe sepsis' and SIRS-based septic shock (SBP<90 or drop >=40 mmHg) have been superseded (p.2316, p.2320).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2316","Harrison 22e p.2317","Harrison 22e p.2318","Harrison 22e p.2319","Harrison 22e p.2320","Harrison 22e p.2321","Harrison 22e p.2353","Harrison 22e p.2354","Harrison 22e p.2355","Harrison 22e p.2356"],"crossLinks":["SEPSIS","cardiogenic_shock","hypovolemic_shock","anaphylaxis","dic","pancreatitis"]},"SEVERE_CAP":{"name":"Severe Community Acquired Pneumonia","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1023-1029","clinicalPearls":["Neither the Pneumonia Severity Index (PSI) nor CURB-65 accurately determines the need for ICU admission; they predict mortality/site of care but not ICU need (p.1026).","Patients with septic shock requiring vasopressors or acute respiratory failure requiring intubation and mechanical ventilation meet a major criterion and should be admitted directly to an ICU (p.1026).","Presence of three or more of the nine minor severe-CAP criteria warrants admission to an ICU or high-level monitoring unit (p.1026).","Late or delayed ICU admission is associated with increased mortality; less-ill patients placed on a general ward who then deteriorate fare worse than equally ill patients monitored in the ICU from the outset (p.1026).","In ICU-admitted CAP specifically, treatment directed at an identified pathogen may be superior to empirical therapy, so etiologic workup carries more weight in severe disease (p.1025).","The microbial spectrum of severe (ICU) CAP differs from milder CAP: Legionella tends to cause more serious disease, and S. aureus and gram-negative bacilli become more prominent (p.1024).","Prior respiratory-tract isolation of MRSA or P. aeruginosa within the past year is a stronger (more robust) risk factor for these pathogens than recent hospitalization plus parenteral antibiotic exposure (p.1028).","Procalcitonin (PCT) is insufficiently accurate to diagnose bacterial CAP and must NOT be used as a basis for withholding initial antibiotics; CRP is even less sensitive than PCT (p.1026).","If only a viral pathogen (e.g., influenza or SARS-CoV-2) is found and no bacterial pathogen is evident, antibacterial therapy can be stopped — BUT in severe illness, bacterial-viral co-infection must still be considered (p.1028).","Gross hemoptysis suggests necrotizing pneumonia, e.g., due to CA-MRSA (p.1024).","In the elderly, severe CAP may present atypically as new-onset or worsening confusion or decompensation of a chronic illness with few classic respiratory findings (p.1025).","Recent data show a mortality benefit for corticosteroids in severe CAP, particularly when there is a high level of systemic inflammation (CRP >15 mg/dL) (p.1029)."],"pathophysiology":"Pneumonia results from proliferation of microbial pathogens in the alveoli combined with the host inflammatory response. Organisms reach the lower respiratory tract by inhalation, microaspiration of oropharyngeal flora, or direct mucosal spread; mechanical defenses (nasal hairs/turbinates, the branching airway, mucociliary clearance, gag and cough reflexes) are insufficient to fully exclude bacteria, and the lung is now understood to harbor a microbiota rather than being sterile. An inflammatory/epithelial-endothelial injury event releases cytokines, chemokines, and catecholamines that can selectively promote growth of organisms such as S. pneumoniae and P. aeruginosa, generating a positive-feedback loop; in CAP the trigger is often a viral infection compounded by microaspiration. Inflammatory mediators (IL-6, TNF) cause fever, while IL-8 and G-CSF recruit neutrophils; mediators from macrophages and neutrophils produce alveolar-capillary leak with impaired oxygenation, hypoxemia, and radiographic infiltrates. Some pathogens interfere with hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction, worsening hypoxemia. Reduced compliance from capillary leak, increased respiratory drive and secretions, and intrapulmonary shunting drive worsening dyspnea, and when severe, reductions in lung volume/compliance and shunting can precipitate respiratory failure. Pathologically, classic (lobar pneumococcal) pneumonia evolves through edema, red hepatization, gray hepatization, and resolution phases.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute bronchitis","Exacerbation of chronic bronchitis","Heart failure / pulmonary edema","Pulmonary embolism","Lung carcinoma (including postobstructive pneumonia)","Tuberculosis (e.g., upper-lobe cavitating lesion)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis","Endemic/invasive fungal pneumonia","Pneumocystis pneumonia (alveolar process despite interstitial radiographic appearance)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pulmonary edema","Pulmonary embolism / pulmonary infarction","Lung carcinoma","Radiation pneumonitis","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Connective tissue disease involving the lungs"],"additionalInvestigations":["CT chest (for suspected loculated effusion, cavitary disease, or postobstructive pneumonia from tumor/foreign body)","Deep-suction aspirate or bronchoalveolar lavage in intubated ICU patients (high culture yield when sent promptly)","Multiplex respiratory PCR / nasopharyngeal swab PCR (influenza, coronaviruses; also Legionella, M. pneumoniae, C. pneumoniae, mycobacteria)","MRSA rapid nasal PCR (to guide adding/de-escalating MRSA coverage)","Diagnostic pleural fluid sampling (parapneumonic effusion: drainage indicated if pH <7.2, glucose <2.2 mmol/L, LDH >1000 U/L, or organisms seen/cultured)","Stains/cultures for M. tuberculosis and fungi in selected cases","Bronchoscopy in delayed responders or worsening disease"],"redFlags":["Septic shock requiring vasopressors (major criterion - direct ICU admission)","Acute respiratory failure requiring invasive mechanical ventilation (major criterion - direct ICU admission)","Three or more minor severe-CAP criteria (warrants ICU/high-level monitoring)","O2 saturation <92% on room air (mandates hospitalization)","Multiorgan failure","Multilobar infiltrates or cavitation on imaging (increased severity)","Gross hemoptysis (suggests necrotizing pneumonia, e.g., CA-MRSA)","Failure to improve / deterioration by ~day 3 of therapy"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on age, comorbidities, and site of treatment. For hospitalized CAP, overall mortality ranges from 12% to 40%, the higher end reflecting ICU-level disease and care-process factors such as timely administration of appropriate antibiotics (vs <5% for outpatients). CURB-65 scores >=3 carry an overall ~22% mortality and may indicate ICU admission; PSI class 5 has a ~29.2% mortality. CAP is a leading infectious cause of death in patients >65 years; about 18% of hospitalized CAP patients are readmitted within a month, and recent data show 1-year mortality after CAP (especially in older patients) exceeds 30-day mortality. Severe-CAP complications (respiratory failure, shock, multiorgan failure) and pneumonia-associated cardiovascular events worsen outcomes.","pitfalls":["Using PSI or CURB-65 to decide ICU admission - neither is accurate for that purpose (p.1026).","Withholding initial antibiotics based on a low procalcitonin - PCT is too inaccurate to exclude bacterial CAP (p.1026).","Assuming hospitalization alone is a significant risk factor for MRSA or P. aeruginosa - it is no longer considered so; severity plus specific risk factors drive coverage decisions (p.1028).","Requiring all three drug-resistance risk factors (recent hospitalization, recent oral/IV antibiotics, local validation) before considering resistant pathogens - any can apply (p.1028).","Stopping antibacterials when a virus is identified in a severely ill patient without considering bacterial-viral co-infection (p.1028).","Attributing failure to improve to the wrong cause: consider resistant or unexpected pathogen (CA-MRSA, M. tuberculosis, fungus), a sequestered focus (lung abscess, empyema), wrong drug/dose, or nosocomial superinfection (p.1029).","Routinely covering anaerobes in aspiration-related severe CAP - unnecessary unless poor dentition, lung abscess, or necrotizing pneumonia (p.1027)."],"severityClassification":"IDSA/ATS severe-CAP criteria (Harrison 22e Table 131-3, p.1026). MAJOR criteria (either qualifies for ICU): (1) respiratory failure requiring invasive mechanical ventilation; (2) septic shock requiring vasopressors. MINOR criteria (>=3 warrant ICU/high-level monitoring): respiratory rate >=30 breaths/min; PaO2/FiO2 ratio <=250; multilobar infiltrates; confusion/disorientation; uremia (BUN >=20 mg/dL); leukopenia (WBC <4000/uL); thrombocytopenia (platelets <100,000/uL); hypothermia (core temperature <36 C); and hypotension requiring aggressive fluid resuscitation. Prognostic tools PSI (5 mortality classes, class 5 ~29.2%) and CURB-65 (confusion, urea >7 mmol/L, RR >=30, SBP <=90 or DBP <=60 mmHg, age >=65; score >=3 ~22% mortality) assess severity/site of care but are NOT accurate for determining ICU need.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1023","Harrison 22e p.1024","Harrison 22e p.1025","Harrison 22e p.1026","Harrison 22e p.1027","Harrison 22e p.1028","Harrison 22e p.1029"],"crossLinks":["CAP","HAP","VAP","SEPSIS","SEPTIC_SHOCK","ASPIRATION_PNEUMONIA","LUNG_ABSCESS","pleural_effusion","PULMONARY_TB"]},"SINUSITIS":{"name":"Acute Sinusitis (Rhinosinusitis)","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e pp. 296, 298-299 (Sinus Symptoms / Acute Sinusitis); orbital complications p. 276","clinicalPearls":["The vast majority of acute sinusitis cases are caused by respiratory viruses; fewer than 2% of episodes are bacterial, yet antibiotics are prescribed at over 70% of office visits, making sinusitis a leading driver of unnecessary antibiotic use in adults (p. 299).","By guideline criteria, no more than about 50% of adults (and probably closer to 20%) with sinusitis actually meet thresholds for antibiotic prescribing (p. 299).","Symptoms are classified by duration: acute (<4 weeks), subacute (4-12 weeks), and chronic (>=12 weeks) (p. 298).","Common features are purulent nasal discharge, facial congestion/fullness, and facial pain or pressure; additional symptoms include fever, hyposmia or anosmia, ear pressure/pain, postnasal drip, halitosis, maxillary toothache, cough, and fatigue (p. 299).","Risk factors include age 45-65 years, smoking, asthma, air travel, and allergies (p. 299).","For an individual patient with a nonspecific upper respiratory illness, an antibiotic is more likely to cause an adverse reaction than to prevent a complication such as bacterial sinusitis (p. 296).","Recurrent acute sinusitis is defined as four or more episodes of acute sinusitis in a year with no signs or symptoms between episodes (p. 299).","Nosocomial sinusitis occurs in critically ill patients, often those who are nasotracheally intubated, and should be suspected in hospitalized patients with fever lacking another identifiable source (p. 299)."],"pathophysiology":"Sinusitis is inflammation of the paranasal sinuses; when the nasal passages are also involved the term rhinosinusitis is used. Most acute disease begins as a viral process. Bacterial sinusitis, when it occurs, is usually a secondary superinfection of the obstructed/inflamed sinus and is most often caused by S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, or M. catarrhalis. Invasive fungal disease arises when host defenses are impaired (uncontrolled diabetes, transplant/immunocompromise), allowing Mucorales or Aspergillus to invade tissue and spread beyond the sinus (pp. 298-299).","additionalDifferentials":["Common cold / nonspecific upper respiratory infection (p. 298)","Allergic rhinitis (p. 298)","Vasomotor rhinitis (p. 298)","Rhinitis medicamentosa from overuse of topical decongestants (p. 298)","Drug-induced rhinitis (e.g., aspirin, ibuprofen, beta blockers) (p. 298)","Cerebrospinal fluid leak (p. 298)","Headache and facial pain syndromes referred to the sinuses (p. 298)","Temporomandibular disorder (p. 298)","Dental pathology (p. 298)","Gastroesophageal reflux causing referred sinus symptoms (p. 298)","Nasal foreign body (especially in children) (p. 298)"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial superinfection masquerading within an otherwise viral course (p. 299)","Invasive fungal rhinosinusitis (Mucorales or Aspergillus) presenting as rapidly progressive sinusitis in immunocompromised hosts (p. 299)","Nosocomial sinusitis in intubated critically ill patients presenting as unexplained fever (p. 299)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Autoimmune disease such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis (p. 298)","Allergic rhinitis and other noninfectious rhinitides (p. 298)","Structural causes such as deviated nasal septum or nasal polyps (noted in chronic disease, p. 300)"],"additionalInvestigations":["No imaging or laboratory testing is routinely required for uncomplicated acute sinusitis; diagnosis is clinical (pp. 299-300).","Sinus transillumination is NOT accurate for diagnosing sinusitis and should not be relied upon (p. 299).","Noncontrast CT is the preferred imaging modality when imaging is indicated (e.g., failure to improve, suspected complication, or alternative diagnosis) (p. 300).","Gadolinium-enhanced MRI is the preferred imaging modality when invasive fungal sinusitis is suspected (p. 299).","Nasal endoscopy with biopsy is part of the evaluation for suspected invasive fungal sinusitis (p. 299).","Direct rhinoscopy may reveal excess mucus or purulence on examination (p. 299).","In recurrent acute sinusitis, consider nasal culture during episodes, imaging between episodes to identify predisposing anatomic abnormalities, and allergic/immunologic evaluation (p. 300)."],"redFlags":["Confusion (possible intracranial complication) (p. 299)","Unilateral weakness (p. 299)","Proptosis (p. 299)","Limited ocular movements / ophthalmoplegia (p. 299)","Acute vision changes (p. 299)","Orbital swelling, cellulitis, or chemosis (p. 299)","Cranial nerve involvement (p. 299)","Rapidly progressive facial pain/pressure with headache and fever in a diabetic or immunocompromised patient (suspect invasive fungal disease - an emergency) (p. 299)","Recurrent fever >102 degrees F (p. 300)","Sinus symptoms that worsen after an initial improvement (double-sickening pattern) (p. 300)","Rapid worsening of facial pain that becomes persistent (p. 300)"],"prognosis":"Most acute sinusitis is viral and self-limited. Symptoms of the underlying upper respiratory illness generally peak early but can persist up to about 14 days (p. 296). Complications of sinusitis can be dramatic but are extremely rare (p. 299). Invasive fungal sinusitis is an emergency: in immunocompromised hosts it can progress within days from rhinosinusitis to cranial nerve involvement, orbital and ophthalmic involvement, and critical illness (p. 299).","pitfalls":["Treating acute sinusitis as bacterial by default - the large majority are viral, and antibiotics are over-prescribed; reserve antibiotics for patients meeting persistent (>=10 days), severe, or worsening criteria (p. 299).","Relying on sinus transillumination, which is inaccurate for diagnosis (p. 299).","Prescribing a macrolide for sinusitis - specifically not recommended because of high rates of macrolide-resistant S. pneumoniae (p. 300).","Missing invasive fungal sinusitis in a diabetic or immunocompromised patient with rapidly progressive disease - delay can be fatal (p. 299).","Overlooking nosocomial sinusitis as a hidden source of unexplained fever in nasotracheally intubated ICU patients (p. 299).","In children, failing to consider a nasal foreign body as the cause of sinus symptoms (p. 298)."],"severityClassification":"Antibiotic-prescribing indications (Harrison Table 37-2, p. 299): Persistent = symptoms lasting >=10 days; Severe = fever >102 degrees F with either purulent nasal discharge or nasal pain for at least 3-4 consecutive days; Worsening = new fever, headache, or increased nasal discharge following an upper respiratory infection that had lasted 5-6 days and was initially improving (double-sickening). Duration-based: acute <4 weeks, subacute 4-12 weeks, chronic >=12 weeks (p. 298).","references":["Harrison 22e p. 296","Harrison 22e p. 298","Harrison 22e p. 299","Harrison 22e p. 300","Harrison 22e p. 276"],"crossLinks":["URTI","PHARYNGITIS","BRAIN_ABSCESS","MENINGITIS"]},"URTI":{"name":"Upper Respiratory Tract Infection (common cold)","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"295-297","clinicalPearls":["The nonspecific URI ('common cold') is defined as a respiratory tract infection in which no single symptom predominates, distinguishing it clinically from more localized syndromes such as pharyngitis, sinusitis, or otitis media (Harrison 22e p.296).","The overwhelming majority of acute respiratory infections are caused by common respiratory viruses and are self-limited; antibiotics neither speed resolution nor prevent complications, which is why at least half of ambulatory antibiotic prescriptions for these infections are inappropriate (Harrison 22e p.295).","Nasal mucus in a viral URI can range anywhere from watery to frankly purulent, so purulent or discolored nasal discharge by itself does not indicate bacterial infection or warrant antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.296).","Symptoms typically begin gradually after a 2-8 day incubation, peak early, and usually last 5-10 days but may persist up to 14 days; patients remain infectious as long as symptoms are present (Harrison 22e pp.296-297).","Adults average 2-4 URIs per year and children 6-10 per year; reinfection is common because immunity to the many rhinovirus and adenovirus serotypes is serotype-specific and immunity to coronaviruses, parainfluenza, RSV, and metapneumovirus is weak or short-lived (Harrison 22e pp.295-296).","Myalgias may occur but are usually less prominent than in influenza, a useful bedside discriminator from influenza (Harrison 22e p.296).","Making a specific viral diagnosis is not practical, cost-effective, or necessary; multiplex RT-PCR panels may be overly sensitive and yield false positives from recent prior infection (Harrison 22e p.296).","URI can be reduced by hand hygiene, physical distancing, masking, isolation of ill persons, and environmental cleaning (Harrison 22e p.295)."],"pathophysiology":"Nonspecific URI is an acute infection of the respiratory tract above the vocal cords, most commonly caused by respiratory viruses acquired through direct contact with infected people, contaminated surfaces, and large and small respiratory droplets. Susceptibility depends on prior exposure and immunity, general health, genetics, microbiome factors, and psychosocial factors including stress. Because immunity is serotype-specific (rhinovirus, adenovirus) or weak/short-lived (coronaviruses, parainfluenza, RSV, metapneumovirus), reinfection is frequent (Harrison 22e pp.295-296).","additionalDifferentials":["Influenza (more prominent myalgias and systemic symptoms)","COVID-19 (SARS-CoV-2 can cause virtually any upper respiratory symptom; new loss of taste or smell appears specific for COVID-19)","Measles (cough, coryza, and conjunctivitis)","Acute HIV infection (sore throat and rash often predominate)","Allergic, inflammatory, autoimmune, or neoplastic causes of upper respiratory symptoms"],"infectionMimics":["Influenza","COVID-19 (SARS-CoV-2)","Acute HIV infection","Measles","Streptococcal pharyngitis","Acute bacterial sinusitis","Acute otitis media"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Allergic rhinitis / allergic upper respiratory symptoms","Other inflammatory upper respiratory conditions","Autoimmune causes of upper respiratory symptoms","Neoplastic causes of upper respiratory symptoms"],"additionalInvestigations":["No specific viral testing is needed for diagnosis; multiplex RT-PCR panels exist but are often unnecessary and may be falsely positive due to recent prior infection (Harrison 22e p.296)"],"redFlags":["Fever above 102 degrees F (38.9 C)","Chest pain not attributable to a pulled muscle","Shortness of breath","Dizziness","Confusion","New ear or sinus pain (suggesting otitis media or bacterial sinusitis complication)","Symptoms lasting longer than 14 days","New loss of taste or smell where SARS-CoV-2 is circulating (suggests COVID-19)"],"prognosis":"Nonspecific URI is self-limited. Symptoms peak early and generally resolve within 5-10 days, though some last up to 14 days. Although the cold can occasionally be complicated by otitis media or bacterial sinusitis, for any individual patient an antibiotic is more likely to cause an adverse reaction than to prevent a complication (Harrison 22e pp.296-297).","pitfalls":["Prescribing antibiotics for a viral URI: they do not shorten illness or prevent complications and risk adverse drug effects, microbiome disruption, C. difficile infection, increased cost, and antibiotic resistance (Harrison 22e p.295).","Interpreting purulent nasal discharge as evidence of bacterial infection; viral URI mucus normally ranges from watery to purulent (Harrison 22e p.296).","Drivers of inappropriate prescribing include time pressure, fear of missing a rare bacterial diagnosis, concern about a rare complication, low salience of antibiotic harms, and the mistaken belief that patients demand antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.295).","Delayed antibiotic prescriptions are conceptually flawed for self-limited respiratory infections, shift decision-making to patients, and send a mixed message; they should be avoided (Harrison 22e p.296).","Procalcitonin testing has been ineffective and CRP testing not durable for reducing inappropriate ambulatory antibiotic use (Harrison 22e p.296).","Many popular cold remedies are ineffective, of questionable benefit, or harmful, including echinacea, zinc, inhaled steam, vitamin C, vitamin D, garlic, antihistamines, intranasal corticosteroids, saline nasal irrigation, and antiviral drugs (Harrison 22e p.296).","Cough and cold medicines should not be used in children under 6 years because of the risk of adverse effects (Harrison 22e p.296)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.295","Harrison 22e p.296","Harrison 22e p.297"],"crossLinks":["SINUSITIS","PHARYNGITIS","ACUTE_BRONCHITIS"]},"VAP":{"name":"Ventilator Associated Pneumonia","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chap. 131 (Pneumonia) p.1029-1033 and Chap. 147 (Health Care-Associated Infections) p.1147-1148","clinicalPearls":["VAP is defined as pneumonia with onset at or beyond 48 h after the start of mechanical ventilation; the same principles extend to ventilated HAP and to non-ICU HAP (p.1029).","About 35% of all HAP patients require mechanical ventilation, which marks ventilated HAP as a distinct entity; roughly 70% of HAP is acquired outside the ICU and 30% within it (p.1029).","Three sequential steps drive VAP: oropharyngeal colonization with pathogens, aspiration of these organisms into the lower airway, and compromise of host defenses; almost all intubated patients are at least transiently colonized, yet only about one-third go on to develop VAP (p.1030).","Colony counts often rise to high levels days before clinical pneumonia appears, supporting the idea that the final step is overwhelming of host defenses by a large bacterial inoculum (p.1030).","Serial changes in oxygenation may detect VAP earlier than other clinical findings and can also be used to track response to therapy (p.1030).","Clinical manifestations of HAP and VAP are nonspecific: fever, leukocytosis, increased respiratory secretions, consolidation on examination, and a new or changing radiographic infiltrate (p.1030).","Absence of bacteria on a Gram stain of an endotracheal aspirate makes pneumonia an unlikely cause of fever or infiltrates, and absence of an MDR pathogen on culture supports de-escalation of empirical therapy (p.1031).","Clinical improvement, when it occurs, is usually evident within 48-72 h of starting therapy, typically as improved oxygenation; chest radiographs often worsen early in treatment and are therefore less useful than clinical criteria for judging response (p.1033).","P. aeruginosa essentially never causes infection in patients without prior antibiotic exposure; antibiotic exposure is by far the greatest risk factor for replacement of normal oropharyngeal flora by pathogens (p.1030).","On any given day in the ICU, an average of about 10% of patients have pneumonia, the overwhelming majority of which is VAP; reported prevalence ranges from 6 to 52 cases per 100 patients depending on the population (p.1030)."],"pathophysiology":"The endotracheal tube is the single most important risk factor for VAP. While it blocks large-volume aspiration, it worsens microaspiration of secretions that pool above the cuff, and the tube plus suctioning damages tracheal mucosa and promotes tracheal colonization. Pathogenic bacteria form a glycocalyx biofilm on the tube surface that shields them from antibiotics and host defenses; suctioning can dislodge organisms that reinoculate the trachea, and biofilm fragments can embolize to distal airways carrying bacteria. Ventilator circuit tubing can harbor organisms that wash back if manipulated, so circuits are changed only when soiled or with a new patient. In critically ill patients, normal oropharyngeal flora is replaced by pathogens, driven mainly by antibiotic selection pressure, cross-infection, severe systemic illness, and malnutrition. Severely ill patients with sepsis or trauma appear to enter a state of immunoparalysis several days after ICU admission, coinciding with peak VAP risk; the mechanism is unclear but hyperglycemia and frequent transfusions adversely affect immunity (p.1029-1030).","additionalDifferentials":["Purulent tracheobronchitis (especially in chronic ventilator units, where it can impede weaning) (p.1030)"],"infectionMimics":["Fungal pneumonia (requires different antibiotics from those used for bacterial VAP) (p.1031)","Viral pneumonia (requires different antibiotics from those used for bacterial VAP) (p.1031)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Atypical pulmonary edema (p.1031)","Pulmonary contusion (p.1031)","Alveolar hemorrhage (p.1031)","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (p.1031)","Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) (p.1031)","Pulmonary infarction / pulmonary embolism (p.1031)","Non-pulmonary causes of fever and/or leukocytosis: antibiotic-associated diarrhea, central line-associated infection, sinusitis, urinary tract infection, pancreatitis, and drug fever (p.1031)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative deep respiratory tract cultures to separate colonization from true infection: more distal sampling is more specific and uses a lower growth threshold; endotracheal aspirate threshold is 10^6 cfu/mL and protected specimen brush threshold is 10^3 cfu/mL, but sensitivity falls with more distal blind sampling (p.1031).","Gram stain of respiratory specimen, differential cell counts, staining for intracellular organisms, and detection of locally elevated infection-related protein levels to increase diagnostic yield (p.1031).","Molecular diagnostic methods, which can exclude a suspected MDR pathogen as a therapy target when results are negative (p.1031).","Serial procalcitonin and other biomarkers, used together with the initial clinical response to help guide therapy duration (p.1032)."],"redFlags":["Worsening oxygenation, tachypnea, tachycardia, and increased minute ventilation in a ventilated patient (p.1030).","Septic shock accompanying suspected VAP (favors broader empirical and combination coverage) (p.1031-1032).","Colony counts above the diagnostic threshold while already on antibiotics, suggesting current therapy is ineffective (p.1031).","Necrotizing pneumonia (e.g., P. aeruginosa or S. aureus) causing significant pulmonary hemorrhage or empyema (p.1032).","VAP complicating COVID-19, which carries higher attributable mortality than other forms of VAP (p.1033)."],"prognosis":"Crude mortality rates for VAP are reported as high as 50-70%, but the clinically meaningful figure is attributable mortality, since many patients have underlying diseases that would be fatal regardless. Attributable mortality exceeded 25% in one matched-cohort study, while more recent estimates are lower (5-10%); VAP complicating COVID-19 has higher attributable mortality. Mortality varies with patient type and ICU; VAP in trauma patients is generally not associated with attributable mortality (these patients were often previously healthy). The causative pathogen matters: MDR pathogens carry significantly greater attributable mortality than non-MDR pathogens, and infection with organisms such as S. maltophilia mainly marks a severely immunocompromised host at high risk. Beyond death, the major complication is prolonged mechanical ventilation with longer ICU and hospital stays; long-term sequelae of necrotizing infection include bronchiectasis and parenchymal scarring with recurrent pneumonia, prolonged oxygen need, a catabolic state, and (in the elderly) loss of independent function (p.1032-1033).","pitfalls":["Applying clinical criteria designed for community-acquired pneumonia consistently overdiagnoses VAP, because of frequent tracheal colonization in intubated patients, multiple alternative causes of radiographic infiltrates, and the high frequency of other fever sources in critically ill patients (p.1030-1031).","No single set of criteria is reliably diagnostic of pneumonia in a ventilated patient, which undermines both prevention and treatment efforts and even estimates of VAP attributable mortality (p.1030).","Recently effective antibiotics given in the 24-48 h before sampling can cause false-negative quantitative cultures; with susceptible organisms a single dose can drop colony counts below threshold, and operating characteristics only normalize after about 3 days (p.1031).","Quantitative cultures can also fall below threshold if obtained very early in infection or if delayed until an effective host response has reduced bacterial counts; the specimen should ideally be taken as soon as VAP is suspected and before antibiotics are started or changed (p.1031).","Recurrent VAP with the same pathogen can occur because endotracheal-tube biofilm allows persistence and reintroduction; however, about half of recurrent Pseudomonas cases are actually due to a new strain (p.1032).","Aggressive early extubation can lead to reintubation and increased aspiration (raising VAP risk), while heavy continuous sedation also increases VAP risk and undersedation risks self-extubation, so tradeoffs must be balanced (p.1033)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not present a formal VAP severity score in this text. Empirical-therapy decisions are instead stratified by (1) the presence of risk factors for MDR pathogens and (2) the predicted risk of death, with a mortality risk of <=15% considered low risk (p.1031).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1029","Harrison 22e p.1030","Harrison 22e p.1031","Harrison 22e p.1032","Harrison 22e p.1033","Harrison 22e p.1147","Harrison 22e p.1148"],"crossLinks":["HAP","CAP","ASPIRATION_PNEUMONIA"]},"VIRAL_HEPATITIS":{"name":"Acute Viral Hepatitis","system":"Tropical Fever / Hepatobiliary","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.350 (Acute Viral Hepatitis), p.2643-2661","clinicalPearls":["Five hepatotropic viruses cause almost all acute viral hepatitis: HAV, HBV, HCV, HDV (delta), and HEV. All are RNA viruses except HBV, which is a DNA virus that replicates via reverse transcription like a retrovirus (p.2643).","All five types produce a clinically similar acute illness, so the type cannot be reliably distinguished by clinical or epidemiologic features alone; specific serologic testing is the accurate way to tell them apart (p.2643, p.2651).","Only the bloodborne agents (HBV, HCV, HDV) can become chronic and progress to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma; HAV and HEV do not cause chronic liver disease in immunocompetent hosts (p.2643, p.2659-2660).","Hepatitis A is diagnosed during acute illness by detecting IgM anti-HAV; IgM persists ~3 months (rarely 6-12). IgG anti-HAV appears in convalescence, remains detectable indefinitely, and confers lifelong immunity to reinfection (p.2643-2644, p.2656).","The serologic/virologic course of acute hepatitis E mirrors hepatitis A: brief fecal shedding and viremia, an early IgM anti-HEV response dominating ~the first 3 months, then long-lasting IgG anti-HEV (p.2658).","Acute hepatitis can be evaluated with five serologic tests: HBsAg, IgM anti-HAV, IgM anti-HBc, anti-HCV, and HCV RNA (p.2657-2658).","HBsAg positive plus IgM anti-HBc indicates acute (or acute reactivation) hepatitis B; HBsAg positive with IgG anti-HBc (IgM negative) indicates chronic HBV (p.2657-2658).","In about 1-5% of acute HBV infections HBsAg is below the detection threshold; in that window the presence of IgM anti-HBc establishes the diagnosis of acute hepatitis B (p.2646, p.2656).","The anti-HBc 'window' period (HBsAg already cleared, anti-HBs not yet detectable) is now rarely encountered because of higher-sensitivity HBsAg/anti-HBs assays (p.2646).","Isolated anti-HBc usually represents remote past HBV infection (IgG class); rarely it reflects low-level viremia with HBsAg below detection, or a false-positive (p.2646).","IgM anti-HBc can reappear with high titer during acute flares of chronic hepatitis B, so IgM vs IgG anti-HBc does not always reliably separate de novo acute hepatitis B from an exacerbation of chronic HBV; history and serial follow-up help (p.2646-2647, p.2657).","HBeAg is a qualitative marker of active HBV replication and high infectivity; HBV DNA is the quantitative marker. HBeAg-positive HBsAg-positive mothers transmit to >90% of offspring versus only 10-15% if anti-HBe positive (p.2645-2646).","Precore/core-promoter mutant HBV cannot make HBeAg, producing HBeAg-negative chronic hepatitis with detectable HBV DNA and anti-HBe; it can run a more aggressive course and is the predominant form in the Mediterranean and Europe (p.2646-2647).","Anti-HCV (IgG) is the available antibody marker (no clinically useful IgM anti-HCV exists); with contemporary assays it is detectable early in acute hepatitis C and persists after recovery and during chronic infection (p.2657).","HCV RNA is the most sensitive test and the diagnostic gold standard; it appears before aminotransferase rise and before anti-HCV. Detectable anti-HCV with negative HCV RNA signifies cleared/cured infection (p.2657-2658).","Recovery from hepatitis C leaves persistent anti-HCV but does NOT protect against reinfection; in people who inject drugs, anti-HCV plus HCV RNA may indicate reinfection rather than chronic infection (p.2657-2658).","Serum aminotransferase levels do not correlate well with the degree of liver cell damage; peak ALT/AST does not predict severity (p.2655-2656).","A low-grade fever (38-39 C) is more typical of hepatitis A and E than of B or C, except when hepatitis B presents with a serum sickness-like syndrome (p.2655).","HDV is a defective virus that requires HBV (HBsAg envelope) for replication; negative HBV serology excludes hepatitis D, and the duration of HDV infection cannot outlast HBV infection (p.2646-2647, p.2658).","In acute hepatitis with HBsAg and anti-HDV, the anti-HBc class clarifies the relationship: IgM anti-HBc indicates simultaneous acute HBV-HDV co-infection, whereas IgG anti-HBc indicates HDV superinfection on chronic HBV (p.2658).","Among healthy adults, acute hepatitis B is self-limited in ~95-99%, whereas acute hepatitis C is self-limited in only ~15-20% (chronicity is the rule for HCV) (p.2655-2656)."],"pathophysiology":"None of the hepatitis viruses is ordinarily directly cytopathic to hepatocytes; the clinical manifestations and outcome of acute liver injury are largely determined by the host immune (chiefly cell-mediated/cytolytic T-cell) response to infected hepatocytes. In hepatitis B the existence of inactive carriers with normal histology and the more severe disease seen with defective cellular immunity support an immune-mediated mechanism; in immunocompetent adults the degree of liver injury correlates with the level of HBV replication (HBV DNA). HBV enters hepatocytes via the sodium taurocholate cotransporting polypeptide (NTCP) receptor and replicates through reverse transcription of a pregenomic RNA, forming covalently closed circular DNA in the nucleus. HDV likewise enters via NTCP but depends on HBV helper function for complete virions and liver injury (p.2643-2645, p.2651).","additionalDifferentials":["Autoimmune hepatitis (suggested by repeated unexplained episodes of acute hepatitis; can also be triggered after self-limited acute hepatitis A, B, or C) (p.2660)","Alcohol-associated hepatitis (aminotransferases usually below ~400 IU/mL with a heavy-drinking history, unlike viral hepatitis) (p.2660)","Drug-induced/toxin-induced liver injury, including acetaminophen, and anesthetic-agent hepatotoxicity (p.2660)","Acute biliary disease: acute cholecystitis, common-duct stone, or ascending cholangitis (RUQ pain, fever, jaundice overlap) (p.2660)","Obstructive jaundice from common-duct stone or pancreatic carcinoma (viral hepatitis in the elderly is often misdiagnosed as this) (p.2660)","Wilson disease and alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (genetic/metabolic liver disorders) (p.2660)","Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD/MASH, formerly NAFLD/NASH) (p.2660)","Ischemic (hypoxic) liver injury (a leading cause of aminotransferases >=1000 IU/L, along with drug injury and acute viral hepatitis) (p.2660)"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious mononucleosis / Epstein-Barr virus (p.2660)","Cytomegalovirus hepatitis (p.2643, p.2660)","Herpes simplex virus (p.2660)","Adenovirus (including the unexplained severe pediatric acute hepatitis linked to adenovirus type 41) (p.2643, p.2660)","Coxsackievirus infection (p.2660)","Toxoplasmosis (p.2660)","SARS-CoV-2 (aminotransferase elevations in ~10% of cases and up to half of severe cases) (p.2660)","Leptospirosis (p.2660)","Brucellosis (p.2660)","Disseminated candidiasis (p.2660)","Mycobacterial infection (p.2660)","Pneumocystis infection (p.2660)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Passive hepatic congestion / hypoperfusion from right ventricular failure, shock, severe hypotension, or severe left ventricular failure (p.2660)","Budd-Chiari syndrome (hepatic vein occlusion) and hepatic veno-occlusive disease (p.2660)","Constrictive pericarditis or right atrial myxoma impeding venous return (p.2660)","Acute fatty liver of pregnancy (p.2660)","Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (p.2660)","Eclampsia (p.2660)","HELLP syndrome (hemolysis, elevated liver tests, low platelets) (p.2660)","Liver metastases mimicking acute or even fulminant hepatitis (rare) (p.2660)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Anti-HCV antibody plus HCV RNA (HCV RNA is the gold standard; needed when anti-HCV is undetectable or in immunosuppressed patients where antibody may be absent) (p.2657-2658)","Anti-HDV (IgM/IgG) and/or HDV RNA in HBsAg-positive patients who are at high risk or have severe/fulminant disease (p.2658, p.2659)","IgM anti-HEV / HEV RNA when standard A/B/C tests are negative and enteric risk factors are present (specialized labs/CDC) (p.2658)","HBeAg and anti-HBe to assess relative infectivity and replication in chronic HBV (p.2656, p.2659)","Quantitative HBV DNA (PCR) to gauge replication level and risk of cirrhosis, decompensation, and HCC in chronic HBV (p.2656-2657)","Serum albumin (a fall is uncommon in uncomplicated acute hepatitis; signals severe disease) (p.2656)","Blood glucose (hypoglycemia can occur in severe hepatitis) (p.2656)","Complete blood count / differential (transient neutropenia and lymphopenia, then relative lymphocytosis with 2-20% atypical lymphocytes) (p.2656)","Serum bilirubin with conjugated/unconjugated fractions (levels >20 mg/dL persisting late suggest severe disease) (p.2655-2656)","Liver biopsy only when the diagnosis is in doubt or chronic/autoimmune hepatitis is suspected; it is rarely needed in acute viral hepatitis (p.2658)"],"redFlags":["Hepatic encephalopathy (confusion, disorientation, somnolence, progression to coma) - hallmark of fulminant hepatitis with very high mortality (>80% in deep coma without transplant) (p.2659)","Markedly prolonged prothrombin time / INR (reflects severe synthetic failure and extensive hepatocellular necrosis) (p.2656, p.2658-2659)","Rapidly shrinking liver size with rapidly rising bilirubin even as aminotransferases fall - indicates massive hepatic necrosis (p.2659)","Ascites and peripheral edema at presentation (poorer prognosis) (p.2658-2659)","Hypoglycemia and falling serum albumin in acute hepatitis (severe hepatocellular disease) (p.2656, p.2658-2659)","Very high or persistently rising serum bilirubin (>20 mg/dL late in the course) (p.2655-2656, p.2659)","Pregnancy with acute hepatitis E - up to 10-20% risk of fulminant hepatitis and death (p.2659, p.2661)","HDV superinfection of chronic hepatitis B - clinical deterioration with substantially increased risk of fulminant hepatitis and death (mortality >20% in 'Labrea fever' outbreaks) (p.2659)","Advanced age or serious underlying disease, especially pre-existing chronic liver disease (raises risk of severe and fulminant course) (p.2658-2659)","Acute hepatitis E or other insult superimposed on chronic liver disease (acute-on-chronic liver failure) (p.2659-2660)"],"prognosis":"Hepatitis A and acute hepatitis B in previously healthy persons usually resolve completely (HBV self-limited in ~95-99% of healthy adults; HAV essentially always), with an overall case-fatality of ~0.1% (1% among hospitalized acute hepatitis B). Hepatitis A and E cause no chronic liver disease in immunocompetent hosts. Acute hepatitis C becomes chronic in ~85-90%; among chronic HCV, cirrhosis develops in up to ~20% over 10-20 years, with an annual decompensation risk ~4% and annual HCC risk ~1-4% (about 3.36%) once cirrhotic - rates declining with effective direct-acting antiviral therapy. Chronic hepatitis B carries an enhanced HCC risk, especially with infection in infancy/childhood and high HBV DNA/HBeAg; risk of cirrhosis and HCC rises with the level of HBV replication. Waterborne hepatitis E (genotypes 1 and 2) in India/Asia has a case-fatality of 1-2%, rising to 10-20% in pregnant women. Acute HBV-HDV co-infection mortality is usually similar to HBV alone (~5% in some injection-drug-user outbreaks), but HDV superinfection of chronic HBV markedly worsens outcome (annual cirrhosis rate ~4% in chronic hepatitis D; annual HCC ~3% once cirrhotic) (p.2658-2660).","pitfalls":["Relying on HBsAg alone can miss acute hepatitis B: in ~1-5% of cases HBsAg is below detection and only IgM anti-HBc reveals the diagnosis (p.2656, p.2657-2658).","IgM anti-HBc can re-emerge during an acute exacerbation of chronic HBV, so it does not always distinguish true acute hepatitis B from a chronic flare (p.2646-2647, p.2657).","False-positive IgM anti-HAV occurs in rheumatologic disease (rheumatoid factor) and rarely in EBV infection; false-positive IgM anti-HBc occurs with high-titer rheumatoid factor (p.2656).","Anti-HCV immunoassays can be falsely positive (low pretest probability, rheumatoid factor); confirm with HCV RNA (p.2657).","In immunosuppressed patients anti-HCV may be undetectable, so HCV RNA testing is required to diagnose hepatitis C (p.2655, p.2657).","HBsAg titer does not reflect disease severity - an inverse correlation exists, with very low HBsAg in fulminant hepatitis (p.2656-2657).","Coexisting HBsAg and anti-HBs in chronic HBV does not signal imminent HBsAg clearance; these patients still have chronic HBV infection (p.2646, p.2657).","A normal or only mildly elevated alkaline phosphatase and normal albumin are expected in uncomplicated acute hepatitis; abnormal values suggest an alternative or complicated course (p.2656).","The serum sickness-like prodrome of acute hepatitis B (arthralgia, rash, angioedema) is often misdiagnosed as a rheumatologic disease before jaundice appears (p.2659).","Retrospective diagnosis of self-limited simultaneous acute HBV-HDV co-infection is difficult because anti-HDV is often transient and disappears once HBsAg clears (p.2658).","Patients with acute viral hepatitis tolerate surgery poorly; biliary disease should be excluded clinically and with imaging before any operation (p.2660)."],"severityClassification":"No formal grading system for acute viral hepatitis is given; severity is judged clinically. Features indicating severe hepatocellular disease warranting prompt hospital admission include prolonged prothrombin time, low serum albumin, hypoglycemia, and very high serum bilirubin; presenting ascites, edema, or encephalopathy indicate a poorer prognosis. Progression to fulminant hepatitis (massive hepatic necrosis) is marked by encephalopathy with rapidly shrinking liver size, rapidly rising bilirubin, and markedly prolonged PT even as aminotransferases fall (p.2658-2659).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2643","Harrison 22e p.2644","Harrison 22e p.2645","Harrison 22e p.2646","Harrison 22e p.2647","Harrison 22e p.2651","Harrison 22e p.2652","Harrison 22e p.2654","Harrison 22e p.2655","Harrison 22e p.2656","Harrison 22e p.2657","Harrison 22e p.2658","Harrison 22e p.2659","Harrison 22e p.2660","Harrison 22e p.2661"],"crossLinks":["toxic_hepatitis","decomp_cirrhosis","hepatic_enceph"]},"VIRAL_MENINGITIS":{"name":"Viral (Aseptic) Meningitis","system":"Central Nervous System","class":"infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1166-1169","clinicalPearls":["Profound alterations in consciousness (stupor, coma, marked confusion), seizures, focal neurologic signs, or neuroimaging evidence of parenchymal involvement do NOT occur in viral meningitis; their presence points instead to encephalitis or another CNS process (p.1166).","Headache is almost invariably present, often frontal or retroorbital, with photophobia and pain on eye movement; nuchal rigidity is usually present but may be mild, appearing only near the limit of neck anteflexion (p.1166).","A specific viral cause is identified in 60-90% of cases using combined CSF PCR, culture, and serology; roughly two-thirds of culture-negative 'aseptic' meningitis cases have a viral etiology found by CSF PCR (p.1166).","Enteroviruses account for >85% of cases with an identified etiology and predominate in summer and fall, especially in children <15 years (p.1167-1168).","HSV-2 causes most adult uncomplicated HSV meningitis (HSV-1 causes ~90% of HSV encephalitis); ~20% of HSV meningitis patients develop recurrent attacks, and most recurrent/'aseptic' meningitis, including cases formerly called Mollaret's meningitis, is due to HSV (p.1168).","Many HSV meningitis patients give no history and show no active genital herpes lesions at presentation, so absence of genital lesions does not exclude the diagnosis (p.1168).","VZV is increasingly recognized as a cause of meningitis even WITHOUT rash, so absence of chickenpox or shingles does not exclude it (p.1168).","Cranial nerve palsies (most often V, VII, or VIII) are more common in HIV meningitis than in other viral meningitides; HIV seroconversion may be delayed, so seronegative suspected cases warrant repeat testing (p.1168).","Mumps meningitis is suggested by parotitis, orchitis, oophoritis, pancreatitis, or raised serum amylase/lipase, but their absence does not exclude it; a documented prior mumps infection (lifelong immunity) does exclude it (p.1168-1169).","Consider LCMV when aseptic meningitis occurs in late fall/winter with exposure to house mice or pet/laboratory rodents or their excreta, particularly when accompanied by leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, or abnormal liver function tests (p.1169)."],"pathophysiology":"Viral meningitis is inflammation of the meninges, most often caused by enteroviruses, with a self-limited course in immunocompetent hosts. Inflammation is largely confined to the meninges, producing meningeal irritation without the parenchymal brain involvement that characterizes encephalitis; preservation of higher cerebral function distinguishes meningitis from encephalitis. CSF oligoclonal IgG bands directed against viral proteins can occur, though they are nonspecific (Harrison 22e p.1166-1167).","additionalDifferentials":["Arthropod-borne (arbovirus) meningitis, notably West Nile virus; consider when meningitis/encephalitis clusters occur in a restricted region in summer/early fall (p.1167-1168)","Epstein-Barr virus (may produce aseptic meningitis with or without infectious mononucleosis; atypical lymphocytes in CSF/blood are suggestive) (p.1168)","Human herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6) (p.1167)","Cytomegalovirus (notably in immunocompromised hosts, where it may cause PMN pleocytosis with low glucose) (p.1167)","HSV-1 (less common cause of meningitis than HSV-2) (p.1167)","Lymphocytic choriomeningitis virus (LCMV) (p.1167, 1169)","Mumps (late winter/early spring, male predominance ~3:1) (p.1167, 1168-1169)","Zika and other non-WNV arboviruses (p.1167)","Human parechovirus (included on the FilmArray ME panel) (p.1167)"],"infectionMimics":["Untreated or partially treated bacterial meningitis (a key pitfall when CSF shifts toward lymphocytic predominance) (p.1167)","Early fungal meningitis (lymphocytic pleocytosis common; hypoglycorrhachia may be absent early) (p.1167)","Early mycobacterial / tuberculous meningitis (p.1167)","Neurosyphilis (Treponema pallidum) (p.1167)","Mycoplasma pneumoniae CNS infection (p.1166-1167)","Listeria spp. (p.1167)","Brucella spp. (p.1167)","Coxiella spp. (p.1167)","Leptospira spp. (p.1167)","Rickettsia spp. (p.1167)","Parameningeal infections (p.1167)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Neoplastic meningitis (p.1167)","Medication-induced hypersensitivity (drug-induced) meningitis (p.1167)","Systemic lupus erythematosus and other rheumatologic diseases (p.1167)","Sarcoidosis (p.1167)","Behcet's syndrome (p.1167)","Uveomeningitic syndromes (p.1167)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CSF reverse transcriptase PCR (RT-PCR) for enteroviruses: diagnostic procedure of choice, highest sensitivity if done within 48 h of symptom onset, falling rapidly after day 5 (p.1166, 1168)","FilmArray Meningitis/Encephalitis multiplex PCR panel covering HSV-1, HSV-2, enteroviruses, VZV, HHV-6, and human parechovirus (does NOT include CMV, WNV, LCMV, or EBV) (p.1166)","WNV-specific CSF IgM (more sensitive than CSF RT-PCR for WNV; RT-PCR ~70% sensitive, useful mainly in immunocompromised patients with poor antibody response) (p.1166, 1168)","Arboviral serology demonstrating CSF viral-specific IgM or seroconversion; repeat testing if early study is negative and suspicion is high (p.1168)","Throat washing and stool PCR/culture to support enteroviral (and Mycoplasma) diagnosis (stool/throat shedding may persist for weeks) (p.1166-1167)","Demonstration of intrathecal HSV-specific or VZV-specific antibody synthesis (less sensitive/specific than PCR; may take >1 week to become positive) (p.1168)","Routine bloods: complete blood count with differential, liver and renal function tests, ESR, C-reactive protein, electrolytes, glucose, creatine kinase, aldolase, amylase, and lipase (p.1167)","Neuroimaging (MRI preferred over CT) when there is altered consciousness, seizures, focal signs, atypical CSF profile, or immunocompromise (not mandatory in uncomplicated viral meningitis) (p.1167)","Serum procalcitonin >0.5 ng/mL as a clue favoring bacterial over aseptic meningitis (p.1167)"],"redFlags":["Depressed or markedly altered consciousness (stupor, coma, marked confusion) — suggests encephalitis or alternative diagnosis, not simple viral meningitis (p.1166)","Seizures (p.1166)","Focal neurologic signs/symptoms or neuroimaging abnormalities indicating parenchymal brain involvement (p.1166)","CSF PMN pleocytosis without an established diagnosis — should prompt consideration of bacterial or parameningeal infection (p.1166-1167)","Lymphocytic pleocytosis WITH low CSF glucose (hypoglycorrhachia) — should raise suspicion of fungal, tuberculous, or Listeria meningoencephalitis, or noninfectious causes such as sarcoid or neoplastic meningitis (p.1167)","Atypical CSF profile or immunocompromising condition/treatment — warrants hospitalization and neuroimaging (p.1167)"],"prognosis":"In adults the prognosis for full recovery from viral meningitis is excellent; treatment of almost all cases is primarily symptomatic. Rare adult patients have persisting headache, mild mental impairment, incoordination, or generalized asthenia for weeks to months. Outcome is less certain in infants and neonates (<1 year), in whom intellectual impairment, learning disabilities, hearing loss, and other lasting sequelae have been reported. Enteroviral meningitis outside the neonatal period is usually benign with recovery without sequelae, but chronic/severe infection can occur in neonates and in people with hypo- or agammaglobulinemia (Harrison 22e p.1168-1169).","pitfalls":["Failing to recognize partially or incompletely treated bacterial meningitis, which can produce a lymphocytic-predominant CSF that mimics viral meningitis (p.1167)","Up to 15% of enteroviral meningitis patients (most often young infants) have a NORMAL CSF leukocyte count, so a normal cell count does not exclude the diagnosis (p.1168)","PMNs may predominate during the first 48 h of viral meningitis (e.g., echovirus 9, WNV, EEE, mumps); WNV PMN pleocytosis occurs in ~45% and can persist a week or more before shifting to lymphocytes — do not assume early neutrophilia means bacterial infection (p.1167-1168)","Treating a normal CSF glucose as ruling out viral meningitis is wrong, but conversely low CSF glucose can occur in 10-30% of mumps or LCMV and rarely with enteroviruses, HSV-2, or VZV (p.1167)","CSF viral culture has generally poor sensitivity for viral meningitis (unlike its utility in bacterial infection), so reliance on culture alone underdiagnoses (p.1167)","Enterovirus detected in stool is NOT diagnostic of acute CNS infection — it may reflect residual shedding from a prior infection or asymptomatic carriage during epidemics (p.1167)","Enteroviral RT-PCR has low sensitivity for EV71; specific testing may be required (p.1168)","EBV CSF PCR false-positives may reflect viral reactivation or latent viral DNA in recruited lymphocytes rather than true EBV meningitis (p.1168)","Routine serology is unhelpful for high-seroprevalence viruses (HSV, VZV, CMV, EBV) (p.1167)"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal severity grading for viral meningitis; it instead frames disposition by risk. Immunocompetent patients with a classic CSF profile (lymphocytic pleocytosis, normal glucose, negative Gram stain), no focal signs, and no significant alteration in consciousness may not require hospitalization if home monitoring and follow-up are assured. Hospitalization is indicated for immunocompromised patients, those with significant alteration in consciousness, seizures, or focal signs suggesting encephalitis/parenchymal involvement, and those with an atypical CSF profile (Harrison 22e p.1169).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1166","Harrison 22e p.1167","Harrison 22e p.1168","Harrison 22e p.1169"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS","ENCEPHALITIS"]},"aaa":{"name":"Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm","system":"Vascular emergency","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.154, p.2207-2212","clinicalPearls":["Most abdominal aortic aneurysms are asymptomatic and are found as an incidental imaging finding or as a palpable, pulsatile, expansile, nontender mass on routine abdominal exam (p.2211).","New aneurysmal pain is usually a harbinger of rupture and constitutes a medical emergency; however, acute rupture more often occurs without any prior warning (p.2211).","Acute pain plus hypotension is the presentation of frank rupture and mandates emergency open or endovascular repair (p.2211).","At least 90% of abdominal aortic aneurysms >4.0 cm are atherosclerotic, and most lie below the level of the renal arteries (infrarenal) (p.2211).","Cigarette smoking is the most potent modifiable risk factor; incidence rises with age and the condition is more common in men (p.2211).","Mural thrombus within the aneurysm sac can be a source of peripheral (distal) embolization (p.2211)."],"pathophysiology":"Aneurysm is a pathologic dilation of the aortic wall; a true aneurysm involves all three wall layers, versus a pseudoaneurysm in which intima and media are disrupted and the dilated segment is contained only by adventitia (and sometimes perivascular clot). Most abdominal aortic aneurysms are degenerative and atherosclerotic. Wall failure results from degradation or abnormal production of the structural matrix proteins elastin and collagen, driven by inflammation, oxidative stress, proteolysis, and biomechanical wall stress. B- and T-lymphocytes, macrophages, inflammatory cytokines, and matrix metalloproteinases degrade elastin and collagen, reducing tensile strength and the aorta's ability to tolerate pulsatile stretch; histology shows elastin/collagen destruction, loss of vascular smooth muscle, neovascularization, and inflammation (p.2208).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute mesenteric ischemia (SMA embolism/thrombosis) — also causes severe diffuse abdominal pain, classically pain out of proportion to exam findings (p.154).","Thoracic / thoracoabdominal aortic aneurysm, which is often contiguous with infradiaphragmatic disease (p.2208)."],"infectionMimics":["Mycotic (infective) aortic aneurysm from staphylococcal, streptococcal, Salmonella, or fungal seeding — typically saccular and often with positive blood cultures (p.2209).","Syphilitic aortitis — uncommon cause, predominantly involves the ascending aorta/arch rather than the abdominal aorta (p.2209).","Tuberculous aneurysm — typically affects the thoracic aorta via contiguous spread from hilar nodes or abscess (p.2209)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Renal/ureteric colic — rupturing AAA pain radiating to flank or genitalia can mimic urolithiasis (p.154).","Rectus sheath hematoma — abdominal wall mass and pain, especially on anticoagulants (p.154)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Abdominal ultrasound — delineates transverse and longitudinal dimensions, can detect mural thrombus, and is the tool for serial size surveillance and screening (p.2211).","Plain abdominal radiograph — may show a calcified aneurysm outline, but ~25% are non-calcified and not visualized (p.2211).","Contrast-enhanced CT or MRI — accurate for location and size and for planning open or endovascular repair (p.2211).","Contrast aortography may underestimate true diameter when mural thrombus reduces luminal size, and carries risks of bleeding, allergy, and atheroembolism (p.2211)."],"redFlags":["New or worsening aneurysmal pain (abdomen, lower back, chest, or scrotum) signals impending rupture (p.2211).","Abdominal pain radiating to sacrum, flank, or genitalia should always raise suspicion of a rupturing AAA (p.154).","Acute pain with hypotension indicates rupture — emergency repair required (p.2211)."],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on aneurysm size and on the severity of coexisting coronary and cerebrovascular disease. Rupture risk rises sharply with diameter: the 5-year rupture risk is roughly 1-2% for aneurysms <5 cm versus 20-40% for aneurysms >5 cm. At smaller diameters the rupture risk is higher in women than in men (p.2211-2212).","pitfalls":["A rupturing AAA can be misattributed to renal/ureteric colic when pain radiates to the flank or genitalia (p.154).","Plain x-ray cannot exclude an aneurysm because roughly a quarter are non-calcified (p.2211).","Aortography may underestimate aneurysm diameter when mural thrombus narrows the lumen (p.2211).","Premonitory pain may persist for several days before rupture and collapse, so an evolving symptomatic aneurysm should not be dismissed (p.154)."],"severityClassification":"Rupture-risk stratification is driven by maximal aortic diameter. Per Harrison: a true abdominal aortic aneurysm is dilation 50% greater than the adjacent normal segment; aneurysms >=4.0 cm affect 1-2% of men >50. 5-year rupture risk is ~1-2% for <5 cm and ~20-40% for >5 cm. Repair thresholds: any size if rapidly expanding or symptomatic; for asymptomatic aneurysms repair is indicated at >=5.5 cm in men and >=5.0 cm in women, with smaller aneurysms followed by ultrasound surveillance (p.2210-2212).","references":["Harrison 22e p.154","Harrison 22e p.2208","Harrison 22e p.2210","Harrison 22e p.2211","Harrison 22e p.2212"],"crossLinks":["aortic_dissection","renal_colic","hypovolemic_shock","mesenteric_ischemia"]},"acs":{"name":"Acute coronary syndrome","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.145-152 (Chap. 15, Chest Discomfort)","clinicalPearls":["ACS encompasses unstable angina, NSTEMI, and STEMI, and the term is generally reserved for ischemia precipitated by acute coronary atherothrombosis (Harrison 22e p.145).","Unstable ischemic heart disease is sub-classified by whether acute myocardial injury is detectable and whether ST-segment elevation is present: unstable angina = no detectable acute injury; NSTEMI/STEMI = acute injury without/with ST elevation (Harrison 22e p.145).","Type 1 MI (acute coronary thrombosis) is distinguished from type 2 MI, which results from another imbalance of myocardial oxygen supply and demand; type 2 may be driven by anemia, hypoxia, hypotension, or intense psychological stress (Harrison 22e p.145, p.150).","An ECG is recommended within 10 minutes of presentation, primarily to identify ST-segment elevation candidates for immediate reperfusion (Harrison 22e p.150).","Resting ECG sensitivity for ischemia is poor (as low as ~20%), so a normal ECG does not exclude ACS; serial ECGs every 30-60 min are recommended early in suspected ACS (Harrison 22e p.150).","Physical examination findings may be entirely normal in unstable ischemic heart disease, so an unremarkable exam is not reassuring (Harrison 22e p.149).","Relief of pain by nitroglycerin is neither sensitive nor specific for ischemia (esophageal spasm also responds); relief delayed beyond ~10 min suggests either a non-ischemic cause or severe ischemia such as acute MI (Harrison 22e p.149).","Chest wall tenderness on palpation does not exclude myocardial ischemia (Harrison 22e p.150)."],"pathophysiology":"Myocardial ischemia (angina pectoris) arises from an imbalance between myocardial oxygen demand (raised by heart rate, ventricular wall stress, and contractility) and oxygen supply (set by coronary blood flow and arterial oxygen content). ACS is typically precipitated by acute coronary atherothrombosis. When ischemia is sufficiently severe and prolonged (as little as ~20 min), irreversible cellular injury and MI occur. Symptoms can also be driven predominantly by increased demand (e.g., intense psychological stress) or reduced delivery (anemia, hypoxia, hypotension), underlying the type 2 MI concept (Harrison 22e p.145).","additionalDifferentials":["Stable angina (chronic, exertional)","Esophageal spasm (can closely mimic angina and may respond to nitroglycerin)","Gastroesophageal reflux / peptic ulcer disease","Costochondritis / chest wall (musculoskeletal) syndromes","Cervical radiculitis / disk disease","Panic disorder and related psychiatric conditions (up to ~10% of ED chest pain)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute pericarditis (pleuritic pain, relieved by sitting forward, friction rub, diffuse ST elevation with PR depression)","Acute myocarditis (non-ischemic myocardial injury; troponin may rise but should not be labeled MI)","Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy (abrupt chest pain/dyspnea after emotional or physical stress; may show ST elevation and elevated biomarkers mimicking MI)","Acute aortic syndrome / aortic dissection (sudden tearing or knifelike pain, may radiate to back)","Pulmonary embolism (massive PE can cause substernal pain mimicking MI)","Pulmonary hypertension (anginal-quality chest pain)","Tension pneumothorax","Esophageal rupture (Boerhaave) / Mallory-Weiss"],"additionalInvestigations":["High-sensitivity cardiac troponin at presentation, repeated at 1-3 h (1-2 h rapid rule-out possible with hs-assays); conventional troponin repeated at 3-6 h","Right-sided ECG leads when ischemia is suspected but standard 12-lead is nondiagnostic","Chest radiograph (often normal in ACS; may show pulmonary edema; helps exclude pneumothorax, wide mediastinum)","Echocardiography for regional wall-motion abnormality, mechanical complications, or tamponade when diagnosis is uncertain or there is hemodynamic instability","Coronary CT angiography for low-intermediate probability (high negative predictive value; also excludes dissection, effusion, PE)","Stress nuclear perfusion imaging or stress echocardiography for intermediate-risk patients eligible for further testing","Cardiac MRI to characterize troponin elevation without definite CAD and to discriminate ischemic from non-ischemic injury","B-type natriuretic peptide and D-dimer as adjuncts (for heart failure and PE exclusion respectively)"],"redFlags":["ST-segment elevation on ECG (candidate for immediate reperfusion)","ST-segment depression or symmetric T-wave inversions >=0.2 mV (higher risk of death or recurrent ischemia)","Hypotension and significant tachycardia (suggest cardiogenic shock or other severe cause)","New murmur of mitral regurgitation or ventricular septal defect (mechanical complication of STEMI)","Pulmonary edema / S3 / rales (high-risk LV dysfunction)","Pain prolonged >=30 min and not relieved by rest (suggests MI rather than stable angina)","Syncope or presyncope with chest pain (consider ischemic arrhythmia, also PE and dissection)"],"prognosis":"Among unselected ED chest-pain populations, only ~10-20% (as low as 5%) are ultimately diagnosed with ACS, while gastrointestinal causes predominate; historically 2-6% of patients discharged with presumed non-ischemic pain were later found to have had a missed MI. In patients stratified as low risk (excluding ST elevation or definite noncardiac pain), the estimated 30-day major cardiovascular event rate was ~2.5%. Validated clinical decision pathways combining ECG and serial high-sensitivity troponin can achieve ACS miss rates <0.5%, and a single very low hs-troponin >2-3 h after symptom onset carries a negative predictive value >99% for MI (Harrison 22e p.145, p.150-151).","pitfalls":["Relying on a normal resting ECG or unremarkable physical exam to exclude ACS - both can be normal in unstable ischemic heart disease (Harrison 22e p.149-150).","Treating nitroglycerin response as diagnostic - esophageal spasm also responds and ischemic relief is non-specific (Harrison 22e p.149).","Attributing pain to the chest wall because palpation reproduces it - tenderness does not exclude ischemia (Harrison 22e p.150).","Some patients with ischemia deny 'pain' and report only dyspnea or vague anxiety; women and the elderly may present with anginal equivalents (dyspnea, diaphoresis, nausea, fatigue, faintness) (Harrison 22e p.149).","Inferior MI may present with nausea/vomiting (vagal / Bezold-Jarisch reflex), simulating a GI disorder (Harrison 22e p.149).","High-sensitivity troponin detects injury in many non-ACS cardiopulmonary conditions; without a dynamic rise/fall and clinical correlation, a single elevated value can be misinterpreted as MI - chronic elevation occurs with structural heart disease, end-stage renal disease, or interfering antibodies (Harrison 22e p.150).","Non-ischemic injury (e.g., myocarditis) can raise troponin but should not be labeled MI (Harrison 22e p.150).","Confusing diagnostic decision-aids (HEART, EDACS) with prognostic risk scores (TIMI, GRACE), which are intended for patients with already-established ACS, not for diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.151)."],"severityClassification":"ACS is categorized along two clinical decision-making lines. (1) Diagnostic/disposition risk in undifferentiated chest pain uses clinical decision pathways such as the HEART score (history, ECG, age, risk factors; low risk 0-3) and EDACS, combined with serial high-sensitivity troponin, to classify patients as low/intermediate/high risk. (2) Prognostic risk in established ACS uses the TIMI and GRACE risk scores to estimate event risk - these are explicitly not intended for diagnostic use. Common high-risk elements across tools: typical ACS symptoms, older age, known/atherosclerotic risk factors, ischemic ECG changes, and elevated troponin (Harrison 22e p.151).","references":["Harrison 22e p.145","Harrison 22e p.146","Harrison 22e p.147","Harrison 22e p.148","Harrison 22e p.149","Harrison 22e p.150","Harrison 22e p.151","Harrison 22e p.152"],"crossLinks":["pericarditis","aortic_dissection","pe","heart_failure"]},"acute_leukemia":{"name":"Acute leukaemia","system":"Hematology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"825-834 (AML, Ch.109), 844-848 (ALL, Ch.111), 591-593 (oncologic emergencies: leukostasis, tumor lysis syndrome), 575 (febrile neutropenia)","clinicalPearls":["AML accounts for ~31% of new acute leukaemias but ~62% of leukaemic deaths; it is the commonest acute leukaemia in older adults (median age ~69), whereas ALL peaks in young children (age 3-4) and again in the elderly (Harrison 22e p.825, p.844).","Presentation is usually nonspecific and reflects marrow failure: fatigue (often the first symptom), anorexia/weight loss, bleeding/easy bruising, and fever; about half of AML patients have symptoms for 3 months or less before diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.828).","Auer rods (rod-shaped cytoplasmic granules) on the blood film establish a myeloid lineage with certainty and are not seen in ALL (Harrison 22e p.828).","Gum (gingival) hypertrophy and skin/soft-tissue infiltration are characteristic of monocytic AML subtypes and KMT2A-rearranged disease (Harrison 22e p.828).","A myeloid sarcoma (chloroma) is a tissue mass of myeloid blasts (skin, lymph node, GI tract, soft tissue, testis); isolated myeloid sarcoma rapidly progresses to marrow/blood involvement and cannot be cured by local therapy alone (Harrison 22e p.828).","Median presenting WBC in AML is ~15,000/uL; 20% present with >100,000/uL and <5% have no circulating blasts. In ALL ~40% have normal or low WBC and 8% have no circulating blasts, so an automated count can miss the diagnosis - always review the smear (Harrison 22e p.828, p.844).","Diagnosis requires examination of blood AND bone marrow; biopsy distinguishes AML from ALL, and cytochemistry, flow-cytometric immunophenotyping, cytogenetics and molecular studies are all needed for subtyping (Harrison 22e p.828, p.844).","Diagnosis no longer rests on the historical >=20% blast threshold alone - the 2022 WHO classification removed the blast-percent requirement for AML defined by specific genetic lesions such as t(15;17), t(8;21), inv(16) and NPM1 mutation (Harrison 22e p.826).","DIC and coagulopathy are classically associated with acute promyelocytic leukaemia (t(15;17)/PML-RARA); APL bleeding can be catastrophic (GI, intrapulmonary, intracranial), and paradoxically thrombosis can also occur (Harrison 22e p.828).","CSF examination is routine in ALL: CNS leukaemia is defined by >=5 cells/uL or blasts on morphology; CNS involvement is present at diagnosis in 5-10% of adults (higher in mature B-ALL and T-ALL). Defer/limit lumbar puncture if platelets <20 x10^9/L or active bleeding (Harrison 22e p.844-845)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute leukaemia is a clonal proliferation of immature, poorly differentiated haematopoietic precursors (blasts) that infiltrate marrow, blood and other tissues; AML arises from myeloid progenitors and ALL from lymphoid (B- or T-lineage) progenitors. Genome sequencing indicates most AML arises from a small number of mutations that accumulate with age - up to 5-6% of normal people over 70 carry premalignant clones (clonal haematopoiesis of indeterminate potential, CHIP, commonly DNMT3A/TET2/ASXL1), which carries a ~10-fold increased risk of haematologic malignancy though additional 'hits' are required to progress to overt leukaemia. Genetic lesions drive both phenotype and behaviour: for example t(15;17)/PML-RARA defines APL and predisposes to DIC, while NPM1 and FLT3 mutations associate with high leukocyte counts. Marrow replacement by blasts produces anaemia, neutropenia and thrombocytopenia, accounting for the dominant clinical features (Harrison 22e p.825-828, p.844).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute myeloid leukaemia vs acute lymphoblastic leukaemia - distinguished by morphology (Auer rods imply AML), cytochemistry, flow-cytometric immunophenotype and genetics (Harrison 22e p.828, p.844)","Myelodysplastic syndrome or myeloproliferative neoplasm evolving to acute leukaemia (antecedent haematologic disorder) (Harrison 22e p.828)","Therapy-related (secondary) acute leukaemia after alkylating agents (4-6 yr) or topoisomerase II inhibitors (1-3 yr) (Harrison 22e p.826)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood smear (Wright-Giemsa) with manual differential - essential at presentation; look for blasts and Auer rods (Harrison 22e p.828, p.844)","Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy for morphology, cytochemistry, flow-cytometric immunophenotyping, cytogenetics and molecular studies (Harrison 22e p.829, p.844)","Cytogenetics/karyotype and molecular panel at diagnosis (NPM1, FLT3-ITD/TKD, CEBPA, TP53, IDH1/IDH2, RUNX1 etc.) - the most important prognostic data in AML (Harrison 22e p.827-828)","Serum uric acid, electrolytes including potassium, phosphate and calcium, creatinine/BUN, and LDH to assess and monitor tumour-lysis risk (Harrison 22e p.829, p.593)","Coagulation panel - PT, aPTT, fibrinogen and D-dimer - to detect DIC, especially when APL is suspected (Harrison 22e p.829)","Measurable (minimal) residual disease assessment by multiparameter flow cytometry or molecular methods (e.g. PCR for PML-RARA in APL) for response monitoring (Harrison 22e p.827, p.834, p.847)","Lumbar puncture/CSF cytology in ALL (and AML with neurologic symptoms) to detect CNS involvement (Harrison 22e p.845)","Urinalysis for uric acid crystals; urinary uric acid:creatinine ratio >1 supports acute uric-acid nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.593)"],"redFlags":["Suspected APL (ecchymoses/oozing from IV sites, DIC) - a haematologic emergency; coagulopathy can be rapidly fatal and warrants prompt differentiation therapy (Harrison 22e p.828, p.834)","Hyperleukocytosis with blast count >100,000/uL and leukostasis - brain (stupor, headache, visual change, confusion, coma, sudden death) or lung (dyspnoea, hypoxaemia, respiratory failure); a potentially fatal emergency, especially in AML (Harrison 22e p.591)","Tumour lysis syndrome - hyperkalaemia (risk of fatal arrhythmia), hyperphosphataemia, hypocalcaemia (tetany), hyperuricaemia and acute kidney injury (Harrison 22e p.592-593)","Febrile neutropenia (fever with granulocytes <1500/uL) - medical emergency requiring urgent empirical antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.575)","CNS symptoms - headache, visual change, focal/non-focal neurologic deficit - may indicate CNS leukaemia or intracranial bleed (Harrison 22e p.829-830)"],"prognosis":"Outcome is dominated by age and genetics. In AML, cytogenetic/molecular findings at diagnosis are the strongest predictors: t(15;17)/APL has a very good prognosis (~85% cured), core-binding-factor AML (t(8;21), inv(16)) is favourable (~55% cured), normal-karyotype AML is intermediate (~40% cured), and TP53 mutation, complex karyotype, t(6;9), inv(3) or monosomy 7 are adverse with little chance of cure without transplant. NPM1 mutation without FLT3-ITD is favourable, whereas FLT3-ITD is adverse. Advancing age, poor performance status, antecedent haematologic disorder/preceding cytopenias, therapy-related disease and high presenting WBC all worsen outcome; only ~32% of AML patients survive 5 years overall. In ALL, outcome is strongly age-dependent - cure rates ~90% in children falling to <10% in elderly/frail adults; achievement of molecular complete remission (MRD negativity) is the most important on-treatment prognostic factor (Harrison 22e p.825-828, p.846-847).","pitfalls":["Relying on an automated blood count can miss acute leukaemia - 8% of ALL patients (and <5% of AML) have no circulating blasts; review the smear (Harrison 22e p.828, p.844).","In hyperleukocytosis, arterial blood gas oxygen tension can be spuriously low because the high WBC mass consumes plasma oxygen in the sample; use pulse oximetry to assess oxygenation (Harrison 22e p.591).","High leukocyte and platelet counts can cause pseudohyperkalaemia from cell lysis after the blood is drawn (no ECG changes) - follow plasma rather than serum potassium (Harrison 22e p.593).","Avoid unnecessary RBC transfusion and intravascular volume depletion in hyperleukocytosis - both raise blood viscosity and can worsen leukostasis (Harrison 22e p.591).","Urinary alkalinisation with sodium bicarbonate is no longer recommended for tumour-lysis prophylaxis (Harrison 22e p.593).","Even minor bleeding in a thrombocytopenic leukaemia patient justifies immediate platelet transfusion because qualitative platelet dysfunction and infection increase bleeding risk independent of the count (Harrison 22e p.829)."],"severityClassification":"AML is classified by the WHO 2022 system primarily on defining genetic abnormalities (e.g. PML-RARA, RUNX1-RUNX1T1, CBFB-MYH11, KMT2A rearrangement, NPM1 or CEBPA mutation) and, where genetics are absent, by differentiation; the historical >=20%-blast diagnostic threshold has been removed for genetically defined entities. Prognostic risk is stratified by the 2022 European LeukemiaNet (ELN) scheme into favourable, intermediate and adverse categories based on cytogenetic and molecular findings at diagnosis (APL is risk-stratified separately, low- vs high-risk by presenting WBC, threshold 10,000/uL). ALL is subtyped immunologically into B-lineage and T-lineage, with risk stratification (standard vs high) using age, WBC count, immunophenotype, cytogenetics/molecular lesions (notably Ph/BCR-ABL) and MRD status (Harrison 22e p.826-827, p.834, p.846-847).","references":["Harrison 22e p.575","Harrison 22e p.591","Harrison 22e p.592","Harrison 22e p.593","Harrison 22e p.825","Harrison 22e p.826","Harrison 22e p.827","Harrison 22e p.828","Harrison 22e p.829","Harrison 22e p.830","Harrison 22e p.834","Harrison 22e p.844","Harrison 22e p.845","Harrison 22e p.846","Harrison 22e p.847"],"crossLinks":["dic","FEBRILE_NEUTROPENIA","hyperkalemia"]},"adrenal_crisis":{"name":"Adrenal crisis","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.636, p.2353, p.3110-3112, p.3133","clinicalPearls":["Adrenal crisis typically emerges after a prolonged prodrome of nonspecific complaints rather than appearing out of the blue (p.3111).","Crisis is more frequent in PRIMARY adrenal insufficiency because both glucocorticoid AND mineralocorticoid secretion are lost (p.3111).","Postural hypotension can progress to frank hypovolaemic shock (p.3111).","Prospective data: about 8.3 adrenal crises per 100 patient-years and roughly 0.5 crisis-related deaths per 100 patient-years (p.3111).","Adrenal crisis is classified as a form of endocrine/distributive shock (p.2353).","In suspected crisis, it is reasonable to draw a baseline cortisol (and ACTH), start replacement, and defer formal stimulation testing — diagnostic testing must never delay treatment (p.3111).","Starting thyroid hormone replacement in an undiagnosed patient can precipitate adrenal crisis, because thyroid hormone shortens cortisol half-life; screen for adrenal insufficiency before treating hypothyroidism in possible polyendocrine syndrome (p.3133)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute adrenal insufficiency reflects an absolute or relative deficiency of cortisol, in primary disease compounded by loss of aldosterone (mineralocorticoid). Glucocorticoid deficiency impairs vascular tone, gluconeogenesis and loss of feedback inhibition of AVP release (contributing to hyponatraemia and hypoglycaemia), while mineralocorticoid deficiency causes renal salt wasting with volume depletion, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia and rising creatinine. The result is hypotension that may decompensate into hypovolaemic shock. Crisis is usually unmasked by a stressor that exceeds the inadequate cortisol reserve — intercurrent illness, surgery or other stress, or increased glucocorticoid inactivation as in hyperthyroidism (p.3111).","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of distributive/endocrine shock (septic, anaphylactic, neurogenic) (p.2353)","Hypovolaemic and cardiogenic shock (p.2353)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute abdomen-like presentation with abdominal tenderness, nausea, vomiting and fever can mimic an intra-abdominal infection (p.3111)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute abdomen / surgical abdomen (abdominal pain, tenderness, nausea, vomiting) (p.3111)","Primary neurologic disease — decreased responsiveness progressing to stupor and coma (p.3111)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Baseline serum cortisol AND plasma ACTH drawn before starting steroids, with formal testing deferred (p.3111)","Short cosyntropin (ACTH stimulation) test once stabilised — failure cutoff roughly cortisol <450-500 nmol/L (16-18 ug/dL) at 30-60 min (p.3111)","Plasma renin to confirm mineralocorticoid deficiency in primary disease (p.3111)","Adrenal steroid autoantibodies to assess for autoimmune adrenalitis (p.3111)","Adrenal CT for hemorrhage, infiltration or masses if autoantibodies negative (p.3111)","Very-long-chain fatty acids in autoantibody-negative males to exclude X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy (p.3111)","Hypothalamic-pituitary MRI when ACTH is inappropriately low (secondary cause) (p.3111)","Associated labs: hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, hypoglycaemia, raised creatinine, normochromic anaemia, lymphocytosis, eosinophilia (p.3110)"],"redFlags":["Refractory or postural hypotension progressing to hypovolaemic shock (p.3111)","Decreased responsiveness, stupor or coma (p.3111)","Hyperpigmentation suggesting underlying primary adrenal insufficiency (p.3110)"],"prognosis":"Acute adrenal insufficiency is potentially lethal (p.636). Even with established care, crises and crisis-related deaths continue to occur (~8.3 crises and ~0.5 deaths per 100 patient-years) (p.3111).","pitfalls":["Do NOT let diagnostic testing delay treatment in suspected crisis (p.3111).","Random/baseline serum cortisol has limited diagnostic value because levels may be coincidentally low from normal diurnal rhythm, and many secondary cases have near-normal baseline cortisol that only fails on stimulation testing (p.3111).","Do NOT start thyroid hormone before excluding/treating adrenal insufficiency — it can precipitate crisis (p.3133).","Hyperthyroidism increases glucocorticoid inactivation and can trigger crisis (p.3111).","Adrenal insufficiency can be masked by concurrent primary hypothyroidism, which prolongs cortisol half-life (p.3133)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.636","Harrison 22e p.2353","Harrison 22e p.3110","Harrison 22e p.3111","Harrison 22e p.3133"],"crossLinks":["SEPTIC_SHOCK","anaphylaxis","hyponatremia","hyperkalemia","hypoglycemia"]},"aki":{"name":"Acute kidney injury","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chap. 55 (Azotemia and Urinary Abnormalities), printed pp. 339-342","clinicalPearls":["AKI is conventionally categorised by site of insult: prerenal (perfusion), intrinsic/renal (vascular, glomerular, tubulointerstitial), and postrenal (obstruction); excluding pre- and post-renal causes points to an intrinsic parenchymal disease (p.340).","Reduced renal perfusion (prerenal) accounts for roughly 40-80% of cases and is typically readily reversible if perfusion is restored promptly (p.340).","Urinary tract obstruction (postrenal) accounts for under 5% of cases but is usually reversible and should be excluded early, generally with renal ultrasound looking for ureteric/pelvic dilatation (p.340).","Ischaemic and toxic acute tubular necrosis (ATN) together account for about 90% of intrinsic AKI (p.340).","Prerenal azotaemia and ATN lie on a spectrum of hypoperfusion: prerenal reverses promptly with restored perfusion, whereas ATN involves structural tubular injury (p.340).","Microscopic examination of urine sediment is a key bedside test in AKI; dark (muddy-brown) granular casts and/or tubular epithelial cells strongly suggest ATN (p.340).","Because a single kidney can clear adequately, complete obstructive AKI requires bladder-outlet/urethral obstruction, bilateral ureteric obstruction, or unilateral obstruction in a solitary kidney (p.340).","RBC casts in the urine indicate glomerular disease (acute nephritic picture) and are an indication for early renal biopsy; they are highly specific but very insensitive for glomerulonephritis (p.341).","The kidney is particularly vulnerable to toxic injury because it receives about 25% of cardiac output and concentrates and metabolises toxins (p.340).","Distinguishing acute from chronic kidney disease can be difficult because anaemia, hypocalcaemia and hyperphosphataemia occur in both; small echogenic kidneys with cortical thinning on ultrasound and radiographic renal osteodystrophy favour chronicity (p.339-340)."],"pathophysiology":"In prerenal states, a fall in true or effective arterial volume lowers mean arterial pressure and triggers sympathetic activation, the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, and vasopressin release; GFR is sustained by prostaglandin-mediated afferent arteriolar dilatation and angiotensin II-mediated efferent arteriolar constriction. Once mean arterial pressure falls below about 80 mmHg, GFR declines steeply. This autoregulation explains why NSAIDs (which block prostaglandin-mediated afferent dilatation) and ACE inhibitors/ARBs (which reduce efferent constriction) can precipitate hemodynamically mediated AKI when volume or perfusion pressure is already reduced, and why patients with bilateral renal artery stenosis (or stenosis of a solitary kidney) are especially susceptible to a precipitous GFR fall on ACEi/ARBs. Sustained hypoperfusion progresses to ischaemic ATN. Nephrotoxic ATN results from a combination of intrarenal vasoconstriction, direct tubular toxicity, and intratubular obstruction (p.340).","additionalDifferentials":["Prerenal azotaemia from true volume loss (GI haemorrhage, burns, diarrhoea, diuretics) (p.340)","Prerenal from volume sequestration (pancreatitis, peritonitis, rhabdomyolysis) (p.340)","Prerenal from reduced effective arterial volume (cardiogenic shock, sepsis, severe heart failure, hepatorenal syndrome) (p.340)","Ischaemic ATN (major surgery, trauma, severe hypovolaemia, overwhelming sepsis, extensive burns) (p.340)","Nephrotoxic ATN (drug/toxin-induced) (p.340)","Acute (allergic) interstitial nephritis from drugs (antibiotics, NSAIDs, proton pump inhibitors) (p.340)","Acute glomerulonephritis / vasculitis (acute nephritic syndrome) (p.341)","Renal microvascular disease (haemolytic-uraemic syndrome, thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, malignant hypertension) (p.341)","Large-vessel occlusion: atheroemboli, thromboemboli, in-situ thrombosis, aortic dissection, vasculitis, renal vein thrombosis (p.341)","Postrenal obstructive uropathy (urethral/bladder-outlet, bilateral ureteric, or solitary-kidney obstruction) (p.340)","Cast nephropathy in multiple myeloma (a cause of anuria) (p.341)"],"infectionMimics":["Severe systemic infection (bacterial or viral) causing tubulointerstitial AKI (p.341)","Sepsis producing prerenal hypoperfusion and/or ischaemic ATN (p.340)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chronic kidney disease presenting de novo (anaemia, hypocalcaemia, hyperphosphataemia overlap with AKI) (p.339-340)","Systemic lupus erythematosus, sarcoidosis, Sjogren syndrome, lymphoma or leukaemia infiltrating the tubulointerstitium (p.341)","Atheroembolic disease (look for livedo reticularis, distal peripheral infarcts, eosinophilia) (p.341)","Rhabdomyolysis (may produce nonoliguric ATN with FENa under 1%) (p.340)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine sediment microscopy for casts (muddy-brown granular and tubular epithelial casts in ATN; hyaline/granular or normal in prerenal; RBC casts in glomerulonephritis; WBC casts in interstitial nephritis) (p.340-341)","Urine sodium and FENa (prerenal: urine Na under 20 mmol/L, FENa under 1%; ATN: FENa typically over 1-2%) (p.340)","Urine osmolality (over 500 mosmol/L concentrated urine in prerenal; under 350 in oliguric ATN) (p.340)","BUN/creatinine ratio (over 20:1 prerenal vs ~10-15:1 in ATN) and urine/plasma creatinine ratio (over 40 prerenal vs under 20 ATN) (p.340)","Renal ultrasound for obstruction (ureteric/pelvic dilatation) and to assess kidney size/echogenicity (acute vs chronic) (p.340)","Furosemide (MAG3) renogram when obstruction is suspected despite a non-dilated ultrasound (early obstruction, encasement by tumour, retroperitoneal fibrosis) (p.340)","Cystatin C-based or combined creatinine/cystatin C eGFR where muscle mass or other factors make creatinine-based eGFR unreliable (p.339)","Serologic/glomerular work-up when RBC casts present (anti-GBM, ANCA, complement, cryoglobulins, hepatitis B/C, HIV) and early renal biopsy (p.341)","Phase-contrast microscopy for dysmorphic red cells (acanthocytes) suggesting glomerular bleeding (p.341)"],"redFlags":["Oliguria (24-h output under 400 mL) or anuria (under 100 mL); oliguria is never normal as at least ~400 mL of maximally concentrated urine is needed to excrete the daily osmolar load (p.341)","Anuria suggesting complete bilateral obstruction, vascular catastrophe (dissection/arterial occlusion), renal vein thrombosis, myeloma cast nephropathy, cortical necrosis, severe ATN, severe rapidly progressive GN, combined NSAID+ACEi/ARB, or shock (p.341)","RBC casts or dysmorphic red cells (acute nephritic/glomerular emergency warranting urgent biopsy) (p.341)","Dialysis-indication complications of AKI (refractory hyperkalaemia, severe metabolic acidosis, refractory volume overload/pulmonary oedema, uraemic complications) (existing entry; supported p.2330 RRT criteria for overload/acidosis/hyperkalaemia)"],"prognosis":"Prerenal AKI is usually readily reversible when perfusion is restored, and postrenal AKI is usually reversible once obstruction is relieved if recognised early. In nephrotoxic ATN, stopping the offending agent and stabilising blood pressure often suffice, with regeneration of tubular cells and recovery without dialysis. Nonoliguric ATN tends to have less severe potassium and acid-base disturbance and a more rapid recovery than oliguric AKI (p.340-341).","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude obstruction early; ultrasound can be falsely negative very early in obstruction or when ureters cannot dilate (tumour encasement, retroperitoneal fibrosis) (p.340)","Measuring urine eosinophils to diagnose interstitial nephritis: eosinophiluria is raised in many causes of AKI, has no diagnostic utility, and is no longer recommended (p.340)","Assuming FENa over 1% always means ATN: it may be under 1% in mild nonoliguric ATN (e.g., rhabdomyolysis) and in prerenal-like states such as CHF, cirrhosis or hepatorenal syndrome (p.340)","Over-relying on creatinine-based eGFR: muscle mass, sex, race assumptions can mislead (e.g., a bodybuilder with high creatinine) (p.339)","Attributing RBC casts to interstitial nephritis: their presence should instead prompt a search for glomerular disease (p.340-341)"],"severityClassification":"AKI severity is staged by the KDIGO criteria (referenced as Kidney Disease: Improving Global Outcomes; p.341, line ref to KDIGO Controversies Conference). Detailed KDIGO stage cut-offs (serum creatinine rise and urine-output thresholds for stages 1-3) are not specified in this source excerpt and are therefore not reproduced. The source's working framework classifies AKI anatomically into prerenal, intrinsic (renal), and postrenal categories, with supporting urinary indices distinguishing prerenal azotaemia from oliguric ATN (Table 55-2, p.340).","references":["Harrison 22e p.339","Harrison 22e p.340","Harrison 22e p.341","Harrison 22e p.342","Harrison 22e p.2330"],"crossLinks":["ckd","hyperkalemia","rhabdo","SEPSIS","glomerulonephritis"]},"anaphylaxis":{"name":"Anaphylaxis","system":"Allergy / Emergency","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch. 364 (Anaphylaxis), pp. 2812-2814; distributive-shock context p. 2358","clinicalPearls":["Onset is typically within seconds to minutes of exposure to the trigger; an exception is the delayed (about 3-6 h) reaction to mammalian meat seen in alpha-gal syndrome (p. 2812-2813).","Most episodes (about 80-90%) are uniphasic, but roughly 10-20% are biphasic, with symptoms recurring an hour or more after the initial reaction has resolved (p. 2812).","Cutaneous features (urticaria, flushing, generalized warmth) are the most common manifestation, present in over 90% of cases (p. 2812).","Cross-linking of allergen with IgE bound to FcepsilonRI receptors on mast cells and basophils is the usual initiating mechanism; sensitization requires prior exposure, so repeated exposure to the antigen is a key risk factor (p. 2812-2813).","Some severe reactions are non-IgE-mediated and can occur on first known exposure, because direct mast-cell activation needs no prior sensitization (e.g., via the MRGPRX2 receptor with neuromuscular blockers, quinolones, and icatibant) (p. 2813).","Severe Hymenoptera sting anaphylaxis, especially with prominent hypotension, may be the first presentation of underlying systemic mastocytosis; checking a baseline tryptase screens for this (p. 2812-2813).","Failure to give epinephrine within the first 20 minutes of symptoms is a risk factor for poor outcomes (p. 2813).","About 6% of Western populations carry hereditary alpha-tryptasemia, an extra TPSAB1 allele giving a high baseline tryptase that can confound interpretation and may itself be a risk factor for severe reactions (p. 2813)."],"pathophysiology":"Anaphylaxis is driven by mediators released chiefly from mast cells, basophils, and eosinophils. The most common mechanism is allergen cross-linking of IgE bound to FcepsilonRI on mast cells and basophils, triggering degranulation. Preformed granule contents include histamine, proteases (tryptase, chymase), proteoglycans (heparin, chondroitin sulfate), and TNF-alpha; newly generated arachidonic-acid products include cysteinyl leukotrienes, prostaglandin D2, and platelet-activating factor (PAF). Histamine produces flushing, urticaria, pruritus and, at high levels, hypotension and tachycardia; cysteinyl leukotrienes and PGD2 cause bronchoconstriction and increased microvascular permeability, with PGD2 also recruiting eosinophils and basophils. Serum PAF correlates with severity and is inversely related to PAF acetylhydrolase. Tryptase and chymase activate complement (generating the anaphylatoxins C3a and C5a) and the kallikrein-kinin system, with mediator effects likely additive or synergistic. Reactions can also be non-IgE-mediated: direct mast-cell activation (e.g., via MRGPRX2 by neuromuscular blockers, quinolones, icatibant; or opiates, vancomycin, radiocontrast, NSAIDs), complement activation (e.g., Cremophor-solubilized paclitaxel), or contact-system/bradykinin generation (e.g., oversulfated chondroitin sulfate heparin contaminant). (p. 2812-2813)","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic mastocytosis (elevated baseline tryptase; spindle-shaped mast cells on bone marrow) (p. 2813)","Pheochromocytoma (elevated urine metanephrines) (p. 2813)","Carcinoid syndrome (elevated urine 5-HIAA) (p. 2813)","Hereditary angioedema (decreased C4 during attacks) (p. 2813)","Acquired angioedema (decreased C1q) (p. 2813)","Systemic capillary leak syndrome (severe hypotension with poor response to first-line agents) (p. 2813)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Scombroid poisoning (ingested histamine from improperly stored histidine-rich fish such as tuna, mackerel, mahi-mahi); clinically mimics anaphylaxis but tryptase is NOT elevated because histamine is exogenous, and skin test/oral challenge to fish are negative (p. 2813)","Direct (pseudoallergic) mast-cell degranulation from opiates, neuromuscular blocking agents, vancomycin, or CT radiocontrast (e.g., via MRGPRX2) - clinically indistinguishable from IgE-mediated reactions but occurs without prior sensitization (p. 2813)","Vasovagal (neurally mediated) syncope - provoked by emotion, pain, or orthostatic stress and accompanied by bradycardia, in contrast to the tachycardia and urticaria/angioedema of anaphylaxis (p. 2813; cf. p. 156-157)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum tryptase peaks 60-90 min after onset and remains measurable for up to about 5 h; apply the '20% + 2' rule (an acute level >20% above the patient's baseline plus 2 ng/mL is diagnostic of clinically significant mast-cell activation) (p. 2813)","Repeat tryptase once the patient is stable to establish the individual baseline, since baseline serum tryptase varies widely and can be raised by hereditary alpha-tryptasemia or high environmental allergen exposure (p. 2813)","Serum histamine is of limited use acutely - its measurable window expires within about 1 h of onset (p. 2813)","Allergen-specific skin testing or serum-specific IgE to confirm a suspected drug or food trigger (p. 2813)","Baseline tryptase in sting-induced anaphylaxis to screen for underlying systemic mastocytosis (p. 2813)"],"redFlags":["Hypotension or hypoxia - the most dangerous features, risking cardiovascular collapse or respiratory failure (p. 2812)","Laryngeal edema (sensation of a lump in the throat, hoarseness, stridor) or angioedema of the epiglottis/larynx threatening mechanical airway obstruction (p. 2812)","Bronchial obstruction (chest tightness, wheezing), especially severe in patients with underlying asthma, who have increased mortality (p. 2812)","Underlying asthma or cardiovascular disease - predisposes to more rapid decompensation (p. 2812)","Severe GI involvement (vomiting, crampy abdominal pain); bowel-wall angioedema can deplete intravascular volume enough to cause cardiovascular collapse (p. 2812)"],"prognosis":"Severe and even fatal complications can develop within minutes of symptom onset, making early recognition critical; deaths occur via airway/bronchial obstruction, laryngeal angioedema, or vascular collapse. Delaying epinephrine beyond the first 20 minutes worsens outcomes. Risk is higher with underlying asthma or cardiovascular disease. Natural history varies by trigger: most childhood egg, milk, soy, and wheat allergy resolves, whereas about 80% of peanut allergy persists for life. (p. 2812-2813)","pitfalls":["Treat with epinephrine first - antihistamines, glucocorticoids, and bronchodilators are ancillary agents for residual urticaria/angioedema and bronchospasm once the patient is hemodynamically stable, not initial therapy (p. 2813).","Biphasic reactions in 10-20% recur an hour or more after apparent recovery, so a period of observation is needed after initial resolution (p. 2812).","A normal tryptase does not exclude anaphylaxis - it is not elevated in scombroid poisoning (exogenous histamine), and an acutely 'normal' value may still be diagnostic relative to a low individual baseline (apply the 20% + 2 rule) (p. 2813).","Non-IgE-mediated reactions can occur on first exposure, so the absence of prior exposure does not rule out an anaphylactic reaction (p. 2813).","Beta blockers can blunt the response to epinephrine; consider an alternative antihypertensive in patients at high risk of needing emergency epinephrine (p. 2813).","Skin/serum specific-IgE testing to foods has relatively poor predictive value, so a positive test alone does not confirm clinical reactivity (p. 2814)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2812","Harrison 22e p.2813","Harrison 22e p.2814","Harrison 22e p.2358"],"crossLinks":["angioedema_acei","asthma_exac","panic"]},"anemia_sympt":{"name":"Symptomatic anaemia","system":"Hematology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.66 Anemia and Polycythemia (printed p.444-451); Ch.106 Anemia Due to Acute Blood Loss (printed p.850-851)","clinicalPearls":["Symptoms and signs of anemia correlate poorly with the actual haemoglobin/haematocrit; clinical severity reflects the rate of onset and the patient's cardiovascular reserve rather than the absolute level (p.444).","Slowly developing anemia is remarkably well tolerated because three compensatory mechanisms develop over time: rapid rise in cardiac output (minutes), a rise in 2,3-DPG that lowers haemoglobin oxygen affinity and improves tissue oxygen offloading (hours to days), and an expansion of plasma volume (weeks) (p.444).","Plasma-volume expansion can occasionally overwhelm the heart and precipitate features of heart failure/oedema in chronic anemia (p.444).","Patients with limited cardiac reserve or atherosclerosis become symptomatic (angina, TIA/stroke) at a higher haematocrit than those with normal cardiovascular function (p.444).","The absolute reticulocyte count is the key first branch point for mechanism: an elevated count points to blood loss or haemolysis, while a low or inappropriately low count points to impaired production (p.448-449).","Manual percentage reticulocyte counts must be corrected for the haematocrit (a 1% count becomes an apparent 2% at a haematocrit of ~23%); the machine-derived absolute reticulocyte count needs no such correction (p.447).","Around 75% of all anemia is hypoproliferative, most often from mild-to-moderate iron deficiency or inflammation (p.449).","Anemia is frequently multifactorial (e.g., chronic inflammation plus occult GI blood loss in a patient on anti-inflammatory drugs), so iron status should be evaluated fully before and during treatment of any anemia (p.450).","Serum ferritin is an acute-phase reactant: it can be raised several-fold by inflammation and mask coexisting iron deficiency, whereas a ferritin <30 ug/L reliably indicates depleted body iron stores (p.449).","A high RDW favours iron deficiency (anisocytosis), whereas a low/normal RDW with microcytosis favours thalassaemia, where cells are more uniform in size (p.449).","Iron-deficiency pica and the neuropathy of B12 deficiency are deficiency-specific clues to seek on history and examination (p.444).","In acute blood loss the haemoglobin/haematocrit initially does NOT reflect the volume lost; the early picture is hypovolaemia, and the haemoglobin only falls over hours as fluid shifts and resuscitation dilute the blood (p.850)."],"pathophysiology":"Anemia results from a defect in any of the three requirements for normal red-cell production: renal erythropoietin (EPO) output, a functioning erythroid marrow, and adequate substrate (especially iron). EPO rises logarithmically once haemoglobin falls below ~100-120 g/L, but in chronic kidney disease and chronic inflammation EPO is inappropriately low for the degree of anemia. With adequate EPO and substrate, marrow red-cell output can rise four- to fivefold over 1-2 weeks. Mechanistically anemia is either hyperproductive/regenerative (blood loss or haemolysis, with reticulocytosis) or hypoproliferative (nutritional deficiency, marrow replacement, absent precursors, stem-cell defects, or EPO deficiency). Inflammatory cytokines such as TNF lower EPO and raise hepcidin, which blocks iron absorption and release from stores, producing the anemia of inflammation (p.443-450).","additionalDifferentials":["Anemia of inflammation/chronic disease (diagnosis of exclusion; MCV often normal; hepcidin-mediated block of iron reutilization) (p.448-450)","Thalassaemia (microcytic, low/normal RDW, defective globin synthesis) (p.448-449)","Sideroblastic anemia (defective heme synthesis; microcytic) (p.448-449)","Megaloblastic anemia from B12 or folate deficiency (oval macrocytes, hypersegmented neutrophils) (p.449)","Myelodysplastic syndrome (macrocytic anemia or pancytopenia; stem-cell defect) (p.449-450)","Anemia of chronic kidney disease (EPO lack; usually present once GFR <30) (p.450)","Anemia of aging/older adults (low EPO for degree of anemia; 11% community-dwelling, up to 40% nursing-home over 65) (p.450)","Aplastic anemia (hypocellular marrow, pancytopenia) (p.450)","Pure red cell aplasia (isolated very low reticulocytes; autoimmune or parvovirus B19) (p.450)","Acute/chronic leukaemia and other primary marrow neoplasms (p.450)","Copper deficiency (with neutropenia and neurologic disease) and vitamin C deficiency/scurvy (p.450)","Drug-induced macrocytosis (chemotherapy, some antiseizure drugs) (p.449)","Alcohol use, liver disease, hypothyroidism (round macrocytosis) (p.449)"],"infectionMimics":["Marrow-replacing infections such as histoplasmosis or tuberculosis causing a myelophthisic anemia (p.450)","Parvovirus B19 causing pure red cell aplasia (p.450)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute haemorrhage where the initial normal haemoglobin masks major blood loss; hypovolaemia dominates and anemia appears only after fluid shifts/resuscitation (p.850)","Spurious/relative changes: serum iron and transferrin saturation vary diurnally, which can mislead iron-status interpretation (p.449)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Corrected (or absolute) reticulocyte count as the first mechanistic discriminator (p.447-448)","Red cell indices and RDW (MCV-based microcytic/normocytic/macrocytic classification; RDW to separate iron deficiency from thalassaemia) (p.445-449)","Full iron studies: serum iron, TIBC, percent transferrin saturation (normal sat 25-50%), and ferritin for total-body stores (p.449)","Haemolysis panel: LDH (raised), haptoglobin (low), free haemoglobin in blood/urine, and direct antiglobulin (antibody) test when reticulocytes are high and bleeding excluded (p.449)","Blood-film morphology review (schistocytes, spherocytes, sickle cells, target cells, teardrop cells, macro-ovalocytes, hypersegmented neutrophils, Howell-Jolly bodies, basophilic stippling) to direct focused testing (p.445-449)","Serum EPO level and BUN/creatinine for the normocytic, hypoproliferative workup (p.450-451)","Serum protein electrophoresis / free light chains when myeloma is considered (p.451)","Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy (biopsy before aspirate) when marrow infiltration or generalised dysfunction is suspected (p.448)"],"redFlags":["Circulating blasts or other immature white cells, severe pancytopenia, or a very low reticulocyte count (<0.1%) mandating urgent marrow examination (p.448)","Myelophthisic blood film (nucleated red cells, teardrop cells, immature white cells) suggesting marrow infiltration/replacement (p.450)","Hypotension, angina, or other signs of cardiac compromise as clear indications that the anemia is not being tolerated (p.450)","Acute haemorrhage with sudden loss of >25-30% of blood volume: vascular instability and decreased organ perfusion supervene and cannot be compensated by sympathetic mechanisms (p.850-851)","Loss of consciousness or acute kidney injury early in major haemorrhage, reflecting hypovolaemic threat to brain and kidneys before any fall in haemoglobin (p.850)","Clinical signs of concealed internal bleeding: Grey Turner sign (flank ecchymosis, retroperitoneal), Cullen sign (umbilical ecchymosis), or dullness to chest percussion (intrapleural) (p.850)"],"prognosis":"Anemia in those over 65 is associated with increased risk of death, hospitalization, and frailty. In acute posthemorrhagic anemia, if bleeding stops the marrow response (reticulocytosis, rising EPO) gradually corrects the anemia. Myelodysplasia has a variable course, from years of stability to rapid evolution into acute leukaemia (p.449-450).","pitfalls":["Anchoring on a normal haemoglobin in early acute haemorrhage and underestimating blood loss; an abrupt fall in haemoglobin should prompt suspicion of acute posthemorrhagic anemia regardless of the given history (p.850).","Estimates of blood volume lost at a bloody scene are often overestimated, while concealed internal bleeding may be missed entirely (p.850).","Relying on ferritin alone during inflammation, which can falsely normalise or elevate it despite true iron deficiency (p.449).","Treating macrocytosis as automatically B12/folate deficiency; if megaloblastic markers (hypersegmentation, macro-ovalocytes) are present but nutritional tests are normal, suspect myelodysplasia and proceed to marrow examination (p.451).","Forgetting that the anemia of chronic disease/inflammation frequently has a normal MCV, so a normal MCV does not exclude it (p.449-451).","Performing marrow aspiration before biopsy, which distorts the biopsy with aspiration-induced bleeding (p.448).","Overlooking drug causes: ACE inhibitors can suppress EPO; chemotherapy and some antiseizure drugs cause macrocytosis (p.449-450)."],"severityClassification":"Two complementary classifications. (1) By red-cell size (Wintrobe): microcytic (iron deficiency, thalassaemia, sideroblastic, anemia of inflammation), normocytic (renal disease, marrow invasion, myeloma, aplastic anemia, pure red cell aplasia, endocrinopathies), and macrocytic - subdivided into oval macrocytes (B12/folate deficiency, myelodysplasia, drugs) and round macrocytes (alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism, hypoxia, reticulocytosis, smoking, dysproteinemia). (2) By mechanism: hyperproductive (blood loss, haemolysis - reticulocytes high) versus underproductive/hypoproliferative (nutritional deficiency, absent precursors, lack of EPO, marrow replacement, stem-cell defects - reticulocytes low/inappropriate). The absolute reticulocyte count plus MCV drive the diagnostic algorithm (p.448-451). For acute blood loss: vascular instability appears with sudden loss of ~25% of total blood volume, and >30% loss exceeds compensatory mechanisms (p.851).","references":["Harrison 22e p.443","Harrison 22e p.444","Harrison 22e p.445","Harrison 22e p.446","Harrison 22e p.447","Harrison 22e p.448","Harrison 22e p.449","Harrison 22e p.450","Harrison 22e p.451","Harrison 22e p.850","Harrison 22e p.851"],"crossLinks":["peptic_ulcer","ckd","myeloma"]},"angioedema_acei":{"name":"ACE-inhibitor / hereditary angioedema","system":"Allergy / Emergency","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Ch. 363 (Urticaria, Angioedema, and Allergic Rhinitis), pp. 2807-2809; with supporting context from Ch. 265 (Heart Failure: Management) p. 1995 and Ch. 271 (Hypertensive Vascular Disease) p. 2024.","clinicalPearls":["ACE-inhibitor angioedema occurs in roughly 0.2-0.7% of exposed patients and is driven by impaired (delayed) degradation of bradykinin rather than by an IgE/mast-cell allergic mechanism (p. 2807).","Recognized risk factors for ACE inhibitor-related angioedema include Black race, prior organ transplant, female sex, smoking, and increasing age (p. 2807).","Bradykinin-mediated angioedema (whether from an ACE inhibitor or from C1-inhibitor deficiency) is characterized by the conspicuous ABSENCE of accompanying urticaria or pruritus (p. 2808).","Compared with histamine-/mast-cell-driven urticaria-angioedema, bradykinin-mediated swelling tends to be more painful than pruritic, with minimal surrounding erythema, and is slower to resolve, persisting >24 h (pp. 2807-2808).","The most common sites of angioedema are periorbital and perioral; lesions are neither symmetric nor dependent in distribution (p. 2808).","Gastrointestinal-tract involvement is a frequent feature of bradykinin-mediated angioedema and may present as abdominal colic with or without nausea and vomiting; this can mimic an acute abdomen and lead to unnecessary surgery (p. 2808).","ACE inhibitors are a recognized (if uncommon) cause of intestinal angioedema, which can present with diarrhea/abdominal symptoms (Ch. on diarrhea, GI section).","No residual scarring follows urticaria or angioedema unless an underlying vasculitic process is present (p. 2808)."],"pathophysiology":"ACE inhibitors block angiotensin-converting enzyme, which normally also degrades the vasodilator peptide bradykinin; inhibition leads to delayed bradykinin breakdown and accumulation, increasing vascular permeability in the deep dermis and subcutaneous/submucosal tissues and producing angioedema (pp. 2807, 2024). This is mechanistically the same bradykinin pathway implicated in hereditary angioedema, where deficient or dysfunctional C1 inhibitor (C1INH) fails to restrain activated factor XII (Hageman factor) and kallikrein, generating excess bradykinin (p. 2808). Because the mechanism is bradykinin- rather than histamine-/IgE-mediated, there is no associated urticaria or pruritus, and the process is fundamentally distinct from allergic mast-cell-driven angioedema (p. 2808). The same bradykinin-accumulation mechanism explains the increased angioedema risk seen with combined ACE/neprilysin inhibition, since neprilysin also degrades bradykinin (Ch. 265, p. 1995).","additionalDifferentials":["Allergic (histaminergic, IgE/mast-cell-mediated) angioedema, typically with concurrent urticaria/pruritus and a more rapid onset and resolution (pp. 2807-2808).","Hereditary angioedema (HAE) due to C1-inhibitor deficiency (type 1) or dysfunctional C1INH (type 2), suggested by childhood/adolescent onset, family history, recurrent attacks, and failure to respond to H1 antihistamines (p. 2808).","Type 3 (normal-C1INH) HAE, sometimes associated with a mutant factor XII and normal complement protein levels (p. 2808).","Acquired C1-inhibitor deficiency from excessive C1INH consumption or an anti-C1INH autoantibody in the setting of lymphoproliferative or autoimmune disease, lacking a family history (p. 2808).","Idiopathic angioedema without an identifiable trigger (p. 2807).","Urticarial vasculitis when lesions are painful rather than pruritic, last >36 h, or scar (p. 2809).","Angioedema from other bradykinin-affecting or culprit drugs listed alongside ACE inhibitors: NSAIDs/aspirin, dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitors, radiocontrast media, opioids, antimicrobials, and angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor (ARNI/sacubitril-valsartan) (Table 363-1, p. 2807; Ch. 265, p. 1995)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Anaphylaxis / allergic angioedema with airway involvement (histamine-mediated) — important to distinguish because the bradykinin form responds poorly to anaphylaxis therapy (pp. 2807-2808).","Hereditary or acquired C1-inhibitor-deficiency angioedema presenting with laryngeal edema or recurrent abdominal colic (p. 2808).","An acute surgical abdomen, mimicked by gastrointestinal angioedema causing abdominal colic, nausea, and vomiting (p. 2808)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis of bradykinin-mediated angioedema is largely clinical; the lack of pruritus/urticaria and failure to respond to H1 antihistamines point away from histaminergic causes (p. 2808).","For isolated angioedema without urticaria: basic chemistries with liver function tests and complement levels (p. 2809).","C1INH antigen level (deficient in type 1 HAE) and a catalytic C1INH functional assay (abnormal in type 2 HAE) to evaluate for C1-inhibitor deficiency (p. 2808).","C4 and C2 levels: chronically depleted in C1INH-deficiency states and fall further during attacks; in acquired forms, C1q and C1 function are also reduced (p. 2808).","Consider evaluation for paraproteinemia / underlying lymphoproliferative or autoimmune disease when acquired C1INH deficiency is suspected (p. 2809)."],"redFlags":["Upper-respiratory-tract / laryngeal angioedema can cause transient airway obstruction and is potentially life-threatening (p. 2808).","Tongue, oropharyngeal, or laryngeal swelling indicating threatened airway (pp. 2807-2808).","Progressive or persistent swelling lasting >24 h, characteristic of bradykinin-mediated angioedema and warning against assuming a self-limited allergic course (p. 2808)."],"prognosis":"Bradykinin-mediated angioedema typically takes hours to days to resolve and persists >24 h, in contrast to the shorter-lived allergic form (pp. 2807-2808). The leading hazard is laryngeal/upper-airway obstruction, which can be life-threatening (p. 2808). No residual scarring occurs unless there is an underlying vasculitis (p. 2808).","pitfalls":["Because the swelling is bradykinin- and not histamine-mediated, it responds poorly to the standard allergic-angioedema/anaphylaxis measures (adrenaline, antihistamines, glucocorticoids); relying on these can give false reassurance (mechanism is non-histaminergic per p. 2808).","Onset is not necessarily immediate after starting the drug — ACE-inhibitor angioedema reflects DELAYED bradykinin degradation and can appear well after therapy has begun, so a long-standing ACE inhibitor should not be dismissed as the cause (delayed-degradation mechanism, p. 2807).","Absence of urticaria/pruritus can lead clinicians to overlook angioedema or misattribute it; isolated angioedema without hives is a hallmark of the bradykinin mechanism, not evidence against it (p. 2808).","Gastrointestinal angioedema mimicking an acute abdomen may prompt unnecessary surgical intervention (p. 2808).","Switching to or overlapping with an angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor (ARNI) is hazardous: neprilysin inhibition further blocks bradykinin breakdown, ARNI is contraindicated in anyone with prior angioedema, and a washout gap is required when transitioning from an ACE inhibitor (Ch. 265, p. 1995)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison classifies urticaria/angioedema by duration as acute (<6 weeks) versus chronic (>6 weeks) rather than by a graded severity scale; this distinction helps identify precipitating factors (pp. 2807, 2809). No formal angioedema severity-grading scheme is given for ACE-inhibitor angioedema in this source.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2807","Harrison 22e p.2808","Harrison 22e p.2809","Harrison 22e p.1995","Harrison 22e p.2024"],"crossLinks":["anaphylaxis"]},"aortic_dissection":{"name":"Aortic dissection","system":"Vascular emergency","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"2168-2170, 105-109","clinicalPearls":["Pain characteristically migrates as the dissection propagates, often shifting to the interscapular region of the back (Harrison 22e p.2169).","Systemic hypertension coexists in about 70% of patients with aortic dissection (Harrison 22e p.2168).","Peak incidence is in the sixth and seventh decades, with men affected about twice as often as women (Harrison 22e p.2169).","A normal ECG showing no ischemia helps distinguish dissection from myocardial infarction in a patient with chest pain, though dissection can rarely involve a coronary ostium and cause an actual MI (Harrison 22e p.2169).","On chest x-ray, ascending dissection often produces a widened superior mediastinum; a pleural effusion (usually left-sided) may appear and is typically serosanguineous rather than a sign of rupture unless accompanied by hypotension and a falling hematocrit (Harrison 22e p.2169).","Although 'tearing' or 'ripping' pain is classic, acute aortic emergencies also commonly present with knifelike pain (Harrison 22e p.106).","Stanford type A or B classification is more practical for management than DeBakey, because DeBakey types I and II are managed similarly (Harrison 22e p.2168)."],"pathophysiology":"Dissection begins with a tear of the intima, most often along the high-shear right lateral wall of the ascending aorta or in the descending thoracic aorta just below the ligamentum arteriosum. Pulsatile flow dissects along the elastic lamellar plates of the media to create a false lumen; the initiating event may be a primary intimal tear with secondary dissection into the media, or a medial hemorrhage that secondarily disrupts the intima. The dissection usually propagates distally (sometimes proximally), may be limited distally by atherosclerotic plaque, and can re-enter the true lumen through a secondary distal intimal disruption. Underlying medial degeneration and conditions that raise aortic wall stress predispose to dissection. Two related radiologic/pathologic variants are intramural hematoma (rupture of vasa vasorum bleeding into the wall, without an intimal flap) and penetrating atherosclerotic ulcer (plaque erosion into the media); both can progress to dissection and rupture (Harrison 22e p.2168-2169).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute myocardial infarction / acute coronary syndrome (chest pain, and dissection can occlude a coronary ostium) (Harrison 22e p.2169)","Pulmonary embolism (Harrison 22e p.108)","Pericarditis (Harrison 22e p.106)","Esophageal pain / spasm, which can mimic angina (Harrison 22e p.106)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Penetrating atherosclerotic ulcer of the aorta (Harrison 22e p.2168)","Acute intramural hematoma of the aorta (Harrison 22e p.2168)","Aortic rupture (Harrison 22e p.2168)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CT (with contrast) — most commonly used given proximity of scanners to emergency rooms and rapidity; sensitivity and specificity >90% for the intimal flap and extent (Harrison 22e p.2169)","MRI — highly accurate for intimal flap, extent, intramural hemorrhage and penetrating ulcers; sensitivity and specificity >90% (Harrison 22e p.2169)","Transesophageal echocardiography — ~98% sensitivity and ~90% specificity for ascending and descending dissection (but not the arch); also assesses aortic regurgitation and pericardial effusion (Harrison 22e p.2169)","Transthoracic echocardiography — rapid bedside test, overall sensitivity 60-85% (>80% for proximal ascending dissection), less useful for arch and descending aorta (Harrison 22e p.2169)","Chest x-ray — may show widened superior mediastinum and/or pleural effusion (Harrison 22e p.2169)","ECG — to look for ischemia and help differentiate from MI (Harrison 22e p.2169)"],"redFlags":["Sudden, very severe tearing chest or interscapular back pain with diaphoresis, especially when it migrates (Harrison 22e p.2169)","New aortic regurgitation murmur (bounding pulses, wide pulse pressure, diastolic murmur along the right sternal border) in a patient with chest pain — common (>50%) in proximal dissection (Harrison 22e p.2169)","Pulse deficit or loss of pulses (Harrison 22e p.2169)","Hypotension with hemopericardium / cardiac tamponade signs (suggests retrograde type A dissection) (Harrison 22e p.2169)","Acute neurologic deficit — hemiplegia/hemianesthesia (carotid involvement) or paraplegia (spinal cord ischemia) (Harrison 22e p.2169)","Acute pulmonary edema (Harrison 22e p.2169, p.107)","Falling hematocrit with hypotension and pleural effusion (suggests rupture) (Harrison 22e p.2169)"],"prognosis":"In-hospital mortality after surgical treatment of dissection is reported at about 15-25%; for medically treated uncomplicated type B dissection it is about 12%. Major perioperative causes of death and morbidity include myocardial infarction, paraplegia, renal failure, tamponade, hemorrhage, and sepsis. With careful follow-up the long-term outlook after discharge is generally good, with roughly 60% 10-year survival; patients with Marfan syndrome are at high risk for post-dissection complications (Harrison 22e p.2170).","pitfalls":["Aortic dissection is a classic disease that is misdiagnosed as acute myocardial infarction — a dangerous error because anticoagulant/thrombolytic therapy for presumed MI can be catastrophic (Harrison 22e p.102, p.2169).","A left pleural effusion in dissection is usually serosanguineous and does not by itself indicate rupture unless there is accompanying hypotension and a falling hematocrit (Harrison 22e p.2169).","Transthoracic echocardiography is insensitive for arch and descending dissections, so a negative TTE does not exclude dissection (Harrison 22e p.2169)."],"severityClassification":"Stanford classification: type A involves the ascending aorta (proximal dissection) regardless of tear site or distal extension; type B is limited to the arch and/or descending aorta (distal dissection). DeBakey classification: type I — intimal tear in ascending aorta with propagation to arch, descending and possibly abdominal aorta; type II — limited to the ascending aorta; type III — tear in the descending aorta with distal propagation. Stanford A/B is more practical for management since DeBakey I and II are managed similarly (Harrison 22e p.2168).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2168","Harrison 22e p.2169","Harrison 22e p.2170","Harrison 22e p.102","Harrison 22e p.106","Harrison 22e p.107","Harrison 22e p.108"],"crossLinks":["acs","tamponade","htn_emergency","aaa","pericarditis"]},"aortic_stenosis":{"name":"Aortic stenosis (syncope)","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"203-204, 285-286, 328-329, 361, 1904-1909, 1933-1934, 1950","clinicalPearls":["The murmur of AS is a crescendo-decrescendo (midsystolic/ejection) murmur, usually loudest in the second right intercostal space and radiating into the carotids; it is the most common midsystolic murmur in adults (Harrison 22e p.328).","Apical transmission of the AS murmur, where it becomes higher-pitched, is common (Gallavardin effect) and may be mistaken for mitral regurgitation; the AS murmur intensifies in the beat following a premature beat and varies directly with cardiac output, whereas the MR murmur stays constant beat-to-beat (Harrison 22e p.328).","Auscultatory features pointing to severe AS include a soft or absent A2, paradoxical splitting of S2, a late-peaking systolic murmur, an apical S4, and (at normal output) a systolic thrill and a grade 4 or louder murmur (Harrison 22e p.328).","An early ejection sound (click) in children/young adults signifies a flexible, noncalcified bicuspid valve and localizes obstruction to the valvular level rather than sub- or supravalvular (Harrison 22e p.328).","Bicuspid aortic valve is one of the most common congenital anomalies and predisposes to both aortic stenosis and aortic regurgitation (Harrison 22e p.1933).","AS can precipitate myocardial ischemia and anginal chest pain even without obstructive coronary atherosclerosis, by creating extreme myocardial oxygen demand with impaired endocardial blood flow (type 2 ischemia mechanism) (Harrison 22e p.285).","Heyde's syndrome is the association of aortic stenosis with bleeding gastrointestinal vascular ectasias (acquired von Willebrand disease); such patients appear to benefit from aortic valve replacement (Harrison 22e p.361)."],"pathophysiology":"Aortic stenosis is most often caused by gradual progression of valvular calcification of either normal trileaflet or congenitally bicuspid valves, producing fixed left ventricular outflow obstruction. The pressure overload drives left ventricular hypertrophy, which manifests on the ECG as LVH that increases with severity. The combination of increased myocardial oxygen demand and impaired endocardial perfusion can cause ischemia/angina even with normal coronaries. Syncope in AS reflects a structural disorder compromising cardiac output, and inappropriate reflex vasodilation may be provoked by increased ventricular contractility against the fixed obstruction (Harrison 22e p.203-204, 285, 329, 1933).","additionalDifferentials":["Aortic sclerosis (focal leaflet thickening/calcification without obstruction; Doppler jet velocity 2.5 m/s or less) can produce a grade 2-3 midsystolic murmur acoustically identical to AS (Harrison 22e p.1908).","Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (subvalvular dynamic outflow obstruction) (Harrison 22e p.328, 1934).","Subaortic membrane (fixed subvalvular obstruction that can mimic valvular AS) (Harrison 22e p.1934).","Supravalvular aortic stenosis (exceedingly rare) (Harrison 22e p.1934).","Pulmonic stenosis (midsystolic murmur, second left interspace) (Harrison 22e p.329).","Atrial septal defect with increased pulmonic flow producing a midsystolic murmur with fixed splitting of S2 (Harrison 22e p.329).","Mitral regurgitation, given the apical Gallavardin transmission of the AS murmur (Harrison 22e p.328)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hyperdynamic flow murmurs without structural disease (fever, thyrotoxicosis, pregnancy, anemia, normal childhood/adolescence) can produce midsystolic murmurs (Harrison 22e p.1908)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography to assess valve anatomy (tricuspid/bicuspid, leaflet calcification, excursion), stenosis severity, LV size/wall thickness/function, and aortic root and proximal ascending aorta (Harrison 22e p.329, 1933).","Doppler estimation of transvalvular peak velocity and pressure gradient (Harrison 22e p.1933).","Continuity-equation estimation of aortic valve area, the most accurate echo technique, especially when gradients are underestimated by LV dysfunction (Harrison 22e p.1933-1934).","Dobutamine stress echocardiography to evaluate low-flow/low-gradient AS and determine whether the valve can open further (Harrison 22e p.1934).","Cardiac MRI for quantitative assessment of severity (Harrison 22e p.1933).","Carotid pulse examination for parvus et tardus contour (Harrison 22e p.329, 1905)."],"redFlags":["A weak and delayed carotid pulse (pulsus parvus et tardus) indicates severe AS (Harrison 22e p.329, 1905).","Soft or absent A2, paradoxical splitting of S2, late-peaking murmur, and an apical S4 indicate severe AS (Harrison 22e p.328).","A softening AS murmur in the setting of heart failure/low cardiac output is misleading and does not indicate mild disease (Harrison 22e p.328).","Syncope (including reflex/exertional) from AS reflects structural compromise of cardiac output (Harrison 22e p.203-204)."],"prognosis":"Patients with good left ventricular function can tolerate severe AS for a considerable period, so valve area or gradient alone should not dictate timing of surgery, which remains a clinical decision (Harrison 22e p.1934).","pitfalls":["Avoid aggressive preload/afterload reduction: aggressive treatment of cardiac failure with diuretics and/or vasodilators can precipitate orthostatic hypotension in patients with structural disease such as AS (Harrison 22e p.203).","Transvalvular pressure gradients underestimate severity in severe LV dysfunction; rely on valve area by continuity equation (Harrison 22e p.1933-1934).","In low-flow/low-gradient AS the valve may behave stenotic simply because of the low gradient; provocative dobutamine testing is needed to distinguish true severe stenosis (Harrison 22e p.1934).","The carotid parvus-et-tardus sign is less discriminatory in older patients with stiffened arteries (Harrison 22e p.329).","Aortic sclerosis can produce a murmur indistinguishable on auscultation from significant AS (Harrison 22e p.1908)."],"severityClassification":"Echo: peak instantaneous velocities of 3.0 m/s or higher (often >4.0 m/s) correspond to gradients of ~36 and ~64 mmHg and indicate at least moderate AS; aortic valve area <1.0 cm2 is generally severe and <0.6 cm2 critical (Harrison 22e p.1933-1934). Catheterization: a valve area <1.0 cm2 with a mean gradient >40 mmHg indicates severe AS (Harrison 22e p.1950). Aortic sclerosis (non-obstructive) is defined by a Doppler jet velocity of 2.5 m/s or less (Harrison 22e p.1908).","references":["Harrison 22e p.203","Harrison 22e p.204","Harrison 22e p.285","Harrison 22e p.286","Harrison 22e p.328","Harrison 22e p.329","Harrison 22e p.361","Harrison 22e p.1905","Harrison 22e p.1908","Harrison 22e p.1909","Harrison 22e p.1933","Harrison 22e p.1934","Harrison 22e p.1950"],"crossLinks":["heart_failure","vasovagal_syncope","acs"]},"asthma_exac":{"name":"Asthma exacerbation","system":"Pulmonary","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chapter 298 (Asthma, Israel), p.2215-2228","clinicalPearls":["An exacerbation is an episode of acute bronchospasm that may be severe enough to require emergency care or hospitalization and can be fatal (p.2216).","During an acute attack patients typically show tachypnea, tachycardia and accessory-muscle use, with wheezing and a prolonged expiratory phase (p.2222).","As airway obstruction worsens the chest may become 'silent' with loss of breath sounds, indicating a more dangerous attack rather than improvement (p.2222).","Most patients in an attack are hypocapnic from a high respiratory rate; a normal or near-normal PaCO2 in a distressed asthmatic signals impending respiratory failure (p.2227).","PEFR, FEV1 and FEV1/FVC are reduced in obstruction, but these may appear normal during an attack or after recent bronchodilator use, so a normal value does not exclude severe disease (p.2222-2223).","In urgent care, PEFR/FEV1 should be measured; patients with PEFR >60% predicted often respond to beta2-agonists alone, whereas failure to reach >60% or persistent severe tachypnea over 4-6 h should prompt hospital admission (p.2227).","Severe exacerbations requiring systemic corticosteroids may themselves accelerate long-term lung-function decline (p.2221).","More than one-third of patients carrying a physician diagnosis of asthma do not actually meet diagnostic criteria, so consider mimics when an 'asthmatic' fails to respond (p.2222).","Some patients cannot distinguish an anxiety attack from an asthma attack, which can confound assessment of an apparent exacerbation (p.2222)."],"pathophysiology":"Asthma is characterized by episodic, usually reversible airway obstruction with airway hyperresponsiveness, generally accompanied by airway inflammation (most often eosinophilic, sometimes neutrophilic, occasionally with no obvious inflammation). Exacerbations are episodes of acute bronchospasm driven by luminal narrowing from airway smooth-muscle constriction, airway edema and mucus secretion, superimposed on chronic airway inflammation. Triggers activate mast cells/basophils and inflammatory cells (releasing bronchoconstrictors and mediators) and stimulate sensory nerves acting on smooth muscle. Allergen exposure can cause an immediate (early) bronchospasm and a late response 2-24 h later with airway narrowing and inflammation. Structural airway-wall thickening (smooth-muscle hypertrophy/hyperplasia, subepithelial collagen deposition, edema, mucosal inflammation) increases the tendency to narrow disproportionately. In severe airflow obstruction there may be air trapping with dynamically elevated FRC and increased residual volume; mild hypoxemia from ventilation-perfusion mismatch (oxygen-responsive) is common (p.2216-2217, p.2221, p.2222-2223).","additionalDifferentials":["Heart failure (overlapping symptoms; can produce obstructive pulmonary function) (p.2222)","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease / asthma-COPD overlap (p.2222, p.2228)","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (p.2222)","Airway obstruction from a mass or foreign body (p.2222)","Inducible laryngeal obstruction / vocal-cord dysfunction (p.2222)","Bronchiolitis obliterans (p.2222)","Bronchiectasis (p.2222)","Tracheobronchomalacia (p.2222)","Anxiety attacks (may be indistinguishable from an asthma attack) (p.2222)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inducible laryngeal obstruction (vocal-cord dysfunction): inappropriate laryngeal narrowing causing airflow resistance; more common in women and in those with anxiety/depression; can both mimic and complicate asthma; diagnosed by laryngoscopy during symptoms (p.2222)","Heart failure / left ventricular dysfunction as a cause of dyspnea and obstructive-pattern symptoms (p.2222)","COPD (asthma-COPD overlap) (p.2222, p.2228)","Anaphylaxis as a cause of acute generalized bronchospasm (p.2222 differential; mechanism noted elsewhere in text)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR) and FEV1 to quantify obstruction and guide disposition; PEFR >60% predicted predicts response to beta2-agonists alone (p.2222-2223, p.2227)","Arterial blood gas / PaCO2 monitoring: hypocapnia is usual, while a normalizing or rising PaCO2 indicates impending respiratory failure (p.2227)","Pulse oximetry / assessment of hypoxemia (V/Q mismatch, oxygen-responsive) (p.2216-2217, p.2227)","Bronchodilator reversibility testing (>=10-12% FEV1 improvement) when not in an acute attack (p.2222-2223)","Methacholine, mannitol or exercise/cold-air challenge to demonstrate airway hyperresponsiveness when PFTs are non-confirmatory (not during an acute attack) (p.2223)","Blood eosinophil count (correlates with severity; very high levels prompt consideration of eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis) (p.2223)","Total/specific IgE, skin or RAST testing; IgE >1000 IU/mL prompts consideration of ABPA (p.2223)","Fractional exhaled nitric oxide (FeNO) as a marker of type 2 inflammation and ICS adherence (p.2223)","Chest CT in poorly responsive disease to assess bronchiectasis/structural causes; sinus imaging and serum alpha-1 antitrypsin in selected cases (p.2223)"],"redFlags":["Silent chest with loss of breath sounds (life-threatening) (p.2222)","Normal or near-normal PaCO2 in a distressed asthmatic, or a rising PaCO2 - signals impending respiratory failure and possible need for mechanical ventilation (p.2227)","Respiratory exhaustion / failure to respond to repeated beta2-agonists (p.2227)","Failure to achieve PEFR >60% predicted or persistent severe tachypnea over 4-6 h (warrants admission) (p.2227)","Need for hourly beta2-agonists for several hours despite treatment (p.2227)","Status asthmaticus: severe attack in which mechanical ventilation is difficult due to high airway resistance/pressures (p.2227)"],"prognosis":"Asthma causes significant morbidity and mortality; roughly 3000-4000 people die from asthma each year in the United States. Death can occur even in mild asthma (at lower rates than severe asthma). Mortality in patients over 65 is about five times that of younger cohorts. High-risk features for asthma death include prior ICU admission or intubation for asthma, illicit drug use, depression, a new diagnosis within the past year, two or more emergency visits in the past 6 months, severe psychosocial problems, lower socioeconomic status, and being on daily prednisone before admission. Severe exacerbations may accelerate long-term lung-function decline (p.2221, p.2227).","pitfalls":["Interpreting a 'silent chest' or quieting wheeze as improvement when it actually signals worsening, life-threatening obstruction (p.2222).","Being reassured by a normal/normalizing PaCO2 - in a distressed asthmatic this is an ominous sign of impending respiratory failure, not stability (p.2227).","Relying on PFT/PEF values that may look normal during an attack or shortly after bronchodilator use (p.2222-2223).","Overlooking mimics such as vocal-cord dysfunction, heart failure or COPD in 'difficult' or treatment-refractory presentations - over one-third of diagnosed asthmatics do not meet criteria (p.2222).","Missing precipitants such as beta-blockers (including ophthalmic drops), aspirin/NSAIDs in aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease, or ACE-inhibitor cough (p.2221).","Giving antibiotics routinely in an exacerbation - they should be used only if there are signs of infection (p.2227)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison stratifies acute deteriorations practically rather than by a fixed table. Mild-to-moderate deteriorations can initially be managed with frequent beta2-agonists; failure to control with hourly beta2-agonists for several hours warrants urgent care. In urgent/ED care, PEFR or FEV1 guides severity: PEFR >60% predicted usually responds to beta2-agonists alone, whereas failure to reach PEFR >60% or persistent severe tachypnea over 4-6 h indicates a severe attack needing admission. Life-threatening / near-fatal features include silent chest, respiratory exhaustion, and a normal or rising PaCO2 signaling impending respiratory failure; status asthmaticus denotes a severe refractory attack with high airway resistance making ventilation difficult (p.2227).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2215","Harrison 22e p.2216","Harrison 22e p.2217","Harrison 22e p.2221","Harrison 22e p.2222","Harrison 22e p.2223","Harrison 22e p.2227"],"crossLinks":["COPD_EXACERBATION","anaphylaxis","pe"]},"atrial_fib":{"name":"Atrial fibrillation / arrhythmia","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"1985, 1989-1997","clinicalPearls":["AF is the most common sustained arrhythmia; prevalence rises steeply with age (~20% of those over 80), and the lifetime risk for a 40-year-old man is roughly one in four (p.1990).","On the ECG, AF shows absence of organized atrial activity (no P waves) with an irregularly irregular ventricular response; the chaotic atrial activity is often best seen in lead V1 (p.1990, 1991).","The conducted ventricular rate is variable and usually rapid (commonly 110-160/min), but may exceed 200/min, or be inappropriately slow when there is high vagal tone or AV nodal conduction disease (p.1990).","Many patients are unaware of the irregular heartbeat, so AF is frequently asymptomatic; absence of AF on intermittent monitoring does not establish low risk (p.1990, 1993).","AF is categorized by temporal pattern: paroxysmal (self-terminating, usually within 7 days), persistent (continuous >7 days but <1 year), and long-standing persistent (>1 year); these patterns correlate with progressive left atrial enlargement and scar burden (p.1990, 1991).","Stroke and bleeding risks are estimated as concepts using the CHA2DS2-VASc score (stroke risk: congestive heart failure, hypertension, age, diabetes, prior stroke/TIA, vascular disease, sex) and HAS-BLED score (bleeding risk), respectively (p.1993).","AF burden does not predict stroke risk; the approach to paroxysmal AF is the same as for persistent AF, and even apparently infrequent episodes carry risk because asymptomatic episodes are common (p.1993).","Subclinical AF detectable only on long-term continuous monitoring is associated with roughly 2.5-fold increased stroke risk (p.1993).","AF detected after cryptogenic stroke using insertable cardiac monitors in up to ~8.9% of patients within 6 months, supporting prolonged rhythm monitoring in this setting (p.1990)."],"pathophysiology":"AF is characterized by disorganized, rapid, irregular atrial electrical activation with loss of organized atrial mechanical contraction; the AV node filters these impulses to produce an irregular ventricular rate. The pathophysiology is multifactorial and incompletely understood, with a proposed 'final common pathway' in which risk factors (age, cardiovascular disease, hypertension, diabetes, obesity, alcohol, sleep-disordered breathing) drive electrophysiologic changes in atrial tissue. Altered membrane channel regulation produces abnormal excitability; pulmonary vein musculature (and other foci such as the posterior left atrial wall, superior vena cava, coronary sinus, vein of Marshall) shows enhanced automaticity generating ectopic beats that initiate AF. Progressive cellular and tissue remodeling shortens atrial refractory periods and creates functional reentry, with eventual fibrosis and left atrial enlargement, explaining the typical progression from paroxysmal to persistent to long-standing persistent disease. Loss of atrial systole reduces cardiac output, and variable ventricular filling produces variable stroke volume (p.1990-1992).","additionalDifferentials":["Multifocal atrial tachycardia (>=3 distinct P-wave morphologies with clear isoelectric intervals and a slower atrial rate than AF; typically in chronic pulmonary disease and acute illness) (p.1989).","Other supraventricular tachycardias including atrial flutter, focal atrial tachycardia, AV nodal reentry, and AV reciprocating tachycardia, distinguished by 12-lead ECG appearance and response to AV nodal blockade (p.1976-1977).","Preexcited (accessory-pathway) AF, presenting as an irregular wide-complex tachycardia with bizarre, beat-to-beat-variable QRS morphology (p.1985-1986)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transesophageal echocardiography to exclude left atrial appendage thrombus before cardioversion when AF duration is >48 h or unknown (p.1992).","Cardiac CT angiography with delayed-acquisition imaging as an alternative to TEE for left atrial appendage thrombus detection, with high sensitivity and specificity (p.1992).","Ambulatory ECG monitoring, implantable loop recorders, or wearable/home ECG devices to capture intermittent or subclinical episodes (p.1991, 1993)."],"redFlags":["Acute AF with significant haemodynamic compromise, pulmonary oedema, or evidence of coronary ischaemia indicates need for emergent cardioversion (p.1992).","Preexcited AF over an accessory pathway with very rapid repetitive conduction (minimum R-R <250 ms occurs in ~25% of preexciting pathways) is potentially life-threatening, predisposing to ventricular fibrillation and sudden death (p.1985-1986).","Syncope or presyncope from bradycardia/pauses, including offset pauses on spontaneous conversion to sinus rhythm, suggesting coexisting sinus node dysfunction (tachy-brady syndrome) (p.1992).","Sustained uncontrolled rapid ventricular rates risk precipitating heart failure and tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy (p.1990, 1992)."],"prognosis":"AF is not benign: it carries a 1.5- to 1.9-fold increased mortality after adjusting for underlying cardiac disease, and causes approximately 25% of all strokes. It increases the risk of dementia, MRI-detected silent embolic infarcts, congestive heart failure, and cardiomyopathy. Tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy is generally reversible once ventricular rate is controlled. AF tends to be progressive, with no definitive durable cure, and patients maintaining sinus rhythm generally have better survival than those who remain in AF (p.1990, 1992, 1994).","pitfalls":["Relying on AF burden or symptom frequency to gauge stroke risk is mistaken; anticoagulation decisions should rest on the risk-factor profile, not how often AF is documented (p.1993).","A normal intermittent monitor does not exclude clinically important AF, since asymptomatic episodes are common (p.1993).","Cardioverting AF of >48 h (or unknown) duration without anticoagulation or thrombus exclusion risks thromboembolism, which can occur even days later because of post-conversion atrial stunning (delayed recovery of mechanical function) (p.1992).","Nonvalvular AF (AF without mitral stenosis) differs in management from valvular AF; rheumatic mitral stenosis and mechanical valves carry especially high thromboembolic risk (p.1993)."],"severityClassification":"By temporal pattern: paroxysmal AF (self-terminates or is cardioverted within 7 days; normal-to-mildly enlarged left atrium, low scar burden), persistent AF (continuous >7 days but <1 year; mild-to-severe LA enlargement, moderate scar), and long-standing persistent AF (>1 year; severely enlarged LA, high scar burden). A newer staged framework (at risk of AF, pre-AF, AF, permanent AF) reframes AF as a disease continuum. Mitral stenosis defines valvular versus nonvalvular AF for risk purposes (p.1991, 1993, 1996).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1985","Harrison 22e p.1989","Harrison 22e p.1990","Harrison 22e p.1991","Harrison 22e p.1992","Harrison 22e p.1993","Harrison 22e p.1994","Harrison 22e p.1996"],"crossLinks":["ischemic_stroke","heart_failure","hyperthyroidism","pe"]},"biliary_colic":{"name":"Biliary colic / cholelithiasis","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.357 (Diseases of the Gallbladder and Bile Ducts) p.2728-2735; Ch.332 (Approach to GI Disease) p.2460-2461; Ch.52 (Jaundice) p.325","clinicalPearls":["Biliary colic is the most specific symptom of gallstone disease, yet 'colic' is a misnomer: the pain is steady rather than waxing-and-waning, reflecting non-relievable distention of the gallbladder/duct against a stone obstructing the cystic duct or CBD (p.2731).","A typical episode begins abruptly and lasts roughly 30 minutes to 5 hours before easing; pain that persists beyond ~5 hours should prompt suspicion of acute cholecystitis rather than uncomplicated colic (p.2731).","Pain is characteristically a severe steady ache or fullness in the epigastrium or right upper quadrant, often radiating to the interscapular region, right scapula, or shoulder (p.2731).","Attacks may be triggered by a fatty meal, a large meal after prolonged fasting, or even a normal meal, and are frequently nocturnal, occurring within a few hours of going to bed (p.2731).","Nausea and vomiting commonly accompany the pain, but fever/chills are NOT features of uncomplicated colic and signal a complication (p.2731).","Vague short-lived epigastric fullness, dyspepsia, belching, or flatulence after fatty food are nonspecific and occur with or without gallstones; they should not be equated with true biliary pain (p.2731).","Most asymptomatic gallstones stay silent: 60-80% remain symptom-free over follow-up up to 25 years, so incidentally found stones without referable symptoms generally do not warrant intervention (p.2731).","Once colic occurs, the disease has 'declared' itself; complications requiring surgery are far more common in patients who have developed biliary pain than in the asymptomatic group, and most patients who develop complications had prior warning symptoms (p.2731).","Sudden awakening from sleep by pain favors an organic cause such as biliary colic over a functional gut-brain disorder (p.2461)."],"pathophysiology":"Biliary colic results from transient obstruction of the cystic duct (or common bile duct) by a gallstone migrating out of the gallbladder. The obstruction raises intraluminal pressure and distends the viscus; because repetitive biliary contractions cannot overcome or relieve the blockage, a steady visceral pain is generated. When the stone disimpacts or falls back, pressure normalizes and the pain resolves spontaneously, so an uncomplicated episode is self-limited and is NOT accompanied by inflammation, fever, or sustained cholestasis. Underlying stones are usually cholesterol stones (>90% in Western populations), which form when bile becomes supersaturated with cholesterol, cholesterol monohydrate crystals nucleate, and gallbladder hypomotility allows stasis and stone growth; gallbladder emptying is frequently impaired in stone formers. Distinct mechanisms (mechanical, chemical from lysolecithin, and bacterial) convert simple obstruction into acute cholecystitis when the cystic duct stays blocked (p.2727-2732).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute cholecystitis (pain >5 h, fever, Murphy's sign) — represents biliary pain that fails to resolve as the cystic duct stays obstructed (p.2731-2732)","Choledocholithiasis with cholangitis — CBD stone causing biliary pain plus jaundice and spiking fevers/chills (Charcot's triad) (p.2735)","Gallstone pancreatitis — stone-related obstruction at the ampulla; pain is severe and persists for days, not hours (p.2461, p.2735)","Peptic ulcer disease / functional dyspepsia — ulcer pain is intermittent over weeks-months and may be relieved by eating, unlike the sudden hours-long onset of colic (p.2461)","Irritable bowel syndrome and other disorders of gut-brain interaction — common causes of abdominal pain, exacerbated by stress rather than fatty meals (p.2460-2461)","Sphincter of Oddi dysfunction / biliary dyskinesia — biliary-type pain without stones, may follow cholecystectomy (p.2734)","Acalculous cholecystopathy — disordered gallbladder motility producing recurrent biliary pain in the absence of stones (p.2732)","Right-lower-lobe pneumonia, mesenteric ischemia, or right renal colic as non-biliary causes of RUQ/flank pain (p.2460)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Functional dyspepsia / non-ulcer dyspepsia presenting with post-prandial epigastric discomfort (p.2461)","Gastroesophageal reflux / pill esophagitis (biliary colic itself can be mistaken for unexplained upper-GI symptoms) (p.2728)","Irritable bowel syndrome (most common cause of abdominal pain alongside functional dyspepsia) (p.2461)","Sphincter of Oddi dysfunction and acalculous cholecystopathy (biliary-type pain without calculi) (p.2732, p.2734)","Cardiac/precordial referral — gallbladder pain may occasionally be referred to the precordium (p.2728)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Right-upper-quadrant ultrasound is the procedure of choice: rapid, not limited by jaundice or pregnancy, and >95% accurate for gallstones, identifying calculi as small as ~1.5 mm by their acoustic shadowing and gravity-dependent movement (p.2729-2730)","Ultrasound also distinguishes biliary sludge (low-echogenicity layer that shifts with position but casts NO acoustic shadow) from true stones, and can assess gallbladder emptying (p.2728-2730)","Serum bilirubin and/or alkaline phosphatase elevation during a biliary attack suggests a common-duct stone rather than simple colic (p.2731)","Cholescintigraphy (HIDA/DISIDA scan): non-visualization of the gallbladder indicates cystic-duct obstruction and supports acute cholecystitis (p.2730)","MRCP, EUS, or ERCP for suspected choledocholithiasis; ERCP is the gold standard and also therapeutic, MRCP often the initial test (p.325)","Plain abdominal film detects only the radiopaque minority of stones (~10-15% of cholesterol, ~50% of pigment) and has low yield (p.2730)"],"redFlags":["Pain persisting beyond ~5 hours — suspect acute cholecystitis (p.2731)","Fever, chills, or rigors with biliary pain — implies a complication (cholecystitis, cholangitis, or pancreatitis), not uncomplicated colic (p.2731)","Jaundice and/or rising bilirubin or alkaline phosphatase — suggests common-bile-duct stone or obstruction (p.2731)","Charcot's triad (biliary pain + jaundice + spiking fever/chills) — acute cholangitis (p.2735)","Murphy's sign (inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation) with RUQ tenderness, fever, and leukocytosis — acute cholecystitis (p.323, p.2732)","Severe pain persisting for days, with confusion/sepsis features — suggests pancreatitis or suppurative cholangitis (p.2461, p.2735)"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated biliary colic is self-limited; the individual attack resolves once the transient obstruction clears. The natural history of the underlying gallstones is generally favorable: 60-80% of asymptomatic stone carriers stay symptom-free for up to 25 years, the chance of developing symptoms is only ~2-4% per year in the first 5 years after diagnosis (falling to 1-2% thereafter), and the yearly incidence of complications is only ~0.1-0.3%. The cumulative risk of death from gallstone disease under expectant management is small. However, once biliary pain has occurred, the likelihood of recurrent attacks and of complications requiring intervention rises substantially; younger patients at diagnosis are more likely to become symptomatic than those over 60 (p.2731).","pitfalls":["Treating the term 'colic' literally and expecting intermittent crampy pain — biliary colic is steady, and waiting for it to fluctuate can delay recognition (p.2731).","Attributing nonspecific fatty-food dyspepsia, belching, or bloating to gallstones; these symptoms are equally common in people without stones and do not establish a biliary cause (p.2731).","Assuming an incidental gallstone explains a patient's symptoms — most stones are silent, and a coexisting non-biliary disorder (e.g., reflux, peptic ulcer, IBS) may be the true cause (p.2731, p.2735).","Missing that fever or jaundice has converted 'colic' into a complication requiring different (often urgent) management (p.2731).","Relying on a plain abdominal film, which misses most cholesterol stones, instead of ultrasound (p.2730).","Overlooking acalculous causes of biliary-type pain (sphincter of Oddi dysfunction, acalculous cholecystopathy) when ultrasound shows no stones (p.2732, p.2734)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames the spectrum by clinical course rather than a formal grading scale: uncomplicated biliary colic = transient cystic-duct obstruction giving self-limited pain (30 min-5 h) WITHOUT fever, inflammation, or jaundice; progression/complication is signalled by pain >5 h, fever/chills, or jaundice, and corresponds to acute cholecystitis (sustained cystic-duct obstruction with inflammation), choledocholithiasis with cholangitis (CBD obstruction + infection, Charcot's triad), or gallstone pancreatitis. Acute cholecystitis is itself triaged by complications (e.g., empyema, gangrene, perforation, Mirizzi's syndrome) (p.2731-2735).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2727","Harrison 22e p.2728","Harrison 22e p.2729","Harrison 22e p.2730","Harrison 22e p.2731","Harrison 22e p.2732","Harrison 22e p.2735","Harrison 22e p.2460","Harrison 22e p.2461","Harrison 22e p.323","Harrison 22e p.325"],"crossLinks":["CHOLECYSTITIS","CHOLANGITIS","pancreatitis"]},"bowel_obstruction":{"name":"Bowel obstruction","system":"Surgical / GI","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Chap. 16 Abdominal Pain pp.110-113; Chap. 53 Abdominal Swelling and Ascites p.327; Chap. 333 Gastrointestinal Endoscopy p.2485; GI symptom evaluation chapter (loss of bowel sounds vs hyperactive sounds)","clinicalPearls":["Small-bowel obstruction characteristically produces poorly localized, intermittent peri-/supra-umbilical colicky pain; as the bowel progressively dilates and loses muscular tone the colicky quality of the pain may actually diminish, which can be falsely reassuring (p.110).","Colonic obstruction tends to cause lower-intensity colicky pain located in the infraumbilical area, often radiating to the lumbar region, in contrast to the more periumbilical pain of small-bowel obstruction (p.110).","High-pitched, hyperactive bowel sounds are characteristic of mechanical obstruction, whereas loss of bowel sounds points toward ileus; however, once proximal bowel becomes markedly distended and edematous, peristaltic sounds may become weak or absent even without peritonitis.","Auscultation is one of the least revealing parts of the abdominal exam: a strangulating small-intestinal obstruction can occur with normal-sounding peristalsis, so normal bowel sounds do not exclude a catastrophe (p.113).","Patients with obstructive colic tend to be restless and may thrash about, in contrast to the peritonitic patient who lies still to avoid movement — a useful bedside discriminator when ischaemia/perforation supervenes (p.110).","Inability to pass stool or flatus combined with nausea/vomiting and increasing abdominal girth suggests bowel obstruction (or severe constipation/ileus); retained feces in the colon itself adds to the distension (p.327)."],"pathophysiology":"Mechanical obstruction of a hollow viscus classically elicits intermittent (colicky) pain that is less well localized than the parietal pain of peritoneal irritation; distension of the viscus may also produce steady pain with only occasional paroxysms. With superimposed strangulating obstruction, traction on the root of the mesentery can refer pain to the lower lumbar region. As the obstructed proximal bowel progressively dilates it loses muscular tone, so colicky pain may wane and peristaltic sounds may weaken. Obstruction must be distinguished from adynamic ileus (loss of peristalsis) and from colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie's syndrome), in which the bowel is dilated without a mechanical lesion (Harrison 22e pp.110-113, p.327).","additionalDifferentials":["Adynamic / paralytic ileus (loss of peristalsis with absent bowel sounds) rather than mechanical obstruction","Colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie's syndrome) — colonic dilatation without a mechanical lesion","Severe constipation / fecal impaction causing abdominal distension and discomfort","Sigmoid or cecal volvulus as a cause of colonic obstruction","Gastric outlet obstruction (malignancy or chronic peptic stenosis of the pylorus) presenting with vomiting of partially digested food hours after eating","Esophageal obstruction by impacted food bolus / foreign body (proximal obstruction mimic)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute mesenteric arterial occlusion — early diffuse cramping pain from hyperperistalsis with 'pain out of proportion to physical findings' can mimic early obstruction; tenderness and rigidity may be absent until vascular collapse","Porphyria and lead colic — severe hyperperistalsis makes the pain difficult to distinguish from intestinal obstruction","Diabetic ketoacidosis — can both precipitate and mimic an acute abdomen; if pain does not resolve with metabolic correction, suspect an organic cause such as obstruction","Irritable bowel syndrome — functional pain with altered bowel habit, but nausea/vomiting are rare and localized tenderness/spasm inconsistent (a non-obstructive mimic of altered bowel habit)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Low-dose CT is preferred over plain abdominal radiography when the indication for surgical/medical intervention is unclear in nontraumatic acute abdominal pain","Plain upright/lateral-decubitus abdominal radiographs can show dilated bowel loops suggesting obstruction or ileus, but have limited utility and may be unnecessary when the cause (e.g., strangulated external hernia) is already evident","Water-soluble contrast enema can be diagnostic in suspected colonic obstruction without perforation; oral barium sulfate should be avoided if colonic obstruction is suspected","Serum amylase may be raised by strangulating intestinal obstruction (also by perforated ulcer, cholecystitis), so an elevation neither confirms nor excludes the need for operation","Leukocytosis is non-specific (a WBC >20,000/uL may follow perforation but also pancreatitis, cholecystitis, infarction) and should never be the sole determinant of operative need; a normal count does not exclude perforation","Endoscopy can diagnose and often manage gastroduodenal and colonic obstruction/pseudo-obstruction, but small-bowel obstruction from surgical adhesions is generally neither diagnosed nor treated endoscopically"],"redFlags":["Continuous diffuse pain with absent tenderness/rigidity ('pain out of proportion to findings') in a patient at vascular risk — suspect superior mesenteric artery occlusion / intestinal ischaemia","Spread of pain to the lower lumbar region — may indicate strangulating obstruction with traction on the mesenteric root","Truly silent abdomen with sudden severe pain — suggests chemical peritonitis from a perforated viscus","Catastrophic intra-abdominal events may present with minimal or no pain/muscle spasm in obtunded, debilitated, immunosuppressed, or elderly patients — a slowly developing process also attenuates guarding"],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Relying on auscultation or bowel-sound quality: strangulating obstruction can occur with normal peristaltic sounds, and distended/edematous proximal bowel may be quiet even without peritonitis (p.113).","Interpreting waning colicky pain as improvement — pain may diminish simply because the dilated bowel has lost muscular tone (p.110).","Using leukocytosis or serum amylase as a deciding factor for surgery — both are non-specific in obstruction and infarction (p.113).","Giving oral barium when colonic obstruction is possible (risk of inspissation above the obstruction); use water-soluble contrast instead.","Missing intestinal ischaemia, which classically elicits severe pain but little tenderness (GI evaluation chapter)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.110","Harrison 22e p.111","Harrison 22e p.113","Harrison 22e p.327","Harrison 22e p.2485"],"crossLinks":["mesenteric_ischemia","peptic_ulcer","ibd_flare"]},"brain_tumour":{"name":"Brain tumour / mass lesion","system":"Neuro-oncology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"716-724, 157","clinicalPearls":["Presenting features split into general (raised-ICP) symptoms - headache +/- nausea/vomiting, cognitive change, personality change, gait disorder - and focal/lateralizing signs such as hemiparesis, aphasia or visual field loss; many patients have both (p.716).","The classic morning-predominant headache that eases through the day occurs in only a minority of patients; brain-tumour headache is more often a nondescript dull ache, and migraine produces this pattern far more often than tumour (p.716, p.157).","Vomiting preceding headache by weeks is highly characteristic of posterior fossa tumours, and headache disturbs sleep in about 10% (p.157).","Seizures from a brain tumour always have a focal onset whether or not this is clinically apparent (p.716).","New de novo headache in a patient with known malignancy should raise suspicion of cerebral metastases or carcinomatous (leptomeningeal) meningitis (p.157).","On MRI, malignant tumours (primary or metastatic) typically enhance with gadolinium, have central necrosis and surrounding white-matter edema, whereas low-grade gliomas usually do NOT enhance and are best seen as increased signal on FLAIR (p.716).","Meningiomas and vestibular schwannomas are extra-axial, densely enhancing lesions; a meningioma characteristically shows a dural tail and compresses but does not invade brain (p.716, p.721).","Leptomeningeal disease produces multilevel neuraxis signs (radiculopathies, cranial neuropathies, seizures, confusion, raised-ICP encephalopathy); isolated focal deficits like hemiparesis are rarely from it unless brain is directly infiltrated (p.724).","Parinaud's syndrome (impaired upgaze and accommodation) points to a pineal-region mass (p.720)."],"pathophysiology":"General/raised-ICP symptoms arise as the enlarging tumour plus surrounding vasogenic edema raises intracranial pressure or obstructs CSF circulation, producing hydrocephalus; focal deficits reflect local invasion or compression (p.716). Primary tumours develop through stepwise acquisition of genetic changes - loss of tumour-suppressor genes (p53, CDKN2A/B, PTEN) and amplification/overexpression of oncogenes such as EGFR and PDGFR - and glioblastomas in particular show marked molecular heterogeneity (p.717). The 2021 WHO CNS classification integrates molecular markers (e.g. IDH mutation, 1p/19q co-deletion) with histology into the diagnosis (p.717-718). Brain metastases spread hematogenously, lodging in terminal arterioles at the grey-white junction in watershed territory, so distribution follows blood flow (~85% supratentorial, ~15% posterior fossa) (p.723).","additionalDifferentials":["Metastatic brain tumour - three times more common than all primary brain tumours combined in adults; commonest sources lung and breast, with melanoma having the greatest propensity to metastasize to brain (p.716, p.723)","Diffuse glioma - the most common malignant PRIMARY brain tumour; subdivided into IDH-mutant astrocytoma, IDH-mutant 1p/19q-codeleted oligodendroglioma, and IDH wild-type glioblastoma (p.717-718)","Meningioma - now the most common primary brain tumour overall (~35%); extra-axial dural-based lesion, WHO grades I-III (p.720-721)","Vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma) - ~9% of primary brain tumours, presents with progressive unilateral hearing loss/tinnitus/dizziness (p.721)","Primary CNS lymphoma - densely enhancing, often periventricular/basal ganglia/corpus callosum; associated with immunosuppression and EBV in HIV (p.720)","Pituitary adenoma - amenorrhoea/galactorrhoea with headache suggests a prolactinoma (p.157, p.721)","Pineal-region tumour presenting with Parinaud's syndrome and hydrocephalus (p.720)","Colloid cyst of the third ventricle - intermittent hydrocephalus, rarely sudden death (p.722)"],"infectionMimics":["Brain abscess - shares a ring-enhancing radiographic appearance with metastasis and high-grade glioma (p.723)","Cerebral toxoplasmosis - in immunocompromised patients, multiple ring-enhancing lesions can be hard to distinguish from PCNSL or metastases (p.721)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Demyelinating lesion - can mimic the nonspecific appearance of a brain metastasis (p.723)","Neurosarcoidosis - listed among lesions resembling metastatic disease on imaging (p.723)","Radiation necrosis in a previously irradiated patient - mimics tumour and may need PET/MR perfusion to distinguish recurrence from treatment effect (p.716, p.723)"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI with gadolinium is the preferred test; CT only if MRI cannot be done (p.716)","Neuroimaging is often the only test needed to diagnose a brain tumour; laboratory tests are rarely useful (p.716)","FLAIR sequences to detect non-enhancing low-grade gliomas (p.716, p.718)","Functional MRI for presurgical mapping of eloquent cortex (p.716)","PET, MR perfusion and MR spectroscopy to assess metabolic activity and help separate tumour progression from radiation/treatment necrosis (p.716)","CSF cytology / flow cytometry when leptomeningeal involvement is suspected (gold standard but positive in only ~50% on first LP, still misses ~10% after three samples) (p.720, p.724)","Stereotactic biopsy for histologic diagnosis when imaging is insufficient; in suspected PCNSL withhold glucocorticoids until biopsy because they are cytolytic and can render tissue non-diagnostic (p.720)","HIV testing and body PET/CT, spine MRI, CSF and slit-lamp eye exam to stage suspected PCNSL (p.720-721)","Serum tumour markers (e.g. beta-hCG) occasionally elevated in metastatic disease (p.716)"],"redFlags":["Progressive, subacute focal/lateralizing deficit (hemiparesis, aphasia, visual field loss) (p.716)","New-onset focal-onset seizures (p.716)","Morning-predominant headache or headache with raised-ICP features (nausea/vomiting, worse with exertion or position change) (p.716, p.157)","Vomiting preceding headache by weeks - suggests a posterior fossa mass (p.157)","New de novo headache in a patient with known systemic cancer (p.157)","Personality change/apathy that may be mistaken for depression (p.716)"],"prognosis":"Outcome is tumour-specific. Low-grade (grade 2) IDH-mutant astrocytoma usually transforms to higher grade with median survival around 10 years; high-grade IDH-mutant astrocytoma roughly 3-9 years (p.718). Oligodendrogliomas (IDH-mutant, 1p/19q co-deleted) are more therapy-responsive with median survival in excess of 10 years (p.718). Glioblastoma (IDH wild-type) is the most common malignant primary tumour and invariably recurs; with standard combined therapy median survival is ~15-18 months and 5-year survival ~10%; adverse factors include older age, unmethylated MGMT promoter, poor performance status and unresectable tumour (p.718-719). Diffuse midline glioma H3K27-altered carries a poor prognosis (~1 year) regardless of grade (p.719). Pilocytic astrocytoma (grade 1) and completely resected meningiomas/ependymomas can be cured (p.719-721). Brain metastases treated with WBRT have median survival of only ~4-6 months, though SRS achieves local control in 80-90% and occasional cure (p.724).","pitfalls":["A ring-enhancing lesion is non-specific - the differential includes brain abscess, toxoplasmosis, PCNSL, demyelination, sarcoidosis, radiation necrosis and primary brain tumour, so do not assume tumour without context (p.721, p.723).","Withholding the diagnosis of glioma can be hard for diffusely infiltrative non-enhancing tumours (formerly gliomatosis cerebri), which often present only with cognitive decline and confluent FLAIR signal without mass effect (p.718).","Do not give glucocorticoids before biopsy in suspected PCNSL - their cytolytic effect can yield non-diagnostic tissue (p.720).","Prophylactic antiepileptic drugs have no role in tumour patients who have not had a seizure (only perioperative use is sometimes appropriate) (p.716).","A normal or migrainous headache pattern is far more often migraine than tumour - tumour headache is frequently nondescript (p.157).","Imaging-negative leptomeningeal disease is common; a single negative CSF cytology does not exclude it (positive in only ~50% on first LP) (p.724)."],"severityClassification":"WHO CNS grading (2021 integrated histologic + molecular classification): diffuse gliomas graded 2-4 by histologic malignancy and molecular markers, with CDKN2A/B homozygous deletion conferring grade 4 to otherwise lower-grade IDH-mutant astrocytomas; pilocytic astrocytoma is grade 1. Meningiomas are graded I (benign), II (atypical) and III (malignant) by increasing aggressiveness (p.717-718, p.720-721).","references":["Harrison 22e p.716","Harrison 22e p.717","Harrison 22e p.718","Harrison 22e p.719","Harrison 22e p.720","Harrison 22e p.721","Harrison 22e p.723","Harrison 22e p.724","Harrison 22e p.157"],"crossLinks":["BRAIN_ABSCESS","seizure_epilepsy","iih","malignancy_b"]},"cardiogenic_shock":{"name":"Cardiogenic shock","system":"Cardiology / Critical care","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.316 (Cardiogenic Shock and Pulmonary Edema, p.2324-2327); Ch.314 (Approach to Shock, p.2311); Ch.286 (STEMI complications, p.2123); valvular MR p.334","clinicalPearls":["Defined haemodynamically as a low cardiac-output state producing life-threatening end-organ hypoperfusion: persistent SBP <90 mmHg (or MAP <60-65 mmHg) unresponsive to volume and/or requiring vasopressors, plus hypoperfusion features such as lactate >2 mmol/L (p.2324).","Objective parameters (cardiac index, pulmonary capillary wedge pressure) help confirm a cardiac cause but are not mandatory for the diagnosis (p.2324).","Compensatory rise in systemic vascular resistance is typical; left-ventricular processes elevate PCWP whereas right-ventricular processes elevate CVP (p.2311).","Occasionally BP is maintained by very high SVR despite established shock, so normotension does not exclude cardiogenic shock (p.2326).","Only about 10% of patients present with shock on admission; roughly 90% develop it during hospitalisation, so serial reassessment is essential (p.2123, p.2325).","Shock complicating a first inferior MI should prompt a search for a mechanical complication or right-ventricular involvement (p.2325).","A new systolic murmur after MI should trigger urgent echocardiography to look for VSR, papillary muscle rupture or free-wall rupture; a murmur may be absent in up to 50% of acute MR cases (p.334, p.1894).","Pericardial thrombus on imaging after MI strongly suggests myocardial rupture and is a surgical emergency (p.1894).","Patients who develop shock typically have severe multivessel disease with 'piecemeal' necrosis extending beyond the original infarct zone (p.2123)."],"pathophysiology":"A profound fall in myocardial contractility initiates a self-perpetuating spiral: reduced cardiac output and stroke volume lower blood pressure and coronary perfusion, worsening ischaemia and further depressing contractility, which left untreated leads to death. Ineffective stroke volume is the inciting event, but inadequate circulatory compensation also contributes. Early peripheral vasoconstriction may transiently improve coronary and peripheral perfusion but raises afterload and can worsen ischaemia. Over time, systemic inflammation from acute cardiac injury induces pathologic vasodilatation; inflammatory cytokines and inducible/endothelial nitric oxide synthase increase NO and its by-product peroxynitrite, which are negatively inotropic and cardiotoxic. Lactic acidosis and hypoxaemia compound the cycle because severe acidosis blunts the efficacy of endogenous and exogenous catecholamines. Bleeding and transfusion during ICU care can further trigger inflammation. Left-ventricular failure accounts for roughly 80% of cardiogenic shock complicating acute MI, with acute severe mitral regurgitation, ventricular septal rupture, predominant RV failure, and free-wall rupture/tamponade making up the remainder (p.2325).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute fulminant myocarditis","End-stage / decompensated cardiomyopathy","Takotsubo (apical ballooning) syndrome as a transient cause","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with severe LV outflow obstruction","Critical valvular disease (critical aortic/mitral stenosis; acute severe aortic or mitral regurgitation)","Aortic dissection with aortic insufficiency or tamponade","Refractory sustained tachyarrhythmia or bradyarrhythmia","Right-ventricular failure from acute MI or acute/decompensated cor pulmonale","Post-cardiac-arrest myocardial stunning; postcardiotomy shock"],"infectionMimics":["Septic shock with myocardial depression (sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy), which can also coexist as a mixed shock state (p.2311, p.2326)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hypovolaemic shock (low CVP and PCWP, elevated SVR) — distinguished by low filling pressures (p.2311)","Obstructive shock from cardiac tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive pulmonary embolism or aortic dissection (p.2311)","Distributive/anaphylactic or neurogenic shock with low SVR (p.2311)","Negative-inotrope or vasodilator drug overdose (beta-blocker, calcium-channel antagonist) and toxic/metabolic causes (pheochromocytoma, scorpion venom) (p.2327)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas (typically hypoxaemia with anion-gap metabolic acidosis) and serial arterial lactate (p.2326)","Chest radiograph (pulmonary vascular congestion/oedema; normal in up to a third; enlarged heart suggests prior MI) (p.2326)","ECG looking for multilead Q waves/ST elevation, LBBB; ST elevation in aVR with diffuse ST depression suggests left main / global ischaemia (p.2326)","Echocardiography to define mechanism (LV/RV/biventricular, VSR, acute MR, tamponade, LVOT obstruction, valve lesions, aortic dissection) (p.2326)","Right-sided ECG leads (e.g. V4R) for suspected RV infarction (p.2123)","Pulmonary artery catheterisation in diagnostic/management uncertainty or severe unresponsive shock; equalised diastolic pressures suggest tamponade (p.2326)","Coronary angiography immediately in all cardiogenic shock complicating MI to guide reperfusion (p.2327)","Renal function (creatinine), hepatic transaminases (elevated in ~20%, marker of high mortality), admission glucose, WBC and CRP (p.2326)"],"redFlags":["Lactate >8 mmol/L (places patient in SCAI stage E) (p.2326)","Cardiac arrest with ongoing CPR and/or ECMO (extremis stage) (p.2326)","Rising hepatic transaminases from liver hypoperfusion (~20%; high-mortality marker) (p.2326)","New systolic murmur, thrill or sudden deterioration suggesting mechanical complication (VSR, papillary rupture, free-wall rupture) (p.334, p.1894)","Progressive oliguria/anuria and altered mental status indicating worsening hypoperfusion (p.2326)","Failure to respond to initial interventions (SCAI stage D, 'deteriorating') (p.2326)"],"prognosis":"In-hospital mortality ranges from roughly 40-60% depending on shock severity and underlying cause; cardiogenic and septic shock carry the highest mortality among shock types (90-day mortality around 52% for cardiogenic shock in one ED cohort). Higher lactate, elevated admission glucose, hepatic hypoperfusion and progressive renal impairment are adverse markers. Untreated shock progresses through compensated (preshock), decompensated, and finally irreversible stages with permanent organ dysfunction and multi-organ failure. Reperfusion and modern care have reduced the incidence of shock complicating acute MI to about 5-10% (p.2311, p.2325).","pitfalls":["Assuming normotension excludes shock — BP may be preserved by markedly elevated SVR (p.2326).","Missing a mechanical complication: a murmur is absent in up to 50% of acute papillary-muscle-rupture MR (p.334).","Overlooking RV infarction in inferior MI, where fluid management differs (volume to maintain RV preload) (p.2123).","Treating presumed cardiogenic shock as hypovolaemia — filling pressures (PCWP/CVP) are elevated, not low (p.2311).","Failing to recognise mixed shock (e.g. sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy superimposed on distributive shock) (p.2311)."],"severityClassification":"SCAI shock classification (2019, updated): Stage A 'at risk' — no current signs/symptoms; Stage B 'beginning'/preshock — relative hypotension or tachycardia without hypoperfusion; Stage C 'classic' — manifest hypoperfusion requiring intervention (inotropes, vasopressors or MCS, excluding ECMO) beyond volume; Stage D 'deteriorating' — like C but worsening and failing initial therapy; Stage E 'extremis' — cardiac arrest with ongoing CPR and/or ECMO. The updated definition adds a three-axis model: (1) shock severity, (2) phenotype/aetiology, (3) risk modifiers such as cardiac arrest. Lactate >8 mmol/L marks stage E (p.2325-2326).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2324","Harrison 22e p.2325","Harrison 22e p.2326","Harrison 22e p.2327","Harrison 22e p.2311","Harrison 22e p.2123","Harrison 22e p.334","Harrison 22e p.1894"],"crossLinks":["acs","heart_failure","SEPTIC_SHOCK","hypovolemic_shock","tamponade","aortic_dissection"]},"ckd":{"name":"Chronic kidney disease","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.322 (Chronic Kidney Disease), pp.2385-2396; Ch.319 staging/clinical, pp.2361-2362 and Table 319-3 p.2362","clinicalPearls":["Progression risk in CKD tracks three things together: the current GFR, the trajectory of GFR over time, and the degree of albuminuria - not GFR alone (p.2385).","Once nephron mass is reduced, surviving nephrons undergo compensatory hyperfiltration and hypertrophy; these short-term adaptations become maladaptive, driving glomerulosclerosis and ongoing GFR loss even after the original insult has resolved (p.2385-2386, 2396).","A normal serum creatinine can mask substantial GFR loss in older patients and women (lower muscle mass); an elderly woman with a 'normal' creatinine may have a GFR under 50 mL/min/1.73 m2 (p.2387).","Loss of urinary concentrating ability gives an obligate output of ~2 L/day and nocturia; paradoxically, resolution of nocturia may feel like improvement but can actually signal worsening oliguria (p.2362).","Cardiac troponin is frequently chronically elevated in CKD without acute ischemia - serial trending over hours is more informative than a single value, and persistently elevated levels independently predict adverse cardiovascular events (p.2392).","Most CKD patients die of cardiovascular disease before ever reaching stage 5; cardiovascular risk is increased roughly 10- to 200-fold versus age/sex-matched controls depending on stage (p.2391).","In advanced CKD a malnutrition-inflammation state can paradoxically reverse classic risk factors (lowering blood pressure and lipids) and is itself a marker of poor prognosis - so 'improving' numbers can be ominous (p.2392).","A drop in GFR of up to ~30% after starting an ACEi/ARB is often a benign reflection of relieved intraglomerular hyperfiltration; if stable it can be tolerated, but a progressive rise in creatinine suggests underlying renovascular disease (p.2392, 2396).","A normal-sized kidney does not exclude CKD: diabetic nephropathy (early), amyloidosis, and HIV nephropathy can show normal or enlarged kidneys despite advanced disease; polycystic kidneys are typically enlarged (p.2394-2395).","Hyperkalemia disproportionate to the GFR decline points to hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism (e.g., diabetes) or distal-nephron disease such as obstructive uropathy or sickle cell nephropathy (p.2389).","PTH is itself a uremic toxin - high levels are associated with muscle weakness, cardiac fibrosis, ESA-resistant anemia (via marrow fibrosis), and constitutional symptoms (p.2390).","Erythrocytosis rather than anemia can occur in some CKD-associated conditions: urinary tract obstruction, polycystic kidney disease, renovascular disease, and renal cell carcinoma (p.2362)."],"pathophysiology":"CKD results from two overlapping processes: (1) initiating mechanisms specific to the underlying cause (developmental/genetic, immune-complex, inflammatory, metabolic, microvascular, or toxic injury to vascular, glomerular, or tubulointerstitial compartments), and (2) a nonspecific final common pathway in which loss of nephron mass triggers compensatory hyperfiltration and hypertrophy of remaining nephrons. Intrarenal renin-angiotensin activation with blunted tubuloglomerular feedback raises intraglomerular pressure; this adaptation becomes maladaptive, distorting glomerular architecture, impairing podocyte function and the filtration barrier, and producing progressive sclerosis and nephron dropout. The uremic syndrome arises from three spheres of dysfunction: accumulation of retained solutes (urea and creatinine are only readily-measured but incomplete surrogates for many retained toxins), loss of non-excretory kidney functions (fluid/electrolyte homeostasis, hormone production/regulation - EPO, calcitriol, with altered PTH, FGF-23, insulin), and progressive systemic inflammation (elevated CRP, falling albumin/fetuin) feeding a malnutrition-inflammation-atherosclerosis/calcification syndrome (p.2385-2389).","additionalDifferentials":["Diabetic nephropathy (commonest cause in North America/Europe, usually type 2 DM) (p.2389)","Glomerulonephritis (p.2389)","Hypertension-associated CKD / nephrosclerosis, including ischemic and vascular kidney disease (p.2389)","Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) and other cystic/tubulointerstitial nephropathy (p.2389)","APOL1-mediated kidney disease and FSGS (higher frequency in sub-Saharan African ancestry) (p.2386-2387)","Hereditary/genetic CKD - Alport syndrome (type IV collagen, hematuria + sensorineural hearing loss + lens defects), Fabry disease, cystinosis (p.2394)","Multiple myeloma (check serum/urine protein electrophoresis in patients >35 with unexplained CKD, especially with anemia and inappropriately normal/high calcium) (p.2394)","Reflux nephropathy (recurrent childhood UTI, asymmetric scarred kidneys) (p.2395)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute or subacute kidney injury that is potentially reversible - the single most important distinction from true CKD; prior creatinine values establish chronicity (p.2395)","Acute-on-chronic kidney failure from a superimposed reversible insult: ECFV depletion (GI losses, over-diuresis), urinary tract infection, new obstructive uropathy, nephrotoxin/contrast/NSAID exposure, or flare of the primary disease (e.g., lupus, vasculitis) (p.2389, 2395-2396)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Estimated GFR using race-free CKD-EPI or MDRD equations - valid only in steady state (creatinine not rising or falling); cystatin-C as an alternative marker where creatinine production is confounded by muscle mass/limb loss (p.2387, 2362)","Urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) on spot first-morning samples (replaces 24-h collection) - persistent elevation flags glomerular and systemic microvascular injury even with a negative dipstick (p.2387)","Kidney Failure Risk equation (age, sex, region, GFR, UACR) to predict progression to stage 5 (p.2387)","Serum/urine protein electrophoresis for myeloma in unexplained CKD >35y (p.2394)","Serologic workup if hematuria/proteinuria: ANA/lupus serology, hepatitis B and C, HIV (p.2394-2395)","Calcium, phosphate, PTH (and FGF-23 conceptually) for CKD-mineral bone disorder; hemoglobin, iron studies/transferrin saturation, ferritin, B12, folate for anemia (p.2394)","Renal ultrasound to assess kidney number, symmetry, size, masses and obstruction; bilaterally small kidneys indicate long-standing CKD; >1 cm length discrepancy suggests unilateral/renovascular disease (p.2394-2395)","Kidney biopsy reserved for early CKD with diagnostic uncertainty or suspected superimposed active process - NOT advised once kidneys are small/scarred (technically hazardous, scarring obscures diagnosis, no therapeutic window) (p.2395)","Genetic testing (large CKD gene panels, microarray, exome/genome sequencing) for familial disease, consanguinity, extrarenal/syndromic features, steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome, cystic disease, or young-onset unexplained CKD (p.2395)"],"redFlags":["Uremic pericarditis (pleuritic chest pain with friction rub; ECG with PR depression and diffuse ST elevation) - absolute indication for urgent dialysis; risk of hemorrhagic effusion and tamponade, so dialyze without heparin (p.2392)","Asterixis, myoclonus, seizures, or coma in advanced untreated kidney failure - uremic encephalopathy (p.2393)","Peripheral neuropathy without another cause is an indication to start renal replacement therapy (p.2393)","Calciphylaxis - painful livedo reticularis and subcutaneous nodules progressing to ischemic skin necrosis/ulceration (legs, thighs, abdomen, breasts); warfarin is a risk factor and should be stopped (p.2390-2391)","Diuretic-resistant intractable edema and hypertension, refractory hyperkalemia, or refractory pulmonary edema in advanced CKD - indications to initiate dialysis (p.2389, 2392)","Accelerating rate of GFR decline - prompts search for a reversible superimposed acute/subacute process (p.2396)","Acute systemic features (fever, polyarthritis, rash) with renal impairment suggest an acute systemic illness rather than chronic CKD (p.2395)"],"prognosis":"Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of morbidity and mortality at every stage, and most patients die of it before reaching stage 5; only 30-45% of those who reach stage 5 have advanced cardiovascular complications at that point. Left ventricular hypertrophy and dilated cardiomyopathy are among the strongest predictors of cardiovascular death. Hyperphosphatemia, high FGF-23, and persistently elevated troponin independently predict worse cardiovascular outcomes. A superimposed malnutrition-inflammation-calcification state in advanced disease carries a poor prognosis. Nephrotic-range proteinuria and heavy persistent proteinuria predict faster progression and worse outcomes. Survivors of dialysis-requiring AKI are at high risk of progressive CKD, with up to ~10% developing ESKD (p.2384, 2391-2392, 2362).","pitfalls":["Treating an elevated creatinine as chronic without checking prior values - missing a reversible acute or acute-on-chronic process (p.2395).","Assuming normal kidney size excludes CKD (diabetic nephropathy early, amyloid, HIV nephropathy retain normal/large kidneys) (p.2394-2395).","Stopping ACEi/ARB for a modest (<30%) non-progressive GFR fall - discontinuation in CKD is associated with increased death/cardiovascular events; the small drop usually reflects relieved hyperfiltration (p.2392).","Combining an ACE inhibitor with an ARB - greater incidence of AKI and adverse cardiac events; avoid dual blockade (p.2396).","Overlooking occult potassium sources (dietary salt substitutes) and potassium-retaining drugs (RAS inhibitors, MRAs, potassium-sparing diuretics) as causes of hyperkalemia (p.2389).","Gadolinium MRI contrast in advanced CKD - risk of nephrogenic systemic/fibrosing dermopathy; minimize in stage 3 and avoid in stages 4-5 unless essential (older agents) (p.2394).","Iodinated radiographic contrast - contrast-induced nephropathy, especially in diabetic nephropathy; avoid where possible (p.2395).","Pruritus and skin pigmentation often persist despite dialysis; first rule out unrelated causes (e.g., scabies) and treat hyperphosphatemia (p.2394).","Metformin and sulfonylureas are contraindicated below ~half-normal GFR; insulin requirements fall as renal degradation of insulin declines (p.2394)."],"severityClassification":"KDIGO classification stratifies CKD by GFR category and albuminuria, both predicting all-cause mortality, cardiovascular mortality, and progression. GFR categories: G1 >=90, G2 60-89, G3a 45-59, G3b 30-44, G4 15-29, G5 <15 mL/min/1.73 m2. Albuminuria (UACR) categories: A1 <30, A2 30-299, A3 >=300 mg/g. Harrison Table 319-3 stages (abnormality >3 months): Stage 1 (>60 with abnormal urinalysis/imaging), Stage 2 (>60, mild progression risk), Stage 3a (45-60), Stage 3b (30-45), Stage 4 (15-30, prepare for RRT), Stage 5 (<15, highest RRT need). Stages 1-2 are usually asymptomatic; complications emerge in stages 3-4; the full uremic syndrome appears at stage 5 (GFR <15) (p.2385-2388, 2362, Table 319-3).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2385","Harrison 22e p.2386","Harrison 22e p.2387","Harrison 22e p.2388","Harrison 22e p.2389","Harrison 22e p.2390","Harrison 22e p.2391","Harrison 22e p.2392","Harrison 22e p.2393","Harrison 22e p.2394","Harrison 22e p.2395","Harrison 22e p.2396","Harrison 22e p.2362"],"crossLinks":["aki","anemia_sympt","hyperkalemia","nephrotic"]},"copd_exac_ni":{"name":"COPD exacerbation (non-infective)","system":"Pulmonary","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"2249-2259 (Chapter 303, Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease); 1756 (Aspergillus/COPD exacerbation note)","clinicalPearls":["An exacerbation is an episodic acute worsening of respiratory symptoms (increased dyspnea, cough, and/or change in sputum amount or character), typically evolving over less than 14 days; it may or may not be accompanied by fever, myalgias, or sore throat (p.2258).","COPD exacerbation is fundamentally a clinical diagnosis; spirometry/pulmonary function testing has not been shown to help diagnose or manage an exacerbation, in contrast to its role in asthma (p.2259).","The single strongest predictor of future exacerbations is a prior exacerbation, and the greatest single risk factor for hospitalization is a previous hospitalization for exacerbation (p.2258).","Exacerbation frequency rises as airflow obstruction worsens; patients with severe obstruction (FEV1 <50% predicted) average about 1-3 episodes per year, though some severely obstructed patients exacerbate infrequently (p.2258).","Assess both the severity of the current exacerbation and the severity of the underlying COPD, because the more severe either component, the greater the likelihood of needing hospital admission (p.2258).","Examination should focus on degree of distress, tachycardia, tachypnea, accessory muscle use, cyanosis, ability to speak in full sentences, mental status, and paradoxical abdominal wall motion (p.2258).","Blood eosinophil count (from CBC differential) is used to stratify exacerbation risk and to guide whether inhaled corticosteroids are added; counts below 100 cells/microliter derive no benefit while benefit rises above 100 and is favored above 300 (p.2255, p.2256).","Non-infective precipitants are well recognized: air pollution (including extreme-heat days), allergens, pulmonary embolism, and medication nonadherence; in a notable minority no precipitant is identified (p.2253, p.2258).","Environmental exposure and Aspergillus sensitization can drive some COPD exacerbations, relevant to the eosinophilic/allergic exacerbation phenotype (p.1756).","Cor pulmonale (overt right heart failure) is now relatively uncommon since supplemental oxygen became standard, but pulmonary hypertension should be considered in patients with persistent activity limitation or lower-extremity edema; it typically arises with FEV1 <25% predicted and chronic hypoxemia (Pao2 <55 mmHg), though some develop it from vascular destruction independent of obstruction severity (p.2252, p.2254)."],"pathophysiology":"A range of stimuli converge on a final common pathway of airway inflammation and worsening respiratory symptoms. The underlying COPD substrate is fixed airflow obstruction (chronically reduced, non-normalizing FEV1/FVC) compounded by hyperinflation and air trapping; a flattened diaphragm becomes mechanically disadvantaged, raising the work of breathing during any acute decompensation. Gas exchange is impaired chiefly by ventilation-perfusion mismatching (shunt is minimal), which explains why modest supplemental oxygen usually corrects COPD hypoxemia. Hypoxemia tends not to appear until FEV1 falls below ~50% predicted, and CO2 retention is not expected until FEV1 is below ~25% predicted. Acutely, worsening obstruction and dynamic hyperinflation can precipitate alveolar hypoventilation with rising arterial CO2 and respiratory acidosis (p.2251-2252, p.2255, p.2258).","additionalDifferentials":["Pneumonia (a common abnormal finding on chest imaging during a presumed exacerbation) (p.2258)","Congestive heart failure / decompensated heart failure (a frequent imaging finding and recognized comorbidity that can mimic or coexist with exacerbation) (p.2258)","Pneumothorax (asymmetric chest findings or large-airway obstruction can mimic an exacerbation) (p.2258)","Pulmonary embolism, whose incidence is increased in the setting of COPD exacerbation and should be actively considered (p.2258, p.2259)"],"infectionMimics":["Pneumonia identified on chest x-ray or CT in a patient presenting as an exacerbation (~25% of x-rays in moderate/severe distress are abnormal, most often pneumonia) (p.2258)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Congestive heart failure (frequent abnormal imaging finding mimicking exacerbation) (p.2258)","Pneumothorax (may produce chest asymmetry mimicking an exacerbation) (p.2258)","Pulmonary embolism (increased incidence during COPD exacerbations) (p.2259)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas in patients with advanced COPD, a history of hypercarbia, mental status change (confusion, sleepiness), or significant distress; hypercarbia is defined as Pco2 >45 mmHg and carries treatment implications (p.2258)","Use of arterial pH alongside Pco2 to classify ventilatory failure (Pco2 >45 mmHg) as acute (acidemic) versus chronic, since pH changes ~0.08/10 mmHg acutely versus ~0.03/10 mmHg chronically (p.2255)","Chest x-ray or chest CT in patients with severe underlying COPD, moderate-to-severe distress, or focal findings, to detect pneumonia, heart failure, or pneumothorax (p.2258)","Consideration of investigation for pulmonary embolism given its increased incidence in exacerbations (p.2259)","Complete blood count with differential for blood eosinophil count to guide exacerbation-risk management (p.2255)","An expert-consensus objective scheme using dyspnea, vital signs, C-reactive protein, and arterial blood gases to diagnose and stage exacerbation severity (more applicable in the emergency department than outpatient setting) (p.2259)"],"redFlags":["Respiratory acidosis with hypercarbia (Paco2 >45 mmHg and pH <=7.35) indicating acute respiratory failure and a need for ventilatory support (p.2255, p.2259)","New or worsening hypoxemia (p.2259)","Mental status change (confusion, sleepiness), inability to speak in complete sentences, or impaired cooperation (p.2258, p.2259)","Paradoxical abdominal wall motion, marked accessory muscle use, and central cyanosis signaling impending respiratory failure (p.2258)","Significant comorbidity such as heart failure, or an unsafe living situation, favoring inpatient management (p.2259)","New digital clubbing is NOT a feature of COPD itself and should prompt a search for another cause such as lung cancer (p.2254)"],"prognosis":"Mechanical ventilatory support for a COPD exacerbation carries a hospital mortality of roughly 17-49%. After a hospitalization for COPD, about 20% of patients are rehospitalized within 30 days and 45% within a year, with mortality around 20% in the year following discharge. Exacerbations are also associated with greater longitudinal loss of lung function (notably in those with better baseline function) and account for the majority of COPD-related health-care expenditure (p.2253, p.2259).","pitfalls":["Withholding needed supplemental oxygen for fear of CO2 retention: oxygen targeted to SpO2 88-92% is appropriate, and although it may modestly raise arterial Pco2 in some hypercarbic patients by altering ventilation-perfusion relationships, this should not deter correction of hypoxemia (p.2259).","Relying on spirometry to diagnose or manage an acute exacerbation, which is not supported by evidence (unlike in asthma) (p.2259).","Overlooking pulmonary embolism, pneumothorax, pneumonia, or heart failure, since each can mimic or accompany an exacerbation (p.2258, p.2259).","Attributing all exacerbations to bacterial infection: PCR-based studies show viral respiratory infections may cause more than 50% of exacerbations, and in a notable minority no specific precipitant is identifiable (p.2258)."],"severityClassification":"Underlying COPD severity is graded by post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC <0.7 plus FEV1 % predicted: GOLD 1 (mild) >=80%, GOLD 2 (moderate) 50-79%, GOLD 3 (severe) 30-49%, GOLD 4 (very severe) <30%. The GOLD ABE assessment further classifies patients using symptom burden (mMRC dyspnea scale or CAT score) and annual exacerbation history: groups A and B for those with 0-1 moderate exacerbations not leading to hospitalization (A = mMRC 0-1/CAT <10, B = mMRC >=2/CAT >=10), and group E for those with >=2 moderate exacerbations or >=1 leading to hospitalization. The ATS/ERS alternative grades obstruction by FEV1 z-scores rather than percent predicted. For an acute exacerbation, severity itself is a clinical determination; an expert-consensus scheme stages it using dyspnea, vital signs, CRP, and arterial blood gases (p.2255, p.2256, p.2259).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2253","Harrison 22e p.2255","Harrison 22e p.2256","Harrison 22e p.2258","Harrison 22e p.2259","Harrison 22e p.1756"],"crossLinks":["COPD_EXACERBATION","asthma_exac","pe","heart_failure"]},"cord_compression":{"name":"Spinal cord compression","system":"Neurology / Emergency","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e: Ch. 453 Diseases of the Spinal Cord pp.3602-3607; Ch. 80 Oncologic Emergencies (MSCC) pp.631-633; Ch. 95 Epidural Metastasis pp.767-768; Ch. 19 Neck Pain pp.173-175; Ch. 26 Acute Paraparesis p.213; Ch. 136 Vertebral Osteomyelitis / Spinal Epidural Abscess p.1105","clinicalPearls":["A horizontally demarcated sensory level (identified by moving a pinprick or cold stimulus up the trunk) below which sensory, motor and autonomic function is impaired is the hallmark of a cord lesion; for the spinothalamic tract the true level may sit one to two segments above the clinically detected level (p.3603).","The upper border of sensory loss often lies one or two vertebrae BELOW the actual site of compression, so the radiographic target may be higher than the clinical level suggests (p.631).","Pain is usually the first symptom of metastatic compression and can precede neurological signs by days to months; it characteristically worsens when supine/recumbent and at night, helping distinguish it from degenerative disk pain which is often relieved by lying down (pp.631, 768, 3605).","Lhermitte's sign (an electric tingling shooting down the back and limbs on neck flexion/extension) can be an EARLY clue to cervical cord compression (p.631).","Loss of bowel/bladder control is usually a LATE feature; motor and sensory loss typically precede sphincter disturbance (p.631).","Useful trunk sensory landmarks for localising a thoracic level are the nipples (T4) and umbilicus (T10) (p.3604).","Spinal column infection (osteomyelitis/abscess) characteristically crosses the disk space to involve the adjacent vertebral body, whereas tumour tends to respect the disk space - a distinguishing imaging feature (p.3605).","Vertebral collapse is not a reliable marker of malignancy - about 20% of collapses, especially in older and postmenopausal patients, are due to osteoporosis rather than tumour (p.632).","Pedicle erosion (the 'winking owl' sign) is the earliest plain-film sign of vertebral tumour, but plain films are insensitive and a normal film does not exclude malignancy (p.632)."],"pathophysiology":"Because the cord packs nearly the entire motor output and sensory input of the trunk and limbs into a small cross-section, even a small compressive mass causes disproportionately severe, often reversible-if-treated-early, deficits (p.3559). In neoplastic compression, metastases lodge in the vertebral body or pedicle (the axial skeleton is rich in marrow); the enlarging deposit compresses the underlying dura, or a paravertebral mass (lymphoma, myeloma, paediatric tumours) extends through the intervertebral foramen into the canal. Expanding extradural tumour injures axons and myelin mechanically and compromises blood flow, producing ischaemia and infarction (pp.589, 3563). In spinal epidural abscess, as the collection expands it causes additional cord damage through venous congestion and thrombosis, so that once weakness appears deterioration can be rapid and irreversible (p.3606). The thoracic spine is the commonest site of metastatic compression (~70%), followed by lumbosacral (~20%) and cervical (~10%) (p.631).","additionalDifferentials":["Intradural extramedullary tumours (meningioma, neurofibroma/schwannoma) - usually slow-growing and benign; long symptom duration favours an intradural origin (p.3606)","Intramedullary lesions (ependymoma, haemangioblastoma, low-grade astrocytoma, rarely metastasis) - present with poorly localised burning pain and SACRAL SPARING rather than radicular pain (p.3604, 3606)","Cervical spondylotic / degenerative cervical myelopathy - osteophytes, ossified posterior longitudinal ligament/ligamentum flavum, disk degeneration; insidious, stepwise; early signs are subtle (paraesthesia, loss of hand dexterity, 'clumsiness') (p.173)","Acute spondylytic myelopathy from hyperextension injury on a pre-existing stenotic canal, sometimes producing a central cord syndrome (p.3606)","Conus medullaris syndrome - saddle anaesthesia (S3-S5), early prominent bladder/bowel dysfunction with lax anal tone, preserved leg strength (p.3604)"],"infectionMimics":["Spinal epidural abscess - midline spinal/neck pain, fever and progressive limb weakness; mostly Staphylococcus aureus (consider MRSA), with gram-negative bacilli, streptococci, anaerobes and fungi less commonly; risk factors include diabetes, IV drug use, alcoholism, renal failure, malignancy, HIV and recent spinal surgery/anaesthesia/trauma (pp.3606-3607)","Vertebral osteomyelitis / diskitis - neurological deficit in about a third of cases, mostly mediated by epidural abscess; fever may be absent, especially in cervical disease (pp.131, 1105)","Tuberculous spondylitis (Pott's disease) with epidural extension - an important cause in the developing world (p.3607)","Infectious noncompressive myelitis to consider once compression excluded (VZV, HSV, CMV, HIV, HTLV-1, Borrelia, Listeria, syphilis, Mycoplasma) (p.3603)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Osteoporotic vertebral collapse (p.631)","Spinal epidural haematoma - acute focal/radicular pain then cord or conus signs; predisposed by anticoagulation, trauma, tumour or blood dyscrasias, occasionally post-LP or epidural anaesthesia (p.3607)","Hematomyelia - acute painful transverse myelopathy from intramedullary haemorrhage (trauma, vascular malformation, vasculitis, coagulopathy) (p.3607)","Spinal cord infarction / anterior spinal artery syndrome - sudden myelopathy without antecedent pain, with preserved vibration/position sense (pp.3604-3605)","Radiation myelopathy, neoplastic leptomeningitis and spinal lipomatosis (p.631)","Subacute combined degeneration (vitamin B12 deficiency), copper deficiency (p.3602)","Inflammatory/demyelinating myelopathy - multiple sclerosis, neuromyelitis optica, sarcoidosis (p.3602)"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI with gadolinium centred on the suspected level is the initial procedure; imaging the entire spine is important because up to ~40% with compression at one level have asymptomatic epidural metastases elsewhere (and multiple epidural deposits are present in ~25%) (pp.632, 3605)","CT myelography reserved for patients who cannot undergo MRI promptly or have non-diagnostic MRI (pp.632, 768)","ESR and CRP are valuable for excluding spinal infection (raised in ~98-100% of vertebral osteomyelitis) (p.1105)","Blood cultures (positive in over half of epidural abscess cases); image-guided or open biopsy/aspiration when cultures are negative (pp.1105, 3607)","If cord compression suspected but only radicular symptoms without myelopathy, imaging may safely be deferred 24-48 h - but suspected myelopathic compression is imaged immediately (p.3605)","In compression from an unknown primary, a simple workup (chest imaging, mammography, PSA, abdominal CT) often reveals the malignancy (p.632)"],"redFlags":["Established motor deficit (paraplegia/quadriplegia) present >12 h rarely improves, and beyond 48 h the prognosis for meaningful motor recovery is poor - urgency is measured in hours (p.3606)","Autonomic dysfunction (decreased anal tone, reduced perineal sensation, distended bladder, absent anal wink or bulbocavernosus reflex) signals cord involvement and is an unfavourable prognostic sign (p.631)","Once weakness appears in spinal epidural abscess, progression can be rapid and irreversible (p.3606)","Recent-onset persistent thoracic back pain in a cancer patient is suspicious for vertebral metastasis (the thoracic spine is rarely involved by spondylosis) (p.3605)","Pain crescendo pattern, pain aggravated by cough/Valsalva/recumbency, and Lhermitte's sign warrant urgent imaging (p.632)"],"prognosis":"Outcome is strongly time- and function-dependent: treatment is most successful in patients who are still ambulatory and have intact sphincter control when therapy begins, and a good response is expected in those ambulatory at presentation - treatment usually prevents new weakness and up to a third regain some motor function (pp.632, 3606). Motor deficits fixed for more than 12 h seldom improve, and beyond 48 h substantial recovery is unlikely (p.3606). For spinal epidural abscess, prompt diagnosis and treatment allow significant recovery in up to two-thirds, but surgery is unlikely to reverse deficits fixed for more than a few days (p.3607). Recovery from paraparesis is better after surgery than radiotherapy alone, though survival may be short with widespread metastatic disease (p.768).","pitfalls":["Mistaking a myelopathy for polyneuropathy or Guillain-Barre syndrome: ascending paraesthesias suggest neuropathy, but a sharply demarcated sensory level plus bladder disturbance points to a cord origin (pp.213, 3605).","Acute severe transverse lesions cause initial FLACCID weakness and areflexia ('spinal shock') that can be misread as anterior horn cell disease or acute polyneuropathy before hyperreflexia supervenes over days to weeks (p.3604).","Cauda equina syndrome (low back/radicular pain, saddle/perineal sensory loss, asymmetric leg weakness, sphincter and sexual dysfunction, lost ankle/bulbocavernosus/anal reflexes) reflects nerve-root rather than cord compression and must be distinguished from conus and cord syndromes - mass lesions low in the canal often produce a mixed picture (pp.132, 632, 3604).","Gadolinium enhancement can deceptively 'normalise' vertebral metastases by raising their signal to that of normal marrow on MRI (p.3605).","Cervical osteomyelitis/epidural abscess may present without fever, delaying recognition - keep it in mind for neck pain with IV drug use, immunosuppression or diabetes (p.131)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames management decisions for malignant compression using the NOMS framework (Memorial Sloan Kettering): Neurologic status (degree of epidural compression, myelopathy, functional radiculopathy), Oncologic status (tumour radiosensitivity), Mechanical stability of the spine, and Systemic factors; high-grade epidural compression from a radioresistant tumour favours separation surgery followed by stereotactic body radiotherapy (pp.589-590). Clinically, prognosis is best stratified by ambulatory and sphincter status at presentation and by duration of any motor deficit (p.3606).","references":["Harrison 22e p.173","Harrison 22e p.174","Harrison 22e p.175","Harrison 22e p.213","Harrison 22e p.589","Harrison 22e p.590","Harrison 22e p.631","Harrison 22e p.632","Harrison 22e p.633","Harrison 22e p.767","Harrison 22e p.768","Harrison 22e p.1105","Harrison 22e p.3559","Harrison 22e p.3560","Harrison 22e p.3561","Harrison 22e p.3562","Harrison 22e p.3563","Harrison 22e p.3564"],"crossLinks":["malignancy_b","gbs","ms"]},"crystal_arthritis":{"name":"Crystal arthritis (gout / pseudogout)","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.384, p.2956-2962","clinicalPearls":["Initial gout flares are usually monoarticular; the first metatarsophalangeal joint (podagra) is involved in 70-90% of cases, followed by tarsal joints, ankles, and knees. Oligo- and polyarticular flares emerge over time (Harrison 22e p.2958).","Gout flares often begin at night to early morning, are among the most painful conditions humans experience, and the affected joint becomes warm, red, tender, and very swollen, frequently mimicking cellulitis (pseudocellulitis) (Harrison 22e p.2958).","Untreated flares usually subside spontaneously within 1-2 weeks, with symptom-free intervals (intercritical gout) until the next episode (Harrison 22e p.2958).","Serum urate can be normal or low during an acute flare because inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6 are uricosuric and can lower urate by about 2 mg/dL; seek historical or post-flare urate values instead (Harrison 22e p.2959).","In CPP crystal arthritis (pseudogout) the knee is most commonly affected, followed by the wrist; the first MTP joint (podagra) is rarely involved, a useful distinguishing clue from gout (Harrison 22e p.2960).","Initial acute CPPD attacks tend to last longer than typical gout flares, sometimes weeks to months (Harrison 22e p.2960).","Acute CPPD can be precipitated by trauma, severe medical illness, or surgery (especially parathyroidectomy, which rapidly lowers serum calcium and magnesium and sheds crystals) (Harrison 22e p.2960).","Women account for only about 5-20% of gout patients; most are postmenopausal, elderly, and often on diuretics with coexisting osteoarthritis, hypertension, or mild renal insufficiency. Premenopausal gout is rare (Harrison 22e p.2958).","Hyperuricemia in a prepubertal boy should prompt consideration of inherited enzyme defects such as HPRT deficiency (Lesch-Nyhan spectrum) or PRPP synthetase superactivity (Harrison 22e p.2958).","Apatite (basic calcium phosphate) arthropathy can cause an extremely destructive arthropathy of the elderly, classically the shoulder (Milwaukee shoulder), with low synovial fluid leukocyte counts despite dramatic symptoms (Harrison 22e p.2961).","Hyperuricemia and gout are part of the insulin-resistance syndrome and associate with cardiometabolic-kidney comorbidities: obesity, hypertension, type 2 diabetes, myocardial infarction, stroke, CKD, and urate nephrolithiasis (Harrison 22e p.2956)."],"pathophysiology":"Gout arises from an increased body urate pool due to chronic hyperuricemia, leading to supersaturation and crystallization of monosodium urate (MSU) within joints and connective tissue. Hyperuricemia is predominantly due to impaired renal urate excretion (~90%) and less often urate overproduction (~10%); a minority (~10%) of cases reflect inherited metabolic disorders (PRPP synthetase superactivity, HPRT deficiency). Crystal shedding from inert synovial deposits, triggered by metabolic factors, diuresis, rapid urate changes (including urate-lowering therapy initiation), local trauma, or medical illness, activates resident synovial phagocytes (monocytes/macrophages, mast cells) and the NLRP3 inflammasome, generating IL-1beta and other proinflammatory cytokines (IL-6, IL-8, TNF-alpha). Phagocytosis of MSU crystals by neutrophils with a respiratory burst amplifies the acute inflammatory response. Persistent MSU deposition produces tophi (granulomatous inflammation around crystals) and chronic erosive gouty arthritis. In CPPD, increased cartilage inorganic pyrophosphate (largely from extracellular ATP breakdown, regulated by the ANKH transporter) combines with calcium to form CPP crystals; their release similarly drives inflammasome-mediated inflammation, analogous to hyperuricemia in gout. Apatite and calcium oxalate crystals can likewise stimulate synovial cell proliferation and release of proteases, collagenases, and prostaglandin E2, contributing to joint destruction (Harrison 22e p.2956-2962).","additionalDifferentials":["Palindromic rheumatism (Harrison 22e p.2958)","Psoriatic arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2958)","Osteoarthritis - CPPD pseudo-osteoarthritis variant mimics progressive OA, often in atypical joints (MCP, wrist, elbow, shoulder, ankle) (Harrison 22e p.2960-2961)","Rheumatoid arthritis - chronic symmetric CPP synovitis can clinically resemble RA (Harrison 22e p.2961)","Neuropathic (Charcot) arthropathy - severe destructive CPPD may radiographically mimic it (Harrison 22e p.2961)"],"infectionMimics":["Cellulitis - acute gout flare with warm, red, swollen periarticular tissue may mimic cellulitis (pseudocellulitis) (Harrison 22e p.2958)","Septic arthritis - crystal arthritis closely mimics it; aspiration with Gram stain and culture is essential, especially as bacterial infection can coexist with crystals in synovial fluid (Harrison 22e p.2958, p.2961)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute CPP crystal arthritis (pseudogout) mimics acute gout flares (Harrison 22e p.2960)","Apatite (basic calcium phosphate) deposition disease (Harrison 22e p.2961)","Calcium oxalate arthropathy - acute presentation may be indistinguishable from MSU, CPP, or apatite crystal disease (Harrison 22e p.2962)","Osteoarthritis (pseudo-osteoarthritis pattern of CPPD) (Harrison 22e p.2961)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Polarized light microscopy of synovial fluid - MSU: needle/rod-shaped, strongly negatively birefringent; CPP: rhomboid/rod-shaped, weakly positively or non-birefringent; apatite: non-birefringent (requires alizarin red S staining or electron microscopy); calcium oxalate: bipyramidal, variable/strong birefringence, alizarin red S positive (Harrison 22e p.2959, p.2961-2962)","Synovial fluid Gram stain and culture to exclude coexisting septic arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2958, p.2961)","Synovial fluid leukocyte count - gout flare typically 5000-75,000/uL; acute CPPD ~24,000/uL (range several thousand to 100,000); apatite and calcium oxalate usually <2000/uL (Harrison 22e p.2959, p.2961-2962)","Arthrocentesis of an unaffected first MTP joint or knee between flares can still demonstrate MSU crystals (Harrison 22e p.2959)","Plain radiographs - advanced gout: well-defined erosions with sclerotic/overhanging margins, soft tissue tophi; CPPD: punctate/linear chondrocalcinosis in fibrocartilage or hyaline cartilage (Harrison 22e p.2959, p.2961)","Musculoskeletal ultrasound - double-contour sign over articular cartilage indicating MSU deposition; can also reveal chondrocalcinosis in CPPD (Harrison 22e p.2959, p.2961)","Dual-energy CT - identifies MSU crystals by chemical composition (Harrison 22e p.2959)","Baseline labs to assess comorbidities and treatment safety: serum creatinine, liver function tests, hemoglobin, WBC count, HbA1c, lipids (Harrison 22e p.2959)","In CPPD patients under 50, evaluate for metabolic/familial causes: serum calcium, phosphorus, alkaline phosphatase, magnesium, iron, transferrin (Harrison 22e p.2960)","HLA-B*5801 screening before allopurinol in high-prevalence populations (Southeast Asian, Pacific Islander/Native Hawaiian, Black patients) to reduce risk of severe cutaneous adverse reactions (knowledge point) (Harrison 22e p.2960)"],"redFlags":["Crystal-proven arthritis does not exclude concurrent septic arthritis - bacterial infection can coexist with MSU or CPP crystals in synovial fluid; always Gram stain and culture (Harrison 22e p.2958, p.2961)","Acute CPPD can produce systemic features (fever, chills, raised acute-phase reactants), with low-grade fever in up to 50% and temperatures occasionally as high as 40C - mandates microbial culture to exclude infection (Harrison 22e p.2960, p.2961)","CPPD onset before age 50 warrants workup for underlying metabolic disease (hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypophosphatasia, hypomagnesemia) or hereditary CPPD (Harrison 22e p.2960)"],"prognosis":"Untreated gout flares typically resolve spontaneously within 1-2 weeks, but recurrent flares over years can progress to chronic gouty arthritis with persistent low-grade synovitis, subcutaneous tophi, joint deformity, and erosive bony destruction. Controlling the underlying chronic hyperuricemia (urate-lowering therapy to a subsaturation target) can prevent recurrent flares and dissolve tophi, generally requiring lifelong therapy. For chronic CPPD there is no means to remove established CPP crystal deposits from cartilage and synovium (unlike urate lowering in gout), and progressive destructive large-joint disease may require joint replacement. Apatite-associated destructive arthropathy (e.g., Milwaukee shoulder) progresses indolently and may also lead to joint replacement (Harrison 22e p.2958-2962).","pitfalls":["Relying on a normal serum urate during an acute flare to exclude gout - urate is often normal or low at flare onset (Harrison 22e p.2959).","Attributing a destructive 'osteoarthritis' in atypical joints (MCP, wrist, elbow, shoulder, ankle) to primary OA rather than recognizing pseudo-osteoarthritis from CPPD (Harrison 22e p.2961).","Assuming chondrocalcinosis on imaging is always CPPD - calcium oxalate deposition in chronic renal failure is an exception (Harrison 22e p.2961).","Missing apatite arthropathy because individual apatite crystals are not visible on routine polarized light microscopy and need alizarin red S staining or electron microscopy (Harrison 22e p.2961).","Overlooking inherited enzyme defects when hyperuricemia/gout presents in a young patient, particularly a prepubertal boy (Harrison 22e p.2958)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2956","Harrison 22e p.2957","Harrison 22e p.2958","Harrison 22e p.2959","Harrison 22e p.2960","Harrison 22e p.2961","Harrison 22e p.2962"],"crossLinks":["CELLULITIS"]},"decomp_cirrhosis":{"name":"Decompensated cirrhosis","system":"Hepatology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.370-373, p.2672-2676, p.2756-2761","clinicalPearls":["Compensated cirrhosis can be asymptomatic for a decade or more; the transition to decompensation is marked by the first major event — ascites/edema, variceal hemorrhage, hepatic encephalopathy, or jaundice. (p.2756, p.2754)","Once clinically significant portal hypertension (HVPG ≥5 mmHg, with substantial decompensation risk at ≥10 mmHg) progresses to overt decompensation, median survival is under 2 years. (p.2756)","Encephalopathy in chronic liver disease is almost always triggered by an identifiable precipitant (GI bleeding, infection, volume depletion/overdiuresis, hyponatremia, constipation, uremia, sedatives/narcotics) rather than spontaneous progression. (p.2673, p.2759)","Ammonia levels correlate poorly with the severity of encephalopathy or liver disease; the diagnosis of hepatic encephalopathy is clinical, and most hepatologists do not rely on ammonia to make the diagnosis or follow progress. (p.2759)","A new or worsening encephalopathy episode mandates a search for occult infection — if ascites is present it should be tapped to exclude SBP. (p.2759)","A hyperdynamic circulation (widened pulse pressure, increased cardiac output, reduced peripheral resistance from splanchnic vasodilation) is a hallmark of advanced cirrhosis. (p.2673)","Acute-on-chronic liver failure (ACLF) is acutely decompensating cirrhosis with one or more extrahepatic organ failures, behaves like a sepsis syndrome, and carries 28-day mortality of 20-70% rising with the number of failing organs; it is most often precipitated by bacterial/fungal infection or alcoholic hepatitis. (p.2760)"],"pathophysiology":"Decompensation is driven by portal hypertension plus renal sodium and water retention. Portal hypertension (elevation of the hepatic venous pressure gradient >5 mmHg) arises from two simultaneous processes: increased intrahepatic resistance from fibrosis disrupting sinusoidal architecture, stellate-cell-mediated smooth-muscle contraction, regenerative nodules, microthrombi, and reduced intrahepatic endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNOS) causing intrahepatic vasoconstriction; and increased splanchnic blood flow from systemic vasodilation. Systemically, raised nitric oxide, VEGF and TNF cause splanchnic arterial vasodilation, pooling blood and reducing effective circulating volume; the kidneys sense this as hypovolemia and activate ADH, the sympathetic nervous system and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, producing free-water and sodium retention (ascites/edema). Encephalopathy results from gut-derived neurotoxins bypassing the liver via portosystemic shunting and reduced hepatic mass. (p.370, p.2756, p.2759)","additionalDifferentials":["Cardiac ascites / constrictive pericarditis (high-SAAG ascites; pro-BNP elevated, ascitic protein typically ≥2.5 g/dL) (p.371)","Budd-Chiari syndrome (hepatic vein thrombosis) and sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (high-SAAG ascites) (p.371)","Massive hepatic metastases (high-SAAG ascites) (p.371)","Peritoneal carcinomatosis (low-SAAG, protein-rich ascites) (p.371)","Tuberculous peritonitis (low-SAAG, lymphocyte-rich ascites; raised ascitic adenosine deaminase) (p.371-372)","Pancreatic ascites (low-SAAG, ascitic amylase typically >1000) (p.371-372)","Nephrotic syndrome / protein-losing enteropathy / malnutrition (low-SAAG ascites from hypoalbuminemia) (p.370-371)"],"infectionMimics":["Secondary bacterial peritonitis from a perforated viscus or abscess — distinguished from SBP by ascitic glucose <50 mg/dL, ascitic LDH above serum LDH, and multiple organisms on culture with a raised PMN count (p.372)","Tuberculous peritonitis presenting with new ascites (p.371-372)","Systemic sepsis producing leukocytosis and altered mental status that can mimic or precipitate decompensation (p.370, p.2759)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Other causes of coma/altered mentation to exclude before attributing to encephalopathy: electrolyte imbalance, sedative use, renal or respiratory failure (p.2673)","Hepatopulmonary syndrome causing dyspnea/hypoxemia (platypnea, orthodeoxia) mimicking primary cardiopulmonary disease (p.2673, p.2760)","Portopulmonary hypertension causing exertional dyspnea and fatigue (p.2760)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnostic paracentesis with serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG): ≥1.1 g/dL indicates portal-hypertensive ascites, <1.1 g/dL indicates non-portal-hypertensive cause (p.371)","Ascitic fluid cell count/differential, total protein, Gram stain and culture inoculated into blood-culture bottles at the bedside (p.371)","In high-SAAG ascites, ascitic protein <2.5 g/dL suggests cirrhosis vs ≥2.5 g/dL suggesting cardiac/early Budd-Chiari sinusoidal sources (p.371)","Serum pro-BNP to identify heart failure as the cause of high-SAAG ascites (p.371)","Upper endoscopy to screen for and grade esophageal/gastric varices once cirrhosis is established (p.2676, p.2757)","Liver ultrasound (and contrast CT/MRI showing nodular liver and collateral circulation) for portal hypertension and HCC surveillance (p.2676, p.2757)","Transient elastography (liver stiffness) to stage fibrosis and predict variceal hemorrhage, ascites, and liver-related death (p.2676)","Hepatic venous pressure gradient measurement in selected cases to confirm sinusoidal portal hypertension (p.370, p.2757)","Pulse oximetry as a screen for hepatopulmonary syndrome (p.2673)","Serial serum creatinine and exclusion of volume depletion/intrinsic renal disease when HRS is suspected (p.2759)"],"redFlags":["Variceal hemorrhage carries a 20-30% mortality per bleeding episode and is an immediate life-threatening event (p.2756)","A stepwise progressive rise in creatinine with large-volume ascites suggests hepatorenal syndrome (HRS-AKI), a poor-prognosis functional renal failure (p.2759)","ACLF with four or more organ failures persisting after 3-7 days of ICU support, when transplant is not an option, signals a transition toward palliative care (p.2760)","Avoid NSAIDs, aminoglycosides, and use ACE inhibitors/ARBs cautiously — they precipitate renal failure in cirrhotic ascites (p.371, p.2758)","New-onset or worsening encephalopathy, jaundice, or tense ascites in known cirrhosis should prompt an urgent search for a precipitant (p.2759)"],"prognosis":"Decompensation defines a poor-prognosis state: with clinically significant portal hypertension that decompensates, median survival is under 2 years, and fewer than 50% of patients survive 2 years after the onset of ascites. Encephalopathy in chronic liver disease is a poor prognostic sign. HRS (both AKI and CKD types) carries a poor prognosis unless liver transplantation is achieved. ACLF 28-day mortality ranges from 20-70%, rising with the number of organ failures. Severity and transplant priority are graded by Child-Pugh class and the MELD/MELD-Na (MELD 3.0) systems. (p.371, p.2675-2676, p.2756, p.2759-2760)","pitfalls":["Relying on ammonia levels to diagnose or track hepatic encephalopathy — they correlate poorly with disease severity (p.2759)","Attributing altered mental status to encephalopathy without excluding electrolyte disturbance, sedatives, and renal/respiratory failure (p.2673)","Failing to tap the ascites in a decompensated cirrhotic with new symptoms — abdominal tenderness is present in only ~40% of SBP and rebound is uncommon, so SBP can be clinically silent (p.372)","Multiple organisms on ascitic culture with a raised PMN count indicate secondary (not spontaneous) peritonitis and a surgical source; multiple organisms without raised PMN suggest bowel perforation by the paracentesis needle (p.372)","Withholding paracentesis for fear of coagulopathy — paracentesis is safe even in coagulopathic patients (p.371)","Restricting dietary protein for encephalopathy — its harm to nutrition outweighs benefit and it is strongly discouraged (p.2760)"],"severityClassification":"Child-Pugh score (5 factors: bilirubin, albumin, prothrombin time/INR, ascites, encephalopathy; range 5-15) classifies cirrhosis as class A (5-6, compensated), B (7-9), or C (10-15); a score ≥7 (class B) defines decompensation. MELD score uses INR, bilirubin, and creatinine to predict short-term mortality and allocate organs; it was extended with serum sodium and reweighted as MELD-Na / MELD 3.0 for transplant listing. PELD is the pediatric equivalent. Clinically significant portal hypertension is defined by HVPG ≥10 mmHg. (p.2675-2676, p.2756, p.2754)","references":["Harrison 22e p.370","Harrison 22e p.371","Harrison 22e p.372","Harrison 22e p.373","Harrison 22e p.2673","Harrison 22e p.2675","Harrison 22e p.2676","Harrison 22e p.2756","Harrison 22e p.2757","Harrison 22e p.2759","Harrison 22e p.2760"],"crossLinks":["variceal_bleed","hepatic_enceph","SBP","aki","VIRAL_HEPATITIS"]},"dic":{"name":"Disseminated intravascular coagulation","system":"Hematology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.976-978 (Ch. 121, Coagulation Disorders); p.974 (Ch. 120, thrombocytopenia algorithm)","clinicalPearls":["DIC is an acquired syndrome of intravascular activation of coagulation with loss of localization, which can damage the microvasculature and, if severe enough, cause organ dysfunction (ISTH 2001 definition) (p.976).","The central event is uncontrolled thrombin generation, compounded by disruption of physiologic anticoagulant mechanisms and abnormal fibrinolysis, producing systemic fibrin deposition in small and mid-size vessels (p.976).","Fibrin deposition can compromise blood supply to lung, kidney, liver and brain, causing organ failure; pulmonary microvascular thrombosis is a component of ARDS (p.976).","Most common causes: bacterial sepsis (also viral and fungal), trauma, obstetric events (abruptio placentae, amniotic fluid embolism), and malignancy (especially mucin-producing adenocarcinomas and acute promyelocytic leukemia) (p.976).","Sepsis-driven DIC reflects thrombo-inflammation: increased tissue factor expression, neutrophil/monocyte activation with cytokine release, neutrophil extracellular traps, and polyphosphate release driving thrombin generation (p.976).","Bleeding manifestations dominate clinically: petechiae, ecchymoses, oozing from venipuncture sites, and severe hemorrhage from GI tract, lung, or CNS; thrombosis of large vessels and cerebral embolism can also occur (p.977).","Chronic (low-grade, compensated) DIC is seen with giant hemangioma, metastatic carcinoma, and late-gestation fetal demise; bleeding is discrete and limited to skin/mucosa, with elevated FDP/d-dimer but PT/aPTT/fibrinogen often normal or high and only mild thrombocytopenia (p.977).","In critically ill patients, coagulation parameters should be repeated over 6-8 h because patients can deteriorate rapidly; an overt-DIC score should be rechecked daily (p.977)."],"pathophysiology":"DIC arises from uncontrolled thrombin generation triggered by multiple mechanisms, with simultaneous disruption of physiologic anticoagulant pathways and abnormal fibrinolysis. In sepsis and trauma, activation of inflammatory pathways increases tissue factor expression, activates neutrophils and monocytes with cytokine release and neutrophil extracellular trap formation, and releases polyphosphates that cross-talk with the coagulation system (thrombo-inflammation); endothelial damage causes loss of native antithrombotic properties. The result is systemic fibrin deposition in small and mid-size vessels causing microvascular occlusion, ischemic organ damage, and red cell fragmentation/hemolysis. Sustained activation consumes platelets and clotting factors, producing systemic bleeding that is aggravated by secondary hyperfibrinolysis in late stages. Purpura fulminans is a severe form of DIC with extensive cutaneous thrombosis, predominantly in young children after viral or bacterial infection, especially with inherited or acquired protein C pathway deficiency (p.976).","additionalDifferentials":["Severe liver disease (thrombocytopenia from sequestration/portal hypertension/hypersplenism, decreased factor and natural-anticoagulant synthesis, elevated d-dimer) — distinguished because liver-disease parameters do not change rapidly, unlike DIC (p.977).","Immune thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) — acute thrombocytopenia, red cell fragmentation, multiorgan failure, but identified by ADAMTS13 inhibitor (p.977).","Vitamin K deficiency (isolated PT prolongation as earliest finding) (p.977-978)."],"infectionMimics":["Gram-negative bacterial systemic infection (DIC is most commonly seen here) (p.974).","COVID-19-associated coagulopathy/thrombocytopenia (p.974)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Severe/fulminant liver disease and cirrhosis (coexisting chronic or acute DIC can also occur) (p.977-978).","Thrombotic microangiopathies (TTP, HUS) (p.977).","Dysfibrinogenemia of liver disease (p.978)."],"additionalInvestigations":["ISTH overt-DIC score using platelet count, d-dimer, PT prolongation, and fibrinogen (p.977).","Peripheral blood smear for schistocytes (red cell fragmentation) (p.977).","Serial/repeated PT, aPTT, fibrinogen, d-dimer measurements (every 6-8 h in critically ill; daily if overt DIC) to assess trajectory and distinguish from liver disease (p.977).","Fibrin degradation products (FDP) (p.976-977).","ADAMTS13 activity/inhibitor when TTP is in the differential (p.977)."],"redFlags":["Hemodynamic instability and shock are common in acute DIC; mortality ranges from 30% to over 80% (p.977).","Purpura fulminans — extensive skin thrombosis, especially in children post-infection or with protein C pathway deficiency (p.976).","Bleeding into the CNS, lung, or GI tract (p.977).","Rapidly deteriorating coagulation parameters in a critically ill patient (p.977)."],"prognosis":"Morbidity and mortality in DIC are primarily driven by the underlying disease; acute DIC carries mortality ranging from 30% to greater than 80%. Many patients with overt DIC are critically ill and require intensive care (p.977).","pitfalls":["Distinguishing DIC from severe liver disease is challenging and requires serial laboratory measurements — both share thrombocytopenia, reduced factor synthesis, and elevated d-dimer, but liver-disease values do not change rapidly (p.977).","The ISTH overt-DIC scoring tool is not validated for pregnant patients; a separate tool exists for pregnancy (p.977).","d-Dimer assays are not standardized and have differing normal ranges; degree of increase must be interpreted against the local laboratory range (p.977).","A single low score does not exclude evolving DIC — a non-overt score (<5) should be repeated every 1-2 days (p.977)."],"severityClassification":"ISTH overt-DIC score: Platelets >100x10^9/L = 0, 50-100 = 1, <50 = 2; d-dimer normal = 0, moderate increase = 2, severe increase = 3; PT prolongation <3 s = 0, 3-6 s = 1, >6 s = 2; fibrinogen >1 g/L = 0, <1 g/L = 1. Total <5 = non-overt/low-grade DIC (repeat in 1-2 days); >5 = overt DIC (repeat labs daily). Not for use in pregnant patients (p.977).","references":["Harrison 22e p.976","Harrison 22e p.977","Harrison 22e p.978","Harrison 22e p.974"],"crossLinks":["ttp_hus","acute_leukemia","SEPTIC_SHOCK"]},"dka":{"name":"Diabetic ketoacidosis","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.93, p.413, p.1761, p.2788","clinicalPearls":["An acetone-like fruity odour on the breath is a classic bedside clue to ketoacidosis (p.93).","Urine dipstick (nitroprusside reaction) detects acetoacetate but not beta-hydroxybutyrate, so it can underestimate the true degree of ketosis; measure plasma beta-hydroxybutyrate directly (p.413).","As the circulation is restored with fluids, beta-hydroxybutyrate is converted to acetoacetate, so the nitroprusside ketone reading may paradoxically become more positive during treatment rather than reflecting worsening disease (p.413).","Glucose may be normal or only mildly raised in euglycaemic DKA (SGLT2-inhibitor associated) and in starvation ketoacidosis, so a normal glucose does not exclude ketoacidosis (p.413).","Excretion of ketoacids obligates loss of cations (sodium, potassium), worsening volume depletion and promoting chloride retention (p.413)."],"pathophysiology":"DKA results from increased fatty acid metabolism with accumulation of the ketoacids acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate, producing hyperglycaemia, ketonaemia and a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. It typically occurs in insulin-dependent diabetes when insulin is stopped or when an intercurrent illness (e.g. infection, gastroenteritis, pancreatitis, myocardial infarction) acutely raises insulin requirements. In euglycaemic DKA caused by SGLT2 inhibitors, glycosuria drives an osmotic diuresis and volume depletion while plasma glucose stays in the normal range despite the ketoacidosis (p.413).","additionalDifferentials":["Alcoholic ketoacidosis — high-anion-gap acidosis (predominantly beta-hydroxybutyrate) without hyperglycaemia, usually with preserved renal function, in chronic alcohol use with vomiting and poor intake (p.413).","Starvation ketoacidosis — ketoacidosis with normal or low glucose (p.413).","Other high-anion-gap acidoses: lactic acidosis, toxic ingestions (e.g. salicylates), and renal failure/uraemia (p.413)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute pancreatitis can mimic DKA (and vice versa), with overlapping abdominal pain; DKA may itself be accompanied by amylase elevation, complicating the distinction (p.2788)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma beta-hydroxybutyrate measured directly (more reliable than urine nitroprusside ketones) (p.413).","Anion gap calculation, including assessment for a mixed non-anion-gap / high-anion-gap acidosis when the change in bicarbonate exceeds the change in anion gap (p.413)."],"redFlags":["Mucormycosis risk: DKA patients are at high risk of rhino-orbital-cerebral mucormycosis; acidosis frees iron from carrier proteins and beta-hydroxybutyrate upregulates fungal adherence/penetration receptors — consider in a DKA patient with sinus/orbital/facial signs (p.1761).","Severe acidaemia (pH <7.00) or shock identifies the small subset where bicarbonate may be considered; cerebral oedema risk limits its use (p.413).","Hyperchloraemic (non-anion-gap) acidosis and volume overload can be precipitated by aggressive isotonic saline during treatment (p.413)."],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Relying on urine ketone dipstick, which misses beta-hydroxybutyrate and underestimates ketosis (p.413).","Interpreting a rising nitroprusside ketone result during fluid resuscitation as deterioration, when it reflects the beta-hydroxybutyrate-to-acetoacetate shift (p.413).","Assuming a normal/near-normal glucose excludes DKA, missing euglycaemic DKA on SGLT2 inhibitors (p.413).","Over-resuscitation with isotonic saline causing volume overload and/or hyperchloraemic acidosis (p.413)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.93","Harrison 22e p.413","Harrison 22e p.1761","Harrison 22e p.2788"],"crossLinks":["hhs"]},"drug_intox":{"name":"Drug intoxication / poisoning","system":"Toxicology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"p.3703-3707","clinicalPearls":["Diagnosis of an unknown poisoning rests on pattern recognition: first classify the overall physiologic state as stimulated, depressed, discordant, or normal, then look for a recognizable toxidrome (p.3704).","Core temperature is the single most reliable predictor of poor outcome in stimulant (e.g. cocaine) poisoning or in drug/alcohol/GHB withdrawal, so it must be measured even in difficult or combative patients (p.3704).","Mydriasis is common to all stimulant states but is most marked with anticholinergics (muscarinic loss of pupil reactivity); cocaine and other sympathomimetics enlarge pupils yet retain some light reactivity (p.3704).","The anticholinergic toxidrome is distinguished from other stimulant states by hot, dry, flushed skin, decreased bowel sounds and urinary retention, whereas sympathomimetic states cause diaphoresis and increased bowel activity (p.3704).","Miosis points to a depressed state from opioids or cholinergic agents; muscarinic/nicotinic features separate cholinergic poisoning from opioid poisoning (p.3704-3705).","In opioid and sedative-hypnotic poisoning, vital-sign derangements are secondary to CNS depression and do not appear until consciousness is markedly reduced (grade 3-4 depression); marked cardiovascular depression with preserved consciousness instead suggests a peripherally acting sympatholytic (p.3705).","Rotatory nystagmus among stimulated patients is essentially specific to phencyclidine and ketamine (p.3704).","A near-normal exam may reflect a nontoxic exposure, psychogenic illness, or a 'toxic time-bomb' (slowly absorbed, slowly distributed, requiring metabolic activation, or disrupting metabolism); once-daily reformulations have made these increasingly common (p.3705).","Rapid response of altered mental status to IV dextrose, naloxone or flumazenil is virtually diagnostic of hypoglycemia, opioid poisoning and benzodiazepine intoxication respectively (p.3706).","Most patients who are or become asymptomatic by 6 h after ingestion are unlikely to develop later toxicity and may be discharged, but longer observation is required after 'toxic time-bomb' ingestions (p.3707)."],"pathophysiology":"Poisoning is a dose-related adverse effect from exposure to a chemical, drug or other xenobiotic. Responses to a given dose vary with genetic polymorphism, enzyme induction or inhibition by co-ingestants, and acquired tolerance. Toxicity may be local or systemic depending on route, the agent's physicochemical properties and mechanism, and its severity/reversibility depends on the functional reserve of the patient or target organ (influenced by age and pre-existing disease) (p.3703).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Psychogenic illness / anxiety reaction (mass hysteria, fear of poisoning) producing symptoms inconsistent with the exposure history (p.3705)","Structural CNS lesion - focal neurologic findings are uncommon in poisoning and should prompt evaluation for a structural cause (p.3703)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Characterise overall physiologic state and toxidrome from vital signs and neuromuscular exam (p.3704)","Anion gap (raised AGMA in methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylate, or any poisoning causing organ failure, seizures or shock; low anion gap with bromide, calcium, iodine, lithium, magnesium) (p.3705)","Osmolal gap (>10 mmol/L suggests a low-molecular-weight solute such as an alcohol or glycol) (p.3705)","Serum lactate (low relative to anion gap in alcohol/glycol intoxication, high and near the anion gap in hypoperfusion) (p.3705)","Glucose, electrolytes, ketones (hypo-/hyperglycaemia and hypo-/hyperkalaemia patterns can implicate specific agents) (p.3705)","ECG (bradycardia/AV block, QRS and QT prolongation, ventricular tachyarrhythmias suggest specific agent classes) (p.3706)","Abdominal/chest radiograph (radiopaque densities suggest heavy metals, drug packets, iron, enteric-coated or salicylate tablets; CXR for pulmonary edema/aspiration) (p.3706)","Quantitative serum levels (acetaminophen, alcohols incl. ethylene glycol/methanol, anticonvulsants, barbiturates, digoxin, iron, lithium, salicylate, theophylline, carboxyhemoglobin, methemoglobin) guide management (p.3706)","Coagulation (PT/INR) for warfarin/rodenticide risk stratification, though unreliable for direct oral anticoagulant overdose (p.3705)"],"redFlags":["Temperature elevation (grade 4 stimulation) - most reliable predictor of poor outcome in stimulant poisoning and withdrawal (p.3704)","Coma, seizures or cardiovascular collapse (grade 4 physiologic stimulation or depression) (p.3705)","Focal neurologic findings - uncommon in poisoning and suggest a structural CNS lesion (p.3703)","Suspicion of 'body packing'/'body stuffing' in patients ill after foreign travel or arrest (p.3703)","High blood levels of agents with more toxic metabolites (acetaminophen, ethylene glycol, methanol) signalling need for antidote or dialysis (p.3707)"],"prognosis":"Overall mortality is low (<1% of all poison exposures) but rises to 1-2% among hospitalised patients with intentional (suicidal) overdose or complications of drugs of abuse, who account for most serious poisonings. About 20-25% of exposures need bedside professional evaluation and ~5% need hospitalisation. Acetaminophen is the pharmaceutical most often implicated in fatal poisoning, while carbon monoxide is the leading overall cause of poisoning death (often fatal before discovery). Most patients asymptomatic at 6 h are unlikely to deteriorate (p.3703, p.3707).","pitfalls":["Rapid hospital urine drug-of-abuse screens are screening tests only, cannot confirm the exact substance, and are not diagnostic or forensic; false positives (e.g. fluoroquinolones triggering false-positive opiate screens) and false negatives are common (p.3706).","A negative drug screen does not exclude poisoning - the agent may be undetectable by the assay (e.g. synthetic cannabinoids/spice, cathinones/bath salts, kratom) or present below the limit of detection at the time of sampling; repeat testing later may turn positive (p.3706).","Qualitative drug screens are not clinically useful or cost-effective when the patient is asymptomatic or the picture fits the reported history; reserve them for severe or unexplained toxicity (coma, seizures, cardiovascular instability, acidosis, nonsinus rhythms) (p.3706).","Blood levels obtained during absorption/distribution may exceed tissue levels and may not correlate with toxicity (p.3707).","In polydrug overdose a clear toxidrome may be obscured when different drug classes counteract each other's physiologic effects (p.3704).","Physostigmine fully reverses anticholinergic poisoning but can also produce some arousal in CNS depression of any cause, so arousal alone is not specific (p.3706).","Overdose effects begin sooner, peak later and last longer than therapeutic-dose effects, and an agent's published (therapeutic) pharmacokinetics differ from its toxicokinetics in overdose (p.3707)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison grades physiologic stimulation and depression in poisoning/withdrawal: Stimulation - grade 1 anxious/tremulous with normal vital signs; grade 2 agitated, mildly-moderately raised vitals but can follow commands; grade 3 delirious with motor hyperactivity and markedly raised vitals, possible tachyarrhythmias; grade 4 coma, seizures, cardiovascular collapse. Depression - grade 1 lethargic but arousable to voice/touch; grade 2 responds to pain not voice, brainstem reflexes intact; grade 3 unresponsive to pain, depressed brainstem reflexes, reduced tone/respiration/temperature; grade 4 flaccid, absent brainstem reflexes and respirations, depressed cardiovascular signs (p.3705).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3703-3707 (Ch. 470 Poisoning and Drug Overdose, Mark B. Mycyk)"],"crossLinks":["opioid_od","salicylate_tox","organophosphate","serotonin_nms"]},"dvt":{"name":"Deep vein thrombosis","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Chap. 290 (Deep-Venous Thrombosis and Pulmonary Thromboembolism), p.2155-2164; Chap. 69 (Bleeding and Thrombosis) p.473-474; Chap. 98 (Cancer and venous thrombosis) p.~705; Chap. 27 (d-Dimer) p.~158","clinicalPearls":["The classic DVT symptom is a calf cramp or 'charley horse' that persists and worsens over several days, rather than a sudden event (Harrison 22e p.2159).","A diffusely edematous (whole-leg) limb actually makes ordinary DVT less likely, with the important exception of phlegmasia cerulea dolens, the most severe form of DVT (Harrison 22e p.2159).","Recurrent left thigh edema, especially in a young woman, should raise suspicion of May-Thurner syndrome, in which the right common iliac artery compresses the left common iliac vein and may precipitate DVT (Harrison 22e p.2159).","Upper extremity DVT may show only subtle asymmetry of the supraclavicular fossa or upper-arm circumference; it is increasingly linked to PICC lines and rises with larger catheter diameter and more lumens (Harrison 22e p.2158-2159, 2161).","Superficial vein thrombosis presents with erythema, tenderness and a palpable cord and carries a risk of extension into the deep venous system (Harrison 22e p.2158).","An idiopathic (no clear precipitant) VTE is the strongest predictor of recurrence in patients without malignancy (Harrison 22e p.473).","Testing for inherited thrombophilia in adults should be reserved for the rare situation where the result would actually change management (Harrison 22e p.473-474)."],"pathophysiology":"DVT arises from Virchow's triad of venous stasis, hypercoagulability and endothelial injury, now understood to be typically coupled with an inflammatory trigger. Activated platelets release proinflammatory microparticles that recruit neutrophils to form neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs); these histone-rich, web-like networks promote platelet aggregation and platelet-dependent thrombin generation. Thrombi form and propagate in an environment of stasis, low oxygen tension and upregulated proinflammatory genes. Lower-extremity thrombi usually begin in the calf veins and can propagate proximally to the popliteal, femoral and iliac veins; detached thrombi embolize through the vena cava and right heart into the pulmonary circulation, and rarely cross a patent foramen ovale or atrial septal defect to cause paradoxical (arterial) embolism. VTE shares inflammation, hypercoagulability and endothelial injury with arterial atherothrombosis, so VTE patients have more than double the risk of future MI or stroke and vice versa. Common genetic prothrombotic states are factor V Leiden (resistance to protein C) and the prothrombin gene mutation (raised prothrombin level); deficiencies of antithrombin, protein C and protein S also predispose but are rare; antiphospholipid syndrome is an acquired thrombophilia causing both venous and arterial thrombosis (Harrison 22e p.2156-2158).","additionalDifferentials":["Ruptured Baker's cyst (sudden severe calf discomfort) (Harrison 22e p.2159)","Muscle strain or injury (Harrison 22e p.2159)","Acute postthrombotic syndrome / chronic venous insufficiency (Harrison 22e p.2159)","May-Thurner syndrome (iliac vein compression) presenting as recurrent leg edema (Harrison 22e p.2159)"],"infectionMimics":["Cellulitis - fever and chills more often herald cellulitis than DVT (Harrison 22e p.2159)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Ruptured Baker's cyst (Harrison 22e p.2159)","Muscle strain or injury (Harrison 22e p.2159)","Chronic venous insufficiency / postthrombotic syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2156, 2159)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Loss of vein compressibility is the primary ultrasound criterion for DVT; a normal vein collapses (a 'wink') under gentle transducer pressure, whereas an acutely thrombosed vein stays distended and may show central, homogeneous, low-echogenicity thrombus (Harrison 22e p.2160).","Doppler interrogation adds flow data: calf compression normally augments flow, and loss of respiratory variation suggests an obstructing DVT or a pelvic obstructive process (Harrison 22e p.2160).","Contrast (gadolinium) MR venography is an excellent option when pelvic or leg ultrasound is equivocal or technically nondiagnostic (Harrison 22e p.2160-2161).","Contrast phlebography is now reserved mainly for when an interventional procedure is planned (Harrison 22e p.2161).","An age-adjusted d-dimer cutoff (age x 10 ng/mL in patients >50 years) is endorsed for ruling out PE but does NOT apply to suspected acute DVT (Harrison 22e p.2159-2160).","d-Dimer rarely helps in hospitalized patients because levels are frequently elevated by systemic illness (MI, pneumonia, sepsis, cancer, postoperative state, pregnancy) (Harrison 22e p.2159)."],"redFlags":["Phlegmasia cerulea dolens - massive proximal DVT with a diffusely swollen, painful, cyanotic limb, the most serious form of DVT (Harrison 22e p.2159).","Signs of pulmonary embolism (unexplained breathlessness, syncope, hypotension, cyanosis) since DVT can embolize; many PE patients have no detectable residual DVT because the leg clot has already embolized (Harrison 22e p.2158-2159).","Active malignancy as the underlying driver - up to ~15% of patients who develop DVT have cancer, and unprovoked VTE may be its first manifestation (Harrison 22e p.~705)."],"prognosis":"Postthrombotic syndrome (chronic venous insufficiency) is the main long-term morbidity: damaged venous valves cause ankle/calf swelling and leg aching after prolonged standing, and in its most severe form deep skin ulceration near the medial malleolus. Notably, graduated compression stockings prescribed for asymptomatic newly diagnosed acute DVT do NOT prevent postthrombotic syndrome (they are used only for symptomatic swelling relief). Recurrence risk after stopping anticoagulation is stratified (ESC 2019) as low (<3%/yr, e.g., major surgery/trauma), intermediate (3-8%/yr, e.g., minor surgery, hospitalization for acute illness, pregnancy/estrogen, long-haul flight, IBD, autoimmune disease, or no identifiable risk factor) and high (>8%/yr, active cancer, antiphospholipid syndrome). Harrison de-emphasizes the 'provoked vs unprovoked' dichotomy because many provoked VTEs recur at rates similar to unprovoked ones, and enduring risk factors (heart failure, chronic lung disease, IBD) raise recurrence risk. The Post-VTE Functional Status scale (grades 0-4) can track functional recovery (Harrison 22e p.2156-2157, 2163).","pitfalls":["Not all leg pain is DVT - sudden severe calf pain may be a ruptured Baker's cyst, and fever/chills point more to cellulitis (Harrison 22e p.2159).","Wells point-score rules are validated in ambulatory but NOT in hospitalized patients (Harrison 22e p.2159).","Using d-dimer to rule out DVT in hospitalized or systemically ill patients is futile because it is frequently elevated by non-thrombotic illness (Harrison 22e p.2159).","The age-adjusted d-dimer threshold validated for PE does not extend to suspected acute DVT (Harrison 22e p.2160).","In cancer patients, elevated d-dimer is far less predictive of DVT than in patients without cancer (Harrison 22e p.~705).","IVC filters can paradoxically increase the DVT rate by acting as a nidus for clot, and may still let small-to-medium clots pass via collateral veins (Harrison 22e p.2163).","Warfarin (not a DOAC) is the standard anticoagulant for antiphospholipid syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2163)."],"severityClassification":"Wells clinical prediction rule for DVT (Harrison 22e p.2159): low likelihood if score <=0, moderate if 1-2, high if >=3. Points: active cancer +1; paralysis/paresis or recent cast +1; bedridden >3 days or major surgery <12 weeks +1; tenderness along distribution of deep veins +1; entire-leg swelling +1; unilateral calf swelling >3 cm +1; pitting edema +1; collateral superficial nonvaricose veins +1; alternative diagnosis at least as likely as DVT -2. Pretest-probability strategy: patients with low DVT likelihood undergo d-dimer first and need no imaging if it is normal, whereas high-likelihood patients should skip d-dimer and proceed straight to compression ultrasound imaging.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2155","Harrison 22e p.2156","Harrison 22e p.2157","Harrison 22e p.2158","Harrison 22e p.2159","Harrison 22e p.2160","Harrison 22e p.2161","Harrison 22e p.2163","Harrison 22e p.473","Harrison 22e p.474"],"crossLinks":["pe","malignancy_b","CELLULITIS"]},"gbs":{"name":"Guillain-Barré syndrome","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3617-3622","clinicalPearls":["About 70% of cases follow an acute infection (usually respiratory or GI) by 1-3 weeks; roughly 20-30% are preceded by Campylobacter jejuni, with a similar share after herpesvirus infection (often CMV or EBV) (p.3617).","Weakness usually evolves over hours to a few days and is classically ascending (legs more than arms), often first noticed as 'rubbery legs', with tingling dysesthesias in the extremities (p.3617).","Facial diparesis occurs in ~50% of patients and lower cranial nerve/bulbar involvement is common; bulbar weakness with difficulty handling secretions can initially be mistaken for brainstem ischemia (p.3617).","Pain (neck, shoulder, back, or diffuse spinal pain) is common early, in ~50% of patients, and deep aching muscle pain plus dysesthetic limb pain also occur (p.3617).","Large-fiber functions (deep tendon reflexes, proprioception) are affected more severely than cutaneous pain/temperature sensation (p.3617).","AMAN and AMSAN are axonal variants associated with anti-GD1a/anti-GM1 antibodies and preceding C. jejuni; AMAN can occur in seasonal outbreaks (e.g., rural China) and is prevalent in China and Mexico (p.3617-3619).","Miller Fisher syndrome (~5% of cases) presents with ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and areflexia without limb weakness and carries anti-GQ1b antibodies in >90% (p.3617, p.3619).","If diagnosis is strongly suspected, treatment should not wait for the characteristic CSF or electrodiagnostic findings to evolve (p.3620)."],"pathophysiology":"GBS is an acute, often fulminant autoimmune polyradiculoneuropathy thought to arise from immune responses to non-self antigens (infections, vaccines) that cross-react with peripheral nerve via molecular mimicry, with gangliosides (glycosphingolipids concentrated at sites such as nodes of Ranvier) as likely targets. In AIDP, the demyelinating and most common form, both cellular and humoral mechanisms operate: complement deposits on the outer Schwann cell surface, triggering membrane-attack-complex formation, vesicular myelin disintegration, and macrophage-mediated removal of myelin. The resulting conduction block (with axons left intact) explains the flaccid paralysis and the potential for rapid recovery once remyelination occurs; secondary axonal degeneration in severe cases slows recovery and worsens residual deficit. In the axonal variants (AMAN/AMSAN), IgG and complement deposit at the nodes of Ranvier of motor axons, with anti-GD1a antibodies preferentially targeting motor over sensory roots; low CMAP amplitudes often reflect reversible nodal/paranodal conduction block rather than true axon loss, though severe cases show axonal degeneration (p.3617-3620).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute myelopathies, especially with prolonged back pain and sphincter disturbance (p.3620)","Diphtheria with early oropharyngeal disturbance (p.3620)","Porphyria (clues: abdominal pain, seizures, psychosis) (p.3620)","Vasculitic neuropathy (check ESR) (p.3620)","Critical illness neuropathy or myopathy (p.3620)","Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP) in patients with late or recurrent relapses (p.3622)"],"infectionMimics":["Poliomyelitis and acute flaccid myelitis (wild-type poliovirus, West Nile virus, enterovirus D68, enterovirus A71, Japanese encephalitis virus) (p.3620)","Lyme polyradiculitis and other tick-borne paralyses (p.3620)","CMV polyradiculitis in immunocompromised patients (p.3620)","Botulism (a neuromuscular junction disorder; pupillary reactivity lost early) (p.3620)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Myasthenia gravis (neuromuscular junction disorder) (p.3620)","Poisoning with organophosphates, thallium, or arsenic (p.3620)","Paralytic shellfish poisoning (p.3620)","Severe hypophosphatemia (rare) (p.3620)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CSF protein may be normal when symptoms have been present <=48 h and usually rises only by the end of the first week, so a normal early CSF does not exclude GBS (p.3619).","A sustained CSF pleocytosis (or counts above the GBS range) should prompt a search for an alternative or concurrent diagnosis such as viral myelitis, HIV, leukemia/lymphoma infiltrating nerves, or neurosarcoidosis (p.3619).","Electrodiagnostic abnormalities are mild or absent early and lag behind the clinical course; earliest AIDP findings are prolonged F-wave and distal latencies and reduced CMAP amplitudes (p.3619).","Antiganglioside antibodies (e.g., anti-GM1 in AMAN/AMSAN, anti-GQ1b in Miller Fisher) can support specific subtypes (p.3618-3619).","Test for HIV in patients with risk factors or CSF pleocytosis (p.3620)."],"redFlags":["Rapid tempo of progression and severe weakness on admission, plus facial and/or bulbar weakness in the first week, predict the need for mechanical ventilation (up to ~30% of patients require ventilatory support) (p.3617).","Bulbar weakness with difficulty handling secretions threatens the airway (p.3617).","Autonomic instability — wide blood pressure swings, postural hypotension, and cardiac dysrhythmias — can be fatal and may occur even when limb weakness is otherwise mild (p.3617).","Presence of fever or constitutional symptoms at onset casts doubt on the diagnosis and should prompt reconsideration (p.3617).","Prominent or early bladder dysfunction, or a sensory level, should raise suspicion for spinal cord disease rather than GBS (p.3617)."],"prognosis":"About 85% of patients reach full functional recovery within several months to a year, though minor signs (e.g., persistent areflexia) and complaints such as fatigue may remain. Mortality is <5% in optimal settings, usually from secondary pulmonary complications. The outlook is worst with severe proximal motor and sensory axonal damage; other adverse factors are advanced age, a fulminant or severe attack, and delayed treatment. Elevated serum neurofilament light chains and high anti-GM1 titers both correlate with greater axonal involvement and poorer recovery. Between 5 and 10% of typical GBS patients have one or more late relapses, many later reclassified as CIDP (p.3621-3622).","pitfalls":["Corticosteroids have NOT been found effective in GBS and should not be relied on as treatment (p.3621).","Treatment timing matters: each day counts, and by ~2 weeks after the first motor symptoms it is unknown whether immunotherapy is still effective; if a patient has reached the plateau, treatment is probably no longer indicated unless severe weakness suggests ongoing immune attack (p.3620).","Lack of improvement after a course of IVIg or plasmapheresis is NOT an indication to switch to the other modality, and combining the two is not significantly better than either alone (p.3621).","Early CSF and electrodiagnostic studies can be normal/minimal; relying on them to confirm before treating can cause harmful delay (p.3619-3620).","Acute flaccid myelitis can closely mimic GBS because sphincter disturbances may be absent (p.3620)."],"severityClassification":"Brighton Collaboration case definitions (2011) grade diagnostic certainty in three levels. Level 1 (highest certainty) requires bilateral flaccid limb weakness, decreased/absent reflexes in weak limbs, a monophasic course with onset-to-nadir of 12 h to 28 days followed by plateau, electrophysiology consistent with GBS, cytoalbuminologic dissociation (elevated CSF protein with CSF white cell count <50/microL), and no alternative diagnosis. Lower levels relax the laboratory/electrophysiologic requirements. Parallel criteria exist for Miller Fisher syndrome (ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, areflexia without limb weakness) (p.3620-3621).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3617","Harrison 22e p.3618","Harrison 22e p.3619","Harrison 22e p.3620","Harrison 22e p.3621","Harrison 22e p.3622"],"crossLinks":["myasthenic_crisis","cord_compression"]},"gerd_chest":{"name":"GERD / non-cardiac chest pain","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e: Chest Discomfort ch.15 p.104-109; Diseases of the Esophagus ch.334 p.2503-2511; Nausea, Vomiting, and Indigestion ch.48 p.300-301","clinicalPearls":["Esophageal pain shares a nerve plexus with the heart and esophageal afferents discriminate stimuli poorly, so distention or acid (chemostimulation) is often perceived as pressure-type mid-chest pain radiating to back, arms, or jaw - closely mimicking angina (p.2503-2504).","Features that favour an oesophageal rather than cardiac source include pain that is nonexertional, prolonged, meal-related, relieved by antacids, accompanied by heartburn/dysphagia/regurgitation, and that interrupts sleep - but every one of these overlaps with cardiac pain, which must remain the primary consideration (p.2509).","Heartburn (pyrosis) is the most common oesophageal symptom and its link to GERD is strong enough that empirical acid-suppression has become accepted management; chest pain and persistent dysphagia are the key exceptions that warrant evaluation rather than empirical treatment (p.2503, p.2510).","Gastrointestinal disorders are the single most common cause of nontraumatic chest discomfort, yet fewer than 15% of ED chest-pain presentations turn out to be ACS (p.104).","A burning quality suggests acid reflux but can also occur with myocardial ischaemia; conversely oesophageal spasm can produce a severe squeezing pain identical to angina - quality alone never establishes a diagnosis (p.105).","Both reflux relief and ischaemia relief overlap with nitroglycerin: oesophageal spasm may be relieved promptly by nitroglycerin just as angina can be, so a nitroglycerin response does not confirm cardiac origin (p.106).","Chest wall tenderness on palpation does NOT exclude myocardial ischaemia, and a normal physical exam is not reassuring in unstable ischaemic heart disease (p.106-107).","Some patients with acid reflux on pH testing report epigastric/abdominal pain or nausea instead of classic heartburn (atypical GERD) (p.301, ch.48).","Visceral hypersensitivity is increasingly recognised as a cofactor in GERD symptom generation, beyond simple mucosal erosion and afferent nerve activation (p.2510)."],"pathophysiology":"Pathologic reflux arises from anatomic and physiologic failure of the oesophagogastric junction valve - widening of the diaphragmatic hiatus with diminished crural diaphragm function, loss of the intra-abdominal oesophageal segment disabling the flap valve, axial (sliding) hiatus hernia causing LES hypotension, and increased junctional compliance allowing liquid to escape during transient LES relaxations (the belch reflex). Some reflux is physiologic and intertwined with belching, but esophagitis follows excessive reflux plus impaired acid clearance, which depends on peristalsis returning fluid to the stomach and bicarbonate in swallowed saliva neutralising residual acid; impaired peristalsis and reduced salivation therefore prolong acid contact. Mucosal injury is driven by a cytokine-mediated inflammatory pathway rather than purely direct caustic injury. Gastric acid hypersecretion is usually NOT the dominant factor (exceptions: Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, severe esophagitis in ~50%); chronic H. pylori gastritis may even be protective by inducing hypoacidity. Pepsin, bile, and pancreatic enzymes also injure the epithelium, and bile persists in refluxate despite acid suppression and is implicated as a cofactor in Barrett's metaplasia and adenocarcinoma. Exacerbating factors include abdominal obesity, pregnancy, gastric hypersecretory states, delayed gastric emptying, disrupted peristalsis, and overeating. Esophageal chest pain itself reflects a shared nerve plexus with the heart and poor discriminative ability of oesophageal afferents (p.2503-2504, p.2509-2510).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute coronary syndrome / myocardial ischaemia (must be excluded first given lethal implications)","Distal (diffuse) esophageal spasm - dysphagia plus chest pain from abnormal contractions with normal LES relaxation; diagnosed only after commoner causes excluded","Hypercontractile (jackhammer) esophagus","Peptic esophagitis / esophageal ulcer","Eosinophilic esophagitis (especially if dysphagia)","Peptic ulcer disease (symptomatic 60-90 min after meals)","Functional dyspepsia (postprandial distress and epigastric pain syndromes)","Biliary colic / gallbladder disease","Pancreatitis","Acute aortic syndrome / aortic dissection","Pulmonary embolism","Pneumothorax","Pericarditis (radiation to trapezius ridge, worse supine, relieved leaning forward)","Costochondritis / Tietze's syndrome and other musculoskeletal chest-wall causes","Cervical radiculitis / cervical disk disease","Panic disorder / anxiety state (up to ~10% of ED chest-pain presentations)","Esophageal rupture (greatest urgency among noncardiopulmonary causes)"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious esophagitis (fungal or viral) - more often causes odynophagia than heartburn; ulcerations are numerous, punctate, and diffuse on endoscopy","Pneumonia / pleuritis (pulmonary infective cause of chest pain via pleural involvement)","Herpes zoster (dermatomal chest pain)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pill (medication-induced) esophagitis - solitary deep ulcer at points of luminal narrowing, often near the carina, sparing distal esophagus","Eosinophilic esophagitis - edema, rings, linear furrows, white punctate exudate, strictures","Distal esophageal spasm and hypercontractile esophagus","Achalasia (with stasis esophagitis)"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG within 10 min of presentation, with serial ECGs every 30-60 min when ACS suspected; note resting ECG sensitivity for ischaemia is poor (as low as 20%)","High-sensitivity cardiac troponin at presentation and repeated (1-3 h hs-assay; 3-6 h conventional) - a rising/falling pattern with a value above the 99th percentile defines MI; a single very low hs-troponin >2-3 h after onset can rule out MI with NPV >99%","Validated clinical decision pathways (e.g., HEART, EDACS) combined with serial troponin to risk-stratify and safely discharge low-probability ACS patients","Coronary CT angiography or functional stress imaging (nuclear perfusion / stress echo) for intermediate/undetermined risk once life-threatening causes excluded; CTA can also exclude dissection, effusion, and PE","Chest radiograph (mediastinal widening, pneumothorax, pneumonia, pulmonary edema)","D-dimer to help exclude pulmonary embolism where indicated","Upper endoscopy (EGD) - first-line for mucosal lesions, Barrett's detection by color, biopsy of suspicious or atypical findings; erosive esophagitis is found in only ~one-third of GERD patients so a normal EGD does NOT exclude GERD","Ambulatory esophageal pH monitoring (24 h, or wireless capsule 48-96 h off acid suppression) - the diagnostic standard for confirming pathologic reflux","Combined esophageal pH-impedance testing (on PPI) to identify oesophageal hypersensitivity or nonacid/incompletely controlled acid regurgitation in refractory/atypical symptoms","High-resolution esophageal manometry (with FLIP/impedance) - to diagnose motility disorders (achalasia, DES) and assess peristalsis before anti-reflux surgery","Barium contrast radiography - higher sensitivity than endoscopy for strictures (especially with a 13-mm barium tablet) and for assessing function/anatomy"],"redFlags":["Dysphagia (concerning for oesophageal obstruction - stricture, EoE, or cancer)","Odynophagia (suggests infectious or pill esophagitis, or extensive ulceration)","Unexplained weight loss","Recurrent vomiting","Occult or gross gastrointestinal bleeding / anaemia","Jaundice","Palpable mass or lymphadenopathy","Family history of gastro-oesophageal malignancy","Nocturnal symptoms","Heartburn >5 years' duration, especially age >50, or new dyspepsia age >60 (screen for Barrett's / malignancy by endoscopy)","Failure to respond to acid suppression (up to one-third of presumed GERD)","Any diagnostic doubt about a cardiac cause - acute exertional/pressure pain, radiation to both arms, diaphoresis, dyspnoea, syncope, hypotension/tachycardia"],"prognosis":"Most patients in whom an acute cardiopulmonary cause is excluded have a favourable prognosis; in low-risk patients the 30-day major cardiovascular event rate is about 2.5%. However, historically 2-6% of presumed-nonischaemic chest pain patients discharged from the ED were later found to have had a missed myocardial infarction, underscoring the cost of misclassification. For GERD itself, erosive esophagitis and peptic strictures have become rare with potent acid suppression, but Barrett's metaplasia and oesophageal adenocarcinoma have increased; progression of Barrett's to adenocarcinoma is estimated at roughly 0.1-0.5% per year, with obese white men in their sixth decade at greatest risk. High-grade dysplasia in Barrett's carries a high rate of progression and a high prevalence of coexisting unrecognised cancer (p.104, p.2510-2511, ch.85 p.92).","pitfalls":["Attributing chest pain to GERD before excluding a cardiac cause - coronary artery disease must be given early consideration because of its potentially lethal implications, and oesophageal pain closely mimics angina.","Relying on pain quality, nitroglycerin response, or chest-wall tenderness to distinguish cardiac from oesophageal pain - all overlap and none is reliable; a normal exam and even a normal resting ECG do not exclude ischaemia.","Treating a normal endoscopy as ruling out GERD - erosive esophagitis is seen in only about one-third of GERD patients.","Overlooking that up to one-third of presumed-GERD patients fail to respond to acid suppression and may have an alternative diagnosis (functional, hypersensitivity, motility, nonacid reflux).","Diagnosing distal esophageal spasm or nonspecific manometric abnormalities prematurely - these are made only after GERD, peptic and infectious esophagitis, and cardiac disease are excluded; minor manometric findings are of unclear significance and often coexist with anxiety/depression and IBS.","Missing alarm features (dysphagia, odynophagia, weight loss, bleeding/anaemia) that mandate endoscopy rather than continued empirical therapy.","Forgetting that nausea/vomiting and epigastric pain can accompany MI (especially inferior MI via vagal/Bezold-Jarisch reflex) and need not indicate a GI cause."],"severityClassification":"GERD spans a spectrum of oesophageal injury: nonerosive reflux disease, erosive esophagitis (graded by antisecretory-responsive severity), peptic stricture, Barrett's metaplasia, and adenocarcinoma. Barrett's dysplasia is staged as low-grade vs high-grade, the latter mandating intervention. For the chest-pain presentation itself, Harrison frames risk by clinical decision pathways categorising patients as low, intermediate, or high probability of ACS using symptoms, age, risk factors, ECG, and serial high-sensitivity troponin (HEART/EDACS scores) (p.107-109, p.2510-2511).","references":["Harrison 22e p.104","Harrison 22e p.105","Harrison 22e p.106","Harrison 22e p.107","Harrison 22e p.108","Harrison 22e p.109","Harrison 22e p.300","Harrison 22e p.301","Harrison 22e p.2503","Harrison 22e p.2504","Harrison 22e p.2509","Harrison 22e p.2510","Harrison 22e p.2511"],"crossLinks":["acs","peptic_ulcer","pericarditis","aortic_dissection","pneumothorax"]},"glomerulonephritis":{"name":"Acute glomerulonephritis","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.319 (Approach to Renal Disease) p.2353-2356; Ch.55 (Azotemia/Urinary Abnormalities) p.341-343","clinicalPearls":["The nephritic urinalysis is the foundation of diagnosis: dysmorphic RBCs, RBC casts, pyuria, and proteinuria; always examine a freshly spun sediment to confirm active GN (p.2353-2354).","Dysmorphic ('acanthocyte') RBCs on phase-contrast microscopy point to a glomerular bleeding source; RBC casts are highly specific but very insensitive for GN, so their absence does not exclude glomerular disease (p.341-342).","The triad of dysmorphic RBCs, RBC casts, and proteinuria >500 mg/day is essentially diagnostic of glomerulonephritis and warrants serologic workup plus biopsy even when creatinine is still normal (p.343).","RPGN is a clinical diagnosis: any nephritic picture with rapidly rising creatinine should be treated as crescentic GN until proven otherwise (p.2355).","At least three immunologic studies (ANCA, anti-GBM, complement) should be sent early to triage the three RPGN categories before immunosuppression (p.2355).","Gross/cola-coloured glomerular hematuria with concurrent pharyngitis (synpharyngitic, day 1-3) suggests IgA nephropathy rather than post-streptococcal GN, which classically follows infection by 10 days to 3 weeks (p.2354).","Both PIGN and IgA nephropathy deposit C3 in the glomerulus, but only PIGN lowers serum complement; a normal serum C3 with mesangial IgA favours IgA nephropathy/IgA vasculitis (p.2354-2356).","Infection-associated GN occurring during an ongoing infection (e.g., MRSA abscess, osteomyelitis, empyema) is characteristically associated with glomerular IgA deposition and differs from classic post-infectious GN (p.2355)."],"pathophysiology":"GN reflects glomerular inflammation producing hypertension, volume expansion (edema, sometimes ascites), and an abnormal (nephritic) urinalysis; intense cellular proliferation reduces filtering surface area, causing oliguria/anuria, sodium and water retention, and concentrated low-sodium urine. Three mechanistic categories underlie RPGN: (1) ANCA-associated pauci-immune vasculitis (anti-proteinase-3/cANCA, anti-myeloperoxidase/pANCA) where autoantibodies recruit complement and drive inflammatory vasculitis of kidney and peripheral vessels; (2) anti-GBM disease, where circulating IgG directed at an epitope in the alpha-3 chain of the non-collagenous domain of type IV collagen binds in situ with C3; and (3) immune-complex disease with low circulating C3 from subendothelial deposits (post-infectious/PIGN, endocarditis, SLE, cryoglobulinemia, hepatitis B/C, syphilis). In PIGN, antibody-bacterial antigen complexes form ~10 days-3 weeks after pharyngeal or skin (impetigo) infection by nephritogenic group A strep, activate complement causing hypocomplementemia and subendothelial deposits, with later subepithelial 'humps'. Pathologically, RPGN shows parietal epithelial and inflammatory cell proliferation forming cellular crescents that compress capillaries; crescents progress to fibrosis and global glomerulosclerosis. IgA nephropathy results from mesangial deposition of C3 binding galactose-deficient IgA-1. (p.2354-2356)","additionalDifferentials":["ANCA-associated (pauci-immune) vasculitis — microscopic polyangiitis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis (formerly Wegener), eosinophilic GPA/Churg-Strauss (p.2355)","Anti-GBM disease / Goodpasture disease (anti-GBM with pulmonary hemorrhage) (p.2355)","Post-infectious / post-streptococcal GN (low C3) (p.2354)","IgA nephropathy and IgA vasculitis / Henoch-Schonlein purpura (normal C3) (p.2354-2356)","Lupus nephritis (SLE) (p.2356)","Cryoglobulinemic GN (low C4, high rheumatoid factor; hepatitis C or paraproteinemia) (p.2356)","Infection-associated GN of endocarditis, hepatitis B/C, syphilis (p.2353, p.2355)","Hereditary nephritis and thin basement membrane disease (isolated glomerular hematuria) (p.342)"],"infectionMimics":["Subacute/acute bacterial endocarditis with immune-complex GN and low C3 (p.2355)","Hepatitis B and C-associated GN (p.2353-2356)","Syphilis-associated GN, usually with vasculitis (p.2353)","Staphylococcal (often MRSA) deep infections — abscess, osteomyelitis, empyema — causing infection-associated IgA-dominant GN (p.2355)","Schistosomiasis-associated glomerular disease (p.2353)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Renal microvasculature disease — hemolytic-uremic syndrome and thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (MAHA with schistocytes, high LDH, low haptoglobin, thrombocytopenia, end-organ injury) (p.341, p.2353)","Malignant hypertension (p.341)","Renal vein thrombosis (heavy proteinuria and hematuria) and renal artery thrombosis (p.341)","Isolated hematuria from stones, neoplasm, tuberculosis, trauma, prostatitis (postrenal/non-glomerular) (p.342-343)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Phase-contrast microscopy of spun sediment for dysmorphic RBCs/acanthocytes and RBC casts (p.341-342)","Serum complement C3 (and C4 for cryoglobulinemia) to separate low-complement immune-complex GN from normal-complement causes (p.2354-2356)","ANCA panel — anti-proteinase-3 (cANCA) and anti-myeloperoxidase (pANCA) (p.2355)","Anti-GBM antibody (p.2355)","ASO / streptozyme / anti-DNase B titres for recent streptococcal infection (p.2355)","Blood cultures (rule out endocarditis), hepatitis B/C serology, HIV, VDRL, cryoglobulins (p.342, p.2355)","Quantify proteinuria — spot albumin-to-creatinine or protein-to-creatinine ratio, or 24-h collection (p.341-342)","Renal biopsy — indicated when RBC casts present; defines pattern, prognosis, and treatment (p.341-343, p.2355)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising creatinine with a nephritic sediment = crescentic RPGN, a nephrology emergency requiring urgent immunology and biopsy (p.2355)","Hemoptysis, pulmonary infiltrates, or alveolar hemorrhage with renal failure = pulmonary-renal syndrome (anti-GBM/Goodpasture, ANCA vasculitis, SLE, cryoglobulinemia) — potentially fatal pulmonary hemorrhage (p.2355-2356)","Upper-airway symptoms (sinusitis, nasal congestion, epistaxis) with GN suggest ANCA-associated vasculitis (p.2355)","Anuria in acute GN indicates severe RPGN among the catastrophic anuric causes (p.342)","Anti-GBM disease typically presents as a single fulminant episode demanding rapid diagnosis and prompt plasma exchange to preserve kidney function (p.2355)"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on acuity vs chronicity: early reversible inflammatory/proliferative/necrotizing lesions can respond to prompt treatment, whereas sclerotic and atrophic biopsy findings indicate chronicity. Untreated RPGN capillaritis leads to complete, irreversible loss of kidney function; crescents that become fibrotic and atrophic cause global glomerulosclerosis, and when >50% of glomeruli are involved (diffuse) the process usually progresses to global glomerulosclerosis. Anti-GBM disease is usually a single acute fulminant event, while ANCA vasculitides may relapse (even subclinically) over time. IgA nephropathy can show rapid GFR decline to end-stage disease, especially in those of African ancestry with high-risk APOL1 alleles. Early diagnosis is critical. (p.2354-2356)","pitfalls":["Relying on dipstick alone: it detects only albumin and can be falsely positive at pH >7 or with concentrated/blood-contaminated urine, and falsely negative in dilute urine or with non-albumin (light-chain) proteinuria (p.341).","False-positive dipstick hematuria when myoglobinuria/rhabdomyolysis is present with no RBCs on microscopy (p.342-343).","Assuming absence of RBC casts excludes GN — casts are highly specific but very insensitive (p.341).","Mislabeling synpharyngitic gross hematuria as post-streptococcal GN; it is usually IgA nephropathy and PIGN instead follows infection by 10 days-3 weeks (p.2354).","Expecting low serum complement in IgA nephropathy/IgA vasculitis — serum C3 is normal despite glomerular C3 deposition (p.2356).","Withholding biopsy in azotemia-free patients with the diagnostic nephritic triad — they still warrant serologic workup and biopsy (p.343).","Both anti-GBM and ANCA can be co-positive with pauci-immune pathology; such cases are managed as ANCA disease (p.2355)."],"severityClassification":"RPGN crescent burden on biopsy stratifies severity: focal and segmental necrotizing GN when <50% of glomeruli are acutely and only segmentally involved (better preserved), versus diffuse involvement when >50% of glomeruli are affected, which usually progresses to global glomerulosclerosis. Healed focal segmental lesions yield secondary FSGS, distinct from primary podocytopathic FSGS. RPGN is mechanistically triaged into three serologic categories: ANCA-associated (pauci-immune), anti-GBM, and low-C3 immune-complex disease. (p.2355-2356)","references":["Harrison 22e p.2353","Harrison 22e p.2354","Harrison 22e p.2355","Harrison 22e p.2356","Harrison 22e p.341","Harrison 22e p.342","Harrison 22e p.343"],"crossLinks":["vasculitis","sle_flare","nephrotic","aki"]},"heart_failure":{"name":"Acute heart failure / pulmonary edema","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Ch. 264 (Heart Failure: Pathophysiology and Diagnosis, p.1975-1986) and Ch. 265 (Heart Failure: Management, p.1987-1988); Killip classification from Ch. 286 (ST-Elevation MI, p.2247).","clinicalPearls":["HF is a clinical syndrome, not a single disease; it is the common final pathway for most chronic cardiovascular conditions, especially hypertension, coronary artery disease and valvular disease (p.1975).","About half of patients with HF have a reduced ejection fraction and half have a near-normal/preserved EF, so a normal LVEF does not exclude HF (p.1977).","The signs and symptoms of HF are neither specific nor sensitive, so a high index of suspicion is needed in higher-risk patients (older, with hypertension, diabetes or CKD) (p.1984).","NYHA functional class correlates poorly with objective measures such as LV size, EF or peak oxygen consumption (p.1982).","An S3 gallop usually accompanies volume overload and tachycardia, suggests significant haemodynamic compromise and carries adverse prognostic weight; an S4 is non-specific and may be heard in HFpEF from hypertension (p.1984).","Bendopnea (breathlessness on bending forward, e.g. tying shoes) reflects raised filling pressure, particularly with low cardiac output (p.1982).","Holosystolic murmurs of mitral/tricuspid regurgitation often appear in advanced HF even when the valves are structurally normal (functional/secondary regurgitation) (p.1984).","In acute or younger chronic HF patients, leg oedema may be absent despite marked systemic venous hypertension (p.1984).","A very low BNP/NT-proBNP can help exclude HF as the cause of dyspnoea in non-obese patients (p.1985-1986).","The obesity paradox: obese HF patients tend to have a more favourable prognosis than those with low or normal BMI (p.1986)."],"pathophysiology":"HFrEF is a progressive disease in which an index injury (e.g. acute MI, chronic pressure/volume overload, genetic or congenital disease) triggers months-to-years of structural and functional cardiovascular remodelling; patients may stay asymptomatic for long periods because compensatory mechanisms are activated that ultimately drive further deterioration. Pressure overload produces concentric hypertrophy and volume overload produces eccentric (dilated) hypertrophy, accompanied by myocyte hypertrophy, interstitial fibrosis, apoptosis and re-expression of fetal genes. Sustained activation of the sympathetic nervous system and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system initially supports cardiac output but becomes maladaptive, causing excessive vasoconstriction, sodium/water retention, electrolyte and renal disturbance, direct myocardial toxicity and arrhythmias; counter-regulatory natriuretic peptides, prostaglandins, bradykinin and nitric oxide are upregulated but inadequate. Therapies that antagonise RAAS and the sympathetic system (RAAS inhibitors, mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists, beta blockers) attenuate or reverse remodelling and improve outcomes. Secondary mitral regurgitation arises from ventricular dilatation, sphericity and annular changes, perpetuating a vicious cycle. HFpEF reflects multiple mechanisms beyond diastolic dysfunction (impaired calcium reuptake, vascular stiffness, renal dysfunction, sodium avidity, inflammation related to adiposity, oxidative/nitrosative stress, insulin resistance). The cardiorenal syndrome is driven not only by low forward flow but importantly by systemic venous congestion and raised intra-abdominal/splanchnic pressure, so relief of congestion improves renal function (p.1977-1982).","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic lung disease (orthopnoea and nocturnal dyspnoea can mimic HF; relieved by coughing/expectoration rather than sitting upright) (p.1986)","Pulmonary arterial hypertension (p.1986)","Neuromuscular disease causing dyspnoea (p.1986)","Anaemia / iron-deficiency anaemia (p.1986)","Venous insufficiency, lymphoedema or obesity as non-cardiac causes of leg oedema (p.1986)","Nephrotic syndrome and deep vein thrombosis (causes of oedema) (p.1986)","Hepatic cirrhosis, portal vein thrombosis, malignant carcinomatosis (causes of ascites) (p.1986)","Constrictive pericarditis, pericardial effusion or superior vena cava syndrome (causes of jugular venous distension) (p.1986)"],"infectionMimics":["Low-grade fever from cytokine activation may occur in severe HF and resolves once compensation is restored, mimicking infection (p.1983)","Congestive hepatomegaly with raised transaminases, alkaline phosphatase and bilirubin can be misread as biliary tract disease (p.1983)","Chronic lower-limb oedema in HF may become weeping or progress to cellulitis (p.1984)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute respiratory distress syndrome and other non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema (p.1986)","Acute or chronic kidney disease causing salt/water retention with normal cardiac structure and function (p.1986)","Restrictive cardiomyopathy versus constrictive pericarditis (distinguished by simultaneous right- and left-heart filling pressures) (p.1985)","Reactive airways disease/bronchial asthma versus cardiac asthma (cardiac asthma more often shows diaphoresis and cyanosis) (p.1986)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Comprehensive metabolic panel, complete blood count, coagulation studies and urinalysis as baseline; targeted testing for diabetes, lipids and thyroid function (p.1984)","Serum sodium and potassium (hyponatraemia is an adverse prognostic marker; hypo-/hyperkalaemia predispose to arrhythmia) (p.1984)","Renal function (urea/creatinine, consider cystatin C which is less affected by muscle mass) (p.1984-1986)","Iron studies/ferritin to detect iron deficiency (with or without anaemia) (p.1985)","Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging for tissue characterisation and aetiology (ischaemic, myocarditis, amyloidosis, haemochromatosis) (p.1985)","Cardiac CT / coronary CT angiography to exclude pericardial disease, apical thrombus or obstructive CAD when CMR not feasible (p.1985)","Cardiac PET for ischaemia/viability and to assess cardiac sarcoid inflammation (p.1985)","Cardiopulmonary exercise testing (peak VO2, VE/VCO2 slope) for prognosis and transplant/MCS evaluation (p.1985)","Transoesophageal echocardiography or contrast cardiac CT to exclude left atrial appendage thrombus before cardioversion (p.1985)","Right-heart catheterisation / pulmonary artery catheter to distinguish cardiogenic from non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema and guide therapy in severe HF (p.1985)","Endomyocardial biopsy in selected cases (suspected myocarditis - lymphocytic, eosinophilic, sarcoid or giant cell - amyloidosis, or allograft rejection) (p.1985)","Newer biomarkers galectin-3 and soluble ST2 are approved for prognosis but not widely used (p.1985)"],"redFlags":["Acute pulmonary oedema with pink frothy sputum reflects marked elevation of pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (p.1982)","Hypotension with narrow pulse pressure and a rapid, thready pulse indicates severe HF; pulsus alternans signals severely depressed LV function (p.1983)","Cool extremities, peripheral cyanosis, mental dullness/confusion and oliguria suggest low-output/advanced-stage HF (p.1982-1983)","Marked elevation of transaminases and lactic acid suggests cardiogenic shock with severe low output (p.1984)","Cardiac cachexia (unintentional oedema-free weight loss >5% over 12 months) marks longstanding severe HF (p.1983)","An S3 gallop carries negative prognostic significance (p.1984)","Cheyne-Stokes/periodic breathing and central sleep apnoea are features of advanced HF (p.1983)"],"prognosis":"Overall 5-year survival after a HF diagnosis is roughly 50%, and in severe HF 1-year mortality may reach 40%; one in eight U.S. deaths lists HF on the certificate, most dying of progressive HF or sudden cardiac death. After a HF admission, mortality runs from 8-14% at 30 days to 26-37% at 1 year and up to 75% at 5 years, with readmission of 20-25% at 60 days and nearly 50% at 6 months; risk of death rises with each subsequent admission. Independent predictors of adverse outcome include older age, male sex, diabetes, CKD, CAD, advanced NYHA class, a third heart sound or raised JVP, reduced LV (or RV) EF, secondary mitral/tricuspid regurgitation, raised wedge pressure, pulmonary hypertension, low peak VO2, hyponatraemia, hyperuricaemia, worsening renal function, raised troponin/natriuretic peptides and neurohormones, wide QRS/LBBB, atrial fibrillation and ventricular arrhythmia. HFrecEF (recovered EF) generally carries a better prognosis than HFrEF or HFpEF, but withdrawing therapy can cause relapse in up to half within 6 months (p.1976-1977).","pitfalls":["Abandon the old terms systolic and diastolic HF: most HF patients, regardless of EF, have both systolic and diastolic abnormalities (p.1977)","BNP/NT-proBNP may be falsely low in obesity and falsely elevated by age, female sex, CKD, atrial fibrillation, pulmonary embolism and pulmonary hypertension, so interpret in context (p.1985-1986)","Inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP, uric acid) are non-specific and do not establish the diagnosis of HF (p.1984)","Congestive hepatopathy and gut-wall oedema can be mistaken for primary biliary or bowel disease (p.1983)","Distinguishing cardiac from pulmonary dyspnoea may be impossible when both coexist (common in older smokers); post-diuresis pulmonary function tests or cardiopulmonary exercise testing can help (p.1986)","Failure to identify and correct a precipitant can leave HF persistent despite adequate treatment; a precipitant is identifiable in 50-90% of admissions (p.1983)","Nonpitting oedema unresponsive to escalating diuretics may be lymphoedema, not HF (p.1984)","High-output HF (anaemia, thyrotoxicosis, AV shunt, cirrhosis, severe obesity, beriberi) will be missed if not actively considered in predisposed patients (p.1978, p.1982)"],"severityClassification":"HFrEF/HFmrEF/HFpEF phenotype by EF: reduced EF (HFrEF) <=40%, mildly reduced EF (HFmrEF) 41-49%, preserved EF (HFpEF) >=50%; LVEF >=50% is considered normal systolic function (p.1977-1978, p.1984). NYHA functional class I-IV grades symptoms by the activity that provokes them: class I none with ordinary activity, class II mild symptoms on ordinary exertion, class III symptoms on less-than-ordinary activity, class IV symptoms (or angina) at rest worsening with any activity (p.1982). ACC/AHA stages describe disease progression: stage B asymptomatic ventricular dysfunction, stage C symptomatic HF, stage D advanced/refractory HF with high mortality where transplant or mechanical circulatory support may be considered (p.1987). Killip classification (originally for acute MI) grades pump failure: class I no congestion; class II basal rales, S3 or right-heart congestion; class III pulmonary oedema; class IV cardiogenic shock (SBP <90 mmHg with peripheral vasoconstriction, cyanosis, confusion, oliguria) - historically rising mortality from class I to IV (p.2247).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1975","Harrison 22e p.1976","Harrison 22e p.1977","Harrison 22e p.1978","Harrison 22e p.1979","Harrison 22e p.1980","Harrison 22e p.1982","Harrison 22e p.1983","Harrison 22e p.1984","Harrison 22e p.1985","Harrison 22e p.1986","Harrison 22e p.1987","Harrison 22e p.1988","Harrison 22e p.2247"],"crossLinks":["acs","atrial_fib","ckd","pe","copd_exac_ni"]},"hepatic_enceph":{"name":"Hepatic encephalopathy","system":"Hepatology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2716-2717 (HE in cirrhosis), p.230-231 (pathophysiology of metabolic/hepatic encephalopathy), p.2629-2630 (signs of hepatic failure)","clinicalPearls":["Hepatic encephalopathy is far more common in chronic liver disease than in acute liver injury; in severe acute liver injury its presence is itself a defining criterion for acute liver failure (Harrison 22e p.2716).","Asterixis (the 'liver flap') is typically present and is elicited by having the patient extend the arms and dorsiflex the wrists, looking for a sudden forward jerk of the wrist; it requires a cooperative patient (Harrison 22e p.2716).","Earliest manifestations are subtle and nonspecific: altered sleep pattern, personality change, irritability, and mental dullness, progressing to confusion, disorientation, stupor and coma (Harrison 22e p.2629).","Patients may be agitated, violent and hard to manage, or alternatively very sleepy and difficult to rouse (Harrison 22e p.2716).","In acute liver failure, presentation may include excitability or mania rather than depressed consciousness (Harrison 22e p.2629).","Fetor hepaticus, a sweetish ammoniacal breath odor, can accompany liver failure, especially with portosystemic shunting of blood (Harrison 22e p.2629).","After a first episode, patients and caregivers often learn to recognize recurrences early and can self-medicate to prevent worsening (Harrison 22e p.2717).","Gut-derived neurotoxins that bypass the liver because of vascular shunting and reduced hepatic mass reach the brain and produce the syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2716)."],"pathophysiology":"Hepatic encephalopathy is an alteration of mental status and cognition resulting from liver failure, in which gut-derived neurotoxins are not cleared because of portosystemic vascular shunting and decreased functional hepatic mass and thus reach the brain. Ammonia is the principal candidate toxin: high ammonia concentrations are thought to drive increased synthesis of glutamine within astrocytes, causing osmotic astrocyte swelling, mitochondrial energy failure, and generation of reactive nitrogen and oxygen species, together with increased levels of the inhibitory neurotransmitter GABA and synthesis of putative 'false' neurotransmitters. No single one of these mechanisms fully explains the confusion and stupor, and which is mainly responsible for hepatic coma is not known. Other contributing compounds include certain false neurotransmitters and mercaptans. Chronic HE is characteristically accompanied by diffuse astrocytosis. In acute liver failure (but not chronic liver disease) cerebral edema with gray-matter swelling can occur and carries a risk of cerebral herniation (Harrison 22e p.230-231, p.2716).","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of altered mental status must always be considered, since the diagnosis is clinical (Harrison 22e p.2716)","Sedative/narcotic intoxication (Harrison 22e p.2629)","Renal failure / uremic encephalopathy (Harrison 22e p.2629)","Respiratory failure (Harrison 22e p.2629)","Electrolyte imbalance as a primary cause of confusion (Harrison 22e p.2629)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Metabolic encephalopathy from other causes (hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, hyperosmolar states, hypercapnia, hypercalcemia, renal failure) — asterixis and multifocal myoclonus are nonspecific and indicate a metabolic or toxic process generally, not HE specifically (Harrison 22e p.230, p.225)","Drug intoxication, which likewise can produce asterixis in a drowsy, confused patient (Harrison 22e p.231)","Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome and alcohol withdrawal in patients with alcohol-associated liver disease (Harrison 22e p.2674)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful mental status examination is the central assessment (Harrison 22e p.2629)","Trail-making (connect-the-dots / number-connection) test: normal 15-30 s, markedly prolonged in early HE (Harrison 22e p.2629)","Bedside cognitive tasks such as drawing abstract objects or comparing the patient's signature with prior examples (Harrison 22e p.2629)","Diagnostic paracentesis of ascites to exclude infection as a precipitant (Harrison 22e p.2716)","Evaluate for GI bleeding (Harrison 22e p.2716)","Serum electrolytes (Harrison 22e p.2716)","EEG and visual evoked potentials can detect mild forms but are rarely clinically useful (Harrison 22e p.2629)"],"redFlags":["Encephalopathy appearing during acute hepatitis is the major criterion for acute liver failure and indicates a poor prognosis (Harrison 22e p.2629)","In acute liver failure, severe encephalopathy with cerebral edema can progress to cerebral herniation (Harrison 22e p.2716)","Other presenting features of poor prognosis in acute hepatitis: ascites, peripheral edema, prolonged PT, hypoalbuminemia, hypoglycemia, very high bilirubin (Harrison 22e p.2672)","HE may herald acute-on-chronic liver failure (brain is one of the failing organ systems), which carries 28-day mortality of 20-70% rising with the number of organ failures (Harrison 22e p.2717)"],"prognosis":"Development of hepatic encephalopathy in chronic liver disease is a poor prognostic sign, although the complication can be managed in the vast majority of patients. Hepatic encephalopathy grade is one of the five Child-Pugh variables (with bilirubin, albumin, prothrombin time, and ascites severity) used to stratify cirrhosis into classes A-C of predicted survival. In acute hepatitis, the appearance of encephalopathy defines acute liver failure and indicates poor prognosis (Harrison 22e p.2717, p.2629).","pitfalls":["Ammonia levels are typically elevated but correlate poorly with severity of liver disease; most hepatologists do not rely on ammonia to make the diagnosis or to follow clinical progress (Harrison 22e p.2716).","Always identify and treat a precipitant — in cirrhosis, encephalopathy is usually triggered by volume depletion/dehydration, GI bleeding, infection, constipation, hyponatremia, overdiuresis, uremia, electrolyte imbalance, or sedatives/narcotic analgesics; correcting hydration and electrolytes alone is sometimes sufficient (Harrison 22e p.2629, p.2716).","Asterixis is nonspecific — it also occurs in other metabolic and toxic encephalopathies, so it does not by itself establish HE (Harrison 22e p.230-231).","The diagnosis is clinical and requires an experienced clinician to integrate the various features; there is no single confirmatory test (Harrison 22e p.2716)."],"severityClassification":"Severity is graded clinically along a continuum from the earliest covert/subtle changes (altered sleep, personality change, irritability, mental dullness — detectable mainly by mental-status testing such as the trail-making test) through confusion and disorientation to stupor and coma. Encephalopathy grade is incorporated as one component of the Child-Pugh classification of cirrhosis severity. (Note: Harrison 22e describes this graded clinical spectrum and 'grade' in the Child-Pugh score but the supplied text does not lay out the explicit numbered West Haven criteria; Harrison 22e p.2629, p.2716, p.2715/HCC staging.)","references":["Harrison 22e p.2716","Harrison 22e p.2717","Harrison 22e p.2629","Harrison 22e p.2630","Harrison 22e p.230","Harrison 22e p.231","Harrison 22e p.2674"],"crossLinks":["decomp_cirrhosis","SBP","variceal_bleed","metabolic_enceph"]},"hhs":{"name":"Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.416, p.3216-3219","clinicalPearls":["Typical patient is an older adult with type 2 diabetes presenting after a several-week prodrome of polyuria, weight loss and falling oral intake, progressing to confusion, lethargy or coma (p.3219).","Examination reflects profound dehydration and hyperosmolality: hypotension, tachycardia and altered mental status (p.3219).","Features classically ABSENT in HHS that are seen in DKA: nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain and Kussmaul respirations (p.3219).","HHS and DKA lie on a continuum of hyperglycaemia; up to one-third of patients have overlapping features of both (p.3216).","Plasma glucose in HHS is usually markedly higher than in DKA and may exceed 55.5 mmol/L (1000 mg/dL) (p.3219).","A debilitating condition (e.g. prior stroke, dementia) or a social situation that limits access to water frequently contributes to HHS by impairing water intake (p.3218-3219).","Worldwide reported cases of both HHS and DKA are rising for reasons that are not clear (p.3216)."],"pathophysiology":"HHS arises from RELATIVE insulin deficiency combined with inadequate fluid intake. Insulin deficiency drives hepatic glucose overproduction (glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis) and impairs glucose uptake by skeletal muscle. The resulting hyperglycaemia causes an osmotic diuresis with intravascular volume depletion that is worsened by inadequate fluid replacement. The reason ketosis is absent is not fully understood; the insulin deficiency is presumed to be only relative and less severe than in DKA, and some studies show lower counterregulatory hormone and free-fatty-acid levels in HHS than in DKA, with possibly reduced hepatic ketone synthesis or an insulin/glucagon ratio that does not favour ketogenesis. Inflammatory markers (cytokines, C-reactive protein) are elevated in both HHS and DKA (p.3217-3219).","additionalDifferentials":["Diabetic ketoacidosis (and the DKA/HHS overlap presentation) (p.3216).","Other causes of an altered/hyperosmolar sensorium - the differential for hyperosmolar coma includes uremia, nonketotic hyperosmolar states and other metabolic derangements (p.3216, general)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma glucose - typically markedly elevated, may be >55.5 mmol/L (1000 mg/dL) (p.3219).","Serum osmolality - characteristically >300 mOsm/L (p.3219).","Corrected serum sodium - measured Na may be normal or low, but corrected Na (add ~1.6 meq/L for each 5.6 mmol/L [100 mg/dL] rise in glucose) is usually elevated (p.3219).","Renal function (BUN/creatinine) - prerenal azotaemia from volume depletion; creatinine typically moderately raised (p.3219).","Ketones/beta-hydroxybutyrate - absent or only mild; serum beta-hydroxybutyrate <1.0 mmol/L; any moderate ketonuria is usually due to starvation (p.3219).","Acid-base status - acidosis absent or mild; a small anion-gap metabolic acidosis may reflect raised lactic acid (p.3219)."],"redFlags":["HHS carries a substantially higher mortality than DKA (up to ~15% in some series), reflecting older patients, more pronounced dehydration and serious precipitating events with comorbidities (p.3219).","Hyperosmolar state must be reversed gradually - too-rapid correction risks worsening neurologic function (p.3219).","Precipitant is often a serious concurrent illness - myocardial infarction, stroke, sepsis, pneumonia or other severe infection - which must be actively sought (p.3219)."],"prognosis":"Even with appropriate treatment, HHS has a substantially higher mortality rate than DKA, reported up to about 15% in some clinical series. The worse outlook reflects that HHS patients are usually older, have more profound fluid losses and dehydration (developed over days to weeks), are more likely to have mental status changes, and more often have a life-threatening precipitating event with accompanying comorbidities (p.3219).","pitfalls":["A normal or low measured serum sodium can be falsely reassuring - it must be corrected for hyperglycaemia, after which it is usually elevated and reveals a profound water deficit (p.3219).","Mild ketonuria may be present but is typically from starvation, not from ketoacidosis - it should not redirect the diagnosis away from HHS (p.3219).","Fever may be absent even when infection is the precipitant; signs of infection should be sought regardless (p.3217, DKA section, applicable to acute hyperglycaemic crises)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a graded severity classification specific to HHS (unlike DKA, which is graded mild/moderate/severe by pH, bicarbonate and mental status). HHS is instead defined by marked hyperglycaemia, hyperosmolality (>300 mOsm/L) and the relative absence of ketosis/acidosis, on a continuum with DKA (p.3218-3219).","references":["Harrison 22e Ch.416 p.3216","Harrison 22e Ch.416 p.3217","Harrison 22e Ch.416 p.3218","Harrison 22e Ch.416 p.3219"],"crossLinks":["dka"]},"htn_emergency":{"name":"Hypertensive emergency","system":"Cardiology / Neuro","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2148 (hypertensive urgencies & emergencies), p.2153-2154 (malignant hypertension / arteriolonephrosclerosis), p.190-192 (coma evaluation, PRES/hyperperfusion syndromes), p.227 (hypertensive retinopathy), p.2871 (scleroderma renal crisis)","clinicalPearls":["The threshold prompting concern for a crisis is typically a markedly elevated BP, often described as SBP/DBP at or above ~180/100-120 mmHg; however the level alone does not define an emergency — the presence of acute, ongoing end-organ injury does (p.2148).","Urgency vs emergency is a functional distinction: an asymptomatic patient without evidence of active end-organ damage has an urgency (manageable with oral agents in the outpatient setting), whereas demonstrable acute target-organ injury makes it an emergency requiring IV therapy with close inpatient/ER supervision (p.2148).","Hypertensive urgency is far more common than true emergency; over-treating urgency as if it were an emergency risks harm from excessive BP lowering (p.2148).","Certain syndromes — acute aortic dissection, eclampsia or severe pre-eclampsia, and pheochromocytoma in crisis — flag a need for particularly rapid, aggressive BP control compared with other emergencies (p.2148).","After the acute BP is brought under control, look for secondary causes of hypertension — renovascular disease in particular is relatively common among patients presenting with a hypertensive emergency (p.2148).","Malignant hypertension most often arises in a patient with known, treated hypertension who has stopped taking medications, or who uses vasospastic drugs such as cocaine (p.2153-2154).","In acute hypertensive retinopathy, BP must not be lowered precipitously: an abrupt drop risks optic disc infarction and worsening visual loss (p.227)."],"pathophysiology":"Severe, rapidly progressive BP elevation produces mechanical/pressure-related vascular injury that overwhelms protective vascular tone. In malignant hypertension the characteristic lesion is fibrinoid necrosis of small vessels — breakdown of the vessel wall with deposition of eosinophilic fibrin-containing material and a perivascular cellular infiltrate; larger interlobular arteries may show a hyperplastic 'onionskin' proliferative lesion. These changes can obliterate glomeruli and tubular structures, driving progressive kidney failure. Multiple pathways can generate this pressure injury, including activation of the renin-angiotensin system and intense vasospasm from catecholamine release; when endothelial injury is severe enough it triggers microangiopathic hemolysis. Cerebral involvement reflects failure of cerebral blood-flow autoregulation: at extreme pressures autoregulatory limits are exceeded, producing hyperperfusion, vasogenic edema and the encephalopathy/PRES picture — which is also why too-rapid lowering below the autoregulatory range can cause cerebral or optic ischemia (p.2153-2154, p.192, p.227).","additionalDifferentials":["Scleroderma renal crisis — accelerated hypertension with AKI, clinically indistinguishable from malignant hypertension; consider in systemic sclerosis, especially diffuse/progressive skin disease, anti-RNA-polymerase-III antibody, or recent glucocorticoid exposure (p.2871).","Pheochromocytoma in crisis — catecholamine surge causing paroxysmal severe hypertension (p.2148).","Eclampsia / severe pre-eclampsia in a pregnant or peripartum patient (p.2148)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cocaine or other vasospastic-drug-induced severe hypertension (can precipitate malignant-phase hypertension) (p.2153-2154).","Thrombotic microangiopathies (TTP/HUS) which overlap with the microangiopathic hemolysis seen in malignant hypertension (p.2153-2154)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Funduscopy for hypertensive end-organ change — arteriolar sclerosis/narrowing, splinter (flame) hemorrhages, cotton-wool spots (nerve-fiber-layer infarcts), hard exudates into the macula, and papilledema (p.227, p.190).","CBC with peripheral smear and reticulocytes to detect microangiopathic hemolysis — anemia, schistocytes, reticulocytosis (p.2153-2154).","Urinalysis — hematuria, proteinuria, granular/nephritic sediment in renal involvement (p.2153-2154, p.2871).","Serum creatinine to detect/track acute kidney injury (p.2153-2154).","Once BP is controlled, work-up for secondary hypertension, especially renovascular imaging (p.2148)."],"redFlags":["Hypertensive encephalopathy — headache, visual disturbance, nausea/vomiting, seizures, altered consciousness (p.2148).","Acute left ventricular failure / pulmonary edema in the setting of severe hypertension (p.2148).","Acute kidney failure with severe hypertension (p.2148).","Papilledema, retinal hemorrhages or acute visual loss (signs of malignant-phase / hypertensive retinopathy) (p.2153-2154, p.227).","Features suggesting a rapid-care syndrome: chest/back pain (aortic dissection), pregnancy with seizures (eclampsia), or paroxysmal catecholamine symptoms (pheochromocytoma) (p.2148)."],"prognosis":"Untreated malignant-phase hypertension with target-organ injury (papilledema, declining kidney function) historically carried mortality exceeding 50% over 6-12 months, with older reports of >90% one-year mortality before effective drug therapy — hence the term 'malignant'. With effective BP reduction, manifestations of vascular injury including microangiopathic hemolysis and renal dysfunction can improve over time, and current 5-year survival exceeds 50% (p.2153-2154).","pitfalls":["Treating an asymptomatic high BP (urgency) with aggressive IV agents as though it were an emergency, risking harm from over-rapid lowering (p.2148).","Aiming for immediate normalization of BP — a controlled reduction rather than normalization is sufficient, followed by transition to oral agents; potent IV vasodilators can overshoot and cause hypotension (p.2148).","Lowering BP precipitously in acute hypertensive retinopathy or cerebral involvement, where falling below the autoregulatory range risks optic disc or cerebral infarction (p.227).","Missing a treatable secondary cause (notably renovascular disease) by not investigating after acute control (p.2148).","Overlooking normotensive scleroderma renal crisis — ~10% present with BP in the normal range and carry a poor outcome (p.2871)."],"severityClassification":"Hypertensive urgency = markedly elevated BP (e.g., SBP/DBP >=180/100 mmHg) in an asymptomatic patient WITHOUT evidence of acute ongoing end-organ damage — manage with oral agents as outpatient. Hypertensive emergency = comparably high BP WITH evidence of active end-organ injury (encephalopathy, acute LV failure/pulmonary edema, acute kidney failure, retinal hemorrhage/papilledema, dissection, eclampsia, pheochromocytoma crisis) — requires immediate, carefully supervised IV therapy in ER/inpatient setting. Malignant hypertension is the severe phenotype defined by rapidly progressive BP elevation with fibrinoid-necrosis vascular injury, retinal hemorrhages/papilledema, encephalopathy and declining kidney function (p.2148, p.2153-2154).","references":["Harrison 22e p.190","Harrison 22e p.192","Harrison 22e p.227","Harrison 22e p.2148","Harrison 22e p.2153","Harrison 22e p.2154","Harrison 22e p.2871"],"crossLinks":["aortic_dissection","ich","ischemic_stroke","heart_failure"]},"hypercalcemia":{"name":"Hypercalcaemia of malignancy","system":"Oncology / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chap 57 (p.362-364), Chap 98 (p.737-738)","clinicalPearls":["Humoral hypercalcaemia of malignancy (HHM) occurs in up to ~20% of cancer patients; the typical picture is a patient with known malignancy found hypercalcaemic on routine labs, though occasionally hypercalcaemia is the presenting feature of an undiagnosed cancer (p.737).","Most malignancy-associated hypercalcaemia runs a relatively acute course, in contrast to the chronic, fluctuating high-normal pattern of primary hyperparathyroidism or occasionally myeloma (p.363).","An elevated PTHrP level confirms HHM and is raised in roughly 80% of hypercalcaemic cancer patients; PTH should be suppressed in true HHM (p.738).","Features favouring HHM over primary hyperparathyroidism: known malignancy, recent onset, and very high serum calcium levels (p.738).","PTHrP shares homology with the first ~13 amino acids of PTH and binds the PTH receptor, explaining the PTH-like biochemical effects on bone and kidney (p.363, 737).","Serum free light chains should be checked when myeloma is suspected, since ~3% of myeloma is missed by serum/urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation (p.364).","Hypercalcaemia of malignancy is one of the few clinical settings where measuring serum 1,25(OH)2D is genuinely useful — it is elevated or inappropriately normal in lymphoma- and granulomatous-mediated hypercalcaemia (p.364).","Always confirm true hypercalcaemia first by correcting for albumin (add ~0.8 mg/dL to total calcium per 1.0 g/dL fall in albumin below 4.0 g/dL) to exclude pseudohypercalcaemia (p.363)."],"pathophysiology":"Calcium balance is governed by PTH and the vitamin D metabolites 1,25(OH)2D and 25(OH)D acting across parathyroid, kidney, intestine and bone. Hypercalcaemia of malignancy arises mainly through three mechanisms. (1) PTHrP overproduction by solid tumours (squamous head/neck, lung, skin; breast; genitourinary; gastrointestinal) — PTHrP mimics PTH at the PTH receptor, raising bone resorption and renal calcium reabsorption. (2) Local osteolysis from skeletal metastases (breast, myeloma), where metastatic deposits secrete PTHrP and chemokines driving bone breakdown. (3) Excess 1,25(OH)2D production, notably by lymphomas (and granulomatous disease) via macrophage CYP27B1 converting 25(OH)D to active 1,25(OH)2D, enhancing intestinal calcium absorption. PTHrP induction is linked to PTHLH hypomethylation, hedgehog/Gli signalling, TGF-beta, oncogene activation (e.g. Ras) and p53 loss. In all these malignant mechanisms, the high serum calcium activates the parathyroid CaSR and appropriately suppresses endogenous PTH (p.363, 737).","additionalDifferentials":["Primary hyperparathyroidism (the other most common cause of hypercalcaemia; chronic, often with low/low-normal phosphate and inappropriately normal/high PTH) (p.362-364)","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia (FHH) — autosomal dominant, inactivating CASR mutations; calcium/creatinine clearance ratio <0.01, parathyroid surgery ineffective (p.363)","Multiple myeloma (consider in malignant setting; can also cause chronic hypercalcaemia) (p.363-364)","Granulomatous disease (sarcoidosis, tuberculosis, leprosy, fungal infection, foreign-body reaction) via extrarenal 1,25(OH)2D (p.362-363)","Vitamin D intoxication (excess 25(OH)D or 1,25(OH)2D; CYP24A1 deficiency) (p.362-363)","Thyrotoxicosis, Paget's disease, prolonged immobilisation (increased bone resorption) (p.363)","Milk-alkali syndrome / excess calcium intake or TPN (p.362-363)","Drugs: thiazides, vitamin A, lithium, foscarnet, teriparatide, aromatase inhibitors; rebound resorption after denosumab discontinuation (p.362)","Endocrine causes: adrenal insufficiency, phaeochromocytoma, acromegaly, VIPoma (p.362)","Tertiary hyperparathyroidism; rare ectopic PTH secretion (p.362-363)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis — can cause hypercalcaemia through granulomatous extrarenal 1,25(OH)2D production, mimicking a malignant cause (p.362-363)","Other granulomatous infections (leprosy, fungal infections) via the same macrophage CYP27B1 mechanism (p.362-363)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Primary/tertiary hyperparathyroidism — PTH-driven hypercalcaemia mimicking malignant hypercalcaemia (p.362-364)","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia — benign mimic where surgery is ineffective (p.363)","Sarcoidosis and other non-infectious granulomatous/foreign-body reactions (p.362-363)","Pseudohypercalcaemia (e.g. calcium-binding IgM in Waldenstrom's macroglobulinaemia, or spuriously high reading from altered albumin binding) (p.362-363)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Intact PTH by two-site assay — the key second test; suppressed in HHM, inappropriately normal/elevated in primary hyperparathyroidism (p.363-364)","PTHrP level — to confirm hypercalcaemia of malignancy when needed (p.364, 738)","Serum phosphate — low/hypophosphataemia with PTHrP and primary hyperparathyroidism; high-normal/elevated with vitamin D-mediated hypercalcaemia (p.363-364, 738)","Serum 25(OH)D and 1,25(OH)2D — 1,25(OH)2D raised/inappropriately normal in lymphoma and granulomatous causes (p.364)","Serum free light chains plus protein electrophoresis with immunofixation to evaluate for myeloma (p.364)","Albumin to calculate corrected calcium (p.363)","Urinary calcium / calcium-creatinine clearance ratio (<0.01 suggests FHH; >0.02 fractional excretion supports pHPT) (p.363)","Genetic testing (CASR, GNA11, AP2S1) if FHH suspected and urine studies inconclusive (p.363)","ECG — may show shortened QTc/ST segments, bradyarrhythmias, AV block, QRS widening, bundle branch block (p.364, 738)"],"redFlags":["Markedly elevated calcium (>14 mg/dL / >3.5 mmol/L) with progressive lethargy, stupor or coma (p.363-364, 737)","Severe GI involvement — nausea, vomiting, anorexia, peptic ulcer or pancreatitis (p.364)","Acute renal failure and volume depletion from hypercalcaemia-induced nephrogenic concentrating defect and polyuria (p.364)","Cardiac conduction abnormalities — bradycardia, AV block, QRS widening, short QTc (p.364)"],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Failing to correct calcium for albumin can produce pseudohypercalcaemia or pseudohypocalcaemia and a false diagnosis (p.363).","FHH may present with mildly elevated PTH and hypercalcaemia and be mistaken for primary hyperparathyroidism — parathyroid surgery is ineffective in FHH and must be excluded (p.363).","Mild hypercalcaemia (10.5-11.9 mg/dL) is often asymptomatic or causes only vague neuropsychiatric or constitutional symptoms, so it may be overlooked (p.363).","Ionised calcium measurement is easily distorted by collection technique (e.g. prolonged tourniquet); corrected total calcium is generally preferable (p.363).","Up to ~3% of myeloma is undetected by serum/urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation — check free light chains (p.364).","In immobilisation-related hypercalcaemia, an unrecognised underlying driver (pHPT, Paget's, myeloma) may coexist and should be sought, especially in older patients (p.363)."],"severityClassification":"By total serum calcium: mild 10.5-11.9 mg/dL (often asymptomatic); moderate >=12-14 mg/dL; severe >=14 mg/dL (progressive lethargy, stupor or coma, prominent GI symptoms and acute renal failure) (Harrison 22e p.363-364).","references":["Harrison 22e p.362","Harrison 22e p.363","Harrison 22e p.364","Harrison 22e p.737","Harrison 22e p.738"],"crossLinks":["myeloma","malignancy_b","sarcoidosis"]},"hyperkalemia":{"name":"Hyperkalaemia","system":"Metabolic / Nephrology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"401-404","clinicalPearls":["Hyperkalaemia (plasma K+ ≥5.5 mM) occurs in up to 10% of hospitalised patients; severe hyperkalaemia (>6.0 mM) in ~1% and carries significantly increased mortality (p.401).","Reduced renal K+ excretion is the single most common underlying cause; transcellular shift and reduced tissue uptake act mainly as acute contributors (p.401).","Excess dietary intake alone is a rare cause because the kidney can normally upregulate secretion — but small intake increments can provoke severe hyperkalaemia in susceptible patients (e.g. diabetics with hyporeninaemic hypoaldosteronism and CKD); occult sources include K+-containing salt substitutes (p.401-402).","The ECG changes of hyperkalaemia are notoriously INSENSITIVE, particularly in CKD/ESRD patients — a normal ECG does NOT exclude dangerous hyperkalaemia (p.403).","Hyperkalaemia per se impairs renal acid excretion (K+ competes with NH4+ for TALH reabsorption), so it can itself contribute to metabolic acidosis; restoring normokalaemia often corrects this hyperkalaemic acidosis (p.403).","Hyperkalaemia can produce a type I Brugada ECG pattern (pseudo-RBBB with coved ST elevation in precordial leads); the hyperkalaemic Brugada sign is distinguished from genetic Brugada syndrome by absent P waves, marked QRS widening and an abnormal QRS axis (p.403).","A urine Na+ concentration <20 mM signals that low distal Na+ delivery is limiting K+ excretion, suggesting volume repletion or a loop diuretic may help lower K+ (p.403)."],"pathophysiology":"Plasma K+ rises when efflux of intracellular K+ outpaces clearance or when renal excretion falls. Acidaemia drives K+ out of cells via effective K+-H+ exchange to buffer extracellular pH — but this shift is seen mainly with non-anion-gap (mineral) and respiratory acidoses; it does NOT occur with the organic anion-gap acidoses (lactic acidosis, ketoacidosis). Hyperosmolality (mannitol, hypertonic saline, IVIG, hypertonic glucose without insulin in diabetics) shifts K+ out by solvent drag. Cationic amino acids (lysine, arginine, epsilon-aminocaproic acid) and Na+/K+-ATPase inhibitors (digoxin and related cardiac glycosides from yellow oleander, foxglove, cane-toad bufadienolide; fluoride) cause efflux. Succinylcholine depolarises muscle via upregulated acetylcholine receptors (contraindicated after burns, neuromuscular injury, disuse atrophy, prolonged immobilisation). Impaired excretion arises from blockade of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis, reduced distal Na+/flow delivery, hyporeninaemic hypoaldosteronism, mineralocorticoid resistance, and reduced functioning nephron mass. Cardiotoxicity reflects altered cardiac membrane excitability: mild rises alter repolarisation (T-wave changes), higher levels depress intracardiac conduction (PR/QRS prolongation, P-wave loss, QRS widening, sine wave). (p.401-403)","additionalDifferentials":["Hyporeninaemic hypoaldosteronism — common in diabetics, the elderly and renal insufficiency; classically suppressed renin and aldosterone, ~50% with associated acidosis (reduced urinary NH4+, positive urinary anion gap, urine pH <5.5) (p.402)","Primary adrenal insufficiency / Addison's disease (autoimmune or polyglandular endocrinopathy) (p.401-402)","Adrenal infiltration (amyloidosis, metastatic cancer) or adrenal haemorrhage/infarction (e.g. antiphospholipid syndrome) (p.402)","Pseudohypoaldosteronism type I (ENaC or mineralocorticoid receptor defects) and type II / hereditary hypertension with hyperkalaemia (WNK1/WNK4, KLHL3, CUL3 mutations) (p.402)","Hereditary hyperkalaemic periodic paralysis (SCN4A mutations) — weakness with K+ intake or rest after exercise (p.403)","Tubulointerstitial disease affecting the distal nephron (amyloidosis, sickle cell, interstitial nephritis, obstructive uropathy) causing hyperkalaemia out of proportion to GFR (p.402)","Tumour lysis syndrome and rhabdomyolysis — release of intracellular K+ from tissue necrosis (p.402)","Massive red-cell transfusion (p.402)"],"infectionMimics":["Adrenal insufficiency from infectious causes (HIV — important cause, usually subclinical and may be unmasked by stress, ketoconazole or steroid withdrawal; cytomegalovirus; tuberculosis; disseminated fungal infection) presenting with hyperkalaemia and hyponatraemia (p.402)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pseudohyperkalaemia (factitious hyperkalaemia) — artefactual K+ release during/after venepuncture from fist clenching, marked thrombocytosis/leukocytosis/erythrocytosis with in vitro efflux, cold storage of the sample, or hereditary red-cell membrane transport defects (e.g. SLC4A1/AE1 mutations) (p.401-402)","Acute anxiety at venepuncture with respiratory alkalosis causing redistributive (true but transient) hyperkalaemia (p.402)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum electrolytes, BUN, creatinine, serum osmolality, Mg2+ and Ca2+, complete blood count (p.403)","Urinary pH, osmolality, creatinine and electrolytes (urine Na+ <20 mM indicates distal Na+ delivery limits K+ excretion) (p.403)","Transtubular K+ gradient (TTKG) as a bedside index of urinary K+ excretion — expected >7-8 in hyperkalaemia; values <8 suggest tubular resistance/low aldosterone (interpret cautiously) (p.403)","Plasma renin activity and aldosterone, with 9-alpha-fludrocortisone response, to characterise hypoaldosteronism (p.403)"],"redFlags":["Hyperkalaemia is a medical emergency because of its cardiac effects — arrhythmias include sinus bradycardia, sinus arrest, slow idioventricular rhythms, VT, VF and asystole (p.402-403).","ECG progression as a severity cue: tall peaked T waves (5.5-6.5 mM) -> loss of P waves (6.5-7.5 mM) -> widened QRS (7.0-8.0 mM) -> sine-wave pattern (>8.0 mM), heralding impending VF/asystole (p.403).","ECG manifestations mandate urgent treatment; but K+ ≥6.5 mM WITHOUT ECG changes should still be managed aggressively, because ECG findings are an unreliable predictor of cardiac toxicity (p.403-404).","Management principle (knowledge): a three-stage sequence — (1) immediately antagonise cardiac effects (IV calcium stabilises the myocardium by raising the action-potential threshold), (2) shift K+ into cells, (3) remove K+ from the body (p.403-404).","Secondary hyperkalaemic paralysis — ascending paralysis that can include diaphragmatic paralysis and respiratory failure (p.403)."],"prognosis":"Severe hyperkalaemia (>6.0 mM) is associated with a significantly increased risk of mortality. Cardiac toxicity can progress rapidly to fatal arrhythmia, but is reversible with prompt treatment; hyperkalaemic metabolic acidosis frequently corrects once normokalaemia is restored. (p.401, p.403)","pitfalls":["Treating a falsely high K+: failing to exclude pseudohyperkalaemia before intervening (haemolysed sample, fist clenching, extreme thrombo/leuko/erythrocytosis, cold storage) (p.401-402).","Relying on a normal/near-normal ECG to exclude dangerous hyperkalaemia — ECG changes are notoriously insensitive, especially in CKD/ESRD (p.403).","Forgetting drug and dietary causes: ACEi/ARB/renin inhibitors and mineralocorticoid-receptor blockers (spironolactone, eplerenone, drospirenone) — additive in combination; K+-sparing diuretics and the structurally similar antibiotics trimethoprim and pentamidine block ENaC; NSAIDs/COX-2 inhibitors, calcineurin inhibitors and beta-blockers cause hyporeninaemic hypoaldosteronism; heparin/LMWH inhibit aldosterone synthesis (p.402).","Drugs blocking the RAA axis also blunt the adrenal aldosterone response to a rising K+, compounding the effect (p.402).","Anion-gap acidoses (lactic acidosis, ketoacidosis) do NOT shift K+ out by the acidosis mechanism, unlike non-anion-gap and respiratory acidoses — so attributing hyperkalaemia to acidosis in these settings can mislead (p.402)."],"severityClassification":"By plasma K+: hyperkalaemia ≥5.5 mM; severe >6.0 mM (markedly increased mortality). ECG-correlated severity bands: peaked T waves 5.5-6.5 mM; loss of P waves 6.5-7.5 mM; widened QRS 7.0-8.0 mM; sine-wave pattern >8.0 mM. Emergency treatment is triggered by any ECG change or K+ ≥6.5 mM. (p.401, p.403-404)","references":["Harrison 22e p.401","Harrison 22e p.402","Harrison 22e p.403","Harrison 22e p.404"],"crossLinks":["aki","ckd"]},"hyperthyroidism":{"name":"Thyrotoxicosis (uncomplicated)","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chap. 396, p.3032-3038, 3041","clinicalPearls":["Thyrotoxicosis (state of thyroid hormone excess) is NOT synonymous with hyperthyroidism (excessive thyroid gland function); destructive thyroiditis and ingestion of exogenous hormone cause thyrotoxicosis without true hyperthyroidism (p.3032).","Graves' disease accounts for 60-80% of thyrotoxicosis; affects up to 2% of women, ~10x less common in men, typically ages 20-50 (p.3032).","In the elderly, features may be subtle or masked, presenting mainly with fatigue and weight loss ('apathetic thyrotoxicosis'), and may be mistaken for depression (p.3033).","Weight loss occurs despite increased appetite due to raised metabolic rate, but ~5% of patients GAIN weight due to increased food intake (p.3033).","Atrial fibrillation is more common in patients >50 years; treating the thyrotoxic state alone reverts AF to sinus rhythm in up to 75% of those without underlying cardiac disease (p.3033).","Symptoms in descending frequency: hyperactivity/irritability, heat intolerance/sweating, palpitations, fatigue/weakness, weight loss with increased appetite, diarrhoea, polyuria, oligomenorrhoea/loss of libido (p.3033 Table 396-2).","Signs: tachycardia (AF in elderly), fine tremor, goitre, warm moist skin, proximal myopathy, lid retraction/lag (p.3033 Table 396-2).","Fine tremor is best elicited by having the patient stretch out the fingers while feeling the fingertips with the palm (p.3033).","Lid retraction (staring appearance) results from sympathetic overactivity and occurs in ANY form of thyrotoxicosis; it is distinct from the specific eye signs of Graves' ophthalmopathy (p.3033).","Graves' ophthalmopathy specific signs (proptosis, periorbital oedema, conjunctival injection, diplopia) occur in ~one-third of Graves' patients clinically; ~10% of ophthalmopathy is unilateral (p.3033-3034).","Graves' ophthalmopathy can occur in the absence of hyperthyroidism in ~10% (euthyroid ophthalmopathy); onset is within a year before/after thyrotoxicosis diagnosis in 75% (p.3034).","A diffuse, firm (non-nodular) goitre with a thrill or bruit at the inferolateral margins points to Graves' disease (p.3033).","Thyrotoxic hypokalaemic periodic paralysis is a recognised association, particularly common in Asian males (p.3033).","Mild hypercalcaemia occurs in up to 20% of patients (hypercalciuria more common) from thyroid-hormone-driven bone resorption; long-standing disease causes osteopenia and a small increased fracture rate (p.3033)."],"pathophysiology":"Graves' hyperthyroidism is caused by thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSIs, detected as TSH-receptor antibodies/TRAb) that activate the TSH receptor. Susceptibility involves genetic (HLA-DR, CTLA-4, CD25, CD40, PTPN22, TSH-R gene) and environmental factors; concordance is 20-30% in monozygotic vs <5% in dizygotic twins. Triggers/risk factors include stress, smoking (moderate for Graves', major for ophthalmopathy), sudden iodine intake increases, the postpartum period (threefold rise), and immune reconstitution after HAART, alemtuzumab, or immune checkpoint inhibitors. Coexisting lymphocytic thyroiditis and TPO/Tg antibodies (up to 80%) mean there is no direct correlation between TSI level and hormone levels. Ophthalmopathy is driven by cytokine-mediated (IFN-gamma, TNF, IL-1) infiltration of extraocular muscles by activated T cells, fibroblast activation, glycosaminoglycan deposition trapping water, and aberrant IGF-1R signalling; the TSH-R is expressed in orbital tissue (p.3032-3033).","additionalDifferentials":["Graves' disease (60-80% of thyrotoxicosis) (p.3032)","Toxic multinodular goitre (p.3032)","Toxic adenoma / hyperfunctioning solitary nodule (p.3032)","Destructive thyroiditis: subacute (de Quervain) or silent/postpartum thyroiditis - transient thyrotoxic phase from preformed hormone release (p.3037)","Iodine excess (Jod-Basedow phenomenon); amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis in up to 10% (p.3032, 3034)","Thyrotoxicosis factitia (exogenous thyroid hormone ingestion) (p.3032, 3037)","Struma ovarii and functional metastatic follicular carcinoma (ectopic thyroid tissue) (p.3032, 3037)","TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma (secondary hyperthyroidism, inappropriately normal/high TSH) (p.3032, 3037)","Gestational thyrotoxicosis / hCG-secreting tumours (p.3032)","Activating TSH-R mutation; activating GSalpha mutation (McCune-Albright) (p.3032)","Subclinical hyperthyroidism (low TSH, normal free T3/T4) (p.3034, 3041)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Panic / anxiety attacks (p.3034)","Mania (p.3034)","Phaeochromocytoma (p.3034)","Malignancy-associated weight loss (p.3034)","Depression (especially apathetic thyrotoxicosis in the elderly) (p.3033)"],"additionalInvestigations":["TRAb (TSH-receptor antibodies) - confirms Graves' when goitre/ophthalmopathy absent; useful for monitoring pregnant Graves' patients (high TSI crosses placenta -> neonatal thyrotoxicosis) (p.3033-3034)","Radionuclide (99mTc, 123I or 131I) scan and uptake - diffuse high uptake in Graves' vs LOW uptake in destructive thyroiditis, iodine excess, and factitious thyrotoxicosis (p.3034)","Color-flow Doppler ultrasonography - increased flow in hyperthyroidism vs destructive thyroiditis; avoids radioactivity (p.3034)","Unbound (free) T3 - to detect T3 toxicosis (2-5% of patients have only elevated T3) (p.3034)","TPO and thyroglobulin (Tg) antibodies; serum Tg increased in all thyrotoxicosis EXCEPT factitia (low in factitia, high in thyroiditis) (p.3033-3034, 3037)","Pituitary MRI/CT and alpha-subunit if TSH inappropriately non-suppressed (suspect TSH-secreting adenoma) (p.3034, 3037)"],"redFlags":["Optic nerve compression at the orbital apex -> papilloedema, peripheral field defects and permanent vision loss; sight-threatening ophthalmopathy is an emergency (p.3034, 3036)","Corneal exposure/ulceration when lids fail to close during severe proptosis (p.3034)","Atrial fibrillation or worsening angina/heart failure, especially in elderly or pre-existing cardiac disease - high cardiac output can precipitate high-output failure (p.3033)","Features suggesting thyroid storm: fever, delirium, seizures, coma, vomiting, diarrhoea, jaundice (p.3036)"],"prognosis":"Untreated, clinical features generally worsen; historical mortality was 10-30% before effective therapy. Mild Graves' may show spontaneous relapses and remissions, and rarely fluctuates between hypo- and hyperthyroidism with changing TRAb activity. About 15% of treated patients in remission develop hypothyroidism 10-15 years later from the destructive autoimmune process. Ophthalmopathy runs an independent course: typically worsens over 3-6 months, plateaus over 12-18 months, then partially improves (soft-tissue changes especially); fulminant in up to 5% requiring acute intervention. Thyrotoxic crisis carries 4-17% mortality even with treatment (p.3034-3036).","pitfalls":["Do NOT assume thyrotoxicosis means hyperthyroidism - low radionuclide uptake indicates destructive thyroiditis, iodine excess or factitia rather than gland overactivity, which changes management (p.3032, 3037).","T3 toxicosis: 2-5% have suppressed TSH with elevated T3 but normal T4, so a normal T4 does not exclude thyrotoxicosis; conversely iodine-induced T4 toxicosis can show elevated T4 with normal T3 (p.3034).","Diagnostic confusion: thyrotoxicosis can elevate bilirubin, liver enzymes and ferritin, and cause microcytic anaemia and thrombocytopenia (p.3034).","Antithyroid drug agranulocytosis (<1%) is idiosyncratic and abrupt - routine prospective blood-count monitoring is NOT useful; instead warn patients to stop the drug and obtain an urgent FBC for sore throat, fever or mouth ulcers (p.3036).","Suppressed TSH may persist for several months after starting antithyroid drugs, so TSH is not a reliable early index of response - titrate on unbound T4; euthyroidism is usually not reached until 6-8 weeks (p.3035).","Thyroid acropachy/clubbing without coincident dermopathy and ophthalmopathy in a Graves' patient should prompt a search for an alternative cause of clubbing (p.3034).","A normal TSH with normal unbound T4/T3 excludes thyrotoxicosis and excludes Graves' as the cause of a diffuse goitre (p.3034)."],"severityClassification":"Subclinical hyperthyroidism = low TSH with normal free T3 and free T4; treatment is increasingly recommended in the elderly to reduce atrial fibrillation and bone-loss risk (p.3034, 3041). Graves' ophthalmopathy severity is graded by the NO SPECS mnemonic (0 no signs/symptoms; 1 only signs e.g. lid retraction/lag; 2 soft tissue involvement; 3 proptosis >22 mm; 4 extraocular muscle involvement/diplopia; 5 corneal involvement; 6 sight loss), though activity-based systems such as EUGOGO are preferred for monitoring/treatment decisions (p.3034).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3032","Harrison 22e p.3033","Harrison 22e p.3034","Harrison 22e p.3035","Harrison 22e p.3036","Harrison 22e p.3037","Harrison 22e p.3041"],"crossLinks":["thyroid_storm","atrial_fib"]},"hypoglycemia":{"name":"Hypoglycemia","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.204, p.635-636, p.721, p.782, p.1807","clinicalPearls":["Symptoms fall into two groups: autonomic/adrenergic features (tremor, palpitations, anxiety, sweating, hunger, paresthesias) driven by counterregulatory activation, and neuroglycopenic features (fatigue, weakness, dizziness, confusion, behavioral change, cognitive impairment) from impaired neuronal function (p.204).","Hypoglycemia can cause transient loss of consciousness and may mimic syncope or seizure; hunger as a premonitory symptom and absence of immediate reorientation help distinguish it (p.204).","Insulinoma may first be misattributed to psychiatric illness, with symptoms (confusion, irrational behavior, visual difficulty, coma) recognized as neuroglycopenic only in retrospect (p.721).","Distinguish endogenous insulin excess (insulinoma) from surreptitious exogenous insulin use: C-peptide and proinsulin are elevated with insulinoma but suppressed with exogenous insulin injection (p.721).","A non-islet-cell tumor cause is suggested by hypoglycemia with suppressed insulin and C-peptide plus an elevated IGF-II/IGF-I ratio (>10:1) and inappropriately low GH and beta-hydroxybutyrate (p.635-636, p.782)."],"pathophysiology":"Falling glucose triggers counterregulatory autonomic activation (catecholamines) producing adrenergic warning symptoms, while inadequate glucose supply to the brain produces neuroglycopenia. Endogenous hyperinsulinism (insulinoma) drives fasting hypoglycemia with elevated insulin, proinsulin, and C-peptide. Non-islet-cell tumors can cause hypoglycemia chiefly through secretion of incompletely processed IGF-II ('big' IGF-II), which weakly activates insulin receptors and more strongly IGF-I receptors; reduced IGFBP-3 and acid-labile subunit increase IGF-II bioavailability to target tissues, and large tumors may also impair hepatic gluconeogenesis or consume glucose. In severe malaria, hypoglycemia arises from failed hepatic gluconeogenesis combined with increased host (and to a lesser extent parasite) glucose consumption, and may be worsened by quinine-induced insulin secretion (p.204, p.635-636, p.721, p.782, p.1807).","additionalDifferentials":["Insulinoma (endogenous hyperinsulinism) — fasting hypoglycemia with elevated insulin, proinsulin, C-peptide (p.721)","Surreptitious/factitious insulin or sulfonylurea use (suppressed C-peptide with exogenous insulin; serum sulfonylurea assay if suspected) (p.721)","Non-islet-cell tumor hypoglycemia from IGF-II excess (mesenchymal tumors, hepatomas, adrenocortical and other large tumors) (p.635-636, p.782)","Severe liver disease, alcoholism, and poor nutrition causing fasting hypoglycemia (p.721)","Post-gastric-bypass postprandial hypoglycemia (p.721)"],"infectionMimics":["Severe falciparum malaria — hypoglycemia is a common complication and its neurologic effects are indistinguishable from cerebral malaria; usual adrenergic signs (sweating, gooseflesh, tachycardia) are often absent (p.1807)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Syncope and seizure — hypoglycemia-related loss of consciousness can be mistaken for either (p.204)","Psychiatric illness — neuroglycopenic behavioral symptoms of insulinoma can be misdiagnosed as psychiatric disease (p.721)","Pseudohypoglycemia — artifactually low glucose from in vitro glucose consumption by markedly elevated leukocytes (hyperleukocytosis in leukemia, myeloproliferative disease, leukemoid reaction, or after colony-stimulating factor) (p.635-636)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting insulin level (p.721)","Proinsulin level (p.721)","C-peptide level — helps separate endogenous from exogenous insulin (p.721)","Serum/plasma sulfonylurea assay when surreptitious agent use suspected (p.721)","IGF-II level and IGF-II/IGF-I ratio (ratio >10:1 suggestive of non-islet-cell tumor hypoglycemia; standard IGF-II assays may miss precursors) (p.782)","Endoscopic ultrasound for localization of insulinoma (typically small, <2 cm) (p.721)"],"redFlags":["Hypoglycemia complicating severe malaria carries a poor prognosis, especially in children and pregnant women, and the usual warning signs may be absent (p.1807)","Recurrent hypoglycemia blunts counterregulation, causing hypoglycemia unawareness — loss of the warning symptoms that normally prompt correction (p.204)","Coma or seizures from neuroglycopenia indicate severe, potentially irreversible CNS compromise (p.721, p.782)"],"prognosis":"Hypoglycemia is rapidly reversible with glucose, but in severe malaria it independently signals a poor prognosis, particularly in children and pregnant women (p.1807).","pitfalls":["Repeated hypoglycemia produces hypoglycemia unawareness by impairing the counterregulatory response, so the absence of adrenergic warning symptoms does not exclude dangerous hypoglycemia (p.204).","In severe malaria the clinical signs of hypoglycemia (sweating, gooseflesh, tachycardia) are typically absent and its neurologic effects mimic the underlying disease, so it is easily missed without glucose measurement (p.1807).","Failing to measure C-peptide/proinsulin can miss surreptitious exogenous insulin use, which mimics insulinoma (p.721).","Circulating IGF-II may not be overtly elevated in tumor-induced hypoglycemia, so a normal IGF-II level does not exclude it; the IGF-II/IGF-I ratio is more informative (p.635-636, p.782).","A markedly raised leukocyte count can cause artifactual low glucose (pseudohypoglycemia) if the sample is not processed promptly (p.635-636)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.204","Harrison 22e p.635-636","Harrison 22e p.721","Harrison 22e p.782","Harrison 22e p.1807"],"crossLinks":[]},"hyponatremia":{"name":"Symptomatic hyponatraemia / SIADH","system":"Metabolic","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"390-394","clinicalPearls":["Hyponatraemia (plasma Na+ <135 mM) is very common, affecting up to ~22% of hospitalised patients (p.390).","The absolute plasma Na+ concentration says nothing about a patient's volume status, which must be assessed separately for both diagnosis and management (p.390).","Hyponatraemia is almost always driven by excess circulating AVP and/or increased renal sensitivity to AVP combined with free-water intake; the main exception is hyponatraemia from low solute intake (p.390).","Diagnostically subdivide by ECF volume into hypovolaemic, euvolaemic, and hypervolaemic groups based on clinical history and volume status (p.390).","In hypovolaemic patients, a urine Na+ <20 mM predicts a brisk rise in plasma Na+ with isotonic saline, because AVP falls and a water diuresis ensues (p.391).","Serum uric acid helps discriminate: SIAD is typically associated with low uric acid (<4 mg/dL), whereas hypovolaemic hyponatraemia is often hyperuricaemic (p.391, p.393).","Thiazides can mimic SIAD and do not impair urinary concentrating ability, so AVP retains full effect; defer diagnosing SIAD until 1-2 weeks after stopping the thiazide (p.391, p.393).","The 48-hour mark distinguishes acute from chronic hyponatraemia and is decisive for how aggressively/cautiously sodium may be corrected (p.392).","Cerebral salt wasting (a rare hypovolaemic cause linked to intracranial disease) responds to aggressive salt-and-water repletion, so distinguishing it from SIAD is critical because their management diverges (p.391)."],"pathophysiology":"Hyponatraemia reflects a relative excess of body water to sodium, almost always from increased circulating AVP (or heightened renal AVP sensitivity) together with free-water intake. Volume status modulates AVP release: hypovolaemia raises AVP at any given osmolality, and 'hypervolaemic' arterial underfilling (heart failure, cirrhosis) triggers neurohumoral activation that likewise elevates AVP, causing water retention. In hypovolaemia, AVP-mediated V2-receptor water reabsorption plus free-water intake lowers Na+. In hypervolaemic oedematous states (CHF, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome), total-body Na+ rises but water rises proportionally more; reduced arterial filling drives the AVP response. In euvolaemic hyponatraemia, SIAD is the commonest cause: free-water intake persists at osmolalities below the normal thirst threshold, with patients subclinically volume-expanded (so-called 'AVP escape' limits distal tubular transport, preserving a modestly hypervolaemic steady state). Four AVP-secretion patterns occur in SIAD: erratic unregulated secretion (~1/3 of patients), failure to suppress at low osmolality, a 'reset osmostat', and a subset with undetectable AVP (gain-of-function V2-receptor mutations causing 'nephrogenic' SIAD). At the brain level, hyponatraemia causes water to move down the osmotic gradient from hypotonic ECF into cells, producing cellular swelling and cerebral oedema within the rigid skull; acute encephalopathy results when volume-regulatory mechanisms (CSF shunting, efflux of Na+/K+/Cl-) are overwhelmed. In chronic hyponatraemia, brain cells extrude organic osmolytes (creatine, betaine, glutamate, myoinositol, taurine), a process largely complete within 48 h, which mitigates but does not abolish symptoms (p.390-392).","additionalDifferentials":["Hypovolaemic: GI losses (vomiting, diarrhoea, tube drainage) and insensible loss (sweating, burns) with inadequate replacement — urine Na+ typically <20 mM (p.390).","Hypovolaemic renal: thiazide diuretics, mineralocorticoid/aldosterone deficiency (primary adrenal insufficiency, hypoaldosteronism), salt-losing nephropathies (reflux nephropathy, interstitial nephropathies, post-obstructive uropathy, medullary cystic disease, recovery phase of ATN) — urine Na+ typically >20 mM (p.390-391).","Osmotic/solute-driven hypovolaemic: glycosuria, ketonuria (starvation, diabetic or alcoholic ketoacidosis), bicarbonaturia (RTA, metabolic alkalosis) (p.391).","Cerebral salt wasting (subarachnoid haemorrhage, traumatic brain injury, craniotomy, encephalitis, meningitis) (p.391).","Hypervolaemic: congestive heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome, and acute or chronic renal failure (the latter uniquely with raised urine Na+) (p.391).","Euvolaemic: SIAD, moderate-to-severe hypothyroidism, secondary adrenal insufficiency from pituitary disease (glucocorticoid deficiency) (p.391).","Low solute intake: beer potomania (alcoholics whose sole nutrient is beer) and nutrient-restricted/extreme vegetarian diets — urine osmolality <100-200 mOsm/kg, urine Na+ <10-20 mM (p.391-392).","SIAD causes: malignancy (especially small-cell lung carcinoma — 75% of malignancy-associated SIAD; ~10% present with Na+ <130 mM), pulmonary disease (pneumonia, TB, abscess, pleural effusion), CNS disease (tumour, subarachnoid haemorrhage, meningitis, encephalitis, head trauma, hydrocephalus), drugs, and transient causes (pain, nausea, stress, endurance exercise, general anaesthesia) (p.391-392).","Pseudohyponatraemia — hyponatraemia coexisting with normal/increased plasma tonicity, from extreme hyperlipidaemia or hyperproteinaemia (p.393).","Hyperglycaemia-related ('true') hyponatraemia — plasma Na+ falls ~1.6-2.4 mM per 100 mg/dL rise in glucose; resolves after correcting hyperglycaemia (p.393)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pseudohyponatraemia from severe hyperlipidaemia/hyperproteinaemia: measured Na+ is low but plasma tonicity is normal or high; exclude by measuring serum osmolality (p.393).","Hyperglycaemic hyponatraemia: a translocational, true hyponatraemia driven by glucose-induced water efflux that corrects once glucose is lowered (p.393)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum osmolality, converted to effective osmolality/tonicity by subtracting urea (in mg/dL divided by 2.8) — true hyponatraemia has effective osmolality <275 mOsm/kg; this also excludes pseudohyponatraemia (p.393).","Serum glucose — to identify and correct hyperglycaemic (translocational) hyponatraemia (p.393).","Serum uric acid — low (<4 mg/dL) in SIAD physiology, often high in volume depletion (p.393).","Serum K+, BUN/creatinine — hyperkalaemia suggests adrenal insufficiency/hypoaldosteronism; raised urea/creatinine suggests renal cause (p.393).","Thyroid, adrenal and pituitary function testing, including a cosyntropin (ACTH) stimulation test to assess primary adrenal insufficiency, in the appropriate setting (p.393).","Urine osmolality — <100 mOsm/kg suggests primary polydipsia; >400 mOsm/kg indicates dominant AVP excess; intermediate values suggest multifactorial physiology (p.393).","Urine Na+ — <20-30 mM suggests hypovolaemia; >30 mM suggests SIAD, but values overlap, especially in the elderly (p.393).","Urine K+ — to calculate the urine-to-plasma electrolyte ratio, useful for predicting response to fluid restriction (p.393).","Demonstration that plasma Na+ corrects after isotonic saline hydration is the diagnostic gold standard for hypovolaemic hyponatraemia (p.393).","Imaging — chest CT in smokers/high-risk patients (screening CXR can miss small-cell lung carcinoma); CNS imaging where a central cause is suspected (p.393)."],"redFlags":["Acute symptomatic hyponatraemia is a medical emergency: nausea/headache/vomiting can rapidly progress to seizures, brainstem herniation, coma and death (p.392).","Acute hyponatraemia can cause normocapnoeic or hypercapnoeic respiratory failure, typically from non-cardiogenic 'neurogenic' pulmonary oedema with normal wedge pressure; resulting hypoxia amplifies neurological injury (p.392).","Premenopausal women are far more likely than men to develop encephalopathy and severe neurological sequelae (p.392).","Overly rapid correction of chronic hyponatraemia (>8-10 mM in 24 h or >18 mM in 48 h) risks osmotic demyelination syndrome (ODS), classically central pontine myelinolysis — presenting days later with para-/quadriparesis, dysphagia, dysarthria, diplopia, 'locked-in' syndrome, or reduced consciousness (p.393).","ODS can occur even with appropriately slow correction in high-risk patients: alcoholism, malnutrition, hypokalaemia, and liver transplantation (p.393).","Plasma Na+ <125 mM is the threshold at which chronic hyponatraemia commonly becomes overtly symptomatic (nausea, vomiting, confusion, seizures) (p.392).","Iatrogenic settings for acute hyponatraemia: postoperative premenopausal women given hypotonic fluids, glycine irrigation (TURP/uterine surgery), colonoscopy prep, recent thiazide initiation (Table 56-2, p.392).","MDMA ('Ecstasy'/'Molly') causes potent thirst and AVP induction leading to severe acute hyponatraemia; exercise-associated (endurance/marathon) hyponatraemia is another acute setting (p.392)."],"prognosis":"Acute symptomatic hyponatraemia carries high risk of severe neurological injury and death; encephalopathy is more severe in premenopausal women. The degree of hyponatraemia is an important prognostic indicator in hypervolaemic states, reflecting the intensity of neurohumoral activation. Chronic hyponatraemia, even when judged 'asymptomatic', is associated with subtle gait and cognitive deficits (reversible with correction), increased risk of falls, and an increased fracture risk due to neurological dysfunction and reduced bone density — so safe correction is warranted even without overt symptoms. Osmotic demyelination from overcorrection can produce permanent disabling neurological deficits (p.392-393).","pitfalls":["Hypovolaemic patients may appear clinically euvolaemic, with only a low urine Na+ to reveal the cause (p.390).","Hyponatraemia is frequently multifactorial, especially when severe — evaluate every source of excess AVP (volume status, drugs, nausea, pain) rather than settling on one (p.393).","Failing to exclude pseudohyponatraemia/hyperglycaemia before correcting; check serum osmolality and glucose first (p.393).","The 93%-water assumption used by ion-sensitive electrodes makes plasma Na+ falsely low in extreme hyperlipidaemia/hyperproteinaemia (p.393).","Urine Na+ values overlap substantially between SIAD and hypovolaemia (particularly in the elderly); the saline-challenge response is the definitive discriminator (p.393).","Diagnosing SIAD in a patient on thiazides — defer the SIAD label until 1-2 weeks after stopping the drug (p.393).","Distinguishing cerebral salt wasting from SIAD is critical because management differs (salt repletion vs fluid restriction) (p.391)."],"severityClassification":"By volume status: hypovolaemic (total body water down, total body Na+ down more), euvolaemic/no oedema (total body water up, Na+ near-normal), and hypervolaemic (total body water up markedly, Na+ up) — Fig. 56-5, p.391. By chronicity: acute hyponatraemia is <48 h (osmolyte extrusion incomplete, higher cerebral-oedema risk) and chronic is >48 h (osmolytes largely extruded, lower acute symptom risk but higher ODS risk on correction), p.392. By tonicity: true hypotonic hyponatraemia has effective osmolality <275 mOsm/kg, distinct from pseudohyponatraemia (normal/high tonicity) and hyperglycaemic translocational hyponatraemia, p.393. Symptomatic threshold for chronic disease is generally plasma Na+ <125 mM (p.392). SIAD diagnostic features include euvolaemic hypotonic hyponatraemia, inappropriately concentrated urine (urine osmolality typically >400 mOsm/kg / not maximally dilute), urine Na+ >30 mM, low serum uric acid (<4 mg/dL), and absence of thyroid/adrenal dysfunction, diuretics, hypovolaemia or hypervolaemia (p.391, p.393).","references":["Harrison 22e p.390","Harrison 22e p.391","Harrison 22e p.392","Harrison 22e p.393","Harrison 22e p.394"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS"]},"hypovolemic_shock":{"name":"Hypovolemic / haemorrhagic shock","system":"Critical care","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.314 (Approach to the Patient with Shock) pp.2309-2315; Ch.311 pp.2335-2337; Ch.106 (Anemia Due to Acute Blood Loss) pp.850-851","clinicalPearls":["Hypovolemic shock is haemodynamically a low-output state: reduced cardiac output and oxygen delivery driven by low preload, with a compensatory rise in systemic vascular resistance and low CVP and PCWP from depleted intravascular volume (p.2311).","Divide causes into haemorrhagic (external/trauma or internal, most often upper or lower GI bleeding) and non-haemorrhagic fluid loss (profuse vomiting/diarrhoea; renal losses such as osmotic diuresis in DKA or diabetes insipidus; skin losses from severe burns or Stevens-Johnson syndrome) (p.2311).","On examination it presents as a cold, low-output shock: cool/clammy peripheries, narrow pulse pressure, delayed capillary refill and a reduced JVP (<8 cm) - the heart is 'empty' - in contrast to the warm peripheries and wide pulse pressure of distributive shock (pp.2312-2313, p.2335).","Tachycardia and tachypnoea are early compensatory signs, but hypotension may be a late finding; younger patients in particular can compensate for an extended period before blood pressure falls (pp.2312-2313).","Signs of vascular instability appear once roughly 25% of blood volume is lost; the usual sympathetic compensation (tachycardia, vasoconstriction, regional flow shift) fails beyond ~30% loss, and loss of >40% (>2 L in an average adult) produces overt shock (confusion, dyspnoea, diaphoresis, hypotension, tachycardia) (pp.850-851).","Among hypovolemic causes, haemorrhage is the most common; non-haemorrhagic GI, renal and skin fluid losses are also recognised causes (p.2311)."],"pathophysiology":"Hypovolemic shock arises when a fall in intravascular volume reduces preload, lowering stroke volume and cardiac output and therefore systemic oxygen delivery; CVP and PCWP are low and systemic vascular resistance rises as a compensatory response (p.2311). Inadequate oxygen delivery forces cells into anaerobic metabolism, so pyruvate is converted to lactate with markedly less ATP generation; failure of ATP-dependent ion pumps disrupts ionic and pH homeostasis, and calcium influx activates phospholipases/proteases causing cell swelling and death, with leaked intracellular contents driving inflammation and microvascular injury (p.2310). In acute haemorrhage, hypovolaemia dominates first and threatens high-flow organs (brain, kidneys); the haemoglobin is initially normal and only falls over hours as fluid shifts from the extravascular to the intravascular space (haemodilution), so the haematocrit does not reflect the volume lost early on (pp.850-851).","additionalDifferentials":["Distributive shock (e.g. sepsis, anaphylaxis, neurogenic, adrenal crisis) - high cardiac output, low SVR, warm peripheries (pp.2310-2311)","Cardiogenic shock - low output but with elevated CVP/PCWP and raised JVP (p.2311, p.2313)","Obstructive shock (tension pneumothorax, cardiac tamponade, pulmonary embolism, aortic dissection) - low output with raised filling pressures/JVP (p.2311)","Mixed shock - more than one mechanism coexisting (e.g. hypovolemic plus septic) (p.2311)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cardiogenic shock - shares cool peripheries and narrow pulse pressure (low output) but is distinguished by raised JVP, peripheral oedema, an S3/S4 gallop and pulmonary crackles, indicating high filling pressures rather than volume depletion (p.2313, pp.2336-2337)","Obstructive shock - low-output state but typically with an elevated JVP; tamponade (pulsus paradoxus, raised JVP, low-voltage ECG with electrical alternans) and tension pneumothorax (absent breath sounds, tracheal deviation) mimic the hypoperfusion of hypovolemia (p.2313)","Distributive shock unmasked after fluid loss (e.g. adrenal insufficiency with aldosterone-deficient hypovolaemia) (pp.2310-2311)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial serum lactate for diagnosis, risk stratification and to gauge response to resuscitation - elevated lactate/lactic acidosis correlates with worse outcome (p.2314)","Haemoglobin/haematocrit - reduced in haemorrhagic loss, but may be normal early in active bleeding before haemodilution occurs (pp.2313, 850-851)","Coagulation profile (PT, PTT, INR) as part of the undifferentiated shock workup, relevant before/around transfusion (p.2313)","Arterial or venous blood gas to quantify the degree of metabolic acidosis (p.2313)","Urine electrolytes with FENa/FEUrea, which may indicate hypovolaemia or reduced effective circulating volume (p.2313)","Point-of-care ultrasound (RUSH/ACES/SESAME protocols): a collapsible IVC at end-expiration in a spontaneously breathing patient and focused views of abdomen/pelvis suggest reduced intravascular volume and can identify intra-abdominal haemorrhage (pp.2314, 2336-2337)","Dynamic fluid-responsiveness assessment - passive leg raise, or >12% stroke-volume/pulse-pressure variation in suitable mechanically ventilated patients - to predict response to volume (p.2315)","Pregnancy test (to detect ruptured ectopic pregnancy as a haemorrhagic source) (p.2313, p.2310)","Localising signs of occult internal bleed: Grey Turner sign (flank ecchymosis) and Cullen sign (umbilical ecchymosis) suggest retro-/intraperitoneal haemorrhage; rectal exam may reveal GI bleeding (pp.851, 2313)"],"redFlags":["Overt features of severe blood-volume loss (>40%): confusion, dyspnoea, diaphoresis, hypotension and tachycardia indicating critical vital-organ hypoperfusion needing immediate volume replacement (pp.850-851)","New altered mental status or new oliguria (<0.5 mL/kg/h) - signs the compensatory mechanisms have been overwhelmed (decompensated shock) (p.2312)","Occult bleeding sites - retroperitoneal haemorrhage, long-bone fracture haematoma, intra-abdominal/intrapleural bleeding - that may not be externally apparent (pp.851, 2315)","Elevated shock index (HR/SBP >0.9) flagging critical bleeding and likely transfusion need in haemorrhagic shock (p.2313)"],"prognosis":"Hypovolemic shock carries a lower mortality than septic or cardiogenic shock, which have the highest death rates among shock types (p.2311). Organ dysfunction in early (compensated) shock is often reversible if perfusion and oxygen delivery are restored, but untreated shock progresses through a decompensated phase to an irreversible phase with permanent multisystem organ dysfunction and death (pp.2311-2312).","pitfalls":["Compensated shock can present with a normal blood pressure: relying on an absolute SBP/MAP threshold misses early shock, since young patients may maintain pressure with tachycardia, narrow pulse pressure and only a mildly raised lactate before decompensating (pp.2312-2313).","Do not be falsely reassured by tachycardia without hypotension, nor exclude shock because the heart rate is normal - patients on nodal-blocking drugs or with conduction disease may have a blunted or absent tachycardic response (pp.2312-2313).","A normal initial haemoglobin/haematocrit does NOT exclude significant active haemorrhage; values fall only later as haemodilution develops, and the haematocrit does not reflect the true volume lost early on (pp.2313, 850-851).","Misclassifying low-output shock: an elevated JVP, peripheral oedema, gallop or crackles point to cardiogenic/obstructive rather than hypovolemic shock and warn against indiscriminate large-volume resuscitation (pp.2313, 2336-2337).","Over-resuscitation is harmful - targeted, reassessed fluid therapy is the goal rather than maximal volume (pp.2336-2337).","Estimates of external blood loss at a bloody scene are often overestimated, while large internal bleeds may be clinically inapparent - examine carefully for localising signs (p.851)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames severity along the shock continuum (compensated/pre-shock to decompensated to irreversible) (pp.2311-2312) and, for haemorrhage, by the fraction of blood volume lost rather than an ATLS Class I-IV table (not present in this source): vascular instability emerges at about 25% loss; compensation fails beyond ~30% (postural hypotension and tachycardia); and loss of >40% (>2 L in an average adult) produces overt hypovolemic shock with confusion, dyspnoea, diaphoresis, hypotension and tachycardia (pp.850-851). The shock index (HR/SBP; normal 0.5-0.7) is a bedside severity marker, with values >0.9 indicating critical bleeding and likely transfusion need (p.2313).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2309","Harrison 22e p.2310","Harrison 22e p.2311","Harrison 22e p.2312","Harrison 22e p.2313","Harrison 22e p.2314","Harrison 22e p.2315","Harrison 22e p.2335","Harrison 22e p.2336","Harrison 22e p.2337","Harrison 22e p.850","Harrison 22e p.851"],"crossLinks":["cardiogenic_shock","SEPTIC_SHOCK","variceal_bleed","aaa"]},"ibd_flare":{"name":"Inflammatory bowel disease flare","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.337 (Inflammatory Bowel Disease), p.2551-2564","clinicalPearls":["UC inflammation is continuous from the rectum proximally without skip areas, whereas Crohn's is segmental with skip lesions and frequently spares the rectum; this distribution difference is a key way to separate the two (p.2555-2556).","Granulomas (noncaseating, in all bowel-wall layers) are characteristic of Crohn's but are found less often on mucosal biopsy than on surgical resection specimens, so their absence does not exclude Crohn's (p.2556).","On biopsy, distorted/branching crypt architecture and basal plasmacytosis/lymphoid aggregates indicate chronicity and help distinguish IBD from acute self-limited or infectious colitis (p.2556).","Faecal calprotectin and lactoferrin correlate with histologic inflammation, help predict relapse, and can detect pouchitis; they are used to distinguish active inflammation from IBS or bacterial overgrowth (p.2556-2557).","Distinguishing UC from Crohn's is not possible initially in up to ~15% of cases (indeterminate colitis), though the true type usually declares itself later (p.2559).","Only ~15% of UC patients present initially with severe disease (p.2557).","Distal/proctitis-limited UC can paradoxically present with constipation due to slowed proximal transit despite distal inflammation (p.2557).","In severely ill UC patients serum albumin falls quickly; leukocytosis can occur but is not a specific marker of disease activity (p.2557)."],"pathophysiology":"IBD is a chronic idiopathic inflammatory disease of the GI tract regarded as a dysregulated, inappropriate immune response to the endogenous commensal microbiota (with or without an autoimmune component) in a genetically susceptible host. Homeostasis among the gut microbiota, intestinal epithelial cells, and mucosal immune system is normally maintained by regulatory pathways (e.g., FoxP3 T-regulatory cells); environmental factors (smoking, antibiotics, enteropathogens, diet) acting on genetic susceptibility (>240 GWAS loci, ~20% of risk explained; rare single-gene defects such as IL10/IL10R in very-early-onset disease) disrupt this balance and produce sustained inflammation. UC is a mucosal/superficial-submucosal disease, while Crohn's is transmural with fissuring and fistula/stricture formation (p.2551-2556).","additionalDifferentials":["Indeterminate colitis (UC vs Crohn's cannot be distinguished early in up to 15% of cases) (p.2559)","Diverticulitis / diverticular-associated colitis (mimics Crohn's clinically and radiographically) (p.2560)","Ischaemic colitis (consider in the elderly, hypercoagulable states, post-AAA repair; abrupt left lower quadrant pain with red rectal bleeding and a normal rectum with a sharp transition) (p.2560-2561)","Radiation colitis/enteritis (p.2561)","Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome (p.2561)","Collagenous and lymphocytic (microscopic) colitis - normal endoscopic appearance, chronic watery diarrhoea (p.2561)","NSAID-related colitis (de novo, or reactivation of IBD) (p.2561)","Immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI)-related colitis (p.2561)","Diversion colitis (p.2561)","Behcet's syndrome, graft-versus-host disease, eosinophilic gastroenteritis, neutropenic colitis (p.2560)","Neoplastic: lymphoma, colorectal carcinoma, carcinoid (p.2560)"],"infectionMimics":["Clostridioides difficile colitis (watery diarrhoea, tenesmus, nausea, vomiting) (p.2560)","Campylobacter colitis (can mimic severe UC endoscopically and can trigger a relapse of established UC) (p.2559)","Salmonella (watery or bloody diarrhoea) (p.2559)","Shigella (watery diarrhoea then tenesmus and bloody mucoid stool) (p.2559)","Yersinia enterocolitica (terminal ileum involvement mimicking Crohn's ileitis) (p.2559-2560)","Enterohaemorrhagic / enteroinvasive / enteroadherent E. coli (bloody diarrhoea) (p.2560)","Cytomegalovirus colitis (abdominal pain, bloody diarrhoea, fever, weight loss; can occur in immunocompetent hosts; severe disease can cause necrosis and perforation; diagnosed by intranuclear inclusions on biopsy) (p.2560)","Herpes simplex proctitis (anorectal/perianal; can occur in immunocompetent individuals) (p.2560)","Gastrointestinal tuberculosis / Mycobacterium avium (distal ileal and cecal disease mimicking Crohn's) (p.2560)","Entamoeba histolytica (amoebic colitis; fulminant form mortality >50%) (p.2560-2561)","Other parasites: Isospora belli, hookworm, Trichuris trichiura, Strongyloides (p.2561)","Gonorrhoea, Chlamydia trachomatis, syphilis (proctitis) (p.2560)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Diverticulitis (p.2560)","Ischaemic colitis (p.2560-2561)","Radiation colitis/enteritis (p.2561)","NSAID-induced colitis (p.2561)","Immune checkpoint inhibitor colitis (p.2561)","Collagenous / lymphocytic (microscopic) colitis (p.2561)","Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome (p.2561)","Diversion colitis (p.2561)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Acute-phase reactants - CRP, platelet count and ESR rise; haemoglobin falls in active disease (p.2556-2557)","Serum albumin (falls quickly in severe illness) (p.2557)","Faecal lactoferrin (sensitive/specific marker of intestinal inflammation) (p.2556)","Sigmoidoscopy to assess activity before treatment; full colonoscopy (deferred during an acute flare) to assess extent and activity, with multiple biopsies including from normal-appearing mucosa (p.2556-2557)","Histology of rectal/colonic biopsies (p.2557)","Intestinal ultrasound to assess activity and mucosal healing (p.2557)","Surveillance colonoscopy with biopsies (chromoendoscopy in higher-risk patients) for dysplasia/cancer in long-standing colitis (p.2570)"],"redFlags":["Toxic megacolon - transverse or right colon dilated >6 cm with loss of haustration during a severe attack; surgical emergency, can be precipitated by electrolyte abnormalities and narcotics (p.2557)","Perforation - the most dangerous local complication; signs of peritonitis may be masked by glucocorticoids; mortality of perforation complicating toxic megacolon is ~15% (p.2557)","Toxic colitis with deep ulceration - bowel may perforate without first dilating (p.2557)","Massive haemorrhage (occurs in ~1% of UC patients) (p.2557)","High-spiking fever in Crohn's suggesting intraabdominal abscess (p.2557)","Colonic stricture that prevents colonoscope passage - presume malignant until proven otherwise (indication for surgery) (p.2557)"],"prognosis":"Toxic megacolon occurs rarely; about 50% of acute dilations resolve with conservative management, but those that do not require urgent colectomy. Perforation complicating toxic megacolon carries ~15% mortality. Risk of colorectal cancer rises with duration and extent of colitis (historically ~2% at 10 yr, 8% at 20 yr, 18% at 30 yr, though rates have fallen with better inflammation control and surveillance; still ~1.5-2x the general population). Extensive long-standing colitis carries the highest cancer risk. Strictures occur in ~5% of UC patients. Up to one-third of IBD patients develop at least one extraintestinal manifestation (p.2557-2563, p.2570).","pitfalls":["Before escalating immunosuppression in a presumed flare, exclude superimposed infection - C. difficile and CMV colitis can mimic or complicate IBD; diagnosis requires negative stool studies (culture, C. difficile toxin, ova and parasites) and, for CMV, identification of intranuclear inclusions on mucosal biopsy (p.2557, p.2560).","A first 'flare' of bloody diarrhoea may actually be an infectious colitis (e.g., Campylobacter, Salmonella, Shigella, C. difficile, amoebic) - infection should be excluded before diagnosing IBD; Campylobacter and others can also reactivate established UC (p.2559-2560).","Granulomas are frequently absent on mucosal biopsy even in Crohn's, so their absence does not rule it out (p.2556).","Effective medical therapy can normalise mucosal appearance and create apparent skip areas in UC, mimicking Crohn's distribution; biopsy normal-appearing mucosa (p.2555-2556).","Extensive perianal lesions in a patient labelled UC should raise suspicion for Crohn's instead (p.2557).","In patients on glucocorticoids, peritoneal signs of perforation may be blunted/absent (p.2557).","An impassable colonic stricture should be presumed malignant rather than benign inflammatory (p.2557)."],"severityClassification":"Montreal classification of UC severity (Harrison Table 337-4, p.2557): S0 clinical remission (no symptoms); S1 mild = up to 4 stools/day +/- blood, no systemic illness, normal ESR; S2 moderate = 4 or more stools/day with minimal systemic toxicity; S3 severe = 6 or more bloody stools/day plus pulse >=90/min, temperature >=37.5 C, haemoglobin <10.5 g/dL, and ESR >=30 mm/h. (These severe-disease physiologic thresholds mirror the classic Truelove-Witts criteria for acute severe colitis.) Montreal extent: E1 proctitis, E2 left-sided (distal to splenic flexure), E3 extensive/pancolitis. Crohn's is categorised by the Vienna/Montreal classification (Table 337-5, p.2558) by age at diagnosis (A), location ileal/colonic/ileocolonic/upper (L), and behaviour nonstricturing-nonpenetrating/stricturing/penetrating (B) with a perianal modifier.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2551","Harrison 22e p.2552","Harrison 22e p.2553","Harrison 22e p.2555","Harrison 22e p.2556","Harrison 22e p.2557","Harrison 22e p.2558","Harrison 22e p.2559","Harrison 22e p.2560","Harrison 22e p.2561","Harrison 22e p.2562","Harrison 22e p.2563","Harrison 22e p.2564","Harrison 22e p.2570"],"crossLinks":["C_DIFF","DYSENTERY","GASTROENTERITIS"]},"ibs":{"name":"Irritable bowel syndrome","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"2571-2575","clinicalPearls":["Rome IV makes IBS a positive symptom-based diagnosis, not purely one of exclusion: recurrent abdominal pain on average at least 1 day/week over the prior 3 months plus at least two of being related to defecation, a change in stool frequency, or a change in stool form, with onset at least 6 months earlier.","Rome IV is stricter than Rome III: it requires pain at least weekly and dropped 'discomfort' as a qualifying symptom; painless diarrhoea or constipation alone does NOT meet criteria.","Abdominal pain is mandatory for the diagnosis; it is typically episodic and crampy, often worsened by eating or emotional stress and relieved by passing flatus or stool.","Subtype by predominant stool form: IBS-C (constipation), IBS-D (diarrhoea), and IBS-M (mixed) — but subtypes are unstable, with about three-quarters of patients changing subtype and ~29% switching between IBS-C and IBS-D over a year.","Onset is usually before age 45 and IBS is two to three times more common in women, who make up ~80% of severe cases.","Symptoms commonly overlap with other functional disorders (fibromyalgia, headache, backache, genitourinary symptoms) and with functional dyspepsia, suggesting a shared, more extensive digestive disorder.","Bloating and a subjective sense of excess gas are common, but most patients actually generate a normal volume of intestinal gas and instead have impaired gas transit/tolerance and proximal reflux of gas.","Malnutrition is exceedingly rare in IBS and the pain almost always occurs only during waking hours, so sleep disruption from pain is unusual."],"pathophysiology":"IBS is a multifactorial functional bowel disorder with no structural lesion. Proposed mechanisms include altered GI motor activity (e.g., exaggerated postprandial and distention-evoked colonic contractions, increased high-amplitude propagating contractions with rapid transit in IBS-D), visceral hypersensitivity (lower thresholds to rectal balloon distention with sparing of somatic pain pathways), and disordered brain-gut interaction with central neural dysregulation (greater activation of mid-cingulate and prefrontal cortices on functional imaging). Psychological factors (anxiety, depression, somatization, prior physical/sexual abuse) modulate pain thresholds, and genetic factors such as serotonin transporter (SERT) polymorphisms are implicated. Peripheral contributors include low-grade mucosal immune activation with mast cells, lymphocytes and proinflammatory cytokines (IL-6, IL-1beta, TNF), upregulated TRPV1 sensory channels, increased intestinal permeability ('leaky gut', chiefly in IBS-D and post-infectious IBS), gut dysbiosis (reduced microbial diversity, fewer Bifidobacterium/Faecalibacterium, more Enterobacteriaceae), abnormal serotonin pathways with increased enterochromaffin-cell serotonin in IBS-D, and bile acid malabsorption in up to ~25% of IBS-D.","additionalDifferentials":["Inflammatory bowel disease (ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease)","Coeliac sprue","Colorectal carcinoma","Microscopic colitis (lymphocytic/collagenous)","Lactase deficiency","Bile acid diarrhoea","Small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth","Diverticular disease of the colon","Gastroparesis or partial intestinal obstruction (postprandial pain with nausea/vomiting and bloating)","Hyperthyroidism and hypothyroidism","Hypoparathyroidism","Laxative abuse","Drug-induced constipation (anticholinergic, antihypertensive, antidepressant agents)","Acute intermittent porphyria and lead poisoning (painful constipation mimic)","Biliary tract disease, peptic ulcer disease, intestinal ischaemia, and gastric or pancreatic carcinoma (for epigastric/periumbilical pain)"],"infectionMimics":["Giardia lamblia or other intestinal parasitic infestation","Infectious diarrhoea","Small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Coeliac disease","Lactase deficiency","Microscopic colitis","Bile acid malabsorption","Inflammatory bowel disease","Hyperthyroidism / hypothyroidism"],"additionalInvestigations":["Complete blood count for most patients","Fecal calprotectin or fecal lactoferrin, and/or C-reactive protein, in IBS-D even without alarm features to help exclude IBD","Serologic testing for coeliac disease in IBS-D where population prevalence is >=1%","Stool examination for ova and parasites in IBS-D when parasitic infection is endemic or suggested by history (e.g., untreated water)","Colonoscopy when alarm features are present, symptom onset is after age 40, or symptoms fail to respond to standard therapy","Random colonic biopsies to exclude microscopic colitis if diarrhoea is persistent/chronic","Hydrogen breath test (or trial of a 3-week lactose-free diet) for suspected lactase deficiency","Glucose hydrogen breath test for suspected small-bowel bacterial overgrowth with excessive gas/bloating","Gallbladder ultrasonography for postprandial right-upper-quadrant pain","Upper GI radiographs or upper endoscopy when dyspeptic symptoms coexist"],"redFlags":["First appearance of symptoms in old age","Progressive course from the time of onset","Rectal bleeding","Persistent diarrhoea after a 48-hour fast","Nocturnal diarrhoea","Steatorrhoeal stools","Anaemia, elevated sedimentation rate, or leukocytes/blood in stool","Stool volume >200-300 mL/day","Family history of colorectal malignancy (lowers threshold for thorough evaluation)"],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Treating IBS as a diagnosis of exclusion only and over-investigating: few tests are needed when symptoms are typical and alarm features are absent, and unnecessary investigations may be costly and even harmful.","Missing organic disease in higher-risk patients: older age, rapidly progressive symptoms, or alarm features should prompt a thorough work-up to exclude IBD, coeliac disease and colorectal cancer rather than labelling as IBS.","Attributing bloating to excess gas when most patients produce a normal gas volume but have impaired gas transit.","Over-interpreting an abnormal hydrogen breath test, which can be positive simply from rapid small-bowel transit and does not reliably predict treatment response."],"severityClassification":"Harrison classifies IBS by predominant bowel pattern under Rome IV into IBS-C (predominant constipation), IBS-D (predominant diarrhoea), and IBS-M (mixed constipation and diarrhoea); these subtypes are noted to be unstable over time. Overall symptom severity ranges from mild to severe with significant quality-of-life impairment, but no formal graded severity score is specified.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2571","Harrison 22e p.2572","Harrison 22e p.2573","Harrison 22e p.2574","Harrison 22e p.2575"],"crossLinks":["ibd_flare","GASTROENTERITIS"]},"ich":{"name":"Intracerebral hemorrhage","system":"Neurology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.439, p.3452-3456","clinicalPearls":["ICH typically presents as abrupt-onset focal deficit; compared with ischaemic stroke, headache is more common, but imaging is still required to distinguish the two (p.3453).","Putaminal haemorrhage damages the adjacent internal capsule, so contralateral hemiparesis is the sentinel sign, often with the eyes deviated away from the side of the weakness (p.3454).","Thalamic haemorrhage produces a prominent all-modality contralateral sensory deficit and characteristic ocular signs from inferior extension into the upper midbrain (e.g. eyes deviated down-and-in 'looking at the nose', poorly reactive unequal pupils, vertical-gaze palsy) (p.3454).","Pontine haemorrhage causes rapid deep coma with quadriplegia, decerebrate rigidity and reactive 'pinpoint' (~1 mm) pupils (p.3454).","Cerebellar haemorrhage classically gives occipital headache, repeated vomiting and gait ataxia; in mild cases gait ataxia may be the only sign (p.3454).","Lobar haemorrhage symptoms mirror the lobe involved: occipital → hemianopia, left temporal → aphasia and confusion, parietal → hemisensory loss, frontal → arm weakness (p.3454).","Seizures are uncommon at presentation but occur in roughly 6-15% of patients within the first 3 days (p.3453).","Lumbar puncture is generally unnecessary and should usually be avoided in ICH because focal signs and raised ICP make it potentially herniation-inducing (p.3455).","ICH accounts for about 10% of all strokes, with disproportionately high incidence in Asian and Black populations (p.3453)."],"pathophysiology":"Hypertensive ICH usually results from spontaneous rupture of a small (~30-100 microns) penetrating artery deep in the brain, with chronic hypertension producing vascular injury preferentially at the basal ganglia (especially putamen), thalamus, cerebellum and pons. The clot may stay small or enlarge to compress adjacent tissue and cause herniation; dissection into the ventricles markedly increases morbidity and can produce hydrocephalus. Most hypertensive bleeds evolve over 30-90 minutes, whereas anticoagulant-related bleeds may keep expanding for 24-48 h. By ~48 h macrophages begin phagocytosing the haemorrhage at its surface, and over 1-6 months it resolves to a slit-like glial-scarred cavity with haemosiderin-laden macrophages. In cerebral amyloid angiopathy, arteriolar degeneration with amyloid deposition in cortical vessel walls causes single and recurrent lobar haemorrhages, the commonest cause of lobar bleeding in the elderly (p.3453-3454).","additionalDifferentials":["Hypertensive deep ICH (putamen, globus pallidus, thalamus, cerebellar hemisphere, pons) (p.3453)","Lobar ICH from cerebral amyloid angiopathy (degenerative, elderly, associated with dementia, rare <60 years) (p.3453)","Anticoagulant- or coagulopathy-related ICH (any site, often lobar or subdural; high risk of haematoma expansion) (p.3453-3455)","Sympathomimetic drug-related ICH (cocaine, amphetamine/methamphetamine) - a leading cause in patients under 45 (p.3453-3454)","Haemorrhage into a brain tumour (choriocarcinoma, melanoma, renal cell, thyroid, lung, hepatocellular metastases; glioblastoma, pilocytic astrocytoma, medulloblastoma) (p.3453,3455)","Vascular malformations: arteriovenous malformation, cavernous angioma, capillary telangiectasia, dural arteriovenous fistula (p.3453)","Ruptured intracranial aneurysm (mycotic or non-mycotic) with intraparenchymal extension (p.3453)","Haemorrhagic transformation of a prior ischaemic infarct (p.3453)","Venous/dural sinus thrombosis causing venous infarction with haemorrhage (e.g. vein of Labbe occlusion → posterior temporal/inferior parietal haematoma) (p.3453,3455)","Vasculitis (e.g. polyarteritis nodosa, lupus) and haematologic disorders (leukaemia, aplastic anaemia, thrombocytopenic purpura) (p.3455)","Hypertensive encephalopathy with haemorrhage; primary intraventricular haemorrhage; Moyamoya disease (p.3455)"],"infectionMimics":["Mycotic aneurysm rupture (p.3453)","Sepsis-associated petechial white-matter haemorrhages (p.3455)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Ischaemic stroke (clinically overlapping; differentiated by CT) (p.3453)","Traumatic intracranial haemorrhage (intraparenchymal temporal/inferior frontal, subarachnoid, subdural, epidural) - consider in any unexplained acute deficit, especially after a fall (p.3455)","Hypertensive encephalopathy / posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome - headache, vomiting, seizures, confusion with posterior-predominant brain oedema; favoured over hypertensive ICH when there is a prodrome and altered mental status before any bleed (p.3455)","Brain tumour presenting with haemorrhage (p.3455)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Non-contrast CT head - highly sensitive/specific for acute intracranial blood and defines location; preferred for acute stroke evaluation (p.3453,3455)","Platelet count, prothrombin time, partial thromboplastin time and INR to detect coagulopathy (p.3455)","CT angiography or post-contrast CT to seek a 'spot sign' (focal enhancement within the haematoma indicating ongoing bleeding) (p.3455)","MRA, CTA or conventional catheter angiography when the cause is uncertain - particularly in young or non-hypertensive patients or atypical (non-deep) haematoma location (p.3455)","Hemosiderin-sensitive MRI (susceptibility-weighted imaging) to detect cortical microbleeds suggesting cerebral amyloid angiopathy (p.3454)","MRI for posterior fossa lesions (more sensitive than CT but not usually needed for primary diagnosis); amyloid-beta PET can image CAA non-invasively (p.3454-3455)"],"redFlags":["'Spot sign' on CTA/post-contrast CT - associated with increased haematoma expansion, higher mortality and lower likelihood of favourable outcome (p.3455)","Haematoma expansion: about a third of patients (even without coagulopathy) have significant expansion within the first day; anticoagulant-related bleeds may keep enlarging for 24-48 h (p.3454)","Progressive decline in consciousness with headache and vomiting signalling rising ICP; deterioration may continue over 24-96 h from perihaematoma oedema (p.3453-3454)","Cerebellar haemorrhage with depressed consciousness or obstructive hydrocephalus, or radiographic hydrocephalus - high risk of rapid brainstem-compression deterioration requiring immediate neurosurgical evaluation (p.3454)","Signs of herniation (stupor/coma, dilated fixed pupil, decerebrate rigidity) (p.3453-3454)","Intraventricular extension of blood - substantially increases morbidity and may cause hydrocephalus (p.3454)"],"prognosis":"Overall, about 35-45% of patients die within the first month, and roughly 40% with a hypertensive ICH die, though survivors can achieve good to complete recovery because peri-haematoma tissue is displaced rather than infarcted and often regains function as the clot resorbs over months. The validated ICH Score stratifies mortality and outcome but should not be used to precisely prognosticate an individual, since withholding early aggressive care risks a self-fulfilling prophecy of poor outcome. Deep pontine haemorrhage with coma usually ends in death or a locked-in state, but small bleeds are compatible with survival; in cerebellar haemorrhage, full recovery is common if the deep cerebellar nuclei are spared (p.3453-3456).","pitfalls":["Cerebellar haemorrhage can deteriorate precipitously from brainstem compression or obstructive hydrocephalus; immediate surgical evacuation before severe brainstem compression may be lifesaving, and hydrocephalus from a cerebellar haematoma should generally be managed by definitive evacuation rather than ventricular drainage alone (p.3454).","Haemorrhage outside the typical deep hypertensive sites, or in a non-hypertensive patient, should prompt a search for other causes (amyloid angiopathy, tumour, vascular malformation, vasculitis, coagulopathy) rather than being assumed hypertensive (p.3454).","Conflating hypertensive encephalopathy/PRES with hypertensive ICH leads to inappropriately aggressive BP lowering - hypertensive encephalopathy warrants less aggressive reduction (p.3455).","ICP is often normal even with large ICHs, so it cannot be assumed; conversely induced hyperventilation can cause ischaemia and should be limited to acute resuscitation only (p.3456).","Glucocorticoids are not recommended for treating ICH (p.3456).","In ICH on antiplatelet drugs without thrombocytopenia, platelet transfusion showed no benefit and possible harm (p.3456).","Despite the presence of haemorrhage, venous sinus thrombosis is an indication for anticoagulation to relieve venous hypertension (p.3455)."],"severityClassification":"ICH Score (Hemphill et al., Stroke 2001) - validated grading scale for mortality/outcome stratification, summing five factors: age >=80 (1 pt), haematoma volume >=30 cc (1 pt), intraventricular haemorrhage present (1 pt), infratentorial origin (1 pt), and Glasgow Coma Scale 13-15 (0), 5-12 (1) or 3-4 (2); total 0-6 (p.3455-3456).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3452","Harrison 22e p.3453","Harrison 22e p.3454","Harrison 22e p.3455","Harrison 22e p.3456"],"crossLinks":["ischemic_stroke","sah","htn_emergency"]},"iih":{"name":"Idiopathic intracranial hypertension","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.117 (Chap. 17, Headache), p.228-229 (Chap. 34, Disorders of the Eye), p.235 (Chap. 34, abducens palsy), p.3379 (Chap. 433, drug-induced).","clinicalPearls":["Headache is typically generalized, present on awakening, eases through the day, and worsens on lying down (p.117).","Transient visual obscurations are a classic feature: brief (usually seconds), in one or both eyes, often triggered by a postural shift, and most frequent when headache is at its worst (p.117, p.228-229).","Obscurations that are prolonged or spontaneous indicate more threatening papilloedema (p.229).","The syndrome can occur without visual symptoms, especially when the fundi look normal, so it must be considered even when funduscopy is unremarkable (p.117).","Persistently raised intracranial pressure can itself trigger a chronic migraine syndrome (p.117).","Visual acuity is usually preserved in papilloedema unless it is severe, longstanding, or complicated by macular oedema and haemorrhage; field loss precedes acuity loss (p.229).","Unilateral or bilateral abducens (6th nerve) palsy is a classic false-localising sign of raised intracranial pressure and is corroborated by papilloedema on fundoscopy (p.235)."],"pathophysiology":"Raised CSF pressure without a space-occupying lesion, occurring in the setting of obesity, female sex, and occasionally pregnancy; partial obstruction of the cerebral venous sinuses is found in a small number of cases (p.117). Stenosis at the transverse-sigmoid sinus junction is commonly present, providing the rationale for venous sinus stenting (p.229). Vitamin A excess is a recognised cause of pseudotumor cerebri (p.3379).","additionalDifferentials":["Optic disc drusen, which produce pseudopapilloedema and can be mistaken for true papilloedema (p.229).","Obstructive sleep apnoea and poorly controlled hypertension, which also cause morning or nocturnal headache (p.117)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Space-occupying intracranial lesion causing raised CSF pressure headache (p.117).","Dural venous sinus thrombosis (p.229).","Arteriovenous (dural) shunt (p.229)."],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI as the initial study, including MR venography, to look for a mass and to search for dural venous sinus thrombosis or an AV shunt (p.117, p.229).","Lumbar puncture performed while the patient is symptomatic, measuring opening pressure in the lateral decubitus position; both the pressure and the symptomatic response to removing 20-30 mL of CSF can be assessed (p.117, p.229).","Formal visual field testing, performed even when there is no overt ophthalmic involvement (p.117).","B-mode ocular ultrasound to detect buried optic disc drusen (hyperechoic due to calcium) when pseudopapilloedema is suspected (p.229)."],"redFlags":["Prolonged or spontaneous transient visual obscurations signal more threatening papilloedema (p.229).","Insidious peripheral visual field loss with progressive optic atrophy under unremitting papilloedema; in this setting a reduction in disc swelling is an ominous sign of a dying nerve, not resolution (p.229).","Fulminant papilloedema can cause rapid-onset blindness and constitutes an emergency (p.229)."],"prognosis":"Headache often improves within weeks of starting medical therapy (p.117). However, unremitting papilloedema leads to progressive peripheral field loss and optic atrophy, and fulminant cases can cause rapid blindness, so visual loss is the feared outcome that drives urgent intervention (p.229).","pitfalls":["Inaccurate CSF opening-pressure readings at lumbar puncture are a common pitfall (p.229).","Failing to exclude a dural venous sinus thrombosis before attributing raised pressure to IIH (p.229).","Mistaking buried optic disc drusen (pseudopapilloedema) for true papilloedema, prompting an unnecessary work-up (p.229).","Assuming a normal fundus excludes the diagnosis; IIH can occur without funduscopic changes, so formal perimetry is still warranted (p.117)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.117","Harrison 22e p.228-229","Harrison 22e p.235","Harrison 22e p.3379"],"crossLinks":["migraine","tension_ha","brain_tumour"]},"ild":{"name":"Interstitial lung disease","system":"Pulmonary","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Ch.304 Interstitial Lung Disease (book pp.2259-2266) and Ch.299 Hypersensitivity Pneumonitis (book pp.2228-2231); supporting material from Ch.295-297 (pp.2199-2213)","clinicalPearls":["Diffuse parenchymal lung disease covers >200 heterogeneous conditions; the interstitial space between epithelium and endothelium is the dominant site, but alveolar epithelium and endothelial (airway and vessel) cells also drive pathogenesis (p.2259).","No single piece of data is diagnostic on its own; a confident ILD diagnosis requires the combined weight of clinical, laboratory, PFT, imaging and (when obtained) histopathologic findings, ideally reviewed in a multidisciplinary conference of pulmonologists, rheumatologists, radiologists and pathologists (pp.2259-2260).","Fine end-inspiratory bibasal crackles described as sounding like Velcro being pulled apart are characteristic of fibrotic ILD such as IPF and may be one of the earliest signs (pp.2200, 2261).","Egophony, whispered pectoriloquy and bronchial breath sounds suggest alveolar filling (e.g. pneumonia) rather than interstitial fibrosis (IPF); their absence helps distinguish fibrotic crackles from consolidation (p.2200).","Age strongly shifts pretest probability: IPF is most common in patients over 60 and rare under 50, whereas sarcoidosis, CTD-ILD, LAM and pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis typically present between ages 20 and 40 (p.2260).","On HRCT, apical-predominant interstitial thickening favours NSIP, whereas a basal and subpleural/dependent predominance favours IPF (p.2211).","A dry cough can be the most prominent symptom of IPF; some patients (even with extensive disease) report no dyspnoea at all (p.2261).","HRCT is now standard of care in initial ILD evaluation and can be diagnostic for IPF in the right clinical context, sparing the patient a lung biopsy (p.2262).","BAL findings can point to specific entities: eosinophilia >25% suggests chronic eosinophilic pneumonia, fat globules in macrophages suggest lipoid pneumonia, and serial bloody lavage indicates diffuse alveolar haemorrhage (p.2262).","A close relative with an idiopathic interstitial pneumonia is among the strongest risk factors for IPF; up to ~20% of pulmonary fibrosis is familial, and diverse IIP subtypes (IPF, NSIP, DIP) can run in the same family (p.2261)."],"pathophysiology":"ILDs are characterised by remodelling of the interstitium with variable degrees of inflammation and fibrosis, but alveolar epithelial and endothelial cell injury is also central to pathogenesis. The idiopathic interstitial pneumonias (IIPs) are the most common group. In IPF the histologic hallmark is a usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) pattern: subpleural reticulation with honeycomb change and fibroblast foci (subepithelial collections of myofibroblasts and collagen) alternating with preserved normal alveolar architecture, producing temporal and spatial heterogeneity. NSIP shows more uniform interstitial inflammation and fibrosis with honeycombing usually absent and fibroblast foci rare. Genetic studies show that a substantial fraction of familial and even sporadic/idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis has a genetic basis: a MUC5B promoter variant (present in ~20% of Caucasians, 35-45% of IPF patients) confers an approximately sixfold risk, and variants in telomere-length genes (TERT, TERC, RTEL1) and surfactant protein genes (SFTPA2) are also implicated. Hypersensitivity pneumonitis is an immune-mediated response to inhaled antigens small enough to reach distal airways and alveoli, driven by dysregulated TH1/TH17 responses with precipitating IgG and innate (Toll-like receptor/MyD88) signalling; chronic HP shares the MUC5B variant with IPF (pp.2259-2266; 2228-2229).","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (UIP pattern; basal/subpleural reticulation, honeycombing, traction bronchiectasis) (p.2262)","Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP) - cellular or fibrotic; often associated with CTD (p.2263)","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (nonfibrotic or fibrotic; exposure-related granulomatous ILD) (pp.2228, 2304)","Connective tissue disease-associated ILD - systemic sclerosis, rheumatoid arthritis, polymyositis/dermatomyositis, Sjogren's, SLE (pp.2265-2266)","Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (COP) - subacute flulike illness, migratory consolidations (p.2264)","Acute interstitial pneumonia (Hamman-Rich syndrome) - acute respiratory distress with diffuse alveolar damage (p.2265)","Smoking-related ILD - respiratory bronchiolitis-ILD (RB-ILD) and desquamative interstitial pneumonia (DIP) (pp.2263-2264)","Sarcoidosis (granulomatous; hilar/mediastinal lymphadenopathy, peribronchovascular nodularity) (p.2260)","Drug-induced ILD - amiodarone, nitrofurantoin, methotrexate, bleomycin, rituximab, anti-TNF agents (p.2261)","Occupational/pneumoconiosis - asbestosis, silicosis (pp.2260-2261)","Lymphangioleiomyomatosis (young women, diffuse cysts, spontaneous pneumothorax) (p.2260)","Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis and pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis (p.2304/Fig.304-1)","Granulomatous vasculitides - granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA) and eosinophilic GPA/Churg-Strauss, which can cause diffuse alveolar haemorrhage (p.2266)"],"infectionMimics":["Pneumocystis pneumonia and other diffuse infections, which should be excluded in the initial diffuse-parenchymal workup (p.2260)","Community-acquired pneumonia - COP is commonly mistaken for pneumonia, and nonfibrotic HP can be misread as a respiratory infection (pp.2229, 2264)","Secondary pulmonary infection in patients with traction bronchiectasis or on immunosuppression, which can produce cough with haemoptysis (p.2261)","Mycobacterial exposure (e.g. Mycobacterium avium complex in hot tub lung) causing HP rather than frank infection (p.2229)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Congestive heart failure / hydrostatic pulmonary oedema - basilar crackles and interstitial thickening on imaging overlap with ILD; must be excluded (pp.2211, 2262)","Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) - shares diffuse alveolar damage histology with AIP and acute exacerbation of fibrosis (p.2265)","Bronchoalveolar (lepidic) cell carcinoma and lymphangitic carcinomatosis (p.2260)","Organic toxic dust syndrome (OTDS), which mimics HP but needs no prior sensitisation and lacks specific IgG antibodies (p.2230)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Multidisciplinary (MDT) ILD review jointly integrating clinical, radiologic, serologic and pathologic data (pp.2259-2260)","Full PFTs with lung volumes plus DLCO; restriction = reduced TLC with symmetric FEV1/FVC reduction, and a low DLCO may precede volume reduction (less specific, more variable) (pp.2200-2201, 2261)","Serial/repeated FVC measurement - decline correlates with higher mortality and tracks disease progression (p.2261)","CTD/autoantibody serology including anti-CCP, anti-synthetase (e.g. anti-Jo-1, anti-PL-12) antibodies - may reveal an underlying CTD even without other features (pp.2261, 2266)","HRCT chest to define UIP vs NSIP vs COP patterns, extent, honeycombing, and guide biopsy site (p.2262)","Bronchoscopy with BAL - lymphocytosis (with CD4:CD8 <1) characteristic of HP; differential cell counts for eosinophilic pneumonia and DAH (pp.2230, 2262)","Transbronchial biopsy or cryobiopsy; cryobiopsy gives larger yields than forceps but with higher bleeding/pneumothorax risk; VATS surgical biopsy when needed (higher pre-treatment yield, up to ~5% short-term mortality) (pp.2209, 2262)","Serum precipitins / specific IgG for HP - supportive but not sufficient (asymptomatic exposed individuals can be positive; antigen panels often falsely negative) (pp.2229-2230)","Detailed occupational, environmental, avocational, medication and family exposure history (pp.2200, 2261)","Echocardiography to assess for pulmonary hypertension and right heart strain (p.2201)","Genetic evaluation in familial pulmonary fibrosis (MUC5B promoter variant, telomere genes TERT/TERC/RTEL1, surfactant genes) (pp.2261, 2267)"],"redFlags":["Acute exacerbation of IPF - acute (<30 days) onset of respiratory distress and hypoxaemia in a patient with underlying fibrosis, not explained by infection or heart failure; reported mortality >85% (p.2265)","Acute interstitial pneumonia (Hamman-Rich) - acute respiratory distress and hypoxaemia with >50% mortality within 6 months (p.2265)","Haemoptysis suggesting diffuse alveolar haemorrhage (Goodpasture's, GPA) or LAM - warrants urgent evaluation (p.2261)","Honeycombing or extensive fibrosis on HRCT indicating advanced disease and worse prognosis (p.2262)","Signs of advanced disease: cyanosis, digital clubbing and cor pulmonale (p.2262)","Rapidly progressive ILD in anti-Jo-1-positive inflammatory myopathy (p.2266)","A declining FVC on serial testing, which signals elevated mortality risk (p.2261)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies widely by ILD subtype and disease extent. IPF carries a poor prognosis (~50% survival at 3-5 years; pre-antifibrotic-era life expectancy 3-5 years from diagnosis). Idiopathic NSIP is comparatively favourable (>80% 5-year survival), with cellular NSIP doing better than fibrosing NSIP. Reported subtype mortality includes RB-ILD ~25% at 7 years and systemic sclerosis-associated ILD ~20-30% at 10 years; sarcoidosis mortality is generally low but state-dependent. RB-ILD usually has prolonged survival with death from progressive ILD rare. AIP has >50% 6-month mortality with frequent recurrences, though survivors can recover substantial lung function. Acute exacerbations of fibrotic ILD carry very high mortality (>85%). In CTD-ILD, a UIP pattern and more extensive fibrosis (e.g. in RA) predict reduced survival. Chronic fibrotic HP can mirror IPF in radiology and prognosis (pp.2260, 2262-2266; 2229).","pitfalls":["Attributing all wheezing to asthma - wheeze can reflect heart failure or other airway narrowing; conversely wheeze is uncommon in ILD but does occur in sarcoidosis, HP and eosinophilic GPA (pp.2200, 2262).","Forgetting that a UIP pattern on biopsy is not unique to IPF - it also occurs in CTD (e.g. RA-associated) ILD (p.2260).","Misreading acute ILD presentations as pneumonia, COPD exacerbation or heart failure, since acute ILD is unusual (p.2260).","Treating presumed IPF with immunosuppression - this has been shown to increase morbidity and mortality in IPF (p.2263).","Relying on PFT pattern to diagnose HP - HP can be restrictive or obstructive, so the pattern is not diagnostic (p.2229).","Over-relying on HP serum precipitin panels - they cover a limited antigen set and frequently give false negatives, and a positive IgG alone does not confirm HP (p.2230).","Confusing HP granulomas with sarcoidosis - HP granulomas are loose/poorly formed and peribronchiolar, whereas sarcoid granulomas are well-formed; caseating granulomas should prompt a search for infection (pp.2230, 2264).","Assuming CTD must be apparent - ILD can precede other CTD features by years or be the sole manifestation (pp.2261, 2265).","Diagnosing IPF without excluding a treatable mimic - heart failure, infection (e.g. PCP) and malignancy must be considered first (p.2260)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames ILD pattern recognition rather than a single numeric severity score. ILDs are first split into known cause (occupational e.g. asbestosis/silicosis; drug-induced e.g. amiodarone, nitrofurantoin, methotrexate, bleomycin; systemic/CTD-associated; granulomatous including HP) versus unknown cause (idiopathic interstitial pneumonias, plus rarer entities such as LAM, PAP, PLCH, sarcoidosis). The idiopathic interstitial pneumonias are further classified (per the ATS/ERS consensus) into chronic fibrosing (IPF, idiopathic NSIP), smoking-related (RB-ILD, DIP), and acute/subacute (COP, acute interstitial pneumonia, lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia). The UIP pattern (subpleural, basal reticulation with honeycombing and traction bronchiectasis) defines IPF, while NSIP shows more ground-glass and rare honeycombing. HP is now classified as nonfibrotic versus fibrotic (replacing the older acute/subacute/chronic terminology). Disease severity is gauged by extent of fibrosis/honeycombing on HRCT, degree of PFT (FVC/DLCO) impairment and its rate of decline, and presence of pulmonary hypertension/cor pulmonale (pp.2259-2266; 2229).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2200","Harrison 22e p.2201","Harrison 22e p.2211","Harrison 22e p.2228","Harrison 22e p.2229","Harrison 22e p.2230","Harrison 22e p.2259","Harrison 22e p.2260","Harrison 22e p.2261","Harrison 22e p.2262","Harrison 22e p.2263","Harrison 22e p.2264","Harrison 22e p.2265","Harrison 22e p.2266","Harrison 22e p.2267"],"crossLinks":["sarcoidosis","rheumatoid","vasculitis","lung_cancer","heart_failure"]},"ischemic_stroke":{"name":"Acute ischemic stroke","system":"Neurology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3427-3450 (Ch 437 Introduction to Cerebrovascular Diseases; Ch 438 Ischemic Stroke)","clinicalPearls":["Stroke is a clinical diagnosis: abrupt-onset focal neurologic deficit from a vascular cause; imaging is used to support it and to separate ischemia from hemorrhage (p.3427).","Time-is-brain: with complete arterial occlusion, neurons die within roughly 4-10 minutes; flow <16-18 mL/100 g/min infarcts within an hour, while 18-20 mL/100 g/min causes reversible ischemia unless prolonged (p.3439).","The ischemic penumbra is reversibly dysfunctional tissue around the infarct core; it is the target of reperfusion and can be visualized as a perfusion-diffusion (or CT-perfusion) mismatch (p.3439).","A useful triage rule of thumb is that a deficit maximal at onset (or one that remits) favors ischemia, whereas a depressed conscious level, very high initial blood pressure, or progressive worsening favors hemorrhage - though no clinical feature reliably separates them, so CT is required (p.3440).","The public FAST acronym (Facial weakness, Arm weakness, Speech difficulty, Time) captures common stroke features and emphasizes time-sensitivity; about 85% of ischemic stroke patients have hemiparesis (p.3428).","Stroke onset is timed from when the patient was last seen well, not when found; wake-up strokes default to bedtime, though advanced imaging may still allow treatment selection (p.3441).","Cerebellar infarction can masquerade as labyrinthitis with vertigo and vomiting; accompanying head/neck pain should prompt suspicion of vertebral dissection, and even small cerebellar edema can obstruct CSF and compress the brainstem (p.3440).","Roughly 30% of ischemic strokes remain cryptogenic despite thorough evaluation (p.3443).","Painful neck or head pain preceding stroke in a young patient (under ~60) suggests arterial dissection (p.3445)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute occlusion of an intracranial artery reduces regional cerebral blood flow; the resulting deficit depends on collateral supply, occlusion site, and systemic pressure. Energy failure from loss of glucose and oxygen halts ATP-dependent ion pumps, causing depolarization, sodium and calcium influx, excess synaptic glutamate, and excitotoxicity with reactive oxygen species that damage membranes and DNA, followed by an innate inflammatory response. Fever and hyperglycaemia (glucose >200 mg/dL) worsen the injury. Mechanistically, ischemic stroke arises by three routes (the basis of TOAST-style classification): (1) embolic occlusion of a large intracranial vessel - either cardioembolic (e.g., atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus, valvular disease) or artery-to-artery from carotid/aortic-arch plaque; (2) in-situ thrombosis of small penetrating arteries from lipohyalinosis or microatheroma, producing lacunar (small-vessel) infarcts; and (3) hypoperfusion from flow-limiting stenosis of a major extracranial or intracranial vessel, producing watershed/border-zone ischemia. Cardioembolism causes ~20%, large-artery/carotid atherosclerosis ~10%, and small-vessel disease ~20% of ischemic strokes (p.3439, p.3443-3446).","additionalDifferentials":["Intracerebral hemorrhage (must be excluded by CT before reperfusion)","Transient ischemic attack (deficit fully resolving within 24 h with no infarction on imaging)","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis (headache, focal signs, seizures; CT often normal)","Hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy from global hypoperfusion (e.g., cardiac arrest)","Brain tumour presenting acutely via hemorrhage, seizure, or hydrocephalus","Functional/non-organic deficit"],"infectionMimics":["Stroke recrudescence: fever or sepsis unmasking a prior deficit, mimicking new hemiparesis, which clears as the infection is treated","Infective endocarditis with septic emboli (multifocal deficits; may evolve to abscess or hemorrhagic infarct)","Brain abscess","CNS infection-related vasculitis (syphilis, tuberculosis, fungal, bacterial, zoster meningitis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Seizure with postictal (Todd's) paralysis - an observer history of no convulsive onset usually excludes seizure, but ongoing complex partial seizures can mimic stroke","Migraine, including acephalgic migraine even without prior history; sensory/motor deficits migrate slowly over minutes and may cross vascular territories","Metabolic encephalopathy (fluctuating mental status, typically without focal signs)","Hypoglycaemia and hyperglycaemia (check finger-stick glucose before thrombolysis)","Intracranial tumour"],"additionalInvestigations":["Finger-stick blood glucose - one of only two tests (with brain imaging) truly required before IV thrombolysis","ECG and continuous cardiac telemetry to detect atrial fibrillation or recent MI; ambulatory monitoring if AF not captured inpatient","Echocardiography (transthoracic/transoesophageal); bubble-contrast study to detect right-to-left shunt / PFO","Carotid duplex ultrasound and transcranial Doppler (can also detect microemboli)","CT/MR perfusion imaging to define the penumbra and select late-window candidates","MRI with diffusion-weighted imaging - most sensitive for acute infarction, especially in the posterior fossa, and detects new infarction after TIA","Catheter cerebral angiography (gold standard for stenosis/vasculopathy; reserved for when noninvasive studies are inadequate)","Secondary-prevention workup: lipid profile, ESR, FBC, electrolytes, renal function, coagulation (PT/PTT); selective thrombophilia/vasculitis screen in young or cryptogenic cases"],"redFlags":["Disabling deficit (NIHSS >5) - candidate for thrombolysis and/or thrombectomy; act fast","Suspected large-vessel occlusion (ICA terminus, M1/M2, basilar) - candidate for mechanical thrombectomy","Cerebellar infarct with declining consciousness or new brainstem signs - impending herniation/hydrocephalus needing emergency decompression","Large hemispheric (malignant MCA) infarct - 5-10% develop space-occupying edema peaking on day 2-3, may need hemicraniectomy","Painful neck/Horner's or recent neck trauma/manipulation - suspect arterial dissection","Multifocal deficits with fever - suspect septic emboli from endocarditis"],"prognosis":"Stroke is the second leading cause of death worldwide; outcome hinges on time to reperfusion ('time-is-brain'). For large-vessel occlusion, the chance of a good outcome falls steeply with delay - the odds of a good result exceed 3 if groin puncture for thrombectomy is within 2 h but fall to about 2 by 8 h, and over 80% do well if the vessel opens within 1 h of arrival versus about one-third at 6 h. In pooled thrombectomy trials ~46% achieved functional independence (modified Rankin 0-2) versus ~26.5% with medical care, with a mortality benefit. Small-vessel (lacunar) strokes generally recover more rapidly and completely than large-vessel strokes, though some stutter or leave severe disability. Without secondary prevention the annual risk of a further stroke after atherothrombotic stroke/TIA is about 8-10%; symptomatic intracranial atherosclerosis and untreated symptomatic carotid stenosis each carry ~15% per year. Care in a dedicated stroke unit followed by rehabilitation improves neurologic outcome and reduces mortality (p.3427, p.3441-3442, p.3445-3446).","pitfalls":["Treating a stroke mimic as stroke: a postictal (Todd's) paralysis, acephalgic migraine, or hypoglycaemia can convincingly imitate acute stroke - always confirm glucose and seek an observer history of convulsive onset.","Mislabelling cerebellar stroke as benign labyrinthitis and missing life-threatening brainstem compression / hydrocephalus.","Omitting the embolic-source workup (carotid, heart) after a lacunar-appearing infarct, since a large-vessel source can present as a small-vessel syndrome.","A normal early CT does not exclude infarction; CT may be only subtly abnormal acutely and the hyperdense MCA sign is specific but seen in only ~20% of MCA occlusions (DWI-MRI is far more sensitive).","Over-attributing stroke to a patent foramen ovale, which is present in ~15% of the general population and is often incidental.","Aggressively lowering blood pressure acutely can reduce pressure-dependent collateral flow and worsen the penumbra; permissive hypertension is generally allowed early.","Distinguish benign reperfusion petechial haemorrhage within an infarct from frank haemorrhagic transformation with mass effect."],"severityClassification":"Severity is graded clinically by the National Institutes of Health Stroke Scale (NIHSS). A disabling deficit (NIHSS >5) flags potential eligibility for thrombolysis/endovascular therapy, whereas minor non-disabling stroke (NIHSS 0-5) may be managed with aspirin or short-term dual antiplatelet therapy. Mechanism-based (TOAST-style) classification - cardioembolic, large-artery atherosclerosis (carotid/intracranial), small-vessel/lacunar, other defined cause, and cryptogenic (~30%) - drives secondary prevention. Carotid disease is further classed as symptomatic vs asymptomatic and by degree of stenosis, with risk rising with stenosis severity except for near-occlusions, which carry lower risk (p.3440-3441, p.3443, p.3445).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3427","Harrison 22e p.3428","Harrison 22e p.3439","Harrison 22e p.3440","Harrison 22e p.3441","Harrison 22e p.3442","Harrison 22e p.3443","Harrison 22e p.3444","Harrison 22e p.3445","Harrison 22e p.3446"],"crossLinks":["ich","tia","sah","atrial_fib","hypoglycemia","seizure_epilepsy","migraine","metabolic_enceph","brain_tumour","dvt","pe","vasculitis","IE"]},"itp":{"name":"Immune thrombocytopenia (ITP)","system":"Hematology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.921-924 (Chap. 120, Disorders of Platelets and Vessel Wall)","clinicalPearls":["ITP is an acquired disorder of immune-mediated platelet destruction, with possible additional inhibition of platelet release from megakaryocytes (p.924).","In children ITP is usually acute, often follows an infection, and is self-limited; in adults it tends to be chronic, though some adults remit spontaneously within months of diagnosis (p.924).","It is largely a diagnosis of exclusion: characterized by mucocutaneous bleeding and a low (often very low) platelet count with otherwise normal peripheral blood cells and smear (p.924).","Presentation is either with ecchymoses/petechiae or with thrombocytopenia found incidentally on a routine CBC (p.924).","Mucocutaneous bleeding may include oral mucosal, gastrointestinal, or heavy menstrual bleeding; rarely life-threatening (including CNS) bleeding occurs (p.924).","Wet purpura (blood blisters in the mouth) and retinal hemorrhages may herald life-threatening bleeding (p.924, p.922).","Petechiae are pinpoint, nonblanching hemorrhages that signal decreased platelet number rather than platelet dysfunction, and first appear in dependent areas of increased venous pressure (ankles/feet in an ambulatory patient) (p.922).","Serologic (antiplatelet antibody) testing is generally not helpful because of low sensitivity and specificity of current tests (p.924).","The peripheral smear may show large platelets with otherwise normal morphology, reflecting young/rapidly turned-over platelets (p.924, p.916)."],"pathophysiology":"ITP results from immune-mediated destruction of antibody-coated platelets by the reticuloendothelial system, and possibly impaired platelet release from megakaryocytes. Primary ITP has no identifiable cause; secondary ITP is associated with an underlying disorder such as autoimmune disease (notably SLE) or infection (HIV, hepatitis C). Childhood ITP commonly follows a viral infection through immune mechanisms and usually resolves spontaneously (Harrison 22e p.922, p.924).","additionalDifferentials":["Drug-induced thrombocytopenia (e.g., quinine, sulfonamides, vancomycin; classic drug-dependent antibodies react with platelet antigens only when drug is present) (p.922-923)","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) - associated with thrombosis rather than bleeding, nadir rarely <20,000/uL; use 4T scoring (p.923)","Congenital/inherited thrombocytopenia (e.g., autosomal dominant macrothrombocytopenia) (p.922, p.965 algorithm)","Pseudothrombocytopenia from platelet clumping (EDTA-dependent) - redraw in sodium citrate or heparin (p.922, p.965 algorithm)","Evans syndrome - combined autoimmune hemolytic anemia with ITP (perform direct antiglobulin/Coombs test if anemia present) (p.924)"],"infectionMimics":["HIV infection - can cause immune thrombocytopenia early; late HIV causes pancytopenia with decreased/dysplastic platelet production (p.922)","Hepatitis C virus infection - recognized cause of secondary ITP (p.924)","Infectious mononucleosis - immune-mediated thrombocytopenia (p.922)","Systemic infection with gram-negative bacteria or COVID-19 - thrombocytopenia that may be associated with DIC (p.922)","Helicobacter pylori - association with ITP is unclear but appears to have a geographic distribution (p.924)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Microangiopathic hemolytic anemias (TTP, DIC) - distinguished by schistocytes/fragmented red cells on smear (p.922, p.965 algorithm)","Hypersplenism/splenomegaly (e.g., chronic liver disease) causing platelet sequestration (p.922)","Bone marrow production failure - low platelet count with absence of large young platelets on smear (p.916, p.922)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CBC with peripheral blood smear review to confirm isolated thrombocytopenia and exclude clumping/schistocytes (p.922, p.924)","HIV and hepatitis C testing to evaluate for secondary ITP (p.924)","Serologic testing for SLE (p.924)","Serum protein electrophoresis and immunoglobulin levels (to detect hypogammaglobulinemia or monoclonal gammopathy) (p.924)","Selective testing for IgA deficiency (p.924)","H. pylori testing depending on clinical circumstance (p.924)","Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test if anemia present, to detect Evans syndrome (p.924)","Bone marrow examination reserved for patients with unexplained additional findings or failure to respond to initial therapy (p.924)"],"redFlags":["Wet purpura (oral blood blisters) - may herald life-threatening bleeding (p.924, p.922)","Retinal hemorrhages - may herald life-threatening bleeding (p.924)","Active mucosal, GI, or CNS bleeding (p.924)","Very severe thrombocytopenia (<5000/uL) or signs of impending bleeding (p.924)"],"prognosis":"Childhood ITP is usually acute and almost always resolves spontaneously. Adult ITP is more often chronic, though some adults remit spontaneously within months. Patients with platelet counts >30,000/uL appear not to have increased mortality related to the thrombocytopenia, so diagnosis does not mandate treatment (Harrison 22e p.924).","pitfalls":["Treating presumed ITP without excluding secondary causes (HIV, HCV, SLE, drugs) (p.924).","Relying on antiplatelet antibody serology - low sensitivity/specificity makes it unhelpful for diagnosis (p.924).","Missing pseudothrombocytopenia from EDTA-induced platelet clumping, which produces a falsely low automated count; confirm on smear and redraw in citrate/heparin (p.922, p.916).","Overlooking Evans syndrome - failing to do a Coombs test when anemia accompanies thrombocytopenia (p.924).","Failing to take a careful drug/herbal/OTC history, since drugs are the most common cause of thrombocytopenia (p.922)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal grading scale. Practical thresholds noted: platelet counts >30,000/uL are not associated with increased mortality; ~5000-10,000/uL is the level needed to maintain microvascular integrity; severe thrombocytopenia is cited as <5000/uL for treatment-decision purposes (Harrison 22e p.922, p.924).","references":["Harrison 22e p.921","Harrison 22e p.922","Harrison 22e p.923","Harrison 22e p.924"],"crossLinks":["ttp_hus","sle_flare","dic"]},"lung_cancer":{"name":"Lung cancer","system":"Oncology / Pulmonary","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.83 Neoplasms of the Lung (p.614-622); SVC syndrome from Ch.80 Oncologic Emergencies (p.585-586)","clinicalPearls":["Histology splits into small-cell lung cancer (SCLC) and three non-small-cell types (adenocarcinoma, squamous cell, large-cell) grouped as NSCLC; these four account for about 90% of epithelial lung cancers and the SCLC/NSCLC distinction drives entirely different natural history and management (p.616).","SCLC and NSCLC behave very differently: SCLC is more strongly tobacco-associated, harbours TP53 and RB1 mutations in roughly 90% of cases, and presents with extensive-stage disease in 60-70% of patients (p.617, p.622).","Adenocarcinoma is now the most common subtype as smoking has declined, and predominates in never-smokers/light smokers, women, and younger adults (<60); squamous and small-cell types remain the most tobacco-linked (p.616).","Within NSCLC, distinguishing squamous from nonsquamous is critical for therapy; 'non-small-cell carcinoma, not otherwise specified' is no longer an acceptable diagnosis, and immunohistochemistry (TTF-1/napsin-A for adeno, p40/p63 for squamous) resolves ambiguous small biopsies (p.616).","About two-thirds of new US lung cancer diagnoses occur in former smokers or never-smokers; roughly one in five women and one in 12 men with lung cancer have never smoked, so a never-smoking history does not exclude the diagnosis (p.614).","Smoking cessation benefits patients even after a lung cancer diagnosis (better overall survival, fewer treatment side effects, improved quality of life), yet about 30% continue to smoke after diagnosis (p.614-615).","A normal chest x-ray does not exclude lung cancer: chronic cough with or without haemoptysis in a current/former smoker with COPD aged 40 or older warrants thorough investigation even with a normal CXR (p.618).","Tissue sampling is mandatory to confirm diagnosis in all suspected cases; a core biopsy is preferred over FNA to provide enough material for molecular and PD-L1 testing (p.620).","Central lesions (squamous, small-cell, endobronchial carcinoids) are best reached by bronchoscopy, whereas peripheral lesions (adenocarcinoma, large-cell) favour transthoracic biopsy (p.620).","PET should never be used alone to diagnose lung cancer or confirm mediastinal/metastatic involvement; tissue confirmation is always required, and false positives occur with infection and granulomatous disease such as tuberculosis (p.620)."],"pathophysiology":"Lung cancer arises from respiratory epithelium (bronchi, bronchioles, alveoli). Environmental carcinogens, chiefly tobacco smoke, drive transformation of bronchoepithelial cells, modulated by polymorphisms in carcinogen-metabolising genes (e.g. CYP1A1). Cancers accumulate gain-of-function oncogene mutations and loss-of-function tumour-suppressor mutations; allelic loss on chromosome 3p occurs very early, even in histologically normal smoke-damaged epithelium. NSCLC adenocarcinomas are frequently driven by targetable oncogenic alterations (EGFR ~10-35%, KRAS ~15-25%, ALK rearrangement 3-7%, plus BRAF, HER2, MET, RET, ROS1, NTRK), whereas nearly 90% of SCLCs carry TP53 and RB1 mutations. Clinical manifestations stem from the primary lesion, local/regional invasion, distant metastases, or paraneoplastic syndromes. Paraneoplastic endocrine effects reflect ectopic hormone secretion: PTH-related peptide (squamous cell) causing hypercalcaemia, ADH or atrial natriuretic peptide (SCLC) causing hyponatraemia, and ectopic ACTH (SCLC, pulmonary carcinoids) causing Cushing-type biochemistry. (p.615-619)","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary metastases from non-lung primaries and other neoplasms metastatic to the lung, which constitutional symptoms cannot reliably distinguish from primary lung cancer (p.619)","Endobronchial metastases from carcinoid, breast, colon, kidney and other tumours presenting with haemoptysis (referenced p.591/Ch.80)","Mesothelioma, which can be hard to separate from adenocarcinoma and is identified by CK5/6, calretinin and WT-1 positivity (p.616)","Pulmonary carcinoid tumour, a neuroendocrine tumour that can also secrete ectopic ACTH (p.619, p.662 area)"],"infectionMimics":["Post-obstructive pneumonia or a persistent pneumonia that does not resolve after repeated antibiotic courses can mask an underlying obstructing tumour (p.618)","Lung abscess physiology from tumour cavitation can mimic infective abscess (p.618)","Granulomatous infection such as tuberculosis can cause false-positive PET uptake and simulate malignancy on imaging (p.620)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Paraneoplastic syndromes can mimic metastatic disease and, if unrecognised, lead to inappropriate palliative rather than curative treatment (p.619)","Benign causes of SVC obstruction (central venous catheters, pacemaker/defibrillator leads, aortic aneurysm, thyromegaly, thrombosis, fibrosing mediastinitis from prior radiation/histoplasmosis/Behcet) now account for at least 40% of SVC syndrome cases (p.585)","Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy with periostitis can produce bone pain and a positive bone scan that mimics skeletal metastasis (p.619)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Core biopsy (preferred over FNA) for adequate tissue, obtained by bronchoscopic/transbronchial biopsy, image-guided percutaneous biopsy, or EBUS/EUS-guided sampling depending on location (p.620)","Next-generation sequencing for driver oncogenes (EGFR, ALK, ROS1, BRAF, KRAS, MET, RET, NTRK, HER2) plus PD-L1 immunohistochemistry reported as tumour proportion score, recommended for all advanced NSCLC (p.620)","Circulating tumour DNA 'liquid biopsy' as a non-invasive adjunct when tissue is limited or inaccessible (p.620)","PET-CT for anatomic staging; an SUV >2.5 is highly suspicious for malignancy, with false negatives in lesions <8 mm, diabetes, and slow-growing tumours (p.620)","MRI of the brain for metastases (and to assess brachial plexus in superior sulcus tumours); recommended in SCLC staging where brain metastases are present in ~10% of asymptomatic patients (p.620-621)","EBUS/EUS-guided or invasive mediastinal staging (mediastinoscopy, thoracoscopy) since one-quarter to one-half of NSCLC patients have mediastinal nodal metastases at diagnosis (p.620-621)","Repeat thoracentesis if the first is negative in suspected malignant pleural effusion; spinal MRI/CT and lumbar puncture if cord compression or leptomeningeal disease is suspected (p.620-621)","Immunohistochemistry panel to confirm neuroendocrine differentiation (CD56/NCAM, synaptophysin, chromogranin, INSM1) and to distinguish primary lung from metastatic adenocarcinoma (TTF-1, napsin-A) (p.616)","Routine labs suggesting metastatic spread: anaemia, and elevated alkaline phosphatase, GGT, transaminases, and calcium (p.619)"],"redFlags":["Chronic cough with or without haemoptysis in a smoker/former smoker with COPD aged 40+, even with a normal chest x-ray (p.618)","A persistent pneumonia without constitutional symptoms that fails to clear after repeated antibiotic courses (p.618)","Superior vena cava syndrome: neck/facial swelling, dilated neck and chest-wall collateral veins, plethora worse when supine; cerebral or laryngeal oedema signals a poorer prognosis and demands urgent evaluation (p.585-586)","Tracheal obstruction from a superior mediastinal mass is the one immediately life-threatening complication and needs emergent therapy (p.586)","Pancoast/superior sulcus tumour: shoulder pain radiating in the ulnar distribution of the arm with destruction of the first and second ribs (p.619)","Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos) from sympathetic chain involvement, often coexisting with Pancoast syndrome (p.619)","Spinal cord compression symptoms (pain, weakness, paralysis, urinary retention) requiring urgent spinal imaging (p.621)","Symptomatic hypercalcaemia (nausea, vomiting, constipation, polyuria, thirst, altered mental status) as the most common life-threatening metabolic complication, usually from squamous cell carcinoma (p.619)","Weight loss exceeding 10% of body weight, a poor prognostic sign (p.619)"],"prognosis":"Outcome is closely tied to stage at diagnosis, but over half of patients present with locally advanced or metastatic disease. In NSCLC, about 15% have localised disease amenable to curative surgery or radiotherapy, roughly a quarter have local/regional disease that may or may not be curable, and about half have metastatic disease at diagnosis. SEER data (2022) show a 5-year survival of about 63% for localised disease. Extrathoracic metastases are found at autopsy in >50% of squamous, ~80% of adenocarcinoma/large-cell, and >95% of SCLC cases. SCLC is highly likely to present extensive-stage (60-70%). Adenocarcinoma pattern carries prognostic weight: lepidic-predominant is favourable, acinar/papillary intermediate, and solid or micropapillary poor; adenocarcinoma in situ and minimally invasive adenocarcinoma achieve essentially 100% 5-year disease-free survival after complete resection. Weight loss >10%, and thrombotic disease complicating cancer, are poor prognostic signs. (p.616, p.619, p.621-622)","pitfalls":["Treating a non-resolving pneumonia with further antibiotics rather than investigating risks losing the chance at early cancer diagnosis (analogous principle stated for head/neck cancer p.611; applied to lung p.618).","Mistaking a paraneoplastic syndrome for metastatic disease and switching to palliative care when the patient may still be curable; many paraneoplastic syndromes resolve with successful tumour treatment (p.619).","Relying on PET alone for diagnosis or staging without tissue confirmation, given false positives from infection/granulomatous disease and false negatives in small or indolent lesions (p.620).","Accepting a negative TTF-1 as excluding a lung primary; a negative TTF-1 does not rule out lung cancer (p.616).","Stopping at a single negative thoracentesis in suspected malignant effusion, or a single negative lumbar puncture in suspected leptomeningeal disease, when a repeat is warranted (p.620-621).","Overly rapid correction of SIADH-related hyponatraemia (including with tolvaptan) can cause irreversible neurologic injury; patients with ectopic ANP may worsen unless sodium intake is increased (p.619).","In screening, ignoring lead-time bias, length-time bias and overdiagnosis can overstate apparent survival benefit; LDCT also carries a very high false-positive rate (about 96% of positive screens were false positives in NLST) (p.617-618)."],"severityClassification":"NSCLC: TNM (8th edition, effective 2018; 9th edition expected 2024). T by size/invasion - T1 <=3cm (T1a <=1, T1b >1-2, T1c >2-3), T2 >3-5cm or visceral pleura/main bronchus/atelectasis (T2a >3-4, T2b >4-5), T3 >5-7cm or chest wall/pericardium/phrenic nerve invasion or same-lobe satellite nodule, T4 >7cm or invasion of mediastinum/heart/great vessels/trachea/oesophagus/spine/carina or different ipsilateral-lobe nodule. N: N0 none, N1 ipsilateral peribronchial/hilar/intrapulmonary, N2 ipsilateral mediastinal/subcarinal, N3 contralateral mediastinal/hilar or any scalene/supraclavicular. M: M1a contralateral-lobe nodule or pleural/pericardial nodule/malignant effusion, M1b single distant metastasis to one organ, M1c multiple metastases. SCLC: dual classification recommended - the Veterans Administration two-stage system (limited-stage = confined to ipsilateral hemithorax within a tolerable radiation port, including contralateral supraclavicular nodes, recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement and SVC obstruction; extensive-stage = overt metastatic disease, or cardiac tamponade/malignant effusion/bilateral parenchymal involvement) plus the TNM system, with TNM preferred for the rare apparent stage I SCLC. (p.620-622)","references":["Harrison 22e p.585","Harrison 22e p.586","Harrison 22e p.614","Harrison 22e p.615","Harrison 22e p.616","Harrison 22e p.617","Harrison 22e p.618","Harrison 22e p.619","Harrison 22e p.620","Harrison 22e p.621","Harrison 22e p.622"],"crossLinks":["svc_obstruction","hypercalcemia","hyponatremia","pleural_effusion","LUNG_ABSCESS"]},"malignancy_b":{"name":"Malignancy (B-symptoms)","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.193, 316-318, 476-478, 855, 860-861, 869","clinicalPearls":["B symptoms are formally defined for lymphoma staging (the Ann Arbor 'B' suffix) as unexplained fever (>38C), drenching night sweats, and/or unexplained weight loss exceeding 10% of body weight over the 6 months before diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.861, p.869).","The tempo of symptoms helps separate aggressive from indolent lymphoma: rapid symptomatic progression and the presence of B symptoms suggest a more aggressive process (Harrison 22e p.903).","Among malignancies, lymphoma is by far the commonest cause of malignancy-related fever of unknown origin, and the fever can precede any palpable lymphadenopathy (Harrison 22e p.193).","About one-third of patients with classical Hodgkin lymphoma present with B symptoms; Hodgkin disease can even present as a fever of unknown origin, more often in older patients with abdominal mixed-cellularity disease (Harrison 22e p.869).","Pel-Ebstein fever (cyclical fever lasting days to weeks alternating with afebrile intervals) is a rare but classic Hodgkin lymphoma presentation (Harrison 22e p.869).","Node character matters: lymphomatous nodes tend to be large, firm, rubbery, mobile, symmetric and non-tender, whereas metastatic carcinoma nodes are typically hard, non-tender and fixed to surrounding tissue (Harrison 22e p.477).","Supraclavicular and scalene node enlargement is always abnormal and warrants prompt evaluation; a left supraclavicular node (Virchow node) classically reflects a gastrointestinal primary, but lung, breast, testis and ovarian cancers also seed these nodes (Harrison 22e p.477).","Examination in suspected lymphoma should systematically cover all peripheral nodal sites plus Waldeyer ring, liver and spleen size, effusions/ascites, and testicular, breast or skin involvement, since any of these can change staging and management (Harrison 22e p.903).","Enlarged intra-abdominal or retroperitoneal nodes are usually malignant (lymphoma, or germ-cell tumour in young men), in contrast to the largely benign causes of cervical adenopathy (Harrison 22e p.477).","Half of all cancer patients lose some weight and up to 20% of cancer deaths are attributable directly to cachexia; malignancy that declares itself through marked weight loss generally carries a poor prognosis (Harrison 22e p.317)."],"pathophysiology":"B symptoms in lymphoma are cytokine-mediated systemic effects of the tumour rather than direct mass effects. In classical Hodgkin lymphoma the malignant Hodgkin/Reed-Sternberg cells are rare within a tumour mass that is largely a polyclonal inflammatory infiltrate reacting to tumour-derived cytokines; this inflammatory milieu drives constitutional symptoms and contributes to the generalised immune suppression seen in these patients. Involuntary weight loss in malignancy reflects a combination of anorexia, sarcopenia and a cytokine-driven cachexia syndrome. (Harrison 22e p.316, p.869)","additionalDifferentials":["Hodgkin lymphoma (classical) - nontender cervical/supraclavicular/axillary nodes, frequent mediastinal involvement (Harrison 22e p.869)","Non-Hodgkin lymphoma - nodal and extranodal disease; B symptoms part of staging (Harrison 22e p.860)","Acute or chronic lymphocytic leukaemia (can produce generalised adenopathy and constitutional symptoms) (Harrison 22e p.476)","Richter transformation of CLL - rapidly progressive focal adenopathy with night sweats, fever, weight loss and high LDH (Harrison 22e p.897)","Metastatic carcinoma to nodes (head/neck, lung, breast, thyroid, GI primaries) (Harrison 22e p.477)","Germ-cell tumour (retroperitoneal nodes in young men) (Harrison 22e p.477)","Occult solid tumours presenting with involuntary weight loss - GI/hepatobiliary, lung, breast, genitourinary, ovarian, prostate (Harrison 22e p.317)"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious mononucleosis (EBV or CMV) - generalised adenopathy with constitutional symptoms (Harrison 22e p.476)","Tuberculosis - can cause unilateral mediastinal adenopathy and mesenteric lymphadenitis mimicking lymphoma, with night sweats and weight loss (Harrison 22e p.477)","Histoplasmosis - unilateral paratracheal node involvement that can mimic lymphoma in endemic regions (Harrison 22e p.477)","Toxoplasmosis - generalised reactive adenopathy (Harrison 22e p.476)","HIV / AIDS - generalised lymphadenopathy and a recognised lymphoma risk factor (Harrison 22e p.476, p.903)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sarcoidosis - mediastinal/hilar adenopathy and constitutional symptoms (Harrison 22e p.477)","Systemic lupus erythematosus and mixed connective tissue disease - generalised adenopathy (Harrison 22e p.476)","Drug-induced (phenytoin/diphenylhydantoin and others) lymphadenopathy, including DRESS/DIHS with eosinophilia and extensive adenopathy (Harrison 22e p.193, p.476)","Castleman disease, Kikuchi histiocytic necrotizing lymphadenitis, Rosai-Dorfman disease, angioimmunoblastic lymphadenopathy, IgG4-related disease (Harrison 22e p.476)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Excisional biopsy of the largest abnormal node is preferred; fine-needle aspiration should NOT be the first procedure as it usually yields insufficient tissue and delays diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.478)","Serum protein electrophoresis to detect a monoclonal paraprotein (Harrison 22e p.903)","Serum beta2-microglobulin and LDH - independent prognostic markers in NHL (Harrison 22e p.903)","CBC with differential, comprehensive metabolic/liver panel, uric acid and calcium (Harrison 22e p.317, p.904)","ESR and CRP as part of the constitutional-symptom / weight-loss workup (Harrison 22e p.316, p.869)","Contrast CT of chest/abdomen/pelvis for staging; FDG-PET/CT preferred for aggressive lymphomas and Hodgkin lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.904)","Bone marrow biopsy where indicated; lumbar puncture if CNS-risk histology or neurologic features (Harrison 22e p.903-904)","HIV and hepatitis B and C serology - risk factors for NHL and relevant to hepatitis B reactivation risk (Harrison 22e p.904)","Cervical node ultrasound long-to-short axis ratio (<2.0 favours malignancy) where head/neck cancer is suspected (Harrison 22e p.478)","Age/sex/risk-appropriate cancer screening (e.g. faecal occult blood, colonoscopy, mammography) and abdominal ultrasound in occult-malignancy weight-loss workup (Harrison 22e p.318)"],"redFlags":["Supraclavicular or scalene adenopathy - always abnormal, biopsy promptly (Harrison 22e p.477-478)","Solitary hard non-tender cervical node in an older tobacco user - prompt biopsy after ENT mucosal assessment (Harrison 22e p.478)","Node size >2 cm with an abnormal chest x-ray in younger patients - predicts malignant/granulomatous biopsy yield (Harrison 22e p.521)","Hard fixed nodes, or firm mobile generalised adenopathy suggestive of lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.478)","Airway, oesophageal or SVC compression from thoracic adenopathy (cough/wheeze, hoarseness, dysphagia, facial/arm/neck swelling) (Harrison 22e p.520)","Enlarged intra-abdominal or retroperitoneal nodes (usually malignant) (Harrison 22e p.477)","Involuntary weight loss >=5% over 6 months prompting structured malignancy workup (Harrison 22e p.358)"],"prognosis":"Lymphoma prognosis is stage- and biology-dependent. For NHL the International Prognostic Index uses five factors (age >=60, elevated LDH, ECOG performance status >=2, Ann Arbor stage III/IV, and >1 extranodal site); for DLBCL treated with R-CHOP, 0 factors gives ~94% 4-year survival versus ~53% with 3-5 factors. Across lymphomas, decreased outcome is associated with B symptoms, advanced stage, elevated LDH and bulky disease (e.g. lymph-node mass >7 cm). In occult solid malignancy, presentation through significant involuntary weight loss generally carries a very poor prognosis, whereas when no cause is found after thorough workup the prognosis is comparatively favourable. (Harrison 22e p.317, p.861, NHL prognosis section)","pitfalls":["Do NOT give glucocorticoids for undiagnosed lymphadenopathy - their lympholytic effect can obscure lymphoma, leukaemia and Castleman disease and delay/disrupt diagnosis (the rare exception is life-threatening pharyngeal obstruction from Waldeyer-ring tissue in mononucleosis) (Harrison 22e p.521).","Fine-needle aspiration is inadequate for an initial lymphoma diagnosis - obtain an excisional/core biopsy; FNA is reserved for thyroid nodules and confirming relapse of a known cancer (Harrison 22e p.478).","Antibiotics are NOT indicated for lymphadenopathy unless there is strong evidence of bacterial infection (Harrison 22e p.521).","Localized-versus-generalized adenopathy has limited discriminatory value because many causes produce either pattern; do not over-rely on it (Harrison 22e p.476).","In primary care most lymphadenopathy is benign (>two-thirds nonspecific/URI, <1% malignant) and needs only short-interval follow-up - but persistent or enlarging nodes must be re-evaluated and biopsied (Harrison 22e p.476, p.521).","If a head-and-neck primary is suspected behind a solitary cervical node, perform a careful ENT exam and biopsy any suspicious mucosal lesion first before excising the node (Harrison 22e p.478)."],"severityClassification":"Ann Arbor staging (stages I-IV) with suffix A (no systemic symptoms) or B (presence of B symptoms: fever >38C, drenching night sweats, or weight loss >10% body weight over preceding 6 months); suffix E for localized extralymphatic extension. B-symptom status (the A/B designation) and stage drive prognosis and therapy. (Harrison 22e p.861, p.869)","references":["Harrison 22e p.193","Harrison 22e p.316","Harrison 22e p.317","Harrison 22e p.318","Harrison 22e p.476","Harrison 22e p.477","Harrison 22e p.478","Harrison 22e p.519","Harrison 22e p.520","Harrison 22e p.521","Harrison 22e p.855","Harrison 22e p.860","Harrison 22e p.861","Harrison 22e p.869","Harrison 22e p.897"],"crossLinks":["acute_leukemia","myeloma","PUO","DISSEMINATED_TB"]},"mesenteric_ischemia":{"name":"Acute mesenteric ischemia","system":"Vascular / GI","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2586-2589 (Chapter 340, Mesenteric Vascular Insufficiency)","clinicalPearls":["Severe, acute, non-remitting abdominal pain that is strikingly out of proportion to physical findings is the most common complaint in arterial acute mesenteric ischaemia, present in about 95% of cases; pain precedes signs because ischaemia begins in the mucosa and progresses outward to the serosa (p.2588).","About half of acute mesenteric ischaemia cases are embolic, lodging in the mid-to-distal SMA just past the middle colic artery origin; emboli commonly arise from atrial fibrillation, recent MI, soft atherosclerotic plaque, infective endocarditis, valvular heart disease, or recent cardiac/vascular catheterisation (p.2587).","Acute-on-chronic thrombotic ischaemia (about 25-30% of cases) occurs at sites of severe atherosclerotic narrowing of the SMA or celiac artery, typically in patients with pre-existing mesenteric atherosclerosis who may have had prior chronic mesenteric ischaemia (p.2587, 2588).","Embolic disease is suggested by older patients with a predisposing cardiac source (AF, prior embolic event, recent infective endocarditis), whereas thrombotic disease suggests underlying atherosclerosis with possible prior intestinal angina (p.2588).","Chronic mesenteric ischaemia classically presents as recurrent dull, crampy, postprandial epigastric pain ('intestinal angina') with fear of eating and weight loss; chronic diarrhoea may occur and symptoms typically last 6-12 months before diagnosis (p.2587-2588).","Mesenteric venous thrombosis tends to present more vaguely and subacutely with non-specific abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, distension, mild-to-moderate tenderness and dehydration, making it easy to under-recognise (p.2589).","The decision to operate in suspected acute mesenteric ischaemia is often based on a high index of suspicion from history and examination despite normal laboratory results (p.2588)."],"pathophysiology":"Intestinal ischaemia occurs when splanchnic perfusion fails to meet the bowel's metabolic demand, producing ischaemic tissue injury. The gut is supplied by the celiac artery, SMA and IMA with extensive collateralisation between major trunks and mesenteric arcades; protective responses include this collateral network, autoregulation of flow and the ability to increase oxygen extraction. Watershed colonic zones with inherently lower flow are Griffiths' point (splenic flexure) and Sudeck's point (descending/sigmoid colon), the commonest sites of colonic ischaemia. The splanchnic bed can receive up to 30% of cardiac output. Occlusive ischaemia results from an embolus or progressive thrombosis in a major mesenteric artery (over 75% of emboli are cardiac in origin and lodge in the SMA distal to the middle colic artery). Chronic intestinal angina generally requires progressive stenosis of two of the three major vessels. Non-occlusive ischaemia is driven by disproportionate mesenteric arteriolar vasospasm in response to severe physiological stress such as shock; untreated, early mucosal stress ulceration progresses to full-thickness injury. Mesenteric venous thrombosis is usually precipitated by a hypercoagulable state. (p.2587-2588)","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic mesenteric ischaemia / intestinal angina (postprandial pain, weight loss, fear of eating)","Ischaemic colitis (notably after elective or emergent aortic repair)","Gastrointestinal malignancy (must be excluded in chronic presentations)","Superior mesenteric artery syndrome (duodenal compression after weight loss/immobilisation)","Median arcuate ligament syndrome (celiac artery compression)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute intestinal obstruction (mechanical or strangulated bowel)","Other noninflammatory causes of abdominal pain such as biliary colic and neoplasia","Inflammatory abdominal conditions: pancreatitis, cholecystitis, appendicitis, diverticulitis, peptic ulcer, inflammatory bowel disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["Thin-cut (1 mm or thinner) CT angiography is the gold standard for detecting mesenteric arterial occlusive (embolic/thrombotic) disease and for assessing bowel; venous-phase imaging detects mesenteric venous thrombosis with diffuse bowel-wall thickening and splanchnic thrombus (p.2588-2589).","Mesenteric duplex ultrasound is a sensitive/specific screening tool; a high SMA peak flow velocity carries ~80% positive predictive value, and a negative duplex virtually excludes mesenteric ischaemia (test must be done fasting) (p.2588).","Leukocytosis, metabolic acidosis and lactic acidosis support advanced ischaemia but may be absent in reversible ischaemia and cannot reliably distinguish reversible ischaemia from frank necrosis (p.2589).","ECG and echocardiogram may help identify an embolic cardiac source but must not delay surgical therapy (p.2588).","Hypercoagulability workup in suspected mesenteric venous thrombosis (e.g. factor V Leiden, prothrombin mutation, protein C/S or antithrombin deficiency, antiphospholipid syndrome) (p.2587, 2589).","Colonoscopy if ischaemic colitis is suspected, to grade mucosal injury (p.2589)."],"redFlags":["Pain out of proportion to examination with a high-risk cardiac source (AF, recent MI, endocarditis) — embolic acute mesenteric ischaemia until proven otherwise","Later signs of peritonitis and cardiovascular collapse indicate established transmural infarction","Blood per rectum, marked distension or obtundation in a critically ill/shocked patient (suggests non-occlusive ischaemia)","Rising lactate / metabolic acidosis is a late marker and its absence does not exclude ischaemia"],"prognosis":"Acute mesenteric ischaemia carries a high mortality, roughly 50-80% at presentation (with the acute arterial form quoted at over 50%); it is one of the most challenging diagnoses, and the single most important determinant of survival is the timeliness of diagnosis and treatment. Endovascular stenting for chronic mesenteric ischaemia has about an 80% long-term success rate. (p.2587-2588)","pitfalls":["Treating pain out of proportion to examination as benign — early ischaemia produces severe pain with deceptively unremarkable abdominal signs because injury starts in the mucosa (p.2588).","Waiting for abnormal labs before acting — lactate and acidosis are late and may be normal in reversible ischaemia or even necrosis; the operative decision is often made on clinical suspicion despite normal results (p.2588-2589).","Overlooking non-occlusive ischaemia in obtunded/critically ill patients where examination findings are absent or obscured by the underlying illness (p.2589).","Missing the vaguer, subacute presentation of mesenteric venous thrombosis (non-specific pain, nausea, distension) (p.2589).","Failing to perform mesenteric duplex while fasting — bowel gas obscures flow and the post-prandial vasodilatory response (p.2588)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison classifies intestinal ischaemia into chronic mesenteric ischaemia (CMI) and acute mesenteric ischaemia (AMI). Acute mesenteric vascular insufficiency is subdivided into (1) arterio-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia — further split into arterial embolus (~50%) and arterial thrombosis (~25-30%, often acute-on-chronic), (2) non-occlusive/vasospastic mesenteric ischaemia (~20%, vasospasm or hypoperfusion), and (3) mesenteric venous thrombosis (<10%). Ischaemic colitis mucosal injury is graded as mild (minimal erythema), moderate (pale ulceration extending into muscularis), or severe (black/green discoloration with full-thickness necrosis). (p.2587-2589)","references":["Harrison 22e p.2586","Harrison 22e p.2587","Harrison 22e p.2588","Harrison 22e p.2589"],"crossLinks":["atrial_fib","bowel_obstruction","pancreatitis","aaa"]},"metabolic_enceph":{"name":"Metabolic encephalopathy","system":"Neuro / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.181-186 (Confusion and Delirium, Chap. 29); p.188-190 (Coma, Chap. 30); p.2348-2349 (Metabolic / sepsis-associated encephalopathy, Critical Care)","clinicalPearls":["Delirium is an acute, relatively rapid decline in cognition (hours to days) that characteristically fluctuates; the cardinal neuropsychological deficit is inattention, though memory, executive function, visuospatial skills and language are variably affected (p.181-182).","It is a purely clinical bedside diagnosis. The validated Confusion Assessment Method (CAM) requires (1) acute onset with fluctuating course and (2) inattention, plus either (3) disorganized thinking or (4) altered level of consciousness (p.184).","Recognize two ends of a spectrum: a hyperactive subtype (classic example: delirium tremens, with agitation, hallucinations, tremor, autonomic instability) and a hypoactive subtype (e.g., benzodiazepine intoxication, with apathy and psychomotor slowing); hypoactive cases are frequently overlooked, especially on wards and in the ICU (p.182).","Bilateral asterixis and multifocal myoclonus on motor exam are non-focal signs that, while nonspecific, usually point to a metabolic or toxic encephalopathy / drug intoxication rather than a structural lesion (p.184, p.190).","A simple bedside test of attention is forward digit span: healthy adults manage 5-7 digits, while a span of 4 or fewer suggests an attentional deficit; many delirious patients can repeat only 3 or fewer (p.184).","Worsening in the evening (sundowning) is typical but not required; because the course fluctuates, a single morning assessment can be falsely reassuring and serial examinations are needed (p.184).","About one-third of delirium cases are drug-related, especially agents with anticholinergic or sedative properties, narcotics and benzodiazepines; nearly any drug can precipitate delirium in a predisposed patient (p.184).","Delirium can act as a 'stress test for the brain' - a minor insult (UTI, elective surgery, hospitalization) that unmasks delirium in a previously well person should prompt later outpatient screening for an unrecognized underlying neurodegenerative or cerebrovascular disorder (p.183).","The MMSE is unreliable in delirium because tasks such as spelling 'world' backward and serial subtraction are corrupted by the underlying attentional deficit (p.184)."],"pathophysiology":"Delirium reflects diffuse rather than focal brain dysfunction; the attentional deficit localizes broadly to brainstem, thalamus, prefrontal cortex and parietal lobes, and most cases arise from widespread cortical and subcortical disturbance rather than a single lesion. Acetylcholine deficiency is thought to be central (explaining the potency of anticholinergic drugs), with additional roles for excess dopamine, proinflammatory mediators and individual susceptibility. The concept is that of an insult acting on a vulnerable, low-reserve brain. For most metabolic causes (hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia, hyperosmolarity, hypercapnia, hypercalcaemia, hepatic and renal failure) the brain shows little or no structural damage and the effects are largely reversible, attributed to impaired energy supply, altered ion fluxes across neuronal membranes and neurotransmitter abnormalities. In hepatic encephalopathy, raised ammonia is proposed to drive astrocytic glutamine synthesis and osmotic swelling, mitochondrial energy failure, reactive nitrogen/oxygen species, increased inhibitory GABA tone and 'false' neurotransmitters, with chronic disease producing diffuse astrocytosis. Uraemic encephalopathy is multifactorial (urea itself is not the cause): accumulated neurotoxins, catecholamine depletion, altered glutamate/GABA tone, raised brain calcium and blood-brain-barrier disruption. Importantly, the degree of neurologic change in metabolic encephalopathies depends heavily on the rapidity with which the serum derangement develops, not just its absolute level. In sepsis-associated encephalopathy, circulating inflammatory mediators (TNF, IL-1, IL-2, IL-6) appear to cause diffuse cerebral dysfunction that is not simply explained by the metabolic derangements of organ failure (p.183, p.188-189, p.2348-2349).","additionalDifferentials":["Dementia and pre-existing cognitive impairment - the major predisposing condition; at least two-thirds of delirium occurs in patients with underlying dementia, and the two coexist and overlap (p.183).","Dementia with Lewy bodies - fluctuating course, prominent visual hallucinations and parkinsonism can closely mimic hyperactive delirium (p.183).","Nonconvulsive status epilepticus and recurrent seizures with prolonged postictal confusion (p.185).","Focal stroke or haemorrhage in nondominant (right) parietal lobe or medial dorsal thalamus - rare structural cause of an acute confusional state (p.184-185).","Hypertensive encephalopathy / posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES) and eclampsia (p.184, p.190).","Diffuse neoplastic disease - multiple brain metastases, diffuse glioma, CNS lymphoma, carcinomatous meningitis (p.184).","Postoperative encephalopathy from a shower of microemboli, particularly after cardiac/cardiopulmonary bypass surgery (p.2348).","Acute primary psychiatric disturbance, depression and catatonia - must be distinguished, particularly from the quiet hypoactive subtype (p.184)."],"infectionMimics":["Systemic infections - urinary tract infection, pneumonia and other respiratory infections, skin/soft tissue infection and frank sepsis; a UTI precipitating acute confusion in an elderly patient with baseline dementia is a classic scenario (p.184).","CNS infections - bacterial meningitis, encephalitis (including herpes simplex) and brain abscess; less common causes but high morbidity/mortality if missed (p.184).","Sepsis-associated ('septic') encephalopathy - diffuse cytokine-mediated brain dysfunction occurring in the majority of patients with sepsis and multiorgan failure (p.184, p.2348-2349).","Severe systemic infections causing coma - septicaemia, typhoid fever, malaria (including cerebral malaria), Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome (p.190)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Anticholinergic, opioid, sedative-hypnotic and other prescription drug toxicity, and drugs of abuse (alcohol, opiates, MDMA/ecstasy, GHB, ketamine/PCP, cocaine, 'bath salts', synthetic cannabinoids) (p.184).","Alcohol and benzodiazepine withdrawal - to be considered in all delirium including the elderly, since even modest daily drinkers can withdraw on admission (p.184).","Poison/toxin exposure - carbon monoxide, ethylene glycol, inhalants, pesticides (p.184).","Endocrinopathies - hyper- or hypothyroidism, hyperparathyroidism, adrenal insufficiency / Addisonian crisis (p.184, p.190).","Hypothermia and hyperthermia (p.184, p.190).","Global cerebral hypoperfusion from hypotension - heart failure, septic shock, severe dehydration or anaemia (p.184-185).","Autoimmune and paraneoplastic disorders - CNS vasculitis, cerebral lupus, autoimmune encephalitis (anti-NMDA-receptor encephalitis shows a characteristic 'extreme delta brush' EEG) (p.184, p.190).","Sedative drug overdose mimicking structural brainstem disease or even brain death (e.g., barbiturates) - toxic causes must be excluded before diagnosing brain death (p.189)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Complete blood count and full electrolyte panel including magnesium and phosphorus (p.185).","Serum/urine toxicology screen - performed earlier in younger patients (p.185).","TSH and free T4, and serum cortisol for suspected endocrinopathy (p.185).","Vitamin levels - B12, folate, thiamine (p.185).","EEG - essential when seizures are suspected or when no cause is found after initial workup; shows nonspecific diffuse symmetric slowing in encephalopathy, frontal high-voltage slow or triphasic waves in metabolic (e.g., hepatic) coma, and can reveal nonconvulsive status epilepticus (p.184-185, p.190).","Brain imaging - MRI with diffusion and gadolinium is the preferred study (CT often used first for speed/availability) to exclude structural causes; note imaging is frequently unrevealing in pure metabolic delirium (p.185, p.189-190).","Lumbar puncture (after neuroimaging) when CNS infection, autoimmune, inflammatory or neoplastic disease is suspected, or in any delirium with a negative workup (p.185, p.190).","Inflammatory and autoimmune serologies - ESR, ANA, complement, p-/c-ANCA, and paraneoplastic/autoimmune encephalitis panels in serum and CSF when indicated (p.185).","ECG and infectious serologies (RPR, HIV; fungal/viral if high suspicion) as second-tier tests (p.185)."],"redFlags":["New focal neurologic deficit, meningismus, fever or papilloedema - point away from a simple metabolic cause toward a structural, infectious or raised-ICP process needing urgent imaging/LP (p.184, p.189-190).","Suspected nonconvulsive status epilepticus - if suspicion is high, EEG should be obtained immediately as it is treatable and easily missed in a fluctuating patient (p.185).","Sodium <125 mmol/L (especially if acute) causing confusion, and <119 mmol/L acutely associated with coma and convulsions (p.188-189).","In-hospital delirium carries a 25-33% mortality (comparable to sepsis) and a roughly fivefold higher mortality in following months - it signals serious underlying illness, not a benign transient state (p.182).","Delirium in the ICU should be regarded as organ dysfunction on a par with liver, kidney or heart failure, and is associated with worse outcomes (p.182, p.2348)."],"prognosis":"Once viewed as benign and transient, delirium is now recognized as a marker of serious illness with substantial morbidity and mortality: in-hospital mortality is roughly 25-33% (similar to sepsis), and delirious patients have about a fivefold higher mortality in the ensuing months versus matched non-delirious inpatients, plus longer stays, more nursing-home discharge, higher readmission and recurrent episodes. Most metabolic and toxic encephalopathies are reversible with no or minimal structural brain damage, and drug/toxin-induced coma typically resolves fully provided hypoxia or severe hypotension has not occurred. However, some episodes persist for weeks to months or longer, delirium can cause lasting cognitive decline, and lingering effects (ranging from amnesia for the episode to PTSD-like re-experiencing) are common. Sepsis-associated encephalopathy severe enough to cause coma carries a mortality approaching 50%, though this mainly reflects severity of the underlying critical illness rather than the encephalopathy itself (p.182, p.188-189, p.2349).","pitfalls":["Treating the symptoms of delirium pharmacologically while neglecting the underlying cause merely prolongs the confused state and can mask important diagnostic clues (p.185).","Missing nonconvulsive status epilepticus - its fluctuating presentation mimics ordinary delirium, and only EEG will reveal it (p.185).","Diagnosing delirium without knowledge of the patient's cognitive baseline, so that dementia or long-standing depression is mistaken for an acute confusional state - collateral history is essential (p.184).","Overlooking the quiet hypoactive subtype, which is commonly missed and may be wrongly attributed to depression or sedation (p.182, p.2348).","Attributing coma to incidental imaging findings (small subdural, old stroke) when the true cause is intoxication; a normal CT does not exclude an anatomic lesion, and most coma/confusion is metabolic or toxic in origin (p.190).","Forgetting that the severity of metabolic encephalopathy depends on how rapidly the derangement developed, so a 'mildly' abnormal value reached acutely can produce marked impairment (p.188-189).","Diagnosing brain death without first excluding sedative/toxic causes (e.g., barbiturates) that can mimic its findings (p.189)."],"severityClassification":"No formal grading scale is described; severity is gauged clinically by the level of consciousness (a continuum from hyperarousal through lethargy to coma) and by the depth of the attentional deficit (e.g., forward digit span). The EEG correlates with severity, showing progressive generalized background slowing as the encephalopathy deepens (p.184, p.190).","references":["Harrison 22e p.181","Harrison 22e p.182","Harrison 22e p.183","Harrison 22e p.184","Harrison 22e p.185","Harrison 22e p.188","Harrison 22e p.189","Harrison 22e p.190","Harrison 22e p.2348","Harrison 22e p.2349"],"crossLinks":["hepatic_enceph","wernicke","hypoglycemia","hyponatremia","seizure_epilepsy"]},"migraine":{"name":"Migraine","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.441 p.3462-3471 (Migraine and Other Primary Headache Disorders); Ch.17 p.113-116 (Headache); Ch.34 p.230 (Disorders of the Eye); Ch.433 p.3379; Ch.437 p.3429","clinicalPearls":["Migraine is the second most common cause of headache and the leading neurologic cause of disability worldwide; over a 1-year period it affects roughly 15% of women and 6% of men (p.3462).","An attack classically evolves through three phases - premonitory (prodrome), headache, and postdrome - and only about 20-25% of patients also experience an aura phase (p.3462).","Premonitory symptoms (yawning, sleepiness, fatigue, cognitive 'fog', mood change, neck discomfort, polyuria/polydipsia, food cravings) can begin hours to days before the pain; some apparent 'triggers' such as light sensitivity may actually be part of this early phase rather than true precipitants (p.3462).","Postdromal tiredness, poor concentration and mild neck discomfort can persist for hours and occasionally up to a day after the pain settles (p.3462).","Simplified diagnostic criteria require repeated attacks of headache lasting 4-72 h with a normal examination and no other reasonable cause, plus at least 2 of (unilateral, throbbing, aggravated by movement, moderate/severe) and at least 1 of (nausea/vomiting, photophobia and phonophobia) (Table 441-3, p.3466).","Cranial autonomic symptoms (lacrimation, conjunctival injection, nasal congestion, rhinorrhea, periorbital swelling, ptosis) can occur in migraine - even in children - and may be mistaken for sinus disease, leading to overdiagnosis of sinusitis and inappropriate treatment (p.114).","Migraine is a brain disorder, not a 'vascular headache'; vascular changes when present are secondary, driven by cranial parasympathetic activation, and treatment response cannot be predicted by vascular effects (p.114).","The vast majority of patients presenting with severe headache have a benign cause, most often migraine; brain tumor is a rare cause of isolated headache and an even rarer cause of severe pain (p.114).","Migraineurs do not encounter more stress than headache-free people; the problem is overresponsiveness to changes in stress, and they are characteristically sensitive to environmental/sensory stimuli, failing to habituate easily (p.3462, p.3508).","Vertigo can be a prominent migraine feature - about one-third of patients referred for vertigo/dizziness have a primary diagnosis of migraine (vestibular migraine) (p.3464)."],"pathophysiology":"Migraine is understood as a brain disorder of sensory processing rather than a primary vascular disease. The characteristic sensory sensitivity is attributed to dysfunction of monoaminergic and other sensory-control systems in the brainstem and hypothalamus. The key nociceptive pathway is the trigeminovascular system: trigeminal innervation of large intracranial vessels and dura, with terminals that, when activated, release vasoactive neuropeptides - notably calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) and pituitary adenylate cyclase-activating polypeptide (PACAP) - at vascular terminals and within the trigeminal nucleus. Second-order neurons of the trigeminocervical complex (caudal trigeminal nucleus extending into the upper cervical dorsal horns, receiving C1-C2 input) decussate and project via the quintothalamic tract to ventrobasal and posterior thalamic nuclei, with additional projections to periaqueductal gray and hypothalamus that exert descending antinociceptive modulation; the dorsal raphe nucleus, locus coeruleus, parabrachial nucleus and rostroventromedial medulla are implicated in this modulation. Serotonin (5-HT) is involved - triptans are 5-HT1B/1D agonists (some also 5-HT1F) and ditans are selective 5-HT1F agonists - and dopamine contributes, with dopamine-receptor hypersensitivity in migraineurs. Functional imaging shows hypothalamic activation in the premonitory phase and persistent dorsolateral pontine (including locus coeruleus) activation during attacks and in chronic migraine, with lateralization correlating with the side of head pain. Genetic study of familial hemiplegic migraine implicates ion channels (CACNA1A/Cav2.1 P/Q calcium channel in FHM1 ~50%; ATP1A2 Na-K ATPase in FHM2 ~20%; SCN1A sodium channel in FHM3), suggesting altered membrane excitability predisposes to aura. The slowly advancing visual scotoma with luminous (fortification) edges is recognized as a marker of spreading cortical depression associated with migraine (p.3462-3464, p.3379).","additionalDifferentials":["Tension-type headache - the main differential; distinguished by being completely featureless (no nausea/vomiting, photophobia, phonophobia, osmophobia, throbbing or aggravation with movement), whereas migraine has one or more of these (p.3471).","Cluster headache and other trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias (p.3462).","Acephalgic migraine / typical aura without headache - recurrent neurologic symptoms with little or no headache (p.3464).","Vestibular migraine - common, underdiagnosed cause of episodic vertigo lasting minutes to hours, sometimes without headache (p.3464).","Migraine with brainstem aura (formerly basilar/basilar-type migraine) (p.3464).","Medication-overuse headache complicating an underlying migraine tendency (p.115-116)."],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial/viral meningitis - acute severe headache with stiff neck and fever; can be mistaken for migraine because pounding headache, photophobia, nausea and vomiting are shared cardinal features, and there is often striking accentuation of pain on eye movement; lumbar puncture is mandatory (p.113-114)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Subarachnoid hemorrhage / ruptured aneurysm, AVM or intraparenchymal hemorrhage - thunderclap headache maximal in under 5 min lasting over 5 min, stiff neck without fever; CT may rarely be normal so LP may be needed (p.114).","Brain tumor - intermittent deep dull aching, may worsen with exertion or position change and cause nausea/vomiting; vomiting preceding headache by weeks is highly characteristic of posterior fossa tumors (p.114).","Temporal (giant cell) arteritis - dull/boring headache with superimposed stabbing pains, scalp tenderness, jaw claudication, polymyalgia, fever, weight loss in patients over ~50; migraineurs may report relief with prednisone, confounding interpretation of treatment response (p.114-115).","Acute angle-closure glaucoma - prostrating headache with nausea/vomiting beginning with severe eye pain; red eye with a fixed, mid-dilated pupil (p.115).","Transient ischemic attack / vertebrobasilar insufficiency - acute homonymous visual symptoms with fogging/graying and positive phenomena that can mimic migraine aura (p.230).","Ischemic stroke - migraine can mimic stroke even without prior migraine history, especially acephalgic migraine (p.3429)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Migraine is a clinical diagnosis in patients with a normal examination and no other reasonable cause for headache (Table 441-3, p.3466).","A headache diary aids diagnosis and quantifies disability and attack frequency (p.3462).","The Migraine Disability Assessment Score (MIDAS) is a validated tool to grade disability (p.3465).","CT or MRI of the brain (equally sensitive as initial screen for intracranial pathology) is indicated for an abnormal neurologic examination or recent-onset headache; lumbar puncture may additionally be required when a benign cause cannot otherwise be established (p.113).","General acute-headache workup may include palpation of cranial arteries, assessment of the cervical spine, blood pressure and urine examination, and funduscopy with intraocular pressure measurement (p.113)."],"redFlags":["Sudden-onset (thunderclap) headache or first/worst-ever severe headache (p.114).","Vomiting that precedes the headache (p.114).","Subacute worsening over days or weeks (p.114).","Pain induced by bending, lifting or cough (p.114).","Pain that disturbs sleep or is present immediately on awakening (p.114).","New headache in a patient with a known systemic illness (e.g., malignancy) (p.114).","Onset after age 55 (p.114).","Fever or unexplained systemic signs (p.114).","Abnormal neurologic examination (p.114).","Pain with local tenderness, e.g., over the temporal artery (p.114)."],"prognosis":"Patients should understand that migraine is an inherited tendency to headache that can be modified and controlled by lifestyle measures and medication but not eradicated. Except on some occasions in women taking oral estrogens or contraceptives, migraine is not associated with serious or life-threatening illness (p.3464). Migraine is nonetheless the most common neurologic cause of disability worldwide (p.3462). Recognized complications (ICHD-3) include status migrainosus, persistent aura without infarction, migrainous infarction, and migraine aura-triggered seizure; migraine may also progress to chronic migraine (Table 441-1, p.3463). Overuse of acute analgesics can transform episodic migraine into refractory medication-overuse headache; many patients improve after withdrawal, though residual headache often persists reflecting the underlying primary disorder (p.115-116).","pitfalls":["Mistaking migraine-related cranial autonomic symptoms for sinusitis, leading to overdiagnosis and inappropriate sinus treatment (p.114).","Misdiagnosing meningitis as migraine (or vice versa) because pounding headache, photophobia, nausea and vomiting overlap - hence LP is mandatory when fever and stiff neck are present (p.113-114).","Attributing a steroid-responsive headache in an elderly patient to giant cell arteritis when migraine (far more prevalent in the elderly) also improves with prednisone (p.115).","Treating headache from a reawakened migraine syndrome (e.g., after otologic or endodontic procedures) without first addressing the primary problem - the headache stays refractory until the cause is treated (p.113).","Failing to recognize acephalgic migraine, which can present de novo even after age 65 and is easily confused with stroke; the sensory/motor deficit migrates slowly across a limb over minutes (versus seconds in stroke) (p.3429).","Continuing acute analgesics while starting a preventive - a common cause of treatment failure and of medication-overuse headache (p.115-116)."],"severityClassification":"ICHD-3 (IHS 2018) classifies migraine into migraine without aura, migraine with aura (typical aura with/without headache, migraine with brainstem aura, hemiplegic migraine including FHM1-3 and sporadic, retinal migraine), chronic migraine, complications of migraine (status migrainosus, persistent aura without infarction, migrainous infarction, migraine aura-triggered seizure), probable migraine, and episodic syndromes that may be associated with migraine (cyclical vomiting, abdominal migraine, benign paroxysmal vertigo/torticollis, infantile colic, vestibular migraine). Chronic migraine is defined as migraine on 8 or more days per month with at least 15 total headache days per month (Table 441-1, p.3463; p.3462). Disability is graded by MIDAS: Grade I 0-5, Grade II 6-10, Grade III 11-20, Grade IV over 20 (Fig. 441-4, p.3466).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3462","Harrison 22e p.3463","Harrison 22e p.3464","Harrison 22e p.3465","Harrison 22e p.3466","Harrison 22e p.3471","Harrison 22e p.113","Harrison 22e p.114","Harrison 22e p.115","Harrison 22e p.116","Harrison 22e p.230","Harrison 22e p.3379","Harrison 22e p.3429"],"crossLinks":["tension_ha","sah","MENINGITIS","temporal_arteritis","ischemic_stroke","tia"]},"ms":{"name":"Multiple sclerosis (relapse)","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Ch. 455 (Multiple Sclerosis), p.3577-3589; Ch. 456 (Neuromyelitis Optica) p.3590","clinicalPearls":["MS is defined by lesions disseminated in both time and space: plaques arise at different times and in anatomically separate CNS sites (p.3577).","Typical onset is age 20-40 (slightly later in men); women are affected roughly three times as often as men (p.3577).","The most common presenting symptoms are sensory loss (~37%) and optic neuritis (~36%), followed by limb weakness (~35%) and paresthesias (p.3577).","Optic neuritis usually causes monocular reduced acuity, dimness, and color desaturation, with periorbital pain worsened by eye movement and an afferent pupillary defect; uveitis is uncommon and should prompt thought of sarcoidosis or lymphoma (p.3577).","A bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia (impaired adduction with nystagmus of the abducting eye, from a medial longitudinal fasciculus lesion) is particularly suggestive of MS; related gaze findings include horizontal gaze palsy and the 'one-and-a-half' syndrome (p.3578).","Lhermitte's symptom is a neck-flexion-induced electric-shock sensation radiating down the spine/legs; it is not specific to MS and can occur with other cervical cord disease such as spondylosis (p.3578).","Uhthoff's symptom (heat sensitivity) is transient worsening of neurologic symptoms with elevated core temperature (hot shower, exercise, fever) and reflects temperature-dependent conduction block rather than a true new lesion (p.3578).","Exercise-induced weakness is characteristic; weakness is of upper-motor-neuron type with spasticity, hyperreflexia, and extensor plantar responses, though a tendon reflex can occasionally be lost if a plaque disrupts the afferent reflex arc (p.3578).","Paroxysmal symptoms (tonic spasms, paroxysmal dysarthria/ataxia, sensory bursts) are brief (10 s-2 min), frequent (5-40/day), without altered consciousness, and self-limited; they arise from spontaneous discharges at the edges of demyelinated plaques (p.3578).","Trigeminal neuralgia, hemifacial spasm, or glossopharyngeal neuralgia in a young patient, or with bilateral/atypical features or objective sensory loss, should raise suspicion of MS involving the cranial nerve root entry zone (p.3578).","Fatigue is experienced by most patients and is the leading cause of work-related disability in MS; depression affects about half of patients (p.3578).","On MRI, MS plaques are oriented perpendicular to the ventricular surface (Dawson's fingers) reflecting perivenous demyelination; lesions >6 mm in the corpus callosum, periventricular white matter, brainstem, cerebellum, or cord are especially helpful diagnostically (p.3583).","A central vein sign within plaques on susceptibility-weighted (T2*) imaging supports an MS diagnosis (p.3583).","Two or more discrete CSF oligoclonal bands not present in paired serum are found in >90% of MS patients; bands may be absent at onset and increase over time (p.3583).","Evoked potential abnormalities occur in 80-90% of MS patients; a marked latency delay (rather than reduced amplitude or distorted waveform) is what suggests demyelination, and EPs are most informative when testing a clinically uninvolved pathway (p.3583-3584).","Most MRI-visible lesions and most early disease activity in MS are clinically silent—bursts of focal inflammation on serial MRI far exceed the relapse rate (p.3583).","Even relapse-free RRMS patients undergo subtle 'silent progression,' and disability accumulation is now attributed mainly to progression independent of relapse activity (PIRA) rather than incomplete recovery from relapses (RAW), across RRMS, SPMS, and PPMS (p.3578-3580).","A radiologically isolated syndrome (RIS) is incidental MRI evidence of MS in an asymptomatic person; clinically isolated syndrome (CIS) is a first demyelinating event at high risk for MS (p.3577-3580)."],"pathophysiology":"MS is an autoimmune CNS disease producing chronic inflammation, demyelination, gliosis (sclerotic plaques) and neuronal/axonal loss (p.3577). New lesions begin with perivenular cuffing by T cells and macrophages and blood-brain-barrier (BBB) disruption with a preserved vessel wall (unlike vasculitis); cytotoxic CD8 cells line the leading edge, B cells infiltrate, myelin-specific autoantibodies coat degenerating myelin, and complement is activated (p.3581). Sharply demarcated demyelination is the hallmark; axons are relatively spared early but can be destroyed in highly inflammatory lesions, and partial remyelination produces 'shadow plaques' (p.3581). Cumulative axonal/neuronal loss drives irreversible disability (up to ~70% of corticospinal axons lost in MS paraplegia) via demyelination-related loss of trophic support, ion-channel redistribution, and dying-back axonopathy (p.3581). Progressive MS features more diffuse microglial activation, chronic active (slowly expanding) plaques with microglia at a leading edge behind an intact BBB, cortical/subpial plaques poorly seen on MRI, and meningeal ectopic lymphoid follicles linked to subpial cortical demyelination (p.3581-3582). Physiologically, demyelination causes conduction block (hyperpolarization from exposed potassium channels) and conduction slowing; temperature and metabolic changes worsen block, explaining heat- and fever-related fluctuations (p.3582-3583). Susceptibility is polygenic with the strongest signal at HLA-DRB1*1501 (~10% of risk), and EBV seroconversion appears a near-universal prerequisite, plausibly via molecular mimicry between EBV peptides and myelin autoantigens (p.3580-3581).","additionalDifferentials":["Neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder (NMOSD, anti-AQP4) (p.3584, 3590)","Myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein-associated disease (MOGAD) (p.3584)","Glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP) autoimmunity (p.3584)","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis (ADEM) (p.3584)","Sjogren's syndrome (p.3584-3585)","Sarcoidosis / neurosarcoidosis (p.3584-3585)","Systemic lupus erythematosus and related collagen-vascular disorders (p.3585)","Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (p.3585)","Primary CNS or systemic vasculitis (p.3585)","Behcet's disease (p.3585)","CADASIL (p.3585)","MELAS (mitochondrial encephalopathy, lactic acidosis, stroke) (p.3585)","Congenital leukodystrophies (e.g., adrenoleukodystrophy, metachromatic leukodystrophy) (p.3585)","Vitamin B12 deficiency (subacute combined degeneration) (p.3585)","Ischemic optic neuropathy (arteritic and nonarteritic) (p.3585)","CNS lymphoma; gliomas/meningiomas (p.3584-3585)","Stroke / ischemic cerebrovascular disease (p.3585)","Spinal dural arteriovenous fistula and other vascular malformations (p.3585)"],"infectionMimics":["HIV infection (p.3585)","Neurosyphilis / syphilis (p.3584-3585)","Lyme disease (p.3584-3585)","Tropical spastic paraparesis (HTLV-1/2 infection) (p.3585)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["NMOSD (anti-AQP4) and MOGAD - consider with bilateral/severe optic neuritis or severe transverse myelitis (p.3584)","ADEM - consider with hyperacute or postinfectious presentation (p.3584)","Tumefactive demyelination mimicking a primary or metastatic brain tumor (p.3584, 3589)","Sarcoidosis, Sjogren's syndrome, SLE, antiphospholipid syndrome, vasculitis (p.3584-3585)","Vitamin B12 deficiency, hereditary leukodystrophies, CADASIL, MELAS (p.3585)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CSF analysis for intrathecal IgG synthesis: CSF IgG index and IgG synthesis rate, plus oligoclonal bands by agarose-gel electrophoresis (>90% positive in MS) (p.3583)","CSF cell count/profile: a mild mononuclear pleocytosis (>5 cells/uL) occurs in ~25% (young relapsing patients); pleocytosis >75 cells/uL, polymorphonuclear cells, or protein >1 g/L argues against MS (p.3583)","Gadolinium-enhanced MRI to detect active (BBB-disrupted) lesions; enhancement typically lasts <1 month (p.3583)","Susceptibility-weighted (T2*) MRI to look for the central vein sign (p.3583)","Quantitative brain and spinal cord atrophy / T2 lesion burden (correlates with disability; cord atrophy correlates more strongly) (p.3583)","T1 imaging for 'black holes' (markers of irreversible demyelination and axonal loss) (p.3583)","Visual, somatosensory, brainstem-auditory, and motor evoked potentials (p.3583-3584)","Workup to exclude mimics in suspected MS: ESR, serum B12, antinuclear antibodies, and treponemal antibody (p.3585)"],"redFlags":["Distinguish a true relapse from a pseudoexacerbation driven by raised ambient temperature, fever, or infection - glucocorticoids are inappropriate for a pseudoexacerbation (p.3585).","Features atypical for MS that should prompt an alternative diagnosis: symptoms confined to posterior fossa/craniocervical junction/spinal cord; onset <15 or >60 years; progressive course from onset; never any visual, sensory, or bladder symptoms; or atypical MRI/CSF/EP findings (p.3584).","Symptoms rare/uncommon in MS that favor another diagnosis: aphasia, parkinsonism, chorea, isolated dementia, severe muscle atrophy, peripheral neuropathy, episodic loss of consciousness, fever, headache, seizures, or coma (p.3584).","Bilateral and/or severe optic neuritis or severe transverse myelitis should raise NMOSD, MOGAD, or GFAP autoimmunity rather than MS (p.3584).","Uveitis is uncommon in MS and should suggest sarcoidosis or lymphoma (p.3577).","Intense inflammatory swelling can produce a mass lesion mimicking tumor (tumefactive MS), sometimes requiring brain biopsy (p.3584, 3589)."],"prognosis":"The clinical course is highly variable. RRMS accounts for ~90% of cases with attacks evolving over days to weeks and substantial early recovery; PPMS (~10%) declines steadily from onset, begins later (mean ~40 years), has a more even sex ratio, and accrues disability faster (p.3578-3580). In the pretreatment era, by 15 years after onset only ~20% had no functional limitation and one-third to one-half of relapsing patients had progressed to SPMS needing ambulatory assistance, with ~80% reaching that level by 25 years; the prior RRMS-to-SPMS conversion rate of ~3%/year has fallen to <1%/year in the high-efficacy-therapy era (p.3579, 3588). Favorable prognostic features (pretreatment data): optic neuritis or sensory onset, fewer than two relapses in the first year, and minimal impairment at 5 years; adverse predictors: older onset age, male sex, greater disability, and motor signs in the first year. About <10% have a benign variant that never produces disability even untreated (p.3588). Pregnancy: fewer attacks during gestation (especially the third trimester) but more in the first 3 months postpartum, with the overall pregnancy-year course unchanged (p.3588).","pitfalls":["Do not over-diagnose MS from a single isolated event or from MRI white-matter lesions alone - the diagnosis requires dissemination in space and time (clinical, MRI, or CSF evidence) per the McDonald criteria, and there is no single confirmatory test (p.3582-3583).","A pseudoexacerbation from heat/fever/infection (Uhthoff phenomenon) can mimic a relapse but reflects transient conduction block, not a new lesion; treating it as a relapse with steroids is inappropriate (p.3578, 3585).","Failing to distinguish NMOSD, MOGAD, and GFAP autoimmunity from MS - clues are bilateral/severe optic neuritis, severe longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis (>=3 vertebral segments), brain MRI lesions that are non-destructive/cloud-like and can fully resolve, and CSF pleocytosis greater than in MS with neutrophils/eosinophils and oligoclonal bands in <20% (p.3584, 3590).","ADEM should be considered for hyperacute or postinfectious demyelinating presentations rather than assuming a first MS attack (p.3584).","MS-associated genetic variants (including HLA-DRB1*1501) lack the sensitivity and specificity to be used for diagnosis (p.3581).","Highly restricted CNS oligoclonal antibodies are characteristic of MS but their specific antigenic targets differ between patients and their pathogenic role is uncertain - they aid diagnosis, not mechanistic certainty (p.3581).","Lhermitte's symptom and trigeminal neuralgia are not specific to MS and occur with other cervical cord/cranial nerve pathology (p.3578)."],"severityClassification":"Course-based subtypes: relapsing-remitting MS (RRMS, ~90%), secondary progressive MS (SPMS, always evolves from RRMS), and primary progressive MS (PPMS, ~10%); 'relapsing MS (RMS)' covers all relapsing patients and 'active progressive MS' denotes SPMS/PPMS with ongoing relapses or new MRI lesions (p.3578-3579). Neurologic impairment is graded with the Expanded Disability Status Scale (EDSS), 0 (normal) to 10 (death), built from Functional Status (FS) subscores; EDSS <3.5 generally indicates normal walking and little disability, whereas EDSS >4.0 indicates gait-impaired progressive (SPMS/PPMS) disease. An EDSS >=4 plus a motor FS score >=2 supports an SPMS diagnosis (p.3579-3580, 3585). Fulminant clinical variants include Marburg's (acute fulminant) MS, tumefactive MS, and Balo's concentric sclerosis (p.3589).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3577","Harrison 22e p.3578","Harrison 22e p.3579","Harrison 22e p.3580","Harrison 22e p.3581","Harrison 22e p.3582","Harrison 22e p.3583","Harrison 22e p.3584","Harrison 22e p.3585","Harrison 22e p.3588","Harrison 22e p.3589","Harrison 22e p.3590"],"crossLinks":["ENCEPHALITIS","cord_compression","gbs"]},"myasthenic_crisis":{"name":"Myasthenic crisis","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Chapter 459 (Myasthenia Gravis and Other Diseases of the Neuromuscular Junction), p.3624-3633","clinicalPearls":["Myasthenic crisis is an exacerbation of weakness severe enough to endanger life, typically with ventilatory failure from diaphragmatic and intercostal muscle weakness; by definition, ventilatory weakness requiring intubation or noninvasive ventilation to avoid intubation constitutes crisis, whereas lesser worsening is termed exacerbation (p.3625, 3632).","Weakness is characteristically fatigable: it worsens with repeated use and late in the day and improves with rest or sleep, and strength can improve with serial effort (contrast with LEMS) (p.3625).","Cranial muscles are often involved early, with diplopia and ptosis common initial symptoms; facial weakness gives a 'snarling' expression on attempted smile, and bulbar involvement causes nasal/'mushy' dysarthria, hoarseness, and dysphagia with risk of nasal regurgitation or aspiration (p.3625).","Bulbar, neck, and ventilatory weakness can be especially prominent in MuSK antibody-positive MG; neck extensor weakness can cause head drop (p.3625).","Deep tendon reflexes are typically preserved and sensory symptoms, sensory loss, and pain are absent in MG (p.3625).","Anti-AChR antibodies are present in ~85% of generalized MG and ~50% of ocular MG; a positive test is virtually diagnostic, but a negative test does not exclude MG, and antibody level does not correlate well with severity (p.3627).","About 10% of MG patients (~40% of AChR-negative generalized MG) have anti-MuSK antibodies, and 1-3% have anti-LRP4; ~10% of generalized MG is double-seronegative (p.3625, 3627).","The ice-pack test (ice over a ptotic lid for 2 min, positive if lid rises >=2 mm) is a quick, very sensitive bedside test for an NMJ cause of ptosis (p.3627).","Edrophonium (anticholinesterase) testing was historically used for diagnosis but is no longer used due to side effects and lack of availability (p.3627).","Thymic abnormality occurs in ~75% of AChR-positive patients; thymoma is present in ~10-15% of MG patients, so chest CT/MRI is performed at diagnosis (thymic enlargement in a patient >40 years is highly suspicious for thymoma) (p.3625, 3630)."],"pathophysiology":"MG is an autoimmune neuromuscular junction disorder in which the number of available postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors (AChRs) is reduced and postsynaptic folds are flattened/simplified, decreasing the efficiency of neuromuscular transmission; ACh is released normally but produces small end-plate potentials that may fail to trigger muscle action potentials. The normal decline in ACh release on repeated activity (presynaptic rundown) combined with reduced transmission efficiency activates fewer fibers with successive impulses, producing fatigable weakness (myasthenic fatigue) and the decremental response to repetitive nerve stimulation. Anti-AChR antibodies (IgG, T-cell dependent) reduce receptors by three mechanisms: accelerated AChR turnover via cross-linking/endocytosis, complement-mediated damage to the postsynaptic membrane, and blockade of the ACh-binding site. Anti-MuSK antibodies (IgG4, non-complement-activating) impair MuSK-LRP4 binding and AChR clustering, so MuSK-positive MG does not respond to complement inhibition; anti-LRP4 antibodies (IgG1) cause complement-mediated destruction. Thymic abnormality (hyperplasia in ~65%, thymoma in ~10%) and dysfunction of regulatory T cells contribute to the autoimmune process (p.3624-3626).","additionalDifferentials":["Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS): presynaptic P/Q-type calcium-channel antibody disorder; proximal lower-limb weakness predominates, reflexes are depressed/absent, autonomic features (dry mouth, orthostasis, impotence) occur, and an incremental response follows high-rate stimulation or brief exercise; associated with small-cell lung cancer in older adults (p.3628).","Congenital myasthenic syndromes (non-autoimmune, >30 genes; usually onset in infancy/childhood, AChR and MuSK antibodies absent) (p.3627-3628).","Drug-induced myasthenia, including penicillamine-associated MG and immune checkpoint inhibitor-related MG (p.3627, 3633).","Botulism (Clostridium botulinum toxin): myasthenia-like bulbar weakness with dilated/poorly reactive pupils and autonomic features; CMAP amplitudes increase after high-frequency stimulation (p.3628).","Hyperthyroidism/Graves disease and thyroid dysfunction, which can also aggravate myasthenic weakness (p.3627-3628, 3630).","Intracranial mass lesion compressing extraocular nerves (e.g., sphenoid ridge meningioma) causing diplopia (p.3628).","Oculopharyngeal dystrophy and progressive external ophthalmoplegia / mitochondrial myopathy (e.g., Kearns-Sayre) (p.3627-3628).","ICI-associated myositis (and myocarditis), which can mimic MG with ocular/bulbar weakness but without a decrementing response on repetitive nerve stimulation (p.3627, 3633)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cholinergic crisis from excessive anticholinesterase medication can mimic worsening MG; it is considered in the differential but is unlikely now because very high cholinesterase-inhibitor doses are rarely used (p.3632).","ICI-associated myositis can clinically resemble MG (prominent or exclusive ocular weakness) but lacks a decrementing response on repetitive nerve stimulation (p.3633)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Repetitive nerve stimulation showing a decrement of >10% at 2-3 Hz in weak/proximal muscles supports MG; acetylcholinesterase inhibitors should be stopped 12-24 h beforehand (p.3627).","Single-fiber EMG (jitter and blocking with normal fiber density) when repetitive stimulation is normal or symptoms are exclusively ocular; confirmatory but not specific (p.3627).","Ice-pack test for improvement in ptosis (very sensitive) (p.3627).","Anti-MuSK and (if AChR/MuSK negative with clear MG phenotype) anti-LRP4 antibody testing (p.3627).","Chest CT or MRI to evaluate for thymoma/thymic abnormality at diagnosis (p.3627, 3630).","Thyroid function tests, antinuclear antibodies, and rheumatoid factor to detect associated/exacerbating conditions; testing for tuberculosis, fasting glucose/HbA1c, and bone densitometry before immunotherapy (p.3630).","MRI of the brain/orbits to exclude intracranial lesions in ocular or cranial presentations (p.3627-3628)."],"redFlags":["Ventilatory failure from diaphragmatic/intercostal weakness is the life-threatening feature defining crisis; need for intubation or noninvasive ventilation to avoid intubation marks the transition from exacerbation to crisis (p.3625, 3632).","Intercurrent infection is the most common cause of crisis and must be treated immediately, as the patient's mechanical and immunologic defenses are compromised; fever with early infection should be managed as in an immunocompromised patient (p.3632).","Exposure to drugs that exacerbate weakness (e.g., aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, macrolides, beta-blockers, magnesium, procainamide, quinine derivatives, neuromuscular blocking agents) can precipitate or worsen crisis (p.3633).","Tapering of MG therapies, unrelated infections, systemic disorders, or surgery can precipitate exacerbation or crisis; some exacerbations have no identifiable precipitant (p.3625).","Prominent bulbar/neck weakness (notably in MuSK-positive MG) signals aspiration and airway risk (p.3625)."],"prognosis":"Mortality from MG has fallen dramatically over the last century (from ~70% a century ago to ~2-30% by the 1950s) to contemporary estimates of ~1-5%. About 20% of patients achieve sustained remission and can be tapered off immunotherapy, with no clear correlation between disease severity and likelihood of remission. Anti-MuSK MG was historically harder to treat, but rituximab has improved its prognosis. Most patients can be returned to full productive lives with proper therapy (p.3629, 3633).","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish myasthenic crisis from cholinergic crisis (deterioration due to excessive anticholinesterase medication); the latter is now unlikely because very high cholinesterase-inhibitor doses are rarely used but should still be considered (p.3632).","Treating before the diagnosis is confirmed: confirmation is essential because other treatable conditions closely resemble MG and treatment involves surgery and long-term drugs with side effects (p.3625).","Interpreting a negative anti-AChR antibody test as excluding MG, or using antibody level to gauge severity (it correlates poorly) (p.3627).","Overlooking that anti-MuSK MG generally responds poorly to anticholinesterase agents and may actually worsen with them (p.3629).","Ordering anti-striated muscle (titin) antibodies expecting them to confirm MG or thymoma: they are non-pathogenic and do not confirm either (p.3627)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison distinguishes ocular MG (weakness confined to ocular muscles; if restricted for 3 years, generalization is unlikely) from generalized MG (facial, bulbar, axial, or limb involvement, occurring in ~85%). Worsening is graded as exacerbation (any deterioration short of needing ventilatory support) versus myasthenic crisis (life-threatening weakness, usually with ventilatory failure requiring intubation or noninvasive ventilation to avoid intubation) (p.3625, 3632).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3624","Harrison 22e p.3625","Harrison 22e p.3626","Harrison 22e p.3627","Harrison 22e p.3628","Harrison 22e p.3629","Harrison 22e p.3630","Harrison 22e p.3632","Harrison 22e p.3633"],"crossLinks":["gbs","thyroid_storm"]},"myeloma":{"name":"Multiple myeloma","system":"Hematology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.116 Plasma Cell Disorders, p.883-890 (PDF PAGE 926-933); hypercalcemia of malignancy Ch.57","clinicalPearls":["Bone pain is the most common presenting symptom, affecting nearly 70% of patients; lesions are purely lytic (osteoblast suppression by DKK-1), so a radioisotope bone scan is LESS useful than plain radiography (p.886-887).","Serum alkaline phosphatase is typically NORMAL despite extensive bony disease because osteoblastic activity is suppressed (p.889).","Myeloma bone lesions are classically 'punched-out' lytic lesions (e.g. skull); diffuse osteopenia may also occur (p.887-889).","A reduced or negative anion gap may be a clue: the cationic M protein causes chloride retention; an associated pseudohyponatremia reflects reduced serum water from the high protein load (p.887).","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is characteristically elevated (p.889).","Urine dipstick is unreliable for light chains and the heat test for Bence Jones protein is falsely negative in ~50% of light-chain myeloma; a 24-h urine and serum free light chains are needed (p.888-889).","About 20% of myelomas secrete only light chains; <1% are truly non-secretory (M protein undetectable even by serum free light chain assay) (p.884, p.888-889).","Median age at diagnosis is ~69 years; uncommon under age 40; nearly twice the incidence in Black versus White populations; male predominance (p.885).","Susceptibility to infection is driven by hypogammaglobulinemia (of the non-M-component immunoglobulins), impaired antibody responses to polysaccharide antigens, and granulocyte/complement/T-cell dysfunction (p.886).","Most common infections are pneumonia (S. pneumoniae, S. aureus, K. pneumoniae) and pyelonephritis/UTI (E. coli and other gram-negatives) (p.886).","Renal light-chain cast nephropathy, light chain deposition disease, and AL amyloidosis are at least partially reversible with effective antimyeloma therapy (p.887).","Patients are prone to acute kidney injury when dehydrated or after iodinated contrast; NSAIDs and hypercalcemia further worsen renal function (p.887).","Plasma cell leukemia (>2000 circulating plasma cells/microL) is rare (~1%) but disproportionately frequent in IgD (12%) and IgE (25%) myeloma (p.889)."],"pathophysiology":"Multiple myeloma is a malignant proliferation of a single plasma-cell clone. Myeloma cells adhere to bone marrow stromal cells and extracellular matrix, triggering cytokine loops (IL-6, IGF-1, VEGF) and signaling (Ras/Raf/MAPK, PI3K/Akt, PKC) that drive growth, survival, drug resistance, and migration. Osteoclast-activating factors (RANK ligand, MIP-1alpha, IL-1, lymphotoxin, TNF, VEGF) increase bone resorption while DKK-1 suppresses osteoblasts, producing purely lytic bone disease, hypercalcemia, fractures, and cord compression. The immune microenvironment is remodeled (expansion of MDSCs, plasmacytoid dendritic cells, TH17; suppression of effective antimyeloma T/B/NKT responses). Recurrent cytogenetic events include hyperdiploidy (trisomies) in ~half, and 14q32 IGH translocations [t(11;14), t(4;14), t(14;16)] in the rest, plus del13q, 1q amplification/1p deletion, and del17p; KRAS/NRAS are the most frequently mutated genes (~20% each), with TP53, DIS3, FAM46C, BRAF in 5-10%. No single unifying mutation exists; subclonal heterogeneity present at diagnosis drives genomic evolution. All myeloma is preceded by MGUS (p.883-885).","additionalDifferentials":["Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS) - serum M protein <30 g/L, clonal marrow plasma cells <10%, no myeloma-defining events; ~1%/yr progression (p.887, Table 116-1)","Smoldering (asymptomatic) multiple myeloma - M protein >=30 g/L or urine M protein >=500 mg/24h and/or marrow plasma cells 10-60%, without myeloma-defining events (p.887-888, Table 116-1)","Solitary bone plasmacytoma and solitary extramedullary plasmacytoma (often younger patients; median survival >=10 years; M component in <30%) (p.888)","Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia / other IgM monoclonal gammopathies (p.883)","Primary (AL) amyloidosis (p.883, Ch.117)","POEMS syndrome (polyneuropathy, organomegaly, endocrinopathy, M-protein, skin changes; sclerotic bone lesions)","Reactive/secondary causes of an M component: CLL and B-/T-cell lymphomas, CML, breast and colon cancer, cirrhosis, sarcoidosis, parasitic disease, Gaucher disease, rheumatoid arthritis, myasthenia gravis, cold agglutinin disease, post-transplant (p.883)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Metastatic carcinoma with lytic bone lesions (e.g. breast) (p.887)","Other causes of hypercalcemia of malignancy (PTHrP-mediated); myeloma must be specifically excluded with serum free light chains as ~3% are missed by serum/urine electrophoresis (Ch.57, p.349-350 region)","Other causes of marrow-infiltrative normocytic anemia in the elderly (p.886)","MGUS or smoldering myeloma misclassified as active disease without myeloma-defining events (p.887)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Bone marrow aspirate AND core biopsy with kappa/lambda clonality (flow cytometry or immunohistochemistry); marrow plasma cells are CD138+ (p.887, Table 116-2)","Serum protein electrophoresis with immunofixation; quantitative IgG/IgA/IgM (p.888-889, Table 116-2)","Serum free light chains and involved:uninvolved ratio (p.888-889, Table 116-2)","24-h urine protein electrophoresis and immunofixation to quantify Bence Jones protein (p.888-889, Table 116-2)","Immunofixation for IgD or IgE in selected cases (p.889, Table 116-2)","Chemistry panel: calcium, creatinine/urea, uric acid; CBC; LDH (p.889, Table 116-2)","Serum beta-2-microglobulin and albumin for ISS staging (p.889, Table 116-2/116-3)","FISH or DNA sequencing for del17p/TP53, t(4;14), t(11;14), t(14;16), t(14;20), amp1q, del1p, del13 (p.889, Table 116-2/116-3)","Whole-body skeletal imaging: skeletal survey, low-dose CT, or 18F-FDG PET/CT (PET/CT standard to distinguish smoldering from active disease and to confirm solitary plasmacytoma); MRI spine/pelvis for marrow infiltration and cord/root compression (p.889, Table 116-2)","Serum viscosity if IgM, high IgA, or serum M component >7 g/dL (p.889, Table 116-2)","Abdominal fat-pad aspirate for amyloid in selected cases (p.889, Table 116-2)"],"redFlags":["Spinal cord compression from vertebral collapse or plasmacytoma - radicular pain, loss of bowel/bladder control (oncologic emergency) (p.887)","Symptomatic hypercalcemia - lethargy, weakness, confusion, AKI (p.886-887)","Acute kidney injury, especially with dehydration, contrast, NSAIDs, or hypercalcemia (p.887)","Hyperviscosity syndrome - headache, visual disturbance, mucosal bleeding, somnolence, heart failure, retinopathy; symptoms above ~4 cP, most common with IgM > IgG3 > IgA (p.887)","Sepsis / serious bacterial infection (recurrent infection is the presenting feature in ~25%) (p.886)","Pathologic fracture (p.887)"],"prognosis":"Serum beta-2-microglobulin is the single most powerful survival predictor; combined with albumin it defines the 3-stage International Staging System (ISS): Stage I (b2M <3.5 mg/L, albumin >=3.5 g/dL) median survival ~62 months; Stage II ~44 months; Stage III (b2M >5.5 mg/L) ~29 months. High LDH, circulating plasma cells, high labeling index, and poor performance status indicate worse outcome. Cytogenetic risk: high-risk = del(17p) (>=20% clonality)/TP53 mutation, t(4;14)/t(14;16)/t(14;20) with 1q gain or 1p deletion, biallelic/del1p with 1q gain, and high b2M with normal creatinine (~20-25% of patients, expected survival 3-4 years); standard-risk includes t(11;14), del(13), hyperdiploidy (~75-80%, survival 8-10+ years). del(13q) and t(11;14) are NOT high-risk markers. MGUS progresses ~1%/yr; SMM higher (high-risk SMM ~76% at 5 years). Solitary plasmacytomas carry median survival >=10 years. The Durie-Salmon system is no longer used (p.888-889).","pitfalls":["Relying on urine dipstick (detects albumin, not light chains) or the Bence Jones heat test (false-negative ~50%) to exclude light-chain myeloma - use 24-h urine immunofixation and serum free light chains (p.888-889).","Excluding myeloma in hypercalcemia of malignancy without serum free light chains - ~3% of myeloma is missed by serum/urine electrophoresis with immunofixation.","Ordering a radionuclide bone scan instead of skeletal radiography/CT/PET-CT - lytic lesions lack osteoblastic activity and may be missed (p.887).","Misreading a normal alkaline phosphatase as evidence against bone disease (p.889).","Interpreting hyponatremia at face value - it is often pseudohyponatremia from high serum protein (p.887).","Labeling all monoclonal proteins as myeloma - M components occur in many neoplastic, autoimmune, and reactive conditions and require myeloma-defining events for the diagnosis (p.883, p.887).","Failing to distinguish MGUS/smoldering myeloma from active myeloma; active disease requires marrow clonal plasmacytosis (and/or plasmacytoma) plus a myeloma-defining event (CRAB or a malignancy biomarker)."],"severityClassification":"Diagnosis (IMWG, Table 116-1): clonal marrow plasma cells (or biopsy-proven plasmacytoma) PLUS >=1 myeloma-defining event. CRAB end-organ damage: hyperCalcemia (>2.75 mmol/L or >0.25 mmol/L above ULN); Renal insufficiency (CrCl <40 mL/min or creatinine >177 micromol/L [>2 mg/dL]); Anemia (Hb <100 g/L or >20 g/L below LLN); Bone lesions (>=1 lytic lesion on radiograph/CT/PET-CT). SLiM biomarkers of malignancy (any one): clonal marrow plasma cells >=60%; involved:uninvolved serum free light chain ratio >=100; >1 focal lesion (>=5 mm) on MRI. Staging severity: International Staging System (ISS) I-III by serum beta-2-microglobulin and albumin; cytogenetic high-risk vs standard-risk further stratifies (R-ISS combines ISS with high-risk cytogenetics and LDH). MGUS = M protein <30 g/L, marrow plasma cells <10%, no myeloma-defining events; smoldering myeloma = M protein >=30 g/L (or urine >=500 mg/24h) and/or marrow plasma cells 10-60%, no myeloma-defining events (p.887-889).","references":["Harrison 22e p.883","Harrison 22e p.884","Harrison 22e p.885","Harrison 22e p.886","Harrison 22e p.887","Harrison 22e p.888","Harrison 22e p.889","Harrison 22e Ch.57 (hypercalcemia of malignancy)","IMWG diagnostic criteria"],"crossLinks":["hypercalcemia","aki","anemia_sympt","cord_compression"]},"myxedema":{"name":"Myxoedema coma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3029-3030, 3032 (Hypothyroidism / Myxedema coma); p.3768-3769 (Hypothermia)","clinicalPearls":["Myxedema coma carries a 20-40% mortality despite intensive care, and outcome does not correlate with the degree of T4 or TSH abnormality (p.3032).","It occurs almost exclusively in the elderly, often in winter, and may arise either in a known hypothyroid patient with poor treatment adherence or in someone not previously diagnosed (p.3032).","Hypothermia in myxedema can be profound, reaching as low as 23 degrees C (74 degrees F) (p.3032).","Reduced consciousness is the hallmark and may be accompanied by seizures, alongside the usual signs of hypothyroidism (p.3032).","A distinguishing examination clue in hypothyroidism is a deep tendon reflex (e.g., Achilles) whose relaxation phase is prolonged out of proportion to the contraction phase (p.3769).","Hypothyroidism, especially extreme as in myxedema coma, is a recognised endocrine cause of hypothermia by reducing metabolic rate and impairing thermogenesis and behavioural responses (p.3768).","Bradycardia, reduced myocardial contractility and stroke volume, and pericardial effusion (up to ~30% of hypothyroid patients) reflect the cardiac depression that underlies the haemodynamic picture (p.3030).","Carotene accumulation can give the skin a yellow tinge, and nonpitting (myxedematous) tissue swelling distinguishes the oedema from pitting fluid overload (p.3029-3030)."],"pathophysiology":"Long-standing thyroid hormone deficiency depresses the metabolic rate and thermogenesis, producing hypothermia, bradycardia, and reduced myocardial contractility. Dermal accumulation of glycosaminoglycans traps water, giving nonpitting myxedematous swelling. In myxedema coma, hypoventilation is central to pathogenesis: it leads to hypoxia and hypercapnia (CO2 retention). Dilutional hyponatremia (reduced renal perfusion with inappropriate vasopressin) and hypoglycemia further contribute. There is impaired adrenal reserve in profound hypothyroidism, and impaired peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion (p.3032; p.3768).","additionalDifferentials":["Sick euthyroid syndrome confounding thyroid function tests in a critically ill or hypothermic patient (p.3769).","Adrenal insufficiency / hypopituitarism, which independently increase susceptibility to hypothermia and may coexist (p.3768).","Hashimoto's encephalopathy - a steroid-responsive syndrome with TPO antibodies, myoclonus and EEG slow-wave activity (relationship to hypothyroidism not established) (p.3030)."],"infectionMimics":["Sepsis should be actively suspected as a precipitant; hypothermia obscures fever and leukocytosis, and infectious rigors may be mistaken for shivering (p.3032, p.3769).","Pneumonia as a respiration-impairing precipitant (p.3032)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Environmental / accidental hypothermia from cold exposure, drugs (ethanol, sedatives, antipsychotics, antidepressants), or malnutrition (p.3768).","Hypoglycemia from insulin or oral hypoglycemic agents, which itself causes hypothermia via neuroglycopenic hypothalamic effects (p.3768)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas to detect hypoxia and hypercapnia from hypoventilation (p.3032).","Glucose to detect contributing hypoglycemia (p.3032).","Septic screen / cultures, since infection is a common precipitant and is masked by hypothermia (p.3032, p.3769).","Core temperature measurement (may be very low, down to ~23 degrees C) (p.3032)."],"redFlags":["Reduced level of consciousness with seizures in a hypothyroid patient (p.3032).","Profound hypothermia (toward 23 degrees C) (p.3032).","Hypoventilation with hypoxia/hypercapnia signalling impending respiratory failure (p.3032).","Severe dilutional hyponatremia and hypoglycemia (p.3032).","An identifiable precipitant such as infection, MI, CHF, GI bleeding, cerebrovascular accident, or respiratory-depressant drugs (p.3032)."],"prognosis":"Mortality remains 20-40% even with intensive treatment, and prognosis is independent of the measured T4 and TSH levels. Hypothermia associated with concurrent sepsis is a poor prognostic sign (p.3032, p.3769).","pitfalls":["Sedatives, anesthetics, and antidepressants can precipitate or worsen myxedema coma, and impaired drug metabolism means standard doses may accumulate (p.3032).","Hypothermia masks fever and leukocytosis, so infection can be missed; rigors may be misread as shivering (p.3769).","Giving parenteral levothyroxine to a euthyroid hypothermic patient is potentially hazardous, so clinical/historical evidence of hypothyroidism should be sought first (p.3769).","Sick euthyroid syndrome can confound thyroid function tests in the acutely ill patient (p.3769)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.3029","Harrison 22e p.3030","Harrison 22e p.3032","Harrison 22e p.3768","Harrison 22e p.3769"],"crossLinks":["adrenal_crisis","hyponatremia","hypoglycemia","SEPSIS"]},"nephrotic":{"name":"Nephrotic syndrome","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e: p.324 (edema in nephrotic syndrome), p.384-385 (proteinuria & vascular causes, Ch.55), p.2356-2359 (proteinuric states & nephrotic syndrome, Ch.319)","clinicalPearls":["Heavy proteinuria (>3.5 g/d) can occur WITHOUT the full nephrotic picture (no hypoalbuminaemia/oedema/hyperlipidaemia) in several renal diseases, notably diabetes; proteinuria is not synonymous with nephrotic syndrome (p.385, p.2357).","Periorbital, facial and eyelid swelling (worst on waking) helps distinguish nephrotic oedema from the dependent oedema/orthopnoea of heart failure; penile and scrotal oedema may be severe enough to obstruct urinary flow in men (p.324, p.2358).","Severe oedema can produce skin bullae that rupture and predispose to ulceration and cellulitis; vocal cord oedema may cause hoarseness (p.2358).","Urine microscopy may show oval fat bodies (lipid-laden tubular epithelial cells), cholesterol monohydrate Maltese-cross crystals under polarised light, and fatty casts (p.2358).","Rapid-onset hypoalbuminaemia may produce horizontal white nail-bed lines (Muehrcke's lines) (p.2358).","Abrupt adult-onset nephrotic syndrome with markedly elevated cholesterol suggests a podocytopathy such as minimal change disease, which is often preceded by upper respiratory infection, allergy or immunisation (p.2358).","Dipstick detects predominantly albumin and the most acidic proteins; it misses kappa/lambda light chains (myeloma), so a normal dipstick does not exclude overflow proteinuria — send urine protein electrophoresis/immunoelectrophoresis (p.385, p.2357).","Nephrotic-range proteinuria without oedema, with rapid renal function loss, raises HIV-associated nephropathy (collapsing glomerulopathy, a severe treatment-resistant FSGS variant), more common in patients of African ancestry (p.2358)."],"pathophysiology":"Nephrotic syndrome reflects increased glomerular permeability to plasma proteins. Minimal change disease causes diffuse fusion (effacement) of podocyte foot processes producing predominantly 'selective' albumin loss, whereas immune-complex disruption of the basement membrane and slit diaphragms yields 'nonselective' loss of albumin and larger proteins. Hypoalbuminaemia results from urinary loss plus increased proximal-tubule catabolism of filtered albumin, exceeding hepatic synthesis (a hypercatabolic, negative-nitrogen-balance state). Oedema arises from renal sodium retention and reduced plasma oncotic pressure; perceived reduction in effective intravascular volume activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, AVP and the sympathetic nervous system, and filtered proteases can directly activate the epithelial Na channel (ENaC) in principal cells, promoting further salt/water retention. Hyperlipidaemia/hypercholesterolaemia reflect increased hepatic lipoprotein synthesis. Notably, hypertension is uncommon in primary kidney diseases causing nephrotic syndrome (p.385). Hereditary nephrotic syndrome can result from podocyte slit-diaphragm gene mutations (nephrin NPHS1, podocin NPHS2); APOL1 risk variants predispose to FSGS, with disease expression triggered by a 'second hit' of infection/inflammation damaging podocytes and glomerular endothelium (p.2357-2358).","additionalDifferentials":["Minimal change disease (idiopathic; secondary to Hodgkin's lymphoma or other lymphoproliferative disorders) — predominant cause in children <6 years (p.2358)","Primary FSGS / podocytopathy versus secondary FSGS (remnant kidney after partial nephrectomy, adaptive injury, chronic hypoxaemia, sickle cell disease, obesity, heroin, congenital urogenital anomalies) (p.2358)","Membranous nephropathy — ~70% of primary cases associated with anti-PLA2R antibodies; secondary forms with malignancy (GI, prostate, breast, lung, bladder), SLE, drugs (gold, penicillamine, NSAIDs), infections (p.2358 Fig.319-3)","Membranoproliferative / C3 glomerulopathy and dense-deposit disease (hypocomplementaemia); secondary to SLE, cryoglobulins, SBE (p.2358 Fig.319-3)","Diabetic glomerulosclerosis (subnephrotic albuminuria progressing to nephrotic range) (p.324, p.2356)","Amyloidosis (AL light-chain, AA/SAA from chronic inflammation) (p.2358 Fig.319-3)","Multiple myeloma / plasma cell dyscrasia with light-chain (overflow) proteinuria not detected by dipstick (p.385, p.2357)"],"infectionMimics":["HIV-associated nephropathy (collapsing FSGS) (p.2358)","Postinfectious glomerulonephritis and COVID-19-associated glomerular injury (p.2358 Fig.319-3)","Subacute bacterial endocarditis (immune-complex glomerulonephritis) (p.2358 Fig.319-3)","Hepatitis C-associated cryoglobulinaemic disease (p.2358)","Schistosomiasis (p.2358 Fig.319-3)","Quartan malarial nephropathy — chronic Plasmodium malariae immune-complex injury causing focal/segmental glomerulonephritis with capillary basement-membrane splitting, mainly in children, responds poorly to therapy (p.~1670 malaria chapter)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Heart failure oedema — dependent with orthopnoea, contrasts with periorbital/facial nephrotic oedema (p.324, p.2358)","Hepatic cirrhosis with hypoalbuminaemia and oedema/ascites (p.325)","Severe malnutrition and protein-losing enteropathy as alternative hypoalbuminaemic states (p.324)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Spot morning urine albumin-to-creatinine or protein-to-creatinine ratio (normalised to creatinine to discount urine dilution) or 24-h urine protein/albumin — preferred over dipstick for quantification (p.385, p.2357)","Serum and urine protein immunoelectrophoresis / UPEP to detect light chains when myeloma is possible (dipstick misses light chains) (p.385, p.2357)","Urine microscopy for oval fat bodies, Maltese-cross lipid crystals and fatty casts (p.2358)","Anti-PLA2R antibody testing in suspected primary membranous nephropathy (p.2358)","Complement levels (membranoproliferative/C3 glomerulopathy is hypocomplementaemic) (p.2358)","Imaging/angiography for suspected renal vein thrombosis presenting with heavy proteinuria and haematuria (p.384)"],"redFlags":["Heavy proteinuria with new haematuria suggesting renal vein thrombosis (p.384)","Rapidly declining renal function with very heavy proteinuria and no oedema suggesting collapsing glomerulopathy/HIVAN (p.2358)","Cellulitis or ulceration from ruptured oedema bullae (p.2358)","Hoarseness from vocal cord oedema or scrotal/penile oedema obstructing urinary flow (p.2358)"],"prognosis":"Glomerular proteinuria in diabetes typically appears after years of small-vessel disease (hence frequent coexistent retinopathy) and predicts progression to nephrotic syndrome and renal failure; the rate of progression is faster with hypertension, obesity and poor glucose control. Diffuse glomerular involvement (most glomeruli) makes nephrotic syndrome likely, whereas focal disease (<50% of glomeruli) tends to give subnephrotic albuminuria. In membranous nephropathy, ~70% of primary cases are anti-PLA2R associated. Collapsing glomerulopathy (HIVAN) is a severe, treatment-resistant FSGS variant. Quartan malarial nephropathy responds poorly to therapy (p.2356-2358).","pitfalls":["Assuming any nephrotic-range proteinuria equals classic nephrotic syndrome — heavy proteinuria can occur without hypoalbuminaemia/oedema (p.385).","Relying on dipstick alone — it misses light chains, so myeloma-associated overflow proteinuria can be overlooked; total protein:creatinine ratio is not specific for albumin (p.385, p.2357).","Equating subnephrotic albuminuria with absence of significant glomerular disease — focal disease causes lower albuminuria yet can be significant (p.2357).","Misreading biopsy-proven FSGS as a single entity — primary podocytopathic FSGS, secondary/adaptive FSGS, and post-GN healing scars are distinct (p.2356, p.2398)."],"severityClassification":"Albuminuria categories (Ch.55 Fig.55-3): moderately increased 30-300 mg/d or mg/g (formerly microalbuminuria); severely increased 300-3500 mg/d or mg/g (formerly macroalbuminuria); nephrotic range >3500 mg/d or mg/g. Full nephrotic syndrome defined by the triad of oedema, hypoalbuminaemia (<3.5 g/dL) and proteinuria >3.5 g/d, usually with lipid abnormalities (high LDL, low HDL, lipiduria) (p.385, p.2357).","references":["Harrison 22e p.324","Harrison 22e p.325","Harrison 22e p.384","Harrison 22e p.385","Harrison 22e p.2356","Harrison 22e p.2357","Harrison 22e p.2358"],"crossLinks":["glomerulonephritis","ckd","dvt","myeloma"]},"opioid_od":{"name":"Opioid overdose","system":"Toxicology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"p.3688-3707","clinicalPearls":["The classic overdose picture is shallow/slow respirations, miosis, bradycardia, hypothermia, and stupor or coma; toxicology can confirm but immediate management is clinical (p.3690).","Pinpoint pupils are characteristic, BUT mydriasis (dilated pupils) appears once significant cerebral anoxia supervenes — dilated pupils do not exclude opioid toxicity in a deteriorating patient (p.3690).","Hypoxia, not the drug itself, is what kills: in opioid and sedative-hypnotic poisoning, vital-sign changes are secondary to CNS depression or consequent hypoxemia and do not appear until consciousness is markedly reduced (grade 3-4 depression) (p.3748).","Aspiration pneumonia is common in overdose because of loss of the gag reflex (p.3733).","Opioids generally do NOT cause seizures, except in polydrug use, with meperidine, with high-dose tramadol, or in the newborn (p.3690).","Fentanyl and its derivatives may require severalfold higher reversal doses than other opioids, and adulteration of illicit fentanyl with xylazine markedly worsens respiratory suppression (p.3689, p.3733).","Prompt reversal of altered mental status and vital signs within minutes of IV naloxone is virtually diagnostic of opioid poisoning (alongside dextrose for hypoglycemia and flumazenil for benzodiazepine intoxication) (p.3748).","Fluoroquinolones commonly cause false-positive opiate urine screens; rapid urine drug screens are screening-only, prone to false results, and miss agents such as kratom (p.3748).","When naloxone has only a limited effect, suspect a co-ingested sedative — most commonly a benzodiazepine, which has caused fatal overdoses in combination with buprenorphine (p.3691)."],"pathophysiology":"Opioids act mainly through the mu receptor (also kappa, delta, nociceptin/orphanin), all G protein-coupled, inhibiting the cAMP system and opening GIRK potassium channels to hyperpolarize and silence neurons. Life-threatening respiratory depression results from opioid-induced insensitivity of brainstem (medullary) respiratory neurons to rising carbon dioxide; analgesia and respiratory depression are mediated by receptors in the periaqueductal grey and medulla oblongata. Untreated, progression goes to respiratory and then cardiovascular collapse and death (p.3733, p.3690).","additionalDifferentials":["Cholinergic (muscarinic/nicotinic) poisoning — also causes miosis plus a depressed physiologic state (p.3748)","Sedative-hypnotic / GABAergic poisoning (alcohols, barbiturates, benzodiazepines, GHB) — depressed state, distinguished as naloxone-unresponsive (p.3748)","Sympatholytic poisoning (alpha-2 agonists e.g. clonidine, beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, cardiac glycosides) (p.3747)","Other causes of coma including hypoglycemia (reversed by dextrose) (p.3748)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hypoglycemia (responds to dextrose, mimics opioid coma) (p.3748)","Benzodiazepine intoxication (responds to flumazenil) (p.3748)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood/urine toxicology can confirm a suspected diagnosis but must not delay clinical management (p.3690)","ECG to detect QT prolongation (e.g. methadone), which can cause sudden death (p.3733)","Chest imaging for noncardiogenic pulmonary edema / ARDS, a recognized complication of opioid poisoning (p.3749)","In IV users, screen for blood-borne infection (hepatitis B, HIV) and bacterial complications (meningitis, osteomyelitis, abscesses, endocarditis) (p.3733)"],"redFlags":["Dilated pupils in a suspected opioid overdose suggest cerebral anoxia has already occurred (p.3690)","Re-sedation: naloxone lasts only a few hours while most opioids (especially methadone, sustained-release/'toxic time-bomb' formulations) last far longer (p.3691, p.3747)","Noncardiogenic pulmonary edema / ARDS (p.3749)","Methadone-associated QT prolongation and sudden death (p.3733)","Necrosis and limb-loss risk with fentanyl-xylazine injection (p.3733)"],"prognosis":"Potentially lethal but rapidly reversible if respiratory depression is recognized and supported; untreated overdose progresses to respiratory and cardiovascular collapse and death. Opioid use disorder is chronic and relapsing, and most patients relapse during or after detoxification (p.3690, p.3691). Some opioid intoxications can leave delayed diffuse white-matter injury producing a persistent coma syndrome (p.232).","pitfalls":["Do not assume normal-sized or dilated pupils exclude opioids — mydriasis does NOT occur until significant brain anoxia supervenes (p.3690).","Significant vital-sign abnormalities do NOT occur in opioid poisoning until there is a marked decrease in level of consciousness, so early near-normal vitals are falsely reassuring (p.3748).","A negative urine drug screen does NOT rule out opioid toxicity (concentration too low, or agent such as kratom not detected); opiate screens also give false positives (e.g. fluoroquinolones) (p.3748).","Primary buprenorphine overdose is nearly impossible because it is a partial agonist with increasing antagonist activity at higher doses; failure to respond to naloxone should prompt search for a co-ingested sedative (p.3690-3691).","Be sure a patient is not also withdrawing from alcohol or sedatives, which may be more dangerous to manage than opioid withdrawal (p.3733)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames poisoning severity by physiologic state (Tables 470-1/470-2): opioids produce a 'depressed' physiologic state (decreased pulse, BP, respiratory rate, temperature, neuromuscular activity, with miosis). Severity is graded by depth of CNS/physiologic depression (grades 1-4); marked vital-sign abnormalities indicate grade 3-4 depression (p.3747-3748). Note: opioid WITHDRAWAL is a separate 'stimulated' syndrome — onset 8-10 h after the last dose with lacrimation, rhinorrhea, yawning and sweating, progressing to gooseflesh, nausea/vomiting, myalgia, hyperpnea, hyperthermia, hypertension, mydriasis over a 7-10 day acute course (p.3690).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3688-3691 (Ch.467 Opioid-Related Disorders)","Harrison 22e p.3747-3750 (Ch.470 Poisoning and Drug Overdose)","Harrison 22e p.232 (Coma)"],"crossLinks":["drug_intox","serotonin_nms"]},"organophosphate":{"name":"Organophosphate / cholinergic poisoning","system":"Toxicology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"p.3610, p.3620, p.3705-3713","clinicalPearls":["Belongs to the depressed physiologic state / cholinergic toxidrome; miosis is common and is most pronounced in opioid and cholinergic poisoning, distinguished from other depressant syndromes by muscarinic and nicotinic signs (p.3705).","Muscarinic features: seizures, excessive secretions (lacrimation, salivation, bronchorrhoea and wheezing, diaphoresis), and increased bowel/bladder activity with nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, abdominal cramps, and incontinence of faeces and urine (p.3712).","Nicotinic features: hypertension, tachycardia, muscle cramps, fasciculations, weakness, and paralysis — note these oppose the muscarinic picture, so vital signs can be mixed (p.3712).","Death is usually due to respiratory failure (p.3712).","Organophosphates and carbamates can produce bradycardia and atrioventricular block on ECG (p.3706).","Organophosphate (and amanita/mushroom) poisoning can present as acute, often systemic-compromising diarrhoea (p.46/Acute Diarrhea chapter).","Organophosphates can cause a delayed organophosphate-induced neuropathy: early generalised weakness from neuromuscular blockade, then an axonal sensorimotor polyneuropathy ensues (p.3610)."],"pathophysiology":"Organophosphate (and carbamate) insecticides, and nerve agents such as sarin, soman, tabun and VX, inhibit acetylcholinesterase, leading to accumulation of synaptic acetylcholine at both muscarinic and nicotinic cholinergic receptor sites; the resulting overstimulation produces the combined muscarinic, nicotinic and CNS toxidrome. Organophosphates are 'toxic time-bombs' in that they may require metabolic activation, so onset of toxicity can be delayed. Separately, organophosphates bind and inhibit neuropathy target esterase, with axonal degeneration of the gracile fasciculus and corticospinal tracts producing a delayed sensorimotor polyneuropathy. (p.3610, p.3712, p.3704)","additionalDifferentials":["Carbamate insecticide poisoning (aldicarb, carbaryl, propoxur) — same cholinergic mechanism (p.3712)","Other acetylcholinesterase inhibitors: medicinal agents neostigmine, physostigmine, tacrine (p.3712)","Muscarinic agonists: bethanechol, pilocarpine, and muscarine-containing mushrooms (Boletus, Clitocybe, Inocybe spp.) (p.3712)","Nicotine / tobacco and lobeline (nicotinic agonists) (p.3712)","Opioid poisoning (also causes miosis and physiologic depression) (p.3705)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Guillain-Barré syndrome / acute flaccid paralysis — organophosphate, thallium and arsenic poisonings are listed in the GBS differential (p.3620)","Botulism and myasthenia gravis (neuromuscular junction disorders in the acute paralysis differential) (p.3620)","Paralytic shellfish poisoning, ciguatera, type E botulism and tetrodotoxin poisoning — organophosphate poisoning is in their shared differential (ciguatera chapter)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Cholinesterase activity in plasma and red cells is <50% of normal in acetylcholinesterase-inhibitor poisoning (p.3712)","ECG — may show bradycardia and atrioventricular block (p.3706)","EMG/nerve conduction studies in delayed neuropathy — early repetitive firing of CMAPs and decrement on repetitive nerve stimulation; later, axonal sensorimotor polyneuropathy (p.3610)"],"redFlags":["Bronchorrhoea, wheezing and excessive secretions with progression to respiratory failure — the usual cause of death (p.3712)","Nicotinic weakness, fasciculations and paralysis (p.3712)","Seizures (p.3712)","Bradycardia / atrioventricular block (p.3706)"],"prognosis":"Death is usually due to respiratory failure (p.3712). Outcome and reversibility of any poisoning depend on the functional reserve of the individual or target organ, influenced by age and preexisting disease (p.3704).","pitfalls":["Vital signs may be discordant: muscarinic effects cause bradycardia while nicotinic stimulation can cause hypertension and tachycardia, so a 'normal' or mixed heart rate does not exclude poisoning (p.3712).","Organophosphates are 'toxic time-bombs' requiring metabolic activation, so a patient may be initially well or minimally symptomatic and deteriorate later (p.3704).","Cholinesterase activity is <50% of normal in acetylcholinesterase-inhibitor poisoning — a key confirmatory threshold not to overlook (p.3712)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison grades poisoning by degree of physiologic depression rather than by a disease-specific scale: grade 1 — arousable by voice/touch, can converse and follow commands; grade 2 — responds to pain but not voice, brainstem reflexes intact; grade 3 — unresponsive to pain, brainstem reflexes depressed, decreased respirations and temperature; grade 4 — flaccid paralysis with absent brainstem reflexes and respirations and decreased cardiovascular vital signs (p.3705).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3610","Harrison 22e p.3620","Harrison 22e p.3705","Harrison 22e p.3706","Harrison 22e p.3712"],"crossLinks":["drug_intox"]},"pancreatitis":{"name":"Acute pancreatitis","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.358-359, p.2738-2749 (esp. p.2744-2749)","clinicalPearls":["Diagnosis requires 2 of 3: typical epigastric pain radiating to the back, serum lipase/amylase >=3x ULN, or characteristic findings on cross-sectional imaging (Harrison 22e p.2745).","Lipase is more pancreas-specific than amylase and is the single best enzyme to measure; it stays elevated longer (7-14 days) whereas amylase often normalises by 3-7 days (Harrison 22e p.2740, p.2745).","Gallstones and alcohol together account for ~80-90% of cases; gallstones are the single most common cause (30-60%), alcohol the second (15-30%) (Harrison 22e p.2744).","ALT >3x ULN strongly points to a gallstone aetiology in acute pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2745).","Abdominal ultrasound is the recommended initial imaging study, chiefly to assess for gallstones and bile-duct dilation; contrast CT is best deferred to ~3-5 days if the patient is not improving and is usually not needed in the first 48-72 h (Harrison 22e p.2745-2746).","Persistent organ failure (>48 h) is the most important determinant of severity; if SIRS has resolved by 24 h, organ failure or necrosis is unlikely to develop (Harrison 22e p.2746, p.2748).","Hemoconcentration (hematocrit >44%) and a rising BUN are early warning signs of more severe disease and higher mortality (Harrison 22e p.2745-2748).","Cullen's sign (periumbilical bruising) and Grey Turner's sign (flank discoloration) are rare but signal severe necrotizing/hemorrhagic disease (Harrison 22e p.2745)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute pancreatitis is thought to begin with autodigestion: intracellular premature activation of trypsin (via lysosomal cathepsin B colocalising with digestive zymogens) within acinar cells, which then activates other proteolytic and lipolytic enzymes (chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, phospholipase A2). A second phase recruits and sequesters leukocytes/macrophages, amplifying intrapancreatic inflammation, and a third phase results from activated enzymes and cytokines acting on distant organs, producing edema, hemorrhage, vascular damage, fat necrosis and coagulation/cellular necrosis. Release of bradykinin, vasoactive substances and histamine drives vasodilation, increased vascular permeability and edema, and can culminate in SIRS, ARDS and multiorgan failure. Pathologically the spectrum runs from interstitial (perfusion preserved, self-limited) to necrotizing pancreatitis (perfusion interrupted). Genetic susceptibility/modifier variants include PRSS1 (cationic trypsinogen), SPINK1, CFTR, CTRC, CASR and CLDN2, all converging on control of intrapancreatic trypsin activity (Harrison 22e p.2744-2745).","additionalDifferentials":["Perforated viscus, especially peptic ulcer","Acute cholecystitis / biliary colic","Acute intestinal obstruction","Mesenteric vascular occlusion / ischemia","Renal colic","Inferior myocardial infarction","Dissecting aortic aneurysm","Connective-tissue disorders with vasculitis (e.g., SLE, polyarteritis nodosa)","Pneumonia","Diabetic ketoacidosis"],"infectionMimics":["Pneumonia (may present with upper abdominal pain and is in the differential of acute pancreatitis) (Harrison 22e p.2745)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Diabetic ketoacidosis - abdominal pain with raised TOTAL amylase mimics pancreatitis, but lipase is often NOT elevated and pancreatic imaging is normal (Harrison 22e p.2745-2746)","Perforated peptic ulcer - can raise serum/ascitic amylase (Harrison 22e p.2739, p.2745)","Acute cholecystitis / biliary colic - amylase may be raised; pain is more right-sided/epigastric and ileus is usually absent (Harrison 22e p.2745)","Mesenteric ischemia/infarction - can raise ascitic-fluid amylase (Harrison 22e p.2739, p.2745)","Intestinal obstruction - can raise ascitic-fluid amylase; differentiated by crescendo-decrescendo pain and obstruction pattern on CT (Harrison 22e p.2739, p.2745)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum lipase (>3x ULN) - preferred enzyme; more specific than amylase; remains elevated 7-14 days (Harrison 22e p.2740, p.2745)","Liver profile incl. ALT - ALT >3x ULN supports gallstone aetiology (Harrison 22e p.2745)","Serum triglycerides - to detect hypertriglyceridaemic cause (levels usually >1000 mg/dL) (Harrison 22e p.2744, p.2748)","Serum calcium - to detect hypercalcaemic cause and to monitor for hypocalcaemia (~25% of patients) (Harrison 22e p.2744-2745)","Hematocrit - hemoconcentration >44% is a severity marker (Harrison 22e p.2745-2746)","Blood urea nitrogen (BUN) at admission and 48 h - rising BUN predicts mortality (Harrison 22e p.2745, p.2748)","White cell count - leukocytosis 15,000-20,000/uL is common (Harrison 22e p.2745)","MRI/MRCP - more sensitive than CT for mild pancreatitis, necrosis, choledocholithiasis and ductal abnormalities (Harrison 22e p.2739)","EUS - useful for occult microlithiasis/biliary cause and choledocholithiasis; best performed ~4 weeks after resolution to find predisposing lesions (Harrison 22e p.2741)","Contrast-enhanced CT - to assess necrosis and local complications when not improving, ideally at 3-5 days (Harrison 22e p.2745-2746)","C-reactive protein - severity marker during hospitalization (thresholds >100 mg/L reported) (Harrison 22e p.2748)"],"redFlags":["Persistent organ failure >48 h (respiratory, cardiovascular or renal) - hallmark of severe disease (Harrison 22e p.2746)","Modified Marshall organ-failure signs: systolic BP <90 mmHg, HR >130/min, PaO2 <60 mmHg, or creatinine >2.0 mg/dL (Harrison 22e p.2748)","BISAP score >=3 - increased in-hospital mortality (Harrison 22e p.2748)","APACHE II >=8 at 24 h (Harrison 22e p.2748)","Hemoconcentration (hematocrit >44%) and rising/admission BUN - predict severe disease and mortality (Harrison 22e p.2745-2748)","Persistent SIRS at 24 h - predisposes to organ failure and necrosis (Harrison 22e p.2746, p.2748)","Pancreatic necrosis on CT - greatest mortality risk (3-10% with single-organ failure, ~50% with multiorgan failure) (Harrison 22e p.2747)","Hypoxemia (PaO2 <=60 mmHg) - may herald ARDS (Harrison 22e p.2745)","Cullen's sign / Grey Turner's sign - severe necrotizing/hemorrhagic pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2745)","Ascending cholangitis (sepsis + biliary obstruction) complicating gallstone pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2748)"],"prognosis":"Overall mortality is low (~1%) and median hospital stay ~4 days; most cases are mild and self-limited within 3-7 days. Interstitial pancreatitis (90-95% of admissions) carries low mortality, whereas necrotizing pancreatitis (5-10%) drives most deaths. In necrotizing disease the median prevalence of organ failure exceeds 50% (slightly higher with infected vs sterile necrosis); mortality is 3-10% with single-organ failure but approaches 50% with multiorgan failure. Patients with only extrapancreatic necrosis fare better than those with pancreatic parenchymal necrosis. About 25% of patients have a recurrence, most commonly from alcohol or gallstones (Harrison 22e p.2747, p.2749).","pitfalls":["Enzyme magnitude does NOT track severity - degree or serial trend of lipase/amylase does not correlate with how severe the pancreatitis is (Harrison 22e p.2745).","Serum amylase can be falsely normal or low in hypertriglyceridaemic pancreatitis, masking the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2739, p.2745).","Amylase is non-specific - raised in salivary, tubo-ovarian, pulmonary, malignant and other conditions, and spuriously high in acidemia/DKA (arterial pH <=7.32) without true pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2740, p.2745).","Lipase can be mildly elevated in renal failure, and >3x ULN can occur in hepatobiliary/GI malignancy, sepsis, cirrhosis, SLE, head injury, chronic alcoholism, diabetes and post-ERCP without pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2740).","Routine/early CT (within 72 h) is overused and usually unnecessary, especially in mild disease; necrosis may not be visible until several days in (Harrison 22e p.2746-2747).","Prophylactic antibiotics are NOT recommended, even in severe or necrotizing pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2746, p.2749).","Enzyme levels may be normal if blood is drawn 2-5 days after onset or if the underlying problem is chronic rather than acute pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2740)."],"severityClassification":"Revised Atlanta classification (Banks et al., Gut 2013). PHASES: early (<2 weeks; severity judged clinically, not by morphology) and late (>2 weeks; imaging for local complications). SEVERITY: (1) Mild - no local complications and no organ failure (most interstitial cases; self-limited 3-7 days); (2) Moderately severe - transient organ failure (resolves <48 h) and/or local or systemic complications without persistent organ failure; (3) Severe - persistent organ failure >48 h in >=1 organ system (respiratory, cardiovascular, renal; defined by modified Marshall score >=2). MORPHOLOGY: two types - interstitial (parenchymal enhancement, no necrosis) vs necrotizing (lack of parenchymal enhancement and/or peripancreatic necrosis); local collections - acute pancreatic fluid collection and pseudocyst (interstitial, pseudocyst usually >4 wk) vs acute necrotic collection (ANC) and walled-off necrosis (WON, usually >4 wk, encapsulated). SEVERITY SCORES/MARKERS: BISAP >=3 (BUN >25 mg/dL, Impaired mental status/GCS<15, SIRS >=2/4, Age >60, Pleural effusion); APACHE II >=8 at 24 h; SIRS >=2 of 4 (temp <36 or >38C, HR >90, RR >20 or PCO2 <32, WCC >12,000 or <4000 or >10% bands); hematocrit >44%; admission BUN >20 mg/dL; persistent organ failure >=48 h, and pancreatic/extrapancreatic necrosis as in-hospital markers. Ranson criteria are a traditional severity index (referenced contextually).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2738","Harrison 22e p.2739","Harrison 22e p.2740","Harrison 22e p.2744","Harrison 22e p.2745","Harrison 22e p.2746","Harrison 22e p.2747","Harrison 22e p.2748","Harrison 22e p.2749"],"crossLinks":["biliary_colic","CHOLANGITIS","decomp_cirrhosis"]},"panic":{"name":"Panic attack / anxiety","system":"Functional","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.103-107 (Chest Discomfort, Chap. 15); p.161, p.165 (Syncope/Dizziness); p.215 (Sleep Disorders); p.291 (Palpitations); p.376 (Hyperventilation syndrome / Respiratory Alkalosis)","clinicalPearls":["Up to ~10% of patients presenting to emergency departments with acute chest discomfort have an underlying panic disorder or a related condition (p.104).","Psychiatric/emotional chest discomfort is variable in duration (fleeting to prolonged), often felt as chest tightness and breathlessness with a sense of panic or impending doom, and may be retrosternal; situational triggers and a prior history of panic attacks or depression support the diagnosis (p.103).","A psychiatric cause for palpitations is favoured when the sensation is reported as lasting longer (>15 min) and is accompanied by additional symptoms, in contrast to most arrhythmic causes (p.291).","Panic attacks can arise out of sleep and must be distinguished from parasomnias and other nocturnal events (p.215).","Apparent (psychogenic) loss of consciousness can occur in panic disorder and related conditions; suspect it in patients who faint frequently without prodrome, are rarely injured despite repeated falls, and have no concurrent hemodynamic change (no hypotension/bradycardia) during the event (p.161)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute attacks are commonly accompanied by hyperventilation; the resulting hypocapnia (Paco2 often 15-30 mmHg) produces an acute respiratory alkalosis without hypoxemia, which underlies many of the somatic symptoms (paresthesia, circumoral numbness, chest-wall tightness, dizziness, air hunger, and rarely tetany) and can perpetuate the disorder. Catecholaminergic/hyperadrenergic activation contributes to palpitations and the hyperdynamic sensation (Harrison 22e p.376, p.291).","additionalDifferentials":["Hyperventilation syndrome (respiratory alkalosis) — itself a diagnosis of exclusion (p.376)","Generalized anxiety disorder and somatization presenting with palpitations or pseudo-syncope (p.161, p.291)","Vasovagal syncope precipitated by fear/stress/anxiety (distinguished by accompanying hypotension +/- bradycardia, unlike psychogenic pseudo-syncope) (p.161)","Cataplexy / narcolepsy as a cause of episodic collapse (consciousness preserved) (p.161)","Persistent postural-perceptual dizziness (PPPD) and chronic subjective dizziness, in which anxiety disorders are common comorbidities (p.165)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute coronary syndrome / myocardial ischemia (aching, heavy, squeezing retrosternal discomfort) (p.103)","Pulmonary embolism (chest discomfort, sinus tachycardia, ECG axis shift/S1Q3T3) (p.107)","Supraventricular or ventricular tachyarrhythmia and atrial fibrillation (palpitations) (p.291)","Thyrotoxicosis / hyperthyroidism (palpitations, hyperdynamic state) (p.291, p.376)","Pheochromocytoma (catecholamine-driven palpitations, hyperdynamic cardiovascular state) (p.291)","Hypoglycemia (tremor, palpitations, anxiety, diaphoresis, hunger, paresthesias from autonomic activation) (p.161)","Systemic mastocytosis; stimulant/drug effects (caffeine, cocaine, amphetamines, cannabis, ethanol); post-COVID syndrome — as causes of palpitations (p.291)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Resting 12-lead ECG, with serial tracings, to exclude arrhythmia and ischemia in palpitations/chest discomfort (p.291); note hyperventilation in panic can itself produce nonspecific ST-segment and T-wave abnormalities (p.107)","Ambulatory rhythm monitoring (Holter, event/loop recorder, implantable loop recorder, or mobile cardiac outpatient telemetry) for infrequent recurrent palpitations (p.291)","Arterial blood-gas analysis when hyperventilation suspected: shows respiratory alkalosis with hypocapnia (Paco2 ~15-30 mmHg) but no hypoxemia (p.376)"],"redFlags":["Normocapnia together with hypoxemia in a hyperventilating patient is a warning sign that may herald impending respiratory failure — do not assume benign anxiety (p.376)","High-risk syncope features that argue against a psychogenic cause: new chest discomfort, dyspnea, exertional or supine syncope, palpitations preceding syncope, structural/coronary heart disease (p.161)","Chest discomfort with features of an acute cardiopulmonary catastrophe (ACS, acute aortic syndrome, pulmonary embolism, tension pneumothorax, pericarditis with tamponade) must be excluded first (p.105)"],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Anchoring on anxiety/panic prematurely: because attacks mimic ACS, PE, arrhythmia, thyrotoxicosis, pheochromocytoma and hypoglycemia, organic emergencies must be excluded — panic disorder and the hyperventilation syndrome are diagnoses of exclusion (p.105, p.376).","Misreading hyperventilation-related nonspecific ST-segment/T-wave changes as evidence of ischemia (or vice versa) (p.107).","When ruling in a hyperventilation/psychiatric cause, still exclude pulmonary embolism, coronary artery disease and hyperthyroidism (p.376)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.103","Harrison 22e p.104","Harrison 22e p.105","Harrison 22e p.107","Harrison 22e p.161","Harrison 22e p.165","Harrison 22e p.215","Harrison 22e p.291","Harrison 22e p.376"],"crossLinks":["acs","pe","hyperthyroidism","pheo"]},"pe":{"name":"Pulmonary embolism","system":"Pulmonary / Vascular","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.290 (p.2154-2164), with chest-pain differential context Ch.11/15 (p.2153, supportive)","clinicalPearls":["PE is the 'great masquerader' because its symptoms and signs are nonspecific; unexplained breathlessness is the most common symptom (p.2159).","Suspect occult PE when a patient with congestive heart failure or pneumonia fails to improve as expected on standard treatment (p.2159).","Pleuritic chest pain typically reflects a small, peripherally lodged embolus near pleural nerves, whereas massive PE can cause severe substernal pain mimicking MI from pulmonary-artery distention and RV strain (p.2153, p.2159).","Pulmonary infarction usually signals a small (peripheral) PE and is exquisitely painful (p.2159).","May-Thurner syndrome (left iliac vein compressed by the right iliac artery) should be considered in young women with recurrent left thigh swelling and associated DVT (p.2159).","A diffusely edematous leg makes ordinary DVT unlikely, with phlegmasia cerulea dolens being the exception (p.2159).","Many patients with PE have no demonstrable DVT because the leg thrombus has already embolized to the lungs (p.2158).","McConnell's sign on echocardiography (RV free-wall hypokinesis with preserved/hyperkinetic RV apex) is the best-known indirect echo sign of PE (p.2161)."],"pathophysiology":"Virchow's triad of venous stasis, hypercoagulability, and endothelial injury, typically with an inflammatory trigger, recruits activated platelets that release microparticles and drive neutrophil extracellular trap formation, promoting thrombin generation; venous thrombi flourish in stasis, low oxygen tension, and proinflammatory gene upregulation. Detached lower-limb thrombi embolize through the vena cava and right heart to lodge in the pulmonary arteries (rarely paradoxically to the arterial circulation via a patent foramen ovale or atrial septal defect). The dominant gas-exchange defects are arterial hypoxemia and a widened alveolar-arterial oxygen gradient with increased dead space; additional mechanisms include raised pulmonary vascular resistance from obstruction and vasoconstrictors (e.g., serotonin), ventilation-perfusion mismatch remote from the embolus, reflex hyperventilation, increased airway resistance, and reduced compliance. Pulmonary-artery obstruction plus neurohumoral mediators raise pulmonary pressure and resistance; rising RV wall tension produces RV dilation, stretch and dysfunction (releasing BNP), septal bowing that impairs LV filling, and compression of the right coronary artery causing RV ischemia/microinfarction (releasing troponin), ultimately reducing LV output and systemic pressure, leading to circulatory collapse and death. Prothrombotic states include factor V Leiden, the prothrombin gene mutation, and deficiencies of antithrombin/protein C/protein S (rare); antiphospholipid syndrome is an acquired thrombophilia predisposing to both venous and arterial thrombosis. (p.2156-2158)","additionalDifferentials":["Congestive heart failure","Pericarditis","Pleurisy / costochondritis / musculoskeletal chest discomfort","Anxiety","Vasovagal syncope","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH) for persistent exertional breathlessness"],"infectionMimics":["Pneumonia","Cellulitis (mimics DVT; fever and chills favor cellulitis over DVT)","Sepsis (elevates D-dimer and can confound)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute coronary syndrome (massive PE can give substernal pain mimicking MI)","Aortic dissection","Pericardial tamponade","Pneumothorax / rib fracture","Asthma / COPD exacerbation","Ruptured Baker's cyst, muscle strain, and postthrombotic syndrome/venous insufficiency (mimic DVT)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Age-adjusted D-dimer (age x 10 ng/mL upper limit) for patients >50 years with low/intermediate clinical probability of PE; does not apply to suspected acute DVT (p.2159-2160)","Wells point-score clinical decision rule to stratify pretest probability (validated in ambulatory, not hospitalized, patients); low/low-to-moderate probability with normal D-dimer can avoid imaging (p.2159)","BNP / NT-proBNP as markers of RV myocardial stretch (p.2160)","ECG looking for S1Q3T3 (specific but insensitive) and T-wave inversion in V1-V4 from RV strain (p.2160)","Chest x-ray signs: focal oligemia (Westermark's sign), peripheral pleural-based density (Hampton's hump, from infarction), enlarged right descending pulmonary artery (Palla's sign); often normal (p.2160)","Echocardiography to assess RV dysfunction and to detect mimics (MI, tamponade, aortic dissection); usually normal in PE so not a reliable rule-out (p.2161)","Compression venous ultrasonography for DVT (loss of vein compressibility) (p.2160)","Ventilation/perfusion lung scan as second-line when IV contrast cannot be given (p.2161)","MR pulmonary angiography / MR venography when CT or ultrasound is unsuitable (p.2161)","RV-to-LV diameter ratio >1 on chest CT as a marker of RV enlargement (p.2161)"],"redFlags":["Systemic arterial hypotension / cardiogenic shock indicating massive (high-risk) PE (p.2158)","Syncope or cyanosis (p.2158, p.2153)","RV dysfunction on echocardiography or RV enlargement on CT despite normal blood pressure (submassive/intermediate-risk PE) (p.2158, p.2162)","Elevated troponin (from RV microinfarction) signaling high risk of clinical deterioration (p.2158, p.2162)","Phlegmasia cerulea dolens (diffusely swollen, threatened limb) (p.2159)"],"prognosis":"Low-risk PE (about 65-75% of cases) with normal blood pressure and normal RV carries an excellent prognosis; submassive (intermediate-risk) PE (~20-25%) has RV dysfunction despite normal pressure with raised cardiac biomarkers and a high risk of deterioration; massive (high-risk) PE (5-10%) presents with hypotension/shock and can lead to multisystem organ failure and death. A high-probability V/Q scan confers ~90% diagnostic certainty for PE. Roughly half of survivors report persistent dyspnea, fatigue, and reduced exercise capacity, and about one-quarter have persistent RV dysfunction on echo ('post-PE syndrome'); CTEPH is a less frequent late complication. Risk of recurrent VTE after stopping anticoagulation ranges from low (<3%/yr, e.g., major surgery/trauma) to intermediate (3-8%/yr) to high (>8%/yr, e.g., active cancer, antiphospholipid syndrome) (ESC 2019). (p.2154-2155, 2158, 2161, 2163-2164)","pitfalls":["D-dimer is sensitive (>95%) but not specific; it rises with MI, pneumonia, sepsis, cancer, postoperative state, and the 2nd/3rd trimester of pregnancy, so it rarely helps in hospitalized patients (p.2159-2160).","Wells criteria are validated in ambulatory patients, not in hospitalized patients (p.2159).","A normal or near-normal chest x-ray and a normal ECG (or only sinus tachycardia) do not exclude PE (p.2160).","Echocardiography is not a reliable rule-out test because most PE patients have normal echocardiograms (p.2161).","Not all leg pain is DVT and not all dyspnea is PE; consider ruptured Baker's cyst, cellulitis, and muscle injury (p.2159)."],"severityClassification":"PE is stratified by hemodynamics and RV status: massive (high-risk, 5-10%) with systemic hypotension and extensive thrombosis (>=half of the pulmonary vasculature), hallmarks dyspnea/syncope/hypotension/cyanosis and possible cardiogenic shock; submassive (intermediate-risk, ~20-25%) with RV dysfunction and raised troponin despite normal blood pressure; and low-risk (~65-75%) with normal pressure and normal RV. RV strain markers (troponin, BNP/NT-proBNP, echo RV dysfunction, CT RV enlargement with RV/LV diameter ratio >1) drive risk stratification. (p.2158, 2160-2162)","references":["Harrison 22e p.2153","Harrison 22e p.2154","Harrison 22e p.2155","Harrison 22e p.2156","Harrison 22e p.2157","Harrison 22e p.2158","Harrison 22e p.2159","Harrison 22e p.2160","Harrison 22e p.2161","Harrison 22e p.2162","Harrison 22e p.2163","Harrison 22e p.2164"],"crossLinks":["dvt","heart_failure","acs","pneumothorax"]},"peptic_ulcer":{"name":"Peptic ulcer disease / upper GI bleed","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.335, pp.2514-2536 (peptic ulcer disease and related disorders)","clinicalPearls":["Around two-thirds of patients with peptic ulcer disease have no abdominal pain, and up to ~87% of those with NSAID-related mucosal injury first present with a complication (bleeding, perforation, or obstruction) without any preceding symptoms (p.2522).","Classic duodenal ulcer pain comes on roughly 90 minutes to 3 hours after eating and is typically eased by food or antacids; pain that wakes the patient between midnight and 3 a.m. is the most discriminating symptom and is reported by about two-thirds of duodenal ulcer patients (p.2522).","Gastric ulcer pain can instead be provoked by eating, and nausea and weight loss are commoner with gastric than duodenal ulcers (p.2522).","Elderly and immunosuppressed patients are less likely to have typical pain and may present directly with bleeding or perforation (pp.2522-2523).","H. pylori and NSAIDs are the dominant risk factors (US odds ratios ~3.7 and ~3.3); NSAIDs are now described as the most common cause of PUD, with only ~one-quarter of duodenal and ~one-sixth of gastric ulcers now attributable to H. pylori (pp.2518, 2520).","More than half of patients with ulcer-related haemorrhage bleed without any preceding warning signs or symptoms (p.2522).","Endoscopy detects an ulcer in fewer than 30% of patients investigated for dyspepsia, reflecting how poorly symptoms predict an actual ulcer (p.2522)."],"pathophysiology":"Peptic ulcers are mucosal breaks >5 mm extending into the submucosa, occurring in the stomach (gastric ulcer, GU) or duodenum (duodenal ulcer, DU); >95% of DUs lie in the first part of the duodenum, within about 3 cm of the pylorus. The disease reflects an imbalance between aggressive factors (acid, pepsin) and mucosal defence. H. pylori and NSAIDs cause the majority of ulcers but act differently: H. pylori-associated benign GUs show antral gastritis, whereas NSAID-related GUs typically lack chronic active gastritis and instead show a chemical gastropathy (foveolar hyperplasia, lamina propria oedema). Duodenal ulcer patients tend to have increased basal and nocturnal acid secretion (with substantial overlap with controls) and reduced duodenal bicarbonate, both potentially influenced by H. pylori; gastric acid output in GU patients is usually normal or reduced, implying impaired mucosal defence. H. pylori is a gram-negative microaerophilic urease-producing rod living in the gastric mucous layer; virulence factors such as the cag pathogenicity island (delivering CagA), VacA, and urease determine the clinical outcome among colonisation, PUD, MALT lymphoma, and gastric adenocarcinoma (pp.2518-2520).","additionalDifferentials":["Functional (non-ulcer/essential) dyspepsia - the commonest cause of upper abdominal discomfort, subdivided into postprandial distress syndrome and epigastric pain syndrome; up to ~80% of patients evaluated for dyspepsia have a negative work-up (p.2523).","Proximal gastrointestinal tumours (p.2523).","Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (p.2523).","Pancreaticobiliary disease - biliary colic, chronic pancreatitis (p.2523).","Gastroduodenal Crohn's disease (p.2523).","Vascular (mesenteric/ischaemic) disease presenting as upper abdominal pain (p.2523)."],"infectionMimics":["Cytomegalovirus-related ulceration (a non-Hp, non-NSAID ulcer cause) (p.2523).","Herpes simplex virus-related ulceration (p.2523).","Helicobacter heilmannii infection (p.2523)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Functional/non-ulcer dyspepsia closely mimics ulcer symptoms without an actual ulcer (p.2523).","Drug- or toxin-induced ulceration not from NSAIDs/H. pylori - bisphosphonates, immune checkpoint inhibitors, chemotherapy, clopidogrel, crack cocaine, glucocorticoids combined with NSAIDs, mycophenolate mofetil, potassium chloride (p.2523).","Infiltrative/inflammatory mimics - Crohn's disease, sarcoidosis, eosinophilic infiltration, amyloidosis, lymphoma (pp.2523, 2532).","Ischaemia and radiation-induced mucosal injury (p.2523).","Idiopathic acid-hypersecretory states distinct from Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (p.2523)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Endoscopy is the most sensitive and specific test, allowing direct visualisation, biopsy to exclude malignancy (GU) and to test for H. pylori, and identification of a bleeding source (p.2523).","Barium studies of the upper GI tract can document an ulcer but are now rarely used as a first test (p.2523).","Biopsy urease tests (e.g. CLOtest, PyloriTek, Hpfast, Pronto Dry) with sensitivity/specificity >90-95% for H. pylori (p.2523).","Non-invasive H. pylori tests - serology, 13C-urea breath test, and monoclonal stool antigen test; the urea breath test or monoclonal stool antigen test is preferred to document eradication, performed off antisecretory drugs for at least 7 days (serology is not useful for confirming eradication) (pp.2523, 2531).","Fasting serum gastrin and gastric acid analysis, or secretin stimulation testing, in complicated or refractory ulcers to exclude Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (pp.2523, 2531-2532).","Screening for surreptitious aspirin/NSAID use (blood or urine) in refractory H. pylori-negative PUD (p.2523).","Multiple biopsies of any gastric ulcer at the index endoscopy, with repeat endoscopy at 8-12 weeks to document healing and re-biopsy if the ulcer persists (pp.2531-2532)."],"redFlags":["Alarm features warranting prompt endoscopy rather than empirical test-and-treat - nausea, vomiting, weight loss, evidence of GI bleeding, or age >=60 years with new dyspepsia (pp.2523, 2531).","Constant pain no longer relieved by food/antacids and radiating to the back suggests a penetrating ulcer (e.g. into the pancreas) (p.2522).","Sudden severe generalised abdominal pain with a board-like, rigid abdomen (the triad of acute pain, tachycardia, and rigidity) suggests perforation (pp.2522-2523).","Pain worsening with meals plus vomiting of undigested food, early satiety, weight loss, or a succussion splash suggests gastric outlet obstruction (pp.2522-2523).","Tarry stools or coffee-ground emesis indicate bleeding; tachycardia and orthostasis suggest hypovolaemia from blood loss or vomiting (p.2522)."],"prognosis":"More than 90% of both gastric and duodenal ulcers heal with conventional therapy; a GU not healed by 12 weeks or a DU not healed by 8 weeks is considered refractory. Eradicating H. pylori has cut duodenal ulcer recurrence by >80%. GI bleeding complicates roughly 15% of PUD with a 30-day mortality of about 2.5-10% (most deaths from non-bleeding causes such as multiorgan failure, pulmonary complications, and malignancy). Perforation occurs in ~6-7% of patients with 30-day mortality >20%, and penetration carries comparable mortality; gastric outlet obstruction is least common (~1-2%). Complications and mortality rise in patients over 60, partly reflecting greater NSAID use (pp.2522-2523, 2532).","pitfalls":["Every gastric ulcer must be biopsied (multiple samples) at discovery because a GU may be malignant, and healing on therapy does not exclude cancer - about 70% of GUs ultimately found malignant show significant (usually incomplete) healing; repeat endoscopy with biopsy at 8-12 weeks is needed if the ulcer persists (pp.2519, 2531-2532).","Abdominal pain has poor predictive value for an ulcer, and a normal exam or absent pain does not exclude PUD - many patients, especially the elderly or those on NSAIDs, present first with a complication (pp.2522-2523).","Right-sided epigastric tenderness in about 20% of patients can mislead the examiner; epigastric tenderness overall has low predictive value (p.2522).","Widespread PPI use can mask and delay the diagnosis of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and increase false-positive gastrin results (p.2534).","Consider gastrinoma/ZES when ulcers occur in unusual locations (second part of duodenum or beyond), are multiple, are refractory, recur after acid-reducing surgery, present with frank complications, or arise without H. pylori or NSAID use (p.2535)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2514","Harrison 22e p.2518","Harrison 22e p.2519","Harrison 22e p.2520","Harrison 22e p.2522","Harrison 22e p.2523","Harrison 22e p.2531","Harrison 22e p.2532","Harrison 22e p.2534","Harrison 22e p.2535"],"crossLinks":["gerd_chest","variceal_bleed","pancreatitis","biliary_colic"]},"pericarditis":{"name":"Acute pericarditis","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.90 (PE summary box); p.102 (tamponade as cause of shock); p.145-150 (Chest Discomfort: pericardial pain, exam, ECG); p.312 (dyspnea/pericardial disease table); p.324 (uraemic friction rub)","clinicalPearls":["Diagnosis rests on the combination of characteristic chest pain, a pericardial friction rub, and characteristic ECG changes; echocardiography can be normal in roughly a third to half of cases, so a normal echo does not exclude pericarditis (p.90).","The pericardial pain is typically pleuritic and positional: worse supine and on inspiration/coughing, relieved by sitting up and leaning forward (p.146, p.149).","Pain referred to the trapezius ridge is characteristic of pericardial inflammation and is not typical of angina (p.149).","Most of the pericardium is insensitive to pain; the discomfort of pericarditis is thought to arise largely from accompanying pleural inflammation, explaining its pleuritic, positional quality and referral to shoulder/neck (p.147).","A pericardial friction rub on auscultation reflects pericardial inflammation and is a key physical sign (p.149).","Pericarditis is an uncommon final diagnosis among undifferentiated acute chest pain presentations (about 4% in one ED series), so other causes are statistically more likely (p.145)."],"pathophysiology":"Inflammation of the pericardium from infectious or noninfectious causes. Because the visceral and most of the parietal pericardium are insensitive to pain, the discomfort is attributed mainly to associated pleural inflammation, producing pleuritic, position-dependent pain that, via overlapping phrenic and C3-C5 somatic innervation, may be referred to the shoulder, neck, and trapezius ridge (p.147, p.149).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute myocardial ischaemia / STEMI","Acute aortic syndrome (dissection)","Pulmonary embolism","Pleuritis / pneumonia","Musculoskeletal chest wall pain (e.g. costochondritis)","Gastro-oesophageal reflux / oesophageal disease","Herpes zoster"],"infectionMimics":["Acute myocarditis (often viral; chest discomfort and ST/T-wave changes can overlap)","Pneumonia / pleuritis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute coronary syndrome / STEMI (diffuse ST elevation of pericarditis can be mistaken for infarction)","Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy (ST elevation and biomarker rise mimicking MI)","Aortic dissection extending into the pericardium","Pulmonary embolism"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG looking for diffuse ST-segment elevation with PR-segment depression (p.149)","Chest radiograph (pericardial calcification suggests chronic pericarditis; usually unremarkable in acute disease) (p.150)","Echocardiography to detect effusion (may be normal in 34-50% of acute cases) (p.90)","Measurement of pulsus paradoxus when tamponade is suspected (p.312)"],"redFlags":["Hypotension with significant tachycardia, suggesting tamponade and haemodynamic compromise (pericarditis with tamponade is a high-risk, potentially life-threatening cause of chest pain) (p.147, p.150)","Abnormal jugular venous pulse pattern consistent with pericardial tamponade (p.149)","Pulsus paradoxus (points to tamponade physiology) (p.312)","Tamponade is a recognised cause of obstructive shock (p.102)"],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Diffuse (multi-lead) ST-segment elevation not confined to a single coronary territory, together with PR-segment depression, helps distinguish pericarditis from acute MI; ascribing these ECG changes to ischaemia is a key pitfall (p.149).","A normal echocardiogram does not exclude pericarditis (effusion absent in 34-50%) (p.90).","Chest-wall tenderness does not exclude myocardial ischaemia, and an unremarkable physical exam is not reassuring (p.149).","Low-grade fever is nonspecific and can accompany MI and thromboembolism as well as pericardial inflammation (p.149)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.90","Harrison 22e p.102","Harrison 22e p.145","Harrison 22e p.146","Harrison 22e p.147","Harrison 22e p.149","Harrison 22e p.150","Harrison 22e p.312","Harrison 22e p.324"],"crossLinks":["tamponade","acs","aortic_dissection","ckd"]},"pheo":{"name":"Phaeochromocytoma crisis","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e — referenced across chapters: hypertension (book p.2140-2141), cardiomyopathy/heart failure, palpitations, cancer genetics, cardiac tumors, mastocytosis; dedicated pheochromocytoma chapter (Ch. 399, book p.3073)","clinicalPearls":["The classic triad is episodic headache, palpitations and sweating (diaphoresis) occurring with paroxysmal hypertension (p.2140).","Most patients present with hypertension at a relatively young age, often between 30 and 50 years (p.2140).","Many patients have only non-specific symptoms; the florid paroxysmal presentation is not universal, so the diagnosis is easily missed (p.2140).","Catecholamine-secreting tumours arise from chromaffin cells: roughly 80-85% are in the adrenal medulla, the remainder being extra-adrenal paraganglioma in paraganglion tissue (p.2140).","Patients with pheochromocytoma frequently have postural (orthostatic) hypotension despite the hypertension, reflecting volume contraction and impaired sympathetic reflexes (cardiomyopathy chapter).","Catecholamine excess can produce a hyperdynamic state and palpitations, and is one of the recognised endocrine causes alongside thyrotoxicosis and mastocytosis (palpitations chapter)."],"pathophysiology":"Excess production of catecholamines, usually by a benign neuroendocrine tumour of chromaffin cells in the adrenal medulla (80-85%) or in extra-adrenal paraganglion tissue (paraganglioma). The catecholamine surge drives paroxysmal hypertension, headache, sweating and cardiac arrhythmias, and produces a hyperdynamic catecholaminergic state. Chronic/episodic catecholamine exposure can cause microvascular myocardial ischaemia (microinfarcts similar to those seen with stimulant toxicity) and a catecholamine-mediated cardiomyopathy; the same mechanism of sudden catecholamine release underlies stress (takotsubo / apical ballooning) cardiomyopathy (hypertension, cardiomyopathy and cardiac-tumour chapters).","additionalDifferentials":["Cushing's syndrome and other secondary causes of hypertension (more common than pheochromocytoma, so the classic triad is frequently misleading) (p.2140).","Thyrotoxicosis / hyperthyroidism (overlapping palpitations, sweating, tremor; an endocrine cause of palpitations) (palpitations chapter).","Panic / anxiety disorder and somatization (episodic palpitations, sweating, sense of doom mimicking paroxysms) (palpitations chapter).","Stimulant toxicity — cocaine, amphetamines and related catecholaminergic drugs (produce hypertension, tachyarrhythmia, microvascular ischaemia resembling pheochromocytoma) (cardiomyopathy chapter).","Systemic mastocytosis / mast-cell activation (flushing, palpitations, hypotensive episodes) (mastocytosis chapter)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Systemic mastocytosis — flushing and palpitation attacks; urinary metanephrines help exclude pheochromocytoma when evaluating mast-cell symptoms (mastocytosis chapter).","Carcinoid tumour — overlapping flushing/paroxysmal symptoms; assessed together with metanephrines in differential testing (mastocytosis chapter).","Stimulant (cocaine/amphetamine) toxicity producing a catecholaminergic crisis with hypertension and arrhythmia (cardiomyopathy chapter).","Takotsubo (stress / apical ballooning) cardiomyopathy — catecholamine-mediated, can follow a surge resembling pheochromocytoma (cardiac trauma / cardiomyopathy chapters)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood and 24-hour urinary catecholamine measurement (in addition to plasma/urine metanephrines) (p.2140).","Functional/positron emission tomography (PET) imaging in addition to CT/MRI of the abdomen for localisation (p.2140).","Nuclear localisation with 131-I-metaiodobenzylguanidine (MIBG) scanning, e.g. for extra-adrenal/cardiac paraganglioma (cardiac-tumour chapter).","Genetic evaluation when a hereditary predisposition is suspected (MEN2/RET, von Hippel-Lindau, neurofibromatosis type 1, hereditary paraganglioma syndromes) (p.2140)."],"redFlags":["Pheochromocytoma in crisis is named alongside aortic dissection and eclampsia as a hypertensive emergency demanding particularly rapid management; control blood pressure with a titratable IV agent (p.2141).","Very labile blood pressure and heart rate with episodic palpitations occurring together with new heart failure should raise suspicion of pheochromocytoma (cardiomyopathy chapter).","Coexisting postural hypotension with paroxysmal hypertension is a clue to catecholamine excess rather than essential hypertension (cardiomyopathy chapter)."],"prognosis":"A benign tumour is usual; definitive treatment is surgical extirpation (complete or partial adrenalectomy, typically minimally invasive), which is generally curative. Surgery requires prior alpha-adrenergic receptor blockade. Untreated catecholamine excess can precipitate hypertensive emergency, arrhythmia, catecholamine cardiomyopathy and heart failure (hypertension and cardiomyopathy chapters).","pitfalls":["Representativeness-heuristic trap: clinicians overestimate the probability of pheochromocytoma when the classic headache-palpitations-sweating triad is present, because they neglect its low prevalence relative to common causes of hypertension; the triad also occurs in patients who do not have the tumour (clinical-reasoning chapter, p.~? — diagnostic reasoning).","Conversely, non-specific or atypical presentations can lead to the diagnosis being missed entirely (clinical-reasoning chapter).","Postural hypotension may be wrongly attributed to over-treatment of hypertension rather than recognised as a catecholamine-excess clue (cardiomyopathy chapter)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2140","Harrison 22e p.2141","Harrison 22e Ch.399 (Pheochromocytoma)"],"crossLinks":[]},"pleural_effusion":{"name":"Pleural effusion","system":"Pulmonary","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"89, 126, 314, 366, 373, 662, 663, 1235, 1430, 2243","clinicalPearls":["On examination an effusion gives dullness to percussion, reduced or absent breath sounds, and decreased tactile fremitus over the fluid; this contrasts with consolidation, where fremitus is increased (Harrison 22e p.2243).","Percussion helps separate the two causes of a quiet hemithorax: an effusion is dull, whereas a pneumothorax is hyper-resonant (Harrison 22e p.2243).","Skodaic resonance, a band of hyper-resonance just above the dull effusion, is a classic percussion sign (Harrison 22e p.89).","Bilateral effusions are characteristic of congestive heart failure (and some collagen-vascular disorders), whereas a unilateral effusion should raise suspicion of malignancy or pulmonary embolism, though heart failure and parapneumonic effusion can also be unilateral (Harrison 22e p.314).","A right-sided pleural effusion can occur in advanced cirrhosis even when no ascites is clinically detectable (Harrison 22e p.366).","In a malignant effusion an initially negative thoracentesis does not exclude cancer; repeating the tap is essential because cytologic yield improves with repeat sampling (Harrison 22e p.663)."],"pathophysiology":"Fluid abnormally accumulates in the pleural space when the balance of hydrostatic and oncotic forces, lymphatic drainage, or capillary permeability is disturbed. Transudates arise from systemic forces (raised hydrostatic pressure or low plasma oncotic pressure) as in the sodium-avid edematous states of heart failure, cirrhosis, and nephrotic syndrome (Harrison 22e p.422). Exudates result from local pleural or capillary processes such as infection, malignancy, or inflammation. In hepatic hydrothorax, ascitic fluid migrates through diaphragmatic fenestrae into the pleural space (Harrison 22e p.373). Tuberculous effusion typically reflects a hypersensitivity reaction to mycobacterial antigens after recent primary infection, or contiguous spread from parenchymal disease (Harrison 22e p.1430). Malignant effusions arise from pleural involvement or from lymphatic obstruction by tumor (Harrison 22e p.662).","additionalDifferentials":["Congestive heart failure (typically bilateral transudative effusions) (Harrison 22e p.314)","Cirrhosis / hepatic hydrothorax (often right-sided) (Harrison 22e p.366, p.373)","Nephrotic syndrome and other hypoalbuminemic states (Harrison 22e p.422)","Parapneumonic effusion / empyema complicating pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1235)","Malignant effusion (e.g., lung cancer, metastatic disease) (Harrison 22e p.662)","Tuberculous pleurisy (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Pulmonary embolism (Harrison 22e p.314)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculous pleural effusion — straw-colored (sometimes hemorrhagic) lymphocyte-predominant exudate that can mimic a parapneumonic or malignant effusion (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Tuberculous empyema, usually from rupture of a cavity into the pleural space, sometimes with a bronchopleural fistula and air in the pleural space (Harrison 22e p.1431).","Spontaneous bacterial empyema complicating hepatic hydrothorax in cirrhosis (Harrison 22e p.373)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Heart-failure transudate, which can be unilateral and be mistaken for a localized pathologic effusion (Harrison 22e p.314).","Hepatic hydrothorax from cirrhosis, mimicking other right-sided effusions (Harrison 22e p.366, p.373).","Malignant effusion, which may be exudative or bloody and can coexist with or be confused for a parapneumonic effusion (Harrison 22e p.663)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnostic thoracentesis to determine whether the fluid is a transudate or an exudate and to identify the underlying cause (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Pleural fluid pH — a value of ≤7.1 in a parapneumonic setting indicates empyema requiring drainage (Harrison 22e p.1235).","Pleural fluid protein, glucose, and cell count/differential; in TB the fluid is an exudate with protein >50% of serum (often ~4-6 g/dL), normal-to-low glucose, pH ~7.3 (occasionally <7.2), and 500-6000 WBC/uL, with neutrophils early and lymphocyte predominance later (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Pleural fluid adenosine deaminase (ADA) — an elevated level is consistent with TB and a very low level helps exclude it (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Pleural fluid IFN-gamma and lysozyme as adjuncts for suspected tuberculous effusion (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Pleural fluid cytology for malignancy, with repeat thoracentesis if the first sample is negative (Harrison 22e p.663).","Needle (closed) pleural biopsy for suspected TB, which reveals granulomas and/or yields positive culture in up to ~80% of cases and is favored over fluid analysis (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Xpert MTB/RIF on pleural biopsy tissue (positive in ~75%); testing pleural fluid itself with this assay is not recommended owing to low sensitivity (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Serum-to-pleural-fluid albumin gradient — a value >=1.1 g/dL suggests hepatic hydrothorax (Harrison 22e p.373)."],"redFlags":["Frank pus, organisms on microscopy, or pleural fluid pH <=7.1 in a parapneumonic effusion — diagnostic of empyema and mandates aggressive complete drainage, usually via chest tube (Harrison 22e p.1235).","New or persistent fever and leukocytosis after 4-5 days of appropriate antibiotics for pneumonia — suspect empyema (Harrison 22e p.1235).","Exudative or bloody effusion — raises concern for malignancy (Harrison 22e p.663).","Unilateral effusion without an obvious cause — investigate for malignancy or pulmonary embolism (Harrison 22e p.314)."],"prognosis":"Tuberculous pleural effusion responds rapidly to antituberculous chemotherapy and may even resolve spontaneously; concurrent glucocorticoids may shorten fever and chest pain but are of unproven benefit (Harrison 22e p.1430). Parapneumonic effusions are usually a self-limited inflammatory response, whereas a true empyema requires drainage and carries a worse course if undrained (Harrison 22e p.1235).","pitfalls":["Avoid attributing every quiet, dull hemithorax to effusion — confirm with percussion and fremitus, since pneumothorax and consolidation differ (Harrison 22e p.2243).","A negative initial pleural cytology does not rule out malignancy; failing to repeat the tap is a diagnostic pitfall (Harrison 22e p.663).","Do not assume a unilateral effusion is benign heart failure — it may be malignant or embolic (Harrison 22e p.314).","Testing pleural fluid (rather than biopsy tissue) with Xpert MTB/RIF is unreliable for TB because of low sensitivity (Harrison 22e p.1430).","AFB smears are rarely positive and cultures are often falsely negative in tuberculous effusions, so a negative smear/culture does not exclude pleural TB (Harrison 22e p.1430).","In hepatic hydrothorax, chest-tube placement should be avoided (Harrison 22e p.373)."],"severityClassification":"Parapneumonic effusions are distinguished from empyema by their character: an uncomplicated parapneumonic effusion is a self-limited reaction to adjacent pneumonia, whereas frank pus, visible organisms, or a pleural fluid pH <=7.1 defines empyema (a complicated/infected collection) that requires complete drainage (Harrison 22e p.1235).","references":["Harrison 22e p.89","Harrison 22e p.126","Harrison 22e p.314","Harrison 22e p.366","Harrison 22e p.373","Harrison 22e p.662","Harrison 22e p.663","Harrison 22e p.1235","Harrison 22e p.1430","Harrison 22e p.2243"],"crossLinks":["CAP","heart_failure","PULMONARY_TB","lung_cancer","decomp_cirrhosis"]},"pmr":{"name":"Polymyalgia rheumatica","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2945-2946 (Giant Cell Arteritis and Polymyalgia Rheumatica, Ch.375); supporting context p.2920 (Ch.370 RA), p.2938-2939, p.2965 (Ch.385 Fibromyalgia), and Ch.385 (muscle disease approach)","clinicalPearls":["PMR causes stiffness, aching, and pain in the muscles of the neck, shoulders, lower back, hips, and thighs in a patient typically aged >50 years (p.2945).","PMR and giant cell arteritis are regarded as different clinical expressions of a single underlying disease process, supported by clinical association and pathophysiologic data (p.2945).","Although PMR most often occurs in isolation, it is found in roughly 40-50% of patients with giant cell arteritis (p.2945).","About 10-20% of patients presenting initially with apparently isolated PMR later go on to develop giant cell arteritis, so reassessment for GCA features should occur at follow-up visits (p.2945).","Inflammatory stiffness in PMR tends to be prolonged (lasting hours) and improves with activity, in contrast to the brief gel-phenomenon stiffness (<=45 min, worse with activity) of noninflammatory disorders such as osteoarthritis (p.2938).","Fatigue can be profound in PMR, mirroring other inflammatory conditions (p.2938).","Annual incidence of PMR in those aged >=50 years was 58.7 per 100,000 in Olmsted County, Minnesota (p.2945)."],"pathophysiology":"PMR sits on the same disease spectrum as giant cell arteritis, an antigen-driven panarteritis of medium- and large-sized vessels with mononuclear infiltrates and frequent giant cell formation. Activated CD4+ T lymphocytes, macrophages, and dendritic cells are central; restricted clonal T-cell receptor expansion suggests an antigen residing in the arterial wall. Two CD4 T-cell lineages (IFN-gamma-producing TH1 and IL-17-producing TH17) participate in vascular inflammation, with IL-6 signaling driving hepatic acute-phase protein (CRP, and effects on ESR) production. Familial aggregation and HLA-DR4 / HLA-DRB1*04 associations have been reported for the GCA end of the spectrum (p.2945).","additionalDifferentials":["Seronegative rheumatoid arthritis in the elderly, which can be difficult to distinguish from PMR; RA predominantly affects wrists/hands and ankles/feet at onset whereas PMR is girdle-predominant (p.2920).","Remitting seronegative symmetrical synovitis with pitting edema (RS3PE) — prominent distal limb pitting edema, prompt response to low-dose prednisone (p.2920).","Other inflammatory arthropathies / seronegative RA in patients who develop a worsening pattern of peripheral arthritis (p.2945).","Fibromyalgia — listed among conditions confused with PMR; PMR appears in the fibromyalgia differential (p.2965).","Osteoarthritis and other noninflammatory nonarticular conditions (bursitis, tendinitis) (p.2938-2939)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inflammatory myopathy/myositis (polymyositis): PMR is included among nonarticular conditions to consider, but unlike myopathy it is not associated with true muscle weakness, and serum CK, EMG, and muscle biopsy are normal in PMR (p.2939, Ch.385).","Fibromyalgia: a painful, noninflammatory myofascial syndrome with normal CK, ESR, EMG, and muscle biopsy — distinguished from PMR by normal inflammatory markers.","Paraneoplastic syndromes (noted as mimics in the elderly inflammatory-arthritis context) (p.2920).","Chronic tophaceous gout and hepatitis C-related arthropathy (mimics of inflammatory arthritis in the elderly) (p.2920)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Rheumatoid factor and anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (anti-CCP) should be negative; positive results point toward seronegative-RA spectrum or other inflammatory arthropathy (p.2946).","Serum creatine kinase is NOT elevated in PMR; elevation should prompt consideration of a myopathic process (p.2946).","Normochromic or slightly hypochromic anemia and elevated alkaline phosphatase (liver function abnormalities) may accompany the inflammatory state in the GCA/PMR spectrum (p.2945).","If GCA is suspected, evaluate temporal arteries (biopsy, or ultrasonography showing a noncompressible 'halo' sign) and consider vascular imaging (MR/CT, PET investigational) for large-vessel disease; therapy should not be delayed pending these studies (p.2945-2946)."],"redFlags":["New or worsening peripheral arthritis pattern — reconsider seronegative RA or another inflammatory arthropathy rather than PMR (p.2946).","Elevated serum CK or true muscle weakness — argues against PMR and suggests inflammatory myopathy (p.2946).","Jaw or tongue claudication, scalp tenderness, new headache, or a tender/thickened/nodular temporal artery — signal evolution to GCA with risk of ischemic optic neuropathy and sudden blindness (p.2945).","Arm claudication, diminished pulses, or bruits — may indicate large-vessel (e.g., subclavian) GCA involvement or aortic aneurysm risk (p.2945-2946)."],"prognosis":"Isolated PMR generally responds promptly to low-dose treatment, and most patients can be managed without IL-6-directed agents. Recurrent polymyalgia symptoms develop in the majority of patients during steroid tapering. The principal long-term concern is progression toward giant cell arteritis (10-20% of initially isolated PMR), which carries risks of visual loss and, late, thoracic aortic aneurysm/dissection (p.2945-2946).","pitfalls":["Treating an IL-6-inhibitor-exposed patient: blockade of IL-6 signaling normalizes CRP and ESR, so these markers can no longer be used to monitor disease activity or detect infection (p.2945).","Minor rises in ESR/CRP during tapering do not necessarily indicate disease exacerbation, particularly in an asymptomatic patient; ESR/CRP can also rise from infection (p.2946).","Assuming PMR explains distal/peripheral arthritis — PMR can mimic RA, and persistent or worsening peripheral arthritis warrants reconsideration (p.2946)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2945","Harrison 22e p.2946","Harrison 22e p.2920","Harrison 22e p.2938","Harrison 22e p.2939","Harrison 22e p.2965"],"crossLinks":["temporal_arteritis","rheumatoid"]},"pneumothorax":{"name":"Pneumothorax","system":"Pulmonary","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"104, 105, 148, 2271, 2273, 2313, 2314, plus shock/cardiac-arrest and respiratory-exam chapters","clinicalPearls":["Pneumothorax is simply gas within the pleural space; classify it as spontaneous (no preceding trauma) versus traumatic, and spontaneous further as primary (no underlying lung disease) versus secondary (underlying lung disease present) (Harrison 22e p.2313).","A tension pneumothorax is defined physiologically as one in which pleural pressure stays positive throughout the entire respiratory cycle (Harrison 22e p.2313).","Tension pneumothorax should be treated as a clinical emergency on the basis of bedside findings; do not wait for a confirmatory chest radiograph before relieving the pressure, because untreated tension leads to death from inadequate cardiac output or severe hypoxemia (Harrison 22e p.2314).","When a tension pneumothorax is suspected, decompression precedes imaging: a large-bore needle is placed into the pleural space and a rush of escaping gas confirms the diagnosis (knowledge principle; no dose/technique detail) (Harrison 22e p.2314).","Primary spontaneous pneumothorax typically results from rupture of small apical subpleural blebs (cystic spaces within or just beneath the visceral pleura) (Harrison 22e p.2313).","Classic primary spontaneous pneumothorax patient is a young, tall, thin man; smoking, male sex, family history, and Marfan syndrome are recognized risk factors (Harrison 22e p.104, p.105).","Symptom onset in primary spontaneous pneumothorax is usually sudden but dyspnea can be mild, so presentation to care is sometimes delayed (Harrison 22e p.104).","Tension pneumothorax most often arises during mechanical ventilation or resuscitation; difficulty ventilating during resuscitation or high peak inspiratory pressures on the ventilator should strongly raise suspicion (Harrison 22e p.2314).","Persistent air leak is a recognized entity, defined as a non-resolving pneumothorax with an air leak lasting longer than about 5-7 days (Harrison 22e p.2273)."],"pathophysiology":"Pneumothorax is the accumulation of gas in the pleural space. In primary spontaneous pneumothorax, apical subpleural blebs rupture, allowing air to enter the pleural space without preceding trauma and in the absence of underlying lung disease. Secondary pneumothorax occurs when the same process complicates established lung disease, where loss of pulmonary reserve makes the event more dangerous. Traumatic pneumothorax results from penetrating or non-penetrating chest injury, and iatrogenic pneumothorax (a traumatic subtype) follows procedures such as transthoracic needle aspiration, thoracentesis, or central venous catheter insertion. In a tension pneumothorax, a one-way valve effect produces pleural pressure that remains positive across the whole respiratory cycle; this both severely compromises ventilation and transmits positive pressure to the mediastinum, reducing venous return to the heart and thereby cardiac output (Harrison 22e p.2313, p.2314).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute coronary syndrome / myocardial infarction (Harrison 22e p.105)","Acute aortic syndrome / aortic dissection (Harrison 22e p.104, p.105)","Pericarditis with tamponade (Harrison 22e p.105)","Pleurisy / pleuritis (Harrison 22e p.104)","Pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.104)","Esophageal disorders such as reflux or motility disorder mimicking angina (Harrison 22e p.105)","Musculoskeletal chest-wall pain including costochondritis (Tietze syndrome) (Harrison 22e p.105)","Panic disorder / anxiety state (Harrison 22e p.105)"],"infectionMimics":["Pneumonia or pleuritis presenting with sudden pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea (Harrison 22e p.104)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pulmonary embolism, which like pneumothorax can cause sudden dyspnea, pleuritic pain, and hemodynamic compromise (Harrison 22e p.104, p.105)","Acute coronary syndrome / myocardial infarction (Harrison 22e p.105)","Aortic dissection / acute aortic syndrome (Harrison 22e p.104, p.105)","Pericarditis with cardiac tamponade (Harrison 22e p.105, p.2293)","Esophageal reflux or spasm mimicking angina (Harrison 22e p.105)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph to confirm or exclude pneumothorax; it is the routine first-line image in acute chest discomfort and is useful for pneumothorax, though for tension it must not delay decompression (Harrison 22e p.105, p.2272).","Point-of-care lung ultrasound (POCUS), which can rapidly diagnose pneumothorax (as well as pleural effusion and consolidation) at the bedside (Harrison 22e p.2272, shock evaluation chapter).","Bedside physical examination as a diagnostic tool for tension pneumothorax: enlarged hemithorax with absent breath sounds, hyperresonance to percussion, mediastinal shift to the contralateral side, and hypotension (Harrison 22e p.2314).","Percussion to distinguish causes of diminished breath sounds: pneumothorax is hyper-resonant whereas pleural effusion is dull (Harrison 22e respiratory-examination chapter).","Palpation for subcutaneous emphysema as a sign of associated barotrauma (Harrison 22e respiratory-examination chapter)."],"redFlags":["Tracheal/mediastinal shift to the contralateral side, absent breath sounds over an enlarged hemithorax, hyperresonant percussion, and hypotension — the bedside signs of tension pneumothorax (Harrison 22e p.2314).","Hemodynamic collapse from trapped intrathoracic air defines tension pneumothorax as a medical emergency (Harrison 22e p.104).","Tension pneumothorax is a cause of obstructive shock and of pulseless electrical activity (PEA) arrest, and is a reversible cause to consider during resuscitation (Harrison 22e shock and cardiac-arrest chapters).","Pulsus paradoxus (inspiratory systolic fall greater than 10 mmHg) can accompany tension pneumothorax and signals significant hemodynamic compromise (Harrison 22e p.1813).","Tachycardia and hypotension in a patient with chest discomfort should trigger rapid evaluation for the most lethal causes, including tension pneumothorax (Harrison 22e p.105).","Difficulty ventilating during resuscitation or rising peak inspiratory pressures on mechanical ventilation should prompt urgent suspicion of tension pneumothorax (Harrison 22e p.2314)."],"prognosis":"Roughly half of patients with an initial primary spontaneous pneumothorax experience a recurrence. Secondary pneumothorax carries a worse outlook than pneumothorax in otherwise normal lungs because the underlying lung disease leaves little pulmonary reserve, making the event potentially more life-threatening. Untreated tension pneumothorax is rapidly fatal from inadequate cardiac output or severe hypoxemia (Harrison 22e p.2313, p.2314).","pitfalls":["Waiting for a chest radiograph to confirm a suspected tension pneumothorax instead of decompressing immediately — tension is a clinical diagnosis and delay can be fatal (Harrison 22e p.2314).","Underestimating a secondary pneumothorax in a patient with COPD, asthma, or cystic fibrosis; because of poor reserve it can be far more dangerous than a primary event (Harrison 22e p.104, p.2313).","Mild dyspnea in primary spontaneous pneumothorax can lead to delayed presentation, so a benign-seeming history does not exclude it (Harrison 22e p.104).","Forgetting iatrogenic pneumothorax after thoracentesis, transthoracic needle aspiration, or central line placement (Harrison 22e p.2314).","Attributing absent or diminished breath sounds to effusion when percussion (hyper-resonant in pneumothorax, dull in effusion) would distinguish them (Harrison 22e respiratory-examination chapter).","Overlooking tension pneumothorax as a reversible cause of PEA arrest and obstructive shock (Harrison 22e shock and cardiac-arrest chapters)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison classifies pneumothorax by mechanism and risk rather than a numeric score: (1) primary spontaneous (no underlying lung disease, often young tall thin smokers, frequently manageable conservatively if asymptomatic/minimally symptomatic); (2) secondary spontaneous (underlying lung disease present, e.g., COPD, asthma, cystic fibrosis — more life-threatening because of limited reserve); (3) traumatic, including the increasingly common iatrogenic subtype; and (4) tension pneumothorax, defined by positive pleural pressure throughout the respiratory cycle and treated as a medical emergency. Persistent air leak is a recognized complication defined as a non-resolving pneumothorax with air leak beyond about 5-7 days (Harrison 22e p.2313, p.2314, p.2273).","references":["Harrison 22e p.104","Harrison 22e p.105","Harrison 22e p.2271","Harrison 22e p.2272","Harrison 22e p.2273","Harrison 22e p.2313","Harrison 22e p.2314"],"crossLinks":["pe","COPD_EXACERBATION","asthma_exac"]},"renal_colic":{"name":"Renal / ureteric colic","system":"Urology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.154, p.381, p.383, p.406-407, p.551, p.627, p.631, p.747, p.1118, p.1334","clinicalPearls":["Renal colic is classic visceral pain from distention of a hollow viscus (the obstructed collecting system/ureter): cramping and intermittent rather than constant (p.551).","Stones obstructing urinary flow are the single most important risk factor for perinephric/renal abscess; 20-60% of patients with perinephric abscess have renal stones (p.1118).","Urea-splitting organisms (notably Proteus, also other urease producers) alkalinize the urine, favoring precipitation of struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate) and carbonate-apatite, which can form staghorn calculi in the renal pelvis (p.1118, p.1334).","Struvite stones act as a persistent nidus for recurrent UTI and typically cannot be eradicated by antibiotics alone without removal of the stone (p.1118, p.1334).","Stone-associated obstruction is a recognized cause of postrenal azotemia/AKI; because one functioning kidney clears adequately, obstructive renal failure from stones generally requires bilateral ureteric obstruction or unilateral obstruction in a solitary/single functioning kidney (p.383).","Nephrolithiasis can be the presenting feature of an underlying metabolic disorder such as primary hyperparathyroidism/hypercalcemia (p.406).","Imaging of the upper tracts is advised in any patient with hematuria, a history of calculi, or prior urinary tract problems (p.747)."],"pathophysiology":"Colicky flank pain arises from acute distention of the renal collecting system and ureter when a stone obstructs urinary outflow, producing visceral cramping pain (p.551). Obstruction proximal to the bladder produces hydroureter and renal pelvic dilation (hydronephrosis); if unrelieved this impairs glomerular filtration and causes postrenal azotemia (p.383). In urease-producing infection (e.g., Proteus), hydrolysis of urea to ammonia raises urinary pH; the resulting alkaline environment precipitates struvite and carbonate-apatite, building staghorn calculi that perpetuate infection and obstruction (p.1118, p.1334). Stones also serve as a chronic nidus that promotes recurrent and recalcitrant urinary infection (p.1118).","additionalDifferentials":["Ruptured/impending-rupture abdominal aortic aneurysm — abdominal pain radiating to the flank, sacral region, or genitalia should always raise suspicion of a leaking AAA; pain may precede rupture by days (p.154).","Acute mesenteric ischemia (pain out of proportion to exam in a vascular patient) (p.154).","Other causes of acute lower abdominal pain such as appendicitis, salpingitis, ectopic pregnancy, inguinal hernia, inflammatory bowel disease, and bowel obstruction (p.18 abdominal pain differential table).","Ureteric obstruction due to extrinsic malignancy (pelvic/prostatic/gynecologic tumors, retroperitoneal nodes) or post-radiation ureteric fibrosis, which can mimic stone-related obstruction (p.631)."],"infectionMimics":["Acute pyelonephritis (flank pain, fever, pyuria) (p.1118).","Perinephric or renal abscess — flank/abdominal pain, fever in at least half; suspect when pyelonephritis remains febrile after 4-5 days, when urine culture is polymicrobial, when stones are known, or when fever and pyuria coexist with a sterile urine culture (p.1118)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm with flank/genital radiation, especially in older patients (p.154).","Ureteric obstruction from malignancy or retroperitoneal fibrosis presenting with flank pain (p.631)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal ultrasonography — useful to identify hydronephrosis/ureteric and pelvic dilation, though it may be falsely negative early or when ureteric dilation is prevented (e.g., retroperitoneal fibrosis or tumor encasement) (p.383, p.631).","Abdominal CT and renal ultrasound are the most useful modalities when perinephric/renal abscess is suspected (p.1118).","Urinalysis showing alkaline urine plus a urease-producing organism (e.g., Proteus) may prompt evaluation for urolithiasis, though Harrison notes clinical support for routine action on this finding is lacking (p.1334).","Serum calcium / PTH and metabolic workup when an underlying stone-promoting metabolic disorder (e.g., primary hyperparathyroidism) is suspected (p.406)."],"redFlags":["Pain radiating to flank, sacral region, or genitalia in an older patient — exclude a leaking abdominal aortic aneurysm (p.154).","Rising serum creatinine, oliguria, or anuria — suggests obstruction of a solitary kidney or bilateral obstruction and warrants immediate evaluation (p.383, p.631).","Persistent fever after several days of treatment for presumed pyelonephritis, polymicrobial urine, or fever/pyuria with sterile culture — suspect perinephric/renal abscess complicating an obstructing stone (p.1118)."],"prognosis":"Stone-related obstruction is usually reversible if relieved promptly; urinary tract obstruction accounts for under 5% of acute renal failure and must be excluded early because it is correctable (p.383). When obstruction is complicated by infection (abscess), diagnosis is frequently delayed and mortality is appreciable, though lower than historically (p.1118). Struvite/staghorn stones tend to recur and perpetuate infection unless the stone is removed (p.1118, p.1334).","pitfalls":["Assuming flank pain radiating to the groin is always a stone — in older patients a ruptured/leaking AAA can present identically and is rapidly fatal if missed (p.154).","Relying on a negative ultrasound to exclude obstruction; dilation may be absent early or when the ureter cannot distend (p.383).","Failing to recognize an infected obstructed system / perinephric abscess in a patient with known stones who stays febrile or has polymicrobial or sterile-but-pyuric urine (p.1118).","Overlooking an underlying metabolic cause (e.g., hyperparathyroidism) in a stone former (p.406)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.154","Harrison 22e p.381","Harrison 22e p.383","Harrison 22e p.406","Harrison 22e p.407","Harrison 22e p.551","Harrison 22e p.627","Harrison 22e p.631","Harrison 22e p.747","Harrison 22e p.1118","Harrison 22e p.1334"],"crossLinks":["PYELONEPHRITIS","aaa","hypercalcemia"]},"rhabdo":{"name":"Rhabdomyolysis","system":"Nephrology / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.102, p.343, p.358-359, p.363, p.818, p.1096; supporting p.223, p.1653","clinicalPearls":["Myoglobinuria characteristically produces a positive urine dipstick for blood while urine microscopy shows no (or few) red cells — a key clue distinguishing pigment nephropathy from true haematuria (Harrison 22e p.343).","In rhabdomyolysis-associated acute tubular necrosis the fractional excretion of sodium (FENa) may be paradoxically low (<1%), unlike the classic >1% of established ATN, because the injury is often early/non-oliguric (Harrison 22e p.343).","Rhabdomyolysis is listed among the 'don't-miss' metabolic emergencies whose urgency can be underappreciated, alongside malignant hyperthermia and tumour lysis syndrome (Harrison 22e p.102).","Heat-related exertional rhabdomyolysis is a recognised, albeit rare, cause of sudden death in sickle cell trait carriers during strenuous exercise — a reason to counsel carriers before sports participation (Harrison 22e p.818)."],"pathophysiology":"Massive skeletal muscle breakdown releases intracellular contents — including myoglobin, potassium, phosphate and creatine kinase — into the circulation. Filtered myoglobin (a heme protein) is nephrotoxic and contributes to pigment-induced acute tubular necrosis; the released heme pigment also causes a false-positive dipstick reaction for blood. Concurrent volume sequestration into injured muscle produces a prerenal/hypovolaemic insult that compounds the tubular injury (Harrison 22e p.343).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Clostridial myositis/myonecrosis — predictably precipitates acute rhabdomyolysis (Harrison 22e p.1096).","Streptococcal (group A) myositis — also predictably causes acute rhabdomyolysis (Harrison 22e p.1096).","Viral myositis — influenza, echovirus, coxsackievirus and Epstein-Barr virus infections may be associated with rhabdomyolysis (Harrison 22e p.1096).","Legionella infection — reported association with rhabdomyolysis (Harrison 22e p.1096)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine dipstick for blood with microscopy — dipstick blood-positive but RBC-negative supports myoglobinuria rather than true haematuria (Harrison 22e p.343).","Urine sediment / microscopy for muddy-brown (pigmented) granular casts and tubular epithelial cells, indicating accompanying acute tubular necrosis (Harrison 22e p.343).","Serum phosphate and calcium — hyperphosphataemia and hypocalcaemia accompany acute muscle injury (Harrison 22e p.359, p.363).","Fractional excretion of sodium (FENa) — may be <1% in early rhabdomyolysis-associated ATN, limiting its discriminatory value (Harrison 22e p.343)."],"redFlags":["Hyperkalaemia from release of intracellular potassium during muscle necrosis — predictable and potentially life-threatening (Harrison 22e p.359).","Rising creatinine with pigmented granular casts signalling evolving pigment-induced acute tubular necrosis (Harrison 22e p.343)."],"prognosis":"Rhabdomyolysis-associated acute kidney injury is frequently of the milder, non-oliguric ATN type and is potentially reversible with adequate perfusion; the recovery phase may be complicated by rebound hypercalcaemia (Harrison 22e p.343, p.362).","pitfalls":["Interpreting a dipstick-positive 'haematuria' as bleeding from the urinary tract when no RBCs are seen on microscopy — this pattern reflects myoglobinuria in rhabdomyolysis (Harrison 22e p.343).","Relying on a low FENa to exclude ATN: rhabdomyolysis-related ATN can present with FENa <1% (Harrison 22e p.343).","Acute rhabdomyolysis typically causes hypocalcaemia, but the recovery phase (with renal failure) can produce rebound hypercalcaemia — calcium should not be over-corrected early (Harrison 22e p.362, p.363)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.102","Harrison 22e p.343","Harrison 22e p.359","Harrison 22e p.363","Harrison 22e p.818","Harrison 22e p.1096"],"crossLinks":["aki","hyperkalemia","hypercalcemia"]},"rheumatoid":{"name":"Rheumatoid arthritis","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chapter 370 (Rheumatoid Arthritis), pp. 2839-2849","clinicalPearls":["Early-morning joint stiffness lasting more than 1 hour that improves with activity is characteristic; the earliest joints involved are typically the small joints of the hands and feet (Harrison 22e p.2839).","Wrists, MCP and PIP joints are most often affected; isolated DIP involvement usually reflects coexistent osteoarthritis rather than RA itself (Harrison 22e p.2839).","Flexor tendon tenosynovitis is a frequent hallmark, producing reduced range of motion, weakened grip and trigger fingers, and can lead to tendon rupture (most commonly flexor pollicis longus) (Harrison 22e p.2839-2840).","Anti-CCP (ACPA) and RF are useful both diagnostically and as prognostic biomarkers; anti-CCP carries the most value for predicting worse outcomes (Harrison 22e p.2839, p.2847).","Anti-CCP has sensitivity similar to RF but much higher specificity (approaching 95%), so a positive anti-CCP in early inflammatory arthritis helps distinguish RA from other arthritides; testing both RF and anti-CCP adds incremental yield because some patients are positive for only one (Harrison 22e p.2847).","About three-quarters of RA patients are seropositive (RF and/or ACPA) and one-quarter are seronegative; seronegative RA is a clinical diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2847).","A fever above 38.3C (101F) at any point in the course should raise suspicion of systemic vasculitis or superimposed infection rather than being attributed to RA activity alone (Harrison 22e p.2840).","Approximately 30% of RA patients are ANA-positive and some have p-ANCA, but anti-MPO and anti-PR3 are not expected and their presence points to an ANCA-associated vasculitis instead (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Synovial fluid in RA is inflammatory (typically 5,000-50,000 WBC/uL, neutrophil-predominant) versus under 2,000 WBC/uL in osteoarthritis; aspiration is most useful to confirm inflammation and to exclude infection or crystal arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2847)."],"pathophysiology":"RA is a chronic, systemic, symmetric erosive polyarthritis driven by an interaction of genetic predisposition (e.g., the HLA shared epitope) and environmental triggers such as cigarette smoking. Antigen-presenting cells activate synovial CD4+ T cells (via TCR-MHC class II and CD28-CD80/86 co-stimulation), which differentiate into TH1 and TH17 subsets. These activate B cells that mature into plasma cells producing autoantibodies (RF and anti-CCP/ACPA); immune complexes form within the joint and activate complement, amplifying inflammation. T effector cells stimulate synovial macrophages and fibroblasts to release pro-inflammatory cytokines, with TNF-alpha playing a central role by upregulating endothelial adhesion molecules and driving IL-1, IL-6 and GM-CSF production. TNF-alpha also disrupts the bone balance: it raises DKK-1 (inhibiting Wnt-driven osteoblastic bone formation) and promotes osteoclastogenesis through M-CSF and RANKL signalling, with osteoprotegerin acting as a decoy that limits bone loss. The net result is pannus formation, cartilage and bone erosion, and generalised inflammatory bone loss (Harrison 22e p.2845).","additionalDifferentials":["Primary Sjogren's syndrome - polyarthralgia and mild synovitis can mimic RA, and ~50% are RF-positive; distinguished by predominant sicca symptoms, ANA positivity and anti-Ro/SSA in about two-thirds (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Spondyloarthropathies (psoriatic arthritis, enteropathy-associated arthritis) - distinguished by sacroiliitis, enthesopathy, and presence of psoriasis or inflammatory bowel disease (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Polymyalgia rheumatica in elderly patients - can resemble seronegative RA; PMR centres on neck/shoulder and hip girdle, whereas RA predominantly affects wrists/hands and ankles/feet, especially at onset (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Remitting seronegative symmetrical synovitis with pitting edema (RS3PE) - features prominent distal-limb pitting edema (unusual in RA) and responds promptly to low-dose prednisone (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Paraneoplastic syndromes - may be confused with early RA (Harrison 22e p.2847)."],"infectionMimics":["Virus-related polyarthritis syndromes - can mimic RA and cause transient RF production, but usually resolve within 2-3 weeks (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Hepatitis C-related arthropathy - often involves small joints of the hands and is RF-positive in about half of cases, but generally anti-CCP negative (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Subacute bacterial endocarditis and chronic hepatitis B/C - chronic infections that can produce a positive RF and confound interpretation (Harrison 22e p.2847)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chronic tophaceous gout - can mimic severe RA, and tophi may be mistaken for rheumatoid nodules (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Pseudogout (CPPD crystal arthritis) - inflammatory arthritis excluded via synovial fluid crystal analysis (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Osteoarthritis - non-inflammatory; DIP involvement and synovial fluid under 2,000 WBC/uL favour OA over RA (Harrison 22e p.2839, p.2847)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Anti-CCP (ACPA) antibodies - high specificity (~95%) for RA; useful diagnostically and prognostically (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Synovial fluid aspiration with cell count and crystal/microbiology analysis - confirms inflammatory arthritis and excludes infection or crystal disease (Harrison 22e p.2847).","MRI - greatest sensitivity for synovitis, effusions and early bone/bone-marrow changes; bone marrow edema predicts subsequent erosions (Harrison 22e p.2848).","Musculoskeletal ultrasound with power Doppler - detects synovitis, increased joint vascularity and erosions, often more than plain films; portable and radiation-free (Harrison 22e p.2848).","ANA and ANCA - may be positive in RA (~30% ANA, occasional p-ANCA), but anti-MPO/anti-PR3 should be negative (Harrison 22e p.2847).","High-resolution chest CT - to detect and characterise interstitial lung disease (UIP vs NSIP patterns) when respiratory symptoms arise (Harrison 22e p.2840)."],"redFlags":["Fever above 38.3C (101F) during the course should prompt evaluation for systemic vasculitis or infection rather than being ascribed to RA activity (Harrison 22e p.2840).","RA patients have the highest incidence of septic (most often S. aureus) arthritis due to chronically inflamed joints, glucocorticoid therapy and breakdown of nodules/vasculitic ulcers; TNF inhibitors add risk of mycobacterial and other infections (Harrison 22e Chap. 124, p.~).","Atlantoaxial (C1-C2) subluxation can cause compressive myelopathy; neurologic signs may evolve slowly with progressive instability (now seen in under 10% of patients) (Harrison 22e p.2840).","New-onset numbness, tingling or focal weakness (mononeuritis multiplex) suggests systemic rheumatoid vasculitis (Harrison 22e p.2841).","Two- to fourfold increased risk of lymphoma (most often diffuse large B-cell), greatest with high disease activity or Felty's syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2841)."],"prognosis":"Most patients show persistent, waxing-and-waning progressive disease; about 10% (particularly seronegative) remit spontaneously within 6 months, while a minority follow either a relapsing explosive course or an aggressively erosive course (now less common with modern therapy). Early disability is driven mainly by joint inflammation (potentially reversible) and later by cumulative irreversible joint damage. Overall mortality is roughly twice that of the general population, with excess deaths from respiratory illness, cardiovascular disease and infection; median life expectancy is shortened by about 7 years in men and 3 years in women. Worse survival is associated with systemic extraarticular involvement, low functional capacity, low socioeconomic status, low education and chronic prednisone use. Cardiovascular disease is the most common cause of death (Harrison 22e p.2848). Seropositivity for RF and especially anti-CCP predicts worse outcomes (Harrison 22e p.2847).","pitfalls":["The 2010 ACR-EULAR criteria are classification (not diagnostic) criteria intended to identify early patients likely to progress; a positive RF or anti-CCP alone does not establish the diagnosis without supporting clinical features (Harrison 22e p.2847).","A negative RF does not exclude RA - IgM RF is found in only ~75% of patients, and RF also occurs in other connective tissue diseases, chronic infections and 1-5% of healthy people (Harrison 22e p.2847).","Accelerated growth of subcutaneous nodules can occur in up to 10% of patients on long-term methotrexate and should not automatically be read as worsening disease (Harrison 22e p.2840).","Rheumatoid nodules and chronic tophaceous gout tophi can be confused with one another (Harrison 22e p.2840, p.2847).","Periarticular osteopenia, the classic earliest x-ray finding, is difficult to appreciate on plain (especially digitised) films, so early radiographs may appear normal (Harrison 22e p.2848)."],"severityClassification":"Diagnosis is supported by the 2010 ACR-EULAR classification criteria (for patients with at least one joint of definite clinical synovitis not better explained by another disease), scored 0-10 across four domains - joint involvement (0-5), serology (RF/ACPA, 0-3), acute-phase reactants (CRP/ESR, 0-1), and symptom duration (0-1); a score of 6 or more fulfils requirements for definite RA. Disease activity (the main driver of treatment decisions) is tracked with composite indices: ACR 20/50/70 improvement criteria (mainly for trials) and continuous measures including DAS, SDAI, CDAI and RAPID3 used in practice. Functional disability is measured with the Health Assessment Questionnaire (HAQ) (Harrison 22e p.2847-2848).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2839","Harrison 22e p.2840","Harrison 22e p.2841","Harrison 22e p.2842","Harrison 22e p.2845","Harrison 22e p.2847","Harrison 22e p.2848"],"crossLinks":["crystal_arthritis","pmr","sle_flare"]},"sah":{"name":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage","system":"Neurology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch. 440, p.3457-3461","clinicalPearls":["The single most important feature of aneurysmal SAH headache is its sudden (abrupt) onset, even more than its severity; ruptures may occasionally present as only moderate headache or as a change in the patient's usual headache pattern (p.3458).","Transient loss of consciousness occurs in nearly half of patients at the moment of rupture, reflecting an abrupt rise in intracranial pressure; many patients only report the headache after regaining consciousness (p.3458).","A third nerve palsy with pupillary dilation and pain around/behind the eye suggests an expanding aneurysm at the posterior communicating artery-internal carotid junction; a sixth nerve palsy may indicate a cavernous-sinus aneurysm (p.3458).","Quivering, jerking, or extensor posturing accompanying loss of consciousness at SAH onset is usually caused by the sharp ICP rise rather than true seizure activity (p.3461).","ECG changes mimicking cardiac ischaemia (ST/T changes, long QT, peaked or deeply inverted T waves) and a reversible (catecholamine-mediated) cardiomyopathy with regional wall-motion abnormalities can occur after SAH; an asymptomatic troponin rise is common (p.3459-3460).","Nimodipine improves outcomes in aneurysmal SAH, apparently by limiting ischaemic injury rather than by reducing vasospasm itself (knowledge, no dose) (p.3461).","Perimesencephalic (idiopathic) SAH localised to the perimesencephalic cisterns is benign, probably venous/capillary in origin, and angiography is typically unrevealing (p.3458)."],"pathophysiology":"Excluding trauma, the commonest cause is rupture of a saccular ('berry') aneurysm, which forms at bifurcations of large- to medium-sized intracranial arteries (about 89% in the anterior circulation, mostly on the circle of Willis). Aneurysm walls show loss of the internal elastic lamina at the neck base with medial thinning and replacement of smooth muscle by connective tissue; rupture is usually at the thin-walled dome through a tear often <=0.5 mm. Rupture deposits blood in the subarachnoid space of the basal cisterns (and sometimes adjacent parenchyma), abruptly raising ICP. Other causes include vascular malformations (AVM, dural arteriovenous fistula) and subarachnoid extension of a primary intracerebral haemorrhage. Roughly 2% of adults harbour intracranial aneurysms and about 20% of affected patients have multiple aneurysms (frequently mirror-image bilateral). Delayed cerebral ischaemia is thought to arise from clot breakdown products acting on basal arteries to cause vasospasm and vessel-wall thickening, but also from vasospasm-independent mechanisms (microthrombosis, inflammation, microvascular dysregulation, cortical spreading depolarisation). Hyponatraemia results from natriuresis with volume depletion ('cerebral salt wasting'), mediated by atrial and brain natriuretic peptides (p.3457-3461).","additionalDifferentials":["Primary thunderclap headache / reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome (RCVS) — many otherwise-undiagnosed thunderclap headaches are attributed to RCVS (p.3462)","Cervicocephalic arterial dissection (p.3462)","Cerebral venous (sinus) thrombosis (p.3462)","Ruptured arteriovenous malformation — but blood is usually intraparenchymal, not deposited in the basal cisterns, and symptomatic vasospasm is rare (p.3457)","Dural arteriovenous fistula (a recognised cause of SAH) (p.3457)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Thunderclap migraine — a migraine variant that can simulate SAH; a definitive workup to exclude aneurysm/other intracranial pathology is required before attributing sudden severe headache to it (p.3458)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Noncontrast CT detects >95% of cases when obtained within 72 h; sensitivity falls as time from ictus increases (p.3459)","Four-vessel conventional x-ray (catheter) angiography — gold standard to localise/define aneurysm anatomy and detect additional aneurysms; ruptured aneurysm can be treated at the same sitting (p.3459)","CT perfusion imaging — adjunct to identify potentially reversible ischaemic deficits in suspected delayed cerebral ischaemia (p.3459)","Transcranial Doppler ultrasound — daily/alternate-day bedside monitoring detects rising flow velocities of vasospasm (p.3459)","Serial (daily or twice-daily) serum electrolytes to detect early hyponatraemia (p.3459)","ECG and echocardiography to detect neurogenic cardiac changes and stress (catecholamine) cardiomyopathy (p.3459-3460)"],"redFlags":["Sudden unexplained headache of any location may represent a sentinel bleed (small aneurysmal leak) heralding imminent major haemorrhage and must be investigated (p.3459)","Declining mental status with worsening headache 4-14 days after SAH suggests delayed cerebral ischaemia from vasospasm (p.3459-3460)","High ICP refractory to treatment is a poor prognostic sign (p.3461)"],"prognosis":"Overall mortality of aneurysmal SAH is about 35%, with roughly a third dying before reaching hospital; over half of survivors are left with significant neurologic deficits from the initial bleed, delayed cerebral ischaemia, or hydrocephalus. Outcome worsens with increasing presentation grade: grade 1 (Hunt-Hess) patients rarely die if treated, whereas mortality for grades 4-5 may reach about 60%. In an untreated aneurysm, rebleeding risk is about 20% in the first 2 weeks, 30% in the first month, then about 3% per year; rerupture (~30% in the first month, peaking in the first 7 days) carries about 50% mortality. Delayed cerebral ischaemia occurs in about 30% and is the leading cause of delayed morbidity and death among initial survivors. Unruptured aneurysms <7 mm had ~0% 5-year rupture risk in a large study (though many ruptured aneurysms are <6 mm); giant aneurysms (>25 mm) carry ~8-10% annual rupture risk (p.3458-3460).","pitfalls":["A normal CT does not exclude SAH, especially as time elapses or with small or infratentorial bleeds; if CT is non-diagnostic and there is no mass lesion/obstructive hydrocephalus, lumbar puncture for xanthochromia is required (p.3458-3459).","Xanthochromia (yellow CSF from haemoglobin-to-bilirubin conversion) takes 6-12 h to appear, peaks at ~48 h, and persists 1-4 weeks; an LP performed too early may miss it (p.3459).","Free-water restriction should NOT be used to treat SAH-associated hyponatraemia (cerebral salt wasting): the resulting hypovolaemia/hypotension can precipitate cerebral ischaemia (p.3459, p.3461).","Marked hyponatraemia of several days' duration must not be corrected too rapidly because of the risk of osmotic demyelination syndrome (p.3461).","Lumbar puncture before imaging is appropriate only when CT is unavailable at the time of suspected SAH (p.3459)."],"severityClassification":"Two presentation-based clinical grading scales are used: the Hunt-Hess scale (1 = asymptomatic/minimal headache and slight nuchal rigidity through 5 = coma with reflex posturing or flaccidity) and the World Federation of Neurosurgical Societies (WFNS) scale based on Glasgow Coma Scale score and motor deficits (1 = GCS 15 no motor deficit through 5 = GCS 3-6). Higher grade predicts worse outcome. The modified Fisher CT grading system predicts symptomatic vasospasm by the amount and distribution of subarachnoid/intraventricular blood ('thin' <1 mm vs 'thick' >=1 mm), with risk rising from 0% (grade 0) to ~40% (grade 4, thick blood with intraventricular blood) (p.3459-3460).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3457","Harrison 22e p.3458","Harrison 22e p.3459","Harrison 22e p.3460","Harrison 22e p.3461","Harrison 22e p.3462"],"crossLinks":["ich","ischemic_stroke","MENINGITIS","hyponatremia"]},"salicylate_tox":{"name":"Salicylate toxicity","system":"Toxicology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"p.370, p.376, p.3705-3706, p.3714-3715","clinicalPearls":["Classic course: early nausea/vomiting and hyperventilation with respiratory alkalosis and alkaline urine, evolving to a mixed respiratory alkalosis plus high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with paradoxical aciduria; late stages show acidemia with CNS and respiratory depression (p.3714).","Salicylates are the most common cause of drug-induced respiratory alkalosis, acting by direct stimulation of the medullary respiratory chemoreceptor (p.376).","Only part of the elevated anion gap is attributable to salicylate itself; concurrent lactic acid production and other endogenous unmeasured anions also contribute (p.370, p.3714).","Cerebral and pulmonary edema can develop in severe poisoning; non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema (ARDS) is a recognized complication (p.3705, p.3714).","Hypoglycemia, hypocalcemia, hypokalemia, and seizures can occur in salicylate toxicity (p.3714).","Ketosis can accompany salicylate poisoning and may mimic other causes of ketoacidosis (p.3705).","Salicylate is detected on quantitative serum assay; serum levels guide management and are usually available within an hour (p.3706).","Enteric-coated and sustained-release salicylate preparations cause delayed/slow absorption and can form gastric concretions (bezoars), acting as a 'toxic time-bomb' with continued rising levels (p.3705, p.3752)."],"pathophysiology":"Salicylate increases sensitivity of the CNS respiratory center and directly stimulates the medullary chemoreceptor, driving hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis. It also uncouples oxidative phosphorylation, inhibits Krebs cycle enzymes, and stimulates carbohydrate and lipid metabolism, generating unmeasured endogenous anions (and increased lactate) that produce a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis (p.376, p.3714).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Other high-anion-gap metabolic acidoses: methanol, ethylene glycol, alcoholic/diabetic/starvation ketoacidosis, lactic acidosis, kidney failure (p.412)","Other causes of ketosis: acetone, isopropyl alcohol, alcoholic ketoacidosis (p.3705)","Other causes of drug-induced respiratory alkalosis: theophylline/aminophylline; also sepsis, hepatic failure, pregnancy/progesterone (p.376)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial quantitative serum salicylate level (guides management) (p.3706)","Anion gap and serum lactate (lactate typically lower than the anion gap in salicylate vs near-equal in shock/organ failure) (p.3705)","Serum glucose and calcium (risk of hypoglycemia, hypocalcemia) (p.3714)","Potassium (hypokalemia) (p.3714)","Abdominal x-ray/CT may show radiopaque enteric-coated salicylate tablets or concretions (p.3706)"],"redFlags":["Coma, seizures, cerebral edema (p.3714)","Pulmonary edema / ARDS (p.3705, p.3714)","Renal failure (impairs clearance) (p.376)","Progressive acid-base disturbance / late acidemia with CNS and respiratory depression (p.3714)","Salicylate level >7 mmol/L (100 mg/dL) after acute overdose (p.3714)"],"prognosis":null,"pitfalls":["Vigorous gastric lavage, if used, should be with isotonic saline, NOT NaHCO3 (p.370).","Only a portion of the anion gap is due to salicylate itself; do not assume the whole gap reflects drug level (p.370).","Acid (acidic) diuresis is never indicated and is potentially harmful (p.3752).","Acetazolamide can cause systemic metabolic acidosis if excreted bicarbonate is not replaced, which markedly reduces salicylate clearance (p.370).","Delayed/slow absorption from enteric-coated, sustained-release, or concretion-forming salicylate means a single early level can underestimate toxicity; repeat levels (p.3705, p.3752)."],"severityClassification":"Stages by clinical/acid-base course: (1) early respiratory alkalosis with alkalemia, alkaluria, nausea/vomiting and hyperventilation; (2) mixed respiratory alkalosis plus high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with paradoxical aciduria; (3) late acidemia with CNS and respiratory depression, cerebral and pulmonary edema in severe cases. Severe-toxicity markers include coma, cerebral edema, seizures, pulmonary edema, renal failure, progressive acid-base disturbance, and salicylate level >7 mmol/L (100 mg/dL) after acute overdose (p.3714).","references":["Harrison 22e p.370 (Fluid/Electrolyte/Acid-Base — drug- and toxin-induced acidosis: salicylates)","Harrison 22e p.376 (Respiratory alkalosis — salicylate mechanism)","Harrison 22e p.412 (Causes of high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis)","Harrison 22e p.3705-3706 (Poisoning and Drug Overdose — laboratory/radiologic assessment)","Harrison 22e p.3714-3715 (Table 470-4 — pathophysiologic features of salicylate poisoning)","Harrison 22e p.3752 (Enhancement of poison elimination; toxic time-bombs)"],"crossLinks":["drug_intox"]},"sarcoidosis":{"name":"Sarcoidosis","system":"Pulmonary / Multisystem","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.379 (Sarcoidosis), pp.2921-2929","clinicalPearls":["Sarcoidosis is a systemic granulomatous disease of unknown cause; the lung is involved in over 90% of cases, but roughly half of patients have extrapulmonary disease and almost any organ can be affected (p.2921, p.2924).","Diagnosis rests on three criteria: a compatible clinical picture, nonnecrotizing granulomatous inflammation on tissue, AND exclusion of other causes of granulomas — it is never made with absolute certainty, so new signs during follow-up should be re-evaluated (p.2923-2924).","Löfgren syndrome (fever, erythema nodosum, ankle arthralgias, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy) occurs in up to ~10% of cases and, like Heerfordt syndrome, carries an excellent prognosis with frequent spontaneous resolution (p.2923).","Heerfordt syndrome = facial nerve palsy, fever, anterior uveitis, and bilateral parotid gland enlargement (p.2923, p.2928).","In very compelling presentations (classic Löfgren, Heerfordt, lupus pernio, or asymptomatic symmetrical bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy), biopsy may be deferred, but close clinical follow-up is still required (p.2924).","A BAL CD4:CD8 lymphocyte ratio >3.5 supports the diagnosis when combined with consistent clinical/radiographic findings, but a normal ratio does NOT exclude sarcoidosis; BAL is also important to exclude infection (p.2924).","Endobronchial ultrasound-guided lymph node sampling has high diagnostic yield with low procedural risk when hilar/mediastinal nodes are the prominent feature (p.2924).","Sarcoidosis is upper-lobe predominant on imaging with a lymphangitic (perilymphatic/subpleural/peribronchovascular) nodular distribution; airflow obstruction can occur in up to one-third of patients despite the classically restrictive pattern (p.2924-2925).","Every sarcoidosis patient should be screened for cardiac involvement (baseline ECG) because severity of lung disease does not predict cardiac sarcoidosis and sudden death can be the initial presentation (p.2925-2926).","Baseline workup in every patient should include PFTs, chest imaging, an ophthalmologic exam (to detect clinically silent uveitis), an ECG, and serum creatinine/alkaline phosphatase/calcium/CBC (p.2924).","Hypercalcemia from dysregulated macrophage/granuloma production of calcitriol (1,25-(OH)2 vitamin D) occurs despite suppressed PTH; patients should limit vitamin D intake, avoid excess sunlight, and stay hydrated (p.2927-2928).","Optic neuropathy in ocular sarcoidosis is an emergency requiring immediate immunosuppression to prevent sudden permanent vision loss (p.2927).","Smoking is a consistent NEGATIVE predictor of incident sarcoidosis (reduced odds), an unusual feature among lung diseases (p.2921-2922)."],"pathophysiology":"Sarcoidosis is thought to arise from a heightened, antigen-driven host immune response to an undetermined (likely inhaled bioaerosol or inorganic) environmental antigen in a genetically susceptible person; the inciting antigen may differ between individuals/populations. A poorly degradable material is presented to lymphocytes by dendritic cells/macrophages via MHC, triggering an innate cytokine/chemokine cascade and recruitment of adaptive immune cells. The immunopathologic hallmark is highly polarized CD4+ TH1 lymphocytes (less so CD8+) producing IFN-gamma, with upregulation of TH1-promoting cytokines/chemokines (IL-1, IL-2, IL-15, IL-18, CXCR3) that activate macrophages and drive formation of tightly organized nonnecrotizing (noncaseating) granulomas. Altered regulatory T-cell and TH17 responses are implicated in failure to resolve and in progression to fibrosis. Granulomas comprise macrophages, epithelioid cells, and multinucleated giant cells rimmed by lymphocytes/fibroblasts (CD4+ central, CD8+ and B cells peripheral). Mycobacterial proteins (mKatG, ESAT-6, superoxide dismutase A, antigen 85A, heat-shock proteins) and Cutibacterium acnes have been disproportionately implicated; the transmissibility seen with the Kveim-Siltzbach reagent and via organ transplantation supports an antigenic trigger (p.2922-2923).","additionalDifferentials":["Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (granulomas are loosely formed and nonnecrotizing)","Chronic beryllium disease","Silicosis","Talc inhalation or injection (foreign-body reaction)","Granulomatous reaction to tattoo ink","IgG4-related disease","ANCA-associated vasculitis (e.g., GPA)","Granulomatous-lymphocytic interstitial lung disease (associated with common variable immunodeficiency)","Rheumatoid nodules","Inflammatory bowel disease / Crohn-related granulomas","Bronchocentric granulomatosis","Necrotizing sarcoid granulomatosis (rare, vasculitis-associated)","Drug-induced sarcoid-like reaction (immune checkpoint inhibitors, antiretrovirals, interferon, TNF-alpha antagonists)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis (caseating/necrotizing granulomas)","Atypical/nontuberculous mycobacterial infection","Histoplasmosis","Coccidioidomycosis","Blastomycosis","Aspergillosis","Cryptococcosis","Toxoplasmosis","Schistosomiasis","Herpes zoster","Tularemia (Francisella tularensis)","Q fever (Coxiella burnetii)","Bartonella henselae infection"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Chronic beryllium disease (berylliosis)","Silicosis","Aspiration pneumonitis","IgG4-related disease","Granulomatous reaction surrounding malignancy or systemic sarcoid-like reaction to malignancy/chemoimmunotherapy","Lymphoma presenting with hilar/mediastinal adenopathy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Pulmonary function tests at baseline and serially to track impairment/progression","Baseline ophthalmologic examination to detect clinically silent uveitis","Baseline ECG in every patient to screen for conduction block, ST-T changes, Q waves, frequent PVCs, or resting tachycardia","Chest CT to define disease extent and guide biopsy (not required in all patients)","Bronchoscopy with endobronchial ultrasound-guided lymph node sampling and/or transbronchial biopsy when hilar/mediastinal nodes or lung infiltrates predominate","BAL with lymphocyte subset analysis (CD4:CD8 ratio >3.5 is supportive) and to exclude infection","18F-FDG PET-CT to localize active inflammation and guide biopsy in difficult cases","Serum creatinine, alkaline phosphatase, complete blood count, and liver enzymes","25- and 1,25-hydroxyvitamin D levels and urinary calcium (hypercalciuria up to ~50%)","Cardiac MRI with gadolinium (preferred) or cardiac PET-CT for suspected cardiac sarcoidosis; Holter/signal-averaged ECG/echocardiography as indicated","Gadolinium-enhanced brain/spinal MRI plus CSF analysis (mononuclear pleocytosis, raised protein; CSF ACE/sIL-2R/lysozyme are supportive but not diagnostic) for suspected neurosarcoidosis","Echocardiography to screen for pulmonary hypertension; right heart catheterization is the gold standard","Soluble IL-2 receptor and serum ACE as adjuncts to monitor disease activity/burden (not diagnostic)"],"redFlags":["Exclude infection (especially TB and fungi such as histoplasmosis) before starting immunosuppression — abundant necrosis on biopsy points away from sarcoidosis toward infection","Acute symptomatic hypercalcemia can require hospital admission and urgent corticosteroids","Optic neuropathy threatens sudden permanent vision loss and is an ophthalmologic emergency","Cardiac involvement (new-onset AV block, ventricular arrhythmia, cardiomyopathy in younger patients) carries risk of sudden death and can be the first presentation","Neurologic involvement (basal/cranial nerve disease, mass lesions, meningitis) can cause neurologic demise","Untreated hypercalcemia leads to nephrocalcinosis and renal failure","New reproductive-organ nodules/masses require exclusion of malignancy given the rarity of genital sarcoidosis"],"prognosis":"Disease resolves spontaneously in just over half of patients, but 30-40% develop chronic disease threatening vital organs or causing debilitating symptoms. Most patients show radiographic or spirometric improvement by 2 years (treated or untreated). Death attributable to sarcoidosis occurs in roughly 1-5%, most often from progressive respiratory insufficiency/pulmonary fibrosis (leading cause in the US/Northern Europe) or cardiac involvement (predominant cause of death in Japan). A robust acute initial immune response predicts a better outcome: Löfgren and Heerfordt syndromes remit spontaneously in up to ~80%, and immunosuppression is rarely needed. Adverse prognostic factors include lupus pernio, chronic uveitis, older age, chronic hypercalcemia, nephrocalcinosis, African ancestry, pulmonary hypertension, fibrotic pulmonary disease, sinus involvement, cystic bone lesions, neurosarcoidosis, and cardiac involvement. Relapses are common and associated with shorter treatment duration (p.2928-2929).","pitfalls":["A normal BAL CD4:CD8 ratio, normal PFTs, or a normal lung exam does NOT exclude sarcoidosis despite active granulomatous inflammation (p.2924).","Serum ACE has low sensitivity and is not a reliable diagnostic or prognostic test; it only roughly tracks granuloma burden and is best used to monitor activity (p.2929).","Scadding (CXR) stages reveal overall resolution rates but do NOT predict disease activity, lung-function progression, or extrapulmonary involvement in an individual (p.2929).","Cardiac involvement is frequently subclinical and endomyocardial biopsy has poor sensitivity (patchy granulomas, basal predominance, sparing of the right ventricle), so a negative biopsy does not exclude cardiac sarcoidosis (p.2926).","Failing to screen for cardiac, ocular, and neurologic disease at baseline misses life- or sight-threatening involvement because pulmonary severity does not predict it (p.2924-2926).","Isolated extrapulmonary sarcoidosis (cardiac, hepatic, neurologic) can occur without lung disease and is easily overlooked (p.2926-2927).","Loosely formed granulomas suggest hypersensitivity pneumonitis and abundant necrosis suggests infection — misreading these as sarcoidosis is a classic error (p.2924).","Always re-evaluate alternative causes for isolated hepatic granulomas, which carry a broad differential (p.2927)."],"severityClassification":"Scadding chest x-ray stages describe the pulmonary pattern and correlate with spontaneous resolution rates (NOT with activity or prognosis in the individual): Stage 1 = bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy without parenchymal involvement (resolution ~55-90%); Stage 2 = bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy WITH parenchymal involvement (resolution ~40-70%); Stage 3 = parenchymal involvement without lymphadenopathy (resolution ~10-20%); Stage 4 = pulmonary fibrosis with architectural distortion and volume loss (resolution ~0%) (p.2925).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2921","Harrison 22e p.2922","Harrison 22e p.2923","Harrison 22e p.2924","Harrison 22e p.2925","Harrison 22e p.2926","Harrison 22e p.2927","Harrison 22e p.2928","Harrison 22e p.2929"],"crossLinks":["PULMONARY_TB","ild","hypercalcemia","lung_cancer"]},"seizure_epilepsy":{"name":"Seizure / epilepsy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Chapter 436 (Seizures and Epilepsy), p.3408-3428","clinicalPearls":["A seizure is a transient event from abnormal excessive or synchronous neuronal activity; epilepsy is the distinct condition of recurrent unprovoked seizures from a chronic underlying process. A single seizure, or seizures due to a correctable/avoidable cause, is not by itself epilepsy (p.3408).","Operationally, epilepsy is defined as two or more unprovoked seizures, though a single seizure with clinical or EEG features portending high recurrence risk can establish the diagnosis (p.3408-3409).","Seizures are fundamentally classified as focal (network limited to one hemisphere; often structural) versus generalized (rapidly engaging both hemispheres) versus unknown onset; the older terms 'partial', 'simple/complex partial' are no longer used (p.3409).","Focal seizures are subclassified by awareness (intact vs impaired) and onset (motor vs nonmotor); a focal seizure can evolve to a bilateral tonic-clonic seizure (formerly 'secondary generalization') (p.3409).","Distinguishing a focal seizure that evolves to generalized from a primary generalized tonic-clonic seizure is critical but difficult, because bystanders emphasize the dramatic convulsive phase and overlook the subtle focal onset; a careful history for a preceding aura or focal features, and EEG, are needed because evaluation and treatment differ substantially (p.3410).","Status epilepticus is continuous seizures, or repetitive discrete seizures with impaired consciousness between them; for generalized convulsive status epilepticus the practical operational threshold is seizures lasting beyond 5 minutes (older definitions used 15-30 min) (p.3425).","Generalized convulsive status epilepticus (GCSE) is a medical emergency: prolonged seizures cause cardiorespiratory dysfunction, hyperthermia, and metabolic derangements that can produce irreversible CNS injury (p.3425-3426).","Treatment principle for status epilepticus is an escalating sequence beginning with a benzodiazepine, followed by an IV antiseizure drug, with anesthetic agents for refractory cases - alongside attention to airway, oxygenation, glucose, and reversible metabolic causes (no doses given here) (p.3426).","After 30-45 min of uninterrupted GCSE the motor signs can become very subtle (only fine finger twitching or rapid eye movements); if a patient stops overt convulsions yet remains comatose, an EEG is required to exclude ongoing (nonconvulsive) status epilepticus (p.3426).","If the sole cause of a seizure is a correctable metabolic disturbance (e.g., electrolyte or glucose abnormality), or a precipitant drug/illicit substance, treatment targets the underlying problem and chronic antiseizure drugs are usually unnecessary (p.3419).","An apparently unprovoked first seizure carries a recurrence risk estimated at roughly 31-71% in the first 12 months; accepted risk factors favoring treatment are prior brain insult (stroke/trauma), epileptiform EEG, a significant imaging abnormality, or a nocturnal seizure (p.3420).","A serum prolactin rise in the 30 min after most generalized and some focal seizures can support an epileptic cause, but it is unreliable because baseline levels are rarely available and some drugs elevate prolactin (p.3419).","The clinical range of focal seizure behaviors is extremely broad, so caution is advised before dismissing paroxysmal stereotypic bizarre episodes as non-epileptic (p.3409).","The age of the patient strongly informs the likely etiology of new seizures - e.g., febrile seizures and genetic/developmental causes in young children versus cerebrovascular disease, tumor, metabolic disturbance and degenerative disease in older adults (p.3411-3414)."],"pathophysiology":"A seizure reflects a shift in the normal balance between excitation and inhibition in the CNS. At the cellular level the hallmark of focal seizure activity is the electrographic spike, generated by near-simultaneous bursting (the 'paroxysmal depolarization shift') of local excitatory neurons - a prolonged membrane depolarization driven by calcium influx that opens voltage-gated sodium channels, followed by a GABA- or potassium-mediated hyperpolarizing afterpotential. Surrounding inhibition normally limits spread; when recruitment overwhelms it (via rising extracellular potassium, presynaptic calcium accumulation with enhanced transmitter release, NMDA-receptor activation, and ephaptic effects), activity propagates through local cortical and long commissural pathways (e.g., corpus callosum). Generalized 3-Hz spike-and-wave discharges of absence seizures arise from thalamocortical oscillatory circuits involving GABA-B receptors, T-type calcium channels, and potassium channels. Many causes converge on three interacting factor classes: endogenous (genetic/developmental seizure-threshold) factors, epileptogenic factors (CNS injury such as severe penetrating head trauma, stroke, infection, or neurodegeneration that, over months to years, transforms a normal network into a chronically hyperexcitable one - 'epileptogenesis'), and precipitating factors (sleep deprivation, stress, hormonal change, toxins, certain drugs, photic stimulation). Many inherited epilepsies are channelopathies due to ion-channel mutations (p.3411-3415).","additionalDifferentials":["Syncope (vasovagal, cardiac arrhythmia, valvular heart disease, cardiac failure, orthostatic hypotension) - the most frequent diagnostic dilemma versus generalized seizure (p.3418-3419)","Transient ischemic attack, particularly basilar artery TIA (p.3419)","Migraine - confusional migraine, basilar migraine (p.3419)","Acute psychosis (p.3416)","Sleep disorders - narcolepsy/cataplexy, benign sleep myoclonus (p.3419)","Movement disorders - tics, nonepileptic myoclonus, paroxysmal choreoathetosis (p.3419)","In children: breath-holding spells, benign paroxysmal vertigo, night terrors, sleepwalking, apnea, abdominal migraine with cyclic vomiting (p.3419)"],"infectionMimics":["CNS infection (meningitis/encephalitis) presenting with seizures and altered mentation - lumbar puncture indicated if suspected and mandatory in all HIV-infected patients even when asymptomatic (p.3417)","Parasitic CNS infection such as neurocysticercosis as a cause of seizures in adolescents/young adults from endemic areas (p.3413)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Psychogenic nonepileptic seizures (PNES) - nonepileptic behaviors, often a conversion reaction; clues include side-to-side head turning, ictal eye closure, asynchronous large-amplitude limb shaking, four-limb twitching without loss of consciousness, pelvic thrusting, and prolonged waxing/waning episodes; surface EEG may be normal especially for frontal-lobe-like events, and PNES can coexist with true epilepsy (p.3419)","Convulsive syncope - a brief (1-10 s) burst of convulsive movements at the onset of a faint, especially if the patient stays upright; postictal confusion is much less prominent than after a true seizure (p.3419)","Alcoholic blackouts and delirium tremens (p.3419)","Hypoglycemia and hypoxia as metabolic mimics of seizure (p.3419)","Hyperventilation and panic attack (p.3419)","Effects of psychoactive drugs / hallucinogens (p.3419)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Complete blood count, magnesium, liver and renal function tests, and urinalysis in addition to glucose/electrolytes/calcium to find metabolic causes (p.3416)","Toxicology screen of blood and urine, especially when no clear precipitant is identified (p.3417)","Lumbar puncture when meningitis/encephalitis is suspected; mandatory in all HIV-infected patients even without signs of infection (p.3417)","Serum and CSF autoantibody testing when epilepsy onset is fulminant or accompanied by psychiatric/cognitive disturbance (autoimmune/paraneoplastic encephalitis) (p.3417)","MRI preferred over CT for detecting structural lesions; CT performed emergently when MRI is not immediately available or when a mass/infection is suspected (p.3417-3418)","Interictal and ictal EEG, with activating procedures (hyperventilation, photic stimulation, sleep, sleep deprivation); a normal routine interictal EEG does NOT exclude epilepsy - it is normal in 50-80% of known epileptics initially (p.3416-3417)","Continuous video-EEG telemetry for poorly characterized events or seizures that are difficult to control, and to distinguish epileptic from psychogenic events (p.3417, p.3419)","Genetic testing in infants/children with suspected genetic epilepsy syndromes, and selected older patients with an undiagnosed early-life epilepsy syndrome (p.3418)"],"redFlags":["Generalized convulsive status epilepticus - immediate emergency due to risk of cardiorespiratory failure, hyperthermia, metabolic derangement, and irreversible CNS injury (p.3425-3426)","Persistent coma after overt convulsions stop - suspect ongoing nonconvulsive status epilepticus and obtain EEG (p.3426)","Focal features, focal neurologic deficit, or abnormal/changing neurologic exam - points to a structural cause (mass, stroke, infection, trauma, degenerative disease) requiring imaging (p.3416)","Fulminant new-onset seizures with psychiatric symptoms and cognitive change - suspect autoimmune/antibody-mediated encephalitis (p.3413-3414, p.3417)","First seizure in a patient with HIV - lumbar puncture is mandatory even without signs of infection (p.3417)","Atonic ('drop attack') seizures - substantial risk of direct head injury from the fall (p.3410)"],"prognosis":"About half of people respond to the first antiseizure drug; roughly 30% have ongoing seizures despite drug combinations (refractory epilepsy). People with epilepsy have a 2-3 times higher mortality than matched controls, mostly driven by the underlying etiology (e.g., tumor, stroke); excess deaths also arise from accidents, status epilepticus, and sudden unexpected death in epilepsy (SUDEP), which typically affects young patients with convulsive seizures and tends to occur at night, possibly via brainstem-mediated cardiorespiratory/arousal effects. Simple febrile seizures do not raise the risk of later epilepsy, whereas complex febrile seizures carry a 2-5% risk. Severe penetrating head trauma carries up to a ~45% (penetrating/depressed fracture/hemorrhage/prolonged coma: 30-50%) risk of subsequent epilepsy, closed head injury with contusion 5-25%, and mild concussion only slightly increased risk. Favorable factors for staying seizure-free after planned drug withdrawal include 1-5 years of control, a single seizure type, normal exam/intelligence, normal EEG, and no family history (p.3411, p.3414, p.3420, p.3424, p.3426-3427).","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude a treatable provoking cause (metabolic, toxic, structural, or infective) before labeling a patient as having epilepsy - many such seizures need only correction of the underlying problem, not chronic antiseizure drugs (p.3408, p.3419, p.3416-3417).","Assuming a normal interictal EEG rules out epilepsy - the initial routine EEG is normal in 50-80% of patients with known epilepsy, and EEG has limited sensitivity (p.3417).","Missing nonconvulsive status epilepticus: in altered consciousness or persistent coma without overt convulsions, NCSE can be present and is only revealed by EEG; intermittent seizures with prolonged postictal states are easily overlooked as causes of unexplained encephalopathy (p.3426).","Overlooking a focal onset by focusing on the dramatic generalized convulsive phase, thereby misclassifying the seizure and choosing the wrong evaluation/treatment pathway (p.3410).","Mistaking psychogenic nonepileptic seizures for epilepsy (or vice versa) on clinical grounds alone - even experienced epileptologists err; PNES and epilepsy can coexist, and video-EEG is the arbiter (p.3419).","Misinterpreting convulsive syncope (brief convulsive jerks at the start of a faint) as a primary seizure (p.3419).","Relying on headache or incontinence to distinguish seizure from syncope - these features are unreliable (p.3419).","Forgetting that the postictal Todd's paralysis (focal weakness for minutes to hours after a focal seizure) can mimic an acute structural deficit such as stroke (p.3409).","Misdiagnosing childhood absence epilepsy as attention deficit disorder or simple 'daydreaming' (p.3410)."],"severityClassification":"Seizures are classified (ILAE 2017) by onset: (1) Focal onset (with intact or impaired awareness; motor or nonmotor onset; may evolve to bilateral tonic-clonic); (2) Generalized onset - motor (tonic-clonic, or other such as atonic/myoclonic) or nonmotor (absence); (3) Unknown onset. Status epilepticus is staged by duration/refractoriness in management terms: impending/early (5-30 min), established and early-refractory (30 min to 48 h), and late refractory (>48 h), with subtypes generalized convulsive (GCSE) versus nonconvulsive (NCSE) (p.3409, p.3425-3426).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3408","Harrison 22e p.3409","Harrison 22e p.3410","Harrison 22e p.3411","Harrison 22e p.3413","Harrison 22e p.3414","Harrison 22e p.3415","Harrison 22e p.3416","Harrison 22e p.3417","Harrison 22e p.3418","Harrison 22e p.3419","Harrison 22e p.3420","Harrison 22e p.3424","Harrison 22e p.3425","Harrison 22e p.3426","Harrison 22e p.3427"],"crossLinks":["ENCEPHALITIS","hypoglycemia","metabolic_enceph","ischemic_stroke"]},"serotonin_nms":{"name":"Serotonin syndrome / NMS","system":"Toxicology / Neuro","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3517 (Ch.446/447, drug-induced movement disorders), p.3667 (Ch.463, SSRIs), p.3715 (Ch.470, toxidromes), p.3541 (Ch.451, hypothalamic autonomic disorders), p.3773 (Ch.478, heat illness differential), p.1172 (Ch.149, antibacterial drug interactions)","clinicalPearls":["Serotonin syndrome and NMS overlap closely (both cause fever, altered mental status, rigidity and autonomic instability), but prominent myoclonus is characteristically a feature of serotonin syndrome and helps separate it from NMS (p.3517).","NMS typically evolves over days to weeks after a neuroleptic/dopamine-antagonist is started or up-titrated, i.e. a comparatively slow, subacute onset (p.3517).","NMS is not only triggered by antipsychotics: it can also be precipitated by abrupt withdrawal of dopaminergic medication in Parkinson's disease patients (p.3517).","Serotonin syndrome arises from agents with serotonin-like activity or that block serotonin reuptake (e.g. tryptophan, MDMA/'ecstasy', meperidine, SSRIs), and is rare but potentially fatal (p.3517).","The serotonin-syndrome picture is attributed to hyperstimulation of brainstem 5-HT1A (and 5-HT2) receptors (p.3667).","The antibiotic linezolid (and to a lesser degree tedizolid) is a monoamine-oxidase inhibitor: combined with serotonergic agents such as SSRIs it can precipitate serotonin syndrome (p.1172).","Combining an MAOI with an SSRI is an absolute contraindication because of serotonin-syndrome risk; serotonergic agonists (tryptophan, fenfluramine, triptans) carry a similar hazard (p.3667).","Both syndromes are toxidromes in the CNS-syndrome / 'stimulated' physiologic category, alongside lithium toxicity, extrapyramidal reactions and strychnine (p.3715)."],"pathophysiology":"Serotonin syndrome results from excess CNS and peripheral serotonergic activity — promotion of serotonin release, inhibition of its reuptake, or direct stimulation of serotonin receptors (chiefly 5-HT1A and 5-HT2), occurring alone or when serotonergic drugs are combined. NMS, in contrast, is linked to dopamine-antagonist (neuroleptic) exposure or abrupt loss of dopaminergic tone, producing central hyperthermia, rigidity and autonomic dysfunction; central dopamine depletion impairs hypothalamic (preoptic/anterior) thermoregulation. Both are classified among the hypothalamic disorders of autonomic/temperature regulation (Harrison 22e p.3541, p.3517, p.3715).","additionalDifferentials":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome vs serotonin syndrome (mutual differential; distinguish by onset speed, neuromuscular pattern and offending drug class)","Lithium toxicity (tremor, hyperreflexia, myoclonus, encephalopathy, may include hyperthermia and seizures in severe cases)","Anticholinergic toxidrome","Sympathomimetic toxicity (amphetamines, cocaine)","Strychnine poisoning","Withdrawal syndromes (ethanol, sedative-hypnotics)"],"infectionMimics":["Meningitis","Encephalitis (including brainstem encephalitis)","Cerebral abscess","Sepsis / systemic inflammatory response syndrome","Malaria","Typhoid","Tetanus"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Malignant hyperthermia (e.g. after general anesthesia)","Heatstroke / exertional and classic heat illness","Thyroid storm","Pheochromocytoma","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Status epilepticus","Anticholinergic toxidrome","Stimulant/sympathomimetic toxicity"],"additionalInvestigations":["Core temperature measurement (most reliable prognosticator of poor outcome in stimulant/withdrawal-type toxidromes)","Creatine kinase (markedly elevated in NMS; rhabdomyolysis complicates both)","Lactate / arterial blood gas (lactic acidosis is a recognised complication)","Coagulation studies (monitor for disseminated intravascular coagulation when hyperthermia is sustained)","Toxicology screen and detailed medication review (serotonergic agents, dopamine antagonists, recent dose changes or dopaminergic withdrawal)","Cranial CT and CSF analysis if mental status fails to improve, to exclude structural or infectious CNS causes"],"redFlags":["Hyperthermia — sustained core temperature elevation predicts poor outcome and can progress to irreversible cellular injury","Rhabdomyolysis with markedly elevated creatine kinase","Autonomic instability — labile blood pressure, tachycardia, diaphoresis","Acute renal failure","Lactic acidosis","Disseminated intravascular coagulation / coagulopathy","Coma, seizures or multisystem organ failure"],"prognosis":"Both syndromes are potentially fatal; serotonin syndrome is described as rare but able to progress to death, and NMS can be lethal (e.g. typical neuroleptics in Lewy body disease can cause NMS and death). The duration of hyperthermia drives morbidity and mortality, and progression to rhabdomyolysis, renal failure, DIC and multisystem organ failure marks severe disease (Harrison 22e p.3517, p.3715, p.3773).","pitfalls":["Mistaking one syndrome for the other: rapid-onset hyperreflexia/myoclonus/clonus after a serotonergic drug points to serotonin syndrome, whereas slow (days-to-weeks) lead-pipe rigidity with bradyreflexia after a dopamine-antagonist points to NMS — the offending drug class and onset tempo are the key discriminators (p.3517).","Overlooking non-psychiatric culprits: the antibiotic linezolid is an MAOI and can trigger serotonin syndrome with concurrent SSRIs (p.1172).","Forgetting that NMS can be precipitated by stopping dopaminergic drugs (not only by starting antipsychotics) in Parkinson's disease (p.3517).","Anchoring on infection: the fever-plus-rigidity-plus-altered-mental-status picture closely mimics meningitis/encephalitis and sepsis, and structural/infectious causes should be excluded if mental status does not improve (p.3715, p.3773).","Dantrolene is ineffective for hyperthermia not caused by malignant hyperthermia, so a hyperthermia mechanism must be identified rather than assumed (p.3776).","Some atypical antipsychotics (e.g. chlorpromazine used for agitation) can themselves cause or worsen NMS — adding a neuroleptic to an undifferentiated hyperthermic patient can be harmful (p.3776)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames severity by the physiologic toxidrome state (stimulated vs depressed vs discordant) and by the degree of physiologic derangement on serial vital signs; escalating findings — hyperthermia, lactic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis and multisystem organ failure — mark severe serotonin syndrome, while marked CK elevation with renal failure and labile autonomic signs marks severe NMS. No formal numeric grading scale is given in the source (Harrison 22e p.3715, p.3517).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3517","Harrison 22e p.3667","Harrison 22e p.3715","Harrison 22e p.3541","Harrison 22e p.3773","Harrison 22e p.3776","Harrison 22e p.1172"],"crossLinks":["drug_intox","rhabdo","thyroid_storm","metabolic_enceph"]},"sjs_ten":{"name":"Stevens-Johnson syndrome / TEN","system":"Dermatology / Emergency","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.63 (Cutaneous Drug Reactions) p.416-426, esp. p.467-468; Ch.21 (Fever and Rash) Table 21-1 p.142","clinicalPearls":["SJS/TEN is a single disease spectrum of full-thickness epidermal necrosis with blistering and mucosal/epidermal detachment, separated only by the extent of body surface area (BSA) detachment.","Drugs cause roughly 80% of cases; the most frequently implicated agents are sulfonamide antibiotics, allopurinol, antiepileptics (lamotrigine, phenytoin, carbamazepine), oxicam NSAIDs, beta-lactam and other antibiotics, and nevirapine.","Onset is typically delayed, around 5-28 days after starting the culprit drug.","Prodrome: fever >39 C (102.2 F), sore throat, and conjunctivitis precede or accompany the rash; lesions are painful, dusky, atypical and target-like.","It is uncommon in children and over-represented among people with HIV, SLE, certain HLA types, and slow acetylators.","Mucosal surfaces are characteristically involved, and Nikolsky's sign is positive.","SJS/TEN is mediated by CD8+ cytotoxic T cells recognizing a drug-HLA class I complex; granulysin released by these cells is thought to be the principal mediator of keratinocyte death.","Strong, drug- and phenotype-specific HLA associations exist: HLA-B*15:02 with carbamazepine SJS/TEN (Han Chinese), HLA-B*58:01 with allopurinol, HLA-B*13:01 with dapsone/sulfonamide.","Frozen-section skin biopsy can aid rapid diagnosis and distinguish SJS/TEN (full-thickness epidermal necrosis) from mimics.","Loss of skin barrier integrity leads to dehydration and secondary sepsis, which are major causes of death."],"pathophysiology":"A delayed, T-cell-mediated (type IVa, cytotoxic) severe cutaneous adverse reaction. A drug or metabolite-altered antigen is presented in association with a risk HLA class I allele by keratinocytes or other antigen-presenting cells; a restricted T-cell receptor on skin-enriched CD8+ T cells recognizes this drug-HLA complex, triggering activation and release of cytolytic mediators (notably granulysin) that cause full-thickness epidermal keratinocyte death and detachment. Proposed antigen-presentation models include hapten/prohapten covalent binding, the pharmacologic-interaction (p-i) noncovalent model, and the altered-peptide-repertoire model (as proven for abacavir-HLA-B*57:01). Carbamazepine SJS/TEN shows a highly restricted V-alpha/V-beta TCR repertoire shared among HLA-B*15:02 patients. A risk HLA allele alone is insufficient; non-MHC factors such as drug metabolism (e.g., CYP2C9*3 slow metabolism with phenytoin) contribute. (Harrison 22e p.417-420)","additionalDifferentials":["Acute generalized exanthematous pustulosis (AGEP) - may resemble early SJS but erosions are superficial and prominent mucosal involvement is lacking","Drug-induced hypersensitivity syndrome / DRESS (DIHS) - can have TEN-like overlap features","Overlap hypersensitivity syndromes (DIHS-TEN-like, AGEP-TEN-like) - up to ~20% of severe drug eruptions show overlap, supporting a clinical spectrum"],"infectionMimics":["Reactive infectious mucocutaneous eruption (RIME) - Mycoplasma and other respiratory infections in children cause prominent mucositis with limited cutaneous involvement","Erythema multiforme associated with herpes simplex virus - painful mucosal erosions and target lesions, typically acral, with limited skin detachment"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Staphylococcal scalded-skin syndrome (SSSS) - diffuse tender erythema with bullae/desquamation and positive Nikolsky's sign; cleavage is superficial (subcorneal), spares mucosa","DRESS / drug-induced hypersensitivity syndrome - maculopapular eruption, facial edema, eosinophilia, multiorgan (especially hepatic) involvement","Erythema multiforme major - target lesions with mucous-membrane involvement","Acute generalized exanthematous pustulosis (AGEP) - sterile pustules on erythema, flexural predominance","Exfoliative erythroderma syndrome - diffuse erythema/scaling, may be drug-induced","Autoimmune blistering disease (e.g., bullous pemphigoid, which can also be drug-induced/exacerbated)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Frozen-section skin biopsy for rapid diagnosis; histology shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis (distinguishes from AGEP's superficial/subcorneal neutrophilic pustules)","Calculate BSA of epidermal detachment to classify SJS vs overlap vs TEN","Assess for renal insufficiency and consider underlying malignancy (both poor-prognosis markers)","Patch testing with the responsible drug (after recovery) and use of the ALDEN algorithm to rank likelihood of drug causality"],"redFlags":["Extensive/rapidly progressing epidermal detachment (BSA toward TEN range) - maximal-severity form","Older age, malignancy, or renal insufficiency - independently associated with poor prognosis","Signs of sepsis or dehydration from loss of skin barrier integrity","Mucosal (ocular, oral, urogenital, respiratory) involvement - risk of acute and long-term sequelae"],"prognosis":"Mortality is substantial and rises with the extent of epidermal detachment: at least ~10% of SJS and ~30% of TEN patients die from the disease (overall reported mortality up to ~30%). Older age, greater BSA detachment, malignancy, and renal insufficiency are independent poor-prognostic factors. Best outcomes follow early diagnosis and immediate withdrawal of the culprit drug. (Harrison 22e p.142, p.424-425)","pitfalls":["Early SJS may be hard to distinguish from AGEP; in AGEP erosions are superficial and prominent mucosal involvement is NOT present.","Drugs may be implicated even when the eruption appears shortly AFTER the drug has been discontinued (delayed-type reactions).","A risk HLA allele does NOT by itself cause SJS/TEN - most carriers remain drug-tolerant; other factors (e.g., drug metabolism) are required.","Designation of a single diagnosis is NOT always possible in overlap hypersensitivity syndromes; manage according to the dominant evolving clinical features.","Skin biopsy helps characterize the reaction but does NOT by itself establish drug causality."],"severityClassification":"Classified by percent body surface area (BSA) of epidermal detachment: SJS = <10% BSA; SJS/TEN overlap = 10-30% BSA; TEN = >30% BSA (maximal form). (Harrison 22e p.142, p.424). SCORTEN (severity-of-illness score for TEN) is the standard prognostic tool; note the source text does not enumerate its parameters.","references":["Harrison 22e p.142","Harrison 22e p.416","Harrison 22e p.417","Harrison 22e p.418","Harrison 22e p.419","Harrison 22e p.420","Harrison 22e p.424","Harrison 22e p.425"],"crossLinks":["anaphylaxis","angioedema_acei"]},"sle_flare":{"name":"SLE / autoimmune flare","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.368 (Systemic Lupus Erythematosus), pp.2827-2836","clinicalPearls":["Roughly 90% of SLE patients are women, most of childbearing age; prevalence is highest among Black women, then Hispanic, White, and Asian/Pacific Islander women; disease tends to be more severe in non-White patients (p.2827).","Autoantibodies can be detectable years before clinical diagnosis, so a positive serology in a symptomatic patient supports attributing nonspecific complaints to SLE (p.2827, 2829).","Most patients have active disease despite therapy or recurrent flares; treatment goals are low disease activity (SLEDAI 0-4) or remission on ≤5 mg/day prednisone equivalent (p.2829, 2832).","A proposed clinical split distinguishes type 1 lupus (classic inflammatory/organ manifestations such as nephritis, arthritis, vasculitis that respond to immunosuppression) from type 2 lupus (fatigue, diffuse pain, brain fog, depression, sleep disturbance that respond poorly to immunosuppression) (p.2827).","Anti-dsDNA titers and C3/C4 levels track disease activity: anti-dsDNA is produced by short-lived plasmablasts that turn over quickly, and complement falls during high activity as it is consumed by immune complexes (p.2832).","Rising anti-dsDNA and falling C3/C4 can serve as warning signs of impending active nephritis even in an asymptomatic patient (p.2830).","Lupus arthritis is typically non-erosive (Jaccoud-like reversible deformity from ligamentous laxity); overlap with rheumatoid arthritis is termed 'rhupus' (p.2829).","Pain out of proportion in a single hip, shoulder, or knee — especially with prior corticosteroid use — should raise concern for avascular necrosis of bone (p.2829).","Accelerated atherosclerosis is a major cause of premature death, notably myocardial infarction and stroke in young patients without traditional risk factors, and is the leading cause of death after ~5 years of disease (p.2829, 2836).","Antiphospholipid antibodies/APS coexist in about a third of SLE patients and markedly raise risk of arterial/venous thrombosis, stroke (reported in up to 19%), Libman-Sacks endocarditis, and pregnancy loss (p.2829, 2830, 2831)."],"pathophysiology":"SLE arises from overactive innate and adaptive immunity with autoantibodies directed at nuclear components (nucleic acids, nucleosomes, RNA-binding proteins). Impaired clearance of cellular debris — driven by high neutrophil turnover/NETosis and defective complement-mediated phagocytosis — overwhelms macrophages and dendritic cells. Accumulated nucleic acids engage toll-like receptors and nucleic-acid sensors in plasmacytoid dendritic cells, driving type 1 interferon production that primes the immune response. Autoreactive B cells (including expanded age-associated B cells) lose tolerance and, with help from T follicular/T peripheral helper cells, mature into autoantibody-secreting plasma cells, while regulatory T-cell function is defective. Resulting immune complexes deposit in tissues and cause inflammation, repair, and eventual fibrosis after recurrent flares. Strong genetic component (monozygotic twin concordance 24% vs 2% dizygotic; >150 GWAS loci, with the MHC the strongest and nearly half of loci linked to type 1 interferon; monogenic complement-pathway deficiencies — C1q, C1r/s, C2, C4 — cause severe SLE). Environmental triggers include UV light, smoking, crystalline silica, mercury/toxins, sex hormones, EBV, and gut microbiome/permeability changes (p.2827-2829).","additionalDifferentials":["Rosacea (malar rash mimic — but rosacea involves the nasolabial folds, whereas lupus malar rash spares them) (p.2829)","Rheumatoid arthritis, or RA-SLE overlap ('rhupus') (p.2829)","Drug-induced lupus (antihistone antibodies; ~6-12% of lupus cases; rarely renal/CNS; resolves on stopping the drug) (p.2827, 2835)","Other systemic autoimmune diseases sharing ANA subtypes: myositis/dermatomyositis, Sjögren's syndrome, systemic sclerosis, mixed connective tissue/overlap syndromes (anti-RNP) (p.2832)","Migraine or tension headache (lupus headache can be difficult to distinguish) (p.2829)","Glucocorticoid-induced psychosis (must be excluded before attributing psychosis to neuropsychiatric lupus) (p.2829)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute lupus pneumonitis — pulmonary infiltrates can be radiographically indistinguishable from pneumonia (p.2829)","Infectious encephalitis — should be ruled out before attributing acute psychosis/confusional state to CNS lupus (p.2829)","Infection as a cause of cytopenias — SLE cytopenias are multifactorial (disease activity, medication, or infection) (p.2830)","Epstein-Barr virus and other viral infections — may both trigger and mimic flare via interferon induction (p.2828)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Atypical hemolytic-uremic syndrome / TTP-like microvascular thrombotic crisis (hemolysis, thrombocytopenia, microvascular thrombosis, schistocytes, low ADAMTS13, high LDH) (p.2831)","Antiphospholipid syndrome thrombotic events presenting as stroke, DVT/PE, Budd-Chiari, or Libman-Sacks embolism (p.2829-2831)","Glucocorticoid-induced complications mimicking active disease (steroid myopathy, hyperglycemia, psychosis, avascular necrosis) (p.2829, 2833)","Macrophage activation syndrome / hemophagocytic syndrome (acute hemophagocytic syndrome listed among severe SLE manifestations) (p.2833, and HLH associated with SLE p. table 67824 region)"],"additionalInvestigations":["ANA by immunofluorescence is the best screening test (positive in ~95%; titer ≥1:80 favors SLE); repeated negatives make SLE unlikely but ~5% are ANA-negative (p.2831-2832)","Anti-dsDNA (highly specific, ~40-70% prevalence) and anti-Sm (most specific, ~30%) — the only ANA subtypes in the EULAR/ACR criteria (p.2832)","Anti-Ro/SSA — useful when ANA-negative but clinically suspicious; linked to neonatal lupus, congenital heart block, sicca, photosensitivity, SCLE (p.2831-2832)","Antihistone antibodies — suggest drug-induced lupus (positive in ~75% of DIL) (p.2832, 2835)","Antiphospholipid panel: anticardiolipin and anti-beta-2-glycoprotein-1 (IgG/IgM/IgA) plus lupus anticoagulant by dilute Russell viper venom time (~50% prevalence) (p.2831-2832)","Random urine protein-to-creatinine ratio (UPCr) or 24-h urine protein with eGFR and serum creatinine; renal biopsy when UPCr >0.5 and/or declining function (p.2830, 2832)","Renal biopsy with ISN/RPS lupus nephritis classification (the only single finding that alone meets EULAR/ACR criteria — class III or IV) (p.2827, 2830)","CK and aldolase with muscle biopsy if inflammatory myopathy suspected (p.2829)","Emerging urinary biomarkers of renal inflammation/fibrosis: urinary IL-16, CD163, type 1 IFN, TGF-beta (p.2832)","Quarterly screening of CBC and renal indices in asymptomatic patients, since hematologic and renal flares can be silent (p.2832)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly declining eGFR, rising proteinuria, or active urinary sediment — class III/IV proliferative lupus nephritis; ~20% of class IV patients die or reach ESRD within 10 years (p.2830)","Crescentic / rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (cellular crescents or fibrinoid necrosis) — poor prognostic features (p.2834)","Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage with capillaritis (p.2829)","Mesenteric vasculitis — risk of intestinal perforation, bleeding, and ischemia (p.2830)","Neuropsychiatric flare: seizures, acute psychosis/confusion, transverse myelitis (p.2829)","Atypical HUS/TTP-like crisis: hemolysis, thrombocytopenia, schistocytes, low ADAMTS13, microvascular thrombosis (p.2831)","Severe thrombocytopenia (<20-50 x10^9/L) with bleeding risk; severe hemolytic anemia (p.2830, 2833)","Stroke or arterial/venous thrombosis suggesting APS (p.2829-2830)","Pulmonary arterial hypertension, prior arterial thrombosis, or severe end-organ damage — highest maternal mortality; pregnancy should strongly be reconsidered (p.2836)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis and survival vary widely by geography, race/ethnicity, and access to care/medications. Renal disease, especially class IV nephritis, carries substantial risk of ESRD and death (~20% within 10 years), and is more severe in non-White patients. Accelerated atherosclerosis is a major cause of premature death and the leading cause after ~5 years of disease, driven by both traditional risk factors and lupus-specific factors (antiphospholipid antibodies, high disease activity, low C3, high glucocorticoid dose, high homocysteine/leptin). Glucocorticoid exposure independently accrues irreversible organ damage (cataracts, osteoporotic fractures, avascular necrosis, diabetes) even at doses as low as 5 mg prednisone equivalent. Class V (membranous) nephritis has a better prognosis than class III/IV. Sustained low disease activity (LLDAS) for 2+ years is associated with significantly less damage. Recent meta-analyses suggest an increased overall cancer risk and cancer-related mortality (p.2830, 2835-2836).","pitfalls":["Distinguishing flare from infection is a central challenge: cytopenias and constitutional symptoms can stem from disease activity, medication, OR infection, and acute lupus pneumonitis can look identical to pneumonia on imaging — infection must be excluded before escalating immunosuppression (p.2829-2830).","Classification criteria (2019 EULAR/ACR; 2012 SLICC) were built for trial enrollment, not diagnosis — diagnosis remains an individualized clinical judgment; relying on criteria alone can mislead, and ANA-negative or incomplete-lupus patients are excluded by EULAR/ACR (p.2827).","A positive ANA or even antihistone antibody alone does not confirm SLE — many drugs induce a positive ANA in patients who never develop symptoms, and antihistone antibodies occur in most SLE cases too (p.2835).","Drug-induced lupus can be hard to separate from SLE because skin biopsy and many labs overlap; presence of additional SLE-specific autoantibodies (rarely seen in DIL) and symptom resolution after stopping the drug help distinguish (p.2835).","Acute psychosis/confusion may be glucocorticoid-induced rather than neuropsychiatric lupus — and infectious encephalitis must also be ruled out (p.2829).","Single-joint pain out of proportion may be avascular necrosis (steroid-related), not arthritis flare (p.2829).","Estrogen-containing contraceptives/HRT should be avoided in patients with antiphospholipid antibodies or moderate-severe/active lupus due to thromboembolism risk (p.2836)."],"severityClassification":"Disease activity is graded for treatment using SLEDAI/SLEDAI-2K (low disease activity = SLEDAI 0-4; remission/DORIS = SLEDAI 0). EULAR severity tiers: mild (constitutional symptoms, mild arthritis, rash <=9% BSA, platelets 50-100, SLEDAI <=6); moderate (RA-like arthritis, rash 9-18% BSA, platelets 20-50, serositis, SLEDAI 7-12); severe (major organ-threatening disease e.g. cerebritis, myelitis, pneumonitis, mesenteric vasculitis; platelets <20; TTP-like or acute hemophagocytic syndrome; rash >18% BSA; SLEDAI >12). Lupus nephritis is graded by ISN/RPS renal biopsy classes I-V (class I mesangial deposits; II mesangial proliferative; III focal proliferative; IV diffuse proliferative; V membranous; III/IV +/- V combinations), with class III/IV (and III/IV+V) being proliferative forms warranting aggressive therapy (p.2827, 2830-2831, 2833-2834).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2827","Harrison 22e p.2828","Harrison 22e p.2829","Harrison 22e p.2830","Harrison 22e p.2831","Harrison 22e p.2832","Harrison 22e p.2833","Harrison 22e p.2834","Harrison 22e p.2835","Harrison 22e p.2836"],"crossLinks":["vasculitis","glomerulonephritis","ttp_hus"]},"subdural":{"name":"Subdural haematoma","system":"Neurology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e pp.3572-3573, with pp.160, 222-223, 238, 3498, 3527, 3619, 3927, 3929","clinicalPearls":["Direct cranial trauma may be minor and is not always required for an acute subdural bleed, particularly in the elderly and those on anticoagulants; acceleration forces alone (e.g. whiplash) can suffice (p.3572).","Up to one-third of patients with acute subdural haematoma have a lucid interval of minutes to hours before coma supervenes, but most are drowsy or comatose from the moment of injury (p.3572).","A unilateral headache with a slightly enlarged pupil on the side of the haematoma is frequently, though not invariably, present (p.3572).","Small subdural haematomas may be asymptomatic and usually do not require evacuation unless they enlarge (p.3572).","In chronic subdural haematoma, drowsiness, inattentiveness and incoherent thinking are generally more prominent than focal signs such as hemiparesis (p.3573).","Chronic subdural haematoma headache typically fluctuates in severity, sometimes with changes in head position (p.3573).","A history of trauma may be absent in chronic subdural haematoma; the injury may have been trivial and forgotten, especially in the elderly and those with clotting disorders (p.3572).","Recurrent falls in older adults place them at risk for subdural haematoma; brain imaging is warranted when neurologic signs are present (pp.222-223, 3927, 3929).","Trauma should be considered in any patient with an unexplained acute neurologic deficit (hemiparesis, stupor, confusion), particularly after a fall (p.3498)."],"pathophysiology":"Bleeding occurs beneath the dura (subdural space). The bleeding producing larger subdural haematomas is primarily venous in origin, classically from torn bridging veins, although arterial bleeding sites are occasionally found at operation and a few large haematomas are purely arterial. Chronic subdural haematomas develop days to weeks after (often trivial) injury, favoured by age-related cerebral atrophy that stretches bridging veins; they organise into a vascular fibrous capsule, and weeks-old collections often contain a mixture of blood and serous fluid. Anticoagulant-related intracranial haemorrhages are often subdural and may continue to evolve over 24-48 h if coagulopathy is insufficiently reversed (pp.3572-3573, 3498).","additionalDifferentials":["Epidural haematoma (lenticular/lens-shaped on CT, usually arterial from a torn middle meningeal artery, classic lucid interval, more rapid evolution)","Cerebral contusion / convexity contusion (can produce focal deficits such as aphasia or mild hemiparesis)","Carotid or vertebral artery dissection (can mimic focal deficits seen with subdural)","Posttraumatic headache syndrome (chronic subdural may mimic this) (p.160)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Transient ischemic attack (rarely chronic subdural causes brief episodes of hemiparesis or aphasia indistinguishable from TIA) (p.3573)","Dementia / Alzheimer or other neurodegenerative disease (chronic subdural is a treatable cause of dementia, often on a background of cortical atrophy) (pp.238, 3527)","Stroke (subdural is considered when excluding stroke in older fallers) (p.3929)","Normal-pressure hydrocephalus and other reversible dementia causes (listed alongside subdural among treatable dementias) (p.238)"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI reliably identifies both subacute and chronic subdural haematomas and is useful when CT is equivocal (p.3573)","Contrast-enhanced imaging demonstrates enhancement of the vascular fibrous capsule surrounding a chronic collection (p.3573)","Serial imaging for clinical observation of small chronic collections without mass effect (p.3573)"],"redFlags":["Stupor or coma, hemiparesis, and unilateral pupillary enlargement indicate a larger acute haematoma (p.3572)","Acutely deteriorating patient requiring emergency craniotomy (p.3572)","Repetitive vomiting, nystagmus, drowsiness and unsteadiness may signal a posterior fossa subdural haematoma (p.3619)","Large bleed after only minor trauma should raise concern for coagulopathy or anticoagulant therapy (p.3573)"],"prognosis":"In contrast to epidural haematomas, acute subdural haematomas requiring surgery carry significant morbidity and mortality, partly reflecting frequently associated underlying cortical brain damage. Chronic subdural collections evacuated through burr holes are usually treated successfully when a postoperative cranial drain is used; encapsulating fibrous membranes may require craniotomy to prevent recurrent fluid accumulation (pp.3572-3573).","pitfalls":["Subacute pitfall: between roughly 2 and 6 weeks after the initial bleed the clot becomes isodense relative to adjacent brain on non-contrast CT and may be inapparent/missed (p.3573).","Acute subdural produces more horizontal displacement (mass effect) than expected from its thickness because of its large rostral-caudal extent, so mass effect can be underestimated from clot thickness alone (p.3572).","Absence of a trauma history does not exclude chronic subdural haematoma in the elderly or anticoagulated (p.3572).","Skull fractures are markers of injury site/severity, not the haematoma itself, and may be absent (p.3572)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames subdural haematoma by acuity and imaging appearance rather than a numeric grade: ACUTE — non-contrast CT shows a hyperdense crescentic clot with an irregular brain interface; SUBACUTE (~2-6 weeks) — clot becomes isodense and easily overlooked, often with mixed blood and serous fluid; CHRONIC — hypodense crescentic collection over one or both convexities, most often frontotemporal (pp.3572-3573).","references":["Harrison 22e p.160","Harrison 22e p.222","Harrison 22e p.223","Harrison 22e p.238","Harrison 22e p.3498","Harrison 22e p.3527","Harrison 22e p.3572","Harrison 22e p.3573","Harrison 22e p.3619","Harrison 22e p.3927","Harrison 22e p.3929"],"crossLinks":["ich","ischemic_stroke"]},"svc_obstruction":{"name":"Superior vena cava obstruction","system":"Oncology / Emergency","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.585-586, 628-629 (oncologic emergencies); p.642 (lung cancer regional spread); p.1859 (cardiovascular exam)","clinicalPearls":["SVC syndrome is a clinical diagnosis: dusky/cyanotic head and neck with grossly elevated venous pressure but no visible pulsations should prompt the diagnosis.","Malignancy (lung cancer, lymphoma, metastatic tumours) accounts for most cases, but benign causes now make up at least 40% owing to widespread use of intravascular devices (central venous catheters, pacemaker/defibrillator leads).","Lung cancer—particularly small-cell and squamous-cell histology—causes around 85% of malignant SVCS.","In a young adult/young man with a mediastinal mass and SVCS, think lymphoma versus primary mediastinal germ cell tumour.","Symptoms are characteristically worse on bending forward or lying supine; facial swelling and plethora are exacerbated when supine.","The clinical picture is milder when the obstruction is above the azygos vein.","Symptoms may actually improve over time as collateral venous circulation develops, and most patients improve partly for this reason.","Small-cell lung cancer patients with SVCS have a higher incidence of brain metastases than those without SVCS; new seizures more likely reflect brain mets than venous cerebral oedema.","Tissue diagnosis (bronchoscopy, percutaneous needle biopsy, mediastinoscopy, endobronchial/oesophageal ultrasound-guided aspiration, or even thoracotomy) can generally be obtained safely without major bleeding risk despite the venous congestion.","For a patient with no prior cancer history, a full evaluation is essential to exclude benign causes and define a specific diagnosis before directing therapy; in known cancer, treatment may begin after a thoracic CT."],"pathophysiology":"SVC syndrome results from obstruction of the superior vena cava causing a severe reduction in venous return from the head, neck, and upper extremities. Obstruction may be due to external compression, direct tumour invasion, intraluminal thrombosis (often device-related), or fibrosis. Venous congestion produces facial/upper-body swelling, distended neck veins, and development of collateral venous channels over the chest wall. The 'hot quadrate' focal hepatic hotspot on CT reflects portosystemic shunting between the SVC and portal vein. Chronic SVCS can produce 'downhill' oesophageal varices (cephalad-to-caudad flow, opposite to portal-hypertension 'uphill' varices), which may bleed late.","additionalDifferentials":["Lung cancer (small-cell and squamous-cell predominate among malignant causes)","Malignant lymphoma (leading cause in young adults; Hodgkin involves mediastinum often but rarely causes SVCS)","Primary mediastinal germ cell tumour (esp. young man with mediastinal mass)","Metastatic carcinoma to mediastinal nodes (e.g., testicular, breast — small proportion)","Benign mediastinal tumours","Aortic aneurysm","Thyromegaly (goitre)","Intravascular thrombosis (central venous catheter, pacemaker/defibrillator lead related)","Fibrosing mediastinitis (post-irradiation, histoplasmosis, or Behcet's syndrome)"],"infectionMimics":["Fibrosing mediastinitis secondary to histoplasmosis (a post-infectious cause of SVC obstruction)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Behcet's syndrome — SVCS may be its initial manifestation, from SVC inflammation with thrombosis","Post-radiation fibrosing mediastinitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph — superior mediastinal widening (most often right-sided) is the most significant finding; a normal film does not exclude SVCS if other features present","CT of the thorax — most reliable view of mediastinal anatomy; shows diminished/absent opacification of central veins with prominent collaterals; may show 'hot quadrate' hepatic sign","MRI — reported 100% sensitivity and specificity, though dyspnoeic patients may not tolerate lying supine","Pleural fluid sampling if effusion present (effusion in only ~25%, often right-sided, usually exudative, occasionally chylous)","Tissue-yielding procedures: bronchoscopy, percutaneous needle biopsy, mediastinoscopy, endobronchial/oesophageal ultrasound-guided needle aspiration"],"redFlags":["Signs/symptoms of cerebral and/or laryngeal oedema — rare but carry a poorer prognosis and demand urgent evaluation","Upper airway/tracheal obstruction from a superior mediastinal mass — the one potentially life-threatening complication, demands emergent therapy","Cardiorespiratory symptoms at rest, especially with positional change — indicate significant airway/vascular obstruction and limited physiologic reserve","Risk of cardiac arrest or respiratory failure, particularly with sedation or general anaesthesia","Proptosis, glossal and laryngeal oedema, and obtundation indicate severe disease","Postural hypotension with severe symptoms","Variceal (downhill oesophageal varices) bleeding as a late complication of chronic SVCS"],"prognosis":"Clinical improvement occurs in most patients, partly attributable to the development of adequate collateral circulation. Mortality is determined by the underlying cause rather than by the caval obstruction itself. SVCS recurs in roughly 10-30% of patients. Cerebral or laryngeal oedema portends a worse prognosis.","pitfalls":["Do not assume SVCS is always malignant — benign device/catheter-related causes now account for at least 40% of cases.","A normal chest radiograph does not exclude SVCS when other characteristic findings are present.","Attributing seizures to venous cerebral oedema when brain metastases are the more likely cause (especially in small-cell lung cancer).","Sedatives or general anaesthesia in a patient with airway/vascular obstruction may precipitate cardiac arrest or respiratory failure.","In a patient without known cancer, omitting a full diagnostic workup risks missing benign causes and a treatable specific diagnosis."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal severity grading scale. Severity is described clinically by features such as presence of cerebral/laryngeal oedema, proptosis, glossal/laryngeal oedema, obtundation, postural hypotension, and cardiorespiratory symptoms at rest with positional change, which together denote severe, potentially life-threatening disease; obstruction above the azygos vein produces a milder picture.","references":["Harrison 22e p.585","Harrison 22e p.586","Harrison 22e p.628","Harrison 22e p.629","Harrison 22e p.642"],"crossLinks":["lung_cancer","malignancy_b"]},"tamponade":{"name":"Cardiac tamponade","system":"Cardiology / Emergency","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e: p.102, p.147, p.630, p.1432, p.1641, p.1906, p.1910, p.1917, p.1938","clinicalPearls":["Tamponade is fundamentally a clinical diagnosis in an unstable patient; the textbook triad is hypotension, raised jugular venous pressure with neck-vein distention, and quiet/muffled heart sounds (p.630).","It is the balance between intrapericardial pressure and ventricular (especially right ventricular) filling pressure that drives compression, not the absolute volume of fluid (p.1938).","Rate of accumulation matters more than total volume: slowly forming effusions (e.g. malignant) can become large without classic tamponade physiology, whereas rapid accumulation (e.g. cardiac rupture, perforation, aortic dissection into the pericardium) can cause tamponade with only a small effusion (p.1938).","Pulsus paradoxus is an inspiratory fall in systolic pressure of more than 10 mmHg and represents an exaggerated form of normal interventricular dependence (p.1906).","Measure pulsus paradoxus by deflating the cuff slowly and noting the gap between the pressure where Korotkoff sounds are first heard (only in expiration) and the pressure where they are heard with every beat regardless of respiration; a difference >15 mmHg may even be palpable at the brachial or femoral pulse (p.1906).","A pulsus paradoxus exceeding 12 mmHg in a patient with a large pericardial effusion is highly predictive of tamponade (reported sensitivity ~98%, specificity ~83%) (p.1910).","The ECG triad of sinus tachycardia, low QRS voltage, and total electrical alternans (beat-to-beat P-QRS-T alternation) strongly suggests pericardial effusion with tamponade physiology, though it is insensitive (p.1917).","Total electrical alternans (whole-complex) is relatively specific for effusion/tamponade; in contrast, pure repolarization (ST-T/U-wave) alternans signals electrical instability and risk of ventricular arrhythmia, not tamponade (p.1917).","Echocardiography is the initial test of choice for suspected effusion/tamponade and can usually be done rapidly at the bedside (p.1938)."],"pathophysiology":"Pericardial fluid accumulates between the visceral (epicardial) and parietal pericardial layers. Tamponade develops when intrapericardial pressure rises enough to exceed the filling pressures of the cardiac chambers, generally the right ventricle, impairing diastolic filling. Because the relatively non-distensible pericardial sac determines tolerance, the rate of fluid accumulation is more important than absolute volume: slow accumulation allows large effusions without compromise, while rapid accumulation (trauma, cardiac rupture, procedural perforation, aortic dissection into the pericardium) causes tamponade at small volumes. Inspiratory augmentation of right-heart filling at the expense of the left, mediated by exaggerated interventricular dependence within the fixed pericardial space, produces pulsus paradoxus (p.1906, p.1938).","additionalDifferentials":["Constrictive pericarditis (also impairs cardiac filling via a thick, noncompliant pericardium; on the differential for dyspnea) (p.1938)","Acute right ventricular dysfunction (can mimic the elevated JVP / characteristic jugular waveform pattern) (p.147)"],"infectionMimics":["Sepsis (can produce a paradoxical-type arterial waveform) (p.1906)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Massive pulmonary embolism (shares hypotension/tachycardia and pulsus paradoxus) (p.147, p.1906)","Tension pneumothorax (life-threatening cause of obstructive shock and pulsus paradoxus) (p.147, p.1906)","Hemorrhagic shock (cause of pulsus paradoxus) (p.1906)","Severe obstructive lung disease / asthma (cause of pulsus paradoxus) (p.1906)","Acute MI with cardiogenic shock (hypotension + tachycardia in chest-pain patient) (p.147)","Acute aortic dissection (may extend into the pericardium and itself cause tamponade) (p.147, p.1938)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Pericardiocentesis under echocardiographic guidance, with pericardial fluid sent for biochemical, cytologic, and microbiologic analysis (p.630, p.1432)","Pericardial fluid cytology when malignancy is suspected (diagnostic in most malignant cases; fluid may be serous, serosanguineous, or hemorrhagic) (p.630)","Pericardial/epicardial biopsy and pericardioscopy to distinguish neoplastic from benign disease (p.630)","Chest CT, CMR, or echocardiography to assess effusion size and pericardial thickness (p.1432, p.1938)","In suspected tuberculous etiology, pericardial fluid adenosine deaminase, lysozyme, and IFN-gamma, plus culture/biopsy (fluid is exudative, lymphocyte/monocyte-rich, often hemorrhagic) (p.1432)"],"redFlags":["Hypotension with tachycardia in a chest-pain patient mandates rapid evaluation for tamponade as one of the immediately life-threatening causes (p.147)","Life-threatening hemodynamic instability with acute tamponade requires immediate fluid drainage (p.630)","Hemorrhagic tamponade from cardiac rupture, procedural perforation, or aortic dissection into the pericardium can be catastrophic at small effusion volumes (p.1938)"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on cause and timeliness of drainage. Untreated tuberculous pericarditis is usually fatal and even treated TB pericarditis carries case-fatality rates up to ~40% in some series, with risk of progression to constrictive pericarditis. Cancer patients whose pericardial fluid contains malignant cells on cytology have very poor survival. After percutaneous catheter drainage of malignant effusions the recurrence rate is ~20%, and a subset develop paradoxical hemodynamic worsening after drainage (post-drainage low cardiac output syndrome) (p.630, p.1432).","pitfalls":["Do not delay decompression in a hemodynamically unstable patient waiting for confirmatory tests; tamponade is a clinical/emergency diagnosis and instability requires immediate drainage (p.147, p.630).","Failing to appreciate the urgency of the situation is a recognized diagnostic error for tamponade (listed among can't-miss time-critical diagnoses) (p.102).","Pulsus paradoxus can be hard to measure or unreliable in tachycardia, atrial fibrillation, or tachypnea (p.1906).","A large slowly-accumulating effusion may lack classic tamponade signs, while a small rapidly-accumulating effusion may already be causing tamponade; judging severity by volume alone is misleading (p.1938).","In malignant tamponade, relatively specific signs (paradoxical pulse, diminished heart sounds, pulsus alternans, friction rub) are LESS common than in nonmalignant disease, so their absence does not exclude tamponade (p.630).","ECG and chest radiograph are abnormal in ~90% of malignant pericardial cases but about half of those abnormalities are nonspecific (p.630)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.102","Harrison 22e p.147","Harrison 22e p.630","Harrison 22e p.1432","Harrison 22e p.1641","Harrison 22e p.1906","Harrison 22e p.1910","Harrison 22e p.1917","Harrison 22e p.1938"],"crossLinks":["pericarditis","cardiogenic_shock","aortic_dissection"]},"temporal_arteritis":{"name":"Giant cell (temporal) arteritis","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.115 (Ch.17 Headache), p.227 (Ch.34 Disorders of the Eye / AION), p.2902-2903 (Ch.375 Vasculitis Syndromes)","clinicalPearls":["Occurs almost exclusively in people over 50; mean age of onset around 70, with women making up roughly two-thirds of cases. Rare in Black patients; higher incidence in Scandinavian populations (Harrison 22e p.115, p.2902).","Four phenotypic patterns can occur together, in isolation, or at different times: cranial arteritis, polymyalgia rheumatica, large-vessel disease, and systemic inflammation (Harrison 22e p.2902).","Headache is the dominant cranial symptom; it is usually dull/boring rather than throbbing, may be unilateral or bilateral, is temporally located in only about half of patients, often worse at night, and aggravated by cold (Harrison 22e p.115).","Scalp tenderness can be marked - brushing hair or resting the head on a pillow may be intolerable; the temporal (or less often occipital) artery may be tender, thickened, nodular, reddened or have overlying red skin streaks (Harrison 22e p.115).","The temporal artery may pulsate early in disease but can become occluded later (Harrison 22e p.2902).","Jaw and tongue claudication and scalp pain are recognized cranial-ischemic features; rarer cranial ischemic events include stroke and scalp or tongue infarction (Harrison 22e p.115, p.2902).","ESR and/or CRP are elevated in most patients, but a normal ESR does NOT exclude giant cell arteritis (Harrison 22e p.115).","Other supportive labs: normochromic or slightly hypochromic anemia, raised alkaline phosphatase (liver function abnormalities common), and increased IgG and complement (Harrison 22e p.2902).","Most patients report head or eye complaints before any visual loss, so attention to early cranial symptoms with prompt therapy reduces the risk of blindness (Harrison 22e p.2902).","Polymyalgia rheumatica overlaps strongly: PMR is seen in 40-50% of GCA patients, and 10-20% who present with isolated PMR later develop GCA - GCA and PMR are regarded as a spectrum of one disease process (Harrison 22e p.2902).","In PMR, creatine kinase is not elevated and rheumatoid factor / anti-CCP should be negative; a worsening peripheral arthritis pattern should prompt reconsideration of seronegative RA or other inflammatory arthropathy (Harrison 22e p.2903)."],"pathophysiology":"A panarteritis of medium- and large-sized arteries, classically branches of the carotid (especially the temporal artery) but systemically involving the aorta and its branches. Histology shows mononuclear inflammatory infiltrate within the vessel wall with frequent giant cell formation, intimal proliferation, and fragmentation of the internal elastic lamina; organ damage results from ischemia of involved vessels. It is considered an antigen-driven process: CD4+ T cells enter via the adventitial vasa vasorum, become activated, and drive macrophage differentiation, with restricted clonal T-cell expansion suggesting an arterial-wall antigen. Lesional T cells produce mainly IL-2 and IFN-gamma; both IFN-gamma-producing TH1 and IL-17-producing TH17 lineages contribute and may differ in glucocorticoid responsiveness. Associated with HLA-DR4 and HLA-DRB1*04 variants. (Harrison 22e p.2902)","additionalDifferentials":["Migraine - common in the elderly (more prevalent than GCA) and may improve with prednisone, so a steroid response does not by itself confirm GCA (Harrison 22e p.115).","Nonarteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy - associated with a crowded 'disc-at-risk' / small optic cup, in which glucocorticoids should NOT be prescribed, contrasting with the arteritic form (Harrison 22e p.227).","Isolated polymyalgia rheumatica without arteritis (Harrison 22e p.2903).","Other inflammatory causes of secondary headache such as sarcoidosis and Behcet's syndrome (Harrison 22e p.115)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Seronegative rheumatoid arthritis or other inflammatory arthropathy mimicking the peripheral arthritis of PMR (Harrison 22e p.2903)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Temporal artery biopsy: yield improved by harvesting a long segment (3-5 cm) with serial sectioning because involvement is segmental/patchy; biopsy may still show vasculitis even after about 14 days of glucocorticoids, but prolonged treatment can mask findings (Harrison 22e p.227, p.2903).","Temporal artery ultrasonography (alone or with axillary arteries) showing a noncompressible 'halo' sign supports the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2903).","Large-vessel imaging by MR or CT angiography to confirm large-vessel disease suggested by diminished pulses or bruits; PET is increasingly studied but its diagnostic/monitoring role remains unclear (Harrison 22e p.2903).","Note: in patients treated with an IL-6 inhibitor, CRP and ESR normalize and can no longer be used to monitor disease activity or assess for infection (Harrison 22e p.2902)."],"redFlags":["Ischemic optic neuropathy is the dreaded complication; about half of untreated patients develop blindness from ophthalmic-artery involvement, and arteritic AION is the leading cause of rapidly developing bilateral blindness in patients over 60 (Harrison 22e p.115).","In suspected arteritic AION / visual loss, start glucocorticoids IMMEDIATELY without waiting for biopsy to protect vision in the fellow eye - a sight-saving principle; do not delay therapy pending diagnostic studies (Harrison 22e p.227, p.2903).","Sudden visual loss / blindness can be the presenting or emergent feature (Harrison 22e p.2902).","Aortic aneurysm: GCA patients are 18 times more likely than the general population to develop thoracic aortic aneurysms, carrying risk of rupture or dissection (Harrison 22e p.2903).","Subclavian stenosis presenting as arm claudication signals large-vessel involvement (Harrison 22e p.2902)."],"prognosis":"Acute mortality directly from GCA is uncommon; when fatal it is usually due to cerebrovascular events or myocardial infarction. The main late mortality risk is rupture or dissection of an aortic aneurysm (18-fold increased thoracic aortic aneurysm risk). Disease is highly glucocorticoid-responsive but relapsing: symptom recurrence during steroid tapering occurs in 60-85% of patients, most require at least 2 years of treatment, and glucocorticoid toxicity affects 35-65% and is a major source of morbidity. (Harrison 22e p.2903)","pitfalls":["A normal ESR does not rule out GCA (Harrison 22e p.115).","A single negative temporal artery biopsy does not exclude the disease because lesions are segmental/patchy - take a long segment and serial sections (Harrison 22e p.2903).","Improvement of headache on prednisone is non-specific (migraine also responds), so do not use the steroid response alone to confirm GCA (Harrison 22e p.115).","Once an IL-6 inhibitor is started, ESR/CRP can no longer track activity or flag infection (Harrison 22e p.2902).","Minor ESR/CRP rises during tapering may reflect other causes (including infection) rather than a true flare, especially in a symptom-free patient (Harrison 22e p.2903).","Glucocorticoids should not be given for the nonarteritic form of AION - distinguishing arteritic from nonarteritic AION matters (Harrison 22e p.227)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.115","Harrison 22e p.227","Harrison 22e p.2902","Harrison 22e p.2903"],"crossLinks":["pmr","vasculitis"]},"tension_ha":{"name":"Tension-type headache","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Ch.441 p.3514 (running p.3471); Ch.17 p.156-159","clinicalPearls":["TTH is recognized as bilateral, tight, band-like discomfort that builds gradually, fluctuates in intensity, and can persist near-continuously for days (Harrison 22e p.3514).","The most reliable bedside approach is to reserve the TTH label for headaches entirely lacking accompanying features such as nausea, vomiting, photophobia, phonophobia, osmophobia, throbbing quality, or worsening with movement (Harrison 22e p.3514).","Migraine is the principal differential of TTH; the presence of any migrainous accompaniment points away from TTH and toward migraine (Harrison 22e p.3514).","Patients who fit the TTH phenotype but also have migraine at other times, a family history of migraine, childhood migrainous syndromes, or typical migraine triggers may be biologically distinct from those with pure featureless TTH (Harrison 22e p.3514).","Diagnostic boundaries are genuinely blurred: the International Headache Society main definition permits some nausea, photophobia, or phonophobia, whereas the stricter appendix definition does not, and Harrison favors the appendix (feature-free) definition to separate TTH from migraine (Harrison 22e p.3514).","Among primary-care patients with headache lasting 3 months or more and no neurologic symptoms or signs, fewer than 10% actually have TTH and more than 90% have migraine, so a confident TTH label in this setting is uncommon (Harrison 22e p.3514).","TTH is the most prevalent primary headache type overall, accounting for roughly 69% of primary headaches in a population series (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-1)."],"pathophysiology":"The mechanism of TTH is incompletely understood. Harrison favors a primary disorder of central nervous system pain modulation alone, contrasting it with migraine, which involves more generalized disturbance of sensory modulation. Despite the name, there is no clear evidence that nervous tension is causative; importantly, muscle contraction does NOT reliably distinguish TTH from migraine, as no difference in contraction is seen between the two headache types. A genetic contribution has been suggested but is unreliable, since current diagnostic criteria likely contaminated those studies with migraine patients (Harrison 22e p.3514).","additionalDifferentials":["Migraine (the main differential, defined by migrainous accompaniments TTH lacks) (Harrison 22e p.3514)","Migraine-TTH overlap / mixed phenotype with intercurrent migraine attacks (Harrison 22e p.3514)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chronic migraine within the chronic daily headache spectrum (Harrison 22e p.158, Table 17-3)","Medication-overuse headache (refractory daily/near-daily headache from analgesic overuse) (Harrison 22e p.158-159)","Other secondary causes of chronic daily headache: post-traumatic, inflammatory (e.g., giant cell arteritis, sarcoidosis, Behcet syndrome), and chronic CNS infection (Harrison 22e p.158, Table 17-3)"],"additionalInvestigations":["TTH is a clinical diagnosis; neuroimaging is not routine but CT or MRI of the brain is indicated when the examination is abnormal or the headache is recent-onset, with CT and MRI being comparably sensitive as initial screens (Harrison 22e p.157).","Lumbar puncture may be required when a serious secondary cause is suspected and a benign etiology cannot otherwise be established (Harrison 22e p.157).","General evaluation of acute headache may add palpation of cranial arteries, cervical spine assessment, blood pressure and urine for cardiovascular/renal status, and ocular examination including funduscopy and intraocular pressure (Harrison 22e p.157)."],"redFlags":["Sudden-onset or first severe headache, or the 'worst headache ever' (Harrison 22e p.156-157, Table 17-2)","Vomiting preceding the headache (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-2)","Subacute worsening over days or weeks (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-2)","Pain provoked by bending, lifting, or coughing (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-2)","Pain that disturbs sleep or is present immediately on awakening (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-2)","Onset after age 55 (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-2)","Fever or unexplained systemic signs, or a known systemic illness (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-2)","Abnormal neurologic examination (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-2)","Local tenderness, e.g., over the temporal artery region (Harrison 22e p.156, Table 17-2)","Serious secondary causes to consider with new severe headache include meningitis, subarachnoid hemorrhage, epidural/subdural hematoma, glaucoma, tumor, and purulent sinusitis (Harrison 22e p.157)"],"prognosis":"TTH is classified as episodic when infrequent and chronic when occurring on 15 or more days per month; the pain fluctuates and may persist near-continuously for many days. Chronic TTH falls within the chronic daily headache group, which carries considerable disability, and may be complicated by medication overuse (Harrison 22e p.3514, p.158).","pitfalls":["Over-applying the TTH label: in chronic headache without neurologic signs, more than 90% of patients actually have migraine, so TTH is frequently overdiagnosed (Harrison 22e p.3514).","Assuming muscle contraction confirms TTH; it does NOT distinguish TTH from migraine (Harrison 22e p.3514).","Overlooking medication overuse, which can convert episodic headache into refractory daily/near-daily headache and blunt the effect of preventive treatment (Harrison 22e p.158-159).","Mistaking a serious secondary headache for benign primary headache when an underlying cause has activated or worsened the primary syndrome (Harrison 22e p.158)."],"severityClassification":"Pain is characteristically mild to moderate, bilateral, tight and band-like, and non-throbbing. By frequency the IHS scheme distinguishes infrequent episodic, frequent episodic, chronic (>=15 days/month), and probable TTH; Harrison emphasizes the episodic-versus-chronic distinction (Harrison 22e p.3514, p.155 IHS classification).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3514","Harrison 22e p.156","Harrison 22e p.157","Harrison 22e p.158","Harrison 22e p.159"],"crossLinks":["migraine","iih"]},"thyroid_storm":{"name":"Thyroid storm","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"3076-3080","clinicalPearls":["Thyrotoxic crisis (thyroid storm) is a rare, life-threatening exacerbation of hyperthyroidism (Harrison 22e p.3080).","Characteristic features include fever, delirium, seizures, coma, vomiting, diarrhoea and jaundice (Harrison 22e p.3080).","Often precipitated by an acute intercurrent illness (e.g. stroke, infection, trauma, diabetic ketoacidosis), by surgery (especially thyroid surgery), or by radioiodine treatment given to a patient whose hyperthyroidism is only partially treated or untreated (Harrison 22e p.3080).","Apathetic thyrotoxicosis: in the elderly, features may be subtle or masked, presenting mainly with fatigue and weight loss, and can be mistaken for depression (Harrison 22e p.3076).","Thyrotoxicosis may be complicated by a form of hypokalaemic periodic paralysis, particularly common in Asian males (Harrison 22e p.3076).","Treatment of the thyrotoxic state alone restores sinus rhythm in up to 75% of patients with thyrotoxicosis-associated atrial fibrillation who have no underlying cardiac disease (Harrison 22e p.3077)."],"pathophysiology":"Thyroid storm represents a severe, decompensated form of thyroid hormone excess (thyrotoxicosis), typically arising when an acute stressor is superimposed on partially treated or untreated hyperthyroidism. Excess circulating thyroid hormone drives a hypermetabolic, hyperadrenergic state; high cardiac output produces a bounding pulse, widened pulse pressure and may precipitate or worsen heart failure or angina, particularly in the elderly or those with pre-existing heart disease (Harrison 22e p.3077, p.3080).","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of thyrotoxicosis besides Graves' disease must be considered (e.g. toxic adenoma, toxic multinodular goitre, destructive thyroiditis, ectopic thyroid tissue, factitious thyrotoxicosis); radionuclide scan/uptake or color-flow Doppler ultrasonography helps distinguish hyperthyroidism from destructive thyroiditis (Harrison 22e p.3078)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Suppressed TSH with elevated total and unbound (free) T4 and T3 (Harrison 22e p.3078).","T3 toxicosis (only T3 raised) in 2-5% of patients, more in areas of borderline iodine intake; T4 toxicosis (raised T4, normal T3) is occasionally seen when hyperthyroidism is iodine-induced (Harrison 22e p.3078).","TRAb (TSH receptor antibody) measurement to support a diagnosis of Graves' disease (Harrison 22e p.3078).","Watch for confounding biochemistry: elevated bilirubin, liver enzymes and ferritin, plus microcytic anaemia and thrombocytopenia, may accompany thyrotoxicosis (Harrison 22e p.3078)."],"redFlags":["Fever, delirium, seizures or coma in a thyrotoxic patient indicate thyroid storm (Harrison 22e p.3080).","Death is most often due to cardiac failure, arrhythmia or hyperthermia (Harrison 22e p.3080)."],"prognosis":"Mortality remains high (4-17%) even with treatment, usually from cardiac failure, arrhythmia or hyperthermia (Harrison 22e p.3080).","pitfalls":["Radioiodine therapy in a patient with partially treated or untreated hyperthyroidism can itself precipitate thyrotoxic crisis (Harrison 22e p.3080).","In the elderly, thyrotoxicosis may be masked (apathetic thyrotoxicosis), delaying recognition (Harrison 22e p.3076)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.3076","Harrison 22e p.3077","Harrison 22e p.3078","Harrison 22e p.3080"],"crossLinks":["hyperthyroidism","atrial_fib"]},"tia":{"name":"Transient ischaemic attack","system":"Neurology / Vascular","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3378, 3428, 3430, 3445, 3447, 3448","clinicalPearls":["TIA classically produces 'negative' symptoms (transient loss of function); positive sensory symptoms or involuntary movements point instead to seizure (p.3378).","Most TIAs last under 1 hour, well within the historical 24-hour window (p.3448).","About 25% of symptomatic internal carotid disease is heralded by recurrent transient monocular blindness (amaurosis fugax), typically described as a horizontal shade sweeping down or up across one eye, lasting a few minutes (p.3430).","A high-pitched carotid bruit fading into diastole suggests a tight stenosis; paradoxically the bruit may fade or vanish as occlusion becomes imminent and flow drops (p.3430).","Causes of TIA and ischaemic stroke are identical, so the diagnostic evaluation of a TIA should parallel that of a completed stroke (p.3448)."],"pathophysiology":"A TIA results from transient focal cerebral ischaemia when blood flow falls for longer than several seconds; neurons lack glycogen so deficits appear within seconds, but because the occluded vessel reopens and flow is restored before infarction occurs, symptoms resolve completely. Mechanisms mirror ischaemic stroke: emboli to the brain (cardiac or proximal arterial source) or in-situ thrombosis of an intracranial vessel. Small-vessel (lacunar) TIAs may recur several times a day and last only minutes (Harrison 22e p.3428, 3448, 3447).","additionalDifferentials":["Seizure (esp. ongoing focal seizure without convulsive activity) — positive symptoms and a marching spread over seconds favour seizure (p.3378, p.3428)","Migraine, including acephalgic migraine even in older first-time patients — slower march over minutes with headache, nausea or scintillating visual symptoms (p.3428)","Intracranial tumour presenting acutely via haemorrhage, seizure or hydrocephalus (p.3428)","Metabolic encephalopathy — fluctuating mental status without focal findings (p.3428)","Stroke recrudescence — fever/sepsis unmasking a prior deficit that clears as infection is treated (p.3428)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Seizure (focal, may mimic transient deficit) (p.3378, p.3428)","Migraine / acephalgic migraine with transient aura (p.3428)","Syncope from systemic hypotension (cardiac arrhythmia, sepsis, haemorrhagic shock) (p.3428)","Metabolic encephalopathy (p.3428)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CT- or MRI-based angiography of neck and intracranial vessels to assess large-vessel stenosis/occlusion (MRA is highly sensitive for extracranial carotid stenosis but tends to overestimate severity) (p.3445, p.3430)","Echocardiography (transthoracic or transoesophageal), with bubble-contrast/agitated-saline study to detect right-to-left shunt (PFO/ASD) for suspected paradoxical embolism (p.3445)","Cardiac telemetry following the initial ECG; ambulatory/prolonged ECG monitoring to detect intermittent atrial fibrillation if none is found in hospital (p.3445)","Retinal/funduscopic exam for cholesterol (Hollenhorst) plaques and examination of cervical/peripheral vessels and heart (p.3445)","Baseline labs: CBC, ESR, electrolytes, glucose, lipid profile, creatinine/BUN, PT/PTT, urinalysis, chest x-ray (p.3445)"],"redFlags":["Most strokes after a TIA occur within the first 2 days — the early window is the highest-risk period and warrants urgent (not deferred) workup (p.3448)","Stuttering or crescendo course of small-vessel TIAs heralding lacunar infarct can progress to severe permanent disability (p.3447)","Amaurosis fugax indicates a symptomatic internal carotid source needing prompt carotid evaluation (p.3430)"],"prognosis":"Stroke risk after a TIA is approximately 10-15% within the first 3 months, with most events occurring in the first 2 days; this early risk is well predicted by the ABCD2 score, so urgent evaluation and treatment are justified (Harrison 22e p.3448).","pitfalls":["A TIA is a clinical diagnosis: a normal brain imaging study does NOT rule it out (p.3448).","If a relevant infarction is seen on imaging, the event is reclassified as a stroke regardless of how briefly symptoms lasted — the modern definition is tissue-based, not purely time-based (p.3428, p.3448).","Patients with carotid/vertebrobasilar TIA may report symptoms 'in one eye' when the deficit is actually a homonymous hemifield loss affecting both eyes (p.3430-equivalent / cortical visual TIA).","Acephalgic migraine can mimic TIA even with no prior migraine history and even after age 65 (p.3428)."],"severityClassification":"ABCD2 score (Harrison 22e p.3448) stratifies early stroke risk after TIA: 1 point each for Age >=60, BP (SBP >140 or DBP >90 mmHg), and Diabetes; for Clinical features 2 points for unilateral weakness or 1 for speech disturbance without weakness; for Duration 2 points for >=60 min or 1 for 10-59 min. The 3-month stroke rate rises with the total: ~0% at score 0-1, ~3% at 2-3, ~8% at 4, ~12% at 5, ~17% at 6, and ~22% at 7.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3378","Harrison 22e p.3428","Harrison 22e p.3430","Harrison 22e p.3445","Harrison 22e p.3447","Harrison 22e p.3448"],"crossLinks":["ischemic_stroke","ich","atrial_fib","vasovagal_syncope"]},"toxic_hepatitis":{"name":"Drug-induced / toxic hepatitis","system":"Hepatology / Toxicology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Chapter 351 (Toxic and Drug-Induced Hepatitis), pp.2665-2672; anti-TB hepatotoxicity monitoring p.1463; liver transplantation/ALF context Chapter 356 pp.2718-2719","clinicalPearls":["DILI is the most common cause of acute liver failure, and signals of hepatotoxicity in clinical trials are the leading reason candidate drugs fail to reach approval (p.2665).","Two broad mechanisms are recognised: direct (intrinsic) toxicity that is predictable and dose-dependent with a short latency, versus idiosyncratic reactions that are infrequent (roughly 1 in 1,000 to 1 in 100,000), unpredictable, and may appear at any time but typically 5-90 days after starting the drug (p.2665, p.2667).","Although idiosyncratic injury is regarded as not dose-related, most implicated drugs are given at relatively high daily doses (often >100 mg); high-potency drugs given in milligram amounts rarely cause hepatotoxicity (p.2665).","The hepatocellular-versus-cholestatic pattern is defined by the R value, the ratio of ALT to alkaline phosphatase (each as multiples of the upper limit of normal): R >5.0 = hepatocellular, R <2.0 = cholestatic, R 2.0-5.0 = mixed (p.2667).","Hy's Law (named for Hyman Zimmerman): the appearance of jaundice during drug therapy predicts more serious injury, with roughly a 10:1 ratio of jaundiced cases to acute liver failure (about 1 in 10 jaundiced patients progresses to liver failure) (p.2667).","Amoxicillin-clavulanate (Augmentin) is the most frequently implicated single antibiotic/agent causing DILI in the US and Europe, typically producing mixed or predominantly cholestatic injury that may begin after the drug has already been stopped (p.2667, p.2670).","Acetaminophen (paracetamol) is the prototype direct hepatotoxin and the most prevalent cause of acute liver failure in the Western world (p.2667, p.2668).","A characteristic biochemical signature of acetaminophen injury is very high aminotransferases (often >10,000 IU/L, far above viral hepatitis) with relatively low bilirubin in the hyperacute phase; this should prompt focused questioning even without a clear overdose history (p.2668).","Complementary and alternative (herbal/dietary) medicines now account for over 20% of DILI in the US; weight-loss products and anabolic steroids are common culprits and are often unregulated (p.2671).","Acetaminophen toxicity is potentiated by chronic alcohol use (CYP2E1 induction plus glutathione depletion), so the toxic dose may be as low as ~2 g in chronic alcoholics; starvation/fasting and enzyme-inducing drugs (phenobarbital, isoniazid) also increase susceptibility (p.2668-2670).","Four agents are notable for causing a chronic, autoimmune-hepatitis-like picture with positive ANA: nitrofurantoin, minocycline, hydralazine, and alpha-methyldopa (p.2667, p.2670).","DILI is nearly always a presumptive diagnosis of exclusion; causality assessment relies on temporal association, injury pattern, the drug's prior record, and ruling out other causes (scoring tools: RUCAM, DILIN expert opinion, newer RECAM) (p.2667-2668).","Drug hepatotoxicity is generally not more frequent in those with pre-existing chronic liver disease, although the severity of outcome may be greater; statins specifically can be used safely in chronic hepatitis C and steatosis (p.2668, p.2671-2672)."],"pathophysiology":"Liver injury can follow ingestion of essentially any xenobiotic (industrial toxins, drugs, herbal/dietary supplements). Most drugs are lipophilic and undergo phase I oxidation/methylation by cytochrome P450 enzymes followed by phase II conjugation (glucuronidation, sulfation) or glutathione inactivation to a water-soluble form for renal/biliary excretion; most hepatotoxicity arises from a reactive phase I metabolite, and glutathione depletion compounds the injury. Damage occurs through several overlapping routes: direct hepatocyte injury via free radicals/reactive intermediates causing membrane lipid peroxidation; activation of innate or adaptive immune responses (HLA-haplotype-dependent hapten binding, cytotoxic lymphocytes, antibody-mediated cytotoxicity); triggering of apoptosis via TNF-alpha/Fas pathways; disruption of bile canalicular transport pumps (e.g., MRP3) leading to toxic bile-acid accumulation and cholestasis; and inhibition of mitochondrial beta-oxidation/respiration producing lactic acidosis and microvesicular steatosis. Nuclear receptors such as CAR and PXR can also induce hepatotoxicity. For acetaminophen specifically, a minor fraction is converted by CYP2E1 to the reactive metabolite NAPQI, normally detoxified by conjugation with hepatoprotective glutathione; when NAPQI overwhelms or glutathione is depleted, NAPQI binds covalently to hepatocyte macromolecules forming protein adducts that drive centrilobular necrosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Viral hepatitis (idiosyncratic DILI from isoniazid or ciprofloxacin can be clinically and histologically indistinguishable from acute hepatitis A/B/C) (p.2667)","Autoimmune hepatitis (nitrofurantoin, minocycline, hydralazine, methyldopa produce an AIH-like picture with positive ANA) (p.2667, p.2670)","Alcohol-associated (alcoholic) hepatitis (amiodarone causes a pseudoalcoholic injury with steatosis, neutrophilic infiltration, and Mallory's hyaline) (p.2670)","Primary sclerosing cholangitis (a PSC-like picture can follow chemotherapy and long-term ketamine use) (p.2667)","Primary biliary cholangitis-like lesion (rarely associated with chlorpromazine) (p.2669)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute viral hepatitis A, B, or C (hepatocellular idiosyncratic DILI mimics it; differentiate with viral serologies) (p.2667)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Autoimmune hepatitis (p.2667, p.2670)","Alcoholic hepatitis / pseudoalcoholic liver injury (amiodarone) (p.2670)","Ischemic (hypoxic) hepatitis and other causes of marked transaminase elevation (p.2668)","Cholestasis of pregnancy / estrogen-induced cholestasis (genetically determined canalicular transporter defects) (p.2667)","Budd-Chiari syndrome / hepatic vein occlusion and venoocclusive (sinusoidal obstruction) disease as drug-related vascular mimics (oral contraceptives; pyrrolizidine alkaloids; pre-transplant chemotherapy) (p.2667, p.2670, p.2671)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Calculate the R value (ALT/ALP, each as multiples of ULN) to classify hepatocellular vs cholestatic vs mixed injury (p.2667)","Detailed exposure history including occupational/home chemicals, prescription and OTC drugs, and herbal/dietary supplements (p.2665, p.2671)","Serum acetaminophen level interpreted against the time-based nomogram (levels 4-8 h post-ingestion guide risk; >300 ug/mL at 4 h predicts severe injury, <150 ug/mL makes injury highly unlikely) (p.2668, p.2670)","Viral hepatitis serologies and autoantibodies (ANA, anti-smooth muscle, anti-mitochondrial, anti-LKM2) to exclude/identify autoimmune-pattern DILI (p.2667, p.2670)","Eosinophil count (eosinophilia supports a hypersensitivity reaction, e.g., trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, phenytoin) (p.2665, p.2671)","Liver biopsy where the pattern is unclear (helps distinguish injury types; e.g., electron microscopy showing phospholipid-laden lamellar bodies in amiodarone toxicity) (p.2670)","Arterial lactate in severe acetaminophen injury (>3.5 mmol/L identifies patients much more likely to need transplantation) (p.2670)","Causality assessment tools (RUCAM, DILIN structured expert opinion, RECAM) and reference resources (LiverTox) (p.2668)"],"redFlags":["Jaundice arising during drug therapy (Hy's Law) - predicts severe injury and a substantial risk of progression to liver failure (p.2667)","Rising aminotransferases to >5x the upper limit of normal (or >3x ULN with symptoms) - threshold to withdraw the suspected agent (p.2668)","Acute liver failure features: coagulopathy plus hepatic encephalopathy; develops in ~10% of DILI cases (p.2668)","Cerebral edema in progressing acute liver failure (p.2667, Chap.356)","Acute kidney injury and myocardial injury accompanying severe acetaminophen toxicity (renal injury in ~75% of severe cases) (p.2668, p.2670)","Lactic acidosis (mitochondrial toxicity, e.g., with nucleoside analogues/antiretrovirals or valproate) (p.2665, p.2672)","Hypersensitivity/DRESS features (rash, fever, eosinophilia) accompanying liver injury (e.g., trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) (p.2671)","Continued use of the offending agent despite symptoms - most fatalities (e.g., isoniazid) occur in this setting (p.2668, p.2670)"],"prognosis":"Most DILI is self-limited and resolves after the offending agent is withdrawn. However, acute liver failure develops in about 10% of DILI cases; once that threshold (coagulopathy plus hepatic encephalopathy) is reached, spontaneous recovery occurs in fewer than 30%, and liver transplantation is undertaken in more than 40% of these patients (p.2668). For acetaminophen, up to 72% of poisoned patients in some Scandinavian series progress to encephalopathy and coagulopathy (lower in the UK/US); a single dose of 10-15 g can cause clinical injury and fulminant fatal disease is usually associated with >=25 g; the N-acetylcysteine antidote markedly reduces severity and is most effective given early (within ~8-12 h), though it retains some benefit up to 24-36 h. Survivors of acetaminophen overdose rarely have ongoing liver injury (p.2668, p.2670). Several agents cause chronic injury or progression to cirrhosis (methotrexate, tamoxifen, amiodarone), and the autoimmune-pattern agents (nitrofurantoin, minocycline, hydralazine, methyldopa) can lead to cirrhosis if not recognised early (p.2668-2670).","pitfalls":["Treating mild, transient aminotransferase rises ('adaptation', e.g., with isoniazid, valproate, phenytoin, statins) as significant injury - these often resolve despite continued use; conversely, ignoring symptomatic/progressive rises is dangerous (p.2665, p.2669, p.2671-2672).","Missing the acetaminophen diagnosis because of denial, altered mentation, or staggered/unintentional overdose - very high transaminases with low bilirubin should raise suspicion even without a clear history (p.2668).","Forgetting herbal/dietary supplements and OTC products in the drug history - they now cause >20% of US DILI and may contain undeclared chemicals (e.g., diclofenac in 'antirheumatic herbs') (p.2671).","Assuming standard acetaminophen doses are safe in chronic alcohol users - the toxic threshold can fall to ~2 g (p.2670).","Attributing cholestatic jaundice in a young male body-builder to hepatitis rather than anabolic/androgenic steroid use (p.2670).","Expecting injury only early in therapy - amoxicillin-clavulanate and nitrofurantoin can have long latency and may declare after the drug is stopped (p.2670).","In direct toxins, focusing on the liver while missing concurrent renal or other organ involvement that may threaten survival (p.2668)."],"severityClassification":"Injury pattern is classified by the R value (ALT/ALP as multiples of ULN): R >5.0 hepatocellular, R <2.0 cholestatic, R 2.0-5.0 mixed (p.2667). Hy's Law serves as a severity/prognostic marker: jaundice (significant bilirubin rise) accompanying hepatocellular DILI predicts a markedly higher risk of acute liver failure (about a 10:1 jaundice-to-failure ratio) (p.2667). The aminotransferase threshold of >5x ULN (or >3x ULN with symptoms) marks the point at which the offending agent should be withdrawn (p.2668). Causality (not severity) is graded on a five-point likelihood scale by DILIN (definite, highly likely, probable, possible, unlikely) (p.2668). The clinical endpoint of severity is acute liver failure, defined by coagulopathy plus hepatic encephalopathy (p.2668).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2665","Harrison 22e p.2666","Harrison 22e p.2667","Harrison 22e p.2668","Harrison 22e p.2669","Harrison 22e p.2670","Harrison 22e p.2671","Harrison 22e p.2672","Harrison 22e p.1463","Harrison 22e p.2718"],"crossLinks":["VIRAL_HEPATITIS","hepatic_enceph","decomp_cirrhosis","drug_intox"]},"ttp_hus":{"name":"Thrombotic microangiopathy (TTP/HUS)","system":"Hematology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e Chap. 120 pp.967-968 (TTP/HUS); Chap. 105 pp.800-801 (familial/atypical HUS); Chap. on cutaneous purpura p.450; oncologic-emergencies HUS p.638; blood-smear schistocytes p.~640","clinicalPearls":["The thrombotic microangiopathies are unified by microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia (MAHA): thrombocytopenia plus fragmented red cells (schistocytes) on the smear, biochemical haemolysis (high LDH, high unconjugated bilirubin, low haptoglobin) and microvascular thrombosis (Harrison 22e p.967).","TTP was classically described as a pentad of MAHA, thrombocytopenia, renal failure, neurologic changes and fever; the full pentad is now seen less often because earlier diagnosis and treatment intervene before it evolves (Harrison 22e pp.967-968).","ADAMTS13 activity below 10% is diagnostic of TTP; the assay (activity level plus anti-ADAMTS13 antibody) is central to working up any MAHA (Harrison 22e p.968).","ADAMTS13 levels are generally normal in HUS, although they have occasionally been reported as decreased — so a normal ADAMTS13 does not exclude a thrombotic microangiopathy other than TTP (Harrison 22e p.968).","Schistocytes are red-cell fragments produced by physical disruption against intravascular platelet/fibrin strands or external forces, and are the morphologic hallmark of MAHA (Harrison 22e p.~640).","The Coombs (direct antiglobulin) test is negative in microangiopathic haemolysis, helping separate it from autoimmune haemolytic anaemia (Harrison 22e p.638).","TTP is more common in women than men, and is over-represented in HIV infection and in pregnancy; congenital ADAMTS13 deficiency (Upshaw-Schulman) may first declare itself during a first pregnancy (Harrison 22e p.968).","In a classic HUS presentation, a preceding episode of diarrhoea (often bloody) in a child points to Shiga-toxin-producing E. coli O157:H7 as the commonest, though not the only, cause (Harrison 22e p.968).","Atypical HUS is essentially a complement-alternative-pathway disease — driven by mutations in complement components/regulators (C3, CFB, CFH, CD46/MCP, CFI, THBD) or anti-factor-H antibodies — and clinical disease usually requires a trigger such as infection, so penetrance is incomplete (Harrison 22e pp.800-801)."],"pathophysiology":"TTP arises from deficient or antibody-inhibited ADAMTS13, the metalloprotease that cleaves von Willebrand factor (VWF). VWF is secreted by endothelium as ultra-large multimers; when these are not cleaved they persist and drive pathological platelet adhesion and aggregation, producing microvascular thrombi. ADAMTS13 deficiency alone is not sufficient — congenitally ADAMTS13-deficient individuals develop TTP only episodically, implying a second trigger. In HUS the dominant lesion is endothelial injury with deposition of fibrin in capillary/arteriolar walls, predominantly in the kidney; typical (diarrhoea-associated) HUS is driven by Shiga toxin from E. coli O157:H7, while atypical HUS results from dysregulated activation of the complement alternative pathway with consequent endothelial damage. The shared downstream picture is microvascular platelet/fibrin thrombi causing red-cell fragmentation (MAHA), consumption thrombocytopenia and end-organ ischaemia (Harrison 22e pp.967-968, 800-801).","additionalDifferentials":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) — also gives thrombocytopenia and microangiopathy, but a consumptive coagulopathy predominates with prolonged PT and often prolonged aPTT (Harrison 22e p.967)","Atypical/complement-mediated HUS (aHUS) as a distinct entity from TTP (Harrison 22e pp.800-801)","Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (Harrison 22e p.450)","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia and thrombosis (HIT) (Harrison 22e p.450)","Monoclonal cryoglobulinaemia with intravascular thrombi (Harrison 22e p.450)","Malignant hypertension affecting the renal microvasculature (Harrison 22e — listed among renal-microvascular causes of MAHA)"],"infectionMimics":["Shiga-toxin-producing E. coli O157:H7 gastroenteritis (and other enterohaemorrhagic strains) preceding diarrhoea-associated HUS (Harrison 22e p.968)","HIV infection, which is associated with a higher incidence of TTP (Harrison 22e p.968)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation (distinguished by abnormal PT/aPTT and clotting-factor/fibrinogen consumption) (Harrison 22e p.967)","Drug-induced microangiopathy — immune-mediated (ticlopidine, possibly clopidogrel, quinine) or by direct endothelial toxicity (cyclosporine, tacrolimus, gemcitabine, mitomycin C) (Harrison 22e p.968)","Chemotherapy-/cancer-associated HUS (mitomycin and gemcitabine most often; also cisplatin, bleomycin, proteasome and VEGF inhibitors) (Harrison 22e p.638)","Malignancy-induced HUS from microvascular metastases or extensive marrow invasion (Harrison 22e p.638)","Bone-marrow / haematopoietic stem cell transplant-associated microangiopathy (Harrison 22e pp.638, 967)","Pregnancy/postpartum-associated thrombotic microangiopathy (including HELLP in the obstetric context) (Harrison 22e pp.967-968)","Systemic vasculitis (Harrison 22e p.967)"],"additionalInvestigations":["ADAMTS13 activity level AND anti-ADAMTS13 inhibitor/antibody assay (activity <10% diagnostic of TTP) (Harrison 22e p.968)","PT and aPTT — characteristically NORMAL in TTP/HUS; abnormal results point toward DIC (Harrison 22e p.967)","Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test — negative in MAHA (Harrison 22e p.638)","Full blood count with platelet count and peripheral smear for schistocytes (Harrison 22e p.968)","Reticulocyte count (reticulocytosis supports haemolysis) (Harrison 22e p.638)","Renal function / serum creatinine and urinalysis (haematuria, proteinuria, granular or hyaline casts in HUS) (Harrison 22e p.638)","Complement-regulatory gene testing (C3, CFB, CFH, CD46, CFI, THBD) and anti-factor-H antibody for suspected atypical HUS — though assigning pathogenicity to variants is difficult and no functional diagnostic assay is established (Harrison 22e pp.968, 800-801)"],"redFlags":["New unexplained thrombocytopenia with MAHA is a haematologic emergency — TTP is devastating if not recognised and treated promptly (Harrison 22e p.968)","Plasma exchange should be initiated urgently on clinical suspicion without waiting for confirmatory ADAMTS13 results, and likewise re-initiated promptly on suspected relapse (Harrison 22e p.968)","Fluctuating neurologic abnormalities (especially headache and confusion) in the setting of MAHA suggest TTP (Harrison 22e p.450)","Persistently low ADAMTS13 carries a greater risk of ongoing sequelae including stroke (Harrison 22e p.968)","In cancer-/transfusion-associated HUS, severe hypertension, pulmonary oedema and rapid worsening of haemolysis and renal function can follow a blood or blood-product transfusion (Harrison 22e p.638)"],"prognosis":"Plasma exchange transformed TTP outcomes, reducing mortality from roughly 85-100% to about 10-30%. Relapse is common: 25-45% of plasma-exchange-treated patients relapse within 30 days of apparent remission and 12-40% relapse late, with relapse more frequent in those with severe ADAMTS13 deficiency at presentation. In diarrhoea-associated HUS roughly 40% of children need some dialysis but overall mortality is <5%; non-diarrhoeal HUS carries higher mortality (~26%). Untreated atypical HUS had acute-phase mortality up to ~15% with up to 50% progressing to end-stage renal disease, though spontaneous remission occurs and relapse is likely with re-exposure to a trigger. Cancer-/chemotherapy-associated HUS has a high case-fatality rate, with most patients dying within months (Harrison 22e pp.968, 800-801, 638).","pitfalls":["Do not reflexively transfuse platelets in TTP — platelet transfusion may fuel further microvascular thrombosis and is reserved for life-threatening bleeding (principle, Harrison 22e Chap. 120).","Do not assume normal coagulation excludes a thrombotic microangiopathy: PT and aPTT are characteristically normal in TTP/HUS, whereas they are abnormal in DIC — use them to discriminate, not to exclude (Harrison 22e p.967).","A normal ADAMTS13 does not point to TTP; ADAMTS13 is usually normal in HUS, so the diagnosis there rests on the clinical picture and complement work-up (Harrison 22e p.968).","Atypical HUS is a diagnosis of exclusion; patients may be started on plasma exchange until ADAMTS13 returns and the diagnosis clarifies, even though plasma exchange has not been shown to change clinical outcomes in aHUS (Harrison 22e p.968).","Plasma infusion/exchange does not alter the course of typical HUS or aHUS except in the subgroup with anti-factor-H antibodies (Harrison 22e p.968).","Drug-induced microangiopathy may improve simply on withdrawing or reducing the offending endothelial-toxic agent, so reflexive long-term plasma exchange may be unnecessary (Harrison 22e p.968).","Cancer-associated HUS can present without an evident malignancy — in one series 35% had no apparent cancer when the syndrome appeared, and it may surface weeks to months after the last chemotherapy dose (Harrison 22e p.638)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal TTP/HUS severity score in this material. Functional stratification used: ADAMTS13 activity <10% is diagnostic of TTP and severe deficiency at presentation predicts relapse and worse sequelae. HUS is grouped clinically into diarrhoea-associated (typical, Shiga-toxin) HUS versus non-diarrhoea-associated/atypical (complement-mediated) HUS, which carries higher mortality. (No PLASMIC score is described in the source and none is asserted here.) (Harrison 22e pp.968, 800-801).","references":["Harrison 22e p.967","Harrison 22e p.968","Harrison 22e p.800","Harrison 22e p.801","Harrison 22e p.638","Harrison 22e p.450"],"crossLinks":["dic","itp","glomerulonephritis"]},"variceal_bleed":{"name":"Variceal bleeding","system":"Hepatology / GI","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.360-363, 371-372, 2713-2715","clinicalPearls":["Portal hypertension is defined as a hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG) >5 mmHg; compensated cirrhosis typically has an HVPG of 5-10 mmHg, while clinically significant portal hypertension (CSPH), defined as HVPG ≥10 mmHg, carries substantial risk of decompensation (variceal bleeding, ascites, encephalopathy). Patients with a gradient >12 mmHg are at risk for variceal haemorrhage (p.2713-2714).","Each episode of variceal haemorrhage carries a 20-30% mortality rate, making it an immediate life-threatening complication of portal hypertension (p.2713).","In cirrhotic patients presenting with upper GI bleeding, prophylactic antibiotics (typically ceftriaxone) started before endoscopy reduce bacterial infections, recurrent bleeding AND mortality — this is a mortality-altering intervention, not merely supportive (p.362, p.2714).","A vasoactive/vasoconstricting agent (somatostatin, octreotide, terlipressin, vapreotide) improves initial bleeding control, reduces transfusion needs and reduces all-cause mortality; combined endoscopic plus pharmacologic therapy is superior to either alone (p.360, p.2714).","A restrictive transfusion strategy (transfuse when haemoglobin <7 g/dL, target 7-9 g/dL) reduced early rebleeding and mortality compared with a liberal threshold in randomised trial; haemoglobin may be normal at initial presentation because plasma and red-cell volumes fall proportionately (\"people bleed whole blood\") and may take up to 72 h to fall (p.362, p.2714).","Roughly one-third of patients with histologically confirmed cirrhosis have varices; about 5-15% of cirrhotics develop varices per year, and roughly one-third of patients with varices will bleed (p.2713).","IV erythromycin given shortly before endoscopy is suggested as a promotility agent to improve endoscopic visualisation in UGI bleeding, reducing the need for repeat endoscopy (p.362, p.2713).","Routine correction of an elevated prothrombin time with fresh frozen plasma is NOT recommended in acute variceal bleeding unless there is evidence of coagulopathy such as bleeding at other sites (e.g. IV-line sites) (p.2714).","Bleeding gastric varices, especially when entirely within the stomach or extending into the proximal stomach, are often not amenable to band ligation; tissue-adhesive injection (e.g. cyanoacrylate), TIPS, or balloon-occluded retrograde transvenous obliteration (BRTO) are alternative principles (p.361, p.2714)."],"pathophysiology":"Oesophageal varices are a consequence of portal hypertension, defined as an HVPG >5 mmHg. Portal hypertension arises from two simultaneous haemodynamic processes: (1) increased intrahepatic resistance to portal blood flow (from cirrhosis, regenerative nodules and microthrombi) and (2) increased splanchnic blood flow secondary to splanchnic vasodilation. Cirrhosis is associated with decreased intrahepatic endothelial nitric oxide synthase and nitric oxide, causing intrahepatic vasoconstriction, while systemically nitric oxide, VEGF and TNF rise, driving splanchnic vasodilation. In advanced stages, neurohumoral and vasoconstrictor activation causes sodium/water retention, increased blood volume and a hyperdynamic circulation that further raises portal pressure. Intrahepatic causes (mainly cirrhosis) account for >95% of portal hypertension; pre-hepatic (portal/splenic vein thrombosis) and post-hepatic (Budd-Chiari, right-heart congestion) causes also occur. Elevated portal pressure decompresses into portosystemic collaterals, producing gastro-oesophageal varices that can rupture and bleed. (p.2713-2714)","additionalDifferentials":["Peptic ulcer disease — the most common cause of upper GI bleeding (~50% of UGIB hospitalisations) (p.360)","Mallory-Weiss tear — ~2-10% of UGIB, classically preceded by vomiting/retching/coughing, usually on the gastric side of the gastro-oesophageal junction; stops spontaneously in ~80-90% (p.360)","Bleeding gastric varices, intestinal varices, and portal hypertensive gastropathy/enterocolopathy — other portal-hypertension-related sources distinct from oesophageal varices (p.361, p.2714)","Erosive disease — erosive oesophagitis, gastritis, duodenitis (up to ~30% of UGIB), commonly NSAID-related (p.361)","Stress-related gastric mucosal injury in the critically ill (p.361)","Less common: neoplasm, vascular ectasias (including hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia, gastric antral vascular ectasia), Dieulafoy's lesion, aortoenteric fistula, haemobilia/haemosuccus pancreaticus (p.361)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Portal hypertensive gastropathy — snakeskin-like mosaic mucosal pattern on endoscopy; can cause acute or chronic (iron-deficiency) GI bleeding and mimics variceal bleeding as a cause of haematemesis/melaena in the same cirrhotic patient (p.2714)","Gastric varices — also driven by portal hypertension but managed differently from oesophageal varices (p.361, p.2714)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper endoscopy within 24 h is first-line both to diagnose the bleeding source and to treat acutely; more urgent endoscopy (6-12 h) does not improve outcomes and may worsen them in high-risk patients (p.362, p.2714)","Endoscopic surveillance for varices: recommended at diagnosis of compensated cirrhosis, then every 2 years (active liver disease) or every 3 years (inactive); also at the time of hepatic decompensation (p.2714)","Liver stiffness by transient elastography — if <20 kPa and no thrombocytopenia, high-risk varices are rare and screening endoscopy may be deferred (p.2714)","HVPG measurement by interventional radiology (hepatic vein cannulation) in selected cases to confirm/quantify portal hypertension (p.371, p.2714)","Complete blood count (thrombocytopenia from hypersplenism is often the first marker of portal hypertension), liver biochemistry, albumin, and prothrombin time/INR to assess hepatic function and severity (p.371, p.2714)","Endoscopic stigmata predicting bleeding risk: red wale signs, hematocystic spots, diffuse erythema, bluish colour, cherry-red spots, white-nipple spots; larger varices and worse Child-Pugh/MELD also predict bleeding (p.2713)"],"redFlags":["Variceal haemorrhage is immediately life-threatening with 20-30% mortality per bleeding episode — treat as a major haemorrhage with airway/haemodynamic priority (p.2713)","Hemodynamic compromise (tachycardia, hypotension), increasing age and comorbidities predict rebleeding and death (p.362)","Increased frequency of spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP) in patients with variceal haemorrhage — SBP prophylaxis is recommended at presentation with upper GI bleeding (p.2715)","Persistent or recurrent bleeding despite endoscopic and medical therapy, and advanced liver disease (Child-Pugh B score 8-9 with active bleeding, or Child-Pugh C score 10-13), mark patients who benefit from early/salvage TIPS (p.360-361)"],"prognosis":"Variceal haemorrhage carries a 20-30% mortality rate with each bleeding episode and patients with variceal haemorrhage have poorer outcomes than those with other UGIB sources. Control of acute bleeding is achievable in the vast majority of cases, but bleeding recurs in the majority of patients unless definitive endoscopic therapy is instituted; oesophageal varices reflect clinically significant portal hypertension (HVPG ≥10 mmHg), and once cirrhosis decompensates the median survival is <2 years. Prophylactic antibiotics, vasoactive drugs and a restrictive transfusion strategy each independently reduce mortality. (p.360, p.2713-2714)","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal initial haemoglobin excludes severe bleeding — haemoglobin may be normal or only minimally reduced at presentation and can take up to 72 h to fall as extravascular fluid re-equilibrates (p.362)","Reflexively correcting a prolonged prothrombin time with FFP — not recommended in variceal bleeding without evidence of true coagulopathy, and FFP can raise portal pressure and cause volume overload (p.2714)","Over-liberal transfusion — a liberal threshold (Hb 9 g/dL) increased early rebleeding and mortality versus a restrictive (<7 g/dL) threshold (p.362, p.2714)","Omitting antibiotics in a cirrhotic UGI bleed — prophylactic antibiotics reduce infection, rebleeding and mortality and are a core, not optional, intervention (p.362, p.2714)","Attempting band ligation on gastric or proximal-stomach varices, where it is often unsuccessful — consider tissue adhesive, TIPS or BRTO (p.2714)","Forgetting that TIPS causes new or worsened hepatic encephalopathy in as many as 20% of patients, especially the elderly and those with pre-existing encephalopathy (p.2714)"],"severityClassification":"Bleeding/decompensation risk is stratified by Child-Pugh class and MELD score, by HVPG/wedged-hepatic-vein pressure (CSPH ≥10 mmHg; variceal bleeding risk at >12 mmHg), by varix size and location, and by endoscopic high-risk stigmata (red wale signs, cherry-red spots, etc.). For triage of UGIB generally, the Glasgow-Blatchford score (0-23) identifies very-low-risk patients (score 0-1) suitable for outpatient management. Advanced-disease thresholds favouring early TIPS: Child-Pugh B 8-9 with active bleeding at endoscopy, or Child-Pugh C 10-13. (p.362, p.2713-2714)","references":["Harrison 22e p.360","Harrison 22e p.361","Harrison 22e p.362","Harrison 22e p.363","Harrison 22e p.371","Harrison 22e p.372","Harrison 22e p.2713","Harrison 22e p.2714","Harrison 22e p.2715"],"crossLinks":["decomp_cirrhosis","peptic_ulcer","hepatic_enceph","SBP"]},"vasculitis":{"name":"Systemic vasculitis","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Chapter 375 (The Vasculitis Syndromes), pp. 2893-2948","clinicalPearls":["Suspect vasculitis in any patient with an unexplained multisystem illness; specific clues include palpable purpura, pulmonary infiltrates with microscopic hematuria, chronic sinusitis, mononeuritis multiplex, unexplained ischemic events, and glomerulonephritis with multisystem disease (Harrison 22e p.2937).","The first step in suspected vasculitis is to exclude conditions that mimic it - especially infections - particularly before starting empirical immunosuppression in a deteriorating patient (Harrison 22e p.2937).","Definitive diagnosis usually rests on biopsy of clinically involved tissue; 'blind' biopsy of organs with no clinical evidence of involvement has very low yield and should be avoided. Arteriography is used when PAN, Takayasu, or primary CNS vasculitis is suspected (Harrison 22e p.2937-2938).","ANCA-associated vasculitides (GPA, MPA, EGPA) share small-vessel involvement and ANCA positivity but have distinct clinical phenotypes and should be regarded as separate entities (Harrison 22e p.2937).","cANCA targets proteinase-3 (PR3) - present in >90% of active GPA; pANCA targets myeloperoxidase (MPO) - only anti-MPO antibodies are convincingly linked to vasculitis (MPA, EGPA, GPA, isolated necrotizing crescentic GN) (Harrison 22e p.2937).","A pANCA pattern NOT due to anti-MPO can occur in nonvasculitic conditions: rheumatic/nonrheumatic autoimmune disease, inflammatory bowel disease, certain drugs, and infections including endocarditis and bacterial airway infection in cystic fibrosis (Harrison 22e p.2937).","ANCA titres do not reliably track disease activity - GPA can be active despite negative ANCA, titres correlate poorly with activity, and patients in remission may keep high ANCA for years (Harrison 22e p.2937, p.2941).","Predictive value of a positive ANCA in GPA is ~90% with active glomerulonephritis but falls to 30-60% in sinus/lung disease without renal involvement; tissue biopsy is then needed to exclude infection or malignancy that may give a false-positive ANCA (Harrison 22e p.2941).","In GPA the renal lesion is a pauci-immune (no immune-complex deposition) focal segmental glomerulitis that can evolve into rapidly progressive crescentic glomerulonephritis (Harrison 22e p.2939-2940).","Saddle-nose deformity (from nasal septal perforation) and subglottic stenosis (~16% of GPA, may cause severe airway obstruction) are characteristic upper-airway sequelae of GPA (Harrison 22e p.2940).","Mononeuritis multiplex is a recurring vasculitic clue - common in EGPA (up to ~72%) and PAN (~51%), and also seen in GPA and MPA (Harrison 22e p.2940, p.2943, p.2944).","EGPA (Churg-Strauss) requires asthma, peripheral blood eosinophilia (>1000 cells/uL in >80%), and clinical features of vasculitis; eosinophilic tissue infiltration and extravascular granulomas are characteristic (Harrison 22e p.2943-2944).","Cardiac involvement (myocarditis, pericarditis, coronary vasculitis) is the leading cause of death in EGPA, accounting for ~39% of mortality; echocardiography is advised in all newly diagnosed patients (Harrison 22e p.2943-2944).","PAN does NOT involve pulmonary arteries and does NOT cause glomerulonephritis, granuloma, or significant eosinophilia; venule involvement points away from PAN and toward microscopic polyangiitis (Harrison 22e p.2944).","PAN classically causes microaneurysms of small- and medium-sized renal, hepatic, and visceral arteries; catheter-directed dye arteriography (MR/CT lack resolution) can be diagnostic when accessible tissue is unavailable (Harrison 22e p.2944-2945).","Giant cell arteritis occurs almost exclusively in those over 50; jaw/tongue claudication, scalp tenderness, and headache precede the dreaded complication of ischemic optic neuropathy with sudden blindness - therapy should not be delayed for diagnostic testing (Harrison 22e p.2945).","GCA and polymyalgia rheumatica are considered spectrums of one disease - PMR occurs in 40-50% of GCA, and 10-20% of isolated PMR later develop GCA (Harrison 22e p.2945).","Takayasu arteritis should be strongly suspected in a young woman with diminished/absent peripheral pulses, an inter-arm blood pressure discrepancy, or arterial bruits (Harrison 22e p.2946-2947).","Hemoptysis may be the first sign of alveolar haemorrhage in MPA (~12%); upper-airway disease and pulmonary nodules are atypical for MPA and instead suggest GPA (Harrison 22e p.2942).","GPA carries an increased risk of venous thrombotic events (DVT/PE); maintain a heightened awareness even though routine anticoagulation is not recommended (Harrison 22e p.2940)."],"pathophysiology":"Vasculitis is inflammation of and damage to blood vessel walls, usually compromising the lumen and causing ischaemia of supplied tissues; any size, type, or location of vessel may be affected, and disease may be primary/single-organ or secondary to another illness. Most syndromes are at least partly immune-mediated through three broad mechanisms (Harrison 22e p.2936): (1) Immune-complex formation/deposition (e.g., IgA vasculitis, lupus vasculitis, serum sickness, hepatitis-B-associated vasculitis, hepatitis-C cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis) - resembling serum sickness, complexes deposit in vessel walls, activate complement (notably C5a, chemotactic for neutrophils), and neutrophils infiltrate and release enzymes that damage the wall. (2) ANCA production (GPA, MPA, EGPA) - when neutrophils/monocytes are primed by TNF-alpha, IL-1, or alternative-complement activation, PR3 and MPO translocate to the cell membrane where ANCA can engage them, triggering degranulation, reactive oxygen species, endothelial injury, and release of IL-1/IL-8. (3) Pathogenic T-lymphocyte responses and granuloma formation (GCA, Takayasu, GPA, EGPA) - cytokine (IFN-gamma) activated endothelium expresses HLA class II and interacts with CD4+ T cells, with IL-1/TNF-alpha inducing adhesion molecules (ELAM-1, VCAM-1). The 1992/2012 Chapel Hill Consensus nomenclature underpins the classification of these syndromes by predominant vessel size (Harrison 22e p.2936, p.2942).","additionalDifferentials":["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA) - upper + lower respiratory tract granulomatous vasculitis plus pauci-immune glomerulonephritis; PR3/cANCA in >90% of active disease (Harrison 22e p.2939-2940)","Microscopic polyangiitis (MPA) - pauci-immune small-vessel necrotizing vasculitis with frequent glomerulonephritis and pulmonary capillaritis but no granulomas; MPO/pANCA in ~75% (Harrison 22e p.2942)","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis / Churg-Strauss (EGPA) - asthma, eosinophilia, extravascular granuloma, vasculitis; ANCA (anti-MPO) in ~48% (Harrison 22e p.2943-2944)","Polyarteritis nodosa (PAN) - necrotizing vasculitis of small/medium muscular arteries with microaneurysms, sparing lungs and glomeruli; associated with hepatitis B (and C); consider DADA2 in early-onset disease (Harrison 22e p.2944-2945)","Giant cell (temporal) arteritis with polymyalgia rheumatica - medium/large-vessel arteritis in patients over 50 (Harrison 22e p.2945-2946)","Takayasu arteritis - large-vessel arteritis of the aorta and its branches in young women (Harrison 22e p.2946-2947)","IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein) - small-vessel immune-complex vasculitis with palpable purpura, arthralgia, GI symptoms, glomerulonephritis; IgA/C3 on biopsy (Harrison 22e p.2947-2948)","Cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis - strongly associated with hepatitis C virus infection (Harrison 22e p.2936)","Anti-glomerular basement membrane disease (Goodpasture's syndrome) - other cause of pulmonary-renal syndrome to distinguish from ANCA-associated vasculitis (Harrison 22e p.2941)","Lymphomatoid granulomatosis - EBV-positive angiocentric B-cell proliferation with lung/skin/CNS/kidney involvement that mimics GPA but is not a true vasculitis (Harrison 22e p.2941)"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial (infective) endocarditis - can produce a false-positive pANCA and a vasculitis-like multisystem picture (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Disseminated gonococcal infection (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Pulmonary histoplasmosis (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Coccidioidomycosis (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Syphilis (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Lyme disease (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Rocky Mountain spotted fever (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Whipple's disease (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Bacterial airway infection in cystic fibrosis - associated with a non-anti-MPO pANCA pattern (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis and rhinoscleroma - midline destructive infections that mimic GPA upper-airway disease (Harrison 22e p.2941)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Atheroembolic (cholesterol embolisation) disease - listed as a mimic of vasculitis (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Antiphospholipid syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Atrial myxoma (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Lymphoma / carcinomatosis (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma (nasal type) - EBV-associated cause of midline destructive disease mimicking GPA; never irradiate true GPA airway lesions (Harrison 22e p.2941)","Drug toxicity - cocaine (cocaine-induced midline destructive lesions can show anti-human-neutrophil-elastase ANCA), levamisole (cocaine adulterant; causes cutaneous infarction, granulocytopenia, vasculitis-mimicking serology), amphetamines, ergot alkaloids, methysergide, arsenic (Harrison 22e p.2937, p.2941)","Sarcoidosis (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Anti-GBM disease (Goodpasture's) (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Amyloidosis (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Migraine (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Fibromuscular dysplasia (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Heritable disorders of connective tissue (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Segmental arterial mediolysis (SAM) (Harrison 22e p.2937)","Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2937)","IgG4-related disease - cause of aortitis/periaortitis distinguished histologically from Takayasu (IgG4+ plasma cells, storiform fibrosis, obliterative phlebitis) (Harrison 22e p.2947)"],"additionalInvestigations":["ANCA with antigen specificity - anti-proteinase-3 (cANCA) and anti-myeloperoxidase (pANCA) testing, interpreting in light of pretest probability (Harrison 22e p.2937, p.2941)","Tissue biopsy of a clinically involved organ for definitive diagnosis; in GPA, lung biopsy has the highest yield (necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis), upper-airway biopsy often shows only inflammation, renal biopsy shows pauci-immune GN (Harrison 22e p.2941)","Skin biopsy with immunofluorescence in IgA vasculitis (leukocytoclastic vasculitis with IgA and C3 deposition) (Harrison 22e p.2948)","Catheter-directed dye arteriography for PAN (microaneurysms of renal/hepatic/visceral arteries) - MR/CT angiography lack adequate resolution (Harrison 22e p.2945)","Temporal artery biopsy (3-5 cm segment, serial sections, because involvement is patchy/segmental); may remain positive up to ~14 days after starting glucocorticoids (Harrison 22e p.2946)","Temporal +/- axillary artery ultrasonography for the noncompressible 'halo' sign in GCA (Harrison 22e p.2946)","MR/CT angiography (and investigational PET) to assess large-vessel disease in GCA and to image the whole aorta and branches in Takayasu arteritis (Harrison 22e p.2946, p.2947)","Acute-phase reactants - ESR and/or CRP (note IL-6 inhibitors suppress CRP/ESR, removing them as activity markers in treated GCA) (Harrison 22e p.2946)","Complete blood count - anaemia, leukocytosis, thrombocytosis as inflammatory markers; striking eosinophilia in EGPA (Harrison 22e p.2940, p.2943)","Hepatitis B and C serology in all suspected PAN (and in cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis) (Harrison 22e p.2945)","Echocardiography in all newly diagnosed EGPA because cardiac involvement drives mortality (Harrison 22e p.2944)","Serum complement and IgA levels in IgA vasculitis (complement normal; IgA elevated in about half) (Harrison 22e p.2948)"],"redFlags":["Active glomerulonephritis is always classed as severe disease even with normal renal function (Harrison 22e p.2941)","Alveolar/pulmonary haemorrhage - severe manifestation of GPA/MPA; hemoptysis may be the first symptom of alveolar haemorrhage in MPA (Harrison 22e p.2941, p.2942)","Visual symptoms in a patient over 50 with headache - ischemic optic neuropathy in GCA can cause sudden, irreversible blindness; do not delay treatment for diagnostic workup (Harrison 22e p.2945, p.2946)","Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis with rising creatinine in ANCA-associated vasculitis (Harrison 22e p.2939-2940, p.2942)","Jaw/tongue claudication, scalp tenderness, or a tender/nodular temporal artery - cranial ischaemic features of GCA (Harrison 22e p.2945)","Inter-arm blood pressure discrepancy, absent pulses, or bruits in a young woman - suggests Takayasu large-vessel involvement (Harrison 22e p.2947)","Bowel infarction/perforation and GI bleeding in PAN - leading causes of death (Harrison 22e p.2944-2945)","Cardiac involvement in EGPA - the chief cause of death (Harrison 22e p.2944)","Signs of deep-vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism in GPA given the increased venous thrombotic risk (Harrison 22e p.2940)"],"prognosis":"Untreated GPA was historically fatal within months; with cyclophosphamide-based therapy >90% improve, ~75% achieve complete remission, and 5-year survival exceeds 80%, but 50-70% of remissions later relapse and many accrue irreversible damage (renal insufficiency, hearing loss, subglottic stenosis, saddle-nose deformity, chronic sinus dysfunction) (Harrison 22e p.2941). Treated MPA has a ~74% 5-year survival, with deaths from alveolar haemorrhage or GI/cardiac/renal disease, and relapse in at least ~34% (Harrison 22e p.2942). Untreated EGPA has a ~25% 5-year survival; treated, prognosis is favourable (~72% at 78 months), but myocardial involvement causes ~39% of deaths (Harrison 22e p.2944). Untreated PAN has a poor 5-year survival of 10-20% (deaths from GI and cardiovascular causes); prednisone plus cyclophosphamide markedly improves survival, with relapse in ~10-20% (Harrison 22e p.2944-2945). In GCA acute death is uncommon, but patients face late mortality from aortic aneurysm rupture/dissection (18x more likely to develop thoracic aortic aneurysm than the general population) (Harrison 22e p.2946). Takayasu outcomes vary widely (overall survival >=94% in some North American series; 5-year mortality 0-35% elsewhere), and the disease is usually chronic and relapsing (Harrison 22e p.2947). IgA vasculitis has an excellent prognosis with rare mortality and only 1-5% of children progressing to end-stage renal disease (Harrison 22e p.2948).","pitfalls":["Treating a rise in ANCA titre as a relapse - only ~43% relapse within 1 year of an ANCA increase, so a rising ANCA alone should not prompt reinstitution or escalation of immunosuppression (Harrison 22e p.2941).","Relying on ANCA to diagnose vasculitis in sinus/lung disease without renal involvement, where positive predictive value is only 30-60% and biopsy is needed to exclude infection/malignancy with false-positive ANCA (Harrison 22e p.2941).","Performing 'blind' biopsies of clinically uninvolved organs - yield is very low (Harrison 22e p.2937).","Starting immunosuppression before excluding infectious mimics, especially in a rapidly deteriorating patient (Harrison 22e p.2937).","Irradiating upper-airway lesions assuming GPA when the diagnosis is actually NK/T-cell lymphoma - GPA airway lesions should never be irradiated (Harrison 22e p.2941).","Overlooking cocaine/levamisole-induced midline destruction, which can produce vasculitis-mimicking serology (anti-elastase ANCA) and granulocytopenia (Harrison 22e p.2941).","Using CRP/ESR to monitor disease activity or assess infection in GCA patients on IL-6 inhibitors, which suppress these acute-phase reactants (Harrison 22e p.2946).","Attributing every minor ESR/CRP rise during a GCA prednisone taper to a flare - small increases may not reflect arteritis, and can also be caused by infection (Harrison 22e p.2946).","Mislabelling a necrotizing vasculitis with venule involvement as PAN - venule involvement indicates microscopic polyangiitis, not PAN (Harrison 22e p.2944).","Missing DADA2 (adenosine deaminase 2 deficiency) in early-onset PAN-like vasculitis, which responds to TNF inhibitors rather than standard PAN therapy (Harrison 22e p.2944)."],"severityClassification":"Vasculitides are classified by predominant vessel size per the Chapel Hill Consensus nomenclature (Harrison 22e p.2936, p.2942): LARGE-VESSEL (giant cell/temporal arteritis, Takayasu arteritis); MEDIUM-VESSEL (polyarteritis nodosa, Kawasaki disease); SMALL-VESSEL, which splits into ANCA-associated (GPA, MPA, EGPA) and immune-complex (IgA vasculitis/Henoch-Schonlein, cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis). The chapter also separates PRIMARY syndromes from SECONDARY vasculitis (associated with probable etiology e.g. drug-induced, hepatitis-B/C-related, cancer-associated; or with systemic disease e.g. lupus, rheumatoid, sarcoid vasculitis), and single-organ vasculitis (cutaneous leukocytoclastic angiitis, cutaneous arteritis, primary CNS vasculitis, isolated aortitis) (Harrison 22e p.2936). For management stratification, ANCA-associated vasculitis is divided into SEVERE (life- or organ-threatening; e.g., alveolar haemorrhage, and active glomerulonephritis is severe even with normal renal function) versus NONSEVERE/limited disease - severity depends on both extent and the specific organ involved (e.g., nodular pulmonary disease without respiratory compromise is nonsevere, whereas alveolar haemorrhage is severe) (Harrison 22e p.2941).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2893","Harrison 22e p.2936","Harrison 22e p.2937","Harrison 22e p.2938","Harrison 22e p.2939","Harrison 22e p.2940","Harrison 22e p.2941","Harrison 22e p.2942","Harrison 22e p.2943","Harrison 22e p.2944","Harrison 22e p.2945","Harrison 22e p.2946","Harrison 22e p.2947","Harrison 22e p.2948"],"crossLinks":["glomerulonephritis","IE","temporal_arteritis","sle_flare"]},"vasovagal_syncope":{"name":"Vasovagal / orthostatic syncope","system":"Neurology / Cardiology","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e, Chapter 23 (Syncope), pp.155-162","clinicalPearls":["Syncope is a transient, self-limited loss of consciousness from acute global cerebral hypoperfusion, with rapid onset, brief duration, and spontaneous complete recovery; reorientation is almost immediate after a syncopal event.","Causes fall into three categories: neurally mediated (reflex/vasovagal) syncope, orthostatic hypotension, and cardiac syncope; clinical features, mechanisms, and prognosis differ markedly among them.","Neurally mediated syncope is the most common cause overall in population-based studies and accounts for the vast majority of syncope in the young (peak incidence ~age 15); incidence is higher in women, and a first-degree family history is common in young patients.","Vasovagal syncope (the common faint) is provoked by intense emotion, pain, the sight of blood, venipuncture, and/or orthostatic stress; situational reflex syncopes have specific localized triggers (cough, micturition, swallow, defecation, carotid sinus pressure).","Premonitory (prodromal) features of autonomic activation in neurally mediated syncope include diaphoresis, pallor, palpitations, nausea, lightheadedness, hyperventilation, and yawning.","A functioning autonomic nervous system is required to develop neurally mediated syncope, in contrast to syncope from autonomic failure (orthostatic hypotension).","Neurally mediated syncope can be subdivided by efferent pathway: vasodepressor (sympathetic vasoconstrictor failure), cardioinhibitory (bradycardia/asystole from vagal outflow), or mixed.","Orthostatic hypotension is defined as a fall in systolic BP of at least 20 mmHg or diastolic BP of at least 10 mmHg within 3 min of standing or head-up tilt; supine and standing BP/HR at 3 min should be measured in every initial evaluation.","Initial clinical assessment plus ECG identifies a cause in about 50% of patients and stratifies cardiac mortality risk.","A clinically meaningful syncope usually corresponds to a fall in systolic BP to roughly 50 mmHg or lower; cessation of cerebral blood flow for 6-8 s produces loss of consciousness."],"pathophysiology":"Standing pools 500-1000 mL of blood in the lower extremities, buttocks, and splanchnic circulation, reducing venous return, ventricular filling, cardiac output, and blood pressure. Normally baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch trigger a compensatory reflex that raises sympathetic outflow and lowers vagal activity, increasing peripheral resistance, venous return, and cardiac output. In neurally mediated (reflex/vasovagal) syncope this reflex transiently fails: there is a paroxysmal shift in autonomic efferent activity with increased parasympathetic outflow plus sympathoinhibition, producing bradycardia, vasodilation/loss of vasoconstrictor tone (vasodepressor response), and reduced cardiac output. The resulting fall in systemic blood pressure drops cerebral blood flow below the limits of autoregulation, causing transient global cerebral hypoperfusion and loss of consciousness. Multiple afferent triggers (orthostatic stress, carotid sinus, gastrointestinal tract, bladder) converge on the central autonomic network in the medulla. Hyperventilation-induced hypocarbia (cerebral vasoconstriction) and raised intrathoracic pressure (impaired venous return) contribute to many situational reflex syncopes. By contrast, orthostatic hypotension reflects chronic impairment of these homeostatic reflexes (autonomic failure), and cardiac syncope results from arrhythmias or structural disease reducing cardiac output.","additionalDifferentials":["Generalized and partial/partial-complex seizures (with secondary generalization, usually preceded by an aura such as an unpleasant smell, fear, anxiety, or abdominal/visceral sensation).","Autonomic seizures (autonomic epilepsy), whose cardiovascular, GI, pulmonary, urogenital, pupillary, and cutaneous manifestations mimic syncopal prodrome; accompanying non-autonomic auras help distinguish them.","Hypoglycemia, typically in insulin-treated type 1 or type 2 diabetes (tremor, palpitations, anxiety, diaphoresis, hunger, paresthesias; hunger is not a typical syncope prodrome).","Cataplexy (abrupt loss of muscle tone triggered by strong emotion such as laughter or anger; consciousness is preserved, lasts 30 s to 2 min, no prodrome; occurs in 60-75% of narcolepsy).","Vertebrobasilar ischemia and hypoxemia as other causes of transient loss of consciousness.","Falls due to vestibular dysfunction, cerebellar disease, extrapyramidal/gait disorders, and falls with head trauma (especially difficult in patients with dementia and repeated falls).","Psychogenic apparent loss of consciousness from psychiatric disorders (generalized anxiety, panic disorder, major depression, somatization), suggested by frequent fainting without prodrome and rare injury despite numerous falls, with no significant hemodynamic change."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG when arrhythmia or underlying cardiac disease is suspected; look for brady-/tachyarrhythmias, AV block, acute or old myocardial ischemia/infarction, long QT, and bundle branch block.","Supine and standing (3 min) blood pressure and heart rate to detect orthostatic hypotension.","Tilt-table testing (autonomic testing) to distinguish autonomic-failure orthostatic hypotension from the hypotensive-bradycardic response of neurally mediated syncope, and to identify immediate or delayed orthostatic hypotension; may also reproduce psychogenic syncope when symptoms occur without hemodynamic change.","Autonomic function testing in specialized centers: heart rate variability to deep breathing and Valsalva (parasympathetic), thermoregulatory sweat test and quantitative sudomotor axon reflex test (sympathetic cholinergic), and BP response to Valsalva/tilt (sympathetic adrenergic).","Carotid sinus massage (under continuous ECG and BP monitoring) for suspected carotid sinus syncope and in patients >40 years with recurrent unexplained syncope; avoid with carotid bruits, plaque, or stenosis.","Inpatient ECG monitoring for high arrhythmia risk; ambulatory Holter monitoring for frequent (near-daily) episodes; external or implantable loop recorders for less frequent episodes (monitor for at least twice the interspell interval).","Echocardiography for patients with cardiac history or abnormal exam/ECG (may reveal aortic stenosis, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, cardiac tumors, aortic dissection, pericardial tamponade; also stratifies sudden-death risk by ejection fraction).","Treadmill exercise testing with ECG and BP monitoring for syncope during or shortly after exercise.","Invasive electrophysiologic studies (low sensitivity/specificity, rarely used) only when pretest probability is high in structural heart disease with ECG abnormalities.","Baseline blood tests are rarely helpful and should be reserved for suspected specific disorders (e.g., myocardial infarction, anemia, secondary autonomic failure); psychiatric screening for recurrent unexplained syncope."],"redFlags":["Syncope during exertion, or while seated or supine.","Sudden onset of palpitations immediately followed by syncope, or palpitations at the time of syncope.","Chest pain suggesting coronary ischemia, or features of congestive heart failure.","Moderate or severe valvular or structural cardiac disease, or known severe coronary artery disease.","ECG features: ischemia, history of ventricular arrhythmias, prolonged QT (>500 ms), repetitive SA block or sinus pauses, persistent sinus bradycardia, bi-/trifascicular block or QRS >=120 ms, atrial fibrillation, nonsustained VT, preexcitation, or a Brugada pattern.","Family history of sudden death.","New chest discomfort, abdominal pain, shortness of breath, or headache associated with the episode.","Exam: unexplained systolic BP <90 mmHg, signs of GI hemorrhage, persistent bradycardia <40 beats/min, or an undiagnosed systolic murmur."],"prognosis":"Syncope of noncardiac and unexplained origin in younger individuals carries an excellent prognosis with unaffected life expectancy. In contrast, cardiac syncope (structural heart disease or primary arrhythmia) is associated with an increased risk of sudden cardiac death and overall mortality, and mortality is also increased with orthostatic hypotension related to age and comorbidities. Hospitalization and mortality risk are higher in older adults.","pitfalls":["Convulsive syncope mimics seizure: myoclonic and other movements occur in up to ~90% of syncopal episodes, but syncope-related jerks are typically arrhythmic, multifocal/generalized, and brief (<30 s), with mild flexor/extensor posturing; EEG seizure discharges are absent during syncope.","Distinguishing features from seizure: seizure loss of consciousness usually lasts >5 min with prolonged postictal drowsiness/disorientation, whereas reorientation after syncope is almost immediate; muscle aches are longer and more severe after seizures; seizures are rarely provoked by emotion or pain.","Urinary incontinence can occur in both syncope and seizure and does not distinguish them; fecal incontinence is very rare in syncope and postictal confusion is rare after syncope.","During neurally mediated syncope the eyes typically remain open and deviate upward with dilated pupils, and grunting/moaning/stertorous breathing may occur, which can be mistaken for a seizure.","Orthostatic hypotension syncope is usually preceded by warning symptoms but may occur suddenly, mimicking a seizure or cardiac cause.","Supine hypertension is common (>50%) in autonomic-failure orthostatic hypotension; treating one condition can unmask or worsen the other.","Initial orthostatic hypotension (BP fall within 15 s, resolving by 45 s) and delayed orthostatic hypotension (beyond 3 min) can be missed by a single 3-min standing measurement; initial orthostatic hypotension does not represent autonomic failure."],"severityClassification":"Harrison categorizes syncope by mechanism rather than a graded severity score: (1) neurally mediated (reflex/vasovagal) syncope, (2) orthostatic hypotension, and (3) cardiac syncope. For risk stratification, Table 23-1 lists high-risk features indicating hospitalization or intensive evaluation (e.g., ischemic chest pain, heart failure, significant valvular/structural disease, ECG ischemia, ventricular arrhythmia history, QT >500 ms, SA block/sinus pauses, persistent sinus bradycardia, bi-/trifascicular block or QRS >=120 ms, atrial fibrillation, nonsustained VT, family history of sudden death, preexcitation, Brugada pattern, palpitations at syncope, syncope at rest or during exercise). Neurally mediated efferent subtypes are vasodepressor, cardioinhibitory, and mixed.","references":["Harrison 22e p.155","Harrison 22e p.156","Harrison 22e p.157","Harrison 22e p.158","Harrison 22e p.159","Harrison 22e p.160","Harrison 22e p.161","Harrison 22e p.162"],"crossLinks":["seizure_epilepsy","aortic_stenosis","atrial_fib","tia"]},"wernicke":{"name":"Wernicke encephalopathy","system":"Neurology / Nutrition","class":"non_infective","source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e (2025)","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2348-2350 (Ch.318 Nervous System Disorders in Critical Care); p.2607 (Ch.344 Vitamin/Trace Mineral Deficiency); p.235-236 (Ch. Disorders of the Eye); p.3526 (Ch.464 Alcohol/neurology of amnesia); p.3719 (Ch. Alcohol Use Disorders)","clinicalPearls":["The classic triad (ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, global confusion) is present in only about one-third of acute cases, so a complete triad should not be required to make the diagnosis.","Most patients are profoundly disoriented, apathetic/indifferent, and inattentive rather than agitated; agitated delirium, when present, is usually from coexisting alcohol withdrawal.","Thiamine deficiency causing Wernicke is not confined to alcohol use; non-alcoholic risk groups include hyperemesis, starvation/malnutrition, renal dialysis, cancer, HIV/AIDS, gastric/bariatric surgery, and chronic diuretic therapy (increased urinary thiamine loss).","Certain drugs (e.g., metformin, verapamil) can inhibit intestinal thiamine transporters (ThTR-2) and add to deficiency risk.","Ocular signs include horizontal nystagmus on lateral gaze, bilateral lateral rectus (abducens) palsy, conjugate gaze palsies, and rarely ptosis; pupils are usually spared but may become miotic in advanced disease.","Gait ataxia is multifactorial, arising from a combination of peripheral polyneuropathy, cerebellar involvement, and vestibular paresis.","Treat on clinical suspicion as a medical emergency: it is common, preventable, and reversible if thiamine is given early.","Korsakoff amnestic state may only become apparent as the acute confusion recedes, characterized by recent-memory/learning impairment, confabulation, and disordered temporal sequencing.","The amnestic deficit correlates with lesions of the dorsomedial (dorsal medial) thalamic nuclei."],"pathophysiology":"Wernicke disease results from thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency. Thiamine is a cofactor for several enzymes including transketolase, pyruvate dehydrogenase, and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase. Deficiency causes a diffuse fall in cerebral glucose utilization and mitochondrial damage; impaired alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase leads to glutamate accumulation, which together with the energy deficit may produce excitotoxic neuronal injury. Lesions are periventricular, surrounding the third ventricle, aqueduct, and fourth ventricle, with predilection for midline structures (dorsomedial thalamus, mammillary bodies, midline cerebellum, periaqueductal gray, and trochlear/abducens nuclei). Acute cases may show petechial hemorrhages; chronic cases show mammillary body atrophy.","additionalDifferentials":["Supranuclear gaze disorder mimicking combined abducens and oculomotor nerve palsy (Wernicke can itself cause this pattern)","Other metabolic encephalopathies (hepatic, hypercarbic, hyper-/hyponatremic, hyper-/hypoglycemic)","Sepsis-associated encephalopathy","Osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis)","Marchiafava-Bignami disease (corpus callosum degeneration in chronic alcoholism)","Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration"],"infectionMimics":["Encephalitis (including herpes simplex encephalitis as a cause of an amnestic state)","Whipple's disease (cause of supranuclear gaze palsy)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Autoimmune limbic encephalitis (e.g., AMPAR, LGI1 antibodies) causing amnesia","Paraneoplastic encephalitis (e.g., Ma antibodies)","Thalamic, medial temporal, or posterior corpus callosum tumor causing amnesia","Anterior/posterior cerebral artery territory infarction causing amnesia","Stroke (infarct/hemorrhage), multiple sclerosis, vasculitis, or tumor causing supranuclear gaze palsy"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI may show changes including abnormal enhancement of the mammillary bodies (coronal T1 postcontrast), typical of acute Wernicke","Erythrocyte transketolase activity assay measured before and after adding thiamine pyrophosphate; >25% stimulation (activity coefficient >=1.25) is abnormal","Serum/blood thiamine or its phosphorylated esters by high-performance liquid chromatography","Serum magnesium (required cofactor for thiamine activation)"],"redFlags":["Untreated disease can progress to stupor, coma, and death","Giving glucose (parenteral dextrose) before thiamine can precipitate Wernicke in a previously unaffected at-risk patient or rapidly worsen an early form","New amnestic state emerging as confusion clears signals evolution to Korsakoff syndrome"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on early thiamine administration. Ocular palsies improve within hours of thiamine; horizontal nystagmus may persist. Ataxia improves more slowly than ocular signs, and roughly half of patients recover incompletely, left with a slow, wide-based, shuffling gait and inability to tandem walk. Apathy, drowsiness, and confusion resolve gradually. Korsakoff psychosis is frequently persistent, with permanent gaps in memory, confabulation, and disordered temporal sequencing; prolonged untreated deficiency can cause an irreversible profound amnestic syndrome or death. Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is underdiagnosed despite a typical history and picture.","pitfalls":["Waiting for the full classic triad before treating, when only about one-third of patients show all three features.","Attributing the quiet, indifferent, inattentive confusional state to depression, sedation, or simple intoxication and missing the diagnosis.","Assuming Wernicke occurs only in alcoholics and overlooking it in hyperemesis, malnutrition, dialysis, bariatric surgery, cancer, or HIV patients.","Administering glucose-containing fluids before thiamine in an at-risk patient, which can trigger or worsen Wernicke.","Thiamine deficiency being overlooked in the setting of head injury (and possibly contributing to motor vehicle accidents)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2348","Harrison 22e p.2349","Harrison 22e p.2350","Harrison 22e p.2607","Harrison 22e p.235","Harrison 22e p.3526","Harrison 22e p.3719"],"crossLinks":["metabolic_enceph","hepatic_enceph","hypoglycemia"]},"ACHALASIA":{"name":"Achalasia","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["achalasia cardia","esophageal achalasia","cardiospasm","failure of LES relaxation","esophageal aperistalsis","megaesophagus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Progressive dysphagia to both solids AND liquids from the outset is the classic clue distinguishing achalasia from a mechanical stricture or malignancy, where solid-food dysphagia usually predominates and progresses later to liquids.","Regurgitation of undigested food (especially at night, with risk of aspiration) and slow, gradual weight loss are common accompanying features.","Retrosternal chest pain/discomfort with meals occurs in a subset of patients, more often early in the disease course.","Patients often learn compensatory maneuvers — eating slowly, holding arms overhead, or performing repeated swallows/Valsalva — to help push food through the non-relaxing lower esophageal sphincter.","Barium esophagram classically shows a dilated esophagus tapering to a smooth 'bird's beak' narrowing at the gastroesophageal junction.","Manometry is the gold standard: it shows absent/incomplete lower esophageal sphincter relaxation together with loss of normal peristalsis in the esophageal body.","New-onset 'achalasia-like' symptoms in an older adult, especially with rapid weight loss, should prompt evaluation for pseudoachalasia from a malignancy at the gastroesophageal junction.","Chagas disease should be considered in patients from or with travel history to endemic areas of Latin America presenting with an achalasia-like picture."],"pathophysiology":"Achalasia results from degeneration of inhibitory neurons in the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus of the esophagus, leading to loss of coordinated peristalsis in the esophageal body and failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax appropriately with swallowing. The cause is usually idiopathic, though an autoimmune or postviral inflammatory process targeting these neurons is suspected in many cases. Similar clinical pictures can arise secondarily from infiltrative or destructive processes affecting the myenteric plexus, such as Chagas disease or tumor invasion at the gastroesophageal junction (pseudoachalasia).","additionalDifferentials":["Distal esophageal spasm and other esophageal motility disorders","Gastroesophageal reflux disease with peptic stricture","Esophageal or gastric cardia malignancy causing pseudoachalasia","Chagas disease (Trypanosoma cruzi infection)","Eosinophilic esophagitis","Scleroderma-related esophageal dysmotility"],"infectionMimics":["Chagas disease (Trypanosoma cruzi) causing destruction of myenteric plexus with achalasia-like esophageal dysfunction"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Peptic or malignant esophageal stricture","Esophageal or gastroesophageal junction carcinoma (pseudoachalasia)","Diffuse esophageal spasm","Scleroderma esophagus","Eosinophilic esophagitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Barium esophagram — shows esophageal dilation with distal 'bird's beak' tapering and poor emptying","High-resolution esophageal manometry — confirms diagnosis via absent peristalsis and impaired LES relaxation, and subtypes achalasia (I–III)","Upper endoscopy — mandatory to exclude a mechanical or malignant cause (pseudoachalasia) at or near the gastroesophageal junction","Endoscopic ultrasound or CT chest/abdomen — used selectively when pseudoachalasia from tumor infiltration is suspected","Serology for Trypanosoma cruzi — considered in patients from endemic regions to exclude Chagas disease","Timed barium swallow — assesses esophageal emptying and is useful for monitoring treatment response","Chest imaging — may incidentally reveal a dilated, air/fluid-filled esophagus or absent gastric air bubble"],"redFlags":["Rapid symptom onset or significant weight loss in an older patient, raising concern for pseudoachalasia due to malignancy","Recurrent aspiration pneumonia from nocturnal regurgitation","Severe odynophagia or evidence of esophageal perforation","Marked, progressive esophageal dilation (megaesophagus) with risk of retained food and aspiration","New or worsening symptoms despite treatment, warranting re-evaluation for an underlying malignant cause"],"prognosis":"With appropriate treatment (pneumatic dilation, surgical myotomy, or peroral endoscopic myotomy), most patients achieve durable symptomatic improvement, though the underlying esophageal aperistalsis is not reversed and long-term surveillance is often needed. Untreated or longstanding disease carries risk of progressive esophageal dilation, aspiration, malnutrition, and a modestly increased long-term risk of esophageal squamous cell carcinoma.","pitfalls":["Attributing dysphagia to reflux disease and delaying manometry, since achalasia can be misdiagnosed as GERD for years","Failing to perform endoscopy to exclude a malignant or infiltrative cause before treating for idiopathic achalasia","Overlooking Chagas disease in patients with relevant epidemiologic exposure","Assuming a normal-caliber esophagus on barium study excludes achalasia; early disease may show minimal dilation despite abnormal manometry"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Esophageal Motility Disorders (Dysphagia/Diseases of the Esophagus section)"],"crossLinks":[]},"ACUTE_LIVER_FAILURE":{"name":"Acute liver failure","system":"Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute liver failure","fulminant hepatic","fulminant hepatic failure","ALF","acute hepatic failure","acute liver injury with encephalopathy","hyperacute liver failure","subacute liver failure"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by acute severe liver injury with coagulopathy (INR generally above 1.5) and any degree of encephalopathy in a patient without pre-existing chronic liver disease, developing within about 26 weeks of illness onset","Speed of onset matters clinically: hyperacute presentations (encephalopathy within roughly a week or two of jaundice) are typically from acetaminophen toxicity or viral hepatitis and, despite dramatic cerebral edema risk, often carry better spontaneous recovery odds than slower subacute forms","Acetaminophen overdose and drug-induced liver injury are the leading causes in resource-rich settings, while viral hepatitis (especially hepatitis B and E) dominates in many other regions","Cerebral edema and raised intracranial pressure are the most feared early complications and a major cause of death, particularly in hyperacute presentations","Coagulopathy in ALF reflects a rebalanced but fragile hemostatic state rather than pure bleeding tendency; INR is used mainly for prognosis and monitoring, not as a routine transfusion trigger","Early transfer to a liver transplant center should be considered as soon as ALF is recognized, since deterioration can be rapid and unpredictable","Look actively for a treatable cause (acetaminophen, autoimmune hepatitis, Wilson disease, HELLP/acute fatty liver of pregnancy, vascular causes) since specific therapy can be life-saving","Infection and sepsis are common complications during the course of ALF and should be surveilled for proactively rather than only when clinically obvious"],"pathophysiology":"Massive hepatocyte necrosis or dysfunction outstrips the liver's regenerative capacity, causing simultaneous failure of synthetic function (coagulation factors, albumin), detoxification (ammonia, other neurotoxins), and metabolic homeostasis. Accumulating ammonia and inflammatory mediators drive astrocyte swelling and cerebral edema, producing hepatic encephalopathy and raised intracranial pressure. The systemic inflammatory response and impaired hepatic clearance of pathogens also predispose to circulatory dysfunction, renal impairment, and secondary infection, contributing to a multiorgan failure picture.","additionalDifferentials":["Decompensated chronic liver disease (cirrhosis with acute-on-chronic liver failure)","Severe acute hepatitis without encephalopathy (acute liver injury short of failure)","Ischemic hepatitis (shock liver)","Acute Budd-Chiari syndrome and other vascular liver outflow obstruction","Acute fatty liver of pregnancy / HELLP syndrome","Malignant infiltration of the liver causing acute hepatic dysfunction"],"infectionMimics":["Viral hepatitis A, B, D (with B), and E","Herpes simplex virus hepatitis","Cytomegalovirus hepatitis","Leptospirosis with hepatic involvement","Severe sepsis with secondary hepatic dysfunction"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acetaminophen (paracetamol) toxicity","Idiosyncratic drug-induced liver injury (including certain antituberculous and antiepileptic agents, and herbal/dietary supplements)","Autoimmune hepatitis presenting acutely","Wilson disease presenting with acute decompensation","Acute fatty liver of pregnancy and HELLP syndrome","Mushroom (Amanita) poisoning and other hepatotoxins","Acute Budd-Chiari syndrome / ischemic hepatopathy","Malignant hepatic infiltration (lymphoma, metastatic disease)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Prothrombin time/INR — core diagnostic criterion and key prognostic marker","Arterial ammonia level — correlates with risk of cerebral edema and severe encephalopathy","Acetaminophen level and detailed drug/toxin/supplement history — identifies the most common treatable etiology","Viral hepatitis serologies (A, B, C, D, E) and, where relevant, HSV/CMV testing — identifies infectious causes","Autoimmune markers (ANA, smooth muscle antibody, immunoglobulins) and ceruloplasmin/copper studies — screens for autoimmune hepatitis and Wilson disease","Doppler ultrasound of hepatic and portal vasculature — evaluates for vascular outflow obstruction","Arterial blood gas, lactate, and renal function — assess systemic perfusion and evolving multiorgan dysfunction","Blood and other cultures with a low threshold for infection screening — ALF patients are prone to occult sepsis"],"redFlags":["Any new alteration in mental status or asterixis in a patient with acute severe liver injury — signals encephalopathy and true liver failure","Rapidly rising INR with jaundice — suggests impending or established failure requiring urgent transplant center contact","Signs of raised intracranial pressure (hypertension with bradycardia, pupillary changes, posturing) — medical emergency","Hemodynamic instability, oliguria, or lactic acidosis — herald multiorgan involvement","Grade III–IV encephalopathy — generally warrants intensive care and consideration of airway protection","Any suspicion of acetaminophen ingestion, even with a vague or denied history — treatable and time-sensitive"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on etiology and speed of onset: hyperacute presentations from acetaminophen or hepatitis A/E often have a better chance of spontaneous recovery than subacute presentations, which have lower native-liver survival. Emergency liver transplantation substantially improves survival in appropriately selected patients who do not recover spontaneously.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal or mildly elevated bilirubin excludes liver failure — hyperacute acetaminophen-related ALF can present with striking coagulopathy and encephalopathy before marked jaundice develops","Using INR alone to guide prophylactic correction with plasma products — this does not reflect true bleeding risk in the rebalanced coagulopathy of ALF and can complicate assessment of hepatic synthetic recovery","Delaying transfer to a transplant-capable center while pursuing additional outpatient-type workup — deterioration can be abrupt","Overlooking a treatable etiology (Wilson disease, autoimmune hepatitis, pregnancy-related liver disease) because acetaminophen or viral hepatitis seems statistically likely"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Acute Liver Failure"],"crossLinks":["AUTOIMMUNE_HEPATITIS"]},"ADULT_ONSET_STILL":{"name":"Adult-onset Still disease","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["adult onset still","AOSD","still disease","adult still's disease","systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis adult equivalent"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic triad is quotidian (once- or twice-daily) high spiking fever, evanescent salmon-pink rash that appears with fever spikes, and arthritis/arthralgia","Rash is typically truncal and on proximal limbs, non-pruritic, and often elicited or worsened by scratching (Koebner-like phenomenon)","Sore throat and cervical lymphadenopathy are common early features and can mimic infection","Markedly elevated ferritin, often more than five times normal, is a strong clue but not specific","Rheumatoid factor and antinuclear antibody are typically negative, helping distinguish AOSD from other connective tissue diseases","Neutrophilic leukocytosis with left shift and elevated inflammatory markers accompany the fever spikes","Diagnosis is one of exclusion after infections, malignancy, and other rheumatologic diseases are ruled out","Watch for macrophage activation syndrome, a life-threatening complication marked by falling counts and rapidly rising ferritin"],"pathophysiology":"AOSD is an autoinflammatory disorder driven by innate immune activation rather than autoantibody-mediated disease, with overproduction of proinflammatory cytokines including interleukin-1, interleukin-6, interleukin-18, and tumor necrosis factor. This cytokine storm produces the systemic febrile illness, marked acute-phase response, and hyperferritinemia seen clinically. The absence of a clear autoantibody or defined trigger, along with strong similarity to systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis, supports its classification as an autoinflammatory rather than autoimmune condition.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Lymphoma and other malignancy","Viral or bacterial systemic infection","Reactive arthritis","Vasculitis (e.g., polyarteritis nodosa)","Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms"],"infectionMimics":["Viral exanthems (parvovirus B19, rubella, hepatitis)","Infective endocarditis","Occult bacterial sepsis","Tuberculosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Lymphoma","Kikuchi disease","Drug hypersensitivity reaction","Familial Mediterranean fever and other periodic fever syndromes"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ferritin and glycosylated ferritin fraction — marked elevation with low glycosylated fraction supports diagnosis","Complete blood count with differential — neutrophilic leukocytosis typical; cytopenias raise concern for macrophage activation syndrome","Liver function tests — transaminitis is common","Blood cultures and viral serologies — to exclude infectious mimics before immunosuppression","ANA and rheumatoid factor — expected negative, helps exclude other connective tissue disease","Imaging or lymph node biopsy — to exclude lymphoma when lymphadenopathy is prominent","Joint imaging — assess for erosive arthritis, notably of the wrist, in chronic disease","Triglycerides, fibrinogen, soluble CD25/IL-2 receptor — screen for macrophage activation syndrome if clinical deterioration occurs"],"redFlags":["Cytopenias, coagulopathy, or precipitous rise in ferritin suggesting macrophage activation syndrome","Hepatic failure or disseminated intravascular coagulation","Cardiac tamponade from serositis","Adult respiratory distress syndrome from pulmonary involvement","Persistent fever despite broad antimicrobial coverage without an identified infectious source"],"prognosis":"Course is variable: some patients have a self-limited monophasic illness, others a chronic intermittent pattern, and a subset develop chronic destructive polyarthritis. Most patients respond well to anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive therapy, though macrophage activation syndrome significantly increases morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Treating as fever of unknown origin with prolonged empiric antibiotics before considering an autoinflammatory cause","Missing the diagnosis because the evanescent rash is absent at the time of examination","Anchoring on markedly elevated ferritin as diagnostic without excluding lymphoma and macrophage activation syndrome","Delaying immunosuppressive therapy while pursuing an exhaustive infectious workup in a clinically deteriorating patient"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Rheumatoid Arthritis and related autoinflammatory/systemic rheumatic disease section covering Adult-onset Still disease"],"crossLinks":["systemic_lupus_erythematosus"]},"AUTOIMMUNE_ENCEPHALITIS":{"name":"Autoimmune encephalitis","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["autoimmune encephalitis","anti-NMDA","limbic encephalitis","anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis","NMDAR encephalitis","paraneoplastic encephalitis","autoimmune limbic encephalitis","anti-LGI1 encephalitis","voltage-gated potassium channel complex encephalitis","ovarian teratoma encephalitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Subacute (days to a few weeks) behavioral change, memory decline, psychiatric symptoms, seizures, or movement disorder should prompt consideration of autoimmune encephalitis, especially in young adults or children.","Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis classically evolves through a viral-like prodrome, then psychiatric/behavioral disturbance, followed by seizures, movement disorder (orofacial dyskinesias), autonomic instability, and decreased consciousness.","Faciobrachial dystonic seizures are a highly characteristic, near-pathognomonic sign of anti-LGI1 encephalitis and often precede overt cognitive decline.","Always search for an underlying tumor (ovarian teratoma in anti-NMDAR disease, thymoma, small-cell lung cancer) since paraneoplastic triggers are common and tumor removal aids recovery.","MRI can be normal or show only subtle mesial temporal T2/FLAIR signal change; a normal scan does not exclude the diagnosis.","CSF often shows lymphocytic pleocytosis and oligoclonal bands; antibody testing should be sent on both serum and CSF since CSF sensitivity is higher for some antibodies (e.g., NMDAR).","Response to immunotherapy can be slow (weeks to months), and early aggressive treatment plus tumor removal improves long-term outcome.","Relapses occur in a meaningful proportion of patients, particularly with anti-NMDAR encephalitis, warranting longitudinal follow-up."],"pathophysiology":"Autoimmune encephalitis results from antibody-mediated or T-cell-mediated attack on neuronal surface or intracellular antigens, producing limbic or diffuse cortical dysfunction. Surface-antigen antibodies (e.g., against the NMDA receptor, LGI1, CASPR2, GABA-B receptor) directly disrupt synaptic receptor function and are often reversible with immunotherapy, whereas antibodies against intracellular (onconeural) antigens reflect cytotoxic T-cell-mediated neuronal injury and are less responsive to treatment. A subset is paraneoplastic, triggered by tumor expression of neuronal antigens (e.g., ovarian teratoma expressing NMDA receptors), while others arise post-infection (e.g., following herpes simplex encephalitis) or without any identifiable trigger.","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex virus encephalitis","Other viral or infectious encephalitides","Primary psychiatric disorder (new-onset psychosis, catatonia)","Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease and other rapidly progressive dementias","Toxic-metabolic encephalopathy","Non-convulsive status epilepticus of other etiology"],"infectionMimics":["Herpes simplex encephalitis","Enteroviral or arboviral encephalitis","Bacterial meningoencephalitis","Neurosyphilis","HIV-associated encephalopathy"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Primary psychiatric illness (schizophreniform psychosis, catatonia, bipolar disorder)","Rapidly progressive dementia (Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease)","Toxic-metabolic encephalopathy (drug intoxication, hepatic/uremic encephalopathy)","Hashimoto (steroid-responsive) encephalopathy","Central nervous system vasculitis","Non-convulsive status epilepticus from other causes"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and CSF autoimmune/paraneoplastic antibody panel (NMDAR, LGI1, CASPR2, GABA-B, AMPA, and onconeural antibodies) — confirms specific antibody-mediated syndrome","MRI brain with contrast — assesses for mesial temporal/limbic T2-FLAIR signal change and excludes structural or infectious mimics","CSF analysis (cell count, protein, oligoclonal bands, HSV PCR) — distinguishes autoimmune from infectious causes and supports diagnosis","EEG — detects epileptiform activity, extreme delta brush pattern (suggestive of anti-NMDAR encephalitis), or non-convulsive status epilepticus","Whole-body imaging (CT/MRI chest-abdomen-pelvis, pelvic ultrasound/MRI in women, testicular ultrasound in men) — screens for occult tumor, especially ovarian teratoma or thymoma","FDG-PET brain — may show characteristic regional hyper- or hypometabolism when MRI is unrevealing","Thyroid function and thyroid antibodies — evaluates for Hashimoto encephalopathy as a mimic","Tumor markers/gynecologic evaluation as guided by antibody subtype — directs targeted tumor search"],"redFlags":["Rapid decline in consciousness, autonomic instability (labile blood pressure, arrhythmia, central hypoventilation) requiring ICU-level monitoring","Refractory or super-refractory seizures/status epilepticus","Faciobrachial dystonic seizures with rapidly progressive cognitive decline (LGI1 encephalitis)","New psychiatric presentation in a young woman with subsequent neurologic signs — evaluate urgently for ovarian teratoma and anti-NMDAR encephalitis","Failure to improve or worsening despite first-line immunotherapy — consider escalation and repeat tumor search","Signs suggesting an alternative treatable infectious cause (fever, meningismus) that must be excluded/treated empirically first"],"prognosis":"Many patients, particularly those with anti-NMDAR or anti-LGI1 encephalitis, achieve substantial or full recovery with prompt immunotherapy and tumor removal when applicable, though recovery may take many months and some residual cognitive or behavioral deficits can persist. Relapses occur in a meaningful minority, and outcomes are generally worse with delayed treatment, intracellular (onconeural) antibody syndromes, or need for prolonged intensive care.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing early psychiatric or behavioral presentations as a primary psychiatric disorder, delaying immunotherapy","Excluding the diagnosis because MRI or initial antibody testing is negative — antibody testing can be falsely negative early, and CSF testing may be more sensitive than serum","Failing to empirically treat for herpes simplex encephalitis while autoimmune work-up is pending","Overlooking the need for repeated or interval tumor screening, since a causative tumor may not be apparent at initial presentation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Autoimmune and Paraneoplastic Encephalitis (Nervous System Disorders section)"],"crossLinks":["STATUS_EPILEPTICUS"]},"AUTOIMMUNE_HEPATITIS":{"name":"Autoimmune hepatitis","system":"Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["autoimmune hepatitis","AIH","lupoid hepatitis","chronic active hepatitis (autoimmune)","autoimmune chronic hepatitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Consider AIH in any patient with unexplained acute or chronic hepatocellular injury, especially young to middle-aged women, but it can occur at any age and in either sex.","Presentation is highly variable, from asymptomatic transaminase elevation to fulminant acute liver failure or established cirrhosis at diagnosis.","Marked hypergammaglobulinemia (especially elevated IgG) out of proportion to the degree of liver injury is a classic clue.","Two broad serologic patterns exist: type 1 (ANA and/or anti-smooth-muscle antibody positive) and type 2 (anti-LKM1 positive, more common in children).","Concurrent autoimmune conditions (autoimmune thyroiditis, type 1 diabetes, celiac disease, ulcerative colitis) support the diagnosis.","Liver biopsy showing interface hepatitis with a dense plasma cell infiltrate is characteristic and helps confirm diagnosis and grade severity.","A dramatic biochemical response to immunosuppressive therapy is itself supportive of the diagnosis and is used as a diagnostic criterion when the picture is ambiguous.","Overlap syndromes with primary biliary cholangitis or primary sclerosing cholangitis occur and should be considered when cholestatic features coexist."],"pathophysiology":"AIH results from a breakdown in immune self-tolerance leading to T-cell-mediated attack on hepatocytes in a genetically susceptible host, often triggered by an environmental factor such as a viral infection or drug exposure. Autoantibody production (ANA, anti-smooth-muscle antibody, or anti-LKM1) accompanies the T-cell-driven injury but is thought to be a marker rather than the primary mediator of hepatocyte damage. Chronic interface hepatitis with ongoing necroinflammation can progress to fibrosis and cirrhosis if untreated.","additionalDifferentials":["Drug-induced liver injury","Viral hepatitis (acute or chronic hepatitis B or C)","Wilson disease","Primary biliary cholangitis","Primary sclerosing cholangitis","Nonalcoholic steatohepatitis"],"infectionMimics":["Acute viral hepatitis A, B, C, or E","Epstein-Barr virus or cytomegalovirus hepatitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Drug-induced or toxin-induced hepatitis","Wilson disease","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease/steatohepatitis","Primary biliary cholangitis and primary sclerosing cholangitis overlap"],"additionalInvestigations":["ANA and anti-smooth-muscle antibody — supports type 1 AIH diagnosis","Anti-LKM1 antibody — supports type 2 AIH, more typical in pediatric cases","Serum IgG level — characteristic hypergammaglobulinemia aids diagnosis and monitoring","Liver biopsy — confirms interface hepatitis and plasma cell infiltrate, stages fibrosis","Viral hepatitis serologies (hepatitis B and C) — exclude viral etiology","Ceruloplasmin and copper studies — exclude Wilson disease, especially in younger patients","Alkaline phosphatase and antimitochondrial antibody — screen for overlap with primary biliary cholangitis","Standardized AIH scoring system (simplified diagnostic criteria) — integrates autoantibodies, IgG, histology, and viral exclusion"],"redFlags":["Acute presentation with coagulopathy or encephalopathy suggesting evolution to acute liver failure","Jaundice with rapidly rising bilirubin and synthetic dysfunction","Evidence of established cirrhosis or portal hypertension at initial presentation","Lack of biochemical response after an adequate trial of immunosuppression, raising concern for an alternate or overlapping diagnosis","Signs of decompensation (ascites, variceal bleeding) warranting hepatology and transplant center referral"],"prognosis":"With timely immunosuppressive treatment, most patients achieve biochemical remission and have a favorable long-term prognosis, though relapse after treatment withdrawal is common and many require long-term maintenance therapy. Untreated or refractory disease can progress to cirrhosis, liver failure, and the need for liver transplantation.","pitfalls":["Attributing transaminase elevation solely to another cause (e.g., fatty liver or medication) without considering AIH, delaying treatment","Relying on autoantibody titers alone; seronegative AIH exists, and titers do not reliably track disease activity","Missing an overlap syndrome with cholestatic liver disease when only hepatocellular pattern is evaluated","Discontinuing therapy too early without confirming sustained biochemical and histologic remission, risking relapse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Autoimmune Hepatitis chapter (Chronic Hepatitis section)"],"crossLinks":["PRIMARY_BILIARY_CHOLANGITIS","PRIMARY_SCLEROSING_CHOLANGITIS"]},"CARCINOID_SYNDROME":{"name":"Carcinoid syndrome","system":"Endocrinology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["carcinoid","carcinoid tumor syndrome","neuroendocrine tumor with carcinoid syndrome","NET carcinoid syndrome","serotonin-secreting tumor syndrome","flushing-diarrhea syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic triad is episodic flushing, secretory diarrhea, and bronchospasm/wheezing, but full triad is present in only a minority of patients","Syndrome typically only manifests once hepatic metastases are present, since liver normally clears vasoactive mediators from portal circulation before they reach systemic circulation","Midgut (small bowel/appendiceal) tumors are the classic cause; bronchial and other extra-intestinal primaries can bypass hepatic first-pass metabolism and cause symptoms without liver metastases","Right-sided (and occasionally left-sided) valvular heart disease from fibrotic plaque deposition is a hallmark late complication and a major driver of morbidity/mortality","Flushing is usually dry, reddish-violet, and involves face/neck/upper trunk, lasting minutes; triggers include alcohol, certain foods, emotional stress, and palpation of the tumor","Niacin deficiency (pellagra-like features) can occur because tryptophan is diverted toward serotonin synthesis rather than niacin production","Carcinoid crisis — profound flushing, bronchospasm, and hemodynamic instability — can be precipitated by anesthesia, tumor manipulation, or invasive procedures and requires pre-procedural prophylaxis"],"pathophysiology":"Carcinoid syndrome arises from a neuroendocrine tumor (most often midgut origin) that secretes serotonin and other vasoactive substances (histamine, tachykinins, prostaglandins, kallikrein) directly into the systemic circulation, bypassing hepatic inactivation once liver metastases develop or when the primary tumor drains outside the portal system. These circulating mediators produce the characteristic flushing, diarrhea, and bronchoconstriction, while chronic exposure to serotonin and related factors promotes fibrous plaque deposition on cardiac valves, producing carcinoid heart disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic mastocytosis / mast cell activation syndrome","Pheochromocytoma","Menopausal flushing","VIPoma or other functional pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","Medullary thyroid carcinoma with calcitonin/other peptide secretion","Anaphylaxis or food/alcohol-induced flushing reactions"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Rosacea","Idiopathic flushing syndrome","Irritable bowel syndrome (for the diarrhea component)","Alcohol-induced flushing (especially in those with aldehyde dehydrogenase variants)"],"additionalInvestigations":["24-hour urinary 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA) — main biochemical marker of serotonin turnover, though affected by diet and certain drugs","Plasma chromogranin A — general neuroendocrine tumor marker, useful for diagnosis and monitoring tumor burden","Serum serotonin level — supportive but less specific/standardized than urinary 5-HIAA","Somatostatin receptor imaging (e.g., 68Ga-DOTATATE PET/CT) — localizes primary tumor and metastases with high sensitivity","Cross-sectional imaging (contrast CT or MRI abdomen) — assesses primary tumor site, liver metastases, and mesenteric fibrosis","Echocardiography — screens for carcinoid heart disease (tricuspid/pulmonic valve involvement)","Endoscopy/enteroscopy or capsule endoscopy — identifies small bowel primary when suspected"],"redFlags":["Signs of carcinoid heart disease (new murmur, right heart failure symptoms) warrant urgent echocardiography and cardiology referral","Pre-procedural or pre-anesthetic patients with known/suspected carcinoid require prophylaxis to prevent carcinoid crisis","Acute severe flushing with hypotension or bronchospasm suggests evolving carcinoid crisis and needs emergent management","Bowel obstruction or ischemia from mesenteric fibrosis/desmoplastic reaction is a surgical emergency","Refractory diarrhea causing dehydration or electrolyte derangement needs prompt correction"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends primarily on tumor grade, extent of metastatic disease, and presence of carcinoid heart disease; with modern somatostatin analog therapy and multidisciplinary management, many patients experience prolonged survival and symptom control, though cardiac involvement significantly worsens outcomes if untreated.","pitfalls":["Attributing episodic flushing/diarrhea to anxiety, menopause, or irritable bowel syndrome, delaying diagnosis for years","Obtaining urinary 5-HIAA without dietary restriction (serotonin-rich foods) or accounting for interfering medications, leading to false results","Overlooking carcinoid heart disease until advanced right heart failure develops","Failing to anticipate and prevent carcinoid crisis before surgery, biopsy, or anesthesia in a patient with known tumor"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Neuroendocrine Tumors and Carcinoid Syndrome"],"crossLinks":[]},"CASTLEMAN_DISEASE":{"name":"Castleman disease","system":"Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["castleman","angiofollicular lymph node hyperplasia","castleman's disease","unicentric castleman disease","multicentric castleman disease","HHV-8 associated multicentric castleman disease","giant lymph node hyperplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Consider Castleman disease when lymphadenopathy (localized or generalized) is accompanied by unexplained constitutional symptoms, cytopenias, or hypergammaglobulinemia.","Unicentric disease is usually indolent, presenting as a single enlarged lymph node mass, and is often curable with excision.","Multicentric disease is a systemic illness with fevers, weight loss, organomegaly, and multi-organ dysfunction driven by excess IL-6 signaling.","HHV-8 infection drives most multicentric cases in HIV-positive patients; idiopathic multicentric disease occurs without HHV-8 or HIV.","POEMS syndrome and TAFRO syndrome are important clinical variants/associations that change management and prognosis.","Excisional lymph node biopsy with expert hematopathology review is essential — needle biopsies are often non-diagnostic.","Multicentric disease carries an increased risk of progression to lymphoma and of concurrent Kaposi sarcoma in HHV-8-positive/HIV-positive patients."],"pathophysiology":"Castleman disease is a heterogeneous group of lymphoproliferative disorders characterized by distinctive lymph node architectural changes, most driven by dysregulated interleukin-6 (IL-6) signaling. In unicentric disease the process is localized and follicular/vascular changes predominate, while multicentric disease reflects a systemic cytokine-driven process, frequently linked to lytic HHV-8 infection in immunocompromised hosts, producing viral IL-6 and human IL-6 that fuel plasmablast proliferation and systemic inflammation. Idiopathic multicentric disease lacks HHV-8 but shares a similar IL-6-driven inflammatory phenotype, sometimes with an associated clonal or autoimmune-like process.","additionalDifferentials":["Lymphoma (Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin)","Autoimmune/rheumatologic disease with lymphadenopathy (e.g., systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis)","IgG4-related disease","Reactive lymphadenopathy from chronic infection","POEMS syndrome","Kaposi sarcoma / HHV-8-associated disease"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculous lymphadenitis","HIV-associated persistent generalized lymphadenopathy","Infectious mononucleosis (EBV)","Cat-scratch disease"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lymphoma","Sarcoidosis","IgG4-related disease","Autoimmune lymphoproliferative conditions","Plasma cell dyscrasias (e.g., POEMS-associated)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Excisional lymph node biopsy — required for definitive histopathologic diagnosis and subtype classification","HIV serology and HHV-8 testing (blood PCR or tissue staining) — identifies virus-associated multicentric disease","Complete blood count — screens for anemia, thrombocytopenia, or thrombocytosis seen in multicentric/TAFRO variants","Serum IL-6 and inflammatory markers (CRP, ESR) — supports the systemic inflammatory phenotype","Serum protein electrophoresis and immunoglobulin levels — assesses hypergammaglobulinemia and screens for paraproteins/POEMS","Cross-sectional imaging (CT chest/abdomen/pelvis) — defines extent of nodal disease and organomegaly, distinguishes unicentric from multicentric involvement","Renal and liver function testing — evaluates end-organ involvement from systemic cytokine excess","Bone marrow examination — helps exclude lymphoma or plasma cell dyscrasia when systemic disease is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive multi-organ failure (renal, hepatic) suggesting TAFRO syndrome","Severe cytopenias or bleeding diathesis","Concurrent Kaposi sarcoma or hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis-like picture","New neurologic findings or organomegaly suggesting POEMS syndrome","Rapid nodal growth or B symptoms raising concern for transformation to lymphoma"],"prognosis":"Unicentric Castleman disease has an excellent prognosis after complete surgical excision, with cure in most cases. Multicentric disease, particularly HHV-8-associated or TAFRO-variant forms, follows a more relapsing and potentially life-threatening course, with outcomes improved by IL-6-pathway-targeted therapy but still carrying elevated risk of lymphoma and organ dysfunction.","pitfalls":["Attributing lymphadenopathy and systemic symptoms solely to infection or malignancy without considering Castleman disease, delaying diagnosis","Relying on core needle biopsy alone, which frequently yields non-diagnostic or misleading architecture compared to excisional biopsy","Failing to test for HIV and HHV-8 in patients with multicentric disease, missing a treatable driver","Overlooking associated syndromes (POEMS, TAFRO) that substantially alter prognosis and treatment approach"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Peripheral Lymphadenopathy and Splenomegaly / Castleman Disease"],"crossLinks":["POEMS_SYNDROME"]},"CATASTROPHIC_APS":{"name":"Catastrophic antiphospholipid syndrome","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["catastrophic antiphospholipid","CAPS","Asherson syndrome","catastrophic APS","malignant antiphospholipid syndrome","microvascular antiphospholipid syndrome","thrombotic storm"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A fulminant, multi-organ variant of antiphospholipid syndrome causing widespread small-vessel thrombosis over days, in contrast to the single large-vessel events typical of classic APS","Suspect in any patient with known or suspected antiphospholipid antibodies who develops rapidly progressive multi-organ dysfunction","Roughly half of cases are precipitated by an identifiable trigger such as infection, surgery, trauma, anticoagulation withdrawal, or malignancy","Renal, pulmonary, cerebral, cardiac, cutaneous, and adrenal involvement are the most commonly affected sites","Thrombocytopenia and schistocytes may be present, creating overlap with thrombotic microangiopathy syndromes and complicating diagnosis","Mortality remains high despite treatment, so early recognition and aggressive combination therapy are critical","A definite diagnosis requires histopathologic confirmation of small-vessel occlusion in at least one organ, though treatment should not be delayed pending biopsy in critically ill patients"],"pathophysiology":"Catastrophic APS represents an accelerated, disseminated form of antiphospholipid antibody-mediated thrombosis affecting predominantly small vessels and multiple organs simultaneously, rather than the single medium/large-vessel thrombi seen in classic APS. Antiphospholipid antibodies (anticardiolipin, anti-beta2-glycoprotein I, lupus anticoagulant) activate endothelium, platelets, and complement, driving a systemic thrombo-inflammatory cascade that resembles disseminated intravascular coagulation but with predominant thrombosis rather than bleeding. A triggering event (infection, surgery, trauma, or abrupt anticoagulant discontinuation) is thought to initiate a self-amplifying cytokine storm and complement activation, leading to a vicious cycle of endothelial injury and microthrombosis across organs. This results in a systemic inflammatory response with multi-organ failure occurring within days to weeks.","additionalDifferentials":["Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura / atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome","Disseminated intravascular coagulation (from sepsis or malignancy)","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia with thrombosis","Systemic vasculitis (e.g., ANCA-associated vasculitis)","Severe sepsis with multi-organ dysfunction","Thrombotic microangiopathy secondary to malignancy or drugs"],"infectionMimics":["Severe sepsis / septic shock with multi-organ failure and coagulopathy","Disseminated intravascular coagulation triggered by systemic infection"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","ANCA-associated vasculitis with pulmonary-renal involvement","Malignancy-associated thrombotic microangiopathy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Antiphospholipid antibody panel (lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, anti-beta2-glycoprotein I) — confirms underlying antibody-driven process","Complete blood count with peripheral smear — detects thrombocytopenia and schistocytes suggesting microangiopathic hemolysis","Coagulation studies (PT, aPTT, fibrinogen, D-dimer) — assesses for overlapping consumptive coagulopathy","Renal and liver function panels — screens for organ involvement and severity","ADAMTS13 activity — helps exclude thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura as an alternative or coexisting diagnosis","Imaging (CT/MRI) of suspected organs — identifies infarcts in brain, lung, kidney, or bowel","Tissue biopsy of an accessible affected organ (skin, kidney) — provides histologic confirmation of small-vessel thrombosis when feasible","Blood and other relevant cultures — evaluates for an infectious trigger or mimicking sepsis"],"redFlags":["Rapid development of dysfunction in three or more organ systems within days","New thrombocytopenia with schistocytes in a patient with known antiphospholipid antibodies","Recent surgery, infection, or anticoagulant interruption in a patient with lupus or prior thrombosis","Adrenal hemorrhage/infarction presenting as unexplained hypotension or adrenal insufficiency","Concurrent cardiac (valvulopathy, myocardial infarction) and pulmonary (ARDS) involvement","Rapid clinical deterioration despite therapeutic anticoagulation"],"prognosis":"Catastrophic APS carries a high mortality rate even with aggressive combination treatment, though survival has improved with earlier recognition and multimodal therapy. Survivors remain at risk for recurrent thrombotic events and organ damage, requiring long-term anticoagulation and monitoring.","pitfalls":["Mistaking catastrophic APS for straightforward sepsis-related multi-organ failure or DIC and delaying antibody testing","Withholding anticoagulation and immunosuppression while awaiting confirmatory biopsy in a rapidly deteriorating patient","Failing to identify and treat the precipitating trigger (e.g., untreated infection) alongside the antibody-driven process","Overlooking coexisting thrombotic microangiopathy, which may require additional therapies beyond standard APS management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Antiphospholipid Syndrome chapter (Rheumatologic and Immunologic Disorders section)"],"crossLinks":["antiphospholipid_syndrome","systemic_lupus_erythematosus"]},"CELIAC_DISEASE":{"name":"Coeliac disease","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["coeliac","celiac","gluten enteropathy","celiac sprue","gluten-sensitive enteropathy","non-tropical sprue","celiac enteropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Consider in any patient with chronic diarrhoea, bloating, weight loss, or unexplained iron-deficiency anaemia, even without overt GI symptoms","Can present atypically with isolated osteoporosis, infertility, elevated transaminases, peripheral neuropathy, or dental enamel defects","Dermatitis herpetiformis is a specific cutaneous marker and is essentially pathognomonic when biopsy-confirmed","Serologic testing must be performed while the patient is still on a gluten-containing diet, otherwise antibody titres normalise and results are falsely negative","First-degree relatives and patients with type 1 diabetes, autoimmune thyroid disease, or Down/Turner syndrome have markedly increased prevalence and warrant screening","Selective IgA deficiency can cause false-negative IgA-based serology, so total IgA should be checked concurrently","Symptomatic response to a gluten-free diet supports but does not confirm the diagnosis; histology and serology should ideally precede dietary change"],"pathophysiology":"Coeliac disease is an immune-mediated enteropathy triggered by dietary gluten (from wheat, barley, and rye) in genetically susceptible individuals, most of whom carry HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8. Deamidation of gluten peptides by tissue transglutaminase enhances their binding to these HLA molecules, driving an aberrant T-cell response that damages small intestinal enterocytes. The resulting villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis impair mucosal absorptive surface area, leading to malabsorption of macronutrients and micronutrients.","additionalDifferentials":["Tropical sprue","Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth","Autoimmune enteropathy","Common variable immunodeficiency with enteropathy","Crohn disease of the small bowel","Irritable bowel syndrome"],"infectionMimics":["Giardiasis","Tropical sprue (presumed infectious/environmental etiology)","Whipple disease"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Non-coeliac gluten sensitivity","Lactose intolerance","Autoimmune enteropathy","Collagenous sprue","Eosinophilic gastroenteritis","Olmesartan-induced enteropathy"],"additionalInvestigations":["IgA tissue transglutaminase antibody — first-line serologic screen, high sensitivity and specificity","Total serum IgA — excludes IgA deficiency causing false-negative serology","IgA or IgG deamidated gliadin peptide antibody — useful adjunct, especially in young children or IgA-deficient patients","Upper endoscopy with multiple duodenal biopsies — confirms diagnosis via villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis","HLA-DQ2/DQ8 genotyping — high negative predictive value, useful to exclude disease when serology/histology are equivocal","Complete blood count and iron studies — screens for iron-deficiency anaemia from malabsorption","Bone mineral density (DEXA) — assesses for osteopenia/osteoporosis from calcium/vitamin D malabsorption","Fat-soluble vitamin levels (A, D, E, K) and folate/B12 — evaluates extent of malabsorption"],"redFlags":["Persistent or worsening symptoms despite strict gluten-free diet — consider refractory coeliac disease or alternative diagnosis","New abdominal pain, obstruction, or GI bleeding in a known coeliac patient — raises concern for enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma or ulcerative jejunoileitis","Severe malnutrition, hypoalbuminemia, or electrolyte derangement requiring urgent nutritional support","Unexplained weight loss with anaemia in an older adult — exclude malignancy alongside coeliac workup","Signs of adrenal or severe metabolic crisis from long-standing malabsorption"],"prognosis":"Most patients respond well to strict, lifelong adherence to a gluten-free diet, with resolution of symptoms and mucosal healing over months to years. A small subset develops refractory disease or, rarely, enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma, particularly with prolonged undiagnosed or poorly controlled disease.","pitfalls":["Testing serology after the patient has already started a gluten-free diet, producing false-negative results","Relying on serology alone without confirmatory duodenal biopsy, or biopsying too few sites reducing diagnostic yield","Missing the diagnosis in atypical presentations (isolated anaemia, infertility, elevated liver enzymes) because classic diarrhoea is absent","Failing to screen at-risk relatives or patients with associated autoimmune conditions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Celiac Disease (Gastroenterology section, Disorders of Malabsorption)"],"crossLinks":["iron_deficiency_anemia"]},"CEREBRAL_VENOUS_THROMBOSIS":{"name":"Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cerebral venous","sinus thrombosis","CVST","cerebral venous thrombosis","CVT","dural venous sinus thrombosis","cortical vein thrombosis","venous stroke"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Suspect in a young patient (especially female on estrogen-containing contraception or postpartum) with new headache plus seizure, focal deficit, or altered consciousness","Headache is the most common presenting symptom and can be subacute, progressive, or thunderclap-like, sometimes mimicking idiopathic intracranial hypertension","Papilledema from raised intracranial pressure may occur even without a clear focal deficit","Deficits often do not respect a single arterial territory and may be bilateral or migrate, reflecting venous rather than arterial infarction","Hemorrhagic venous infarcts are common and should not by themselves prompt withholding anticoagulation","Seizures, especially with focal onset, occur more frequently than in typical arterial ischemic stroke","A normal non-contrast CT does not exclude the diagnosis; dedicated venous imaging is required","Consider an underlying prothrombotic, infectious, or inflammatory trigger in every confirmed case"],"pathophysiology":"Thrombosis of a dural venous sinus or cortical vein obstructs cerebral venous drainage, raising venous and capillary pressure. This leads to impaired CSF resorption with intracranial hypertension, and can progress to vasogenic edema, venous congestion, and eventually venous infarction with secondary hemorrhage. Because venous drainage territories overlap and are variable between individuals, resulting deficits are often atypical for a single vascular distribution.","additionalDifferentials":["Arterial ischemic stroke","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Meningitis or encephalitis","Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome","Primary or metastatic brain tumor with mass effect"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial meningitis","Cerebral abscess","Septic (infective) intracranial venous sinus thrombosis from adjacent otitis, mastoiditis, or sinusitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri)","Arterial ischemic stroke","Subarachnoid or intraparenchymal hemorrhage from other causes","Migraine with aura","Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome"],"additionalInvestigations":["MR venography or CT venography — confirms sinus/cortical vein occlusion and is the primary diagnostic study","Non-contrast head CT — may show hyperdense sinus or hemorrhagic venous infarct but can be normal early on","MRI brain with venous sequences — detects venous infarction, edema, and hemorrhage better than CT","D-dimer — supportive if elevated but a normal level does not exclude the diagnosis, especially with isolated headache","Coagulation and thrombophilia workup — evaluates for underlying prothrombotic states once acute treatment is underway","Lumbar puncture with opening pressure — assesses for raised intracranial pressure and excludes infectious causes when imaging is inconclusive","Ophthalmologic exam for papilledema — assesses risk of visual loss from raised intracranial pressure","Screening for precipitating factors (pregnancy/postpartum status, oral contraceptive or hormonal therapy use, malignancy, recent infection or dehydration)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly declining consciousness or signs of impending herniation","New or worsening papilledema with visual field loss threatening vision","Extensive bilateral hemorrhagic venous infarction on imaging","Status epilepticus or recurrent seizures at presentation","Clinical deterioration despite anticoagulation, suggesting need for endovascular or surgical intervention"],"prognosis":"Most patients who receive timely anticoagulation have a favorable outcome with good functional recovery, though a minority develop persistent neurological deficits, chronic intracranial hypertension, or, rarely, death from extensive venous infarction or herniation. Recurrence risk depends on the presence and persistence of underlying prothrombotic risk factors.","pitfalls":["Dismissing headache as migraine or tension-type without considering CVST in at-risk patients (pregnancy, postpartum, hormonal contraception, thrombophilia)","Withholding anticoagulation because of hemorrhagic venous infarction, which is generally still an indication for treatment rather than a contraindication","Relying on a normal non-contrast CT to exclude the diagnosis instead of obtaining dedicated venous imaging","Attributing bilateral or non-arterial-territory deficits to a psychiatric or functional cause rather than pursuing venous imaging"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Cerebrovascular Diseases (Cerebral Venous Thrombosis)"],"crossLinks":["ischemic_stroke"]},"CIDP":{"name":"Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CIDP","chronic inflammatory demyelinating","chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy","chronic relapsing polyneuropathy","steroid-responsive polyneuropathy","chronic acquired demyelinating polyneuropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as a progressive or relapsing symmetric polyneuropathy evolving over at least 8 weeks, distinguishing it from Guillain-Barre syndrome which peaks by 4 weeks","Weakness typically affects both proximal and distal muscles, unlike the length-dependent pattern of most axonal neuropathies","Large-fiber sensory loss (vibration, proprioception) predominates over pain, and reflexes are diffusely diminished or absent","Cranial nerve and autonomic involvement can occur but are less prominent and severe than in Guillain-Barre syndrome","CSF classically shows albuminocytologic dissociation (elevated protein with normal cell count), similar to acute demyelinating polyneuropathy","Electrodiagnostic studies showing multifocal demyelination (conduction block, temporal dispersion, prolonged distal latencies, slowed conduction velocities) are central to diagnosis","A meaningful subset responds to immunotherapy, so early recognition matters because untreated disease can lead to irreversible axonal loss and disability","Some patients have an underlying paraprotein, diabetes, HIV, or other systemic association that should be screened for"],"pathophysiology":"CIDP results from a T-cell and antibody-mediated autoimmune attack on peripheral nerve myelin and, in some cases, the nodal/paranodal region, producing segmental demyelination and secondary remyelination with onion-bulb formation. Some patients harbor antibodies against paranodal proteins (e.g., neurofascin, contactin-1), which behave more like a nodopathy than classic demyelination and may predict differential treatment response. Chronic or repeated inflammatory injury can eventually cause secondary axonal degeneration, which correlates with residual disability.","additionalDifferentials":["Guillain-Barre syndrome (acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy)","POEMS syndrome and other paraproteinemic neuropathies","Hereditary demyelinating neuropathies (e.g., Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 1)","Diabetic or other metabolic polyneuropathy","Multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block","Neuropathy associated with lymphoma, amyloidosis, or vasculitis"],"infectionMimics":["HIV-associated demyelinating polyneuropathy","Hepatitis C-associated neuropathy","Lyme disease-related polyradiculoneuropathy"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Diabetic lumbosacral radiculoplexus neuropathy","Monoclonal gammopathy-associated neuropathy","Hereditary sensorimotor neuropathies","Toxic or drug-induced peripheral neuropathy","Paraneoplastic sensory neuronopathy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Nerve conduction studies/EMG — demonstrates demyelinating features such as conduction block and slowed velocities","Lumbar puncture with CSF protein and cell count — supports diagnosis via albuminocytologic dissociation and helps exclude infection or malignancy","Serum protein electrophoresis with immunofixation — screens for an associated monoclonal gammopathy","Fasting glucose/HbA1c — evaluates for coexisting diabetic neuropathy that can confound the picture","Nerve biopsy (selected cases) — shows demyelination and onion-bulb formation when diagnosis remains uncertain","Anti-paranodal antibody testing (e.g., neurofascin-155, contactin-1) — identifies a nodopathy subgroup with distinct treatment response","MRI of spinal nerve roots/plexus with contrast — may show root or plexus enhancement/hypertrophy supporting a proximal demyelinating process","HIV and hepatitis serologies — excludes infectious associations"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive weakness with respiratory compromise mimicking Guillain-Barre syndrome requires urgent evaluation and monitoring","Significant axonal loss on electrodiagnostic testing suggests a worse prognosis and need for prompt treatment escalation","Asymmetric or purely motor presentations should prompt consideration of multifocal motor neuropathy rather than CIDP","Coexisting monoclonal gammopathy warrants hematologic workup to exclude POEMS syndrome or malignancy","Lack of any response to appropriate first-line immunotherapy should trigger diagnostic reconsideration"],"prognosis":"Most patients improve substantially with timely immunotherapy, though relapses are common and some require long-term maintenance treatment. Delayed diagnosis or predominant axonal involvement is associated with greater residual disability.","pitfalls":["Mistaking CIDP for a slowly evolving Guillain-Barre syndrome, or vice versa, based solely on time course without full electrodiagnostic correlation","Over-reliance on CSF protein elevation alone, which is nonspecific and can be seen in diabetes and other conditions","Missing an underlying paraproteinemia or nodopathy antibody profile that changes expected treatment response","Attributing residual deficits solely to inadequate treatment when secondary axonal degeneration has already occurred"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Guillain-Barre Syndrome and Other Immune-Mediated Neuropathies"],"crossLinks":["gbs"]},"DRESS_SYNDROME":{"name":"DRESS syndrome","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dress syndrome","drug reaction eosinophilia","drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms","drug hypersensitivity syndrome","drug-induced hypersensitivity syndrome","DIHS","DRESS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Onset is classically delayed, typically 2-8 weeks after starting the culprit drug, distinguishing it from more immediate hypersensitivity reactions.","Look for the triad of fever, widespread morbilliform (often confluent and edematous) rash, and facial edema, especially periorbital swelling.","Systemic involvement is the hallmark: lymphadenopathy plus at least one internal organ (liver most common, also kidney, lung, heart) helps separate DRESS from simple drug rash.","Peripheral eosinophilia and/or atypical lymphocytosis on blood smear strongly support the diagnosis but may lag behind skin findings.","Common culprits include aromatic anticonvulsants, allopurinol, sulfonamides, and certain antibiotics — always take a careful medication history covering the prior 2 months.","Symptoms can worsen for days to weeks even after the offending drug is stopped, and relapses/flares are common during the recovery phase.","Human herpesvirus-6 reactivation is frequently detected during the illness and may contribute to prolonged or relapsing disease course.","Cross-reactivity exists among structurally related anticonvulsants, so the entire drug class should generally be avoided going forward."],"pathophysiology":"DRESS is a severe, delayed T-cell-mediated drug hypersensitivity reaction, thought to arise from impaired drug detoxification pathways or specific HLA associations that lead to a robust immune response against drug-modified proteins. The resulting systemic activation of eosinophils and lymphocytes drives multi-organ inflammation. Reactivation of latent herpesviruses, particularly HHV-6, is commonly observed and may amplify and prolong the immune response, contributing to the characteristic waxing-waning course.","additionalDifferentials":["Stevens-Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis","Acute generalized exanthematous pustulosis","Viral exanthem (e.g., EBV, CMV, HHV-6 primary infection)","Hypereosinophilic syndrome","Serum sickness-like reaction","Autoimmune connective tissue disease with rash and organ involvement"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious mononucleosis (EBV)","Primary CMV infection","Acute HIV seroconversion","Measles or other viral exanthems"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Stevens-Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis","Acute generalized exanthematous pustulosis","Hypereosinophilic syndrome","Lymphoma with paraneoplastic skin findings","Autoimmune/connective tissue disease flare"],"additionalInvestigations":["Complete blood count with differential — assesses eosinophilia and atypical lymphocytosis","Liver function tests — screens for hepatitis, the most common visceral involvement","Serum creatinine and urinalysis — evaluates renal involvement","Chest imaging — assesses for pulmonary infiltrates or pneumonitis","Skin biopsy — supports diagnosis and helps exclude other severe cutaneous adverse reactions","HHV-6 (and other herpesvirus) serology/PCR — assesses viral reactivation contributing to disease course","Cardiac enzymes/echocardiography if myocarditis suspected — detects cardiac involvement","Thyroid function tests at follow-up — late autoimmune thyroiditis can occur after recovery"],"redFlags":["Evidence of fulminant hepatitis or rising transaminases suggesting hepatic failure","Signs of myocarditis (chest pain, arrhythmia, heart failure) or interstitial pneumonitis","Acute kidney injury with rising creatinine","Rapidly progressive skin involvement raising concern for overlap with SJS/TEN","Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis-like features (cytopenias, hyperferritinemia, hepatosplenomegaly)"],"prognosis":"DRESS carries a mortality risk of roughly 5-10%, primarily related to fulminant hepatic, cardiac, or pulmonary involvement. Even after recovery, patients can develop delayed autoimmune sequelae such as thyroiditis, type 1 diabetes, or other autoimmune conditions over the following months to years, warranting longitudinal follow-up.","pitfalls":["Attributing the rash only to the most recently started drug while missing a culprit introduced weeks earlier due to the characteristic delayed onset.","Discontinuing suspected culprit and expecting immediate improvement — worsening despite drug withdrawal is common and should not prompt reinstatement.","Underestimating disease severity because eosinophilia or organ involvement may be absent or delayed at initial presentation.","Failing to screen for and monitor internal organ involvement once the skin findings are recognized, since mortality is driven by visceral disease, not the rash itself."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Cutaneous Drug Reactions"],"crossLinks":[]},"EOSINOPHILIC_ESOPHAGITIS":{"name":"Eosinophilic oesophagitis","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["eosinophilic esophagitis","eosinophilic oesophagitis","EoE","allergic esophagitis","eosinophilic esophageal disease","food impaction-associated esophagitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Consider EoE in young adults (often male) presenting with recurrent solid-food dysphagia or food bolus impaction, especially with a personal or family history of atopy, asthma, or allergic rhinitis.","In children, presentation is often less specific — feeding difficulty, vomiting, abdominal pain, or failure to thrive rather than classic dysphagia.","Endoscopy may show characteristic esophageal rings ('trachealization'), linear furrows, whitish exudates/plaques, and mucosal fragility ('crepe-paper' esophagus), but can appear normal in a subset of patients.","Diagnosis requires both compatible clinical symptoms and esophageal biopsies showing eosinophil-predominant inflammation, after excluding other causes of esophageal eosinophilia.","Proton pump inhibitor-responsive esophageal eosinophilia exists on the same clinical spectrum and no longer excludes an EoE diagnosis; PPIs are considered a treatment option rather than purely a diagnostic exclusion test.","Take multiple biopsies (typically several from proximal and distal esophagus) since eosinophilic infiltration can be patchy.","Dietary elimination approaches (empiric or allergy-testing-directed) and topical swallowed corticosteroids are mainstay therapies alongside consideration of PPIs.","Esophageal dilation may be needed for fibrostenotic disease but carries a higher perforation risk than in other esophageal strictures due to mucosal fragility."],"pathophysiology":"Eosinophilic oesophagitis is a chronic, immune/antigen-mediated condition in which food and aeroallergens drive a Th2-skewed inflammatory response leading to dense eosinophilic infiltration of the esophageal mucosa. Persistent inflammation promotes epithelial barrier dysfunction and, over time, subepithelial fibrosis and smooth muscle remodeling, producing the strictures, rings, and narrow-caliber esophagus seen in longstanding disease. It is closely linked to atopic disease and shares overlapping pathways with asthma, allergic rhinitis, and atopic dermatitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) with secondary esophageal eosinophilia","Achalasia and other esophageal motility disorders","Eosinophilic gastroenteritis with esophageal involvement","Infectious esophagitis (candidal, viral)","Pill-induced esophagitis","Esophageal webs, Schatzki ring, or peptic stricture from other causes"],"infectionMimics":["Candida esophagitis","Herpes simplex virus esophagitis","Cytomegalovirus esophagitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["GERD-related reflux esophagitis","Pill-induced esophageal injury","Achalasia","Eosinophilic gastroenteritis","Hypereosinophilic syndrome with esophageal involvement","Crohn disease affecting the esophagus"],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper endoscopy with multiple esophageal biopsies (proximal and distal) — required to confirm eosinophil-predominant inflammation and exclude mimics","Histology quantifying peak eosinophil count per high-power field — supports diagnostic threshold for EoE","Trial of acid suppression before/around diagnostic biopsy — helps distinguish reflux-related eosinophilia from primary EoE","Barium esophagram — useful to characterize strictures, rings, or narrow-caliber esophagus, particularly pre-dilation","Allergy evaluation (skin prick/patch or specific IgE testing) — may help guide elimination diet strategy though imperfect at predicting food triggers","Esophageal functional/impedance-pH testing — considered when reflux contribution is uncertain","Peripheral eosinophil count and total IgE — sometimes elevated but neither sensitive nor specific","Repeat endoscopy with biopsy after treatment — used to assess histologic response to dietary or pharmacologic therapy"],"redFlags":["Acute food bolus impaction causing complete dysphagia — requires urgent endoscopic evaluation and possible removal","Suspected esophageal perforation, especially after dilation given increased mucosal fragility","Progressive weight loss or failure to thrive in pediatric patients","Signs of a tight fibrostenotic stricture on imaging/endoscopy before attempting dilation","Unexplained esophageal eosinophilia not responding to standard measures — reconsider alternative diagnoses (hypereosinophilic syndrome, Crohn disease)"],"prognosis":"EoE is a chronic, relapsing condition requiring long-term management; without maintenance therapy, symptoms and inflammation typically recur. Untreated or undertreated disease can progress to fibrostenotic remodeling with strictures and recurrent food impactions, but sustained dietary or pharmacologic control can maintain remission and reduce complications.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal-appearing esophagus on endoscopy excludes EoE — mucosal appearance can be unremarkable despite significant histologic eosinophilia.","Relying on a single biopsy site, missing patchy involvement and underestimating eosinophil counts.","Treating all esophageal eosinophilia as pure GERD without considering the overlapping PPI-responsive EoE spectrum.","Performing aggressive dilation without recognizing the higher perforation risk from mucosal fragility and lack of prior gentle approach."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Diseases of the Esophagus / Eosinophilic Esophagitis"],"crossLinks":["ACHALASIA"]},"ERYTHEMA_NODOSUM":{"name":"Erythema nodosum","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["erythema nodosum","EN","septal panniculitis","nodular erythema"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as tender, erythematous, non-ulcerating nodules typically over the bilateral pretibial surfaces, sometimes extending to knees, ankles, or extensor forearms","Lesions evolve in color like a bruise (bright red to violaceous to yellow-green) over 1-2 weeks without ulcerating or scarring","Often accompanied by fever, malaise, arthralgia, and sometimes frank arthritis of ankles/knees","Far more common in women, with peak incidence in the second to fourth decades of life","Most cases are idiopathic or post-streptococcal, but a broad secondary workup is warranted, especially with atypical features","Lesions are typically bilateral and symmetric; unilateral or ulcerating nodules should prompt reconsideration of the diagnosis","Represents a hypersensitivity reaction pattern rather than a single disease, so the search for an underlying trigger is central to management","Usually self-limited, resolving over 3-6 weeks even without treatment of an identifiable cause"],"pathophysiology":"Erythema nodosum is a delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction resulting in inflammation centered on the subcutaneous fat septae (septal panniculitis), triggered by circulating immune complexes or antigen-driven T-cell responses to a variety of infectious, inflammatory, or drug antigens. The septal inflammatory infiltrate initially contains neutrophils and later becomes predominantly lymphohistiocytic with multinucleated giant cells, without primary vasculitis. The overlying epidermis and dermis are typically spared, which explains the absence of ulceration or scarring despite the deep tenderness.","additionalDifferentials":["Nodular vasculitis / erythema induratum (lobular panniculitis, often with ulceration, seen more on posterior calves)","Superficial thrombophlebitis","Cellulitis or erysipelas","Polyarteritis nodosa with cutaneous nodules","Sweet syndrome (acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis)","Insect bite reaction or panniculitis from trauma"],"infectionMimics":["Streptococcal pharyngitis-associated panniculitis","Tuberculosis-related erythema nodosum (erythema induratum can mimic classic EN)","Deep fungal infection (coccidioidomycosis, histoplasmosis) presenting with tender nodules","Yersinia enterocolitis-associated panniculitis","Cat-scratch disease with nodular skin lesions"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sarcoidosis (with or without Löfgren syndrome triad of EN, hilar adenopathy, and arthritis)","Inflammatory bowel disease-associated panniculitis","Drug hypersensitivity reaction (e.g., to sulfonamides, oral contraceptives)","Behçet syndrome with nodular lesions","Pregnancy-associated erythema nodosum","Malignancy-associated panniculitis (rare, e.g., lymphoma)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Throat culture and antistreptolysin O titer — screen for antecedent streptococcal infection, the most common identifiable trigger","Chest radiograph — evaluate for hilar lymphadenopathy suggesting sarcoidosis or for findings of tuberculosis","Complete blood count and inflammatory markers (ESR/CRP) — assess systemic inflammatory activity, often markedly elevated","Stool studies or IBD-directed workup if gastrointestinal symptoms present — assess for inflammatory bowel disease","Tuberculin skin test or interferon-gamma release assay — evaluate for underlying tuberculosis, especially in endemic regions","Medication history review — identify possible drug-induced etiology","Skin biopsy (deep incisional, including subcutaneous fat) — confirms septal panniculitis when diagnosis is uncertain or lesions are atypical","Pregnancy test in women of childbearing age — pregnancy is a recognized trigger"],"redFlags":["Ulcerating, unilateral, or persistent nodules beyond 6-8 weeks, suggesting an alternative diagnosis such as nodular vasculitis or vasculitic panniculitis","Significant weight loss, chronic cough, or night sweats raising concern for tuberculosis or malignancy","Bloody diarrhea or chronic abdominal symptoms suggesting undiagnosed inflammatory bowel disease","Marked hilar adenopathy on imaging warranting evaluation for sarcoidosis or lymphoma","Recurrent or refractory episodes despite removal of an identified trigger, warranting rheumatologic referral"],"prognosis":"Erythema nodosum is typically self-limited, with lesions resolving spontaneously over 3-6 weeks without scarring, though it may recur if the underlying trigger persists or recurs. Prognosis is generally excellent and closely tied to management of any identified underlying condition.","pitfalls":["Assuming erythema nodosum is always idiopathic without pursuing a directed search for streptococcal infection, sarcoidosis, IBD, tuberculosis, or drug causes","Confusing erythema nodosum with nodular vasculitis/erythema induratum, which favors the posterior calf and can ulcerate, changing management implications","Overlooking Löfgren syndrome (EN plus bilateral hilar adenopathy and arthritis) as a distinct, generally self-limited sarcoid presentation with a favorable prognosis","Performing a superficial punch biopsy instead of a deep incisional biopsy, which may miss the septal panniculitis needed for histologic confirmation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Skin Manifestations of Internal Disease (Panniculitis/Erythema Nodosum)"],"crossLinks":["sarcoidosis","inflammatory_bowel_disease"]},"FSGS":{"name":"Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","FSGS","primary FSGS","secondary FSGS","collapsing glomerulopathy","focal sclerosis","APOL1-associated nephropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["FSGS is a histologic lesion with multiple causes (primary/idiopathic, genetic, virus-associated, drug-induced, and adaptive/secondary to nephron loss or hyperfiltration), not a single disease","Primary FSGS typically presents with abrupt-onset nephrotic-range proteinuria, low serum albumin, and edema, resembling minimal change disease clinically","Secondary (adaptive) FSGS from obesity, reduced nephron mass, or reflux tends to present with subnephrotic proteinuria, preserved albumin, and a more indolent course","Collapsing variant is the most aggressive subtype, historically linked to HIV infection and other viral triggers, and carries the worst renal prognosis","APOL1 high-risk genotype markedly increases risk of FSGS and more rapid progression in patients of recent African ancestry","Diagnosis requires kidney biopsy with light microscopy showing segmental sclerosis in a subset of glomeruli, since sampling can miss focal lesions","Response to immunosuppression is more likely in primary FSGS with diffuse podocyte foot-process effacement than in secondary/adaptive forms, which generally do not respond to immunosuppressive therapy","Recurrence in the transplanted kidney is common in primary FSGS, particularly in patients whose native disease progressed rapidly to kidney failure"],"pathophysiology":"FSGS results from podocyte injury and loss that a glomerulus cannot adequately repair, leading to segmental capillary collapse and scarring in a portion of glomeruli. In primary disease a circulating permeability factor is suspected to injure podocytes directly, whereas secondary forms arise from maladaptive hyperfiltration in remnant or overworked nephrons, viral cytopathic effects on podocytes, direct podocyte toxicity from certain drugs, or inherited defects in podocyte structural proteins. Ongoing podocyte depletion below a critical threshold triggers a self-perpetuating cycle of glomerulosclerosis and eventual global glomerular loss.","additionalDifferentials":["Minimal change disease","Membranous nephropathy","IgA nephropathy","Diabetic nephropathy","Amyloidosis / other glomerular deposition diseases","Alport syndrome and other hereditary nephropathies"],"infectionMimics":["HIV-associated nephropathy (collapsing FSGS variant)","Parvovirus B19-associated collapsing glomerulopathy"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Obesity-related glomerulopathy","Reflux nephropathy / oligomeganephronia with reduced nephron number","Sickle cell nephropathy","Heroin nephropathy and other drug-induced podocytopathies","Interferon or calcineurin-inhibitor-associated glomerular injury"],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis with microscopy — assess proteinuria magnitude and look for associated hematuria/casts","24-hour urine protein or spot protein-to-creatinine ratio — quantify and monitor proteinuria","Serum albumin and lipid panel — assess for nephrotic syndrome physiology","Kidney biopsy with light, immunofluorescence, and electron microscopy — required to confirm FSGS and identify the histologic variant","HIV testing and other relevant viral serologies — evaluate for virus-associated collapsing variant","Genetic testing for podocyte-associated gene mutations (including APOL1) — consider in familial, pediatric, or steroid-resistant cases","Renal ultrasound — assess kidney size/echogenicity and screen for reduced nephron mass or reflux-related changes","Estimated GFR trend over time — gauge rate of progression and guide need for renal replacement planning"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising creatinine or oliguria suggesting an aggressive or collapsing variant","Nephrotic syndrome with severe hypoalbuminemia and high thrombotic/infectious risk requiring closer monitoring","Resistance to standard immunosuppressive therapy, suggesting secondary/genetic FSGS that will not benefit from continued immunosuppression","New heavy proteinuria in a transplant recipient with a history of primary FSGS, raising concern for disease recurrence","Uncontrolled hypertension or severe edema/anasarca needing urgent management"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary widely by FSGS subtype: primary FSGS achieving remission (spontaneous or treatment-induced) has a favorable long-term renal outlook, whereas resistant primary disease and most secondary/collapsing forms carry a substantial risk of progression to kidney failure over years. The collapsing variant has the most aggressive course among the subtypes.","pitfalls":["Treating all FSGS as a single steroid-responsive disease rather than recognizing that secondary/adaptive forms generally do not benefit from immunosuppression","Missing the diagnosis on a biopsy specimen that under-samples glomeruli, since the sclerotic lesions are segmental and focal rather than global","Failing to search for an underlying secondary cause (obesity, reduced nephron mass, viral infection, drug exposure) before committing a patient to prolonged immunosuppressive therapy","Overlooking genetic/familial forms in young patients or those with a family history, which alters both prognosis and treatment approach"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Glomerular Diseases chapter"],"crossLinks":["MINIMAL_CHANGE_DISEASE","MEMBRANOUS_NEPHROPATHY"]},"GOODPASTURE":{"name":"Anti-GBM disease (Goodpasture)","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["anti-gbm","anti glomerular basement","goodpasture syndrome","goodpasture disease","anti-GBM antibody disease","pulmonary-renal syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic triad is rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis plus alveolar hemorrhage, though renal-limited disease occurs without lung involvement","Caused by circulating autoantibodies against the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen in glomerular and alveolar basement membranes","Bimodal age distribution — young men (often smokers, with lung hemorrhage) and older adults of both sexes (often renal-limited)","Smoking, hydrocarbon exposure, viral respiratory infection, and cocaine use are recognized triggers of alveolar hemorrhage in susceptible patients","Roughly a third of patients have concurrent ANCA positivity (usually MPO-ANCA), which affects prognosis and relapse risk","Renal biopsy showing linear IgG deposition along the glomerular basement membrane on immunofluorescence is the diagnostic hallmark","Prompt recognition and treatment before oliguria and severe renal failure develop is the strongest determinant of kidney survival","Once ESRD is established, native kidney recovery is unlikely and prophylactic bilateral nephrectomy is not standard practice"],"pathophysiology":"Anti-GBM disease results from autoantibody binding to a conformational epitope on the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen, an antigen shared by the glomerular and alveolar basement membranes. Antibody deposition activates complement and recruits neutrophils and macrophages, producing crescentic glomerulonephritis and, when pulmonary capillaries are involved, diffuse alveolar hemorrhage. Loss of pulmonary capillary integrity (from smoking, infection, or inhalational injury) is thought to expose the alveolar antigen and precipitate lung bleeding in genetically susceptible individuals (HLA-DR15 association).","additionalDifferentials":["ANCA-associated vasculitis (granulomatosis with polyangiitis, microscopic polyangiitis)","Lupus nephritis with diffuse alveolar hemorrhage","IgA nephropathy / IgA vasculitis with pulmonary involvement","Post-infectious or other immune-complex crescentic glomerulonephritis","Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis with pulmonary-renal syndrome","Thrombotic microangiopathy causing combined renal and pulmonary injury"],"infectionMimics":["Severe pneumonia with concurrent acute kidney injury mimicking a pulmonary-renal syndrome","Leptospirosis with pulmonary hemorrhage and renal failure"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["ANCA-associated vasculitis","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage from anticoagulation or coagulopathy","Cardiogenic pulmonary edema with coincident renal failure","Cocaine/levamisole-induced vasculitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum anti-GBM antibody titer (ELISA) — confirms circulating autoantibody, main serologic diagnostic test","ANCA serology (MPO/PR3) — detects dual-positive cases which alter relapse risk and monitoring","Renal biopsy with immunofluorescence — linear IgG (and often C3) along GBM confirms diagnosis and grades crescent burden for prognosis","Urinalysis with microscopy — dysmorphic red cells and red cell casts confirm glomerular bleeding","Chest imaging (chest x-ray or CT) — detects alveolar infiltrates from pulmonary hemorrhage","Diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide (DLCO) — an elevated DLCO supports active alveolar hemorrhage","Complete blood count — unexplained drop in hemoglobin can be an early clue to occult pulmonary hemorrhage","Serum creatinine and renal function trend — guides urgency of treatment and predicts recovery potential"],"redFlags":["Hemoptysis or unexplained falling hemoglobin in a patient with acute glomerulonephritis","Oliguria or rapidly rising creatinine suggesting need for urgent biopsy and immunosuppression before irreversible fibrosis","Respiratory distress or hypoxemia from active alveolar hemorrhage requiring ICU-level support","Dialysis-dependent renal failure at presentation, a marker of poor renal recovery","Concurrent ANCA positivity, which is associated with a distinct relapse pattern requiring longer surveillance"],"prognosis":"Outcomes depend heavily on renal function and the extent of crescent formation at diagnosis — patients presenting with dialysis-dependent renal failure or near-total crescents rarely recover independent kidney function, whereas earlier treatment before advanced renal failure preserves function in many cases. Pulmonary hemorrhage, when treated promptly, generally resolves without long-term sequelae, but disease recurrence and progression to end-stage renal disease remain the major long-term concerns.","pitfalls":["Attributing hemoptysis and anemia to an unrelated pulmonary process, delaying recognition of the pulmonary-renal syndrome","Waiting for antibody results before starting empiric treatment when clinical suspicion is high and biopsy is pending","Missing renal-limited anti-GBM disease because the classic pulmonary component is absent","Failing to test for concomitant ANCA, which can alter both prognosis and the duration of immunosuppressive follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Glomerular Diseases / Anti-GBM (Goodpasture) Disease chapter"],"crossLinks":[]},"HEPATORENAL_SYNDROME":{"name":"Hepatorenal syndrome","system":"Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hepatorenal","HRS","HRS-AKI","HRS-NAKI","type 1 hepatorenal syndrome","type 2 hepatorenal syndrome","functional renal failure of cirrhosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A diagnosis of exclusion: renal failure in advanced cirrhosis/portal hypertension (with or without ascites) that fails to improve after volume expansion and stopping diuretics","Kidneys are structurally normal — HRS is a functional, potentially reversible form of renal hypoperfusion driven by splanchnic vasodilation and effective arterial underfilling","Newer nomenclature classifies HRS as HRS-AKI (rapidly progressive, formerly type 1) versus HRS-NAKI (slower, chronic course, formerly type 2)","Urinalysis and urine sediment are typically bland, with minimal proteinuria and few cells or casts","Urine sodium is usually very low, reflecting intense renal sodium avidity from neurohormonal activation","A common precipitant is spontaneous bacterial peritonitis or other infection, so sepsis must always be actively sought and excluded","Trial of volume expansion (typically with albumin) for 48 hours with cessation of diuretics/nephrotoxins is required before the diagnosis can be confirmed","Definitive treatment is liver transplantation; medical therapy is a bridge, not a cure"],"pathophysiology":"Advanced liver disease with portal hypertension causes marked splanchnic arterial vasodilation, which reduces effective circulating arterial volume despite total-body volume overload. This triggers compensatory activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, sympathetic nervous system, and vasopressin release, producing intense renal vasoconstriction and a progressive fall in glomerular filtration despite normal renal histology. Systemic inflammation and bacterial translocation from the gut further worsen splanchnic vasodilation and renal hypoperfusion, and superimposed infection frequently precipitates the acute form.","additionalDifferentials":["Prerenal azotemia from volume depletion (vomiting, diarrhea, over-diuresis, GI bleeding)","Acute tubular necrosis from ischemia, sepsis, or nephrotoxin exposure","Drug-induced acute kidney injury (NSAIDs, aminoglycosides, contrast, ACE inhibitors/ARBs)","Glomerulonephritis associated with chronic liver disease (e.g., IgA nephropathy, membranoproliferative pattern in hepatitis C)","Abdominal compartment syndrome from tense ascites","Obstructive uropathy"],"infectionMimics":["Sepsis-associated acute kidney injury","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis with secondary renal hypoperfusion","Urinary tract infection with associated prerenal physiology"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute tubular necrosis","Prerenal azotemia from diuretic overuse or GI losses","Contrast-associated or drug-induced nephropathy","Cardiorenal syndrome from cirrhotic cardiomyopathy or coexistent heart failure","Abdominal compartment syndrome"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatinine trend and estimated GFR — establishes rate and severity of renal decline defining HRS-AKI vs HRS-NAKI","Urinalysis with microscopy — bland sediment supports HRS over intrinsic renal disease","Urine sodium and fractional excretion of sodium — typically very low, reflecting avid sodium retention","Renal ultrasound — excludes obstruction and assesses for underlying structural kidney disease","Diagnostic paracentesis with ascitic fluid cell count and culture — excludes spontaneous bacterial peritonitis as a precipitant","Blood and urine cultures — identify occult infection precipitating renal decline","Response to albumin volume challenge with diuretic withdrawal — required to confirm the diagnosis","Liver function panel and coagulation studies — assess severity of underlying hepatic decompensation and transplant urgency"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising creatinine meeting acute kidney injury criteria in a cirrhotic patient (HRS-AKI)","Concurrent signs of sepsis or spontaneous bacterial peritonitis requiring urgent source control","Oliguria or anuria with worsening encephalopathy or hemodynamic instability","Failure to respond to albumin challenge and vasoconstrictor therapy, indicating need for urgent transplant evaluation","Coexistent gastrointestinal bleeding masking or worsening true volume depletion","Multiorgan involvement suggesting acute-on-chronic liver failure"],"prognosis":"Historically associated with very high short-term mortality, particularly the rapidly progressive form, unless renal function improves with therapy or the patient undergoes timely liver transplantation. Even with treatment response, underlying hepatic decompensation continues to drive long-term outcomes.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing HRS before adequately excluding hypovolemia and completing an albumin challenge with diuretic withdrawal","Overlooking an occult infection (especially spontaneous bacterial peritonitis) as the precipitating trigger","Assuming a bland urinalysis excludes intrinsic renal disease entirely without considering overlap syndromes","Delaying nephrology and hepatology/transplant referral while pursuing prolonged medical trials in a rapidly deteriorating patient"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Cirrhosis and Its Complications; Acute Kidney Injury"],"crossLinks":[]},"HLH":{"name":"Hemophagocytic Lymphohistiocytosis (HLH)","system":"Hematology / Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HLH","Hemophagocytic syndrome","Haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis","Macrophage activation syndrome","MAS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e — histiocytic / hemophagocytic syndromes","clinicalPearls":["HLH is a hyperinflammatory syndrome of uncontrolled immune activation: over-activated CD8 T cells and macrophages drive a cytokine storm and widespread hemophagocytosis (immune cells engulfing blood cells), producing fever, cytopenias and organ injury rather than effective clearance of the trigger.","Two broad forms exist — primary/familial HLH from inherited defects of lymphocyte cytotoxicity (e.g. perforin/PRF1, UNC13D), usually presenting in infants and children; and secondary/acquired HLH triggered by infection (classically EBV), malignancy (especially lymphoma), or autoimmune/autoinflammatory disease, where it is termed macrophage activation syndrome (MAS).","The clinical hallmark is persistent high fever that does NOT respond to antibiotics, with hepatosplenomegaly and progressive cytopenias affecting two or more lineages — a picture that closely mimics severe sepsis but fails to resolve with antimicrobials alone.","A markedly elevated serum ferritin is the most useful bedside clue: values in the tens of thousands are strongly suggestive in adults, and a rising ferritin with falling cell counts and falling fibrinogen should prompt urgent evaluation.","Diagnosis commonly uses the HLH-2004 framework (five or more of eight features): fever; splenomegaly; cytopenias in two or more lineages; hypertriglyceridemia and/or hypofibrinogenemia; hemophagocytosis on marrow, spleen or lymph node; low or absent NK-cell activity; ferritin at or above 500 micrograms/L; and elevated soluble CD25 (soluble IL-2 receptor). The H-score is an alternative probability tool for secondary HLH in adults."],"pathophysiology":"Defective or overwhelmed cytotoxic lymphocyte function fails to clear antigen-presenting and infected cells, so antigen persists and continuously stimulates CD8+ T cells and macrophages. The result is unrestrained secretion of pro-inflammatory cytokines (notably interferon-gamma, IL-6, IL-10, TNF and IL-18) — a cytokine storm — that activates macrophages to phagocytose erythrocytes, leukocytes and platelets (hemophagocytosis) in the marrow and reticuloendothelial system. This drives the cardinal features: fever from systemic inflammation, cytopenias from marrow consumption and hemophagocytosis, hepatosplenomegaly and transaminitis from organ infiltration, hyperferritinemia and hypertriglyceridemia from macrophage activation, and hypofibrinogenemia with coagulopathy that can progress to disseminated intravascular coagulation and multi-organ failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Severe sepsis / septic shock — the principal mimic, with overlapping fever, cytopenias and organ dysfunction","Disseminated infection (EBV, CMV and other viruses; tuberculosis; visceral leishmaniasis; severe rickettsial disease)","Lymphoma and other haematological malignancies, which can both trigger and mimic HLH","Adult-onset Still's disease and systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis complicated by macrophage activation syndrome","Thrombotic microangiopathy (TTP/HUS) and other causes of fever with cytopenias","Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS)"],"infectionMimics":["Severe sepsis / septic shock","Disseminated viral infection (EBV, CMV)","Visceral leishmaniasis","Miliary / disseminated tuberculosis","Severe rickettsial or scrub typhus infection"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lymphoma / leukaemia","Adult-onset Still's disease / systemic JIA with macrophage activation syndrome","Thrombotic microangiopathy (TTP/HUS)","DRESS syndrome"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ferritin — very high values (often above 10,000 micrograms/L in adults) are highly suggestive","Fasting triglycerides and fibrinogen (hypertriglyceridemia and/or hypofibrinogenemia)","Full blood count with film (bi- or tri-lineage cytopenias)","Soluble CD25 (soluble IL-2 receptor) and NK-cell activity where available","Bone marrow aspirate / biopsy for hemophagocytosis (may be absent early — a normal marrow does NOT exclude HLH)","Liver function tests and coagulation profile (transaminitis, coagulopathy / DIC)","Trigger work-up: EBV / CMV and other viral PCR, blood cultures, and evaluation for lymphoma and autoimmune disease","H-score calculation to estimate the probability of secondary HLH in adults"],"redFlags":["Persistent high fever unresponsive to broad-spectrum antibiotics with worsening cytopenias","Extreme hyperferritinemia (tens of thousands) with falling fibrinogen — impending decompensation","Rapidly progressive multi-organ dysfunction, coagulopathy or DIC","Neurological involvement (seizures, altered sensorium) indicating CNS-HLH","Underlying lymphoma or EBV-driven disease, which carry high mortality and need urgent trigger-directed therapy"],"prognosis":"Untreated HLH is frequently fatal. Outcome depends on prompt recognition, the underlying trigger (malignancy- and EBV-associated disease carry the worst prognosis), and timely trigger-directed and immunosuppressive therapy. Macrophage activation syndrome arising in rheumatic disease generally has a better prognosis when recognised and treated early.","pitfalls":["Anchoring on 'sepsis' and continuing antibiotics while HLH progresses — suspect HLH when a septic picture does not improve and the ferritin is very high.","Assuming a normal early bone marrow excludes HLH; hemophagocytosis can appear later, and the diagnosis is clinical plus biochemical, not marrow-dependent.","Treating the syndrome without an aggressive search for the trigger (infection, malignancy, autoimmune) — outcome depends on identifying and treating it.","Over-relying on a ferritin of 500 micrograms/L alone, which is not specific; it is the very high values and the overall pattern that matter."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — histiocytic / hemophagocytic syndromes","HLH-2004 diagnostic criteria (Histiocyte Society)","H-score for reactive haemophagocytic syndrome (Fardet et al., Arthritis Rheumatol 2014)"],"crossLinks":[]},"IGA_NEPHROPATHY":{"name":"IgA nephropathy","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["iga nephropathy","berger disease","iga glomerulonephritis","synpharyngitic hematuria","ign","berger's disease","mesangial iga disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most common primary glomerulonephritis worldwide, typically presenting in the second or third decade of life.","Classic presentation is episodic gross (macroscopic) hematuria occurring within 1-2 days of an upper respiratory or mucosal infection, distinguishing it from post-streptococcal GN which lags 1-3 weeks.","Can also present as asymptomatic microscopic hematuria with mild proteinuria found incidentally on urinalysis.","Diagnosis requires kidney biopsy showing dominant or codominant mesangial IgA deposition on immunofluorescence.","Course is highly variable: some patients have a benign lifelong course while roughly 20-40% progress to end-stage kidney disease over 20 years.","Recurrent gross hematuria episodes typically resolve completely between attacks, but persistent proteinuria or hypertension signals higher risk.","IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein purpura) is considered a systemic variant of the same underlying disease process affecting skin, gut, and joints.","Renal biopsy findings are graded using the Oxford (MEST-C) classification to help predict prognosis and guide therapy intensity."],"pathophysiology":"Disease arises from circulating poorly galactosylated IgA1 molecules that form immune complexes, which deposit in the glomerular mesangium and trigger mesangial cell proliferation and matrix expansion. This deposition activates complement and local inflammatory pathways, leading to variable degrees of mesangial hypercellularity, segmental sclerosis, endocapillary proliferation, and interstitial fibrosis depending on disease severity. Mucosal immune stimulation (e.g., respiratory infection) is thought to transiently boost production of these abnormal IgA1 complexes, explaining the synpharyngitic timing of gross hematuria flares.","additionalDifferentials":["Thin basement membrane nephropathy (benign familial hematuria)","Alport syndrome (hereditary nephritis)","Post-infectious (post-streptococcal) glomerulonephritis","Lupus nephritis","IgA vasculitis with nephritis","Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis"],"infectionMimics":["Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis","Infective endocarditis-associated glomerulonephritis","Hepatitis-associated glomerulonephritis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Thin basement membrane nephropathy","Alport syndrome","Lupus nephritis","ANCA-associated vasculitis with glomerulonephritis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis with microscopy — confirms dysmorphic red cells and red cell casts indicating glomerular bleeding","Urine protein-to-creatinine ratio or 24-hour urine protein — quantifies proteinuria for staging and prognosis","Serum creatinine and estimated GFR — establishes baseline kidney function and tracks progression","Serum complement levels (C3, C4) — typically normal in IgA nephropathy, helping exclude complement-mediated GN","Kidney biopsy with light microscopy, immunofluorescence, and electron microscopy — required for definitive diagnosis and Oxford classification grading","Serum IgA level — often elevated but neither sensitive nor specific enough to confirm diagnosis alone","ANA, ANCA, and hepatitis serologies — helps exclude secondary causes and mimicking systemic diseases","Renal ultrasound — assesses kidney size and excludes obstructive or structural causes of hematuria"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising creatinine suggesting crescentic (rapidly progressive) IgA nephropathy requiring urgent nephrology evaluation","Nephrotic-range proteinuria or nephrotic syndrome at presentation","New or worsening hypertension poorly responsive to therapy","Systemic features (purpura, arthralgia, abdominal pain) suggesting IgA vasculitis with renal involvement","Persistent proteinuria greater than 1 gram per day despite optimized supportive therapy"],"prognosis":"Course ranges from indolent with preserved kidney function over decades to progressive decline, with an estimated 20-40% of patients reaching end-stage kidney disease within 20 years of diagnosis. Higher risk features include persistent proteinuria, hypertension, reduced GFR at presentation, and unfavorable histology on Oxford classification.","pitfalls":["Attributing recurrent gross hematuria solely to urologic causes and delaying nephrology referral or biopsy.","Assuming normal complement levels rule out all forms of glomerulonephritis rather than supporting IgA nephropathy specifically.","Underestimating long-term risk in patients with isolated microscopic hematuria who lack proteinuria monitoring over time.","Confusing timing of hematuria onset relative to infection with post-streptococcal GN, missing the synpharyngitic pattern typical of IgA nephropathy."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Glomerular Diseases (IgA Nephropathy)"],"crossLinks":[]},"INSULINOMA":{"name":"Insulinoma","system":"Endocrinology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Insulinoma","Beta-cell tumor","Islet cell tumor","Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor - insulin secreting","Insulin-secreting pNET","Whipple triad tumor","Nesidioblastosis (adult, related entity)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with Whipple triad: symptoms of neuroglycopenia/adrenergic surge, documented hypoglycemia, and relief with glucose intake.","Neuroglycopenic symptoms (confusion, behavioral change, visual disturbance, seizure, coma) often dominate and can mimic a primary neuropsychiatric or seizure disorder.","Fasting-induced hypoglycemia is classic, but symptoms can also occur postprandially or with exertion in some patients.","Most insulinomas are small (<2 cm), solitary, benign, and confined to the pancreas; only a minority are malignant or multiple.","Multiple or recurrent insulinomas should raise suspicion for MEN1 syndrome, especially in younger patients or with a family history.","Weight gain is common because patients learn to eat frequently to avoid symptomatic hypoglycemia.","Diagnosis rests on a supervised fasting study demonstrating inappropriately elevated insulin and C-peptide during hypoglycemia, not just cross-sectional imaging.","Localization can be difficult since many tumors are very small; a negative scan does not exclude the diagnosis."],"pathophysiology":"Insulinoma is a neuroendocrine tumor arising from pancreatic beta cells that autonomously secretes insulin independent of glucose feedback regulation. This unregulated insulin release drives glucose into tissues and suppresses hepatic glucose output, producing hypoglycemia. The resulting fall in plasma glucose triggers counter-regulatory adrenergic activation and, when severe or prolonged, neuroglycopenia due to inadequate glucose delivery to the brain. Most tumors are sporadic and benign, but a subset occurs in the context of multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1.","additionalDifferentials":["Factitious or surreptitious insulin/sulfonylurea administration","Non-islet cell tumor hypoglycemia (IGF-2-secreting mesenchymal or epithelial tumors)","Post-bariatric surgery hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia / nesidioblastosis","Adrenal insufficiency","Autoimmune insulin (Hirata) syndrome or insulin receptor autoantibodies","Severe hepatic failure or sepsis-related hypoglycemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Reactive (postprandial) hypoglycemia","Alcohol-induced hypoglycemia","Adrenal insufficiency","Hypopituitarism","Factitious hypoglycemia from exogenous insulin or sulfonylureas"],"additionalInvestigations":["72-hour supervised fasting test — reference standard to document Whipple triad with paired glucose, insulin, C-peptide, and proinsulin","Plasma insulin and C-peptide during hypoglycemia — inappropriately elevated levels confirm endogenous hyperinsulinemia","Sulfonylurea/meglitinide screen — excludes factitious hypoglycemia from oral secretagogues","Insulin antibody testing — helps exclude autoimmune insulin syndrome or exogenous insulin use","Contrast-enhanced CT or MRI of pancreas — initial anatomic localization of tumor","Endoscopic ultrasound — high sensitivity for small intrapancreatic lesions not seen on cross-sectional imaging","Somatostatin-receptor or GLP-1 receptor based functional imaging — localizes lesion when anatomic imaging is negative","Genetic/family screening for MEN1 — indicated when tumors are multiple, recurrent, or early-onset"],"redFlags":["Severe neuroglycopenia with seizure, coma, or altered mental status requiring urgent glucose correction","Recurrent or multifocal tumors suggesting MEN1 and need for broader endocrine screening","Imaging or biochemical features suggesting malignant insulinoma with metastatic potential","Hypoglycemia unresponsive to standard glucose repletion, suggesting an alternative or coexisting cause","Signs of another MEN1-associated tumor (parathyroid, pituitary) in a patient with insulinoma"],"prognosis":"Most insulinomas are benign and solitary, and surgical resection is curative in the large majority of cases. Malignant or MEN1-associated multifocal disease carries a less favorable outlook and requires longer-term surveillance.","pitfalls":["Relying on a single random glucose or insulin level instead of a supervised fasting protocol, leading to missed or false diagnosis.","Failing to exclude factitious hypoglycemia (exogenous insulin/sulfonylurea) before pursuing extensive tumor imaging.","Assuming a negative CT/MRI excludes insulinoma, when small tumors frequently require endoscopic ultrasound or functional imaging for localization.","Overlooking the possibility of MEN1 in patients with multiple or recurrent insulinomas, delaying appropriate family and genetic evaluation."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Hypoglycemia and Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Tumors chapters"],"crossLinks":["hypoglycemia"]},"IPF":{"name":"Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","system":"Pulmonology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","IPF","usual interstitial pneumonia","UIP","cryptogenic fibrosing alveolitis","idiopathic UIP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Consider IPF in an older adult (usually over age 60) with progressive exertional dyspnea and dry cough lasting months, especially with a smoking history.","Bibasilar, fine 'Velcro-like' end-inspiratory crackles are the classic auscultatory finding.","Digital clubbing is present in roughly half of patients and supports the diagnosis.","High-resolution CT showing a definite UIP pattern (subpleural, basal-predominant reticulation with honeycombing, traction bronchiectasis, absence of features suggesting an alternative diagnosis) can obviate the need for lung biopsy.","IPF is a diagnosis of exclusion — connective tissue disease, chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis, drug toxicity, and occupational/environmental exposures must be ruled out first.","Course is highly variable: most patients decline gradually, but acute exacerbations with rapid, often fatal deterioration can occur unpredictably.","Antifibrotic therapy slows the rate of decline in lung function but does not reverse existing fibrosis, so early diagnosis and referral matter.","Early referral for lung transplant evaluation should be considered at diagnosis given the poor natural history."],"pathophysiology":"IPF is thought to result from repeated micro-injury to alveolar epithelium in a genetically susceptible, typically older individual, triggering an aberrant wound-healing response rather than a primarily inflammatory process. This dysregulated repair leads to fibroblast/myofibroblast proliferation and excessive extracellular matrix deposition, producing the patchy, temporally heterogeneous fibrosis and architectural distortion characteristic of the usual interstitial pneumonia pattern. Progressive scarring impairs gas exchange and lung compliance, culminating in restrictive physiology and hypoxemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Connective tissue disease-associated interstitial lung disease (e.g., rheumatoid arthritis, systemic sclerosis)","Chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia","Drug-induced pulmonary toxicity","Asbestosis or other pneumoconiosis","Combined pulmonary fibrosis and emphysema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Congestive heart failure with pulmonary edema","Sarcoidosis","Chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Connective tissue disease-associated interstitial lung disease","Pulmonary drug toxicity"],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution CT chest — identifies UIP pattern and excludes alternative diagnoses","Pulmonary function tests with DLCO — documents restrictive pattern and gas-transfer impairment, tracks progression","Serologic autoimmune panel (ANA, rheumatoid factor, myositis antibodies) — screens for occult connective tissue disease","Six-minute walk test with oximetry — assesses functional capacity and exertional desaturation","Surgical or transbronchial cryobiopsy — considered when HRCT is indeterminate and histopathology would change management","Echocardiogram — evaluates for pulmonary hypertension as a complication","Bronchoalveolar lavage — helps exclude infection or alternative diagnoses in select atypical presentations","Detailed exposure and medication history — identifies occupational, environmental, or drug-related causes of fibrosis"],"redFlags":["Acute worsening of dyspnea over days to weeks (acute exacerbation), which carries high short-term mortality","Resting hypoxemia or significant desaturation on exertion","Rapidly declining forced vital capacity or DLCO on serial testing","Signs of cor pulmonale or pulmonary hypertension","Superimposed respiratory infection, which can precipitate acute decompensation"],"prognosis":"IPF carries a poor prognosis with median survival historically around 3-5 years from diagnosis, though the trajectory is heterogeneous and antifibrotic therapy has modestly slowed functional decline. Acute exacerbations and progression to respiratory failure are the major causes of death, and lung transplantation remains the only intervention altering long-term survival.","pitfalls":["Attributing crackles and dyspnea to heart failure or COPD without pursuing HRCT, delaying diagnosis","Missing an underlying connective tissue disease or exposure history that would reclassify the disease and change management","Relying on chest x-ray alone, which is insensitive for early fibrotic change","Assuming a stable course; failing to monitor for acute exacerbations or gradual functional decline"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Interstitial Lung Diseases / Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis"],"crossLinks":[]},"KAWASAKI_DISEASE":{"name":"Kawasaki disease","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["kawasaki","mucocutaneous lymph node","mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome","kawasaki syndrome","infantile polyarteritis","kd"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Acute self-limited vasculitis of childhood, mainly under age 5, that preferentially targets medium-sized arteries, especially the coronary arteries.","Classic diagnosis needs fever for five or more days plus four of five criteria: bilateral non-exudative conjunctivitis, oral mucosal changes (cracked lips, strawberry tongue), polymorphous rash, extremity changes (edema, erythema, later periungual desquamation), and cervical lymphadenopathy usually unilateral and over 1.5 cm.","Incomplete (atypical) presentations are common in infants under 6 months and older children, and this group carries a higher, not lower, risk of coronary aneurysm.","Irritability out of proportion to exam findings and marked perineal desquamation in the diaper area are supportive clues.","Untreated disease leads to coronary artery aneurysms in roughly a quarter of children; timely therapy sharply reduces this risk.","Think of Kawasaki disease in any young febrile child with unexplained sterile pyuria, gallbladder hydrops, or aseptic meningitis.","Highest risk of coronary aneurysm formation is in the subacute phase, roughly one to four weeks after fever onset, coinciding with marked thrombocytosis."],"pathophysiology":"Kawasaki disease is thought to arise from an exaggerated innate and adaptive immune response, possibly triggered by an unidentified infectious agent, in a genetically susceptible host. The resulting medium-vessel vasculitis targets the coronary arteries, causing panvasculitis with destruction of the internal elastic lamina and media, which weakens the vessel wall and predisposes to aneurysm formation and later stenosis or thrombosis. Systemic inflammation also produces the mucocutaneous and lymph node findings that define the clinical syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Scarlet fever / group A streptococcal infection","Measles and other viral exanthems","Staphylococcal or streptococcal toxic shock syndrome","Stevens-Johnson syndrome / drug hypersensitivity reaction","Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (MIS-C)"],"infectionMimics":["Scarlet fever (group A Streptococcus)","Measles","Adenovirus infection","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome / toxic shock syndrome","Epstein-Barr virus infection","Leptospirosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (MIS-C)","Stevens-Johnson syndrome / drug reaction","Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Polyarteritis nodosa"],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiogram — baseline and serial imaging to detect coronary artery dilation or aneurysm","CBC with differential — leukocytosis early, thrombocytosis typically emerging in the subacute phase","ESR and CRP — markedly elevated, supports diagnosis and tracks inflammatory activity","Liver function tests — mild transaminitis and hypoalbuminemia are common","Urinalysis — sterile pyuria from urethritis supports the diagnosis","ECG — screen for arrhythmia or ischemic changes related to coronary involvement","Blood culture and throat culture — help exclude streptococcal and other bacterial mimics","Lipid panel — baseline assessment given long-term cardiovascular risk in those with aneurysms"],"redFlags":["Evidence of coronary artery dilation or aneurysm on echocardiogram warrants urgent cardiology involvement","Signs of myocarditis, pericardial effusion, or hemodynamic instability (Kawasaki disease shock syndrome)","Persistent or recrudescent fever after initial therapy suggesting treatment resistance","Very young infants or children with incomplete presentations, who are at higher risk of coronary complications","Marked thrombocytosis in the subacute phase increasing thrombotic risk in dilated vessels"],"prognosis":"With prompt recognition and treatment most children recover fully with resolution of fever and inflammation within days. Coronary artery aneurysms, when they occur, may regress over time but can also persist, predisposing to late stenosis, thrombosis, or ischemic events, so children with cardiac involvement need long-term surveillance.","pitfalls":["Dismissing the diagnosis because not all classic criteria are present, especially in infants who often have incomplete disease with greater cardiac risk","Delaying treatment while awaiting full criteria, since early therapy within the first ten days of fever meaningfully lowers aneurysm risk","Overlooking overlap with MIS-C in the appropriate epidemiologic context, which requires additional testing and different considerations","Failing to arrange long-term cardiology follow-up in children with documented coronary changes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Vasculitis (Kawasaki Disease)"],"crossLinks":[]},"MCTD":{"name":"Mixed connective tissue disease","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mixed connective tissue","sharp syndrome","MCTD","mixed connective tissue disease","overlap connective tissue disease","anti-U1-RNP disease","undifferentiated connective tissue disease overlap"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Consider MCTD in a patient with overlapping features of SLE, systemic sclerosis, and polymyositis who does not neatly fit any single diagnosis.","Puffy, swollen ('sausage') fingers and severe Raynaud phenomenon are classic early findings, often preceding other manifestations by years.","High-titer anti-U1-ribonucleoprotein (anti-U1-RNP) antibody is the serologic hallmark and is required for diagnosis.","Pulmonary arterial hypertension is the leading cause of death and can develop insidiously, so periodic screening is warranted even when patients feel well.","Esophageal dysmotility and other sclerodermoid features are common, similar to limited systemic sclerosis.","Renal disease and severe CNS involvement are less frequent than in classic SLE, but can occur and should not be dismissed.","Clinical phenotype can evolve over time, and some patients eventually reclassify into a more defined connective tissue disease such as scleroderma or SLE.","Myositis in MCTD tends to respond well to glucocorticoids compared with some other overlap myopathies."],"pathophysiology":"MCTD is an overlap autoimmune connective tissue disorder characterized by immune-mediated vasculopathy and fibrosis affecting skin, synovium, muscle, esophagus, lungs, and vasculature. It is strongly associated with high titers of antibody against the U1-RNP component of the spliceosome, which is thought to contribute to immune complex formation and endothelial injury. The vascular pathology, particularly intimal proliferation in pulmonary and other vessels, resembles that seen in systemic sclerosis and underlies the propensity for pulmonary arterial hypertension.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma)","Polymyositis/dermatomyositis","Rheumatoid arthritis","Undifferentiated connective tissue disease","Sjögren syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Limited or diffuse systemic sclerosis","Idiopathic inflammatory myopathy","Antisynthetase syndrome","Primary Raynaud phenomenon with isolated ANA positivity"],"additionalInvestigations":["ANA with reflex extractable nuclear antigen panel — screens for anti-U1-RNP, the defining autoantibody","Anti-U1-RNP antibody titer — confirms diagnosis when present in high titer, typically without anti-Sm or anti-dsDNA","Creatine kinase and aldolase — assess for accompanying myositis","Pulmonary function tests with diffusion capacity (DLCO) — detect early interstitial lung disease or pulmonary vascular involvement","Transthoracic echocardiogram — screen for pulmonary arterial hypertension, a major prognostic driver","High-resolution chest CT — evaluate for interstitial lung disease","Esophageal manometry or barium swallow — assess for dysmotility seen in sclerodermoid overlap","Urinalysis and renal function panel — screen for renal involvement, which is less common but clinically important"],"redFlags":["New dyspnea on exertion or signs of right heart strain suggesting evolving pulmonary arterial hypertension","Rapidly progressive proteinuria, hematuria, or rising creatinine suggesting renal involvement","Severe digital ischemia or ulceration from vasculopathy","New neurologic deficits or severe headache raising concern for CNS or cerebrovascular involvement","Acute severe muscle weakness or dysphagia suggesting active myositis with aspiration risk"],"prognosis":"Overall prognosis is generally better than diffuse systemic sclerosis but variable, with outcomes largely determined by the degree of pulmonary vascular and interstitial lung involvement. Pulmonary arterial hypertension is the most significant predictor of mortality, making regular cardiopulmonary surveillance essential.","pitfalls":["Attributing puffy hands and Raynaud phenomenon solely to early scleroderma without checking anti-U1-RNP and considering MCTD.","Failing to screen asymptomatic patients periodically for pulmonary arterial hypertension, the dominant cause of mortality.","Assuming a single high anti-U1-RNP titer alone confirms diagnosis without correlating with the required overlapping clinical features.","Overlooking that clinical features can evolve over years, sometimes prompting reclassification into a more defined connective tissue disease."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Mixed Connective Tissue Disease and Overlap Syndromes (Section on Disorders of Immune-Mediated Injury)"],"crossLinks":["systemic_sclerosis"]},"MELIOIDOSIS":{"name":"Melioidosis","system":"Infectious","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["burkholderia pseudomallei","whitmore","whitmore's disease","pseudoglanders","nightcliff gardener's disease","paddy field disease","b. pseudomallei infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by the gram-negative soil and water saprophyte Burkholderia pseudomallei, endemic to Southeast Asia and northern Australia, with expanding recognition in South Asia, including India","Acquisition is via percutaneous inoculation, inhalation, or ingestion of contaminated soil/water, especially during monsoon/rainy season and after flooding","Diabetes mellitus is the dominant risk factor; other predispositions include chronic kidney disease, alcohol excess, chronic lung disease, thalassemia, and immunosuppression","Clinical spectrum ranges from localized cutaneous/subcutaneous infection to pneumonia (most common presentation) to overwhelming septicemia with multiple abscesses","Classically described as 'the great mimicker' because it can resemble tuberculosis radiographically (cavitary upper-lobe disease) and can produce abscesses in almost any organ, including spleen, liver, and prostate","Can present as an acute fulminant illness or as a chronic, indolent infection persisting for months to years, and may reactivate after decades of latency ('Vietnamese time bomb')","Requires laboratory alert to the microbiology lab, since B. pseudomallei can be mistaken for a contaminant or misidentified as another Burkholderia/Pseudomonas species on automated systems","Treatment is biphasic: an initial intensive intravenous phase followed by a prolonged oral eradication phase, reflecting the organism's propensity to relapse if therapy is inadequate"],"pathophysiology":"Burkholderia pseudomallei is a motile, aerobic, gram-negative bacillus that survives in soil and surface water in endemic tropical regions and enters the host through skin breaks, inhalation of aerosols, or ingestion. It is a facultative intracellular organism capable of escaping the phagosome, moving between cells via actin-based motility, and inducing multinucleated giant cell formation, which contributes to its capacity for chronic latency and later reactivation. Hematogenous dissemination from a primary site produces multifocal abscesses, and impaired cell-mediated and neutrophil function, as occurs in diabetes, markedly increases susceptibility to severe and disseminated disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary or disseminated tuberculosis","Community-acquired or nosocomial gram-negative sepsis/pneumonia","Staphylococcal bacteremia with metastatic abscesses","Fungal infection (histoplasmosis, coccidioidomycosis) in returning travelers","Enteric fever or other systemic febrile illness in returning travelers","Actinomycosis or nocardiosis"],"infectionMimics":["Pulmonary tuberculosis","Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia with septic emboli","Nocardiosis","Actinomycosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Metastatic malignancy with multiple organ lesions","Vasculitis with pulmonary cavitation","Lymphoma with splenic/hepatic lesions"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood, sputum, urine, and wound/abscess cultures with explicit lab alert — definitive diagnosis rests on culture isolation of B. pseudomallei","Contrast CT of chest/abdomen — to detect occult multifocal abscesses (splenic, hepatic, prostatic, renal)","Chest radiography — may show cavitary upper-lobe infiltrates mimicking tuberculosis","Serology (indirect hemagglutination) — supportive but limited by background seropositivity in endemic areas, so not diagnostic alone","HbA1c/fasting glucose — screen for underlying diabetes mellitus, the principal risk factor","Renal function tests — assess for chronic kidney disease as a predisposing condition and to guide drug dosing considerations","Blood cultures for sepsis workup — essential given the high risk of bacteremic dissemination","MRI or CT brain in patients with neurologic signs — to detect rare but described CNS melioidosis (brainstem encephalitis)"],"redFlags":["Septic shock or multiorgan dysfunction at presentation, which carries high mortality if treatment is delayed","Diabetic patient with fever and pneumonia or unexplained abscesses returning from or residing in an endemic region","Evidence of multifocal abscesses (splenic, hepatic, prostatic) suggesting disseminated disease","Neurologic signs suggesting brainstem encephalitis, a recognized severe complication","Delayed or incorrect microbial identification (mistaken for a contaminant) in a patient with sepsis and tropical exposure history","Relapse or recrudescence of symptoms after apparent treatment response, suggesting inadequate eradication-phase therapy"],"prognosis":"Untreated septicemic melioidosis has historically carried very high mortality, and even with modern intensive-phase antimicrobial therapy, severe disseminated or septic shock presentations remain associated with substantial mortality, particularly in patients with diabetes or other comorbidities. With appropriate biphasic treatment and adequate follow-up, localized and non-septicemic disease generally carries a favorable outcome, though relapse can occur if eradication therapy is incomplete.","pitfalls":["Dismissing B. pseudomallei growth in culture as an environmental contaminant, leading to missed or delayed diagnosis","Attributing cavitary lung disease to tuberculosis without considering melioidosis in endemic-exposure patients, delaying appropriate therapy","Underestimating the need for prolonged eradication-phase oral therapy, which risks relapse if truncated","Failing to actively search for occult abscesses (spleen, liver, prostate) once bacteremia or sepsis is confirmed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Melioidosis and Glanders (Burkholderia pseudomallei and B. mallei infections)"],"crossLinks":[]},"MEMBRANOUS_NEPHROPATHY":{"name":"Membranous nephropathy","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["membranous nephropathy","MN","membranous glomerulonephritis","membranous glomerulopathy","epimembranous nephropathy","PLA2R-associated nephropathy","idiopathic membranous nephropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most common cause of primary nephrotic syndrome in non-diabetic White adults, typically presenting with heavy proteinuria and edema but preserved renal function early on.","About 70-80% of primary (idiopathic) cases are associated with circulating anti-PLA2R antibodies; a minority have anti-THSD7A antibodies instead.","Always screen for an underlying secondary cause (malignancy, autoimmune disease, infection, or drug exposure) before labeling disease as primary/idiopathic, especially in patients over 50-60 years.","Follows a 'rule of thirds' natural history in untreated primary disease: roughly one-third spontaneously remit, one-third have persistent proteinuria with stable function, and one-third progress to kidney failure over years.","Renal vein thrombosis and other thromboembolic events are disproportionately common because of the profound hypercoagulable state associated with heavy proteinuria and urinary loss of anticoagulant proteins.","Microscopic hematuria may be present, but heavy nephritic-range findings (red cell casts, marked hematuria) suggest an alternative or superimposed diagnosis.","Immunofluorescence classically shows granular capillary loop deposits of IgG (often IgG4-dominant in primary disease) and C3, with electron microscopy showing subepithelial deposits and spike formation on the basement membrane.","Response to therapy is slow (months), so treatment decisions and reassessment of remission should be based on sustained trends in proteinuria rather than short-term fluctuations."],"pathophysiology":"Membranous nephropathy results from in-situ formation of immune complexes along the subepithelial aspect of the glomerular basement membrane, most often triggered by autoantibodies against a native podocyte antigen (chiefly PLA2R, less commonly THSD7A or other targets) or against a planted exogenous/tumor antigen in secondary forms. Complement activation at the site of deposits leads to podocyte injury, cytoskeletal disruption, and a marked increase in glomerular capillary wall permeability to protein without significant inflammatory cell infiltration. Ongoing antigen-antibody deposition drives basement membrane thickening and the characteristic 'spike and dome' appearance seen on silver-stained biopsy over time. This process explains the disease's predominantly nephrotic (rather than nephritic) presentation.","additionalDifferentials":["Minimal change disease","Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","Diabetic nephropathy","Amyloidosis (AL or AA)","Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","Lupus nephritis (class V)"],"infectionMimics":["Hepatitis B virus-associated membranous nephropathy","Hepatitis C virus-associated glomerulonephritis","Syphilis-associated membranous nephropathy","Chronic malaria or schistosomiasis-associated glomerulopathy"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Solid organ malignancy-associated (paraneoplastic) membranous nephropathy","Drug-induced membranous nephropathy (e.g., certain NSAIDs or other implicated agents)","Systemic lupus erythematosus (class V lupus nephritis)","Sarcoidosis-associated glomerulopathy","Autoimmune thyroiditis-associated nephropathy"],"additionalInvestigations":["24-hour urine protein or spot urine protein-to-creatinine ratio — quantify and monitor nephrotic-range proteinuria","Serum anti-PLA2R antibody titer — confirms primary autoimmune membranous nephropathy and aids in monitoring immunologic activity","Renal biopsy with light microscopy, immunofluorescence, and electron microscopy — establishes diagnosis and stages disease","Age-appropriate malignancy screening — excludes paraneoplastic secondary membranous nephropathy","Hepatitis B and C serologies, syphilis serology, ANA/anti-dsDNA, and complement levels — screen for secondary infectious or autoimmune causes","Serum albumin and lipid panel — assess severity of nephrotic syndrome and cardiovascular risk","Renal function tests (creatinine, eGFR) trended over time — monitor for progression","Doppler imaging or cross-sectional imaging of renal veins if clinical suspicion — evaluate for renal vein thrombosis in severe nephrotic syndrome"],"redFlags":["Sudden worsening of renal function, flank pain, or asymmetric limb swelling suggesting renal vein thrombosis or other thromboembolic event","Rapidly progressive decline in eGFR or emergence of an active urinary sediment suggesting a superimposed crescentic or proliferative process","Severe nephrotic syndrome with albumin markedly low, raising risk of thrombosis, infection, and volume complications requiring closer monitoring","New signs pointing to occult malignancy or systemic autoimmune disease that would change management from primary to secondary disease pathway","Anasarca with respiratory compromise or signs of pulmonary embolism"],"prognosis":"Renal outcomes are highly variable, with roughly equal proportions of patients undergoing spontaneous remission, persistent stable proteinuria, or progression to kidney failure over years if untreated; lower proteinuria, preserved renal function at diagnosis, and declining PLA2R antibody titers portend a better outcome, while persistent heavy proteinuria and reduced eGFR predict progression.","pitfalls":["Assuming all membranous nephropathy is primary/idiopathic without adequately excluding secondary causes, particularly malignancy in older patients","Making treatment decisions based on a single proteinuria measurement rather than sustained trends, given the naturally fluctuating course and slow treatment response","Overlooking the markedly elevated thromboembolic risk and failing to consider prophylactic anticoagulation in severe nephrotic syndrome","Misinterpreting a negative anti-PLA2R antibody test as excluding membranous nephropathy, since a meaningful subset of primary cases are antibody-negative or have other target antigens"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Glomerular Diseases chapter, section on Membranous Nephropathy"],"crossLinks":["MINIMAL_CHANGE_DISEASE"]},"MICROSCOPIC_COLITIS":{"name":"Microscopic colitis","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["microscopic colitis","collagenous colitis","lymphocytic colitis","MC","chronic watery diarrhea syndrome","minimal change colitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic presentation is chronic, non-bloody, watery diarrhea in an older adult (often 60s-70s) with a grossly and endoscopically normal-appearing colon","Female predominance is marked, especially for the collagenous subtype","Strongly associated with NSAID use, proton pump inhibitors, SSRIs, and statins — a careful drug history is essential","Frequently coexists with autoimmune conditions such as celiac disease, autoimmune thyroiditis, and other connective tissue or rheumatologic disorders","Diagnosis requires random biopsies from multiple colonic segments even when mucosa looks endoscopically normal, since gross findings are typically absent","Two histologic subtypes — collagenous (thickened subepithelial collagen band) and lymphocytic (increased intraepithelial lymphocytes) — share similar clinical behavior and management","Nocturnal diarrhea and fecal urgency/incontinence are common and can significantly impair quality of life despite the benign histology","Weight loss and abdominal pain can occur but are typically milder than in classic inflammatory bowel disease"],"pathophysiology":"Microscopic colitis is thought to result from a dysregulated mucosal immune response to luminal antigens (including bile acids, bacterial products, and certain drugs) in a genetically or immunologically susceptible host, producing intraepithelial lymphocytic infiltration with or without subepithelial collagen deposition. This impairs colonic epithelial barrier function and fluid/electrolyte absorption, producing a secretory-type watery diarrhea without gross mucosal ulceration. The strong drug and autoimmune associations support an immune-mediated rather than structural mechanism.","additionalDifferentials":["Bile acid diarrhea/malabsorption","Celiac disease","Irritable bowel syndrome (diarrhea-predominant)","Inflammatory bowel disease (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis)","Drug-induced diarrhea (independent of microscopic colitis itself)","Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth"],"infectionMimics":["Chronic Clostridioides difficile infection","Giardiasis","Chronic bacterial or parasitic enterocolitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Diarrhea-predominant irritable bowel syndrome","Bile acid malabsorption","Celiac disease","Endocrine causes of diarrhea (e.g., hyperthyroidism, neuroendocrine tumors)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Colonoscopy with random biopsies from multiple colonic segments — required for histologic diagnosis since mucosa often appears normal","Histopathology — distinguishes collagenous (thickened collagen band) from lymphocytic (intraepithelial lymphocytosis) subtype","Celiac serology (tissue transglutaminase IgA) — screens for coexisting celiac disease","Thyroid function tests — screens for associated autoimmune thyroid disease","Stool studies (culture, C. difficile testing, ova and parasites) — excludes infectious causes of chronic diarrhea","Medication review — identifies causative drugs such as NSAIDs, PPIs, SSRIs, and statins","Fecal calprotectin — typically normal or only mildly elevated, helps differentiate from classic IBD","SeHCAT or serum C4/FGF-19 testing where available — evaluates for concurrent bile acid diarrhea"],"redFlags":["Rectal bleeding, anemia, or weight loss suggesting an alternative or coexisting diagnosis rather than uncomplicated microscopic colitis","Severe dehydration or electrolyte disturbance from high-volume diarrhea","Failure to respond to standard therapy — warrants reassessment for IBD, celiac disease, or bile acid diarrhea","New abdominal pain, fever, or peritoneal signs, which are atypical and require evaluation for another process","Persistent symptoms despite discontinuation of an implicated drug"],"prognosis":"Microscopic colitis is a benign, non-progressive condition that does not increase colorectal cancer risk and often responds well to drug withdrawal or medical therapy, though relapses are common after treatment discontinuation and a subset of patients follow a chronic intermittent course.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal-appearing colonoscopy excludes colitis — biopsies are mandatory since gross findings are usually absent","Failing to obtain biopsies from multiple segments, as histologic changes can be patchy or segment-specific","Overlooking a causative medication and treating chronically without attempting drug discontinuation","Missing coexisting celiac disease or bile acid diarrhea, which can perpetuate symptoms despite colitis-directed therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Inflammatory Bowel Disease and related colitides (Gastroenterology section, diarrhea and colitis topics)"],"crossLinks":["CELIAC_DISEASE","inflammatory_bowel_disease"]},"MINIMAL_CHANGE_DISEASE":{"name":"Minimal change disease","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["minimal change","MCD","minimal change nephropathy","nil disease","lipoid nephrosis","MCNS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children (peak age 2-6 years) but also occurs in adults, sometimes elderly patients.","Presents with abrupt-onset heavy proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, and edema; renal function and blood pressure are typically preserved at onset.","Light microscopy is essentially normal ('minimal change'); diagnosis relies on electron microscopy showing diffuse podocyte foot-process effacement.","Excellent response to corticosteroids is characteristic and considered almost diagnostic in children; failure to respond should prompt reconsideration of the diagnosis.","Frequent relapses and steroid dependence are common, often requiring steroid-sparing agents over time.","Can be triggered or associated with recent upper respiratory infection, atopy/allergy, immunizations, NSAID use, or (in adults) underlying lymphoproliferative disease (especially Hodgkin lymphoma).","Selective proteinuria (predominantly albumin, low IgG) on urine protein electrophoresis supports the diagnosis.","Thromboembolic risk and increased infection susceptibility parallel the severity of nephrotic syndrome regardless of underlying cause."],"pathophysiology":"Minimal change disease is thought to result from a circulating permeability factor or T-cell/immune dysregulation that injures podocytes, causing effacement of their foot processes and loss of the glomerular filtration barrier's charge/size selectivity without structural glomerular damage visible by light microscopy. This leads to massive selective loss of albumin into the urine, producing hypoalbuminemia, decreased plasma oncotic pressure, and edema, along with compensatory hepatic lipoprotein synthesis causing hyperlipidemia. The glomerular basement membrane and capillary architecture otherwise remain intact, distinguishing it from other primary glomerulopathies.","additionalDifferentials":["Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS)","Membranous nephropathy","Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","Diabetic nephropathy","IgA nephropathy with heavy proteinuria","Amyloidosis or other infiltrative nephropathies"],"infectionMimics":["Post-infectious glomerulonephritis","Hepatitis B/C-associated glomerulonephritis","HIV-associated nephropathy"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["NSAID-induced acute interstitial nephritis with nephrotic-range proteinuria","Paraneoplastic nephrotic syndrome (Hodgkin lymphoma, other lymphoproliferative disorders)","Diabetic nephropathy","Amyloidosis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis with microscopy — assess for selective proteinuria and absence of significant hematuria or cellular casts","24-hour urine protein or spot urine protein-to-creatinine ratio — quantify nephrotic-range proteinuria","Serum albumin and lipid panel — confirm hypoalbuminemia and hyperlipidemia typical of nephrotic syndrome","Renal function panel (creatinine, eGFR) — establish baseline and monitor for atypical renal impairment","Renal biopsy with electron microscopy — required in adults or steroid-resistant cases to confirm diagnosis and exclude FSGS/other glomerulopathies","Serum complement levels — typically normal in minimal change disease, helps exclude complement-mediated glomerulonephritides","Age-appropriate malignancy screening in adults — evaluate for underlying lymphoproliferative disease, especially if steroid-resistant","Coagulation studies/consideration of thromboembolic risk — nephrotic syndrome predisposes to venous thromboembolism"],"redFlags":["Steroid resistance or failure to achieve remission — reconsider diagnosis (likely FSGS) and pursue biopsy","Significant hematuria, hypertension, or renal impairment at presentation — atypical for minimal change disease, suggests alternative diagnosis","Signs of thromboembolism (limb swelling, chest pain, dyspnea) given hypercoagulable state of nephrotic syndrome","Severe hypovolemia or acute kidney injury from aggressive diuresis in the setting of low oncotic pressure","Signs of serious infection (peritonitis, cellulitis, sepsis) due to functional hypogammaglobulinemia and immunosuppressive therapy","New systemic symptoms (weight loss, lymphadenopathy, night sweats) in adults raising concern for occult malignancy"],"prognosis":"Minimal change disease has an excellent overall prognosis, with the large majority of children and most adults achieving remission with corticosteroid therapy and rarely progressing to chronic kidney disease. However, relapses are common and some patients become steroid-dependent or frequently relapsing, necessitating steroid-sparing immunosuppressive strategies.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal light microscopy biopsy excludes glomerular disease without electron microscopy to assess podocyte foot processes","Delaying biopsy in adults presenting with nephrotic syndrome, where minimal change disease is less common and other etiologies must be excluded","Misclassifying steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome as minimal change disease when it more likely represents FSGS or another glomerulopathy","Overlooking secondary causes (drugs, malignancy) in adult-onset minimal change disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Glomerular Diseases chapter"],"crossLinks":["FSGS"]},"PNH":{"name":"Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria","system":"Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["paroxysmal nocturnal","PNH","paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","Marchiafava-Micheli syndrome","PIGA mutation clone","complement-mediated hemolytic anemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Acquired clonal stem-cell disorder from a somatic PIGA gene mutation, leading to loss of GPI-anchored surface proteins (including CD55 and CD59) that normally protect red cells from complement attack.","Classic triad is intravascular hemolysis, marrow failure/cytopenias, and a marked thrombotic tendency, though most patients present with only one or two of these features.","Morning hemoglobinuria is a well-known feature but is actually seen in only a minority of patients; hemolysis is often chronic and low-grade rather than dramatically episodic.","Thrombosis is the leading cause of death and characteristically occurs at unusual sites — hepatic (Budd-Chiari), portal, mesenteric, cerebral (dural sinus), and dermal veins.","Consider PNH in any patient with unexplained hemolysis plus cytopenia, or with venous thrombosis at an atypical site, especially with a normal or low platelet count.","PNH clones frequently coexist with, precede, or follow aplastic anemia and myelodysplastic syndrome, reflecting a shared marrow-failure background.","Smooth muscle dystonia (esophageal spasm, erectile dysfunction, abdominal pain) results from free-hemoglobin-mediated nitric oxide scavenging rather than mechanical obstruction.","Flow cytometry for GPI-anchored proteins (e.g., FLAER, CD55/CD59) on granulocytes and red cells is the diagnostic test of choice, replacing older Ham's and sucrose lysis tests."],"pathophysiology":"PNH arises from an acquired somatic mutation in the X-linked PIGA gene within a hematopoietic stem cell, impairing synthesis of the glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) anchor required to display several surface proteins. Loss of the complement regulators CD55 (decay-accelerating factor) and CD59 (membrane inhibitor of reactive lysis) leaves the abnormal red cell clone vulnerable to unchecked complement-mediated intravascular lysis. The clonal population expands, often in the setting of an immune-mediated marrow injury that gives PIGA-mutant cells a relative survival advantage, producing the combination of hemolysis, cytopenias, and a pro-thrombotic state linked to platelet and microparticle complement activation.","additionalDifferentials":["Autoimmune hemolytic anemia (warm or cold antibody type)","Aplastic anemia","Myelodysplastic syndrome","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura / atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome","Hereditary spherocytosis or other membrane defects","G6PD deficiency and other hemolytic enzymopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Autoimmune hemolytic anemia","Microangiopathic hemolytic anemias (TTP/HUS, DIC)","Aplastic anemia without a PNH clone","Budd-Chiari syndrome from other hypercoagulable states","Cold agglutinin disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["Flow cytometry for GPI-anchored proteins (FLAER, CD55, CD59) on granulocytes and erythrocytes — confirms and sizes the PNH clone","Complete blood count with reticulocyte count — documents hemolysis and identifies coexisting cytopenias","LDH, indirect bilirubin, haptoglobin — supports intravascular hemolysis","Urinalysis for hemoglobinuria and urinary hemosiderin — evidence of chronic intravascular hemolysis","Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test — should be negative, helping exclude autoimmune hemolysis","Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy with cytogenetics — assesses for underlying aplastic anemia or MDS","Iron studies — chronic hemoglobinuria can cause iron deficiency despite hemolysis","Imaging (Doppler ultrasound, CT/MR venography) if thrombosis at an atypical site is suspected"],"redFlags":["Sudden abdominal pain, ascites, or hepatomegaly suggesting Budd-Chiari or mesenteric venous thrombosis","New neurologic deficit or severe headache raising concern for cerebral or dural venous sinus thrombosis","Rapidly worsening cytopenias suggesting evolution to aplastic anemia or MDS/leukemia","Severe abdominal or chest pain with hemolysis flare, which may signal smooth-muscle dystonia or thrombotic event","Pregnancy in a known PNH patient, which carries substantially increased maternal thrombotic and fetal risk","Acute kidney injury from massive hemoglobinuria or renal vein thrombosis"],"prognosis":"Historically PNH carried significant mortality, largely from thrombosis and marrow failure, but complement-inhibitor therapy has markedly improved survival and quality of life. Long-term outcome also depends on clone size and any associated bone marrow failure syndrome, with a small risk of progression to MDS or acute leukemia over time.","pitfalls":["Assuming hemoglobinuria must be present and grossly visible; many patients have only subclinical or intermittent hemolysis","Missing the diagnosis in a patient with unexplained thrombosis at an atypical site because cytopenias are mild or absent","Relying on outdated Ham's/sucrose lysis tests instead of flow cytometry, which is far more sensitive and specific","Overlooking a coexisting or antecedent marrow failure syndrome (aplastic anemia, MDS) when a PNH clone is found"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Hemolytic Anemias and Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria"],"crossLinks":[]},"POEMS_SYNDROME":{"name":"POEMS syndrome","system":"Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["poems","osteosclerotic myeloma","Crow-Fukase syndrome","Takatsuki syndrome","PEP syndrome","polyneuropathy organomegaly endocrinopathy M-protein skin changes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rare paraneoplastic disorder driven by an underlying clonal plasma cell process, almost always with lambda light-chain restriction.","Peripheral polyneuropathy is typically the presenting and most disabling feature — usually a symmetric, ascending, demyelinating sensorimotor pattern resembling CIDP.","Suspect POEMS when 'CIDP' fails to respond to standard immunotherapy, especially if accompanied by systemic features.","Sclerotic bone lesions (solitary or multiple) are common and often visible on skeletal survey or CT even without lytic disease typical of myeloma.","Papilledema, extravascular volume overload (ascites, edema, effusions), and thrombocytosis/erythrocytosis are distinctive clues that separate POEMS from typical myeloma or CIDP.","Markedly elevated serum VEGF is a hallmark biomarker reflecting disease activity.","Endocrinopathies (commonly hypogonadism, hypothyroidism, or glucose intolerance) are frequent but nonspecific, and should not be over-interpreted alone.","Skin changes (hyperpigmentation, thickening, hypertrichosis, glomeruloid hemangiomas) support the diagnosis when present alongside neuropathy."],"pathophysiology":"POEMS syndrome arises from an underlying monoclonal, lambda-restricted plasma cell disorder that drives systemic overproduction of proinflammatory and proangiogenic cytokines, most notably vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF). This cytokine excess is thought to underlie the vascular, neurologic, and organ-based manifestations rather than direct tumor infiltration, distinguishing the disease from classic multiple myeloma. The resulting syndrome combines a chronic demyelinating polyneuropathy with multisystem involvement including bone, endocrine, dermatologic, and extravascular fluid abnormalities.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP) without associated plasma cell disorder","Multiple myeloma with peripheral neuropathy","Waldenström macroglobulinemia / IgM-related neuropathy","Castleman disease (may overlap with or occur alongside POEMS)","Amyloidosis (AL) with neuropathy and organomegaly","Diabetic or other systemic causes of sensorimotor polyneuropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["CIDP (idiopathic)","Amyloid neuropathy","Diabetic polyneuropathy with unrelated monoclonal gammopathy (MGUS)","Castleman disease without POEMS features","Hypothyroid-associated neuropathy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum protein electrophoresis with immunofixation — detects and characterizes the monoclonal (typically lambda) protein","Serum free light chain ratio — supports clonality assessment","Serum VEGF level — supports diagnosis and can track disease activity","Skeletal survey and/or whole-body imaging (CT or PET) — identifies characteristic sclerotic or mixed bone lesions","Nerve conduction studies/EMG — confirms demyelinating polyneuropathy pattern","Bone marrow biopsy — quantifies clonal plasma cells and excludes overt myeloma","Endocrine panel (thyroid, gonadal, adrenal, glucose) — screens for associated endocrinopathies","Echocardiography and pulmonary function testing — assesses for pulmonary hypertension and extravascular volume overload"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive neuropathy with respiratory or bulbar involvement","New papilledema suggesting raised intracranial pressure","Signs of extravascular volume overload (ascites, pleural effusion, peripheral edema) indicating advancing systemic disease","Evidence of pulmonary hypertension or thrombotic events, which portend higher morbidity","Rapid rise in serum VEGF or new osteolytic transformation suggesting progression or evolution toward myeloma"],"prognosis":"With clone-directed therapy, many patients achieve meaningful and durable improvement in neuropathy and systemic features, and overall survival is generally more favorable than multiple myeloma. However, residual neurologic deficits are common, and relapse or progression can occur, warranting long-term surveillance of the monoclonal marker and VEGF level.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing as idiopathic CIDP and treating with standard immunotherapy (e.g., IVIG/plasma exchange) alone, which is typically ineffective since the underlying plasma cell clone is not addressed","Overlooking sclerotic bone lesions on imaging because they lack the classic lytic appearance of multiple myeloma","Failing to check serum VEGF or overinterpreting an isolated endocrinopathy without considering the full multisystem picture","Anchoring on a single feature (e.g., organomegaly) rather than recognizing the required combination of core diagnostic criteria"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Plasma Cell Disorders chapter (POEMS syndrome/osteosclerotic myeloma section)"],"crossLinks":["CIDP","CASTLEMAN_DISEASE","amyloidosis"]},"PRIMARY_ALDOSTERONISM":{"name":"Primary aldosteronism (Conn syndrome)","system":"Endocrinology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["aldosteronism","conn syndrome","hyperaldosteronism","primary hyperaldosteronism","aldosterone-producing adenoma","bilateral adrenal hyperplasia","idiopathic hyperaldosteronism","APA","PA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Consider in any hypertensive patient with spontaneous or diuretic-induced hypokalemia, treatment-resistant hypertension, or an incidentally found adrenal mass","Most patients are normokalemic at presentation; hypokalemia is present in only a minority, so a normal potassium does not exclude the diagnosis","Plasma aldosterone-to-renin ratio is the preferred screening test and should be checked before starting mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists","Screening should target patients with sustained BP above standard thresholds, resistant hypertension, hypertension with an adrenal incidentaloma, or hypertension with sleep apnea or a family history of early-onset hypertension/stroke","Unilateral aldosterone-producing adenoma and bilateral adrenal hyperplasia are the two dominant subtypes and require different management (surgery versus mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist)","Adrenal vein sampling is generally required to distinguish unilateral from bilateral disease before considering surgery, since imaging alone is unreliable in this regard","Correcting the underlying aldosterone excess improves blood pressure control and reduces the excess cardiovascular and renal risk associated with this condition beyond what is expected from hypertension alone"],"pathophysiology":"Autonomous, renin-independent overproduction of aldosterone from one or both adrenal glands leads to sodium retention, potassium and hydrogen ion wasting, and volume-mediated hypertension. The excess mineralocorticoid activity suppresses plasma renin and can produce hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, though many patients remain normokalemic. Beyond hemodynamic effects, chronic aldosterone excess produces direct pro-fibrotic and pro-inflammatory effects on the heart, vasculature, and kidney that account for disproportionate target-organ damage relative to blood pressure alone.","additionalDifferentials":["Essential (primary) hypertension","Renovascular hypertension","Pheochromocytoma/paraganglioma","Cushing syndrome","Renin-secreting tumor","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia variants (11-beta-hydroxylase or 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Licorice or chewing-tobacco-induced pseudohyperaldosteronism (apparent mineralocorticoid excess)","Liddle syndrome","Diuretic-induced hypokalemia with secondary hyperreninemic hyperaldosteronism","Renal artery stenosis with secondary hyperaldosteronism","Chronic laxative abuse or bulimia causing hypokalemia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma aldosterone-to-renin ratio — first-line screening test, best done after correcting hypokalemia and adjusting interfering medications","Confirmatory suppression testing (e.g., oral sodium loading, saline infusion, or fludrocortisone suppression) — establishes autonomous aldosterone secretion when screening is positive","Adrenal CT — identifies adrenal masses and helps exclude adrenocortical carcinoma, but cannot reliably lateralize functional source","Adrenal vein sampling — gold standard for lateralizing unilateral versus bilateral aldosterone hypersecretion prior to surgical decision-making","Serum potassium and bicarbonate — assess for hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, though normal values do not exclude disease","Plasma renin activity or direct renin concentration — confirms renin suppression characteristic of primary aldosteronism","Genetic testing — considered in early-onset hypertension or strong family history to evaluate for familial hyperaldosteronism syndromes","24-hour urinary aldosterone excretion — used as part of some confirmatory protocols"],"redFlags":["Severe or refractory hypokalemia with muscle weakness, cramps, or cardiac arrhythmia","Resistant hypertension requiring three or more antihypertensive agents including a diuretic","Adrenal incidentaloma with hypertension or hypokalemia raising concern for functioning adenoma or carcinoma","Very young age of hypertension onset or hypertensive stroke in a young relative, suggesting a familial syndrome","Large or irregular adrenal mass on imaging raising concern for adrenocortical carcinoma"],"prognosis":"With correct subtype classification, unilateral disease treated surgically often achieves cure or major improvement of hypertension and hypokalemia, while bilateral disease managed medically with mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists generally achieves good blood pressure control. Untreated or unrecognized disease carries excess risk of cardiovascular events, stroke, and chronic kidney disease compared with blood-pressure-matched essential hypertension.","pitfalls":["Failing to correct hypokalemia and withdraw interfering drugs (mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists, high-dose diuretics) before aldosterone-to-renin ratio testing, leading to false results","Relying on adrenal CT alone to decide unilateral versus bilateral disease instead of adrenal vein sampling, risking inappropriate or missed surgery","Assuming normokalemia excludes the diagnosis and therefore not screening appropriately hypertensive patients","Misattributing resistant hypertension to poor adherence or essential hypertension without screening for secondary causes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Disorders of the Adrenal Cortex (Primary Aldosteronism)"],"crossLinks":["pheochromocytoma"]},"PRIMARY_BILIARY_CHOLANGITIS":{"name":"Primary biliary cholangitis","system":"Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["primary biliary","PBC","primary biliary cirrhosis","chronic non-suppurative destructive cholangitis","autoimmune cholangitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Consider in middle-aged women with fatigue, pruritus, and an isolated cholestatic (alkaline phosphatase-predominant) liver panel","Pruritus often precedes jaundice by months to years and may be the presenting complaint","Antimitochondrial antibody (AMA) positivity at meaningful titer is highly specific and often clinches the diagnosis without biopsy","Associated with other autoimmune conditions — Sjögren syndrome, autoimmune thyroiditis, scleroderma/CREST, celiac disease","Hyperlipidemia is common but does not appear to confer the usual cardiovascular risk seen in the general population","Fat-soluble vitamin deficiency and metabolic bone disease (osteoporosis) develop as cholestasis progresses","AMA-negative patients with a compatible clinical/biochemical picture still warrant workup as 'AMA-negative PBC'"],"pathophysiology":"PBC is an autoimmune, T-cell-mediated process that progressively destroys small and medium intrahepatic bile ducts, producing chronic cholestasis. Ongoing bile duct injury leads to periportal inflammation, fibrosis, and eventually biliary-type cirrhosis if untreated. Genetic susceptibility combined with environmental triggers is thought to break tolerance to mitochondrial autoantigens, driving the characteristic antimitochondrial antibody response.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary sclerosing cholangitis","Drug-induced cholestatic liver injury","Autoimmune hepatitis (including PBC-AIH overlap syndrome)","Extrahepatic biliary obstruction (choledocholithiasis, stricture, malignancy)","Sarcoidosis with hepatic involvement","Idiopathic adulthood ductopenia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Drug-induced cholestasis","Primary sclerosing cholangitis","Autoimmune hepatitis","Hepatic sarcoidosis","Mechanical biliary obstruction"],"additionalInvestigations":["Alkaline phosphatase and GGT — establish and quantify cholestatic pattern","Antimitochondrial antibody (AMA) — key serologic marker, positive in most cases","Antinuclear antibody (ANA), including PBC-specific patterns (anti-gp210, anti-sp100) — useful when AMA is negative","Serum IgM — characteristically elevated in PBC","Abdominal ultrasound or cross-sectional imaging — exclude extrahepatic biliary obstruction","Liver biopsy — confirms diagnosis in AMA-negative cases and stages disease when diagnosis is uncertain","Transient elastography (FibroScan) or other noninvasive fibrosis assessment — staging and monitoring","Fat-soluble vitamin levels and bone density (DEXA) — assess complications of chronic cholestasis"],"redFlags":["Development of jaundice, ascites, variceal bleeding, or encephalopathy signaling decompensated cirrhosis","Inadequate biochemical response to first-line therapy — warrants consideration of second-line agents and closer surveillance","New or worsening features suggesting overlap with autoimmune hepatitis","Rising alpha-fetoprotein or new liver lesion — evaluate for hepatocellular carcinoma once cirrhosis is established","Severe intractable pruritus refractory to standard measures","Symptomatic metabolic bone disease or fragility fracture"],"prognosis":"Course is highly variable; many patients with early-stage disease and a good biochemical response to therapy have a life expectancy similar to the general population, while incomplete responders or those presenting with advanced fibrosis progress toward cirrhosis and its complications over years.","pitfalls":["Attributing isolated cholestatic labs and pruritus in an older woman to cholelithiasis or aging skin without checking AMA","Assuming a negative AMA excludes PBC — a subset of patients are AMA-negative and require biopsy or specific autoantibodies","Missing an overlap syndrome by not screening for autoimmune hepatitis features when transaminases are disproportionately elevated","Overlooking screening for associated autoimmune diseases and osteoporosis once diagnosis is established"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Primary Biliary Cholangitis / Approach to the Patient with Liver Disease"],"crossLinks":["PRIMARY_SCLEROSING_CHOLANGITIS","AUTOIMMUNE_HEPATITIS"]},"PRIMARY_SCLEROSING_CHOLANGITIS":{"name":"Primary sclerosing cholangitis","system":"Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sclerosing cholangitis","PSC","fibro-obliterative cholangitis","pericholangitis","IBD-associated cholangiopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Chronic cholestatic disease marked by progressive fibrosing inflammation and stricturing of intra- and/or extrahepatic bile ducts","Strongly linked to inflammatory bowel disease, particularly ulcerative colitis; colitis may be quiescent or diagnosed after PSC","Predominantly affects young to middle-aged men; often discovered incidentally via persistently elevated alkaline phosphatase","Fluctuating cholestatic pattern (elevated alkaline phosphatase and GGT) is typical, with variable bilirubin depending on stricture burden","Cholangiography (MRCP preferred over ERCP for initial diagnosis) shows characteristic multifocal strictures alternating with normal or dilated segments (beaded appearance)","Any patient with IBD and unexplained cholestatic liver tests should be evaluated for PSC","Carries increased lifetime risk of cholangiocarcinoma, gallbladder cancer, and colorectal cancer (in those with concurrent colitis)","No medical therapy has been proven to alter the natural history; liver transplantation is the only definitive treatment for advanced disease"],"pathophysiology":"PSC is thought to result from an immune-mediated process in genetically susceptible individuals, possibly triggered by altered gut permeability and microbial translocation related to colonic inflammation, leading to periductal inflammation and concentric fibrosis of bile ducts. Progressive fibro-obliteration produces multifocal strictures with intervening dilations, eventually causing biliary obstruction, secondary bacterial cholangitis, and biliary cirrhosis. The disease can involve both intra- and extrahepatic ducts, and a small-duct variant exists without visible cholangiographic changes.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary biliary cholangitis","IgG4-related sclerosing cholangitis","Secondary sclerosing cholangitis (from prior surgery, ischemia, or stones)","Autoimmune hepatitis (including overlap syndrome with PSC)","Cholangiocarcinoma","Chronic bacterial cholangitis"],"infectionMimics":["Recurrent pyogenic (bacterial) cholangitis","AIDS cholangiopathy","Parasitic cholangitis (e.g., liver flukes)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["IgG4-related sclerosing cholangitis","Ischemic cholangiopathy","Post-surgical bile duct strictures","Choledocholithiasis with secondary strictures"],"additionalInvestigations":["Alkaline phosphatase and GGT — screen for and monitor cholestasis","MRCP — noninvasive imaging of choice to visualize characteristic ductal beading and strictures","ERCP — reserved for cases needing therapeutic intervention or when MRCP is nondiagnostic","Liver biopsy — helpful mainly for suspected small-duct PSC or overlap with autoimmune hepatitis; may show onion-skin periductal fibrosis","Serum IgG4 — to exclude IgG4-related sclerosing cholangitis","Colonoscopy with biopsies — to evaluate for concurrent inflammatory bowel disease","CA 19-9 and repeat cross-sectional imaging — surveillance for cholangiocarcinoma in patients with dominant strictures or clinical deterioration","Autoantibody panel (ANA, ASMA, p-ANCA) — supportive workup, though p-ANCA lacks specificity"],"redFlags":["Rapid clinical deterioration, weight loss, or new dominant stricture — raise concern for cholangiocarcinoma","Fever, jaundice, and right upper quadrant pain suggesting superimposed acute bacterial cholangitis","Marked rise in CA 19-9 or new mass on imaging","New-onset or worsening ascites, variceal bleeding, or encephalopathy indicating progression to cirrhosis and decompensation","Colonic dysplasia or malignancy found on surveillance colonoscopy in patients with concurrent IBD"],"prognosis":"PSC follows a variable but generally progressive course over years to decades, often culminating in biliary cirrhosis and end-stage liver disease; median transplant-free survival historically has been reported around a decade or more from diagnosis, though course varies widely between patients. Cholangiocarcinoma and colorectal cancer (in those with IBD) are major contributors to morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Attributing isolated alkaline phosphatase elevation in an IBD patient to the bowel disease alone without pursuing cholangiographic evaluation","Missing small-duct PSC, which has a normal cholangiogram and requires biopsy for diagnosis","Failing to distinguish IgG4-related sclerosing cholangitis, which is often steroid-responsive, from classic PSC","Underestimating cholangiocarcinoma risk and not maintaining surveillance in long-standing disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Diseases of the Gallbladder and Bile Ducts / Sclerosing Cholangitis"],"crossLinks":["PRIMARY_BILIARY_CHOLANGITIS","AUTOIMMUNE_HEPATITIS"]},"PYODERMA_GANGRENOSUM":{"name":"Pyoderma gangrenosum","system":"Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pyoderma gangrenosum","PG","pyoderma gangraenosum","neutrophilic ulcerative dermatosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Starts as a tender pustule or nodule that rapidly breaks down into an ulcer with a violaceous, undermined, overhanging border","Classic feature is pathergy — new or worsening ulceration at sites of minor trauma, injection, or surgical incision","About half of cases are associated with an underlying systemic disease, most commonly inflammatory bowel disease, rheumatoid arthritis, or a hematologic malignancy/paraproteinemia","Diagnosis is one of exclusion; there is no single confirmatory laboratory or histologic test","Ulcer base often shows purulent, necrotic tissue with a boggy, undermined edge rather than a clean granulating base","Lower extremities (especially pretibial region) are the most common site, but PG can occur anywhere including peristomal skin","Debridement and skin grafting can worsen lesions via pathergy and should generally be avoided until disease activity is controlled","Response to immunosuppressive therapy, when it occurs, supports the diagnosis retrospectively"],"pathophysiology":"Pyoderma gangrenosum is a neutrophilic dermatosis thought to arise from dysregulated innate immune activation and neutrophil chemotaxis/function, often in a genetically predisposed host. Cytokine-driven neutrophilic infiltration in the skin, without a demonstrable infectious trigger, produces sterile abscess-like tissue destruction. The tendency for lesions to develop at sites of trauma (pathergy) reflects this exaggerated local inflammatory response. Its frequent association with inflammatory bowel disease, inflammatory arthritides, and myeloid dyscrasias suggests a shared systemic inflammatory or clonal process in many patients.","additionalDifferentials":["Necrotizing fasciitis or other necrotizing soft-tissue infection","Vasculitic ulcer (e.g., ANCA-associated vasculitis)","Venous or arterial (ischemic) leg ulcer","Calciphylaxis","Cutaneous malignancy (e.g., ulcerated squamous cell carcinoma)","Brown recluse spider bite / factitial ulceration"],"infectionMimics":["Necrotizing fasciitis","Ecthyma gangrenosum","Deep fungal or atypical mycobacterial ulcer","Cutaneous anthrax or other bacterial ulcerative infection"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Vasculitic ulceration","Venous stasis or arterial insufficiency ulcer","Calciphylaxis","Factitial (self-induced) ulcer","Ulcerated cutaneous malignancy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy with histology and tissue culture — helps exclude infection and vasculitis, though PG findings are nonspecific","Tissue and blood cultures — to rule out an infectious ulcer before immunosuppression is started","Complete blood count with differential and peripheral smear — screens for underlying myeloproliferative or myelodysplastic disease","Serum protein electrophoresis/immunofixation — evaluates for paraproteinemia associated with PG","Colonoscopy or evaluation for inflammatory bowel disease — assesses for a common associated condition","ANCA, rheumatoid factor, and autoimmune serologies — helps exclude vasculitis and screens for associated rheumatologic disease","Ankle-brachial index or vascular studies — excludes arterial/venous insufficiency contributing to ulceration","Coagulation/hypercoagulability workup if calciphylaxis or thrombotic ulcer is a concern"],"redFlags":["Rapidly expanding ulcer with systemic toxicity — reconsider necrotizing infection and seek urgent surgical/infectious disease input","Worsening of the ulcer after debridement or grafting, consistent with pathergy, warrants stopping surgical intervention and escalating immunosuppression","New PG lesions in a patient with known IBD may signal a disease flare requiring gastroenterology involvement","Unexplained cytopenias or monoclonal gammopathy alongside PG should prompt hematologic evaluation for underlying malignancy","Peristomal PG in patients with ostomies can be mistaken for a wound infection and mismanaged"],"prognosis":"Course is variable and often relapsing, particularly when an underlying systemic disease remains active; lesions can heal with characteristic cribriform (atrophic, often hyperpigmented) scarring. Prompt recognition, avoidance of unnecessary surgical intervention, and treatment of any associated systemic condition improve outcomes.","pitfalls":["Treating PG as an infected wound and pursuing aggressive debridement, which can worsen the ulcer through pathergy","Assuming a positive wound culture confirms infection rather than colonization of a chronic ulcer","Delaying diagnosis by relying on a single confirmatory test rather than the clinical picture and exclusion of mimics","Missing an underlying systemic disease (IBD, arthritis, hematologic disorder) that drives disease activity and recurrence risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Cutaneous Manifestations of Internal Disease / Neutrophilic Dermatoses"],"crossLinks":["inflammatory_bowel_disease"]},"REACTIVE_ARTHRITIS":{"name":"Reactive arthritis","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["reactive arthritis","reiter","reiter's syndrome","reiter syndrome","ReA","post-infectious arthritis","urethritis-arthritis-conjunctivitis syndrome","sexually acquired reactive arthritis","SARA","post-dysenteric arthritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Asymmetric oligoarthritis, predominantly of the lower limbs, arising 1-4 weeks after a genitourinary or gastrointestinal infection","Classic triad of arthritis, conjunctivitis/uveitis, and urethritis/cervicitis occurs in only a minority of patients — most present with incomplete forms","Enthesitis (especially Achilles tendon and plantar fascia) and dactylitis ('sausage digit') are characteristic clues pointing away from a purely infectious arthritis","Strongly associated with HLA-B27, particularly in cases following genitourinary infection or with axial/sacroiliac involvement","Triggering organisms include Chlamydia trachomatis, and enteric pathogens such as Salmonella, Shigella, Yersinia, and Campylobacter","Mucocutaneous findings — keratoderma blennorrhagicum and circinate balanitis — can mimic psoriasis or STI-related dermatoses","Synovial fluid is inflammatory and sterile; cultures and PCR are negative for the triggering organism, distinguishing it from septic arthritis","Most cases are self-limited over months, but a subset develops chronic or relapsing joint disease, especially those who are HLA-B27 positive"],"pathophysiology":"Reactive arthritis is thought to result from an aberrant immune response to bacterial antigens (or bacterial components/DNA persisting in synovium) following a distant mucosal infection, rather than from direct joint infection. HLA-B27 appears to shape the immune response to these triggering pathogens, promoting a sterile inflammatory synovitis, enthesitis, and mucocutaneous inflammation. It is classified with the spondyloarthritis family, sharing pathogenic overlap with ankylosing spondylitis and psoriatic arthritis.","additionalDifferentials":["Septic arthritis (bacterial or gonococcal)","Disseminated gonococcal infection","Psoriatic arthritis","Ankylosing spondylitis / other spondyloarthritides","Rheumatic fever","Crystal arthropathy (gout, pseudogout)"],"infectionMimics":["Septic arthritis","Disseminated gonococcal infection","Lyme arthritis","Viral arthritis (parvovirus B19, hepatitis B/C, rubella)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Psoriatic arthritis","Ankylosing spondylitis","Rheumatic fever","Gout or pseudogout","Inflammatory bowel disease-associated arthritis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Synovial fluid analysis with cell count, Gram stain, and culture — to exclude septic and crystal arthritis","HLA-B27 testing — supports diagnosis and prognostic stratification, though not required for diagnosis","Stool culture or PCR for enteric pathogens (Salmonella, Shigella, Yersinia, Campylobacter) if diarrheal prodrome","Chlamydia trachomatis nucleic acid amplification testing of urine/genital swab","Inflammatory markers (ESR/CRP) — usually elevated but nonspecific","Blood cultures and gonococcal NAAT — to exclude disseminated gonococcal infection when presentation overlaps","Plain radiographs of affected joints/sacroiliac joints — may show enthesophytes or sacroiliitis in chronic disease","Ophthalmologic exam if uveitis/conjunctivitis symptoms present"],"redFlags":["Fever with a hot, markedly swollen single joint — must exclude septic arthritis before attributing to reactive arthritis","Acute visual symptoms or eye pain suggesting uveitis — requires urgent ophthalmology referral","Signs of aortitis or cardiac conduction disease in longstanding HLA-B27-associated disease","Rapidly progressive axial disease or neurologic symptoms suggesting cauda equina involvement","Systemic sepsis features that suggest disseminated gonococcal or other bacteremic process rather than reactive arthritis"],"prognosis":"Most episodes resolve within 3-12 months with appropriate anti-inflammatory management, but relapses and progression to chronic spondyloarthritis occur in a meaningful minority, particularly HLA-B27-positive individuals. Chronic disease may involve persistent enthesitis, axial disease, or recurrent ocular inflammation requiring long-term rheumatologic follow-up.","pitfalls":["Assuming a sterile inflammatory joint effusion excludes infection without adequate synovial fluid culture and clinical correlation","Missing the diagnosis in incomplete presentations lacking the full urethritis-arthritis-conjunctivitis triad","Overlooking a preceding GI or GU infection because the interval before joint symptoms can be several weeks","Confusing mucocutaneous lesions (keratoderma blennorrhagicum, circinate balanitis) with psoriasis or sexually transmitted skin disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Reactive Arthritis (Spondyloarthritis section)"],"crossLinks":[]},"SARCOID_NEURO":{"name":"Neurosarcoidosis","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neurosarcoid","CNS sarcoidosis","neurologic sarcoidosis","sarcoid neuropathy","sarcoidosis of the nervous system"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Symptomatic nervous-system involvement occurs in roughly 5-10% of sarcoidosis patients, though subclinical granulomas are more common at autopsy","Facial nerve palsy (often bilateral or recurrent) is the single most characteristic cranial neuropathy seen","Basilar leptomeningeal enhancement on MRI is a classic pattern, reflecting the predilection for meninges at the base of the brain","Hypothalamic-pituitary involvement can cause diabetes insipidus, hyperprolactinemia, or other endocrinopathies and should prompt pituitary axis testing","CSF findings are nonspecific — lymphocytic pleocytosis, elevated protein, and sometimes low glucose — mimicking tuberculous or fungal meningitis","Neurologic disease can be the presenting manifestation of sarcoidosis before systemic disease is recognized, so tissue confirmation elsewhere (e.g., mediastinal nodes, skin) is valuable","Spinal cord involvement can mimic a longitudinally extensive myelitis or an intramedullary tumor on imaging","Response to immunosuppression supports but does not confirm the diagnosis, since some infections also transiently respond to steroids"],"pathophysiology":"Neurosarcoidosis results from non-caseating granulomatous inflammation infiltrating the meninges, cranial nerves, hypothalamic-pituitary axis, brain parenchyma, or spinal cord. The granulomas provoke a perivascular and leptomeningeal inflammatory response that can cause direct compressive or infiltrative neurologic deficits, hydrocephalus from impaired CSF flow, or vasculopathy. Because it is part of a systemic multisystem granulomatous disease, isolated CNS presentations without other organ involvement are diagnostically challenging.","additionalDifferentials":["Tuberculous meningitis","Fungal meningitis (cryptococcal, coccidioidal)","CNS lymphoma","Multiple sclerosis / other demyelinating disease","CNS vasculitis","Lymphomatoid granulomatosis or other granulomatous CNS disorders"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculous meningitis","Cryptococcal meningitis","Neurosyphilis","Lyme neuroborreliosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Multiple sclerosis","CNS lymphoma","Primary CNS vasculitis","IgG4-related disease","Idiopathic hypertrophic pachymeningitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain and spine MRI with gadolinium — assesses leptomeningeal enhancement, parenchymal lesions, and cord involvement","CSF analysis with cell count, protein, glucose, ACE, and cytology — evaluates for lymphocytic pleocytosis and excludes malignancy/infection","Serum and CSF ACE levels — supportive but neither sensitive nor specific","Chest CT and/or whole-body FDG-PET — identifies occult systemic (especially thoracic) granulomatous disease for biopsy targeting","Tissue biopsy of an accessible extraneural site (lymph node, skin, lacrimal gland) — establishes histologic diagnosis of non-caseating granulomas","Pituitary hormone panel — screens for hypothalamic-pituitary axis dysfunction when relevant symptoms present","Ophthalmologic slit-lamp exam — detects concurrent uveitis supporting systemic sarcoidosis","Infectious workup (AFB stain/culture, fungal antigen, treponemal serology) on CSF — excludes key infectious mimics before immunosuppression"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive myelopathy or brainstem signs suggesting need for urgent imaging and treatment","Hydrocephalus or signs of raised intracranial pressure from basilar meningeal involvement","Unexplained diabetes insipidus or panhypopituitarism in a patient with known or suspected sarcoidosis","New seizures or mass-like parenchymal lesions requiring exclusion of malignancy or infection before immunosuppressive therapy","Optic neuropathy, which can threaten vision and warrants prompt treatment"],"prognosis":"Course is variable — many patients respond to immunosuppressive therapy with partial or complete resolution, but a subset experience chronic, relapsing, or progressive disease, particularly with parenchymal brain or spinal cord involvement, which carries a worse functional outcome.","pitfalls":["Starting empiric immunosuppression before adequately excluding infectious mimics (especially TB and fungal disease) can worsen unrecognized infection","Relying solely on elevated CSF or serum ACE for diagnosis, as this is neither sensitive nor specific","Assuming a favorable steroid response confirms sarcoidosis, since infections and other inflammatory conditions may also improve transiently","Overlooking neurosarcoidosis in a patient without known systemic sarcoidosis, since isolated CNS presentation occurs in a meaningful subset of cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Sarcoidosis chapter (Nervous System Involvement)"],"crossLinks":["sarcoidosis"]},"STATUS_EPILEPTICUS":{"name":"Status epilepticus","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["status epilepticus","SE","convulsive status epilepticus","non-convulsive status epilepticus","NCSE","refractory status epilepticus","seizure emergency","prolonged seizure"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined operationally as a single seizure lasting beyond the time most seizures of that type would normally stop (roughly 5 minutes for convulsive seizures) or recurrent seizures without recovery of consciousness in between.","Treat convulsive status epilepticus as a time-critical emergency — earlier treatment initiation strongly predicts easier termination and better outcome.","Airway, breathing, circulation, glucose, and thiamine should be addressed simultaneously with, not after, anticonvulsant therapy.","Always check a bedside glucose early; hypoglycemia is a rapidly reversible cause that mimics or precipitates status epilepticus.","Non-convulsive status epilepticus should be suspected in any patient with unexplained persistent altered consciousness, especially after a convulsive seizure has been treated but the patient fails to wake up.","Continuous EEG monitoring is essential once convulsions have stopped to confirm electrographic seizure resolution, particularly in patients who remain unresponsive.","Refractory status epilepticus (persisting despite adequate first- and second-line therapy) generally requires escalation to continuous anesthetic infusions with EEG guidance in an ICU setting.","Identifying and treating the underlying etiology is as important as stopping the seizure itself, since ongoing precipitants (e.g., infection, toxin, structural lesion) will otherwise perpetuate the episode."],"pathophysiology":"Status epilepticus arises from a failure of the mechanisms that normally terminate seizure activity, leading to sustained abnormal neuronal hypersynchronization. This results from a relative excess of excitatory (glutamatergic) neurotransmission and depletion or dysfunction of inhibitory (GABAergic) signaling, with progressive receptor trafficking changes that make prolonged seizures increasingly resistant to GABA-acting drugs over time. Prolonged seizure activity can itself cause excitotoxic neuronal injury independent of the underlying cause, and is accompanied by systemic derangements including hyperthermia, acidosis, hypoxia, and autonomic instability that compound neuronal damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Psychogenic non-epileptic seizures (functional/dissociative attacks)","Syncope with convulsive movements","Movement disorders (e.g., dystonic storm, severe tremor)","Toxic or metabolic encephalopathy without true seizure activity","Rigors or shivering from severe sepsis","Post-anoxic myoclonus"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial meningitis or meningoencephalitis","Viral encephalitis (e.g., herpes simplex encephalitis)","Cerebral abscess or CNS tuberculosis with seizures","Neurocysticercosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute stroke or intracerebral hemorrhage","Severe metabolic derangement (hyponatremia, hypoglycemia, uremia, hepatic encephalopathy)","Drug intoxication or withdrawal (alcohol, benzodiazepine, sedative withdrawal; proconvulsant drug toxicity)","Autoimmune or paraneoplastic encephalitis","Hypoxic-ischemic brain injury","Eclampsia in pregnancy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Bedside capillary glucose — rapid identification of reversible hypoglycemic cause","Serum electrolytes, calcium, magnesium, renal and liver function — screen for metabolic precipitants","Anticonvulsant drug levels (if patient on prior therapy) — detect subtherapeutic levels or nonadherence","Toxicology screen — identify intoxication or withdrawal etiology","Continuous EEG monitoring — detect non-convulsive status epilepticus and confirm electrographic seizure termination","Neuroimaging (CT or MRI brain) — identify structural lesion, hemorrhage, or stroke, generally once initially stabilized","Lumbar puncture (after imaging if indicated) — evaluate for CNS infection or inflammation when fever or clinical suspicion present","Arterial blood gas and core temperature — assess for acidosis and hyperthermia from prolonged seizure activity"],"redFlags":["Seizure activity continuing beyond the expected duration without response to initial therapy — escalate treatment promptly","Persistent unresponsiveness after convulsions have visibly stopped — evaluate for non-convulsive status epilepticus","Hemodynamic instability, hyperthermia, or severe acidosis during prolonged seizure activity","Focal neurologic signs or papilledema suggesting an acute structural intracranial process","Fever with meningismus suggesting an infectious CNS cause requiring urgent antimicrobial coverage","Failure to respond to first- and second-line agents, indicating refractory status epilepticus requiring ICU-level anesthetic therapy"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on etiology, age, and duration of status before treatment, with prolonged or refractory episodes carrying substantially higher morbidity and mortality. Even with successful seizure termination, patients face risk of cognitive sequelae and recurrent epilepsy, particularly when status is due to an acute severe brain insult.","pitfalls":["Assuming convulsions have stopped means the seizure is over — non-convulsive status epilepticus can persist electrographically with only subtle clinical signs.","Delaying anticonvulsant administration while awaiting imaging or laboratory results, which worsens the chance of terminating the seizure.","Underdosing initial therapy out of caution about respiratory depression, which increases the risk of treatment failure and need for escalation.","Failing to search for and treat the underlying precipitant, allowing the episode to recur despite initial seizure control."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Seizures and Epilepsy (Status Epilepticus)"],"crossLinks":["hyponatremia"]},"TOXIC_SHOCK_SYNDROME":{"name":"Toxic shock syndrome","system":"Infectious","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["toxic shock","TSS","staphylococcal toxic shock syndrome","streptococcal toxic shock syndrome","STSS","menstrual TSS","tampon-associated shock"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Consider TSS in any acute febrile illness with diffuse macular erythroderma, hypotension, and multi-organ involvement, even without an obvious focal infection source.","A retained tampon, nasal packing, surgical wound packing, or postpartum/post-surgical site should always be actively sought and removed as part of initial assessment.","Streptococcal TSS typically presents with a clear, severe, deep soft-tissue focus (necrotizing fasciitis or bacteremia) and is far more often bacteremic than the staphylococcal form.","Desquamation of the palms and soles is a late finding, occurring one to two weeks after illness onset, and should never be relied upon to make the initial diagnosis.","Hypotension plus involvement of three or more organ systems (renal, hepatic, hematologic, mucosal, CNS, muscular/GI) in a febrile diffuse-rash illness supports the syndromic diagnosis.","Early aggressive fluid resuscitation is often required because of profound capillary leak, and volume needs can be extreme in the first 24-48 hours.","Source control (removing foreign material, draining/debriding infected tissue) is as critical as antimicrobial therapy and delays worsen outcomes.","Blood cultures are frequently negative in staphylococcal TSS since the syndrome is toxin-mediated rather than driven by bloodstream invasion."],"pathophysiology":"TSS results from exotoxins produced by Staphylococcus aureus (notably TSST-1) or by Streptococcus pyogenes acting as superantigens that bind directly to MHC class II molecules and T-cell receptors outside the conventional antigen groove. This bypasses normal antigen processing and triggers massive, non-specific polyclonal T-cell activation with a resulting cytokine storm (IL-1, IL-2, TNF, interferon-gamma). The surge in inflammatory mediators produces capillary leak, vasodilation, hypotension, and secondary multi-organ dysfunction, explaining why the clinical picture can be severe even with a small, localized focus of infection or colonization.","additionalDifferentials":["Kawasaki disease (especially in children with fever, rash, and mucosal changes)","Necrotizing fasciitis without full toxic shock physiology","Rocky Mountain spotted fever and other rickettsial infections","Severe scarlet fever","Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS) or Stevens-Johnson spectrum","Septic shock from another gram-negative or gram-positive source"],"infectionMimics":["Kawasaki disease","Scarlet fever","Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Leptospirosis (Weil syndrome)","Meningococcemia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Stevens-Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis","Drug hypersensitivity reaction (DRESS)","Systemic lupus erythematosus flare with vasculitic rash","Adult-onset Still disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cultures (times two) — often negative in staphylococcal TSS but essential to identify streptococcal bacteremia and guide therapy","Wound, vaginal, or device-site cultures — to isolate toxin-producing S. aureus or S. pyogenes and confirm source","Complete metabolic panel with renal and hepatic function — documents multi-organ involvement required for diagnosis","Complete blood count with differential — assesses for thrombocytopenia and left shift","Creatine kinase — elevated with muscular involvement/myositis component","Coagulation studies (PT/INR, fibrinogen) — screens for evolving disseminated intravascular coagulation","Imaging (CT/MRI or bedside ultrasound) of suspected soft-tissue focus — evaluates for necrotizing infection requiring surgical debridement","Serologic/toxin studies where available — can support etiology in atypical or culture-negative presentations"],"redFlags":["Refractory hypotension unresponsive to initial fluid resuscitation, suggesting need for vasopressors and ICU-level care","Rapidly spreading pain, swelling, or crepitus over a soft-tissue site raising concern for necrotizing fasciitis requiring emergent surgical exploration","Evolving coagulopathy or falling platelet count concerning for disseminated intravascular coagulation","Altered mental status or new organ dysfunction indicating rapid progression to multi-organ failure","Any retained foreign material (tampon, packing, prosthetic material) not yet identified or removed"],"prognosis":"Staphylococcal TSS has a lower mortality (roughly a few percent) when recognized early and the source is controlled, whereas streptococcal TSS carries a substantially higher mortality, often cited around 30-70%, particularly when associated with necrotizing soft-tissue infection or bacteremia. Prompt source control, resuscitation, and antimicrobial therapy are the main determinants of outcome.","pitfalls":["Dismissing the diagnosis because blood cultures are negative — staphylococcal TSS is toxin-mediated and frequently culture-negative.","Waiting for classic desquamation before considering the diagnosis, which delays treatment since desquamation occurs late in the course.","Failing to perform a thorough examination for retained foreign bodies (vaginal, nasal, surgical) as the driving source.","Underestimating streptococcal TSS severity because the cutaneous findings can be subtler than staphylococcal disease despite higher mortality."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Staphylococcal Infections and Streptococcal Infections chapters (Toxic Shock Syndrome sections)"],"crossLinks":["NECROTIZING_FASCIITIS","SEPTIC_SHOCK"]},"TUMOR_LYSIS_SYNDROME":{"name":"Tumour lysis syndrome","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tumour lysis","tumor lysis","TLS","acute tumor lysis syndrome","spontaneous tumor lysis syndrome","cytolytic syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Emerges when bulky, rapidly proliferating tumours (especially high-grade lymphomas and acute leukemias) undergo massive cell death, either spontaneously or within hours to days of starting cytotoxic therapy","Classic biochemical tetrad is hyperuricemia, hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, and secondary hypocalcemia","Risk correlates with tumour bulk, proliferation rate, and baseline chemosensitivity — Burkitt lymphoma and acute lymphoblastic leukemia carry the highest risk","Pre-existing renal impairment, volume depletion, and acidic urine markedly increase risk of uric acid nephropathy","Onset is typically 12–72 hours after initiating effective therapy but can occur before any treatment is given in highly proliferative tumours","Acute kidney injury is usually the dominant clinical threat, driven by uric acid and/or calcium phosphate precipitation in renal tubules","Cardiac arrhythmia from hyperkalemia and hypocalcemia is the most immediately life-threatening complication","Aggressive risk stratification and prophylaxis before chemotherapy is far more effective than treating established syndrome"],"pathophysiology":"Rapid destruction of malignant cells releases large quantities of intracellular potassium, phosphate, and nucleic acids into the circulation. Nucleic acid catabolism generates a surge of uric acid that can precipitate in the relatively acidic, low-flow environment of the renal tubules, producing obstructive nephropathy. Hyperphosphatemia from cell lysis exceeds the solubility product for calcium phosphate, causing tissue and renal deposition and consequent hypocalcemia. The resulting acute kidney injury further impairs excretion of potassium, phosphate, and uric acid, creating a self-amplifying metabolic crisis.","additionalDifferentials":["Sepsis-associated multiorgan dysfunction with acute kidney injury","Rhabdomyolysis with hyperkalemia and hyperphosphatemia","Chemotherapy-induced acute kidney injury from nephrotoxic agents","Acute uric acid nephropathy unrelated to malignancy","Adrenal insufficiency presenting with hyperkalemia","Massive hemolysis syndromes"],"infectionMimics":["Severe sepsis with acute kidney injury and metabolic derangement mimicking the electrolyte pattern of TLS"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Rhabdomyolysis","Acute kidney injury from contrast nephropathy or nephrotoxic drugs","Hemolytic states with hyperkalemia","Refeeding syndrome (overlapping electrolyte shifts)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum potassium, phosphate, calcium, uric acid — core panel to confirm the metabolic tetrad and grade severity","Creatinine and estimated GFR — assess renal function and monitor for acute kidney injury","ECG — screen for hyperkalemia- or hypocalcemia-related conduction changes and arrhythmia risk","Urinalysis and urine output monitoring — detect early oliguria or uric acid crystalluria","LDH — surrogate marker of tumour burden and cell turnover, useful for risk stratification","Baseline and serial renal function before and during cytoreductive therapy","Coagulation profile if disseminated intravascular coagulation is suspected concurrently"],"redFlags":["Oliguria or rising creatinine suggesting evolving acute kidney injury","ECG changes (peaked T waves, widened QRS, QT prolongation) indicating hyperkalemia or hypocalcemia","Symptomatic hypocalcemia (tetany, seizures, arrhythmia)","Rapidly rising potassium or phosphate despite supportive measures","High tumour bulk with elevated baseline LDH and uric acid before therapy initiation","Need for urgent renal replacement therapy in refractory metabolic derangement"],"prognosis":"With prompt recognition, risk stratification, and preventive management, most cases resolve without permanent renal impairment; outcomes worsen substantially once oliguric acute kidney injury or refractory hyperkalemia develops, occasionally requiring dialysis.","pitfalls":["Assuming TLS only occurs after chemotherapy — spontaneous lysis can precede any treatment in highly proliferative tumours","Correcting hypocalcemia aggressively without addressing hyperphosphatemia first, which can worsen calcium-phosphate precipitation","Underestimating risk in patients with bulky, chemosensitive tumours and pre-existing renal impairment or dehydration","Delaying prophylactic measures until after laboratory abnormalities appear rather than initiating them proactively"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Oncologic Emergencies (Tumor Lysis Syndrome)"],"crossLinks":["hyperkalemia"]},"WALDENSTROM":{"name":"Waldenström macroglobulinaemia","system":"Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["macroglobulinaemia","lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma","Waldenstrom's macroglobulinemia","WM","IgM lymphoma","IgM monoclonal gammopathy-associated lymphoma","lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma with IgM paraproteinemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Consider WM in an older adult with fatigue, weight loss, and an incidental monoclonal IgM spike on protein electrophoresis.","Hyperviscosity symptoms (visual blurring, headache, mucosal bleeding, dizziness) reflect the very large size of the pentameric IgM molecule rather than tumor burden alone.","Bone lesions are characteristically absent, unlike multiple myeloma, which helps distinguish the two at the bedside.","Peripheral neuropathy (often demyelinating, anti-MAG antibody mediated) can be a presenting or dominant feature.","Cold agglutinin disease and cryoglobulinemia are important IgM-related complications that can cause hemolysis or vasculitis-like skin findings.","Bone marrow involvement by small lymphoplasmacytic cells with a mast-cell-rich infiltrate is a classic morphologic clue.","MYD88 L265P mutation is present in the great majority of cases and is a useful diagnostic and prognostic marker.","Asymptomatic patients with low IgM levels can often be observed without treatment (watch-and-wait)."],"pathophysiology":"Waldenström macroglobulinaemia is a lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma in which clonal B-lymphoplasmacytic cells infiltrate the bone marrow (and sometimes lymph nodes and spleen) and secrete large quantities of monoclonal IgM. The pentameric structure of IgM raises serum viscosity disproportionately to its molar concentration, producing the hyperviscosity syndrome, and the paraprotein may also deposit in nerves, precipitate in the cold (cryoglobulinemia), or bind red cells (cold agglutinin disease). Somatic activating mutations in MYD88 (and often CXCR4) drive NF-κB signaling and are central to disease pathogenesis.","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple myeloma (IgM myeloma is rare but distinguishable by lytic bone lesions and different marrow morphology)","IgM monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS)","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia / small lymphocytic lymphoma","Marginal zone lymphoma with plasmacytic differentiation","Other B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphomas with paraproteinemia","Amyloidosis (AL) presenting with neuropathy and paraproteinemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["IgM MGUS","Multiple myeloma","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","Marginal zone lymphoma","Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis from other causes","Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP) unrelated to paraproteinemia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum protein electrophoresis and immunofixation — identifies and characterizes the monoclonal IgM spike","Serum viscosity — quantifies hyperviscosity risk when symptomatic","Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy with immunophenotyping — confirms lymphoplasmacytic infiltration","MYD88 L265P mutation testing (allele-specific PCR) — supports diagnosis and distinguishes from other lymphomas","CXCR4 mutation testing — informs prognosis and response to certain targeted therapies","Complete blood count — assesses cytopenias from marrow infiltration","Anti-MAG antibody testing — when peripheral neuropathy is present","CT imaging of chest/abdomen/pelvis — evaluates lymphadenopathy and organomegaly"],"redFlags":["Visual disturbance, headache, confusion, or mucosal bleeding suggesting hyperviscosity syndrome requiring urgent plasmapheresis","Rapidly progressive peripheral neuropathy","Evidence of cryoglobulinemia with skin ulceration, digital ischemia, or renal involvement","Cold agglutinin-mediated hemolysis with severe anemia","Marked cytopenias suggesting extensive marrow replacement","Transformation to a more aggressive (diffuse large B-cell) lymphoma"],"prognosis":"The disease course is typically indolent with survival often measured in many years, and asymptomatic low-burden disease can be observed safely. Prognosis is influenced by age, cytopenias, beta-2-microglobulin level, and genetic features such as CXCR4 mutation status, with most patients eventually requiring treatment for symptomatic progression.","pitfalls":["Attributing an incidental IgM paraprotein to benign MGUS without adequate marrow and clinical evaluation when other features are present","Delaying plasmapheresis in symptomatic hyperviscosity while awaiting definitive chemoimmunotherapy","Missing WM as a cause of unexplained demyelinating peripheral neuropathy in older adults","Confusing WM with IgM myeloma or misinterpreting lytic bone lesions as consistent with WM (they are not typical)"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Plasma Cell Disorders and Waldenström Macroglobulinemia"],"crossLinks":[]},"ZOLLINGER_ELLISON":{"name":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (gastrinoma)","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["zollinger","gastrinoma","ZES","gastrin-secreting tumor","gastrinoma syndrome","hypergastrinemic peptic ulcer disease","pancreatic gastrinoma","duodenal gastrinoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Suspect ZES in patients with severe, recurrent, or refractory peptic ulcers, ulcers in unusual locations (distal duodenum, jejunum), or ulcers with prominent diarrhea that improves with acid suppression.","Chronic secretory diarrhea plus peptic ulcer disease is a classic combination that should prompt gastrin testing.","About 20-30% of gastrinomas occur as part of MEN1 (multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1) — screen for hyperparathyroidism and pituitary tumors.","Most gastrinomas arise within the 'gastrinoma triangle' bounded by the cystic duct, junction of the pancreatic head/neck, and duodenal junction.","Fasting serum gastrin should be measured off proton pump inhibitors when clinically feasible, since acid suppression can falsely elevate gastrin.","A markedly elevated fasting gastrin with an inappropriately low gastric pH strongly supports the diagnosis.","Roughly half of gastrinomas are malignant with a tendency for lymph node and hepatic metastases at diagnosis.","Somatostatin-receptor imaging (e.g., DOTATATE PET) is the preferred modality for tumor localization given high sensitivity."],"pathophysiology":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome results from a gastrin-secreting neuroendocrine tumor (gastrinoma), most often located in the duodenum or pancreas, that drives unregulated gastric acid hypersecretion. The resulting profound acid load overwhelms mucosal defenses and inactivates pancreatic enzymes, producing severe/recurrent peptic ulceration and secretory diarrhea with malabsorption. A substantial minority of cases occur in association with MEN1, reflecting a hereditary predisposition to multiple endocrine tumors.","additionalDifferentials":["Helicobacter pylori-associated peptic ulcer disease","NSAID-induced peptic ulcer disease","Idiopathic gastric acid hypersecretion","Antral G-cell hyperplasia","Other causes of chronic secretory diarrhea (VIPoma, carcinoid syndrome)","Retained gastric antrum syndrome after prior gastric surgery"],"infectionMimics":["Helicobacter pylori peptic ulcer disease"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["NSAID-induced ulcer disease","Idiopathic gastric acid hypersecretion","VIPoma (pancreatic cholera)","Carcinoid syndrome","Antral G-cell hyperplasia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting serum gastrin — markedly elevated levels raise suspicion for gastrinoma","Gastric pH measurement — low pH with high gastrin confirms inappropriate acid-driven hypergastrinemia","Secretin stimulation test — paradoxical rise in gastrin supports the diagnosis when basal levels are equivocal","Upper endoscopy — assesses ulcer burden, location, and mucosal complications","Somatostatin-receptor imaging (e.g., DOTATATE PET/CT) — preferred for primary tumor and metastasis localization","Cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI abdomen) — evaluates tumor size, local invasion, and hepatic metastases","Endoscopic ultrasound — useful for detecting small pancreatic/duodenal lesions","Serum calcium and parathyroid hormone, prolactin — screen for coexisting MEN1"],"redFlags":["Multiple or recurrent ulcers despite adequate acid-suppressive therapy","Ulcers in atypical locations (distal duodenum, jejunum)","Evidence of hepatic or nodal metastases at diagnosis","Signs of MEN1 (hypercalcemia, pituitary symptoms) suggesting hereditary syndrome","Complications of ulcer disease such as perforation or bleeding","Severe secretory diarrhea causing dehydration or electrolyte derangement"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends primarily on tumor biology and extent of disease at diagnosis; localized, resectable gastrinomas carry a favorable long-term outlook, while metastatic disease (especially hepatic spread) is associated with reduced survival. Effective acid suppression has greatly reduced ulcer-related morbidity, shifting long-term outcome toward tumor-related factors.","pitfalls":["Failing to hold proton pump inhibitors (when safe to do so) before gastrin testing, leading to false-positive hypergastrinemia","Attributing recurrent ulcers solely to H. pylori or NSAIDs without considering gastrinoma in refractory cases","Overlooking associated MEN1 features, delaying recognition of a hereditary tumor syndrome","Relying on gastrin level alone without confirming inappropriate acid hypersecretion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e — Chapter on Neuroendocrine Tumors of the Gastrointestinal Tract and Pancreas (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome)"],"crossLinks":[]},"abnormal_uterine_bleeding":{"name":"Abnormal uterine bleeding","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AUB","dysfunctional uterine bleeding","menorrhagia","heavy menstrual bleeding"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Abnormal uterine bleeding describes any bleeding that is abnormal in frequency, regularity, duration, or volume in a non-pregnant woman of reproductive age","The FIGO PALM-COEIN system classifies causes into structural (polyp, adenomyosis, leiomyoma, malignancy and hyperplasia) and non-structural (coagulopathy, ovulatory dysfunction, endometrial, iatrogenic, not otherwise classified) categories","Excluding pregnancy is the essential first step in any woman of reproductive age presenting with abnormal bleeding","Ovulatory dysfunction, common at the extremes of reproductive life, produces irregular unpredictable bleeding from unopposed oestrogen effect on the endometrium","An underlying bleeding disorder, particularly von Willebrand disease, should be considered in women with heavy menstrual bleeding since menarche","Endometrial sampling is indicated in women at risk of hyperplasia or malignancy, such as those over a threshold age, with persistent bleeding, or with risk factors like obesity and chronic anovulation","Medical management includes hormonal options and antifibrinolytic and anti-inflammatory agents, with the levonorgestrel intrauterine system being highly effective for heavy menstrual bleeding","Surgical options such as endometrial ablation or hysterectomy are reserved for failed medical therapy or specific structural causes"],"pathophysiology":"Normal menstruation depends on cyclical ovulation producing coordinated oestrogen and progesterone effects, followed by orderly progesterone withdrawal and controlled endometrial shedding with effective local haemostasis. Abnormal bleeding arises when this is disrupted: structural lesions (polyps, adenomyosis, fibroids, hyperplasia or cancer) distort the endometrium and its vasculature; anovulation leads to unopposed oestrogen with an unstable proliferative endometrium that sheds erratically; systemic coagulopathy impairs haemostasis; and local disturbances of endometrial prostaglandins and fibrinolysis increase menstrual loss.","additionalDifferentials":["Pregnancy-related bleeding including miscarriage and ectopic pregnancy","Endometrial or cervical polyp","Uterine fibroids and adenomyosis","Endometrial hyperplasia or carcinoma","Cervical or vaginal lesions including malignancy","Bleeding disorders and thyroid dysfunction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pregnancy test in women of reproductive age","Full blood count to assess for iron-deficiency anaemia","Transvaginal ultrasound to assess endometrial thickness and detect structural lesions","Endometrial biopsy or hysteroscopy with directed sampling where malignancy or hyperplasia is a concern","Coagulation screen and von Willebrand testing when a bleeding disorder is suspected","Thyroid function tests and other endocrine investigations as indicated"],"redFlags":["Postmenopausal bleeding, which must be investigated to exclude endometrial carcinoma","Heavy bleeding causing haemodynamic instability or severe symptomatic anaemia","Bleeding with risk factors for endometrial cancer such as obesity, chronic anovulation, or tamoxifen use","A suspicious cervical or vaginal lesion on examination","Intermenstrual or postcoital bleeding suggesting cervical pathology"],"prognosis":"Most abnormal uterine bleeding is benign and responds well to medical or targeted surgical treatment, with good symptom control and correction of anaemia. Prognosis in structural or malignant causes depends on the underlying diagnosis, so timely investigation to identify hyperplasia or carcinoma is key to a favourable outcome.","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude pregnancy before investigation or treatment","Attributing postmenopausal bleeding to benign causes without excluding malignancy","Overlooking an inherited bleeding disorder in a young woman with lifelong heavy periods","Not sampling the endometrium in women with risk factors for hyperplasia or cancer","Treating presumed dysfunctional bleeding without excluding structural lesions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"acinetobacter_infections":{"name":"Acinetobacter Infections","system":"Multisystem / Infectious Disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Acinetobacter baumannii infection","A. baumannii infection","Acinetobacter calcoaceticus-baumannii complex infection","MDR Acinetobacter","Carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter (CRAB)"],"chapter":167,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1294-1298","clinicalPearls":["Acinetobacter are gram-negative, oxidase-negative, nonmotile, nonfermenting coccobacilli readily grown on standard media; A. baumannii is the most clinically relevant species. Phenotypic differentiation between species is difficult, so molecular methods such as MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry or quantitative real-time PCR are often needed for accurate identification.","A. baumannii can survive environmental desiccation for weeks, allowing it to persist on hospital surfaces and equipment - a key reason it drives health care-associated outbreaks.","A. baumannii is a notorious cause of nosocomial pneumonia, especially with prolonged mechanical ventilation; onset tends to be later than with other gram-negative bacilli, and the usual clues are fever and increased sputum production.","Airway colonization with A. baumannii does not by itself confirm pneumonia, but it is a recognized risk factor for subsequent infection - distinguishing colonization from true infection is a recurring clinical challenge.","On a CSF Gram stain, Acinetobacter can mimic Neisseria meningitidis since both appear as gram-negative paired cocci; a petechial rash was described in up to 30% of one meningitis case series.","Bloodstream infection most often arises in ICU patients with a central venous catheter or as a secondary complication of ventilator-associated/hospital-acquired pneumonia; fever occurs in over 95% of cases and polymicrobial growth is reported in roughly 20-36% of bacteremia episodes.","In combat-trauma soft tissue infection, the described clinical evolution progresses from an edematous peau d'orange appearance to a sandpaper appearance with overlying vesicles, then to a necrotizing process with hemorrhagic bullae; necrotizing fasciitis has been reported.","Community-acquired Acinetobacter pneumonia is relatively rare but severe, with fever, prominent respiratory symptoms, multi-organ dysfunction, lobar consolidation on imaging, and mortality exceeding 50%.","A. baumannii is an important burn-unit pathogen; large burns provide ideal growth conditions, facilitate patient-to-patient transmission, and wound colonization is a risk factor for bloodstream infection.","Risk factors for health care-associated colonization/infection include nursing home residence, prolonged ICU stay, central venous catheterization, tracheostomy, mechanical ventilation, enteral feeding, and prior third-generation cephalosporins, fluoroquinolones, or carbapenems; carbapenem-resistant strains are most common after carbapenem exposure.","Urinary tract infection is infrequent and usually catheter-associated, reflecting biofilm formation on devices; rare community-acquired cases occur with nephrolithiasis or after renal transplantation.","Less common manifestations include contact lens-associated keratitis and native- or prosthetic-valve endocarditis."],"pathophysiology":"A. baumannii has greater virulence potential than other Acinetobacter species, partly through its ability to grow at 37 degrees C and resist macrophage uptake. Colonization is facilitated by adherence to surfaces and human cells and by biofilm formation, which is linked to exopolysaccharide production and pilus formation; a quorum-sensing molecule encoded by the abaI autoinducer synthase gene contributes to biofilm on abiotic surfaces. The organism modifies its lipid A structure to survive harsh environments, decreasing susceptibility to antibiotics and antimicrobial peptides and enhancing desiccation survival. An extracellular capsule protects against complement-mediated killing and can be upregulated in the presence of subinhibitory antibiotic levels, producing a hypervirulent phenotype. Phospholipase C and D act as cytotoxic virulence factors aiding epithelial invasion, and siderophore-based iron-acquisition systems permit growth despite host iron limitation. Multiple protein-secretion systems (type II with the LipA lipase substrate, type V trimeric autotransporter mediating biofilm and matrix adherence, and a type VI system secreting antibacterial toxins) plus outer-membrane vesicles carrying OmpA, proteases, and phospholipases contribute to pathogenesis and innate immune activation. Antimicrobial resistance arises from reduced porin expression, overexpressed efflux pumps (removing quinolones, tetracyclines, chloramphenicol, disinfectants, and tigecycline), and beta-lactamases including intrinsic AmpC cephalosporinases (upregulated when the ISAba1 insertion sequence is placed next to the AmpC gene) and acquired serine and metallo-beta-lactamases. Carbapenem resistance is mostly tied to Ambler class D oxacillinases (group 2d), some intrinsic/chromosomal (e.g., OXA-51-like) and others acquired (e.g., OXA-23-, 24-, 58-, 143-, 235-like). The small A. baumannii core genome with a large accessory genome confers high genomic plasticity and ready uptake of resistance genes by horizontal gene transfer.","additionalDifferentials":["Ventilator-associated/hospital-acquired pneumonia due to other nosocomial gram-negative bacilli (Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Enterobacterales)","Stenotrophomonas maltophilia or Burkholderia infection in the ventilated/immunocompromised host","Catheter-related bloodstream infection from other nosocomial pathogens","Necrotizing fasciitis or other necrotizing soft tissue infection from streptococcal, clostridial, or polymicrobial causes following trauma","Bacterial meningitis due to Neisseria meningitidis (similar gram-negative paired cocci on CSF Gram stain)"],"infectionMimics":["Neisseria meningitidis on CSF Gram stain (both appear as gram-negative diplococci/paired cocci)","Other nonfermenting gram-negative nosocomial pathogens such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Stenotrophomonas maltophilia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Airway colonization without true pneumonia (positive respiratory culture not equating to active infection)","Wound colonization rather than soft tissue infection, particularly in burns and trauma"],"additionalInvestigations":["MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry for species-level identification","Quantitative real-time PCR for identification of A. baumannii","Antimicrobial susceptibility testing to guide definitive therapy (given extensive and variable resistance)","Respiratory cultures interpreted in clinical context to distinguish colonization from pneumonia","Blood cultures (assessing for polymicrobial growth in suspected bacteremia)","Rectal surveillance cultures for outbreak detection and infection-control purposes","Molecular typing of isolates to differentiate outbreak from nonoutbreak strains"],"redFlags":["Septic shock and disseminated intravascular coagulation, each reported in roughly 25-30% of bloodstream infection cases","Infection caused by carbapenem-resistant (extremely drug-resistant) isolates, where delayed adequate therapy drives poor outcomes","Evolving necrotizing soft tissue picture (hemorrhagic bullae, necrotizing fasciitis) after combat or disaster-related trauma","New A. baumannii in a long-term ventilated ICU patient with fever and rising sputum","Multi-organ dysfunction with severe community-acquired pneumonia"],"prognosis":"Infections carry high mortality, driven largely by the severity of the patient's underlying illness and by drug resistance of the infecting strain. Crude mortality with nosocomial pneumonia has been reported as high as 65%, though attributable mortality is hard to establish because affected patients are typically debilitated. Community-acquired pneumonia mortality exceeds 50%. Crude bloodstream infection mortality reaches about 40% overall and as high as 70% with carbapenem-resistant isolates. Wound colonization/infection contributes to healing delays and graft loss in burn patients.","pitfalls":["Treating positive respiratory or wound cultures as proof of infection when they may represent colonization, leading to overtreatment.","Underestimating environmental persistence: failure to address desiccation-resistant contamination of surfaces and equipment perpetuates outbreaks.","Mistaking Acinetobacter for Neisseria meningitidis on CSF Gram stain.","Assuming susceptibility - intrinsic and acquired beta-lactamases (including OXA-type carbapenemases) make empirical choices unreliable without local epidemiology and susceptibility testing.","Overlooking Acinetobacter in the differential of soft tissue infection following exposure to tropical/subtropical environments or natural disasters."],"severityClassification":"No formal disease-specific severity score is provided in the source. Severity is gauged clinically by site and host factors: presence of septic shock or DIC in bloodstream infection (~25-30%), need for prolonged mechanical ventilation in pneumonia, and carbapenem/extreme drug resistance, all of which predict markedly higher mortality.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1294-1298"],"crossLinks":[]},"acl_injury":{"name":"Anterior cruciate ligament injury","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ACL tear","ACL rupture","anterior cruciate ligament rupture","cruciate ligament injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically follows a non-contact pivoting, twisting, or deceleration injury, often in sport, with the patient reporting a 'pop' and rapid swelling of the knee","A tense, blood-filled effusion (haemarthrosis) developing within hours of injury strongly suggests an ACL rupture (or other significant intra-articular injury)","The Lachman test is the most sensitive clinical examination; the anterior drawer and pivot-shift tests also demonstrate anterior tibial translation and rotational instability","MRI confirms the diagnosis and, importantly, identifies associated meniscal tears, collateral ligament injury, and bone bruising","Symptomatic instability with the knee 'giving way' during turning or pivoting is the key indication for reconstruction","Management is individualised: reconstruction (usually with a tendon graft) is favoured for young, active, or pivoting-sport patients, while rehabilitation-led treatment suits lower-demand individuals","Rehabilitation to restore quadriceps strength and neuromuscular control is essential whether or not surgery is performed","Untreated instability predisposes to recurrent meniscal tears and secondary osteoarthritis over time"],"pathophysiology":"The anterior cruciate ligament runs from the posterior femoral intercondylar notch to the anterior tibial plateau and is the principal restraint to anterior translation of the tibia and a key stabiliser against rotational and pivoting forces. Sudden deceleration, landing, or a valgus-external rotation force with the foot planted overloads the ligament, causing partial or complete rupture, frequently near its femoral origin. Because the ligament is intra-articular and vascular, rupture produces a rapid haemarthrosis. The ACL has poor intrinsic healing capacity, so complete tears generally do not reunite. Persistent instability increases shear on the menisci and articular cartilage, accelerating degenerative change.","additionalDifferentials":["Meniscal tear","Collateral (medial or lateral) ligament injury","Posterior cruciate ligament injury","Patellar dislocation","Osteochondral fracture or tibial spine avulsion","Knee dislocation with multiligament injury","Extensor mechanism rupture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the knee to confirm the tear and detect associated meniscal, chondral, and ligamentous injury","Clinical stability testing (Lachman, anterior drawer, pivot-shift)","Plain radiographs to exclude fracture, including a Segond fracture as an indirect sign","Aspiration of a tense haemarthrosis for symptom relief and diagnostic information where appropriate","Assessment for associated multiligament injury if a knee dislocation is suspected"],"redFlags":["Features of knee dislocation with multiligament injury requiring urgent vascular and neurological assessment (risk to the popliteal artery and peroneal nerve)","A locked knee suggesting a displaced bucket-handle meniscal tear needing prompt treatment","Signs of neurovascular compromise after significant knee trauma","Open injury or associated fracture"],"prognosis":"Outcomes depend on activity demands and associated injuries; reconstruction restores stability and enables return to pivoting sport in most, while appropriately selected patients do well with rehabilitation alone, but chronic instability and untreated meniscal damage increase the long-term risk of osteoarthritis regardless of treatment.","pitfalls":["Missing an ACL rupture by not recognising the significance of an acute haemarthrosis","Failing to identify associated meniscal or collateral ligament injury","Overlooking a knee dislocation that has spontaneously reduced, with its vascular risk","Proceeding to surgery on a stiff, poorly rehabilitated knee, increasing the risk of postoperative stiffness"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"acne_vulgaris":{"name":"Acne vulgaris","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acne","common acne","pimples"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A disorder of the pilosebaceous unit affecting the face, chest, and back, typically beginning around puberty","The hallmark non-inflammatory lesion is the comedo (open blackheads and closed whiteheads); its presence distinguishes acne from other papulopustular eruptions like rosacea","Inflammatory lesions range from papules and pustules to deep nodules and cysts, and severe forms lead to scarring and post-inflammatory pigmentation","Treatment is chosen by severity: topical retinoids and benzoyl peroxide for comedonal and mild inflammatory disease, adding topical or oral antibacterials for moderate disease","Combining benzoyl peroxide with antibacterials reduces bacterial resistance, and antibacterials should not be used as prolonged monotherapy","Systemic retinoid therapy is reserved for severe, nodulocystic, or scarring acne resistant to other measures and requires careful monitoring and strict pregnancy precautions given teratogenicity","Hormonal therapy can help women with acne linked to androgen excess; consider investigating for hyperandrogenism when there are virilising features or menstrual irregularity","Sudden severe nodular acne with fever and joint pain (acne fulminans) is a systemic emergency needing urgent specialist care"],"pathophysiology":"Acne results from four interacting processes within the pilosebaceous unit: increased sebum production driven by androgens, abnormal keratinisation of the follicular lining producing microcomedones, proliferation of Cutibacterium acnes, and inflammation. Trapped sebum and keratin form comedones; bacterial activity and the host immune response generate inflammatory mediators that produce papules, pustules, and deeper nodules. Rupture of the follicular wall extends inflammation into the dermis, and disordered wound healing can result in atrophic or hypertrophic scarring. Hormonal influences, genetic predisposition, and certain drugs modulate severity.","additionalDifferentials":["Rosacea (lacks comedones, features flushing and telangiectasia)","Perioral dermatitis","Folliculitis (bacterial or fungal)","Keratosis pilaris","Drug-induced acneiform eruptions (e.g. corticosteroids)","Hidradenitis suppurativa when lesions involve flexures"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis; investigations rarely needed for typical presentations","Hormonal workup (androgens, and related endocrine tests) when hyperandrogenism or polycystic ovary syndrome is suspected","Pregnancy testing and lipid and liver monitoring before and during systemic retinoid therapy","Swabs when Gram-negative folliculitis or resistant infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Acne fulminans: abrupt severe ulcerating nodular acne with fever, malaise, and arthralgia","Signs of underlying endocrine disorder such as virilisation or rapid-onset severe acne","Extensive scarring or psychological distress requiring escalation of care","Suspected retinoid-associated mood disturbance during systemic treatment"],"prognosis":"Acne usually improves after adolescence but may persist into adulthood, particularly in women; early effective treatment limits permanent scarring and psychosocial impact.","pitfalls":["Prolonged antibacterial monotherapy promoting resistance and relapse","Under-treating scarring or nodular acne and delaying referral for systemic retinoid therapy","Overlooking the significant psychological burden and risk of low mood","Failing to enforce pregnancy prevention during teratogenic systemic retinoid use"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"acoustic_neuroma":{"name":"Acoustic neuroma","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vestibular schwannoma","acoustic schwannoma","eighth nerve schwannoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Acoustic neuroma is a benign, slow-growing schwannoma that usually arises from the vestibular division of the eighth cranial nerve, so vestibular schwannoma is the more accurate term","It typically presents with unilateral or asymmetrical sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus, and any such asymmetry should prompt investigation","Speech discrimination is often disproportionately poor relative to the pure tone loss, a clue to a retrocochlear lesion","Balance disturbance is often mild because the slow growth allows central compensation, so vertigo is usually less prominent than expected","As the tumour enlarges within the cerebellopontine angle it can compress the trigeminal nerve, causing facial numbness and a reduced corneal reflex","Facial nerve function is usually preserved until late despite the nerve's proximity, and early facial weakness should raise doubt about the diagnosis","MRI with contrast of the internal auditory meatus is the gold standard investigation and detects even small intracanalicular tumours","Management is individualised between observation with serial imaging, stereotactic radiosurgery, and microsurgical excision, depending on size, growth, symptoms, hearing and age"],"pathophysiology":"Acoustic neuroma is a benign neoplasm of Schwann cells, most often originating at the transition zone of the vestibular nerve within or near the internal auditory canal. As it grows it first fills the canal, compressing the cochlear and vestibular fibres and their blood supply to produce sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus and imbalance. Continued expansion into the cerebellopontine angle displaces and stretches adjacent structures: the trigeminal nerve above, the facial nerve alongside, and eventually the brainstem and cerebellum. Large tumours can obstruct cerebrospinal fluid flow and raise intracranial pressure. Most cases are sporadic and unilateral, while bilateral tumours are the hallmark of neurofibromatosis type 2.","additionalDifferentials":["Meniere disease","Presbycusis or noise-induced asymmetrical hearing loss","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss","Meningioma or other cerebellopontine angle tumour","Facial nerve schwannoma","Epidermoid or arachnoid cyst of the cerebellopontine angle","Vascular loop compression of the eighth nerve"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Gadolinium-enhanced MRI of the internal auditory meatus and cerebellopontine angle","Pure tone and speech audiometry to characterise asymmetry and poor discrimination","Auditory brainstem response testing where MRI is contraindicated","Vestibular testing such as caloric responses","CT of the temporal bone in selected cases to assess bony anatomy for surgery"],"redFlags":["Unilateral or asymmetrical sensorineural hearing loss or tinnitus mandating MRI to exclude the tumour","Facial numbness or an absent corneal reflex suggesting trigeminal involvement by a larger tumour","Headache, ataxia, vomiting or altered consciousness suggesting brainstem compression or raised intracranial pressure (an emergency)","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss, which can occasionally be the presenting feature","Bilateral tumours or a family history suggesting neurofibromatosis type 2"],"prognosis":"Because the tumour is benign and usually slow growing the prognosis is generally good, and small tumours may simply be observed; treatment outcomes depend on tumour size at diagnosis, with hearing preservation and facial nerve function being more achievable when tumours are small.","pitfalls":["Attributing unilateral hearing loss or tinnitus to age or noise without imaging","Overlooking disproportionately poor speech discrimination as a retrocochlear clue","Assuming prominent vertigo must be present, when balance symptoms are often mild","Missing the significance of facial numbness or an absent corneal reflex","Failing to consider neurofibromatosis type 2 in young or bilateral cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"acromegaly":{"name":"Acromegaly","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["growth hormone excess","GH excess","somatotroph adenoma","gigantism (if before epiphyseal fusion)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Insidious onset means diagnosis is often delayed by years; comparing old photographs can reveal the gradual coarsening of features","Characteristic findings include enlarging hands and feet, coarse facial features, frontal bossing, prognathism, and interdental spacing","Soft-tissue overgrowth commonly causes carpal tunnel syndrome, arthralgia, excessive sweating, and obstructive sleep apnoea","Systemic complications include hypertension, glucose intolerance or diabetes, cardiomyopathy, and an increased risk of colonic polyps","Because GH secretion is pulsatile, a random GH level is not reliable; IGF-1 is the preferred screening test as it reflects integrated secretion","Biochemical confirmation rests on failure of GH to suppress during an oral glucose load","Most cases arise from a GH-secreting pituitary adenoma, so pituitary MRI and assessment of other pituitary axes and visual fields are essential","Transsphenoidal surgical resection is the usual first-line treatment, with medical therapy or radiotherapy for residual disease"],"pathophysiology":"Acromegaly is caused by chronic excess of growth hormone, almost always from a somatotroph pituitary adenoma, and rarely from ectopic GH or GHRH secretion. Excess GH stimulates hepatic production of insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1), which mediates most of the peripheral growth effects on bone, cartilage, and soft tissue. When GH excess occurs before epiphyseal fusion it produces gigantism; after fusion it produces the acral and soft-tissue overgrowth of acromegaly. The expanding pituitary mass may also compress the optic chiasm and impair other pituitary hormone axes, while GH's counter-regulatory metabolic effects promote insulin resistance and cardiovascular disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Constitutional large body habitus or familial coarse features","Pachydermoperiostosis and other causes of acral enlargement","Hypothyroidism causing coarse features and soft-tissue changes","Other pituitary masses causing mass effect without GH excess","Ectopic GHRH or GH secretion from a neuroendocrine tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum IGF-1 interpreted against age-matched reference ranges as the initial screen","Oral glucose tolerance test with GH measurement to confirm non-suppression","Pituitary MRI to define adenoma size and relation to the optic chiasm","Formal visual field assessment when a macroadenoma abuts the chiasm","Evaluation of remaining anterior pituitary axes for co-secretion or hypopituitarism","Screening for complications including echocardiography, sleep study, and colonoscopy"],"redFlags":["Visual field loss or new headache suggesting chiasmal compression by a macroadenoma","Features of pituitary apoplexy such as sudden severe headache with ophthalmoplegia","Signs of heart failure from acromegalic cardiomyopathy","Coexisting hypopituitarism, particularly unrecognised adrenal insufficiency"],"prognosis":"Biochemical control that normalises IGF-1 and GH restores life expectancy toward normal and improves soft-tissue and metabolic features, whereas persistent disease increases cardiovascular, respiratory, and metabolic mortality and the risk of colonic neoplasia.","pitfalls":["Relying on a single random GH level, which is uninterpretable given pulsatile secretion","Attributing carpal tunnel syndrome, sleep apnoea, or new diabetes to common causes without considering acromegaly","Missing coexisting hypopituitarism when focusing on GH excess","Overlooking the need for long-term surveillance for cardiovascular disease and colonic polyps"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"actinic_keratosis":{"name":"Actinic Keratosis","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AK","solar keratosis","senile keratosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as rough, scaly, erythematous macules or thin papules on chronically sun-exposed skin such as the face, scalp of balding men, ears, forearms, and backs of hands","Lesions are often more easily felt than seen, giving a sandpaper-like texture to palpation","They are markers of cumulative ultraviolet damage and indicate field change, so surrounding skin often harbours further subclinical lesions","A minority progress to invasive squamous cell carcinoma, and the risk rises with the number of lesions and degree of sun damage","Features that raise concern for progression include thickening, tenderness, induration, rapid growth, bleeding, or ulceration","Management options span lesion-directed treatments such as cryotherapy and curettage and field-directed topical therapies, with photoprotection central to prevention","Immunosuppressed patients, particularly organ transplant recipients, develop more numerous and aggressive lesions and need closer surveillance","Sun-protection advice and regular skin self-examination reduce the development of new lesions and help detect malignant change early"],"pathophysiology":"Actinic keratosis is an intraepidermal proliferation of atypical, dysplastic keratinocytes driven by cumulative ultraviolet radiation. UV exposure induces characteristic DNA mutations, including in the TP53 tumour-suppressor gene, that impair repair and apoptosis and allow clonal expansion of abnormal keratinocytes confined to the epidermis. The result is disordered maturation and hyperkeratosis producing the palpable scale. Because the whole photo-damaged area shares this mutational burden (field cancerisation), multiple lesions and recurrences are common, and continued genetic damage can lead to full-thickness dysplasia and invasion characteristic of squamous cell carcinoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Squamous cell carcinoma (invasive)","Bowen's disease (squamous cell carcinoma in situ)","Superficial basal cell carcinoma","Seborrhoeic keratosis","Discoid lupus erythematosus","Localised patch of eczema or psoriasis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Largely a clinical and dermoscopic diagnosis","Skin biopsy when a lesion is thick, indurated, tender, or otherwise suspicious for squamous cell carcinoma","Full skin examination to assess overall field damage and detect other skin cancers","Closer surveillance protocols for immunosuppressed and transplant patients"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth, induration, tenderness, ulceration, or bleeding suggesting progression to invasive squamous cell carcinoma","A lesion that recurs quickly or fails to respond to appropriate treatment","Numerous or aggressive lesions in an immunosuppressed or organ transplant patient","A hyperkeratotic horn-like lesion with a firm, tender base warranting histology"],"prognosis":"Individual actinic keratoses are common and most remain stable or regress, but they mark significant sun damage and carry a small per-lesion risk of transforming into squamous cell carcinoma; with a high lesion burden the cumulative risk becomes clinically important, so treatment and long-term photoprotection and surveillance are worthwhile.","pitfalls":["Dismissing an indurated or growing lesion as a simple actinic keratosis when it is an invasive squamous cell carcinoma","Treating individual visible lesions while ignoring the wider field of sun damage","Underestimating risk and undertreating in immunosuppressed patients","Neglecting to reinforce sun protection and self-examination, allowing recurrent disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"actinomycosis":{"name":"Actinomycosis","system":"Multisystem / Infectious Disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Actinomyces infection","lumpy jaw","cervicofacial actinomycosis"],"chapter":180,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1363","clinicalPearls":["An uncommon, indolent, slowly progressive infection caused by anaerobic or microaerophilic bacteria—primarily Actinomyces species—that normally colonize the mouth, colon, and vagina; it has been called one of the most frequently misdiagnosed diseases (Harrison 22e p.1363).","Three classic presentations should trigger suspicion: a chronic mass-like lesion that crosses tissue planes and mimics malignancy, a sinus tract that spontaneously resolves and recurs, and a refractory or relapsing infection after a short course of therapy (Harrison 22e p.1363).","Most infections are polymicrobial; companion organisms such as Aggregatibacter actinomycetemcomitans, Eikenella corrodens, Enterobacteriaceae, Fusobacterium, and Bacteroides are commonly co-isolated, though their pathogenic contribution is uncertain (Harrison 22e p.1363).","Disease spreads contiguously, ignoring tissue planes; the characteristic chronic phase produces single or multiple indurated lesions with fibrotic walls classically described as wooden (Harrison 22e p.1364).","Central necrosis containing neutrophils and sulfur granules is virtually diagnostic; over time, sinus tracts to skin, adjacent organs, or bone develop (Harrison 22e p.1364).","Lymphatic spread with lymphadenopathy and distant hematogenous seeding are uncommon—an important contrast with malignancy and tuberculosis (Harrison 22e p.1364).","Oral–cervicofacial disease is the most frequent presentation, typically at the angle of the jaw, often precipitated by dental disease or procedures (Harrison 22e p.1364).","Pelvic actinomycosis is strongly associated with intrauterine contraceptive devices (IUDs) and other foreign bodies such as surgical mesh; risk increases the longer the device remains in place and is low when in place under one year (Harrison 22e p.1364-1365).","Sulfur granules represent an in vivo matrix of bacteria, calcium phosphate, and host material; on hematoxylin-eosin staining they may be surrounded by a radiating fringe of eosinophilic clubs (Splendore-Hoeppli phenomenon) (Harrison 22e p.1365).","Foreign bodies (especially IUDs) and immunocompromising states—HIV, transplantation, common variable immunodeficiency, chronic granulomatous disease, and certain immunosuppressive therapies—are recognized predisposing factors (Harrison 22e p.1364).","FDG-PET may show hypermetabolism in actinomycotic lesions, potentially compounding confusion with malignancy (Harrison 22e p.1365)."],"pathophysiology":"The etiologic agents are members of the normal oral, gastrointestinal, and female genital flora. The critical initiating step is disruption of the mucosal barrier, allowing local infection. Once established, the infection spreads contiguously in a slow, progressive fashion that disregards normal tissue planes. Although acute inflammation may occur initially, the hallmark is a chronic indolent phase with indurated lesions, central necrosis (neutrophils plus sulfur granules), and dense fibrotic walls. Sinus tracts to skin, adjacent organs, or bone may develop over time. Foreign bodies, particularly IUDs, facilitate infection, and ulcerative mucosal infections (e.g., HSV, CMV) may promote disease (Harrison 22e p.1364).","additionalDifferentials":["Malignancy / solid tumor (most common misdiagnosis given mass-like, tissue-plane-crossing behavior)","Tuberculosis","Nocardiosis","Inflammatory bowel disease (when abdominal sinus tracts/fistulas develop)","Endometriosis (in pelvic disease)","Bacterial pneumonia or lung neoplasm (in thoracic disease)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis","Nocardiosis","Mycetoma due to Nocardia or Actinomadura species","Botryomycosis (chronic suppurative bacterial infection forming granule-like clumps)","Bacterial pneumonia/empyema from usual organisms"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Malignant neoplasm / solid tumor","Endometriosis","Inflammatory bowel disease","Lung neoplasm"],"additionalInvestigations":["Microscopic identification of sulfur granules in pus or tissue (most common diagnostic means; yield improves with additional histopathologic sections and positively charged slides)","Anaerobic culture—isolation maximized under anaerobic conditions, typically requiring 5–7 days and potentially up to 2–4 weeks; even a single antibiotic dose can preclude recovery","16S rRNA gene amplification and sequencing to enhance sensitivity and specificity","MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry (databases still being optimized)","Histologic stains: periodic acid–Schiff (PAS), Grocott methenamine silver (GMS), and Gram stains to identify actinomycotic aggregates","CT and MRI for lesion characterization and monitoring; FDG-PET may demonstrate hypermetabolism","CT/ultrasound-guided aspiration or biopsy to obtain material in a minimally invasive fashion; endometrial biopsy for pelvic disease"],"redFlags":["Chronic mass-like lesion crossing tissue planes and mimicking cancer","Sinus tract that spontaneously resolves and recurs","Refractory or relapsing infection after a short antibiotic course","Lesions crossing fissures/pleura, extending into mediastinum, bone, or chest wall (empyema necessitatis)","Wound or fistula that fails to heal, or recurrent abdominal disease","CNS involvement (brain abscess, meningitis, cavernous sinus syndrome)—a critical-site presentation"],"prognosis":"Actinomycosis is usually curable with medical therapy alone, even in extensive disease, and an awareness of its clinical spectrum can expedite diagnosis and minimize unnecessary surgery, morbidity, and mortality. Cure of established disease requires prolonged treatment, and relapse is a clinical hallmark if therapy is stopped too early; refractory disease has been described in HIV-infected individuals (Harrison 22e p.1363, p.1366).","pitfalls":["Diagnosis is rarely considered prospectively—actinomycosis is often first identified by the pathologist after extensive (and avoidable) surgery (Harrison 22e p.1365).","Prior antibiotic exposure or failure to obtain appropriate anaerobic cultures frequently precludes microbiologic identification; even a single antibiotic dose can prevent isolation (Harrison 22e p.1365).","Because actinomycetes are normal oral and genital-tract flora, their detection in sputum, bronchial washings, or cervicovaginal secretions without sulfur granules may reflect colonization rather than infection (Harrison 22e p.1365).","Detection of Actinomyces-like organisms on Papanicolaou smear in IUD users has low positive predictive value; in an asymptomatic patient this warrants education and follow-up, not routine IUD removal (Harrison 22e p.1365).","Isolation of Actinomyces from blood cultures without a defined focus may represent contamination or transient bacteremia from a mucosal site (Harrison 22e p.1366).","Elevated CA-125 in pelvic disease and FDG-PET hypermetabolism can reinforce a mistaken cancer diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1365)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1363","Harrison 22e p.1364","Harrison 22e p.1365","Harrison 22e p.1366"],"crossLinks":[]},"acute_abdomen":{"name":"Acute abdomen","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["surgical abdomen","acute abdominal pain","peritonitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The immediate task is to identify the patient who needs urgent surgery from among the many with self-limiting abdominal pain","Assess airway, breathing, and circulation first, since perforation, ischaemia, or bleeding can cause rapid haemodynamic collapse","The character, onset, and migration of pain guide the diagnosis; sudden severe pain suggests perforation, rupture, or vascular catastrophe","Peritonism (guarding, rigidity, rebound, pain on movement) indicates peritoneal irritation and a likely surgical cause","Pain out of proportion to examination findings should raise concern for mesenteric ischaemia","Always consider extra-abdominal and vascular causes such as myocardial infarction, ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm, and ectopic pregnancy","Serial examination and reassessment are valuable when the diagnosis is uncertain, as the picture often evolves","Resuscitate, provide analgesia, and involve surgery early rather than delaying for prolonged investigation in the unstable patient"],"pathophysiology":"Acute abdomen results from inflammation, obstruction, perforation, ischaemia, or haemorrhage of intra-abdominal organs. Visceral pain is initially poorly localised because of autonomic innervation, but once inflammation reaches the parietal peritoneum the pain becomes sharp and localised. Perforation releases luminal contents causing chemical then bacterial peritonitis; obstruction causes distension, third-space fluid loss, and potential strangulation; and vascular occlusion causes bowel ischaemia and infarction. These processes can precipitate hypovolaemia, sepsis, and shock, making the acute abdomen a potentially life-threatening presentation.","additionalDifferentials":["Ruptured or leaking abdominal aortic aneurysm","Mesenteric ischaemia or infarction","Perforated viscus (peptic ulcer, diverticulum, appendix)","Bowel obstruction with or without strangulation","Ectopic pregnancy or other gynaecological emergency","Inferior myocardial infarction or lower-lobe pneumonia mimicking abdominal pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Erect chest and abdominal radiographs to detect free air or obstruction","Bedside ultrasound and CT abdomen for definitive assessment when stable","Serum lactate as a marker of ischaemia or hypoperfusion","Pregnancy test in women of childbearing age","Amylase/lipase, renal and liver function, and inflammatory markers as part of the workup"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe pain with peritonism suggesting perforation or vascular rupture needing immediate surgery","Haemodynamic instability indicating haemorrhage, ischaemia, or septic shock","Pain disproportionate to findings, raising suspicion of mesenteric ischaemia","A pulsatile mass or back pain with collapse suggesting ruptured aortic aneurysm","Rapidly progressive distension, fever, and toxicity signalling advancing peritonitis"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the underlying cause and the speed of definitive treatment. Early recognition and surgery for perforation, ischaemia, or haemorrhage improve survival, while delay allows sepsis, bowel infarction, or exsanguination with markedly higher mortality.","pitfalls":["Withholding analgesia for fear of masking signs, which is not supported and delays comfort","Attributing pain to a benign cause in the elderly, in whom signs are often muted despite serious disease","Overlooking vascular and extra-abdominal causes such as aortic aneurysm or myocardial infarction","Delaying surgery to complete imaging in an unstable patient","Failing to reassess when the initial diagnosis is uncertain"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"acute_agitation":{"name":"Acute agitation","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute behavioural disturbance","agitated delirium","acute agitation and aggression"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Treat agitation as a symptom with a potentially life-threatening organic cause until proven otherwise, not as purely psychiatric behaviour","Rapidly exclude and correct reversible medical causes, especially hypoxia and hypoglycaemia, which can present as agitation","Ensure the safety of the patient and staff first, using de-escalation before considering restraint or sedation","A structured approach to reversible causes includes hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, hypoperfusion, intoxication or withdrawal, head injury, and infection","Fluctuating attention and conscious level point to delirium and an underlying acute medical problem rather than a primary psychiatric illness","Sudden agitation in a trauma patient may signal hypoxia or shock and should trigger reassessment of the airway, breathing, and circulation","Physical restraint carries real risks and should be minimised, monitored closely, and combined with treatment of the cause","Do not attribute agitation to alcohol or drugs before excluding hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, and head injury"],"pathophysiology":"Acute agitation is a final common pathway of many disturbances of brain function. Global cerebral dysfunction (delirium) arises from hypoxia, hypoperfusion, hypoglycaemia, metabolic derangement, sepsis, drug intoxication or withdrawal, and structural brain injury, all of which impair the neural networks governing attention and behaviour. In the trauma setting, agitation may be the earliest external sign of inadequate cerebral oxygen delivery from hypoxia or haemorrhagic shock. Primary psychiatric illness and severe pain also contribute, but organic causes must be excluded because they are frequently reversible and can be fatal if missed.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypoxia or hypercapnia","Hypoglycaemia or other metabolic disturbance","Hypoperfusion or shock, including occult haemorrhage","Intoxication or withdrawal (alcohol, drugs)","Head injury or intracranial pathology","Sepsis or CNS infection","Primary psychiatric illness (diagnosis of exclusion)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bedside capillary glucose and pulse oximetry as immediate first checks","Arterial blood gas to detect hypoxia, hypercapnia, and acidosis","Basic bloods including electrolytes, renal function, and infection markers","Toxicology and alcohol assessment where relevant","CT head and further imaging when a structural or traumatic cause is suspected"],"redFlags":["Agitation accompanied by hypoxia or hypoglycaemia demanding immediate correction","Fluctuating consciousness or new focal neurology suggesting a serious intracranial cause","Agitation in a trauma patient indicating possible hypoxia or shock and prompting reassessment","Autonomic instability with hyperthermia suggesting a severe toxidrome or serotonin/neuroleptic syndrome","Rapid physical exhaustion during prolonged struggle, risking sudden collapse"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends entirely on the underlying cause. When a reversible medical precipitant is identified and treated, agitation usually resolves, but a missed organic cause such as hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, or intracranial pathology can be rapidly fatal.","pitfalls":["Labelling agitation as psychiatric or drug-related before excluding hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, and head injury","Sedating the patient without monitoring, risking airway obstruction and respiratory depression","Using prolonged or unmonitored physical restraint","Failing to reassess the trauma patient whose agitation reflects worsening shock or hypoxia","Overlooking withdrawal states that can escalate dangerously"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"acute_and_chronic_myocarditis":{"name":"Acute and Chronic Myocarditis","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Myocarditis","Inflammatory cardiomyopathy","Viral myocarditis","Giant cell myocarditis","Eosinophilic myocarditis","Cardiac sarcoidosis","Lyme carditis","Chagas cardiomyopathy","Checkpoint inhibitor myocarditis"],"chapter":268,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2011","clinicalPearls":["Myocarditis is inflammation of heart muscle that can present acutely, subacutely, or insidiously, with outcomes ranging from full resolution to a relapsing course or progression to chronic cardiomyopathy; it is termed acute when symptoms have lasted under a month, often only days (p.2011).","Chest pain is the most common symptom of acute myocarditis, occurring in more than 80% of patients, who frequently also have dyspnea or arrhythmias; fever is present in over half of cases and a viral prodrome may precede onset by days to weeks (p.2011).","Acute myocarditis in adults typically occurs between roughly 30 and 45 years and is more common in men, whereas women tend to present after age 45 (p.2011).","At presentation about one-fourth of acute myocarditis cases have an LVEF below 0.50, ventricular arrhythmias, or shock; fulminant myocarditis (circulatory shock) is reported in about 3-9% of cases (p.2011).","Diagnosis can now be made noninvasively when a typical symptomatic presentation is accompanied by elevated troponin plus cardiac MRI showing both myocardial edema and nonischemic injury without another cause; biopsy is no longer strictly required for a 'definite' diagnosis (p.2011-2012).","Because chest pain, ECG changes, and elevated troponin overlap between acute myocarditis and myocardial infarction, coronary imaging is often the first differential step; myocarditis is later diagnosed in up to one-third of patients with MINOCA (myocardial infarction with nonobstructed coronary arteries) (p.2011).","Eosinophil counts should be checked routinely because hypereosinophilia is present in most cases of eosinophilic myocarditis; right bundle branch block should raise suspicion for sarcoidosis or Chagas rather than typical cardiomyopathies, in which left bundle branch block predominates (p.2011, p.2013, p.2014).","Around one in five patients with inflammatory myocarditis carry genetic variants considered pathogenic for cardiomyopathy, and 20-40% of dilated cardiomyopathy patients have variants sufficient to explain their disease; CMR fibrosis patterns once attributed to prior viral myocarditis are now recognized as common in genetic cardiomyopathy too (p.2011, p.2012).","Giant cell myocarditis accounts for 10-20% of biopsy-proven myocarditis, occurs equally in men and women (typically older than viral cases), is more often linked to systemic autoimmune disease, and usually presents acutely with rapidly progressive heart failure and tachyarrhythmias (p.2014).","Chagas disease (Trypanosoma cruzi) is the most common infective cause of cardiomyopathy worldwide; after a usually silent initial infection patients enter an 'indeterminate phase' lasting decades, during which persistent low-level parasitemia is now recognized as the major driver of progression to chronic heart failure (p.2013).","Diphtheria toxin (Corynebacterium diphtheriae) interferes with cardiac protein synthesis and especially affects the conduction system; cardiac involvement is the most common cause of death in this infection, and clinical suspicion alone justifies giving antitoxin with higher priority than antibiotics (p.2013).","Combined immune checkpoint inhibitor myocarditis is the most dramatic noninfectious form: troponin is often elevated, biopsy shows extensive lymphocytic infiltration, and it should be suspected immediately with any acute cardiac presentation in a treated patient; CCU admission is recommended for elevated troponin with ECG changes (p.2014)."],"pathophysiology":"Infectious agents injure the myocardium both by direct cellular invasion (disrupting normal cellular processes) and by activating immune responses, with or without persistent infection. Viral myocarditis has classically been described in three phases from murine enterovirus (coxsackie B) models: (1) direct myocardial invasion, with viral entry sometimes enhanced by coxsackie/adenovirus receptor variants and enteroviral protease degrading dystrophin to trigger apoptosis and impair autophagy; (2) host immune response to conserved antigenic patterns, activating macrophages and expanding T- and B-cell populations, which can produce 'virally triggered' myocarditis even without direct viral injury, possibly via molecular mimicry between viral and cardiac antigens; and (3) progression to chronic cardiomyopathy, demonstrated in animal models. Direct genome entry occurs with enteroviruses, HIV, and dengue, but most common viruses do not appear to damage the heart directly, and early injury is often driven by cytokine storm and nonspecific immune activation. Notably, this initial immune response appears crucial to recovery, since early immunosuppression worsened viral replication and cardiac injury in animals. Viral 'hijacking' of cellular machinery to produce extracellular vesicles carrying viral RNA may allow protected spread to other cells. In Chagas disease, acute infection produces dense parasitic pseudocysts that rupture and cause diffuse myocyte necrosis, while chronic disease reflects persistent parasitemia plus depopulation of cardiac and gastrointestinal parasympathetic neurons. Noninfective inflammatory myocarditis (sarcoid granulomas, giant cell, eosinophilic, autoimmune, checkpoint inhibitor) reflects antigen-driven, dysregulated immune attack on myocardium; the paradigm is cardiac transplant rejection, which showed that antibodies and cytokines, not only lymphocytes, mediate reversible myocardial depression and that antigens are exposed by prior injury or infection (p.2011-2014).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute coronary syndrome / myocardial infarction (shared chest pain, ECG, troponin) and MINOCA","Genetic dilated or arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (overlapping CMR fibrosis patterns)","Stress (takotsubo) cardiomyopathy and other dilated cardiomyopathies","Pericarditis and other COVID-associated cardiac involvement","Sepsis-related reversible myocardial depression from systemic inflammatory response"],"infectionMimics":["Sepsis with reversible systemic-inflammation-mediated myocardial depression (mimics myocarditis but is generally reversible if the patient survives)","Chagas cardiomyopathy presenting as dilated cardiomyopathy with conduction disease","Lyme carditis (Borrelia burgdorferi) presenting with conduction block","Granulomatous infections such as tuberculosis or histoplasmosis that must be excluded before immunosuppression for presumed sarcoidosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cardiac sarcoidosis (noncaseating granulomas; may mimic arrhythmogenic RV cardiomyopathy)","Giant cell myocarditis","Eosinophilic myocarditis / hypereosinophilic syndrome with Loeffler endocarditis","Systemic autoimmune disease: polymyositis/dermatomyositis, systemic lupus erythematosus (which also causes accelerated CAD, sterile endocarditis, pericarditis, vasculitis)","Immune checkpoint inhibitor myocarditis and vaccine-associated myocarditis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Cardiac troponin (often elevated) and B-type natriuretic peptide","Cardiac MRI showing myocardial edema plus nonischemic late gadolinium enhancement (typical mid-wall location)","ECG (ST elevation mimicking infarction, conduction block, tachyarrhythmias, nonspecific ST-T changes)","Coronary artery imaging to exclude infarction","Eosinophil count (hypereosinophilia in most eosinophilic myocarditis); creatine phosphokinase and C-reactive protein","Respiratory viral panel; selected serologies for HIV, Chagas, CMV, dengue, Lyme, and systemic autoimmune disease (viral titers rarely guide initial therapy)","Endomyocardial biopsy (with immunohistochemistry) when ventricular arrhythmias, conduction block, eosinophilia, or autoimmune disease suggest a specific etiology","Chest CT (mediastinal lymphadenopathy) and whole-chest FDG-PET for active sarcoid lesions; PCR for T. cruzi in acute Chagas"],"redFlags":["Fulminant presentation with cardiogenic shock or need for high-dose inotropes/mechanical circulatory support (3-9% of cases)","Ventricular arrhythmias or syncope, which may precede sudden cardiac death (inflammatory myocarditis found in 3-10% of sudden deaths in previously healthy young adults)","New conduction block, especially right bundle branch block (suggests sarcoidosis or Chagas)","LVEF <0.50 at presentation (marks higher-risk 'complicated' course and chronic cardiomyopathy risk)","Hypereosinophilia (suggests eosinophilic myocarditis, a cause of fulminant disease)","Acute cardiac symptoms in a patient on immune checkpoint inhibitors, often with concurrent involvement of other organs"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is good for 'uncomplicated' acute myocarditis (LVEF >=0.50 without ventricular arrhythmias or shock), which represents about three-fourths of patients; spontaneous recovery is common, almost all retain LVEF >=0.50 on follow-up, and they survive without transplantation at 5 years. Among the one-fourth with complicated presentations, roughly 18% die or undergo transplantation over the next 5 years. In fulminant myocarditis, more than half of affected patients recover to near-normal LVEF despite presenting with refractory cardiogenic shock. Reduced LVEF at presentation predicts chronic cardiomyopathy. Chagas cardiomyopathy has lower survival than other cardiomyopathies once overt heart failure develops, and LVEF improvement is uncommon. Most patients with giant cell myocarditis in cardiogenic shock require urgent mechanical support or transplant. Checkpoint inhibitor myocarditis fatality was initially very high but is improving with earlier recognition and treatment (p.2011-2014).","pitfalls":["Assuming any chest pain/ECG change/troponin elevation is infarction can miss myocarditis, which underlies up to one-third of MINOCA cases (p.2011).","Sarcoid granulomas are scattered and commonly missed on endomyocardial biopsy, so a negative biopsy does not exclude cardiac sarcoidosis (p.2014).","Eosinophilic myocarditis may be overlooked in milder presentations if hypereosinophilia is not assessed (p.2014).","Viral titers and detection of viral genome fragments or anticardiac antibodies in chronic cardiomyopathy are of uncertain significance, as the same viruses (e.g., parvovirus B19, HHV6) are also found in normal control hearts and these assays are not in routine clinical use (p.2011, p.2012).","There are no data supporting routine immunosuppression for presumed viral acute myocarditis, and immunosuppression for nonspecific lymphocytic chronic myocarditis has not shown convincing benefit (p.2012).","Initial care of suspected checkpoint inhibitor myocarditis should generally not be delayed to obtain endomyocardial biopsy (p.2014)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison distinguishes uncomplicated acute myocarditis (LVEF >=0.50, no ventricular arrhythmias, no shock; ~75% of cases, good prognosis) from complicated acute myocarditis (LVEF <0.50, ventricular arrhythmias, or shock; ~25%, higher 5-year death/transplant rate). Fulminant myocarditis (~3-9%) denotes circulatory shock requiring high-dose inotropes or mechanical circulatory support. Diagnostically, 'probable myocarditis' requires at least one symptom plus one supporting finding (elevated troponin, consistent ECG, or reduced LVEF/wall-motion abnormality on imaging), while 'definite myocarditis' historically required a positive endomyocardial biopsy with lymphocytic infiltrate (p.2011).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2011","Harrison 22e p.2012","Harrison 22e p.2013","Harrison 22e p.2014"],"crossLinks":[]},"acute_angle_closure_glaucoma":{"name":"Acute angle-closure glaucoma","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AACG","acute angle closure","acute primary angle closure","acute closed-angle glaucoma","angle-closure crisis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with sudden severe unilateral eye pain, marked blurring, and haloes around lights, often with nausea, vomiting, and headache that can mimic a systemic illness","The affected eye is red with circumcorneal (ciliary) injection, a hazy oedematous cornea, and a fixed mid-dilated oval pupil","Intraocular pressure is markedly elevated, frequently well above 40 mmHg, and the globe feels stony hard to gentle palpation","Typically affects hypermetropic (long-sighted) eyes with shallow anterior chambers, and is often precipitated in dim light, by stress, or by pupil-dilating drugs","Immediate goal is to lower intraocular pressure with combined topical and systemic pressure-lowering therapy plus a topical miotic to break the attack","Definitive treatment is laser peripheral iridotomy once the cornea clears, and the fellow eye should receive prophylactic iridotomy because it is anatomically at risk","Gonioscopy of both eyes confirms a closed or occludable angle and distinguishes it from open-angle mechanisms"],"pathophysiology":"In predisposed eyes with a shallow anterior chamber and short axial length, the peripheral iris crowds the drainage angle. When the pupil becomes mid-dilated, relative pupillary block impedes aqueous flow from the posterior to the anterior chamber, so pressure builds behind the iris and bows it forward against the trabecular meshwork. This apposition abruptly obstructs aqueous outflow, causing a rapid and severe rise in intraocular pressure that compromises the optic nerve head and corneal endothelium if not relieved promptly.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute anterior uveitis","Acute bacterial conjunctivitis","Corneal abrasion or ulcer","Scleritis","Endophthalmitis","Cluster headache or migraine (for the pain component)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Applanation or non-contact tonometry to quantify the raised intraocular pressure","Slit-lamp examination to assess corneal oedema, chamber depth, and pupil reaction","Gonioscopy of both eyes to demonstrate the occludable/closed angle","Optic disc assessment for glaucomatous cupping once the media clear","Anterior segment OCT or ultrasound biomicroscopy to document angle configuration"],"redFlags":["Sight-threatening ophthalmic emergency — irreversible optic nerve damage can occur within hours of sustained high pressure","Stony-hard eye with a fixed mid-dilated pupil and corneal haze requires immediate ophthalmology referral","Systemic nausea and vomiting can mask the ocular origin and delay treatment","Failure of the fellow eye to receive prophylactic iridotomy risks a subsequent contralateral attack"],"prognosis":"Prompt reduction of pressure and timely iridotomy usually preserve vision, but delayed treatment can cause permanent optic nerve damage and irreversible loss of sight.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the vomiting and headache for a gastrointestinal or neurological illness and missing the ocular cause","Pharmacologically dilating a shallow-chambered eye and precipitating an attack","Forgetting prophylactic treatment of the anatomically predisposed fellow eye","Assuming a normal pressure once the acute attack settles without confirming the angle is open"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"acute_appendicitis":{"name":"Acute Appendicitis and Peritonitis","system":"Gastroenterology / Surgical","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["appendicitis","peritonitis"],"chapter":342,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2594-2598","clinicalPearls":["Classic history is migration of vague, intermittent, crampy epigastric or periumbilical pain to the right lower quadrant over 12-24 h, where it becomes sharper and localizes as transmural inflammation irritates the parietal peritoneum (p.2595).","Maximal tenderness is classically at or near McBurney's point in the right lower quadrant, about one-third of the way along a line from the anterior iliac spine to the umbilicus (p.2596).","Nausea, if present, characteristically follows the onset of abdominal pain (helping distinguish appendicitis from gastroenteritis, in which nausea precedes pain); emesis is typically mild and scant and also occurs after pain onset (p.2595).","Anorexia is so common that the diagnosis of appendicitis should be questioned in its absence (p.2595).","Rovsing's sign: palpation/gentle pressure in the left lower quadrant elicits pain in the right lower quadrant (p.2596).","Iliopsoas sign: extending the right hip causes pain along the posterolateral back and hip, suggesting a retrocecal appendix (p.2596).","Obturator sign: internal rotation of the hip causes pain, suggesting an inflamed appendix located in the pelvis (p.2596).","Patients with appendicitis tend to lie quite still to avoid peritoneal irritation; some report discomfort with a bumpy car ride, coughing, sneezing, or Valsalva-type maneuvers. Parietal peritoneal irritation is often best elicited by gentle percussion, jiggling the bed/gurney, or mildly bumping the feet (p.2596).","Simple appendicitis patients usually appear only mildly ill with pulse and temperature only slightly above normal; temperature >38.3 C (~101 F) or rigors should raise concern for another process or for complications such as perforation, phlegmon, or abscess (p.2596).","Examine the entire abdomen systematically, starting in a non-tender area; all patients should have a rectal examination, and a pelvic examination in women is mandatory to exclude PID, ectopic pregnancy, and ovarian torsion (p.2596).","Appendicitis should be included in the differential diagnosis of abdominal pain for every patient of any age unless it is certain the appendix has been previously removed (p.2595)."],"pathophysiology":"Etiology is not completely understood. Obstruction of the appendiceal lumen (by fecaliths/appendicoliths, incompletely digested food residue, lymphoid hyperplasia, intraluminal scarring, tumors, bacteria, viruses, or inflammatory bowel disease) is believed, though not proven, to be an important early step at least in some cases. Obstruction leads to bacterial overgrowth and luminal distension with increased intraluminal pressure that can inhibit lymph and blood flow; vascular thrombosis and ischemic necrosis with perforation of the distal appendix may then follow. Perforation near the base of the appendix should raise concern for another disease process. Appendicoliths are found in ~50% of patients with gangrenous appendicitis who perforate but are rarely seen in simpler disease, suggesting differing underlying pathophysiology. Uncomplicated appendicitis does not always progress to perforation; some cases of simple acute appendicitis may temporarily resolve or be managed with antibiotics, though at least one-third will subsequently require appendectomy. When perforation occurs, the leak may be contained by omentum or surrounding tissues to form an abscess, whereas free perforation normally causes severe peritonitis (p.2594-2595).","additionalDifferentials":["Crohn's disease","Cholecystitis or other gallbladder disease","Diverticulitis","Ectopic pregnancy","Endometriosis","Gastroenteritis or colitis","Gastric or duodenal ulceration","Hepatitis","Kidney disease including nephrolithiasis","Liver abscess","Meckel's diverticulitis","Mittelschmerz","Mesenteric adenitis","Omental torsion","Pancreatitis","Lower lobe pneumonia","Pelvic inflammatory disease","Ruptured ovarian cyst or other cystic ovarian disease","Small-bowel obstruction","Urinary tract infection","Ovarian torsion (Harrison 22e p.2596)"],"infectionMimics":["Gastroenteritis or colitis (nausea precedes pain, unlike appendicitis) (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Pelvic inflammatory disease (Harrison 22e p.2596)","Urinary tract infection / genitourinary infection (Harrison 22e p.2596)","Mesenteric adenitis (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Hepatitis / liver abscess (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Lower lobe pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.2595)","COVID-19 infection occasionally coincident (Harrison 22e p.2595)","In immunocompromised patients: atypical infections from mycobacteria, Cytomegalovirus, or fungi, and neutropenic enterocolitis (Harrison 22e p.2596)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Ectopic pregnancy (Harrison 22e p.2596)","Ovarian torsion (Harrison 22e p.2596)","Ruptured ovarian cyst / cystic ovarian disease (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Mittelschmerz (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Endometriosis (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Nephrolithiasis / ureterolithiasis (Harrison 22e p.2595-2596)","Small-bowel obstruction (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Omental torsion (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2595)","Appendiceal tumors / neoplasms (incidence increasing) (Harrison 22e p.2594)"],"additionalInvestigations":["WBC count: only mildly to moderately elevated (~10,000-18,000 cells/uL) in ~70% of simple appendicitis; laboratory testing alone does not identify patients with appendicitis (Harrison 22e p.2596).","A left shift toward immature polymorphonuclear leukocytes is present in >95% of cases (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Serum amylase and lipase should be measured; sickle cell preparation may be prudent in patients of African, Spanish, Mediterranean, or Indian ancestry (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Urinalysis to help exclude genitourinary mimics; a few red or white cells may be a nonspecific finding, and an inflamed appendix abutting the ureter or bladder may cause sterile pyuria or hematuria (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Pregnancy test in every woman of childbearing age; cervical cultures if PID is suspected (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Anemia and guaiac-positive stools should raise concern for other disease or complications such as cancer (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Ultrasonography: highly operator dependent; overall sensitivity ~0.86 and specificity ~0.81; appendix may not be visualized even in skilled hands; especially useful (including intravaginal techniques) for pelvic pathology in women. Suggestive findings: wall thickening, increased appendiceal diameter, free fluid (Harrison 22e p.2596).","CT: sensitivity at least 0.94 and specificity at least 0.95; high negative predictive value; suggestive findings include dilatation >6 mm with wall thickening, a lumen that does not fill with enteric contrast, and periappendiceal fatty stranding or air. Presence of luminal air or contrast is NOT consistent with appendicitis; nonvisualization of the appendix is nonspecific and should NOT be used to rule out appendiceal/periappendiceal inflammation (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Plain abdominal films are rarely helpful and not routinely obtained; <5% show an opaque fecalith, and a fecalith is not diagnostic of appendicitis (Harrison 22e p.2596).","For peritonitis: in stable patients with ascites, diagnostic paracentesis is indicated with testing of fluid for protein and LDH and a cell count; radiographs may show bowel dilatation and wall edema, and free air or other leakage may represent a surgical emergency (Harrison 22e p.2597)."],"redFlags":["Free perforation normally causes severe (generalized) peritonitis; perforation risk is ~10-20% and is significantly higher in patients <5 or >65 years of age (Harrison 22e p.2594).","Temperature >38.3 C (~101 F) or rigors suggest a complication (perforation, phlegmon, abscess) or another diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Persistent symptoms not improved over 48 h are more likely to perforate or develop other complications (Harrison 22e p.2595).","Rare but dreaded complication: infective suppurative thrombosis of the portal vein and its tributaries with intrahepatic abscesses, with very poor prognosis (Harrison 22e p.2595).","Diffuse abdominal tenderness with guarding and rigidity indicates generalized peritonitis; tachycardia, hypotension/volume depletion, significant leukocytosis, and severe acidosis are signs of serious disease (Harrison 22e p.2597).","In pregnancy, perforation raises fetal mortality fourfold (from ~5% to ~20%) (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Postoperative fever and leukocytosis persisting beyond 5 days should raise concern for an intraabdominal abscess (Harrison 22e p.2597)."],"prognosis":"Mortality from simple appendicitis is currently <1%, and for uncomplicated nonperforated appendicitis the operative mortality rate is 0.1-0.5% (approximating the risk of general anesthesia). Mortality for perforated or otherwise complicated disease is much higher, ranging from ~3% overall to as high as 15% in the elderly. For acute peritonitis, mortality can be <10% in reasonably healthy patients with relatively uncomplicated, localized disease but >40% in the elderly or immunocompromised (Harrison 22e p.2597).","pitfalls":["Atypical presentations and pain patterns are common, especially in the very old and very young; signs and symptoms can be subtle in the elderly, who may not react vigorously, with nausea/anorexia/emesis predominating (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Retrocecal or pelvic (below the pelvic brim) appendix may prompt very little anterior abdominal wall tenderness; pelvic appendicitis may present with dysuria, urinary frequency, diarrhea, or tenesmus and only suprapubic or rectal/pelvic-exam tenderness (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Appendiceal location varies widely (right lower quadrant, pelvis, right flank, right upper quadrant, or even the left side with malrotation/redundant colon), influencing presentation; delay in diagnosis is more likely when the appendix is unusually positioned (Harrison 22e p.2595).","Diagnosing appendicitis in children is challenging (dramatic response to stimulation, difficult history); the smaller pediatric omentum may be less able to wall off a perforation (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Pregnancy: appendicitis is the most common extrauterine general surgical emergency in pregnancy; early symptoms (nausea, anorexia) may be overlooked and the enlarging/gravid uterus may displace the appendix higher and obscure typical findings (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Immunocompromised patients may present with only mild tenderness; do not be overly cautious and delay operation in those believed to have appendicitis (Harrison 22e p.2596).","Laboratory testing alone does not identify appendicitis, and no currently available decision tool obviates the need for expert clinical opinion (Harrison 22e p.2596).","A fecalith is not diagnostic of appendicitis, though its presence in an appropriate location with pain is suggestive and associated with greater likelihood of complications (Harrison 22e p.2596)."],"severityClassification":"No formal grading scale is presented; disease is broadly distinguished as simple/uncomplicated appendicitis (without gangrenous necrosis, appendicoliths, perforation, or tumors) versus complicated disease (gangrene, perforation, phlegmon, abscess). Peritonitis is classified as secondary (most commonly from perforation of a hollow viscus, e.g., appendix, colonic diverticula, stomach/duodenum) versus primary or spontaneous (no identifiable intraabdominal source; >90% occur in patients with ascites or hypoproteinemia <1 g/L), and either type may be localized or diffuse; aseptic/chemical peritonitis (e.g., from gastric juice, bile, pancreatic enzymes, blood, or urine) is also distinguished (Harrison 22e p.2595-2597).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2594-2598"],"crossLinks":["bowel_obstruction"]},"acute_epiglottitis":{"name":"Acute Epiglottitis","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["epiglottitis","supraglottitis","acute supraglottitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rapidly progressive, life-threatening bacterial infection of the epiglottis and supraglottic structures that can cause sudden complete airway obstruction.","Historically caused by Haemophilus influenzae type b; its incidence has fallen dramatically since Hib immunisation, so other organisms and unimmunised children now feature prominently.","Classic presentation is abrupt onset of high fever, severe sore throat, drooling, muffled 'hot potato' voice, and difficulty swallowing, usually without the barking cough of croup.","The child characteristically sits upright in a tripod position, leaning forward with the neck extended to maximise the airway.","This is an airway emergency: keep the child calm, avoid distressing procedures such as throat examination or venepuncture, and do not lay the child flat.","Definitive airway management is secured in a controlled setting, ideally the operating theatre with experienced anaesthetic and ENT support, before other interventions.","Once the airway is secure, broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics targeting likely pathogens are given.","A lateral neck radiograph may show the classic 'thumbprint' sign, but imaging must never delay airway management and is unnecessary in an unstable child.","Direct visualisation of a cherry-red swollen epiglottis confirms the diagnosis but should only be performed where the airway can be immediately secured."],"pathophysiology":"Bacterial invasion of the supraglottic tissues produces intense inflammation and oedema of the epiglottis, aryepiglottic folds, and surrounding structures. Because these tissues sit at the laryngeal inlet, swelling rapidly narrows the airway, and the loose submucosa allows oedema to accumulate quickly. Progressive obstruction, worsened by agitation and secretions, can culminate in acute total airway occlusion.","additionalDifferentials":["Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis)","Bacterial tracheitis","Retropharyngeal or peritonsillar abscess","Foreign body aspiration","Anaphylaxis with airway angioedema","Diphtheria (in unimmunised populations)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Direct laryngoscopy under controlled conditions to confirm the diagnosis and secure the airway","Blood cultures and epiglottic surface cultures after the airway is protected","Lateral neck radiograph only if the child is stable and it will not delay care","Full blood count and inflammatory markers once safe to obtain","Hib and other relevant serological or PCR testing where indicated"],"redFlags":["Stridor, drooling, and respiratory distress indicating imminent obstruction","Tripod positioning and refusal to lie flat","Cyanosis, exhaustion, or decreasing level of consciousness","Muffled voice with inability to swallow secretions","Rapid clinical deterioration"],"prognosis":"With prompt airway control and antibiotics, recovery is usually rapid and complete; outcomes are poor if the airway obstructs before it is secured, making early recognition critical.","pitfalls":["Examining the throat or performing painful procedures that can precipitate obstruction","Laying the child supine or forcing separation from a caregiver","Delaying airway management to obtain imaging or bloods","Assuming the diagnosis is excluded in an immunised child, since non-Hib causes still occur"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"acute_glomerulonephritis_child":{"name":"Acute Glomerulonephritis (Child)","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis","APSGN","acute nephritic syndrome","paediatric acute glomerulonephritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The classic nephritic picture is gross haematuria (often described as tea- or cola-coloured urine), oedema, and hypertension.","Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is the most common cause in children and typically follows a group A streptococcal throat infection by about 1-2 weeks or a skin infection by 3-6 weeks.","Although labelled non-infective as a glomerular process, it is an immune-mediated sequela of a preceding streptococcal infection rather than active kidney infection.","Look for evidence of recent streptococcal infection with elevated antibody titres (such as ASO) and a low serum C3 that typically normalises within 6-8 weeks.","Fluid overload from sodium and water retention can precipitate hypertension, pulmonary oedema, and hypertensive encephalopathy.","Urinalysis characteristically shows red blood cells, red cell casts, and mild to moderate proteinuria.","Management is largely supportive with salt and fluid restriction and control of hypertension; the condition is generally self-limiting in children.","Persistently low complement beyond 8 weeks should prompt consideration of an alternative diagnosis such as membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis or lupus nephritis."],"pathophysiology":"Immune complexes involving streptococcal antigens deposit in or form within the glomerular basement membrane, activating the complement cascade (predominantly the alternative pathway). This drives glomerular inflammation with endothelial and mesangial proliferation and neutrophil influx, reducing the glomerular filtration surface. The result is a fall in filtration rate, sodium and water retention, haematuria, and proteinuria producing the nephritic syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["IgA nephropathy","Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","Lupus nephritis","Henoch-Schonlein (IgA) vasculitis nephritis","Haemolytic uraemic syndrome","Alport syndrome or thin basement membrane disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis and microscopy for red cell casts and dysmorphic red cells","Serum complement (C3 and C4)","Evidence of streptococcal infection (ASO, anti-DNase B, throat or skin swab)","Renal function and electrolytes","Urine protein quantification","Renal biopsy if the course is atypical, complement stays low, or renal function deteriorates"],"redFlags":["Severe hypertension or hypertensive encephalopathy (headache, seizures, altered consciousness)","Pulmonary oedema or respiratory distress from volume overload","Rapidly rising creatinine suggesting rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis","Oligoanuria or features of acute kidney injury requiring dialysis","Hyperkalaemia with associated arrhythmia risk"],"prognosis":"The great majority of children with post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis recover completely, with resolution of gross haematuria within weeks and normalisation of complement within about two months; microscopic haematuria may persist for a year or more. A small minority develop chronic kidney impairment.","pitfalls":["Assuming all paediatric nephritis is post-streptococcal and missing lupus or membranoproliferative disease when complement stays persistently low","Overlooking hypertension, which may be asymptomatic yet dangerous, by not measuring blood pressure","Fluid overloading a child with impaired filtration","Attributing dark urine to dehydration rather than examining the urine sediment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"acute_infectious_diarrheal_diseases_and":{"name":"Acute Infectious Diarrheal Diseases and Bacterial Food Poisoning","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute infectious diarrhea","acute gastroenteritis","bacterial food poisoning","infectious diarrhea"],"chapter":138,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1076-1081","clinicalPearls":["Diarrhea is defined as the passage of three or more loose or watery stools (conforming to the shape of the container) within a 24-hour period; illness lasting more than 2 weeks is generally classified as chronic and shifts the likely differential away from typical acute infectious causes (Harrison 22e p.1078, 1079).","The first practical step is to separate noninflammatory from inflammatory disease: watery diarrhea with no fecal leukocytes and minimal/no fecal lactoferrin points to proximal small-bowel enterotoxin-mediated disease, whereas fecal polymorphonuclear leukocytes and high fecal lactoferrin point to colonic invasion or cytotoxin (dysentery/inflammatory diarrhea) (Harrison 22e p.1077-1079).","Infective dose varies enormously and shapes transmission: as few as 10-100 organisms/cysts of Shigella, enterohemorrhagic E. coli, Giardia, or Entamoeba cause infection and allow person-to-person spread, while 10^5-10^8 Vibrio cholerae must be ingested, often requiring growth in food first (Harrison 22e p.1076).","Neurotoxin-mediated food poisoning (Staphylococcus aureus, Bacillus cereus emetic form) is caused by toxin preformed outside the host, giving the shortest incubation (1-6 h), prominent vomiting, and usually no fever (Harrison 22e p.1076-1077, 1080).","Stool appearance is diagnostically informative: bloody/mucoid stool suggests large-bowel ulceration/inflammation; profuse rice-water stools suggest cholera or a similar toxigenic process; bulky white/gray stools suggest a malabsorptive small-bowel process (Harrison 22e p.1078).","Bloody stools WITHOUT fecal leukocytes should specifically raise suspicion for Shiga toxin-producing enterohemorrhagic E. coli (e.g., O157:H7) (Harrison 22e p.1078).","Severity is judged largely by dehydration: thirst and dry mouth indicate mild dehydration; orthostatic blood-pressure fall, skin tenting, and sunken eyes/fontanelle indicate moderate dehydration; lethargy, obtundation, feeble pulse, hypotension, and shock indicate severe dehydration (Harrison 22e p.1078).","Recent or current antibiotic use shifts suspicion toward Clostridioides difficile; C. difficile is the predominant cause of nosocomial diarrhea in U.S. adults, and stool culture for other pathogens in the hospital setting is low-yield (Harrison 22e p.1078-1080).","Host defenses against enteric pathogens include the intestinal microbiota (colonization resistance, >99% anaerobic colonic flora with acidic pH/volatile fatty acids), gastric acid, peristalsis, the mucus barrier, mucosal/secretory IgA immunity, and host genetics (Harrison 22e p.1077).","Blood group O confers increased susceptibility to disease from V. cholerae, Shigella, E. coli O157, and norovirus (Harrison 22e p.1077).","Salmonella Typhi and Yersinia enterocolitica can penetrate intact mucosa, multiply in Peyer patches/lymph nodes, and disseminate to cause enteric fever - fever, headache, relative bradycardia, abdominal pain, splenomegaly, and leukopenia (Harrison 22e p.1077).","Cholera toxin is the prototype enterotoxin: its A subunit ADP-ribosylates a GTP-binding protein, persistently activating adenylate cyclase, raising cyclic AMP, increasing Cl- secretion and decreasing Na+ absorption to drive secretory diarrhea; E. coli LT acts identically, while ST acts via guanylate cyclase and cyclic GMP (Harrison 22e p.1076-1077)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute infectious diarrhea results from a contest between ingested enteric pathogens and host defenses. Pathogens overcome defenses through small infective inoculum, adherence/colonization factors (e.g., V. cholerae toxin-coregulated pilus; enterotoxigenic E. coli colonization factor antigen; attaching-effacing lesions of enteropathogenic and enterohemorrhagic E. coli), and the elaboration of toxins or direct invasion. Three broad mechanisms produce three clinical patterns: (1) Noninflammatory enterotoxin-mediated disease in the proximal small bowel causes watery diarrhea without fecal leukocytes - the cholera-toxin/LT pathway elevates cyclic AMP (increased Cl- secretion, decreased Na+ absorption) and ST elevates cyclic GMP. (2) Inflammatory disease in the colon/distal small bowel results from cytotoxins (e.g., Shiga toxin of S. dysenteriae type 1 and Shiga toxin-producing E. coli, V. parahaemolyticus, C. difficile) or from mucosal invasion (Shigella, enteroinvasive E. coli, Salmonella), producing dysentery with fecal leukocytes and blood. (3) Penetrating organisms (Salmonella Typhi, Y. enterocolitica) traverse intact mucosa, multiply in lymphoid tissue, and disseminate to cause enteric fever. Neurotoxins (staphylococcal, B. cereus emetic) are typically preformed outside the host and act on the nervous system to cause rapid-onset vomiting. Host protection depends on the intestinal microbiota's colonization resistance, gastric acidity, intestinal motility/peristalsis, the mucin barrier, mucosal and systemic immunity, and genetic factors; impairment of any of these (achlorhydria/acid suppression, antimotility drugs, antibiotic-altered flora, immunocompromise) raises infection risk and severity.","additionalDifferentials":["Noninflammatory watery diarrhea: enterotoxigenic and enteroaggregative E. coli, Vibrio cholerae, Clostridium perfringens, Bacillus cereus, Staphylococcus aureus, Aeromonas hydrophila, Plesiomonas shigelloides, rotavirus, norovirus, enteric adenovirus, Giardia, Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, microsporidia","Inflammatory diarrhea/dysentery: Shigella, Salmonella, Campylobacter jejuni, enterohemorrhagic E. coli, enteroinvasive E. coli, Yersinia enterocolitica, Listeria monocytogenes, Vibrio parahaemolyticus, Clostridioides difficile, Entamoeba histolytica, Klebsiella oxytoca","Enteric fever (penetrating): Salmonella Typhi, Yersinia enterocolitica","Traveler's diarrhea: most often enterotoxigenic and enteroaggregative E. coli, with Campylobacter jejuni notably common in Asia","Nonbacterial / noninfectious food poisoning: capsaicin (hot peppers) and fish/shellfish toxins"],"infectionMimics":["Malaria (fever and diarrhea, and prominent vomiting, may occur with infection outside the GI tract)","Clostridioides difficile / antibiotic-associated colitis in patients on or recently on antibiotics","Yersinia enterocolitica infection presenting as an appendicitis-like (pseudoappendicitis) syndrome"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inflammatory bowel disease (acute diarrheal illness may be its initial presentation or an exacerbation, sometimes precipitated by traveler's diarrhea)","Intestinal obstruction (vomiting prominent)","Colonic diverticulitis","Graft-versus-host disease","Noninfectious food poisoning from capsaicin and fish/shellfish toxins","Irritable bowel syndrome (postinfectious)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool examination for gross blood/mucus and a fecal leukocyte smear (thin smear + methylene blue wet mount) to identify inflammatory disease, although its predictive value is debated","Fecal lactoferrin (latex agglutination or ELISA) - a more sensitive marker of fecal leukocytes than the WBC smear","Stool culture for Salmonella, Shigella, and Campylobacter in febrile inflammatory diarrhea (MacConkey/Salmonella-Shigella agar/selenite broth; Campylobacter requires 42 degrees C microaerophilic culture)","Selective culture for V. cholerae on TCBS or TTG agar, plus rapid diagnostic tests when cholera is suspected","Sorbitol-MacConkey culture and/or Shiga toxin/PCR testing of visibly bloody stools for E. coli O157:H7","C. difficile toxin A/B testing (enzyme immunoassay, latex agglutination, or PCR) for nosocomial or antibiotic-associated diarrhea","Rapid antigen, immunofluorescence, or PCR (and less sensitive microscopy) for Giardia, Cryptosporidium, and Entamoeba histolytica","Multiplex nucleic acid amplification panels for viral/bacterial/parasitic pathogens - faster and more sensitive than culture, but may detect multiple organisms and complicate interpretation; CDC advises follow-up culture to allow susceptibility testing and outbreak typing"],"redFlags":["Signs of severe dehydration: lethargy, obtundation, feeble pulse, hypotension, and shock","Fever, which often signals invasive disease","Bloody or mucoid stools (dysentery) indicating large-bowel ulceration","Bloody stools without fecal leukocytes - alert for Shiga toxin-producing enterohemorrhagic E. coli and risk of hemolytic-uremic syndrome","Diarrhea in an immunocompromised host (cell-mediated immunity defects/AIDS, hypogammaglobulinemia, cancer), where infection can be life-threatening with persistent infection, bacteremia, and metastatic seeding","Profuse rice-water stools suggesting cholera, with risk of rapid life-threatening volume loss","Diarrhea in infants <2 years with moderate dehydration, fever >24 h, bloody stools, or prolonged duration"],"prognosis":"Most acute diarrheal episodes are mild and self-limited. Traveler's diarrhea is typically self-limited, lasting 1-5 days, and Clostridium perfringens food poisoning rarely lasts more than 24 h; toxin-mediated staphylococcal/B. cereus emetic illness generally lasts under 12 h. Globally, however, acute diarrheal disease remains a leading cause of illness, associated with an estimated 1.57 million deaths per year, and in low- and middle-income countries it accounts for about one in ten deaths in children under 5; recurrent intestinal infection contributes to stunting, wasting, micronutrient deficiency, and malnutrition. Outcome is worse in immunocompromised hosts, where dehydration can also compromise renal function and increase immunosuppressive drug toxicity. Postdiarrhea complications include chronic diarrhea (~1% of travelers; protozoa account for about one-third), postinfectious irritable bowel syndrome (~10% of those with traveler's diarrhea), reactive arthritis (especially after Shigella, Salmonella, Campylobacter, Yersinia), hemolytic-uremic syndrome (Shiga toxin-producing bacteria), and Guillain-Barre syndrome (especially after Campylobacter).","pitfalls":["Relying on the fecal leukocyte smear alone - its predictive value is debated, and fecal lactoferrin is more sensitive (Harrison 22e p.1079).","Routine stool culture cannot distinguish pathogenic E. coli from normal flora, and enterotoxin assays are unavailable in most clinical labs, so a negative routine culture does not exclude E. coli disease (Harrison 22e p.1080).","Sending broad stool cultures for nosocomial diarrhea is low-yield and not cost-effective; initial workup should focus on C. difficile (Harrison 22e p.1080).","Antimotility agents (e.g., diphenoxylate-atropine) and opioids can prolong fever and organism shedding in Shigella infection and increase bacteremia risk in Salmonella - impaired motility favors bacterial overgrowth (Harrison 22e p.1077).","Multiplex NAAT panels may detect multiple pathogens, complicating interpretation, and the absence of an isolate prevents susceptibility testing and outbreak typing - CDC advises follow-up culture (Harrison 22e p.1080).","Reheating/frying contaminated fried rice may not destroy preformed heat-stable B. cereus toxin or its heat-resistant spores (Harrison 22e p.1080).","Missing infection outside the GI tract (e.g., malaria) as a cause of fever and diarrhea/vomiting (Harrison 22e p.1078)."],"severityClassification":"Severity is graded clinically by degree of dehydration (Harrison 22e p.1078). Mild dehydration: thirst, dry mouth, decreased axillary sweat, decreased urine output, slight weight loss. Moderate dehydration: orthostatic fall in blood pressure, skin tenting, sunken eyes (or sunken fontanelle in infants). Severe dehydration: lethargy, obtundation, feeble pulse, hypotension, and shock. The complementary classification is mechanistic/syndromic per Table 138-1: noninflammatory (enterotoxin, proximal small bowel, watery diarrhea, no fecal leukocytes), inflammatory (invasion or cytotoxin, colon/distal small bowel, dysentery, fecal PMNs and high lactoferrin), and penetrating (distal small bowel, enteric fever, fecal mononuclear cells).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1076","Harrison 22e p.1077","Harrison 22e p.1078","Harrison 22e p.1079","Harrison 22e p.1080","Harrison 22e p.1081"],"crossLinks":[]},"acute_intestinal_obstruction":{"name":"Acute Intestinal Obstruction","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Bowel obstruction","Mechanical bowel obstruction","Small-bowel obstruction","Large-bowel obstruction","Ileus","Pseudo-obstruction"],"chapter":341,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2589","clinicalPearls":["The cardinal features are colicky abdominal pain, abdominal distention, emesis, and obstipation; distal obstruction tends to produce more distention and later, sometimes feculent, vomiting, whereas proximal obstruction produces more pronounced vomiting with less distention (Harrison 22e p.2591).","Obstruction is characterized as partial, high grade, or complete, which generally tracks with the risk of complications and the urgency of intervention; it is termed simple versus strangulated when vascular insufficiency and ischemia are present (Harrison 22e p.2589).","Roughly 80% of cases involve the small bowel, and about one-third of small-bowel cases show significant ischemia; obstruction accounts for about 1-3% of hospitalizations and a quarter of urgent/emergent general surgery admissions (Harrison 22e p.2589).","Postoperative adhesions are the single most common cause (>50% overall), followed by neoplasms (~20%) and hernias (~10%); lower abdominal operations (appendectomy, colorectal, gynecologic) are especially adhesiogenic (Harrison 22e p.2589-2590).","Classic early small-bowel obstruction produces high-pitched 'musical' tinkling bowel sounds and peristaltic rushes (borborygmi), which later become absent or hypoactive; in ileus/pseudo-obstruction sounds are typically absent or hypoactive from the start (Harrison 22e p.2591).","A tender abdominal or groin mass strongly suggests an incarcerated hernia; pain out of proportion to physical findings can mimic acute mesenteric ischemia (Harrison 22e p.2591).","Carcinomatosis causing obstruction most often originates from the ovary, but can arise from the pancreas, stomach, or colon, and rarely from distant sites such as breast or skin (Harrison 22e p.2589).","In adult intussusception the lead point is typically a polyp or tumor; in gallstone 'ileus' the most common obstruction site is the ileum (~60%), with the stone usually entering via a cholecystoduodenal fistula (Harrison 22e p.2590, 2593).","Sigmoid colon volvulus comprises about two-thirds of all volvulus cases and ~4% of large-bowel obstruction; risk factors include institutionalization, neuropsychiatric conditions on psychotropics, chronic constipation, and advanced age (seventies-eighties) (Harrison 22e p.2589-2590).","Stasis raises bacterial counts in the jejunum and ileum; organisms such as Escherichia coli, Streptococcus faecalis, and Klebsiella may be recovered from intestinal cultures, mesenteric nodes, and blood (Harrison 22e p.2590).","CT is the most frequently used modality, with sensitivity ~95% (78-100%) and specificity ~96% for high-grade obstruction, but accuracy for closed-loop obstruction is much lower (~60%) (Harrison 22e p.2592).","Contrast appearing within the cecum 4-24 h after oral water-soluble contrast predicts improvement with high (~95%) sensitivity and specificity (Harrison 22e p.2592)."],"pathophysiology":"Acute obstruction is either mechanical (extrinsic, intrinsic bowel-wall, or intraluminal lesions) or functional (dysmotility without blockage). Increased contractility proximal and distal to the block is characteristic; peristalsis then slows as proximal bowel dilates and fills with secretions and swallowed air, with additional luminal gas from fermentation and CO2 production. Rising intraluminal pressure exceeding venous pressure impairs venous and lymphatic drainage, producing wall edema and proximal hypoxemia, with epithelial necrosis identifiable within 12 h. Progressive arterial compromise can cause full-thickness ischemia, necrosis, and perforation. Inflammatory edema generates reactive oxygen species and recruits neutrophils and macrophages, disrupting secretory and neuromotor function. Loss of absorptive capacity plus fluid sequestration in the bowel wall and peritoneum causes dehydration; anorexia and emesis worsen volume depletion, and high-grade distal obstruction with vomiting causes loss of gastric potassium, hydrogen, and chloride while dehydration drives proximal tubular bicarbonate reabsorption (metabolic alkalosis). Closed-loop obstruction, in which both ends of a segment are occluded (e.g., volvulus, hernia), is the most common precursor to strangulation and carries high risk of vascular insufficiency and irreversible ischemia (Harrison 22e p.2590-2591).","additionalDifferentials":["Postoperative ileus (functional obstruction following intraabdominal surgery)","Colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie's syndrome)","Obstructive colon cancer (may mimic small-bowel obstruction when the ileocecal valve is incompetent)","Crohn's disease and other inflammatory strictures","Acute mesenteric ischemia (pain out of proportion to findings)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Adynamic ileus from metabolic/electrolyte disturbance (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hyponatremia, uremia, severe hyperglycemia)","Drug-induced dysmotility (opiates, antihistamines, anticholinergics, some psychotropics)","Colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie's syndrome)","Intestinal ischemia presenting with functional dysmotility","Collagen vascular disease (e.g., lupus, scleroderma) causing visceral dysmotility"],"additionalInvestigations":["Complete blood count and serum electrolytes/creatinine with serial assessment (mild hemoconcentration and slight leukocytosis are common in simple obstruction)","Upright/cross-table lateral abdominal radiographs ('staircasing' of dilated small-bowel loops >2.5 cm, air-fluid levels, 'coffee bean' shadow in volvulus)","CT with enteral and IV contrast (location, closed-loop, and ischemia features such as altered wall enhancement, mesenteric venous gas, pneumatosis, pneumoperitoneum)","Markers possibly suggestive of strangulation: serum D-lactate, creatine kinase BB isoenzyme, intestinal fatty acid binding protein","Contrast enema or colonoscopy for suspected colonic obstruction; ultrasound when radiation is contraindicated (e.g., pregnancy)"],"redFlags":["Fever, which raises concern for strangulation or systemic inflammation","Severe localized pain or peritoneal signs, suspicious for strangulated or closed-loop obstruction","Leukocytosis with immature forms plus metabolic acidosis, worrisome for ischemic necrosis and sepsis","Cecal diameter exceeding 10-12 cm in distal colonic obstruction with a competent ileocecal valve (impending ischemic perforation)","Free intraperitoneal air in a patient who has not recently had surgery (perforation)","Oliguria, hypotension, and tachycardia indicating severe intravascular volume depletion"],"prognosis":"Overall morbidity and mortality appear to be decreasing. The mortality rate for patients with strangulation operated on within 24-30 h of symptom onset is about 8% but roughly triples thereafter. Most postoperative adhesive obstructions are partial and resolve spontaneously, and about 60-80% of selected mechanical obstructions can be managed conservatively; recurrence requiring readmission within 10 years occurs in ~20% of conservatively treated and 5-30% of operatively managed patients. Major complications after operation occur in roughly 12-47%, with higher risk for resection therapies and for patients with ASA class III or higher (Harrison 22e p.2589, 2593).","pitfalls":["Barium should never be given orally until obstruction is excluded; in significant obstruction it may form concretions and renders later cross-sectional imaging or angiography uninterpretable (Harrison 22e p.2592-2593).","No laboratory test reliably distinguishes simple from strangulated obstruction; reliance on labs may delay needed intervention (Harrison 22e p.2592).","Bowel sounds are notoriously unreliable for diagnosis, and pain may be out of proportion to physical findings, masking strangulation or mimicking mesenteric ischemia (Harrison 22e p.2591).","Distal colonic obstruction with an incompetent ileocecal valve can present late and mimic distal small-bowel obstruction (Harrison 22e p.2591).","Imaging intervention should not be delayed when signs strongly suggest high-grade/complete obstruction or bowel compromise (Harrison 22e p.2592)."],"severityClassification":"Mechanical obstruction is graded as partial, high grade, or complete, correlating with complication risk and urgency. It is further classified as simple versus strangulated when vascular insufficiency and ischemia supervene, and closed-loop when both ends of a segment are occluded (highest risk of strangulation) (Harrison 22e p.2589-2590).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2589","Harrison 22e p.2590","Harrison 22e p.2591","Harrison 22e p.2592","Harrison 22e p.2593"],"crossLinks":[]},"acute_lymphoid_leukemia":{"name":"Acute Lymphoid Leukemia","system":"Hematologic/Oncologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia","ALL"],"chapter":111,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.844","clinicalPearls":["In ALL the malignant clone arises from hematopoietic progenitors in marrow or lymphatic tissue, producing immature nonfunctioning blasts; marrow infiltration causes anemia, granulocytopenia, and thrombocytopenia, so fatigue, infection, and hemorrhage (rather than tumor bulk) are usually the presenting complaints.","Tumor-bulk features such as lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly from leukemic infiltration, and CNS involvement (meningeosis leukemica) may occur but are less often the reason a patient first seeks care.","ALL is the most frequent neoplasm of childhood with an early peak at age 3-4 years; in adults the incidence ranges from about 0.7 to 1.8 per 100,000 per year, higher in adolescents/young adults and rising again in the elderly.","The WBC count is normal or reduced in roughly 40% of patients, and in about 8% no circulating blasts are seen, so automated counters may fail to flag the diagnosis; only about 16% present with WBC above 100 x 10^9/L.","Bone marrow is typically heavily packed, with more than 90% blasts in about 70% of patients and near-absent normal hematopoiesis; a biopsy is essential to confirm acute leukemia and distinguish ALL from AML.","Immunophenotyping is the principal tool for subclassification: more than 70% of adult ALL is B-lineage (common ALL being most frequent, CD10+), while about 25% is T-lineage (all express CD7 and cytoplasmic/surface CD3).","An immunologic marker is considered positive when more than 20% of cells stain with the monoclonal antibody.","Philadelphia chromosome-positive ALL (t(9;22), BCR-ABL1) is seen in roughly half of elderly B-lineage patients; subtype frequencies shift substantially with age.","Minimal residual disease detected by flow cytometry, RT-qPCR for clonal Ig/TCR rearrangements or fusion transcripts (limit ~0.1-0.001%, lower with NGS/ddPCR) is the most important prognostic factor during therapy; patient-specific markers should be banked from diagnostic marrow.","Achieving a molecular complete remission after induction predicts disease-free survival around 70% versus under 40% for MRD-positive patients, the single most relevant independent prognostic factor in both pediatric and adult ALL.","Mature B-ALL (Burkitt leukemia, ~3-4% of adults) expresses surface IgM, shows L3 morphology, and has short relapse kinetics (up to ~1-1.5 years) with frequent organ and CNS involvement.","Down syndrome carries a roughly 20-fold increased leukemia risk (ALL predominating in childhood, AML at older age), and other congenital disorders raising risk include Klinefelter, Fanconi anemia, Bloom syndrome, ataxia-telangiectasia, and neurofibromatosis."],"pathophysiology":"Malignant transformation of lymphoid progenitor cells in the bone marrow or lymphatic system produces a clone of immature, nonfunctioning lymphoblasts. Progressive marrow infiltration crowds out normal hematopoiesis, yielding anemia, neutropenia, and thrombocytopenia. Blasts may also infiltrate lymph nodes, liver, spleen, mediastinum, and the central nervous system (a pharmacologic sanctuary site). Subtypes correspond to arrest at defined stages of B-cell or T-cell differentiation and carry characteristic cytogenetic/molecular lesions such as t(9;22)/BCR-ABL1, t(4;11)/MLL-AF4, t(1;19)/PBX-E2A, and t(8;14) in mature B-ALL, as well as newer entities including early T-precursor (ETP) ALL and Ph-like (BCR-ABL1-like) ALL driven by IKZF1 deletion, CRLF2 overexpression, and tyrosine kinase-activating rearrangements (ABL1, JAK2, PDGFRB).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute myeloid leukemia (distinguished by marrow biopsy, cytochemistry, and immunophenotype)","Biphenotypic/mixed-phenotype acute leukemia (markers of both lymphoid and myeloid lineages on the same cells)","Bilineage leukemia (two separate blast populations, lymphoid and myeloid)","Lymphoblastic lymphoma (e.g., precursor T- or B-lymphoblastic lymphoma)","Chronic myeloid leukemia in lymphoid blast crisis","Aplastic anemia or other marrow failure presenting with pancytopenia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute myeloid leukemia","Lymphoblastic lymphoma with marrow involvement","Marrow failure syndromes presenting as pancytopenia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood count with Wright-Giemsa-stained differential smear at presentation","Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy for morphology, immunophenotype, cytogenetics, and molecular markers","Cytochemical stains and immunologic marker panel to assign B- versus T-lineage","Standard cytogenetics, FISH, and RT-PCR to detect t(9;22)/BCR-ABL1, t(4;11)/MLL, t(1;19)/PBX-E2A and other entities","Gene expression profiling, SNP array, array-CGH, and next-generation sequencing to identify ETP-ALL and Ph-like ALL","Lumbar puncture with CSF examination for CNS leukemia (diagnostic if >=5 cells/uL or blasts on morphology)","MRD assessment by flow cytometry or RT-qPCR/NGS/ddPCR with collection of patient-specific markers at diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Platelet count below 20 x 10^9/L, signaling high bleeding risk","Neutropenia with neutrophils below 0.5 x 10^9/L, signaling high infection risk","Hemoglobin below 7-8 g/dL (present in nearly one-third of patients)","Hyperleukocytosis (WBC above 100 x 10^9/L), more common in t(4;11) and T-ALL","CNS involvement at diagnosis (5-10% overall, higher in mature B-ALL and T-ALL)","Large mediastinal mass in T-ALL (frequent, up to ~60%) with potential airway/vascular compromise","Risk of tumor lysis syndrome at treatment initiation given high tumor burden"],"prognosis":"Outcome is strongly age-dependent, with cure rates around 90% in children falling to under 10% in elderly or frail patients. Prognostic stratification rests on age, WBC count at diagnosis, immunophenotype, cytogenetic/genetic lesions, and especially MRD response: molecular complete remission after induction predicts disease-free survival near 70% versus under 40% if MRD-positive. The greatest relapse risk is within the first 2 years and is much lower after 5 years. Relapse kinetics vary by subtype, being short (up to ~1-1.5 years) in mature B-ALL, intermediate (~3-4 years) in T-ALL, and prolonged (up to 5-7 years) in common ALL. CNS relapse, usually accompanied by marrow involvement, carries a dismal outcome.","pitfalls":["Because the WBC is normal or low in about 40% of patients and roughly 8% have no circulating blasts, automated blood counts may miss the diagnosis; a manual smear and marrow biopsy are required.","Failing to bank diagnostic marrow for patient-specific markers compromises later MRD follow-up.","Assuming an MRD-negative result guarantees cure: 20-30% of MRD-negative adults still relapse, attributable to loss of assay sensitivity, evolving subclones, or extramedullary disease.","Performing lumbar puncture without an adequate platelet count (>20 x 10^9/L) or in the presence of clinical hemorrhage; potentially seeded blasts should be addressed with intrathecal therapy at the first tap.","Overlooking that L1/L2 FAB morphology has no clinical bearing while only L3 morphology indicates mature B-ALL.","Misclassifying Ph-like ALL as Ph-positive: Ph-like ALL is BCR-ABL1 negative despite similar genetic lesions and requires molecular testing to identify."],"severityClassification":"Lineage/immunophenotypic subtyping (EGIL-type) divides ALL into B-lineage (pro-B, common, pre-B, mature B/Burkitt) and T-lineage (early/ETP, cortical-thymic, mature) categories. MRD-based response categories (Harrison Table 111-3) include complete hematologic remission (<5% marrow blasts), complete molecular remission/MRD negativity (<=0.01%, i.e. <=1 leukemic cell in 10,000 marrow cells), molecular failure/MRD positivity (>0.01% with hematologic remission), molecular relapse, and hematologic relapse (>5% blasts in marrow or blood). Risk stratification assigns standard-risk patients (no risk factors) versus high-risk patients (one or more of advanced age, high WBC, adverse immunophenotype, or adverse cytogenetic/genetic lesions).","references":["Harrison 22e p.844","Harrison 22e p.845","Harrison 22e p.846","Harrison 22e p.847"],"crossLinks":[]},"acute_meningitis":{"name":"Acute Meningitis","system":"Central Nervous System","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Bacterial meningitis","Viral meningitis","Aseptic meningitis","Meningoencephalitis"],"chapter":143,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1118","clinicalPearls":["The classic triad of fever, headache, and nuchal rigidity each occurs in more than 80% of adults with acute bacterial meningitis, but the complete triad is frequently absent; a depressed level of consciousness is seen in over 75% (Harrison 22e p.1119).","Acute presentation can be either fulminant over hours or subacute over several days; nausea, vomiting, and photophobia are common (Harrison 22e p.1119).","Nuchal rigidity is the pathognomonic sign of meningeal irritation; Kernig's and Brudzinski's signs are classic but of uncertain sensitivity/specificity and may be absent in the very young, elderly, immunocompromised, or obtunded (Harrison 22e p.1119-1120).","S. pneumoniae (~50%) and N. meningitidis (~25%) are the leading causes of community-acquired bacterial meningitis; group B streptococci (~15%) and L. monocytogenes (~10%) follow, with H. influenzae type b now <10% (Harrison 22e p.1118).","Petechial or purpuric skin lesions are an important clue to meningococcal infection; the rash starts as a maculopapular eruption resembling a viral exanthem then becomes petechial on trunk, extremities, mucosae, conjunctivae, and sometimes palms/soles (Harrison 22e p.1118, p.1120).","Listeria meningitis should be considered in neonates, pregnant women, those over 60, and immunocompromised hosts; infection is acquired from contaminated foods such as soft cheeses, unpasteurized milk, deli meats, and uncooked hotdogs (Harrison 22e p.1119).","Seizures occur in 15-40% of bacterial meningitis cases; focal seizures suggest focal ischemia/infarction, cortical venous thrombosis with hemorrhage, or focal edema, while generalized seizures may reflect hyponatremia or cerebral anoxia (Harrison 22e p.1120).","More than 90% of bacterial meningitis patients have a CSF opening pressure above 180 mmH2O, and 20% exceed 400 mmH2O; raised ICP is the major cause of obtundation/coma (Harrison 22e p.1120).","Viral meningitis is distinguished from encephalitis by the absence of stupor, coma, marked confusion, seizures, or focal neurologic signs; their presence points toward encephalitis or another process (Harrison 22e p.1123).","Profound CNS depression, seizures, or focal deficits should never be attributed to uncomplicated viral meningitis (Harrison 22e p.1123).","A lymphocytic pleocytosis combined with a low CSF glucose should raise suspicion for fungal, tuberculous, or Listeria meningitis or noninfectious causes such as sarcoid or neoplastic meningitis rather than typical viral meningitis (Harrison 22e p.1124).","Neurologic injury in bacterial meningitis can progress even after CSF sterilization because much of the damage stems from the host inflammatory response rather than direct bacterial injury (Harrison 22e p.1119)."],"pathophysiology":"Bacterial meningitis is an acute purulent infection of the subarachnoid space accompanied by a CNS inflammatory reaction that may cause decreased consciousness, seizures, raised ICP, and stroke, with meninges, subarachnoid space, and parenchyma often jointly involved (meningoencephalitis). The leading organisms (S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis) colonize the nasopharynx, cross or bypass epithelial tight junctions to enter the bloodstream, evade neutrophil phagocytosis and complement via a polysaccharide capsule, then reach the CSF through the choroid plexus or cerebral capillary endothelium. Bacteria multiply readily in CSF given its scarce leukocytes, complement, and immunoglobulin, which impair opsonization. Lysis of bacteria releases cell-wall components (LPS of gram-negatives, teichoic acid and peptidoglycan of pneumococci) that drive microglia, astrocytes, monocytes, and endothelium to produce TNF-alpha and IL-1 beta; these cytokines synergistically increase blood-brain barrier permeability (vasogenic edema, protein leakage), and the proteinaceous leukocytic exudate obstructs CSF flow and arachnoid resorption causing hydrocephalus and interstitial edema. Leukocyte adhesion and degranulation, excitatory amino acids, and reactive oxygen/nitrogen species produce cytotoxic edema and cell death (notably in the hippocampal dentate gyrus). Loss of cerebrovascular autoregulation, basal arterial narrowing/vasculitis with thrombosis, and venous/sinus thrombosis cause ischemia and infarction; combined interstitial, vasogenic, and cytotoxic edema raise ICP and may produce herniation (Harrison 22e p.1118-1120). Viral (aseptic) meningitis produces meningeal irritation with a CSF inflammatory profile but, by definition, does not produce the parenchymal dysfunction of encephalitis (Harrison 22e p.1123).","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex virus encephalitis (HSV-1) and other viral meningoencephalitides (Harrison 22e p.1121)","Rickettsial disease, notably Rocky Mountain spotted fever (R. rickettsii), and ehrlichiosis/anaplasmosis (Harrison 22e p.1121)","Focal suppurative CNS infections: subdural or epidural empyema and brain abscess (Harrison 22e p.1121)","Subarachnoid hemorrhage (the major noninfectious consideration) and pituitary apoplexy (Harrison 22e p.1121-1122)","Subacute meningitis from M. tuberculosis, Cryptococcus neoformans, Histoplasma capsulatum, Coccidioides immitis, and Treponema pallidum (Harrison 22e p.1122)","Mycoplasma pneumoniae CNS infection, which can mimic viral meningitis/encephalitis (Harrison 22e p.1124)"],"infectionMimics":["HSV encephalitis presenting with headache, fever, altered consciousness, focal deficits, and seizures (Harrison 22e p.1121)","Rocky Mountain spotted fever with high fever, headache, and a rash evolving from maculopapular to petechial/purpuric, easily confused with meningococcemia (Harrison 22e p.1121)","Ehrlichiosis/anaplasmosis with fever, headache, confusion, and leukopenia/thrombocytopenia with elevated transaminases (Harrison 22e p.1121)","Untreated or partially treated bacterial meningitis mimicking aseptic meningitis (Harrison 22e p.1123)","Early fungal, mycobacterial, or syphilitic (T. pallidum) meningitis with lymphocytic pleocytosis and not-yet-low glucose (Harrison 22e p.1123)","Meningitis due to Mycoplasma, Listeria, Brucella, Coxiella, Leptospira, and Rickettsia species (Harrison 22e p.1123)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Subarachnoid hemorrhage (Harrison 22e p.1121)","Medication-induced hypersensitivity meningitis (Harrison 22e p.1121)","Chemical meningitis from rupture of tumor contents (cystic glioma, craniopharyngioma, epidermoid or dermoid cyst) into CSF (Harrison 22e p.1121)","Carcinomatous or lymphomatous (neoplastic) meningitis (Harrison 22e p.1121)","Inflammatory disorders: sarcoidosis, systemic lupus erythematosus, Behcet's syndrome (Harrison 22e p.1121-1122)","Uveomeningitic syndromes including Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada syndrome, and pituitary apoplexy (Harrison 22e p.1122)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CSF examination is the diagnostic cornerstone; classic bacterial findings are PMN pleocytosis >100/uL (90%), low glucose <2.2 mmol/L (<40 mg/dL) and/or CSF/serum glucose ratio <0.4 (~60%), elevated protein >0.45 g/L (90%), and raised opening pressure >180 mmH2O (90%) (Harrison 22e p.1120-1121)","CSF Gram stain positive in >60% and bacterial culture positive in >70-80%; culture remains required for susceptibility testing (Harrison 22e p.1120-1121)","Blood cultures should be obtained immediately when bacterial meningitis is suspected (Harrison 22e p.1120)","CSF multiplex PCR (e.g., FilmArray Meningitis/Encephalitis panel) detects S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, E. coli, L. monocytogenes, H. influenzae, S. agalactiae, plus HSV-1/2, enteroviruses, VZV, HHV-6, and parechovirus, but does not replace culture (Harrison 22e p.1121, p.1124)","Neuroimaging before LP (CT or MRI) when immunocompromised, recent head trauma, depressed consciousness, papilledema, or focal deficits; MRI preferred for edema/ischemia and shows diffuse meningeal enhancement after gadolinium (Harrison 22e p.1120-1121)","For suspected viral meningitis: CBC with differential, liver and renal function, ESR, C-reactive protein, electrolytes, glucose, creatine kinase, aldolase, amylase, and lipase; CSF PCR is the diagnostic procedure of choice for enteroviral and HSV CNS infection (Harrison 22e p.1124)","Biopsy of petechial skin lesions may reveal organisms on Gram stain in meningococcemia (Harrison 22e p.1121)"],"redFlags":["Petechial or purpuric rash suggesting meningococcemia, which can be fulminant and fatal within hours of symptom onset (Harrison 22e p.1118)","Deteriorating consciousness, papilledema, dilated poorly reactive pupils, sixth nerve palsy, decerebrate posturing, or the Cushing reflex (bradycardia, hypertension, irregular respirations) indicating raised ICP (Harrison 22e p.1120)","Cerebral herniation, the most disastrous complication of raised ICP, reported in 1-8% of bacterial meningitis cases (Harrison 22e p.1120)","Focal neurologic findings, which mandate prompt MRI to detect focal suppurative infection (Harrison 22e p.1121)","Seizures or status epilepticus at presentation (Harrison 22e p.1120)","Stupor, coma, or marked confusion in a presumed viral meningitis, which instead suggest encephalitis or an alternative diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1123)"],"prognosis":"Mortality from bacterial meningitis varies by organism: about 3-7% for H. influenzae, N. meningitidis, or group B streptococci; ~15% for L. monocytogenes; and ~20% for S. pneumoniae. Risk of death rises with decreased consciousness on admission, seizures within 24 h, signs of raised ICP, age in infancy or over 50, comorbidities such as shock or need for mechanical ventilation, and delayed treatment; very low CSF glucose (<2.2 mmol/L) and very high CSF protein (>3 g/L) also predict worse outcomes. Moderate-to-severe sequelae occur in roughly 25% of survivors, including impaired intellect, memory loss, seizures, hearing loss, dizziness, and gait disturbance. In adults, recovery from viral meningitis is excellent, with only rare lingering headache, mild cognitive complaints, or asthenia; outcomes in infants/neonates under 1 year are less certain, with reported intellectual impairment, learning disability, and hearing loss (Harrison 22e p.1123, p.1125).","pitfalls":["Awaiting LP or neuroimaging to start therapy: blood cultures and empirical treatment must not be delayed in suspected bacterial meningitis (Harrison 22e p.1120)","Assuming antibiotics given a few hours before LP invalidate the CSF; they do not significantly alter WBC count or glucose, nor reliably prevent Gram stain or PCR detection (Harrison 22e p.1120)","Relying on multiplex PCR alone: it omits S. aureus, coagulase-negative staphylococci, and many gram-negatives, and cannot provide susceptibility data (Harrison 22e p.1121)","Interpreting meningeal enhancement on MRI as diagnostic of meningitis; it occurs in any condition with increased blood-brain barrier permeability (Harrison 22e p.1121)","Overreliance on Kernig's and Brudzinski's signs, which have uncertain sensitivity/specificity and may be absent in vulnerable groups; cervical spine disease in the elderly can cause false-positive nuchal rigidity (Harrison 22e p.1119-1120)","Misreading an early PMN pleocytosis in viral meningitis (e.g., echovirus 9, WNV, EEE, mumps) as bacterial; a PMN-predominant CSF without a diagnosis should prompt consideration of bacterial or parameningeal infection (Harrison 22e p.1123-1124)","Forgetting that giving IV 50% glucose before LP can take hours to alter CSF glucose, so it rarely confounds early sampling (Harrison 22e p.1120-1121)"],"severityClassification":"No single formal grading scale is given for acute meningitis; instead Harrison enumerates prognostic risk factors for death in bacterial meningitis (decreased consciousness on admission, seizures within 24 h, raised ICP, age <1 or >50, shock or need for ventilation, treatment delay, CSF glucose <2.2 mmol/L, CSF protein >3 g/L). For distinguishing bacterial from aseptic meningitis, a prospectively validated bacterial meningitis score yields a negative predictive value of about 99.7% in children, and CSF protein >0.5 g/L and serum procalcitonin >0.5 ng/mL favor bacterial over aseptic meningitis (Harrison 22e p.1123-1125).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1118","Harrison 22e p.1119","Harrison 22e p.1120","Harrison 22e p.1121","Harrison 22e p.1122","Harrison 22e p.1123","Harrison 22e p.1124","Harrison 22e p.1125"],"crossLinks":[]},"acute_myeloid_leukemia":{"name":"Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)","system":"Oncology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AML"],"chapter":109,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.825","clinicalPearls":["AML is a clonal neoplasm characterized by infiltration of blood, bone marrow, and other tissues by proliferative, poorly differentiated myeloid cells (myeloblasts); historically the diagnosis required a marrow or blood myeloid blast count of >=20%, though the 2022 WHO classification eliminated the blast-percent requirement for cases with specific defining genetic aberrations (Harrison 22e p.825, p.826).","Most cases are idiopathic; genome sequencing suggests the majority arise from a limited number of mutations that accumulate with age. AML is the most common acute leukemia in older patients (median age at diagnosis 69 years) and causes 62% of leukemic deaths; only ~32% of patients survive 5 years (Harrison 22e p.825).","Auer rods are abnormal rod-shaped cytoplasmic granules; they are NOT uniformly present, but when seen they establish AML (and exclude acute lymphocytic leukemia) with certainty. AML blasts show fine lacy chromatin with one or more nucleoli and primary (nonspecific) cytoplasmic granules (Harrison 22e p.829).","Presentation is usually nonspecific and gradual (sometimes abrupt), reflecting marrow failure: fatigue (frequent first symptom) and anemia, abnormal hemostasis/bleeding and easy bruising from thrombocytopenia, and fever +/- infection from neutropenia/leukocyte dysfunction. Fever is the initial symptom in ~10% of patients (Harrison 22e p.828).","Multiparameter flow cytometry helps distinguish AML from acute lymphoblastic leukemia and identify subtypes (e.g., myeloid antigens CD13/CD117 in minimally differentiated AML; platelet antigens CD41/CD61 in acute megakaryoblastic leukemia); it has only a supportive role for WHO subtyping given the paramount importance of genetics, and is increasingly used to measure measurable residual disease (MRD) (Harrison 22e p.827).","Cytogenetic/molecular testing at diagnosis provides the most important prognostic information and drives risk stratification (European LeukemiaNet 2022 categories: favorable, intermediate, adverse). All AML cells contain genetic mutations, most recurring; the majority of patients now have a genetic lesion that can be specifically targeted (Harrison 22e p.826, p.827).","Acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL), defined by t(15;17)(q22;q12) / PML-RARA fusion, is a distinct, highly curable subtype (~85% long-term survival) treated uniquely with tretinoin (ATRA) and arsenic trioxide; it is excluded from the ELN risk table and is a coagulopathy emergency (Harrison 22e p.826, p.833).","Myeloid sarcoma is a tumor mass of myeloid blasts at sites other than marrow (commonly skin, lymph node, GI tract, soft tissue, testis); it may precede or coincide with blood/marrow involvement, is associated with t(8;21), and patients with isolated myeloid sarcoma typically develop blood/marrow disease quickly and cannot be cured by local therapy alone (Harrison 22e p.828).","Infiltration of gingiva, skin, soft tissues, or meninges with leukemic blasts at diagnosis is characteristic of the monocytic subtypes and those with KMT2A chromosomal abnormalities; gum hypertrophy is a classic monocytic finding (Harrison 22e p.828, p.830).","Infectious complications during neutropenia are the major cause of morbidity and death during induction and postremission chemotherapy; fever develops in most patients but infection is documented in only about half of febrile patients, and empiric broad-spectrum antibacterial/antifungal therapy at onset of neutropenic fever has substantially reduced infectious deaths (Harrison 22e p.832)."],"pathophysiology":"AML results from clonal proliferation of poorly differentiated myeloid progenitor (blast) cells that infiltrate the marrow, blood, and tissues, crowding out normal hematopoiesis and producing pancytopenia (anemia, neutropenia/leukocyte dysfunction, thrombocytopenia). Leukemogenesis is driven by accumulating somatic mutations; clonal hematopoiesis of indeterminate potential (CHIP), most often involving epigenetic regulators DNMT3A, TET2, and ASXL1, carries a ~10-fold increased risk of hematologic malignancy, though additional 'hits' are required to progress to leukemia. Recurrent genetic lesions define disease biology and presentation: fusion genes (e.g., PML-RARA from t[15;17]; RUNX1-RUNX1T1 from t[8;21]; CBFB-MYH11 from inv[16]/t[16;16] core-binding-factor AML; MLLT3-KMT2A; DEK-NUP214) and gene mutations (NPM1, present in ~30% of AML and ~60% of cytogenetically normal AML; FLT3, activating ITD or TKD mutations in ~30%; CEBPA; TP53; IDH1/IDH2 producing the oncometabolite 2-hydroxyglutarate that disrupts epigenetic processes). KMT2A rearrangements and monocytic differentiation favor extramedullary/tissue infiltration; t(15;17) APL drives DIC.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) - distinguished by flow cytometry/immunophenotype; presence of Auer rods confirms AML and excludes ALL","Myelodysplastic syndrome (MDS) - may precede/evolve into AML; AML-MR is defined by antecedent MDS or MDS/MPN and/or specific cytogenetic/molecular features","Myeloproliferative neoplasm (including chronic myeloid leukemia) - antecedent hematologic disorder that can transform to AML","Therapy-related (post-chemotherapy) AML - after alkylating agents (4-6 yr latency) or topoisomerase II inhibitors (1-3 yr latency)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","Myelodysplastic syndrome","Aplastic anemia / marrow failure syndromes (also present with pancytopenia)","Myeloproliferative neoplasms (e.g., CML in blast crisis)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CBC with manual differential (anemia normocytic normochromic; median presenting WBC ~15,000/uL; platelets <100,000/uL in ~75%)","Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy with morphology, cytogenetics, flow cytometry, and molecular studies (cornerstone of diagnosis and classification)","Cytogenetics and molecular testing at diagnosis (NPM1, FLT3-ITD/TKD, CEBPA bZIP, TP53, IDH1/IDH2, RUNX1, ASXL1 and others) for ELN risk stratification","Peripheral blood smear for blasts and Auer rods; peroxidase staining","Chemistry including uric acid, LDH, phosphorus, calcium, creatinine/BUN (tumor lysis screen); ~50% have mild-moderate uric acid elevation","Coagulation studies - PT, PTT, fibrinogen, D-dimer (screen for DIC, especially APL)","Cryopreservation of viable leukemia cells for future testing","HLA typing for potential allogeneic HCT; echocardiogram/MUGA for cardiac function; chest radiograph","Infection screen including SARS-CoV-2 and other viruses in patients with respiratory symptoms before chemotherapy","Measurable residual disease (MRD) assessment by flow cytometry or quantitative RT-PCR (e.g., NPM1, RUNX1-RUNX1T1, CBFB-MYH11, PML-RARA transcripts)"],"redFlags":["Acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL, t[15;17]/PML-RARA): coagulopathy/DIC emergency - presents with DIC-associated hemorrhage (may include significant GI, intrapulmonary, or intracranial bleeding); requires immediate recognition and ATRA-based therapy","Disseminated intravascular coagulation - classically in APL but also in monocytic AML and with extreme leukocytosis or thrombocytopenia; note thrombosis is also a recognized feature of DIC in APL","Hyperleukocytosis (WBC >100,000/uL, seen in ~20% at presentation) with pulmonary leukostasis and early CNS bleeding - associated with poor outcomes","Intracranial hemorrhage / CNS bleed - headache, vision changes, nonfocal neurologic abnormalities","Febrile neutropenia - fever in a neutropenic patient mandates immediate evaluation and empiric broad-spectrum antibacterials covering gram-negative organisms","Tumor lysis syndrome / uric acid nephropathy - hyperuricemia (aggravated by initiating chemotherapy); allopurinol/hydration started at diagnosis, rasburicase for severe cases (avoid in G6PD deficiency)","APL differentiation syndrome - within first 3 weeks of ATRA (or less commonly ATO): fever, fluid retention, dyspnea, chest pain, pulmonary infiltrates, pleural/pericardial effusions, hypoxemia; ~10% mortality if unrecognized"],"prognosis":"Untreated AML is uniformly fatal; overall only ~32% of US patients survive 5 years. Cytogenetic/molecular risk is the dominant prognostic determinant: t(15;17)/APL has a very good prognosis (~85% cured), t(8;21) and inv(16) (core-binding-factor AML) a good prognosis (~55% cured), cytogenetically normal AML an intermediate outcome (~40% cured), and TP53 mutation, complex karyotype, t(6;9), inv(3), or monosomy 7 confer adverse risk with very poor outcomes and virtually no cure without transplantation. NPM1 mutation without FLT3-ITD is favorable; in-frame bZIP CEBPA mutation is favorable; FLT3-ITD carries negative prognostic impact. Clinical adverse factors include advancing age (the most important clinical factor), poor performance status, high presenting leukocyte count, antecedent hematologic disorder (MDS/MPN) or prolonged preceding cytopenias, and therapy-related (post-chemotherapy) AML. Achievement of complete remission (CR) and MRD-negative status predict longer survival.","pitfalls":["Auer rods are not uniformly present in AML, so their absence does NOT exclude AML; their presence, however, confirms AML over ALL","Diagnosis based on blast percent alone is outdated - cases with defining genetic abnormalities (e.g., t[15;17], t[8;21], inv[16], KMT2A rearrangement, NPM1 mutation) qualify as AML even with <20% blasts; genetic/cytogenetic testing is mandatory for precise diagnosis","APL must be recognized urgently - delay or treating it like ordinary AML risks fatal DIC; high-risk APL (WBC >10,000/uL) requires immediate cytoreduction because ATRA can precipitate life-threatening differentiation syndrome and rapidly rising leukocyte count","Even infirm older patients generally benefit from treatment over supportive care alone - age and comorbidity should not automatically exclude therapy","Even patients with undetectable MRD remain at risk for relapse; without postremission therapy virtually all CR patients eventually relapse, and there is no consensus on the optimal MRD technique or its use to direct therapy","NPM1 prognostic favorability is negated by co-existing FLT3-ITD; prognostic mutations are not mutually exclusive (>80% of patients carry two or more)","Anemia at diagnosis is usually present but not typically severe and is normocytic normochromic - a 'mild' blood count abnormality can still herald AML"],"severityClassification":"Risk stratification per 2022 European LeukemiaNet (ELN) by genetics at diagnosis (APL excluded): Favorable - t(8;21)/RUNX1-RUNX1T1, inv(16) or t(16;16)/CBFB-MYH11, mutated NPM1 without FLT3-ITD, in-frame bZIP-mutated CEBPA. Intermediate - mutated NPM1 with FLT3-ITD, wild-type NPM1 with FLT3-ITD (without adverse lesions), t(9;11)/MLLT3-KMT2A, and abnormalities not classified as favorable or adverse. Adverse - t(6;9)/DEK-NUP214, KMT2A rearrangements, t(9;22)/BCR-ABL1, inv(3)/t(3;3) MECOM(EVI1), -5/del(5q), -7, -17/abn(17p), complex or monosomal karyotype, mutated ASXL1/BCOR/EZH2/RUNX1/SF3B1/SRSF2/STAG2/U2AF1/ZRSR2, and mutated TP53 (>=10% VAF). Classification (2022 WHO): AML with defining genetic abnormalities versus AML defined by differentiation; the >=20% blast requirement is removed for cases with specific defining genetic aberrations.","references":["Harrison 22e p.825","Harrison 22e p.826","Harrison 22e p.827","Harrison 22e p.828","Harrison 22e p.829","Harrison 22e p.832","Harrison 22e p.833"],"crossLinks":["acute_leukemia","FEBRILE_NEUTROPENIA","dic"]},"acute_otitis_media":{"name":"Acute otitis media","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AOM","acute suppurative otitis media","acute middle ear infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most common in infants and young children because of a shorter, more horizontal Eustachian tube; often follows an upper respiratory tract infection","Presents with ear pain, fever, irritability and conduction-type hearing loss; young children may only tug at the ear or feed poorly","Otoscopy progresses through stages: initial hyperaemia, then a bulging congested drum, then perforation with discharge, and finally resolution","Sudden relief of pain accompanied by ear discharge usually signals spontaneous perforation of the tympanic membrane","Common organisms include Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis","Management principles centre on analgesia and appropriate antibiotics; myringotomy is considered when the drum is bulging, pain is severe, or complications threaten","Watchful waiting without immediate antibiotics may be appropriate in older children with mild, non-severe disease","Recurrent episodes or persistent effusion warrant assessment for adenoid hypertrophy and Eustachian tube dysfunction"],"pathophysiology":"Acute otitis media is an acute suppurative inflammation of the middle ear cleft, usually arising when Eustachian tube dysfunction (commonly from a viral upper respiratory infection or adenoid obstruction) impairs middle ear ventilation and drainage. Negative middle ear pressure and mucosal oedema allow pathogens to ascend from the nasopharynx and proliferate, producing an inflammatory exudate that becomes purulent. Rising pressure causes the tympanic membrane to bulge and may lead to necrosis and perforation with discharge, after which the process typically resolves. Untreated or aggressive infection can extend to adjacent structures.","additionalDifferentials":["Otitis externa","Otitis media with effusion","Referred otalgia from teeth, throat or temporomandibular joint","Bullous myringitis","Early mastoiditis","Furuncle of the external auditory canal"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Otoscopy to assess drum colour, position, mobility and integrity","Tuning fork tests and audiometry to characterise conductive hearing loss","Tympanometry where effusion or Eustachian tube dysfunction is suspected","Culture of ear discharge in severe, recurrent or non-resolving cases","Imaging of the temporal bone if intratemporal or intracranial complications are suspected"],"redFlags":["Postauricular swelling, tenderness or a protruding pinna suggesting acute mastoiditis","Persistent high fever, severe headache, neck stiffness or altered consciousness indicating intracranial spread","Facial nerve weakness or vertigo suggesting facial canal or labyrinthine involvement","Failure to improve despite adequate treatment"],"prognosis":"Most cases resolve completely with prompt treatment and restoration of normal hearing, but neglected or recurrent infection can progress to chronic otitis media, persistent effusion, or intratemporal and intracranial complications.","pitfalls":["Mistaking otitis externa or referred otalgia for middle ear infection","Overlooking early mastoiditis in a child who is not improving","Assuming a red drum alone confirms the diagnosis without assessing bulging and mobility","Failing to follow up to ensure resolution of effusion and return of hearing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"acute_pain_management":{"name":"Acute pain management","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute postoperative pain","perioperative pain control","acute pain"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Effective acute pain control improves comfort, supports early mobilisation and recovery, and reduces complications such as respiratory compromise and the risk of persistent postsurgical pain","A multimodal approach combines analgesics with different mechanisms (paracetamol, anti-inflammatories, regional or neuraxial techniques, and opioids when needed) to improve relief while limiting side effects","Opioid-sparing strategies reduce nausea, sedation, ileus, and respiratory depression, and are central to enhanced recovery pathways","Regular assessment using pain scores at rest and on movement guides titration, and analgesia should be tailored to the individual and the surgical procedure","Regional and neuraxial techniques provide excellent targeted analgesia but require monitoring for block-related complications and, with neuraxial opioids, delayed respiratory depression","Opioid therapy demands vigilance for oversedation and respiratory depression, particularly in the elderly, those with sleep apnoea, and when combined with other sedatives","Special populations, including opioid-tolerant patients, those with substance use disorders, children, and the elderly, need individualised plans and often specialist input","Poorly controlled acute pain is a risk factor for developing chronic postsurgical pain, so proactive, adequate management has longer-term benefits"],"pathophysiology":"Acute pain after surgery or injury arises from tissue damage that activates peripheral nociceptors and releases inflammatory mediators, sensitising both peripheral nerves and central pathways. Nociceptive signals travel via the dorsal horn to higher centres, where they are modulated by descending inhibitory and facilitatory systems. Peripheral and central sensitisation lower pain thresholds and amplify responses, contributing to hyperalgesia. Because pain is transmitted and modulated at multiple sites, combining agents that act at different points in these pathways (peripheral inflammation, nerve conduction, spinal transmission, and central perception) produces additive or synergistic relief with fewer dose-related adverse effects.","additionalDifferentials":["Pain from a surgical complication such as bleeding, ischaemia, or infection","Compartment syndrome presenting as disproportionate pain","Neuropathic pain from nerve injury","Referred pain from another organ system","Anxiety or distress amplifying pain report","Undiagnosed acute cardiac or abdominal event"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Regular pain scoring at rest and on movement","Assessment for sedation and respiratory rate when opioids are used","Evaluation for a surgical or vascular cause when pain is severe or escalating","Assessment for compartment syndrome when pain is disproportionate","Review of the analgesic regimen and its effectiveness and side effects"],"redFlags":["Pain that is severe, escalating, or disproportionate, suggesting compartment syndrome or a surgical complication","Excessive sedation or a falling respiratory rate indicating opioid-induced respiratory depression","New neurological deficit after neuraxial or regional analgesia","Pain accompanied by signs of ischaemia, sepsis, or haemorrhage"],"prognosis":"Most acute pain resolves as healing progresses, and good multimodal control aids recovery; inadequate management increases complications and the likelihood of chronic postsurgical pain.","pitfalls":["Relying on opioids alone rather than a multimodal, opioid-sparing plan","Missing compartment syndrome or a surgical complication behind disproportionate pain","Underestimating opioid respiratory-depression risk in vulnerable patients","Failing to reassess and titrate analgesia to individual response","Neglecting the special needs of opioid-tolerant patients and those with substance use disorders"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"acute_respiratory_failure":{"name":"Acute respiratory failure","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ARF","respiratory failure","acute hypoxaemic respiratory failure"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Recognise failure clinically before blood gases confirm it: rising respiratory rate, accessory muscle use, altered consciousness, and inability to speak in full sentences are early warnings","Type 1 (hypoxaemic) failure reflects a problem with oxygenation, whereas type 2 (hypercapnic) failure reflects inadequate ventilation and carbon dioxide clearance","Secure the airway and support oxygenation and ventilation as the first priority, in parallel with seeking the cause","A falling respiratory rate or conscious level in a tiring patient can herald impending arrest rather than improvement","Non-invasive ventilation can be effective in selected causes, but a deteriorating or obtunded patient needs intubation without delay","Titrate oxygen to a target saturation, being mindful of the risk of worsening hypercapnia in chronic carbon dioxide retainers","Arterial blood gases distinguish the type of failure and reveal the acid-base disturbance guiding urgency","Treat the reversible precipitant (e.g. bronchospasm, pulmonary oedema, pneumothorax) alongside respiratory support"],"pathophysiology":"Respiratory failure occurs when gas exchange fails to maintain adequate oxygenation or carbon dioxide elimination. Hypoxaemic (type 1) failure arises mainly from ventilation-perfusion mismatch and shunt, as in pneumonia, pulmonary oedema, and acute respiratory distress syndrome, where alveoli are filled or collapsed. Hypercapnic (type 2) failure results from reduced alveolar ventilation due to depressed respiratory drive, neuromuscular weakness, increased airway resistance, or chest wall problems, so carbon dioxide accumulates. Both cause tissue hypoxia; hypercapnia additionally produces respiratory acidosis. Fatigue of the respiratory muscles can convert a compensated state into rapid decompensation and arrest.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute respiratory distress syndrome","Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema","Severe pneumonia or aspiration","Acute exacerbation of asthma or COPD","Pulmonary embolism","Neuromuscular weakness or central respiratory depression"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas to classify the type of failure and assess acid-base status","Chest radiograph to identify consolidation, oedema, effusion, or pneumothorax","Continuous pulse oximetry and capnography where available","Bedside lung and cardiac ultrasound to differentiate causes","CT pulmonary angiography if embolism is suspected"],"redFlags":["Exhaustion with a falling respiratory rate or rising carbon dioxide indicating impending respiratory arrest","Deteriorating conscious level or inability to protect the airway","Refractory hypoxaemia despite maximal oxygen therapy","Silent chest or minimal air entry in severe bronchospasm","Haemodynamic instability accompanying the respiratory compromise"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the underlying cause and reversibility. Prompt recognition and support of oxygenation and ventilation with treatment of the precipitant allow recovery in many cases, whereas established acute respiratory distress syndrome and multi-organ involvement carry substantial mortality.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a tiring patient's slowing respiratory rate for clinical improvement","Uncontrolled high-flow oxygen worsening hypercapnia in chronic retainers","Persisting with non-invasive ventilation in a patient who needs intubation","Treating the numbers on the blood gas without addressing the underlying cause","Underestimating fatigue and delaying escalation of respiratory support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"acute_rheumatic_fever":{"name":"Acute Rheumatic Fever","system":"Rheumatology / Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ARF"],"chapter":371,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2854-2858","clinicalPearls":["ARF is a multisystem autoimmune sequela of group A streptococcal infection; almost all manifestations resolve completely, the major exception being cardiac valvular damage (rheumatic heart disease, RHD), which may persist after other features disappear (Harrison 22e p.2854).","Mainly a disease of children aged 5-14 years; initial episodes are rare in persons aged >30 years, whereas recurrent episodes remain relatively common in adolescents and young adults. RHD prevalence peaks between 25 and 40 years (Harrison 22e p.2854).","There is no clear gender association for ARF, but RHD more commonly affects females, sometimes up to twice as often as males (Harrison 22e p.2854).","Latent period of ~3 weeks (range 1-5 weeks) between the precipitating group A streptococcal infection and onset of ARF; the exceptions are chorea and indolent (low-grade) carditis, which may follow prolonged latent periods of up to 6 months (Harrison 22e p.2855).","Most common clinical features are polyarthritis (60-75% of cases) and carditis (50-75%); chorea prevalence varies from <2% to 30% between populations; erythema marginatum and subcutaneous nodules are now rare, found in <5% of cases (Harrison 22e p.2855).","Up to 75% of patients with ARF progress to RHD; the mitral valve is almost always affected, sometimes with the aortic valve. Isolated aortic valve involvement is rare. The characteristic manifestation of carditis in previously unaffected individuals is mitral regurgitation, sometimes with aortic regurgitation (Harrison 22e p.2855).","ARF arthritis is typically a migratory polyarthritis of large joints (knees, ankles, hips, elbows), asymmetric, severe and disabling, and highly responsive to salicylates/NSAIDs (Harrison 22e p.2855-2856).","Sydenham's chorea often occurs in the absence of other manifestations, follows a prolonged latent period, and is found mainly in females; more than 50% of patients presenting with chorea will have carditis, so echocardiography should be part of the workup (Harrison 22e p.2856).","Erythema marginatum is an evanescent serpiginous rash (pink macules clearing centrally) usually on the trunk, sometimes the limbs, but almost never on the face. Subcutaneous nodules are painless, mobile, occur over bony prominences, appear 2-3 weeks after onset, and are commonly associated with carditis (Harrison 22e p.2856).","Revised Jones criteria (2015 AHA): for all populations with evidence of preceding group A strep infection, initial ARF requires 2 major OR 1 major plus 2 minor manifestations; recurrent ARF requires 2 major OR 1 major plus 2 minor OR 3 minor manifestations. Separate, more sensitive criteria apply to moderate/high-risk populations versus low-risk populations (Harrison 22e p.2856-2857).","Major manifestations: carditis (clinical and/or subclinical), arthritis, chorea, erythema marginatum, and subcutaneous nodules; in moderate/high-risk populations arthritis includes monoarthritis and polyarthralgia (Harrison 22e p.2857).","Secondary prevention with long-term penicillin prophylaxis is the mainstay of controlling ARF and RHD, best delivered through a register-based RHD control program; patients should be entered on an ARF registry, educated, and linked to follow-up before discharge (Harrison 22e p.2857-2858)."],"pathophysiology":"ARF results from an autoimmune reaction to group A streptococcal infection, classically of the upper respiratory tract, with increasing evidence for a role of streptococcal skin infection. The most widely accepted theory is molecular mimicry: an immune response to streptococcal antigens (mainly the M protein) cross-reacts with human tissues. Antigen-presenting cells of the innate immune system present streptococcal antigens to T cells, activating humoral and cellular immunity; cross-reactive antibodies bind heart-valve endothelial cells, activating VCAM-1 with recruitment of activated lymphocytes and complement-mediated lysis, releasing peptides (laminin, keratin, tropomyosin) that activate cross-reactive T cells invading the heart, amplifying damage and causing epitope spreading. An alternative hypothesis proposes initial damage from streptococcal invasion of epithelial surfaces with M-protein binding to type IV collagen, rendering it immunogenic but not through molecular mimicry. Susceptibility is inherited (~3-6% of a population susceptible; 44% concordance in monozygotic vs 12% in dizygotic twins; heritability ~60%), with associations to HLA class II alleles, TNF and mannose-binding-lectin polymorphisms, the IGH locus (IGHV4-61*02), and complement factor H (Harrison 22e p.2854-2855).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Always request: electrocardiogram (ECG), echocardiogram, complete blood count (CBC), C-reactive protein (CRP), and streptococcal serology (antistreptolysin O [ASO] and anti-DNase B [ADB]) (Harrison 22e p.2857).","Echocardiography should be performed on all possible cases to aid diagnosis (including detection of subclinical carditis/valvulitis) and to establish baseline carditis severity; auscultation is poorly sensitive and specific for RHD in asymptomatic patients (Harrison 22e p.2855, p.2857).","Because most cases lack a positive throat swab culture or rapid antigen test, serologic evidence of preceding group A strep infection is usually needed (ASO and anti-DNase B titers), ideally with locally derived age-specific reference ranges (Harrison 22e p.2856).","In relevant situations: throat swab, skin sore swab, blood cultures, synovial fluid aspirate (cell count, microscopy, culture, gonococcal PCR), pregnancy test, and creatinine/UEC (since NSAIDs can affect renal function) (Harrison 22e p.2857).","Tests to exclude alternative diagnoses (per clinical presentation/local endemic infections): autoantibodies, double-stranded DNA, anti-CCP antibodies; urine molecular testing for Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis; serology for viral hepatitis, Yersinia, CMV, parvovirus B19, respiratory viruses, Ross River virus, Barmah Forest virus (Harrison 22e p.2857).","Inflammatory markers should be monitored every 1-2 weeks until normalized (usually within 4-6 weeks), and a repeat echocardiogram performed after 1 month to assess for progression of carditis (Harrison 22e p.2858)."],"redFlags":["Severe carditis causing congestive heart failure; treatment of heart failure may be lifesaving (Harrison 22e p.2857).","Myocardial inflammation affecting conduction, causing P-R interval prolongation (first-degree AV block, rarely higher-level block) (Harrison 22e p.2855).","More than 50% of patients presenting with chorea have underlying carditis, mandating echocardiographic workup (Harrison 22e p.2856).","Advanced RHD complications: heart failure, arrhythmia, stroke, and infective endocarditis (Harrison 22e p.2856)."],"prognosis":"Untreated, ARF lasts on average 12 weeks; with treatment, patients are usually discharged within 1-2 weeks. Almost all non-cardiac manifestations resolve completely. Inflammatory markers usually normalize within 4-6 weeks. Up to 75% of patients with ARF progress to RHD, the long-term consequence being valvular damage; people with RHD are often asymptomatic for many years before progression to cardiac failure. There is no treatment proven to alter the likelihood or severity of RHD; apart from heart-failure management, treatment of ARF is symptomatic. RHD is the most common cause of acquired heart disease in children in low- and middle-income countries and a major cause of adult mortality and morbidity, with >40 million people affected worldwide and >300,000 deaths annually (Harrison 22e p.2854, p.2857-2858).","pitfalls":["The preceding group A streptococcal infection is commonly subclinical, so the absence of a reported sore throat or a negative throat swab/rapid antigen test does not exclude ARF; serologic confirmation is usually required (Harrison 22e p.2855-2856).","Diagnosis of ARF is often missed, particularly in resource-poor settings, so children, adolescents, and young adults may have undiagnosed RHD; echocardiographic screening can detect these cases (Harrison 22e p.2855).","Early commencement of anti-inflammatory medication before the typical migratory pattern is established may produce an atypical presenting picture such as aseptic monoarthritis (Harrison 22e p.2856).","Recurrence of fever, joint manifestations, and elevated acute-phase reactants up to 3 weeks after stopping anti-inflammatory medication does NOT indicate a true ARF recurrence and is managed by briefly recommencing anti-inflammatory agents (Harrison 22e p.2857).","Glucocorticoid use in ARF carditis remains controversial; two meta-analyses failed to demonstrate benefit over placebo or salicylates, though included studies were >40 years old and used outdated medications (Harrison 22e p.2857-2858).","Salicylates/NSAIDs are of no proven value in the treatment of carditis or chorea (they treat arthritis, arthralgia, and fever) (Harrison 22e p.2857).","Medications to control choreiform movements do NOT alter the duration or outcome of chorea; IVIg shows no benefit on short- or long-term carditis outcome and is not recommended except in severe chorea refractory to other treatments (Harrison 22e p.2858)."],"severityClassification":"RHD detected by echocardiography is staged (2023 World Heart Federation guidelines) into: Stage A (minimal echocardiographic criteria for RHD, individuals aged <=20 years, mild MR or AR without morphologic features); Stage B (mild RHD, mild valvular regurgitation plus morphologic feature(s)); Stage C (advanced RHD at risk of clinical complications - moderate/severe MR or AR, any MS or AS, pulmonary hypertension, decreased LV systolic function); Stage D (advanced RHD with established clinical complications such as cardiac surgery, heart failure, arrhythmia, stroke, infective endocarditis). The 2015 revised Jones criteria additionally separate low-risk populations (ARF incidence <=2 per 100,000 school-age children, or all-age RHD prevalence <=1 per 1000) from moderate/high-risk populations, applying more sensitive criteria to the latter (Harrison 22e p.2856-2857).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2854-2858"],"crossLinks":["PHARYNGITIS"]},"acute_stress_disorder":{"name":"Acute stress disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ASD","acute stress reaction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The disorder develops after exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violation, with symptoms arising and lasting from three days up to one month after the trauma","Symptoms span intrusion, negative mood, dissociation, avoidance, and arousal, including intrusive memories, distressing dreams, flashbacks, and a sense of unreality or detachment","If characteristic symptoms persist beyond one month, the diagnosis is reconsidered as post-traumatic stress disorder rather than acute stress disorder","Dissociative features such as depersonalisation, derealisation, or amnesia for part of the event can be prominent in the acute aftermath","Many people exposed to trauma recover without progressing to a chronic disorder, so the diagnosis helps identify those at higher risk who may benefit from early support","Trauma-focused cognitive behavioural therapy is the recommended intervention, whereas single-session psychological debriefing is not beneficial and may be harmful","Medication has a limited role, mainly for specific symptoms such as severe sleep disturbance, and benzodiazepines are generally avoided","Assess suicide risk and provide practical support, safety, and psychoeducation as part of early management"],"pathophysiology":"Acute stress disorder reflects the immediate dysregulation of fear and stress-response systems following trauma, with heightened amygdala-driven threat processing, altered prefrontal regulation, and disturbed hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis and noradrenergic activity. Impaired encoding and contextualisation of the traumatic memory, together with dissociative responses, are thought to underlie the intrusive and dissociative symptoms, modulated by prior vulnerability and severity of exposure.","additionalDifferentials":["Post-traumatic stress disorder once symptoms exceed one month","Adjustment disorder in response to a stressor","Panic disorder or other anxiety disorders","Major depressive episode following trauma","Traumatic brain injury or medical sequelae of the event","Substance intoxication or withdrawal","Brief psychotic disorder with marked stressor"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical interview characterising the traumatic exposure and symptom onset","Structured suicide risk assessment","Assessment of dissociative symptoms","Medical evaluation for injuries or head trauma related to the event","Screening for comorbid depression and substance use"],"redFlags":["Suicidal or self-harm ideation following the trauma","Ongoing exposure to danger, such as continuing abuse or violence","Severe dissociation impairing safety or the ability to seek help","Concurrent physical injury or head trauma requiring medical attention"],"prognosis":"Many individuals with acute stress disorder recover within weeks, but a significant proportion progress to post-traumatic stress disorder, and the acute diagnosis identifies people who may benefit from early, evidence-based support. Prognosis is better with early trauma-focused intervention, safety, and social support, and worse with severe or ongoing trauma, prominent dissociation, and prior psychiatric illness.","pitfalls":["Providing single-session psychological debriefing, which is ineffective and potentially harmful","Prescribing benzodiazepines, which may impair recovery","Assuming all acutely distressed trauma survivors will develop a chronic disorder","Overlooking suicide risk or ongoing danger to the patient","Missing concurrent physical or head injury from the traumatic event"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"acute_tonsillitis":{"name":"Acute tonsillitis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute tonsillopharyngitis","acute sore throat","tonsillar infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with sore throat, painful swallowing, fever, malaise and tender enlarged jugulodigastric lymph nodes","The follicular (spotted) form shows yellowish exudate at the tonsillar crypt openings, while the membranous form has a coalescent whitish membrane","Group A beta-haemolytic streptococcus is the most important bacterial cause because of its non-suppurative sequelae","Viral causes are common; features such as prominent cough, coryza and conjunctivitis point away from streptococcal infection","Consider infectious mononucleosis when there is marked tonsillar enlargement, membrane formation, generalised lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly","Management principles include adequate hydration, analgesia and antibiotics when streptococcal infection is likely, avoiding aminopenicillins if glandular fever is suspected because of the rash","Non-suppurative complications of streptococcal infection include rheumatic fever and acute glomerulonephritis, which justify appropriate antibiotic treatment","Recurrent tonsillitis meeting frequency criteria, or complications such as abscess, may warrant tonsillectomy"],"pathophysiology":"Acute tonsillitis is acute inflammation of the palatine tonsils, usually from viral or bacterial infection of the tonsillar crypts and parenchyma. Pathogens invade the crypt epithelium, provoking an inflammatory response with hyperaemia, oedema and crypt exudate. Streptococcal infection can trigger immune-mediated sequelae elsewhere in the body. Local spread of infection beyond the tonsillar capsule can produce peritonsillar or deep neck space abscesses, and marked tonsillar and adenoid enlargement may compromise the airway.","additionalDifferentials":["Viral pharyngitis","Infectious mononucleosis","Peritonsillar abscess","Diphtheria","Candidal or other fungal pharyngitis","Agranulocytosis or leukaemic infiltration presenting with sore throat"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Throat examination to assess tonsillar size, exudate and membrane","Throat swab or rapid antigen test for group A streptococcus where indicated","Full blood count and monospot or heterophile antibody test when mononucleosis is suspected","Assessment for splenomegaly and generalised lymphadenopathy","Blood glucose and further workup if recurrent or atypical infection suggests immunosuppression"],"redFlags":["Trismus, drooling, muffled voice or unilateral swelling suggesting peritonsillar or deep neck abscess","Stridor or respiratory difficulty from airway obstruction","A grey adherent membrane bleeding on removal, raising concern for diphtheria","Persistent unilateral tonsillar enlargement or ulceration suggesting malignancy"],"prognosis":"Most episodes are self-limiting or resolve promptly with treatment; complications such as abscess formation and, in streptococcal disease, rheumatic and renal sequelae are largely preventable with appropriate management.","pitfalls":["Prescribing ampicillin-type antibiotics in undiagnosed glandular fever and precipitating a rash","Missing a developing peritonsillar abscess in a patient with worsening unilateral symptoms","Treating all sore throats as bacterial when many are viral","Overlooking a haematological cause in unusually severe or persistent cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"acute_urinary_retention":{"name":"Acute urinary retention","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AUR","acute retention of urine","acute bladder outlet obstruction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as the sudden, painful inability to pass urine with a palpable, tender, distended bladder that is dull to percussion","In men the commonest precipitant is benign prostatic obstruction, often triggered by infection, constipation, alcohol, immobility, or anticholinergic and sympathomimetic drugs","Immediate management is urgent bladder decompression with a urethral catheter; suprapubic catheterisation is used when urethral access fails or is contraindicated","Painless retention with a grossly distended bladder suggests chronic-on-acute (high-pressure or high-residual) retention rather than a purely acute event","Record the drained volume; a large residual and any renal impairment point toward chronic high-pressure retention with risk of post-obstructive diuresis","Post-obstructive diuresis after decompression can cause marked fluid and electrolyte losses, requiring monitoring of urine output, weight, and electrolytes","A trial without catheter, often facilitated by an alpha-blocker, is appropriate once the precipitant is treated; failure indicates need for ongoing catheterisation or definitive surgery","Always exclude and treat urinary infection, and consider spinal cord compression or cauda equina when neurological features accompany retention"],"pathophysiology":"Acute urinary retention is the abrupt failure to empty the bladder, arising from mechanical obstruction (most often prostatic enlargement, but also urethral stricture, clot, or stone), increased outlet tone, impaired detrusor contractility, or neurogenic causes. Bladder overdistension raises intravesical pressure and, in high-pressure chronic retention, is transmitted to the upper tracts causing hydronephrosis and renal impairment. Precipitating factors such as anticholinergic drugs, alpha-agonists, infection, pain, and prostatic congestion tip a compensated bladder into decompensation and acute failure to void.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign prostatic hyperplasia with decompensation","Prostate cancer causing outlet obstruction","Urethral stricture","Blood clot retention (clot from haematuria)","Neurogenic bladder / cauda equina syndrome","Constipation or pelvic mass causing extrinsic compression","Drug-induced retention (anticholinergics, opioids, sympathomimetics)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bladder ultrasound to confirm distension and measure retained volume","Urinalysis and culture to identify infection","Serum creatinine and electrolytes to detect obstructive renal impairment","Renal tract ultrasound to assess for hydronephrosis in suspected chronic retention","PSA (interpreted with caution, as retention and catheterisation raise levels)","Neurological assessment and spinal imaging when cauda equina or cord compression is suspected"],"redFlags":["Acute urinary retention is itself a urological emergency requiring immediate bladder decompression","High-pressure chronic retention with hydronephrosis and renal impairment","Post-obstructive diuresis causing dehydration and electrolyte disturbance after decompression","Retention with saddle anaesthesia, bilateral leg symptoms, or bowel dysfunction suggesting cauda equina syndrome","Associated fever or urosepsis in an obstructed system"],"prognosis":"Prompt decompression relieves symptoms rapidly; many men can eventually void after a trial without catheter and treatment of precipitants, but recurrent retention often signals the need for definitive prostatic surgery, and delayed relief risks lasting renal damage.","pitfalls":["Delaying catheterisation and allowing prolonged painful overdistension","Missing high-pressure chronic retention by not measuring residual volume or checking renal function","Failing to anticipate and monitor for post-obstructive diuresis","Overlooking neurological causes such as cauda equina compression","Rapid complete decompression without awareness of possible haematuria ex vacuo"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"adenoid_hypertrophy":{"name":"Adenoid hypertrophy","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["enlarged adenoids","adenoid enlargement","nasopharyngeal tonsil hypertrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The adenoids are a mass of lymphoid tissue at the back of the nasopharynx that normally enlarge in early childhood and regress toward puberty","Hypertrophy commonly causes nasal obstruction, mouth breathing, snoring and a characteristic hyponasal (blocked) voice","Long-standing obstruction and mouth breathing can produce the adenoid facies, with an open mouth, pinched nose, high-arched palate and dull expression","By blocking the Eustachian tube openings the adenoids predispose to recurrent otitis media and persistent middle ear effusion with conductive hearing loss","They also contribute to recurrent or chronic rhinosinusitis and postnasal discharge","Sleep-disordered breathing, ranging from snoring to obstructive sleep apnoea, is an important consequence and can affect growth and behaviour","Assessment uses history, examination, and either nasal endoscopy or a lateral nasopharyngeal radiograph to gauge the size of the adenoid pad","Adenoidectomy, often combined with grommet insertion or tonsillectomy, is indicated for significant obstruction, sleep apnoea or recurrent middle ear disease not responding to conservative measures"],"pathophysiology":"The adenoid (nasopharyngeal tonsil) is part of Waldeyer's ring and undergoes physiological enlargement with repeated exposure to inhaled and ingested antigens in childhood. Recurrent infection and allergy drive lymphoid hyperplasia so the pad enlarges enough to obstruct the posterior nasal choanae and the nasopharyngeal openings of the Eustachian tubes. Choanal obstruction forces mouth breathing and impairs sinus drainage, while Eustachian tube dysfunction interferes with middle ear ventilation, favouring effusion and recurrent otitis media. Chronic mouth breathing during the years of facial growth alters craniofacial and dental development, and narrowing of the upper airway during sleep produces snoring and obstructive sleep apnoea. The adenoid tissue can also act as a reservoir of infection and biofilm.","additionalDifferentials":["Allergic rhinitis","Chronic rhinosinusitis with or without polyps","Deviated nasal septum or turbinate hypertrophy","Choanal atresia (particularly if present from birth)","Nasopharyngeal mass such as an angiofibroma in adolescent males","Foreign body in the nose (unilateral obstruction)","Nasopharyngeal tumour in adults with apparent adenoid tissue"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nasal endoscopy to visualise and grade the adenoid pad","Lateral soft tissue radiograph of the nasopharynx to estimate adenoid size","Tympanometry and audiometry to detect middle ear effusion and conductive loss","Sleep assessment or polysomnography where obstructive sleep apnoea is suspected","Allergy assessment when an allergic component is likely"],"redFlags":["Features of obstructive sleep apnoea such as witnessed apnoeas, restless sleep and daytime effects, which need timely evaluation","Strictly unilateral nasal obstruction or bloody discharge suggesting a mass or foreign body rather than adenoids","In an adolescent boy, profuse epistaxis with a nasopharyngeal mass suggesting juvenile angiofibroma","Apparent adenoid enlargement in an adult, which is unusual and should prompt exclusion of a nasopharyngeal tumour","Failure to thrive or significant growth and behavioural disturbance from chronic obstruction"],"prognosis":"Adenoid tissue tends to regress naturally with age, so many children improve over time, and adenoidectomy gives prompt and lasting relief of obstruction, sleep-disordered breathing and associated ear disease in those who need surgery.","pitfalls":["Assuming any nasopharyngeal fullness in an adult is benign adenoid tissue and missing a tumour","Overlooking obstructive sleep apnoea and its effect on growth and behaviour","Attributing nasal obstruction solely to adenoids while missing allergy, septal deviation or a foreign body","Failing to assess and treat coexisting middle ear effusion and hearing loss","Neglecting the possibility of juvenile angiofibroma in an adolescent boy with obstruction and bleeding"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"adhd":{"name":"Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ADHD","attention deficit hyperactivity disorder","ADD","hyperkinetic disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A neurodevelopmental disorder defined by a persistent pattern of inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity that interferes with functioning or development","Several symptoms must be present before around age twelve and across more than one setting, such as home and school or work","Presentations are described as predominantly inattentive, predominantly hyperactive-impulsive, or combined","Inattentive features include distractibility, disorganisation, forgetfulness, and difficulty sustaining attention; hyperactive-impulsive features include restlessness, excessive talking, and interrupting","Diagnosis relies on history and collateral information from multiple informants and standardised rating scales, not a single test","Management principles combine behavioural and educational interventions with pharmacotherapy; stimulants are first-line, with non-stimulant options available, all described here without dosing","Screen cardiovascular history before stimulant treatment and monitor growth, blood pressure, and heart rate during therapy","Comorbidities are common, including learning disorders, oppositional and conduct problems, anxiety, depression, and tics"],"pathophysiology":"Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder is a highly heritable neurodevelopmental condition involving dysregulation of dopaminergic and noradrenergic signalling in fronto-striatal and fronto-cerebellar circuits that support attention, executive function, and response inhibition. Delayed maturation of these networks and gene-environment interactions, including prematurity and prenatal exposures, contribute to the phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Anxiety disorders","Mood disorders including bipolar disorder","Oppositional defiant disorder and conduct disorder","Learning disorders and intellectual disability","Autism spectrum disorder","Sleep disorders such as obstructive sleep apnoea","Thyroid dysfunction","Substance use disorder in adolescents and adults"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Standardised rating scales completed by multiple informants across settings","Developmental, educational, and collateral history","Baseline cardiovascular assessment before stimulant initiation","Screening for comorbid learning, mood, anxiety, and sleep disorders","Growth, blood pressure, and heart rate monitoring during treatment"],"redFlags":["Cardiac symptoms or family history of sudden death before or during stimulant therapy","Emerging psychosis, mania, or marked mood instability","Stimulant misuse or diversion, especially in adolescents and adults","Significant growth suppression during treatment in children"],"prognosis":"Symptoms, particularly hyperactivity, often lessen with age, but many individuals continue to experience inattention and functional difficulties into adulthood. Outcomes improve with treatment and support, while untreated ADHD is associated with academic underachievement, accidents, and increased risk of substance use.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing on the basis of symptoms in a single setting or from one informant","Overlooking comorbid conditions such as anxiety, sleep disorders, or learning difficulties that mimic or worsen inattention","Starting stimulants without assessing cardiovascular history","Attributing normal age-appropriate activity to a disorder","Failing to reassess ongoing need for medication and monitor growth and vital signs"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"adpkd":{"name":"Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ADPKD","adult polycystic kidney disease","polycystic kidney disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["ADPKD is the most common inherited kidney disease and is characterised by progressive bilateral cyst formation that gradually replaces functioning renal tissue","Most cases result from mutations in one of two genes, with the more common mutation generally causing earlier and more severe disease than the other","Common presentations include hypertension, flank or abdominal pain, haematuria, kidney stones, urinary infections, and a family history of kidney disease","Diagnosis is usually established by ultrasound using age-adjusted criteria for the number of cysts, with a positive family history strengthening the diagnosis","Extrarenal manifestations are important and include hepatic cysts, intracranial aneurysms, cardiac valve abnormalities, and abdominal wall hernias","Screening for intracranial aneurysms is targeted rather than universal, focusing on those with a family history of aneurysm or subarachnoid haemorrhage or high-risk occupations","Blood pressure control with renin-angiotensin system blockade and generous water intake are core management principles, and a vasopressin antagonist can slow cyst growth in selected rapidly progressing patients","Total kidney volume and the specific genotype help predict the rate of progression and identify candidates for disease-modifying therapy"],"pathophysiology":"ADPKD is caused by mutations in genes encoding polycystin proteins, which are located in the primary cilium and are important for sensing tubular flow and regulating tubular cell proliferation and differentiation. Loss of normal polycystin function disrupts intracellular calcium signalling and increases cyclic AMP, promoting abnormal epithelial proliferation and fluid secretion into forming cysts. Cysts arise from tubular segments, enlarge progressively, and detach from the parent tubule, expanding relentlessly and compressing adjacent normal parenchyma. Vasopressin-driven cyclic AMP signalling is a key driver of cyst fluid secretion and growth, which underlies the therapeutic role of vasopressin blockade. Progressive cyst expansion, interstitial inflammation, and fibrosis gradually reduce functioning nephron mass, while activation of the renin-angiotensin system contributes to the early and often severe hypertension.","additionalDifferentials":["Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease","Acquired cystic kidney disease of chronic renal failure","Simple renal cysts","Tuberous sclerosis or von Hippel-Lindau associated cystic disease","Autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease","Medullary cystic disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal ultrasound applying age-adjusted cyst count criteria","Cross-sectional imaging and total kidney volume measurement to assess prognosis","Blood pressure monitoring and assessment of renal function","Urinalysis for haematuria, proteinuria, and infection","Liver imaging for hepatic cysts when relevant","Targeted cerebral vascular imaging for aneurysm screening in high-risk patients; genetic testing when the diagnosis is uncertain"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe headache suggesting rupture of an intracranial aneurysm","Cyst infection or haemorrhage causing fever and severe flank pain","Rapidly declining renal function or refractory hypertension","Obstructing or infected kidney stones"],"prognosis":"ADPKD typically progresses over decades, with many patients reaching end-stage kidney disease in mid to later adult life, though the rate varies widely with genotype and total kidney volume. Blood pressure control, treatment of complications, and disease-modifying therapy in appropriate patients can slow progression, and outcomes on dialysis and after transplantation are generally good. Intracranial aneurysm rupture is a serious extrarenal risk in susceptible individuals.","pitfalls":["Applying uniform ultrasound cyst criteria without adjusting for the patient's age","Recommending universal aneurysm screening rather than targeting high-risk patients","Missing a cyst infection because it can present without the usual urinary findings","Failing to identify rapidly progressing patients who might benefit from disease-modifying therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"adrenal_disorders":{"name":"Adrenal Cortical Disorders","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Cushing syndrome","Addison disease","adrenal insufficiency","hyperaldosteronism"],"chapter":398,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3050-3072","clinicalPearls":["Cushing's: most clinical features (obesity, diabetes, diastolic hypertension, hirsutism, depression) are nonspecific; the more discriminatory signs are thinning/fragile skin with easy bruising, broad (>1 cm) purplish striae, and proximal myopathy (difficulty rising from a chair or climbing stairs). Suspect when several features cluster, or when a feature appears at an unusual age (e.g., osteoporosis in a young patient). (p.3056-3057)","Screen for endogenous Cushing's only after excluding exogenous (iatrogenic) glucocorticoids in any form. First-line screening tests: increased 24-h urinary free cortisol (three collections), failure to suppress morning cortisol after a 1 mg overnight dexamethasone test, and loss of diurnal rhythm shown by raised late-night (midnight) serum/salivary cortisol. Diagnosis is considered established when several tests are consistently abnormal. (p.3057)","Establish the diagnosis of Cushing's beyond doubt BEFORE doing any differential-diagnosis or imaging tests; most management errors (unnecessary imaging or surgery) come from not following this sequence, because pituitary and adrenal nodules are common incidental findings in the general population. (p.3057)","Cushing's differential diagnosis order: measure plasma ACTH first. Suppressed ACTH = ACTH-independent (adrenal) excess -> unenhanced adrenal CT. Normal/high ACTH = ACTH-dependent -> pituitary MRI plus dynamic tests (high-dose dexamethasone, CRH or desmopressin); pituitary MRI can be normal in up to 40% because microadenomas are small. (p.3057-3058)","Inferior petrosal sinus sampling (central:peripheral ACTH ratio >2 at baseline, >3 after CRH) distinguishes Cushing's disease from ectopic ACTH when dynamic tests are discordant; it cannot be used to lateralize the tumor within the pituitary, and no cortisol-lowering drugs should be used before it. (p.3058)","Primary aldosteronism is far more common than historically appreciated: prevalence 5-12% of hypertensives, higher in hypokalemic or drug-resistant hypertension. Screen only selected hypertensives (drug-resistant/severe hypertension, hypokalemia, adrenal mass, onset <40 years, or family history of primary aldosteronism), not all comers. (p.3060-3061)","Primary aldosteronism screen = aldosterone-renin ratio (ARR); correct hypokalemia first. Stop MR antagonists at least 4-6 weeks before testing; beta-blockers cause false positives and ACE inhibitors/ARBs cause false negatives. A positive ARR is followed by confirmatory testing (e.g., saline infusion test) by an endocrinologist. (p.3061)","After biochemical confirmation of aldosteronism, unenhanced adrenal CT plus adrenal vein sampling distinguish unilateral adenoma (Conn's, surgically curable by unilateral adrenalectomy) from bilateral hyperplasia (treated medically with an MR antagonist). AVS is only needed when surgery is feasible and desired. (p.3061)","Adrenal insufficiency: short cosyntropin (Synacthen) test is the safe, reliable first-line diagnostic test; failure is cortisol <450-500 nmol/L (16-18 microg/dL) at 30-60 min. Then measure plasma ACTH: high ACTH = primary (Addison's), inappropriately low ACTH = secondary/central. Increased plasma renin confirms mineralocorticoid deficiency in primary disease. (p.3068)","Primary adrenal insufficiency loses both glucocorticoid AND mineralocorticoid secretion; secondary (central) disease loses glucocorticoid only because the adrenal stays responsive to the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. Adrenal androgen secretion is lost in both. (p.3067)","Hyperpigmentation (palmar creases, friction/shear areas, sun-exposed skin) from excess POMC/ACTH is the hallmark distinguishing primary adrenal insufficiency; secondary insufficiency instead causes an alabaster-pale skin from ACTH lack. (p.3067)","Hyponatremia is present in ~80% of primary adrenal insufficiency at presentation and hyperkalemia in ~40%. Hyponatremia can also occur in secondary insufficiency via reduced cortisol inhibition of ADH (mild SIADH-like picture). (p.3067)","All patients with an incidentally discovered adrenal mass need a 1 mg dexamethasone suppression test to exclude cortisol excess; add plasma/urine metanephrines if HU >10 (exclude pheochromocytoma), and paired renin/aldosterone plus potassium if hypertensive. Confirm any Cushing's as ACTH-independent by measuring plasma ACTH. (p.3063-3064)","On unenhanced CT, adrenal lesion attenuation is the key discriminator: lipid-rich adenomas are typically <10-20 HU, whereas adrenocortical carcinoma and pheochromocytoma have high attenuation (>20 HU). Hounsfield assessment only applies to unenhanced CT, not post-contrast. (p.3064)","Mild autonomous cortisol secretion (MACS) = abnormal 1 mg dexamethasone test with no overt clinical Cushing's; it still carries excess risk of hypertension, diabetes, dyslipidemia, osteoporosis, cardiovascular events, and mortality. A subset of primary aldosteronism patients have co-existing MACS ('Connshing' syndrome). (p.3061, p.3064)","CYP21A2 (21-hydroxylase) deficiency causes 90-95% of congenital adrenal hyperplasia; it impairs glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid synthesis and drives ACTH-mediated adrenal androgen excess. 17-hydroxyprogesterone is the diagnostic marker steroid. Severity spans classic salt-wasting (neonatal), simple virilizing, to nonclassic (presents in adolescence/adulthood). (p.3071)"],"pathophysiology":"The adrenal cortex produces glucocorticoids (cortisol, zona fasciculata under HPA/ACTH control), mineralocorticoids (aldosterone, zona glomerulosa under renin-angiotensin-aldosterone control), and adrenal androgens. Cushing's syndrome reflects chronic glucocorticoid excess: ~90% of endogenous cases are ACTH-dependent (mostly a pituitary corticotrope adenoma = Cushing's disease, or ectopic ACTH from carcinoid/small-cell lung cancer), ~10% are ACTH-independent adrenal causes (adenoma, carcinoma, or bilateral macronodular/pigmented nodular hyperplasia). Cortisol excess upregulates gluconeogenesis, lipolysis, and protein catabolism, and when 11beta-HSD2 is overwhelmed cortisol activates the mineralocorticoid receptor, producing hypertension, hypokalemia, and edema. Primary mineralocorticoid excess (primary aldosteronism) is autonomous aldosterone production by the zona glomerulosa (bilateral hyperplasia or unilateral aldosterone-producing adenoma/Conn's); MR over-activation causes sodium retention with volume expansion, potassium and hydrogen loss (hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis), and direct cardiac/renal damage. Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison's) is destruction or dysfunction of the adrenal cortex (most often autoimmune adrenalitis, also TB, adrenoleukodystrophy, hemorrhage, infiltration, CAH), causing combined glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid deficiency; secondary insufficiency is HPA/pituitary-hypothalamic failure (tumor, surgery, irradiation, apoplexy/Sheehan's, or iatrogenic glucocorticoid suppression) with isolated glucocorticoid deficiency. CAH results from inherited steroidogenic enzyme defects (commonly CYP21A2) causing glucocorticoid deficiency, loss of feedback, raised ACTH, and adrenal androgen excess, with mineralocorticoid deficiency or excess depending on the enzymatic block.","additionalDifferentials":["Cushing's disease (ACTH-producing pituitary corticotrope adenoma) vs ectopic ACTH syndrome (bronchial/pancreatic carcinoid, small-cell lung cancer, medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma)","ACTH-independent adrenal Cushing's: cortisol-producing adenoma, adrenocortical carcinoma, primary bilateral macronodular adrenal hyperplasia, primary pigmented nodular adrenal disease (Carney complex), McCune-Albright syndrome","Mineralocorticoid excess differential: bilateral micronodular hyperplasia vs unilateral aldosterone-producing adenoma (Conn's); glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism (GRA/FH-I) and other familial primary aldosteronism; apparent mineralocorticoid excess (HSD11B2 deficiency); Liddle's syndrome (ENaC mutations); deoxycorticosterone excess from CAH (CYP11B1 or CYP17A1 deficiency)","Primary vs secondary (central) adrenal insufficiency, distinguished by plasma ACTH and renin","Pseudo-Cushing states: alcohol-related hypercortisolism, major depression, morbid obesity, and cyclic Cushing's"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculous adrenalitis (still a frequent cause of primary adrenal insufficiency in developing countries)","HIV-associated adrenal involvement (usually subclinical adrenal insufficiency)","Sepsis-associated bilateral adrenal hemorrhage (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome) causing acute primary adrenal insufficiency","Adrenal crisis presenting like an acute abdomen with fever, abdominal tenderness, nausea and vomiting"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Common population conditions overlapping Cushing's: obesity, type 2 diabetes, diastolic hypertension, hirsutism, depression, osteoporotic vertebral fractures","Pseudo-Cushing states (alcohol, major depression, morbid obesity)","Chronic adrenal insufficiency mistaken for depression or anorexia (nonspecific fatigue, weight loss)","Adrenal crisis mimicking acute abdomen or neurologic disease (stupor/coma)","Polycystic ovary syndrome phenotype mimicked by nonclassic CAH (hirsutism, oligomenorrhea)","Other causes of hypokalemic hypertension: licorice ingestion inhibiting 11beta-HSD2; renovascular/malignant hypertension (secondary hyperaldosteronism)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Cushing's screening: 1 mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test, late-night (midnight) salivary cortisol, 24-h urinary free cortisol (three collections); low-dose dexamethasone test if confirmation needed","Cushing's differential: plasma ACTH; pituitary MRI; high-dose dexamethasone test; CRH or desmopressin stimulation; bilateral inferior petrosal sinus sampling; unenhanced adrenal CT for ACTH-independent disease","Aldosteronism: aldosterone-renin ratio with serum potassium normalized; confirmatory saline infusion / oral sodium loading / fludrocortisone suppression test; unenhanced adrenal CT; adrenal vein sampling; 24-h urinary steroid profile (GC/MS) for familial/mimicking forms","Adrenal insufficiency: short cosyntropin (Synacthen/ACTH) stimulation test; plasma ACTH; plasma renin; adrenal steroid (21-hydroxylase) autoantibodies; insulin tolerance test as alternative; adrenal CT if autoantibody-negative; very-long-chain fatty acids in autoantibody-negative males (exclude X-ALD); pituitary MRI for secondary disease","Incidentaloma: 1 mg-DST in all; plasma/urine metanephrines if HU >10; paired renin and aldosterone plus potassium if hypertensive; 17-OHP, DHEAS, androstenedione (and sex steroids) if ACC suspected; unenhanced CT for size and attenuation (HU); chemical-shift MRI for indeterminate lesions","CAH: measurement of precursor steroids accumulating before the enzymatic block (notably 17-hydroxyprogesterone for 21-hydroxylase deficiency), preferably by mass spectrometry; androstenedione and testosterone for monitoring"],"redFlags":["Adrenal crisis (acute adrenal insufficiency): postural hypotension progressing to hypovolemic shock, often after a prolonged period of nonspecific complaints; may mimic acute abdomen or neurologic decline (stupor/coma); can be triggered by intercurrent illness, surgery/stress, or increased glucocorticoid inactivation (e.g., hyperthyroidism) - treat immediately, do not let testing delay therapy","In suspected adrenal crisis, draw a baseline cortisol, start replacement, and defer formal stimulation testing - tests must never delay treatment","Cushing's hypercoagulable state with risk of deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism","Acute paranoid or depressive psychosis in severe cortisol excess","Severe hypokalemia in aldosteronism causing arrhythmias, rhabdomyolysis, or hypokalemic paralysis; severe alkalosis causing tetany","Classic salt-wasting CAH in male neonates presenting as life-threatening adrenal crisis in the first weeks of life"],"prognosis":"After successful removal of an ACTH- or cortisol-producing tumor the HPA axis remains suppressed and may take months to years (or never) to recover; recovery rates differ by cause - ectopic ACTH best (~80%, median ~7 months), Cushing's disease intermediate (~60%, ~17 months), adrenal Cushing's lowest (~40%, ~30 months). Even after cure, long-term quality of life can remain impaired with increased cardiovascular and osteoporotic-fracture risk depending on duration and degree of cortisol excess. Primary aldosteronism carries increased osteoporosis, type 2 diabetes, and cognitive dysfunction. Adrenal crisis is potentially lethal, with prospective data of 8.3 crises and 0.5 crisis-related deaths per 100 patient-years. MACS is associated with increased mortality. In CAH, fertility may be compromised (anovulation in women, testicular adrenal rest tumors and fibrosis in men).","pitfalls":["Performing imaging or surgery before biochemically confirming Cushing's beyond doubt - pituitary/adrenal nodules are common incidental findings and lead to misdiagnosis","Forgetting to exclude exogenous (iatrogenic) glucocorticoid use as the cause before working up endogenous Cushing's","Dexamethasone test pitfalls: false non-suppression from oral estrogens/contraceptives (raise CBG/total cortisol; retest after 4-6 weeks off estrogen) and rapid dexamethasone inactivation by CYP3A4 inducers (antiepileptics, rifampicin); antibody-based urinary free cortisol assays have specificity problems improved by tandem mass spectrometry","Not normalizing potassium before, or failing to stop MR antagonists (4-6 weeks) before, ARR testing; beta-blockers give false-positive and ACE inhibitors/ARBs false-negative ARR results","Relying on the ARR alone when renin is very low increases false positives; only 10-40% of primary aldosteronism patients are hypokalemic, so normokalemia does not exclude it","Using random serum cortisol to diagnose adrenal insufficiency - baseline levels may be coincidentally low (diurnal rhythm), and secondary insufficiency often shows near-normal baseline cortisol that fails only on stimulation","Stopping exogenous glucocorticoids abruptly can precipitate iatrogenic adrenal insufficiency even though the patient still looks cushingoid","Judging adrenal mass attenuation on post-contrast CT - Hounsfield-unit discrimination of benign vs malignant only applies to unenhanced CT; lipid-poor adenomas (high HU) overlap with ACC and pheochromocytoma","Mistaking testicular adrenal rest tumors (TART) in CAH for testicular neoplasms"],"severityClassification":"Adrenal insufficiency is classified as primary (Addison's, combined glucocorticoid + mineralocorticoid deficiency, high ACTH) vs secondary/central (isolated glucocorticoid deficiency, low/inappropriate ACTH), with acute adrenal crisis as the life-threatening acute form. Endogenous Cushing's is classified as ACTH-dependent (~90%; Cushing's disease vs ectopic ACTH) vs ACTH-independent (~10%; adenoma, carcinoma, hyperplasia). Cortisol excess severity ranges from overt Cushing's to mild autonomous cortisol secretion (MACS). Primary aldosteronism is divided into unilateral (adenoma/Conn's, surgically curable) vs bilateral (hyperplasia, medical therapy). CAH (21-hydroxylase deficiency) is graded by severity into classic salt-wasting, simple virilizing, and nonclassic forms. Adrenocortical carcinoma is staged by the ENSAT/TNM system (Stage I T1 <=5 cm to advanced/metastatic).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3050-3072"],"crossLinks":["adrenal_crisis","pheo"]},"adult_still_disease":{"name":"Adult-onset Still's disease","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AOSD","adult Still disease","Still's disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A systemic autoinflammatory disorder classically presenting with high spiking (quotidian) fevers, an evanescent salmon-pink rash, and arthritis or arthralgia","The rash is typically transient, appearing with fever spikes and fading between them","Sore throat is a common and underappreciated early feature","Laboratory findings include striking neutrophilic leucocytosis and very high serum ferritin, often with a low glycosylated (fraction) ferritin","It is a diagnosis of exclusion, requiring careful search for infection, malignancy (especially lymphoma), and other autoimmune disease","Rheumatoid factor and antinuclear antibodies are characteristically negative","Macrophage activation syndrome (haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis) is a feared, potentially fatal complication","Management principles use NSAIDs for mild disease, corticosteroids for systemic disease, and IL-1 or IL-6 targeted biologics for refractory or relapsing cases"],"pathophysiology":"Adult-onset Still's disease is an autoinflammatory condition driven predominantly by innate immune activation rather than autoantibodies. Excessive activation of macrophages and neutrophils and a cytokine storm involving interleukin-1, interleukin-6, interleukin-18, and interferon-gamma produce the systemic inflammatory picture. Marked hyperferritinaemia reflects macrophage activation. Uncontrolled activation of macrophages and cytotoxic dysfunction can precipitate macrophage activation syndrome, characterised by cytopenias, coagulopathy, liver dysfunction, and extreme hyperferritinaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Sepsis and occult infection (including endocarditis and viral illness)","Lymphoma and other haematological malignancy","Systemic lupus erythematosus and other connective tissue diseases","Systemic vasculitis","Other autoinflammatory/periodic fever syndromes","Drug reaction with systemic features"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count (neutrophilic leucocytosis), inflammatory markers, and liver function tests","Serum ferritin, including glycosylated ferritin fraction where available","Extensive exclusion of infection (cultures, serology) and malignancy (imaging, marrow or node biopsy as indicated)","Autoantibody panel (rheumatoid factor and ANA, expected negative)","Monitoring for macrophage activation syndrome with triglycerides, fibrinogen, and evidence of haemophagocytosis"],"redFlags":["Features of macrophage activation syndrome: persistent fever, cytopenias, falling fibrinogen, rising ferritin, and organ dysfunction","Haemodynamic instability or evidence of overwhelming infection mimicking the presentation","Fulminant hepatic involvement or disseminated intravascular coagulation","New lymphadenopathy or organomegaly raising concern for lymphoma","Cardiopulmonary complications such as myocarditis, pericarditis, or pleuritis"],"prognosis":"Course is variable, described as monocyclic (self-limiting), polycyclic (relapsing), or chronic articular with persistent destructive arthritis. Most systemic cases respond to treatment, but macrophage activation syndrome carries substantial mortality and requires prompt recognition and intensive therapy.","pitfalls":["Anchoring on Still's disease before adequately excluding infection and malignancy","Missing early macrophage activation syndrome when a paradoxically falling ESR or ferritin trend is misread","Discounting the diagnosis because autoantibodies are negative (they are expected to be)","Overlooking sore throat and the transient rash as diagnostic clues","Failing to monitor for chronic destructive arthritis in the articular subtype"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"age_related_macular_degeneration":{"name":"Age-related macular degeneration","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ARMD","AMD","macular degeneration","senile macular degeneration","dry AMD","wet AMD","neovascular AMD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A leading cause of irreversible central visual loss in older adults, affecting fine detail, reading, and face recognition while peripheral vision is spared","Dry (non-exudative) form is commonest and progresses slowly with drusen and retinal pigment epithelial changes, culminating in geographic atrophy","Wet (neovascular/exudative) form is less common but causes rapid, severe loss due to choroidal neovascularisation, haemorrhage, and fluid","New distortion of straight lines (metamorphopsia) or a central blur/scotoma, often monitored with an Amsler grid, suggests conversion to wet AMD and warrants urgent referral","Smoking is the most important modifiable risk factor; age, family history, and pale iris/light exposure also contribute","Intravitreal anti-VEGF injection is the mainstay for neovascular AMD and can stabilise or improve vision","Dietary antioxidant and zinc supplementation can reduce progression risk in intermediate dry AMD"],"pathophysiology":"Age-related macular degeneration results from cumulative damage to the retinal pigment epithelium, Bruch's membrane, and choriocapillaris at the macula. Impaired clearance of metabolic debris leads to drusen deposition, and oxidative stress, complement-mediated inflammation, and lipofuscin accumulation injure the photoreceptor-RPE complex. In the atrophic form this causes progressive loss of central photoreceptors, whereas in the neovascular form ischaemia and VEGF release drive abnormal choroidal new vessels that leak and bleed beneath and within the retina, rapidly destroying central vision.","additionalDifferentials":["Diabetic maculopathy","Central serous chorioretinopathy","Macular dystrophy or pattern dystrophy","Myopic macular degeneration","Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy","Cystoid macular oedema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination and colour fundus photography","Optical coherence tomography to detect intraretinal/subretinal fluid and atrophy","OCT angiography or fluorescein angiography to identify choroidal neovascularisation","Amsler grid for self-monitoring of distortion","Visual acuity and near vision testing"],"redFlags":["Sudden central distortion, blurring, or a new scotoma suggesting conversion to neovascular AMD — urgent referral","Sudden severe drop in central vision from submacular haemorrhage","Rapid progression in the second eye of a patient with unilateral wet AMD","Any acute change while on anti-VEGF therapy indicating recurrent activity"],"prognosis":"Dry AMD progresses slowly and central vision may remain functional for years, whereas untreated wet AMD can cause rapid severe central loss; prompt anti-VEGF therapy substantially improves outcomes, though peripheral vision is preserved in all forms.","pitfalls":["Dismissing new metamorphopsia as needing new glasses and delaying urgent referral for wet AMD","Failing to advise smoking cessation as a key modifiable risk","Assuming peripheral vision loss is due to AMD, when it points elsewhere","Not checking and monitoring the fellow eye, which is at high risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"alcohol_and_alcohol_use_disorders":{"name":"Alcohol and Alcohol Use Disorders","system":"Neurologic / Psychiatric (multisystem)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Alcoholism","Alcohol dependence","Alcohol use disorder","AUD"],"chapter":464,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3675-3680","clinicalPearls":["A standard drink contains roughly 10-12 g of ethanol; one typical drink raises the blood ethanol level by about 0.02 g/dL (Harrison 22e p.3675).","Behavioral, psychomotor and cognitive impairment appears at only 0.02-0.04 g/dL (one to two drinks); 'legal intoxication' in most U.S. states is 0.08 g/dL, with 0.04 g/dL applied to pilots and to drivers in some countries (Harrison 22e p.3676).","In a person without tolerance, light coma with depressed vital signs occurs around 0.30 g/dL and death between 0.30 and 0.40 g/dL; alcohol probably causes more overdose deaths than any other drug (Harrison 22e p.3676).","Heavy drinking while fasting can cause transient hypoglycemia within 6-36 h by suppressing gluconeogenesis, producing temporarily abnormal glucose tolerance that can be misread as diabetes until 2-4 weeks of abstinence (Harrison 22e p.3675).","Most ethanol is metabolized in the liver: the cytosolic alcohol dehydrogenase pathway generates acetaldehyde (then cleared by aldehyde dehydrogenase), while the microsomal ethanol-oxidizing system (MEOS) handles >=10% at high blood levels (Harrison 22e p.3675).","Blackouts (temporary anterograde amnesia while awake) occur in about 35% of drinkers, with en bloc blackouts at levels >0.20 and fragmentary blackouts at >0.12 (Harrison 22e p.3676).","Alcohol disrupts sleep architecture, reducing early-night REM and deep sleep and relaxing pharyngeal muscles to worsen snoring and apnea; sleep apnea symptoms occur in about 75% of men with AUD aged >=60 (Harrison 22e p.3676).","DSM-5 diagnoses AUD when >=2 of 11 criteria cluster in the same 12-month period; severity is mild (2-3), moderate (4-5) or severe (>=6 items) (Harrison 22e p.3677-3678).","Roughly 60% of the risk for AUD is genetic; a flushing response (ALDH variant seen in Japanese, Chinese and Korean individuals) lowers risk, while a low level of response to alcohol predicts future heavy drinking and problems (Harrison 22e p.3678).","Useful blood markers of regular heavy intake are GGT (>35 U) and carbohydrate-deficient transferrin (CDT >20 U/L or >2.6%), with the combination more accurate than either alone; high-normal MCV (>=91) and elevated uric acid also support the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.3678-3679).","Continued heavy drinking shortens life span by about 10 years, with leading causes of early death being heart disease, cancer, accidents and suicide (Harrison 22e p.3678).","'Holiday heart' refers to atrial or ventricular arrhythmias, especially paroxysmal tachycardia, occurring temporarily after heavy drinking in people with no other evidence of heart disease (Harrison 22e p.3677)."],"pathophysiology":"Ethanol is a CNS depressant that acutely enhances GABA activity (notably at GABA-A receptors) and inhibits postsynaptic NMDA excitatory glutamate receptors; chronic exposure produces compensatory downregulation of GABA-related activity and upregulation of NMDA receptor subunits. Falling blood levels after chronic use therefore unmask a hyperexcitable state, explaining withdrawal phenomena. Acute alcohol raises dopamine in the ventral tegmentum and nucleus accumbens, releases beta-endorphins, and increases serotonin and stress hormones (cortisol, ACTH), driving reward, craving and relapse. Cellular (pharmacodynamic), metabolic (up to ~30% faster hepatic metabolism after 1-2 weeks) and learned/behavioral mechanisms contribute to acquired tolerance. Cellular adaptations may take weeks to resolve, so rapid drops in blood level produce withdrawal that is most intense in the first 5 days but with protracted symptoms (disturbed sleep, anxiety) lasting up to 4-6 months (Harrison 22e p.3675-3676).","additionalDifferentials":["Alcohol ketoacidosis versus diabetic ketoacidosis (alcohol-related shows raised ketones, only mild glucose elevation, large anion gap, mild-moderate lactate rise, beta-hydroxybutyrate/lactate ratio between 2:1 and 9:1) (Harrison 22e p.3675)","Substance-induced mood, anxiety or psychotic disorder versus an independent primary psychiatric disorder; about 40% of those with AUD meet criteria for a major depressive episode but roughly half are substance-induced and resolve within a month of abstinence (Harrison 22e p.3676-3677)","Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome and alcohol-related cognitive impairment versus other dementias; there is no single 'alcoholic dementia' entity (Harrison 22e p.3676)","Peripheral neuropathy of alcohol versus diabetic neuropathy (both bilateral, distal, with numbness, tingling, paresthesias) (Harrison 22e p.3676)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Diabetes mellitus (alcohol-induced transient hypoglycemia and abnormal glucose tolerance) (Harrison 22e p.3675)","Primary depressive, anxiety or psychotic disorder (alcohol-induced psychiatric symptoms during intoxication/withdrawal) (Harrison 22e p.3675, 3676-3677)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT >35 U) - marker of heavy consumption (Harrison 22e p.3678)","Carbohydrate-deficient transferrin (CDT >20 U/L or >2.6%); combined with GGT for higher accuracy (Harrison 22e p.3679)","Mean corpuscular volume (high-normal >=91) and serum uric acid (>7 mg/dL) (Harrison 22e p.3679)","Blood ethanol level (e.g., 100 mg/dL = 0.10 g/dL) (Harrison 22e p.3675)","Toxicology screen for other CNS depressants (benzodiazepines) and opioids in severe intoxication (Harrison 22e p.3679)","AUDIT 10-item screening questionnaire (score >=8 may indicate harmful use) - screening only, not diagnostic (Harrison 22e p.3679-3680)","MRI/CT may show ventricular enlargement and widened cortical sulci in ~50% of long-term AUD, usually reversible with abstinence (Harrison 22e p.3676)"],"redFlags":["Withdrawal seizure (about 2% of patients), with higher risk in older age, concomitant medical problems, polydrug misuse and larger alcohol quantities (Harrison 22e p.3680)","Withdrawal delirium / delirium tremens (about 1%): agitated delirium with confusion, fluctuating consciousness, tremor and marked autonomic overactivity (Harrison 22e p.3680)","Autonomic overactivity during withdrawal: rising pulse, respiratory rate, sweating and body temperature (Harrison 22e p.3680)","Severe intoxication with respiratory depression, cardiac arrhythmias or blood pressure instability; light coma near 0.30 g/dL and death 0.30-0.40 g/dL (Harrison 22e p.3676, 3679)","Gastrointestinal bleeding from hemorrhagic gastritis or a Mallory-Weiss tear after violent vomiting (Harrison 22e p.3677)","Pregnancy: any drinking risks fetal alcohol spectrum disorder; complete abstinence advised (Harrison 22e p.3677)"],"prognosis":"With at least a year of follow-up after treatment, more than half of people with AUD achieve a marked reduction in drinking and related problems or full abstinence, and even without formal treatment or self-help groups there is at least a 20% chance of spontaneous long-term remission. Many alcohol-related organ changes are reversible with abstinence: blood pressure normalizes within weeks, GGT/CDT return toward normal within weeks, thrombocytopenia usually resolves within a week (unless cirrhosis or congestive splenomegaly), MRI/CT brain changes generally reverse, and acute myopathy improves though may not fully remit. Continued heavy drinking shortens life span by roughly 10 years, mainly through heart disease, cancer, accidents and suicide (Harrison 22e p.3677-3678).","pitfalls":["Misreading alcohol-induced transient hypoglycemia/abnormal glucose tolerance as diabetes before 2-4 weeks of abstinence (Harrison 22e p.3675)","Misdiagnosing alcohol ketoacidosis as diabetic ketosis (Harrison 22e p.3675)","Treating substance-induced psychiatric symptoms with long-term medication when they typically resolve within days to ~4 weeks of abstinence (only short-term antipsychotics for substance-induced psychosis are usually needed) (Harrison 22e p.3676-3677)","Giving routine IV fluids in withdrawal - most patients are normally hydrated or mildly overhydrated, so fluids should be avoided unless there is a specific medical indication, bleeding, vomiting or diarrhea (Harrison 22e p.3680)","Relying on questionnaires (e.g., AUDIT) alone - they are screening tools and a face-to-face interview is required for diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.3679)","A false-negative tuberculin skin test from impaired delayed-hypersensitivity in chronic heavy drinkers (Harrison 22e p.3677)","Assuming AUD spares affluent or professional patients - lifetime risk among physicians is similar to the general population (Harrison 22e p.3678)"],"severityClassification":"DSM-5 alcohol use disorder severity by number of criteria endorsed in a 12-month period: mild = 2-3 items, moderate = 4-5 items, severe = >=6 of the 11 items (Harrison 22e p.3678). Effects of blood alcohol levels without tolerance: 0.02 decreased inhibitions; 0.08 reduced complex cognition and motor performance; 0.20 slurred speech, incoordination, poor judgment; 0.30 light coma with depressed vital signs; 0.40 death (Harrison 22e p.3676).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3675","Harrison 22e p.3676","Harrison 22e p.3677","Harrison 22e p.3678","Harrison 22e p.3679","Harrison 22e p.3680"],"crossLinks":[]},"alcohol_liver_disease":{"name":"Alcohol-Associated Liver Disease","system":"Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["alcoholic hepatitis","alcoholic cirrhosis","ALD"],"chapter":353,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2697-2700","clinicalPearls":["ALD is a spectrum: alcohol-associated fatty liver (steatosis) and steatohepatitis progress to fibrosis and cirrhosis; acute alcohol-associated hepatitis is an acute-on-chronic form associated with liver failure and high mortality (p.2697).","Characteristic biochemical signature: AST/ALT ratio >1 (commonly cited as >2:1 for alcoholic liver disease), with serum AST rarely exceeding 300 IU/L; in alcohol-associated hepatitis AST and ALT do not exceed 400 IU/L and the AST/ALT ratio is >1.5 with serum bilirubin >3 mg/dL (p.2699).","Steatosis and steatohepatitis are clinically silent; symptoms typically appear only when alcohol-associated cirrhosis decompensates or alcohol-associated hepatitis develops (p.2699).","Alcohol-associated hepatitis is defined clinically by heavy drinking (>40 g/d women, >50-60 g/d men) for >6 months with <60 days abstinence before symptom onset, presenting with rapid-onset jaundice (bilirubin >3 mg/dL), often with fever, malaise, and tender hepatomegaly (p.2698).","Prolonged alcohol abstinence is the most effective therapy to reduce progression and reverse ALD; steatosis and steatohepatitis are reversible with cessation of alcohol consumption (p.2699).","There are currently NO approved drugs for treatment of alcohol-associated steatosis or steatohepatitis with or without fibrosis (p.2699).","Severity of alcohol-associated hepatitis is stratified by Maddrey discriminant function (MDF) and MELD: severe disease (MDF >=32 or MELD >20) carries high short-term mortality (~30% at 3 months) and may benefit from glucocorticoids; moderate disease (MDF <32 or MELD <=20) still carries ~20% 1-year mortality (p.2700).","Lille score >=0.45 after 7 days of glucocorticoid treatment identifies non-responders unlikely to benefit from continued therapy; glucocorticoids should be stopped and early liver transplantation considered (p.2700).","Liver transplantation for alcohol-associated decompensated cirrhosis or HCC is the leading indication for liver transplantation in the United States (p.2699-2700).","In confirmed cirrhosis, HCC surveillance with liver ultrasonography and serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) every 6 months and upper GI endoscopy to screen for varices are recommended (p.2699).","Only 10-20% of individuals with alcohol-induced hepatic steatosis develop progressive liver disease and cirrhosis; women develop ALD at a lower daily alcohol intake (p.2698)."],"pathophysiology":"Ethanol is rapidly absorbed in the upper GI tract and metabolized predominantly in the liver, reaching it via the portal vein. Most ethanol is oxidized by alcohol dehydrogenase 1 (ADH1) to acetaldehyde in hepatocytes; chronic consumption also induces CYP2E1, which similarly generates acetaldehyde. Beyond direct acetaldehyde toxicity, ethanol metabolism generates reactive oxygen species causing hepatocyte injury through lipid peroxidation and DNA damage. Acetaldehyde is then oxidized to acetate by acetaldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH); inherited ALDH2 deficiency (common in Asian populations) causes acetaldehyde accumulation with nausea and cutaneous flushing. Steatosis arises because acetate becomes acetyl-CoA feeding fatty acid/triglyceride synthesis, alcohol upregulates lipogenic genes (partly epigenetically) while suppressing fatty acid transport/oxidation, and the increased NADH/NAD+ ratio reduces mitochondrial beta-oxidation; alcohol also mobilizes fatty acids from adipose and intestine into the liver. A subset develop gut dysbiosis and increased intestinal permeability, allowing microbial components (e.g., bacterial LPS) to reach the liver via the portal vein, driving inflammation, hepatocyte death, and fibrotic pathway activation; sustained alcohol use progresses to cirrhosis with portal hypertension. Impaired liver regeneration may contribute to acute alcohol-associated hepatitis arising in underlying cirrhosis (p.2698).","additionalDifferentials":["Viral hepatitis (e.g., hepatitis B or C)","Wilson's disease","Severe autoimmune liver disease","Drug-induced liver injury (DILI), including amiodarone-related pseudoalcoholic injury","Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD/NAFLD) and MetALD","Hemochromatosis","Ischemic hepatitis","Biliary obstruction","Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral hepatitis (hepatitis B or C) producing aminotransferase elevation that can be confused with ALD"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["MASLD/NAFLD (key distinction: ALD typically shows AST/ALT >2:1, whereas NAFLD usually has ALT >= AST)","Amiodarone hepatotoxicity producing pseudoalcoholic liver injury (steatosis to alcoholic-hepatitis-like neutrophilic infiltration with Mallory-Denk bodies)","Wilson's disease","Autoimmune hepatitis","Hemochromatosis","Ischemic hepatitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Liver function tests with AST, ALT (AST/ALT ratio), GGT, bilirubin, INR, albumin, platelet count","AUDIT (Alcohol Use Disorders Inventory Test) to identify alcohol use disorder","Abdominal ultrasound, MRI, or CT to detect steatosis, nodularity, and signs of portal hypertension","Controlled attenuation parameter (CAP) or MR proton density fat fraction (MR-PDFF) for noninvasive hepatic fat quantification","Transient elastography / liver stiffness measurement (e.g., FibroScan) for fibrosis staging","Fib-4 score (age, AST, ALT, platelets) to exclude advanced fibrosis/cirrhosis (threshold <3.25)","Transjugular liver biopsy when confounding factors are present (to confirm alcohol-associated hepatitis)","Imaging to exclude biliary obstruction and HCC; AFP and ultrasonography for HCC surveillance in cirrhosis","Infection workup: chest x-ray plus blood, urine, and ascites cultures in alcohol-associated hepatitis"],"redFlags":["Severe alcohol-associated hepatitis (MDF >=32 or MELD >20) - high short-term mortality (~30% at 3 months)","Rapid-onset jaundice with serum bilirubin >3 mg/dL, fever, and tender hepatomegaly","Clinical signs of hepatic decompensation: ascites, variceal bleeding, hepatic encephalopathy","Bacterial infection (occurs in 12-26% of severe alcohol-associated hepatitis at admission)","Systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS)","Acute kidney injury / hepatorenal syndrome","Lille score >=0.45 at day 7 (glucocorticoid non-response) - high 30-day mortality","Multiorgan failure and shock"],"prognosis":"Steatosis and steatohepatitis are reversible with abstinence, but only 10-20% of those with steatosis progress to cirrhosis. In alcohol-associated hepatitis, short-term mortality is predicted by MDF, MELD, or ABIC scores: moderate disease (MDF <32 or MELD <=20) carries ~20% 1-year mortality, whereas severe disease (MDF >=32 or MELD >20) carries ~30% 3-month mortality. Glucocorticoids reduce risk of death in severe disease within 28 days but not over the following 6 months. While short-term prognosis depends on disease severity at presentation, long-term prognosis (>1 year) largely depends on alcohol abstinence and underlying cirrhosis. Non-responders to medical therapy have high 30-day mortality (p.2700).","pitfalls":["Diagnosis of ALD requires exclusion of other liver diseases in heavy drinkers; do not attribute liver disease to alcohol without ruling out viral hepatitis, Wilson's disease, autoimmune liver disease, and DILI.","Atypical laboratory values (AST <50 IU/L or >400 IU/L, or AST/ALT ratio <1.5) argue against alcohol-associated hepatitis and warrant transjugular liver biopsy - 10-20% of clinically diagnosed cases have a different disease on biopsy.","Do not give glucocorticoids when contraindications are present: uncontrolled infection/sepsis, AKI/hepatorenal syndrome, uncontrolled upper GI bleeding, concomitant viral hepatitis/HCC/pancreatitis/DILI/active TB/HIV, multiorgan failure, or shock; glucocorticoids may be used only once infection, sepsis, and GI bleeding are adequately controlled.","Liver biopsy is rarely needed to diagnose simple steatosis or steatohepatitis - reserve invasive biopsy for confounding/atypical cases.","Distinguish ALD from NAFLD/MASLD by the AST/ALT pattern (ALD favors AST/ALT >2:1)."],"severityClassification":"Alcohol-associated hepatitis severity: Moderate AH = MDF <32 or MELD <=20 (~20% 1-year mortality); Severe AH = Maddrey discriminant function (MDF) >=32 or MELD >20 (~30% 3-month mortality), candidates for glucocorticoids if no contraindication. MDF = 4.6 x (prothrombin time prolongation above control in seconds) + serum bilirubin (mg/dL). Day-7 Lille score >=0.45 defines glucocorticoid non-response. Cirrhosis prognosis assessed by Child-Pugh-Turcotte (CPT) and MELD (or sodium-MELD). Fibrosis staging by liver stiffness: <6 kPa normal, >8 kPa indicates >=F3 advanced fibrosis, >12.5 kPa indicates F4 cirrhosis.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2697-2700"],"crossLinks":["decomp_cirrhosis","hepatic_enceph","toxic_hepatitis","chronic_hepatitis"]},"alcohol_withdrawal":{"name":"Alcohol withdrawal syndrome","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["alcohol withdrawal","delirium tremens","DTs","alcohol withdrawal delirium"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Occurs on reduction or cessation of heavy, sustained alcohol use, with symptoms typically emerging within hours to a few days","Early features include tremor, anxiety, agitation, sweating, nausea, tachycardia, and hypertension; insomnia and irritability are common","Withdrawal seizures (typically generalised tonic-clonic) tend to occur early, usually within the first day or two","Alcoholic hallucinosis produces hallucinations, often visual, with an otherwise clear sensorium, distinguishing it from delirium tremens","Delirium tremens is a later, life-threatening complication with clouded consciousness, disorientation, severe autonomic hyperactivity, and hallucinations, usually appearing after a couple of days","Management principles rest on benzodiazepine-based regimens, often symptom-triggered using a standardised withdrawal scale, in a supportive monitored setting","Give thiamine before or with glucose to prevent precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy, and correct fluid and electrolyte disturbances","Assess withdrawal severity and complication risk with a validated instrument and identify high-risk patients for closer monitoring"],"pathophysiology":"Chronic alcohol exposure enhances inhibitory gamma-aminobutyric acid signalling and suppresses excitatory glutamatergic (NMDA receptor) activity; the brain adapts by downregulating GABA and upregulating glutamate systems. When alcohol is withdrawn, this adaptation leaves a state of central nervous system hyperexcitability and autonomic overactivity, producing the tremor, seizures, and delirium of the withdrawal syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Delirium from other causes (infection, metabolic derangement)","Sedative-hypnotic or benzodiazepine withdrawal","Stimulant intoxication","Thyroid storm","Sepsis or central nervous system infection","Wernicke encephalopathy","Hepatic encephalopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Validated withdrawal severity scale to guide symptom-triggered treatment","Electrolytes including magnesium and phosphate, glucose, and renal function","Liver function tests and full blood count","Assessment for infection and other precipitants of delirium","Thiamine status and evaluation for Wernicke encephalopathy"],"redFlags":["Delirium tremens with severe autonomic instability, a medical emergency with appreciable mortality if untreated","Withdrawal seizures or status epilepticus","Signs of Wernicke encephalopathy (confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia)","Hyperthermia, marked tachycardia, and haemodynamic instability"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated withdrawal usually resolves within several days with appropriate treatment. Delirium tremens carries significant mortality if unrecognised but is greatly reduced with timely benzodiazepine therapy and supportive care. Long-term prognosis depends on engagement with relapse prevention and treatment of alcohol use disorder.","pitfalls":["Giving glucose before thiamine and precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy","Underestimating severity and under-dosing benzodiazepines in early withdrawal","Attributing agitation solely to withdrawal while missing infection, bleeding, or metabolic causes","Failing to identify patients at high risk of seizures or delirium tremens","Not addressing underlying alcohol use disorder after acute treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"allergic_bronchopulmonary_aspergillosis":{"name":"Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ABPA","allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","aspergillus hypersensitivity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["ABPA is a hypersensitivity reaction to Aspergillus fumigatus colonising the airways, occurring almost exclusively in patients with asthma or cystic fibrosis","Suspect it in poorly controlled asthma with recurrent exacerbations, fleeting pulmonary infiltrates, and expectoration of brownish mucus plugs","Markedly elevated total serum IgE and Aspergillus-specific IgE and IgG, with peripheral eosinophilia, support the diagnosis","A positive immediate skin test or specific IgE to Aspergillus reflects the underlying sensitisation","Imaging classically shows central bronchiectasis and mucoid impaction, sometimes with transient infiltrates","Treatment centres on suppressing the immune and inflammatory response with corticosteroids, with antifungal therapy used to reduce fungal burden in selected cases","The disease evolves through stages including acute presentation, remission, exacerbation, and, if untreated, fibrotic end-stage lung disease","It is a hypersensitivity phenomenon rather than tissue-invasive infection, distinguishing it from invasive aspergillosis and aspergilloma"],"pathophysiology":"In predisposed patients, inhaled Aspergillus fumigatus spores germinate and persist within the viscid airway mucus rather than being cleared. Fungal antigens provoke a vigorous combined type I (IgE-mediated) and type III (immune-complex) hypersensitivity response, with strong Th2 activation, eosinophil recruitment, and high IgE production. The resulting intense airway inflammation, mucus hypersecretion, and mucoid impaction damage the bronchial walls, leading to central bronchiectasis. Recurrent inflammatory episodes can ultimately produce fibrosis and irreversible loss of lung function.","additionalDifferentials":["Poorly controlled asthma without ABPA","Cystic fibrosis exacerbation","Eosinophilic pneumonia","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Churg-Strauss)","Bronchiectasis of other cause","Invasive or semi-invasive aspergillosis and aspergilloma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Total serum IgE, which is characteristically very high and useful for monitoring","Aspergillus-specific IgE and IgG (precipitins) and skin testing for sensitisation","Peripheral blood eosinophil count","High-resolution CT to detect central bronchiectasis and mucoid impaction","Sputum examination for Aspergillus and eosinophils"],"redFlags":["Progressive fixed airflow obstruction or fibrosis indicating advanced disease","Frequent severe asthma exacerbations unresponsive to standard therapy","Features suggesting invasive aspergillosis in an immunocompromised patient","Massive mucoid impaction causing lobar collapse"],"prognosis":"With early recognition and appropriate immunosuppressive treatment, exacerbations can be controlled and lung damage limited; however, repeated undertreated flares lead to progressive bronchiectasis and fibrosis with permanent loss of lung function, so long-term monitoring of symptoms and IgE is important.","pitfalls":["Mistaking ABPA for ordinary difficult asthma and missing the diagnosis","Confusing this hypersensitivity condition with invasive aspergillosis and mismanaging treatment","Failing to monitor total IgE to gauge disease activity and treatment response","Overlooking ABPA as a treatable cause of bronchiectasis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"allergic_rhinitis":{"name":"Allergic rhinitis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hay fever","seasonal allergic rhinitis","perennial allergic rhinitis","allergic rhinosinusitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An IgE-mediated hypersensitivity of the nasal mucosa presenting with sneezing, watery rhinorrhoea, nasal itching and congestion","Often classified as seasonal (pollen-driven) or perennial (dust mite, animal dander, mould), and by severity and effect on quality of life","Commonly associated with allergic conjunctivitis, asthma and eczema as part of the atopic spectrum","Examination typically reveals pale, boggy, oedematous nasal mucosa with clear secretions, and children may show an allergic salute crease and allergic shiners","Diagnosis is largely clinical, supported by skin prick testing or specific IgE assays to identify triggers","Management principles begin with allergen avoidance and saline measures, then intranasal corticosteroids and antihistamines according to severity","Poorly controlled disease contributes to sinusitis, otitis media with effusion, sleep disturbance and reduced school or work performance","Immunotherapy may be considered for confirmed single-allergen sensitivity that is inadequately controlled by standard therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Allergic rhinitis is an IgE-mediated inflammatory response of the nasal mucosa to inhaled allergens. On re-exposure, allergen cross-links IgE bound to mast cells, triggering an early-phase release of histamine and other mediators that produce sneezing, itching, rhinorrhoea and congestion within minutes. A late-phase reaction, driven by recruited eosinophils, basophils and T cells, sustains mucosal inflammation and congestion hours later and heightens nasal reactivity. Chronic inflammation causes mucosal oedema and hyperresponsiveness, and shared airway inflammation links the condition to asthma.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-allergic (vasomotor) rhinitis","Infective rhinitis or rhinosinusitis","Rhinitis medicamentosa from overuse of decongestant sprays","Nasal polyps","Structural causes such as deviated nasal septum","Hormonal or drug-induced rhinitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anterior rhinoscopy and nasal endoscopy to assess mucosa and exclude polyps","Skin prick testing to identify causative allergens","Serum allergen-specific IgE testing where skin testing is unsuitable","Nasal smear for eosinophils in selected cases","Assessment for coexisting asthma and sinus disease"],"redFlags":["Unilateral nasal symptoms, bleeding or a mass suggesting a tumour or foreign body","Progressive obstruction with facial pain and purulent discharge indicating complicated sinusitis","Anosmia or orbital or visual symptoms requiring further evaluation","Symptoms of poorly controlled coexisting asthma"],"prognosis":"Allergic rhinitis is generally a chronic but benign condition; with allergen avoidance and appropriate pharmacotherapy most patients achieve good symptom control, though it may persist for years and contribute to comorbidities.","pitfalls":["Missing coexisting asthma that needs concurrent treatment","Encouraging prolonged use of topical decongestants leading to rebound rhinitis","Attributing unilateral or bloodstained symptoms to allergy rather than investigating for tumour or foreign body","Underusing intranasal corticosteroids, the most effective agents, in moderate to severe disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"alopecia_areata":{"name":"Alopecia Areata","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AA","patchy hair loss","alopecia totalis","alopecia universalis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as sudden, sharply bordered round or oval bald patches on otherwise normal-appearing scalp skin, without scarring or scale","Look for exclamation-mark hairs at the margins of active patches, where the shaft narrows towards the scalp","A positive hair-pull test at the periphery indicates ongoing disease activity","Extensive forms include loss of all scalp hair (totalis) and loss of all body hair (universalis); the ophiasis pattern along the occipital and temporal rim tends to respond poorly","Nail involvement such as fine regular pitting or roughening may accompany more severe or persistent disease","Associations with thyroid disease, vitiligo, atopy, and other autoimmune conditions warrant a focused history and examination","Regrowth often begins as fine depigmented or white hairs that later regain colour","Management ranges from watchful waiting in limited disease to intralesional or topical anti-inflammatory therapy, contact immunotherapy, and newer targeted systemic agents for extensive involvement; psychological impact should be addressed directly"],"pathophysiology":"Alopecia areata is an organ-specific autoimmune disorder in which the immune privilege of the hair follicle breaks down, allowing a T-cell-mediated attack focused on the anagen-phase follicular bulb. Interferon-gamma and other cytokines signalling through the JAK-STAT pathway drive a peribulbar lymphocytic infiltrate classically likened to a swarm of bees. Affected follicles prematurely enter catagen and telogen and produce dystrophic hairs, but the follicular stem-cell compartment is preserved, which is why regrowth remains possible even after long-standing loss. A polygenic predisposition interacts with environmental triggers, and there is overlap with other autoimmune diseases.","additionalDifferentials":["Tinea capitis (scaly, inflamed, often with broken hairs and positive mycology)","Trichotillomania (irregular patches with hairs of varying length)","Telogen effluvium (diffuse shedding rather than discrete patches)","Traction alopecia at sites of chronic tension","Early scarring alopecias such as lichen planopilaris or discoid lupus","Secondary syphilis producing a moth-eaten pattern"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Usually a clinical diagnosis aided by dermoscopy showing exclamation-mark hairs, yellow dots, and black dots","Mycology (scrapings or culture) when tinea capitis cannot be excluded","Thyroid function and autoantibody testing when clinically indicated","Scalp biopsy reserved for atypical or diagnostically uncertain cases","Screening for associated autoimmune disease guided by symptoms"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive or extensive loss suggesting evolution towards totalis or universalis","Scarring, scale, or follicular destruction indicating a different, potentially permanent alopecia","Significant psychological distress or impact on functioning requiring support","Diffuse hair loss with systemic symptoms prompting evaluation for an underlying disorder"],"prognosis":"Outcome is variable and unpredictable; limited single patches frequently regrow spontaneously within months, whereas early onset, extensive involvement, the ophiasis pattern, nail changes, and atopy predict a more prolonged and relapsing course.","pitfalls":["Confusing non-scarring alopecia areata with a scarring alopecia and missing the window for treatment","Overlooking tinea capitis in children by not performing mycology","Attributing patchy loss to alopecia areata when hair-length variation points to trichotillomania","Underestimating the psychological burden and failing to offer support or counselling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"alport_syndrome":{"name":"Alport syndrome","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hereditary nephritis","hereditary nephritis with deafness","Alport's syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Alport syndrome is an inherited disorder of type IV collagen affecting the basement membranes of the kidney, cochlea, and eye","The classic triad is progressive glomerular disease, sensorineural hearing loss, and characteristic ocular abnormalities such as lenticonus","Persistent microscopic haematuria, often from early childhood, is the earliest and most consistent renal finding, later accompanied by proteinuria and declining function","The most common form is X-linked, so affected males typically develop severe progressive disease while female carriers have a more variable course","A family history of haematuria, hearing loss, or renal failure across generations is an important diagnostic clue","Electron microscopy of a kidney biopsy shows characteristic splitting and lamellation of the glomerular basement membrane","Renin-angiotensin system blockade is a core management principle to reduce proteinuria and slow progression, and early treatment appears to delay kidney failure","Genetic diagnosis is increasingly used to confirm the disorder, define inheritance, and guide family screening and counselling"],"pathophysiology":"Alport syndrome arises from mutations in the genes encoding the alpha-3, alpha-4, or alpha-5 chains of type IV collagen, the principal structural component of specialised basement membranes. Defective assembly of the collagen IV network weakens the glomerular basement membrane, which progressively thickens, thins, splits, and develops the characteristic basket-weave lamellation seen on electron microscopy. The abnormal membrane cannot maintain a normal filtration barrier, leading to haematuria, progressive proteinuria, glomerulosclerosis, and interstitial fibrosis. Because the same collagen chains are found in the cochlea and the lens and retina, the disorder also produces sensorineural hearing loss and ocular changes. Inheritance is most often X-linked, with autosomal recessive and dominant forms accounting for the remainder and influencing severity and sex distribution.","additionalDifferentials":["Thin basement membrane nephropathy (benign familial haematuria)","IgA nephropathy","Other hereditary glomerulopathies","Post-infectious glomerulonephritis","Fabry disease with renal involvement","Acquired causes of persistent haematuria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis and microscopy documenting persistent haematuria and quantifying proteinuria","Audiometry to detect sensorineural hearing loss","Ophthalmological examination for lenticonus and retinal changes","Kidney biopsy with electron microscopy of the glomerular basement membrane","Genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis and define the inheritance pattern","Family screening for haematuria and hearing loss"],"redFlags":["Emergence of proteinuria and rising creatinine signalling progression toward kidney failure","Rapidly worsening renal function suggesting a superimposed process","Progressive hearing loss affecting communication","Acute visual disturbance from ocular complications"],"prognosis":"In classic X-linked disease, affected males commonly progress to end-stage kidney disease by early to middle adulthood, whereas female carriers and some autosomal forms have a more variable and often milder course. Early renin-angiotensin blockade can delay progression. Transplantation is generally successful, though a small number of patients develop anti-GBM disease in the graft against the previously absent collagen antigen.","pitfalls":["Dismissing persistent childhood haematuria as benign without a family and hearing assessment","Confusing Alport syndrome with thin basement membrane nephropathy on light microscopy alone","Delaying renin-angiotensin blockade until proteinuria is advanced","Overlooking the small post-transplant risk of anti-GBM disease in affected recipients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"altitude_illness":{"name":"Altitude Illness","system":"Environmental / Multisystem (Neurologic-Pulmonary)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Acute mountain sickness","AMS","High-altitude cerebral edema","HACE","High-altitude pulmonary edema","HAPE","Mountain sickness","Altitude sickness"],"chapter":475,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3754-3760","clinicalPearls":["Altitude illness encompasses a spectrum: acute mountain sickness (AMS, the benign form) and the two life-threatening forms, high-altitude cerebral edema (HACE) and high-altitude pulmonary edema (HAPE) (Harrison 22e p.3754).","AMS is a clinical diagnosis presenting 6-12 h after ascent with nonspecific symptoms (headache, nausea, fatigue, dizziness) and few physical findings; the Lake Louise Scoring System is used for research uniformity (Harrison 22e p.3755).","AMS and HACE are viewed as opposite ends of one neurologic continuum; HACE (not AMS) is an encephalopathy whose hallmarks are ataxia and altered consciousness with diffuse cerebral involvement, typically without focal deficits, and progression can be rapid (Harrison 22e p.3755).","The two most important risk factors for altitude illness are a rapid rate of ascent and a prior history of high-altitude illness; exertion is a risk factor, but lack of physical fitness is not (Harrison 22e p.3755).","Illness is likely above 2500 m but has been documented at 1500-2500 m; in the Everest region about 50% of trekkers ascending above 4000 m over 5+ days develop AMS, rising to 84% with direct flight to 3860 m, whereas HACE and HAPE incidence is far lower (roughly 0.1-4%) (Harrison 22e p.3754).","HAPE is primarily a pulmonary disorder and need not be preceded by AMS; it develops within 2-4 days of arrival and rarely after 4-5 days at the same altitude owing to vascular remodeling and adaptation (Harrison 22e p.3757).","HAPE is a noncardiogenic pulmonary edema with normal pulmonary artery wedge pressure, driven by patchy hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction causing regional overperfusion, capillary pressures above 18 mmHg, and capillary stress failure (Harrison 22e p.3757).","Retinal hemorrhages are common at altitudes of 5000 m or more even without AMS/HACE symptoms; most resolve on descent, but macular hemorrhage can cause devastating, occasionally permanent visual loss (Harrison 22e p.3755, 3759).","Gradual ascent with time for acclimatization is the best preventive measure; a conservative target above 3000 m is graded gain of no more than ~300 m per sleeping-altitude night with an extra acclimatization day every third day of gain (Harrison 22e p.3756).","Sickle cell disease is one of the rare exposures in which altitude can provoke vaso-occlusive crisis, reported even at passes as low as 2500 m; thalassemia has not been linked to high-altitude problems (Harrison 22e p.3759).","Hypertensive patients are not more prone to altitude illness, and most with well-controlled hypertension can be reassured that blood pressure stays relatively stable; antihypertensives should be continued at altitude (Harrison 22e p.3759).","High-altitude cough can be severe enough to cause rib fracture above 5000 m; it is multifactorial (cold dry air plus hypoxia and respiratory water loss), and infection is generally not a common cause (Harrison 22e p.3758)."],"pathophysiology":"Ascent lowers barometric pressure and thus the partial pressure of inspired oxygen, reducing the pressure gradient driving oxygen diffusion along the oxygen cascade. Hypobaric hypoxia is the central trigger for all forms of altitude illness. The normal struggle response is increased ventilation (the cornerstone of acclimatization) mediated by the carotid bodies; this can produce marked respiratory alkalosis (arterial pH may exceed 7.7 at extreme altitude) and dehydration, with alkalosis blunting ventilatory drive in sleep and causing periodic breathing and hypoxemia. Early acclimatization includes renal carbonic-anhydrase suppression with dilute alkaline urine to correct pH, plus increased sympathetic tone, rising erythropoietin/hemoglobin and red cell mass, greater tissue capillary density and mitochondrial numbers, and elevated 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate. Hypoxia-inducible factor governs transcriptional hypoxia responses; the EPAS1 gene (HIF-2alpha) and EGLN1/PPARA influence adaptation in Tibetan highlanders (lower hemoglobin than Han Chinese or Andeans). In AMS/HACE, raised intracranial pressure, increased sympathetic activity, relative hypoventilation, fluid retention/redistribution, and impaired gas exchange contribute; MRI evidence points to vasogenic (interstitial) cerebral edema, especially in the splenium of the corpus callosum, that may become cytotoxic in severe HACE, with hemosiderin deposits as long-lasting footprints. Impaired cerebral autoregulation with hypoxic vasodilation, altered blood-brain-barrier permeability via histamine, arachidonic acid, and VEGF, and venous outflow obstruction raising capillary pressure all promote edema. HAPE results from patchy hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction with regional overperfusion and capillary stress failure at normal wedge pressure; endothelial dysfunction with reduced nitric oxide availability and elevated endothelin-1, exaggerated sympathetic drive (pulmonary venoconstriction), and impaired transepithelial alveolar fluid clearance are implicated. Inflammation in HAPE is regarded as an epiphenomenon rather than the primary cause, although inflammatory/viral respiratory processes can predispose to it.","additionalDifferentials":["Exhaustion","Dehydration","Hypothermia","Alcoholic hangover","Hyponatremia","Migraine","Anxiety attack (for HAPE)","Pneumonia (for HAPE)","Pneumothorax (for HAPE)","Pulmonary embolism (for HAPE)"],"infectionMimics":["Respiratory tract infection / viral illness (HAPE historically misdiagnosed as viral pneumonia or as a viral illness in reentry HAPE)","Pneumonia (HAPE radiographic opacities simulating pneumonic consolidation)","Bacterial or Giardia enteric infection (high-altitude GI bloating/distension must be distinguished from infectious diarrhea)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cardiogenic pulmonary edema / heart failure (HAPE was historically mistaken for heart failure)","Acute respiratory distress syndrome (another noncardiogenic edema, but lung architecture is preserved in HAPE)","Pulmonary embolism","Pneumothorax","Anxiety/panic attack","Stroke or TIA unrelated to altitude illness","Subarachnoid hemorrhage, transient global amnesia, delirium, cranial nerve palsy occurring at altitude outside altitude sickness"],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment with Lake Louise Scoring System (used chiefly for research uniformity)","Pulse oximetry (oxygen saturation; arterial blood gas usually not necessary for HAPE)","Chest radiography (patchy/localized opacities or streaky interstitial edema in HAPE; Kerley B lines and bat-wing pattern are NOT seen)","Electrocardiography (may show right ventricular strain or hypertrophy in HAPE)","Brain MRI (T2 signal change in white matter and splenium of corpus callosum confirmatory of HACE; hemosiderin deposits as footprints)","Echocardiography (recommended when HAPE occurs below 3000 m or when predisposing cardiopulmonary abnormality is suspected)","Lung ultrasound comet-tail/B-line scoring (sensitive but not specific for HAPE)"],"redFlags":["Ataxia or altered consciousness (hallmarks of HACE) - mandates immediate descent","Any hint of HAPE or HACE mandates descent","Tachypnea and tachycardia at rest as HAPE progresses","Blood-tinged sputum following a dry persistent cough","Disproportionate fall in exercise tolerance, or fatigue/dyspnea at rest as the only initial HAPE signs","Debilitating fatigue consistent with severe AMS on descent from a summit (important risk factor for death in mountaineers)","Papilledema or retinal hemorrhages, especially macular hemorrhage with visual loss","Worsening symptoms despite halting ascent"],"prognosis":"AMS is benign and usually self-limited; once symptoms abate (with or without acetazolamide) the patient may reascend gradually. HACE and HAPE are life-threatening but generally reversible with prompt recognition and descent. In HAPE the lung architecture is typically well preserved with rapid reversibility (radiographic opacities can clear in about 2 days), allowing some patients to reascend slowly after a few days of descent and rest; after HACE, reascent within the same trip may be inadvisable. Severe AMS-related debilitating fatigue on descent is an important predictor of death in mountaineers, and macular hemorrhage can rarely cause permanent visual loss. HACE complicated by globus pallidus lesions has been reported to lead to parkinsonism.","pitfalls":["Treating AMS as trivial - symptoms overlap with exhaustion, dehydration, hypothermia, hangover, and hyponatremia, and it can progress to life-threatening HACE.","Relying on pulse oximetry alone to predict AMS during a trek - readings should not be used predictively unless guided by clinical signs.","Assuming HAPE is always preceded by AMS - it is primarily pulmonary and may present without headache or nausea, sometimes only as fatigue and rest dyspnea.","Misreading HAPE chest opacities as pneumonia (cold) or as heart failure - Kerley B lines and bat-wing pattern are absent in HAPE.","Confusing sudden neurologic events (TIA, stroke, subarachnoid hemorrhage, cranial nerve palsy) at altitude with altitude illness - AMS evolves gradually whereas these typically occur suddenly.","Mistaking reentry HAPE in returning highlanders for a viral illness.","Injudicious prophylactic glucocorticoid use by climbers to boost performance - can be tragic owing to severe side effects, especially with prolonged use.","Overhydration - adequate (not excessive) hydration is intended; potent diuretics such as furosemide should be avoided in HAPE.","Comet-tail/B-line lung ultrasound is sensitive but not specific, since B-lines also appear in individuals who never develop clinical HAPE - clinical correlation is essential."],"severityClassification":"AMS is graded clinically as mild versus moderate, a subjective judgment based on headache severity and the presence/severity of nausea, fatigue, and dizziness (the Lake Louise Scoring System, without the sleep score, is used for research). The overall spectrum runs from benign AMS to the life-threatening forms HACE (neurologic, the severe end of the AMS-HACE continuum) and HAPE (pulmonary), which can coexist.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3754","Harrison 22e p.3755","Harrison 22e p.3756","Harrison 22e p.3757","Harrison 22e p.3758","Harrison 22e p.3759","Harrison 22e p.3760"],"crossLinks":[]},"aluminium_phosphide_poisoning":{"name":"Aluminium phosphide poisoning","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["aluminum phosphide poisoning","celphos poisoning","rice tablet poisoning","phosphine poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A widely used grain fumigant that reacts with moisture and gastric acid to liberate phosphine gas, a potent mitochondrial poison, and is a common agent of fatal deliberate self-harm in some regions","There is no specific antidote, so management is entirely supportive and directed at aggressive haemodynamic and metabolic resuscitation","Profound, often refractory circulatory shock from direct myocardial toxicity and vasodilatation is the dominant and most lethal feature","Severe high anion-gap metabolic acidosis, myocarditis, arrhythmias, and multi-organ failure develop early","A garlicky or decaying-fish odour on the breath or vomitus is a clinical clue to phosphine exposure","Rescuers must be protected, as phosphine off-gassed from vomitus and gastric contents can poison staff; ventilate the area and avoid mouth-to-mouth","Reported adjuncts include gastric decontamination with agents intended to reduce phosphine liberation, magnesium, high-dose steroids, intravenous lipid emulsion, and vasopressors, though evidence is limited","Early intensive-care support with vasopressors, correction of acidosis, and consideration of extracorporeal circulatory support offers the best chance in a highly lethal poisoning"],"pathophysiology":"On contact with water or gastric acid, aluminium phosphide releases phosphine gas, which inhibits mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase and generates reactive oxygen species, arresting oxidative phosphorylation across tissues. The heart is especially vulnerable, developing myocardial depression, myocarditis, and arrhythmias, while widespread vasodilatation and cellular energy failure produce profound refractory shock. Lipid peroxidation and oxidative injury drive a severe metabolic (lactic) acidosis and multi-organ dysfunction affecting the liver, kidneys, and adrenal glands.","additionalDifferentials":["Other cellular asphyxiants (cyanide, hydrogen sulfide)","Cardiogenic shock from other causes","Septic shock","Other lethal pesticide ingestions","Fulminant myocarditis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous ECG and cardiac monitoring for arrhythmia and myocarditis","Echocardiography to assess ventricular function","Arterial blood gas with anion gap and lactate","Serum electrolytes including magnesium, renal and liver function","Silver nitrate paper test on breath or gastric aspirate to detect phosphine where available"],"redFlags":["Refractory hypotension and shock unresponsive to fluids and vasopressors","Severe metabolic acidosis with rising lactate","Arrhythmias or echocardiographic evidence of myocardial dysfunction","Large ingestion of a fresh (unexposed) tablet, associated with very high mortality","Risk of secondary poisoning of healthcare staff from off-gassing"],"prognosis":"Poisoning carries very high mortality, particularly with fresh tablets and established shock or severe acidosis; death commonly occurs within the first day or two from cardiovascular collapse, and survival is largely determined by ingested dose and speed of intensive supportive care.","pitfalls":["Expecting a specific antidote to exist and delaying aggressive supportive resuscitation","Underestimating lethality because the patient initially looks stable before rapid deterioration","Neglecting staff protection against phosphine off-gassing from the patient","Inadequate correction of acidosis and under-resuscitation of shock"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"alzheimers_disease":{"name":"Alzheimer's Disease","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AD","Alzheimer dementia"],"chapter":442,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3475-3483","clinicalPearls":["Most common cause of dementia, contributing an estimated 60-70% of all cases; in the U.S. ~10% of people over 65 have AD (3% at age 65-74, 17% at 75-84, 32% at 85+) (Harrison 22e p.3475, p.3478).","Typical presentation is insidious loss of episodic memory followed by slowly progressive multidomain dementia, with cognitive change progressing from memory to executive, language, and visuospatial deficits (Harrison 22e p.3475-3476).","About 20% of patients present with nonmemory complaints; atypical variants include posterior cortical atrophy (visual processing), logopenic progressive aphasia, corticobasal (akinetic-rigid-dystonic) syndrome, and a dysexecutive/behavioral (frontal) variant (Harrison 22e p.3475-3476).","Depression, social withdrawal, and anxiety may occur early and represent a prodrome before cognitive symptoms are apparent (Harrison 22e p.3476).","~50% of patients with mild cognitive impairment (MCI), roughly 12% per year, progress to dementia over 4 years; the MCI construct is increasingly replaced by 'early symptomatic AD' (Harrison 22e p.3476).","Preclinical biomarker changes may precede clinical symptoms by 20 years or more, creating a window for early treatment and prevention (Harrison 22e p.3476).","Core 1 biomarkers (amyloid PET, CSF/plasma Abeta and p-Tau) are considered necessary and sufficient for biological diagnosis of AD under the revised Alzheimer's Association criteria; tau PET is a core 2 biomarker used for staging (Harrison 22e p.3477).","A negative amyloid PET scan in a patient with dementia makes an AD diagnosis unlikely; a positive scan can establish eligibility for amyloid-lowering therapy (Harrison 22e p.3477).","Decreases in the CSF Abeta42/Abeta40 ratio and increases in p-Tau181 or p-Tau217 correlate with AD pathology; plasma biomarkers show high concordance with amyloid PET and are entering clinical use (Harrison 22e p.3477).","ApoE epsilon4 is the most important genetic risk factor for sporadic AD: one allele raises risk ~2-3 fold (higher in women), two alleles raise it ~10-15 fold; each allele shifts onset ~10 years earlier (Harrison 22e p.3475, p.3479-3480).","Lecanemab (anti-Abeta protofibril) and donanemab (anti-pyroglutamate Abeta) are FDA-approved disease-modifying antiamyloid antibodies for early-stage AD, showing ~22-27% slowing of clinical decline; restricted to MCI/mild dementia with biomarker-confirmed Abeta pathology (Harrison 22e p.3480-3481).","Antiamyloid antibodies carry risk of amyloid-related imaging abnormalities (ARIA-E edema, ARIA-H microhemorrhage/siderosis); risk rises with each ApoE epsilon4 allele and is highest in epsilon4 homozygotes, prompting an FDA box warning (Harrison 22e p.3481).","Treatment with lecanemab or donanemab should be considered a contraindication to peripheral thrombolysis, as catastrophic multifocal hemorrhages and death have been reported when antiamyloid-treated patients received thrombolytics for stroke (Harrison 22e p.3482).","Medications with strong anticholinergic effects (e.g., diphenhydramine sleep aids, oxybutynin) should be vigilantly avoided in AD (Harrison 22e p.3482).","Typical symptomatic duration is 8-10 years (range 1-25 years); death often results from malnutrition, secondary infections, pulmonary emboli, heart disease, or, most commonly, aspiration (Harrison 22e p.3476)."],"pathophysiology":"AD is characterized by extracellular neuritic plaques containing amyloid beta (Abeta) and intracellular neurofibrillary tangles (NFTs) of hyperphosphorylated tau, with Abeta also accumulating in cortical and leptomeningeal blood vessel walls (amyloid angiopathy). Abeta is a 39-42 amino acid peptide cleaved from amyloid precursor protein (APP) by beta and gamma secretases; excess production of Abeta42 is a key initiator of cellular damage, and soluble oligomers are thought to be the early toxic species before fibril/plaque formation. Hyperphosphorylated tau can no longer stabilize microtubules and redistributes from axon to cytoplasm and dendrites, with prion-like templating proposed to drive tau spread. Earliest and most severe degeneration is in the medial temporal lobe (entorhinal/perirhinal cortex, hippocampus), inferolateral temporal cortex, and nucleus basalis of Meynert; atrophy spreads to temporoparietal and frontal cortices. Biochemically there is depletion of acetylcholine (degeneration of cholinergic nucleus basalis of Meynert neurons) plus noradrenergic and serotonergic loss from locus coeruleus and dorsal raphe. Genetic contributors include autosomal dominant mutations in APP (chromosome 21), PSEN-1 (chromosome 14, most common cause of early-onset familial AD), and PSEN-2 (chromosome 1), plus the ApoE epsilon4 risk allele (chromosome 19) and >40 minor GWAS risk variants in innate immunity, lipid metabolism, and synaptic pathways (e.g., CLU, PICALM, CR1, TREM2). (Harrison 22e p.3475, p.3478-3480)","additionalDifferentials":["Vascular dementia (early prominent gait disturbance with only mild memory loss) (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Dementia with Lewy bodies (early parkinsonism with fluctuating alertness, visual hallucinations, delusional misidentification) (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Parkinson's disease / Parkinson's disease dementia (resting tremor, stooped posture, bradykinesia, masked facies) (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Frontotemporal dementia (prominent behavioral changes with intact navigation and focal anterior-predominant atrophy) (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease / prion disease (rapid progression over weeks-months with rigidity and myoclonus) (Harrison 22e p.3476, p.3482)","Limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy (LATE) and primary age-related tauopathy (PART) - can mimic AD clinically, especially in biomarker-negative older patients with progressive amnestic dementia (Harrison 22e p.3476, p.3477)","Huntington's disease and rare hereditary ataxias when family history of dementia is present (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Primary or metastatic brain neoplasm (early focal seizure) (Harrison 22e p.3476)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral or bacterial encephalitides (listed as causes of rapidly progressive dementia in the CJD differential) (Harrison 22e p.3483)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Vitamin B12 deficiency (loss of joint position and vibration sensibility with Babinski signs; especially with autoimmune disease, small bowel resection/irradiation, or veganism) (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Normal-pressure hydrocephalus (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Depression-related cognitive impairment (depressive pseudodementia) (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Chronic drug intoxication (history of treatment for insomnia, anxiety, psychiatric disturbance, or epilepsy) (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Thyroid and other reversible/metabolic causes of dementia to be excluded per Tables 31-1, 31-3, 31-4 (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Hashimoto's encephalopathy, CNS vasculitis, CNS lymphoma, paraneoplastic/autoimmune syndromes (rapidly progressive dementia differential) (Harrison 22e p.3483)"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI/CT structural imaging - mainly to exclude other disorders; AD shows posterior-predominant cortical and medial temporal (hippocampal) atrophy and may be normal early (Harrison 22e p.3476)","FDG-PET - shows hypometabolism in posterior temporal-parietal cortex (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Amyloid PET ([11C]PIB, [18F]florbetapir, [18F]florbetaben, [18F]flutemetamol) - confirms neuritic/diffuse Abeta plaques (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Tau PET ([18F]flortaucipir and research tracers) - estimates density/distribution of neurofibrillary tangles (Harrison 22e p.3476-3477)","CSF biomarkers - decreased Abeta42 and Abeta42/Abeta40 ratio, increased p-Tau181/p-Tau217 (Harrison 22e p.3477)","Plasma biomarkers - decreased Abeta42/Abeta40 ratio, increased p-Tau181/p-Tau217/p-Tau231 (Harrison 22e p.3477)","EEG - usually normal or nonspecific slowing; prolonged EEG to seek intermittent nonconvulsive seizures (Harrison 22e p.3476)","ApoE genotyping - recommended before initiating anti-Abeta antibody therapy to inform ARIA risk (Harrison 22e p.3480-3481)","Systemic laboratory tests to exclude reversible dementia mimics (Harrison 22e p.3476)"],"redFlags":["Rapid progression over weeks to months with rigidity and myoclonus suggests CJD rather than AD (Harrison 22e p.3476, p.3482)","Early prominent gait disturbance with mild memory loss suggests vascular dementia or normal-pressure hydrocephalus (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Early focal seizure suggests a metastatic or primary brain neoplasm (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Loss of joint position/vibration sense with Babinski signs suggests vitamin B12 deficiency (Harrison 22e p.3476)","Stroke-like symptoms in a patient on anti-Abeta monoclonal antibody require immediate MRI to differentiate acute stroke from ARIA before any thrombolysis (Harrison 22e p.3482)","Seizures (focal or generalized) can herald AD and occur even before dementia onset, especially in younger patients and autosomal dominant mutation carriers (Harrison 22e p.3476)"],"prognosis":"Typical duration of symptomatic AD is 8-10 years, with a course ranging from 1 to 25 years. Some patients decline steadily while others have prolonged plateaus. Late stages bring rigidity, mutism, incontinence, and a bedridden state with dependence for eating, dressing, and toileting; hyperactive tendon reflexes and myoclonic jerks may occur. Death most commonly results from aspiration, and also from malnutrition, secondary infections, pulmonary emboli, or heart disease. Approved disease-modifying antiamyloid antibodies slow but do not halt clinical decline (~22-27% slowing over the trial periods). (Harrison 22e p.3476, p.3480-3481)","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude reversible/treatable mimics early in the course - vitamin B12 deficiency, thyroid disease, normal-pressure hydrocephalus, and depression-related cognitive impairment should be excluded before attributing dementia to AD (Harrison 22e p.3476).","A clinical diagnosis of AD is confirmed at autopsy only ~70-80% of the time; misdiagnoses are usually LATE (with or without hippocampal sclerosis), PART, Lewy body disease, vascular pathology, or FTLD (Harrison 22e p.3476).","Amyloid PET positivity is not specific: ~25% of cognitively unimpaired older adults have positive scans, and a positive scan can reflect comorbid (incidental) AD pathology rather than the primary cause of impairment (Harrison 22e p.3476).","A completely negative tau PET does not rule out early neurofibrillary pathology (Braak stages I-III) (Harrison 22e p.3477).","Increased total tau in CSF is nonspecific - seen in AD but also other neurodegeneration (Harrison 22e p.3477).","Plasma Abeta and p-Tau concentrations can be altered by comorbidities (reduced creatinine clearance, elevated BMI), causing false-positive or false-negative results near the threshold (Harrison 22e p.3477).","ApoE epsilon4 is neither necessary nor sufficient to cause AD - many AD patients lack epsilon4 and many epsilon4 carriers never develop dementia (Harrison 22e p.3479-3480).","Estrogen, statins, vitamin E, ginkgo biloba, and NSAIDs appeared protective in observational studies but did NOT show efficacy in randomized controlled trials; consumer 'memory-enhancing' supplements lack evidence (Harrison 22e p.3478, p.3482).","Antipsychotics carry an FDA black box warning in elderly dementia patients (increased risk of death) and should be used with caution for neuropsychiatric symptoms (Harrison 22e p.3482)."],"severityClassification":"Clinical staging is performed independent of biomarkers, based on functional impairment along a continuum: preclinical AD (biomarker evidence of amyloid pathology without symptoms) -> subjective cognitive decline -> mild cognitive impairment / early symptomatic AD (functionally compensated) -> dementia stages (early/middle/late/end-stage). Biological staging under the revised Alzheimer's Association criteria uses core 1 biomarkers (amyloid PET, CSF/plasma Abeta and p-Tau) for diagnosis and tau PET (core 2) for biomarker-based disease staging. (Harrison 22e p.3476-3478)","references":["Harrison 22e p.3475-3483","Jack CR et al: Revised criteria for the diagnosis and staging of Alzheimer's disease. Nat Med 30:2121, 2024","Van Dyck CH et al: Lecanemab in early Alzheimer's disease. N Engl J Med 388:9, 2023","Sims JR et al: Donanemab in early symptomatic Alzheimer disease: The TRAILBLAZER-ALZ 2 randomized clinical trial. JAMA 330:512, 2023","Belloy ME et al: A quarter century of APOE and Alzheimer's disease. Neuron 101:820, 2019","Andrews SJ et al: Interpretation of risk loci from genome-wide association studies of Alzheimer's disease. Lancet Neurol 19:326, 2020"],"crossLinks":[]},"amblyopia":{"name":"Amblyopia","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lazy eye","strabismic amblyopia","anisometropic amblyopia","deprivation amblyopia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Amblyopia is reduced best-corrected visual acuity in one or both eyes due to abnormal visual development, in the absence of a structural cause that fully explains the deficit","The main types are strabismic (from ocular misalignment), anisometropic or refractive (from unequal or high refractive error), and stimulus deprivation (from media opacity such as congenital cataract or ptosis)","It develops only during the sensitive period of visual development in early childhood, which is why early detection through vision screening is critical","It is usually unilateral but can be bilateral, for example with high symmetrical refractive error or bilateral media opacity","A crowding phenomenon is characteristic, with worse acuity for lines of letters than for single optotypes","Deprivation amblyopia from causes such as congenital cataract is the most severe and least responsive type and needs urgent treatment","Management is to correct any refractive error and to treat the underlying cause, then to encourage use of the amblyopic eye with occlusion (patching) or pharmacological penalisation of the better eye","Treatment is most effective when started young; response diminishes with increasing age although some improvement can still occur in older children"],"pathophysiology":"Amblyopia results from abnormal visual experience during the critical period of cortical visual development. When the two eyes receive unequal or degraded input (from misalignment, unequal refractive error, or media opacity), competitive cortical mechanisms favour the better eye and suppress the input from the affected eye. This leads to persistent cortical under-development of that eye's pathway and reduced acuity that is not correctable by optical means alone once the sensitive period has passed, though it remains treatable if addressed early.","additionalDifferentials":["Uncorrected refractive error without amblyopia","Structural ocular disease (e.g. optic nerve or macular pathology) causing reduced vision","Retinoblastoma or other intraocular tumour presenting with poor vision","Cortical visual impairment","Functional (non-organic) visual loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Age-appropriate best-corrected visual acuity testing, ideally with crowded (linear) optotypes","Cycloplegic refraction to detect anisometropia or significant refractive error","Cover testing and motility assessment to detect associated strabismus","Dilated fundus and media examination to exclude a structural or deprivational cause"],"redFlags":["An identifiable structural lesion (e.g. cataract, retinoblastoma, optic nerve disease) that could account for reduced vision and must not be attributed to amblyopia alone","Deprivation amblyopia from congenital cataract or dense media opacity requiring urgent intervention","Failure of vision to improve with adequate treatment, prompting re-evaluation for organic pathology","Poor vision persisting beyond the sensitive period with no prior treatment"],"prognosis":"The prognosis depends on the type of amblyopia and the age at which treatment begins; anisometropic and strabismic amblyopia treated early in childhood often recover good acuity, whereas deprivation amblyopia and late-treated cases have a more guarded outcome. Untreated amblyopia results in lifelong reduced vision in the affected eye and vulnerability if the better eye is later lost.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing amblyopia without excluding a structural cause of visual loss","Delaying treatment beyond the sensitive period when response is greatly reduced","Failing to prescribe full refractive correction before or alongside occlusion therapy","Poor compliance with patching leading to treatment failure or reverse amblyopia in the occluded eye"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"amoebiasis":{"name":"Amoebiasis (Intestinal)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Entamoeba histolytica infection","amebic dysentery"],"chapter":230,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1753","clinicalPearls":["Amebiasis is infection with Entamoeba histolytica, an intestinal protozoan; ~90% of infections are asymptomatic cyst passage and only ~10% are invasive (p.1753).","Symptomatic amebic colitis develops 2-6 weeks after ingestion of infectious cysts, with gradual-onset lower abdominal pain and mild diarrhea progressing to malaise, weight loss, and dysentery (p.1755).","In full-blown dysentery patients may pass 10-12 stools per day consisting mainly of blood and mucus with little fecal material; virtually all have heme-positive stools (p.1755).","In contrast to bacterial diarrhea, fewer than 40% of patients with amebic dysentery are febrile and stools show a paucity of neutrophils (p.1755).","Cecal involvement may mimic acute appendicitis (p.1755).","Definitive diagnosis of amebic colitis requires demonstration of hematophagous (erythrocyte-ingesting) trophozoites of E. histolytica; this is the only way microscopy can identify the species (p.1756).","Because trophozoites are rapidly killed by water, drying, or barium, at least three fresh stool specimens should be examined; wet mounts, iodine-stained concentrates, and trichrome-stained preparations confirm diagnosis in 75-95% of cases (p.1756).","Stool antigen detection (Gal/GalNAc lectin EIA, sensitivity 87%, specificity >90%) and multiplex PCR (sensitivity/specificity approaching 100%) are more sensitive, specific, and rapid than microscopy (p.1757).","Serology (ELISA) is positive in >90% of cases of colitis, ameboma, or liver abscess; results usually revert to negative within 6-12 months, so positivity with a compatible syndrome suggests active disease (p.1756).","E. histolytica infection is widespread in the Indian subcontinent, Africa, parts of East Asia, and Central/South America, often affecting children under 5 in areas of poor sanitation; in developed countries returning travelers, immigrants, MSM, military personnel, and institutional inmates are the main at-risk groups (p.1753).","Invasive amebiasis does not elicit eosinophilia (p.1757)."],"pathophysiology":"E. histolytica is acquired by ingesting viable cysts from fecally contaminated water, food, or hands (food-borne exposure being most common). Motile trophozoites excyst in the small intestine; in most patients they remain as harmless commensals in the large bowel and re-encyst, shedding infectious cysts in stool. In some patients trophozoites invade the colonic mucosa (causing colitis) or enter the portal bloodstream (causing distant abscesses, most often hepatic). Trophozoites attach to colonic mucus and epithelium via the Gal/GalNAc adherence lectin and release glycosidases, proteases, and cysteine proteinases that degrade mucus, collagen, elastin, immunoglobulins, and complement anaphylatoxins; mucosal damage follows via contact-dependent cytotoxicity (apoptosis, trogocytosis, lysis of inflammatory and epithelial cells) and contact-independent mechanisms (inflammatory mediators such as prostaglandin E2). Resistance to reactive oxygen/nitrogen species and to complement-mediated lysis are virulence factors. The earliest lesions are mucosal microulcerations of the cecum, sigmoid, or rectum; submucosal extension beneath viable surface mucosa produces the classic 'flask-shaped' ulcer with trophozoites at the margins. Human infection is notable for a paucity of inflammatory cells (in part because trophozoites kill neutrophils) (p.1753-1755).","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial dysentery / inflammatory diarrhea (Campylobacter, enteroinvasive E. coli, Shigella, Salmonella, Vibrio)","Inflammatory bowel disease (ulcerative colitis, Crohn disease)","Carcinoma of the colon (especially when an ameboma mass is present)","Acute appendicitis (with cecal involvement)"],"infectionMimics":["Shigellosis (bacillary dysentery)","Campylobacter colitis","Enteroinvasive Escherichia coli infection","Salmonella enterocolitis","Vibrio enteritis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inflammatory bowel disease (ulcerative colitis / Crohn disease)","Colonic carcinoma (mimicked by ameboma on barium studies)","Acute appendicitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Examination of at least three fresh stool specimens (wet mount, iodine-stained concentrate, trichrome-stained preparation) for cysts/trophozoites","Stool test for occult blood (heme) and microscopy for neutrophils","Stool antigen detection assay (monoclonal anti-Gal/GalNAc lectin enzyme immunoassay)","Stool multiplex PCR / nucleic acid amplification for GI protozoa","Serology (ELISA / indirect hemagglutination) for invasive amebiasis","Sigmoidoscopy with biopsy of the edge of ulcers if stool studies negative (avoid in fulminant colitis)","Biopsy of a colonic mass to confirm ameboma and exclude carcinoma","Stool culture for bacterial pathogens to exclude bacterial dysentery"],"redFlags":["Fulminant intestinal infection with severe abdominal pain, high fever, and profuse diarrhea (rare; predominantly in children)","Toxic megacolon with severe bowel dilation and intramural air","Full-thickness necrosis and bowel perforation","Patients receiving glucocorticoids are at risk for severe amebiasis"],"prognosis":"Outcome relates to the clinical syndrome: about 90% of infections are asymptomatic and self-limited, and uncomplicated amebic colitis responds well to therapy with ulcers characteristically healing with little or no scarring. Fulminant colitis, toxic megacolon, and perforation are rare but carry a poor prognosis. Treated colitis generally resolves, but severe disease (notably in those given glucocorticoids or in children with fulminant infection) markedly worsens outcome (p.1755-1756).","pitfalls":["E. histolytica cannot be distinguished microscopically from the non-pathogenic commensal E. dispar (nor from E. moshkovskii or E. bangladeshi); most asymptomatic carriers actually harbor E. dispar, which is incapable of invasive disease and does not warrant treatment, so species-specific antigen detection or PCR is needed (p.1754, p.1756).","Cysts must also be differentiated from non-pathogenic Entamoeba hartmanni, Entamoeba coli, and Endolimax nana, which do not cause disease (p.1756).","Amebiasis must be excluded before starting glucocorticoid therapy for suspected inflammatory bowel disease; unsuspected amebiasis treated with steroids can precipitate severe colitis or toxic megacolon (p.1755-1757).","An ameboma can be mistaken for cancer on barium studies; positive serology or biopsy can prevent unnecessary surgery (p.1755).","Sigmoidoscopy/biopsy and barium studies are dangerous during fulminant or acute colitis because of the risk of perforation (p.1756-1757).","Trophozoites are rapidly killed by water, drying, and barium and may be confused with neutrophils, so examination must use multiple fresh specimens with experienced microscopy (p.1756)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison describes a clinical spectrum rather than a formal grading scheme: (1) asymptomatic cyst passage (~90% of infections); (2) symptomatic amebic colitis/dysentery with bloody mucoid stools; and (3) rare fulminant intestinal infection (severe pain, high fever, profuse diarrhea, predominantly in children) that may progress to toxic megacolon, full-thickness necrosis, or perforation (p.1753, p.1755).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1753","Harrison 22e p.1754","Harrison 22e p.1755","Harrison 22e p.1756","Harrison 22e p.1757"],"crossLinks":["AMOEBIC_LIVER_ABSCESS"]},"amyloidosis":{"name":"Amyloidosis","system":"Hematology / Multisystem","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AL amyloidosis","ATTR amyloidosis","AA amyloidosis"],"chapter":117,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.895","clinicalPearls":["Amyloidosis is a group of protein-misfolding disorders defined by extracellular deposition of insoluble polymeric protein fibrils with a common antiparallel beta-pleated-sheet structure; nomenclature is AX, where A = amyloidosis and X = the fibril precursor protein (p.895).","AL amyloidosis (formerly primary systemic amyloidosis) is composed of monoclonal immunoglobulin light chains from a clonal B-cell/plasma-cell disorder and is the most common systemic type in North America; it can occur with myeloma, non-Hodgkin lymphoma, or Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia (p.895, p.897).","In AL amyloidosis the kidneys are involved in 60-70% (proteinuria, often nephrotic range, with hypoalbuminemia) and the heart in 70-80%; cardiac involvement is the leading cause of death (p.897).","Macroglossia and periorbital ('raccoon-eye') ecchymoses are pathognomonic signs of AL amyloidosis but each is seen in only about 10% of patients (p.897).","Easy bruising in AL amyloidosis is due to amyloid deposits in capillaries or to acquired factor X deficiency from binding of factor X to amyloid fibrils (p.897).","ATTR amyloidosis is the most prevalent hereditary amyloidosis and derives from wild-type or mutated transthyretin (TTR); it targets the peripheral and autonomic nerves and the heart (p.895, p.899).","The TTR variant V122I occurs in nearly 4% of African-American and Afro-Caribbean populations and causes late-onset cardiac amyloidosis; suspect it in African-American patients with concentric cardiac hypertrophy and diastolic heart failure, especially without hypertension or valvular disease (p.899).","Wild-type ATTR (ATTRwt, formerly senile systemic amyloidosis) is a disease of aging, not inheritance; it predominates in men from the seventh decade and is found at autopsy in 25% of hearts of men >=80 years, and is now the most prevalent and fastest-growing form of amyloidosis worldwide (p.899).","Carpal tunnel syndrome, tendinopathy, and spinal stenosis (soft-tissue disease) can precede ATTR nerve or heart disease by one to two decades, and ATTRwt patients frequently report bicipital, patellar, or Achilles tendon rupture (p.899).","AA amyloidosis arises from the acute-phase reactant serum amyloid A (SAA) in chronic inflammatory or infectious disease and typically begins in the kidneys; cardiomyopathy is a late manifestation in about 10-25% (p.895, p.899).","Abdominal subcutaneous fat aspiration is the most accessible diagnostic tissue, positive in >80% of systemic amyloidosis, avoiding surgical biopsy (p.895).","Amyloid deposits show characteristic apple-green birefringence under polarized light when stained with Congo red, whereas other regular protein structures such as collagen appear white (p.895)."],"pathophysiology":"Genetic mutation, abnormal processing, or aging-related decline in protein quality control causes precursor proteins to undergo reversible unfolding/misfolding. Misfolded subunits adopt a common antiparallel beta-pleated-sheet conformation and assemble into oligomers, higher-order polymers, and ultimately 10-nm fibrils that deposit extracellularly in tissues/organs. Soluble oligomeric intermediates are thought to be the most toxic species (generating reactive oxygen species and stress signaling), while fibrillar deposits mechanically interfere with organ function. Fibrils co-deposit accessory molecules including serum amyloid P component, apolipoproteins E and A-IV, glycosaminoglycans, and metal ions. Precursors include monoclonal immunoglobulin light chains (AL; lambda subtypes predominate), transthyretin (ATTR; wild-type or >130 described mutations), and the hepatic acute-phase protein serum amyloid A (AA) (p.895, p.897, p.899).","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple myeloma and other plasma cell dyscrasias (AL arises from the same clonal disorder)","Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS) and smoldering myeloma","Hypertensive or hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (vs amyloid cardiomyopathy)","Other causes of nephrotic syndrome / proteinuric renal disease","Other causes of length-dependent peripheral and autonomic neuropathy"],"infectionMimics":["AA amyloidosis is driven by chronic infections such as tuberculosis, osteomyelitis, or subacute bacterial endocarditis, which may be the presenting or underlying illness"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hypertensive concentric left ventricular hypertrophy mimicking amyloid cardiomyopathy","Hereditary (AF) amyloidoses from variant fibrinogen, lysozyme, gelsolin, or apolipoproteins can clinically mimic AL disease","Idiopathic or other axonal/autonomic neuropathies mimicking ATTR neuropathy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Congo red staining of aspirated abdominal subcutaneous fat (positive in >80%); if negative, biopsy of involved organ (kidney, heart, liver, tongue, GI tract)","Amyloid typing: laser-capture microdissection with tandem mass spectrometry is the diagnostic standard (sensitivity ~88%, specificity ~96%); immunoelectron microscopy more reliable than immunohistochemistry","Serum/urine immunofixation electrophoresis (SIFE/UIFE) and nephelometric serum free light chain assay with kappa/lambda ratio (>90% of AL patients have a detectable monoclonal light chain)","Bone marrow examination for clonal plasma cells (kappa/lambda clonality by flow cytometry, immunohistochemistry, or in situ hybridization)","Cardiac biomarkers NT-proBNP/BNP and troponin for staging, prognosis, and disease monitoring; ECG (low voltage, pseudo-infarct pattern) and echocardiography (concentric thickening, diastolic dysfunction, abnormal global longitudinal strain)","Cardiac MRI showing increased wall thickness and characteristic delayed subendocardial gadolinium enhancement","DNA/gene sequencing for hereditary (TTR and other AF) mutations","CT imaging plus serologic/microbiologic studies in AA disease to identify underlying tumor, inflammation, or infection","Electron microscopy of paraformaldehyde-fixed tissue to visualize 10-nm fibrils"],"redFlags":["Macroglossia or periorbital ('raccoon-eye') ecchymoses (pathognomonic of AL)","Concentric ventricular hypertrophy with diastolic dysfunction plus low-voltage ECG (voltage-mass mismatch)","Easy bruising or bleeding diathesis from acquired factor X deficiency","Multisystem involvement (unexplained nephropathy, cardiomyopathy, neuropathy, enteropathy) in a patient with fatigue/weight loss","Serum albumin <2 g/dL with pedal edema or anasarca from nephrotic-range proteinuria","Atrial contractile dysfunction even in sinus rhythm, with risk of atrial thrombi and thromboembolism"],"prognosis":"AL amyloidosis is often rapidly progressive; historically untreated median survival was only ~1-2 years, but outcomes have improved markedly over four decades with newer plasma-cell-directed therapy. Cardiac stage (Mayo system based on NT-proBNP and troponin) is the strongest determinant of management and survival; very low dFLC (<50 mg/L) predicts better outcome regardless of cardiac stage. High-risk patients (~15-20%) with advanced cardiac stage IIIb or NYHA III-IV heart failure are largely excluded from trials. ATTR cardiac disease is better tolerated than AL: untreated median survival from heart failure presentation is ~42-48 months for ATTR versus ~6 months for AL. Untreated ATTR survival after onset ranges 4-15 years depending on predominant cardiac versus neurologic involvement (p.897, p.899).","pitfalls":["Amyloid typing pitfall: commercial immunohistochemistry antibodies may be inaccurate and some AL deposits bind light-chain antibodies nonspecifically; accurate typing (mass spectrometry / immunoelectron microscopy / genetic testing) is essential because therapy differs by type (p.895, p.899).","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis are NOT useful screening tests for AL because the clonal light chain is often insufficient to produce a detectable M-spike; immunofixation and free light chain assays are required (p.897).","A monoclonal protein alone is not diagnostic of amyloidosis since MGUS is common in older patients; conversely, AF carriers may have a coincidental monoclonal gammopathy, so screen for BOTH plasma-cell disorder and protein mutations (p.897, p.899).","Renal insufficiency can falsely elevate cardiac biomarkers (NT-proBNP, troponin) and nonspecifically raise both free light chain isotypes, so the free light chain ratio (not just absolute level) must be examined (p.896, p.897).","In amyloid cardiomyopathy digitalis, calcium channel blockers, and beta blockers are relatively contraindicated because they interact with amyloid fibrils and can cause heart block and worsening heart failure (p.899).","Interstitial (rather than glomerular) renal amyloid can cause azotemia WITHOUT proteinuria (p.897).","Apparent sporadic ATTRv (no family history) often reflects incomplete penetrance rather than a true spontaneous case (p.899).","Mass spectrometry detects amino-acid sequence variation but does not definitively identify specific protein variants; DNA sequencing is the standard for hereditary (AF) mutations (p.899)."],"severityClassification":"AL amyloidosis is staged primarily by the Mayo 2004 cardiac staging system (NT-proBNP and cardiac troponins), modified by European investigators to identify very-high-risk patients; the Mayo 2012 revision adds clonal burden via dFLC (difference between involved and uninvolved free light chains). A Boston University system uses BNP and troponin I. A separate renal staging system based on 24-h urine protein and eGFR predicts progression to dialysis at 2 years. ATTR cardiac involvement is graded by stage (e.g., advanced stage IIIb) and NYHA functional class (p.897).","references":["Harrison 22e p.895"],"crossLinks":["myeloma","nephrotic","heart_failure"]},"amyloidosis_and_other_restrictive_cardiomy":{"name":"Amyloidosis and Other Restrictive Cardiomyopathies","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Cardiac amyloidosis","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","RCM","AL amyloid cardiomyopathy","Transthyretin cardiac amyloidosis","ATTR cardiomyopathy","ATTRwt","ATTRv"],"chapter":270,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2020","clinicalPearls":["Restrictive cardiomyopathy is dominated by abnormal diastolic function in a noncompliant ventricle with usually only mildly reduced contractility; ejection fraction is often 30-50% and both atria are typically enlarged, sometimes massively (Harrison 22e p.2020).","Despite biventricular elevation of filling pressures, restrictive disease and cardiac amyloidosis tend to present with predominantly right-sided symptoms such as peripheral edema, abdominal discomfort, and ascites (Harrison 22e p.2020-2021).","In sinus rhythm a fourth heart sound is more typical than a third in RCM generally, but in cardiac amyloidosis both an S3 and an S4 are unusual because restrictive physiology impairs relaxation and the infiltrated atrium generates little contractile pressure; jugular venous pressure is elevated and may rise paradoxically on inspiration (positive Kussmaul sign) (Harrison 22e p.2020-2021).","The two precursor proteins causing most cardiac amyloidosis are transthyretin (TTR, produced in the liver) and light chains (produced by abnormal plasma cells); precise typing of the precursor is essential because therapy is type-specific and incorrect typing leads to inappropriate treatment (Harrison 22e p.2020).","Periorbital purpura is virtually pathognomonic of AL amyloidosis (occurring in about 20-30% of cases) and macroglossia occurs in roughly 10%; an unusually hard liver may reflect amyloid hepatic infiltration, which occurs in AL but not TTR amyloidosis (Harrison 22e p.2021).","A history of bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome, spinal stenosis, or a ruptured biceps tendon — often 5-8 years before heart failure — points toward TTR amyloidosis, reflecting TTR's predilection for ligaments and tendons (Harrison 22e p.2021-2022).","Longitudinal strain imaging classically shows relatively preserved apical strain with severely impaired basal strain, producing a 'bull's-eye' color pattern highly suggestive of cardiac amyloidosis (Harrison 22e p.2022-2023).","On cardiac MRI, abnormal nulling of the myocardium with poor myocardium-to-cavity distinction plus extensive transmural late gadolinium enhancement and markedly increased extracellular volume are near-pathognomonic of amyloid infiltration (Harrison 22e p.2022-2024).","Bone-avid tracers (technetium pyrophosphate/PYP, DPD, HMDP) are normally not taken up by the heart but show avid myocardial uptake in TTR amyloidosis; a positive scan with a negative plasma cell dyscrasia workup confirms TTR amyloidosis without biopsy (Harrison 22e p.2023-2026).","In the United States the most common variant causing TTR amyloid cardiomyopathy is Val122Ile, present in about 3.5% of people of African descent, presenting as a late-onset restrictive cardiomyopathy usually from the seventh decade with incomplete penetrance (Harrison 22e p.2022).","Wild-type TTR amyloidosis (ATTRwt) is now considered the most common form of amyloidosis, affecting predominantly older men (men roughly 20 times more likely than women); the heart is usually the sole clinically involved organ, though lung and gut deposits are often found histologically (Harrison 22e p.2022).","Atrial arrhythmia, especially atrial fibrillation or flutter, is a common presenting feature (particularly in TTR amyloidosis) and its onset often precipitates or worsens heart failure; refractory atrial arrhythmia in a patient in the mid-60s or older without other cause should raise suspicion for TTR cardiac amyloidosis (Harrison 22e p.2021-2027)."],"pathophysiology":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy results from a noncompliant ventricle with markedly impaired diastolic filling; most cases are 'infiltrative,' due to deposition of abnormal substances between myocytes (amyloid being the most common), with additional causes from intramyocyte storage of metabolic products (e.g., iron in hemochromatosis, Fabry disease, glycogen storage disease) or acquired fibrosis (radiation, scleroderma, endomyocardial/hypereosinophilic injury, carcinoid). In amyloidosis, fibrillar proteinaceous material is deposited extracellularly; amyloid stains with Congo red and shows apple-green birefringence under polarized light, and electron microscopy reveals nonbranching fibrils about 10 nm in diameter. Extracellular amyloid deposition expands the extracellular space, increasing heart mass and ventricular wall thickness while reducing compliance; the left ventricular cavity may be relatively small (restrictive physiology) and both atria dilate from chronically elevated biventricular filling pressures. AL amyloidosis is a plasma cell disorder closely related to multiple myeloma in which excess abnormal free light chains (more often lambda than kappa) deposit in multiple organs, with heart and kidney most commonly affected. TTR amyloidosis arises from liver-produced transthyretin, either wild-type (ATTRwt) or from a destabilizing genetic variant (ATTRv); variant TTR is generally considered less stable than wild-type. Both wild-type and variant TTR cardiomyopathy have a subclinical phase of probably several years, and by the time heart failure occurs 30-50% of the involved heart weight may be amyloid (Harrison 22e p.2020-2022).","additionalDifferentials":["Constrictive pericardial disease (the classic and most important differential, sharing dominant right-sided failure, edema, ascites, elevated JVP and Kussmaul sign; echocardiography readily distinguishes the two today) (Harrison 22e p.2020-2022)","Hypertensive heart disease (usually shows LVH on ECG and requires years of severe uncontrolled hypertension to reach amyloid-degree wall thickening) (Harrison 22e p.2021)","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (rarely presents with peripheral edema and usually has LVH on ECG; distinction from amyloidosis is usually fairly clear) (Harrison 22e p.2021)","Fabry disease and other infiltrative cardiomyopathies (may have typical skin manifestations and usually LVH on ECG) (Harrison 22e p.2021-2022)","Mitochondrial cardiomyopathy (echocardiographic appearance can mimic amyloidosis but with distinct noncardiac features such as maternally inherited diabetes, deafness, and recurrent strokes) (Harrison 22e p.2022)","Advanced diabetic heart disease with heavy proteinuria (obvious diabetes history and echocardiogram lacking amyloid features) (Harrison 22e p.2022)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Constrictive pericarditis","Hypertensive heart disease with left ventricular hypertrophy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (including sarcomere-variant forme fruste presenting as RCM)","Fabry disease and other infiltrative or storage cardiomyopathies","Mitochondrial cardiomyopathy","Hemochromatosis (iron storage)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography showing increased LV wall thickness without dilation, frequent biatrial enlargement, increased RV free-wall thickness, restrictive diastolic filling pattern, and a small transmitral A wave (the latter associated with increased thromboembolic risk) (Harrison 22e p.2022-2023)","Longitudinal strain imaging demonstrating apical-sparing 'bull's-eye' pattern (Harrison 22e p.2022-2023)","Cardiac magnetic resonance with extracellular volume measurement, abnormal myocardial nulling, and extensive late gadolinium enhancement (Harrison 22e p.2023-2024)","Bone-avid radionuclide imaging (technetium PYP, DPD, or HMDP) with SPECT and ideally cardiac CT to confirm myocardial rather than blood-pool uptake (Harrison 22e p.2023-2026)","Serum free light chains plus serum and urine protein electrophoresis and immunofixation to detect or exclude a plasma cell dyscrasia (immunofixation is more sensitive than electrophoresis for the low paraprotein levels of AL amyloidosis) (Harrison 22e p.2023-2026)","Tissue biopsy (subcutaneous fat pad aspirate or deep-skin biopsy first; endomyocardial biopsy if needed, positive in well over 90% of cardiac amyloid cases) with amyloid typing ideally by mass spectrometry (Harrison 22e p.2024-2026)","TTR gene sequencing to distinguish familial (variant) from wild-type TTR when AL is not clearly established (Harrison 22e p.2026)","NT-proBNP (often disproportionately high for the degree of heart failure, frequently 1000-2000 pg/mL even with mild symptoms) and high-sensitivity troponin (frequently mildly elevated, risking misdiagnosis as ischemic disease) (Harrison 22e p.2023-2024)","ECG, which may show abnormally low voltage (particularly in AL) though not invariably (Harrison 22e p.2022)"],"redFlags":["Heart failure with a thick left ventricle but without a history of severe hypertension and without LVH on ECG","Disproportionately elevated NT-proBNP relative to clinical heart failure severity","Periorbital purpura or macroglossia (suggesting AL amyloidosis)","Refractory atrial fibrillation/flutter in a patient in the mid-60s or older without other cause (suggesting TTR amyloidosis)","Bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome, spinal stenosis, or ruptured biceps tendon preceding heart failure (suggesting TTR amyloidosis)","Abnormal postural blood-pressure drop indicating autonomic neuropathy (AL or some variant TTR forms)","Unexplained nonpostural dizziness or syncope with bifascicular or left bundle branch block in TTR amyloidosis, indicating risk of high-degree atrioventricular block","Significant proteinuria with hypoalbuminemia (renal AL involvement)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends heavily on amyloid type. AL cardiac amyloidosis is aggressive and, with cardiac involvement, carries the worst survival, with median survival from onset of heart failure to death of about 6 months in untreated patients, underscoring the urgency of diagnosis. Among TTR forms, untreated Val122Ile variant disease tends to progress relatively rapidly with median survival of 2-3 years from onset of heart failure, whereas wild-type TTR amyloidosis has an untreated median survival of about 4-5 years, with death usually from progressive congestive heart failure rather than sudden death; wild-type disease occurring in women tends to present later and follow a more indolent course. There is a high risk of thromboembolism in cardiac amyloidosis, and sudden cardiac death may occur (more common in advanced AL disease). Overall, cardiac amyloidosis is now recognized as far more common than previously appreciated, and earlier diagnosis with the advent of disease-modifying therapy has improved outlook (Harrison 22e p.2021-2028).","pitfalls":["Mildly elevated high-sensitivity troponin may lead to misdiagnosis as ischemic heart disease (Harrison 22e p.2023-2024).","AL amyloidosis can occasionally show myocardial uptake on bone-avid tracer scans, so a positive nuclear scan must never be interpreted as diagnostic of TTR without first excluding a plasma cell dyscrasia (Harrison 22e p.2023-2026).","Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS) is common in older patients and not uncommonly coexists with TTR amyloidosis, so abnormal plasma cell dyscrasia testing does not automatically equal AL amyloidosis and may require hematology input (Harrison 22e p.2023-2026).","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis may miss the low-level paraprotein of AL amyloidosis; immunofixation is more sensitive and should be performed (Harrison 22e p.2024).","The sedimentation rate is usually normal or only minimally elevated in AL amyloidosis, unlike the marked elevation in multiple myeloma, so a normal ESR does not exclude AL amyloidosis (Harrison 22e p.2024).","Immunohistochemical typing of amyloid is fraught with diagnostic uncertainty and can be misleading; typing is ideally done by mass spectrometry (Harrison 22e p.2024-2026).","There is no definitive noninvasive test for AL cardiac amyloidosis, so a tissue biopsy is always required to confirm it (Harrison 22e p.2024).","Rare variant TTR forms can have negative bone-tracer imaging, so a negative scan does not entirely exclude amyloidosis when clinical suspicion remains high (Harrison 22e p.2026)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a single formal staging scale within this chapter; severity is gauged clinically and prognostically by amyloid type and stage of cardiac involvement. Adverse markers include disproportionately elevated NT-proBNP (often 1000-2000 pg/mL even with mild heart failure) and elevated high-sensitivity troponin, with the proportion of heart weight composed of amyloid reaching 30-50% by the time heart failure occurs in TTR disease. AL cardiac involvement confers the worst prognosis among the types (Harrison 22e p.2022-2024).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2020","Harrison 22e p.2021","Harrison 22e p.2022","Harrison 22e p.2023","Harrison 22e p.2024","Harrison 22e p.2026","Harrison 22e p.2027","Harrison 22e p.2028"],"crossLinks":[]},"amyotrophic_lateral_sclerosis":{"name":"Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ALS","motor neuron disease","Lou Gehrig disease"],"chapter":448,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3519-3524","clinicalPearls":["ALS is the most common progressive motor neuron disease and ultimately causes progressive loss of BOTH upper (corticospinal) and lower motor neurons; in the absence of clear involvement of both motor neuron types, the diagnosis of ALS is questionable (p.3519-3520).","Lower motor neuron features: insidious asymmetric weakness usually first distal in one limb, progressive wasting/atrophy, and fasciculations (spontaneous twitching of motor units); in the hands a preponderance of extensor over flexor weakness is common (p.3521).","Upper (corticospinal) motor neuron features: hyperactive muscle-stretch reflexes (tendon jerks), spastic resistance to passive movement, and muscle stiffness often out of proportion to weakness (p.3521).","Bulbar onset presents with difficulty chewing, swallowing, and moving the face and tongue; corticobulbar degeneration causes dysarthria and pseudobulbar affect (involuntary excess weeping or laughing) (p.3521).","World Federation of Neurology criteria: simultaneous upper AND lower motor neuron involvement with progressive weakness and exclusion of all alternative diagnoses; 'definite' when 3-4 regions involved (bulbar, cervical, thoracic, lumbosacral), 'probable' when 2, 'possible' when 1 (p.3521).","Elevated CSF/serum neurofilament light chains (Nfl) or phosphorylated neurofilament heavy chains are markers of neurodegeneration in most cases; higher levels correlate with more aggressive disease and more rapid progression (p.3521).","About 10% of cases are inherited as an autosomal dominant trait (familial ALS); FALS is clinically indistinguishable from sporadic ALS apart from its inheritance. C9orf72 is the most common genetic cause (~45-50% of FALS), followed by SOD1 (~20%) (p.3521).","ALS may arise concurrently with frontotemporal dementia (particularly in familial cases), with early behavioral abnormalities reflecting frontal lobe dysfunction (p.3520-3521)."],"pathophysiology":"Motor neuron degenerative disorder marked by death of lower motor neurons (anterior horn cells and their brainstem bulbar homologues) and upper/corticospinal motor neurons (layer-five motor cortex neurons descending via the pyramidal tract). Affected neurons shrink and accumulate lipofuscin; the motor neuron cytoskeleton is affected early, with proximal axonal 'spheroids' composed of neurofilament accumulations. Both sporadic and familial ALS show ubiquitin-positive aggregates frequently associated with the protein TDP43, plus astroglial/microglial proliferation (neuroinflammation). Loss of cortical motor neurons thins the corticospinal tracts; lateral-column fiber loss and fibrillary gliosis impart firmness (lateral sclerosis), while muscle denervation and atrophy give rise to the term amyotrophy. Neuronal cell death is strikingly selective: the sensory apparatus, cerebellar coordination structures, ocular-motility neurons, and the sacral parasympathetic sphincter neurons (nucleus of Onuf) are characteristically spared. Familial ALS most commonly involves mutations in C9orf72, SOD1, TDP43, and FUS/TLS (p.3519-3521).","additionalDifferentials":["Cervical spondylosis / compression of the cervical spinal cord or cervicomedullary junction (tumors, foramen magnum lesions, osteophytes)","Multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block (MMCB)","Diffuse lower motor axonal neuropathy associated with hematopoietic disorders (lymphoma, multiple myeloma) with serum M-component","X-linked spinobulbar muscular atrophy (Kennedy's disease)","Adult Tay-Sachs disease (hexosaminidase A deficiency)","Spinal muscular atrophy","Primary lateral sclerosis (upper motor neuron only)","Progressive muscular atrophy (lower motor neuron only)"],"infectionMimics":["Poliomyelitis","Herpes zoster","Coxsackie virus","West Nile virus","Retroviral myelopathy (HTLV-1)","Lyme disease","Tetanus"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cervical spondylotic myelopathy / structural compressive lesions (parasagittal or foramen magnum tumors, Chiari malformation/syrinx, spinal AVM)","Multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block (treatable - responds to IVIG or chemotherapy)","Plasma cell dyscrasias and motor neuropathy with paraproteinemia or cancer (paraneoplastic)","Metabolic: hyperparathyroidism, hyperthyroidism, hypoglycemia; deficiency of folate, vitamin B12, vitamin E, copper or zinc","Heavy metal / toxin intoxication (lead, aluminum)","Kennedy's disease (spinobulbar muscular atrophy)"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of head including foramen magnum and cervical spine to exclude compressive structural lesions","Electrophysiologic testing (EMG/nerve conduction) to demonstrate denervation and to detect motor conduction block (suggests MMCB rather than ALS)","CSF examination and culture","Serum/CSF neurofilament light chains (Nfl) - prognostic marker of disease aggressiveness","Complete blood count, sedimentation rate, total protein, anti-GM1 antibodies (should be obtained in all cases)","Anti-Hu antibody (paraneoplastic), serum protein for M-component, bone marrow biopsy if indicated","Fasting blood sugar, calcium, thyroid function, vitamin B12/E/folate, serum zinc and copper","WBC DNA for mutational analysis (C9orf72, SOD1, TDP43, FUS/TLS) in suspected familial cases","Contrast myelography to visualize the cervical cord where doubt exists"],"redFlags":["Respiratory involvement: weakness of the muscles of respiration may rarely cause death before the disease is advanced elsewhere; the illness is relentlessly progressive, leading to death from respiratory paralysis - respiratory failure determines prognosis.","Bulbar dysfunction (difficulty chewing/swallowing) risks aspiration pneumonia and compromised nutrition/hydration.","Atypical features warranting search for a remediable mimic: restriction to only upper OR only lower motor neurons, involvement of non-motor neurons, or evidence of motor conduction block on electrophysiology."],"prognosis":"Relentlessly progressive; median survival is 3 to 5 years, with death typically from respiratory paralysis. Stabilization or regression is very rare. Higher serum/CSF neurofilament light chain levels correlate with more aggressive disease and more rapid progression. No treatment arrests the underlying pathologic process (p.3521, 3524).","pitfalls":["Because ALS is currently untreatable, it is imperative to exclude potentially remediable mimics - especially cervical cord compression/cervical spondylotic myelopathy, which can produce weakness, wasting, fasciculations in the arms and leg spasticity that closely resembles ALS.","Multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block (MMCB) must be excluded: unlike ALS it lacks corticospinal signs, often has elevated anti-GM1 antibodies, and may respond dramatically to IVIG or chemotherapy.","Diagnosing ALS without clear involvement of BOTH upper and lower motor neurons is questionable - reconsider the diagnosis.","Features favoring ALS over a compressive mimic: absence of pain or sensory changes, normal bowel and bladder function, and normal spinal radiology and CSF. Sensory, sphincter, ocular-motility, and cognitive functions are preserved even late (except when concurrent frontotemporal dementia is present)."],"severityClassification":"World Federation of Neurology diagnostic categories based on number of body regions with combined upper and lower motor neuron involvement (bulbar, cervical, thoracic, lumbosacral): 'definite' ALS = 3-4 regions; 'probable' = 2 regions; 'possible' = 1 region. Exception: progressive upper and lower motor neuron signs at one site plus a SOD1 mutation also qualifies (p.3521).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3519-3524"],"crossLinks":["cord_compression"]},"anal_fissure":{"name":"Anal fissure","system":"GI / Anorectal / Surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["fissure-in-ano","anal fissure","chronic anal fissure"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with severe, sharp anal pain during and often for a prolonged period after defaecation, frequently with a small amount of bright red blood on the paper","Most fissures occur in the posterior midline; anterior fissures are more common in women, and fissures away from the midline should raise suspicion of underlying disease","Pain and reflex internal sphincter spasm create a cycle of ischaemia that impairs healing and perpetuates the fissure","A chronic fissure shows a triad of a sentinel skin tag, exposed internal sphincter fibres in the base, and a hypertrophied anal papilla","Examination is often too painful for a digital rectal examination, which may need to be deferred or performed under anaesthesia","First-line management aims to break the spasm-ischaemia cycle with stool softening, fibre, and topical agents that relax the internal sphincter such as glyceryl trinitrate or a calcium channel blocker","Refractory fissures may be treated with botulinum toxin injection or lateral internal sphincterotomy, the latter carrying a small risk of incontinence","Multiple, lateral, or atypical fissures warrant investigation for Crohn's disease, tuberculosis, sexually transmitted infection, or malignancy"],"pathophysiology":"An anal fissure is a longitudinal tear in the anoderm of the distal anal canal, usually initiated by the passage of a hard or bulky stool. Resulting pain triggers spasm and hypertonia of the internal anal sphincter, which raises resting anal pressure and reduces blood flow to the relatively poorly perfused posterior midline. This local ischaemia impairs mucosal healing, converting an acute tear into a chronic non-healing ulcer. Persistent inflammation produces the sentinel tag, papilla hypertrophy, and exposure of sphincter fibres seen in chronic fissures.","additionalDifferentials":["Thrombosed external haemorrhoid or perianal haematoma","Anorectal abscess","Fistula-in-ano","Crohn's disease or ulcerative proctitis","Anal carcinoma","Sexually transmitted infection (herpes, syphilis)","Pruritus ani with excoriation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Gentle inspection of the anal verge, often with the patient positioned to allow the fissure to be seen","Examination under anaesthesia when pain precludes assessment or the diagnosis is unclear","Endoscopic evaluation and biopsy for atypical, multiple, or non-healing fissures","Investigations for inflammatory bowel disease or infection when clinically suspected"],"redFlags":["Atypical, lateral, or multiple fissures suggesting Crohn's disease, infection, or malignancy","A non-healing indurated ulcer that may represent anal carcinoma","Associated perianal sepsis, abscess, or fistula","Systemic features of inflammatory bowel disease or immunosuppression"],"prognosis":"Acute fissures commonly heal with conservative treatment and stool softening. A substantial proportion of chronic fissures heal with topical sphincter-relaxing therapy, and most of the remainder respond to botulinum toxin or sphincterotomy, though a small risk of minor incontinence follows surgery.","pitfalls":["Forcing a digital or instrumental examination through severe pain rather than deferring or examining under anaesthesia","Overlooking Crohn's disease or malignancy in atypical or non-healing fissures","Proceeding to sphincterotomy without considering continence risk, especially in women and older patients","Failing to address ongoing constipation, allowing recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"anemia_due_to_acute_blood":{"name":"Anemia Due to Acute Blood Loss","system":"Hematologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Acute posthemorrhagic anemia","APHA","Posthemorrhagic anemia"],"chapter":106,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.807-808","clinicalPearls":["In the first hours after a sudden large bleed the routine blood count typically does NOT reveal anemia, because hemoglobin concentration is initially unchanged; the dominant problem at this stage is hypovolemia, not a low hemoglobin (p.807).","Hemoglobin falls only gradually over several hours to days as the intravascular volume is restored — either by intravenous fluids given acutely, or by the body's own fluid shift from the extravascular into the intravascular compartment (hemodilution) — so the measured hemoglobin lags behind the true blood loss (p.807).","The degree of anemia eventually reflects the amount lost: as a rough guide, a hemoglobin around 7 g/dL after about 3 days implies roughly half of the total blood has been lost (p.807).","If bleeding stops, the marrow response (rising reticulocyte count and erythropoietin) gradually corrects the anemia; this reticulocyte rise mirrors the marrow response seen in hemolysis (p.807).","Acute blood loss and hemolysis are the two principal causes of anemia accompanied by an elevated reticulocyte count (p.807).","Signs of vascular instability appear once roughly 25% or more of total blood volume is lost; donating a unit of blood (~20% of volume) is usually only minimally symptomatic (p.807).","Bedside signs of concealed bleeding include the Grey Turner sign (flank ecchymosis, suggesting retroperitoneal hemorrhage), the Cullen sign (periumbilical ecchymosis, suggesting intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal bleeding), and dullness to chest percussion (suggesting intrapleural blood) (p.807).","The amount of blood lost is frequently overestimated when a scene appears bloody; careful repeated examination and directed investigations (e.g., ultrasound, endoscopy) are needed to localize occult bleeding (p.807).","Whenever hemoglobin drops abruptly, acute posthemorrhagic anemia should be suspected regardless of the history initially offered by the patient (p.807).","Compensation is partly achieved by a rightward shift of the oxygen–hemoglobin dissociation curve: with mild loss via the Bohr effect (lower pH / higher CO2), and in more chronic anemia via rising intracellular 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate, which aids oxygen unloading (p.807).","Surgery is an important iatrogenic cause of acute posthemorrhagic anemia; blood loss can be substantial (e.g., up to about 2 L with radical prostatectomy) and should be measured and monitored (p.807-808)."],"pathophysiology":"Blood loss produces anemia by two mechanisms: direct loss of red cells, and — if the bleeding is protracted — gradual depletion of iron stores leading to iron-deficiency anemia (the latter covered separately). Acute posthemorrhagic anemia follows the former. Hemorrhage may be external (trauma, obstetric) or internal (gastrointestinal tract, splenic rupture, ruptured ectopic pregnancy, subarachnoid hemorrhage, leaking aneurysm). After a sudden large loss there are three sequential clinical/pathophysiologic stages: (1) hypovolemia dominates and threatens high-flow organs (brain, kidneys), so impaired consciousness and acute renal failure are early dangers, while the blood count still appears normal because hemoglobin concentration is unchanged; (2) baroreceptor and stretch-receptor activation triggers vasopressin and other peptide release and a shift of fluid from the extravascular into the intravascular space, producing hemodilution that converts hypovolemia into measurable anemia proportional to the volume lost; (3) provided bleeding ceases, increased marrow erythropoiesis (reflected by elevated reticulocytes and erythropoietin) gradually ameliorates the anemia. Tissue oxygen delivery is defended by rightward shifts of the oxygen–hemoglobin dissociation curve (acute Bohr effect from lower pH/higher CO2; chronic rise in 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate) and by shunting blood away from organs with relatively rich supply (kidney, gut, skin) (p.807).","additionalDifferentials":["Hemolytic anemia (the other major cause of anemia with reticulocytosis) (p.807)","Iron-deficiency anemia from chronic/protracted blood loss (p.807)","Other hypoproliferative or production-failure anemias when reticulocyte count is not elevated (p.807)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hemolysis — also presents as anemia with an elevated reticulocyte count and a brisk marrow response, mimicking the recovery phase of acute blood loss (p.807)","Hypovolemic shock from non-hemorrhagic causes (dehydration, fluid sequestration) can reproduce the early hypovolemic picture before any fall in hemoglobin (p.807)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial hemoglobin/hematocrit measurements — recognizing that early values are misleadingly normal and fall over hours as volume is restored (p.807)","Reticulocyte count and erythropoietin level — rise during the marrow recovery phase (p.807)","Imaging or endoscopy (e.g., sonogram, endoscopy) to localize occult internal bleeding (p.807)","Targeted physical examination for signs of concealed hemorrhage (Grey Turner sign, Cullen sign, chest dullness) (p.807)"],"redFlags":["Loss of consciousness or acute renal failure early on, reflecting hypovolemic threat to brain and kidneys before any anemia is detectable (p.807)","Postural hypotension and tachycardia with a preference to remain supine, indicating sudden loss exceeding ~30% of blood volume that overwhelms sympathetic compensation (p.807)","Hypovolemic shock — confusion, dyspnea, diaphoresis, hypotension, tachycardia — indicating loss >40% (>~2 L in an average adult) and significant vital-organ hypoperfusion (p.807)","Tachycardia, tachypnea, narrowed pulse pressure, cold, pale, mottled skin and reduced urine output signaling sympathetic compensation for ongoing loss (p.807)"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the rapidity and volume of loss and on whether the source can be controlled. If bleeding is stopped, the marrow's compensatory rise in red cell production gradually corrects the anemia. The acute danger is not the anemia itself but hypovolemia and impaired organ perfusion: losses above ~30% of blood volume overwhelm normal compensatory mechanisms, and losses >40% (>~2 L in an average adult) produce hypovolemic shock with significant vital-organ perfusion deficits (p.807).","pitfalls":["Mistakenly excluding significant hemorrhage because an early blood count shows a normal hemoglobin — the fall is delayed until volume is restored (p.807).","Overestimating blood loss from the bloody appearance of a scene rather than from careful clinical assessment (p.807).","Failing to suspect occult internal bleeding after trauma, where even large losses may not be immediately obvious (p.807).","Focusing on the anemia number rather than on hypovolemia and organ perfusion, which are the true early threats in large acute losses (p.807)."],"severityClassification":"Severity tracks the fraction of total blood volume lost: ~20% (one unit) is usually minimally symptomatic; signs of vascular instability emerge at ~25% or more; sudden loss of >30% overwhelms sympathetic compensation and produces postural hypotension and tachycardia; loss of >40% (>~2 L in an average-sized adult) causes hypovolemic shock (confusion, dyspnea, diaphoresis, hypotension, tachycardia). Symptomatic chronic/progressive anemia from slower loss often becomes apparent only when hemoglobin falls below ~70-80 g/L (7-8 g/dL), and intrinsic compensation maintains normal tissue oxygen delivery only across about a 20-30 g/L (2-3 g/dL) hemoglobin deficit (p.807).","references":["Harrison 22e p.807-808"],"crossLinks":[]},"ankylosing_spondylitis":{"name":"Ankylosing spondylitis","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AS","radiographic axial spondyloarthritis","Bechterew disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically presents in young adults, more often men, with insidious chronic low back pain and stiffness that improves with activity and worsens with rest, especially in the second half of the night","Morning stiffness lasting more than 30 minutes and buttock pain from sacroiliitis are characteristic inflammatory back pain features","Progressive loss of spinal mobility can be assessed with the modified Schober test and chest expansion measurement","Enthesitis (notably at the Achilles tendon and plantar fascia) and asymmetric large-joint peripheral arthritis are common","Extra-articular manifestations include acute anterior uveitis, inflammatory bowel disease, and psoriasis","Strongly associated with HLA-B27, though a positive test alone is neither diagnostic nor required","Early sacroiliitis is often better seen on MRI (bone marrow oedema) before radiographic changes such as sclerosis, erosions, and eventual ankylosis appear","Management combines a regular exercise and physiotherapy programme with NSAIDs first-line, escalating to biologic therapy (TNF or IL-17 inhibitors) for inadequate control"],"pathophysiology":"Ankylosing spondylitis is a chronic immune-mediated inflammatory disease of the axial skeleton and the prototype of the seronegative spondyloarthritides. Inflammation centres on the entheses (sites where ligaments and tendons attach to bone) and the sacroiliac joints. A strong genetic association with HLA-B27 implicates aberrant antigen presentation and dysregulated IL-23/IL-17 pathway signalling. Chronic enthesitis and osteitis drive a cycle of erosion followed by reactive new bone formation, leading to syndesmophyte development, bony bridging across vertebrae, and ultimately spinal fusion (the 'bamboo spine').","additionalDifferentials":["Mechanical or degenerative low back pain","Psoriatic arthritis with axial involvement","Reactive arthritis","Arthritis associated with inflammatory bowel disease","Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis (DISH)","Infective sacroiliitis or vertebral osteomyelitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the sacroiliac joints to detect early active inflammation before radiographic change","Plain radiographs of the pelvis and spine for sacroiliitis, syndesmophytes, and ankylosis","HLA-B27 typing to support the diagnosis in the right clinical context","Inflammatory markers (CRP and ESR), which may be normal despite active disease","Assessment of extra-articular features including ophthalmology review for uveitis"],"redFlags":["Acute painful red eye suggesting anterior uveitis requiring urgent ophthalmology assessment","New or worsening neurological deficit, or acute severe pain after minor trauma, raising concern for spinal fracture through a rigid fused spine","Cauda equina syndrome from arachnoiditis","Symptoms of cardiac conduction block or aortic regurgitation from cardiac involvement","Restrictive lung disease or apical pulmonary fibrosis"],"prognosis":"Disease course is variable; many patients maintain good function with exercise and modern therapy, but persistent inflammation can lead to progressive spinal ankylosis, fixed kyphotic deformity, and increased fracture risk. Early diagnosis and biologic therapy have improved symptom control and quality of life.","pitfalls":["Attributing chronic back pain in a young adult to mechanical causes and delaying diagnosis for years","Relying on HLA-B27 as a diagnostic test rather than a supportive finding","Assuming normal inflammatory markers exclude active disease","Overlooking the high fracture risk of a rigid, osteoporotic spine after minor trauma","Neglecting extra-articular manifestations such as uveitis, bowel disease, and cardiac involvement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"anorectal_abscess":{"name":"Anorectal abscess","system":"GI / Anorectal / Surgery","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["perianal abscess","ischiorectal abscess","perirectal abscess","intersphincteric abscess"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with acute, constant, throbbing perianal or rectal pain, often with a tender fluctuant swelling, fever, and malaise","Classified by anatomical space into perianal, ischiorectal, intersphincteric, and supralevator abscesses, each with characteristic examination findings","Most arise from infection of the anal glands opening into the crypts at the dentate line (the cryptoglandular theory)","A superficial perianal abscess is visibly red and fluctuant, whereas a deeper ischiorectal or supralevator abscess may cause deep pain and systemic upset with little external sign","Treatment is prompt surgical incision and drainage; antibiotics alone are inadequate and delay is dangerous","A significant proportion of drained abscesses are associated with, or later develop, a fistula-in-ano","Diabetes, immunosuppression, and Crohn's disease predispose to abscess formation and to more aggressive, spreading infection","Spreading perianal sepsis with systemic toxicity may herald necrotising infection (Fournier's gangrene), which is a surgical emergency"],"pathophysiology":"Anorectal abscesses usually begin with obstruction and infection of an anal gland at the base of an anal crypt (cryptoglandular infection). Pus tracks along planes of least resistance from the intersphincteric space into the perianal, ischiorectal, or supralevator spaces, and can extend circumferentially in a horseshoe pattern. The causative organisms are typically gut flora, including coliforms and anaerobes. Underlying conditions such as Crohn's disease, diabetes, and immunosuppression predispose to formation and to more extensive spread. Persistence of the primary gland infection after drainage creates the epithelialised track of a fistula-in-ano.","additionalDifferentials":["Thrombosed external haemorrhoid","Pilonidal abscess","Bartholin abscess (in women)","Hidradenitis suppurativa","Perianal Crohn's disease","Anal or rectal malignancy with sepsis","Fournier's gangrene (necrotising perineal infection)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination and, where pain permits, digital rectal examination to assess deeper spaces","Examination under anaesthesia to fully assess and drain the collection and look for a fistula","MRI pelvis or endoanal ultrasound for complex, recurrent, or supralevator disease","Blood glucose and screening for immunosuppression, and blood cultures if systemically septic","Consideration of investigations for Crohn's disease in recurrent or atypical abscesses"],"redFlags":["Spreading cellulitis, crepitus, skin necrosis, or disproportionate pain suggesting Fournier's gangrene (surgical emergency)","Systemic sepsis with tachycardia, hypotension, or confusion","Immunocompromise or poorly controlled diabetes with rapid progression","Deep or supralevator sepsis with minimal external signs but severe pain and toxicity"],"prognosis":"Simple abscesses drained promptly resolve well, but recurrence and fistula formation are common. Delayed treatment, deep spaces, immunosuppression, or necrotising infection markedly worsen outcomes and can be life-threatening.","pitfalls":["Relying on antibiotics instead of prompt incision and drainage","Missing a deep ischiorectal or supralevator abscess because external signs are minimal","Failing to recognise early Fournier's gangrene in a diabetic or immunosuppressed patient","Overzealous probing for a fistula at the time of acute drainage, risking a false track"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"anorexia_nervosa":{"name":"Anorexia nervosa","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["anorexia","AN"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by restriction of energy intake leading to significantly low body weight, intense fear of weight gain, and a disturbance in how body weight or shape is experienced","Two patterns are recognised: a restricting type and a binge-eating/purging type","Physical consequences include amenorrhoea, bradycardia, hypotension, hypothermia, lanugo hair, and osteopenia","Purging behaviours can cause electrolyte disturbances such as hypokalaemia, metabolic alkalosis, and dental erosion","Management principles centre on structured nutritional rehabilitation and weight restoration alongside psychotherapy, with family-based treatment favoured for adolescents","Medical stabilisation takes priority in severe cases; feeding must be advanced cautiously to avoid refeeding syndrome","Comorbid depression, anxiety, and obsessive-compulsive features are common, and suicide risk is elevated","Weigh patients and monitor electrolytes and cardiac status, since bradycardia and QT prolongation can be dangerous"],"pathophysiology":"Anorexia nervosa arises from an interaction of genetic and temperamental vulnerability (perfectionism, anxiety, harm avoidance), sociocultural pressures, and neurobiological factors including altered reward and interoceptive processing and serotonergic and dopaminergic dysregulation. Starvation itself perpetuates the disorder through neuroendocrine and cognitive changes, and complications reflect the systemic effects of chronic malnutrition.","additionalDifferentials":["Bulimia nervosa","Avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder","Major depressive disorder with appetite loss","Hyperthyroidism","Malabsorption and gastrointestinal disease","Malignancy or chronic infection causing weight loss","Body dysmorphic disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Weight, height, and body mass index with growth trajectory in young people","Electrolytes including potassium, phosphate, and magnesium","ECG to assess for bradycardia and QT prolongation","Full blood count, renal and liver function, and glucose","Bone density assessment in prolonged illness","Assessment for refeeding risk before and during nutritional rehabilitation"],"redFlags":["Severe bradycardia, hypotension, hypothermia, or arrhythmia indicating cardiac instability","Dangerous electrolyte derangement, particularly hypokalaemia and hypophosphataemia","Refeeding syndrome during nutritional restoration","Rapid or extreme weight loss and very low body mass index","Suicidal ideation"],"prognosis":"Anorexia nervosa has one of the highest mortality rates among psychiatric disorders, from both medical complications and suicide. Many patients recover, particularly with early intervention in adolescence, but a substantial proportion follow a chronic or relapsing course.","pitfalls":["Refeeding too rapidly and precipitating life-threatening refeeding syndrome","Focusing on weight alone while neglecting electrolyte and cardiac monitoring","Underestimating suicide risk","Being reassured by a normal weight in the binge-purge type despite dangerous electrolyte disturbance","Delaying medical stabilisation while awaiting psychiatric transfer"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"anterior_uveitis":{"name":"Anterior uveitis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["iritis","iridocyclitis","acute anterior uveitis","AAU"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with a painful red eye, photophobia, tearing, and blurred vision, typically with circumcorneal (ciliary) injection rather than diffuse redness","The pupil is often small (miotic) and may be irregular if posterior synechiae have formed","Slit-lamp examination shows cells and flare in the anterior chamber; a hypopyon (layered pus) indicates severe inflammation","Keratic precipitates on the corneal endothelium help characterise the inflammation as granulomatous or non-granulomatous","Strongly associated with HLA-B27 and its related systemic conditions (ankylosing spondylitis, reactive arthritis, inflammatory bowel disease, psoriatic arthritis)","Management principles are topical corticosteroids to suppress inflammation and cycloplegic/mydriatic drops to relieve ciliary spasm and prevent or break synechiae","Recurrent, bilateral, or granulomatous uveitis warrants investigation for underlying systemic or infective causes"],"pathophysiology":"Anterior uveitis is inflammation of the iris and ciliary body (anterior uveal tract). Breakdown of the blood-aqueous barrier allows inflammatory cells and protein to leak into the anterior chamber, seen clinically as cells and flare. The process may be immune-mediated (often HLA-B27 associated or linked to systemic autoimmune disease), infective, or triggered by trauma. Inflammatory adhesions can form between the iris and lens (posterior synechiae) or the iris and trabecular meshwork (peripheral anterior synechiae), and secondary complications include raised intraocular pressure, cataract, and macular oedema.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Bacterial or viral conjunctivitis","Bacterial keratitis or corneal ulcer","Scleritis and episcleritis","Endophthalmitis","Acute red eye from subconjunctival haemorrhage"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination to grade anterior chamber cells and flare and identify keratic precipitates","Intraocular pressure measurement to detect secondary glaucoma","Dilated fundus examination to exclude intermediate/posterior involvement and macular oedema","HLA-B27 typing and targeted systemic workup in recurrent or bilateral disease","Investigations for infective or granulomatous causes (e.g. syphilis, tuberculosis, sarcoidosis) when indicated"],"redFlags":["Hypopyon or severe inflammation suggesting aggressive or infective uveitis, or endophthalmitis","Marked pain with a red eye and raised intraocular pressure requiring differentiation from angle closure","Bilateral, recurrent, or granulomatous disease pointing to serious systemic illness","Progressive visual loss from macular oedema or dense synechiae"],"prognosis":"Most acute, non-granulomatous, HLA-B27-associated episodes respond well to prompt topical treatment, but recurrent or chronic uveitis can lead to cataract, glaucoma, and macular oedema with lasting visual loss.","pitfalls":["Prescribing topical steroids without excluding herpetic or other infective keratitis, which steroids can worsen","Failing to use a cycloplegic, allowing painful ciliary spasm and permanent posterior synechiae","Missing raised intraocular pressure or steroid-induced pressure rise during treatment","Not investigating recurrent or bilateral cases for an underlying systemic cause"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"anthrax":{"name":"Anthrax","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Bacillus anthracis infection","cutaneous anthrax","inhalational anthrax","woolsorter's disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by the spore-forming Gram-positive rod Bacillus anthracis, a zoonosis of herbivores acquired through contact with infected animals or contaminated animal products","Cutaneous anthrax is the commonest form and presents as a painless papule that ulcerates into a characteristic black eschar with marked surrounding oedema","Inhalational (pulmonary) anthrax follows spore inhalation and causes a fulminant illness with a widened mediastinum from haemorrhagic lymphadenitis and rapidly fatal shock","Gastrointestinal and oropharyngeal forms follow ingestion of contaminated meat and carry high mortality","It is a recognised agent of bioterrorism, so clustered or unexplained inhalational cases warrant urgent public-health alert","Prompt antibacterial therapy is effective for cutaneous disease, while systemic forms also require antitoxin and intensive supportive care","In endemic parts of India it occurs sporadically among people handling livestock, hides and wool","Diagnosis rests on Gram stain and culture of lesion, blood or other fluids, with PCR and toxin assays as confirmatory tests"],"pathophysiology":"Bacillus anthracis spores enter through skin abrasions, inhalation or ingestion, germinate, and multiply while producing a poly-D-glutamic acid capsule that resists phagocytosis and a tripartite toxin. Protective antigen mediates entry of lethal factor and oedema factor into cells; lethal factor is a protease that disrupts intracellular signalling and triggers massive inflammatory and cytotoxic effects, while oedema factor raises cyclic AMP causing the striking tissue oedema. Systemic dissemination produces toxaemia, vascular leak, haemorrhage and shock, which drive the high mortality of non-cutaneous disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Staphylococcal or streptococcal skin and soft-tissue infection with eschar","Necrotising fasciitis","Plague (bubonic or pneumonic)","Tularaemia","Community-acquired pneumonia or mediastinitis for the inhalational form","Severe cellulitis with lymphadenitis"],"infectionMimics":["Brown recluse spider bite with necrotic eschar","Rickettsial eschar (e.g. scrub typhus)","Ecthyma gangrenosum","Orf or other zoonotic skin lesions"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Gram stain and culture of skin lesion fluid, blood, pleural fluid or CSF","Blood cultures in systemic disease","Chest imaging looking for a widened mediastinum and pleural effusions in inhalational anthrax","PCR and toxin/antigen detection for rapid confirmation","Notification to and testing by a reference/public-health laboratory"],"redFlags":["Widened mediastinum with dyspnoea and shock indicating inhalational anthrax, a medical emergency","Extensive oedema of the head and neck threatening the airway in cutaneous or oropharyngeal disease","Signs of meningitis (anthrax meningoencephalitis is often haemorrhagic and rapidly fatal)","A cluster of unexplained severe febrile respiratory illnesses suggesting deliberate release"],"prognosis":"Cutaneous anthrax usually responds well to timely antibacterial treatment with low mortality, though the eschar heals with scarring. Inhalational, gastrointestinal and meningeal forms are frequently fatal even with treatment, and outcome depends critically on how early therapy and antitoxin are started.","pitfalls":["Incising or debriding a cutaneous anthrax lesion, which can worsen dissemination","Mistaking early inhalational anthrax for ordinary influenza or community-acquired pneumonia and delaying therapy","Failing to consider occupational or bioterrorism exposure history","Overlooking the need for antitoxin and public-health notification in systemic disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"antiphospholipid_syndrome":{"name":"Antiphospholipid Syndrome","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["APS","Hughes syndrome"],"chapter":369,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2837","clinicalPearls":["APS is an autoantibody-mediated acquired thrombophilia defined by recurrent arterial or venous thrombosis, microvascular manifestations, and/or pregnancy morbidity; it affects primarily young females (Harrison 22e p.2837).","APS may occur alone (primary) or in association with other autoimmune diseases, mainly SLE; about one-third of SLE patients carry aPL antibodies and 10-15% of those develop APS (Harrison 22e p.2837).","Diagnosis should be seriously considered with thrombosis, cerebrovascular accidents in individuals younger than 55 years, or pregnancy morbidity, particularly in the presence of livedo reticularis or thrombocytopenia (Harrison 22e p.2838).","The three classical laboratory antibodies are lupus anticoagulant (LA), anticardiolipin (aCL), and anti-beta2-glycoprotein I (anti-beta2GPI); higher-risk profiles include triple positivity, IgG isotype, moderate-to-high titers, and persistent positivity (Harrison 22e p.2838-2839).","Laboratory criteria require persistence: moderate (40-79 units) or high (>=80 units) aCL/anti-beta2GPI titers confirmed on two occasions 12 weeks apart (Harrison 22e p.2838-2839).","In patients receiving anticoagulation, LA test positivity should be interpreted with caution; when features are highly reminiscent of APS but classical aPL are absent, antiphosphatidylserine/prothrombin antibody testing may have a diagnostic role (Harrison 22e p.2837).","Deep venous thrombosis occurs primarily in the lower extremities and often causes pulmonary emboli; cerebral venous thrombosis may present with intracranial hypertension and focal deficits (Harrison 22e p.2837).","Arterial thrombosis most commonly affects cerebral arteries, manifesting as transient ischemic attack, stroke, migraine, or cognitive dysfunction (Harrison 22e p.2837).","Libman-Sacks endocarditis (small, usually <1 cm, organized platelet-fibrin valve vegetations) and valve thickening are characteristic cardiac manifestations (Harrison 22e p.2837).","Thrombocytopenia and autoimmune hemolytic anemia are the main hematologic manifestations of APS (Harrison 22e p.2837).","DOACs have recently been shown to increase the risk of arterial events, especially in patients with triple positivity or previous arterial thrombosis, and a meta-analysis found prior thrombosis type did not change this increased arterial risk versus VKAs (Harrison 22e p.2839)."],"pathophysiology":"APS is an autoantibody-mediated acquired thrombophilia. The major autoantibodies target phospholipid-binding plasma proteins, chiefly beta2-glycoprotein I (beta2GPI, a 43-kDa apolipoprotein of 326 amino acids in five domains) and prothrombin; anti-domain I IgG is associated with increased thrombotic risk, while the antithrombotic function of beta2GPI depends on its fifth domain. Lupus anticoagulant is a separate group of antibodies against phospholipid-binding proteins that prolongs in vitro clotting time which is not corrected by adding normal plasma. Antibody induction is thought to begin with endothelial injury (from infections, oxidative stress, or major physical stress such as surgery or trauma) in a permissive genetic background (HLA associations), driving increased endothelial apoptosis, autoantigen presentation, and an autoimmune response. Binding of aPL to disrupted endothelium produces a proinflammatory, prothrombotic state with monocyte and platelet activation, adhesion molecules and proinflammatory cytokines, complement and neutrophil activation, neutrophil extracellular trap (NET) formation, and thrombus formation. Type I interferon pathway activation and an imbalance between type I and III interferons have recently been proposed as mechanisms of thrombosis and obstetric complications (Harrison 22e p.2837).","additionalDifferentials":["Inherited or acquired thrombophilias including factor V Leiden and other causes","Coombs-positive hemolytic anemia","Thrombocytopenia of other cause","Atherosclerosis","Polyarteritis nodosa","SLE","Cryoglobulinemia","Lymphomas","Myeloproliferative disorders","Hypercholesterolemia","For catastrophic APS: thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, hemolytic-uremic syndrome, disseminated intravascular coagulation, and heparin-induced thrombocytopenia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Other inherited or acquired thrombophilias (e.g., factor V Leiden)","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Hemolytic-uremic syndrome","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","Livedo reticularis from vascular-wall disease (atherosclerosis, polyarteritis nodosa, SLE, cryoglobulinemia, lymphomas) or vascular-lumen disease (myeloproliferative disorders, hypercholesterolemia)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Lupus anticoagulant testing as a three-step procedure: screening with activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) and dilute Russell viper venom test (DRVVT), mixing study, and confirmation","Anticardiolipin and anti-beta2GPI IgG/IgM antibodies by standardized ELISA","Antiphosphatidylserine/prothrombin antibodies when APS features are present without classical aPL","Repeat aPL testing 12 weeks apart to confirm persistence","Assessment per 2023 ACR/EULAR classification criteria (>=3 points from clinical domains and >=3 points from laboratory domains)","Renal biopsy for suspected aPL-nephropathy (thrombotic microangiopathy in acute phase; fibrous intima hyperplasia, fibrous/fibrocellular arteriolar occlusions, focal cortical atrophy in chronic phase)","Cardiac MRI or histology for suspected myocardial disease; imaging for adrenal hemorrhage; BAL or pathology for pulmonary hemorrhage"],"redFlags":["Catastrophic APS (CAPS): a rare, life-threatening, rapidly progressive thromboembolic disease simultaneously involving three or more organs, typically with microvascular thrombosis developing in less than a week","Thrombotic microangiopathy (microangiopathic hemolytic anemia with severe thrombocytopenia) as a typical CAPS finding","Cerebrovascular accident in an individual younger than 55 years","Acute limb ischemia or digital gangrene from peripheral arterial thrombosis","Budd-Chiari syndrome from hepatic vein thrombosis","Pulmonary embolism or pulmonary hypertension from pulmonary arterial thrombosis"],"prognosis":"Thrombotic and obstetric event risk is closely tied to the underlying aPL profile, depending on antibody type (IgG high risk vs IgM low risk), number of antibodies (two or three classical autoantibodies confer higher risk than a single antibody), titer (moderate-high vs low), and persistence on repeated measurements. Catastrophic APS is life-threatening and rapidly progressive. APS antibodies increase with age, though it is questionable whether they induce thrombotic events in elderly individuals (Harrison 22e p.2837-2839).","pitfalls":["Paradoxical pitfall: lupus anticoagulant prolongs in vitro clotting tests (e.g., aPTT) yet is associated with thrombosis in vivo, not bleeding; the prolongation is NOT corrected by mixing with normal plasma, distinguishing it from a factor deficiency.","aPTT has variable sensitivity to lupus anticoagulant depending on the reagent used; aPTT prolongation is not specific because factor deficiencies or other inhibitors also prolong it, so confirmation requires lack of correction on mixing and correction with added phospholipids (Harrison 22e p.~700).","In patients receiving anticoagulation, lupus anticoagulant positivity should be interpreted with caution.","Classification requires that clinical manifestations with other equally or more likely explanations than APS not be counted toward the criteria.","aPL antibodies are frequently transiently positive, so persistence must be confirmed (titers on two occasions 12 weeks apart) before attributing disease."],"severityClassification":"2023 ACR/EULAR classification criteria require at least 3 points from clinical domains AND at least 3 points from laboratory domains. Clinical domains: (1) venous thromboembolism, (2) arterial thrombosis, (3) microvascular events, (4) obstetric complications, (5) cardiac valve disease, (6) hematologic manifestations. Laboratory domains: (1) lupus anticoagulant, (2) anticardiolipin, and/or (3) anti-beta2GPI antibodies at moderate (40-79 units) or high (>=80 units) titers on two occasions 12 weeks apart. Definite CAPS requires all four: thrombosis in >=3 organs/systems; development in less than a week; histologic small-vessel thrombosis in at least one organ; and aPL presence (probable CAPS when a combination is present) (Harrison 22e p.2838-2839).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2837","Harrison 22e p.2838","Harrison 22e p.2839"],"crossLinks":["sle_flare","dvt","pe","ischemic_stroke"]},"antithrombin_deficiency":{"name":"Antithrombin deficiency","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AT deficiency","antithrombin III deficiency","ATIII deficiency","hereditary antithrombin deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An inherited thrombophilia in which reduced antithrombin activity removes a key brake on coagulation, producing a strong predisposition to venous thromboembolism","Among the heritable thrombophilias it confers one of the highest relative risks of thrombosis, often presenting at a young age or in unusual sites such as mesenteric or cerebral veins","Thrombosis is frequently provoked by additional risk factors including pregnancy, the postpartum period, surgery, immobility, and oestrogen-containing contraception","A characteristic clue is apparent resistance to unfractionated heparin, because heparin works by potentiating antithrombin, which is deficient","Diagnosis uses a functional antithrombin activity assay; levels can be lowered acutely by active clot, heparin therapy, liver disease, nephrotic syndrome, and disseminated intravascular coagulation","Testing is best deferred until after the acute event and away from anticoagulant interference to avoid misclassification, and family screening is worthwhile","Acute venous thromboembolism is treated with anticoagulation, and antithrombin concentrate may be used in high-risk situations such as surgery or delivery when heparin alone is inadequate","Long-term anticoagulation is considered after unprovoked or recurrent thrombosis given the high recurrence risk"],"pathophysiology":"Antithrombin is a circulating serine protease inhibitor that neutralises thrombin and factor Xa (and other activated clotting factors), acting as a principal natural anticoagulant; its activity is greatly accelerated by heparin and endothelial heparan sulphate. Inherited deficiency, usually autosomal dominant, arises from mutations that reduce either the amount (type I) or the function (type II) of the protein. With less effective inhibition of thrombin and factor Xa, coagulation proceeds unchecked, tipping haemostatic balance towards thrombosis. Acquired reductions occur through loss (nephrotic syndrome), reduced synthesis (liver disease), or consumption (acute thrombosis, DIC, heparin therapy).","additionalDifferentials":["Protein C deficiency","Protein S deficiency","Factor V Leiden and activated protein C resistance","Prothrombin gene mutation","Antiphospholipid syndrome","Acquired antithrombin loss (nephrotic syndrome, liver disease, DIC)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Functional antithrombin activity assay as the key diagnostic test","Antithrombin antigen level to distinguish type I from type II deficiency","A broader thrombophilia panel (protein C, protein S, factor V Leiden, prothrombin mutation, antiphospholipid antibodies)","Repeat testing away from acute thrombosis and heparin therapy to confirm a persistent defect","Renal and liver assessment to exclude acquired causes","Family testing of first-degree relatives once a hereditary defect is confirmed"],"redFlags":["Acute venous thromboembolism, including extensive deep vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism","Thrombosis at unusual sites such as cerebral, mesenteric, or hepatic veins","Apparent heparin resistance with failure to achieve therapeutic anticoagulation","Pregnancy or the peripartum period, when thrombotic risk is greatly increased and needs proactive management"],"prognosis":"Antithrombin deficiency carries a high lifetime and recurrence risk of venous thromboembolism relative to other inherited thrombophilias; with appropriate anticoagulation, risk-situation prophylaxis, and use of antithrombin concentrate when indicated, most affected individuals can be managed effectively.","pitfalls":["Interpreting a low antithrombin level measured during acute thrombosis or heparin therapy as hereditary deficiency without confirmatory repeat testing","Failing to recognise heparin resistance as a clue to the diagnosis","Overlooking acquired causes such as nephrotic syndrome or liver disease","Not providing intensified prophylaxis or antithrombin support during pregnancy, surgery, or delivery","Omitting family counselling and screening after a hereditary defect is identified"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"aortic_regurgitation":{"name":"Aortic Regurgitation","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AR","aortic insufficiency"],"chapter":273,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2043-2047","clinicalPearls":["Chronic severe AR can have a long latent period, with patients remaining relatively asymptomatic for 10-15 years; exertional dyspnea is usually the first symptom of diminished cardiac reserve, followed by orthopnea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea (p.2044).","A wide arterial pulse pressure (systolic hypertension plus low diastolic pressure) is characteristic of chronic severe AR, producing peripheral signs such as the water-hammer (Corrigan's) pulse, Quincke's capillary pulsations, Traube's pistol-shot femoral sounds, and Duroziez's sign (p.2043-2044).","The murmur of chronic AR is a high-pitched, blowing, decrescendo diastolic murmur heard best in the third intercostal space along the left sternal border; it is best appreciated with the patient sitting up, leaning forward, breath held in forced expiration (p.2044).","A diastolic murmur louder along the right sternal border suggests AR caused by aneurysmal dilation of the aortic root rather than primary valvular disease (p.2044).","The Austin Flint murmur is a soft, low-pitched rumbling mid-to-late diastolic murmur in severe AR, probably from diastolic displacement of the anterior mitral leaflet by the regurgitant stream; it is NOT associated with hemodynamically significant mitral obstruction (p.2044).","Anginal chest pain can occur in severe AR even without coronary artery disease and even in younger patients, may occur at rest, and often responds poorly to sublingual nitroglycerin (p.2044).","On echocardiography, a rapid high-frequency diastolic fluttering of the anterior mitral leaflet, produced by the impact of the regurgitant jet, is a characteristic finding (p.2044).","Auscultatory features of AR are intensified by sustained handgrip, which augments systemic vascular resistance and increases LV afterload (p.2044).","In acute severe AR, the elevated LV end-diastolic pressure leads to early (premature) closure of the mitral valve, a soft S1, and a soft, short early diastolic murmur (p.2044)."],"pathophysiology":"The total LV stroke volume (effective forward stroke volume plus regurgitant volume) is increased; in severe AR the regurgitant volume may equal the effective forward stroke volume. Unlike MR, the entire LV stroke volume is ejected into the high-pressure aorta. The major compensation is an increase in LV end-diastolic volume (increased preload); dilation and eccentric hypertrophy allow a larger stroke volume without increased myofibril shortening, so severe AR can occur with a normal effective forward stroke volume and normal LVEF together with elevated LV end-diastolic pressure and volume. By Laplace's law LV dilation increases systolic wall tension, so chronic AR is a state of both increased preload and afterload. Adaptive measures eventually fail; deterioration of LV function often precedes symptom onset. Considerable LV wall thickening occurs and hearts can weigh >1000 g. The reverse aorta-to-LV diastolic pressure gradient decreases through diastole, accounting for the decrescendo murmur. In acute severe AR the LV is unprepared for the volume load, compliance is normal or reduced, diastolic pressures rise rapidly (occasionally >40 mmHg), and the reversed pressure gradient closes the mitral valve prematurely. Myocardial ischemia may occur because of elevated oxygen demand (LV dilation, hypertrophy, increased systolic tension) combined with reduced coronary perfusion (most coronary flow is diastolic, when aortic pressure is low), affecting particularly the subendocardium even without epicardial CAD (p.2043-2044).","additionalDifferentials":["Aortic stenosis (a mid-systolic ejection murmur and systolic thrill are frequently audible in isolated AR and do not necessarily signify coexisting AS)","Combined aortic stenosis and aortic regurgitation (coexistence of hemodynamically significant AS with AR occurs almost exclusively in rheumatic or congenital disease)","Mitral regurgitation (contrasted hemodynamically; severe AR can also coexist with MR)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["ECG: LV hypertrophy signs in chronic severe AR, with ST-segment depression and T-wave inversion in leads I, aVL, V5, V6 ('LV strain'); left axis deviation and/or QRS prolongation may be present (p.2044)","Transthoracic echocardiography (TTE): cornerstone of longitudinal follow-up; assesses LV size/function and cause/severity of AR; LV global longitudinal strain (GLS) by speckle tracking may detect systolic dysfunction before EF falls (p.2044)","Color flow Doppler: with severe AR the central jet width exceeds 65% of the LV outflow tract, regurgitant volume >=60 mL/beat, regurgitant fraction >=50%, and there is diastolic flow reversal in the proximal descending thoracic aorta; continuous-wave Doppler shows rapid deceleration time in acute severe AR (p.2044)","Transesophageal echocardiography (TEE): detailed anatomic assessment of valve, root, and portions of the aorta (p.2044)","Cardiac magnetic resonance (CMR): accurate LV volumes and aortic size/contour when TTE is limited; can screen for increased LV interstitial (extracellular volume fraction) and replacement fibrosis (late gadolinium enhancement) (p.2044)","Cardiac CT: detailed assessment of aortic valve, root, and thoracic aortic anatomy; coronary CT angiography can provide preoperative coronary information (p.2044-2045)","Chest X-ray: downward and leftward apex displacement, posterior LV displacement encroaching on the spine, and possible aneurysmal aortic dilation when root disease is the cause (less sensitive than echo/CMR/CT for root/ascending aortic enlargement) (p.2045)","Cardiac catheterization with contrast aortography: confirms magnitude of regurgitation and LV status when needed; coronary angiography routinely before surgery in those at coronary risk (p.2045)"],"redFlags":["Acute severe AR (as in infective endocarditis, aortic dissection, or trauma): the LV cannot dilate enough to maintain stroke volume, LV diastolic pressure rises rapidly with marked LA and PA wedge pressure elevation, and pulmonary edema and/or cardiogenic shock may develop rapidly - a surgical emergency requiring operation usually within 24 h (prompt 24-48 h surgery may be lifesaving) (p.2044, p.2046-2047).","Acute severe AR may show only slight widening of pulse pressure and invariably presents with tachycardia (heart rate increases to preserve cardiac output), unlike the wide pulse pressure of chronic AR (p.2044).","In acute severe AR, intra-aortic balloon counterpulsation is contraindicated and beta blockers are best avoided (p.2045).","Onset of symptoms or LV systolic dysfunction in chronic severe AR is an indication for surgery (p.2045)."],"prognosis":"Operative and late mortality risks depend largely on disease stage and myocardial function at operation. Overall operative mortality for isolated AVR for pure AR is ~1-2%; the risk doubles when aortic surgery is added. Patients with marked cardiac enlargement and established LV dysfunction have an operative mortality of ~5-10% and a late mortality of ~3-5% per year from LV failure despite technically satisfactory operation; nonetheless, because of the very poor prognosis with medical management, even patients with advanced LV systolic dysfunction should be considered for operation. If surgery is delayed too long (>1 year from onset of symptoms or LV dysfunction), it often does not restore normal LV size and function (p.2046-2047).","pitfalls":["Sphygmomanometer diastolic pressure can be hard to read because systolic sounds may persist with the cuff fully deflated; the muffling point (Korotkoff phase IV) corresponds reasonably to true intra-arterial diastolic pressure (p.2044).","As chronic AR progresses and LV end-diastolic pressure rises, the arterial diastolic pressure may actually rise (aortic diastolic pressure cannot fall below LV end-diastolic pressure), so a less-wide pulse pressure does not exclude severe disease (p.2044).","A mid-systolic ejection murmur and systolic thrill in isolated AR can be loud without signifying aortic valve obstruction and do not necessarily indicate coexisting AS (p.2044).","Deterioration of LV function often precedes the development of symptoms, so waiting for symptoms risks irreversible dysfunction; serial noninvasive follow-up is essential to time surgery (p.2043-2044, p.2046).","Transcatheter aortic valve implantation (TAVI) is NOT recommended for patients with severe AR who are surgical candidates (technical success limited by annular dilation and paucity of valvular/annular calcium) (p.2046)."],"severityClassification":"Severe AR is characterized by central jet width >65% of the LV outflow tract on color Doppler, vena contracta >0.6 cm, regurgitant volume >=60 mL/beat, regurgitant fraction >=50%, effective regurgitant orifice >=0.3 cm2, and holodiastolic flow reversal in the proximal descending thoracic aorta. AHA/ACC staging (per Fig. 273-2): asymptomatic (Stage C, with C2 denoting LV dysfunction EF <=55%) versus symptomatic (Stage D). Surgical thresholds for asymptomatic severe AR include LVEF <55%, LV end-systolic dimension >50 mm (>25 mm/m2), or LV end-diastolic dimension >65 mm on serial studies (p.2044, p.2046).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2043-2047"],"crossLinks":["aortic_dissection","IE","heart_failure"]},"aortoiliac_occlusive":{"name":"Aortoiliac occlusive disease","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["aortoiliac occlusive disease","Leriche syndrome","inflow disease","aortoiliac atherosclerosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Represents atherosclerotic inflow disease of the distal aorta and iliac arteries and often coexists with more distal (femoropopliteal) disease","The classic Leriche triad comprises buttock, hip, and thigh claudication, diminished or absent femoral pulses, and erectile dysfunction in men","Because obstruction is proximal, claudication is typically felt in the buttocks and thighs rather than the calves alone","Well-developed collateral pathways can maintain limb viability despite extensive occlusion, so rest pain is less common than in multilevel disease","Duplex ultrasound, CT angiography, or MR angiography define the pattern and length of disease to guide endovascular versus open reconstruction","Focal iliac stenoses are well suited to endovascular treatment, while extensive aortoiliac occlusion may require aortobifemoral bypass or extra-anatomic reconstruction","Risk-factor modification, particularly smoking cessation, antiplatelet therapy, and statins, underpins all management","Presence of aortoiliac disease signals widespread atherosclerosis with a high burden of coronary and cerebrovascular risk"],"pathophysiology":"Aortoiliac occlusive disease is caused by progressive atherosclerotic plaque within the infrarenal aorta and common and external iliac arteries, reducing arterial inflow to the pelvis and lower limbs. Because the obstruction is proximal, symptoms involve the buttocks and thighs and, in men, erectile function through compromise of the internal iliac supply. Gradual stenosis stimulates collateral development via lumbar, mesenteric, and pelvic pathways, which can preserve limb perfusion. When inflow disease coexists with infrainguinal disease (multilevel disease), the additive resistance more readily precipitates rest pain and tissue loss.","additionalDifferentials":["Femoropopliteal or infrapopliteal occlusive disease","Spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication","Hip or lumbar spine osteoarthritis","Nerve root or sciatic pathology","Chronic venous disease","Buerger disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ankle-brachial index with segmental pressures to localise the level of disease","Arterial duplex ultrasound of the aortoiliac and femoral segments","CT or MR angiography, or catheter angiography, for reconstructive planning","Cardiovascular risk assessment including lipids, glucose, and smoking status","Cardiac and functional assessment before major open reconstruction"],"redFlags":["Rest pain, ulceration, or gangrene indicating limb-threatening ischaemia and urgent vascular referral","Acute-on-chronic occlusion with sudden severe limb ischaemia, a limb-threatening emergency","Rapidly progressive symptoms or tissue loss","Concurrent features of severe cardiovascular disease"],"prognosis":"Isolated aortoiliac disease with good collateral flow often has a favourable limb outlook, and revascularisation, whether endovascular or bypass, provides durable symptom relief in appropriately selected patients. Overall survival is chiefly limited by concurrent coronary and cerebrovascular atherosclerosis.","pitfalls":["Misattributing buttock and thigh claudication to hip or spinal disease","Overlooking coexisting infrainguinal disease that limits the benefit of inflow correction alone","Underestimating cardiovascular risk and omitting systemic prevention","Failing to recognise Leriche syndrome in men presenting with erectile dysfunction and claudication"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"aphthous_stomatitis":{"name":"Aphthous stomatitis","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["recurrent aphthous stomatitis","canker sores","aphthous ulcers","RAS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Recurrent aphthous stomatitis produces painful, recurrent, round or oval ulcers with a yellow-grey base and an erythematous halo","Ulcers characteristically affect non-keratinised, movable mucosa such as the labial and buccal mucosa, sparing the hard palate and attached gingiva","Minor aphthae are small and heal without scarring in one to two weeks, while major aphthae are larger, deeper, and can scar","Herpetiform ulcers are numerous tiny lesions that may coalesce, despite the name they are not caused by herpes virus","It is a clinical diagnosis of recurrent ulceration in an otherwise well patient, and lesions recur in the same tissue types","Precipitants include stress, local trauma, certain foods, hormonal changes, and cessation of smoking","Aphthous-like ulceration can be a marker of haematinic deficiency, coeliac disease, inflammatory bowel disease, or Behcet disease","Management is largely symptomatic with topical agents to relieve pain and inflammation, alongside correcting any deficiency"],"pathophysiology":"The precise cause is not fully understood, but recurrent aphthous stomatitis is considered a T-cell mediated immune reaction directed at the oral epithelium, producing focal ulceration. A genetic predisposition is common, and episodes are triggered by factors such as local trauma, stress, hormonal fluctuation, and specific foods. Systemic contributors, including deficiencies of iron, folate, or vitamin B12, and gastrointestinal or systemic inflammatory conditions, can precipitate or worsen ulceration. The lesions represent localised epithelial destruction with a mixed inflammatory infiltrate that heals as the immune response subsides.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary or recurrent herpetic ulceration","Traumatic ulcer","Oral manifestations of Behcet disease","Ulceration from coeliac or inflammatory bowel disease","Erosive lichen planus or immunobullous disease","Oral squamous cell carcinoma presenting as a non-healing ulcer"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical history focusing on frequency, pattern, and triggers","Haematinics (iron studies, folate, vitamin B12) and full blood count","Coeliac serology when gastrointestinal features or deficiency present","Assessment for systemic features suggesting Behcet or inflammatory bowel disease","Biopsy of any solitary ulcer that fails to heal to exclude malignancy"],"redFlags":["A single ulcer persisting beyond two to three weeks, which must be investigated to exclude oral cancer","Ulceration with systemic features such as genital ulcers or ocular inflammation suggesting Behcet disease","Severe or atypical ulceration pointing to underlying systemic or immunodeficiency disease"],"prognosis":"Most patients have self-limiting recurrent episodes that heal without scarring and can be controlled symptomatically, though major aphthae may scar and some cases reflect a treatable underlying systemic condition.","pitfalls":["Dismissing a persistent solitary ulcer as aphthous when it may be malignant","Failing to screen for haematinic deficiency or coeliac disease in severe or new-onset cases","Confusing herpetiform aphthae with herpes simplex infection","Overlooking systemic disease such as Behcet when extra-oral features are present"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"aplastic_anemia":{"name":"Aplastic anaemia","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["aplastic anemia","AA","bone marrow aplasia","hypoplastic anaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by pancytopenia with a hypocellular bone marrow in the absence of abnormal infiltrating cells or fibrosis, reflecting failure of the haematopoietic stem cell compartment","Presentation reflects the affected lineages: fatigue and breathlessness from anaemia, mucocutaneous bleeding and petechiae from thrombocytopenia, and infections from neutropenia","Most cases are acquired and immune-mediated, with T-cell attack on stem cells; drugs, chemicals (e.g. benzene), viral hepatitis (seronegative), and radiation are recognised triggers","Inherited causes such as Fanconi anaemia and dyskeratosis congenita should be considered in younger patients or those with characteristic physical or family features","Severity is graded by degree of neutropenia, reticulocytopenia, and thrombocytopenia; very severe disease carries the highest risk of early death from infection or haemorrhage","Definitive management is either allogeneic stem cell transplant (favoured in younger patients with a matched sibling donor) or immunosuppressive therapy combining antithymocyte globulin and a calcineurin inhibitor","A thrombopoietin receptor agonist can augment haematopoietic recovery when added to immunosuppression","Overlap exists with paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria and hypoplastic myelodysplasia, so flow cytometry for PNH clones and marrow cytogenetics are important at diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"Aplastic anaemia results from destruction or profound suppression of pluripotent haematopoietic stem cells, leaving an empty (hypocellular) marrow and peripheral pancytopenia. In most acquired cases the mechanism is autoimmune: activated cytotoxic T lymphocytes and their cytokines (interferon-gamma, tumour necrosis factor) drive stem cell apoptosis. Environmental exposures, certain drugs, and viral illness can initiate this immune attack, while inherited marrow failure syndromes reflect defects in DNA repair or telomere maintenance that exhaust the stem cell pool. Clonal disorders such as PNH and MDS can emerge from a damaged marrow because surviving abnormal clones gain a survival advantage.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypoplastic myelodysplastic syndrome","Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria","Acute leukaemia (particularly hypocellular presentations)","Marrow infiltration or fibrosis causing pancytopenia","Severe megaloblastic anaemia (B12 or folate deficiency)","Overwhelming infection or hypersplenism causing peripheral cytopenias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count with reticulocyte count and blood film to confirm pancytopenia and exclude abnormal cells","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine biopsy to demonstrate hypocellularity and exclude infiltration or dysplasia","Cytogenetic and molecular studies to differentiate from myelodysplasia","Peripheral blood flow cytometry for a PNH clone","Chromosomal fragility testing and telomere length in suspected inherited syndromes","Viral serology (including hepatitis) and screening for autoimmune disease"],"redFlags":["Severe neutropenia with fever, indicating potentially fatal sepsis requiring urgent broad-spectrum antibiotics","Profound thrombocytopenia with mucosal bleeding or signs of intracranial haemorrhage","Rapidly falling counts defining very severe aplastic anaemia and the need for prompt referral to a transplant centre","Emergence of dysplastic or cytogenetic abnormalities suggesting clonal evolution to MDS or leukaemia"],"prognosis":"Untreated severe aplastic anaemia is frequently fatal from infection or bleeding, but outcomes have transformed with early allogeneic transplantation and effective immunosuppression; younger patients with matched sibling donors have high survival, while older patients rely on immunosuppressive therapy with a meaningful risk of relapse and late clonal disease.","pitfalls":["Attributing pancytopenia to a peripheral cause and omitting a marrow biopsy that would reveal hypocellularity","Overlooking a coexisting PNH clone or evolving myelodysplasia","Delaying HLA typing and transplant referral in young patients with severe disease","Failing to recognise an inherited marrow failure syndrome, which changes treatment and family counselling","Giving platelet or red cell transfusions without irradiation or leucodepletion in a potential transplant candidate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"apparent_life_threatening_event":{"name":"Apparent Life-Threatening Event","system":"Respiratory / General","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ALTE","BRUE","brief resolved unexplained event"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The older term ALTE described a frightening episode in an infant involving some combination of apnoea, colour change, altered tone, and choking or gagging as perceived by the observer.","Because ALTE was poorly defined, it has largely been replaced by the concept of a brief resolved unexplained event (BRUE) in infants under one year.","A BRUE applies only when the infant is asymptomatic on presentation, the episode was brief and has fully resolved, and no explanation is found after appropriate history and examination.","A qualifying event involves one or more of: cyanosis or pallor, absent, decreased, or irregular breathing, marked change in tone, or altered responsiveness.","The main clinical task is to distinguish low-risk infants, who need little or no intervention, from higher-risk infants requiring evaluation.","Low-risk criteria generally include age over about two months, no prematurity, a single brief event, no need for cardiopulmonary resuscitation by a trained provider, and a reassuring history and examination.","A careful history and examination frequently reveal an underlying explanation such as reflux, a respiratory infection, a seizure, or feeding difficulty, in which case the event is not a BRUE.","Extensive investigation is often unnecessary in low-risk infants, and shared decision-making with caregivers, including reassurance and safety advice, is central to management."],"pathophysiology":"These events are a clinical presentation rather than a single disease, and reflect a transient disturbance of cardiorespiratory or neurological control in the infant. Immature regulation of breathing, airway protective reflexes, and arousal can be perturbed by triggers such as gastro-oesophageal reflux, airway obstruction, infection, seizure activity, or autonomic instability, producing a brief episode that resolves as the infant recovers. When no cause is identified despite adequate assessment, the event is classified as unexplained.","additionalDifferentials":["Gastro-oesophageal reflux with laryngospasm","Respiratory infection (including pertussis, RSV)","Seizure or other neurological event","Cardiac arrhythmia or structural heart disease","Inborn error of metabolism","Non-accidental injury or inflicted harm","Airway anomaly or feeding-related aspiration"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thorough history and examination to risk-stratify and seek an underlying cause","Observation and pulse oximetry as appropriate","ECG when a cardiac cause or arrhythmia is a concern","Targeted testing (e.g. for infection, reflux, or seizures) guided by history rather than routinely","Evaluation for non-accidental injury when the history is inconsistent or concerning","Metabolic screening only if clinical features suggest it"],"redFlags":["Age under two months or history of prematurity","Recurrent or multiple events, or a prolonged episode","Cardiopulmonary resuscitation required by a trained provider","Abnormal examination findings, ongoing symptoms, or failure to return to baseline","History inconsistent with the injury or features suggesting non-accidental harm","Family history of sudden unexplained death or a sibling with a similar event"],"prognosis":"Most infants meeting low-risk BRUE criteria have an excellent outcome with a very low risk of recurrence or serious underlying disease, whereas higher-risk infants and those with an identified cause have a prognosis that depends on the specific diagnosis.","pitfalls":["Applying the BRUE label when the infant is symptomatic or an explanation exists","Over-investigating and hospitalising clearly low-risk infants","Under-investigating higher-risk infants who need closer evaluation","Missing non-accidental injury when the history does not fit the presentation","Failing to provide caregiver reassurance, safety advice, and clear follow-up guidance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"ards":{"name":"Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS)","system":"Critical Care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ARDS"],"chapter":312,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2299","clinicalPearls":["ARDS is a clinical syndrome of severe dyspnea of rapid onset, hypoxemia, and diffuse bilateral pulmonary infiltrates leading to respiratory failure, caused by diffuse lung injury (Harrison 22e p.2299).","By 2012 expert consensus, ARDS is graded as mild, moderate, or severe by the degree of hypoxemia (PaO2/FiO2 ratio), and these stages correlate with mortality risk and duration of mechanical ventilation in survivors (Harrison 22e p.2299, p.2300).","Most cases (>80%) arise from a small number of disorders: pneumonia and sepsis (~40-60%), followed by aspiration of gastric contents, trauma, multiple transfusions, and drug overdose; risk rises with more than one predisposing condition (Harrison 22e p.2299).","The natural history has three phases - exudative (first ~7 days), proliferative (~day 7-21), and fibrotic - each with characteristic clinical and pathologic features (Harrison 22e p.2299, p.2301).","Cornerstone of management is recognizing and treating the underlying trigger (pneumonia, sepsis, aspiration, trauma) plus standardized bundled ICU care, rather than ARDS-specific drug therapy (Harrison 22e p.2302).","Lung-protective ventilation with low tidal volume (6 mL/kg predicted body weight) and limited plateau pressure (<=30 cmH2O) significantly lowers mortality versus conventional higher tidal volumes; this is the only therapy graded by the strongest evidence (Harrison 22e p.2302, p.2303).","Prone positioning early in severe ARDS (PaO2/FiO2 <150 mmHg) reduced 28-day mortality in a randomized trial; it requires a team experienced in proning because repositioning critically ill patients can be hazardous (Harrison 22e p.2302).","A conservative fluid strategy - reducing left atrial filling pressure with fluid restriction and diuretics - shortens ICU stay and duration of ventilation, limited only by hypotension and organ hypoperfusion (Harrison 22e p.2302).","Mortality in ARDS is largely due to nonpulmonary causes, with sepsis and nonpulmonary organ failure accounting for >80% of deaths (Harrison 22e p.2303)."],"pathophysiology":"ARDS results from diffuse lung injury, either direct (e.g., pneumonia, aspiration, toxic inhalation, pulmonary contusion) or indirect (e.g., sepsis, severe trauma, multiple transfusions, pancreatitis). It evolves through three phases. In the EXUDATIVE phase (first ~7 days), alveolar capillary endothelial cells and type I pneumocytes are injured, the tight alveolar barrier is lost, and protein-rich edema fluid accumulates in the interstitial and alveolar spaces. Proinflammatory cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, IL-8, TNF-alpha) and lipid mediators recruit neutrophils, and condensed plasma proteins plus cellular debris and dysfunctional surfactant form hyaline membranes (diffuse alveolar damage). Pulmonary vascular injury occurs early with microthrombi. Dependent alveolar collapse reduces lung compliance, producing intrapulmonary shunting, hypoxemia, and increased work of breathing; microvascular occlusion raises dead space and pulmonary vascular resistance, so hypercapnia and pulmonary hypertension can also occur. The PROLIFERATIVE phase (~day 7-21) brings lung repair, organization of exudates, a shift from neutrophil- to lymphocyte-predominant infiltrates, and proliferation of type II pneumocytes that regenerate surfactant and differentiate into type I cells; most patients recover. Some progress to the FIBROTIC phase, with alveolar-duct and interstitial fibrosis, emphysema-like bullae, vascular occlusion and pulmonary hypertension, increased pneumothorax risk, reduced compliance, and increased dead space (Harrison 22e p.2299, p.2300, p.2301, p.2302).","additionalDifferentials":["Cardiogenic (hydrostatic) pulmonary edema","Bilateral pneumonia","Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage","Acute interstitial lung disease (e.g., acute interstitial pneumonitis)","Acute immunologic injury (e.g., hypersensitivity pneumonitis)","Toxin injury (e.g., radiation pneumonitis)","Neurogenic pulmonary edema"],"infectionMimics":["Bilateral pneumonia (may also coexist with ARDS)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cardiogenic pulmonary edema","Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage","Acute interstitial pneumonitis","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Radiation pneumonitis","Neurogenic pulmonary edema"],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas to determine PaO2/FiO2 ratio for Berlin severity grading","Chest radiograph (bilateral opacities consistent with pulmonary edema, often involving >=three-quarters of lung fields)","Echocardiography to exclude hydrostatic/cardiac etiology when no ARDS risk factor is present","Chest CT (shows bilateral infiltrates with heterogeneous, often dependent lung involvement)","Lung ultrasound permitted as an alternative imaging modality under proposed 2023 Berlin updates, especially in resource-limited settings"],"redFlags":["Progressive hypoxemia and increasing work of breathing leading to respiratory fatigue and respiratory failure requiring mechanical ventilation","Severe ARDS (PaO2/FiO2 <=100 mmHg) carrying the highest mortality","Concurrent sepsis or multiorgan failure, which account for most ARDS deaths","Lung biopsy evidence of pulmonary fibrosis in any phase, associated with increased mortality"],"prognosis":"Hospital mortality in the LUNG SAFE study was approximately 34.9% for mild, 40.3% for moderate, and 46.1% for severe ARDS, with a trend toward improved outcomes over time. Increasing Berlin-defined severity predicts higher mortality. Most deaths are nonpulmonary, with sepsis and nonpulmonary organ failure causing >80%. Major mortality risk factors are nonpulmonary: advanced age (~60% mortality in patients >75 vs ~20% in those <45), preexisting organ dysfunction (chronic liver disease, chronic alcohol abuse, chronic immunosuppression); direct lung injury carries nearly twice the death risk of indirect injury. Among survivors, the lung has notable resolving power: most regain nearly normal lung function, usually within 6 months, and more than a third have normal spirometry and diffusion capacity one year after extubation. However, exercise limitation and reduced physical quality of life are often documented at 5 years despite near-normal pulmonary function, and survivors and family caregivers carry a substantial burden of depression and posttraumatic stress disorder (Harrison 22e p.2303, p.2304).","pitfalls":["ARDS radiographic findings are characteristic but not specific and can be indistinguishable from cardiogenic pulmonary edema; unlike cardiogenic edema, ARDS often lacks cardiomegaly, pleural effusions, or pulmonary vascular redistribution. Hydrostatic edema must not be the primary cause - if no ARDS risk factor is present, objective evaluation (e.g., echocardiography) is required to exclude a cardiac etiology (Harrison 22e p.2300, p.2301).","Mechanical ventilation, though life-saving, can aggravate lung injury via volutrauma (alveolar overdistention from excess tidal volume), barotrauma (high alveolar pressure), and atelectrauma (recurrent alveolar collapse); because ARDS lung is heterogeneous, fully inflating consolidated regions can overdistend and injure more normal lung (Harrison 22e p.2302).","Routine glucocorticoids are NOT supported by current evidence for ARDS itself; recruitment maneuvers combined with higher baseline PEEP increased mortality in one study; routine early neuromuscular blockade is not beneficial in all moderate-to-severe ARDS (reserve for ventilatory dyssynchrony despite sedation); surfactant replacement and inhaled vasodilators (e.g., inhaled nitric oxide) do not improve survival (Harrison 22e p.2302, p.2303).","Pulse oximetry may overestimate oxygen saturation in patients with darker skin tones, so correlation with PaO2 should be considered when feasible (Harrison 22e p.2300)."],"severityClassification":"Berlin definition (2012): severity is graded by oxygenation (PaO2/FiO2 assessed on at least 5 cmH2O PEEP) - Mild: 200 mmHg < PaO2/FiO2 <=300 mmHg; Moderate: 100 mmHg < PaO2/FiO2 <=200 mmHg; Severe: PaO2/FiO2 <=100 mmHg. Onset must be acute (within 1 week of a clinical insult or new/worsening respiratory symptoms), chest imaging must show bilateral opacities consistent with pulmonary edema not fully explained by effusions, lobar/lung collapse, or nodules, and respiratory failure must not be primarily due to hydrostatic (cardiogenic) edema (objective evaluation such as echocardiography required if no ARDS risk factor present). Proposed 2023 updates extend criteria to nonintubated patients on high-flow nasal oxygen or noninvasive ventilation, add SpO2/FiO2 (S/F) ratios, permit ultrasound imaging, and allow application in resource-limited settings. A more recent global definition has also been proposed that does not rely on arterial blood gases, chest radiography, or PEEP (Harrison 22e p.2299, p.2300).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2299","Harrison 22e p.2300","Harrison 22e p.2301","Harrison 22e p.2302","Harrison 22e p.2303","Harrison 22e p.2304"],"crossLinks":["SEVERE_CAP","SEPTIC_SHOCK","pancreatitis"]},"arterial_and_venous_thrombosis":{"name":"Arterial and Venous Thrombosis","system":"Hematologic / Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Thrombosis","Venous thromboembolism","VTE","Arterial thrombosis","Pathologic thrombosis"],"chapter":122,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.937","clinicalPearls":["Thrombosis is essentially hemostasis occurring in the wrong place at the wrong time; the same processes that protect against hemorrhage can, under certain conditions, produce pathologic arterial or venous occlusion.","Hemostasis and thrombosis arise from interplay among three elements: the vessel wall, the coagulation/fibrinolytic proteins, and platelets.","Arterial thrombosis is dominated by platelet adhesion to an injured vessel wall and the state of the wall itself, whereas venous thrombosis is driven mainly by activation of the coagulation cascade in the setting of stasis or hypercoagulability.","Platelets are small anucleate cells (1-5 microns) circulating at roughly 200,000-400,000/microL with a 7-10 day lifespan, derived from marrow megakaryocytes under the control of thrombopoietin.","The platelet GPIb-IX-V complex binds exposed subendothelial von Willebrand factor to initiate adhesion, and signaling converts the GPIIb/IIIa integrin from a low-affinity to a high-affinity fibrinogen receptor that cross-links platelets into an aggregate.","Inflammation and infection transiently raise thrombotic risk: acute upper respiratory infection, bacterial and viral infections (including SARS-CoV-2, HIV, hepatitis C, dengue) are linked to increased myocardial infarction, stroke, and VTE.","Roughly 5-8% of the U.S. population carries a genetic risk factor predisposing to venous thrombosis, and multiple risk factors frequently coexist in a single patient.","Cancer confers an approximately fourfold increased risk of VTE compared with the general population, and VTE in cancer patients is associated with reduced survival.","Although factor V Leiden and the prothrombin G20210A mutation are established risk factors for DVT and PE, their contribution to arterial thrombotic events such as acute coronary syndromes is not well established.","Loss-of-function CYP2C19 polymorphisms are the strongest single determinant of reduced antiplatelet response to clopidogrel (a prodrug requiring hepatic activation) but account for only about 5-12% of the variability in ADP-induced platelet aggregation.","Antithrombin reactions governed by normal endothelium become pivotal in limiting clot extension into adjacent uninjured vessel once a protective clot has formed; antithrombin, tissue factor pathway inhibitor, heparin cofactor II, and protein C/protein S all restrain thrombin generation."],"pathophysiology":"Thrombus formation reflects dysregulated hemostasis involving the vessel wall, coagulation and fibrinolytic proteins, and platelets. In arterial thrombosis, vessel-wall damage exposes subendothelial collagen and von Willebrand factor; the platelet GPIb-IX-V complex mediates adhesion, platelets are activated through seven-transmembrane receptors (thrombin/PAR1, PAR4, ADP receptors P2Y1 and P2Y12, prostaglandin and lipid receptors), release thromboxane A2, serotonin, and ADP from granules, and undergo a calcium-dependent conformational change in GPIIb/IIIa that allows high-affinity fibrinogen binding and irreversible aggregation. Most arterial thrombi are superimposed on disrupted atherosclerotic plaque whose thrombogenic core triggers platelet-rich thrombus. In venous thrombosis, the coagulation cascade is initiated chiefly by exposure of tissue factor: factor VII/VIIa-tissue factor complex activates factor X (directly or via factor IX/VIIIa), FXa with cofactor FVa converts prothrombin to thrombin, thrombin converts fibrinogen to insoluble fibrin and activates FXIII to cross-link the clot. Amplification lets a small stimulus generate large amounts of fibrin. Venous thrombi typically originate in valve cusps of calf deep veins or muscular sinuses where sluggish flow lowers oxygen delivery, activating endothelial cells that express adhesion molecules; tissue-factor-bearing leukocytes and microvesicles adhere, neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs) provide a scaffold that binds and activates platelets and activates factor XII, and impaired clearance of activated clotting factors worsens local thrombosis. Hypercoagulability (heritable or acquired), stasis, and vascular dysfunction (Virchow's elements) underlie venous events. Inflammation links the two: platelet P-selectin binds leukocyte PSGL-1, induces monocyte tissue factor and CD40 ligand expression, and Toll-like receptors (TLR2/3/4, TLR7/8) connect infection to platelet-dependent thrombosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Atherosclerotic plaque rupture causing acute coronary syndrome","Embolism (cardiac source) versus in-situ thrombosis","Aortic or arterial dissection","Vasculitis causing arterial occlusion","Postthrombotic syndrome as sequela of DVT","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension after PE"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute limb ischemia from arterial embolism rather than local thrombosis","Superficial venous thrombophlebitis","Lymphedema or cellulitis mimicking DVT-related limb swelling","Extrinsic venous compression mimicking thrombotic obstruction"],"additionalInvestigations":["Thrombophilia evaluation (factor V Leiden, prothrombin G20210A, antithrombin, protein C, protein S deficiencies) in selected patients, recognizing many older genetic associations are being questioned in larger contemporary studies","Antiphospholipid antibody testing when autoimmune prothrombotic state suspected","Plasma homocysteine and MTHFR considerations as historically reported independent risk factors","Assessment of acquired risk factors: malignancy screening, recent surgery/trauma, pregnancy status, hormonal therapy use","Markers reflecting inflammation-thrombosis crosstalk (e.g., soluble CD40 ligand) noted as research-relevant"],"redFlags":["Sudden death from pulmonary embolism (a substantial fraction of new VTE cases die early, with high 30-day mortality)","PE in the setting of cancer carries roughly a 25% fatality rate","Homozygous protein C or protein S deficiency can produce fatal purpura fulminans","Coagulation abnormalities and increased PE/DVT/peripheral thrombosis in hospitalized COVID-19 patients (noted in nearly 20%)","Calf-vein thrombus extension into popliteal and proximal veins, where fragments can embolize to the lungs"],"prognosis":"Thrombosis is a major cause of morbidity and mortality. Coronary disease and stroke account for a large share of deaths; arterial thrombosis underlies most acute MI, ischemic stroke, and limb gangrene. For VTE, up to 30% of patients die within 30 days, about one-fifth experience sudden death from PE, and roughly 30% develop recurrent VTE within 10 years. The ARIC study reported a 9% 28-day fatality from DVT and 15% from PE; cancer-associated PE carries about 25% fatality. Incidence of first DVT rises sharply with age (from about 2-3 per 10,000 person-years at 30-49 years to about 20 per 10,000 at 70-79 years). Increasing age, obesity, malignancy, and acute extremity paresis independently predict recurrence.","pitfalls":["Assuming hypercoagulable (thrombophilic) states that drive venous thrombosis are equally important for arterial events; their role in acute coronary syndromes is poorly defined and often not clinically significant.","Genotyping to optimize aspirin therapy is not currently clinically indicated, as ex vivo studies have not firmly established a pharmacogenetic interaction between aspirin and COX1.","Over-relying on CYP2C19 genotype for clopidogrel decisions, since loss-of-function alleles explain only a small fraction (about 5-12%) of antiplatelet response variability and genetics do not appreciably affect outcomes with prasugrel or ticagrelor.","Forgetting that up to 10% of venous thromboembolic events occur outside the lower extremities and lungs.","Treating many older single-gene thrombophilia associations as definitive when larger contemporary studies have called several into question."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.937","Harrison 22e p.938","Harrison 22e p.939","Harrison 22e p.940","Harrison 22e p.941"],"crossLinks":[]},"arterial_diseases_of_the_extremities":{"name":"Arterial Diseases of the Extremities","system":"Cardiovascular (peripheral vascular)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Peripheral artery disease","PAD","Peripheral arterial disease","Intermittent claudication","Chronic limb-threatening ischemia","Acute limb ischemia"],"chapter":292,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2172-2178","clinicalPearls":["Peripheral artery disease (PAD) means stenosis or occlusion of the aorta or limb arteries; atherosclerosis is the dominant cause in patients over 40, while thrombosis, embolism, vasculitis, fibromuscular dysplasia, entrapment, cystic adventitial disease, and trauma account for the remainder.","Risk factors mirror those for coronary and cerebral atherosclerosis: cigarette smoking, diabetes mellitus, hypercholesterolemia, elevated lipoprotein(a), hypertension, and renal insufficiency; prevalence is similar between sexes but greater in Black than non-Hispanic white persons and in lower socioeconomic groups.","More than half of PAD patients are asymptomatic; the classic symptom is intermittent claudication (pain, ache, cramp, numbness, or fatigue in muscle that appears with exertion and resolves with rest), and the symptomatic muscle group lies distal to the obstructing lesion (e.g., buttock/hip/thigh with aortoiliac disease, calf with femoral-popliteal disease).","Chronic limb-threatening ischemia produces rest pain or a cold/numb foot that worsens at night when legs are horizontal and improves with dependency; signs include hair loss, thickened nails, shiny atrophic skin, coolness, pallor or cyanosis, and ultimately ulcers or gangrene.","Ankle-brachial index (ABI) is the key noninvasive test: normal 1.00-1.40, borderline 0.91-0.99, and abnormal/diagnostic of PAD at 0.90 or below; values above 1.40 indicate noncompressible, calcified vessels (as in diabetes or chronic kidney disease).","When the ABI is unreliable because of medial calcification, a toe-brachial index of 0.70 or below is considered abnormal and supports the diagnosis.","A drop in ABI immediately after treadmill exercise helps confirm PAD when resting findings are equivocal; MRA, CTA, and catheter angiography are reserved for revascularization planning, not routine diagnosis.","Fibromuscular dysplasia is a non-inflammatory hyperplastic arteriopathy of medium and small (sometimes large) arteries, predominant in women, classically renal and carotid/vertebral, with a multifocal 'string of beads' or focal pattern, and can cause stenosis, dissection, aneurysm, or thrombosis.","Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease) is an inflammatory occlusive disorder of distal small and medium arteries and veins, strongly linked to tobacco, typically in men under 40, presenting with a triad of distal claudication, Raynaud phenomenon, and migratory superficial thrombophlebitis; brachial and popliteal pulses are normal while distal pulses are reduced or absent.","Acute limb ischemia is sudden cessation of limb blood flow most often from cardiac embolism (atrial fibrillation, MI, ventricular aneurysm, endocarditis, prosthetic valves, atrial myxoma), in-situ thrombosis, dissection, or trauma; emboli lodge at bifurcations, most commonly the femoral artery in the leg.","Atheroembolism (cholesterol/fibrin/platelet debris from proximal plaque or aneurysm) classically spares large vessels, so distal pulses remain palpable; it causes the painful 'blue toe' syndrome, livedo reticularis, and may show cholesterol crystals on skin or muscle biopsy.","Raynaud phenomenon is episodic digital ischemia with sequential pallor (vasospasm), cyanosis (deoxygenated blood in dilated capillaries), and rubor (reactive hyperemia) after cold or emotional stress; over half of cases are primary/idiopathic, with women affected roughly five times more often and onset typically between ages 20 and 40."],"pathophysiology":"In atherosclerotic PAD, segmental plaques with calcium deposition, medial thinning, patchy loss of muscle and elastic fibers, fragmentation of the internal elastic lamina, and platelet-fibrin thrombi narrow large and medium vessels, preferentially at branch points where turbulence, altered shear stress, and intimal injury occur. Principal sites are the abdominal aorta/iliac arteries (about 30% of symptomatic patients), femoral/popliteal arteries (80-90%), and tibial/peroneal vessels (40-50%); distal disease predominates in the elderly and in diabetics. Hemodynamically significant stenoses lower distal systolic pressure (reflected in a reduced ABI). During exercise, vasodilation distal to a stenosis drops perfusion pressure below interstitial tissue pressure generated by working muscle, producing claudication. Buerger disease shows early neutrophilic infiltration of vessel walls with a preserved internal elastic lamina and a cellular inflammatory luminal thrombus, evolving to mononuclear/giant-cell infiltration, then perivascular fibrosis, organized thrombus, and recanalization. Raynaud phenomenon reflects digital arterial vasospasm; inherited erythromelalgia involves gain-of-function SCN9A (Nav1.7 sodium channel) mutations in sensory/sympathetic nerves.","additionalDifferentials":["Atherosclerotic peripheral artery disease","Fibromuscular dysplasia","Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease)","Takayasu arteritis and giant cell (temporal) arteritis","Acute limb ischemia from embolism or in-situ thrombosis","Atheroembolism / cholesterol embolization","Thoracic outlet compression syndrome (arterial, venous, neurogenic)","Popliteal artery entrapment","Popliteal artery aneurysm","Arteriovenous fistula (congenital or acquired)","Raynaud phenomenon (primary and secondary)","Acrocyanosis","Livedo reticularis / livedo racemosa","Pernio (chilblains)","Erythromelalgia","Frostbite"],"infectionMimics":["Infective endocarditis as an embolic source producing acute limb ischemia and digital infarcts"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Erythromelalgia (warm, erythematous, painful extremity with preserved pulses, opposite trigger to ischemia)","Acrocyanosis (persistent, non-episodic cyanosis with normal pulses, no blanching, distinguishing it from Raynaud)","Atheroembolic 'blue toe' confined to one or two digits, not to be confused with Raynaud phenomenon","Spinal/neurogenic causes (intervertebral disk disease, syringomyelia, complex regional pain syndrome) mimicking vasospasm","Myeloproliferative disorders and hyperviscosity states presenting with digital ischemia or erythromelalgia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Ankle-brachial index (ABI) with Doppler at dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial arteries","Toe-brachial index (TBI) when arteries are noncompressible","Segmental limb pressures and pulse volume recordings to localize stenoses","Duplex ultrasonography of native arteries and bypass grafts","Treadmill exercise testing with post-exercise ABI","Transcutaneous oximetry","MRA, CTA, or catheter angiography for revascularization planning","Nailfold capillaroscopy in Raynaud evaluation (normal hairpin loops in primary disease)","Excisional vessel biopsy with pathology to confirm thromboangiitis obliterans","Skin or muscle biopsy showing cholesterol crystals in atheroembolism","Chest x-ray for cervical rib in thoracic outlet syndrome; provocative maneuvers (abduction/external rotation, scalene, costoclavicular, hyperabduction)","Serologic and hematologic workup for secondary Raynaud (connective tissue disease, cold agglutinins, cryoglobulins, cryofibrinogen)"],"redFlags":["Rest pain, ulceration, or gangrene indicating chronic limb-threatening ischemia","Sudden severe pain, paresthesia, numbness, coldness, pallor, and pulselessness within an hour signaling acute limb ischemia","Paralysis, loss of sensation, and absent Doppler signal in both arteries and veins, marking a nonviable limb requiring amputation","Blue toe syndrome with progressive digital necrosis suggesting ongoing atheroembolism","Ischemic fingertip ulcers progressing to gangrene and autoamputation in scleroderma-associated Raynaud","Subclavian/axillary vein thrombosis (Paget-Schroetter) in venous thoracic outlet syndrome","High-output cardiac failure and cardiomegaly from a large arteriovenous fistula"],"prognosis":"PAD natural history is driven largely by coexisting coronary and cerebrovascular disease: roughly one-third to one-half of symptomatic patients have clinical/ECG evidence of CAD and over half have significant disease on coronary angiography. PAD carries a 15-25% 5-year mortality and a two- to fourfold increased risk of cardiovascular death, with the highest mortality in those with most severe disease. Over 5 years the ABI worsens in nearly 40% and symptoms progress in about 20-25%; roughly 11% of symptomatic patients ultimately develop chronic limb-threatening ischemia, and without revascularization about 25% of those with chronic limb-threatening ischemia undergo amputation within a year. Prognosis is worse in continued smokers and diabetics. In thromboangiitis obliterans the outlook is worse in continued smokers and remains discouraging even after cessation. Primary Raynaud disease is generally mild, with fewer than 1% losing part of a digit, spontaneous improvement in about 15%, and progression in about 30%; primary acrocyanosis and benign livedo reticularis have favorable outcomes without ulceration or gangrene.","pitfalls":["Relying on the ABI in calcified, noncompressible vessels (ABI above 1.40) where it falsely appears normal or high; use the toe-brachial index instead.","Ordering routine MRA/CTA/angiography for diagnosis when history, examination, and ABI usually suffice and imaging is meant for revascularization planning.","Confusing atheroembolic digital ischemia (often one or two digits, palpable distal pulses) with Raynaud phenomenon.","Mistaking acrocyanosis for Raynaud phenomenon; acrocyanosis is persistent rather than episodic, extends proximally from the digits, and lacks a blanching phase.","Missing the strong tobacco link in Buerger disease and overlooking it in young male smokers presenting with distal ischemia and Raynaud features.","Overlooking secondary causes of Raynaud (scleroderma, SLE, blood dyscrasias, drugs, vibration injury) and labeling the disease primary prematurely.","Failing to perform provocative maneuvers in suspected thoracic outlet or popliteal entrapment syndromes, where the resting examination is often normal.","Applying massage, ice water, or extreme heat to frostbite, which are contraindicated; rewarming should use a 40-44 C water bath."],"severityClassification":"PAD severity is graded hemodynamically by ABI: normal 1.00-1.40, borderline 0.91-0.99, abnormal/diagnostic at 0.90 or below, and noncompressible above 1.40 (toe-brachial index 0.70 or below abnormal). Clinically the spectrum runs from asymptomatic disease, to intermittent claudication, to chronic limb-threatening ischemia (rest pain, ulceration, gangrene). Acute limb ischemia is assessed for limb viability by presence of sensation, motor function, and Doppler-detectable arterial and venous flow; loss of all three defines a nonviable limb.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2172","Harrison 22e p.2173","Harrison 22e p.2174","Harrison 22e p.2175","Harrison 22e p.2176","Harrison 22e p.2177","Harrison 22e p.2178"],"crossLinks":[]},"arteriovenous_fistula":{"name":"Arteriovenous fistula","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AV fistula","AVF","arteriovenous malformation","acquired arteriovenous fistula","congenital arteriovenous fistula"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An abnormal direct communication between an artery and a vein that bypasses the capillary bed","May be congenital, acquired through trauma or iatrogenic injury, or surgically created for haemodialysis access","Classic local signs include a palpable thrill and a continuous machinery-like bruit over the lesion","Compression of the fistula can transiently slow the heart rate as venous return and cardiac output fall (Branham or Nicoladoni-Branham sign)","Large or longstanding fistulae shunt blood away from distal tissue and can cause limb ischaemia, venous hypertension, and swelling","High-flow shunting increases venous return and cardiac output and, if substantial, can precipitate high-output cardiac failure","Duplex ultrasound, CT or MR angiography, and catheter angiography characterise the anatomy and haemodynamic impact","Treatment options include observation, endovascular embolisation or stent grafting, and open surgical repair depending on cause, size, and symptoms"],"pathophysiology":"An arteriovenous fistula creates a low-resistance channel that diverts arterial blood directly into the venous system, bypassing the capillary bed. This produces a steal effect that can reduce distal arterial perfusion, while transmission of arterial pressure into the veins causes venous hypertension, dilatation, and secondary tissue changes. The reduced systemic vascular resistance increases venous return, stroke volume, and cardiac output; a large chronic shunt can lead to cardiac chamber enlargement and high-output heart failure. Congenital lesions reflect abnormal vascular development, whereas acquired fistulae follow penetrating trauma, procedures, or erosion between an adjacent artery and vein.","additionalDifferentials":["Pseudoaneurysm","True arterial aneurysm","Venous malformation or haemangioma","Deep vein thrombosis with limb swelling","Varicose veins and chronic venous insufficiency","Soft-tissue vascular tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Duplex ultrasound to demonstrate the communication and flow characteristics","CT or MR angiography to map anatomy and feeding and draining vessels","Catheter angiography for detailed assessment and to guide endovascular treatment","Echocardiography to assess cardiac output and chamber size in high-flow lesions","Assessment of distal perfusion and venous hypertension in the affected limb"],"redFlags":["Features of high-output cardiac failure from a large chronic shunt","Distal limb ischaemia from arterial steal threatening tissue viability","Rapid enlargement, haemorrhage, or an expanding pulsatile mass suggesting associated pseudoaneurysm","Severe venous hypertension with skin breakdown or ulceration"],"prognosis":"Small fistulae may remain asymptomatic and be safely observed, while large or high-flow lesions can progressively cause limb complications or cardiac strain and generally require intervention. Outcomes after embolisation or surgical repair are favourable when the lesion is anatomically suitable, though congenital malformations can recur.","pitfalls":["Missing an acquired fistula after penetrating trauma or vascular procedures","Overlooking high-output cardiac failure as a consequence of a large chronic shunt","Confusing a fistula with a pseudoaneurysm and choosing the wrong treatment","Underestimating distal ischaemia caused by arterial steal"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"arteriovenous_malformation_brain":{"name":"Cerebral arteriovenous malformation","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AVM","brain AVM","cerebral AVM","intracranial arteriovenous malformation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A brain arteriovenous malformation is an abnormal tangle (nidus) of vessels where arteries connect directly to veins without an intervening capillary bed","The commonest serious presentation is intracranial haemorrhage, which may be intracerebral, intraventricular, or subarachnoid","Seizures are another frequent presentation, and some patients report chronic headache or progressive focal deficits","The high-flow shunt can cause a steal effect and venous hypertension, contributing to symptoms even without bleeding","Digital subtraction angiography is the reference standard for defining the feeding arteries, nidus, and draining veins, complementing CT and MRI","Grading systems that account for size, venous drainage pattern, and eloquence of adjacent brain help estimate surgical risk","Treatment options include microsurgical resection, endovascular embolisation, and stereotactic radiosurgery, used alone or in combination and individualised to the lesion","An associated flow-related aneurysm may coexist and can itself be a source of haemorrhage"],"pathophysiology":"A cerebral arteriovenous malformation is a congenital vascular anomaly in which feeding arteries drain directly into veins through a nidus of abnormal channels, bypassing the normal capillary network. The absence of intervening capillaries exposes the draining veins to high arterial pressures and flow, causing vessel wall changes and predisposing to rupture. Diversion of blood through the low-resistance shunt can reduce perfusion of surrounding brain (vascular steal), while venous hypertension and gliosis contribute to seizures and focal deficits. Haemorrhage results from rupture of the fragile nidus, draining veins, or associated aneurysms.","additionalDifferentials":["Cerebral aneurysm with subarachnoid haemorrhage","Hypertensive intracerebral haemorrhage","Cavernous malformation","Dural arteriovenous fistula","Neoplasm with haemorrhage","Primary seizure disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT and CT angiography to detect haemorrhage and vascular abnormality","MRI and MR angiography to localise the nidus and assess surrounding brain","Digital subtraction angiography to fully characterise angioarchitecture and any associated aneurysm","EEG when seizures are a prominent feature"],"redFlags":["Acute intracranial haemorrhage with headache, focal deficit, or reduced consciousness","New or worsening seizures, especially with focal neurological signs","Evidence of an associated flow-related aneurysm raising rupture risk","Signs of raised intracranial pressure or rapid neurological deterioration"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on whether the malformation has bled, its size and location, and its venous drainage; haemorrhage carries significant morbidity and a risk of recurrent bleeding, whereas complete obliteration by surgery, radiosurgery, or embolisation can substantially reduce future haemorrhage risk.","pitfalls":["Assuming all subarachnoid haemorrhage is aneurysmal and overlooking an underlying malformation","Underestimating the risk contributed by an associated aneurysm","Failing to fully characterise angioarchitecture before choosing treatment","Attributing new seizures or deficits to other causes without vascular imaging"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"arthritis_associated_with_systemic_disease":{"name":"Arthritis Associated with Systemic Disease, and Other Arthritides","system":"Musculoskeletal/Rheumatologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Acromegalic arthropathy","Hemochromatosis arthropathy","Hemophilic arthropathy","Sickle cell arthropathy","Thalassemic arthropathy","Charcot joint","Neuropathic joint disease","Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy","Pachydermoperiostitis"],"chapter":386,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2966","clinicalPearls":["Acromegaly drives an osteoarthritis-like picture from growth hormone/IGF-I overstimulation of cartilage and bone; prominent wrist involvement (unusual for primary OA), early radiographic joint-space WIDENING from cartilage hypertrophy, carpal tunnel syndrome in roughly half, and Raynaud phenomenon in about a third.","In hemochromatosis, arthropathy may be the FIRST clinical clue: degenerative-type involvement that characteristically starts at the second and third metacarpophalangeal joints (joints generally spared by routine OA), often with hooklike MCP osteophytes and frequent calcium pyrophosphate deposition.","An early hemochromatosis diagnosis is suggested by a high serum transferrin saturation, which is more sensitive than ferritin elevation; dominant 2nd/3rd MCP findings warrant ferritin and iron/total iron-binding capacity testing.","Hemophilic recurrent spontaneous hemarthrosis (factor VIII or IX deficiency) typically begins after age 1 when the child walks/runs and targets knees, ankles, elbows, shoulders, and hips, progressing to chronic fibrotic deforming arthropathy.","Hemarthrosis is characteristic of hemophilia but NOT of von Willebrand disease, factor V deficiency, oral anticoagulation, or thrombocytopenia.","Low-grade temperature can accompany acute hemarthrosis, but fever above 38.3 C (101 F) should raise concern for joint infection and prompt aspiration; clotting factor should be corrected before arthrocentesis.","Sickle cell dactylitis (hand-foot syndrome) in children under 5 produces diffuse swelling/tenderness of hands and feet from marrow and cortical bone infarction and is rarely seen after age 5; it generally resolves without residual damage.","Distinguishing osteomyelitis from bone infarction in sickle cell disease is clinically difficult; Salmonella is a notably associated cause of osteomyelitis, whereas S. aureus and Streptococcus are the more common organisms in sickle cell septic arthritis.","Up to ~40% of sickle cell patients are hyperuricemic (urate overproduction plus interstitial renal disease) and can develop acute, sometimes polyarticular, gouty arthritis; arthrocentesis distinguishes gout from infection or synovial infarction.","Achilles tendinitis and migratory large-joint arthritis can be the first clinical sign of familial hypercholesterolemia, preceding the appearance of tendinous xanthomas; synovial fluid is noninflammatory and crystal-negative.","Neuropathic (Charcot) joint disease arises from loss of pain sensation and/or proprioception with impaired protective reflexes; diabetes mellitus is the most frequent cause, classically affecting the tarsal and tarsometatarsal joints.","Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy combines digital clubbing with periosteal new-bone formation; secondary HOA points to intrathoracic malignancy, suppurative/hypoxemic lung disease, congenital heart disease, or hepatopulmonary disease and may precede the underlying illness."],"pathophysiology":"These arthritides share the theme of an underlying systemic disorder injuring joints rather than a primary autoimmune synovitis. In acromegaly, excess growth hormone and IGF-I stimulate cartilage, periarticular connective tissue, and bone, producing hypertrophic then degenerating cartilage that is prone to fissuring, with ligamentous laxity worsening joint discomfort. In hemochromatosis, parenchymal iron deposition damages cartilage: iron catalyzes superoxide-dependent lipid peroxidation, interferes with collagen formation, increases lysosomal enzyme release from synovium, and inhibits synovial pyrophosphatase, favoring chondrocalcinosis. Hemophilic arthropathy results from repeated intraarticular bleeding; blood persists because intrinsic clotting factors and tissue thromboplastin are absent in the synovium, and recurrent hemarthrosis leads to chronic noninflammatory fibrotic joints. Sickle cell musculoskeletal disease stems from vasoocclusion and marrow/cortical bone infarction, splenic dysfunction predisposing to osteomyelitis and septic arthritis, and avascular necrosis (notably femoral head); marrow hyperplasia widens medullary cavities and thins cortices. In neuropathic joints, loss of deep pain/proprioception plus dysregulated autonomic blood flow leads to bone resorption, repeated microtrauma, ligament tears, and fractures with progressive disorganization. HOA pathogenesis centers on circulating cells/platelet particles and growth factors (PDGF, VEGF) bypassing pulmonary clearance and stimulating peripheral connective tissue and periosteal proliferation, with endothelial activation reflected by elevated von Willebrand factor antigen.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary osteoarthritis (vs acromegalic and hemochromatotic arthropathy, distinguished by atypical joint distribution such as wrist or 2nd/3rd MCP involvement)","Calcium pyrophosphate deposition disease / pseudogout (frequent in acromegaly and hemochromatosis)","Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis (spine changes in acromegaly can mimic it)","Gout (sickle cell hyperuricemia; statin/niacin-associated)","Avascular necrosis","Osteomyelitis (especially diabetic neuropathic foot and sickle cell disease)","Stress fracture (neuropathic joint differential)","Systemic lupus erythematosus (transfused thalassemia patients may have circulating ANA without SLE)"],"infectionMimics":["Septic arthritis (difficult to distinguish from acute hemarthrosis in hemophilia and from sickle cell joint effusions)","Osteomyelitis (mimics neuropathic arthritis in the diabetic foot and complicates sickle cell disease)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute gout/pseudogout flare (hyperlipidemia-associated migratory arthritis can resemble gout)","Advanced osteoarthritis (overlaps radiographically with neuropathic arthritis)","Sterile synovial infarction in sickle cell crisis (can give a high-neutrophil effusion mimicking infection)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum transferrin saturation (sensitive early marker) plus ferritin and iron/total iron-binding capacity for suspected hemochromatosis","Synovial fluid analysis for cell count and crystals (noninflammatory in most of these arthropathies; xanthochromic/bloody with synovial, cartilage, bone fragments in neuropathic joints)","Clotting factor assays and correction prior to arthrocentesis in hemophilia","MRI (sensitive for early avascular necrosis and helpful in osteomyelitis vs neuropathic joint)","Indium-111-labeled WBC nuclear scan (may show uptake in osteomyelitis but not neuropathic joint; technetium bone scan does NOT discriminate the two)","Anti-HMGCR autoantibody testing in patients with elevated muscle enzymes on statin therapy","Joint/tissue cultures when osteomyelitis cannot be excluded radiographically"],"redFlags":["Fever above 38.3 C (101 F) with an acute hemarthrosis in hemophilia, suggesting joint infection","Femoral neuropathy from iliopsoas hematoma in hemophilia (hip held in flexion with preserved rotation)","Closed-compartment hemorrhage (calf or volar forearm) causing muscle necrosis, neuropathy, and flexion contractures","Diabetic foot with callus/ulcer over metatarsal heads progressing to infected ulcer and osteomyelitis","Rapidly disorganizing (Charcot) joint developing over weeks with pain disproportionately mild for the degree of destruction","New digital clubbing or HOA, which may signal occult intrathoracic malignancy","Statin-associated rising muscle enzymes raising concern for necrotizing autoimmune myopathy"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are determined largely by the underlying disorder. Acromegalic joint pain may persist even after the acromegaly is treated. In hemochromatosis, established arthritis and chondrocalcinosis often progress despite phlebotomy. Hemophilic hemarthrosis usually resolves with joint function returning to baseline over about two weeks, but recurrent bleeding causes chronic fibrotic, flexion-deformed, function-limiting arthropathy. Sickle cell dactylitis resolves without residual damage, but avascular necrosis (about 5% femoral head involvement) and osteomyelitis carry greater morbidity. Neuropathic joints can disorganize rapidly and are largely unaffected by treating the underlying disease; early recognition and offloading of a diabetic Charcot foot can prevent severe disease. In primary HOA, symptoms typically disappear on reaching adulthood.","pitfalls":["Attributing acromegalic or hemochromatotic arthropathy to ordinary osteoarthritis and missing the atypical joint distribution (wrist; 2nd/3rd MCP) that should prompt endocrine or iron-overload workup.","Relying on ferritin alone for early hemochromatosis when transferrin saturation is the more sensitive marker.","Mistaking acute hemarthrosis for septic arthritis (or vice versa) in hemophilia; failing to correct clotting factor before aspirating.","Aspirating a hemophilic intramuscular cyst, which can provoke further bleeding (relatively contraindicated unless secondarily infected, and then only after factor repletion).","Using a technetium bone scan to try to separate osteomyelitis from neuropathic arthritis, since uptake is increased in both.","Overlooking that nonselective NSAIDs impair platelet function and should be avoided where feasible in hemophilic bleeding.","Treating isolated clubbing as benign and missing an associated malignancy or lung/cardiac disease."],"severityClassification":"No single formal grading scheme; severity is judged by the staging of the underlying arthropathy. Hemophilic arthropathy progresses radiographically from capsular distention, to juxtaarticular osteopenia with marginal erosions and subchondral cysts, to late joint-space narrowing with osteoarthritis-like bony overgrowth. Neuropathic arthritis evolves from early osteoarthritis-like changes (joint-space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, osteophytes, effusions) to marked destructive/hypertrophic disorganization. HOA progresses from periostitis with a radiolucent zone between new periosteal bone and cortex to multilayered cortical thickening.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2966","Harrison 22e p.2967","Harrison 22e p.2968","Harrison 22e p.2969","Harrison 22e p.2970"],"crossLinks":[]},"arthropod_borne_and_rodent_borne":{"name":"Arthropod-Borne and Rodent-Borne Virus Infections","system":"Infectious / Multisystem","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Arboviral infections","Arbovirus infections","Zoonotic arthropod-borne and rodent-borne viral diseases"],"chapter":215,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1655","clinicalPearls":["Most arthropod-borne (arbovirus) and rodent-borne zoonotic viral infections in humans are subclinical or unrecognized; humans are usually accidental, dead-end hosts and are not important amplifiers of these viruses (Harrison 22e p.1664-1665).","Recognized illness usually falls into one of five overlapping syndromic categories: fever and myalgia (F&M), arthritis and rash (A&R), encephalitis, pulmonary disease, and viral hemorrhagic fever (VHF); a single virus may cause several of these (Harrison 22e p.1664-1665).","Fever and myalgia is by far the most common and most benign syndrome, with abrupt fever, chills, intense myalgia, malaise, anorexia, and headache with photophobia/retroorbital pain; true arthritis is absent and physical findings are minimal (Harrison 22e p.1665-1666).","A history of tick bite has more diagnostic significance than a history of mosquito exposure; clinical and epidemiologic clues may allow presumptive diagnosis in epidemics, but laboratory confirmation is required in all cases (Harrison 22e p.1664).","Arthropod vectors acquire arboviruses from viremic vertebrate blood meals; the virus must disseminate to the salivary glands (extrinsic incubation, typically 1-3 weeks in mosquitoes) before the vector can transmit; transovarial transmission to progeny is an alternative maintenance mechanism (Harrison 22e p.1664).","Rodent-borne viruses are maintained by chronically infected rodents; humans are typically infected by inhalation of or contact with aerosolized rodent excreta/secreta, and most show a high degree of rodent-virus specificity (Harrison 22e p.1664).","Lymphocytic choriomeningitis virus (LCMV) is acquired from the house mouse and classically causes a biphasic illness with a febrile phase followed by a CNS (meningeal) phase; patients with neuroinvasive LCM, including frank encephalitis, almost always recover fully, but the virus can cause fetal microcephaly and has been transmitted by organ transplantation (Harrison 22e p.1666-1667).","Hantavirus pulmonary syndrome (HPS) begins after a 2-3 week incubation with a nonspecific 3-4 day prodrome, then rapidly progresses to noncardiogenic pulmonary edema and hypoxemic respiratory failure; coincident thrombocytopenia, circulating immunoblasts (atypical lymphocytes), and a left shift are considered almost diagnostic (Harrison 22e p.1673).","In suspected HPS, aggressive IV fluid resuscitation is hazardous because of widespread capillary leak and depressed cardiac output; management is hemodynamic/respiratory support, with ECMO for the most critically ill (Harrison 22e p.1673).","Lassa fever: most Lassa virus infections are subclinical or mild, onset is gradual (characteristic of mammarenavirus VHFs), and overt hemorrhage occurs in only about 15-30% of patients; about 30% of survivors develop sensorineural deafness (Harrison 22e p.1675-1676).","Hemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome (HFRS) is the most significant VHF today, with over 100,000 severe cases annually in Asia; major agents are Hantaan (Eastern Asia), Dobrava (the Balkans), and Puumala (Europe) (Harrison 22e p.1676).","Arboviral CNS disease can leave residual deficits; spinal/motor-neuron (poliomyelitis-like) disease has been documented after West Nile virus and Japanese encephalitis virus infection, and difficulty concentrating, fatigability, tremor, and personality change are common during recovery (Harrison 22e p.1671)."],"pathophysiology":"These taxonomically diverse zoonotic RNA viruses (Togaviridae/Alphavirus; Flaviviridae/Orthoflavivirus; the bunyaviricete families Arenaviridae, Hantaviridae, Nairoviridae, Peribunyaviridae, Phenuiviridae; Orthomyxoviridae; Bornaviridae and Rhabdoviridae; and the reovirus families) circulate in nature between arthropod vectors (mosquitoes, ticks, sandflies, midges) or chronically infected rodents and vertebrate reservoirs. Arboviruses establish chronic systemic infection of the vector after a blood meal and reach the salivary glands during extrinsic incubation (1-3 weeks in mosquitoes); vertical transovarial transmission also maintains some viruses. In arboviral encephalitis, an initial lymphoid-origin viremia seeds multifocal CNS entry, presumably via olfactory neuroepithelium/cribriform plate or brain capillary endothelium; CNS injury results partly from direct neuronal infection and partly from edema, inflammation, and host immune response, with focal neuronal necrosis, glial nodules, and perivascular lymphoid cuffing. LCM meningitis is T-cell-mediated immunopathology. VHF agents share a core pathophysiology of disrupted vascular function, stability, and integrity producing multisystem organ dysfunction and capillary leak; in HPS, widespread pulmonary capillary leak with depressed cardiac output produces noncardiogenic pulmonary edema and shock.","additionalDifferentials":["Malaria and other community-acquired parasitic infections","Anicteric leptospirosis","Rickettsial diseases (e.g., spotted fevers, Q fever)","Influenza and other respiratory viral infections","Bacterial meningitis and herpes simplex meningoencephalitis (for the encephalitis syndrome)","Undifferentiated sepsis / severe acute respiratory infection (for HPS)","Meningococcemia, septicemic plague, tularemia (for HPS)","More recently recognized viral encephalitides such as Nipah virus infection"],"infectionMimics":["Herpes simplex meningoencephalitis","Bacterial meningitis","Leptospirosis (including neuroleptospirosis)","Neurosyphilis","Lyme disease / Lyme neuroborreliosis","Cat-scratch disease","Rickettsial disease and Q fever","Legionella, Mycoplasma atypical pneumonia (HPS mimics)","Opportunistic CNS infections in immunocompromised hosts"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Anti-NMDA receptor and other autoimmune/paraneoplastic encephalitides","Metabolic encephalopathy (hyperammonemia, liver failure)","Toxic/intoxication states","Brain abscess and cerebrovascular events (stroke)","Oncologic CNS disease","Surgical abdomen / acute pyelonephritis (mimicking the febrile prodrome of HPS)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and CSF IgM (capture) ELISA against suspected virus","Plaque-reduction neutralization testing on acute and convalescent sera","RT-PCR for viral nucleic acid (blood early in encephalitis/F&M; CSF; tissue, thrombus, or autopsy specimens in HPS)","Metagenomic / high-throughput sequencing of CSF for arboviral encephalitis","CSF analysis (typically modest WBC elevation, early PMN then monocyte predominance, usually normal glucose; lymphocyte counts in the thousands with low glucose in LCM)","CBC for thrombocytopenia, leukopenia or leukocytosis with left shift, and circulating atypical lymphocytes/immunoblasts (HPS)","Hematocrit/hemoconcentration, serum albumin, lactate, and arterial blood gas in suspected HPS","Renal panel (creatinine, potassium), liver transaminases (AST/ALT), and urinalysis for proteinuria in VHF","Chest radiography for interstitial then bilateral central alveolar edema with normal cardiac size in HPS"],"redFlags":["Altered mental status, seizures, focal neurologic signs, or coma indicating neuroinvasive disease","New dry cough, tachypnea, hypoxemia, and early pulmonary edema heralding the HPS cardiopulmonary phase","Hypotension, hemoconcentration, and marked thrombocytopenia with left shift","Mucosal/overt hemorrhage, marked thrombocytopenia, and CNS dysfunction in suspected VHF","CNS features, facial/neck edema, hypotension, hemorrhage, or jaundice in Lassa fever (markers of fatal outcome)","Pregnancy, especially the last trimester, in Lassa fever (very high case fatality and ~90% fetal death)","Oliguria and rising creatinine signaling acute kidney injury in HPS/VHF","Marked confusion, tremor, and cerebellar signs in South American mammarenavirus hemorrhagic fevers"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on syndrome and specific virus. The F&M syndrome generally resolves completely within 2-5 days, though prolonged asthenia may follow LCMV or dengue infection. A&R illness resolves over days, but joint pain/arthritis may persist for months to years without deformity. Encephalitis ranges from full recovery (often slow, over weeks to months) to permanent deficits or death. HPS carries a case-fatality rate of approximately 30-40% even with appropriate care, though most patients surviving the first 48 hours of hospitalization recover without long-term sequelae. Argentinian and Bolivian hemorrhagic fevers can reach case-fatality rates over 50% in individual outbreaks. About 20% of Lassa virus infections become Lassa fever; survivors have roughly a 30% rate of (usually bilateral) sensorineural deafness, and pregnancy markedly worsens prognosis.","pitfalls":["Anchoring on the most severe known phenotype of a virus; many agents most commonly cause mild F&M rather than their eponymous severe disease (e.g., West Nile, Venezuelan equine encephalitis, Rift Valley fever).","Treating the F&M syndrome as benign and self-limited without watching for evolution into A&R, neuroinvasive, pulmonary, or VHF syndromes for which it can be a prodrome.","Using acetylsalicylic acid (aspirin) in these febrile illnesses, given the risk of exacerbated bleeding and Reye syndrome.","Giving aggressive IV fluids in HPS, which worsens noncardiogenic pulmonary edema because of capillary leak and depressed cardiac output.","Relying on a negative CSF RT-PCR to exclude arboviral encephalitis; viruses generally cannot be isolated from blood or CSF and RT-PCR cannot rule out the diagnosis.","Misreading the gradual onset of mammarenavirus VHF (Lassa, Argentinian/Bolivian) and expecting prominent early hemorrhage, which is often absent or late.","Treating a single positive heterotypic IgG cross-reaction as diagnostic in HPS serology given very low background seroprevalence.","Overlooking person-to-person/nosocomial transmission risk (Lassa virus, Andes virus, mammarenaviruses) and sexual transmission during early convalescence."],"severityClassification":"No single unified severity scale; severity is framed by syndrome. The five-syndrome heuristic (F&M, arthritis and rash, encephalitis, pulmonary disease, viral hemorrhagic fever) stratifies expected severity, with F&M most benign and encephalitis/pulmonary/VHF most severe. Encephalitis severity grades clinically from asymptomatic through febrile headache and aseptic meningitis to full encephalitis with coma. HPS is staged by progression from nonspecific prodrome to the cardiopulmonary phase with respiratory failure and shock. Lassa fever severity/fatality is predicted by high viral load, high serum creatinine and AST, leukocytosis, thrombocytopenia, CNS features, face/neck swelling, hypotension, hemorrhage, jaundice, and pregnancy.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1655","Harrison 22e p.1656","Harrison 22e p.1664","Harrison 22e p.1665","Harrison 22e p.1666","Harrison 22e p.1667","Harrison 22e p.1671","Harrison 22e p.1673","Harrison 22e p.1675","Harrison 22e p.1676"],"crossLinks":[]},"ascariasis":{"name":"Ascariasis","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Ascaris lumbricoides infection","roundworm infection","giant intestinal roundworm"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common soil-transmitted helminth worldwide, acquired by ingesting embryonated eggs from faecally contaminated food, water or soil","Most infections are light and asymptomatic; heavy worm loads, especially in children, cause malnutrition and growth impairment","During larval lung migration patients may develop a transient eosinophilic pneumonitis (Loeffler syndrome) with cough, wheeze and pulmonary infiltrates","Heavy intestinal burdens can cause bowel obstruction, particularly in children, and adult worms can migrate into the biliary or pancreatic ducts causing obstruction, cholangitis or pancreatitis","It is highly prevalent in parts of India with inadequate sanitation and is targeted by school-based mass deworming programmes","Diagnosis is usually by detecting characteristic eggs on stool microscopy; adult worms may be seen passed in stool or vomit or on imaging","Antiparasitic (anthelmintic) therapy is highly effective for uncomplicated intestinal infection","Biliary or obstructive complications may require endoscopic or surgical intervention in addition to anthelmintics"],"pathophysiology":"Ingested Ascaris eggs hatch in the small intestine; larvae penetrate the gut wall, travel via the portal circulation to the liver and then the lungs, break into the alveoli, ascend the tracheobronchial tree, are swallowed, and mature into large adult worms in the small intestine. The pulmonary phase provokes an eosinophilic hypersensitivity reaction. In the gut, large numbers of worms can form a bolus that obstructs the lumen, and individual worms may migrate into the biliary tree, pancreatic duct or appendix, producing obstructive and inflammatory complications. Chronic infection contributes to malabsorption and nutritional deficits.","additionalDifferentials":["Other intestinal helminth infections (hookworm, Trichuris, Strongyloides)","Non-parasitic causes of eosinophilic pneumonia","Mechanical bowel obstruction of other cause","Gallstone disease or other biliary obstruction","Pancreatitis of other aetiology"],"infectionMimics":["Asthma or allergic bronchopulmonary disease during the pulmonary phase","Drug or allergic eosinophilic pneumonitis","Functional abdominal pain in children"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool microscopy for Ascaris eggs","Full blood count for eosinophilia, especially during larval migration","Abdominal ultrasound or imaging to detect worms in bowel or biliary tree","Chest imaging if eosinophilic pneumonitis is suspected","Endoscopic evaluation for hepatobiliary or pancreatic complications"],"redFlags":["Features of intestinal obstruction (distension, vomiting, absent stool/flatus), particularly in a child","Biliary obstruction, cholangitis or pancreatitis from worm migration","Respiratory distress during the pulmonary migratory phase","Signs of peritonitis suggesting bowel perforation from a heavy worm bolus"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated infection has an excellent prognosis and responds promptly to anthelmintic treatment. Morbidity arises from heavy infections causing nutritional compromise in children and from obstructive complications, which can occasionally be life-threatening if not treated in time.","pitfalls":["Overlooking ascariasis as a cause of paediatric bowel obstruction in endemic areas","Missing biliary or pancreatic migration as the cause of acute abdominal pain","Attributing migratory pulmonary infiltrates with eosinophilia to asthma alone","Neglecting sanitation and re-treatment, allowing reinfection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"aspergillosis":{"name":"Aspergillosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Aspergillus infection","invasive aspergillosis","ABPA"],"chapter":223,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1713","clinicalPearls":["A. fumigatus causes most invasive aspergillosis, >95% of chronic aspergillosis, and most allergic syndromes; only species that grow at 37C cause invasive infection (Harrison 22e p.1713)","The disease spectrum spans three categories: invasive (acute/subacute in the immunocompromised), chronic/saprophytic (chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis, aspergilloma/fungal ball), and allergic (ABPA, severe asthma with fungal sensitization, allergic fungal rhinosinusitis) (Harrison 22e p.1713, 1714)","Invasive aspergillosis is one of the most common diagnostic errors revealed at autopsy; keys to early diagnosis are a high index of suspicion, circulating antigen screening (in leukemia), and urgent thoracic CT (Harrison 22e p.1714)","On thoracic CT the halo sign (ground-glass hemorrhagic infarction around a nodule/consolidation) appears for ~7 days early in neutropenic patients and is a good prognostic feature reflecting early diagnosis; the air-crescent/cavitation appears later (Harrison 22e p.1716)","Galactomannan antigen detection on respiratory samples (e.g., tracheobronchial/BAL fluid) is more sensitive than serum and is critical in ventilated/ICU patients where radiology is nonspecific; positive serum antigen usually precedes clinical or radiologic features by several days (Harrison 22e p.1714, 1716)","Antigen, beta-D-glucan, and real-time PCR are faster and far more sensitive than culture, as only 10-30% of invasive aspergillosis cases yield a positive culture; antifungal therapy reduces antigen sensitivity (Harrison 22e p.1716)","Aspergillus IgG antibody is detectable in ~90% of chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis and ~65% of Aspergillus nodules; ABPA is diagnosed by Aspergillus-specific IgE (or positive skin-prick test) plus elevated total IgE, usually >1000 IU/mL (Harrison 22e p.1714, 1715)","Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis follows pulmonary tuberculosis in ~6-13% of treated cases and is often initially mistaken for tuberculosis; >90% of chronic cavitary cases occur in patients with prior lung disease (Harrison 22e p.1713, 1715)","On tissue histology Aspergillus hyphae are hyaline, narrow, and septate with branching at 45 degrees; angioinvasion with vascular invasion by hyphae is characteristic in neutropenia (Harrison 22e p.1716)","HEPA filtration is often protective and should be installed and monitored in operating rooms, burn units, and areas housing high-risk patients; hospital outbreaks usually relate to a contaminated air source or construction (Harrison 22e p.1713)"],"pathophysiology":"Aspergillus is a ubiquitous hyaline septate branching mold whose conidia (spores) are inhaled from indoor/outdoor air; only species growing at 37C cause invasive disease. Disease form depends on host immunity. Invasive disease arises with profound neutropenia, glucocorticoid use, phagocyte dysfunction (CGD, advanced HIV, relapsed leukemia) and underlying respiratory disease, with angioinvasion and hemorrhagic infarction; from the lungs (>80% of cases) it may disseminate hematogenously (most often to brain). Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis occurs in apparently immunocompetent hosts with prior lung disease and a suboptimal TH1-like inflammatory response. Allergic forms (ABPA, SAFS) reflect a TH2-like hypersensitivity response to A. fumigatus, classically complicating asthma and cystic fibrosis. Multiple gene variants underlie susceptibility to each form (Harrison 22e p.1713-1714).","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary tuberculosis (chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis and aspergilloma classically mimic and follow TB)","Nontuberculous mycobacterial infection","Lung carcinoma (Aspergillus nodule may resemble early lung cancer)","Bacterial sinusitis (early invasive Aspergillus sinusitis not radiologically distinguishable)","Other angioinvasive molds, notably mucormycosis and Fusarium/Scedosporium (histologically can mimic Aspergillus)"],"infectionMimics":["Mucormycosis (Mucorales) causing invasive pulmonary or sinus disease","Fusariosis (Fusarium spp.)","Scedosporiosis (Scedosporium/Pseudallescheria boydii)","Pulmonary tuberculosis and nontuberculous mycobacterial disease","Bacterial sinusitis or pneumonia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lung carcinoma (Aspergillus nodule mimics early carcinoma)","COVID-19 itself (nodules with halo signs are a feature of COVID-19; invasive aspergillosis requires supportive evidence)","Atelectasis or nonspecific consolidation","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis / other cavitary lung disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["Galactomannan antigen detection (respiratory sample more sensitive than serum; critical in ICU/ventilated patients)","Novel protein antigen assay","Serum beta-D-glucan","Real-time PCR of respiratory samples and blood (and sputum PCR in chronic disease)","Aspergillus IgG antibody (chronic forms) and Aspergillus-specific IgE plus total IgE (allergic forms)","High-volume fungal culture on fungal media (also checks for azole resistance)","Microscopy/cytology and histopathology of affected tissue (hyaline narrow septate hyphae branching at 45 degrees)","Thoracic CT (halo sign, air-crescent, nodules, cavitation); MRI for cerebral and sinus disease","Bronchoscopy with tracheobronchial lavage; positron emission tomography for chronic cavitary disease and nodules"],"redFlags":["Life-threatening / massive hemoptysis (the most significant complication of chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis and may be the presenting manifestation)","Neurologic deterioration: mood change, focal signs, seizures, declining mental status suggesting cerebral aspergillosis (hemorrhagic infarction/abscess)","Orbital apex syndrome (blindness and proptosis), facial swelling, cavernous sinus thrombosis or carotid occlusion in invasive/chronic sinusitis","Fever progressing despite glucocorticoids in an immunocompromised host","Acute keratitis threatening sight"],"prognosis":"Invasive aspergillosis is curable if immune reconstitution occurs, whereas allergic and chronic forms are not curable. Mortality of invasive aspergillosis is 30-70% if treated but ~100% if the diagnosis is missed; cerebral aspergillosis, Aspergillus endocarditis, bilateral extensive invasive pulmonary disease, late-stage AIDS, relapsed uncontrolled leukemia, and liver failure carry very poor outcomes. Untreated chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis causes clinical deterioration in ~70%; with antifungal treatment mortality is 15-20% in the first year, ~40% over 5 years, and 50-60% over 10 years, with treatment failure in ~30% (more with azole resistance). ABPA/SAFS respond to glucocorticoids and antifungals (~60% to itraconazole, ~80% to voriconazole/posaconazole) but relapse after discontinuation is common (Harrison 22e p.1717).","pitfalls":["Requesting only bacterial culture rather than fungal culture may miss the diagnosis (bacterial agar is less sensitive); only 10-30% of invasive cases have a positive culture","Fluconazole is commonly used for prophylaxis but has NO activity against Aspergillus species","No prophylactic regimen is completely successful","Antifungal therapy reduces the sensitivity of antigen detection","Aspergillus IgG may not be positive on initial testing in subacute/nonneutropenic invasive disease","A positive respiratory culture may represent only noninvasive disease or airway colonization, not invasion","Contrast CT of the brain is often contraindicated by poor renal function and unenhanced CT is usually nonspecific; MRI is the most useful immediate investigation for cerebral disease","Surgery is problematic in chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis and often causes serious complications; bronchial artery embolization is preferred for problematic hemoptysis"],"severityClassification":"By disease category: INVASIVE (acute course <=1 month, subacute 1-3 months) - angioinvasive/nonangioinvasive/granulomatous pulmonary, invasive sinusitis, tracheobronchitis, disseminated, cerebral, endocarditis, cutaneous, keratitis/endophthalmitis. CHRONIC - chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis, chronic fibrosing pulmonary aspergillosis (destroyed lung), Aspergillus bronchitis, Aspergillus nodule, chronic invasive and chronic granulomatous sinusitis. SAPROPHYTIC - aspergilloma (single fungal ball), airway colonization, maxillary sinus fungal ball. ALLERGIC - ABPA, severe asthma with fungal sensitization (SAFS), allergic fungal rhinosinusitis, hypersensitivity pneumonitis (Harrison 22e p.1714-1715).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1713","Harrison 22e p.1714","Harrison 22e p.1715","Harrison 22e p.1716","Harrison 22e p.1717"],"crossLinks":["FEBRILE_NEUTROPENIA","asthma_exac","PULMONARY_TB"]},"aspiration_pneumonitis":{"name":"Aspiration pneumonitis","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mendelson's syndrome","perioperative pulmonary aspiration","acid aspiration syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from inhalation of gastric contents, most often during induction, emergence, or in patients with impaired airway protection","Chemical injury from acidic gastric fluid produces a rapid inflammatory pneumonitis, which is distinct from later bacterial aspiration pneumonia","Risk is highest with a full stomach, delayed gastric emptying, bowel obstruction, pregnancy, obesity, reflux, and emergency or difficult airway situations","Signs include hypoxaemia, wheeze or bronchospasm, cough, tachypnoea, and diffuse crackles, sometimes with visible gastric material in the airway","Immediate management is to position to limit further soiling, apply suction, secure the airway, and provide oxygen and supportive ventilation with lung-protective strategies","Routine antibiotics and corticosteroids are not indicated for pure chemical pneumonitis; antibiotics are reserved for established infection or high-risk contamination","Prevention centres on appropriate fasting, rapid-sequence induction with cricoid pressure in at-risk patients, and airway protection with a cuffed tube","Aspiration prophylaxis with agents that reduce gastric volume and acidity may be considered in high-risk patients"],"pathophysiology":"Aspiration occurs when protective airway reflexes are obtunded, allowing gastric contents to enter the tracheobronchial tree. Acidic fluid causes rapid chemical burn of the alveolar-capillary membrane, triggering an inflammatory cascade with increased permeability, alveolar oedema, surfactant dysfunction, and ventilation-perfusion mismatch that produces hypoxaemia. Particulate matter can cause mechanical obstruction and atelectasis. The initial insult is sterile, but secondary bacterial infection may supervene, and severe injury can progress to acute respiratory distress syndrome. Severity depends on the volume, acidity, and particulate content of the aspirate.","additionalDifferentials":["Bronchospasm from asthma or anaphylaxis","Pulmonary oedema (cardiogenic or negative-pressure)","Pulmonary embolism","Acute respiratory distress syndrome from other causes","Airway obstruction or laryngospasm","Bacterial aspiration pneumonia (later infective complication)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pulse oximetry and arterial blood gas to quantify hypoxaemia","Chest radiograph or CT to identify infiltrates, often in dependent lung zones","Bronchoscopy to clear particulate material when obstruction is suspected","Sputum or bronchoalveolar lavage culture if secondary infection is suspected","Serial observation for progression to acute respiratory distress syndrome"],"redFlags":["Witnessed massive aspiration with rapid desaturation and airway soiling","Progressive hypoxaemia or rising oxygen requirement suggesting evolving acute respiratory distress syndrome","Severe bronchospasm with difficult ventilation","Haemodynamic instability or the need for escalating respiratory support"],"prognosis":"Mild chemical pneumonitis often resolves within days with supportive care, but large-volume acidic or particulate aspiration can cause severe respiratory failure, acute respiratory distress syndrome, and death.","pitfalls":["Giving prophylactic antibiotics and steroids for uncomplicated chemical pneumonitis","Underestimating aspiration risk and omitting rapid-sequence induction in at-risk patients","Attributing hypoxaemia solely to aspiration and missing another cause","Failing to protect the airway promptly and allowing continued soiling","Assuming a normal early chest radiograph excludes significant aspiration"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"ataxic_disorders":{"name":"Ataxic Disorders","system":"Nervous system (cerebellum and spinocerebellar pathways)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Cerebellar ataxia","Spinocerebellar ataxia (SCA)","Inherited ataxias","Friedreich's ataxia","Machado-Joseph disease"],"chapter":450,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3533","clinicalPearls":["The core ataxic syndrome is gait impairment, scanning (unclear) speech, blurred vision from nystagmus, limb incoordination, and tremor on movement, reflecting disease of the cerebellum and its afferent/efferent pathways including the spinocerebellar and frontopontocerebellar tracts (p.3533).","True cerebellar ataxia is an unsteady, imbalanced gait without vertigo, light-headedness, or sense of movement; vestibular/labyrinthine disorders by contrast produce gait disturbance accompanied by prominent dizziness (p.3533).","Sensory (proprioceptive) ataxia can mimic cerebellar imbalance but is distinguished because instability worsens markedly when visual input is removed (positive Romberg sign); rarely proximal leg weakness also mimics cerebellar disease (p.3533).","The tempo and pattern of onset guide etiology: gradual, bilateral, symmetric progression points to genetic, metabolic, immune, or toxic causes, whereas focal unilateral signs with headache and depressed consciousness, ipsilateral cranial nerve palsies, and contralateral weakness suggest a space-occupying cerebellar lesion (p.3533).","Acute, reversible symmetric ataxias commonly follow intoxication with alcohol, phenytoin, lithium, or barbiturates and exposures such as toluene, gasoline/glue sniffing, methyl mercury, or bismuth (p.3533-3534).","Subacute symmetric ataxia raises suspicion of alcoholic-nutritional vermal degeneration (thiamine/B12 deficiency), paraneoplastic cerebellar degeneration, immune-mediated ataxia, or a prion disorder (p.3533-3534).","Friedreich's ataxia is the most common inherited ataxia, accounting for about half of all hereditary ataxias, and is autosomal recessive (p.3536).","Friedreich's ataxia features onset before age 25 with progressive staggering gait, areflexia, extensor plantar responses, loss of vibratory/proprioceptive sensation, and frequently pes cavus and scoliosis (p.3536).","Among the dominant ataxias, the polyglutamine SCAs (SCA1, SCA2, SCA3/Machado-Joseph, SCA6, SCA7, SCA17) arise from expanded CAG triplet repeats; genotype, not phenotype, is the diagnostic gold standard because clinical pictures overlap (p.3534).","Retinal pigmentary degeneration (blue-yellow color blindness progressing to macular degeneration and visual loss) is the distinguishing feature of SCA7 among the spinocerebellar ataxias (p.3535-3536).","Episodic ataxia type 1 causes brief (minutes-long) attacks of ataxia with myokymia and nystagmus, while type 2 produces longer attacks lasting hours to days; type 2 stems from CACNA1A mutations (the same calcium-channel gene as SCA6 and familial hemiplegic migraine) (p.3536).","A treatable mimic to always exclude in chronic symmetric gait ataxia is hypothyroidism; infectious causes to consider include meningovascular syphilis/tabes dorsalis and Lyme disease (p.3534)."],"pathophysiology":"Ataxia results from dysfunction of the cerebellum and the pathways connecting it to the rest of the nervous system, including the spinocerebellar tracts and the frontopontocerebellar pathway arising in the rostral frontal lobe. In the inherited polyglutamine spinocerebellar ataxias, expanded CAG triplet repeats produce mutant ataxin proteins with abnormally long polyglutamine tracts (generally toxic beyond ~40 glutamines); these proteins misfold into beta-pleated aggregates, are abnormally transported into the nucleus, bind and impair other polyglutamine proteins such as TATA-binding protein and CREB-binding protein, disrupt the ubiquitin-proteasome protein-turnover system, and trigger neuronal apoptosis, yielding a relatively disease-specific pattern of neuronal loss and gliosis. Longer repeats correlate with earlier onset and more aggressive disease across generations (anticipation). In Friedreich's ataxia, an expanded GAA repeat in the first intron of the frataxin gene silences frataxin expression; frataxin is a mitochondrial protein involved in iron homeostasis, so its loss causes mitochondrial iron accumulation, deficiency of iron-sulfur cluster enzymes, reduced ATP production, oxidative injury, and irreversible cell death, with predominant degeneration of the spinal cord (spinocerebellar tracts, lateral corticospinal tracts, posterior columns), dorsal root ganglia, and peripheral nerves.","additionalDifferentials":["Vestibular/labyrinthine disorder (gait disturbance with vertigo, distinguished from cerebellar ataxia)","Sensory ataxia from peripheral neuropathy or posterior column disease (Romberg-positive)","Proximal leg weakness mimicking cerebellar imbalance","Toxic/drug-induced cerebellar ataxia (alcohol, phenytoin, lithium, barbiturates, chemotherapeutic agents such as fluorouracil and paclitaxel)","Alcoholic-nutritional cerebellar degeneration with thiamine/B12 deficiency","Hypothyroidism","Cerebellar mass (infarction, hemorrhage, abscess, glioma, metastasis, subdural hematoma)","Multiple sclerosis and demyelinating disease","Congenital malformations: Chiari malformation, Dandy-Walker posterior fossa cyst"],"infectionMimics":["Acute viral cerebellitis (e.g., poliovirus, coxsackievirus, echovirus, Epstein-Barr virus)","Postinfectious cerebellar ataxia, especially after varicella (reversible gait ataxia and mild dysarthria)","Lyme disease","Legionella infection","Toxoplasmosis","Meningovascular syphilis and tabes dorsalis (posterior column/spinocerebellar degeneration)","Cerebellar abscess","AIDS-related progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy or CNS lymphoma presenting as a focal cerebellar syndrome","Prion disorder (subacute progressive ataxia)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Paraneoplastic cerebellar degeneration (anti-Yo with breast/ovarian cancer, anti-PQ calcium-channel antibody with small-cell lung cancer, anti-Tr with Hodgkin's disease; anti-Ri opsoclonus-myoclonus with breast/lung cancer and neuroblastoma)","Anti-GAD65 antibody-associated progressive ataxia without underlying cancer","Gluten/antigliadin antibody-associated ataxia (HLA DQB1*0201, may have villus atrophy on small-bowel biopsy)","Hyponatremia-associated ataxia","Vitamin E deficiency states (abetalipoproteinemia/Bassen-Kornzweig and ataxia with vitamin E deficiency)"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI/CT of brain and posterior fossa to detect cerebellar atrophy or mass lesions; spinal cord MRI shows atrophy in Friedreich's ataxia","Genetic (genotype) testing for triplet-repeat expansions, now the diagnostic gold standard for the inherited ataxias","Serum vitamin B1, B12, and vitamin E levels","Thyroid function testing for hypothyroidism","Paraneoplastic and autoimmune antibody panels (anti-Yo, anti-PQ calcium channel, anti-Tr, anti-Ri, anti-GAD65, antigliadin/antiendomysium)","CSF examination, including testing for syphilis and for evidence of acute viral infection","Lyme disease and Legionella antibody titers","Toxicology screen for intoxicants","ECG/echocardiography in Friedreich's ataxia (hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, conduction defects) and glucose testing for associated diabetes mellitus","Serum alpha-fetoprotein and immunoglobulin levels in suspected ataxia-telangiectasia"],"redFlags":["Acute focal ataxia with headache, depressed consciousness, and ipsilateral pontine/cranial nerve signs suggesting cerebellar infarction or hemorrhage with brainstem compression","Rapidly worsening focal cerebellar signs raising concern for posterior fossa hematoma, abscess, or tumor at risk of rostral (tentorial) or downward (foramen magnum/tonsillar) herniation, which can be devastating and may require emergent surgical decompression","Subacute progressive ataxia as the presenting feature of an occult malignancy (paraneoplastic syndrome)","Cardiac symptoms in Friedreich's ataxia (cardiomegaly, hypertrophy, conduction defects), since cardiac involvement occurs in about 90% of patients","Recurrent pulmonary infections and lymphoreticular malignancy risk in ataxia-telangiectasia"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on cause: several acquired ataxias (intoxications, postinfectious ataxia, hypothyroidism, vitamin deficiencies) are reversible, whereas the inherited degenerative ataxias progress relentlessly. In Machado-Joseph disease (SCA3) deficits invariably progress to death from debilitation within about 15 years of onset (especially types I and II), with intellect usually preserved. Friedreich's ataxia has a median age of death around 35 years, and women have a significantly better prognosis than men. Larger triplet-repeat expansions correlate with earlier onset and more aggressive disease, and anticipation produces earlier, more severe disease in successive generations.","pitfalls":["Mislabeling vestibular or sensory (proprioceptive) imbalance as cerebellar ataxia; absence of vertigo and a positive Romberg sign help distinguish them.","Overlooking readily treatable and reversible causes such as hypothyroidism, vitamin E/B1/B12 deficiency, and intoxications.","Relying on clinical phenotype alone to classify inherited ataxias, since SCA phenotypes overlap and genotype is the diagnostic gold standard.","In Machado-Joseph disease type I, the stiff broad-based lurching gait results from spasticity rather than true ataxia and lacks truncal titubation, which can be misread.","Failing to recognize an acute focal cerebellar lesion as a neurosurgical emergency because of herniation risk.","Forgetting that some SCAs have prominent non-cerebellar features (e.g., retinal degeneration in SCA7, parkinsonism/extrapyramidal signs in SCA1/SCA2, peripheral neuropathy in MJD type III)."],"severityClassification":"No single formal severity score is given; etiologic and temporal frameworks are used instead. Cerebellar ataxia is categorized as symmetric/progressive versus focal/ipsilateral, each subdivided by tempo into acute (hours to days), subacute (days to weeks), or chronic (months to years) per Table 450-1. Inherited ataxias are classified genomically by mode of inheritance: autosomal dominant (SCAs, DRPLA, episodic ataxias), autosomal recessive (Friedreich's ataxia, ataxia-telangiectasia, RFC1-related CANVAS), and mitochondrial. Machado-Joseph disease is further graded into clinical types I (ALS-parkinsonism-dystonia), II (ataxic, most common), and III (ataxic-amyotrophic).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3533","Harrison 22e p.3534","Harrison 22e p.3535","Harrison 22e p.3536","Harrison 22e p.3537"],"crossLinks":[]},"atopic_dermatitis":{"name":"Atopic dermatitis","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["atopic eczema","eczema","infantile eczema"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as an intensely itchy, chronic, relapsing dermatitis; itch is the cardinal symptom and often precedes visible changes","Distribution shifts with age: face and extensor surfaces in infants, flexural creases (antecubital and popliteal fossae) in older children and adults","Look for supporting features such as dry skin (xerosis), keratosis pilaris, and a personal or family history of atopy (asthma, allergic rhinitis)","The disease reflects impaired skin barrier plus immune dysregulation, so consistent emollient use is the foundation of every management plan","Flares are treated with topical corticosteroids of potency matched to site and severity, or with topical calcineurin inhibitors on delicate areas","Sudden worsening with weeping, crusting, or painful clustered vesicles suggests bacterial superinfection or eczema herpeticum, which needs prompt antiviral treatment","Identify and reduce trigger exposure (soaps, heat, sweat, aeroallergens, stress) and consider phototherapy or systemic immunomodulators for severe refractory disease"],"pathophysiology":"Atopic dermatitis arises from the interplay of a defective epidermal barrier and a skewed type-2 immune response. Loss-of-function variants in the filaggrin gene and reduced barrier lipids increase transepidermal water loss and allow penetration of allergens and microbes. This drives activation of type-2 cytokines (including interleukins 4, 13, and 31) that promote inflammation, further barrier breakdown, and the sensation of itch. Scratching perpetuates an itch-scratch cycle, causing lichenification, and the barrier defect predisposes to colonisation and infection with Staphylococcus aureus and herpes simplex virus.","additionalDifferentials":["Allergic or irritant contact dermatitis","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Scabies (especially with generalised itch)","Psoriasis","Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma in adult-onset recalcitrant disease","Immunodeficiency-associated dermatitis in infants with failure to thrive"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Primarily a clinical diagnosis based on morphology, distribution, and history","Bacterial swabs when infection or antibiotic resistance is suspected","Viral swab or PCR when eczema herpeticum is considered","Allergy testing (specific IgE or patch testing) only when a specific trigger is clinically suspected","Skin biopsy in atypical or treatment-resistant cases to exclude other diagnoses"],"redFlags":["Eczema herpeticum: rapidly spreading painful monomorphic vesicles or punched-out erosions with systemic upset","Extensive secondary bacterial infection or signs of sepsis","Erythroderma with widespread inflammation and impaired thermoregulation","Failure to thrive or recurrent severe infection suggesting underlying immunodeficiency"],"prognosis":"Many children improve substantially or clear by adolescence, but a proportion have persistent or adult-onset disease; a relapsing course is typical and long-term control depends on barrier maintenance and trigger avoidance.","pitfalls":["Under-treating flares with insufficiently potent topical steroids while over-worrying about steroid side effects","Neglecting regular generous emollient use, the cornerstone of maintenance","Missing eczema herpeticum and treating it as a routine bacterial flare","Attributing all disease to food allergy and pursuing unnecessary elimination diets"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"atrial_myxoma_and_other_cardiac":{"name":"Atrial Myxoma and Other Cardiac Tumors","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Cardiac myxoma","Left atrial myxoma","Primary cardiac tumor","Cardiac sarcoma","Cardiac neoplasm"],"chapter":282,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2084","clinicalPearls":["Cardiac tumors are broadly divided into primary tumors (arising in the heart) and metastatic tumors from a distant source; metastatic involvement is far more common and is seen in up to roughly 20% of patients with end-stage metastatic cancer.","Primary cardiac tumors are rare (about 1 in 2000 in autopsy series); roughly three-quarters are histologically benign, and most of those benign tumors are myxomas, while malignant primaries (almost all sarcomas) make up about 25%.","Myxoma is the most common primary cardiac tumor in adults, comprising one-third to one-half of cases at postmortem and about three-quarters of surgically treated tumors; it favors the third through sixth decades with a female predilection.","Roughly 90% of myxomas are sporadic; the remainder are familial (autosomal dominant), often as part of the Carney complex (cardiac/skin/breast myxomas, lentigines or pigmented nevi, and endocrine overactivity), associated in about 70% of cases with inactivating mutations in the PRKAR1A tumor-suppressor gene.","Sporadic myxomas are typically solitary, pedunculated on a fibrovascular stalk, and arise from the interatrial septum near the fossa ovalis, most often in the left atrium; familial/syndromic myxomas occur in younger patients, are often multiple, may be ventricular, and recur more often.","Myxomas most commonly produce obstructive features mimicking mitral valve disease (stenosis from prolapse into the mitral orifice, or regurgitation from valvular trauma); symptoms may be sudden or positional because of gravitational effects on tumor position.","A characteristic low-pitched early or mid-diastolic 'tumor plop' may be heard, attributed to the tumor striking the mitral valve or ventricular wall.","Myxomas can cause peripheral or pulmonary embolic events (tumor fragments or associated thrombus) and constitutional/cytokine-mediated features (often interleukin-6), so they are frequently misdiagnosed as endocarditis, collagen vascular disease, or a paraneoplastic syndrome.","Papillary fibroelastomas are the most common tumors of the cardiac valves (friable, frond-like, usually solitary) and, although often silent, may embolize to cause TIA, stroke, or myocardial infarction.","Rhabdomyomas and fibromas are the most common cardiac tumors of infants and children, usually ventricular; rhabdomyomas occur in about 50% of children with tuberous sclerosis (TSC1/TSC2 mutations) and tend to regress, whereas fibromas (PTCH1) tend to grow.","Malignant primaries are almost all sarcomas (angiosarcoma most common in adults, rhabdomyosarcoma in children), frequently right-sided, rapidly growing, and often invade the pericardium; about one-third are metastatic at diagnosis, usually to the lungs.","Tissue histology remains the gold standard for diagnosis; echocardiography is the usual initial test, with cardiac MRI (gadolinium) helping differentiate tumor from thrombus and benign from malignant lesions."],"pathophysiology":"Cardiac tumors are classified as primary (benign, chiefly myxoma; or malignant, chiefly sarcoma) or secondary/metastatic. Myxomas are gelatinous masses of myxoma cells in a glycosaminoglycan-rich stroma, usually arising from the interatrial septum near the fossa ovalis and most often projecting into the left atrium on a fibrovascular stalk. Clinical effects arise from three mechanisms: mechanical obstruction or valvular interference (mimicking mitral stenosis/regurgitation, or outflow obstruction for ventricular tumors), embolization of tumor fragments or associated thrombus, and constitutional effects from tumor-secreted cytokines such as interleukin-6. Familial myxomas relate to PRKAR1A inactivation within the Carney complex. Metastatic tumors reach the heart by hematogenous or lymphangitic spread or direct invasion, most often involving the pericardium, then myocardium of any chamber, and rarely the endocardium or valves.","additionalDifferentials":["Mitral stenosis","Mitral regurgitation","Subaortic or subpulmonic (outflow tract) stenosis from a ventricular tumor","Restrictive or hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (mimicked by pediatric rhabdomyoma/fibroma)","Pericardial constriction"],"infectionMimics":["Infective endocarditis (myxoma with embolic phenomena, fever, and constitutional symptoms is frequently misdiagnosed as endocarditis)","Valvular vegetation"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Intracardiac thrombus","Inflammatory myofibroblastic tumor","Myocardial hypertrophy mistaken for a mass","Collagen vascular disease (mimicked by myxoma constitutional/laboratory picture)","Paraneoplastic syndrome"],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography (initial modality; tumor location, size, attachment site, impact on valves/pericardium)","Transesophageal echocardiography (improved characterization and spatial resolution; aids surgical planning)","Cardiac MRI with gadolinium (tissue characterization, local invasion, differentiating tumor from thrombus)","Gated cardiac CT (anatomic and tissue assessment, calcified lesions, extracardiac involvement; useful when MRI not feasible)","Nuclear imaging including FDG-PET (extracardiac disease; some tumor types) and 131I-MIBG scanning for paraganglioma","Echocardiographic screening of first-degree relatives when myxoma is familial/syndromic","Pericardiocentesis with cytology for malignant pericardial effusion (reported sensitivity ~67-92%)"],"redFlags":["Syncope or positional symptoms suggesting intermittent obstruction by a mobile tumor","Embolic events (stroke, TIA, peripheral or pulmonary embolism) as the presenting feature","Cardiac tamponade or large pericardial effusion (especially with metastatic disease)","Rapidly progressive heart failure or chamber/vena caval obstruction suggesting sarcoma","New heart block or conduction disturbance (tumors near the AV node, e.g., hemangioma/mesothelioma, can cause sudden death)"],"prognosis":"Benign myxoma is generally cured by surgical excision; recurrence is about 1-2% in sporadic cases but 12-22% in familial cases (multifocal disease or incomplete resection). Malignant primary tumors carry a poor prognosis: sarcomas typically progress rapidly to death within weeks to months from hemodynamic compromise, local invasion, or metastasis, and respond poorly to radiotherapy/chemotherapy. The notable exception is primary cardiac lymphoma, the most chemotherapy-sensitive primary cardiac malignancy, with long-term survival in about 40% of treated patients. Metastatic cardiac involvement usually occurs with advanced disease, is clinically apparent only about 10% of the time, is rarely the cause of death, and carries an overall poor prognosis.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing myxoma as infective endocarditis, collagen vascular disease, or a paraneoplastic syndrome because of fever, embolism, and laboratory abnormalities (e.g., elevated ESR/CRP, anemia, hypergammaglobulinemia).","Catheterizing the chamber harboring a tumor, which risks tumor embolization; angiography/catheterization is no longer mandatory when noninvasive imaging is adequate and coronary disease is unlikely.","Failing to screen first-degree relatives of young patients with multiple myxomas or syndromic features.","Attributing cardiac symptoms in cancer patients solely to the tumor when radiotherapy/chemotherapy-induced myocarditis, pericarditis, or cardiomyopathy can produce identical findings.","Overlooking that a left-sided sarcoma may be mistaken for a myxoma."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2084","Harrison 22e p.2085","Harrison 22e p.2086","Harrison 22e p.2087"],"crossLinks":[]},"atrial_septal_defect":{"name":"Atrial Septal Defect","system":"Paediatric cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ASD","atrial septal defects"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A communication in the atrial septum permitting left-to-right shunting; the secundum type in the region of the fossa ovalis is the most common form.","Most children are asymptomatic and are detected through an incidental murmur or, later, right heart enlargement; heart failure in infancy is uncommon.","The characteristic auscultatory finding is a widely and fixedly split second heart sound, accompanied by a soft systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border from increased pulmonary flow.","A larger shunt may produce a mid-diastolic rumble at the lower left sternal border from increased flow across the tricuspid valve.","The murmur reflects increased flow through the pulmonary valve rather than turbulent flow across the defect itself, which is low-velocity and inaudible.","Echocardiography defines the defect type and size, direction of shunt, and degree of right atrial and right ventricular volume overload.","Small secundum defects frequently close spontaneously in early childhood; a defect must be distinguished from a normal patent foramen ovale.","Significant defects are closed, often by transcatheter device for suitable secundum defects, to prevent later right heart enlargement, arrhythmias, and pulmonary vascular disease.","Untreated large defects may lead in adulthood to atrial arrhythmias, pulmonary hypertension, and, rarely, paradoxical embolism."],"pathophysiology":"An opening in the atrial septum allows blood to shunt from the higher-compliance left atrium toward the right atrium, the direction favoured because the right ventricle is more compliant than the left. This left-to-right shunt volume-loads the right atrium, right ventricle, and pulmonary circulation while the left-sided output is maintained by the shunt returning through the lungs. Chronic volume overload dilates the right heart; over many years a minority develop elevated pulmonary vascular resistance and pulmonary hypertension, and the resulting right atrial stretch predisposes to atrial arrhythmias.","additionalDifferentials":["Ventricular septal defect","Partial anomalous pulmonary venous return","Atrioventricular septal defect (primum type)","Innocent pulmonary flow murmur","Pulmonary valve stenosis","Patent foramen ovale (a normal variant, not a true defect)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography to define defect type, size, and right heart volume overload","Chest radiograph for cardiomegaly and increased pulmonary vascular markings in significant shunts","Electrocardiogram, often showing right axis deviation and right ventricular volume overload (rsR' pattern)","Transoesophageal echocardiography or advanced imaging when transthoracic views are inadequate","Cardiac catheterisation if pulmonary hypertension needs assessment"],"redFlags":["Signs of significant right heart volume overload or pulmonary hypertension","Development of atrial arrhythmias","Failure to thrive or heart failure in an infant, suggesting an additional or larger lesion","New cyanosis suggesting shunt reversal in longstanding, untreated disease","Paradoxical embolic events"],"prognosis":"Isolated secundum defects, whether closed spontaneously, by device, or surgically, have an excellent outlook when addressed before irreversible pulmonary vascular disease. Late, unrepaired large defects carry increasing risk of arrhythmia and pulmonary hypertension in adulthood.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a normal patent foramen ovale for a true atrial septal defect","Dismissing the subtle findings and missing a significant defect until right heart enlargement is advanced","Attributing fixed splitting of the second heart sound to a benign murmur","Assuming all defects require closure when many small secundum defects close spontaneously","Overlooking associated anomalous pulmonary venous drainage"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"autism_spectrum_disorder":{"name":"Autism Spectrum Disorder","system":"Neurodevelopmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ASD","autism","autistic spectrum disorder","pervasive developmental disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A neurodevelopmental disorder defined by persistent deficits in social communication and interaction together with restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests, or activities.","Symptoms are present from early development, though they may become fully apparent only when social demands exceed the child's capacities.","It is more frequently diagnosed in boys and shows a strong genetic contribution, with increased recurrence risk in siblings.","Early signs include reduced eye contact, limited joint attention and pointing, absent or delayed social smiling, and lack of response to name.","Repetitive behaviours may include hand-flapping, lining up objects, insistence on routines, and unusual sensory responses.","Diagnosis is clinical and based on developmental history and structured observation rather than a laboratory test.","Routine developmental surveillance and autism-specific screening in toddlerhood aid early identification.","Early intensive behavioural and educational intervention improves functional outcomes.","Common co-occurring conditions include intellectual disability, ADHD, anxiety, epilepsy, sleep problems, and gastrointestinal symptoms, and no evidence links vaccines to autism."],"pathophysiology":"Autism arises from atypical early brain development influenced by a strong and heterogeneous genetic basis, including many single-gene syndromes, copy number variants, and polygenic risk, interacting with prenatal and perinatal factors. These influences alter neuronal connectivity, synaptic function, and the balance of excitation and inhibition during critical periods, affecting circuits underlying social cognition, language, and behavioural flexibility. No single mechanism or biomarker accounts for all cases, reflecting the disorder's biological heterogeneity.","additionalDifferentials":["Global developmental delay or intellectual disability","Isolated language disorder or hearing impairment","Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder","Social (pragmatic) communication disorder","Anxiety disorders and selective mutism","Rett syndrome and other genetic or metabolic syndromes","Severe early deprivation or attachment disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Formal audiology assessment to exclude hearing loss","Structured diagnostic observation and developmental history","Genetic evaluation (chromosomal microarray, fragile X testing, targeted panels)","Assessment of cognitive and adaptive functioning","Screening for co-occurring conditions such as epilepsy or sleep disorders","Metabolic testing when clinical features suggest an inborn error"],"redFlags":["Regression or loss of previously acquired language or social skills","Absence of babbling, pointing, or other gestures by around 12 months","No single words by 16 months or no two-word phrases by 24 months","Seizures or focal neurological signs suggesting an underlying disorder","Concern for self-injurious behaviour or safety"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary widely; language ability and cognitive level are important predictors, and early intervention improves adaptive functioning, though most individuals have lifelong needs requiring ongoing support.","pitfalls":["Attributing early social and language concerns to shyness and delaying referral","Failing to test hearing before attributing delay to autism","Overlooking treatable co-occurring conditions such as epilepsy, anxiety, or sleep disturbance","Assuming intellectual ability from verbal fluency alone"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"autoimmune_polyendocrine_syndromes":{"name":"Autoimmune Polyendocrine Syndromes","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["APS","APS-1","APS-2","APECED","autoimmune polyglandular syndrome","polyglandular deficiency syndrome"],"chapter":401,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3089","clinicalPearls":["Autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes (APS) are divided into two principal categories: APS type 1 (APS-1) and APS type 2 (APS-2); some authors further split APS-2 into APS-3 and APS-4, but this added subdivision adds little to understanding pathogenesis or guiding management (Harrison 22e p.3089).","Although the underlying autoimmunity predominantly targets endocrine glands, many nonendocrine tissues can be affected, so a range of nonendocrine disorders is recognized as part of these syndromes (Harrison 22e p.3089).","APS-1 (APECED) is defined by a triad of chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism, and Addison's disease; the diagnosis is usually made clinically when two of these three major components are present (Harrison 22e p.3089).","APS-1 is rare (<500 reported cases) and classically is autosomal recessive, caused by mutations in the AIRE gene on chromosome 21; AIRE is most highly expressed in thymic medullary epithelial cells where it drives expression of tissue-specific self-antigens such as insulin (Harrison 22e p.3089).","Anticytokine autoantibodies are characteristic of APS-1: anti-IL-17A, anti-IL-17F, and anti-IL-22 underlie the mucocutaneous candidiasis, while detection of anti-interferon alpha and anti-interferon omega antibodies identifies nearly 100% of APS-1 cases and is not seen in other autoimmune disorders (Harrison 22e p.3089-3090).","APS-1 typically begins in infancy with chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis (mouth and nails more than skin/esophagus), followed by hypoparathyroidism (>85% of cases) and then Addison's disease (nearly 80%); the interval between components can be many years and the order is variable (Harrison 22e p.3089-3090).","APS-2 is more common (prevalence 1-2 per 100,000), shows a female predominance (at least 3:1), has multigenerational inheritance, and usually presents in adulthood with peak incidence between 20 and 60 years (Harrison 22e p.3090).","APS-2 is defined by two or more of: primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison's disease, 50-70%), Graves' disease or autoimmune thyroiditis (15-69%), type 1 diabetes (40-50%), and primary hypogonadism; associated conditions include celiac disease, myasthenia gravis, vitiligo, alopecia, serositis, and pernicious anemia (Harrison 22e p.3090).","Adrenal insufficiency can be masked by coexisting primary hypothyroidism because low thyroid hormone prolongs the half-life of cortisol; patients with hypothyroidism and possible APS should be screened for adrenal insufficiency, since starting thyroid hormone first can precipitate an adrenal crisis (Harrison 22e p.3090).","Antibody screening can detect risk years before clinical disease: anti-21-hydroxylase antibodies are highly predictive of future adrenal insufficiency, and presence of two or more islet autoantibodies with normal glucose tolerance is defined as stage 1 type 1 diabetes with a near-100% lifetime risk of clinical disease (Harrison 22e p.3091).","About 50% of patients with Addison's disease develop another autoimmune disorder during their lifetime, so a new diagnosis of Addison's should prompt broader screening for linked conditions (Harrison 22e p.3091)."],"pathophysiology":"APS-1 is a monogenic, classically autosomal recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the AIRE (autoimmune regulator) gene on chromosome 21. AIRE is most highly expressed in thymic medullary epithelial cells, where it promotes expression of tissue-specific self-antigens; its loss reduces this expression and is thought to allow autoreactive T cells to escape central (thymic) deletion. Over 100 mutations are described, with higher frequency in certain ethnic groups (Iranian Jews, Sardinians, Finns, Norwegians, Irish). Newly recognized autosomal dominant mutations cluster in the PHD1 domain and tend to cause later-onset, less aggressive disease. Patients develop anticytokine autoantibodies (anti-IL-17A, IL-17F, IL-22, and type 1 interferons). In contrast, APS-2 is polygenic, with the dominant risk in the HLA complex on chromosome 6: Addison's disease in APS-2 is strongly linked to HLA-DR3 and HLA-DR4, with additional contributions from HLA-B8, DQ2, DQ8, and DRB1 subtypes. Non-HLA immune genes including MIC-A (the 5.1 allele strongly associated with Addison's), PTPN22, CTLA4, and IL-2R-alpha variants further modulate susceptibility. Organ damage in both syndromes is mediated by organ-specific autoantibodies and autoreactive T cells against targets such as 21-hydroxylase, GAD65, thyroid peroxidase, transglutaminase, and intrinsic factor (Harrison 22e p.3089-3091).","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated autoimmune Addison's disease without polyglandular involvement","Isolated autoimmune (Hashimoto's) thyroiditis or Graves' disease","Isolated type 1 diabetes mellitus","Idiopathic/isolated hypoparathyroidism","IPEX syndrome (immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, X-linked)","Insulin autoimmune syndrome (Hirata's syndrome)","Adult combined pituitary hormone deficiency with anti-Pit1 autoantibodies (anti-Pit1 syndrome)","POEMS syndrome","Kearns-Sayre and DIDMOAD (Wolfram) syndromes as causes of multiple endocrine deficiencies"],"infectionMimics":["Chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis from primary immunodeficiencies other than APS-1 (e.g., STAT1 gain-of-function)","Congenital rubella associated with thyroiditis and/or diabetes"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Immune checkpoint inhibitor-induced endocrinopathies producing multiple gland failure","Sequential isolated autoimmune endocrinopathies occurring by chance","Trisomy 21 and Turner's syndrome associated autoimmune endocrine disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["AIRE gene sequencing in suspected APS-1","Anti-interferon alpha and anti-interferon omega antibodies (identify nearly 100% of APS-1)","Anticytokine antibodies to IL-17 and IL-22 (support candidiasis of APS-1)","Anti-21-hydroxylase (and anti-17-hydroxylase) autoantibodies for Addison's disease risk","Morning ACTH and cortisol; cosyntropin (ACTH) stimulation test if abnormal","Serum calcium, phosphate, and PTH for hypoparathyroidism","TSH with anti-TPO/anti-thyroglobulin and anti-TSH receptor antibodies","Islet autoantibodies (insulin, GAD65, IA-2, ZnT8) and hemoglobin A1c for type 1 diabetes","Anti-tissue transglutaminase antibodies for celiac disease","Vitamin B12, CBC, and anti-parietal cell/intrinsic factor antibodies for pernicious anemia","Peripheral blood smear for Howell-Jolly bodies to detect asplenism (with splenic ultrasound if positive)"],"redFlags":["New thyroid hormone replacement in an undiagnosed patient with adrenal insufficiency can precipitate an adrenal crisis","Treatment of mucocutaneous candidiasis with ketoconazole in a patient with subclinical adrenal insufficiency may also precipitate adrenal crisis","Hyperpigmentation, unexplained hyponatremia/hyperkalemia, or hypotension signaling evolving Addison's disease","Tetany or seizures from hypocalcemia indicating hypoparathyroidism","Howell-Jolly bodies on smear indicating functional asplenism and increased infection risk in APS-1","Refractory diarrhea or obstipation suggesting autoimmune destruction of enterochromaffin(-like) cells","Chronic oral candidiasis with leukoplakia-like atrophic change carrying a risk of future carcinoma"],"prognosis":"These are chronic, progressive disorders in which individual component diseases continue to emerge over years to decades, so cumulative prevalence rises with age and lifelong surveillance is required. APS-1 typically presents earliest (often infancy) and its chronic candidiasis is nearly always present and poorly responsive to treatment. In APS-2, autoantibodies can precede clinical disease by many years, allowing early detection; about half of patients with Addison's disease develop an additional autoimmune disorder over their lifetime, and individuals with two or more islet autoantibodies have a near-100% lifetime risk of clinical type 1 diabetes. With timely hormone replacement and monitoring, prognosis for individual endocrinopathies is generally favorable, but unrecognized adrenal insufficiency or precipitated adrenal crisis is potentially fatal (Harrison 22e p.3089-3091).","pitfalls":["Initiating thyroid hormone before excluding/treating adrenal insufficiency, precipitating adrenal crisis","Failing to recognize that one component disorder warrants screening for others, and that siblings of an APS-1 patient should be considered affected even with a single component","Attributing chronic candidiasis purely to local infection rather than recognizing it as a marker of APS-1","Missing functional asplenism (Howell-Jolly bodies) in APS-1, which raises infection risk","Overlooking subclinical/preclinical disease detectable only by autoantibodies and physiologic testing"],"severityClassification":"No formal severity grading is defined; classification is by type (APS-1 vs APS-2, with proposed APS-3/APS-4 subtypes) based on the constellation of component autoimmune disorders. For type 1 diabetes risk within APS-2, presence of two or more islet autoantibodies with normal glucose tolerance is staged as stage 1 disease (Harrison 22e p.3089-3091).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3089","Harrison 22e p.3090","Harrison 22e p.3091"],"crossLinks":[]},"autoimmunity_and_autoimmune_diseases":{"name":"Autoimmunity and Autoimmune Diseases","system":"Immune-mediated / Rheumatologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Autoimmune disease","AD","Loss of immune tolerance","Autoimmunity"],"chapter":367,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2821","clinicalPearls":["Autoimmunity (development of autoantibodies or autoreactive T-cell responses against self-antigens) is present in essentially all individuals and tends to increase with age; it does not by itself imply disease. Autoimmune disease (AD) arises only when a loss of tolerance produces clinically relevant, pathogenic responses (p.2821-2822).","Roughly 150 organ-specific and systemic ADs have been described; in 2009 the U.S. prevalence was estimated at 7.6-9.4% of the population (about 25-31 million people), likely an underestimate, with an apparent ongoing rise in incidence and prevalence (p.2822).","ADs predominantly affect women and, as a group, rank among the top 10 leading causes of death in women; an affected person carries a higher risk of developing a second autoimmune condition, of autoimmunity in relatives, and of certain cancers and premature cardiovascular disease (p.2822).","AD is a multistep process: genetic and environmental factors drive immune dysregulation and loss of tolerance, and autoantibodies can be detectable years before clinical symptoms appear (p.2822-2825).","HLA haplotype is considered the best single predictor of AD risk; specific HLA-DR types, complement-component deficiencies, and genes in the type I interferon and B-/T-cell pathways raise risk, and many susceptibility genes (e.g., STAT4, PTPN22) are shared across several ADs (p.2822-2823).","Autoantibodies serve as diagnostic tools and, in some diseases, as markers of activity or worse outcome; however, for several autoantibodies a specific pathogenic role has not been established, and the relevant autoantigens of many ADs remain unknown (p.2825).","Natural autoantibodies are mainly IgM, encoded by unmutated V(D)J genes with moderate self-affinity, and are not pathogenic; the harmful autoantibodies of AD are high-affinity, somatically mutated IgG generated by somatic hypermutation, class switching, and antigen-driven clonal selection (p.2824-2825).","Dysregulated type I interferon signaling is implicated across SLE, primary Sjogren syndrome, and idiopathic inflammatory myopathies; neutrophil extracellular trap (NET) formation externalizes modified autoantigens and is implicated in SLE, ANCA-associated vasculitis, myopathies, antiphospholipid syndrome, and RA (p.2823-2824).","Many ADs are female-predominant; two X chromosomes (women, and men with Klinefelter XXY) increase expression of X-linked immunity genes such as TLR-7/8 and IRAK-1, and breakdown of X-inactivation has been linked to autoimmunity (p.2823).","Infections can both trigger AD and provoke flares via molecular mimicry, epitope spreading, bystander/adjuvant effects, release of hidden self-antigens, and epigenetic changes; EBV is linked to SLE, MS, RA, celiac disease, and Sjogren, and SARS-CoV-2 has been associated with myocarditis, new APS, MIS-C, Kawasaki disease, and Guillain-Barre (p.2825-2826).","Immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) cause immune-related adverse events in up to 85% of treated patients (dermatitis, arthritis, thyroiditis, colitis among the most common); most are mild and overall survival/time to progression is generally not influenced by their occurrence (p.2826).","Drug-induced lupus is a well-recognized iatrogenic AD characterized by antihistone antibodies; in many cases discontinuing the offending drug is sufficient to abate symptoms (p.2826)."],"pathophysiology":"Autoimmune disease reflects a failure of self-tolerance in a genetically predisposed host exposed to environmental and stochastic 'hits'. Central tolerance (deletion of autoreactive T cells in the thymus and high-affinity autoreactive B cells in the marrow) and peripheral tolerance (anergy from lack of costimulation, regulatory lymphocyte suppression, inhibitory costimulation, removal of autoreactive cells) normally restrain autoreactivity; impairment of these mechanisms allows pathogenic autoimmune responses. Susceptibility is largely polygenic (HLA-DR types, complement deficiencies, type I IFN-pathway and B-/T-cell genes such as STAT4 and PTPN22), with rare monogenic forms, and epigenetic programming (histone modifications, altered DNA methylation, trained immunity) further shapes risk. On the innate side, dysregulated or proinflammatory cell death (apoptosis, pyroptosis, necroptosis, NETosis) plus impaired clearance of dead-cell debris and defective nucleases prolong exposure of modified nucleic-acid autoantigens; endosomal TLR-7/8/9 and cytosolic sensors then drive type I interferon production and inflammasome activation. Plasmacytoid dendritic cells (major IFN-alpha producers), conventional dendritic cells, neutrophils/low-density granulocytes with aberrant NET formation, monocytes/macrophages with defective phagocytosis, and NK cells are all implicated. On the adaptive side, autoreactive CD4/CD8 T cells, Treg deficiency, and imbalance between costimulatory and inhibitory pathways (PD-1, CTLA-4) and cytokines (IL-2, IL-6, IL-17, IL-33) promote disease; pathogenic high-affinity IgG autoantibodies form immune complexes that deposit in tissue, fix complement, opsonize, engage activating Fc receptors, and drive type I IFN production. Inflammatory cytokines (TNF, IL-1beta, IL-6, IL-17, type I IFNs) sustain and amplify tissue injury. Female sex (sex hormones, X-chromosome dosage) and immune aging (inflammaging with senescent, proinflammatory cells) further potentiate autoimmunity.","additionalDifferentials":["Organ-specific AD (e.g., Hashimoto thyroiditis, Graves disease, type 1 diabetes, pernicious anemia/autoimmune gastritis, myasthenia gravis, multiple sclerosis, vitiligo, autoimmune hepatitis, primary biliary cholangitis) (p.2822)","Systemic AD (SLE, rheumatoid arthritis, primary antiphospholipid syndrome, Sjogren syndrome, systemic sclerosis, idiopathic inflammatory myopathies, mixed connective tissue disease) (p.2822)","Systemic vasculitides (giant cell arteritis, Takayasu arteritis, polyarteritis nodosa, ANCA-associated vasculitis, anti-GBM/Goodpasture disease, cryoglobulinemic vasculitis, IgA/Henoch-Schonlein vasculitis, Behcet disease) (p.2822)","Paraneoplastic autoimmune syndromes (Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome, paraneoplastic pemphigus, dermatomyositis, anti-NMDA-receptor encephalitis with ovarian cancer) (p.2826)","Drug-induced lupus (antihistone-antibody positive) and immune checkpoint inhibitor-related immune adverse events (p.2826)"],"infectionMimics":["Infections that trigger or mimic autoimmune flares: group A streptococcal infection underlying rheumatic fever via M-protein/myosin cross-reactivity (p.2825)","Coxsackievirus (myocarditis, Hashimoto thyroiditis, T1DM) and influenza A (T1DM, IgA vasculitis) as proposed autoimmune triggers (p.2825)","Epstein-Barr virus linked to SLE, MS, RA, celiac disease, and Sjogren syndrome (p.2825)","SARS-CoV-2-associated myocarditis, antiphospholipid syndrome, MIS-C, Kawasaki disease, and Guillain-Barre syndrome (p.2826)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Paraneoplastic syndromes presenting before a cancer diagnosis (e.g., dermatomyositis, autoimmune hemolytic anemia, immune thrombocytopenia, vitiligo, thyroiditis) (p.2826)","Drug-induced autoimmunity from antibiotics, antihypertensives, antiarrhythmics, TNF inhibitors, antiseizure agents (p.2826)","Hereditary alpha-tryptasemia and mast-cell activation disorders presenting with multisystem symptoms (contextual, adjacent chapter) (p.2821)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Disease-specific autoantibody panels: anti-dsDNA and anti-RNP (SLE); anti-Ro/anti-La and rheumatoid factor (Sjogren); ACPA, anti-carbamylated antibodies, rheumatoid factor (RA); anti-MPO and anti-PR3 (ANCA-associated vasculitis); anti-type IV collagen (Goodpasture) (p.2825)","Anti-TSH-receptor antibodies (Graves); anti-thyroid peroxidase and anti-thyroglobulin (Hashimoto) (p.2825)","Islet-related antibodies in T1DM: anti-insulin, anti-GAD (glutamic acid decarboxylase), anti-protein-tyrosine-phosphatase/IA-2; antibodies to multiple targets confer higher risk of progression (p.2825)","Anti-parietal cell antibodies in pernicious anemia (present in 70-90% of patients) and antimitochondrial antibodies (anti-pyruvate dehydrogenase) in primary biliary cholangitis (p.2825)","Myositis-specific antibodies (anti-aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase, anti-Mi2, anti-MDA5, anti-TIF1-gamma, anti-HMGCR, anti-cN1A) for risk stratification in inflammatory myopathies (p.2825)","Antihistone antibodies for suspected drug-induced lupus (p.2826)","Serum tryptase, 24-h urinary 5-HIAA, and metanephrines to exclude mast-cell, carcinoid, and pheochromocytoma mimics when relevant (contextual) (p.2821)"],"redFlags":["New autoimmune-pattern symptoms in a patient on an immune checkpoint inhibitor (irAE such as colitis, thyroiditis, arthritis, dermatitis) — can be severe and may persist after stopping therapy (p.2826)","Sudden new autoimmunity or autoantibody synthesis following SARS-CoV-2 or other viral infection (APS, myocarditis, MIS-C, Guillain-Barre) (p.2826)","Autoimmune presentation that may herald an occult malignancy (paraneoplastic syndromes often precede cancer diagnosis) (p.2826)","Anti-Ro-positive pregnancy: IgG anti-Ro crosses the placenta and can cause neonatal lupus and congenital heart block in the fetus (p.2825)","Multiple positive autoantibody specificities (e.g., several islet antibodies in at-risk individuals) signaling high likelihood of progression to overt disease (p.2825)"],"prognosis":"ADs are described as chronic, incurable conditions that significantly impair quality of life and, in many cases, carry accelerated mortality; as a group they are among the top 10 leading causes of death in women. An individual with one AD has elevated risk of additional autoimmune conditions, certain cancers, and premature cardiovascular disease. Autoantibodies (e.g., ACPA in RA, islet antibodies in T1DM, antimitochondrial antibodies in PBC) may precede clinical disease by years to a decade and can mark worse prognosis. Better understanding of pathophysiology is hoped to enable primary or secondary prevention (e.g., trials of oral insulin in T1DM-autoantibody-positive infants and at-risk RA studies), though more data are needed (p.2822, p.2825-2826).","pitfalls":["Equating autoimmunity with autoimmune disease: autoantibodies/autoreactivity occur in healthy individuals (and relatives) and increase with age without necessarily causing disease (p.2821-2822, p.2825).","Assuming any detected autoantibody is pathogenic — several are used only as diagnostic or activity/outcome markers without a proven causal role (p.2825).","Overlooking that classification criteria (e.g., for SLE) were designed for clinical-trial enrollment, not for individual diagnosis (contextual, p.2827).","Attributing AD to infection on circumstantial grounds — causality is often unproven and supported mainly by animal models (p.2825-2826).","Missing iatrogenic causes: failing to recognize drug-induced lupus (antihistone antibodies) or checkpoint-inhibitor irAEs, where stopping the agent may resolve symptoms (p.2826)."],"severityClassification":"No single unifying severity grading exists for autoimmunity as a whole; severity and classification are disease-specific and covered in the individual disease chapters. Autoantibody breadth/titer (e.g., number of islet antibodies in T1DM risk) and specific specificities (e.g., anti-TIF1-gamma marking cancer-associated myositis, anti-HMGCR statin-induced myositis, anti-cN1A inclusion body myositis) help stratify risk and outcome (p.2825).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2821","Harrison 22e p.2822","Harrison 22e p.2823","Harrison 22e p.2824","Harrison 22e p.2825","Harrison 22e p.2826"],"crossLinks":[]},"awareness_under_anaesthesia":{"name":"Awareness under anaesthesia","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["intraoperative awareness","accidental awareness during general anaesthesia","AAGA","anaesthesia awareness"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Refers to a patient becoming conscious during intended general anaesthesia and later recalling events, which may be explicit (remembered) or implicit","Experiences range from vague auditory recall to distressing sensations of paralysis and pain, and can lead to lasting psychological harm including post-traumatic stress","Risk factors include use of neuromuscular blockade (which masks movement), difficult airway management, haemodynamic instability limiting anaesthetic depth, and certain high-risk settings such as obstetric, cardiac, and trauma anaesthesia","Total intravenous techniques carry particular risk if drug delivery fails or is interrupted, so vigilance over the infusion is essential","Prevention includes confirming delivery and adequate dosing of anaesthetic, monitoring end-tidal volatile concentration, checking equipment, and considering processed EEG depth-of-anaesthesia monitoring in high-risk cases","When paralysed, patients cannot signal distress, so clinical signs of light anaesthesia (movement, autonomic changes) may be blunted and must be interpreted with care","If awareness is suspected or reported, a compassionate response, honest explanation, and structured psychological support and follow-up are important","Documentation and use of a structured interview help confirm the event and guide support and any formal reporting"],"pathophysiology":"General anaesthesia suppresses consciousness by acting on the brain, and adequate depth depends on delivering sufficient anaesthetic to the effect site relative to individual requirement and surgical stimulation. Awareness occurs when brain anaesthetic concentration is inadequate to prevent consciousness while the patient is unable to communicate, often because neuromuscular blockade prevents movement. Contributing mechanisms include underdosing to protect a compromised circulation, equipment or infusion failure, increased anaesthetic requirement, and pharmacokinetic variability. Explicit memory formation during light anaesthesia can lead to conscious recall and psychological sequelae.","additionalDifferentials":["Dreaming during anaesthesia without true awareness","Recall of events during emergence rather than maintenance","Sedation-level consciousness where full anaesthesia was not intended","False memory or confabulation","Postoperative delirium affecting recall"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured interview (such as a modified Brice questionnaire) to characterise recall","Review of anaesthetic records, drug delivery, and end-tidal agent concentrations","Equipment and infusion pump checks to identify delivery failure","Processed EEG depth-of-anaesthesia data where recorded","Psychological assessment and referral for affected patients"],"redFlags":["Reports of explicit recall with pain or the sensation of paralysis","Evidence of severe psychological distress or post-traumatic stress after surgery","Recognised anaesthetic delivery failure during a paralysed case","Signs of inadequate anaesthesia in a paralysed patient at high risk"],"prognosis":"Many episodes are brief with limited recall, but distressing awareness can cause persistent anxiety, sleep disturbance, and post-traumatic stress disorder; early acknowledgement and psychological support improve outcomes.","pitfalls":["Relying on movement to gauge depth in a neuromuscularly blocked patient","Failing to notice interrupted or failed intravenous anaesthetic delivery","Dismissing or disbelieving a patient's report of awareness","Not offering timely psychological support and follow-up","Omitting depth monitoring in recognised high-risk situations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"babesiosis":{"name":"Babesiosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Babesia"],"chapter":232,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1775","clinicalPearls":["Emerging tick-borne infection caused by intraerythrocytic protozoa of the genus Babesia that invade and lyse red blood cells; most U.S. cases are due to Babesia microti, transmitted by the deer tick Ixodes scapularis, with ~90% presenting June through August (p.1775, 1777).","Presents as a flu-like illness: gradual-onset fatigue/malaise followed within days by fever (can reach 40.9 C) with chills, night sweats, headache, myalgia, and anorexia; dark urine and jaundice raise suspicion of severe hemolytic anemia (p.1777).","Because the same I. scapularis tick co-transmits Borrelia burgdorferi and Anaplasma phagocytophilum, co-infection is common: about one-half of patients diagnosed with babesiosis are also infected with B. burgdorferi, so a babesiosis diagnosis should prompt evaluation for Lyme disease, and vice versa (p.1778).","Most symptomatic patients (~80%) are 50 years or older; hospitalized patients are roughly a decade older (median 68 years) (p.1777).","Splenic complications (infarction, subcapsular hematoma, rupture) can occur even with low-grade parasitemia (median ~1%) and typically affect young, healthy patients (p.1777, 1780)."],"pathophysiology":"Babesia parasites invade and lyse red blood cells. RBC debris from hemolysis can accumulate in the renal vasculature and cause renal failure. Free hemoglobin is complexed by haptoglobin, then the oxidized heme group by hemopexin; excess free hemoglobin scavenges nitric oxide, promoting thrombus formation and vascular inflammation. RBC lysis in splenic capillaries can trigger localized necrosis and splenic infarction; oxidatively stressed, poorly deformable RBCs are filtered and phagocytosed by splenic macrophages, and erythrophagocytosis with the immune response contributes to splenomegaly and rupture. Fever, chills, and sweats reflect a systemic inflammatory response to RBC lysis, and excessive inflammation drives end-organ (renal and pulmonary) damage. The spleen is the immunodominant organ, explaining why asplenia is a major risk factor for severe disease; protective immunity involves CD4+ Th1 cells (severe disease in AIDS and transplant recipients), and B cells/antibodies appear critical for clearance in some patients (relapsing disease after rituximab). Persistent anemia after infection clears has been attributed to autoantibodies tagging RBCs for clearance (p.1778).","additionalDifferentials":["Lyme disease (concurrent or co-transmitted by the same tick)","Anaplasmosis (Anaplasma phagocytophilum)","Borrelia miyamotoi infection","HELLP syndrome in pregnant women at risk of tick exposure (babesiosis is an imitator)","Other causes of febrile hemolytic anemia"],"infectionMimics":["Malaria (Plasmodium falciparum and others) — intraerythrocytic febrile illness most readily confused with babesiosis","Lyme disease (Borrelia burgdorferi)","Anaplasmosis (Anaplasma phagocytophilum)","Borrelia miyamotoi infection"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["HELLP syndrome (hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets) in pregnancy","Other hemolytic anemias / thrombotic microangiopathies presenting with hemolysis and thrombocytopenia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Giemsa-stained thin blood smear (microscopy) — definitive diagnosis; trophozoites appear as ring/ameboid forms, and merozoites arranged in tetrads form the pathognomonic 'Maltese cross'","Real-time PCR amplifying the parasite 18S rRNA gene (detects 1–10 parasites/uL; allows speciation; used to monitor low-grade infection and relapse risk)","Complete blood count (anemia, often severe thrombocytopenia preceding severe anemia; WBC may be low, normal, or high) and reticulocyte count","Hemolysis markers: low haptoglobin, elevated LDH, elevated total bilirubin","Liver enzymes (alkaline phosphatase, AST, ALT) and renal function (BUN, creatinine)","Urinalysis (urobilinogen, hemoglobinuria, proteinuria)","Indirect fluorescent antibody serology (IgM and IgG titers) — a single positive result is NOT sufficient for diagnosis because antibodies can persist for over a year","CT for suspected splenic infarction or rupture"],"redFlags":["Severe disease especially in asplenic (congenital, functional, or post-splenectomy), elderly, and immunocompromised (HIV/AIDS, transplant, malignancy, B-cell-depleting/rituximab therapy) patients","Dark urine and jaundice indicating severe hemolytic anemia, with scleral icterus on examination","Left-sided abdominal pain, unexplained hypotension with tachycardia, or hemodynamic instability suggesting splenic infarction or rupture","Acute renal failure (leading complication, ~20%) and acute respiratory failure (~7%); also DIC, shock, arrhythmia, heart failure","Parasitemia >4% (risk factor for severe, complicated disease; can reach 30–40% in immunocompromised patients)","Diagnosis delayed >7 days after symptom onset predisposes to severe complicated disease","Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis from massive phagocytosis of blood cells (fatal)"],"prognosis":"With first-line atovaquone plus azithromycin, a fatal outcome is rare; of 7612 CDC-reported cases in 2011–2015, 46 (0.6%) ended in death. Older regimens (clindamycin plus quinine) had fatality rates of 5–9%. Death is more frequent in those 65 years or older. Among Medicare recipients diagnosed 2006–2013, ~1% died within 30 days; fatality was ~3% among hospitalized patients. Fatality is high in immunocompromised patients (~20%) and in those with splenic rupture or infarction (~6%). Fatigue may persist for weeks to months after treatment. Highly immunocompromised patients are at risk of persistent, relapsing infection (p.1778).","pitfalls":["Misidentifying Babesia as malaria on the blood smear: distinguish by absence of central brownish hemozoin pigment, presence of extracellular merozoites, absence of gametocytes and schizonts, and the pathognomonic tetrad ('Maltese cross'); a negative BinaxNOW malaria test helps rule out falciparum malaria when microscopy is ambiguous (p.1779).","Relying on a single positive serology for diagnosis — antibodies persist for over a year after the parasite is cleared (p.1779).","Missing co-infection: failing to evaluate for Lyme disease (and other tick-borne pathogens) when babesiosis is diagnosed, or for babesiosis when Lyme symptoms worsen or fail to abate on appropriate antibiotics (p.1778).","Overlooking splenic complications, which can occur with low-grade parasitemia and in young, otherwise healthy patients (p.1777, 1780).","Routine follow-up PCR is not recommended in immunocompetent patients (parasite DNA detectable up to 3 months) since relapse is unlikely, and persistent fatigue alone does not warrant extending or resuming therapy (p.1779)."],"severityClassification":"Severe babesiosis requires hospital admission (median stay 4 days). Severe, complicated babesiosis is defined as illness requiring ICU admission; complicated by ARDS, heart failure, or shock; or requiring dialysis, intubation, or RBC exchange. Diarrhea and nausea/vomiting are strong predictors of severe disease, and asplenia and autoimmune disorders predispose to it. Parasitemia is typically 0.1–10% in immunocompetent patients (up to 30–40% in immunocompromised); parasitemia >4% is a risk factor for severe complicated disease (p.1777-1778).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1775"],"crossLinks":["MALARIA","lyme_disease","hemolytic_anemia"]},"bacterial_keratitis":{"name":"Bacterial keratitis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["microbial keratitis","bacterial corneal ulcer","infective keratitis","corneal ulcer"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with a painful red eye, photophobia, watering, discharge, and reduced vision, usually developing over hours to days","Examination shows a focal white or grey corneal infiltrate with an overlying epithelial defect that stains with fluorescein; a hypopyon may be present in severe cases","Contact lens wear (especially overnight or poor hygiene) is the leading risk factor, alongside corneal trauma, ocular surface disease, and previous surgery","Pseudomonas is an important and rapidly destructive pathogen in contact lens wearers; Staphylococci and Streptococci are also common","Corneal scraping for Gram stain and culture should ideally be taken before starting intensive treatment in significant ulcers","Management is with intensive topical broad-spectrum antibiotics, initially given very frequently, and stopping contact lens use","Central ulcers, large infiltrates, or delayed treatment risk scarring, perforation, and permanent visual loss"],"pathophysiology":"Bacterial keratitis develops when the corneal epithelial barrier is breached (by trauma, contact lens wear, dry eye, or surgery), allowing bacteria to invade the corneal stroma. Bacterial proliferation and the release of enzymes and toxins, together with the host inflammatory response and recruited neutrophils, cause stromal necrosis and an infiltrate. Collagen-degrading enzymes can rapidly thin the cornea, leading to ulceration, descemetocele formation, and perforation. Healing occurs with scarring that reduces corneal clarity, particularly when the visual axis is involved.","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex (dendritic) keratitis","Fungal keratitis","Acanthamoeba keratitis","Marginal (hypersensitivity) keratitis","Sterile contact-lens-related infiltrates","Anterior uveitis or acute angle-closure glaucoma (for the red painful eye)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination with fluorescein staining to define the infiltrate and epithelial defect","Corneal scraping for Gram stain, culture, and sensitivities","Contact lens and lens case cultures where relevant","Measurement of infiltrate size and assessment of corneal thinning","Confocal microscopy or specialised staining if Acanthamoeba or fungal infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Sight-threatening — central or large ulcers can scar the visual axis or perforate the cornea","Rapid progression, especially in a contact lens wearer, suggesting Pseudomonas","Hypopyon, corneal thinning, or descemetocele indicating impending perforation","Worsening despite treatment raising suspicion of fungal or Acanthamoeba infection"],"prognosis":"Prompt intensive antibiotic treatment usually controls infection, but a central scar, large ulcer, or delayed care can leave permanent visual impairment; perforation may require corneal grafting.","pitfalls":["Prescribing topical steroids or a patch that can worsen infection, particularly with undiagnosed herpetic or fungal disease","Failing to scrape a significant ulcer for culture before starting treatment","Underestimating the aggressiveness of contact-lens-associated Pseudomonas keratitis","Missing Acanthamoeba in a contact lens wearer with pain out of proportion to signs"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"bacterial_vaginosis":{"name":"Bacterial Vaginosis","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["BV","vaginal dysbiosis","Gardnerella vaginitis","anaerobic vaginosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Bacterial vaginosis reflects a shift in the vaginal microbiome away from protective lactobacilli toward an overgrowth of anaerobes rather than a single infecting pathogen","The characteristic feature is a thin, homogeneous, greyish-white discharge with a fishy odour that is often most noticeable after intercourse","It is typically not associated with significant inflammation, so vulval itching and soreness are usually mild or absent, distinguishing it from candidiasis and trichomoniasis","Clinical diagnosis using the Amsel criteria combines discharge characteristics, raised vaginal pH, a positive whiff test with potassium hydroxide, and clue cells on microscopy","Clue cells are vaginal epithelial cells studded with adherent bacteria and are a hallmark microscopic finding","Treatment targets anaerobes with agents such as metronidazole or clindamycin, but recurrence is common and reflects failure to re-establish a lactobacillus-dominant flora","In pregnancy it is associated with an increased risk of preterm birth, late miscarriage, and postpartum endometritis, so symptomatic pregnant women are treated","It also increases susceptibility to sexually transmitted infections including HIV and to post-procedural pelvic infection"],"pathophysiology":"The healthy vagina is dominated by lactobacilli that produce lactic acid and hydrogen peroxide, maintaining an acidic environment that suppresses other organisms. In bacterial vaginosis this population is depleted and replaced by a polymicrobial overgrowth of anaerobes such as Gardnerella vaginalis, Prevotella, Mobiluncus, and Atopobium, often organised into a biofilm on the epithelium. The resulting rise in vaginal pH and production of amines generates the typical odour and discharge, while the biofilm contributes to treatment resistance and recurrence.","additionalDifferentials":["Vulvovaginal candidiasis","Trichomonas vaginalis infection","Physiological or cyclical vaginal discharge","Retained foreign body such as a tampon","Desquamative inflammatory vaginitis","Cervicitis from gonorrhoea or chlamydia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Measurement of vaginal pH, which is typically elevated above 4.5","Whiff (amine) test with potassium hydroxide to detect a fishy odour","Saline wet-mount microscopy for clue cells and reduced lactobacilli","Gram stain scoring of vaginal flora where laboratory diagnosis is used","Testing for co-existing sexually transmitted infections when risk factors are present"],"redFlags":["Symptomatic bacterial vaginosis in pregnancy given the association with preterm birth and miscarriage","Signs of ascending infection such as pelvic pain, fever, or cervical motion tenderness suggesting pelvic inflammatory disease","Bacterial vaginosis before termination or intrauterine procedures, increasing post-procedure infection risk","Persistent atypical discharge not responding to treatment, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Coexisting features of a sexually transmitted infection"],"prognosis":"Symptoms usually respond well to appropriate antibiotic therapy, but recurrence within months is frequent because the protective lactobacillus flora and the biofilm are difficult to eradicate permanently. It is not itself a serious infection but its associations with adverse pregnancy outcomes, post-procedural infection, and enhanced susceptibility to sexually transmitted infections give it clinical importance.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for candidiasis and treating with antifungals","Overtreating asymptomatic women outside pregnancy or pre-procedure settings","Failing to consider it in recurrent or malodorous discharge with minimal inflammation","Not screening for concurrent sexually transmitted infections when risk exists","Overlooking its role in preterm birth in symptomatic pregnant women"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"bartholin_abscess":{"name":"Bartholin's Abscess","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bartholin abscess","bartholin gland abscess","bartholin cyst abscess","infected bartholin cyst"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The Bartholin glands lie at the posterior aspect of the vaginal introitus and secrete mucus for lubrication; obstruction of their duct leads to cyst formation and secondary infection produces an abscess","An abscess typically presents as a painful, tender, fluctuant swelling in the posterolateral labium majus that can make sitting and walking difficult","Infection is usually polymicrobial with vaginal and enteric flora, though sexually transmitted organisms such as gonorrhoea and chlamydia should be considered","Definitive treatment relies on drainage rather than antibiotics alone, and simple incision and drainage alone has a high recurrence rate","Placement of a Word catheter or marsupialisation creates an epithelialised tract that allows continued drainage and reduces recurrence","Antibiotics are reserved for surrounding cellulitis, systemic features, immunocompromise, or high-risk sexually transmitted infection rather than routine drained abscesses","In women over the age of forty, and particularly postmenopausal women, a persistent or atypical Bartholin mass should raise concern for the rare Bartholin gland carcinoma and prompt biopsy","Sitz baths and analgesia support symptom relief and can encourage spontaneous drainage of small abscesses"],"pathophysiology":"Obstruction of the Bartholin duct, often following inflammation or inspissated secretions, causes retention of mucus and cystic dilatation of the gland. Stasis within the obstructed duct favours bacterial proliferation, and secondary infection with mixed vaginal, enteric, and occasionally sexually transmitted organisms produces a walled-off collection of pus. The resulting abscess generates local inflammation, oedema, and pain, and if untreated may point and rupture spontaneously or recur once the duct re-obstructs.","additionalDifferentials":["Uninfected Bartholin duct cyst","Vulvar epidermal inclusion or sebaceous cyst","Vulvar abscess from other skin flora or hidradenitis suppurativa","Skene gland cyst or abscess","Bartholin gland carcinoma in older women","Vulvar haematoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination to confirm location, fluctuance, and surrounding cellulitis","Swab of abscess fluid at drainage for culture and sensitivity","Testing for gonorrhoea and chlamydia including nucleic acid amplification where appropriate","Biopsy of the gland wall in women over forty or with atypical or recurrent masses","Consideration of glucose testing to identify undiagnosed diabetes in recurrent infection"],"redFlags":["Spreading vulval cellulitis, systemic sepsis, or features suggesting necrotising infection","Rapidly enlarging or irregular mass, or one in a postmenopausal woman, raising suspicion of carcinoma","Recurrent abscesses that may indicate underlying diabetes or immunosuppression","Severe pain with induration extending beyond the gland","Failure to resolve after adequate drainage"],"prognosis":"Most Bartholin abscesses resolve well after adequate drainage, and techniques that create a lasting drainage tract, such as catheter placement or marsupialisation, substantially lower recurrence. Recurrent disease may require repeat procedures or gland excision, and the outlook is excellent overall provided malignancy is excluded in older or atypical cases.","pitfalls":["Treating with antibiotics alone without adequate drainage","Relying on simple incision and drainage, which frequently recurs, instead of a definitive drainage procedure","Failing to test for sexually transmitted infections when relevant","Overlooking Bartholin carcinoma in an older woman with a persistent solid mass","Not investigating recurrent abscesses for underlying diabetes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"bartter_syndrome":{"name":"Bartter syndrome","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Bartter's syndrome","salt-wasting tubulopathy","loop-type salt-wasting nephropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Bartter syndrome is an inherited salt-wasting tubulopathy that behaves as if the patient were chronically taking a loop diuretic, because the defect lies in transport at the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle","The typical biochemistry is hypokalaemia, metabolic alkalosis, and renal salt wasting with normal or low blood pressure despite high renin and aldosterone","Unlike Gitelman syndrome, urinary calcium excretion is usually normal or increased, and nephrocalcinosis may develop","Antenatal and neonatal forms present early with polyhydramnios, prematurity, and severe volume depletion, whereas the classic form presents later in childhood","Renin, aldosterone, and urinary prostaglandin levels are elevated, reflecting the compensatory response to persistent volume and chloride loss","Blood pressure is characteristically normal or low, which helps distinguish it from hyperaldosteronism where hypertension is expected","Management principles include generous potassium and salt repletion, potassium-sparing strategies, and blockade of prostaglandin synthesis to reduce salt wasting"],"pathophysiology":"Bartter syndrome comprises a group of autosomal recessive defects in the ion transporters of the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, the site of action of loop diuretics. Loss of function in the sodium-potassium-chloride cotransporter or its associated potassium and chloride channels impairs sodium chloride reabsorption, causing renal salt and water wasting. The resulting volume contraction activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis, and the combination of increased distal sodium delivery and high aldosterone drives potassium and hydrogen secretion, producing hypokalaemia and metabolic alkalosis. Because the transporter normally contributes to the electrochemical gradient favouring calcium reabsorption, its loss leaves calcium excretion normal or elevated. Overproduction of prostaglandins amplifies the salt wasting and the activation of renin.","additionalDifferentials":["Gitelman syndrome","Surreptitious loop diuretic use","Chronic vomiting or bulimia","Laxative abuse","Primary hyperaldosteronism (distinguished by hypertension)","Congenital chloride diarrhoea"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum potassium, bicarbonate, magnesium, and chloride to characterise the alkalosis and electrolyte losses","Plasma renin and aldosterone, typically both elevated","Urinary calcium-to-creatinine ratio to separate it from Gitelman syndrome","Urine electrolytes and screening for surreptitious diuretic use","Renal ultrasound for nephrocalcinosis","Genetic testing when the diagnosis is uncertain or for family counselling"],"redFlags":["Severe hypokalaemia causing arrhythmia or profound weakness","Marked volume depletion and dehydration, especially in neonates","Failure to thrive or growth retardation in children","Progressive nephrocalcinosis with declining renal function"],"prognosis":"With consistent electrolyte and fluid replacement and prostaglandin inhibition, most patients maintain reasonable growth and function, though lifelong therapy is required. Severe antenatal forms carry higher perinatal risk, and chronic hypokalaemia and nephrocalcinosis can gradually impair renal function.","pitfalls":["Mistaking surreptitious diuretic or laxative abuse for a genetic tubulopathy without appropriate screening","Confusing Bartter with Gitelman syndrome by not checking urinary calcium and serum magnesium","Overlooking the diagnosis in a normotensive patient with unexplained hypokalaemic alkalosis","Inadequate potassium and magnesium repletion leaving the patient symptomatic"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"basal_cell_carcinoma":{"name":"Basal Cell Carcinoma","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["BCC","rodent ulcer","basal cell cancer"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The classic nodular type is a slow-growing, pearly or translucent papule with rolled edges, surface telangiectasia, and sometimes central ulceration (the rodent ulcer)","It is the most common human cancer and arises chiefly on chronically sun-exposed skin, especially the head and neck","Recognise the main subtypes: nodular, superficial (often a scaly erythematous patch on the trunk), morphoeic/infiltrative (a scar-like indurated plaque with ill-defined margins), and pigmented","Dermoscopy showing arborising vessels, blue-grey ovoid nests, and leaf-like areas supports the diagnosis","Although BCC almost never metastasises, it is locally invasive and can cause substantial tissue destruction if neglected, particularly around the eyes, nose, and ears","Treatment is guided by subtype, size, site, and risk, ranging from surgical excision (with margin-controlled surgery for high-risk facial lesions) to topical or destructive therapies for low-risk superficial disease","Multiple or early-onset BCCs should prompt consideration of predisposing conditions such as basal cell naevus (Gorlin) syndrome or prior radiation exposure","Advise lifelong photoprotection and skin surveillance, as a diagnosis of BCC predicts further skin cancers"],"pathophysiology":"Basal cell carcinoma is a malignancy of the basal keratinocytes of the epidermis and follicular structures, driven predominantly by ultraviolet-induced DNA damage. Aberrant activation of the Hedgehog signalling pathway, commonly through inactivating mutations in the PTCH1 tumour-suppressor gene or activating changes in SMO, is central to tumour development, alongside UV signature mutations in TP53. The tumour grows as nests of basaloid cells with peripheral palisading and a surrounding fibromyxoid stroma. Its behaviour is locally invasive rather than metastatic, and infiltrative or morphoeic patterns spread with subclinical extension that explains higher recurrence when margins are inadequate.","additionalDifferentials":["Squamous cell carcinoma","Amelanotic or pigmented melanoma (for a pigmented BCC)","Intradermal or other benign melanocytic naevus","Sebaceous hyperplasia","Bowen's disease or a plaque of psoriasis or eczema (for superficial BCC)","Scar or morphoea (for morphoeic BCC)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnostic skin biopsy to confirm the diagnosis and define the histological subtype, which guides treatment","Dermoscopy as an adjunct to clinical assessment","Full skin examination for synchronous skin cancers","Imaging in advanced or deeply infiltrative disease to assess local extension","Consideration of genetic assessment when multiple lesions occur at a young age"],"redFlags":["A non-healing, ulcerating, or bleeding lesion on sun-exposed skin, which is a hallmark of malignancy","An ill-defined, indurated, scar-like plaque suggesting an infiltrative or morphoeic tumour with deep spread","Lesions at high-risk facial sites (eyes, nose, ears) where local destruction threatens vital structures","Numerous or early-onset tumours suggesting an inherited cancer syndrome or prior radiotherapy"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent because metastasis is exceedingly rare, and appropriately treated tumours are usually cured; however, incompletely excised or high-risk subtypes recur and can cause significant local morbidity, and patients remain at higher lifetime risk of further skin cancers requiring ongoing surveillance.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a morphoeic BCC for a scar and underestimating its true extent","Treating a superficial BCC as eczema or psoriasis and delaying diagnosis","Choosing a non-surgical or narrow-margin approach for high-risk or recurrent facial tumours","Failing to arrange ongoing skin surveillance after a first diagnosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"basic_biology_of_the_cardiovascular":{"name":"Basic Biology of the Cardiovascular System","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Cardiovascular system biology","Cardiac and vascular cell biology","Cardiovascular developmental biology"],"chapter":244,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1843","clinicalPearls":["The heart arises from crescent-shaped fields of lateral splanchnic mesoderm; a primary (first) heart field forms the linear heart tube while a second heart field, marked by Islet-1 expression, later adds myocardium to the inflow/outflow poles and contributes much of the right ventricle and outflow tract.","Neural crest cells are required for aortic arch remodeling and truncus arteriosus septation; because these cells are sensitive to vitamin A and folic acid, maternal deficiency of these vitamins is associated with congenital defects of aortic arch remodeling.","Coronary arteries depend on epicardial cells derived from the proepicardial organ (a septum transversum derivative that also forms the fibrous diaphragm and contributes to the liver), which supply coronary smooth muscle and help pattern the vessels.","The cardiac conduction system differentiates from cardiomyocyte precursors into slow-conducting proximal components (SA and AV nodes) and fast-conducting distal components (His bundle, bundle branches, Purkinje fibers); developmental defects can produce congenital heart block or pre-excitation (Wolff-Parkinson-White).","Decremental conduction across the AV node delays atrial-to-ventricular impulse transmission to allow sequential contraction and protects the ventricle by limiting transmission of rapid atrial rates.","The endothelium is a selectively permeable barrier and a major homeostatic organ: under physiologic conditions it favors vasodilation, an antithrombotic/profibrinolytic state, anti-inflammatory and antioxidant properties; endothelial dysfunction reverses each of these (vasoconstriction, prothrombotic, proinflammatory, prooxidant, impaired barrier).","Endothelium-derived vasodilators include nitric oxide (NO), prostacyclin, endothelium-derived hyperpolarizing factor, and hydrogen peroxide; flow-mediated dilatation (brachial artery cuff occlusion/release with ultrasound) is used to assess endothelial vasodilator function in humans.","Excess reactive oxygen species (e.g., superoxide generated under high angiotensin II exposure) inactivate NO, and inflammatory stimuli/endotoxin/angiotensin II drive endothelial production of PAI-1 and tissue factor, shifting balance toward thrombosis (contributing to DIC in sepsis and to hyperglycemia-related prothrombotic states).","Vascular smooth-muscle contraction is governed by cytosolic calcium and the balance of myosin light chain kinase (calcium/calmodulin-activated) versus myosin light chain phosphatase; voltage-dependent L-type calcium channels and IP3-triggered sarcoplasmic reticulum release raise intracellular calcium.","Cardiac contraction follows the sliding-filament model: an L-type calcium current during the action potential plateau triggers calcium-induced calcium release from the SR via the ryanodine receptor RyR2; calcium binds troponin C, relieving troponin-tropomyosin inhibition so actin and myosin cross-bridge cycling (ATP-dependent) can occur.","Relaxation depends on lowering cytosolic calcium by SR reuptake via SERCA2A (regulated by phospholamban) and sarcolemmal Na+/Ca2+ exchange; PKA phosphorylation of phospholamban speeds reuptake and produces the lusitropic (enhanced relaxation) effect.","Cytoplasmic calcium is the principal determinant of inotropic state: positive inotropes such as digitalis glycosides and beta-adrenergic agonists act by raising cytosolic calcium (beta agonists via Gs-adenylyl cyclase-cyclic AMP-PKA phosphorylation of sarcolemmal calcium channels).","Ventricular stroke volume is set by three factors: preload (muscle length at contraction onset), afterload (tension developed during contraction), and contractility; contractile force is optimal near a sarcomere length of about 2.2 micrometers, the structural basis of the Frank-Starling relationship."],"pathophysiology":"The cardiovascular system develops from cardiac progenitors in lateral splanchnic mesoderm that fuse into a linear heart tube of endocardium and spontaneously beating cardiomyocytes, then undergo asymmetric looping and chamber specification with continued addition of second-heart-field cells (Islet-1 positive). Septation separates the cardiac chambers and divides the truncus arteriosus into aorta and pulmonary artery; endocardial cushions, rich in hyaluronic-acid 'cardiac jelly', undergo epithelial-mesenchymal transformation to form valves. Neural crest cells direct aortic arch remodeling and supply smooth muscle to the great vessels, while proepicardial cells form coronary vasculature. Blood vessels share a trilaminar architecture (intimal endothelium, smooth-muscle media, fibroblast-rich adventitia), with capillaries reduced to an endothelial tube and pericytes and large vessels nourished by vasa vasorum. The endothelium maintains vascular homeostasis through balanced production of vasodilators (NO, prostacyclin) and vasoconstrictors (endothelin), regulation of leukocyte adhesion, and modulation of coagulation; loss of these functions underlies atherosclerosis, hypertension, capillary-leak states, and immune-mediated vascular injury. Vascular smooth-muscle tone is set by intracellular calcium and myosin light chain phosphorylation, integrated through sympathetic, parasympathetic, and nonadrenergic/noncholinergic (nitrergic, peptidergic) autonomic signaling. In the myocardium, the sarcomere is the contractile unit; excitation-contraction coupling links the action potential and T-tubular calcium entry to RyR2-mediated SR calcium release, troponin C activation, and actin-myosin cross-bridge cycling, with relaxation driven by SERCA2A reuptake and Na+/Ca2+ exchange. Cardiac output reflects the interplay of preload, afterload, and contractility (Frank-Starling mechanism).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flow-mediated dilatation of the brachial artery by ultrasound (cuff occlusion/release) to assess endothelial vasodilator function in vivo"],"redFlags":["Inherited or acquired disturbances of SR calcium handling (RyR2/regulatory protein abnormalities) can impair contraction and predispose to heart failure or ventricular arrhythmias.","Mutations in sarcomeric and cytoskeletal proteins (including titin, dystrophin/dystroglycan complex) cause Mendelian cardiomyopathies and sensitize the heart to toxic insults such as alcohol or chemotherapy and to inflammatory or peripartum cardiomyopathy.","Developmental conduction-system defects may present as congenital heart block or pre-excitation (Wolff-Parkinson-White)."],"prognosis":"As a foundational chapter, prognosis is framed at the level of mechanism: increased arterial stiffness with aging or disease raises left ventricular afterload and portends a poor outcome, and sustained high-flow states (e.g., left-to-right shunts) drive pulmonary smooth-muscle proliferation and pulmonary hypertension. Outcomes in specific cardiovascular diseases are detailed in their respective chapters.","pitfalls":["Treating these molecular and developmental concepts as purely theoretical: shared embryonic origins of cardiovascular cell types explain syndromic associations between congenital heart disease and extracardiac anomalies.","Overlooking that true regeneration of a complete three-layered blood vessel does not normally occur in adult mammals, so adult arteriogenesis/angiogenesis reflects growth and collateral expansion rather than new full-thickness vessel formation.","Assuming adult vascular smooth-muscle cells are quiescent; they proliferate and migrate after injury or inflammatory activation, contributing to atherosclerotic stenosis, hypertensive arteriolar remodeling, and restenosis."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1843","Harrison 22e p.1845","Harrison 22e p.1847","Harrison 22e p.1849"],"crossLinks":[]},"beh_et_syndrome":{"name":"Behçet Syndrome","system":"Rheumatologic / Multisystem vasculitis","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Behçet's disease","Behcet syndrome","Behcet disease"],"chapter":376,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2908-2909","clinicalPearls":["A systemic vasculitis presenting with various combinations of oral and genital ulcers, skin lesions, uveitis, arthritis, large vessel (arterial and venous) disease, and gastrointestinal and neurologic involvement, with manifestations appearing in differing sequences over time.","Oral ulcers occur in essentially all patients (~98%) and are usually the first manifestation; they are typically multiple, resemble ordinary canker sores, last about 10 days, and recur unless treated, with only the uncommon major ulcers tending to scar.","Genital ulcers are the most specific lesion, most often on the scrotum or labia; they are larger, deeper, slower to heal than oral ulcers, and tend to scar.","The pathergy reaction (a papule or pustule forming 24-48 h after a needle prick) is a fairly unique nonspecific skin hyperreactivity and is part of the diagnostic criteria.","Hypopyon (intense anterior chamber inflammation), seen in about 10% of patients with eye disease, is quite specific for Behçet syndrome.","Superficial thrombophlebitis often occurs in men and is associated with deep-vein thrombosis; its presence should prompt a workup for other vascular involvement including pulmonary artery aneurysm.","HLA-B*51 is positive in up to 50-60% of patients depending on geographic origin, but it is not diagnostic because roughly 20% of the normal population also carries it.","Most prevalent in Turkey (about 1 in 250 adults); patients are most commonly from the Middle East, Mediterranean, and Far East. Onset before the late teens or after age 50 is uncommon.","Sexes are affected roughly equally, but males tend to have more severe disease and worse outcomes; eye involvement may reach ~70% in males.","Ocular disease (most often bilateral panuveitis) usually appears within the first 2 years after diagnostic criteria are met, is most severe early, then tends to abate.","Most new manifestations appear within the first 5 years, and the natural history for most patients is one of diminishing symptoms that may culminate in remission."],"pathophysiology":"The cause and pathogenesis are unknown. Family studies suggest a possible genetic predisposition, and heightened inflammation with involvement of both innate and adaptive immunity is implicated. Neutrophil hyperreactivity is present, though it is unclear whether this is primary or secondary to cytokine-driven activation. The disorder sits between classic autoimmune and autoinflammatory conditions: unlike typical autoimmune disease it is not generally associated with autoantibodies, Raynaud's phenomenon, Sjögren's syndrome, thrombocytopenia, hemolytic anemia, photosensitivity, serosal involvement, or increased risk of other autoimmune diseases; unlike classic autoinflammatory disease it tends to abate over time, lacks the mutations seen in autoinflammatory syndromes, and is more prevalent than disorders such as familial Mediterranean fever. Retrospective cohort data suggest distinct disease clusters (e.g., acne lesions clustering with arthritis and enthesitis) that may have differing pathogeneses.","additionalDifferentials":["Crohn's disease (gastrointestinal Behçet can be clinically and endoscopically indistinguishable; distinction often relies on extraintestinal features)","Recurrent aphthous stomatitis / ordinary canker sores","Acne vulgaris (papulopustular lesions are indistinguishable in appearance and pathology)","Other systemic vasculitides","Inflammatory bowel disease more broadly"],"infectionMimics":["Herpetic and other infectious genital/oral ulceration causing recurrent ulcers"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Crohn's disease (especially with ileocecal ulcers)","Acne vulgaris","Recurrent aphthous stomatitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis is clinical; no specific laboratory, imaging, or histologic test confirms it, so investigations mainly serve to exclude mimics.","International Study Group (ISG) criteria: recurrent oral ulceration (at least 3 times in 12 months) plus two of recurrent genital ulcers, skin lesions, eye lesions, or a positive pathergy test (sensitivity ~95%, specificity ~96%).","Pathergy test: dermal insertion of a 20-gauge (20-21 gauge) needle, read at 24-48 h.","HLA-B*51 testing may support but cannot establish the diagnosis.","Endoscopy for suspected gastrointestinal involvement (ulcers typically single or fewer than five, deep, round, confined to the ileocecal area, prone to perforation).","Vascular and neuroimaging when vascular or CNS involvement is suspected (e.g., pulmonary artery aneurysm, cerebral venous sinus thrombosis, parenchymal CNS lesions)."],"redFlags":["Superficial thrombophlebitis in a male should trigger evaluation for deeper vascular involvement including pulmonary artery aneurysm.","Pulmonary artery aneurysms carry a 5-year mortality of about 20-25%.","Subacute severe headache with cranial nerve palsy, dysarthria, ataxia, or hemiparesis suggests parenchymal CNS involvement (telencephalic-diencephalic junction, brainstem, spinal cord).","Deep, round ileocecal ulcers are prone to perforation.","Clustering thrombotic events (deep-vein thrombosis, Budd-Chiari syndrome, IVC syndrome, intracardiac thrombosis, cerebral venous sinus thrombosis)."],"prognosis":"Most patients follow a course of diminishing symptoms over time with potential remission, often eventually requiring no ongoing medication, and most new manifestations emerge within the first 5 years. Young male patients tend to have worse outcomes. Poor ocular prognostic factors include male sex, posterior involvement, frequent attacks (>3 per year), strong vitreous opacity, and macular edema. Pulmonary artery aneurysms are associated with a 5-year mortality of about 20-25%.","pitfalls":["Diagnosis may take months to years as the full constellation of features evolves; a tentative diagnosis often precedes a definitive one.","HLA-B*51 is not a diagnostic test because it is common in the general population.","Gastrointestinal Behçet is easily mistaken for Crohn's disease unless extraintestinal manifestations are evident.","Pathergy and eye lesions each occur in only about half of patients, so their absence does not exclude the diagnosis.","Regional variation matters: gastrointestinal involvement is common in Japan and seen in ~30% of US patients but rare in Turkey."],"severityClassification":"No formal severity grade; management and outlook are stratified by the type and severity of organ involvement (mucocutaneous, ocular, vascular, gastrointestinal, neurologic), with young male sex and posterior/severe eye disease marking higher-risk disease.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2908","Harrison 22e p.2909"],"crossLinks":[]},"behcet_disease":{"name":"Behçet disease","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Behcet disease","Behçet syndrome","Silk Road disease","variable-vessel vasculitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A variable-vessel vasculitis defined clinically by recurrent oral aphthous ulceration together with other features, as there is no specific diagnostic test","Recurrent painful genital ulcers and skin lesions such as erythema nodosum and pseudofolliculitis are characteristic","Ocular disease, particularly relapsing posterior uveitis and retinal vasculitis, is a major cause of vision loss","Pathergy (a papulo-pustular skin reaction to needle prick) supports the diagnosis where present","It is more prevalent along the historical Silk Road and is associated with HLA-B51","Vascular involvement is distinctive in affecting both arteries and veins, causing venous thromboses and arterial aneurysms, including pulmonary artery aneurysms","Neurological (neuro-Behçet) and gastrointestinal involvement carry significant morbidity","Treatment is tailored to organ involvement, using colchicine and topical measures for mucocutaneous disease and systemic immunosuppression or biologics for major organ threat"],"pathophysiology":"Behçet disease is a chronic relapsing inflammatory disorder classified as a variable-vessel vasculitis because it can affect vessels of any size and type, both arterial and venous. Its cause is uncertain but involves genetic susceptibility (notably HLA-B51) and dysregulated innate and adaptive immunity with prominent neutrophil hyperactivity, elevated Th1/Th17 responses, and inflammation triggered in predisposed individuals. Vascular inflammation produces thrombosis, aneurysm formation, and tissue damage, while immune-mediated inflammation drives the recurrent mucocutaneous, ocular, neurological, and gastrointestinal lesions.","additionalDifferentials":["Recurrent aphthous stomatitis without systemic disease","Inflammatory bowel disease with oral and skin manifestations","Reactive arthritis and other spondyloarthritides","Herpes simplex and other infective causes of genital ulceration","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Sarcoidosis and other causes of uveitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment against recognised diagnostic/classification criteria (no confirmatory laboratory test)","Pathergy test where available","Ophthalmological examination for uveitis and retinal vasculitis","Vascular imaging for venous thrombosis and arterial aneurysms, including pulmonary vessels","MRI brain and cerebrospinal fluid analysis when neuro-Behçet is suspected; HLA-B51 typing as supportive"],"redFlags":["Sudden or progressive visual loss from posterior uveitis or retinal vasculitis requiring urgent ophthalmology and immunosuppression","Neurological deficits (brainstem or cerebral involvement) or cerebral venous sinus thrombosis indicating neuro-Behçet","Pulmonary artery aneurysm presenting with haemoptysis, a life-threatening complication","Major vessel thrombosis or arterial aneurysm at risk of rupture","Severe gastrointestinal ulceration with bleeding or perforation"],"prognosis":"Course is relapsing and remitting and tends to be more severe in young men. Mucocutaneous disease is troublesome but not dangerous, whereas ocular, neurological, and major vascular involvement drive the risk of blindness, disability, and death. Early aggressive treatment of organ-threatening disease improves outcome.","pitfalls":["Treating recurrent oral ulcers in isolation and missing systemic Behçet disease","Delaying immunosuppression for eye involvement and risking irreversible vision loss","Managing vascular thrombosis with anticoagulation alone without addressing the underlying inflammation, and overlooking coexisting aneurysms","Attributing neurological symptoms to other causes rather than neuro-Behçet","Assuming absence of pathergy or HLA-B51 excludes the diagnosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"bells_palsy":{"name":"Bell's palsy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Bell palsy","idiopathic facial paralysis","acute peripheral facial palsy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acute idiopathic lower motor neuron facial palsy that involves the whole of one side of the face, including the forehead","Forehead sparing points instead to an upper motor neuron (central) lesion, because the upper face has bilateral cortical representation, and warrants a search for a stroke or other central cause","Onset is usually rapid over hours to a couple of days, and patients may report ipsilateral ear or postauricular pain preceding weakness","Associated features can include altered taste, hyperacusis, and reduced tear production depending on the level of the lesion along the facial nerve","Incomplete eye closure risks exposure keratopathy, so eye protection with lubrication and taping is an essential part of care","Early corticosteroid treatment improves the likelihood of complete recovery, and antiviral therapy is sometimes added in severe cases","Bilateral facial palsy or additional cranial nerve involvement suggests an alternative diagnosis and requires further investigation","A vesicular rash in the ear or palate indicates Ramsay Hunt syndrome from herpes zoster rather than Bell's palsy"],"pathophysiology":"Bell's palsy is an acute mononeuropathy of the facial (seventh cranial) nerve, widely attributed to inflammation and oedema of the nerve, often linked to reactivation of herpes simplex virus. Swelling of the nerve within the narrow bony facial canal causes compression, ischaemia, and demyelination, producing unilateral weakness of all facial muscles on the affected side. Depending on the segment involved, taste, lacrimation, and stapedius function may also be impaired.","additionalDifferentials":["Ischaemic or haemorrhagic stroke causing central facial weakness","Ramsay Hunt syndrome (herpes zoster oticus)","Lyme disease, especially with bilateral palsy","Cerebellopontine angle or parotid tumour compressing the facial nerve","Guillain-Barre syndrome and its variants","Otitis media, cholesteatoma, or skull base pathology; sarcoidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful clinical examination to confirm the lower motor neuron pattern and exclude other cranial nerve involvement","Otoscopy and skin inspection for vesicles suggesting Ramsay Hunt syndrome","Serology for Lyme disease in endemic areas or with bilateral palsy","MRI of the brain and facial nerve when the presentation is atypical, progressive, or recurrent","Investigation for sarcoidosis or other systemic causes when clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Forehead-sparing weakness or other focal deficits suggesting a central cause such as stroke","Gradually progressive or recurrent weakness suggesting a compressive or neoplastic lesion","Bilateral facial palsy prompting evaluation for systemic disease","Failure of eye closure with corneal exposure risking sight-threatening keratopathy"],"prognosis":"Most patients with Bell's palsy recover well, and the majority regain normal or near-normal function, particularly with early corticosteroid treatment and when weakness is incomplete. A minority, especially those with complete paralysis, are left with residual weakness, synkinesis, or contracture. Recovery beginning within the first few weeks predicts a favourable outcome.","pitfalls":["Missing a central cause by not checking for forehead involvement","Neglecting eye protection and allowing exposure keratopathy","Overlooking Ramsay Hunt syndrome or Lyme disease as specific, treatable causes","Failing to reassess a palsy that is progressive, recurrent, or slow to recover for an underlying structural lesion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"benign_prostatic_hyperplasia":{"name":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["BPH","benign prostatic enlargement","BPE","prostatic hyperplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically affects men over 50 and presents with lower urinary tract symptoms that are grouped into voiding (hesitancy, weak stream, intermittency, straining, incomplete emptying) and storage (frequency, urgency, nocturia) categories","Symptom severity correlates poorly with prostate size, so a large gland may be asymptomatic while a small gland can cause significant obstruction","Digital rectal examination usually reveals a smooth, firm, symmetrically enlarged gland; nodularity, asymmetry, or a hard area should raise suspicion of malignancy","The International Prostate Symptom Score (IPSS) is useful for quantifying symptom burden and monitoring response to treatment","Medical management principles include alpha-blockers to relax prostatic smooth muscle for rapid symptom relief and 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors to shrink the gland over months in larger prostates","Absolute surgical indications include refractory retention, recurrent urinary infection, bladder stones, recurrent haematuria, and upper tract dilatation or renal impairment from obstruction","Transurethral resection of the prostate remains the reference surgical standard, with newer minimally invasive and laser techniques offering alternatives","PSA may be mildly elevated due to gland volume, but a significantly raised or rising level warrants evaluation for prostate cancer"],"pathophysiology":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia is a proliferation of glandular epithelial and stromal cells in the transition zone of the prostate, driven by ageing and androgen (dihydrotestosterone) activity. The enlarging gland produces a static component of obstruction by mechanical narrowing of the prostatic urethra and a dynamic component from increased smooth muscle tone mediated by alpha-1 adrenergic receptors. Chronic outflow obstruction leads to detrusor muscle hypertrophy, trabeculation, and eventually detrusor decompensation, incomplete emptying, and rising post-void residual volumes, with risk of upper tract dilatation and renal impairment in advanced cases.","additionalDifferentials":["Prostate cancer","Urethral stricture","Bladder neck stenosis or contracture","Neurogenic bladder / detrusor dysfunction","Overactive bladder","Urinary tract infection or prostatitis","Bladder stone or bladder tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis to exclude infection and haematuria","Serum creatinine and renal function assessment","PSA measurement with counselling about interpretation","Post-void residual volume by bladder ultrasound","Uroflowmetry to assess flow rate and obstructive pattern","Renal and bladder ultrasound to assess upper tracts, bladder wall, and residual urine","Pressure-flow urodynamics in equivocal or complex cases"],"redFlags":["Acute urinary retention, a urological emergency requiring prompt catheterisation","Obstructive uropathy with hydronephrosis and renal impairment","Recurrent urinary infection or urosepsis associated with an obstructed, incompletely emptying bladder","Gross or persistent haematuria requiring exclusion of malignancy","Hard, nodular, or asymmetric prostate on examination suggesting cancer"],"prognosis":"BPH is a slowly progressive condition; many men remain stable or improve with medical therapy, but a subset progress to retention or complications requiring surgery, which generally produces durable symptom relief.","pitfalls":["Attributing all lower urinary tract symptoms to the prostate and missing bladder dysfunction, cancer, or stricture","Overlooking chronic high-residual retention presenting with overflow incontinence and silent renal impairment","Relying on prostate size alone to judge the need for treatment","Failing to counsel that 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors lower PSA and take months to act","Missing prostate cancer masked by a benign clinical picture"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"benzodiazepine_overdose":{"name":"Benzodiazepine overdose","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["benzodiazepine poisoning","benzodiazepine toxicity","sedative overdose"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Produces a sedative-hypnotic toxidrome of drowsiness, slurred speech, ataxia, and depressed consciousness, typically with preserved or only mildly depressed vital signs","Taken alone, benzodiazepines rarely cause death; serious respiratory depression usually reflects co-ingestion of alcohol, opioids, or other sedatives, or the parenteral route","The mainstay of care is supportive - airway protection, breathing support, and observation - rather than any specific antidote in most cases","Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist that reverses sedation, but its use is limited because it can precipitate seizures and a withdrawal state, particularly in chronic users or mixed overdoses","Flumazenil is especially hazardous when a proconvulsant (such as a tricyclic antidepressant) has been co-ingested, as it removes the seizure threshold-raising effect of the benzodiazepine","Marked or fluctuating conscious level, disproportionate hypotension, or significant respiratory depression should prompt a search for a co-ingested agent","Activated charcoal offers little benefit and risks aspiration in a drowsy patient, so it is generally not recommended","Consider alternative causes of coma (hypoglycaemia, head injury, other intoxicants) if the clinical picture does not fit a pure benzodiazepine effect"],"pathophysiology":"Benzodiazepines enhance the inhibitory action of gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) at the GABA-A receptor by increasing the frequency of chloride channel opening, producing central nervous system depression. Because they modulate rather than directly open the channel, there is a ceiling to their respiratory depressant effect when taken alone, which explains their relatively wide margin of safety in isolated overdose. When combined with other central depressants such as opioids or alcohol, this ceiling is lost and dangerous synergistic respiratory depression can occur.","additionalDifferentials":["Opioid overdose","Alcohol or other sedative-hypnotic intoxication","Hypoglycaemia","Structural or metabolic causes of coma","Carbon monoxide or other toxic causes of reduced consciousness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Capillary blood glucose to exclude hypoglycaemia","Pulse oximetry and, if indicated, arterial blood gas for hypoventilation","Paracetamol and salicylate levels and an ECG to screen for co-ingestants","Urine or serum toxicology where it will change management","CT head if reduced consciousness is unexplained or focal signs are present"],"redFlags":["Significant respiratory depression or hypoxaemia","Deep or fluctuating coma suggesting a co-ingested agent","Hypotension out of proportion to isolated benzodiazepine effect","Loss of airway protective reflexes with aspiration risk","Seizures, which should raise concern for a proconvulsant co-ingestant rather than benzodiazepine alone"],"prognosis":"Isolated benzodiazepine overdose usually has an excellent prognosis with supportive care, but outcome worsens considerably with co-ingestion of other central depressants or when aspiration or prolonged hypoxia occurs.","pitfalls":["Reflexively giving flumazenil and precipitating seizures or acute withdrawal, especially in chronic users or mixed overdose","Attributing deep coma or hypotension to benzodiazepines alone and missing a co-ingestant","Overlooking hypoglycaemia or another treatable cause of reduced consciousness","Administering charcoal to a drowsy patient and causing aspiration"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"beta_thalassemia":{"name":"Beta Thalassaemia","system":"Haematological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["beta-thalassaemia major","Cooley anaemia","beta-thalassaemia intermedia","beta thalassemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An inherited disorder of reduced or absent beta-globin chain production, most common in Mediterranean, Middle Eastern, South Asian, and Southeast Asian populations.","Beta-thalassaemia major typically presents in the second half of the first year of life as protective fetal haemoglobin declines, with progressive pallor, poor growth, and hepatosplenomegaly.","The blood film shows a severe microcytic hypochromic anaemia with marked anisopoikilocytosis, target cells, and nucleated red cells.","Haemoglobin electrophoresis or HPLC demonstrates elevated HbF and often raised HbA2, with little or no HbA in the major form.","Chronic transfusion is required in transfusion-dependent disease to sustain growth and suppress ineffective erythropoiesis.","Repeated transfusion inevitably causes iron overload, so iron chelation is essential to prevent cardiac, hepatic, and endocrine damage.","Untreated or under-transfused children develop characteristic skeletal changes from marrow expansion, including frontal bossing and maxillary overgrowth.","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation offers a potential cure, and newer gene-based therapies are emerging.","Splenomegaly may lead to hypersplenism and rising transfusion requirements; genetic counselling and antenatal screening are important for at-risk families."],"pathophysiology":"Mutations in the beta-globin gene reduce (beta+) or abolish (beta0) beta-chain synthesis, creating an imbalance with relatively excess alpha-globin chains. Unpaired alpha chains precipitate within red cell precursors, causing their destruction in the marrow (ineffective erythropoiesis) and shortened survival of circulating red cells (haemolysis). The resulting chronic anaemia drives erythropoietin-mediated marrow expansion and extramedullary haematopoiesis, while increased gastrointestinal iron absorption and transfusional iron together produce progressive tissue iron overload.","additionalDifferentials":["Iron deficiency anaemia","Alpha thalassaemia","Sickle cell disease and other haemoglobinopathies","Anaemia of chronic disease","Sideroblastic anaemia","Congenital dyserythropoietic anaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count with red cell indices (low MCV and MCH)","Peripheral blood film","Haemoglobin electrophoresis or HPLC quantifying HbA, HbA2, and HbF","Reticulocyte count","Iron studies and serum ferritin to distinguish iron deficiency and monitor overload","Genetic mutation analysis of the beta-globin gene","Serial ferritin and cardiac/hepatic MRI (T2*) to monitor iron burden"],"redFlags":["Symptomatic severe anaemia with cardiac decompensation","Signs of iron overload cardiomyopathy or arrhythmia","Rapidly enlarging spleen with falling counts (hypersplenism)","Growth failure and delayed puberty from chronic anaemia or endocrine iron toxicity","Features of extramedullary masses causing compressive symptoms"],"prognosis":"With regular transfusion and effective iron chelation, survival into adulthood with good quality of life is achievable; outcomes are limited chiefly by iron-related cardiac and endocrine complications, and stem cell transplant can be curative in selected patients.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing microcytic anaemia as simple iron deficiency and giving unnecessary iron","Under-transfusing and allowing marrow expansion and skeletal deformity to progress","Neglecting or delaying iron chelation, leading to preventable organ damage","Overlooking endocrine complications such as hypothyroidism, diabetes, and delayed puberty"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"biliary_atresia":{"name":"Biliary Atresia","system":"Hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["extrahepatic biliary atresia","EHBA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common cause of neonatal cholestasis leading to liver transplantation, resulting from progressive inflammatory obliteration of the extrahepatic bile ducts.","Classic presentation is a term infant with prolonged jaundice, pale (acholic) stools, and dark urine, usually with otherwise good early growth.","Any infant jaundiced beyond about two weeks of age should have a fractionated bilirubin measured; a raised conjugated (direct) fraction signals cholestasis and needs urgent evaluation.","Early diagnosis is critical because the success of surgery falls sharply with age, ideally before about 8 weeks (60 days) of life.","The Kasai hepatoportoenterostomy aims to restore bile drainage; the earlier it is performed, the better the chance of clearing jaundice.","Even after a successful Kasai procedure, many children progress to cirrhosis and ultimately require liver transplantation.","A subset have the biliary atresia splenic malformation syndrome with situs and other congenital anomalies.","Ultrasound may show an absent or small gallbladder and the 'triangular cord' sign; a hepatobiliary scan shows hepatic uptake without excretion into the bowel."],"pathophysiology":"A fibro-obliterative process destroys the extrahepatic and often intrahepatic bile ducts, obstructing bile flow. Retained bile causes progressive hepatocellular injury, portal tract inflammation, and bile ductular proliferation, leading to fibrosis and, if untreated, biliary cirrhosis with portal hypertension and liver failure. The initiating cause is uncertain and probably heterogeneous, involving developmental, immune, and possibly viral mechanisms.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal (idiopathic) hepatitis","Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency","Choledochal cyst","Alagille syndrome and other intrahepatic cholestatic syndromes","Progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis","Inspissated bile / bile plug syndrome","Congenital infection (TORCH)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fractionated (total and conjugated) serum bilirubin","Liver function tests including GGT and aminotransferases","Abdominal ultrasound (gallbladder, triangular cord sign, exclusion of choledochal cyst)","Hepatobiliary scintigraphy assessing excretion into the intestine","Percutaneous liver biopsy","Intraoperative cholangiogram to confirm the diagnosis","Studies to exclude alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency and metabolic causes"],"redFlags":["Acholic (pale, clay-coloured) stools","Conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia at any age in an infant","Jaundice persisting beyond two weeks of life","Signs of coagulopathy or vitamin K deficiency bleeding","Hepatosplenomegaly, ascites, or other features of advanced liver disease"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on the age at surgery; early Kasai portoenterostomy can restore bile flow and delay or avoid transplantation, but many children eventually progress to end-stage liver disease. Overall long-term survival is good when transplantation is available.","pitfalls":["Dismissing prolonged neonatal jaundice as physiological or breast-milk jaundice without checking a split bilirubin","Failing to inspect stool colour","Delaying referral and surgery beyond the window where the Kasai procedure is most effective","Relying on a normal ultrasound to exclude the diagnosis","Overlooking associated congenital anomalies in the splenic malformation syndrome"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"bipolar_disorder":{"name":"Bipolar disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bipolar affective disorder","manic depression","manic-depressive illness","BPAD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by episodes of abnormally elevated, expansive, or irritable mood with increased energy and activity, alternating over time with depressive episodes","A manic episode involves a distinct sustained period (about a week or requiring hospitalisation) with features such as inflated self-esteem or grandiosity, reduced need for sleep, pressured speech, racing thoughts, distractibility, and risky goal-directed behaviour","Bipolar I requires at least one full manic episode; bipolar II is defined by hypomania plus major depressive episodes without a full mania","Bipolar depression is easily misdiagnosed as unipolar depression; a careful history for past highs is essential, since antidepressant monotherapy can precipitate mania or rapid cycling","Mood stabilisers (such as lithium or certain anticonvulsants) and atypical antipsychotics are the mainstays of acute and maintenance treatment, described here as principles without specific dosing","Lithium reduces suicide risk but has a narrow therapeutic index and requires monitoring of serum levels, renal function, and thyroid function","Psychoeducation, regularising sleep and daily routines, and treatment adherence are central to preventing relapse","Screen for psychotic features, which may accompany severe mania or depression and can be mood-congruent or incongruent"],"pathophysiology":"Bipolar disorder is a highly heritable condition involving dysregulation of monoaminergic and intracellular signalling systems, disturbances of circadian rhythm and sleep, and abnormalities in the connectivity and function of prefrontal-limbic circuits governing mood and reward. Episodes are often precipitated by sleep disruption, stress, or substances acting on an underlying biological vulnerability.","additionalDifferentials":["Major depressive disorder (unipolar)","Schizoaffective disorder","Schizophrenia","Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder","Borderline personality disorder","Substance-induced mood or psychotic disorder","Hyperthyroidism or steroid-induced mood elevation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Collateral history to identify prior manic or hypomanic episodes","Thyroid, renal function, and pregnancy testing prior to mood stabiliser use","Serum drug-level monitoring for agents with narrow therapeutic indices such as lithium","Substance use screening","Structured mood charting to track episode pattern"],"redFlags":["Suicidal ideation, which carries high risk across both manic and depressive phases","Psychotic symptoms or severe agitation requiring urgent stabilisation","Reckless behaviour endangering the patient or others during mania","Signs of lithium toxicity such as tremor, confusion, vomiting, and ataxia"],"prognosis":"Bipolar disorder is a lifelong, recurrent illness with substantial risk of functional impairment and elevated suicide rate. Prognosis improves with early diagnosis, mood-stabiliser adherence, and relapse-prevention strategies, whereas repeated episodes, substance misuse, and poor adherence worsen outcomes.","pitfalls":["Treating bipolar depression with an antidepressant alone and triggering a manic switch or rapid cycling","Missing a history of hypomania and misdiagnosing unipolar depression","Failing to monitor lithium levels and organ function","Overlooking suicide risk during depressive and mixed states","Attributing early mania to intoxication and delaying appropriate treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"bladder_cancer":{"name":"Bladder & Urinary Tract Cancer","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["urothelial carcinoma","transitional cell carcinoma"],"chapter":91,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.690-693","clinicalPearls":["Painless hematuria, either gross or microscopic, is the initial manifestation of urinary tract cancer in most patients; in males painless hematuria is almost always abnormal and must be worked up (Harrison 22e p.691).","In females, hematuria due to malignancy is often mistaken for a urinary tract infection or menstrual bleeding; antibiotics are warranted if a concurrent UTI is found, but persistent hematuria still requires further workup (Harrison 22e p.691).","Initial investigation in either sex includes urine cytology plus visual examination of the bladder by cystoscopy, together with radiographic evaluation of the kidneys and upper tract by CT urogram (MR urogram if renal function is poor) (Harrison 22e p.691).","Smoking of tobacco products is the overwhelming leading risk factor; 90% of new bladder cancer diagnoses occur in current or former smokers, and roughly one-third of cases could be prevented by smoking cessation (Harrison 22e p.692).","Urothelial carcinoma (formerly transitional cell carcinoma) is the most common histology, seen in about 90% of cases; nonurothelial variants (squamous cell, adenocarcinoma, small-cell, carcinosarcoma) collectively account for 10% or less (Harrison 22e p.690-691).","Disease is divided into non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer (NMIBC: carcinoma in situ, Ta, or T1 not reaching the muscularis propria; ~75% at presentation) and muscle-invasive bladder cancer (MIBC: T2 invasion of muscularis propria and beyond) (Harrison 22e p.691-692).","Urothelial carcinoma shows a biphasic phenotype: low-grade papillary tumors that frequently recur but rarely invade or metastasize, and high-grade often flat tumors that invade early and lead to lethal metastatic disease (Harrison 22e p.691)."],"pathophysiology":"Urothelial carcinoma arises from the lining of the bladder, renal pelvis, ureter, and urethra and displays a biphasic clinical behavior. Loss of portions of chromosomes 9q and 9p (loss of heterozygosity) is an early molecular event in both phenotypes; candidate genes in these regions include CDKN2A and TSC1. Low-grade papillary tumors are characterized by RAS/RAF pathway alterations with activating FGFR3 mutations or fusions in 60-80% of patients, whereas the high-grade invasive phenotype features early deleterious TP53 and RB1 mutations, CDH1 (E-cadherin) alterations, and increased VEGFR2 expression. Chronic inflammatory states drive squamous-type bladder cancer, classically chronic schistosomiasis infection and chronic indwelling catheters in paraplegic patients. Carcinogenic exposures include aromatic amines (benzidine, beta-naphthylamine) in industrial dyes and arsenic-contaminated drinking water. The Cancer Genome Atlas defined five intrinsic molecular subtypes (luminal papillary, luminal infiltrated, luminal, basal squamous, neuronal) resembling breast cancer subclasses (Harrison 22e p.691-692).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Hematuria from malignancy in females is frequently mistaken for a urinary tract infection (Harrison 22e p.691)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["In females, malignant hematuria may be misattributed to menstrual bleeding (Harrison 22e p.691).","Intravesical BCG side effects (dysuria, urinary frequency, bladder spasms, hematuria) overlap with infection/cystitis, and in <5% a systemic inflammatory response can mimic disseminated BCG infection (Harrison 22e p.692)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine cytology (detects cancer in only about 50% of high-grade bladder cancers) (Harrison 22e p.691).","Cystoscopy for direct bladder visualization; blue light or narrow-band imaging cystoscopy improves detection of small or flat noninvasive tumors and is used routinely in monitoring (Harrison 22e p.691).","CT urogram (or MR urogram if renal function is impaired) for the kidneys and upper tract (Harrison 22e p.691).","Adjunct urine molecular tests (FISH for chromosomal changes, nuclear mitotic proteins, bladder tumor-associated antigens) used mainly for detecting recurrence, as they can be falsely positive (Harrison 22e p.691).","Ureteroscopy or retrograde pyelography when upper-tract tumors are suspected with a normal bladder (Harrison 22e p.691).","Transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT) or endoscopic resection of upper tract tumors for histologic diagnosis and staging (Harrison 22e p.691).","Genomic sequencing of tumor tissue and/or circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) recommended in all metastatic patients (Harrison 22e p.690)."],"redFlags":["Painless gross or microscopic hematuria warrants investigation for urothelial cancer; in males it is almost always abnormal (Harrison 22e p.691).","Persistent hematuria despite treatment of a concurrent UTI requires further workup (Harrison 22e p.691).","Flank pain with an upper tract renal pelvis/ureter cancer, or hydronephrosis from a bladder tumor obstructing the ureteral orifice (Harrison 22e p.691).","Cachexia and widespread metastatic disease at presentation (rare) (Harrison 22e p.691)."],"prognosis":"Outcomes correlate closely with stage at diagnosis. About 75% present with non-muscle-invasive disease, 18% with muscle-invasive disease, and only 3% with metastatic spread. 5-year overall survival is 70-90% for disease confined to the bladder (stage I-II), 39-50% for disease penetrating through the bladder or involving regional lymph nodes (stage III), and only 8% for metastatic disease (stage IV) (Harrison 22e p.692).","pitfalls":["Urine cytology is insensitive, identifying cancer in only about 50% of high-grade bladder cancers, so a negative cytology does not exclude malignancy (Harrison 22e p.691).","Treating presumed UTI without follow-up risks missing malignancy; persistent hematuria after antibiotics still requires workup (Harrison 22e p.691).","Adjunct urine molecular tests may be abnormal in patients without cancer (false positives) and are best reserved for surveillance of known cancer (Harrison 22e p.691).","Systemic inflammatory reaction after intravesical BCG can mimic disseminated BCG infection (Harrison 22e p.692)."],"severityClassification":"Staging is driven primarily by depth of invasion (T stage). NMIBC: Tis (CIS) and Ta (epithelial layer only) or T1 (into connective tissue/lamina propria below urothelium but not muscularis propria). MIBC: T2 (into muscularis propria), T3 (through muscularis propria to serosa), T4 (adjacent pelvic organs - rectum, prostate, vagina, cervix). Nodal: N1 (single true pelvic node), N2 (two true pelvic nodes), N3 (common iliac nodes); disease beyond common iliac nodes or distant spread is M1. Stages 0a-II are defined by T stage alone without nodal/metastatic disease; regional pelvic or common iliac nodal involvement is stage III; any distant metastasis is stage IV (Harrison 22e p.691-692).","references":["Harrison 22e p.690-693"],"crossLinks":["schistosomiasis"]},"blast_injury":{"name":"Blast injury","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["explosion injury","blast trauma"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classify blast injury into primary (pressure wave), secondary (flying fragments), tertiary (body displacement), and quaternary (burns, inhalation, and other effects) mechanisms to avoid missing occult injury","Primary blast injury preferentially damages gas-containing organs: the ears, lungs, and bowel","Blast lung, presenting as respiratory distress and hypoxaemia, is a leading cause of early death and may evolve over hours","Ruptured tympanic membranes are common but their absence does not exclude significant primary blast injury elsewhere","Apply the standard primary survey, as penetrating fragments and blunt displacement often cause the injuries that need immediate treatment","Suspect occult bowel injury, which can present in a delayed fashion with perforation","In enclosed-space explosions injuries are more severe, and consider inhalation and toxic exposure","Beware air embolism from blast lung, which can cause sudden cardiovascular or neurological collapse"],"pathophysiology":"An explosion generates a high-pressure blast wave that transmits energy across tissue interfaces of differing density. Where gas meets fluid or solid, as in the alveoli, middle ear, and gut, shearing and implosion-reexpansion forces rupture delicate structures, producing pulmonary haemorrhage and oedema (blast lung), tympanic perforation, and bowel contusion or perforation. Secondary injury results from fragments propelled by the blast causing penetrating wounds, tertiary injury from the body being thrown, and quaternary effects from burns, crush, inhalation, and toxic exposure. Blast lung can allow air to enter pulmonary veins, producing systemic air embolism to the brain or coronary circulation.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated penetrating trauma from fragments","Blunt polytrauma from displacement","Thermal or inhalational burn injury","Crush injury from structural collapse","Toxic gas or chemical exposure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph and CT for blast lung and pneumothorax","Arterial blood gas to assess oxygenation","Otoscopy to identify tympanic membrane rupture","Imaging and serial abdominal examination for occult bowel injury","Assessment for penetrating fragments including radiographs to localise foreign bodies"],"redFlags":["Respiratory distress and hypoxaemia from blast lung, which may progress rapidly","Sudden cardiovascular or neurological collapse suggesting systemic air embolism","Signs of occult bowel perforation appearing hours after the blast","Uncontrolled haemorrhage from penetrating fragment wounds","Enclosed-space exposure with combined burns, inhalation, and toxic effects"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the dominant mechanism and injury burden. Blast lung and multi-mechanism injuries carry significant early mortality, while many fragment and displacement injuries are survivable with prompt trauma care; delayed presentations such as bowel perforation add later risk.","pitfalls":["Considering only the visible wounds and missing primary blast injury to lung or bowel","Assuming intact eardrums exclude significant blast exposure","Aggressive positive-pressure ventilation aggravating blast lung and air embolism","Failing to anticipate delayed bowel perforation","Underestimating severity of injuries sustained in enclosed spaces"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"blastomycosis":{"name":"Blastomycosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Blastomyces dermatitidis"],"chapter":220,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1699-1703","clinicalPearls":["A pyogranulomatous disease acquired by inhaling Blastomyces conidia or hyphal fragments from disturbed soil; typically a pulmonary infection, with a subset progressing to disseminated disease involving skin, bone, brain, or genitourinary system (p.1699).","A primary fungal pathogen: it infects persons with intact as well as impaired immune systems (p.1699).","Endemic mainly in North America (Mississippi/Ohio and St. Lawrence River basins, Great Lakes states, southeastern states) but of global importance with cases in Africa and Asia; outbreaks linked to construction, soil excavation, water sports, compost piles, and beaver dams (p.1699-1700).","Yeast form (37 degrees C) is a broad-based budding yeast, 4-29 micrometers, with a doubly refractile cell wall, allowing distinction from other fungi (p.1700, 1702).","After a 3-week to 3-month incubation, about 50% of those exposed develop symptomatic infection; the long incubation means cases present year-round (p.1701).","Pulmonary disease occurs in 69-93% of symptomatic patients (most common feature) and may present as asymptomatic infection, influenza-like illness, acute pneumonia, chronic pneumonia, or ARDS (p.1701).","Disseminated disease occurs in 15-48%; skin is the most common site (papules, ulcers, verrucous lesions, abscesses), followed by bone (osteomyelitis of spine, long bones, ribs) (p.1701).","CNS (meningitis/abscess/mass), laryngeal, and genitourinary (prostate, epididymis) dissemination each occur in fewer than 10%, usually with concurrent lung or skin involvement (p.1701).","Diagnosis combines microscopy of stained specimens, fungal culture, and antigen testing; serology (complement fixation 9%, immunodiffusion 28%) is diagnostically dispensable, though a newer anti-BAD1 antibody test has higher sensitivity/specificity (p.1702).","Urine galactomannan antigen testing (sensitivity 85-93% urine, 57-82% serum) has supplanted older serology and can monitor treatment response as levels decline (p.1702-1703).","Treatment is recommended for ALL patients because there are no criteria to identify which untreated cases will self-resolve; antifungals are amphotericin B formulations and triazoles (p.1699, 1703)."],"pathophysiology":"Blastomyces is a thermally dimorphic species complex (including B. dermatitidis, B. gilchristii, B. helicus, B. percursus, B. emzantsi). In soil at 22-25 degrees C it grows as septate hyphae producing infectious conidia; inhalation into the lungs and conversion at the mammalian core temperature of 37 degrees C yields a pathogenic broad-based budding yeast that upregulates yeast-phase virulence factors and downregulates host immune defenses, eliciting pyogranulomatous inflammation. Shared virulence traits with other dimorphic fungi (Histoplasma, Coccidioides, Sporothrix, Paracoccidioides) include thermotolerance at 37 degrees C, intracellular survival, and the ability to infect immunocompetent hosts. Blastomyces can also replicate outside macrophages. Notably, immunosuppression has only minimal influence on dissemination, suggesting fungal virulence factors outweigh host defenses; the infecting species and duration of pulmonary symptoms (and concomitant AIDS) influence dissemination, with B. dermatitidis more likely to disseminate than B. gilchristii. (p.1700-1702)","additionalDifferentials":["Community-acquired bacterial pneumonia (p.1701)","Viral pneumonia (p.1701)","Tuberculosis (p.1701-1702)","Lung cancer / malignancy (p.1701-1702)","Skin cancer (for cutaneous lesions) (p.1701)","Sarcoidosis (p.1701)","Pyoderma gangrenosum (p.1701)","Laryngeal cancer (rare mimic) (p.1701)"],"infectionMimics":["Community-acquired bacterial pneumonia (p.1701)","Viral pneumonia (p.1701)","Tuberculosis (p.1701-1702)","Histoplasmosis (B. helicus small yeast can be confused with Histoplasma; antigen cross-reactivity with H. capsulatum, Paracoccidioides, T. marneffei) (p.1702-1703)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lung cancer / malignancy (p.1701-1702)","Skin cancer (cutaneous lesions) (p.1701)","Sarcoidosis (cutaneous lesions) (p.1701)","Pyoderma gangrenosum (cutaneous lesions) (p.1701)","Laryngeal cancer (rare) (p.1701)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Microscopy of stained clinical specimens for broad-based budding yeast with doubly refractile cell wall (sputum, tracheal aspirate, BAL fluid, or purulent drainage stained with calcofluor, 10% KOH, or Papanicolaou; sensitivity 50-90%) (p.1702)","Histopathology of tissue stained with Gomori methenamine silver or periodic acid-Schiff, assessed for pyogranulomatous inflammation and broad-based budding yeast (Gram and H&E do not visualize the yeast well) (p.1702)","Fungal culture on special media (Sabouraud dextrose, potato dextrose, brain-heart infusion); 5-28 days incubation; definitive diagnosis (p.1702)","Chemiluminescent DNA probe or PCR to confirm identification (does not distinguish among species) (p.1702)","Urine, serum, BAL, and CSF galactomannan antigen testing (urine sensitivity 85-93%, serum 57-82%); useful to monitor response (p.1702-1703)","Anti-BAD1 antibody serologic test (sensitivity 87%, specificity 94-99%) (p.1702)"],"redFlags":["Unexplained respiratory failure or ARDS in a person from or visiting an endemic area; 4-15% of pulmonary cases develop ARDS with a fulminant course and 40-89% mortality (p.1701)","Pneumonia that fails to improve despite antibiotics, or pneumonia with extrapulmonary features (skin lesions, osteomyelitis, CNS involvement) (p.1701)","Skin ulcers unresponsive to standard therapy (p.1701)","AIDS with CD4+ count <100/microliter: increased risk of CNS dissemination (p.1702)","B. helicus infection (commonly causes fungemia, usually in immunosuppressed hosts, high mortality ~71%) (p.1702)"],"prognosis":"Overall mortality ranges from 5 to 13% (7.9% in 11,776 U.S. hospital admissions, 2010-2020), with most deaths from respiratory failure due to ARDS. Mortality is higher in older patients and those with COPD or concomitant solid/hematologic malignancy, and is increased when diagnosis is delayed. The vast majority who recover from pulmonary blastomycosis do not have long-term loss of pulmonary function; cutaneous blastomycosis typically heals with scarring. (p.1703)","pitfalls":["Called 'the great pretender' because it mimics infectious and noninfectious diseases; pulmonary blastomycosis is commonly misdiagnosed, and patients often receive two or three antibiotic courses before diagnosis (p.1701).","No distinctive radiographic features differentiate blastomycosis from other pulmonary diseases (consolidation, mass, nodules, miliary, cavitary patterns all possible); a mass lesion can mimic malignancy (p.1701).","Diagnosis requires a high index of suspicion and a detailed exposure history (about 50-60% of patients have environmental risk factors); failure to consider Blastomyces in the differential delays treatment (p.1701).","Serology by complement fixation/immunodiffusion has poor sensitivity and is diagnostically dispensable (p.1702).","Echinocandins are ineffective (minimal beta-(1,3)-glucan in the yeast cell wall) and should not be used (p.1703).","Antigen testing cross-reacts with other dimorphic fungi, notably H. capsulatum which shares the same endemic region (p.1703)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal numeric severity scale. Severe blastomycosis is operationally defined as disease requiring hospitalization (medical ward, intermediate care, or ICU). Risk factors for severe pulmonary disease include diabetes, solid organ transplantation, immunosuppression, and multilobar pneumonia; ARDS (in 4-15%) represents the most severe pulmonary form with high mortality. Special high-risk/severity categories warranting intensified management include CNS, severe pulmonary, and severe disseminated disease. (p.1701-1703)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1699-1703"],"crossLinks":["CAP","PULMONARY_TB","lung_cancer"]},"blepharitis":{"name":"Blepharitis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["anterior blepharitis","posterior blepharitis","meibomian gland dysfunction","lid margin inflammation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common, chronic inflammation of the eyelid margins presenting with burning, grittiness, crusting, and redness that is typically bilateral and worse in the mornings","Divided into anterior blepharitis (affecting the lash bases, often staphylococcal or seborrhoeic) and posterior blepharitis (meibomian gland dysfunction)","Anterior staphylococcal disease shows hard crusts and collarettes around the lashes, with lash loss and misdirection in chronic cases","Posterior disease shows capped, plugged, or inspissated meibomian orifices and a poor-quality, foamy tear film with associated evaporative dry eye","Strongly associated with rosacea and seborrhoeic dermatitis; recurrent chalazia and styes are common complications","The mainstay of management is lid hygiene with warm compresses and lid margin cleaning; topical antibiotic ointment helps anterior disease and oral tetracyclines help refractory meibomian dysfunction","Recurrent, unilateral, or asymmetrical lid margin disease with lash loss should raise suspicion of eyelid malignancy such as sebaceous gland carcinoma","Patients should be counselled that it is a chronic, relapsing condition controlled rather than cured"],"pathophysiology":"Blepharitis is chronic inflammation of the eyelid margins with overlapping mechanisms. Anterior blepharitis involves bacterial (chiefly staphylococcal) colonisation of the lash bases and an inflammatory reaction to bacterial products, or seborrhoeic changes with excessive scaling. Posterior blepharitis reflects meibomian gland dysfunction, in which altered and inspissated meibomian secretions block the glands, destabilise the lipid layer of the tear film, and promote evaporative dry eye. Bacterial lipases and inflammatory mediators contribute to ocular surface irritation, and the condition is frequently linked to rosacea.","additionalDifferentials":["Dry eye disease","Allergic or bacterial conjunctivitis","Sebaceous gland carcinoma of the eyelid (in atypical, unilateral disease)","Rosacea-associated ocular surface disease","Demodex lash infestation","Contact dermatitis of the eyelids"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination of the lid margins, lashes, and meibomian gland orifices","Assessment of tear film stability and ocular surface staining for associated dry eye","Lid margin or lash swab in severe or atypical cases","Lid margin biopsy when malignancy is suspected (unilateral disease with lash loss and induration)"],"redFlags":["Unilateral or markedly asymmetrical disease with localised lash loss and lid thickening suggesting sebaceous gland carcinoma","Associated corneal involvement (marginal keratitis or ulceration) threatening vision","Failure to respond to appropriate treatment prompting re-evaluation of the diagnosis","Recurrent chalazia at the same site raising concern for malignancy"],"prognosis":"Blepharitis is a chronic relapsing condition that is generally controlled well with sustained lid hygiene, though symptoms recur if treatment lapses; complications such as recurrent chalazia and evaporative dry eye can be troublesome but vision is usually preserved.","pitfalls":["Expecting a cure rather than long-term control and stopping lid hygiene prematurely","Missing sebaceous gland carcinoma masquerading as recurrent unilateral blepharitis or chalazion","Overlooking associated dry eye and rosacea that require concurrent management","Prolonged unmonitored topical steroid use with risk of raised intraocular pressure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"bone_and_mineral_metabolism_in":{"name":"Bone and Mineral Metabolism in Health and Disease","system":"Endocrine/Musculoskeletal (bone and mineral metabolism)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Bone and mineral metabolism","Calcium, phosphorus, and magnesium homeostasis","Vitamin D metabolism and bone remodeling"],"chapter":421,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3259","clinicalPearls":["Bone is continuously remodeled by a basic multicellular unit: osteoblasts (mesenchymal origin) lay down and mineralize osteoid, osteoclasts (hematopoietic/macrophage lineage) resorb matrix, and long-lived embedded osteocytes coordinate both and act as mechanosensors (Harrison 22e p.3259).","Roughly half of total serum calcium is ionized; the rest is bound to albumin/immunoglobulins or complexed to anions, so total calcium must be interpreted with the serum protein level. A useful correction adds about 0.8 mg/dL to total calcium for each 1 g/dL the albumin is below normal, but in acute illness measure ionized calcium directly with a calcium-selective electrode (Harrison 22e p.3261-3262).","Acidosis raises ionized calcium by reducing calcium binding to protein; alkalosis does the opposite, which helps explain tetany seen with respiratory alkalosis (Harrison 22e p.3262).","Serum phosphate varies by up to 50% over a day with a morning nadir, and IV dextrose, insulin, or alkalosis can drop it acutely, so phosphate should be measured fasting and in the basal state (Harrison 22e p.3262-3263).","Because only about 1% of body magnesium is in the extracellular fluid, a normal serum magnesium does not exclude significant total-body magnesium depletion (Harrison 22e p.3266-3267).","Hypomagnesemia frequently causes refractory hypocalcemia and hypokalemia; at very low serum magnesium (below roughly 0.4 mmol/L) PTH secretion is impaired and there is cellular PTH resistance, so calcium and potassium will not correct until magnesium is repleted (Harrison 22e p.3267).","FGF23, secreted by osteocytes, is a phosphaturic hormone that both lowers proximal-tubule phosphate reabsorption and suppresses 1-alpha-hydroxylase (lowering 1,25(OH)2D); excess FGF23 underlies XLH, ADHR, and tumor-induced osteomalacia, whereas deficient/inactive FGF23 causes tumoral calcinosis (Harrison 22e p.3263-3265).","Vitamin D is effectively a hormone, not a true vitamin: skin synthesizes it from 7-dehydrocholesterol under UV light, the liver performs unregulated 25-hydroxylation (25(OH)D is the storage/measured form), and the kidney performs the tightly regulated 1-alpha-hydroxylation to active 1,25(OH)2D (Harrison 22e p.3268).","Renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase is stimulated by PTH and hypophosphatemia and suppressed by calcium, FGF23, and 1,25(OH)2D itself; granuloma/lymphoma macrophages express an unregulated 1-alpha-hydroxylase, explaining hypercalcemia in sarcoidosis and lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.3268).","Optimal intestinal calcium absorption requires gastric acid; achlorhydria, proton pump inhibitors, or post-bariatric surgery impair absorption of poorly dissociable salts like calcium carbonate, so calcium citrate or dosing with meals is preferred (Harrison 22e p.3262).","Active vitamin D-dependent transcellular calcium and phosphate absorption uses specific apical channels/transporters (TRPV6 and Npt2b in the gut, TRPV5 in the distal tubule); thiazides lower urinary calcium (useful in hypercalciuria) while loop diuretics and saline promote calciuresis (Harrison 22e p.3262).","Serious neuromuscular sequelae of hypophosphatemia (seizures, paralysis, rhabdomyolysis) occur mainly when phosphate falls below about 0.25 mmol/L, reflecting ATP depletion; up to 30% of patients with acute hypophosphatemia have CPK elevations peaking 1-2 days after the phosphate nadir (Harrison 22e p.3264)."],"pathophysiology":"Mineral homeostasis is maintained by integrated handling of calcium, phosphate, and magnesium across gut, kidney, and bone under hormonal control by PTH, 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, and FGF23. Bone provides both mechanical support and the major reservoir of these ions; it is constantly remodeled, with osteoblasts (mesenchymal lineage, driven by Runx2, osterix, Ihh, Wnt and BMP signaling) forming and mineralizing type I collagen matrix and osteoclasts (hematopoietic lineage, driven by M-CSF and RANK ligand, opposed by osteoprotegerin) resorbing it via acidification and proteases such as cathepsin K. Osteocytes sense mechanical load, secrete the Wnt inhibitor sclerostin and the phosphaturic hormone FGF23, and orchestrate remodeling. Ionized calcium in the extracellular fluid is sensed by the calcium-sensing receptor on parathyroid cells and renal tubule; low calcium raises PTH, which mobilizes bone calcium, enhances distal renal calcium reabsorption, lowers renal phosphate reabsorption, and stimulates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase to make 1,25(OH)2D, which in turn increases intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption. Phosphate balance is governed largely by proximal tubular NaPi-2a/2c transporters, which are downregulated by PTH and FGF23; FGF23 from osteocytes additionally suppresses 1,25(OH)2D synthesis. Vitamin D is produced in skin or absorbed from diet, 25-hydroxylated in liver, and 1-alpha-hydroxylated in kidney, with 24-hydroxylase (CYP24A1) providing inactivation. Disturbances at any node (dietary extremes, renal failure, hormone deficiency/excess, intestinal disease, or genetic transporter/enzyme defects) disrupt the narrow regulation of ionized calcium and lead to skeletal and systemic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypophosphatemia: primary/secondary hyperparathyroidism, vitamin D deficiency, FGF23 excess (XLH, ADHR, ARHP, tumor-induced osteomalacia, McCune-Albright/fibrous dysplasia), Fanconi syndrome, Dent disease, alcoholism, poorly controlled diabetes, refeeding/insulin therapy, hungry bone syndrome","Hyperphosphatemia: renal insufficiency, hypoparathyroidism/pseudohypoparathyroidism, acromegaly, tumoral calcinosis, tumor lysis syndrome, rhabdomyolysis/crush injury, exogenous phosphate loads","Hypomagnesemia: malabsorption, proton pump inhibitor use, alcoholism, Gitelman and Bartter syndromes, claudin 16/19 mutations, TRPM6 mutation, drug-induced (cisplatin, aminoglycosides, amphotericin B, cyclosporine, cetuximab), diabetic glycosuria","Hypermagnesemia: renal failure with magnesium load, familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia, adrenal insufficiency, hypothyroidism, eclampsia treatment","Hypercalcemia with elevated 1,25(OH)2D: sarcoidosis and other granulomatous disease, lymphoma, vitamin D intoxication, CYP24A1 inactivating mutation","Osteomalacia/rickets vs other causes of bone pain and proximal myopathy"],"infectionMimics":["Granulomatous infections (e.g., tuberculosis, other mycobacterial or fungal granulomas) can drive macrophage 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity and produce 1,25(OH)2D-mediated hypercalcemia, mimicking non-infectious hypercalcemia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sarcoidosis and lymphoma as causes of 1,25(OH)2D-mediated hypercalcemia","Tumor-induced osteomalacia mimicking idiopathic chronic bone pain and myopathy","Milk-alkali syndrome mimicking other hypercalcemic states with renal impairment"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum total and ionized calcium (ionized preferred in acute illness), with albumin for correction","Fasting serum phosphate and serum magnesium","Intact PTH and PTHrP where malignancy-associated hypercalcemia is suspected","25(OH)D (status/stores) and 1,25(OH)2D (suspected granulomatous/lymphoma-related or 1-alpha-hydroxylase-related disorders)","Serum FGF23 in unexplained hypophosphatemic osteomalacia or tumoral calcinosis","Fractional excretion of phosphate / renal phosphate threshold to confirm renal phosphate wasting","Bone formation markers (bone-specific alkaline phosphatase, osteocalcin, procollagen I N-terminal propeptide) and resorption markers (collagen cross-linked telopeptides in urine/blood)","Renal function (creatinine/GFR) given its central role in phosphate retention and vitamin D activation","Calcium-phosphorus product monitoring during phosphate repletion (avoid exceeding 50 mg2/dL2)","Imaging/localization for tumor-induced osteomalacia (skeletal survey, bone scan, octreotide or sestamibi scanning)"],"redFlags":["Severe hypophosphatemia below about 0.25 mmol/L (0.8 mg/dL): risk of seizures, ascending paralysis, rhabdomyolysis, respiratory and cardiac failure, coma","Acute severe hyperphosphatemia: widespread calcium-phosphate precipitation with tetany, seizures, acute renal failure, and pulmonary/cardiac calcification including heart block","Hypermagnesemia above ~2 mmol/L: refractory hypotension, neuromuscular blockade, hyporeflexia progressing to respiratory failure, heart block, and asystole near 10 mmol/L","Severe hypomagnesemia: tetany, seizures, cardiac arrhythmias, prolonged QT and enhanced digitalis toxicity","Very high calcium intake or milk-alkali syndrome: hypercalciuria, nephrocalcinosis, progressive renal failure"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends entirely on the underlying disorder rather than on bone and mineral metabolism per se. Acute redistributive electrolyte derangements (hypophosphatemia, hypomagnesemia) are typically reversible with appropriate repletion, and respiratory/cardiac dysfunction from severe hypophosphatemia improves with phosphate replacement. Tumor-induced osteomalacia resolves within hours to days after successful tumor resection. Chronic renal phosphate-wasting disorders (XLH, ADHR) generally cannot be fully normalized but can be controlled. Persistent dysregulation, as in chronic kidney disease with secondary hyperparathyroidism and hyperphosphatemia, leads to progressive skeletal and vascular consequences.","pitfalls":["Relying on total serum calcium without correcting for albumin, or on corrected calcium during acute illness, can mislead; ionized calcium should be measured directly when accuracy matters","A normal serum magnesium does not exclude total-body magnesium depletion because the extracellular pool is tiny","The body phosphate deficit cannot be predicted from a single serum phosphate; repletion must be empirical and monitored, since the nadir often occurs hours into insulin/glucose therapy","Giving IV magnesium alone to a patient with combined hypomagnesemia, hypocalcemia, and hypophosphatemia can worsen hypophosphatemia by abruptly restoring PTH secretion; give calcium and magnesium together","Failing to recognize that hypocalcemia and hypokalemia are refractory until magnesium is repleted","Overlooking 1,25(OH)2D-mediated hypercalcemia from granulomatous disease or lymphoma, where calcium does not suppress the ectopic 1-alpha-hydroxylase","Phosphate measured non-fasting or after IV dextrose can be spuriously low; rapid phosphate correction risks heterotopic calcification if the calcium-phosphorus product is allowed to rise"],"severityClassification":"Hypophosphatemia is graded by serum phosphate (mild-moderate ~0.5-0.8 mmol/L [1.5-2.5 mg/dL] with mainly musculoskeletal/reversible cardiorespiratory effects; severe <0.5 mmol/L [<1.5 mg/dL]; life-threatening <0.25 mmol/L [<0.8 mg/dL] with paralysis, seizures, rhabdomyolysis). Hyperphosphatemia in adults is defined as fasting phosphate >1.8 mmol/L (5.5 mg/dL). Hypomagnesemia is usually asymptomatic above ~0.5 mmol/L; defects in PTH secretion appear below ~0.4 mmol/L. Hypermagnesemia produces vasodilation/neuromuscular blockade above ~2 mmol/L, coma and hyporeflexia above ~4 mmol/L, and asystole near 10 mmol/L.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3259","Harrison 22e p.3260","Harrison 22e p.3261","Harrison 22e p.3262","Harrison 22e p.3263","Harrison 22e p.3264","Harrison 22e p.3265","Harrison 22e p.3266","Harrison 22e p.3267","Harrison 22e p.3268","Harrison 22e p.3269"],"crossLinks":[]},"bone_marrow_failure_syndromes_including":{"name":"Bone Marrow Failure Syndromes Including Aplastic Anemia and Myelodysplasia","system":"Hematologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Aplastic anemia","Myelodysplastic syndrome","MDS","Pure red cell aplasia","PRCA","Bone marrow failure","Inherited bone marrow failure syndromes","IBMFS","Pancytopenia (marrow failure)"],"chapter":107,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.808","clinicalPearls":["Marrow failure cytopenias reflect deficient production, distinguishing them from peripheral destruction (hemolysis, ITP, immune leukopenia, hypersplenism); the syndromes are categorized by the dominant marrow morphology, with aplastic anemia (fatty/empty marrow), MDS (dysplastic marrow), and single-lineage aplasias forming a related spectrum.","Immune (acquired) aplastic anemia is classically a young person's disease with abrupt pancytopenia in a previously well adolescent or young adult; the age distribution is biphasic with peaks in the teens-twenties and again in older adults.","In aplastic anemia bleeding (bruising, gum oozing, epistaxis, menorrhagia, petechiae) is typically the earliest symptom, whereas infection as a first symptom is unusual; lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly are highly atypical and should prompt search for another cause of pancytopenia.","Clues to an inherited bone marrow failure syndrome: short stature and cafe au lait spots with thumb/radial anomalies (Fanconi anemia); dystrophic nails, mucosal leukoplakia and reticular hyperpigmentation (dyskeratosis congenita); premature hair graying plus a family history of pulmonary or hepatic fibrosis (telomere biology disorders); recurrent warts and unusual infections (GATA2 deficiency).","A detectable PNH clone by flow cytometry is found in up to half of patients at presentation of aplastic anemia and strongly suggests immune-mediated pathophysiology and likely responsiveness to immunosuppression; patients can later cross over between aplastic and hemolytic PNH phenotypes.","MDS is predominantly a disease of older adults (mean onset over 70) with a slight male predominance; anemia (often macrocytic) dominates the early course and at least half of patients are asymptomatic, discovered incidentally on routine blood counts.","Persistent parvovirus B19 infection is a treatable cause of pure red cell aplasia; the marrow shows giant pronormoblasts (the cytopathic signature), and diagnosis requires detection of viral DNA because IgG/IgM antibodies are commonly absent in immunodeficient hosts.","B19 parvovirus uses the erythrocyte P antigen as its receptor; in patients with underlying hemolysis it can precipitate a transient aplastic crisis with abrupt worsening of anemia from arrested erythropoiesis.","Macrocytosis (elevated MCV) is a frequent finding across marrow failure states; in aplastic anemia the smear shows large red cells with a paucity of platelets and granulocytes, near-absent reticulocytes, and the elevated MCV often persists even after recovery.","Marrow blast percentage is the strongest morphologic prognostic determinant in MDS; the WHO threshold of 20% marrow blasts separates AML from MDS, and blasts should be enumerated manually on smear and by flow cytometry.","5q deletion MDS arises from heterozygous loss of a ribosomal protein gene, effectively mimicking constitutional red cell aplasia; SF3B1 mutation is strongly associated with ring sideroblasts and a relatively favorable outcome.","Severity grading in aplastic anemia and recovery thresholds are defined by blood counts (see severity criteria); absolute reticulocyte and lymphocyte numbers are better predictors of treatment response than the classic severity definition in the immunosuppression era."],"pathophysiology":"Bone marrow failure results from severe damage to the hematopoietic cell compartment. In aplastic anemia the marrow is replaced by fat, CD34+ early hematopoietic cells are greatly diminished, and the stem cell pool in severe disease may be reduced to about 1% or less of normal. Three principal mechanisms underlie an empty marrow: physical/chemical damage (high-dose radiation, benzene and its reactive hydroquinone/quinolone metabolites, cytotoxic chemotherapy), immune-mediated stem cell destruction, and constitutional genetic defects. Acquired aplastic anemia is most often immune: activated cytotoxic T cells suppress hematopoietic progenitors, type 1 cytokines including interferon-gamma upregulate Fas on CD34+ cells driving apoptosis, and immune selection can produce HLA-loss and PNH clones. Genetically determined immune-response features (HLA, cytokine and T-cell polarization gene polymorphisms) likely convert a normal response into sustained autoimmunity, explaining why aplasia is rare despite common exposures. Idiosyncratic drug-induced aplasia is attributed to abnormal generation of or failure to detoxify reactive electrophilic drug intermediates that bind cellular macromolecules. Constitutional syndromes are intrinsic stem cell defects: Fanconi anemia involves a DNA interstrand cross-link repair pathway (most often FANCA; the gene products monoubiquitinate FANCD2), telomere biology disorders involve telomerase complex (DKC1, TERC, TERT) or shelterin (TINF2) defects that limit replicative capacity, and GATA2/RUNX1 mutations disrupt hematopoietic transcriptional regulation; Diamond-Blackfan and Shwachman-Diamond syndromes are ribosomopathies. MDS is a clonal hematopoietic stem cell disorder of aging marrow characterized by disordered proliferation, impaired differentiation and ineffective hematopoiesis producing cytopenias plus risk of leukemic progression; both chromosomal instability (non-random losses of 5, 7, 20, trisomy 8) and accumulating somatic mutations in ~100 recurrently mutated myeloid genes drive disease, with clonal succession of founder clones acquiring further mutations during evolution to AML.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypoplastic/hypocellular MDS (most important and difficult distinction from aplastic anemia)","Constitutional vs acquired aplastic anemia (Fanconi anemia, telomere biology disorders, GATA2 and RUNX1 predisposition)","Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","Acute leukemia / aleukemic leukemia and bone marrow lymphoma","Myelofibrosis and myelophthisis (marrow infiltration by tumor)","Hairy cell leukemia","Vitamin B12, folate or copper deficiency causing cytopenias with dysplastic marrow","VEXAS syndrome (somatic UBA1 mutation) in MDS with inflammatory features","Systemic lupus erythematosus with marrow hypoplasia","Large granular lymphocytosis or thymoma associated cytopenias"],"infectionMimics":["HIV infection","Disseminated tuberculosis / Mycobacterium","Brucellosis","Leishmaniasis","Q fever","Legionnaires' disease","Sepsis","Epstein-Barr virus (post-infectious mononucleosis aplasia)","Persistent parvovirus B19 (pure red cell aplasia)","Seronegative (non-A, non-B, non-C) hepatitis preceding aplastic anemia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Megaloblastic anemia from B12 or folate deficiency","Copper deficiency (and zinc or alcohol toxicity) causing dysplastic marrow with vacuolated precursors","Alcohol-related marrow suppression","Hypersplenism / splenomegaly of cirrhosis","Metastatic carcinoma infiltrating marrow","Anorexia nervosa or starvation","Sarcoidosis","Drug-induced cytopenias (idiosyncratic reactions and chemotherapy)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Bone marrow aspirate and trephine biopsy (biopsy >1 cm preferred) for cellularity and morphology; hypocellular marrow with <25% hematopoietic cells supports aplastic anemia","Peripheral smear and reticulocyte count (look for absent reticulocytes, large red cells, immature myeloid forms or nucleated red cells suggesting MDS/myelophthisis)","Flow cytometry for a PNH clone","Chromosome breakage testing with diepoxybutane or mitomycin C for Fanconi anemia (children and younger adults)","Peripheral blood leukocyte telomere length measurement for telomere biology disorders","Bone marrow cytogenetics and FISH (abnormal in MDS, expected normal in typical aplastic anemia)","Next-generation sequencing germline panels (50+ genes) for constitutional syndromes and myeloid neoplasm somatic panels (~100 genes) for MDS/AML, including spliceosome and cohesin genes","Serologies for EBV, HIV and viral hepatitis","Vitamin B12, folate and serum copper levels","Spinal MRI may demonstrate fatty marrow replacement in aplastic anemia","Parvovirus B19 DNA detection in suspected PRCA","Chest imaging to seek thymoma in PRCA and to exclude miliary tuberculosis"],"redFlags":["Profound neutropenia (absolute neutrophil count <500/uL) with fever mandates urgent empirical broad-spectrum parenteral antibiotics without awaiting cultures","Persistent or recrudescent fever despite antibacterials implies invasive fungal disease (Candida, Aspergillus)","Severe thrombocytopenia with central nervous system or retinal bleeding can be catastrophic (intracranial or retinal hemorrhage)","Platelet count below 5000/uL sharply increases risk of gut bleeding","Systemic symptoms, weight loss, lymphadenopathy or splenomegaly point away from simple aplastic anemia toward malignancy or systemic disease","Emergence of new cytogenetic abnormalities, rising blasts, marrow fibrosis or abrupt worsening of pancytopenia signals clonal evolution to MDS/leukemia","Family history of AML/MDS or unexplained cytopenias should trigger evaluation for an inherited predisposition syndrome"],"prognosis":"Untreated severe aplastic anemia deteriorates rapidly and is fatal; spontaneous recovery is rare. The dominant prognostic factor is the blood count: severe disease has historically required two of three of an absolute neutrophil count <500/uL, platelets <20,000/uL and corrected reticulocyte count <1% (or absolute reticulocytes <60,000/uL), while in the immunosuppression era higher absolute reticulocytes (>25,000/uL) and lymphocytes (>1000/uL) better predict response and survival. A PNH clone, very short telomeres and somatic myeloid mutations (e.g., ASXL1) further influence outcome and risk of clonal evolution. Clonal evolution to MDS or leukemia occurs in roughly 10-15% of immunosuppression-treated patients over a decade. In MDS, median survival ranges from years (5q- or sideroblastic anemia) to only months (high blast count disease); the IPSS-R and emerging molecularly informed IPSS-M stratify risk. Even lower-risk MDS carries meaningful morbidity and mortality; most patients die of complications of pancytopenia rather than leukemic transformation, and about a third die of unrelated comorbidities. Therapy-related MDS has an extremely poor prognosis with rapid progression to refractory AML. Pure red cell aplasia is generally compatible with long-term survival with supportive care, and persistent parvovirus B19 PRCA almost always responds to immunoglobulin.","pitfalls":["Clinical drug/chemical and viral associations with aplastic anemia are not proof of causation; the implicated agent may merely have been used to treat the earliest symptoms (antibiotics for fever, NSAIDs causing petechiae in an already thrombocytopenic patient).","Marrow cellularity correlates imperfectly with disease severity: 'empty' biopsies can occur in moderate disease and focal 'hot spots' of hematopoiesis can be seen in severe aplasia, so a single small or dilute sample can mislead (a dry tap suggests fibrosis or myelophthisis rather than aplasia).","Distinguishing acquired from constitutional aplastic anemia is essential; constitutional marrow is usually morphologically indistinguishable and physical anomalies may be subtle or absent (adults with telomere disorders can present without anomalies, only premature graying).","Hypocellular MDS can be mistaken for aplastic anemia; the boundary with low-risk MDS is blurred (del 13q/20q may respond to immunosuppression, and DNMT3A/ASXL1 mutations occur in both).","Dysplastic marrow changes are not specific to MDS, occurring with vitamin deficiencies, copper deficiency, drug effects, alcohol and acute viral infection; interobserver agreement on dysplasia morphology is imperfect.","Germline genomic panel results may take weeks to return, a problem in the severely pancytopenic patient who needs prompt management.","In transplant-eligible aplastic anemia patients, transfusing blood from family members risks alloimmunization and should be avoided; HLA typing should be ordered as soon as the diagnosis is made.","Glucocorticoids are not effective primary therapy for aplastic anemia and increase fungal infection risk."],"severityClassification":"Severe aplastic anemia has historically been defined by the presence of at least two of three criteria: absolute neutrophil count <500/uL, platelet count <20,000/uL, and corrected reticulocyte count <1% (or absolute reticulocyte count <60,000/uL). In the immunosuppressive-therapy era, absolute reticulocyte count >25,000/uL and absolute lymphocyte count >1000/uL are better predictors of response and long-term outcome. For MDS, prognostic severity is assessed by the Revised International Prognostic Scoring System (IPSS-R), which integrates marrow blast percentage (categories at <=2%, >2-<5%, 5-10%, >10-30%), refined cytogenetic risk groups, depth of cytopenias (hemoglobin, platelets, neutrophils), and additional features (age, performance status, ferritin, LDH, beta-2-microglobulin) into five risk categories; the molecularly enhanced IPSS-M is emerging as the current standard.","references":["Harrison 22e p.808","Harrison 22e p.809","Harrison 22e p.810","Harrison 22e p.811","Harrison 22e p.812","Harrison 22e p.813","Harrison 22e p.814","Harrison 22e p.815","Harrison 22e p.816","Harrison 22e p.817","Harrison 22e p.818"],"crossLinks":[]},"borderline_personality_disorder":{"name":"Borderline personality disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["BPD","emotionally unstable personality disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The disorder is a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image, and affect, together with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood and evident across contexts","Characteristic features include frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, intense and unstable relationships that swing between idealisation and devaluation, identity disturbance, and chronic feelings of emptiness","Recurrent self-harm and suicidal behaviour, affective instability, transient stress-related paranoia or dissociation, and difficulty controlling anger are common and clinically important","Self-harm and suicide attempts are frequent, so ongoing risk assessment and a clear crisis plan are essential parts of care","Structured evidence-based psychotherapies, such as dialectical behaviour therapy and mentalisation-based treatment, are the mainstay of management","Pharmacotherapy is adjunctive and targets specific symptoms or comorbidities rather than the disorder itself, and polypharmacy should be avoided","A consistent, validating, and boundaried therapeutic stance reduces crises and helps prevent unhelpful splitting among care providers","Comorbid mood, anxiety, eating, and substance use disorders are common and should be identified and treated"],"pathophysiology":"Borderline personality disorder is understood through a biosocial model in which a heritable predisposition to emotional vulnerability and impulsivity interacts with an invalidating or traumatic developmental environment. Neurobiological findings include altered function of limbic structures such as the amygdala and of prefrontal regulatory regions, contributing to heightened emotional reactivity and impaired regulation, with disturbances in serotonergic and stress-response systems.","additionalDifferentials":["Bipolar disorder, particularly with rapid mood shifts","Major depressive disorder or persistent depressive disorder","Post-traumatic stress disorder and complex trauma presentations","Other personality disorders such as histrionic or narcissistic","Substance use disorders causing affective and behavioural instability","Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder with impulsivity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Longitudinal history and collateral information to establish the enduring pattern","Structured suicide and self-harm risk assessment with a crisis plan","Screening for comorbid mood, anxiety, eating, and substance use disorders","Trauma history taken sensitively","Standardised personality assessment tools where appropriate"],"redFlags":["Acute suicidal ideation, escalating self-harm, or a recent attempt","Rapid escalation of impulsive behaviour placing the patient at serious risk","Emergence of psychotic-level symptoms beyond transient stress-related phenomena","Severe comorbid substance misuse increasing danger during crises"],"prognosis":"Contrary to older pessimistic views, many patients with borderline personality disorder show substantial improvement over time, with reduced impulsivity and self-harm and often remission of full criteria, though interpersonal and functional difficulties can persist. Prognosis is better with engagement in structured psychotherapy and stable support, and worse with severe early trauma, persistent substance misuse, and repeated high-lethality self-harm.","pitfalls":["Reacting to crises with escalating polypharmacy rather than psychotherapy and structure","Dismissing chronic suicidality and thereby missing genuine acute risk","Allowing splitting to divide the treating team through inconsistent boundaries","Misdiagnosing affective instability as bipolar disorder","Overlooking comorbid trauma and substance use disorders"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"botulism":{"name":"Botulism","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":[],"chapter":158,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1233-1238","clinicalPearls":["Classic syndrome: bilateral cranial nerve palsies followed by symmetric, descending flaccid paralysis, in a patient who is afebrile and fully conscious with normal sensorium (Harrison 22e p.1235).","First neurologic manifestation is usually ptosis, which can be striking; ocular findings include blurred/fuzzy vision or diplopia from extraocular palsies of cranial nerves III, IV, and VI (Harrison 22e p.1235).","Bulbar onset: dysarthria, difficulty swallowing, and nasal/oral regurgitation reflect cranial nerve involvement; descending weakness then spreads to neck, shoulders, and limbs, with proximal groups affected before distal (Harrison 22e p.1235).","Paralysis descends symmetrically and distal tendon reflexes diminish symmetrically; constipation from intestinal paralysis develops in almost all patients (Harrison 22e p.1235-1236).","In a U.S. series of 332 cases, the most common reported symptoms were difficulty swallowing (86%), fatigue (85%), blurred vision (80%), and slurred speech (78%); signs included afebrile temperature (99%), descending paralysis (93%), alert/oriented status (93%), and ptosis (81%) (Harrison 22e p.1236).","BoNTs irreversibly block acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction, so paralysis persists for weeks to months until nerve endings regenerate; the clinical approach is the same for all toxin types causing human illness (Harrison 22e p.1234, p.1236).","Four naturally occurring forms (foodborne, infant, wound, adult intestinal colonization) plus iatrogenic and inhalational forms; the neurologic syndrome is essentially identical across all forms and all ages (Harrison 22e p.1233-1235).","Every case of foodborne botulism is a public health emergency, as it may be the first in an outbreak; clinicians should contact the state health department emergency line to reach a CDC botulism consultant (Harrison 22e p.1235, p.1237).","More than 95% of noninfant botulism patients in the U.S. recover, and the infant botulism survival rate is near 100% (Harrison 22e p.1236)."],"pathophysiology":"Disease is caused by botulinum neurotoxins (BoNTs) produced by Clostridium botulinum (rarely C. butyricum or C. baratii), gram-positive, spore-forming, anaerobic bacteria whose heat-resistant spores are ubiquitous in soil. Serotypes A, B, E, and F cause human disease. BoNT is a metalloprotease whose heavy chain mediates neurospecific binding and internalization and whose light chain enters the cytosol and cleaves SNARE-family proteins (e.g., SNAP-25, VAMP), disrupting synaptic vesicle fusion and blocking acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction, producing flaccid paralysis of voluntary muscles. Toxin binding is effectively irreversible, so recovery requires regeneration of nerve endings over weeks to months. Routes differ by form: foodborne (ingestion of preformed toxin in improperly preserved foods, often home-canned low-acid vegetables), wound (spore germination in wounds/necrotic tissue, notably from black tar heroin injection), infant and adult intestinal colonization (in situ toxin production by colonizing Clostridium), iatrogenic (high-dose therapeutic BoNT injection), and inhalational (aerosolized toxin as a bioweapon). (Harrison 22e p.1233-1234)","additionalDifferentials":["Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS)","Myasthenia gravis","Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome","Tick paralysis","Basilar stroke producing nonlateralizing/symmetric bulbar palsies","Tetrodotoxin or shellfish poisoning","Antimicrobial-associated paralysis"],"infectionMimics":["Guillain-Barre syndrome following Campylobacter jejuni or viral infection"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Myasthenia gravis","Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (proximal limb weakness, often with advanced cancer)","Tick paralysis","Basilar stroke","Intoxication from alcohol or other substances (ptosis, dysarthria, and gait instability can be misattributed)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed neurologic examination (the key to clinical diagnosis, uncovering invariable cranial nerve deficits)","Lumbar puncture / CSF analysis (protein usually normal in botulism, in contrast to GBS; may help distinguish)","Electromyography by an experienced operator (neuromuscular junction blockage with normal axonal conduction and potentiation on rapid repetitive stimulation)","Brain imaging (normal in botulism; helps exclude basilar stroke)","Serologic antibody tests and rapid nerve stimulation tests (to evaluate myasthenia gravis)","Mouse bioassay (gold standard for BoNT identification and serotyping in clinical or food specimens)","Detection of BoNT in serum or stool, or isolation of BoNT-producing Clostridium from stool or wound cultures; toxin in suspected food","In vitro assays: real-time PCR for bont genes, Endopep mass spectrometry, immune-based and cell-based assays"],"redFlags":["Pharyngeal muscle flaccidity causing airway collapse and respiratory arrest early in the course","Falling vital capacity / diaphragmatic and accessory muscle weakness signaling impending respiratory failure","Rapidly progressive descending paralysis (especially BoNT type A or F)","A placid, expressionless appearance masking the agitation expected with respiratory distress","Autonomic dysfunction producing hemodynamic instability","Two or more patients with the same syndrome (near-pathognomonic and signals an outbreak / public health emergency)"],"prognosis":"More than 95% of noninfant botulism patients in the United States recover, often after protracted intensive care and rehabilitation, even without antitoxin; the survival rate for infant botulism is near 100%. Paralysis lasts weeks to months while affected nerve endings regenerate. Serotype A is associated with more rapid progression, more frequent respiratory compromise and mechanical ventilation, and longer-duration paralysis; type B is milder and shorter; type F progresses rapidly but recovers faster. Untreated, early death results from airway obstruction and inadequate tidal volume from respiratory muscle paralysis. (Harrison 22e p.1236)","pitfalls":["Misclassifying the paralysis as ascending: botulism is a DESCENDING (cranial-first, then downward) symmetric flaccid paralysis, whereas GBS presents as an ASCENDING paralysis in about 95% of cases - this descending-versus-ascending distinction is central, though the Miller Fisher GBS variant (ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, areflexia) may resemble early descending paralysis (Harrison 22e p.1236).","Mistaking intact sensorium for altered consciousness: cranial nerve palsies, ptosis, dysarthria, and gait instability can give a false impression of obtundation or be wrongly attributed to alcohol/substance intoxication, even though patients remain fully conscious (Harrison 22e p.1235-1236).","Sporadic (lone) cases are commonly misdiagnosed because of the rarity of botulism and clinician unfamiliarity; an incomplete neurologic examination is a frequent cause of delayed or missed diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1236).","Waiting for laboratory confirmation: confirmation may take >=24 h, so treatment must rely on rapid clinical diagnosis rather than awaiting lab results (Harrison 22e p.1236-1237).","Assuming normal CSF excludes GBS too early: in GBS, CSF protein elevation may lag symptom onset by days, so a normal early CSF must be interpreted with symptom duration and the lumbar puncture may need repeating (Harrison 22e p.1236).","Reported paresthesias are not explained by the known activity of botulinum toxin, and GI symptoms (nausea/vomiting) are seen in foodborne but have NOT been reported in wound botulism (Harrison 22e p.1235-1236)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal numeric severity score; severity is described as varying greatly between patients and is probably governed by toxin dose. At the mild end, some untreated patients progress no further than ptosis and mild palsy of one or two cranial nerves; at the severe end, others develop fulminant cranial nerve palsies and rapidly progressive descending flaccid paralysis affecting most or all voluntary muscles with respiratory failure requiring intubation and mechanical ventilation within hours. (Harrison 22e p.1236)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1233-1238"],"crossLinks":["gbs","myasthenic_crisis"]},"bppv":{"name":"Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["BPPV","positional vertigo","benign positional vertigo"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common cause of peripheral vertigo, characterised by brief spinning episodes triggered by changes in head position","Typical triggers include rolling over in bed, lying down, looking up or bending forward","Each episode is short-lived, usually lasting seconds to under a minute, and there is no associated hearing loss or tinnitus","The posterior semicircular canal is most often involved because of its dependent position","The Dix-Hallpike manoeuvre reproduces symptoms and elicits characteristic positional nystagmus with a brief latency and fatigability","Management principles rely on canalith repositioning manoeuvres such as the Epley manoeuvre rather than long-term drug therapy","Vestibular sedatives are generally unhelpful and may hinder central compensation if used long term","Recurrences are common but usually respond again to repositioning; atypical or persistent features should prompt reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo is caused by displacement of otoconia (calcium carbonate crystals) from the utricular macula into a semicircular canal, most often the posterior canal. In the common canalithiasis form, these free-floating particles move under gravity when the head changes position, creating abnormal endolymph flow that deflects the cupula and generates a transient, mismatched sensation of rotation. The brief latency, short duration and fatigability of symptoms reflect the settling of the particles. A less common cupulolithiasis form, with particles adherent to the cupula, produces more persistent positional nystagmus.","additionalDifferentials":["Vestibular neuritis","Meniere's disease","Central positional vertigo from posterior fossa pathology","Vestibular migraine","Orthostatic hypotension","Perilymph fistula"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dix-Hallpike test for posterior canal involvement","Supine roll (head-turn) test for suspected horizontal canal disease","Otoscopy and hearing assessment to exclude other otological causes","Neurological examination to exclude central causes","Imaging of the brain and posterior fossa only if central pathology is suspected"],"redFlags":["Atypical nystagmus that is purely vertical, direction-changing or non-fatiguing suggesting a central cause","Associated neurological signs, severe headache or gait ataxia","Accompanying hearing loss or tinnitus pointing to alternative inner ear disease","Persistent continuous vertigo rather than brief positional episodes"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and often self-limiting, with excellent response to repositioning manoeuvres, although recurrences over time are common and occasionally require repeat treatment.","pitfalls":["Mistaking central positional vertigo for BPPV and missing serious pathology","Relying on vestibular suppressant drugs instead of repositioning manoeuvres","Failing to identify the affected canal, leading to ineffective treatment","Overlooking coexisting hearing loss that indicates a different diagnosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"brachial_neuritis":{"name":"Brachial neuritis","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neuralgic amyotrophy","Parsonage-Turner syndrome","idiopathic brachial plexopathy","acute brachial plexus neuropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by abrupt, severe pain around the shoulder and upper arm, followed within days to weeks by patchy weakness and wasting as the pain subsides","The distribution is often patchy and does not conform to a single nerve or root, reflecting involvement of individual nerves or plexus branches","The long thoracic nerve is frequently affected, producing scapular winging, and the anterior interosseous and suprascapular nerves are also commonly involved","Often follows an antecedent trigger such as infection, immunisation, surgery, or physical stress","Sensory symptoms are usually less prominent than the motor deficit and the early pain","A hereditary recurrent form exists (linked to SEPT9), so a family history and recurrent episodes should be explored","Diagnosis is largely clinical, supported by electromyography showing multifocal denervation, with imaging used to exclude structural or compressive lesions","Management is mainly supportive with analgesia and physiotherapy, as most patients recover over months"],"pathophysiology":"Brachial neuritis (neuralgic amyotrophy) is thought to be an immune-mediated inflammatory disorder of the brachial plexus and its branches, often triggered by antecedent infection, vaccination, surgery, or other immunological stressors. Inflammation causes multifocal axonal damage to individual nerves or fascicles, sometimes with focal nerve constrictions or torsions demonstrable on high-resolution imaging. The predominantly axonal injury explains the muscle wasting and the protracted recovery as reinnervation occurs. A hereditary form associated with SEPT9 mutations produces recurrent attacks.","additionalDifferentials":["Cervical radiculopathy from disc or spondylosis","Rotator cuff disease or other primary shoulder pathology","Compressive or traumatic brachial plexopathy","Neoplastic or radiation-induced plexopathy","Thoracic outlet syndrome","Mononeuritis multiplex from vasculitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electromyography and nerve conduction studies to confirm multifocal, mainly axonal involvement","MRI of the brachial plexus or high-resolution nerve ultrasound to identify constrictions and exclude structural lesions","Cervical spine MRI when radiculopathy is a competing diagnosis","Basic inflammatory and infective screen when a systemic cause is suspected","Consideration of genetic testing (SEPT9) in recurrent or familial cases"],"redFlags":["Progressive or non-resolving deficit suggesting a compressive, neoplastic, or infiltrative plexopathy","A history of cancer or radiotherapy raising concern for malignant or radiation plexopathy","Bilateral or systemic features suggesting an alternative diagnosis such as vasculitis","Rapidly worsening weakness that warrants urgent structural imaging"],"prognosis":"Most patients recover useful function over many months to a few years, though recovery can be incomplete with residual weakness or scapular winging, and the hereditary form tends to recur.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the initial severe pain for a primary shoulder or rotator cuff problem and delaying diagnosis","Confusing it with cervical radiculopathy because both cause arm pain and weakness","Overlooking scapular winging from long thoracic nerve involvement","Failing to consider a hereditary recurrent form in patients with repeated attacks","Assuming a compressive lesion and pursuing unnecessary surgery in a self-limiting inflammatory condition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"bradyarrhythmias":{"name":"Bradyarrhythmias & Heart Block","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sinus node dysfunction","AV block","heart block","sick sinus syndrome"],"chapter":251,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1917-1930","clinicalPearls":["Sinus node dysfunction (SND), also termed sick sinus syndrome, typically presents with fatigue, exercise intolerance, dyspnea, or syncope from a slow rate or pauses; the key diagnostic step is correlating symptoms with bradycardia/pauses on ECG or ambulatory monitoring (p.1917-1918).","Tachycardia-bradycardia (tachy-brady) syndrome is a subset of SND: high rates (most commonly atrial fibrillation) alternating with symptomatic bradycardia or offset pauses after the tachycardia terminates; the drugs needed for rate control often worsen the bradycardia, frequently prompting pacemaker implantation (p.1919).","AV block is classified on ECG: first-degree = fixed PR prolongation >200 ms with all impulses conducted; second-degree = intermittent failure of conduction (Mobitz I/Wenckebach with progressive PR prolongation before a dropped beat vs Mobitz II with fixed PR before a dropped beat); third-degree = complete AV dissociation with ventricular rate slower than atrial rate (p.1925-1926).","Mobitz I (Wenckebach) AV block typically involves the AV node, is hemodynamically stable, and in the absence of symptoms does NOT usually warrant pacing; Mobitz II is usually infranodal, associated with QRS widening and a less reliable escape rhythm, carries a risk of sudden death, and requires permanent pacing even when asymptomatic (p.1925).","Escape morphology localizes block: a narrow QRS escape implies AV-nodal/proximal-His block with a faster, more stable junctional rhythm, whereas a wide QRS escape implies distal His or bundle-branch block with a slower, less reliable rhythm (p.1926).","AV block from acute MI is more common with inferior MI but is usually proximal (AV node), with a stable narrow escape >40/min and often reversible; AV block in anterior MI is usually distal/infranodal, gives wide unstable escape rhythms, and carries a worse prognosis with high mortality (p.1926-1927).","Lyme carditis is a classic reversible infectious cause of AV block (usually nodal, narrow junctional escape >40/min) that typically resolves within about a week of antibiotic therapy; AV block with infective endocarditis should raise concern for a perivalvular abscess requiring urgent surgical consultation (p.1927).","AV block that develops at a younger age (≤60 years) warrants advanced imaging (cardiac MRI, CT, or FDG-PET) to evaluate for infiltrative disease such as sarcoidosis, since age-related progressive conduction-system fibrosis is otherwise the most common cause (p.1928).","Permanent pacing is the only reliable long-term therapy for symptomatic irreversible bradycardia; there is NO established heart-rate threshold below which pacing is automatically indicated, and the decision is driven by symptom-bradycardia correlation (p.1917, p.1922).","Initial management of any bradyarrhythmia is to identify and eliminate reversible causes and assess the safety/reliability of the escape rhythm before deciding on temporary or permanent pacing (p.1922, p.1929)."],"pathophysiology":"Bradyarrhythmias arise from abnormalities of the specialized conduction tissues. The sinoatrial (SA) node is the natural pacemaker (resting rate 60-100/min); its pacemaker cells have no true resting potential and depolarize spontaneously in phase 4 driven by the funny current (If) plus T- and L-type calcium currents, with calcium (not sodium) carrying the slow phase-0 upstroke. SA node function is dominated by parasympathetic tone at baseline (intrinsic heart rate ~100-110/min after autonomic blockade); intrinsic rate declines ~5-6 beats/min per decade of age. SA node dysfunction reflects problems of impulse formation (sinus arrest) or impulse conduction (sinoatrial exit block), and is most often due to age-related fibrosis and loss of pacemaker myocytes. In the AV node, conduction is decremental and depends on L-type calcium current; the node and proximal His are richly innervated by autonomic input, whereas the His bundle and distal conduction system are NOT influenced by autonomic tone. Acquired AV conduction disease is most commonly due to fibrosis/sclerosis/calcification of the conduction system (~50% of cases: senile degeneration/Lev's disease, Lenegre's disease, aortic-annular calcification). Block is anatomically nodal (vagally modulated, stable narrow escape) or infranodal (His-Purkinje, unstable wide escape, higher risk).","additionalDifferentials":["Sinus arrest vs sinoatrial exit block (type I fixed delay; type II Mobitz I progressive vs Mobitz II fixed pattern)","Tachy-brady (sick sinus) syndrome with post-conversion offset pauses","Chronotropic incompetence (failure to raise rate with demand; can be missed on standard exercise testing)","Carotid sinus hypersensitivity and cardioinhibitory vasovagal syncope","Physiologic/athletic sinus bradycardia and sleep-related vagal pauses","First- vs second-degree (Mobitz I vs II) vs third-degree (complete) AV block","AV-nodal vs infranodal (His-Purkinje) level of block; bifascicular/trifascicular block","Congenital AV block (maternal SLE, congenital heart defects)"],"infectionMimics":["Lyme carditis (Borrelia burgdorferi) causing AV block","Infective endocarditis with perivalvular abscess","Viral myocarditis","Chagas' disease","Toxoplasmosis","COVID-19 (listed cause of sinus bradycardia)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Drug/medication effect (beta blockers, non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, digoxin, amiodarone and other antiarrhythmics, clonidine, lithium, etc.)","Hypothyroidism and other endocrine disease","Hyperkalemia/electrolyte disturbance","Increased vagal tone (sleep, well-conditioned athlete, cough/micturition/vasovagal, carotid sinus hypersensitivity)","Sleep apnea (high vagal tone during apneic events)","Hypothermia, hypoxia, raised intracranial pressure","Infiltrative cardiomyopathy (sarcoidosis, amyloidosis, hemochromatosis)","Neuromuscular disease (myotonic dystrophy, Kearns-Sayre, Lamin A/C cardiomyopathy)","Iatrogenic: cardiac/valve surgery, TAVR, alcohol septal ablation, catheter ablation injury, spinal cord injury, post heart transplant"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG (cornerstone; capture rhythm and assess QRS width of escape)","Ambulatory/longer-term monitoring (Holter, mobile cardiac telemetry, event/implantable loop recorders, wearable ECG watches) to obtain symptom-rhythm correlation","Exercise/treadmill testing to assess maximum heart rate and chronotropic competence","Transthoracic echocardiography for structural heart disease","Advanced cardiac imaging (cardiac MRI, CT, FDG-PET, Tc-PYP) when infiltrative disease suspected or AV block at ≤60 years","Vagal maneuvers / carotid sinus massage and atropine/isoproterenol challenge to localize level of block","Invasive electrophysiology study with His-bundle electrogram (AH interval for AV node <130 ms; HV interval for His-Purkinje ≤55 ms) reserved for selected/uncertain cases; SNRT/SACT rarely used and NOT an absolute pacing indication"],"redFlags":["Syncope or presyncope (Stokes-Adams attack) with documented bradyarrhythmia","Mobitz type II second-degree AV block (risk of sudden death; infranodal; pace even if asymptomatic)","High-grade or third-degree (complete) AV block, especially with a wide-complex/slow escape rhythm","Wide-complex escape rhythm at ~30/min (implies low/unreliable block) vs benign narrow junctional escape ~45/min","AV block with anterior MI (extensive infarction, distal block, high mortality)","AV block with infective endocarditis (suggests perivalvular abscess; urgent surgical consult)","HV interval >100 ms in bundle branch block (~10% annual incidence of complete block; indicates need for pacing)","Bifascicular/trifascicular block with unexplained syncope"],"prognosis":"Asymptomatic sinus bradycardia has NOT been associated with adverse outcomes and generally does not warrant pacing; symptomatic SND is benign in terms of mortality and is treated to relieve symptoms. AV block prognosis depends critically on the level of block: AV-nodal (Mobitz I) block tends to be stable with a reliable junctional escape and good prognosis, whereas infranodal block (Mobitz II, distal third-degree) has unreliable escape rhythms and risk of sudden death. AV block complicating anterior MI carries high mortality, while inferior-MI nodal block is often transient and reversible. With appropriate permanent pacing, symptomatic bradyarrhythmias have an excellent prognosis. An HV interval >100 ms predicts ~10% annual progression to complete block.","pitfalls":["Treating asymptomatic sinus bradycardia or sleep/athletic vagal pauses with a pacemaker - these generally are NOT pacing indications; always check whether pauses occurred during sleep.","Missing reversible causes (drugs, hypothyroidism, hyperkalemia/electrolytes, ischemia, sleep apnea, increased vagal tone, hypothermia, raised ICP) before committing to permanent pacing.","Overlooking Lyme carditis as a reversible cause of AV block (typically resolves within ~1 week of antibiotics) - a treatable cause that should not lead to an unnecessary permanent pacemaker.","Failing to suspect a perivalvular abscess when AV block develops during infective endocarditis.","Treating Mobitz II or high-grade/complete infranodal block as benign - it requires pacing even without symptoms, unlike Mobitz I.","In 2:1 AV block, ECG cannot reliably distinguish Mobitz I from Mobitz II; clues (PR <160 ms with wide QRS suggesting infranodal) or EP study may be needed.","Missing chronotropic incompetence because a patient achieves a normal maximum heart rate on standard exercise testing despite inadequate rate during daily activity.","Misjudging a normal heart-transplant resting rate (~90-110/min due to denervation) as normal when it may represent chronotropic incompetence."],"severityClassification":"ECG-based AV block grading: First-degree (PR >200 ms, all impulses conducted, usually AV-nodal delay) -> Second-degree (intermittent failure; Mobitz I/Wenckebach = progressive PR prolongation before dropped beat, usually nodal/stable; Mobitz II = fixed PR before dropped beat, usually infranodal/wide QRS/unstable) -> Third-degree/complete (complete AV dissociation, ventricular rate slower than atrial). SND subtypes: sinus arrest, sinoatrial exit block (type I, type II Mobitz I/II), tachy-brady syndrome, chronotropic incompetence. Pacing-indication classes (ACC/AHA/HRS): Class I (complete/high-grade or acquired Mobitz II block when irreversible - pace regardless of symptoms; symptomatic irreversible 2nd/3rd-degree block), Class IIa/IIb (symptom- or disease-dependent for milder block), Class III: Harm (asymptomatic physiologic bradycardia/pauses).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1917-1930","Harrison 22e ch.251-252"],"crossLinks":["vasovagal_syncope","hyperkalemia","acs"]},"breast_abscess":{"name":"Breast abscess","system":"Breast / Surgery","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lactational breast abscess","non-lactational breast abscess","puerperal abscess"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as a painful, hot, tender, often fluctuant breast lump with overlying erythema and systemic features such as fever and malaise","Lactational abscesses typically follow mastitis with milk stasis and are most often due to Staphylococcus aureus, frequently entering through a cracked nipple","Non-lactational abscesses are strongly associated with smoking and periductal mastitis and tend to occur near the areola, often with mixed and anaerobic organisms","Established abscesses are best managed by ultrasound-guided needle aspiration, repeated if necessary, with incision and drainage reserved for large, multiloculated, or overlying-necrosis cases","Appropriate antibiotics active against the likely organisms are given alongside drainage; antibiotics alone rarely cure a collection","Breastfeeding or expression from the affected side should generally continue to relieve stasis in lactational cases","A non-lactational or non-resolving abscess, particularly in an older woman, requires exclusion of inflammatory breast cancer and other malignancy","Recurrent periareolar abscess with a mammary duct fistula may need duct excision once inflammation settles"],"pathophysiology":"A breast abscess is a localised collection of pus within breast tissue. In lactating women, milk stasis and nipple trauma allow skin organisms, chiefly Staphylococcus aureus, to enter and proliferate, progressing from mastitis to a walled-off abscess. In non-lactating women, periductal mastitis, strongly linked to smoking, causes duct damage, squamous metaplasia, and duct obstruction, with secondary infection by mixed aerobic and anaerobic organisms producing periareolar collections and sometimes a mammary duct fistula. Untreated collections enlarge, may point to the skin, and can cause tissue necrosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Mastitis without abscess","Inflammatory breast carcinoma","Fat necrosis of the breast","Infected breast cyst","Duct ectasia with periductal mastitis","Tuberculous or granulomatous mastitis","Hidradenitis of the inframammary or axillary skin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Breast ultrasound to confirm a collection and guide aspiration","Culture and sensitivity of aspirated pus or milk to direct antibiotic choice","Mammography and core biopsy where malignancy must be excluded, especially non-lactational or non-resolving cases","Assessment of smoking status and diabetes in recurrent non-lactational disease"],"redFlags":["Non-resolving or recurrent inflammatory changes suggesting inflammatory breast cancer","Overlying skin necrosis or spreading cellulitis with systemic sepsis","Immunosuppression or diabetes with rapidly progressive infection","A persistent mass after treatment requiring triple assessment"],"prognosis":"Most breast abscesses resolve fully with timely drainage and appropriate antibiotics. Non-lactational and smoking-related disease is prone to recurrence and mammary duct fistula, and outcome improves with smoking cessation and, when indicated, duct excision.","pitfalls":["Treating with antibiotics alone and failing to drain an established collection","Missing inflammatory breast cancer masquerading as a non-resolving abscess","Advising cessation of breastfeeding unnecessarily, worsening milk stasis","Not addressing smoking in recurrent non-lactational disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"breast_cancer":{"name":"Breast Cancer","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["breast carcinoma"],"chapter":84,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.632","clinicalPearls":["Most common nonskin cancer in women worldwide; predominantly a disease of prolonged estrogen exposure, with female gender and increasing age the most common risk factors (about 70% diagnosed at 55 years or older, median age 63) (Harrison 22e p.632).","Clinical signs and symptoms suggestive of breast cancer may include a breast lump, skin or nipple changes, or palpable regional nodes; with mammographic screening many patients are asymptomatic and present only with an imaging abnormality (mass, distortion, or suspicious microcalcifications) (Harrison 22e p.634).","Diagnosis rests on pathologic confirmation, generally by image-guided core biopsy, which also assesses tumor grade and morphology and performs biomarker evaluation for estrogen receptor (ER), progesterone receptor (PR), and HER2 protein expression and potentially HER2 gene amplification (the triple assessment of clinical/imaging/pathology with biomarkers) (Harrison 22e p.634).","Suspicious axillary nodes should be biopsied via image-guided techniques, and both breasts should be imaged because up to 3% of women with newly diagnosed breast cancer have unsuspected contralateral disease (Harrison 22e p.634).","Invasive breast cancer is divided into at least four molecular subtypes: luminal A and luminal B (both ER positive; luminal B has reduced PR or higher Ki67 proliferation and worse outcome), HER2 subtype (high HER2 expression, benefits from HER2-targeted therapy), and basal/triple-negative (TNBC, lacking ER, PR, and HER2) (Harrison 22e p.636).","Most invasive breast cancers are ductal; about 10-15% are lobular, which are usually ER positive and HER2 negative, distinguished by absence of E-cadherin, and have a propensity to spread to serosal surfaces (omentum, pleura, meninges) and parenchyma (ovaries, GI tract) (Harrison 22e p.636).","Family history is a critical risk factor, yet only 20% of women diagnosed with breast cancer have a family history; a first-degree relative with breast cancer doubles the risk (Harrison 22e p.633)."],"pathophysiology":"Breast cancer is predominantly a disease resulting from prolonged exposure of the breast to estrogen. Most breast cancers begin in epithelial cells within the lobules or ducts and proceed through a conceptual continuum of atypia and hyperplasia to in situ malignancy, then invasion into surrounding tissue, intravasation into lymph and blood channels, spread to regional lymph nodes, and distant metastases (though not all cancers progress through every stage). Hereditary breast cancer susceptibility genes account for 5-10% of cases; these genes participate in DNA damage repair, and inherited autosomal dominant mutations generally cause protein truncation and loss of function of DNA repair proteins. The most common are BRCA1 (chromosome 17q21) and BRCA2 (chromosome 13q12), conferring a 50-80% lifetime risk of invasive breast cancer by age 80 and a 30% risk of ovarian cancer; other germline mutations include TP53 (Li-Fraumeni), PALB2, ATM, STK11 (Peutz-Jeghers), and PTEN (Cowden). Ashkenazi Jews have a higher BRCA mutation incidence (Harrison 22e p.632-633).","additionalDifferentials":["Inflammatory breast cancer is sometimes confused with mastitis or other breast infection (Harrison 22e p.638).","Benign breast pathology such as atypical ductal or lobular hyperplasia (raises risk about four- to fivefold) and lobular carcinoma in situ (LCIS, raises risk 7- to 12-fold) (Harrison 22e p.633)."],"infectionMimics":["Mastitis or other breast infection can be mimicked by inflammatory breast cancer, which presents with rapid onset of erythema, swelling, and tenderness of the breast; breast or skin biopsy is critical when breast inflammation fails to resolve after antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.638)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Evolving changes in the pregnant breast can make diagnosis of breast cancer during pregnancy difficult, so suspicious lumps or skin or nipple changes still require formal evaluation (Harrison 22e p.638)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Image-guided core biopsy for pathologic confirmation, tumor grade/morphology, and biomarker testing (ER, PR, HER2 protein, and potentially HER2 gene amplification) (Harrison 22e p.634).","Diagnostic breast imaging: mammography, ultrasonography, and MRI if indicated; bilateral breast imaging because up to 3% have unsuspected contralateral disease (Harrison 22e p.634).","Germline panel testing for breast cancer susceptibility mutations on DNA from peripheral blood or saliva after appropriate counseling (not recommended for the general population) (Harrison 22e p.633).","For symptoms or findings of advanced disease (large tumor, skin changes, extensive adenopathy): CT and radionuclide bone scanning to look for overt metastatic disease, with biopsy of suspicious lesions to confirm metastatic disease (Harrison 22e p.635).","Genomic transcriptional profiling assays for early-stage ER-positive disease (Oncotype Dx 21-gene, MammaPrint 70-gene, ProSigna 50-gene, EndoPredict 12-gene, Breast Cancer Index) to inform adjuvant chemotherapy decisions; ordering more than one per case is discouraged (Harrison 22e p.636).","Skin biopsy showing dermal lymphatic invasion with tumor cells supports diagnosis of inflammatory breast cancer (Harrison 22e p.638)."],"redFlags":["Inflammatory breast cancer: rapid onset of erythema, swelling, and tenderness of the breast (a subset of locally advanced disease); breast inflammation failing to resolve after antibiotics warrants breast or skin biopsy, and up to one-third have detectable metastasis at diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.638).","Any unexplained mass or lesion palpable on physical examination needs further assessment including biopsy where warranted; a normal mammogram does NOT rule out cancer in a woman with a palpable breast lump (Harrison 22e p.45).","Locally advanced features: large primary tumor, involvement of skin or chest wall, or fixed tumor or axillary nodes (Harrison 22e p.638)."],"prognosis":"5-year relative survival in the United States is currently about 91%. Outcomes are directly related to stage at presentation (and vary by race/ethnicity): 5-year relative survival is over 99% for stage I, about 93% for stage II, about 75% for stage III, and about 29% for stage IV. Less than 10% present with de novo metastatic (M1) disease. Luminal B tumors carry a poorer outcome than luminal A; TNBC carries a poorer prognosis; mucinous and pure medullary cancers have very favorable prognoses with local therapy only; higher Nottingham grade is associated with poorer prognosis. About 20-25% of patients treated for early breast cancer subsequently develop metastatic disease (Harrison 22e p.635-638).","pitfalls":["A common disease-specific diagnostic pitfall is ordering a mammogram for a woman with a palpable breast lump and, when it returns normal, reassuring her that cancer has been ruled out; any unexplained palpable mass needs further testing and biopsy where warranted (Harrison 22e p.45).","Lobular cancers can recur at unusual sites (serosal surfaces, ovaries, GI tract), producing atypical presentations that may delay diagnosis if metastatic disease is not considered (Harrison 22e p.636).","Up to 3% of newly diagnosed patients have unsuspected contralateral disease, so both breasts must be imaged (Harrison 22e p.634).","Inflammatory breast cancer may be mistaken for mastitis or infection; biopsy is critical when inflammation fails to resolve after antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.638).","Germline panel testing can yield variants of unknown significance (VUS) and includes genes with poorly defined clinical risk, causing patient confusion and discomfort (Harrison 22e p.633)."],"severityClassification":"Staging follows the American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) eighth edition TNM (tumor-node-metastasis) system, which has evolved to incorporate molecular biomarkers (ER, PR, HER2) and certain genomic tests (e.g., Oncotype 21-gene assay). Conceptually, stages I and II are early-stage disease confined to breast and ipsilateral nodes; stage III is locally advanced disease; and stage IV (M1) is metastatic disease. Staging may be clinical (c), pathologic after surgery (p), or post-neoadjuvant (y prefix). Common metastatic sites are soft tissues, lung, bone, liver, and brain (Harrison 22e p.634-635).","references":["Harrison 22e p.632"],"crossLinks":[]},"bronchiectasis":{"name":"Bronchiectasis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bronchiectasis","non-CF bronchiectasis","chronic suppurative lung disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by permanent, abnormal dilatation of the bronchi, presenting with chronic productive cough, copious purulent sputum, and recurrent chest infections","Haemoptysis, breathlessness, and coarse crackles with occasional wheeze are common, and clubbing may be present in advanced disease","High-resolution CT is the diagnostic standard, showing airway dilatation greater than the accompanying vessel (signet-ring sign), lack of tapering, and airway wall thickening","Causes include prior severe infection, cystic fibrosis, primary ciliary dyskinesia, immunodeficiency, ABPA, and obstruction; a cause should be actively sought","Sputum microbiology guides therapy; chronic Pseudomonas aeruginosa colonisation is associated with more frequent exacerbations and worse outcomes","Airway clearance techniques and pulmonary rehabilitation are cornerstones of long-term management","Exacerbations are treated with appropriately targeted antimicrobials guided by prior culture results, and long-term suppressive strategies are considered in frequent exacerbators","Assessment for treatable contributors such as immunoglobulin deficiency or ABPA can meaningfully change management"],"pathophysiology":"A vicious cycle of airway injury drives bronchiectasis: an initial insult (infection, impaired mucociliary clearance, immune deficiency, or obstruction) impairs clearance of secretions, allowing bacterial colonisation. Persistent bacterial presence provokes chronic neutrophilic inflammation with release of proteases and reactive oxygen species that progressively damage the bronchial wall, weakening and dilating the airways. The damaged, dilated airways clear secretions still less effectively, perpetuating infection and inflammation and leading to further structural destruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease with chronic bronchitis","Cystic fibrosis","Chronic asthma with mucus hypersecretion","Pulmonary tuberculosis and non-tuberculous mycobacterial disease","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Chronic aspiration"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution CT of the chest to confirm and map the extent of airway dilatation","Sputum culture including for mycobacteria and fungi to guide targeted therapy","Immunoglobulin levels and other immune workup to detect underlying immunodeficiency","Testing for cystic fibrosis and, where suspected, ciliary function studies","Aspergillus-specific serology and total IgE to evaluate for ABPA"],"redFlags":["Massive or recurrent haemoptysis requiring urgent intervention","Rapid clinical decline or systemic sepsis during an exacerbation","New isolation of resistant organisms or non-tuberculous mycobacteria","Progressive respiratory failure or features of cor pulmonale"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies widely with the underlying cause and disease extent; many patients remain stable for years with good airway clearance and infection control, but frequent exacerbations, chronic Pseudomonas colonisation, and extensive disease predict faster decline in lung function and higher mortality.","pitfalls":["Treating recurrent chest infections symptomatically without imaging to establish the diagnosis","Failing to search for a reversible or treatable underlying cause","Neglecting airway clearance in favour of antibiotics alone","Not obtaining sputum cultures before escalating antimicrobial therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"bronchiolitis":{"name":"Bronchiolitis","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute bronchiolitis","RSV bronchiolitis","viral bronchiolitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common lower respiratory tract infection of infants, typically under 2 years and peaking around 2-6 months, most often caused by respiratory syncytial virus (RSV).","Begins with upper respiratory symptoms (coryza, cough, low-grade fever) that progress over a few days to tachypnoea, wheeze, crackles, and increased work of breathing.","Diagnosis is clinical; routine viral testing, chest radiographs, and blood tests are usually unnecessary and can be misleading.","Management is supportive: ensure adequate hydration and feeding, maintain oxygenation, and provide gentle nasal suction as needed.","Bronchodilators, corticosteroids, and antibiotics are generally not beneficial in typical bronchiolitis and are not routinely recommended.","Apnoea can be an early and sometimes presenting sign, particularly in young or premature infants.","Risk factors for severe disease include prematurity, age under 2-3 months, congenital heart disease, chronic lung disease, and immunodeficiency.","Most cases are self-limiting over 1-2 weeks, though cough can persist longer."],"pathophysiology":"Viral infection of the small airway epithelium causes inflammation, oedema, and necrosis of epithelial cells with increased mucus production. Sloughed cellular debris and mucus, together with airway wall swelling, partially or completely obstruct the small bronchioles. Because infant airways are narrow, this obstruction produces air trapping, hyperinflation, atelectasis, and ventilation-perfusion mismatch, leading to wheeze, hypoxaemia, and increased work of breathing.","additionalDifferentials":["Viral-induced wheeze / early asthma","Pneumonia","Congenital heart disease with heart failure","Foreign body aspiration","Gastro-oesophageal reflux with aspiration","Pertussis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pulse oximetry to assess oxygenation","Nasopharyngeal viral testing (mainly for cohorting or in severe/atypical cases)","Chest radiograph only if diagnosis is unclear or deterioration is unexplained","Blood gas in severe respiratory distress or impending failure","Capillary or venous blood tests only when a bacterial complication is suspected"],"redFlags":["Apnoea or episodes of colour change","Persistent hypoxaemia or rising oxygen requirement","Severe respiratory distress: marked recession, grunting, nasal flaring, head bobbing","Inability to feed or signs of dehydration","Exhaustion, lethargy, or signs of impending respiratory failure"],"prognosis":"Most previously healthy infants recover fully within one to two weeks; a minority, especially high-risk infants, require hospitalisation, and some children have recurrent wheeze afterwards.","pitfalls":["Overusing bronchodilators, steroids, or antibiotics that provide no benefit in typical disease","Relying on transient oxygen desaturation readings and over-treating stable, feeding infants","Missing apnoea as a presenting feature in very young infants","Ordering routine chest radiographs, which may prompt unnecessary antibiotics for atelectasis mistaken for infiltrate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"bronchopulmonary_dysplasia":{"name":"Bronchopulmonary Dysplasia","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["BPD","chronic lung disease of prematurity","chronic lung disease of infancy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic lung disease of prematurity, usually defined by a persisting need for supplemental oxygen or respiratory support at around 36 weeks postmenstrual age.","Occurs mainly in very preterm, very-low-birth-weight infants, often those who required prolonged mechanical ventilation and oxygen for respiratory distress syndrome.","The modern ('new') form reflects arrested alveolar and pulmonary vascular development rather than the severe fibrotic airway injury seen historically.","Contributing factors include oxygen toxicity, ventilator-induced volutrauma and barotrauma, inflammation, infection, and a patent ductus arteriosus.","Lung-protective strategies, such as early CPAP, gentle ventilation targeting acceptable gas exchange, and judicious oxygen targeting, help prevent it.","Management emphasises adequate nutrition and calories for lung growth, careful fluid balance, and weaning of respiratory support as tolerated.","Systemic corticosteroids can facilitate extubation but carry neurodevelopmental risks, so their use is weighed carefully and generally limited.","Affected infants are prone to recurrent wheeze, respiratory infections, and pulmonary hypertension, and benefit from RSV prophylaxis and immunisation.","Caffeine therapy for apnoea of prematurity is associated with a lower rate of bronchopulmonary dysplasia."],"pathophysiology":"In the immature lung, the combination of oxidative stress from supplemental oxygen, mechanical injury from positive-pressure ventilation, inflammation, and antenatal or postnatal infection disrupts normal alveolar septation and pulmonary microvascular growth. The result is fewer, larger, simplified alveoli with an abnormal vascular bed, variable airway smooth-muscle and interstitial changes, and impaired gas exchange. Pulmonary vascular remodelling can lead to pulmonary hypertension.","additionalDifferentials":["Recurrent aspiration","Congenital heart disease with left-to-right shunt or pulmonary oedema","Pulmonary hypertension from other causes","Recurrent or persistent lower respiratory infection","Interstitial lung disease of infancy","Tracheobronchomalacia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph showing hyperinflation, cystic or coarse interstitial changes","Pulse oximetry and, where indicated, blood gas analysis","Echocardiography to screen for pulmonary hypertension","Assessment of growth and nutritional status","Selected infants may need chest CT or bronchoscopy for airway evaluation"],"redFlags":["Escalating oxygen or ventilatory requirement","Signs of pulmonary hypertension or cor pulmonale","Failure to gain weight despite adequate calories","Frequent acute deteriorations or hypoxaemic spells","Recurrent lower respiratory tract infections requiring hospitalisation"],"prognosis":"Most infants gradually improve as the lung grows during early childhood, but many have increased respiratory morbidity, exercise limitation, and abnormal lung function later; severe disease with pulmonary hypertension carries higher mortality and long-term risk.","pitfalls":["Using liberal oxygen targets that worsen lung and retinal injury","Overlooking pulmonary hypertension as a cause of persistent oxygen need","Excess fluid administration precipitating pulmonary oedema","Prolonged or high-dose systemic steroid courses despite neurodevelopmental risk","Underestimating nutritional needs required for lung repair and growth"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"brucellosis":{"name":"Brucellosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["undulant fever","Malta fever"],"chapter":174,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1331-1335","clinicalPearls":["A bacterial zoonosis caused by Brucella species, transmitted directly or indirectly from infected animals, predominantly domesticated ruminants and swine; B. melitensis (sheep, goats, camels) is the most common cause of symptomatic human disease, with B. abortus (cattle/buffalo), B. suis (swine), and B. canis (dogs) also implicated (p.1331).","Human infection is usually associated with occupational or domestic animal exposure (farmers, shepherds, goatherds, veterinarians, slaughterhouse and meat-processing workers, laboratory staff); travelers and urban residents typically acquire it through contaminated food (p.1332).","Dairy products, especially soft cheeses, unpasteurized milk, and ice cream, are the most frequently implicated sources; acquisition may occur by ingestion, inhalation, or mucosal/percutaneous exposure (p.1332).","Almost invariably causes fever, which may be accompanied by profuse sweats, especially at night; untreated, the fever classically shows an undulating pattern persisting for weeks before an afebrile period that may be followed by relapse (p.1332-1333).","Fever is associated with musculoskeletal symptoms and signs in about half of patients; nonspecific features include apathy, fatigue, anorexia, weight loss, myalgia, headache, and chills (p.1333).","Presentation often fits one of three patterns: a typhoid-like febrile illness but less severe; fever with acute monoarthritis (typically hip or knee) in a young child; and long-lasting fever with misery and low-back or hip pain in an older person, especially men (p.1333).","Focal musculoskeletal involvement is the most common complication (~40%): osteomyelitis favors lumbar and low thoracic vertebrae, and septic arthritis most often affects knee, hip, sacroiliac, shoulder, and sternoclavicular joints, as monoarthritis or polyarthritis (p.1333).","Roughly one-quarter of patients have hepatosplenomegaly and 10-20% have significant lymphadenopathy; up to 10% of men develop acute epididymo-orchitis (p.1333).","Endocarditis occurs in about 1% of cases, most often affecting the aortic valve (native or prosthetic), and is the usual cause of death when the outcome is fatal (p.1333, p.1335).","Diagnosis rests on a history of potential exposure, a compatible presentation, and supporting laboratory findings, because the clinical picture is not distinctive (p.1333).","Isolation of brucellae from blood, CSF, bone marrow, joint fluid, or a tissue aspirate/biopsy is definitive and succeeds in about 50-70% of cases; modern automated blood culture systems (e.g., Bactec) usually turn positive within 7 days (p.1333).","Serology often provides the only positive laboratory finding; in endemic or occupationally exposed settings agglutinin titers of 1:320-1:640 or higher are considered diagnostic, while in nonendemic areas a titer of at least 1:160 is significant, with no single universal cutoff (p.1334)."],"pathophysiology":"Brucellae are small, gram-negative, unencapsulated, nonsporulating coccobacilli that behave as facultative intracellular parasites. Following entry by ingestion, inhalation, or mucosal/percutaneous exposure, initial replication is presumed to occur within cells of the lymph nodes draining the entry point, with subsequent hematogenous spread that can establish chronic localizing infection at almost any site, most often the reticuloendothelial system, musculoskeletal tissues, and genitourinary system. The macrophage is the key target cell; Brucella is a \"stealth\" pathogen that evades innate immunity and survives within monocytic cells by restricting fusion of type IV secretion system-dependent Brucella-containing vacuoles with lysosomes, inhibiting apoptosis of infected cells, and preventing dendritic cell maturation and T-cell activation. Smooth LPS has low endotoxin activity and contributes to pyrogenicity and resistance to phagocytosis and serum killing. Antibodies aid clearance of extracellular organisms but alone cannot eradicate infection; control depends on macrophage activation and IFN-gamma/TH1 responses. Tissue responses range from acute and chronic inflammation to granuloma formation, with or without necrosis and caseation, and abscesses may develop in chronic localized infection (p.1332).","additionalDifferentials":["Tuberculosis is the most important differential for vertebral osteomyelitis or septic arthritis; vertebral destruction or spinal cord impingement is rare in brucellosis and usually suggests tuberculosis (p.1333).","Typhoid fever and malaria as causes of fever in endemic areas (p.1333).","Glandular fever-like illness (EBV, Toxoplasma, cytomegalovirus, HIV, or Mycobacterium tuberculosis) when hepatosplenomegaly or lymphadenopathy is present (p.1333).","Mumps and surgical conditions such as testicular torsion when epididymo-orchitis occurs (p.1333).","Neurotuberculosis, atypical leptospirosis, and noninfectious conditions when lymphocytic meningoencephalitis develops (p.1333)."],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis","Typhoid fever","Malaria","Leptospirosis","Epstein-Barr virus infection","Cytomegalovirus infection","Toxoplasmosis","HIV infection","Mumps"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Noninfectious causes of lymphocytic meningoencephalitis","Chronic fatigue syndromes (in the context of a suspected chronic brucellosis state)","Other causes of excessive sweating such as alcohol abuse and obesity"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood, CSF, bone marrow, joint fluid, or tissue aspirate/biopsy culture under containment conditions for dangerous pathogens; alert the laboratory when brucellosis is suspected (p.1333).","Standard tube/plate/microagglutination serology as the mainstay, with complement fixation, Coombs antiglobulin, and ELISA tests; repeat after 2-4 weeks to detect a rising titer (p.1334).","Peripheral blood PCR / nucleic acid amplification (e.g., targeting rrs-rrl spacer, outer-membrane protein genes, IS711, BCSP31) which is more sensitive and quicker than culture and avoids biohazard risk (p.1334).","MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry for identification in well-resourced laboratories, with the caveat that earlier implementations often failed to identify Brucella correctly (p.1333).","Isotope bone scanning, more sensitive than plain x-ray for bony disease (p.1333).","CSF/joint fluid analysis showing lymphocytosis and low glucose; tissue biopsy may show noncaseating granulomas without acid-fast bacilli (p.1333)."],"redFlags":["Endocarditis, most often of the aortic valve (native or prosthetic), the leading cause of death in fatal cases (p.1333, p.1335).","Neurobrucellosis: lymphocytic meningoencephalitis mimicking neurotuberculosis, and rarely intracerebral abscess, cranial nerve deficits, or ruptured mycotic aneurysms (p.1333).","Vertebral osteomyelitis (spondylitis) and sacroiliitis (p.1333).","Disseminated intravascular coagulation with raised fibrinogen degradation products and thrombocytopenia (p.1333).","Increased fetal loss in infected pregnant women (though teratogenicity has not been described and abortion is much less pronounced in humans than in animals) (p.1333)."],"prognosis":"Fewer than 1% of patients die of brucellosis; when fatal, death is usually due to cardiac involvement and, more rarely, severe neurologic disease. Despite low mortality, recovery is slow and the illness can cause prolonged inactivity. Relapse occurs in up to 30% of poorly compliant patients, so clinical follow-up for up to 2 years is recommended; relapse responds to a prolonged repeat course of the original therapy. General well-being and body weight are better guides to absence of relapse than serology, since IgG agglutination titers can stay in the diagnostic range for more than 2 years after cure. Immunity is not solid and reinfection can occur after repeated exposures (p.1335).","pitfalls":["The clinical picture is not distinctive and definitive diagnostic signs may be lacking, so exposure history and laboratory support are essential (p.1331, p.1333).","Routine biochemistry is usually normal; peripheral leukocyte counts are typically normal or low with relative lymphocytosis, so normal blood counts do not exclude the disease (p.1333).","Brucella may be misidentified by gallery strips as Agrobacterium, Ochrobactrum, or Psychrobacter (Moraxella) phenylpyruvicus; after taxonomic changes Ochrobactrum isolates may now be reported as a Brucella subspecies despite completely different management, so culture reports should be confirmed with the laboratory before therapeutic decisions (p.1333-1334).","Rough strains such as B. canis and B. ovis do not react in conventional serologic tests and will give false-negative results; consult the laboratory if B. canis is suspected (p.1334).","Serologic cross-reactions occur with other gram-negative bacteria including Yersinia enterocolitica O:9, E. coli O157, Francisella tularensis, Salmonella enterica group N, Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, and Vibrio cholerae (p.1334).","Live B. abortus vaccine strain RB51 does not elicit antibody in tests using smooth antigens, which must be considered when following accidental vaccine exposures (p.1334).","Elevated CSF adenosine deaminase cannot be used to distinguish tubercular meningitis, as it may also be raised in brucellosis (p.1333).","Existence of a prolonged chronic brucellosis state after successful treatment remains controversial; evaluation requires careful exclusion of malingering, chronic fatigue syndromes, and other causes of sweating such as alcohol abuse and obesity (p.1335)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison distinguishes acute nonfocal brucellosis (duration under about 1-2 months) from complex or focal disease (e.g., spondylitis, neurobrucellosis, endocarditis), the latter requiring more prolonged and tailored therapy. Patterns by species are also noted: B. melitensis tends toward a more acute, aggressive presentation, B. suis toward focal abscess formation, and B. abortus toward a more insidious onset that is more likely to become chronic (p.1333-1334).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1331-1335"],"crossLinks":[]},"brugada_syndrome":{"name":"Brugada syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Brugada","Brugada pattern","SCN5A channelopathy","sudden unexpected nocturnal death syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Brugada syndrome is an inherited arrhythmia disorder characterised by a distinctive coved ST-segment elevation in the right precordial leads and a risk of polymorphic ventricular arrhythmia and sudden death in a structurally normal heart","Only the type 1 coved pattern is diagnostic; the saddleback type 2 pattern is suggestive and may need provocation or repositioned high leads to confirm","Arrhythmic events characteristically occur at rest or during sleep and may be triggered by fever, which can unmask the pattern","Certain drugs (including some sodium-channel blockers, and various psychotropic and anaesthetic agents) can precipitate or accentuate the pattern and should be avoided","It disproportionately affects young to middle-aged men and can present as syncope, nocturnal agonal breathing, or aborted sudden death","Diagnosis integrates the ECG pattern with clinical and family history, and a drug provocation test may be used when the resting ECG is non-diagnostic","An implantable cardioverter-defibrillator is the mainstay of protection in high-risk patients, and prompt treatment of fever is advised","Family screening and genetic counselling are important given autosomal dominant inheritance with variable expression"],"pathophysiology":"Brugada syndrome is most often linked to loss-of-function mutations in the cardiac sodium channel gene SCN5A, reducing inward sodium current. The leading explanation involves heterogeneity of repolarisation and depolarisation predominantly in the right ventricular outflow tract, producing a transmural voltage gradient that generates the characteristic ST-segment changes and a substrate for phase 2 reentry. This can trigger polymorphic ventricular tachycardia and ventricular fibrillation. Factors that reduce sodium current or increase transient outward current, such as fever and specific drugs, accentuate the abnormality and increase arrhythmic risk, which is why events cluster at rest, during sleep, and with febrile illness.","additionalDifferentials":["Right bundle branch block","Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy","Acute right precordial ST elevation from ischaemia or pericarditis","Electrolyte disturbance mimicking a Brugada pattern","Early repolarisation variants","Drug-induced or fever-induced phenocopies of the pattern"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG including high right precordial lead placement to detect the coved pattern","Sodium-channel-blocker provocation testing under monitoring when the resting ECG is non-diagnostic","Assessment during febrile illness given fever-induced unmasking","Genetic testing and cascade family screening","Echocardiography or cardiac imaging to exclude structural disease such as ARVC","Electrophysiology study in selected cases for risk stratification"],"redFlags":["MEDICAL EMERGENCY: polymorphic ventricular tachycardia or ventricular fibrillation causing syncope, cardiac arrest, or sudden death","Syncope or nocturnal agonal respiration in a patient with a type 1 pattern indicating high arrhythmic risk","Spontaneous (not drug-induced) type 1 pattern, which carries greater risk","Fever in a known patient, which can precipitate malignant arrhythmia"],"prognosis":"Asymptomatic individuals with only a drug-induced pattern generally have a low event rate, whereas those with a spontaneous type 1 pattern and prior syncope or aborted arrest are at substantially higher risk; defibrillator therapy and avoidance of triggers markedly improve outcomes in high-risk patients.","pitfalls":["Treating a non-diagnostic saddleback pattern as definitive Brugada syndrome","Failing to place high right precordial leads or to consider provocation when suspicion is high","Prescribing drugs that can precipitate the pattern or omitting prompt fever control","Neglecting family screening after a diagnosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"budd_chiari_syndrome":{"name":"Budd-Chiari syndrome","system":"GI / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hepatic venous outflow obstruction","hepatic vein thrombosis","Budd Chiari syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AASLD & ACG","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by obstruction of hepatic venous outflow anywhere from the small hepatic veins to the junction of the inferior vena cava and right atrium, producing hepatic congestion","The classic triad is abdominal pain, ascites, and hepatomegaly, though presentation ranges from asymptomatic to fulminant liver failure","An underlying prothrombotic state is present in most patients, most notably myeloproliferative neoplasms (including JAK2-associated disease), so a thrombophilia workup is essential","Doppler ultrasound is the usual first-line test, with CT or MR venography to define the level and extent of the obstruction","The caudate lobe often drains directly into the inferior vena cava and is characteristically spared and hypertrophied while the rest of the liver is congested","Management is stepwise: anticoagulation for essentially all patients, then angioplasty or stenting of short-segment stenoses, transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunting, and finally liver transplantation for refractory or fulminant cases","Acute presentation with rapid-onset jaundice, coagulopathy, and encephalopathy represents acute liver failure and is a medical emergency","Chronic outflow obstruction leads to congestive fibrosis and eventually cirrhosis with portal hypertension"],"pathophysiology":"Obstruction of hepatic venous outflow raises sinusoidal pressure and causes hepatic congestion, hypoxic injury to hepatocytes, and centrilobular necrosis. The resulting portal hypertension produces ascites and, over time, congestive fibrosis progressing to cirrhosis. Most cases stem from thrombosis on a background prothrombotic disorder, commonly a myeloproliferative neoplasm, inherited thrombophilia, antiphospholipid syndrome, paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria, pregnancy, or oral contraceptive use; in some regions membranous webs of the inferior vena cava predominate. The caudate lobe, with its independent venous drainage into the vena cava, escapes congestion and hypertrophies. Speed and completeness of obstruction determine whether the course is fulminant, acute, or chronic.","additionalDifferentials":["Right heart failure or constrictive pericarditis causing hepatic congestion","Cirrhosis from other causes with portal hypertension","Sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (veno-occlusive disease)","Portal vein thrombosis","Malignant infiltration of the liver or vena cava","Fulminant hepatitis of other aetiology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Doppler ultrasound of the hepatic veins and inferior vena cava as the initial test","CT or MR venography to map the site and length of obstruction and assess caudate lobe changes","Thrombophilia and myeloproliferative neoplasm workup, including JAK2 mutation testing","Liver function tests, coagulation profile, and assessment for encephalopathy to gauge severity","Ascitic fluid analysis and, where needed, liver biopsy or hepatic venography"],"redFlags":["Acute liver failure with jaundice, coagulopathy, and encephalopathy requiring emergency specialist and transplant assessment","Rapidly worsening liver function or tense refractory ascites","Concurrent inferior vena cava obstruction with lower-limb oedema and renal impairment","Evidence of an aggressive underlying myeloproliferative disorder"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the rapidity and extent of obstruction and the response to a staged treatment strategy. Many patients do well with anticoagulation and interventional or shunt procedures, but fulminant presentations carry high mortality without transplantation, and untreated chronic disease progresses to cirrhosis.","pitfalls":["Attributing ascites and hepatomegaly to more common cirrhosis and missing outflow obstruction","Failing to search for and treat an underlying prothrombotic disorder, allowing rethrombosis","Delaying escalation from anticoagulation to interventional or transplant options in deteriorating patients","Misinterpreting caudate lobe hypertrophy as a mass lesion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AASLD/ACG guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"buerger_disease":{"name":"Buerger disease","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thromboangiitis obliterans","TAO","Buerger's disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A non-atherosclerotic segmental inflammatory occlusive disease of small and medium arteries and veins of the distal limbs","Overwhelmingly affects young tobacco users, typically men under forty, with a strong and causal relationship to smoking","Presents with distal ischaemia including digital pain, claudication of the foot or hand, cold sensitivity, ulceration, and gangrene of the toes or fingers","Migratory superficial thrombophlebitis and Raynaud phenomenon are recognised associated features","Both upper and lower limbs may be involved, distinguishing it from typical atherosclerotic peripheral disease","Diagnosis is clinical and by exclusion, supported by angiographic findings of distal segmental occlusions with corkscrew collaterals and absence of proximal atheroma or an embolic source","Complete and permanent tobacco cessation is the single most effective intervention and can halt progression and prevent amputation","Standard revascularisation is often not feasible because of the distal small-vessel pattern, so wound care and abstinence are central"],"pathophysiology":"Buerger disease (thromboangiitis obliterans) is a segmental non-atherosclerotic vasculitis affecting small and medium arteries and veins of the extremities. There is an inflammatory, highly cellular thrombus with relative sparing of the vessel wall and internal elastic lamina, distinguishing it histologically from atherosclerosis and other vasculitides. Tobacco exposure is central to pathogenesis, likely through an immune-mediated and endothelial mechanism, and continued use drives progression. Occlusion of distal vessels produces critical ischaemia of the digits with limited collateral compensation.","additionalDifferentials":["Premature atherosclerotic peripheral arterial disease","Embolic occlusion from a cardiac or proximal arterial source","Systemic vasculitides (e.g. polyarteritis nodosa)","Connective tissue disease with digital ischaemia (e.g. scleroderma)","Thrombophilia or hypercoagulable states","Ergotism or vasospastic disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Angiography demonstrating distal segmental occlusions with corkscrew collaterals and absence of proximal atheroma","Echocardiography and proximal arterial imaging to exclude an embolic source","Autoimmune and vasculitis serology to exclude systemic vasculitis and connective tissue disease","Thrombophilia screening where clinically indicated","Tobacco use assessment including confirmatory testing when abstinence is claimed"],"redFlags":["Rest pain, ulceration, or digital gangrene indicating critical ischaemia and threatened tissue loss","Rapidly progressive digital necrosis, particularly with continued smoking","Ascending infection of an ischaemic digit","Involvement of multiple limbs signalling extensive disease"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is closely tied to smoking behaviour: complete cessation usually arrests the disease and greatly reduces amputation risk, whereas continued tobacco use commonly leads to recurrent ischaemia and progressive tissue loss. Life expectancy is generally near normal, but limb and digit loss is the major morbidity.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing atherosclerotic disease in a young smoker and missing thromboangiitis obliterans","Underemphasising that continued smoking, including any tobacco or nicotine exposure, drives progression","Pursuing revascularisation where the distal small-vessel pattern makes it unsuitable","Failing to exclude an embolic source or systemic vasculitis before settling on the diagnosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"bulimia_nervosa":{"name":"Bulimia nervosa","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bulimia","BN"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by recurrent episodes of binge eating with a sense of loss of control, followed by compensatory behaviours to prevent weight gain","Compensatory behaviours include self-induced vomiting, laxative or diuretic misuse, fasting, or excessive exercise","Self-evaluation is unduly influenced by body shape and weight, and patients are typically of normal or near-normal weight, unlike in anorexia nervosa","Physical signs may include salivary gland enlargement, dental enamel erosion, and Russell's sign (knuckle calluses from self-induced vomiting)","Purging can cause dangerous electrolyte disturbances such as hypokalaemia and metabolic alkalosis, with risk of cardiac arrhythmia","First-line treatment is cognitive behavioural therapy adapted for eating disorders; SSRIs (notably fluoxetine) are the pharmacological principle of choice","Comorbid depression, anxiety, substance use, and impulsivity are common, and suicide risk should be assessed","Behaviours are often concealed, so specific, non-judgemental enquiry is needed to detect them"],"pathophysiology":"Bulimia nervosa involves interacting genetic, temperamental, and sociocultural factors, with dysregulation of serotonergic systems affecting satiety, mood, and impulse control. Dietary restraint promotes binge episodes, and the binge-purge cycle is maintained by both physiological drives and psychological reinforcement, while overvaluation of shape and weight sustains the disorder.","additionalDifferentials":["Anorexia nervosa, binge-eating/purging type","Binge-eating disorder","Major depressive disorder","Borderline personality disorder with impulsive eating","Gastrointestinal causes of vomiting","Kleine-Levin syndrome and other causes of hyperphagia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electrolytes, particularly potassium, with bicarbonate and chloride to detect metabolic alkalosis","ECG where electrolyte disturbance or arrhythmia is suspected","Renal function assessment","Dental examination for enamel erosion","Screening for comorbid depression, substance use, and self-harm"],"redFlags":["Severe hypokalaemia or other electrolyte disturbance with arrhythmia risk","Haematemesis or oesophageal tear (Mallory-Weiss or Boerhaave) from forceful vomiting","Suicidal ideation or self-harm","Marked dehydration and haemodynamic instability from purging"],"prognosis":"Bulimia nervosa often has a fluctuating course, but many patients improve substantially with cognitive behavioural therapy. Comorbid substance use, personality disorder, and persistent purging are associated with a more chronic course and greater medical risk.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal body weight excludes serious medical risk from purging","Missing dangerous hypokalaemia and its arrhythmic potential","Not asking directly about purging behaviours that patients tend to conceal","Overlooking comorbid depression and suicide risk","Failing to offer evidence-based cognitive behavioural therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"bullous_pemphigoid":{"name":"Bullous pemphigoid","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pemphigoid","BP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest autoimmune blistering disease, typically affecting older adults","Classically produces large, tense, fluid-filled blisters on an erythematous or urticarial base, favouring the trunk, flexures, and limbs","Blisters are tense and relatively robust because the split is subepidermal, unlike the fragile flaccid blisters of pemphigus","A prodromal phase of intense itch with urticarial or eczematous plaques may precede blistering by weeks or months and is easily misdiagnosed","Mucosal involvement is uncommon and usually mild, which helps distinguish it from pemphigus vulgaris","Diagnosis is confirmed by subepidermal blistering on histology and linear IgG and C3 deposition along the basement membrane zone on direct immunofluorescence","Management uses potent topical corticosteroids for localised disease and systemic corticosteroids or steroid-sparing immunomodulators for widespread involvement","Consider a drug trigger, since certain medications (including some diabetes and immunotherapy agents) can precipitate pemphigoid"],"pathophysiology":"Bullous pemphigoid is caused by autoantibodies directed against hemidesmosomal proteins of the dermal-epidermal junction, principally BP180 (collagen XVII) and BP230. Antibody binding activates complement and recruits inflammatory cells, particularly eosinophils, which release proteases that degrade the anchoring structures holding the epidermis to the dermis. This produces a subepidermal split, giving tense blisters with an intact epidermal roof. The urticarial prodrome reflects the inflammatory phase before frank blistering. Disease predominates in the elderly and may be associated with neurological disease and, in some cases, drug exposure.","additionalDifferentials":["Pemphigus vulgaris (flaccid blisters, mucosal erosions, intraepidermal split)","Dermatitis herpetiformis","Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita","Bullous drug eruption","Bullous impetigo","Urticaria or eczema during the non-bullous prodromal phase"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lesional skin biopsy showing a subepidermal blister with eosinophils","Perilesional direct immunofluorescence showing linear IgG and C3 at the basement membrane zone","Serum indirect immunofluorescence and ELISA for BP180 and BP230 antibodies","Salt-split skin studies to localise antibody binding when distinguishing from related disorders","Review of medication history for possible drug-induced pemphigoid"],"redFlags":["Extensive blistering with widespread skin barrier loss and secondary infection risk","Signs of sepsis or systemic deterioration, particularly in frail elderly patients","Complications of systemic corticosteroid or immunosuppressive therapy","Rapidly progressive disease unresponsive to initial treatment"],"prognosis":"Bullous pemphigoid is often a self-limiting disease over months to a few years and generally responds to treatment, but morbidity and mortality are notable in elderly patients, largely driven by comorbidity and treatment-related complications.","pitfalls":["Missing the non-bullous urticarial or eczematous prodrome and delaying diagnosis","Confusing it with pemphigus vulgaris and misjudging prognosis and mucosal risk","Over-reliance on high-dose systemic steroids in frail patients when potent topical steroids may suffice","Failing to identify a drug trigger that could be withdrawn"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"burn_wound_management":{"name":"Burn wound management","system":"Plastic / Reconstructive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thermal burn","burn injury","acute burn care"],"chapter":null,"source":"Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery / standard references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Burn depth is classified as superficial (epidermal), superficial and deep partial thickness (dermal), and full thickness, with depth guiding whether a wound will heal spontaneously or require excision and grafting","Extent of burn is estimated using the rule of nines, Lund-Browder charts, or the patient's palm as roughly one percent of body surface area, and only partial and full thickness areas are counted for resuscitation","Fluid resuscitation for major burns is guided by a weight and burn-area formula (Parkland formula) with crystalloid titrated to urine output rather than to a fixed volume","Deep circumferential burns of a limb or the torso can produce a tourniquet or restrictive effect, and escharotomy may be needed to restore perfusion or ventilation","Early wound care emphasises cleaning, appropriate dressings, and preventing infection, while deep dermal and full thickness wounds benefit from early tangential excision and skin grafting","Inhalation injury, facial burns, singed nasal hair, soot in the airway, and hoarseness signal potential airway compromise that can progress rapidly to obstruction","Adequate analgesia, tetanus prophylaxis, nutritional support, and prevention of hypothermia are integral to burn care","Referral to a specialist burn centre is indicated for large burns, burns of the face, hands, feet, perineum or major joints, electrical and chemical burns, and burns with inhalation injury"],"pathophysiology":"A burn causes coagulative tissue necrosis whose severity depends on temperature and contact time. Jackson described three zones: a central zone of coagulation (irreversible necrosis), a surrounding zone of stasis with compromised perfusion that can be salvaged or progress to necrosis, and an outer zone of hyperaemia. Large burns trigger a systemic inflammatory response with release of mediators that increase capillary permeability, causing fluid shift from the intravascular space into the interstitium and burn oedema. This produces hypovolaemia (burn shock) and, if inadequately treated, organ hypoperfusion. Loss of the skin barrier impairs thermoregulation and fluid balance and predisposes to infection and hypermetabolism.","additionalDifferentials":["Toxic epidermal necrolysis and Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","Necrotising soft tissue infection","Chemical injury (acid or alkali)","Cold injury (frostbite) mimicking burn appearance","Non-accidental injury in children with suspicious patterns"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assessment of burn depth and total body surface area using Lund-Browder chart","Carboxyhaemoglobin level and arterial blood gases when inhalation injury or carbon monoxide exposure is suspected","Bronchoscopy to assess airway and inhalation injury","Baseline and serial electrolytes, renal function, and haematocrit to guide resuscitation","ECG and cardiac monitoring after high-voltage electrical injury","Creatine kinase and urine myoglobin when deep or electrical injury raises concern for rhabdomyolysis"],"redFlags":["Signs of inhalation injury or impending airway obstruction (facial burns, stridor, hoarseness, soot in the airway) requiring early intubation","Large burns causing burn shock and needing prompt fluid resuscitation and burn centre transfer","Circumferential deep burns of a limb or the chest threatening perfusion or ventilation and requiring escharotomy","High-voltage electrical injury with cardiac arrhythmia or rhabdomyolysis","Features of burn wound sepsis or necrotising infection"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on burn size and depth, age, inhalation injury, and comorbidity. Superficial and superficial partial thickness burns generally heal without surgery and with minimal scarring, whereas deep dermal and full thickness burns require grafting and are prone to hypertrophic scarring and contracture. Timely resuscitation, early excision, infection control, and rehabilitation markedly improve survival and functional recovery.","pitfalls":["Overestimating or underestimating burn size, leading to inappropriate fluid resuscitation and either oedema or under-resuscitation","Counting superficial erythema when calculating burn area for resuscitation","Delaying recognition of inhalation injury until airway swelling makes intubation difficult","Failing to reassess deep dermal burns, which may convert to full thickness and need grafting","Neglecting hypothermia, analgesia, and nutrition during the acute phase","Missing the need for escharotomy in circumferential deep burns"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"candidemia":{"name":"Candidemia","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["candidaemia","Candida bloodstream infection","invasive candidiasis","candidal sepsis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / IDSA","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The presence of Candida species in the bloodstream and a leading cause of healthcare-associated bloodstream infection in critically ill and immunocompromised patients","Major risk factors include central venous catheters, broad-spectrum antibiotics, total parenteral nutrition, abdominal surgery, neutropenia, and prolonged ICU stay","Candida albicans remains common, but non-albicans species with variable azole susceptibility, including the multidrug-resistant Candida auris, are increasingly important","Any positive blood culture for Candida should be regarded as significant and never dismissed as a contaminant","Echinocandins are generally favoured as initial therapy in unstable or seriously ill patients, with de-escalation to an azole guided by species and susceptibility","Prompt removal or exchange of infected intravascular catheters is a key part of source control","Dilated fundoscopic examination is advised to detect ocular involvement (chorioretinitis or endophthalmitis) that can affect treatment duration","Follow-up blood cultures to confirm clearance, and evaluation for endocarditis or deep-seated foci in persistent candidemia, are recommended"],"pathophysiology":"Candida species are common commensals of the gastrointestinal tract, skin, and mucosa. Disruption of these barriers, by mucosal breakdown, surgery, or indwelling devices, together with impaired host defences from neutropenia, immunosuppression, or broad-spectrum antibiotics that eliminate competing bacterial flora, allows Candida to translocate into the bloodstream or seed from a catheter biofilm. Once in the circulation, the organism can disseminate haematogenously to the eyes, heart valves, bones, and deep organs, producing metastatic foci that are difficult to eradicate without prolonged therapy and source control.","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial bloodstream infection or sepsis","Catheter-related bacterial bloodstream infection","Disseminated mould infection in the neutropenic host","Non-infective causes of persistent fever in the critically ill","Contaminant growth in blood cultures (rare with Candida and should not be assumed)"],"infectionMimics":["Drug fever or transfusion reaction in a critically ill patient","Bacterial sepsis with negative fungal cultures","Malignancy-associated fever","Non-infective systemic inflammatory response"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cultures with species identification and antifungal susceptibility testing","Repeat blood cultures to document clearance and define duration of therapy","Dilated ophthalmological examination for chorioretinitis or endophthalmitis","Echocardiography and imaging for deep-seated foci when candidemia is persistent","Culture of the catheter tip after removal and assessment of other potential sources"],"redFlags":["Septic shock or haemodynamic instability from candidemia requiring urgent resuscitation and source control","Persistent positive cultures despite therapy suggesting endocarditis, a retained infected catheter, or another deep focus","Ocular involvement threatening vision","Isolation of Candida auris or other resistant species with implications for treatment and infection control"],"prognosis":"Candidemia carries high attributable mortality, especially with delayed effective antifungal therapy, failure to remove infected catheters, persistent fungaemia, or severe underlying illness; earlier appropriate treatment and source control improve survival.","pitfalls":["Dismissing a positive Candida blood culture as a contaminant","Delaying effective antifungal therapy while awaiting species identification","Leaving an infected central catheter in place","Omitting fundoscopy and follow-up cultures, missing ocular disease or persistent infection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","IDSA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"candidiasis":{"name":"Candidiasis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Candida infection","invasive candidiasis","candidemia"],"chapter":222,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1706; supporting text Chap. 34 p.269, Chap. 60 p.432-433, Chap. 79 p.578-621","clinicalPearls":["Candidiasis spans a spectrum: mucocutaneous disease (oral thrush, esophageal, vulvovaginal, intertriginous, paronychia/onycholysis) that is usually localized, versus rare systemic, life-threatening invasive disease such as candidemia and disseminated candidiasis (Chap. 60, p.432-433).","The usual causative organism is Candida albicans; these yeasts are normal saprophytic inhabitants of the gastrointestinal tract that overgrow and cause disease when host defenses are altered (Chap. 60, p.432-433).","Oral candidiasis (thrush) appears as white plaques on the tongue or buccal mucosa; angular fissured macerated lesions at the mouth corners (perleche) are seen with poorly fitting dentures and may be candidal (Chap. 60, p.432-433).","Intertriginous candidal lesions are characteristically edematous, erythematous, and scaly with scattered 'satellite pustules'; in males the penis, scrotum, and inner thighs are often involved (Chap. 60, p.432-433).","In contrast to dermatophyte infections, candidal infections are frequently painful and accompanied by a marked inflammatory response (Chap. 60, p.432-433).","Disseminated candidiasis (often due to Candida tropicalis) may present with a triad of fever, myalgias, and eruptive nodules; hematogenous cutaneous disease shows multiple raised papules with systemic signs (rash chapter, p.190; Chap. 79, p.621).","Candidemia and chronic disseminated candidiasis are recognized complications in neutropenic patients because Candida species are endogenous gastrointestinal flora (Chap. 79, p.578).","Candidemia is a recognized cause of endogenous endophthalmitis from hematogenous seeding; Roth's spots and retinal hemorrhages were illustrated in a liver transplant patient with candidemia from immunosuppression (Chap. 34, p.269)."],"pathophysiology":"Candida species (most often C. albicans) are normal saprophytic commensals of the gastrointestinal tract and skin. They overgrow and become pathogenic when local or systemic host defenses are disrupted, e.g., by broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy, diabetes mellitus, or immunosuppression. Mucocutaneous disease results from overgrowth at moist, macerated surfaces; invasive disease arises when the organism gains bloodstream access, frequently via intravascular catheters or the gut in neutropenic hosts (endogenous flora), producing candidemia and hematogenous (disseminated) seeding to organs, skin, and the eye (Chap. 60, p.432-433; Chap. 79, p.578).","additionalDifferentials":["Dermatophytosis (tinea) on glabrous skin (Chap. 60, p.432)","Tinea (pityriasis) versicolor due to Malassezia furfur (Chap. 60, p.432)","Lichen planus / oral leukoplakia for oral white lesions","Other invasive fungal infections in neutropenia: invasive aspergillosis and other molds (Chap. 79, p.578)"],"infectionMimics":["Invasive aspergillosis and other molds (necrotic nodules) in immunocompromised hosts (Chap. 79, p.578; rash chapter)","Disseminated cryptococcosis (molluscum-like nodular lesions) (rash chapter)","Mucormycosis (rash chapter)","Bacterial/septic endophthalmitis, including from endocarditis (Roth's spots also in leukemia, diabetes) (Chap. 34, p.269)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Erythema nodosum (tender lower-extremity nodules) for nodular eruptions (rash chapter)","Sweet syndrome (acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis) (rash chapter)","Psoriasis / eczema in intertriginous areas"],"additionalInvestigations":["KOH preparation of skin/mucosal scrapings demonstrating yeast and pseudohyphae for mucocutaneous candidiasis (Chap. 60, p.433)","Culture of lesional material (Chap. 60, p.433)","Blood cultures for candidemia (note insensitivity; see pitfalls) (Chap. 34, p.269; Chap. 79)","Dilated funduscopic examination to detect candidal endophthalmitis in candidemia (Chap. 34, p.269)"],"redFlags":["Candidemia or suspected disseminated candidiasis in a neutropenic or immunosuppressed host (Chap. 79, p.578)","Visual loss, ocular pain, or floaters in a patient with candidemia or indwelling IV catheters (endophthalmitis) (Chap. 34, p.269)","Fever, myalgias, and eruptive cutaneous nodules suggesting disseminated candidiasis (rash chapter, p.190)","Presence of an indwelling intravascular catheter or central line in a patient with candidemia (Chap. 34, p.269; Chap. 79)"],"prognosis":"Mucocutaneous candidiasis is generally benign and responds to removal of predisposing factors plus topical or, when needed, systemic antifungal therapy. Systemic involvement is rare but life-threatening; candidemia and chronic disseminated candidiasis are serious complications in neutropenic and immunosuppressed patients (Chap. 60, p.432-433; Chap. 79, p.578).","pitfalls":["Systemic therapy is usually reserved for immunosuppressed patients or those with chronic/recurrent disease who fail topical therapy, NOT routine mucocutaneous cases (Chap. 60, p.433).","Oral nystatin is effective ONLY for candidiasis of the gastrointestinal tract, not for systemic disease (Chap. 60, p.433).","Failure to remove predisposing factors (broad-spectrum antibiotics, chronic moisture/maceration) leads to treatment failure (Chap. 60, p.433).","In endogenous endophthalmitis, visual loss may sometimes be the ONLY symptom even without prominent ocular pain or injection (Chap. 34, p.269)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames candidiasis along a clinical spectrum rather than a formal grading scale: localized mucocutaneous disease (oral, esophageal, vulvovaginal, intertriginous, cutaneous) versus rare systemic/invasive disease (candidemia, chronic disseminated/hepatosplenic candidiasis). Invasive disease risk rises with prolonged profound neutropenia (ANC <500/uL) (Chap. 60, p.432-433; Chap. 79, p.578).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1706 (Chap. 222, Candidiasis)","Harrison 22e p.432-433 (Chap. 60, mucocutaneous candidiasis)","Harrison 22e p.269 (Chap. 34, endophthalmitis / candidemia)","Harrison 22e p.578-621 (Chap. 79, infections in cancer patients)","Harrison 22e p.190 (nodular eruptions in immunocompromised hosts)"],"crossLinks":["FEBRILE_NEUTROPENIA","DEVICE_INFECTION"]},"cannabis_and_cannabis_use_disorder":{"name":"Cannabis and Cannabis Use Disorder","system":"Neuropsychiatric / Substance Use","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Marijuana use disorder","CUD","Cannabis dependence","Marijuana abuse"],"chapter":466,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3686","clinicalPearls":["Cannabis is among the most widely used psychoactive substances; surveys cited estimate tens of millions of monthly users in the U.S., including a substantial number of adolescents and young adults aged 12-25.","Marijuana is legally distinguished from hemp by Delta-9-THC content: material exceeding 0.3% Delta-9-THC by dry weight is marijuana, while 0.3% or less is hemp; the principal euphoric and medicinal effects are mediated by Delta-9-THC acting on type 1 cannabinoid receptors (CB1R).","Product potency has risen sharply: flower can contain 20-30% Delta-9-THC, vape concentrates are advertised up to ~75%, and solid extracts ('shatter') up to ~95%, though many high claims appear exaggerated; higher potency is linked to greater addiction and psychosis risk.","Inhalation (smoking, vaping, 'dabbing') gives rapid, highly bioavailable absorption with quick onset, conditions associated with increased addiction risk; edibles have slower, less predictable onset and carry a higher risk of accidental overdose, particularly in children and inexperienced users.","Following smoking, plasma Delta-9-THC peaks within 5-10 minutes, but a hysteresis exists because brain levels keep rising during the early distribution (alpha) phase (t1/2 ~6 min) even as plasma falls; subjective peak effects occur at 20-30 minutes and total effect lasts roughly 4-8 hours.","Urine drug testing detects the inactive metabolite 11-nor-carboxy-THC (11-COOH-THC), whose terminal half-life is ~20 to 35+ hours, so cannabis remains detectable for days in occasional users and weeks in frequent users with saturated fat stores.","Regular use produces tolerance, so a given plasma Delta-9-THC level does not reliably predict impairment, which has hampered regulation of cannabis-impaired driving; Delta-9-THC nonetheless impairs judgment, motor coordination, and reaction time, increasing crash risk.","Cannabis use disorder (CUD) is a DSM-5 diagnosis defined by a problematic pattern of use causing clinically significant impairment or distress; criteria include craving, tolerance, a withdrawal syndrome, and failure to meet role obligations from recurrent use and drug seeking.","The withdrawal syndrome peaks within 1-3 days of stopping and manifests as anxiety, restlessness, insomnia, depression, and reduced appetite; most symptoms resolve within about 2 weeks, but insomnia often persists longer and may drive relapse.","Chronic high-dose use can cause cannabis hyperemesis syndrome, presenting as recurrent cycles of severe nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain, and is a growing reason for ED visits and hospitalization.","High-potency cannabis (>10% THC) can trigger acute psychotic episodes even on first exposure; these are usually transient but may become chronic with continued use and can trigger or worsen schizophrenia in vulnerable individuals.","Synthetic Delta-9-THC (dronabinol) is approved for chemotherapy-induced nausea and appetite stimulation, and a Delta-9-THC:CBD formulation is approved in some countries for chronic pain and MS-related spasticity (efficacy in MS largely based on patient report); there are currently no FDA-approved medications specifically for CUD."],"pathophysiology":"Delta-9-THC is mainly a partial agonist at G protein-coupled cannabinoid receptors CB1R and CB2R, with euphorigenic effects mediated through CB1Rs on excitatory glutamatergic and inhibitory GABAergic interneurons and glial cells in brain regions processing stress, mood, and reward. These receptors are effectors of the endocannabinoid system (ECS), physiologically driven by endogenous ligands 2-arachidonoylglycerol (2-AG, a full agonist) and anandamide (a partial agonist) that are synthesized and degraded on demand to give temporally and regionally precise modulation. Exogenous cannabinoids, defined by dose and duration rather than this 'just-in-time' synaptic signaling, disrupt ECS neuroregulation. Reward is thought to be mediated by modulation of glutamatergic and GABAergic activity in the ventral tegmental area, from which dopaminergic neurons project to the nucleus accumbens where rewarding experiences are reinforced; anxiolytic effects are mediated via the amygdala. Regular use is associated with broad but reversible reduction in cannabinoid receptor density (recovering by ~28 days of abstinence). Perturbation of ECS signaling during early fetal development can affect neuronal development, migration, and connectivity.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary psychotic disorder (schizophrenia) versus cannabis-induced psychosis","Other substance use disorders (alcohol, nicotine, opioids, stimulants) often co-occurring","Primary anxiety, depressive, or panic disorder versus cannabis-induced symptoms","Cyclic vomiting syndrome versus cannabis hyperemesis syndrome","Other causes of impaired cognition, low IQ, or poor school performance in adolescents"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cyclic vomiting syndrome (mimics cannabis hyperemesis syndrome)","Primary schizophrenia or other psychotic disorder (mimics cannabis-induced psychosis)","Primary anxiety/panic disorder (mimics acute high-dose cannabis intoxication)","EVALI (e-cigarette/vaping product use-associated lung injury) linked to vitamin E acetate diluent in black-market THC e-liquids"],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine immunoassay/confirmatory testing for 11-nor-carboxy-THC (11-COOH-THC), the inactive long-lived metabolite","DSM-5 criteria-based clinical assessment for cannabis use disorder severity","Screening for comorbid substance use (alcohol, nicotine, opioids, stimulants) and for psychiatric comorbidity (psychosis, depression, suicidality)","In pregnancy: assessment of fetal growth given association with lower birth weight and preterm delivery"],"redFlags":["Acute psychosis, severe anxiety, paranoia, or panic after high-potency or high-dose exposure","Tachycardia with acute cannabis toxicity, especially in children or naive users who accidentally ingest edibles","Cannabis hyperemesis syndrome with intractable vomiting, dehydration, and abdominal pain","Depression and suicidality, particularly with adolescent onset of use","Respiratory symptoms suggesting EVALI in vaping users","Use in pregnancy given fetal growth and neurodevelopmental concerns"],"prognosis":"Most acute psychotic episodes are transient, but regular use can render them chronic, and cannabis may trigger or worsen schizophrenia in vulnerable individuals; adolescent and higher-frequency, higher-potency use carries the strongest associations with later psychotic disorder. Receptor downregulation is largely reversible, recovering toward control levels within about 28 days of abstinence. Withdrawal generally resolves within roughly 2 weeks, although insomnia may persist and promote relapse. Earlier age of initiation, higher use frequency, and higher-THC products increase the risk of developing CUD and of progression to other substance use disorders. Adolescent use has been associated with lower grades, lower IQ, and higher school dropout, though causation is not established.","pitfalls":["Assuming a plasma Delta-9-THC level reliably reflects impairment; tolerance breaks this correlation and complicates impaired-driving assessment.","Underestimating edible dosing: delayed and variable onset (amplified by fatty meals increasing bioavailability 200-400%) leads naive users to redose and overdose.","Overstating the clinical role of minor cannabinoids (e.g., CBD) or terpenes at the concentrations found in marijuana, where evidence of relevance is limited and largely unproven.","Trusting product potency labels, which testing has shown to be frequently inflated.","Perceiving intoxicating hemp-derived products (e.g., Delta-8-THC and others) as safe or 'legal'; some such products can be considerably more potent than Delta-9-THC.","Overlooking EVALI risk historically tied to vitamin E acetate diluent rather than cannabis itself."],"severityClassification":"Cannabis use disorder is diagnosed and graded by DSM-5 criteria (problematic use pattern causing clinically significant impairment or distress), with features including craving, tolerance, a withdrawal syndrome, and failure to fulfill role obligations from recurrent use and drug seeking.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3686","Harrison 22e p.3687","Harrison 22e p.3688"],"crossLinks":[]},"cannabis_use_disorder":{"name":"Cannabis use disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["marijuana use disorder","cannabis dependence","CUD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The disorder is a problematic pattern of cannabis use leading to clinically significant impairment or distress, graded by severity according to features such as impaired control, craving, tolerance, and continued use despite harm","Intoxication features include conjunctival injection, increased appetite, dry mouth, tachycardia, and impaired coordination, judgement, and short-term memory","A recognised withdrawal syndrome occurs, with irritability, anxiety, sleep disturbance, vivid dreams, reduced appetite, and restlessness that can drive continued use","Heavy or early-onset use is associated with increased risk of psychotic disorders, particularly in genetically vulnerable individuals, and can worsen anxiety and mood","High-potency products and concentrates increase the likelihood of adverse effects and of developing a use disorder","Management is predominantly psychosocial, including motivational interviewing, cognitive behavioural therapy, and contingency management, as no specific pharmacotherapy is firmly established","Cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome, with cyclical vomiting relieved by hot bathing, is an increasingly recognised complication of chronic heavy use","Assess and address comorbid mental illness, since cannabis is frequently used to self-medicate anxiety, insomnia, or low mood"],"pathophysiology":"Cannabis use disorder involves the endocannabinoid system, with delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol acting as a partial agonist at CB1 receptors and modulating dopaminergic reward signalling in the mesolimbic pathway. Chronic exposure produces receptor downregulation and neuroadaptation underlying tolerance and withdrawal, and heavy adolescent use during neurodevelopment may have lasting effects on cognition and psychosis risk, modulated by genetic and environmental vulnerability.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary anxiety or mood disorder","Primary psychotic disorder versus cannabis-induced psychosis","Other substance use disorders or intoxication","Cannabinoid hyperemesis versus other causes of cyclical vomiting","Cognitive impairment from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed substance use history including potency and route","Urine toxicology for cannabinoids and other substances","Screening for comorbid psychiatric disorders and psychosis risk","Assessment of withdrawal features where relevant","Evaluation for cannabinoid hyperemesis in patients with cyclical vomiting"],"redFlags":["Emergence of psychotic symptoms, particularly in adolescents or those with a family history","Suicidal ideation, especially with comorbid depression","Severe dehydration or electrolyte disturbance from cannabinoid hyperemesis","Escalating use with functional decline in education, work, or relationships"],"prognosis":"Cannabis use disorder often follows a chronic course, particularly with early-onset heavy use, but many people reduce or stop use with psychosocial treatment and maturation. Prognosis is better with later onset, lower potency use, strong social support, and treatment of comorbid disorders, and worse with adolescent onset, high-potency use, and untreated psychiatric comorbidity.","pitfalls":["Dismissing cannabis use as harmless and missing a genuine use disorder","Overlooking cannabis as a contributor to anxiety, low mood, or psychosis","Missing cannabinoid hyperemesis and attributing vomiting to other causes","Neglecting the recognised withdrawal syndrome that maintains use","Failing to address comorbid psychiatric illness driving self-medication"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"carbon_monoxide_poisoning":{"name":"Carbon monoxide poisoning","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CO poisoning","carbon monoxide toxicity","carboxyhaemoglobinaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Carbon monoxide binds haemoglobin with far greater affinity than oxygen, forming carboxyhaemoglobin and causing profound tissue hypoxia despite a normal arterial oxygen tension","Symptoms are non-specific (headache, nausea, dizziness, confusion) and are frequently misdiagnosed as viral illness or food poisoning, especially when several household members are affected","Standard pulse oximetry is misleading because it cannot distinguish carboxyhaemoglobin from oxyhaemoglobin; a co-oximeter or blood carboxyhaemoglobin level is required","The classic cherry-red skin appearance is uncommon and unreliable, and its absence does not exclude poisoning","The cornerstone of treatment is high-flow 100 percent oxygen, which greatly shortens the elimination half-life of carboxyhaemoglobin","Hyperbaric oxygen therapy is considered for severe poisoning (loss of consciousness, neurological signs, cardiac involvement, pregnancy, or very high levels) to reduce delayed neurological sequelae","Delayed neuropsychiatric syndrome (cognitive impairment, movement disorders, personality change) can develop days to weeks after apparent recovery","Suspect concurrent cyanide poisoning in victims of smoke inhalation from enclosed-space fires"],"pathophysiology":"Carbon monoxide is a colourless, odourless gas produced by incomplete combustion. It binds haemoglobin with roughly 200-250 times the affinity of oxygen, forming carboxyhaemoglobin, which both reduces oxygen-carrying capacity and shifts the oxyhaemoglobin dissociation curve leftward, impairing oxygen release to tissues. CO also binds myoglobin and inhibits mitochondrial cytochrome oxidase, causing cellular hypoxia and oxidative stress. The brain and heart, with high oxygen demand, are most vulnerable, and a lipid peroxidation and inflammatory cascade underlies the delayed neurological injury.","additionalDifferentials":["Viral illness or gastroenteritis","Migraine or tension headache","Cyanide poisoning (smoke inhalation)","Diabetic or other metabolic coma","Acute coronary syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood carboxyhaemoglobin level by co-oximetry (venous or arterial)","Arterial blood gas with metabolic acidosis and lactate","ECG and cardiac biomarkers to detect myocardial ischaemia","Neuroimaging in severe cases (basal ganglia changes)","Pregnancy test in women of childbearing age given fetal risk"],"redFlags":["Any loss of consciousness, seizure, or focal neurological deficit","Chest pain, arrhythmia, or ECG evidence of ischaemia","Metabolic acidosis with elevated lactate indicating severe tissue hypoxia","Pregnancy, as the fetus is disproportionately affected","Multiple people from the same environment falling ill simultaneously"],"prognosis":"Mild poisoning usually resolves fully with oxygen, but severe exposure can cause death or lasting neurological and cardiac damage; delayed neuropsychiatric sequelae may appear after seemingly complete recovery, and early high-flow or hyperbaric oxygen reduces this risk.","pitfalls":["Trusting a normal pulse oximeter reading and normal arterial oxygen tension to exclude poisoning","Attributing symptoms to a viral illness and discharging the patient back to the source","Relying on the cherry-red sign, which is usually absent","Overlooking coexisting cyanide toxicity in fire victims"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"carcinoid_heart":{"name":"Carcinoid heart disease","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Hedinger syndrome","carcinoid syndrome heart disease","carcinoid valvular disease","carcinoid cardiac involvement"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Carcinoid heart disease is a complication of the carcinoid syndrome caused by vasoactive substances, particularly serotonin, released by metastatic neuroendocrine tumours","It characteristically affects the right-sided heart valves, producing tricuspid regurgitation and pulmonary stenosis or regurgitation with fibrous plaque-like thickening of the endocardium","Left-sided valve involvement is uncommon because the lungs inactivate circulating serotonin, but it can occur with a patent foramen ovale or bronchial carcinoid","It usually develops in patients with liver metastases, since hepatic clearance of tumour products is then bypassed","Right heart failure with peripheral oedema, ascites, and elevated venous pressure is the typical late presentation","Urinary 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid and echocardiography are central to diagnosis, and elevated NT-proBNP supports cardiac involvement","Somatostatin analogues control syndrome symptoms and tumour secretion, while significant valve disease may require surgical valve replacement"],"pathophysiology":"Metastatic neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumours, typically of midgut origin with hepatic metastases, secrete serotonin and other vasoactive mediators into the systemic circulation. These substances stimulate fibroblast proliferation and deposition of fibrous tissue on the endocardium and valve leaflets, predominantly on the right side of the heart. The resulting plaque-like fibrosis retracts and immobilises the tricuspid and pulmonary valves, causing regurgitation and stenosis that lead to volume overload and right ventricular failure. Pulmonary inactivation of serotonin largely protects the left heart.","additionalDifferentials":["Rheumatic valvular heart disease","Right heart failure from pulmonary hypertension or cor pulmonale","Constrictive pericarditis or restrictive cardiomyopathy","Ebstein anomaly or other congenital tricuspid disease","Drug-induced valvulopathy (for example from serotonergic agents)","Infective endocarditis of right-sided valves"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography to characterise valve involvement and right ventricular function","24-hour urinary 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid","Serum chromogranin A as a neuroendocrine tumour marker","NT-proBNP as a marker of cardiac involvement and severity","Cross-sectional and functional imaging (including somatostatin receptor imaging) to localise and stage the tumour"],"redFlags":["Progressive right heart failure with ascites and oedema","Carcinoid crisis with flushing, haemodynamic instability, and bronchospasm, especially around procedures","Severe symptomatic valve dysfunction requiring surgical evaluation","Rapidly rising cardiac biomarkers indicating advancing cardiac disease"],"prognosis":"Cardiac involvement is a major determinant of survival in carcinoid syndrome and historically indicated advanced disease with a poor outlook, but somatostatin analogue therapy, tumour-directed treatment, and timely valve surgery have improved outcomes.","pitfalls":["Attributing right heart failure to more common causes and missing an underlying neuroendocrine tumour","Not screening for cardiac involvement in patients with established carcinoid syndrome","Failing to prevent and treat carcinoid crisis around surgery and interventions","Delaying valve surgery until right ventricular function is irreversibly impaired"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"carcinoma_of_unknown_primary":{"name":"Carcinoma of Unknown Primary","system":"Oncology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CUP","Cancer of Unknown Primary","Occult primary malignancy","Metastatic carcinoma of unknown origin"],"chapter":97,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.731","clinicalPearls":["CUP is a biopsy-proven malignancy whose anatomic site of origin stays unidentified despite a standardized, detailed diagnostic workup; it ranks among the 10 most frequently diagnosed cancers worldwide and accounts for roughly 3-5% of all malignancies (Harrison 22e p.731).","Most authorities restrict the CUP label to epithelial or undifferentiated cancers and exclude lymphoma, metastatic melanoma, and metastatic sarcoma, since those have distinct histology and stage-based management even without an identified primary (Harrison 22e p.731).","On light microscopy about 60-65% of CUP is adenocarcinoma and roughly 5% is squamous cell carcinoma, with the remaining 30-35% being poorly differentiated adenocarcinoma/carcinoma or poorly differentiated neoplasm; neuroendocrine tumors make up about 2% (Harrison 22e p.732).","Most serum tumor markers (CEA, CA-125, CA 19-9, CA 15-3) are nonspecific and do not localize the primary; in contrast, PSA tissue/serum staining and thyroglobulin are among the most specific markers but have low yield because prostate and thyroid cancers rarely present as CUP (Harrison 22e pp.731-732).","CK7 and CK20 cytokeratin patterns help narrow the putative primary: CK7 marks lung, ovary, endometrium, breast, and upper GI/pancreaticobiliary tumors, while CK20 is expressed in GI epithelium, urothelium, and Merkel cells (Harrison 22e p.733).","TTF-1 nuclear staining is positive in about 68% of lung adenocarcinomas and roughly 25% of squamous lung cancers and helps distinguish a lung primary from metastatic adenocarcinoma in pleural, mediastinal, or parenchymal sites (Harrison 22e p.733).","Comprehensive genomic profiling shows about 85% of CUP samples carry at least one clinically relevant genomic alteration, averaging about 4.2 alterations per tumor; the most common are TP53 (about 55%), KRAS (about 20%), CDKN2A (about 19%), and ARID1A (about 11%) (Harrison 22e pp.733-734).","Tissue-agnostic biomarkers matter in a minority of CUP cases: MSI-high / mismatch-repair-deficient tumors and high tumor mutational burden tumors, as well as NTRK fusion-positive tumors, identify candidates for established histology-agnostic therapy (Harrison 22e p.734).","Adequate tissue acquisition (preferably excisional or core needle biopsy rather than fine-needle aspiration alone) is critical, both for histology/IHC and for molecular profiling that can suggest a primary and actionable biomarkers (Harrison 22e p.732).","Liver is a common site of CUP presentation, so intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma is frequently misdiagnosed as CUP; cholangiocarcinoma incidence is rising while CUP incidence is declining, partly due to improved molecular diagnostics and clinician awareness (Harrison 22e p.731).","DNA methylation profiling predicted a primary in about 87% of 216 CUP patients in one study, and tissue-of-origin classifier assays suggest a plausible primary in roughly 70-80% of patients, though accuracy cannot be fully verified because the true primary is by definition unconfirmed (Harrison 22e pp.733-734).","Latent primary cancers (a new primary emerging after months to years) are uncommon with modern imaging, occurring in 5% or fewer of CUP patients, typically in indolent presentations or highly treatment-responsive disease (Harrison 22e p.732)."],"pathophysiology":"The reasons cancers present without an identifiable primary remain unclear. One hypothesis is that the primary tumor regresses after seeding metastases or stays too small to detect, possibly contained or eliminated by host defenses including the immune system. Alternatively, CUP may reflect a distinct malignant event producing increased metastatic spread or survival relative to the primary; whether CUP metastases represent a genetically and phenotypically unique clone is unresolved. Searches for a unique CUP signature have been unrevealing: aneuploidy occurs in about 70% of metastatic adenocarcinoma/undifferentiated CUP, and overexpression of RAS, BCL2 (about 40%), HER2 (about 11%), and P53 (about 26-53%) is seen but is shared with many other malignancies. Adenocarcinoma CUP is more often driven by RTK/Ras/MAPK pathway alterations than non-adenocarcinoma CUP. No distinct molecular signature has been established (Harrison 22e pp.731-734).","additionalDifferentials":["Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma (commonly misdiagnosed as liver-presenting CUP) (Harrison 22e p.731)","Extragonadal germ cell tumor in men with poorly/undifferentiated midline adenopathy (Harrison 22e pp.732, 735)","Occult breast carcinoma in women with isolated axillary adenopathy (Harrison 22e pp.732, 735)","Primary peritoneal papillary serous carcinoma versus colon or pancreaticobiliary cancer in women with peritoneal carcinomatosis (Harrison 22e p.735)","Prostate carcinoma in men with blastic bone-only metastases and elevated PSA (Harrison 22e p.735)","Lung or head-and-neck primary in older smokers presenting with mediastinal adenopathy (Harrison 22e p.735)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lymphoma, metastatic melanoma, and metastatic sarcoma (excluded from CUP because they have specific histology/staging) (Harrison 22e p.731)","Peritoneal mesothelioma mislabeled as peritoneal carcinomatosis (calretinin/D2-40 positive; BerEp4/MOC-31 negative) (Harrison 22e p.735)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast CT of chest, abdomen, and pelvis is the standard baseline workup in all CUP patients absent contraindication (Harrison 22e p.732)","Thorough history (risk factors, prior surgeries, family history) and physical exam including digital rectal exam in men and breast/pelvic exam in women (Harrison 22e p.731)","Mammography (and breast MRI if mammogram/ultrasound negative) in women with metastatic adenocarcinoma, especially isolated axillary adenopathy (Harrison 22e p.732)","For cervical squamous CUP: CT/MRI plus laryngoscopy, bronchoscopy, upper endoscopy, and consideration of staging tonsillectomy (Harrison 22e p.732)","18-FDG-PET, most useful in squamous cervical CUP (identifies a head/neck primary in about 21-30%) and in solitary metastasis or bone-metastasis candidates for aggressive multimodality therapy (Harrison 22e p.732)","Serum PSA in men with adenocarcinoma and osteoblastic metastasis; beta-hCG and AFP for suspected extragonadal germ cell tumor (Harrison 22e pp.731-732)","Detailed pathology with hematoxylin-eosin and a tiered IHC panel (e.g., CK7/CK20, TTF-1, CDX-2, GCDFP-15/GATA3, PSA, thyroglobulin) plus molecular/tissue-of-origin profiling and next-generation sequencing (Harrison 22e pp.732-734)","Endoscopy/colonoscopy/bronchoscopy reserved for symptomatic patients or those with findings suggesting high diagnostic yield (Harrison 22e p.732)"],"redFlags":["Liver, brain, or adrenal metastatic involvement, which carries a poor prognosis (Harrison 22e p.735)","Disseminated disease with two or more involved sites (Harrison 22e pp.734-735)","Poor performance status and elevated serum LDH, which are adverse prognostic factors (Harrison 22e p.734)","Peritoneal carcinomatosis from metastatic adenocarcinoma, raising concern for gastric, appendiceal, colon, or pancreaticobiliary primaries (Harrison 22e p.735)"],"prognosis":"Median survival for most patients with disseminated CUP is about 6-10 months, and the prognosis for advanced disease remains poor with early resistance to available cytotoxic therapy. Prognosis is heterogeneous: favorable subsets (e.g., women with isolated axillary adenopathy treated as breast cancer, women with primary peritoneal serous carcinoma treated as ovarian cancer, men with midline poorly differentiated carcinoma resembling germ cell tumor, squamous cervical adenopathy, solitary metastatic sites, low-grade neuroendocrine tumors, and men with blastic bone metastases and elevated PSA) can achieve prolonged survival and occasionally cure. Recognized prognostic factors include performance status, site and number of metastases, response to therapy, and serum LDH; prognostic models (Culine using performance status and LDH; Raghav nomogram using gender, ECOG performance status, histology, number of metastatic sites, and neutrophil-lymphocyte ratio) stratify outcomes (Harrison 22e pp.734-735).","pitfalls":["No IHC stain is 100% sensitive or specific; under- or overinterpretation should be avoided, and poor differentiation reduces sensitivity even of hallmark markers (Harrison 22e p.732).","CDX-2 positivity, while used for GI adenocarcinomas, can also occur in tumors with enteric/mucinous differentiation from other sites such as mucinous ovarian cancer (Harrison 22e p.733).","Tissue antigenicity and IHC interpretation are affected by antigen retrieval, fixation/processing, observer variability, antibody performance, and tissue heterogeneity or inadequacy from small biopsies (Harrison 22e p.733).","Pathologic features should not always override clinical or radiologic findings when deciding to test for therapeutic biomarkers such as EGFR, ALK, or HER2 (Harrison 22e p.733).","More IHC stains are not necessarily better; staining must be interpreted alongside clinical presentation and imaging, with close clinician-pathologist communication (Harrison 22e p.732).","Not all peritoneal carcinomatosis in women is primary peritoneal serous carcinoma; careful pathology can reveal a colon or pancreaticobiliary profile or mislabeled mesothelioma (Harrison 22e p.735).","Two randomized trials of gene-expression-directed site-specific therapy versus empiric chemotherapy failed to show a significant survival benefit, so molecular classifier results must be interpreted cautiously (Harrison 22e pp.733-734)."],"severityClassification":"No single formal staging system; risk stratification distinguishes favorable prognostic subsets (treatable as a specific putative primary, with potential for prolonged survival/cure) from unfavorable disseminated disease. Prognostic models include the Culine model (performance status plus serum LDH, dividing patients into two outcome subgroups) and the Raghav nomogram (gender, ECOG performance status, histology, number of metastatic sites, and neutrophil-lymphocyte ratio) (Harrison 22e pp.734-735).","references":["Harrison 22e p.731","Harrison 22e p.732","Harrison 22e p.733","Harrison 22e p.734","Harrison 22e p.735"],"crossLinks":[]},"cardiac_arrest_management":{"name":"Cardiac arrest management","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cardiopulmonary resuscitation","CPR","advanced life support","ALS"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Survival depends on early recognition, high-quality chest compressions, early defibrillation of shockable rhythms, and prompt treatment of reversible causes","Prioritise minimally interrupted, good-quality compressions of adequate depth and rate, allowing full chest recoil","Rapidly determine whether the rhythm is shockable (ventricular fibrillation or pulseless ventricular tachycardia) or non-shockable (asystole or pulseless electrical activity), as this directs the algorithm","Defibrillate shockable rhythms without delay and resume compressions immediately after each shock","Systematically seek and correct the reversible causes, remembered as the four Hs and four Ts","Effective teamwork, clear leadership, and minimising pauses around rhythm checks and shocks improve outcomes","After return of spontaneous circulation, focus on post-resuscitation care: oxygenation and ventilation targets, haemodynamic support, and identifying the underlying cause","In traumatic arrest, address the specific reversible causes such as hypovolaemia, tension pneumothorax, and tamponade rather than relying on standard medical algorithms alone"],"pathophysiology":"Cardiac arrest is the sudden cessation of effective cardiac output. Shockable rhythms arise from disorganised (ventricular fibrillation) or excessively rapid (pulseless ventricular tachycardia) electrical activity that prevents coordinated contraction, most often on a background of ischaemic heart disease. Non-shockable arrest reflects either absent electrical activity (asystole) or organised electrical activity without a pulse (pulseless electrical activity), commonly due to a profound reversible insult. Regardless of mechanism, cessation of circulation causes global ischaemia; the brain tolerates only minutes before injury begins, so restoration of perfusion by chest compressions and treatment of the cause is time-critical.","additionalDifferentials":["Reversible causes: hypoxia, hypovolaemia, hypo/hyperkalaemia and metabolic disorders, hypothermia","Reversible causes: tension pneumothorax, tamponade, toxins, thrombosis (coronary or pulmonary)","Profound bradyarrhythmia or heart block preceding arrest","Near-arrest states (severe shock) mimicking pulselessness","Traumatic cardiac arrest with mechanism-specific causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous rhythm analysis and cardiac monitoring throughout resuscitation","Point-of-care blood gas, potassium, and glucose to identify reversible metabolic causes","Bedside ultrasound to assess for tamponade, hypovolaemia, and cardiac activity during rhythm checks","Waveform capnography to confirm airway placement and gauge resuscitation quality and return of circulation","Post-arrest 12-lead ECG and coronary assessment when a cardiac cause is suspected"],"redFlags":["A shockable rhythm requiring immediate defibrillation, where every minute of delay reduces survival","Failure to identify and treat a reversible cause (the four Hs and four Ts)","Prolonged interruptions in chest compressions degrading perfusion","Tension pneumothorax, tamponade, or major haemorrhage as an immediately treatable cause in traumatic arrest","Post-resuscitation hypoxia, hypotension, or hyperthermia worsening neurological outcome"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the initial rhythm, the promptness of high-quality resuscitation and defibrillation, and whether a reversible cause is corrected. Witnessed shockable arrests with early bystander CPR and defibrillation have the best prognosis, whereas unwitnessed asystolic arrest and prolonged downtime carry poor survival and higher risk of neurological injury.","pitfalls":["Interrupting chest compressions excessively for rhythm checks, intubation, or vascular access","Delaying defibrillation of a shockable rhythm","Focusing on drugs and airway while neglecting the search for reversible causes","Applying standard medical algorithms to traumatic arrest without treating haemorrhage, tension pneumothorax, and tamponade","Neglecting structured post-resuscitation care after return of spontaneous circulation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"cardiac_transplantation_and_prolonged_assi":{"name":"Cardiac Transplantation and Prolonged Assisted Circulation","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Heart transplantation","Orthotopic heart transplant","Left ventricular assist device therapy","LVAS","LVAD","Mechanical circulatory support","Total artificial heart"],"chapter":271,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2028","clinicalPearls":["Advanced heart failure is a distinct syndrome marked by refractoriness to conventional therapy, intolerance of guideline-dose neurohormonal antagonists, worsening renal function, poor cardiopulmonary reserve, and a low-output state with elevated pulmonary pressures; therapeutic goals shift toward surgical support of the failing heart (Harrison 22e p.2028).","Transplant candidacy generally requires refractory heart failure with a peak VO2 below ~14 mL/kg per min (below 12 if on beta blockers) or percent-predicted VO2 under 50%, combined with intolerance of disease-modifying therapy, cardiorenal syndrome, inotrope dependence, or need for an LVAS (Harrison 22e p.2028).","Age is not an absolute contraindication, but frailty is a relative one; BMI above 35 should prompt weight loss, eGFR below 30 mL/min/1.73 m2 is a relative contraindication possibly requiring combined kidney transplant, and tobacco use in any form must cease for at least 6 months (Harrison 22e p.2028).","The transplanted heart is surgically denervated and unresponsive to sympathetic or parasympathetic stimuli; early postoperatively high-dose catecholamines are needed, and allograft bradycardia does NOT respond to atropine, so isoproterenol or temporary pacing is used (Harrison 22e p.2029).","Donor ischemia (out-of-body) time is typically limited to about 4 hours unless novel ex situ normothermic perfusion transport techniques are used; allocation considers urgency, waiting-list time, distance, and ABO blood group, while HLA matching is usually impractical given time constraints (Harrison 22e p.2029).","Acute cellular rejection (ACR) is a T cell-mediated process with myocardial lymphocytic infiltrates that occurs early and declines after about 6 months, whereas antibody-mediated rejection (AMR) is a noncellular, donor-specific antibody-driven process with complement fixation, endothelial swelling, and arteriolar vasculitis that carries worse allograft survival and predisposes to vasculopathy (Harrison 22e p.2029-2030).","Cardiac allograft vasculopathy (CAV) is an accelerated, diffuse coronary intimal proliferation that is commonly clinically silent; intravascular ultrasound or optical coherence tomography is more sensitive than routine angiography for detection (Harrison 22e p.2030).","Angiographic CAV is present in roughly 30% of patients by 5 years and about 50% by 10 years after transplant; retransplantation is the only definitive therapy for advanced CAV (Harrison 22e p.2030).","Continuous-flow LVAS generate forward flow dependent on the pressure gradient between the LV cavity and aorta, so flow is critically sensitive to systemic blood pressure; because pulse is often undetectable, blood pressure is measured by Doppler (mean/opening pressure) and the target mean arterial pressure is at or below 90 mmHg (Harrison 22e p.2032).","LVAS-related shear stress can cause low-grade hemolysis and acquired von Willebrand disease (loss of high-molecular-weight vWF multimers), predisposing to gastrointestinal bleeding from intestinal arteriovenous malformations and to iron-deficiency anemia (Harrison 22e p.2032).","Lactate dehydrogenase is an excellent though nonspecific biomarker of hemolysis and can herald pump thrombosis (Harrison 22e p.2032).","Posttransplant lymphoproliferative disorders are usually Epstein-Barr virus driven and are the most frequent malignancy; solid cancers most often manifest as basal- and squamous-cell skin malignancies, so routine sunscreen is advised (Harrison 22e p.2030)."],"pathophysiology":"In advanced (stage D) heart failure the myocardium can no longer maintain adequate output despite maximal medical therapy, producing a low cardiac output state with elevated filling and pulmonary pressures, intolerance of neurohormonal antagonists, and progressive cardiorenal dysfunction. Cardiac transplantation replaces the failing heart with an allograft that is surgically denervated, so it depends initially on circulating catecholamines and cannot mount reflex heart-rate responses. Allograft rejection follows two mechanisms: ACR, a T cell-mediated lymphocytic assault on donor myocardium (perivenular in mild disease, diffuse interstitial with myocyte necrosis and hemorrhage in severe disease), and AMR, an antibody/complement-mediated injury with immunoglobulin deposition, endothelial swelling, interstitial edema, and arteriolar vasculitis. Over the long term, immunologic insults (brain death, ischemia-reperfusion, alloimmunity) combined with nonimmunologic metabolic factors (hypertension, hyperlipidemia, dysglycemia from chronic immunosuppression) drive cardiac allograft vasculopathy, a diffuse fibrotic intimal proliferation narrowing the coronary tree. For mechanical support, continuous-flow LVAS divert blood from the LV apex to the aorta via a pump whose output depends on the LV-to-aorta pressure gradient; the non-physiologic flow path imposes shear stress that degrades von Willebrand factor multimers and causes hemolysis. LV unloading lowers right-sided afterload, but increased device flow raises RV preload and the displaced septum reduces RV contractile efficiency, promoting RV dilatation and maladaptation.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of advanced/refractory heart failure warranting evaluation: restrictive and infiltrative cardiomyopathy (e.g., amyloidosis), congenital heart disease, and chronic-infection-associated cardiomyopathy (Chagas disease, viral hepatitis-related, tuberculosis)","Allograft dysfunction differential after transplant: acute cellular rejection vs antibody-mediated rejection vs cardiac allograft vasculopathy vs primary graft dysfunction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Surveillance endomyocardial biopsy to grade and monitor rejection","Immunofluorescence for immunoglobulin deposition and complement fixation, plus circulating donor-specific antibody testing, when AMR is suspected","Cardiopulmonary exercise testing for peak VO2 to assess transplant/LVAS candidacy","Invasive hemodynamic assessment of pulmonary vascular resistance and transpulmonary gradient (PVR >3 Wood units or transpulmonary gradient >15 are relative transplant contraindications unless reactive)","Intravascular ultrasound or optical coherence tomography (more sensitive than angiography) and noninvasive coronary flow reserve for CAV detection and prognosis","Prospective or virtual cross-match in allosensitized recipients with preformed antibodies","Doppler-derived mean arterial pressure measurement in continuous-flow LVAS patients","Lactate dehydrogenase as a hemolysis biomarker to screen for pump thrombosis"],"redFlags":["Hemodynamic compromise during acute cellular rejection signals severe rejection and warrants rescue polyclonal antibody therapy with corticosteroids","Suspected LVAD thrombosis carries a high mortality if pump exchange or transplant is not performed; rising LDH may herald it","Neurologic events (ischemic or hemorrhagic stroke) are major hemocompatibility-related complications of LVAS","Driveline infection (occurs in 20-50% of LVAS patients by 5 years) predisposes to thrombosis and neurologic complications","Gastrointestinal bleeding from acquired von Willebrand disease and intestinal arteriovenous malformations","Progressive right ventricular failure and congestion after LVAS implantation"],"prognosis":"More than 150,000 patients have undergone cardiac transplantation worldwide, with 1-year survival above 80%, median survival of ~12.5 years, and conditional survival of ~14.8 years for those surviving the first year. With the fully magnetically levitated HeartMate 3 LVAS, median survival exceeds 5 years (about 60% survival at 5 years), and in younger patients (under 50) without comorbidity and an uncomplicated course the 5-year survival can rival transplantation (~75%). Without such support, patients with severe refractory systolic heart failure (peak VO2 <12 mL/kg per min or inotrope dependence) often have a median survival under 1 year. CAV grade correlates with prognosis and remains a leading limit on late allograft survival.","pitfalls":["Treating allograft bradycardia with atropine, which is ineffective because the transplanted heart is denervated (use isoproterenol or pacing instead)","Relying on routine coronary angiography alone, which underdiagnoses early cardiac allograft vasculopathy compared with intravascular ultrasound or OCT","Attempting to palpate or auscultate a peripheral pulse for blood pressure in continuous-flow LVAS patients, who often have no detectable pulse; Doppler must be used","Overlooking acquired von Willebrand disease as the cause of GI bleeding in LVAS patients","Continuing aspirin in HeartMate 3 patients, which increases nonsurgical bleeding; its elimination (with a vitamin K antagonist targeting INR 2-3) reduces bleeding-related hospitalizations","Failing to anticipate right ventricular failure after LV unloading by an LVAS"],"severityClassification":"Cardiac allograft vasculopathy is graded by the ISHLT 2010 scheme as CAV0 (absent), CAV1 (mild), CAV2 (moderate), and CAV3 (severe), based on extent of angiographic disease and presence of allograft dysfunction by echocardiography or restrictive physiology. Transplant allocation urgency is tiered, with the highest status for critically ill patients on ECMO or temporary biventricular mechanical support, then those needing daily invasive hemodynamic monitoring plus IV inotropes or with durable LVAD complications, and lower status for ambulatory patients stable at home.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2028","Harrison 22e p.2029","Harrison 22e p.2030","Harrison 22e p.2031","Harrison 22e p.2032","Harrison 22e p.2033"],"crossLinks":[]},"cardiac_trauma":{"name":"Cardiac Trauma","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Blunt cardiac injury","BCI","Myocardial contusion","Penetrating cardiac injury","Commotio cordis"],"chapter":283,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2087","clinicalPearls":["Cardiac injury is broadly divided into penetrating trauma (most often gunshot or knife wounds, where the entry site is usually obvious) and nonpenetrating, or blunt cardiac injury (BCI).","BCI most commonly follows motor vehicle collisions (rapid deceleration or chest impact) but can also result from falls, crush and blast injuries, assault, or vigorous contact in sports; significant cardiac injury may occur even when there are no external signs of thoracic trauma.","There is no single sign, symptom, or test that confirms BCI, and clinical, laboratory, and radiographic findings are frequently nonspecific in the setting of major trauma, so a high index of suspicion is essential.","An ECG should be obtained in every patient presenting with chest trauma; abnormalities can resemble those of infarction or pericarditis and include sinus tachycardia, RBBB, heart block, ST-T changes, and atrial or ventricular arrhythmias.","Cardiac troponin is more specific than CK-MB for myocardial damage; a normal troponin combined with a normal ECG at 6-8 hours after chest trauma essentially excludes BCI.","Echocardiography is the most useful modality for detecting the structural and functional sequelae of BCI, including regional wall motion abnormalities, pericardial effusion, valvular dysfunction, and ventricular or septal rupture; the RV, interventricular septum, and LV apex are most commonly involved.","Commotio cordis is ventricular fibrillation triggered by a blunt, often innocent-appearing chest impact in the absence of structural injury, occurring most often in adolescents during sports when the blow lands during the vulnerable phase of repolarization (just before the T-wave peak); survival depends on prompt defibrillation.","Sudden emotional or physical stress, even without direct cardiac trauma, may precipitate a transient catecholamine-mediated cardiomyopathy (takotsubo / apical ballooning syndrome).","Because of its anterior position in the chest, the right ventricle is the most frequently injured chamber in penetrating trauma, followed by the left ventricle; isolated atrial injury is uncommon.","Iatrogenic cardiac perforation can complicate central/intracardiac catheter placement, pacemaker or defibrillator lead insertion, RV endomyocardial biopsy, and intracoronary stent deployment.","Post-cardiac injury syndrome (an inflammatory process resembling the postpericardiotomy syndrome) can develop weeks to months after blunt chest trauma, presenting with pericarditis and/or pericardial effusion.","Survivors of penetrating cardiac injury may present days to weeks later with a new murmur or heart failure from an occult or evolving valvular regurgitation or intracardiac shunt, so trauma patients should be re-examined several weeks after injury."],"pathophysiology":"Blunt forces (rapid deceleration, direct chest impact, crush, or blast) and penetrating objects damage the myocardium, valves, coronary arteries, pericardium, and great vessels. Myocardial contusion produces injury pathologically similar to infarction, with necrosis arising either directly or from traumatic coronary laceration, dissection, or thrombosis, predisposing to arrhythmias and conduction block. More severe injury causes free wall rupture (hemopericardium and tamponade) or ventricular septal rupture (intracardiac shunting). Valvular apparatus rupture (most often tricuspid or aortic) causes acute incompetence. Aortic rupture or transection, typically just above the aortic valve or at the ligamentum arteriosum tethering point, is the most common vascular deceleration injury. Commotio cordis represents a purely electrical mechanism: a chest blow during the susceptible repolarization window induces ventricular fibrillation without structural damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute coronary syndrome / myocardial infarction (traumatic coronary dissection or thrombosis can produce STEMI)","Aortic dissection (traumatic aortic rupture can mimic its presentation)","Acute pericarditis","Cardiac tamponade from other causes","Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy following emotional or physical trauma","Musculoskeletal chest wall pain from rib or sternal injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Non-traumatic acute myocardial infarction","Non-traumatic aortic dissection","Idiopathic or other-cause acute pericarditis","Takotsubo cardiomyopathy"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG in all patients with chest trauma (repeat as needed for evolving conduction disturbance/arrhythmia)","Serial cardiac troponin (I or T); normal serial levels make cardiac injury very unlikely","Transthoracic echocardiography in all suspected BCI and all penetrating chest injuries to assess wall motion, effusion, valves, and shunts","Transesophageal echocardiography when adequate TTE images cannot be obtained","Chest x-ray (mediastinal widening suggests aortic injury)","Cardiac catheterization to confirm a suspected mechanical complication (shunt/valvular lesion)"],"redFlags":["Hemodynamic instability or collapse at presentation (penetrating injury mortality approaches 90% in those in hemodynamic collapse)","New loud murmur with rapidly progressive heart failure (traumatic valvular rupture)","Signs of pericardial tamponade (pericardial rub, distant heart sounds, hypotension)","ST-segment elevation suggesting coronary dissection/occlusion","Mediastinal widening on chest x-ray with upper-extremity hypertension and diminished lower-extremity pulses (aortic rupture)","Sudden collapse during chest impact in an athlete (commotio cordis requiring immediate defibrillation)","Even a small pericardial effusion on echo in a penetrating chest injury (suggests occult cardiac perforation)"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on injury mechanism, chambers involved, and clinical condition at presentation. Although myocardial rupture is generally fatal, up to ~40% of patients reach a specialized trauma center alive. In penetrating injury, gunshot wounds carry higher mortality than stab wounds (about 20% of shooting victims survive versus up to ~65% of stabbing victims), partly because ballistic injuries more often involve multiple chambers; multichamber involvement carries a worse prognosis than single-chamber injury, and RV injury may carry a better prognosis than LV injury. Patients in hemodynamic collapse on arrival have a mortality near 90%, whereas roughly 75% of those stable enough to reach the operating room survive. Iatrogenic penetrating injuries generally have a better prognosis owing to more limited injury and prompt corrective therapy.","pitfalls":["Overlooking cardiac injury because attention is focused on more obvious external injuries.","Relying on sternal or rib fractures to gauge cardiac risk; there is no proven association, and significant cardiac injury can occur with a normal-appearing chest wall.","Using CK-MB to confirm BCI; it is elevated in only ~20% of blunt chest trauma and is falsely raised by massive skeletal muscle injury.","Attributing post-traumatic chest pain solely to musculoskeletal causes when it may reflect myocardial ischemia or pericardial injury.","Assuming a hemodynamically stable penetrating chest injury excludes cardiac involvement; up to ~20% harbor occult cardiac trauma.","Missing delayed complications (post-cardiac injury syndrome, late valvular regurgitation, intracardiac shunt) by not re-examining the patient weeks after injury."],"severityClassification":"Blunt cardiac abnormalities span a spectrum: ECG changes (sinus tachycardia, RBBB, heart block, ST-T changes, arrhythmias), elevated biomarkers (troponin most specific), focal wall motion abnormality or hematoma (RV free wall, LV apex, septum), valvular insufficiency (most often mitral/tricuspid, occasionally aortic), myocardial rupture (VSD or free wall), coronary artery injury (often LAD, presenting as STEMI), and pericardial effusion/tamponade. Risk stratification: stable patients with a normal ECG and normal serial troponins are low risk; an abnormal ECG and/or elevated troponin with a normal echo warrants telemetry monitoring; mechanical complications and hemodynamic instability constitute the highest-acuity group.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2087","Harrison 22e p.2088","Harrison 22e p.2089"],"crossLinks":[]},"cardiovascular_collapse_cardiac_arrest_and":{"name":"Cardiovascular Collapse, Cardiac Arrest, and Sudden Cardiac Death","system":"cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sudden cardiac death","SCD","Sudden cardiac arrest","SCA","Cardiovascular collapse"],"chapter":317,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2332","clinicalPearls":["Harrison distinguishes three overlapping terms: cardiovascular collapse is sudden loss of effective circulation that may revert spontaneously (e.g., neurocardiogenic syncope) or require intervention; cardiac arrest is abrupt cessation of cardiac function with loss of circulation potentially reversible by prompt intervention; and sudden cardiac death is unexpected death from cardiac arrest occurring within roughly 1 hour of symptom onset (p.2332-2333).","Sudden cardiac death accounts for about 15% of all deaths and roughly half of all cardiac deaths; in the United States the text cites an estimated 350,000 EMS-attended out-of-hospital arrests and 210,000 sudden cardiac deaths in adults yearly (p.2332).","Up to two-thirds of sudden cardiac deaths occur as the first clinical manifestation of previously undiagnosed heart disease, limiting the ability to predict and prevent events at the population level (p.2332).","Coronary heart disease underlies the majority of sudden cardiac deaths, but the proportion attributable to coronary disease varies markedly by demographics: roughly 70% in white men versus 40-50% in women and black individuals, and under 40% in Asian populations (p.2333-2334).","The text emphasizes the chain of survival for out-of-hospital arrest, and that survival is strongly time-dependent: neurologic recovery falls rapidly and is under 30% by 5 minutes from collapse to return of spontaneous circulation when no bystander CPR is given (p.2335-2338).","Gasping (agonal) respirations and brief seizure-like activity are common during arrest and may be misinterpreted as breathing or responsiveness, leading to delayed resuscitation; a pulse check by a trained provider should take no longer than 10 seconds (p.2335-2336).","The initial recorded rhythm carries prognostic and etiologic weight: ventricular tachycardia/fibrillation (shockable rhythms) have the best outcomes, while pulseless electrical activity and asystole carry a poor prognosis (p.2335-2336).","The proportion of arrests found in ventricular fibrillation has fallen from over half in the 1970s to only 20-25% in recent series, with pulseless electrical activity and asystole now most common; because arrhythmias are unstable after collapse, the rhythm at EMS arrival may not reflect the initiating rhythm (p.2334).","Inherited arrhythmia syndromes without structural heart disease (long QT, Brugada, catecholaminergic polymorphic VT, early repolarization, idiopathic VF) are a more important cause of sudden death in the young and in Asian populations, accounting for around 10% of cases in Asia versus 1-2% in Western countries (p.2334).","Depressed left ventricular function is the best-validated risk marker in structural heart disease; after myocardial infarction, SCD risk rises gradually as LVEF falls toward 40% and then increases exponentially below that (p.2338-2339).","Following an MI, the risk of sudden death rises up to roughly 10-fold, with the highest absolute rates in the first 30 days; early deaths are more often non-arrhythmic (myocardial rupture, reinfarction) while later deaths are predominantly scar-related ventricular arrhythmias (p.2334-2335).","Commotio cordis - ventricular fibrillation triggered by blunt, non-penetrating precordial impact - is a rare cause of sudden death in otherwise healthy individuals, especially after a chest blow during the vulnerable repolarization window (p.2335)."],"pathophysiology":"Most sudden cardiac death stems from a lethal ventricular tachyarrhythmia (polymorphic VT degenerating to ventricular fibrillation) arising on a vulnerable myocardial substrate, though bradyarrhythmia, asystole, and pulseless electrical activity are increasingly common terminal rhythms. Coronary artery disease is the predominant substrate, acting through acute ischemia/infarction or through reentry around scar from a healed infarct. Nonischemic processes - dilated, hypertrophic, infiltrative, and arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathies, valvular disease, congenital surgical scars, and myocarditis - produce replacement fibrosis, hypertrophy, ventricular stretch, electrical heterogeneity, altered ion-channel function, and abnormal calcium handling that lower the threshold for sustained arrhythmia. In structurally normal hearts, inherited channelopathies (long QT, Brugada, CPVT), pre-excited atrial fibrillation in Wolff-Parkinson-White, and QT-prolonging drug toxicity cause primary electrical arrest. Pulseless electrical activity reflects mechanical or metabolic catastrophe - massive MI, pulmonary embolism, exsanguination, tamponade, tension pneumothorax, profound hypoxia or acidosis. Heightened autonomic (sympathetic) tone is the common thread linking precipitants such as vigorous exertion, emotional and environmental stress to arrhythmogenesis. After return of circulation, global ischemia-reperfusion brain injury becomes the dominant determinant of outcome.","additionalDifferentials":["Vasovagal/neurocardiogenic syncope (transient, self-limited circulatory loss rather than true arrest)","Massive pulmonary embolism","Acute aortic dissection","Hypovolemic shock / exsanguination","Cardiac tamponade","Tension pneumothorax","Profound metabolic derangement (severe hyperkalemia, severe acidosis, severe hypokalemia)","Drug overdose (including opioid and QT-prolonging agents)","Hypothermia","Primary respiratory arrest with secondary circulatory collapse"],"infectionMimics":["Myocarditis presenting with malignant ventricular arrhythmia","Sepsis-associated cardiovascular collapse/pulseless electrical activity"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Neurocardiogenic (vasovagal) syncope","Massive pulmonary embolism","Aortic dissection","Tension pneumothorax","Cardiac tamponade","Severe hyperkalemia causing sinusoidal arrest","Opioid or other drug overdose","Hypothermia","Seizure with transient unresponsiveness"],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous ECG/rhythm monitoring and retrieval of AED rhythm strips to determine the initiating rhythm","Arterial blood gas to assess hypoxia and metabolic acidosis during and after resuscitation","Serum electrolytes (potassium, magnesium) and assessment for reversible causes","Post-ROSC 12-lead ECG to detect ST-elevation MI and consideration of coronary angiography","Echocardiography to assess LVEF, prior infarction, valvular and structural disease","Cardiac MRI to characterize scar burden/heterogeneity and nonischemic cardiomyopathy","Cardiac PET and/or endomyocardial biopsy when inflammatory cardiomyopathy (sarcoidosis, myocarditis) is suspected","Electrophysiology study and provocative testing (epinephrine challenge for LQTS, sodium-channel-blocker challenge for Brugada) and genetic evaluation when a heritable arrhythmia syndrome is suspected","EEG monitoring in comatose post-arrest patients to detect seizures and prognosticate"],"redFlags":["Unresponsiveness with absent or only agonal/gasping respirations","Absent central pulse on a brief (<=10 second) check","Pulseless electrical activity or asystole as the presenting rhythm (poor prognosis)","Long interval (>5 minutes) between collapse and start of resuscitation","Hemodynamic instability, recurrent VT/VF, or signs of ongoing ischemia after ROSC","Post-ROSC absent pupillary light reflex, status myoclonus, absent EEG reactivity, or persistent burst suppression (predict poor neurologic recovery)","Sustained monomorphic VT or VF without a clearly reversible cause, implying high recurrence risk","Markedly depressed LVEF (e.g., <=35%) or clinical heart failure after MI"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are dominated by time to resuscitation and the presenting rhythm. Survival to hospital discharge for out-of-hospital arrest has improved but remains low - the chapter states about 90% of patients do not survive to discharge, and roughly 20% of survivors are left with severe neurologic or physical disability. Shockable rhythms (VT/VF) carry the best prognosis, with survival approaching 30% in some regions, whereas PEA and asystole carry poor outcomes. Success of resuscitation declines sharply after 5 minutes from collapse. Ischemia-reperfusion brain injury accounts for over two-thirds of post-arrest deaths; reliable neurologic prognostication is generally deferred at least 72 hours after return to normothermia (typically 4-5 days post-arrest). Features predicting poor neurologic outcome include absent pupillary light reflex, status myoclonus, absent EEG reactivity, and persistent burst suppression.","pitfalls":["Mistaking agonal gasping or brief seizure activity for effective breathing or responsiveness, delaying CPR","Spending more than 10 seconds on a pulse check and thereby delaying chest compressions","Assuming the rhythm at EMS arrival reflects the initiating rhythm, when VF and bradyarrhythmias commonly degenerate to asystole","Prognosticating neurologic recovery too early - while still under sedation/hypothermia or before the recommended 72 hours of normothermia","Implanting an ICD for arrhythmia driven by a transient reversible cause (e.g., hyperkalemia, drug-induced torsades, or VF within 48 h of acute MI), where it is generally not indicated","Withholding consideration that arrhythmia may signal end-stage heart disease, in which an ICD will not change the course absent transplant or assist-device candidacy","Overlooking treatable PEA/asystole causes such as pulmonary embolism, hypovolemia, tamponade, tension pneumothorax, hypoxia, acidosis, hyperkalemia, hypothermia, toxins, and massive MI","Attributing sudden death in the young to coronary disease, when HCM, coronary anomalies, myocarditis, ARVC, and channelopathies are more often responsible"],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames the spectrum by terminology and reversibility: cardiovascular collapse (may revert spontaneously, includes syncope) -> cardiac arrest (rarely reverts spontaneously; reversibility depends on mechanism, setting, and speed of intervention) -> sudden cardiac death (death within ~1 hour of symptom onset). Acute risk and outcome stratify by presenting rhythm - shockable (VT/VF, better) versus non-shockable (PEA/asystole, worse). Chronic SCD risk in structural heart disease is graded chiefly by LVEF and presence of heart failure, with risk rising gradually as LVEF falls to 40% and exponentially below it.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2332","Harrison 22e p.2333","Harrison 22e p.2334","Harrison 22e p.2335","Harrison 22e p.2336","Harrison 22e p.2337","Harrison 22e p.2338","Harrison 22e p.2339"],"crossLinks":[]},"carotid_artery_stenosis":{"name":"Carotid artery stenosis","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["carotid stenosis","internal carotid artery stenosis","carotid occlusive disease","extracranial carotid disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most lesions occur at the carotid bifurcation and proximal internal carotid artery, where atherosclerotic plaque and turbulence predispose to thromboembolism","Symptomatic disease typically presents as ipsilateral transient ischaemic attack, amaurosis fugax, or hemispheric stroke rather than global symptoms","A cervical bruit is neither sensitive nor specific and its absence does not exclude significant stenosis","Duplex ultrasound is the usual first-line imaging, with CT or MR angiography used to confirm the degree of narrowing and assess the aortic arch and intracranial circulation","Risk stratification hinges on whether the lesion is symptomatic and on the percentage stenosis, as benefit from intervention is greatest in recently symptomatic high-grade lesions","Best medical therapy with antiplatelet agents, lipid lowering, blood pressure control, and smoking cessation is fundamental for all patients regardless of intervention","Carotid endarterectomy and carotid artery stenting are the two revascularisation options, with choice guided by anatomy, age, comorbidity, and operative risk","Intervention for symptomatic disease is most effective when performed early after the index neurological event"],"pathophysiology":"Carotid artery stenosis is usually caused by atherosclerotic plaque forming at the carotid bifurcation, a site of low and oscillating wall shear stress. Progressive plaque growth narrows the lumen, but the dominant mechanism of stroke is artery-to-artery thromboembolism from an unstable, ulcerated, or haemorrhagic plaque rather than simple flow limitation. Plaque rupture exposes thrombogenic material, generating platelet-fibrin and cholesterol emboli that lodge in the ophthalmic or middle cerebral territory. In severe stenosis or occlusion, cerebral perfusion may also become haemodynamically compromised, particularly when collateral supply through the circle of Willis is inadequate.","additionalDifferentials":["Cardioembolic stroke from atrial fibrillation or structural heart disease","Small-vessel (lacunar) cerebrovascular disease","Intracranial atherosclerosis","Carotid or vertebral artery dissection","Vertebrobasilar insufficiency","Fibromuscular dysplasia of the carotid artery"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Carotid duplex ultrasound to estimate stenosis severity and plaque characteristics","CT angiography or MR angiography to confirm stenosis and evaluate arch and intracranial vessels","Brain imaging (CT or MRI) to identify infarction in symptomatic patients","Cardiac evaluation including ECG and echocardiography to exclude a cardioembolic source","Vascular risk-factor profiling including lipids and glucose"],"redFlags":["Recent transient ischaemic attack, amaurosis fugax, or stroke with high-grade ipsilateral stenosis, indicating high early stroke risk and need for urgent assessment","Crescendo TIAs or stroke-in-evolution suggesting an unstable plaque","Acute focal neurological deficit requiring emergency stroke pathway activation","Contralateral occlusion or near-occlusion increasing haemodynamic vulnerability"],"prognosis":"Recently symptomatic high-grade stenosis carries a substantial short-term risk of recurrent stroke that is markedly reduced by timely revascularisation combined with medical therapy. Asymptomatic disease has a lower annual stroke risk, and modern medical therapy has narrowed the margin of benefit from intervention.","pitfalls":["Relying on a bruit or its absence to gauge stenosis severity","Delaying revascularisation in recently symptomatic patients, forfeiting the greatest window of benefit","Overestimating benefit of intervention in asymptomatic patients with limited life expectancy","Neglecting global vascular risk-factor modification while focusing only on the carotid lesion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"carotid_body_tumor":{"name":"Carotid body tumour","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["carotid body paraganglioma","chemodectoma","carotid body tumor","cervical paraganglioma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A paraganglioma arising from the chemoreceptor tissue of the carotid body at the carotid bifurcation","Typically presents as a slow-growing, painless, pulsatile neck mass at the angle of the jaw","The mass is classically mobile from side to side but not vertically because it is tethered to the bifurcation (Fontaine sign)","Most are benign and non-functional, but a minority secrete catecholamines or behave malignantly with nodal or distant spread","A significant proportion are familial and linked to succinate dehydrogenase gene mutations, warranting genetic assessment and screening for multiple or bilateral tumours","Imaging shows a highly vascular mass splaying the internal and external carotid arteries, producing the characteristic lyre sign on angiography","Biopsy is generally avoided because of the vascularity and bleeding risk; diagnosis relies on characteristic imaging","Surgical resection is the mainstay for suitable lesions, with radiotherapy considered for high-risk or unresectable disease, and preoperative embolisation used in selected large tumours"],"pathophysiology":"The carotid body is a small chemoreceptor organ at the carotid bifurcation that senses arterial oxygen, carbon dioxide, and pH. A carotid body tumour is a paraganglioma arising from its neural crest-derived chief cells. Chronic hypoxic stimulation, such as living at high altitude, can promote hyperplasia and tumour development, and hereditary forms result from germline mutations in succinate dehydrogenase subunit genes. The tumour is intensely vascular and grows within the crotch of the bifurcation, progressively splaying and encasing the internal and external carotid arteries and potentially involving adjacent cranial nerves. Most are non-functional, but some secrete catecholamines.","additionalDifferentials":["Cervical lymphadenopathy (reactive or malignant)","Branchial cleft cyst","Vagal or other head and neck paraganglioma","Carotid artery aneurysm","Salivary gland tumour","Metastatic neck node or nerve sheath tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Duplex ultrasound and CT or MR angiography demonstrating a vascular mass splaying the carotid bifurcation","Catheter angiography where detailed vascular mapping or embolisation is planned","Functional and biochemical testing for catecholamine secretion when a secretory tumour is suspected","Genetic testing (including succinate dehydrogenase genes) and screening for multifocal or bilateral disease","Cross-sectional imaging to assess local extent and identify additional paragangliomas"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting malignant paraganglioma with regional or distant spread","Catecholamine excess with paroxysmal hypertension or arrhythmia requiring assessment before intervention","Rapid growth or new cranial nerve deficits indicating local invasion","Bilateral or familial disease prompting genetic evaluation and family screening"],"prognosis":"Most carotid body tumours are benign and slow-growing, and complete resection offers an excellent outcome, though larger lesions carry greater risk of cranial nerve injury and vascular complications. A minority are malignant, and prognosis then depends on the extent of spread and completeness of treatment.","pitfalls":["Performing biopsy of a highly vascular mass and precipitating significant haemorrhage","Failing to test for catecholamine secretion before surgery in a functional tumour","Overlooking hereditary disease and the need for genetic testing and screening for additional paragangliomas","Underestimating the risk of cranial nerve injury with resection of large tumours"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"carpal_tunnel_syndrome":{"name":"Carpal tunnel syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["median nerve compression at the wrist","CTS","median neuropathy at the carpal tunnel"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest peripheral nerve entrapment, caused by compression of the median nerve beneath the flexor retinaculum at the wrist","Typically presents with numbness, tingling, and pain in the thumb, index, middle, and radial half of the ring finger, characteristically worse at night and relieved by shaking the hand","Symptoms may radiate up the forearm; in advanced cases there is weakness and wasting of the thenar muscles (abductor pollicis brevis)","Often idiopathic but associated with pregnancy, diabetes, hypothyroidism, rheumatoid arthritis, obesity, and repetitive wrist use","Provocative signs include Tinel's (tapping over the nerve) and Phalen's (sustained wrist flexion) tests, though their sensitivity is limited","Nerve conduction studies confirm the diagnosis and grade severity, and are useful before surgery or in atypical cases","Mild or intermittent disease often responds to nocturnal wrist splinting, activity modification, and treatment of any underlying condition; corticosteroid injection can give temporary relief","Surgical division of the flexor retinaculum (carpal tunnel release) is indicated for persistent symptoms, failure of conservative treatment, or established motor deficit and sensory loss"],"pathophysiology":"The carpal tunnel is a rigid osseofibrous canal bounded by the carpal bones and roofed by the flexor retinaculum, through which the median nerve passes alongside the flexor tendons. Any increase in the contents or reduction in the capacity of this fixed space raises pressure on the median nerve. Compression impairs the nerve's microcirculation and, if sustained, causes segmental demyelination and eventually axonal loss. Conditions that increase tissue volume (fluid retention in pregnancy, synovial thickening in rheumatoid arthritis, tenosynovitis from overuse) or reduce tunnel size (wrist fractures, deformity) precipitate symptoms. Sensory fibres are usually affected first, with motor weakness and thenar wasting appearing later.","additionalDifferentials":["Cervical radiculopathy (particularly C6/C7)","Proximal median nerve entrapment (e.g. pronator syndrome)","Peripheral polyneuropathy (e.g. diabetic)","Thoracic outlet syndrome","Other nerve entrapments (ulnar neuropathy)","Thenar osteoarthritis (first carpometacarpal joint)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nerve conduction studies and electromyography to confirm and grade median nerve compression","Clinical provocative tests (Tinel's and Phalen's) and thenar strength assessment","Blood tests for associated conditions (glucose/HbA1c, thyroid function) where indicated","Wrist radiographs if there is a history of fracture, deformity, or arthritis","Ultrasound of the nerve in selected or atypical cases"],"redFlags":["Progressive thenar muscle wasting and weakness indicating advanced nerve damage warranting timely decompression","Persistent dense sensory loss that may not fully recover","Acute severe symptoms after a wrist fracture suggesting acute median nerve compression","Bilateral or rapidly progressive neuropathy suggesting a systemic cause needing investigation"],"prognosis":"Most patients with mild to moderate disease improve with conservative measures or gain lasting relief from surgical release; however, longstanding severe compression with thenar wasting and marked axonal loss may leave incomplete recovery of strength and sensation despite decompression.","pitfalls":["Attributing hand symptoms to carpal tunnel syndrome when the cause is cervical radiculopathy or a more proximal lesion","Delaying surgery until irreversible thenar wasting and sensory loss develop","Overlooking a treatable underlying condition such as hypothyroidism or diabetes","Relying solely on provocative clinical tests, which can be falsely negative"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"cat_scratch_disease":{"name":"Bartonellosis (incl. Cat-Scratch Disease)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Bartonella infection","cat-scratch disease"],"chapter":177,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1350-1356","clinicalPearls":["Bartonella are fastidious, facultative intracellular, slow-growing gram-negative bacteria; clinical presentation depends on both the infecting species and the host's immune status. Of the >40 species/subspecies, B. bacilliformis, B. quintana and B. henselae are most commonly identified human pathogens (p.1350).","B. henselae is the principal etiologic agent of cat-scratch disease (CSD); rare cases are associated with Afipia felis and other Bartonella species. Apparently healthy bacteremic cats (especially kittens) are the major reservoir; person-to-person transmission does not occur and intrafamilial clustering is rare (p.1351).","Typical CSD (85-90% of cases) is a usually self-limited subacute regional lymphadenopathy; a small (0.3-1 cm) painless erythematous papule/pustule appears at the inoculation site within days to 2 weeks in about one-third to two-thirds of patients, with nodes enlarging 1-3 weeks (or longer) after cat contact (p.1351-1352).","Affected nodes are usually painful and suppurate in ~10% of cases; axillary/epitrochlear nodes are most commonly involved, then head/neck, then inguinal/femoral. About 50% have fever, malaise and anorexia; weight loss and night sweats can mimic lymphoma (p.1351-1352).","Atypical (extranodal) CSD occurs in 10-15% and includes Parinaud's oculoglandular syndrome, neuroretinitis (often unilateral visual deterioration), hepatosplenic disease, encephalitis/seizures/myelitis/cranial-nerve palsies, FUO, pneumonitis, debilitating myalgia, arthritis/arthralgia (mostly women >20 yr), osteomyelitis (incl. multifocal) and erythema nodosum (p.1353).","CSD-associated FUO is a unique, sometimes severe and debilitating syndrome that often mimics malignancy and may involve multiple organs (hepatosplenic space-occupying lesions, abdominal/mediastinal lymphadenopathy, ocular disease, multifocal osteomyelitis); fever may be continuous or relapsing (p.1353).","In elderly patients (>60 yr), lymphadenopathy is more often absent, whereas encephalitis and FUO are more common than in younger patients (p.1353).","Coxiella burnetii and Bartonella species are the most common causes of culture-negative endocarditis; in one French series Bartonella accounted for 28% of 348 culture-negative endocarditis cases. B. quintana and B. henselae are the species most often implicated (p.1353).","Bartonella endocarditis often presents as a prolonged, minimally febrile or even afebrile indolent illness lasting weeks to months; initial echocardiography may not show vegetations and acute aggressive disease is rare. Most cases are native-valve, ~60% aortic; B. henselae usually infects preexisting valvulopathy while B. quintana often infects normal valves (p.1353).","Bacillary angiomatosis (caused by B. henselae or B. quintana) is a neovascular proliferative disease of severely immunocompromised patients, presenting as non-painful tan/red/purple cutaneous lesions, tender subcutaneous nodules, ulcerated plaques or verrucous growths; nodular forms resemble fungal or mycobacterial infection (p.1354-1355).","Bacillary peliosis is a related angioproliferative disorder caused by B. henselae involving primarily the liver (peliosis hepatis) and also spleen and lymph nodes, with blood-filled cystic structures (microscopic to several mm). In advanced immunodeficiency, B. henselae and B. quintana are important causes of FUO (p.1355).","Trench fever (5-day/quintan fever) is caused by B. quintana, transmitted by the human body louse; it has reemerged as chronic bacteremia in homeless people (urban/contemporary trench fever), with alcoholism and louse infestation as risk factors (p.1354)."],"pathophysiology":"Bartonella adapt to specific domestic/wild mammalian hosts where prolonged intraerythrocytic and endothelial-cell infection creates an immune-protected reservoir, producing chronic bacteremia that may persist even after prolonged antimicrobial therapy. Adhesion and entry to endothelial cells and erythrocytes is facilitated by Bartonella adhesin A or variably expressed outer-membrane proteins; the organism evades host immunity by modifying virulence factors (lipopolysaccharide, flagella) and attenuating the immune response. Humans are incidental hosts for most species except B. quintana and B. bacilliformis (which are not zoonotic). In CSD, the cat-to-human transmission mode is undetermined: B. henselae-infected cat saliva spreads to claws by self-licking and contaminated flea feces can be inoculated by a scratch or bite (mucous-membrane/conjunctival inoculation also possible). Lymphatic drainage to regional nodes in immunocompetent hosts triggers a TH1 response producing necrotizing granulomatous lymphadenitis, with dendritic cells and chemokines driving granuloma formation. In bacillary angiomatosis, Bartonella angiogenic factor A (a proangiogenic autotransporter in B. henselae) activates the host vascular endothelial growth factor pathway to drive angiogenesis (p.1350-1355).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Pyogenic (bacterial) lymphadenitis","Mycobacterial infection (incl. tuberculous lymphadenitis)","Brucellosis","Syphilis","Tularemia","Plague","Toxoplasmosis","Sporotrichosis","Histoplasmosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lymphoma / malignancy (weight loss and night sweats of CSD, and CSD-associated FUO, can mimic malignancy)","Kaposi's sarcoma (differential for cutaneous bacillary angiomatosis)","Pyogenic granuloma (differential for cutaneous bacillary angiomatosis)","Subcutaneous tumors (differential for cutaneous bacillary angiomatosis)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serology (immunofluorescence or enzyme immunoassay) is the most commonly used diagnostic, but has variable sensitivity/specificity; CSD serodiagnosis is often based on IgG alone (in the absence of IgM) and seroconversion may take a few weeks, complicating interpretation (p.1352).","PCR of pus aspirated from a lymph node or the primary inoculation lesion is highly sensitive and specific and is particularly useful for definitive, rapid diagnosis in seronegative patients; PCR of a lymph node biopsy specimen may be less sensitive (possibly due to sampling error) (p.1352).","Histopathology shows early lymphoid hyperplasia, later stellate granulomata with necrosis, coalescing microabscesses and occasional multinucleated giant cells (nonspecific but may narrow the differential) (p.1352).","For endocarditis: blood cultures (even with lysis-centrifugation or EDTA tubes) are positive in only ~25% of cases (mostly B. quintana, rarely B. henselae) and require prolonged incubation up to 6 weeks; high-titer (>=1:800) IgG serology supports the diagnosis but cross-reacts between B. quintana and B. henselae and with C. burnetii and Chlamydia; species-level identification usually requires PCR and DNA sequencing of valve tissue (p.1353).","For bacillary angiomatosis/peliosis: diagnosis is by histologic examination; Warthin-Starry silver staining of lesions reveals clusters of bacilli; cultures are usually negative though blood cultures may be positive (p.1355)."],"redFlags":["Persistent constitutional symptoms with failure of spontaneous regression of node size, and negative acute- and convalescent-phase sera (10-20% of CSD patients) — malignancy must be ruled out (p.1352).","Seronegative CSD with lymphadenopathy and severe complications (encephalitis or neuroretinitis) — early biopsy is important to establish a specific diagnosis (p.1352).","Unilateral visual deterioration (neuroretinitis) in suspected CSD — ophthalmologic manifestations may occasionally cause moderate to severe vision loss (p.1353).","Culture-negative endocarditis, especially in a homeless/alcoholic patient with body louse infestation (B. quintana) or significant cat exposure (B. henselae) (p.1353)."],"prognosis":"In immunocompetent individuals, CSD (whether typical or atypical) usually resolves without treatment and without sequelae; resolution is slow, requiring weeks for fever/pain and months for node shrinkage, and lifelong immunity is the rule. Some ophthalmologic manifestations may occasionally result in moderate to severe vision loss. Bartonella endocarditis is often diagnosed late, and the proportion of patients requiring valvular surgery (~60%) is high, probably as a consequence of delayed diagnosis (p.1352-1353).","pitfalls":["Bartonella is a key but easily missed cause of culture-negative endocarditis; routine blood cultures are positive in only ~25% of cases and serology cross-reacts with C. burnetii and Chlamydia, so a high titer to one organism may reflect cross-reactivity rather than the true pathogen (p.1353).","CSD is a frequently overlooked cause of FUO; CSD-associated FUO can mimic malignancy with multiorgan space-occupying lesions, leading to unnecessary investigation for cancer (p.1353).","Negative serology does not exclude CSD: 10-20% of patients have negative acute- and convalescent-phase sera, and seroconversion may take weeks; PCR of aspirated pus is preferred in seronegative patients (p.1352).","Suppurative nodes should be drained by large-bore needle aspiration, NOT by incision and drainage, to avoid chronic draining tracts (p.1353).","In elderly patients, lymphadenopathy is more often absent, so CSD may be missed when it presents instead as encephalitis or FUO (p.1353)."],"severityClassification":"Cat-scratch disease is described along clinical lines rather than a formal grading scale: (1) Typical CSD (85-90%) — subacute regional lymphadenopathy, usually self-limited; (2) Atypical/extranodal CSD (10-15%) — extranodal manifestations (Parinaud's oculoglandular syndrome, neuroretinitis, hepatosplenic disease, neurologic disease, FUO, osteomyelitis, etc.). Severe/disseminated Bartonella disease (bacillary angiomatosis and peliosis) occurs primarily in HIV-infected persons with CD4+ T-cell counts <100/uL and in other immunosuppressed patients; Bartonella endocarditis is a separate severe culture-negative-endocarditis presentation (p.1351-1355).","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Chapter 177: Bartonella Infections, Including Cat-Scratch Disease (Giladi M, Ephros M), pp.1350-1356","Edouard S et al: Bartonella quintana and Bartonella henselae endocarditis: A study (cited in Ch.177)","Koehler JE et al: Molecular epidemiology of Bartonella infections in patients with bacillary angiomatosis-peliosis. N Engl J Med 337:1876, 1997","Landes M et al: Cat scratch disease presenting as fever of unknown origin (cited in Ch.177)","Rolain JM et al: Antimicrob Agents Chemother 48:1921, 2004 (basis for Table 177-2)"],"crossLinks":[]},"cataract":{"name":"Cataract","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lens opacity","senile cataract","age-related cataract","nuclear sclerosis","cortical cataract","posterior subcapsular cataract"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with gradual painless blurring of vision, glare and haloes around lights, and reduced contrast, often worse in bright sunlight or oncoming headlights","Nuclear sclerotic cataract can cause a temporary myopic shift ('second sight') allowing reading without glasses before vision deteriorates","Posterior subcapsular cataract disproportionately impairs near vision and glare and is associated with steroid use and diabetes","The red reflex is reduced or shows dark opacities against the glow on ophthalmoscopy","Age is the commonest cause; other associations include diabetes, trauma, chronic steroid use, uveitis, and congenital or metabolic causes in children","Surgery (phacoemulsification with intraocular lens implant) is offered when the cataract limits daily function, not merely on its presence","In children a white pupil (leukocoria) demands urgent assessment to exclude congenital cataract and retinoblastoma"],"pathophysiology":"A cataract is any opacification of the normally transparent crystalline lens. With age, lens proteins (crystallins) undergo aggregation, oxidation, and cross-linking, while accumulation of pigment and changes in hydration reduce lens transparency and scatter incoming light. Depending on where these changes concentrate, opacity develops in the nucleus, cortex, or beneath the posterior capsule, each producing a distinct visual disturbance. Metabolic, traumatic, inflammatory, and toxic insults accelerate similar biochemical changes.","additionalDifferentials":["Refractive error","Age-related macular degeneration","Diabetic retinopathy or macular oedema","Open-angle glaucoma","Corneal opacity or dystrophy","Vitreous opacities"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Visual acuity and refraction","Slit-lamp examination to grade and localise the opacity","Assessment of the red reflex","Dilated fundus examination to exclude co-existing macular or retinal disease","Biometry (keratometry and axial length) for intraocular lens power calculation before surgery"],"redFlags":["Leukocoria in an infant or child — exclude congenital cataract and retinoblastoma urgently","Sudden rather than gradual visual loss, suggesting an alternative diagnosis","A mature or hypermature (intumescent) lens that may precipitate secondary (phacomorphic or phacolytic) glaucoma","Associated pain or redness pointing to lens-induced inflammation"],"prognosis":"Cataract surgery is one of the most effective and successful operations, restoring good vision in the great majority provided the retina and optic nerve are healthy.","pitfalls":["Attributing all of a patient's visual loss to cataract while missing co-existing macular degeneration or glaucoma","Delaying assessment of childhood leukocoria and missing retinoblastoma","Overlooking a myopic shift as an early sign of nuclear cataract","Recommending surgery on lens appearance alone rather than functional impairment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"catheter_related_bloodstream":{"name":"Catheter-related bloodstream infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CRBSI","CLABSI","central line-associated bloodstream infection","line infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / IDSA","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A bloodstream infection attributable to an intravascular catheter, most often a central venous line, in a patient with no other apparent source","Coagulase-negative staphylococci, Staphylococcus aureus, enterococci, Gram-negative bacilli, and Candida species are the usual culprits","Diagnosis is supported by paired blood cultures drawn simultaneously from the catheter and a peripheral vein, with a shorter time to positivity from the catheter (differential time to positivity)","Purulence or erythema at the exit site, or clinical improvement after line removal, supports the diagnosis but is not always present","Management principles centre on removing or exchanging the catheter, particularly for S. aureus, Candida, Pseudomonas, or complicated infection, alongside targeted antibiotics","Antibiotic lock therapy may allow catheter salvage in selected long-term lines infected with less virulent organisms","S. aureus and candidal line infections mandate a search for metastatic complications such as endocarditis, and echocardiography should be considered","Prevention relies on strict insertion and maintenance bundles, hand hygiene, and prompt removal of unnecessary lines"],"pathophysiology":"Intravascular catheters can become infected by several routes: migration of skin flora along the external catheter surface to the tip, contamination of the catheter hub and lumen during handling, haematogenous seeding from a distant focus, or, rarely, contaminated infusate. Microorganisms adhere to the catheter and form a biofilm within a matrix that shields them from host defences and antibiotics, allowing persistent shedding of organisms into the bloodstream. This biofilm explains why eradication frequently requires removing the device rather than antibiotics alone.","additionalDifferentials":["Bloodstream infection from another source (urinary, respiratory, abdominal)","Contaminated blood culture from skin flora","Infective endocarditis","Localised exit-site or tunnel infection without bacteraemia","Non-infective causes of fever in a hospitalised patient"],"infectionMimics":["Blood culture contamination with coagulase-negative staphylococci","Drug fever or transfusion reaction","Thrombophlebitis without infection","Post-operative or malignancy-associated fever"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Paired peripheral and catheter-drawn blood cultures for differential time to positivity","Culture of the catheter tip after removal","Echocardiography when S. aureus, candidal, or persistent bacteraemia is present","Repeat blood cultures to document clearance after starting treatment","Imaging to seek metastatic foci if bacteraemia persists"],"redFlags":["Severe sepsis or septic shock arising from the line infection requiring urgent resuscitation and line removal","Persistent bacteraemia despite appropriate therapy suggesting endocarditis, septic thrombophlebitis, or a retained infected device","S. aureus or Candida bloodstream infection with risk of metastatic seeding","Signs of tunnel or pocket infection or septic thrombosis of the cannulated vein"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the organism and host; uncomplicated coagulase-negative staphylococcal infection often resolves with line removal, whereas S. aureus, Candida, or complicated infections carry higher morbidity, mortality, and risk of metastatic complications.","pitfalls":["Continuing to use an infected catheter, especially with S. aureus or Candida, instead of removing it","Misinterpreting a single coagulase-negative staphylococcal culture as contamination or as true infection without corroboration","Failing to seek endocarditis or metastatic foci in S. aureus or candidal bacteraemia","Not confirming clearance of bacteraemia with repeat cultures"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","IDSA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"cauda_equina_syndrome":{"name":"Cauda equina syndrome","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CES","cauda equina compression"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A neurosurgical emergency caused by compression of the lumbosacral nerve roots below the conus, requiring rapid recognition and decompression","Classic features include bilateral sciatica, saddle (perineal) sensory loss, bladder and bowel dysfunction, and lower limb weakness","Urinary symptoms progress from difficulty voiding and reduced sensation to painless retention with overflow incontinence, a late and ominous sign","Reduced anal tone and impaired perineal sensation on examination support the diagnosis and should be specifically sought","Because it involves nerve roots rather than the cord itself, signs are typically lower motor neuron with areflexia and flaccid weakness","The most common cause is a large central lumbar disc herniation, but tumour, epidural abscess, haematoma, and trauma are important alternatives","Urgent MRI of the whole lumbosacral spine is the investigation of choice to confirm and localise the compression","Early surgical decompression offers the best chance of preserving bladder, bowel, and sexual function and neurological recovery"],"pathophysiology":"Cauda equina syndrome results from compression of the bundle of lumbar and sacral nerve roots within the spinal canal below the termination of the spinal cord. Because these are peripheral nerve roots, compression produces lower motor neuron deficits, radicular pain, and disruption of the sacral parasympathetic and somatic supply to the bladder, bowel, and perineum. Sustained compression causes ischaemia and mechanical injury to the roots, and delayed relief risks irreversible loss of sphincter and sexual function.","additionalDifferentials":["Conus medullaris syndrome","Acute transverse myelitis or other myelopathy","Peripheral polyneuropathy such as Guillain-Barre syndrome","Isolated lumbar radiculopathy","Spinal cord or vertebral metastatic disease","Functional or non-organic urinary retention"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Emergency MRI of the lumbosacral spine to identify the site and cause of compression","CT myelography when MRI is contraindicated or unavailable","Post-void residual bladder volume measurement or bladder scan to assess retention","Assessment of anal tone and perineal sensation as part of the neurological examination","Investigations directed at the underlying cause, such as inflammatory markers when abscess is suspected"],"redFlags":["New urinary retention, overflow incontinence, or loss of bladder/bowel control demanding emergency imaging and surgical decompression","Saddle anaesthesia and reduced anal sphincter tone","Bilateral leg pain, weakness, or numbness with sphincter disturbance","Fever and severe back pain suggesting an epidural abscess requiring urgent intervention"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on the speed of diagnosis and decompression. Surgery performed before the onset of established urinary retention gives the best functional results, whereas delayed treatment is associated with persistent bladder, bowel, sexual, and motor deficits. Some residual dysfunction is common even after timely surgery in severe cases.","pitfalls":["Dismissing back pain with subtle urinary or perineal symptoms and missing the diagnosis until retention is established","Delaying MRI or specialist referral, forfeiting the window for effective decompression","Failing to examine perineal sensation and anal tone","Assuming normal voiding excludes the syndrome without checking post-void residual volume"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"central_retinal_artery_occlusion":{"name":"Central retinal artery occlusion","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CRAO","retinal artery occlusion","retinal stroke","central retinal arterial occlusion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as sudden, painless, profound loss of vision in one eye, often to counting fingers or worse","A relative afferent pupillary defect is present in the affected eye","Fundoscopy shows a pale, oedematous retina with a classic cherry-red spot at the fovea and attenuated, segmented ('cattle-trucking') arterioles","It is effectively a stroke of the eye — treat as a time-critical emergency and investigate the same as an acute cerebral ischaemic event","Always exclude giant cell arteritis in older patients by asking about jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, and headache and checking inflammatory markers","Immediate measures aim to dislodge or reperfuse (ocular massage, lowering intraocular pressure), though evidence for benefit is limited once the window has passed","Urgent referral for stroke workup (carotid imaging, cardiac source, vascular risk factors) is essential to prevent a subsequent cerebral stroke"],"pathophysiology":"Central retinal artery occlusion results from acute interruption of blood flow in the central retinal artery, most often from an embolus (carotid or cardiac) or from thrombosis, arteritis, or vasospasm. The inner retina, supplied by this artery, is exquisitely sensitive to ischaemia; sudden occlusion causes rapid infarction and oedema of the inner retinal layers. The fovea, supplied by the underlying choroid, retains its colour and stands out as a cherry-red spot against the surrounding pale, opaque ischaemic retina. Irreversible neuronal damage occurs within a short ischaemic window.","additionalDifferentials":["Central retinal vein occlusion","Ischaemic (arteritic or non-arteritic) optic neuropathy","Vitreous haemorrhage","Retinal detachment","Acute optic neuritis","Ophthalmic artery occlusion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundoscopy for retinal pallor and cherry-red spot","Assessment for a relative afferent pupillary defect","ESR, CRP, and platelets to screen for giant cell arteritis, with temporal artery biopsy if suspected","Carotid Doppler ultrasound to identify an embolic source","Cardiac evaluation including ECG and echocardiography","Blood pressure, glucose, and lipid assessment; OCT and fluorescein angiography as adjuncts"],"redFlags":["Time-critical emergency — inner retinal infarction becomes irreversible within a short window, so treat and refer immediately","Suspected giant cell arteritis threatens the fellow eye and requires urgent systemic treatment without waiting for biopsy","It is a warning of systemic embolic disease with high risk of subsequent cerebral stroke","Bilateral or ophthalmic artery involvement suggesting a major vascular or arteritic process"],"prognosis":"Visual prognosis is generally poor, with permanent severe visual loss common; the main priority becomes preventing a devastating cerebral stroke and protecting the fellow eye.","pitfalls":["Failing to consider and treat giant cell arteritis in an older patient, risking loss of the second eye","Treating it as an isolated eye problem rather than initiating urgent stroke and vascular workup","Dilating or delaying assessment and missing the narrow reperfusion window","Confusing it with vein occlusion, which shows haemorrhages rather than retinal pallor"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"central_retinal_vein_occlusion":{"name":"Central retinal vein occlusion","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CRVO","central retinal venous occlusion","retinal vein thrombosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically presents as sudden, painless, unilateral loss or blurring of vision, most often in older patients with vascular risk factors such as hypertension, diabetes, or hyperviscosity","Fundus shows the classic 'blood and thunder' picture: widespread retinal haemorrhages in all four quadrants, dilated tortuous veins, cotton-wool spots, and optic disc swelling","Clinically divided into non-ischaemic (perfused) and ischaemic (non-perfused) types, with the ischaemic form carrying much higher risk of neovascular complications","A relative afferent pupillary defect and poor initial visual acuity are markers of an ischaemic occlusion","The most feared complication is neovascular ('90-day') glaucoma from rubeosis iridis; the eye must be monitored for iris and angle new vessels","Macular oedema is the main cause of reduced central vision and is managed with intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy or intravitreal steroid","Ischaemic retina and neovascularisation are treated with pan-retinal photocoagulation to reduce the angiogenic drive","A younger patient with CRVO warrants evaluation for thrombophilia, inflammatory, and hyperviscosity causes"],"pathophysiology":"Central retinal vein occlusion results from thrombosis of the central retinal vein at or posterior to the lamina cribrosa, where the central retinal artery and vein share a common adventitial sheath; arteriosclerotic thickening of the artery compresses the vein and promotes turbulent flow and thrombus formation. The resulting rise in venous and capillary pressure causes widespread retinal haemorrhage, oedema, and, when perfusion fails, capillary non-perfusion and retinal ischaemia. Ischaemic retina releases vascular endothelial growth factor, driving macular oedema and neovascularisation of the retina, optic disc, and iris, which can lead to neovascular glaucoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Central retinal artery occlusion","Ocular ischaemic syndrome","Diabetic retinopathy with widespread haemorrhage","Hypertensive retinopathy","Hyperviscosity retinopathy (e.g. myeloma, Waldenstrom macroglobulinaemia)","Papilloedema or anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination to assess haemorrhage distribution, ischaemia, and disc swelling","Optical coherence tomography to detect and quantify macular oedema","Fluorescein angiography to distinguish ischaemic from non-ischaemic disease and map capillary non-perfusion","Intraocular pressure measurement and gonioscopy to detect angle neovascularisation","Blood pressure, blood glucose, and lipid assessment; thrombophilia and inflammatory screen in younger patients"],"redFlags":["New iris or angle vessels (rubeosis) or a rising intraocular pressure heralding neovascular glaucoma","Relative afferent pupillary defect with poor acuity indicating an ischaemic occlusion","Rapidly worsening vision or pain suggesting progression to neovascular glaucoma","Bilateral or young-patient presentation suggesting a systemic hyperviscosity or thrombophilic disorder"],"prognosis":"Non-ischaemic CRVO often has a reasonable visual outcome, though some cases convert to the ischaemic form over time. Ischaemic CRVO carries a poor visual prognosis and a high risk of neovascular glaucoma; timely anti-VEGF therapy for macular oedema and laser for ischaemia improve outcomes but vision is frequently limited.","pitfalls":["Assuming a good early prognosis without follow-up, missing conversion from non-ischaemic to ischaemic disease","Failing to examine the iris and angle for neovascularisation at each review","Overlooking treatable systemic risk factors such as uncontrolled hypertension","Not considering a hyperviscosity or thrombophilic cause in younger or bilateral cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"cerebellar_ataxia":{"name":"Cerebellar ataxia","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cerebellar syndrome","ataxia","cerebellar dysfunction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A clinical syndrome of incoordination arising from cerebellar dysfunction, causing a wide-based unsteady gait, limb dysmetria, and impaired balance","Characteristic signs include intention tremor, dysdiadochokinesia, dysmetria on finger-nose and heel-shin testing, and scanning dysarthria","Midline (vermis) lesions predominantly affect gait and truncal stability, whereas hemispheric lesions cause ipsilateral limb ataxia","Nystagmus and impaired smooth pursuit and saccades commonly accompany cerebellar disease","Cerebellar ataxia is distinguished from sensory ataxia, in which imbalance worsens markedly with eyes closed (positive Romberg) due to proprioceptive loss","Causes are broad, spanning stroke, tumour, demyelination, alcohol and toxins, hereditary and paraneoplastic syndromes, and metabolic disturbance","Acute cerebellar signs, particularly with headache or reduced consciousness, may indicate a cerebellar stroke or haemorrhage that can cause life-threatening brainstem compression and hydrocephalus","Investigation is guided by tempo of onset, with acute presentations requiring urgent imaging and chronic ones prompting evaluation for hereditary, degenerative, and metabolic causes"],"pathophysiology":"Cerebellar ataxia reflects impaired function of the cerebellum or its afferent and efferent connections, which normally coordinate the timing, force, and accuracy of movement and maintain balance and posture. Damage to the cerebellar hemispheres, vermis, or connecting peduncles disrupts these predictive and error-correcting mechanisms, producing dysmetria, tremor, and incoordination. The underlying pathology may be vascular, inflammatory, neoplastic, toxic, metabolic, degenerative, or genetic.","additionalDifferentials":["Sensory ataxia from dorsal column or large-fibre neuropathy disease","Vestibular disorders causing imbalance","Frontal gait disorder and normal pressure hydrocephalus","Intoxication (alcohol, anticonvulsants, other drugs)","Functional gait disorder","Weakness or extrapyramidal disorders mimicking incoordination"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain focusing on the posterior fossa to identify structural, vascular, or demyelinating lesions","Blood tests for metabolic, endocrine, nutritional (including vitamin B12 and E), and toxic causes","Paraneoplastic antibody panel and search for underlying malignancy when subacute cerebellar degeneration is suspected","Genetic testing for hereditary ataxias in chronic progressive cases","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis when an inflammatory, infective, or paraneoplastic cause is considered"],"redFlags":["Acute cerebellar signs with headache, vomiting, or depressed consciousness suggesting cerebellar stroke or haemorrhage that can cause brainstem compression and obstructive hydrocephalus requiring emergency neurosurgical assessment","Rapidly progressive ataxia suggesting a paraneoplastic, inflammatory, or toxic cause","Associated cranial nerve or long-tract signs indicating brainstem involvement","New ataxia in a patient on potentially toxic drug levels such as anticonvulsants"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends entirely on the underlying cause. Some causes, such as drug toxicity, vitamin deficiency, or an inflammatory process, are reversible with treatment, whereas hereditary and neurodegenerative ataxias are typically progressive. Acute cerebellar strokes can be life-threatening but may recover substantially with timely intervention.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish cerebellar from sensory or vestibular ataxia, leading to the wrong workup","Missing a cerebellar stroke or haemorrhage that can rapidly deteriorate into brainstem compression","Overlooking reversible causes such as drug toxicity, vitamin deficiency, or hypothyroidism","Not pursuing a paraneoplastic cause in subacute progressive cerebellar degeneration"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"cerebral_aneurysm":{"name":"Cerebral aneurysm","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["intracranial aneurysm","berry aneurysm","saccular aneurysm"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most intracranial aneurysms are saccular (berry) lesions arising at branch points of the circle of Willis, and many are asymptomatic until they rupture","Rupture causes aneurysmal subarachnoid haemorrhage, classically a sudden thunderclap headache described as the worst ever, often with vomiting, neck stiffness, photophobia, and reduced consciousness","A non-contrast CT performed early is highly sensitive for subarachnoid blood; if negative but suspicion remains, lumbar puncture looking for xanthochromia is used","An enlarging unruptured aneurysm can cause warning signs such as a painful third nerve palsy from a posterior communicating artery lesion","Risk factors include hypertension, smoking, family history, polycystic kidney disease, and connective tissue disorders","After rupture, priorities are securing the aneurysm by endovascular coiling or surgical clipping to prevent rebleeding, alongside management of complications","Delayed cerebral ischaemia from vasospasm, hydrocephalus, hyponatraemia, and seizures are important complications after subarachnoid haemorrhage","Management of unruptured aneurysms weighs rupture risk (influenced by size, site, and morphology) against the risks of treatment, and small stable lesions may be monitored"],"pathophysiology":"A cerebral aneurysm is a focal outpouching of an intracranial artery wall, typically forming at bifurcations where haemodynamic stress is greatest and the muscular and elastic layers are relatively deficient. Over time, wall degeneration and turbulent flow allow the sac to enlarge. Rupture releases arterial blood into the subarachnoid space, abruptly raising intracranial pressure and irritating the meninges. Subsequent breakdown products can trigger arterial vasospasm days later, causing delayed cerebral ischaemia, while blood interfering with cerebrospinal fluid circulation can produce acute or communicating hydrocephalus.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of thunderclap headache (reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome)","Perimesencephalic non-aneurysmal haemorrhage","Arteriovenous malformation","Intracerebral haemorrhage from hypertension","Bacterial meningitis","Migraine or primary headache"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Non-contrast CT head to detect subarachnoid blood","Lumbar puncture for xanthochromia when CT is negative but suspicion persists","CT angiography or digital subtraction angiography to define aneurysm location and morphology","MRI/MR angiography for detection and follow-up of unruptured aneurysms","Monitoring of sodium, and transcranial Doppler or imaging for vasospasm after haemorrhage"],"redFlags":["Sudden thunderclap headache suggesting aneurysmal subarachnoid haemorrhage requiring emergency imaging","Reduced or fluctuating consciousness and focal deficit indicating a large bleed or rebleeding","New painful third nerve palsy as a warning sign of an enlarging or symptomatic aneurysm","Acute hydrocephalus or deterioration from delayed cerebral ischaemia after haemorrhage"],"prognosis":"Unruptured aneurysms may remain quiescent, but rupture carries substantial mortality and morbidity, with many deaths occurring before or soon after hospital arrival; early securing of the aneurysm and management of vasospasm and hydrocephalus improve outcomes for survivors.","pitfalls":["Dismissing a sudden severe headache as migraine and missing subarachnoid haemorrhage","Relying on a late CT alone, when sensitivity falls and lumbar puncture may be needed","Failing to secure a ruptured aneurysm promptly, risking early rebleeding","Overlooking delayed complications such as vasospasm, hyponatraemia, and hydrocephalus"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"cerebral_palsy":{"name":"Cerebral Palsy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CP","spastic diplegia","static encephalopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of permanent disorders of movement and posture caused by a non-progressive injury or malformation of the developing brain, though the clinical picture can change as the child grows.","It is the most common cause of chronic motor disability beginning in childhood.","Classified by motor type (spastic, dyskinetic, ataxic, or mixed) and by topography (hemiplegia, diplegia, quadriplegia).","Spastic forms are most common; early signs include abnormal tone, persistent primitive reflexes, delayed motor milestones, and early hand preference before one year.","Preterm birth, low birth weight, intrauterine growth restriction, perinatal hypoxic-ischaemic injury, infection, kernicterus, and brain malformations are recognised risk factors.","Frequent comorbidities include intellectual disability, epilepsy, visual and hearing impairment, feeding and swallowing difficulties, communication problems, and orthopaedic complications such as hip subluxation and contractures.","Diagnosis is largely clinical; MRI often identifies the underlying lesion and helps establish aetiology.","Management is multidisciplinary and supportive, aiming to maximise function and participation with therapy, orthotics, spasticity treatment, and orthopaedic care rather than cure.","Because the disorder is static, clear progression or regression of neurological signs should prompt a search for an alternative, evolving diagnosis."],"pathophysiology":"Cerebral palsy results from injury or maldevelopment of the immature brain from any of a range of prenatal, perinatal, or early postnatal insults. In preterm infants, periventricular white-matter injury (periventricular leukomalacia) commonly affects descending motor tracts, producing spastic diplegia; in term infants, hypoxic-ischaemic injury, malformations, stroke, or kernicterus may be responsible. The primary lesion is non-progressive, but secondary musculoskeletal changes such as contractures and bony deformity evolve over time.","additionalDifferentials":["Progressive neurodegenerative or metabolic disorders","Spinal muscular atrophy and other neuromuscular disorders","Dopa-responsive dystonia","Hereditary spastic paraplegia","Structural spinal cord lesions","Global developmental delay without a motor disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain to identify structural or ischaemic lesions","EEG when seizures are suspected","Metabolic or genetic testing if the presentation is atypical or progressive","Assessment of vision and hearing","Hip surveillance imaging to detect subluxation","Evaluation of feeding, swallowing, and nutrition"],"redFlags":["Loss of previously acquired skills or clear neurological progression (suggests an alternative diagnosis)","New or worsening seizures","Signs of aspiration or faltering growth from feeding difficulty","Progressive scoliosis or hip dislocation","Rapidly increasing spasticity or dystonia causing pain or care difficulty"],"prognosis":"The motor impairment itself is non-progressive, and life expectancy is often near normal in milder forms; outcome depends on severity, mobility level, and comorbidities, with severe quadriplegia, epilepsy, intellectual disability, and feeding problems associated with greater disability and shorter survival.","pitfalls":["Labelling a child with cerebral palsy when the course is actually progressive (missing a treatable metabolic or genetic disorder)","Focusing only on motor impairment and overlooking epilepsy, sensory, or feeding comorbidities","Neglecting hip surveillance, allowing silent dislocation","Underestimating cognition in non-verbal children with good comprehension","Delaying nutritional and communication support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"cervical_cancer":{"name":"Cervical Cancer","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["carcinoma of the cervix","cervical carcinoma","cervical malignancy","invasive cervical cancer"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Persistent infection with high-risk human papillomavirus, especially types 16 and 18, underlies the great majority of cases and drives progression from precursor lesions to invasive disease","Squamous cell carcinoma is the commonest histological subtype, followed by adenocarcinoma, which tends to arise higher in the endocervical canal and can be missed on cytology","Abnormal vaginal bleeding, particularly postcoital or intermenstrual bleeding, together with an offensive or blood-stained discharge, are classic early symptoms","Diagnosis is confirmed by biopsy of visible lesions or by colposcopically directed biopsy, not by cytology alone","Staging is clinical and increasingly incorporates imaging, with tumour size, parametrial spread, and nodal involvement determining management","Early-stage disease may be treated with surgery (radical hysterectomy or fertility-sparing trachelectomy in selected women) while locally advanced disease is managed with concurrent chemoradiation","Organised cytology and HPV-based screening programmes with prophylactic HPV vaccination markedly reduce incidence and mortality","Advanced disease may present with pelvic or leg pain, lymphoedema, ureteric obstruction, or fistula formation into the bladder or rectum"],"pathophysiology":"Oncogenic HPV integrates into the host genome of cervical epithelial cells, and its E6 and E7 oncoproteins inactivate the tumour suppressor proteins p53 and retinoblastoma protein, deregulating the cell cycle and permitting accumulation of genetic damage. Most infections clear spontaneously, but persistent infection produces cervical intraepithelial neoplasia that may progress over years to invasive carcinoma. Invasion typically begins at the transformation zone and extends locally into the cervical stroma, vagina, and parametrium, then to pelvic and para-aortic lymph nodes and distant sites.","additionalDifferentials":["Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (pre-invasive disease)","Cervical polyp or ectropion","Cervicitis from infection","Endometrial carcinoma extending to the cervix","Vaginal carcinoma","Metastatic disease involving the cervix"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Colposcopy with directed biopsy of suspicious areas","Histopathological examination of biopsy or cone specimen for subtype and depth of invasion","Pelvic MRI to assess tumour size, parametrial and vaginal extension","CT or PET-CT to evaluate nodal and distant metastatic disease","Examination under anaesthesia with cystoscopy and proctoscopy when local invasion is suspected","Renal function and imaging of the urinary tract to detect ureteric obstruction"],"redFlags":["Heavy or uncontrolled vaginal haemorrhage from a friable tumour","Features of ureteric obstruction with hydronephrosis and renal impairment","Development of vesicovaginal or rectovaginal fistula","Signs of pelvic sidewall involvement with leg swelling and neuropathic pain","Evidence of distant metastatic spread requiring urgent oncology referral"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends strongly on stage at diagnosis: disease confined to the cervix carries high cure rates, whereas parametrial extension, nodal involvement, and distant spread substantially reduce survival. Tumour size, histological subtype, and depth of invasion further influence outcome, and screening plus vaccination continue to shift diagnosis toward earlier, more curable stages.","pitfalls":["Relying on cytology alone and failing to biopsy a visibly abnormal or friable cervix","Missing endocervical adenocarcinoma that lies above the reach of routine sampling","Attributing postcoital or postmenopausal bleeding to benign causes without adequate assessment","Underestimating local extent without appropriate cross-sectional imaging","Overlooking renal impairment from silent ureteric obstruction in advanced disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"cervical_spondylosis":{"name":"Cervical spondylosis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cervical degenerative disc disease","cervical osteoarthritis","cervical spondylotic myelopathy","cervical radiculopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Age-related degeneration of the cervical discs and facet joints causing neck pain, stiffness, and sometimes referred occipital headache; it is extremely common and often asymptomatic on imaging","Nerve-root compression by osteophytes or disc material produces radiculopathy with dermatomal arm pain, sensory change, weakness, and reflex loss, most often at C5/6 and C6/7","Cord compression from a narrowed canal produces cervical spondylotic myelopathy, the most important complication, which can develop insidiously","Myelopathy is suggested by gait disturbance, clumsy hands, loss of dexterity, and upper motor neuron signs (hyperreflexia, Hoffmann's sign, extensor plantars, sometimes bladder involvement)","Most patients with axial neck pain or radiculopathy improve with conservative measures such as analgesia, activity modification, and physiotherapy","MRI is the investigation of choice to demonstrate cord and root compression when myelopathy or persistent radiculopathy is suspected","Progressive myelopathy is an indication for surgical decompression to halt deterioration, since established cord damage recovers poorly","Radiographic degenerative change correlates poorly with symptoms, so imaging findings must be interpreted alongside the clinical picture"],"pathophysiology":"With ageing the cervical intervertebral discs lose height and hydration, transferring load to the facet and uncovertebral joints, which develop osteophytes. These bony spurs, together with disc bulging, ligamentum flavum thickening, and reduced canal diameter, encroach on the neural foramina and spinal canal. Foraminal narrowing compresses nerve roots to cause radiculopathy, while central canal stenosis compresses the spinal cord, producing myelopathy through direct pressure and impaired vascular supply. Dynamic factors during neck movement can aggravate cord compression. The degenerative process is gradual, and neurological compromise may accumulate slowly before becoming clinically apparent.","additionalDifferentials":["Cervical disc prolapse","Peripheral nerve entrapment (e.g. carpal or cubital tunnel syndrome)","Brachial plexopathy or thoracic outlet syndrome","Motor neuron disease and other neurodegenerative conditions","Multiple sclerosis or intramedullary cord lesion","Shoulder pathology (rotator cuff disease) referring pain to the arm","Spinal tumour or infection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the cervical spine to assess cord and root compression","Plain cervical radiographs (including flexion/extension views) for alignment and degenerative change","Nerve conduction studies and electromyography to distinguish radiculopathy from peripheral entrapment","CT for detailed bony anatomy when MRI is contraindicated or surgery is planned","Neurological examination for upper and lower limb signs of myelopathy"],"redFlags":["Signs of cervical myelopathy (gait disturbance, hand clumsiness, hyperreflexia, sphincter dysfunction) requiring urgent specialist assessment","Rapidly progressive neurological deficit","Features suggesting infection or malignancy (fever, weight loss, night pain, cancer history)","Acute deterioration after neck trauma in a spondylotic, stenotic spine"],"prognosis":"Axial neck pain and radiculopathy usually follow a fluctuating but generally benign course with conservative care; cervical spondylotic myelopathy tends to progress in a stepwise manner, and while surgery can arrest decline, neurological recovery of established deficits is often incomplete, making timely intervention important.","pitfalls":["Missing early myelopathy by attributing gait or hand-function changes to age or arthritis","Over-relying on degenerative radiographic findings that are common and often asymptomatic","Delaying decompression in progressive myelopathy until irreversible cord damage occurs","Aggressive manipulation of a stenotic, myelopathic cervical spine"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"cestode_infections":{"name":"Cestode (Tapeworm) Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neurocysticercosis","Taenia","echinococcosis","hydatid disease"],"chapter":242,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1832","clinicalPearls":["Cestodes (tapeworms) cause two clinically distinct patterns: intestinal infection by adult worms (taeniasis) and somatic/larval-tissue infection (e.g., cysticercosis, hydatid disease) (Harrison 22e p.1832).","Neurocysticercosis is the most common parasitic disease of the CNS worldwide; humans acquire it by ingesting food contaminated with Taenia solium eggs, not by eating cysticerci-containing pork (which instead causes intestinal taeniasis) (Harrison 22e p.1833, p.1138).","Seizures are the most frequent manifestation of neurocysticercosis and are associated with inflammation of brain parenchyma around the cysticercus; new-onset partial seizures with or without secondary generalization are the classic presentation (Harrison 22e p.1833, p.1138).","On neuroimaging a cystic lesion with a mural nodule representing the scolex ('dot-in-hole') is diagnostic of neurocysticercosis; lesions evolve through vesicular, colloidal, granulonodular and nodular-calcified stages (Harrison 22e p.1833-1834, p.1138).","CT is more sensitive for calcified lesions, whereas MRI is more sensitive for small cystic lesions, scolices and enhancement; FIESTA/3D-CISS sequences best detect ventricular and subarachnoid cysts (Harrison 22e p.1834).","The enzyme-linked immunoelectrotransfer blot (EITB) using lentil-lectin-purified glycoproteins is >99% specific/sensitive in patients with multiple cysts, but patients with a single intracranial lesion or only calcifications may be seronegative; serum is more sensitive than CSF (Harrison 22e p.1834).","Crude-antigen ELISA antibody tests have high false-positive and false-negative rates and should be avoided for neurocysticercosis (Harrison 22e p.1834).","In endemic-area patients with a single enhancing 5-20 mm round CT lesion, seizures, a normal exam and no systemic disease (no fever, adenopathy or chest abnormality), the lesion is almost always neurocysticercosis (validated in India) (Harrison 22e p.1834).","Antiparasitic treatment of neurocysticercosis is never an emergency; initial management focuses on controlling seizures and hydrocephalus and on excluding intraocular disease before any cysticidal therapy (Harrison 22e p.1834).","High-dose glucocorticoids should always accompany antiparasitic therapy of neurocysticercosis, since dying parasites provoke an inflammatory response that can worsen seizures or hydrocephalus (Harrison 22e p.1834).","Bruns' syndrome is intermittent acute hydrocephalus triggered by positional change, caused by a ventricular cysticercus acting as a ball valve (Harrison 22e p.1833).","In Latin America and immigrants from Latin America, T. solium (neurocysticercosis) is the most common cause of brain abscess / focal CNS mass lesion (Harrison 22e p.1136).","Echinococcosis (hydatid disease) is larval Echinococcus infection: E. granulosus sensu lato causes unilocular cystic hydatid disease; E. multilocularis causes locally invasive, tumor-like alveolar disease (Harrison 22e p.1835-1836).","In cystic echinococcosis the liver is involved in about two-thirds and the lungs in about 20% of cases; alveolar echinococcosis involves the liver in nearly all cases (Harrison 22e p.1836).","Echinococcal cysts often remain asymptomatic for years and are frequently found incidentally on x-ray or ultrasound (Harrison 22e p.1836).","Identification of daughter cysts within a larger cyst, or eggshell/mural calcification on CT, is diagnostic of cystic echinococcosis; 'hydatid sand' is visible protoscolices/brood capsules within cyst fluid (Harrison 22e p.1837).","ELISA/immunoblot serology is positive in about 90% of hepatic hydatid disease but only about 50% of pulmonary cysts, and a negative test cannot exclude echinococcosis (Harrison 22e p.1837).","The fish tapeworm Dibothriocephalus latus (formerly Diphyllobothrium latum) is the longest human tapeworm (up to 25 m) and its avid vitamin B12 receptors can cause B12 deficiency with megaloblastic/pernicious-type anemia and neurologic sequelae (Harrison 22e p.1838-1839).","Rodentolepis nana (dwarf tapeworm, formerly Hymenolepis nana) is the most common human cestode infection and the only one not requiring an intermediate host, perpetuated by intestinal autoinfection (Harrison 22e p.1838)."],"pathophysiology":"Cestodes are segmented flatworms (Platyhelminthes, subphylum Cestoda) whose adult worms live in the gut of the definitive host while larval cystic forms inhabit tissues of the intermediate host. The adult tapeworm attaches to intestinal mucosa by suckers or hooks on a scolex; proglottids bud from the neck to form the ribbon-like strobila and shed eggs. In intestinal taeniasis humans are definitive hosts (T. saginata, T. asiatica, T. solium, D. latus, D. caninum). In somatic disease, ingested eggs release oncospheres that penetrate the gut wall, disseminate via blood, and encyst as a cysticercus (single scolex), coenurus (multiple scolices) or hydatid (cyst with daughter cysts and protoscolices). Cysticercosis follows ingestion of T. solium eggs (fecal-oral, including autoinfection in tapeworm carriers); lodged cysticerci initially provoke little inflammation, but as the cyst degenerates an inflammatory response develops, producing seizures, and accompanying arachnoiditis or ventricular obstruction can cause hydrocephalus. In echinococcosis ingested eggs of canine tapeworms release oncospheres that seed liver, lung and other organs; E. granulosus forms slowly enlarging unilocular hydatid cysts containing brood capsules and protoscolices, while E. multilocularis grows by invasive peripheral budding of the germinal layer like a malignancy. (Harrison 22e p.1832-1837)","additionalDifferentials":["Intestinal taeniasis presenting only with passage of proglottids or vague abdominal symptoms (T. saginata, T. asiatica, T. solium, D. latus, D. caninum)","Megaloblastic / pernicious-type anemia from vitamin B12 deficiency in D. latus fish tapeworm infection","Seizure disorder / epilepsy of other cause in regions where neurocysticercosis is endemic","Obstructive or communicating hydrocephalus from ventricular or subarachnoid cysticerci","Sparganosis presenting as a migrating subcutaneous, periorbital or CNS mass (Spirometra plerocercoid)","Coenurosis presenting as a CNS or subcutaneous space-occupying cystic lesion (T. multiceps, T. serialis)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculoma / CNS tuberculosis (major cause of focal CNS mass lesions in India and East Asia)","Toxoplasma encephalitis (ring-enhancing CNS lesions, especially in immunocompromised/HIV)","Pyogenic brain abscess","Other tissue helminths producing CNS or hepatic cysts (e.g., schistosomiasis, sparganosis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Primary or metastatic brain tumor (parenchymal neurocysticercosis or spinal cysticercus can mimic intraspinal tumor)","CNS lymphoma","Hepatic neoplasm or simple/biliary hepatic cyst (alveolar echinococcosis mimics a slow-growing liver tumor; cystic hydatid mimics benign liver cyst)","Cholelithiasis (biliary leakage/compression by a hepatic hydatid cyst)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool microscopy for eggs/proglottids; perianal cellophane-tape swab when taeniasis suspected (eggs of the three Taenia species are morphologically indistinguishable)","Examination of mature proglottids or scolex, or molecular testing, to distinguish T. solium from T. saginata/T. asiatica","Neuroimaging (MRI and CT) for neurocysticercosis, including FIESTA/3D-CISS for ventricular/subarachnoid cysts and susceptibility-weighted imaging for calcifications","Enzyme-linked immunoelectrotransfer blot (EITB) on serum for cysticercosis antibody","CSF analysis (lymphocytic, neutrophilic or eosinophilic pleocytosis; protein may be raised; glucose usually normal but occasionally markedly reduced)","Funduscopic / dilated ophthalmologic exam in all patients with suspected neurocysticercosis","Ultrasound, CT or MRI with WHO ultrasound staging (CL, CE1-CE5) for hepatic cystic echinococcosis; chest x-ray/CT for pulmonary cysts","ELISA/immunoblot serology for echinococcosis (cannot exclude disease if negative)","PET to follow disease activity in alveolar echinococcosis"],"redFlags":["New-onset seizures or focal neurologic deficit in a patient from a cysticercosis-endemic area (e.g., Latin America, sub-Saharan Africa, China, India, Southeast Asia)","Signs of raised intracranial pressure or obstructive hydrocephalus (headache, vomiting, papilledema, altered mental status) from intraventricular/subarachnoid cysticerci","Bruns' syndrome: intermittent acute hydrocephalus precipitated by head position change","Subarachnoid/basal cysticerci causing chronic meningitis, arachnoiditis, vasculitis, infarction or hemorrhage","Sudden pruritus, urticaria, eosinophilia or anaphylaxis from rupture/leakage of a hydatid cyst","Pulmonary hydatid rupture into the bronchial tree (sudden expectoration of cyst fluid/membranes) or pleura (hydatid empyema)"],"prognosis":"Intestinal tapeworm infections (taeniasis, R. nana, diphyllobothriasis, dipylidiasis) are usually mild or asymptomatic and respond to a single course of treatment. In neurocysticercosis, single enhancing lesions often resolve and seizure medication can frequently be tapered after about 6 months; calcified lesions carry a higher risk of recurrent seizures, particularly with perilesional edema, and subarachnoid/ventricular (extraparenchymal) disease is more difficult to treat and requires prolonged therapy. Cystic echinococcosis is often curable with stage-appropriate medical therapy, PAIR or surgery, with relapse rates after skilled PAIR comparable to surgery; inactive CE4/CE5 cysts need only periodic surveillance. Alveolar echinococcosis behaves like a malignancy: most cases are diagnosed too late for complete resection and require indefinite antiparasitic therapy, since even after liver transplantation immunosuppression promotes parasite recurrence. (Harrison 22e p.1834-1838)","pitfalls":["Do not aspirate or rupture a hydatid cyst carelessly: spillage of protoscolices can disseminate infection and provoke anaphylaxis; aspiration/PAIR should only be done by experienced operators with albendazole cover and through solid tissue.","Never start antiparasitic drugs in neurocysticercosis with elevated intracranial pressure, and avoid them in diffuse cerebral edema from multiple inflamed lesions (glucocorticoids are the mainstay there).","Always give high-dose glucocorticoid cover when treating neurocysticercosis, because dying cysticerci can worsen seizures and hydrocephalus.","Treating T. solium intestinal taeniasis with praziquantel may occasionally trigger a CNS inflammatory response if occult (cryptic) cysticercosis is present.","Avoid antiparasitic drugs before neurosurgical removal of ventricular cysticerci: they make the cyst friable and harder to extract.","Crude-antigen ELISA antibody tests for cysticercosis are unreliable (high false-positive/false-negative rates) and should not be used.","A negative echinococcosis serology cannot exclude the diagnosis, and sensitivity is especially low (~50%) for pulmonary cysts.","Eggs/proglottids are shed intermittently, so single stool examination may miss intestinal taeniasis; Taenia species eggs are morphologically indistinguishable.","For ocular and spinal cysticercosis, drug-induced inflammation may cause irreversible damage; intraocular disease should be managed surgically.","Infusion of scolicidal agents during surgery is no longer recommended because of hypernatremia, intoxication and sclerosing cholangitis."],"severityClassification":"Cystic echinococcosis of the liver is staged by the WHO Informal Working Group ultrasound classification, reflecting cyst viability: active cysts (CL = cystic lesion with no visible wall, CE1 = visible wall with internal echoes/snowflake sign, CE2 = visible wall with internal septation), transitional cysts (CE3a = detached laminar membranes, CE3b = partially collapsed), and inactive cysts (CE4 = nonhomogeneous mass, CE5 = thick calcified wall). Neurocysticercosis is categorized by parasite location and viability and by lesion stage on imaging (vesicular, colloidal, granulonodular, nodular-calcified); diagnosis uses the Del Brutto revised criteria (absolute, neuroimaging [major/confirmative/minor], and clinical/exposure criteria). (Harrison 22e p.1834-1837)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1832","Harrison 22e p.1136-1138","Del Brutto OH et al: Revised diagnostic criteria for neurocysticercosis. J Neurol Sci 372:202, 2017","Brunetti E et al: Expert consensus for the diagnosis and treatment of cystic and alveolar echinococcosis in humans. Acta Trop 114:1, 2010","White AC Jr et al: Diagnosis and treatment of neurocysticercosis: 2017 clinical practice guidelines"],"crossLinks":["seizure_epilepsy","BRAIN_ABSCESS","LIVER_ABSCESS"]},"chagas_trypanosomiasis":{"name":"Trypanosomiasis (Chagas & Sleeping Sickness)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Chagas disease","American trypanosomiasis","African sleeping sickness"],"chapter":234,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1788-1796","clinicalPearls":["Three trypanosome species cause human disease: T. cruzi causes Chagas disease (American trypanosomiasis); T. brucei gambiense and T. b. rhodesiense cause human African trypanosomiasis (HAT, 'sleeping sickness'). Both are vector-borne neglected tropical diseases (p.1788).","Chagas is transmitted mainly by intradomiciliary triatomine ('reduviid') bugs whose feces, deposited during a nighttime blood meal, carry the infective metacyclic form that enters through skin breaks, mucosae, or conjunctivae; nonvectorial routes include congenital, blood transfusion, organ/tissue transplant, and oral (contaminated food/drink) transmission (p.1788).","Acute Chagas is asymptomatic or a mild febrile illness in >90% of cases; characteristic local swelling at the inoculation site is the Romaña sign (eyelid) or chagoma (skin) (p.1789-1790).","After acute infection T. cruzi persists for life in most patients; 60-70% remain in the indeterminate form (no apparent tissue damage), while 30-40% progress over decades to determinate disease - cardiac (20-30%), digestive (5-20%), or mixed (5-10%) (p.1789).","Chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy produces a dilated cardiomyopathy with fibrosis, segmental contractility loss, left ventricular apical aneurysm, ventricular arrhythmias, and conduction system disease; advanced cardiac damage carries a worse prognosis than other cardiomyopathies including ischemic disease (p.1789-1790).","Digestive Chagas (megaviscera) results from destruction of intramural autonomic ganglion cells: esophageal involvement mimics achalasia and colonic involvement mimics Hirschsprung's disease, with severe constipation, fecaloma, and risk of volvulus and toxic dilation; megacolon is diagnosed when sigmoid/descending colon diameter is >=6.5 cm (p.1789, 1791).","Chagas diagnosis is stage-dependent: detect circulating parasites by blood microscopy after concentration (Strout, microhematocrit) or PCR during acute phase/reactivation; rely on serology (anti-T. cruzi IgG) once parasitemia is subpatent in the chronic phase - two positive tests using different techniques and antigens confirm chronic infection (p.1790).","12-lead ECG (with 30-s strip) is a good screening test for Chagas cardiomyopathy; common abnormalities are right bundle branch block, left anterior fascicular block, ventricular premature beats, repolarization disorders, Q waves, and low QRS voltage (p.1791).","HAT is restricted to sub-Saharan Africa, matching the range of its tsetse fly (Glossina) vector; T. b. gambiense (western/central Africa, humans are main reservoir, slow chronic course) differs from T. b. rhodesiense (eastern/southeastern Africa, zoonotic animal reservoirs, acute course) (p.1792-1793).","T. b. gambiense HAT is a slowly evolving illness with a long incubation (months to years); T. b. rhodesiense HAT is an acute febrile illness with a short (<3-week) incubation and a shorter (weeks to months) course (p.1794).","A trypanosomal chancre may develop at the tsetse bite site (more common in travelers and in T. b. rhodesiense); it is nonpurulent, painful, itchy, and resolves spontaneously in 1-3 weeks (p.1794-1795).","Enlarged cervical lymph nodes are a classic HAT sign (38-85% of patients); posterior cervical involvement is the Winterbottom sign - cervical palpation is essential in suspected HAT (p.1794).","HAT is called 'sleeping sickness' because of CNS (second-stage) sleep disturbances - daytime somnolence, nocturnal insomnia, dysregulated sleep/wake cycle and fragmented sleep - plus neuropsychiatric features (mood/behavioral change, psychosis, motor/extrapyramidal/cerebellar syndromes) that may mimic primary psychiatric illness (p.1794).","HAT staging is by CSF examination: second-stage (CNS) disease is defined by CSF leukocytes >5/uL and/or trypanosomes; T. b. gambiense screening uses serology (card agglutination test, CATT, or lateral flow tests) and confirmation by microscopy of trypanosomes in lymph, blood, skin, or CSF (p.1795).","T. b. rhodesiense diagnosis is usually simpler because parasites are more numerous in blood; wet mounts, thin/thick Giemsa smears, and concentration techniques serve for both screening and confirmation, and parasites may be seen in chancre aspirate (p.1795).","T. cruzi evades immunity to persist as low-grade amastigote replication; immunosuppression (HIV, post-transplant, hematologic malignancy) can cause reactivation with increased parasitemia and CNS/cardiac/skin lesions - clinically manifest reactivation is an AIDS-defining opportunistic infection (~20% risk in untreated HIV when CD4 <100/uL) (p.1789)."],"pathophysiology":"Chagas (T. cruzi): trypomastigotes inoculated via triatomine feces enter the bloodstream, infect nucleated cells (notably cardiac, skeletal, and smooth muscle) and differentiate into amastigotes. An innate response to parasite mucins/DNA drives a predominantly Th1 response with proinflammatory cytokines and CD8+ T-cell activation that reduces parasitemia to subpatent levels in 4-8 weeks (end of acute phase). Immune evasion permits lifelong low-intensity amastigote proliferation. Chronic damage is thought to arise from parasite persistence plus a dysregulated host immune response (Th1/Th2 imbalance, excess proinflammatory cytokines), with secondary microcirculatory abnormalities and dysautonomia; the role of autoimmune mechanisms remains unresolved. In myocardium, chronic inflammation causes cellular destruction and fibrosis, segmental loss of contractility, chamber dilation, apical aneurysm, ventricular arrhythmias, and conduction-system scarring, plus autonomic (vagal/sympathetic) denervation. In the gut, a direct toxic effect on intramural autonomic ganglion cells causes loss of neural cells, motility disorders, and organ dilation (megaviscera). African trypanosomiasis (T. b. gambiense/rhodesiense): these subspecies infect humans because they resist human serum lytic factor apolipoprotein L-1 (T. b. rhodesiense via serum resistance-associated protein; T. b. gambiense via TgsGP glycoprotein and other mechanisms). After tsetse bite and local inflammation (chancre), parasites disseminate through the hematolymphatic system, multiply in blood (with cyclic density fluctuation due to variant surface glycoprotein antigenic switching - a repertoire of ~1000 VSGs) and extravascular tissues (skin, muscle, serous membranes, heart). CNS invasion (second stage) follows weeks-to-months (rhodesiense) or months-to-years (gambiense), with predominantly white-matter perivascular astrocyte/microglial proliferation and Mott (morular) cells containing IgM; the cortex and neurons are spared until terminal stages, and inflammatory lesions are largely reversible so neuropsychiatric features may resolve with treatment (p.1789-1790, 1793-1794).","additionalDifferentials":["Chagas chronic cardiomyopathy: other dilated cardiomyopathies including ischemic heart disease (advanced Chagas carries a worse prognosis) (p.1790)","Chagas megaesophagus: achalasia and other causes of dysphagia (p.1789)","Chagas megacolon: Hirschsprung's disease and other causes of severe chronic constipation (p.1789)","T. b. gambiense HAT systemic features: hypothyroidism and adrenal insufficiency (but thyroid/adrenal function tests are normal) (p.1794)","HAT neuropsychiatric presentation: primary psychiatric illness (mental disorder is a key HAT feature easily misdiagnosed) (p.1794)","T. b. rhodesiense acute febrile illness: malaria (suspect HAT especially if malaria tests are negative) (p.1794)"],"infectionMimics":["Malaria (acute febrile illness overlapping T. b. rhodesiense HAT; HAT suspected when malaria tests are negative) (p.1794)","Other febrile/systemic infections in returned travelers from endemic areas (p.1794)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Achalasia (Chagas megaesophagus) (p.1789)","Hirschsprung's disease (Chagas megacolon) (p.1789)","Dilated/ischemic cardiomyopathy (Chagas cardiomyopathy) (p.1790)","Hypothyroidism and adrenal insufficiency (HAT systemic features, but endocrine tests normal) (p.1794)","Primary psychiatric illness (HAT neuropsychiatric features) (p.1794)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Chagas acute/reactivation: blood microscopy after concentration (Strout method, microhematocrit) or PCR of peripheral blood, CSF, or other body fluids (p.1790)","Chagas chronic: serology for anti-T. cruzi IgG - conventional/recombinant ELISA and immunofluorescence; two positive tests with different techniques/antigens to confirm (third test if discordant); immunochromatographic rapid tests for first-line screening where labs are limited (p.1790)","Chagas congenital: cord/peripheral blood microscopy or PCR in first days/weeks (testing after 4 weeks most accurate; earlier PCR may be falsely positive); serology at 9 months once maternal antibodies cleared (p.1790)","Chagas cardiac staging: 12-lead ECG with 30-s strip; echocardiography and 24-h Holter for chamber dilation, apical aneurysm, ventricular dysfunction, arrhythmias; MRI or electrophysiologic studies as indicated (p.1791)","Chagas digestive staging: barium esophagography and barium enema (first-line); esophageal manometry (p.1791)","Chagas comorbidity workup: screen for other cardiovascular risk factors, immunosuppressive conditions, and other chronic infections (e.g., Strongyloides stercoralis, HIV) (p.1791)","HAT T. b. gambiense screening: card agglutination test for trypanosomiasis (CATT) or lateral flow tests; ELISA/immunofluorescence/indirect hemagglutination in reference labs (p.1795)","HAT confirmation: microscopy of trypanosomes in lymph (cervical lymph node puncture), blood (microhematocrit centrifugation; most sensitive is miniature anion-exchange centrifugation, ~90%), skin biopsy, or CSF; thin/thick Giemsa smears and concentration for T. b. rhodesiense (p.1795)","HAT staging: lumbar puncture with CSF examination (leukocyte count, trypanosomes after centrifugation) (p.1795)","Supportive HAT labs (nonspecific): anemia, thrombocytopenia, elevated C-reactive protein and IgM (p.1794)"],"redFlags":["Chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy with high risk of sudden death - bundle branch block is often the first sign and may be asymptomatic for years before serious conduction disease, arrhythmias, and LV dysfunction; 5-year mortality 2-63% depending on cardiac damage, leading causes cardiac failure and sudden death, then stroke (p.1790)","Chagas digestive determinate disease: dysphagia, regurgitation, severe constipation, fecaloma, volvulus, and toxic colonic dilation (p.1790)","Intramural thrombus or LV apical aneurysm in Chagas cardiomyopathy - risk of cardioembolic stroke (p.1790-1791)","Chagas reactivation in immunosuppression (HIV with CD4 <100/uL, post-transplant, hematologic malignancy): myocarditis, Toxoplasma-like focal brain lesion, meningoencephalitis - AIDS-defining when clinically manifest (p.1789-1790)","Untreated HAT is usually lethal - early diagnosis is crucial; second-stage (CNS) disease with decreased consciousness, dementia, epilepsy progressing to coma and death (p.1794)","T. b. rhodesiense terminal sepsis-like illness with disseminated intravascular coagulation and multi-organ failure, plus more rapid evolution to coma and death than gambiense (p.1794)","Severe second-stage HAT signs warranting non-fexinidazole staging/treatment: mental confusion, abnormal behavior, logorrhea, anxiety, ataxia, tremor, motor weakness, speech impairment, abnormal gait/movements, or seizures (p.1795)"],"prognosis":"Chagas: 5-10% of acute (early) infections resolve spontaneously without treatment; most become chronic, of whom 60-70% never develop tissue damage (indeterminate form) and 30-40% progress over decades to cardiac, digestive, or mixed disease. Acute-phase mortality is low (0.1-5% with oral transmission or myocarditis/encephalitis). Determinate chronic disease 5-year mortality is 2-63% depending on extent of cardiac damage, with cardiac failure, sudden death, and stroke the leading causes; advanced cardiac damage carries a worse prognosis than other cardiomyopathies. African trypanosomiasis: untreated HAT is usually fatal. With properly followed guidelines, >95% of first- and second-stage T. b. gambiense HAT patients are definitively cured (fexinidazole, pentamidine, nifurtimox-eflornithine), overall case-fatality <1% except in very advanced cases; relapses can occur long after treatment, so follow-up every 6 months for at least 2 years is advised. Second-stage T. b. rhodesiense HAT carries a 5-10% risk of death during/after melarsoprol treatment, though relapses are very rare (p.1790, 1796).","pitfalls":["More than 90% of Chagas infections go undiagnosed and are often identified late once chronic complications develop; the majority of infected individuals are asymptomatic (indeterminate form) (p.1790)","International tourists visiting endemic areas are at very low risk of Chagas and travel-related cases are rare - the at-risk group is migrants from endemic regions, not casual travelers (p.1789)","There is no evidence that bedbugs transmit T. cruzi to humans despite laboratory potential (p.1788)","There is no predictor of which chronic-phase Chagas patients will progress to clinical disease (p.1790)","Chronic-phase Chagas PCR has limited sensitivity (50-80%): a negative result cannot be interpreted, so patients with negative PCR but persistent positive serology should still be considered potentially infective (p.1790, 1792)","Early congenital Chagas PCR may be falsely positive (maternal T. cruzi DNA fragments); testing after 4 weeks of age is most accurate (p.1790)","In adults with chronic Chagas cardiomyopathy, benznidazole has no impact on disease progression or mortality, and neither benznidazole nor nifurtimox is effective against digestive complications (KNOWLEDGE only - no dosing) (p.1791-1792)","HAT clinical/biologic features (anemia, thrombocytopenia, raised CRP and IgM) are not specific enough to treat on suspicion - diagnostic confirmation is required in all patients (p.1794)","Absence of fever does not rule out T. b. gambiense HAT (fever is more frequent in travelers than natives) (p.1794)","HAT systemic features may mimic hypothyroidism or adrenal insufficiency but thyroid/adrenal tests are normal; mental disorder may be misdiagnosed as primary psychiatric illness (p.1794)","Trypanids (HAT rashes) are almost impossible to detect on dark skin and have been reported only in Caucasians (p.1794)","HIV co-infection does not appear to increase HAT risk, and the virus's effect on HAT presentation is unknown (p.1794)","Eflornithine is ineffective against T. b. rhodesiense, so melarsoprol remains in use for second-stage rhodesiense disease (KNOWLEDGE only) (p.1796)"],"severityClassification":"Chagas (T. cruzi infection stages, Table 234-1): Acute phase (including congenital, vector-borne, oral, transfusion, transplant) lasting 2-8 weeks, mostly asymptomatic; Chronic indeterminate form (normal exam and ECG, no attributable mortality, lifelong or until determinate phase); Chronic determinate form (cardiac, digestive, or mixed organ damage years to decades after infection); Acute reactivation in severe immunosuppression. Diagnostic procedure of choice by stage: microscopy after concentration / PCR for acute, early congenital, and reactivation; serology (two positive tests) for chronic indeterminate and determinate. HAT staging: First stage (hemolymphatic, no CNS involvement) vs Second stage (CNS/meningoencephalitic), defined by CSF leukocytes >5/uL and/or trypanosomes; nonsevere second stage = 6-99 leukocytes/uL CSF, severe second stage = >=100 leukocytes/uL CSF (p.1790, 1795).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1788-1796","Bern C et al. Chagas disease in the United States: A public health approach. Clin Microbiol Rev 33:e00023-19, 2019","Buscher P et al. Human African trypanosomiasis. Lancet 390:2397, 2017","de Sousa AS et al. Chagas disease. Lancet 403:203, 2024","Lindner AK et al. New WHO guidelines for treatment of gambiense human African trypanosomiasis including fexinidazole. Lancet Infect Dis 20:e38, 2020"],"crossLinks":["dilated_cardiomyopathy","heart_failure"]},"chalazion":{"name":"Chalazion","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["meibomian cyst","tarsal cyst","meibomian granuloma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chalazion is a chronic, sterile lipogranulomatous swelling of a blocked meibomian gland, presenting as a firm, painless nodule within the tarsal plate","Unlike a stye (hordeolum), it is typically non-tender once the acute phase has passed and points towards the conjunctival rather than skin surface","It is strongly associated with blepharitis, meibomian gland dysfunction, and rosacea, and recurrences are common in these patients","A large chalazion can press on the cornea and induce astigmatism with blurred vision","Many small lesions resolve spontaneously or with conservative treatment using warm compresses and lid massage over several weeks","Persistent lesions are treated with incision and curettage through a conjunctival approach, or with intralesional steroid injection","A recurrent chalazion at the same site, or one with atypical features, should be biopsied to exclude sebaceous gland carcinoma","It should be distinguished from an internal hordeolum, which is an acute infective abscess of a meibomian gland"],"pathophysiology":"A chalazion forms when a meibomian gland orifice becomes obstructed and retained sebaceous secretions leak into the surrounding tarsal tissue. This lipid material provokes a chronic granulomatous (lipogranulomatous) inflammatory reaction rather than an infection, producing a firm nodule within the tarsal plate. Predisposing factors include meibomian gland dysfunction, blepharitis, and rosacea, all of which alter meibomian secretions and promote gland blockage.","additionalDifferentials":["Internal or external hordeolum (stye)","Sebaceous gland carcinoma of the eyelid","Basal cell or squamous cell carcinoma of the lid","Preseptal cellulitis (if acutely inflamed)","Pyogenic granuloma","Epidermal inclusion cyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical slit-lamp examination and eversion of the eyelid to assess the tarsal conjunctival surface","Refraction and assessment for induced astigmatism in large lesions","Histopathological examination of excised or curetted tissue in recurrent or atypical cases to exclude malignancy"],"redFlags":["Recurrence at the same site or failure to resolve, raising suspicion of sebaceous gland carcinoma","Associated madarosis (lash loss), lid margin distortion, or induration suggesting malignancy","Rapid growth or ulceration of the overlying skin","Signs of secondary infection with spreading cellulitis"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is excellent; most chalazia resolve with conservative measures or minor surgery, though recurrences are common in patients with underlying meibomian gland disease. Rarely, a persistent or atypical lesion proves to be a malignancy, which is why histology is important in suspicious cases.","pitfalls":["Assuming any recurrent lid nodule is benign and not sending atypical or recurrent lesions for histology","Confusing a sterile chalazion with an infective hordeolum and prescribing unnecessary antibiotics","Overlooking underlying blepharitis and meibomian gland dysfunction that drive recurrence","Missing chalazion-induced astigmatism as a cause of blurred vision"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"chemical_eye_injury":{"name":"Chemical eye injury","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chemical burn of the eye","ocular chemical injury","alkali burn","acid burn","caustic eye injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A true ocular emergency in which immediate, copious irrigation takes priority over history-taking and detailed examination","Alkali burns are generally more damaging than acid burns because alkalis penetrate tissues rapidly by liquefactive necrosis, whereas acids cause coagulative necrosis that tends to limit deeper penetration","Irrigation should continue until the tear film pH returns to neutral, and the fornices must be swept and everted to remove any retained particulate matter (e.g. lime or cement)","The degree of limbal ischaemia (blanching) and corneal clarity are the key prognostic signs, forming the basis of grading systems such as Roper-Hall and Dua","A deceptively white, non-injected eye after an alkali burn can indicate severe limbal ischaemia rather than a mild injury","Later management principles aim to promote epithelial healing, control inflammation with topical steroids, prevent infection, support the ocular surface, and control intraocular pressure","Severe burns can be complicated by limbal stem cell deficiency, symblepharon, corneal melting and perforation, glaucoma, and eventual scarring","Bilateral injury and workplace or assault-related exposures require attention to both eyes and appropriate safeguarding and occupational follow-up"],"pathophysiology":"Chemical injury damages the ocular surface through direct chemical action on cells and the extracellular matrix. Alkalis dissolve cell membrane lipids and penetrate deeply by liquefactive necrosis, reaching the anterior chamber and damaging the limbal stem cells, corneal stroma, trabecular meshwork, and intraocular structures. Acids denature and precipitate proteins, producing coagulative necrosis that can form a barrier limiting deeper spread, though strong acids still cause severe injury. Destruction of limbal stem cells impairs corneal epithelial regeneration, leading to persistent epithelial defects, conjunctivalisation, and scarring, while ischaemia and inflammation predispose to stromal melting and perforation.","additionalDifferentials":["Thermal burn of the ocular surface","Ultraviolet or radiation keratitis (e.g. welder's flash)","Severe infective keratitis or corneal ulcer","Toxic keratoconjunctivitis from other agents","Corneal foreign body or abrasion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Measurement of ocular surface pH before and after irrigation to guide continued irrigation","Slit-lamp examination with fluorescein to assess epithelial defect size and corneal clarity","Assessment and grading of limbal and conjunctival ischaemia","Intraocular pressure measurement, as burns can cause acute and chronic pressure rise","Eyelid eversion and fornix inspection for retained particulate chemical"],"redFlags":["Any chemical exposure requires immediate copious irrigation before other assessment, as delay worsens outcome","Extensive limbal blanching/ischaemia and a hazy or opaque cornea indicating a severe, sight-threatening burn","A deceptively white, quiet eye masking severe ischaemic damage after alkali injury","Rising intraocular pressure, corneal melting, or threatened perforation requiring urgent specialist care"],"prognosis":"Prognosis correlates closely with the initial extent of limbal ischaemia and corneal opacification; mild burns with minimal limbal involvement usually heal well, whereas severe burns with extensive limbal stem cell loss risk chronic surface failure, scarring, and permanent visual loss despite intensive treatment. Prompt and thorough irrigation is the single most important factor improving outcome.","pitfalls":["Delaying irrigation to complete history or examination first","Stopping irrigation before the pH has normalised or failing to check pH","Not everting the lids and sweeping the fornices, leaving retained caustic particles","Underestimating a white, non-inflamed eye that actually reflects severe limbal ischaemia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"chiari_malformation":{"name":"Chiari malformation","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Arnold-Chiari malformation","Chiari I malformation","Chiari II malformation","cerebellar tonsillar ectopia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Chiari malformation describes downward displacement of the cerebellar tonsils (and, in some types, the brainstem and fourth ventricle) through the foramen magnum","The type I form often presents in adolescence or adulthood with a suboccipital headache classically worsened by coughing, straining, or Valsalva","Associated features can include neck pain, dizziness, imbalance, and lower cranial nerve or brainstem symptoms such as swallowing difficulty","A frequent and important association is syringomyelia, a fluid-filled cavity in the cord that can cause a dissociated (cape-like) sensory loss and hand wasting","The type II form is closely linked with myelomeningocele and hydrocephalus and typically presents in infancy or early childhood","MRI of the brain and whole spine is the key investigation, quantifying tonsillar descent and detecting an associated syrinx","Asymptomatic incidental tonsillar ectopia is often simply monitored, whereas symptomatic cases or those with a progressive syrinx may need posterior fossa decompression","Surgical decompression aims to restore cerebrospinal fluid flow at the craniocervical junction and can stabilise or improve symptoms and an associated syrinx"],"pathophysiology":"Chiari malformations involve herniation of hindbrain structures below the foramen magnum. In the type I form the cerebellar tonsils descend into the upper cervical canal, often with a small or crowded posterior fossa, obstructing normal cerebrospinal fluid movement across the craniocervical junction. This altered CSF dynamics is thought to drive syrinx formation within the spinal cord. The type II form is a more complex hindbrain anomaly with caudal displacement of the brainstem and fourth ventricle, strongly associated with open neural tube defects and hydrocephalus. Symptoms arise from direct compression of neural structures and from disturbed CSF flow.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary headache disorders (migraine, tension-type)","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension","Posterior fossa tumour","Multiple sclerosis or other myelopathy","Craniocervical junction anomalies (e.g. basilar invagination)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI brain and whole spine to measure tonsillar descent and identify a syrinx","Cine or CSF flow MRI at the craniocervical junction in selected cases","Assessment for hydrocephalus and, in type II, for an associated neural tube defect","Dynamic craniocervical imaging when bony instability or basilar invagination is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive brainstem or lower cranial nerve dysfunction, including swallowing or breathing problems","Rapidly enlarging syrinx with advancing weakness or sensory loss","Signs of raised intracranial pressure or associated obstructive hydrocephalus","Acute deterioration in an infant with type II malformation and myelomeningocele"],"prognosis":"Many patients with mild or incidental type I malformation remain stable; symptomatic patients often improve or stabilise after decompression, though an established syrinx and any fixed neurological deficits may only partially recover. Type II outcomes depend heavily on the severity of associated anomalies.","pitfalls":["Attributing coughing-related occipital headache to migraine without considering the malformation","Failing to image the whole spine and missing an associated syringomyelia","Over-treating small, asymptomatic incidental tonsillar ectopia","Overlooking coexisting hydrocephalus or craniocervical instability"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"childhood_obesity":{"name":"Childhood Obesity","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["paediatric obesity","childhood overweight","obesity in children and adolescents"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["In children and adolescents obesity is defined using body mass index relative to age- and sex-specific percentiles rather than fixed adult cut-offs, with obesity typically at or above the 95th percentile.","The vast majority of cases are related to lifestyle and energy balance; a monogenic or endocrine cause is uncommon but should be considered in specific circumstances.","A tall child growing well who is obese is usually exogenously obese, whereas an obese child who is short or growing poorly raises suspicion of an endocrine or syndromic cause.","Ask about diet, physical activity, screen time, sleep, family history, and psychosocial factors, as these shape both cause and management.","Screen for comorbidities including type 2 diabetes, dyslipidaemia, hypertension, non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, and obstructive sleep apnoea.","Acanthosis nigricans is a clinical clue to insulin resistance and increased type 2 diabetes risk.","Management centres on sustainable family-based lifestyle change addressing nutrition, activity, sleep, and behaviour rather than short-term restrictive dieting.","Early-onset severe obesity, particularly with hyperphagia, may suggest a monogenic cause and warrants specialist evaluation."],"pathophysiology":"Childhood obesity results from a chronic positive energy balance in which caloric intake exceeds expenditure, leading to excess adipose tissue. This arises from a complex interplay of genetic susceptibility, dietary patterns, physical inactivity, sleep, and environmental and socioeconomic influences. Expanded and dysfunctional adipose tissue drives insulin resistance, low-grade inflammation, and altered adipokine signalling, which underlie the associated metabolic, hepatic, cardiovascular, and endocrine complications. Rare monogenic and syndromic disorders disrupt central appetite regulation (notably the leptin-melanocortin pathway).","additionalDifferentials":["Exogenous (lifestyle-related) obesity","Hypothyroidism","Cushing syndrome","Growth hormone deficiency","Monogenic obesity (e.g. leptin or MC4R pathway defects)","Syndromic obesity (e.g. Prader-Willi, Bardet-Biedl syndromes)","Hypothalamic obesity (e.g. after CNS tumour or its treatment)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["BMI plotted on age- and sex-specific percentile charts, with blood pressure measurement","Fasting glucose and/or HbA1c and fasting lipid profile","Liver function tests and assessment for fatty liver disease","Thyroid function testing where clinically indicated","Evaluation for obstructive sleep apnoea when symptoms suggest it","Genetic or endocrine work-up for early-onset severe or syndromic obesity"],"redFlags":["Obesity combined with poor linear growth or short stature suggesting an endocrine cause","Symptoms of type 2 diabetes such as polyuria, polydipsia, or an incidental very high glucose","Signs of obstructive sleep apnoea including loud snoring, apnoeic pauses, or daytime somnolence","Features of Cushing syndrome (central obesity, striae, hypertension, growth deceleration)","Early-onset severe obesity with extreme hyperphagia suggesting a monogenic disorder","Hip or knee pain suggesting a slipped capital femoral epiphysis"],"prognosis":"Childhood obesity frequently persists into adulthood and raises the long-term risk of type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and other complications; early sustained family-based lifestyle intervention can improve trajectory and reduce comorbidity.","pitfalls":["Assuming all obesity is lifestyle-related and missing an endocrine, syndromic, or monogenic cause when growth is impaired","Failing to screen for comorbidities such as type 2 diabetes, fatty liver, and sleep apnoea","Focusing on the child alone rather than engaging the whole family in change","Overlooking psychosocial factors, stigma, and mental health in management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"chlamydial_infection":{"name":"Chlamydial Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Chlamydia trachomatis","Chlamydophila pneumoniae","trachoma","LGV"],"chapter":194,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1467","clinicalPearls":["C. trachomatis genital infection is the most prevalent bacterial STI worldwide and is reported more than any other infectious disease in the United States; case counts are heavily dependent on screening coverage (Harrison 22e p.1469).","Most genital infections are silent: up to 80-90% of women and >50% of men with C. trachomatis genital infection lack symptoms, and pharyngeal/rectal infection is typically asymptomatic and may persist for years, sustaining a large transmission reservoir (Harrison 22e p.1469).","C. trachomatis is the most common cause of nongonococcal urethritis (NGU) and postgonococcal urethritis (PGU), implicated in 30-50% of NGU cases (Harrison 22e p.1469-1470).","NAATs are the CDC-recommended diagnostic assay of choice; less-invasive specimens (urine for both sexes, vaginal swabs for women) are acceptable, and self-collected or clinician-collected vaginal swabs are slightly more sensitive than urine in women (Harrison 22e p.1472-1473).","Because of frequent co-infection, testing for or treating chlamydia should be considered in patients diagnosed with N. gonorrhoeae (Harrison 22e p.1474).","Trachoma (serovars A, B, Ba, C) remains a leading cause of preventable infectious blindness worldwide; the WHO SAFE-era programs target elimination as a public health problem by 2030 (Harrison 22e p.1474-1475).","LGV (serovars L1, L2, L3) is an invasive STI presenting classically as a transient painless genital ulcer followed by painful inguinal lymphadenopathy with bubo formation, or as hemorrhagic proctocolitis in MSM (Harrison 22e p.1469, 1472).","C. pneumoniae causes atypical (community-acquired) pneumonia, bronchitis, pharyngitis and sinusitis, accounting for as many as 10% of CAP cases though more typically 1-2%; PCR of respiratory secretions is the preferred diagnostic method (Harrison 22e p.1476).","Psittacosis (C. psittaci) is a zoonosis acquired from birds (psittacines, poultry) presenting as atypical pneumonia or systemic febrile illness; person-to-person transmission has never been demonstrated (Harrison 22e p.1475).","Neonatal exposure during vaginal delivery causes inclusion conjunctivitis (in ~20-30% of exposed infants) and, in 10-15%, pneumonia at 2 weeks to 4 months of age; standard gonococcal ocular prophylaxis does NOT prevent chlamydial conjunctivitis (Harrison 22e p.1471-1472)."],"pathophysiology":"Chlamydiae are nonmotile, gram-negative, obligate intracellular bacteria that lack peptidoglycan (structural integrity depends on disulfide cross-linking of outer-membrane proteins) and are energy parasites using host-cell ATP. They have a unique biphasic growth cycle alternating between the infectious, extracellular-adapted elementary body (0.25-0.35 um) and the metabolically active, non-infectious intracellular reticulate body (0.5-1 um), which divides by binary fission within a membrane-bound cytoplasmic inclusion while preventing phagosome-lysosome fusion; the cycle lasts ~48-72 h, giving a long clinical incubation (generally 1-3 weeks). C. trachomatis has two biovars (trachoma and LGV) and 18 serovars (trachoma A/B/Ba/C; oculogenital D-K; LGV L1-L3); the trachoma biovar parasitizes squamocolumnar epithelium whereas the more invasive LGV biovar involves lymphoid cells. Disease is driven largely by immunopathology rather than direct cytotoxicity: the chlamydial heat-shock protein shares epitopes with human and other bacterial proteins and, with repeated/persistent infection, drives fibrosis and mucosal scarring leading to tubal-factor infertility, ectopic pregnancy and chronic pelvic pain (genital) or cicatricial trichiasis and blindness (ocular). Antigenic variation in the major outer-membrane protein (omp1) permits reinfection because immunity to one serovar is not protective against others (Harrison 22e p.1467-1469).","additionalDifferentials":["Neisseria gonorrhoeae urethritis/cervicitis/proctitis (clinically indistinguishable; frequent co-infection)","Mycoplasma genitalium (now a leading cause of Chlamydia-negative and persistent NGU)","Trichomonas vaginalis and herpes simplex virus as causes of NGU","Mycoplasma pneumoniae and other atypical pneumonia pathogens (for C. pneumoniae)","Crohn's disease of the rectum (mimicked by LGV proctocolitis on sigmoidoscopy and histology)","Adenovirus or HSV follicular conjunctivitis (mimics adult inclusion/oculogenital conjunctivitis)"],"infectionMimics":["Neisseria gonorrhoeae (urethritis, cervicitis, PID, proctitis, ophthalmia neonatorum)","Mycoplasma genitalium urethritis/cervicitis","Trichomonas vaginalis urethritis","Herpes simplex virus (urethritis, proctitis, ulcerative conjunctivitis)","Haemophilus influenzae or Streptococcus pneumoniae neonatal conjunctivitis","Mycoplasma pneumoniae and other atypical CAP agents (for C. pneumoniae pneumonia)","Adenovirus follicular conjunctivitis","Haemophilus ducreyi and other genital-ulcer pathogens (for LGV primary lesion)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Crohn's disease of the rectum (resembles LGV/serovar proctocolitis: granulomas, giant cells, crypt abscesses)","Testicular torsion or testicular tumor (mimic chlamydial epididymitis; torsion must be excluded promptly)","Intrauterine contraceptive device-induced cervical inflammation","Functional urinary symptoms / non-infectious dysuria (acute urethral syndrome in women)","Reactive arthritis / spondyloarthropathy in the differential of HLA-B27-associated joint disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["NAAT (nucleic acid amplification test) on urine, vaginal/cervical, urethral, rectal, pharyngeal or conjunctival specimens - diagnostic assay of choice","First-void urine or vaginal swab self-collection for screening programs and outreach","Microscopy for leukocytes (Gram stain of urethral exudate or cervical mucus; first-void urine pyuria; leukocyte esterase) to support presumptive diagnosis of urethritis/cervicitis","Giemsa-stained smears for intracytoplasmic inclusions (conjunctival scrapings, neonatal specimens; less sensitive than NAAT/culture)","Microimmunofluorescence (MIF) serology - test of choice for LGV and neonatal pneumonia and the serologic gold standard, though labor-intensive and largely research-only","Complement fixation (CF) serology (genus-level only via LPS; fourfold titer rise supports psittacosis)","PCR of respiratory secretions for C. pneumoniae (preferred) and respiratory NAAT for C. psittaci","Cell culture (former gold standard) - now performed only in specialized laboratories","Sigmoidoscopy with rectal biopsy for proctitis/LGV (granulomas, crypt abscesses)","Laparoscopy / endometrial biopsy (plasma-cell endometritis) in suspected salpingitis/PID and perihepatitis","Serovar/molecular genotyping when LGV is suspected (longer treatment duration required)"],"redFlags":["Acute unilateral testicular pain - exclude testicular torsion promptly by imaging or surgical exploration before attributing to epididymitis","Failure of unilateral intrascrotal pain/swelling to respond to antimicrobials - exclude testicular tumor or chronic infection such as tuberculosis","Hemorrhagic proctocolitis with severe anorectal pain in MSM - consider invasive LGV serovars (often with HIV co-infection)","Right-upper-quadrant pain/fever/nausea in a sexually active woman - suspect perihepatitis (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis), associated with extensive tubal scarring","Trichiasis and entropion in trachoma - lashes abrade the cornea leading to ulceration and blindness if untreated","Severe psittacosis pneumonia - can be fatal in up to 10% of untreated cases and may require ICU care, with hepatitis, endocarditis or myocarditis","Untreated maternal infection - risk of neonatal inclusion conjunctivitis and infant pneumonia"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated genital chlamydial infection responds well to recommended antibiotics, and clinically significant drug resistance has not been observed in C. trachomatis; apparent treatment failure usually reflects non-adherence or reinfection. However, untreated or recurrent genital infection causes substantial long-term morbidity through mucosal scarring - PID with tubal-factor infertility, ectopic pregnancy, and chronic pelvic pain - and women bear the greatest burden of sequelae. Reactive arthritis episodes typically last 2-6 months. Untreated ocular disease progresses to blinding trachoma; the WHO estimates ~6 million people have been blinded and ~1.3 million remain blind from trachoma. Untreated psittacosis pneumonia can be fatal in up to 10% of cases. Previous chlamydial infection raises the risk of reinfection, prompting rescreening at 3 months (Harrison 22e p.1469-1475).","pitfalls":["NAATs detect nucleic acid, not viable organisms - residual DNA may yield a positive result for up to 3 weeks after effective therapy, so NAATs should not be used as a test of cure before 3 weeks; routine test of cure is not recommended except in pregnancy.","Single-dose penicillins or cephalosporins used for gonorrhea lack activity against chlamydiae - hence postgonococcal urethritis and the need to co-treat or exclude chlamydia.","Standard gonococcal ocular prophylaxis does NOT prevent neonatal chlamydial conjunctivitis; the most effective prevention is antenatal screening and treatment.","Chlamydial and gonococcal conjunctivitis cannot be distinguished from other neonatal conjunctivitis on clinical grounds - laboratory diagnosis is required.","CF and LPS-based serologic tests identify chlamydiae only to the genus level (cross-reactive across all chlamydiae) and must be interpreted cautiously.","Serology (including a single high IgG titer) over-attributes C. pneumoniae to atypical pneumonia; cross-reactivity limits specificity, and PCR is preferred.","Serology is never recommended for diagnosis of uncomplicated genital cervical/urethral/lower-tract infection or for asymptomatic screening.","Fluoroquinolones are contraindicated in pregnancy; beta-lactams and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole are not active against C. pneumoniae.","Most genital infections are asymptomatic, so clinical recognition of cervicitis requires a high index of suspicion - no genital symptom is specific for chlamydial cervical infection.","C. psittaci is a biohazard category B biothreat agent associated with laboratory-acquired infections."],"severityClassification":"No formal grading scale. Genital C. trachomatis infections are categorized as uncomplicated (lower genital tract) versus complicated (e.g., PID, epididymitis) requiring longer therapy; LGV is treated as invasive disease with extended therapy. Trachoma is described by phase - active trachoma (follicular conjunctivitis) versus cicatricial disease (conjunctival scarring, trichiasis, entropion) - underpinning the WHO clinical signs used for diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1474-1475).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1467","Harrison 22e p.1468","Harrison 22e p.1469","Harrison 22e p.1470","Harrison 22e p.1471","Harrison 22e p.1472","Harrison 22e p.1473","Harrison 22e p.1474","Harrison 22e p.1475","Harrison 22e p.1476","Harrison 22e p.1477"],"crossLinks":["gonorrhea"]},"cholera":{"name":"Cholera","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Vibrio cholerae infection"],"chapter":173,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1325","clinicalPearls":["Cholera is an acute toxin-mediated diarrhoeal disease caused by Vibrio cholerae serogroup O1 or O139 (the serogroups with epidemic potential) that can progress to profound, rapidly progressive dehydration and death within hours (p.1325-1326).","After a 24-48 h incubation in a non-immune individual, illness begins with the sudden onset of painless watery diarrhoea that can rapidly become voluminous; vomiting is common and fever is usually absent (p.1327).","The characteristic stool is non-bilious, gray, slightly cloudy fluid flecked with mucus, containing no blood, with a fishy, inoffensive odour, described as rice-water stool because it resembles water in which rice has been washed (p.1327).","In severe cases (cholera gravis) volume loss can exceed 250 mL/kg in the first 24 h, and muscle cramps from electrolyte disturbance are common (p.1327).","Symptoms parallel the degree of volume contraction: thirst at <5% body-weight loss; postural hypotension, weakness, tachycardia and decreased skin turgor at 5-10%; and oliguria, weak or absent pulses, sunken eyes (sunken fontanelles in infants), wrinkled washerwoman skin, somnolence and coma at >10% (p.1327-1328).","If fluids and electrolytes are replaced adequately, complications are averted and the illness is self-limited, resolving in a few days (p.1328)."],"pathophysiology":"Cholera is fundamentally a toxin-mediated disease. After ingestion (most often via faecally contaminated water, though contaminated food and human-to-human transmission also contribute), V. cholerae colonises the small intestine, a process dependent on the toxin-coregulated pilus (TCP), with cholera toxin, TCP and other virulence factors coordinately regulated by ToxR and by quorum sensing. The organism elaborates cholera toxin, a protein enterotoxin made of a monomeric enzymatic A subunit and a pentameric B-subunit binding moiety. The B pentamer binds GM1 ganglioside on the epithelial cell surface, delivering the A subunit, whose activated form (A1) irreversibly ADP-ribosylates the GTP-binding regulatory component of adenylate cyclase. This upregulates adenylate cyclase, raising intracellular cyclic AMP, which inhibits absorptive sodium transport in villus cells and activates secretory chloride transport in crypt cells, driving accumulation of sodium chloride in the lumen; water follows passively to maintain osmolality, producing isotonic fluid that exceeds the gut's reabsorptive capacity and results in watery diarrhoea. Cholera toxin also enhances secretion via prostaglandins and/or neural histamine receptors. Unless lost fluid and electrolytes are replaced, profound dehydration leads to hypovolaemic shock and bicarbonate loss leads to acidosis. The cholera toxin genes (ctxAB) are carried on the bacteriophage CTX-phi, which uses TCP as its receptor, allowing horizontal transfer to generate new toxigenic strains (p.1326-1327).","additionalDifferentials":["Other secretory/toxigenic watery diarrhoeas: enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli (produces a heat-labile toxin like cholera toxin), and other dehydrating secretory diarrhoeal illnesses to which the term cholera has historically and loosely been applied (p.1325)","Diarrhoea due to non-O1/O139 (noncholera) V. cholerae, which generally do not produce cholera toxin and do not cause large epidemics (p.1330)"],"infectionMimics":["Enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli (heat-labile enterotoxin resembling cholera toxin causing secretory diarrhoea) (p.1325-1326)","Other Vibrio gastroenteritis, e.g. Vibrio parahaemolyticus and non-O1/O139 V. cholerae (p.1330)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Severely dehydrating secretory diarrhoea of non-infectious aetiology, to which the term cholera has at times been loosely applied (p.1325)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool culture on a selective medium such as taurocholate-tellurite-gelatin (TTG) agar or thiosulfate-citrate-bile salts-sucrose (TCBS) agar; the organism must be specifically sought (p.1328)","Direct dark-field microscopy of a wet mount of fresh stool, with serotype discernible by immobilisation with specific antisera (p.1328)","Point-of-care antigen-detection cholera dipstick (rapid diagnostic test) for field use or where laboratory facilities are lacking (p.1328)","Cary-Blair transport medium and/or alkaline-peptone water-enrichment medium if sample processing will be delayed (p.1328)","Vibrios are oxidase-positive; standard Enterobacteriaceae biochemical testing suffices for identification (p.1328)","Supportive labs in dehydrated patients: elevated haematocrit (haemoconcentration) in non-anaemic patients, mild neutrophilic leukocytosis, raised blood urea nitrogen and creatinine (prerenal azotaemia), normal sodium/potassium/chloride, markedly reduced bicarbonate (<15 mmol/L), elevated anion gap and low arterial pH (~7.2) (p.1328)"],"redFlags":["Profound, rapidly progressive dehydration developing within hours (p.1325)","Signs of severe dehydration: unconsciousness, lethargy or floppiness, weak or absent pulse, inability to drink, sunken eyes (sunken fontanelles in infants), >10% loss of body weight (p.1328)","Voluminous ongoing stool losses (volume loss exceeding 250 mL/kg in the first 24 h in severe cases) (p.1327)","Features of hypovolaemic shock, oliguria, somnolence and coma (p.1327-1328)","Acute watery diarrhoea or death from acute watery diarrhoea in a patient >=5 years, even where cholera is not known to be present (p.1328)"],"prognosis":"Death from cholera is due to hypovolaemic shock. Historically the disease carried high mortality, but prompt aggressive fluid repletion and supportive care can obviate this. With adequate fluid and electrolyte replacement, complications are averted and the illness is self-limited, resolving over a few days. Complications derive exclusively from volume and electrolyte depletion and include renal failure due to acute tubular necrosis. Untreated cholera carries very high case-fatality (historically >50% in untreated severe cases), which falls dramatically with rehydration (p.1326, p.1328).","pitfalls":["The organism is not recovered on routine media; V. cholerae must be specifically sought using selective medium, or the diagnosis will be missed (p.1328).","Diagnosis is often syndromic and many endemic countries under-report to the WHO, so the true burden and distribution are unknown and cases may be unrecognised (p.1326).","Cholera should still be suspected in severe acute watery diarrhoea or death even in areas not known to have cholera (p.1328).","Fever is usually absent, so its absence should not dissuade from the diagnosis (p.1327).","Immunity to V. cholerae O1 is not protective against O139 (and vice versa), so prior infection does not guarantee protection against the other serogroup (p.1327)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison stratifies dehydration in cholera into: (1) None or mild (diarrhoea present) - thirst in some cases, <5% loss of total body weight; (2) Moderate - thirst, postural hypotension, weakness, tachycardia, decreased skin turgor, dry mouth/tongue, no tears, 5-10% loss of total body weight; and (3) Severe - unconsciousness, lethargy or floppiness, weak or absent pulse, inability to drink, sunken eyes (sunken fontanelles in infants), >10% loss of total body weight. This dehydration assessment guides the intensity of rehydration (p.1328).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1325","Harrison 22e p.1326","Harrison 22e p.1327","Harrison 22e p.1328","Harrison 22e p.1329"],"crossLinks":["hypovolemic_shock"]},"cholesteatoma":{"name":"Cholesteatoma","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["keratoma","epidermosis of middle ear","unsafe chronic otitis media"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Cholesteatoma is a collection of keratinising squamous epithelium in the middle ear cleft; despite the name it contains neither cholesterol nor a tumour","It is characteristically associated with attic or posterosuperior marginal perforations, which is why this is called the unsafe or atticoantral type of chronic otitis media","Typical symptoms are scanty, foul-smelling ear discharge with progressive conductive hearing loss","Otoscopy may reveal a retraction pocket, whitish debris or granulations and a crust in the attic; a polyp or aural granulation should raise suspicion","It is locally destructive because the matrix and surrounding inflammation erode bone, threatening the ossicles, facial nerve, labyrinth and dura","Congenital cholesteatoma can occur behind an intact drum with no history of infection","Imaging with high-resolution CT maps disease extent and bony erosion, and MRI (including diffusion-weighted sequences) helps detect or confirm residual or recurrent disease","The mainstay of treatment is surgical eradication by mastoidectomy and tympanoplasty, since medical therapy alone cannot cure it"],"pathophysiology":"Cholesteatoma consists of a sac of keratinising stratified squamous epithelium (matrix) that sheds keratin into the middle ear and mastoid, where it accumulates and cannot escape. Acquired disease most often follows persistent Eustachian tube dysfunction that produces a retraction pocket in the pars flaccida or posterosuperior pars tensa; trapped desquamated epithelium enlarges the pocket, or squamous epithelium migrates through a marginal perforation. The expanding mass and its associated chronic inflammation release enzymes and mediators that resorb surrounding bone, progressively destroying the ossicular chain and eroding toward the facial nerve, semicircular canals and intracranial cavity. Superimposed infection produces the characteristic foul discharge and accelerates the destructive process.","additionalDifferentials":["Tubotympanic (safe) chronic suppurative otitis media","Keratosis obturans of the external canal","Aural polyp or granulation from other causes","Middle ear or external canal malignancy","Glomus tumour of the middle ear","Foreign body with surrounding inflammation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Otoscopy and microscopy to identify retraction pockets, keratin debris and granulations","High-resolution CT of the temporal bone to define extent and bony erosion","Diffusion-weighted MRI to detect residual or recurrent cholesteatoma","Pure tone audiometry to document conductive or mixed hearing loss","Culture of discharge to guide treatment of secondary infection","Fistula test if erosion into the labyrinth is suspected"],"redFlags":["Vertigo or a positive fistula sign suggesting labyrinthine erosion","Facial nerve weakness indicating involvement of the facial canal","Severe headache, fever, neck stiffness or altered consciousness suggesting intracranial complication (an emergency)","Sudden worsening of hearing or new sensorineural loss","Postauricular swelling or abscess signalling spread beyond the mastoid"],"prognosis":"With complete surgical removal the disease can be controlled and complications prevented, but cholesteatoma has a tendency to recur or leave residual matrix, so long-term follow-up is essential; neglected disease leads to serious intratemporal and intracranial complications.","pitfalls":["Dismissing scanty foul discharge as trivial and missing an unsafe ear","Failing to inspect the attic and posterosuperior quadrant where retraction pockets hide","Relying on medical treatment alone when surgery is needed for cure","Not recognising an aural polyp as a possible marker of underlying cholesteatoma","Neglecting long-term surveillance for residual or recurrent disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"chorioamnionitis":{"name":"Chorioamnionitis","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["intra-amniotic infection","amnionitis","intrauterine infection","triple I"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Chorioamnionitis is inflammation or infection of the fetal membranes and amniotic fluid, most often from ascending organisms from the lower genital tract","It is strongly associated with prolonged rupture of membranes, prolonged labour, repeated vaginal examinations, and preterm labour","Clinical diagnosis is suggested by maternal fever combined with features such as maternal or fetal tachycardia, uterine tenderness, and purulent or foul-smelling amniotic fluid","Infection is typically polymicrobial, involving genital tract flora including anaerobes, group B streptococcus, and enteric organisms","Management centres on prompt broad-spectrum antibiotics and expediting delivery, as the infection will not resolve while the pregnancy continues","It is an important cause of maternal sepsis and can lead to postpartum endometritis, wound infection, and, rarely, septic shock","For the neonate it raises the risk of early-onset sepsis, pneumonia, and, particularly in preterm infants, neurological injury and cerebral palsy","Delivery does not preclude ongoing infection, so mother and baby require continued observation and treatment after birth"],"pathophysiology":"Chorioamnionitis most commonly results from ascent of bacteria from the vagina and cervix into the normally sterile amniotic cavity, particularly once the membranes are ruptured or the cervix is dilated. The organisms provoke a maternal and fetal inflammatory response with release of cytokines and prostaglandins that can precipitate or accelerate labour. Inflammation of the membranes, cord, and placenta may compromise the fetus directly through infection and indirectly through the fetal inflammatory response syndrome, which is implicated in preterm birth and neurological injury. Less commonly infection reaches the cavity haematogenously or via invasive procedures.","additionalDifferentials":["Maternal fever from another source such as urinary tract infection or respiratory infection","Epidural-associated maternal pyrexia without infection","Placental abruption causing uterine tenderness","Preterm labour without infection","Viral illness in pregnancy","Appendicitis or other intra-abdominal infection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Maternal observations including temperature, heart rate, and continuous fetal heart rate monitoring","Full blood count and inflammatory markers such as C-reactive protein","Blood cultures and cultures of genital tract and amniotic fluid where feasible","Amniocentesis for Gram stain, glucose, and culture in selected preterm cases","Placental and membrane histology and culture after delivery to confirm the diagnosis","Screening for group B streptococcus and other genital pathogens"],"redFlags":["Features of maternal sepsis such as high fever, tachycardia, hypotension, or confusion requiring urgent resuscitation and delivery","Non-reassuring or abnormal fetal heart rate patterns indicating fetal compromise","Foul-smelling or purulent amniotic fluid with significant uterine tenderness","Failure to improve despite antibiotics, suggesting the need to expedite delivery","Signs of neonatal sepsis after birth requiring immediate paediatric assessment"],"prognosis":"With prompt antibiotics and timely delivery most mothers recover fully, though the condition carries risks of postpartum infection, haemorrhage from uterine atony, and rarely septic shock. Neonatal outcome depends on gestation and the severity of the fetal inflammatory response; term infants generally do well with treatment, whereas preterm infants exposed to intrauterine infection face higher risks of sepsis and long-term neurodevelopmental impairment.","pitfalls":["Attributing maternal fever solely to an epidural and missing intra-amniotic infection","Delaying delivery while attempting to treat the infection conservatively","Underestimating the risk of maternal sepsis and uterine atony after delivery","Failing to alert the neonatal team to the risk of early-onset neonatal sepsis","Overlooking anaerobic and polymicrobial cover in the choice of antibiotics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"chronic_and_recurrent_meningitis":{"name":"Chronic and Recurrent Meningitis","system":"Central nervous system","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Chronic meningitis","Recurrent meningitis","Chronic lymphocytic meningitis","Mollaret's meningitis","Basal meningitis"],"chapter":144,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1128","clinicalPearls":["Chronic meningitis is defined by a characteristic neurologic syndrome persisting beyond 4 weeks together with a sustained CSF inflammatory response (white cell count >5/uL); appropriate treatment hinges entirely on identifying the underlying cause.","Five disease categories account for most cases: meningeal infections, malignancy, autoimmune inflammatory disorders, chemical meningitis, and parameningeal infections; some recurrent cases reflect monogenic autoinflammatory disorders.","Cardinal manifestations are persistent nonfocal headache, signs of hydrocephalus, cranial neuropathies, radiculopathies, and cognitive or personality change; classic meningeal signs (Kernig/Brudzinski) are actually uncommon in the chronic form.","Basal meningitis, where exudate collects at the skull base, presents as multiple cranial neuropathies; lower cranial nerves are involved more often because the inflammatory exudate gravitates to the base of the brain.","Spinal involvement produces multiple radiculopathies (radicular pain, sensory loss, weakness, sphincter dysfunction) and may progress to arachnoiditis with clumping of lower nerve roots, again because cells settle at the bottom of the intrathecal space.","Two clinical forms exist: a chronic persistent form and a recurrent form in which all clinical and CSF abnormalities resolve completely between discrete episodes (e.g., Mollaret's meningitis from HSV-2, chemical leak from epidermoid tumor, autoimmune conditions, drug hypersensitivity).","Worldwide, M. tuberculosis is the most common infectious cause, followed by Cryptococcus neoformans; in immunosuppressed patients these may present with little or no headache, fever, or meningeal signs and even a pauci-cellular CSF.","In most chronic (non-recurrent) meningitides mononuclear cells predominate; a neutrophilic CSF after 3 weeks points to Nocardia, Actinomyces, Brucella, certain fungi (Blastomyces, Candida, Histoplasma, Aspergillus, Pseudallescheria, Cladophialophora), or noninfectious causes (SLE, chemical meningitis).","An eosinophilic CSF response suggests parasitic disease (Angiostrongylus, Gnathostoma, Baylisascaris, Toxocara, cysticercosis, schistosomiasis, echinococcosis, toxoplasmosis), coccidioidal fungal meningitis (6-20% eosinophils with lymphocytic pleocytosis), neoplasia, or sarcoidosis.","Meningeal enhancement patterns localize the process: leptomeningeal enhancement follows gyri and basal cisterns (typical of infection), whereas pachymeningeal enhancement is thick, linear or nodular along dura/falx/tentorium (e.g., lymphoma, IgG4-related disease).","In about one-third of cases the cause remains undiagnosed despite thorough evaluation; many causative organisms require weeks of culture, so diagnostic patience is balanced against the risk of progressive neurologic deterioration.","Repeated large-volume lumbar CSF samples (three or more), and if needed cervical cisternal sampling near the basal meninges, raise the yield for fungal and malignant causes; ventricular fluid may appear sterile despite active lower lumbar infection."],"pathophysiology":"Chronic inflammation of the meninges (pia, arachnoid, dura) generates neurologic deficits whose pattern is determined by the anatomic site of inflammation and its consequences. CSF, produced by the choroid plexus and circulating from the ventricles through the basal cisterns over the convexities to arachnoid villi, provides a conduit for rapid spread of infectious or infiltrative processes over the brain, cord, and nerve roots; organisms may also track along perivascular (Virchow-Robin) spaces into brain parenchyma. Intracranial inflammation stimulates meningeal nociceptive fibers (headache, neck/back pain) and may obstruct CSF flow at the cerebral aqueduct or arachnoid villi, causing hydrocephalus and raised intracranial pressure. Inflammatory exudate seeded around the brainstem and cranial nerves at the skull base produces basal meningitis with multiple cranial neuropathies, while spinal exudate injures nerve roots and may encircle the cord to cause myelopathy. Vasculitis of subarachnoid vessels can lead to infarction and cognitive/behavioral decline. A recently identified dural lymphatic system drains the dura, but its role in chronic meningitis is not yet established.","additionalDifferentials":["Infectious: M. tuberculosis, Cryptococcus neoformans/gattii, syphilis, Lyme disease (Bannwarth's syndrome), Brucella, Nocardia, Actinomyces, Whipple's disease, partially treated bacterial meningitis, parameningeal infection","Fungal: Coccidioides, Histoplasma, Blastomyces, Candida, Aspergillus, Sporothrix","Parasitic/helminthic: cysticercosis (Taenia solium), Gnathostoma, Angiostrongylus, Baylisascaris, Toxoplasma, trypanosomiasis, Acanthamoeba/Balamuthia","Viral: HSV-2 (Mollaret's, recurrent), mumps, lymphocytic choriomeningitis virus, enteroviruses (in agammaglobulinemia/B-cell depletion), HIV acute retroviral syndrome","Malignancy: carcinomatous/lymphomatous meningitis (breast, lung, stomach, pancreas, melanoma, leukemia, gliomatosis)","Inflammatory/autoimmune: neurosarcoidosis, Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada syndrome, Behcet's, SLE, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, IgG4-related hypertrophic pachymeningitis, primary CNS vasculitis, Sjogren's","Monogenic autoinflammatory: TRAPS, CAPS, NOMID, complement factor I deficiency","Chemical: leakage from epidermoid/dermoid tumor, craniopharyngioma, cholesteatoma; drug hypersensitivity (NSAIDs, sulfonamides, IVIG, others)"],"infectionMimics":["Carcinomatous or lymphomatous meningitis (CSF seeding by malignant cells)","Neurosarcoidosis (low CSF glucose can mimic tuberculous/fungal meningitis)","Chemical meningitis from leaking epidermoid/dermoid cyst or craniopharyngioma","Drug-induced aseptic meningitis","Primary CNS or systemic vasculitis with meningitis and stroke"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Subarachnoid hemorrhage in the preceding week presenting with xanthochromia and pleocytosis","IgG4-related hypertrophic pachymeningitis","Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada syndrome and Behcet's syndrome (recurrent forms)","Multiple sclerosis and Sjogren's syndrome"],"additionalInvestigations":["Measure opening CSF pressure; brain imaging before LP if raised ICP is suspected, as a mass or obstructive hydrocephalus risks herniation","CSF cell count and differential, glucose, protein, Gram's stain; bacterial, fungal, and mycobacterial cultures (large volumes)","CSF VDRL (highly specific, low sensitivity); serum treponemal antibodies; consider empiric neurosyphilis treatment if CSF VDRL negative but serum positive with unexplained pleocytosis","Cryptococcal antigen, India ink/fungal wet mount, oligoclonal bands, and cytology/flow cytometry for malignant cells","Pathogen-specific PCR; broad-range 16s rRNA PCR (bacteria, useful in partially treated meningitis), 18s/28s rRNA PCR (fungi), CSF beta-glucan assay, and unbiased metagenomic next-generation sequencing for enigmatic cases","Contrast-enhanced MRI/CT of brain and spinal cord to detect meningeal enhancement, parameningeal infection, cord encasement, or nodular deposits; angiography if arteritis/stroke suspected","Systemic workup: tuberculin skin test or interferon-gamma release assay, chest radiograph or chest CT/MRI and sputum exam, urinalysis, CBC, renal/liver function, alkaline phosphatase, ESR, ANA, anti-Ro/anti-La, rheumatoid factor, serum IgG4","FDG-PET to locate a systemic biopsy site in suspected carcinomatous meningitis or sarcoidosis; genetic testing for monogenic autoinflammatory disorders","Meningeal biopsy targeting contrast-enhancing regions when CSF is non-diagnostic and the patient is severely disabled, needs ventricular decompression, or is deteriorating rapidly"],"redFlags":["Signs of raised intracranial pressure (vomiting, drowsiness, papilledema, visual loss, impaired upgaze, sixth-nerve palsy) warranting imaging before lumbar puncture","Rapidly progressive neurologic deterioration requiring urgent treatment rather than awaiting culture results","Multiple progressive cranial neuropathies or evolving radiculopathy/myelopathy indicating widespread CSF dissemination","Focal cerebral signs suggesting brain abscess, parameningeal infection, or infarction","Immunosuppression or untreated HIV, where meningeal signs are attenuated and any persistent headache or mental status change mandates CSF examination","Low CSF glucose with lymphocytic pleocytosis, prompting empiric coverage for tuberculosis given the potential for devastation within weeks"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the underlying etiology and the speed of diagnosis and treatment; many causes are effectively treatable, but untreated disease produces progressive, potentially fatal damage to the CNS, cranial nerves, and roots. Even with treatment, tuberculous meningitis carries high rates of morbidity. Fatal levels of raised intracranial pressure can occur (notably in cryptococcal meningitis) even without ventricular enlargement, and repetitive or continuous CSF drainage may be needed to prevent abrupt deterioration and death. Some recurrent forms (e.g., chronic benign lymphocytic meningitis) resolve over 2-6 months.","pitfalls":["Performing lumbar puncture before imaging when raised ICP from a mass or obstructive hydrocephalus exists risks brain herniation","Assuming a normal or pauci-cellular CSF excludes meningitis in immunosuppressed or HIV patients, in whom signs are attenuated","Relying on a single small-volume CSF sample; fungal and malignant causes often require repeated large-volume sampling, and some organisms take weeks to grow in culture","Interpreting a transient response to empiric glucocorticoids as confirmation of a noninfectious cause, since tuberculosis, cysticercosis, and lymphoma may also respond temporarily","Overlooking false-negative cryptococcal antigen or CSF VDRL results due to the prozone effect at high antibody/antigen titers","Attributing dural enhancement to disease when it can be a benign post-LP, post-neurosurgical, post-concussion, or post-CSF-leak finding","Failing to recognize that ventricular CSF can appear sterile while active infection persists in the lower lumbar space"],"severityClassification":"No formal grading scale is given; severity is gauged clinically by the rate of neurologic progression, the presence of raised intracranial pressure or hydrocephalus, and the degree of disability, which together determine whether to await culture results or proceed to meningeal biopsy and empiric therapy.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1128","Harrison 22e p.1129","Harrison 22e p.1130","Harrison 22e p.1131","Harrison 22e p.1132","Harrison 22e p.1133","Harrison 22e p.1134","Harrison 22e p.1135"],"crossLinks":[]},"chronic_constipation":{"name":"Chronic constipation","system":"GI / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chronic idiopathic constipation","functional constipation","slow-transit constipation","defecatory dysfunction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Constipation is defined by symptoms including infrequent stools, straining, hard stools, and a sense of incomplete evacuation rather than by stool frequency alone","Most chronic constipation is functional and can be categorised as normal-transit, slow-transit, or a defecatory (pelvic floor) disorder","A careful history and rectal examination are essential and can reveal a defecatory disorder such as dyssynergic defaecation","Secondary causes including hypothyroidism, hypercalcaemia, diabetes, neurological disease, and constipating medications should be identified and treated","Increasing dietary fibre and fluid with graded use of osmotic and stimulant laxatives is the mainstay for most patients","Defecatory disorders respond poorly to laxatives alone and are best treated with biofeedback-directed pelvic floor retraining","Anorectal manometry with a balloon expulsion test and, where needed, transit studies guide management of refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"Chronic constipation reflects disordered colonic transit, defecatory mechanics, or both. In slow-transit constipation, reduced colonic propulsive activity, often linked to abnormalities of enteric neurons and interstitial cells of Cajal, delays stool passage. In defecatory disorders, impaired coordination of the pelvic floor and anal sphincter (dyssynergia) or structural pelvic floor abnormalities prevent effective evacuation. Normal-transit constipation, the most common type, involves normal stool movement with perceived difficulty, often overlapping with irritable bowel syndrome. Secondary causes act through metabolic, endocrine, neurological, or pharmacological effects on gut motility.","additionalDifferentials":["Colorectal cancer or luminal obstruction","Hypothyroidism, hypercalcaemia, or hypokalaemia","Diabetic or other autonomic neuropathy","Neurological disease such as Parkinson disease or spinal cord lesions","Medication effects (opioids, anticholinergics, calcium and iron supplements)","Irritable bowel syndrome with constipation","Pelvic floor dyssynergia or rectal prolapse"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Digital rectal examination to assess sphincter tone and defecatory function","Blood tests including thyroid function, calcium, glucose, and electrolytes","Colonoscopy when alarm features or age-appropriate screening indications are present","Colonic transit study for suspected slow transit","Anorectal manometry and balloon expulsion test for suspected defecatory disorder","Defecography in selected refractory cases"],"redFlags":["Rectal bleeding or iron-deficiency anaemia","Unintentional weight loss","New or progressive constipation in an older patient","Family history of colorectal cancer or inflammatory bowel disease","Features of obstruction such as severe pain, distension, and vomiting"],"prognosis":"Chronic constipation is generally benign but frequently persistent and can significantly reduce quality of life; most patients improve with lifestyle measures and laxatives, and biofeedback is effective for defecatory disorders, though a minority remain refractory.","pitfalls":["Escalating laxatives without recognising an underlying defecatory disorder that needs biofeedback","Failing to exclude secondary and structural causes before treating symptomatically","Overlooking constipating medications, particularly opioids","Omitting the rectal examination, which often reveals the mechanism of constipation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"chronic_granulomatous_disease":{"name":"Chronic granulomatous disease","system":"Immunology / Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CGD","NADPH oxidase deficiency","phagocyte oxidative burst defect"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Middleton's Allergy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["CGD is a primary phagocyte disorder in which defective NADPH oxidase prevents the respiratory burst, so neutrophils and macrophages ingest but cannot kill certain microbes","Infections are characteristically caused by catalase-positive organisms, notably Staphylococcus aureus, Serratia, Burkholderia, Nocardia, and the mould Aspergillus","Recurrent deep-seated infections-pneumonia, lymphadenitis, liver and other abscesses, and osteomyelitis-are typical and often present in early childhood","Exuberant granuloma formation reflects dysregulated inflammation and can obstruct hollow viscera, causing gastrointestinal or genitourinary problems that mimic inflammatory bowel disease","Most cases are X-linked (gp91phox/CYBB) and therefore affect boys, with autosomal recessive forms tending to run a milder course","Diagnosis relies on tests of the oxidative burst, historically the nitroblue tetrazolium test and now the more quantitative dihydrorhodamine (DHR) flow-cytometric assay, with genetic confirmation","Long-term prophylaxis against bacteria and fungi plus interferon-gamma reduces serious infections, and prompt aggressive treatment of intercurrent infection is essential","Invasive aspergillosis and other fungal infections are leading causes of death, so a high index of suspicion and early antifungal therapy are important","Allogeneic haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation is the established curative option, and gene therapy is an evolving approach"],"pathophysiology":"CGD is caused by mutations in any of the subunits of the phagocyte NADPH oxidase complex (gp91phox, p22phox, p47phox, p67phox, p40phox). This enzyme normally generates superoxide and downstream reactive oxygen species that drive the microbicidal respiratory burst within the phagosome. Without a functional oxidase, phagocytes engulf organisms but fail to kill catalase-positive microbes that can neutralise their own hydrogen peroxide. Persistence of viable organisms and impaired resolution of inflammation lead to chronic granuloma formation and tissue damage. The most common form is X-linked (CYBB), with autosomal recessive forms accounting for the remainder.","additionalDifferentials":["Other primary immunodeficiencies with recurrent infection (e.g. leukocyte adhesion deficiency, hyper-IgE syndrome)","Congenital or acquired neutropenia","Inflammatory bowel disease (granulomatous colitis can mimic Crohn disease)","Mycobacterial infection and Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease","Cystic fibrosis or bronchiectasis with recurrent pulmonary infection","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency causing a CGD-like oxidative defect"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dihydrorhodamine (DHR) flow-cytometric assay of the neutrophil oxidative burst","Nitroblue tetrazolium test where DHR is unavailable","Genetic testing to identify the affected oxidase subunit and inheritance pattern","Microbiologic sampling and culture of infected sites, including for fungi and Nocardia","Cross-sectional imaging to localise deep abscesses and granulomatous complications","Carrier testing and genetic counselling for family members"],"redFlags":["Invasive fungal infection, particularly aspergillosis, with respiratory or systemic decline","Deep abscess, sepsis, or rapidly progressive pneumonia requiring urgent intervention","Bowel or urinary obstruction from granulomatous inflammation","Infection with Burkholderia or Nocardia signalling severe, potentially fatal disease"],"prognosis":"Prophylactic antibacterial and antifungal therapy with interferon-gamma has substantially improved survival, though serious infections and inflammatory complications still occur. Invasive fungal disease remains a leading cause of death. Haematopoietic stem-cell transplantation can be curative, especially with well-matched donors performed before major organ damage.","pitfalls":["Underestimating fungal risk and delaying antifungal therapy in a febrile CGD patient","Mistaking granulomatous colitis for ordinary inflammatory bowel disease and missing the underlying immunodeficiency","Stopping antimicrobial and antifungal prophylaxis during periods of apparent wellness","Failing to pursue genetic diagnosis, carrier testing, and consideration of curative transplantation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"chronic_hepatitis":{"name":"Chronic Hepatitis","system":"Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chronic hepatitis B","chronic hepatitis C","autoimmune hepatitis"],"chapter":352,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2673","clinicalPearls":["Chronic hepatitis is defined as hepatic inflammation and necrosis continuing for at least 6 months; milder forms are nonprogressive or slowly progressive, whereas more severe forms cause scarring and architectural reorganization that, when advanced, lead ultimately to cirrhosis (p.2673).","Classification rests on three axes: (1) cause, (2) histologic activity or grade (necroinflammatory activity), and (3) degree of progression based on fibrosis or stage; neither clinical features alone nor histology alone are sufficient to characterize disease (p.2673).","Grade reflects necroinflammatory activity (periportal/piecemeal necrosis or interface hepatitis, bridging necrosis, focal lobular necrosis, portal inflammation) and is scored by the histologic activity index (HAI, used in the U.S.) or METAVIR (used in Europe); chronic hepatitis is graded mild, moderate, or severe (p.2673-2674).","Stage reflects fibrosis on a numerical scale (0-6 HAI or 0-4 METAVIR); when fibrous septa surround parenchymal nodules and alter normal lobular architecture, the lesion is defined as cirrhosis (p.2674).","Hyperglobulinemia and circulating autoantibodies are distinctly ABSENT in chronic hepatitis B, in contrast to autoimmune hepatitis; this helps distinguish viral from autoimmune disease (p.2674).","Autoimmune hepatitis has a strong female predominance (~75% of cases) with a bimodal age distribution (one peak age <30, a second often >60); fatigue is the most common symptom (~85%) and ~25% are diagnosed when asymptomatic on abnormal liver tests (p.2694-2695).","Type I AIH (classic, North America/northern Europe, young women) is associated with ANA in a homogeneous pattern and anti-smooth-muscle antibody (ASMA); type II AIH (often children, Mediterranean) is associated with anti-LKM1 (directed against cytochrome P450 2D6) rather than ANA (p.2695).","Polyclonal hypergammaglobulinemia (>2.5 g/dL) is common in AIH; smooth-muscle antibodies are detected in 63% of cases and, combined with a positive ANA, have high specificity (p.2695).","Anti-LKM1 (against cytochrome P450 2D6) seen in type II AIH is the same antibody found in some patients with chronic hepatitis C; rarely AIH patients with hyperglobulinemia give false-positive anti-HCV immunoassays (p.2685-2695).","Cirrhosis from any cause of chronic hepatitis carries a risk of hepatocellular carcinoma; in cirrhotic hepatitis C the annual HCC rate is 1-4%, occurring primarily after 30 or more years of infection, warranting HCC surveillance (p.2685).","Liver biopsy is imperative to diagnose AIH; classic features include interface hepatitis extending beyond portal tracts, plasma cells in the infiltrate, central vein perivenulitis, hepatocellular rosette formation, and frequent septal/bridging fibrosis and cirrhosis (p.2695)."],"pathophysiology":"Chronic hepatitis comprises liver disorders of varying cause in which hepatic inflammation and necrosis persist for at least 6 months. Broad categories include chronic viral hepatitis (B, B plus D, C), drug- or toxin-induced chronic hepatitis, alcohol-associated liver disease, metabolic dysfunction-related (steatotic) liver disease, and autoimmune chronic hepatitis; idiopathic/cryptogenic cases are also believed largely autoimmune. In chronic hepatitis B, all forms can be progressive but progression occurs primarily in patients with active HBV replication, and HCC risk is highest with continued high-level replication. In autoimmune hepatitis, progressive injury results from a cell-mediated immunologic attack on hepatocytes in the setting of lost/failed tolerance for self-liver antigens; predisposition is inherited (HLA-B8, DR3, DR4, DRB1*0301/*0401) while liver specificity is triggered by environmental factors (chemicals, drugs such as minocycline, viruses, and reported triggers including COVID-19 and COVID-19 vaccination); molecular mimicry activates infiltrating CD4+ T cells and impaired regulatory CD4+CD25+ T-cell control contributes (p.2673, 2693-2694).","additionalDifferentials":["Wilson disease (especially in adolescents, before neurologic signs/Kayser-Fleischer rings)","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Primary biliary cholangitis","Cryptogenic cirrhosis","Celiac disease","Ischemic liver disease","Budd-Chiari syndrome (hepatic venous outflow obstruction)","Immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) hepatitis"],"infectionMimics":["Chronic hepatitis B","Chronic hepatitis C","Chronic hepatitis D (delta) superimposed on hepatitis B","Hepatitis E (chronic in immunosuppressed/post-transplant hosts)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Autoimmune hepatitis","Drug- or toxin-induced liver injury (including minocycline)","Alcohol-associated hepatitis","Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH/NASH)","Wilson disease","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Primary biliary cholangitis","Rheumatologic disorders (rheumatoid arthritis, systemic lupus erythematosus) given extrahepatic features and autoantibodies"],"additionalInvestigations":["Liver biochemical tests (AST, ALT, alkaline phosphatase, bilirubin, albumin, prothrombin time)","HBV markers: HBsAg, IgG anti-HBc, HBeAg/anti-HBe, HBV DNA","HCV markers: anti-HCV, HCV RNA","HDV markers: anti-HDV, HDV RNA","Autoantibodies: ANA (homogeneous), anti-smooth-muscle antibody (anti-actin), anti-LKM1, anti-SLA, anti-LC1, F-actin, atypical pANCA","Serum immunoglobulins/globulins (hypergammaglobulinemia >2.5 g/dL in AIH; IgG level)","Liver biopsy for grading (HAI/METAVIR) and staging of fibrosis","Noninvasive fibrosis assessment: FIB-4, Enhanced Liver Fibrosis (ELF) score, liver elastography/stiffness imaging","Ceruloplasmin, serum/urinary copper and liver copper (to exclude Wilson disease)"],"redFlags":["Complications of cirrhosis: ascites, edema, bleeding gastroesophageal varices, hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, hypersplenism","Intermittent deepening of jaundice with worsening malaise/anorexia/fatigue, often coinciding with virologic reactivation, which may cause hepatic decompensation when superimposed on cirrhosis","Severe AIH: aminotransferases >10x normal, marked hyperglobulinemia, multilobular collapse or bridging necrosis on histology (6-month untreated mortality up to ~40%)","Failure of serum bilirubin to improve after 2 weeks of therapy in AIH (poor prognostic sign)","Multilobular collapse at initial presentation in AIH (poor prognostic sign)","Development of hepatocellular carcinoma in established cirrhosis"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on grade and stage. In untreated chronic hepatitis B, 5-year survival was 97% for mild, 86% for moderate-to-severe, and 55% for those with cirrhosis (15-year survival 77%, 66%, 40% respectively); all forms can progress, primarily with active HBV replication. In chronic hepatitis C, liver histology is the best prognostic indicator; mild disease has an excellent prognosis, while moderate-to-severe necroinflammation or bridging fibrosis makes cirrhosis highly likely over 10-20 years. In compensated HCV cirrhosis, 10-year survival is ~80%, with mortality 2-6%/year, decompensation 4-5%/year, and HCC 1-4%/year. In autoimmune hepatitis, 10-year survival is 80-98% for treated versus 67% for untreated patients; severe symptomatic disease carries up to ~40% 6-month mortality without therapy; in a Dutch cohort, mortality exceeded the general population only in patients with cirrhosis (p.2674, 2685-2686, 2694-2695).","pitfalls":["Histopathologic-only classification (chronic persistent/lobular vs chronic active hepatitis) has been replaced because its presumed prognostic implications were not corroborated; classification now combines cause, grade, and stage (p.2673).","Laboratory features of chronic hepatitis B do not reliably distinguish histologically mild from severe disease (p.2674).","Low-titer ANA or ASMA occurs in other liver diseases including MASLD (~20% ANA-positive, ~14% ASMA-positive in one series) and viral hepatitis, so positive autoantibodies are not specific for AIH (p.2695).","Rarely AIH patients with hyperglobulinemia have false-positive anti-HCV; conversely some confirmed chronic hepatitis C patients have circulating anti-LKM1, blurring the distinction between viral and autoimmune disease (p.2685-2695).","ICI hepatitis resembles AIH but lacks female preponderance and is less likely to have ANA (low-titer if present) or plasma cell infiltrates (p.2695).","Overlapping types of hepatitis (e.g., viral plus steatotic, alcohol plus nonalcohol steatotic) are encountered not infrequently given the high prevalence of steatotic liver disease (p.2673)."],"severityClassification":"Graded by necroinflammatory activity as mild, moderate, or severe using the histologic activity index (HAI, 0-18) or METAVIR (A0-A3). Staged by fibrosis: HAI 0-6 or METAVIR F0-F4, with cirrhosis defined when fibrous septa surround parenchymal nodules and alter lobular architecture (Table 352-2, p.2674).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2673","Harrison 22e p.2674","Harrison 22e p.2685","Harrison 22e p.2686","Harrison 22e p.2694","Harrison 22e p.2695"],"crossLinks":["decomp_cirrhosis","toxic_hepatitis"]},"chronic_inflammatory_demyelinating":{"name":"Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CIDP","chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy","chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acquired immune-mediated demyelinating neuropathy defined by a progressive or relapsing course evolving over at least two months, distinguishing it from Guillain-Barre syndrome","Typically produces symmetrical proximal and distal weakness with large-fibre sensory loss, and reduced or absent reflexes","Unlike most axonal neuropathies, both proximal and distal muscles are affected, and weakness often dominates over sensory symptoms","Nerve conduction studies show demyelinating features: conduction slowing, conduction block, temporal dispersion, and prolonged distal latencies","Cerebrospinal fluid usually shows raised protein with a normal cell count (albuminocytological dissociation)","Responds to immunotherapy including corticosteroids, intravenous immunoglobulin, and plasma exchange, unlike axonal neuropathies","Several variants exist, including multifocal (Lewis-Sumner) and pure sensory or motor forms","Should prompt screening for associated conditions such as monoclonal gammopathy, diabetes, HIV, and lymphoma"],"pathophysiology":"CIDP is an autoimmune disorder in which cell-mediated and humoral responses target components of peripheral nerve myelin and Schwann cells, producing segmental demyelination and remyelination with characteristic onion-bulb formation on biopsy. Inflammatory infiltration occurs around endoneurial vessels and nerve roots. Demyelination slows or blocks conduction, causing weakness and sensory loss; secondary axonal degeneration accumulates over time and correlates with fixed deficits. A subset has antibodies against nodal or paranodal proteins (e.g. neurofascin, contactin), which behave somewhat differently and may respond poorly to immunoglobulin.","additionalDifferentials":["Guillain-Barre syndrome (acute, monophasic course under 4 weeks)","Hereditary demyelinating neuropathy (Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease)","Multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block","Paraproteinaemic neuropathy including anti-MAG and POEMS syndrome","Diabetic polyneuropathy or diabetic lumbosacral radiculoplexus neuropathy","Vasculitic neuropathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nerve conduction studies and electromyography to confirm demyelinating physiology","Lumbar puncture for cerebrospinal fluid protein and cell count","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis with immunofixation for monoclonal protein","Nodal/paranodal antibody testing (neurofascin-155, contactin-1, CASPR1) in atypical or treatment-resistant cases","MRI of nerve roots and plexus showing hypertrophy or enhancement in selected cases","Nerve biopsy reserved for diagnostically uncertain presentations"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive weakness threatening ambulation or respiratory or bulbar function","Features suggesting an underlying malignancy or POEMS syndrome such as weight loss, organomegaly, or skin change","Poor or absent response to first-line immunotherapy prompting reassessment of the diagnosis","Superimposed acute deterioration requiring differentiation from an axonal or systemic process"],"prognosis":"Most patients respond to immunotherapy and many achieve remission or long-term stabilisation, though the disease is often chronic and relapsing; delayed treatment allowing secondary axonal loss predicts greater residual disability.","pitfalls":["Confusing a slowly evolving CIDP with Guillain-Barre syndrome and stopping treatment prematurely","Over-diagnosing CIDP in length-dependent axonal neuropathies that will not respond to immunotherapy","Missing an associated paraprotein or POEMS syndrome that changes management","Failing to recognise nodopathy variants that respond poorly to immunoglobulin","Not objectively documenting treatment response, leading to prolonged ineffective immunotherapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"chronic_limb_ischemia":{"name":"Chronic limb ischaemia","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["peripheral arterial disease","PAD","chronic limb-threatening ischaemia","CLTI","critical limb ischaemia","lower extremity arterial disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Intermittent claudication is exertional muscle pain relieved by rest, with the site of pain located one level below the culprit arterial segment","Chronic limb-threatening ischaemia is defined by rest pain, non-healing ulceration, or gangrene and represents the severe end of the disease spectrum","An ankle-brachial index below the normal range supports the diagnosis, while calcified incompressible vessels can spuriously elevate readings, making toe pressures useful","Examination may reveal diminished pulses, cool skin, hair loss, dependent rubor, and delayed capillary refill","Duplex ultrasound and CT or MR angiography map disease and guide revascularisation planning","Structured exercise therapy, smoking cessation, antiplatelet therapy, and statin use are cornerstones of management for claudication","Revascularisation, whether endovascular or open bypass, is reserved for lifestyle-limiting claudication that fails medical therapy or for limb-threatening ischaemia","Peripheral arterial disease is a marker of systemic atherosclerosis, so cardiovascular risk reduction is as important as limb-directed treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Chronic limb ischaemia results from progressive atherosclerotic narrowing of the aortoiliac, femoropopliteal, or tibial arteries, reducing perfusion to the lower limb. In claudication, resting flow is adequate but cannot meet the increased metabolic demand of exercising muscle, producing reversible ischaemic pain. As stenoses progress and collateral capacity is exceeded, resting perfusion falls below tissue requirements, causing rest pain, ulceration, and tissue loss. Diabetes and renal disease accelerate distal and calcified disease, and the combination of impaired inflow and microvascular dysfunction underlies poor wound healing.","additionalDifferentials":["Spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication","Venous claudication from chronic venous obstruction","Chronic compartment syndrome","Diabetic peripheral neuropathy","Musculoskeletal or arthritic limb pain","Buerger disease (thromboangiitis obliterans)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ankle-brachial index and toe pressures, with exercise testing when resting indices are equivocal","Arterial duplex ultrasound to localise stenoses and occlusions","CT or MR angiography, or catheter angiography, for revascularisation planning","Assessment of cardiovascular risk factors including lipids, glucose, and renal function","Wound assessment and, where infection or osteomyelitis is suspected, appropriate imaging"],"redFlags":["Rest pain, non-healing ulcer, or gangrene indicating chronic limb-threatening ischaemia requiring urgent vascular referral","Rapidly progressing tissue loss or spreading infection threatening limb viability","Sudden worsening suggesting acute-on-chronic thrombosis or embolism, a limb-threatening emergency","Systemic sepsis arising from an ischaemic infected foot"],"prognosis":"Most patients with claudication remain stable and only a minority progress to limb-threatening ischaemia, but overall mortality is high because of concurrent coronary and cerebrovascular disease. Chronic limb-threatening ischaemia carries a substantial risk of amputation and death without timely revascularisation and wound care.","pitfalls":["Interpreting a normal ankle-brachial index as excluding disease when vessels are calcified and incompressible","Attributing all leg pain to arterial disease and missing neurogenic or musculoskeletal causes","Focusing on the limb while neglecting the high systemic cardiovascular risk","Delaying referral in limb-threatening ischaemia, allowing avoidable tissue loss"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"chronic_lymphocytic_leukemia":{"name":"Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)","system":"Oncology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CLL","small lymphocytic lymphoma","SLL"],"chapter":112,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.849-855","clinicalPearls":["CLL is a monoclonal proliferation of mature B lymphocytes defined by an absolute clonal B-cell count of ≥5 × 10⁹/L in the blood; it is a disease of older adults (median age at diagnosis 71) (p.849).","Most commonly diagnosed incidentally on routine blood work showing an elevated lymphocyte count in an asymptomatic individual; the natural history is heterogeneous, with some patients never requiring therapy (p.849, p.853).","On the peripheral blood smear, the small lymphocytes are increased and many rupture during smear preparation, leaving nuclear material without surrounding cytoplasm — smudge cells, which are rare in the absence of chronic lymphoid leukemia (p.443).","Diagnosis is confirmed by flow cytometry on peripheral blood; the typical immunophenotype is CD19+, CD20+, CD22+, CD23+, CD200+, the T-cell marker CD5+, and dim surface immunoglobulin (kappa or lambda). A clonal B-cell count ≥5 × 10⁹/L needs no further workup to confirm CLL (p.852).","Small lymphocytic lymphoma (SLL) is the tissue-based counterpart of CLL (lymphadenopathy/splenomegaly with a small clonal blood population); genetic and molecular features are identical and SLL is managed identically to CLL (p.852).","Monoclonal B-cell lymphocytosis (MBL) is a precursor: clonal B cells below the CLL threshold without nodal/organ involvement or cytopenias; high-count MBL progresses to overt CLL at ~1–2% per year, whereas low-count MBL has a negligible progression rate (p.849, p.852).","IGHV mutational status is a key prognostic factor: ~60% have IGHV ≥2% mutated from germline (more indolent course), while ~40% are IGHV-unmutated (more rapid progression, shorter survival before BCR-targeted therapy) (p.850).","B-cell receptor (BCR) signaling is central to CLL pathogenesis, characterized by low-level IgM expression and tonic activation of antiapoptotic pathways promoting tumor survival (p.850).","FISH (with or without karyotype) should be checked before every line of therapy because cytogenetics can evolve even without treatment, mainly to detect acquisition of del(17)(p13.1) (p.851).","Bone marrow biopsy is not undertaken until therapy is initiated, except in cases of unexplained cytopenias; CT imaging is usually unnecessary at diagnosis (p.854).","A watchful-waiting strategy is used for most patients, with therapy reserved for symptomatic disease, as multiple trials show no survival advantage to earlier therapy (p.854).","Hypogammaglobulinemia affects all immunoglobulin subclasses and occurs in ~85% of patients at some point; low IgG and IgA correlate with infection risk, but isolated IgM reduction does not seem to confer excess infection risk (p.852)."],"pathophysiology":"CLL is a monoclonal expansion of mature B lymphocytes; the definitive cell of origin is not established (mature/memory B cell, stepwise transformation, or hematopoietic stem cell are all proposed). Distinct BCR signaling — low-level IgM, tonic antiapoptotic pathway activation — drives tumor survival, with upregulation of BCR-pathway genes in lymph nodes and marrow versus blood. The CLL genome is relatively simple (~20 nonsynonymous and ~5 structural changes) with no unifying driver; recurrent mutations include SF3B1, NOTCH1, MYD88, ATM, and TP53 (mostly enriched in IGHV-unmutated disease, except MYD88 which favors IGHV-mutated/indolent disease). Key cytogenetic lesions: del(13)(q14.3) (sole, indolent; causes loss of the miR15/16 cluster that normally suppresses BCL2 and other antiapoptotic genes), trisomy 12 (intermediate), del(11)(q22.3)/ATM (bulky disease, aggressive), and del(17)(p13.1)/TP53 (poorest prognosis). Immune dysregulation pervades the disease: innate defects (reduced complement, neutrophil and NK cell defects), adaptive defects (CD4+ T-cell exhaustion, Th1-to-Th2 shift, expanded T regulatory cells), nurse-like tumor-associated macrophages, and humoral hypogammaglobulinemia (p.849-852).","additionalDifferentials":["Mantle cell lymphoma (CD5+, but CD23-negative, cyclin D1+, moderate/bright CD20 and surface Ig) — distinguished by immunophenotype (p.852)","Marginal zone lymphoma (CD5 -/+, CD23 -/+, bright surface Ig) (p.852)","Follicular lymphoma (CD5-negative, CD10-positive, CD23-positive) (p.852)","Monoclonal B-cell lymphocytosis (clonal B cells below the CLL threshold without organ involvement or cytopenias) (p.852)","Small lymphocytic lymphoma (tissue-based counterpart with identical molecular features) (p.852)","B-cell prolymphocytic leukemia arising from CLL (clinically and therapeutically distinct, though classified under Richter's transformation) (p.853-854)"],"infectionMimics":["Reactive lymphocytosis from viral infection — viral infections produce larger reactive lymphocytes with abundant cytoplasm, in contrast to the increased small lymphocytes and smudge cells of CLL (p.443)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Other CD5+ or low-grade B-cell lymphoproliferative disorders (mantle cell, marginal zone, follicular lymphoma) distinguishable by flow cytometry immunophenotype, morphology, or cytogenetics (p.852)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Flow cytometry of peripheral blood for the characteristic immunophenotype (CD19, CD20, CD22, CD23, CD200, CD5, dim surface Ig) — diagnostic test of choice (p.852)","FISH for recurrent abnormalities del(13)(q14.3), trisomy 12, del(11)(q22.3), del(17)(p13.1); repeated before each line of therapy to detect acquired del(17p) (p.851)","Stimulated metaphase karyotype, including assessment for complex karyotype (≥3 abnormalities) (p.851)","Sequencing for TP53 and IGHV mutational status for prognostication (p.854)","β2-microglobulin and LDH (prognostic; component of CLL International Prognostic Index) (p.853-854)","Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test for warm IgG antibody when autoimmune hemolytic anemia is suspected (Coombs-negative cases can occur) (p.853)","Reticulocyte count, bilirubin, LDH, haptoglobin to evaluate suspected hemolysis (p.853)","18FDG-PET/CT to localize a biopsy site when Richter's transformation is suspected (SUV <5 consistent with CLL; SUV >5 suspicious; ≥10 very concerning); excisional biopsy preferred over fine-needle aspiration (p.853-854)","CLL International Prognostic Index integrating TP53 status, IGHV status, β2-microglobulin, clinical stage, and age (p.854)"],"redFlags":["Richter's transformation — transformation to an aggressive lymphoma (most commonly DLBCL; Hodgkin lymphoma is a recognized variant). Suspect with rapid progression of adenopathy (often localized), constitutional symptoms (fatigue, night sweats, fever, weight loss), and usually high LDH (p.853-854)","del(17)(p13.1)/TP53 loss or mutation — poorest prognosis, rapid progression, poor response to DNA-damaging therapy (p.850-851)","Complex karyotype (≥3 abnormalities) — adverse impact on time to treatment and overall survival, worsening with increasing complexity (p.851)","Autoimmune hemolytic anemia — death from uncontrolled AIHA can occur without appropriate supportive care; immediate therapy is almost always necessary (p.853)","Symptoms indicating need for therapy: progressive marrow failure, massive/progressive/symptomatic splenomegaly or lymphadenopathy, lymphocyte doubling time <6 months, autoimmune cytopenia unresponsive to standard therapy, or constitutional symptoms (p.854)"],"prognosis":"Heterogeneous: many patients are asymptomatic and never require therapy while others need multiple lines and die of disease. Overall survival has improved markedly (5-year OS 70% in 1980, 92% in 2015, likely higher today), and only a small minority now die of CLL itself. Infections cause ~30–50% of CLL-related deaths. Favorable markers: IGHV-mutated disease, sole del(13)(q14.3), MYD88 mutation. Adverse markers: IGHV-unmutated disease, del(17p)/TP53, del(11q)/ATM, NOTCH1, complex karyotype, and high β2-microglobulin/LDH. The CLL International Prognostic Index stratifies 5-year survival from ~93% (low risk) to ~23% (very high risk) in training-set data, though it has not been validated in the era of novel targeted therapies. Richter's transformation carries a poor prognosis, with median survival of 6–16 months for clonally related DLBCL versus ~5 years for clonally unrelated lymphomas (p.849, p.851, p.853-854).","pitfalls":["When anemia or thrombocytopenia appear, distinguish autoimmune cytopenia from marrow replacement — the therapeutic approaches differ, and autoimmune cytopenias often respond without treating the underlying CLL (p.853)","AIHA can be Coombs-negative, so a negative direct antiglobulin test does not exclude it (p.853)","ITP can be hard to distinguish from disease progression because no lab test directly identifies the platelet-destruction mechanism; isolated thrombocytopenia and rapid platelet decline without alternative cause point to ITP (p.853)","del(13)(q14.3) seen on banded karyotype (rather than only on FISH) indicates a larger deletion involving the retinoblastoma gene, which negates the favorable prognosis usually associated with this marker (p.850)","Live vaccines should be avoided in CLL because of the small risk of viral reactivation in an immunocompromised host (p.853)","Fine-needle aspiration should be discouraged for diagnosing suspected Richter's transformation; excisional biopsy is preferred (p.854)","Cytogenetics can change without therapy, so prior FISH results may be outdated — recheck before each line of treatment (p.851)"],"severityClassification":"Two staging systems, both based on physical exam and labs (no imaging or marrow required). Rai system (more common in the US): low risk (stage 0) = lymphocytosis only; intermediate risk (stage I/II) = lymphocytosis with lymphadenopathy, with or without splenomegaly/hepatomegaly; high risk (stage III/IV) = lymphocytosis with anemia or thrombocytopenia from marrow involvement. Binet system (more common in Europe): A = <3 areas of lymphadenopathy; B = ≥3 areas of lymphadenopathy; C = hemoglobin ≤10 g/dL and/or platelets <100,000/μL. The CLL International Prognostic Index refines outcome prediction using TP53 status, IGHV status, β2-microglobulin, clinical stage, and age (p.854).","references":["Harrison 22e p.849-855","Harrison 22e p.443"],"crossLinks":[]},"chronic_mesenteric_ischemia":{"name":"Chronic mesenteric ischaemia","system":"GI / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chronic mesenteric ischemia","intestinal angina","abdominal angina"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AASLD & ACG","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Almost always caused by atherosclerotic narrowing of the mesenteric arteries, so patients typically have widespread vascular disease and risk factors such as smoking","The hallmark is postprandial abdominal pain (intestinal angina) beginning shortly after eating, leading to fear of food, reduced intake, and progressive weight loss","Because of the rich collateral circulation between the coeliac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric arteries, symptoms usually require significant stenosis in more than one of the three main vessels","It is frequently underdiagnosed, and unexplained weight loss with food aversion in an arteriopath should prompt consideration of the diagnosis","CT or MR angiography, and duplex ultrasound, are used to demonstrate the arterial stenoses","Revascularisation, most often by endovascular angioplasty and stenting or by surgical bypass, relieves symptoms and prevents progression to acute intestinal infarction","Untreated disease can progress to acute-on-chronic mesenteric ischaemia with bowel infarction, a surgical emergency","Nutritional support and cardiovascular risk-factor modification are important adjuncts to revascularisation"],"pathophysiology":"Chronic mesenteric ischaemia develops when atherosclerotic stenosis of the splanchnic arteries limits the increase in blood flow required to meet the metabolic demand of the gut after meals. The resulting mismatch produces postprandial ischaemic pain. Because of extensive collateral pathways among the three main mesenteric arteries, symptomatic disease typically requires high-grade stenosis or occlusion of at least two vessels, or a critical single-vessel lesion where collaterals are inadequate. Chronic hypoperfusion impairs absorption and causes food avoidance and weight loss. Acute thrombosis superimposed on a stenosed vessel can precipitate acute mesenteric ischaemia with bowel infarction.","additionalDifferentials":["Peptic ulcer disease and other upper GI causes of postprandial pain","Chronic pancreatitis or pancreatic malignancy","Biliary colic","Gastrointestinal malignancy causing weight loss","Median arcuate ligament syndrome (coeliac axis compression)","Functional dyspepsia or gastroparesis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT or MR angiography of the mesenteric vessels to demonstrate stenoses","Mesenteric duplex ultrasound as a non-invasive screening test","Catheter angiography when revascularisation is planned","Nutritional assessment given chronic weight loss","Exclusion of gastrointestinal malignancy and other causes of weight loss by endoscopy and imaging"],"redFlags":["Sudden, severe, constant abdominal pain out of proportion to examination signalling acute-on-chronic mesenteric ischaemia and bowel infarction","Rising lactate, acidosis, peritonism, or shock requiring emergency surgical evaluation","Rapid, marked weight loss with intractable food aversion","Progression of symptoms despite conservative measures indicating imminent vascular compromise"],"prognosis":"With timely revascularisation, symptoms usually resolve, weight is regained, and the risk of catastrophic bowel infarction falls. Untreated disease carries a substantial risk of progression to acute mesenteric ischaemia, which has high mortality. Overall outlook is also influenced by the patient's generalised atherosclerotic burden.","pitfalls":["Attributing chronic postprandial pain and weight loss to malignancy or functional disease and missing a vascular cause","Failing to recognise that significant multi-vessel disease is usually needed to cause symptoms, leading to under-treatment of critical single-vessel lesions","Delaying revascularisation until acute infarction supervenes","Neglecting cardiovascular risk-factor management after treating the mesenteric lesions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AASLD/ACG guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"chronic_myeloid_leukemia":{"name":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)","system":"Oncology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CML"],"chapter":110,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.834","clinicalPearls":["CML is a clonal hematopoietic myeloproliferative stem cell neoplasm driven by the BCR::ABL1 chimeric gene, which encodes a constitutively active tyrosine kinase (Harrison 22e p.834).","BCR::ABL1 results from a balanced reciprocal translocation between the long arms of chromosomes 9 and 22, t(9;22)(q34.1;q11.2), known as the Philadelphia chromosome (Ph); the t(9;22) is present in >90% of classical CML cases (Harrison 22e p.834-835).","The most common transcript in CML encodes the p210BCR::ABL1 oncoprotein; p190BCR::ABL1 is present in two-thirds of Ph-positive ALL but rare in CML, and p230BCR::ABL1 is rare and associated with a more indolent course (Harrison 22e p.835).","Untreated, CML is typically biphasic or triphasic: an early indolent chronic phase, often followed by an accelerated phase, and a terminal blastic phase (Harrison 22e p.834).","Most patients (about 90%) present in chronic phase; in developed countries 50-60% are diagnosed on routine blood tests with minimal symptoms such as fatigue (Harrison 22e p.836).","Common symptoms, when present, are manifestations of anemia and splenomegaly: fatigue, malaise, weight loss, early satiety, and left upper quadrant pain or mass (Harrison 22e p.836).","Splenomegaly is the most common physical finding (20-70% depending on screening frequency); hepatomegaly (5-10%) and lymphadenopathy (5%) are less common (Harrison 22e p.836-837).","Untreated CML shows leukocytosis (10-500 x 10^9/L) with left-shifted myeloid hematopoiesis: neutrophils plus bands, myelocytes, metamyelocytes, promyelocytes, and blasts (usually <=5%); basophils and/or eosinophils are frequently increased (Harrison 22e p.837).","Thrombocytosis is common; thrombocytopenia is rare and, when present, suggests worse prognosis, disease acceleration, or an unrelated etiology. Anemia is present in one-third of patients (Harrison 22e p.837).","Diagnosis depends on documenting t(9;22)(q34.1;q11.2), identified by G-banding in 90% of cases; FISH and PCR aid diagnosis and estimate disease burden, and can be done on peripheral blood (Harrison 22e p.837).","TKIs have revolutionized CML: the estimated 10-year survival with imatinib now exceeds 85% and approaches that of a normal age-matched population; allogeneic HSCT (curative but riskier) is offered as later-line therapy after TKI failure (Harrison 22e p.834).","Molecular monitoring on the International Scale (IS) guides therapy: complete cytogenetic response (0% Ph) is roughly equivalent to BCR::ABL1 <=1% (MR2); major molecular response (MMR/MR3) is BCR::ABL1 <=0.1%; deep molecular response MR4 is <=0.01% and MR4.5 is <=0.0032% (Harrison 22e p.837-838).","The T315I 'gatekeeper' kinase-domain mutation prevents binding of, and causes resistance to, imatinib and the second-generation TKIs (dasatinib, nilotinib, bosutinib); third-generation agents (e.g., ponatinib, asciminib) retain activity against it (Harrison 22e p.836)."],"pathophysiology":"CML is a clonal myeloproliferative stem cell neoplasm caused by the BCR::ABL1 fusion gene arising from the reciprocal balanced translocation t(9;22)(q34.1;q11.2) (the Philadelphia chromosome). DNA from the ABL1 proto-oncogene on chromosome 9 is juxtaposed to the breakpoint cluster region (BCR) gene on chromosome 22, generating a hybrid oncogene whose product (most commonly the p210BCR::ABL1 oncoprotein from e13a2 or e14a2 transcripts) has constitutive tyrosine kinase activity. This drives increased proliferation and reduced apoptosis, giving CML cells a growth advantage and progressively suppressing normal hematopoiesis. Constitutive BCR::ABL1 activation triggers autophosphorylation and downstream signaling via RAS, MAP kinases, STAT, PI3K, MYC and others, mediated by adapter proteins (GRB-2, CRK, CRK-L, SHC). Most TKIs bind the ATP-binding domain to inhibit kinase activity; asciminib (a STAMP inhibitor) instead binds the ABL1 myristoyl pocket. BCR::ABL1 induces genetic instability favoring acquisition of additional defects (e.g., double Ph, trisomy 8, isochromosome 17/17p loss of TP53, 3q26 abnormalities, mutations in TP53, RB1, RUNX1, p16) that drive transformation from chronic to accelerated to blastic phase. Notably, the BCR::ABL1 rearrangement is detectable in the blood of up to 25% of normal adults and 5% of infants but is not sufficient to cause overt CML in the overwhelming majority. (Harrison 22e p.834-836)","additionalDifferentials":["Leukemoid reaction (reactive leukocytosis) - distinguished from CML by a high/normal leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score (CML has a LOW LAP) and absence of BCR::ABL1 (Harrison 22e p.837)","Atypical CML (BCR::ABL1-negative) - lacks the Ph chromosome/BCR::ABL1, does not respond to TKI therapy, associated with SETBP1 (about 25%) and some CSF3R mutations, poor prognosis with median survival ~2-3 years (Harrison 22e p.835)","Chronic neutrophilic leukemia - frequently CSF3R-mutated (about 80%); may respond to ruxolitinib (Harrison 22e p.835)","Chronic myelomonocytic leukemia (Harrison 22e p.835)","Myelodysplastic syndrome/myeloproliferative neoplasms, including MDS/MPN-RS-T (SF3B1-mutated) (Harrison 22e p.835)","Essential thrombocythemia and other Ph-negative MPNs presenting with thrombocytosis - when JAK2 V617F is absent, cytogenetics/FISH for BCR::ABL1 are required to exclude CML (Harrison 22e p.832-833)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Leukemoid reaction (reactive leukocytosis from infection, inflammation, or other stress) - mimics the leukocytosis with left shift of CML but lacks BCR::ABL1 and shows a high/normal (not low) leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score (Harrison 22e p.837)","Other myeloproliferative neoplasms and MDS/MPN overlap syndromes that can produce leukocytosis, basophilia, or splenomegaly without the Philadelphia chromosome (Harrison 22e p.835)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Bone marrow aspiration with routine G-banding cytogenetics - required to confirm the Ph chromosome, detect additional chromosomal abnormalities (which FISH will not detect), and quantify marrow blasts and basophils; a diagnosis of CML must always rely on marrow analysis with cytogenetics (Harrison 22e p.837)","FISH analysis for BCR::ABL1 on peripheral blood - quantifies percentage of Ph-positive cells; preferred when a Ph cytogenetic study is negative or for monitoring (Harrison 22e p.837)","Quantitative RT-PCR for BCR::ABL1 transcripts on the International Scale - baseline transcript typing at diagnosis (to avoid spurious 'undetectable' results on follow-up with atypical transcripts) and serial molecular monitoring of response (Harrison 22e p.837-838)","ABL1 kinase domain mutation analysis - assessed when resistance/loss of response is suspected (e.g., to detect T315I) (Harrison 22e p.836-837)","Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score - characteristically LOW in CML, helping distinguish it from a leukemoid reaction (Harrison 22e p.837)","Supportive biochemistry - high vitamin B12, uric acid, lactate dehydrogenase, and lysozyme are commonly seen (Harrison 22e p.837)"],"redFlags":["Unexplained, sustained leukocytosis (with or without splenomegaly) should prompt marrow examination and cytogenetic analysis (Harrison 22e p.837)","Increasing/therapy-resistant leukocytosis, worsening anemia, fever and constitutional symptoms, and rising blasts and basophils signal disease progression (Harrison 22e p.838)","New thrombocytopenia (<100 x 10^9/L unrelated to therapy) suggests worse prognosis or disease acceleration (Harrison 22e p.837-838)","Extramedullary disease (skin/subcutaneous lesions, soft tissue, or lytic bone lesions) with biopsy showing blast predominance indicates transformation (Harrison 22e p.837-838)","Hyperleukocytosis/hyperviscosity or marked thrombocytosis with thrombotic events (priapism, MI, venous thrombosis, visual disturbance, CVA) or bleeding diathesis (Harrison 22e p.836)"],"prognosis":"TKIs have transformed CML prognosis. Before the TKI era median survival was 3-7 years with annual mortality of 10% in the first 2 years and 15-20% thereafter. With imatinib, annual mortality has fallen to about 1-2%, the 10-year survival rate is about 85% (CML-specific survival ~90%), and the 10-year incidence of blastic transformation is only 5-6%; more than half of deaths are now from non-CML causes. Treatment-related response milestones are the dominant modern prognostic factors: achievement of complete cytogenetic response (MR2) is the major endpoint associated with improved survival, while durable deep molecular response (MR4/MR4.5) may permit treatment-free remission (TFR rate about 50% after 2+ years of durable DMR and 80%+ after 5+ years). Historical pretreatment models (Sokal, Hasford, EUTOS) and adverse features (age, splenomegaly, anemia, high blasts/basophils, marrow fibrosis, additional chromosomal abnormalities) have lost much of their prognostic weight in the TKI era. Accelerated phase (historical median survival <2 years) and blastic phase carry the worst outcomes; de novo accelerated phase on TKI now has an estimated 8-year survival of 60-70%, and lymphoid blastic phase responds to ALL-type chemotherapy plus TKIs (complete response ~70%, median survival ~3 years). (Harrison 22e p.834, 838-839)","pitfalls":["PITFALL: Mistaking a reactive leukemoid reaction for CML - the leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score is LOW in CML but high/normal in a leukemoid reaction, and CML requires demonstration of BCR::ABL1 (Harrison 22e p.837).","Atypical transcripts (e13a3, e14a3, e19a2, etc.) not detected at diagnosis can produce falsely 'negative/undetectable' BCR::ABL1 on follow-up PCR, giving a false impression of complete molecular response; transcript type must be documented at diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.835, 837).","FISH does NOT detect additional chromosomal abnormalities, so a marrow cytogenetic analysis is still required at diagnosis; relying on FISH/PCR alone can miss clonal evolution (Harrison 22e p.837).","Both FISH and PCR can be falsely positive at low levels or falsely negative for technical reasons, so diagnosis must always rely on marrow analysis with routine cytogenetics (Harrison 22e p.837).","In about 10% of patients marrow blasts and basophils are significantly higher than in peripheral blood, conferring poorer prognosis or representing transformation that peripheral counts alone would miss (Harrison 22e p.837).","Failure to achieve MMR or DMR should NOT automatically be considered 'failure' of a given TKI or an indication to switch TKI or proceed to allogeneic HSCT (Harrison 22e p.838)."],"severityClassification":"CML is staged by clinical phase. Chronic phase: indolent, marrow blasts typically <=5%, where ~90% of patients present. Accelerated phase (historically median survival <2 years): >=15% peripheral blasts, >=30% peripheral blasts plus promyelocytes, >=20% peripheral basophils, cytogenetic clonal evolution (additional chromosomal abnormalities beyond Ph), or thrombocytopenia <100 x 10^9/L unrelated to therapy. Blastic phase: >=30% peripheral or marrow blasts, or sheets of blasts in extramedullary disease (commonly myeloid in ~60%, lymphoid in ~25%). The newest WHO classification suggests eliminating accelerated phase and reclassifying such patients as high-risk CML, but the text argues the accelerated-phase definition should be maintained because their survival is significantly worse than high-risk CML. Risk models (Sokal, Hasford, EUTOS) were used historically. (Harrison 22e p.838)","references":["Harrison 22e p.834"],"crossLinks":[]},"chronic_obstructive_pulmonary_disease":{"name":"Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["COPD","Emphysema","Chronic bronchitis","Chronic obstructive lung disease"],"chapter":303,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2249-2259","clinicalPearls":["COPD is defined by persistent respiratory symptoms with airflow obstruction, and the umbrella term spans emphysema (alveolar destruction with air-space enlargement), chronic bronchitis (chronic cough and phlegm), and small airway disease (narrowing and loss of small bronchioles); these coexist in varying proportions between patients.","Fixed airflow obstruction is the spirometric hallmark: a chronically reduced FEV1/FVC ratio that does not normalize after bronchodilator. The classic threshold is FEV1/FVC <0.7 (GOLD), with the ATS/ERS alternative using Z-scores of FEV1 below the lower limit of normal.","The three cardinal symptoms are cough, sputum production, and exertional dyspnea; many patients have symptoms for months to years before presenting, and a careful history often reveals respiratory symptoms preceding an apparent acute onset.","Activities involving arm work at or above shoulder level are particularly poorly tolerated, whereas activities that let the patient brace the arms and recruit accessory muscles (pushing a shopping cart, treadmill) are better tolerated.","Early disease typically has a normal physical exam; advanced disease shows prolonged expiration, wheeze, barrel chest, poor diaphragmatic excursion, accessory muscle use, the tripod posture, and cyanosis of lips/nail beds.","Digital clubbing is NOT a feature of COPD; its appearance should trigger a search for another cause, most commonly lung cancer in this population.","The blood eosinophil count (on CBC with differential) is used to guide therapy aimed at reducing exacerbation risk; an elevated hematocrit suggests chronic hypoxemia.","Chest CT is the definitive test for establishing presence, pattern, and distribution of emphysema and for medium/large airway disease; chest x-ray may be consistent with COPD (hyperinflation, flat diaphragm, bullae, hyperlucency) but cannot reliably make the diagnosis.","Guidelines recommend testing for alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency in ALL patients with COPD or with asthma plus chronic airflow obstruction; serum alpha-1 antitrypsin level is the initial test, with PI-type determination for confirmation when levels are low.","Approximately only 1% of COPD patients have severe alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, yet it is the proven monogenic risk factor and demonstrates the powerful role of genetics in susceptibility; PiZ individuals often develop early-onset COPD, accelerated by smoking.","The strongest single predictor of future exacerbations is a prior exacerbation, and the single greatest risk factor for hospitalization with an exacerbation is a history of previous hospitalization.","The BODE index (Body mass index, airflow Obstruction, Dyspnea, Exercise capacity by 6-minute walk) predicts mortality better than FEV1 alone."],"pathophysiology":"Chronic inhaled exposures (most commonly cigarette smoke, also biomass fuels, and increasingly vaping/inhaled cannabis) in genetically susceptible individuals trigger inflammatory and immune cell recruitment in large and small airways and terminal air spaces. Large-airway changes (mucus gland enlargement, goblet cell hyperplasia, squamous metaplasia) produce cough and sputum and define chronic bronchitis. Small airways (<=2 mm) are the major site of increased airflow resistance, narrowed by goblet cell metaplasia replacing surfactant-secreting club cells, smooth-muscle hypertrophy, fibrosis, mucus, edema, and cellular infiltration; small-airway narrowing and drop-out precede emphysematous destruction. Emphysema results from a four-step paradigm: inflammatory/immune recruitment; proteinase release damaging extracellular matrix; oxidant-induced structural cell death, cellular senescence and proteolytic loss of cell-matrix attachments; and disordered repair of elastin. The elastase:antielastase imbalance (epitomized by alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency permitting unopposed neutrophil elastase) plus matrix metalloproteinases (notably MMP12) degrade elastin and collagen, generating chemotactic fragments that fuel a positive-feedback inflammatory loop. Oxidative stress (with NRF2 and SOD3 implicated, and oxidant inactivation of HDAC2 unleashing NF-kB-driven transcription of MMPs, IL-8, TNF-alpha) and impaired macrophage clearance of apoptotic cells and bacteria contribute. Physiologically, airflow obstruction with air trapping causes hyperinflation (raised residual volume, RV/TLC, and total lung capacity); a flattened diaphragm becomes mechanically inefficient. Ventilation-perfusion mismatch accounts for essentially all of the reduction in PaO2 (shunt is minimal), explaining why modest supplemental oxygen corrects hypoxemia. Inflammation can persist even after smoking cessation in susceptible individuals.","additionalDifferentials":["Asthma (substantial overlap in older patients; both feature airway hyperresponsiveness and may share genetic/environmental factors)","Bronchiectasis","Interstitial lung disease","Congestive heart failure","Chronic thromboembolic disease / pulmonary hypertension","Lung cancer (especially when new clubbing appears)","Tuberculosis-associated chronic airflow limitation (important in absence of tobacco history)"],"infectionMimics":["Pneumonia (frequently found on imaging during presumed exacerbation)","Acute bronchitis","Tuberculosis (can produce chronic airflow limitation with emphysematous and fibrotic change and airway remodeling)","HIV-associated emphysema/COPD (increased risk with lower CD4 counts; often underdiagnosed)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Congestive heart failure","Pulmonary embolism (incidence increased during exacerbations; should be considered)","Pneumothorax (asymmetric chest findings can mimic an exacerbation)","Large airway obstruction"],"additionalInvestigations":["Post-bronchodilator spirometry (FEV1, FVC, FEV1/FVC) for diagnosis and severity grading (GOLD vs ATS/ERS Z-scores)","Lung volumes (TLC, FRC, RV, RV/TLC) and diffusing capacity (DLCO) - DLCO reduced in emphysema","Arterial blood gas and oximetry for resting/exertional hypoxemia, alveolar ventilation and acid-base status (key in exacerbation assessment)","Complete blood count with differential (blood eosinophil count; hematocrit for chronic hypoxemia)","Chest CT (definitive for emphysema pattern/distribution, airway disease, coexisting ILD/bronchiectasis; annual low-dose CT for lung cancer screening in eligible smokers)","Serum alpha-1 antitrypsin level with PI-type determination (isoelectric focusing, mass spectrometry/genotyping, or DNA sequencing) when low","6-minute walk distance for exercise capacity and BODE index","C-reactive protein (incorporated into proposed objective criteria for staging exacerbation severity)"],"redFlags":["New digital clubbing - investigate for lung cancer, not attributable to COPD","Respiratory acidosis with hypercarbia (PaCO2 >45 mmHg) signaling acute ventilatory failure","New or worsening hypoxemia","Mental status changes (confusion, sleepiness), inability to speak in full sentences, paradoxical abdominal wall motion","Tachycardia, tachypnea, accessory muscle use, perioral/peripheral cyanosis","Cachexia with significant weight loss (independent poor prognostic factor)","Signs of cor pulmonale / right heart failure (lower extremity edema, persistent activity limitation)"],"prognosis":"Risk of death from COPD is closely tied to reduced FEV1 and the presence of emphysema. Mortality is predicted better by the multidimensional BODE index than by FEV1 alone. The absolute annual loss of FEV1 is highest in mild COPD and lowest in very severe disease. Cachexia/wasting is an independent poor prognostic factor. Smoking cessation slows the rate of FEV1 decline (greatest benefit when earlier) and improves survival. Among survival-improving interventions are smoking cessation, long-term oxygen in chronically hypoxemic patients, and lung volume reduction surgery in selected emphysema patients. Patients with PRISm (preserved ratio-impaired spirometry) physiology carry increased risk of mortality and respiratory and cardiovascular events. Exacerbations are episodic acute worsenings (usually <14 days) that increase in frequency as obstruction worsens (severe obstruction with FEV1 <50% predicted averages 1-3 episodes per year) and account for most COPD-related health-care costs; there is currently no cure except lung transplantation.","pitfalls":["Chest x-ray cannot reliably diagnose COPD or establish chronicity; do not rely on it to confirm emphysema.","Pulmonary function testing during an exacerbation has not been shown helpful in diagnosis or management (unlike in asthma exacerbations).","Smokers with normal spirometry are not necessarily spared - many have reduced quality of life, reduced exercise capacity, emphysema/airway disease on CT, and may have exacerbations (pre-COPD).","Attributing new clubbing to COPD itself - it is not a feature and warrants a cancer workup.","Failing to test for alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency in COPD patients, missing a treatable genetic cause; only a small minority of the ~1 in 3000 with severe deficiency in the US have been identified.","Overlooking pulmonary embolism, pneumonia, heart failure, or pneumothorax during a presumed exacerbation (about 25% of x-rays in moderate/severe distress are abnormal).","Underestimating viral causes of exacerbations - PCR studies show viruses may cause >50% of exacerbations.","Race-specific reference equations for pulmonary function may be inappropriate; race-neutral interpretation may be preferable."],"severityClassification":"Spirometric severity of airflow obstruction (GOLD, post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC <0.7): Mild FEV1 >=80% predicted; Moderate FEV1 50 to <80%; Severe FEV1 30 to <50%; Very severe FEV1 <30% predicted. ATS/ERS alternative uses FEV1/FVC < lower limit of normal with FEV1 Z-scores (borderline zFEV1 > -1.645; mild -1.65 to -2.5; moderate -2.51 to -4; severe < -4.1). The GOLD ABE assessment additionally incorporates respiratory symptom burden (mMRC dyspnea scale or COPD Assessment Test) and annual exacerbation frequency to guide therapy. Multidimensional BODE index (BMI, obstruction, dyspnea, exercise capacity) is used for prognosis. Ventilatory failure is defined as PaCO2 >45 mmHg, classified as acute (with acidemia) or chronic by arterial pH.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2249-2259","Harrison 22e Chap. 303 (Tables 303-1; Figs. 303-1 to 303-6)"],"crossLinks":[]},"chronic_pain_management":{"name":"Chronic pain management","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chronic pain","persistent pain","chronic pain syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Chronic pain persists beyond normal tissue healing, commonly defined as lasting more than about three months, and often becomes a condition in its own right rather than a symptom","It is best understood through a biopsychosocial model, since biological, psychological, and social factors all influence the pain experience and disability","Assessment should characterise the pain (nociceptive, neuropathic, or mixed and nociplastic), functional impact, mood, sleep, and the patient's goals","Management is multidisciplinary and multimodal, combining physical rehabilitation, psychological therapies such as cognitive behavioural approaches, and carefully selected medications","Neuropathic pain often responds better to agents such as certain antidepressants and anticonvulsants than to conventional analgesics","Interventional options, including nerve blocks, radiofrequency procedures, and neuromodulation, may help selected patients within a broader plan","Long-term opioid therapy has limited benefit for most chronic non-cancer pain and carries risks of tolerance, dependence, hyperalgesia, and harm, so it requires careful selection, review, and often deprescribing","Realistic goals emphasising improved function and quality of life, rather than complete pain elimination, guide effective long-term care"],"pathophysiology":"Chronic pain involves persistent changes in the peripheral and central nervous systems. Ongoing nociceptor activity and inflammation can sensitise peripheral nerves, while repeated input drives central sensitisation with amplified spinal and cortical responses, reduced descending inhibition, and reorganisation of pain-processing networks. Neuropathic pain follows damage or disease of the somatosensory system, producing ectopic firing and maladaptive plasticity. In nociplastic pain, altered central processing generates pain without clear tissue damage. Psychological and social factors modulate these pathways, and this neuroplasticity explains why chronic pain often persists independently of the original injury.","additionalDifferentials":["Progressive or undiagnosed structural disease (e.g. malignancy)","Inflammatory or autoimmune conditions","Neuropathic pain from a specific nerve lesion","Fibromyalgia and other nociplastic pain conditions","Depression and anxiety contributing to pain and disability","Medication-related problems including opioid-induced hyperalgesia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Comprehensive biopsychosocial assessment including function, mood, and sleep","Validated pain and disability questionnaires to track outcomes","Targeted imaging or nerve conduction studies only when a specific treatable cause is suspected","Screening for depression, anxiety, and substance use","Medication review including opioid risk assessment"],"redFlags":["New or changing pain with features suggesting malignancy, infection, or fracture","Progressive neurological deficit or signs of cord or cauda equina compression","Systemic features such as weight loss, fever, or night pain","Signs of opioid misuse, escalating use, or overdose risk"],"prognosis":"Chronic pain is frequently a long-term condition; while cure is often not achievable, multidisciplinary management can substantially improve function, mood, and quality of life, whereas reliance on escalating opioids tends to worsen outcomes.","pitfalls":["Managing chronic pain with escalating opioids despite limited benefit and rising harm","Neglecting psychological and social contributors within a biomedical-only approach","Failing to identify neuropathic pain that needs different medication","Missing red-flag features that indicate serious underlying pathology","Setting unrealistic goals of complete pain elimination rather than improved function"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"chronic_pancreatitis":{"name":"Chronic Pancreatitis","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":[],"chapter":359,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2750","clinicalPearls":["Patients typically present with abdominal pain or symptoms of maldigestion; pain is variable in location, severity, and frequency, may be constant or intermittent, and can be exacerbated by eating, leading to fear of eating and weight loss (p.2752).","There is often a disparity between the reported severity of abdominal pain and the physical findings, which mainly consist of nonfocal abdominal tenderness (p.2752).","In contrast to acute pancreatitis, serum amylase and lipase are usually NOT strikingly elevated in chronic pancreatitis; low serum pancreatic enzyme levels are moderately specific but have poor sensitivity (p.2752).","Cumulative prevalence of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency exceeds 80%; overt steatorrhea in a patient with chronic pancreatitis is highly suggestive of this complication (p.2752).","A random fecal elastase-1 level on a formed stool specimen can help confirm exocrine pancreatic insufficiency in patients with milder symptoms (p.2752).","Diffuse calcifications on a plain abdominal film usually indicate significant pancreatic damage and are pathognomonic for chronic pancreatitis; tropical pancreatitis (often SPINK1-associated) characteristically shows bulky calcifications (p.2752).","Lifetime prevalence of chronic pancreatitis-related diabetes exceeds 80%; most develop hyperglycemia from insulin deficiency due to islet cell loss, but diabetic ketoacidosis, diabetic coma, and end-organ damage (retinopathy, neuropathy, nephropathy) are uncommon (p.2752).","Cigarette smoking is an independent, dose-dependent risk factor for chronic pancreatitis and recurrent acute pancreatitis; continued alcohol and smoking exposure are associated with disease progression including fibrosis and calcifications (p.2750).","Metabolic bone disease is highly prevalent, with about 65% of patients having osteopenia or osteoporosis (p.2752)."],"pathophysiology":"Chronic pancreatitis is characterized by irreversible damage to the pancreas (in contrast to the reversible changes of acute pancreatitis), defined by histologic abnormalities including chronic inflammation, fibrosis, and progressive destruction (atrophy) of both exocrine and endocrine tissue. Irrespective of the mechanism of injury, pancreatic stellate cell activation leads to cytokine expression and production of extracellular matrix proteins contributing to inflammation and collagen deposition. Proinflammatory cytokines (TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6) and oxidants induce stellate cell activity with new collagen synthesis; TGF-beta-mediated autocrine self-activation pathways may explain disease progression even after removal of noxious stimuli. Etiologies are framed by the TIGAR-O classification (Toxic-metabolic, Idiopathic, Genetic, Autoimmune, Recurrent and severe acute pancreatitis, Obstructive). Heavy alcohol use is the most common clinically apparent cause in U.S. adults, though additional factors are required for disease development (not all heavy drinkers develop disease). Genetic predisposition (e.g., PRSS1 cationic trypsinogen mutations preventing destruction of prematurely activated trypsin; CFTR and SPINK1 disease modifiers) accounts for up to 15% of previously idiopathic cases. Cardinal manifestations are abdominal pain, steatorrhea, weight loss, diabetes mellitus, and, less commonly, pancreatic cancer (p.2750-2752).","additionalDifferentials":["Pancreatic adenocarcinoma (must be excluded on imaging)","Autoimmune pancreatitis (type 1 AIP / IgG4-related disease; type 2 / idiopathic duct-centric)","Pancreatic pseudocyst"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pancreatic cancer (a poor response to glucocorticoids in suspected autoimmune pancreatitis should raise suspicion of pancreatic cancer)","Autoimmune pancreatitis (type 1 can mimic primary sclerosing cholangitis via IgG4-related sclerosing cholangitis)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fecal elastase-1 (random level on a formed stool specimen) to confirm exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","Abdominal CT (initial imaging modality of choice) to show calcifications, dilated pancreatic/biliary ducts, atrophy, and to exclude pseudocyst and cancer","MRI/MRCP, secretin-enhanced MRCP","Endoscopic ultrasonography (EUS); five or more of nine endosonographic features considered diagnostic, though not specific for early disease","EUS with endoscopic pancreatic function testing (EUS-ePFT)","Direct pancreatic function test using secretin (best sensitivity; becomes abnormal when >=60% of exocrine function is lost)","Plain abdominal film for diffuse calcifications","Serum amylase/lipase (usually not strikingly elevated), serum bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase (may indicate cholestasis from biliary stricture)","Serum IgG4 (supportive of type 1 autoimmune pancreatitis)"],"redFlags":["Increased risk of pancreatic cancer: cumulative risk is 4% at 20 years after diagnosis of chronic calcific pancreatitis; hereditary PRSS1 and tropical pancreatitis carry an even higher risk than other forms.","Jaundice, cholestasis, and biliary cirrhosis from chronic inflammation around the intrapancreatic common bile duct.","Gastrointestinal bleeding from peptic ulceration, gastritis, a pseudocyst eroding into the duodenum, arterial bleeding into the pancreatic duct (hemosuccus pancreaticus), or ruptured varices from splenic vein thrombosis."],"prognosis":"Chronic pancreatitis is an irreversible disease; there are currently no therapies to reverse or delay disease progression, so management is focused on screening for and treating complications. Patients have impaired quality of life and significant morbidity with frequent health encounters. Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (cumulative prevalence >80%) and chronic pancreatitis-related diabetes (lifetime prevalence >80%) commonly develop. The 20-year cumulative risk of pancreatic cancer in chronic calcific pancreatitis is 4%, higher in hereditary PRSS1 and tropical forms (p.2752-2754).","pitfalls":["Diagnosis of early or mild chronic pancreatitis is challenging because there is no accurate biomarker, and serum amylase/lipase are usually not strikingly elevated (unlike acute pancreatitis) (p.2752).","EUS is not specific enough to diagnose early chronic pancreatitis alone and may show positive features in patients with diabetes, a smoking history, or even normal aging individuals (p.2752).","Elevated serum IgG4 has a low positive predictive value for autoimmune pancreatitis when used in isolation from other clinical findings (p.2751).","Complete correction of steatorrhea with pancreatic enzyme replacement is uncommon, even though enzyme therapy usually controls diarrhea and improves fat absorption (p.2753).","Meta-analyses show no consistent benefit of enzyme therapy at reducing pain; any pain relief may reflect improvement of dyspepsia from maldigestion (p.2753)."],"severityClassification":"Etiologic classification follows the TIGAR-O system: Toxic-metabolic (alcohol, tobacco smoking, hypercalcemia, hyperlipidemia/hypertriglyceridemia, chronic renal failure), Idiopathic (early onset, late onset, tropical), Genetic (PRSS1 cationic trypsinogen, CFTR, CASR, CTRC, SPINK1), Autoimmune (type 1 IgG4-related; type 2 idiopathic duct-centric), Recurrent and severe acute pancreatitis (postnecrotic, recurrent acute), and Obstructive (pancreas divisum, duct obstruction by tumor, preampullary duodenal wall cysts, posttraumatic duct strictures). Some genetic conditions are disease modifiers requiring additional factors to cause disease (p.2750).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2750","Harrison 22e p.2752","Harrison 22e p.2753","Harrison 22e p.2754","Gardner TB et al: ACG clinical guideline: Chronic pancreatitis. Am J (Harrison 22e ch.359)","Vege SS, Chari ST: Chronic pancreatitis. N Engl J Med 386:869, 2022 (Harrison 22e ch.359)"],"crossLinks":["pancreatitis","pancreatic_cancer","diabetes_mellitus"]},"chronic_rhinosinusitis":{"name":"Chronic rhinosinusitis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CRS","chronic sinusitis","chronic rhinosinusitis with or without nasal polyps"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Chronic rhinosinusitis is symptomatic inflammation of the nose and paranasal sinuses persisting for at least twelve weeks","Diagnosis requires two or more cardinal symptoms, at least one being nasal blockage or nasal discharge, alongside facial pressure/pain or reduction in sense of smell","It is broadly classified into disease with nasal polyps and disease without polyps, which differ in inflammatory pattern and treatment response","Objective evidence of inflammation on nasal endoscopy or CT is needed to confirm the diagnosis, as symptoms alone are non-specific","Obstruction of the osteomeatal complex impairing sinus ventilation and mucociliary clearance is central to the disease","Contributing factors include allergy, asthma, aspirin sensitivity, structural abnormalities, biofilms and, in some cases, fungal disease and immune deficiency","First-line treatment is medical, centred on saline irrigation and topical intranasal corticosteroids, with other measures directed at the underlying driver","Functional endoscopic sinus surgery is reserved for disease that fails maximal medical therapy or where complications or specific anatomy demand it"],"pathophysiology":"Chronic rhinosinusitis reflects a persistent inflammatory process of the sinonasal mucosa rather than simple ongoing infection. Anatomical narrowing or mucosal swelling at the osteomeatal complex obstructs sinus outflow, disrupting ventilation and mucociliary clearance so that secretions stagnate and mucosal inflammation is perpetuated. Host factors (allergy, asthma, aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease, immune dysfunction, ciliary disorders) and environmental factors (microbial biofilms, fungi, irritants) drive different inflammatory endotypes; polypoid disease is often eosinophilic and type 2 driven, whereas non-polypoid disease tends to be more neutrophilic. Chronic inflammation causes mucosal oedema, polyp formation and epithelial remodelling that further obstruct drainage, creating a self-sustaining cycle.","additionalDifferentials":["Allergic and non-allergic rhinitis","Recurrent acute rhinosinusitis","Nasal polyps from other causes","Fungal sinusitis (allergic or invasive)","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis and other granulomatous diseases","Cerebrospinal fluid rhinorrhoea mimicking chronic discharge","Sinonasal neoplasm, especially with unilateral disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nasal endoscopy to document mucopus, oedema and polyps","CT of the paranasal sinuses to assess extent and osteomeatal complex anatomy","Allergy testing where an allergic component is suspected","Nasal or sinus swab for culture in resistant or complicated cases","Biopsy of atypical or unilateral lesions to exclude neoplasia or granulomatous disease","Investigations for immune deficiency or ciliary dysfunction in recalcitrant disease"],"redFlags":["Strictly unilateral symptoms or bloody discharge suggesting neoplasm","Periorbital swelling, proptosis, visual disturbance or ophthalmoplegia indicating orbital complication (an emergency)","Severe headache, high fever, neck stiffness or neurological signs suggesting intracranial spread","Clear watery rhinorrhoea after trauma or surgery suggesting a cerebrospinal fluid leak","Rapidly progressive symptoms with facial numbness or palatal changes in an immunocompromised patient (invasive fungal disease)"],"prognosis":"Most patients achieve good symptom control with appropriate medical therapy and, where needed, surgery, but the condition is often chronic and relapsing, particularly the polypoid, eosinophilic and aspirin-sensitive forms, which frequently recur and may require long-term treatment including biologics.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing chronic rhinosinusitis on symptoms alone without objective endoscopic or CT confirmation","Overlooking a unilateral lesion that could be a tumour","Treating repeatedly with antibiotics rather than addressing the underlying inflammatory driver","Missing orbital or intracranial complications during an acute exacerbation","Failing to consider allergy, asthma or immune deficiency in recurrent disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"chronic_subdural_hematoma":{"name":"Chronic subdural haematoma","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cSDH","chronic SDH","subdural haematoma (chronic)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically affects older adults, often with only trivial or forgotten head trauma weeks earlier, and is more common in those on antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy","Presentation is usually insidious over days to weeks, with headache, confusion, gait disturbance, focal weakness, or a fluctuating conscious level that can mimic dementia or stroke","Brain atrophy in the elderly and in chronic alcohol use increases the subdural space and stretches bridging veins, predisposing to slow venous bleeding","On CT the collection is classically crescent-shaped over the convexity and hypodense or mixed density relative to acute blood; layering or membranes suggest recurrent bleeding of differing ages","Symptomatic collections causing mass effect are commonly managed with surgical drainage (burr-hole or craniotomy), while small asymptomatic collections may be observed","Correction of coagulopathy and careful review of anticoagulation are central to management and to reducing recurrence","Recurrence after drainage is relatively frequent, and subdural drains plus adequate brain re-expansion help lower this risk","Bilateral collections can mask midline shift, so symmetry on imaging should not be taken as reassurance"],"pathophysiology":"A chronic subdural haematoma is a slowly accumulating collection of blood and its breakdown products in the subdural space, between the dura and arachnoid. It often begins with minor tearing of bridging cortical veins, particularly where cerebral atrophy has enlarged the subdural space. An encapsulating membrane forms with fragile, leaky neovessels, and repeated micro-haemorrhages together with osmotic and inflammatory factors allow the collection to expand over weeks. Gradual enlargement produces mass effect on the underlying cortex, causing progressive or fluctuating neurological signs.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute or subacute subdural haematoma","Ischaemic stroke or transient ischaemic attack","Intracranial neoplasm or metastasis","Normal pressure hydrocephalus","Neurodegenerative dementia","Subdural empyema or hygroma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Non-contrast CT head to identify the crescentic collection, its density, and midline shift","MRI brain when the diagnosis or age of blood is uncertain or isodense collections are suspected","Full blood count, coagulation profile, and platelet function assessment","Review of antiplatelet and anticoagulant medication and relevant drug levels or reversal targets"],"redFlags":["Rapid deterioration in conscious level or new pupillary asymmetry suggesting acute rebleeding or herniation","Large collection with significant midline shift and effacement of basal cisterns","New or worsening focal deficit or seizures","Bilateral collections with clinical deterioration despite symmetric imaging"],"prognosis":"Many patients recover well after drainage of a symptomatic collection, but outcome is worse in the frail elderly and those with marked preoperative neurological impairment; recurrence and the need for repeat surgery are recognised problems.","pitfalls":["Attributing gradual cognitive or gait decline to dementia and overlooking a treatable collection","Missing an isodense collection on CT, especially when bilateral and symmetric","Resuming anticoagulation without weighing rebleeding risk against thrombotic risk","Assuming complete resolution immediately after drainage when residual or recurrent collections are common"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"chronic_suppurative_otitis_media":{"name":"Chronic suppurative otitis media","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CSOM","chronic otitis media","chronic middle ear infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by a persistent or recurrent ear discharge through a chronic tympanic membrane perforation with associated hearing loss","Traditionally divided into a tubotympanic (mucosal, safe) type and an atticoantral (squamous, unsafe) type associated with cholesteatoma","The tubotympanic type typically causes intermittent painless mucoid discharge and a central perforation, with a comparatively low complication risk","The atticoantral type is dangerous because cholesteatoma erodes bone and predisposes to serious intratemporal and intracranial complications","Foul-smelling scanty discharge, an attic or marginal perforation, or visible keratin debris should raise suspicion of cholesteatoma","Management principles include aural toileting, topical and systemic antibiotics for active disease, and surgery to eradicate disease and reconstruct hearing","Cholesteatoma is essentially a surgical disease requiring mastoid exploration to remove the sac and prevent complications","Audiometry usually shows conductive loss, but a sensorineural component suggests inner ear involvement"],"pathophysiology":"Chronic suppurative otitis media is long-standing inflammation of the middle ear cleft with a non-intact tympanic membrane. In the tubotympanic type, persistent Eustachian tube dysfunction and recurrent infection maintain mucosal inflammation and a central perforation, with reversible mucosal changes. In the atticoantral type, retraction pockets or migration of squamous epithelium form a cholesteatoma, an expanding keratinising sac whose enzymatic and pressure effects erode ossicles and surrounding bone. This bony destruction can breach the facial canal, labyrinth, or dural plates, opening routes for intracranial spread.","additionalDifferentials":["Otitis externa with discharge","Otitis media with effusion","Tuberculous otitis media","Middle ear or external canal malignancy","Granulomatous disease of the ear","Retained foreign body with secondary infection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Otoscopy and otomicroscopy to define perforation type and detect cholesteatoma","Pure tone audiometry to characterise conductive or mixed hearing loss","Culture and sensitivity of ear discharge","High-resolution CT of the temporal bone to map cholesteatoma extent and bony erosion","MRI where intracranial complications or residual cholesteatoma are suspected"],"redFlags":["Foul discharge with an attic or marginal perforation suggesting cholesteatoma","Vertigo or a positive fistula sign indicating labyrinthine erosion","Facial nerve palsy from facial canal involvement","Severe headache, fever, neck stiffness or altered consciousness suggesting intracranial extension"],"prognosis":"The mucosal (tubotympanic) type generally does well with medical treatment and reconstructive surgery, while the atticoantral type with cholesteatoma carries a higher risk of serious complications and requires surgical eradication with ongoing follow-up for recurrence.","pitfalls":["Treating a discharging ear as simple otitis externa and missing an underlying cholesteatoma","Failing to recognise attic or marginal perforations as high-risk","Overlooking early complications such as vertigo or facial weakness","Relying on medical therapy alone when surgical clearance of cholesteatoma is required"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"chronic_venous_disease":{"name":"Chronic Venous Disease & Lymphedema","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["venous insufficiency","varicose veins","lymphedema"],"chapter":293,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2179-2184","clinicalPearls":["Chronic venous disease spans a spectrum: telangiectasias and reticular veins, to varicose veins, to chronic venous insufficiency with edema, skin changes, and ulceration (p.2179).","Chronic venous insufficiency arises from incompetent veins with venous hypertension and extravasation of fluid and blood elements into the limb; it may occur with superficial venous disease, but more advanced manifestations are usually caused by deep-vein disease (p.2180).","Secondary deep-venous insufficiency most often follows deep-vein thrombosis (post-thrombotic syndrome): valve leaflets become thickened and contracted, losing the ability to prevent retrograde flow, and the vein wall becomes rigid and thick-walled (p.2180).","Lipodermatosclerosis is the combination of induration, hemosiderin deposition, and inflammation, typically in the lower leg just above the ankle (the gaiter area) (p.2180).","Atrophie blanche is a white patch of scar tissue, often with focal telangiectasias and a hyperpigmented border, usually developing near the medial malleolus (p.2180).","A venous ulcer is typically shallow with an irregular border, a base of granulation tissue, and exudate, and occurs near the medial and lateral malleoli (p.2180).","Venous edema is usually pitting early on, but over time may become less pitting and more indurated, particularly with secondary development of lymphatic dysfunction (phlebolymphedema) (p.2180).","Lymphedema is generally a painless condition; it initially involves the foot and progresses up the leg, with early soft pitting edema that later becomes nonpitting with a woody, indurated, fibrotic texture (p.2183-2184).","Stemmer's sign (inability to tent the skin at the base of the toes) and peau d'orange (orange-peel dimpling), along with squaring of the toes, are characteristic skin findings of lymphedema (p.2184).","Worldwide, the most common cause of secondary lymphedema is lymphatic filariasis; in developed countries it is surgical excision or irradiation of axillary/inguinal lymph nodes for cancer (p.2183).","Recurrent bacterial lymphangitis, usually streptococcal, is a very common cause of lymphedema, and lymphedema in turn predisposes to recurrent cellulitis/lymphangitis - a vicious cycle (p.2183-2184)."],"pathophysiology":"In chronic venous disease, incompetent (refluxing) or obstructed veins produce sustained venous hypertension with extravasation of fluid and blood elements into limb tissues. Primary varicose veins arise from defective saphenous valves, intrinsic vein-wall weakness, and high intraluminal pressure; secondary varicosities and chronic venous insufficiency result from deep-venous obstruction or valvular incompetence, classically post-thrombotic (valve leaflets thicken and contract after DVT, the vein becomes rigid, and distal valves become incompetent as high pressure distends the vein). Other secondary causes include May-Thurner syndrome, extrinsic compression by tumor/retroperitoneal fibrosis, arteriovenous fistulas, deep-vein agenesis, and venous malformations. Lymphedema results from impaired lymph transport: deficiency, reflux, or obstruction of lymph vessels causes an imbalance between lymph production and absorption (governed by Starling forces), so protein-rich fluid accumulates in the interstitium and raises tissue osmotic load. Persistent lymphedema triggers inflammatory/immune responses with infiltration of mononuclear cells, fibroblasts, and adipocytes, leading to adipose and collagen deposition in skin and subcutaneous tissues (p.2180, 2182-2183).","additionalDifferentials":["Congestive heart failure (bilateral leg swelling)","Hypoalbuminemia from nephrotic syndrome or severe hepatic disease","Myxedema from hypothyroidism, or pretibial myxedema with Graves' disease","Drug-induced edema (dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, thiazolidinediones)","Lipedema (adipose accumulation thigh-to-ankle sparing the feet, usually in women)","Ruptured leg muscle, hematoma from trauma, popliteal (Baker's) cyst (unilateral swelling)","Acute deep-vein thrombosis"],"infectionMimics":["Cellulitis (erythema and swelling of the affected limb)","Bacterial lymphangitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lymphedema vs chronic venous insufficiency (venous edema is softer with stasis dermatitis, hyperpigmentation, and superficial varicosities)","Lipedema (spares the feet; differentiated by MRI)","Myxedema","Leg ulcers from severe peripheral artery disease / chronic limb-threatening ischemia, diabetic neuropathy, skin cancer, vasculitis, or rarely hydroxyurea (distinct from venous ulcers by location and characteristics)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Venous duplex ultrasonography - principal test; detects obstruction (no flow, echogenic thrombus, non-compressible vein) and reflux (prolonged flow reversal on Valsalva or cuff release)","Air/strain-gauge plethysmography to quantify venous reflux and calf-muscle-pump efficiency","MR, CT, or conventional venography for suspected IVC/iliofemoral obstruction, IVC filter occlusion, extrinsic compression, or May-Thurner syndrome","Bedside Brodie-Trendelenburg test (deep vs superficial reflux) and Perthes test (deep-venous obstruction) - now used infrequently","For lymphedema: abdominal/pelvic ultrasound and CT for obstructing neoplasm; MRI of the limb (honeycomb epifascial pattern; distinguishes lipedema)","Lymphoscintigraphy and lymphangiography (rarely needed) to confirm and distinguish primary from secondary lymphedema","Near-infrared fluorescence optical imaging for quantitative superficial lymph-flow assessment, useful operatively"],"redFlags":["Acute unilateral leg swelling raising concern for deep-vein thrombosis (duration of edema helps distinguish DVT from chronic venous insufficiency)","Suspected proximal venous obstruction (IVC/iliofemoral) warranting intervention","Infected or rapidly worsening venous ulcer","Lymphangiosarcoma - a rare but highly lethal complication of long-standing uncontrolled lymphedema","Recurrent cellulitis/lymphangitis in a lymphedematous limb"],"prognosis":"Approximately 20% of patients with chronic venous insufficiency develop venous ulcers. Recurrent varicose veins occur in up to 50% of patients by 5 years after ligation and stripping. Endovenous thermal ablation achieves 1- and 5-year occlusion rates exceeding ~90%; sclerotherapy averages ~81% and ~74% at 1 and 5 years. Iliac venous stenting has technical success >85% with long-term patency ~75%; venous bypass long-term patency generally exceeds 60%. Lymphedema is a chronic, generally progressive condition; failure to control limb edema may lead to recurrent infections, progressive trophic skin changes, and rarely the highly lethal complication of lymphangiosarcoma (p.2179, 2182-2184).","pitfalls":["Lymphedema is often confused with chronic venous insufficiency, and the two can coexist as phlebolymphedema when venous insufficiency impairs lymphatic function - do not assume a single cause (p.2180).","Distinguish bilateral venous edema and lymphedema from systemic causes (heart failure, hypoalbuminemia, myxedema, drug-induced edema) before attributing swelling to venous disease (p.2180).","Distinguish unilateral leg swelling of chronic venous insufficiency/lymphedema from acute deep-vein thrombosis - the duration of edema helps differentiate them (p.2180, 2183).","Compression garments improve symptoms and aid ulcer healing but are NOT curative and do NOT prevent progression of varicose veins (p.2181).","Antibiotics are NOT indicated for a venous ulcer unless it is infected (p.2181).","Diuretics are contraindicated in lymphedema - they may cause intravascular volume depletion and metabolic abnormalities (p.2184).","No drugs are FDA-approved for chronic venous insufficiency, and there is insufficient evidence to recommend general use of venoactive herbal supplements (p.2182)."],"severityClassification":"CEAP (Clinical, Etiologic, Anatomic, Pathophysiologic) classification of chronic venous disease. Clinical: C0 no signs; C1 telangiectasias/reticular veins; C2 varicose veins (C2r recurrent); C3 edema; C4 skin/subcutaneous changes (C4a pigmentation or eczema, C4b lipodermatosclerosis or atrophie blanche, C4c corona phlebectatica); C5 healed venous ulcer; C6 active venous ulcer (C6r recurrent). Etiologic: Ep primary, Es secondary (Esi intravenous, Ese extravenous), Ec congenital, En none identified. Anatomic: As superficial, Ap perforator, Ad deep, An none. Pathophysiologic: Pr reflux, Po obstruction, Pr,o both, Pn none. Lymphedema is staged by the International Society of Lymphology: Stage 0/Ia latent/subclinical; Stage I early protein-rich edema reduced by elevation, may pit; Stage II elevation rarely reduces swelling, pitting manifest then later non-pitting as fat/fibrosis supervene; Stage III lymphostatic elephantiasis with trophic skin changes, acanthosis, fat/fibrosis, warty overgrowths (p.2181, 2184).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2179-2184","Harrison 22e Chapter 293: Chronic Venous Disease and Lymphedema (Creager, Eberhardt, Loscalzo)"],"crossLinks":["dvt"]},"chylothorax":{"name":"Chylothorax","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chylothorax","chylous effusion","chylous pleural effusion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Chylothorax is the accumulation of lymphatic fluid (chyle) in the pleural space due to disruption or obstruction of the thoracic duct","The aspirated fluid is classically milky and opalescent, though it may appear serous when the patient is fasting or malnourished","The diagnosis is confirmed by an elevated pleural fluid triglyceride level and, when uncertain, the demonstration of chylomicrons","Common causes are malignancy (especially lymphoma) obstructing lymphatic flow and trauma or surgery injuring the thoracic duct","Non-traumatic causes also include lymphangioleiomyomatosis, cirrhosis, and central venous thrombosis","Persistent drainage of chyle leads to loss of protein, fat, fat-soluble vitamins, and lymphocytes, causing malnutrition and immunosuppression","Initial management often includes drainage and a low-fat or medium-chain-triglyceride diet, or bowel rest, to reduce chyle flow","Refractory or high-output leaks may require thoracic duct ligation, embolisation, pleurodesis, or shunting"],"pathophysiology":"The thoracic duct conveys chyle, rich in triglycerides absorbed from the gut, from the abdomen to the systemic venous circulation. When the duct is disrupted by surgery or trauma, or obstructed by tumour or fibrosis, chyle leaks into or backs up into the pleural space. Because chyle contains chylomicrons and high triglyceride content, the effusion has a characteristic milky appearance and high triglyceride level. Ongoing leakage depletes the body of nutrients, immunoglobulins, and lymphocytes, and the volume loss can cause hypovolaemia and metabolic disturbance.","additionalDifferentials":["Empyema (which can also appear turbid)","Pseudochylothorax (cholesterol effusion in chronic effusions)","Malignant pleural effusion without chyle","Parapneumonic effusion","Transudative effusion from heart failure or cirrhosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pleural fluid triglyceride measurement and, if equivocal, lipoprotein analysis for chylomicrons","Cross-sectional imaging to identify malignancy or thoracic duct obstruction","Lymphangiography or lymphoscintigraphy to localise the site of leak in selected cases","Assessment of nutritional status and lymphocyte count with prolonged drainage","Investigation for underlying causes such as lymphoma or lymphangioleiomyomatosis"],"redFlags":["High-output chyle leak causing rapid nutritional and immune depletion","Signs of malnutrition, hypovolaemia, or lymphopenia-related infection","Underlying malignancy such as lymphoma requiring urgent evaluation","Respiratory compromise from a large or rapidly reaccumulating effusion"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the underlying cause and leak volume; traumatic and postsurgical chylothoraces often settle with conservative measures, whereas malignant or high-output leaks are more difficult to control and prolonged chyle loss risks serious malnutrition and immunosuppression if not addressed.","pitfalls":["Assuming turbid pleural fluid is empyema without measuring triglycerides","Overlooking a false-negative milky appearance in a fasting patient","Allowing prolonged high-volume drainage without addressing nutritional and immune depletion","Failing to investigate for an underlying malignancy in non-traumatic cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"cirrhosis_and_its_complications":{"name":"Cirrhosis and Its Complications","system":"Gastrointestinal/Hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Chronic liver disease with fibrosis","Hepatic cirrhosis","Decompensated cirrhosis","Alcohol-associated cirrhosis","Posthepatitic cirrhosis"],"chapter":355,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2708","clinicalPearls":["Cirrhosis is the end stage of many chronic liver injuries: progressive fibrosis disrupts normal architecture and replaces hepatocytes with regenerative nodules; the main etiologic groups are alcohol-associated, chronic viral hepatitis B/C, biliary, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH, formerly NASH), and miscellaneous (cardiac, cryptogenic) (p.2708).","Alcohol-associated cirrhosis is classically micronodular (nodules <3 mm); with abstinence larger nodules may form, giving a mixed micronodular/macronodular pattern (p.2708).","In alcohol-related liver disease the AST is typically higher than the ALT, usually in roughly a 2:1 ratio, and aminotransferases are generally <400 IU/mL (p.2708-2709).","Stigmata of chronic liver disease include scleral icterus, palmar erythema, spider angiomas, parotid enlargement, digital clubbing, muscle wasting, and in men decreased body hair, gynecomastia and testicular atrophy; these are often reversible with abstinence (p.2710).","Portal hypertension is defined as a hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG) >5 mmHg; clinically significant portal hypertension is an HVPG >=10 mmHg, carrying substantial risk of decompensation (variceal bleeding, ascites, encephalopathy) (p.2713).","A serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) >=1.1 g/dL indicates portal hypertension as the cause of ascites; cardiac ascites also shows SAAG >=1.1 g/dL but with ascitic protein >2.5 g/dL, whereas SAAG <1.1 g/dL points to infectious or malignant causes (p.2715).","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis is diagnosed when the ascitic absolute neutrophil count is >250/uL; ascitic protein <1.5 g/dL marks patients at higher risk for SBP (p.2715-2716).","Most common SBP organisms are Escherichia coli and other gut flora; growth of more than two organisms should raise suspicion for secondary peritonitis from a perforated viscus (p.2716).","Hepatorenal syndrome is functional renal failure without renal histopathology; HRS-AKI (old type 1) is rapidly progressive while HRS-CKD (old type 2) is more stable with a better prognosis (p.2716).","In hepatic encephalopathy ammonia is usually elevated, but its level correlates poorly with disease severity, so most hepatologists do not rely on ammonia to diagnose or follow the condition; asterixis (liver flap) is a key bedside sign (p.2716-2717).","Standard PT/INR do not reliably measure clotting capacity in cirrhosis because both pro- and anticoagulant factor synthesis fall (rebalanced hemostasis); adequate thrombin generation occurs at platelet counts above ~50,000-60,000/L (p.2717).","Acute-on-chronic liver failure is acute decompensation of cirrhosis with failure of one or more organ systems, behaves like a sepsis-type systemic inflammatory syndrome, and carries 28-day mortality of roughly 20-70% rising with the number of organ failures (p.2717)."],"pathophysiology":"Chronic, repeated hepatocellular injury (toxic, viral, immune, metabolic, cholestatic, or congestive) drives Kupffer cell activation and release of profibrogenic cytokines that activate hepatic stellate cells, producing excess collagen and extracellular matrix. Connective tissue links portal triads to central veins, hepatocytes are lost, and regenerative nodules form, so the liver contracts and loses normal architecture - a process taking years to decades. The resulting distorted vasculature raises intrahepatic resistance, which together with splanchnic arterial vasodilation (driven by mediators such as nitric oxide) produces portal hypertension. Splanchnic vasodilation causes effective arterial underfilling, activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone and sympathetic systems, leading to renal sodium and water retention, plasma volume expansion, and a hyperdynamic circulation; combined with low oncotic pressure from hypoalbuminemia, this generates ascites and edema. Portosystemic shunting plus reduced hepatic mass allows gut-derived neurotoxins (ammonia, false neurotransmitters, mercaptans) to reach the brain and cause encephalopathy (p.2708, p.2713-2717).","additionalDifferentials":["Non-cirrhotic portal hypertension (prehepatic portal/splenic vein thrombosis, presinusoidal schistosomiasis or congenital hepatic fibrosis)","Budd-Chiari syndrome and sinusoidal obstruction syndrome as posthepatic outflow causes","Congestive hepatopathy from right-sided heart failure (cardiac cirrhosis)","Cryptogenic cirrhosis later reclassified as burned-out MASH","Infiltrative or malignant liver disease and peritoneal carcinomatosis as alternative causes of ascites"],"infectionMimics":["Schistosomiasis causing presinusoidal portal hypertension that mimics cirrhotic portal hypertension","Chronic viral hepatitis B or C as an underlying driver where the inflammatory/fibrotic picture overlaps with other cirrhosis etiologies"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cardiac cirrhosis / congestive hepatopathy from chronic right heart failure","Budd-Chiari syndrome (extravasation of red cells on biopsy helps distinguish from cardiac hepatopathy)","Sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (venoocclusive disease) after marrow transplant conditioning or pyrrolizidine alkaloid ingestion","Malignant or cardiac ascites distinguished from cirrhotic ascites by SAAG and ascitic protein"],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnostic paracentesis with ascitic total protein, albumin (for SAAG), cell count with differential, and cultures (inject into blood-culture bottles); amylase and cytology when indicated","Upper GI endoscopy to screen for and characterize gastroesophageal varices and portal hypertensive gastropathy","Etiology workup: HCV RNA and genotype; HBsAg, HBeAg, anti-HBe and HBV DNA; autoimmune markers (ANA, ASMA); iron studies with transferrin saturation/ferritin and HFE testing; ceruloplasmin and 24-h urinary copper; alpha-1 antitrypsin level and phenotype","Contrast-enhanced CT or MRI to show nodular liver and portosystemic collaterals; noninvasive fibrosis tests and, when needed, liver biopsy to confirm cirrhosis","HVPG measurement in selected cases; MRCP (and ERCP if needed) for suspected primary sclerosing cholangitis","DEXA for osteoporosis in cholestatic disease; bubble echocardiography and right-heart catheterization for hepatopulmonary syndrome and portopulmonary hypertension"],"redFlags":["Variceal hemorrhage - immediately life-threatening, with about 20-30% mortality per bleeding episode","New or worsening hepatic encephalopathy, especially with violent agitation or progressive obtundation","Rapidly rising creatinine in a patient with tense ascites suggesting HRS-AKI","Fever, abdominal pain, altered mental status or new AKI in a patient with ascites suggesting SBP","Acute-on-chronic liver failure with multiorgan failure","Refractory ascites failing maximal diuretics"],"prognosis":"Severity and bleeding risk are stratified by Child-Pugh class and MELD score. Once clinically significant portal hypertension leads to decompensation, median survival is under 2 years. Less than half of patients survive 2 years after the onset of ascites. Patients with complicated cirrhosis who keep drinking have under 50% 5-year survival, whereas abstinence markedly improves outlook. Development of encephalopathy is a poor prognostic sign, and HRS (AKI or CKD type) carries a poor prognosis without transplantation. ACLF 28-day mortality ranges from roughly 20-70%, rising with the number of failing organs. Liver transplantation is the definitive option for end-stage disease and can reverse HRS (p.2709, p.2713, p.2715-2717).","pitfalls":["Relying on PT/INR (or routinely correcting it before procedures) to gauge bleeding risk in cirrhosis - hemostasis is rebalanced and these tests are unreliable","Trusting ammonia levels to diagnose or grade hepatic encephalopathy despite poor correlation with severity","Restricting dietary protein for encephalopathy, which worsens malnutrition and is strongly discouraged","Missing SBP because classic signs may be absent - a high index of suspicion and paracentesis are needed in hospitalized ascitic patients","Using nephrotoxic drugs (NSAIDs, aminoglycosides) or unmonitored ACE inhibitors/ARBs in patients with ascites, precipitating renal failure","Forgetting albumin replacement after large-volume paracentesis (>=5 L) to prevent post-paracentesis circulatory dysfunction","Overlooking sarcopenia masked by obesity or edema in decompensated cirrhosis"],"severityClassification":"Compensated cirrhosis (HVPG 5-10 mmHg) may be asymptomatic for >=10 years; clinically significant portal hypertension is HVPG >=10 mmHg. Decompensation is marked by variceal bleeding, ascites, or encephalopathy. Overall severity is graded by Child-Pugh class and MELD score, which also predict variceal bleeding risk and guide transplant prioritization. Acute kidney injury is defined as a creatinine rise of 0.3 mg/dL over 48 h or a 50% rise from baseline; SBP is defined by ascitic neutrophils >250/uL (p.2713, p.2715-2716).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2708","Harrison 22e p.2709","Harrison 22e p.2710","Harrison 22e p.2711","Harrison 22e p.2713","Harrison 22e p.2714","Harrison 22e p.2715","Harrison 22e p.2716","Harrison 22e p.2717"],"crossLinks":[]},"classification_of_cardiomyopathy":{"name":"Classification of Cardiomyopathy","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Cardiomyopathy classification","Cardiomyopathy phenotypes","HCM/DCM/RCM classification"],"chapter":266,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2001","clinicalPearls":["Cardiomyopathy denotes primary disease of the heart muscle itself; strictly it excludes myocardial dysfunction secondary to other cardiovascular disease, though common usage also folds in ischemic, valvular, and hypertensive cardiomyopathy.","The traditional morphologic scheme recognizes three major phenotypes: hypertrophic (HCM), dilated (DCM), and restrictive (RCM) cardiomyopathy.","HCM is characterized by increased left ventricular wall thickness (roughly 13-15 mm depending on context) with a normal or high ejection fraction; LV chamber volume is normal or smaller, and EF is usually above 0.60.","DCM is defined when LVEF is at or below 0.50, although most patients present clinically with LVEF at or below 0.40; an LVEF persistently at or below 0.30-0.35 is the threshold considered for primary-prevention ICD.","RCM is the least common phenotype and is defined more by physiology (moderate-to-severe diastolic dysfunction and/or elevated cardiac filling pressures) than by morphology; wall thickness is often increased but may appear normal, EF is usually only mildly reduced, and marked atrial enlargement with predominant right heart failure is typical.","Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (ARVC, originally arrhythmogenic right ventricular dysplasia) is a fourth, predominantly genetic phenotype marked by life-threatening arrhythmias and abnormal RV structure/function with variable LV involvement.","Evolving genotype-driven terminology reframes arrhythmogenic disease as ACM-RV (RV-predominant, formerly ARVC), ACM-LV (LV-predominant), and biventricular arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy; in ACM-LV ventricular arrhythmias can dominate before LVEF is severely reduced, so primary-prevention ICD may be considered even with LVEF above 0.35.","Phenotypes overlap and shift over time: DCM can undergo reverse remodeling toward higher EF and smaller ventricles with recommended therapy, and about 5% of HCM evolves to a reduced-EF, more restrictive state (HCM-rEF).","Mid-range LVEF (0.40-0.50) commonly represents a transition state, either deterioration from early DCM or improvement into DCM remission.","Clinical expression frequently follows a two-hit model in which an inherited predisposition is triggered by an acquired insult such as infection, toxic exposure, pregnancy, or tachycardia.","Echocardiography is the initial imaging modality; cardiac MRI adds tissue characterization, with late gadolinium enhancement mapping fibrosis and T1/T2 mapping identifying focal and diffuse inflammation.","Left ventricular noncompaction is a trait (often assessed by a noncompacted-to-compacted myocardium ratio above 2.3) that can accompany HCM, DCM, dystrophies, and syndromes, but can also appear in normal hearts, in pregnancy, and more often in athletes."],"pathophysiology":"Cardiomyopathy reflects intrinsic disease of the myocardium producing characteristic structural and functional abnormalities. In HCM, abnormal myocyte/sarcomere biology drives increased wall thickness with preserved or supranormal systolic function but impaired filling. In DCM, the ventricle dilates with depressed systolic function (low LVEF). In RCM, the dominant abnormality is impaired ventricular filling from a stiff myocardium causing markedly elevated filling pressures and atrial enlargement, often with relatively preserved EF. Arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy involves fibrofatty or fibrotic remodeling that creates a substrate for ventricular arrhythmias with variable RV and/or LV structural change. An expanding catalog of pathogenic genetic variants underlies heritable cardiomyopathy, and inherited differences in immune-response pathways contribute to infectious and noninfectious inflammation that can manifest as myocarditis and cardiomyopathy. Clinical disease often requires a second acquired insult superimposed on a genetic predisposition.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) and its variants of LV hypertrophy: asymmetric septal, sigmoid/inverse septal, concentric, and apical hypertrophy","Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM), including arrhythmogenic ACM-LV which usually presents with a DCM phenotype","Restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM)","Arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy: ACM-RV (ARVC), ACM-LV, and biventricular forms","Left ventricular noncompaction trait"],"infectionMimics":["Chagas' disease (Trypanosoma cruzi) protozoal myocarditis, which can produce an arrhythmogenic phenotype (ACM-RV/ACM-LV)","Clinical myocarditis from infectious inflammation that can mimic or trigger a cardiomyopathy phenotype"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Athlete's heart (mimics HCM and can show LV noncompaction)","Severe chronic hypertension (mimics HCM)","Aortic stenosis (mimics HCM and should be excluded)","Cardiac amyloidosis (causes RCM and can produce asymmetric septal thickening mimicking HCM, especially in older patients)","Storage diseases mimicking HCM morphology: GLA/Anderson-Fabry, PRKAG2, and LAMP2/Danon disease","Cardiac sarcoidosis (often DCM-pattern or regional wall-motion abnormalities, or RV involvement with arrhythmias, rather than classic restrictive morphology)","Coronary artery disease/prior infarction and primary valve disease as structural causes mimicking DCM","Radiation, scleroderma-type connective tissue disease, and drugs such as doxorubicin causing an RCM phenotype","PVC-related cardiomyopathy versus genetic ACM in patients with frequent PVCs or nonsustained VT"],"additionalInvestigations":["Two-dimensional and Doppler echocardiography as first-line imaging","Cardiac MRI for late gadolinium enhancement (fibrosis) and T1/T2 mapping (focal and diffuse inflammation)","Electrocardiogram and chest radiograph","Multigene cardiomyopathy panel with genetic counseling; detailed family history of cardiomyopathy, skeletal myopathy, conduction disease, tachyarrhythmia, and sudden death","Chemistries including sodium, potassium, calcium, magnesium, glucose/glycohemoglobin, creatinine/BUN, albumin and liver tests, lipid profile, TSH, serum iron and transferrin saturation, creatine kinase isoforms, and cardiac troponin","Hematology including CBC with differential, total eosinophil count if abnormal, and ESR","Targeted infection workup when suspected: HIV, Chagas', Lyme and other tick-borne disease, toxoplasmosis, trichinosis; respiratory pathogen panel in acute respiratory syndromes","Endomyocardial biopsy (with electron microscopy sample) when a specific diagnosis with therapeutic implications is suspected","Coronary angiography/catheterization in patients with evidence of ischemia or infarction who are candidates for intervention"],"redFlags":["Ventricular tachyarrhythmias or nonsustained VT, which may dominate before EF is severely reduced in arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy","Family history of sudden death, cardiomyopathy, conduction disorders, or skeletal myopathy","Embolic events from atrial fibrillation or apical ventricular thrombus as a presentation of unrecognized cardiomyopathy","Progressive exertional breathlessness, fatigue, orthopnea, edema, and abdominal symptoms indicating the congestion syndrome","Marked wall thickening suggesting amyloidosis, inherited storage disease, or inborn metabolic disease"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the specific phenotype and underlying cause rather than on classification alone. DCM may improve substantially with reverse remodeling on guideline-directed therapy, whereas about 5% of HCM deteriorates to a reduced-EF, restrictive state. Arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy carries a risk of life-threatening ventricular arrhythmias that can precede heart failure. Persistent severe LV systolic dysfunction (LVEF at or below 0.30-0.35) identifies patients considered for primary-prevention ICD.","pitfalls":["Treating 'congestive heart failure' as a diagnosis or etiology; it is a nonspecific congestion syndrome common to many cardiac conditions (including congenital heart disease, primary pulmonary hypertension, and valve disease) and requires full etiologic evaluation.","Over-relying on morphologic phenotype, since phenotypes overlap and shift over time and many entities (sarcomeric variants, storage diseases) can present as either HCM or RCM.","Missing amyloidosis in older patients, where it can both cause RCM and mimic the asymmetric septal thickening of HCM.","Misclassifying sarcoidosis as restrictive when it more often produces DCM-pattern, regional wall-motion abnormalities, or RV involvement with arrhythmias.","Failing to distinguish PVC-related cardiomyopathy from a genetic arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy that itself causes both the arrhythmias and the muscle disease.","Interpreting LV noncompaction as inherently pathologic, since it can occur in normal hearts, in pregnancy, and in athletes."],"severityClassification":"Morphologic/functional classification: Hypertrophic (HCM) - septal thickness >=15 mm in men, >=13 mm in women (13-14 mm may suffice in relatives of a proband or with a positive genetic test), LVEF normal or high (usually >0.60), normal/small LV volume. Dilated (DCM) - early: LVEF <=0.50 and/or LV end-diastolic volume >112% of normal for age/sex or LVEDD >95th percentile; LVEF <=0.40 is the threshold for traditionally recommended heart-failure therapy; persistent LVEF <=0.30-0.35 is the threshold for primary-prevention ICD. Restrictive (RCM) - functional diagnosis based on moderate-to-severe diastolic dysfunction and/or elevated filling pressures, usually mildly reduced LVEF, with marked atrial enlargement. Mid-range LVEF (0.40-0.50) represents a transition zone. Arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy - ACM-LV (usually DCM morphology), ACM-RV/ARVC (2010 modified task force criteria combining major and minor criteria), and biventricular forms. LV noncompaction trait assessed by noncompacted/compacted ratio >2.3.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2001","Harrison 22e p.2002"],"crossLinks":[]},"cleft_lip_palate":{"name":"Cleft lip and palate","system":"Plastic / Reconstructive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cleft lip","cleft palate","orofacial cleft","CLP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery / standard references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Orofacial clefts are among the most common congenital anomalies and may involve the lip only, the palate only, or both, and may be unilateral or bilateral, complete or incomplete","Cleft lip with or without cleft palate and isolated cleft palate are considered distinct entities with different embryology, epidemiology, and syndromic associations","A cleft may be an isolated finding or part of a syndrome, so a thorough assessment for other anomalies and genetic evaluation is important","Feeding difficulty is common with palatal clefts because the infant cannot generate adequate suction, and specialised bottles, teats, or feeding plates help maintain nutrition","Care is delivered by a multidisciplinary cleft team including surgery, orthodontics, speech and language therapy, audiology, ENT, genetics, and psychology","Lip repair and palate repair are staged over infancy, with palate closure timed to optimise speech development while balancing effects on facial growth","Cleft palate is associated with middle ear effusion and hearing loss from Eustachian tube dysfunction, requiring ongoing audiological monitoring","Long-term issues include speech and resonance problems (velopharyngeal insufficiency), dental and maxillary growth abnormalities, and the potential need for alveolar bone grafting and later orthognathic surgery"],"pathophysiology":"Orofacial clefting results from failure of fusion of the embryonic facial processes. Cleft lip arises from incomplete fusion of the medial nasal and maxillary processes, which normally unite to form the primary palate, during early facial development. Cleft palate results from failure of the palatal shelves to elevate, meet, and fuse in the midline to form the secondary palate. The aetiology is multifactorial, involving genetic susceptibility and environmental influences such as maternal smoking, alcohol, folate deficiency, and certain medications, and clefts can also occur as part of recognised syndromes.","additionalDifferentials":["Submucous cleft palate (occult, with intact overlying mucosa)","Bifid uvula as a marker of possible submucous cleft","Pierre Robin sequence with cleft palate and airway compromise","Median or oblique facial clefts","Van der Woude and other syndromic clefts","Velopharyngeal insufficiency from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Prenatal ultrasound detection of cleft lip and counselling","Comprehensive newborn examination for associated anomalies","Genetic evaluation and karyotype or microarray when a syndrome is suspected","Audiological assessment and tympanometry for middle ear effusion","Speech and resonance assessment, including nasendoscopy or videofluoroscopy for velopharyngeal function","Dental and orthodontic evaluation and imaging before alveolar bone grafting"],"redFlags":["Airway obstruction in Pierre Robin sequence or syndromic clefts, particularly with retrognathia","Failure to thrive from inadequate feeding and poor weight gain","Recurrent or persistent middle ear disease causing hearing loss and speech delay","Features suggesting an underlying syndrome with cardiac or other organ involvement"],"prognosis":"With coordinated multidisciplinary care most children achieve good functional and aesthetic outcomes, normal feeding, and intelligible speech, though some require secondary speech surgery, alveolar bone grafting, orthodontics, and orthognathic surgery into adolescence. Outcomes are influenced by cleft severity, associated syndromes, and access to specialist team care.","pitfalls":["Missing a submucous cleft palate in a child with speech or feeding problems and a bifid uvula","Overlooking associated anomalies or an underlying syndrome","Underestimating feeding and airway difficulties in the neonatal period","Neglecting audiological follow-up, allowing undetected hearing loss to impair speech development","Timing palate repair without balancing speech benefit against effects on midface growth"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"clubfoot":{"name":"Clubfoot","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["congenital talipes equinovarus","CTEV","talipes","congenital clubfoot"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A congenital deformity present at birth in which the foot is held in equinus, hindfoot varus, midfoot cavus, and forefoot adductus (remembered as the components of talipes equinovarus)","The key clinical distinction is between a rigid, structural clubfoot and a flexible 'postural' foot that corrects with gentle manipulation","It is often idiopathic but may be associated with neuromuscular conditions (e.g. spina bifida), syndromes, or oligohydramnios, so a general and neurological examination is important","Diagnosis is clinical; the deformity is frequently detected on antenatal ultrasound, and examination should exclude associated abnormalities such as developmental dysplasia of the hip","The Ponseti method of serial casting is the mainstay of treatment, gradually correcting the deformity, usually followed by a percutaneous Achilles tenotomy to address residual equinus","Maintenance with a foot-abduction (boots-and-bar) orthosis is essential to prevent relapse, and poor brace compliance is the commonest cause of recurrence","Treatment should begin early in the neonatal period when the tissues are most amenable to correction","Surgery is reserved for resistant, relapsed, or neglected deformities not corrected by casting"],"pathophysiology":"Congenital talipes equinovarus arises from abnormal development and positioning of the bones, joints, muscles, and soft tissues of the foot and ankle, with contracture of the medial and posterior soft tissues (including the tendo Achillis, tibialis posterior, and plantar structures) and abnormal alignment of the talus, calcaneus, and navicular. The exact cause is multifactorial, with genetic predisposition and intrauterine factors implicated; in syndromic or neuromuscular cases an underlying disorder disturbs the muscle balance across the developing foot. The result is a fixed deformity combining hindfoot equinus and varus with midfoot cavus and forefoot adduction, which becomes increasingly rigid and structurally established if untreated.","additionalDifferentials":["Postural (positional) talipes that corrects fully with manipulation","Metatarsus adductus","Congenital vertical talus","Neuromuscular clubfoot (e.g. secondary to spina bifida or arthrogryposis)","Syndromic foot deformity","Tibial or fibular deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination to assess rigidity and classify severity","Full musculoskeletal and neurological examination to detect associated conditions","Examination and, if indicated, imaging of the hips for developmental dysplasia","Antenatal ultrasound detection and postnatal confirmation","Spinal imaging or specialist assessment where a neuromuscular cause is suspected"],"redFlags":["Associated neuromuscular disorder or spinal dysraphism requiring broader assessment","Failure to progress with casting, or early relapse, indicating a resistant or syndromic foot","Neglected or late-presenting rigid deformity limiting non-surgical correction","Signs of an alternative diagnosis such as congenital vertical talus"],"prognosis":"With early Ponseti treatment and disciplined brace use, most idiopathic clubfeet achieve a functional, plantigrade, pain-free foot, though a minority relapse and need repeat casting or surgery; syndromic and neuromuscular clubfeet are stiffer, more prone to recurrence, and have a less predictable outcome.","pitfalls":["Mistaking a rigid structural clubfoot for a flexible postural foot and delaying treatment","Under-emphasising brace compliance, the leading cause of relapse","Overlooking an associated neuromuscular or syndromic condition, or concurrent hip dysplasia","Attempting forceful correction or premature surgery instead of graduated casting"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"cluster_headache":{"name":"Cluster headache","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia","histamine headache","suicide headache"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by attacks of severe, strictly unilateral pain centred on the orbit or temple, usually lasting between 15 minutes and 3 hours","Attacks are accompanied by ipsilateral cranial autonomic features such as lacrimation, conjunctival redness, nasal congestion or rhinorrhoea, and sometimes a partial Horner syndrome","Unlike migraine sufferers who prefer to lie still, patients are typically restless and agitated during an attack, often pacing or rocking","Attacks cluster into bouts lasting weeks to months separated by remission periods, and there is a striking circadian and seasonal rhythm with nocturnal attacks common","Alcohol and other vasodilators can reliably provoke attacks during an active cluster period but not during remission","High-flow oxygen and fast-acting triptan therapy are the mainstays of acute abortive treatment, while verapamil is a common preventive principle","The condition shows a male predominance and is strongly linked with tobacco use","Neuroimaging is warranted at presentation to exclude a structural or pituitary lesion mimicking the syndrome"],"pathophysiology":"Cluster headache is the most common trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia. The pain reflects activation of the trigeminovascular system, while the accompanying autonomic signs arise from cranial parasympathetic outflow and sympathetic dysfunction. The pronounced circadian and seasonal periodicity implicates the posterior hypothalamus, which shows activation on functional imaging during attacks and is thought to act as the disorder's generator or pacemaker.","additionalDifferentials":["Migraine, particularly unilateral migraine","Paroxysmal hemicrania (indomethacin-responsive)","SUNCT/SUNA syndromes","Trigeminal neuralgia","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Secondary headache from a pituitary or posterior fossa lesion or carotid dissection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain with attention to the pituitary and cavernous sinus to exclude secondary causes","MR or CT angiography when a vascular cause such as dissection or aneurysm is suspected","Pituitary hormone screen if imaging suggests a sellar lesion","ECG before starting verapamil and monitoring during dose changes"],"redFlags":["First or worst attack, or a change in established attack pattern, suggesting a secondary cause","Persistent Horner syndrome or other fixed neurological deficit between attacks","Features suggesting carotid dissection such as neck pain with Horner syndrome","Papilloedema or other signs of raised intracranial pressure"],"prognosis":"Episodic cluster headache typically follows a relapsing course with prolonged remissions, whereas a minority develop chronic cluster headache with little or no remission. Preventive therapy substantially reduces attack frequency in most patients, but the disorder can be highly disabling during active bouts.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing the attacks as migraine or sinusitis and delaying effective treatment","Overlooking paroxysmal hemicrania, which is exquisitely responsive to indomethacin, in short frequent attacks","Failing to obtain neuroimaging to exclude a secondary or pituitary cause","Starting verapamil without baseline and follow-up ECG monitoring for conduction effects"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"coagulation_disorders":{"name":"Coagulation Disorders","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hemophilia","von Willebrand disease","bleeding disorders"],"chapter":121,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.928-932","clinicalPearls":["The most common inherited factor deficiencies are the hemophilias, X-linked recessive diseases due to deficiency of factor VIII (hemophilia A, ~80% of cases) or factor IX (hemophilia B); hemophilia A and B are clinically indistinguishable (Harrison 22e p.928, 930).","Disease phenotype correlates with factor activity level: severe (<1%), moderate (1-5%), mild (6-30%). Severe and moderate disease cause bleeding into joints (hemarthrosis), soft tissues, and muscles after minor trauma or spontaneously; mild disease bleeds infrequently and usually only after trauma (Harrison 22e p.930).","Recurrent hemarthroses (knees, elbows, ankles, shoulders, hips) damage a joint to create a chronically affected 'target joint' with synovitis and progressive deformity; deep-muscle hematomas can cause compartment syndrome by compressing arteries, veins, or nerves (Harrison 22e p.930).","Von Willebrand disease (VWD) is the most common inherited bleeding disorder (symptomatic prevalence ~1 in 1000 to 1 in 10,000); VWF tethers platelets to subendothelium and is the carrier protein that prolongs the FVIII half-life (Harrison 22e p.927).","Most VWD symptoms are 'platelet-like' (mucocutaneous: bruising, epistaxis, heavy menstrual bleeding); type 1 (parallel reduction in VWF protein, function, and FVIII) is most common (>=80% of cases) (Harrison 22e p.926-927).","Type 2N VWD ('autosomal hemophilia') is caused by VWF variants that impair FVIII binding, markedly shortening the FVIII half-life and lowering FVIII levels (Harrison 22e p.927).","Screening hemostasis tests: an isolated prolonged PT suggests FVII deficiency; a prolonged aPTT indicates an intrinsic-pathway deficiency, most commonly FVIII, FIX, or FXI; prolongation of both PT and aPTT suggests deficiency of FV, FX, FII, or a fibrinogen abnormality (Harrison 22e p.928).","Mixing study principle: adding normal pooled plasma corrects a prolonged PT/aPTT due to a factor deficiency but fails to correct when an inhibitor (antibody) is present; if it corrects, individual factor activity assays identify the deficient factor (Harrison 22e p.928).","Inhibitor (alloantibody) formation is the major complication of hemophilia treatment, with prevalence ~30% in severe hemophilia A, ~10% in nonsevere hemophilia A, and only 3-5% in hemophilia B; inhibitors increase morbidity and mortality (Harrison 22e p.930-931).","Acquired coagulation factor deficiencies are more frequent than congenital disorders; the most common are the hemorrhagic diathesis of liver disease, DIC, and vitamin K deficiency, each typically involving more than one clotting factor (Harrison 22e p.928)."],"pathophysiology":"Plasma coagulation factor deficiencies impair secondary hemostasis (the coagulation cascade). Inherited hemophilia A and B are X-linked recessive disorders from mutations in F8 and F9 respectively; affected males bleed while carrier women are generally asymptomatic, although a wide range of factor activity from random X-inactivation (lyonization) can produce bleeding in some carriers. FVIII has a very short intrinsic circulating half-life (~25-30 min) that is extended to roughly 12 h when complexed with von Willebrand factor (VWF). VWD reflects quantitative or qualitative defects of VWF, which serves both as the adhesion molecule tethering platelets to exposed subendothelium (function dependent on large VWF multimers) and as the FVIII carrier; loss of high-molecular-weight multimers (e.g., via excessive ADAMTS13 cleavage in type 2A/2B, or shear-induced unfolding) impairs platelet adhesion. Acquired deficiencies (liver disease, DIC, vitamin K deficiency) usually involve multiple factors and perturb both primary (platelet/vessel wall) and secondary (coagulation) hemostasis. (Harrison 22e p.926-930)","additionalDifferentials":["Hemophilia A versus hemophilia B (clinically indistinguishable; require factor assays to differentiate) (Harrison 22e p.930)","Von Willebrand disease with low FVIII versus hemophilia A (VWD must be excluded in a patient with prolonged aPTT and low FVIII and no known hemophilia history) (Harrison 22e p.930)","Factor XI deficiency (hemophilia C) and other rare inherited factor deficiencies (FII, FV, FVII, FX, FXIII, fibrinogen) (Harrison 22e p.928, 931-932)","Qualitative platelet function disorders (e.g., Glanzmann thrombasthenia, Bernard-Soulier syndrome, storage pool disorder) as a cause of mucocutaneous bleeding with normal coagulation factor levels (Harrison 22e p.926)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acquired von Willebrand syndrome from lymphoproliferative disorders / MGUS / multiple myeloma / Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia (suspect with new-onset severe mucosal bleeding, especially in the elderly) (Harrison 22e p.927)","Acquired VWD due to high shear: cardiac valvular disease and Heyde syndrome (aortic stenosis with GI bleeding from angiodysplasia; shear unfolds VWF causing loss of large multimers, reversed by valve replacement) (Harrison 22e p.927)","Acquired VWD in myeloproliferative disorders with markedly elevated platelet counts (>1.5 million) due to platelet-VWF binding and clearance (Harrison 22e p.926)","Acquired inhibitors (autoantibodies) to clotting factors in patients without genetic deficiency: postpartum, autoimmune or neoplastic disease, idiopathic, or rarely after topical bovine thrombin exposure in complex surgery (Harrison 22e p.928)","Liver disease, DIC, and vitamin K deficiency as acquired multifactor coagulopathies (Harrison 22e p.928)"],"additionalInvestigations":["PT and aPTT as first-line screens for clotting factor deficiency (Harrison 22e p.928)","Mixing study (correction with normal plasma indicates factor deficiency; failure to correct indicates an inhibitor or anticoagulant) (Harrison 22e p.928)","Individual factor activity assays to identify the specific deficient factor (Harrison 22e p.928)","Bethesda assay to define inhibitor specificity and titer (expressed in Bethesda units; classifies low- vs high-responder patients) with annual aPTT/mixing-study screening at hemophilia centers (Harrison 22e p.930-931)","VWD laboratory panel: aPTT, VWF antigen, VWF activity, FVIII activity, and multimer analysis to classify type 1, 2A, 2B, 2M, 2N, and 3 (Harrison 22e p.926-927)","Chromogenic FVIII activity assay using bovine substrates when emicizumab is present (routine aPTT and FVIII activity are inaccurate) (Harrison 22e p.931)","Molecular/genetic testing for F8 or F9 mutations to enable precise carrier diagnosis in affected families (Harrison 22e p.930)"],"redFlags":["Bleeding into the oropharyngeal spaces, central nervous system, or retroperitoneum is life-threatening and requires immediate therapy (Harrison 22e p.930)","Retroperitoneal hemorrhage may form a calcified inflammatory mass (pseudotumor syndrome) and damage the femoral nerve; pseudotumors can also form in long bones (Harrison 22e p.930)","Deep-muscle hematomas of the distal limbs can cause compartment syndrome from external compression of arteries, veins, or nerves (Harrison 22e p.930)","Failure to respond to factor replacement at therapeutic doses suggests an inhibitor; early detection is critical (Harrison 22e p.930)","In carriers/women, postpartum hemorrhage risk rises after delivery as the pregnancy-induced rise in clotting factor levels falls rapidly (Harrison 22e p.931)"],"prognosis":"Without treatment severe hemophilia may limit life expectancy; with modern recombinant factor concentrates, prophylaxis, and viral-inactivated products, life expectancy of severe hemophilia is now only ~10 years shorter than the general male population and near normal in mild/moderate disease. Since 1998 no new viral hepatitis or HIV infections have been reported in hemophilia patients, though aging patients carry burdens of arthropathy, chronic pain, and prior HCV/HIV infection (hepatocellular carcinoma is the most common cause of death in HIV-negative patients). TTP-related microangiopathy and inhibitors worsen prognosis when present. (Harrison 22e p.930-932)","pitfalls":["FXII (Hageman factor), high-molecular-weight kininogen, and prekallikrein deficiencies markedly prolong the aPTT but do NOT cause bleeding (no bleeding phenotype; treatment is not indicated) — do not treat the lab abnormality (Harrison 22e p.928).","Not all patients with low VWF levels bleed; mildly decreased VWF should be diagnosed as VWD only with bleeding symptoms and/or a family history, and type O blood physiologically lowers VWF (overlapping the diagnostic range) (Harrison 22e p.926-927).","Bleeding phenotype does not always correlate with factor activity in FXI deficiency, and a negative bleeding history with prior procedures does NOT exclude increased hemorrhage risk (Harrison 22e p.931-932).","Family history of hemophilia is absent in ~30% of cases (de novo mutations), so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.930).","Antifibrinolytic drugs are NOT indicated for hematuria because of the risk of an occlusive clot obstructing the genitourinary tract (Harrison 22e p.931)."],"severityClassification":"Hemophilia A/B by factor VIII or IX activity: severe (<1%), moderate (1-5%), mild (6-30%); patients with baseline activity >25% may be detected only after major trauma or as an isolated prolonged aPTT. VWD classified into types 1, 2 (subtypes 2A, 2B, 2M, 2N), and 3 (severe, with virtually absent VWF and FVIII usually <10%) using VWF antigen, VWF activity, FVIII activity, and multimer pattern. Inhibitor patients classified as low responders (<5 Bethesda units) or high responders (>5 BU or anamnestic rise). (Harrison 22e p.926-931)","references":["Harrison 22e p.928-932","Harrison 22e Ch.120 p.926-927 (von Willebrand disease)"],"crossLinks":["dic"]},"coarctation_of_aorta":{"name":"Coarctation of the Aorta","system":"Paediatric cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CoA","aortic coarctation","coarctation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A narrowing of the aorta, most often just distal to the left subclavian artery near the ductal region, obstructing flow to the lower body.","The hallmark examination finding is diminished, delayed, or absent femoral pulses compared with the upper limbs, with a blood pressure gradient higher in the arms than the legs.","Severe coarctation may be duct-dependent in the neonate: as the ductus arteriosus closes, lower-body perfusion falls, producing shock, acidosis, and heart failure.","A prostaglandin infusion is used to reopen or maintain ductal patency and restore lower-body perfusion while stabilising a critically ill neonate before repair.","Older children may be asymptomatic and present with an incidentally found murmur, upper-limb hypertension, headaches, or leg claudication.","Coarctation is commonly associated with a bicuspid aortic valve and may occur with other left-sided obstructive lesions and Turner syndrome.","Chest radiograph in older children can show rib notching from collateral vessels and a figure-3 aortic contour.","Echocardiography defines the site and severity of narrowing and associated lesions; CT or MR angiography is useful for detailed anatomy.","Repair by surgery or catheter intervention relieves the obstruction, but lifelong follow-up is needed for recoarctation and persistent hypertension."],"pathophysiology":"A discrete or long-segment narrowing of the aortic lumen creates a mechanical obstruction to systemic outflow. Proximal to the obstruction, pressure rises, producing upper-body hypertension and left ventricular pressure overload; distal to it, perfusion pressure falls, causing weak lower-body pulses. In severe neonatal coarctation, lower-body perfusion depends on right-to-left flow through the ductus arteriosus, so ductal closure precipitates hypoperfusion, metabolic acidosis, and circulatory collapse. In slowly progressive disease, collateral arterial channels develop to bypass the obstruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Interrupted aortic arch","Hypoplastic left heart syndrome","Critical aortic stenosis","Other duct-dependent systemic circulation lesions","Essential hypertension in an older child","Peripheral vascular disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Four-limb blood pressure measurement and simultaneous upper- and lower-limb pulse assessment","Echocardiography to define coarctation site, severity, and associated lesions such as bicuspid valve","Chest radiograph for rib notching and aortic contour in older children","CT or MR angiography for detailed arch anatomy","Assessment for acidosis and end-organ perfusion in the collapsing neonate"],"redFlags":["Neonatal circulatory collapse with shock and acidosis as the ductus arteriosus closes in duct-dependent coarctation","Absent or markedly weak femoral pulses with an upper-to-lower limb blood pressure gradient","Signs of heart failure and poor lower-body perfusion in an infant","Severe upper-limb hypertension with headaches or hypertensive complications","Evidence of end-organ hypoperfusion such as oliguria or feeding intolerance"],"prognosis":"Early repair of significant coarctation gives a good outlook, but patients need lifelong surveillance for recoarctation, residual or late hypertension, aneurysm formation, and associated valve disease. Unrecognised severe neonatal coarctation can be rapidly fatal.","pitfalls":["Failing to palpate femoral pulses and compare limb blood pressures in an unwell neonate or hypertensive child","Missing a duct-dependent coarctation until the neonate collapses as the ductus closes","Attributing upper-limb hypertension in a child to essential hypertension without checking for a gradient","Overlooking associated bicuspid aortic valve and other left-sided lesions","Assuming successful repair eliminates the need for ongoing hypertension and recoarctation surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"cocaine_other_psychostimulants_and_halluci":{"name":"Cocaine, Other Psychostimulants, and Hallucinogens","system":"Neurologic / Substance Use (Toxicology)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Cocaine intoxication","Methamphetamine use disorder","Stimulant use disorder","MDMA (ecstasy/Molly)","Cathinone / bath salts","Hallucinogen use disorder","LSD / PCP / psilocybin / ketamine"],"chapter":468,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3692","clinicalPearls":["Cocaine blocks reuptake at the dopamine transporter, raising synaptic dopamine, norepinephrine, and serotonin in both CNS and PNS; this monoaminergic excess underlies its euphoric and sympathomimetic effects.","Smoked or vaporized cocaine (freebase/crack) reaches the brain almost as fast as the IV route because of the large pulmonary vasculature, producing a rapid, intense, transient high that increases addictive potential.","Acute psychostimulant intoxication produces a uniform syndrome regardless of agent: euphoria, increased energy, reduced need for sleep, decreased appetite, heightened alertness, increased libido, plus peripheral tremor, diaphoresis, hypertonia, tachypnea, hyperreflexia, and hyperthermia.","Many psychostimulant effects are biphasic: low doses improve psychomotor performance and increase vagal tone (lowering heart rate), whereas high doses cause tremor/convulsions and raise heart rate and blood pressure.","Combining cocaine with alcohol generates the metabolite cocaethylene, whose cardiovascular toxicity is additive to cocaine's, intensifying pathophysiologic harm.","Methamphetamine is more potent than amphetamine, producing higher synaptic dopamine and greater nerve-terminal toxicity; the dextrorotatory (d-) enantiomer has 3-5 times the CNS activity of the l-form, and smoked bioavailability (~90%) far exceeds oral (~67%).","MDMA acts mainly on serotonin transporters, increasing release of serotonin, norepinephrine, and dopamine; animal data show moderate-to-high doses can cause loss of serotonergic nerve endings and permanent damage.","Classic (serotonergic) hallucinogens such as LSD, psilocybin, mescaline/peyote, DMT, and ayahuasca disrupt serotonin and glutamate signaling; oral onset is ~20-90 min with duration up to 6-12 h (salvia ~30 min).","Dissociative agents (PCP, ketamine, high-dose dextromethorphan, salvia) distort perception of time, motion, color, sound, and self and can cause respiratory depression, heart-rate abnormalities, and a withdrawal syndrome with craving, confusion, headache, and sweating.","Psychostimulant withdrawal features hypersomnia, increased appetite, and depressed mood; acute withdrawal lasts ~7-10 days but residual symptoms may persist for months. Conditioned (cue-triggered) craving, not physical withdrawal, is considered the primary driver of ongoing use.","Stimulant use disorder is diagnosed by DSM-5 when at least 2 of 11 criteria occur within 12 months; for hallucinogen use disorder only the first 10 criteria apply because the withdrawal criterion does not apply (use is typically not chronic).","Adulterants are a major and underappreciated hazard: cocaine/methamphetamine are increasingly cut with fentanyl-related opioids (overdose deaths), levamisole (agranulocytosis, leukoencephalopathy, cutaneous vasculitis with skin necrosis), clenbuterol (tachycardia, hyperglycemia, hypokalemia), and xylazine (sedation, necrotic soft-tissue lesions)."],"pathophysiology":"Psychostimulants increase synaptic monoamines. Cocaine binds the dopamine transporter and blocks reuptake of dopamine, norepinephrine, and serotonin, raising their levels centrally and peripherally; repeated exposure induces long-term neuronal changes including adaptations in glutamate-releasing neurons. Methamphetamine, a cationic lipophilic molecule structurally similar to monoamine transporters, reverses transporter function to dump newly synthesized catecholamines from storage vesicles into the synapse and also inhibits monoamine oxidase, attenuating monoamine metabolism; it is more potent than amphetamine with greater nerve-terminal toxicity. MDMA acts predominantly on serotonin transporters to release serotonin (and norepinephrine/dopamine) and can damage serotonergic nerve endings. Cathinone (from khat) increases dopamine release and reduces its reuptake. Classic hallucinogens disrupt serotonin (affecting mood, perception, sleep, appetite, temperature, sexual behavior, muscle control) and glutamate (affecting pain perception, environmental responses, emotion, learning/memory). The sympathomimetic surge drives the cardiovascular, neurologic, pulmonary, renal, and hyperthermic complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Sympathomimetic toxidrome from other agents (e.g., synthetic cathinones/bath salts, prescription stimulant misuse)","Anticholinergic toxidrome","Serotonin syndrome","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome / malignant hyperthermia (hyperthermia, rigidity)","Thyroid storm","Pheochromocytoma / catecholamine excess","Primary psychiatric psychosis or mania","Alcohol or sedative withdrawal"],"infectionMimics":["Sepsis with hyperthermia and tachycardia","CNS infection (meningitis/encephalitis) presenting with agitation, fever, altered mental status"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hyperthyroidism / thyroid storm","Pheochromocytoma","Serotonin syndrome","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Primary psychotic or manic episode","Hypoglycemia or hypoxemia (must be excluded before treating agitation)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Electrocardiogram for chest pain or arrhythmia evaluation","Chest radiograph in cocaine-associated chest pain","Cardiac biomarkers to exclude myocardial infarction","Core body temperature monitoring (hyperthermia)","Note that emerging substances/NPS are often NOT detected on standard ED drug screens or postmortem toxicology"],"redFlags":["Severe psychomotor agitation, hypertension, tachycardia, headache, and mydriasis indicating sympathetic overactivity / overdose","Hyperthermia (goal to cool to core temperature <39 C within 30 min)","Seizures, coma, or focal neurologic signs","Intracranial hemorrhage or cerebral infarction","Chest pain, cardiac ischemia/arrhythmia, left ventricular dysfunction, aortic dissection/rupture","Respiratory failure, airway burns or bronchospasm from inhaled crack ('crack lung')","Rhabdomyolysis with metabolic acidosis / renal infarction","Concurrent opioid co-ingestion (e.g., fentanyl-adulterated cocaine) precipitating fatal overdose","'Bad trip' with panic, paranoia, or psychosis (hallucinogens), which may persist up to 24 h"],"prognosis":"Acute overdose is a medical emergency that can be fatal through cerebral hemorrhage/infarction, cardiac arrhythmia or ischemia, respiratory failure, hyperthermia, or rhabdomyolysis, and management is largely supportive in an ICU/telemetry setting. Co-ingestion of other drugs (especially opioids) markedly increases morbidity and mortality, and overdose deaths involving cocaine and psychostimulants have risen sharply (cocaine-related overdose deaths more than quintupled from 2011 to 2022; psychostimulant-involved deaths reached ~36,000 in 2023). Early abstinence is frequently complicated by depression, guilt, insomnia, and anorexia that can be as severe as a major affective disorder and may persist for months to years. Contingency management has robust evidence of efficacy for stimulant use disorder with benefit lasting up to 2 years after treatment ends; no FDA-approved pharmacotherapy exists for stimulant or hallucinogen addiction. Chronic hallucinogen/dissociative use can leave persistent psychosis, memory loss, anxiety, depression, suicidal ideation, and flashbacks lasting a year or more.","pitfalls":["Beta blockers should generally be avoided in cocaine users because unopposed alpha-adrenergic stimulation from beta blockade may cause coronary vasoconstriction, ischemia, and infarction; if essential, mixed alpha/beta agents (e.g., labetalol, carvedilol) are preferred over nonselective beta blockers.","Succinylcholine is relatively contraindicated for rapid-sequence intubation in cocaine use; a nondepolarizing agent (e.g., rocuronium) is the alternative.","Before treating psychomotor agitation, hypoglycemia and hypoxemia must be ruled out first.","Drugs sold as 'Molly'/MDMA frequently contain methylone, ethylone, or other synthetic cathinones rather than MDMA, so the reported drug may not match the actual exposure.","Emerging/new psychoactive substances are often missed on standard drug tests, so a negative screen does not exclude intoxication.","Fentanyl, xylazine, levamisole, and other adulterants can produce effects (and overdose) not anticipated from the intended drug.","MDMA, LSD, and psilocybin remain Schedule I in the United States despite FDA breakthrough-therapy designations; investigational use does not equal approved therapy."],"severityClassification":"No single graded severity scale; DSM-5 stimulant use disorder severity is rated mild (2-3 criteria), moderate (4-5), or severe (>=6) of 11 criteria over 12 months. Acute clinical severity ranges from intoxication to life-threatening overdose marked by sympathetic overactivity (agitation, hypertension, tachycardia), hyperthermia, seizures, intracranial or cardiac events, respiratory failure, and rhabdomyolysis. Complications of psychostimulant use are catalogued in Table 468-1 across cardiovascular, CNS/PNS, pulmonary, gastrointestinal, renal, endocrine, and other organ systems.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3692","Harrison 22e p.3693","Harrison 22e p.3694","Harrison 22e p.3695","Harrison 22e p.3696","Harrison 22e p.3697"],"crossLinks":[]},"coccidioidomycosis":{"name":"Coccidioidomycosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Valley fever","Coccidioides"],"chapter":219,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1696","clinicalPearls":["Caused by dimorphic soil-dwelling fungi of the genus Coccidioides (C. immitis and C. posadasii), which are clinically indistinguishable; infection follows inhalation of airborne arthroconidia (Harrison 22e p.1696).","Endemic to the Western Hemisphere between 40 degrees N and 40 degrees S; areas of high endemicity in the US include the southern San Joaquin Valley of California (hence 'Valley fever') and south-central Arizona, with other foci across the southwestern US, northern Mexico, Central America, and parts of South America (Harrison 22e p.1696).","In endemic areas most human cases occur without obvious soil or dust exposure; periods of aridity following rainy seasons are associated with increases in symptomatic infections (Harrison 22e p.1696).","After infection, 60% of individuals are completely asymptomatic; the other 40% have symptoms related primarily to pulmonary infection (fever, cough, pleuritic chest pain) appearing several days to up to 3 weeks after inhalation, with risk of symptomatic illness increasing with age (Harrison 22e p.1697).","Immunologic manifestations of primary pulmonary disease include cutaneous reactions: a diffuse erythematous maculopapular rash ('toxic erythema'), erythema nodosum (typically over the lower extremities), erythema multiforme (often in a necklace distribution), and Sweet syndrome lesions; cutaneous manifestations are especially common in women (Harrison 22e p.1697).","Symmetrical arthralgias ('desert rheumatism'), often involving the ankles, knees, and hips, may occur with or without cutaneous manifestations (Harrison 22e p.1697).","Serology is the most common diagnostic method; EIA for IgM and IgG is recommended for initial diagnosis, with positive results confirmed by immunodiffusion (IDTP/IDCF) or traditional tube-precipitin and complement-fixation assays (Harrison 22e p.1697).","Rising complement-fixation (CF) titers reflect fungal growth and correlate with clinical progression; high CF titers (e.g., 1:32 or greater) suggest unrestrained fungal growth and the possibility of extrathoracic dissemination, while CF antibody in CSF is indicative of coccidioidal meningitis (Harrison 22e p.1698).","Spherules (up to 200 microns) are unique to Coccidioides and can be demonstrated in respiratory specimens after Papanicolaou, Gomori methenamine silver, or calcofluor white staining, or in fixed tissue with hematoxylin-eosin or Gomori methenamine silver (Harrison 22e p.1698).","A commercial coccidioidal antigen test (antigenuria/antigenemia) is useful in immunosuppressed patients with severe or disseminated disease and when assaying CSF in suspected meningitis (Harrison 22e p.1698).","Coccidioidal meningitis is the most dire disseminated syndrome and is uniformly fatal if untreated; patients present with a persistent dull headache often with lethargy and confusion, and nuchal rigidity, if present, is not severe (Harrison 22e p.1697)."],"pathophysiology":"Coccidioides exists as filamentous mold (mycelia) in the environment, producing barrel-shaped airborne spores (arthroconidia, ~2 x 5 microns) that, when inhaled, are small enough to evade mucosal defenses and reach deep into the bronchial tree to initiate infection. Within the host, arthroconidia enlarge into spherules (up to 200 microns, unique to Coccidioides) containing endospores; spherules rupture and release endospores that form new spherules, propagating infection locally. A robust cellular immune response is critical to host control: resolved pulmonary infection shows necrotizing granulomas containing spherules, whereas uncontrolled disseminated disease shows poorly formed or absent granulomas and a polymorphonuclear leukocyte response. In persons with protective cellular immunity, spherules may persist in the lungs for prolonged periods (possibly lifelong) in a latent state, conferring protective immunity but creating a risk of recurrent active disease if that immunity wanes (Harrison 22e p.1696-1697).","additionalDifferentials":["Community-acquired bacterial pneumonia (primary pulmonary coccidioidomycosis is often misdiagnosed as this) (Harrison 22e p.1697)","Pulmonary malignancy (coccidioidal nodules are difficult to distinguish radiographically and, like malignancies, may enhance on PET) (Harrison 22e p.1697)"],"infectionMimics":["Histoplasmosis (can cause false-positive coccidioidal antigen results) (Harrison 22e p.1698)","Blastomycosis (can cause false-positive coccidioidal antigen results) (Harrison 22e p.1698)","Tuberculosis / other chronic granulomatous pulmonary infections (upper lobe infiltrate, cavities, hilar/mediastinal lymphadenopathy mimic) (Harrison 22e p.1697)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pulmonary malignancy (solitary or multiple nodules, PET-avid, may require biopsy to distinguish) (Harrison 22e p.1697)"],"additionalInvestigations":["EIA for IgM and IgG antibodies (recommended for initial diagnosis) (Harrison 22e p.1698)","Confirmatory immunodiffusion (IDTP/IDCF) or traditional tube-precipitin and complement-fixation assays with CF titer (CF titer is an important prognostic tool) (Harrison 22e p.1698)","Culture of sputum/respiratory fluids and tissue (grows within 3-7 days at 37 degrees C on artificial media including blood agar) (Harrison 22e p.1698)","Direct microscopy of respiratory specimens (Papanicolaou, Gomori methenamine silver, or calcofluor white) or fixed tissue (H&E or GMS) for spherules (Harrison 22e p.1698)","Coccidioidal antigen test (antigenuria/antigenemia), including CSF assay in suspected meningitis (Harrison 22e p.1698)","CSF examination in suspected meningitis (lymphocytic pleocytosis, profound hypoglycorrhachia, elevated protein, occasional CSF eosinophilia; CF antibody in CSF) (Harrison 22e p.1697)","MRI with gadolinium in meningitis (frequently shows basilar meningeal enhancement; dilated ventricles indicate hydrocephalus) (Harrison 22e p.1697)","Chest imaging/CT for nodules and cavities (multiple nodules and microsatellite lesions favor coccidioidomycosis over malignancy) (Harrison 22e p.1697)"],"redFlags":["Disseminated disease (occurs in approximately 1% of infections; defined as fungus found outside the thoracic cavity), more likely in male patients, those of African and perhaps Filipino ancestry, and persons with depressed cellular immunity (Harrison 22e p.1697)","Coccidioidal meningitis: persistent dull headache, lethargy, confusion; uniformly fatal if untreated, may progress to communicating hydrocephalus with declining mental status and gait disturbance (Harrison 22e p.1697)","Immunocompromised hosts at risk for dissemination: HIV-1 infection with CD4 count <250/microL, chronic glucocorticoid therapy, allogeneic solid-organ transplant recipients, and patients on TNF-alpha antagonists or other biological response modifiers (Harrison 22e p.1697)","New coccidioidal infection acquired during the second or third trimester of pregnancy or postpartum (significant risk for disseminated disease) (Harrison 22e p.1697)","Diffuse reticulonodular pneumonia with dyspnea and fever in intense exposure or profoundly suppressed cellular immunity (e.g., untreated HIV with markedly low CD4), associated with unrestrained fungal growth and frequent fungemia (Harrison 22e p.1697)","High serum CF titer (e.g., 1:32 or greater) predicting disease activity and possible extrathoracic dissemination (Harrison 22e p.1698)","Pulmonary cavity rupture into the pleural space causing pyopneumothorax (acute dyspnea, collapsed lung with pleural air-fluid level) (Harrison 22e p.1697)"],"prognosis":"In most patients, primary pulmonary coccidioidomycosis resolves without sequelae over several weeks. Clinical dissemination occurs in approximately 1% of infected individuals and, when it occurs, is usually evident within the first 6 months after primary infection. Coccidioidal meningitis is uniformly fatal if untreated; even with treatment, relapse is common after stopping therapy. Antibodies disappear over time in persons whose illness resolves, but the presence of coccidioidal antibodies in serum does not indicate immunity or control of infection (Harrison 22e p.1696-1698).","pitfalls":["Primary pulmonary coccidioidomycosis is often misdiagnosed as community-acquired bacterial pneumonia; rash, symmetrical arthralgias, night sweats, marked fatigue, peripheral-blood eosinophilia, failure to improve with antibacterial therapy, or upper lobe infiltrate / hilar or mediastinal adenopathy should suggest the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1697).","Coccidioides poses a significant laboratory hazard if inadvertently inhaled; the clinical laboratory should always be alerted to the possibility of this diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1698).","Coccidioidal pulmonary nodules can be difficult to distinguish radiographically from malignancy and, like malignancies, often enhance on PET; biopsy may be required (Harrison 22e p.1697).","The IgM EIA in particular is occasionally falsely positive; results should be interpreted cautiously when screening asymptomatic individuals (Harrison 22e p.1698).","The EIA IgG optical density does not correlate with the IDCF/traditional CF titer and should not be used as a surrogate for a titer (Harrison 22e p.1698).","Coccidioidal antigen testing may yield false-positive results in histoplasmosis or blastomycosis (Harrison 22e p.1698).","The fungus is isolated from CSF in fewer than one-third of meningitis cases, so a negative CSF culture does not exclude the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1697).","Potassium hydroxide preparation of sputum is rarely fruitful for identifying Coccidioides (Harrison 22e p.1698).","PCR genomic detection does not appear to be more sensitive than culture, though it can be more rapid (Harrison 22e p.1698)."],"severityClassification":"Clinical presentations and their approximate frequencies (Table 219-1, Harrison 22e p.1698): asymptomatic infection (60%); primary focal pneumonia (40%); diffuse pneumonia (<1%); pulmonary sequelae (~5%: nodule, cavity, chronic pneumonia); and disseminated disease (1% or less, including skin/bone/joint/soft tissue disease and meningitis). High CF titers (e.g., 1:32 or greater) and features such as night sweats >3 weeks, weight loss >10%, CF titer >1:16, and extensive pulmonary involvement indicate greater severity.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1696"],"crossLinks":["CAP","PULMONARY_TB","MENINGITIS"]},"colles_fracture":{"name":"Colles' fracture","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["distal radius fracture","dorsally displaced distal radial fracture","dinner-fork deformity fracture"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically a fracture of the distal radius within a few centimetres of the wrist with dorsal displacement and angulation, producing the 'dinner-fork' deformity","Typically caused by a fall onto the outstretched hand in an older, osteoporotic patient; its occurrence should prompt bone-health assessment","Radiographs show dorsal tilt, radial shortening, and often an associated ulnar styloid fracture; the degree of intra-articular involvement guides management","Undisplaced or minimally displaced fractures are treated with a cast; displaced fractures usually require closed reduction and immobilisation, checking that radial length and volar tilt are restored","Unstable, markedly comminuted, or intra-articular fractures often need surgical fixation such as a volar locking plate or K-wires to maintain alignment","Median nerve symptoms (acute carpal tunnel) can arise from swelling or displacement and must be sought before and after reduction","Watch for later complications including malunion, extensor pollicis longus tendon rupture, stiffness, and complex regional pain syndrome","Early finger, elbow, and shoulder mobilisation during cast treatment helps prevent stiffness and disuse"],"pathophysiology":"A Colles' fracture occurs when a fall onto the dorsiflexed, outstretched hand transmits force through the distal radius, which fails in its metaphyseal cancellous bone. Osteoporosis lowers the threshold for injury, explaining its predominance in postmenopausal women and older adults. The distal fragment tilts and displaces dorsally and radially and impacts proximally, shortening the radius and disrupting the normal volar tilt and radial inclination of the articular surface. Associated soft-tissue swelling or bony displacement may compress the median nerve in the carpal tunnel, and disruption of the distal radioulnar joint or triangular fibrocartilage can affect forearm rotation.","additionalDifferentials":["Smith's fracture (volar displacement) and Barton's fracture (intra-articular shear)","Scaphoid fracture","Distal radial physeal injury in adolescents","Wrist sprain or ligamentous injury","Carpal dislocation (perilunate/lunate)","Distal radioulnar joint injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Posteroanterior and lateral wrist radiographs to assess dorsal tilt, radial length and inclination, and articular involvement","CT for complex intra-articular or comminuted patterns to plan fixation","Post-reduction radiographs to confirm and monitor alignment","Assessment for osteoporosis and secondary fracture prevention in older patients","Clinical assessment of median nerve function before and after reduction"],"redFlags":["Progressive median nerve symptoms indicating acute carpal tunnel syndrome requiring urgent decompression or reduction","Open fracture or skin compromise","Marked deformity with vascular compromise of the hand","Fracture from trivial trauma signalling significant underlying osteoporosis"],"prognosis":"Most stable distal radial fractures unite and recover good function with appropriate reduction and rehabilitation, but malunion with residual dorsal tilt and radial shortening can leave weakness, reduced grip, and cosmetic deformity, and intra-articular incongruity predisposes to post-traumatic arthritis.","pitfalls":["Accepting a poor reduction with persistent dorsal tilt or radial shortening, leading to symptomatic malunion","Failing to recognise or monitor acute median nerve compression","Immobilising the wrist in excessive flexion (Cotton-Loder position), which risks stiffness and nerve compression","Neglecting finger and shoulder movement during cast treatment, causing stiffness and complex regional pain syndrome"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"colonic_pseudo_obstruction":{"name":"Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction","system":"GI / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Ogilvie","Ogilvie syndrome","ACPO","acute colonic pseudo-obstruction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AASLD & ACG","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Massive dilatation of the colon, especially the caecum and right colon, in the absence of any mechanical obstructing lesion","Typically occurs in hospitalised or debilitated patients after surgery, severe illness, trauma, infection, or in the setting of electrolyte disturbance and certain medications","Presents with progressive abdominal distension, often with relatively little pain, and variable nausea, vomiting, and altered bowel output","A mechanical obstruction and toxic megacolon must be excluded, usually with abdominal imaging and often a water-soluble contrast study or CT","The main danger is progressive caecal dilatation leading to ischaemia and perforation, with risk rising sharply as caecal diameter increases and as distension persists","Initial management is conservative: correct electrolyte abnormalities, stop contributing drugs (especially opioids and anticholinergics), decompress with a nasogastric tube, and mobilise the patient","If conservative measures fail and there is no perforation, pharmacological decompression with a parasympathomimetic agent is effective, given under cardiac monitoring","Colonoscopic decompression or, as a last resort, surgery is reserved for refractory cases or when ischaemia or perforation is suspected"],"pathophysiology":"Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction reflects a disturbance of the autonomic regulation of colonic motility, with a relative imbalance favouring reduced parasympathetic (excitatory) drive or excess sympathetic (inhibitory) tone in the distal colon. This produces a functional adynamic segment and proximal dilatation without any mechanical block. Precipitants include major surgery, severe systemic illness, sepsis, metabolic and electrolyte derangements, and drugs that impair gut motility. As the caecum distends, its wall tension rises (in keeping with the law of Laplace, tension increases with radius), compromising perfusion and risking ischaemic necrosis and perforation.","additionalDifferentials":["Mechanical large-bowel obstruction (tumour, stricture, volvulus)","Toxic megacolon from severe colitis","Sigmoid or caecal volvulus","Generalised paralytic ileus","Faecal impaction with distension","Chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Abdominal radiograph to document colonic and caecal diameter","CT of the abdomen or water-soluble contrast enema to exclude a mechanical cause and assess for ischaemia or perforation","Electrolyte panel including potassium, magnesium, calcium, and phosphate","Review of the medication chart for motility-impairing drugs","Serial clinical and radiological monitoring of caecal size and abdominal signs"],"redFlags":["Progressive caecal dilatation, especially beyond a critical diameter, indicating high risk of ischaemia and perforation","Abdominal tenderness, peritonism, fever, or rising lactate suggesting colonic ischaemia or perforation","Failure to improve with conservative management over 24-48 hours","Sudden clinical deterioration or signs of sepsis pointing to perforation requiring emergency surgery"],"prognosis":"Most patients recover with correction of precipitating factors and conservative or pharmacological decompression. Prognosis worsens substantially if ischaemia or perforation develops, which carries high mortality, so early recognition and monitoring of caecal size are critical.","pitfalls":["Assuming distension is a simple ileus and failing to exclude mechanical obstruction or monitor caecal diameter","Continuing opioids or anticholinergic drugs that perpetuate the problem","Delaying decompression until the caecum reaches a dangerous size and perforation risk is high","Giving pharmacological decompression without cardiac monitoring or ignoring its contraindications"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AASLD/ACG guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"colorectal_cancer":{"name":"Colorectal Cancer","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["colon cancer","rectal cancer","bowel cancer"],"chapter":86,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.653","clinicalPearls":["Symptoms vary by tumor location. Right-sided cancers (cecum, ascending colon) can grow large without obstruction because stool is still liquid, and they tend to ulcerate, causing chronic insidious blood loss and presenting as iron-deficiency anemia (Harrison 22e p.656).","Unexplained iron-deficiency anemia in any adult mandates a thorough endoscopic and/or radiographic visualization of the entire large bowel; the possible exception is a premenopausal, multiparous woman (Harrison 22e p.656).","Left-sided (transverse/descending colon) tumors impede passage of more formed stool, producing abdominal cramping, occasional obstruction, and even perforation, classically the annular constricting 'apple-core' lesion on imaging (Harrison 22e p.656).","Rectosigmoid cancers cause hematochezia, tenesmus, and narrowing of stool caliber; these may be mistaken for hemorrhoids, but rectal bleeding and/or altered bowel habits demand prompt digital rectal exam and sigmoidoscopy (Harrison 22e p.656).","Most colorectal cancers arise from adenomatous polyps (not hyperplastic, hamartomatous, or serrated polyps); only a minority of adenomas progress, with risk tied to polyp size and histology, and villous adenomas becoming cancer about threefold more often than tubular adenomas (Harrison 22e p.653).","Universal testing of colorectal tumors for MMR defects is recommended, by immunohistochemistry for MMR proteins or microsatellite instability testing; MSI-high tumors are dramatically sensitive to immune checkpoint inhibition and less sensitive to chemotherapy (Harrison 22e p.653-654, 656).","Serum CEA is used for staging workup and follow-up; some authorities measure plasma CEA at 3-month intervals after curative resection because of its sensitivity as a marker for otherwise undetectable recurrence (Harrison 22e p.656-658).","Liver is the most frequent visceral metastatic site, being the initial site of distant spread in about one-third of recurring cancers and involved in over two-thirds of patients at death; complete resection of metastases isolated to one site (liver, lung, peritoneum) is considered curative (Harrison 22e p.657-658)."],"pathophysiology":"The majority of colorectal cancers develop through the adenoma-to-carcinoma sequence, in which adenomatous polyps accumulate sequential genetic and epigenetic events involving tumor suppressor genes and oncogenes. Three major sporadic molecular pathways exist: the chromosomal instability (CIN) pathway (the most common), characterized by early loss of the APC tumor suppressor gene followed by sequential MAP-kinase oncogene mutations such as KRAS and BRAF, with loss of p53 as a common final step; the mismatch repair (MMR) pathway, often caused by hypermethylation of MMR gene promoters, producing a microsatellite-instability-high (MSI-high) phenotype; and the CpG island methylator phenotype (CIMP), with tumors that are often right-sided and associated with MLH1 methylation. These pathways are not mutually exclusive and can overlap in individual cancers (Harrison 22e p.653-654).","additionalDifferentials":["Hemorrhoids or benign anorectal disease as a cause of rectal bleeding (rectal bleeding and altered bowel habits still demand DRE and sigmoidoscopy) (Harrison 22e p.656)","Other causes of iron-deficiency anemia from chronic occult GI blood loss (Harrison 22e p.656)","Inflammatory bowel disease (both a risk factor and a differential for altered bowel habit and bleeding) (Harrison 22e p.655)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Colonoscopy of the entire large bowel (gold standard for detecting cancer and synchronous neoplasms/polyps) (Harrison 22e p.656)","Flexible sigmoidoscopy as a screening modality (Harrison 22e p.655-656)","Stool-based tests: fecal occult blood test, fecal immunochemical test (FIT), and fecal DNA testing (Harrison 22e p.655-656)","Serum carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) (Harrison 22e p.656)","Complete blood count and comprehensive metabolic panel (Harrison 22e p.656)","Chest, abdomen, and pelvic CT scan; MRI and PET as dictated by CT findings (Harrison 22e p.656)","Pelvic MRI for rectal cancer to assess mesorectal margin and nodal status (Harrison 22e p.658)","Tumor MMR/microsatellite instability testing (IHC or MSI) and germline genetic testing where indicated (Harrison 22e p.654, 656)","Investigational: plasma circulating tumor DNA as a biomarker for residual/recurrent disease (Harrison 22e p.658)"],"redFlags":["Unexplained iron-deficiency anemia in any adult (mandates evaluation of the entire large bowel)","Change in bowel habit / altered bowel habits","Rectal bleeding (hematochezia) or guaiac-positive stool","Tenesmus and narrowing of the caliber of the stool (rectosigmoid lesions)","Abdominal cramping or obstruction (left-sided lesions)","Strong family history or known hereditary syndrome (FAP, MUTYH-associated polyposis, Lynch syndrome)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is stage-related and depends on depth of tumor penetration into the bowel wall, regional lymph node involvement, and distant metastases (TNM). Stage I (T1-2N0M0) confers a high cure rate (>90-95% 5-year survival); 5-year survival declines through stage II (T3-4N0M0), stage III (node-positive), and stage IV (distant metastasis, <5% in colon). Stage I patients are considered cured after complete resection. A minimum of 12 sampled lymph nodes is considered necessary to accurately define stage. Poor-prognosis features include penetration through the bowel wall into pericolic fat, poorly differentiated histology, perforation and/or adherence to adjacent organs, venous invasion, preoperative CEA >5 ng/mL, BRAF mutation, and right-sided primary location. Tumors with MSI/MMR defects have an improved prognosis (enhanced immune response), and left-colon tumors fare better than right-colon tumors. Notably, primary tumor size does not influence prognosis once adjusted for nodal involvement and differentiation. Most recurrences occur within the first 5 years (Harrison 22e p.656-658).","pitfalls":["Attributing rectal bleeding to hemorrhoids and not pursuing DRE and sigmoidoscopy (Harrison 22e p.656)","Relying on fecal occult blood testing alone: about 50% of patients with documented colorectal cancer have a negative FOBT due to intermittent bleeding, and a colorectal neoplasm is NOT found in most asymptomatic individuals with a positive test (Harrison 22e p.655-656)","Sigmoidoscopy alone misses proximal lesions: about 1.5% of individuals with a normal sigmoidoscopy harbor a high-risk polyp or adenocarcinoma in the proximal colon (Harrison 22e p.656)","Lynch syndrome may not be associated with multiple polyps and is therefore often unrecognized (Harrison 22e p.654)","Disease stage cannot be determined accurately before surgical resection and pathologic analysis unless gross metastatic disease is present (Harrison 22e p.656)","Initial treatment should be delayed until MMR/microsatellite status is known when possible, since MSI-high rectal cancers may be cured with checkpoint inhibitors (PD-1 antibodies) without surgery, radiation, or chemotherapy (Harrison 22e p.656)"],"severityClassification":"TNM classification (American Joint Committee on Cancer / Union for International Cancer Control): T = depth of tumor penetration (T1 submucosa, T2 muscularis, T3 through muscularis, T4 adjacent structures), N = regional lymph node involvement (N1 = 1-3 nodes, N2 = >=4 nodes), M = distant metastasis. Stage I = T1-2N0M0; Stage II = T3-4N0M0; Stage III = any T, N1-2, M0; Stage IV = any T, any N, M1. The older Dukes classification was historically used for colorectal cancer (Harrison 22e p.656).","references":["Harrison 22e p.653"],"crossLinks":["ibd_flare"]},"common_atrial_flutter_and_macroreentrant":{"name":"Common Atrial Flutter and Macroreentrant and Multifocal Atrial Tachycardias","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Typical atrial flutter","Cavotricuspid isthmus-dependent atrial flutter","Atypical atrial flutter","Macroreentrant atrial tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","MAT"],"chapter":257,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1943","clinicalPearls":["Common (typical) right atrial flutter is a macroreentrant circuit that revolves around the tricuspid valve annulus, passing through the cavotricuspid (sub-Eustachian) isthmus, which is why it is termed cavotricuspid isthmus-dependent flutter and is amenable to interruption at that isthmus (Harrison 22e p.1943).","Counterclockwise rotation of the typical flutter circuit produces the characteristic negative sawtooth flutter waves in the inferior leads II, III, and aVF and positive flutter (P) waves in V1; clockwise (reverse typical) rotation produces the opposite vectors (Harrison 22e p.1943).","The atrial rate in typical flutter is usually 240-300 beats/min but may be slower with atrial disease or antiarrhythmic drugs (Harrison 22e p.1943).","Typical flutter often conducts to the ventricle with 2:1 AV block, yielding a regular tachycardia near 140-150 beats/min, where flutter waves can be obscured within the T wave (Harrison 22e p.1943).","Maneuvers or AV nodal disease/agents that increase AV block raise the conduction ratio and unmask flutter waves, aiding diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1943).","Typical flutter frequently coexists with atrial fibrillation and is associated with atrial scarring from aging or prior cardiac surgery; AF may precede the onset of flutter (Harrison 22e p.1943).","Atrial conduction-slowing antiarrhythmics (flecainide, propafenone, amiodarone) can convert a patient's AF presentation into atrial flutter by promoting reentry while suppressing ectopic triggers (Harrison 22e p.1943).","Atypical (non-cavotricuspid isthmus-dependent) flutters arise in either atrium, are almost universally tied to atrial scar, and are commonly seen after right atriotomy, extensive left atrial ablation for AF, or mitral valve surgery (Harrison 22e p.1943-1944).","Left atrial flutter and perimitral left atrial flutter resemble common flutter clinically but show different P-wave morphologies and can mimic focal atrial tachycardia (Harrison 22e p.1944).","Multifocal atrial tachycardia (MAT) is defined by at least three distinct P-wave morphologies with rates typically 100-150 beats/min and is mechanistically attributed to triggered automaticity from multiple atrial foci (Harrison 22e p.1946).","Unlike atrial fibrillation, MAT shows clear isoelectric intervals between P waves and a slower atrial rate, helping distinguish the two (Harrison 22e p.1946).","MAT is characteristically seen in patients with chronic pulmonary disease during acute illness (Harrison 22e p.1946)."],"pathophysiology":"Macroreentrant atrial tachycardia depends on a large anatomic reentry circuit, frequently involving areas of atrial scar with slow conduction. In common (typical) flutter, the wavefront encircles the tricuspid valve annulus, bounded anteriorly by the annulus and posteriorly by the inferior vena cava and Eustachian ridge, passing through the cavotricuspid isthmus between the IVC and tricuspid annulus. The circuit usually rotates counterclockwise as viewed from the ventricular apex; reversed (clockwise) rotation gives reverse typical flutter. AF and typical flutter are mechanistically interrelated: a line of functional block or coalescence of fibrillatory wavelets around barriers such as the crista terminalis and Eustachian valve may convert AF into organized flutter, and conduction-slowing antiarrhythmics promote this reentry. Atypical flutters are macroreentrant circuits not using the cavotricuspid isthmus, almost always associated with atrial scar. MAT arises from triggered automaticity in multiple atrial foci, producing multiple P-wave morphologies (Harrison 22e p.1943-1946).","additionalDifferentials":["Atrial fibrillation","Focal atrial tachycardia","Atypical (non-CTI-dependent) atrial flutter vs typical flutter","Left atrial / perimitral flutter","Sinus tachycardia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Atrial fibrillation (irregular, no isoelectric baseline between atrial activations) versus MAT (isoelectric intervals present, slower atrial rate)","Focal atrial tachycardia versus left atrial/atypical flutter (distinguished by P-wave morphology and often only by electrophysiology study)"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG to identify sawtooth flutter waves and conduction ratio","Vagal/AV-nodal-blocking maneuvers to unmask flutter waves at higher conduction ratios","Electrophysiology study with electroanatomic mapping to define circuit and confirm mechanism in atypical flutter or to distinguish from focal AT","CHA2DS2-VASc scoring for thromboembolic risk and bleeding-risk assessment"],"redFlags":["Hemodynamic instability with rapid ventricular response (may warrant urgent electrical cardioversion)","Severe symptoms attributable to the arrhythmia","Difficult-to-control ventricular rate, as rate control in flutter is frequently more challenging than in AF","Elevated thromboembolic risk by CHA2DS2-VASc requiring attention before rhythm conversion"],"prognosis":"Typical flutter is highly treatable, with cavotricuspid isthmus catheter ablation achieving success rates greater than 95% with low complication and recurrence rates; however, up to half of patients undergoing such ablation later develop atrial arrhythmias, most often atrial fibrillation. With antiarrhythmic drug therapy alone, over 70% of patients experience recurrence. MAT prognosis is largely tied to the underlying pulmonary or systemic illness; electrical cardioversion is ineffective for MAT (Harrison 22e p.1944-1946).","pitfalls":["In 2:1 conducted flutter, flutter waves hidden in the T wave can be mistaken for another regular narrow-complex tachycardia; failing to use AV-nodal-slowing maneuvers may miss the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1943).","Assuming AF and flutter are independent: antiarrhythmics given for AF (e.g., flecainide, propafenone, amiodarone) may unmask or organize the rhythm into flutter (Harrison 22e p.1943).","Attempting electrical cardioversion for MAT, which is ineffective for this automatic arrhythmia (Harrison 22e p.1946).","Mislabeling atypical/left atrial flutter as focal atrial tachycardia without electrophysiologic confirmation (Harrison 22e p.1944).","Assuming MAT carries the same thromboembolic risk as AF or flutter; in MAT this risk remains unclear and is not considered equivalent (Harrison 22e p.1946)."],"severityClassification":"No formal severity grading scale; thromboembolic risk in flutter is stratified by the CHA2DS2-VASc score, and hemodynamic stability/symptom severity guides urgency of intervention (Harrison 22e p.1944).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1943","Harrison 22e p.1944","Harrison 22e p.1945","Harrison 22e p.1946"],"crossLinks":[]},"common_variable_immunodeficiency":{"name":"Common variable immunodeficiency","system":"Immunology / Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CVID","common variable immune deficiency","acquired hypogammaglobulinemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Middleton's Allergy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["CVID is the most common symptomatic primary antibody deficiency and typically presents in adolescence or adulthood with recurrent sinopulmonary infections by encapsulated bacteria","The hallmark is low IgG with low IgA and/or IgM plus a poor antibody response to vaccines, in the absence of another defined cause of hypogammaglobulinaemia","It is far more than an infection disorder: autoimmune cytopenias, granulomatous disease, enteropathy, lymphoid hyperplasia, and an increased risk of lymphoma are common and drive much of the morbidity","Diagnosis is often delayed for years, so recurrent bacterial infections or unexplained bronchiectasis in an adult should prompt measurement of immunoglobulins","Bronchiectasis from repeated or inadequately treated infection is a leading cause of chronic morbidity, making early recognition and airway surveillance important","The mainstay of treatment is immunoglobulin replacement, given intravenously or subcutaneously, titrated to reduce infection frequency","Live vaccines should generally be avoided and are unreliable, and antibody responses to immunisation are used diagnostically rather than for protection","Autoimmune and inflammatory complications may need immunosuppression, which must be balanced carefully against the underlying immune deficiency","Because it is a heterogeneous diagnosis of exclusion, secondary causes such as protein loss, drugs, and haematologic malignancy must be ruled out"],"pathophysiology":"CVID is a heterogeneous group of primary immunodeficiencies characterised by defective differentiation of B cells into immunoglobulin-secreting plasma cells and impaired class switching and memory B-cell formation, resulting in low serum immunoglobulins and failure of specific antibody responses. Although predominantly a humoral defect, many patients also have T-cell and innate immune abnormalities and dysregulated immune tolerance, which explain the frequent autoimmune and granulomatous-lymphoproliferative complications. Most cases are sporadic; a minority carry identifiable monogenic defects (e.g. in TACI/TNFRSF13B, ICOS, CD19 complex, NFKB pathways). The combined immune dysfunction predisposes to both infection and immune dysregulation, including malignancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary hypogammaglobulinaemia from drugs (e.g. rituximab, anticonvulsants) or protein loss","X-linked agammaglobulinaemia and other defined primary antibody deficiencies","Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia, lymphoma, or myeloma causing immunoglobulin deficiency","Good syndrome (thymoma with immunodeficiency)","Specific antibody deficiency with normal immunoglobulin levels","HIV infection and protein-losing states (nephrotic syndrome, enteropathy)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum IgG, IgA, and IgM with IgG subclasses","Specific antibody titres before and after protein and polysaccharide vaccination","Lymphocyte subset and B-cell memory phenotyping","Exclusion of secondary causes (serum protein electrophoresis, HIV, drug review, urinary protein)","Chest imaging (including CT) for bronchiectasis and lung disease","Targeted biopsy and genetic testing when complications or a monogenic cause are suspected"],"redFlags":["Severe or overwhelming bacterial infection or sepsis in an untreated antibody-deficient patient","Rapidly enlarging lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, or systemic symptoms suggesting lymphoma","Progressive respiratory decline from bronchiectasis or interstitial lung disease","Severe autoimmune cytopenia or refractory enteropathy causing haemodynamic or nutritional compromise"],"prognosis":"With regular immunoglobulin replacement and prompt treatment of infection, life expectancy has improved substantially. Prognosis is worst in patients who develop non-infectious complications-bronchiectasis, chronic lung disease, autoimmune disease, granulomatous-lymphoproliferative disease, and lymphoma-which are now the main determinants of mortality.","pitfalls":["Delaying diagnosis by not measuring immunoglobulins in adults with recurrent sinopulmonary infection or unexplained bronchiectasis","Attributing autoimmune or granulomatous complications to a separate disease rather than to CVID itself","Giving live vaccines or relying on vaccination for protection in an antibody-deficient patient","Failing to exclude secondary and malignant causes of hypogammaglobulinaemia before labelling CVID"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"common_viral_respiratory_infections_other":{"name":"Common Viral Respiratory Infections, Other Than COVID-19","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Common cold","Viral upper respiratory tract infection","Non-COVID respiratory virus infection","Acute viral rhinosinusitis"],"chapter":204,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1531","clinicalPearls":["Respiratory virus infections are the most frequent infections in humans; many agents (rhinovirus, RSV, hMPV, parainfluenza, adenovirus, influenza, non-COVID coronaviruses) produce overlapping syndromes, so epidemiology, season, age, and exposure history narrow the differential more reliably than symptoms alone (Harrison 22e p.1531-1532).","The vocal cords mark the boundary between upper and lower respiratory tract; syndrome correlates with the cell types a virus prefers, e.g. rhinitis/common cold, sinusitis, otitis media, pharyngitis and laryngitis above versus tracheitis, bronchitis, bronchiolitis and pneumonia below (Harrison 22e p.1532).","Rhinoviruses are the single most common cause of the common cold, account for at least half of cases, and exist as more than 100 serotypes; because most adults develop radiographic sinus changes during a cold that resolve spontaneously, the entity is better termed rhinosinusitis (Harrison 22e p.1534-1535).","RSV is among the most transmissible human viruses and a leading cause of severe lower respiratory tract illness and hospitalization in both infants and the frail elderly; it has a single serotype with two antigenic subgroups (A and B) that are only about 25 percent cross-related, partly explaining lifelong reinfection (Harrison 22e p.1533).","Human metapneumovirus resembles RSV but is roughly half as likely to cause severe lower-tract disease, attributed to its lack of the nonstructural interferon-antagonist genes RSV uses to blunt innate immunity (Harrison 22e p.1533-1534).","Measles is probably the most contagious respiratory virus, spreading by small-particle aerosol; the classic triad is the '3 Cs' (cough, coryza, conjunctivitis) with high fever, a maculopapular rash within days, and pathognomonic Koplik's spots in the setting of typical rash and fever (Harrison 22e p.1534).","Coxsackievirus pharyngitis presents as herpangina (palatal vesicles/ulcers with fever, odynophagia and throat pain), and enterovirus A71 outbreaks of hand-foot-and-mouth disease can be complicated by neurologic disease; enterovirus D68 is linked to wheezing and acute flaccid myelitis (Harrison 22e p.1535).","Adenovirus species B and C dominate respiratory disease, can establish persistent shedding from tonsils/adenoids for months to years, and classically cause pharyngoconjunctival fever; acute respiratory disease (ARD, types 4 and 7) historically devastated crowded military-recruit settings (Harrison 22e p.1535-1536).","Acute bronchitis is a cough of under 3 weeks without pneumonia signs or radiographic infiltrate and is predominantly viral, whereas bacteria figure more prominently in chronic bronchitis; antibiotics do not improve uncomplicated viral respiratory infection in healthy patients (Harrison 22e p.1537).","RT-PCR (including multiplex panels) is the most sensitive and specific diagnostic, but extreme sensitivity is a pitfall: prolonged genome persistence in secretions for weeks after recovery means a positive result may reflect a recently resolved rather than a currently active infection (Harrison 22e p.1537).","Primary infection is generally far more severe than reinfection for the acute respiratory viruses; most primary (and thus many severe) infections occur in the first years of life, yet severe disease rises again in the elderly due to immune senescence (Harrison 22e p.1536).","Contact and droplet precautions with rigorous hand hygiene suffice for most respiratory viruses; measles virus and varicella-zoster virus are notable exceptions that require airborne precautions because they spread by small-particle aerosol (Harrison 22e p.1537)."],"pathophysiology":"Respiratory viruses enter via the respiratory tract and replicate in epithelium whose cell type dictates the resulting syndrome. Purely respiratory viruses (e.g. rhinoviruses) replicate only in respiratory epithelium, whereas systemic viruses (e.g. measles, SARS-CoV) disseminate via the bloodstream and may also injure the airway. Wheezing arises from smooth-muscle contraction plus luminal obstruction by mucus and sloughed epithelium, predominantly at the narrow-caliber bronchioles (bronchiolitis); croup reflects inflammation of larynx, trachea and bronchi causing inspiratory difficulty. Because non-viremic respiratory viruses elicit incomplete or waning immunity, reinfection with the same virus and even the same strain is common across life. Each virus type harbors vast antigenic diversity (e.g. influenza drift/shift, >100 rhinovirus serotypes), limiting durable cross-protection. Influenza pneumonia is notable for a high rate of secondary staphylococcal and streptococcal bacterial superinfection (Harrison 22e p.1531-1534).","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial pneumonia or secondary bacterial superinfection (notably staphylococcal/streptococcal after influenza)","Streptococcal pharyngitis versus viral pharyngitis","Bacterial sinusitis complicating viral rhinosinusitis","Asthma or COPD exacerbation triggered by viral infection","Allergic rhinitis mimicking the common cold"],"infectionMimics":["Influenza","COVID-19 / SARS-CoV-2","Bacterial pharyngitis, sinusitis, otitis media","Bacterial or atypical pneumonia","Pertussis presenting as prolonged cough","EBV mononucleosis with exudative pharyngitis","Primary HIV infection with pharyngitis and lymphadenopathy"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Allergic rhinitis / vasomotor rhinitis","Asthma or reactive airway disease","COPD exacerbation from non-infectious triggers","Aspiration or chemical/pollutant airway irritation","Cardiac dyspnea masquerading as lower respiratory illness"],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR molecular testing and multiplex respiratory panels (most sensitive/specific)","Rapid antigen detection for influenza and RSV in nasopharyngeal secretions","Viral culture in a reference laboratory (gold standard for isolation)","Direct/indirect fluorescent antibody detection in nasal secretions","Chest radiograph in all patients with suspected pneumonia","Pulse oximetry / oxygenation assessment in lower-tract disease"],"redFlags":["Hypoxia or respiratory failure requiring oxygen or mechanical ventilation","Tachypnea, dyspnea, chest pain or hypoxemia indicating pneumonia","EBV tonsillar swelling severe enough to threaten airway occlusion","Severe disease in immunocompromised hosts (HSCT/solid-organ transplant, chemotherapy-induced leukopenia, advanced HIV)","Neurologic complications with enterovirus A71 or acute flaccid myelitis with enterovirus D68","Exposure history suggesting high-mortality zoonotic agents (avian influenza H5N1/H7N9, MERS-CoV)","Dehydration from fever, tachypnea or poor oral intake (esp. young children with primary HSV stomatitis)"],"prognosis":"Most acute viral respiratory infections in immunocompetent hosts are self-limited, with reinfection generally milder than primary infection. Severe and sometimes fatal disease concentrates in infants, the frail/institutionalized elderly, those with underlying lung disease (especially COPD and oxygen-dependent disease) or cardiovascular disease, and immunocompromised patients, in whom conventional viruses may cause chronic and even fatal infection. Selected agents carry high lethality: the 2002-2003 SARS-CoV epidemic (>8000 cases) had mortality approaching 10 percent, and MERS-CoV mortality is approximately 35 percent. Measles, though often milder in immunocompetent children, causes high mortality in low-resource settings, especially with malnutrition (Harrison 22e p.1533-1536).","pitfalls":["Treating uncomplicated viral respiratory infection with antibiotics, which does not improve outcome in otherwise healthy patients","Over-interpreting RT-PCR positives, since viral genome can persist for weeks post-recovery and asymptomatic individuals (e.g. rhinovirus, bocavirus) frequently test positive","Applying rapid tests indiscriminately, lowering positive predictive value; testing is best targeted to high-risk exposures in the appropriate season","Laboratory PCR contamination from prior reactions producing false positives","Assuming rhinovirus is confined to the upper tract; it is now linked to wheezing, asthma exacerbations and (rhinovirus C) pneumonia/COPD exacerbation","Missing secondary bacterial pneumonia complicating influenza"],"severityClassification":"No formal grading scheme; severity is stratified clinically by anatomic level (upper vs lower respiratory tract), presence of pneumonia or respiratory failure/hypoxia, and host risk (extremes of age, underlying lung/cardiac disease, COPD, obesity, immunocompromise). Accumulation of multiple risk factors markedly increases risk of severe lower respiratory tract disease (Harrison 22e p.1536).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1531","Harrison 22e p.1532","Harrison 22e p.1533","Harrison 22e p.1534","Harrison 22e p.1535","Harrison 22e p.1536","Harrison 22e p.1537"],"crossLinks":[]},"compartment_syndrome":{"name":"Acute compartment syndrome","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["compartment syndrome","acute limb compartment syndrome","ACS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A surgical emergency in which raised pressure within a closed fascial compartment compromises tissue perfusion, leading to muscle and nerve ischaemia if not decompressed","The cardinal early sign is pain out of proportion to the injury and pain on passive stretch of the muscles in the affected compartment","It commonly follows tibial or forearm fractures, crush injuries, reperfusion after vascular occlusion, or tight casts and dressings","Pulselessness, pallor, paraesthesia, and paralysis are late signs; waiting for them risks irreversible damage, so a normal distal pulse does not exclude the diagnosis","Diagnosis is largely clinical, supported by compartment pressure measurement, particularly in the unconscious or unreliable patient","Definitive treatment is urgent open fasciotomy to decompress all involved compartments; casts and constrictive dressings must be split or removed immediately while preparing for surgery","The limb should be kept at heart level rather than elevated, and hypotension avoided, to maintain perfusion pressure","Missed or delayed treatment leads to Volkmann's ischaemic contracture, permanent deficit, rhabdomyolysis with renal failure, and potential limb loss"],"pathophysiology":"Acute compartment syndrome develops when pressure within a non-compliant osseofascial compartment rises to a level that impedes capillary perfusion. Bleeding, oedema, or external constriction increases compartment volume or reduces its capacity, raising interstitial pressure until it exceeds the perfusion gradient. Ischaemia increases capillary permeability, causing further oedema in a vicious cycle. Muscle and nerve are highly sensitive to ischaemia; sustained pressure elevation for a few hours produces irreversible necrosis. Because arterial inflow pressure is much higher than compartment pressure, distal pulses are often preserved until very late, making them an unreliable marker.","additionalDifferentials":["Arterial injury or acute limb ischaemia","Deep vein thrombosis","Cellulitis or necrotising soft-tissue infection","Fracture or crush injury pain without compartment syndrome","Peripheral nerve injury","Chronic exertional compartment syndrome (in the appropriate setting)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial clinical assessment of pain, pain on passive stretch, and neurology (the mainstay)","Intracompartmental pressure measurement, especially in obtunded or equivocal cases","Serum creatine kinase and renal function to detect rhabdomyolysis","Urinalysis for myoglobinuria","Radiographs to characterise any underlying fracture"],"redFlags":["SURGICAL EMERGENCY: pain out of proportion and pain on passive stretch mandate urgent fasciotomy without delay","Do not wait for pulselessness or paralysis, which are late and indicate established ischaemic damage","Rapidly rising creatine kinase with dark urine indicating rhabdomyolysis and impending renal failure","A tense, swollen limb after fracture, crush, or reperfusion, or beneath a tight cast"],"prognosis":"With prompt fasciotomy, usually within a few hours of onset, muscle and nerve can recover fully; delay leads to ischaemic necrosis, fixed contracture (Volkmann's), sensory and motor deficits, chronic pain, and in severe cases systemic complications or amputation.","pitfalls":["Relying on the presence of distal pulses to exclude compartment syndrome","Attributing escalating analgesic requirements solely to the fracture rather than recognising ischaemic pain","Elevating the limb above heart level, which reduces perfusion pressure","Delaying decompression pending imaging or specialist availability, allowing irreversible injury"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"complement_deficiency":{"name":"Complement deficiency","system":"Immunology / Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["complement component deficiency","inherited complement deficiency","terminal complement deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Middleton's Allergy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The infection and disease pattern in complement deficiency tracks the part of the cascade affected, so localising the defect predicts clinical risk","Deficiencies of the terminal components (C5-C9) and of properdin impair formation of the membrane attack complex and cause a striking susceptibility to recurrent Neisseria infection, especially meningococcal disease","Early classical-pathway deficiencies (C1q, C1r/s, C4, C2) predispose strongly to systemic lupus erythematosus and other immune-complex diseases because complement is needed to clear apoptotic cells and immune complexes","C3 deficiency, at the hub of all pathways, produces both severe pyogenic infection with encapsulated bacteria and immune-complex disease","Recurrent or unusual neisserial infection, or a single episode with an uncommon meningococcal serogroup, should prompt complement evaluation","The CH50 screens the classical pathway and the AH50 the alternative pathway; a near-absent total haemolytic complement points to a specific component deficiency to be confirmed by individual assays","Prevention centres on vaccination against encapsulated organisms-meningococcus (including serogroup B), pneumococcus, and Haemophilus influenzae type b-with prompt treatment of febrile illness","C1-inhibitor deficiency is a distinct entity causing bradykinin-mediated hereditary angioedema rather than the infection-prone phenotypes of other complement defects","Acquired complement consumption from active immune-complex disease must be distinguished from inherited deficiency, since low levels may simply reflect ongoing disease activity"],"pathophysiology":"The complement system comprises the classical, lectin, and alternative pathways that converge on C3 and the terminal pathway that assembles the C5b-9 membrane attack complex. Its functions include opsonisation, chemotaxis and inflammation, direct lysis of certain pathogens, and clearance of immune complexes and apoptotic debris. Inherited deficiency of a component removes the corresponding function: loss of early classical components impairs immune-complex handling and predisposes to lupus-like autoimmunity; deficiency of C3 or opsonic components causes pyogenic infection with encapsulated bacteria; and deficiency of terminal components or properdin cripples membrane-attack-complex killing of Neisseria. Regulatory-protein defects produce different phenotypes, such as C1-inhibitor deficiency causing angioedema and factor H/I defects causing atypical haemolytic uraemic syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Asplenia or hyposplenism with encapsulated-organism infection","Antibody deficiencies (e.g. CVID, specific antibody deficiency)","Acquired complement consumption from active autoimmune or immune-complex disease","Systemic lupus erythematosus and other connective-tissue diseases","Atypical haemolytic uraemic syndrome from complement-regulatory defects","Hereditary angioedema from C1-inhibitor deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CH50 (classical pathway) and AH50 (alternative pathway) functional screens","Individual complement component assays to identify the specific deficiency","C3, C4, and C1q levels, distinguishing inherited deficiency from consumption","Autoimmune serology (e.g. ANA, dsDNA) when immune-complex disease is suspected","Genetic testing in selected or familial cases","Microbiologic confirmation and serogrouping of neisserial and other invasive infections"],"redFlags":["Meningococcal or other invasive neisserial infection, or meningitis with rapid deterioration, requiring emergency antibiotics","Recurrent invasive infection with encapsulated bacteria signalling significant deficiency","Sepsis in a known complement-deficient or asplenic patient","Features of severe systemic lupus with major organ involvement in early-component deficiency"],"prognosis":"With appropriate vaccination, prompt treatment of infection, and in some cases antibiotic prophylaxis, most patients with complement deficiency do well. Prognosis is worse when invasive infection is not recognised early, and outcomes in early-component deficiency are additionally shaped by the severity of associated autoimmune disease.","pitfalls":["Failing to evaluate complement after recurrent or unusual neisserial infection","Interpreting a low complement level as inherited deficiency when it reflects consumption by active disease","Neglecting to give and update the full schedule of vaccines against encapsulated organisms","Confusing C1-inhibitor deficiency (angioedema) with the infection-prone complement defects"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"complete_heart_block":{"name":"Complete heart block","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["third-degree AV block","complete AV block","CHB","third-degree atrioventricular block"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["In complete heart block there is no conduction from atria to ventricles, so atrial and ventricular activity are entirely independent, giving AV dissociation with a slower, regular ventricular rate driven by an escape rhythm","The ECG shows P waves and QRS complexes marching independently, with the atrial rate faster than the ventricular rate","The reliability and rate of the escape rhythm depend on its origin: a narrow junctional escape is usually faster and more stable, whereas a wide ventricular escape is slower and less dependable","Presentations include fatigue, breathlessness, presyncope, syncope, and haemodynamic collapse, and it can precipitate cardiac arrest if the escape fails","Common causes are age-related conduction system fibrosis, ischaemia or infarction (particularly inferior), drugs affecting the AV node, and infiltrative or post-procedural disease","Reversible causes such as offending drugs, hyperkalaemia, and ischaemia should be sought and corrected","Symptomatic or high-risk complete heart block is an indication for cardiac pacing, temporary in the acute setting and permanent for irreversible block","In acute inferior infarction the block is often transient and nodal, whereas anterior infarction with block implies extensive damage and a worse prognosis"],"pathophysiology":"Complete heart block arises when conduction through the AV node or the His-Purkinje system is entirely interrupted, so no atrial impulse reaches the ventricles. Ventricular activity then depends on a subsidiary escape pacemaker distal to the block. Block within the AV node typically produces a junctional escape with a narrow QRS and a relatively faster, more stable rate, whereas block in the His-Purkinje system produces a ventricular escape with a wide QRS, slower rate, and greater risk of asystole. Causes include degenerative fibrosis of the conduction system, ischaemic damage, increased vagal or drug-induced nodal suppression, electrolyte disturbance, and infiltrative processes.","additionalDifferentials":["Second-degree AV block (Mobitz I and II)","High-grade AV block with occasional conduction","AV dissociation without true complete block","Sinus bradycardia or sinus arrest with a junctional escape","Drug or electrolyte induced bradyarrhythmia","Junctional rhythm from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG and continuous rhythm monitoring to confirm AV dissociation and characterise the escape","Electrolytes including potassium and review of AV-nodal-blocking medications","Assessment for acute ischaemia or infarction including cardiac biomarkers","Echocardiography to assess ventricular function and structural or infiltrative disease","Evaluation for infiltrative, inflammatory, or infective conduction system disease when suspected"],"redFlags":["MEDICAL EMERGENCY: haemodynamic instability, syncope, or an unreliable slow wide-complex escape rhythm at risk of asystole, requiring urgent temporary pacing","Complete heart block complicating acute anterior myocardial infarction, indicating extensive damage","Recurrent syncope (Stokes-Adams attacks) from transient ventricular standstill","Escape rhythm failure progressing to asystole and cardiac arrest"],"prognosis":"Untreated symptomatic complete heart block carries a high risk of syncope and sudden death, but permanent pacing restores a normal prognosis in most; outcome is worse when block complicates extensive anterior infarction or advanced structural heart disease, and better when a reversible cause is corrected.","pitfalls":["Mistaking marked AV dissociation from other mechanisms for true complete block, or vice versa","Failing to identify and reverse causes such as AV-nodal drugs, hyperkalaemia, or ischaemia before committing to permanent pacing","Underestimating the instability of a wide-complex ventricular escape rhythm","Delaying temporary pacing in a symptomatic or unstable patient"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"concussion_and_other_traumatic_brain":{"name":"Concussion and Other Traumatic Brain Injuries","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Traumatic brain injury","TBI","Concussion","Mild TBI","mTBI","Head injury","Closed head injury"],"chapter":454,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3570","clinicalPearls":["TBI is defined as an externally caused alteration in brain function or other evidence of brain pathology, marked by any of the following: loss/decrease in consciousness, retrograde or posttraumatic amnesia, neurologic deficits, or altered mental state at the moment of injury (p.3571).","Incidence shows a bimodal age distribution: motor vehicle crashes predominate in younger persons (more often men), while falls are the leading cause in older adults (more often women); roughly 70-90% (most estimates ~85%) of treated TBIs are mild (p.3570, p.3572).","Severity is graded with the Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS): mild 13-15, moderate 9-12, severe 3-8; the individual eye/verbal/motor components (e.g., E3V4M6) are more informative than the summed total, which should only describe groups (p.3571).","In moderate-to-severe TBI, GCS plus the duration of loss of consciousness (LOC) and posttraumatic amnesia (PTA) predict outcome well; in mild TBI, LOC and PTA are far less predictive of recovery time and outcome, and postinjury symptom burden is the most robust predictor (p.3571, p.3574).","Epidural hematoma classically follows tearing of the middle meningeal artery beneath a temporal-bone fracture, evolves rapidly, and may be preceded by a lucid interval; rapid surgical evacuation usually yields a favorable outcome (p.3572).","Acute subdural hematoma is usually venous in origin, can occur without major trauma (especially in the elderly or anticoagulated, or from acceleration/whiplash forces), and carries significant morbidity and mortality when surgery is required - in contrast to the generally better-treated epidural hematoma (p.3572).","Contusions follow coup (under the impact) and contrecoup (antipolar) mechanisms; blunt deceleration typically bruises the orbitofrontal and anterior/basal temporal lobes, and old contusion sites become gliotic scars that are highly epileptogenic (p.3573).","Traumatic axonal injury (TAI) from shearing forces is among the most common TBI lesions; four or more foci constitute diffuse axonal injury (DAI), which when widespread is proposed to underlie prolonged coma and the vegetative state (p.3573).","Anosmia and apparent loss of taste occur in ~10% of serious head injuries (especially falls onto the back of the head) from shearing of olfactory filaments at the cribriform plate; persistent bilateral anosmia for several months carries a poor prognosis (p.3573-3574).","Second-impact syndrome is a rare, often fatal diffuse cerebral swelling that follows a second minor head injury before the first has resolved - a key reason no athlete should return to play until fully asymptomatic and cleared (p.3575).","Blood biomarkers GFAP (astroglial injury) and UCHL1 (neuronal injury) are now usable at point of care; GFAP in particular discriminates intracranial abnormalities and helps stratify CT+, CT-/MRI+, and CT-/MRI- patients, with both diagnostic and prognostic value (p.3574).","Chronic traumatic encephalopathy (CTE) - tau deposition in superficial cortical layers, perivascular regions, and the depths of sulci - is linked to repetitive head impacts but remains a postmortem diagnosis; proposed clinical criteria lack specificity and in vivo imaging/biomarker findings have been inconsistent (p.3576-3577)."],"pathophysiology":"TBI results from external mechanical force - the head being struck or striking an object, acceleration/deceleration of the brain, penetration, or blast/explosion - producing primary structural lesions and secondary injury. A skull fracture marks the site and severity of impact and, when the arachnoid is torn, opens a pathway for CSF leak and bacterial entry (meningitis risk); basilar fractures may cause CSF leak, pneumocephalus, and delayed cavernous-carotid fistula. Extra-axial hematomas arise from torn meningeal arteries (epidural) or bridging veins (subdural) and exert mass effect that raises intracranial pressure. Cortical surface bruising (contusion) occurs at coup and contrecoup sites with petechial hemorrhage, edema, and tissue destruction. Shearing of axons (traumatic/diffuse axonal injury) at impact, with associated microhemorrhages, disrupts white-matter connectivity. Traumatic subarachnoid hemorrhage from rupture of small cortical vessels is common and, when diffuse after severe TBI, increases mortality. In concussion/mild TBI, conventional CT is often normal while higher-resolution 3T MRI may reveal contusion or microhemorrhage; advanced imaging shows reduced cerebral blood flow, altered functional connectivity, and white-matter microstructural change that broadly track injury severity and recovery, though subtle changes may persist after clinical recovery (a neurobiologic recovery 'tail').","additionalDifferentials":["Postconcussion symptom overlap with preinjury mood or anxiety disorders (e.g., depression, PTSD)","Transient ischemic attack (chronic subdural hematoma may mimic with brief hemiparesis or aphasia)","Intracranial neoplasm or abscess (evolving contusion with surrounding enhancement and edema can be mistaken for these)","Carotid or vertebral artery dissection presenting as focal deficit after trauma","Vasovagal syncope after injury","Drug or alcohol intoxication confounding the neurologic exam","Cervical spine injury (may be clinically inapparent)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chronic subdural hematoma mimicking TIA, dementia, or personality change in the elderly","Contusion with delayed contrast enhancement mimicking tumor or abscess","Trochlear (fourth nerve) palsy after minor head injury mimicking primary ocular motility disorder","Persistent postconcussion symptoms attributable instead to preinjury psychiatric illness, migraine, or adjustment reaction","CTE clinical criteria overlapping with other neurodegenerative conditions (low specificity)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Noncontrast CT - first-line for skull fracture, extra-axial hematoma, contusion, traumatic SAH, and large TAI lesions with blood","MRI (including gradient-echo/susceptibility-weighted and diffusion sequences) - more sensitive than CT for microhemorrhage and axonal injury; detects intracranial abnormalities in ~27% of CT-negative mild TBI patients and has prognostic value","Advanced/multimodal MRI (functional MRI, cerebral blood flow, diffusion) - increasing sensitivity for subtle injury","Blood-based biomarkers GFAP and UCHL1 - point-of-care detection and prognostication across the TBI spectrum","GCS scoring before sedation/paralysis or intubation","Validated symptom inventories (e.g., Rivermead Post-Concussion Symptom Questionnaire) to quantify and track recovery","Intracranial pressure monitoring (ventricular catheter or fiberoptic device) in severe TBI to guide therapy (note: has not been shown to improve outcome)","Cervical spine imaging given risk of concurrent occult injury"],"redFlags":["Lucid interval followed by neurologic deterioration (suggests epidural or, less often, subdural hematoma)","Stupor or coma, hemiparesis, and unilateral pupillary enlargement (larger hematoma with mass effect)","Coma from the moment of injury - requires immediate resuscitation and neurologic attention","Persistent confusion, repeated vomiting (two or more episodes), palpable skull fracture, seizure, or >30 min of retrograde/persistent anterograde amnesia - sensitive indicators of intracranial hemorrhage warranting CT","Age >65 years, underlying coagulopathy or anticoagulant use, or concurrent serious bodily injury - lower the threshold for imaging","CSF leak (orthostatic headache, rhinorrhea/otorrhea), hemotympanum, Battle sign, or raccoon eyes - signs of basilar skull fracture","Large contusion or hemorrhage after only minor trauma - raises concern for coagulopathy or anticoagulation","Bilateral anosmia persisting for months - poor prognostic sign"],"prognosis":"The typical concussion syndrome has a good prognosis with little risk of subsequent deterioration. After sport-related concussion, recovery is generally favorable: in a large collegiate cohort, median symptom resolution was about 6.4 days and median return to sport 12.8 days, with ~92% symptom resolution and ~85% clearance to return by one month; most athletes across ages fully recover over 1-4 weeks. A subset develop persistent postconcussion symptoms (PPCS) beyond ~3 months; preinjury mental health disorder, migraine, and prior concussion predispose to prolonged recovery. Recovery from severe TBI can take years, and long-term outcome prediction is limited and often incorrect; best practice recommends aggressive therapy for at least 72 h (absent brain death). TBI is now recognized as a chronic, dynamic condition, with meaningful improvement or decline observed 2-7 years post-injury regardless of initial severity. TBI (mild to severe) is associated with a 63-96% increased risk of all-cause dementia, with risk greatest after severe injury; TBI with LOC >1 h has been linked to subsequent Parkinson's disease and Lewy body accumulation.","pitfalls":["Grading severity solely on acute injury characteristics can mislead - some severe TBI patients recover fully while some mild TBI patients have lifelong disability.","Relying on the summed GCS total rather than its components loses information; the score is confounded if recorded after sedation, paralysis, or intubation.","Assuming a normal CT excludes injury in mild TBI - MRI reveals intracranial abnormalities in about 27% of CT-negative patients and 3T MRI can show contusion/microhemorrhage.","Prescribing prolonged rest after concussion - inactivity beyond ~5 days can be detrimental and prolong recovery; supervised subthreshold exercise shortens symptom duration.","Overusing dependence-producing drugs for postconcussion symptoms.","Missing chronic subdural hematoma in the elderly, where trauma may be trivial or forgotten and the clot can become isodense and inapparent on CT between 2-6 weeks.","Overlooking concurrent occult cervical spine injury or drug/alcohol intoxication in injuries of intermediate severity.","Diagnosing CTE in vivo - it remains a postmortem diagnosis and proposed clinical criteria lack specificity.","Therapeutic nihilism in severe TBI, reinforced by the common but premature 6-month outcome endpoint - recovery may continue for years."],"severityClassification":"Glasgow Coma Scale-based grading: mild/concussion GCS 13-15, moderate GCS 9-12, severe GCS 3-8 (scores range 3-15). Mild TBI with CT and/or MRI abnormality is termed 'complicated mTBI' and carries a higher risk of unfavorable outcome. Loss of consciousness and posttraumatic amnesia duration further characterize severity. CTE neuropathology has been proposed to be staged as 'low' or 'high' severity (criteria not yet established).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3570","Harrison 22e p.3571","Harrison 22e p.3572","Harrison 22e p.3573","Harrison 22e p.3574","Harrison 22e p.3575","Harrison 22e p.3576","Harrison 22e p.3577"],"crossLinks":[]},"conduct_disorder":{"name":"Conduct disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The disorder is a repetitive and persistent pattern of behaviour in children and adolescents that violates the basic rights of others or major age-appropriate societal norms","Behaviours cluster into aggression to people and animals, destruction of property, deceitfulness or theft, and serious rule violations such as truancy and running away","Childhood-onset forms, appearing before adolescence, tend to be more severe and carry a worse prognosis than adolescent-onset forms","A specifier for limited prosocial emotions, marked by lack of remorse, callousness, and shallow affect, identifies a subgroup with poorer outcomes","It must be distinguished from oppositional defiant disorder, which involves defiance and irritability without the serious rights-violating behaviours of conduct disorder","Management is primarily psychosocial, including parent management training, family and multisystemic interventions, and school-based support, with medication reserved for comorbid conditions","Comorbidity with attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder, mood and anxiety disorders, learning problems, and substance misuse is common and should be treated","Early identification and intervention improve outcomes and may reduce progression toward antisocial personality disorder in adulthood"],"pathophysiology":"Conduct disorder arises from an interaction of genetic and temperamental factors, such as difficult temperament and low behavioural inhibition, with adverse environmental influences including harsh or inconsistent parenting, maltreatment, and socioeconomic adversity. Neurobiological correlates include altered function in circuits underlying emotional processing, reward, and threat appraisal, and blunted autonomic responsiveness, particularly in the callous-unemotional subgroup.","additionalDifferentials":["Oppositional defiant disorder","Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder","Intermittent explosive disorder","Mood disorder with irritability","Substance use disorder","Adjustment disorder in response to a stressor","Emerging antisocial personality traits in older adolescents"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Comprehensive developmental, family, and psychosocial history","Collateral information from school and caregivers","Screening for comorbid ADHD, mood, anxiety, and learning disorders","Substance use screening in adolescents","Assessment of the home environment and any safeguarding concerns"],"redFlags":["Serious violence or use of weapons posing danger to others","Cruelty to people or animals and fire-setting","Suicidal ideation or self-harm, which can coexist with the disorder","Signs of abuse, neglect, or exploitation requiring safeguarding action"],"prognosis":"The course is variable; adolescent-onset conduct disorder often remits by adulthood, whereas childhood-onset and callous-unemotional presentations carry a higher risk of persistence, antisocial personality disorder, substance misuse, and poor social outcomes. Prognosis improves with early, sustained psychosocial intervention, treatment of comorbidities, and a supportive family and school environment.","pitfalls":["Confusing oppositional defiant disorder with the more severe conduct disorder","Missing treatable comorbid ADHD or mood disorder that worsens behaviour","Relying on medication as a primary treatment rather than psychosocial intervention","Overlooking safeguarding concerns and adverse home circumstances","Failing to assess for coexisting suicidality and self-harm"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"congenital_adrenal_hyperplasia":{"name":"Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CAH","21-hydroxylase deficiency","adrenogenital syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of autosomal recessive disorders of adrenal steroid synthesis, the great majority caused by 21-hydroxylase deficiency.","The classic salt-wasting form presents in the first weeks of life with vomiting, poor feeding, weight loss, dehydration, and shock from combined cortisol and aldosterone deficiency.","Female infants with the classic form often show virilisation of the external genitalia (ambiguous genitalia), whereas affected males may appear normal at birth and are at particular risk of unrecognised salt-wasting crisis.","Characteristic biochemistry includes hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, and metabolic acidosis in the salt-wasting crisis, with markedly elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone.","A non-classic (late-onset) form presents later with signs of androgen excess such as premature pubarche, hirsutism, or menstrual irregularity.","Newborn screening measuring 17-hydroxyprogesterone allows early detection before a life-threatening crisis.","Treatment principle is glucocorticoid replacement to correct cortisol deficiency and suppress excess adrenal androgen, with mineralocorticoid replacement (and salt supplementation in infancy) for salt-wasting forms.","Additional stress steroid cover is essential during illness, surgery, or major stress to prevent adrenal crisis.","Management of affected girls includes multidisciplinary care regarding virilised genitalia, and genetic counselling is important for families."],"pathophysiology":"A deficiency of an enzyme in the adrenal cortisol synthesis pathway, most often 21-hydroxylase, reduces cortisol production. The resulting loss of negative feedback drives increased ACTH secretion, which stimulates adrenal hyperplasia and diverts steroid precursors into the androgen pathway, causing androgen excess and virilisation. In the severe salt-wasting form, aldosterone synthesis is also impaired, leading to sodium loss, hyperkalaemia, and volume depletion, while accumulation of the precursor 17-hydroxyprogesterone serves as a diagnostic marker.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of ambiguous genitalia and disorders of sex development","Pyloric stenosis or sepsis in a vomiting, dehydrated neonate","Other forms of primary adrenal insufficiency","Rarer enzyme defects (11-beta-hydroxylase, 3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency)","Precocious puberty from other causes","Polycystic ovary syndrome (for non-classic presentations)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum 17-hydroxyprogesterone (baseline and, if needed, after ACTH stimulation)","Serum electrolytes, glucose, and acid-base status","Plasma renin activity and aldosterone","Adrenal androgens (androstenedione, testosterone)","Karyotype and pelvic ultrasound in ambiguous genitalia","Genetic analysis of the CYP21A2 gene"],"redFlags":["Salt-wasting adrenal crisis with dehydration, hypotension, or shock","Severe hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia","Hypoglycaemia","Vomiting and poor feeding in a neonate, particularly a male infant","Intercurrent illness or surgery without adequate stress steroid cover"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis, hormone replacement, and stress dosing, children generally grow and develop well; risks include life-threatening adrenal crises, effects of over- or under-treatment on growth and puberty, and fertility and psychosocial issues in classic disease.","pitfalls":["Missing salt-wasting crisis in a male infant with normal genitalia","Failing to provide stress steroid cover during illness or surgery","Over-treating with glucocorticoids, impairing growth, or under-treating, allowing androgen excess","Misattributing a neonatal collapse to sepsis or pyloric stenosis without checking electrolytes and adrenal steroids"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"congenital_cmv":{"name":"Congenital Cytomegalovirus Infection","system":"Neonatal / Infectious","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cCMV","congenital CMV","congenital cytomegalovirus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common congenital viral infection and a leading non-genetic cause of sensorineural hearing loss and neurodevelopmental disability in children.","Most infected newborns are asymptomatic at birth, yet a proportion of these still go on to develop late-onset hearing loss.","Symptomatic disease may include small size for gestational age, microcephaly, petechiae, jaundice, hepatosplenomegaly, and low platelets.","Neurological features can include intracranial calcifications (often periventricular), chorioretinitis, and seizures.","Both primary maternal infection and non-primary (reactivation or reinfection) during pregnancy can transmit to the fetus, with primary infection carrying higher risk.","Diagnosis must be confirmed within the first three weeks of life to distinguish congenital from perinatally acquired infection, using viral detection from urine or saliva.","Because of the risk of progressive hearing loss, confirmed cases need structured audiological surveillance over time, not just a single newborn screen.","Antiviral therapy is considered for symptomatic infants, particularly those with central nervous system involvement, to improve hearing and neurodevelopmental outcomes."],"pathophysiology":"Cytomegalovirus, a herpesvirus, crosses the placenta following maternal infection and disseminates in the fetus, where it replicates in multiple organs. The developing central nervous system is particularly vulnerable: infection disrupts neuronal migration and brain development and causes an inflammatory response that produces calcifications, microcephaly, and migrational abnormalities. Involvement of the inner ear leads to progressive sensorineural hearing loss, while infection of the liver, bone marrow, and reticuloendothelial system accounts for hepatosplenomegaly, thrombocytopenia, and jaundice.","additionalDifferentials":["Other congenital infections in the TORCH group (toxoplasmosis, rubella, syphilis, herpes simplex, Zika)","Neonatal sepsis or bacterial infection","Congenital toxoplasmosis (also causes intracranial calcification)","Haemolytic disease of the newborn","Inherited metabolic or genetic disorders causing microcephaly","Neonatal haematological disorders causing thrombocytopenia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CMV detection by PCR from urine or saliva within the first three weeks of life","Newborn and serial audiological (hearing) assessment","Cranial imaging (ultrasound and/or MRI) for calcification and brain abnormalities","Ophthalmological examination for chorioretinitis","Full blood count, platelets, and liver function tests","Dried blood spot testing when late confirmation is needed"],"redFlags":["Microcephaly or abnormal neuroimaging indicating central nervous system involvement","Confirmed or progressive sensorineural hearing loss","Symptomatic disease with thrombocytopenia, hepatitis, or petechiae","Chorioretinitis threatening vision","Seizures in the neonatal period","Failure to confirm the diagnosis within three weeks, losing the ability to prove congenital infection"],"prognosis":"Asymptomatic infants generally do well but remain at risk of late-onset hearing loss, warranting ongoing audiological follow-up; symptomatic infants, especially those with neurological involvement, have a higher risk of hearing loss, developmental delay, and other long-term deficits, though early antiviral therapy can improve outcomes in selected cases.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnostic window by testing after three weeks of age and being unable to distinguish congenital from acquired infection","Assuming a normal newborn hearing screen excludes future hearing loss","Overlooking asymptomatic infection, which still carries a risk of late sequelae","Attributing intracranial calcification solely to toxoplasmosis without confirming CMV","Failing to arrange long-term audiological and developmental surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"congenital_diaphragmatic_hernia":{"name":"Congenital Diaphragmatic Hernia","system":"Neonatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CDH","Bochdalek hernia","diaphragmatic hernia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A defect in the diaphragm allows abdominal viscera to herniate into the thorax during fetal development, impairing lung growth; the posterolateral (Bochdalek) defect, more often left-sided, is most common.","The principal problems are pulmonary hypoplasia and pulmonary hypertension rather than the anatomical defect itself.","Many cases are detected on antenatal ultrasound, allowing delivery at a centre with neonatal surgical and intensive care support.","A typical presentation is respiratory distress soon after birth with a scaphoid (sunken) abdomen and a shifted apex or heart sounds, and reduced breath sounds on the affected side.","Bowel sounds may be audible in the chest, and the chest radiograph shows bowel loops in the thorax with mediastinal shift.","Bag-mask ventilation should be avoided because it distends the herniated bowel and worsens lung compression; early endotracheal intubation and gastric decompression are preferred.","Initial management focuses on gentle, lung-protective ventilation, cardiorespiratory stabilisation, and treatment of pulmonary hypertension before surgery.","Surgical reduction and repair of the defect is undertaken after the infant is physiologically stabilised, not as an emergency.","Severe cases may need advanced support such as inhaled pulmonary vasodilators or extracorporeal membrane oxygenation."],"pathophysiology":"Failure of the diaphragm to close completely during embryogenesis permits abdominal organs to occupy the thoracic cavity while the lungs are developing. The space-occupying viscera restrict growth of the ipsilateral (and to a lesser extent contralateral) lung, producing pulmonary hypoplasia with fewer alveoli and abnormal, muscularised pulmonary arterioles. After birth these abnormal vessels fail to relax normally, causing persistent pulmonary hypertension with right-to-left shunting, hypoxaemia, and acidosis. The combination of reduced functional lung tissue and elevated pulmonary vascular resistance drives the respiratory failure that dominates the clinical course.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital pulmonary airway malformation","Congenital lobar emphysema","Pulmonary sequestration","Pneumothorax or large pleural effusion","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn of other cause","Eventration of the diaphragm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antenatal ultrasound and, where available, fetal MRI with lung volume estimation","Chest and abdominal radiograph showing thoracic bowel loops and mediastinal shift","Echocardiography to assess pulmonary hypertension and exclude associated cardiac lesions","Blood gas and pre- and post-ductal oxygen saturations to gauge shunting","Evaluation for associated anomalies and genetic conditions"],"redFlags":["Severe respiratory distress and hypoxaemia at or shortly after birth","Persistent pulmonary hypertension with right-to-left shunting refractory to therapy","Deterioration after bag-mask ventilation distends intrathoracic bowel","Signs of inadequate oxygenation and rising acidosis","Associated major cardiac or other anomalies worsening the outlook"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends chiefly on the degree of pulmonary hypoplasia and pulmonary hypertension and on associated anomalies. With optimal perinatal care many infants survive, though survivors may have chronic lung disease, feeding and growth problems, and neurodevelopmental sequelae. Severe hypoplasia and refractory pulmonary hypertension carry a poor prognosis.","pitfalls":["Using bag-mask ventilation and inflating herniated bowel, worsening lung compression","Rushing to surgery before stabilising pulmonary hypertension and gas exchange","Attributing neonatal distress to primary lung disease and missing the diagnosis on examination and radiograph","Using aggressive high-pressure ventilation that risks barotrauma to hypoplastic lungs","Overlooking associated cardiac and genetic anomalies that affect management and prognosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"congenital_heart_disease":{"name":"Adult Congenital Heart Disease","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ASD","VSD","tetralogy of Fallot","Eisenmenger"],"chapter":280,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2067","clinicalPearls":["At least 1.4 million U.S. adults are living with congenital heart disease (CHD); the majority were diagnosed in childhood, but a substantial percentage have CHD first recognized in adulthood, so lifelong follow-up with ACHD expertise is recommended (Harrison 22e p.2067).","ASD physiology is predominantly a left-to-right shunt; the degree of shunting (and thus right heart volume loading) depends on defect size and the diastolic properties of the heart. As patients age, diabetes, hypertension, and atherosclerosis reduce left-heart compliance and may increase left-to-right shunting and symptoms (Harrison 22e p.2070).","The classic ASD physical finding is a wide, FIXED splitting of the second heart sound, due to prolonged RV ejection and increased pulmonary artery capacitance delaying pulmonary valve closure; the ECG commonly shows incomplete right bundle branch block (Harrison 22e p.2070).","Secundum ASD (defect in the region of the fossa ovalis) is the most common type and can often be closed percutaneously with an occluder device; a patent foramen ovale (PFO) is distinct, is NOT associated with right-sided dilation, and persists in up to 25% of adults (Harrison 22e p.2070).","Surgical closure (not percutaneous) is required for primum ASDs, sinus venosus defects, and coronary sinus septal defects; a sinus venosus defect is not actually a defect of the atrial septum but a communication near the SVC- or IVC-atrial junction with right pulmonary veins (Harrison 22e p.2070-2071).","VSDs are the most common congenital anomaly recognized at birth but account for only ~10% of adult CHD, owing to the high rate of spontaneous closure of small VSDs in early life; the most common location is the membranous (perimembranous/outlet) septum (Harrison 22e p.2071-2072).","Outcome for adults with small VSDs WITHOUT ventricular dilation or pulmonary hypertension is generally excellent; small muscular defects persisting into adulthood are often pressure- and flow-restricted with no significant hemodynamic consequence (Harrison 22e p.2072).","Tetralogy of Fallot is the most common form of cyanotic CHD (~0.5 per 1000 live births) and comprises anterior conal-septal deviation causing RV outflow tract obstruction, a VSD, RV hypertrophy, and an overriding aorta (Harrison 22e p.2073).","In repaired tetralogy of Fallot, pulmonary regurgitation is common and is usually associated with RV dilation; accurate quantification of RV size, function, and mass is particularly important because RV dilation, dysfunction, and hypertrophy are associated with adverse outcomes, and cardiac MRI is routinely used for surveillance (Harrison 22e p.2073-2074).","Eisenmenger syndrome is the consequence of a long-standing high-volume or pressurized left-to-right shunt: excessive pulmonary blood flow leads to severely increased pulmonary vascular resistance that eventually REVERSES the shunt, creating bidirectional or right-to-left flow and cyanosis; it can complicate any CHD with an initial left-to-right shunt (Harrison 22e p.2078).","Selective pulmonary vasodilators such as bosentan or sildenafil are efficacious in Eisenmenger syndrome; select patients may be candidates for combined heart-lung transplantation, or preferably lung transplantation with concomitant repair of the intracardiac defect when feasible (Harrison 22e p.2078).","Endocarditis prophylaxis before dental procedures is advised in high-risk CHD lesions: unrepaired cyanotic CHD (including palliative shunts/conduits), completely repaired defects during the first 6 months after repair, and repaired CHD with residual defects adjacent to prosthetic material (Harrison 22e p.745, Table 133-9)."],"pathophysiology":"The normal heart is analyzed segmentally (atria, ventricles, great arteries joined by the atrioventricular canal and conus), with attention to alignment (what drains into what) and connection (how segments are physically linked). The heart forms from the third week of gestation and is nearly complete by 8 weeks; cardiac neural crest cells migrating in weeks 5-6 are essential for outflow septation, semilunar valve formation, and aortic arch patterning, so disturbance of these processes underlies conotruncal defects. Shunt lesions are defined by the relative compliance, pressure, and outflow resistance of the connected chambers/vessels and the defect size. Left-to-right shunts (ASD, VSD, PDA) move oxygenated blood toward the deoxygenated (right) side, volume-loading the pulmonary circulation: ASD/PAPVR (proximal to the tricuspid valve) cause right heart dilation, whereas VSD/PDA (below the tricuspid valve) cause left heart dilation. Sustained high-volume or pressurized left-to-right shunting can raise pulmonary vascular resistance to the point that the shunt reverses (Eisenmenger physiology, right-to-left, cyanosis). Tetralogy of Fallot produces a right-to-left shunt across the VSD because RV outflow obstruction raises right-sided systolic pressure, mixing deoxygenated blood into the LV and causing systemic arterial cyanosis. In transposition of the great arteries the aorta arises from the RV and the pulmonary artery from the LV, creating parallel rather than series circulations that are incompatible with life unless mixing exists (ASD, VSD, or PDA).","additionalDifferentials":["Atrial septal defect (secundum, primum, sinus venosus, coronary sinus types) — most common cause of right heart dilation from a shunt","Patent foramen ovale (distinct from ASD; not associated with right heart dilation)","Ventricular septal defect (membranous/perimembranous, muscular, AV-canal/inlet, subpulmonary/conal types)","Patent ductus arteriosus","Partial anomalous pulmonary venous return","Ebstein anomaly of the tricuspid valve","Tetralogy of Fallot (most common cyanotic CHD)","Transposition of the great arteries (D-loop and congenitally corrected L-loop)","Coarctation of the aorta (with associated bicuspid aortic valve)","Single ventricle physiology (tricuspid atresia, double inlet LV, hypoplastic left heart) post-Fontan","Eisenmenger syndrome (any unrepaired left-to-right shunt that has reversed)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Atrial septal defect may be mistaken for mitral stenosis; features favoring ASD over MS include a pulmonary mid-to-upper left sternal border flow murmur, and large left-to-right shunts can produce findings overlapping with MS (Harrison 22e p.2065 / Chap. on MS).","Arrhythmogenic RV cardiomyopathy and Uhl's anomaly are non-shunt myocardial causes of right heart dilation that can mimic the dilated right heart of an ASD."],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic and transesophageal echocardiography with agitated saline (bubble) contrast — needed to diagnose PFO and non-secundum ASDs; color flow Doppler for VSDs and shunt-ratio estimation","Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging — routine surveillance of RV size, function, and mass in repaired tetralogy of Fallot and other complex CHD; cross-sectional imaging detects PAPVR","Surface ECG — incomplete right bundle branch block in ASD; QRS duration >=180 ms in repaired TOF flags risk of ventricular tachycardia/sudden death; ventricular pre-excitation (WPW) in up to 20% of Ebstein anomaly","Cardiac catheterization — shunt quantification and hemodynamic assessment, particularly when pulmonary hypertension is suspected","Genetic evaluation — testing for 22q11 deletion (DiGeorge) in conotruncal defects (clues: right aortic arch, aberrant subclavian artery); recognition of trisomy 21, Noonan (PTPN11), Williams (7q11.23), and Turner syndromes","Fetal echocardiogram between 18 and 22 weeks of gestation for pregnant patients with CHD"],"redFlags":["Reversal of a left-to-right shunt to right-to-left flow with cyanosis = Eisenmenger syndrome; develops from severely elevated pulmonary vascular resistance and is a multi-organ condition","Right heart dilation in an unrepaired ASD without other cause signals risk of progression to symptomatic right heart failure, atrial arrhythmias, and pulmonary arterial hypertension","QRS duration >=180 ms on resting ECG in repaired tetralogy of Fallot — associated with increased risk of ventricular tachycardia and sudden cardiac death","Systemic RV dysfunction after atrial switch (Mustard/Senning) for D-loop TGA — the most worrisome long-term complication, with no proven effective medical therapy","Pregnancy in a woman with Eisenmenger syndrome is contraindicated due to high risk of maternal mortality; pulmonary arterial hypertension and severe systemic ventricular dysfunction are WHO class IV (extremely high maternal risk)","New exercise-capacity decline in ACHD may be unrecognized by the patient; by the time symptoms appear, irreversible physiologic changes may already have occurred"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary widely by lesion. Adults with small VSDs without ventricular dilation or pulmonary hypertension generally have an excellent prognosis. Repaired coarctation patients generally do well but remain at risk for systemic hypertension, premature atherosclerosis, LV failure, and aortic aneurysm/dissection and recoarctation. In repaired tetralogy of Fallot, one prospective cohort of 144 adults showed 72% survival at 40 years but only 25% cumulative event-free survival (events: reintervention, most commonly pulmonary valve replacement; symptomatic arrhythmia; heart failure); LV dysfunction is present in at least 20%. The arterial switch operation for D-loop TGA has produced substantial long-term survival, whereas atrial switch leaves a systemic RV prone to dysfunction. Adults with Eisenmenger syndrome have significant morbidity but generally survive longer than patients with other forms of pulmonary arterial hypertension. In aggregate, adults with CHD have quality-of-life-limiting comorbidities and premature mortality far exceeding age-matched controls, and nearly all can be classified as stage A heart failure or greater; care within a center that has an ACHD specialty program is associated with improved overall survival.","pitfalls":["Confusing a PFO (flap-valve patency, present in up to 25% of adults, no right heart dilation) with a true secundum ASD.","Assuming all ASDs are amenable to percutaneous closure — large defects, inadequate tissue rims, and anomalous draining pulmonary veins make device closure unfavorable, and primum/sinus venosus/coronary sinus defects require surgery.","Overlooking that a sinus venosus defect is not in the atrial septum itself, so it may be missed without dedicated imaging.","Routine phlebotomy in an asymptomatic adult with Eisenmenger syndrome is contraindicated; elevated hematocrit appropriate to the degree of cyanosis should not be lowered reflexively, and iron deficiency should be corrected because it impairs quality of life and function.","Using teratogenic medications (ACE inhibitors, ARBs, direct oral anticoagulants, endothelin receptor blockers) in CHD patients of childbearing potential; estrogen-containing contraception should be avoided in Eisenmenger syndrome due to thrombogenicity.","Failing to test adults with conotruncal defects for 22q11 deletion (DiGeorge) — many were never tested, and unrecognized cognitive/psychiatric disability may go untreated.","Treating an ACHD patient's seemingly minor lesion without recognizing limited hemodynamic reserve and subclinical multi-organ dysfunction before noncardiac surgery."],"severityClassification":"The chapter applies the modified World Health Organization (mWHO) classification of maternal risk in heart disease during pregnancy, graded mWHO I (no detectable increased mortality risk, e.g., small/mild lesions) through mWHO IV (extremely high maternal mortality risk; pregnancy generally contraindicated). Examples by class: mWHO II includes unoperated ASD/VSD and repaired tetralogy of Fallot; mWHO III includes mechanical valve, systemic RV, and Fontan circulation; mWHO IV includes pulmonary arterial hypertension, severe systemic ventricular dysfunction (EF <30% or NYHA III-IV), severe aortic dilatation, and Fontan with any complication. The CARPREG II weighted-risk score is also described (highest 3-point factors: prior cardiac event/arrhythmia, NYHA >=III, mechanical valve), stratifying predicted cardiac-event risk from 5% (<=1 point) to 41% (>4 points). For repaired tetralogy of Fallot, potential sequelae are catalogued in Table 280-3 and long-term sequelae of D-loop TGA surgery in Table 280-4.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2067"],"crossLinks":["pulmonary_hypertension"]},"congenital_hypothyroidism":{"name":"Congenital hypothyroidism","system":"Endocrinology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CH","neonatal hypothyroidism","cretinism","congenital thyroid deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["One of the most common preventable causes of intellectual disability, resulting from inadequate thyroid hormone at birth or in early infancy.","Most affected newborns appear normal at birth because of transplacental maternal thyroid hormone, so clinical detection is unreliable and newborn screening is essential.","The commonest cause worldwide is thyroid dysgenesis (aplasia, hypoplasia, or an ectopic gland); dyshormonogenesis and, in some regions, iodine deficiency also contribute.","Later clinical features include prolonged jaundice, poor feeding, lethargy, constipation, a large fontanelle, macroglossia, umbilical hernia, hypotonia, and hoarse cry.","Screening measures TSH and/or T4 on a heel-prick sample; a raised TSH with low T4 confirms primary hypothyroidism.","Treatment is early thyroid hormone replacement with levothyroxine, and the key to normal neurodevelopment is starting therapy as early as possible.","Regular monitoring of growth, development, and thyroid function is needed to keep levels in the target range during infancy.","Delay in starting treatment is associated with irreversible loss of cognitive potential, making prompt recognition critical."],"pathophysiology":"Thyroid hormone is essential for normal brain growth and myelination, particularly in the first years of life. In congenital hypothyroidism, deficient hormone production results from failure of thyroid gland development, an enzymatic defect in hormone synthesis (dyshormonogenesis), inadequate iodine substrate, or, less commonly, hypothalamic-pituitary (central) dysfunction. In primary disease, low circulating thyroid hormone removes negative feedback on the pituitary, raising TSH. Because maternal thyroid hormone crosses the placenta, the fetus is partly protected in utero, but after birth the untreated infant becomes progressively hypothyroid, and prolonged deficiency during the critical window of brain development produces permanent neurological injury.","additionalDifferentials":["Transient neonatal hypothyroidism (e.g. maternal antithyroid drugs, iodine exposure, maternal antibodies)","Central (secondary/tertiary) hypothyroidism","Other causes of prolonged neonatal jaundice","Down syndrome and other conditions with hypotonia and feeding difficulty","Constitutional causes of poor feeding and lethargy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Newborn screening TSH and/or T4 on heel-prick blood","Confirmatory serum free T4 and TSH","Thyroid imaging (ultrasound and/or radionuclide scan) to define aetiology","Maternal thyroid history and antibody status where relevant","Knee radiograph for bone age in some cases to assess in utero severity"],"redFlags":["Positive newborn screen requiring urgent confirmation and treatment","Signs of significant hypothyroidism (marked lethargy, poor feeding, hypothermia)","Prolonged unexplained neonatal jaundice","Delayed diagnosis beyond the early weeks of life, risking neurodevelopmental harm"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis through screening and prompt, adequately dosed replacement, neurodevelopmental outcome is typically normal; delayed or inadequate treatment risks permanent intellectual impairment.","pitfalls":["Relying on clinical signs, which are usually absent or subtle in the newborn period","Delaying treatment while awaiting further tests once screening is clearly abnormal","Under- or over-treating due to infrequent monitoring during infancy","Missing central hypothyroidism when screening is TSH-based only"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"congenital_pneumonia":{"name":"Congenital Pneumonia","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neonatal pneumonia","intrauterine pneumonia","early-onset neonatal pneumonia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pneumonia acquired before or during birth typically presents as respiratory distress in the first hours to days of life and often forms part of early-onset neonatal sepsis.","Infection may be acquired transplacentally, by ascending spread from the genital tract (especially with prolonged rupture of membranes or chorioamnionitis), or during passage through the birth canal.","Group B streptococcus and Escherichia coli are leading bacterial causes; other organisms include Listeria, and congenital infections such as certain viruses can also involve the lungs.","Clinical features overlap with other causes of neonatal respiratory distress, so it is often difficult to distinguish pneumonia from surfactant deficiency or transient tachypnoea on presentation.","Maternal and perinatal risk factors, particularly chorioamnionitis, prolonged membrane rupture, and maternal group B streptococcus colonisation, raise the likelihood and guide management.","Chest radiographs may show diffuse or patchy opacities, but findings can mimic respiratory distress syndrome, so imaging is supportive rather than definitive.","Because differentiating infection from non-infectious causes is difficult, empirical antibiotics covering likely organisms are usually started while cultures and the clinical course are followed.","Supportive care to maintain oxygenation, ventilation, temperature, and perfusion is essential alongside antimicrobial therapy."],"pathophysiology":"Organisms reach the fetal or newborn lung by several routes: transplacental spread during maternal bloodstream infection, ascending colonisation of amniotic fluid after membrane rupture or with chorioamnionitis, and aspiration of infected amniotic fluid or genital secretions around delivery. Once in the alveoli, pathogens trigger an inflammatory response with exudate, impaired gas exchange, and interference with surfactant function. In early-onset disease this frequently coexists with bacteraemia and systemic sepsis, so lung involvement is often one component of a generalised infection in the vulnerable newborn.","additionalDifferentials":["Respiratory distress syndrome (surfactant deficiency)","Transient tachypnoea of the newborn","Meconium aspiration syndrome","Early-onset sepsis without pneumonia","Congenital heart disease","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph","Blood culture and full blood count with differential","Inflammatory markers such as C-reactive protein","Pulse oximetry and blood gas","Lumbar puncture when sepsis or meningitis is suspected","Maternal history and cultures (group B streptococcus status, chorioamnionitis)"],"redFlags":["Severe or worsening respiratory distress and rising oxygen requirement","Signs of septic shock or poor perfusion","Apnoea","Persistent pulmonary hypertension with labile oxygenation","Deterioration despite initial supportive measures"],"prognosis":"With prompt recognition and appropriate antibiotics and supportive care many infants recover well, but outcome depends on the organism, gestational age, and severity; fulminant early-onset sepsis with pneumonia, particularly from group B streptococcus, can be life-threatening.","pitfalls":["Attributing respiratory distress solely to surfactant deficiency and delaying antibiotics in an at-risk infant","Underweighting maternal risk factors such as chorioamnionitis or prolonged membrane rupture","Relying on chest radiograph appearance to exclude infection given its overlap with other conditions","Failing to evaluate for accompanying bacteraemia or meningitis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"conjunctivitis":{"name":"Conjunctivitis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pink eye","red eye","bacterial conjunctivitis","viral conjunctivitis","allergic conjunctivitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with a red, gritty, or irritable eye with discharge, but crucially with normal vision, a clear cornea, normal pupil, and no significant pain — features that distinguish it from serious causes of the red eye","Bacterial conjunctivitis produces purulent, sticky discharge with lids stuck together in the morning; it is usually self-limiting","Viral (often adenoviral) conjunctivitis gives watery discharge, follicles, a preauricular node, and is highly contagious, frequently following an upper respiratory infection","Allergic conjunctivitis is bilateral and itchy with watery discharge, chemosis, and papillae, often with a personal history of atopy","Redness is diffuse and superficial, sparing the circumcorneal zone seen in keratitis and uveitis","Most cases are managed supportively with hygiene measures; topical antibiotics are used for bacterial cases and antihistamine/mast-cell stabiliser drops for allergic disease","Hyperacute, profuse purulent discharge suggests gonococcal infection and is an emergency because it can rapidly perforate the cornea"],"pathophysiology":"Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the conjunctiva, the mucous membrane lining the eyelids and covering the sclera. It arises from infection (bacterial or viral), allergy, or irritation. Inflammatory mediators dilate conjunctival vessels (causing redness) and increase vascular permeability (causing chemosis and discharge), while the cellular response produces follicles in viral and chlamydial disease or papillae in bacterial and allergic disease. Because the process is confined to the conjunctiva and spares the cornea, anterior chamber, and intraocular structures, vision and pupil responses remain normal in uncomplicated cases.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute anterior uveitis","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Bacterial keratitis or corneal ulcer","Scleritis and episcleritis","Subconjunctival haemorrhage","Dry eye and blepharitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination assessing discharge type, follicles/papillae, and preauricular nodes","Visual acuity and fluorescein staining to exclude corneal involvement","Conjunctival swabs for culture in severe, hyperacute, neonatal, or non-resolving cases","Testing for chlamydia and gonorrhoea in suspected sexually transmitted or neonatal conjunctivitis","Slit-lamp examination to exclude keratitis, uveitis, and angle closure"],"redFlags":["Reduced vision, significant pain, or photophobia — points away from simple conjunctivitis to a sight-threatening cause","Circumcorneal injection, a hazy cornea, or an abnormal pupil suggesting keratitis, uveitis, or angle closure","Hyperacute profuse purulent discharge (possible gonococcal infection) risking rapid corneal perforation","Neonatal conjunctivitis (ophthalmia neonatorum), which requires urgent evaluation for chlamydia and gonococcus"],"prognosis":"Most bacterial, viral, and allergic conjunctivitis is self-limiting or responds readily to treatment with an excellent visual prognosis; serious outcomes occur mainly with gonococcal, chlamydial, or misdiagnosed sight-threatening mimics.","pitfalls":["Labelling any red eye as conjunctivitis and missing keratitis, uveitis, or angle-closure glaucoma","Prescribing topical steroids inappropriately and worsening an undiagnosed herpetic keratitis","Overlooking contact lens wear, which raises the risk of sight-threatening keratitis","Failing to recognise hyperacute gonococcal or neonatal conjunctivitis as emergencies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"contact_dermatitis":{"name":"Contact dermatitis","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["contact eczema","allergic contact dermatitis","irritant contact dermatitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Distinguish irritant contact dermatitis (direct chemical or physical damage, no prior sensitisation, often stinging or burning) from allergic contact dermatitis (a delayed hypersensitivity reaction that is typically itchy)","The distribution of the rash is the key clue to the culprit; sharply demarcated eruptions localised to sites of contact point to an external cause","Common irritants include water, soaps, detergents, and solvents, while frequent allergens include nickel, fragrances, preservatives, rubber accelerators, and topical medicaments","Acute lesions are erythematous, oedematous, and vesicular; chronic exposure produces dryness, scaling, fissuring, and lichenification","Patch testing is the definitive method to identify a contact allergen, whereas irritant dermatitis is largely a clinical diagnosis","Cornerstone management is identification and avoidance of the offending agent alongside barrier restoration with emollients and, for flares, topical corticosteroids","Occupational exposure is a common driver (e.g. hairdressers, healthcare and construction workers), so take a detailed work and hobby history"],"pathophysiology":"Contact dermatitis has two principal mechanisms. Irritant contact dermatitis results from direct, non-immunological damage to the epidermal barrier by chemical or physical agents, triggering innate inflammation; severity relates to the concentration and duration of exposure. Allergic contact dermatitis is a type IV, T-cell-mediated delayed hypersensitivity reaction: after an initial sensitisation phase in which small-molecule haptens bind skin proteins and prime allergen-specific T cells, re-exposure elicits an inflammatory eczematous response over one to several days. Repeated insult impairs the barrier further, perpetuating inflammation and, over time, chronic lichenified changes.","additionalDifferentials":["Atopic dermatitis","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Nummular (discoid) eczema","Tinea (dermatophyte infection)","Psoriasis on the hands or feet","Cellulitis when acute unilateral limb involvement mimics infection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Patch testing to identify causative allergens in suspected allergic contact dermatitis","Detailed occupational, cosmetic, and hobby exposure history","Skin swabs if secondary bacterial infection is suspected","Skin scrapings or mycology to exclude dermatophyte infection","Skin biopsy in atypical or diagnostically uncertain cases"],"redFlags":["Widespread or erythrodermic involvement with systemic upset","Rapidly spreading eruption with fever suggesting secondary infection or an alternative diagnosis","Airborne or facial involvement with eyelid swelling affecting vision","Occupational disease threatening ability to work and requiring formal assessment"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent when the causative agent is identified and avoided; persistent exposure, especially occupational, leads to chronic relapsing dermatitis and may necessitate a change of work.","pitfalls":["Failing to perform patch testing and thus missing a treatable allergen","Overlooking a relevant occupational or cosmetic exposure in the history","Assuming persistent hand eczema is purely irritant when a superimposed allergy exists","Applying a topical product that itself contains a sensitising allergen and perpetuates the dermatitis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"contrast_induced_nephropathy":{"name":"Contrast-induced nephropathy","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CIN","contrast-associated acute kidney injury","CA-AKI","contrast nephropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Contrast-induced nephropathy is an acute, usually reversible decline in renal function occurring within a few days of intravascular iodinated contrast, once other causes have been excluded","The rise in creatinine typically begins within one to three days and peaks around three to five days, with recovery over one to two weeks in most cases","Pre-existing chronic kidney disease is the strongest risk factor, and the risk rises steeply when diabetic nephropathy is also present","Volume depletion, heart failure, higher contrast volumes, and concurrent nephrotoxins compound the risk","The most effective preventive measure is adequate intravenous volume expansion with isotonic fluid before and after contrast exposure","Non-essential nephrotoxic drugs should be withheld around the time of the study and the smallest necessary volume of contrast used","The true incidence of contrast-related injury after intravenous contrast in modern practice is lower than once believed, and the need for imaging should be weighed against a frequently modest risk"],"pathophysiology":"Contrast-induced nephropathy is thought to arise from a combination of renal medullary ischaemia and direct tubular toxicity. Iodinated contrast alters intrarenal haemodynamics, causing vasoconstriction and reduced medullary blood flow, which is particularly damaging to the metabolically active outer medulla. Contrast media also generate reactive oxygen species and exert direct toxic effects on tubular epithelial cells, and their osmotic and viscosity properties can impair tubular flow. The result is a predominantly acute tubular injury pattern. Patients with reduced baseline renal reserve, impaired autoregulation, or reduced effective circulating volume are least able to compensate for these insults, which explains the clustering of risk in chronic kidney disease, diabetes, and volume depletion.","additionalDifferentials":["Ischaemic acute tubular necrosis from hypotension or the underlying illness","Atheroembolic (cholesterol embolisation) renal disease after arterial procedures","Prerenal azotaemia from volume depletion","Other nephrotoxic drug injury","Obstructive uropathy","Progression of underlying chronic kidney disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Baseline and serial serum creatinine with estimated GFR before and after contrast exposure","Urinalysis and urine microscopy to help distinguish tubular injury from other causes","Assessment of volume status and review of the medication list for other nephrotoxins","Consideration of atheroembolic disease (eosinophilia, skin changes) after arterial catheterisation","Renal ultrasound if obstruction or another structural cause is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive or non-recovering renal function suggesting an alternative or additional cause","Oliguria or features of established acute kidney injury requiring supportive care","Signs of atheroembolism such as livedo, digital ischaemia, and eosinophilia after arterial procedures","Hyperkalaemia, acidosis, or fluid overload indicating a need for closer monitoring or dialysis"],"prognosis":"Most episodes are mild and self-limiting, with renal function returning toward baseline within one to two weeks. However, an episode is associated with longer hospital stay, higher risk of needing dialysis in susceptible patients, and worse long-term cardiovascular and renal outcomes, so prevention in high-risk individuals remains important.","pitfalls":["Withholding a clinically necessary contrast study out of exaggerated fear of nephropathy in a low-risk patient","Failing to provide adequate periprocedural volume expansion in high-risk patients","Attributing all post-contrast creatinine rises to contrast without considering atheroembolism or ischaemic injury","Overlooking continued exposure to other nephrotoxic drugs during the vulnerable period"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"cor_pulmonale":{"name":"Cor pulmonale","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cor pulmonale","pulmonary heart disease","right heart failure from lung disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Cor pulmonale is right ventricular enlargement and eventual failure caused by disorders of the lungs, pulmonary vasculature, or ventilation, excluding left heart disease and congenital defects","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is the most common underlying cause, with pulmonary hypertension the central intermediary","Symptoms include exertional dyspnoea, fatigue, and, as it progresses, ankle swelling and abdominal distension","Signs of right heart failure include raised jugular venous pressure, a left parasternal heave, a loud pulmonary component of the second heart sound, hepatomegaly, and peripheral oedema","Echocardiography helps assess right ventricular size and function and estimate pulmonary artery pressure, while right heart catheterisation confirms pulmonary hypertension","Treatment is directed primarily at the underlying lung disease and at correcting hypoxaemia, since chronic hypoxia drives pulmonary vasoconstriction","Long-term oxygen therapy in chronically hypoxaemic patients can reduce pulmonary vascular resistance and improve survival","Diuretics can relieve congestion but require caution to avoid reducing preload excessively or worsening metabolic alkalosis"],"pathophysiology":"Chronic lung disease or pulmonary vascular disease raises pulmonary vascular resistance through several mechanisms: alveolar hypoxia causes pulmonary arteriolar vasoconstriction, destruction or remodelling of the pulmonary vascular bed reduces its cross-sectional area, and acidosis and polycythaemia further increase resistance. The right ventricle, facing sustained pressure overload, initially hypertrophies to maintain output but ultimately dilates and fails. Rising right-sided filling pressures produce systemic venous congestion, hepatic congestion, and peripheral oedema.","additionalDifferentials":["Left ventricular failure causing pulmonary congestion","Primary (idiopathic) pulmonary arterial hypertension","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension","Valvular heart disease","Constrictive pericarditis","Right heart failure from congenital heart disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography to assess right ventricular size and function and estimate pulmonary pressures","Arterial blood gas analysis to detect hypoxaemia and hypercapnia","Electrocardiography for signs of right ventricular strain and right axis deviation","Chest imaging and pulmonary function testing to characterise the underlying lung disease","Right heart catheterisation to confirm and quantify pulmonary hypertension when indicated"],"redFlags":["Rapidly worsening right heart failure with hypotension or syncope","Severe hypoxaemia or hypercapnic respiratory failure","Signs suggesting acute or chronic pulmonary embolism as a cause","Refractory fluid overload despite therapy"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is largely determined by the severity and reversibility of the underlying pulmonary disorder; the onset of overt right heart failure generally marks advanced disease and portends a poorer outlook, though correcting hypoxaemia and optimising the underlying condition can improve symptoms and survival.","pitfalls":["Attributing right heart failure to lung disease without excluding left heart disease or thromboembolism","Failing to correct hypoxaemia, a key reversible driver of pulmonary hypertension","Over-diuresis reducing right ventricular preload and cardiac output","Overlooking treatable causes such as chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"corneal_abrasion":{"name":"Corneal abrasion","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["corneal epithelial defect","scratched cornea"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with sudden severe pain, foreign body sensation, tearing, photophobia, and blepharospasm, usually after minor trauma such as a fingernail, branch, or contact lens","Fluorescein staining under cobalt blue light reveals the epithelial defect as a bright green area","Always evert the upper lid and inspect for a retained subtarsal foreign body, especially with vertical linear (ice-rink) abrasions","Topical anaesthetic aids examination but must never be prescribed for ongoing use as it delays healing and masks damage","Small uncomplicated abrasions heal within one to two days; treatment is supportive with lubrication and topical antibiotic prophylaxis","Contact lens-related abrasions and those from vegetable or organic matter carry higher infection risk and warrant closer follow-up","Patching is generally avoided, particularly in contact lens wearers, as it may promote infection"],"pathophysiology":"A corneal abrasion is a defect in the corneal epithelium, most often from mechanical trauma. Loss of the epithelial barrier exposes richly innervated subepithelial nerve endings, producing intense pain and reflex tearing and blepharospasm. The epithelium normally regenerates rapidly by sliding and mitosis of surrounding cells. Contamination, retained foreign material, or contact lens wear can convert a simple abrasion into a microbial keratitis, and repeated or poorly healing defects may reflect an underlying epithelial basement membrane dystrophy causing recurrent erosions.","additionalDifferentials":["Microbial (bacterial) keratitis","Herpes simplex dendritic keratitis","Recurrent corneal erosion syndrome","Retained corneal or subtarsal foreign body","Ultraviolet (arc eye) photokeratitis","Corneal ulcer"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fluorescein staining to delineate the epithelial defect","Upper lid eversion to exclude a retained subtarsal foreign body","Slit-lamp examination to assess depth and look for infiltrate suggesting infection","Contact lens history and, if infiltrate present, corneal scrape for microbiology"],"redFlags":["A white corneal infiltrate, mucopurulent discharge, or anterior chamber reaction suggesting microbial keratitis","Contact lens wearers with pain out of proportion, indicating possible Pseudomonas or Acanthamoeba infection","Failure to heal within expected time or worsening pain and redness","Suspicion of penetrating injury or high-velocity foreign body"],"prognosis":"Simple abrasions heal quickly with no lasting visual effect, but infection, deep central lesions, or recurrent erosions can cause scarring and reduced vision.","pitfalls":["Sending patients home with topical anaesthetic, which delays healing and can cause severe corneal damage","Failing to evert the lid and missing a retained foreign body","Patching a contact lens wearer and precipitating infection","Assuming all painful epithelial defects are simple abrasions and missing herpetic or microbial keratitis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"corrosive_ingestion":{"name":"Corrosive ingestion","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["caustic ingestion","acid ingestion","alkali ingestion","corrosive poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Alkalis typically cause liquefactive necrosis with deep, penetrating oesophageal injury, whereas acids cause coagulative necrosis that more often damages the stomach","The absence of visible oral or oropharyngeal burns does not exclude significant oesophageal or gastric injury","Early features include drooling, odynophagia, dysphagia, retrosternal or epigastric pain, and stridor if the airway is involved","Do not induce emesis, give neutralising agents, or attempt gastric lavage, as these can worsen tissue injury and increase the risk of perforation and aspiration","Activated charcoal is not useful and obscures endoscopic assessment, so it should be avoided","Early upper GI endoscopy (usually within the first day, once the patient is stable) is used to grade injury depth and guide management and prognosis","Immediate airway assessment is critical, as laryngeal oedema can cause rapid airway compromise requiring early intubation or a surgical airway","Late complications include oesophageal and gastric outlet strictures and an increased long-term risk of oesophageal carcinoma"],"pathophysiology":"Corrosive agents produce tissue destruction on contact. Strong alkalis (e.g. lye, drain cleaner) cause liquefactive necrosis, saponifying fats and dissolving proteins, allowing deep penetration and full-thickness oesophageal injury with risk of perforation and mediastinitis. Strong acids cause coagulative necrosis with eschar formation that can limit depth of penetration but tends to pool in and damage the stomach, risking gastric perforation and peritonitis. Injury severity depends on the agent, concentration, volume, and contact time. Healing of deep circumferential burns leads to fibrosis and stricture, and chronic injury raises long-term malignancy risk.","additionalDifferentials":["Foreign body ingestion","Thermal or scald injury to the upper aerodigestive tract","Acute epiglottitis or other airway infection","Peptic ulcer disease or spontaneous oesophageal perforation","Pill oesophagitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Early upper GI endoscopy to grade mucosal injury","Erect chest and abdominal imaging to detect free air or mediastinal air","CT of the chest and abdomen to assess transmural injury and perforation","Arterial blood gas and lactate as markers of tissue necrosis","Full blood count, electrolytes, and group and save in case surgery is needed"],"redFlags":["Stridor, hoarseness, or respiratory distress indicating airway involvement","Severe chest, back, or abdominal pain suggesting oesophageal or gastric perforation","Signs of mediastinitis or peritonitis","Haemodynamic instability, metabolic acidosis, or rising lactate","Subcutaneous emphysema or free air on imaging"],"prognosis":"Minor mucosal injury usually heals well, but deep circumferential burns commonly progress to strictures needing repeated dilatation or surgery, and full-thickness injury with perforation carries high mortality; survivors have a lifelong increased risk of oesophageal cancer.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal mouth excludes serious oesophageal or gastric damage","Attempting to neutralise the corrosive or induce vomiting, which aggravates injury","Giving activated charcoal, which is ineffective and hinders endoscopy","Delaying airway management in a patient with evolving laryngeal oedema"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"corticobasal_degeneration":{"name":"Corticobasal degeneration","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CBD","corticobasal syndrome","CBS","corticobasal ganglionic degeneration"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A tauopathy presenting with markedly asymmetric parkinsonism combined with cortical signs, poorly responsive to levodopa","The alien limb phenomenon, in which a limb moves or interferes without volitional control, is a striking but not invariable feature","Cortical sensory loss, apraxia, and focal dystonia or myoclonus of the affected limb are characteristic","Rigidity and bradykinesia are typically one-sided at onset before spreading","Cognitive and language impairment, including a nonfluent aphasia, may dominate in some patients","The clinical picture (corticobasal syndrome) can be produced by several pathologies, so clinical and pathological diagnoses do not always match","MRI often shows asymmetric cortical atrophy, typically greatest in the frontoparietal regions opposite the affected side","Distinguishing features from Parkinson disease include marked asymmetry, cortical signs, and poor levodopa response"],"pathophysiology":"Corticobasal degeneration is a four-repeat tauopathy with neuronal and glial tau deposition, including astrocytic plaques, and ballooned achromatic neurons, affecting the cerebral cortex (especially frontoparietal) and the basal ganglia asymmetrically. Cortical involvement produces apraxia, cortical sensory loss, and the alien limb phenomenon, while basal ganglia and nigral degeneration cause the akinetic-rigid, dystonic parkinsonism. Because the corticobasal syndrome can arise from Alzheimer, progressive supranuclear palsy, or other pathologies, the underlying substrate is confirmed only at autopsy.","additionalDifferentials":["Parkinson disease","Progressive supranuclear palsy","Multiple system atrophy","Alzheimer disease (which can present as corticobasal syndrome)","Frontotemporal dementia and primary progressive aphasia","Focal structural lesions or vascular parkinsonism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain to demonstrate asymmetric frontoparietal atrophy and exclude structural lesions","Neuropsychological testing to characterise apraxia, cortical sensory loss, and language impairment","Trial of dopaminergic therapy to document limited benefit","Biomarkers for Alzheimer pathology when it is a plausible underlying cause","Functional imaging (e.g. FDG-PET) showing asymmetric hypometabolism in selected cases"],"redFlags":["Rapid cognitive decline affecting safety, decision-making, and capacity","Progressive dysphagia with aspiration risk","Severe apraxia or dystonia causing loss of limb function and falls","Features pointing to a potentially treatable alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The disorder is progressive and untreatable at its root, with worsening motor disability, apraxia, and cognition over several years; survival typically spans several years from diagnosis, with aspiration and immobility complications determining outcome.","pitfalls":["Labeling asymmetric parkinsonism as Parkinson disease and persisting with ineffective levodopa","Equating the corticobasal syndrome with corticobasal degeneration pathology, which often differs","Overlooking cortical signs such as apraxia and cortical sensory loss","Missing an underlying Alzheimer pathology that alters counselling and management","Attributing the alien limb or dystonia to a functional or psychiatric cause"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"covid19":{"name":"COVID-19 (SARS-CoV-2)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SARS-CoV-2","coronavirus disease 2019"],"chapter":205,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1539","clinicalPearls":["SARS-CoV-2 is an enveloped, positive-sense RNA betacoronavirus; its trimeric spike (S) protein is primed by the host protease TMPRSS2 and attaches via a receptor-binding domain to the human ACE2 receptor (p.1539).","Disease course varies widely: asymptomatic infection, mild disease, moderate disease, or severe disease needing hospitalization, oxygen, intensive care, and mechanical ventilation. Possibly about a third of those infected are asymptomatic yet can still transmit the virus (p.1540).","Onset of disease typically manifests within 4-5 days after exposure and nearly always within 14 days; common symptoms are cough, fever, myalgia, headache, dyspnea, sore throat, and GI symptoms (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea) (p.1541).","Sudden-onset dysgeusia and anosmia (loss of taste and smell) occur in a substantial number of cases and often resolve over weeks to months (p.1541).","Spread is primarily via respiratory droplets between persons in close proximity; airborne transmission by small particles may occur but airborne transmission over long distances is unlikely. Fomite (contaminated-surface) transmission occurs, so hand washing is advised (p.1540).","The basic reproduction number (R0) of SARS-CoV-2 has been estimated to peak around 6, substantially higher than seasonal influenza (typically 1-2); densely populated settings (prisons, cruise ships, nursing homes, airplanes, large indoor gatherings) facilitate high transmission, while outdoor transmission is less common (p.1540).","RNA-dependent RNA polymerases are error-prone, so variants arise frequently; the WHO classifies variants under monitoring (VUMs), of interest (VOIs), and of concern (VOCs). Rapid variant evolution affecting transmission, severity, antigenicity, therapy effectiveness, and diagnostic accuracy is a distinguishing feature of the pandemic (p.1540).","An excessive proinflammatory host response to infection likely contributes directly to pulmonary pathology and disease severity; later in the clinical course pathogenesis is driven more by tissue and systemic inflammation than by direct viral cytopathic effect (p.1541-1542).","Children have lower rates of severe disease than adults, and schools have not been considered a primary driver of population transmission (p.1540).","Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (MIS-C / PMIS) is associated with COVID-19: persistent fever, multi-organ involvement (GI, dermatologic, cardiac, renal, hematologic, neurologic), and elevated inflammatory markers; highest US risk is in Black and Latino children aged 3-12 years. A similar adult syndrome (MIS-A) may rarely occur (p.1540).","Long COVID is a chronic condition occurring after infection, present for at least 3 months, with a wide range of symptoms (respiratory, metabolic, neuropsychiatric, pain) that may improve, worsen, or be ongoing; affected people may have increased suicidal ideation and suicide risk (p.1541).","Vaccination's principal goal is preventing severe COVID-19 (critical illness, death, hospitalization, emergency/urgent-care visits); preliminary data also suggest vaccinated persons with COVID are less likely than unvaccinated persons to develop long COVID (p.1543)."],"pathophysiology":"SARS-CoV-2 (genus Betacoronavirus) is an enveloped positive-sense RNA virus with a ~30,000-nucleotide genome encoding four structural proteins (S spike, E envelope, M membrane, N nucleocapsid) plus a large polyprotein cleaved into 16 nonstructural proteins. The trimeric S protein is a type-1 fusion machine primed by the host transmembrane protease TMPRSS2 and mediates attachment via a receptor-binding domain (RBD) to the human angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (hACE2) receptor, allowing viral entry into respiratory epithelium. Infection spreads chiefly by respiratory droplets, with possible short-range airborne and fomite spread. Disease results from direct viral pathogenesis in tissues and the associated immune response. An excessive proinflammatory host response contributes directly to pulmonary pathology; early in infection viral load (peak titer) correlates with severity, whereas later disease is increasingly driven by tissue and systemic inflammatory responses (cytokine storm) rather than direct viral cytopathic effect. The hyperinflammatory and hypercoagulable state drives severe multi-organ complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Influenza and other viral lower respiratory tract infections (RSV, human metapneumovirus, parainfluenza, rhinovirus)","Other coronaviruses: SARS-CoV-1, MERS-CoV, and common-cold coronaviruses HCoV-OC43 and HCoV-HKU1","Community-acquired or health care-associated bacterial pneumonia","Any respiratory symptom occurring where SARS-CoV-2 is circulating should be considered a potential manifestation of COVID-19"],"infectionMimics":["Influenza","Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) infection","Human metapneumovirus and parainfluenza virus infections","Rhinovirus / common cold (including HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 coronaviruses)","SARS-CoV-1 and MERS-CoV infections","Bacterial community-acquired pneumonia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Molecular nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT), usually RT-PCR on nasopharyngeal swab or saliva - most sensitive; cannot be done at home and is processed in a reference laboratory","Antigen tests (rapid / at-home) detecting SARS-CoV-2 proteins - reliable but less accurate than molecular tests, especially in exposed asymptomatic persons","CBC: lymphopenia and thrombocytopenia","Inflammatory markers: elevated IL-6, TNF-alpha, ferritin, C-reactive protein","Elevated liver enzymes and lactate dehydrogenase","Elevated markers of acute kidney injury","Elevated D-dimer and prothrombin time (coagulopathy)","Elevated troponin and creatine phosphokinase (cardiac/muscle injury)","Chest radiograph: bilateral consolidation and ground-glass opacities, especially in lower lung regions (may be normal despite respiratory compromise)","Chest CT: ground-glass opacification +/- consolidation, pleural thickening, interlobular septal thickening, air bronchograms - interpretable as typical, indeterminate, or atypical; more sensitive than radiograph but should be used for management, not as a primary diagnostic tool","Lung ultrasound to detect lung abnormalities"],"redFlags":["Oxygen saturation <=94% on room air","Respiratory rate >30 breaths/min","PaO2/FiO2 <300 mmHg","Lung infiltrates occupying >50% of lung fields on chest radiograph","Moderate to severe dyspnea or cyanosis","Change in mental status (confusion, delirium) or new-onset mental status changes","Chest pain","Reduced urine output or anuria","Acute respiratory distress syndrome / respiratory failure requiring mechanical ventilation","Multiorgan failure or shock","Persistent fever with multiorgan disease and elevated inflammatory markers (excessive inflammatory response - high risk of fatal outcome)","Thromboembolic events (VTE/PE/DVT, arterial thrombosis, acute stroke, limb ischemia)"],"prognosis":"Symptomatic infection is usually an acute syndrome that resolves within about 2 weeks in roughly 80% of persons, especially after mild or moderate disease. Severe disease often needs several months for recovery. Advanced age is the principal risk factor for severe illness: >95% of deaths occur in persons older than 45 years and >80% in those older than 65; male sex carries higher risk (odds ratio ~1.8), and most who die have preexisting comorbidities. Risk rises markedly with elevated BMI (overweight, obesity, severe obesity). As of September 2024 the virus had caused >700 million cases and >7 million deaths worldwide. A subset progress to recurring or persisting symptoms (long COVID); those with severe acute pulmonary or cardiac injury may have persisting respiratory or cardiac impairment, and adverse long-term mental-health consequences are common.","pitfalls":["Antibiotics are NOT generally indicated, as bacterial infection is an uncommon complication of COVID-19; reserve empiric antibiotics for diagnostic uncertainty or proven secondary infection, and deescalate or stop if no evidence of bacterial infection.","Chest CT should NOT be used as a primary diagnostic tool for COVID-19; it is principally for medical management of respiratory disease.","COVID-19-specific therapy is NOT recommended for individuals with asymptomatic SARS-CoV-2 infection.","Antigen (rapid/at-home) tests are less accurate than molecular tests, especially when testing exposed persons without symptoms.","Chest radiographs may be normal despite respiratory compromise, so a normal film does not exclude significant disease.","Possible association between NSAID use and worse outcomes has been raised; acetaminophen is the preferred antipyretic where possible (knowledge point - doses omitted).","Antibody-based therapies and EUAs are repeatedly revoked as new variants escape their activity, so confirm current authorization status before relying on a monoclonal antibody or long-acting antibody product.","Effectiveness of masking in community or health settings has been difficult to assess (most studies observational and confounded), so evidence is uncertain rather than absent."],"severityClassification":"Spectrum: asymptomatic infection; mild disease (symptomatic, no pneumonia - most symptomatic cases); moderate disease; severe disease (pneumonia with dyspnea, radiographic involvement of more than half the lung, and/or hypoxia with oxygen saturation <=94%, typically requiring hospitalization and oxygen); critical disease (respiratory failure requiring mechanical ventilation, multiorgan failure, or shock - requires intensive care).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1539","Harrison 22e p.1540","Harrison 22e p.1541","Harrison 22e p.1542","Harrison 22e p.1543"],"crossLinks":["ards","SEVERE_CAP","pe"]},"cows_milk_protein_allergy":{"name":"Cow's Milk Protein Allergy","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CMPA","cow's milk protein intolerance","milk allergy in infants"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["This is an immune-mediated reaction to cow's milk proteins, distinct from lactose intolerance, which is a non-immune enzyme deficiency.","Reactions are broadly divided into IgE-mediated (rapid onset urticaria, angioedema, vomiting, wheeze, and rarely anaphylaxis) and non-IgE-mediated (delayed gastrointestinal symptoms such as vomiting, diarrhoea, blood or mucus in stool, and colic-like distress).","It is one of the most common food allergies in infancy and can occur in both formula-fed and exclusively breastfed infants exposed to milk protein through maternal diet.","Diagnosis is largely clinical, supported by a compatible history, a trial of milk protein elimination, and improvement followed by recurrence on reintroduction.","For IgE-mediated disease, specific IgE testing or skin prick testing can support the diagnosis, whereas these tests are unhelpful in non-IgE-mediated presentations.","Management centres on strict avoidance of cow's milk protein, using an appropriate hypoallergenic formula in formula-fed infants and maternal dietary exclusion in breastfed infants.","A supervised oral food challenge is the most reliable way to confirm or refute the diagnosis and to assess whether tolerance has developed.","Most children outgrow cow's milk protein allergy during early childhood, so periodic reassessment for acquired tolerance is important to avoid unnecessary long-term restriction."],"pathophysiology":"The disorder results from an abnormal immune response to proteins in cow's milk, principally caseins and whey proteins. In IgE-mediated allergy, sensitisation leads to specific IgE that, on re-exposure, triggers mast cell and basophil degranulation with rapid release of histamine and other mediators, producing immediate symptoms. In non-IgE-mediated allergy, cell-mediated and mixed immune mechanisms cause a more delayed inflammatory response in the gastrointestinal tract, resulting in mucosal inflammation, altered motility, and enteropathy. Because the offending proteins can pass into breast milk, exclusively breastfed infants can react to maternal dietary intake.","additionalDifferentials":["Lactose intolerance","Infantile colic","Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease","Infectious gastroenteritis","Other food protein-induced enteropathies or proctocolitis","Eosinophilic gastrointestinal disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Trial of dietary elimination and planned reintroduction","Specific IgE or skin prick testing (for IgE-mediated disease)","Supervised oral food challenge","Stool examination for blood if allergic proctocolitis suspected","Growth monitoring and, if needed, nutritional assessment"],"redFlags":["Anaphylaxis or significant respiratory compromise after milk exposure","Significant gastrointestinal bleeding or severe enteropathy","Faltering growth or failure to thrive","Severe dehydration or shock (as in food protein-induced enterocolitis reactions)","Signs suggesting an alternative serious diagnosis"],"prognosis":"The prognosis is generally favourable, with the majority of children achieving tolerance during the preschool years; IgE-mediated allergy, particularly with high specific IgE levels, may persist longer than non-IgE-mediated disease.","pitfalls":["Confusing cow's milk protein allergy with lactose intolerance and giving inappropriate dietary advice","Relying on IgE tests to diagnose non-IgE-mediated disease","Maintaining prolonged, unnecessary dietary restriction without reassessing for tolerance","Using inadequately hypoallergenic substitutes or overlooking nutritional adequacy of exclusion diets"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"craniosynostosis":{"name":"Craniosynostosis","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["premature suture fusion","scaphocephaly","plagiocephaly (synostotic)","syndromic craniosynostosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Craniosynostosis is premature fusion of one or more cranial sutures, restricting skull growth perpendicular to the fused suture and producing a characteristic head shape","Sagittal suture fusion gives a long, narrow head (scaphocephaly) and is the most common single-suture form","The head-shape abnormality is typically present at or soon after birth and progresses, unlike positional plagiocephaly which usually improves with repositioning","A palpable ridge along the affected suture and an abnormal skull contour are important clinical clues","Most single-suture cases are non-syndromic and isolated, but multi-suture or syndromic forms (such as Apert, Crouzon, or Pfeiffer) carry higher risks and associated anomalies","Multi-suture involvement can raise intracranial pressure and threaten vision and neurodevelopment, so fundoscopy and developmental monitoring are important","Cranial CT with three-dimensional reconstruction confirms the diagnosis and defines the sutures involved when clinical assessment is uncertain","Treatment is usually surgical remodelling to correct the deformity and allow normal brain growth, with timing and approach tailored to the sutures affected and the child's age"],"pathophysiology":"Craniosynostosis arises when one or more cranial sutures fuse prematurely, before the underlying brain has finished its rapid early growth. Because bone cannot expand across the fused suture, compensatory growth occurs at the remaining open sutures, distorting the skull into a predictable shape determined by which suture is involved. When several sutures fuse, the overall cranial volume may become inadequate for the growing brain, leading to raised intracranial pressure. Syndromic forms are often driven by identifiable genetic mutations affecting fibroblast growth factor signalling and are associated with midface and limb anomalies.","additionalDifferentials":["Positional (deformational) plagiocephaly","Microcephaly with a small but normally shaped skull","Normal cranial shape variants","Metabolic bone disease affecting the skull","Syndromic craniofacial disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination of head shape, suture ridging, and head circumference trend","Cranial CT with 3D reconstruction to confirm fused sutures and plan surgery","Fundoscopy to detect papilloedema suggesting raised intracranial pressure","Genetic assessment when a syndromic form or multiple sutures are involved","Developmental and ophthalmological surveillance"],"redFlags":["Multi-suture fusion with signs of raised intracranial pressure such as papilloedema","Progressive skull deformity with developmental delay or visual compromise","Features of a craniofacial syndrome with airway or midface concerns","Rapidly abnormal head growth or a bulging appearance suggesting elevated pressure"],"prognosis":"Isolated single-suture craniosynostosis generally has an excellent outcome after timely surgical correction; multi-suture and syndromic forms are more complex, carry a greater risk of raised intracranial pressure and neurodevelopmental problems, and often require staged, multidisciplinary management.","pitfalls":["Confusing positional plagiocephaly, which improves with repositioning, with true synostosis","Missing raised intracranial pressure in multi-suture disease by omitting fundoscopy","Underestimating associated anomalies and genetic implications in syndromic cases","Delaying referral so that the optimal surgical window is missed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"croup":{"name":"Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis)","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["laryngotracheobronchitis","laryngotracheitis","viral croup","acute laryngotracheobronchitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common viral illness of young children, typically 6 months to 3 years, most often caused by parainfluenza viruses, producing upper airway obstruction at the subglottis.","Characterised by a barking (seal-like) cough, inspiratory stridor, and hoarseness, usually preceded by coryza and worse at night.","Diagnosis is clinical; a frontal neck radiograph may show subglottic narrowing (the steeple sign) but is not required.","Corticosteroids are the mainstay and benefit essentially all severities, reducing symptoms and the need for further intervention.","Nebulised adrenaline (epinephrine) provides rapid, temporary relief in moderate to severe cases and mandates a period of observation for rebound.","Keeping the child calm and comfortable is important, as agitation and crying worsen airway obstruction.","Stridor at rest, marked recession, or hypoxaemia indicates significant obstruction requiring active treatment.","Consider alternative diagnoses when the presentation is atypical: sudden toxic appearance suggests epiglottitis or bacterial tracheitis, and drooling with dysphagia is a warning sign."],"pathophysiology":"Viral infection produces inflammation and oedema of the larynx, trachea, and subglottic tissues. Because the subglottis is the narrowest part of a child's airway and is encircled by rigid cricoid cartilage, even modest mucosal swelling markedly narrows the lumen, dramatically increasing airway resistance and producing turbulent airflow heard as inspiratory stridor and the characteristic barking cough. Increased work of breathing follows, and severe obstruction can cause hypoxaemia and fatigue.","additionalDifferentials":["Epiglottitis","Bacterial tracheitis","Foreign body aspiration","Retropharyngeal or peritonsillar abscess","Anaphylaxis / angioedema","Spasmodic (recurrent) croup"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical severity assessment (e.g. presence of stridor at rest and recession)","Pulse oximetry","Anteroposterior neck radiograph only if diagnosis is uncertain (steeple sign)","Avoid interventions that distress the child until the airway is secured in severe cases"],"redFlags":["Stridor at rest with marked chest wall recession","Cyanosis, hypoxaemia, or altered mental status / lethargy","Drooling, dysphagia, or a toxic-appearing child suggesting epiglottitis","Rapidly worsening obstruction or exhaustion despite treatment","Biphasic stridor indicating severe fixed obstruction"],"prognosis":"Most children have mild, self-limiting illness that resolves within a few days; severe airway compromise is uncommon and the need for intubation is rare with appropriate treatment.","pitfalls":["Distressing the child with unnecessary examination or procedures, worsening obstruction","Withholding corticosteroids in mild cases where they still provide benefit","Discharging a child after nebulised adrenaline without an adequate observation period for rebound","Failing to recognise epiglottitis or bacterial tracheitis presenting with a croup-like picture"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"crush_syndrome":{"name":"Crush syndrome","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["traumatic rhabdomyolysis","crush injury syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Anticipate systemic collapse when a compressed limb or torso is released, as reperfusion floods the circulation with potassium and other cell contents","Hyperkalaemia is the most immediately life-threatening consequence and can precipitate fatal arrhythmia at or shortly after extrication","Begin generous intravenous fluid resuscitation early, ideally before release of the crushed part, to protect the kidneys and dilute the metabolic load","Myoglobin released from damaged muscle causes pigment-induced acute kidney injury, often heralded by dark, tea-coloured urine","Monitor the electrocardiogram continuously for peaked T waves and other signs of hyperkalaemia during and after extrication","The crushed limb can develop compartment syndrome, so examine repeatedly for tense swelling and disproportionate pain","Correct hypovolaemia, acidosis, and electrolyte disturbance in parallel while maintaining a good urine output"],"pathophysiology":"Prolonged compression of muscle causes ischaemic and pressure necrosis (rhabdomyolysis). While compressed, the injury is largely contained, but on release reperfusion washes the accumulated intracellular contents into the systemic circulation. Potassium, myoglobin, phosphate, and organic acids surge, producing hyperkalaemia, metabolic acidosis, and hypocalcaemia. Myoglobin precipitates in the renal tubules, and combined with hypovolaemia from fluid sequestration into damaged muscle, causes acute kidney injury. Massive fluid shifts and the release of vasoactive and inflammatory mediators can cause shock, while swelling within fascial compartments produces compartment syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-traumatic rhabdomyolysis (prolonged immobility, drugs, seizures)","Acute kidney injury from other causes","Hyperkalaemia from renal failure or other aetiology","Compartment syndrome without systemic crush features","Hypovolaemic or haemorrhagic shock from associated trauma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum potassium and other electrolytes, including calcium and phosphate","Creatine kinase and myoglobin as markers of muscle breakdown","Renal function and urine output monitoring","Urinalysis showing pigment (dark urine positive for blood without red cells)","Electrocardiogram and continuous cardiac monitoring for hyperkalaemic changes"],"redFlags":["Hyperkalaemia with electrocardiographic changes threatening fatal arrhythmia around the time of extrication","Progressive acute kidney injury with falling urine output and dark urine","Signs of compartment syndrome in the crushed limb requiring urgent assessment","Metabolic acidosis and haemodynamic instability on reperfusion","Sudden cardiovascular collapse immediately after release of the crushed part"],"prognosis":"Early and aggressive fluid resuscitation with prevention and treatment of hyperkalaemia and pigment nephropathy improves outcome. Untreated crush syndrome carries high mortality from arrhythmia and renal failure, though renal function often recovers with timely supportive care and dialysis where needed.","pitfalls":["Delaying fluid resuscitation until after the limb is freed, when the potassium surge is already underway","Overlooking hyperkalaemia and failing to monitor the electrocardiogram during extrication","Missing evolving compartment syndrome in the injured limb","Under-resuscitating and allowing pigment nephropathy to progress","Attributing dark urine to visible blood rather than myoglobin"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"cryptococcosis":{"name":"Cryptococcosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Cryptococcus neoformans infection","cryptococcal meningitis"],"chapter":221,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1703-1706","clinicalPearls":["Caused by Cryptococcus, a genus of yeast-like fungi; WHO declared C. neoformans a critical priority pathogen in 2022 (p.1703).","C. neoformans and C. gattii are now regarded as species complexes; for clinical purposes both cause indistinguishable disease termed cryptococcosis (p.1703-1704).","High-risk hosts for C. neoformans: hematologic malignancy, solid-organ transplant, advanced liver disease, diabetes, glucocorticoid therapy, and advanced HIV with CD4+ counts <200/uL (p.1704).","C. gattii disease is NOT generally associated with specific immune deficits and often occurs in immunocompetent individuals; associated with autoantibodies to GM-CSF (p.1704).","Disease usually presents as pulmonary disease and/or chronic meningoencephalitis, but cryptococcosis can affect any tissue or organ (p.1704-1705).","Cryptococcal meningitis differs from bacterial meningitis: many patients present with symptoms of several weeks' duration and classic meningismus may be absent; indolent cases can present as subacute dementia or depression (p.1705).","In AIDS patients, cryptococcal meningitis is the initial AIDS-defining illness in ~2% and generally occurs with CD4+ counts <100/uL; about one-third also have pulmonary disease (p.1606).","Diagnosis requires demonstration of cryptococcal cells or antigen in normally sterile tissue; India ink of CSF shows encapsulated yeast and CRAg (serum/CSF) is both sensitive and specific with rapid point-of-care lateral flow assays (p.1705).","Increased intracranial pressure in cryptococcal meningoencephalitis is managed by repeated therapeutic lumbar punctures and shunt placement; mannitol and acetazolamide are NOT effective (p.1706).","Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) can occur after starting ART in HIV; recommended to start ART 4-6 weeks after initiating antifungal therapy (p.1706).","Serum CRAg screening is recommended for asymptomatic people with HIV and CD4+ counts <100-200/uL to identify those at high risk of cryptococcal disease (p.1705-1706)."],"pathophysiology":"Cryptococcal infection is almost always acquired by inhalation of aerosolized particles (small desiccated yeast cells or basidiospores); occasionally by direct skin inoculation. C. neoformans is found in soils contaminated with avian (pigeon) excreta, whereas C. gattii inhabits arboreal species such as eucalyptus and is not found in bird feces. Infection is acquired in childhood and is common, but disease is rare in the absence of impaired immunity, indicating that intact pulmonary defenses effectively contain the fungus. Some infections lead to latency with viable organisms harbored in granulomas. Extrapulmonary dissemination to the CNS may occur via direct fungal migration across endothelium and via macrophage 'Trojan horse' carriage. Virulence factors include the antiphagocytic polysaccharide capsule, melanin production, and enzymes (phospholipase, urease). Copious release of capsular polysaccharide into tissues interferes with local immune responses, and infection may elicit little or no inflammatory response; capsular polysaccharide is the antigen measured diagnostically (p.1704).","additionalDifferentials":["Tuberculous (chronic) meningitis","Other fungal meningitides (e.g., Coccidioides, Histoplasma)","Subacute dementia or depression (indolent CNS cryptococcosis mimic)","Carcinomatous meningitis","Pulmonary malignancy or other causes of lung nodules/masses (pulmonary cryptococcosis found incidentally on chest radiograph)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis (chronic meningitis and pulmonary disease)","Molluscum contagiosum (cutaneous cryptococcosis in highly immunosuppressed patients resembles its lesions)","Other disseminated/endemic fungal infections (histoplasmosis, coccidioidomycosis, blastomycosis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Carcinomatous (neoplastic) meningitis","Subacute dementia","Depression","Pulmonary malignancy (cryptococcoma/nodule mimicking neoplasm)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CSF examination: opening pressure, protein, glucose, cell count, Gram stain, cultures","India ink stain of CSF for encapsulated yeast","Cryptococcal antigen (CRAg) assay in CSF and blood; point-of-care lateral flow assay","Fungal cultures of CSF and blood (blood cultures often positive)","Serum CRAg screening in HIV with low CD4+ counts","PCR (confirm positive or negative results with independent methods such as CRAg and culture)","Chest imaging for pulmonary disease (nodules, infiltrates, masses)","Biopsy for CNS cryptococcoma or to distinguish inadequately treated infection from immune reconstitution syndrome"],"redFlags":["Raised intracranial pressure in cryptococcal meningoencephalitis requiring serial therapeutic lumbar puncture drainage (and possible shunt placement)","Sudden catastrophic vision loss from meningeal cryptococcosis","Cranial nerve paresis, vision and hearing loss","Altered mental status, headache, and blurred vision as clues to elevated ICP","Refractory cryptococcosis despite antifungal therapy (prompt susceptibility testing)"],"prognosis":"Even with antifungal therapy, cryptococcosis carries high morbidity and mortality. The most important prognostic factors are the extent and duration of the underlying immunologic deficit. Disease is often curable in those with no apparent immune dysfunction, but in irreversible immunosuppression the goal is remission maintained by lifelong suppressive therapy. Before ART, median survival for AIDS patients with cryptococcosis was <1 year. Cryptococcosis with underlying neoplastic disease has a particularly poor prognosis. Poor prognostic markers for CNS disease: positive India ink on initial CSF exam (heavy fungal burden), high CSF pressure, low CSF glucose, low CSF pleocytosis (<2/uL), recovery of yeast from extraneural sites, absence of antibody to capsular polysaccharide, CSF or serum cryptococcal antigen level >=1:32, and concomitant glucocorticoid therapy or hematologic malignancy. Relapse is common even with relatively intact immunity (p.1706).","pitfalls":["India ink and Gram stain may be falsely negative with low fungal burden or misread as positive by inexperienced operators (p.1705).","CRAg is often negative in pulmonary cryptococcosis, so it is less useful for diagnosing pulmonary disease and of only limited use in monitoring response to therapy (p.1705).","Cryptococcal cells with negative cultures on CSF staining and persistently elevated CSF/blood cryptococcal antigen long after culture clearance do NOT indicate treatment failure (p.1705).","Categorizing cryptococcosis as 'extrapulmonary without CNS involvement' should be made only after CSF evaluation reveals no evidence of cryptococcal infection (p.1705).","Prophylactic dexamethasone in HIV-associated cryptococcosis is NOT recommended (associated with reduced fungal clearance and increased mortality); glucocorticoids are not helpful for raised ICP unless from a mass lesion with edema (p.1706).","Echinocandins are NOT effective against Cryptococcus species (p.1706).","IRIS can be difficult to distinguish from relapsing/refractory infection (p.1706)."],"severityClassification":"Nonsevere disease limited to the lungs or other non-CNS sites is distinguished from severe disease or CNS involvement; severe/CNS cryptococcosis is treated with induction, consolidation, and maintenance phases (p.1705).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1703-1706","Harrison 22e p.1606-1607"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS","ENCEPHALITIS"]},"cryptosporidiosis":{"name":"Cryptosporidiosis","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Cryptosporidium infection","crypto","cryptosporidial diarrhoea"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A protozoal infection caused by Cryptosporidium species, transmitted faecal-orally through contaminated water, food or contact, with a very low infectious dose","In immunocompetent hosts it causes a self-limiting watery diarrhoea, but in the immunocompromised, especially advanced HIV, it can cause severe, chronic and life-threatening diarrhoea","A recognised cause of waterborne outbreaks because the oocysts are highly resistant to routine chlorination","An important contributor to childhood diarrhoea and to malnutrition and growth faltering in Indian children","In profound immunosuppression infection may extend beyond the gut to the biliary tract, causing sclerosing cholangitis, and to the respiratory tract","Restoration of immune function, particularly antiretroviral therapy in HIV, is central to control in immunocompromised patients","Diagnosis requires specific tests: modified acid-fast staining of stool, antigen detection or PCR, as the oocysts are missed on routine microscopy","Management emphasises rehydration; antiprotozoal drug options are limited and work best when immunity is restored"],"pathophysiology":"Ingested Cryptosporidium oocysts release sporozoites that invade the apical surface of intestinal epithelial cells, residing in an intracellular but extracytoplasmic compartment. Infection blunts villi, impairs absorption and increases secretion, producing a watery, non-bloody diarrhoea. In immunocompetent people a robust cellular immune response clears the parasite, but when T-cell immunity is deficient the organism proliferates unchecked, causing high-volume chronic diarrhoea, malabsorption and spread to biliary and respiratory epithelium.","additionalDifferentials":["Other protozoal enteritides (giardiasis, cyclosporiasis, microsporidiosis)","Bacterial and viral gastroenteritis","Other opportunistic enteric infections in HIV","Inflammatory bowel disease","Non-infective malabsorption syndromes"],"infectionMimics":["Other opportunistic causes of chronic diarrhoea in advanced HIV","Small bowel malabsorption of non-infective cause","Secretory diarrhoea from other pathogens"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Modified acid-fast (Kinyoun) staining of stool for oocysts","Stool antigen detection or PCR for sensitive diagnosis","HIV testing and CD4 count assessment when severe or chronic","Evaluation of hydration and electrolyte status","Imaging or biliary studies if cholangiopathy is suspected in the immunocompromised"],"redFlags":["Severe or high-volume dehydrating diarrhoea, especially in infants or the immunocompromised","Chronic diarrhoea with weight loss in a patient with advanced HIV/AIDS","Right upper quadrant pain and cholestasis suggesting biliary involvement","Failure of diarrhoea to settle, signalling underlying immunodeficiency"],"prognosis":"In immunocompetent individuals the illness resolves spontaneously over one to two weeks. In severely immunocompromised patients it can be chronic, debilitating and fatal unless immune function is restored, and outcome is closely tied to recovery of cellular immunity.","pitfalls":["Failing to request specific staining, antigen or PCR tests, so oocysts are missed on routine stool microscopy","Not testing for HIV in a patient with severe or persistent cryptosporidial diarrhoea","Relying on antiprotozoal drugs alone in the immunocompromised without addressing the underlying immune deficit","Underestimating waterborne outbreak potential given oocyst resistance to chlorination"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"cushing_syndrome":{"name":"Cushing syndrome","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hypercortisolism","Cushing's syndrome","cortisol excess","Cushing disease (pituitary-dependent)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typical features include central weight gain, a rounded plethoric face, a dorsocervical fat pad, thin skin, and easy bruising","Wide purple abdominal striae, proximal muscle weakness, and unexplained osteoporosis are among the more discriminating clinical signs","Exogenous glucocorticoid use is by far the most common cause and should be excluded by careful drug history before pursuing endogenous disease","Diagnosis proceeds in two stages: first confirm cortisol excess, then determine whether it is ACTH-dependent or ACTH-independent","Screening rests on demonstrating loss of normal cortisol regulation using late-night salivary cortisol, 24-hour urinary free cortisol, or a low-dose dexamethasone suppression test","A suppressed ACTH points to an adrenal source, whereas a normal or elevated ACTH indicates a pituitary tumour or an ectopic source","Pituitary-dependent disease (Cushing disease) accounts for most endogenous ACTH-dependent cases, while ectopic ACTH is often from neuroendocrine tumours","Associated metabolic consequences include hypertension, glucose intolerance or diabetes, dyslipidaemia, and increased thrombotic and infection risk","Definitive management is directed at the underlying cause, most often surgical removal of the responsible tumour"],"pathophysiology":"Cushing syndrome results from chronic exposure to excess glucocorticoid. This may be exogenous, from prescribed or surreptitious steroid use, or endogenous. Endogenous disease is divided into ACTH-dependent forms, in which a pituitary corticotroph adenoma or an ectopic ACTH-secreting tumour drives adrenal cortisol output, and ACTH-independent forms, in which an adrenal adenoma, carcinoma, or bilateral hyperplasia autonomously produces cortisol. Sustained cortisol excess disrupts the normal negative-feedback of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis, abolishes the circadian rhythm of cortisol, and produces widespread catabolic, metabolic, immunosuppressive, and mineralocorticoid effects on multiple tissues.","additionalDifferentials":["Exogenous (iatrogenic) glucocorticoid effect","Pseudo-Cushing states from severe obesity, depression, or heavy alcohol use","Metabolic syndrome with central obesity","Polycystic ovary syndrome","Poorly controlled diabetes with weight gain","Ectopic ACTH from neuroendocrine malignancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Late-night salivary cortisol to detect loss of the normal evening nadir","24-hour urinary free cortisol, ideally on more than one collection","Low-dose (overnight or 48-hour) dexamethasone suppression testing","Plasma ACTH to separate ACTH-dependent from ACTH-independent disease","Pituitary MRI, adrenal CT, or cross-sectional imaging for ectopic sources as directed by biochemistry","Inferior petrosal sinus sampling to localise ACTH-dependent disease when imaging is equivocal"],"redFlags":["Rapid onset with marked hypokalaemia and pigmentation suggesting aggressive ectopic ACTH secretion","New severe hypertension, hyperglycaemia, or thromboembolism from florid hypercortisolism","Features of adrenal carcinoma such as very high androgen or cortisol levels with a large adrenal mass","Severe proximal weakness, fractures, or psychosis reflecting advanced disease"],"prognosis":"With successful treatment of the underlying cause many features regress over months, though hypertension, bone loss, and psychological effects may persist; untreated hypercortisolism carries substantial cardiovascular, metabolic, infective, and thrombotic mortality, and ectopic ACTH from malignancy has a poor outlook.","pitfalls":["Overlooking exogenous steroid exposure, including inhaled, topical, or injected preparations","Interpreting a single borderline test as diagnostic rather than repeating and combining screening tests","Confusing pseudo-Cushing states with true disease during acute illness, depression, or alcohol excess","Assuming a pituitary microadenoma on imaging proves the source without confirmatory biochemistry"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"cutaneous_larva_migrans":{"name":"Cutaneous Larva Migrans","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CLM","creeping eruption","sandworm disease","hookworm-related cutaneous larva migrans"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The hallmark is an intensely itchy, serpiginous (snake-like), slightly raised erythematous track that advances a short distance each day","It commonly affects the feet, buttocks, and other skin that contacts contaminated sand or soil, reflecting exposure during beach holidays or barefoot activity in warm climates","Take a travel and exposure history, as most cases in temperate regions are imported from tropical and subtropical areas","The larva itself lies just ahead of the visible track, so the leading edge marks its current position","It is a self-limiting zoonotic infestation because the human is a dead-end host in which the animal hookworm larva cannot complete its life cycle","Antihelminthic therapy shortens symptoms and is warranted for troublesome disease; the itch responds as the larva dies and the track resolves","Secondary bacterial infection from scratching and, less often, a localised allergic reaction can complicate the picture","Preventive advice includes wearing footwear and using barriers on contaminated sand or soil"],"pathophysiology":"Cutaneous larva migrans is caused by the larvae of animal hookworms, most often Ancylostoma species from dogs and cats, whose eggs are shed in animal faeces and hatch into infective larvae in warm, moist sand or soil. On contact these larvae penetrate intact human skin but lack the enzymes to cross the basement membrane, so they remain trapped in the epidermis. As the larva migrates it provokes a local eosinophilic inflammatory and hypersensitivity response that produces the itchy, advancing serpiginous track. Because humans are accidental dead-end hosts, the parasite cannot mature or disseminate and eventually dies, giving the self-limiting course.","additionalDifferentials":["Larva currens due to Strongyloides (faster-moving track, often perianal)","Scabies","Contact dermatitis","Tinea (especially of the feet)","Erythema migrans of Lyme disease","Loiasis or other migratory parasitic eruptions in the appropriate exposure setting"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis is clinical, based on the characteristic migrating track and exposure history","Dermoscopy may help visualise the larval track","Blood eosinophilia is variable and non-specific","Biopsy is usually unhelpful because the larva lies ahead of the visible track and is rarely captured","Assessment for secondary bacterial infection when the site is inflamed or purulent"],"redFlags":["Extensive involvement or numerous tracks suggesting heavy exposure or an immunocompromised host","Rapidly moving perianal tracks or features suggesting Strongyloides, which can disseminate and requires different management","Signs of secondary bacterial infection such as spreading erythema, pain, or pus","Systemic symptoms or respiratory features prompting reconsideration of a diagnosis with visceral involvement"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and self-limiting, as the larva dies within weeks to a few months even without treatment; antihelminthic therapy shortens the intensely itchy course and speeds resolution, and lasting sequelae are uncommon apart from transient post-inflammatory change.","pitfalls":["Failing to elicit a travel or sand-exposure history and missing the diagnosis","Confusing it with the faster-moving larva currens of Strongyloides, which carries a risk of dissemination","Performing an unhelpful biopsy at the visible track rather than treating clinically","Overlooking secondary bacterial infection driven by scratching"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"cystic_fibrosis":{"name":"Cystic Fibrosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CF","CFTR disease"],"chapter":302,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2245","clinicalPearls":["Cystic fibrosis is an autosomal recessive exocrinopathy affecting multiple epithelial tissues; the gene product CFTR is an apical anion (chloride and bicarbonate) channel that regulates the volume and composition of exocrine secretion (p.2245).","Major morbidity and mortality arise from pulmonary disease: copious hyperviscous adherent secretions obstruct small and medium airways, and a complex flora including Staphylococcus aureus, Haemophilus influenzae, and Pseudomonas aeruginosa is routinely cultured from CF sputum (p.2245).","P. aeruginosa shows stereotypic pathogenesis: an early sentinel colonization often becomes lifelong infection by the same strain, and over years evolves a mucoid (alginate-producing) phenotype that confers selective advantage and poor host prognosis (p.2245).","Radiographic sinusitis occurs in most patients with CF; the sinuses may act as a reservoir for bacterial seeding of the lower airways (p.2245).","Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency causes chronic malabsorption, poor growth, and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency; high blood immunoreactive trypsinogen (used in newborn screening) and low fecal elastase-1 are characteristic (p.2245-2246).","CF-related diabetes mellitus occurs in >30% of adults and is multifactorial (endocrine pancreatic destruction plus, in some, insulin resistance); annual diabetes testing is recommended for adults with CF (p.2246, p.2248).","Men typically show complete involution of the vas deferens despite functioning spermatogenesis, and ~99% of males with CF are infertile (unable to conceive without in vitro fertilization) (p.2246).","Diagnosis is confirmed by disease-causing variants on both CFTR alleles and/or sweat chloride >=60 mEq/L together with characteristic exocrine manifestations; sweat electrolyte testing follows pilocarpine iontophoresis (p.2246-2247).","Several severe defects (F508del, G551D, nonsense alleles) predict pancreatic insufficiency, present in ~80% of patients; F508del alone accounts for ~85% of defective CFTR alleles in the United States (p.2247).","CFTR modulators (ivacaftor potentiator; lumacaftor/tezacaftor/elexacaftor correctors) are highly effective modulator therapy (HEMT); triple combination therapy is projected to benefit >90% of patients and improves FEV1, reduces exacerbations, and lowers sweat chloride (p.2247-2248).","Pulmonary exacerbation poor-prognosis pathogens that are rigorously monitored include Burkholderia cepacia complex, Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, Achromobacter, mucoid P. aeruginosa, and atypical mycobacteria; MRSA may also signal worse outcomes (p.2248).","Infection commonly persists despite modulator therapy, so chronic respiratory infection and inflammation continue even after substantial clinical improvement on HEMT (p.2248)."],"pathophysiology":"Mutation of CFTR, an apical epithelial anion channel that conducts chloride and bicarbonate (~1480 amino acids, gated by ATP hydrolysis without active transport against gradient), impairs hydration and clearance of exocrine secretions. In the airway, loss of CFTR-dependent anion secretion depletes the periciliary fluid layer, causing ciliary collapse and failure to clear overlying mucus; mucoobstruction with chronic infection drives an aggressive, unrelenting neutrophilic inflammatory response with proteases and oxidants, leading to airway remodeling and bronchiectasis. Low airway-surface-fluid pH may impair bacterial killing. Similar mechanisms affect pancreatic acini/ducts (fibrotic destruction, cyst formation, exocrine and eventually endocrine insufficiency), hepatobiliary tissue (biliary obstruction, focal/multilobular cirrhosis, portal hypertension), and intestine (meconium ileus, distal intestinal obstructive syndrome). Over 2000 CFTR allelic variants are described and grouped into classes I-VI by molecular mechanism (e.g., F508del is a class II protein-maturation/folding defect degraded in the endoplasmic reticulum; G551D is a class III gating defect).","additionalDifferentials":["Non-CF bronchiectasis (CF carrier status predisposes; heterozygous CFTR mutations implicated) (p.2247)","Chronic rhinosinusitis (associated with relative CFTR deficiency) (p.2247)","Congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens as an isolated forme fruste of CFTR mutation (p.2247)","Idiopathic/recurrent pancreatitis associated with CFTR mutation in at least one allele (p.2247)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Sweat chloride after pilocarpine iontophoresis (chloride >=60 mEq/L supports diagnosis; highly specific) (p.2246-2247)","CFTR mutation analysis / panels of several hundred variants, or complete CFTR sequencing with splice-junction analysis (p.2246-2247)","Immunoreactive trypsinogen (newborn screening) (p.2246)","Fecal elastase-1 (low in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency) (p.2246)","Sputum culture surveillance for resistant pathogens (Burkholderia cepacia complex, Stenotrophomonas, Achromobacter, mucoid P. aeruginosa, atypical mycobacteria, MRSA) (p.2248)","FEV1 (% predicted) for monitoring and transplant referral thresholds (p.2248)","Annual diabetes mellitus testing in adults; monitor liver function and CYP3A drug interactions on modulator therapy (p.2248)"],"redFlags":["Hemoptysis requiring hospitalization (p.2248)","Pneumothorax requiring hospitalization (p.2248)","Sputum culture growing Burkholderia cepacia complex, Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, Achromobacter, mucoid P. aeruginosa, or atypical mycobacteria (poor prognostic indicators) (p.2248)","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (~5% of patients) suggested by failure to respond to aggressive inpatient antibiotics (p.2248)","FEV1 <30% predicted (threshold for lung transplant referral) (p.2248)"],"prognosis":"Modulator and other therapies have transformed CF from a disease that historically led to death in early childhood to one with frequent survival well into the fifth decade and beyond; US median survival now exceeds 60 years. Genotype is of limited value for predicting respiratory rate of decline or longevity. End-stage pulmonary failure may be treated with lung transplantation (median survival >9 years among adults). Marked geographic disparity exists: median survival is <30 years in much of Latin America versus >60 years in the United States (p.2248-2249).","pitfalls":["Hyperviscosity of eccrine sweat is NOT a feature of CF; the sweat test reflects defective ductal chloride reabsorption, not thick sweat (p.2247).","Anion flux through CFTR does NOT involve active transport against a concentration gradient (p.2245-2246).","Genotype does NOT reliably predict respiratory prognosis or longevity, even though some severe alleles predict pancreatic insufficiency (p.2247).","A subset of patients with F508del respond poorly to triple combination therapy, so modulator benefit is not universal (p.2248).","HEMT may reduce sputum bacterial density but does NOT eradicate infection; chronic respiratory infection and inflammation persist (p.2248).","CF has been underdiagnosed outside historically white populations (Eastern Europe, Latin America, Asia, India, and US minorities), often due to rare/poorly characterized variants and limited access to DNA/sweat testing (p.2249)."],"severityClassification":"CFTR mutations are categorized by molecular mechanism into classes I-VI: class I, absent synthesis (e.g., nonsense mutations G542X, R553X, W1282X); class II, defective protein maturation and premature degradation (e.g., F508del, ~85% of US alleles, the most common); class III, disordered gating/regulation (e.g., G551D, ~4% of patients); class IV, defective conductance through the channel pore; class V, reduced transcript number from promoter/splicing abnormality; class VI, accelerated turnover from the cell surface. Severity is not formally graded by stage; FEV1 <30% predicted is used as a lung-transplant referral threshold (p.2246, p.2248).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2245"],"crossLinks":[]},"cystic_fibrosis_paed":{"name":"Cystic Fibrosis (Paediatric)","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CF","cystic fibrosis","mucoviscidosis","paediatric cystic fibrosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive multisystem disorder caused by mutations in the CFTR gene, the most common of which is F508del.","It is a genetic disease, not an infection, though recurrent respiratory infections dominate its course and morbidity.","Suspect it in a child with chronic productive cough, recurrent chest infections, failure to thrive, and greasy, bulky, foul-smelling stools from fat malabsorption.","Meconium ileus in a newborn is highly suggestive and is often the earliest presentation.","Many countries diagnose it through newborn screening, but a positive screen must be confirmed, classically with an elevated sweat chloride test.","Pancreatic exocrine insufficiency is common and requires enzyme replacement and fat-soluble vitamin supplementation, with attention to overall nutrition.","Chronic airway infection, notably with Pseudomonas aeruginosa, drives progressive bronchiectasis and lung function decline.","Care is delivered by a specialist multidisciplinary team, and CFTR modulator therapies have transformed outcomes for eligible genotypes."],"pathophysiology":"Mutations in the CFTR gene impair the CFTR chloride and bicarbonate channel at epithelial surfaces. Defective ion transport produces thick, dehydrated secretions across multiple organs. In the airways, viscous mucus impairs mucociliary clearance, promoting chronic infection, neutrophilic inflammation, and progressive bronchiectasis. In the pancreas, ductal obstruction leads to exocrine insufficiency and malabsorption; similar mechanisms affect the intestine, biliary tree, sweat glands (hence high sweat chloride), and reproductive tract.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary ciliary dyskinesia","Immunodeficiency with recurrent infections","Asthma with recurrent chest infections","Protracted bacterial bronchitis","Coeliac disease or other causes of malabsorption and failure to thrive","Shwachman-Diamond syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Newborn screening (immunoreactive trypsinogen) with confirmatory testing","Sweat chloride testing","CFTR genetic analysis","Assessment of pancreatic function (e.g. faecal elastase)","Respiratory cultures to identify airway pathogens","Chest imaging and, in older children, lung function testing","Nutritional assessment and fat-soluble vitamin levels"],"redFlags":["Failure to thrive or faltering growth reflecting malabsorption and increased energy demands","New or persistent Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection requiring eradication","Acute pulmonary exacerbation with increased cough, sputum, breathlessness, or falling lung function","Haemoptysis or pneumothorax","Distal intestinal obstruction syndrome or meconium ileus in the newborn","Signs of CF-related diabetes or liver disease emerging over time"],"prognosis":"Survival has improved dramatically with multidisciplinary care, aggressive infection and nutrition management, and CFTR modulator therapy, with many people now living well into adulthood; prognosis is largely determined by the rate of progressive lung disease and nutritional status.","pitfalls":["Attributing recurrent chest infections and loose stools to common childhood illness and missing the diagnosis","Assuming a negative newborn screen fully excludes CF, since screening can miss some cases","Underestimating nutritional needs and failing to optimise pancreatic enzyme and vitamin replacement","Delaying treatment of new Pseudomonas infection when early eradication is most effective","Overlooking emerging complications such as CF-related diabetes and liver disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"cysticercosis":{"name":"Cysticercosis","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Taenia solium larval infection","tissue cysticercosis","pork tapeworm cyst disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from ingestion of Taenia solium eggs, which develop into larval cysts (cysticerci) in human tissues; this is distinct from intestinal tapeworm infection acquired by eating undercooked pork","Faecal-oral spread of eggs from a tapeworm carrier means infection can occur even in people who do not eat pork, including through contaminated food and poor sanitation","Cysts lodge preferentially in muscle, subcutaneous tissue, the eyes and the central nervous system; neurological involvement is the most important form","Subcutaneous and muscular cysts may be palpable as painless nodules and can calcify, sometimes visible on imaging","Ocular cysticercosis can threaten vision and requires careful assessment before any antiparasitic therapy","It is a major public-health problem in India and other regions where pigs are raised with poor sanitation","Serology (antibody detection) supports the diagnosis, but imaging is central to defining cyst location and burden","Antiparasitic therapy is used with adjunctive corticosteroids to control the inflammation provoked by dying cysts, and treatment decisions depend heavily on the site and stage of cysts"],"pathophysiology":"When humans ingest Taenia solium eggs, oncospheres hatch in the intestine, penetrate the mucosa and disseminate through the bloodstream to tissues, where they mature into fluid-filled cysticerci. Living cysts often provoke little inflammation, but as they degenerate they release antigens that trigger a host inflammatory and immune response. This inflammation, together with mass effect and eventual calcification, produces most of the clinical manifestations, especially when cysts lie within the brain or eye.","additionalDifferentials":["Other soft-tissue parasitic cysts (e.g. hydatid disease)","Lipomas or other benign subcutaneous nodules","Tuberculoma when brain lesions are present","Pyogenic or fungal abscess","Neoplastic lesions in relevant tissues"],"infectionMimics":["Toxoplasmosis (for CNS lesions)","Tuberculosis with tissue or CNS involvement","Metastatic disease","Sarcoidosis with granulomatous nodules"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT or MRI to localise and stage cysts, particularly in the brain","Serological antibody or antigen assays (e.g. EITB) as supportive evidence","Ophthalmological examination to exclude ocular cysts before therapy","Plain radiography or ultrasound to detect calcified muscular/subcutaneous cysts","Stool examination for Taenia eggs to identify a coexisting tapeworm carrier"],"redFlags":["New-onset seizures or focal neurological deficits indicating central nervous system involvement","Features of raised intracranial pressure requiring urgent evaluation","Visual disturbance suggesting an ocular cyst that could be harmed by antiparasitic-induced inflammation","Rapid neurological deterioration during or after starting antiparasitic therapy"],"prognosis":"Isolated subcutaneous or muscular cysticercosis is generally benign and often asymptomatic. Prognosis is largely determined by neurological and ocular involvement, where outcome depends on cyst location, number, and the inflammatory response to treatment; many patients do well but some suffer chronic epilepsy or visual loss.","pitfalls":["Starting antiparasitic drugs without first excluding ocular cysts, risking sight-threatening inflammation","Overlooking a coexisting intestinal tapeworm that perpetuates transmission","Attributing all soft-tissue nodules to benign causes without imaging in endemic areas","Failing to give adjunctive anti-inflammatory cover when treating heavy or CNS cyst burdens"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"cytomegalovirus_infection":{"name":"Cytomegalovirus (CMV) Infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["CMV"],"chapter":200,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1512","clinicalPearls":["CMV is a beta-herpesvirus carried for life after infection; the infection is usually silent, and reactivation syndromes emerge predominantly when T-lymphocyte-mediated immunity is compromised (organ transplantation, lymphoid neoplasms, HIV, or critical illness in the ICU) (p.1512).","In immunocompetent hosts beyond the neonatal period, the most common manifestation is a heterophile-antibody-negative mononucleosis syndrome; unlike EBV mononucleosis, exudative pharyngitis and cervical lymphadenopathy are rare, though splenomegaly, prolonged high fever, fatigue and malaise are common (p.1513-1514).","The characteristic laboratory finding in CMV mononucleosis is relative lymphocytosis with >10% atypical lymphocytes (predominantly activated CD8+ T cells); heterophile antibodies are absent (p.1514).","Congenital CMV is the commonest congenital infection; most congenital infections are clinically inapparent at birth, yet 7-11% of asymptomatically infected infants develop sensorineural hearing loss over 5 years (p.1512-1513).","Clinical disease in the fetus/newborn is related largely to PRIMARY maternal infection rather than reactivation; primary first-trimester infection carries up to 40-50% risk of sensorineural complications (p.1513).","Preferred diagnosis is detection of virus/antigen/DNA, most commonly quantitative nucleic acid testing (QNAT) by PCR; the pp65 antigenemia immunofluorescence assay is an alternative, and CMV DNA in CSF aids diagnosis of CMV encephalitis or polyradiculopathy (p.1515).","In persons with HIV, end-organ CMV disease (especially retinitis) occurs particularly when CD4+ counts fall below 50/uL; flares of CMV retinitis can occur shortly after starting antiretroviral therapy as an immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) (p.1514).","An immune-complex glomerulopathy has been observed in some CMV-infected patients after renal transplantation, and CMV mononucleosis can produce transient autoantibodies (cryoglobulins, rheumatoid factors, cold agglutinins, antinuclear antibodies) via polyclonal B-cell activation (p.1512-1514).","Pathology shows enlarged 'cytomegalic' cells (2-4x normal) with an eccentric intranuclear inclusion surrounded by a clear halo producing the classic 'owl's eye' appearance (p.1513)."],"pathophysiology":"CMV is a beta-herpesvirus with double-stranded DNA, icosahedral symmetry and a lipoprotein envelope; it replicates in the cell nucleus and can establish either lytic/productive or latent infection. After primary infection the virus persists indefinitely in multiple cell types and organs (silent latency). Primary infection in late childhood/adulthood elicits a vigorous CD8+ T-lymphocyte response producing atypical lymphocytes and a mononucleosis-like illness, with polyclonal B-cell activation generating autoantibodies. When host T-cell responses are compromised by disease or iatrogenic immunosuppression, latent virus reactivates; chronic antigenic stimulation under immunosuppression (e.g., post-transplant) is an ideal setting for CMV disease, and potent T-cell suppressants (antithymocyte globulin, alemtuzumab) markedly raise CMV syndrome rates. CMV itself further depresses T-lymphocyte responsiveness, predisposing to superinfection by other opportunists. Congenital disease arises chiefly from primary maternal infection; transmission to transplant/transfusion recipients reflects silent infection in donor tissues (p.1512-1513).","additionalDifferentials":["Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infectious mononucleosis (heterophile-positive, with prominent exudative pharyngitis and cervical adenopathy) versus CMV mononucleosis (heterophile-negative)","Congenital cytomegalic inclusion disease must be distinguished from congenital syphilis, toxoplasmosis, rubella, Zika, herpes simplex virus, and bacterial sepsis (p.1513)","CMV pneumonitis differential includes Pneumocystis, other viral/bacterial/fungal pneumonias, pulmonary hemorrhage, and irradiation or cytotoxic drug injury (p.1514)","CMV retinopathy must be distinguished from toxoplasmosis, candidiasis, and herpes simplex virus retinitis (p.1514)"],"infectionMimics":["Epstein-Barr virus mononucleosis","Toxoplasmosis","Congenital rubella, Zika, herpes simplex virus, and syphilis (in neonates)","Pneumocystis pneumonia","Herpes simplex virus and Candida retinitis/esophagitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pulmonary hemorrhage and radiation- or cytotoxic-drug-induced lung injury (mimicking CMV pneumonitis)","Allograft rejection (overlapping with CMV syndrome in transplant recipients)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative nucleic acid testing (QNAT) for CMV DNA by PCR in blood or other specimens (preferred; same assay should be used for serial comparison)","CMV pp65 antigenemia immunofluorescence assay on peripheral-blood leukocytes","CMV DNA PCR in cerebrospinal fluid for suspected encephalitis or polyradiculopathy","Viral culture on human fibroblast monolayers, including the rapid overnight shell vial assay with monoclonal antibody to immediate-early antigen","Serology: CMV IgG (may take up to 4 weeks to rise after primary infection) and CMV IgM (false positives possible with rheumatoid factor); serology mainly stratifies transplant risk and is NOT recommended to diagnose acute disease in immunocompromised patients","Histopathology/biopsy demonstrating 'owl's eye' cytomegalic inclusions for tissue-invasive disease"],"redFlags":["Retinitis in an immunocompromised host (especially HIV with CD4+ <50/uL) - threat to vision","Colitis or gastrointestinal ulceration with risk of bleeding or perforation","Pneumonitis (tachypnea, hypoxemia, nonproductive cough with bilateral interstitial/reticulonodular infiltrates)","Encephalitis/ventriculoencephalitis or subacute progressive polyradiculopathy","Persistent viremia with multi-organ involvement, pancytopenia and hypotension (associated with fatal disease)","Symptomatic congenital infection (petechiae, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, microcephaly with cerebral calcifications, intrauterine growth retardation)"],"prognosis":"Immunocompetent CMV mononucleosis is almost always self-limited (illness lasting 2-6 weeks); most patients recover without sequelae, though postviral asthenia may persist for months and fatal infection is rare. Severely affected congenitally infected infants have a poor prognosis with a mortality of about 5% and few survivors escaping intellectual or hearing impairment; 7-11% of asymptomatically infected neonates still develop sensorineural hearing loss over 5 years. In immunocompromised hosts, untreated CMV may progress to severe end-organ disease; CMV disease is an independent risk factor for both graft loss and death after organ transplantation, and high blood viral loads in HIV predict severe disease and death (p.1513-1514).","pitfalls":["Tissue-invasive CMV disease (e.g., gastrointestinal) may be present despite NEGATIVE blood CMV antigenemia and viral-load testing - diagnosis may require endoscopic biopsy with histopathology, not blood viremia alone (p.1514).","Isolation of CMV from urine, stool, or saliva does NOT by itself prove acute infection, since excretion may persist for months to years; detection of viremia by QNAT or antigenemia is a better predictor (p.1515).","Serology is NOT recommended to diagnose acute disease in immunocompromised patients; IgG may not rise for up to 4 weeks and rheumatoid factor can cause false-positive IgM (p.1515).","CMV detection in blood does not equate to end-organ disease; clinical and tissue correlation is required.","Universal ganciclovir/valganciclovir prophylaxis is bone-marrow suppressive, favoring preemptive strategies in HSCT recipients (p.1515)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames CMV by host and syndrome rather than a formal grading scale: (1) congenital CMV - subclinical to severe disseminated cytomegalic inclusion disease; (2) perinatal CMV - usually asymptomatic; (3) immunocompetent CMV mononucleosis - heterophile-negative, self-limited; (4) CMV in the immunocompromised host - asymptomatic infection/viremia, 'CMV syndrome' (febrile leukopenia), and tissue-invasive end-organ disease (pneumonitis, colitis/GI disease, hepatitis, esophagitis, retinitis, encephalitis/polyradiculopathy), stratified by population in Table 200-1 (fetus/neonate, organ transplant recipient, HSCT recipient, person with HIV) (p.1513).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1512"],"crossLinks":["infectious_mononucleosis"]},"dacryocystitis":{"name":"Dacryocystitis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lacrimal sac infection","acute dacryocystitis","chronic dacryocystitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Infection of the lacrimal sac, usually secondary to obstruction of the nasolacrimal duct causing stasis of tears and secretions","Acute dacryocystitis presents with a painful, red, tender swelling at the medial canthus just below the medial canthal tendon, often with epiphora and discharge","Pressure over the sac may express mucopurulent material through the puncta, supporting the diagnosis","The swelling lies below and medial to the medial canthal tendon, which helps distinguish it from preseptal cellulitis and other medial canthal masses","Common organisms include Staphylococcus species and streptococci; management is with systemic antibiotics and warm compresses in the acute phase","Incision and drainage may be needed for a pointing abscess, but definitive treatment of the underlying obstruction is dacryocystorhinostomy once inflammation settles","Congenital dacryocystitis in infants relates to a persistent membrane at the valve of Hasner and may need probing if it does not resolve","Acute dacryocystitis can be complicated by preseptal or orbital cellulitis and rarely by fistula formation"],"pathophysiology":"Dacryocystitis is inflammation of the lacrimal sac, almost always resulting from obstruction of the nasolacrimal duct. Obstruction leads to retention and stagnation of tears and mucus within the sac, providing a medium for bacterial overgrowth. Acute infection produces a tense, inflamed sac that may progress to abscess formation, while chronic obstruction causes a mucocele with recurrent low-grade infection and persistent watering. In infants, incomplete canalisation of the duct at the valve of Hasner is the usual cause.","additionalDifferentials":["Preseptal cellulitis","Orbital cellulitis","Mucocele of the lacrimal sac","Ethmoidal or frontal sinus disease","Lacrimal sac tumour (in atypical or bloody presentations)","Infected sebaceous or dermoid cyst at the medial canthus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination with expression of the lacrimal sac to demonstrate reflux of discharge","Swab of expressed discharge for microscopy, culture, and sensitivities","Syringing and probing of the lacrimal system once acute infection has settled to confirm obstruction","Imaging (CT or dacryocystography) for atypical, recurrent, or mass-like presentations to exclude tumour"],"redFlags":["Spread to preseptal or orbital cellulitis with proptosis, restricted eye movements, or reduced vision","A firm, irregular, or bloody lacrimal sac mass suggesting a lacrimal sac tumour rather than simple infection","Systemic features of sepsis in an unwell patient","Neonatal dacryocystitis, which can progress rapidly and needs prompt treatment"],"prognosis":"Acute dacryocystitis usually responds well to antibiotics, but recurrence is common until the underlying nasolacrimal duct obstruction is corrected surgically; dacryocystorhinostomy is highly effective at relieving obstruction and preventing further episodes.","pitfalls":["Attempting probing or surgery during acute inflammation rather than treating the infection first","Mislabelling the medial canthal swelling as preseptal cellulitis and missing the underlying sac infection","Failing to recognise a lacrimal sac tumour presenting as atypical dacryocystitis","Not addressing the underlying duct obstruction, leading to repeated infections"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"deep_venous_thrombosis_and_pulmonary":{"name":"Deep-Venous Thrombosis and Pulmonary Thromboembolism","system":"Cardiovascular / Venous (Vascular)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VTE","Venous thromboembolism","DVT","Deep vein thrombosis","PE","Pulmonary embolism","Pulmonary thromboembolism"],"chapter":290,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2154","clinicalPearls":["PE is described as the great masquerader; symptoms and signs are nonspecific, and unexplained breathlessness is its most common symptom. Failure to improve despite standard therapy for concurrent heart failure or pneumonia is a clue to coexisting PE.","The most common DVT symptom is a persistent, intensifying calf cramp or 'charley horse'; physical findings may be limited to mild lower-calf tenderness, although massive DVT can produce marked thigh swelling, tenderness, and erythema.","A diffusely edematous leg makes ordinary DVT unlikely, with the important exception of phlegmasia cerulea dolens, the most severe form of DVT.","Recurrent left thigh edema, especially in young women, should raise suspicion for May-Thurner syndrome (right common iliac artery compressing the left iliac vein), which may be associated with DVT.","Wells point scores estimate clinical likelihood of DVT/PE and are validated in ambulatory but not hospitalized patients; low-likelihood DVT or low-to-moderate-likelihood PE can be evaluated with d-dimer alone, while high-likelihood patients should skip d-dimer and go straight to imaging.","Age-adjusted d-dimer (age x 10 ng/mL as the upper limit for patients over 50 with low/intermediate PE probability) reduces unnecessary CT pulmonary angiograms, but it does NOT apply to suspected acute DVT.","Venous ultrasound diagnoses DVT chiefly through loss of vein compressibility; a normal vein collapses with gentle transducer pressure (the 'wink'), whereas an acutely thrombosed vein stays distended and non-compressible.","CT pulmonary angiography is the principal imaging test for PE; an RV-to-LV diameter ratio greater than 1 (RV enlargement) on CT predicts increased 30-day mortality.","ECG S1Q3T3 is relatively specific but insensitive for PE; the most common ECG abnormalities are sinus tachycardia and T-wave inversion in leads V1-V4 from RV strain/ischemia.","McConnell's sign on echocardiography (RV free-wall hypokinesis with preserved or hyperkinetic RV apex) is a recognized indirect sign of PE, but most PE patients have a normal echocardiogram, so echo is not a reliable rule-out test.","Many patients with PE have no demonstrable DVT because the leg thrombus has already embolized; paradoxical embolism to the arterial circulation can occur through a patent foramen ovale or atrial septal defect.","Antiphospholipid syndrome is an acquired (not inherited) thrombophilia predisposing to both venous and arterial thrombosis, and patients often warrant lifetime anticoagulation even when the index VTE was provoked."],"pathophysiology":"VTE arises from Virchow's triad of venous stasis, hypercoagulability, and endothelial injury, typically coupled with an inflammatory trigger. Activated platelets release microparticles bearing proinflammatory mediators that recruit neutrophils to form neutrophil extracellular traps; the contained histones drive platelet aggregation and platelet-dependent thrombin generation, and thrombi flourish in conditions of stasis, low oxygen tension, and upregulated proinflammatory genes. Inherited prothrombotic states include factor V Leiden (resistance to protein C) and the prothrombin gene mutation; deficiencies of antithrombin, protein C, and protein S are associated with VTE but rare. Lower-extremity DVT usually begins in the calf and may propagate to the popliteal, femoral, and iliac veins; when thrombi detach they embolize through the vena cava and right heart to lodge in the pulmonary arteries. PE impairs gas exchange, causing arterial hypoxemia and a widened alveolar-arterial oxygen gradient, increased anatomic and physiologic dead space, increased pulmonary vascular resistance (from mechanical obstruction and vasoconstrictors such as serotonin), reflex hyperventilation, increased airway resistance, and reduced pulmonary compliance. Rising pulmonary artery pressure increases RV wall tension, causing RV dilation and dysfunction with BNP release; the interventricular septum bows into and compresses the left ventricle, impairing LV filling; elevated RV wall tension also compresses the right coronary artery, producing RV ischemia/microinfarction with troponin release, and LV underfilling can culminate in falling cardiac output, hypotension, circulatory collapse, and death.","additionalDifferentials":["Ruptured Baker's cyst (suggested by sudden severe calf discomfort)","Muscle strain or injury","Cellulitis (fever and chills favor cellulitis over DVT)","Acute postthrombotic syndrome / chronic venous insufficiency","Pneumonia, asthma, or COPD","Congestive heart failure","Pericarditis","Pleurisy / viral syndrome / costochondritis / musculoskeletal chest discomfort","Rib fracture or pneumothorax","Acute coronary syndrome","Anxiety","Vasovagal syncope"],"infectionMimics":["Cellulitis mimicking DVT (fever and chills herald cellulitis rather than DVT)","Pneumonia mimicking PE"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Ruptured Baker's cyst","Muscle strain/injury","Postthrombotic syndrome / venous insufficiency","Congestive heart failure","Acute coronary syndrome and myocardial infarction","Pericarditis and pericardial tamponade","Aortic dissection","Pneumothorax / rib fracture","Anxiety and vasovagal syncope"],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative plasma d-dimer ELISA (sensitivity over 95% for PE; useful rule-out at low pretest probability but nonspecific - elevated in MI, pneumonia, sepsis, cancer, postoperative state, and 2nd/3rd-trimester pregnancy, so rarely useful in hospitalized patients)","Serum troponin (rises with RV microinfarction)","BNP or NT-proBNP (released with RV/myocardial stretch)","ECG (sinus tachycardia, S1Q3T3, T-wave inversion V1-V4)","Compression venous ultrasonography with Doppler (first-line for DVT)","CT pulmonary angiography with contrast (principal PE imaging test; also assesses RV size and alternative diagnoses)","Ventilation/perfusion lung scan (second-line, for patients unable to tolerate contrast)","Chest radiograph (Westermark's sign, Hampton's hump, Palla's sign)","MR venography with gadolinium (for equivocal leg/pelvic ultrasound) and MR pulmonary angiography (detects large proximal PE only)","Echocardiography (to detect PE mimics and indirect signs such as McConnell's sign)","Invasive pulmonary angiography and contrast phlebography (reserved for nondiagnostic CT or when intervention is planned)"],"redFlags":["Systemic arterial hypotension with extensive thrombosis - massive (high-risk) PE, accounting for 5-10% of cases, presenting with dyspnea, syncope, hypotension, cyanosis, and possible cardiogenic shock and multisystem organ failure","RV dysfunction on echocardiography or RV enlargement on CT despite normal systemic pressure - submassive (intermediate-risk) PE","Combination of right heart failure with elevated cardiac biomarkers (troponin), indicating high risk of clinical deterioration","Phlegmasia cerulea dolens - the most serious form of DVT with diffuse leg edema","Hemodynamic instability portending high risk of adverse outcomes despite anticoagulation"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on PE severity and RV function. Low-risk PE (about 65-75% of cases) carries an excellent prognosis. Submassive (intermediate-risk) PE (20-25%) with RV dysfunction and biomarker elevation has a high risk of clinical deterioration, and massive (high-risk) PE (5-10%) can cause cardiogenic shock, multisystem organ failure, and death. US PE-related mortality fell from 6 to about 4.5 per 100,000 between 2000 and 2017 but is rising among young and middle-aged adults, with the median age at PE death dropping from 73 (2000) to 68 (2018) and roughly 50% higher mortality in African Americans than Caucasians; mortality is consistently higher in socioeconomically disadvantaged communities. The 30-day all-cause readmission rate after PE hospitalization is about 11% and the 90-day rate about 15%, with recurrent PE the most common readmission cause. Long-term, roughly half of PE patients report persistent dyspnea, fatigue, and reduced exercise capacity and about a quarter have persistent RV dysfunction - the post-PE syndrome; chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH) develops in about 2% of acute PE patients. DVT can lead to postthrombotic syndrome (chronic venous insufficiency) with calf/ankle swelling, leg aching, and, in severe cases, deep skin ulceration.","pitfalls":["Relying on d-dimer in hospitalized patients - levels are frequently elevated by systemic illness, so d-dimer rarely has a useful role in inpatients.","Applying the age-adjusted d-dimer cutoff to suspected acute DVT - it is validated only for ruling out PE in patients over 50 with low/intermediate probability.","Using echocardiography as a rule-out test for PE - most PE patients have normal echocardiograms; echo is better for detecting mimics like MI, tamponade, and aortic dissection.","Assuming absence of DVT excludes PE - the leg thrombus may have already fully embolized.","Treating antiphospholipid syndrome with DOACs - warfarin, not DOACs, is the standard for APS.","Mistaking a diffusely edematous leg for routine DVT and overlooking phlegmasia cerulea dolens; conversely attributing all calf pain to DVT (consider ruptured Baker's cyst) and all dyspnea to PE.","Confusing cellulitis (fever, chills) with DVT.","Overlooking nonthrombotic embolism (fat, tumor, bone marrow, air, amniotic fluid, cement, talc/hair in IV drug users) as a cause of PE."],"severityClassification":"Pulmonary embolism is stratified into three categories: Massive (high-risk) PE - 5-10% of cases, systemic arterial hypotension with thrombosis involving at least half the pulmonary vasculature, presenting with dyspnea, syncope, hypotension, and cyanosis and capable of causing cardiogenic shock and death. Submassive (intermediate-risk) PE - 20-25% of cases, RV dysfunction despite normal systemic arterial pressure; right heart failure with biomarker (troponin) release marks high risk of deterioration. Low-risk PE - about 65-75% of cases, excellent prognosis. (Wells clinical decision rules in Table 290-2 stratify pretest probability of DVT and PE rather than anatomic severity.)","references":["Harrison 22e p.2154","Harrison 22e p.2155","Harrison 22e p.2156","Harrison 22e p.2157","Harrison 22e p.2158","Harrison 22e p.2159","Harrison 22e p.2160","Harrison 22e p.2161","Harrison 22e p.2162","Harrison 22e p.2163"],"crossLinks":[]},"delirium":{"name":"Delirium","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute confusional state","acute brain syndrome","toxic-metabolic encephalopathy (psychiatric framing)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acute, fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness accompanied by additional cognitive changes such as disorientation, memory deficit, or perceptual disturbance","Onset is acute (hours to days) and the course typically fluctuates over the day, often with worsening in the evening and at night (sundowning)","It is a direct physiological consequence of a medical condition, substance intoxication or withdrawal, medication, or multiple causes, and is best regarded as acute brain failure","Recognised as hyperactive, hypoactive, or mixed; the hypoactive form is easily missed and carries a worse prognosis","The essential intervention is identifying and treating the underlying cause and reviewing offending medications, together with supportive care","Non-pharmacological measures are first-line: reorientation, sensory aids, sleep-wake regulation, mobilisation, hydration, and a calm familiar environment","Antipsychotics are reserved for severe agitation or distress that threatens safety, at the lowest effective level, while benzodiazepines are generally avoided except in alcohol or sedative withdrawal","Distinguish from dementia (chronic, stable, with preserved alertness early) and from primary psychiatric illness; delirium can be superimposed on dementia"],"pathophysiology":"Delirium reflects diffuse, usually reversible cerebral dysfunction arising from systemic insults. Proposed mechanisms include neurotransmitter imbalance (notably cholinergic deficiency and dopaminergic excess), neuroinflammation, impaired oxidative metabolism, and disruption of neuronal networks supporting attention and arousal. Common precipitants include infection, hypoxia, metabolic and electrolyte derangements, drug effects and withdrawal, and the disorder is often multifactorial in vulnerable individuals such as the elderly.","additionalDifferentials":["Dementia and other major neurocognitive disorders","Depression, especially where hypoactive delirium mimics low mood","Acute psychosis or mania","Non-convulsive status epilepticus","Wernicke encephalopathy","Alcohol or sedative withdrawal states","Focal neurological events such as stroke"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bedside attention testing and validated delirium screening instruments","Search for precipitants: infection screen, oxygenation, glucose, electrolytes, renal and liver function","Medication review for deliriogenic drugs","Neuroimaging when a structural cause is suspected","EEG if non-convulsive seizures are considered"],"redFlags":["Underlying life-threatening cause such as sepsis, hypoxia, or hypoglycaemia","Sudden focal neurological signs suggesting stroke or intracranial pathology","Delirium tremens or severe withdrawal states","Profound hypoactive delirium with reduced consciousness and aspiration risk"],"prognosis":"Delirium often resolves once the precipitant is treated, but it is associated with prolonged hospital stay, functional decline, increased risk of subsequent dementia, and higher mortality, particularly in older and hypoactive cases. Recovery can be incomplete and protracted in vulnerable patients.","pitfalls":["Missing hypoactive delirium and mislabelling it as depression or fatigue","Attributing acute confusion to dementia and failing to seek a reversible cause","Using benzodiazepines routinely and worsening confusion (outside alcohol or sedative withdrawal)","Sedating agitation without identifying and treating the underlying medical trigger","Overlooking medication and polypharmacy as precipitants"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"delirium_postoperative":{"name":"Postoperative delirium","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["POD","postoperative confusion","acute postoperative brain dysfunction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acute, fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness developing after surgery, often within the first few postoperative days","It may be hyperactive with agitation, hypoactive with withdrawal and drowsiness (frequently missed), or mixed, and the hypoactive form carries a worse prognosis","Key predisposing factors include older age, pre-existing cognitive impairment or dementia, frailty, sensory impairment, and multiple comorbidities","Common precipitants include pain, infection, metabolic and electrolyte disturbance, hypoxia, dehydration, urinary retention, constipation, and deliriogenic medications","Prevention through multicomponent non-pharmacological measures (reorientation, early mobilisation, sleep hygiene, sensory aids, hydration, and pain control) is the most effective strategy","Screening tools such as the Confusion Assessment Method aid recognition, and diagnosis requires distinguishing delirium from dementia and depression","Management focuses on identifying and treating underlying causes and using supportive measures; antipsychotics are reserved for severe agitation posing a safety risk","Benzodiazepines and anticholinergic drugs generally worsen delirium and should be minimised except for specific indications such as alcohol withdrawal"],"pathophysiology":"Postoperative delirium reflects acute global brain dysfunction arising from the interaction of a vulnerable brain and perioperative stressors. Proposed mechanisms include neuroinflammation from the surgical stress response, neurotransmitter imbalance (notably cholinergic deficiency and dopamine excess), impaired cerebral oxygen delivery, disrupted sleep-wake regulation, and the effects of sedative and analgesic drugs. Patients with reduced cognitive reserve are more susceptible, so relatively minor insults can precipitate delirium. The condition is typically multifactorial, and cumulative contributions from pain, infection, metabolic derangement, and medications drive the acute confusional state.","additionalDifferentials":["Dementia or pre-existing cognitive impairment","Depression or acute psychiatric illness","Non-convulsive seizure activity","Stroke or other acute neurological event","Drug or alcohol withdrawal","Residual anaesthetic or sedative effect"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Delirium screening with a validated tool such as the Confusion Assessment Method","Screening for infection, hypoxia, and metabolic or electrolyte disturbance","Medication review for deliriogenic drugs","Assessment for urinary retention, constipation, and pain","Neuroimaging or EEG only if a focal neurological cause or seizures are suspected"],"redFlags":["Sudden confusion with focal neurological signs suggesting a stroke","Delirium accompanied by hypoxia, sepsis, or severe metabolic derangement","Features suggesting alcohol or sedative withdrawal with autonomic instability","Severe agitation posing a risk of harm to the patient or others"],"prognosis":"Delirium is often reversible when precipitants are treated, but it is associated with longer hospital stay, functional decline, increased risk of long-term cognitive impairment, and higher mortality, particularly in the hypoactive form.","pitfalls":["Missing hypoactive delirium because the patient is quiet rather than agitated","Mislabelling delirium as dementia and failing to seek reversible causes","Using benzodiazepines or anticholinergics that worsen confusion","Reaching for antipsychotics before addressing underlying precipitants","Neglecting non-pharmacological preventive measures in high-risk patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"dementia_with_lewy_bodies":{"name":"Dementia with Lewy Bodies","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DLB","Lewy body disease","Lewy body dementia"],"chapter":445,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3491","clinicalPearls":["DLB and Parkinson's disease dementia (PDD) are viewed as points along a single spectrum of Lewy body disease (LBD); LBD is the second most common neurodegenerative dementia after Alzheimer's disease (Harrison 22e p.3491).","The timing between parkinsonism and dementia distinguishes the two: when dementia and neuropsychiatric symptoms precede or emerge alongside parkinsonism, the diagnosis is DLB; when dementia appears in a patient with long-standing PD, it is PDD (Harrison 22e p.3492).","Core diagnostic features of DLB are early psychosis with well-formed visual hallucinations, fluctuating cognition, REM sleep behavior disorder (RBD), and parkinsonism (Harrison 22e p.3491).","The cognitive profile shows severe executive, attentional, and visuospatial deficits with relatively preserved episodic memory; prominent early episodic memory impairment instead suggests comorbid Alzheimer pathology (Harrison 22e p.3491-3492).","Fluctuations in attention and concentration can be marked, with short episodes of confusion or severe lethargy that may resolve rapidly (Harrison 22e p.3492).","Patients are highly sensitive to infectious and metabolic disturbances, and delirium precipitated by infection, a new medication, or systemic illness can be the first manifestation (Harrison 22e p.3492).","RBD, in which dream enactment occurs because normal dreaming muscle atonia is lost (sometimes causing injury to patient or bed partner), is a characteristic and often prodromal feature that may precede the syndrome by years (Harrison 22e p.3492).","A sense of a presence behind the person may precede well-formed hallucinations; delusions are less frequent and usually involve misidentification, infidelity, theft, or persecution (Harrison 22e p.3491-3492).","Autonomic and nonmotor features such as anosmia, constipation, depression, anxiety, orthostatic hypotension, and erectile dysfunction may appear early (Harrison 22e p.3492).","Incidence of DLB is roughly 7 per 100,000 person-years; prevalence of both DLB and PDD rises with age and both are more common in men (Harrison 22e p.3491).","About 10% of PD patients develop PDD per year, with most eventually progressing to dementia over time (Harrison 22e p.3491).","A profound cholinergic deficit from basal forebrain and pedunculopontine nucleus involvement underlies the fluctuations, inattention, and visual hallucinations (Harrison 22e p.3492)."],"pathophysiology":"DLB is a synucleinopathy: the defining neuropathologic lesions are Lewy bodies and Lewy neurites distributed through specific brainstem nuclei, the substantia nigra, amygdala, cingulate gyrus, and ultimately the neocortex. Lewy bodies are intraneuronal cytoplasmic inclusions composed of straight 7-20 nm neurofilaments with surrounding amorphous material; they stain with PAS and ubiquitin and are now identified by antibodies to the presynaptic protein alpha-synuclein. Accumulation of alpha-synuclein aggregates in neurons and glia classifies DLB and PDD molecularly as synucleinopathies, though neuronal and synaptic loss (rather than Lewy pathology itself) best predicts clinical deficits. Pathology progresses through three stages: brainstem-predominant, transitional limbic, and diffuse neocortical. Disease is thought to propagate via a prion-like mechanism in which misfolded alpha-synuclein spreads transneuronally along connection pathways; PD often begins in the enteric nervous system and ascends via the vagus nerve, and/or in the olfactory bulb. A profound cholinergic deficit from basal forebrain and pedunculopontine nucleus loss drives fluctuations, inattention, and hallucinations, while locus coeruleus adrenergic deficits impair arousal and alerting. Both genetic and environmental factors contribute; alpha-synuclein gene duplications/triplications and GBA (glucocerebrosidase) mutations are linked to PDD/DLB presentations, supporting a double-hit hypothesis of toxins acting on a susceptible genetic background (Harrison 22e p.3492).","additionalDifferentials":["Parkinson's disease dementia (PDD)","Alzheimer's disease (and DLB with co-existing AD pathology)","Multiple system atrophy (MSA)","Vascular parkinsonism / vascular cognitive impairment","Delirium from drugs, infection, or metabolic disturbance"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Multiple system atrophy, in which autonomic disturbance occurs earlier and is more severe and cognition is relatively preserved (anosmia favors LBD over MSA)","Alzheimer's disease, suggested by prominent early episodic memory and semantic naming impairment","Vascular parkinsonism or subdural hematoma identifiable on MRI","Drug-induced, infectious, or metabolic encephalopathy producing reversible cognitive change"],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI to exclude vascular parkinsonism or subdural hematoma and to look for the MSA 'hot-cross buns' pontine sign","Polysomnography demonstrating REM sleep behavior disorder without atonia","Seed amplification assays (e.g., RT-QuIC) detecting alpha-synuclein in CSF","Skin biopsy showing cutaneous alpha-synuclein deposition","I-123 MIBG cardiac scintigraphy showing postganglionic sympathetic denervation","Dopamine transporter imaging by SPECT or PET showing reduced striatal (particularly putaminal) dopamine transporter","FDG-PET showing occipital hypometabolism with relative posterior cingulate sparing (cingulate island sign)","CSF or blood phospho-tau217/phospho-tau181 when concurrent Alzheimer pathology is suspected"],"redFlags":["New-onset delirium precipitated by infection, a new medicine, or systemic illness as the presenting feature","Severe sensitivity to dopaminergic medications and to typical neuroleptics, the latter risking neuroleptic malignant syndrome and death","Recurrent falls from early postural instability and orthostatic hypotension causing syncope","Violent dream enactment in RBD causing injury to patient or bed partner"],"prognosis":"DLB is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder with no currently available disease-modifying therapy; only symptomatic management exists. Severity of behavioral, sleep, and autonomic symptoms correlates with substantial caregiver burden, depression, and reduced caregiver quality of life (Harrison 22e p.3492-3493).","pitfalls":["Misclassifying DLB as Alzheimer's disease because frequent AD co-pathology makes prediction of underlying disease challenging; prominent early episodic memory loss points to comorbid AD","Confusing DLB with MSA when autonomic features appear early; MSA autonomic dysfunction is earlier and more severe with relatively preserved cognition","Exposing patients to typical neuroleptics, anticholinergics, or dopamine agonists, which can trigger neuroleptic malignant syndrome or worsen symptoms","Overlooking that scattered Lewy body pathology in substantia nigra, amygdala, or olfactory bulb can be an incidental finding in healthy older individuals","Attributing fluctuating confusion solely to delirium and missing the underlying DLB diagnosis"],"severityClassification":"Neuropathologic staging recognizes three stages of LBD progression: (1) brainstem-predominant, (2) transitional limbic, and (3) diffuse neocortical. Both DLB and PDD have a prodromal mild cognitive impairment (MCI) phase; prodromal MCI-Lewy body (MCI-LB) is distinguished from prodromal MCI-AD by clinical and biomarker features (Harrison 22e p.3492).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3491","Harrison 22e p.3492","Harrison 22e p.3493"],"crossLinks":[]},"dengue_hemorrhagic_fever":{"name":"Dengue haemorrhagic fever","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DHF","severe dengue","dengue shock syndrome","DSS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A severe form of dengue, a mosquito-borne flaviviral infection spread by Aedes aegypti, with recurrent large outbreaks across India","The defining features are plasma leakage (haemoconcentration, effusions, ascites), bleeding tendency and thrombocytopenia, which can progress to dengue shock syndrome","The critical, most dangerous period begins around the time of defervescence, when capillary leak peaks, so patients can deteriorate just as fever settles","WHO warning signs (abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, mucosal bleeding, lethargy, hepatomegaly, fluid accumulation and a rising haematocrit with falling platelets) flag patients at risk of severe disease","Secondary infection with a different dengue serotype increases the risk of severe disease through antibody-dependent enhancement","Management is meticulous fluid balance guided by haematocrit and clinical status; there is no specific antiviral, and both under- and over-resuscitation are harmful","Platelet transfusion is not indicated purely for a low count and should be reserved for significant bleeding","Diagnosis uses NS1 antigen early and IgM/IgG serology or PCR, with serial full blood counts to track haematocrit and platelets"],"pathophysiology":"Following an infective Aedes bite, dengue virus replicates and causes a systemic febrile illness. In severe dengue an intense host immune and cytokine response transiently increases vascular permeability, allowing plasma to leak from the intravascular compartment; this manifests as haemoconcentration, serous effusions and, if severe, hypovolaemic shock. Concurrent thrombocytopenia, platelet dysfunction, coagulopathy and vascular fragility produce the bleeding diathesis. Pre-existing heterotypic antibodies from a prior infection can enhance viral uptake into cells (antibody-dependent enhancement), heightening the risk of severe disease on secondary infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Other viral haemorrhagic fevers","Chikungunya and Zika co-circulating arboviral illness","Severe malaria","Leptospirosis","Enteric fever","Rickettsial infection (e.g. scrub typhus)"],"infectionMimics":["Sepsis with disseminated intravascular coagulation","Acute leukaemia or other causes of thrombocytopenia with bleeding","Immune thrombocytopenia","Hantavirus or other capillary-leak syndromes"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["NS1 antigen in early illness and dengue IgM/IgG serology or RT-PCR","Serial full blood counts monitoring haematocrit and platelet trends","Coagulation profile and liver enzymes to assess severity","Ultrasound to detect plasma leakage (ascites, pleural effusion, gallbladder wall oedema)","Blood grouping and cross-match if significant bleeding occurs"],"redFlags":["WHO warning signs: severe abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, mucosal bleeding, restlessness or lethargy, and clinical fluid accumulation","Rising haematocrit with a rapidly falling platelet count indicating intensifying plasma leak","Signs of shock (narrow pulse pressure, cold clammy peripheries, tachycardia, hypotension) marking dengue shock syndrome","Deterioration around defervescence, the classic timing of the critical phase"],"prognosis":"With early recognition and careful fluid management the great majority recover fully. Untreated or poorly managed severe dengue with prolonged shock or major haemorrhage carries significant mortality, but timely, judicious supportive care dramatically reduces death rates.","pitfalls":["Assuming a patient is improving when fever settles, missing the onset of the critical plasma-leak phase","Over-transfusing platelets based on the count alone rather than for clinical bleeding","Excessive intravenous fluids causing fluid overload and respiratory compromise, or inadequate fluids allowing shock","Giving NSAIDs or other agents that worsen bleeding and gastric irritation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"dental_caries":{"name":"Dental caries","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tooth decay","cavities","carious lesion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caries is a chronic, biofilm-mediated disease driven by repeated acid production from fermentable carbohydrates, not a simple one-off infection","Early lesions appear as chalky white spots that represent subsurface demineralisation and can still remineralise before cavitation occurs","Pit-and-fissure surfaces, interproximal contacts, and exposed root surfaces in older patients are the sites most prone to decay","Symptoms often lag behind disease; sensitivity to sweet, cold, or hot stimuli suggests progression toward the pulp","Bitewing radiographs are key for detecting interproximal lesions that are hidden on visual examination","Management balances remineralisation strategies (fluoride, diet control, oral hygiene) for early lesions against operative restoration once cavitation is established","High caries risk should prompt assessment of salivary flow, diet, fluoride exposure, and plaque control rather than only drilling and filling","Untreated caries is the usual precursor to pulpitis, periapical infection, and eventual tooth loss"],"pathophysiology":"Dental caries results from an ecological shift in the dental plaque biofilm favouring acidogenic and aciduric bacteria such as mutans streptococci and lactobacilli. Frequent intake of fermentable sugars fuels bacterial acid production, lowering plaque pH below the critical threshold and driving demineralisation of enamel hydroxyapatite. When acid attacks outpace the buffering and remineralising capacity of saliva and fluoride, subsurface mineral loss progresses to surface cavitation. The lesion then advances through dentine, where tubular structure allows faster spread, ultimately reaching and infecting the pulp if untreated.","additionalDifferentials":["Dental erosion from intrinsic or extrinsic acids","Enamel hypoplasia or developmental defects","Dentine hypersensitivity from gingival recession","Fluorosis or intrinsic staining mimicking lesions","Cracked tooth or fracture line"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Visual-tactile examination with good lighting and drying of surfaces","Bitewing and periapical radiographs for interproximal and root lesions","Caries risk assessment including diet and salivary evaluation","Pulp sensibility testing when pulpal involvement is suspected","Adjunctive detection methods (transillumination, laser fluorescence) where available"],"redFlags":["Spreading facial swelling, fever, or trismus indicating odontogenic infection extending beyond the tooth","Rampant or early childhood caries suggesting major dietary or salivary dysfunction","Signs of pulpal necrosis or periapical involvement requiring urgent endodontic assessment"],"prognosis":"Early non-cavitated lesions can be arrested or reversed with preventive measures, while cavitated lesions require restoration but carry a good prognosis when risk factors are controlled; neglected disease progresses to pulpitis, abscess, and tooth loss.","pitfalls":["Restoring cavities without addressing underlying diet and hygiene, leading to recurrent decay","Overlooking interproximal or hidden root-surface lesions on clinical inspection alone","Mistaking arrested or remineralised lesions for active disease and over-treating","Ignoring salivary hypofunction as a driver of rapid caries progression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"dental_trauma":{"name":"Dental trauma","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["traumatic dental injury","tooth injury","dentoalveolar trauma","avulsion and luxation injuries"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Dental trauma ranges from enamel fractures through crown-root fractures to luxation and complete avulsion, and injuries frequently coexist","The maxillary central incisors are the teeth most commonly injured, especially in children and young adults","Avulsion of a permanent tooth is a time-critical emergency, as the survival of the periodontal ligament falls rapidly the longer the tooth is out of the socket and dry","An avulsed permanent tooth should ideally be reimplanted promptly or stored in a suitable physiological medium such as milk or saliva rather than water","Avulsed primary teeth are generally not reimplanted because of the risk of damaging the developing permanent successor","Pulp status can change over time after luxation injuries, so baseline and repeated sensibility testing and follow-up are essential","Ellis-type crown fractures involving dentine or pulp need timely protection or pulp management to preserve vitality","Always assess for associated alveolar, jaw, and soft-tissue injuries, and consider non-accidental injury when the history is inconsistent"],"pathophysiology":"Dental trauma results from a direct or indirect force exceeding the resistance of the tooth and its supporting structures. Depending on the vector and magnitude, energy is dissipated as enamel or dentine fracture, pulp exposure, displacement of the tooth within or out of its socket (luxation or avulsion), or fracture of the alveolar bone. Injury to the neurovascular supply at the apex can lead to reversible or irreversible pulpal damage and later necrosis, while disruption of the periodontal ligament and its cells governs the likelihood of successful healing after repositioning or reimplantation. Extra-oral drying and delay compromise ligament cell viability and predispose to replacement resorption.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated alveolar fracture","Mandibular or midface fracture","Pre-existing caries or fracture unrelated to the acute event","Soft-tissue laceration without dental injury","Non-accidental injury in vulnerable patients"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Systematic clinical examination of teeth, occlusion, and soft tissues","Pulp sensibility testing at baseline and on follow-up","Periapical and occlusal radiographs, with further imaging for suspected jaw fractures","Assessment of tooth mobility, displacement, and percussion note","Documentation of injury time and storage medium for avulsed teeth"],"redFlags":["Airway compromise, significant haemorrhage, or associated head and facial injuries requiring emergency management","Signs of jaw fracture such as malocclusion, trismus, or step deformity","Inconsistent history or injury pattern suggesting non-accidental injury","Aspirated or unaccounted-for tooth fragments needing chest imaging"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on injury type, maturity of the tooth, and speed of appropriate care; minor fractures and promptly managed luxations often do well, whereas avulsions with prolonged dry time and mature apices frequently develop pulp necrosis or root resorption.","pitfalls":["Storing an avulsed tooth in water or letting it dry, damaging periodontal ligament cells","Reimplanting avulsed primary teeth and harming the permanent successor","Assuming an initially vital tooth will remain so and skipping follow-up sensibility testing","Focusing on the obvious dental injury and missing alveolar or jaw fractures or non-accidental injury"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"dermatitis_herpetiformis":{"name":"Dermatitis Herpetiformis","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DH","Duhring's disease","cutaneous coeliac disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with intensely itchy, grouped (herpetiform) vesicles and papules symmetrically distributed over the extensor surfaces such as elbows, knees, buttocks, and scalp","The itch and burning are often so severe that intact blisters are rarely seen, replaced by excoriations and crusts from scratching","It is the cutaneous expression of gluten sensitivity and is strongly associated with coeliac disease, although many patients have few or no bowel symptoms","Diagnosis rests on skin biopsy for direct immunofluorescence, which shows granular IgA deposits in the dermal papillae of perilesional skin","Supportive serology includes anti-tissue transglutaminase and anti-endomysial antibodies, reflecting the shared autoimmunity with coeliac disease","A strict lifelong gluten-free diet is the cornerstone of management and addresses both the skin and the associated enteropathy","A sulphone-class agent can rapidly control itch and blistering while the diet takes effect, but requires screening for enzyme deficiency and monitoring for haemolysis","Screen for associated conditions including autoimmune thyroid disease and be aware of a small increased long-term risk of small-bowel lymphoma, which dietary control reduces"],"pathophysiology":"Dermatitis herpetiformis is an autoimmune blistering disease driven by gluten sensitivity in genetically predisposed individuals carrying HLA-DQ2 or DQ8. Dietary gluten triggers production of IgA antibodies against tissue transglutaminase and, in the skin, cross-reactivity with epidermal transglutaminase. IgA immune complexes deposit in the dermal papillae, recruiting neutrophils that form the characteristic papillary microabscesses and cause subepidermal separation with vesicle formation. The same gluten-driven autoimmunity produces the small-bowel villous atrophy of coeliac disease, so skin and gut manifestations share a common mechanism and both respond to gluten withdrawal.","additionalDifferentials":["Bullous pemphigoid","Linear IgA bullous dermatosis","Scabies (intensely itchy, but with burrows and different distribution)","Atopic or other eczematous dermatitis","Papular urticaria or insect-bite reactions","Excoriated prurigo"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy of perilesional skin for direct immunofluorescence showing granular papillary IgA (the diagnostic standard)","Coeliac serology including anti-tissue transglutaminase and anti-endomysial IgA antibodies","Total IgA level to interpret serology correctly","Consideration of small-bowel evaluation in conjunction with gastroenterology","Screening for enzyme deficiency before starting sulphone therapy and for associated autoimmune disease, such as thyroid function"],"redFlags":["New abdominal symptoms, weight loss, or anaemia raising concern for complications of coeliac disease including small-bowel lymphoma","Signs of haemolysis or methaemoglobinaemia in a patient on sulphone therapy","Widespread blistering with systemic upset prompting reconsideration of an alternative autoimmune bullous disease","Features of malabsorption or nutritional deficiency requiring investigation"],"prognosis":"Dermatitis herpetiformis is a chronic condition, but a strict gluten-free diet usually controls the skin disease, allows medication to be reduced or stopped over time, heals the associated enteropathy, and lowers the long-term lymphoma risk; lapses in the diet typically provoke relapse.","pitfalls":["Taking the biopsy for immunofluorescence from lesional rather than perilesional skin and obtaining a false-negative result","Relying on the diet alone while overlooking the need to screen for and manage associated coeliac and autoimmune disease","Starting sulphone therapy without checking for enzyme deficiency and monitoring blood counts","Attributing the intense itch to eczema or scabies and missing the diagnosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"dermatomyositis":{"name":"Dermatomyositis","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DM","idiopathic inflammatory myopathy","amyopathic dermatomyositis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Combines symmetric proximal muscle weakness with distinctive skin findings, though skin disease can precede, follow, or occur without myositis","Characteristic rashes include the heliotrope discolouration of the eyelids, Gottron papules over the knuckles, the shawl and V-signs, and periungual telangiectasia","An amyopathic (clinically hypomyopathic) form has typical skin changes with little or no measurable muscle weakness","Elevated creatine kinase, myopathic EMG, and muscle MRI support the diagnosis; biopsy shows perifascicular atrophy and perivascular inflammation","There is a well-recognised association with occult malignancy, so age-appropriate cancer screening is essential, particularly around diagnosis","Interstitial lung disease is common and may be rapidly progressive, especially with anti-MDA5 antibodies, which also carry a characteristic cutaneous ulcerating phenotype","Myositis-specific antibodies (e.g. anti-Mi-2, anti-TIF1-gamma, anti-MDA5, antisynthetase) predict distinct patterns of skin, lung, and malignancy risk","Treatment uses corticosteroids with steroid-sparing immunosuppressants, photoprotection and topical therapy for skin, and prompt management of lung involvement"],"pathophysiology":"Dermatomyositis is an idiopathic inflammatory myopathy characterised by humorally mediated, complement-dependent microangiopathy. Immune complex deposition and complement activation (membrane attack complex) on endomysial capillaries cause capillary loss, ischaemia, and the perifascicular atrophy typical of muscle biopsy. A strong type I interferon signature is present in muscle and skin. The same immune-mediated vasculopathy affects skin and other organs, and paraneoplastic mechanisms link it to underlying malignancy in a substantial proportion of adults.","additionalDifferentials":["Polymyositis and immune-mediated necrotising myopathy","Systemic lupus erythematosus and other connective tissue diseases with rash","Cutaneous drug eruptions and photosensitive dermatoses","Psoriasis or contact dermatitis (for Gottron/knuckle lesions)","Inclusion body myositis","Thyroid and other metabolic myopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase and myositis antibody panel (anti-Mi-2, anti-TIF1-gamma, anti-MDA5, antisynthetase)","Electromyography and muscle MRI to localise and characterise inflammation","Muscle and/or skin biopsy demonstrating characteristic histology","High-resolution CT chest and pulmonary function tests for interstitial lung disease","Age- and risk-appropriate malignancy screening at diagnosis and during follow-up"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive interstitial lung disease, particularly with anti-MDA5 antibodies, which can be life-threatening","Clinical features pointing to an underlying malignancy (weight loss, new mass, cytopenias)","Dysphagia and respiratory muscle weakness with aspiration or ventilatory risk","Cutaneous ulceration and severe vasculopathy associated with anti-MDA5 disease","Cardiac involvement with arrhythmia or heart failure"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends heavily on associated interstitial lung disease, malignancy, and cardiac involvement rather than the myositis itself. Skin and muscle disease often respond to immunosuppression, but rapidly progressive lung disease and paraneoplastic cases carry significant mortality.","pitfalls":["Failing to perform malignancy screening in newly diagnosed adults","Overlooking amyopathic disease when weakness is absent despite classic skin findings","Missing or underestimating rapidly progressive lung disease, especially with anti-MDA5 antibodies","Undertreating skin disease and neglecting photoprotection","Assuming normal creatine kinase excludes active or amyopathic disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"dermatophytosis":{"name":"Dermatophytosis","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ringworm","tinea","dermatophyte infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A superficial fungal infection of keratinised tissue named by body site: tinea corporis (body), cruris (groin), pedis (feet), capitis (scalp), unguium (nails), and faciei (face)","The classic lesion is an annular, scaly plaque with a raised, advancing, more inflamed edge and relative central clearing","Confirm the diagnosis before treatment with skin scrapings, hair, or nail clippings for microscopy and culture, as morphology alone can mislead","Prior use of topical corticosteroids alters the appearance (tinea incognito), masking scale and the active edge and delaying diagnosis","Localised skin infection responds to topical antifungals, whereas tinea capitis and onychomycosis require systemic antifungal therapy because topical agents cannot penetrate hair and nail adequately","Tinea capitis is largely a disease of children and can cause a boggy inflamed mass (kerion) with a risk of scarring alopecia if treatment is delayed","Address sources and reservoirs: treat tinea pedis to prevent recurrent groin and body infection, and consider animal contacts for zoophilic species","Consider underlying immunosuppression or diabetes in extensive, recurrent, or unusually severe infection"],"pathophysiology":"Dermatophytosis is caused by dermatophyte fungi of the genera Trichophyton, Microsporum, and Epidermophyton, which possess keratinases enabling them to invade and digest keratin in the stratum corneum, hair, and nails. Infection is acquired from other people (anthropophilic), animals (zoophilic), or soil (geophilic), with zoophilic species often producing more inflammatory reactions. The fungus spreads centrifugally through the stratum corneum, producing the characteristic annular lesion with an active advancing scaly margin. The host inflammatory response accounts for erythema and, in strongly inflammatory infections such as kerion, pustulation and potential scarring. Warmth, moisture, occlusion, and impaired immunity favour infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Nummular (discoid) eczema","Psoriasis","Pityriasis rosea","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Granuloma annulare (for annular non-scaly lesions)","Erythrasma or candidal intertrigo in flexural disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin scrapings, hair, or nail clippings for direct microscopy with potassium hydroxide and fungal culture","Wood's lamp examination, which fluoresces in certain Microsporum scalp infections","Nail sampling for onychomycosis before starting prolonged systemic therapy","Consideration of underlying diabetes or immunosuppression in extensive or recurrent disease","Skin biopsy with fungal stains in diagnostically difficult cases"],"redFlags":["Kerion or severe inflammatory tinea capitis risking permanent scarring alopecia","Extensive, widespread, or rapidly progressive infection suggesting immunosuppression","Secondary bacterial infection of macerated or fissured lesions","Deep or invasive fungal infection in an immunocompromised patient"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent with correct diagnosis and appropriate topical or systemic antifungal therapy; nail infections require prolonged treatment and relapse is common, while delayed treatment of inflammatory scalp disease can leave permanent scarring alopecia.","pitfalls":["Treating with topical corticosteroids and producing tinea incognito that obscures the diagnosis","Failing to confirm the diagnosis mycologically before long courses of systemic antifungals","Using topical-only therapy for scalp or nail infection, which will not eradicate it","Neglecting reservoirs such as untreated tinea pedis or animal contacts, leading to recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"desensitization":{"name":"Desensitization","system":"Immunologic/Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Drug desensitization","Rapid drug desensitization","Temporary drug tolerance induction"],"chapter":365,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2814","clinicalPearls":["Drug desensitization (DD) is a temporary immunotherapy that delivers sequential, incremental small doses of a culprit drug over hours to safely reach the full therapeutic dose, taking advantage of mast cell/basophil inhibitory pathways rather than producing lasting tolerance.","Tolerance achieved by DD is transient: it does not produce sustained tolerance and must be repeated for each new course, or when the interval between doses equals two or more drug half-lives; continued exposure (e.g., ongoing antibiotics or aspirin/COX-1 inhibitors) can maintain the desensitized state.","Roughly 10-20% of the health-care-engaged population claims a penicillin allergy, but most labels are unconfirmed; low-risk patients can be delabeled, and single-dose oral challenges have been shown safe in low-risk children and adults.","Serum tryptase is the specific biomarker of anaphylaxis: a level above the normal range (11.4 ng/mL) or above a patient's baseline (a 1.6-fold rise), measured within 30 min to 4 h of symptom onset, supports type I or mixed reactions.","A baseline tryptase of 7.5 ng/mL or higher may signal hereditary alpha tryptasemia (HαT) from TPSAB1 gene duplication on chromosome 16, an autosomal dominant trait in 4-6% of Caucasians that can worsen anaphylaxis severity.","Cytokine release reactions (CRRs) present with fever, chills, and back/chest/pelvic pain and headache with a transient IL-6 rise; mixed reactions combine type I and CRR features with both tryptase and IL-6 elevations.","The most widely used standardized protocol uses three bags (1/100, 1/10, and undiluted target concentration) with 12 doubling steps every 15 min, reaching target dose in about 5.7 h, adaptable to IV, oral, SC, IM, intraperitoneal, intraocular, and intrathecal routes.","Breakthrough reactions occur in roughly 10-30% of protocols, are usually mild and resemble the initial reaction, and do not prevent completion of DD in about 99% of cases; no deaths from DD have been reported.","Corticosteroids do NOT protect against anaphylaxis during desensitization; omalizumab (anti-IgE) has been used as an adjuvant in highly sensitized patients with severe IgE-mediated reactions.","Skin testing reliability falls with greater time since the index reaction and after severe anaphylaxis (mediator depletion can cause false negatives); anaphylaxis to penicillin or carboplatin has not been reported in skin-test-negative patients.","Platinum-drug IgE sensitization usually requires six or more exposures (fewer in BRCA-mutation carriers); skin testing is positive in over 85% of patients with recent reactions, and reexposure of skin-test-positive patients carries high anaphylaxis risk."],"pathophysiology":"Drug hypersensitivity reactions are classified by timing, mechanism (endotype), and biomarkers. Type I reactions arise from mast cell and basophil activation, either IgE-dependent (requiring prior repeated sensitizing exposures, as with antibiotics, chemotherapy, and some biologics) or IgE-independent (first-exposure reactions via complement activation, COX-1 inhibition, or MRGPRX2 receptor activation by quinolones, vancomycin, icatibant, basic compounds, and certain THIQ-motif general anesthetics). Cytokine release reactions reflect T-cell and other immune-cell activation with transient IL-6 elevation. Delayed type IV reactions are T-cell mediated. Desensitization works because rapid, stepwise delivery of incremental antigen doses recruits inhibitory phosphatases to the IgE receptor, blocking signal transduction and mediator release so that the target dose can be reached without anaphylaxis; desensitized patients show transient conversion of skin tests from positive to negative. Mechanisms for non-IgE phenotypes are less well understood.","additionalDifferentials":["Type I IgE-mediated drug allergy","IgE-independent mast cell activation (complement-mediated, COX-1 inhibition, MRGPRX2-mediated)","Cytokine release reaction (IL-6 driven)","Mixed reaction (combined type I and CRR features)","Delayed type IV maculopapular eruption","Hereditary alpha tryptasemia","Infusion reaction (non-allergic)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Non-allergic infusion reaction","Anxiety/vasovagal episode during infusion","Kounis syndrome (allergic coronary vasospasm)","Takotsubo cardiomyopathy from drug-induced anaphylaxis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum tryptase within 30 min to 4 h of reaction (and baseline tryptase)","Baseline tryptase with TPSAB1 genotyping if elevated (hereditary alpha tryptasemia)","Serum IL-6 (elevated in CRR and mixed reactions)","Skin testing (epicutaneous/intradermal) for IgE-mediated reactions","Basophil activation test (in vitro evidence of IgE sensitization)","Drug-specific serum IgE (e.g., penicillin, platins; low sensitivity)","Skin patch testing for delayed type IV reactions (high specificity, low sensitivity)","Lymphocyte transformation test for SCARS-causing drugs","HLA genotyping for SCARS risk (abacavir, anticonvulsants, allopurinol, vancomycin)"],"redFlags":["Severe cutaneous adverse reactions (SJS, TEN, DRESS, AGEP) - contraindication to desensitization, avoid the drug","Type II cytotoxic, type III immune-complex, vasculitis, and single-organ toxic reactions - contraindications to DD","Hypotension with cardiovascular collapse, throat tightening, oxygen desaturation (grade 3 anaphylaxis)","Kounis syndrome or takotsubo cardiomyopathy from drug-induced anaphylaxis - contraindications to DD","Oxaliplatin-induced immune-mediated thrombocytopenia with bleeding - precludes further DD","Markedly elevated tryptase well above baseline indicating severe mast cell activation"],"prognosis":"Rapid multistep desensitization is highly effective and safe across thousands of patients with infections, chronic inflammatory diseases, and malignancies. Protocols are completed in roughly 95-100% of cases (taxane series completed 95-100%), breakthrough reactions occur in about 10-30% and are usually mild, and no deaths attributable to DD have been reported. Drug delivery via DD achieves efficacy equal to standard administration (expected clearance of infection, reduced inflammation, comparable cancer responses); survival of platinum-desensitized cancer patients is noninferior to that of nonallergic patients, and ovarian cancer patients undergoing carboplatin DD have shown a 5-year life-expectancy advantage in one large series.","pitfalls":["Assuming corticosteroid premedication prevents anaphylaxis during DD - it does not.","Treating SCARS or other contraindicated phenotypes with DD, where even minute drug amounts can trigger severe reactions.","Expecting durable tolerance - DD is temporary and must be repeated each course or after a gap of two or more half-lives.","Relying on a negative skin test performed long after the index reaction or after severe anaphylaxis (false negatives from mediator depletion or time elapsed).","Maintaining unconfirmed penicillin allergy labels, leading to use of less effective, costlier second-line antibiotics.","Overusing one-bag protocols in highly sensitized type I patients, associated with more epinephrine use and failure to complete treatment.","Missing phenotype switching in oxaliplatin DD (type I converting to CRR/mixed reactions)."],"severityClassification":"Hypersensitivity reactions are graded by organ involvement and vital signs: grade 1 (mild, one organ), grade 2 (moderate, two or more organs), grade 3 (severe, with changes in vital signs/cardiovascular collapse). Reactions are also categorized by timing as acute/immediate (during or within 1-6 h of exposure) versus delayed (6 h to days or weeks), and by endotype (type I IgE/non-IgE, cytokine release, mixed, and type II-IV delayed reactions).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2814","Harrison 22e p.2815","Harrison 22e p.2816","Harrison 22e p.2817","Harrison 22e p.2818","Harrison 22e p.2819"],"crossLinks":[]},"developmental_dysplasia_of_hip":{"name":"Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DDH","congenital dislocation of the hip","hip dysplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A spectrum of abnormal development of the hip ranging from mild acetabular dysplasia to frank dislocation, present at or developing after birth.","Major risk factors include female sex, breech presentation, oligohydramnios, first-born status, and a positive family history.","Newborn screening uses the Barlow manoeuvre (to dislocate a reducible hip) and the Ortolani manoeuvre (to relocate a dislocated hip).","In older infants, signs include limited hip abduction, asymmetric thigh or gluteal skin creases, and apparent limb-length discrepancy (Galeazzi sign).","Ultrasound is the imaging test of choice in early infancy before the femoral head ossifies; plain radiographs become useful after a few months of age.","Early treatment of a reducible hip with an abduction device such as the Pavlik harness is usually successful and avoids surgery.","The later the diagnosis, the more likely that closed or open reduction and casting, or surgical procedures, will be needed.","Untreated or late-treated dysplasia leads to a limp, gait abnormality, and premature degenerative hip arthritis in adulthood.","Persistent clicks alone are usually benign, whereas a palpable 'clunk' of dislocation or relocation is significant."],"pathophysiology":"Laxity of the hip capsule and abnormal relationship between the femoral head and acetabulum, influenced by maternal hormones, intrauterine packing (as in breech position and oligohydramnios), and genetic factors, allow the femoral head to sit incompletely within or outside a shallow acetabulum. Without a well-seated femoral head, the acetabulum fails to develop its normal depth and shape, perpetuating instability and, over time, leading to secondary degenerative change.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign hip clicks","Septic arthritis of the hip","Transient synovitis","Neuromuscular hip dysplasia (e.g. in cerebral palsy or spina bifida)","Proximal focal femoral deficiency or other congenital femoral anomalies","Leg-length discrepancy from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Hip ultrasound in the first months of life","Anteroposterior pelvic radiograph after the femoral head ossifies","Careful serial clinical examination including Barlow, Ortolani, and Galeazzi tests","Targeted imaging for high-risk infants (breech, family history)"],"redFlags":["A frankly dislocated or non-reducible hip on examination","Progressive limitation of abduction","Delayed diagnosis presenting as a limp or abnormal gait in a walking child","Bilateral dislocation, which can be harder to detect due to symmetry","Failure of a hip to stabilise despite bracing"],"prognosis":"When detected and treated in early infancy, most hips develop normally with excellent long-term function; delayed diagnosis increases the need for surgery and the risk of residual dysplasia, avascular necrosis, and early osteoarthritis.","pitfalls":["Missing dysplasia in bilateral cases where symmetry masks the abnormality","Over-relying on benign hip clicks or reassurance from a single normal newborn examination","Not imaging or closely following high-risk infants (breech, family history) with a normal exam","Applying an abduction device too tightly, risking femoral head avascular necrosis","Failing to recognise late-presenting DDH in a child with a limp or waddling gait"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"deviated_nasal_septum":{"name":"Deviated nasal septum","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DNS","nasal septal deviation","septal deviation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A deviated septum is a displacement of the bony or cartilaginous nasal septum from the midline and is extremely common, often asymptomatic","It may be developmental (from differential growth of the septum and palate) or acquired following nasal trauma, sometimes at birth","The dominant symptom is nasal obstruction, which may be unilateral or, with an S-shaped deviation, affect both sides","Deviations take various forms including a spur, a C- or S-shaped bend, or dislocation of the caudal septum into the vestibule","A prominent spur can press on the lateral wall and trigger headache or referred facial pain, and can contribute to recurrent epistaxis over the exposed area","Septal deviation predisposes to sinus drainage problems and recurrent sinusitis and can worsen snoring and obstructive symptoms","Diagnosis rests on anterior rhinoscopy and nasal endoscopy, decongesting the mucosa to distinguish a structural deviation from turbinate hypertrophy","Symptomatic deviation is corrected surgically by septoplasty (or submucous resection), often with turbinate reduction, while asymptomatic deviation needs no treatment"],"pathophysiology":"The nasal septum is formed by the septal cartilage, the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid and the vomer, meeting the maxillary and palatine crests. Deviation arises when these components grow at differing rates or are displaced by trauma, so the septum buckles or angulates away from the midline. The deviation narrows the airway on the concave side and may obstruct the osteomeatal complex, impairing sinus ventilation and mucociliary drainage. Compensatory hypertrophy of the turbinate on the wider side can paradoxically cause obstruction there as well. Mucosa stretched over a spur becomes dry and prone to bleeding, and contact between the deviated septum and the lateral nasal wall can generate pressure-referred headache.","additionalDifferentials":["Inferior turbinate hypertrophy","Nasal polyps","Chronic rhinosinusitis","Allergic or non-allergic rhinitis with mucosal congestion","Nasal valve collapse","Adenoid hypertrophy (particularly in children)","Intranasal mass or tumour causing unilateral obstruction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anterior rhinoscopy after decongestion to assess the septum and turbinates","Nasal endoscopy for a full view of the posterior septum and osteomeatal complex","CT of the paranasal sinuses when sinus disease or surgery is planned","Assessment of nasal airflow and, where indicated, allergy testing to identify a mucosal component","Photographic and functional assessment if external nasal deformity coexists"],"redFlags":["Strictly unilateral obstruction with blood-stained discharge suggesting a tumour rather than simple deviation","A septal swelling after trauma suggesting a septal haematoma or abscess needing urgent drainage","Progressive external deformity, crusting or perforation raising concern for granulomatous or destructive disease","Persistent facial pain or visual symptoms suggesting significant sinus complication"],"prognosis":"Septoplasty relieves obstruction and associated symptoms in most patients with good long-term results, though some residual deviation or persistent turbinate-related congestion may remain; untreated symptomatic deviation can perpetuate sinus problems and sleep-disordered breathing.","pitfalls":["Attributing all nasal obstruction to the septum while overlooking turbinate hypertrophy, polyps or allergy","Operating on an asymptomatic deviation found incidentally","Missing a septal haematoma after trauma, risking cartilage necrosis and saddle deformity","Failing to examine after decongestion, which distinguishes structural from mucosal obstruction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"diabetes_insipidus":{"name":"Diabetes insipidus","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DI","arginine vasopressin deficiency","AVP deficiency (central DI)","AVP resistance (nephrogenic DI)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with persistent polyuria and polydipsia, typically with large volumes of dilute (hypotonic) urine and a strong thirst that drives high fluid intake","The key distinction is between central DI, from deficient vasopressin secretion, and nephrogenic DI, from renal resistance to vasopressin","Primary polydipsia, in which excessive drinking suppresses vasopressin, must be separated from true DI as management differs completely","Serum sodium is often high-normal or elevated in true DI, whereas primary polydipsia tends toward low-normal sodium","A water deprivation test with subsequent desmopressin assesses urinary concentrating ability and response to exogenous hormone","Copeptin measurement, particularly after hypertonic saline stimulation, is increasingly used as a more reliable discriminator","Central DI usually responds to desmopressin, whereas nephrogenic DI is managed by treating the underlying cause and supportive measures","Where thirst perception is intact and access to water is preserved, patients can defend their sodium; the danger is when thirst or water access is impaired"],"pathophysiology":"Diabetes insipidus reflects an inability to concentrate urine appropriately. In central (cranial) DI there is deficient secretion of arginine vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone) due to disease of the hypothalamus or posterior pituitary, such as tumours, infiltrative disease, trauma, or surgery. In nephrogenic DI the kidney fails to respond to circulating vasopressin, from inherited receptor or aquaporin defects, drugs such as lithium, hypercalcaemia, or hypokalaemia. In both, the collecting duct cannot reabsorb enough free water, producing large volumes of dilute urine; if water intake cannot keep pace, plasma osmolality and sodium rise. Primary polydipsia is a separate entity in which excessive fluid intake physiologically suppresses vasopressin.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary (psychogenic or dipsogenic) polydipsia","Osmotic diuresis from uncontrolled diabetes mellitus (hyperglycaemia)","Diuretic use","Hypercalcaemia or hypokalaemia causing acquired nephrogenic DI","Chronic kidney disease with impaired concentrating ability","Post-obstructive or recovery-phase polyuria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Paired serum and urine osmolality with serum sodium","24-hour urine volume to confirm true polyuria","Water deprivation test followed by desmopressin challenge","Plasma copeptin, ideally with hypertonic saline or arginine stimulation","Serum glucose, calcium, and potassium to exclude secondary causes","Pituitary and hypothalamic MRI when central DI is confirmed to seek a structural or infiltrative lesion"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising sodium with altered consciousness from unreplaced free water losses","DI arising after pituitary surgery or head injury, which may fluctuate through a triphasic pattern","Absent or impaired thirst (adipsic DI) predisposing to dangerous hypernatraemia","Loss of a previously present suprasellar lesion signal or new mass suggesting infiltrative or neoplastic disease"],"prognosis":"Central DI is generally well controlled with vasopressin replacement and normal life expectancy when thirst and water access are intact; nephrogenic DI depends on reversibility of the underlying cause, and adipsic forms carry ongoing risk of severe sodium disturbances.","pitfalls":["Confusing osmotic diuresis from hyperglycaemia with true DI","Giving desmopressin to a patient with primary polydipsia and precipitating water intoxication","Restricting fluids in a patient who cannot concentrate urine, causing hypernatraemic dehydration","Overlooking lithium or hypercalcaemia as reversible causes of nephrogenic DI"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"diabetes_mellitus":{"name":"Diabetes Mellitus","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["type 1 diabetes","type 2 diabetes","DM"],"chapter":415,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3195","clinicalPearls":["DM is a group of metabolic disorders sharing the phenotype of hyperglycemia; it is defined as the level of glycemia at which a diabetes-specific complication (e.g., retinopathy) occurs, not as a deviation from a population mean (p.3195, 3197).","Diagnostic criteria (any one, confirmed by repeat testing on a different day unless unequivocal hyperglycemia/acute metabolic decompensation): HbA1c >=6.5% (>=48 mmol/mol); fasting plasma glucose (FPG) >=7.0 mmol/L (126 mg/dL, fasting = no calories >=8 h); 2-h plasma glucose >=11.1 mmol/L (200 mg/dL) on a 75-g OGTT; or a random plasma glucose >=11.1 mmol/L (200 mg/dL) PLUS classic symptoms (polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss) (p.3197).","Prediabetes (each identifies different individuals via different biologic mechanisms): HbA1c 5.7-6.4%; impaired fasting glucose (IFG) FPG 5.6-6.9 mmol/L (100-125 mg/dL) per ADA; impaired glucose tolerance (IGT) 2-h glucose 7.8-11.0 mmol/L (140-199 mg/dL). WHO defines IFG as FPG >6.1 mmol/L (110 mg/dL). All three confer increased risk of type 2 DM and cardiovascular disease (p.3197).","Type 1 DM = autoimmune beta cell destruction with insulin deficiency; 25-50% present with DKA; ~40% of new type 1 DM now has onset after age 30 and is often initially misdiagnosed as type 2. Islet cell autoantibodies (ICAs: insulin, GAD, IA-2, ZnT8) are present in >85% at new onset and define a 3-stage model: stage 1 = >=2 autoantibodies + normoglycemia, stage 2 = autoimmunity + dysglycemia, stage 3 = overt hyperglycemia (p.3199-3201).","Type 2 DM = heterogeneous: insulin resistance + impaired insulin secretion + increased hepatic glucose production; >=80% of patients are obese (central/visceral). Insulin resistance usually precedes the secretory defect, but diabetes appears only once secretion becomes inadequate for the degree of resistance (p.3195, 3201-3202).","Type 2 DM may be asymptomatic for up to a decade before diagnosis, so some patients already have diabetes-specific complications at the time of diagnosis (p.3198, 3221).","HbA1c can be misleading with hemoglobinopathies and varies by ancestry; OGTT is reserved mainly for pregnancy/GDM and cystic fibrosis-related diabetes screening, not routine care (p.3197).","Symptoms of hyperglycemia: polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss, fatigue, weakness, blurred vision (lens water-content change, resolves with control), recurrent superficial infections (vaginitis, fungal skin infections), slow wound healing (p.3203).","Microvascular complications usually do not appear until the second decade of hyperglycemia; macrovascular (ASCVD) risk, driven by insulin resistance and dyslipidemia, may develop before hyperglycemia is established (p.3221).","Distal symmetric polyneuropathy (DSPN) is the most common diabetic neuropathy and occurs in ~50% of long-standing DM; up to 50% are asymptomatic, so annual screening for loss of protective sensation (LOPS) is required - LOPS is a major risk factor for foot ulceration and amputation (p.3226)."],"pathophysiology":"Hyperglycemia results from reduced insulin secretion, decreased peripheral glucose utilization, and/or increased hepatic glucose production, with the relative contribution depending on the DM type. Insulin is produced by pancreatic beta cells (from preproinsulin -> proinsulin -> insulin + C-peptide); glucose is the key secretagogue: it enters the beta cell via GLUT transporters, glucokinase phosphorylation is rate-limiting, glycolysis generates ATP that closes the ATP-sensitive K+ channel (target of sulfonylureas/meglitinides), causing depolarization, calcium influx, and insulin release; incretins (GLP-1, GIP) amplify glucose-stimulated secretion only when glucose is above fasting. Type 1 DM arises from interacting genetic (HLA region accounts for ~50% of risk; DR3-DQ2 and DR4-DQ8 predisposing), environmental, and immunologic factors leading to CD8+ T cell-mediated beta cell destruction and absolute insulin deficiency; alpha-cell dysfunction adds fasting/postprandial hyperglucagonemia with impaired glucagon response to hypoglycemia. Type 2 DM combines impaired insulin secretion, insulin resistance (post-receptor defects in skeletal muscle, liver, fat; lipid intermediate accumulation; low-grade metabolic inflammation), excessive hepatic glucose production (failure to suppress gluconeogenesis), abnormal fat metabolism (increased free fatty acids, altered adipokines, reduced adiponectin), giving the characteristic dyslipidemia (high triglycerides, low HDL, small dense LDL); beta cell function is reduced ~50% at onset and declines progressively. Complications stem from chronic hyperglycemia driving advanced glycosylation end products (AGEs), increased mitochondrial reactive oxygen species, epigenetic changes (\"metabolic memory\"), and growth factors such as VEGF-A in proliferative retinopathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Monogenic diabetes / MODY (autosomal dominant, onset usually <25 y, impaired secretion; HNF-4alpha, glucokinase, HNF-1alpha/1beta mutations; glucokinase MODY = mild stable hyperglycemia; HNF-1alpha MODY often responds to sulfonylureas) (p.3195-3196, 3203)","Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) - glucose intolerance arising in 2nd/3rd trimester; diabetes in 1st trimester is classified as pre-existing pregestational diabetes (p.3196)","Atypical diabetes (features of both type 1 and type 2, not single-gene; includes ketosis-prone diabetes) and latent autoimmune diabetes of adults (5-10% of phenotypic type 2 have GAD/ICA) (p.3196, 3204)","Pancreatogenic (type 3c) DM from pancreatitis, pancreatectomy, cystic fibrosis (CF-related diabetes prevalence ~50%), hemochromatosis (p.3203)","Endocrinopathy-related hyperglycemia: acromegaly, Cushing's syndrome, glucagonoma, pheochromocytoma, somatostatinoma, hyperthyroidism (p.3196)","Drug/chemical-induced diabetes: glucocorticoids, calcineurin/mTOR inhibitors, pentamidine, nicotinic acid, statins, HIV/protease-inhibitor therapy, thiazides, second-generation antipsychotics, immune checkpoint inhibitors (p.3196, 3203)","Neonatal diabetes (onset <6 months; ATP-sensitive K+ channel subunit mutations may respond to sulfonylureas) (p.3196, 3203)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Stress/illness-related transient hyperglycemia from counterregulatory hormone excess (surgery, infection, critical illness) - hyperglycemia in a hospitalized patient need not indicate established DM (p.3217)","Other causes of increased anion-gap acidosis when DKA is suspected: starvation ketosis and alcoholic ketoacidosis (bicarbonate usually >15 meq/L) (p.3216)"],"additionalInvestigations":["HbA1c (lab method NGSP-aligned to DCCT reference) for diagnosis and monitoring; affected by hemoglobinopathies and ancestry (p.3196-3197)","Fasting plasma glucose and 75-g OGTT (OGTT mainly for pregnancy/GDM and CF-related diabetes screening) (p.3197)","Islet cell autoantibodies (GAD, IA-2, ZnT8, insulin) to classify type 1 vs type 2 and identify presymptomatic type 1 DM (p.3200-3201, 3204)","C-peptide as a marker of endogenous insulin secretion (distinguishes endogenous vs exogenous insulin) (p.3198)","Spot urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) plus estimated GFR annually for nephropathy (screen 5 y after type 1 onset and at type 2 diagnosis); albuminuria = UACR >30 mg/g, severe >300 mg/g (p.3224)","Annual dilated retinal exam (or retinal photography) - nondilated primary-care exams are inadequate (p.3224)","Annual foot exam with 10-g monofilament, 128-Hz vibration, ankle reflexes, pedal pulses; annual DSPN screening for LOPS (p.3204, 3226)","In DKA/HHS: serum/plasma beta-hydroxybutyrate (preferred over nitroprusside which detects acetoacetate), glucose, electrolytes with corrected sodium, anion gap, bicarbonate, BUN/creatinine (p.3216)"],"redFlags":["Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA): hyperglycemia (glucose >13.9 mmol/L [250 mg/dL]; may be near-normal = euglycemic DKA, especially on SGLT-2 inhibitors), ketosis, anion-gap metabolic acidosis (bicarbonate <15-18 mmol/L); nausea/vomiting, severe abdominal pain (may mimic acute pancreatitis), Kussmaul respirations, fruity breath, volume depletion, lethargy progressing to coma; cerebral edema is a serious complication mainly in children (p.3216)","Hyperglycemic hyperosmolar state (HHS): older patient, more pronounced dehydration, marked hyperglycemia (often 33.3-66.6 mmol/L [600-1200 mg/dL]) with mental status changes; mortality up to 15% (higher than DKA) (p.3217)","Hypoglycemia (<3.0 mmol/L, <54 mg/dL); hypoglycemia unawareness from autonomic neuropathy raises risk of severe events and can prolong QTc - sudden death has been attributed to cardiac autonomic neuropathy (p.3203, 3226)","New blindness risk: proliferative retinopathy with neovascularization and vitreous hemorrhage; clinically significant macular edema (p.3222-3223)","Loss of protective sensation / foot ulceration predisposing to lower-extremity amputation (p.3226)","Silent (painless) myocardial ischemia is common - absence of chest pain does not exclude coronary disease; consider cardiac evaluation before major surgery (p.3228)"],"prognosis":"DM is a major cause of mortality (8th leading cause of death in the US, 2021; likely underreported) and was responsible for ~6.7 million deaths worldwide in 2021 (12.2% of global all-cause mortality in adults 20-79); about one-third of diabetes-related deaths occur before age 60. Cardiovascular disease is the main cause of morbidity and mortality, with ASCVD events and mortality 2-4 times greater in type 2 DM. In the US, DM is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease, nontraumatic lower-extremity amputation, and adult blindness; ~20-40% of patients develop diabetic nephropathy. Intensive glycemic control has durable benefit (\"metabolic memory\"/legacy effect lasting >=10 years): DCCT/EDIC showed reductions in retinopathy (47%), albuminuria (39%), clinical nephropathy (54%), neuropathy (60%), later a 57% reduction in cardiovascular events and 33% reduction in mortality; UKPDS showed each 1% HbA1c reduction lowered microvascular complications by 35%, and blood pressure control reduced complications even more than glycemic control. Diagnosis at younger age worsens prognosis (~3-4 years of reduced life expectancy per decade of earlier diagnosis).","pitfalls":["Do not over-rely on classic features (age of onset, obesity, insulin need) to classify DM - many adult-onset type 1 cases are misdiagnosed as type 2, and many new type 1 patients are now overweight/obese; measure ICAs when type 1 features are present (p.3199, 3204).","Euglycemic DKA can occur (notably with SGLT-2 inhibitors) - a near-normal glucose does not exclude DKA; check ketones and acid-base (p.3216).","In DKA, hyperamylasemia is usually salivary in origin and does NOT diagnose pancreatitis; obtain serum lipase if pancreatitis is suspected (p.3216).","Measured serum sodium is falsely lowered by hyperglycemia (correct ~1.6 mmol/L per 5.6 mmol/L [100 mg/dL] glucose rise); a normal sodium in DKA signals a profound water deficit (p.3216).","Serum potassium may be normal or high at DKA presentation despite a large total-body potassium deficit (acidosis/volume depletion shift) (p.3216).","Nitroprusside ketone tests detect acetoacetate, not the predominant beta-hydroxybutyrate, and give false positives with captopril, penicillamine, or valproic acid - prefer serum beta-hydroxybutyrate (p.3216).","Glycemic control may transiently worsen established retinopathy in the first 6-12 months of improvement, and GLP-1 receptor agonists with marked glycemic improvement have been associated with worsening retinopathy (p.3224).","CKD without retinopathy in type 1 DM should prompt a search for an alternative (non-diabetic) cause of kidney disease (p.3224).","Very strict glycemic control in acutely/critically ill patients (NICE-SUGAR) increased mortality and severe hypoglycemia - avoid overly tight targets in hospital (p.3217)."],"severityClassification":"Glucose homeostasis is a spectrum: normal -> impaired (IFG/IGT/HbA1c 5.7-6.4%, i.e., prediabetes) -> diabetes mellitus. DKA severity by ADA: mild (pH 7.25-7.30, bicarbonate 15-18 meq/L, normal mental status), moderate (pH 7.0-7.25, bicarbonate 10-15 meq/L, mildly reduced mental status), severe (pH <7.0). Diabetic retinopathy: nonproliferative (microaneurysms, blot hemorrhages, cotton-wool spots) vs proliferative (neovascularization) +/- clinically significant macular edema. Albuminuria: moderately increased UACR 30-299 mg/g vs severely increased >300 mg/g creatinine. Neuropathy categories: distal symmetric polyneuropathy, autonomic neuropathy, mononeuropathy, and radiculopathy/polyradiculopathy.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3195"],"crossLinks":["dka","hhs","hypoglycemia","ckd"]},"diabetes_mellitus_complications":{"name":"Diabetes Mellitus: Complications","system":"Endocrine/Metabolic (multisystem)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Diabetic complications","Microvascular and macrovascular complications of diabetes","Diabetic retinopathy","Diabetic nephropathy","Diabetic neuropathy","Diabetic foot"],"chapter":417,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3221","clinicalPearls":["Diabetes-related complications are split into vascular (microvascular: retinopathy, neuropathy, nephropathy; macrovascular: coronary heart disease, peripheral arterial disease, cerebrovascular disease, heart failure) and nonvascular (infections, dermatologic changes, cheiroarthropathy, hearing loss, cataracts, glaucoma, fractures, cognitive impairment); patterns are similar in type 1 and type 2 disease.","Microvascular complications generally do not become evident until the second decade of hyperglycemia, whereas atherosclerotic cardiovascular risk (driven by insulin resistance and dyslipidemia) may precede frank hyperglycemia, so type 2 patients often have complications already present at diagnosis.","Landmark trials established that chronic hyperglycemia is causative for microvascular disease: in the DCCT, intensive control (HbA1c 7.3% vs 9.1%) reduced retinopathy ~47%, albuminuria ~39%, clinical nephropathy ~54%, and neuropathy ~60%; the UKPDS showed each 1-percentage-point HbA1c reduction lowered microvascular complications by about 35%.","A 'metabolic memory' / legacy effect exists: a period of improved glycemic control yields reduced cardiovascular events and mortality more than a decade later even after glycemic separation between groups disappears (DCCT/EDIC showed ~57% fewer cardiovascular events and ~33% lower mortality long term).","Diabetic retinopathy is staged as nonproliferative (microaneurysms, blot hemorrhages, cotton-wool spots, venous caliber change, intraretinal microvascular abnormalities) and proliferative (neovascularization from retinal hypoxemia, risking vitreous hemorrhage and traction retinal detachment); neovascularization is the hallmark of the proliferative stage.","Macular edema can occur at either retinopathy stage and is a key cause of moderate vision loss; duration of diabetes and degree of glycemic control are the strongest predictors of retinopathy, with hypertension, nephropathy, and dyslipidemia as additional risk factors.","Diabetic nephropathy is the leading cause of chronic kidney disease and stage 5 CKD; screening combines a spot urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) and estimated GFR because some patients lose GFR without albuminuria. Albuminuria is a persistently increased UACR >30 mg/g (moderate 30-299 mg/g, severe >300 mg/g).","Diabetic neuropathy affects roughly half of long-standing patients and includes distal symmetric polyneuropathy (DSPN), autonomic neuropathy, and mononeuropathy/radiculopathy; up to 50% of those with DSPN are asymptomatic, so screening for loss of protective sensation (10-g monofilament, vibration) is essential.","Cardiovascular disease (CHD, PAD, cerebrovascular disease, heart failure) is the major cause of death in diabetes; heart failure is about twice as common (HFpEF, HFmrEF, or HFrEF) and 'silent ischemia' with absent chest pain is frequent, warranting a cardiac evaluation before major surgery.","Diabetes is the leading cause of nontraumatic lower-extremity amputation; foot ulcers arise from the interaction of neuropathy, abnormal biomechanics, peripheral arterial disease, and poor wound healing, and a wound that probes to bone is highly likely to harbor osteomyelitis.","Patients have greater frequency and severity of infections; certain severe infections occur almost exclusively in diabetes: rhinocerebral mucormycosis, emphysematous cholecystitis/pyelonephritis/cystitis, and malignant (invasive) otitis externa (usually Pseudomonas aeruginosa).","Characteristic dermatologic findings include xerosis/pruritus, diabetic dermopathy (pigmented pretibial papules), bullosis diabeticorum, necrobiosis lipoidica diabeticorum (predominantly young women), acanthosis nigricans (severe insulin resistance), and vitiligo/alopecia areata in type 1 disease."],"pathophysiology":"Chronic hyperglycemia is the principal etiologic driver of microvascular complications, though the precise mechanisms remain incompletely defined and are likely multifactorial. Proposed pathways include hyperglycemia-induced epigenetic changes altering gene expression, formation of advanced glycosylation end products (e.g., pentosidine, glucosepane, carboxymethyllysine) that cross-link proteins and bind cell-surface receptors causing endothelial and glomerular dysfunction and accelerated atherosclerosis, local growth-factor effects (VEGF-A drives proliferative retinopathy), and a unifying hypothesis that hyperglycemia raises mitochondrial reactive oxygen species/superoxide that activate downstream injury pathways. In nephropathy, soluble mediators (angiotensin II, endothelin, AGEs), hemodynamic alterations (glomerular hyperfiltration, increased capillary pressure), and structural changes (basement-membrane thickening, mesangial expansion, fibrosis) lead from early hyperfiltration/renal hypertrophy to irreversible albuminuria and GFR decline. Macrovascular disease additionally reflects insulin resistance, dyslipidemia, hypertension, and inflammation, with insulin failing to suppress adipose lipolysis and causing ectopic lipid accumulation (triglycerides, diacylglycerol, ceramides). In neuropathy both myelinated and unmyelinated fibers are lost; foot ulceration arises from sensory loss removing protective mechanisms, autonomic anhidrosis causing dry/fissured skin, motor changes producing deformity, and PAD impairing healing.","additionalDifferentials":["Diabetic neuropathy is a diagnosis of exclusion - confirm only after excluding other neuropathy causes (e.g., B12/folate deficiency, alcohol, other metabolic/toxic neuropathies)","Chronic kidney disease without retinopathy in type 1 diabetes should prompt investigation for an alternative (non-diabetic) cause of renal disease","Type IV renal tubular acidosis (hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism) as a cause of hyperkalemia/acidemia in diabetic patients","Renovascular hypertension when blood pressure is not readily controlled in type 2 diabetes","Celiac disease in type 1 diabetes presenting with diarrhea/constipation (associated anti-tissue transglutaminase antibodies)","Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison's disease, possibly autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome) in type 1 diabetes with orthostatic hypotension"],"infectionMimics":["Gas gangrene appearance in a diabetic foot may develop in the absence of clostridial infection","Superficial culture of any foot ulcer yields multiple bacterial species of uncertain significance - infected ulcer is a clinical diagnosis, not a culture diagnosis","Asymptomatic bacteriuria is common with diabetic cystopathy and generally does not require treatment"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Transient worsening of established retinopathy during the first 6-12 months of improved glycemic control (and with GLP-1 receptor agonist-associated rapid glycemic improvement) can mimic disease progression but is temporary","UACR can be falsely elevated by strenuous exercise, fever, infection, congestive heart failure, marked hyperglycemia, marked hypertension, or prostate disease - confirm on 2-3 occasions over 3-6 months","Acute painful diabetic neuropathy may be treatment-related, triggered by improved glycemic control"],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated retinal examination by optometrist/ophthalmologist or retinal photography with remote reading (nondilated primary-care exams are inadequate); fluorescein angiography and optical coherence tomography to detect macular edema","Spot urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) plus estimated GFR, screening annually (start 5 years after type 1 diagnosis, at diagnosis for type 2)","Annual neuropathy screening with 10-g monofilament and vibration testing to detect loss of protective sensation; begin 5 years after type 1 diagnosis and at diagnosis of type 2","Gastric scintigraphy after a radiolabeled meal to document delayed gastric emptying (may not correlate with symptoms) in suspected gastroparesis","Ankle-brachial index for screening asymptomatic PAD in diabetic patients over 50 with risk factors","Foot ulcer workup: probe-to-bone testing with a blunt sterile instrument, plain radiographs for osteomyelitis in chronic non-healing ulcers, MRI (most specific) with nuclear/labeled-WBC studies as alternatives; cultures from debrided ulcer base or purulent aspirate","Cardiac stress testing in diabetic patients with symptoms (even atypical) suggestive of ischemia (routine screening of asymptomatic patients is not recommended)"],"redFlags":["Neovascularization, vitreous hemorrhage, or traction involving the macula - urgent panretinal laser photocoagulation and/or anti-VEGF; surgical vitrectomy for severe proliferative disease with vitreous hemorrhage","Foot wound that probes to bone (high likelihood of osteomyelitis) or rapidly spreading cellulitis/severe infection requiring IV broad-spectrum coverage and possible surgical debridement","Severe infections seen largely in diabetes: rhinocerebral mucormycosis, malignant (invasive) otitis externa progressing to osteomyelitis/meningitis, emphysematous pyelonephritis/cystitis/cholecystitis - consider especially with severe hyperglycemia","Cardiovascular autonomic neuropathy with resting tachycardia, reduced heart-rate variability, orthostatic hypotension, and QTc prolongation - associated with silent ischemia and reports of sudden death","Hypoglycemia unawareness from autonomic neuropathy/reduced counterregulation, increasing the risk of severe hypoglycemia","Hyperkalemia/acidemia from type IV RTA, potentially exacerbated by ACE inhibitors, ARBs, or mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists","Risk of radiocontrast-induced nephrotoxicity (preexisting nephropathy, volume depletion)"],"prognosis":"Complications account for most diabetes-related morbidity and mortality. Diagnosis at a younger age increases lifetime complication burden (one estimate: 3-4 fewer years of life expectancy per decade of earlier diagnosis). Intensive glycemic control substantially improves outcomes: DCCT projections estimated several additional years free of vision loss, end-stage renal disease, and amputation, with overall added life expectancy, and 30-year follow-up showed durable reductions in retinopathy, nephropathy, and cardiovascular events (legacy/metabolic-memory effect). About 20-40% of patients develop diabetic nephropathy, and CKD on dialysis carries a poor prognosis. Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death and outcomes after CHD are worse than in nondiabetic patients; CHD tends to involve multiple vessels and revascularization (PCI/CABG) is less durable, though CABG plus optimal medical therapy generally outperforms PCI. Diabetic polyradiculopathies (including amyotrophy) are usually self-limited, resolving over 6-12 months. Foot ulcers carry a substantial amputation risk (about 14-24% with that or subsequent ulceration). Some patients never develop retinopathy or nephropathy despite long-standing disease, suggesting genetic protection.","pitfalls":["Routine urinalysis does not detect low-level albuminuria - use UACR; and some patients lose GFR without albuminuria, so always assess both eGFR and UACR","Nondilated eye exams by primary providers miss diabetic eye disease - a dilated/photographic retinal exam is required","Once advanced retinopathy or moderate albuminuria is established, improving glycemic control provides less benefit - early detection is critical","Diagnosing diabetic neuropathy without excluding other etiologies; metformin can lower vitamin B12 and pernicious anemia is more common in type 1 disease","Treating asymptomatic bacteriuria unnecessarily; basing diabetic foot infection diagnosis on superficial swab cultures rather than clinical judgment and deep cultures","Failure to hold metformin around iodinated contrast and to monitor creatinine 24-48 h afterward; failing to reassess glucose-lowering drug choice/dose as GFR falls (sulfonylureas and metformin contraindicated in advanced renal insufficiency; SGLT-2 inhibitors ineffective below eGFR 20)","Overlooking silent ischemia given frequent absence of chest pain, and failing to evaluate before major surgery"],"severityClassification":"Retinopathy: nonproliferative (mild to severe, with microaneurysms, hemorrhages, cotton-wool spots, venous beading, IRMA) versus proliferative (neovascularization), with clinically significant macular edema as a separate sight-threatening category. Albuminuria/CKD: UACR normal (<30 mg/g), moderately increased (30-299 mg/g), severely increased (>300 mg/g), staged alongside eGFR; nephrology referral when eGFR <30 mL/min/1.73 m2, UACR >300 mg/g, or atypical features (hematuria, rapid decline). Diabetic neuropathy categorized as diffuse (distal symmetric polyneuropathy and/or autonomic), focal mononeuropathy, or radiculopathy/polyradiculopathy. Foot infection severity stratified clinically from mild/non-limb-threatening (oral antibiotics) to severe (IV broad-spectrum antibiotics, offloading, debridement).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3221","Harrison 22e p.3223","Harrison 22e p.3224","Harrison 22e p.3227","Harrison 22e p.3228","Harrison 22e p.3229","Harrison 22e p.3230"],"crossLinks":[]},"diabetes_mellitus_management_and_therapies":{"name":"Diabetes Mellitus: Management and Therapies","system":"Endocrine/Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DM management","comprehensive diabetes care","diabetic ketoacidosis","DKA","hyperglycemic hyperosmolar state","HHS","euglycemic DKA"],"chapter":416,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3205","clinicalPearls":["The three overarching goals of diabetes care are to relieve hyperglycemic symptoms, reduce or prevent long-term microvascular and macrovascular complications, and allow as normal a lifestyle as possible; symptoms usually resolve once plasma glucose is below ~200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L), so therapy mainly targets complication prevention. (Harrison 22e p.3205)","HbA1c reflects average glycemia over the prior 2-3 months because erythrocytes live ~120 days, with the most recent month contributing roughly half the value; it does not capture short-term variability or recent intercurrent illness the way continuous (CGM) or fingerstick (BGM) monitoring does. (Harrison 22e p.3207-3208)","Continuous glucose monitoring measures interstitial glucose, which is in equilibrium with blood glucose but can lag when glucose changes rapidly or during exercise; an ambulatory glucose profile summarizes time-in-range, time above/below target, the glucose management indicator (correlating with HbA1c), and glucose variability. (Harrison 22e p.3207)","The general HbA1c goal is below 7% (or <=6.5% for selected patients), but a more lenient target of <7.5-8% is appropriate for those with cognitive impairment, hypoglycemia unawareness, or limited life expectancy; about one in four people over 65 has diabetes, so elderly targets must be individualized. (Harrison 22e p.3208)","A consensus CGM time-in-range goal for most patients with type 1 or type 2 DM is >70% of time within 70-180 mg/dL (3.9-10.0 mmol/L), with <4% below 70 mg/dL and <1% below 54 mg/dL. (Harrison 22e p.3208)","Ketones (preferably blood beta-hydroxybutyrate rather than urine nitroprusside assays, which detect only acetoacetate and acetone) should be checked in type 1 DM whenever plasma glucose persistently exceeds 250 mg/dL (13.9 mmol/L) or during intercurrent illness with nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain. (Harrison 22e p.3216)","DKA arises mostly in type 1 DM and HHS mostly in type 2 DM, but the two exist along a continuum of hyperglycemia and up to one-third of patients show features of both. (Harrison 22e p.3216)","Ketosis-prone diabetes can present with DKA at diagnosis in obese young adults of Hispanic or African descent who later recover insulin secretory capacity and can discontinue insulin after weeks to months. (Harrison 22e p.3216)","SGLT-2 inhibitor therapy can precipitate euglycemic DKA in type 2 DM, where glucose is normal or only mildly elevated because of glucosuria, so a normal glucose does not exclude ketoacidosis; resolution should be tracked by bicarbonate correction rather than by glucose. (Harrison 22e p.3216-3217)","In DKA the measured serum sodium is artifactually low; the correction adds ~1.6 mmol/L of sodium for each 100 mg/dL (5.6 mmol/L) rise in glucose, and a normal measured sodium in DKA signals a profound free-water deficit. (Harrison 22e p.3217)","Inpatient hyperglycemia predicts worse outcomes, yet very strict glycemic control in the critically ill worsens outcomes; the NICE-SUGAR trial showed higher mortality and more severe hypoglycemia with a target of 81-108 mg/dL versus a moderate goal of <180 mg/dL, supporting a hospital target of roughly 140-180 mg/dL. (Harrison 22e p.3219)","Sliding-scale insulin alone (reactive treatment only when glucose is already high) is inadequate for inpatient management; scheduled basal plus prandial and corrective dosing is preferred, and CGM/automated insulin delivery are not FDA approved for ICU use. (Harrison 22e p.3219-3220)"],"pathophysiology":"Hyperglycemia in diabetes reflects relative or absolute insulin deficiency with impaired glucose utilization and unrestrained hepatic glucose production. In acute decompensation, severe hyperglycemia drives osmotic diuresis with volume depletion. DKA results from relative or absolute insulin deficiency combined with excess counterregulatory hormones (glucagon, catecholamines, cortisol, growth hormone); the low insulin-to-glucagon ratio promotes hepatic gluconeogenesis, glycogenolysis, and ketone body formation, while increased lipolysis releases free fatty acids that are shifted toward ketogenesis, producing ketonemia and high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Inflammatory markers (cytokines, C-reactive protein) are elevated in both DKA and HHS. HHS is caused by relative (less severe) insulin deficiency plus inadequate fluid intake; insulin deficiency raises hepatic glucose output and impairs muscle uptake, and osmotic diuresis with poor fluid replacement produces extreme hyperglycemia, hyperosmolality, and dehydration. The absence of significant ketosis in HHS is incompletely understood but is attributed to only partial insulin deficiency, lower counterregulatory hormone and free-fatty-acid levels, and an insulin/glucagon balance that does not favor ketogenesis. During exercise or acute illness, surges in counterregulatory hormones and cytokines cause transient insulin resistance and can tip toward hyperglycemia or, with relative hyperinsulinemia, hypoglycemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Starvation ketosis (bicarbonate typically preserved)","Alcoholic ketoacidosis (bicarbonate usually >15 meq/L)","Other causes of high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis","Acute pancreatitis or surgical abdomen (DKA abdominal pain can mimic these; hyperamylasemia in DKA is usually salivary, so serum lipase is needed)","Lactic acidosis (a small anion-gap acidosis from lactate can accompany HHS)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute pancreatitis / acute surgical abdomen presenting with abdominal pain mimicking DKA","Other high-anion-gap metabolic acidoses (starvation, alcohol, toxins)","Alternative causes of altered mental status or coma (stroke, hypoxemia, intoxication) overlapping with severe DKA/HHS presentation"],"additionalInvestigations":["HbA1c at initial evaluation and at least twice yearly when at goal, every 3 months when control is inadequate or therapy changed (interpret with caution in hemoglobinopathies, anemias, reticulocytosis, transfusion, uremia, G6PD variants, hemodialysis, erythropoietin or HIV therapy)","Continuous glucose monitoring or capillary blood glucose monitoring for short-term control and time-in-range","Blood beta-hydroxybutyrate (preferred over urine nitroprusside ketone assays)","Serum electrolytes with anion gap, potassium, phosphate and magnesium (every 4 h in early DKA), bicarbonate, arterial pH","Serum/plasma glucose every 1-2 h during acute crises","BUN and creatinine (intravascular volume depletion / prerenal azotemia), serum osmolality","Serum lipase if pancreatitis suspected (amylase in DKA is often salivary and nondiagnostic)","Workup for precipitant: cultures, chest x-ray, ECG; corrected sodium calculation","Fructosamine or glycated albumin as alternative measures reflecting glycemia over the prior 2-4 weeks"],"redFlags":["Nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain in a person with type 1 DM (warrants immediate evaluation for DKA)","Kussmaul respirations and a fruity (acetone) breath odor","Lethargy progressing to obtundation or coma; cerebral edema (especially in children during DKA resolution)","Hypotension and tachycardia from volume depletion","Plasma glucose persistently >250 mg/dL or, in HHS, markedly elevated (>1000 mg/dL) with hyperosmolality (>300 mOsm)","Normal measured serum sodium during DKA (indicates profound water deficit)","Hypoglycemia, particularly recurrent, nocturnal, or with hypoglycemia unawareness","Signs of a precipitating event (infection, myocardial infarction, stroke, pancreatitis) even without fever"],"prognosis":"With appropriate therapy, DKA mortality is low (<1%) and relates more to the underlying or precipitating event (infection/pneumonia, COVID-19, pregnancy, end-stage renal disease, myocardial infarction) than to the metabolic derangement itself; venous thrombosis, upper GI bleeding, ARDS, and cerebral edema (chiefly in children, during resolution) can complicate the course. Even a single DKA episode carries a substantially increased 1-year mortality, and recurrent DKA may signal underlying mental health issues or barriers to care such as insulin cost. HHS carries a substantially higher mortality (up to ~15% in some series) because patients are typically older with serious precipitating illness and comorbidities. Improved glycemic control reduces microvascular complications regardless of baseline level, and early intensive control in type 1 DM (DCCT/EDIC) lowered later cardiovascular events nearly two decades on.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal or only mildly elevated glucose excludes ketoacidosis; euglycemic DKA occurs with SGLT-2 inhibitors and resolution must be judged by bicarbonate, not glucose","Relying on urine nitroprusside ketone tests, which miss beta-hydroxybutyrate (the dominant ketone) and give false positives with captopril, penicillamine, or valproic acid","Interpreting hyperamylasemia in DKA as pancreatitis when it is usually salivary in origin; obtain lipase","Failing to correct serum sodium for hyperglycemia, masking a true free-water deficit","Misreading the early/at-presentation serum potassium as adequate; total-body potassium is depleted despite a normal or high initial level, and hypokalemia develops during insulin/fluid therapy","Trusting HbA1c in conditions that alter red-cell turnover (hemoglobinopathy, anemia, reticulocytosis, transfusion, uremia, dialysis, erythropoietin or HIV therapy)","Using sliding-scale insulin alone for inpatients, which is inadequate","Applying overly strict inpatient glucose targets, which increases severe hypoglycemia and can worsen outcomes (NICE-SUGAR)","Substances interfering with glucose sensors/meters (e.g., acetaminophen, ascorbic acid, hydroxyurea, mannitol/sorbitol for CGM; uric acid, galactose, xylose, L-DOPA for BGM)"],"severityClassification":"DKA severity is graded by pH, serum bicarbonate, and mental status: mild (pH 7.25-7.30, bicarbonate 15-18 meq/L, normal mentation); moderate (pH 7.0-7.25, bicarbonate 10-15 meq/L, mildly reduced mentation); severe (pH <7.0, bicarbonate <10-15 meq/L, reduced mental status or coma). Representative presenting laboratory ranges: DKA glucose ~250-600 mg/dL with bicarbonate <18 meq/L, beta-hydroxybutyrate >3.0 mmol/L, and elevated anion gap; HHS glucose ~600-1200 mg/dL (may exceed 1000) with osmolality >300, bicarbonate >18, pH >7.3, and absent/minimal ketones; euglycemic DKA glucose ~100-250 mg/dL with bicarbonate <18 and beta-hydroxybutyrate >3.0.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3205","Harrison 22e p.3207","Harrison 22e p.3208","Harrison 22e p.3216","Harrison 22e p.3217","Harrison 22e p.3218","Harrison 22e p.3219","Harrison 22e p.3220"],"crossLinks":[]},"diabetic_foot_infection":{"name":"Diabetic foot infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DFI","diabetic foot ulcer infection","infected diabetic foot","diabetic pedal infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / IDSA","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Infection typically complicates a neuropathic or neuroischaemic ulcer and is diagnosed clinically by local signs of inflammation or purulence, not by culture alone","Peripheral neuropathy, peripheral arterial disease, and poor glycaemic control combine to create and perpetuate the wound","Severity should be graded (mild, moderate, severe) to guide the need for hospitalisation, imaging, and route of therapy","Mild infections are often caused by staphylococci and streptococci, while chronic, previously treated, or severe wounds tend to be polymicrobial with Gram-negatives and anaerobes","Probing to bone or an ulcer that is large, deep, or long-standing raises the likelihood of underlying osteomyelitis","Assessment of the arterial supply is essential, as ischaemia impairs healing and antibiotic delivery and may require revascularisation","Management principles combine wound debridement, pressure offloading, glycaemic control, vascular assessment, and antibiotics targeted to severity and likely organisms","Deep abscess, extensive necrosis, or gas-forming infection requires urgent surgical drainage or debridement"],"pathophysiology":"Diabetic foot infection develops when skin integrity is breached, usually over a pressure point, in a foot compromised by sensory neuropathy (loss of protective sensation), motor and autonomic dysfunction (deformity and dry, fissured skin), and often peripheral arterial disease. Impaired neutrophil function and microvascular disease blunt the immune response and reduce tissue perfusion, allowing colonising skin flora to invade soft tissue, tendon sheaths, and bone. Infection can spread rapidly along fascial and tendon planes, producing deep abscesses and osteomyelitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Uninfected neuropathic ulcer with colonisation only","Acute Charcot neuroarthropathy","Gout or acute crystal arthropathy of the foot","Deep vein thrombosis causing a swollen limb","Critical limb ischaemia without infection"],"infectionMimics":["Charcot foot presenting as a warm, swollen, erythematous foot","Cellulitis mimicked by lipodermatosclerosis or venous stasis changes","Acute gouty arthritis of the first metatarsophalangeal joint","Contact dermatitis around a chronic wound dressing"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Deep tissue or bone specimens for culture rather than superficial swabs","Inflammatory markers and glucose or HbA1c to gauge control","Plain radiographs, with MRI when osteomyelitis or deep collection is suspected","Probe-to-bone test at the bedside","Vascular assessment including ankle-brachial index and arterial imaging"],"redFlags":["Deep abscess, extensive necrosis, or gas in tissues requiring urgent surgical drainage or debridement","Rapidly spreading infection with systemic toxicity indicating limb- or life-threatening sepsis","Critical limb ischaemia threatening tissue viability and demanding urgent vascular input","Probing to bone or radiographic bone destruction suggesting osteomyelitis"],"prognosis":"Outcomes depend on infection severity, adequacy of perfusion, and glycaemic control; mild soft-tissue infection often resolves, whereas deep infection with ischaemia or osteomyelitis carries a substantial risk of amputation and is associated with reduced long-term survival.","pitfalls":["Treating on the basis of a superficial swab that reflects colonisation rather than a deep specimen","Overlooking peripheral arterial disease that undermines healing and antibiotic delivery","Missing underlying osteomyelitis and treating for too short a duration","Neglecting offloading and debridement while relying on antibiotics alone"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","IDSA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"diabetic_kidney_disease":{"name":"Diabetic kidney disease","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DKD","diabetic nephropathy","diabetic glomerulosclerosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Diabetic kidney disease is the leading cause of end-stage kidney disease worldwide and develops in a substantial proportion of people with long-standing type 1 or type 2 diabetes","The classic course progresses from glomerular hyperfiltration through rising albuminuria to declining glomerular filtration, though not every patient follows this sequence","Persistent albuminuria is both an early marker of kidney involvement and an independent predictor of cardiovascular risk","Coexisting diabetic retinopathy supports the diagnosis, and its absence in type 1 diabetes should prompt consideration of a non-diabetic cause","Features that argue against pure diabetic disease include an active urinary sediment, rapid loss of function, heavy proteinuria without retinopathy, or a short duration of diabetes","Control of blood pressure, glycaemia, and albuminuria, together with blockade of the renin-angiotensin system, are the mainstays of slowing progression","Newer agents that reduce intraglomerular pressure and albuminuria have been shown to slow decline and reduce cardiovascular events, changing modern management principles","Regular screening with albuminuria measurement and estimated GFR allows early intervention before irreversible damage accumulates"],"pathophysiology":"Diabetic kidney disease develops from chronic hyperglycaemia acting through several interlinked pathways. Excess glucose drives formation of advanced glycation end products, activation of protein kinase C, and oxidative stress, which damage the glomerular filtration barrier. Early on, altered afferent and efferent arteriolar tone produces glomerular hypertension and hyperfiltration, increasing shear stress on the capillary wall. Over time the glomerular basement membrane thickens, the mesangium expands with accumulation of matrix, and podocytes are lost, culminating in the nodular and diffuse glomerulosclerosis characteristic of the disease. These changes progressively increase albumin leakage and reduce filtration surface area. Systemic and intraglomerular hypertension, along with tubulointerstitial fibrosis, accelerates the decline toward end-stage kidney disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypertensive nephrosclerosis","Primary or secondary glomerulonephritis","Ischaemic nephropathy from renovascular disease","Membranous nephropathy","Obstructive uropathy","Other causes of chronic kidney disease coexisting with diabetes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantification of albuminuria (urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio) and serial estimated GFR","Urinalysis with microscopy to detect an active sediment suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Fundoscopy to assess for diabetic retinopathy","Blood pressure assessment and cardiovascular risk evaluation","Renal ultrasound to assess kidney size and exclude obstruction","Consideration of biopsy when the clinical picture is atypical for diabetic disease"],"redFlags":["Rapid decline in renal function suggesting a superimposed or alternative process","Nephrotic-range proteinuria with hypoalbuminaemia and oedema","Active urinary sediment or haematuria pointing to glomerulonephritis","Refractory hypertension or hyperkalaemia complicating renin-angiotensin blockade"],"prognosis":"Without intervention, diabetic kidney disease tends to progress steadily to end-stage kidney disease, and it markedly increases cardiovascular mortality. Intensive control of glycaemia and blood pressure, renin-angiotensin blockade, reduction of albuminuria, and modern kidney-protective therapies can substantially slow decline and reduce cardiovascular events, so early detection and comprehensive risk-factor management materially improve outcomes.","pitfalls":["Assuming all kidney disease in a diabetic patient is diabetic nephropathy and missing a treatable alternative","Neglecting albuminuria screening so that intervention starts only after significant function is lost","Failing to intensify renin-angiotensin blockade and other kidney-protective therapy in albuminuric patients","Overlooking the high cardiovascular risk that accompanies diabetic kidney disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"diabetic_nephropathy":{"name":"Diabetic nephropathy","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["diabetic kidney disease","diabetic glomerulosclerosis","Kimmelstiel-Wilson disease","diabetic renal disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Diabetic kidney disease is a leading cause of end-stage renal failure and usually develops after years of diabetes","The earliest clinical marker is moderately increased albuminuria (microalbuminuria), which can progress to overt proteinuria and declining filtration","Screening with an annual urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio and estimated GFR allows early detection and intervention","Coexistent diabetic retinopathy supports a diabetic aetiology, and its absence in type 1 diabetes should prompt consideration of an alternative renal diagnosis","Tight control of both blood glucose and blood pressure slows progression, and blockade of the renin-angiotensin system is renoprotective in albuminuric patients","SGLT2 inhibitors reduce progression of diabetic kidney disease and cardiovascular risk and have become central to management","Features such as active urinary sediment, rapid GFR loss, or heavy proteinuria without retinopathy warrant evaluation for a non-diabetic renal disease"],"pathophysiology":"Sustained hyperglycaemia and glomerular hypertension drive haemodynamic and metabolic injury to the kidney. Early glomerular hyperfiltration, mesangial expansion, glomerular basement membrane thickening, and podocyte injury progress to nodular and diffuse glomerulosclerosis, the nodular lesions being the classic Kimmelstiel-Wilson change. Advanced glycation end-products, oxidative stress, and activation of the renin-angiotensin system promote albuminuria, tubulointerstitial fibrosis, and progressive loss of filtration.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypertensive nephrosclerosis","Glomerulonephritis or other primary glomerular disease","Ischaemic or atheroembolic renal disease","Drug-induced or contrast-associated nephropathy","Obstructive uropathy or recurrent pyelonephritis","Membranous nephropathy or minimal change disease presenting with nephrotic-range proteinuria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio for albuminuria","Serum creatinine with estimated GFR","Urinalysis to assess for haematuria or active sediment suggesting an alternative cause","Dilated retinal examination to look for concurrent retinopathy","Renal ultrasound and, in atypical presentations, consideration of renal biopsy"],"redFlags":["Rapidly declining GFR out of proportion to expected diabetic course","Active urinary sediment, red cell casts, or nephrotic syndrome without retinopathy","Uncontrolled hypertension with worsening renal function","Signs of advanced uraemia or fluid overload requiring renal replacement planning"],"prognosis":"Without intervention diabetic nephropathy progresses to end-stage renal disease and carries a high cardiovascular mortality, but early detection and aggressive control of glucose and blood pressure, renin-angiotensin blockade, and SGLT2 inhibition can substantially slow progression.","pitfalls":["Assuming all renal impairment in a diabetic patient is diabetic nephropathy and missing a treatable alternative","Failing to screen for albuminuria and losing the window for early renoprotective therapy","Under-using renin-angiotensin blockade and SGLT2 inhibitors in eligible patients","Neglecting cardiovascular risk, which is the dominant cause of death in these patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"diabetic_neuropathy":{"name":"Diabetic neuropathy","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["diabetic peripheral neuropathy","distal symmetric polyneuropathy","diabetic autonomic neuropathy","diabetic polyneuropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common form is a distal symmetric sensorimotor polyneuropathy that begins in the feet and progresses proximally in a stocking-and-glove distribution","Sensory loss predisposes to unnoticed foot injury, ulceration, and Charcot arthropathy, making regular foot examination essential","Painful neuropathy can coexist with sensory loss, causing burning, tingling, and allodynia that are often worse at night","Autonomic involvement can produce postural hypotension, resting tachycardia, gastroparesis, bladder dysfunction, and erectile dysfunction","It is a diagnosis of exclusion, so other causes of neuropathy such as vitamin B12 deficiency, alcohol, uraemia, and hypothyroidism should be considered","Optimised glycaemic control is central to prevention and slowing progression, particularly in type 1 diabetes","Management of painful neuropathy relies on agents active in neuropathic pain rather than simple analgesics, alongside foot protection strategies"],"pathophysiology":"Chronic hyperglycaemia damages peripheral nerves through several linked mechanisms, including accumulation of advanced glycation end-products, activation of the polyol and protein kinase C pathways, oxidative stress, and microvascular ischaemia of the vasa nervorum. These processes injure axons and Schwann cells, producing a length-dependent loss of both small and large fibres. Involvement of autonomic fibres disrupts cardiovascular, gastrointestinal, and genitourinary regulation, while focal ischaemia or entrapment can cause mononeuropathies and radiculopathies.","additionalDifferentials":["Vitamin B12 or other nutritional deficiency neuropathy","Alcoholic neuropathy","Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy","Uraemic neuropathy","Hypothyroid neuropathy","Toxic or drug-induced neuropathy","Lumbosacral radiculopathy or spinal stenosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical foot examination with monofilament and vibration testing","HbA1c and glucose assessment to gauge control","Serum vitamin B12, thyroid function, and renal function to exclude alternative causes","Nerve conduction studies in atypical or asymmetric presentations","Autonomic function testing including orthostatic blood pressure measurement when indicated"],"redFlags":["Foot ulceration, deformity, or signs of Charcot arthropathy","Rapidly progressive, asymmetric, or predominantly motor deficits suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Severe autonomic features such as symptomatic postural hypotension or gastroparesis","Signs of limb ischaemia or infection complicating a neuropathic foot"],"prognosis":"Neuropathy tends to progress with duration and poor control of diabetes, and established nerve damage is largely irreversible, so the emphasis is on prevention of ulceration and amputation and on symptom control; autonomic neuropathy carries an adverse cardiovascular prognosis.","pitfalls":["Attributing all neuropathy to diabetes without excluding treatable alternative causes","Neglecting routine foot inspection and patient education, allowing preventable ulcers","Undertreating painful neuropathy or relying on ineffective simple analgesics","Missing autonomic neuropathy as the cause of unexplained postural symptoms or gastrointestinal complaints"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"diabetic_retinopathy":{"name":"Diabetic retinopathy","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DR","diabetic eye disease","proliferative diabetic retinopathy","PDR","non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy","NPDR","diabetic maculopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A leading cause of blindness in working-age adults and directly related to duration of diabetes and quality of glycaemic control","Early (non-proliferative) disease is usually asymptomatic, underscoring the value of regular screening fundus photography","Non-proliferative signs include microaneurysms, dot-and-blot haemorrhages, hard exudates, cotton-wool spots, and venous beading","Proliferative disease is defined by neovascularisation of the disc or retina, which risks vitreous haemorrhage and tractional retinal detachment","Diabetic maculopathy (macular oedema) is the commonest cause of visual loss and can occur at any stage","Anti-VEGF intravitreal therapy is first-line for centre-involving macular oedema, while panretinal laser photocoagulation is used for proliferative disease","Blood pressure, lipid, and glycaemic control materially slow progression alongside ophthalmic treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Chronic hyperglycaemia damages retinal capillaries through pathways including accumulation of advanced glycation end-products, oxidative stress, and pericyte loss. This weakens vessel walls (forming microaneurysms), increases permeability (causing exudation and macular oedema), and causes capillary occlusion leading to retinal ischaemia. Ischaemic retina releases vascular endothelial growth factor and other mediators that drive pathological new-vessel formation; these fragile vessels bleed and contract, producing vitreous haemorrhage and tractional detachment in advanced proliferative disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypertensive retinopathy","Retinal vein occlusion","Radiation retinopathy","Ocular ischaemic syndrome","Retinal macroaneurysm","Other causes of macular oedema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundoscopy and fundus photography for screening and staging","Optical coherence tomography to detect and quantify macular oedema","Fluorescein angiography to map ischaemia and neovascularisation","Visual acuity assessment","Systemic review of HbA1c, blood pressure, lipids, and renal function"],"redFlags":["Sudden visual loss or floaters suggesting vitreous haemorrhage","New vessels on the disc or elsewhere indicating sight-threatening proliferative disease","Centre-involving macular oedema threatening central vision","Rubeosis iridis (iris new vessels) heralding neovascular glaucoma"],"prognosis":"With timely screening, good systemic control, and prompt laser or anti-VEGF treatment, most severe visual loss is preventable; neglected proliferative disease and macular oedema carry a high risk of blindness.","pitfalls":["Assuming good vision means no retinopathy and skipping screening","Missing macular oedema that requires OCT rather than fundoscopy alone to detect","Rapid tightening of glycaemic control causing transient worsening of retinopathy","Overlooking pregnancy as an accelerator of progression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"difficult_airway":{"name":"Difficult airway","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["difficult intubation","difficult mask ventilation","CICO","can't intubate can't oxygenate"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A difficult airway describes difficulty with mask ventilation, supraglottic device placement, laryngoscopy, intubation, or surgical airway access, and may be anticipated or unexpected","Preoperative assessment combines several predictors (mouth opening, Mallampati class, thyromental distance, neck mobility, prognathism, prior difficulty, and pathology) since no single test is reliable","A structured plan with pre-formulated backup steps and clear failure criteria is essential, and progression through a difficult-airway algorithm should be deliberate and time-limited","Preoxygenation and apnoeic oxygenation extend safe apnoea time and are central to preventing hypoxic injury","When intubation fails, the priority shifts from securing the tube to maintaining oxygenation using face mask or supraglottic devices","A can't-intubate-can't-oxygenate situation is a life-threatening emergency requiring immediate front-of-neck access (cricothyroidotomy) without delay","For a known or predicted difficult airway, an awake technique with a flexible bronchoscope preserves spontaneous ventilation and airway reflexes","Limiting repeated laryngoscopy attempts reduces trauma, bleeding, and oedema that can convert a difficult airway into an impossible one; documentation and patient alerting are vital afterwards"],"pathophysiology":"Airway difficulty arises from anatomical or pathological factors that impair alignment of the oral, pharyngeal, and laryngeal axes or obstruct airflow. Congenital syndromes, obesity, limited neck extension, restricted mouth opening, tumours, infection, trauma, previous surgery, radiation, and airway oedema all contribute. Under anaesthesia, loss of muscle tone can collapse the pharynx and worsen obstruction. When ventilation and oxygenation fail, progressive hypoxaemia leads within minutes to bradycardia, hypoxic brain injury, cardiac arrest, and death, so oxygenation rather than intubation is the overriding physiological goal.","additionalDifferentials":["Laryngospasm","Bronchospasm or anaphylaxis limiting ventilation","Airway foreign body or obstructing tumour","Airway oedema from angioedema or infection","Oesophageal intubation mistaken for a difficult airway","Equipment failure or circuit disconnection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bedside airway assessment scores and examination of relevant pathology","Nasendoscopy or flexible airway endoscopy to evaluate obstructing lesions","Imaging (radiograph, CT) to characterise airway anatomy and masses when indicated","Capnography to confirm tracheal placement and ventilation","Review of previous anaesthetic records and airway alerts"],"redFlags":["Can't-intubate-can't-oxygenate scenario requiring immediate front-of-neck access","Progressive desaturation despite optimal mask and supraglottic ventilation","Rapidly worsening airway obstruction from oedema, bleeding, or an expanding mass","Repeated failed attempts causing trauma, bleeding, and deteriorating airway conditions"],"prognosis":"Most anticipated difficult airways are managed safely with planning and appropriate techniques; unanticipated failure with delayed rescue oxygenation can cause hypoxic brain injury or death within minutes.","pitfalls":["Persisting with repeated intubation attempts instead of prioritising oxygenation","Failing to declare and act on a can't-intubate-can't-oxygenate emergency in time","Inadequate preoxygenation before induction","Not preparing backup equipment and a clear stepwise plan in advance","Failing to document the difficulty and alert the patient for future anaesthetics"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"digoxin_toxicity":{"name":"Digoxin toxicity","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["digoxin poisoning","digitalis toxicity","cardiac glycoside poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with nausea, vomiting, anorexia, confusion, and characteristic visual disturbances such as blurred or yellow-green tinted vision (xanthopsia)","Digoxin can cause almost any arrhythmia; the combination of increased automaticity with atrioventricular block (for example atrial tachycardia with block, or bidirectional ventricular tachycardia) is highly suggestive","Chronic toxicity is often precipitated by renal impairment, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hypercalcaemia, and interacting drugs, and occurs at lower levels than acute overdose","In acute overdose, hyperkalaemia results from poisoning of the sodium-potassium pump and is an important marker of severity and prognosis","Digoxin-specific antibody fragments (digoxin immune Fab) are the definitive antidote and are indicated for life-threatening arrhythmia, significant hyperkalaemia in acute poisoning, or haemodynamic instability","In acute toxicity, treat hyperkalaemia cautiously and avoid intravenous calcium, which is traditionally cautioned against for fear of worsening myocardial contracture","Correct hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia in chronic toxicity, as these potentiate glycoside effects and provoke arrhythmias","Bradyarrhythmias may respond to atropine and temporary pacing, but definitive treatment of serious toxicity is the specific antibody"],"pathophysiology":"Digoxin inhibits the myocardial sodium-potassium ATPase, raising intracellular sodium and, through sodium-calcium exchange, intracellular calcium, which increases contractility but also enhances automaticity and the tendency to arrhythmia. It also augments vagal tone, slowing sinoatrial and atrioventricular conduction. In acute overdose, widespread inhibition of the pump drives potassium out of cells, producing hyperkalaemia that reflects the degree of poisoning. Electrolyte disturbances (low potassium or magnesium, high calcium) and renal impairment increase myocardial sensitivity and reduce clearance, precipitating chronic toxicity at therapeutic or near-therapeutic levels.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of bradyarrhythmia or heart block","Hyperkalaemia from other causes","Beta-blocker or calcium-channel-blocker toxicity","Acute coronary syndrome","Gastroenteritis (for the GI symptoms) or other cause of delirium"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum digoxin level, interpreted with timing of ingestion and clinical picture","Serum potassium, magnesium, and calcium","Renal function tests","12-lead ECG and continuous cardiac monitoring for arrhythmia and conduction block","Review of interacting medications and precipitating factors"],"redFlags":["Life-threatening ventricular arrhythmia or bidirectional ventricular tachycardia","High-grade atrioventricular block or symptomatic bradycardia","Significant hyperkalaemia in acute poisoning","Haemodynamic instability","Suggestive arrhythmia combining enhanced automaticity with conduction block"],"prognosis":"With timely recognition, correction of electrolytes, and use of digoxin-specific antibody fragments for severe cases, most patients recover; untreated life-threatening arrhythmias and marked hyperkalaemia in acute overdose carry high mortality.","pitfalls":["Overlooking chronic toxicity because the digoxin level is only modestly raised","Failing to correct hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia that perpetuate arrhythmia in chronic toxicity","Giving intravenous calcium for hyperkalaemia in acute digoxin poisoning, traditionally avoided","Delaying digoxin-specific antibody in a patient with life-threatening arrhythmia or severe hyperkalaemia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"dilated_cardiomyopathy":{"name":"Dilated Cardiomyopathy","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DCM","cardiomyopathy"],"chapter":269,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2001-2020","clinicalPearls":["DCM is defined by decreased left ventricular systolic function, typically with increased LV dimensions, although dilation may be minimal in some cases (p.2015).","Quantitatively, DCM is defined when LVEF is <=0.50, but most clinical presentations occur with LVEF <=0.40; persistent LVEF <=0.30-0.35 is the threshold for primary-prevention ICD (p.2001-2002).","Despite multiple etiologies and variable initial presentations, DCM often progresses into a convergent clinical phenotype similar to that following acute myocardial infarction, with myocyte death, apoptosis, compensatory hypertrophy, and neurohormonal activation (p.2015).","Mitral regurgitation commonly develops as the ventricle dilates and the valvular apparatus is distorted, and is commonly moderate to severe by the time heart failure is advanced (p.2015).","Right ventricular dilation and dysfunction may result directly from the initial injury but more often develop later from elevated afterload due to secondary pulmonary hypertension and mechanical interaction with the failing LV (p.2015).","Alcohol is the most common toxin implicated in chronic DCM, but alcoholic cardiomyopathy is relatively rare and remains a diagnosis of exclusion; atrial fibrillation occurs commonly both early ('holiday heart') and in advanced stages (p.2016).","Truncating variants in TTN and other DCM disease genes can be found in about 10% of patients with presumed alcohol cardiomyopathy and about 15% of peripartum cardiomyopathy, indicating that environmental triggers can unmask genetic predisposition (p.2016-2017).","Chemotherapy agents are the most common drugs implicated in toxic cardiomyopathy; doxorubicin cardiotoxicity generally does NOT cause marked LV dilation, so stroke volume and perfusion can be low with only a modest fall in ejection fraction (p.2016).","Tests of thyroid function are part of the routine evaluation of cardiomyopathy because clinical signs of thyroid disease may be masked; hyperthyroidism should always be considered with new-onset atrial fibrillation or ventricular tachycardia (p.2017).","Heritability is present in at least 30% of DCM without other clear etiology, and expanded genetic testing reveals pathogenic variants sufficient to cause cardiomyopathy in 20-40% of DCM patients (p.2003, 2013)."],"pathophysiology":"DCM is characterized by decreased LV systolic function, typically with LV chamber dilation (LVEF <=0.50, usually <=0.40 at presentation). Multiple causes (idiopathic, familial/genetic, prior myocarditis, ischaemic, toxic/alcohol, chemotherapy, peripartum, tachycardia-mediated, metabolic/endocrine) converge on a common phenotype resembling post-infarct injury: early myocyte death, later apoptosis, and compensatory hypertrophy of remaining myocytes in response to increased wall stress. Local and circulating neurohormonal factors drive deleterious secondary remodeling; dynamic remodeling of the interstitial scaffold affects diastolic function and the degree of dilation. As the ventricle dilates, the mitral valve apparatus is distorted, producing functional mitral regurgitation. RV dilation/dysfunction develops directly from injury or, more often, secondarily from pulmonary hypertension and ventricular interaction. Chronic inflammation contributes to progression even when not initially implicated, and many 'acute' presentations have progressed silently over months to years (Harrison 22e p.2015).","additionalDifferentials":["Ischaemic cardiomyopathy / coronary artery disease or prior infarction (structural heart disease that can mimic or cause DCM, p.2002)","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM), including the ~5% that evolve to reduced EF with restrictive physiology (HCM-rEF) (p.2001-2002)","Restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM) - functional diagnosis based on diastolic dysfunction/elevated filling pressures; some sarcomeric variants and storage diseases overlap (p.2002)","Arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy (ACM-LV / ACM-RV/ARVC) where ventricular arrhythmias dominate before severely reduced LVEF (p.2002)","Primary valvular disease","Takotsubo (stress-induced) cardiomyopathy - distinct acute presentation with apical ballooning, often resolving (p.2019)","Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF) - overlaps with proposed obesity and diabetic cardiomyopathy (p.2018)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral myocarditis (coxsackie, adenovirus, COVID-19, influenza, HIV) progressing to dilated cardiomyopathy (p.2015-2016)","Chagas' disease (Trypanosoma cruzi) and other parasitic infection (toxoplasmosis, trypanosomiasis) (p.2016)","Bacterial (diphtheria), spirochetal (Lyme disease - Borrelia burgdorferi), rickettsial (Q fever), and fungal myocarditis (p.2016)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Granulomatous inflammatory disease - sarcoidosis (often regional wall-motion abnormality, DCM phenotype, or RV involvement) and giant cell myocarditis (p.2002, 2016)","Eosinophilic myocarditis / Loeffler endocarditis (can progress to restrictive physiology) (p.2015-2016)","Collagen vascular / connective tissue disease, polymyositis, dermatomyositis, systemic lupus erythematosus (p.2015-2016)","Immune checkpoint-inhibitor myocarditis (most dramatic noninfectious inflammatory myocarditis) (p.2016)","Hemochromatosis / iron overload (variably classified as metabolic or restrictive but often presents as DCM) (p.2018-2019)","Endocrinopathy - thyroid disease, pheochromocytoma; very-high-renin states (renal artery stenosis) (p.2017-2018)","Nutritional deficiency - thiamine (beriberi), selenium (Keshan's disease), carnitine, calcium, phosphate (p.2018)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography (initial imaging modality to define morphology and function) (p.2002)","Cardiac MRI for myocardial tissue characterization, late gadolinium enhancement patterns of fibrosis, and T1/T2 mapping for focal/diffuse inflammation (note: avoid gadolinium in pregnancy) (p.2002, 2018)","Electrocardiogram and chest radiograph (p.2003)","Chemistry including sodium, potassium, calcium, magnesium, glucose/glycohemoglobin, renal and liver function, lipids, thyroid-stimulating hormone, serum iron and transferrin saturation, urinalysis, creatine kinase isoforms, cardiac troponin (p.2003)","Hematology including CBC with differential, eosinophil count, ESR (p.2003)","Genetic counseling and multigene cardiomyopathy panel (p.2003)","Targeted infectious work-up when suspected (HIV, Chagas', Lyme, toxoplasmosis, trichinosis); rheumatologic serologies (p.2003)","Endomyocardial biopsy (including electron microscopy) when a specific diagnosis with therapeutic implications is suspected (p.2003)","Coronary angiography in patients with evidence of ischemia/infarction who are candidates for intervention (p.2003)"],"redFlags":["Sudden cardiac death risk: ventricular arrhythmias may be presenting events; persistent LVEF <=0.30-0.35 is the threshold for primary-prevention ICD (p.2001-2002, 2002)","Embolic events from atrial fibrillation or apical LV thrombi can be the first presentation (p.2002)","Peripartum cardiomyopathy with LVEF <0.35 carries higher risk of life-threatening arrhythmias at presentation and early after discharge, and marked dilation/LVEF <0.35 raises LV thrombus and embolic risk (p.2017-2018)","Acute cardiac presentation (elevated troponin, conduction blocks, bizarre arrhythmias) in a patient on immune checkpoint inhibitors warrants immediate coronary-care-unit admission and high-dose glucocorticoids without delay for biopsy (p.2016)","Hyperthyroidism combined with heart failure is a dangerous combination that may decompensate precipitously and fatally during titration of antithyroid therapy (p.2017)"],"prognosis":"DCM secondary responses are often modifiable or reversible. Almost a third of patients with recent-onset cardiomyopathy without coronary artery disease show substantial spontaneous recovery to normal ejection fraction, and partial recovery to LVEF >0.40 ('heart failure with improved ejection fraction') is common with guideline-directed neurohormonal therapy, CRT for LBBB, and diuretics. Alcoholic cardiomyopathy can improve markedly within 3-6 months of abstinence but has a grim prognosis if heavy drinking continues. In peripartum cardiomyopathy, LVEF improves to >=0.50 in 50-80% of cases (often within 6 months); recovery is less likely with LVEF <0.30, LV end-diastolic dimension >=0.60, black race, and presentation >6 weeks postpartum; U.S. 1-year mortality ranged 4-11% and up to twofold higher in Africa. Takotsubo syndrome has a generally good long-term prognosis (lowest mortality with emotional rather than physical triggers) but in-hospital complications/mortality resemble acute MI, with ~10% recurrence (Harrison 22e p.2002, 2016-2019).","pitfalls":["'Congestive heart failure' should NOT be considered a diagnosis or an etiology of cardiomyopathy - it is a nonspecific syndrome of congestion common to many cardiac diagnoses and requires thorough etiologic evaluation (p.2002).","Phenotypes overlap and evolve over time (DCM can reverse-remodel to higher EF; HCM can evolve to reduced EF), so reliance on a single morphologic phenotype is diminishing (p.2001-2002).","Alcoholic cardiomyopathy is a diagnosis of exclusion, and many patients are fully functional without apparent stigmata of alcoholism (p.2016).","Anthracycline cardiotoxicity can produce low stroke volume/perfusion with only modestly reduced EF because marked LV dilation is usually absent (p.2016).","In peripartum cardiomyopathy, edema and dyspnea may be mistakenly attributed to pregnancy itself; renin-angiotensin system inhibitors should NOT be given before delivery due to adverse fetal effects, and gadolinium should NOT be used on MRI during pregnancy (p.2017-2018).","Detection of anticardiac antibodies and viral genome fragments in chronic cardiomyopathy is of uncertain significance (possible cause vs neutral bystander) and is not in routine clinical use (p.2013).","Immunosuppression for nonspecific chronic lymphocytic myocarditis has not demonstrated convincing benefit (p.2013)."],"severityClassification":"Classification of cardiomyopathies: DCM phenotype - Early: LVEF <=0.50 and/or LVEDV >112% normal for age/sex or LVEDD >95% predicted for sex/height; LVEF <=0.40 is the threshold for traditional heart-failure therapies for low LVEF; persistent LVEF <=0.30-0.35 is the threshold for primary-prevention ICD. Mid-range LVEF 0.40-0.50 is often a transitional state (deterioration from early DCM or improvement into DCM remission). DCM is distinguished from HCM (increased wall thickness, septal >=15 mm men / >=13 mm women, LVEF >=normal usually >0.60), RCM (functional diagnosis from diastolic dysfunction/elevated filling pressures, often marked atrial enlargement), and arrhythmogenic CM (ACM-LV/ACM-RV/ARVC, ventricular arrhythmias dominating). (Harrison 22e p.2001-2002).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2001-2020"],"crossLinks":["heart_failure","atrial_fib"]},"diphtheria":{"name":"Diphtheria","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Corynebacterium diphtheriae infection"],"chapter":155,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1221-1225","clinicalPearls":["Respiratory diphtheria is clinically diagnosed by the constellation of sore throat, adherent tonsillar/pharyngeal/nasal pseudomembranous lesions, and low-grade fever (p.1223).","The diphtheritic pseudomembrane is gray or whitish and sharply demarcated; unlike the exudate of streptococcal pharyngitis it is tightly adherent to underlying tissue, and attempts to dislodge it may cause bleeding (p.1223).","A few patients develop 'bull-neck' diphtheria from edema of the submandibular and paratracheal region, with massive tonsillar swelling, foul breath, thick speech, and stridorous breathing (p.1223).","Diphtheria should be considered in any patient with severe pharyngitis, especially with difficulty swallowing, respiratory compromise, or signs of systemic disease (myocarditis, generalized weakness); a pharyngeal pseudomembrane or extensive exudate should prompt consideration (p.1222-1223).","Toxigenic strains most often cause pharyngeal disease, while nontoxigenic strains commonly cause cutaneous disease; in tropical latitudes cutaneous diphtheria is more common than respiratory diphtheria (p.1221-1222).","Once a clinical diagnosis is made, diphtheria antitoxin should be obtained and administered as rapidly as possible because antitoxin cannot neutralize cell-bound toxin (p.1224).","Hoarseness suggests laryngeal diphtheria, in which laryngoscopy may be diagnostically helpful (p.1223).","Antibiotics in diphtheria are used primarily to prevent transmission to contacts and to halt further toxin production; they are not a substitute for antitoxin (p.1224).","Patients who recover should be vaccinated, since natural infection does not reliably confer protective immunity; antitoxin levels should be measured (6 months after antitoxin administration) (p.1224)."],"pathophysiology":"Disease is driven by diphtheria toxin produced by toxigenic (tox+) strains of C. diphtheriae. The tox structural gene is carried by lysogenic corynebacteriophage beta, and nontoxigenic strains can be converted to the toxigenic phenotype; iron-limiting conditions optimize toxin expression. The toxin is a single-chain 535-amino-acid protein produced in the pharyngeal pseudomembranous lesion, taken up into the bloodstream and distributed to all organs. After binding its receptor (a heparin-binding EGF-like precursor) it is internalized by receptor-mediated endocytosis; the A fragment enters the cytosol and irreversibly inhibits protein synthesis via NAD+-dependent ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2, causing cell death. Pseudomembranes consist of an inner fibrin band and a luminal neutrophil band overlying toxin-induced mucosal ulcers with edema, hyperemia, and vascular congestion; initially white and firmly adherent, they turn gray/green/black as necrosis advances and may extend into bronchial airways (p.1221-1223).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Group A streptococcal pharyngitis (exudative, but its exudate is not tightly adherent unlike the diphtheritic pseudomembrane) (p.1223)","Group C streptococcal pharyngitis (p.1223)","Respiratory virus pharyngitis (rhinovirus, influenza, parainfluenza, coronavirus, adenovirus) (p.1223)","Herpes simplex virus and Epstein-Barr virus (infectious mononucleosis) pharyngitis (p.1223)","Atypical bacterial pharyngitis (Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydia pneumoniae) (p.1223)","Acute HIV infection, gonococcal pharyngitis, and fusobacterial infection / Lemierre's syndrome (p.1223)","Candida (thrush) pharyngitis (p.1223)","Corynebacterium ulcerans / C. pseudotuberculosis, which express the same toxin and cause diphtheria-like disease (p.1222)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Culture of pseudomembrane fragments or submembranous swabs on selective medium (Loffler's or Tinsdale's, tellurite/colistin/nalidixic-acid based) plus nonselective medium such as blood agar (p.1223-1224)","Histopathologic demonstration of compatible gram-positive organisms / characteristic local lesions (p.1223-1224)","Notify the laboratory that diphtheria is being considered so special selective medium and biochemical differentiation from commensal corynebacteria are performed (p.1224)","Toxigenicity testing; report all toxigenic C. diphtheriae isolates to the state health department (reportable disease) (p.1224)","Nasopharyngeal culture in patients with cutaneous diphtheria (p.1224)","Repeat throat culture ~2 weeks after completing antimicrobial therapy to document eradication (p.1224)","Cardiac workup to assess for myocarditis (p.1225)","Antitoxin (antibody) titer measurement after recovery, performed 6 months after antitoxin administration (p.1224)"],"redFlags":["Neck (submandibular/paratracheal) edema and difficulty breathing in advanced cases carry a poor prognosis (p.1223)","Airway obstruction is a major early risk and accounts for 60-65% of deaths, usually in the first 1-2 weeks; sloughing pseudomembranes may obstruct the airway or extend to larynx/tracheobronchial tree (children especially prone) (p.1224)","Toxic cardiomyopathy/myocarditis (cardiac arrhythmias or congestive heart failure), a late manifestation arising >=1 week after respiratory symptoms, accounting for 20-25% of deaths (p.1223-1224)","Bull-neck diphtheria, ventricular tachycardia, atrial fibrillation, or complete heart block (p.1224)","Polyneuropathy 3-5 weeks after onset: cranial nerve involvement (lingual/facial numbness, dysphonia, dysphagia), then weakness of respiratory and abdominal muscles, limb paresis, sensory ataxia, and autonomic dysfunction with hypotension (~15% of deaths) (p.1224)","Large or extensive pseudomembranes and laryngeal/tracheal/bronchial involvement indicate severe disease and poor prognosis (p.1223-1224)"],"prognosis":"A systematic review of >20 outbreaks found a case-fatality ratio of 29% among unvaccinated, untreated individuals, with children under 5 at 1.5-fold higher mortality risk. Fatal pseudomembranous diphtheria typically occurs in unimmunized patients or those with nonprotective antibody titers. Risk factors for death include bull-neck diphtheria; myocarditis with ventricular tachycardia; atrial fibrillation; complete heart block; age >60 or <6 months; alcoholism; extensive pseudomembrane elongation; and laryngeal/tracheal/bronchial involvement. A key predictor of fatal outcome is the interval between onset of local disease and administration of antitoxin. Cutaneous diphtheria has a low mortality rate and is rarely associated with myocarditis or peripheral neuropathy. Polyneuropathy is typically reversible in patients who survive the acute phase (p.1224-1225).","pitfalls":["Antitoxin cannot neutralize cell-bound toxin, so delay in administration worsens outcome; do not wait for laboratory confirmation once diphtheria is clinically suspected (p.1224).","Antibiotics are not a substitute for antitoxin; they mainly prevent transmission and further toxin production (p.1224).","Glucocorticoids have NOT been shown to reduce the risk of myocarditis or polyneuropathy (p.1225).","Cutaneous diphtheria lesions are not characteristic and are indistinguishable from other dermatoses, so diagnosis requires laboratory confirmation; the punched-out appearance is only occasional, not consistent (p.1224).","Corynebacterium pseudodiphtheriticum is a nontoxigenic normal throat commensal and does not pose significant risk; it must be differentiated from C. diphtheriae (p.1224).","Drug-resistant and multidrug-resistant C. diphtheriae strains exist and are increasing; consider resistance when eradication efforts fail (p.1224).","Natural infection does not reliably confer immunity, so survivors must be vaccinated (p.1224)."],"severityClassification":"CDC case definitions: confirmed respiratory diphtheria (laboratory proven or epidemiologically linked to a culture-confirmed case) vs probable respiratory diphtheria (clinically compatible but not laboratory proven or epidemiologically linked). Carriers are individuals with positive C. diphtheriae cultures who are asymptomatic or symptomatic but lack pseudomembranes (p.1223).","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Ch.155 (Diphtheria and Other Corynebacterial Infections), pp.1221-1225","Sharma NC et al: Diphtheria. Nat Rev Dis Primers 5:81, 2019"],"crossLinks":["PHARYNGITIS"]},"diseases_caused_by_gram_negative":{"name":"Diseases Caused by Gram-Negative Enteric Bacilli","system":"Multisystem / Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Enterobacterales infections","Gram-negative enteric bacilli infections","Escherichia coli infections","Klebsiella infections","Proteus infections","Enterobacter infections","Serratia infections","Carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales (CRE)"],"chapter":166,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1281","clinicalPearls":["The enteric gram-negative bacilli (GNB) that most often cause extraintestinal infection belong to the order Enterobacterales and include Escherichia coli, Klebsiella, Proteus, Enterobacter, Serratia, Citrobacter, Morganella, Providencia, Cronobacter, and Edwardsiella; Salmonella, Shigella, and Yersinia are covered separately as mainly enteric pathogens.","Isolation of a GNB from a normally sterile site almost always signifies true infection, whereas recovery from nonsterile sites (open wounds, respiratory tract, catheterized urine) needs clinical correlation because it may represent only colonization.","E. coli and, to a lesser extent, Klebsiella are the most virulent of this group and can cause severe disease even in previously healthy ambulatory people; the other genera behave mainly as opportunists in long-term-care and hospitalized patients.","The urinary tract is the single most common extraintestinal site infected by extraintestinal pathogenic E. coli (ExPEC), and E. coli is the leading uropathogen across essentially all UTI syndromes and host groups.","The urinary tract is also the most common source of E. coli bacteremia (roughly one-half to two-thirds of episodes); the abdomen is the second most common source (about one-quarter), and some abdominal sources such as abscesses can be clinically silent and require imaging.","E. coli and group B Streptococcus are leading causes of neonatal meningitis; most neonatal E. coli meningitis strains carry the K1 capsular antigen, and ventriculomegaly is a common complication.","Hypervirulent K. pneumoniae (hvKp), first recognized in Taiwan in 1986, classically presents as community-acquired monomicrobial pyogenic liver abscess in patients without prior hepatobiliary disease, often diabetic, with a striking tendency to spread metastatically (e.g., endophthalmitis, brain/splenic/prostatic abscess, meningitis, necrotizing fasciitis).","Proteus produces urease, which hydrolyzes urea to ammonia and alkalinizes the urine; this promotes struvite and carbonate-apatite crystals, catheter biofilms, and staghorn calculi, and the organism typically cannot be cleared without removing the stone or catheter.","Carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales (CRE) are classified by CDC and WHO as an urgent, critical-priority threat and were estimated to cause more than 100,000 deaths globally in 2019, with the heaviest burden in low- and middle-income regions such as the Indian subcontinent.","Pneumonia, sepsis, and septic shock arising from any GNB focus are especially dangerous, carrying reported mortality rates of about 20 to 60 percent.","Serratia marcescens is notable for environmental and outbreak associations (contaminated infusates, prefilled syringes, soaps, even chlorhexidine); some strains produce red pigment that can cause distinctive findings such as pink breast milk, pink hypopyon, or pseudohemoptysis.","In children with Shiga toxin-producing E. coli (STEC/EHEC) infection, antibiotics should be avoided because they may increase the risk of hemolytic-uremic syndrome (HUS), and adequate volume expansion may help protect the kidneys."],"pathophysiology":"Enteric GNB are predominantly extracellular pathogens that normally reside in the colonic microbiota and environmental niches. Their outer membrane (lipopolysaccharide and capsule) is central both to virulence and to antimicrobial resistance via permeability barriers and efflux pumps. Disease requires accessory virulence genes: multiple adhesins (e.g., type 1, S, and F1C fimbriae and P pili in E. coli) for attachment; iron-acquisition systems (siderophores) and other nutrient-scavenging tools; capsule and O-antigen to resist complement and phagocytosis in the absence of antibody; and toxins (e.g., E. coli hemolysin) that cause tissue damage facilitating spread. The lipid A moiety of lipopolysaccharide acts as a PAMP that, through pattern-recognition receptors, can trigger an exuberant proinflammatory cascade leading to septic shock, with DAMPs such as HMGB1 propagating injury. Extensive antigenic variation (E. coli alone has more than 150 O, ~80 K, and ~53 H antigens) permits immune evasion and repeated infection and has hampered vaccine development. Host defenses are innate (intact skin/mucosa, complement, antimicrobial peptides, phagocytes, nutrient withholding) and humoral; impairment of these increases both susceptibility and severity. Intestinal pathogenic E. coli use distinct, pathotype-specific mechanisms (Shiga toxin, heat-stable/labile enterotoxins, attaching-and-effacing lesions, epithelial invasion, aggregative adherence).","additionalDifferentials":["Other facultative and anaerobic members of the intestinal microbiota in polymicrobial intraabdominal infection","Group B Streptococcus as a co-leading cause of neonatal meningitis","Streptococcus pneumoniae and other community-acquired pneumonia pathogens (hvKp pneumonia can mimic pneumococcal disease in Asia)","Pseudomonas and other nonfermenting gram-negative bacilli causing health care-associated infection","Giardia or Cryptosporidium (and opportunists in immunocompromised hosts) when diarrhea persists beyond about 10 days"],"infectionMimics":["Colonization without infection (positive sputum culture without pneumonia, or bacteriuria without UTI), which can be mistaken for active infection particularly in elderly or functionally compromised patients","Other urea-splitting uropathogens producing alkaline urine and struvite stones","Polymicrobial intraabdominal and soft-tissue infections in which the pathogenic role of any single GNB is uncertain"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Scombroid (fish) poisoning, in which Proteus-mediated histamine generation in spoiled fish produces a histamine-toxicity syndrome rather than infection","Adherent-invasive E. coli associated with Crohn disease (causal role unproven; does not produce acute diarrheal illness)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Culture of the primary site of infection before starting antimicrobials, plus blood cultures in systemically ill patients","MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry, nucleic acid amplification tests, sequencing, and immunoassays for rapid organism identification and detection of resistance genes","Genotypic and phenotypic carbapenemase detection to guide infection control and therapy","Cross-sectional imaging (e.g., CT) to find occult abdominal sources such as abscesses underlying bacteremia","For suspected STEC/EHEC: CDC recommends evaluating all community-acquired diarrhea by simultaneous stool culture plus detection of Shiga toxin or its genes, since bloody stool and fecal leukocytes are not reliably present","Identification of the five biomarkers iucA, iroB, peg-344, rmpA, and rmpA2 is currently the most accurate way to identify hypervirulent K. pneumoniae; the string/hypermucoviscosity test is suboptimally sensitive and specific","Sorbitol-nonfermentation screening with serotyping and Shiga toxin testing to identify O157 STEC"],"redFlags":["Fever and/or back/flank pain in a patient with cystitis, suggesting progression to pyelonephritis","Persistently rising fever, flank pain, and neutrophilia during treated pyelonephritis, prompting evaluation for intrarenal or perinephric abscess or obstruction","Sepsis or septic shock arising from any GNB focus (organ dysfunction, hypotension)","Community-acquired pyogenic liver abscess in a patient without hepatobiliary disease, especially of Asian background and/or diabetic, raising concern for hvKp with metastatic spread (endophthalmitis, CNS abscess, necrotizing fasciitis)","Grossly bloody diarrhea without fever, suggesting STEC/EHEC and risk of hemolytic-uremic syndrome","Myositis or fasciitis of the thigh due to E. coli, which should prompt a search for an abdominal source spreading contiguously","Neonatal infection (bacteremia, necrotizing enterocolitis, meningitis), including Cronobacter sakazakii linked to contaminated powdered infant formula"],"prognosis":"Outcomes correlate with severity of illness, underlying host status, and the resistance profile of the infecting organism, which can lead to suboptimal therapy. Pneumonia, sepsis, and septic shock from any GNB focus carry mortality rates of roughly 20 to 60 percent; ExPEC pneumonia specifically has high crude (20-60%) and attributable (10-20%) mortality. Uncomplicated cystitis is largely self-limited and rarely progresses, whereas pyelonephritis can take 5-7 days for fever to resolve even with effective therapy and only uncommonly causes renal parenchymal loss (mainly with obstruction). hvKp infections can be life- and organ-threatening even in young healthy hosts, with survivors often left with catastrophic sequelae such as vision loss or major neurologic deficits. Pandrug-resistant strains exist, and extensive resistance can leave few or no ideal therapeutic options.","pitfalls":["Treating colonization rather than true infection (e.g., asymptomatic bacteriuria or a positive sputum culture without pneumonia), which drives resistance and harm without benefit","Attributing E. coli bacteremia to a urinary source in the absence of characteristic UTI signs, especially given high rates of asymptomatic bacteriuria in elderly and functionally compromised patients, thereby missing an occult abdominal focus","Relying on third-generation cephalosporins for organisms prone to inducible/derepressed AmpC beta-lactamase (e.g., Enterobacter cloacae, Klebsiella aerogenes, Citrobacter freundii), in which resistance can emerge during therapy and cause clinical failure, particularly in bacteremia","Giving antibiotics for STEC/EHEC infection, which may increase the incidence of HUS through increased Shiga toxin release","Assuming carbapenems cover all Enterobacterales: Proteus, Morganella, and Providencia are intrinsically resistant to imipenem, and tetracycline derivatives and polymyxins are poorly active against the tribe Proteeae and Serratia and may achieve inadequate urine/blood levels","Failing to achieve source control (abscess drainage, debridement of necrotic tissue, removal of infected foreign bodies), without which antimicrobial therapy often fails","Empirical reliance on fluoroquinolones in critically ill patients, which is now unreliable given widespread GNB resistance often linked to ESBL production"],"severityClassification":"Disease is stratified clinically rather than by a single formal score: localized/uncomplicated infection (e.g., uncomplicated cystitis with dysuria, frequency, urgency, suprapubic pain) versus systemic/severe infection (febrile or systemic UTI/urosepsis, pyelonephritis, prostatitis, pneumonia, bacteremia, sepsis syndrome defined as dysfunction of at least one organ/system, and septic shock). Resistance is graded as multidrug-resistant (MDR), extensively drug-resistant (XDR), and pandrug-resistant; resistance mechanisms range from broad-spectrum beta-lactamases through ESBLs and AmpC enzymes to carbapenemases (Ambler class A serine [KPC], class B metallo-beta-lactamases [NDM, VIM, IMP], and class D [OXA-48]).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1281","Harrison 22e p.1282","Harrison 22e p.1283","Harrison 22e p.1284","Harrison 22e p.1285","Harrison 22e p.1286","Harrison 22e p.1287","Harrison 22e p.1289","Harrison 22e p.1290","Harrison 22e p.1291","Harrison 22e p.1292","Harrison 22e p.1293"],"crossLinks":[]},"diseases_of_esophagus":{"name":"Diseases of the Esophagus","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["GERD","achalasia","eosinophilic esophagitis","esophageal motility disorders","Barrett esophagus"],"chapter":334,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2503-2513","clinicalPearls":["The major esophageal symptoms are heartburn, regurgitation, chest pain, dysphagia, odynophagia, and globus sensation; the clinical history is central to evaluation (Harrison 22e p.2503).","Dysphagia for both liquids and solids suggests a motility disorder such as achalasia, whereas dysphagia for solids alone suggests a structural lesion (stricture, web, ring, or tumor) (Harrison 22e p.2504).","Patient localization of where food 'hangs up' is notoriously imprecise; about 30% of distal esophageal obstructions are perceived as cervical (neck) dysphagia (Harrison 22e p.2504).","Intermittent solid-food dysphagia that is unchanged or slowly progressive over years points to a benign process (Schatzki ring or eosinophilic esophagitis), whereas dysphagia progressing over weeks to months raises concern for neoplasia (Harrison 22e p.295).","Odynophagia is uncommon with reflux esophagitis; its presence should raise suspicion of an alternative etiology, particularly pill-induced or infectious esophagitis (Harrison 22e p.2505, p.2513).","Achalasia is diagnosed by barium swallow and/or esophageal manometry; the barium study shows a dilated esophagus with poor emptying, an air-fluid level, and a tapered 'beak-like' narrowing at the LES (Harrison 22e p.2507).","Manometric hallmarks of achalasia are impaired (incomplete) deglutitive LES relaxation plus absent peristalsis; manometry is the most sensitive test as it detects disease before esophageal dilatation (Harrison 22e p.2507-2508).","Endoscopy is a necessary part of the achalasia evaluation to exclude pseudoachalasia from tumor infiltration at the cardia/distal esophagus, which accounts for up to 5% of suspected cases (Harrison 22e p.2507).","The GERD injury spectrum progresses from esophagitis to stricture to Barrett metaplasia to adenocarcinoma; esophageal adenocarcinoma incidence is rising in parallel with GERD (Harrison 22e p.2509).","Erosive esophagitis at the esophagogastric junction is the endoscopic hallmark of GERD but is found in only about one-third of GERD patients (Harrison 22e p.2510).","Eosinophilic esophagitis should be strongly considered in children and adults presenting with dysphagia and food impaction, especially with an atopic history (Harrison 22e p.2511).","EoE is confirmed histologically by esophageal mucosal eosinophilia with a peak density of 15 or more eosinophils per high-power field, in the setting of esophageal symptoms (Harrison 22e p.2512)."],"pathophysiology":"The esophagus is a muscular tube with a sphincter at each end (UES, LES) that transports food and otherwise remains empty. Disease manifests as impaired function (swallowing disorders, excessive gastroesophageal reflux) or pain. Dysphagia is subclassified by mechanism: structural/mechanical narrowing (lumen <13 mm reliably causes solid-food dysphagia) versus propulsive/motor disorders of peristalsis and deglutitive inhibition. In achalasia, autoimmune-mediated loss of myenteric plexus ganglion cells (involving both excitatory cholinergic and inhibitory nitric-oxide neurons), linked to latent HSV-1 infection plus genetic susceptibility, produces impaired deglutitive LES relaxation and absent peristalsis. GERD results from excessive reflux of gastric acid and pepsin causing a cytokine-mediated (rather than directly caustic) inflammatory mucosal injury; pathologic reflux follows progressive distortion of esophagogastric junction architecture (hiatus widening, loss of intraabdominal esophageal segment/flap valve, axial hiatal hernia, increased junction compliance) compounded by impaired acid clearance from disrupted peristalsis or reduced salivation. Eosinophilic esophagitis is an immunologic disorder driven by antigen (chiefly food allergen) sensitization in susceptible individuals, with eosinophil-predominant inflammation. (Harrison 22e p.2503, p.2506-2510, p.2511)","additionalDifferentials":["Achalasia (primary)","Distal esophageal spasm (DES)","Hypercontractile/jackhammer esophagus","Scleroderma esophagus (absent contractility with weak LES)","Pseudoachalasia from tumor infiltration of the cardia/distal esophagus","Chagas disease (Trypanosoma cruzi)","Opioid-induced esophageal dysmotility","Peptic stricture","Schatzki ring and esophageal webs (Plummer-Vinson/Paterson-Kelly syndrome)","Esophageal carcinoma (adenocarcinoma, squamous cell)","Pill-induced esophagitis","Coronary artery disease (for esophageal-type chest pain)","Functional heartburn / disorders of gut-brain interaction","Zenker and epiphrenic diverticula","Hiatal hernia"],"infectionMimics":["Candida esophagitis (white plaques/exudate; C. albicans most common; associated with stasis and immunosuppression)","Herpes simplex virus esophagitis (vesicles and small superficial ulcers; coexisting nose/lip vesicles)","Cytomegalovirus esophagitis (large serpiginous distal ulcers in immunocompromised hosts)","Acute HIV seroconversion esophageal ulceration (with oral ulcers and maculopapular rash)","Tuberculosis or histoplasmosis causing traction midesophageal diverticula"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Coronary artery disease / angina mimicking esophageal chest pain","Eosinophilic esophagitis mimicking GERD","Scleroderma and connective tissue disease","Drug hypersensitivity causing esophageal eosinophilia","Crohn disease and eosinophilic gastroenteritis","Pill esophagitis (singular deep ulcer at points of luminal narrowing, often near the carina, sparing distal esophagus)","Caustic/corrosive and radiation injury","Mucocutaneous diseases involving the esophagus (pemphigoid, lichen planus, epidermolysis bullosa)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper endoscopy (EGD) - single most useful test for esophageal dysphagia; allows biopsy and dilation","Barium contrast radiography / video fluoroscopic swallow study (better for proximal/cricopharyngeal lesions and esophageal function; 13-mm barium tablet increases stricture detection)","Esophageal high-resolution manometry / pressure topography (diagnose achalasia and DES; assess peristalsis before antireflux surgery)","Functional lumen imaging probe (FLIP) panometry (can evaluate esophageal dysphagia in lieu of manometry; strong correlation for achalasia and normal contractility)","Ambulatory 24-96 h esophageal pH monitoring (pH <4 for >6% of the day indicates GERD), optionally with intraluminal impedance for nonacidic reflux","Esophageal mucosal biopsies (required to distinguish infectious vs eosinophilic vs reflux esophagitis; peak >=15 eosinophils/HPF for EoE)","Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) for staging esophageal cancer and assessing Barrett dysplasia / submucosal lesions","CT scanning when pseudoachalasia is suspected and endoscopy nondiagnostic"],"redFlags":["Progressive solid-food dysphagia with weight loss - classic presentation of esophageal cancer; warrants prompt endoscopy (Harrison 22e p.2507).","Odynophagia (painful swallowing) - usually indicates ulceration; raises suspicion of pill-induced or infectious esophagitis (Harrison 22e p.295, p.2513).","Iron-deficiency anemia / chronic occult GI bleeding accompanying esophageal symptoms.","Hoarseness developing after dysphagia - may indicate recurrent laryngeal nerve compromise by malignancy (Harrison 22e p.295).","Abrupt onset of symptoms (<1 year), advanced age, and weight loss in apparent achalasia - suggests pseudoachalasia from malignancy (Harrison 22e p.2507).","Food impaction with inability to pass a bolus even with liquids - typical of structural dysphagia (Harrison 22e p.295).","Severe cough with swallowing - may signal tracheoesophageal fistula.","Finding of high-grade dysplasia in Barrett esophagus - high rate of progression to and coexisting prevalence of adenocarcinoma; mandates intervention (Harrison 22e p.2511)."],"prognosis":"Esophageal cancer carries poor survival even when small because abundant esophageal lymphatics permit early regional nodal metastasis. Barrett metaplasia progresses to adenocarcinoma at an estimated 0.1-0.3% per year through low- and high-grade dysplasia, with greatest risk in obese white males in the sixth decade. Untreated achalasia predisposes to stasis esophagitis and increases squamous cell cancer risk roughly 17-fold; durable therapies (pneumatic dilation, Heller myotomy, POEM) achieve symptom relief in about 80-90% of patients, though peristalsis does not recover and chest pain responds less well than dysphagia. EoE is chronic with nearly universal recurrence on stopping therapy, requiring maintenance treatment. Erosive esophagitis and peptic strictures have become increasingly rare in the era of potent acid suppression. (Harrison 22e p.2507-2508, p.2510-2512)","pitfalls":["Treating presumed GERD empirically without recognizing that persistent dysphagia or chest pain may instead reflect eosinophilic esophagitis, peptic stricture, motility disorder, esophageal cancer, or coronary artery disease (Harrison 22e p.2510).","Attributing 'heartburn' to GERD when the term is misused for indigestion or other sensations - clarify the patient's meaning (Harrison 22e p.2503).","Assuming neck-localized dysphagia is oropharyngeal - about 30% of distal esophageal lesions refer pain proximally (Harrison 22e p.2504).","Mistaking pseudoachalasia (tumor infiltration, up to 5% of cases) for primary achalasia - omit endoscopy at peril (Harrison 22e p.2507).","Diagnosing distal esophageal spasm before excluding the more common peptic and infectious esophagitis and, above all, cardiac disease (Harrison 22e p.2509).","Aggressive esophageal dilation in EoE - the stiff-walled esophagus risks deep mural laceration or perforation (Harrison 22e p.2512).","Relying on peripheral blood eosinophilia (present in only 25-50%) or allergy testing (poor sensitivity/specificity) to diagnose EoE or identify trigger foods (Harrison 22e p.2512).","Over-interpreting nonspecific minor manometric findings (hypertensive/hypotensive peristalsis) as a defined motility disorder; reflux and anxiety/depression are common in these patients (Harrison 22e p.2509)."],"severityClassification":"GERD esophageal injury spectrum: esophagitis -> stricture -> Barrett metaplasia -> adenocarcinoma; reflux testing defines GERD as esophageal pH <4 for >6% of the day. Barrett progression staged histologically as no dysplasia -> low-grade dysplasia -> high-grade dysplasia -> adenocarcinoma. High-resolution manometry differentiates three achalasia subtypes: type I classic (minimal esophageal-body pressurization), type II with esophageal compression/pressurization, and type III spastic achalasia. EoE histologic threshold: peak >=15 eosinophils per high-power field. (Harrison 22e p.2508-2511)","references":["Harrison 22e p.2503-2513","Harrison 22e ch.47 (Dysphagia) p.293-296","Shaheen NJ et al. ACG guideline: diagnosis and management of Barrett's esophagus. Am J Gastroenterol 117:1177, 2022 (Harrison 22e p.2503 Further Reading)"],"crossLinks":["gerd_chest","peptic_ulcer"]},"diseases_of_the_aorta":{"name":"Diseases of the Aorta","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Aortic aneurysm","Aortic dissection","Aortitis","Thoracic aortic aneurysm","Abdominal aortic aneurysm","Acute aortic syndrome"],"chapter":291,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2165","clinicalPearls":["The aorta is uniquely prone to aneurysm and rupture because, per Laplace's law, wall tension rises with the product of pressure and radius, so dilation begets further dilation; normal adult diameters are roughly 3.5 cm at origin/ascending, 2.5 cm in descending thoracic, and 1.8-2.0 cm in the abdomen.","True aneurysms involve all three wall layers; pseudoaneurysms have disrupted intima and media, with the dilated segment contained only by adventitia (and sometimes perivascular clot). Aneurysms are described as fusiform (whole circumference) or saccular (focal outpouching).","A contemporary threshold for an aneurysm of the aortic root/ascending aorta is diameter >=4.5 cm, while 4.0-4.4 cm is termed aortic dilation; descending thoracic and abdominal aneurysms are defined when dilated >=50% beyond the adjacent normal segment.","Medial degeneration (loss of medial smooth muscle and breakdown of collagen/elastin with mucoid/proteoglycan clefts) preferentially affects the proximal aorta and underlies ascending aneurysms in Marfan, Loeys-Dietz, vascular Ehlers-Danlos, bicuspid aortic valve, Turner, and familial thoracic aortic aneurysm syndromes; atherosclerosis is the dominant cause of descending thoracic and abdominal aneurysms.","Thoracic aortic aneurysms grow on average about 0.1-0.2 cm/year and are usually asymptomatic; symptoms (chest pain, dyspnea, cough, hoarseness, dysphagia) arise from compression/erosion, and ascending dilation can cause aortic regurgitation and heart failure.","Aortic dissection classically presents with sudden, severe, tearing chest or interscapular pain with diaphoresis that migrates as the dissection propagates; complications include syncope, pulse deficits, acute aortic regurgitation, pulmonary edema, and end-organ ischemia (stroke, paraplegia, bowel/renal/myocardial ischemia).","Stanford type A dissection involves the ascending aorta; type B is limited to arch and/or descending aorta. The type A/B scheme is more practical for management than the DeBakey I/II/III classification because DeBakey I and II are managed similarly.","Acute aortic regurgitation complicates more than 50% of proximal (type A) dissections, producing bounding pulses, wide pulse pressure, and a diastolic murmur along the right sternal border; retrograde type A dissection can cause hemopericardium and tamponade.","Intramural hematoma (rupture of the vasa vasorum, no intimal flap) and penetrating atherosclerotic ulcer are dissection variants, mostly in the descending thoracic aorta, and either can progress to dissection, false aneurysm, or rupture.","Abdominal aortic aneurysms are more common in men, increase with age, and at least 90% of those >4.0 cm are atherosclerotic and infrarenal; they are usually silent and found as a pulsatile, expansile, nontender mass, while new aneurysmal pain is a harbinger of impending rupture.","An ECG without ischemic changes helps distinguish aortic dissection from myocardial infarction in a patient with chest pain; rarely the dissection involves a coronary ostium (usually right) and causes acute MI.","Acute occlusion of the distal abdominal aorta is an emergency threatening limb viability, usually from a cardiac-origin saddle embolus, presenting with rest pain, coolness, pallor, and bilaterally absent distal pulses.","Aortic occlusive disease from atherosclerosis is typically infrarenal and, when extending to the iliacs, can cause Leriche syndrome (buttock/thigh/calf claudication with impotence in men) and absent femoral pulses with an abdominal bruit."],"pathophysiology":"The aortic wall comprises a thin endothelial intima, a thick smooth muscle/elastin/collagen tunica media, and a connective-tissue adventitia carrying the vasa vasorum. Continuous high pulsatile pressure and shear stress predispose it to injury. Aneurysms result from degradation or abnormal production of structural elastin and collagen; degenerative (most common, atherosclerosis-associated) aneurysms involve inflammation, oxidative stress, proteolysis (B/T lymphocytes, macrophages, cytokines, matrix metalloproteinases) and biomechanical stress, with histology showing elastin/collagen destruction, reduced smooth muscle, neovascularization, and inflammation. Heritable aneurysms reflect medial degeneration: fibrillin-1 deficiency (FBN1 mutation in Marfan) impairs elastic-fiber microfibrils and increases TGF-beta signaling, while Loeys-Dietz arises from mutations in TGF-beta isoforms/receptors and SMAD3; vascular Ehlers-Danlos involves COL3A1 (type III procollagen) mutations. Dissection begins with an intimal tear (often the right lateral ascending aorta or just below the ligamentum arteriosum) or medial hemorrhage, with pulsatile flow splitting the elastic lamellae to create a false lumen that usually propagates distally. Aortitis is inflammation of the wall (vasculitic, rheumatic, or infective) that may produce aneurysmal dilation, aortic regurgitation, or branch occlusion.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute myocardial infarction","Pulmonary embolism","Acute pericarditis with effusion","Musculoskeletal or pleuritic chest pain","Esophageal rupture/spasm","Acute mesenteric ischemia (when abdominal pain predominates)","Renal colic or other causes of flank/back pain"],"infectionMimics":["Syphilitic aortitis (late luetic infection, typically proximal ascending aorta)","Tuberculous aortic aneurysm (extension from hilar nodes/contiguous abscess)","Mycotic aneurysm from Staphylococcus, Streptococcus, Salmonella, or fungi seeding atherosclerotic plaque"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Takayasu arteritis (large-vessel vasculitis, 'pulseless disease')","Giant cell arteritis with aortic involvement","IgG4-related aortitis with retroperitoneal fibrosis","Isolated aortitis","Rheumatic aortitis (rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis and other HLA-B27 spondyloarthropathies, relapsing polychondritis, IBD-associated)","Behcet syndrome and Cogan syndrome","Connective-tissue aortopathies (Marfan, Loeys-Dietz, vascular Ehlers-Danlos) and fibromuscular dysplasia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest x-ray (widened mediastinum, displaced/compressed trachea or left main bronchus; left pleural effusion in dissection)","Contrast-enhanced CT angiography (rapid, most commonly used for dissection; sensitivity/specificity >90%)","MRI/MR angiography (intimal flap, extent, branch involvement, intramural hematoma, penetrating ulcer)","Transthoracic echocardiography (overall sensitivity 60-85%; >80% for proximal ascending dissection)","Transesophageal echocardiography (about 98% sensitivity, ~90% specificity for ascending and descending dissection; less useful for the arch)","Abdominal ultrasound (sizing and surveillance of AAA, detection of mural thrombus; screening tool)","Blood cultures (frequently positive in mycotic aneurysm and infective aortitis)","ESR and C-reactive protein (elevated in active Takayasu and other aortitides)","Genetic testing in ascending thoracic aortic disease with features of Marfan/Loeys-Dietz/vascular Ehlers-Danlos or presentation under age 60"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe tearing/migrating chest or interscapular pain with diaphoresis (acute dissection)","Acute hypotension or shock suggesting rupture or tamponade","New aortic regurgitation murmur, pulse deficits, or blood-pressure differential between limbs","Neurologic deficits (hemiplegia, paraplegia) indicating carotid or spinal cord ischemia","New or worsening aneurysmal pain (back, flank, scrotum) as a harbinger of AAA rupture","Acute limb ischemia with bilaterally absent distal pulses (acute aortic occlusion)","Fever, sepsis, and aortic/back pain with positive blood cultures (mycotic aneurysm/infective aortitis)"],"prognosis":"Rupture risk rises with aneurysm size and symptoms: thoracic aneurysms carry roughly 2-3%/year rupture risk below 4.0 cm rising to about 7%/year above 6 cm; for abdominal aneurysms the 5-year rupture risk is about 1-2% under 5 cm versus 20-40% above 5 cm. Marfan and certain Loeys-Dietz/nonsyndromic familial aortopathies dissect at smaller diameters and expand faster. Elective AAA surgery with careful evaluation carries about 1-2% operative mortality, whereas emergent repair after rupture carries roughly 45-50% mortality. For dissection, in-hospital mortality is about 15-25% after type A surgery and around 12% for medically managed type B; with careful follow-up the 10-year survival after treated dissection is approximately 60%, though Marfan patients remain at high risk for post-dissection complications.","pitfalls":["A left pleural effusion in dissection is usually serosanguineous and reactive, NOT necessarily a sign of rupture unless accompanied by hypotension and falling hematocrit.","About 25% of abdominal aortic aneurysms are not calcified and cannot be seen on plain x-ray, so a normal radiograph does not exclude an AAA.","Mural thrombus can reduce the luminal diameter, so contrast aortography may underestimate true aneurysm size.","Isolated direct vasodilators such as hydralazine are contraindicated in acute dissection because they raise hydraulic shear and heart rate and can propagate the dissection.","Transthoracic and even transesophageal echocardiography are relatively poor at imaging the aortic arch, a potential blind spot for dissection.","Serum IgG4 levels may be elevated but are not diagnostic of IgG4-related aortitis.","With adequate collateral flow, complete abdominal aortic occlusion can occur without ischemic symptoms, masking the severity of occlusive disease.","Genetic/heritable aortopathies warrant lower size thresholds for intervention; applying standard atherosclerotic thresholds risks under-treatment."],"severityClassification":"Aortic dissection: Stanford type A (involves ascending aorta, proximal) vs type B (arch and/or descending only, distal); DeBakey type I (ascending with propagation to arch/descending/abdominal), type II (limited to ascending), type III (descending with distal propagation). Type A/B is preferred for management decisions. Acute aortic syndromes encompass aortic rupture, dissection, intramural hematoma, and penetrating atherosclerotic ulcer.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2165","Harrison 22e p.2166","Harrison 22e p.2167","Harrison 22e p.2168","Harrison 22e p.2169","Harrison 22e p.2170","Harrison 22e p.2171"],"crossLinks":[]},"diseases_of_the_spinal_cord":{"name":"Diseases of the Spinal Cord","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Myelopathy","Spinal cord disease","Spinal cord disorders"],"chapter":453,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3559","clinicalPearls":["A horizontally defined level below which sensory, motor, and autonomic function is impaired is the hallmark of a spinal cord lesion; the sensory level for pinprick/temperature is sought by moving the stimulus upward and is typically one to two segments below a unilateral cord lesion because second-order spinothalamic fibers ascend before crossing.","Because the cord segments do not align with the like-numbered vertebrae, lesion localization must account for offset: a sensory level at the umbilicus reflects T10 cord pathology, located adjacent to the seventh or eighth thoracic vertebral body.","Spinal shock can produce flaccid, areflexic paralysis acutely (lasting days, rarely weeks) and may be mistaken for extensive anterior horn cell damage or an acute polyneuropathy; hyperreflexia and spasticity supervene later, but persistent areflexic paralysis with a sensory level usually signifies necrosis across multiple cord segments.","Brown-Sequard (hemicord) syndrome combines ipsilateral weakness and loss of joint position/vibration sense with contralateral loss of pain and temperature one to two levels below the lesion; partial forms are more common than the full syndrome.","Central cord syndrome produces arm weakness out of proportion to leg weakness and a dissociated, suspended cape-distribution sensory loss (loss of pain/temperature with preserved touch, position, and vibration), classically from trauma, syringomyelia, or intrinsic cord tumor.","Anterior cord syndrome (anterior spinal artery territory) abolishes motor, pain/temperature, and autonomic function while strikingly sparing vibration and position sense carried in the posterior columns.","Sacral sparing favors an intramedullary process, reflecting the lamination of the spinothalamic tract with sacral fibers outermost; extramedullary lesions instead tend to cause prominent radicular pain and early sacral sensory loss.","Conus medullaris lesions cause symmetric saddle anesthesia (S3-S5), early prominent bladder/bowel dysfunction with lax anal tone and impotence, and relatively preserved strength; cauda equina lesions instead cause low back and radicular pain, asymmetric leg weakness/sensory loss, areflexia, and relative sparing of bowel/bladder.","A recent onset of persistent thoracic back pain that worsens with movement, coughing, or recumbency and awakens the patient at night should raise concern for vertebral metastasis, since the thoracic spine is uncommonly involved by spondylosis.","Spinal epidural abscess classically presents as the triad of midline back/neck pain, fever, and progressive limb weakness; once myelopathic signs appear progression can be rapid and irreversible, so early recognition is critical.","Spondylotic (degenerative cervical) myelopathy is the most common cause of myelopathy and gait disturbance in the elderly, suspected with progressive spastic paraparesis, foot/hand paresthesias, or wasting of intrinsic hand muscles.","Subacute combined degeneration from vitamin B12 deficiency classically shows the combination of an absent ankle reflex (peripheral neuropathy) with an extensor plantar (Babinski) response, plus loss of vibration/position sense and a positive Romberg sign."],"pathophysiology":"The spinal cord packs nearly the entire motor output and sensory input of the trunk and limbs into a small cross-sectional area, so focal lesions produce disproportionately severe deficits (quadriplegia, paraplegia, sensory loss). Peripherally located white matter tracts carry ascending sensory (posterior columns for position/vibration, spinothalamic for pain/temperature) and descending motor (corticospinal) pathways, while gray matter contains neuronal cell bodies. The crossing pattern of the spinothalamic tract (decussating anterior to the central canal one to two segments above entry) and the same-side course of the posterior columns and corticospinal tracts generate the characteristic clinical syndromes. The cord is supplied by a single anterior spinal artery (anterior two-thirds) and paired posterior spinal arteries (posterior columns); a watershed zone in the upper thoracic cord makes T3-T4 vulnerable to infarction during hypotension or aortic cross-clamping. Disease mechanisms are grouped into compressive (tumor, abscess, hematoma, disk, spondylosis), vascular/ischemic, inflammatory/immune (MS, NMO, sarcoid, systemic autoimmune), infectious/postinfectious, metabolic/nutritional (B12, copper deficiency), developmental (syringomyelia, tethered cord), and degenerative/genetic categories.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute polyneuropathy (e.g., Guillain-Barre) when ascending paresthesias begin in the feet -- bladder dysfunction and a sharp sensory level point instead to the cord","Compressive causes: epidural/intradural/intramedullary neoplasm, epidural abscess, epidural hematoma, herniated disk, cervical spondylosis, post-traumatic vertebral displacement","Vascular: anterior spinal artery infarction, dural arteriovenous fistula/AVM, hematomyelia, antiphospholipid syndrome and hypercoagulable states","Inflammatory/immune: multiple sclerosis, neuromyelitis optica (AQP-4), anti-MOG and anti-GFAP disorders, sarcoidosis, SLE, Sjogren's, Behcet's, vasculitis, CLIPPERS, Erdheim-Chester","Metabolic/nutritional: vitamin B12 deficiency (subacute combined degeneration), folate deficiency, copper deficiency, nitrous oxide-induced myelopathy","Degenerative/genetic: hereditary spastic paraplegia, primary lateral sclerosis, adrenomyeloneuropathy","Developmental: syringomyelia, tethered cord syndrome, meningomyelocele","Cancer-related: radiation myelopathy, intrathecal methotrexate toxicity, paraneoplastic (anti-amphiphysin, CRMP5, Hu), checkpoint-inhibitor myelopathy"],"infectionMimics":["Spinal epidural abscess (most commonly Staphylococcus aureus, including MRSA)","Tuberculous spondylitis (Pott's disease) with epidural extension","Viral myelitis: herpes zoster, HSV-1 and HSV-2 (Elsberg syndrome with recurrent sacral neuritis), EBV, CMV, rabies, Zika","Poliomyelitis and polio-like acute flaccid paralysis (enterovirus A-71, D-68, West Nile and other flaviviruses)","Bacterial/mycobacterial myelitis: Borrelia burgdorferi (Lyme), Listeria, Mycobacterium tuberculosis, Treponema pallidum (syphilis, tabes dorsalis)","Retroviral myelopathy: HTLV-1 (tropical spastic paraparesis) and HIV vacuolar myelopathy","Parasitic: schistosomiasis (granulomatous), toxoplasmosis (especially in AIDS), cysticercosis","Postinfectious/postvaccinal myelitis (EBV, CMV, mycoplasma, influenza, measles, varicella, mumps, SARS-CoV-2)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Multiple sclerosis and neuromyelitis optica presenting as acute or chronic myelitis","Anterior spinal artery infarction mimicking acute transverse myelitis","Dural arteriovenous fistula causing progressive myelopathy that can simulate MS or, with mixed upper/lower motor neuron signs, ALS","Foix-Alajouanine syndrome (subacute necrotic myelopathy from intramedullary vascular malformation)","Subacute combined degeneration and hypocupric myelopathy mimicking each other (B12 normal in copper deficiency)","Primary lateral sclerosis and hereditary spastic paraplegia presenting as slowly progressive spastic paraparesis","Spinal sarcoidosis mimicking tumor or NMO on imaging","Toxic myelopathies: lathyrism (BOAA in chickpeas) and Konzo (cyanogenic cassava)"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the spinal cord with and without gadolinium, centered on the suspected level, as the initial procedure to exclude compression; imaging the entire spine is often appropriate since up to 40% of patients with cord compression at one level have asymptomatic epidural metastases elsewhere","CSF analysis: cell count, protein, glucose, IgG index/synthesis rate, oligoclonal bands, VDRL, Gram/acid-fast/India ink stains, PCR for VZV/HSV/EBV/CMV/HHV-6/enterovirus/HIV, antibodies for HTLV-1, Borrelia, Mycoplasma, Chlamydia","Serum autoantibodies: anti-aquaporin-4, anti-MOG, anti-GFAP for NMO and related disorders","Serum vitamin B12 with macrocytic indices, and confirmatory elevated homocysteine and methylmalonic acid; serum copper and ceruloplasmin for hypocupric myelopathy","Workup for systemic autoimmune disease: ESR, ANA, dsDNA, rheumatoid factor, anti-SSA/SSB, complement, antiphospholipid/anticardiolipin antibodies, pANCA; serum/CSF ACE, calcium, chest CT, gallium scan for sarcoid","Vascular evaluation: MR/CT angiography and selective spinal angiography (definitive) for suspected AVM or dural fistula; CT myelogram when MRI unavailable","Paraneoplastic antibody panel (amphiphysin, CRMP5, Hu) when malignancy suspected","Diffusion-weighted MRI and recognition of cord-infarction signs (anterior 'pencil-like' T2 signal, 'owl's eyes' axial hyperintensities, adjacent vertebral body infarction)"],"redFlags":["Neck or back pain with progressive limb weakness, bladder disturbance, or a sensory level -- treatable cord compression must be excluded urgently with MRI","Fever with spinal pain and progressive weakness suggesting epidural abscess","New persistent thoracic back pain in a patient with known or suspected malignancy","Hyperextension neck injury in a patient with degenerative cervical stenosis (acute spondylotic myelopathy / central cord syndrome)","Autonomic dysreflexia (paroxysmal hypertension, headache, flushing, diaphoresis, bradycardia) from a noxious stimulus below a lesion at or above T6","Quadriplegic fever and loss of normal somatic/visceral pain signaling masking infection, thrombophlebitis, or intra-abdominal pathology in chronic cord injury","Diaphragmatic weakness with upper cervical lesions"],"prognosis":"Many spinal cord diseases are reversible if recognized and treated early, making them among the most critical neurologic emergencies. For neoplastic compression, ambulatory status at presentation predicts a good outcome; motor deficits established for more than 12 hours rarely improve, and beyond 48 hours the prognosis for substantial motor recovery is poor. With prompt diagnosis and treatment of spinal epidural abscess, up to two-thirds of patients achieve significant recovery, but fixed deficits more than several days old are unlikely to reverse. After cord infarction, severe motor weakness and persistent areflexia predict poor recovery, though more than half of patients in one series regained some ambulation. The prospects for recovery from an acute destructive lesion fade after about 6 months, and there are currently no effective means to repair injured cord tissue. Even complete high cervical lesions can be compatible with a productive life; ultimate disability is determined chiefly by lesion level and whether the deficit is complete or incomplete.","pitfalls":["Mistaking spinal shock (acute flaccid areflexic paralysis) for a polyneuropathy or for diffuse anterior horn cell disease","Attributing ascending foot paresthesias to peripheral neuropathy and overlooking bladder dysfunction and a sensory level indicating a cord origin","Relying on plain radiographs or radionuclide bone scans, which miss 15-20% of metastatic vertebral lesions and fail to detect paravertebral masses reaching the epidural space through the foramina","Gadolinium contrast can deceptively 'normalize' the appearance of a vertebral metastasis on T1 imaging by raising its signal to that of normal marrow","Failing to image the entire spine when cord compression is found at one level, missing clinically silent additional metastases","Assuming MRI is sensitive for cord infarction early -- it may be normal in the first day","Interpreting HTLV-1 seropositivity alone as proof of causation for a myelopathy in endemic areas","Lumbar puncture in severe thrombocytopenia/coagulopathy (risk of epidural hematoma) or through tissue overlying an epidural abscess (risk of seeding meningitis) -- a high cervical tap may be safer","Overlooking copper deficiency (with normal B12) as a cause of a subacute-combined-degeneration-like myelopathy, particularly after bariatric surgery or with excess zinc intake"],"severityClassification":"Functional outcome after complete cord lesions is stratified by level (Harrison Table 453-4): high quadriplegia (C1-C4) -- dependent on others, requires respiratory support, motorized wheelchair; low quadriplegia (C5-C8) -- partially independent with adaptive equipment, may use a manual wheelchair and drive with adaptations; paraplegia (below T1) -- independent in self-care and transfers, ambulates short distances with aids. Lesions are also characterized clinically as complete versus incomplete, and intramedullary versus extramedullary (and extradural versus intradural, the former generally malignant and the latter benign).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3559","Harrison 22e p.3560","Harrison 22e p.3562","Harrison 22e p.3565","Harrison 22e p.3567","Harrison 22e p.3569"],"crossLinks":[]},"disorders_caused_by_venomous_snakebites":{"name":"Disorders Caused by Venomous Snakebites and Marine Animal Exposures","system":"Toxicology / Envenomation (multisystem)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Venomous snakebite","Snake envenomation","Marine envenomation","Marine animal stings","Scombroid (histamine) fish poisoning","Ciguatera poisoning"],"chapter":471,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3716-3727","clinicalPearls":["Medically important venomous snakes fall into Viperidae (Old World vipers and New World/Asian pit vipers), Elapidae (cobras, kraits, coral snakes, sea snakes, Australian elapids), the burrowing asps (Atractaspidinae), and a few colubrids; snake envenomation is described as the world's deadliest neglected tropical disease, with roughly 63,000 deaths globally in 2019.","A large fraction of bites are 'dry' (no venom injected) - about 20-25% of pit viper bites and up to 75% of sea snake bites - so the presence of fang marks alone does not equal envenomation.","Viperid/cytotoxic patterns produce progressive local pain, soft-tissue swelling, ecchymosis, and hemorrhagic or serum-filled blisters, while neurotoxic elapids (kraits, some cobras, Australian elapids) and certain vipers cause cranial nerve dysfunction (ptosis, dysphagia) progressing to descending paralysis and respiratory failure.","Onset and tempo of symptoms vary with species, bite location, and venom dose; coral snakes, sea snakes, and some non-North American vipers (e.g., hump-nosed pit viper) can have systemic effects delayed by several hours, mandating prolonged observation.","In resource-limited settings the bedside 20-minute whole-blood clotting test detects venom-induced coagulopathy: 1-2 mL venous blood left undisturbed in a clean dry glass tube that fails to clot at 20 minutes indicates coagulopathy.","Antivenom binds and neutralizes circulating (unbound) venom; it is largely ineffective at reversing damage already established (e.g., fixed paralysis, established renal failure) and offers little cross-protection outside the species it was raised against, so species-appropriate selection is essential.","Compartment syndrome is uncommon after snakebite (fasciotomy needed in under 1% of cases); in North American crotalid bites fasciotomy lacks supporting evidence and may increase morbidity, since effective antivenom lowers compartment pressure.","Recurrent or delayed coagulopathy can appear during the first 2-3 weeks after a bite because venom antigens may outlast antivenom, so patients are warned to avoid elective surgery/high-trauma activity and to have repeat coagulation testing.","Long-term sequelae include permanent limb functional loss from muscle/nerve/vascular injury or scar contracture, and pituitary insufficiency described after Russell's viper (Daboia russelii) envenomation.","Among marine animals, jellyfish/box jellyfish (Chironex fleckeri), Portuguese man-of-war (Physalia), and the Irukandji jellyfish (Carukia barnesi) cause the most dangerous cnidarian stings; Irukandji syndrome is a catecholamine-excess state with hypertension, tachycardia, severe pain, and risk of pulmonary edema and cardiomyopathy.","Blue-ringed octopus (Hapalochlaena) and pufferfish (order Tetraodontiformes, e.g., fugu) both cause tetrodotoxin toxicity - a sodium-channel blocker producing rapid flaccid paralysis with preserved mentation; consciousness is typically intact even during complete paralysis, so analgesia/sedation matter.","Scombroid (histamine) fish poisoning mimics an allergic reaction (flushing, urticaria, headache, palpitations) and arises when bacteria decarboxylate histidine to histamine in poorly refrigerated fish; the toxin is heat-stable and reactions are more severe in patients on isoniazid (histaminase blockade).","Ciguatera, caused by heat-stable ciguatoxins (Gambierdiscus toxicus) bioaccumulated in large reef fish, classically produces GI symptoms plus neurologic features including reversal of hot/cold temperature sensation and a sensation of 'loose teeth'; neurologic symptoms can persist for weeks."],"pathophysiology":"Snake venoms are complex mixtures of enzymes, polypeptides, and glycoproteins whose composition varies by species, age, diet, geography, and even within a species. They produce harm through several mechanisms: direct local tissue necrosis, disruption of the coagulation cascade at multiple steps (consumptive coagulopathy, defibrination, thrombocytopenia), organ dysfunction (notably renal), and action at the neuromuscular junction causing paralysis. Early hypotension is partly mediated by bradykinin-potentiating factors causing vasodilation and pooling, compounded by systemic bleeding, hemolysis, and third-spacing of intravascular volume. Renal injury is usually acute tubular necrosis (often reversible), but bilateral cortical necrosis carries a poorer prognosis. Marine envenomations operate by distinct toxins: cnidarian nematocysts inject venom containing phospholipases, metalloproteases, pore-forming toxins, neurotoxins, and bioactive amines (histamine, serotonin); cone snail conotoxins block acetylcholine receptors and ion channels; blue-ringed octopus and pufferfish deliver tetrodotoxin, a potent peripheral sodium-channel blocker. Marine seafood poisonings are ingestion toxidromes: scombroid from bacterial conversion of histidine to histamine in spoiled fish, and ciguatera from ciguatoxins that activate neuronal sodium channels and antagonize voltage-gated potassium channels.","additionalDifferentials":["Nonvenomous snakebite or 'dry' bite (fang marks without envenomation)","Bite by a harmless species whose saliva causes mild local pain/edema (can mimic mild envenomation)","Cellulitis or other local soft-tissue infection","Allergic reaction / anaphylaxis (especially scombroid, which mimics food allergy)","Ciguatera differential per Harrison: paralytic shellfish poisoning, eosinophilic meningitis, type E botulism, organophosphate poisoning, tetrodotoxin poisoning, psychogenic hyperventilation"],"infectionMimics":["Cellulitis (rapidly spreading local swelling and erythema after a bite can resemble bacterial cellulitis)","Secondary bacterial wound infection complicating an envenomation (marine wounds: Vibrio in saltwater, Aeromonas in freshwater; Staphylococcus/Streptococcus)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Anaphylaxis to venom or to antivenom itself","Compartment syndrome from other causes of limb swelling/trauma","Primary cardiac dysrhythmia or shock from non-toxic causes","Acute neuromuscular paralysis from other etiologies (e.g., botulism, Guillain-Barre) when neurotoxic envenomation presents with descending weakness"],"additionalInvestigations":["Complete blood count (assess hemorrhage, hemolysis, thrombocytopenia)","Blood type and cross-match","Renal and hepatic function panels","Coagulation studies: prothrombin time and fibrinogen level; fibrin degradation products / D-dimer","Creatine kinase for suspected rhabdomyolysis; urine for blood/myoglobin","20-minute whole-blood clotting test where laboratory access is limited","Serial limb circumference measurements at three points every 15 min until local effects stabilize","ECG and chest radiography in severe envenomation or significant comorbidity","Intracompartmental pressure measurement if compartment syndrome suspected (threshold 30-40 mmHg)","Imaging (soft-tissue radiography, ultrasound, or MRI) to locate retained marine spines/foreign bodies","Repeat coagulation and CBC monitoring for recurrent coagulopathy after discharge"],"redFlags":["Bite to the face or neck (risk of rapid airway-threatening swelling)","Cranial nerve signs such as ptosis or difficulty swallowing (herald impending respiratory compromise in neurotoxic envenomation)","Rapidly progressing swelling involving more than half the bitten limb, extensive blistering/bruising, or severe pain","Spontaneous systemic bleeding, hemolysis, or laboratory evidence of coagulopathy","Hypotension or shock unresponsive to fluids","Rhabdomyolysis with falling urine output / acute renal failure","Irukandji syndrome features: severe hypertension, chest/abdominal pain, then risk of pulmonary edema and cardiomyopathy","Tetrodotoxin exposure (blue-ringed octopus, pufferfish): oral/facial numbness progressing to flaccid paralysis and respiratory failure"],"prognosis":"In settings with rapid access to care and appropriate antivenom, mortality is low - under 1% in the United States among victims who receive antivenom. Eastern and western diamondback rattlesnakes account for most U.S. snakebite deaths; globally, major killers include cobras, saw-scaled/carpet vipers (Echis), Russell's vipers, large African vipers (Bitis), lanceheads (Bothrops), and tropical rattlesnakes (Crotalus durissus). Morbidity from permanent limb functional loss is substantial and can be devastating in the developing world; in the U.S. it is more common and severe after rattlesnake bites than after copperhead or water moccasin bites. Renal failure from acute tubular necrosis is frequently reversible, whereas bilateral cortical necrosis carries a worse renal prognosis. For tetrodotoxin/blue-ringed octopus envenomation, recovery usually occurs within 24-48 h and long-term sequelae are uncommon unless hypoxia/hypoperfusion supervened. Most scombroid cases are mild and self-limited (resolving in 8-12 h), and ciguatera GI symptoms typically settle in 24-48 h though neurologic symptoms may persist for days to weeks (and a delayed 'second-phase' syndrome can last 2-6 weeks after parrotfish exposure).","pitfalls":["Treating fang marks alone as proof of envenomation - many bites are dry and antivenom in such cases is useless and potentially harmful.","Relying on color pattern, head shape, or pupil shape to judge whether a snake is venomous - nonvenomous species frequently mimic these features.","Discharging a patient too early: significant toxicity from an apparently 'dry' viperid bite can develop after several hours, and elapid/sea snake/some viper systemic effects are characteristically delayed.","Using outdated first aid (incision, suction, tourniquets/venom-sequestration devices) - these worsen local damage and increase infection risk; pressure-immobilization is reserved for reliably identified primarily neurotoxic species, not viperids.","Relying on antivenom skin testing for hypersensitivity - it is neither sensitive nor specific; prophylactic antihistamines/glucocorticoids have not proven beneficial.","Performing fasciotomy reflexively for limb swelling in North American crotalid bites - evidence does not support it and it may increase morbidity.","Giving blood products before adequate antivenom - this can fuel ongoing consumptive coagulopathy.","In marine cnidarian stings, applying vinegar indiscriminately - it relieves pain for Indo-Pacific species (C. fleckeri, C. barnesi) but can increase nematocyst discharge in Physalia physalis and Chrysaora quinquecirrha; rubbing the area also worsens stinging.","Mislabeling scombroid poisoning as a seafood allergy, leading to unnecessary lifelong fish avoidance.","Using beta-blockers in Irukandji syndrome hypertension - risks worsening hypertension via unopposed alpha effects."],"severityClassification":"Harrison grades pit viper envenomation severity (U.S./Canada framework) as: None - fang marks only ('dry' bite); Mild - local findings only (pain, ecchymosis, nonprogressive swelling); Moderate - clearly progressing swelling, systemic symptoms/signs, and/or laboratory abnormalities; Severe - neurologic dysfunction, respiratory distress, and/or cardiovascular instability/shock.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3716","Harrison 22e p.3717","Harrison 22e p.3718","Harrison 22e p.3719","Harrison 22e p.3722","Harrison 22e p.3723","Harrison 22e p.3724","Harrison 22e p.3725","Harrison 22e p.3726","Harrison 22e p.3727"],"crossLinks":[]},"disorders_of_absorption":{"name":"Malabsorption (Disorders of Absorption)","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["malabsorption","celiac disease","coeliac disease"],"chapter":336,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2540-2550","clinicalPearls":["Steatorrhea (fecal fat excretion >7% of dietary fat) is a common manifestation of malabsorption, producing large, bulky, malodorous stools; malabsorptive diarrhea is typically precipitated by eating and lessens or resolves with fasting, which helps distinguish it from secretory (e.g., enterotoxigenic) diarrhea that persists during fasting (Harrison 22e p.2540-2541).","Celiac disease (celiac sprue / gluten-sensitive enteropathy) is a small-intestinal enteropathy from an immune response to gluten (wheat, rye, barley, some oats), characterized by autoantibodies to tissue transglutaminase; it primarily affects the proximal small bowel, so duodenal-limited disease may present as isolated iron-deficiency anemia while diffuse jejunal disease causes severe diarrhea, weight loss, and multinutrient malabsorption (Harrison 22e p.2545).","Tissue transglutaminase IgA (TTG-IgA) is the celiac screening test of choice; serum IgA should be measured concurrently because IgA deficiency causes false-negative TTG-IgA, in which case IgG-based testing (TTG-IgG or anti-deamidated gliadin peptide DGP-IgG) is used (Harrison 22e p.2545).","In adults with positive celiac serology, diagnosis is confirmed by endoscopy with small-intestinal biopsy showing villus blunting, crypt hyperplasia, and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes; the Marsh classification grades severity (Harrison 22e p.2545).","PITFALL: celiac serologic and histologic testing must be performed while the patient is consuming gluten, since self-discontinuation of gluten before testing produces false-negative results; HLA-DQ2/DQ8 testing is then useful because a negative result rules out celiac disease (negative predictive value >99%) (Harrison 22e p.2544-2545).","HLA-DQ2 and DQ8 are necessary but not sufficient for celiac disease and are present in 25-35% of the general population, so their detection is NOT useful for diagnosis; their value lies in the very high negative predictive value when absent (Harrison 22e p.2544).","Dermatitis herpetiformis, a vesicular rash mediated by IgA skin deposits, is highly associated with celiac disease; celiac disease also carries increased risk of other autoimmune disorders (type 1 diabetes, autoimmune thyroid disease) and is more frequent in Down and Turner syndromes (Harrison 22e p.2545).","The mainstay of celiac treatment is a strict lifelong gluten-free diet with rigorous dietitian instruction; the most common cause of persistent symptoms or residual disease activity is dietary nonadherence or inadvertent gluten exposure (Harrison 22e p.2545-2546).","Tropical sprue presents with chronic diarrhea, steatorrhea, weight loss, and deficiencies of BOTH folate and vitamin B12; malabsorption of two unrelated substances is required for diagnosis, and it is associated with residence in or travel to specific tropical regions (southern India, Pakistan, Philippines, Puerto Rico, Haiti, Cuba) (Harrison 22e p.2546-2547).","In India, tropical sprue is regarded by some as a postinfectious complication occurring in 8-20% of people after an attack of infectious gastroenteritis, with southern Indian patients often reporting acute enteritis preceding steatorrhea; tropical sprue is rarely seen in Africa, Jamaica, or Southeast Asia, and its overall incidence has declined (Harrison 22e p.2546-2547).","Whipple's disease (Tropheryma whipplei, a PAS-positive non-acid-fast rod) most often affects middle-aged men; arthralgias/arthritis precede GI symptoms by an average of ~6 years (joint disease in >80%), and CNS and cardiac involvement occur later; diagnosis uses biopsy with PCR and PAS-positive macrophages (Harrison 22e p.2546).","Small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth arises from stasis (scleroderma, pseudo-obstruction, blind loops, strictures, diverticulosis) and from hypochlorhydria (chronic PPI use); it causes B12 deficiency (macrocytic anemia, peripheral neuropathy) from bacterial B12 metabolism, yet serum folate may be ELEVATED due to bacterial folate synthesis (Harrison 22e p.2543).","In ileal disease/resection, <100 cm involvement causes bile-acid spillage into the colon and secretory (cholerheic) diarrhea responsive to bile-acid sequestrants, whereas >100 cm exceeds hepatic synthetic capacity to replace lost bile acids, making fat malabsorption (steatorrhea) the dominant mechanism (Harrison 22e p.2542-2543).","Chronic pancreatitis and exocrine pancreatic insufficiency cause voluminous steatorrhea with deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins (most commonly D and K), and stool elastase is helpful for diagnosing pancreatic insufficiency (Harrison 22e p.2542, 2549).","In short-bowel/lipid malabsorption with an intact colon in continuity, luminal fatty acids saponify calcium, freeing oxalate for colonic absorption and increasing the risk of calcium-oxalate renal stones (hyperoxaluria) (Harrison 22e p.2548)."],"pathophysiology":"Malabsorption results from disruption of one or more of the three phases of nutrient assimilation. (1) Luminal/digestive phase: intraluminal hydrolysis by gastric and pancreatic enzymes and solubilization of fat in bile-salt mixed micelles; impaired by postgastrectomy rapid transit, pancreatic insufficiency, hepatobiliary disease, ileal disease interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile acids, and small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth (which deconjugates bile acids causing luminal bile-acid deficiency). (2) Mucosal/absorptive phase: brush-border enzymatic digestion (disaccharidases, peptidases, enterokinase) and enterocyte uptake; impaired by isolated brush-border defects (lactase deficiency, glucose-galactose malabsorption, sucrase-isomaltase deficiency) or by diffuse enteropathies (celiac disease, tropical sprue, Whipple's disease, Crohn's, infections). In celiac disease, tissue transglutaminase deamidates gluten-derived peptides, promoting their presentation by antigen-presenting cells in HLA-DQ2/DQ8 individuals, producing proximal villus atrophy with crypt hyperplasia; steatorrhea arises from mucosal malabsorption plus a secretory component from crypt hyperplasia/hypersecretion, and secondary lactase deficiency may develop. (3) Postmucosal/delivery phase: re-esterification of long-chain fatty acids into chylomicrons exiting via lymphatics; impaired by abetalipoproteinemia (failed chylomicron formation) and by lymphatic obstruction/dysfunction (lymphangiectasia, lymphoma, retroperitoneal fibrosis) or circulatory causes (Fontan physiology, constrictive pericarditis, CHF), which also cause protein-losing enteropathy. Specific nutrient deficiencies localize disease: duodenal/proximal disease causes iron and folate deficiency; ileal disease/resection causes B12 deficiency and bile-acid malabsorption; steatorrhea causes fat-soluble vitamin (A, D, E, K) deficiency (Harrison 22e p.2540-2549).","additionalDifferentials":["Celiac disease (gluten-sensitive enteropathy) (Harrison 22e p.2545)","Tropical sprue (Harrison 22e p.2546)","Whipple's disease (Tropheryma whipplei) (Harrison 22e p.2546)","Small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth (Harrison 22e p.2543)","Chronic pancreatitis / exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (Harrison 22e p.2542)","Lactose intolerance from lactase deficiency (Harrison 22e p.2544)","Bile-acid (cholerheic) diarrhea from ileal disease/resection or primary bile-acid diarrhea (Harrison 22e p.2543)","Short-bowel syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2547-2548)","Crohn's disease with ileal involvement (Harrison 22e p.2542-2543)","Protein-losing enteropathy and intestinal lymphangiectasia (Harrison 22e p.2548-2549)","Abetalipoproteinemia (Harrison 22e p.2545)","Environmental enteric dysfunction (formerly tropical enteropathy) (Harrison 22e p.2547)","Nonceliac gluten sensitivity (Harrison 22e p.2546)"],"infectionMimics":["Giardia lamblia (giardiasis) (Harrison 22e p.2547)","Whipple's disease (Tropheryma whipplei) (Harrison 22e p.2546)","Tropical-environment enteric infections to exclude before diagnosing tropical sprue: Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium parvum, Cystoisospora (Isospora) belli, Strongyloides stercoralis, Cyclospora cayetanensis, Yersinia enterocolitica, Clostridioides difficile (Harrison 22e p.2547)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Irritable bowel syndrome (celiac disease can mimic IBS; SIBO and bile-acid malabsorption occur in IBS-D) (Harrison 22e p.2543, 2546)","Microscopic colitis (Harrison 22e p.2546)","Intestinal lymphoma, including enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.2546)","Crohn's disease (Harrison 22e p.2542)","Hyperthyroidism causing rapid-transit diarrhea/malabsorption (Harrison 22e p.2542)","Long-standing diabetes mellitus with enteric autonomic neuropathy (Harrison 22e p.2542)","Scleroderma and other connective-tissue/motility disorders (Harrison 22e p.2542)","Menetrier's disease (hypertrophic gastropathy) as a cause of protein-losing enteropathy (Harrison 22e p.2549)"],"additionalInvestigations":["72-h quantitative fecal fat collection on a 100-g fat diet (gold standard for steatorrhea); qualitative spot Sudan III stain for fecal fat (Harrison 22e p.2549)","24-h stool weight/volume to confirm clinically significant diarrhea; stool osmotic gap (from spot fecal Na and K) to identify osmotic diarrhea (Harrison 22e p.2549)","Tissue transglutaminase IgA (TTG-IgA) with concurrent serum total IgA; TTG-IgG or anti-deamidated gliadin peptide (DGP-IgG) if IgA-deficient (Harrison 22e p.2545)","HLA-DQ2/DQ8 testing chiefly to rule OUT celiac disease (high negative predictive value), especially if gluten was discontinued before testing (Harrison 22e p.2544-2545)","Endoscopy with small-intestinal (duodenal) biopsy; Marsh grading; in celiac, follow-up serology and a follow-up biopsy to document mucosal healing (Harrison 22e p.2545)","Stool elastase for pancreatic insufficiency (Harrison 22e p.2549)","SIBO testing: duodenal aspirate for bacterial titers (gold standard, limited availability) or lactulose/glucose breath hydrogen testing (interpret carefully for false positives) (Harrison 22e p.2543)","Lactose intolerance: lactose-exclusion diet trial, lactose-tolerance test (serum glucose rise <20 mg/L is positive), or breath hydrogen test (>20 ppm rise) (Harrison 22e p.2544-2545)","Protein-losing enteropathy: stool alpha-1 antitrypsin (spot or 24-h) or alpha-1 antitrypsin clearance; lymphopenia with hypoproteinemia supports GI protein loss (Harrison 22e p.2548-2549)","Whipple's disease: tissue PCR for T. whipplei, PAS staining, immunohistochemistry on biopsy (Harrison 22e p.2546)","Labs reflecting site-specific deficiency: iron studies, B12 and folate, fat-soluble vitamins, PT/INR as a functional marker of vitamin K, serum carotene; cross-sectional imaging (MR enterography/CT), video capsule and double-balloon enteroscopy for occult mucosal/lymphatic disease (Harrison 22e p.2549)"],"redFlags":["Persistent diarrhea, weight loss, and villus atrophy after >=1 year of a strict gluten-free diet with negative serology, indicating refractory celiac disease (Harrison 22e p.2546)","Type 2 refractory celiac disease (clonal CD3+ intraepithelial lymphocytes with monoclonal T-cell receptor gamma-chain rearrangement), which progresses to enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma in 33-50% within 5 years (Harrison 22e p.2546)","New or worsening malabsorption with abdominal mass/imaging lymphadenopathy suggesting small-bowel adenocarcinoma or intestinal lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.2546)","Hypoproteinemia with peripheral edema, diarrhea, and lymphopenia (protein-losing enteropathy) requiring exclusion of cardiac and lymphatic causes (Harrison 22e p.2548)","Severe vitamin K deficiency causing coagulopathy (elevated PT/INR) and significant B12-related peripheral neuropathy (Harrison 22e p.2549)","Whipple's disease relapse, frequently heralding first CNS involvement (Harrison 22e p.2546)"],"prognosis":"Most patients with celiac disease respond fully to a strict lifelong gluten-free diet with mucosal healing and symptom resolution; about 10% of those with persistent active disease develop refractory celiac disease, and type 2 refractory disease carries a poor prognosis with enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma in 33-50% of cases at 5 years (requiring intensive chemotherapy and sometimes stem cell transplantation). Tropical sprue is most often curative with broad-spectrum antibiotics plus folic acid, especially if the patient leaves the tropical area and does not return. Whipple's disease requires prolonged antibiotics, but relapses are common. In short-bowel syndrome the intestine adapts over up to 2 years (with reports of continued improvement at 3-5 years); prognosis and parenteral-nutrition dependence relate to the extent of resection, presence of colon in continuity, and retention of the ileocecal valve, with intestinal failure defined by persistent parenteral-nutrition dependence (Harrison 22e p.2542-2548).","pitfalls":["Testing for celiac disease (serology and biopsy) only after the patient has already self-removed gluten gives false-negative results; testing must be done ON a gluten-containing diet, or use HLA-DQ2/DQ8 to exclude disease (Harrison 22e p.2544-2545).","Interpreting HLA-DQ2/DQ8 positivity as diagnostic of celiac disease is wrong, as 25-35% of the general population carry these alleles without disease; only a negative result is clinically useful (rules out) (Harrison 22e p.2544).","Missing IgA deficiency when only TTG-IgA is ordered will produce false-negative celiac screening; order total serum IgA and use IgG-based tests if deficient (Harrison 22e p.2545).","Diagnosing tropical sprue before excluding enteric infection: it should NOT be entertained until cysts and trophozoites are excluded in three stool samples, and a gluten-free diet does NOT improve tropical sprue (unlike celiac disease) (Harrison 22e p.2547).","Breath hydrogen testing for lactose intolerance or SIBO can be inaccurate with abnormal gastric emptying or transit, and SIBO can itself produce false-positive/abnormal breath tests and mimic lactose intolerance (Harrison 22e p.2543, 2545).","In SIBO, expecting low folate is a trap; serum folate may be ELEVATED from bacterial synthesis while B12 is low (Harrison 22e p.2543).","In protein-losing enteropathy, isolated low albumin with normal globulins suggests renal/hepatic disease rather than GI loss; alpha-1 antitrypsin cannot assess gastric protein loss because it is degraded in acid (Harrison 22e p.2548-2549).","Treating the hypoproteinemia of protein-losing enteropathy directly rather than the underlying disease is misdirected; therapy should target the underlying cause (Harrison 22e p.2549)."],"severityClassification":"There is no single unified severity scale for malabsorption; severity is assessed by the degree of nutrient deficiency and steatorrhea and by disease-specific schemes. Celiac mucosal injury is graded by the Marsh classification of biopsy lesions (intraepithelial lymphocytosis, crypt hyperplasia, and degrees of villus atrophy). Celiac complications are stratified into refractory celiac disease type 1 (normal intraepithelial lymphocyte population, better prognosis) versus type 2 (clonal CD3+ intraepithelial lymphocytes with monoclonal T-cell receptor gamma rearrangement, worse prognosis with high lymphoma risk). Bile-acid versus fatty-acid diarrhea are distinguished by extent of ileal disease (<100 cm vs >100 cm), degree of steatorrhea, and response to cholestyramine versus a low-fat diet. Short-bowel syndrome is defined as <200 cm of remaining small bowel, and intestinal failure as persistent parenteral-nutrition dependence (generally <100 cm of small bowel with no colon in continuity) (Harrison 22e p.2543, 2545-2548).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2540-2550"],"crossLinks":["whipple_disease","chronic_pancreatitis","iron_deficiency_anemia"]},"disorders_of_the_autonomic_nervous":{"name":"Disorders of the Autonomic Nervous System","system":"Nervous system (autonomic)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Dysautonomia","Autonomic failure","Autonomic neuropathy","Orthostatic hypotension","Postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome (POTS)","Multiple system atrophy (Shy-Drager syndrome)","Pure autonomic failure","Autoimmune autonomic ganglionopathy"],"chapter":451,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3538","clinicalPearls":["The autonomic nervous system has parasympathetic (craniosacral), sympathetic (thoracolumbar, T1-L2), and enteric divisions; acetylcholine is the preganglionic transmitter for both arms and the postganglionic parasympathetic transmitter, while norepinephrine is the postganglionic sympathetic transmitter (except eccrine sweat glands, which use acetylcholine).","Dysautonomia is best classified clinically by organ specificity (heart, vessels, sweat glands, pupils, gastrointestinal, genitourinary) and by time course (acute/subacute/chronic, progressive/static), which helps narrow the differential.","Classical orthostatic hypotension is a sustained fall of systolic BP >=20 mmHg or diastolic BP >=10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing or head-up tilt >=60 degrees; in hypertensive patients the threshold is >=30/15 mmHg.","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension is suggested by a blunted compensatory heart-rate rise (<15 beats/min); a heart-rate-rise/systolic-BP-fall ratio below 0.5 beats/min/mmHg after 3 minutes more accurately indicates neurogenic OH and reflects impaired cardiac baroreflex gain.","POTS is defined as chronic (>3 months) orthostatic intolerance WITHOUT sustained orthostatic hypotension, with a heart-rate rise of >=30 beats/min (>=40 if under age 19) or a rate above 120 beats/min within 10 minutes of standing; it is a sinus-rhythm disorder, four to five times more common in women aged 15-45.","About half of POTS cases follow a viral illness (notably EBV, coronavirus, enteroviruses); it is the leading manifestation of long COVID, and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome predisposes in up to 25% of patients.","Heart-rate variation to deep breathing tests the vagal (parasympathetic) baroreflex arm; the Valsalva maneuver assesses both parasympathetic (Valsalva ratio) and sympathetic (adrenergic index, BP recovery) arms; both are diminished in most autonomic failure, diabetes, and alpha-synuclein disorders.","Sudomotor localization: a postganglionic lesion shows absent sweating on BOTH QSART and thermoregulatory sweat test (TST), whereas a preganglionic lesion preserves QSART but shows anhidrosis on TST.","Multiple system atrophy (Shy-Drager) combines autonomic failure with parkinsonism (MSA-P) or cerebellar features (MSA-C); the parkinsonism responds poorly/transiently to levodopa and rest tremor is not prominent.","Cardiac sympathetic innervation imaging (MIBG scintigraphy or fluorodopamine PET) is typically PRESERVED in MSA but reduced in Parkinson disease, Lewy body disease, and pure autonomic failure, aiding differentiation.","Autoimmune autonomic ganglionopathy is a treatable cause associated with high-titer antibodies to the alpha-3 ganglionic acetylcholine nicotinic receptor; it may be paraneoplastic (small cell lung cancer, thymoma, lymphoma), so malignancy screening is prudent.","Autonomic dysreflexia is a dramatic BP surge triggered by bladder, bowel, or skin irritation in spinal cord lesions above T6; lesions below T6 permit splanchnic vasodilation and rarely cause it."],"pathophysiology":"The autonomic nervous system operates as an integrated reflex circuit linking sensory feedback from organs with central processing (insular and prefrontal cortex, limbic system, hypothalamus; brainstem nucleus tractus solitarius, dorsal motor vagal nucleus, nucleus ambiguus) and the spinal intermediolateral cell columns, then to peripheral ganglia and target organs. Dysfunction may arise from decreased activity, overactivity, or dysregulation at the afferent limb, central processor, or efferent limb. In orthostatic hypotension, standing shifts roughly 500-700 mL of blood to the lower limbs and splanchnic bed; the normal compensatory baroreflex (slight tachycardia, veno- and arteriolar constriction) maintains pressure, and neurogenic OH results from inadequate peripheral norepinephrine release with loss of vascular tone. The synucleinopathies (MSA, Parkinson disease with autonomic failure, dementia with Lewy bodies, pure autonomic failure) share aggregation of misfolded, phosphorylated alpha-synuclein in central and peripheral autonomic structures; MSA characteristically shows glial cytoplasmic inclusions in oligodendrocytes (versus neuronal Lewy bodies in PD/LBD), with possible prion-like cell-to-cell transfer. Peripheral autonomic neuropathies damage small myelinated and unmyelinated sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers. POTS pathophysiology is heterogeneous, including distal sympathetic denervation with preserved cardiac sympathetic function (neuropathic POTS), venous pooling, hypovolemia, deconditioning, altered baroreflex regulation, and sympathetic overactivity.","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple system atrophy (MSA-P and MSA-C / Shy-Drager syndrome)","Parkinson disease and dementia with Lewy bodies with autonomic failure","Pure autonomic failure","Diabetic autonomic neuropathy","Amyloid autonomic neuropathy (AL and hereditary transthyretin)","Autoimmune autonomic ganglionopathy and seronegative autoimmune autonomic neuropathy","Guillain-Barre syndrome with dysautonomia","Porphyria-associated autonomic dysfunction","Familial dysautonomia (Riley-Day) and hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathies","Spinal cord lesions (trauma, syringomyelia, subacute combined degeneration, MS/NMO) with autonomic dysreflexia","Baroreflex failure (e.g., after neck irradiation injuring carotid sinus baroreceptors)","Fabry disease and POTS related to Ehlers-Danlos syndrome"],"infectionMimics":["Post-viral POTS following EBV, coronavirus, or enteroviral illness (and long COVID)","Botulism producing cholinergic failure (blurred vision, sluggish pupils, dry mouth, ileus, urinary retention)","HIV-related autonomic neuropathy","Postinfectious autoimmune autonomic ganglionopathy"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Medication and supplement effects (diuretics, antihypertensives, tricyclics, antipsychotics, dopamine agonists; sympathomimetic supplements causing tachycardia/hypertension)","Pheochromocytoma and hyperthyroidism mimicking POTS sympathetic activation","Primary cardiac arrhythmias and chronotropic insufficiency","Sjogren sicca syndrome resembling muscarinic failure","Anxiety/panic disorder versus adrenergic dysautonomia","Low-pressure CSF headache versus orthostatic headache of OH"],"additionalInvestigations":["Orthostatic vital signs (supine and standing BP/HR at 1 and 3 minutes); tilt-table testing for orthostatic intolerance and syncope subtyping","Heart-rate variation to deep breathing and Valsalva maneuver for cardiovagal and adrenergic baroreflex function","QSART and thermoregulatory sweat test to localize sudomotor (pre- vs postganglionic) lesions","Skin punch biopsy (2-4 mm) for epidermal nerve fiber density and intraneural alpha-synuclein deposition","Supine and upright plasma catecholamines (failure to rise on standing in central/preganglionic; low supine and no rise in postganglionic)","Nutritional/metabolic labs: B12, folate, vitamin D, iron/ferritin, and in GI involvement thiamine, B6, niacin, riboflavin, vitamin C, vitamin E, copper, coenzyme Q10, carnitine","2-hour glucose tolerance test in small-fiber neuropathy with normal HbA1c (impaired glucose tolerance is a frequent cause)","Serum free light chains and protein electrophoresis for amyloidosis; autoantibodies (ganglionic AChR alpha-3, voltage-gated K and P/Q Ca channel, plexin, GAD); paraneoplastic panel (anti-Hu, CRMP5, amphiphysin)","Brain MRI in suspected MSA (striatal iron in MSA-P; cerebellar/pontine atrophy with 'hot cross bun' sign in MSA-C); MIBG scintigraphy or fluorodopamine PET to distinguish MSA from PD/LBD/PAF","5-HIAA for carcinoid; rheumatologic screen (Sjogren, scleroderma, mixed connective tissue disease); genetic testing for porphyria, Fabry, TTR mutations"],"redFlags":["Impaired olfaction, impaired balance, abnormal movements, and dream-enactment sleep behavior (apnea, stridor) signal a degenerative extrapyramidal/synucleinopathy disorder","Symptomatic orthostatic hypotension within 2 years of parkinsonism onset, early urinary and erectile dysfunction, and rapid disability progression favor MSA over idiopathic Parkinson disease","Supine hypertension with reverse dipping in autonomic failure predicts end-organ (renal, cardiac, cerebrovascular) damage","Autonomic dysreflexia BP surges above a T6 cord lesion can precipitate intracranial vasospasm or hemorrhage","Life-threatening cardiovascular dysautonomia in Guillain-Barre syndrome, especially severe cases","Subacute autonomic failure after infection, surgery, or with suspected malignancy points to autoimmune/paraneoplastic ganglionopathy requiring workup","Acroparesthesias/burning feet and hands suggesting autonomic small-fiber neuropathy","Macroglossia suggesting amyloidosis; angiokeratoma suggesting Fabry disease"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on etiology. Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension carries high mortality, approaching 44% over 30 months and exceeding 60% over 10 years in some series, and OH overall raises risk of falls, coronary disease, heart failure, stroke, and death. MSA progresses relentlessly to death typically 7-10 years after onset, though some survive beyond 10 years; early autonomic and bladder dysfunction, female sex, and rapid disability progression imply worse prognosis. Pure autonomic failure may evolve into a central synucleinopathy (PD, DLB, or MSA) after 5-10 years. Diabetic autonomic neuropathy increases diabetic mortality up to threefold. POTS is often chronic and self-perpetuating through deconditioning, but psychiatric comorbidity rates do not exceed the general population; graded exercise typically needs at least 6 months for benefit. Autoimmune autonomic ganglionopathy is important because it is treatable; about one-third of untreated seronegative cases improve spontaneously.","pitfalls":["OH is frequently overlooked because most patients are asymptomatic or only mildly symptomatic, and chronic neurogenic OH may stay mild despite large BP drops due to cerebral autoregulation","Relying only on the absolute heart-rate rise can misclassify neurogenic OH; cardioactive drugs and intrinsic rhythm disturbances can blunt the expected tachycardia","Delayed OH may not appear until after 3-5 (sometimes 10) minutes of tilt, so short observation can miss it; postprandial and initial OH have distinct timing and triggers","Pharmacologic provocation during tilt testing increases sensitivity at the expense of specificity (normal subjects may faint)","Typical MSA MRI findings may appear only in advanced disease, so a normal early scan does not exclude it; the 'hot cross bun' sign is characteristic but not pathognomonic (also seen in some spinocerebellar atrophies)","Failing to screen for occult malignancy in autoimmune autonomic ganglionopathy can miss a paraneoplastic small cell lung cancer, thymoma, or lymphoma","Overlap of preganglionic and postganglionic involvement occurs in autoimmune, paraneoplastic, and neurodegenerative disorders, so localization rules are not absolute","Attributing POTS symptoms to psychiatric disease; significant psychiatric disorder rates are no higher than in the general population"],"severityClassification":"Orthostatic hypotension can be graded into four functional classes: class I, asymptomatic (may still have syncope/falls); class II, weekly-to-monthly symptoms with mild-to-moderate limitation of daily living; class III, more severe and frequent symptoms markedly limiting daily activities; class IV, daily severe symptoms causing significant disability. OH is further distinguished as nonneurogenic (compensatory HR rise >15 beats/min) versus neurogenic (HR rise <15 beats/min, or HR-rise/SBP-fall ratio <0.5 beats/min/mmHg), and by timing as initial, classical, delayed, and postprandial variants.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3538","Harrison 22e p.3539","Harrison 22e p.3540","Harrison 22e p.3541","Harrison 22e p.3542","Harrison 22e p.3546","Harrison 22e p.3547","Harrison 22e p.3548","Harrison 22e p.3549","Harrison 22e p.3550"],"crossLinks":[]},"disorders_of_the_female_reproductive":{"name":"Disorders of the Female Reproductive System","system":"Reproductive / Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Female reproductive endocrinology","Ovarian function disorders","Disorders of puberty in girls"],"chapter":404,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3124","clinicalPearls":["Normal cyclic reproductive function depends on coordinated signaling among the hypothalamus (GnRH), pituitary (FSH and LH), and ovary, producing repeating cycles of follicle growth, ovulation, and endometrial preparation; disruption at any level produces menstrual dysfunction (Harrison 22e p.3124, p.3133).","The ovarian germ cell pool is non-renewable: oogonia peak at roughly 6-7 million by mid-gestation (about 20 weeks) and fall to only 1-2 million primordial follicles at birth, declining further through atresia thereafter (Harrison 22e p.3125, p.3126).","In a normal ovulatory adult, cycle length averages about 28 days (range 25-35), bleeding lasts about 4-6 days, and the luteal phase is relatively fixed at 12-14 days, so most cycle-length variability reflects differences in follicular phase length (Harrison 22e p.3130).","Cycle-to-cycle variability for a consistently ovulating woman is only about plus or minus 2 days; cycles tend to shorten after age 35 and become more erratic and anovulatory as menopause approaches (Harrison 22e p.3130).","Documentation of ovulation can use a mid-luteal serum progesterone above 3 ng/mL (about 7 days after ovulation), a sustained basal body temperature rise of over 0.5 degrees F from the progesterone thermogenic effect, or a urinary LH surge; an apparent LH surge does not guarantee that ovulation actually occurs (Harrison 22e p.3130).","Estrogen synthesis requires cooperation of two ovarian cell types: theca cells make androgens under LH control, and granulosa cells aromatize these androgens to estradiol under FSH control (the two-cell, two-gonadotropin model) (Harrison 22e p.3127).","Anti-mullerian hormone (AMH), produced by small growing preantral follicles, restrains FSH-driven recruitment, limiting the number of follicles entering the growing pool; AMH is a clinical marker of ovarian reserve and is elevated in PCOS (Harrison 22e p.3126).","GnRH neurons originate in the olfactory placode and migrate to the hypothalamus alongside olfactory neurons; failure of this migration links anosmia with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (Kallmann phenotype) (Harrison 22e p.3126, p.3127).","FSH and LH are high in the neonatal period, fall into a quiescent childhood phase, and rise again at puberty; early in puberty gonadotropin secretion is detectable only during sleep before extending across the day (Harrison 22e p.3127).","Menarche occurs late in the pubertal sequence: it is preceded by adrenarche (pubic and axillary hair) and thelarche (breast development), with breast development typically about 2 years before menarche; U.S. average age at menarche is roughly 12.5 years (Harrison 22e p.3130).","Central precocious puberty in girls is idiopathic in about 85% of cases, but a neurogenic cause must be excluded; it shows pulsatile LH and a brisk LH/FSH response to GnRH stimulation, whereas peripheral precocity has suppressed gonadotropins with elevated estradiol (Harrison 22e p.3130, p.3131).","Delayed puberty in girls (absence of secondary sexual characteristics by age 13) is of ovarian origin in 25-40% of cases, with Turner syndrome the leading cause; reversible functional causes such as undernutrition, excessive exercise, eating disorders, and chronic illness account for about 25% (Harrison 22e p.3131, p.3132)."],"pathophysiology":"The female reproductive axis is a tightly regulated hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian system. Pulsatile GnRH from roughly 7000 hypothalamic neurons, coordinated by pulsatile kisspeptin secretion (modulated by stimulatory neurokinin B and inhibitory dynorphin in KNDy neurons), drives pituitary FSH and LH release. FSH recruits secondary follicles from the resting pool (cyclic recruitment), restrained by AMH from small growing follicles. A single dominant follicle is selected within the first week of the cycle through high FSH-receptor density, high aromatase activity, and rising estradiol and inhibin A, which suppress FSH and the growth of competing follicles. Estradiol is produced by the cooperative two-cell mechanism (LH-driven theca androgen synthesis, FSH-driven granulosa aromatization). The mid-cycle estradiol-induced LH surge triggers resumption of oocyte meiosis, induction of COX-2, prostaglandins, and the progesterone receptor, and ovulation, followed by luteinization of theca and granulosa cells to form the progesterone-secreting corpus luteum. Disorders arise from defects at any level: genetic or anatomic GnRH-neuron migration defects, functional hypothalamic suppression from energy deficit, pituitary lesions, ovarian failure (gonadal dysgenesis, premature ovarian insufficiency, mutations in over 50 genes), or outflow-tract obstruction. Over 50 genes have been implicated in premature ovarian insufficiency, reflecting the genetic complexity of follicle endowment and survival (Harrison 22e p.3125-p.3132).","additionalDifferentials":["Pregnancy (the most common cause of amenorrhea, to be excluded in all cases) (Harrison 22e p.3133)","Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) - leading cause of oligo-/anovulation (Harrison 22e p.3133)","Premature ovarian insufficiency / gonadal dysgenesis including Turner syndrome (Harrison 22e p.3132, p.3134)","Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea from undernutrition, excessive exercise, or eating disorders (Harrison 22e p.3132)","Hyperprolactinemia (pituitary lesions or medications) (Harrison 22e p.3134)","Hypothalamic/pituitary tumors and infiltrative disease; Sheehan syndrome and lymphocytic hypophysitis postpartum (Harrison 22e p.3134)","Mullerian agenesis (Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser syndrome) and androgen insensitivity syndrome as causes of primary amenorrhea (Harrison 22e p.3133)","Late-onset (nonclassic) congenital adrenal hyperplasia and androgen-secreting tumors in heterosexual precocity (Harrison 22e p.3131)"],"infectionMimics":["Genital tuberculosis as a cause of intrauterine adhesions (Asherman syndrome) in endemic regions, mimicking primary endometrial or ovulatory failure (Harrison 22e p.3133)","CNS infection as a cause of central precocious puberty (Harrison 22e p.3131)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Constitutional delay of growth and puberty mimicking pathologic delayed puberty (Harrison 22e p.3132)","Premature thelarche (isolated breast development before age 2) without progression mimicking true precocious puberty (Harrison 22e p.3131)","Premature adrenarche without progressive pubertal development, to be distinguished from nonclassic CAH and androgen-secreting tumors (Harrison 22e p.3131)","McCune-Albright syndrome and congenital adrenal hyperplasia, in which initially peripheral precocity may convert to central precocity (Harrison 22e p.3131)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Pregnancy test (beta-hCG) as the first step in any menstrual disturbance (Harrison 22e p.3133)","Serum FSH and LH to localize the defect (ovarian vs central) (Harrison 22e p.3131, p.3134)","Estradiol and testosterone; DHEAS and 17-hydroxyprogesterone when hyperandrogenism is suspected (Harrison 22e p.3131)","Prolactin in all patients with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (Harrison 22e p.3134)","TSH and free T4 (Harrison 22e p.3131)","Bone age and assessment of growth velocity in pubertal disorders (Harrison 22e p.3131)","Pelvic ultrasound to assess follicle growth, endometrial thickness, corpus luteum, and reproductive-tract anatomy (Harrison 22e p.3130, p.3131)","Cranial MRI for suspected central/neuroanatomic causes; pelvic MRI to define tract anomalies before surgery (Harrison 22e p.3131, p.3133)","Karyotype in delayed puberty and suspected gonadal dysgenesis (Harrison 22e p.3131)","AMH as a marker of ovarian reserve (Harrison 22e p.3126)","Mid-luteal serum progesterone (>3 ng/mL) to confirm ovulation (Harrison 22e p.3130)"],"redFlags":["Primary amenorrhea with absent secondary sexual characteristics, short stature, or height below the third percentile suggesting Turner syndrome or another pathologic cause requiring evaluation by age 13 (Harrison 22e p.3133)","Amenorrhea with galactorrhea, headache, visual symptoms, or diabetes insipidus suggesting a hypothalamic-pituitary mass (Harrison 22e p.3134)","Cyclic pelvic pain with primary amenorrhea suggesting outflow-tract obstruction (imperforate hymen, transverse vaginal septum) requiring prompt surgery to limit endometriosis from retrograde flow (Harrison 22e p.3133)","Secondary sexual development before age 8 (or breast/pubic hair before 6-7) warranting evaluation for precocious puberty, including exclusion of CNS tumors (Harrison 22e p.3130, p.3131)","Postpartum amenorrhea raising concern for pituitary necrosis (Sheehan syndrome) or lymphocytic hypophysitis (Harrison 22e p.3134)","Heavy or prolonged bleeding (over 7 days) or markedly irregular intervals (over 35 or under 21 days) prompting evaluation for AUB causes including malignancy/hyperplasia (Harrison 22e p.3133)"],"prognosis":"Many disorders are highly treatable when the level of the defect is identified. Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea from energy deficit is often reversible with restoration of energy balance. Outflow-tract obstruction has a good outcome with timely surgery, but delay increases the risk of endometriosis from retrograde menstruation. Premature ovarian insufficiency and gonadal dysgenesis carry permanent loss of fertility and require attention to long-term estrogen-deficiency consequences for bone and other tissues. In precocious puberty, untreated central precocity risks premature epiphyseal closure and compromised adult height, while appropriate management preserves height and addresses psychosocial impact (Harrison 22e p.3126, p.3131, p.3132, p.3133).","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude pregnancy before any other workup of amenorrhea, since it is the most common cause (Harrison 22e p.3133).","Assuming the absence of premenstrual moliminal symptoms proves anovulation - their absence does not (Harrison 22e p.3130).","Treating a detected urinary LH surge as proof of ovulation when ovulation may still fail to occur (Harrison 22e p.3130).","Overlooking infrequent or absent menses because the patient does not report them, missing an underlying disorder with long-term health consequences (Harrison 22e p.3133).","Not measuring prolactin in hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, since hyperprolactinemia from lesions or drugs is a common reversible cause (Harrison 22e p.3134).","Forgetting that peripheral precocious puberty (e.g., McCune-Albright, CAH) can secondarily trigger central precocity, requiring reassessment (Harrison 22e p.3131).","Misattributing irregular cycles in adolescence or perimenopause to pathology when anovulatory irregularity is physiologic in the first few years after menarche and the years before menopause (Harrison 22e p.3130, p.3133)."],"severityClassification":"Amenorrhea is classified as primary (menses never occurred without hormonal treatment) versus secondary (cessation for 3-6 months). Abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB) replaces the older term dysfunctional uterine bleeding and is categorized etiologically by the PALM-COEIN system into structural causes (Polyp, Adenomyosis, Leiomyoma, Malignancy/hyperplasia) and nonstructural causes (Coagulopathy, Ovulatory dysfunction, Endometrial, Iatrogenic, Not yet classified). Precocious puberty is divided into central (GnRH-dependent) and peripheral (GnRH-independent) forms, and delayed puberty into hypergonadotropic (ovarian) and hypogonadotropic (central) forms (Harrison 22e p.3131, p.3133).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3124","Harrison 22e p.3125","Harrison 22e p.3126","Harrison 22e p.3127","Harrison 22e p.3130","Harrison 22e p.3131","Harrison 22e p.3132","Harrison 22e p.3133","Harrison 22e p.3134"],"crossLinks":[]},"disorders_of_the_mediastinum":{"name":"Disorders of the Mediastinum","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mediastinal masses","Mediastinitis","Fibrosing mediastinitis","Pneumomediastinum"],"chapter":306,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2271","clinicalPearls":["The mediastinum lies between the pleural sacs, bounded superiorly by the thoracic inlet and inferiorly by the diaphragm, and is divided into anterior, middle, and posterior compartments, each prone to characteristic lesions.","Localizing a mediastinal mass to one of the three compartments is the essential first diagnostic step, since each compartment harbors a distinct set of typical lesions.","The anterior compartment is the source of thymomas, germ cell tumors (including teratomas), lymphomas, and thyroid goiter; thymoma is the most common anterior mediastinal neoplasm and is closely linked to paraneoplastic syndromes, most notably myasthenia gravis.","Anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies in serum support a diagnosis of myasthenia gravis in the setting of an anterior mediastinal mass.","Teratomas are the most common germ cell tumor and appear as multilobulated cystic lesions containing fat, soft tissue, and calcium; elevated alpha-fetoprotein and beta-hCG distinguish nonseminomatous from seminomatous germ cell tumors.","Most middle mediastinal masses are benign and developmental in origin, including bronchogenic cysts (abnormal ventral foregut budding), duplication cysts (persistent dorsal foregut diverticulum), and pericardial cysts (abnormal pericardial recess fusion).","Neurogenic tumors are the most common cause of posterior mediastinal masses and are frequently benign, arising from peripheral, autonomic, or paraganglionic nervous tissue.","Superior vena cava syndrome is a medical emergency presenting with severe dyspnea and facial/upper-extremity edema; malignancy causes most cases, with lung cancer (especially small-cell carcinoma) accounting for up to 50%.","Acute mediastinitis most often follows esophageal perforation, median sternotomy for cardiac surgery, or infection descending from the neck, oral cavity, or face.","Hamman's sign — a crunching/clicking sound synchronous with the heartbeat, best heard over the anterior chest in the left lateral decubitus position — together with suprasternal subcutaneous emphysema suggests pneumomediastinum.","Most fibrosing mediastinitis follows prior granulomatous infection, commonly histoplasmosis or tuberculosis, though sarcoidosis, silicosis, and fungal disease may also be causative.","Barium gastrointestinal studies are useful in many posterior mediastinal lesions because hernias, diverticula, and achalasia are readily identified this way."],"pathophysiology":"Disorders of the mediastinum arise from the anatomical structures of its three compartments. Anterior compartment lesions stem from the thymus, lymph nodes, germinal/thyroid tissue, and mammary vessels, giving rise to thymomas, germ cell tumors, lymphomas, and goiter. Middle compartment disease typically reflects embryologic developmental abnormalities producing foregut-derived cysts (bronchogenic, duplication, pericardial), or malignant lymphadenopathy and vascular masses; because central vasculature resides here, compression of structures such as the SVC can produce acute life-threatening physiology. Posterior compartment lesions are predominantly neurogenic, with contributions from diaphragmatic hernia, meningocele, esophageal diverticula, and extramedullary hematopoiesis. Acute mediastinitis is an inflammatory/infectious process driven by esophageal breach, post-sternotomy wound contamination, or descending cervicofacial infection. Chronic and fibrosing mediastinitis represents a spectrum from granulomatous lymph node inflammation to dense fibrosis, usually following granulomatous infection, with progressive encasement and obstruction of mediastinal structures (SVC, large airways, pulmonary vessels, phrenic/recurrent laryngeal nerves). Pneumomediastinum results from alveolar rupture with air dissection into the mediastinum, perforation/rupture of esophagus or airway, or dissection of air from the neck or abdomen.","additionalDifferentials":["Thymoma and thymic carcinoma","Germ cell tumors / teratoma (seminomatous and nonseminomatous)","Lymphoma (anterior and middle mediastinal lymphadenopathy)","Intrathoracic thyroid goiter","Parathyroid and mesenchymal tumors; giant lymph node hyperplasia","Bronchogenic, duplication, and pericardial (pleuropericardial) cysts","Metastatic and granulomatous lymph node enlargement; vascular masses","Neurogenic tumors, meningocele/meningomyelocele, gastroenteric cysts, esophageal diverticula, extramedullary hematopoiesis, hernia through foramen of Morgagni or Bochdalek"],"infectionMimics":["Granulomatous mediastinitis from histoplasmosis or tuberculosis","Fungal mediastinal infection","Descending cervicofacial/oropharyngeal infection involving the mediastinum"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lymphoma presenting as anterior/middle mediastinal lymphadenopathy","Sarcoidosis and silicosis causing fibrosing mediastinitis","Autoimmune/fibro-inflammatory fibrosing mediastinitis","Vascular masses and aortic pathology widening the mediastinum"],"additionalInvestigations":["CT scanning to characterize fat, water, calcification, and air within mediastinal masses","Diffusion-weighted MRI to differentiate cystic from solid lesions and improve tumor characterization","Mediastinoscopy for sampling of paratracheal and subcarinal lymph nodes","Percutaneous fine-needle aspiration or endoscopic transesophageal/endobronchial ultrasound biopsy","Radioactive iodine scintigraphy to confirm intrathoracic goiter","Serum alpha-fetoprotein and beta-hCG for germ cell tumor classification; serum anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies when thymoma/myasthenia is suspected","Barium GI studies for posterior mediastinal lesions (hernia, diverticulum, achalasia)","Mediastinal needle aspiration to confirm post-sternotomy mediastinitis","Chest radiograph to confirm pneumomediastinum; tissue biopsy in fibrosing mediastinitis to exclude malignancy"],"redFlags":["Superior vena cava syndrome (severe dyspnea, facial and upper-extremity edema) — a medical emergency","Acutely ill patient with chest pain and dyspnea suggesting esophageal rupture with mediastinal infection","Post-sternotomy wound drainage with sepsis and a widened mediastinum","Severe substernal chest pain radiating to neck/arms with subcutaneous emphysema (pneumomediastinum)","Hemoptysis, dysphagia, or Horner's syndrome in fibrosing mediastinitis indicating structural invasion/obstruction"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the underlying disorder. Most middle mediastinal masses and posterior neurogenic tumors are benign. Anterior mediastinal neoplasms vary widely, and disease severity across mediastinal masses spans a broad spectrum; clinical symptoms, patient age, and tumor location guide assessment of malignancy and need for intervention. Acute post-sternotomy mediastinitis carries a mortality rate exceeding 20% despite treatment. Fibrosing mediastinitis can be devastating in severe cases owing to obstruction/encasement of vital structures. Pneumomediastinum usually requires no treatment and resolves with absorption of air.","pitfalls":["Failing to compartmentalize a mediastinal mass before working it up, since each compartment has different characteristic lesions.","Overlooking that advanced malignancy may not remain confined to one compartment and can cross borders via hematogenous and lymphatic spread.","Underappreciating SVC compression in the middle mediastinum as an acute, life-threatening emergency.","Missing the link between an anterior mediastinal mass (thymoma) and paraneoplastic myasthenia gravis.","Assuming fibrosing mediastinitis is purely structural without recognizing its common antecedent granulomatous infection and need to exclude malignancy by biopsy."],"severityClassification":"No formal grading system; mediastinal disorders are stratified anatomically into anterior, middle, and posterior compartments, and severity is judged by clinical symptoms, patient age, lesion location, likelihood of malignancy, and presence of acute complications such as SVC obstruction or airway compromise.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2271","Harrison 22e p.2272","Harrison 22e p.2273"],"crossLinks":[]},"disorders_of_the_neurohypophysis":{"name":"Disorders of the Neurohypophysis","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Posterior pituitary disorders","Diabetes insipidus","AVP deficiency","AVP resistance","Central diabetes insipidus","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuresis","SIADH","Adipsic hypernatremia"],"chapter":393,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3014-3020","clinicalPearls":["The neurohypophysis releases arginine vasopressin (AVP/ADH) and oxytocin from axons whose cell bodies sit in the hypothalamic paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei; AVP conserves water by concentrating urine, while oxytocin drives milk letdown and socioemotional behaviors (Harrison 22e p.3014).","AVP secretion is governed mainly by an osmotic set point in anteromedial hypothalamic osmoreceptors, with the average threshold near a plasma osmolality of ~275 mosmol/L and sodium ~135 meq/L; only a 2-4% rise above this drives maximum antidiuresis (Harrison 22e p.3015).","Nausea/emetic stimuli are extremely potent AVP releasers, producing 50- to 100-fold increases even without vomiting, far exceeding osmotic stimulation; AVP is also released by a >10-20% fall in blood pressure or volume, hypoglycemia, glucocorticoid deficiency, and smoking (Harrison 22e p.3015).","AVP acts on V2 receptors of collecting-duct principal cells to insert aquaporin-2 water channels, enabling water reabsorption; maximum antidiuresis (urine osmolality up to ~1200 mosmol/L) occurs at plasma AVP of roughly 1-3 pg/mL (Harrison 22e p.3016).","The thirst osmostat is set about 3% higher than the AVP osmostat, so antidiuresis is the first-line defense and thirst/polydipsia only engage once AVP's capacity is exceeded (Harrison 22e p.3016).","Polyuria-polydipsia is defined practically by a 24-h urine volume exceeding ~40-50 mL/kg and/or >3 L/d on unrestricted intake; dehydration and hypernatremia are uncommon in diabetes insipidus unless thirst or access to water is also impaired (Harrison 22e p.3016-3017).","Serum sodium at presentation is a key discriminator: a low sodium (<135 mmol/L) points toward primary polydipsia (patients can drink into hyponatremia), whereas a high sodium suggests AVP deficiency or resistance (Harrison 22e p.3017).","Copeptin, the stable C-terminal fragment of the AVP prohormone, has largely replaced direct AVP assays: a baseline copeptin >21.4 pmol/L without prior water deprivation identifies AVP resistance, and a copeptin >4.9 pmol/L at plasma sodium >=150 mmol/L after hypertonic saline distinguishes AVP deficiency from primary polydipsia with ~96.5% accuracy (Harrison 22e p.3017-3018).","The MRI posterior pituitary bright spot reflects AVP-containing neurosecretory vesicles and is often absent in AVP deficiency, but its absence is not diagnostic since it can persist early or be absent in the elderly (Harrison 22e p.3018).","In pregnancy, placental N-terminal aminopeptidase (vasopressinase) accelerates AVP clearance three- to fourfold, producing gestational AVP deficiency that typically remits weeks after delivery; the synthetic analogue DDAVP resists this peptidase (Harrison 22e p.3016-3017).","Hypoosmolality is the hallmark of SIAD; acute or severe hyponatremia can cause headache, confusion, nausea, vomiting, seizures, and coma, while even mild chronic hyponatremia raises the risk of falls, fractures, neurocognitive symptoms, and mortality (Harrison 22e p.3019).","In SIAD, persistent water retention modestly expands total body water and triggers compensatory urinary sodium loss, so edema does not develop; the harm comes from intracellular (notably cerebral) swelling proportional to the speed and degree of the sodium fall (Harrison 22e p.3019)."],"pathophysiology":"Disorders of the neurohypophysis arise from deficient or excessive AVP secretion/action. In AVP deficiency (formerly central/neurogenic diabetes insipidus) and AVP resistance (formerly nephrogenic diabetes insipidus), impaired urine concentration increases free-water excretion, causing a small (1-2%) reduction in body water and a rise in plasma osmolality/sodium that stimulates thirst and compensatory drinking; severity ranges from near-complete deficiency unresponsive to maximal stimuli to partial defects in which mild stimuli can still partially concentrate urine, though prior lack of AVP transiently blunts renal concentrating capacity. Central deficiency reflects destruction or dysfunction of AVP-producing neurons (trauma, tumors, vascular, granulomatous, infectious, autoimmune/IgG4 hypophysitis, or genetic AVP-neurophysin II mutations); resistance reflects reduced renal V2-receptor/aquaporin-2 responsiveness from drugs (lithium), hypokalemia, hypercalcemia, infiltrative or vascular disease, or AVPR2/AQP2 mutations. Primary polydipsia is the mirror image: excess intake lowers plasma osmolality, physiologically suppresses AVP, and produces a dilute compensatory diuresis. Hypodipsic/adipsic hypernatremia results from anterior hypothalamic osmoreceptor damage impairing osmotic thirst (occasionally from anti-subfornical-organ autoantibodies), so obligatory renal/insensible losses go unreplaced. In SIAD, AVP is inappropriately elevated when it should be osmotically suppressed, causing water retention, slight volume expansion with renal sodium loss (no edema), and dilutional hypoosmolality with cellular swelling (Harrison 22e p.3014-3019).","additionalDifferentials":["Complete vs partial central AVP deficiency","Nephrogenic AVP resistance (lithium, hypercalcemia, hypokalemia, AVPR2/AQP2 mutations)","Primary (dipsogenic, psychogenic, compulsive, or health-enthusiast) polydipsia","Gestational AVP deficiency from placental vasopressinase","Osmotic diuresis from uncontrolled hyperglycemia/glucosuria","Hypodipsic/adipsic hypernatremia","Pseudohyponatremia (hyperlipidemia, hyperproteinemia) and translocational hyponatremia (hyperglycemia, mannitol) as non-hypotonic mimics of SIAD","Hypovolemic and hypervolemic hyponatremia (heart failure, cirrhosis, GI losses, diuretics, adrenal insufficiency)"],"infectionMimics":["Meningitis, encephalitis, and tuberculosis affecting the hypothalamic-neurohypophyseal axis (causes of AVP deficiency)","Cytomegalovirus encephalitis and AIDS-related hypothalamic involvement causing adipsic hypernatremia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Osmotic diuresis from diabetes mellitus mimicking diabetes insipidus polyuria","Pseudohyponatremia and translocational hyponatremia mimicking SIAD","Hypovolemic/hypervolemic hyponatremia mimicking euvolemic SIAD","Secondary adrenal insufficiency and (rarely) hypothyroidism presenting with hyponatremia"],"additionalInvestigations":["24-h urine volume on unrestricted fluids (>40-50 mL/kg/day and/or >3 L/d defines significant polyuria)","Serum sodium and plasma osmolality with paired urine osmolality","Hypertonic saline-stimulated copeptin (>4.9 pmol/L at sodium >=150 mmol/L favors AVP deficiency over primary polydipsia)","Baseline copeptin (>21.4 pmol/L identifies AVP resistance)","Desmopressin challenge to separate AVP deficiency from AVP resistance","Water deprivation test (older indirect gold standard, limited accuracy)","MRI of sella/suprasellar region including pituitary stalk and posterior pituitary bright spot","Urine sodium (<30 mmol/L suggests low effective arterial blood volume rather than SIAD)","Exclusion of adrenal insufficiency and assessment of thyroid function in hyponatremia workup"],"redFlags":["Severe or acute hyponatremia with headache, confusion, vomiting, seizures, or coma (cerebral edema)","Hypernatremia with signs of hypovolemia (tachycardia, postural hypotension, azotemia) in adipsic patients","Denial of thirst or failure to drink when conscious and hypernatremic (virtually diagnostic of adipsia)","Rhabdomyolysis, acute renal failure, or thromboembolic disease complicating hypertonic dehydration","Polyuria with rising serum sodium signaling inadequate water replacement","Lithium use with persistent polyuria suggesting drug-induced AVP resistance"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated AVP deficiency is generally well managed with vasopressin replacement, restoring normal water balance. Hyponatremia is the most common complication of replacement and ranges from mild to severe. AVP resistance is difficult to treat and may respond only partially. Adipsic/hypodipsic hypernatremia carries higher risk because the absent thirst sensation predisposes to recurrent hypertonic dehydration and complications such as rhabdomyolysis, acute renal failure, and thromboembolism; long-term structured fluid-balance management is needed to prevent recurrence. In SIAD, even mild chronic hyponatremia is associated with increased falls, fractures, neurocognitive and neuromuscular symptoms, and increased morbidity and mortality (Harrison 22e p.3017-3019).","pitfalls":["Low basal AVP/V2 stimulation (e.g., in primary polydipsia) transiently blunts renal concentrating capacity, so the urine osmolality rise after exogenous AVP can falsely suggest a concentrating defect, undermining the traditional indirect water deprivation test (accuracy only ~70%, and ~41% in primary polydipsia) (Harrison 22e p.3016-3017).","Measurement of plasma AVP or copeptin has no diagnostic value in SIAD itself; copeptin is used for the polyuria differential, not for confirming SIAD (Harrison 22e p.3020).","Failing to measure serum osmolality risks misclassifying pseudohyponatremia or translocational hyponatremia as true hypotonic SIAD (Harrison 22e p.3019-3020).","Clinical assessment of volume status is inaccurate, so relying on examination alone to classify hyponatremia can mislead (Harrison 22e p.3020).","Secondary adrenal insufficiency must be excluded before diagnosing SIAD in euvolemic hyponatremia (Harrison 22e p.3020).","Hypertonic saline-stimulated copeptin testing requires close sodium monitoring; absence of the MRI bright spot is not by itself sufficient to diagnose AVP deficiency (Harrison 22e p.3018).","Overly rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia or hypernatremia is dangerous; serum sodium must be corrected slowly with close monitoring (Harrison 22e p.3019-3020)."],"severityClassification":"Diabetes insipidus is graded as complete versus partial AVP deficiency, and complete versus partial AVP resistance, distinguished by copeptin response, desmopressin response, and the magnitude of urine osmolality change. Hyponatremia severity is gauged by plasma sodium and tempo: severe/acute hyponatremia with neurologic symptoms warrants urgent treatment, whereas gradual or mild hyponatremia may be paucisymptomatic; replacement-related hyponatremia is categorized as mild (sodium 131-134 mmol/L) or more severe (<130 mmol/L). Hypernatremia severity in adipsic patients varies widely and correlates with hypovolemic complications (Harrison 22e p.3017-3019).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3014","Harrison 22e p.3015","Harrison 22e p.3016","Harrison 22e p.3017","Harrison 22e p.3018","Harrison 22e p.3019","Harrison 22e p.3020"],"crossLinks":[]},"disorders_of_the_pleura":{"name":"Disorders of the Pleura","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pleural effusion","Pneumothorax","Empyema","Hemothorax","Chylothorax","Mesothelioma"],"chapter":305,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2267","clinicalPearls":["The pleural space normally holds only a thin film of fluid acting as a coupling system, kept at roughly -3 to -5 cmH2O by the balance between lung elastic recoil (visceral pleura) and chest-wall outward expansion (parietal pleura).","The first diagnostic step in any pleural effusion is distinguishing transudate from exudate via thoracentesis; this dictates whether further work-up is needed, since exudates require investigation to define their cause.","Light's criteria define an exudate when at least one is met: pleural fluid/serum protein >0.5, pleural fluid/serum LDH >0.6, or pleural fluid LDH greater than two-thirds the upper normal serum limit; transudates meet none.","Light's criteria misclassify about 25% of transudates as exudates ('pseudoexudates'), often in a diuresed transudate; a serum-to-pleural-fluid protein gradient >3.1 g/dL or albumin gradient >1.2 g/dL helps reclassify these (high sensitivity in heart failure and hepatothorax).","Left ventricular failure is the single most common cause of pleural effusion (a transudate from raised hydrostatic pressure); cirrhosis is the next leading transudative cause in the United States.","Parapneumonic effusions occur in up to 50% of community-acquired pneumonias and are generally the most common exudative effusion in the U.S.; about 10% become complicated or progress to empyema (fibrinopurulent or grossly purulent fluid).","Malignant pleural effusions are the second most common exudate; lung carcinoma, breast carcinoma, and lymphoma together cause roughly 75% of cases, and dyspnea is often out of proportion to effusion size.","Pulmonary embolism is the diagnosis most commonly overlooked in an undiagnosed exudative effusion; the fluid is almost always exudative and dyspnea is the predominant symptom.","Tuberculous pleuritis arises largely as a hypersensitivity reaction to tuberculous protein in the pleural space and yields a lymphocyte-predominant exudate; pleural biopsy culture yield exceeds that of pleural fluid.","Chylothorax produces milky fluid with a triglyceride level exceeding 110 mg/dL (1.2 mmol/L); prolonged chest-tube drainage should be avoided because chyle loss causes malnutrition and immunologic incompetence.","Hemothorax is defined when pleural fluid hematocrit exceeds half the peripheral blood hematocrit; most are traumatic.","About half of patients with an initial primary spontaneous pneumothorax (from rupture of apical subpleural blebs) will have a recurrence.","Tension pneumothorax is a medical emergency: positive intrapleural pressure throughout the respiratory cycle impairs ventilation and reduces venous return, lowering cardiac output."],"pathophysiology":"Pleural fluid normally enters from parietal pleural capillaries and is cleared by parietal pleural lymphatics, which can absorb up to 20 times the normal rate of fluid formation; fluid can also enter from lung interstitium via the visceral pleura or from the peritoneal cavity through diaphragmatic openings. An effusion forms when the hydrostatic-oncotic balance is disturbed and fluid production overwhelms lymphatic removal. Transudates result from altered systemic factors (e.g., elevated left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure in heart failure raising hydrostatic pressure, or low oncotic pressure), whereas exudates result from altered local factors such as inflammation, infection, or malignancy increasing capillary permeability or impairing lymphatic drainage. Parapneumonic effusions begin as a sterile reactive exudate and can progress through exudative, fibrinopurulent, and organizing (pleural peel) phases. Hepatic hydrothorax reflects direct movement of ascitic fluid through small diaphragmatic defects into the pleural space. Pneumothorax results from gas entering the pleural space, abolishing the negative pressure that couples lung to chest wall; in tension pneumothorax a one-way mechanism produces sustained positive pleural pressure.","additionalDifferentials":["Transudates: congestive heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome, peritoneal dialysis, superior vena cava obstruction, myxedema, urinothorax","Exudates from neoplasm: metastatic disease, mesothelioma, lymphoma","Exudates from infection: bacterial (parapneumonic/empyema), tuberculous, fungal, viral, parasitic","Pulmonary embolism","Gastrointestinal causes: esophageal perforation, pancreatic disease, intraabdominal abscess, diaphragmatic hernia, post-abdominal surgery, post liver transplant, variceal sclerotherapy","Collagen vascular disease: rheumatoid pleuritis, SLE, drug-induced lupus, Sjogren syndrome, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, Churg-Strauss","Other: post-CABG, asbestos exposure, sarcoidosis, uremia, Meigs' syndrome, yellow nail syndrome, trapped lung, radiation, post-cardiac injury syndrome, hemothorax, chylothorax, ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome, pericardial disease, drug-induced (nitrofurantoin, dantrolene, methysergide, bromocriptine, procarbazine, amiodarone, dasatinib)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculous pleuritis presenting with fever, weight loss, and pleuritic pain mimicking malignancy or other chronic exudate","Intraabdominal abscess producing a febrile, polymorphonuclear effusion without pulmonary parenchymal abnormality","Hepatic hydrothorax that can be complicated by spontaneous bacterial empyema"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pulmonary embolism mimicking infectious pleuritis with an exudative effusion and pleuritic pain","Malignant effusion (including mesothelioma) mimicking empyema or chronic infection","Rheumatoid or lupus pleuritis mimicking infectious exudate (low glucose in rheumatoid disease)","Pleural effusion ex vacuo / trapped lung mimicking persistent active disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest imaging: chest x-ray with lateral decubitus films, chest ultrasound, and CT to assess extent, free-flowing versus loculated fluid, and to guide sampling/drainage","Diagnostic thoracentesis with pleural fluid protein and LDH (Light's criteria)","Pleural fluid glucose, differential cell count, Gram stain/culture, and cytology","Disease-specific pleural fluid tests: amylase (pancreatic disease/esophageal rupture), eosinophils (drug-induced), NT-proBNP (heart failure, >1500 pg/mL suggests cardiac origin), cholesterol/triglycerides (chylothorax, triglyceride >110 mg/dL), hematocrit (hemothorax), glucose and pH (rheumatoid disease), Congo red staining (amyloidosis), flow cytometry (lymphoma)","TB markers: pleural fluid adenosine deaminase >40 IU/L or interferon-gamma >140 pg/mL; AFB culture, pleural biopsy","CT/ultrasound-guided pleural biopsy or thoracoscopy when cytology is non-diagnostic","CT angiography for suspected pulmonary embolism; lymphangiogram and chest CT for chylothorax without obvious trauma","Pleural manometry to help confirm trapped lung"],"redFlags":["Tension pneumothorax: enlarged hemithorax with absent breath sounds, hyperresonance, contralateral mediastinal shift, and hypotension; treat as an emergency with immediate needle decompression (large-bore needle in the second anterior intercostal space).","Difficulty ventilating or high peak inspiratory pressures during mechanical ventilation/resuscitation strongly suggest tension pneumothorax.","Complicated parapneumonic effusion/empyema markers indicating need for drainage: loculated fluid, pleural fluid pH <7.20, glucose <60 mg/dL (<3.3 mmol/L), positive Gram stain or culture, gross pus, and elevated pleural fluid LDH (>900 IU/L).","Hemothorax with ongoing pleural hemorrhage exceeding 200 mL/h warrants angiographic embolization, thoracoscopy, or thoracotomy.","Secondary pneumothorax in a patient with underlying lung disease can be more life-threatening because of reduced pulmonary reserve."],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the underlying cause. Transudative effusions from heart failure or cirrhosis improve when the primary condition is treated, and undiagnosed viral effusions usually resolve spontaneously without long-term residua. A malignant pleural effusion usually portends a poor prognosis (often less than 6-month survival) and reflects disseminated disease that is generally not curable with chemotherapy. Untreated complicated parapneumonic effusions can organize into a restrictive pleural peel (trapped lung). Tension pneumothorax is rapidly fatal from inadequate cardiac output or marked hypoxemia if not relieved. Surgical pleurodesis prevents pneumothorax recurrence in nearly 100% of cases.","pitfalls":["Relying on Light's criteria alone can misclassify roughly 25% of transudates as exudates; failing to apply protein/albumin gradients in a clinically transudative setting leads to unnecessary work-up.","Overlooking pulmonary embolism as the cause of an otherwise undiagnosed exudative effusion.","Being overly aggressive in pursuing a diagnosis in a clinically improving patient whose effusion is likely viral and self-limited.","Failing to recognize trapped lung: a thick pleural rind, air on imaging after thoracentesis (pneumothorax ex vacuo), and rapid re-accumulation indicate a lung that will not re-expand without decortication.","Prolonged tube thoracostomy drainage of a chylothorax, which can cause malnutrition and immunologic incompetence.","Not performing diagnostic thoracentesis in a presumed heart-failure effusion that is unilateral, asymmetric, febrile, or pleuritic, or that persists despite therapy."],"severityClassification":"Parapneumonic effusions are staged along a continuum from simple (free-flowing, reactive) to complicated (loculated, pH <7.20, glucose <60 mg/dL, positive Gram stain/culture) to empyema (frank pus), with progression through exudative, fibrinopurulent, and organizing (pleural peel) phases guiding the need for escalating intervention. Pneumothorax is categorized as primary spontaneous, secondary, traumatic (including iatrogenic), and tension.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2267","Harrison 22e p.2268","Harrison 22e p.2269","Harrison 22e p.2270"],"crossLinks":[]},"disorders_of_the_testes_and":{"name":"Disorders of the Testes and Male Reproductive System","system":"Endocrine / Male Reproductive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Male hypogonadism","Testicular failure","Testosterone deficiency syndrome","Androgen deficiency in men"],"chapter":403,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3102","clinicalPearls":["A low serum testosterone with an elevated LH localizes the defect to the testis (primary/hypergonadotropic hypogonadism), whereas a low or inappropriately normal LH points to a hypothalamic-pituitary defect (secondary/hypogonadotropic hypogonadism) (p.3107, p.3118).","Acquired androgen-deficient men commonly report decreased libido, fewer sexual thoughts and acts, low energy, low mood, and (less often) hot flushes; eunuchoid body proportions (arm span >2 cm greater than height) indicate that deficiency predated epiphyseal fusion (p.3110).","Klinefelter syndrome (classically 47,XXY) is the most common chromosomal cause of testicular dysfunction and male infertility, occurring in about 1 in 600 live-born males, with markedly small testes (often 1-2 mL), azoospermia, low testosterone, raised estradiol, undervirilization, and gynecomastia (p.3110, p.3114).","True glandular gynecomastia (palpable firm, cord-like tissue >4 cm, often tender) reflects an increased estrogen-to-androgen ratio and must be distinguished from excess adipose (lipomastia); recent rapid onset, tenderness, or occurrence in a lean man warrants workup (p.3115).","Testicular volume is a useful clinical index: normal adult testes are 3.5-5.5 cm long (about 12-25 mL by Prader orchidometer), and very small testes in a hypogonadal man should prompt a karyotype to exclude Klinefelter syndrome (p.3110, p.3119).","Mumps is the classic cause of viral orchitis; orchitis develops in up to one-fourth of affected adult men, is unilateral in about two-thirds, and semen analysis normalizes in three-fourths after unilateral but only one-third after bilateral involvement (p.3114).","Total testosterone can mislead when SHBG is altered (low in obesity, diabetes, hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome; high in aging, hyperthyroidism, estrogen, HIV/hepatitis B and C, some anticonvulsants); measure free testosterone (ideally by equilibrium dialysis) in these settings (p.3106, p.3108).","Cryptorchidism, even when corrected before puberty, carries increased risk of malignancy, infertility, inguinal hernia, and torsion, and is considered part of the testicular dysgenesis syndrome along with hypospadias, impaired spermatogenesis, and testicular cancer (p.3114).","Selective elevation of FSH (with relatively normal LH) suggests seminiferous tubule/Sertoli cell damage, because reduced inhibin B removes the negative feedback restraining FSH (p.3104, p.3107).","Up to two-thirds of pubertal boys and about half of hospitalized men have benign palpable breast tissue, so not all gynecomastia requires extensive investigation (p.3116).","Kallmann syndrome is the anosmic form of congenital GnRH-deficient hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, arising from defective olfactory bulb morphogenesis and failed migration of GnRH neurons; micropenis and a history of cryptorchidism favor a congenital central defect over self-limited delayed puberty (p.3112, p.3110)."],"pathophysiology":"The hypothalamic-pituitary-testicular axis governs androgen production and spermatogenesis. Pulsatile hypothalamic GnRH (regulated by kisspeptin, neurokinin B, and dynorphin in KNDy neurons) drives pituitary LH and FSH roughly every 1-3 h. LH stimulates Leydig cell testosterone synthesis (rate-limited by StAR-mediated cholesterol transport into mitochondria and CYP11A1 side-chain cleavage); FSH acts on Sertoli cells to support spermatogenesis and inhibin B production, the latter selectively suppressing FSH. Testosterone is converted by 5-alpha-reductase (SRD5A2) to the more potent DHT (required for external genital virilization and prostate growth) and aromatized by CYP19 to estradiol (mediating effects on bone, epiphyseal closure, libido). Disorders arise from gonadotropin deficiency (secondary/hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, congenital such as GnRH-deficient/Kallmann syndrome or acquired from sellar masses, hyperprolactinemia, hemochromatosis, illness, drugs, obesity) or primary testicular failure (hypergonadotropic), e.g., Klinefelter syndrome, cryptorchidism, orchitis, chemo/radiation, torsion. Resistance syndromes (androgen receptor mutations, 5-alpha-reductase or aromatase deficiency) impair androgen action or conversion. Gynecomastia results from a relative excess of estrogen action on breast tissue (p.3102-3116).","additionalDifferentials":["Erectile dysfunction without testosterone deficiency (the two are distinct disorders that may coexist)","Self-limited (constitutional) delayed puberty versus congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism in adolescents","Hyperprolactinemia / prolactinoma causing secondary hypogonadism","Hemochromatosis (pituitary and/or testicular iron deposition)","Sellar mass lesions / pituitary adenoma impairing gonadotropin secretion","Functional hypogonadotropic hypogonadism from systemic illness, malnutrition, eating disorders, or opioid use","Androgen insensitivity / 5-alpha-reductase deficiency / aromatase (CYP19) deficiency"],"infectionMimics":["Mumps orchitis (and orchitis from echovirus, lymphocytic choriomeningitis virus, group B arboviruses) causing acquired primary testicular failure","HIV infection associated with low testosterone (primary or central)","Lepromatous leprosy causing testicular atrophy through direct mycobacterial invasion"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lipomastia (excess adipose) mimicking glandular gynecomastia","Retractile testis mimicking cryptorchidism","Age-related decline in testosterone overlapping with symptomatic hypogonadism","Obesity-related lowering of total testosterone (with often normal free testosterone) due to reduced SHBG"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting early-morning total testosterone (LC-MS/MS preferred, CDC HoST-certified); repeat if borderline (200-400 ng/dL) and add free testosterone","Free testosterone by equilibrium dialysis when SHBG is altered (aging, obesity, chronic illness)","LH and FSH to separate primary (high LH/FSH) from secondary (low/inappropriately normal LH) hypogonadism","Karyotype in men with low testosterone, elevated LH, and small testes to diagnose Klinefelter syndrome","Serum prolactin and ferritin/transferrin saturation; pituitary MRI when secondary hypogonadism is found","Inhibin B (Sertoli cell product) to assess seminiferous tubule integrity","Semen analysis and Y-chromosome microdeletion testing (AZFa/AZFb/AZFc) in oligo-/azoospermia","For gynecomastia: testosterone, estradiol, estrone, androstenedione, LH, hCG (beta), TSH; testicular ultrasound and adrenal/chest imaging if an estrogen- or hCG-secreting tumor is suspected"],"redFlags":["Recent-onset, rapidly growing, or tender gynecomastia in a lean man, or breast tissue fixed to underlying tissue (evaluate for tumor/malignancy)","Markedly elevated estradiol with suppressed LH, suggesting a testicular or adrenal estrogen-secreting tumor","Elevated hCG (beta) prompting search for an hCG-producing germ cell or other tumor","Very small testes with low testosterone and high LH (consider Klinefelter syndrome and its associated breast cancer risk)","Diabetes insipidus accompanying hypogonadism, suggesting craniopharyngioma or infiltrative hypothalamic lesion","Hyperprolactinemia with visual field defects suggesting a pituitary mass"],"prognosis":"Outcomes depend on the underlying disorder. Secondary (hypogonadotropic) hypogonadism is highly amenable to fertility restoration with pulsatile GnRH or gonadotropins, and 10-20% of congenital GnRH-deficient men may have spontaneous reversal in adulthood. In Klinefelter syndrome azoospermia is the rule, though sperm can sometimes be retrieved surgically (including in some non-mosaic men) for ICSI. After mumps orchitis the testis may recover or atrophy. Radiation effects on spermatogenesis are dose-dependent (transient decline below 1 Gy, temporary azoospermia at 1-2 Gy, usually permanent azoospermia above 2 Gy), while Leydig cells are relatively radioresistant (dysfunction generally only above 20 Gy). The age-related testosterone decline is gradual and progressive without a discrete abrupt fall (p.3107, p.3114-3115, p.3117).","pitfalls":["Relying on a single random or afternoon testosterone level — values are pulsatile, diurnal, lower in the late afternoon and after meals, and depressed by acute illness, so confirm with a fasting early-morning sample (p.3108, p.3118).","Interpreting total testosterone without accounting for SHBG changes, leading to over- or under-diagnosis in obesity, aging, thyroid disease, or chronic illness (p.3106, p.3108).","Mislabeling the normal aging decline as 'andropause' — there is no discrete andropause and population screening of all older men is not recommended (p.3117).","Subjecting every man with gynecomastia to extensive workup, when most pubertal and many hospitalized men have benign glandular tissue (p.3116).","Confusing retractile testis (no treatment needed) with true cryptorchidism (p.3114).","Assuming corrected unilateral cryptorchidism guarantees normal fertility — sperm counts can remain reduced (p.3114).","Overlooking drug, alcohol, opioid, marijuana, or anabolic-androgenic steroid use as reversible causes of suppressed testosterone (p.3114, p.3119)."],"severityClassification":"Diagnostic stratification of male testosterone deficiency (Harrison Fig. 403-8): early-morning total testosterone >350-400 ng/dL makes androgen deficiency unlikely; borderline values 200-350 ng/dL should be repeated with free testosterone; consistently low levels (notably <200 ng/dL) in association with symptoms confirm deficiency. Localization: high LH/FSH = primary (hypergonadotropic) testicular failure; low/inappropriately normal LH = secondary (hypogonadotropic). Harmonized normal total testosterone reference range in healthy young men is 264-916 ng/dL (p.3108, p.3117-3118).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3102","Harrison 22e p.3104","Harrison 22e p.3106","Harrison 22e p.3108","Harrison 22e p.3110","Harrison 22e p.3112","Harrison 22e p.3114","Harrison 22e p.3115","Harrison 22e p.3116","Harrison 22e p.3117","Harrison 22e p.3118","Harrison 22e p.3119"],"crossLinks":[]},"disorders_of_ventilation":{"name":"Disorders of Ventilation","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Alveolar hypoventilation","Hypoventilation syndromes","Chronic hyperventilation","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Central hypoventilation syndrome","Ondine's curse"],"chapter":307,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2273","clinicalPearls":["Normal arterial CO2 (Paco2) sits between roughly 37 and 43 mmHg at sea level; all ventilatory disorders manifest as an abnormal Paco2 (Harrison 22e p.2273).","Because Paco2 is governed by Paco2 = k(VCO2)/VA, every disturbance reflects altered CO2 production, minute ventilation, or dead-space fraction; a patient can breathe rapidly with a high minute ventilation yet still hypoventilate at the alveolar level if dead space is increased (Harrison 22e p.2273).","Respiratory rhythm is generated in the brainstem (dorsal respiratory group and ventral respiratory column); the pre-Botzinger complex within the VRC is critical for inspiration, and its lesioning abolishes breathing entirely (Harrison 22e p.2273).","Normal sleep brings an attenuated response to hypercapnia and hypoxemia, producing mild nocturnal hypoventilation that corrects on awakening (Harrison 22e p.2273).","New-onset orthopnea is one of the earliest signs of reduced diaphragmatic force generation; 'paradoxical breathing' (abdomen moving inward on deep inspiration while supine) signals significant diaphragmatic weakness or paralysis (Harrison 22e p.2274).","Chronic hypoventilation follows a characteristic sequence: an asymptomatic stage with normal daytime gases, then nocturnal hypoventilation appearing first in REM then non-REM sleep, then falling vital capacity, and finally daytime hypercapnia (Harrison 22e p.2274).","The shared hallmark of all alveolar hypoventilation syndromes is a rise in alveolar and arterial Pco2 with a compensatory increase in plasma bicarbonate; the rising Paco2 displaces alveolar oxygen and commonly causes hypoxemia (Harrison 22e p.2274).","Chronic hypoxemia from hypoventilation can drive secondary erythrocytosis, and the combination of chronic hypoxemia and hypercapnia can cause pulmonary vasoconstriction leading to pulmonary hypertension, right ventricular hypertrophy, and right heart failure (Harrison 22e p.2274).","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome (OHS) requires BMI >=30 kg/m2 plus chronic daytime hypoventilation (Paco2 >=45 mmHg) with no other explanation; obstructive sleep apnea coexists in nearly 90% of cases, with about 70% having severe OSA (Harrison 22e p.2275).","In OHS, the depressed ventilatory responsiveness appears to be a consequence rather than a primary cause, since central drive often improves once sleep-disordered breathing is treated without weight change (Harrison 22e p.2276).","Congenital central hypoventilation syndrome (Ondine's curse, CCHS) is linked to PHOX2b gene abnormalities; affected patients lack ventilatory responses to hypoxia/hypercapnia, have mild waking hypercapnia, markedly elevated Paco2 in non-REM sleep, yet can paradoxically normalize Paco2 during exercise and REM sleep (Harrison 22e p.2276).","In chronic hyperventilation, anxiety/panic are not synonymous with the condition; sustaining hypocapnia needs only about a 10% increase in alveolar ventilation, achievable through subtle pattern changes such as occasional sigh breaths or frequent yawning (Harrison 22e p.2277)."],"pathophysiology":"Adequate ventilation depends on a balance between respiratory drive and neuromuscular strength on one side and the elastic plus resistive loads of the respiratory system on the other. Reduced drive, impaired neuromuscular competence, or a markedly increased load lowers effective minute ventilation and produces hypercapnia; conversely, normal muscle strength combined with excessive drive yields hyperventilation and hypocapnia. Diseases reducing minute ventilation or increasing dead space fall into four broad categories: parenchymal lung/chest wall disease, obesity hypoventilation, neuromuscular disease, and respiratory drive disorders. 'Pump failure' encompasses disorders of drive, neural pathways, the neuromuscular junction, and muscle. In OHS the mechanism is multifactorial, combining OSA, increased work of breathing, respiratory muscle impairment against an excess-adiposity load, ventilation-perfusion mismatch, and blunted central responsiveness to hypoxemia and hypercapnia. Chronic hyperventilation is conceptualized as having initiating and sustaining factors, with an initial event dropping Paco2 toward ~20 mmHg and resultant symptoms perpetuating overbreathing (Harrison 22e p.2273-2277).","additionalDifferentials":["Parenchymal lung disease (obstructive such as COPD; restrictive such as interstitial lung disease; atelectasis; pulmonary embolus)","Chest wall disorders (kyphoscoliosis, obesity, abdominal distention/ascites)","Neuromuscular disease (amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, myasthenia gravis, phrenic nerve injury, spinal cord lesion, myopathy, muscular dystrophy)","Depressed respiratory drive (narcotic/sedative use, brainstem stroke, hypothyroidism, primary alveolar hypoventilation)","Brainstem structural lesions of the pons or medulla (stroke, tumor)"],"infectionMimics":["Recurrent pneumonia from ineffective cough in neuromuscular disease can mimic or precipitate acute respiratory deterioration"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Anxiety disorders and panic attacks (overlap with but are not equivalent to chronic hyperventilation)","Mild asthma producing respiratory alkalosis (must be excluded before diagnosing chronic hyperventilation)","Diabetic ketoacidosis presenting as acute hyperventilation"],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas (the diagnostic hallmark): Paco2 >=45 mmHg defines hypoventilation; venous blood gas is an alternative, recognizing venous Pco2 runs a few points higher than arterial","Serum bicarbonate (total CO2) as a sensitive but nonspecific screen: a level <27 mmol/L with normal renal function makes hypoventilation very unlikely, while >=27 mmol/L should prompt Paco2 measurement","Chest x-ray and/or CT to identify lung/chest wall disease","Pulmonary function testing including seated-versus-supine FVC (a 15-25% supine drop suggests diaphragmatic weakness) plus maximal inspiratory and expiratory pressures","Polysomnography to demonstrate nocturnal hypercapnia, central versus obstructive apneas/hypopneas, and to screen for OSA","Brain CT or MRI to detect pontine/medullary structural lesions","OSA screening tools (Epworth Sleepiness Scale, Berlin Questionnaire, STOP-Bang) recognizing their limited sensitivity/specificity","Genetic testing for PHOX2b abnormalities in suspected congenital central hypoventilation syndrome"],"redFlags":["Paradoxical (abdominal) breathing while supine indicating significant diaphragmatic weakness or paralysis","New-onset orthopnea heralding reduced respiratory muscle force in neuromuscular disease","Progressive 15-25% supine fall in FVC signaling impending chronic respiratory failure","Daytime hypercapnia developing on top of established nocturnal hypoventilation","Signs of secondary erythrocytosis, pulmonary hypertension, or right heart failure from chronic hypoxemia/hypercapnia"],"prognosis":"Chronic hypercapnia in COPD marks more advanced disease and carries worse survival than in equally severe normocapnic patients. Documented daytime hypercapnia treated with nocturnal noninvasive positive-pressure ventilation is associated with improved daytime gas exchange, prolonged survival, and better health-related quality of life. In OHS with severe OSA, both CPAP and NIPPV improve long-term Paco2/bicarbonate and reduce cardiovascular and overall mortality. Progressive neuromuscular disease tends to advance from sleep-only to round-the-clock ventilatory support need, though modern home ventilation can delay tracheostomy and institutionalization (Harrison 22e p.2275-2276).","pitfalls":["Equating high minute ventilation with adequate alveolar ventilation: increased dead space can produce alveolar hypoventilation despite rapid breathing (Harrison 22e p.2273).","Diagnosing primary respiratory drive disorder without excluding far more common secondary causes such as sedatives, chronic narcotics, and significant hypothyroidism (Harrison 22e p.2275).","Misattributing chronic hyperventilation symptoms to cardiopulmonary disease and failing to exclude acute causes (e.g., diabetic ketoacidosis, mild asthma) before labeling it chronic (Harrison 22e p.2276-2277).","Overlooking that supplemental oxygen, even at low concentrations, can worsen hypercapnia in some hypoventilating patients (Harrison 22e p.2276).","Failing to recognize that a serum bicarbonate elevated out of proportion to chronic respiratory acidosis (excess metabolic alkalosis) can itself aggravate hypoventilation (Harrison 22e p.2276)."],"severityClassification":"Diagnostic thresholds rather than a formal grading scale: hypoventilation is defined by Paco2 >=45 mmHg; OHS requires BMI >=30 kg/m2 plus Paco2 >=45 mmHg without another cause; severe OSA is an apnea-hypopnea index >30 events/h; stable chronic hypercapnic COPD considered for home high-intensity ventilation typically has Paco2 >52 mmHg with normal pH (Harrison 22e p.2275-2276).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2273","Harrison 22e p.2274","Harrison 22e p.2275","Harrison 22e p.2276","Harrison 22e p.2277"],"crossLinks":[]},"disturbances_of_respiratory_function":{"name":"Disturbances of Respiratory Function","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Respiratory dysfunction","Disorders of lung ventilation and gas exchange","Pulmonary physiology disturbances"],"chapter":296,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2201","clinicalPearls":["The respiratory system can be conceptualized as three functionally independent components: the lung (with its airways), the neuromuscular system (the force generated by respiratory muscles), and the chest wall (everything else, including the muscle mass itself, an obese abdomen, and an enlarged heart).","Functional residual capacity (FRC) is the passive resting volume where outward recoil of the chest wall is exactly balanced by inward recoil of the lung; at FRC, negative intrapleural pressure offsets positive intrapulmonary pressure giving an airway pressure of 0.","The combined respiratory system pressure-volume relationship is sigmoid: stiffness at high lung volumes is imparted by the lung, stiffness at low volumes by the chest wall (or airway closure), with compliance greatest in the mid-range where normal tidal breathing occurs.","Expiratory airflow limitation is a normal lung property arising from collapsible (not rigid) bronchial airways: as gas accelerates toward the mouth the Bernoulli effect lowers intraluminal and transmural pressure, narrowing the airway, so harder effort yields no net increase in flow.","Inspiratory airflow limitation is rarely caused by diffuse intrathoracic airway disease because more negative pleural pressure during inspiration increases airway transmural pressure and expands airways; instead, inspiratory flow limitation points toward extrathoracic/upper-airway narrowing (e.g., tracheal lesion, post-tracheostomy stricture).","Adequacy of alveolar ventilation is reflected in PaCO2: alveolar and arterial CO2 tensions are equal, the controller normally targets ~40 mmHg, so PaCO2 below 40 indicates hyperventilation and above 40 indicates hypoventilation, with ventilatory failure being extreme hypoventilation.","Only the tidal volume that exceeds the anatomic dead space delivers fresh gas to alveoli; dead space is increased functionally when ventilated lung receives no perfusion (e.g., distal to a large pulmonary embolus), 'wasting' that ventilation.","Oxygen uptake in normal lung is perfusion-limited rather than diffusion-limited: hemoglobin is typically fully saturated by the time blood traverses only about one-third of the alveolar capillary.","Hypoxemia from true shunt (perfusion of nonventilated lung) is refractory to supplemental oxygen, whereas hypoxemia from ventilation/perfusion heterogeneity (low V/Q regions) typically improves with oxygen therapy — a key bedside discriminator.","DLCO interpretation: it falls in diseases that thicken or destroy alveolar membrane (pulmonary fibrosis, emphysema), curtail the pulmonary vasculature (pulmonary hypertension), or reduce capillary hemoglobin (anemia); it can be elevated in asthma, polycythemia, and pulmonary hemorrhage.","In severe airflow obstruction, slowed expiratory flow prevents complete exhalation, raising operating FRC (dynamic hyperinflation / auto-PEEP); at these high volumes the system is less compliant, so the patient senses difficulty INHALING even though the root problem is EXPIRATORY obstruction.","A normal FEV1/FVC ratio does not exclude airflow obstruction: obstruction can raise residual volume and lower FVC, rendering the ratio 'artifactually normal' — lung volumes should scale together (~85-115% predicted) in health, so disproportion is a clue."],"pathophysiology":"Effective respiration requires tidal ventilation to freshen alveolar gas, perfusion of each alveolus proportional to its ventilation, and adequate diffusion of O2 and CO2 across the thin alveolar membrane (roughly 70 m2 of surface in about 7 L of thorax via multigenerational airway and vascular branching). Ventilation depends on the volume-related (elastic/static) and flow-related (dynamic) mechanical properties of lung, neuromuscular system, and chest wall; lung recoil rises with inflation while the passive chest wall stiffens at low volume. Disease deranges these properties in a limited number of ways: increased lung elastic recoil restricts volumes (e.g., idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis lowers TLC/FRC/RV/FVC and DLCO but raises flow relative to volume); chest-wall abnormality (e.g., moderate obesity) blunts outward chest recoil and lowers FRC; reduced muscle strength (e.g., myasthenia gravis) lowers TLC and raises RV, reducing FVC/FEV1 as bystanders; decreased airway diameter (e.g., acute asthma) raises resistance and scoops the flow-volume loop; and decreased elastic recoil (e.g., severe emphysema) causes hyperinflation with very elevated TLC/FRC/RV and reduced DLCO. Gas-exchange failure (hypoxemia) results from low inspired oxygen tension, severe alveolar hypoventilation, perfusion of low-V/Q or unventilated (shunt) units, and, rarely, diffusion limitation; shunt blood disproportionately lowers arterial PaO2 because of the nonlinear oxyhemoglobin relationship. Hypercarbia accompanies disorders of ventilation, including severe obstruction and progressive restriction.","additionalDifferentials":["Restrictive pattern from increased lung elastic recoil (e.g., idiopathic/interstitial pulmonary fibrosis)","Restrictive pattern from chest-wall abnormality (e.g., obesity, kyphoscoliosis)","Restrictive pattern from respiratory muscle weakness (e.g., myasthenia gravis, diaphragmatic paralysis, neuromuscular disease)","Obstructive pattern from airway narrowing (e.g., asthma, chronic bronchitis)","Obstructive pattern from loss of elastic recoil (e.g., emphysema)","Pulmonary vascular disease (suggested by isolated low DLCO with otherwise preserved volumes)","Upper/central airway obstruction (e.g., tracheal adenoma, post-tracheostomy stricture, tracheomalacia)"],"infectionMimics":["Pneumonia or other alveolar-filling processes reduce respiratory system compliance and increase work of breathing, mimicking other causes of restrictive dyspnea"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pulmonary edema (alveolar filling reducing compliance)","Anemia (lowers DLCO and oxygen content, mimicking a gas-transfer defect)","Polycythemia or pulmonary hemorrhage (can elevate DLCO)","Obstructive sleep apnea with associated pulmonary arterial hypertension lowering DLCO in an obese patient","Cardiac causes of dyspnea (enlarged heart functioning as part of the chest-wall load)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Spirometry (FVC, FEV1, FEV1/FVC, flow-volume loop) to characterize obstructive vs restrictive physiology and localize airway obstruction","Absolute lung volumes by inert-gas (helium/neon) dilution or body plethysmography (plethysmography preferred in airflow obstruction, as dilution underestimates trapped gas)","Single-breath diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide (DLCO)","Arterial blood gas with calculation of the alveolar-arterial oxygen tension difference (A-a)DO2 and the alveolar gas equation","Pulse oximetry for noninvasive hemoglobin saturation; capnography (end-tidal CO2) for noninvasive ventilation assessment","Maximal inspiratory/expiratory mouth pressures and supine-vs-upright FVC to detect respiratory muscle / diaphragmatic weakness","Chest imaging: ultrasound, plain radiograph (PA and lateral), CT (with contrast and/or PET when indicated)","Further studies as guided by suspicion: bronchoscopy with lavage/biopsy, hypercoagulable and serologic testing, sputum analysis, echocardiography, surgical lung biopsy"],"redFlags":["Hypoxemia refractory to supplemental oxygen, suggesting true shunt physiology rather than simple V/Q mismatch","Rising PaCO2 above 40 mmHg indicating alveolar hypoventilation, with extreme elevation signaling impending ventilatory failure","Inspiratory flow plateau on the flow-volume loop, indicating extrathoracic/upper-airway obstruction","Precipitous fall in PaO2 on exertion in emphysema, reflecting inability to raise cardiac output across a destroyed pulmonary vascular bed","Supine FVC decrement greater than about 10-25% (versus the normal 3-8%) indicating significant diaphragmatic/neuromuscular weakness","Isolated reduction in DLCO with preserved spirometry and volumes, prompting evaluation for pulmonary vascular disease"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is determined by the underlying disorder rather than the physiologic pattern itself; the same abnormalities may arise from very different diseases, so pulmonary function testing alone often cannot discriminate among conditions. Many functional disturbances are reversible (e.g., bronchodilator-responsive airflow obstruction in acute asthma), whereas others reflect fixed structural loss (e.g., capillary and alveolar destruction in emphysema or fibrosis) that progressively impairs gas exchange and exercise capacity.","pitfalls":["Interpreting a normal FEV1/FVC ratio as excluding obstruction when raised RV has lowered FVC and made the ratio artifactually normal","Relying on single-breath inert-gas dilution in airflow obstruction, which underestimates true lung volumes because poorly ventilated regions do not receive the inert gas","Assuming alveolar hypoventilation alone explains severe hypoxemia at sea level breathing air — the alveolar gas equation shows this is uncommon unless barometric pressure is reduced (altitude)","Forgetting that a normal chest radiograph does not exclude respiratory disease, especially airway and pulmonary vascular disorders","Attributing a low DLCO to parenchymal disease without considering anemia, while overlooking that DLCO may be elevated in polycythemia or pulmonary hemorrhage","Misreading dyspnea on inhalation in severe obstruction as a primary inspiratory problem when it stems from dynamic hyperinflation due to expiratory flow limitation"],"severityClassification":"No single formal severity grade applies; physiologic severity is judged by the degree of deviation from predicted values. Most lung-function measures are normal at 85-115% of predicted, and in health volumes scale together. Examples of marked derangement cited include severe emphysema (TLC ~130%, RV ~310%, FEV1 ~35% predicted, low DLCO ~40%) versus pulmonary fibrosis (TLC/FVC/FRC/RV ~60%, low DLCO). Airway resistance (Raw) is normally <2 cmH2O/L/s, and maximal mouth pressures >±60 cmH2O at FRC make significant muscle weakness unlikely.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2201","Harrison 22e p.2202","Harrison 22e p.2203","Harrison 22e p.2204","Harrison 22e p.2205","Harrison 22e p.2206","Harrison 22e p.2207"],"crossLinks":[]},"diverticular_disease":{"name":"Diverticular Disease","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["diverticulitis","diverticulosis"],"chapter":339,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2578-2581","clinicalPearls":["Diverticulosis is the most common cause of hematochezia (brisk lower-GI bleed) in patients >60 years; bleeding is abrupt in onset, usually painless, sometimes massive, and often from the right colon. Chronic or occult bleeding is NOT characteristic (Harrison 22e p.2496, 2579).","Only ~20% of patients with diverticulosis will ever have GI bleeding; most bleeds are self-limited and stop spontaneously with bowel rest (colonic diverticula stop bleeding spontaneously in >=90% of patients). Lifetime risk of rebleeding is ~25% (Harrison 22e p.2496, 2579).","Acute uncomplicated diverticulitis characteristically presents with fever, anorexia, left lower quadrant abdominal pain, and obstipation (Harrison 22e p.2579).","Diagnosis of diverticulitis is best made on a contrast-enhanced abdominal and pelvic CT showing sigmoid diverticula, thickened colonic wall >4 mm, and pericolic fat inflammation WITHOUT extravasation of contrast or fluid (Harrison 22e p.2579).","Diverticula commonly affect the left and sigmoid colon and the rectum is always spared; however in Asian populations 70% of diverticula are in the right colon and cecum (Harrison 22e p.2579).","The pseudodiverticulum (protrusion of mucosa and submucosa only, through the muscularis propria) is the most common type seen in the colon; a true diverticulum is herniation of the entire bowel wall (Harrison 22e p.2578).","Perforated diverticular disease is staged using the Hinchey classification (I: confined paracolic abscess; II: distant/pelvic abscess after spontaneous closure; III: noncommunicating perforation with purulent peritonitis; IV: free perforation with fecal peritonitis); modified to add stage Ia for phlegmon/early abscess (Harrison 22e p.2580).","Pneumaturia or passage of stool/air through the vagina or skin signals a fistula (colovesical, colovaginal, or colocutaneous); colovaginal fistulas are more common in women who have undergone hysterectomy (Harrison 22e p.2579).","Colonoscopy should be considered ~6 weeks AFTER resolution of a first episode, because colon cancer prevalence is low overall (<2%) but higher after complicated diverticulitis (6-8%) (Harrison 22e p.2580).","Routine antibiotics do NOT shorten time to symptom resolution or reduce complications/recurrence in immunocompetent patients with uncomplicated diverticular disease (AVOD and DIABOLO trials, plus meta-analysis) (Harrison 22e p.2580).","Diverticulosis affects about half the population aged >60 years and the majority remain asymptomatic; only ~5% develop acute diverticular disease (Harrison 22e p.2578)."],"pathophysiology":"Diverticula form where the nutrient artery (vasa recta) penetrates the muscularis propria, creating a point of weakness in the colonic wall; most arise in the sigmoid colon, likely reflecting its high-pressure zone where higher-amplitude contractions and firm/constipated stool act on a weak segment. Pathogenesis is now viewed as multifactorial rather than simply low-fiber diet: gut dysbiosis, chronic low-grade inflammation driving neuronal degeneration with resulting dysmotility and high intraluminal pressure, and abnormal collagen cross-linking causing loss of compliance all contribute. Genome-wide association studies (Iceland, Denmark, UK biobanks) link LAMB4 (extracellular-matrix laminin family) and TNFSF15 (TNF family, inflammation) variants to early-onset severe disease. Complications occur when the vasa recta is compressed or eroded (bleeding) or a diverticulum perforates (Harrison 22e p.2578-2579).","additionalDifferentials":["Colorectal cancer (parallel epidemiology and overlapping presentation; must exclude with colonoscopy after an episode)","Irritable bowel syndrome (SCAD can mimic it)","Crohn's disease (diverticulitis can be confused with CD clinically and radiographically)","Appendicitis","Ischemic colitis","Twisted ovarian cyst / gynecologic pathology"],"infectionMimics":["Acute appendicitis","Infectious colitis","Pelvic inflammatory disease / tubo-ovarian abscess"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Colorectal cancer","Inflammatory bowel disease (Crohn's disease, ulcerative colitis)","Irritable bowel syndrome","Ischemic colitis","Giant sigmoid diverticulum (air-fluid level in LLQ on plain film)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced abdominal/pelvic CT (diagnostic modality of choice for diverticulitis)","Multiplanar CT angiography for active diverticular bleeding localization","Nuclear medicine tagged red-cell scan for bleeding localization","Mesenteric angiography (allows superselective coil embolization if active bleeding is localized)","Colonoscopy ~6 weeks after resolution to exclude malignancy (avoid during the acute attack)","CRP and WBC count to gauge inflammation/disease progression risk"],"redFlags":["Signs of peritonitis indicating diverticular perforation","Abscess formation (~16% of complicated disease) with abdominal distention and localized peritonitis","Free perforation with fecal peritonitis (Hinchey IV)","Fistula (colovesical with pneumaturia, colovaginal, colocutaneous)","Obstruction/stricture","Air-fluid level in LLQ on plain film suggesting giant sigmoid diverticulum (impending perforation)","Massive or refractory lower-GI hemorrhage, hemodynamic instability, or large-volume transfusion requirement","Immunocompromise, chronic renal failure, or collagen-vascular disease (fivefold greater perforation risk on recurrent attacks)"],"prognosis":"Most patients with diverticulosis stay asymptomatic; only ~5% develop acute diverticular disease, and 10-25% of those with diverticular disease have recurrent symptoms with up to 10% requiring surgery. Diverticular bleeds are largely self-limited (spontaneous cessation in >=90%), with lifetime rebleeding risk ~25%. After complicated diverticulitis the risk of underlying colon cancer rises to 6-8% (vs <2% overall). Elective surgical resection for recurrent SUDD improved quality of life and was more cost-effective at 5 years (DIRECT trial) (Harrison 22e p.2578-2581).","pitfalls":["Do NOT perform colonoscopy during acute diverticulitis (risk of perforation); defer it ~6 weeks until the attack resolves.","Do not reflexively give antibiotics for uncomplicated diverticulitis in immunocompetent patients - trials show no benefit.","The historical advice to avoid nuts, corn, and popcorn rests on anecdotal data only and is not evidence-based; higher intake was actually associated with lower recurrence risk.","Mesalazine appears to help symptoms in SUDD but shows no benefit once objective inflammation (CRP, imaging) is considered.","A systematic review found no benefit to probiotics used alone.","Right-sided diverticula predominate in Asian populations, so a right-colon source should not be dismissed."],"severityClassification":"Hinchey classification of perforated diverticulitis: Stage I - perforation with confined paracolic abscess (modified stage Ia = phlegmon/early abscess); Stage II - perforation that closed spontaneously with distant (pelvic) abscess; Stage III - noncommunicating perforation with purulent peritonitis (diverticular neck closed off); Stage IV - free perforation communicating with the peritoneum producing fecal peritonitis. Clinically, disease is divided into uncomplicated (~75%) and complicated (~25%: abscess 16%, perforation 10%, stricture 5%, fistula 2%) (Harrison 22e p.2579-2580).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2578-2581","Harrison 22e p.2496"],"crossLinks":["colorectal_cancer","bowel_obstruction"]},"donovanosis":{"name":"Donovanosis","system":"Genitourinary / Sexually Transmitted Infection","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Granuloma inguinale","Granuloma venereum"],"chapter":178,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1356","clinicalPearls":["Chronic, progressive bacterial infection that predominantly involves the genital region and is regarded as a sexually transmitted infection of low infectivity (Harrison 22e p.1356).","Caused by Klebsiella granulomatis comb nov; the older name Calymmatobacterium granulomatis is still favored by some authorities based on 16S rRNA gene sequence analysis (Harrison 22e p.1356).","Diagnosis is confirmed by microscopic identification of intracytoplasmic Donovan bodies (about 1.5 x 0.7 micrometers) within large mononuclear Pund cells, where they appear as gram-negative cysts with a characteristic safety-pin appearance (Harrison 22e p.1356-1357).","When donovanosis is suspected clinically, the smear for Donovan bodies should be obtained before swabs for other genital ulcer pathogens so adequate material is collected; smears can be read at the bedside with rapid Giemsa or Wright stain (Harrison 22e p.1357).","An experienced clinician's diagnosis based on lesion appearance has a high positive predictive value, and donovanosis is probably the most readily recognizable cause of genital ulceration (Harrison 22e p.1357-1358).","Four lesion types are described: classic beefy-red ulcerogranulomatous ulcer that bleeds easily on touch, hypertrophic/verrucous ulcer with a raised irregular edge, necrotic foul-smelling destructive ulcer, and sclerotic/cicatricial fibrous lesion (Harrison 22e p.1357).","Genitalia are affected in about 90% of patients and the inguinal region in about 10%; lymphadenitis is uncommon, in contrast to other ulcerative STIs (Harrison 22e p.1357).","Cervical lesions can mimic cervical carcinoma, and in men lesions are associated with lack of circumcision (Harrison 22e p.1357).","Extragenital lesions occur in about 6% of cases and may involve the lip, gums, cheek, palate, pharynx, larynx, and chest; hematogenous spread to liver and bone has been reported (Harrison 22e p.1357).","Donovanosis is a recognized risk factor for HIV infection, and its global decline likely reflects more effective management of genital ulcers given their role in HIV transmission (Harrison 22e p.1357).","The estimated incubation period is approximately 50 days based on experimental human infection, though it remains uncertain (Harrison 22e p.1357).","A diagnostic PCR exploits two unique base changes in the phoE gene that eliminate Hae111 restriction sites, distinguishing K. granulomatis from related Klebsiella species; a multiplex genital-ulcer PCR including K. granulomatis exists (Harrison 22e p.1357)."],"pathophysiology":"Donovanosis is a chronic granulomatous infection caused by the intracellular gram-negative organism Klebsiella granulomatis (formerly Calymmatobacterium granulomatis). The lesion begins as a papule or subcutaneous nodule that ulcerates, often after trauma. The organism resides within macrophages and the stratum malpighii as intracytoplasmic Donovan bodies inside large mononuclear (Pund) cells; these cysts rupture and release infective organisms. Histology shows chronic inflammation with infiltration by plasma cells and neutrophils, and epithelial changes including ulceration, microabscesses, and elongation of the rete ridges. The organism resisted reproducible culture until the mid-1990s, when isolation in peripheral-blood monocytes and human epithelial cell lines was achieved (Harrison 22e p.1356-1357).","additionalDifferentials":["Primary syphilitic chancre (Harrison 22e p.1357)","Secondary syphilis with condylomata lata (Harrison 22e p.1357)","Chancroid (Harrison 22e p.1357)","Lymphogranuloma venereum (Harrison 22e p.1357)","Genital herpes (Harrison 22e p.1357)","Genital neoplasm / carcinoma (Harrison 22e p.1357)","Amebiasis (Harrison 22e p.1357)"],"infectionMimics":["Rhinoscleroma on histology (Harrison 22e p.1357)","Leishmaniasis on histology (Harrison 22e p.1357)","Histoplasmosis on histology (Harrison 22e p.1357)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cervical carcinoma (cervical lesions may mimic it) (Harrison 22e p.1357)","Genital neoplasm (Harrison 22e p.1357)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Tissue smear for Donovan bodies stained with rapid Giemsa or Wright stain (Harrison 22e p.1357)","Crush preparation of granulation tissue spread between two slides (Harrison 22e p.1357)","Histologic biopsy showing chronic inflammation, microabscesses, and rete ridge elongation (Harrison 22e p.1357)","phoE gene PCR with colorimetric detection to differentiate K. granulomatis from related Klebsiella species (Harrison 22e p.1357)","Multiplex genital-ulcer PCR including K. granulomatis (Harrison 22e p.1357)"],"redFlags":["Late presentation with extensive ulceration (Harrison 22e p.1357-1358)","Cervical lesion mimicking carcinoma requiring exclusion of malignancy (Harrison 22e p.1357)","Neoplastic change developing within chronic lesions (Harrison 22e p.1357)","Stenosis of the urethra, vagina, or anus (Harrison 22e p.1357)","Pseudoelephantiasis from lymphatic involvement (Harrison 22e p.1357)","Lesions in newborns (may present as ear infection) or children, prompting evaluation for source of infection (Harrison 22e p.1357)"],"prognosis":"Donovanosis is a treatable condition, and reassurance is an important part of care given the embarrassment and low self-esteem many patients experience. It is now confined to a few specific geographic locations, has been almost entirely eliminated in Australia and is close to elimination in South Africa, and its global eradication is considered a distinct possibility. Surgery may be required for very advanced lesions, and patients treated for shorter courses require monitoring until lesions heal completely (Harrison 22e p.1357-1358).","pitfalls":["Collecting other genital-ulcer swabs before the Donovan body smear can leave insufficient material for diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1357).","Serologic tests are only poorly specific and are not currently used, so they should not be relied upon (Harrison 22e p.1357).","Mixed infections with other genital ulcer pathogens are common and may obscure the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1357).","Lymphadenitis is uncommon, so its absence should not exclude the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1357).","Cervical lesions can be mistaken for cervical carcinoma and vice versa (Harrison 22e p.1357).","During pregnancy lesions develop more quickly and respond more slowly, so closer monitoring is needed (Harrison 22e p.1357)."],"severityClassification":"No formal severity grading is given. Lesions are categorized morphologically into four types: classic ulcerogranulomatous, hypertrophic/verrucous, necrotic/destructive, and sclerotic/cicatricial (Harrison 22e p.1357).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1356","Harrison 22e p.1357","Harrison 22e p.1358"],"crossLinks":[]},"down_syndrome":{"name":"Down Syndrome","system":"Genetic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["trisomy 21","Down's syndrome","trisomy 21 syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common autosomal chromosomal disorder and a leading genetic cause of intellectual disability, resulting from an extra copy of chromosome 21.","Most cases are due to meiotic nondisjunction, with incidence rising markedly with advancing maternal age; a minority result from translocation or mosaicism.","Recognisable features include hypotonia, upslanting palpebral fissures, epicanthic folds, a flat nasal bridge, small ears, a single transverse palmar crease, and a sandal gap between the toes.","Congenital heart disease is common, particularly atrioventricular septal defects, so early echocardiography is recommended.","Gastrointestinal anomalies such as duodenal atresia and Hirschsprung disease occur more frequently.","There is increased risk of hypothyroidism, hearing and visual impairment, obstructive sleep apnoea, and atlantoaxial instability, warranting scheduled surveillance.","Children have a higher risk of transient myeloproliferative disorder in the newborn period and of leukaemia in childhood.","Diagnosis is confirmed by karyotype, which also identifies translocations relevant to recurrence risk and family counselling.","Early developmental support and coordinated multidisciplinary care improve developmental and health outcomes."],"pathophysiology":"The presence of a third copy of chromosome 21 leads to over-expression of the genes it carries, disrupting normal development across multiple organ systems in a dosage-dependent manner. This gene-dosage imbalance affects neurodevelopment, cardiac and gastrointestinal morphogenesis, immune function, and haematopoiesis. Most trisomy 21 arises from failure of chromosomal separation during meiosis (nondisjunction), while translocation and mosaic forms produce the extra genetic material through other mechanisms.","additionalDifferentials":["Other trisomies (trisomy 18, trisomy 13)","Congenital hypothyroidism (overlapping hypotonia and coarse features)","Zellweger and other syndromes with hypotonia","Other syndromic causes of intellectual disability","Non-syndromic congenital heart disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Karyotype to confirm trisomy 21 and identify translocation or mosaicism","Echocardiography to detect congenital heart disease","Thyroid function testing at birth and on a scheduled basis","Hearing and vision assessments","Full blood count to screen for transient myeloproliferative disorder and later leukaemia","Cervical spine assessment when symptoms suggest atlantoaxial instability"],"redFlags":["Signs of significant congenital heart disease or heart failure","Bilious vomiting or abdominal signs suggesting intestinal obstruction","Neurological signs of cervical cord compression from atlantoaxial instability","Persistent pallor, bruising, or abnormal blood counts suggesting leukaemia","Severe obstructive sleep apnoea"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy has improved substantially with modern medical and surgical care and now commonly extends into adulthood; outcomes depend largely on cardiac disease and other complications, and most individuals have mild to moderate intellectual disability with early-onset Alzheimer-type changes in later life.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal heart without echocardiographic screening","Overlooking hypothyroidism, which can mimic or worsen developmental delay","Missing atlantoaxial instability before activities or anaesthesia in symptomatic children","Failing to follow structured health surveillance recommendations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"drowning":{"name":"Drowning","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["submersion injury","near-drowning","immersion injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Drowning is the process of respiratory impairment from submersion or immersion in liquid; outdated terms such as near-drowning, wet, dry, and secondary drowning are no longer recommended","The central injury is hypoxaemia; the historical distinction between fresh and salt water has little practical bearing on resuscitation","Correcting hypoxia takes priority - rescue breathing and early effective ventilation are the most important interventions, and resuscitation should begin with airway and breathing rather than the usual circulation-first approach","Aspirated water washes out surfactant and injures the alveolar-capillary membrane, and pulmonary oedema or acute respiratory distress syndrome may evolve over the following hours","Symptomatic survivors need a period of observation, as respiratory deterioration can be delayed; a completely asymptomatic patient with normal oxygenation after several hours is at low risk","Suspect and manage hypothermia, which frequently accompanies submersion and can be protective of the brain, so resuscitation should continue until the patient is rewarmed","Consider precipitating and associated conditions, including cervical spine injury (in diving), seizures, arrhythmia, intoxication, and hypoglycaemia","Prophylactic antibiotics and routine corticosteroids are not recommended; antibiotics are reserved for established infection or grossly contaminated water"],"pathophysiology":"Submersion triggers breath-holding, then involuntary aspiration of water or laryngospasm, leading to progressive hypoxaemia, hypercapnia, and acidosis. Aspirated fluid disrupts and washes out pulmonary surfactant and damages the alveolar-capillary membrane, causing atelectasis, ventilation-perfusion mismatch, non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, and potentially acute respiratory distress syndrome. Sustained hypoxia causes global tissue injury, with the brain most vulnerable to hypoxic-ischaemic damage. Cardiac arrhythmias and arrest follow severe hypoxia and acidosis, and coexisting hypothermia alters both the injury and the resuscitation.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary cardiac arrest with secondary immersion","Seizure or syncope precipitating submersion","Cervical spinal cord injury from diving","Intoxication (alcohol or drugs) as a contributing factor","Acute coronary syndrome or arrhythmia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas to quantify hypoxaemia and acidosis","Chest imaging to assess for aspiration and pulmonary oedema","Core temperature measurement for hypothermia","ECG and cardiac monitoring","Capillary glucose, electrolytes, and cervical spine imaging where trauma is suspected"],"redFlags":["Hypoxaemia, respiratory distress, or need for ventilatory support","Reduced conscious level or coma indicating hypoxic brain injury","Cardiac arrest or significant arrhythmia","Severe hypothermia requiring continued resuscitation during rewarming","Delayed worsening of oxygenation over the observation period"],"prognosis":"Outcome is largely determined by the duration of submersion and hypoxia and the neurological status at the scene and on arrival; prolonged submersion and persistent coma predict poor neurological recovery, whereas patients who are alert with normal oxygenation generally do well, and hypothermia can occasionally allow good recovery after prolonged arrest.","pitfalls":["Prioritising chest compressions over ventilation in a hypoxia-driven arrest","Discharging a symptomatic patient too early and missing delayed respiratory deterioration","Terminating resuscitation before adequately rewarming a hypothermic patient","Overlooking a precipitating cause such as seizure, arrhythmia, cervical injury, or intoxication"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"drug_allergy":{"name":"Drug allergy","system":"Immunology / Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["drug hypersensitivity","medication allergy","adverse drug reaction (immune-mediated)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Middleton's Allergy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["True drug allergy is an immune-mediated subset of adverse drug reactions and must be distinguished from predictable dose-related side effects and non-immune intolerance","The Gell and Coombs framework is useful: immediate IgE-mediated reactions (type I) cause urticaria, angioedema, and anaphylaxis, while delayed T-cell-mediated reactions (type IV) cause most drug-induced rashes","The tempo matters: immediate reactions arise within minutes to an hour of exposure, whereas delayed maculopapular eruptions and severe cutaneous reactions typically appear days into a course","Beta-lactam antibiotics are the commonest cause of drug-induced anaphylaxis, but a reported penicillin allergy is frequently inaccurate and unnecessarily drives use of broader, more toxic alternatives","Severe cutaneous adverse reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis, and DRESS are potentially fatal and are suggested by mucosal involvement, skin pain, blistering, fever, facial oedema, and organ dysfunction","Skin testing is validated mainly for penicillins and perioperative agents; specific IgE and in-vitro tests are limited, so the supervised graded challenge remains the definitive test for many drugs","Management principles are immediate cessation of the culprit drug, treatment of the reaction, clear documentation and patient warning, and selection of a structurally unrelated alternative","Desensitisation can allow temporary tolerance when a culprit drug is essential and no alternative exists, but it is contraindicated in severe cutaneous reactions such as SJS/TEN and DRESS","Careful de-labelling of unverified penicillin allergy through history and, where appropriate, challenge is an important antimicrobial-stewardship intervention"],"pathophysiology":"Immune-mediated drug reactions occur when a drug or its reactive metabolite, often after binding host protein as a hapten, is recognised by the adaptive immune system. Type I reactions involve drug-specific IgE on mast cells and basophils, producing immediate mediator release. Type II reactions are antibody-mediated cytotoxicity against drug-coated cells (e.g. immune haemolysis, thrombocytopenia), and type III reactions involve immune-complex deposition (serum sickness, vasculitis). Type IV delayed reactions are T-cell-driven and range from benign exanthems to severe cutaneous reactions; the p-i concept describes direct, non-covalent drug interaction with immune receptors, and specific HLA alleles predispose to particular severe reactions.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-immune (pharmacologic) intolerance and dose-related toxicity","Viral exanthem coinciding with drug therapy","Direct mast-cell activation (e.g. by opioids, vancomycin infusion reaction)","NSAID- or ACE-inhibitor-induced angioedema through non-IgE mechanisms","Underlying autoimmune or infectious disease producing rash or organ dysfunction","Chronic spontaneous urticaria unrelated to the drug"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin prick and intradermal testing where validated (penicillins, perioperative agents)","Serum drug-specific IgE where available and serum tryptase during acute reactions","Supervised graded drug challenge to confirm or exclude allergy","Delayed-reading intradermal or patch testing for suspected type IV reactions","HLA genotyping before selected high-risk drugs where guidelines recommend it","Blood count, eosinophils, liver and renal function in suspected DRESS or systemic involvement"],"redFlags":["Anaphylaxis with hypotension or airway compromise requiring immediate intramuscular adrenaline","Mucosal erosions, skin blistering or pain, or positive Nikolsky sign suggesting SJS/TEN","Fever, facial oedema, lymphadenopathy, eosinophilia, and organ involvement suggesting DRESS","Any severe cutaneous adverse reaction, in which re-exposure or desensitisation is contraindicated"],"prognosis":"Most benign exanthems and immediate reactions resolve fully once the drug is withdrawn and future exposure avoided. Severe cutaneous adverse reactions carry significant morbidity and mortality, particularly toxic epidermal necrolysis, and require urgent specialist and supportive care.","pitfalls":["Accepting an unverified allergy label and defaulting to broader, more toxic alternatives","Attempting desensitisation or re-challenge after a severe cutaneous reaction","Mistaking a viral rash or predictable side effect for true allergy, or vice versa","Failing to document the reaction clearly and counsel the patient about cross-reacting agents"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"dry_eye_disease":{"name":"Dry eye disease","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["keratoconjunctivitis sicca","dry eye syndrome","ocular surface disease","DED"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with grittiness, burning, foreign body sensation, fluctuating vision, and paradoxical reflex tearing","Broadly divided into aqueous-deficient (reduced tear production) and evaporative (mostly meibomian gland dysfunction) types, though many patients have mixed disease","Symptoms often exceed clinical signs, and vision that clears on blinking is characteristic","Useful clinical tests include tear film break-up time, ocular surface staining with fluorescein or lissamine green, and the Schirmer test","Aqueous-deficient dry eye may signal Sjogren syndrome, so ask about dry mouth and joint symptoms","A stepwise management approach begins with lubricants, lid hygiene, and environmental measures, escalating to anti-inflammatory therapy, punctal occlusion, and secretagogues","Meibomian gland dysfunction is managed with warm compresses, lid hygiene, and treatment of associated blepharitis and rosacea","Untreated severe disease can cause filamentary keratitis, persistent epithelial defects, and, rarely, corneal melting or perforation"],"pathophysiology":"Dry eye disease is a multifactorial disorder of the ocular surface characterised by loss of tear film homeostasis. Reduced aqueous production (as in Sjogren and non-Sjogren lacrimal dysfunction) or excessive evaporation (chiefly from meibomian gland dysfunction with an unstable lipid layer) leads to tear hyperosmolarity. Hyperosmolarity triggers a self-perpetuating cycle of ocular surface inflammation, epithelial damage, and neurosensory dysfunction, which further destabilises the tear film. Goblet cell loss and reduced mucin worsen wetting, sustaining the inflammatory cascade.","additionalDifferentials":["Allergic conjunctivitis","Blepharitis and meibomian gland dysfunction","Chronic conjunctivitis (infective or toxic)","Corneal epithelial disease or recurrent erosion","Neurotrophic keratopathy","Medication-induced ocular surface toxicity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Tear film break-up time to assess tear stability","Ocular surface staining with fluorescein and lissamine green","Schirmer test to quantify aqueous production","Tear osmolarity measurement where available","Meibomian gland assessment and expression","Autoantibody and systemic testing when Sjogren syndrome is suspected"],"redFlags":["Persistent epithelial defect, corneal thinning, or melt indicating severe surface breakdown","Features of Sjogren syndrome or other systemic autoimmune disease requiring investigation","Marked reduction in corneal sensation suggesting neurotrophic keratopathy","Rapidly worsening pain, photophobia, or infiltrate suggesting secondary infection"],"prognosis":"Dry eye disease is usually chronic and requires ongoing management; most patients achieve good symptom control, but severe or autoimmune-associated disease can cause persistent surface damage and visual impairment.","pitfalls":["Focusing on aqueous supplementation while neglecting meibomian gland dysfunction and lid disease","Overlooking underlying Sjogren syndrome in aqueous-deficient patients","Attributing all irritation to dryness and missing allergy, infection, or medication toxicity","Long-term use of preserved drops aggravating ocular surface toxicity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"dry_socket":{"name":"Dry socket","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["alveolar osteitis","localised osteitis","fibrinolytic alveolitis","post-extraction alveolitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Dry socket is a painful complication of tooth extraction caused by disintegration or loss of the protective blood clot, exposing alveolar bone","Severe throbbing pain characteristically begins two to four days after extraction, later than the pain of the surgery itself","The socket appears empty with exposed bone and often malodour, but there is usually no purulent discharge or significant systemic upset","It is most common after mandibular molar extractions, particularly lower third molars","Recognised risk factors include smoking, oral contraceptive use, traumatic or difficult extractions, and poor oral hygiene","It is primarily an inflammatory and fibrinolytic process rather than a true infection, so antibiotics are usually not indicated","Management is supportive: irrigation, gentle debridement, and placement of a sedative dressing to relieve pain and protect the bone","Preventive measures include atraumatic technique, avoiding smoking, and good postoperative care"],"pathophysiology":"Dry socket, or alveolar osteitis, develops when the blood clot that normally fills and protects an extraction socket is lost prematurely, often through excessive local fibrinolysis. Breakdown of the clot exposes the alveolar bone and nerve endings to the oral environment, producing intense pain. Contributing factors such as smoking, bacterial contamination, trauma during extraction, and elevated fibrinolytic activity impair clot stability and delay the normal organisation and healing of the socket. The condition is characterised by inflammation of the exposed bone rather than an established suppurative infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Post-extraction infection with suppuration","Retained root fragment or foreign body","Osteomyelitis of the jaw","Medication-related osteonecrosis of the jaw","Referred or neuropathic post-surgical pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination of the socket for an absent clot and exposed bone","Assessment for systemic signs to distinguish from spreading infection","Radiography if a retained root, foreign body, or bony pathology is suspected","Review of risk factors including smoking and medication history","Consideration of osteonecrosis risk in patients on antiresorptive therapy"],"redFlags":["Spreading swelling, fever, trismus, or purulent discharge suggesting true infection rather than simple dry socket","Failure to heal or exposed necrotic bone in a patient on antiresorptive or antiangiogenic drugs raising concern for osteonecrosis","Persistent numbness or worsening pain suggesting nerve injury or osteomyelitis"],"prognosis":"Dry socket is self-limiting and typically resolves over one to two weeks with supportive care and adequate analgesia, without long-term sequelae in most patients.","pitfalls":["Prescribing antibiotics for uncomplicated dry socket, which is not an infection","Mistaking spreading infection or osteomyelitis for simple alveolar osteitis","Overlooking a retained root fragment as the cause of persistent socket pain","Failing to consider medication-related osteonecrosis in at-risk patients with non-healing sockets"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"duchenne_muscular_dystrophy":{"name":"Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy","system":"Neuromuscular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["DMD","Duchenne dystrophy","pseudohypertrophic muscular dystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common and most severe childhood muscular dystrophy, an X-linked recessive disorder that therefore affects boys, caused by absence of functional dystrophin.","Symptoms usually appear in early childhood with delayed walking, frequent falls, difficulty running and climbing stairs, and a waddling gait.","Gower sign - using the hands to 'climb up' the legs when rising from the floor - reflects proximal, especially pelvic girdle, weakness.","Calf pseudohypertrophy from fatty and fibrous replacement of muscle is a characteristic finding.","Serum creatine kinase is markedly elevated, often into the thousands, even before overt weakness.","Weakness is progressive; most boys lose independent ambulation by around the early teens without disease-modifying therapy.","Cardiomyopathy and respiratory muscle weakness are the main causes of morbidity and death, so cardiac and pulmonary surveillance is essential.","Glucocorticoids slow progression and prolong ambulation and are a mainstay of care; multidisciplinary management improves outcomes.","Diagnosis is confirmed by genetic testing for dystrophin gene mutations, with muscle biopsy reserved for uncertain cases; learning and behavioural difficulties are more common than in the general population."],"pathophysiology":"Mutations in the DMD gene abolish functional dystrophin, a cytoskeletal protein that links the muscle fibre cytoskeleton to the extracellular matrix through the dystrophin-glycoprotein complex. Without it the sarcolemma is unstable and is damaged by contraction, leading to repeated cycles of muscle fibre degeneration and regeneration, calcium influx, inflammation, and eventual replacement of muscle by fat and fibrous tissue. Dystrophin deficiency in cardiac muscle produces cardiomyopathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Becker muscular dystrophy (milder allelic disorder)","Limb-girdle muscular dystrophies","Spinal muscular atrophy","Congenital myopathies","Inflammatory myopathy","Metabolic myopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase (markedly elevated)","Genetic testing for DMD gene deletions, duplications, and point mutations","Muscle biopsy with dystrophin immunostaining when genetics are inconclusive","Electrocardiogram and echocardiography for cardiomyopathy surveillance","Pulmonary function testing to monitor respiratory muscle strength"],"redFlags":["Declining respiratory function or nocturnal hypoventilation","Symptoms or signs of cardiomyopathy or arrhythmia","Rapid loss of motor milestones or ambulation","Recurrent chest infections with weak cough","Perioperative risk, including reactions to certain anaesthetic agents"],"prognosis":"Without disease-modifying treatment, progressive weakness leads to loss of ambulation in the early teens, with respiratory and cardiac complications limiting survival; corticosteroids, cardiac and respiratory support, and multidisciplinary care have substantially improved life expectancy.","pitfalls":["Attributing early motor delay and clumsiness to normal variation and delaying creatine kinase testing","Overlooking the diagnosis in a boy presenting with isolated raised transaminases (from muscle, not liver)","Neglecting cardiac and respiratory surveillance in an ambulant child","Using certain anaesthetic agents that can trigger dangerous reactions","Failing to offer genetic counselling and carrier testing to the family"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"dyslipidemia":{"name":"Dyslipidemia","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hyperlipidemia","hypercholesterolemia","hypertriglyceridemia"],"chapter":419,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3237","clinicalPearls":["Elevated plasma LDL cholesterol is a major modifiable driver of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease; globally high LDL is estimated to contribute to ~41% of ischemic heart disease deaths and ~9% of stroke deaths (Harrison 22e p.2017).","Lipoprotein(a) appears to be an independent predictor of cardiovascular risk beyond total or LDL cholesterol, and is elevated in roughly 20% of the global population; levels are reported higher in those of African descent and intermediate in East and South Asians (Harrison 22e p.2017).","Tendon xanthomas (e.g., Achilles and extensor finger tendons), xanthelasma, and plane/tuberous xanthomas reflect hypercholesterolemia (hyperlipoproteinemia types II and III); eruptive xanthomas are most commonly associated with hypertriglyceridemia (types I, IV, V) (Harrison 22e p.234, p.1860).","Palmar (palmar crease) xanthomas are specific for type III hyperlipoproteinemia, and severe hypertriglyceridemia can produce eruptive xanthomatosis and lipemia retinalis (Harrison 22e p.1860).","Loss-of-function PCSK9 variants are associated with lower LDL cholesterol and reduced coronary artery disease, which led to development of PCSK9 monoclonal antibodies; rare gain-of-function PCSK9 variants are a cause of familial hypercholesterolemia (Harrison 22e p.260)."],"pathophysiology":"Atherogenic lipoproteins, principally LDL, deposit cholesterol within the arterial wall and disturb normal endothelial functions (control of vascular tone, maintenance of an antithrombotic surface, and regulation of inflammatory cell adhesion and diapedesis), promoting atherosclerosis; LDL, cigarette smoking, hypertension, and diabetes mellitus vary in their relative impact on these endothelial functions. Lipoprotein(a) is thought to add risk through increased cellular lipid accumulation, endothelial dysfunction, and effects on coagulation. Severe hypertriglyceridemia (chylomicron/VLDL accumulation) can precipitate acute pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.2017, p.2046).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting lipid profile: total cholesterol, LDL, HDL, and triglycerides (Harrison 22e p.2046).","Lipoprotein(a) measurement, given its role as an independent cardiovascular risk predictor (Harrison 22e p.2017, p.2046).","Apolipoprotein B as a marker of atherogenic particle burden (Harrison 22e p.3237).","Screening for secondary contributors: glucose/HbA1c and urinalysis for diabetes and renal disease, and thyroid function testing when indicated (Harrison 22e p.2046).","High-sensitivity C-reactive protein may be considered as an independent cardiovascular risk marker informing therapeutic decisions (Harrison 22e p.2046)."],"redFlags":["Tendon xanthomas with premature coronary artery disease, suggesting familial hypercholesterolemia (Harrison 22e p.234, p.260).","Severe hypertriglyceridemia, which can precipitate acute pancreatitis and may manifest with eruptive xanthomatosis and lipemia retinalis (Harrison 22e p.1860)."],"prognosis":"LDL-driven dyslipidemia is a leading contributor to atherosclerotic cardiovascular morbidity and mortality, including ischemic heart disease and stroke; lowering LDL (e.g., a 50% or greater reduction with high-intensity statin therapy in patients with established atherosclerotic disease) reduces the risk of myocardial infarction, stroke, and death (Harrison 22e p.2017, p.2056).","pitfalls":["Attributing dyslipidemia solely to primary lipid disorders while overlooking secondary causes such as diabetes mellitus, hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome, and excess alcohol intake (Harrison 22e p.2046).","Underrecognizing familial hypercholesterolemia in patients with tendon xanthomas and premature coronary disease (Harrison 22e p.234, p.260)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.3237","Harrison 22e p.2017","Harrison 22e p.2046","Harrison 22e p.234","Harrison 22e p.1860","Harrison 22e p.260"],"crossLinks":["acs","pancreatitis","diabetes_mellitus"]},"dysmenorrhea":{"name":"Dysmenorrhoea","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dysmenorrhea","painful periods","menstrual cramps","period pain"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Primary dysmenorrhoea is painful menstruation without underlying pelvic pathology and typically begins within a year or two of menarche once ovulatory cycles are established","Secondary dysmenorrhoea arises from identifiable pelvic disease, most often endometriosis, adenomyosis, fibroids, or pelvic inflammatory disease, and tends to begin later or change in character","The pain of primary dysmenorrhoea is cramping and suprapubic, often radiating to the back and thighs, and coincides with the onset of menstrual flow","Prostaglandin-driven myometrial contractions and uterine ischaemia underlie primary dysmenorrhoea, which explains the effectiveness of prostaglandin synthesis inhibitors","First-line management combines non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs with hormonal suppression of ovulation such as combined hormonal contraception","Features suggesting secondary causes include pain that starts before bleeding or persists after it, deep dyspareunia, abnormal bleeding, or poor response to standard treatment","A careful history and examination, with pelvic ultrasound and consideration of laparoscopy, help identify secondary pathology when suspected","Symptoms of primary dysmenorrhoea often improve with age and after childbirth"],"pathophysiology":"In primary dysmenorrhoea, the fall in progesterone at the end of the luteal phase triggers release of arachidonic acid and increased endometrial production of prostaglandins, particularly PGF2-alpha. These stimulate intense, poorly coordinated myometrial contractions that raise intrauterine pressure and reduce uterine blood flow, producing ischaemic pain analogous to angina; leukotrienes and vasopressin contribute. In secondary dysmenorrhoea the pain results instead from a structural or inflammatory disorder such as ectopic endometrial tissue, myometrial invasion by endometrium, leiomyomas, or infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Endometriosis","Adenomyosis","Uterine fibroids","Pelvic inflammatory disease","Ovarian cyst","Cervical stenosis or outflow obstruction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Focused history and pelvic examination to distinguish primary from secondary causes","Transvaginal pelvic ultrasound to assess for fibroids, adenomyosis, and adnexal pathology","Screening for sexually transmitted infection where pelvic inflammatory disease is suspected","Diagnostic laparoscopy when endometriosis is suspected and symptoms persist","Pregnancy test to exclude pregnancy-related pain"],"redFlags":["Pain beginning well after menarche or changing markedly in character, suggesting secondary pathology","Associated abnormal or heavy uterine bleeding","Deep dyspareunia, dyschezia, or cyclical bowel or bladder symptoms suggesting endometriosis","Fever, abnormal discharge, or pelvic tenderness suggesting infection","Failure to respond to first-line therapy, warranting further evaluation"],"prognosis":"Primary dysmenorrhoea is benign and commonly improves over time and after childbirth, with most women achieving good symptom control from anti-inflammatory and hormonal treatment. The prognosis of secondary dysmenorrhoea depends on the underlying condition, and outcomes improve when that cause is correctly identified and managed.","pitfalls":["Assuming all menstrual pain is primary and missing endometriosis or adenomyosis","Failing to reassess when standard treatment does not work","Overlooking pelvic inflammatory disease as a treatable secondary cause","Not offering hormonal suppression alongside anti-inflammatory therapy","Attributing new-onset severe pain in an older woman to simple period pain"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"dystonia":{"name":"Dystonia","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dystonic syndrome","torsion dystonia","focal dystonia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by sustained or intermittent involuntary muscle contractions producing abnormal, often twisting or patterned postures and repetitive movements","Movements are typically patterned and directional, may be action-specific (such as writer's cramp), and often worsen with fatigue or stress and lessen with rest or sleep","A characteristic feature is the sensory trick (geste antagoniste), where a light touch to the affected region temporarily reduces the dystonic posture","Classified by body distribution (focal, segmental, multifocal, hemidystonia, or generalised) and by age of onset and cause","Focal forms such as cervical dystonia (torticollis), blepharospasm, and laryngeal dystonia are the most common adult presentations","Botulinum toxin injection is a mainstay for focal and segmental dystonia, while oral agents and deep brain stimulation are options for more widespread disease","Young-onset generalised dystonia may be genetic (for example DYT1) and warrants a therapeutic trial of levodopa to exclude dopa-responsive dystonia","Secondary dystonia should be considered when there are additional neurological signs, a fixed posture, or hemidystonia pointing to a structural lesion"],"pathophysiology":"Dystonia arises from dysfunction of motor networks centred on the basal ganglia and their connections with the cerebellum, thalamus, and cortex, leading to loss of inhibition, abnormal sensorimotor integration, and maladaptive plasticity. Co-contraction of agonist and antagonist muscles with overflow to adjacent muscles produces the abnormal postures. Causes range from inherited (isolated or combined genetic forms) and idiopathic disease to acquired lesions, drugs, and neurodegenerative or metabolic disorders.","additionalDifferentials":["Spasticity and other causes of abnormal posturing","Parkinson disease and atypical parkinsonism with dystonic features","Tardive and acute drug-induced dystonia","Wilson disease","Functional (psychogenic) dystonia","Stiff person syndrome and orthopaedic causes of fixed posture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain, particularly for hemidystonia or when a secondary structural cause is suspected","A trial of levodopa in young-onset generalised dystonia to identify dopa-responsive dystonia","Serum copper and ceruloplasmin and slit-lamp examination to exclude Wilson disease in younger patients","Genetic testing for hereditary dystonias where indicated","Medication review for dopamine-blocking and other offending drugs"],"redFlags":["Acute severe dystonic reaction, especially involving the larynx or airway, requiring urgent treatment","Hemidystonia or fixed dystonia with other focal signs suggesting a structural lesion needing imaging","Young onset with hepatic or psychiatric features suggesting Wilson disease","Status dystonicus with continuous severe spasms causing rhabdomyolysis or respiratory compromise"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies with cause and distribution. Focal dystonias are usually chronic but not life-threatening and often respond well to botulinum toxin. Dopa-responsive dystonia can improve dramatically with treatment. Young-onset genetic generalised dystonia tends to spread and be more disabling, though deep brain stimulation can help selected patients.","pitfalls":["Failing to trial levodopa in young-onset dystonia and missing highly treatable dopa-responsive dystonia","Overlooking Wilson disease, a treatable cause, in a young patient","Not recognising drug-induced dystonia and continuing the offending dopamine-blocking agent","Mislabelling dystonia as functional, or vice versa, without adequate assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"eclampsia":{"name":"Eclampsia","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["eclamptic seizure","eclamptic convulsion","convulsive pre-eclampsia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined as new-onset generalised tonic-clonic seizures in a woman with pre-eclampsia that cannot be attributed to another cause","Seizures may occur antepartum, intrapartum, or postpartum, and a substantial proportion arise after delivery","Premonitory features include severe headache, visual disturbance, epigastric pain, and hyperreflexia, though seizures can occur without warning","Magnesium sulfate is the treatment of choice for controlling and preventing recurrent seizures and is superior to conventional anticonvulsants in this setting","Immediate priorities are maternal airway protection, oxygenation, and prevention of injury and aspiration during the seizure","Blood pressure must be controlled with antihypertensives to reduce the risk of intracranial haemorrhage","Delivery is undertaken once the mother is stabilised; eclampsia itself is not an indication for immediate caesarean if vaginal delivery is achievable and conditions are favourable","Magnesium toxicity is monitored clinically (loss of deep tendon reflexes, respiratory depression) and calcium gluconate is the antidote"],"pathophysiology":"Eclampsia represents the cerebral manifestation of the endothelial dysfunction and vasospasm of pre-eclampsia. Loss of cerebral autoregulation and endothelial injury lead to hyperperfusion, vasogenic and cytotoxic oedema (often in the posterior circulation, akin to posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome), and focal ischaemia, which lower the seizure threshold and provoke generalised convulsions. Cerebral haemorrhage is the most feared and lethal complication.","additionalDifferentials":["Epilepsy and other primary seizure disorders","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis","Intracranial haemorrhage or ischaemic stroke","Hypoglycaemia or other metabolic derangement","CNS infection (meningitis, encephalitis)","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bloods as for severe pre-eclampsia: full blood count, liver enzymes, renal function, coagulation, and lactate dehydrogenase","Blood glucose to exclude hypoglycaemia","Neuroimaging (CT or MRI) if seizures are atypical, focal, recurrent despite magnesium, or if focal neurological deficit persists","Continuous fetal monitoring after maternal stabilisation","Magnesium level monitoring where clinically indicated during infusion"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or prolonged seizures despite magnesium sulfate (status eclampticus)","Focal neurological deficit or reduced conscious level suggesting intracranial haemorrhage","Severe uncontrolled hypertension threatening stroke","Signs of magnesium toxicity such as respiratory depression or absent reflexes","Maternal hypoxia, aspiration, or airway compromise during a seizure"],"prognosis":"With prompt seizure control, blood pressure management, and delivery, most women recover without lasting neurological deficit. However, eclampsia is associated with significant maternal mortality and morbidity worldwide, particularly from intracranial haemorrhage, aspiration, and organ failure, and with increased perinatal loss.","pitfalls":["Attributing a peripartum seizure to epilepsy and delaying magnesium therapy","Discontinuing magnesium too early after delivery when postpartum eclampsia remains a risk","Rushing to caesarean during an active seizure rather than stabilising the mother first","Overlooking magnesium toxicity by not monitoring reflexes and respiratory rate","Neglecting ongoing postpartum blood pressure surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"ectoparasite_infestations_and_arthropod_in":{"name":"Ectoparasite Infestations and Arthropod Injuries","system":"Dermatologic / Skin and Soft Tissue (with neurologic and systemic envenomation involvement)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Scabies","Pediculosis","Pthiriasis","Lice infestation","Myiasis","Tungiasis","Chigger bites","Tick bites and tick paralysis","Spider bites","Scorpion stings","Hymenoptera stings","Pentastomiasis","Delusional infestation"],"chapter":472,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3728","clinicalPearls":["Of all organisms in this chapter, only the human itch mite (Sarcoptes scabiei var. hominis) and human-infesting lice are obligate human parasites; humans are accidental or dead-end hosts for most others.","Scabies affects roughly 250 million people worldwide and is spread mainly by direct skin-to-skin contact; transmission via shared bedding or clothing is less common than widely assumed, and mites usually die within about a day off a suitable host.","Scabies pruritus reflects a sensitization reaction; an initial infestation can stay asymptomatic for up to about 6 weeks, but reinfestation provokes prompt hypersensitivity. Itch typically worsens at night and after hot showers.","Crusted (formerly Norwegian) scabies arises in glucocorticoid use, immunodeficiency (including HIV/AIDS), and neuropsychiatric conditions that blunt itching/scratching; it harbors thousands of mites, is highly contagious, often non-pruritic, lacks classic burrows, and can mimic psoriasis.","Confirmation of louse infestation should rest on finding a live louse, not on nits alone, since hatched/dead eggs (and pseudo-nits of debris/dander/fungi) remain cemented to hair for months and are frequently misread as active disease.","Body lice (not head lice) are the recognized vectors of epidemic (louse-borne) typhus, louse-borne relapsing fever, and trench fever; outbreaks track societal upheaval, crowding, and inability to launder clothing.","Furuncular myiasis (e.g., Dermatobia hominis, Cordylobia anthropophaga) presents as a boil-like nodule with a central breathing pore that emits bubbles when submerged in water, and the patient may sense movement within the skin.","Brown recluse (Loxosceles) envenomation classically produces a painful lesion with central induration, a pale ischemic zone, and surrounding erythema, sometimes evolving to a necrotic black eschar; widow spider (Latrodectus) bites cause minimal local injury but severe neurotoxic muscle cramps via alpha-latrotoxin.","Removing hard ticks within the first 24 hours of attachment generally prevents transmission of Lyme disease, babesiosis, anaplasmosis, and ehrlichiosis, though tick-borne viruses may transmit faster.","Tick paralysis is an acute ascending flaccid Guillain-Barre-like paralysis from a salivary toxin; sensory exam and lumbar puncture are typically normal, and finding/removing the hidden tick (often under scalp hair) reverses it within hours.","Alpha-gal syndrome (red-meat allergy) follows tick bites that induce IgE against galactose-alpha-1,3-galactose, a carbohydrate present in non-primate mammalian muscle; in the US it is linked to the lone star tick.","Most reported 'spider bites' are not spider-related; acute tender noduloulcerative lesions are frequently due to minor trauma or bacterial infection, particularly MRSA."],"pathophysiology":"Ectoparasites are arthropods and organisms of other phyla that infest skin or hair; they may remain superficial (anchored by mouthparts or claws) or penetrate into epidermis, dermis, or subcutaneous tissue. Injury arises through several mechanisms: direct mechanical damage, consumption of blood or nutrients, hypersensitivity reactions to saliva/secretions/excretions, inoculation of toxins or venoms, transmission of pathogens, and creation of skin portals for secondary bacterial infection. In scabies, gravid female mites burrow within the stratum corneum and lay eggs; rash and pruritus result from delayed-type sensitization, with burrows surrounded by infiltrates of eosinophils, lymphocytes, and histiocytes. Lice are obligate blood feeders whose saliva produces a pruritic morbilliform/urticarial reaction in sensitized hosts. Venom-mediated injury is prominent in arachnid and hymenopteran envenomation: widow spider alpha-latrotoxin binds irreversibly to presynaptic terminals causing release then depletion of acetylcholine and norepinephrine; bark scorpion neurotoxins hold sodium channels open producing cranial-nerve and neuromuscular hyperexcitability; some scorpion venoms trigger massive catecholamine release with cardiopulmonary toxicity. Hymenopteran venoms contain amines, kinins, and enzymes (e.g., melittin, phospholipases, hyaluronidase) producing local and systemic effects, with most fatalities due to IgE-mediated type 1 anaphylaxis. Tick salivary secretions are biologically active anticoagulants that can transmit pathogens and provoke sterile inflammatory responses, fever, and toxin-mediated paralysis.","additionalDifferentials":["Psoriasis (mimicked by crusted scabies)","Eczematous dermatitis / contact dermatitis","Papular urticaria from other arthropod bites","Cutaneous small-vessel vasculitis (purpuric papules resembling chigger bites)","Cutaneous larva migrans (resembling serpiginous migratory fly-larva eruptions)","Bacterial cellulitis (mimicked by large local hymenopteran sting reactions)","Peritonitis / acute surgical abdomen (mimicked by widow spider envenomation abdominal rigidity)","Seizure (mimicked by scorpion-induced muscle twitching/jerking)","Guillain-Barre syndrome (mimicked by tick paralysis)"],"infectionMimics":["MRSA and other bacterial skin infections misattributed to spider bites","Impetigo / cellulitis as secondary infection of scabies or louse excoriations","Bacterial cellulitis confused with large local Hymenoptera sting reactions"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Delusional infestation (Ekbom syndrome / delusory parasitosis), with self-induced excoriations and ulcers and no true parasite","Pseudo-nits (debris, dry skin flakes, hair fungi) mistaken for louse eggs","Cutaneous small-vessel vasculitis","Psoriasis versus crusted scabies"],"additionalInvestigations":["Microscopic examination of skin scrapings from unroofed burrows for mites, eggs, and fecal pellets (scabies)","Skin biopsy, cellophane-tape or cyanoacrylate adhesive lift, and dermatoscopy of papulovesicular lesions","Direct identification of the offending arthropod from a collected specimen (taken from patient, traps, or environment) with proper fixation/preservation for taxonomic review","Examination of clothing seams for body lice and nits; inspection of hair for live lice","Skin tests and venom-specific RAST for Hymenoptera hypersensitivity to guide immunotherapy","PCR of captured lice has detected pathogenic bacteria in research settings"],"redFlags":["Ascending flaccid paralysis with cranial-nerve involvement after tick attachment (tick paralysis) progressing to respiratory failure if the tick is not found and removed","Rapid anaphylaxis after Hymenoptera or reduviid bite (upper-airway edema, bronchospasm, hypotension, shock) usually beginning within 10 minutes","Stings to the tongue or mouth causing life-threatening upper-airway edema","Scorpion envenomation with cranial-nerve dysfunction, neuromuscular hyperexcitability, hypertensive crisis, arrhythmia, pulmonary edema, or rhabdomyolysis","Widow spider envenomation with severe muscle cramping, respiratory distress, hypertension, or premature labor","Recluse spider envenomation with systemic hemolysis, renal failure, or expanding necrosis","Lonomia obliqua caterpillar contact causing disseminated coagulopathy and hemorrhagic shock","Halzoun/marrara (Linguatula serrata pentastomiasis) with severe pharyngeal edema requiring tracheostomy","Aeromonas hydrophila sepsis, myonecrosis, or intractable bleeding complicating medicinal leech therapy"],"prognosis":"Most infestations and bites are self-limited or readily treated. Scabies becomes noncommunicable within about a day of proper treatment, though hypersensitivity pruritus may persist for weeks as dead mites and debris are cleared; secondary streptococcal infection can lead to poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis and possibly acute rheumatic fever. Brown recluse lesions usually heal within about 3 months, and verified deaths from North American brown recluse bites have not been confirmed. Widow spider pain typically begins subsiding within the first 12 hours but may recur over days to weeks; fatal outcomes are rare and concentrated in the very young, elderly, or debilitated. Bark scorpion envenomation peaks around 5 hours and subsides over a day or two, with fatal respiratory arrest mainly in young children and the elderly. Tick paralysis generally resolves within hours to days after tick removal but can be fatal from respiratory paralysis or aspiration if untreated. Hymenoptera-related deaths (>=100/year in the US) result almost entirely from anaphylaxis.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing scabies or an 'outbreak' without confirmation; unrelated skin diseases are frequently misdiagnosed as scabies.","Treating residual nits as active louse infestation; onerous 'no-nit' school exclusion policies are increasingly viewed as unjustified and counterproductive.","Over-treating with topical scabicides/pediculicides, which can cause irritant contact dermatitis (especially repeated permethrin).","Treating environmental (non-burrowing) mites such as chiggers with acaricides on the patient, which is discouraged because these mites do not reside on humans.","Attributing necrotic skin lesions to spider bites without observation of a spider; many are bacterial (often MRSA) or traumatic.","Delaying tick removal by applying heat or occlusive substances, which merely prolongs attachment; ticks should be removed by traction with fine-tipped forceps at the mouthparts.","Mistaking widow spider abdominal rigidity for a surgical abdomen and performing unnecessary surgery (the abdomen is not tender on palpation).","Interpreting childhood eyelash (pubic louse) infestation as incontrovertible proof of sexual abuse.","Failing to recognize delusional infestation, leading to repeated futile antiparasitic treatment."],"severityClassification":"No formal numeric staging is given; severity is graded clinically by host reaction and systemic involvement. Scabies ranges from ordinary (typically <=10-15 burrows) to crusted/hyperinfested (thousands of mites, highly contagious). Arthropod envenomations are stratified from local-only reactions (most scorpion and spider bites: pain, edema) to systemic/life-threatening envenomation (cranial-nerve and neuromuscular dysfunction, cardiopulmonary toxicity, hemolysis, renal failure). Hymenoptera reactions span uncomplicated local stings, large local hypersensitivity reactions spreading >=10 cm over 1-2 days, and systemic type 1 anaphylaxis.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3728","Harrison 22e p.3729","Harrison 22e p.3730","Harrison 22e p.3731","Harrison 22e p.3732","Harrison 22e p.3733","Harrison 22e p.3734","Harrison 22e p.3735","Harrison 22e p.3736","Harrison 22e p.3737","Harrison 22e p.3738"],"crossLinks":[]},"ectopic_pregnancy":{"name":"Ectopic pregnancy","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tubal pregnancy","extrauterine pregnancy","EP"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Any woman of reproductive age with abdominal pain, amenorrhoea, and vaginal bleeding should be assumed to have an ectopic pregnancy until proven otherwise","The great majority implant in the fallopian tube; less common sites include the cornua, ovary, cervix, caesarean scar, and abdominal cavity","Risk factors include previous ectopic pregnancy, tubal surgery or damage, pelvic inflammatory disease, endometriosis, assisted reproduction, and intrauterine contraceptive devices","Diagnosis combines serial serum beta-hCG measurement with transvaginal ultrasound; an empty uterus with a beta-hCG above the discriminatory zone raises strong suspicion","Management options are expectant, medical with methotrexate for suitable stable cases, or surgical (usually laparoscopic salpingectomy or salpingotomy)","Rupture causes intraperitoneal haemorrhage and is a life-threatening surgical emergency requiring immediate resuscitation and operative control of bleeding","A pregnancy of unknown location with rising or plateauing beta-hCG and no intrauterine gestation warrants close follow-up and a low threshold for intervention","Anti-D prophylaxis should be given to rhesus-negative women where indicated"],"pathophysiology":"An ectopic pregnancy occurs when the blastocyst implants outside the endometrial cavity, most often in a fallopian tube whose transport function has been impaired by inflammation, scarring, or altered motility. The invading trophoblast erodes the tubal wall, which lacks the capacity to accommodate a growing gestation. This leads to distension and bleeding into the tube, and eventually to tubal rupture with potentially massive intraperitoneal haemorrhage, or to tubal abortion where the products are expelled through the fimbrial end.","additionalDifferentials":["Miscarriage (threatened, incomplete, or complete)","Ruptured or haemorrhagic ovarian cyst","Ovarian torsion","Acute pelvic inflammatory disease","Appendicitis","Urinary tract infection or renal colic"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial quantitative serum beta-hCG to assess trend against expected intrauterine rise","Transvaginal ultrasound to locate the pregnancy and detect free fluid","Full blood count and blood group with cross-match if bleeding suspected","Serum progesterone as an adjunct to assess pregnancy viability in some settings","Diagnostic laparoscopy when imaging is inconclusive and suspicion remains high"],"redFlags":["Haemodynamic instability, syncope, or signs of shock indicating ruptured ectopic with intraperitoneal haemorrhage","Severe or worsening abdominal pain with peritonism","Shoulder-tip pain suggesting diaphragmatic irritation from haemoperitoneum","Rapidly falling haemoglobin or free fluid on scan","Cervical, cornual, or scar-site implantation carrying high haemorrhage risk"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and treatment the maternal outlook is excellent and fertility is often preserved. Rupture remains an important cause of maternal death, especially where access to care is delayed. Women who have had one ectopic pregnancy carry an increased risk of recurrence and of subtly reduced fertility.","pitfalls":["Excluding pregnancy on history alone without a beta-hCG test","Falsely reassuring interpretation of a single beta-hCG value rather than the trend","Assuming an intrauterine pregnancy excludes ectopic, missing rare heterotopic pregnancy after assisted conception","Choosing medical management without ensuring reliable follow-up","Delaying surgery in an unstable patient while awaiting investigations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"ectropion":{"name":"Ectropion","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["outturned eyelid","everted eyelid"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The eyelid margin turns outward away from the globe, most often affecting the lower lid, leading to tearing, irritation, and a red, exposed conjunctiva","Involutional ectropion from age-related horizontal lid laxity is the commonest form","Paralytic ectropion follows facial nerve palsy with loss of orbicularis tone and is often accompanied by lagophthalmos","Cicatricial ectropion results from vertical shortening of the anterior lamella due to skin scarring, burns, or actinic damage","Chronic exposure causes conjunctival keratinisation, punctal eversion with epiphora, and risk of exposure keratopathy","Interim management centres on lubrication and protecting the ocular surface, with taping or moisture chambers for exposure","Definitive treatment is surgical and directed at the specific cause, such as lid-tightening for involutional disease or skin grafting for cicatricial cases"],"pathophysiology":"Ectropion is outward rotation of the eyelid margin. Involutional ectropion stems from horizontal lid laxity and weakening of the canthal tendons and retractors with age. Paralytic ectropion follows loss of orbicularis oculi function in facial nerve palsy, so the lid sags and everts. Cicatricial ectropion arises when scarring and contracture of the eyelid skin (anterior lamella) mechanically pull the margin away from the eye. Mechanical ectropion is caused by a mass or oedema everting the lid. Eversion disrupts the normal tear drainage and exposes the cornea and conjunctiva to drying and keratinisation.","additionalDifferentials":["Involutional versus cicatricial versus paralytic subtypes","Eyelid retraction (e.g. thyroid eye disease)","Floppy eyelid syndrome","Mechanical eversion from an eyelid or orbital mass","Punctal stenosis as an alternative cause of epiphora"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination to assess corneal exposure and conjunctival keratinisation","Fluorescein staining to detect exposure keratopathy","Assessment of lid laxity and canthal tendon integrity","Evaluation of facial nerve function in suspected paralytic ectropion","Examination of eyelid skin for scarring or actinic change in cicatricial cases"],"redFlags":["Exposure keratopathy with corneal breakdown threatening vision, particularly with associated lagophthalmos","New facial nerve palsy requiring evaluation of its cause","A causative eyelid mass needing exclusion of malignancy","Progressive cicatricial change from an underlying dermatological or systemic disorder"],"prognosis":"Surgical correction usually restores lid position and relieves symptoms with good outcomes; untreated ectropion can lead to persistent epiphora, chronic conjunctival changes, and exposure-related corneal damage.","pitfalls":["Managing epiphora without recognising the underlying lid malposition","Overlooking corneal exposure in paralytic ectropion until keratopathy develops","Failing to address anterior lamella shortening in cicatricial cases, leading to recurrence","Missing an eyelid tumour causing mechanical eversion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"electrical_injury":{"name":"Electrical injury","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["electric shock injury","electrocution","electrical burn","lightning injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The visible skin injury often grossly underestimates deep tissue damage, because current follows low-resistance structures (nerves, blood vessels, muscle) and injures tissue along its path","Alternating current is particularly dangerous as it can cause tetanic muscle contraction that prevents release from the source and prolongs exposure","Cardiac effects range from benign ECG changes to ventricular fibrillation or asystole; all significant exposures need an ECG and a period of cardiac monitoring","Deep muscle necrosis causes rhabdomyolysis and myoglobinuria, threatening acute kidney injury, so aggressive fluid resuscitation and monitoring of urine output are essential","Compartment syndrome and progressive deep tissue necrosis may develop, requiring serial examination and sometimes fasciotomy or debridement","Always look for associated trauma - falls and violent muscle contractions can cause fractures, dislocations (including posterior shoulder dislocation), and spinal injury","Lightning injury differs from high-voltage contact: it is a brief massive shock that commonly causes cardiac and respiratory arrest, transient limb paralysis (keraunoparalysis), and a characteristic feathering skin pattern","High-voltage and lightning victims warrant a low threshold for prolonged resuscitation, as recovery from apparent cardiorespiratory arrest can occur"],"pathophysiology":"Electrical injury results from the passage of current through the body, converting electrical energy to heat and causing direct cellular membrane damage (electroporation). Tissue injury depends on voltage, current type and magnitude, resistance of tissues, current pathway, and contact duration. Current traversing the thorax can disrupt cardiac rhythm, causing ventricular fibrillation or asystole, while passage through the head or chest can arrest respiration centrally or by paralysing respiratory muscles. Deep tissues, especially muscle, sustain extensive thermal and electroporation injury that may exceed the visible cutaneous burn, releasing myoglobin that can precipitate acute kidney injury, and swelling within fascial compartments can compromise perfusion.","additionalDifferentials":["Thermal burns from other causes","Primary cardiac arrest","Traumatic injury from another mechanism","Seizure with secondary injury","Crush injury with rhabdomyolysis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG and cardiac monitoring","Serum creatine kinase and urine myoglobin for rhabdomyolysis","Renal function and electrolytes including potassium","Urinalysis for pigment and monitoring of urine output","Imaging for suspected fractures, dislocations, or spinal injury"],"redFlags":["Cardiac arrhythmia, arrest, or ischaemic ECG changes","Dark pigmented urine or markedly raised creatine kinase indicating rhabdomyolysis","Signs of compartment syndrome (severe pain, tense swelling, sensory or perfusion deficit)","High-voltage or lightning exposure with deep injury along the current path","Associated major trauma such as spinal injury or fractures from falls or tetany"],"prognosis":"Low-voltage injuries without loss of consciousness or ECG change usually do well, whereas high-voltage injuries can cause extensive deep tissue loss, limb amputation, renal failure, and death; lightning strike mortality is driven mainly by immediate cardiorespiratory arrest, and survivors may have lasting neurological sequelae.","pitfalls":["Judging severity from the surface burn and missing extensive deep tissue injury","Under-resuscitating with fluids and allowing myoglobinuric renal failure to develop","Failing to screen for and monitor cardiac arrhythmia after significant exposure","Overlooking associated fractures, dislocations, or spinal injury from falls and tetanic contraction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"electrical_storm_and_incessant_ventricular":{"name":"Electrical Storm and Incessant Ventricular Tachycardia","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VT storm","Ventricular tachycardia storm","Incessant VT","Arrhythmic storm"],"chapter":263,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1972","clinicalPearls":["Electrical storm (VT/VF storm) is defined as three or more episodes of ventricular tachycardia or ventricular fibrillation within 24 hours, each requiring intervention to terminate (Harrison 22e p.1972).","It is uncommon in the general population but carries great concern because, untreated, it follows a rapid course that can lead to death; prompt recognition and intervention are essential (Harrison 22e p.1972).","Storm occurs in about 4% of patients with a primary-prevention ICD but in as many as 20% of patients with prior known VT or resuscitated sudden death (Harrison 22e p.1972).","VT is termed incessant when it recurs shortly after electrical, pharmacologic, or spontaneous conversion to sinus rhythm; it is typically monomorphic (Harrison 22e p.1972).","A slow incessant monomorphic VT may rarely escape ICD detection because its rate falls below the programmed detection threshold (Harrison 22e p.1972).","Antiarrhythmic drugs themselves can be proarrhythmic and render VT incessant (e.g., amiodarone or a sodium-channel blocker such as flecainide) (Harrison 22e p.1972).","Multiple ICD shocks constitute a medical emergency warranting activation of emergency medical services; patients should never drive themselves to hospital after receiving a shock (Harrison 22e p.1972).","After any ICD shock, device interrogation is crucial to confirm therapy was delivered for a true ventricular arrhythmia rather than for lead malfunction or an atrial arrhythmia (Harrison 22e p.1972).","Antitachycardia pacing terminates over 70% of VT episodes—even rapid VT—so appropriate ICD programming markedly reduces shocks and improves quality of life (Harrison 22e p.1972).","Programming VF detection zones above 220 beats/min in primary-prevention ICDs and using longer detection times significantly reduce unnecessary and inappropriate shocks (Harrison 22e p.1972).","Determining whether the storming rhythm is monomorphic or polymorphic guides management, as does inspecting initiation/termination patterns on stored ICD electrograms for precipitating factors (Harrison 22e p.1973).","Reducing sympathetic drive is a core management principle: stellate ganglion block and upper thoracic epidural anesthesia can restore stability in some patients (Harrison 22e p.1973)."],"pathophysiology":"Electrical storm reflects repeated, often rapidly recurring ventricular tachyarrhythmias arising from an unstable myocardial substrate combined with heightened sympathetic drive and acute triggering factors. Substrates vary: in the setting of acute myocardial ischemia/infarction, surviving Purkinje cells within infarcted myocardium can show triggered automaticity, generating recurrent polymorphic VT/VF. PVC-initiated polymorphic VT/VF can also occur without infarction or ischemia (idiopathic VF), where triggering premature ventricular contractions arise from fascicular tissue or papillary muscles, sometimes from scarred myocardium seen on cardiac MRI. Other substrates include acquired or congenital long QT (torsades des pointes from abnormal repolarization), Brugada syndrome (right ventricular outflow tract/epicardial substrate), and inflammatory cardiomyopathies. Incessant VT is usually monomorphic and may be driven or sustained by the proarrhythmic action of antiarrhythmic drugs (Harrison 22e p.1972-1974).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute coronary syndrome / myocardial ischemia precipitating polymorphic VT or VF","Acquired or congenital long QT syndrome with torsades des pointes","Brugada syndrome","Inflammatory cardiomyopathy (giant cell myocarditis, cardiac sarcoidosis, viral myocarditis)","Idiopathic PVC-initiated polymorphic VT/VF from fascicular tissue or papillary muscles","Proarrhythmia from antiarrhythmic therapy"],"infectionMimics":["Viral myocarditis presenting with VT/VF storm"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lead malfunction or atrial arrhythmia causing inappropriate ICD shocks rather than true ventricular arrhythmia","Electrolyte disturbance provoking ventricular arrhythmia"],"additionalInvestigations":["ICD interrogation to confirm appropriate ventricular detection/therapy and to review stored electrograms for arrhythmia onset and triggers","ECG monitoring to classify the rhythm as monomorphic versus polymorphic and to assess the QT interval and Brugada pattern (Rsr' with ST elevation in V1/V2)","Serum electrolyte assessment, especially potassium and magnesium","Evaluation for ischemia / acute coronary syndrome and cardiac function","Cardiac MRI to detect scarred myocardium when PVC-initiated polymorphic VT/VF is suspected","Endomyocardial biopsy when a new inflammatory cardiomyopathy is suspected in a patient without prior cardiac disease"],"redFlags":["Multiple ICD shocks (a medical emergency)","Polymorphic VT or VF, especially if accompanied by features of acute coronary syndrome","Hemodynamically unstable or recurrent ventricular arrhythmia requiring repeated cardioversion","Signs of gradual cardiac decompensation in a hemodynamically stable but incessant VT","New-onset frequent ventricular arrhythmia in a patient with no known prior cardiac disease (consider inflammatory myocarditis)"],"prognosis":"Spontaneous ventricular arrhythmias—particularly those requiring shock for termination—are associated with an increased subsequent risk of death and hospitalization in patients with depressed ventricular function. Untreated storm progresses rapidly and can be fatal, but catheter ablation, antiarrhythmic drug therapy, and adjunctive measures can be life-saving. An arrhythmic episode should prompt reevaluation for declining cardiac function, ischemia, or intercurrent illness (Harrison 22e p.1972).","pitfalls":["Assuming every ICD shock reflects a true ventricular arrhythmia—lead malfunction or an atrial arrhythmia can trigger inappropriate shocks, so interrogation is required (Harrison 22e p.1972).","Overlooking that interventions to control the arrhythmia may themselves worsen outcome—most antiarrhythmics can cause bradycardia requiring ICD pacing, which can itself impair ventricular function (Harrison 22e p.1972).","Failing to recognize antiarrhythmic proarrhythmia as a cause of incessant VT (Harrison 22e p.1972).","Missing a slow incessant monomorphic VT that falls outside programmed ICD detection parameters (Harrison 22e p.1972).","Not searching for a treatable substrate (ischemia, long QT, Brugada, inflammatory cardiomyopathy) that dictates specific therapy (Harrison 22e p.1974)."],"severityClassification":"Defined event: electrical storm = >=3 episodes of VT or VF within 24 h each requiring intervention to terminate. VT is classified as monomorphic versus polymorphic, and as sustained versus incessant; substrate-based categories include ischemic, long QT, Brugada, PVC-initiated, and inflammatory (Harrison 22e p.1972-1974).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1972","Harrison 22e p.1973","Harrison 22e p.1974"],"crossLinks":[]},"endometrial_cancer":{"name":"Endometrial Cancer","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["carcinoma of the endometrium","endometrial carcinoma","uterine cancer","cancer of the uterine corpus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Postmenopausal bleeding is the cardinal presenting symptom and must be investigated to exclude malignancy in every woman","The commonest type is endometrioid adenocarcinoma, which is oestrogen-driven and associated with obesity, nulliparity, late menopause, and unopposed oestrogen exposure","Serous and clear cell carcinomas are less common, oestrogen-independent, tend to occur in older women, and behave more aggressively","Endometrial thickness on transvaginal ultrasound helps triage postmenopausal bleeding, but a normal measurement does not exclude the aggressive non-endometrioid subtypes","Diagnosis rests on endometrial sampling by pipelle biopsy or hysteroscopy with directed biopsy, particularly when bleeding persists despite a thin endometrium","Most cases are diagnosed early because bleeding prompts presentation, and total hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy is the mainstay of treatment","Lynch syndrome should be considered, especially in younger women, and molecular classification increasingly guides adjuvant therapy and prognosis","Tamoxifen use, polycystic ovary syndrome, and diabetes are additional risk factors reflecting chronic unopposed oestrogen and metabolic influences"],"pathophysiology":"Type 1 (endometrioid) tumours develop through prolonged oestrogenic stimulation of the endometrium unopposed by progesterone, progressing through endometrial hyperplasia with atypia to invasive carcinoma; they commonly harbour PTEN, PIK3CA, and mismatch repair defects. Type 2 (serous and clear cell) tumours arise in atrophic endometrium independent of oestrogen, frequently carry TP53 mutations, and are more likely to invade deeply and metastasise early. Spread occurs by myometrial invasion, transtubal and peritoneal dissemination, and lymphatic routes to pelvic and para-aortic nodes.","additionalDifferentials":["Endometrial hyperplasia with or without atypia","Endometrial or endocervical polyp","Atrophic vaginitis or endometritis","Cervical carcinoma","Submucosal fibroid causing abnormal bleeding","Oestrogen-secreting ovarian tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal ultrasound to measure endometrial thickness and assess the myometrium","Endometrial biopsy (pipelle) or hysteroscopy with directed sampling","Histopathology with grade, subtype and molecular classification","Pelvic MRI to assess depth of myometrial invasion and cervical involvement","CT chest, abdomen and pelvis for staging in high-risk histology","Mismatch repair immunohistochemistry and testing for Lynch syndrome"],"redFlags":["Postmenopausal bleeding, which requires prompt investigation to exclude cancer","Recurrent or persistent bleeding despite a reassuring initial ultrasound","Aggressive serous or clear cell histology with deep myometrial invasion","Evidence of nodal or distant metastatic spread on imaging","Pyometra or haematometra in an elderly woman suggesting obstructing tumour"],"prognosis":"The majority of women present with disease confined to the uterus and have an excellent prognosis after surgery, but outcome worsens with deep myometrial invasion, high tumour grade, aggressive histological subtypes, and nodal or distant spread. Molecular subgroups, notably POLE-mutated tumours with favourable and p53-abnormal tumours with adverse outcomes, are refining prognostic estimates and adjuvant treatment decisions.","pitfalls":["Failing to investigate postmenopausal bleeding because it is light or intermittent","Relying on a thin endometrial stripe to exclude aggressive non-endometrioid tumours","Attributing bleeding to atrophy or polyps without histological confirmation","Missing Lynch syndrome by not testing younger patients or those with a suggestive family history","Overlooking an oestrogen-secreting ovarian tumour as an underlying cause"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"endometriosis":{"name":"Endometriosis","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["endometriotic disease","pelvic endometriosis","endometrioma (ovarian)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Endometriosis is the presence of endometrial-like tissue outside the uterine cavity, most often on the ovaries, uterosacral ligaments, and pelvic peritoneum","Cardinal symptoms are cyclical and chronic pelvic pain, dysmenorrhoea, deep dyspareunia, and subfertility, though some women are asymptomatic","Symptom severity correlates poorly with the anatomical extent of disease","Ovarian endometriomas (chocolate cysts) contain altered, dark, degenerated blood","Definitive diagnosis has traditionally required laparoscopic visualisation, ideally with histological confirmation, though imaging can identify deep disease and endometriomas","Medical treatment suppresses cyclical ovarian activity to reduce pain, using hormonal therapies that induce a hypo-oestrogenic or progestogen-dominant state","Surgical management aims to excise or ablate deposits and restore anatomy, and can improve both pain and fertility","There is a recognised diagnostic delay of years, so a high index of suspicion in women with chronic cyclical pelvic pain is important"],"pathophysiology":"The most widely cited mechanism is retrograde menstruation, in which viable endometrial cells reflux through the fallopian tubes and implant on peritoneal surfaces. Additional contributions come from coelomic metaplasia, altered immune surveillance that fails to clear ectopic cells, lymphatic or vascular spread, and genetic predisposition. The ectopic tissue is oestrogen-dependent and responds to cyclical hormonal changes, causing local inflammation, repeated microhaemorrhage, fibrosis, adhesion formation, and distortion of pelvic anatomy. Chronic inflammation and nerve involvement generate pain and contribute to subfertility.","additionalDifferentials":["Pelvic inflammatory disease and chronic pelvic infection","Irritable bowel syndrome and other gastrointestinal disorders","Interstitial cystitis or painful bladder syndrome","Ovarian cysts or masses of other aetiology","Adenomyosis","Musculoskeletal or myofascial pelvic pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal ultrasound to detect endometriomas and signs of deep infiltrating disease","Pelvic MRI for mapping deep infiltrating endometriosis before surgery","Diagnostic laparoscopy with biopsy for definitive diagnosis and staging","Assessment of ovarian reserve and semen analysis when subfertility is a concern","Investigations to exclude bowel or bladder involvement when symptoms suggest it"],"redFlags":["Cyclical bowel or bladder symptoms such as haematochezia or haematuria suggesting deep infiltrating disease","Ureteric involvement threatening silent renal obstruction and loss of kidney function","An enlarging or complex ovarian mass requiring exclusion of malignancy","Acute severe pain from a ruptured or torted endometrioma","Bowel obstruction from extensive adhesive or infiltrating disease"],"prognosis":"Endometriosis is a chronic, often relapsing condition. Pain and fertility frequently improve with medical or surgical treatment, but recurrence after stopping hormonal suppression or following surgery is common. Many affected women conceive spontaneously or with assisted reproduction, and symptoms typically regress after the menopause.","pitfalls":["Dismissing severe dysmenorrhoea as normal and delaying diagnosis","Assuming minimal imaging findings exclude significant disease","Overlooking ureteric or bowel involvement in deep infiltrating disease","Relying on symptom severity to gauge disease extent","Neglecting the long-term and psychological impact and the need for a multidisciplinary approach"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"endophthalmitis":{"name":"Endophthalmitis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["intraocular infection","postoperative endophthalmitis","endogenous endophthalmitis","bacterial endophthalmitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A sight-threatening ophthalmic emergency involving suppurative inflammation of the intraocular cavities that requires immediate specialist referral and intervention","Presents with rapidly worsening vision, increasing pain, redness, lid swelling, and often a hypopyon in the anterior chamber","Most commonly occurs after intraocular surgery (especially cataract surgery), intravitreal injection, or penetrating trauma; endogenous cases arise from bloodborne spread of infection","Acute postoperative cases are frequently caused by coagulase-negative staphylococci; more virulent organisms and trauma-related cases (including Bacillus) cause fulminant disease","Loss of the red reflex and vitritis on examination, with a fibrinous anterior chamber reaction, point to intraocular infection","Fungal and endogenous causes should be considered in immunocompromised patients, intravenous drug users, and those with indwelling lines or systemic sepsis","Management centres on urgent vitreous and aqueous sampling with intravitreal antibiotics; vitrectomy is considered in severe cases with very poor vision","A low threshold for suspicion after any intraocular procedure is essential because delay markedly worsens outcome"],"pathophysiology":"Endophthalmitis is infection and severe inflammation of the intraocular contents, involving the aqueous and vitreous. Organisms gain access exogenously through a surgical wound, injection, or penetrating injury, or endogenously by haematogenous seeding from a distant source. Microbial proliferation within the relatively immune-privileged vitreous triggers an intense inflammatory response; bacterial toxins and inflammatory mediators cause rapid retinal necrosis and irreversible photoreceptor damage, which accounts for the poor prognosis when treatment is delayed.","additionalDifferentials":["Severe anterior uveitis with hypopyon (e.g. HLA-B27 associated)","Toxic anterior segment syndrome after surgery","Retained lens fragments or postoperative inflammation","Panuveitis from non-infective causes","Orbital cellulitis","Intraocular tumour with pseudohypopyon"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent aqueous and vitreous sampling for Gram stain, culture, and PCR before or at the time of intravitreal treatment","B-scan ultrasonography to assess vitreous involvement when the fundus view is obscured","Blood cultures and a search for a systemic source in suspected endogenous disease","Consideration of fungal cultures and specific stains in at-risk patients"],"redFlags":["Rapidly declining vision with pain and hypopyon after intraocular surgery, injection, or trauma demands immediate referral and treatment","Loss of the red reflex and dense vitritis indicating established intraocular infection","Signs of endogenous spread with systemic sepsis or fever","Fulminant progression suggesting a virulent organism such as Bacillus or a fungus"],"prognosis":"Visual outcome depends heavily on the causative organism and the speed of treatment; low-virulence coagulase-negative staphylococcal cases treated promptly can recover useful vision, whereas virulent bacterial, fungal, or trauma-related infection often results in severe, permanent visual loss despite aggressive therapy.","pitfalls":["Attributing early postoperative pain and inflammation to routine recovery and delaying referral","Failing to obtain intraocular samples before starting treatment, losing microbiological diagnosis","Overlooking an endogenous source in a febrile, immunocompromised, or drug-using patient","Not considering fungal infection in indolent or treatment-resistant cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"enterococcal_infections":{"name":"Enterococcal Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases (multisystem)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Enterococcus faecalis infection","Enterococcus faecium infection","VRE infection","Vancomycin-resistant enterococcal infection"],"chapter":154,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1215","clinicalPearls":["Two species cause the overwhelming majority of human enterococcal disease: E. faecalis (still predominant in nosocomial isolates) and E. faecium (now roughly one-third of species-identified infections and the far more resistant, harder-to-treat species).","Enterococci are gram-positive cocci appearing as single cells, diplococci, or short chains; they were historically grouped with streptococci but reclassified as a distinct genus on the basis of DNA hybridization and 16S rRNA sequencing.","Distinguishing laboratory features: hydrolysis of esculin in 40% bile salts, growth at 6.5% salt and at 46 degrees C, and PYR hydrolysis (the latter helping separate enterococci from the S. gallolyticus/S. bovis group and Leuconostoc).","Urinary tract infection is the most common enterococcal infection and is typically nosocomial, linked to indwelling catheters, instrumentation, or genitourinary anatomic abnormalities; removal of the catheter alone may eradicate the organism without antibiotics.","Enterococci rank second after staphylococci as agents of central line-associated bacteremia in the United States and are the third most common overall isolates from hospital-associated infections (after E. coli and staphylococci).","Enterococci are an important cause of community- and healthcare-associated endocarditis (second after staphylococci in healthcare-associated cases), with the gut or genitourinary tract the presumed bacteremic source; E. faecalis is the leading cause of endocarditis after transcatheter aortic valve implantation (TAVI).","Typical enterococcal endocarditis is subacute (fever, weight loss, malaise, murmur) in elderly debilitated men; classic peripheral stigmata such as petechiae, Osler's nodes, and Roth's spots occur in only a minority.","VRE gut colonization is the critical step preceding invasive enterococcal disease; colonized patients can carry the organism for prolonged periods (sometimes more than a year) and are more likely to develop Enterococcus-related illness such as bacteremia.","Two meta-analyses found that VRE infection independently increases the risk of death compared with infection by a glycopeptide-susceptible strain, irrespective of the patient's clinical status.","E. faecium isolation from blood is associated with worse outcomes and higher mortality than other enterococcal species, largely reflecting more frequent vancomycin and ampicillin resistance and consequently fewer therapeutic options.","Enterococci are normal large-bowel commensals (usually less than 1% of the gut microbiota); antibiotics that are biliary-excreted and broad-spectrum (e.g., certain cephalosporins and anti-anaerobic agents) promote heavy gut overgrowth, the key precursor to infection.","Enterococcal cytolysin (a heterodimeric toxin of some E. faecalis strains) can lyse human and equine but not ovine RBCs as well as neutrophils and macrophages, which is why routine ovine/bovine RBC agar usually reports these strains as nonhemolytic."],"pathophysiology":"Enterococci are intestinal symbionts that become pathogens when host barriers and colonization resistance are disrupted, most often in hospitalized or chronically ill patients. A higher level of gut colonization is the pivotal step in pathogenesis: suppression of gastric acid, degradation of the protective mucin layer, altered carbon-source availability (e.g., lactose favoring outgrowth, depletion of fructose by Olsenella reducing VRE burden), and especially antibiotic exposure all increase enteric burden. Antibiotic disruption of commensal gram-negative bacteria reduces lipopolysaccharide-driven, NF-kappaB/Toll-like-receptor-mediated production of the antimicrobial lectin RegIIIgamma and of bacteriocins/lantibiotics from organisms such as Blautia producta, impairing clearance of VRE. Three groups of virulence factors are described: (1) secreted factors including hemolysin/cytolysin, the gelatinase and serine proteases (which degrade host tissue and immune components), and a newly described pore-forming toxin Epx targeting HLA class 1; (2) surface components/adhesins (aggregation substance, the collagen adhesins Ace/Acm, pili, Esp, and other MSCRAMM-like proteins) mediating attachment to collagen, fibronectin, and fibrinogen and to biofilm/host tissue; and (3) less-characterized factors such as the stress protein Gls24 (bile-salt resistance, endocarditis), hylEfm-containing transferable plasmids that enhance gut colonization, and the oxidative-stress regulator AsrR. From colonized gut, prostate, intravascular devices, biliary/intraabdominal foci, wounds, or the urinary tract, enterococci enter the bloodstream and can seed heart valves, meninges, bone, and other sites. Genomic plasticity (recombination of core chromosomal DNA plus a malleable accessory genome of plasmids, phages, and mobile elements) drives rising resistance, concentrated in hospital-associated E. faecium clade A, which frequently lacks a functional CRISPR-Cas defense and carries high-expressing PBP5 alleles.","additionalDifferentials":["Streptococcal infection (especially viridans streptococci and the S. gallolyticus/S. bovis group) presenting as bacteremia or subacute endocarditis","Staphylococcal device-associated bacteremia and endocarditis","Polymicrobial intraabdominal or pelvic infection with enteric gram-negative bacilli and anaerobes","Coagulase-negative staphylococci or diphtheroids as causes of indolent prosthetic-device infection"],"infectionMimics":["Viridans streptococcal subacute endocarditis (similar indolent fever, malaise, murmur)","S. gallolyticus (S. bovis) bacteremia/endocarditis associated with colonic pathology","Coagulase-negative staphylococcal catheter-associated or prosthetic-valve infection","Other catheter-associated bloodstream infections producing fever in a hospitalized patient"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Asymptomatic bacteriuria/colonization in a chronically catheterized patient (versus true UTI)","Enterococcal isolation from intraabdominal or surgical-wound specimens representing colonization rather than true infection","Noninfectious causes of fever and inflammation in the critically ill (e.g., postoperative inflammation)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cultures (enterococcal bacteremia is frequently device- or gut-related and may be polymicrobial when the GI tract is the source)","Quantitative urine culture (>=10^5 CFU/mL) with urinary leukocytes plus systemic or local signs to distinguish UTI from colonization; consider a catheterized specimen to confirm","Echocardiography to evaluate for endocarditis; the DENOVA clinical scoring system can help decide which bacteremic patients need echo","CSF analysis in suspected meningitis: neutrophil-predominant pleocytosis (average ~500/microL), protein usually >100 mg/dL, glucose decreased (average ~28 mg/dL); Gram's stain positive in about half, with high CSF culture yield","Species identification (E. faecalis vs E. faecium) and susceptibility testing including ampicillin, vancomycin, and high-level aminoglycoside resistance","Detection of vanA/vanB genes to confirm vancomycin resistance","Imaging for deep foci (perinephric/splenic/lung/liver/skin abscesses, vertebral osteomyelitis/spondylodiscitis) when bacteremia is persistent or recurrent"],"redFlags":["Persistent or recurrent enterococcal bacteremia, suggesting endocarditis or an undrained deep focus (e.g., chronic prostatitis, abscess)","New or changing cardiac murmur with heart failure in enterococcal bacteremia, signaling endocarditis with valve involvement","E. faecium or VRE bloodstream isolate, predicting higher mortality and limited treatment options","Meningitis after neurosurgery, shunt, CNS trauma, or CSF leak with neutrophilic CSF pleocytosis and low glucose","Enterococcal bacteremia or meningitis in an immunocompromised patient, which has been linked to Strongyloides stercoralis hyperinfection syndrome","Neonatal enterococcal sepsis (often late-onset), especially with prematurity, low birth weight, indwelling devices, or recent abdominal surgery","Infection of prosthetic material/hardware where biofilm reduces antimicrobial efficacy and hardware removal may be required"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on species, resistance, and host comorbidity. Patients with enterococcal bacteremia are typically chronically ill, long-hospitalized, and previously treated with multiple antibiotics. E. faecium bacteremia carries worse outcomes and higher mortality than other species, largely because of widespread vancomycin and ampicillin resistance; VRE infection independently raises mortality relative to glycopeptide-susceptible strains. For E. faecalis endocarditis, relapse risk is higher with antibiotics alone (about 2-11%) than after surgery (0-3%) and may occur up to a year after treatment. Heart failure is a common endocarditis complication, and valve replacement may be critical for cure when multidrug-resistant organisms or major complications are present. After hospital discharge, gut VRE carriage clears at a median of 2-4 months in patients without ongoing risk factors (continued antibiotics, long-term care residence, hemodialysis).","pitfalls":["Treating enterococcal growth from urine, intraabdominal samples, or surgical wounds as true infection when it often represents colonization, particularly in chronically catheterized or critically ill patients.","Failing to recognize that cephalosporins lack reliable anti-enterococcal activity, so empiric cephalosporin-based regimens leave enterococci uncovered.","Misclassifying E. faecalis as nonhemolytic when some strains carry an acquired hemolysin/cytolysin that lyses human/equine/rabbit RBCs (not the ovine/bovine RBCs in standard plates).","Confusing enterococci with the S. gallolyticus (S. bovis) group or Leuconostoc; PYR hydrolysis aids differentiation.","Underestimating chronic enterococcal prostatitis as an occult source of recurrent bacteremia, especially after urologic manipulation, where the most active agents penetrate prostatic tissue poorly.","Overlooking the markedly different resistance profiles and management implications of E. faecium versus E. faecalis.","Not pursuing echocardiography in enterococcal bacteremia where endocarditis risk is real (DENOVA scoring can guide this decision)."],"severityClassification":"No single formal severity grading scale for enterococcal infection is given. Severity is stratified clinically by syndrome and resistance: localized/device-related infections (e.g., catheter-associated UTI or bacteremia, often resolved by source removal) are least severe, whereas endovascular infection (endocarditis), meningitis, and multidrug-resistant E. faecium/VRE bloodstream infection represent severe disease. High APACHE (Acute Physiology and Chronic Health Evaluation) scores are noted as a risk factor associated with VRE colonization and persistence. Endocarditis severity is influenced by heart failure, multidrug resistance, and the need for valve replacement.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1215","Harrison 22e p.1216","Harrison 22e p.1217","Harrison 22e p.1218","Harrison 22e p.1219"],"crossLinks":[]},"enterovirus_parechovirus_and_reovirus_infe":{"name":"Enterovirus, Parechovirus, and Reovirus Infections","system":"Multisystem / Infectious Disease (neurologic, cardiac, mucocutaneous)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Nonpolio enterovirus infection","Coxsackievirus infection","Echovirus infection","Poliovirus infection / poliomyelitis","Human parechovirus (HPeV) infection","Reovirus infection","Summer grippe","Hand-foot-and-mouth disease","Herpangina","Bornholm disease (pleurodynia)"],"chapter":210,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1632-1638","clinicalPearls":["Enteroviruses are Picornaviridae with single-stranded RNA and a non-enveloped icosahedral capsid; being non-enveloped they are acid-stable (survive the stomach), resist alcohol and detergent disinfectants, persist for days at room temperature, but are susceptible to chlorine-containing cleansers.","More than 50% of nonpolio enterovirus infections and over 90% of poliovirus infections are subclinical; when symptomatic, the most common picture is a nonspecific febrile illness ('summer grippe'), and in temperate climates disease clusters in summer and fall.","In children and young adults enteroviruses account for up to 90% of aseptic meningitis cases with an identifiable agent; CSF shows pleocytosis that shifts from neutrophil to lymphocyte predominance within a day, total cell count usually under 1000/uL, normal glucose, and normal-to-slightly-elevated protein.","A normal CSF glucose in enteroviral meningitis helps distinguish it from mumps meningitis, in which CSF glucose is low; enteroviral meningitis tends to be more severe in adults than children but neurologic sequelae are rare.","Hand-foot-and-mouth disease (most often coxsackievirus A16 or enterovirus 71) is highly infectious with attack rates near 100% in young children; coxsackievirus A6 causes a more extensive atypical form, and a B16/EV71 epidemic in Taiwan (1998) and yearly EV71 epidemics in China since 2008 produced brainstem encephalitis, pulmonary edema/hemorrhage, and deaths, mostly in children 5 years or younger.","Herpangina (usually coxsackievirus A) produces grayish-white papulovesicular ulcerating lesions concentrated in the posterior mouth (soft palate, anterior tonsillar pillars, uvula) and, unlike herpetic stomatitis, is NOT associated with gingivitis.","Pleurodynia (Bornholm disease), usually coxsackievirus B, causes paroxysms of knifelike pleuritic chest or upper-abdominal pain lasting 15-30 minutes with tender muscles and possible pleural rub; WBC and chest x-ray are typically normal.","Enteroviruses cause up to one-third of acute myocarditis cases; patients (over two-thirds male, often neonates/adolescents/young adults) present after a respiratory prodrome with chest pain, arrhythmia, ST/T-wave changes and elevated cardiac enzymes; up to 10% progress to dilated cardiomyopathy.","Acute hemorrhagic conjunctivitis (enterovirus 70, coxsackievirus A24) presents with sudden severe eye pain, subconjunctival hemorrhage, chemosis, and preauricular adenopathy; its abrupt onset and short course (recovery in ~10 days) distinguish it from adenoviral or chlamydial eye disease, and EV70 cases have been linked to paralysis during epidemics.","Humoral immunity is critical: patients with agamma-/hypogammaglobulinemia can develop chronic enteroviral meningoencephalitis (often echovirus 11) with a dermatomyositis-like syndrome and chronic hepatitis, and may relapse even while on immunoglobulin; hypogammaglobulinemic patients can shed poliovirus for more than 20 years.","Human parechoviruses (HPeV, also Picornaviridae) mimic enteroviral disease (neonatal generalized disease, aseptic meningitis, encephalitis, seizures, hepatitis, rash) but pan-enterovirus PCR does NOT detect HPeV, so a dedicated PCR is required; infant deaths have been associated with HPeV-1, HPeV-3, and HPeV-6.","Reoviruses are double-stranded RNA viruses; most childhood infection is asymptomatic or causes mild upper respiratory symptoms, and reovirus is only a rare cause of mild gastroenteritis or meningitis, with speculative (unproven) links of reovirus type 3 to neonatal hepatitis and biliary atresia."],"pathophysiology":"Enteroviruses multiply in the gastrointestinal tract. Based largely on poliovirus studies, after ingestion the virus infects mucosal epithelium and replicates in submucosal lymphoid tissue (tonsils, Peyer's patches), spreads to regional lymph nodes and into a viremic phase with replication in reticuloendothelial organs; a secondary viremia may seed other tissues and cause symptomatic disease. CNS entry is thought to occur via the bloodstream (viremia precedes neurologic disease), but evidence also supports direct neural spread: the poliovirus receptor is an immunoglobulin-superfamily member expressed at the neuromuscular end-plate, and animal/transgenic-mouse studies show that cutting the sciatic nerve prevents spinal-cord involvement after IM virus injection, implying axonal transport from muscle to anterior horn cells. Poliovirus is culturable from blood 3-5 days after infection (before neutralizing antibody); it is shed from the oropharynx up to ~3 weeks and from the GI tract up to ~12 weeks (over 20 years in hypogammaglobulinemia), and attenuated oral vaccine virus can revert toward neurovirulence during GI replication. Capsid protein VP1 is the main neutralizing-antibody target, conferring lifelong serotype-specific protection that does not prevent reinfection or shedding; IgM persists under ~6 months and IgG for life. Mucosal/secretory IgA limits GI replication and shedding, and breast-milk IgA is protective. Acute flaccid myelitis and poliomyelitis both target anterior horn cells of the spinal cord.","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial sepsis (especially in neonatal generalized enteroviral disease)","Partially treated bacterial meningitis (can be hard to exclude from enteroviral meningitis)","Mumps meningitis (distinguished by low CSF glucose, vs normal in enterovirus)","Rubella (enteroviral rubelliform rash lacks lymphadenopathy and occurs in summer)","Measles (enteroviral enanthems may mimic Koplik's spots)","Herpetic stomatitis (vs herpangina, which lacks gingivitis)","Adenoviral or Chlamydia trachomatis conjunctivitis (vs acute hemorrhagic conjunctivitis)"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial sepsis of the newborn","Bacterial (including partially treated) meningitis","Mumps virus meningitis","Herpes simplex stomatitis / encephalitis","Adenovirus and Chlamydia trachomatis ocular infection","Other respiratory viruses causing cold/pneumonia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Guillain-Barre syndrome (also associated with enterovirus but a key paralytic mimic)","Idiopathic dilated cardiomyopathy (as a sequela/overlap of enteroviral myocarditis)","Erythema multiforme and other drug or immune exanthems","Type 1 diabetes mellitus (epidemiologically associated with prolonged enteroviral shedding)","Idiopathic dermatomyositis (chronic enteroviral infection in agammaglobulinemia mimics it)"],"additionalInvestigations":["PCR is now the primary diagnostic method; pan-enterovirus reverse-transcription PCR detects all human enteroviruses (CSF PCR ~70-100% sensitive, over 80% specific, faster than culture) but does NOT detect parechoviruses.","CSF PCR is less likely positive if presentation is 3 or more days after meningitis onset or with EV71; in those cases throat or rectal swab PCR (less specific) should be considered.","Serum PCR is highly sensitive/specific for disseminated disease and useful for diagnosis/monitoring in immunodeficient patients on immunoglobulin whose cultures may be negative; antigen detection is less sensitive than PCR.","Viral culture of CSF, serum, body-cavity fluid, or tissue is positive less often but a positive result is diagnostic; throat isolation is more meaningful than stool because shedding is shorter; coxsackievirus A may need special cell lines or suckling-mouse inoculation.","For suspected poliomyelitis: obtain two or more fecal and throat swabs at least 1 day apart for culture; a positive stool culture within the first 2 weeks confirms diagnosis when CSF culture is negative, and any poliovirus isolate must be sent to CDC to distinguish wild-type from vaccine virus.","Serotype identification is mainly for epidemiology; serologic diagnosis (paired acute and ~4-week convalescent neutralizing titers, the most accurate method) is limited by the many serotypes and absence of a common antigen.","Dedicated parechovirus PCR (performed by CDC and research labs; often unavailable commercially) when HPeV is suspected.","In acute flaccid myelitis: spinal-cord MRI may show gray-matter changes; CSF shows lymphocytic pleocytosis and mildly elevated protein; EV-D68 antibodies are most often found in CSF though the virus itself is rarely detected there."],"redFlags":["Neonatal enteroviral illness resembling sepsis with myocarditis/hypotension, fulminant hepatitis with DIC, meningoencephalitis, or pneumonia (serious disease most often in the first week of life, up to 3 months).","Rapid onset of flaccid limb paralysis after fever/respiratory symptoms (acute flaccid myelitis or paralytic enteroviral disease).","Brainstem encephalitis with myoclonus, tremor/ataxia, plus pulmonary edema or hemorrhage in young children (severe enterovirus 71 disease).","Bulbar involvement causing dysphagia, dysphonia, or respiratory insufficiency/circulatory collapse in poliomyelitis.","Chest pain, arrhythmia, ST-segment changes, and elevated cardiac enzymes after a respiratory prodrome (enteroviral myocarditis, risk of dilated cardiomyopathy).","Chronic meningoencephalitis or dermatomyositis-like syndrome in an agamma-/hypogammaglobulinemic patient."],"prognosis":"Most enterovirus infections are mild and self-limited, resolving within about a week. Enteroviral aseptic meningitis carries an excellent prognosis with rare neurologic sequelae (though CSF abnormalities can persist weeks); immunocompetent encephalitis patients generally do well. Pleurodynia resolves in days with rare recurrence; acute hemorrhagic conjunctivitis usually recovers completely in ~10 days. Myocarditis in older children and adults usually resolves fully, but neonates often have severe disease and up to 10% progress to chronic dilated cardiomyopathy, with chronic constrictive pericarditis a possible sequela. In paralytic poliomyelitis most patients regain some function over weeks to months but about two-thirds have residual neurologic sequelae; postpolio syndrome has a generally good prognosis with slow progression and plateaus of 1-10 years. Acute flaccid myelitis usually leaves persistent neurologic deficits. EV71 CNS disease can leave lasting dysphagia, cranial-nerve palsies, hypoventilation, limb weakness/atrophy, and impaired cognition. HPeV infection is usually mild (HPeV-1) but has caused infant deaths.","pitfalls":["Glucocorticoids are contraindicated in enterovirus infections.","A positive enterovirus culture from stool, nasopharynx, or throat does NOT prove disease causation, since these sites are colonized for weeks during subclinical infection; throat isolation is more meaningful than stool.","Pan-enterovirus PCR does not detect human parechoviruses, so HPeV can be missed unless a dedicated assay is used.","CSF PCR may be falsely negative if obtained 3 or more days into meningitis or in EV71 infection.","Neonatal enteroviral disease can be indistinguishable from bacterial sepsis; a recent viral illness in the mother is a useful clue.","Cultures may be falsely negative due to neutralizing antibody, unsuitable cell lines, or improper specimen handling.","Oral poliovirus vaccine can revert to neurovirulence (vaccine-associated/vaccine-derived poliomyelitis), with risk roughly 2000-fold higher in immunodeficient persons; tonsillectomy predisposes to bulbar polio and IM injections increase paralysis risk in the involved limb."],"severityClassification":"Poliovirus infection severity spectrum: asymptomatic (most) -> minor/abortive poliomyelitis (~5%: fever, malaise, sore throat, myalgia, headache, resolving in ~3 days) -> nonparalytic poliomyelitis/aseptic meningitis (~1%) -> paralytic poliomyelitis (least common; asymmetric, proximal-predominant weakness, may be bulbar/respiratory). Paralytic disease is more frequent in older individuals, pregnant women, and those exercising or experiencing trauma at symptom onset. Postpolio syndrome occurs 20-40 years after initial paralytic disease. Vaccine-associated paralytic risk is about 1 per 2.5 million OPV doses.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1632","Harrison 22e p.1633","Harrison 22e p.1634","Harrison 22e p.1635","Harrison 22e p.1636","Harrison 22e p.1637","Harrison 22e p.1638"],"crossLinks":[]},"entropion":{"name":"Entropion","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["inturned eyelid","inverted eyelid"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The eyelid margin turns inward so that lashes rub against the cornea and conjunctiva, causing irritation, foreign body sensation, tearing, and redness","The involutional (age-related) type is the commonest and usually affects the lower lid, driven by tissue laxity","Cicatricial entropion follows conjunctival scarring from conditions such as trachoma, chemical burns, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, and ocular cicatricial pemphigoid","Chronic lash-cornea contact risks epithelial breakdown, secondary microbial keratitis, and corneal scarring","Temporary measures include lubrication, taping the lid outward, and botulinum toxin, while lid-everting sutures give short-term relief","Definitive management is surgical correction tailored to the underlying mechanism","Distinguish from trichiasis, where lashes are misdirected but the lid margin itself is correctly positioned"],"pathophysiology":"Entropion is inward rotation of the eyelid margin. Involutional entropion arises from age-related horizontal lid laxity, attenuation or disinsertion of the lower lid retractors, and overriding of the preseptal orbicularis, which together destabilise the lid and allow it to rotate inward. Cicatricial entropion results from contracture of scarred tarsoconjunctiva pulling the margin toward the globe. Spastic entropion is triggered by orbicularis spasm from ocular irritation, and congenital entropion reflects a structural lid defect. In all forms the inturned lashes abrade the ocular surface, causing chronic irritation and potential corneal damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Trichiasis (misdirected lashes with normal lid position)","Epiblepharon (extra lower lid skin fold, often in children)","Distichiasis (aberrant accessory lash row)","Spastic entropion from ocular irritation","Blepharospasm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination to assess lash-cornea contact and corneal integrity","Fluorescein staining to detect epithelial damage","Assessment of horizontal lid laxity (snap-back and distraction tests)","Evaluation of the conjunctival fornices for scarring in suspected cicatricial disease"],"redFlags":["Corneal epithelial defect or infiltrate indicating secondary keratitis and threatened vision","Progressive conjunctival scarring suggesting cicatrising disease such as pemphigoid requiring systemic workup","Persistent lash-cornea contact despite temporising measures"],"prognosis":"Surgical correction is generally effective with good long-term results, though cicatricial cases may recur and require repeated intervention; untreated chronic disease can cause corneal scarring and visual loss.","pitfalls":["Treating symptoms with lubricants alone while ongoing lash contact damages the cornea","Failing to identify a cicatricial cause requiring different surgical planning and systemic evaluation","Confusing entropion with trichiasis and choosing the wrong intervention","Overlooking recurrence after involutional repair due to unaddressed lid laxity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"eosinophilia":{"name":"Eosinophilia","system":"Immunology / Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["peripheral blood eosinophilia","hypereosinophilia","eosinophilic disorders"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Middleton's Allergy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Eosinophilia is defined by an absolute eosinophil count above the normal range, with hypereosinophilia denoting persistently very high counts; the degree and persistence guide the urgency of evaluation","A practical framework separates causes into reactive/secondary (allergy, parasites, drugs, malignancy) and primary/clonal (myeloid or lymphoid neoplasms) plus idiopathic disease","Worldwide, helminth infection is the leading cause, so travel and exposure history and stool and serologic studies are essential, especially before immunosuppression that could disseminate strongyloidiasis","In high-income settings, atopic disease and drug reactions are common causes, and a careful medication review can reveal the culprit","Hypereosinophilic syndrome is diagnosed when marked eosinophilia persists with evidence of eosinophil-mediated organ damage and no identifiable secondary cause","Cardiac involvement (endomyocardial fibrosis and thrombosis) is a major cause of morbidity and mortality, so troponin and echocardiography are important in significant hypereosinophilia","The FIP1L1-PDGFRA fusion defines an imatinib-responsive myeloid variant, making molecular testing pivotal because it changes treatment entirely","Corticosteroids are the mainstay for symptomatic reactive and idiopathic hypereosinophilia, but empiric anti-parasitic cover for Strongyloides is prudent before starting them where exposure is possible","Peripheral counts do not always reflect tissue eosinophilia, so organ-specific disease can occur with only modestly raised or normal blood counts"],"pathophysiology":"Eosinophil production and survival are driven principally by interleukin-5, along with IL-3 and GM-CSF. In reactive eosinophilia these cytokines are overproduced by activated T cells or tumour cells in response to allergens, parasites, drugs, or malignancy. In clonal (primary) eosinophilia, a genetic lesion in the myeloid lineage-most classically the FIP1L1-PDGFRA fusion, or rearrangements of PDGFRB, FGFR1, and JAK2-produces autonomous eosinophil proliferation. A lymphocytic variant arises from an aberrant T-cell population secreting eosinophilopoietic cytokines. Tissue injury results from release of eosinophil granule proteins (major basic protein, eosinophil cationic protein), reactive oxygen species, and prothrombotic and profibrotic mediators, damaging heart, lungs, skin, gut, and nervous system.","additionalDifferentials":["Helminth and other parasitic infections","Atopic disease: asthma, atopic dermatitis, allergic rhinitis","Drug hypersensitivity, including DRESS","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis and eosinophilic lung diseases","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis and other vasculitides","Myeloid/lymphoid neoplasms with eosinophilia and reactive eosinophilia in solid tumours or lymphoma","Adrenal insufficiency and, rarely, HIV infection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Repeat full blood count to confirm and quantify the absolute eosinophil count","Stool microscopy and relevant parasite serology, with Strongyloides testing before immunosuppression","Serum tryptase, vitamin B12, and IgE with review of medications","Molecular testing for FIP1L1-PDGFRA and other tyrosine-kinase fusions, plus marrow examination and cytogenetics when clonal disease is suspected","Troponin and echocardiography (and cardiac MRI if indicated) to assess cardiac involvement","Targeted imaging and biopsy of affected organs"],"redFlags":["Cardiac involvement with troponin rise, heart failure, or intracardiac thrombus","Neurologic deficits, thromboembolism, or rapidly progressive organ dysfunction","Very high, persistent counts (hypereosinophilia) with systemic symptoms suggesting evolving organ damage","Features of an underlying haematologic malignancy such as splenomegaly, cytopenias, or blasts"],"prognosis":"Reactive eosinophilia usually resolves once the trigger is removed or treated. Idiopathic hypereosinophilic syndrome outcome depends on organ involvement and treatment response; the FIP1L1-PDGFRA variant generally responds very well to targeted tyrosine-kinase inhibition, whereas untreated cardiac disease carries a poor prognosis.","pitfalls":["Starting corticosteroids without excluding Strongyloides, risking fatal hyperinfection","Attributing eosinophilia to allergy without considering clonal or parasitic causes","Relying on blood counts alone and missing tissue eosinophilic organ damage","Overlooking treatable molecular subtypes by not sending fusion-gene testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"eosinophilic_granulomatosis":{"name":"Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis","system":"Immunology / Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["EGPA","Churg-Strauss syndrome","Churg-Strauss","allergic granulomatosis and angiitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Middleton's Allergy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["EGPA is an eosinophil-rich, often ANCA-associated small-vessel vasculitis that almost always arises on a background of adult-onset, frequently difficult-to-control asthma","The illness classically evolves through phases: an allergic phase with asthma and rhinosinusitis, an eosinophilic phase with blood and tissue eosinophilia, and a vasculitic phase with systemic involvement","Peripheral blood eosinophilia is a hallmark, and its degree helps distinguish EGPA from other ANCA-associated vasculitides","Neurologic involvement, especially mononeuritis multiplex causing asymmetric sensorimotor deficits, is a characteristic and clinically important feature","Cardiac involvement-myocarditis, heart failure, and coronary or pericardial disease-is a leading cause of death and mandates cardiac assessment even when asymptomatic","ANCA (usually anti-MPO/p-ANCA) is positive in a minority; ANCA-positive disease tends toward vasculitic manifestations such as neuropathy and glomerulonephritis, while ANCA-negative disease more often shows cardiac and lung involvement","Diagnosis integrates the clinical picture with eosinophilia and, where feasible, biopsy showing eosinophilic tissue infiltration, extravascular granulomas, and necrotising vasculitis","Corticosteroids are the foundation of treatment, with additional immunosuppression for organ-threatening disease and anti-IL-5 (eosinophil-directed) biologic therapy used to control eosinophilic disease and spare steroids","A recognised though debated association exists between leukotriene-receptor antagonists and unmasking of EGPA, often linked to reduction of background corticosteroids"],"pathophysiology":"EGPA is a systemic disorder combining marked eosinophilia with small- and medium-vessel necrotising vasculitis and extravascular eosinophilic granulomas. Th2-driven immune responses with elevated interleukin-5 promote eosinophil production, survival, and tissue infiltration; eosinophil granule proteins cause direct tissue and endothelial damage, particularly in the heart, lungs, nerves, skin, and gut. In a subset, anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (typically anti-myeloperoxidase) contribute to a neutrophil-mediated necrotising vasculitis, giving rise to glomerulonephritis, purpura, and peripheral neuropathy. The two mechanisms-eosinophilic tissue infiltration and ANCA-associated vasculitis-broadly correspond to the different clinical phenotypes.","additionalDifferentials":["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis and microscopic polyangiitis","Hypereosinophilic syndrome (including clonal/myeloproliferative variants)","Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia and allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Parasitic infection with eosinophilia","Drug reaction with eosinophilia (DRESS)","Polyarteritis nodosa and other systemic vasculitides"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count documenting peripheral eosinophilia and inflammatory markers","ANCA testing with anti-MPO and anti-PR3 specificities","Chest imaging for pulmonary infiltrates and sinus assessment","Cardiac evaluation with ECG, troponin, echocardiography, and cardiac MRI as indicated","Nerve conduction studies for suspected mononeuritis multiplex","Urinalysis and renal function for glomerulonephritis, with tissue biopsy of an affected organ where feasible"],"redFlags":["Cardiac involvement with myocarditis, heart failure, or arrhythmia, the principal cause of mortality","Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis with rising creatinine","Mononeuritis multiplex or other acute neurologic deficit indicating active vasculitis","Gastrointestinal ischaemia, alveolar haemorrhage, or other organ-threatening manifestations requiring urgent immunosuppression"],"prognosis":"With corticosteroids and, when needed, additional immunosuppression and eosinophil-directed biologics, remission is achievable in most patients, though relapse is common and asthma often persists. Prognosis is worst when there is cardiac, severe gastrointestinal, or renal involvement, which are the main determinants of mortality.","pitfalls":["Attributing worsening asthma and eosinophilia to poorly controlled asthma alone and missing evolving vasculitis","Failing to assess the heart in apparently stable patients, given the high mortality of cardiac disease","Overlooking mononeuritis multiplex as a marker of active organ-threatening vasculitis","Confusing EGPA with hypereosinophilic syndrome or other eosinophilic lung disease without appropriate ANCA testing and biopsy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"epididymo_orchitis":{"name":"Epididymo-orchitis","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["epididymitis","orchitis","epididymo orchitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with gradual-onset unilateral scrotal pain and swelling, often with fever, dysuria, and urethral discharge, developing over hours to days rather than abruptly","The aetiology is age-related: sexually transmitted organisms (Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoeae) predominate in younger sexually active men, and coliform urinary pathogens in older men and those with urinary tract abnormalities","Prehn's sign (relief of pain on elevating the testis) and a present cremasteric reflex favour epididymo-orchitis over torsion, but these signs are unreliable and torsion must always be excluded","Examination shows a tender, swollen, warm epididymis and testis, sometimes with a reactive hydrocele and overlying scrotal erythema","Management principles are empirical antibiotics guided by the likely organism and patient age, supportive measures such as scrotal support, rest, and analgesia, and treatment plus screening of sexual partners in STI-related cases","Mumps orchitis should be considered in unvaccinated patients, typically following parotitis, and is usually managed supportively","Failure to improve or a fluctuant swelling raises concern for abscess formation requiring imaging and possible drainage","In older men or recurrent cases, evaluate for underlying urinary tract obstruction, instrumentation, or structural abnormality"],"pathophysiology":"Epididymo-orchitis is inflammation of the epididymis, frequently extending to the adjacent testis, usually caused by retrograde ascent of organisms from the urethra or bladder along the vas deferens. In younger men sexually transmitted pathogens predominate, whereas in older men Gram-negative urinary organisms associated with obstruction, catheterisation, or instrumentation are more common. The resulting inflammatory oedema, hyperaemia, and swelling can progress to suppuration, abscess, or testicular infarction from compromised blood supply. Viral causes such as mumps produce orchitis through haematogenous spread.","additionalDifferentials":["Testicular torsion (must always be excluded)","Torsion of a testicular appendage","Testicular tumour","Strangulated inguinal hernia","Testicular trauma","Tuberculous or granulomatous epididymitis","Scrotal abscess"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis and midstream urine culture","First-void urine nucleic acid amplification testing and urethral swab for sexually transmitted organisms","Colour Doppler ultrasound showing increased blood flow, and to exclude abscess or tumour","Full STI screen and partner notification when a sexually transmitted cause is likely","Evaluation of the urinary tract for structural abnormality in older or recurrent cases"],"redFlags":["Inability to confidently exclude testicular torsion, which is a surgical emergency","Progression to scrotal or testicular abscess, or testicular infarction","Systemic sepsis, particularly in immunocompromised or diabetic patients (Fournier's gangrene must be considered with rapidly spreading pain and necrosis)","Failure to respond to appropriate antibiotics, suggesting resistant organism, abscess, or alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Most cases resolve with appropriate antibiotics and supportive care, though swelling and discomfort can take weeks to settle; complications include abscess, chronic epididymitis, testicular atrophy, and, particularly with mumps or bilateral disease, impaired fertility.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing torsion as epididymo-orchitis and delaying surgical exploration","Choosing antibiotics that do not cover the likely organism for the patient's age and risk profile","Neglecting partner treatment and STI screening in younger men","Missing an evolving abscess in a patient not responding to therapy","Failing to investigate underlying urinary tract pathology in older or recurrent cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"epidural_abscess":{"name":"Spinal epidural abscess","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SEA","spinal epidural abscess","epidural empyema","extradural abscess"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / IDSA","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A collection of pus in the spinal epidural space that is a neurosurgical emergency because of the risk of irreversible cord compression","The classic triad of back pain, fever, and neurological deficit is present in only a minority, so a high index of suspicion is essential","Symptoms often progress through localised back pain, then radicular pain, then motor or sensory deficit and sphincter dysfunction, and finally paralysis","Staphylococcus aureus is the commonest organism, and infection may spread haematogenously or contiguously from vertebral osteomyelitis or paraspinal infection","Risk factors include injecting drug use, diabetes, immunosuppression, indwelling catheters, spinal instrumentation, and recent epidural procedures","Urgent MRI with contrast is the investigation of choice and should not be delayed once the diagnosis is considered","Management principles combine emergency surgical decompression and drainage for neurological compromise with prolonged targeted antibiotics; selected stable patients without deficit may be managed medically with close monitoring","Once a significant neurological deficit is established, the window for functional recovery is short, making speed of intervention critical"],"pathophysiology":"A spinal epidural abscess forms when bacteria reach the epidural space by haematogenous seeding, contiguous spread from vertebral osteomyelitis or a paraspinal focus, or direct inoculation during a spinal procedure. Accumulating pus causes neurological injury through two mechanisms: direct mechanical compression of the spinal cord or cauda equina and vascular compromise, with septic thrombophlebitis and arterial disruption producing cord ischaemia and infarction. This combination explains why deficits can progress rapidly and become irreversible if decompression is delayed.","additionalDifferentials":["Vertebral osteomyelitis or discitis without abscess","Transverse myelitis","Spinal cord tumour or metastasis","Acute disc herniation with cord compression","Guillain-Barre syndrome in the setting of ascending weakness"],"infectionMimics":["Metastatic epidural cord compression from malignancy","Epidural haematoma","Demyelinating myelopathy","Mechanical radiculopathy from disc prolapse"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent MRI of the whole spine with contrast to localise the abscess and assess cord compression","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers","Culture of aspirated or surgically drained pus to guide antibiotics","Screening for a primary source such as endocarditis or vertebral osteomyelitis","Assessment for skip lesions, since abscesses may involve multiple non-contiguous levels"],"redFlags":["Any new or progressing motor weakness, sensory level, or bladder or bowel dysfunction indicating cord or cauda equina compression requiring emergency decompression","Rapid neurological deterioration signalling a closing window for recovery","Back pain with fever in an injecting drug user or immunocompromised patient","Sepsis complicating the abscess"],"prognosis":"Outcome hinges on neurological status at the time of intervention; patients treated before a major deficit develops usually do well, whereas established paralysis, especially if present for more than a day or two before decompression, often does not recover fully.","pitfalls":["Waiting for the full triad of back pain, fever, and deficit before considering the diagnosis","Delaying MRI or surgical referral once suspicion is raised","Failing to image the entire spine and missing non-contiguous abscesses","Underestimating how quickly a mild deficit can progress to permanent paralysis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","IDSA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"episcleritis":{"name":"Episcleritis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["simple episcleritis","nodular episcleritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common, benign, usually self-limiting inflammation of the episclera presenting with acute redness and mild discomfort or grittiness rather than severe pain","Redness may be sectoral or diffuse with a bright red appearance, and visual acuity is preserved","The superficial episcleral vessels blanch with topical phenylephrine, unlike the deeper vessels of scleritis","Classified as simple (diffuse) or nodular; in nodular disease a mobile, tender nodule is seen over the sclera","Most cases are idiopathic, though a minority are linked to systemic inflammatory conditions such as gout, rosacea, or connective-tissue disease","It is distinguished from scleritis by the absence of deep boring pain, vessel blanching with phenylephrine, and a benign course","Management is largely reassurance with lubricants; topical or short courses of oral NSAIDs and, occasionally, mild topical steroids help troublesome or nodular cases"],"pathophysiology":"Episcleritis is inflammation confined to the episclera, the thin vascular layer of connective tissue between the conjunctiva and the sclera. The process is typically a localised, non-granulomatous inflammatory response affecting the superficial episcleral vascular plexus, producing vasodilatation and mild oedema without the deep scleral destruction of true scleritis. It is usually idiopathic and self-limiting, though recurrent or nodular episodes can occasionally be associated with systemic inflammatory disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Scleritis","Conjunctivitis","Anterior uveitis","Inflamed pterygium or pinguecula","Subconjunctival haemorrhage","Foreign body or localised irritation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination and topical phenylephrine test to confirm superficial vessel involvement","Assessment of visual acuity to confirm it is preserved","Limited systemic workup only in recurrent, nodular, or atypical cases (e.g. inflammatory markers, autoimmune screen, urate)"],"redFlags":["Severe boring pain, deep violaceous vessels, or failure to blanch with phenylephrine suggesting scleritis instead","Reduced visual acuity, which should not occur in simple episcleritis","Frequently recurrent disease suggesting an underlying systemic inflammatory condition"],"prognosis":"Episcleritis is benign and self-limiting, typically resolving within one to two weeks without lasting visual consequences, although recurrences are common.","pitfalls":["Confusing episcleritis with scleritis and over-investigating or over-treating a benign condition","Conversely, dismissing genuine scleritis as episcleritis and missing sight-threatening disease","Prolonged unnecessary topical steroid use with its attendant risks of raised intraocular pressure","Overlooking an associated systemic disease in recurrent cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"epistaxis":{"name":"Epistaxis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nosebleed","nasal haemorrhage"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Bleeding is broadly classified as anterior or posterior, which differ in likely source, severity and management","Most bleeds are anterior and arise from Little's area on the nasal septum, where several vessels anastomose (Kiesselbach's plexus)","Posterior bleeds tend to occur in older patients, are more profuse, and are harder to control, with a higher risk of airway soiling and significant blood loss","Causes are grouped into local (trauma, digital picking, foreign body, infection, tumour) and general (hypertension, coagulopathy, anticoagulants, hereditary telangiectasia)","First aid principles are sitting upright, leaning forward and pinching the soft part of the nose, with attention to airway and circulation","Stepwise management progresses from cautery of an identified bleeding point to anterior packing and then posterior packing or balloon devices","Refractory bleeding may require endoscopic or surgical arterial ligation or embolisation of the offending vessel","Unilateral persistent or recurrent bleeding, especially with nasal obstruction, warrants evaluation for a tumour such as juvenile angiofibroma in adolescent males"],"pathophysiology":"Epistaxis is bleeding from the nasal mucosa, whose rich, superficial vascular supply from branches of both the internal and external carotid systems makes it prone to haemorrhage. Anterior bleeding usually comes from the low-pressure anastomotic vessels of Little's area, provoked by drying, trauma or minor mucosal injury. Posterior bleeding tends to arise from larger branches such as the sphenopalatine artery and is aggravated by hypertension and vascular fragility in older patients. Systemic factors such as bleeding disorders, anticoagulation and platelet dysfunction impair haemostasis and prolong or worsen bleeding.","additionalDifferentials":["Bleeding from a nasal or sinonasal tumour","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma","Hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia","Coagulopathy or anticoagulant-related bleeding","Haemoptysis or upper gastrointestinal bleeding mimicking nasal bleeding","Trauma including base of skull fracture with CSF or blood"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anterior rhinoscopy and nasal endoscopy to localise the bleeding point","Blood pressure measurement and assessment of haemodynamic status","Full blood count, coagulation profile and group and save or crossmatch in significant bleeds","Review of anticoagulant and antiplatelet medication","Imaging (CT or MRI) and referral where a sinonasal tumour or vascular lesion is suspected"],"redFlags":["Profuse or posterior bleeding with hypovolaemia or haemodynamic instability requiring resuscitation","Airway compromise from blood aspiration in heavy or bilateral bleeding","Bleeding uncontrolled by packing needing arterial ligation or embolisation","Recurrent unilateral bleeding with a mass suggesting malignancy or angiofibroma"],"prognosis":"Most anterior nosebleeds settle with simple first aid or cautery, whereas posterior and recurrent bleeds, particularly in patients with hypertension or coagulopathy, can be severe or life-threatening and may need aggressive intervention.","pitfalls":["Failing to assess and resuscitate the patient while focusing only on the nose","Overlooking a posterior source when anterior examination appears clear","Missing an underlying tumour in recurrent unilateral bleeding","Neglecting to review anticoagulation and correct coagulopathy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"erectile_dysfunction":{"name":"Erectile dysfunction","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ED","impotence","erectile failure"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Erectile dysfunction is the persistent inability to achieve or maintain an erection sufficient for satisfactory sexual activity, and it is common with increasing age","Causes are broadly organic (vascular, neurogenic, hormonal, anatomical, drug-induced) or psychogenic, and the two frequently coexist","Vascular disease is the leading organic cause, so ED often shares risk factors with, and can be an early warning sign of, cardiovascular disease and diabetes","A history distinguishing gradual organic onset from sudden situational psychogenic ED is key; preserved spontaneous nocturnal or early-morning erections point toward a psychogenic cause","Assessment should review medications (antihypertensives, antidepressants, antiandrogens), lifestyle factors (smoking, alcohol, obesity), and endocrine and psychological status","First-line pharmacological management is with phosphodiesterase type 5 inhibitors, alongside optimisation of cardiovascular risk factors and lifestyle modification","PDE5 inhibitors are contraindicated with nitrate therapy because of the risk of profound hypotension","Second-line options include intracavernosal or intraurethral vasoactive agents and vacuum devices, with penile prosthesis implantation reserved for refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"Penile erection depends on neurally mediated relaxation of cavernosal smooth muscle, increased arterial inflow, and veno-occlusion that traps blood within the corpora cavernosa; nitric oxide and the cyclic GMP pathway are central to this process. Erectile dysfunction arises when any of these mechanisms fails: arterial insufficiency from atherosclerosis reduces inflow, veno-occlusive dysfunction allows leak, neuropathy (for example from diabetes or pelvic surgery) impairs signalling, and hormonal deficiency (notably hypogonadism) reduces desire and erectile capacity. Psychogenic factors act through central inhibition of the erectile response. Many patients have a mixed, multifactorial aetiology.","additionalDifferentials":["Psychogenic erectile dysfunction","Vascular (arteriogenic or veno-occlusive) dysfunction","Neurogenic causes (diabetic neuropathy, pelvic surgery, spinal cord disease)","Endocrine causes (hypogonadism, hyperprolactinaemia, thyroid disease)","Drug-induced erectile dysfunction","Peyronie's disease","Reduced libido or relationship factors"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting glucose or HbA1c and lipid profile to assess cardiovascular and metabolic risk","Morning serum testosterone, with prolactin and other hormones if deficiency is suspected","Blood pressure and cardiovascular risk assessment","Validated questionnaires such as the International Index of Erectile Function","Penile Doppler ultrasound and specialist vascular studies in selected complex or younger patients"],"redFlags":["ED as a sentinel marker of undiagnosed cardiovascular disease or diabetes","Erectile dysfunction with neurological signs suggesting spinal cord or cauda equina pathology","Concurrent nitrate use, which contraindicates PDE5 inhibitors","Features suggesting a pituitary lesion (hyperprolactinaemia, visual field changes) requiring endocrine evaluation"],"prognosis":"Erectile dysfunction is highly treatable in most men, with many responding to PDE5 inhibitors and risk-factor modification; prognosis depends on the underlying cause, and it should prompt evaluation and management of associated cardiovascular risk.","pitfalls":["Prescribing PDE5 inhibitors to a patient taking nitrates","Treating ED symptomatically without addressing underlying cardiovascular or metabolic disease","Overlooking hypogonadism or hyperprolactinaemia as reversible causes","Failing to consider medication side effects as a contributor","Neglecting the psychological and relationship dimensions of the problem"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"erythema_multiforme":{"name":"Erythema multiforme","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["EM","erythema multiforme minor","erythema multiforme major"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acute, usually self-limiting hypersensitivity reaction characterised by target (iris) lesions with three concentric zones","Lesions are typically symmetrical and acral, favouring the extensor surfaces of the hands, forearms, feet, and often the palms and soles","Herpes simplex virus is the commonest trigger, and Mycoplasma pneumoniae is an important cause, particularly in children","Erythema multiforme major involves significant mucosal disease (oral, ocular, or genital) in addition to skin lesions, whereas the minor form has little or no mucosal involvement","It is now regarded as distinct from Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis, which have different triggers (mainly drugs), morphology, and far greater skin detachment and mortality","Management is largely supportive with removal or treatment of the trigger; recurrent herpes-associated disease may warrant suppressive antiviral therapy","Recurrent or persistent disease should prompt a search for an underlying herpes infection or other trigger"],"pathophysiology":"Erythema multiforme is a cell-mediated immune reaction, most often provoked by infection, in which cytotoxic T lymphocytes target keratinocytes expressing antigens derived from the trigger. In herpes-associated disease, viral DNA fragments are transported to the skin and drive a T-cell response with interferon-gamma-mediated inflammation at the dermo-epidermal junction, producing the characteristic target lesions with central epidermal damage. This immunopathology and its infective triggers distinguish it from the drug-driven, tumour-necrosis-factor and granulysin-mediated widespread keratinocyte apoptosis of Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis.","additionalDifferentials":["Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis","Urticaria and urticarial vasculitis","Fixed drug eruption","Bullous or autoimmune blistering disease","Viral exanthem","Acute cutaneous lupus erythematosus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on the characteristic target lesions and distribution","Testing for herpes simplex virus and Mycoplasma pneumoniae when a trigger is sought","Skin biopsy in atypical cases to distinguish from other reactions","Review of recent medications to help differentiate from drug-induced severe reactions","Assessment of mucosal and ocular involvement, with ophthalmology input where indicated"],"redFlags":["Extensive skin detachment or a positive Nikolsky sign suggesting Stevens-Johnson syndrome or toxic epidermal necrolysis","Severe mucosal involvement impairing oral intake, vision, or urination","Systemic upset with fever and mucosal disease requiring urgent assessment","Ocular involvement threatening long-term visual complications"],"prognosis":"Erythema multiforme minor is generally self-limiting and resolves over a few weeks without scarring; the herpes-associated form is prone to recurrence, and prognosis is far better than for the more severe drug-induced blistering syndromes it must be distinguished from.","pitfalls":["Confusing erythema multiforme with Stevens-Johnson syndrome or toxic epidermal necrolysis and mismanaging severity","Overlooking an underlying herpes simplex or Mycoplasma trigger, especially in recurrent disease","Failing to assess and manage mucosal and ocular involvement","Attributing a target-like drug eruption to infection and missing a culprit medication"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"essential_thrombocythemia":{"name":"Essential thrombocythaemia","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ET","essential thrombocythemia","essential thrombocytosis","primary thrombocythaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A myeloproliferative neoplasm characterised by a sustained rise in platelet count from clonal overproduction of megakaryocytes","Many patients are asymptomatic and detected on a routine blood count, while others present with vasomotor symptoms such as headache, erythromelalgia (burning red extremities), and visual disturbance","Both arterial and venous thrombosis and, paradoxically, bleeding can occur; bleeding risk rises with very high platelet counts due to acquired von Willebrand factor deficiency","Most cases carry a driver mutation in JAK2, CALR, or MPL, which supports clonality and helps distinguish ET from reactive thrombocytosis","Reactive causes of a high platelet count (iron deficiency, infection, inflammation, malignancy, post-splenectomy) must be excluded before diagnosing ET","Treatment intensity is guided by thrombotic risk, factoring in age, prior thrombosis, and JAK2 status; low-dose aspirin is used for microvascular symptoms and thrombosis prevention","Cytoreduction (for example with hydroxyurea) is reserved for higher-risk patients to lower the platelet count and reduce thrombotic events","Long-term risks include transformation to myelofibrosis or, less commonly, acute leukaemia"],"pathophysiology":"ET is a clonal disorder of the haematopoietic stem cell in which constitutive activation of the JAK-STAT signalling pathway, most often through JAK2 V617F, CALR, or MPL mutations, drives excessive megakaryocyte proliferation and platelet production independent of normal regulatory signals. The expanded platelet mass and qualitatively abnormal platelets promote thrombosis in the microcirculation and larger vessels. At very high counts, platelets adsorb and clear large von Willebrand factor multimers, producing an acquired von Willebrand syndrome and a paradoxical bleeding tendency. Over years the clone may evolve towards marrow fibrosis or acute leukaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Reactive (secondary) thrombocytosis from iron deficiency, infection, or inflammation","Chronic myeloid leukaemia","Polycythaemia vera","Primary myelofibrosis (prefibrotic stage)","Myelodysplastic syndrome with thrombocytosis","Post-splenectomy thrombocytosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film to confirm sustained thrombocytosis and assess morphology","Molecular testing for JAK2 V617F, and if negative, CALR and MPL mutations","BCR-ABL1 testing to exclude chronic myeloid leukaemia","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine showing enlarged, clustered megakaryocytes","Iron studies, inflammatory markers, and screening for occult malignancy to exclude reactive causes","Assessment for acquired von Willebrand syndrome when counts are very high or bleeding occurs"],"redFlags":["Acute arterial or venous thrombosis, including stroke, myocardial infarction, or splanchnic vein thrombosis","Significant haemorrhage, particularly with extreme thrombocytosis and acquired von Willebrand deficiency","Transient neurological or visual symptoms indicating microvascular ischaemia","Features of transformation to myelofibrosis or acute leukaemia such as worsening cytopenias or new blasts"],"prognosis":"Life expectancy in ET is close to that of the general population for many patients, with the principal threats being thrombosis and haemorrhage; a minority progress over years to myelofibrosis or acute leukaemia, which markedly worsens outlook.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing ET without excluding reactive thrombocytosis and chronic myeloid leukaemia","Prescribing aspirin at very high platelet counts without recognising acquired von Willebrand syndrome and bleeding risk","Underestimating thrombotic risk in JAK2-positive or older patients","Attributing microvascular symptoms to unrelated causes and missing the underlying disorder","Failing to reassess for transformation when the blood picture changes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"essential_tremor":{"name":"Essential tremor","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ET","benign essential tremor","familial tremor"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The hallmark is a bilateral, largely symmetric postural and kinetic (action) tremor of the upper limbs, in contrast to the resting tremor of Parkinson disease","The head, voice, and jaw are often involved, and a titubating head tremor or quavering voice can be prominent","The tremor characteristically worsens with sustained posture and goal-directed movement and is absent at complete rest","A family history is common, and inheritance frequently appears autosomal dominant","Many patients report transient improvement of tremor after alcohol, a useful diagnostic clue","First-line pharmacological principles include a non-selective beta-blocker such as propranolol or the anticonvulsant primidone","Deep brain stimulation of the ventral intermediate thalamic nucleus can help severe, medication-refractory cases","Onset spans a wide age range, and prevalence and severity tend to increase with age"],"pathophysiology":"Essential tremor is a common movement disorder thought to arise from abnormal oscillatory activity within cerebello-thalamo-cortical circuits, with the inferior olive and cerebellum implicated as key nodes of a central oscillator. It is often familial with autosomal dominant inheritance and variable penetrance, though the precise genetic and neurodegenerative substrate remains incompletely defined, and some evidence points to cerebellar pathology.","additionalDifferentials":["Parkinson disease (resting tremor, bradykinesia, rigidity)","Enhanced physiological tremor (e.g. anxiety, caffeine, thyrotoxicosis, drugs)","Dystonic tremor","Cerebellar (intention) tremor","Drug-induced or withdrawal tremor","Wilson disease in younger patients"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment to distinguish action tremor from resting and cerebellar tremor","Thyroid function tests to exclude thyrotoxicosis as a cause of enhanced tremor","Serum copper and ceruloplasmin in younger patients to exclude Wilson disease","Review of medications and stimulant intake that may produce or amplify tremor","DaTscan or specialist assessment when distinguishing from Parkinson disease is difficult"],"redFlags":["Strictly unilateral tremor, a resting component, or associated bradykinesia and rigidity suggesting Parkinson disease","Abrupt onset or rapid progression suggesting a structural, toxic, or metabolic cause","Young age of onset with Kayser-Fleischer rings or hepatic dysfunction suggesting Wilson disease","Accompanying cerebellar signs, ataxia, or other focal neurological deficits"],"prognosis":"Essential tremor is typically slowly progressive over years. Although often labelled benign, it can become functionally and socially disabling, impairing writing, eating, and drinking; many patients gain useful relief from medication or, in refractory cases, from surgical neuromodulation.","pitfalls":["Confusing essential tremor with early Parkinson disease, which delays appropriate management","Overlooking a reversible cause such as thyrotoxicosis, medication, or excess caffeine","Missing Wilson disease in a young patient with tremor","Assuming the disorder is trivial and underestimating its impact on daily function and quality of life"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"ethylene_glycol_poisoning":{"name":"Ethylene glycol poisoning","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["antifreeze poisoning","ethylene glycol toxicity","glycol poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Ethylene glycol, found in antifreeze and coolants, is itself relatively harmless but is metabolised to glycolic and oxalic acids that cause severe high anion-gap metabolic acidosis and organ injury","The course classically evolves in stages: an early neurological phase resembling alcohol intoxication, then a cardiopulmonary phase, and finally a renal phase with acute kidney injury","Precipitation of calcium oxalate crystals causes renal tubular damage and produces hypocalcaemia, which may manifest as tetany or QT prolongation","Suspect it in unexplained high anion-gap metabolic acidosis with a raised osmolal gap; calcium oxalate crystals may be seen on urine microscopy","Fomepizole is the antidote of choice, inhibiting alcohol dehydrogenase to prevent formation of toxic acids; ethanol is an alternative where fomepizole is not available","Cofactor therapy with thiamine and pyridoxine is given to divert metabolism toward non-toxic products","Haemodialysis removes the parent compound and its metabolites and corrects acidosis, and is indicated for severe acidosis, renal failure, or high levels","Urinary fluorescence under Wood's lamp (from fluorescein added to some antifreeze) is unreliable and should not be used to confirm or exclude the diagnosis"],"pathophysiology":"Ethylene glycol is oxidised by alcohol dehydrogenase to glycoaldehyde, then to glycolic acid and finally oxalic acid. Glycolic acid is the main contributor to the profound high anion-gap metabolic acidosis, while oxalic acid combines with calcium to form insoluble calcium oxalate crystals that deposit in renal tubules, causing acute kidney injury, and lower serum calcium. Accumulating toxic metabolites also impair oxidative metabolism, producing lactic acidosis. The parent alcohol raises serum osmolality, giving an early osmolal gap that later falls as the anion gap widens.","additionalDifferentials":["Methanol poisoning","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Lactic acidosis from other causes","Salicylate poisoning","Uraemia and other high anion-gap acidoses"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ethylene glycol level where available","Arterial blood gas with calculated anion gap","Serum osmolality with calculated osmolal gap","Serum calcium, electrolytes, urea, and creatinine","Urine microscopy for calcium oxalate crystals and ECG for QT interval"],"redFlags":["Severe high anion-gap metabolic acidosis","Acute kidney injury with oliguria","Hypocalcaemia with tetany, seizures, or QT prolongation","Reduced consciousness or coma","Rising anion gap with a falling osmolal gap indicating ongoing metabolism to toxic acids"],"prognosis":"Prompt antidote and, when needed, dialysis before significant metabolite accumulation usually allow full recovery; delayed treatment can lead to permanent renal failure, neurological injury, and death.","pitfalls":["False reassurance during the early intoxication-like phase before acidosis and renal injury develop","Delaying antidote to await a confirmatory ethylene glycol level in a suggestive presentation","Relying on urine fluorescence to make or exclude the diagnosis","Missing life-threatening hypocalcaemia and its cardiac effects"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"evaluation_and_management_of_obesity":{"name":"Evaluation and Management of Obesity","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Obesity evaluation","Overweight and obesity assessment","Adiposity-based chronic disease"],"chapter":414,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3187","clinicalPearls":["More than 70% of U.S. adults are overweight or have obesity, and prevalence is rising in most industrialized nations, with accelerating rates in children and adolescents.","BMI (weight in kg divided by height in m squared, or weight in lb divided by height in in squared times 703) is used to classify weight status: healthy weight 18.5-24.9, overweight 25.0-29.9, obesity class I 30.0-34.9, class II 35.0-39.9, and extreme/class III obesity 40 or greater.","BMI only reflects body size; it does not capture body composition, fat distribution, or individual health, so it functions as a screening and population tool rather than a direct measure of adiposity.","Waist circumference is a surrogate for visceral adipose tissue and independently predicts metabolic syndrome, diabetes, and cardiovascular disease; it should be measured in the horizontal plane above the iliac crest, particularly when BMI is 35 or below.","Ethnic-specific waist cutpoints differ: for Europeans, greater than 94 cm in men and greater than 80 cm in women; for South Asians and Chinese, greater than 90 cm in men and greater than 80 cm in women.","Lower BMI thresholds for overweight and obesity have been proposed for the Asia-Pacific population because metabolic risk appears at lower body weights.","The obesity-focused history is best approached using neutral language (weight, excess weight, BMI, unhealthy weight) because terms like obesity, morbid obesity, or fatness are perceived as stigmatizing.","Recommended baseline workup for all patients includes a fasting lipid profile (total, LDL, and HDL cholesterol plus triglycerides), a chemistry panel, glycated hemoglobin, and blood pressure measurement.","Physical fitness and sedentary behavior are independent predictors of all-cause mortality regardless of BMI or body composition, underscoring the importance of an activity history.","Staging tools such as the AACE/ACE complication-based system (stage 0 with no complications, stages 1-2 with mild-moderate or severe complications) and the Edmonton Obesity Staging System (graded 0-4 across medical, functional, and psychological domains, independent of BMI) help identify high-risk patients beyond BMI alone.","As BMI rises by 5 kg/m2, cancer mortality is estimated to increase by about 10%, and obesity has reportedly overtaken smoking as the leading risk factor for cancer in some countries.","Not every patient meeting a BMI obesity threshold requires treatment, but those with obesity-related complications and comorbidities should be managed proactively."],"pathophysiology":"Obesity arises from a chronic imbalance in which energy intake exceeds energy expenditure, leading to expansion of adipose depots. It is a highly heterogeneous condition strongly influenced by underlying genetic variation, with environmental factors ubiquitous in developed and developing countries; in some individuals it is attributable entirely to genetic causes. Insulin resistance is one of the earliest complications: muscle (glucose disposal) and liver (glucose production) become less responsive to insulin, prompting compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Two main hypotheses link adiposity to insulin resistance—macrophage- and inflammatory-cell products in obese adipose tissue disturbing insulin action, and the limited storage capacity of adipose tissue causing ectopic lipid deposition in muscle and liver (human data are stronger for the ectopic-fat mechanism). The metabolic, endocrine, and cardiovascular consequences may stem from chronic overnutrition and ectopic fat rather than fat mass per se, whereas certain complications (osteoarthritis, reflux esophagitis, obstructive sleep apnea) relate more directly to mass and volume effects.","additionalDifferentials":["Polycystic ovarian syndrome","Hypothyroidism","Cushing's syndrome","Hypothalamic disease","Monogenic/syndromic obesity (e.g., Bardet-Biedl syndrome, POMC, PCSK1, or leptin receptor deficiency)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Drug-induced weight gain (diabetes agents such as insulin, sulfonylureas, thiazolidinediones; steroid hormones; antipsychotics such as clozapine, olanzapine, risperidone; lithium; tricyclic antidepressants, MAO inhibitors, paroxetine, mirtazapine; antiepileptics such as valproate, gabapentin, carbamazepine)","Peripheral edema mimicking fat gain (NSAIDs, calcium channel blockers do not increase body fat)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting lipid profile (total, LDL, HDL cholesterol, triglycerides)","Glycated hemoglobin and chemistry panel for diabetes and metabolic screening","Blood pressure measurement","Waist circumference / waist-to-hip ratio for visceral adiposity","Targeted endocrine workup (thyroid, cortisol/Cushing evaluation) when secondary obesity is suspected","Sleep evaluation for obstructive sleep apnea when symptoms or risk factors present"],"redFlags":["Features suggesting a secondary cause (e.g., signs of Cushing's syndrome, hypothyroidism, hypothalamic disease) warranting further evaluation","Severe hypertriglyceridemia sufficient to risk pancreatitis","Symptoms of obstructive sleep apnea or hypoventilation (heavy snoring, daytime somnolence, dyspnea)","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension presentation in a patient with obesity","Rapidly progressive metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease/NASH with signs of fibrosis or cirrhosis"],"prognosis":"Obesity is a serious chronic disease associated with increased morbidity and mortality through multiple complications—type 2 diabetes, hypertension, dyslipidemia, atherothrombotic cardiovascular disease, stroke, heart failure (often diastolic dysfunction) and atrial fibrillation, obstructive sleep apnea, fatty liver disease that can progress to NASH and cirrhosis, several cancers, osteoarthritis, and gout. Risk generally rises with increasing levels of obesity and with central (visceral) fat distribution. Obesity also worsens outcomes from some infections (notably COVID-19) and raises susceptibility to wound infection and postoperative sepsis. Realistic initial treatment goals (around 8-10% weight loss over 6 months) can meaningfully improve obesity-related complications and quality of life.","pitfalls":["Relying on BMI alone, which does not measure body composition or fat distribution and can misclassify individual risk.","Using stigmatizing language (obesity, morbid obesity, fatness) that leaves patients feeling judged and can undermine engagement.","Overlooking secondary or drug-induced causes of weight gain before attributing obesity solely to lifestyle.","Initiating lifestyle change when the patient is not ready, which can cause frustration and hamper future efforts.","Assuming all patients with a BMI in the obesity range require treatment, when BMI is an imperfect measure of disease.","Neglecting waist circumference, which adds independent risk information about visceral adiposity."],"severityClassification":"WHO/BMI classification: underweight under 18.5; healthy weight 18.5-24.9; overweight 25.0-29.9 (increased disease risk); obesity class I 30.0-34.9 (high risk); class II 35.0-39.9 (very high risk); class III/extreme obesity 40 or greater (extremely high risk). Complication-based staging (AACE/ACE): stage 0 no complications, stage 1 one or more mild-moderate complications, stage 2 at least one severe complication. The Edmonton Obesity Staging System grades 0-4 across medical, functional, and psychological domains independent of BMI.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3187","Harrison 22e p.3188","Harrison 22e p.3189","Harrison 22e p.3190"],"crossLinks":[]},"evaluation_of_liver_function":{"name":"Evaluation of Liver Function","system":"Hepatobiliary / Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Liver function tests","LFTs","Liver biochemical tests","Hepatic panel"],"chapter":348,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2633","clinicalPearls":["Serum biochemical tests labeled 'liver function tests' are useful to detect liver disease, distinguish disorder types, gauge severity, and follow treatment response, but most do not actually measure synthetic liver function; aminotransferases and alkaline phosphatase instead detect hepatocyte damage or impaired bile flow (Harrison 22e p.2633).","Tests should be used as a battery rather than singly; when more than one is abnormal, or abnormalities persist on serial testing, the probability of liver disease is high, whereas normal results make occult disease unlikely (Harrison 22e p.2633).","Total bilirubin normally runs about 1-1.5 mg/dL (95% of normals 0.2-0.9 mg/dL); if the direct fraction is under 15% of total, the bilirubin is considered essentially all indirect (unconjugated) (Harrison 22e p.2634).","Isolated unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia points to hemolysis or genetic conditions such as Gilbert's or Crigler-Najjar syndromes; in an otherwise healthy person without hemolysis it can be attributed to Gilbert's syndrome with no further workup needed (Harrison 22e p.2634).","Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia almost always implies hepatobiliary disease; because the rate-limiting step is canalicular transport rather than conjugation, the conjugated fraction can rise in any liver disease, including fulminant failure (Harrison 22e p.2634).","Aminotransferase elevations above 1000 IU/L occur almost exclusively with extensive hepatocellular injury: viral hepatitis, ischemic/hypoxic injury, or drug/toxin injury; levels up to ~300 IU/L are nonspecific (Harrison 22e p.2635).","The absolute aminotransferase level does not correlate with degree of liver cell damage and has no prognostic significance in acute hepatocellular disease; necrosis is not required for enzyme release, only increased membrane permeability (Harrison 22e p.2635).","An AST:ALT ratio over 2:1 suggests, and over 3:1 strongly suggests, alcohol-related liver disease; AST is rarely above 300 IU/L in alcoholic liver disease and ALT is often normal/low because of pyridoxal phosphate deficiency (Harrison 22e p.2635).","Alkaline phosphatase elevation greater than ~4x normal points to cholestatic disorders, infiltrative diseases (tumor, amyloidosis), or high-turnover bone disease such as Paget's; less than 3x elevation is nonspecific (Harrison 22e p.2635).","Because clotting factors (except factor VIII, made by endothelium) are made only in hepatocytes and turn over rapidly (factor VII half-life ~6 h), the prothrombin time/INR is the single best acute marker of hepatic synthetic function (Harrison 22e p.2636).","Any bilirubin in the urine is conjugated, since unconjugated bilirubin is albumin-bound and not filtered; bilirubinuria implies liver disease or obstructive jaundice and clears before serum bilirubin during recovery (Harrison 22e p.2634).","Ultrasonography is the first test when cholestasis is suspected (to assess for dilated ducts or gallstones) and is also the first study ordered when Budd-Chiari syndrome is suspected, using Doppler to assess vessel patency and flow direction (Harrison 22e p.2636)."],"pathophysiology":"Serum liver tests reflect distinct mechanisms rather than overall hepatic function. Aminotransferases (AST, ALT) leak from hepatocytes when cell-membrane permeability increases; ALT is comparatively liver-specific while AST is also present in cardiac and skeletal muscle, kidney, brain, pancreas, lung, leukocytes, and red cells. Cholestasis-associated enzymes (alkaline phosphatase, 5'-nucleotidase, GGT) rise with impaired bile flow; alkaline phosphatase and 5'-nucleotidase localize near the canalicular membrane, whereas GGT, found in endoplasmic reticulum and bile-duct epithelium, is more diffuse and less specific. Bilirubin, the end product of heme catabolism, circulates unconjugated and albumin-bound until hepatocellular uptake, intracellular binding, glucuronide conjugation, and canalicular secretion render it water-soluble and excretable; the rate-limiting step is canalicular transport of conjugated bilirubin. Biosynthetic capacity is reflected by albumin (hepatocyte-synthesized, half-life 18-20 days, so insensitive to acute injury) and the clotting factors (rapid turnover, making PT/INR a sensitive acute marker). Gamma globulins rise in chronic liver disease because the cirrhotic liver fails to clear gut-derived bacterial antigens, increasing antibody synthesis (Harrison 22e p.2634-2636).","additionalDifferentials":["Hepatocellular pattern (predominant aminotransferase elevation): viral hepatitis, ischemic/hypoxic hepatitis, drug- or toxin-induced injury, autoimmune hepatitis, nonalcoholic/metabolic-associated fatty liver disease, alcohol-related liver disease","Cholestatic pattern (predominant alkaline phosphatase/bilirubin elevation): bile duct obstruction by stone, stricture, or tumor; primary biliary cholangitis; sclerosing cholangitis; drug-induced cholestasis","Isolated unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia: hemolysis, Gilbert's syndrome, Crigler-Najjar syndrome","Isolated conjugated hyperbilirubinemia without other test abnormalities: Dubin-Johnson and Rotor syndromes","Isolated alkaline phosphatase elevation: infiltrative liver disease, Paget's bone disease, Hodgkin's disease, hyperthyroidism, diabetes, congestive heart failure, amyloidosis"],"infectionMimics":["AIDS cholangiopathy due to cytomegalovirus or cryptosporidial infection causing marked alkaline phosphatase elevation","Hepatic tuberculosis with biliary involvement","Sepsis producing an intrahepatic cholestatic pattern"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hypoalbuminemia from protein malnutrition, protein-losing enteropathy, or nephrotic syndrome mimicking the low albumin of chronic liver disease","Vitamin K deficiency or warfarin therapy prolonging the prothrombin time independent of hepatic synthetic failure","Bone-origin alkaline phosphatase elevation (Paget's disease, growing children, late pregnancy from placental isoenzyme) mimicking hepatic cholestasis","Postprandial intestinal alkaline phosphatase rise in blood group O and B individuals"],"additionalInvestigations":["Battery of bilirubin (with fractionation), AST, ALT, alkaline phosphatase, albumin, and prothrombin time/INR; repeat over days to weeks for a diagnostic pattern","Fractionation of alkaline phosphatase, or 5'-nucleotidase or GGT, to confirm hepatic versus bone origin of an isolated alkaline phosphatase elevation","Serum globulin isotypes: IgG in autoimmune hepatitis, IgM in primary biliary cholangitis, IgA in alcoholic liver disease","Etiologic workup for chronically abnormal tests: hepatitis C antibody, hepatitis B surface antigen, iron studies/ferritin, ANA and serum protein electrophoresis, ceruloplasmin if under 40, antimitochondrial antibody, and ultrasound for fatty liver","Blood ammonia (limited value; poor correlation with encephalopathy severity, though arterial levels correlate with outcome in fulminant failure)","Liver biopsy (percutaneous with ultrasound guidance, or transjugular when ascites or prolonged INR present) for unexplained hepatocellular disease, hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, indeterminate lesions, fever of unknown origin, or lymphoma staging","Noninvasive fibrosis assessment: FibroTest/FibroSure, Enhanced Liver Fibrosis (ELF) test, FIB-4 index, transient elastography (FibroScan), and magnetic resonance elastography","Ultrasonography (first-line for suspected cholestasis or Budd-Chiari), with CT, MRCP, or ERCP as directed"],"redFlags":["Aminotransferases above 1000 IU/L indicating extensive hepatocellular injury (viral, ischemic, or toxin/drug)","Prothrombin time prolonged more than 5 seconds above control and uncorrected by parenteral vitamin K, a poor prognostic sign in acute viral and other liver disease","Albumin below 3 g/dL in apparent hepatitis raising concern for underlying chronic liver disease","Elevated total bilirubin in drug-induced liver injury signaling more severe injury","Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, which nearly always implies hepatobiliary disease and can occur even in fulminant liver failure"],"prognosis":"Individual tests carry prognostic weight: in viral hepatitis a higher serum bilirubin reflects greater hepatocellular damage, total bilirubin correlates with poor outcome in alcoholic hepatitis and with prognosis in primary biliary cholangitis, and an elevated bilirubin signals more severe drug-induced injury. A prothrombin time prolonged more than 5 seconds above control and not corrected by parenteral vitamin K is a poor prognostic indicator in acute viral and other liver diseases, and elevated arterial ammonia correlates with outcome in fulminant hepatic failure. Bilirubin, INR, creatinine, albumin, and sodium are integrated into the MELD 3.0 score used to gauge hepatic decompensation and prioritize transplant allocation (Harrison 22e p.2634-2636).","pitfalls":["Liver tests lack sensitivity and specificity; results can be normal in serious liver disease and abnormal in non-hepatic disease, and rarely indicate a specific diagnosis on their own (Harrison 22e p.2633).","Relying on the absolute aminotransferase value for prognosis is an error, since it does not correlate with the degree of damage in acute hepatocellular disease (Harrison 22e p.2635).","Except with isolated hyperbilirubinemia, fractionating bilirubin rarely identifies the cause of jaundice (Harrison 22e p.2634).","Alkaline phosphatase level does not distinguish intrahepatic from extrahepatic cholestasis (Harrison 22e p.2635).","Albumin is insensitive to acute or mild hepatic dysfunction owing to its long half-life and is not specific for liver disease; it should not be ordered to screen patients without suspected liver disease (Harrison 22e p.2636).","Liver biopsy should not be the first procedure for cholestasis; the biliary tree must first be evaluated for obstruction, and biopsy is subject to sampling error in focal disease (Harrison 22e p.2636).","Blood ammonia correlates poorly with both presence and severity of encephalopathy and with hepatic function, and can be elevated from portal shunting despite near-normal liver function (Harrison 22e p.2636)."],"severityClassification":"Liver test patterns are categorized by pattern recognition (Harrison Table 348-1): hemolysis/Gilbert's (isolated indirect bilirubin, no bilirubinuria, otherwise normal); acute hepatocellular necrosis (aminotransferases often above 500 IU/L with ALT exceeding AST, bilirubinuria, PT prognostic); chronic hepatocellular disorders (aminotransferases usually below 300 IU/L, often low albumin and prolonged PT not corrected by vitamin K); alcoholic hepatitis/cirrhosis (AST:ALT above 2); and intra/extrahepatic cholestasis or infiltrative disease (alkaline phosphatase often above 4x normal). Disease severity in chronic liver disease/cirrhosis is graded by the Child-Pugh classification and, for transplant prioritization, the MELD 3.0 score (bilirubin, INR, creatinine, albumin, sodium) (Harrison 22e p.2636-2637).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2633","Harrison 22e p.2634","Harrison 22e p.2635","Harrison 22e p.2636","Harrison 22e p.2637"],"crossLinks":[]},"extradural_hematoma":{"name":"Extradural haematoma","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["EDH","epidural haematoma","extradural haemorrhage"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically follows a temporal or temporoparietal skull fracture that tears the middle meningeal artery, often in younger patients after a blow to the side of the head","The textbook lucid interval, where an initial brief loss of consciousness is followed by a period of apparent recovery and then rapid deterioration, is a warning of an expanding arterial collection","On CT the haematoma is typically biconvex (lens-shaped) and does not usually cross suture lines because the dura is firmly attached there","Deterioration can be abrupt as the clot enlarges, producing a falling conscious level, contralateral weakness, and an ipsilateral dilated pupil from uncal herniation","Prompt surgical evacuation of a significant symptomatic clot can be life-saving and, if performed before herniation, is often associated with good recovery","Small extradural collections without significant mass effect in a neurologically intact patient may be monitored closely with repeat imaging","Venous extradural collections (from a dural sinus or diploic bleed) can evolve more slowly than classic arterial bleeds","Because deterioration may be sudden, any patient with a skull fracture and altered consciousness needs urgent imaging and neurosurgical discussion"],"pathophysiology":"An extradural haematoma is a collection of blood between the inner table of the skull and the dura mater. It most often arises when a skull fracture lacerates a meningeal artery, characteristically the middle meningeal artery in the temporal region, producing brisk arterial bleeding that strips the dura from the bone. Because the dura is tightly adherent at sutures, the clot forms a biconvex shape confined by these attachments. Rapid expansion raises intracranial pressure and displaces brain tissue, culminating in transtentorial (uncal) herniation with brainstem compression if not relieved.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute subdural haematoma","Traumatic intracerebral haemorrhage or contusion","Diffuse axonal injury","Subarachnoid haemorrhage","Depressed skull fracture with underlying injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent non-contrast CT head to confirm the biconvex collection, clot volume, and midline shift","CT bone windows to define the associated skull fracture","Coagulation profile and full blood count","Serial neurological observations (including GCS and pupils) to detect early deterioration"],"redFlags":["Lucid interval followed by declining consciousness, heralding rapid clot expansion and impending herniation","Ipsilateral fixed dilated pupil with contralateral hemiparesis indicating uncal herniation","Cushing response (hypertension with bradycardia) signalling raised intracranial pressure","Any drop in GCS or new focal deficit after head injury with a temporal fracture"],"prognosis":"Outcome is often excellent when a significant clot is evacuated before herniation, since the underlying brain may be relatively undamaged; however, delay to surgery after signs of herniation markedly worsens survival and neurological recovery.","pitfalls":["Being falsely reassured by the lucid interval and delaying imaging or transfer","Underestimating the speed of arterial bleeding and the potential for sudden collapse","Failing to correct coagulopathy urgently before or during evacuation","Discharging a head-injured patient with a skull fracture without adequate observation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"facial_fractures":{"name":"Facial fractures","system":"Plastic / Reconstructive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["maxillofacial fractures","midface fracture","Le Fort fracture","zygomatic fracture","orbital fracture"],"chapter":null,"source":"Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery / standard references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Facial fractures often accompany high-energy trauma, so assessment follows trauma principles with airway, cervical spine, and associated head and neck injuries addressed first","Midface fractures are classically described by the Le Fort patterns (I, II, III) reflecting different levels of maxillary separation, and higher patterns can produce a mobile or displaced midface","Zygomaticomaxillary complex fractures may cause flattening of the cheek, trismus, altered infraorbital sensation, and a step at the orbital rim","Orbital floor (blowout) fractures can trap the inferior rectus and cause diplopia, restricted upgaze, enophthalmos, and infraorbital numbness","Cerebrospinal fluid rhinorrhoea, a nasoethmoidal injury, or telecanthus points to fractures extending toward the skull base and cribriform region","CT of the facial skeleton is the imaging standard for defining fracture patterns and planning reconstruction","Treatment principles are restoring facial height, width, and projection, re-establishing occlusion, and rigid fixation of key buttresses, with surgery timed once swelling allows","A dental and occlusal assessment is essential, as restoring the pre-injury bite guides reduction of maxillary and mandibular fractures"],"pathophysiology":"The facial skeleton is organised into vertical and horizontal buttresses that transmit and dissipate forces of mastication and absorb trauma. Fractures occur when applied force exceeds bone strength, and the pattern reflects the direction and energy of impact and the relative weakness of certain regions such as the orbital floor and the Le Fort lines. High-energy injuries can separate the midface from the skull base along these predictable planes. Associated soft tissue swelling, haemorrhage, and displaced bone fragments can compromise the airway, the orbit and globe, cranial nerves, and the lacrimal and paranasal sinus systems, and disruption near the cribriform plate can breach the dura and cause CSF leak.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated nasal fracture","Isolated mandibular fracture","Dentoalveolar injury without facial skeleton fracture","Traumatic globe injury or retrobulbar haemorrhage","Temporal bone or skull base fracture","Soft tissue contusion and haematoma without fracture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT of the facial skeleton with fine cuts and three-dimensional reconstruction","Ophthalmological assessment including visual acuity and forced duction testing when orbital involvement is suspected","Assessment for CSF rhinorrhoea (e.g. beta-2 transferrin) with suspected skull base extension","Dental and occlusal evaluation, with panoramic imaging for the mandible","Cranial nerve and infraorbital sensory examination","Trauma workup including cervical spine imaging and evaluation for head injury"],"redFlags":["Airway compromise from midface instability, haemorrhage, or swelling, particularly with high Le Fort injuries (emergency)","Visual loss, a tense proptotic orbit, or suspected retrobulbar haemorrhage requiring urgent decompression","Orbital fracture with a trapped muscle and the oculocardiac reflex (bradycardia, nausea) in children","CSF rhinorrhoea or other evidence of skull base and dural breach","Associated cervical spine or intracranial injury"],"prognosis":"With accurate reduction and fixation most facial fractures heal with good functional and aesthetic results and restored occlusion. Outcomes are less favourable with delayed treatment, comminution, or associated orbital and skull base injury, where diplopia, enophthalmos, malunion, sensory disturbance, and asymmetry may persist and require secondary correction.","pitfalls":["Focusing on obvious facial injury while missing airway compromise or cervical spine injury","Overlooking an orbital floor fracture with entrapment, especially the white-eyed blowout in children","Failing to detect and manage CSF leak in fractures extending to the skull base","Neglecting occlusion, resulting in malocclusion after fixation","Operating before periorbital swelling settles, compromising assessment and fixation","Missing retrobulbar haemorrhage, which can cause preventable blindness"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"failure_to_thrive":{"name":"Failure to Thrive","system":"Nutritional/Growth","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["faltering growth","growth faltering","weight faltering"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Failure to thrive describes inadequate physical growth in a young child, most often identified as weight gain that is poor for age or crosses downward through growth chart percentiles over time.","A single low measurement is less meaningful than the trajectory of serial measurements plotted on an appropriate growth chart.","The traditional split into organic and non-organic causes is artificial; many children have mixed contributors, and inadequate caloric intake is the final common pathway in most cases.","The three broad mechanisms are insufficient intake, inadequate absorption or increased losses, and increased energy requirements, and the history usually distinguishes among them.","A careful feeding, dietary, developmental, social, and psychosocial history plus observation of a feed often yields more than extensive laboratory testing.","Investigations should be guided by clues from the history and examination rather than performed as an indiscriminate panel.","Management prioritises adequate catch-up nutrition while addressing any medical, developmental, feeding, and psychosocial factors, often through a multidisciplinary approach.","Signs of neglect, abuse, or a threatening environment must be considered, but most children with faltering growth are not victims of maltreatment."],"pathophysiology":"Growth requires that energy and nutrient intake exceed the demands of maintenance and activity. Faltering growth arises when this balance is disturbed: intake may be insufficient because of feeding difficulties, poor appetite, incorrect feed preparation, or limited food availability; nutrients may be inadequately absorbed or lost through malabsorption, chronic diarrhoea, or vomiting; or requirements may be raised by chronic illness such as cardiac, respiratory, renal, or inflammatory disease. Prolonged deficits deplete energy stores and, if sustained, can impair linear growth and, in severe or early cases, brain development. Psychosocial and caregiving factors frequently interact with these physiological mechanisms.","additionalDifferentials":["Constitutional growth patterns and familial short stature (not true failure to thrive)","Malabsorption (e.g., coeliac disease, cystic fibrosis)","Chronic cardiac, respiratory, renal, or gastrointestinal disease","Endocrine disorders","Chronic or recurrent infection","Neglect or psychosocial deprivation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed dietary and feeding assessment with observation of a feed","Plotting of serial weight, length/height, and head circumference","Targeted first-line tests (full blood count, electrolytes, urinalysis) guided by findings","Coeliac serology and stool studies when malabsorption is suspected","Further specific testing directed by history and examination"],"redFlags":["Severe or rapidly progressive weight loss or wasting","Signs of dehydration or micronutrient deficiency","Developmental regression or neurological signs","Features suggesting abuse or neglect","Symptoms or signs pointing to serious underlying organic disease"],"prognosis":"With timely recognition and adequate nutritional rehabilitation most children achieve catch-up growth and do well, but prolonged or severe undernutrition, especially in infancy, can be associated with lasting effects on growth and neurodevelopment.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing failure to thrive from a single measurement rather than the growth trend","Ordering broad laboratory panels instead of history-directed testing","Assuming an organic cause and overlooking feeding technique, caloric adequacy, or psychosocial factors","Mislabelling normal constitutional or genetically small children as failing to thrive"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"familial_mediterranean_fever_and_other":{"name":"Familial Mediterranean Fever and Other Hereditary Autoinflammatory Diseases","system":"Immune-mediated / Rheumatologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["FMF","Hereditary recurrent fever syndromes","Autoinflammatory diseases","Periodic fever syndromes"],"chapter":381,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2934","clinicalPearls":["FMF is the prototype hereditary autoinflammatory disease: recurrent self-limited fever with serosal, synovial, or cutaneous inflammation, distinguished from autoimmune disease by the relative absence of high-titer autoantibodies or antigen-specific T cells and a predominant role of the innate immune system (Harrison 22e p.2934).","Roughly 90% of patients have their first attack by age 20; typical episodes last about 24-72 hours, with arthritic attacks tending to run longer (Harrison 22e p.2934).","Over 90% of FMF patients have abdominal (peritoneal) attacks; laparotomy reveals a sterile neutrophil-rich peritoneal exudate, and ascites is rare (Harrison 22e p.2934).","Erysipelas-like erythema, a raised erythematous rash typically on the dorsum of the foot, ankle, or lower leg, is the most characteristic skin finding and occurs most often in M694V homozygotes (Harrison 22e p.2934).","FMF arthritis is usually monoarticular (knee, ankle, hip) with large sterile neutrophil-rich effusions and little erythema or warmth; radiographic changes are rare even after repeated attacks (Harrison 22e p.2934).","The MEFV gene encodes pyrin (~95 kDa); its N-terminal PYRIN domain nucleates the pyrin inflammasome that activates caspase-1, driving IL-1beta and IL-18 secretion and gasdermin-D-mediated pyroptosis (Harrison 22e p.2934).","FMF-associated MEFV mutations are gain-of-function, lowering the threshold for pyrin inflammasome activation; severe disease-associated mutations cluster in exon 10, and M694V homozygosity predicts earlier onset and more arthritis, rash, and amyloidosis (Harrison 22e p.2935-2936).","The principal hereditary recurrent fever syndromes are differentiated by attack length, ethnicity, and features: TRAPS (TNFRSF1A, dominant, attacks often >7 days, migratory myalgia with overlying erythema, periorbital edema/conjunctivitis), HIDS/MKD (MVK, recessive, painful cervical adenopathy and oral ulcers), and the NLRP3-associated cryopyrinopathies FCAS, MWS, and NOMID (Harrison 22e p.2935-2937).","Cryopyrinopathies form a spectrum: FCAS (cold-induced urticaria-like rash, fever, arthralgia, conjunctivitis), MWS (urticarial rash not cold-induced, progressive sensorineural hearing loss), and NOMID/neonatal-onset multisystem inflammatory disease (chronic aseptic meningitis, characteristic arthropathy, rash) (Harrison 22e p.2937).","About one-third of clinically typical NOMID patients lack germline NLRP3 mutations but are mosaic for somatic NLRP3 mutations; somatic NLRP3 mosaicism has rarely been reported in Schnitzler's syndrome (middle-age recurrent fever, urticaria, monoclonal IgM gammopathy, abnormal bone remodeling) (Harrison 22e p.2936-2937).","PFAPA (periodic fever with aphthous stomatitis, pharyngitis, cervical adenitis) is the most common periodic fever syndrome of childhood, notable for clock-like regularity, familial but non-Mendelian clustering, and a tendency to resolve by early adulthood (Harrison 22e p.2937).","Over 60 monogenic autoinflammatory diseases are now recognized, grouped mechanistically into inflammasomopathies (FMF, HIDS, CAPS, PAPA, NLRC4-related), type I interferonopathies (STING-associated vasculopathy, COPA syndrome, Aicardi-Goutieres, CANDLE), and relopathies affecting NF-kB / TNF-induced cell death (Harrison 22e p.2937)."],"pathophysiology":"These disorders reflect dysregulated innate immunity rather than adaptive autoimmunity. In FMF, gain-of-function MEFV mutations lower the activation threshold of the pyrin inflammasome; pyrin (via its PYRIN domain and an adaptor) assembles a macromolecular complex that activates caspase-1, producing IL-1beta and IL-18 and triggering gasdermin-D-mediated pyroptosis. Pyrin normally senses bacterial toxins that inactivate RhoA GTPase as part of host defense. The other syndromes map to specific innate pathways: TRAPS arises from misfolding, aggregation, and retention of mutant TNFR1 with ligand-independent kinase activation, mitochondrial reactive oxygen species, and proinflammatory cytokine release; HIDS/MKD results from mevalonate kinase deficiency that impairs isoprenoid (geranylgeranyl pyrophosphate) synthesis, mislocalizing and inactivating RhoA and thereby activating the pyrin inflammasome; FCAS/MWS/NOMID arise from NLRP3 (cryopyrin) mutations forming an overactive NLRP3 inflammasome with excess IL-1beta and IL-18. Broader categories include interferonopathies driven by aberrant intracellular nucleic-acid sensing (excess type I interferon) and relopathies that dysregulate NF-kB activation or TNF-induced cell death. Untreated chronic inflammation can lead to systemic AA amyloidosis from deposition of a serum amyloid A fragment.","additionalDifferentials":["Other hereditary recurrent fever syndromes (TRAPS, HIDS/MKD, FCAS, MWS, NOMID)","PFAPA syndrome","Systemic-onset juvenile rheumatoid arthritis / adult Still's disease","Porphyria","Hereditary angioedema","Inflammatory bowel disease","Gynecologic disorders (in women presenting with recurrent abdominal pain)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Systemic-onset juvenile rheumatoid arthritis / adult Still's disease","Porphyria","Hereditary angioedema","Inflammatory bowel disease","Other monogenic autoinflammatory syndromes"],"additionalInvestigations":["MEFV genetic testing (severe mutations cluster in exon 10; ~70% of clinically typical FMF have two mutations in trans; expert-curated mutation databases such as Infevers aid interpretation)","Acute-phase markers during attacks: elevated ESR, leukocytosis, thrombocytosis (in children), and raised C-reactive protein, fibrinogen, haptoglobin, and serum immunoglobulins","Urinalysis (transient albuminuria/hematuria during attacks; persistent proteinuria between attacks should raise concern for amyloidosis)","Renal or rectal biopsy to confirm suspected AA amyloidosis","Genotype-specific testing for other syndromes: TNFRSF1A (TRAPS), MVK with markedly elevated urinary mevalonate during attacks (HIDS/MKD), NLRP3 (cryopyrinopathies, including testing for somatic mosaicism)","Imaging during attacks: chest radiograph (atelectasis/effusion in pleural attacks), upright abdominal radiograph or CT (air-fluid levels, small amount of free fluid)"],"redFlags":["Persistent proteinuria between attacks, suggesting developing AA amyloidosis","Severe hyperpyrexia or febrile seizures in infants","Protracted febrile myalgia lasting several weeks","In cryopyrinopathies, sensorineural hearing loss (MWS) or chronic aseptic meningitis and progressive visual/CNS involvement (NOMID)","Features of secondary vasculitis (IgA vasculitis, polyarteritis nodosa) occurring at increased frequency in FMF","Adult-onset relapsing polychondritis, Sweet's syndrome, vasculitis, or myelodysplasia in older men suggesting VEXAS syndrome"],"prognosis":"FMF attacks are self-limited and recur with variable frequency over a lifetime; episodes may even be associated with remission during pregnancy. The major determinant of long-term outcome is systemic AA amyloidosis, historically a common and serious complication. Amyloidosis risk is increased by the M694V homozygous genotype, positive family history, the SAA1 genotype, male gender, non-adherence to therapy, and national origin (paralleling infant mortality rate). Among the related syndromes, untreated TRAPS leads to amyloidosis in about 15% of cases, MWS in roughly 25%, while amyloidosis is uncommon in FCAS and is a late complication in NOMID. PFAPA generally has a benign course with resolution by early adulthood. NOMID is the most severe cryopyrinopathy.","pitfalls":["A positive family history can be elicited in only about half of cases (especially where families are small), so its absence does not exclude FMF; FMF can also occur without known Mediterranean ancestry.","Only about 70% of clinically typical FMF patients carry two identifiable MEFV mutations in trans, and many clinical cases have only a single demonstrable mutation, so genetic testing cannot rule out the diagnosis.","Certain variants are functional polymorphisms rather than disease-causing: E148Q (common in some Asian populations) often affects overall inflammation more than causing clinical FMF, and TNFRSF1A R92Q and P46L may behave as polymorphisms in TRAPS.","Serum IgD elevation is neither sensitive nor specific for HIDS/MKD: disease activity does not track IgD levels, some FMF/TRAPS patients have modestly raised IgD, and some MVK-mutation patients have normal IgD (markedly elevated urinary mevalonate during attacks is more reliable).","About one-third of NOMID patients lack germline NLRP3 mutations and require testing for somatic mosaicism, which can be missed on standard germline sequencing."],"severityClassification":"No formal numeric severity scale is given; the hereditary recurrent fevers are clinically stratified by syndrome (FMF, TRAPS, HIDS/MKD, and the NLRP3-associated spectrum of FCAS [mildest] -> MWS -> NOMID [most severe]) and by attack length, organ involvement, and genotype. In FMF, M694V homozygosity marks more severe disease (earlier onset; higher frequency of arthritis, rash, and amyloidosis). Development of systemic AA amyloidosis defines the severe end of the disease spectrum.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2934","Harrison 22e p.2935","Harrison 22e p.2936","Harrison 22e p.2937"],"crossLinks":[]},"fanconi_syndrome":{"name":"Fanconi syndrome","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["renal Fanconi syndrome","proximal tubular dysfunction syndrome","De Toni-Debre-Fanconi syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Fanconi syndrome is a generalised defect of proximal tubular reabsorption, so multiple solutes normally reclaimed there are lost into the urine at once","The hallmark laboratory picture combines glycosuria with normal blood glucose, phosphaturia with hypophosphataemia, aminoaciduria, uricosuria, and a proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis","Phosphate and vitamin D handling are disturbed, producing metabolic bone disease that presents as rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults","In children the commonest cause is inherited cystinosis, whereas in adults acquired causes such as drugs, heavy metals, and monoclonal light chains predominate","Bicarbonate wasting causes a hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis, and potassium wasting frequently leads to hypokalaemia","Recognising the full constellation is important because finding one abnormality (e.g. isolated glycosuria) should prompt a search for the others","Treatment is directed at the underlying cause where possible, together with replacement of the wasted solutes: alkali, phosphate, potassium, and active vitamin D as needed"],"pathophysiology":"Fanconi syndrome results from global dysfunction of the proximal convoluted tubule, the segment responsible for reabsorbing the bulk of filtered glucose, amino acids, phosphate, uric acid, bicarbonate, and low-molecular-weight proteins. When proximal transport fails, all of these are wasted in the urine, giving the characteristic combination of glycosuria with euglycaemia, generalised aminoaciduria, hypophosphataemia, hypouricaemia, proximal renal tubular acidosis from bicarbonate loss, and tubular proteinuria. Impaired proximal conversion of vitamin D and phosphate depletion drive the bone disease. Underlying mechanisms include inherited defects of tubular transporters or metabolism (such as cystinosis or Wilson disease), toxic injury from drugs and heavy metals, and deposition of filtered monoclonal light chains, all of which impair the energy-dependent transport machinery of the proximal tubule.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated proximal renal tubular acidosis without full Fanconi features","Diabetes mellitus with glycosuria from hyperglycaemia","Primary hypophosphataemic rickets","Multiple myeloma or other plasma cell dyscrasia","Heavy metal or drug-induced tubular toxicity","Chronic interstitial nephritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Paired urine and blood glucose to demonstrate glycosuria despite normal glycaemia","Serum and urine phosphate, potassium, bicarbonate, and uric acid to document tubular wasting","Urine amino acid screen and low-molecular-weight (tubular) protein measurement","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis with free light chains to exclude a plasma cell disorder in adults","Assessment for underlying causes such as cystinosis, Wilson disease, or nephrotoxic drug exposure","Bone imaging or biochemistry when rickets or osteomalacia is suspected"],"redFlags":["Severe hypophosphataemia causing muscle weakness or respiratory compromise","Progressive metabolic bone disease with fractures or deformity","Evidence of an underlying plasma cell dyscrasia in an adult","Declining glomerular filtration suggesting progression of the causative disease"],"prognosis":"Outlook depends on the cause: reversible drug- or toxin-induced Fanconi syndrome may resolve when the offending agent is removed, while inherited forms such as cystinosis and light-chain-related disease can progress to chronic kidney failure. Early replacement therapy prevents or lessens the associated bone disease and growth impairment.","pitfalls":["Dismissing glycosuria as diabetes without checking blood glucose or looking for other tubular losses","Missing an underlying paraproteinaemia in an adult presenting with proximal tubular dysfunction","Undertreating the bone disease by giving phosphate without adequate active vitamin D","Failing to review the medication list for a reversible tubulotoxic drug"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"fat_embolism_syndrome":{"name":"Fat embolism syndrome","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["FES","fat embolism"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Suspect the syndrome in a patient who develops respiratory distress, neurological change, and a petechial rash one to three days after long-bone or pelvic fractures","The classic triad is hypoxaemia, neurological dysfunction, and a petechial rash, though not all three are always present","The petechial rash is characteristically distributed over the upper trunk, axillae, neck, and conjunctivae and is a helpful clue when present","It is largely a clinical diagnosis of exclusion, since no single test is confirmatory","Management is supportive, centred on maintaining oxygenation and ventilation and treating respiratory failure","Early fracture immobilisation and timely fixation reduce the risk of developing the syndrome","Consider alternative causes of the same picture, particularly pulmonary embolism, pneumonia, and evolving brain injury"],"pathophysiology":"Fat embolism syndrome typically follows fractures of long bones or the pelvis, or orthopaedic instrumentation, when marrow fat enters the venous circulation. A mechanical component arises as fat globules occlude pulmonary and systemic capillaries, while a biochemical component follows as circulating free fatty acids are released and produce a toxic inflammatory injury to the pulmonary capillary endothelium. The result is impaired gas exchange resembling acute lung injury, cerebral microembolism causing confusion and reduced consciousness, and dermal capillary involvement producing the characteristic petechiae. Symptoms characteristically appear after a latent interval of roughly one to three days.","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary thromboembolism","Aspiration pneumonia or hospital-acquired pneumonia","Acute respiratory distress syndrome from other causes","Evolving traumatic brain injury","Sepsis with disseminated intravascular coagulation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas demonstrating hypoxaemia","Chest imaging that may show diffuse infiltrates as lung injury evolves","Full blood count that may reveal thrombocytopenia and anaemia","Imaging to exclude pulmonary embolism when the diagnosis is unclear","Neuroimaging where cerebral involvement or alternative brain pathology is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly worsening hypoxaemia progressing towards acute respiratory distress syndrome","Deteriorating conscious level from cerebral involvement","New petechial rash with respiratory and neurological change after long-bone fracture","Haemodynamic instability suggesting massive embolic load","Failure to improve prompting reconsideration of pulmonary embolism or sepsis"],"prognosis":"Most cases are self-limiting with supportive care and resolve over days, but severe presentations with marked respiratory failure or cerebral involvement carry higher morbidity and can be fatal.","pitfalls":["Attributing early hypoxaemia solely to the injury and missing the evolving syndrome","Assuming the absence of a rash excludes the diagnosis","Failing to exclude pulmonary embolism, which requires different treatment","Delaying fracture stabilisation, which increases risk","Over-investigating for a confirmatory test rather than supporting the patient"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"febrile_seizure":{"name":"Febrile seizure","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["febrile convulsion","fever fit","simple febrile seizure","complex febrile seizure"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A seizure occurring with fever in children roughly 6 months to 5 years of age, without central nervous system infection or a metabolic cause, and not in a child with prior afebrile seizures.","Simple febrile seizures are generalised, last under 15 minutes, and do not recur within 24 hours; complex seizures are focal, prolonged, or recur within a day.","The diagnosis is clinical, and the main task is to exclude meningitis, encephalitis, and other acute causes of the fever.","Routine blood tests, neuroimaging, and EEG are not indicated for simple febrile seizures in an otherwise well child.","Lumbar puncture is considered when meningeal signs are present, in very young or incompletely immunised infants, or when the picture is atypical.","Most febrile seizures are brief and self-terminating; management centres on reassurance, identifying the febrile illness, and safety advice for parents.","Prolonged seizures are treated as status epilepticus with benzodiazepine-based abortive therapy and airway support.","Antipyretics improve comfort but do not reliably prevent recurrence, and daily prophylactic anticonvulsants are generally not recommended for simple febrile seizures."],"pathophysiology":"Febrile seizures reflect the heightened seizure susceptibility of the developing brain when exposed to fever, likely mediated by fever-associated cytokines (such as interleukin-1 beta), the effect of raised temperature on neuronal excitability, and a genetic predisposition, as demonstrated by strong familial clustering. The rate of temperature rise and the immaturity of the young brain contribute, and in most children the tendency resolves with neurological maturation.","additionalDifferentials":["Meningitis or encephalitis","Epilepsy with a coincidental febrile illness","Rigors or shivering mistaken for a seizure","Metabolic disturbance (e.g. hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia)","Breath-holding spell","Intracranial pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assessment to identify and treat the source of fever","Blood glucose in any child who is drowsy or slow to recover","Lumbar puncture when CNS infection is suspected or the child is very young","Electrolytes only when clinically indicated (e.g. dehydration, atypical features)","EEG and neuroimaging reserved for complex or recurrent focal features or an abnormal neurological exam"],"redFlags":["Signs of meningitis or encephalitis (neck stiffness, altered consciousness, bulging fontanelle)","Prolonged seizure or febrile status epilepticus","Focal features, or focal neurological deficit after the seizure","Failure to return to baseline alertness within a reasonable period","Seizure outside the typical age range or in an unwell, toxic-appearing child"],"prognosis":"The outlook is excellent: most children have normal development and neurological outcome, though about a third experience a recurrence, and the increase in later epilepsy risk is small for simple febrile seizures.","pitfalls":["Assuming fever excludes meningitis and skipping evaluation in a young or unwell infant","Over-investigating a well child after a simple febrile seizure","Reassuring families that antipyretics prevent recurrence","Starting long-term anticonvulsants for simple febrile seizures unnecessarily"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"febrile_uti_children":{"name":"Febrile Urinary Tract Infection in Children","system":"Genitourinary","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["febrile UTI","paediatric pyelonephritis","upper urinary tract infection in children"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Fever with a urinary tract infection in a young child usually indicates upper tract involvement (pyelonephritis) and should be taken seriously because of the risk of renal scarring.","In infants and preverbal children, features are non-specific, so urinary tract infection must be considered in any young child with unexplained fever.","A properly collected urine specimen is essential; contamination from bag or non-clean-catch samples is a frequent source of misleading results.","Diagnosis rests on the combination of pyuria and a significant growth of a single uropathogen on culture from an appropriately obtained specimen.","Escherichia coli is the predominant pathogen, and prompt appropriate antibiotic treatment reduces the likelihood of renal injury.","Imaging, typically renal and bladder ultrasound, is used to detect structural abnormalities, with further studies such as voiding cystourethrography reserved for selected children.","Vesicoureteral reflux and other urinary tract abnormalities predispose to recurrent infection and scarring and should be considered, especially after atypical or recurrent infections.","Recurrent febrile infections and any resulting renal scarring can contribute to later hypertension and reduced renal function, so follow-up matters."],"pathophysiology":"Most urinary tract infections are ascending: bowel-derived uropathogens, chiefly Escherichia coli, colonise the perineum, ascend the urethra, and multiply in the bladder. When bacteria and inflammation reach the kidney, pyelonephritis develops with fever and systemic signs. Bacterial virulence factors and host factors, including urinary stasis, incomplete bladder emptying, and vesicoureteral reflux that carries infected urine toward the kidney, promote upper tract infection. The inflammatory response within the renal parenchyma can injure tissue and, particularly with delayed treatment or recurrent episodes, lead to permanent scarring.","additionalDifferentials":["Other sources of occult fever (e.g., bacteraemia, pneumonia)","Viral illness with incidental contaminated urine sample","Vulvovaginitis or balanitis","Appendicitis or other intra-abdominal infection","Contaminated specimen giving a false-positive culture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis or dipstick (leucocyte esterase, nitrites) and microscopy for pyuria","Urine culture from an appropriately collected specimen","Inflammatory markers and blood culture when systemically unwell","Renal and bladder ultrasound","Voiding cystourethrography or renal scintigraphy in selected or recurrent cases"],"redFlags":["Toxic appearance, signs of sepsis, or haemodynamic instability","Very young infant with fever","Inability to tolerate oral fluids or oral antibiotics","Failure to improve on appropriate treatment","Known or suspected urinary tract abnormality or recurrent febrile infections"],"prognosis":"Most children with a promptly treated first febrile urinary tract infection recover fully, but recurrent infections, significant vesicoureteral reflux, or renal scarring carry a risk of long-term complications including hypertension and impaired renal function.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing infection from a contaminated bag specimen instead of a properly collected sample","Failing to consider urinary tract infection in an infant with unexplained fever","Treating asymptomatic bacteriuria as if it were true infection","Neglecting follow-up imaging or evaluation after atypical or recurrent febrile infections"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"femoral_hernia":{"name":"Femoral hernia","system":"GI / Abdominal wall / Surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["crural hernia","femoral canal hernia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as a groin lump located below and lateral to the pubic tubercle, in contrast to an inguinal hernia which lies above and medial to it","More common in women than men, particularly in older and multiparous women, though inguinal hernia remains the commonest groin hernia overall in both sexes","The femoral canal has a narrow, rigid neck bounded by the inguinal ligament, lacunar ligament, and femoral vein, which makes strangulation far more likely than with inguinal hernias","A high proportion of femoral hernias present as an emergency with incarceration or strangulation rather than as an elective referral","A Richter's hernia, where only part of the bowel wall is trapped, can strangulate while the lumen stays patent, so bowel obstruction may be absent despite ischaemia","Because of the high strangulation risk, femoral hernias should generally be repaired promptly rather than managed conservatively","Repair may be performed by low (crural), high (McEvedy), or laparoscopic approaches, closing the femoral canal","A small, deep femoral hernia can be easily missed on examination, especially in obese patients"],"pathophysiology":"A femoral hernia protrudes through the femoral canal, the medial-most compartment of the femoral sheath, below the inguinal ligament. The canal normally contains fat and a lymph node (node of Cloquet) and has an unyielding boundary. Raised intra-abdominal pressure forces peritoneum and abdominal contents through the femoral ring into this confined space. The rigidity and narrowness of the neck predispose trapped contents to incarceration and rapid compromise of blood supply, causing strangulation and bowel ischaemia. The sac may turn upward over the inguinal ligament, mimicking an inguinal hernia.","additionalDifferentials":["Inguinal hernia","Saphena varix","Enlarged inguinal or femoral lymph node","Lipoma of the groin","Femoral artery aneurysm","Psoas abscess or bursa","Ectopic testis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful clinical localisation relative to the pubic tubercle","Ultrasound of the groin to confirm the diagnosis and characterise contents","CT abdomen and pelvis in obstruction or when the diagnosis is unclear","Bloods including lactate if strangulation is suspected"],"redFlags":["Irreducible tender groin lump below the inguinal ligament indicating incarceration or strangulation (surgical emergency)","Signs of small bowel obstruction with a femoral lump","Localised ischaemia despite absent obstruction in a Richter's-type hernia","Systemic sepsis or peritonism suggesting perforation of strangulated bowel"],"prognosis":"Prompt elective or urgent repair has a good outcome, but the high emergency presentation rate and frequent need for bowel resection mean femoral hernias carry greater morbidity and mortality than inguinal hernias, especially in elderly patients.","pitfalls":["Confusing a femoral hernia with an inguinal hernia and adopting a conservative approach","Missing a small femoral hernia in an obese patient presenting with unexplained bowel obstruction","Overlooking a Richter's hernia because bowel obstruction is absent despite strangulation","Delaying repair given the disproportionately high strangulation risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"fibromyalgia":{"name":"Fibromyalgia","system":"Musculoskeletal / Rheumatologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["FM","fibromyalgia syndrome"],"chapter":385,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2963","clinicalPearls":["Defined by chronic widespread musculoskeletal pain and tenderness, with pain present most of the day on most days for at least 3 months and distributed both above and below the waist, on both sides, and involving the axial skeleton (Harrison 22e p.2963).","Beyond pain, patients commonly report fatigue, unrefreshing sleep, cognitive dysfunction (\"fibro fog\"), anxiety, and depression, which may affect function and quality of life as much as or more than the pain itself (Harrison 22e p.2963-2964).","Elevated evoked pain sensitivity may be detected clinically by pain on blood-pressure cuff inflation or skin-roll tenderness; a formal tender-point exam applies ~4 kg/m2 of thumbnail pressure (enough to blanch the nail bed) at defined musculotendinous sites (Harrison 22e p.2963).","Older ACR criteria required 11 of 18 tender points; newer 2016 criteria abandon tender-point counting and instead use a Widespread Pain Index plus Symptom Severity Score (e.g., WPI >=7 and SSS >=5, or WPI 4-6 and SSS >=9, with generalized pain in at least 4 of 5 regions for >=3 months) (Harrison 22e p.2963-2964).","Tenderness is a continuous variable; strict application of a categorical threshold is not required for diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2963).","Peripheral pain generators such as arthritis, bursitis, tendinitis, neuropathies, joint hypermobility, and scoliosis can trigger and perpetuate the centrally maintained widespread pain of FM (Harrison 22e p.2963).","FM is one of the chronic overlapping pain conditions and frequently coexists with myalgic encephalitis/chronic fatigue syndrome, temporomandibular disorder, chronic headaches, irritable bowel syndrome, and interstitial cystitis/painful bladder syndrome, likely sharing central mechanisms (Harrison 22e p.2963).","The lifetime prevalence of mood disorders in FM approaches 80%; depression is neither necessary nor sufficient for diagnosis, but screening for depressed mood and anhedonia is important (Harrison 22e p.2964).","Symptom onset and flares often cluster around periods of perceived stress, and there is a high prevalence of prior interpersonal or other violence exposure; consider posttraumatic stress disorder where relevant (Harrison 22e p.2964).","Some patients meeting FM criteria have a small-fiber neuropathy, and early reports suggest possible autoimmune changes in the peripheral nervous system in subsets of patients (Harrison 22e p.2964).","Functional assessment across physical, mental, and social domains is central, since improved function (rather than complete pain elimination) is the principal measure of management success (Harrison 22e p.2964)."],"pathophysiology":"FM is regarded as a disorder in which the central nervous system maintains pain and the other core symptoms. Psychophysical testing shows altered afferent pain processing and impaired descending noxious inhibitory control, producing hyperalgesia and allodynia, and functional MRI demonstrates activation of pain-experiencing brain regions in response to stimuli that are innocuous in unaffected individuals. Pain perception is modulated by emotional and cognitive factors such as catastrophizing and perceived control. Genetic susceptibility appears polygenic, involving pathways that control pain and stress responses, including genes for metabolism, transport, and receptors of serotonin and other monoamines, with some genetic underpinnings shared across chronic pain conditions and overlapping with mood disorders. Peripheral pain generators (e.g., arthritis, bursitis, neuropathy, hypermobility) can serve as triggers that alter central pain processing (Harrison 22e p.2964).","additionalDifferentials":["Polymyalgia rheumatica","Inflammatory arthritis (rheumatoid arthritis, spondyloarthritides)","Connective tissue disease (systemic lupus erythematosus, Sjogren's syndrome)","Degenerative joint/spine/disk disease","Myofascial pain syndromes","Bursitis, tendinitis, repetitive strain injuries","Hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism","Hyperparathyroidism","Multiple sclerosis and neuropathic pain syndromes","Major depressive disorder","Drug-induced myalgia (statins, aromatase inhibitors)"],"infectionMimics":["Hepatitis C","HIV infection","Lyme disease","Parvovirus B19 infection","Epstein-Barr virus infection"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Polymyalgia rheumatica","Rheumatoid arthritis and spondyloarthritides","Systemic lupus erythematosus and Sjogren's syndrome","Hypo- or hyperthyroidism and hyperparathyroidism","Degenerative joint/spine/disk disease and myofascial pain syndromes","Neuropathic pain syndromes and multiple sclerosis","Major depressive disorder","Statin- or aromatase-inhibitor-induced myalgia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Routine screen: ESR or CRP, complete blood count, and thyroid-stimulating hormone (results are normal in uncomplicated FM)","Guided by history/exam: complete metabolic panel, ANA, anti-SSA/anti-SSB, rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP, creatine phosphokinase","Viral (e.g., hepatitis C, HIV) and bacterial (e.g., Lyme) serologies when clinically indicated","Radiographs used sparingly and only when inflammatory arthritis is suspected; spine MRI reserved for features of inflammatory spine disease or neurologic symptoms","Repeat testing is discouraged unless the symptom complex changes"],"redFlags":["Pain extending beyond what a known triggering or inflammatory condition could reasonably explain, signaling emergence of central FM-type pain requiring a different management focus","Objective inflammatory, neurologic, or systemic findings (abnormal ESR/CRP, focal neurologic deficits) that point toward an alternative or comorbid diagnosis rather than FM","Screen-positive major depression with anhedonia, and history suggestive of posttraumatic stress disorder, given high comorbidity and impact on outcome"],"prognosis":"FM is a chronic condition with substantial adverse effects on physical and social functioning and on health-related quality of life. Outcomes are best when there is prompt diagnosis, assessment of pain, function, and psychosocial context, patient education, and a partnership-based strategy directed at improving function rather than eliminating pain; illness behaviors such as frequent physician visits are to be discouraged while function-promoting behaviors are encouraged (Harrison 22e p.2964-2965).","pitfalls":["Failing to consider FM, leading to missed or delayed diagnosis since musculoskeletal pain is so common (Harrison 22e p.2964).","Over-investigating with repeated laboratory tests or, especially, spine MRI in the absence of inflammatory or neurologic features (Harrison 22e p.2964).","Continuing to treat only peripheral or inflammatory pain generators when central FM pain has emerged, instead of addressing central pain processing (Harrison 22e p.2964).","Setting complete pain elimination, rather than improved function and quality of life, as the treatment goal (Harrison 22e p.2965)."],"severityClassification":"Severity is captured by the 2016 diagnostic instrument combining the Widespread Pain Index (WPI, range 0-19) and the Symptom Severity Score (SSS, range 0-12, grading fatigue, unrefreshing waking, and cognitive symptoms plus selected somatic items). Diagnosis requires WPI >=7 and SSS >=5, or WPI 4-6 and SSS >=9, with generalized pain in at least 4 of 5 body regions, sustained for at least 3 months; higher WPI and SSS values reflect greater symptom burden (Harrison 22e p.2964).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2963","Harrison 22e p.2964","Harrison 22e p.2965"],"crossLinks":[]},"filariasis":{"name":"Filariasis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lymphatic filariasis","elephantiasis","Wuchereria bancrofti","onchocerciasis"],"chapter":240,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1819","clinicalPearls":["Eight filarial species infect humans, but four cause most symptomatic disease: Wuchereria bancrofti, Brugia malayi, Onchocerca volvulus, and Loa loa (Harrison 22e p.1819).","Filarial disease is characteristically more acute and intense in newly exposed travelers/transmigrants than in lifelong natives of endemic areas; infection usually requires repeated, prolonged exposure to infective larvae (Harrison 22e p.1819).","Lymphatic filariasis (W. bancrofti, B. malayi, B. timori) is widely distributed; W. bancrofti affects ~51 million people and B. malayi occurs primarily in eastern India, Indonesia, Malaysia, and the Philippines; humans are the only definitive host for W. bancrofti (Harrison 22e p.1819-1820).","Most W. bancrofti is nocturnally periodic (microfilariae scarce by day, increasing at night); blood for microfilaria detection must be timed to the regional periodicity (Harrison 22e p.1819-1821).","Common presentations of lymphatic filariasis are asymptomatic/subclinical microfilaremia, hydrocele, acute adenolymphangitis (ADL), and chronic lymphatic disease (lymphedema/elephantiasis) (Harrison 22e p.1820).","Genital lymphatic involvement (funiculitis, epididymitis, hydrocele, scrotal elephantiasis) occurs almost exclusively with W. bancrofti, not Brugia (Harrison 22e p.1820).","ADL lymphangitis is retrograde (spreads peripherally from the draining node), a feature that distinguishes filarial lymphangitis from ascending bacterial lymphangitis (Harrison 22e p.1820-1821).","Dermatolymphangioadenitis (DLA) presents with fever, chills, myalgias, demarcated edematous plaques, and entry lesions (often interdigital) and is frequently misdiagnosed as cellulitis (Harrison 22e p.1820).","Obstruction of retroperitoneal lymphatics can rupture renal lymphatics and produce intermittent chyluria, typically most prominent in the morning (Harrison 22e p.1820).","On high-frequency ultrasound, live adult worms in dilated lymphatics show a distinctive motion called the 'filarial dance sign'; worms are seen in the spermatic cord in up to 80% of W. bancrofti-infected men (Harrison 22e p.1821).","Circulating antigen assays (ELISA and rapid lateral-flow) for W. bancrofti have sensitivities of 93-100% and specificity approaching 100% and detect both microfilaremic and cryptic amicrofilaremic infection; there is currently NO circulating-antigen test for brugian filariasis (Harrison 22e p.1821).","Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia (TPE) presents with nocturnal paroxysmal cough/wheeze, weight loss, low-grade fever, marked blood eosinophilia (>3000/uL), and very high IgE and antifilarial antibody; microfilariae are cleared from blood by the lungs and are typically NOT found in peripheral blood (Harrison 22e p.1821-1822).","Onchocerciasis ('river blindness'), caused by O. volvulus, primarily affects skin, eyes, and lymph nodes; pruritus and a generalized papular rash are most common, and visual impairment is the most serious complication (Harrison 22e p.1822-1823).","Sclerosing keratitis (in 1-5% of infected persons) is the leading cause of onchocercal blindness; 'snowflake' punctate keratitis around dying microfilariae is common in younger patients and usually resolves (Harrison 22e p.1823).","Onchocercal lymphadenopathy can produce 'hanging groin'; rarely Nakalanga dwarfism and an association with epilepsy/nodding syndrome are seen; blind adults have a 3- to 4-fold higher mortality (Harrison 22e p.1823).","Loiasis (Loa loa, the African eye worm) is suggested by transient subconjunctival worm migration and episodic Calabar swellings (evanescent angioedema); microfilariae have a diurnal periodicity peaking ~10:00 a.m.-2:00 p.m. (Harrison 22e p.1823).","Wolbachia, an intracellular bacterial endosymbiont present in Brugia, Wuchereria, Mansonella, and Onchocerca, is a target for antifilarial (anti-Wolbachia) therapy (Harrison 22e p.1819, 1824)."],"pathophysiology":"Filariae are tissue-dwelling nematodes transmitted by mosquitoes or other arthropods; insect-borne infective larvae mature into adult worms that reside in lymphatics (W. bancrofti, Brugia) or subcutaneous tissue (Loa loa, O. volvulus). Adults release microfilariae that circulate in blood or migrate through skin; adults live many years while microfilariae survive 3-36 months. In lymphatic filariasis, pathology results mainly from inflammatory damage caused by ADULT worms (not microfilariae) in afferent lymphatics, with plasma-cell/eosinophil/macrophage infiltration, lymphatic dilation, valve incompetence, and progressive granulomatous reaction and fibrosis (worsened by worm death) leading to obstruction, lymphedema, and elephantiasis; injury is due both to the worm and to host inflammatory response. In contrast, onchocerciasis pathology is elicited by MICROFILARIAE (not adults), causing chronic dermal inflammation/atrophy/fibrosis, fibrous subcutaneous nodules (onchocercomata) around adults, and corneal neovascularization/scarring and intraocular inflammation leading to blindness. In TPE, microfilariae are trapped and cleared by the lungs, provoking allergic/eosinophilic intra-alveolar inflammation that can progress to interstitial fibrosis. Calabar swellings of loiasis are thought to be a hypersensitivity reaction to adult-worm antigens. The Wolbachia endosymbiont contributes to inflammatory reactions (Harrison 22e p.1819-1823).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute adenolymphangitis (ADL) vs thrombophlebitis, bacterial infection, and trauma (Harrison 22e p.1821)","Filarial lymphangitis vs ascending bacterial lymphangitis (distinguished by retrograde evolution) (Harrison 22e p.1821)","Chronic filarial lymphedema vs lymphedema of malignancy, postoperative scarring, trauma, chronic edematous states, and congenital lymphatic abnormalities (Harrison 22e p.1821)","DLA frequently misdiagnosed as cellulitis (Harrison 22e p.1820)","TPE vs asthma, Loffler's syndrome, allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis, eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Churg-Strauss), other systemic vasculitides (notably periarteritis nodosa), chronic eosinophilic pneumonia, and hypereosinophilic syndromes (Harrison 22e p.1822)"],"infectionMimics":["Ascending bacterial lymphangitis (mimics filarial ADL) (Harrison 22e p.1821)","Bacterial cellulitis (DLA is often diagnosed as cellulitis) (Harrison 22e p.1820)","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (TPE differential) (Harrison 22e p.1822)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lymphedema of malignancy, postoperative scarring, trauma, chronic edematous states, congenital lymphatic abnormalities (mimic chronic filarial lymphedema) (Harrison 22e p.1821)","Asthma and Loffler's syndrome (mimic TPE) (Harrison 22e p.1822)","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Churg-Strauss), other systemic vasculitides including periarteritis nodosa, chronic eosinophilic pneumonia, hypereosinophilic syndromes (mimic TPE) (Harrison 22e p.1822)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Microscopy of blood, hydrocele fluid, or other body fluids for microfilariae, with concentration by polycarbonate membrane filtration (3 um pore) or Knott's formalin centrifugation technique; collection timed to microfilarial periodicity (Harrison 22e p.1821)","Circulating filarial antigen assays for W. bancrofti (ELISA and rapid lateral-flow); no antigen test available for brugian filariasis (Harrison 22e p.1821)","PCR for W. bancrofti and B. malayi DNA in blood (sensitivity equal to or greater than parasitologic methods) (Harrison 22e p.1821)","High-frequency ultrasound with Doppler of scrotum/lymph nodes/breast to visualize motile adult worms ('filarial dance sign') (Harrison 22e p.1821)","Radionuclide lymphoscintigraphy (mainly a research/lymphedema-assessment tool) (Harrison 22e p.1821)","Eosinophilia, elevated serum IgE, and elevated antifilarial antibody (supportive, with extensive helminth cross-reactivity; W. bancrofti- and B. malayi-specific antigen serology has specificity >98%) (Harrison 22e p.1821)","Skin snip (incubated in saline/medium; microfilariae emerge in 2-4 h or overnight) or excised nodule for onchocerciasis; Onchocerca-specific antibody assays (Harrison 22e p.1823)","Chest x-ray/CT and pulmonary function tests in TPE (restrictive in most, obstructive in half) (Harrison 22e p.1822)","Detection of L. loa microfilariae in blood, removal of adult worm from eye/subcutaneous biopsy, or PCR/recombinant-antigen serology for loiasis (Harrison 22e p.1823)"],"redFlags":["Sclerosing keratitis, anterior uveitis, chorioretinal lesions, or optic atrophy in onchocerciasis (impending/established blindness) (Harrison 22e p.1823)","High Loa loa microfilaremia (>30,000 microfilariae/mL): risk of severe post-treatment encephalopathy with ivermectin or DEC (Harrison 22e p.1822-1823)","Loiasis with CNS involvement during treatment of heavy microfilaremia (coma, encephalitis) (Harrison 22e p.1823)","Chyluria from retroperitoneal lymphatic obstruction in lymphatic filariasis (Harrison 22e p.1820)","Progressive interstitial pulmonary fibrosis in untreated TPE (Harrison 22e p.1822)"],"prognosis":"Filarial infections are chronic with potential long-term debilitating effects; adult worms live for many years (W. bancrofti/Brugia >2 decades; O. volvulus up to ~18 years, average ~9). Most microfilaremic persons remain clinically asymptomatic for years, and only a minority progress to acute or chronic disease, but lymphatic damage can become permanent (elephantiasis, hydrocele). In onchocerciasis, visual loss is the most serious outcome and blind adults have a 3- to 4-fold increased mortality. Loiasis, historically considered relatively benign in endemic populations, is now linked to chronic morbidity and increased mortality. TPE responds to therapy but relapses in ~12-25% of cases (sometimes years later); untreated TPE can progress to pulmonary fibrosis (Harrison 22e p.1819-1823).","pitfalls":["Seropositivity (antifilarial antibody) cannot be equated with active infection; endemic residents can become sensitized via mosquito exposure without patent infection (Harrison 22e p.1821).","Antifilarial antibody/antigen serology has extensive cross-reactivity with other helminths; W. bancrofti antigen tests can be falsely positive in Loa loa infection (Harrison 22e p.1821).","Many infected individuals are amicrofilaremic, and travelers are often amicrofilaremic, so a negative blood smear does not exclude filariasis (Harrison 22e p.1821, 1823).","Timing of blood collection to the local microfilarial periodicity is critical; mistimed samples miss nocturnally or diurnally periodic microfilariae (Harrison 22e p.1821).","DLA is often misdiagnosed as cellulitis (Harrison 22e p.1820).","Eosinophilia and elevated IgE are nonspecific (common to many parasitic infections) and are not diagnostic by themselves (Harrison 22e p.1823).","For every palpable onchocercal nodule there are an estimated four deeper nonpalpable ones (Harrison 22e p.1823)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison describes lymphatic filariasis along a clinical spectrum rather than a formal grading: asymptomatic/subclinical microfilaremia, hydrocele, acute adenolymphangitis (ADL)/dermatolymphangioadenitis (DLA), and chronic lymphatic disease (lymphedema progressing to elephantiasis). Onchocerciasis severity is gauged by infection burden (skin/eye involvement increasing with moderate-to-heavy microfilarial load). Loa loa microfilaremia is stratified by treatment risk, with >30,000 microfilariae/mL marking high risk of severe post-treatment encephalopathy (Harrison 22e p.1820-1823).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1819"],"crossLinks":[]},"fistula_in_ano":{"name":"Fistula-in-ano","system":"GI / Anorectal / Surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["anal fistula","fistula in ano","perianal fistula"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with persistent or recurrent perianal discharge, irritation, and intermittent swelling or abscess, often following drainage of a previous anorectal abscess","A fistula is an abnormal epithelialised track connecting the anal canal or rectum (internal opening) to the perianal skin (external opening)","Goodsall's rule helps predict the internal opening: anterior external openings tend to track radially, while posterior ones tend to curve to the posterior midline","Fistulae are classified by their relationship to the sphincter complex (intersphincteric, transsphincteric, suprasphincteric, extrasphincteric) which determines treatment and continence risk","The primary treatment goal is to eradicate sepsis and close the track while preserving continence","Low, simple fistulae may be laid open (fistulotomy), whereas high or complex tracks require sphincter-sparing options such as seton drainage, advancement flaps, or LIFT procedures","Multiple, complex, or recurrent fistulae, especially with proctitis, should raise suspicion of Crohn's disease, tuberculosis, or malignancy","A draining (loose) seton controls sepsis and maintains drainage while definitive management is planned"],"pathophysiology":"Most anal fistulae are the chronic sequel of a cryptoglandular anorectal abscess. After the abscess drains, the primary infected anal gland at the dentate line persists and an epithelialised or granulation-lined track remains between the internal opening in the anal canal and the external opening on the skin. Ongoing low-grade infection prevents healing. The track's course through the sphincter complex determines its classification and the surgical risk to continence. Secondary causes include Crohn's disease, tuberculosis, radiation, trauma, and malignancy, which produce more complex and refractory fistulae.","additionalDifferentials":["Recurrent anorectal abscess","Pilonidal sinus","Hidradenitis suppurativa","Perianal Crohn's disease","Infected perianal sebaceous cyst","Anal or rectal carcinoma","Tuberculous or actinomycotic sinus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Examination under anaesthesia to define internal and external openings and the track","MRI pelvis for complex, recurrent, or suspected Crohn's-related fistulae","Endoanal ultrasound, sometimes with hydrogen peroxide enhancement, to map the track","Endoscopy and biopsy when inflammatory bowel disease or malignancy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Complex, branching, or recurrent fistulae suggesting Crohn's disease or malignancy","Associated undrained sepsis with systemic upset requiring urgent drainage","Rapidly progressive perianal sepsis suggesting necrotising infection","Induration or a mass raising concern for underlying carcinoma"],"prognosis":"Simple low fistulae have high cure rates after fistulotomy with minimal continence impact. Complex, high, or Crohn's-related fistulae are prone to recurrence and require staged sphincter-preserving strategies, with a persistent risk of continence disturbance.","pitfalls":["Laying open a high transsphincteric or suprasphincteric fistula and causing incontinence","Failing to identify and address the internal opening, leading to recurrence","Overlooking Crohn's disease in complex or recurrent disease","Creating a false track by forceful probing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"flail_chest":{"name":"Flail chest","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["flail segment"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Flail chest is defined by a segment of chest wall that has lost bony continuity, typically from multiple ribs each fractured in two or more places","The hallmark is paradoxical movement, where the flail segment moves inward on inspiration and outward on expiration, opposite to the rest of the chest","The main threat to oxygenation is the underlying lung contusion rather than the chest wall movement itself","It is identified during the breathing step of the primary survey and demands close monitoring for respiratory deterioration","Adequate analgesia is central to management, allowing the patient to breathe deeply, cough, and clear secretions","Deterioration in gas exchange may require escalating respiratory support, including positive-pressure ventilation","Watch for associated injuries such as pneumothorax, haemothorax, and injury to underlying structures"],"pathophysiology":"When several adjacent ribs are each fractured in two or more places, an isolated segment of chest wall loses its bony connection to the rest of the thoracic cage. This segment then moves according to intrapleural pressure rather than muscular effort, producing paradoxical motion that reduces the efficiency of ventilation. More importantly, the force required to cause the injury bruises the lung beneath, and this pulmonary contusion impairs gas exchange, worsening over the first day or two. Pain from the fractures limits chest expansion and coughing, promoting atelectasis and retained secretions, all of which combine to cause hypoxaemia and, in severe cases, respiratory failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple rib fractures without a true flail segment","Isolated pulmonary contusion","Pneumothorax or haemothorax","Sternal fracture with associated cardiac contusion","Diaphragmatic injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph and CT to define rib fractures and pulmonary contusion","Arterial blood gas to monitor oxygenation and ventilation","Continuous pulse oximetry and respiratory monitoring","Assessment for associated pneumothorax or haemothorax","Serial evaluation as pulmonary contusion typically worsens over the first days"],"redFlags":["Progressive hypoxaemia from evolving pulmonary contusion requiring escalation of respiratory support","Increasing respiratory distress and fatigue signalling impending failure","Associated tension pneumothorax or large haemothorax","Inadequately controlled pain preventing effective breathing and coughing","Signs of underlying great-vessel or cardiac injury"],"prognosis":"Outcome is largely determined by the severity of the underlying lung contusion and associated injuries. With good analgesia, chest physiotherapy, and respiratory support many patients recover, but extensive contusion and respiratory failure increase morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Focusing on the chest wall movement while underestimating the underlying lung contusion","Providing inadequate analgesia, leading to poor ventilation and secretion retention","Missing an associated pneumothorax or haemothorax","Assuming a stable early gas exchange will remain stable as contusion evolves","Excessive fluid administration worsening the pulmonary contusion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"flexor_tendon_injury":{"name":"Flexor tendon injury","system":"Plastic / Reconstructive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["flexor tendon laceration","flexor tendon repair","zone II flexor injury","digital flexor tendon injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery / standard references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Flexor tendon injuries are described by anatomical zones, and zone II (within the fibro-osseous flexor sheath, historically 'no man's land') is the most technically challenging because both flexor tendons run within a tight sheath","Examination should test flexor digitorum profundus and flexor digitorum superficialis separately, as one may be intact while the other is divided","The resting cascade of the fingers is disrupted when a tendon is cut, and loss of the normal flexion posture on inspection is an important clue","Associated digital nerve and vessel injuries are common because of their proximity to the tendons and should be actively sought","Partial lacerations may retain some active movement but can still catch, rupture, or trigger, so they require careful assessment","Repair aims for a strong core suture with an epitendinous suture to allow early controlled motion, and preservation of key pulleys (particularly A2 and A4) prevents bowstringing","Structured early rehabilitation with a hand therapist is critical, balancing tendon glide against the risk of rupture, using controlled active or passive motion protocols","The main complications are adhesions causing stiffness, repair rupture, and joint contracture, and delayed presentation may require staged grafting or tendon reconstruction"],"pathophysiology":"Flexor tendons transmit force from forearm muscles to flex the digits and glide within synovial sheaths and beneath a series of annular and cruciate pulleys that hold them close to bone. Laceration divides the tendon and causes proximal retraction of the cut end, particularly when the muscle is contracting at the time of injury. Healing occurs through both intrinsic mechanisms, from tenocytes and synovial nutrition, and extrinsic mechanisms, through ingrowth of surrounding tissue that forms adhesions. Excessive extrinsic healing produces adhesions that restrict glide, while inadequate healing or excessive load risks gap formation and rupture. Within the confined zone II sheath, even small amounts of scarring markedly impair function.","additionalDifferentials":["Extensor tendon injury or combined flexor-extensor injury","Digital nerve injury without tendon division","Closed flexor tendon avulsion (jersey finger)","Trigger finger or stenosing tenosynovitis","Musculotendinous junction or forearm-level injury","Retained foreign body limiting movement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful clinical testing of profundus and superficialis function in each digit","Assessment of digital nerve sensation and vascular status","Plain radiographs to detect fractures, foreign bodies, or bony avulsion","Ultrasound or MRI to localise a retracted tendon end or characterise closed or partial injuries","Surgical exploration, which remains definitive for confirming and treating suspected injury"],"redFlags":["Associated neurovascular injury threatening digital viability requiring urgent exploration","Signs of flexor sheath infection following an open injury (a surgical emergency)","Complete loss of active flexion with a retracted tendon needing timely repair before further retraction","Devascularised or grossly contaminated wound requiring prompt surgical management"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the zone of injury, associated damage, quality of repair, and adherence to rehabilitation. Zone II injuries carry the highest risk of adhesions and stiffness. With sound repair and supervised early motion, many patients regain good function, but adhesions, rupture, and contracture can necessitate tenolysis or staged reconstruction and may leave lasting deficits.","pitfalls":["Failing to test profundus and superficialis separately and missing a partial or isolated tendon division","Overlooking associated digital nerve or vessel injury","Damaging or dividing critical pulleys (A2, A4), leading to bowstringing","Immobilising rather than starting an appropriate early motion protocol, causing dense adhesions","Being too aggressive with rehabilitation and precipitating repair rupture","Delaying repair so that tendon retraction complicates later reconstruction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"focal_atrial_tachycardia":{"name":"Focal Atrial Tachycardia","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Focal AT","Atrial tachycardia","AT"],"chapter":255,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1937","clinicalPearls":["Focal AT arises from a discrete atrial site and is driven by one of three mechanisms: abnormal automaticity, triggered activity, or microreentry within diseased atrial tissue; the term 'focal' distinguishes it from typical and atypical (macroreentrant) atrial flutter and does not by itself specify the underlying mechanism (p.1937).","It accounts for roughly 10% of paroxysmal SVTs in patients referred for catheter ablation, and may be sustained, nonsustained, paroxysmal, or incessant (p.1937).","Because focal AT is not dependent on AV nodal conduction, it does NOT terminate when AV block is induced and the atrial rate is unaffected by AV-nodal blockade — a key feature separating it from AV-nodal-dependent SVTs such as AVNRT and AVRT (p.1937).","P waves are typically discrete with an intervening isoelectric baseline (because atrial activation from a single focus occupies only a small part of the cycle), in contrast to the continuous undulating activation of atrial flutter / macroreentrant AT (p.1937).","A 'warm-up' phase (gradual acceleration of atrial rate after onset) or a 'cool-down' phase (gradual slowing before termination) favors focal AT with enhanced automaticity over AV-nodal-dependent SVT (p.1937).","Foci tend to cluster in areas of complex atrial anatomy — the crista terminalis, valve annuli, atrial septum, and atrial muscle sleeves extending into the thoracic veins (superior vena cava, coronary sinus, pulmonary veins) — and the focus location can often be approximated from P-wave morphology (p.1937).","P-wave localization clues: septal foci give a narrower P than sinus; left atrial foci give a monophasic positive P in V1 with negative P in I and aVL; superior foci (SVC, superior pulmonary veins) are positive in II, III, aVF; inferior foci (e.g., coronary sinus ostium) are negative in II, III, aVF (p.1937).","Atrial tachycardia WITH AV block is the characteristic arrhythmia of digitalis toxicity, so emergence of AT should prompt consideration of drug toxicity or another underlying illness; sympathetic stimulation promotes AT (p.1937).","Incessant focal AT can cause tachycardia-induced (tachycardia-mediated) cardiomyopathy, which is an important reversible cause of LV dysfunction (p.1937).","Asymptomatic nonsustained AT seen on monitoring is frequently mislabeled 'SVT' on monitor reports; treatment is NOT recommended for asymptomatic nonsustained AT identified on ECG monitoring (p.1937).","Frequent atrial ectopy and nonsustained AT are often precursors to more significant arrhythmias such as atrial fibrillation and atrial flutter (p.1937).","Vagal maneuvers, carotid sinus massage, Valsalva, or AV-nodal-blocking agents (e.g., adenosine) are used diagnostically to create transient AV block and unmask P waves when they are buried in the T wave or QRS (p.1937-1938)."],"pathophysiology":"Focal AT originates from a single discrete atrial site and is caused by abnormal automaticity, triggered activity, or a small reentry circuit in diseased atrial tissue. It may occur in structurally normal hearts or in association with any condition causing atrial fibrosis, including atrial tissue scarred by prior catheter ablation. Zones of fibrosis serve as a nidus for abnormal automaticity arising from injured but partially viable cells, or for microreentry within and at the border of regions of slow conduction. Sympathetic stimulation is a facilitating factor, and the appearance of AT may signal underlying illness or drug toxicity (digitalis being characteristic). The arrhythmia activates the atria from a focal source through only a small portion of the cycle, producing discrete P waves with isoelectric intervals (p.1937).","additionalDifferentials":["Sinus tachycardia (especially when the focus lies high on the crista terminalis near the sinus node, producing a near-sinus P-wave morphology; abrupt onset/offset favors AT)","Inappropriate sinus tachycardia","AV nodal reentry tachycardia (AVNRT)","AV reentry tachycardia using an accessory pathway (AVRT)","Atrial flutter / macroreentrant atrial tachycardia (continuous flutter waves, no isoelectric baseline)","Multifocal atrial tachycardia (multiple distinct P-wave morphologies)","Junctional tachycardia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sinus tachycardia (physiologic or inappropriate)","Atrial flutter and macroreentrant atrial tachycardia","AV-nodal-dependent SVTs (AVNRT, AVRT)"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG to characterize P-wave morphology and the R-P / P-R relationship and to localize the focus","Ambulatory/Holter ECG monitoring (nonsustained AT is commonly detected and its prevalence rises with age)","Maneuvers to induce AV block (carotid sinus massage, Valsalva, adenosine) to expose buried P waves and to demonstrate that the atrial tachycardia persists despite AV block","Assessment for digitalis toxicity and other precipitating illness/drug toxicity when AT with AV block is seen","Echocardiography to evaluate for structural heart disease and tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy","Electrophysiology study with electroanatomic mapping to identify the earliest activation (focal) site, typically prior to catheter ablation"],"redFlags":["Incessant AT — risk of tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy","Hemodynamic instability with the tachycardia","AT with AV block — suggests digitalis toxicity or other underlying illness/drug toxicity","Rapid ventricular response causing angina, hypotension, pulmonary edema, or syncope","AT as a harbinger of atrial fibrillation or atrial flutter"],"prognosis":"Symptoms vary widely and parallel those of other SVTs. Many nonsustained, asymptomatic episodes require no treatment. The principal adverse outcome is tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy from incessant AT, which is typically reversible once rhythm is controlled. Focal AT is frequently a precursor to atrial fibrillation or atrial flutter; however, the stroke risk and the indications for long-term anticoagulation are not considered equivalent to those of AF/flutter and remain unclear. Catheter ablation targeting the focus is successful in more than 80% of patients (p.1937-1938).","pitfalls":["Mislabeling asymptomatic nonsustained AT on a monitor as actionable 'SVT' and over-treating it — treatment is not recommended in this setting (p.1937).","Confusing focal AT with sinus tachycardia when the focus is near the sinus node; rely on abrupt onset/offset and subtle P-wave morphology differences (p.1937).","Expecting AV-nodal blockade (adenosine, vagal maneuvers) to terminate the tachycardia — it usually only increases AV block while the atrial rhythm continues, since AT is independent of the AV node (p.1937-1938).","Overlooking digitalis toxicity or another precipitant when AT presents with AV block (p.1937).","Missing reversible tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy as the cause of new LV dysfunction in a patient with incessant AT (p.1937)."],"severityClassification":"No formal grading scale; clinical stratification is by hemodynamic tolerance (stable vs unstable), by burden/pattern (nonsustained vs sustained, paroxysmal vs incessant), and by symptom status (asymptomatic vs symptomatic), since incessant disease carries the risk of tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy (p.1937).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1937","Harrison 22e p.1938"],"crossLinks":[]},"food_allergy":{"name":"Food allergy","system":"Immunology / Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["food hypersensitivity","IgE-mediated food allergy","food-induced anaphylaxis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Middleton's Allergy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most life-threatening reactions are IgE-mediated and begin within minutes to a couple of hours of ingestion, with skin, respiratory, gastrointestinal, and cardiovascular signs in varying combinations","A short list of foods accounts for the majority of reactions: cow's milk, egg, peanut, tree nuts, wheat, soy, fish, and shellfish, with peanut and tree nut allergy tending to persist into adult life","Diagnosis rests on a careful history correlating a specific food with reproducible symptoms, supported by skin prick testing or serum specific IgE, with the oral food challenge remaining the reference standard","A positive skin or blood test signals sensitisation but does not by itself confirm clinical allergy, so results must be interpreted against the history to avoid needless dietary restriction","Pollen-food (oral allergy) syndrome causes localised oral itching from raw fruits and vegetables that cross-react with pollens and is usually mild because the allergens are heat-labile","Cornerstones of management are strict avoidance of the culprit food, patient education on label reading and hidden exposures, and a written emergency action plan with a self-injectable adrenaline device","Adrenaline given intramuscularly is the first-line treatment for anaphylaxis; antihistamines and corticosteroids are adjuncts only and must never delay adrenaline","Cofactors such as exercise, alcohol, NSAIDs, and intercurrent infection can lower the reaction threshold, as in food-dependent exercise-induced anaphylaxis","Early rather than delayed introduction of allergenic foods such as peanut in infancy reduces the risk of developing allergy in high-risk children"],"pathophysiology":"IgE-mediated food allergy arises when sensitisation to dietary protein leads to allergen-specific IgE bound to mast cells and basophils. On re-exposure the allergen cross-links surface IgE, triggering degranulation with release of histamine, tryptase, leukotrienes, and other mediators that cause vasodilation, increased vascular permeability, smooth-muscle contraction, and mucus secretion. Non-IgE and mixed mechanisms (e.g. eosinophil- and T-cell-driven) underlie disorders such as eosinophilic oesophagitis and food protein-induced enterocolitis, which produce more delayed, predominantly gastrointestinal disease. Loss of oral tolerance, epithelial barrier dysfunction, and cutaneous sensitisation (notably through eczematous skin) are contributing factors.","additionalDifferentials":["Food intolerance (e.g. lactase deficiency) without immune mechanism","Scombroid (histamine) poisoning and other food toxin reactions","Idiopathic or drug-induced anaphylaxis","Eosinophilic oesophagitis and other eosinophilic gastrointestinal disorders","Carbohydrate (alpha-gal) allergy causing delayed reactions to mammalian meat","Panic or vasovagal episodes mimicking systemic reactions"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin prick testing to suspected foods interpreted alongside the clinical history","Serum allergen-specific IgE, including component-resolved testing where available","Supervised oral food challenge as the definitive diagnostic test","Serum tryptase during and after a suspected anaphylactic event","Elimination-and-reintroduction dietary trials for suspected non-IgE disease"],"redFlags":["Anaphylaxis with airway compromise, stridor, wheeze, hypotension, or collapse requiring immediate intramuscular adrenaline","Rapidly progressive symptoms after a known trigger, especially in patients with asthma who are at higher risk of fatal reactions","Biphasic reaction recurring hours after apparent recovery","Repeated accidental exposures or reactions despite avoidance suggesting inadequate education or hidden allergens"],"prognosis":"Milk, egg, wheat, and soy allergy commonly resolve during childhood, whereas peanut, tree nut, fish, and shellfish allergy usually persist for life. With reliable avoidance and prompt access to adrenaline, most patients do well, but unrecognised or undertreated anaphylaxis can be fatal.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing allergy from a positive test alone without a supporting history, leading to unnecessary and potentially harmful dietary elimination","Relying on antihistamines or steroids for anaphylaxis instead of giving adrenaline promptly","Failing to prescribe, or not teaching correct use of, a self-injectable adrenaline device","Overlooking cofactors and hidden or cross-contaminating allergens in processed foods and restaurant meals"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"foreign_body_aspiration":{"name":"Foreign Body Aspiration","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["airway foreign body","aspirated foreign body","inhaled foreign body"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Aspiration of a foreign body is most common in toddlers, who explore with their mouths and have immature airway protective reflexes; food items such as nuts and seeds are frequent culprits.","The classic history is a sudden episode of choking, coughing, or gagging in a previously well young child, often while eating or playing with small objects.","Clinical presentation varies with the site and degree of obstruction; a lodged object may produce unilateral wheeze, reduced air entry, or persistent cough.","A witnessed choking event is a strong clue, but the initial episode can be unwitnessed or followed by a symptom-free interval that leads to delayed diagnosis.","Chest radiographs may be normal because most aspirated objects are radiolucent; indirect signs include localised air trapping or hyperinflation, atelectasis, or a persistent infiltrate.","Rigid bronchoscopy is the definitive procedure for both confirming and removing an airway foreign body.","Complete upper airway obstruction is a life-threatening emergency requiring immediate basic choking manoeuvres appropriate to the child's age.","Delayed or missed diagnosis can lead to recurrent or persistent pneumonia, bronchiectasis, or chronic lung damage, so a high index of suspicion is important."],"pathophysiology":"When a small object is inhaled it may lodge anywhere from the larynx to the distal bronchi, with the site determining the clinical picture. A large object at the larynx or trachea can cause complete or near-complete obstruction and rapid asphyxia. More commonly the object passes into a bronchus, producing partial obstruction: air may move past on inspiration but be trapped on expiration, causing distal hyperinflation, or the airway may become fully blocked, leading to distal atelectasis. Retained material provokes inflammation and, over time, granulation tissue, mucus retention, and secondary infection, which underlie the chronic complications seen with delayed removal.","additionalDifferentials":["Croup or other infectious airway obstruction","Asthma or viral wheeze","Pneumonia","Anaphylaxis with airway compromise","Retropharyngeal or peritonsillar abscess","Structural airway lesion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph including inspiratory and expiratory (or lateral decubitus) views","Assessment for asymmetric air entry or localised findings on examination","Rigid bronchoscopy for diagnosis and removal","Neck radiographs if a laryngotracheal object is suspected","Further imaging when diagnosis remains uncertain despite strong suspicion"],"redFlags":["Complete airway obstruction with inability to cough, speak, or breathe","Cyanosis or rapidly progressive respiratory distress","Stridor with severe distress suggesting a laryngotracheal foreign body","Persistent or recurrent focal pneumonia","Altered consciousness from hypoxia"],"prognosis":"With timely recognition and bronchoscopic removal the outlook is generally excellent, whereas complete obstruction can be rapidly fatal and delayed diagnosis may result in chronic pulmonary complications such as bronchiectasis.","pitfalls":["Excluding aspiration because the chest radiograph is normal","Being falsely reassured by a symptom-free interval after the initial choking event","Attributing persistent focal respiratory symptoms to recurrent infection or asthma rather than a retained foreign body","Performing blind finger sweeps or inappropriate manoeuvres that may push an object further"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"fourniers_gangrene":{"name":"Fournier's gangrene","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Fournier gangrene","necrotising fasciitis of the perineum","perineal necrotising soft-tissue infection","scrotal gangrene"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / IDSA","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A fulminant necrotising fasciitis of the perineal, genital, and perianal region that constitutes a surgical emergency","Usually polymicrobial, mixing aerobic and anaerobic organisms from the gut and skin flora, producing synergistic tissue destruction","Diabetes mellitus, obesity, alcohol excess, immunosuppression, and local trauma or anorectal or urogenital infection are major predisposing factors","Severe pain, systemic toxicity, and rapidly spreading swelling with skin discolouration, crepitus, or foul-smelling discharge should raise immediate suspicion","Diagnosis is clinical and surgical; imaging may show subcutaneous gas but must never delay operative exploration","Treatment principles are immediate wide surgical debridement of all necrotic tissue, broad-spectrum antibiotics covering Gram-positives, Gram-negatives, and anaerobes, and haemodynamic resuscitation","Repeat debridement is commonly required, and diversion of urine or faeces may be needed to protect the wound","Delay in surgery is the strongest driver of mortality, so early recognition and theatre are paramount"],"pathophysiology":"Fournier's gangrene is a necrotising infection of the fascial planes of the genitalia, perineum, and perianal area. Mixed aerobic and anaerobic organisms act synergistically, generating enzymes and toxins that thrombose subcutaneous vessels and cause progressive ischaemic necrosis of skin, subcutaneous tissue, and fascia. Bacterial gas production leads to crepitus, and infection tracks rapidly along fascial planes, often outpacing the overlying skin changes, culminating in systemic sepsis and multi-organ failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Perianal or ischiorectal abscess without necrosis","Scrotal or epididymo-orchitis","Simple cellulitis of the perineum","Strangulated inguinoscrotal hernia","Cutaneous or Bartholin abscess"],"infectionMimics":["Testicular torsion presenting with acute scrotal pain","Severe contact or irritant dermatitis of the groin","Pyoderma gangrenosum of the perineum","Extensive hidradenitis suppurativa flare"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cultures and cultures of operative tissue and any discharge","Full blood count, renal and liver function, glucose, lactate and coagulation to gauge sepsis severity","CT or plain imaging to define the extent of gas and tissue involvement when it will not delay surgery","Surgical exploration for definitive diagnosis and debridement","Glycaemic assessment and screening for underlying immunosuppression"],"redFlags":["Rapidly spreading perineal swelling with crepitus or skin necrosis demanding immediate surgical debridement","Pain out of proportion to visible findings suggesting deep fascial necrosis","Systemic signs of septic shock requiring urgent resuscitation and critical care","Subcutaneous gas on examination or imaging indicating gas-forming necrotising infection"],"prognosis":"Mortality is significant and rises steeply with delayed surgery, extensive disease, advanced age, and multi-organ dysfunction; timely aggressive debridement and organ support improve survival, though survivors often need multiple operations and reconstructive procedures.","pitfalls":["Mistaking early disease for a simple abscess or cellulitis and delaying theatre","Waiting for imaging before surgical exploration in a deteriorating patient","Underestimating the extent of necrosis and performing inadequate initial debridement","Neglecting planned re-exploration to remove ongoing necrotic tissue"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","IDSA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"friedreichs_ataxia":{"name":"Friedreich's ataxia","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Friedreich ataxia","FRDA","FA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common inherited ataxia, typically autosomal recessive and presenting in childhood or adolescence with progressive gait and limb ataxia","Characteristic neurological signs include areflexia in the legs with extensor plantar responses, reflecting combined peripheral neuropathy and corticospinal involvement","Loss of proprioception and vibration sense is prominent owing to dorsal column and large sensory fibre degeneration","Dysarthria develops, and there is often nystagmus and impaired eye movements as the disease advances","Systemic features are important, especially a hypertrophic cardiomyopathy that is a major cause of morbidity and mortality","Diabetes mellitus, scoliosis, and pes cavus foot deformity are commonly associated","Caused in most cases by a homozygous GAA trinucleotide repeat expansion in the frataxin (FXN) gene, confirmable by genetic testing","Management is supportive and multidisciplinary, with particular attention to cardiac surveillance, glucose monitoring, and orthopaedic and rehabilitative care"],"pathophysiology":"Friedreich's ataxia is usually caused by a GAA repeat expansion in the FXN gene, reducing production of frataxin, a mitochondrial protein involved in iron-sulphur cluster assembly and iron handling. Frataxin deficiency impairs mitochondrial energy metabolism and increases oxidative stress, leading to degeneration of the dorsal root ganglia, dorsal columns, spinocerebellar and corticospinal tracts, and the cerebellar dentate nucleus. The same metabolic vulnerability affects the heart and pancreatic islets, explaining the cardiomyopathy and diabetes.","additionalDifferentials":["Other hereditary and spinocerebellar ataxias","Ataxia with vitamin E deficiency","Vitamin B12 deficiency with subacute combined degeneration","Hereditary sensory and motor neuropathies","Acquired sensory ataxic neuropathies","Multiple sclerosis and other acquired cerebellar disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for the FXN GAA repeat expansion to confirm the diagnosis","Echocardiography and ECG for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and rhythm disturbance","Fasting glucose and HbA1c to screen for diabetes mellitus","Nerve conduction studies showing a sensory axonal neuropathy","Vitamin E level and B12 to exclude treatable mimics, and spinal imaging for scoliosis assessment"],"redFlags":["Symptoms or signs of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy or arrhythmia, a leading cause of death requiring cardiology involvement","New or worsening dysphagia with aspiration risk in advanced disease","Rapidly progressive deficits suggesting an alternative or additional diagnosis","Severe progressive scoliosis compromising respiratory function"],"prognosis":"Friedreich's ataxia is progressive, with most patients losing independent ambulation over one to two decades from onset. Life expectancy is often reduced, largely because of cardiac complications. Careful multidisciplinary management, particularly cardiac and metabolic surveillance and rehabilitation, can improve function and quality of life.","pitfalls":["Overlooking the cardiac and diabetic complications that drive much of the morbidity and mortality","Missing treatable mimics such as vitamin E or B12 deficiency before confirming the genetic diagnosis","Attributing areflexia with upgoing plantars to a single localisation and missing the combined pathology","Neglecting proactive orthopaedic management of scoliosis and foot deformity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"frontotemporal_dementia":{"name":"Frontotemporal Dementia","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["FTD","behavioral variant FTD","primary progressive aphasia"],"chapter":443,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3485","clinicalPearls":["FTD is a group of clinical syndromes united by underlying frontotemporal lobar degeneration (FTLD) pathology; like other major neurodegenerative diseases it is considered a disorder of abnormal protein aggregation, with either tau or TDP-43 (transactive response DNA-binding protein of 43 kDa) implicated in most cases (Harrison 22e p.3485).","Younger onset than typical Alzheimer's: FTD most often begins in the fifth to seventh decades of life and is nearly as prevalent as Alzheimer's disease in this age group (Harrison 22e p.3485).","Three core clinical syndromes are described: the behavioral variant (bvFTD, the most common) and two forms of primary progressive aphasia (PPA) - the semantic and nonfluent/agrammatic variants (Harrison 22e p.3485-3486).","bvFTD manifests as social and emotional dysfunction - apathy, disinhibition, compulsivity, loss of empathy, and overeating - often but not always accompanied by deficits in executive control (Harrison 22e p.3486).","In semantic variant PPA patients slowly lose the ability to decode word, object, person-specific, and emotion meaning; in nonfluent/agrammatic variant PPA patients develop profound inability to produce words, often with prominent motor speech impairment (Harrison 22e p.3486).","Parietal functions such as visuospatial processing and arithmetic calculation are typically unaffected even late in the FTD syndromes, helping distinguish it from Alzheimer's disease (Harrison 22e p.3486).","Any of the three syndromes, most often bvFTD, may be accompanied by motor neuron disease (MND), in which case the term FTD-MND is applied; corticobasal syndrome (CBS) and progressive supranuclear palsy-Richardson syndrome (PSP-RS) are also considered part of the FTD clinical spectrum (Harrison 22e p.3486).","In contrast to Alzheimer's disease, the cholinergic system is relatively spared in FTD, which accounts for the poor efficacy of acetylcholinesterase inhibitors in this group (Harrison 22e p.3486).","Treatment is symptomatic only; there are currently no therapies known to slow progression or improve symptoms, and no disease-modifying treatments have shown efficacy to date (Harrison 22e p.3487)."],"pathophysiology":"FTD is linked to frontotemporal lobar degeneration (FTLD), characterized by focal atrophy of frontal, insular, and/or temporal cortex; atrophy often begins focally in one hemisphere before spreading to anatomically interconnected cortical and subcortical regions. bvFTD shows medial/orbital frontal cortex and anterior insula degeneration, semantic variant PPA the anterior temporal region, and nonfluent/agrammatic PPA the opercular frontal and precentral gyrus of the dominant hemisphere. Microscopically there is gliosis, microvacuolation, and neuronal loss; the disease is subtyped by the protein composition of neuronal and glial inclusions, which contain either tau or TDP-43 in roughly 90% of patients, with the remaining ~10% showing inclusions containing the FET family of proteins (FUS, Ewing sarcoma protein, TAF-15). Loss of cortical serotonergic innervation is common while the cholinergic system is relatively spared. Misfolded proteins are increasingly recognized to have prion-like/corruptive templating properties that may drive network-rooted patterns of disease spread. Autosomal dominant inheritance is seen in only 10-20% of cases, with mutations in C9orf72 (the most common genetic cause, a noncoding GGGGCC hexanucleotide expansion, presenting as bvFTD with or without MND and overlapping with ALS), GRN/progranulin, and MAPT/tau (Harrison 22e p.3485-3487).","additionalDifferentials":["Alzheimer's disease (FTD has relatively preserved memory and parietal function early; a small percentage of FTD syndromes show Alzheimer's pathology at autopsy) (Harrison 22e p.3486-3487)","Progressive supranuclear palsy-Richardson syndrome (PSP-RS) - within the FTD spectrum; falls, vertical supranuclear ophthalmoparesis, axial rigidity (Harrison 22e p.3487)","Corticobasal syndrome (CBS) - asymmetric rigidity, dystonia, myoclonus, apraxia, alien limb phenomena (Harrison 22e p.3488)","Idiopathic Parkinson's disease - PSP is often confused with PD, but PD does not produce downgaze paresis (Harrison 22e p.3488)","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis / motor neuron disease (FTD-MND overlap) (Harrison 22e p.3486)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Psychiatric disease - depression, idiopathic mania, or acting out: the abulia/blandness of frontal degeneration may be misinterpreted as depression and the disinhibition as mania or acting out; FTD can begin with schizophrenia-like features leading to misdiagnosis of a psychiatric condition (Harrison 22e p.3485, p.209)","Other focal frontal lobe causes: head trauma, ruptured aneurysms, hydrocephalus, tumors (metastases, glioblastoma, meningioma) (Harrison 22e p.208)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Structural neuroimaging (MRI) to demonstrate focal frontal/insular/temporal atrophy patterns characteristic of each syndrome (Harrison 22e p.3486)","Genetic testing for C9orf72, GRN, and MAPT mutations in suspected familial cases (Harrison 22e p.3486)","Molecular (tau) PET imaging shows promise but to date binds robustly only to Alzheimer's-related misfolded tau; amyloid PET may help when negative (Harrison 22e p.3487)"],"redFlags":["New-onset disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy, compulsivity, or altered dietary preference in a middle-aged adult (Harrison 22e p.3486)","Progressive language breakdown - loss of word/object meaning or inability to produce words (Harrison 22e p.3486)","Emergence of motor neuron disease features in a patient with behavioral or language decline (FTD-MND) (Harrison 22e p.3486)"],"prognosis":"Caregivers carry a heavy burden, especially as the illness disrupts core emotional and personality functions. There are currently no therapies known to slow progression or improve symptoms; treatment is symptomatic. For the related PSP-RS within the FTD spectrum, death occurs within 5-10 years of onset (Harrison 22e p.3487).","pitfalls":["Behavioral variant FTD is frequently mistaken for a primary psychiatric disease - the apathy/blandness can be misread as depression and the disinhibition as mania or acting out; FTD may even begin with schizophrenia-like features and be misdiagnosed as a psychiatric condition. Later age of onset, significant cognitive deficits, or abnormal neuroimaging point to a degenerative process (Harrison 22e p.3485, p.209).","Clinical features do not allow reliable prediction of the underlying FTLD molecular subtype (tau vs TDP-43 vs FET) for all syndromes (Harrison 22e p.3487).","Acetylcholinesterase inhibitors are largely ineffective in FTD because the cholinergic system is relatively spared, unlike Alzheimer's disease (Harrison 22e p.3486).","Antipsychotics must be used with caution because FTD is often accompanied by parkinsonism, which they can exacerbate (Harrison 22e p.3487)."],"severityClassification":"No formal severity grading is given; FTD is categorized by core clinical syndrome - behavioral variant (bvFTD), semantic variant PPA, and nonfluent/agrammatic variant PPA - and by underlying FTLD molecular class (FTLD-tau, FTLD-TDP, FTLD-FET) (Harrison 22e p.3486-3487).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3485","Harrison 22e p.3486","Harrison 22e p.3487","Harrison 22e p.3488"],"crossLinks":["alzheimers_disease","amyotrophic_lateral_sclerosis"]},"frostbite":{"name":"Frostbite","system":"Plastic / Reconstructive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cold injury","freezing cold injury","frostnip"],"chapter":null,"source":"Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery / standard references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Frostbite most often affects peripheral and exposed parts such as the fingers, toes, ears, nose, and cheeks, where tissue actually freezes, in contrast to non-freezing cold injuries","Severity is graded from superficial injury with numbness and blistering to deep injury involving muscle, tendon, and bone, though the final depth is often unclear at presentation","Frostnip is a reversible superficial cold response without ice crystal formation and resolves with rewarming, whereas true frostbite involves tissue freezing and potential necrosis","Rewarming should be rapid using a warm water bath and should only begin when there is no risk of refreezing, as refreezing dramatically worsens tissue damage","During and after rewarming the tissue is intensely painful, and adequate analgesia along with wound care and tetanus prophylaxis is important","Clear or milky blisters generally indicate more superficial injury, while haemorrhagic blisters suggest deeper damage with a worse prognosis","A conservative approach to debridement and amputation is preferred, allowing demarcation of nonviable tissue over weeks, because early appearances overestimate tissue loss","Predisposing factors include alcohol, homelessness, psychiatric illness, peripheral vascular disease, and inadequate protection at altitude, and prevention through insulation and avoiding constriction is key"],"pathophysiology":"Frostbite results from tissue freezing when heat loss exceeds the body's ability to maintain local warmth. Extracellular and then intracellular ice crystals form, causing direct mechanical cell damage and osmotic shifts. Vasoconstriction and increased blood viscosity reduce perfusion, and on rewarming there is a reperfusion phase with endothelial damage, inflammatory mediator release, microvascular thrombosis, and progressive dermal ischaemia. This secondary vascular injury and thrombosis, rather than the initial freezing alone, is responsible for much of the eventual tissue loss, which is why final demarcation takes time to declare.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-freezing cold injury (trench foot / immersion foot)","Chilblains (pernio)","Peripheral arterial occlusion or acute limb ischaemia","Raynaud phenomenon with digital ischaemia","Thermal or chemical burn","Cellulitis or early necrotising infection of the extremity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of tissue viability and blister character during and after rewarming","Core temperature measurement to detect and treat associated hypothermia","Angiography or bone/technetium scanning to assess perfusion and predict tissue viability","MRI or MR angiography to help delineate viable from nonviable tissue","Inflammatory markers and wound cultures if secondary infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Associated systemic hypothermia requiring urgent core rewarming and resuscitation before focusing on the extremity","Deep or extensive frostbite with haemorrhagic blistering and threatened tissue loss","Signs of secondary infection, wet gangrene, or systemic sepsis","Risk of refreezing, which mandates delaying rewarming until definitively out of the cold"],"prognosis":"Superficial frostbite and frostnip usually recover fully, though long-term cold sensitivity, numbness, and neuropathic symptoms are common. Deep frostbite frequently leads to tissue loss and amputation, but because early appearance overstates the damage, delaying surgery until demarcation improves the extent of salvage. Overall outcome depends on depth, duration of exposure, refreezing, and timeliness of correct rewarming.","pitfalls":["Rewarming when refreezing is still possible, which greatly worsens injury","Using dry or excessive heat, or rubbing the tissue, causing further damage","Performing early debridement or amputation before nonviable tissue has demarcated","Treating the local injury while overlooking life-threatening systemic hypothermia","Underestimating eventual tissue loss based on the early appearance","Neglecting analgesia, tetanus prophylaxis, and prevention of recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"frozen_shoulder":{"name":"Frozen shoulder","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["adhesive capsulitis","shoulder contracture","periarthritis of the shoulder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by painful, progressive global restriction of shoulder movement with the hallmark loss of passive external rotation, distinguishing it from rotator cuff disease","The condition typically evolves through overlapping phases: a painful freezing phase, a stiff frozen phase, and a gradual thawing phase of recovery","It most often affects middle-aged patients and is strongly associated with diabetes mellitus, which also predicts a more resistant course","May be primary (idiopathic) or secondary to trauma, immobilisation, or shoulder surgery","Diagnosis is clinical; radiographs are typically normal and mainly serve to exclude other causes such as arthritis or a locked posterior dislocation","Management is largely supportive: analgesia, maintaining movement, physiotherapy, and reassurance about eventual improvement","Intra-articular corticosteroid injection can help pain and stiffness, particularly in the early painful phase","Refractory cases may be treated with capsular hydrodilatation, manipulation under anaesthesia, or arthroscopic capsular release"],"pathophysiology":"Frozen shoulder involves inflammation and progressive fibrosis of the glenohumeral joint capsule, particularly the rotator interval and coracohumeral ligament. Fibroblastic proliferation and contracture thicken and tighten the capsule, reducing the intra-articular volume and mechanically limiting movement, with external rotation lost first and most severely. The initial inflammatory, hypervascular stage is painful; as the capsule matures into dense fibrous tissue the pain lessens but stiffness dominates; finally the capsule gradually remodels and mobility returns. The strong association with diabetes and certain endocrine and metabolic states points to a systemic predisposition to abnormal fibrosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Rotator cuff tear or tendinopathy","Glenohumeral or acromioclavicular osteoarthritis","Calcific tendinitis","Posterior shoulder dislocation (locked)","Subacromial impingement or bursitis","Referred pain from the cervical spine","Septic arthritis (if acute, hot, and systemically unwell)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment demonstrating global restriction with markedly reduced passive external rotation","Plain shoulder radiographs to exclude arthritis, dislocation, or bony lesions","Blood glucose or HbA1c given the association with diabetes","MRI or ultrasound if a rotator cuff tear or other structural lesion is suspected","Thyroid function tests where clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Constitutional symptoms or a mass suggesting tumour rather than capsulitis","Hot, swollen, systemically unwell shoulder raising concern for septic arthritis","History of significant trauma with fixed internal rotation suggesting a missed posterior dislocation","Rapidly progressive neurological symptoms pointing to an alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Frozen shoulder is usually self-limiting, with most patients recovering good function over one to three years, though some, particularly those with diabetes, are left with residual stiffness; interventions can shorten symptoms and relieve pain but recovery is generally slow.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with a rotator cuff tear by not testing passive external rotation","Forcing painful manipulation during the acute inflammatory phase, worsening symptoms","Missing an underlying posterior dislocation or arthritis by omitting radiographs","Overlooking undiagnosed diabetes in a patient presenting with adhesive capsulitis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"functional_dyspepsia":{"name":"Functional dyspepsia","system":"GI / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["non-ulcer dyspepsia","idiopathic dyspepsia","postprandial distress syndrome","epigastric pain syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Functional dyspepsia is chronic epigastric pain, burning, postprandial fullness, or early satiety without an identifiable structural cause on evaluation","It is subdivided into postprandial distress syndrome, dominated by meal-related fullness and early satiety, and epigastric pain syndrome","Diagnosis rests on symptom-based (Rome) criteria after appropriate exclusion of organic disease","Testing for and treating Helicobacter pylori benefits a subset of patients and is a recommended early step","Alarm features such as weight loss, bleeding, dysphagia, persistent vomiting, or older age at onset warrant endoscopy to exclude malignancy and ulcer","Acid suppression is a reasonable first-line therapy, with neuromodulators and prokinetics used for persistent symptoms","There is substantial overlap with gastro-oesophageal reflux disease and irritable bowel syndrome, and psychological factors commonly modulate symptoms"],"pathophysiology":"Functional dyspepsia is a disorder of gut-brain interaction without structural disease. Contributing mechanisms include delayed gastric emptying, impaired fundic accommodation to a meal, visceral hypersensitivity to gastric distension and acid, low-grade duodenal inflammation and increased mucosal permeability, and altered central processing of visceral signals. Prior infection, Helicobacter pylori, psychological stress, and genetic predisposition can all contribute, producing symptoms disproportionate to any measurable physiological abnormality.","additionalDifferentials":["Peptic ulcer disease","Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease","Gastric or oesophageal malignancy","Gastroparesis","Biliary or pancreatic disease","Medication-induced dyspepsia (for example from NSAIDs)","Irritable bowel syndrome with upper gut overlap"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Helicobacter pylori testing (breath, stool antigen, or biopsy)","Upper endoscopy when alarm features are present or symptoms are refractory","Full blood count to screen for anaemia","Consideration of gastric emptying study when gastroparesis is suspected","Targeted biliary or pancreatic imaging when the history suggests these"],"redFlags":["Unintentional weight loss","Gastrointestinal bleeding or iron-deficiency anaemia","Progressive dysphagia or odynophagia","Persistent vomiting or a palpable epigastric mass","New dyspepsia in an older patient"],"prognosis":"Functional dyspepsia is a benign but often chronic and relapsing condition that can substantially impair quality of life; symptoms fluctuate over time and respond variably to treatment, but there is no increase in serious organic disease.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise and evaluate alarm features before labelling symptoms as functional","Repeated normal investigations without addressing the gut-brain nature of the disorder","Overlooking Helicobacter pylori as a treatable contributor","Escalating acid suppression indefinitely without considering neuromodulators or psychological factors"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"g6pd_deficiency":{"name":"Glucose-6-Phosphate Dehydrogenase Deficiency","system":"Haematological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["G6PD deficiency","favism","G6PD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common human enzyme defect, inherited in an X-linked pattern so that males are predominantly affected while heterozygous females show variable expression.","Most individuals are asymptomatic between episodes and present only with acute haemolysis triggered by oxidative stress.","Common triggers include certain infections, fava (broad) beans, and oxidant drugs such as some antimalarials, sulphonamides, and nitrofurantoin.","Acute haemolysis produces sudden pallor, jaundice, dark urine, and fatigue, typically 1-3 days after exposure to a trigger.","It is an important cause of neonatal jaundice, which can be severe and lead to kernicterus if unrecognised.","The blood film during a crisis may show bite cells, blister cells, and Heinz bodies, with a rise in reticulocytes.","Enzyme activity assay is diagnostic but can be falsely normal during an acute crisis because young reticulocytes have higher enzyme levels, so retesting after recovery is advised.","Management centres on avoiding known triggers; acute severe haemolysis may require transfusion, and neonatal jaundice is managed with phototherapy or exchange transfusion as needed."],"pathophysiology":"Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase is the rate-limiting enzyme of the pentose phosphate pathway and generates NADPH, which maintains reduced glutathione in red cells. Deficiency impairs this antioxidant defence, so under oxidative stress haemoglobin is oxidised and denatured, precipitating as Heinz bodies within red cells. These damaged cells are removed by splenic macrophages (producing bite cells) and undergo intravascular haemolysis, resulting in episodic anaemia, jaundice, and haemoglobinuria.","additionalDifferentials":["Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia","Hereditary spherocytosis","Other red cell enzyme defects (e.g. pyruvate kinase deficiency)","Unstable haemoglobin disorders","Drug- or toxin-induced haemolysis from other causes","Neonatal jaundice of other aetiology (ABO/Rh incompatibility, sepsis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative G6PD enzyme activity assay (repeat after recovery if measured during crisis)","Full blood count and reticulocyte count","Peripheral blood film for bite cells, blister cells, and Heinz body preparation","Markers of haemolysis: bilirubin, lactate dehydrogenase, haptoglobin","Urinalysis for haemoglobinuria","Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test to help exclude immune haemolysis"],"redFlags":["Rapidly falling haemoglobin with haemodynamic compromise","Massive haemolysis with haemoglobinuria and acute kidney injury","Severe neonatal hyperbilirubinaemia risking kernicterus","Marked jaundice with lethargy or poor feeding in a neonate","Ongoing exposure to an unrecognised oxidative trigger"],"prognosis":"Most affected individuals lead normal lives with trigger avoidance; haemolytic episodes are usually self-limiting once the trigger is removed, though neonatal jaundice and rare severe crises can cause serious harm if not promptly managed.","pitfalls":["Interpreting a normal enzyme level during acute haemolysis as excluding the diagnosis","Failing to counsel families about drug, food, and infection triggers","Underestimating the risk of severe neonatal jaundice and kernicterus","Prescribing oxidant medications without checking G6PD status in at-risk populations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"galactosemia":{"name":"Galactosemia","system":"Genetic-metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["classic galactosemia","galactosaemia","GALT deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive disorder of galactose metabolism; the classic and most severe form results from deficiency of galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase (GALT).","Symptoms typically begin in the first days to weeks of life once milk feeding (which contains lactose, a source of galactose) is established.","Presenting features include poor feeding, vomiting, jaundice, hepatomegaly, failure to thrive, and lethargy, often progressing to liver dysfunction and coagulopathy.","Affected neonates are at markedly increased risk of Escherichia coli sepsis, which may be the presenting or dominant feature.","Cataracts can develop early owing to accumulation of galactitol in the lens.","Diagnosis is supported by newborn screening and confirmed by demonstrating reduced GALT enzyme activity and characteristic metabolite abnormalities; a reducing substance may be found in the urine.","The cornerstone of management is strict, lifelong elimination of galactose and lactose from the diet, which reverses the acute toxicity.","Despite early dietary treatment, long-term complications such as speech and cognitive difficulties, ataxia, and ovarian insufficiency in females may still occur.","Milder variant forms (for example some Duarte variants) may have residual enzyme activity and a more benign course."],"pathophysiology":"Deficiency of galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase blocks the conversion of galactose-1-phosphate to UDP-galactose in the Leloir pathway. Ingested galactose, mainly from lactose in milk, cannot be normally metabolised, so galactose-1-phosphate accumulates in tissues including the liver, kidney, and brain and exerts toxic effects. Excess galactose is also shunted to alternative pathways, generating galactitol, which accumulates in the lens and causes cataract, and galactonate. The metabolite toxicity produces hepatocellular injury, renal tubular dysfunction, and central nervous system effects, and impairs host defences, predisposing to Gram-negative sepsis.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal sepsis of other cause","Other causes of neonatal cholestasis and liver failure","Tyrosinemia and other hepatorenal metabolic disorders","Hereditary fructose intolerance","Neonatal hemochromatosis","Urea cycle disorders presenting with encephalopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Newborn screening result and confirmatory GALT enzyme activity assay","Quantitative galactose-1-phosphate and molecular genetic testing","Liver function tests and coagulation studies","Urine for reducing substances (a supportive, non-specific finding)","Blood cultures and sepsis evaluation given the risk of E. coli infection","Ophthalmological examination for cataracts"],"redFlags":["Signs of Escherichia coli or other Gram-negative sepsis in a jaundiced neonate","Acute liver failure with coagulopathy and bleeding","Progressive lethargy, poor feeding, and encephalopathy","Severe hypoglycaemia or metabolic decompensation","Continued galactose exposure delaying recognition and worsening toxicity"],"prognosis":"Early diagnosis and strict dietary galactose restriction reverse the life-threatening acute manifestations. However, even with good control, a substantial proportion of children experience long-term neurodevelopmental, speech, and motor difficulties, and most affected females develop primary ovarian insufficiency. Variant forms are generally milder.","pitfalls":["Treating neonatal sepsis without considering an underlying metabolic disorder such as galactosemia","Delaying dietary galactose elimination while awaiting confirmatory results in a decompensating infant","Assuming that early dietary treatment fully prevents all long-term complications","Overlooking milder variants that may still need dietary attention","Failing to arrange ophthalmological and developmental follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"gallbladder_disease":{"name":"Gallbladder & Biliary Tract Disease","system":"Hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cholelithiasis","gallstones","choledocholithiasis","PSC","PBC"],"chapter":357,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2727-2737","clinicalPearls":["Two gallstone types: cholesterol stones (>90% in Western countries, contain >50% cholesterol monohydrate) and pigment stones (mainly calcium bilirubinate, <20% cholesterol, split into 'black' and 'brown' types) (p.2728-2729).","Cholesterol gallstones arise from three defects: (1) bile supersaturation with cholesterol, (2) accelerated nucleation of cholesterol monohydrate crystals, and (3) gallbladder hypomotility/stasis (p.2729).","Black pigment stones associate with chronic hemolysis, alcohol-related cirrhosis, Gilbert's syndrome, cystic fibrosis, and ileal disease/resection/bypass; brown pigment stones form secondary to chronic biliary infection/parasitism with deconjugation of bilirubin (p.2729).","Biliary colic is the most specific symptom: a steady (not intermittent) severe ache or fullness in the epigastrium/RUQ radiating to the interscapular area, right scapula, or shoulder, lasting 30 min to 5 h; the word 'colic' is a misnomer (p.2730-2731).","60-80% of asymptomatic gallstones remain asymptomatic over up to 25 years; symptom development is ~2-4% per year (falling to 1-2% later) and yearly complication rate is ~0.1-0.3%, so prophylactic cholecystectomy is not warranted (p.2731).","Ultrasound is the procedure of choice for detecting gallstones (>95% accuracy) and can identify stones as small as 1.5 mm using acoustic shadowing criteria (p.2730).","Biliary sludge (lecithin-cholesterol liquid crystals, cholesterol monohydrate crystals, calcium bilirubinate, mucin) shifts with posture but does NOT cast an acoustic shadow, distinguishing it from stones; it can be a gallstone precursor (p.2730).","Acute cholecystitis usually follows cystic duct obstruction by a stone; Murphy's sign (inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation) is characteristic, and the triad of sudden RUQ tenderness, fever, and leukocytosis is highly suggestive (p.2732-2733).","Charcot's triad (biliary pain, jaundice, spiking fevers with chills) characterizes acute cholangitis; suppurative cholangitis with a completely obstructed duct under pressure causes mental confusion, bacteremia, and septic shock (Reynolds' pentad features) (p.2735).","Choledocholithiasis (CBD stones) occurs in ~10-15% of patients with cholelithiasis; incidence rises with age (up to 25% of elderly at cholecystectomy); most are cholesterol stones that migrated from the gallbladder (p.2735).","MRCP is the first choice to assess for choledocholithiasis (comparable sensitivity/specificity to ERCP); ERCP is the cholangiogram of choice when intervention is needed and ERCP with sphincterotomy is preferred for both diagnosis and therapy of bacterial cholangitis (p.2736, 2735).","PBC: female-predominant (~50 yr median age) autoimmune disease with portal inflammation and necrosis of cholangiocytes in small/medium bile ducts; antimitochondrial antibodies (AMA) present in ~95%; key symptoms are fatigue and pruritus (Harrison 22e ch.355 p.2711).","PSC is strongly associated with inflammatory bowel disease (60-80%, predominantly ulcerative colitis); cholangiography shows multifocal strictures with intervening normal/dilated segments producing a 'beaded' appearance; pANCA positive in ~65% (ch.357 p.2737; ch.355 p.2712).","Cholangiocarcinoma is classified as intrahepatic (~20-30%), perihilar (~50-60%), and distal (~20-30%); PSC is a clear risk factor for iCCA and pCCA with a 5-10% lifetime incidence, and is a relative contraindication to liver transplantation (ch.87 p.667; ch.355 p.2712).","Gallbladder cancer: cholelithiasis is the major risk factor but <1% of patients with gallstones develop it; polyps >=10 mm are at risk of transformation; early cases are found incidentally at cholecystectomy and symptoms (jaundice, pain, weight loss) indicate advanced disease (ch.87 p.670)."],"pathophysiology":"Bile flow depends on sphincter of Oddi tone and cholecystokinin (CCK)-mediated gallbladder contraction after fatty/amino-acid meals. Gallstones form from abnormal bile composition. Cholesterol stones (>90% in Western countries) require three converging defects: hepatic hypersecretion of cholesterol producing lithogenic/supersaturated bile (driven by obesity, metabolic syndrome, estrogens, rapid weight loss, age, CYP7A1 or MDR3/ABCB4 gene defects, ABCG5/G8 variants), accelerated nucleation of cholesterol monohydrate crystals (excess pronucleating mucin/immunoglobulins vs deficient antinucleating apolipoproteins), and gallbladder hypomotility with stasis (fasting, TPN, pregnancy, octreotide). Pigment stones contain calcium bilirubinate: black stones from chronic hemolysis, cirrhosis, Gilbert's, cystic fibrosis, or ileal disease (enterohepatic bilirubin recycling); brown stones from deconjugation of bilirubin by bacterial beta-glucuronidase in chronically infected/parasitized bile. Symptoms arise when stones obstruct the cystic duct (cholecystitis) or migrate into the CBD (choledocholithiasis) causing biliary obstruction, cholangitis, or gallstone pancreatitis as they pass through the ampulla of Vater. PBC and PSC are chronic cholestatic syndromes from immune-mediated destruction of bile ducts (small/medium ducts in PBC; entire biliary tree in PSC) progressing to biliary cirrhosis and portal hypertension.","additionalDifferentials":["Acalculous cholecystitis (5-14% of acute cholecystitis; associated with prolonged fasting, severe trauma/burns, postpartum, major nonbiliary surgery, TPN) (p.2733)","Acalculous cholecystopathy / biliary dyskinesia (gallbladder ejection fraction <40% on CCK cholescintigraphy reproducing pain) (p.2733)","Sphincter of Oddi dysfunction (stenosis or dyskinesia) (p.2734)","Choledochal cysts and Caroli's disease / congenital biliary ectasia (recurrent cholangitis, increased cholangiocarcinoma risk) (p.2735)","Benign bile duct strictures (post-surgical, ~1 in 500 cholecystectomies) (p.2737)","Extrinsic bile duct compression (most commonly carcinoma of the head of the pancreas) (p.2737)","IgG4-associated cholangitis (mimics PSC; elevated serum IgG4; steroid-responsive) (p.2737)","Hyperplastic cholecystoses (adenomyomatosis, cholesterolosis/'strawberry gallbladder') (p.2737)","Postcholecystectomy syndrome (overlooked reflux esophagitis, peptic ulcer, IBS, retained stones, bile-salt diarrhea) (p.2734)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute (ascending) cholangitis from bacterial superinfection of obstructed bile (p.2735)","Hepatobiliary parasitism (Clonorchis sinensis, Opisthorchis viverrini/felineus, Fasciola hepatica, Ascaris, Echinococcus) causing recurrent pyogenic cholangitis (p.2737)","Emphysematous cholecystitis from gas-producing organisms (Clostridium welchii/perfringens, E. coli) in elderly/diabetic patients (p.2733)","AIDS cholangiopathy (Cryptosporidium, Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare, CMV, Microsporidia, Isospora) mimicking sclerosing cholangitis (p.2737)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Malignant biliary obstruction (pancreatic head carcinoma, cholangiocarcinoma, ampullary tumor) - Courvoisier's law: a palpably enlarged gallbladder suggests malignancy rather than stones (p.2736)","Functional/vague dyspepsia, eructation, flatulence after fatty meals - common with or without gallstones but not specific for biliary calculi (p.2731)","Other postcholecystectomy nonbiliary disorders (reflux esophagitis, peptic ulcer, pancreatitis, irritable bowel syndrome) (p.2734)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound - procedure of choice for gallstone detection (>95%), gallbladder wall thickening, pericholecystic fluid, dilated ducts (p.2730, 2733)","MRCP - first choice for choledocholithiasis and the cholangiographic technique of choice in suspected PSC (beaded ducts) (p.2736-2737)","ERCP with endoscopic sphincterotomy - diagnostic and therapeutic for CBD stones and bacterial cholangitis (p.2735-2736)","Cholescintigraphy (HIDA/DISIDA) - confirms cystic duct obstruction / acute cholecystitis; CCK-stimulated for ejection fraction in acalculous cholecystopathy (p.2730, 2733)","Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) - most sensitive for ampullary stones and head-of-pancreas pathology; detects microlithiasis (p.2736)","Plain abdominal film - calcified stones, porcelain gallbladder, limey bile, emphysematous cholecystitis, gallstone ileus (pneumobilia) (p.2730)","Serum bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase - elevation suggests common duct stone; ALP rise often precedes jaundice (p.2731, 2736)","AMA (and sp100/gp210 if AMA-negative) for PBC; IgM elevation; serum IgG4 to exclude IgG4 cholangitis; pANCA in PSC; CA 19-9 for cholangiocarcinoma surveillance in PSC (ch.355 p.2711, ch.357 p.2737, ch.87 p.667)","Endoscopic brushing of biliary strictures (more accurate than bile cytology alone) in suspected neoplastic stricture, especially in PSC (p.2737)"],"redFlags":["Biliary pain persisting beyond 5 h - raises suspicion of acute cholecystitis (p.2731)","Fever or chills (rigors) with biliary pain - implies a complication (cholecystitis, pancreatitis, or cholangitis) (p.2731)","Charcot's triad (biliary pain, jaundice, spiking fevers/chills) - acute cholangitis (p.2735)","Mental confusion, bacteremia, septic shock (suppurative cholangitis) - mortality approaches 100% without prompt drainage (p.2735)","Serum bilirubin >=342 micromol/L (20 mg/dL) - suggests neoplastic rather than calculous obstruction (p.2736)","Painless jaundice and/or a palpably enlarged gallbladder (Courvoisier) - points to malignancy (p.2736)","Sudden transient relief of RUQ pain followed by generalized peritonitis - free gallbladder perforation (~30% mortality) (p.2733)","Pruritus preceding jaundice in PBC - indicates severe disease and poor prognosis (ch.355 p.2711)","New/rising bilirubin or dominant stricture in PSC - concern for cholangiocarcinoma (ch.355 p.2712; ch.87 p.667)"],"prognosis":"Most asymptomatic gallstones stay silent (60-80% over up to 25 years); cumulative death risk on expectant management is small. Laparoscopic cholecystectomy is the gold standard with complications ~4%, conversion ~5%, death rate <0.1%, and bile duct injury 0.2-0.6%; it relieves preoperative symptoms in 75-90%. Acute cholecystitis: ~75% remit medically within 2-7 days but ~25% recur within 1 year and 60% within 6 years; free perforation carries ~30% mortality and suppurative cholangitis approaches 100% mortality without drainage. PSC is progressive with a median survival of 12-18 years regardless of therapy and is a common indication for liver transplantation; small-duct PSC has a significantly better prognosis. PBC: UDCA slows but does not reverse disease; transplantation for decompensated cirrhosis. Cholangiocarcinoma has a 5-year survival of ~10%; gallbladder cancer is curable (5-yr survival 60-90%) only when local and resected (~20% of patients), with median survival 10-12 months in advanced disease (ch.357 p.2731-2737; ch.355 p.2711-2712; ch.87 p.667-670).","pitfalls":["'Biliary colic' is a misnomer - the pain is steady, not intermittent (p.2731)","Vague postprandial dyspepsia, eructation, and flatulence are not specific for gallstones and should not be confused with biliary pain (p.2731)","Pigment stones do NOT respond to UDCA dissolution therapy, and stones >10 mm rarely dissolve - only ~10% of symptomatic patients are dissolution candidates with 30-50% recurrence over 3-5 years (p.2731-2732)","Jaundice is unusual early in acute cholecystitis; its presence (or bilirubin >5 mg/dL) should prompt suspicion of a CBD stone (p.2732, 2736)","Missing Mirizzi's syndrome preoperatively risks CBD injury at surgery (p.2733)","AMA is negative in 5-10% of PBC - liver biopsy and sp100/gp210 are needed, and PSC should be excluded by cholangiography (ch.355 p.2711)","Every patient diagnosed with sclerosing cholangitis should have serum IgG4 checked to exclude IgG4-associated (secondary) cholangitis, especially without IBD (p.2737)","Routine prophylactic cholecystectomy in asymptomatic stones (including young patients and diabetics) is not warranted (p.2731)","The carcinoma risk of porcelain gallbladder has been challenged; cholecystectomy is no longer absolute and surveillance is acceptable (p.2734)"],"severityClassification":"Stone-based spectrum: asymptomatic gallstones -> biliary colic (cystic duct/CBD obstruction) -> acute cholecystitis -> complications (empyema, hydrops/mucocele, gangrene, perforation, fistula with gallstone ileus, emphysematous cholecystitis, Mirizzi's syndrome). CBD stone (choledocholithiasis) complications: cholangitis (nonsuppurative vs suppurative), obstructive jaundice, gallstone pancreatitis, secondary biliary cirrhosis. Cholangitis severity by Charcot's triad (pain + jaundice + fever/chills) advancing to suppurative cholangitis (adds confusion + septic shock). PBC has four histologic stages from chronic nonsuppurative destructive cholangitis to cirrhosis. PSC: small-duct (better prognosis) vs classic (large-duct) disease. Cholangiocarcinoma classified anatomically as intrahepatic, perihilar, and distal; gallbladder cancer staged as local vs advanced (ch.357 p.2731-2737; ch.355 p.2711; ch.87 p.667-670).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2727-2737","Harrison 22e ch.355 p.2710-2712 (PBC/PSC, Chronic Hepatitis & Cirrhosis)","Harrison 22e ch.87 p.667-670 (Cholangiocarcinoma and Gallbladder Cancer)"],"crossLinks":["biliary_colic","CHOLECYSTITIS","CHOLANGITIS","pancreatitis","inflammatory_bowel_disease"]},"gas_gangrene":{"name":"Gas Gangrene (Clostridial Myonecrosis)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["clostridial myonecrosis"],"chapter":159,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1238-1243","clinicalPearls":["C. perfringens myonecrosis (gas gangrene) is one of the most fulminant gram-positive bacterial infections of humans; it is a true emergency requiring immediate surgical debridement and is invariably fatal if untreated (p.1241).","Traumatic gas gangrene presents with sudden onset of excruciating pain at the affected site and rapid development of a foul-smelling wound with thin serosanguineous discharge and gas bubbles; brawny edema and induration give way to cutaneous blisters with bluish to maroon-colored fluid (p.1241).","The patient's pain and toxic appearance are characteristically out of proportion to physical findings; the patient may be febrile, apathetic, tachycardic, tachypneic, and may express a feeling of impending doom (p.1218, cross-ref Chap.159).","The margin between healthy and necrotic tissue often advances several inches per hour despite appropriate antibiotic therapy; radical amputation remains the single best life-saving intervention (p.1241).","Incubation period for traumatic gas gangrene can be as short as 6 h and is usually <4 days (p.1241).","Spontaneous (nontraumatic) gas gangrene arises by hematogenous seeding of normal muscle, principally with C. septicum (aerotolerant), C. perfringens, and C. novyi, from a gastrointestinal portal of entry (p.1241).","First symptom of spontaneous gas gangrene may be confusion followed by abrupt onset of excruciating pain in the absence of trauma; with fever, these findings should heighten suspicion (p.1242).","Gram staining of drainage or tissue biopsy is usually definitive in traumatic disease, showing large gram-positive (or gram-variable) rods, an ABSENCE of inflammatory cells, and widespread soft tissue necrosis (p.1241).","CT and MRI help determine whether infection is localized or spreading along fascial planes, but must NOT replace surgical exploration, Gram's staining, and histopathologic examination (p.1242).","Emergent thorough surgical debridement with wide resection back to healthy viable muscle and skin is of extreme importance; closure of traumatic wounds or compound fractures should be delayed 5-6 days until sites are certain to be free of infection (p.1242)."],"pathophysiology":"Development requires an anaerobic environment and wound contamination with clostridial spores or vegetative organisms. Devitalized tissue, foreign bodies, and ischemia lower local oxygen and favor outgrowth. For C. perfringens and C. novyi, trauma must be sufficient to interrupt the blood supply to establish the anaerobic niche; the more aerotolerant C. septicum and C. tertium can seed normal tissue from GI lesions without this. Once established, organisms proliferate and elaborate exotoxins. The major C. perfringens toxins in gas gangrene are alpha toxin (a lethal hemolysin with phospholipase C and sphingomyelinase activity; the major virulence factor) and theta toxin (perfringolysin, a cholesterol-dependent cytolysin). Severe pain, rapid progression, marked tissue destruction, and the characteristic paucity of neutrophils in tissue are largely attributable to alpha-toxin-induced vascular occlusion by heterotypic platelet/neutrophil aggregates (via activation of platelet gpIIb/IIIa). Late cardiovascular collapse reflects theta toxin causing reduced systemic vascular resistance with increased cardiac output ('warm shock') and alpha toxin directly suppressing myocardial contractility, producing profound hypotension (p.1242).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Necrotizing fasciitis (including type I polymicrobial and C. perfringens necrotizing fasciitis); distinction from clostridial myonecrosis is made by muscle biopsy (p.1218/1241)","Other histotoxic clostridial necrotizing soft tissue infections (C. histolyticum, C. septicum, C. novyi, C. sordellii) (p.1241)","Synergistic necrotizing cellulitis / crepitant (crepitus) cellulitis due to mixed anaerobic and aerobic flora (p.gas-forming soft-tissue infections)","Fournier's gangrene (C. perfringens with mixed aerobic/anaerobic microbes can cause aggressive type I necrotizing fasciitis or Fournier's gangrene) (p.1239)","Clostridial sepsis / bacteremia with myonecrosis (grave prognosis when bacteremia accompanies myonecrosis) (p.1240)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Gas tracking/dissecting tissue planes from non-clostridial gas-forming processes; note gas gangrene may develop in the ABSENCE of clostridial infection (other gas-producing organisms) (p.crepitus/gas-forming infections)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Gram stain of a smear of involved tissue (direct examination is of major importance): large gram-positive/gram-variable rods with paucity of leukocytes (p.1242)","Surgical exploration with histopathologic examination (widespread tissue destruction, paucity of leukocytes in infected tissue with accumulation of leukocytes in adjacent vessels, gram-positive rods with or without spores) (p.1242)","Blood cultures (anaerobic) - obtain when spontaneous gas gangrene is suspected, as bacteremia usually precedes cutaneous manifestations by several hours (p.1242)","CT and MRI to define localized vs fascial-plane spread (adjunct only; must not delay surgery) (p.1242)","Needle aspiration or punch biopsy may provide an etiologic diagnosis in at least 20% of cases (p.1242)","In survivors of C. septicum bacteremia/spontaneous gangrene: aggressive diagnostic workup to rule out gastrointestinal pathology (e.g., colonic malignancy) (p.1241)"],"redFlags":["Pain out of proportion to physical findings - excruciating pain at the affected site (p.1241/1218)","Crepitus and gas in the tissues / gas on imaging with rapidly progressive necrosis (p.1241-1242)","Tissue margin advancing several inches per hour despite antibiotics (p.1241)","Systemic toxaemia: shock, hypotension, multiorgan failure, bacteremia, intravascular hemolysis (p.1241-1242)","Foul-smelling serosanguineous wound discharge with bluish/maroon hemorrhagic bullae and brawny induration (p.1241)","Spontaneous excruciating pain with confusion and fever and NO obvious portal of entry - suggests spontaneous gas gangrene (correct diagnosis frequently delayed or missed) (p.1242)","Feeling of impending doom with apathy, tachycardia, tachypnea (p.1218)"],"prognosis":"Invariably fatal if untreated. When patients become bacteremic the mortality rate exceeds 50%. Spontaneous (nontraumatic) gas gangrene mortality ranges from 67-100% in adults and 59% in children (most childhood deaths within 24 h of onset). Prognosis is more favorable when an extremity is involved (easier debridement) than the trunk or visceral organs. Disease is most likely to progress to shock and death in patients with associated bacteremia and intravascular hemolysis; mortality is highest for patients in shock at diagnosis. Gas gangrene associated with bacteremia represents a later stage with the worst outcomes. Survivors may undergo multiple debridements and face prolonged hospitalization and rehabilitation (p.1242-1243).","pitfalls":["Diagnosis depends on clinical signs and symptoms, not solely on bacteriologic findings - 30-80% of open traumatic wounds are contaminated with clostridia and clostridia are isolated equally from suppurative and well-healing wounds (p.1239).","In spontaneous gas gangrene the lack of an obvious portal of entry means the correct diagnosis is frequently delayed or missed (p.1242).","Adjunctive procedures (CT, MRI) or transport to hyperbaric oxygen (HBO) units must NOT delay emergent surgical debridement (p.1243).","The value of HBO remains controversial; HBO inhibits C. perfringens but not the more aerotolerant C. septicum, and blood/macerated muscle inhibit its bactericidal potential in vitro - HBO provides benefit only if surgery and antibiotics precede it (p.1243).","Avoid prolonged tourniquet application and surgical closure of contaminated traumatic wounds; patients with compound fractures are at significant risk if the wound is closed surgically (p.1243).","C. perfringens remains largely penicillin-susceptible but clindamycin resistance has been reported and is increasing in some regions; C. tertium is resistant to penicillin, cephalosporins, and clindamycin - good anaerobic susceptibility testing matters (p.1242-1243)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bryant AE, Stevens DL. Gas Gangrene and Other Clostridial Infections. Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Chap. 159, pp. 1238-1243.","Bryant AE et al: Clostridial gas gangrene II: Phospholipase C-induced... necrosis in C. perfringens gas gangrene. J Infect Dis 182:808, 2000.","Aronoff DM et al: Infections caused by Clostridium perfringens and... (Harrison's Chap. 159 references)."],"crossLinks":["NECROTIZING_FASCIITIS"]},"gastrointestinal_endoscopy":{"name":"Gastrointestinal Endoscopy","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["GI endoscopy","EGD","esophagogastroduodenoscopy","colonoscopy","ERCP","endoscopic ultrasound","EUS"],"chapter":333,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2464","clinicalPearls":["Flexible endoscopes generate an electronic video image from a CCD or CMOS chip at the tip, with operator tip-deflection controls, light delivery, and working channels for washing, suction, and accessory passage (Harrison 22e p.2465).","Upper endoscopy (EGD) is the best method for examining the upper GI mucosa and is superior to a barium upper-GI series for detecting gastric ulcers and flat lesions such as Barrett's esophagus, while also permitting directed biopsy and therapy (Harrison 22e p.2465).","Colonoscopy is the gold standard for imaging the colonic mucosa, reaching the cecum in over 95% of cases, with greater sensitivity than barium enema for colitis, polyps, and cancer; CT colonography rivals it for many polyps and cancers but is less sensitive for flat serrated lesions (Harrison 22e p.2465).","For obscure small-bowel bleeding, capsule endoscopy visualizes mucosa beyond the reach of a conventional endoscope but is purely diagnostic; push and balloon-assisted enteroscopy allow deeper intubation with biopsy and therapy (Harrison 22e p.2465-2466).","EUS provides the most accurate preoperative local staging of esophageal, pancreatic, and rectal malignancies but does not detect distant metastases beyond its imaging range; it also enables fine-needle aspiration/biopsy and a growing range of therapeutic drainage procedures (Harrison 22e p.2468).","Endoscopic stigmata of a peptic ulcer carry prognostic value: a clean-based ulcer has a low (3-5%) rebleeding risk, flat pigmented spots ~10%, adherent clot ~20%, a visible vessel ~40%, and active spurting ~90% (Harrison 22e p.2478-2479).","Over 90% of patients with melena bleed proximal to the ligament of Treitz and ~85% with hematochezia bleed from the colon, but massive hematochezia can reflect a brisk upper-GI source with rapid transit, warranting urgent upper endoscopy (Harrison 22e p.2476).","Severity of the initial hemorrhage is the most important indicator for urgent endoscopy; falling hematocrit and hemoglobin lag behind the clinical course and are unreliable gauges of acute blood loss (Harrison 22e p.2476).","Acute esophageal obstruction from food impaction or a foreign body is an endoscopic emergency; untreated it can lead to esophageal ulceration, ischemia, and perforation, and an underlying web, ring, or stricture is often present (Harrison 22e p.2484-2485).","In acute colonic pseudoobstruction (Ogilvie's), the risk of cecal perforation rises when cecal diameter exceeds 12 cm, prompting decompression with intravenous neostigmine or colonoscopy (Harrison 22e p.2487).","Charcot's triad (jaundice, abdominal pain, fever) is present in ~70% of ascending cholangitis; the addition of shock and confusion (Reynolds's pentad) signals high mortality and warrants urgent biliary drainage, typically by ERCP (Harrison 22e p.2489).","Long-standing GERD confers a roughly sixfold increased risk of Barrett's esophagus versus reflux of under one year; screening EGD should be considered after a chronic (>=10-year) GERD history even when symptoms are mild (Harrison 22e p.2490-2491)."],"pathophysiology":"Gastrointestinal endoscopy is a diagnostic and therapeutic modality rather than a disease; it directly visualizes mucosal and luminal pathology and adjacent structures (via EUS) and provides access for tissue sampling and intervention. Many of the conditions it manages share a vascular or mechanical basis: peptic ulcers bleed when erosion exposes a submucosal vessel; a Dieulafoy's lesion is an abnormally large-caliber persistent artery running just beneath the epithelium that bleeds through a focal mucosal defect; a Mallory-Weiss tear is a mucosal rent at the gastroesophageal junction caused by retching/vomiting that can disrupt a submucosal arteriole; colonic diverticular bleeding arises from the nutrient artery at the base of a diverticulum; and vascular ectasias are flat mucosal vascular anomalies (e.g., GAVE/watermelon stomach) that cause slow blood loss. Variceal bleeding reflects portal hypertension. Obstructive syndromes (esophageal food impaction, gastric outlet obstruction, colonic obstruction/pseudoobstruction, biliary obstruction by stone) produce luminal blockage that endoscopy can relieve. Barrett's esophagus represents columnar metaplasia of distal esophageal squamous mucosa driven by chronic acid reflux and is a precursor to adenocarcinoma (Harrison 22e p.2464-2491).","additionalDifferentials":["Melena: distinguish proximal upper-GI bleeding from slow right-colon or small-bowel sources, especially in older patients with slow colonic transit","Hematochezia: colonic source vs brisk upper-GI bleed with rapid transit","Dyspepsia: gastroesophageal reflux vs peptic ulcer disease vs nonulcer (functional) dyspepsia vs uncommon gastric/esophageal malignancy","Colonic dilatation: mechanical obstruction (e.g., malignancy, sigmoid volvulus) vs acute colonic pseudoobstruction","Diverticular hematochezia: exclude vascular ectasias, colitis, and colon cancer before attributing bleeding to a diverticulum"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["24-hour ambulatory pH and impedance monitoring as the most sensitive test for GERD, capturing nonerosive reflux disease that endoscopy may miss","MRCP and EUS for bile duct stones (>90% accurate), reserving ERCP for cases needing therapy or high pretest probability","Noninvasive H. pylori testing (serology for past/current infection; urea breath test and stool antigen for current infection) before or in lieu of endoscopy in younger patients without alarm features","Capsule endoscopy as the typical next test after negative upper and lower endoscopy in suspected small-bowel bleeding (diagnostic yield ~55%), with a patency capsule beforehand if stricture risk exists","Plain abdominal radiography (and water-soluble contrast enema or CT) to assess colonic dilatation and differentiate obstruction from pseudoobstruction; neck/chest radiographs before endoscopy for esophageal obstruction with fever, >=24-h impaction, or sharp object"],"redFlags":["Resting hypotension, orthostatic vital-sign change, repeated hematemesis, bloody nasogastric aspirate not clearing with lavage, or transfusion requirement — consider urgent endoscopy within 12-24 h","Dyspepsia or reflux with alarm features (weight loss, obstructive symptoms, dysphagia, GI bleeding, iron-deficiency anemia) — perform endoscopy at the outset","Esophageal food/foreign-body impaction with hypersalivation and inability to swallow water — endoscopic emergency","Reynolds's pentad (Charcot's triad plus shock and confusion) in ascending cholangitis — urgent biliary drainage","Cecal diameter exceeding 12 cm in colonic pseudoobstruction — impending perforation","Age >70, cirrhosis, coagulopathy, or respiratory/renal failure with GI bleeding — higher rebleeding risk warranting prompt evaluation"],"prognosis":"Outcomes depend on the underlying condition and endoscopic findings. Most GI bleeding stops spontaneously. Endoscopic therapy of high-risk peptic ulcer stigmata lowers rebleeding to about 5-10%. Endoscopic variceal ligation controls acute esophageal variceal hemorrhage in up to 90% of patients, though chronic eradication therapy prevents long-term rebleeding in only ~50%. A clean-based ulcer carries a low (3-5%) rebleeding risk, permitting early discharge of suitable patients. Post-ERCP pancreatitis is usually mild and self-limited but can be severe. Delayed biliary drainage in ascending cholangitis increases morbidity and mortality (Harrison 22e p.2464-2491).","pitfalls":["Relying on hematocrit/hemoglobin to gauge acute bleeding — these lag behind the clinical course","Assuming melena always means an upper-GI source or hematochezia always a colonic source — exceptions occur with transit-dependent presentations","Performing ERCP indiscriminately in gallstone pancreatitis — only ~20% retain an ampullary/common-bile-duct stone, and initial MRCP/EUS reduces unnecessary ERCP and its adverse events","Using endoscopy to diagnose nonerosive reflux disease — it will miss symptomatic reflux without esophagitis","Treating a Mallory-Weiss tear bearing a nonbleeding sentinel clot like a peptic ulcer — unlike ulcers, such tears rarely rebleed and need no endoscopic therapy","Relying on epinephrine injection alone for ulcer hemostasis — it slows bleeding but is not definitive monotherapy","Obtaining radiographic contrast studies before endoscopy in suspected esophageal obstruction — contrast interferes with the subsequent endoscopic procedure","Proceeding to endoscopy in marked coagulopathy or thrombocytopenia without correction — hemostatic techniques are limited and correction may itself resolve bleeding"],"severityClassification":"Peptic ulcer rebleeding risk by endoscopic stigmata: clean base 3-5%; flat pigmented spots ~10%; adherent clot ~20%; nonbleeding visible vessel ~40%; active spurting ~90% (Harrison 22e p.2478-2479). Procedural adverse-event risk: bleeding/perforation under 1:1000 for diagnostic EGD, flexible sigmoidoscopy, and colonoscopy, rising to 0.5-5% with therapeutic maneuvers; ERCP carries ~5% pancreatitis risk (up to ~25% in young, anicteric patients with normal ducts) and ~1% post-sphincterotomy bleeding; PEG placement has a 10-15% adverse-event rate (Harrison 22e p.2473-2474).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2464","Harrison 22e p.2465","Harrison 22e p.2466","Harrison 22e p.2468","Harrison 22e p.2473","Harrison 22e p.2474","Harrison 22e p.2476","Harrison 22e p.2478","Harrison 22e p.2479","Harrison 22e p.2484","Harrison 22e p.2487","Harrison 22e p.2489","Harrison 22e p.2490","Harrison 22e p.2491"],"crossLinks":[]},"gastrointestinal_neuroendocrine_tumors":{"name":"Gastrointestinal Neuroendocrine Tumors","system":"Gastrointestinal/Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["GI NETs","Carcinoid tumors","Gastroenteropancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","GEP-NETs","Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","Islet cell tumors"],"chapter":89,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.676","clinicalPearls":["GI NETs are broadly grouped by origin into extrapancreatic NETs (historically called carcinoid tumors) and pancreatic NETs; they typically follow a more indolent course than most malignancies but can synthesize peptides, growth factors, and bioactive amines that are ectopically secreted (Harrison 22e p.676).","Tumors are classified by differentiation (well vs poorly differentiated) and grade 1-3, which closely correlates with mitotic count and Ki-67 index: grade 1 is <2 mitoses/10 HPF and Ki-67 <3%, grade 2 is 2-20/10 HPF and 3-20%, grade 3 is >20/10 HPF and >20%; poorly differentiated high-grade neuroendocrine carcinomas are the most aggressive category (Harrison 22e p.677).","Only about 20% of pancreatic NETs are functional (hormone-secreting); the majority are nonfunctional and present incidentally or with mass-related symptoms such as abdominal pain and weight loss (Harrison 22e p.677).","The most common functional pancreatic NET is insulinoma, followed by glucagonoma and gastrinoma; somatostatinoma, VIPoma, ACTHoma and others are uncommon (Harrison 22e p.677).","Most gastrinomas actually arise in the gastrinoma triangle (bounded by duodenum, pancreas, and confluence of cystic and common bile ducts) rather than the pancreas, and 50-90% of sporadic gastrinomas arise in the duodenum, where they are often small and hard to localize (Harrison 22e p.678).","Glucagonoma classically presents with necrolytic migratory erythema (often intertriginous, waxing and waning), glucose intolerance and weight loss; a hypercoagulable state is reported in up to one-third of patients (Harrison 22e p.679).","Gastric NETs fall into three types: type 1 (chronic atrophic gastritis, most common, generally benign), type 2 (associated with gastrinoma/ZES), and type 3 (sporadic, normal gastrin, potentially far more aggressive) (Harrison 22e p.680).","Small-bowel NETs (most often terminal ileum) arise within the muscularis/submucosa and are notoriously hard to diagnose early; mesenteric fibrosis from serotonin-driven fibroblast proliferation produces a classic calcified spoke-and-wheel mesenteric mass on CT and can cause intermittent obstruction and bowel ischemia (Harrison 22e p.680).","Carcinoid syndrome (flushing, watery diarrhea, right-sided valvular heart disease, mesenteric fibrosis) is most associated with small intestine or appendiceal primaries and usually develops only after hepatic metastases or retroperitoneal lesions allow serotonin and vasoactive substances to enter systemic circulation (Harrison 22e p.681).","More than 90% of NETs express somatostatin receptors, making somatostatin-analogue PET tracers (gallium-68 dotatate, DOTATOC, copper-64 dotatate) highly sensitive for detecting primaries and metastases (Harrison 22e p.681).","Carcinoid heart disease occurs in roughly two-thirds of patients with carcinoid syndrome and is characterized by plaque-like fibrous endocardial thickening classically on the right heart, causing tricuspid regurgitation (nearly universal) plus possible tricuspid stenosis, pulmonary regurgitation, and pulmonary stenosis (Harrison 22e p.683).","A minority of NETs arise within inherited syndromes; MEN 1 (menin gene, 11q13) is the most common, and gastrinoma should prompt a family history and assessment for hyperparathyroidism since up to 25% of ZES patients have MEN 1 (Harrison 22e p.676, 678)."],"pathophysiology":"GI NETs arise from neuroendocrine cells and contain electron-dense neurosecretory granules holding peptides and bioactive amines that can be ectopically secreted. Sporadic pancreatic NETs most frequently harbor MEN1 mutations (about 44%), with frequent mutations in the DAXX/ATRX chromatin-remodeling complex (about 43%) and mTOR-pathway genes (about 15%); recurrent mutations in extrapancreatic NETs are rare, though small-bowel NETs commonly show loss of chromosome 18 and CDKN1B mutations in a minority. In carcinoid syndrome the principal mediator is serotonin (5-HT), synthesized from tryptophan via tryptophan hydroxylase; up to 50% of dietary tryptophan can be diverted into this pathway, which can deplete niacin and produce pellagra-like lesions. Histamine and tachykinins such as substance P also contribute. Serotonin stimulates intestinal secretion and motility and drives fibroblast growth, accounting for mesenteric fibrosis and for valvular fibrosis via activation of 5-HT2B receptors on heart-valve fibroblasts. Right-sided cardiac predominance reflects venous delivery of serotonin from hepatic/retroperitoneal deposits to the right heart, with pulmonary metabolism sparing the left heart. Inherited syndromes (MEN 1, von Hippel-Lindau, neurofibromatosis type 1, tuberous sclerosis) underlie a minority of cases (Harrison 22e p.676-683).","additionalDifferentials":["Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma and other nonneuroendocrine pancreatic cancers","Irritable bowel syndrome (small-bowel NETs are frequently misattributed to IBS for months to years)","Psychiatric illness (insulinoma hypoglycemic symptoms may be mistaken for psychiatric disease)","Mixed neuroendocrine-adenocarcinoma tumors (adenocarcinoids) of the appendix"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Systemic mastocytosis (symptoms can be easily confused with carcinoid syndrome)","Other causes of fasting hypoglycemia: severe liver disease, alcoholism, poor nutrition, post-gastric-bypass postprandial hypoglycemia, surreptitious insulin or sulfonylurea use (mimicking insulinoma)","Causes of secretory diarrhea mimicking VIPoma: inflammatory bowel disease, short-bowel after small-bowel resection, radiation enteritis, surreptitious laxative/diuretic use","Causes of hypergastrinemia other than gastrinoma: proton pump inhibitor use and achlorhydria from chronic atrophic gastritis (mimicking gastrinoma)","Drug-induced valvulopathy mimicking carcinoid heart disease: fenfluramine and ergot-containing dopamine agonists (both act via 5-HT2B receptors)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Somatostatin-receptor imaging: gallium-68 dotatate (or DOTATOC, copper-64 dotatate) PET, highly sensitive for primaries and metastases","Cross-sectional imaging with CT or MRI; endoscopic ultrasound especially for small pancreatic primaries such as insulinomas","FDG-PET (often negative in low-grade well-differentiated NETs but positive in higher-grade tumors)","Fasting insulin with proinsulin and C-peptide (elevated in insulinoma; proinsulin/C-peptide normal with exogenous insulin) and serum/plasma sulfonylurea levels","Fasting serum gastrin (interpret with awareness of PPI and achlorhydria effects)","Plasma glucagon (glucagonoma generally >1000 pg/mL), plasma somatostatin, plasma VIP","24-hour urinary or plasma 5-HIAA for carcinoid syndrome (suggestive, not diagnostic; serotonin-rich foods such as salmon and eggs cause false positives)","Chromogranin A (neither sensitive nor specific; falsely elevated in renal impairment and with PPI use)","Histologic grading with mitotic count and Ki-67 plus chromogranin/synaptophysin immunostaining; vitamin B12 and antiparietal evaluation in type 1 gastric NET with atrophic gastritis"],"redFlags":["Carcinoid crisis: life-threatening profound hypotension or hypertension triggered by tumor manipulation during surgery, hepatic artery embolization, radionuclide therapy, exogenous epinephrine, or anesthesia induction","Right-sided heart failure from carcinoid heart disease, a major source of morbidity and mortality","Severe refractory hypoglycemia from insulinoma causing confusion, coma","Mesenteric fibrosis causing intermittent small-bowel obstruction or bowel ischemia","Profound watery diarrhea with hypokalemia and dehydration in VIPoma (Verner-Morrison/WDHA syndrome)"],"prognosis":"NETs are typically more indolent than other malignancies, and both incidence and survival have risen over recent decades (US incidence rose about 6.4-fold from 1973 to 2012; prevalence exceeded 170,000). Prognosis depends strongly on primary site and histologic grade. Median survival for advanced pancreatic NETs rose from roughly 2 years in early SEER analyses to about 4 years more recently, and median survival for well-differentiated advanced small-intestine NETs exceeds 5 years. Appendiceal NETs that are well differentiated and <2 cm have extremely low metastatic risk and excellent prognosis, while metastatic risk rises to about 20-30% for 2-3 cm tumors and is even higher beyond 3 cm. Most rectal NETs are small (<1 cm), well differentiated and rarely metastasize, but up to one-third of 1-2 cm rectal NETs metastasize and >2 cm rectal NETs metastasize in over 70%. Poorly differentiated high-grade neuroendocrine carcinomas carry markedly worse prognosis. The natural history is ultimately progressive (Harrison 22e p.677-682).","pitfalls":["PPIs and achlorhydria can both raise serum gastrin and confound the diagnosis of gastrinoma","Chromogranin A is frequently falsely elevated (renal impairment, PPI use) and is neither sensitive nor specific; assay variability complicates interpretation","Elevated 5-HIAA is suggestive but not diagnostic of carcinoid syndrome, with false positives from serotonin-rich foods","Somatostatin-receptor PET can be falsely positive (physiologic uptake in the pancreatic uncinate process, sarcoidosis, meningiomas, thyroid goiter/thyroiditis) and falsely negative in higher-grade tumors with lower receptor expression","Insulinomas are less reliably avid on somatostatin scintigraphy and are usually small (<2 cm), making EUS the preferred localization tool","Insulinoma may be misdiagnosed as psychiatric illness; surreptitious insulin/hypoglycemic-agent use can mimic it","Small-bowel NETs are easily missed at colonoscopy due to submucosal location beyond the ileocecal valve and may be misattributed to IBS for years","Reduction of serotonin levels does not reverse established carcinoid valvular lesions"],"severityClassification":"WHO-type histologic classification used in Harrison: well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumor grade 1 (low grade, <2 mitoses/10 HPF, Ki-67 <3%); grade 2 (intermediate, 2-20/10 HPF, Ki-67 3-20%); grade 3 (well-differentiated high grade, >20/10 HPF, Ki-67 >20%); and poorly differentiated neuroendocrine carcinoma (high grade, >20/10 HPF, Ki-67 >20%), the most clinically aggressive category (Harrison 22e p.677).","references":["Harrison 22e p.676","Harrison 22e p.677","Harrison 22e p.678","Harrison 22e p.679","Harrison 22e p.680","Harrison 22e p.681","Harrison 22e p.682","Harrison 22e p.683"],"crossLinks":[]},"gastroparesis":{"name":"Gastroparesis","system":"GI / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["delayed gastric emptying","gastric stasis","diabetic gastroparesis","gastroparetic syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Gastroparesis is delayed gastric emptying in the absence of mechanical obstruction, presenting with nausea, vomiting, early satiety, bloating, and postprandial fullness","The commonest identifiable causes are diabetes mellitus and prior gastric or vagal surgery, but many cases are idiopathic and some follow a viral illness","Mechanical obstruction must be excluded, typically by endoscopy, before the diagnosis is made","A scintigraphic gastric emptying study of a solid meal is the standard confirmatory test, with wireless motility capsule and breath tests as alternatives","Medications that slow gastric emptying, especially opioids and GLP-1 receptor agonists, can cause or worsen symptoms and should be reviewed","Management combines dietary modification toward small, low-fat, low-fibre meals with prokinetic and antiemetic therapy","Refractory disease may require enteral feeding beyond the stomach, gastric electrical stimulation, or endoscopic pyloric procedures"],"pathophysiology":"Gastroparesis arises from impaired neuromuscular coordination of gastric emptying. Injury to the vagus nerve, abnormalities of the interstitial cells of Cajal that pace gastric contractions, smooth muscle dysfunction, and autonomic neuropathy disrupt fundic accommodation, antral contractility, and pyloric relaxation. In diabetes, chronic hyperglycaemia and autonomic neuropathy impair motility, and acute hyperglycaemia itself further slows emptying. The result is retained gastric contents, which produce nausea, vomiting, and impaired nutrient and drug absorption.","additionalDifferentials":["Mechanical gastric outlet obstruction","Functional dyspepsia","Cyclic vomiting syndrome or cannabinoid hyperemesis","Rumination syndrome","Small bowel dysmotility or pseudo-obstruction","Peptic ulcer disease or malignancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper endoscopy to exclude mechanical obstruction","Scintigraphic solid-meal gastric emptying study","Wireless motility capsule or breath test as alternatives","Blood glucose and HbA1c assessment in diabetic patients","Review of medications affecting gastric motility","Nutritional and electrolyte assessment in severe or chronic disease"],"redFlags":["Intractable vomiting with dehydration and electrolyte disturbance","Significant weight loss or malnutrition","Features suggesting mechanical obstruction or malignancy","Marked or unstable glycaemic control complicating diabetic gastroparesis"],"prognosis":"Gastroparesis is often a chronic, fluctuating condition; many patients achieve reasonable symptom control with diet and medication, but a subset have refractory, disabling symptoms with recurrent hospitalisation and nutritional compromise.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing gastroparesis without first excluding mechanical obstruction","Overlooking opioids or GLP-1 receptor agonists as reversible contributors","Ignoring hyperglycaemia, which acutely delays emptying and undermines treatment","Missing alternative diagnoses such as rumination or cyclic vomiting that mimic gastroparesis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"generalized_anxiety_disorder":{"name":"Generalized anxiety disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["GAD","generalised anxiety disorder","chronic anxiety"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by excessive, hard-to-control worry about numerous everyday matters, present on most days over an extended period (on the order of six months or more)","Worry is accompanied by somatic and cognitive symptoms such as restlessness, fatigue, difficulty concentrating, irritability, muscle tension, and disturbed sleep","Patients frequently present first to primary care with physical complaints rather than naming anxiety as the problem","Distinguish from normal worry by the disproportionate intensity, pervasiveness, and functional impairment it causes","Exclude medical mimics such as hyperthyroidism, caffeine or stimulant excess, and arrhythmias, and screen for comorbid depression and substance use","First-line management combines cognitive behavioural therapy with pharmacotherapy, typically SSRIs or SNRIs as maintenance treatment","Benzodiazepines may give rapid short-term relief but carry dependence and tolerance risk and are avoided for long-term routine use","Comorbidity with depression and other anxiety disorders is common and worsens the overall burden"],"pathophysiology":"Generalized anxiety disorder involves dysregulation of fear and worry circuitry, particularly heightened amygdala reactivity with insufficient prefrontal regulation, alongside imbalances in gamma-aminobutyric acid, serotonin, and noradrenaline signalling. Genetic predisposition, temperamental traits such as behavioural inhibition, and chronic stress interact to produce a persistent state of anticipatory apprehension and autonomic arousal.","additionalDifferentials":["Major depressive disorder","Panic disorder","Social anxiety disorder","Obsessive-compulsive disorder","Hyperthyroidism and other endocrine disorders","Caffeine, stimulant, or substance-related anxiety","Cardiac arrhythmia or other medical causes of palpitations"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Standardised anxiety rating scales to quantify severity and monitor response","Thyroid function testing","Screening for depression and other anxiety disorders","Substance and caffeine use history","Targeted cardiac evaluation when palpitations or chest symptoms predominate"],"redFlags":["Emergent suicidal ideation, especially with comorbid depression","Escalating benzodiazepine or alcohol use to self-manage symptoms","New or severe physical symptoms suggesting an undiagnosed medical cause","Marked functional collapse or inability to work or care for self"],"prognosis":"Generalized anxiety disorder tends to be chronic and fluctuating, often worsening during periods of stress. Outcomes improve with sustained psychotherapy and pharmacotherapy, but comorbid depression and substance use are associated with greater chronicity and disability.","pitfalls":["Relying on long-term benzodiazepines and creating dependence","Missing comorbid depression and its associated suicide risk","Ordering repeated investigations for somatic complaints without recognising the underlying anxiety disorder","Discontinuing effective antidepressant treatment prematurely and precipitating relapse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"genetic_cardiomyopathies":{"name":"Genetic Cardiomyopathies","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Hereditary cardiomyopathy","Familial cardiomyopathy","Inherited cardiomyopathy","Sarcomeric cardiomyopathy"],"chapter":267,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2003","clinicalPearls":["Each traditional morphologic form of cardiomyopathy (hypertrophic, dilated, restrictive) can be caused or modified by underlying genetic factors; heritability is well recognized in HCM and present in at least 30% of dilated cardiomyopathy without another clear etiology (p.2003-2004).","Most familial cardiomyopathies are autosomal dominant, with occasional autosomal recessive, matrilineal (mitochondrial), and X-linked inheritance; missense and truncating variants are the commonest abnormalities (p.2003).","Genetic cardiomyopathy shows age-dependent and incomplete penetrance: the phenotype is rarely present at birth and in some carriers may never manifest; relatives carrying the same variant can differ markedly in severity and progression (p.2004).","Penetrance and clinical severity tend to be greater in men for most cardiomyopathies; current therapy is based on phenotype rather than the genetic defect (p.2004).","The greatest current utility of genetic testing is to focus and inform family evaluations; it can also provide risk stratification and occasionally identify a treatable disorder such as Fabry's or Gaucher's disease (p.2004).","A sarcomere gene variant is found in roughly 40-50% of HCM patients, more often with familial disease and asymmetric septal hypertrophy; about 80% of these involve MYH7 or MYBPC3 encoding the thick myofilament (p.2005).","HCM is defined as left ventricular hypertrophy developing without a causative hemodynamic factor (e.g., hypertension, aortic valve disease) or infiltrative/storage disease; prevalence is about 1:500 (p.2005).","Outflow tract obstruction is present at rest in about 30% of HCM patients and provoked by exercise in another ~30%; systolic anterior motion of an enlarged anterior mitral leaflet against the hypertrophied septum produces obstruction and posteriorly directed mitral regurgitation (p.2006).","Truncating variants in TTN (titin) are the commonest genetic cause of dilated cardiomyopathy, accounting for up to 25% of familial DCM; men with TTN truncating variants typically develop disease about a decade before women (p.2008-2009).","DCM is considered present when LVEF is <=0.50 and/or LV diastolic dimension exceeds the 95th percentile predicted for age and sex; some patients have reduced LVEF with minimal dilation (nondilated/minimally dilated cardiomyopathy) (p.2008).","Inherited metabolic and storage disorders can mimic HCM by thickening the myocardium with abnormal products ('pseudohypertrophy'), classically with a short PR interval; most are diagnosed in childhood (p.2010).","Fabry's disease (X-linked alpha-galactosidase A deficiency from GLA variants) can affect female carriers; endomyocardial biopsy shows diagnostic concentric lamellar inclusions on electron microscopy, and diagnosis matters because enzyme replacement can reduce deposits (p.2010)."],"pathophysiology":"Many distinct genes are implicated (locus heterogeneity), each with numerous pathogenic variants (allelic heterogeneity). Mutant proteins can interfere with the normal allele via a dominant-negative mechanism, while nonsense/frameshift variants may yield truncated or unstable proteins whose absence causes disease through haploinsufficiency; some genetic DCM combines both effects. Functional gene groups include sarcomeric proteins, Z-disk/cytoskeletal proteins, nuclear membrane proteins, excitation-contraction coupling proteins, metabolic enzymes, mitochondrial DNA, sarcolemmal membrane proteins, and desmosomal proteins. In HCM, sarcomere variants enhance calcium sensitivity, maximal force generation, and ATPase activity, producing abnormal energetics and impaired relaxation; myofibril and myocyte misalignment/disarray occurs, with interstitial fibrosis appearing before overt hypertrophy and focal replacement fibrosis providing arrhythmic substrate, plus microvascular ischemia from thickened intramural vessels. In DCM and arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy, cytoskeletal, sarcolemmal (dystrophin), nuclear membrane (lamin A/C, emerin), and desmosomal protein defects impair force transmission, mechanical/electrical coupling, and cell survival; desmosomal disruption activates Wnt/beta-catenin and proinflammatory signaling with replacement of myocytes by fat and fibrous tissue, creating arrhythmogenic, sometimes aneurysmal regions.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (sarcomeric)","Dilated cardiomyopathy (e.g., TTN truncating variant)","Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy / arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy (inherited, including DES-related and transthyretin amyloidosis)","Left ventricular noncompaction","Conduction disease with dilated cardiomyopathy (e.g., LMNA, SCN5A)","Inherited metabolic/storage cardiomyopathies (Fabry's, Danon, PRKAG2, carnitine and fatty-acid oxidation defects, mitochondrial syndromes)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Athlete's heart (physiologic hypertrophy that regresses with detraining, with supernormal VO2max >50 mL/kg/min, mild dilation, and normal diastolic function)","Hypertensive or aortic valve disease causing secondary LV hypertrophy","Infiltrative/storage disease mimicking HCM via pseudohypertrophy (often with short PR interval)","Cardiac amyloidosis, particularly transthyretin-variant amyloid causing restrictive physiology"],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed multigenerational family history (heart failure, cardiomyopathy, skeletal myopathy, conduction disease, tachyarrhythmias, sudden death, early atrial fibrillation/pacemaker, muscular dystrophy)","Two-dimensional and Doppler echocardiography (central to HCM/DCM diagnosis; physical exam and ECG are insensitive for HCM)","Electrocardiogram","Cardiac MRI for myocardial fibrosis (late gadolinium enhancement) and inflammation","Genetic counseling and multigene cardiomyopathy panel testing, with confirmatory variant testing in relatives","Endomyocardial biopsy with electron microscopy when a specific diagnosis with therapeutic implications is suspected (e.g., Fabry's lamellar inclusions; not used to diagnose HCM but can exclude infiltrative/metabolic disease)","Enzyme activity assay and/or GLA sequencing when Fabry's disease suspected","Ambulatory ECG monitoring and exercise testing for nonsustained ventricular tachycardia and outflow gradient provocation"],"redFlags":["History of cardiac arrest or spontaneous sustained ventricular tachycardia","Syncope, particularly nonvagal and occurring with or after exertion","Family history of sudden cardiac death at early ages","Maximal LV wall thickness >30 mm","LV systolic dysfunction with ejection fraction <50% in HCM","LV apical aneurysm (especially with apical hypertrophy)","Spontaneous nonsustained ventricular tachycardia (>3 beats at rate >120)","High late gadolinium enhancement burden on cardiac MRI","Pathogenic LMNA or FLNC variants, or right-ventricular cardiomyopathy progressing to LV dysfunction (higher arrhythmic/adverse-event risk)"],"prognosis":"HCM prognosis is better than in early referral-population studies but mortality remains higher than in age-matched individuals without cardiomyopathy; sudden death risk is under 1% per year (about 0.5% per year baseline). Up to 1 in 20 HCM patients progress to overt systolic dysfunction (EF <50%) with or without dilation ('burned out'/end-stage HCM), with increased risk of death from progressive heart failure and sudden death unless transplanted. In DCM and arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy, prognosis is driven by disease stage and sudden-death risk, which is higher for variants in DES, DSP, DSC2, FLNC, LMNA, PKP2, PLN, RBM20, SCN5A, and TMEM43; progression rate is also gene-dependent, and patients with TTN truncating variants frequently recover with medical therapy.","pitfalls":["Identifying a variant in a proband to focus family screening requires high certainty the variant is truly pathogenic and not a benign DNA variant.","Most identified variants are 'private' to individual families, complicating interpretation; growing recognition that multiple intermediate-effect alleles and polygenic risk influence penetrance and expression.","Athlete's heart can mimic mild HCM; distinguishing features include regression with detraining, supernormal exercise capacity, mild dilation, and normal diastolic function.","Late gadolinium enhancement on MRI, once attributed to prior viral myocarditis, is now recognized as common in genetic cardiomyopathy.","Cardiac glycosides should be avoided in HCM with obstruction because they increase contractility and worsen obstruction.","The positive predictive value of most HCM sudden-death risk factors is low, so many patients receiving a defibrillator never receive appropriate device therapy.","Endomyocardial biopsy is not used to diagnose HCM itself but may exclude infiltrative/metabolic disease."],"severityClassification":"HCM sudden-death risk stratification uses major risk factors (prior cardiac arrest or sustained VT, exertional/nonvagal syncope, family history of sudden cardiac death, LV apical aneurysm, maximal wall thickness >30 mm, LVEF <50%) plus modifying factors (late gadolinium enhancement burden, spontaneous nonsustained VT). The externally validated European Society of Cardiology HCM Risk-SCD calculator integrates age, LV outflow tract gradient, left atrial diameter, maximal wall thickness, syncope, family history, and nonsustained VT to estimate 5-year cardiac-arrest risk: ICD generally advised at >=6%, considered at 4-6%, and not advised at <4%. DCM is defined by LVEF <=0.50 and/or LV diastolic dimension >95th percentile for age and sex.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2003","Harrison 22e p.2004","Harrison 22e p.2005","Harrison 22e p.2006","Harrison 22e p.2007","Harrison 22e p.2008","Harrison 22e p.2009","Harrison 22e p.2010"],"crossLinks":[]},"gestational_diabetes_mellitus":{"name":"Gestational diabetes mellitus","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["GDM","gestational diabetes","pregnancy-related glucose intolerance"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined as carbohydrate intolerance first recognised during pregnancy, distinguishing it from pre-existing diabetes detected in early pregnancy","Diagnosis rests on an oral glucose tolerance test, usually performed around 24 to 28 weeks, or earlier in high-risk women","Risk factors include obesity, advanced maternal age, prior GDM, macrosomic infant, family history of diabetes, polycystic ovary syndrome, and certain ethnic backgrounds","First-line management is medical nutrition therapy, glucose self-monitoring, and physical activity, with pharmacotherapy added if glycaemic targets are not met","Fetal complications include macrosomia, shoulder dystocia, birth trauma, neonatal hypoglycaemia, and polycythaemia","Maternal hyperglycaemia drives fetal hyperinsulinaemia, which promotes excessive fetal growth and adiposity","Most women return to normal glucose tolerance after delivery, but there is a substantially increased lifetime risk of type 2 diabetes","Postpartum glucose testing is essential to detect persistent diabetes and to guide long-term risk reduction"],"pathophysiology":"Pregnancy is a progressively insulin-resistant state driven by placental hormones such as human placental lactogen, progesterone, and cortisol. Gestational diabetes develops when maternal pancreatic beta-cell insulin secretion cannot compensate for this physiological resistance, leading to maternal hyperglycaemia. Glucose crosses the placenta freely and stimulates fetal insulin secretion; the resulting fetal hyperinsulinaemia acts as a growth factor causing macrosomia and, after cord clamping removes the maternal glucose supply, predisposes the neonate to hypoglycaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Pre-existing (overt) type 1 or type 2 diabetes unmasked in pregnancy","Impaired glucose tolerance predating pregnancy","Stress or steroid-induced hyperglycaemia","Monogenic diabetes (e.g. MODY)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Oral glucose tolerance test with fasting and post-load values","Home capillary glucose monitoring (fasting and postprandial) to guide therapy","HbA1c to help distinguish pre-existing diabetes when measured early","Serial fetal growth ultrasound and amniotic fluid assessment","Postpartum oral glucose tolerance test to reclassify glucose status"],"redFlags":["Marked fetal macrosomia or polyhydramnios increasing risk of shoulder dystocia and stillbirth","Very poor glycaemic control suggesting undiagnosed overt diabetes","Coexisting hypertension or pre-eclampsia","Reduced fetal movements or non-reassuring fetal surveillance"],"prognosis":"Well-controlled gestational diabetes has an excellent outcome, approaching that of the non-diabetic population. Poor control raises risks of macrosomia, operative delivery, birth trauma, and neonatal metabolic problems. Long term, affected women have a markedly increased risk of type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular disease, and their offspring have higher rates of obesity and glucose intolerance.","pitfalls":["Missing overt pre-existing diabetes by not testing high-risk women in early pregnancy","Relying on symptoms, which are usually absent, rather than screening","Assuming a normal-sized fetus excludes risk of neonatal hypoglycaemia","Failing to arrange postpartum glucose reassessment and long-term follow-up","Neglecting neonatal glucose monitoring after delivery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"gestational_thyroid":{"name":"Gestational thyroid disease","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thyroid disease in pregnancy","gestational thyrotoxicosis","gestational hypothyroidism","pregnancy-associated thyroid dysfunction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pregnancy raises thyroxine-binding globulin and total thyroid hormone levels, so trimester-specific reference ranges for free hormones and TSH must be used to interpret results correctly","High levels of hCG in early pregnancy weakly stimulate the TSH receptor, producing a physiological fall in TSH and sometimes transient gestational thyrotoxicosis, often linked to hyperemesis gravidarum","Gestational thyrotoxicosis from hCG is usually self-limiting and must be distinguished from Graves' disease, which typically has orbitopathy and positive TSH-receptor antibodies","Maternal thyroid hormone is critical for early fetal neurodevelopment, so overt hypothyroidism requires prompt and adequate replacement","Levothyroxine requirements commonly increase during pregnancy, so women on replacement need dose adjustment and monitoring","TSH-receptor antibodies cross the placenta and can cause fetal or neonatal thyroid dysfunction, so antibody levels help assess fetal risk in Graves' disease","When antithyroid drugs are needed, the choice and timing consider the differing teratogenic profiles of the agents across trimesters"],"pathophysiology":"Pregnancy alters thyroid physiology through oestrogen-driven increases in thyroxine-binding globulin, rising total hormone levels, and hCG-mediated stimulation of the TSH receptor that transiently lowers TSH in the first trimester. Increased renal iodide clearance and placental deiodinase activity raise iodine and hormone demand. Autoimmune thyroid disease may worsen or improve with the shifting immune state of pregnancy, and transplacental passage of TSH-receptor antibodies or antithyroid drugs can affect the fetal thyroid.","additionalDifferentials":["Graves' disease in pregnancy","Hyperemesis gravidarum with transient gestational thyrotoxicosis","Hashimoto (autoimmune) hypothyroidism","Iodine deficiency or excess","Gestational trophoblastic disease causing marked hCG-driven thyrotoxicosis","Postpartum thyroiditis in the peripartum period"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Trimester-specific TSH and free thyroid hormone measurement","TSH-receptor antibody testing when Graves' disease is suspected or to assess fetal risk","Thyroid peroxidase antibodies to identify autoimmune thyroid disease","Fetal ultrasound monitoring for goitre or growth concerns in antibody-positive pregnancies","Avoidance of radionuclide scanning in pregnancy in favour of clinical and biochemical assessment"],"redFlags":["Features of thyroid storm in a pregnant woman","Poorly controlled maternal thyrotoxicosis threatening the pregnancy","Signs of fetal thyroid dysfunction such as fetal goitre, tachycardia, or growth restriction","Untreated overt hypothyroidism given the risk to fetal neurodevelopment and pregnancy loss"],"prognosis":"With appropriate monitoring and treatment, most women with thyroid disease have good maternal and fetal outcomes, but poorly controlled disease increases the risk of miscarriage, pre-eclampsia, growth restriction, and impaired fetal neurodevelopment.","pitfalls":["Applying non-pregnant reference ranges and misclassifying normal gestational thyroid changes as disease","Confusing self-limiting hCG-mediated gestational thyrotoxicosis with Graves' disease","Failing to increase levothyroxine early in pregnancy for women already on replacement","Overlooking transplacental antibody effects on the fetal and neonatal thyroid"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"gestational_trophoblastic_disease":{"name":"Gestational Trophoblastic Disease","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["GTD","molar pregnancy","hydatidiform mole","gestational trophoblastic neoplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Gestational trophoblastic disease is a spectrum of proliferative disorders of placental trophoblast ranging from benign hydatidiform mole to malignant gestational trophoblastic neoplasia including choriocarcinoma","A complete mole usually arises from fertilisation of an ovum lacking maternal genetic material and contains no fetal tissue, whereas a partial mole is typically triploid with some fetal parts","Classic presentation includes irregular vaginal bleeding in early pregnancy, a uterus large for dates, markedly elevated hCG, and sometimes early or severe hyperemesis or pre-eclampsia before twenty weeks","Ultrasound may show a vesicular or snowstorm appearance with absent or abnormal fetal development, and diagnosis is confirmed histologically","Uterine evacuation by suction curettage is the treatment for molar pregnancy, followed by structured monitoring of serial hCG to detect persistent disease","Persistently elevated or rising hCG after evacuation indicates gestational trophoblastic neoplasia, which is highly chemosensitive and generally curable even when metastatic","Reliable contraception during hCG surveillance is essential because a new pregnancy would obscure the marker used to detect persistent disease","Choriocarcinoma can follow any pregnancy, including normal or ectopic, and may present with metastatic symptoms such as haemoptysis or neurological signs"],"pathophysiology":"Gestational trophoblastic disease results from abnormal fertilisation and proliferation of trophoblastic tissue. A complete mole typically arises when an anucleate ovum is fertilised and the paternal chromosomes duplicate, giving a diploid androgenetic genome with swollen avascular chorionic villi and no fetus, while a partial mole usually results from dispermic fertilisation producing a triploid conceptus with focal villous change and a nonviable fetus. Excessive trophoblast produces very high hCG. In gestational trophoblastic neoplasia, trophoblast invades the myometrium and vasculature, and choriocarcinoma disseminates haematogenously, favouring the lungs, brain, and liver.","additionalDifferentials":["Threatened or missed miscarriage","Ectopic pregnancy","Multiple pregnancy with high hCG","Normal early intrauterine pregnancy with bleeding","Non-gestational malignancy producing hCG","Retained products of conception"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative serum hCG, which is often markedly elevated","Pelvic ultrasound to assess for molar features and fetal tissue","Histological examination of evacuated tissue, with ploidy and immunostaining where needed","Serial hCG monitoring after evacuation to detect persistent disease","Chest imaging and cross-sectional staging when neoplasia or metastasis is suspected","Baseline full blood count, renal, liver and thyroid function given possible hCG-driven thyroid effects"],"redFlags":["Rising or plateauing hCG after evacuation signalling gestational trophoblastic neoplasia requiring urgent oncology referral","Heavy vaginal bleeding with haemodynamic compromise","Respiratory symptoms or haemoptysis suggesting pulmonary metastases","Neurological signs suggesting cerebral metastatic choriocarcinoma","Pre-eclampsia, thyrotoxic features, or hyperemesis occurring unusually early in pregnancy"],"prognosis":"Most hydatidiform moles are cured by evacuation alone, and even when gestational trophoblastic neoplasia develops it is one of the most curable malignancies, with excellent survival when managed in specialist centres using chemotherapy guided by prognostic scoring. Outcome depends on prompt diagnosis, adherence to hCG surveillance, and risk stratification, with high-risk and heavily metastatic disease requiring more intensive multi-agent treatment.","pitfalls":["Failing to arrange structured hCG follow-up after evacuation","Allowing pregnancy during surveillance, which masks persistent disease","Missing early or atypical pre-eclampsia and hyperthyroid features as clues to molar pregnancy","Not considering choriocarcinoma in a woman with metastatic disease and any recent pregnancy","Assuming a benign miscarriage without histological examination of tissue"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"gi_stromal_tumor":{"name":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumour","system":"GI / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["GIST","gastrointestinal stromal tumor","GI stromal tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AASLD & ACG","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common mesenchymal tumour of the gastrointestinal tract, thought to arise from the interstitial cells of Cajal, most often in the stomach and next most often in the small intestine","Most are driven by activating mutations in the KIT (CD117) or PDGFRA receptor tyrosine kinases, and KIT immunostaining is the diagnostic hallmark","Presentation ranges from an incidental finding to vague abdominal discomfort, a palpable mass, or gastrointestinal bleeding from mucosal ulceration","On endoscopy they typically appear as a smooth submucosal bulge with normal overlying mucosa, sometimes with central umbilication","Malignant potential is estimated mainly from tumour size and mitotic rate together with anatomical site, rather than being simply benign or malignant","Surgical resection with negative margins is the mainstay of treatment for localised disease, and routine lymph node dissection is generally unnecessary because nodal spread is rare","Tyrosine kinase inhibitors targeting KIT/PDGFRA have transformed management of advanced, metastatic, or high-risk disease and are used in adjuvant and neoadjuvant settings","Biopsy should be approached cautiously because of bleeding and rupture risk; endoscopic ultrasound-guided sampling is often preferred when tissue is needed","The liver and peritoneum are the usual sites of metastatic spread"],"pathophysiology":"GISTs originate from or resemble the interstitial cells of Cajal, the pacemaker cells of the gut. Gain-of-function mutations in the KIT proto-oncogene, or less commonly in PDGFRA, produce constitutive receptor tyrosine kinase signalling that drives uncontrolled proliferation. A minority are wild-type for these genes and involve alternative pathways such as succinate dehydrogenase deficiency, which is more common in younger patients and in syndromic disease. Tumours grow as submucosal or intramural masses; larger and more mitotically active lesions have greater potential for haematogenous spread, particularly to the liver and peritoneal surfaces.","additionalDifferentials":["Leiomyoma or leiomyosarcoma of the gut","Gastrointestinal schwannoma","Neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumour","Gastric or small-bowel adenocarcinoma or lymphoma","Extrinsic compression from an adjacent mass","Pancreatic or splenic lesion mimicking a gastric submucosal mass"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Endoscopy with endoscopic ultrasound to characterise the submucosal lesion and guide sampling","Contrast-enhanced CT for staging, size assessment, and detection of liver or peritoneal metastases","Immunohistochemistry for KIT (CD117) and DOG1 to confirm the diagnosis","Mutational analysis of KIT and PDGFRA to guide targeted therapy and predict response","Mitotic count and tumour size assessment for risk stratification"],"redFlags":["Large tumour with high mitotic rate indicating high malignant potential and need for oncological management","Significant or recurrent gastrointestinal bleeding from an ulcerated tumour","Tumour rupture (spontaneous or during biopsy) causing peritoneal dissemination and haemoperitoneum","New liver or peritoneal lesions signalling metastatic progression"],"prognosis":"Small, low-mitotic-rate, completely resected tumours have an excellent outlook, whereas large or mitotically active lesions carry a substantial risk of recurrence and metastasis. Tyrosine kinase inhibitor therapy has markedly improved survival in advanced disease, though resistance can develop over time.","pitfalls":["Assuming a submucosal lesion is benign without adequate risk stratification by size and mitotic rate","Performing incautious biopsy of a friable tumour and precipitating bleeding or rupture","Omitting mutational testing, which can predict poor response to standard targeted therapy","Confusing a GIST with other spindle-cell tumours when immunohistochemistry is not performed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AASLD/ACG guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"giardiasis":{"name":"Giardiasis","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Giardia lamblia infection","Giardia intestinalis infection","Giardia duodenalis infection","beaver fever"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A protozoal infection of the small intestine caused by Giardia, acquired by faecal-oral spread of cysts through contaminated water or food, or person to person","Highly endemic in India, contributing to childhood diarrhoea, malabsorption and faltering growth","Typical illness is prolonged, with foul-smelling greasy diarrhoea, bloating, flatulence, abdominal cramps and weight loss rather than blood or fever","Many infections are asymptomatic, and cyst passage can continue in the absence of symptoms, sustaining transmission","Chronic infection can cause fat and lactose malabsorption and, in children, nutritional deficiencies and stunting","Diagnosis traditionally relies on detecting cysts or trophozoites on repeated stool examination, with stool antigen and PCR tests being more sensitive","Because cyst excretion is intermittent, a single negative stool sample does not exclude infection","Treatment is with antiprotozoal agents; attention to safe water and hygiene prevents reinfection and outbreaks"],"pathophysiology":"Ingested Giardia cysts excyst in the small intestine and release trophozoites that attach to the duodenal and jejunal mucosa via a ventral adhesive disc. Colonisation causes villous shortening, disruption of the brush-border enzymes and a mucosal inflammatory response, impairing absorption of fats, carbohydrates and other nutrients. This produces the characteristic malabsorptive, non-inflammatory diarrhoea. Trophozoites encyst as they pass down the gut, and the environmentally hardy cysts are excreted to continue the cycle.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of chronic or malabsorptive diarrhoea (coeliac disease, tropical sprue)","Cryptosporidiosis and other protozoal enteritides","Bacterial enteritis","Lactose intolerance","Irritable bowel syndrome"],"infectionMimics":["Coeliac disease","Tropical sprue","Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth","Inflammatory bowel disease"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Repeated stool microscopy for cysts and trophozoites","Stool antigen detection (ELISA) or PCR for greater sensitivity","Duodenal aspirate or biopsy in difficult cases","Assessment for malabsorption and nutritional status in chronic disease","Evaluation for immunoglobulin deficiency in recurrent or refractory infection"],"redFlags":["Severe dehydration or significant weight loss, particularly in young children","Malabsorption with nutritional deficiency and growth faltering","Persistent or relapsing infection suggesting underlying immunodeficiency","Failure to respond to standard therapy, prompting reassessment of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Most cases respond well to antiprotozoal therapy with full recovery. Untreated or recurrent infection, especially in children, can cause lasting malabsorption and growth impairment, and reinfection is common where sanitation is poor.","pitfalls":["Excluding giardiasis on the basis of a single negative stool sample despite intermittent cyst shedding","Overlooking it as a treatable cause of chronic diarrhoea and malabsorption","Missing underlying immunodeficiency in recurrent or refractory cases","Neglecting water and hygiene measures, leading to reinfection or household spread"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"gilbert_syndrome":{"name":"Gilbert syndrome","system":"GI / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Gilbert's syndrome","constitutional hepatic dysfunction","familial nonhaemolytic jaundice"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AASLD & ACG","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common, benign inherited disorder of bilirubin conjugation causing mild, fluctuating unconjugated (indirect) hyperbilirubinaemia","Due to reduced activity of the hepatic enzyme UDP-glucuronosyltransferase (UGT1A1), most often from a promoter polymorphism inherited in a recessive pattern","Jaundice is typically intermittent and provoked by fasting, intercurrent illness, dehydration, physical stress, or alcohol","Liver enzymes, liver synthetic function, and the blood film are normal, and there is no haemolysis, which helps distinguish it from other causes of raised bilirubin","The diagnosis is essentially clinical, based on isolated unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with otherwise normal tests and no evidence of haemolysis or liver disease","No treatment is needed; reassurance is the mainstay because the condition is harmless and does not progress to liver damage","Reduced UGT1A1 activity can impair metabolism of certain drugs (for example irinotecan), which is a clinically relevant consideration","It should not be confused with the far more serious Crigler-Najjar syndromes, which cause severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia"],"pathophysiology":"Gilbert syndrome results from partially reduced activity of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1A1, the enzyme that conjugates bilirubin with glucuronic acid to make it water-soluble for biliary excretion. The commonest genetic basis is an expanded TA repeat in the UGT1A1 gene promoter, which lowers enzyme expression. The consequent modest reduction in conjugating capacity produces mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia, accentuated when bilirubin load or physiological stress increases, such as during fasting or illness. There is no structural liver disease and no haemolysis, and bilirubin levels usually remain modestly elevated.","additionalDifferentials":["Haemolytic anaemia","Crigler-Najjar syndrome (types I and II)","Ineffective erythropoiesis","Resolving or physiological neonatal jaundice","Drug-induced impairment of bilirubin conjugation or uptake","Dubin-Johnson and Rotor syndromes (conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fractionated serum bilirubin showing a predominantly unconjugated (indirect) elevation","Normal liver enzymes and synthetic function tests","Full blood count, reticulocyte count, and blood film to exclude haemolysis","Repeat testing to demonstrate fluctuating mild hyperbilirubinaemia, sometimes accentuated by fasting","UGT1A1 genotyping in selected or atypical cases"],"redFlags":["Conjugated (direct) hyperbilirubinaemia or abnormal liver enzymes, which point away from Gilbert syndrome to genuine liver or biliary disease","Anaemia or a raised reticulocyte count suggesting haemolysis rather than a benign conjugation defect","Very high bilirubin levels, especially from infancy, raising concern for Crigler-Najjar syndrome","Bilirubin rising with systemic symptoms indicating an alternative serious diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Excellent. Gilbert syndrome is a lifelong but entirely benign condition that does not cause liver damage or reduce life expectancy, and no specific treatment is required.","pitfalls":["Over-investigating or mislabelling harmless isolated unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia as liver disease","Failing to exclude haemolysis, which can also cause unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia","Not fractionating bilirubin and therefore missing a conjugated (and more concerning) pattern","Overlooking altered metabolism of drugs such as irinotecan in affected patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AASLD/ACG guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"gingivitis":{"name":"Gingivitis","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["gum inflammation","plaque-induced gingivitis","marginal gingivitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Gingivitis is reversible inflammation confined to the gingiva without loss of periodontal attachment or bone","The cardinal sign is bleeding on gentle probing or brushing, alongside redness, swelling, and loss of the normal stippled contour","It is overwhelmingly plaque-induced, so improved oral hygiene and professional cleaning usually reverse it within days to weeks","Hormonal changes in pregnancy and puberty, and certain medications, can exaggerate the gingival response to plaque","Unlike periodontitis, probing depths remain shallow and there is no true pocketing or attachment loss","Untreated gingivitis is a risk state that may progress to periodontitis in susceptible individuals","Necrotising gingivitis is a distinct painful form with ulcerated, punched-out interdental papillae and characteristic malodour","Persistent gingival inflammation despite good hygiene should prompt consideration of systemic or drug-related causes"],"pathophysiology":"Plaque-induced gingivitis develops when microbial biofilm accumulates at the gingival margin and triggers a localised host inflammatory response. Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability cause the classic erythema, oedema, and bleeding tendency, while the junctional epithelium remains intact and attachment is preserved, making the condition reversible. If the biofilm persists, the chronic inflammatory infiltrate can, in susceptible hosts and under modifying influences such as hormonal changes, systemic disease, or medications, evolve toward the attachment and bone loss that define periodontitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Early periodontitis with attachment loss","Necrotising ulcerative gingivitis","Drug-influenced gingival enlargement","Desquamative gingivitis from mucocutaneous disease","Gingival manifestations of leukaemia or other systemic illness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assessment of bleeding on probing and plaque indices","Periodontal probing to confirm absence of attachment loss","Review of medications and hormonal or systemic factors","Radiographs when needed to exclude bone loss","Blood investigations if systemic disease is suspected"],"redFlags":["Spontaneous gingival bleeding or florid enlargement suggesting leukaemia or a bleeding disorder","Painful necrotising gingivitis, particularly in immunocompromised patients","Gingival changes that fail to resolve with good hygiene, indicating an underlying systemic cause"],"prognosis":"Plaque-induced gingivitis is fully reversible with effective plaque control, but if neglected it can act as a precursor to irreversible periodontitis in susceptible people.","pitfalls":["Assuming all gingival inflammation is simple plaque-induced gingivitis and missing early periodontitis","Overlooking systemic, hormonal, or drug-related contributors to gingival changes","Attributing spontaneous bleeding to local causes when a haematological disorder is present","Failing to reinforce oral hygiene, leading to recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"gitelman_syndrome":{"name":"Gitelman syndrome","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Gitelman's syndrome","familial hypokalaemia-hypomagnesaemia","thiazide-like tubulopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Gitelman syndrome is an inherited tubulopathy that mimics chronic thiazide diuretic use because the defect is in sodium chloride transport in the distal convoluted tubule","The distinguishing electrolyte signature is hypokalaemia and metabolic alkalosis together with hypomagnesaemia and low urinary calcium excretion","It usually presents later than Bartter syndrome, often in adolescence or adulthood, with mild symptoms such as cramps, fatigue, tetany, and salt craving","Hypocalciuria and hypomagnesaemia are the key features separating it from Bartter syndrome, which typically shows normal or high calcium excretion","Blood pressure is normal or low, and renin and aldosterone are elevated as a compensatory response to salt wasting","Chondrocalcinosis and joint symptoms can occur, related to chronic magnesium depletion","Management principles are generous replacement of potassium and magnesium, liberal salt intake, and sometimes potassium-sparing agents, with magnesium repletion being essential for correcting the hypokalaemia"],"pathophysiology":"Gitelman syndrome is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the thiazide-sensitive sodium-chloride cotransporter of the distal convoluted tubule. Impaired sodium chloride reabsorption at this site leads to renal salt wasting and mild volume contraction, activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. Increased distal sodium delivery combined with secondary hyperaldosteronism promotes potassium and hydrogen secretion, producing hypokalaemia and metabolic alkalosis. The defect enhances calcium reabsorption downstream, giving the characteristic hypocalciuria, while magnesium wasting from altered distal tubular handling causes hypomagnesaemia. The overall picture closely resembles the effect of long-term thiazide therapy.","additionalDifferentials":["Bartter syndrome","Surreptitious thiazide diuretic use","Chronic vomiting","Laxative abuse","Primary hypomagnesaemia from other causes","Primary hyperaldosteronism (distinguished by hypertension)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum potassium, magnesium, and bicarbonate to define the hypokalaemic, hypomagnesaemic alkalosis","Urinary calcium-to-creatinine ratio demonstrating hypocalciuria","Plasma renin and aldosterone, usually elevated","Urine electrolytes and screening for covert diuretic use","Genetic testing to confirm the diagnosis and for family counselling","ECG when significant hypokalaemia or hypomagnesaemia is present"],"redFlags":["Symptomatic hypomagnesaemia with tetany or seizures","Severe hypokalaemia with arrhythmia risk or marked weakness","Prolonged QT interval predisposing to dangerous arrhythmia","Persistent symptoms despite apparent replacement, suggesting inadequate magnesium correction"],"prognosis":"The long-term outlook is generally good, with preserved renal function in most patients, though lifelong electrolyte replacement is required and symptoms can affect quality of life. Correcting magnesium is often the key to stabilising potassium and preventing arrhythmia.","pitfalls":["Attempting to correct hypokalaemia without also replacing magnesium, so the potassium remains refractory","Confusing Gitelman with Bartter syndrome by not measuring urinary calcium and serum magnesium","Missing surreptitious thiazide use as a mimic of the syndrome","Overlooking the diagnosis in a normotensive adult with chronic unexplained hypokalaemic alkalosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"glioblastoma":{"name":"Glioblastoma","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["GBM","glioblastoma multiforme","grade 4 astrocytoma","high-grade glioma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Glioblastoma is the most common and most aggressive primary malignant brain tumour in adults, classified as a grade 4 glioma","Presentation is usually subacute over weeks, with progressive headache, focal deficits, cognitive or personality change, and seizures","Headaches suggesting raised intracranial pressure, worse in the morning or with Valsalva, and new focal neurology should prompt urgent imaging","On MRI it typically appears as an irregularly enhancing mass with central necrosis and surrounding vasogenic oedema, often crossing the corpus callosum as a butterfly lesion","Definitive diagnosis and molecular characterisation require tissue from biopsy or resection, with markers such as IDH status and MGMT promoter methylation guiding prognosis and therapy","Corticosteroids are used to control symptomatic peritumoural oedema and mass effect while treatment is planned","Standard management combines maximal safe surgical resection with subsequent radiotherapy and chemotherapy, individualised to patient fitness and molecular profile","Despite multimodal treatment the tumour almost always recurs, so realistic goal-setting and supportive care are integral"],"pathophysiology":"Glioblastoma is a highly malignant, infiltrative tumour arising from glial (astrocytic-lineage) cells. It is characterised histologically by marked cellular atypia, brisk mitotic activity, microvascular proliferation, and regions of necrosis. The tumour cells invade diffusely along white matter tracts well beyond the enhancing margin seen on imaging, which underlies the difficulty of complete removal and the near-inevitable recurrence. Aberrant angiogenesis produces leaky vessels that generate surrounding vasogenic oedema and contribute to mass effect and raised intracranial pressure. Distinct molecular alterations define subgroups with differing behaviour and treatment response.","additionalDifferentials":["Brain metastasis","Primary CNS lymphoma","Cerebral abscess","Lower-grade glioma","Tumefactive demyelination","Subacute infarct with mass effect"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI brain with contrast, including advanced sequences (perfusion, spectroscopy) to characterise the lesion","Histopathological and molecular analysis of biopsy or resection tissue (including IDH and MGMT status)","Staging or systemic imaging when metastasis is a differential","Assessment of performance status and comorbidity to guide treatment intensity"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive neurological deficit or declining consciousness from mass effect","Symptoms and signs of raised intracranial pressure with impending herniation","New-onset seizures, including status epilepticus","Acute deterioration from intratumoural haemorrhage or worsening oedema"],"prognosis":"Glioblastoma carries a poor prognosis with median survival typically measured in months to a couple of years even with optimal multimodal therapy; younger age, good performance status, greater extent of resection, and favourable molecular markers such as MGMT methylation are associated with better outcomes.","pitfalls":["Assuming a solitary enhancing lesion is glioblastoma without considering metastasis, lymphoma, or abscess","Starting high-dose steroids before biopsy when lymphoma is a possibility, as this can obscure the diagnosis","Underestimating infiltration beyond the enhancing margin when planning treatment","Neglecting seizure control, symptom management, and early supportive care discussions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"glomerular_diseases":{"name":"Glomerular Diseases (Spectrum)","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["glomerulopathy","IgA nephropathy","FSGS","membranous nephropathy","minimal change disease"],"chapter":326,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2411","clinicalPearls":["Glomerular disease is grouped into six clinical syndromes that overlap and are not mutually exclusive: acute nephritic, pulmonary-renal, nephrotic, basement membrane, glomerular-vascular, and infectious disease-associated (Harrison 22e p.2414-2416).","Nephritic syndrome = inflammatory pattern: hematuria with red blood cell casts, dysmorphic red cells, pyuria, hypertension, fluid retention, only mild-to-moderate (1-2 g/24 h) proteinuria, and a falling GFR (Harrison 22e p.2414, 2416).","Nephrotic syndrome = heavy proteinuria (>3.0-3.5 g/24 h) with hypoalbuminemia, hypercholesterolemia, edema/anasarca, hypertension, and only minimal/microscopic hematuria; isolated heavy proteinuria without these features is termed nephrotic-range proteinuria (Harrison 22e p.2414, 2420).","Red blood cell casts or dysmorphic red cells in the sediment make glomerulonephritis likely; as few as 3-5 red cells in spun first-morning urine is suspicious (Harrison 22e p.2414).","Gross hematuria is rare in glomerular disease except in IgA nephropathy and sickle cell disease; most glomerular hematuria is asymptomatic and microscopic (Harrison 22e p.2414).","Kidney biopsy is the key diagnostic step in most glomerulonephritis; it identifies the lesion and often directs treatment, but is reserved for clinically stable patients with adequate clotting who can receive therapy (Harrison 22e p.2416-2417).","Minimal change disease (MCD) causes 70-90% of childhood nephrotic syndrome but only 10-15% in adults; light microscopy is normal, immunofluorescence negative (or scant mesangial IgM), and electron microscopy shows foot-process effacement; childhood proteinuria is selective (mostly albumin) (Harrison 22e p.2420-2421).","FSGS is the most common cause of primary glomerular disease leading to ESKD in the US; lesions are focal and segmental and concentrated at the corticomedullary junction, so a superficial biopsy can miss them and be misread as MCD (Harrison 22e p.2421-2422).","Membranous glomerulonephritis (MGN) accounts for ~25% of adult nephrotic syndrome; 70-80% of primary cases have circulating IgG4 anti-PLA2R antibodies, and biopsy may no longer be required when anti-PLA2R is positive with normal creatinine and no secondary cause (Harrison 22e p.2422-2423).","IgA nephropathy is defined by dominant/codominant mesangial IgA deposits and is one of the most common glomerulonephritides worldwide; the two classic presentations are persistent asymptomatic microscopic hematuria and recurrent gross hematuria during/just after an upper respiratory infection (synpharyngitic) (Harrison 22e p.2419).","Membranoproliferative GN (MPGN) is now viewed as a pattern of injury (mesangial/subendothelial immune complexes, complement, or monoclonal deposits) producing GBM double contours ('tram-tracking'), rather than a single disease; classify by underlying immune-complex, complement, or monoclonal cause (Harrison 22e p.2420-2421).","C3 glomerulopathy (dense deposit disease and C3 glomerulonephritis) shows sole/dominant glomerular C3 with little or no immunoglobulin and is driven by alternative-complement-pathway dysregulation (e.g., CFHR mutations, C3 nephritic factor) (Harrison 22e p.2420).","RPGN is the clinical picture of acute nephritis with serum creatinine rising over days; crescentic glomerulonephritis is its pathologic equivalent and warrants urgent biopsy and treatment (Harrison 22e p.2414, 2419).","When RPGN presents with lung hemorrhage it is a pulmonary-renal syndrome; consider anti-GBM/Goodpasture disease, ANCA small-vessel vasculitis, lupus, and cryoglobulinemia (Harrison 22e p.2414, 2418).","ANCA small-vessel vasculitis (GPA, MPA, EGPA, renal-limited) causes a pauci-immune (little/no immune-complex deposition) segmental necrotizing/crescentic glomerulonephritis; anti-PR3 is more common in GPA and anti-MPO in MPA/EGPA (Harrison 22e p.2419-2420).","Anti-GBM disease targets the alpha3 NC1 domain of type IV collagen; biopsy shows linear IgG staining along the GBM (versus the granular 'lumpy-bumpy' deposits of immune-complex disease); 10-15% also have anti-MPO ANCA (Harrison 22e p.2418-2419).","Poststreptococcal GN classically shows subepithelial 'humps' on electron microscopy, low C3/CH50 with normal C4 in the first week, and a positive streptozyme test; it rarely requires biopsy and has a good prognosis in children (Harrison 22e p.2417-2418).","Lupus nephritis is classified ISN/RPS class I-VI; proliferative class III/IV lesions (and class IV with crescents) carry the worst prognosis, while mesangial class I/II need little therapy and class V is a membranous pattern (Harrison 22e p.2418).","Kidney failure in glomerulonephritis correlates histologically with the degree of tubulointerstitial nephritis and interstitial fibrosis rather than with the specific glomerular lesion; interstitial fibrosis is an ominous sign of irreversibility (Harrison 22e p.2413-2414, 2416).","On biopsy, <50% glomerular involvement is focal and >50% diffuse; injury within a tuft is segmental (part) or global (most); endocapillary proliferation is within the tuft and extracapillary proliferation (crescents) extends into Bowman's space (Harrison 22e p.2417)."],"pathophysiology":"Each kidney holds ~0.9 million glomerular tufts whose fenestrated endothelium, GBM, and podocyte foot processes with slit-pore membranes form a size- and charge-selective filtration barrier; glomerular injury allows gram quantities of protein (mainly albumin) to escape (Harrison 22e p.2411-2412). Disease arises through genetic mutations (e.g., NPHS1/nephrin, NPHS2/podocin, TRPC6, APOL1 risk variants for FSGS; type IV collagen alpha3/4/5 in Alport's; complement-pathway/CFHR mutations in MPGN and C3 glomerulopathy/aHUS), infection, toxins, autoimmunity, hypertension/atherosclerosis, thrombosis/emboli, and diabetes; many cases remain idiopathic (Harrison 22e p.2411-2412). Inflammation of glomerular capillaries (glomerulonephritis) is typically immune-mediated: preformed circulating immune complexes deposit on the subendothelial side or mesangium, while in-situ formation produces subepithelial deposits; anti-GBM antibodies bind linearly to the GBM. Deposits and Toll-like receptor activation recruit neutrophils, macrophages, and T cells, activate complement to C5-9 membrane-attack complexes, and release proteases and oxidants that damage the mesangium, capillaries, and GBM and efface podocytes; early CD4/CD8 T-cell activation is important. Depending on antigen location and host genetics, injury produces endocapillary or extracapillary (crescentic) proliferation. Progression to chronic kidney failure tracks with downstream tubulointerstitial nephritis and fibrosis, driven by proteinuria-laden filtrate, inflammatory mediators carried from inflamed glomeruli, and fibroblast/myofibroblast expansion (TGF-beta, FGF-2, HIF-1alpha, PDGF) laying down collagen (Harrison 22e p.2412-2414).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute nephritic syndrome: poststreptococcal GN, subacute bacterial endocarditis-associated GN, lupus nephritis, anti-GBM disease, IgA nephropathy, ANCA small-vessel vasculitis (GPA, MPA, EGPA), IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein purpura), cryoglobulinemia, MPGN, C3 glomerulopathy, mesangioproliferative GN (Harrison 22e p.2414-2415).","Nephrotic syndrome: minimal change disease, FSGS, membranous glomerulonephritis, diabetic nephropathy, AL/AA amyloidosis, light-chain deposition disease, fibrillary-immunotactoid disease, Fabry's disease (Harrison 22e p.2415).","Pulmonary-renal syndromes: Goodpasture's/anti-GBM disease, ANCA vasculitis, IgA vasculitis, cryoglobulinemia (Harrison 22e p.2414-2415).","Basement membrane syndromes: anti-GBM disease, Alport's syndrome, thin basement membrane disease, nail-patella syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2415).","Glomerular vascular syndromes: atherosclerotic and hypertensive nephropathy, cholesterol emboli, sickle cell disease, thrombotic microangiopathies, antiphospholipid syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2415).","Microscopic hematuria with minimal proteinuria can also reflect non-glomerular causes: urinary tract malignancy (especially older men), BPH, interstitial nephritis, papillary necrosis, hypercalciuria, kidney stones, cystic kidney disease, or renal vascular injury (Harrison 22e p.2414)."],"infectionMimics":["Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis after nephritogenic streptococcal pharyngitis (1-3 weeks) or skin infection (2-6 weeks) (Harrison 22e p.2417-2418).","Subacute bacterial endocarditis-associated and other persistent-infection (shunt, abscess, infected prosthesis; often Staphylococcus) glomerulonephritis, which can mimic or coexist with ANCA vasculitis (Harrison 22e p.2417).","Hepatitis B and C (membranous, MPGN, cryoglobulinemic patterns), HIV-associated nephropathy (collapsing FSGS), syphilis, leprosy, malaria, and schistosomiasis - globally, malaria and schistosomiasis may be the most common causes of glomerulonephritis (Harrison 22e p.2416, 2459).","IgA-dominant Staphylococcus-associated infectious glomerulonephritis can mimic primary IgA nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2419)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Diabetic nephropathy and amyloidosis both thicken the GBM and can mimic membranous nephropathy on light microscopy (Harrison 22e p.2422-2423).","Secondary FSGS (adaptive hyperfiltration/obesity, reflux, hypertension, drugs such as heroin/interferon/bisphosphonates, viruses) and genetic/familial podocytopathies must be excluded before diagnosing primary FSGS, as immunosuppression is not indicated in secondary/genetic forms (Harrison 22e p.2421-2422).","Secondary membranous nephropathy from malignancy (breast, lung, colon), infection, lupus or other autoimmune disease, IgG4 disease, or drugs (gold, penicillamine, NSAIDs) (Harrison 22e p.2422).","Membranous lupus nephritis (subendothelial deposits/tubuloreticular inclusions) may precede extrarenal lupus and mimic primary MGN (Harrison 22e p.2423).","C3 glomerulopathy can be morphologically indistinguishable from resolving postinfectious glomerulonephritis (Harrison 22e p.2420).","Mesangioproliferative pattern is shared by IgA nephropathy, P. falciparum malaria, resolving postinfectious GN, and lupus class II (Harrison 22e p.2421)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis with microscopy for dysmorphic red cells, red blood cell casts, pyuria; quantify proteinuria by spot urine albumin/creatinine ratio (UACR >30 mg/g abnormal) or 24-h collection; distinguish selective (albumin-predominant, childhood MCD) from nonselective proteinuria (Harrison 22e p.2414).","Serologies: ASO/anti-DNase/antihyaluronidase and streptozyme; ANA and anti-dsDNA; complement C3 and C4 (low C3 with normal C4 in poststreptococcal GN and C3 glomerulopathy; hypocomplementemia in active lupus nephritis); ANCA (anti-PR3, anti-MPO); anti-GBM antibody to the alpha3 NC1 domain specifically; cryoglobulins and rheumatoid factor; antiphospholipid antibodies; HIV and hepatitis B/C serologies (Harrison 22e p.2416).","Target-antigen antibodies in nephrotic disease: anti-PLA2R and anti-THSD7A (membranous nephropathy); DNAJB9 (fibrillary GN) (Harrison 22e p.2416-2417, 2422-2423).","Kidney ultrasound to assess kidney size (small kidneys suggest chronicity) and exclude obstruction (Harrison 22e p.2416).","Kidney biopsy with light microscopy (H&E, PAS, Jones silver, Congo red, Masson trichrome), immunofluorescence (IgG/IgM/IgA, C3/C4, PLA2R/THSD7A/DNAJB9), and electron microscopy to localize deposits and assess the GBM (Harrison 22e p.2416-2417).","C3 nephritic factor, factor H, soluble C5b-9, paraprotein screening, and CFHR genetic testing (with family screening) when C3 glomerulopathy is suspected (Harrison 22e p.2420)."],"redFlags":["RPGN / crescentic glomerulonephritis - serum creatinine rising over days is a nephrologic emergency requiring urgent biopsy and immunosuppression (Harrison 22e p.2414, 2419).","Pulmonary-renal syndrome (glomerulonephritis with lung hemorrhage/hemoptysis and falling hemoglobin) - consider anti-GBM/Goodpasture, ANCA vasculitis, lupus, cryoglobulinemia; lung hemorrhage responds to plasmapheresis (Harrison 22e p.2414, 2418-2419).","Anti-GBM disease with oliguria, serum creatinine >5-6 mg/dL, need for acute dialysis, or >50% crescents on biopsy - markers of a poor prognosis requiring prompt treatment (Harrison 22e p.2419).","ANCA small-vessel vasculitis - high mortality without treatment; virtually all patients need urgent induction therapy (Harrison 22e p.2419).","Malignant hypertension can rapidly complicate glomerulosclerosis with fibrinoid arteriolar necrosis, thrombotic microangiopathy, and acute kidney failure (Harrison 22e p.2412).","Nephrotic syndrome is a hypercoagulable state - membranous nephropathy carries the highest risk of renal vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, and deep-vein thrombosis (Harrison 22e p.2423)."],"prognosis":"Prognosis is lesion-specific and tracks with the degree of tubulointerstitial fibrosis. Poststreptococcal GN resolves in most children within 3-6 weeks with rare progression to ESKD, but is worse in the elderly (up to 60% azotemia) (Harrison 22e p.2418). Minimal change disease responds to steroids in 90-95% of children and 80-90% of adults though relapse is common (70-75% of children) (Harrison 22e p.2421). Primary FSGS with nephrotic-range proteinuria reaches ESKD in ~50% by 10 years and rarely remits spontaneously, but treatment-induced remission improves outcome (Harrison 22e p.2421-2422). In membranous nephropathy roughly one-third remit spontaneously, one-third have relapsing nephrotic syndrome with preserved function, and one-third progress to kidney failure or die of complications (Harrison 22e p.2423). IgA nephropathy progresses to ESKD in only 25-30% over 20-25 years; persistent proteinuria >=6 months is the strongest adverse predictor (Harrison 22e p.2419). Dense deposit disease progresses to ESKD in ~50%. Untreated RPGN/crescentic disease and ANCA vasculitis carry high mortality, but ANCA vasculitis achieves remission in 75-90% with treatment (relapse 25-50%) (Harrison 22e p.2419-2420).","pitfalls":["A superficial kidney biopsy that fails to sample deeper corticomedullary glomeruli can miss the focal/segmental lesions of FSGS and be misdiagnosed as minimal change disease - suspect FSGS in steroid-resistant 'MCD' (Harrison 22e p.2421-2422).","Test serum specifically against the alpha3 NC1 domain of collagen IV: non-nephritic anti-alpha1 NC1 antibodies in paraneoplastic syndromes cannot be distinguished by assays using whole basement-membrane fragments (Harrison 22e p.2419).","ANCA-associated vasculitis can accompany or be confused with subacute bacterial endocarditis-associated GN; it must be ruled out because the treatments differ (Harrison 22e p.2417).","There is no role for immunosuppression in poststreptococcal GN even when crescents are present, nor in secondary or genetic FSGS (Harrison 22e p.2418, 2422).","Glomerular hematuria is usually microscopic and asymptomatic, so the diagnosis is often delayed; isolated microscopic hematuria in older men still requires exclusion of urinary tract malignancy (Harrison 22e p.2414).","Nephrotic-range proteinuria without edema or hypoalbuminemia is not necessarily full nephrotic syndrome, and transient/orthostatic proteinuria (fever, exercise, obesity, CHF, upright posture) must be distinguished from sustained pathologic proteinuria (Harrison 22e p.2414, 2420)."],"severityClassification":"Glomerular disease is organized into six clinical syndromes (acute nephritic, pulmonary-renal, nephrotic, basement membrane, glomerular-vascular, infectious disease-associated) graded semiquantitatively by proteinuria, hematuria, and vascular injury (Table 326-2, Harrison 22e p.2414-2416). Biopsy descriptors: focal (<50% of glomeruli) vs diffuse (>50%); segmental (part of a tuft) vs global; endocapillary vs extracapillary (crescentic) proliferation (Harrison 22e p.2417). Lupus nephritis uses the ISN/RPS class I-VI scheme (I minimal mesangial, II mesangial proliferative, III focal, IV diffuse, V membranous, VI sclerotic) (Table 326-3, Harrison 22e p.2418). IgA nephropathy uses the Oxford MEST-C score (mesangial/endocapillary hypercellularity, segmental sclerosis, tubular fibrosis, crescents) (Harrison 22e p.2419). Albuminuria is staged normal (8-10 mg/24 h), microalbuminuria (30-300 mg/24 h), and frank proteinuria (>300 mg/24 h) (Table 326-1, Harrison 22e p.2414).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2411","Harrison 22e p.2412-2414","Harrison 22e p.2414-2416","Harrison 22e p.2417-2423"],"crossLinks":["glomerulonephritis","nephrotic","ckd"]},"glucagonoma":{"name":"Glucagonoma","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["glucagon-secreting tumour","alpha-cell tumour","glucagonoma syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare pancreatic alpha-cell neuroendocrine tumour that secretes excess glucagon","The characteristic rash, necrolytic migratory erythema, is a migratory, crusting, erosive eruption favouring the perineum, groin, and extremities","Other typical features include weight loss, a sore red tongue and angular stomatitis, mild diabetes or glucose intolerance, and normocytic anaemia","There is an increased risk of venous thromboembolism, which contributes to morbidity","Diagnosis rests on a markedly elevated fasting plasma glucagon level together with the clinical syndrome","The rash may respond partly to correction of associated amino acid, zinc, and fatty acid deficiencies as well as to control of the tumour","Many tumours are large and a substantial proportion have metastasised, usually to the liver, by the time of diagnosis, so imaging for localisation and staging is essential","Consider a possible association with multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1, and manage with somatostatin analogues for symptom control and surgery for resectable disease"],"pathophysiology":"A glucagonoma is a functioning neuroendocrine tumour of pancreatic alpha cells that oversecretes glucagon. Glucagon is a catabolic, counter-regulatory hormone that promotes hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, causing hyperglycaemia, and stimulates lipolysis and amino acid catabolism, leading to weight loss and depletion of circulating amino acids. The resulting hypoaminoacidaemia, together with deficiencies of zinc and essential fatty acids, is thought to underlie necrolytic migratory erythema. Glucagon excess also promotes a prothrombotic state. Because symptoms are non-specific and evolve slowly, diagnosis is often delayed until the tumour is large or metastatic, most commonly to the liver.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of necrolytic migratory erythema-like rashes such as zinc deficiency (acrodermatitis)","Type 2 diabetes mellitus with weight loss","Other functioning pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours","Malabsorption or malnutrition syndromes","Chronic liver disease with skin and nutritional changes","Pemphigus or other blistering dermatoses"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting plasma glucagon concentration","Blood glucose and glycaemic assessment","Full blood count for normocytic anaemia and serum zinc and amino acid profile","Skin biopsy of the rash when the diagnosis is uncertain","Cross-sectional imaging of the pancreas and liver for localisation and staging","Somatostatin-receptor functional imaging and screening for MEN1 where indicated"],"redFlags":["Venous thromboembolism, including pulmonary embolism, from the prothrombotic state","Extensive or infected necrolytic migratory erythema","Metastatic disease, most often hepatic, at presentation","Profound catabolism with severe weight loss and malnutrition"],"prognosis":"Complete resection of localised disease can be curative and reverses the syndrome, but many patients present with metastatic disease; even then, these tumours are often relatively indolent and symptoms can be controlled for prolonged periods with somatostatin analogues and other systemic therapy.","pitfalls":["Treating recurrent necrolytic migratory erythema as a primary dermatosis and missing the underlying tumour","Attributing new diabetes with weight loss solely to typical type 2 diabetes","Overlooking thromboprophylaxis needs given the high thrombotic risk","Failing to correct nutritional deficiencies that perpetuate the rash"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"glycogen_storage_diseases_and_other":{"name":"Glycogen Storage Diseases and Other Inherited Disorders of Carbohydrate Metabolism","system":"Endocrine/Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Glycogenoses","GSD","von Gierke disease (GSD I)","Pompe disease (GSD II)","Cori/Forbes disease (GSD III)","Andersen disease (GSD IV)","McArdle disease (GSD V)","Hers disease (GSD VI)","Tarui disease (GSD VII)","Galactosemia","Hereditary fructose intolerance"],"chapter":430,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3358","clinicalPearls":["Glycogen storage diseases (GSDs) are inherited defects of glycogen metabolism producing glycogen that is abnormal in structure and/or quantity; collectively their prevalence is estimated at roughly 1 in 10,000 to 1 in 25,000 live births, with most being autosomal recessive and a minority X-linked or autosomal dominant.","GSDs are grouped by the predominant organ affected (liver vs muscle). The most common childhood forms are glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency (GSD I), acid alpha-glucosidase deficiency (GSD II/Pompe), debrancher deficiency (GSD III), and liver phosphorylase kinase deficiency (GSD IX); the most common adult form is myophosphorylase deficiency (GSD V/McArdle).","GSD I (von Gierke) classically presents around 3-4 months with hepatomegaly, doll-like facies with round cheeks, thin extremities, short stature, and a protuberant abdomen; lab hallmarks are fasting hypoketotic hypoglycemia with lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia, hypertriglyceridemia, and elevated LDL with low HDL producing milky/cloudy serum.","A key distinction of GSD III from GSD I is that lactate and uric acid are normal in GSD III, whereas aminotransferases and urine glucose tetrasaccharide (Glc4) are markedly elevated early then decline with age; GSD III hypoglycemia is typically ketotic and gluconeogenesis remains intact (unlike GSD I, which impairs both glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis).","GSD I type Ib differs from Ia by additional neutropenia and impaired neutrophil function, causing recurrent bacterial infections, mucosal ulceration, inflammatory-bowel-disease-like findings, and periodontal disease.","Pompe disease (GSD II) is unique in storing glycogen primarily within lysosomes rather than cytoplasm; classic infantile-onset disease presents with hypotonia, generalized weakness, macroglossia, and rapidly progressive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with death usually before age 2 if untreated, whereas late-onset disease is defined by absence of cardiomyopathy in the first year and slowly progressive proximal/respiratory (diaphragm) weakness.","McArdle disease (GSD V) is a prototypical muscle-energy disorder with exercise-induced cramps, myalgia, weakness, and myoglobinuria; the characteristic 'second wind' phenomenon (continuing exercise pain-free after a brief rest, as fatty-acid oxidation takes over) is unique to GSD V and there is no rise in blood lactate with exercise.","Tarui disease (GSD VII, phosphofructokinase-M deficiency) mimics GSD V but shows an 'out of wind' phenomenon worsened by carbohydrate-rich meals and exercise, no spontaneous second wind, plus compensated hemolysis and hyperuricemia; giving IV dextrose or pre-exercise simple carbohydrates is detrimental in GSD VII (opposite to GSD V).","In a young male with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) pattern on ECG strongly suggests Danon disease (X-linked LAMP2), where the WPW prevalence is about five times that in idiopathic/familial HCM.","Classic galactosemia (GALT deficiency) presents after milk feeding with vomiting, diarrhea, jaundice, hepatomegaly, aminoaciduria, and a notable susceptibility to gram-negative (especially E. coli) sepsis that may precede diagnosis; even with early dietary galactose elimination, long-term outcomes remain poor (speech/learning disorders, cataracts, primary ovarian insufficiency in females).","Hereditary fructose intolerance (aldolase B deficiency) patients are asymptomatic until fructose/sucrose/sorbitol is ingested, then develop postprandial hypoglycemia, jaundice, hepatomegaly, vomiting, and convulsions; affected children characteristically develop an aversion to sweets.","Genetic/molecular testing has largely replaced invasive tissue biopsy for diagnosis and subtyping of many GSDs; Pompe disease is now on the U.S. recommended uniform newborn screening panel."],"pathophysiology":"Glycogen is the branched storage polymer of glucose, formed from alpha-1,4 chains branched by alpha-1,6 linkages. Inherited enzyme defects along glycogen synthesis, glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, and the linked galactose/fructose pathways disrupt glucose homeostasis. In GSD I, deficient glucose-6-phosphatase (Ia) or its translocase (Ib) blocks the final conversion of glucose-6-phosphate to glucose, impairing both glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis and causing fasting hypoglycemia with lactic acidosis and secondary hyperuricemia and hyperlipidemia. In disorders of glycogenolysis with intact gluconeogenesis (GSD III debrancher, GSD VI liver phosphorylase, GSD IX phosphorylase kinase), incomplete glycogen breakdown causes hepatomegaly and milder, often ketotic hypoglycemia. GSD IV (brancher deficiency) produces poorly soluble abnormal glycogen (polyglucosan) injuring liver, heart, muscle, and nervous system. Pompe disease (GSD II) results from lysosomal acid alpha-glucosidase deficiency causing lysosomal glycogen accumulation in cardiac and skeletal muscle. Muscle-energy GSDs (V/McArdle myophosphorylase, VII/Tarui phosphofructokinase, and rarer defects of phosphoglycerate mutase, LDH-A, aldolase A, beta-enolase) limit ATP generation from glycogenolysis/glycolysis, producing exercise intolerance, cramps, and rhabdomyolysis. In galactosemia, deficiency along the Leloir pathway (most commonly GALT) leads to accumulation of toxic galactose-1-phosphate injuring liver, kidney, brain, lens, and ovary. In hereditary fructose intolerance, aldolase B deficiency causes fructose-1-phosphate accumulation and ATP depletion that inhibits gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of infantile/childhood hepatomegaly with hypoglycemia (fatty acid oxidation defects, gluconeogenesis defects such as fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase deficiency)","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy of other etiology (idiopathic/familial HCM) versus glycogenoses with cardiac involvement (Pompe, Danon, PRKAG2)","Polymyositis/inflammatory myopathy (McArdle disease may mimic on EMG)","Other metabolic myopathies and channelopathies presenting with exercise intolerance and rhabdomyolysis","Neonatal sepsis and other inborn errors presenting with jaundice and hepatomegaly (versus galactosemia and hereditary fructose intolerance)","Mitochondrial disorders with lactic acidosis"],"infectionMimics":["Gram-negative (E. coli) neonatal sepsis can be the presenting feature of classic galactosemia and may precede the metabolic diagnosis","Recurrent bacterial infections, oral/intestinal mucosal ulceration, and periodontal disease in GSD Ib due to neutropenia and neutrophil dysfunction"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Idiopathic or familial hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (mimicked by Pompe, Danon, PRKAG2 syndrome)","Polymyositis (McArdle disease may produce inflammatory-appearing EMG)","Statin-induced myopathy/rhabdomyolysis (patients with GSD V and VII are at increased risk and may be confused with primary statin myopathy)","Polycystic ovarian syndrome features (hirsutism, irregular menses) reported in GSD I and GSD III"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting glucose, lactate, and uric acid (lactate/uric acid normal in GSD III but elevated in GSD I)","Lipid profile (hypertriglyceridemia, elevated LDL, low HDL in GSD I)","Serum aminotransferases and urine glucose tetrasaccharide (Glc4) as biomarkers in GSD II, III, and IX","Serum creatine kinase (may be normal despite muscle involvement; not always elevated in late-onset Pompe)","Blood ketones during fasting/illness for liver GSDs","Molecular/genetic testing (e.g., AGL, GBE1, PYGL, PHKA2/PHKB/PHKG2, GAA, PYGM, PFKM, GALT, aldolase B genes) now preferred over biopsy","Enzyme assays (e.g., acid alpha-glucosidase in blood/fibroblasts/muscle for Pompe; CRIM status determination)","Nonischemic forearm exercise test for suspected muscle GSD (abnormal lactate response); ischemic test largely abandoned","ECG (high-voltage QRS and short PR in infantile Pompe; WPW pattern in Danon/PRKAG2), echocardiography, and cardiac/muscle MRI or ultrasound","Liver biopsy/histology and abdominal imaging (CT/MRI surveillance for hepatocellular adenoma and carcinoma in liver GSDs)","Newborn screening (Pompe on the U.S. RUSP; galactosemia screening programs)"],"redFlags":["Neonatal/infant hypoglycemia with lactic acidosis (GSD I) or with sepsis-like presentation","Rapidly progressive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and heart failure in infancy (infantile Pompe; congenital PRKAG2; massive myocardial glycogen deposition)","WPW pattern with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy in a young male (Danon disease)","Burgundy/dark urine with myoglobinuria after strenuous exercise (rhabdomyolysis) risking acute renal failure and acute compartment syndrome in muscle GSDs","Respiratory insufficiency/failure as a presenting or progressive feature of late-onset Pompe disease","Easy bruising, epistaxis, and prolonged bleeding time from platelet dysfunction/acquired von Willebrand-like disease in GSD I","Hepatocellular adenoma with hemorrhage or malignant transformation to hepatocellular carcinoma in liver GSDs (I, III, IV, VI, IX)","Risk of malignant hyperthermia with halogenated anesthetics/muscle relaxants in GSD V and VII"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies widely by disorder and treatment. GSD I requires lifelong fasting avoidance; survivors face long-term gout, osteopenia/osteoporosis, hepatocellular adenoma with potential HCC, progressive renal disease/proteinuria advancing to end-stage renal disease, and increased pancreatitis and cardiovascular risk. GSD III liver disease often appears to improve in childhood but can progress to fibrosis, cirrhosis, and HCC, while GSD IIIa muscle and cardiac disease may worsen after the third-fourth decade with risk of sudden death from arrhythmia. Classic infantile Pompe disease is fatal within about 2 years untreated, but enzyme replacement therapy has substantially improved survival in both infantile and late-onset forms; CRIM-negative patients fare worse without immunomodulation. McArdle disease has a generally good prognosis with lifestyle measures, and physically active patients do better than inactive ones. Andersen disease (GSD IV) classic hepatic form is often fatal before age 5 without liver transplantation. Danon disease carries a poor prognosis with progressive end-stage heart failure in early adulthood. Classic galactosemia has poor long-term neurodevelopmental and ovarian outcomes despite early diet, whereas clinical-variant galactosemia and well-treated hereditary fructose intolerance generally have good prognoses; benign fructosuria and biochemical-variant (Duarte) galactosemia are essentially benign.","pitfalls":["Normal serum creatine kinase does not exclude muscle involvement, including in late-onset Pompe disease, and normal muscle histology or EMG can occur due to disease heterogeneity.","GSD VI and hepatic GSD IX were historically considered benign but show clinical heterogeneity, with reported fibrosis, cirrhosis, HCA, and HCC, including in the X-linked form.","Giving IV dextrose or pre-exercise simple carbohydrates worsens GSD VII (Tarui), the opposite of the beneficial effect of pre-exercise carbohydrate in GSD V (McArdle).","PRKAG2 syndrome can be misdiagnosed as infantile-onset Pompe disease due to phenotypic similarity of severe hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.","Diagnosing galactokinase (type II) deficiency is often missed because many newborn screening programs measure GALT activity rather than galactose directly, so it requires excluding GALT deficiency.","Complete galactose elimination is NOT recommended in GALE deficiency (type III), because galactose is needed for nervous-system structural proteins and these patients cannot synthesize UDP-galactose from glucose; dietary restriction rather than elimination is used.","The traditional discrete hepatic versus neuromuscular subtyping of GSD IV is now viewed as an oversimplification; it is better conceptualized as a multidimensional clinical continuum.","McArdle disease may mimic inflammatory myopathy/polymyositis on EMG, risking misdiagnosis."],"severityClassification":"Several GSDs are stratified by clinical severity and onset. Pompe disease (GSD II): classic infantile-onset (cardiomyopathy at/near birth, fatal within ~2 years untreated, GAA typically <1%), nonclassic infantile-onset (less severe cardiomyopathy, slower progression), and late-onset (no cardiomyopathy in first year, slowly progressive proximal/respiratory weakness, GAA ~2-35%); CRIM-positive versus CRIM-negative status further guides immunomodulation. GSD III is subclassified IIIa (~85%, liver plus cardiac/skeletal muscle) versus IIIb (~15%, liver only). GSD I is divided into Ia (~80%) and Ib (~20%, with neutropenia/neutrophil dysfunction). GSD IV/Andersen spans a continuum from lethal perinatal/congenital neuromuscular forms through classic progressive and nonprogressive hepatic forms to adult polyglucosan body disease (APBD). Galactosemia type I is graded as classic (absent/barely detectable GALT, severe), clinical variant (residual activity, milder, common in African Americans), and biochemical/Duarte variant (asymptomatic).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3358","Harrison 22e p.3359","Harrison 22e p.3360","Harrison 22e p.3361","Harrison 22e p.3362","Harrison 22e p.3363","Harrison 22e p.3364","Harrison 22e p.3365","Harrison 22e p.3366"],"crossLinks":[]},"gonorrhea":{"name":"Gonorrhea","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["gonococcal infection","Neisseria gonorrhoeae"],"chapter":161,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1253-1260","clinicalPearls":["Gonorrhea is a sexually transmitted infection of the epithelium that commonly presents as cervicitis, urethritis, proctitis, or conjunctivitis; if untreated it can cause local complications including endometritis, salpingitis, tuboovarian abscess, bartholinitis, peritonitis, and perihepatitis in women, and periurethritis and epididymitis in men (p.1253).","In men, acute urethritis is the most common manifestation, typically after a 2-7 day incubation, with discharge that is initially scant and mucoid but becomes profuse and purulent within a day or two; however, a reservoir of infected men remains asymptomatic (p.1255).","About half of women infected with N. gonorrhoeae develop symptoms; many have only minor symptoms (scant cervical discharge, dysuria) and may delay care, so the asymptomatic/minimally symptomatic reservoir is key to ongoing transmission (p.1256).","Gram stain of urethral exudate showing gram-negative intracellular monococci and diplococci is highly specific and sensitive in symptomatic men, but is only about 50% sensitive for gonococcal cervicitis, so women should be diagnosed by culture or a nonculture (NAAT) method (p.1257).","Nucleic acid amplification tests (NAATs) are now widely used, are more sensitive than culture for both N. gonorrhoeae and C. trachomatis, allow urine and self-collected vaginal swab testing, and simultaneously detect chlamydial co-infection (p.1257).","Disseminated gonococcal infection (DGI) results from gonococcal bacteremia and most commonly presents as an arthritis-dermatitis syndrome with fever, chills, tenosynovitis, polyarthralgias, and characteristic skin lesions (papules/pustules with a hemorrhagic component, ~5-40, on the extremities) seen in about 75% of patients (p.1257).","Menstruation is a risk factor for dissemination and roughly two-thirds of DGI cases occur in women; in about half of affected women symptoms begin within 7 days of onset of menses (p.1257).","Persons deficient in terminal complement components (C5-C9) and those receiving the C5-blocking monoclonal antibody eculizumab are predisposed to recurrent neisserial bacteremia; anyone with more than one episode of DGI should be screened for complement deficiency (p.1255, 1260).","Ceftriaxone is the recommended first-line agent for uncomplicated gonococcal infection, now at twice the previously recommended dose because mean inhibitory concentrations of current strains have doubled relative to 20 years ago (p.1259).","Quinolone-containing regimens and cefixime are no longer recommended as first-line treatment in the United States because of widespread quinolone resistance and rising cefixime MICs (p.1259).","Because co-infection with C. trachomatis occurs frequently, concurrent treatment for chlamydia (doxycycline) is recommended whenever chlamydial infection cannot be excluded (p.1259)."],"pathophysiology":"N. gonorrhoeae is a gram-negative, nonmotile, non-spore-forming, oxidase-positive diplococcus and an exclusively human pathogen; it carries on average three genome copies per coccal unit, and this polyploidy supports a high level of antigenic variation that aids host survival. Pili (fimbriae) mediate initial adherence to nonciliated columnar epithelial cells and enable invasion, transcytosis, genetic competence and transformation; pilus expression is switched off on subculture as a basis for antigenic variation. Opacity-associated protein (Opa) mediates intergonococcal and epithelial adhesion and binds CEACAM receptors, promoting invasion, suppressing CD4+ T-cell activation, and enabling nonopsonic neutrophil phagocytosis. Porin (the most abundant surface protein) forms the basis of serotyping; PorB.1A strains are associated with disseminated infection, resist normal human serum killing, bind complement-inhibitory molecules and may not cause local symptoms, whereas PorB.1B strains usually cause local genital infection. Lipooligosaccharide (LOS) has marked endotoxic activity; sialylation of LOS inhibits classical and alternative complement pathways and reduces neutrophil killing. Iron/zinc scavenging proteins (transferrin-, lactoferrin-, hemoglobin-binding proteins) and IgA1 protease promote survival. Host entry into nonphagocytic cells involves phospholipase C and sphingomyelinase activation with ceramide-mediated apoptosis, and inflammation is driven by LOS and peptidoglycan-induced cytokine release (p.1254-1255).","additionalDifferentials":["Nongonococcal urethritis (C. trachomatis, Mycoplasma genitalium, Ureaplasma urealyticum, Trichomonas vaginalis, HSV) - clinically often impossible to distinguish from gonococcal urethritis (p.1255-1256)","Bacterial cystitis/urinary tract infection versus the urethral syndrome in women presenting with dysuria (p.1256)","For the bacteremic stage of DGI: reactive arthritis, acute rheumatoid arthritis, sarcoidosis, erythema nodosum, drug-induced arthritis, and viral infections including hepatitis B and acute HIV infection (p.1257)","Suppurative gonococcal arthritis in the absence of symptomatic genital infection cannot be distinguished from septic arthritis caused by other pathogens (p.1257)"],"infectionMimics":["Meningococcemia, which can produce a dermatitis-arthritis syndrome, purulent monoarthritis, and reactive polyarthritis similar to DGI (p.1058)","Acute HIV infection and hepatitis B (viral causes mimicking the bacteremic arthritis-dermatitis stage of DGI) (p.1257)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Reactive arthritis (joint distribution differs from DGI; skin and genital manifestations also differ) (p.1257)","Acute rheumatoid arthritis (p.1257)","Sarcoidosis (p.1257)","Erythema nodosum (p.1257)","Drug-induced arthritis (p.1257)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Gram stain of urethral discharge for gram-negative intracellular monococci/diplococci - highly sensitive in symptomatic men, only ~50% sensitive for cervicitis (p.1255, 1257)","NAAT (e.g., Aptima Combo 2, Xpert CT/NG, BD ProbeTec, COBAS) on urine, urethral, cervical or self-collected vaginal swabs - more sensitive than culture and co-detects C. trachomatis; point-of-care NAAT with ~30-minute turnaround available (p.1257)","Culture on modified Thayer-Martin/selective medium with prompt processing (gonococci do not tolerate drying); single endocervical culture ~80-90% sensitive (p.1257)","In MSM, screen rectum and pharynx in addition to urine, since urine alone will miss the majority of cases (p.1257)","Blood cultures (Isolator tubes may improve yield; positivity falls after 48 h of illness) and synovial fluid (inoculate into blood culture broth and chocolate agar) in suspected DGI; organisms are seldom recovered from blood and synovial fluid in the same patient (p.1257)","Standardized culture is the preferred legal-evidence method in children for suspected sexual abuse; two NAATs targeting different sequences may substitute (p.1257)","Total hemolytic complement activity assay in anyone with more than one episode of DGI (p.1255, 1260)"],"redFlags":["Disseminated gonococcal infection with the joint-localized suppurative arthritis stage - hospitalization indicated, may need joint aspiration and higher/longer therapy (p.1259)","Gonococcal meningitis - rare complication of DGI requiring high-dose IV ceftriaxone for 10-14 days (p.1257, 1260)","Gonococcal endocarditis - rare today but historically accounted for about one-quarter of endocarditis cases; requires at least 4 weeks of IV therapy (p.1257, 1260)","Ophthalmia neonatorum and adult ocular gonorrhea - prompt recognition and treatment essential, as corneal ulceration may progress to perforation, panophthalmitis and blindness (p.1256-1257)","Pelvic inflammatory disease should be considered when cervical infection extends to cause dyspareunia or lower abdominal/back pain (p.1256)"],"prognosis":"A single recommended ceftriaxone regimen almost always cures uncomplicated urethral, cervical, rectal, or pharyngeal infection, and true treatment failure with a recommended regimen is rare. Symptomatic pharyngeal gonorrhea is harder to eradicate than genital infection. In the preantibiotic era untreated urethritis persisted about 8 weeks. DGI incidence has fallen (from ~0.5-3% of untreated mucosal infections in the 1970s) likely due to a decline in disseminating strains. A dramatic alleviation of arthritis-dermatitis symptoms within 12-24 h of appropriate antibiotics supports a clinical diagnosis of DGI even when cultures are negative. Rising worldwide resistance, including reduced ceftriaxone susceptibility, threatens future treatment efficacy (p.1257-1260).","pitfalls":["Because co-infection with C. trachomatis is frequent, treatment of gonorrhea must include concurrent therapy for chlamydia (doxycycline) whenever chlamydial infection cannot be excluded - omitting this is a key pitfall (p.1259).","Azithromycin is no longer recommended as part of the first-line regimen because U.S. gonococcal resistance to azithromycin rose more than sevenfold to 4.7% in 2021 (p.1259).","A presumptive diagnosis of gonorrhea cannot be made from gram-negative diplococci on a pharyngeal Gram stain, because commensal Neisseria species are normal pharyngeal flora (p.1257).","Gram stain is only ~50% sensitive for gonococcal cervicitis; relying on it in women risks missed diagnoses, so culture or NAAT is required (p.1257).","DGI strains often do not incite a local genital inflammatory response and may not cause genital symptoms, so disseminated disease can occur without an obvious mucosal source (p.1254, 1257).","Quinolones and cefixime are no longer first-line in the United States due to resistance and rising MICs - using them empirically risks treatment failure (p.1259).","Persistent symptoms after a recommended regimen should prompt evaluation for reinfection, a drug-resistant strain, or an alternative diagnosis rather than simple re-treatment; culture and susceptibility testing should be performed (p.1259).","Because of high reinfection rates within 6-12 months, persons treated for gonorrhea should be retested at 3 months (p.1259)."],"severityClassification":"Clinical manifestations span uncomplicated mucosal infection (urethritis, cervicitis, proctitis, pharyngitis, conjunctivitis) versus complicated/disseminated disease. DGI has historically been described in two overlapping stages: a bacteremic stage (higher fever, chills, polyarthralgias, tenosynovitis, and skin lesions) and a joint-localized stage with suppurative (septic) arthritis, though a clear-cut progression is usually not evident (p.1257).","references":["Ram S, Rice PA. Gonococcal Infections. In: Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd ed., Chapter 161, pp. 1253-1260.","Holmes KK et al: Disseminated gonococcal infection. Ann Intern Med 74:979, 1971.","CDC Gonorrhea Treatment Recommendations 2020 and 2021 Sexually Transmitted Infections (STI) Treatment Guidelines."],"crossLinks":[]},"gout_and_other_crystal_associated":{"name":"Gout and Other Crystal-Associated Arthropathies","system":"Musculoskeletal / Rheumatologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Gout","Podagra","Crystal-induced arthritis","Pseudogout","Calcium pyrophosphate deposition disease","CPPD","Chondrocalcinosis","Basic calcium phosphate disease","Apatite arthropathy","Calcium oxalate arthropathy"],"chapter":384,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2956","clinicalPearls":["Gout is a hyperuricemic metabolic disorder in which chronic hyperuricemia leads to supersaturation and deposition of monosodium urate (MSU) crystals in joints and connective tissue; it typically presents with episodic, intensely painful inflammatory arthritis in middle-aged-to-elderly men and postmenopausal women (Harrison 22e p.2956-2957).","The first metatarsophalangeal joint of the great toe (podagra) is involved in 70-90% of cases; other early targets are tarsal joints, ankles, and knees, while finger, wrist, and elbow involvement is more typical of elderly patients or advanced disease (Harrison 22e p.2958).","Flares often begin at night or in the early morning and are among the most painful conditions humans experience; affected joints rapidly become warm, red, tender, and swollen, sometimes mimicking cellulitis (pseudocellulitis), and usually subside spontaneously within 1-2 weeks (Harrison 22e p.2958).","Definitive diagnosis rests on demonstrating needle-shaped MSU crystals (intracellular and extracellular) showing strong negative birefringence under compensated polarized light; synovial fluid is cloudy with leukocyte counts roughly 5000-75,000/uL (Harrison 22e p.2958-2959).","Serum urate may be normal or even low during an acute flare because inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6 are uricosuric and can lower it by about 2 mg/dL, so a normal level during a flare does not exclude gout; historical or postflare values are more diagnostically useful (Harrison 22e p.2959).","Musculoskeletal ultrasound (double-contour sign over articular cartilage) and dual-energy CT (chemical identification of MSU) aid early diagnosis; plain radiographs in advanced tophaceous gout show well-defined erosions with sclerotic, overhanging margins and soft-tissue masses, though these are absent early (Harrison 22e p.2959).","Hyperuricemia is most often due to impaired renal urate excretion (about 90%) rather than overproduction (about 10%); roughly two-thirds of daily uric acid is eliminated renally and one-third through the gut (intestinal uricolysis by gut bacteria) (Harrison 22e p.2957).","Calcium pyrophosphate deposition disease (CPPD) is strongly age-related, with prevalence in articular tissues roughly doubling each decade after 60 and reaching nearly 50% in those over 85; most deposition is asymptomatic (Harrison 22e p.2960).","Acute CPP crystal arthritis (pseudogout) most often affects the knee, then the wrist, and rarely the first MTP joint; episodes tend to last longer than gout flares (weeks to months) and may be accompanied by fever, chills, and elevated acute-phase reactants (Harrison 22e p.2960).","Chondrocalcinosis (punctate/linear radiodense deposits in fibrocartilage or hyaline cartilage) supports CPPD; definitive diagnosis requires rhomboid or rod-shaped weakly positively birefringent or nonbirefringent crystals in synovial fluid or tissue (Harrison 22e p.2961).","Apatite (basic calcium phosphate) crystals are extremely small and individually visible only by electron microscopy; clumps appear as nonbirefringent globules staining bright red with alizarin red S, and apatite underlies the destructive 'Milwaukee shoulder' of the elderly (Harrison 22e p.2961).","Calcium oxalate (CaOx) arthropathy is largely a disease of patients on long-term dialysis, especially with ascorbic acid supplementation, since ascorbate is metabolized to oxalate that is poorly cleared in uremia; the readily recognized crystals are bipyramidal with strong birefringence (Harrison 22e p.2962)."],"pathophysiology":"Chronic hyperuricemia is a prerequisite for gout but not sufficient alone, since only a minority of hyperuricemic individuals ever develop disease. Humans uniquely develop gout spontaneously because of an evolutionary species-wide loss of uricase, the enzyme that converts urate to highly soluble allantoin; at physiologic pH uric acid (pKa about 5.8) exists mainly as ionized urate. An increased urate body pool leads to MSU supersaturation (favored above roughly 6.85 mg/dL) and crystal formation/deposition in joints and connective tissue, modulated by temperature, pH, intra-articular dehydration, cation concentration, and matrix proteoglycans/collagen. Hyperuricemia stems predominantly from impaired renal excretion (about 90%) and less often overproduction (about 10%); overproduction relates to increased cell turnover (hematologic malignancy, psoriasis), chemotherapy, tissue hypoxia, or inherited defects such as PRPP synthetase superactivity or HPRT deficiency (Lesch-Nyhan spectrum). Renal handling involves sodium-dependent apical reabsorption via URAT1/OAT10, basolateral GLUT9 efflux, and intestinal excretion via ABCG2. Acute flares are triggered when MSU crystals are shed from inert deposits and activate resident synovial macrophages, mast cells, and the NLRP3 inflammasome, generating IL-1beta, IL-6, IL-8, and TNF-alpha; neutrophil phagocytosis of crystals amplifies inflammation via a respiratory burst. Untreated, growing tophaceous MSU deposits with granulomatous inflammation produce chronic gouty arthritis with erosive deformity. In CPPD, increased cartilage inorganic pyrophosphate (largely from extracellular ATP breakdown, regulated by the ANKH transporter) combines with calcium to form CPP crystals that, like MSU, activate phagocytes and the inflammasome. Apatite deposition occurs in tissue damage (dystrophic), hypercalcemic/hyperparathyroid states (metastatic), connective tissue disease (calcinosis), and chronic renal failure (tumoral calcinosis, calciphylaxis). CaOx deposits accumulate in cartilage, synovium, bone, and viscera in chronic renal failure and primary oxalosis (Harrison 22e p.2956-2962).","additionalDifferentials":["Septic (bacterial) arthritis","Other crystal-associated arthropathies (CPPD/pseudogout, apatite, calcium oxalate)","Palindromic rheumatism","Psoriatic arthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis (especially chronic tophaceous gout or pseudo-rheumatoid CPPD)","Osteoarthritis (pseudoosteoarthritis pattern of CPPD)","Cellulitis (pseudocellulitis appearance of an acute flare)","Neuropathic (Charcot) arthropathy (mimicked by severe destructive CPPD)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute septic arthritis can closely resemble an acute crystal flare, and bacterial infection can coexist with MSU or CPP crystals in synovial fluid, so joint fluid should be Gram-stained and cultured","Acute flares with overlying erythema, warmth, and swelling can mimic cellulitis (pseudocellulitis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Osteoarthritis (pseudoosteoarthritis form of CPPD)","Rheumatoid arthritis (chronic tophaceous gout; chronic symmetric synovitis pattern of CPPD)","Neuropathic arthropathy (severe destructive CPPD)","Erosive osteoarthritis of the fingers (apatite-associated)","Spinal stenosis or crowned dens syndrome (CPPD spinal involvement)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Synovial fluid aspiration with polarized light microscopy to identify and distinguish crystal type (MSU vs CPP); apatite is not identifiable by polarized light alone","Synovial fluid leukocyte count and microbial Gram stain and culture to exclude coexisting infection","Serum urate (interpreting with awareness it may be transiently normal during a flare)","Alizarin red S staining for calcium-containing (apatite, CaOx) crystals; electron microscopy with energy-dispersive analysis, x-ray diffraction, infrared or Raman spectroscopy for definitive apatite identification","Plain radiography (erosions with overhanging margins in gout; chondrocalcinosis in CPPD; periarticular calcification in apatite disease)","Musculoskeletal ultrasound (double-contour sign) and dual-energy CT for MSU detection","In CPPD patients under 50: serum calcium, phosphorus, alkaline phosphatase, magnesium, iron, and transferrin, plus screening for familial aggregation and metabolic disease (hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypophosphatasia, hypomagnesemia)","Serum creatinine, liver function tests, hemoglobin, WBC count, HbA1c, and lipids at baseline to assess comorbidities and treatment risk"],"redFlags":["Acutely hot, swollen joint where septic arthritis cannot be excluded clinically, mandating synovial fluid Gram stain and culture","Fever, chills, and elevated acute-phase reactants during a crystal flare (seen in up to 50% of acute CPPD episodes, occasionally to 40 C), which overlap with infection","Hyperuricemia in prepubertal boys, which should prompt evaluation for inherited enzyme defects (HPRT deficiency, PRPP synthetase superactivity)","CPPD arthritis in patients under 50, raising concern for an underlying metabolic disorder (e.g., hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypophosphatasia, hypomagnesemia)","Progressive destructive large-joint disease (e.g., Milwaukee shoulder) signaling severe apatite-associated joint destruction"],"prognosis":"Typical acute gout flares are self-limited, resolving spontaneously within 1-2 weeks, with symptom-free intercritical intervals; however, untreated recurrent flares over years can progress to chronic gouty arthritis with persistent low-grade synovitis, subcutaneous tophi, deformity, and erosive bony destruction. Gout is associated with multiple cardiometabolic-kidney comorbidities (obesity, hypertension, type 2 diabetes, myocardial infarction, stroke, chronic kidney disease, urate nephrolithiasis). Apatite-associated destructive arthropathy (e.g., Milwaukee shoulder) tends to progress indolently but can cause severe pain, instability, and disability requiring joint replacement, and CaOx and chronic CPP arthropathies may progressively destroy joints (Harrison 22e p.2956-2962).","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal serum urate during an acute attack excludes gout; cytokine-mediated uricosuria (e.g., IL-6) can lower it by about 2 mg/dL during flares","Failing to exclude infection: bacterial sepsis can coexist with crystals, and crystals do not rule out a septic joint","Relying on polarized light microscopy to detect apatite, whose individual crystals require electron microscopy and special stains/techniques","Overlooking a secondary metabolic cause when CPPD presents before age 50","Confusing intra-/periarticular apatite calcifications with the linear calcifications of CPPD on radiographs","Diagnosing chondrocalcinosis as CPPD without considering calcium oxalate deposition in chronic renal failure as an alternative"],"severityClassification":"Gout is described as a clinical continuum rather than by a formal grading scale: asymptomatic hyperuricemia; acute (episodic) gout flares with intercritical symptom-free intervals; and chronic (tophaceous) gouty arthritis with persistent synovitis, tophi, deformity, and bony destruction. Crystal-induced disease is categorized by pattern as acute (episodic) mono-, oligo-, or polyarthritis; periarticular inflammation (bursitis, tendinitis, enthesitis); tophaceous deposits; chronic destructive or chronic inflammatory arthropathy; a peculiar osteoarthritis-like form; spinal arthritis; and carpal tunnel syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2956-2957).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2956","Harrison 22e p.2957","Harrison 22e p.2958","Harrison 22e p.2959","Harrison 22e p.2960","Harrison 22e p.2961","Harrison 22e p.2962"],"crossLinks":[]},"granulomatosis_polyangiitis":{"name":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["GPA","Wegener granulomatosis","ANCA-associated vasculitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An ANCA-associated small-vessel vasculitis with a classic triad of upper airway, lung, and kidney involvement","Upper respiratory features include persistent sinusitis, nasal crusting and bleeding, saddle-nose deformity, and subglottic stenosis","Pulmonary disease ranges from nodules and cavities to life-threatening alveolar haemorrhage","Renal involvement is a pauci-immune, rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis with an active urinary sediment","Most patients are positive for c-ANCA directed against proteinase 3 (anti-PR3)","Biopsy shows necrotising granulomatous inflammation with vasculitis; renal biopsy shows crescentic glomerulonephritis with little immune deposition","The pulmonary-renal syndrome (diffuse alveolar haemorrhage with glomerulonephritis) is a medical emergency","Treatment has induction and maintenance phases using glucocorticoids combined with rituximab or cyclophosphamide, and plasma exchange is considered in severe organ-threatening disease"],"pathophysiology":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis is a necrotising vasculitis of small vessels associated with anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies, predominantly against proteinase 3. ANCA activate primed neutrophils, causing degranulation, release of reactive oxygen species and proteases, and endothelial injury, producing pauci-immune (minimal immune complex) inflammation. The disease combines this small-vessel vasculitis with granuloma formation in the respiratory tract. Injury to alveolar capillaries causes diffuse alveolar haemorrhage, and glomerular capillary damage produces crescentic, rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis.","additionalDifferentials":["Microscopic polyangiitis and eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Anti-GBM (Goodpasture) disease","Other pulmonary-renal syndromes including lupus","Chronic infective sinusitis or invasive fungal disease","Sarcoidosis and other granulomatous disorders","Malignancy of the upper airway or lung"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["ANCA testing with PR3 and MPO antigen specificity","Urinalysis and microscopy for dysmorphic red cells and casts, with renal function monitoring","Chest imaging (radiograph and CT) for nodules, cavities, and haemorrhage","Tissue biopsy (renal, lung, or ENT) for necrotising granulomatous vasculitis","Assessment of upper airway including nasendoscopy and evaluation for subglottic stenosis"],"redFlags":["Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage presenting with haemoptysis, hypoxia, and falling haemoglobin","Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis with rising creatinine requiring urgent immunosuppression and possible plasma exchange","Airway compromise from subglottic or tracheal stenosis","Ocular involvement such as orbital pseudotumour or scleritis threatening vision","Mononeuritis multiplex or central nervous system involvement"],"prognosis":"Once almost uniformly fatal, the disease now has good remission rates with modern induction therapy, but relapse is common and requires ongoing surveillance. Prognosis is worse with severe renal impairment at presentation and with alveolar haemorrhage; long-term morbidity includes chronic kidney disease and treatment-related complications.","pitfalls":["Treating recurrent sinusitis or nasal symptoms as isolated ENT disease and missing systemic vasculitis","Delaying immunosuppression in rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, risking irreversible renal failure","Assuming a negative ANCA excludes the diagnosis, especially in limited disease","Overlooking early alveolar haemorrhage when haemoptysis is minimal","Failing to monitor for relapse and treatment-related infection during maintenance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"gynecologic_malignancies":{"name":"Gynecologic Malignancies","system":"Reproductive (Female Genital Tract)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ovarian cancer","fallopian tube cancer","cervical cancer","endometrial cancer","uterine cancer","gestational trophoblastic disease","hydatidiform mole","choriocarcinoma"],"chapter":94,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.709","clinicalPearls":["Epithelial tumors are the most common ovarian neoplasm; ovarian cancers are split into indolent type 1 (low-grade, KRAS/BRAF/PTEN/PIK3CA mutations) and aggressive type 2 high-grade serous cancers, the latter driven by TP53 loss (about 95%) and BRCA1/2 dysfunction, with growing evidence that many high-grade serous cancers actually originate in the fallopian tube epithelium.","Germline BRCA1/2 testing is recommended for all incident epithelial ovarian cancers; lifetime ovarian cancer risk is roughly 39 to 63 percent for BRCA1 and 16.5 to 27 percent for BRCA2, and risk-reducing salpingo-oophorectomy after childbearing (ideally before age 40) is highly protective and also lowers subsequent breast cancer risk by about 50 percent.","About 70 percent of ovarian cancer patients present at stage III; a markedly elevated serum CA-125 (shed MUC16) is characteristic but not specific, and PET is generally not required for evaluation.","Bilateral ovarian masses from metastatic mucin-secreting gastrointestinal cancers are termed Krukenberg tumors; metastatic disease to the ovary also arises from colon, appendix, stomach, and breast primaries, so other malignancies must be considered in the workup.","High-risk HPV infection (especially types 16 and 18, also 31, 33, 35, 52, 58) is the primary initiating event in the vast majority of invasive cervical cancers; viral E6 and E7 proteins inactivate p53 and RB respectively, but full transformation requires additional acquired mutations over years.","Cervical cancer staging is fundamentally clinical: stage I confined to cervix, stage II into upper vagina/paracervical tissue, stage III to lower vagina or pelvic sidewall, stage IV invading bladder/rectum or distant; MRI better estimates uterine and paracervical soft-tissue extension while CT detects hydronephrosis indicating sidewall disease.","Endometrioid endometrial adenocarcinoma is the most common gynecologic malignancy in the United States; unopposed estrogen exposure is the principal risk factor, so obesity, postmenopausal estrogen therapy, estrogen-producing tumors, and tamoxifen (weakly estrogenic in uterine epithelium) all increase risk while progestins are protective.","Most women with uterine corpus cancer present with postmenopausal bleeding (or atypical intermenstrual bleeding premenopausally), leading to early-stage detection and high cure rates; about 75 percent are stage I.","Routine microsatellite instability (MSI) testing of all endometrial cancers is now standard, because MSI/mismatch-repair-deficient tumors that recur or present advanced are likely to respond to PD-1/PD-L1 immune checkpoint therapy.","Gestational trophoblastic disease spans benign hydatidiform mole to choriocarcinoma; complete moles are diploid with entirely paternal genetic material (uniparental disomy, no fetal development), while partial moles are triploid (egg fertilized by two sperm) and may show early fetal development.","Trophoblastic disease is exquisitely chemosensitive and monitored by serial serum beta-hCG; even very advanced disease is almost uniformly curable when managed by an expert.","Gardasil-9 protects against HPV types 6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, and 58; vaccination before sexual activity dramatically lowers high-risk infection and dysplasia, but vaccinated women remain at residual risk and still need Pap screening."],"pathophysiology":"Ovarian cancer arises from epithelial, stromal/sex-cord, or germ cell lineages; high-grade (type 2) serous cancers begin in fallopian tube epithelium with BRCA1/2 loss and TP53 mutation producing intraepithelial cancer, then shed and disseminate across the peritoneum aided by MUC16 expression and mesothelin binding, whereas indolent type 1 cancers carry KRAS/BRAF/PTEN/PIK3CA mutations. Cervical cancer follows persistent high-risk HPV infection near the cervical transformation zone, where viral E6/E7 inactivate p53 and RB to immortalize epithelium; HPV genome integration plus APOBEC-pattern mutagenesis and further hits drive progression from dysplasia to invasive squamous cell carcinoma or adenocarcinoma. Endometrial type 1 (endometrioid) cancers are driven by relative estrogen excess unopposed by progesterone, with secondary PI3K mutation or PTEN loss; type 2 (serous, clear cell) cancers show TP53 loss and behave like high-grade serous ovarian cancer. Gestational trophoblastic neoplasia results from outgrowth/persistence of placental trophoblast, with complete moles being androgenetic diploid tumors and partial moles triploid.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign adnexal mass or functional ovarian cyst","Endometriosis / endometrioma","Pelvic inflammatory disease and tubo-ovarian abscess","Uterine leiomyoma (fibroid) versus leiomyosarcoma","Metastatic gastrointestinal or breast cancer to the ovary (Krukenberg tumor)","Normal intrauterine or ectopic pregnancy versus molar pregnancy","Cervicitis or benign cervical polyp versus invasive cervical carcinoma","Dysfunctional or anovulatory uterine bleeding versus endometrial carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":["Pelvic inflammatory disease / tubo-ovarian abscess mimicking an adnexal malignancy","HPV-related cervicitis or low-grade transient HPV infection mimicking high-grade cervical dysplasia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Benign ovarian cysts and borderline (low malignant potential) tumors mimicking invasive ovarian cancer","Uterine leiomyomas mimicking sarcoma","Endometriosis mimicking peritoneal carcinomatosis","Meigs syndrome (ovarian fibroma with ascites and hydrothorax) mimicking metastatic ovarian cancer"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum CA-125 (and HE-4, mesothelin) for ovarian cancer; trends used to monitor relapse","Transvaginal/pelvic ultrasonography to characterize adnexal masses; CT for carcinomatosis and adenopathy","Serum and serial beta-hCG (and AFP) for germ cell and gestational trophoblastic tumors","Pap (cytology) smear plus HPV PCR testing for cervical screening","Endometrial biopsy for postmenopausal or abnormal bleeding","MRI for cervical uterine/paracervical extension; CT for hydronephrosis","Germline BRCA1/2 and Lynch syndrome (MSH2, MLH1, MSH6, PMS1, PMS2) testing; tumor microsatellite instability and homologous recombination deficiency testing"],"redFlags":["Rapidly increasing abdominal girth from ascites with nausea, early satiety, bloating, and abdominal pain suggesting advanced ovarian cancer","Postmenopausal vaginal bleeding (must exclude endometrial carcinoma)","Postcoital or intermenstrual bleeding or foul/persistent discharge suggesting cervical cancer","Flank pain/hydronephrosis or sacral/pelvic nerve pain indicating advanced cervical pelvic sidewall disease","Persistent or rising beta-hCG after molar evacuation indicating persistent gestational trophoblastic disease","Preeclampsia/eclampsia, hyperthyroidism, or very high beta-hCG in pregnancy raising concern for molar pregnancy","Acute pelvic pain from ovarian torsion, intratumoral hemorrhage, or rupture"],"prognosis":"Ovarian epithelial cancer 5-year survival correlates strongly with stage: roughly 90 to 95 percent stage I, 70 to 80 percent stage II, 25 to 40 percent stage III, and 10 to 15 percent stage IV; prognosis improves with lower histologic grade and BRCA1/2 germline mutation, and complete (R0) cytoreduction yields median survival beyond 60 months versus 28 to 42 months with macroscopic residual disease. Endometrial cancer is usually localized (about 75 percent stage I) with high cure; cervical cancer 5-year survival declines from about 85 percent (stage I) to roughly 7 percent (stage IV). Borderline (low malignant potential) ovarian tumors and ovarian germ cell tumors (including dysgerminoma) are highly curable. Gestational trophoblastic disease is almost uniformly curable, with single-agent therapy curing about 90 percent of low-risk cases.","pitfalls":["Nonspecific GI symptoms (bloating, early satiety, change in bowel/urinary pattern) of ovarian cancer are frequently dismissed until late stages","Needle biopsy of an adnexal mass is contraindicated because it risks seeding malignant cells into the peritoneal cavity","HPV-negative cervical cancers (more common in older women, with KRAS/ARID1A/PTEN mutations) may behave differently","Endocervical canal or glandular cervical lesions may be invisible on inspection and missed by Pap smear","Adding HPV PCR increases sensitivity but lowers specificity, flagging many transient infections needing no intervention","Minimally invasive surgery for early cervical cancer gives inferior outcomes compared with open hysterectomy","Suboptimally debulked ovarian cancer (residual >1 cm) derives very little benefit from surgery","Late relapse (>2 years) of germ cell tumors tends to be chemoresistant and is best approached surgically"],"severityClassification":"Staging per Table 94-1: Ovarian - I confined to ovary (88-95%), II confined to pelvic organs (70-80%), III intra-abdominal spread to omentum/diaphragm/nodes (20-40%), IV spread outside abdominal cavity/parenchymal/pleural (10-20%); most present as stage IIIC (bulky intraperitoneal or positive nodes). Endometrial - I confined to corpus (>90%), II corpus and cervix (~75%), III outside uterus but within true pelvis (45-60%), IV beyond true pelvis or bladder/rectum (~20%). Cervix - 0 carcinoma in situ (100%), I confined to uterus (85%), II beyond uterus not to pelvic wall (65%), III to pelvic wall and/or lower third vagina or hydronephrosis (35%), IV invading bladder/rectal mucosa or beyond true pelvis (7%). Gestational trophoblastic disease is risk-stratified (low/intermediate/high) by scoring algorithms guiding single- versus multiagent therapy.","references":["Harrison 22e p.709","Harrison 22e p.710","Harrison 22e p.711","Harrison 22e p.712","Harrison 22e p.713","Harrison 22e p.714","Harrison 22e p.715"],"crossLinks":[]},"gynecomastia":{"name":"Gynecomastia","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["gynaecomastia","male breast enlargement","glandular breast tissue in males"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Represents proliferation of true glandular breast tissue in males, felt as a firm concentric disc beneath the areola, and must be distinguished from fatty pseudogynaecomastia","Fundamentally reflects a relative excess of oestrogen action over androgen action on breast tissue","Physiological gynaecomastia is common and often benign in the newborn, at puberty, and in older age","Drugs are a frequent cause, including anti-androgens, some antihypertensives, certain psychotropics, and recreational substances","Pathological causes to consider include hypogonadism, hyperthyroidism, chronic liver disease, chronic kidney disease, and oestrogen- or hCG-secreting tumours","Rapid onset, a hard or eccentric mass, fixation, skin change, or nipple discharge should prompt evaluation for breast carcinoma","Recent-onset symptomatic disease may respond to treating the cause, whereas long-standing fibrotic tissue is less likely to regress","A focused history, drug review, testicular examination, and targeted hormonal tests guide investigation rather than testing everyone exhaustively"],"pathophysiology":"Gynaecomastia is benign proliferation of the glandular component of the male breast driven by an imbalance between stimulatory oestrogen and inhibitory androgen effects on breast epithelium. This imbalance can arise from decreased androgen production or action (hypogonadism, androgen resistance), increased oestrogen production (testicular or adrenal tumours, hCG-secreting tumours, aromatisation in obesity), or altered clearance and binding of hormones (liver disease, drugs affecting SHBG or steroid metabolism). Physiological forms in the neonate, at puberty, and with ageing reflect transient shifts in this hormonal balance. Over time, early ductal proliferation gives way to fibrosis, which is less responsive to medical treatment.","additionalDifferentials":["Pseudogynaecomastia (adipose tissue without glandular proliferation)","Male breast carcinoma","Lipoma or other benign chest wall mass","Physiological pubertal or senile gynaecomastia","Drug-induced breast enlargement","Systemic disease such as cirrhosis, renal failure, or thyrotoxicosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful breast and testicular examination to characterise the tissue and seek a testicular mass","Review of prescribed, over-the-counter, and recreational drugs","Testosterone, LH, and oestradiol to assess the androgen-oestrogen balance","hCG measurement to detect germ cell or trophoblastic tumours","Thyroid, liver, and renal function tests","Breast imaging or biopsy when carcinoma is suspected, and testicular ultrasound if a lesion is felt or hormones suggest a tumour"],"redFlags":["A hard, fixed, or eccentric mass with skin dimpling or nipple discharge suggesting malignancy","Rapidly enlarging breast tissue with elevated hCG indicating a hormone-secreting tumour","A palpable testicular mass on examination","Associated systemic features of significant liver, kidney, or thyroid disease"],"prognosis":"Physiological and drug-related gynaecomastia often resolves once the trigger is removed or with time, particularly in early stages; long-standing fibrotic tissue tends to persist and may require surgical treatment, and prognosis in secondary cases depends on the underlying disorder.","pitfalls":["Mistaking fatty pseudogynaecomastia for true glandular tissue and over-investigating","Failing to examine the testes and missing a hormone-secreting tumour","Overlooking an offending drug as the simplest reversible cause","Dismissing a suspicious unilateral mass rather than excluding male breast cancer"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"haemophilia":{"name":"Haemophilia","system":"Haematological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["haemophilia A","haemophilia B","factor VIII deficiency","factor IX deficiency","Christmas disease","hemophilia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An X-linked recessive bleeding disorder affecting mainly males; haemophilia A results from factor VIII deficiency and haemophilia B from factor IX deficiency.","The two types are clinically indistinguishable and are separated by specific factor assays.","Disease severity correlates with residual factor activity: severe disease often presents with spontaneous bleeding, while milder forms bleed mainly after trauma or surgery.","Hallmark manifestations are deep bleeding into joints (haemarthrosis) and muscles rather than mucocutaneous bleeding.","Screening typically shows a prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) with a normal prothrombin time and platelet count.","Recurrent joint bleeds lead to chronic synovitis and progressive haemophilic arthropathy if not prevented.","Management centres on replacing the deficient clotting factor, with prophylactic regimens preventing bleeds in severe disease and on-demand treatment for acute bleeds.","Development of inhibitory antibodies against the replaced factor is a major complication that reduces treatment efficacy.","Intracranial haemorrhage is a life-threatening event that warrants immediate factor replacement, and newer therapies such as non-factor agents and gene therapy are changing management."],"pathophysiology":"Deficiency of factor VIII or factor IX impairs the intrinsic pathway of the coagulation cascade, reducing generation of activated factor X and thereby the thrombin burst required for a stable fibrin clot. Primary haemostasis (platelet plug formation) is intact, so initial bleeding may stop, but the fibrin clot is inadequate and delayed or recurrent bleeding follows, characteristically into joints and muscles. Repeated haemarthroses cause iron deposition, synovial hypertrophy, and cartilage destruction leading to chronic arthropathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Von Willebrand disease","Other clotting factor deficiencies (e.g. factor XI)","Vitamin K deficiency or liver disease","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Platelet function disorders or thrombocytopenia","Non-accidental injury when bruising pattern is unexplained"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["aPTT, prothrombin time, and platelet count as initial screen","Specific factor VIII and factor IX activity assays","Mixing study to demonstrate factor deficiency versus inhibitor","Inhibitor (Bethesda) assay when replacement therapy is failing","Von Willebrand factor studies to exclude von Willebrand disease","Genetic testing for carrier detection and prenatal diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Suspected intracranial haemorrhage (headache, vomiting, altered consciousness, focal signs)","Bleeding into the neck, throat, or retroperitoneum threatening the airway or causing large occult loss","Compartment syndrome from muscle bleeding","Poor response to factor replacement suggesting an inhibitor","Significant bleeding after trauma or before surgery without prophylaxis"],"prognosis":"With modern factor replacement and prophylaxis, life expectancy and quality of life approach normal; outcomes worsen with inhibitor development, chronic arthropathy, and delayed treatment of major bleeds.","pitfalls":["Delaying factor replacement while awaiting imaging in suspected serious bleeding","Attributing bleeding solely to trauma and missing an underlying disorder","Using aspirin or NSAIDs and intramuscular injections that increase bleeding risk","Overlooking inhibitor development when a previously effective factor dose stops working"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"haemophilus_and_moraxella_infections":{"name":"Haemophilus and Moraxella Infections","system":"Respiratory / Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Haemophilus influenzae infection","Hib disease","Nontypeable Haemophilus influenzae","Haemophilus ducreyi (chancroid)","Moraxella catarrhalis infection"],"chapter":162,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1260","clinicalPearls":["H. influenzae is a small, pleomorphic gram-negative coccobacillus that often stains only faintly with safranin in CSF and sputum, so it is easily overlooked on Gram stain.","Aerobic growth of H. influenzae requires both hemin (X factor) and nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (V factor) and needs preheated blood, so it grows on chocolate agar but not on ordinary (unheated) blood agar.","Six capsular serotypes (a-f) exist plus unencapsulated nontypeable strains; type b (Hib) and nontypeable strains are the most clinically important, with Hib being invasive (hematogenous spread) and nontypeable being primarily mucosal pathogens.","Maternally acquired anticapsular (anti-PRP) antibody declines from birth to about 6 months; the antibody nadir corresponds to the peak age of type b disease, and systemic Hib disease is unusual after age 6 owing to acquired protective antibody.","Hib meningitis primarily affects children under 2 years; subdural effusion is the most common complication and should be suspected when seizures, hemiparesis, or persistent obtundation follow 2-3 days of appropriate therapy.","Hib epiglottitis is a life-threatening cellulitis of the epiglottis and supraglottic tissues occurring in an older age group (2-7 years) than other Hib infections; in countries with childhood Hib vaccination it now occurs mainly in adults.","Nontypeable H. influenzae is the most common bacterial cause of COPD exacerbations and, in recent years, has caused community-acquired pneumonia more frequently than S. pneumoniae.","Nontypeable H. influenzae is one of the three most common causes of childhood otitis media (with S. pneumoniae and M. catarrhalis); clinical clues include recurrent episodes, treatment failure, concomitant conjunctivitis, and bilateral disease.","Invasive nontypeable strains causing neonatal/puerperal sepsis after colonizing the female genital tract have been provisionally named Haemophilus quentini (related to but distinct from H. haemolyticus).","Moraxella catarrhalis is an unencapsulated gram-negative diplococcus of the respiratory tract; its colonies can be slid intact across the agar surface (the hockey puck sign) and turn pink after 48 h, distinguishing them from commensal Neisseria.","M. catarrhalis is the second most common bacterial cause of COPD exacerbations after H. influenzae and accounts for roughly 15-20 percent of acute otitis media cases by middle-ear culture (30-50 percent by molecular detection).","Haemophilus ducreyi causes chancroid, a painful genital ulcer disease; prior chancroid does not confer protection, and H. ducreyi is also an important cause of non-sexually transmitted cutaneous ulcers in children in the South Pacific and Africa."],"pathophysiology":"Hib strains produce systemic disease by invading the respiratory mucosa and spreading hematogenously to distant sites such as meninges, bones, and joints; the type b polysaccharide capsule (ribosyl-ribitol phosphate) is a key virulence factor that resists opsonization. Nontypeable strains lack a capsule and cause disease by local mucosal invasion (e.g., otitis media via the eustachian tube, lower respiratory infection in COPD), persisting in airways both extracellularly and intracellularly and forming biofilms in the middle ear that resist host clearance and antibiotics; they vary expression of virulence genes by slipped-strand mispairing and phase-variable DNA methylases. M. catarrhalis colonizes the upper airway and infects by contiguous spread, often following a viral upper respiratory infection (e.g., rhinovirus promotes adhesion); it uses multiple adhesins, invades several cell types with an intracellular lymphoid reservoir, and sheds outer-membrane vesicles that deliver beta-lactamase and induce inflammation, potentially protecting co-pathogens such as group A streptococci and H. influenzae. H. ducreyi infects through an epithelial break during sexual contact, producing ulcers with predominantly T-cell infiltrate.","additionalDifferentials":["Pneumococcal pneumonia and other bacterial pneumonias (clinically indistinguishable from H. influenzae pneumonia)","Bacterial meningitis due to other pathogens","Acute otitis media due to S. pneumoniae or M. catarrhalis","For chancroid: primary syphilis, secondary syphilis (condyloma latum), genital herpes, donovanosis, lymphogranuloma venereum, and folliculitis","For H. ducreyi cutaneous ulcers: yaws (Treponema pallidum subsp. pertenue)"],"infectionMimics":["Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumonia, otitis media, sinusitis, meningitis)","Moraxella catarrhalis vs commensal Neisseria species on respiratory culture","Haemophilus haemolyticus, a respiratory commensal with identical growth requirements that can be mistaken for H. influenzae","Herpes simplex virus and Treponema pallidum in genital ulcer disease"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Culture from a normally sterile site (blood, CSF, joint, pleural, pericardial fluid, or subdural effusion) is the most reliable means of diagnosing invasive H. influenzae and is needed for susceptibility testing","Gram stain of CSF showing gram-negative coccobacilli supports Hib meningitis; predominant gram-negative coccobacilli among abundant neutrophils in sputum suggests nontypeable H. influenzae pneumonia","PCR and other DNA-based detection methods, now common in clinical labs","Genotypic markers (16S rRNA, superoxide dismutase, outer-membrane protein P6, protein D, fuculose kinase) and whole-genome sequencing to distinguish H. influenzae from H. haemolyticus","Pneumatic otoscopy detects middle-ear fluid; tympanocentesis with culture gives etiologic diagnosis of otitis media but is not routine","For chancroid: culture of H. ducreyi (requires selective/supplemented media) from the lesion or node aspirate, and PCR; dark-field exam and syphilis serology to exclude T. pallidum and HSV testing of exudate"],"redFlags":["Sore throat and fever rapidly progressing to dysphagia, drooling, and airway obstruction (epiglottitis) - a medical emergency requiring airway maintenance","Fever with altered CNS function and nuchal rigidity (which may be absent) in a young child suggesting Hib meningitis","Seizures, hemiparesis, or persistent obtundation after 2-3 days of appropriate antibiotics, suggesting subdural effusion","Underlying immunosuppression, HIV, cardiopulmonary disease, alcoholism, or cancer in a patient with H. influenzae bacteremia"],"prognosis":"The overall mortality of Hib meningitis is about 5 percent with a high morbidity rate; about 6 percent of survivors have permanent sensorineural hearing loss, roughly one-fourth have a significant disability, and up to half have some neurologic sequela such as partial hearing loss or delayed language development when sought carefully. Hib epiglottitis is life-threatening due to risk of acute upper-airway obstruction. Glucocorticoids reduce the incidence of neurologic sequelae in Hib meningitis. Nontypeable strains tend to cause recurrent otitis media and recurrent COPD exacerbations because immunity is strain-specific. M. catarrhalis otitis media and exacerbations are clinically milder (less fever, less red bulging tympanic membrane) than pneumococcal disease, and invasive M. catarrhalis infection is rare. Repeated H. ducreyi infections can occur because infection confers no protection; in HIV-positive patients chancroid healing may be slow and require longer therapy.","pitfalls":["H. influenzae may be missed on Gram stain because it stains only faintly with safranin in CSF and sputum.","Ordinary (unheated) blood agar fails to grow H. influenzae, which can lead to false-negative cultures if chocolate agar is not used.","Distinguishing H. influenzae from the commensal H. haemolyticus is difficult because they share growth requirements and many H. haemolyticus isolates are now nonhemolytic.","M. catarrhalis has historically been overlooked as a pathogen because its colonies resemble those of commensal Neisseria species on respiratory cultures.","Approximately 30-40 percent of nontypeable H. influenzae produce beta-lactamase (conferring ampicillin/amoxicillin resistance), and a second mechanism, altered penicillin-binding proteins (beta-lactamase-negative ampicillin-resistant strains), is increasing in Europe and common in Japan.","Over 90 percent of M. catarrhalis strains now produce beta-lactamase and are resistant to amoxicillin; macrolide and fluoroquinolone resistance is emerging, notably in Asia.","Clinical diagnosis of chancroid is often inaccurate, so laboratory confirmation should be attempted; in HIV-positive patients apparent treatment failure may reflect HSV co-infection.","Isolation of M. catarrhalis from expectorated sputum during an exacerbation is suggestive but not diagnostic of it as the cause."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1260","Harrison 22e p.1261","Harrison 22e p.1262","Harrison 22e p.1263","Harrison 22e p.1264","Harrison 22e p.1265"],"crossLinks":[]},"hand_foot_mouth_disease":{"name":"Hand, foot and mouth disease","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HFMD","coxsackievirus exanthem","enteroviral vesicular stomatitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common, usually self-limiting enteroviral illness of young children caused most often by Coxsackievirus A16 and Enterovirus 71","Characterised by fever with painful oral vesicles/ulcers and a vesicular rash on the hands, feet and often the buttocks","Spreads readily by faecal-oral and respiratory routes and by contact with vesicle fluid, causing outbreaks in creches and schools","Enterovirus 71 in particular can cause severe neurological complications such as brainstem encephalitis, aseptic meningitis and acute flaccid paralysis","Management is supportive, focused on hydration and pain relief for painful oral lesions; antibiotics have no role","Poor oral intake from painful mouth ulcers is the commonest reason for medical attention and can lead to dehydration in small children","Diagnosis is usually clinical, with PCR or viral culture reserved for severe or atypical cases","Good hand hygiene and exclusion of unwell children help limit institutional spread"],"pathophysiology":"Non-polio enteroviruses (notably Coxsackievirus A16 and Enterovirus 71) enter via the gastrointestinal or respiratory tract, replicate in lymphoid tissue of the pharynx and gut, and produce a viraemia that seeds the skin and oral mucosa, giving the characteristic vesicular enanthem and exanthem. Enterovirus 71 has neurotropic potential and can invade the central nervous system, particularly the brainstem, producing the severe neurological syndromes and, rarely, neurogenic pulmonary oedema and cardiorespiratory collapse.","additionalDifferentials":["Herpetic gingivostomatitis","Herpangina","Varicella (chickenpox)","Aphthous stomatitis","Erythema multiforme","Contact or vesicular dermatitis"],"infectionMimics":["Stevens-Johnson syndrome / drug eruption","Kawasaki disease in a febrile child with mucocutaneous findings","Immunobullous disorders"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis in typical cases without laboratory testing","Enterovirus PCR from throat, vesicle or stool samples in severe or atypical disease","CSF examination if meningitis or encephalitis is suspected","Assessment of hydration status, especially in young children"],"redFlags":["Lethargy, persistent vomiting, ataxia or myoclonic jerks suggesting Enterovirus 71 neurological involvement","Signs of severe dehydration from inability to drink","Respiratory distress or circulatory collapse indicating brainstem/cardiopulmonary complications","Seizures or rapidly declining consciousness"],"prognosis":"The overwhelming majority of cases resolve within a week to ten days without sequelae. A small subset, particularly Enterovirus 71 infections in young children, develop serious neurological or cardiopulmonary complications that can be fatal or leave lasting deficits.","pitfalls":["Prescribing antibiotics for what is a self-limiting viral illness","Underestimating dehydration risk from painful oral ulcers in toddlers","Missing early neurological warning signs of Enterovirus 71 disease","Confusing severe or atypical rashes with drug reactions or varicella"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"hand_infections":{"name":"Hand infections","system":"Plastic / Reconstructive","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hand infection","felon","paronychia","flexor tenosynovitis","web space abscess"],"chapter":null,"source":"Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery / standard references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Common hand infections include paronychia (nail fold), felon (pulp space of the fingertip), web space and deep palmar space abscesses, and pyogenic flexor tenosynovitis, each with characteristic anatomy","Staphylococcus aureus and streptococci are the usual pathogens, but bite wounds, aquatic exposure, and immunocompromise broaden the range to include anaerobes and atypical organisms","Pyogenic flexor tenosynovitis is a surgical emergency recognised by Kanavel's signs: fusiform swelling of the finger, semiflexed resting posture, tenderness along the flexor sheath, and pain on passive extension","Human bite injuries over the knuckle (fight bite) frequently penetrate the joint and extensor mechanism and are notorious for serious deep infection if underestimated","Early localised infections may settle with elevation, splinting, and antibiotics, but established abscesses and closed-space infections require prompt incision and drainage","A felon is a closed-space infection of the fingertip pulp that can raise pressure enough to threaten the tissue and requires timely drainage","Delayed or inadequate treatment of deep hand infection risks tendon necrosis, stiffness, spread along fascial planes, and permanent loss of function","Elevation, splinting in a safe position, and early mobilisation once infection is controlled help preserve hand function"],"pathophysiology":"Hand infections usually follow inoculation of bacteria through a break in the skin from trauma, bites, splinters, or nail-care injury. The hand's compartmentalised anatomy - closed pulp spaces, synovial flexor sheaths, web spaces, and deep palmar spaces - confines infection under pressure and allows it to track along defined planes. Rising pressure within closed spaces impairs perfusion and can cause tissue and tendon necrosis. In pyogenic flexor tenosynovitis, pus within the closed synovial sheath compromises the gliding surface and blood supply of the flexor tendon, leading to adhesions and stiffness if not decompressed. Bite wounds introduce mixed oral flora deep into tissues and joints.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute gout or pseudogout of the hand","Herpetic whitlow (viral, should not be incised)","Inflammatory or crystal arthropathy","Foreign body reaction or retained foreign body","Acute calcific tendinitis","Complex regional pain syndrome or reactive swelling"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Inflammatory markers and white cell count to gauge severity","Wound and intraoperative cultures, including for anaerobes and atypical organisms when exposure suggests","Plain radiographs to detect foreign body, gas, retained tooth fragment, or osteomyelitis","Blood cultures if systemic sepsis is present","Ultrasound to identify collections or fluid within the flexor sheath","Joint aspiration and crystal analysis when gout or septic arthritis is considered"],"redFlags":["Pyogenic flexor tenosynovitis with Kanavel's signs requiring urgent surgical decompression","Fight bite over a knuckle with possible joint or extensor tendon penetration","Rapidly spreading infection, crepitus, or systemic sepsis suggesting necrotising infection","Deep palmar space abscess or infection tracking proximally along fascial planes","Signs of tissue necrosis or compartment involvement"],"prognosis":"Superficial infections such as early paronychia treated promptly usually resolve fully. Deep and closed-space infections carry a higher risk of stiffness, contracture, and functional loss, and outcome is closely tied to how quickly drainage and appropriate antibiotics are provided. Untreated flexor sheath and deep space infections can lead to tendon necrosis, amputation, or long-term disability.","pitfalls":["Underestimating a fight bite and closing or under-treating a wound that has penetrated the joint","Incising a herpetic whitlow, which worsens the viral infection","Delaying surgical drainage of pyogenic flexor tenosynovitis while relying on antibiotics alone","Missing a retained foreign body as the source of persistent infection","Failing to elevate and splint, leading to persistent oedema and stiffness","Overlooking gout or crystal arthropathy masquerading as infection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"head_and_neck_cancer":{"name":"Head and Neck Cancer","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["head and neck squamous cell carcinoma","HNSCC"],"chapter":82,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.610-613","clinicalPearls":["Most head and neck cancers are squamous cell carcinomas arising from the mucosal surfaces of the paranasal sinuses, oral cavity, nasopharynx, oropharynx, hypopharynx, and larynx (Harrison 22e p.610).","Alcohol and tobacco are the most significant environmental risk factors and act synergistically when used together; smokeless tobacco is an etiologic agent for oral cancers (Harrison 22e p.610).","In Western countries, HPV (predominantly HPV-16) causes over 50% of oropharyngeal cancers (tonsillar bed and base of tongue); these occur in younger patients and carry a better prognosis, especially in nonsmokers (Harrison 22e p.610-611).","EBV infection is frequently associated with nasopharyngeal cancer, especially in endemic areas (East Asia, some Mediterranean countries); EBV antibody titers can be used to screen high-risk populations (Harrison 22e p.610).","HPV testing should be performed at diagnosis for all patients with oropharyngeal tumors; HPV-related disease has a more favorable AJCC 8th-edition staging (advanced nodal stage can still be equivalent to overall early stage) (Harrison 22e p.611-613).","Panendoscopy under anesthesia (laryngoscopy, esophagoscopy, bronchoscopy) with multiple directed biopsies is the definitive staging procedure to establish diagnosis, define extent, and identify second primaries (Harrison 22e p.611).","When a head and neck lesion treated initially with antibiotics does not resolve in a short period, further workup is indicated; simply continuing antibiotics may lose the chance for early diagnosis of a malignancy (Harrison 22e p.611).","For an enlarged cervical node with no visible primary, diagnose by fine-needle aspiration or lymph node excision; if squamous cell carcinoma, perform panendoscopy with directed biopsies of nasopharynx, tonsil, tongue base, and pyriform sinus (Harrison 22e p.611-612).","Most parotid tumors are benign, but about half of submandibular and sublingual tumors and most minor salivary gland tumors are malignant; adenoid cystic carcinoma tends to recur along nerve tracks and metastasize as late as 10-20 years (Harrison 22e p.610-611, 613)."],"pathophysiology":"Squamous cell carcinomas arise from the mucosal surfaces of the upper aerodigestive tract. The entire pharyngeal mucosa is exposed to alcohol- and tobacco-related carcinogens, producing field cancerization with risk of premalignant lesions (erythroplakia, leukoplakia) progressing through hyperplasia, dysplasia, and carcinoma in situ to invasive carcinoma. In HPV-negative tumors, p53 mutations are frequent along with mitotic-signaling and Notch pathway alterations and dysregulated cell-cycle control. HPV oncogenes drive carcinogenesis by directly inhibiting the p53 and RB tumor-suppressor genes; HPV-positive tumors carry a distinct mutational signature with frequent PI3K pathway alteration. EGFR is frequently overexpressed and correlates with poor prognosis. EBV is associated with undifferentiated nasopharyngeal carcinoma (lymphoepithelioma) (Harrison 22e p.610-611).","additionalDifferentials":["Carcinoma of unknown primary presenting as cervical adenopathy without an identifiable mucosal lesion","Lymphoma presenting as cervical adenopathy","Salivary gland tumors (mucoepidermoid, adenoid cystic carcinoma, adenocarcinoma)","Sarcoma, including radiation therapy-induced sarcoma after prior radiotherapy","Metastasis to supraclavicular nodes from a primary site in the chest or abdomen"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Premalignant mucosal lesions (leukoplakia, erythroplakia) representing hyperplasia, dysplasia, or carcinoma in situ"],"additionalInvestigations":["Thorough otolaryngologic examination including inspection of all visible mucosal surfaces and palpation of the floor of mouth, tongue, and neck","CT or MRI of the head and neck to define disease extent (comparably accurate, 65-90%, for cervical nodal metastases)","CT of chest and upper abdomen for patients with lymph node involvement to screen for distant metastases; chest CT also screens for second lung primary in heavy smokers","PET scan to identify or exclude distant metastases and assess treatment response","Endoscopic examination under anesthesia (panendoscopy) with multiple biopsies","Fine-needle aspiration or excisional lymph node biopsy when no primary is visible","HPV testing of oropharyngeal tumors at diagnosis; EBV antibody titers / plasma EBV DNA for nasopharyngeal cancer screening","TNM staging per AJCC (varies by anatomic subsite)"],"redFlags":["Persistent hoarseness requiring referral for indirect laryngoscopy and/or radiographic studies (possible early laryngeal cancer)","Non-healing ulcer of the oral cavity","Persistent neck mass / cervical lymphadenopathy, especially a solitary, hard, nontender node in an older chronic tobacco user","Dysphagia, odynophagia, or decreased tongue mobility","Sore throat and/or otalgia, or a head/neck lesion that does not resolve after antibiotics","Unilateral serous otitis media, nasal obstruction, or epistaxis (nasopharyngeal cancer); cranial neuropathies from skull base involvement","Head and neck symptoms persisting longer than 2-4 weeks"],"prognosis":"Poorly differentiated tumors have a worse prognosis than well-differentiated tumors. Overt distant metastases are present in <10% at diagnosis. HPV-related oropharyngeal cancers consistently carry a better prognosis (reflected in a separate, more favorable AJCC 8th-edition staging system), especially in nonsmokers. For localized disease (about one-third of patients), overall 5-year survival is 60-90%, with most recurrences occurring within the first 2 years and usually local. For locally/regionally advanced disease (>50% of patients at presentation), 5-year survival exceeds 50% in many trials (partly due to the increasing fraction of HPV-related tumors); advanced nasopharyngeal cancer treated with neoadjuvant chemotherapy followed by concomitant chemoradiotherapy has achieved 5-year survival exceeding 80% in a Southeast Asian study. Patients cured of early-stage tobacco/alcohol-related disease are at greater risk of dying from a second malignancy than from recurrence of the primary (Harrison 22e p.611-613).","pitfalls":["Continuing antibiotic treatment for a non-resolving head and neck lesion rather than pursuing workup may delay diagnosis of malignancy (Harrison 22e p.611).","Fine-needle aspiration should not be the first diagnostic procedure for a suspicious cervical node when a head and neck primary is suspected; perform a careful ENT exam and biopsy any suspicious mucosal lesion first (Harrison 22e p.4012-type cross-reference, p.611).","EGFR overexpression does not reliably predict response to EGFR inhibitors (active in only ~10-15% as single agents); substituting cetuximab for cisplatin in HPV-related cancers has shown inferior survival (Harrison 22e p.611, 613).","Retinoid supplements (e.g., cis-retinoic acid) have not been shown to prevent head and neck (or lung) cancer and may increase risk in active smokers (Harrison 22e p.610).","About 50% of patients develop hypothyroidism after treatment; thyroid function should be monitored (Harrison 22e p.613)."],"severityClassification":"Clinically categorized into three groups: (1) localized disease (T1-T2 / stage I-II without nodal or distant spread), (2) locally or regionally advanced disease (large primary and/or cervical nodal metastases, lymph-node positive), and (3) recurrent and/or metastatic disease below the neck. Staged by the AJCC TNM system, which varies by anatomic subsite: primary tumors T1-T3 by increasing size with T4 representing invasion of adjacent structures (bone, muscle, root of tongue), and nodes staged by size, number, and laterality. A separate, more favorable staging system applies to HPV-related oropharyngeal cancer in the AJCC 8th edition (Harrison 22e p.611-613).","references":["Harrison 22e p.610-613"],"crossLinks":[]},"heart_failure_management":{"name":"Heart Failure: Management","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Congestive heart failure","CHF","HFrEF","HFpEF","HFmrEF","Chronic heart failure","Acute decompensated heart failure"],"chapter":265,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1987","clinicalPearls":["Management is driven by clinical phenotype and by left ventricular ejection fraction: HFrEF (LVEF <=40%), HFmrEF (41-49%), and HFpEF (>=50%); the older terms systolic and diastolic heart failure have been abandoned because most patients have both systolic and diastolic abnormalities (Harrison 22e p.1987, 1978).","The disease is staged along the ACC/AHA framework: stage A (at risk), stage B (asymptomatic structural/ventricular dysfunction), stage C (symptomatic HF), and stage D (advanced/refractory HF); approach to therapy escalates across these stages (Harrison 22e p.1987).","Stage B asymptomatic ventricular dysfunction is treated to facilitate ventricular recovery and prevent progression to clinical HF, whereas stage D carries a high risk of death from sudden death or end-stage pump failure and may prompt consideration of transplant, mechanical circulatory support, or palliative care (Harrison 22e p.1987).","The robust randomized-trial evidence base supporting disease-modifying therapy applies to HFrEF (LVEF <=40%); historically HFmrEF and HFpEF management was symptom-focused owing to lack of outcome-improving therapies (Harrison 22e p.1987).","Even with effective therapy, patients across all EF categories remain at risk for clinical deterioration, typically from progressive sodium and fluid retention that drives congestive symptoms and acute decompensated HF (Harrison 22e p.1987).","Trials of agents that improve outcomes in HFrEF (ACE inhibitors, ARBs, beta blockers, digoxin, mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists) have generally been unsuccessful when exported to HFpEF, with several large trials showing reduced HF hospitalizations but no all-cause mortality benefit (Harrison 22e p.1987-1988).","Management of HFpEF has centered on lifestyle modification, control of congestion, rhythm stabilization (especially atrial fibrillation), blood pressure control to guideline targets, and treatment of comorbidities such as obesity, obstructive lung disease, sleep apnea, diabetes/insulin resistance, anemia, iron deficiency, and chronic kidney disease (Harrison 22e p.1987).","A favorable signal for spironolactone in HFpEF emerged mainly from the subgroup randomized in the Americas in the TOPCAT trial, supporting only a weak guideline recommendation in selected patients at low risk for hyperkalemia and worsening renal function (Harrison 22e p.1988).","The microvascular endothelial inflammation paradigm proposes that comorbidity-driven inflammation impairs nitric oxide signaling and increases myocardial stiffness in HFpEF; trials enhancing NO bioavailability (sildenafil in RELAX, nitrates in NEAT-HFpEF, inorganic nitrite in INDIE-HFpEF) failed to improve functional capacity or quality of life (Harrison 22e p.1988-1989).","Approximately one-half of patients with HF have reduced EF and one-half have preserved or mildly reduced EF; classifying by EF matters because of differences in demographics, comorbidities, and treatment response (Harrison 22e p.1978).","Acute heart failure (formerly acute decompensated HF) refers to rapid onset or worsening of symptoms; most episodes are decompensations of chronic HF, but roughly 20% are new-onset, occurring with acute coronary syndrome, acute valvular dysfunction, hypertensive urgency, or postcardiotomy syndrome (Harrison 22e p.1976).","HF with recovered EF (HFrecEF) is a subgroup whose EF normalizes on therapy; predictors include younger age, shorter HF duration, nonischemic etiology, smaller ventricular volumes, and absence of fibrosis, and withdrawal of disease-modifying therapy can cause recurrence in up to half of patients within 6 months (Harrison 22e p.1977)."],"pathophysiology":"HFrEF is a progressive disorder in which an index event (acute myocardial infarction, chronic pressure or volume overload, inherited or congenital disease) is followed by months to years of cardiovascular remodeling. Concentric hypertrophy reduces wall stress in pressure overload, while eccentric hypertrophy (chamber dilation) occurs in volume overload; remodeling involves myocyte hypertrophy, interstitial fibrosis, altered calcium handling, fetal gene reexpression, necrosis, apoptosis, and autophagy. Maladaptive activation of the sympathetic nervous system and renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system initially supports cardiac output but ultimately produces excessive vasoconstriction, volume retention, electrolyte and renal abnormalities, baroreceptor dysfunction, direct myocardial toxicity, and arrhythmias, with antagonism of these systems shown to attenuate or reverse remodeling and reduce mortality. Counterregulatory vasodilatory hormones (ANP, BNP, prostaglandins, bradykinin, adrenomedullin, nitric oxide) are upregulated; neprilysin inactivates natriuretic peptides and bradykinin, the rationale for angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibition. Endothelin, inflammatory cytokines (TNF-alpha, IL-1beta, IL-6), and reactive oxygen species contribute to remodeling, and the cardiorenal syndrome reflects both reduced forward flow and systemic venous/abdominal congestion. HFpEF pathophysiology is less defined and involves diastolic dysfunction together with vascular stiffness, renal dysfunction, sodium avidity, comorbidity-driven microvascular inflammation, and impaired nitric oxide/cGMP/protein kinase G signaling causing myocardial stiffening (Harrison 22e p.1978-1982, 1989).","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic lung disease / COPD (dyspnea, orthopnea, episodic nocturnal breathlessness mimicking PND)","Pulmonary arterial hypertension","Neuromuscular disease causing dyspnea","Anemia / iron-deficiency anemia","Acute or chronic kidney disease with abnormal salt and water retention but normal cardiac structure/function","Noncardiac pulmonary edema (e.g., acute respiratory distress syndrome)","Venous insufficiency, lymphedema, or obesity as causes of lower extremity edema","Nephrotic syndrome and deep vein thrombosis (edema)","Hepatic cirrhosis, portal vein thrombosis, malignant carcinomatosis (ascites)","Constrictive pericarditis, pericardial effusion, superior vena cava syndrome (jugular venous distension)"],"infectionMimics":["Low-grade fever from cytokine activation in severe HF may suggest systemic infection","Pleural effusion in HF versus effusion from chronic infection or lung cancer","Cardiac asthma (wheezing/rhonchi from bronchial congestion) misattributed to a respiratory infection or COPD exacerbation"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Bronchial asthma or COPD (cardiac asthma can mimic reactive airways disease)","Biliary tract disease (right HF with hepatic congestion causes RUQ pain and abnormal liver function tests)","Cellulitis arising from chronic dependent edema versus primary skin/soft-tissue infection","Cardiac cirrhosis with ascites mimicking primary liver failure"],"additionalInvestigations":["Two-dimensional echocardiography with Doppler for chamber size, LVEF, valvular function, intracavitary thrombi, and pericardial effusion (LVEF >=50% deemed normal systolic function)","12-lead ECG for ischemia, infarction (Q waves), LVH, atrial fibrillation, QRS width and LBBB to guide resynchronization decisions","Chest x-ray for cardiomegaly (cardiothoracic ratio >0.5), pulmonary venous congestion, and pleural effusions","Natriuretic peptides (BNP / NT-proBNP) for diagnosis, severity, and prognosis; levels may be falsely low in obesity and raised by age, female sex, CKD, atrial fibrillation, PE, and pulmonary hypertension","Cardiac MRI for ventricular mass/volumes/function and tissue characterization (ischemic cardiomyopathy, myocarditis, amyloidosis, hemochromatosis)","Cardiac CT / coronary CT angiography to exclude obstructive coronary disease or evaluate pericardium and apical thrombus","Cardiopulmonary exercise testing (peak VO2, VE/VCO2 slope) for prognosis and transplant/MCS evaluation","Coronary angiography to exclude reversible ischemic cause; right heart catheterization/pulmonary artery catheter for filling pressures and cardiac output in unstable patients","Endomyocardial biopsy in selected cases (giant cell, eosinophilic, lymphocytic or sarcoid myocarditis, amyloidosis, allograft rejection)","Comprehensive metabolic panel, CBC, coagulation, urinalysis, thyroid function; iron studies for iron deficiency with or without anemia"],"redFlags":["Acute pulmonary edema with severe dyspnea and pink frothy sputum due to markedly elevated pulmonary capillary wedge pressure","Hypotension with narrow pulse pressure and a rapid, thready pulse indicating severe low-output state","Pulsus alternans and an audible S3 gallop signaling severe hemodynamic compromise and adverse prognosis","Kussmaul's sign (inspiratory rise in JVP) as a marker of severe biventricular failure and poor outcome","Marked elevation of transaminases and lactic acid suggesting cardiogenic shock with severe low output","Oliguria from severe reduction in renal blood flow indicating advanced-stage HF","Cheyne-Stokes / periodic breathing and central sleep apnea in advanced HF","Cardiac cachexia (edema-free weight loss >5% over 12 months) and frailty/sarcopenia in longstanding severe HF","Recurrent acute decompensated HF despite optimal therapy heralding advanced (stage D) refractory disease"],"prognosis":"Overall 5-year survival after a primary-care diagnosis of HF is about 50%, and 1-year mortality may reach 40% in severe HF. After an HF admission, mortality ranges from 8-14% at 30 days to 26-37% at 1 year and up to 75% at 5 years, with readmission rates of 20-25% at 60 days approaching 50% at 6 months; risk of death rises with each subsequent admission. Most deaths are cardiovascular, from progressive pump failure or sudden cardiac death. Independent predictors of adverse outcome include older age, male sex, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, coronary disease, advanced NYHA class, presence of an S3 or elevated JVP, reduced LV/RV ejection fraction, elevated wedge pressure, low cardiac index, reduced peak VO2, pulmonary hypertension, worsening renal function, hyponatremia, hyperuricemia, elevated troponin and natriuretic peptides, raised neurohormones, tachycardia, wide QRS/LBBB, atrial fibrillation, and ventricular ectopy. Black patients have higher case-fatality rates and there is regional variation (highest in the US Midwest and rural areas). HFrecEF generally carries a better prognosis than HFrEF or HFpEF. Overall outcomes are improving with guideline-directed therapy and risk-factor control (Harrison 22e p.1976-1977).","pitfalls":["Signs and symptoms of HF are neither sensitive nor specific, so a high index of suspicion is needed, especially in older patients with hypertension, diabetes, or CKD (Harrison 22e p.1985).","Right HF with hepatic congestion can elevate transaminases, alkaline phosphatase, and bilirubin and be misdiagnosed as biliary tract disease (Harrison 22e p.1984).","Cardiac asthma (wheezing and rhonchi from bronchial congestion) can be mistaken for asthma or COPD and treated inappropriately (Harrison 22e p.1983).","Rales may be absent in longstanding HF with chronically elevated wedge pressures because of increased lymphatic drainage, so their absence does not exclude congestion (Harrison 22e p.1983).","NYHA functional class correlates poorly with objective measures of cardiac structure (LV size, EF) and function (peak VO2) (Harrison 22e p.1982).","Natriuretic peptide levels may be falsely low in obesity and elevated by noncardiac factors (age, female sex, CKD) and other cardiac conditions, limiting interpretation (Harrison 22e p.1985).","Lower extremity edema may be absent in acute HF or in younger adults despite marked systemic venous hypertension; unilateral edema suggests DVT, prior trauma, or vein harvest rather than HF (Harrison 22e p.1984).","Drugs causing salt/water retention (NSAIDs) or negative inotropy (calcium channel blockers) can precipitate decompensation and should be recognized as provider-related triggers (Harrison 22e p.1983).","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists, RAAS inhibitors, and potassium supplements can cause hyperkalemia, particularly with reduced GFR, requiring monitoring (Harrison 22e p.1984-1985)."],"severityClassification":"Two complementary frameworks are used. The NYHA functional classification grades symptom-limiting activity: class I (no limitation), class II (mild; symptoms with ordinary activity), class III (moderate; symptoms with less-than-ordinary activity), and class IV (severe; symptoms at rest worsening with any activity). The ACC/AHA staging is progressive and irreversible: stage A (at risk, no structural disease or symptoms), stage B (structural disease/asymptomatic ventricular dysfunction without symptoms), stage C (structural disease with current or prior symptoms), and stage D (advanced/refractory HF requiring specialized interventions). Patients are additionally phenotyped by ejection fraction: HFrEF (LVEF <=40%), HFmrEF (41-49%), and HFpEF (>=50%) (Harrison 22e p.1982-1983, 1987).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1987","Harrison 22e p.1988","Harrison 22e p.1989","Harrison 22e p.1976","Harrison 22e p.1977","Harrison 22e p.1978","Harrison 22e p.1982","Harrison 22e p.1983","Harrison 22e p.1984","Harrison 22e p.1985"],"crossLinks":[]},"heart_failure_pathophysiology_and_diagnosi":{"name":"Heart Failure: Pathophysiology and Diagnosis","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HF","Congestive heart failure","CHF","HFrEF","HFmrEF","HFpEF","Cardiac failure"],"chapter":264,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1975","clinicalPearls":["Heart failure is a clinical syndrome of symptoms and signs arising from any structural or functional impairment of ventricular filling or ejection; it is the common final pathway for most chronic cardiovascular diseases including hypertension, coronary artery disease, and valvular disease (p.1975).","Roughly half of patients with HF have reduced ejection fraction (EF less than or equal to 40%, HFrEF) and about half have preserved (greater than or equal to 50%, HFpEF) or mildly reduced (41-49%, HFmrEF) EF; because most patients have both systolic and diastolic abnormalities, the older terms 'systolic' and 'diastolic' heart failure have been abandoned (p.1977).","An EF that recovers to normal on guideline-directed therapy defines HF with recovered EF (HFrecEF); predictors include younger age, shorter HF duration, nonischemic etiology, smaller ventricular volumes, and absence of fibrosis, and withdrawal of therapy can cause symptom and EF recurrence in up to half of patients within 6 months (p.1977).","Dyspnea progresses in severity as exertional dyspnea, then orthopnea, then paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, then dyspnea at rest; bendopnea (breathlessness when bending forward) reflects elevated filling pressure, especially with low cardiac output (p.1982).","Orthopnea typically occurs within 1-2 minutes of lying down, whereas paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea occurs after prolonged recumbency and may require 30 minutes or more upright for relief; cardiac asthma (wheezing) can mimic primary lung disease (p.1982).","An S3 gallop indicates volume overload, suggests severe hemodynamic compromise, and carries negative prognostic significance, whereas an S4 is nonspecific and often reflects hypertensive HFpEF (p.1984).","Pulmonary rales may be ABSENT in longstanding HF despite chronically elevated wedge pressures because increased lymphatic drainage prevents alveolar spillage, so a clear chest does not exclude congestion (p.1983, p.1985).","Natriuretic peptides (BNP, NT-proBNP) rise with wall stress and aid diagnosis when uncertain, but levels are falsely LOW in obesity and falsely elevated by age, female sex, chronic kidney disease, atrial fibrillation, pulmonary embolism, and pulmonary arterial hypertension (p.1985).","Cardiorenal syndrome is driven not only by reduced forward cardiac output but importantly by systemic and abdominal venous congestion; relieving congestion (diuretics or ultrafiltration) often improves renal function (p.1981).","High-output HF is rare and usually requires underlying structural heart disease; consider it with severe anemia (often hemoglobin less than or equal to 8 g/dL), arteriovenous fistula, severe obesity, cirrhosis, thyrotoxicosis, end-stage renal disease, or beriberi (p.1982).","Iron deficiency (with or without anemia) is common in chronic HF; IV iron (not oral) improves symptoms, exercise capacity, and reduces hospitalizations, though survival benefit is uncertain (p.1985).","Q waves with reduced QRS voltage (pseudo-infarct pattern) suggest infiltrative or restrictive cardiomyopathy such as amyloid, and conduction system disease should raise suspicion for cardiac sarcoid or Chagas in the right setting (p.1985)."],"pathophysiology":"HFrEF is a progressive disease in which an index event (acute MI, chronic pressure/volume overload, genetic or congenital disease) triggers months to years of cardiovascular remodeling. Pressure overload produces concentric hypertrophy and volume overload produces eccentric hypertrophy; both involve myocyte hypertrophy, interstitial fibrosis, altered calcium-handling and cytoskeletal proteins, reexpression of fetal genes, and myocyte necrosis, apoptosis, and autophagy, completing a vicious cycle of pump dysfunction and rising wall stress. Compensatory neurohormonal activation of the sympathetic nervous system and renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system initially supports output but becomes maladaptive, causing vasoconstriction, sodium and water retention, baroreceptor dysfunction, direct myocardial toxicity, arrhythmia, and adverse remodeling of heart, vessels, and kidneys. Counterregulatory vasodilators (ANP, BNP, prostaglandins, bradykinin, adrenomedullin, nitric oxide) are upregulated and act via guanylate cyclase to promote vasodilation and natriuresis; bradykinin and natriuretic peptides are degraded by neprilysin. Endothelin, inflammatory cytokines (TNF-alpha, IL-1 beta, IL-6), and reactive oxygen species contribute to remodeling. HFpEF mechanisms are less defined but extend beyond diastolic dysfunction to vascular stiffness, renal dysfunction, sodium avidity, adiposity-related inflammation, oxidative/nitrosative stress, and insulin resistance. Electrical dyssynchrony (LBBB/IVCD), secondary mitral and tricuspid regurgitation, cardiorenal and splanchnic/hepatic congestion, gut microbiome alterations with endotoxemia, and high-output states further drive progression.","additionalDifferentials":["HFpEF versus noncardiac causes of dyspnea and fluid retention (the diagnosis of HFpEF requires ruling these out)","Asthma or COPD (wheezing/rhonchi from bronchial congestion can mimic these)","Biliary tract disease (right HF hepatic congestion causes RUQ pain, nausea, abnormal liver tests)","Pneumonia and primary lung disease (chest x-ray helps distinguish)","Constrictive pericarditis or restrictive cardiomyopathy (Kussmaul's sign and massive ascites)","Cardiogenic versus noncardiogenic pulmonary edema (may require invasive hemodynamics)"],"infectionMimics":["Chagas cardiomyopathy","HIV-associated cardiomyopathy","Viral, giant cell, or sarcoid myocarditis presenting with new HF","Sepsis as a precipitant or high-output contributor"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pulmonary embolism (precipitant and elevates BNP)","Pulmonary arterial hypertension and right HF","Lymphedema (nonpitting edema unresponsive to diuretics)","Deep venous thrombosis (unilateral leg edema)","Cirrhosis or primary liver failure (ascites)","Cardiac amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, hemochromatosis (infiltrative mimics)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Comprehensive metabolic panel, CBC, coagulation studies, urinalysis; selected testing for diabetes, lipids, thyroid function","12-lead ECG (LVH, prior infarction, conduction disease, atrial fibrillation, QRS width/LBBB for resynchronization eligibility)","Chest x-ray (cardiothoracic ratio greater than 0.5, upper-zone venous redistribution, interlobular septal thickening, alveolar edema, pleural effusions)","Two-dimensional echocardiography with Doppler; speckle-tracking strain imaging for prognosis","Natriuretic peptides (BNP, NT-proBNP) for diagnosis, severity, and prognosis","Cardiac MRI for etiology and tissue characterization; cardiac CT/coronary CT angiography when MRI contraindicated; cardiac PET for ischemia/viability and sarcoid inflammation","Coronary angiography to exclude reversible ischemic cause","Cardiopulmonary exercise testing (peak VO2, VE/VCO2 slope) for transplant/MCS evaluation and unexplained dyspnea","Invasive hemodynamics (pulmonary artery catheter; combined right/left heart catheterization to distinguish restriction from constriction)","Endomyocardial biopsy in selected cases (myocarditis, amyloidosis, chemotherapy/immunotherapy injury, transplant rejection)","Targeted etiologic screening: hemochromatosis, amyloidosis, sarcoidosis; serum free light chains/SPEP, ferritin, ceruloplasmin, ANA, RF, hepatitis C, HIV; three-generation family history and genetic testing for inherited cardiomyopathy"],"redFlags":["Acute pulmonary edema with severe dyspnea and pink frothy sputum (marked wedge pressure elevation)","Hypotension with narrow pulse pressure and rapid thready pulse, cool extremities, peripheral cyanosis (low-output/cardiogenic shock)","Pulsus alternans (severely reduced LV contraction)","Kussmaul's sign (inspiratory rise in JVP) signaling severe biventricular HF and poor outcome","Oliguria from severely reduced renal blood flow (advanced-stage HF)","Cheyne-Stokes/periodic breathing in advanced HF","Marked elevation of transaminases and lactic acid suggesting cardiogenic shock","Cardiac cachexia (unintentional edema-free weight loss greater than 5% over 12 months) with muscle wasting and frailty","Hyponatremia at hospitalization (negative prognostic marker)","New S3 gallop"],"prognosis":"Overall 5-year survival after HF diagnosis in primary care is about 50%, and 1-year mortality may reach 40% in severe HF. Post-admission mortality ranges from 8-14% at 30 days to 26-37% at 1 year and up to 75% at 5 years, with readmission of 20-25% at 60 days and nearly 50% at 6 months; risk of death rises with each subsequent admission. Most deaths are cardiovascular, from progressive HF or sudden cardiac death. Black patients have higher case-fatality, men higher age-adjusted mortality than women. Independent predictors of adverse outcome include older age, male sex, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, coronary disease, advanced NYHA class, S3 or elevated JVP, reduced LV/RV EF, elevated wedge pressure, low cardiac index and peak VO2, pulmonary hypertension, worsening renal function, hyponatremia, hyperuricemia, elevated troponin and natriuretic peptides, elevated neurohormones, widened QRS/LBBB, atrial fibrillation, and ventricular arrhythmias. HFrecEF carries a better prognosis than HFrEF or HFpEF. Overall outcomes are improving with risk-factor treatment and guideline-directed therapies.","pitfalls":["Relying on signs and symptoms alone, which are neither sensitive nor specific for HF; maintain a high index of suspicion in high-risk patients (older, hypertension, diabetes, CKD).","Excluding HF because the lung exam is clear; rales can be absent in chronic HF with chronically elevated wedge pressures.","Misreading right-HF hepatic congestion (elevated transaminases, alkaline phosphatase, bilirubin) as biliary tract disease.","Misdiagnosing cardiac asthma/bronchial congestion as primary asthma or COPD and treating inappropriately.","Over- or under-interpreting natriuretic peptides without accounting for obesity (falsely low) and for age, female sex, CKD, AF, PE, and PAH (falsely elevated).","Equating NYHA functional class with objective cardiac structure or function; it correlates poorly with EF and peak VO2.","Attributing diuretic-refractory nonpitting edema to HF when it represents lymphedema needing different workup.","Failing to seek precipitating factors (identifiable in 50-90% of admissions), such as nonadherence, excess salt/fluid, NSAIDs, negative inotropes, ischemia, arrhythmia, infection, anemia, thyroid disease, or sleep apnea."],"severityClassification":"New York Heart Association (NYHA) functional classification: Class I, no limitation, ordinary activity causes no undue symptoms; Class II, mild limitation, comfortable at rest but ordinary activity (e.g., carrying heavy packages) causes fatigue, dyspnea, palpitations, or angina; Class III, moderate limitation, comfortable at rest but less-than-ordinary activity (e.g., getting dressed) provokes symptoms; Class IV, severe, symptoms of HF or angina at rest, worsening with any activity. EF-based phenotypes: HFrEF (EF less than or equal to 40%), HFmrEF (41-49%), HFpEF (greater than or equal to 50%). The chapter notes NYHA class correlates poorly with objective measures such as LV size, EF, and peak oxygen consumption.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1975","Harrison 22e p.1976","Harrison 22e p.1977","Harrison 22e p.1979","Harrison 22e p.1980","Harrison 22e p.1981","Harrison 22e p.1982","Harrison 22e p.1983","Harrison 22e p.1984","Harrison 22e p.1985"],"crossLinks":[]},"heat_related_illnesses":{"name":"Heat-Related Illnesses","system":"Environmental/Thermoregulatory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Heatstroke","Heat exhaustion","Heat cramps","Heat syncope","Heat edema","Hyperthermia (environmental)"],"chapter":478,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3772","clinicalPearls":["Heat-related illnesses span a spectrum from minor syndromes (heat edema, prickly heat, heat syncope, heat cramps) through heat exhaustion to heatstroke, a true medical emergency.","Heatstroke is distinguished by the historical/physical triad of heat-stress exposure, CNS dysfunction, and a core temperature above 40.5C (104.9F); some patients with impending heatstroke may initially appear lucid.","In contrast to heatstroke, fever reflects intact thermoregulation, whereas heatstroke represents total loss of thermoregulatory control.","Heat exhaustion is characterized by preserved thermoregulatory control and CNS function with core temperature usually below 40.5C (105F); more profound CNS impairment should raise concern for heatstroke or another cause.","Two clinical forms of heatstroke exist: classic (epidemic) heatstroke (CHS) in older or chronically ill, often medicated, sedentary patients during heat waves (skin frequently hot and dry from ceased sweating), and exertional heatstroke (EHS) in young healthy exercising individuals who are often profusely diaphoretic despite dehydration.","Evaporation of skin moisture is the single most efficient mechanism of heat loss but becomes progressively ineffective once relative humidity exceeds 70%; conduction and convection fail once ambient temperature exceeds skin temperature.","Many patients are unaware that their medications impair heat tolerance: anticholinergics, phenothiazines, and heterocyclic antidepressants blunt sweating and central thermoregulation, while beta blockers, calcium channel blockers, and various stimulants reduce cutaneous perfusion or increase heat production.","Hepatocytes are highly heat sensitive: AST is routinely elevated on presentation in heatstroke, and both AST and ALT can eventually rise to over 100 times normal values.","Core-temperature elevation duration directly drives morbidity and mortality; peripheral (non-core) temperature measurement is unreliable, and continuous core monitoring is needed.","Heatstroke commonly produces a thermal cardiomyopathy with potentially elevated CVP despite dehydration, plus deceptive noncardiogenic pulmonary edema and basilar rales in a hypovolemic patient.","The wet-bulb globe temperature (WBGT) is a superior environmental heat-load index to the simple heat index because it incorporates radiant heat and wind speed in addition to air temperature and humidity.","Acclimatization to heat develops over one to several weeks (lowered sweat threshold, increased sweat volume with lower salt content, plasma volume expansion, lower heart rate with higher stroke volume) and dissipates rapidly within weeks after leaving the hot environment."],"pathophysiology":"The central thermostat in the preoptic nucleus of the anterior hypothalamus orchestrates heat dissipation via autonomic-driven cutaneous vasodilation and diaphoresis. Heat is lost through evaporation, radiation, conduction, and convection; skin blood flow can rise 25-30 fold with compensatory splanchnic and renal vasoconstriction. When heat load overwhelms dissipation, illness results. Heat cramps reflect relative intracellular deficiency of sodium, potassium, and fluid, compounded by copious hypotonic fluid replacement that produces hyponatremia and hypochloremia. Heat exhaustion arises from water depletion, sodium depletion, or both. Heatstroke begins when maintaining perfusion forces peripheral vasoconstriction, which halts cutaneous heat radiation and causes core temperature to rise sharply. Above 42C (107.6F) direct cellular injury occurs: thermosensitive enzymes fail, oxidative phosphorylation uncouples irreversibly, heat-shock proteins and cytokines drive a systemic inflammatory response, and endothelial damage activates the coagulation cascade. Splanchnic shunting causes gut ischemia with endotoxin release that further impairs thermoregulation, potentially progressing to hepatic dysfunction, renal failure, DIC, and fulminant multisystem organ failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Malignant hyperthermia (e.g., after general anesthesia)","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Serotonin syndrome","Thyroid storm","Pheochromocytoma","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Drug/toxin toxidromes (amphetamines, cocaine, sympathomimetics, salicylates, anticholinergics, strychnine, dietary supplements, hallucinogens)","Withdrawal syndromes (ethanol, hypnotics)","Stroke / cerebral hemorrhage and other CNS causes of altered thermoregulation","Cardiovascular, neurologic, and metabolic causes of syncope (for heat syncope)"],"infectionMimics":["Sepsis syndrome / SIRS of infectious origin","Meningitis","Encephalitis","Cerebral abscess","Malaria","Typhoid","Tetanus"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Malignant hyperthermia","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Serotonin syndrome","Thyroid storm","Pheochromocytoma","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Systemic inflammatory response syndrome (noninfectious)","Toxicologic causes of hyperthermia (sympathomimetics, anticholinergic toxidrome, salicylates)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Core (central) temperature monitoring; peripheral temperature is unreliable","Serum electrolytes (sodium, chloride, potassium) - hyponatremia/hypochloremia in salt-depletion states","Hepatic aminotransferases (AST, ALT) - markedly elevated in heatstroke, mildly elevated in heat exhaustion","Coagulation studies (platelets, fibrinogen, prothrombin) - decreased; assess for DIC","Creatine kinase and lactate dehydrogenase - elevated, suggesting EHS/rhabdomyolysis","Renal function and calcium/phosphate - evaluate for rhabdomyolysis complications (hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia)","Urinalysis - microscopic hematuria, myoglobinuria, granular or red cell casts","Blood glucose - hypoglycemia is frequent","Lactate - mild acidosis in CHS, marked lactic acidosis in EHS","Cardiac biomarkers and ECG - tachyarrhythmias, nonspecific ST-T changes, heat-related ischemia/infarction","Toxicologic screening, cranial CT, and CSF analysis if mental status does not improve with cooling"],"redFlags":["Core temperature above 40.5C (104.9F) with CNS dysfunction in the setting of heat stress (heatstroke)","Persistent or worsening altered mental status, seizures, or failure to improve with cooling","Hot, dry, anhidrotic skin in a dehydrated patient (classic heatstroke)","Hypotension with widened pulse pressure, tachypnea, and tachyarrhythmia","Evidence of rhabdomyolysis (markedly elevated CK, myoglobinuria, oliguria)","Coagulopathy / disseminated intravascular coagulation","Markedly elevated transaminases indicating hepatic injury","Noncardiogenic pulmonary edema / ARDS, aspiration","Heat exhaustion that progresses to heatstroke even after removal from heat stress"],"prognosis":"Minor heat syndromes (heat edema, prickly heat, heat syncope, hyperventilation tetany, heat cramps) and heat exhaustion generally resolve favorably with removal from heat, cooling, and fluid/electrolyte repletion; younger, previously healthy heat-exhaustion patients without major laboratory abnormalities often do well, while older patients with comorbidities frequently require inpatient management. Heatstroke prognosis is closely tied to the duration of core-temperature elevation: delayed cooling can lead to severe hepatic dysfunction, permanent renal failure, DIC, and fulminant multisystem organ failure. Heat-related cardiac biomarker elevations and thermal cardiomyopathy may be reversible. Heat waves substantially increase mortality, especially among the elderly and malnourished, and secondary vascular events (strokes, myocardial infarctions) occur at least ten times more often during extreme heat.","pitfalls":["Treating heat edema with diuretics, which are ineffective and predispose to volume depletion and more serious heat illness.","Failing to recognize that heat exhaustion can progress to heatstroke after the patient is removed from heat stress.","Relying on peripheral temperature measurements, which are unreliable in heatstroke.","Mistaking cold-induced tonic-clonic muscular rigidity during cooling for seizure activity.","Misinterpreting an elevated CVP as adequate volume status, since thermal cardiomyopathy and a hyperdynamic circulation can elevate CVP despite significant dehydration.","Assuming hemoconcentration is always present in heat exhaustion (it is not), and overlooking that most cases reflect mixed sodium and water depletion.","Correcting free water deficits too rapidly, risking an excessive fall in serum osmolality.","Overlooking medication and substance contributions (anticholinergics, antipsychotics, stimulants) to impaired thermoregulation.","Pursuing the broad infectious/toxic/endocrine differential without simultaneously initiating rapid cooling in suspected heatstroke."],"severityClassification":"Severity ladder of heat-related illness: minor heat-emergency syndromes (heat edema, prickly heat/miliaria rubra, heat syncope, hyperventilation tetany) and heat cramps are mild and self-limited; heat exhaustion is intermediate with core temperature usually below 40.5C (105F) and preserved CNS function; heatstroke is the most severe with core temperature above 40.5C (104.9F), CNS dysfunction, and loss of thermoregulation. Heatstroke is further classified as classic (epidemic) heatstroke (CHS - older/chronically ill, sedentary, anhidrotic, milder coagulopathy and lactic acidosis, mild CK rise) versus exertional heatstroke (EHS - younger/healthy, exercising, diaphoretic, with rhabdomyolysis, marked lactic acidosis, acute renal failure, DIC, hypoglycemia/hypocalcemia, hyperkalemia, and hyponatremia).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3772","Harrison 22e p.3773","Harrison 22e p.3774","Harrison 22e p.3775","Harrison 22e p.3776"],"crossLinks":[]},"heavy_metal_poisoning":{"name":"Heavy Metal Poisoning","system":"Toxicology / Environmental Medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Toxic metal poisoning","Arsenic poisoning","Lead poisoning","Mercury poisoning","Cadmium poisoning"],"chapter":469,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3699","clinicalPearls":["The U.S. ATSDR ranks arsenic, lead, mercury, and cadmium as the 1st, 2nd, 3rd, and 7th most hazardous substances at toxic waste sites; the WHO lists all four among the 10 chemicals of greatest public-health concern globally (Harrison 22e p.3699).","Lead and mercury are xenobiotic metals with no clear safe threshold, whereas copper and selenium are essential trace elements that become toxic only at high exposure (Harrison 22e p.3699).","Acute arsenic poisoning produces hemorrhagic gastroenteritis with intestinal mucosal necrosis, fluid loss and hypotension, plus delayed cardiomyopathy, acute tubular necrosis, and hemolysis; chronic exposure causes peripheral neuropathy, hyperkeratosis/hyperpigmentation, and cancers of skin, lung, liver (angiosarcoma), bladder, and kidney (Harrison 22e p.3700).","Classic arsenic signs include a garlicky breath odor and Mees' lines (transverse white striae on the fingernails); arsenic is radiopaque and may be visible on abdominal x-ray (Harrison 22e p.3700).","Cadmium has a biologic half-life of 10-30 years; severe food/water contamination in Japan caused itai-itai (ouch-ouch) disease with painful bone fractures from cadmium-induced bone toxicity (Harrison 22e p.3699-3700).","Lead in blood is 95-99% sequestered in red cells, so lead must be measured in whole blood rather than serum; about 15% of an absorbed dose is stored in bone with a half-life >20 years, making bone (KXRF) levels a marker of cumulative exposure (Harrison 22e p.3700).","Impairment of childhood IQ from lead occurs with no measurable threshold down to the assay limit of detection (~1 ug/dL); the CDC recommends screening children at about 6 months and intervening if blood lead exceeds 3.5 ug/dL (Harrison 22e p.3700).","Chronic metallic mercury vapor exposure produces an intention tremor and mercurial erethism (excitability, memory loss, insomnia, timidity, delirium) -- the historical 'mad as a hatter' picture (Harrison 22e p.3701).","Children exposed to mercury in any form may develop acrodynia ('pink disease'): flushing, itching, swelling, tachycardia, hypertension, excessive salivation/perspiration, irritability, weakness, and desquamation of palms and soles (Harrison 22e p.3701).","Dimethylmercury is 'supertoxic' -- a few drops absorbed through skin or inhaled can cause fatal cerebellar degeneration (Harrison 22e p.3701).","Manganese overexposure (miners, welders, dry-battery workers) causes a parkinsonian syndrome within 1-2 years that, unlike idiopathic Parkinson disease, is nonresponsive to L-dopa (Harrison 22e p.3702).","The single most important treatment is recognizing and stopping the exposure; activated charcoal does not bind metals and is of limited value in acute metal ingestion (Harrison 22e p.3699)."],"pathophysiology":"Metals enter the body chiefly by inhalation of dusts/fumes, ingestion of contaminated food or water (especially via hand-to-mouth activity in children), and sometimes vapor (e.g., mercury) or dermal absorption (organic lead, thallium). Gastrointestinal absorption varies markedly with the chemical form of the metal and host nutritional status. Once absorbed, metals are transported in blood and sequestered in organs such as bone, liver, and kidney, sometimes for years, then excreted mainly by renal and gastrointestinal routes (with minor losses via sweat, saliva, breath, milk, skin, hair, and nails). Toxicity arises from enzyme inhibition, damage to subcellular organelles and DNA, covalent protein modification, generation of reactive oxygen species, and displacement of essential metals from metal-dependent proteins; metals also alter DNA methylation and other epigenetic marks, dysregulating gene and microRNA expression. Many metals bind sulfhydryl (thiol) groups; cadmium and mercury both bind cellular sulfhydryls, cadmium competing with zinc and calcium, and lead interferes with mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation, ATPases, and calcium-dependent messengers while enhancing oxidation and apoptosis. Genetic polymorphisms affecting metal binding/transport and nutritional/comorbid factors modify individual susceptibility (Harrison 22e p.3699-3700).","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of acute gastroenteritis with hemodynamic collapse (arsenic, mercuric salt, or cadmium ingestion)","Acquired peripheral polyneuropathy from other toxic/metabolic causes (arsenic, thallium, lead)","Parkinsonism from other causes (manganese-induced parkinsonism vs idiopathic Parkinson disease)","Microcytic/normocytic anemia from other causes (lead-related anemia with basophilic stippling; cadmium-related microcytic hypochromic anemia unresponsive to iron)","Encephalopathy of other etiology (lead encephalopathy; aluminum-related dialysis encephalopathy)","Fanconi syndrome / proximal tubulopathy from other nephrotoxins"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial or viral gastroenteritis (mimicked by acute arsenic, mercuric salt, or cadmium ingestion)","Pneumonia (mimicked by acute cadmium or mercury vapor inhalation causing pneumonitis/noncardiogenic pulmonary edema)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Idiopathic Parkinson disease (manganese parkinsonism)","Alzheimer disease / other neurodegeneration (aluminum association)","Iron-deficiency anemia (cadmium-induced anemia unresponsive to iron)","Pyelonephritis and other renal disease causing elevated urinary beta-2-microglobulin (overlaps with cadmium nephropathy marker)","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis and hearing loss (associations described with lead)"],"additionalInvestigations":["24-h urinary arsenic (toxic >67 umol/d or 50 ug/d, after 24 h without seafood); serum arsenic >0.9 umol/L (7 ug/dL) if recent; arsenic in hair/nails","Whole-blood lead level (not serum); blood protoporphyrin (free erythrocyte or zinc); peripheral smear for basophilic stippling; nerve conduction studies; long-bone x-ray for epiphyseal 'lead lines'","KXRF (in vivo x-ray fluorescence) bone lead measurement to assess cumulative lead exposure","Serum cadmium >500 nmol/L (5 ug/dL) if recent; urinary cadmium >100 nmol/L (10 ug/g creatinine); urinary beta-2-microglobulin >750 ug/g creatinine","Blood mercury (recent) or hair mercury (chronic organic exposure); urine mercury; provocation: >20-ug rise in 24-h urine mercury after a 2-g succimer dose for remote exposure","ECG (arsenic: QRS broadening, QT prolongation, ST depression, T-wave flattening); abdominal x-ray for radiopaque arsenic or thallium"],"redFlags":["Acute encephalopathy with convulsions and coma at very high blood lead (>80-120 ug/dL) -- risk of death","Noncardiogenic pulmonary edema after acute cadmium or mercury-vapor inhalation","Hemorrhagic gastroenteritis with hypotension and delayed cardiomyopathy in acute arsenic poisoning","Cardiovascular collapse after acute ingestion of inorganic mercury (lethal dose 10-42 mg/kg)","Garlicky breath, Mees' lines, or a gingival 'lead line' indicating significant metal exposure","Skin or inhalational exposure to dimethylmercury (supertoxic; minute amounts fatal)","Pregnancy exposure to mercury or high maternal bone lead -- risk of severe fetal neurodevelopmental injury"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the metal, dose, acuity, and promptness of exposure termination. Acute high-dose poisoning can be fatal (e.g., lead encephalopathy at BPb >120 ug/dL; inorganic mercury ingestion at 10-42 mg/kg; cadmium or mercury-vapor pulmonary edema; dimethylmercury exposure). Many effects of chronic exposure are irreversible or only partly reversible, including peripheral neuropathy, cognitive decline, manganese parkinsonism (L-dopa nonresponsive), itai-itai bone disease, and cadmium-related chronic renal failure for which no effective chelation exists. Cumulative lead burden is an established risk factor for chronic disease, including hypertension, cardiovascular morbidity/mortality, and chronic kidney disease, and prenatal exposure carries lasting offspring neurodevelopmental risk (Harrison 22e p.3699-3702).","pitfalls":["Measuring lead in serum instead of whole blood (95-99% of lead is in red cells) (Harrison 22e p.3700).","Relying on activated charcoal for metal ingestion -- charcoal does not bind metals (Harrison 22e p.3699); however thallium is an exception, where charcoal interrupts enterohepatic circulation (Harrison 22e p.3702).","Attributing high urinary beta-2-microglobulin solely to cadmium -- it also rises in other renal diseases such as pyelonephritis (Harrison 22e p.3700).","Misreading elevated total arsenic from recent seafood (nontoxic organic arsenobetaine/arsenocholine) as toxic inorganic arsenic -- test after 24 h without seafood (Harrison 22e p.3700).","Attempting chelation for cadmium poisoning -- chelation is not useful and dimercaprol can worsen cadmium nephrotoxicity (Harrison 22e p.3700).","Expecting chelation to improve outcomes in asymptomatic childhood lead exposure -- a large randomized trial showed no benefit on intelligence at BPb 20-40 ug/dL (Harrison 22e p.3700).","Mistaking manganese parkinsonism for idiopathic Parkinson disease and expecting an L-dopa response (Harrison 22e p.3702)."],"severityClassification":"No single unified grading; severity is metal- and level-specific. Lead: subclinical childhood effects at BPb ~25-60 ug/dL, with IQ impairment down to ~1 ug/dL; clinically apparent neurodevelopmental delay at BPb 40-80 ug/dL; acute impaired neurotransmission/neuronal death at BPb >60-80 ug/dL; acute encephalopathy with convulsions/coma at BPb >80-120 ug/dL. Adult occupational removal at BPb >40 ug/dL (proposed newer target <10 ug/dL, removal if >20 ug/dL). Arsenic lethal dose 120-200 mg in adults, 2 mg/kg in children. Inorganic mercury ingestion lethal at 10-42 mg/kg; organic mercury CNS lesions at >1.7 mg/kg. Thallium severe poisoning after single dose >1 g or >8 mg/kg (Harrison 22e p.3700-3702).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3699","Harrison 22e p.3700","Harrison 22e p.3701","Harrison 22e p.3702"],"crossLinks":[]},"helicobacter_pylori_infection":{"name":"Helicobacter pylori Infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["H. pylori"],"chapter":168,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1298; also 302, 416, 646, 649-650, 1009","clinicalPearls":["H. pylori has a proven etiologic role in peptic ulcer disease; together with NSAID use it is one of the two most common causes of gastroduodenal ulcers (p.302).","In dyspeptic patients under 60 with no alarm features, test-and-treat is the recommended initial strategy: assess H. pylori status by fecal (stool) antigen or urea breath testing, and treat those who are positive (p.303).","Eradication of H. pylori in patients with bleeding ulcers lowers rebleeding rates to under 5% (p.272).","H. pylori is a known driver of gastric cancer; the intestinal subtype of gastric adenocarcinoma is associated with H. pylori infection and atrophic/atopic gastritis (p.646, p.647).","Although H. pylori is extremely common (present in roughly half of all humans), gastric cancer develops in only a small subset of those infected, so infection alone is not sufficient (p.646).","Treating H. pylori reduces future gastric cancer risk: eradication lowered gastric cancer incidence in people with a first-degree relative who had gastric cancer, and reduced second cancers in patients with prior early-stage gastric cancer (p.646).","All patients with gastric lymphoma should have H. pylori status evaluated; if histopathology is negative, confirm with a stool antigen test or urea breath test (p.649).","For gastric MALT lymphoma, antibiotic eradication of H. pylori alone is highly effective and is the initial therapy when H. pylori is positive and the t(11;18) translocation assay is negative, producing high response and complete-remission rates (p.649-650)."],"pathophysiology":"H. pylori is a flagellated gastric bacterium whose colonization triggers chronic mucosal inflammation. The flagellum lets the organism access the gastric epithelium and is essential for virulence; H. pylori evades innate immunity by producing a flagellin epitope that does not bind TLR5 and tetra-acylated LPS that does not activate TLR4 (p.1009-1010). Adhesion and gastric tropism are mediated by bacterial outer-membrane proteins BabA and SabA interacting with epithelial GM1 ganglioside (p.1009). Chronic H. pylori inflammation, partly via generation of carcinogenic nitrites from dietary nitrates, drives the gastritis-to-cancer sequence and is a recognized cause of peptic ulcer disease, gastric adenocarcinoma (intestinal subtype), and gastric MALT lymphoma; certain higher-virulence strains carry greater cancer risk (p.646).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urea breath test (noninvasive) for active infection and for confirming eradication.","Stool (fecal) antigen test (noninvasive) for active infection and eradication confirmation.","Endoscopic biopsy with histopathology, including for gastric lymphoma staging.","PCR or FISH for the t(11;18) translocation in gastric MALT lymphoma to predict responsiveness to eradication therapy (p.649)."],"redFlags":["Dyspepsia with alarm features (dysphagia, weight loss, bleeding/anemia, jaundice, palpable mass or adenopathy, family history of gastroesophageal malignancy) or age over 60 warrants upper endoscopy to exclude malignancy rather than test-and-treat alone (p.302-303).","Failure to respond to eradication, or recurrent/refractory symptoms, should prompt further evaluation."],"prognosis":"Eradication markedly improves ulcer outcomes (rebleeding falls below 5%). In gastric MALT lymphoma, H. pylori eradication yields high response rates with complete remission in the majority, though time to remission can be prolonged (averaging ~15-16 months in some studies), requiring patient monitoring before declaring non-response (p.272, p.650). Benefit in functional dyspepsia is limited but statistically significant (about a 9% risk reduction vs placebo) (p.302).","pitfalls":["PITFALL: PPIs and other acid-suppressing drugs can cause false-negative urea breath and stool antigen tests; acid suppression must be stopped before testing for active H. pylori infection.","Confirmation of eradication should be performed 4-6 weeks after completing therapy, not immediately (p.303).","Do not rely on a single negative histopathology result in gastric lymphoma; confirm with noninvasive stool antigen or urea breath testing (p.649).","NEGATION: Even H. pylori-negative MALT tumors may respond to anti-H. pylori therapy, so a negative test does not preclude a trial of eradication; the t(11;18) translocation identifies a subgroup less likely to respond (p.649-650).","NEGATION: H. pylori eradication does NOT worsen GERD symptoms, despite H. pylori infection being associated with a reduced prevalence of GERD (p.302)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Chapter 168 (Helicobacter pylori Infections), p.1298.","Choi IJ et al: Helicobacter pylori and prevention of gastric cancer. N Engl J Med.","Choi IJ et al: Family history of gastric cancer and Helicobacter pylori treatment."],"crossLinks":["peptic_ulcer"]},"hematopoietic_cell_transplantation":{"name":"Hematopoietic Cell Transplantation","system":"Hematology/Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HCT","Bone marrow transplantation","Stem cell transplantation","Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation","HSCT"],"chapter":119,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.914","clinicalPearls":["Hematopoietic cell transplantation (HCT) is the umbrella term that has replaced 'bone marrow transplantation,' reflecting that hematopoietic stem cells can be sourced from marrow, growth-factor-mobilized peripheral blood, or umbilical cord blood (Harrison 22e p.914).","It is used both to replace a defective but non-malignant lympho-hematopoietic system with a normal donor system and to treat malignancy, the latter by permitting higher myelosuppressive doses and, in allogeneic transplant, by adding an immunologic graft-versus-tumor effect (Harrison 22e p.914).","Transplant categories are syngeneic (identical twin, ~1% of cases, no GVHD and no tumor contamination), allogeneic (genetically non-identical donor, risk of GVHD and graft rejection), and autologous (patient's own stored cells, no GVHD/rejection but lacks a graft-versus-tumor effect and may carry contaminating tumor cells) (Harrison 22e p.914-915).","The chance any one full sibling is HLA-matched is 1 in 4; the probability a patient has an HLA-identical sibling is 1 - (0.75)^n where n is the number of siblings (Harrison 22e p.915).","With registries exceeding 40 million volunteer donors, an HLA-matched unrelated donor can be found for about 60% of patients, with higher rates in Caucasians and lower rates in minorities and people of mixed ancestry; a search and scheduling typically takes 3-4 months (Harrison 22e p.915).","Autologous engraftment is reliable when more than 2.5 x 10^6 CD34+ cells/kg are infused; the 5-10% of patients who mobilize poorly with growth factor alone may benefit from plerixafor, a CXCR4 antagonist that releases stem cells from the marrow niche (Harrison 22e p.915).","Stem cell homing depends on P- and E-selectin/integrin (e.g., VLA-4) interactions and on niche-derived CXCL12 binding CXCR4 on the stem cell; human HSCs tolerate cryopreservation, enabling autologous storage (Harrison 22e p.914-915).","After marrow grafting, granulocytes recover to ~100/uL by about day 16 and ~500/uL by day 22; mobilized peripheral blood speeds recovery by ~1 week while cord blood delays it by ~1 week (Harrison 22e p.916).","Innate immunity recovers quickly, but adaptive (T- and B-cell) immunity may take 1-2 years; early T-cell recovery is dominated by infused donor CD8+ T cells with a limited repertoire, with diverse de novo CD4+/CD8+ cells emerging months later (Harrison 22e p.916).","Reduced-intensity and nonmyeloablative conditioning rely heavily on the graft-versus-tumor effect; they carry higher relapse but lower transplant-related mortality, favoring older patients and those with comorbidities, whereas high-dose conditioning is preferred when measurable disease is present and the patient can tolerate it (Harrison 22e p.915-916).","Evidence for the graft-versus-tumor effect: relapse is lowest in patients who develop acute and chronic GVHD, higher without GVHD, and highest after T-cell-depleted allogeneic or syngeneic grafts; donor lymphocyte infusions can induce remission in post-transplant relapse (Harrison 22e p.916).","Worldwide more than 100,000 transplants were performed in 2022, with frequency closely tracking gross national income per capita (Harrison 22e p.914)."],"pathophysiology":"A single hematopoietic stem cell can regenerate the entire lympho-hematopoietic system, including red cells, granulocytes, B and T lymphocytes, platelets, and fixed-tissue macrophages (Kupffer cells, alveolar macrophages, osteoclasts, Langerhans cells). Infused cells home to the marrow via selectin/integrin adhesion and CXCL12-CXCR4 signaling. In allogeneic transplant, the central immunologic axis is HLA matching: HLA molecules present antigenic peptides to T cells, so mismatched 'major antigens' provoke strong T-cell reactions while even HLA-matched pairs can react against 'minor' endogenous antigens. Donor T cells reacting against host tissue cause graft-versus-host disease, whereas residual immunocompetent host cells can mediate graft rejection if conditioning is insufficiently immunosuppressive. The same alloreactivity underlies the beneficial graft-versus-tumor effect. Conditioning regimens combine antineoplastic activity, myelosuppression, and (for allogeneic) immunosuppression to eradicate disease and prevent rejection (Harrison 22e p.914-916).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute GVHD versus conditioning regimen toxicity, drug eruption, or infection as a cause of post-transplant rash, diarrhea, and hepatic dysfunction (biopsy of skin, liver, or gut often needed for confirmation) (Harrison 22e p.917)","Sinusoidal obstruction syndrome versus other causes of tender hepatomegaly, jaundice, and fluid retention early after transplant (Harrison 22e p.916)","Transplant-associated thrombotic microangiopathy versus other microangiopathic processes (schistocytes, elevated LDH, thrombocytopenia, acute kidney injury) (Harrison 22e p.916-917)"],"infectionMimics":["Idiopathic/diffuse interstitial pneumonia from conditioning toxicity can mimic infectious pneumonia early post-transplant (Harrison 22e p.916)","Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia and bronchiolitis obliterans (late, often GVHD-associated) can mimic chronic respiratory infection (Harrison 22e p.916)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Many noninfectious conditions can mimic acute GVHD, so histologic confirmation is usually required (Harrison 22e p.917)","Post-transplant autoimmune phenomena (autoimmune hemolytic anemia, immune thrombocytopenia) in 3-5% can mimic relapse-related or drug-induced cytopenias (Harrison 22e p.918)"],"additionalInvestigations":["HLA typing of HLA-A, -B, -C, and -D (and DRB1) to assess donor-recipient matching; AML/ALL candidates should be HLA-typed soon after diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.915, 919)","Screening recipients for donor-directed anti-HLA antibodies before transplant, since their presence predicts poor engraftment (Harrison 22e p.917)","Chimerism/engraftment documentation by fluorescence in situ hybridization of sex chromosomes (sex-mismatched pairs) or short tandem repeat polymorphism analysis after DNA amplification (Harrison 22e p.916)","CD34 antigen quantification or colony-forming unit assays to gauge stem cell dose adequacy (Harrison 22e p.915)","PCR monitoring of blood for CMV (and other viral) DNA to guide preemptive therapy (Harrison 22e p.918)","Minimal residual disease assessment by multidimensional flow cytometry or molecular methods to refine transplant candidacy in acute leukemia (Harrison 22e p.919)","GVHD severity biomarkers including ST2, REG3a, and TNF R1 (Harrison 22e p.917)"],"redFlags":["Tender hepatomegaly, jaundice, ascites, and fluid retention within the first month (peak ~day 16) suggest sinusoidal obstruction syndrome, which carries ~30% mortality and can progress to fatal hepatorenal syndrome (Harrison 22e p.916)","Erythematous maculopapular rash, persistent anorexia or diarrhea, and rising bilirubin/transaminases/alkaline phosphatase in the first 3 months point to acute GVHD; grades II-IV require aggressive therapy (Harrison 22e p.917)","Schistocytes, elevated LDH, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury about a month post-transplant indicate transplant-associated thrombotic microangiopathy (Harrison 22e p.916-917)","Diffuse fluffy pulmonary infiltrates with alveolar hemorrhage on lavage suggest conditioning-related diffuse alveolar damage rather than infection (Harrison 22e p.916)","Failure of marrow function to return, or loss after initial engraftment, signals graft failure/rejection; persistence of host-origin lymphocytes indicates immunologic rejection (Harrison 22e p.917)","Fever once the granulocyte count falls below 500/uL prompts broad-spectrum empiric antibacterial therapy because infection risk is so high (Harrison 22e p.918)","Community respiratory viruses (RSV, parainfluenza, influenza, metapneumovirus) can be life-threatening or fatal in transplant recipients (Harrison 22e p.918)"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are highly disease- and stage-dependent (CIBMTR estimates, transplants 2010-2020). Reported approximate 3-year survival: severe combined immunodeficiency ~95% allogeneic; aplastic anemia ~98% allogeneic; thalassemia ~95% allogeneic; AML 57% (first remission) and 53% (second remission); ALL 64% (first remission) and 45% (second remission); CML 71% (chronic phase), 67% (accelerated), 28% (blast crisis); CLL ~64%; myelodysplasia ~50%; multiple myeloma ~80% autologous as initial therapy; non-Hodgkin lymphoma 45% allogeneic / 55% autologous (first relapse/second remission); Hodgkin disease 68% allogeneic / 89% autologous (first relapse/second remission). Matched-unrelated-donor survival now essentially equals matched-sibling survival. Sinusoidal obstruction syndrome carries ~30% mortality; chronic GVHD mortality averages ~15% (range 5-50% by severity). HLA-matched sibling allogeneic transplant carries roughly a 1-3% graft rejection risk and ~15% risk of severe acute GVHD (Harrison 22e p.915-920).","pitfalls":["Premedication is not a reliable solution: in transfusion-related GVHD analogies aside, here the key pitfall is assuming infectious causes for early rash/diarrhea/hepatic dysfunction; acute GVHD typically needs biopsy because many conditions mimic it (Harrison 22e p.917)","Grade I acute GVHD is clinically minor, does not affect survival, and does not require treatment; over-treating it adds unnecessary immunosuppression (Harrison 22e p.917)","Mobilized peripheral blood gives faster engraftment but, in matched-unrelated-donor transplant, increases chronic GVHD without a compensatory survival advantage (Harrison 22e p.916)","No prospective randomized trial has shown that purging autologous grafts of tumor cells reduces relapse or improves survival (Harrison 22e p.915)","Standard high-dose conditioning is poorly tolerated if given within 100 days of a first transplant due to cumulative toxicity; reduced-intensity regimens may be needed for graft failure salvage (Harrison 22e p.917)","Patients with Fanconi anemia are abnormally sensitive to alkylating agents, so less intensive conditioning must be used (Harrison 22e p.918)","Vaccine-preventable antibody titers wane after transplant unless recipients are revaccinated; live vaccines (MMR/MMR-V) are generally avoided until 2 years post-transplant (Harrison 22e p.918)","Reinfusing donor stem cells into a patient with immunologic graft rejection is usually futile unless preceded by a second immunosuppressive conditioning regimen (Harrison 22e p.917)"],"severityClassification":"Acute GVHD is staged per organ and combined into an overall grade (Harrison 22e p.917). Skin stage 1-4 (rash <25% body surface up to generalized desquamation/bullae); liver stage by bilirubin from 34-51 umol/L [2-3 mg/dL] up to >257 umol/L [>15 mg/dL]; gut stage by diarrhea volume from 500-1000 mL/d up to ileus. Overall grade I (skin 1-2 only) is clinically insignificant; grades II-IV reflect progressively greater organ involvement, worse survival, and need for aggressive therapy. Myelodysplasia transplant timing uses the International Prognostic Scoring System (IPSS), with transplant generally reserved for Int-2 or higher (or selected Int-1 with poor features) (Harrison 22e p.919).","references":["Harrison 22e p.914","Harrison 22e p.915","Harrison 22e p.916","Harrison 22e p.917","Harrison 22e p.918","Harrison 22e p.919","Harrison 22e p.920"],"crossLinks":[]},"hematopoietic_stem_cells":{"name":"Hematopoietic Stem Cells","system":"Hematologic / Bone Marrow","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HSC","blood stem cells","hematopoiesis"],"chapter":101,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.758","clinicalPearls":["Every blood cell type and certain tissue-resident cells (e.g., microglia, some macrophages) arise from hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs); catastrophic loss of HSC function leaves a person able to survive only about 2-4 weeks without extraordinary support.","The HSC pool is estimated at only about 20,000-200,000 cells, yet it generates hundreds of billions of mature blood cells daily and sustains this output over decades.","Stem cells are defined by two cardinal properties: self-renewal (dividing while staying undifferentiated) and differentiation into mature effector cells; loss of either leads to pool exhaustion or to organ failure/neoplasia.","A dividing HSC can yield two stem cells, two differentiating cells (symmetric division), or one of each (asymmetric division); this asymmetry lets a small pool produce roughly seven orders of magnitude more mature cells daily.","Mature blood cell lifespans vary widely, from hours for neutrophils to a few months for red cells to years for memory lymphocytes, but the durable source is the persistent stem cell pool.","HSCs circulate in blood throughout life, but free-circulating time is brief (minutes in the mouse); circulating HSCs remain functional and usable for transplantation.","The bone marrow niche (notably perivascular regions near trabecular bone) is both nurturing and constraining: mesenchymal and endothelial cells supply kit ligand and CXCL12, and the niche limits how many stem cells are produced.","CXCR4/CXCL12 (SDF1) signaling retains HSCs in marrow; disrupting it (via CXCR4 blockade, CXCL12 cleavage, or receptor downregulation) mobilizes stem cells into blood, enabling collection by leukapheresis rather than operative marrow harvest.","Differentiation proceeds along a branching hierarchy (stem cell to multipotent progenitor to lineage-committed precursor to mature cell), with progressive loss of plasticity and, in most lineages, loss of proliferative capacity.","Maturation of progenitors into mature cells takes roughly 10-14 days in humans, clinically reflected in the lag between cytotoxic chemotherapy and blood count recovery.","HSCs are heterogeneous: individual stem cells can be biased toward particular lineages and differ in proliferation and death responses, so the population behaves as an aggregate.","Clonal hematopoiesis: HSCs accumulate roughly 17 somatic mutations per year of life; variant alleles contributing to over 1% of blood cells become nearly universal by age 70, with an inactivating TET2 mutation able to worsen outcomes of some chronic inflammatory conditions.","Genetically modifying long-lived HSCs underlies curative cell therapies for cerebral adrenoleukodystrophy and the hemoglobinopathies, including a CRISPR gene-edited approach that suppresses BCL11A to reactivate fetal hemoglobin in beta thalassemia and sickle cell anemia."],"pathophysiology":"Hematopoiesis originates from a small self-renewing HSC pool. During development, blood production shifts sequentially from the yolk sac (embryonic-hemoglobin red cells and tissue-resident macrophages) to the placenta and intraembryonic sites near the aorta-gonad-mesonephros region, then to fetal liver, and in the second trimester to bone marrow and spleen; HSCs reach marrow via bloodborne transit from fetal liver after long-bone calcification, and proliferation falls sharply soon after birth. HSCs reside in a specialized perivascular/endosteal marrow niche where mesenchymal, endothelial, neural, and other cells provide regulatory signals (kit ligand, CXCL12) governing survival, expansion, and constraint. Trafficking depends on selectins binding CD162/CD44, integrin-mediated firm adhesion (VCAM-1/VLA-4), and CXCL12-CXCR4 plus calcium-sensing receptor signaling. Most HSCs are deeply quiescent and cytokine-resistant, dividing on the order of months to years; quiescence is enforced by high cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitors (e.g., p57/CDKN1c blocking the G1-S transition) and niche signals such as angiogenin, IL-18, and angiopoietin 1. Self-renewal preserves any acquired mutation in the durable stem pool, whereas progenitor mutations are largely lost through terminal differentiation; epigenetic regulators (DNMT3a, TET2, ASXL1) are critical for pool homeostasis and for restraining outgrowth of potentially pathogenic clones.","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Deep/next-generation sequencing to detect somatic mutations and clonal hematopoiesis (e.g., DNMT3a, TET2, ASXL1 variant allele fractions)","Surface immunophenotyping of stem/progenitor markers (CD34, CD90/Thy-1, CD117/c-Kit, CD133, CD164, CD110/c-Mpl) as described in murine-based studies"],"redFlags":["Complete failure of HSC function (e.g., after a nuclear/radiation accident) predicts survival of only about 2-4 weeks without extraordinary support","Expansion of clones carrying 'driver' mutations seen in myelodysplasia and myeloid leukemias, with low-frequency progression to overt neoplastic disease","Acquired self-renewal via oncogenic mutation in stem or more mature populations, providing a substrate for leukemia"],"prognosis":"In the absence of disease the HSC pool is essentially inexhaustible: serial murine transplants can reconstitute several animals in succession and allogeneic transplant recipients never run out of blood cells over decades, indicating even limiting stem cell numbers suffice. With aging, clonal diversity declines as a small number of expanded clones (estimated 10-20 clones supplying 30-60% of blood cells by age 70) dominate; most such clones are benign, but some carry driver mutations with a low risk of progression to myeloid malignancy.","pitfalls":["Regulators of mature/progenitor cells do not predict HSC behavior: erythropoietin and G-CSF drive precursor proliferation but do not directly cycle stem cells (G-CSF instead mobilizes HSCs by altering CXCL12 tethering).","Deep HSC quiescence and resistance to cytokines make robust in vitro expansion of human HSCs difficult.","Assuming leukemia must arise only from normal stem cells is an oversimplification, since less self-renewing mature populations can also be transformed and self-renewal can be acquired by mutation.","The classic differentiation hierarchy diagram is a simplification; lineage-biased and intermediate cell states are increasingly recognized.","Progenitor (transient amplifying) cells are short-lived and largely lack durable self-renewal, so gene-modifying progenitors rather than true stem cells fails to give lasting benefit."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.758","Harrison 22e p.759","Harrison 22e p.760","Harrison 22e p.761","Harrison 22e p.762"],"crossLinks":[]},"hemochromatosis":{"name":"Hemochromatosis","system":"Hepatology / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["iron overload","HFE hemochromatosis"],"chapter":426,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3333","clinicalPearls":["Hemochromatosis is variably classified as a metabolic or storage disease; the common autosomal recessive form is related to the HFE gene (Harrison 22e p.2062).","Up to 10% of the population is heterozygous for one HFE mutation, so clinical prevalence might be as high as 1 in 500, though observed rates are lower because of limited (incomplete) penetrance (Harrison 22e p.2062).","Increased iron in the skin in types 1 and 2 hemochromatosis stimulates melanin pigment production and leads to the classic bronze color (Harrison 22e p.443).","Systemic iron overload is diagnosed from serum iron and transferrin saturation, with thresholds noted around >60% in men and >45-50% in women; ferritin and transferrin saturation are the main usable laboratory biomarkers of iron status (Harrison 22e p.2062, p.805).","MRI is used to quantitate iron stores in the liver and heart; for cardiac iron overload the diagnosis is made principally by MRI, and endomyocardial biopsy (Prussian blue stain) is not usually needed (Harrison 22e p.2062).","When diagnosed early, hemochromatosis can often be managed by repeated phlebotomy to remove iron (Harrison 22e p.2062).","Hepcidin-regulating proteins HFE, transferrin receptor 2, and hemojuvelin, whose mutations cause different forms of hemochromatosis, normally contribute to hepcidin upregulation; impaired hepcidin signaling allows excess iron entry into plasma (Harrison 22e p.805).","There is no regulated mechanism for iron excretion; physiologic iron loss occurs only through cell desquamation and menses, which is why unregulated absorption produces progressive overload (Harrison 22e p.805)."],"pathophysiology":"Systemic iron homeostasis is controlled by hepcidin, a hepatocyte-derived peptide that blocks the iron exporter ferroportin, limiting iron flux from duodenal enterocytes, recycling macrophages, and storing hepatocytes. High plasma/liver iron normally raises hepcidin via the BMP-SMAD pathway, with HFE, transferrin receptor 2, and hemojuvelin contributing to hepcidin upregulation. Mutations in these genes (most commonly HFE) lead to inappropriately low hepcidin, increased iron absorption, and rising transferrin saturation. Because no regulated route of iron excretion exists, iron accumulates progressively. When transferrin saturation exceeds ~60%, non-transferrin-bound iron (NTBI) appears in the circulation and is taken up by liver, pancreas, and heart, driving parenchymal injury. In the heart, excess iron deposits in the perinuclear compartment of cardiomyocytes, disrupting intracellular architecture and mitochondrial function (Harrison 22e p.805, p.2062).","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary (acquired) iron overload from chronic transfusion in hemoglobinopathies/thalassemia (Harrison 22e p.2062, p.821)","Other metabolic causes of skin hyperpigmentation: porphyria cutanea tarda, vitamin B12 or folate deficiency, pellagra, malabsorption/Whipple's disease (Harrison 22e p.443)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Other causes of restrictive or dilated cardiomyopathy; hemochromatosis is listed among restrictive cardiomyopathies but often presents clinically as a dilated cardiomyopathy (Harrison 22e p.2062)","Other chronic liver diseases progressing to cirrhosis (alcohol, viral hepatitis, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis) (Harrison 22e p.702)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Transferrin saturation (serum iron / total iron-binding capacity) as a key screening test for iron overload (Harrison 22e p.2062, p.805)","Serum ferritin to assess body iron stores (Harrison 22e p.805)","MRI quantitation of hepatic and cardiac iron stores (Harrison 22e p.2062)","Genetic testing for HFE mutations (the gene underlying the common autosomal recessive form) (Harrison 22e p.2062, p.702)","Endomyocardial biopsy with Prussian blue iron stain (reserved; not usually needed when MRI is available) (Harrison 22e p.2062)"],"redFlags":["Hemochromatosis (HFE germline mutations) is a recognized risk factor for hepatocellular carcinoma, typically via cirrhosis (Harrison 22e p.702)","Progression to cirrhosis from hepatic iron loading (Harrison 22e p.2062)","Cardiomyopathy from cardiac siderosis, which may cause heart failure or arrhythmia (Harrison 22e p.2062)","Markedly increased susceptibility to Vibrio vulnificus sepsis (primary sepsis develops most often in patients with cirrhosis or hemochromatosis) (Harrison 22e p.1373)","Increased susceptibility to Yersinia (Y. enterocolitica / Y. pseudotuberculosis) septicemia, with iron overload providing accessible iron; iron chelators such as desferrioxamine can precipitate Yersinia infection (Harrison 22e p.1392)"],"prognosis":"If diagnosed early, iron overload can often be removed by repeated phlebotomy, and cardiac function can improve with iron removal provided myocyte loss and replacement fibrosis are not too severe. Late disease carries the burden of established cirrhosis (with hepatocellular carcinoma risk) and irreversible cardiac and endocrine damage (Harrison 22e p.2062, p.702).","pitfalls":["Incomplete penetrance: many HFE mutation carriers never develop clinical iron-overload disease, so genotype does not equal phenotype, and additional genetic and environmental factors (e.g., alcohol) modify expression (Harrison 22e p.2062).","Hemochromatosis may present clinically as a dilated cardiomyopathy even though it is categorized among restrictive cardiomyopathies (Harrison 22e p.2062).","Iron chelation therapy itself (e.g., desferrioxamine) can supply accessible iron to Yersinia and impair neutrophil function, precipitating Yersinia septicemia in iron-overloaded patients (Harrison 22e p.1392)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.3333","Harrison 22e p.2062","Harrison 22e p.805","Harrison 22e p.702","Harrison 22e p.443","Harrison 22e p.1373","Harrison 22e p.1392"],"crossLinks":["decomp_cirrhosis","dilated_cardiomyopathy","diabetes_mellitus"]},"hemolytic_anemia":{"name":"Hemolytic Anemia","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["haemolytic anaemia"],"chapter":105,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.791","clinicalPearls":["Hemolytic anemia (HA) is anemia from increased red-cell destruction with a normal (usually increased) marrow supply; unlike acute blood loss, cells are destroyed within the body rather than physically lost (p.791).","Classify by primary etiology (inherited vs acquired), tempo (acute vs chronic), severity, site of hemolysis (intravascular vs extravascular), and mechanism (intracorpuscular vs extracorpuscular defects) (p.791, Table 105-1).","Inherited HA generally reflects intracorpuscular defects (hemoglobinopathies, enzymopathies such as G6PD, membrane-cytoskeleton defects); acquired HA generally reflects extracorpuscular factors (autoimmune, microangiopathic/mechanical, toxins, drugs, infection). Exceptions: PNH (inherited-like somatic mutation but acquired) and familial/atypical HUS (p.791-792, Table 105-1).","Core clinical sign is jaundice; patients may report dark urine; splenomegaly is common as a preferential site of hemolysis, the liver may enlarge, and pigment gallstones are common with chronic hemolysis (p.791).","Laboratory hallmarks: increased reticulocytes (both percentage and the more definitive absolute count), raised MCV with macrocytes/polychromasia on smear, increased unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin and AST, raised LDH, and reduced-to-absent haptoglobin (p.791, Table 105-2).","Extravascular hemolysis raises unconjugated bilirubin and urobilinogen; intravascular hemolysis is signalled by hemoglobinuria (often with hemosiderinuria), free plasma hemoglobin, markedly raised LDH (up to ~10x), and low haptoglobin, with bilirubin only mildly elevated (p.791, Table 105-2).","A positive broad-spectrum direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test is on its own almost diagnostic of autoimmune HA; the broad reagent detects bound immunoglobulin and/or complement (C3 fragments) on red cells (p.801).","Warm AIHA (mostly IgG, optimal 37C) causes predominantly extravascular hemolysis with spherocytes; cold agglutinin disease (mostly IgM anti-I, optimal 4-30C) activates complement causing intravascular hemolysis with hemoglobinuria plus Kupffer-cell extravascular destruction in the liver (p.801-803, Fig. 105-9, Table 105-8).","Compensated hemolysis: a brisk EPO-driven erythropoietic response can fully offset red-cell destruction so an inherited hemolytic condition may present without anemia; it can decompensate in pregnancy, folate deficiency, or renal failure (p.792)."],"pathophysiology":"The mature red cell loses mitochondria and ribosomes, leaving anaerobic glycolysis (Embden-Meyerhof pathway) as the sole source of ATP and the hexose-monophosphate shunt (via G6PD-generated NADPH and reduced glutathione) as defense against oxidant stress; damaged proteins cannot be replaced, so any metabolic failure ultimately damages the membrane or the cation pump and shortens the ~120-day life span. Senescent or damaged red cells expose band-3 neoantigens, become opsonized, and are phagocytosed by splenic, hepatic, and other macrophages (extravascular hemolysis); this senescence process is accelerated in many HAs. The essential common feature is increased red-cell turnover; when destruction outpaces marrow production, anemia results. Chronic intravascular hemolysis causes iron loss via hemoglobinuria, whereas chronic extravascular hemolysis (especially if transfused) tends toward iron overload and, untreated, secondary hemochromatosis (p.791-792).","additionalDifferentials":["Inherited intracorpuscular defects: hemoglobinopathies (sickle cell, thalassemia; Chap. 103), enzymopathies (G6PD deficiency, pyruvate kinase deficiency, pyrimidine 5'-nucleotidase deficiency), membrane-cytoskeleton defects (hereditary spherocytosis) (p.791-792, Table 105-1)","Acquired extracorpuscular causes: autoimmune (warm AIHA, cold agglutinin disease, paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria), microangiopathic/mechanical (prosthetic valve, march hemoglobinuria), paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, toxic/drug-induced, infectious (p.792, 801-804, Table 105-1)","Familial (atypical) hemolytic-uremic syndrome from inherited dysregulation permitting excessive complement activation (p.799-800)"],"infectionMimics":["Malaria - the most frequent infectious cause of HA in endemic areas (p.801)","Shiga toxin-producing E. coli O157:H7 causing HUS, more common in children (p.801)","Clostridium perfringens sepsis - life-threatening intravascular hemolysis from lecithinase toxin (open wounds, septic abortion, contaminated blood unit) (p.801, Table 105-7)","Babesia, and Mycoplasma infection (associated with paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria); viral triggers (EBV, CMV, parvovirus B19, HIV, HCV) of secondary autoimmune HA (p.801-802, Table 105-8)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Compensated hemolysis presenting without anemia, mimicking a non-hemolytic state until it decompensates (p.792)","Reticulocytosis-driven macrocytosis that can be mistaken for a megaloblastic process (p.791)","Parvovirus B19 aplastic crisis causing a precipitous hemoglobin fall in a chronic hemolytic patient, mimicking acute worsening of hemolysis (p.792)","Coombs-negative AIHA, which can still be severe when a high-affinity antibody opsonizes red cells with few molecules (p.802)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Reticulocyte count - corrected percentage and absolute count (often neglected in initial anemia workup) (p.791)","Unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin, AST, LDH, haptoglobin (p.791, Table 105-2)","Urine for hemoglobinuria/hemosiderinuria and stool/urine urobilinogen when intravascular hemolysis suspected (p.791)","Peripheral blood smear for schistocytes (microangiopathy/fragmentation), spherocytes (AIHA, hereditary spherocytosis), bite cells (G6PD), basophilic stippling (lead/P5N deficiency) (p.799-804)","Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test - broad-spectrum then specific anti-Ig/anti-C reagents (p.801)","Quantitative G6PD assay (screening tests can miss deficiency soon after a hemolytic attack because young high-G6PD reticulocytes mask it); DNA testing and point-of-care tests available (p.799-800)","Flow cytometry for CD59-negative red cell population in PNH (p.801, Table 105-7)","Donath-Landsteiner antibody for paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria; blood cultures for clostridial septicemia (p.801, Table 105-7)","Red cell survival study (51Cr or nonradioactive 15N) as the gold standard for shortened life span; bone marrow (if done) shows erythroid hyperplasia, usually not required (p.791-792)"],"redFlags":["Schistocytes (fragmented/helmet cells) on smear indicating microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (MAHA) - consider TTP, HUS, and DIC; an emergency requiring urgent evaluation (p.799, 801, Table 105-7)","Abrupt severe drop in hemoglobin (to as low as 4 g/dL within days) in warm AIHA with jaundice and splenomegaly - a medical emergency (p.801)","Acute renal failure - the most serious threat from acute hemolytic attacks in adults (e.g., G6PD-related), and a predictor of poor AIHA outcome (p.799, 801)","Hemoglobinuria signalling intravascular hemolysis (mismatched transfusion, PNH, clostridial sepsis, march hemoglobinuria, favism) (p.801, Table 105-7)","Evans syndrome (AIHA plus autoimmune thrombocytopenia) signals high-risk disease and may indicate underlying immune deficiency (p.801)","Mismatched (usually ABO-incompatible) blood transfusion causing abrupt intravascular hemolysis (p.801, Table 105-7)","Clostridium perfringens sepsis causing life-threatening intravascular hemolysis (p.801)"],"prognosis":"Transient hemolysis usually has no long-term consequences beyond increased erythropoietic (folate) requirement; recurrent or persistent hemolysis predisposes to pigment gallstones, splenomegaly with hypersplenism (neutropenia/thrombocytopenia), and, with chronic extravascular hemolysis or repeated transfusion, iron overload progressing to secondary hemochromatosis affecting liver and heart. Warm AIHA carries ~5-10% mortality even with appropriate management; predictors of worse outcome/relapse include Evans syndrome, hemoglobin <6 g/dL, acute renal failure, infection, and high-affinity antibody. G6PD-related acute hemolysis generally resolves fully once the trigger is removed and in the absence of comorbidity, with full recovery from renal failure the rule when there is no prior kidney disease (p.792, 799, 801).","pitfalls":["Neglecting the reticulocyte count, a key early clue to a hemolytic mechanism (p.791)","Screening (semiquantitative) G6PD tests give falsely normal results immediately after a hemolytic attack because the oldest deficient cells are destroyed and young reticulocytes have higher G6PD - use a quantitative assay; female heterozygotes may also be missed (p.799-800)","Dismissing Coombs-negative AIHA as not serious - high-affinity antibody can cause severe disease with few bound molecules; false-positive Coombs can follow transfusion, organ transplant, or Ig/anti-lymphocyte products (p.801-802)","Assuming an inherited hemolytic disorder always causes anemia - compensated hemolysis can be normo-hemoglobinemic until decompensation (p.792)","In cold agglutinin disease, prednisone and splenectomy are ineffective (unlike warm AIHA), and transfused (I-positive) red cells survive no longer than the patient's; the blood bag and patient extremities must be kept warm (p.803)","Misattributing favism to fava bean pollen inhalation (it does not cause favism) or to other beans (which are safe) (p.799)"],"severityClassification":"No single formal grading. Severity is gauged clinically by the hemoglobin level and tempo (mild compensated hemolysis without anemia through to abrupt severe drops to ~4 g/dL in AIHA) and by the site of hemolysis: intravascular hemolysis is signalled by hemoglobinuria, free plasma hemoglobin, LDH up to ~10x normal, and absent haptoglobin, whereas extravascular hemolysis chiefly raises unconjugated bilirubin and urobilinogen. High-risk markers in AIHA include hemoglobin <6 g/dL, acute renal failure, infection, and Evans syndrome (p.791-792, 801, Tables 105-2, 105-7).","references":["Harrison 22e p.791","Harrison 22e p.792","Harrison 22e p.799","Harrison 22e p.801","Harrison 22e p.802","Harrison 22e p.803"],"crossLinks":["anemia_sympt","ttp_hus"]},"hemolytic_disease_of_newborn":{"name":"Hemolytic Disease of the Newborn","system":"Neonatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HDN","hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn","HDFN","erythroblastosis fetalis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Immune-mediated haemolysis of fetal or neonatal red cells caused by maternal antibodies crossing the placenta and targeting fetal red cell antigens inherited from the father.","Rhesus (anti-D) disease was historically the most severe form; ABO incompatibility is now a more common but generally milder cause, and other minor blood-group antibodies can also be responsible.","Rh disease typically requires prior maternal sensitisation, so it usually spares the first pregnancy and worsens with subsequent affected pregnancies, whereas ABO disease can affect a first-born.","Severe fetal anaemia can cause hydrops fetalis with generalised oedema, effusions, hepatosplenomegaly, and heart failure.","In the newborn the dominant problem is rapidly rising unconjugated bilirubin from ongoing haemolysis, which can appear within the first day of life.","A positive direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test on the infant supports immune haemolysis, along with anaemia, reticulocytosis, and a rising bilirubin.","Rh disease is largely preventable by giving anti-D immunoglobulin to at-risk Rh-negative mothers to prevent sensitisation.","Management ranges from monitoring and phototherapy for hyperbilirubinaemia to exchange transfusion for severe or rapidly rising bilirubin, and intrauterine transfusion for severe fetal anaemia.","Late anaemia can develop over the following weeks as haemolysis and marrow suppression continue, so follow-up haemoglobin monitoring is important."],"pathophysiology":"When fetal red cells carrying a paternally inherited antigen absent in the mother enter the maternal circulation, the mother may form IgG alloantibodies. These IgG antibodies cross the placenta, bind fetal red cells, and cause their destruction, chiefly in the fetal spleen. The resulting anaemia stimulates extramedullary haematopoiesis and, when severe, causes high-output cardiac failure, hepatic dysfunction, hypoalbuminaemia, and hydrops. After birth, loss of placental clearance means the newborn must conjugate the large bilirubin load from continuing haemolysis with an immature liver, producing rapidly rising unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia that risks bilirubin neurotoxicity.","additionalDifferentials":["Non-immune hydrops fetalis","Hereditary red cell membrane or enzyme defects (e.g. spherocytosis, G6PD deficiency)","Sepsis-associated haemolysis or jaundice","Cephalohaematoma or extravascular blood breakdown causing jaundice","Physiological or breast-milk jaundice","Twin-to-twin transfusion and other causes of neonatal anaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Maternal and infant blood group and antibody screening","Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test on the newborn","Serial total and unconjugated bilirubin measurements","Full blood count with reticulocyte count and blood film","Fetal middle cerebral artery Doppler to assess for fetal anaemia antenatally","Follow-up haemoglobin to detect late anaemia"],"redFlags":["Hydrops fetalis with severe anaemia and heart failure","Rapidly rising or very high unconjugated bilirubin threatening bilirubin encephalopathy","Jaundice appearing within the first 24 hours of life","Signs of acute bilirubin encephalopathy (lethargy, poor feeding, hypertonia, abnormal cry)","Severe or late anaemia requiring transfusion"],"prognosis":"With antenatal surveillance, intrauterine transfusion for severe fetal anaemia, and neonatal phototherapy or exchange transfusion, outcomes are generally good. Prophylactic anti-D has dramatically reduced severe Rh disease. Prognosis worsens if severe hyperbilirubinaemia progresses to kernicterus or if profound fetal anaemia and hydrops are untreated.","pitfalls":["Underestimating ABO disease because it can affect a first-born and cause significant jaundice","Failing to monitor bilirubin closely and intervene before it reaches neurotoxic levels","Overlooking late anaemia in the weeks after birth once early jaundice has settled","Missing an opportunity for anti-D prophylaxis in an at-risk mother","Attributing early jaundice to physiological causes when haemolysis is occurring"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"hemolytic_uremic_syndrome":{"name":"Haemolytic uraemic syndrome","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HUS","hemolytic uremic syndrome","atypical HUS","aHUS","STEC-HUS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A thrombotic microangiopathy defined by the triad of microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury","The typical form follows a Shiga-toxin-producing enteric infection (often E. coli O157:H7), classically preceded by bloody diarrhoea, and predominantly affects children","Atypical HUS results from dysregulated activation of the alternative complement pathway, is often familial, and tends to relapse and progress to end-stage renal disease","The blood film shows fragmented red cells (schistocytes) with a raised LDH, low haptoglobin, and a negative direct antiglobulin test, indicating mechanical (non-immune) haemolysis","Renal involvement dominates the clinical picture, distinguishing HUS from thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, in which neurological features and severe ADAMTS13 deficiency predominate","Measuring ADAMTS13 activity helps separate TTP (severely reduced) from HUS (usually normal or only modestly reduced), which is critical because treatments differ","Shiga-toxin-associated HUS is largely managed with meticulous supportive care and dialysis when needed, and antibiotics are generally avoided as they may worsen toxin release","Complement-mediated atypical HUS responds to terminal complement blockade, which has substantially improved renal and overall outcomes"],"pathophysiology":"HUS is a thrombotic microangiopathy in which endothelial injury triggers platelet-rich microthrombi in small vessels, especially the renal microvasculature. In Shiga-toxin-associated disease, toxin absorbed from the gut binds glomerular and endothelial cells, causing injury, platelet activation, and microvascular thrombosis. In atypical HUS, uncontrolled activation of the alternative complement pathway, from inherited or acquired defects in complement regulatory proteins, drives endothelial damage. The resulting microthrombi mechanically shear passing red cells (producing schistocytes and haemolysis), consume platelets (causing thrombocytopenia), and obstruct renal perfusion (causing acute kidney injury).","additionalDifferentials":["Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Severe pre-eclampsia / HELLP syndrome","Malignant hypertension causing microangiopathy","Systemic vasculitis or scleroderma renal crisis","Drug-induced thrombotic microangiopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film for schistocytes and thrombocytopenia","Haemolysis markers: LDH, haptoglobin, bilirubin, and a direct antiglobulin test (expected negative)","Renal function, urinalysis, and monitoring of urine output","Stool testing and culture for Shiga-toxin-producing organisms","ADAMTS13 activity to exclude thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Complement studies and genetic testing for regulatory protein defects in suspected atypical HUS"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive acute kidney injury with oliguria or anuria requiring urgent dialysis","Severe thrombocytopenia with active bleeding","Neurological deterioration raising concern for overlapping TTP or CNS involvement","Hyperkalaemia, fluid overload, or refractory hypertension as life-threatening complications of renal failure"],"prognosis":"Shiga-toxin-associated HUS in children often recovers with supportive care, though a minority sustain chronic kidney damage; atypical, complement-mediated HUS historically carried a high risk of end-stage renal disease and death, but outcomes have improved markedly with complement-blocking therapy.","pitfalls":["Confusing HUS with TTP and delaying the correct treatment; ADAMTS13 testing helps distinguish them","Giving antibiotics in Shiga-toxin-associated HUS, which may increase toxin release and worsen disease","Attributing thrombocytopenia and anaemia to sepsis or DIC without examining the film for schistocytes","Failing to consider atypical HUS in relapsing or familial disease and missing the opportunity for complement inhibition","Transfusing platelets reflexively, which can aggravate microvascular thrombosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"hemorrhoids":{"name":"Haemorrhoids","system":"GI / Anorectal / Surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hemorrhoids","piles","haemorrhoidal disease","internal haemorrhoids","external haemorrhoids"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically present with painless, bright red bleeding on defaecation, often coating the stool or dripping into the pan, with or without a prolapsing lump","Internal haemorrhoids arise above the dentate line and are usually painless, whereas external components below the dentate line are pain-sensitive and hurt when thrombosed","Classified by degree of prolapse: first degree bleed but do not prolapse, second degree prolapse and reduce spontaneously, third degree require manual reduction, and fourth degree remain irreducible","Contributing factors include chronic straining, constipation, a low-fibre diet, pregnancy, and raised intra-abdominal pressure","First-line management is conservative: increased dietary fibre and fluids, stool softeners, and avoidance of straining, which resolves many early cases","Office procedures such as rubber band ligation and injection sclerotherapy treat lower-grade internal haemorrhoids, while haemorrhoidectomy or stapled procedures are reserved for higher grades","Rectal bleeding must not be attributed to haemorrhoids without excluding colorectal cancer and other proximal pathology, especially in older patients or with alarm features","A thrombosed external haemorrhoid causes acute severe perianal pain and a tense tender lump and may be managed conservatively or by evacuation if presenting early"],"pathophysiology":"Haemorrhoids result from abnormal engorgement and downward displacement of the normal anal cushions, which are vascular submucosal structures that contribute to continence. Chronic straining, constipation, and raised intra-abdominal pressure disrupt the supporting connective tissue, allowing the cushions to enlarge, congest, and prolapse. The classical sites are the left lateral, right anterior, and right posterior positions. Bleeding is arteriovenous in origin, giving the characteristic bright red colour. Thrombosis of external components or prolapsed internal haemorrhoids produces acute pain, and long-standing prolapse can cause mucous discharge and pruritus.","additionalDifferentials":["Colorectal carcinoma","Anal fissure","Rectal polyp or prolapse","Inflammatory bowel disease (proctitis)","Anal or rectal cancer","Perianal haematoma","Diverticular or angiodysplastic bleeding"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Digital rectal examination and proctoscopy to visualise internal haemorrhoids","Rigid or flexible sigmoidoscopy to inspect the distal bowel","Colonoscopy in patients with alarm features, age over threshold, or unexplained bleeding to exclude malignancy","Full blood count if bleeding is chronic or significant to assess for anaemia"],"redFlags":["Rectal bleeding with weight loss, change in bowel habit, anaemia, or a mass suggesting colorectal cancer","Profuse or persistent bleeding causing symptomatic anaemia","Irreducible, strangulated, or gangrenous prolapsed haemorrhoids with severe pain","Perianal sepsis complicating thrombosed or ulcerated haemorrhoids"],"prognosis":"Most haemorrhoids respond to conservative measures or office procedures with good symptom control, though recurrence is possible. Surgical treatment of advanced disease is effective but may cause post-operative pain and, rarely, incontinence or stenosis.","pitfalls":["Attributing rectal bleeding to haemorrhoids without excluding colorectal malignancy","Missing an anal fissure as the cause of painful bleeding","Aggressive banding or surgery in patients with portal hypertension or coagulopathy","Overlooking thrombosis or strangulation as the cause of acute severe pain"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"hemothorax":{"name":"Haemothorax","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hemothorax","massive haemothorax","haemothorax"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A massive haemothorax is a circulation problem and a breathing problem at once, combining haemorrhagic shock with impaired ventilation","Suspect it with reduced breath sounds and dullness to percussion on the affected side in a shocked, injured patient","It is recognised during the breathing and circulation steps of the primary survey and treated in parallel with volume resuscitation","Treatment is chest drainage together with restoration of circulating volume, ideally with blood products","A large immediate drainage volume or continuing high hourly blood loss through the drain indicates the need for surgical exploration","Restore blood volume before or as the chest is drained, since drainage can unmask or worsen hypotension","Distinguish it from tension pneumothorax, which is hyperresonant rather than dull, as both cause shock with reduced breath sounds"],"pathophysiology":"Haemothorax is accumulation of blood in the pleural space, usually from injury to the chest wall vessels (such as intercostal or internal mammary arteries), the lung, or the great vessels. The pleural cavity can hold a large proportion of the circulating blood volume, so bleeding into it causes hypovolaemic shock. At the same time, the collected blood compresses the ipsilateral lung and can shift the mediastinum, impairing ventilation and, when large, venous return. Thus a massive haemothorax produces the dual insult of haemorrhage and respiratory compromise, and the blood must be evacuated while the circulation is restored.","additionalDifferentials":["Tension or simple pneumothorax","Pulmonary contusion","Large pleural effusion (non-traumatic)","Diaphragmatic rupture with herniation","Ruptured thoracic aorta"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph and thoracic ultrasound to detect pleural fluid","CT chest for definition when the patient is stable","Measurement of blood drained and ongoing hourly losses via the chest drain","Group and crossmatch with coagulation studies for transfusion","Arterial blood gas and haemodynamic monitoring"],"redFlags":["Massive haemothorax with haemorrhagic shock requiring immediate drainage and blood resuscitation","Large immediate drain output or persistent high hourly blood loss indicating a need for surgery","Hypotension worsening after drainage from unmasked hypovolaemia","Respiratory compromise from lung compression and mediastinal shift","Features suggesting great-vessel injury as the bleeding source"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the rate and source of bleeding and associated injuries. Prompt drainage with adequate transfusion allows recovery in many cases, while massive or ongoing haemorrhage that is not controlled surgically carries high mortality.","pitfalls":["Draining a massive haemothorax without simultaneously restoring blood volume","Confusing it with tension pneumothorax by not distinguishing dullness from hyperresonance","Failing to recognise ongoing bleeding that mandates surgical exploration","Using crystalloid alone rather than blood products in significant haemorrhage","Overlooking a great-vessel injury as the source"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"henoch_schonlein_purpura":{"name":"Henoch-Schonlein purpura (IgA vasculitis)","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HSP","IgA vasculitis","anaphylactoid purpura","Henoch-Schonlein syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common small-vessel vasculitis of childhood, typically affecting children 3-10 years, often following an upper respiratory infection.","The classic tetrad is palpable purpura (predominantly on the buttocks and lower limbs), arthritis/arthralgia, abdominal pain, and renal involvement.","The rash is non-thrombocytopenic palpable purpura, distinguishing it from bleeding disorders; platelet count and coagulation are normal.","Gastrointestinal involvement causes colicky abdominal pain and can be complicated by intussusception, which is typically ileo-ileal.","Renal involvement ranges from isolated microscopic haematuria to nephritis; it is the main determinant of long-term prognosis.","Diagnosis is clinical, supported by palpable purpura plus at least one of abdominal pain, arthritis, or renal involvement; biopsy shows IgA deposition when needed.","Most cases are self-limiting; management is largely supportive with analgesia and hydration, and corticosteroids are considered for severe abdominal or other significant organ involvement.","Prolonged urinalysis and blood pressure monitoring are needed after recovery to detect delayed or progressive nephritis."],"pathophysiology":"Henoch-Schonlein purpura is an immune-complex mediated small-vessel vasculitis characterised by deposition of IgA1-containing immune complexes in the walls of small vessels of the skin, gut, joints, and kidney. This triggers a leukocytoclastic vasculitis with neutrophilic infiltration and vessel wall inflammation, producing the palpable purpura and visceral manifestations. In the kidney, mesangial IgA deposition causes a glomerulonephritis histologically similar to IgA nephropathy. A preceding infection or other antigenic trigger is thought to initiate the abnormal IgA response in susceptible children.","additionalDifferentials":["Meningococcaemia / sepsis with purpura","Immune thrombocytopenia and other bleeding disorders","Haemolytic uraemic syndrome","Acute abdomen from other causes (e.g. appendicitis)","Other systemic vasculitides","Systemic lupus erythematosus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis for haematuria and proteinuria (repeated over follow-up)","Blood pressure measurement","Full blood count and coagulation studies to confirm normal platelets and exclude bleeding disorders","Renal function and serum albumin","Abdominal ultrasound if intussusception or significant gut involvement is suspected","Skin or renal biopsy showing IgA deposition in atypical or severe cases"],"redFlags":["Severe abdominal pain, bilious vomiting, or signs of intussusception or bowel ischaemia","Gastrointestinal bleeding","Hypertension, oliguria, heavy proteinuria, or rising creatinine indicating significant nephritis","Scrotal pain or swelling (testicular involvement mimicking torsion)","Purpura in an unwell, febrile, toxic child raising concern for meningococcaemia"],"prognosis":"Most children recover completely within weeks, though relapses can occur; long-term outcome is dictated by renal involvement, with a small proportion developing chronic kidney disease, so renal follow-up is essential.","pitfalls":["Confusing the purpura with a bleeding disorder and missing that platelets are normal","Failing to monitor urinalysis and blood pressure after the acute illness, missing delayed nephritis","Overlooking intussusception as a cause of worsening abdominal pain","Attributing all abdominal pain to HSP and missing a surgical abdomen"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"hepatocellular_carcinoma":{"name":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","system":"GI / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HCC","hepatoma","primary liver cancer"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AASLD & ACG","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The great majority arise in a background of cirrhosis or chronic liver disease, most often from chronic hepatitis B or C, alcohol-related liver disease, or metabolic (fatty) liver disease","Chronic hepatitis B can cause HCC even without established cirrhosis because of the virus's direct oncogenic effect","Surveillance of at-risk patients with periodic ultrasound (with or without alpha-fetoprotein) allows detection at an earlier, treatable stage","Diagnosis can often be made non-invasively on multiphase CT or MRI when a nodule shows the characteristic arterial-phase enhancement with portal/delayed venous washout in a cirrhotic liver","Alpha-fetoprotein supports the diagnosis and aids monitoring but is neither sufficiently sensitive nor specific to be used alone","Prognosis and treatment depend jointly on tumour burden, vascular invasion or spread, and the degree of underlying liver dysfunction, so staging systems incorporate liver function","Curative options for early disease include surgical resection, liver transplantation, and local ablation, while transarterial and systemic therapies are used for more advanced stages","New or worsening decompensation in a cirrhotic patient, such as sudden ascites or a bloody effusion, should prompt urgent evaluation for HCC and possible tumour rupture or vascular invasion","Portal vein tumour thrombus is an important adverse feature that changes staging and management"],"pathophysiology":"Hepatocellular carcinoma develops through chronic hepatocyte injury, inflammation, and regeneration, which over years produces cirrhosis and accumulating genetic and epigenetic damage. Dysplastic nodules progress to frank carcinoma. As tumours grow they recruit an arterial blood supply distinct from the normal portal-dominant hepatic perfusion, which underlies the arterial hyperenhancement and venous washout seen on imaging. The tumour tends to invade the portal and hepatic veins, promoting intrahepatic spread and portal vein thrombosis, and can metastasise to lung, bone, and lymph nodes. Chronic hepatitis B additionally drives carcinogenesis through viral DNA integration and direct oncogenic proteins, sometimes producing cancer in a non-cirrhotic liver.","additionalDifferentials":["Metastatic liver disease from another primary","Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma","Regenerative or dysplastic nodule in cirrhosis","Focal nodular hyperplasia or hepatic adenoma","Haemangioma","Hepatic abscess or focal fat"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Multiphase contrast-enhanced CT or MRI to assess enhancement pattern, tumour extent, and vascular invasion","Serum alpha-fetoprotein as an adjunct to diagnosis and for monitoring","Assessment of liver function and cirrhosis severity (e.g. Child-Pugh grading and portal hypertension)","Image-guided biopsy in selected cases where imaging is indeterminate","Staging for extrahepatic spread including chest imaging"],"redFlags":["New decompensation in a cirrhotic patient (rapid ascites, jaundice, or variceal bleeding) suggesting HCC or vascular invasion","Sudden severe abdominal pain with haemodynamic instability indicating tumour rupture and haemoperitoneum","Portal or hepatic vein tumour thrombus indicating advanced disease","Bloody ascitic fluid or a rapidly enlarging liver mass"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on stage and residual liver function. Early tumours treated with resection, transplantation, or ablation can achieve long-term survival, whereas advanced disease with vascular invasion, extrahepatic spread, or poor liver function has a poor prognosis. Underlying cirrhosis independently limits life expectancy and treatment options.","pitfalls":["Failing to enrol at-risk cirrhotic and chronic hepatitis B patients in surveillance, leading to late diagnosis","Relying on alpha-fetoprotein alone, which can be normal in HCC or raised in benign liver disease","Overlooking underlying liver dysfunction when planning aggressive treatment","Missing portal vein tumour thrombus, which alters staging and precludes some curative options"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AASLD/ACG guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"hereditary_angioedema":{"name":"Hereditary angioedema","system":"Immunology / Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HAE","C1 inhibitor deficiency","C1-INH deficiency angioedema"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Middleton's Allergy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["HAE causes recurrent, non-pruritic, non-pitting angioedema without urticaria, distinguishing it from mast-cell (histamine)-driven allergic angioedema","Attacks are bradykinin-mediated, so they do not respond to antihistamines, corticosteroids, or adrenaline, which is a key clue when swelling fails to improve with standard allergy treatment","Recurrent painful abdominal attacks from bowel-wall oedema can mimic a surgical abdomen and lead to unnecessary operations","Laryngeal involvement is the feared complication because progressive upper-airway oedema can cause asphyxiation and death","Attacks are commonly triggered by trauma (including dental and surgical procedures), infection, emotional stress, and oestrogens, and many begin in childhood or adolescence","Most cases are due to deficient (type 1) or dysfunctional (type 2) C1 inhibitor; a subgroup has normal C1 inhibitor with mutations such as in factor XII, so functional testing matters","A low C4 level is a useful screening abnormality; diagnosis is confirmed with C1-inhibitor antigenic level and function, and a low C1q helps identify acquired rather than hereditary disease","Management has three arms: treatment of acute attacks, short-term prophylaxis before triggering procedures, and long-term prophylaxis to reduce attack frequency","Targeted therapies act on the bradykinin pathway, including C1-inhibitor replacement, a bradykinin B2-receptor antagonist, and a plasma kallikrein inhibitor; ACE inhibitors and oestrogen-containing agents should be avoided as they worsen disease"],"pathophysiology":"Hereditary angioedema most often results from autosomal dominant deficiency or dysfunction of C1 inhibitor (SERPING1 mutations). C1 inhibitor normally restrains the contact (kallikrein-kinin), complement, and fibrinolytic systems. Its deficiency permits unchecked activation of plasma kallikrein, which cleaves high-molecular-weight kininogen to generate excess bradykinin. Bradykinin acting on B2 receptors increases vascular permeability, producing the characteristic submucosal and subcutaneous oedema. A minority of patients have HAE with normal C1 inhibitor caused by mutations that enhance contact-system activation (e.g. factor XII, plasminogen, angiopoietin-1). Because the mechanism is kinin- rather than histamine-driven, mast-cell-directed therapies are ineffective.","additionalDifferentials":["Acquired C1-inhibitor deficiency (often with lymphoproliferative or autoimmune disease, low C1q)","ACE-inhibitor-induced bradykinin-mediated angioedema","Allergic/histaminergic angioedema with or without urticaria","Idiopathic angioedema","Anaphylaxis with airway involvement","Superior vena cava obstruction or localised infection causing facial swelling"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum C4 level as an initial screen (typically low during and between attacks)","C1-inhibitor antigenic concentration and functional activity","C1q level to help distinguish acquired from hereditary disease","Genetic testing (e.g. SERPING1, factor XII) in atypical or normal-C1-inhibitor cases","Family history assessment and screening of at-risk relatives"],"redFlags":["Laryngeal or tongue swelling, voice change, stridor, or difficulty swallowing indicating life-threatening airway obstruction requiring urgent bradykinin-targeted therapy and airway management","Severe abdominal attacks risking misdiagnosis as an acute surgical abdomen","Swelling unresponsive to adrenaline, antihistamines, and corticosteroids","Rapid progression of facial or oropharyngeal oedema, particularly after dental or surgical procedures"],"prognosis":"With modern on-demand and prophylactic therapy targeting the bradykinin pathway, most patients achieve good disease control and near-normal life expectancy. Untreated or unrecognised laryngeal attacks, however, carry a substantial risk of death from asphyxiation.","pitfalls":["Treating attacks as allergic and relying on antihistamines, steroids, or adrenaline, which are ineffective","Failing to recognise abdominal attacks and proceeding to unnecessary surgery","Prescribing ACE inhibitors or oestrogen-containing contraceptives, which precipitate attacks","Omitting short-term prophylaxis before dental, surgical, or airway procedures"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"hereditary_spherocytosis":{"name":"Hereditary spherocytosis","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HS","hereditary spherocytosis","spherocytosis","congenital spherocytic anaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An inherited haemolytic anaemia caused by defects in red cell membrane structural proteins, producing rigid, spherical red cells that are trapped and destroyed in the spleen","The classic triad is anaemia, jaundice, and splenomegaly, with severity ranging from an incidental finding to transfusion-dependent disease","Most cases follow autosomal dominant inheritance, and a positive family history is common, though sporadic mutations also occur","The blood film shows spherocytes lacking central pallor, with a raised reticulocyte count and increased mean corpuscular haemoglobin concentration","Chronic haemolysis predisposes to pigment (bilirubin) gallstones and to neonatal jaundice; folate demands are increased","Aplastic crisis from parvovirus B19 infection can cause an abrupt, dangerous fall in haemoglobin by shutting down red cell production","Diagnosis is supported by a negative direct antiglobulin test (distinguishing it from autoimmune haemolysis) together with confirmatory tests such as the EMA-binding flow cytometry assay or osmotic fragility","Folate supplementation supports haemolysis, and splenectomy reduces red cell destruction in more severe disease but raises the lifelong risk of encapsulated-organism sepsis, so vaccination and counselling are essential"],"pathophysiology":"Hereditary spherocytosis results from inherited defects in proteins that anchor the red cell membrane to its underlying cytoskeleton, including spectrin, ankyrin, band 3, and protein 4.2. Weakening of these vertical linkages allows portions of the lipid bilayer to be lost, so the cell reduces its surface-area-to-volume ratio and becomes spherical rather than biconcave. These spherocytes are poorly deformable and cannot easily traverse the narrow cords of the splenic red pulp, where they are retained and prematurely destroyed by macrophages. The resulting extravascular haemolysis produces anaemia, unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with jaundice, splenomegaly, and a compensatory reticulocytosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia (spherocytes with positive direct antiglobulin test)","Other hereditary red cell membrane disorders (elliptocytosis, pyropoikilocytosis)","G6PD deficiency and other enzymopathies","Haemoglobinopathies causing haemolysis","Clostridial or other causes of acute haemolysis","Neonatal jaundice of other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count, reticulocyte count, and blood film showing spherocytes","Bilirubin, LDH, and haptoglobin to confirm haemolysis","Direct antiglobulin test to exclude immune-mediated haemolysis","EMA-binding flow cytometry and/or osmotic fragility testing","Family studies and, when needed, molecular analysis of membrane protein genes","Ultrasound for gallstones and monitoring for aplastic crisis during parvovirus infection"],"redFlags":["Aplastic crisis (often parvovirus B19) with a rapid, severe fall in haemoglobin and inappropriately low reticulocytes","Acute haemolytic crisis with worsening anaemia and jaundice","Overwhelming post-splenectomy sepsis from encapsulated organisms","Symptomatic gallstone disease such as biliary obstruction or cholangitis"],"prognosis":"With supportive care and, where indicated, splenectomy, most patients have a good long-term outlook and near-normal life expectancy; the main hazards are aplastic and haemolytic crises, gallstone complications, and, after splenectomy, serious infection.","pitfalls":["Mistaking hereditary spherocytosis for autoimmune haemolytic anaemia by not performing a direct antiglobulin test","Overlooking an aplastic crisis by not checking the reticulocyte count when haemoglobin drops suddenly","Forgetting folate supplementation in chronic haemolysis","Proceeding to splenectomy without appropriate vaccination and infection counselling","Missing gallstones as a cause of abdominal pain in a chronically haemolysing patient"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"heritable_disorders_of_connective_tissue":{"name":"Heritable Disorders of Connective Tissue","system":"Musculoskeletal / Genetic (multisystem connective tissue)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Osteogenesis imperfecta","Ehlers-Danlos syndromes","Marfan syndrome","Loeys-Dietz syndrome","Chondrodysplasias","Heritable thoracic aortic aneurysm disease (HTAD)"],"chapter":425,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3319","clinicalPearls":["This is an umbrella group of several hundred genetic disorders affecting bone, cartilage, skin, tendons and other connective tissues; mutations in over 550 genes underlie 771 defined skeletal disorders, so classification by either clinical phenotype or causative gene remains a continuing challenge.","Connective tissue strength comes from fibrillar collagens (types I, II, III) whose alpha chains have a repeating Gly-X-Y sequence; glycine, the smallest amino acid, must occupy every third position, so substitution of glycine by a bulkier residue is a frequent disease-causing mechanism.","Osteogenesis imperfecta (OI) is dominated by bone fragility, deformity and growth deficiency; ~80-85% of cases stem from heterozygous COL1A1/COL1A2 mutations, and secondary features include blue sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, hearing loss, basilar invagination and cardiac-valve abnormalities.","OI bone is paradoxically hypermineralized: DXA returns a reduced density value, yet quantitative backscattered electron imaging and 3D CT show the bone is over-mineralized, making it both weak and brittle so that it snaps like chalk.","Null COL1A1 mutations (about half-normal but structurally normal collagen) produce mild type I OI, whereas structural glycine-substitution defects produce the more severe types II-IV; type II causes in-utero fractures and perinatal death.","The Ehlers-Danlos syndromes (EDS) share soft hyperextensible skin, abnormal wound healing with atrophic/papyraceous scarring, easy bruising and joint hypermobility; the current classification recognizes 13 clinical types across roughly 20 genes.","Vascular EDS (COL3A1, type III collagen) is the dangerous form: medium- and large-vessel arterial rupture, sigmoid colon perforation without prior colon disease, and uterine rupture in the third trimester can occur, often in skin that is thin and translucent rather than velvety hyperextensible.","Classic EDS is most often due to COL5A1/COL5A2 (type V collagen) mutations in ~90% of patients; hypermobile EDS has no identified gene, so it remains a clinical diagnosis of exclusion.","Marfan syndrome (MFS) classically combines a tall marfanoid habitus with arachnodactyly, ectopia lentis (lens dislocation, ~60%), and aortic root dilation at the sinuses of Valsalva; ~95% of cases are due to fibrillin-1 (FBN1) mutations.","FBN1 defects impair not only microfibril structure but also TGF-beta bioavailability; reduced/altered fibrillin-1 releases sequestered TGF-beta in the aortic wall, driving remodeling, excess MMP-2/MMP-9, and the aneurysmal phenotype.","Loeys-Dietz syndrome (TGFBR1/2, TGFB2/3, SMAD2/3) overlaps with MFS but is distinguished by arterial tortuosity and aneurysms beyond the aortic root, hypertelorism, and cleft palate or bifid uvula; ectopia lentis is typically absent.","Achondroplasia, the common short-limb dwarfism, is caused by a gain-of-function FGFR3 mutation; most cases are sporadic, and the homozygous form is more severe and may be lethal in the neonate."],"pathophysiology":"Connective tissues (skin, bone, cartilage, ligament, tendon, vessel wall) are built from an extracellular matrix of fibrillar collagens, proteoglycans, elastin/fibrillin microfibrils and noncollagenous glycoproteins. Fibrillar collagen monomers are triple helices of alpha chains with a repeating Gly-X-Y motif; glycine at every third position is sterically required, and proline/hydroxyproline confer rigidity. Procollagen undergoes extensive post-translational processing (prolyl/lysyl hydroxylation requiring ascorbate, glycosylation, triple-helix folding aided by chaperones, secretion, N- and C-propeptide cleavage, then self-assembly into fibrils stabilized by lysyl-oxidase cross-links). Mutations may be quantitative (null alleles giving reduced normal collagen, generally milder) or structural (glycine substitutions, splice defects, charged/branched residue substitutions in X/Y positions) that disrupt folding, fibril assembly and mineralization. OI arises mainly from dominant type I collagen defects, plus rarer recessive defects in collagen modification, processing, folding/cross-linking and osteoblast differentiation. EDS reflects defects in collagen types I/III/V, in processing enzymes (ADAMTS2), cross-linking (PLOD1/lysyl hydroxylase 1), glycosaminoglycan biosynthesis, or matrix glycoproteins (tenascin X). Marfan/HTAD reflects fibrillin-1 microfibril dysfunction with secondary dysregulation of TGF-beta signaling; Loeys-Dietz reflects primary mutations in the TGF-beta pathway. Chondrodysplasias reflect intrinsic cartilage/growth-plate abnormalities (e.g., FGFR3 gain-of-function in achondroplasia, COL2A1 in type II collagenopathies).","additionalDifferentials":["Postmenopausal or secondary osteoporosis (can mimic mild adult-onset OI)","Battered child syndrome / non-accidental injury (versus OI fractures in infants)","Nutritional deficiencies (e.g., rickets) and hypophosphatasia","Other chondrodysplasias and skeletal dysplasias presenting with short stature","Homocystinuria (marfanoid habitus with ectopia lentis - lens dislocation typically downward)","Mucopolysaccharidoses and other lysosomal storage disorders","Acquired/secondary causes of joint hypermobility distinct from hereditary EDS"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Osteoporosis and osteopenia of other cause","Acromegaly (paw-like hand/foot enlargement of primary hypertrophic osteopathy can mimic it)","Secondary hypertrophic osteoarthropathy from pulmonary disease","Osteoarthritis (mild adult chondrodysplasia can resemble primary generalized OA)","Homocystinuria mimicking Marfan syndrome","Non-syndromic familial thoracic aortic aneurysm versus syndromic HTAD"],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted candidate-gene sequencing, multigene skeletal/EDS/HTAD panels, or whole-exome sequencing for molecular diagnosis","Skin fibroblast culture biochemistry for collagen synthesis/structure in selected cases","DXA bone densitometry (note it underestimates true mineral in OI; quantitative backscattered electron imaging or 3D CT reveals hypermineralization)","Skeletal radiographs (Wormian skull bones, popcorn metaphyses, beaded ribs, undertubulated long bones in OI)","Echocardiography for aortic root and valve assessment (MFS, LDS, and adults with OI)","Slit-lamp ophthalmologic examination for ectopia lentis in suspected MFS","Plasma amino acid analysis to exclude homocystinuria in marfanoid patients","Spirometry/pulmonary function testing in moderate-to-severe OI","Bone histomorphometry in selected diagnostically difficult OI cases"],"redFlags":["Acute severe chest, back or abdominal pain in MFS/LDS or vascular EDS - possible aortic dissection or arterial rupture","Rapidly enlarging aortic root or sinus of Valsalva dilation on echocardiography","Spontaneous sigmoid colon perforation or organ rupture in vascular EDS","Uterine rupture in the third trimester (vascular EDS) and obstetric complications","Recurrent or in-utero fractures with progressive deformity (severe/lethal OI)","Progressive basilar invagination/impression with neurologic compromise in severe OI","Declining pulmonary function or recurrent pneumonia in severe OI (leading cause of death)"],"prognosis":"Highly variable, even within a single disorder type and among family members carrying the same mutation. In OI, type I is mild and fractures often decline after puberty, while type II is lethal in the perinatal period; pulmonary disease is the leading cause of death in OI. In Marfan and Loeys-Dietz syndromes, cardiovascular disease (aortic dilation, dissection, rupture; mitral valve disease) is the major source of morbidity and mortality, and dilation rate is unpredictable and accelerated by exertion, emotional stress and pregnancy. Vascular EDS carries serious risk from arterial, intestinal and uterine rupture. Milder phenotypes across these disorders may go undiagnosed and have near-normal life expectancy.","pitfalls":["DXA underestimates true bone mineral in OI - a low DXA value coexists with actual hypermineralization, so bone is brittle despite the reading.","Marfan-like presentation may actually be homocystinuria; always check plasma amino acids before settling on MFS.","Hypermobile EDS has no confirmed gene; it is a clinical diagnosis of exclusion and cannot be confirmed by genetic testing.","Same clinical OI subtype may be assigned different type labels over a patient's lifetime or among family members under clinical (versus genetic) classification.","Blue sclerae are not specific to OI - they occur in EDS, MFS and even healthy individuals; conversely severe neonatal OI with white sclerae suggests recessive prolyl 3-hydroxylation defects.","In infants, OI fractures may be mistaken for non-accidental injury (battered child syndrome).","Tissues in MFS/LDS and EDS are friable, complicating surgical repair."],"severityClassification":"Osteogenesis imperfecta is classified by the Sillence system (clinical/radiologic types I-IV: type I mildest with normal-quality but reduced collagen; type II perinatal lethal; type III progressively deforming; type IV mild-to-moderate) extended to genetic types V-XXII grouped by causative gene and mechanism. Ehlers-Danlos syndromes are classified into 13 clinical types (e.g., classic, vascular, hypermobile, arthrochalasia, dermatosparaxis, kyphoscoliotic) by clinical features, inheritance and molecular defect. Marfan syndrome diagnosis uses the revised Ghent nosology, emphasizing aortic root dilation/dissection and ectopia lentis as cardinal features plus a systemic score. Heritable thoracic aortic disease is divided into syndromic and nonsyndromic (familial thoracic aortic aneurysm) forms.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3319","Harrison 22e p.3324","Harrison 22e p.3327","Harrison 22e p.3331"],"crossLinks":[]},"herpes_simplex":{"name":"Herpes Simplex Virus Infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HSV","HSV-1","HSV-2"],"chapter":197,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1050, 1495; encephalitis discussion p.747","clinicalPearls":["Primary HSV infection produces erythema followed by hallmark painful grouped vesicles that may evolve into pustules and ulcerate, especially on mucosal surfaces; lesions appear at the site of inoculation (Harrison p.~1010, App. A1).","HSV-1 classically causes orolabial disease (cold sores, herpes labialis) and HSV-2 causes genital lesions, but either type can infect either site (Harrison p.1050).","Non-genital cutaneous presentations include herpes gladiatorum (head and neck of young wrestlers) and herpetic whitlow (digits of health care workers) (Harrison p.1050).","Primary acute herpetic gingivostomatitis (usually HSV-1) presents with labial vesicles that rupture and crust plus intraoral vesicles that quickly ulcerate; it is extremely painful, with acute gingivitis, fever, malaise, foul odor, and cervical lymphadenopathy, occurring primarily in infants, children, and young adults (Harrison p.~325, Table 38-1).","Recurrent disease is milder than primary infection, and there is typically no fever in recurrent infection; herpes labialis recurrences typically do not involve the oral mucosa (Harrison App. A1).","Sexually transmitted HSV causes most genital ulcer disease worldwide, and there is a documented positive-feedback loop between HSV and HIV transmission (Harrison p.1096).","Although the HSV–HIV epidemiologic link is consistent, randomized trials of antiviral suppression of HSV showed no protective effect against HIV acquisition or transmission (Harrison p.1096)."],"pathophysiology":"HSV-1 and HSV-2 are neurotropic DNA herpesviruses that establish lifelong latency in sensory ganglia after primary mucocutaneous infection; periodic reactivation produces recurrent localized disease (e.g., recurrent herpes labialis with HSV-1 and herpes genitalis with HSV-2). Virus enters and replicates within the epidermis at the site of inoculation. In HSV encephalitis there is characteristic unilateral temporal-lobe involvement. The virus can disseminate, particularly in the setting of disrupted skin (eczema herpeticum) or impaired cell-mediated immunity; visceral dissemination may occur even with only localized mucocutaneous disease (Harrison pp.1050, 1010 App. A1, p.747).","additionalDifferentials":["Varicella-zoster virus (chickenpox/shingles) for vesicular eruptions","Coxsackievirus A16 (hand-foot-and-mouth disease)","Aphthous ulcers (recurrent oral ulceration)","Other causes of genital ulcer disease (e.g., syphilis, chancroid) in genital HSV"],"infectionMimics":["Varicella-zoster virus infection (vesicular lesions are individually similar to HSV; Tzanck smear cannot distinguish them)","Reactive infectious mucocutaneous eruption (RIME) due to Mycoplasma/other respiratory infections, which mimics mucositis seen with HSV-associated erythema multiforme","Disseminated VZV versus disseminated HSV (individual lesions similar for VZV and HSV)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Erythema multiforme major / Stevens-Johnson syndrome / toxic epidermal necrolysis (HSV is itself a common trigger of EM, but drug-induced SJS/TEN must be distinguished)","Pemphigus vulgaris (blistering eruption with mucositis)","Aphthous stomatitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["PCR of CSF for HSV in suspected encephalitis (diagnostic test of choice; may be falsely negative early in the course of disease) (Harrison p.747)","Tzanck smear of an early unroofed vesicle (Giemsa/Wright stain): multinucleated epithelial giant cells suggest HSV or VZV, but cannot identify the specific virus (Harrison p.~480)","Viral culture, immunofluorescence microscopy, or genetic/PCR testing to identify the specific virus type (Harrison p.~480)","In FUO with headache, CSF microbiologic examination including HSV (especially type 2) (Harrison p.~149)"],"redFlags":["Fever with decreased level of consciousness and unilateral temporal-lobe involvement on imaging suggests HSV encephalitis - start empiric aciclovir promptly because CSF PCR may be falsely negative early (Harrison p.747)","Disseminated/visceral HSV in immunocompromised or neonatal patients; HSV can disseminate viscerally even with only localized mucocutaneous disease","Eczema herpeticum (HSV dissemination in eczematous skin)","Neonatal HSV disease (skin lesions are diagnostically helpful when present but may be absent)"],"prognosis":"Primary herpetic gingivostomatitis heals spontaneously in 10-14 days; lesions persisting beyond 3 weeks (unless secondarily infected) are not due to primary HSV infection. Recurrent disease is generally milder than primary infection. Approximately 25% of patients with herpes simplex encephalitis subsequently develop an autoimmune (anti-NMDAR) encephalitis weeks after the viral infection resolves - in children often with choreoathetosis and in adults with cognitive/psychiatric symptoms (Harrison pp.325, 821).","pitfalls":["CSF HSV PCR may be falsely negative early in the course of encephalitis, so a negative early result does not exclude the diagnosis and should not delay empiric aciclovir (Harrison p.747)","Tzanck smear cannot distinguish HSV from VZV; type-specific identification requires culture, immunofluorescence, or genetic testing (Harrison p.~480)","HSV visceral dissemination can occur with only localized mucocutaneous disease, so absence of widespread skin lesions does not exclude serious systemic infection (Harrison App. A1)","Oral lesions lasting more than 3 weeks should prompt consideration of an alternative diagnosis rather than primary HSV (Harrison Table 38-1)"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Ch. 197 (Herpes Simplex Virus Infections), p.1495","Harrison's 22e, Ch. 134 (Skin and Soft Tissue Infections), p.1050","Harrison's 22e, Ch. 38 (Oral Manifestations of Disease), Table 38-1","Harrison's 22e, encephalitis in immunocompromised hosts (HSV encephalitis), p.747","Harrison's 22e, Ch. 141 (Sexually Transmitted Infections: Overview and Clinical Approach), p.1096"],"crossLinks":[]},"herpes_simplex_keratitis":{"name":"Herpes simplex keratitis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HSV keratitis","herpetic keratitis","dendritic keratitis","HSK"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with a red, painful, watery eye, photophobia, and blurred vision, often preceded by mild irritation","The classic sign is a branching dendritic epithelial ulcer with terminal end-bulbs that stains with fluorescein, with the ulcer bed highlighted by rose bengal or lissamine green","Reduced corneal sensation is a valuable clue to a herpetic cause","Disease may be epithelial (active viral replication), stromal (immune-mediated, with the risk of scarring and neovascularisation), or endothelial (disciform keratitis)","Topical corticosteroids are contraindicated in active epithelial (dendritic) disease as they promote unchecked viral replication and can enlarge the ulcer into a geographic form","Epithelial disease is treated with topical or oral antiviral therapy; stromal and endothelial immune disease requires cautious steroids under antiviral cover and specialist supervision","Recurrences are common because the virus remains latent in the trigeminal ganglion; repeated episodes cause cumulative scarring and neurotrophic changes","Long-term oral antiviral prophylaxis reduces recurrence in patients with frequent episodes"],"pathophysiology":"Herpes simplex keratitis is caused by herpes simplex virus, usually type 1, which after primary infection establishes latency in the trigeminal ganglion. Reactivation, precipitated by stress, illness, ultraviolet light, or immunosuppression, sends the virus along sensory nerves to the cornea. Active viral replication in the epithelium produces the dendritic ulcer. Persisting viral antigen and the host immune response drive stromal inflammation (immune stromal keratitis) and endothelial disease (disciform keratitis), which can cause oedema, scarring, neovascularisation, and thinning. Repeated episodes and antiviral toxicity contribute to reduced corneal sensation and a neurotrophic cornea.","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes zoster ophthalmicus with pseudodendrites","Acanthamoeba keratitis (especially in contact lens wearers)","Bacterial or fungal keratitis","Recurrent corneal erosion","Healing epithelial defect with a pseudodendrite","Thygeson superficial punctate keratitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination with fluorescein and rose bengal or lissamine green staining","Testing of corneal sensation before instilling anaesthetic","Corneal scrape for viral PCR or culture in atypical or unclear cases","Assessment of stromal involvement, endothelial oedema, and intraocular pressure","Consideration of immune status in severe or bilateral disease"],"redFlags":["A dendritic (or geographic) ulcer, which mandates avoidance of topical steroids in active epithelial disease","Worsening or enlarging ulcer after steroid exposure suggesting steroid-driven progression","Stromal necrosis, thinning, or a neurotrophic non-healing defect threatening perforation","Deep central scarring or dense keratitis causing significant visual loss","Signs of secondary microbial superinfection"],"prognosis":"Isolated epithelial disease usually heals with antiviral treatment, but recurrent stromal disease can cause progressive scarring, neovascularisation, and neurotrophic complications with permanent visual loss; prophylaxis improves long-term outcomes.","pitfalls":["Prescribing topical steroids for an undiagnosed red eye and worsening a dendritic ulcer","Mistaking a herpetic dendrite for a healing abrasion or zoster pseudodendrite","Overlooking reduced corneal sensation as a diagnostic clue","Failing to consider prophylaxis in patients with frequent recurrences"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"herpes_zoster_ophthalmicus":{"name":"Herpes zoster ophthalmicus","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HZO","ophthalmic shingles","ophthalmic zoster"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from reactivation of varicella-zoster virus in the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve, producing a painful vesicular rash respecting the midline","Hutchinson sign, vesicles at the tip or side of the nose (nasociliary nerve involvement), predicts a higher likelihood of ocular complications","Ocular manifestations are wide-ranging and include conjunctivitis, epithelial pseudodendrites, stromal and disciform keratitis, uveitis, scleritis, raised intraocular pressure, and, less commonly, retinitis and optic neuritis","Corneal sensation is frequently reduced, predisposing to neurotrophic keratopathy and persistent epithelial defects","Prompt systemic antiviral therapy, ideally started early in the rash, reduces the severity and frequency of ocular complications","Zoster pseudodendrites are raised mucous plaques without true end-bulbs, distinguishing them from the ulcerated dendrites of herpes simplex","Post-herpetic neuralgia is a common and debilitating sequela, and zoster in a young or immunocompromised patient should prompt evaluation for underlying immunosuppression"],"pathophysiology":"Herpes zoster ophthalmicus is caused by reactivation of latent varicella-zoster virus residing in the trigeminal ganglion after childhood chickenpox. Declining cell-mediated immunity, with age or immunosuppression, allows the virus to travel down the ophthalmic division, causing a dermatomal vesicular eruption and neuritis. Direct viral cytopathic effect, immune-mediated inflammation, and an occlusive vasculitis of ocular vessels together produce the diverse anterior and posterior segment complications. Sensory nerve damage leads to reduced corneal sensation, neurotrophic keratopathy, and chronic neuropathic pain (post-herpetic neuralgia).","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex keratitis and dermatitis","Bacterial preseptal or orbital cellulitis","Contact or allergic dermatitis of the periocular skin","Impetigo","Other causes of acute anterior uveitis or keratitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination to identify keratitis, pseudodendrites, uveitis, and scleritis","Intraocular pressure measurement, as secondary pressure rise is common","Assessment of corneal sensation","Dilated fundus examination to exclude retinitis or optic nerve involvement","Evaluation for immunosuppression (including HIV) in young or atypical patients","Viral PCR of a lesion or ocular sample in uncertain cases"],"redFlags":["Hutchinson sign indicating high risk of sight-threatening ocular involvement","Acute retinal necrosis or optic neuritis causing rapid visual loss","Neurotrophic keratopathy with a non-healing epithelial defect risking perforation","Uncontrolled raised intraocular pressure","Zoster in a young or immunocompromised patient requiring evaluation of immune status","Signs of orbital or intracranial extension"],"prognosis":"Early antiviral treatment improves outcomes, but ocular involvement can become chronic and relapsing, with neurotrophic keratopathy, uveitis, glaucoma, and post-herpetic neuralgia causing long-term morbidity and potential visual loss.","pitfalls":["Delaying antiviral therapy and increasing the risk and severity of ocular complications","Failing to examine the eye in a patient with a periocular zoster rash","Mistaking zoster pseudodendrites for herpes simplex dendrites","Overlooking reduced corneal sensation and evolving neurotrophic disease","Not screening for underlying immunosuppression in young patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"hidradenitis_suppurativa":{"name":"Hidradenitis suppurativa","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acne inversa","HS","Verneuil's disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic, recurrent, inflammatory disease of the apocrine-gland-bearing flexures: axillae, groin, perineum, buttocks, and inframammary folds","Presents with painful, deep-seated inflammatory nodules, abscesses, discharging sinus tracts, and eventually rope-like scarring","Double-ended (bridged) comedones in affected areas are a helpful diagnostic clue","It is a primarily inflammatory, not infective, disease, though secondary bacterial colonisation and infection occur; recurrent painful lesions in typical sites over time define it","Strongly associated with obesity, smoking, and metabolic syndrome, and weight loss and smoking cessation are important management measures","Severity guides treatment: topical and oral antibacterials for anti-inflammatory effect in milder disease, and biologic immunomodulators, hormonal therapy, or surgery for moderate to severe disease","Definitive control of extensive or recurrent disease often requires wide surgical excision of affected tissue rather than incision and drainage alone","The condition causes considerable pain, malodour, and psychological distress, and quality of life should be actively assessed"],"pathophysiology":"Hidradenitis suppurativa is a chronic inflammatory disorder centred on the hair follicle in apocrine-bearing skin. The primary event is follicular occlusion by hyperkeratosis, leading to follicular dilatation, rupture, and release of keratin and bacteria into the surrounding dermis. This provokes an intense inflammatory response with nodules and abscesses; recurrent inflammation drives formation of epithelialised sinus tracts and progressive scarring. Genetic predisposition, dysregulated innate immunity, hormonal influences, obesity, and smoking all contribute. Although bacteria colonise lesions and secondary infection occurs, the disease is fundamentally inflammatory rather than a primary infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Furunculosis or recurrent bacterial abscesses","Crohn's disease with cutaneous or perianal involvement","Pilonidal disease","Cutaneous tuberculosis or actinomycosis in atypical sinus-forming disease","Epidermoid or pilonidal cysts","Lymphogranuloma venereum in the anogenital region"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on characteristic lesions, sites, and recurrence","Bacterial swabs of discharging lesions when secondary infection is suspected","Imaging such as ultrasound or MRI to map sinus tracts before surgery in extensive disease","Screening for associated metabolic disease, including glucose and lipids","Assessment for inflammatory bowel disease when gastrointestinal symptoms coexist"],"redFlags":["Severe systemic infection or sepsis arising from deep abscesses","Long-standing chronic sinuses with new induration or non-healing ulceration, raising concern for squamous cell carcinoma","Extensive scarring causing contractures or restricted limb movement","Severe unremitting pain and profound impairment of quality of life"],"prognosis":"Hidradenitis suppurativa is a chronic, often progressive disease with a relapsing course; early recognition and treatment can limit scarring, but advanced disease causes substantial morbidity, and long-standing lesions carry a small risk of malignant transformation.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating flares as simple abscesses with incision and drainage while missing the underlying chronic disease","Relying solely on antibacterials and overlooking the inflammatory nature requiring anti-inflammatory or surgical management","Neglecting smoking cessation and weight management as core interventions","Failing to consider squamous cell carcinoma in chronic non-healing lesions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"hip_fracture":{"name":"Hip fracture","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["proximal femoral fracture","fractured neck of femur","femoral neck fracture","intertrochanteric fracture","NOF fracture"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically follows a low-energy fall in an older, often osteoporotic patient; the leg is characteristically shortened and externally rotated with pain on any movement","Fractures are broadly divided into intracapsular (subcapital/transcervical) and extracapsular (intertrochanteric and subtrochanteric), a distinction that governs both blood supply risk and treatment","The femoral head's blood supply runs retrograde up the neck, so displaced intracapsular fractures carry a high risk of avascular necrosis and non-union","Displaced intracapsular fractures in older patients are usually managed with arthroplasty (hemiarthroplasty or total hip replacement), whereas undisplaced or young patients' fractures are fixed to preserve the head","Extracapsular fractures retain a good blood supply and are typically treated with internal fixation such as a dynamic hip screw or intramedullary device","Early surgery (generally within about a day or two) and rapid mobilisation reduce complications; prolonged bed rest causes pressure sores, chest infection, and venous thromboembolism","A minority present with subtle or occult fractures and a normal-appearing radiograph; persistent inability to weight-bear warrants MRI or CT","Outcome depends heavily on comorbidity and pre-injury mobility; orthogeriatric co-management and bone-health assessment for future fracture prevention are integral"],"pathophysiology":"Hip fractures usually result from a fall onto the greater trochanter in bone weakened by osteoporosis, though high-energy trauma or pathological lesions can cause them at any age. The proximal femur is supplied largely by the medial femoral circumflex artery via retinacular vessels ascending the femoral neck beneath the capsule. Intracapsular fractures may tear these vessels, threatening viability of the femoral head and predisposing to avascular necrosis and non-union, especially when displaced. Extracapsular fractures occur through well-vascularised cancellous bone and heal reliably but can bleed substantially. Immobility after fracture drives systemic decompensation in frail patients.","additionalDifferentials":["Pubic ramus or acetabular fracture","Greater trochanteric contusion or avulsion","Trochanteric bursitis or gluteal tendinopathy","Pathological fracture from metastasis or myeloma","Osteoarthritis of the hip with acute-on-chronic pain","Referred pain from lumbar spine"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anteroposterior pelvis and lateral hip radiographs as first-line imaging","MRI (or CT) when a fracture is suspected clinically despite normal radiographs (occult fracture)","Full blood count, renal function and electrolytes, group and save/crossmatch as part of preoperative workup","ECG and assessment of cardiopulmonary fitness for anaesthesia","Bone mineral density assessment and metabolic bone screen for secondary fracture prevention"],"redFlags":["Signs of significant blood loss or haemodynamic instability, particularly with subtrochanteric fractures","Pathological fracture features (bone destruction, minimal trauma in a younger patient) suggesting malignancy","Open fracture or associated neurovascular compromise of the limb","Prolonged surgical delay increasing mortality and complication risk in the frail elderly"],"prognosis":"Hip fracture in the elderly carries substantial morbidity and a significant one-year mortality driven by comorbidity; prompt surgery, early mobilisation, orthogeriatric care, and osteoporosis treatment improve functional recovery, though many patients do not regain full pre-injury independence.","pitfalls":["Dismissing a hip fracture because the initial radiograph looks normal, missing an occult fracture","Delaying surgery unnecessarily for over-investigation of stable comorbidities","Choosing internal fixation for a displaced intracapsular fracture in an older patient, risking avascular necrosis and reoperation","Overlooking underlying osteoporosis and failing to institute secondary prevention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"hirschsprung_disease":{"name":"Hirschsprung Disease","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["congenital aganglionic megacolon","aganglionosis","Hirschsprung's disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by absence of ganglion cells in the distal bowel, producing a tonically contracted, non-relaxing aganglionic segment and functional obstruction.","Most neonates present with delayed passage of meconium beyond 48 hours, abdominal distension, and bilious vomiting.","The aganglionic segment extends proximally from the anus for a variable distance; short-segment disease confined to the rectosigmoid is most common and it is more frequent in boys.","Associated with Down syndrome and other congenital conditions, and there can be a positive family history.","Contrast enema may show a transition zone between the narrow distal aganglionic bowel and dilated proximal colon, but a normal study does not exclude the diagnosis.","Anorectal manometry shows failure of the internal anal sphincter to relax with rectal distension.","Rectal suction biopsy demonstrating absent ganglion cells with hypertrophied nerve trunks is the diagnostic gold standard.","Definitive treatment is surgical resection of the aganglionic segment with a pull-through procedure connecting normally innervated bowel to the anus.","Hirschsprung-associated enterocolitis is the most serious complication and can present with fever, distension, foul explosive diarrhoea, and sepsis."],"pathophysiology":"Failure of the craniocaudal migration of neural crest-derived enteric ganglion cells leaves the distal bowel without submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. The aganglionic segment cannot relax and remains tonically contracted, while abnormal cholinergic and other innervation adds to the dysmotility. This creates a functional obstruction with proximal dilatation (megacolon) and predisposes to stasis, bacterial overgrowth, and enterocolitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Meconium ileus (often cystic fibrosis)","Meconium plug syndrome / small left colon syndrome","Anorectal malformation","Intestinal atresia or stenosis","Functional constipation (in older children)","Hypothyroidism-related constipation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Rectal suction biopsy for absence of ganglion cells (with acetylcholinesterase staining or calretinin)","Contrast (unprepped) enema to identify a transition zone","Anorectal manometry in older children","Abdominal radiograph showing distal obstruction with dilated proximal bowel","Genetic and syndrome evaluation where relevant"],"redFlags":["Failure to pass meconium within the first 48 hours of life","Bilious vomiting with abdominal distension","Fever, explosive foul diarrhoea, and distension suggesting enterocolitis","Signs of sepsis, dehydration, or shock","Toxic megacolon"],"prognosis":"After definitive pull-through surgery most children do well, though some have ongoing issues such as constipation, soiling, or recurrent enterocolitis; outcome is worse with long-segment or total colonic aganglionosis and with severe enterocolitis.","pitfalls":["Attributing neonatal obstruction solely to functional constipation and missing aganglionosis","Relying on a normal contrast enema to exclude the diagnosis","Overlooking Hirschsprung-associated enterocolitis, which can be rapidly fatal","Taking a biopsy too close to the anal verge where ganglion cells are normally sparse","Missing associated syndromes such as trisomy 21"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"hirsutism":{"name":"Hirsutism","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["excessive terminal hair growth in women","androgen-mediated hair growth"],"chapter":406,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3137-3141","clinicalPearls":["Hirsutism is defined as excessive growth of terminal (long, coarse, pigmented) hair in an androgen-dependent distribution and affects roughly 10% of reproductive-age women (Harrison 22e p.3137).","Most cases are idiopathic or due to androgen excess from polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS); PCOS is the single most common cause of ovarian androgen excess (Harrison 22e p.3137-3140).","Hirsutism must be distinguished from hypertrichosis, which is androgen-independent generalized excess vellus/terminal hair often caused by medications such as phenytoin, minoxidil, and cyclosporine (Harrison 22e p.3138).","Virilization (deepened voice, breast atrophy, increased muscle bulk, clitoromegaly, increased libido) signals markedly elevated androgens and raises concern for an ovarian or adrenal neoplasm, or hyperthecosis (Harrison 22e p.3138).","Sudden onset and rapid progression of hirsutism, especially with virilization, suggests an androgen-secreting tumor and warrants urgent evaluation (Harrison 22e p.3138).","The modified Ferriman-Gallwey scale grades nine androgen-sensitive sites from 0 to 4; a normal score is <8, and scores >=8 in most non-Hispanic white and African American women indicate excess androgen-mediated hair growth (Harrison 22e p.3138-3139).","Asian and Native American women are less likely to manifest hirsutism even with androgen excess; pustular acne and scalp hair thinning may be the only cutaneous clues (Harrison 22e p.3138).","Free (unbound) testosterone is often more informative than total testosterone, because hyperinsulinemia and androgen excess lower SHBG, keeping total testosterone high-normal while unbound testosterone is substantially elevated (Harrison 22e p.3139-3140).","Dihydrotestosterone (DHT), formed from testosterone by 5-alpha-reductase within the pilosebaceous unit, is the principal mediator of androgen action on the hair follicle; type 2 isoenzyme predominates in hair follicles and type 1 in sebaceous glands (Harrison 22e p.3139).","Acanthosis nigricans and skin tags are cutaneous markers of associated insulin resistance in hyperandrogenic women (Harrison 22e p.3138).","A BMI >25 kg/m2 (and especially >30) is frequently associated with hirsutism, partly via increased peripheral conversion of androgen precursors to testosterone (Harrison 22e p.3138).","After menopause, ovarian estrogen production falls more than androgen production and SHBG declines, increasing the relative proportion of unbound testosterone, which can exacerbate hirsutism (Harrison 22e p.3140)."],"pathophysiology":"Androgens drive differentiation of pilosebaceous units, transforming vellus hairs into terminal hairs and stimulating sebaceous gland development; the total number of follicles is fixed for life, but follicle size and hair type change with androgen exposure and local growth factors. Testosterone, secreted roughly equally by ovaries (LH-driven) and adrenals (ACTH-driven), is a precursor that is converted within the pilosebaceous unit by 5-alpha-reductase to the more potent dihydrotestosterone, the chief local mediator. End-organ sensitivity, genetic and ethnic background, and local growth factors explain why androgen levels correlate only modestly with the amount of hair. Excess androgen also shortens the scalp-hair anagen phase, producing thinning/androgenic alopecia. Hyperinsulinemia and androgen excess suppress hepatic SHBG, raising bioavailable free testosterone. In nonclassic congenital adrenal hyperplasia (usually 21-hydroxylase deficiency), impaired cortisol synthesis reduces negative feedback, driving compensatory ACTH-mediated adrenal hyperplasia and accumulation of steroid precursors that are shunted to androgens (Harrison 22e p.3138-3141).","additionalDifferentials":["Polycystic ovary syndrome / functional ovarian hyperandrogenism","Nonclassic and classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia (most often 21-hydroxylase deficiency)","Ovarian hyperthecosis","Androgen-secreting ovarian or adrenal neoplasm","Cushing's syndrome","Hyperprolactinemia","Acromegaly","Syndromes of extreme insulin resistance","Idiopathic hirsutism / idiopathic hyperandrogenism","Obesity-related peripheral androgen overproduction","Pregnancy-related hyperandrogenism (hyperreactio luteinalis, thecoma of pregnancy)","Drug-induced (androgens, androgenic progestins, minoxidil, phenytoin, diazoxide, cyclosporine, valproic acid)","Ovotesticular disorders of sex development"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hypertrichosis (androgen-independent generalized hair growth, often drug-induced)","Constitutional/ethnic variation in hair distribution mistaken for pathologic hirsutism"],"additionalInvestigations":["Total testosterone (specialty assay) and free/unbound testosterone, calculated from total testosterone and SHBG or measured by LC/tandem mass spectrometry","DHEAS to assess adrenal androgen contribution","Morning follicular-phase 17-hydroxyprogesterone to screen for 21-hydroxylase deficiency; ACTH (cosyntropin) stimulation test if equivocal","Dexamethasone androgen-suppression test to broadly distinguish adrenal from ovarian androgen overproduction","Overnight 1-mg dexamethasone suppression test with 8 a.m. cortisol when Cushing's syndrome is suspected","Prolactin and thyroid function when galactorrhea or other features present","CT or MRI to localize a suspected adrenal mass; transvaginal ultrasound for a suspected ovarian mass","Note: LH/FSH and ovarian ultrasound have limited diagnostic value for PCOS given pulsatile gonadotropin secretion and low ultrasound specificity"],"redFlags":["Rapid onset or rapid progression of hirsutism","Signs of virilization: voice deepening, clitoromegaly, breast atrophy, increased muscle bulk","Total testosterone >12 nmol/L (>3.5 ng/mL) suggesting an androgen-producing tumor","DHEAS >18.5 micromol/L (>7000 microg/L) suggesting an adrenal tumor","Hypertension, striae, easy bruising, centripetal weight gain suggesting Cushing's syndrome","Galactorrhea suggesting hyperprolactinemia"],"prognosis":"Most hirsutism is benign and chronic, reflecting underlying functional androgen excess (commonly PCOS) or idiopathic causes; with treatment, hair growth typically progresses slowly. Even effective therapy yields only modest reduction in hair, with attenuation usually not evident until 4-6 months and maximal effect requiring 9-12 months because of the length of the hair growth cycle. By contrast, rapidly progressive hirsutism with virilization may herald a malignant or otherwise serious neoplasm and carries a prognosis tied to that underlying tumor (Harrison 22e p.3138-3141).","pitfalls":["Relying on total testosterone alone can miss hyperandrogenism, since low SHBG can keep total levels high-normal while free testosterone is elevated.","Assuming shaving increases hair rate or density; it does not.","Over-interpreting ovarian ultrasound: polycystic-appearing ovaries also occur in hypothalamic amenorrhea and in women without PCOS, so ultrasound has low specificity and is often unnecessary to diagnose PCOS.","Random LH/FSH measurements are unreliable for PCOS because of pulsatile gonadotropin secretion.","Mild DHEAS elevations are common in PCOS and do not by themselves indicate an adrenal tumor.","Failing to distinguish hirsutism from hypertrichosis, leading to inappropriate hormonal work-up.","Underestimating androgen excess in Asian and Native American women, who may show only acne or scalp thinning."],"severityClassification":"Hair growth is quantified objectively with the modified Ferriman-Gallwey scale, scoring nine androgen-sensitive body sites from 0 (no terminal hair) to 4 (frankly virile, male-pattern). A total score <8 is normal in ~95% of non-Hispanic white and African American women; scores >=8 indicate excess androgen-mediated hair growth warranting hormonal evaluation (Harrison 22e p.3138-3139).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3137","Harrison 22e p.3138","Harrison 22e p.3139","Harrison 22e p.3140","Harrison 22e p.3141"],"crossLinks":[]},"histoplasmosis":{"name":"Histoplasmosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Histoplasma capsulatum infection"],"chapter":218,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1692-1696","clinicalPearls":["Caused by Histoplasma capsulatum, a thermal dimorphic fungus; H. capsulatum var. capsulatum predominates in North America, while var. duboisii in Africa produces larger yeasts and a clinically distinct disease with frequent skin and bone involvement (p.1692-1693, 1695).","Most prevalent endemic mycosis in North America, classically the Ohio and Mississippi river valleys, with foci in Mexico, Central/South America (Brazil), Africa and Asia; soil enriched with bird or bat droppings promotes growth, and disruption aerosolizes microconidia (p.1693).","High-exposure, high-attack-rate activities include spelunking (cave exposure), excavation, cleaning chicken coops, demolition/remodeling of old buildings and cutting dead trees (p.1693).","Clinical spectrum spans asymptomatic infection to life-threatening illness; severity depends on intensity of exposure, host immune status and underlying lung architecture (p.1693-1694).","Acute pulmonary disease appears 1-4 weeks after exposure as a flulike illness (fever, chills, sweats, headache, myalgia, anorexia, dry cough, dyspnea, chest pain), often with hilar/mediastinal adenopathy on chest radiograph (p.1694).","Arthralgia/arthritis with erythema nodosum occurs in 5-10% of acute cases and represents an inflammatory response, not extrapulmonary spread; pericarditis may also develop (p.1694).","Chronic cavitary pulmonary histoplasmosis occurs in smokers with structural lung disease (e.g., bullous emphysema), with productive cough, dyspnea, low-grade fever, night sweats, weight loss and upper-lobe cavitary infiltrates resembling tuberculosis (p.1694).","Progressive disseminated histoplasmosis (PDH) involves the reticuloendothelial system - lungs, bone marrow, spleen, liver, adrenal glands and mucocutaneous membranes; common findings are fever, weight loss, hepatosplenomegaly and thrombocytopenia, with possible meningitis, oral/GI ulceration and adrenal insufficiency (p.1694).","Fungal culture is the diagnostic gold standard but may take up to 1 month and is often negative in less severe cases; in PDH yield is highest from BAL fluid, bone marrow aspirate and blood (p.1694).","Detection of Histoplasma antigen in body fluids is extremely useful for PDH and acute diffuse pulmonary disease (sensitivity >95% in PDH, >80% in severe acute pulmonary disease when both urine and serum are tested); urine is often positive in disseminated disease (p.1694-1695, 1693).","Antigen levels correlate with severity in PDH, fall with effective therapy, and a stable or rising level predicts relapse or treatment failure (p.1695-1696).","Serology (immunodiffusion, complement fixation, IgG/IgM EIA) is especially useful in immunocompetent patients and for chronic pulmonary disease; IgM appears first and declines, IgG appears later and rises (p.1695)."],"pathophysiology":"Infection follows inhalation of microconidia, the naturally infectious mycelial form; particles 2-4 micrometers reach terminal bronchioles and alveoli where alveolar macrophages engulf them and they transform into small (2-5 micrometer) narrow-budding yeasts, a calcium- and iron-dependent process central to pathogenesis. Yeasts evade the immune system and multiply within resting macrophages, then translocate via phagosomes to draining lymph nodes and spread hematogenously throughout the reticuloendothelial system. Effective cellular immunity develops about 2 weeks after infection, with T-cell interferon-gamma helping macrophages kill the organism; IL-12 and TNF-alpha are essential to cellular immunity. In immunocompetent hosts, organized granulomas contain the organisms and typically fibrose and calcify (calcified lung nodules, mediastinal nodes, hepatosplenic calcifications), and infection confers protective immunity to reinfection. With impaired cellular immunity, infection is not contained and disseminates as progressive disseminated histoplasmosis. Structural lung disease such as emphysema impairs clearance and leads to chronic pulmonary disease with progressive inflammation, necrosis and fibrosis mimicking cavitary tuberculosis (p.1693-1694).","additionalDifferentials":["Tuberculosis (chronic cavitary disease)","Sarcoidosis","Lymphoma","Primary or metastatic lung cancer (asymptomatic pulmonary nodules)","Other endemic/dimorphic mycoses: blastomycosis, coccidioidomycosis, paracoccidioidomycosis, talaromycosis"],"infectionMimics":["Cavitary tuberculosis (chronic pulmonary histoplasmosis mimics this radiographically and pathologically)","Blastomycosis","Coccidioidomycosis","Paracoccidioidomycosis","Talaromycosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sarcoidosis","Lymphoma","Primary lung cancer (asymptomatic controlled nodules found on screening CT in smokers)","Metastatic cancer"],"additionalInvestigations":["Histoplasma antigen detection in urine and serum (and BAL fluid; CSF for CNS disease)","Lateral flow antigen point-of-care testing for resource-limited settings","Histoplasma serology: immunodiffusion (ID), complement fixation (CF), IgG and IgM enzyme immunoassay (EIA)","Fungal culture (BAL fluid, sputum/bronchial washings, blood, bone marrow aspirate; positive in ~75% of PDH and chronic pulmonary disease)","Fungal stains/cytopathology or biopsy of affected organ (BAL fluid, bone marrow, liver, adrenal) showing small narrow-budding intracellular yeasts","Chest radiograph/CT (pneumonitis, hilar/mediastinal adenopathy, upper-lobe cavitation, calcified nodes)","Workup for rare genetic immunodeficiencies of the IFN-gamma and TNF-alpha pathways in healthy individuals presenting with PDH"],"redFlags":["Progressive disseminated histoplasmosis (PDH), typically in immunocompromised hosts (~70% of cases), especially AIDS with CD4+ count <200/microliter","Acute rapidly fatal PDH with diffuse interstitial/reticulonodular infiltrates, respiratory failure, shock, coagulopathy and multiorgan failure","Onset of PDH while on anti-TNF-alpha therapy, methotrexate or other biologic/immunosuppressive agents, or post-transplant immunosuppression","CNS involvement: meningitis or focal brain lesions","Adrenal insufficiency from adrenal involvement","Fibrosing mediastinitis causing superior vena cava syndrome, pulmonary vessel obstruction or airway obstruction (fatal in up to one-third of cases)","Large mediastinal masses compressing great vessels, airways or esophagus, or rupturing to form fistulae (e.g., bronchoesophageal)"],"prognosis":"Most immunocompetent individuals with low-level exposure are asymptomatic or have a mild, self-limited illness that resolves without therapy; up to 75% of adults in endemic areas have evidence of prior infection without clinical disease. Untreated chronic cavitary disease is slowly progressive. Progressive disseminated histoplasmosis ranges from an acute, rapidly fatal course to a subacute/chronic one and is a devastating illness requiring prompt recognition, particularly in AIDS or anti-TNF therapy. Fibrosing mediastinitis is fatal in up to one-third of cases. Antigen levels track severity and response, with stable or rising levels signaling treatment failure or relapse (p.1693-1696).","pitfalls":["Chronic cavitary histoplasmosis closely mimics cavitary tuberculosis radiographically (upper-lobe infiltrates, cavitation, pleural thickening) and may be misdiagnosed as TB, sarcoidosis, lymphoma or malignancy.","Asymptomatic calcified nodules from controlled prior infection may be mistaken for malignancy on screening CT in smokers from endemic areas.","Fungal stains may misidentify staining artifacts or other fungal elements as Histoplasma yeasts.","Persistent antibodies for several years after infection can cause a false misdiagnosis of active histoplasmosis in a patient with another disease process.","ID and CF serology are insensitive early in infection and have reduced sensitivity in immunosuppressed patients, especially organ transplant recipients.","Antigen testing cross-reacts with African histoplasmosis, blastomycosis, coccidioidomycosis, paracoccidioidomycosis, talaromycosis and rarely aspergillosis.","Fibrosing mediastinitis is a chronic fibrotic reaction to past infection, not active disease, and does NOT respond to antifungal therapy.","Unlike latent tuberculosis, inactive histoplasmosis does NOT reactivate."],"severityClassification":"Clinical forms (Harrison Table 218-1): acute pulmonary, mild to moderate; acute pulmonary, severe with respiratory failure (ARDS); chronic/cavitary pulmonary; progressive disseminated (PDH); and central nervous system disease. Severity is graded by intensity of exposure, host immune status and underlying lung architecture (p.1693-1695).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1692-1696"],"crossLinks":[]},"hiv_aids":{"name":"HIV/AIDS","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HIV infection","AIDS","acquired immunodeficiency syndrome"],"chapter":208,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1556","clinicalPearls":["HIV is a retrovirus whose hallmark is progressive depletion of CD4+ T lymphocytes; the resulting immunodeficiency underlies the susceptibility to opportunistic infections and AIDS-defining illnesses (Harrison 22e ch.208, p.1556).","Consider acute (primary) HIV infection in a patient presenting with a mononucleosis-like illness: sore throat is reported by roughly half of patients with acute HIV infection, often together with fever, headache, myalgias, lymphadenopathy, anorexia and rash (p.1556; cross-ref ch.38 p.293).","The acute retroviral syndrome rash is typically a nonspecific diffuse macular/papular eruption favouring the upper thorax, face and collar region; less commonly urticarial or vesicular lesions occur, and oral or genital ulcers may accompany it (p.1556; cross-ref Appendix A1).","After primary infection there is HIV seroconversion, then a usually asymptomatic phase, and ultimately progression to symptomatic HIV disease/AIDS if untreated (cross-ref ch.38 p.297).","Three diagnostic markers appear sequentially in primary infection: HIV viral load (RNA) first, then p24 antigen, then HIV antibody; modern fourth-generation Ag/Ab combination assays shorten the window period by detecting p24 antigen before antibody is present (cross-ref ch.126, Fig.126-1).","The CD4+ T-cell count stratifies opportunistic-infection risk: as the count falls (notably below ~200 cells/uL) the risk of Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, cerebral toxoplasmosis, cryptococcosis, disseminated Mycobacterium avium complex and CMV end-organ disease rises, guiding prophylaxis decisions (p.1556; cross-ref ch.62, ch.108).","A nondetectable (suppressed) HIV viral load on antiretroviral therapy is the goal of treatment; sustained viral suppression is the basis of the Undetectable = Untransmittable (U=U) principle and of treatment-as-prevention (cross-ref ch.108 p.745).","Antiretroviral therapy is recommended for essentially all people with HIV regardless of CD4 count; in patients with concurrent malignancy or other illness, ART should run concurrently with the primary treatment together with appropriate antimicrobial prophylaxis (cross-ref ch.108 p.745).","Doses and specific drug regimens are deliberately omitted here; ART is presented only as a principle (combination antiretroviral therapy to achieve durable viral suppression)."],"pathophysiology":"HIV is a human retrovirus that infects cells bearing the CD4 receptor together with a chemokine co-receptor (CCR5 or CXCR4), leading to progressive depletion and dysfunction of CD4+ T lymphocytes (Harrison 22e ch.208, p.1556; co-receptor role cross-ref ch.61 p.448, ch.63). Reverse transcriptase copies the viral RNA genome into DNA that integrates into the host genome, establishing lifelong infection and a latent reservoir. Primary infection produces a burst of high-level viremia (the acute retroviral syndrome) followed by partial immune control and a viral set point, then a prolonged clinically latent phase during which CD4+ T cells are gradually lost. Once CD4+ T-cell counts fall sufficiently, the profound cellular immunodeficiency permits opportunistic infections and AIDS-defining illnesses. The mononuclear phagocyte system can also be infected and serves as a reservoir (cross-ref ch.63 p.461).","additionalDifferentials":["Infectious mononucleosis (Epstein-Barr virus) and acute CMV infection (overlapping mononucleosis-like picture; cross-ref Appendix A1)","Acute viral exanthems and other causes of fever with rash (e.g., secondary syphilis, enteroviral exanthem)","Streptococcal/viral pharyngitis when sore throat predominates in acute HIV (cross-ref ch.38 p.293)"],"infectionMimics":["Epstein-Barr virus infectious mononucleosis","Acute cytomegalovirus infection","Secondary syphilis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Drug hypersensitivity reaction with fever and morbilliform rash","Autoinflammatory/connective-tissue disease presenting with fever, rash and lymphadenopathy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fourth-generation HIV-1/2 antigen/antibody combination immunoassay with supplemental confirmatory antibody differentiation assay (cross-ref ch.126, Fig.126-1)","HIV RNA (plasma viral load) quantification — diagnostic in the early window before antibody, and the key on-treatment monitoring test (cross-ref ch.126; ch.108)","CD4+ T-lymphocyte count to stage immunodeficiency and guide opportunistic-infection prophylaxis","HIV genotypic resistance testing recommended before starting antiretrovirals (cross-ref ch.126, Fig.126-1)","Screening for co-infections (hepatitis B, hepatitis C) prior to immunosuppressive or antineoplastic therapy in HIV-positive patients (cross-ref ch.108 p.745)"],"redFlags":["New CD4+ count below ~200 cells/uL — high risk of Pneumocystis pneumonia and other opportunistic infections; prompts prophylaxis (cross-ref ch.62 p.461)","Clinical or radiographic worsening after starting ART suggesting immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS), especially with an underlying opportunistic infection such as PML (cross-ref ch.205 p.1490)","Features suggesting an AIDS-defining opportunistic process (e.g., focal neurologic deficit from toxoplasmosis or CNS lymphoma; cross-ref ch.30)","Adrenal insufficiency precipitated by stress or steroidogenesis-inhibiting drugs in advanced HIV — adrenal involvement is usually subclinical but can become clinically significant (cross-ref ch.49 p.379)"],"prognosis":"Untreated HIV infection is progressive and ultimately fatal once advanced immunodeficiency and AIDS-defining illnesses supervene. Effective antiretroviral therapy that achieves a durable nondetectable viral load markedly improves survival and restores immune function; for example, in HIV-positive patients with PML, institution of ART enhances survival, and those with higher CD4 counts (>300/uL) and low or nondetectable HIV viral loads have a better prognosis (cross-ref ch.205, p.1490).","pitfalls":["Failing to consider acute HIV in a mononucleosis-like illness: although about half of patients with acute HIV infection have a sore throat, in most U.S. settings only about 1% of patients with viral or mononucleosis-like symptoms actually have acute HIV — a negative antibody test early does not exclude it, so check viral load/p24 (cross-ref ch.38 p.293).","Relying on antibody testing alone during the window period can miss acute infection before seroconversion; the dashed limit-of-detection and marker sequence (VL, then p24, then Ab) explain false-negative antibody results (cross-ref ch.126, Fig.126-1).","Attributing post-ART clinical deterioration to treatment failure rather than recognising IRIS, particularly in patients with a treated or unmasked opportunistic infection (cross-ref ch.205 p.1490).","Overlooking subclinical adrenal insufficiency in advanced HIV, which may be unmasked by stress or by drugs that inhibit steroidogenesis (cross-ref ch.49 p.379)."],"severityClassification":"Staging is based on the CD4+ T-lymphocyte count, which predicts the spectrum of opportunistic disease: relatively preserved counts carry low opportunistic-infection risk, whereas progressively lower counts (notably <200 cells/uL) define increasing risk for Pneumocystis pneumonia, cerebral toxoplasmosis, cryptococcosis, disseminated Mycobacterium avium complex and CMV disease, with the lowest counts associated with the broadest range of AIDS-defining illnesses (Harrison 22e ch.208, p.1556; opportunistic-infection associations cross-ref ch.62, ch.222, ch.227, ch.235).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1556"],"crossLinks":["pneumocystis_pneumonia","cryptococcosis","toxoplasmosis","cytomegalovirus_infection","nontuberculous_mycobacterial_infection"]},"hodgkin_lymphoma":{"name":"Hodgkin's Lymphoma","system":"Oncology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Hodgkin disease"],"chapter":114,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.868-871","clinicalPearls":["Hodgkin's lymphoma (HL) is a malignancy of mature B lymphocytes and accounts for ~10% of all lymphomas diagnosed each year; most are classical HL (cHL), with nodular lymphocyte-predominant HL (NLPHL) a distinct second subtype (p.868).","Reed-Sternberg (HRS) cells are diagnostic of cHL: large cells with abundant cytoplasm and bilobed and/or multiple nuclei, comprising <1% of tumor cellularity within a surrounding polyclonal inflammatory infiltrate of lymphocytes, eosinophils, neutrophils, macrophages, plasma cells, fibroblasts, and collagen (p.868-869).","By immunohistochemistry HRS cells are often PAX-5 positive but have low to no expression of other B-cell antigens such as CD19 and CD20; they express CD15 and CD30 in 85% and 100% of cases, respectively (p.868).","Most patients present with nontender palpable lymphadenopathy, typically in the neck, supraclavicular area, and axilla; more than half have mediastinal adenopathy at diagnosis, sometimes as the initial manifestation (p.869).","About one-third of patients present with B symptoms (fevers, night sweats, and/or weight loss); HL can occasionally present as a fever of unknown origin, more common in older patients with mixed-cellularity HL in an abdominal site (p.869).","Pel-Ebstein fever describes a pattern of fevers persisting for days to weeks, followed by afebrile intervals and then recurrence (p.869).","Pain in lymph nodes on alcohol ingestion and severe unexplained itching (pruritus) are recognized unusual manifestations of HL (p.869).","Staging is anatomically based using the Ann Arbor staging system because the disease tends to march from one lymph node group to the next, often contiguous group; staging guides therapy duration and intensity (p.869).","PET/CT is used for staging and is more accurate than bone marrow biopsy for assessing bone marrow involvement, which in cHL tends to be patchy and may be missed on a unilateral marrow biopsy (p.869).","Cure rates now exceed 85%, and the new central challenge in HL care is late therapy-related toxicity, including a high rate of secondary malignancies and cardiovascular disease (p.868)."],"pathophysiology":"HL is of B-cell origin. The malignant Reed-Sternberg (HRS) cells are large bilobed/multinucleate cells that constitute <1% of the tumor mass; the bulk of the tumor is a surrounding polyclonal inflammatory infiltrate, probably a reaction to cytokines produced by the tumor cells. Immunoglobulin genes in the neoplastic cells are rearranged but not expressed. HRS cells interact with their microenvironment via cell-cell contact and elaboration of growth factors and cytokines, producing a cellular milieu that protects them from host immune attack while supporting their proliferation and survival. 97% of HRS cells in cHL harbor genetic aberrations in the PD-L1 locus on chromosome 9p24.1, causing overexpression of PD-L1 (ligand for the inhibitory PD-1 receptor on immune cells), a mechanism for immune evasion that may also contribute to generalized immune suppression in HL patients. An association with Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) is suggested: monoclonal/oligoclonal EBV-infected cells are found in 20-40% of HL patients, but a definitive etiologic role is not settled. Viral oncogenesis appears greater in HIV-related cHL, where EBV is detectable in nearly all cases (versus only one-third of non-HIV-associated cHL) and HRS cells express EBV latent membrane protein 1 (LMP-1) with episomal, clonal EBV genomes suggesting direct involvement in early lymphomagenesis (p.868-869).","additionalDifferentials":["Inflammatory processes (reactive lymphadenopathy)","Infectious mononucleosis","Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma (NHL)","Phenytoin-induced adenopathy","Nonlymphomatous malignancies","Anaplastic large-cell lymphoma (can mimic HL, especially in older patients)","T-cell/histiocyte-rich B-cell lymphoma"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious mononucleosis (EBV)","Reactive/inflammatory lymphadenopathy from infection"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma","Anaplastic large-cell lymphoma","T-cell/histiocyte-rich B-cell lymphoma","Phenytoin-induced (drug-related) adenopathy","Nonlymphomatous malignancies"],"additionalInvestigations":["Adequate biopsy specimen reviewed by an expert hematopathologist (required to establish the diagnosis)","Immunohistochemistry: PAX-5, CD15, CD30 (and CD19/CD20, typically low/absent in cHL)","PET/CT for anatomic staging (more accurate than marrow biopsy for patchy marrow involvement)","Complete blood count with differential","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR)","Chemistry studies reflecting major organ function, including serum albumin","HIV and hepatitis virus testing","Careful history (including presence/absence of B symptoms) and physical exam attentive to peripheral nodes, liver, and spleen size; review of comorbidities such as pulmonary disease and congestive heart failure given lung- and heart-toxic chemotherapy"],"redFlags":["B symptoms: unexplained weight loss >10% of body weight over the 6 months before staging, persistent or recurrent fever >38°C in the previous month, or recurrent drenching night sweats in the previous month","Pel-Ebstein fever (cyclic fever with afebrile intervals)","Bulky disease and elevated ESR (>30 with B symptoms; >50 without) marking unfavorable early-stage disease","Fever of unknown origin as a presentation, particularly in older patients with mixed-cellularity HL at an abdominal site","Superior vena cava syndrome from mediastinal involvement (HL is a recognized cause)"],"prognosis":"cHL is highly curable and a success story of modern oncology, with cure rates now >85%; the overwhelming majority of patients are cured with chemotherapy alone or combined chemotherapy and radiation. Patients are stratified into early-stage (I/II) versus advanced-stage (III/IV) disease. Early-stage patients have a better prognosis overall and are further classified as favorable or unfavorable. In advanced-stage disease, prognosis is best predicted by the International Prognostic Score (IPS), which assigns 1 point each for male sex, age >45 years, stage IV disease, serum albumin <4 g/dL, hemoglobin <10.5 g/dL, white blood cell count >=15,000/uL, and lymphocyte count <600/uL and/or <8% of WBC count; 5-year progression-free survival ranges from 88% (no risk factors) to 62% (four or more factors), though very few patients carry multiple risk factors. NLPHL tends to have a chronic, relapsing course resembling indolent B-cell NHL, with a 15-year nonrelapse survival of 82% for early-stage disease treated with definitive radiotherapy. A major concern is that in some early-stage series more patients died from late treatment complications than from HL itself (p.868-871).","pitfalls":["The late age peak in the bimodal distribution may be artifactual, attributable to confusion with similar-appearing entities such as anaplastic large-cell lymphoma and T-cell/histiocyte-rich B-cell lymphoma (p.868).","Bone marrow involvement in cHL is patchy and can be missed on a unilateral marrow biopsy; PET/CT is more accurate (p.869).","Combined-modality therapy in early-stage disease reduces relapse only slightly while increasing late toxicity, with minimal or no improvement in long-term survival; much of the excess risk is attributable to radiotherapy (p.869-870).","NLPHL is biologically distinct from cHL and resembles indolent B-cell NHL; its 'popcorn'/L&H cells are CD30- and CD15-negative (unlike HRS cells), so it should not be managed identically to cHL (p.868, 871).","Alternative regimens (Stanford V, escalated BEACOPP) have not improved survival over standard therapy in early- or advanced-stage disease and are associated with increased toxicity (p.870).","Subdiaphragmatic presentation of cHL is unusual; it is more common in older males (p.869)."],"severityClassification":"Ann Arbor Staging System: Stage I = single lymph node region or lymphoid structure (e.g., spleen, thymus, Waldeyer's ring); Stage II = two or more node regions on the same side of the diaphragm (mediastinum counts as one site; bilaterally involved hilar nodes constitute stage II); Stage III = node regions/lymphoid structures on both sides of the diaphragm (III1 limited to spleen, splenic hilar, celiac, or portal nodes; III2 adds paraaortic, iliac, or mesenteric nodes); Stage IV = extranodal involvement beyond 'E', more than one extranodal deposit at any location, or any liver/bone marrow involvement. Suffix A = no symptoms; B = unexplained weight loss >10% over 6 months, persistent/recurrent fever >38°C in the previous month, or recurrent drenching night sweats in the previous month; E = localized solitary extralymphatic involvement (excluding liver and bone marrow). Patients are further grouped clinically as early-stage (I/II) versus advanced-stage (III/IV), with early-stage subclassified favorable vs unfavorable and advanced-stage risk-scored by the International Prognostic Score (p.869).","references":["Harrison 22e p.868-871"],"crossLinks":["non_hodgkin_lymphoma"]},"human_papillomavirus_infections":{"name":"Human Papillomavirus Infections","system":"Dermatologic / Mucocutaneous (with anogenital and aerodigestive involvement)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HPV infection","Warts","Verrucae","Papillomavirus infection","Condyloma acuminatum","Anogenital warts"],"chapter":203,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1525","clinicalPearls":["Warts are cutaneous neoplasms caused by human papillomaviruses (HPVs), of which more than 100 distinct types have been described (Harrison 22e p.1525).","Verruca vulgaris, the common wart, is typically a sessile, dome-shaped lesion roughly a centimeter across with a hyperkeratotic surface made up of many fine filamentous projections (Harrison 22e p.1525).","Plantar warts are endophytic and covered by thick keratin; paring them characteristically uncovers a central core of keratinized debris with punctate bleeding points, which helps distinguish them from corns (Harrison 22e p.1525).","Flat warts (verruca plana) are only slightly raised with a velvety, nonverrucous surface, favor the face, arms, and legs, and are frequently spread by shaving (Harrison 22e p.1525).","Filiform warts present as papillomatous lesions on a narrow base and are common on the face, neck, and skinfolds (Harrison 22e p.1525).","Genital warts start as small papillomas that can enlarge into large fungating lesions; in women they may involve the labia, perineum, perianal skin, and the vaginal, urethral, anal, and cervical mucosa, while in men they often begin in the coronal sulcus and may extend to the penile shaft, scrotum, perianal skin, or urethra (Harrison 22e p.1525).","HPV types 16 and 18 are the most intensively studied and are the major risk factors for intraepithelial neoplasia and squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix, anus, vulva, and penis (Harrison 22e p.1525).","Sexual transmission of HPV is described as the fundamental etiologic factor for cervical cancer (Harrison 22e p.1602).","Persistent oral HPV infection, particularly HPV-16, raises the risk of oropharyngeal cancer even without smoking or alcohol use, and the increase appears greater than additive when HPV and smoking coexist; oral HPV is believed to be largely sexually acquired (Harrison 22e p.1601).","A highly effective HPV vaccine for selected types is FDA-approved and reduces the incidence of anogenital and cervical carcinoma (Harrison 22e p.1525).","The available nonavalent HPV vaccine covers strains 6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, and 58; types 6 and 11 cause genital papillomas while the remaining types drive cervical and anal cancer, and reduction of types 16 and 18 alone could theoretically prevent more than 70% of cervical cancers worldwide (Harrison 22e p.1601)."],"pathophysiology":"Human papillomaviruses are DNA viruses that, like other papillomaviruses, gain entry through breaks in the skin and infect epithelial cells, producing benign cutaneous neoplasms (warts). Keratinized cells are not viable hosts for replication, so the virus targets viable epithelium at cutaneous and mucosal surfaces. More than 100 HPV types exist; certain oncogenic types (notably 16 and 18) integrate into host epithelium and drive intraepithelial neoplasia and squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix, anus, vulva, and penis, and persistent oral HPV-16 promotes oropharyngeal carcinoma. Histology of affected sites may show changes of typical warts and/or features of intraepidermal carcinoma (Bowen's disease). The oncogenic potential is markedly amplified in immunosuppressed hosts (Harrison 22e p.1525, p.1601).","additionalDifferentials":["Seborrheic keratosis","Molluscum contagiosum","Squamous cell carcinoma / intraepidermal carcinoma (Bowen's disease)","Acrochordon (skin tag)","Corn or callus (for plantar warts)","Condyloma lata of secondary syphilis (for anogenital lesions)"],"infectionMimics":["Molluscum contagiosum (poxvirus)","Condyloma lata of secondary syphilis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Seborrheic keratosis","Squamous cell carcinoma and intraepidermal carcinoma (Bowen's disease)","Acrochordon (skin tag)","Corn/callus mimicking plantar wart"],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical recognition of lesion morphology (location, surface, punctate bleeding points after paring of plantar warts)","Skin/mucosal biopsy with histologic examination when malignancy is suspected (may show typical wart changes and/or intraepidermal carcinoma)","Cervical cytology (Pap smear) for cervical screening","HPV testing (with or without Pap smear), recommended beginning at age 30 per cited screening guidance"],"redFlags":["Lesions in patients immunosuppressed after solid organ transplantation or with HIV, who carry higher risk of HPV-associated malignancy and warrant monitoring for squamous cell carcinoma and other cutaneous malignancies","Anogenital lesions showing histologic features of intraepidermal carcinoma (Bowen's disease)","Persistent or growing fungating anogenital masses","New oropharyngeal lesions in the setting of persistent HPV-16, particularly with concurrent tobacco use"],"prognosis":"Most warts in normal (immunocompetent) individuals resolve spontaneously within 1-2 years. However, oncogenic HPV types, especially 16 and 18, can lead to intraepithelial neoplasia and squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix, anus, vulva, penis, and oropharynx, with higher risk in immunosuppressed patients. The HPV vaccine is highly effective at preventing persistent strain-specific infection and preneoplastic lesions (cervical or anal intraepithelial neoplasia grades I-III) in individuals not previously infected, though it does not affect established infections (Harrison 22e p.1525, p.1601).","pitfalls":["Assuming all warts require aggressive treatment: most resolve spontaneously within 1-2 years in healthy people (Harrison 22e p.1525).","Overlooking the malignant potential of oncogenic HPV types and failing to monitor immunosuppressed (transplant or HIV) patients for squamous cell carcinoma (Harrison 22e p.1525).","Expecting the HPV vaccine to clear existing infection: it does not appear to affect preexisting infections (Harrison 22e p.1601).","Screening cervical cytology more frequently than recommended adds little benefit and can cause harm through unnecessary procedures and overtreatment of transient lesions (Harrison 22e p.1602)."],"severityClassification":"HPV-associated cervical and anal disease is described histologically as intraepithelial neoplasia graded I, II, and III (CIN/AIN), reflecting progressive preneoplastic change preceding invasive squamous cell carcinoma (Harrison 22e p.1601).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1525","Harrison 22e p.1601","Harrison 22e p.1602"],"crossLinks":[]},"hungry_bone_syndrome":{"name":"Hungry bone syndrome","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HBS","post-parathyroidectomy hypocalcaemia","rapid bone remineralisation syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A state of profound and prolonged hypocalcaemia that follows correction of long-standing hyperparathyroidism, classically after parathyroidectomy","It occurs because the abrupt fall in parathyroid hormone allows rapid, avid uptake of calcium into demineralised bone","The hallmark is low calcium accompanied by low phosphate and low magnesium, distinguishing it from hypocalcaemia due to hypoparathyroidism, where phosphate tends to be high","Symptoms reflect neuromuscular irritability, including perioral and peripheral paraesthesia, muscle cramps, carpopedal spasm, and in severe cases seizures or laryngospasm","Chvostek and Trousseau signs may be elicitable, and severe hypocalcaemia can prolong the QT interval and cause arrhythmia","Risk is greatest in patients with high preoperative PTH and markedly raised bone turnover markers, large adenomas, or significant preoperative bone disease","Management requires substantial and often prolonged calcium replacement together with active vitamin D, and correction of coexisting magnesium deficiency","It must be distinguished from surgical hypoparathyroidism, which is a separate cause of post-operative hypocalcaemia"],"pathophysiology":"Hungry bone syndrome develops when chronic overstimulation of bone by excess parathyroid hormone, as in long-standing primary or secondary hyperparathyroidism, is suddenly removed, typically by parathyroidectomy but occasionally after other treatments that rapidly lower PTH. During the period of hyperparathyroidism, high bone turnover leaves the skeleton demineralised and primed for repair. Once PTH falls abruptly, bone resorption ceases while bone formation continues, so calcium, phosphate, and magnesium are rapidly deposited into the remineralising skeleton. This net influx into bone outstrips supply, producing the characteristic combination of hypocalcaemia, hypophosphataemia, and hypomagnesaemia that can persist for days to weeks until skeletal mineral demand is met.","additionalDifferentials":["Post-surgical hypoparathyroidism (with typically high phosphate)","Vitamin D deficiency causing hypocalcaemia","Hypomagnesaemia impairing PTH secretion and action","Acute pancreatitis with hypocalcaemia","Sepsis or critical illness related hypocalcaemia","Medication effects such as bisphosphonate-induced hypocalcaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial serum calcium (ionised or corrected) monitoring in the postoperative period","Serum phosphate and magnesium to identify the characteristic pattern","Intact PTH to distinguish hungry bone syndrome from hypoparathyroidism","Preoperative markers of bone turnover and PTH to assess baseline risk","25-hydroxyvitamin D level, as deficiency increases risk and needs correction","ECG monitoring when hypocalcaemia is severe or symptomatic"],"redFlags":["Tetany, laryngospasm, or seizures from severe symptomatic hypocalcaemia","Prolonged QT interval or cardiac arrhythmia","Rapidly falling calcium in the early postoperative period","Refractory hypocalcaemia unresponsive to replacement until magnesium is corrected"],"prognosis":"The syndrome is usually self-limiting once bone mineral demand is satisfied, but recovery can take days to several weeks and occasionally longer, requiring intensive and sustained calcium and vitamin D replacement; with appropriate monitoring and treatment, outcomes are generally good, and identifying at-risk patients preoperatively allows anticipatory management.","pitfalls":["Mistaking it for surgical hypoparathyroidism, missing the low-phosphate, low-magnesium pattern","Underestimating the magnitude and duration of calcium replacement required","Failing to correct hypomagnesaemia, which perpetuates hypocalcaemia","Not anticipating the syndrome in high-risk patients with severe preoperative bone disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"huntington_disease":{"name":"Huntington disease","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HD","Huntington's chorea","chronic progressive hereditary chorea"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal dominant neurodegenerative disorder classically presenting in mid-adult life with the triad of a movement disorder, cognitive decline, and psychiatric disturbance","The characteristic movement disorder is chorea, though rigidity and bradykinesia predominate in juvenile and late-stage disease","Caused by an expanded CAG trinucleotide repeat in the huntingtin (HTT) gene, and genetic testing confirms the diagnosis","The disease shows anticipation, with larger repeat expansions producing earlier onset, particularly with paternal transmission","Psychiatric features including depression, irritability, apathy, and a raised suicide risk often precede overt motor signs","Management is symptomatic and multidisciplinary; agents that deplete or block dopamine can reduce disabling chorea while psychiatric symptoms are treated on their own merits","Predictive genetic testing requires careful pre- and post-test counselling because of profound psychological and familial implications","MRI typically shows atrophy of the caudate and putamen with enlargement of the frontal horns of the lateral ventricles"],"pathophysiology":"Huntington disease results from an expanded CAG repeat in the HTT gene encoding a polyglutamine tract in the huntingtin protein. The mutant protein exerts a toxic gain of function, causing progressive neuronal loss most marked in the medium spiny neurons of the striatum (caudate and putamen), with later cortical involvement. Disruption of basal ganglia circuitry underlies the chorea and other movement abnormalities, while cortical and striatal degeneration drives cognitive and psychiatric decline.","additionalDifferentials":["Other genetic choreas including Huntington disease-like syndromes and neuroacanthocytosis","Sydenham chorea and other autoimmune/post-infectious choreas","Drug-induced chorea or tardive dyskinesia","Wilson disease","Systemic causes of chorea such as thyrotoxicosis, polycythaemia, and antiphospholipid syndrome","Dentatorubral-pallidoluysian atrophy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for the CAG repeat expansion in HTT, with appropriate counselling","MRI of the brain to assess striatal atrophy","Screening for reversible or secondary causes of chorea when the diagnosis is uncertain (thyroid function, copper studies, autoimmune and antiphospholipid screen, blood film for acanthocytes)","Psychiatric assessment including screening for depression and suicide risk","Neuropsychological evaluation to characterise cognitive impairment"],"redFlags":["Emergent depression, hopelessness, or expressed suicidal ideation requiring urgent psychiatric intervention","Rapid functional decline, dysphagia, and aspiration risk in advanced disease","Juvenile-onset (Westphal) variant presenting with rigidity, seizures, and rapid progression","Psychosis or severe behavioural disturbance placing the patient or others at risk"],"prognosis":"Huntington disease is relentlessly progressive over one to two decades from motor onset, leading to severe motor, cognitive, and functional decline. Death commonly results from complications such as aspiration pneumonia; treatment remains symptomatic without proven disease-modifying therapy in routine practice.","pitfalls":["Attributing early psychiatric or behavioural changes to a primary psychiatric illness and missing the underlying diagnosis","Performing predictive genetic testing without adequate counselling and consideration of familial impact","Failing to recognise and treat depression and suicide risk, which are major sources of morbidity","Overlooking the rigid, non-choreic presentation of juvenile-onset or advanced disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"hydrocele":{"name":"Hydrocele","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hydrocoele","scrotal hydrocele","processus vaginalis fluid collection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A hydrocele is a collection of serous fluid within the tunica vaginalis, presenting as a painless, fluctuant scrotal swelling that transilluminates brightly","A key examination finding is the ability to get above the swelling, distinguishing it from an inguinoscrotal hernia, and the testis is often impalpable within a large tension hydrocele","In infants, communicating hydroceles arise from a patent processus vaginalis and commonly resolve spontaneously within the first year or two of life","Adult hydroceles are usually idiopathic (primary) but may be secondary to underlying pathology such as tumour, infection, trauma, or torsion, so the testis must be assessed","Ultrasound is indicated when the testis cannot be palpated or when a secondary cause is suspected, to exclude an underlying testicular tumour","A rapidly developing or blood-stained hydrocele after trauma or in a young man should prompt evaluation for testicular malignancy or haematocele","Treatment principles include observation for asymptomatic cases, with surgical repair (hydrocelectomy) preferred for large or symptomatic adult hydroceles and aspiration reserved for those unfit for surgery due to high recurrence","In children, persistent or symptomatic hydroceles beyond infancy are managed by ligation of the patent processus via an inguinal approach"],"pathophysiology":"A hydrocele results from an imbalance between secretion and absorption of fluid within the tunica vaginalis surrounding the testis. Congenital (communicating) hydroceles occur when the processus vaginalis fails to obliterate, allowing peritoneal fluid to track into the scrotum. Non-communicating hydroceles arise from excess fluid production or impaired reabsorption by the tunica. Secondary hydroceles develop as a reactive effusion in response to underlying testicular or epididymal disease such as tumour, infection, torsion, or trauma. Chronic large hydroceles may occasionally impair testicular perfusion or spermatogenesis.","additionalDifferentials":["Inguinoscrotal hernia","Testicular tumour with reactive hydrocele","Epididymal cyst or spermatocele","Varicocele","Haematocele (post-traumatic)","Epididymo-orchitis with reactive effusion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transillumination of the scrotal swelling","Scrotal ultrasound when the testis is impalpable or a secondary cause is suspected","Assessment for underlying infection, trauma, or torsion as clinically indicated","Tumour markers if testicular malignancy is a concern on imaging"],"redFlags":["An impalpable testis within the hydrocele, mandating ultrasound to exclude tumour","Rapidly enlarging, tense, or blood-stained (haematocele) swelling following trauma","A reactive hydrocele masking an underlying testicular malignancy","Signs of associated infection or torsion requiring urgent evaluation"],"prognosis":"Infantile communicating hydroceles usually resolve spontaneously; primary adult hydroceles are benign and surgery is generally curative, whereas the outlook of secondary hydroceles depends on the underlying cause.","pitfalls":["Assuming a scrotal swelling is a simple hydrocele without confirming a normal underlying testis","Aspirating an adult hydrocele as definitive treatment, given the high recurrence and risk of missing tumour","Failing to recognise a reactive hydrocele overlying a testicular tumour","Confusing a hydrocele with an inguinoscrotal hernia when unable to get above the swelling","Operating on infantile hydroceles prematurely before allowing spontaneous resolution"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"hydrocephalus":{"name":"Hydrocephalus","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["obstructive hydrocephalus","communicating hydrocephalus","normal pressure hydrocephalus","NPH"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Hydrocephalus reflects an imbalance between cerebrospinal fluid production and absorption or a blockage to its flow, producing ventricular enlargement","It is broadly divided into obstructive (non-communicating), where flow is blocked within the ventricular system, and communicating, where absorption at the arachnoid granulations is impaired","Acute obstructive hydrocephalus is a neurosurgical emergency presenting with headache, vomiting, reduced consciousness, and papilloedema from rapidly rising intracranial pressure","In infants, before fusion of the sutures, features include an enlarging head circumference, bulging fontanelle, splayed sutures, and the sunsetting sign of downward-deviated eyes","Normal pressure hydrocephalus classically presents in older adults with the triad of gait apraxia, cognitive decline, and urinary incontinence, and gait often improves after CSF drainage","Treatment principles include relieving the obstruction where possible and diverting CSF, commonly by ventriculoperitoneal shunting or endoscopic third ventriculostomy for suitable obstructive cases","Shunt complications such as infection, blockage, over-drainage, and disconnection are common and should be considered in any shunted patient who deteriorates","Imaging helps distinguish true hydrocephalus with raised pressure from ex vacuo ventricular enlargement due to brain atrophy"],"pathophysiology":"Hydrocephalus is an abnormal accumulation of cerebrospinal fluid within the ventricular system, usually resulting in ventricular dilatation and, when acute, raised intracranial pressure. CSF is produced largely by the choroid plexus, circulates through the ventricles and subarachnoid space, and is absorbed at the arachnoid granulations. Obstruction along this pathway (for example at the aqueduct, fourth ventricular outflow, or by a mass or blood clot) causes non-communicating hydrocephalus, while impaired absorption after haemorrhage, infection, or inflammation causes communicating hydrocephalus. Rising pressure and ventricular expansion compress adjacent white matter and, if unrelieved, can lead to herniation.","additionalDifferentials":["Cerebral atrophy with ex vacuo ventriculomegaly","Intracranial mass causing focal obstruction","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension","Neurodegenerative dementia (in suspected NPH)","Shunt malfunction in a previously treated patient"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT or MRI brain to assess ventricular size, the level of obstruction, and periventricular changes","MRI with CSF flow studies when aqueductal or outflow obstruction is suspected","Head circumference monitoring and cranial ultrasound in infants with an open fontanelle","Large-volume lumbar puncture or external drainage trial to predict shunt response in suspected NPH","Shunt series and imaging to evaluate suspected shunt malfunction"],"redFlags":["Acute obstructive hydrocephalus with falling conscious level, severe headache, and vomiting requiring emergency CSF diversion","Signs of impending herniation from rapidly rising intracranial pressure","Rapidly enlarging head with bulging fontanelle and sunsetting eyes in an infant","Shunt malfunction or shunt infection presenting with headache, drowsiness, or fever in a shunted patient"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the cause, speed of onset, and timeliness of treatment; well-selected patients do well after CSF diversion, but outcomes are limited by the underlying pathology and by recurrent shunt complications, and untreated acute obstruction can be rapidly fatal.","pitfalls":["Mistaking ex vacuo ventriculomegaly from atrophy for treatable hydrocephalus","Delaying CSF diversion in acute obstructive hydrocephalus while awaiting further tests","Overlooking shunt malfunction as a cause of deterioration in a shunted patient","Attributing the NPH triad solely to ageing or dementia and missing a reversible component"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"hyperbaric_and_diving_medicine":{"name":"Hyperbaric and Diving Medicine","system":"Environmental and Occupational Medicine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HBO2T","hyperbaric oxygen therapy","decompression sickness","the bends","DCS","cerebral arterial gas embolism","CAGE","diving barotrauma","dysbarism"],"chapter":476,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3760","clinicalPearls":["Hyperbaric oxygen therapy (HBO2T) means breathing physician-prescribed medical-grade oxygen while the whole body is enclosed in a chamber pressurized to at least 2.0 ATA; most regimens use 2-2.8 ATA for roughly 90-120 minutes per session.","At 2.8 ATA, arterial Po2 can theoretically rise above ~2025 mmHg and dissolved plasma oxygen to ~60 mL/L, enough to sustain life without hemoglobin in animal studies, which sharply improves oxygen diffusion through tissues.","Even a single HBO2T exposure produces durable cell-signaling effects (on fibroblasts, leukocytes, angiogenesis, antioxidant defenses) that persist for hours after oxygen levels return to baseline, mediated by reactive oxygen and nitrogen species.","The UHMS recognizes 15 accepted indications, including air/gas embolism, carbon monoxide poisoning, gas gangrene, crush injury, decompression sickness, problem wounds, intracranial abscess, necrotizing soft tissue infection, refractory osteomyelitis, delayed radiation injury, and sudden sensorineural hearing loss.","For chronic problem wounds, candidate selection is often guided by transcutaneous oximetry around the wound while breathing air and oxygen at pressure; in diabetic patients a transcutaneous Po2 threshold of <50 mmHg may be more appropriate than the usual <40 mmHg cutoff.","Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin with more than 200 times the affinity of oxygen and shifts the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve leftward; HBO2T shortens the COHb half-life to about 21 min at 2.0 ATA versus roughly 5.5 h breathing air at sea level.","Admission carboxyhemoglobin levels confirm CO exposure but do NOT reliably predict severity or prognosis; delayed neuropsychological sequelae can appear after days to months of apparent recovery.","Ambient pressure increases by 1 atmosphere (101.3 kPa) for every 10.1 m of seawater, so a diver at 20 m experiences about 3 ATA.","In diving, the maximum acceptable inspired Po2 is generally considered 1.6 ATA (lower than therapeutic HBO2T pressures) because immersion and exercise raise the risk of oxygen-toxic seizures; helium replaces nitrogen at depth to cut narcosis and gas density.","Cerebral arterial gas embolism (CAGE) from pulmonary barotrauma presents within minutes of surfacing with loss of consciousness, confusion, hemiplegia, visual or speech disturbance, and can be mistaken for stroke or TIA by clinicians unfamiliar with diving.","Decompression sickness occurs in roughly 1 in 5000-10,000 recreational dives; most cases are mild (limb pain, fatigue, patchy paresthesias), and serious DCS usually appears within 60 min of surfacing.","Symptoms beginning more than 24 h after a dive are very unlikely to be DCS unless the diver was subsequently exposed to reduced ambient pressure such as air travel or higher-altitude surface travel.","About 25% of adults have a patent foramen ovale; right-to-left shunting (PFO, atrial septal defect, intrapulmonary shunt) lets venous bubbles 'arterialize' and raises the risk of cerebral, spinal cord, inner ear, and skin DCS manifestations."],"pathophysiology":"Hyperbaric oxygen exerts effects through both physical mechanisms and biochemical cell signaling. Physically, raising ambient pressure shrinks gas bubbles per Boyle's law and steepens inert-gas diffusion gradients from bubble to tissue to blood and lungs, the basis for treating decompression sickness and arterial gas embolism. The markedly elevated arterial Po2 fully saturates hemoglobin and greatly increases dissolved plasma oxygen, improving diffusion into hypoxic and irradiated tissue. Beyond simple oxygen delivery, intermittent hyperoxia generates reactive oxygen species (superoxide, hydrogen peroxide) and reactive nitrogen species (nitric oxide) that drive cytokine and angiogenic growth-factor production, stimulate fibroblast activity, enhance macrophage bacterial killing, mobilize vasculogenic progenitor cells from marrow, and paradoxically produce anti-inflammatory effects such as reduced leukocyte beta-2 integrin adhesion. Hyperoxia also causes vasoconstriction and an osmotic gradient that reduce tissue edema and is directly toxic or bacteriostatic to anaerobes. In late radiation tissue injury, irradiation produces fibrosis and obliterative endarteritis leaving hypovascular hypoxic tissue (an alternative model emphasizes cytokine-driven fibrosis from oxidative stress and mitochondrial dysfunction); HBO2T's most important benefit is promoting angiogenesis and increasing microvascular density. In carbon monoxide poisoning, CO impairs oxygen carriage, acts as an anesthetic, disrupts cellular oxidative processes, binds heme proteins including cytochrome a/a3, and peroxidizes brain lipids, with brain and heart most vulnerable. In diving, breathing gas at elevated ambient pressure loads inert gas (usually nitrogen) into tissues; if washout during ascent lags the falling ambient pressure, tissues become supersaturated and bubbles form, causing mechanical distortion of tissues and, in the venous circulation, inflammatory and coagulation cascades, endothelial injury, capillary leak, hemoconcentration, and pulmonary vascular obstruction. Barotrauma arises when noncompliant gas-filled spaces fail to equalize with changing ambient pressure.","additionalDifferentials":["Stroke or transient ischemic attack (can mimic or be mimicked by cerebral arterial gas embolism)","Inner ear barotrauma versus vestibulocochlear decompression sickness (both cause sensorineural hearing loss and vertigo)","Middle ear barotrauma (should never be invoked to explain post-dive sensorineural hearing loss or true vertigo)","Acute coronary syndrome or musculoskeletal injury versus DCS limb pain","Other causes of post-dive neurologic symptoms not explained by an alternative mechanism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Ischemic stroke / TIA mimicking CAGE","Migraine or other transient neurologic events after diving","Decompression sickness mimicking CAGE and vice versa (clinically the distinction is unimportant because first aid and definitive management are identical)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Transcutaneous oximetry (transcutaneous Po2 mapping) around a wound on air and on oxygen at pressure to assess HBO2T suitability","Ankle-brachial index and skin perfusion pressure as alternative wound perfusion assessments","Carboxyhemoglobin level in suspected CO poisoning (confirms exposure but does not gauge severity or prognosis)","Arterial blood-gas analysis for metabolic acidosis in CO poisoning (a marker of poor prognosis)","Audiometry / otologic evaluation for suspected inner ear barotrauma","Dive profile review combined with clinical examination (there are no useful confirmatory diagnostic tests for DCS)"],"redFlags":["Neurologic symptoms after diving (loss of consciousness, confusion, hemiplegia, visual or speech disturbance) suggesting CAGE or serious DCS — discuss with a diving medicine specialist and consider recompression","Spinal cord involvement in DCS (weakness, paralysis, bladder/sphincter dysfunction) which frequently causes permanent disability","Pulmonary and cardiovascular DCS manifestations (cough, dyspnea, hypotension, hemoconcentration, coagulopathy) which can be life-threatening","Sensorineural hearing loss or true vertigo with nausea, vomiting, nystagmus, ataxia after diving (inner ear barotrauma or vestibulocochlear DCS)","Untreated pneumothorax before any hyperbaric exposure (intrapleural gas can expand and tension on decompression)","Tonic-clonic seizure during HBO2T or diving from cerebral oxygen toxicity, sometimes preceded by facial twitching, anxiety, and agitation","Moderate-to-severe CO poisoning, especially age over 35, prolonged exposure, acidosis, or loss of consciousness"],"prognosis":"Most HBO2T adverse events are mild and self-limiting, and the therapy is generally safe. For late radiation tissue injury pooled data suggest improved tissue healing and reduced wound dehiscence in jaw osteoradionecrosis. About 70% of indolent problem ulcers are substantially improved or healed 6 months after a course, and meta-analyses report increased complete healing and reduced amputation of diabetic foot ulcers. In CO poisoning the most rigorous trial showed fewer cognitive sequelae at 6 weeks (25% vs 46%) and 12 months (18% vs 32%) with HBO2T, though a Cochrane review found overall evidence insufficient. Survivors of CO poisoning may develop delayed neuropsychological sequelae; risk factors for poor outcome are age over 35, exposure beyond 24 h, acidosis, and loss of consciousness. In diving, CAGE often shows substantial or complete early resolution as bubbles involute, but secondary deterioration or re-embolization can occur. Most DCS cases are mild, but spinal cord DCS frequently causes permanent disability and pulmonary/cardiovascular forms can be fatal.","pitfalls":["Attributing post-dive sensorineural hearing loss or true vertigo to middle ear barotrauma — these point instead to inner ear barotrauma or vestibulocochlear DCS.","Misdiagnosing CAGE as a typical stroke or TIA when the clinician is unfamiliar with diving medicine.","Relying on admission carboxyhemoglobin level to judge severity or prognosis of CO poisoning.","Assuming there is a confirmatory diagnostic test for DCS — diagnosis rests on dive profile, symptom timing, and examination.","Treating CAGE/DCS distinction as clinically critical — first aid and definitive management are the same for both.","Compressing a patient with an untreated pneumothorax or with gas-trapping lesions such as bullae without careful risk assessment and, for pneumothorax, a patent chest drain.","Overstating the risk of pulmonary oxygen toxicity from remote bleomycin exposure; published experience suggests the relationship may have been exaggerated, though recent bleomycin or mitomycin C with pneumonitis warrants careful evaluation.","Promoting HBO2T (sometimes with air) as a panacea for unproven indications, general well-being, or anti-aging — practice should be confined to accepted, periodically updated indications."],"severityClassification":"Decompression sickness manifestations (Harrison Table 476-2) are grouped by organ system: musculoskeletal (limb pain); neurologic cerebral (confusion, visual and speech disturbances), spinal (weakness, paralysis, upper motor neuron signs, bladder/sphincter dysfunction, dermatomal sensory disturbance, abdominal/girdle pain), vestibulocochlear (hearing loss, vertigo, ataxia, nausea/vomiting), and peripheral (patchy nondermatomal sensory disturbance); pulmonary (cough, dyspnea); cardiovascular (hemoconcentration, coagulopathy, hypotension); cutaneous (rash, itch); lymphatic (localized soft tissue edema); and constitutional (fatigue, malaise). Most cases are mild; serious DCS usually manifests within 60 min of surfacing.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3760","Harrison 22e p.3761","Harrison 22e p.3762","Harrison 22e p.3763","Harrison 22e p.3764","Harrison 22e p.3765","Harrison 22e p.3766","Harrison 22e p.3767","Harrison 22e p.3768"],"crossLinks":[]},"hyperemesis_gravidarum":{"name":"Hyperemesis gravidarum","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HG","severe pregnancy sickness","intractable vomiting of pregnancy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Hyperemesis gravidarum is severe, persistent nausea and vomiting of early pregnancy causing dehydration, weight loss (often more than 5 percent of pre-pregnancy weight), and electrolyte disturbance","It typically begins in the first trimester and is a diagnosis reached after excluding other causes of vomiting","Ketonuria, hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, and a hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis from loss of gastric acid are characteristic","Association with high levels of human chorionic gonadotrophin means it is more common in multiple and molar pregnancies","Management is supportive: rehydration with intravenous fluids and electrolyte correction, antiemetics, and thiamine to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy","Thiamine supplementation before giving glucose-containing fluids is essential to avoid precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy","A stepwise antiemetic approach is used, escalating therapy for refractory cases, with nutritional support if intake remains inadequate","Transient biochemical hyperthyroidism can occur due to the thyroid-stimulating activity of hCG and usually needs no antithyroid treatment"],"pathophysiology":"The cause is multifactorial. Rising human chorionic gonadotrophin and oestrogen are strongly implicated, explaining the peak in the first trimester and the higher frequency in molar and multiple pregnancies where hCG is elevated. Genetic factors, including variants affecting the appetite-regulating hormone GDF15, and altered gut motility contribute. Protracted vomiting leads to loss of fluid, hydrogen, chloride, and potassium ions, producing dehydration, hypokalaemia, and a hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis, while starvation drives ketosis and depletes thiamine stores.","additionalDifferentials":["Molar pregnancy (gestational trophoblastic disease)","Gastroenteritis or other gastrointestinal infection","Urinary tract infection or pyelonephritis","Thyrotoxicosis of another cause","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Hepatobiliary or pancreatic disease and raised intracranial pressure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine dipstick for ketones and assessment of hydration","Serum electrolytes, urea, and creatinine","Full blood count and liver function tests","Thyroid function tests where clinically indicated","Pelvic ultrasound to confirm viability and exclude multiple or molar pregnancy"],"redFlags":["Severe dehydration, oliguria, or haemodynamic instability","Confusion, ataxia, or ophthalmoplegia suggesting Wernicke encephalopathy","Refractory vomiting with significant weight loss and inability to maintain any oral intake","Severe electrolyte derangement or acid-base disturbance","Haematemesis from Mallory-Weiss tears or signs of an alternative serious diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Most cases settle by the second trimester and, with adequate rehydration and antiemetic therapy, have a good outcome for mother and baby. Severe untreated disease can cause serious maternal complications including Wernicke encephalopathy, and recurrence in subsequent pregnancies is common.","pitfalls":["Giving intravenous glucose before thiamine and precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy","Attributing all vomiting to pregnancy without excluding molar pregnancy, infection, or metabolic causes","Undertreating dehydration and electrolyte loss","Overtreating the transient hCG-mediated hyperthyroidism with antithyroid drugs","Overlooking the substantial psychological impact and risk of inadequate nutrition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"hypernatremia":{"name":"Hypernatraemia","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hypernatremia","hypertonic dehydration","elevated serum sodium"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Hypernatraemia reflects a deficit of water relative to sodium and almost always signifies a problem with water intake or excessive water loss rather than sodium excess","Because thirst is a powerful defence, sustained hypernatraemia usually occurs in those who cannot access or request water, such as infants, the elderly, and patients with impaired consciousness","The main causes are pure or hypotonic water loss (renal or extrarenal), including diabetes insipidus, and less commonly a gain of sodium","Assessment of urine osmolality helps localise the problem: dilute urine in the face of hypernatraemia points to a renal concentrating defect such as diabetes insipidus","Clinical features are predominantly neurological, ranging from lethargy and irritability to seizures and coma, reflecting brain cell shrinkage","Management principles are to restore water gradually, address ongoing losses, and treat the underlying cause, while calculating and replacing the water deficit","Correction of chronic hypernatraemia must be slow because the brain accumulates osmoles to adapt, and rapid rehydration risks cerebral oedema","Diabetes insipidus is distinguished into central and nephrogenic forms by the response to desmopressin, which guides treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Hypernatraemia results from a relative water deficit that raises plasma osmolality. Normally, a rise in osmolality stimulates thirst and antidiuretic hormone release, restoring water balance; hypernatraemia therefore develops when water is unavailable or the thirst mechanism is impaired, or when water losses overwhelm intake. Losses may be extrarenal (through the gut, skin, or lungs) or renal, the latter including osmotic diuresis and the failure to concentrate urine in diabetes insipidus. Central diabetes insipidus reflects deficient antidiuretic hormone production, whereas the nephrogenic form reflects renal resistance to the hormone. Less commonly, administration or ingestion of excess sodium produces hypernatraemia with volume expansion. The rise in extracellular osmolality draws water out of cells; shrinkage of brain cells produces the neurological manifestations, and the brain's compensatory accumulation of intracellular solutes over time is what makes rapid correction hazardous.","additionalDifferentials":["Central diabetes insipidus","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","Osmotic diuresis (hyperglycaemia, high protein feeds, mannitol)","Extrarenal water loss from gastrointestinal or insensible routes","Inadequate water intake in dependent patients","Sodium overload from hypertonic fluids or salt ingestion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum sodium and plasma osmolality with assessment of volume status","Urine osmolality and urine volume to characterise water handling","Water deprivation testing and response to desmopressin when diabetes insipidus is suspected","Blood glucose and review of osmotic loads or hypertonic fluids","Assessment of renal function and other electrolytes","Neuroimaging or pituitary evaluation when central diabetes insipidus is considered"],"redFlags":["Depressed consciousness, seizures, or coma indicating severe hypernatraemia","Rapidly rising sodium or profound water deficit","Signs of severe volume depletion with haemodynamic compromise","Overly rapid correction risking cerebral oedema"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on the severity, rate of onset, and underlying condition. Acute severe hypernatraemia and hypernatraemia in critically ill or elderly patients carry significant mortality, largely reflecting the underlying illness. With appropriate identification of the cause and carefully paced water replacement, milder cases generally recover fully, while cautious correction avoids the danger of cerebral oedema.","pitfalls":["Correcting chronic hypernatraemia too rapidly and causing cerebral oedema","Failing to account for ongoing water losses when calculating replacement","Overlooking diabetes insipidus in a patient passing large volumes of dilute urine","Assuming hypernatraemia means too much sodium rather than too little water"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"hypersensitivity_pneumonitis":{"name":"Hypersensitivity Pneumonitis & Eosinophilic Lung Disease","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["extrinsic allergic alveolitis","eosinophilic pneumonia","ABPA"],"chapter":299,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2228","clinicalPearls":["Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (HP, extrinsic allergic alveolitis) results from inhalational exposure to a wide range of antigens, producing an inflammatory response of the alveoli and small airways; systemic symptoms such as fever and fatigue can accompany the respiratory complaints (p.2228).","Sensitization to an inhaled antigen (specific circulating IgG) is necessary but NOT sufficient to define HP, because many sensitized individuals never develop disease (p.2228-2229).","There is an as-yet-unexplained DECREASED risk of developing HP in smokers (p.2229).","US risk groups include farmers (farmer's lung: thermophilic actinomycetes/Aspergillus from grain, moldy hay, silage), bird owners (bird fancier's lung: feather, dropping and serum-protein antigens), industrial workers (chemical worker's lung: diphenylmethane and toluene diisocyanate), and hot tub users; mycobacteria can cause HP rather than frank infection in hot tub lung and metalworking-fluid HP (p.2229).","Recent guidelines advocate ABANDONING the acute/subacute/chronic terminology in favor of categorizing HP as either nonfibrotic or fibrotic; cluster analysis splits patients into a recurrent-systemic-symptom group and a more-severe-respiratory group (p.2229).","Antigen avoidance generally results in resolution of symptoms; acute symptoms may resolve within hours to days, while a more subacute course resolves over weeks to months (p.2229).","Chronic/fibrotic HP can present insidiously with progressive dyspnea, cough, fatigue, weight loss and digital clubbing, and—unlike other forms—can have an irreversible respiratory component NOT responsive to antigen removal; bird-antigen exposure tends toward a fibrotic phenotype while farmer's lung may show an emphysematous phenotype (p.2229).","Diagnosis depends foremost on a history of exposure to an offending antigen that correlates with symptoms; a careful occupational and home exposure history is essential and may be supplemented by a clinician visit to the work or home environment, with reexposure sometimes used to confirm the diagnosis (p.2229).","BAL lymphocytosis is characteristic of HP though not specific; most cases have a CD4+/CD8+ ratio <1, but a lower lymphocyte threshold should be applied in active smokers (p.2230).","Characteristic HP histology is loose, poorly defined NONcaseating granulomas near small airways—contrasting with the well-formed granulomas of sarcoidosis—plus a patchy lymphocyte-predominant interstitial infiltrate and bronchiolitis with organizing exudate (p.2230).","HP Study Group clinical prediction rule: six significant predictors, the strongest being exposure to a known HP antigen; others are serum precipitins, recurrent symptoms, symptoms 4-8 h after exposure, inspiratory crackles, and weight loss (p.2230).","Pulmonary eosinophilic syndromes share pulmonary infiltrates plus increased eosinophils in tissue, sputum and/or BAL; the first classifying step is distinguishing PRIMARY disorders from eosinophilia SECONDARY to a drug, infection, malignancy, or another pulmonary condition such as asthma (p.2231).","Acute eosinophilic pneumonia (AEP): acute febrile illness <1 month with hypoxemic respiratory failure (often needing ventilation), diffuse infiltrates and BAL eosinophilia >25%; typically affects men aged 20-40 with NO asthma history and is often linked to recent tobacco initiation, vaping, or dust from indoor renovation (p.2231).","AEP is often initially mistaken for acute lung injury/ARDS until BAL reveals >25% eosinophils; peripheral eosinophilia is often ABSENT at presentation but appears 7-30 days later; it is distinguished by absence of organ failure beyond the lungs, high corticosteroid responsiveness, excellent prognosis, and recovery without relapse (p.2231-2232).","Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia (CEP): indolent illness, mostly female nonsmokers (mean age 45), many with asthma; the 'photographic negative of pulmonary edema' (peripheral/pleural-based opacities) is pathognomonic but seen in <25% of patients; ~90% have peripheral eosinophilia and >50% relapse, often needing prolonged corticosteroids (p.2232).","EGPA (formerly Churg-Strauss): eosinophilic vasculitis with asthma and multi-organ involvement (lung, heart, skin, GI, nerve, kidney); peripheral eosinophilia >1000/uL (or >10% WBC) and ANCA (perinuclear/anti-MPO) in about one-third of patients; cardiac, GI and renal involvement portend a poorer prognosis (p.2232-2233).","ABPA is an eosinophilic disorder from allergic sensitization to Aspergillus, presenting asthmatically with brownish mucus plugs; it features prominent peripheral eosinophilia, IgE >500 (often >1000) IU/mL, and Aspergillus sensitivity—central bronchiectasis is a classic finding but is NOT required for diagnosis; consider ABPA in refractory asthma and as a complication of cystic fibrosis (p.2233-2234).","Loffler syndrome is transient pulmonary infiltrates with eosinophilia from helminthic larvae passing through the lung, most commonly Ascaris (also hookworm); symptoms are generally self-limited (p.2234)."],"pathophysiology":"HP is an immune-mediated reaction to inhaled antigens small enough to deposit in distal airways and alveoli, characterized by dysregulated TH1 and TH17 responses. Precipitating IgG antibodies indicate adaptive immunity, but innate mechanisms also contribute, with Toll-like receptors and downstream MyD88 signaling driving neutrophil recruitment. No clear genetic basis is established, though polymorphisms in antigen-processing genes (TAP1, MHC class II) occur in some cohorts. In chronic HP, bone marrow-derived fibrocytes may contribute to inflammation and fibrosis, and chronic HP shares the MUC5B promoter polymorphism with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. In the pulmonary eosinophilic syndromes, the eosinophil is central; pathology shows tissue eosinophil infiltration, and in EGPA extravascular granulomas and necrotizing vasculitis with fibrinoid necrosis/thrombosis may involve lung, heart, skin, muscle, liver, spleen and kidney. These syndromes are thought to arise from dysregulated eosinophilopoiesis or autoimmunity (elevated IgE, IgG4, rheumatoid factor; heightened T-cell immunity); IL-5 is hypothesized to be etiologic, and ANCAs binding vascular walls likely contribute to vascular injury in EGPA (p.2229, 2231).","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF) - hard to distinguish from fibrotic HP even on biopsy; in HP the lung bases are frequently spared","Nonspecific interstitial pneumonitis (NSIP)","Sarcoidosis - distinguished by prominent hilar adenopathy, extrapulmonary involvement and well-formed noncaseating granulomas","Organic toxic dust syndrome (OTDS) - neither requires prior sensitization nor shows specific IgG antibodies","Acute lung injury / ARDS (mimicked by acute eosinophilic pneumonia)","Hypereosinophilic syndromes (HES) and EGPA when nonrespiratory features accompany an eosinophilic pneumonia"],"infectionMimics":["Respiratory infection (nonfibrotic HP can be mistaken for it)","Loffler syndrome from Ascaris or hookworm larvae","Strongyloides stercoralis (chronic infection and, in immunocompromised/steroid-treated hosts, fatal hyperinfection)","Paragonimiasis, filariasis (tropical eosinophilia from Wuchereria), visceral larval migrans","Nonparasitic infections: coccidioidomycosis, paracoccidioidomycosis, basidiobolomycosis, tuberculosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Sarcoidosis","Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia","Pulmonary Langerhans cell granulomatosis","Drug/toxin reaction (NSAIDs, systemic antibiotics, particulate metals, inhalational drugs of abuse, post-radiation pneumonitis)","Malignancy-associated eosinophilia (leukemia, lymphoma, lung cancer, adenocarcinoma/squamous cell carcinoma)","Systemic disease (rheumatoid arthritis, Sjogren syndrome)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Careful occupational/home/avocational exposure history (cornerstone of HP diagnosis), possibly with site visit","HRCT: ground-glass opacities and centrilobular nodules in nonfibrotic HP, expiratory air trapping; reticular changes/traction bronchiectasis and basal-sparing honeycombing in fibrotic HP","Pulmonary function tests (restrictive OR obstructive; not diagnostic but track impairment and response; DLCO may be markedly reduced in fibrotic HP)","Serum precipitins / specific IgG to suspected antigens (presence alone insufficient; limited panels give false negatives)","Bronchoscopy with BAL (lymphocytosis, CD4/CD8 <1 in most)","Lung biopsy (transbronchial, cryobiopsy, or surgical) for noncaseating granulomas - not absolutely required","For eosinophilic syndromes: blood eosinophil count, BAL eosinophil percentage (>25% in AEP; ~60% mean in CEP), ESR/CRP/IgE, ANCA (anti-MPO in EGPA), and as needed echocardiogram, electromyogram, bone marrow biopsy","ABPA: total IgE (>500, often >1000 IU/mL), Aspergillus skin test/specific IgE, Aspergillus precipitins/specific IgG, chest imaging for central bronchiectasis and mucus impaction"],"redFlags":["Hypoxemic respiratory failure with diffuse infiltrates in a previously healthy young man - consider acute eosinophilic pneumonia","Progressive dyspnea, weight loss and digital clubbing with honeycombing - chronic/fibrotic HP that may be irreversible and IPF-like","Asthma with neuropathy (mononeuritis multiplex), purpura, cardiac or renal involvement and eosinophilia - EGPA; cardiac involvement portends worse prognosis and up to 50% of untreated EGPA die within 3 months","Refractory asthma with brownish mucus plugs and very high IgE - evaluate for ABPA","Eosinophilia in an immunocompromised or steroid-treated patient - risk of fatal Strongyloides hyperinfection"],"prognosis":"HP that has not progressed to chronic lung disease has a favorable outcome with likely resolution if antigen avoidance is achieved; chronic HP with lung fibrosis has a poorer prognosis, with chronic pigeon breeder's lung showing mortality similar to IPF. Acute eosinophilic pneumonia has an excellent prognosis with near-complete recovery and no recurrence. Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia responds rapidly to corticosteroids but >50% of patients relapse. In EGPA, up to 50% of untreated patients die within 3 months of diagnosis whereas treated patients have >70% six-year survival; clinical remission is achievable in >90% with ~25% relapsing, and myocardial, GI and renal involvement portend a poor prognosis. HES respond less reliably to corticosteroids (<50% first-line responders) (p.2229, 2232, 2233).","pitfalls":["Failing to obtain a detailed exposure history - the single most important step, as the strongest HP predictor is exposure to a known antigen","Relying on specific IgG/precipitins alone: asymptomatic exposed individuals (farmers, pigeon breeders) can be IgG positive, and limited antigen panels often yield false negatives","Expecting a particular PFT pattern - HP can be restrictive OR obstructive, so PFTs do not establish the diagnosis","Misdiagnosing AEP as ARDS/acute lung injury because peripheral eosinophilia is often absent at presentation (BAL eosinophilia is the key)","Treating EGPA-prone severe asthma with corticosteroids can mask systemic eosinophilia and vasculitis and delay the EGPA diagnosis","Confusing fibrotic HP with IPF/NSIP even on biopsy (basal sparing favors HP)"],"severityClassification":"Current guidance categorizes HP as nonfibrotic versus fibrotic (replacing the older acute/subacute/chronic scheme). Acute eosinophilic pneumonia diagnostic criteria: acute febrile respiratory illness <1 month, hypoxemic respiratory failure, diffuse infiltrates on chest x-ray, BAL eosinophilia >25%, absence of parasitic/fungal/other infection, absence of drugs known to cause pulmonary eosinophilia, quick corticosteroid response, and failure to relapse after stopping corticosteroids.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2228","Harrison 22e p.2229","Harrison 22e p.2230","Harrison 22e p.2231","Harrison 22e p.2232","Harrison 22e p.2233","Harrison 22e p.2234"],"crossLinks":["ild","aspergillosis","asthma_exac"]},"hyperthyroidism_and_other_causes_of":{"name":"Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Thyrotoxicosis","Graves' disease","Toxic multinodular goiter","Toxic adenoma","Hyperthyroidism"],"chapter":396,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3032","clinicalPearls":["Thyrotoxicosis means a state of thyroid hormone excess and is not synonymous with hyperthyroidism, which specifically denotes excessive thyroid gland function; the major hyperthyroid causes are Graves' disease, toxic multinodular goiter, and toxic adenoma (Harrison 22e p.3032).","Graves' disease accounts for roughly 60-80% of thyrotoxicosis, occurs in up to 2% of women (about one-tenth as frequent in men), and typically presents between 20 and 50 years of age (Harrison 22e p.3032).","Graves' hyperthyroidism is driven by thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins acting on the TSH receptor; TSH receptor antibodies (TRAb) can be measured and, because they cross the placenta, are useful for monitoring pregnant Graves' patients for neonatal thyrotoxicosis (Harrison 22e p.3033).","Symptoms in descending frequency include hyperactivity/irritability, heat intolerance with sweating, palpitations, fatigue and weakness, weight loss with increased appetite, and diarrhea; signs include tachycardia (atrial fibrillation in the elderly), tremor, goiter, warm moist skin, and proximal myopathy (Harrison 22e p.3033).","Apathetic thyrotoxicosis in the elderly may present mainly with fatigue and weight loss and can be mistaken for depression; features may be subtle or masked in older patients (Harrison 22e p.3033).","Treating the thyrotoxic state alone converts atrial fibrillation to normal sinus rhythm in up to about 75% of patients without underlying cardiac disease (Harrison 22e p.3033).","Graves' ophthalmopathy (thyroid eye disease) is specific to Graves'; about one-third of patients have clinical eye disease, it can occur without hyperthyroidism in ~10%, and onset is within the year before or after thyrotoxicosis diagnosis in 75% (Harrison 22e p.3033).","Destructive (subacute or silent) thyroiditis causes a transient thyrotoxic phase from release of preformed hormone with characteristically LOW radionuclide uptake and elevated thyroglobulin; true hyperthyroidism is absent (Harrison 22e p.3035).","Subacute (de Quervain's) thyroiditis classically evolves through three phases over about 6 months: thyrotoxic, then hypothyroid, then recovery; it is supported by a high ESR and a tender goiter, and the T4/T3 ratio is higher (T3 less disproportionately elevated) than in Graves' or thyroid autonomy (Harrison 22e p.3038).","Toxic multinodular goiter typically affects elderly patients with subclinical or mild overt hyperthyroidism, often presenting with atrial fibrillation; iodine exposure (e.g., contrast dye) can precipitate thyrotoxicosis, and the scan shows heterogeneous patchy uptake (Harrison 22e p.3042).","A toxic (hyperfunctioning) adenoma usually causes only mild thyrotoxicosis, generally detectable when the nodule exceeds about 3 cm, and over 90% harbor activating somatic mutations in the TSH receptor or GSalpha (Harrison 22e p.3043).","Mild hypercalcemia occurs in up to 20% of thyrotoxic patients (hypercalciuria is more common), and longstanding thyrotoxicosis causes osteopenia with a small increase in fracture rate due to direct stimulation of bone resorption (Harrison 22e p.3033)."],"pathophysiology":"Graves' disease results from a combination of genetic susceptibility (HLA-DR and immunoregulatory genes such as CTLA-4, CD25, CD40, PTPN22, plus the TSH-R gene) and environmental triggers (smoking, stress, sudden iodine excess, the postpartum period, and immune reconstitution after HAART, alemtuzumab, or immune checkpoint inhibitors). Hyperthyroidism is caused by thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins that bind and stimulate the TSH receptor; coexisting lymphocytic thyroiditis (TPO and thyroglobulin antibodies in up to 80%) means there is no direct correlation between TSI level and hormone levels. Ophthalmopathy reflects activated T-cell infiltration of extraocular muscles with cytokine-driven (IFN-gamma, TNF, IL-1) fibroblast activation, glycosaminoglycan accumulation that traps water and causes muscle swelling, increased orbital fat, and late irreversible fibrosis; the TSH-R is an orbital autoantigen and aberrant IGF-1 receptor signaling on orbital fibroblasts contributes. In destructive thyroiditis, follicular disruption releases preformed hormone (low radionuclide uptake). Toxic multinodular goiter reflects acquired functional autonomy of nodules, while toxic adenoma reflects constitutive cyclic AMP signaling from activating TSH-R or GSalpha mutations.","additionalDifferentials":["Graves' disease (diffuse goiter, ophthalmopathy, positive TRAb, high diffuse radionuclide uptake)","Toxic multinodular goiter (elderly, heterogeneous uptake)","Toxic adenoma (focal hot nodule, suppressed surrounding uptake)","Subacute (de Quervain's) thyroiditis (painful tender goiter, high ESR, low uptake)","Silent/postpartum thyroiditis (painless, low uptake, TPO antibodies, normal ESR)","Thyrotoxicosis factitia (exogenous hormone; low thyroglobulin, low uptake)","Iodine-induced (Jod-Basedow) thyrotoxicosis and amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis","TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma (non-suppressed TSH with high T4)","Struma ovarii and functioning metastatic follicular carcinoma (ectopic thyroid tissue)"],"infectionMimics":["Subacute viral thyroiditis from mumps, coxsackie, influenza, adenovirus, echovirus, or SARS-CoV-2, which can mimic pharyngitis with sore throat and fever during the thyrotoxic phase (Harrison 22e p.3038)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Panic attacks","Mania","Pheochromocytoma","Weight loss associated with malignancy","Depression (especially apathetic thyrotoxicosis in the elderly)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum TSH (suppressed) with unbound (free) T4 and T3; isolated T3 elevation indicates T3 toxicosis (2-5%), and isolated T4 elevation can occur with iodine-induced disease","TSH receptor antibodies (TRAb) to confirm Graves' when classic features are absent","Radionuclide (99mTc, 123I, or 131I) thyroid scan and uptake to distinguish high diffuse uptake of Graves' from low uptake of destructive thyroiditis, factitious thyrotoxicosis, and to identify toxic adenoma or toxic MNG","Color-flow Doppler ultrasonography (increased flow in hyperthyroidism vs destructive thyroiditis) when avoiding radioactivity","ESR (elevated in subacute thyroiditis, normal in silent thyroiditis) and thyroglobulin (low in factitia)","Orbital CT or MRI and ophthalmologic assessment (lid-fissure width, corneal fluorescein staining, extraocular muscle/Hess chart, intraocular pressure, visual fields, acuity, color vision) for ophthalmopathy","Pituitary CT/MRI when TSH is non-suppressed with elevated T4 (suspected TSH-secreting adenoma)","Fine-needle aspiration of cold nodules in toxic MNG when sonographic features warrant"],"redFlags":["Thyrotoxic crisis (thyroid storm): fever, delirium, seizures, coma, vomiting, diarrhea, and jaundice with a high mortality (about 4-17% even with treatment)","New or worsening atrial fibrillation or high-output heart failure, particularly in the elderly or those with preexisting cardiac disease","Sight-threatening ophthalmopathy from optic nerve compression (papilledema, peripheral field defects, loss of vision) or corneal ulceration/exposure","Thyrotoxic hypokalemic periodic paralysis (notably in Asian males)","Diplopia or severe proptosis with corneal exposure during sleep"],"prognosis":"Untreated, clinical features generally worsen and mortality was historically 10-30% before effective therapy. Some patients with mild Graves' disease have spontaneous relapses and remissions, and about 15% of those who enter remission develop hypothyroidism 10-15 years later from ongoing autoimmune destruction. Ophthalmopathy follows an independent course, typically worsening over 3-6 months, plateauing over the next 12-18 months, then partially improving, though up to ~5% have a fulminant course. Subacute thyroiditis usually resolves completely, with late permanent hypothyroidism in about 15%. Thyrotoxic crisis carries a high mortality (about 4-17%) even with treatment, due to cardiac failure, arrhythmia, or hyperthermia. Toxic multinodular goiter and toxic adenoma do not remit spontaneously and require definitive therapy.","pitfalls":["Equating thyrotoxicosis with hyperthyroidism: destructive thyroiditis, factitia, and iodine excess cause hormone excess without true gland hyperfunction (low uptake)","Missing apathetic thyrotoxicosis in the elderly, where it can present as fatigue/weight loss or be mistaken for depression or malignancy","Overlooking that there is no direct correlation between TSI level and hormone levels because coexisting thyroiditis affects function","Interpreting thyroid function tests during acute severe illness (sick euthyroid/nonthyroidal illness) as true thyroid disease; routine testing in acutely ill patients should be avoided, though an undetectable TSH (<0.01 mIU/L) makes primary thyroid disease more likely","Failing to recognize that radioiodine therapy can worsen ophthalmopathy in a small proportion of patients, especially smokers","Attributing clubbing to thyroid acropachy in a Graves' patient who lacks coincident dermopathy and ophthalmopathy, where another cause should be sought"],"severityClassification":"Graves' ophthalmopathy is graded by the NO SPECS mnemonic (0 no signs/symptoms; 1 only signs such as lid retraction/lag; 2 soft tissue involvement/periorbital edema; 3 proptosis >22 mm; 4 extraocular muscle involvement/diplopia; 5 corneal involvement; 6 sight loss), although activity-based systems such as EUGOGO are preferred for monitoring and treatment decisions. Thyrotoxicosis itself ranges from subclinical (low TSH, normal free T4/T3) to overt disease and may escalate to thyrotoxic crisis/thyroid storm.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3032","Harrison 22e p.3033","Harrison 22e p.3034","Harrison 22e p.3035","Harrison 22e p.3038","Harrison 22e p.3039","Harrison 22e p.3042","Harrison 22e p.3043"],"crossLinks":[]},"hypertrophic_cardiomyopathy":{"name":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HCM","hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy","HOCM","asymmetric septal hypertrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["HCM is an inherited condition of unexplained left ventricular hypertrophy, classically asymmetric and septal, not explained by loading conditions such as hypertension or aortic stenosis","It is a leading cause of sudden cardiac death in young people and athletes and may be the first manifestation of the disease","Symptoms include exertional breathlessness, chest pain, palpitations, presyncope, and syncope, though many patients are asymptomatic","A subset has dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction that worsens with reduced preload or afterload and increased contractility, producing a murmur that intensifies with manoeuvres that decrease venous return","Because obstruction is dynamic, agents that reduce preload or afterload can worsen the gradient and should be used cautiously","Echocardiography and cardiac MRI define hypertrophy, obstruction, systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve, and fibrosis","Sudden death risk stratification guides consideration of an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator, taking account of family history, unexplained syncope, wall thickness, non-sustained VT, and other markers","Management includes symptom control, treatment of obstruction, screening for arrhythmia, and family screening with genetic counselling given autosomal dominant inheritance"],"pathophysiology":"HCM is most often caused by mutations in genes encoding sarcomeric proteins, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with variable penetrance. The result is myocyte hypertrophy and disarray with interstitial fibrosis, producing a thickened, stiff, non-compliant left ventricle and diastolic dysfunction. Asymmetric septal hypertrophy, together with systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve, can create dynamic obstruction of the left ventricular outflow tract and associated mitral regurgitation. Myocardial fibrosis and disarray provide a substrate for ventricular arrhythmia and sudden death, while microvascular dysfunction contributes to ischaemia despite normal epicardial arteries.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypertensive heart disease with concentric hypertrophy","Aortic stenosis","Athlete's heart (physiological hypertrophy)","Infiltrative or storage cardiomyopathies such as amyloidosis and Fabry disease","Other causes of a dynamic or systolic murmur","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography to assess hypertrophy pattern, outflow gradient, systolic anterior motion, and diastolic function","Cardiac MRI for wall thickness and fibrosis characterisation","12-lead ECG, which is frequently abnormal even in early disease","Ambulatory monitoring for non-sustained ventricular tachycardia","Exercise testing including assessment of provocable obstruction and blood pressure response","Genetic testing and cascade family screening"],"redFlags":["Syncope, particularly exertional or unexplained, indicating high arrhythmic risk","Sustained or non-sustained ventricular tachycardia, or a family history of premature sudden death","Aborted sudden cardiac death","Severe outflow obstruction or progression to heart failure"],"prognosis":"Many patients have a near-normal life expectancy, but HCM carries risks of sudden cardiac death, atrial fibrillation with embolism, and progression to heart failure; risk stratification and defibrillator therapy in high-risk individuals substantially reduce sudden death, and outcomes are worse with extensive fibrosis or progression to a dilated, hypokinetic end stage.","pitfalls":["Using preload- or afterload-reducing agents that can worsen dynamic outflow obstruction","Misclassifying HCM as physiological athlete's heart or hypertensive hypertrophy","Missing an infiltrative mimic such as cardiac amyloidosis","Neglecting sudden death risk assessment and family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"hypertrophic_scar":{"name":"Hypertrophic scar","system":"Plastic / Reconstructive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hypertrophic scarring","abnormal scar","pathological scar"],"chapter":null,"source":"Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery / standard references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A hypertrophic scar is raised, red, and often itchy but remains confined within the boundaries of the original wound, unlike a keloid which extends beyond the wound margins","Hypertrophic scars typically appear within weeks of injury and tend to improve or partially regress over months to years, whereas keloids persist and often recur","Risk is increased by wounds under tension, wounds crossing joints or perpendicular to skin tension lines, delayed healing, infection, and burns","Prevention centres on meticulous wound closure, tension reduction, and avoiding infection and delayed healing","First-line management commonly includes silicone gel sheeting, pressure therapy, and scar massage, with intralesional corticosteroid injection for thicker or symptomatic scars","Adjuncts such as laser therapy and, in selected cases, surgical revision can improve appearance, though surgery alone risks recurrence without additional measures","Burn scars are particularly prone to hypertrophy and contracture, and early pressure garments and physiotherapy help limit deformity","Realistic counselling about the prolonged and often incomplete nature of scar improvement is important for patient expectations"],"pathophysiology":"Hypertrophic scarring reflects a dysregulated wound-healing response in which the proliferative phase is prolonged and remodelling is impaired. Excessive and persistent inflammation drives fibroblast proliferation and overproduction of collagen, with an imbalance between collagen synthesis and breakdown. The collagen is deposited in comparatively organised bundles aligned parallel to the skin surface, distinguishing it histologically from the thick, disorganised collagen of keloids. Mechanical tension across the wound and prolonged inflammation from infection or delayed healing promote this fibrotic response, and myofibroblast activity contributes to scar contraction.","additionalDifferentials":["Keloid scar","Dermatofibroma","Scar contracture","Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans","Foreign body granuloma within a scar","Cutaneous sarcoidosis in a scar"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment and scar scoring (e.g. Vancouver Scar Scale) for baseline and monitoring","Photographic documentation to track evolution and treatment response","Biopsy when the diagnosis is uncertain or malignancy is suspected","Assessment of joint range of motion when scars cross a joint or cause contracture"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth, ulceration, or atypical change suggesting malignancy rather than a simple scar","Progressive contracture across a joint causing functional loss","Signs of secondary infection within the scar or wound"],"prognosis":"Hypertrophic scars often mature and flatten over one to two years and generally respond better to conservative therapy than keloids, with a lower recurrence rate after appropriately combined treatment. Outcomes are less favourable for burn-related scars and scars under tension or across joints, which may require ongoing management to prevent contracture.","pitfalls":["Confusing a hypertrophic scar with a keloid, leading to inappropriate treatment expectations","Performing surgical revision alone without adjuncts, resulting in recurrence","Delaying preventive measures such as pressure garments in burn scars","Repeated or excessive corticosteroid injection causing skin atrophy, telangiectasia, or hypopigmentation","Overlooking functional contracture while focusing only on cosmetic appearance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"hyphema":{"name":"Hyphema","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hyphaema","blood in anterior chamber"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined as blood in the anterior chamber, usually seen as a layered red fluid level in the inferior chamber when the patient is upright","Most commonly follows blunt ocular trauma, but spontaneous hyphema should prompt consideration of neovascularisation, tumour, or a bleeding tendency","Grading is by the height of the layered blood; a total chamber filled with dark clotted blood is termed an eight-ball hyphema","Raised intraocular pressure is a major early concern, particularly in sickle cell disease where even modest pressures endanger the optic nerve","Rebleeding, typically within the first several days, tends to be more severe than the original bleed and worsens prognosis","Management principles include rest with head elevation, limiting activity, shielding the eye, and close monitoring of pressure; avoiding antiplatelet and anticoagulant agents where possible","Corneal blood staining and glaucoma are the main sight-threatening complications requiring surgical washout if uncontrolled","All patients of African, Mediterranean, or Middle Eastern descent should be screened for sickle cell status"],"pathophysiology":"Hyphema results from disruption of iris or ciliary body vessels, most often by blunt trauma that causes rapid globe compression and a sudden shift of intraocular structures, tearing vessels at the iris root or angle. Red cells settle inferiorly under gravity. Clot formation and subsequent fibrinolysis create a window of rebleeding risk. Blood and inflammatory debris obstruct the trabecular meshwork, raising intraocular pressure; in sickle cell disease, red cells sickle in the low-oxygen, acidotic aqueous, further impairing outflow and increasing susceptibility to optic nerve damage. Prolonged high pressure with an intact endothelial insult leads to corneal blood staining.","additionalDifferentials":["Traumatic microhyphema without layering","Neovascular disease (diabetic retinopathy, retinal vein occlusion, ocular ischaemia)","Intraocular tumour (e.g. retinoblastoma in children, melanoma)","Bleeding diathesis or anticoagulation","Herpetic or other uveitis with bleeding","Juvenile xanthogranuloma of the iris in children"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination to grade the hyphema and detect associated trauma","Intraocular pressure measurement and serial monitoring","Gonioscopy (deferred until rebleeding risk passes) to assess angle recession","Dilated fundus examination or B-scan ultrasound if the view is obscured, to exclude posterior segment injury","Sickle cell screening in at-risk patients","Coagulation studies when a bleeding tendency is suspected"],"redFlags":["Uncontrolled raised intraocular pressure threatening the optic nerve, especially in sickle cell disease","Rebleeding with a larger, darker hyphema","Early corneal blood staining indicating endothelial compromise","Signs of globe rupture or associated posterior segment injury","Spontaneous hyphema in a child raising concern for retinoblastoma"],"prognosis":"Small hyphemas usually clear without sequelae, but larger bleeds, rebleeding, sickle cell disease, and sustained pressure elevation carry a higher risk of glaucoma, corneal staining, and permanent visual loss.","pitfalls":["Failing to screen for sickle cell disease, in which tolerable pressures for others become sight-threatening","Missing associated injuries such as angle recession, lens dislocation, or globe rupture","Underestimating rebleeding risk and permitting excessive activity","Overlooking a spontaneous hyphema as a marker of neovascular disease or tumour"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"hypogonadism_male":{"name":"Male hypogonadism","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["testosterone deficiency","androgen deficiency","primary hypogonadism","secondary (hypogonadotropic) hypogonadism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Symptoms range from reduced libido, erectile dysfunction, and infertility to fatigue, low mood, and loss of muscle mass and bone density","The core classification is primary (testicular) versus secondary (hypothalamic-pituitary) hypogonadism, guided by gonadotrophin levels","High LH and FSH with low testosterone indicate primary testicular failure, whereas low or inappropriately normal gonadotrophins indicate a central cause","Diagnosis requires a low morning total testosterone confirmed on a repeat sample, as levels have a diurnal rhythm and vary day to day","Interpretation should account for sex hormone-binding globulin, since altered SHBG can make total testosterone misleading and free testosterone more informative","Acute illness, obesity, sleep deprivation, and certain drugs transiently lower testosterone and should not be mistaken for true hypogonadism","Secondary hypogonadism warrants evaluation for pituitary disease, hyperprolactinaemia, and iron overload, with imaging when indicated","Testosterone replacement can relieve symptoms but suppresses spermatogenesis, so fertility goals must be addressed before starting therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Male hypogonadism is deficient testicular function affecting testosterone production, sperm production, or both. Primary hypogonadism arises from disease of the testes themselves, such as Klinefelter syndrome, cryptorchidism, orchitis, trauma, chemotherapy, or radiation, and is characterised by low testosterone with compensatory elevation of LH and FSH. Secondary (hypogonadotropic) hypogonadism arises from impaired hypothalamic or pituitary signalling, from congenital GnRH deficiency, pituitary tumours or infiltration, hyperprolactinaemia, haemochromatosis, or suppression by systemic illness and drugs, and shows low testosterone with low or inappropriately normal gonadotrophins. Testosterone deficiency reduces libido and sexual function, impairs spermatogenesis, and, if prolonged, decreases muscle mass, bone density, and erythropoiesis.","additionalDifferentials":["Obesity and metabolic syndrome lowering SHBG and total testosterone","Functional suppression from acute or chronic illness","Hyperprolactinaemia suppressing the gonadal axis","Erectile dysfunction of vascular, neurogenic, or psychogenic origin with normal testosterone","Depression or thyroid dysfunction mimicking symptoms","Opioid- or glucocorticoid-induced suppression of the axis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting morning total testosterone, repeated to confirm a low result","LH and FSH to classify primary versus secondary hypogonadism","SHBG with calculated or measured free testosterone when total levels are equivocal","Serum prolactin and iron studies in suspected secondary hypogonadism","Semen analysis when fertility is a concern","Karyotype for suspected Klinefelter syndrome and pituitary MRI for central causes"],"redFlags":["Visual field defects or headache suggesting a pituitary or hypothalamic mass","Low testosterone with hyperprolactinaemia pointing to a prolactinoma","Features of haemochromatosis such as arthropathy, skin pigmentation, and diabetes","Very low testosterone with fragility fractures indicating significant bone loss"],"prognosis":"Outcomes depend on cause and reversibility; central hypogonadism from a treatable pituitary lesion or hyperprolactinaemia may recover, whereas primary testicular failure is usually permanent, and appropriate testosterone replacement improves symptoms, body composition, and bone health when fertility is not the immediate goal.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing hypogonadism from a single non-fasting or afternoon testosterone sample","Ignoring SHBG effects so that total testosterone misrepresents androgen status","Starting testosterone in a man wishing to conceive, thereby suppressing sperm production","Overlooking a treatable central cause such as prolactinoma or iron overload"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"hyponatremia_siadh":{"name":"Hyponatraemia and SIADH","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SIADH","syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion","hyponatremia","hyponatraemia","inappropriate ADH secretion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Hyponatraemia is the most common electrolyte disturbance in hospitalised patients and reflects a relative excess of water compared with sodium, usually driven by impaired water excretion","The diagnostic approach begins by confirming true hypotonic hyponatraemia, then assessing volume status and measuring urine osmolality and urine sodium","SIADH is a euvolaemic hypotonic hyponatraemia with inappropriately concentrated urine, elevated urine sodium, and normal thyroid and adrenal function, diagnosed after excluding other causes","Common triggers of SIADH include malignancy, pulmonary disease, central nervous system disorders, and numerous drugs","The severity of symptoms depends more on how quickly sodium falls than on the absolute value, ranging from mild confusion to seizures and cerebral oedema in acute severe cases","The cornerstone of management in SIADH is fluid restriction, with the underlying cause treated wherever possible","Correction must be gradual in chronic hyponatraemia because overly rapid rises in sodium risk osmotic demyelination, whereas acute symptomatic hyponatraemia with severe neurological signs warrants prompt but controlled correction with hypertonic saline","Exclude pseudohyponatraemia and hyperglycaemia-related dilution before attributing a low sodium to a water-handling disorder"],"pathophysiology":"Hyponatraemia develops when water intake exceeds the kidney's ability to excrete free water, diluting plasma sodium. Antidiuretic hormone is central: when its release is appropriate (as in volume depletion or reduced effective circulating volume in heart failure and cirrhosis) or inappropriate (as in SIADH), water is retained and urine remains concentrated. In SIADH, ADH is secreted or acts independently of osmotic and volume signals, so the kidney retains water despite plasma hypotonicity; patients are clinically euvolaemic because the retained water distributes across body compartments and mild volume expansion promotes natriuresis. The fall in plasma osmolality drives water into brain cells, and the resulting cerebral oedema accounts for the neurological features. In chronic hyponatraemia the brain adapts by extruding solutes, which is why rapid correction can dehydrate neurons and cause osmotic demyelination.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypovolaemic hyponatraemia from renal or extrarenal sodium loss","Hypervolaemic hyponatraemia in heart failure, cirrhosis, or nephrotic syndrome","Hypothyroidism and adrenal insufficiency","Primary polydipsia and low-solute intake","Thiazide-induced hyponatraemia","Pseudohyponatraemia and hyperglycaemia-related dilutional hyponatraemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma osmolality to confirm true hypotonic hyponatraemia","Urine osmolality and urine sodium to assess water handling and volume status","Thyroid function and assessment of adrenal function","Careful clinical evaluation of volume status","Review of medications that can cause hyponatraemia","Investigation for underlying malignancy, pulmonary, or central nervous system disease when SIADH is suspected"],"redFlags":["Seizures, marked confusion, or reduced consciousness indicating severe or acute hyponatraemia","Rapid onset of symptoms suggesting acute cerebral oedema","Signs of raised intracranial pressure","Overly rapid correction risking osmotic demyelination"],"prognosis":"Mild chronic hyponatraemia is often well tolerated but is associated with falls, cognitive impairment, and worse outcomes in the underlying illness. Acute severe hyponatraemia can be life-threatening from cerebral oedema, while inappropriately rapid correction can cause devastating osmotic demyelination. With cause-directed treatment and appropriately paced correction most patients recover, and SIADH prognosis largely follows that of its underlying cause.","pitfalls":["Correcting chronic hyponatraemia too quickly and precipitating osmotic demyelination","Diagnosing SIADH without excluding hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency, and volume depletion","Giving isotonic saline in SIADH, which can paradoxically worsen the sodium","Overlooking a drug or occult malignancy as the driver of persistent SIADH"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"hypoparathyroidism":{"name":"Hypoparathyroidism","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PTH deficiency","hypoparathyroid state","postsurgical hypoparathyroidism","idiopathic hypoparathyroidism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The hallmark is hypocalcaemia with an inappropriately low or undetectable parathyroid hormone level, usually accompanied by hyperphosphataemia","Neck surgery is the most common cause, from inadvertent removal of or damage to the parathyroid glands","Symptoms of hypocalcaemia range from perioral and acral paraesthesiae and muscle cramps to tetany, laryngospasm, and seizures","Chvostek and Trousseau signs are useful bedside indicators of neuromuscular irritability from low calcium","Severe symptomatic or acute hypocalcaemia is a medical emergency requiring intravenous calcium and cardiac monitoring","Long-term management uses calcium and active vitamin D (calcitriol) analogues, aiming to relieve symptoms while avoiding hypercalciuria and renal complications","Autoimmune, genetic, and magnesium-related causes should be considered when there is no surgical history, as hypomagnesaemia impairs PTH secretion and action"],"pathophysiology":"Hypoparathyroidism results from deficient secretion or action of parathyroid hormone, most often after surgical injury to the glands but also from autoimmune destruction, genetic syndromes, infiltration, or severe magnesium disturbance. Lack of PTH reduces renal calcium reabsorption and phosphate excretion, decreases bone resorption, and impairs conversion of vitamin D to its active form, together producing hypocalcaemia and hyperphosphataemia. Low ionised calcium increases neuromuscular excitability, causing the characteristic clinical features.","additionalDifferentials":["Vitamin D deficiency causing hypocalcaemia","Chronic kidney disease with mineral bone disorder","Hypomagnesaemia impairing PTH secretion","Pseudohypoparathyroidism (PTH resistance)","Acute pancreatitis or massive transfusion causing hypocalcaemia","Hungry bone syndrome after parathyroidectomy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium (ideally ionised or albumin-corrected) and phosphate","Intact parathyroid hormone level","Serum magnesium","25-hydroxyvitamin D and renal function","ECG to assess for QT prolongation","24-hour urinary calcium during treatment to monitor for hypercalciuria"],"redFlags":["Tetany, laryngospasm, seizures, or carpopedal spasm","Prolonged QT interval or cardiac arrhythmia from acute hypocalcaemia","Rapidly falling calcium in the early postoperative period after neck surgery","Altered mental status or stridor accompanying severe hypocalcaemia"],"prognosis":"With appropriate calcium and active vitamin D therapy most patients maintain adequate calcium and lead normal lives, though lifelong treatment and monitoring are needed and chronic therapy carries risks of hypercalciuria, nephrocalcinosis, and renal impairment.","pitfalls":["Overlooking coexisting hypomagnesaemia, which must be corrected before hypocalcaemia will respond","Interpreting calcium without correcting for albumin or measuring the ionised fraction","Aiming for fully normal serum calcium and inducing hypercalciuria and renal damage","Failing to anticipate and monitor for hypocalcaemia after thyroid or parathyroid surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"hypopituitarism":{"name":"Hypopituitarism","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["panhypopituitarism","Sheehan syndrome"],"chapter":391,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2992","clinicalPearls":["Deficient production of one or more anterior pituitary trophic hormones produces the clinical features of hypopituitarism; most adult cases are acquired (Harrison 22e p.2992).","With pituitary compression or destruction, trophic hormone failure usually occurs sequentially: GH first, then FSH, LH, TSH, and ACTH last; in children growth retardation is often the presenting feature, in adults hypogonadism is the earliest symptom (Harrison 22e p.2992).","Clinical manifestations depend on which hormones are lost and the degree of deficiency: GH deficiency causes growth disorders in children and abnormal body composition in adults; gonadotropin deficiency causes menstrual disorders/infertility/loss of secondary sexual characteristics; TSH deficiency causes hypothyroidism; ACTH deficiency causes secondary adrenal insufficiency; PRL deficiency causes failure of lactation (Harrison 22e p.2996).","Secondary adrenal insufficiency from ACTH deficiency causes hypocortisolism with relative preservation of mineralocorticoid production (unlike primary adrenal failure) (Harrison 22e p.2996).","Pituitary apoplexy is an endocrine emergency: acute intrapituitary hemorrhage/infarction presenting with severe headache, meningeal signs, bilateral visual changes, ophthalmoplegia, and in severe cases cardiovascular collapse and loss of consciousness (Harrison 22e p.2993).","Lymphocytic hypophysitis occurs most often in postpartum women, presents with hyperprolactinemia and an MRI mass resembling an adenoma, and may be transient or permanent; it should be considered before unnecessary surgery in a postpartum woman with a new pituitary mass (Harrison 22e p.2993).","Up to 20% of patients receiving CTLA-4 inhibitor immunotherapy (e.g., ipilimumab) develop hypophysitis; PD-1/PD-L1 inhibitors (pembrolizumab, nivolumab) can also cause it, sometimes with delayed presentation (Harrison 22e p.2993).","Patients on glucocorticoid replacement require careful dose escalation (stress dosing) during acute illness, dental procedures, trauma, and hospitalization (Harrison 22e p.2996)."],"pathophysiology":"Impaired synthesis or secretion of one or more anterior pituitary trophic hormones (GH, FSH, LH, TSH, ACTH, PRL), and sometimes posterior pituitary AVP. Causes are developmental/genetic (transcription factor mutations such as PROP1, PIT-1, TPIT, midline defects) or, more commonly in adults, acquired from compressive mass effect of tumors or from local pituitary/hypothalamic traumatic, autoimmune, inflammatory, infiltrative, or vascular damage. The same processes may impair hypothalamic hormone production with secondary pituitary failure. With progressive compression/destruction, hormone loss is typically sequential (GH > FSH > LH > TSH > ACTH) (Harrison 22e p.2992).","additionalDifferentials":["Pituitary adenoma (mass on MRI may be indistinguishable from lymphocytic hypophysitis)","Craniopharyngioma, Rathke's cyst, meningioma, germinoma, glioma, ependymoma","Pituitary metastases (breast, lung, colon) and lymphoma/leukemia","Empty sella syndrome","Primary (target-organ) endocrine failure as opposed to central deficiency"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis involving the pituitary/hypothalamus","Fungal infection (histoplasmosis) and opportunistic fungal infection in AIDS","Parasitic infection (toxoplasmosis)","Pneumocystis jirovecii","Tertiary syphilis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lymphocytic and granulomatous hypophysitis","Sarcoidosis","Hemochromatosis","Histiocytosis X (Langerhans cell histiocytosis)","Amyloidosis","Checkpoint-inhibitor immunotherapy hypophysitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Low target-organ hormone with low or inappropriately normal trophic hormone (e.g., low free thyroxine with low/inappropriately normal TSH = secondary hypothyroidism; low testosterone without elevated gonadotropins = hypogonadotropic hypogonadism)","Dynamic/provocative testing of pituitary reserve","GH reserve: insulin-induced hypoglycemia, arginine, glucagon, l-dopa, GHRH, or macimorelin; AGHD defined by peak GH response to hypoglycemia <3 ug/L","ACTH/cortisol reserve: most reliably assessed during insulin-induced hypoglycemia; CRH stimulation and cosyntropin (synthetic ACTH) testing also used","Pituitary MRI or CT to identify mass, hemorrhage, stalk deviation, or empty sella; age-matched IGF-1 as an index of GH therapeutic response"],"redFlags":["Pituitary apoplexy: an endocrine emergency that may cause severe hypoglycemia, hypotension/shock, CNS hemorrhage, and death; severe ophthalmoplegia or progressive visual loss indicates need for urgent surgical decompression","Secondary adrenal insufficiency / adrenal crisis: hypocortisolism that can precipitate life-threatening shock, especially during stress, illness, surgery, or trauma","Acute severe headache with meningeal signs, bilateral visual changes, or loss of consciousness in a patient with known/suspected pituitary mass"],"prognosis":"Hormone replacement is usually safe and effective at maintaining homeostasis when it mimics physiologic secretion. However, long-standing pituitary damage carries an increased mortality rate, primarily from cardiovascular and cerebrovascular disease; adult hypopituitarism is associated with an approximately threefold increase in cardiovascular mortality versus age- and sex-matched controls. Prior head/neck irradiation is an additional determinant of increased mortality, especially from cerebrovascular disease. Lymphocytic hypophysitis may resolve with recovery of pituitary function after glucocorticoid treatment depending on extent of damage; hypopituitarism is common after apoplexy (Harrison 22e p.2996).","pitfalls":["Critical sequencing pitfall: in suspected combined deficiency, glucocorticoid replacement must be started BEFORE thyroid hormone, because giving thyroxine first can accelerate cortisol clearance and precipitate an adrenal crisis in a patient with unrecognized secondary adrenal insufficiency (Harrison 22e p.3133).","Insulin-induced hypoglycemia testing must be performed cautiously in suspected adrenal insufficiency (enhanced susceptibility to hypoglycemia/hypotension) and is contraindicated in active coronary artery disease or known seizure disorders (Harrison 22e p.2996).","Lymphocytic hypophysitis may be radiologically indistinguishable from a pituitary adenoma and should be considered in a postpartum woman with a new pituitary mass before surgery is undertaken (Harrison 22e p.2993).","Radiation-induced and brain-injury-related hypopituitarism develops insidiously over 5-15 years (and in 25-40% after brain injury), so long-term periodic endocrine follow-up is required even after apparently successful treatment (Harrison 22e p.2993).","Empty sella patients usually have normal pituitary function, but hypopituitarism may develop insidiously (Harrison 22e p.2994)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.2992","Harrison 22e p.2993","Harrison 22e p.2996","Harrison 22e p.3133"],"crossLinks":["adrenal_crisis"]},"hypothermia_and_peripheral_cold_injuries":{"name":"Hypothermia and Peripheral Cold Injuries","system":"Environmental / Multisystem","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Accidental hypothermia","Primary accidental hypothermia","Secondary hypothermia","Frostbite","Peripheral cold injury","Chilblain","Pernio","Trench foot","Immersion foot","Nonfreezing cold injury"],"chapter":477,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3768","clinicalPearls":["Accidental hypothermia is defined as an unintentional fall in core temperature below 35C (95F); at this threshold the compensatory mechanisms that conserve heat begin to fail. Primary hypothermia follows direct cold exposure of a previously healthy person, while secondary hypothermia complicates a systemic illness or injury and carries a much higher mortality.","Core temperature cannot be reliably predicted from the clinical picture, so confirmation requires direct measurement; a probe in the lower third of the esophagus with continuous monitoring is preferred because rectal and bladder readings lag behind true core changes.","Severity grading by core temperature: mild 35C to 32.2C (initial tachycardia then bradycardia, shivering, apathy, dysarthria, impaired judgment, cold diuresis); moderate 32.2C to 28C (declining consciousness, pupillary dilation, paradoxical undressing, atrial/ventricular arrhythmias, J-wave changes, loss of protective airway reflexes); severe below 28C (coma, loss of cerebrovascular autoregulation, maximal risk of ventricular fibrillation and asystole, apnea, poikilothermia).","Vital signs are difficult to interpret without the core temperature: respiratory rate should be low because CO2 production declines, so persistent hyperventilation suggests a CNS lesion or organic acidosis, and tachycardia out of proportion to temperature suggests secondary causes such as hypoglycemia, hypovolemia, or toxin overdose.","Paradoxical undressing (inappropriate removal of clothing in response to cold stress) is a classic maladaptive behavior; cold-induced ileus and rectus abdominis spasm can mimic or mask an acute abdomen, and confusion or combativeness resolves faster with rewarming than with chemical or physical restraint.","The J (Osborn) wave at the QRS-ST junction supports the diagnosis and is frequently misread by automated ECG software as an injury current.","Laboratory values are systematically distorted by cold: hematocrit rises about 2% per 1C drop (masking anemia), white-cell sequestration and marrow suppression can mask infection, and coagulation tests run at 37C appear deceptively normal despite a genuine in-vivo coagulopathy, so platelet and fresh-frozen-plasma transfusion is ineffective for the cold-induced coagulopathy.","Arterial blood gases should NOT be temperature-corrected; an uncorrected pH of 7.42 and PCO2 of 40 mmHg reflect appropriate ventilation and acid-base balance at any core temperature, and overzealous hyperventilation is a common error given depressed CO2 production.","Frostbite occurs when tissue temperature falls below 0C (32F): ice-crystal formation distorts cellular architecture, endothelial damage produces microvascular thrombosis, and after thawing there is progressive dermal ischemia, arteriovenous shunting, edema, and superficial necrosis, with mummification and demarcation taking weeks to months.","Frostbite always produces a sensory deficit (light touch, pain, or temperature); superficial frostbite causes anesthesia and erythema without tissue loss, whereas vesiculation with surrounding edema/erythema indicates deeper injury and hemorrhagic vesicles signal severe microvascular damage that may lead to amputation.","Chilblain (pernio) is a nonfreezing injury from repetitive damp-cold exposure above freezing, most common in young women with a history of Raynaud phenomenon, producing erythema, edema, pruritus, then plaques, blue nodules, and ulcerations on the dorsa of hands and feet.","Trench foot / immersion foot results from repetitive wet-cold exposure above freezing; feet are initially cyanotic, cold, and edematous, with bullae progressing to ulceration and liquefaction gangrene, and survivors may have hyperhidrosis, cold sensitivity, and painful ambulation for years."],"pathophysiology":"Heat is lost by radiation (55-65%), conduction (10-15%, markedly increased in cold water), convection (increased with wind), respiration, and evaporation. The preoptic anterior hypothalamus orchestrates thermoregulation: the immediate autonomic defense releases norepinephrine, raises muscle tone, and triggers shivering to increase the basal metabolic rate, while cutaneous cold reception drives reflex vasoconstriction; prolonged cold also stimulates the thyroid axis. When these defenses are overwhelmed, hypothermia first stimulates and then progressively depresses every organ system. In peripheral freezing injury, tissue freezing causes ice-crystal formation that destroys cellular architecture; endothelial damage leads to stasis and microvascular thrombosis, and after thawing progressive dermal ischemia, arteriovenous shunting, rising tissue pressures, edema, and necrosis develop. Nonfreezing injuries (chilblain, trench/immersion foot) arise from neuronal and endothelial damage caused by repetitive cold exposure above the freezing point.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypoglycemia","Hypothyroidism / myxedema coma","Adrenal insufficiency and hypopituitarism","Sepsis (hypothermia is a poor prognostic sign)","Drug or ethanol intoxication / overdose","Diabetic ketoacidosis and other metabolic acidoses","Uremia","CNS injury (cerebrovascular accident, subarachnoid hemorrhage, hypothalamic lesion, spinal cord injury)","Shapiro syndrome (agenesis of corpus callosum with episodic hypothermia)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cold-induced ileus with rectus abdominis spasm mimicking an acute abdomen","Areflexia attributed to hypothermia masking a spinal cord lesion","J (Osborn) waves misinterpreted as an injury current / ischemia","Trench/immersion foot bullae indistinguishable from frostbite","Shaking rigors of infection mistaken for shivering"],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous core temperature monitoring, ideally esophageal (lower third); rectal/bladder as ancillary","ECG (look for J/Osborn wave, bradydysrhythmias, atrial and ventricular arrhythmias)","Arterial blood gas read uncorrected for temperature","Serum potassium (prognostic in arrest), glucose, electrolytes, renal function","Coagulation studies, fibrinogen, and platelet count (interpret with caution)","Hematocrit (artificially elevated by cold) and white-cell count","Bedside echocardiography and end-tidal CO2 to confirm perfusion status","For frostbite: angiography, fluorescence microangiography, technetium-99 / pyrophosphate bone scan, or MR angiography to assess tissue viability and demarcation"],"redFlags":["Core temperature below 28C (severe): coma, maximal risk of ventricular fibrillation and asystole","Potassium >12 mmol/L during resuscitation indicates widespread cell lysis and grave prognosis","Core temperature <10-12C, pH <6.5, or fibrinogen <0.5 g/L may preclude continued resuscitation","Hypothermia complicating sepsis is a poor prognostic sign","Asphyxia preceding cooling (e.g., avalanche burial) is the strongest negative predictor of survival","Hemorrhagic vesicles or persistent cyanosis after rewarming in frostbite (risk of amputation, compartment syndrome)","Frostbite with rhabdomyolysis and completely frozen extremities"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on whether hypothermia is primary or secondary, the precipitating cause, and the presence of asphyxia. Secondary hypothermia complicating systemic illness carries much higher mortality, and hypothermia with sepsis is a poor prognostic sign. Because cold itself is protective, survival has been reported after cardiac arrest times exceeding 7 hours, and intact neurologic recovery has occurred at extremely low core temperatures; the patient is not dead until warm and dead. Markers of a grave prognosis during resuscitation include potassium >12 mmol/L (cell lysis), pH <6.5, fibrinogen <0.5 g/L, and core temperature <10-12C, while preceding asphyxia (avalanche) is the most important negative predictor. The HOPE score uses six covariates to predict which arrested hypothermic patients may benefit from extracorporeal life support. Frostbite sequelae often persist as paresthesia, thermal misperception, and hyperhidrosis, with delayed nail deformities, cutaneous carcinomas, and epiphyseal damage in children.","pitfalls":["Correcting arterial blood gases for temperature; the uncorrected values guide management","Overzealous hyperventilation despite depressed CO2 production, dangerously raising pH","Applying direct heat to extremities in chronic severe hypothermia, which causes peripheral vasodilation and core 'afterdrop'","Transfusing platelets or fresh-frozen plasma for the cold coagulopathy (ineffective, as the defect is temperature-dependent enzyme inhibition)","Deep insertion of central venous catheters or pulmonary artery catheters into the cold heart, which can precipitate refractory arrhythmias or perforation","Trusting coagulation tests or hematocrit at face value (assays run at 37C; hematocrit falsely elevated)","Premature termination of frostbite thawing because reperfusion is intensely painful","Repeated dosing of drugs (e.g., digoxin, insulin) that are ineffective while cold but become toxic during rewarming"],"severityClassification":"Hypothermia is graded by core temperature: Mild = 35C (95F) to 32.2C (90F); Moderate = below 32.2C (90F) to 28C (82.4F); Severe = below 28C (82.4F). Frostbite is classified as superficial (anesthesia and erythema, no tissue loss) versus deep (vesiculation, hemorrhagic vesicles, possible amputation); an alternative scheme grades by the proximal extent of presenting cyanosis: grade 1 none, grade 2 distal phalanx, grade 3 to the metacarpophalangeal joint, grade 4 proximal to the MP joint.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3768","Harrison 22e p.3769","Harrison 22e p.3770","Harrison 22e p.3771","Harrison 22e p.3772"],"crossLinks":[]},"hypothyroidism":{"name":"Hypothyroidism","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Hashimoto thyroiditis","underactive thyroid"],"chapter":395,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3027","clinicalPearls":["In iodine-sufficient regions the most common causes are autoimmune disease (Hashimoto's thyroiditis) and iatrogenic injury (treatment of hyperthyroidism); iodine deficiency remains a leading cause worldwide (p.3027).","Biochemical hallmark of primary hypothyroidism is an elevated TSH with a low unbound (free) T4; a normal TSH excludes primary, but not secondary/central, disease (p.3030-3031).","Subclinical hypothyroidism is biochemical thyroid hormone deficiency (raised TSH, usually still-normal free T4) with few or no symptoms; overt hypothyroidism typically corresponds to TSH >10 mIU/L with a low free T4 (p.3029, p.3032).","Symptoms (descending frequency) include tiredness/weakness, dry skin, cold intolerance, hair loss, poor concentration and memory, constipation, and modest weight gain with poor appetite (the weight gain is mainly fluid retention in myxedematous tissue) (p.3028-3029).","Signs include dry coarse skin, cool extremities, nonpitting puffiness of face/hands/feet (myxedema), diffuse alopecia with thinning of the outer eyebrow, bradycardia, peripheral edema, and delayed relaxation of tendon reflexes (p.3028-3029).","TPO and thyroglobulin antibodies are present in >95% of autoimmune hypothyroidism and confirm the etiology once hypothyroidism is established (p.3030).","Onset is usually insidious, so patients may only recognize symptoms once euthyroidism is restored; Hashimoto's patients may present with goiter (irregular, firm) rather than hypothyroid symptoms (p.3029-3030).","Associated laboratory abnormalities include raised creatine phosphokinase, elevated cholesterol and triglycerides, and a normocytic or macrocytic anemia, which generally resolve with thyroxine replacement (p.3030).","Autoimmune hypothyroidism clusters with other autoimmune disorders, particularly vitiligo, pernicious anemia, Addison's disease (Schmidt's syndrome), type 1 diabetes, and the autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes (p.3030)."],"pathophysiology":"Hypothyroidism is deficient thyroid hormone action, most often from primary thyroid failure. In autoimmune (Hashimoto's) disease, T cell-mediated lymphocytic infiltration with germinal-center formation, follicular atrophy, oxyphil metaplasia, and fibrosis progressively destroy the gland; atrophic thyroiditis represents its fibrotic end stage. As function declines there is a compensated phase in which a rising TSH maintains hormone levels (subclinical hypothyroidism); later free T4 falls and TSH rises further, producing overt disease. Susceptibility reflects genetic factors (HLA-DR3/DR4/DR5, PTPN22, CTLA-4) and environmental factors including high iodine intake. TPO and thyroglobulin antibodies are markers; up to 20% of patients have TSH-receptor-blocking antibodies that prevent TSH binding and can cause thyroid atrophy and, by transplacental passage, transient neonatal hypothyroidism. Increased dermal glycosaminoglycan deposition traps water, producing nonpitting myxedema (Harrison 22e p.3029-3030).","additionalDifferentials":["Hyperthyroidism / thyrotoxicosis (opposite end of thyroid dysfunction; thyroiditis can pass through both phases)","Sick euthyroid (nonthyroidal illness) syndrome","Secondary (central) hypothyroidism from hypopituitarism or hypothalamic disease","Multinodular goiter or thyroid carcinoma (asymmetric Hashimoto goiter may mimic these)","Primary thyroid lymphoma (rare, strongly associated with pre-existing autoimmune thyroiditis)","Depression and dementia (overlapping fatigue, poor concentration, slowing)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Depression","Dementia","Nephrotic syndrome (edema, hyperlipidemia)","Chronic fatigue states","Anemia of other cause"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum TSH as the primary screening test (most sensitive for primary disease)","Free/unbound T4 to confirm clinical hypothyroidism when TSH is elevated (T3 measurement is not indicated)","TPO and thyroglobulin antibodies to establish autoimmune etiology","Lipid panel (cholesterol, triglycerides), creatine phosphokinase, and full blood count for associated abnormalities","Anterior pituitary function testing when central hypothyroidism is suspected (after excluding drug effect and sick euthyroid syndrome)","Thyroid ultrasound to distinguish heterogeneous Hashimoto echotexture from true nodules warranting FNA"],"redFlags":["Myxedema coma — the severe end of untreated hypothyroidism: reduced level of consciousness (sometimes seizures), hypothermia (can reach 23 C), with 20-40% mortality despite treatment; almost always in the elderly and often precipitated by infection, cold, sedatives, or acute cardiopulmonary events.","Hypoventilation with hypoxia and hypercapnia, plus hypoglycemia and dilutional hyponatremia, in profound hypothyroidism.","Impaired adrenal reserve in profound hypothyroidism (consider co-existing adrenal insufficiency before/with thyroid replacement).","Neonatal/congenital hypothyroidism — risk of permanent neurologic damage if treatment is delayed, mandating neonatal screening.","Significant pericardial or other serous effusions, conductive deafness, or carpal tunnel/entrapment syndromes as manifestations of severe disease."],"prognosis":"With levothyroxine replacement and dose titration to a normal TSH, prognosis is excellent and most laboratory and clinical abnormalities resolve, though full symptomatic relief may take months and about 10-15% report persistent symptoms despite biochemical euthyroidism. Untreated congenital hypothyroidism causes permanent neurologic damage, and myxedema coma still carries a 20-40% mortality independent of T4 and TSH levels (Harrison 22e p.3031-3032).","pitfalls":["Central (secondary) hypothyroidism is missed if TSH is used alone: in pituitary/hypothalamic disease TSH may be low, normal, or even slightly raised, so a low free T4 is needed to make the diagnosis and TSH cannot be used to monitor therapy.","A normal TSH excludes primary but NOT secondary/central hypothyroidism.","T4 alone is an inferior screening test because it will not detect subclinical hypothyroidism; T3 is normal in ~25% of patients and is not indicated.","An elevated TSH with normal/high free T4 in a patient on adequate levothyroxine usually reflects poor adherence (taking medication only shortly before testing), not a TSH-secretion disorder.","Confirm that a raised TSH is sustained over ~3 months before treating subclinical hypothyroidism.","TSH should not be relied on in the months immediately after radioiodine for Graves' disease (it stays suppressed); use unbound T4 instead.","External rewarming in myxedema coma can precipitate cardiovascular collapse, and hypotonic fluids can worsen hyponatremia."],"severityClassification":"Spectrum from subclinical hypothyroidism (raised TSH, normal free T4, few/no symptoms) to overt/clinical hypothyroidism (raised TSH usually >10 mIU/L with low free T4 and symptoms); myxedema coma represents the severe, life-threatening end. Etiologically classified as primary (thyroidal), secondary/central (pituitary or hypothalamic), congenital, or transient (Harrison 22e p.3027-3032).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3027"],"crossLinks":[]},"igg4_related_disease":{"name":"IgG4-Related Disease","system":"Immune-Mediated / Multisystem (Rheumatology)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IgG4-RD","IgG4-related systemic disease","Type 1 (IgG4-related) autoimmune pancreatitis","Lymphoplasmacytic sclerosing pancreatitis","Mikulicz's disease"],"chapter":380,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2930","clinicalPearls":["A fibroinflammatory disorder whose hallmark is a tendency to form tumefactive (mass-like) lesions that frequently mimic malignancy; initial misdiagnosis as cancer is extremely common.","Protean and multisystem: can affect virtually any organ, with predilection for pancreas, biliary tree, major salivary glands (submandibular, parotid), periorbital/lacrimal tissues, kidneys, lungs, lymph nodes, and retroperitoneum; meninges, aorta, prostate, thyroid, pericardium, and skin are also described.","Regarded as a variable-vessel vasculitis because it can target arteries and veins of any size, with venous involvement (obliterative phlebitis) being a particular hallmark.","Typical patient is a middle-aged to elderly man (male-to-female ratio about 2:1), contrasting with classic autoimmune diseases that favor young women; head-and-neck (orbit, lacrimal, salivary) disease has a sex ratio closer to 1:1.","Usually presents subacutely; most patients lack fever or marked C-reactive protein elevation even with multiorgan disease. Substantial weight loss can occur, often from exocrine pancreatic failure.","Mikulicz's disease (lacrimal, parotid, and submandibular gland enlargement), once thought to be a Sjogren's subset, is one of the most common presentations of IgG4-RD.","Allergic features are common: atopy, eczema, asthma, nasal polyps, sinusitis, and modest peripheral eosinophilia.","Type 1 autoimmune pancreatitis classically shows a diffusely enlarged 'sausage-shaped' pancreas with loss of normal lobularity; 20-50% of patients present with acute glucose intolerance and a pancreatic mass mimicking adenocarcinoma.","Diagnosis requires correlation of clinical, serologic, radiologic, and pathologic findings; pathology alone is never sufficient, and biopsy is often done mainly to exclude malignancy.","Renal involvement (tubulointerstitial nephritis) is strongly associated with hypocomplementemia; membranous glomerulonephropathy is a less common renal manifestation that must be distinguished from idiopathic disease.","Clinical course is variable: disease may relapse and remit within a given organ, accumulate new organ involvement over time, or be found incidentally on imaging.","Disease can evolve over months, years, or even decades before an organ becomes symptomatic enough to prompt medical attention."],"pathophysiology":"IgG4-RD is characterized by a dense lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate rich in IgG4-positive plasma cells, a distinctive storiform (woven-mat / basket-weave) pattern of fibrosis, obliterative phlebitis reflecting vascular tropism, and a mild-to-moderate tissue eosinophilia. The infiltrate combines B cells (organized in germinal centers, with IgG4+ CD19/CD138 plasma cells radiating outward) and a more diffusely distributed, predominant population of CD4+ T cells. Despite the disease's name, the IgG4 molecule itself is not thought to directly drive injury in most organs: IgG4 undergoes Fab-arm exchange, binds antigen weakly, and has low affinity for Fc receptors and C1q, making it a generally noninflammatory immunoglobulin that may even play a counterregulatory role. A unique oligoclonally expanded CD4+ cytotoxic T cell, abundant at tissue sites, secretes interferon-gamma, TGF-beta, and interleukin-1 (driving storiform fibrosis) as well as perforin, granzymes A/B, and granulysin (mediating cytotoxicity), and appears to be a major disease driver. Oligoclonal plasmablast expansions in blood, with continuous B-cell/plasmablast antigen presentation, are thought to support these profibrotic, tissue-injuring CD4+ cytotoxic T cells.","additionalDifferentials":["Pancreatic adenocarcinoma (mimicked by type 1 autoimmune pancreatitis)","Primary sclerosing cholangitis and cholangiocarcinoma (mimicked by IgG4-related sclerosing cholangitis)","Sjogren's syndrome (Mikulicz's disease presentation)","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (when destructive sinus/middle-ear/nasopharyngeal lesions occur)","Lymphoma and other causes of lymphadenopathy or pseudotumor","Riedel's thyroiditis and fibrosing variant of Hashimoto's thyroiditis","Idiopathic retroperitoneal fibrosis","Idiopathic membranous glomerulonephropathy","Orbital pseudotumor / orbital myositis from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Malignancy (carcinoma, lymphoma) presenting as a tumefactive mass","Sarcoidosis","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Sjogren's syndrome","Idiopathic retroperitoneal fibrosis and inflammatory abdominal aortic aneurysm"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum IgG4 concentration (commonly elevated, but normal in ~30% of histologically proven cases; usually measured by nephelometry).","Awareness of the prozone effect: very high serum IgG4 can yield spuriously low/normal nephelometric values, correctable by serum dilution; failure to detect dramatic elevations misses the subset at greatest risk for multiorgan disease and end-organ injury.","Cross-sectional imaging (CT/MRI) to map multiorgan involvement and detect subclinical or incidental disease.","MRI of brain/orbits for pachymeningeal thickening and enhancement or enlarged extraocular muscles in CNS/orbital disease.","Tissue biopsy with immunohistochemical staining for IgG4 and IgG, quantifying IgG4+ plasma cells per high-power field and the IgG4:total IgG ratio.","Complement levels (hypocomplementemia associated with renal/tubulointerstitial involvement).","Serial serum IgG4 monitoring after treatment to detect rising levels signaling possible flare."],"redFlags":["New tumefactive mass mimicking cancer in pancreas, salivary gland, orbit, lung, or other organ.","Obstructive jaundice from pancreatic or biliary involvement (undiagnosed/undertreated sclerosing cholangitis can lead to hepatic failure within months).","Ureteral entrapment, hydronephrosis, postobstructive uropathy, and renal atrophy from retroperitoneal fibrosis.","Aortic involvement causing aneurysm or dissection.","Anterior pituitary hormone deficiency and/or central diabetes insipidus from hypothalamic-pituitary involvement.","Spinal cord compression, cranial nerve palsies, or radiculopathy from hypertrophic pachymeningitis.","Destructive bone lesions of sinuses, head, and middle ear mimicking granulomatosis with polyangiitis.","Persistently rising serum IgG4 concentrations signaling risk of clinical flare and serious organ involvement.","Coronary arteritis (wall thickening, periarterial encasement, stenosis, calcification, aneurysm/ectasia) in longstanding disease with high IgG4 and biomarker levels."],"prognosis":"Course is generally subacute but can cause major morbidity and organ failure. Aggressive disease may rapidly produce end-stage liver disease, permanent pancreatic exocrine and endocrine (diabetes) impairment, renal atrophy, aortic dissection or aneurysm, and destructive sinonasal lesions. Some manifestations (e.g., IgG4-related lymphadenopathy) can remain indolent and asymptomatic for years. Even with apparently effective immunosuppression, renal atrophy is a frequent sequel to tubulointerstitial nephritis, and end-organ fibrotic damage may be irreversible if diagnosis is delayed. Relapses are common, including in patients with persistently normal IgG4 levels.","pitfalls":["Treating the disease as purely IgG4-antibody-mediated; the IgG4 molecule is largely noninflammatory and may be counterregulatory rather than a primary driver.","Excluding the diagnosis because serum IgG4 is normal: ~30% of histologically proven cases have normal levels, and retroperitoneal fibrosis (often advanced/fibrotic at presentation) frequently shows normal IgG4.","Missing markedly elevated IgG4 because of the prozone effect on nephelometry; suspect this when results are normal despite strong clinical features and re-test with dilution.","Relying on pathology alone for diagnosis; histopathology, while characteristic, is essentially never diagnostic by itself and must be interpreted with clinical, serologic, and radiologic data.","Mistaking tumefactive lesions for malignancy and proceeding to major resections (e.g., Whipple procedure, thyroidectomy) before the correct diagnosis is recognized.","Histologic features such as intense neutrophilic infiltration, leukocytoclasis, granulomatous inflammation, multinucleated giant cells, or fibrinoid necrosis are uncommon in IgG4-RD and argue against the diagnosis.","Assuming serum IgG4 reliably tracks disease activity; correlations are imperfect, levels often do not fully normalize, and remissions can occur with persistently elevated levels."],"severityClassification":"No formal numeric severity score is given. Practical stratification distinguishes vital/organ-threatening involvement (e.g., pancreas, biliary tree/liver, kidneys, aorta, sinonasal destruction, pituitary, meninges) requiring prompt treatment from indolent manifestations (e.g., asymptomatic lymphadenopathy) amenable to watchful waiting with close monitoring; classification of disease itself relies on correlation of clinical, serologic, radiologic, and pathologic findings (ACR/EULAR classification criteria are referenced).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2930","Harrison 22e p.2931","Harrison 22e p.2932","Harrison 22e p.2933"],"crossLinks":[]},"impetigo":{"name":"Impetigo","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["impetigo contagiosa","school sores","bullous impetigo","non-bullous impetigo"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Non-bullous impetigo, the most common form, begins as fragile vesicles or pustules that rupture to leave the characteristic golden-yellow (honey-coloured) crusts, often around the nose and mouth","Bullous impetigo produces larger flaccid blisters that leave a collarette of scale, and is driven by exfoliative toxins from Staphylococcus aureus","It is highly contagious and spreads readily among children through close contact and shared items","It frequently arises as a secondary infection over broken skin such as eczema, insect bites, or scabies, so look for and treat the underlying condition","Management centres on hygiene measures and antibacterial therapy: topical treatment for localised disease and systemic antibiotics for widespread, bullous, or systemically unwell cases, guided by local resistance patterns","Advise on reducing transmission, including handwashing, not sharing towels, and keeping children away from school or nursery until lesions have crusted or treatment has been under way for the recommended period","Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is a recognised complication following certain streptococcal strains, so watch for signs such as dark urine or oedema","Recurrent impetigo warrants consideration of staphylococcal carriage and decolonisation"],"pathophysiology":"Impetigo is a superficial bacterial infection of the epidermis caused predominantly by Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes, alone or together. Organisms colonise and then invade skin whose barrier has been breached by minor trauma, bites, or pre-existing dermatoses. In bullous disease, staphylococcal exfoliative toxins cleave desmoglein-1 in the superficial epidermis, causing intraepidermal separation and blister formation. The host inflammatory response and serous exudate dry to form the typical crusts. Certain nephritogenic streptococcal strains can trigger immune-complex-mediated post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis.","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex infection (grouped vesicles, may recur at the same site)","Eczema herpeticum in a child with atopic dermatitis","Acute allergic or irritant contact dermatitis with exudate and crusting","Tinea with secondary infection","Bullous conditions such as bullous pemphigoid or linear IgA disease when blistering predominates","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome in a systemically unwell young child"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Often a clinical diagnosis","Bacterial swab of exudate for culture and sensitivities in extensive, recurrent, or treatment-failing cases","Nasal and skin swabs to identify staphylococcal carriage in recurrent disease","Urinalysis and blood pressure monitoring if post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is suspected","Assessment for and treatment of underlying dermatoses such as eczema or scabies"],"redFlags":["Rapidly spreading erythema, pain out of proportion, or systemic toxicity suggesting cellulitis or a deeper, necrotising infection","Widespread tender blistering and skin peeling in a young child indicating staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","Features of eczema herpeticum such as punched-out erosions and systemic upset in an atopic patient","Dark urine, facial oedema, or hypertension after infection suggesting post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis"],"prognosis":"Impetigo generally responds well to treatment and often heals without scarring, though it can recur, particularly with staphylococcal carriage or an untreated underlying skin condition; serious sequelae are uncommon but include post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis.","pitfalls":["Missing an underlying dermatosis such as scabies or eczema that will drive recurrence if untreated","Failing to recognise eczema herpeticum and treating presumed impetigo with antibiotics alone","Overusing topical antibiotics and promoting resistance","Neglecting infection-control advice, allowing continued spread among household and school contacts"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"incisional_hernia":{"name":"Incisional hernia","system":"GI / Abdominal wall / Surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ventral incisional hernia","post-operative hernia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Occurs through the scar of a previous abdominal wound where the fascial closure has failed, presenting as a bulge that enlarges with straining","Risk factors include wound infection, obesity, poor surgical technique, emergency surgery, chronic cough, steroids, diabetes, malnutrition, and smoking","The defect and sac often enlarge over time, and large long-standing hernias may develop multiple loculations and adhesions","Small defects with a narrow neck carry a higher risk of incarceration and strangulation than broad-necked defects","Very large hernias may result in loss of abdominal domain, where returning contents to the abdomen risks respiratory compromise and abdominal compartment syndrome","Repair is usually with mesh, either open (onlay, sublay/retrorectus, or intraperitoneal) or laparoscopic, as suture-only repair of larger defects has high recurrence","Optimising modifiable risk factors such as weight, glycaemic control, and smoking before elective repair improves outcomes","A previous wound dehiscence or a burst abdomen strongly predisposes to later incisional herniation"],"pathophysiology":"An incisional hernia develops when the musculo-aponeurotic layers of a healed surgical wound fail to maintain their integrity, allowing peritoneum and abdominal contents to protrude beneath the intact skin. Impaired wound healing, whether from infection, ischaemia, raised intra-abdominal pressure, technical factors, or patient factors such as obesity, diabetes, and malnutrition, weakens the fascial repair. Over time the defect enlarges and the sac expands, sometimes housing much of the abdominal contents. Narrow-necked defects can trap and strangulate bowel, while very large defects lead to loss of domain and altered abdominal wall mechanics.","additionalDifferentials":["Divarication of the recti","Recurrent primary ventral hernia at another site","Seroma or haematoma at the operative site","Abdominal wall abscess","Desmoid or other abdominal wall tumour","Intra-abdominal mass bulging through a weak scar"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination of the scar, defect size, and reducibility","CT abdomen and pelvis to define defect size, contents, and loss of domain and to plan repair","Ultrasound for smaller or uncertain defects","Assessment and optimisation of comorbidities before elective repair"],"redFlags":["Irreducible, tender bulge with loss of cough impulse indicating incarceration or strangulation (surgical emergency)","Bowel obstruction associated with the hernia","Skin breakdown or ischaemia over a large or rapidly enlarging hernia","Systemic sepsis or peritonism from strangulated bowel"],"prognosis":"Recurrence rates are higher than for primary hernias but are substantially reduced by mesh repair and by optimising risk factors. Large hernias with loss of domain require specialised techniques and carry greater perioperative risk, while emergency presentation with strangulation increases morbidity.","pitfalls":["Performing suture-only repair of a sizeable defect and accepting a high recurrence risk","Failing to optimise obesity, diabetes, and smoking before elective repair","Underestimating loss of domain and precipitating abdominal compartment syndrome at repair","Overlooking a small tender defect that is at high risk of strangulation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"infant_gord":{"name":"Infant Gastro-oesophageal Reflux Disease","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["infant GORD","infant GERD","gastro-oesophageal reflux disease in infancy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["It is essential to distinguish physiological gastro-oesophageal reflux, which is common and benign in healthy infants, from reflux disease, in which reflux causes troublesome symptoms or complications.","Effortless regurgitation or posseting in a thriving, comfortable infant (the happy spitter) is normal and typically improves as the infant matures and begins to sit and take solids.","Reflux disease is suggested by poor weight gain, feeding refusal or distress, persistent irritability, back-arching with feeds, or respiratory symptoms attributable to reflux.","Diagnosis is largely clinical from the history and examination; investigations are reserved for atypical presentations, complications, or diagnostic uncertainty.","First-line management uses conservative measures such as optimising feeding volume and technique, thickened feeds where appropriate, and positioning advice consistent with safe sleep guidance.","A trial of cow's milk protein elimination is reasonable when allergy is suspected, since it can mimic or coexist with reflux disease.","Acid-suppressing medication is not appropriate for uncomplicated physiological reflux and should be limited to infants with clear evidence of reflux disease.","Warning features such as bilious or forceful vomiting, gastrointestinal bleeding, or onset of vomiting after six months point away from simple reflux and toward an alternative diagnosis."],"pathophysiology":"Gastro-oesophageal reflux occurs when gastric contents pass back into the oesophagus, largely through transient relaxations of the lower oesophageal sphincter. Reflux is especially common in infancy because of a short oesophagus, a predominantly liquid and high-volume diet, frequent recumbency, and immaturity of the antireflux barrier. In most infants this is physiological and self-resolving. Reflux disease develops when refluxate causes oesophageal mucosal injury and pain, impairs feeding and growth, or reaches the airway to provoke respiratory symptoms. Contributing factors include delayed gastric emptying and, in some infants, cow's milk protein allergy that produces overlapping symptoms.","additionalDifferentials":["Physiological gastro-oesophageal reflux (normal regurgitation)","Cow's milk protein allergy","Pyloric stenosis (with projectile non-bilious vomiting)","Overfeeding","Infection (including urinary tract infection)","Intestinal obstruction or malrotation (particularly with bilious vomiting)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment with growth monitoring (usually sufficient)","Trial of cow's milk protein elimination when allergy is suspected","Upper gastrointestinal contrast study to assess anatomy if obstruction or malrotation is a concern","Oesophageal pH or impedance monitoring in selected complex cases","Endoscopy when oesophagitis or another mucosal complication is suspected"],"redFlags":["Bilious vomiting or haematemesis","Forceful or projectile vomiting suggesting pyloric stenosis","Faltering growth or feeding refusal","Onset of vomiting after six months or persistence beyond infancy","Systemic signs such as fever, lethargy, or a bulging fontanelle"],"prognosis":"Physiological reflux resolves in the great majority of infants during the first year to eighteen months as the infant matures; reflux disease also generally improves with time and appropriate management, though some infants with underlying conditions require more prolonged treatment.","pitfalls":["Treating benign physiological reflux with acid suppression or unnecessary investigation","Missing an alternative diagnosis such as pyloric stenosis or malrotation presenting with vomiting","Overlooking cow's milk protein allergy as a mimicking or coexisting cause","Recommending positioning that conflicts with safe sleep advice"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"infantile_colic":{"name":"Infantile Colic","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["colic","excessive infant crying","baby colic"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Infantile colic describes recurrent, prolonged episodes of inconsolable crying and fussiness in an otherwise healthy, well-fed, and thriving young infant.","It is often characterised by the rule of threes: crying for more than about three hours a day, on more than three days a week, for more than three weeks, though clinical judgement matters more than strict criteria.","Episodes commonly cluster in the late afternoon and evening and may be accompanied by drawing up of the legs, a flushed face, and clenched fists.","It is a diagnosis of exclusion, established when a careful history and thorough examination reveal no underlying organic cause and growth is normal.","Typical onset is in the first few weeks of life, with peak intensity around six weeks and spontaneous resolution by around three to four months of age.","The cause is not fully understood and is likely multifactorial, involving normal developmental crying, gut and feeding factors, and infant-caregiver interaction.","Management centres on parental reassurance, education about the benign and self-limiting nature, soothing techniques, and support for exhausted caregivers.","Because the crying is intense and distressing, clinicians should assess caregiver stress and coping and be alert to the risk of frustration-related harm such as abusive head trauma."],"pathophysiology":"Infantile colic does not have a single established mechanism and is best understood as excessive crying at the upper end of the normal developmental crying curve, which peaks in early infancy. Proposed contributors include transient gastrointestinal factors such as immature motility, gas, or feeding-related discomfort, alterations in the gut microbiome, cow's milk protein sensitivity in a subset of infants, heightened infant sensitivity or difficult temperament, and stress within the infant-caregiver relationship. In most infants no pathological process is identified, and the self-limited course paralleling normal maturation supports a developmental rather than a disease-based explanation.","additionalDifferentials":["Cow's milk protein allergy","Gastro-oesophageal reflux disease","Infection (including urinary tract infection or otitis media)","Feeding difficulties or underfeeding","Incarcerated hernia, intussusception, or other acute abdominal cause","Corneal abrasion, hair tourniquet, or other occult source of pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thorough history and full physical examination (colic is clinical and one of exclusion)","Growth assessment to confirm normal weight gain","Targeted tests only if history or examination suggests an organic cause","Trial of maternal or infant dietary modification when cow's milk protein allergy is suspected","Urinalysis if infection is a concern"],"redFlags":["Poor weight gain or failure to thrive","Fever or signs of systemic illness","Vomiting (especially bilious), blood in stool, or abdominal distension","Lethargy, abnormal tone, or an unwell appearance","Signs of possible non-accidental injury or a caregiver at breaking point"],"prognosis":"Infantile colic is benign and self-limiting, resolving spontaneously by around three to four months of age in most infants, with no adverse effect on long-term growth or development.","pitfalls":["Labelling crying as colic without excluding organic causes or confirming normal growth","Missing a serious acute condition such as intussusception or infection presenting as excessive crying","Overmedicalising a benign condition or making unnecessary formula changes","Neglecting caregiver distress and the associated risk of abusive head trauma"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"infantile_spasms":{"name":"Infantile Spasms","system":"Neurological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["West syndrome","epileptic spasms","salaam attacks"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An age-specific epileptic encephalopathy of infancy, with onset usually in the first year of life and peaking around 4-8 months.","Spasms are brief, often symmetrical contractions producing sudden flexion or extension of the trunk and limbs, typically occurring in clusters especially on waking.","The full West syndrome triad comprises epileptic spasms, the EEG pattern of hypsarrhythmia, and arrest or regression of development.","Developmental regression or loss of previously acquired skills, including loss of social smile or visual attention, is a crucial and sometimes early sign.","Spasms are frequently mistaken for colic, startle, or normal infant movements, causing dangerous delays in diagnosis.","An urgent EEG is essential; hypsarrhythmia is a chaotic, high-amplitude, disorganised interictal pattern that supports the diagnosis.","Many cases have an identifiable underlying cause such as a structural brain abnormality, tuberous sclerosis, genetic or metabolic disease, or perinatal injury, so investigation for aetiology is important.","Rapid initiation of effective treatment is critical, as prompt control of spasms and resolution of hypsarrhythmia is associated with better developmental outcomes."],"pathophysiology":"Infantile spasms represent an age-dependent epileptic encephalopathy in which diverse insults to the developing brain converge on a common electroclinical expression. Structural, genetic, metabolic, or acquired abnormalities disrupt the maturing cortical and subcortical networks during a vulnerable developmental window, producing the hypersynchronous discharges seen as hypsarrhythmia and the characteristic spasms. The ongoing epileptic activity itself further disrupts normal brain development, contributing to the associated cognitive and developmental regression.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign non-epileptic myoclonus of infancy","Sandifer syndrome (dystonic posturing with reflux)","Exaggerated startle response or hyperekplexia","Infantile colic","Other early-onset epileptic encephalopathies (e.g. Ohtahara syndrome)","Tonic seizures of other origin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent EEG, ideally capturing sleep, to identify hypsarrhythmia","MRI brain to detect structural or malformative causes","Skin examination and evaluation for tuberous sclerosis","Metabolic and genetic testing to establish an underlying aetiology","Developmental assessment to document baseline and progress","Fundoscopy and ophthalmological review where indicated"],"redFlags":["New clusters of spasms in an infant require urgent EEG and prompt treatment (EMERGENCY)","Developmental regression or loss of acquired milestones","Hypsarrhythmia on EEG","Delay between symptom onset and treatment, which worsens prognosis","Signs suggesting an underlying structural, metabolic, or genetic disorder","Evolution to other seizure types or epileptic encephalopathy"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is variable and strongly influenced by the underlying cause and by how quickly spasms and hypsarrhythmia are controlled; delayed treatment and identifiable structural or genetic causes are associated with worse developmental outcomes, while prompt effective therapy in previously normal infants offers the best chance of favourable development.","pitfalls":["Dismissing spasms as colic, reflux, or benign startle and delaying EEG","Failing to appreciate developmental regression as a warning sign","Not pursuing a thorough search for an underlying cause","Delaying initiation of effective therapy, which reduces the chance of good outcome","Under-recognising subtle spasms occurring in clusters around sleep transitions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"infections_acquired_in_health_care":{"name":"Infections Acquired in Health Care Facilities","system":"Infectious Disease / Hospital Epidemiology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Nosocomial infections","Health care-associated infections","HAI","Hospital-acquired infections"],"chapter":147,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1146","clinicalPearls":["Infections appearing within the first 48-72 hours of admission are generally regarded as community-acquired (or attributed to a transferring institution); those arising afterward are considered health care-associated (nosocomial).","Patient-related vulnerabilities to nosocomial infection include indwelling invasive devices, immune deficiency (congenital or acquired), renal insufficiency, diabetes, and other major comorbidities.","Disruption of a patient's own microbiota by antibiotics or other drugs promotes colonization with multidrug-resistant organisms; colonized patients then act as silent reservoirs for transmission to others and to the environment.","Colonization pressure (the proportion of patients on a ward who are colonized) is itself a risk factor for the spread of resistant organisms.","Catheter-associated bloodstream infections roughly triple the risk of in-hospital death; central lines are foreign objects in direct contact with the bloodstream and each handling is a potential contamination event.","Ventilator-associated pneumonia occurs in up to 10% of mechanically ventilated patients and can prolong ventilation and ICU stay or be fatal.","Clostridioides difficile infection is both the most frequent cause of health care-associated diarrhea and the most frequent hospital-acquired infection in the United States; recent antibiotic exposure is the most common precipitant.","Alcohol-based hand gel and standard hospital cleaners do NOT kill C. difficile spores or inactivate norovirus, so handwashing with soap and water plus sporicidal (e.g., bleach) cleaning is required.","Candida species account for the vast majority of health care-associated fungal infections; antifungal prophylaxis can select for non-albicans species such as Candida glabrata.","Candida auris is a multidrug-resistant, environment-adapted pathogen that survives long periods on surfaces and is resistant to some standard disinfectants, requiring select agents and strict contact precautions.","Tap water is not sterile; waterborne organisms such as Legionella pneumophila and nontuberculous mycobacteria colonize plumbing biofilm, favored by low disinfectant levels, warm temperatures, and stagnation.","Apart from pregnant patients, asymptomatic bacteriuria in catheterized patients should generally not be cultured or treated; in the elderly nonspecific symptoms like confusion are often unrelated to bacteriuria, and overtreatment drives a cycle of escalating antimicrobial use and resistance.","Surveillance infection rates are expressed as standardized infection ratios (observed infections divided by predicted infections), benchmarked through the CDC National Healthcare Safety Network."],"pathophysiology":"Hospitalized patients are exposed to pathogens from other patients, visitors, staff, and the inanimate environment. Health care personnel carry multidrug-resistant organisms between patients on hands or shared equipment (stethoscopes, portable imaging machines); spread also occurs via contaminated plumbing, person-to-person respiratory routes, or contamination of food, water, and medications. Antibiotic exposure disrupts the host microbiota, permitting colonization with resistant organisms; colonized but immunocompromised patients, or those whose barriers are breached by surgery or invasive devices, then develop infection either from their own flora or from cross-transmitted pathogens. Invasive devices (urinary catheters, central venous catheters, endotracheal tubes) create direct portals of entry, bypassing normal defenses. Resistant organisms frequently colonize the gastrointestinal and respiratory tracts and can establish enduring environmental reservoirs, with potential horizontal transfer of resistance genes between co-colonizing strains.","additionalDifferentials":["Community-acquired infection (onset within 48-72 h of admission)","Asymptomatic bacteriuria versus true catheter-associated urinary tract infection","Noninfectious causes of fever or organ dysfunction in the critically ill","Drug-related or transfusion-related complications mimicking infection"],"infectionMimics":["Asymptomatic bacteriuria mistaken for symptomatic urinary tract infection (especially in the elderly with nonspecific confusion)","Colonization with multidrug-resistant organisms misread as active infection","Ventilator-associated events that are noninfectious complications of mechanical ventilation"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Aspiration pneumonitis without bacterial infection","Postoperative inflammatory fever","Thrombotic or chemical phlebitis at vascular access sites"],"additionalInvestigations":["Laboratory-based surveillance supplemented by clinical chart review by infection preventionists","Whole genome sequencing as the gold standard for establishing clonality and detecting plasmid-borne resistance genes during outbreak investigation","Molecular typing of bacterial/fungal (less commonly viral) isolates to confirm an outbreak","PCR or culture screening for Candida auris in patients transferred from long-term care facilities (isolate pending results)","Interferon-gamma release assay (preferred over tuberculin skin test) for preemployment tuberculosis screening of health care personnel","Targeted water testing for Legionella pneumophila in facilities with prior nosocomial cases","Dedicated public health laboratory testing (nasopharyngeal swabs/washes, conjunctival swabs) for strain identification when novel influenza such as H5N1 is suspected, as commercial multiplex PCR panels are insufficient"],"redFlags":["New bloodstream infection in a patient with a central venous catheter (markedly elevated in-hospital mortality)","Nosocomial diarrhea progressing toward toxic megacolon in C. difficile colitis","A single case of nosocomial Legionnaires disease, which should trigger a full investigation","Suspected active tuberculosis requiring immediate airborne precautions","Identification of Candida auris, carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales, or other high-priority multidrug-resistant organisms requiring coordinated containment","Presentation of a returning traveler with possible viral hemorrhagic fever (Ebola, Marburg)"],"prognosis":"Health care-associated infections are a substantial source of morbidity and mortality in hospitalized patients, frequently caused by antimicrobial-resistant organisms that prolong hospital stays and worsen outcomes. Catheter-associated bloodstream infections triple in-hospital mortality risk, and ventilator-associated pneumonia can be fatal or prolong ICU stay. Multidrug-resistant organism infections are associated with higher morbidity, higher mortality, and added costs; globally about 1.27 million deaths were attributed to antibiotic-resistant infections in 2019. After years of decline from prevention efforts, device-related and other nosocomial infection rates rose during the COVID-19 pandemic.","pitfalls":["Culturing and treating asymptomatic bacteriuria in catheterized patients, then re-testing for 'clearance', fueling a cycle of escalating antimicrobial use and resistance.","Relying on alcohol-based hand gel against C. difficile spores or norovirus, which it does not eliminate.","Failing to remove indwelling urinary catheters and central lines promptly when no longer needed, the principal driver of these preventable device infections.","Overlooking that patients already colonized with C. difficile on admission are by far the most likely to develop infection during the stay.","Missing exogenous transmission of Candida auris because it resists some standard disinfectant cleaners and recontaminates surfaces rapidly.","Treating commercial multiplex respiratory PCR results as adequate to exclude or identify novel influenza strains such as H5N1."],"severityClassification":"C. difficile colitis spans a clinical spectrum from mild diarrhea to life-threatening toxic megacolon. Pathogen transmission risk is operationally classified by route into contact, droplet, and airborne precautions (per WHO and CDC); some conditions (e.g., concurrent varicella and SARS-CoV-2) warrant combined precautions. Airborne isolation requires a negative-pressure room with at least 12 air changes per hour, exhausted outdoors or through a HEPA filter, with respirator use; protective (positive-pressure, HEPA-filtered) isolation is used for highly susceptible patients such as those with prolonged neutropenia or stem cell transplants to exclude mold spores.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1146","Harrison 22e p.1147","Harrison 22e p.1148","Harrison 22e p.1149","Harrison 22e p.1150","Harrison 22e p.1151"],"crossLinks":[]},"infections_due_to_campylobacter_and":{"name":"Infections Due to Campylobacter and Related Organisms","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Campylobacteriosis","Campylobacter jejuni enteritis","Campylobacter enteritis","Campylobacter fetus infection"],"chapter":169,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1303","clinicalPearls":["Campylobacters are motile, non-spore-forming, curved (vibrio-like) gram-negative rods that are microaerophilic, and the genus was split off from Vibrio in 1973; over 20 species now span the genera Campylobacter, Arcobacter, and Helicobacter (Harrison 22e p.1304).","C. jejuni is the dominant diarrheal pathogen, accounting for roughly 80-90% of recognized illness in this group, while C. fetus is the principal cause of extraintestinal/systemic disease (Harrison 22e p.1304).","Undercooked poultry is the leading source in developed countries (30-70% of cases); other routes include unpasteurized milk, untreated water, infected pets, contaminated seafood, foreign travel, and oral-anal sexual contact (Harrison 22e p.1304).","Enteritis typically begins 2-4 days after exposure (range 1-7 days) and is often preceded 12-48 h by a prodrome of fever, headache, myalgia, and malaise (Harrison 22e p.1304-1305).","Visibly bloody stool occurs in about 10% of adult cases; most patients presenting for care have 10 or more bowel movements on the worst day, and cramping abdominal pain may be the most prominent feature (Harrison 22e p.1305).","C. jejuni can produce pseudoappendicitis, and isolated fever can be the only early sign, mimicking the onset of typhoid fever; myocarditis and pericarditis have been seen, especially in young men, and febrile young children may have convulsions (Harrison 22e p.1305).","C. fetus has a tropism for vascular sites, causing endocarditis, mycotic aneurysm, and septic thrombophlebitis; its protective S-layer resists complement-mediated killing and opsonization, enabling bacteremia and antigenic variation (Harrison 22e p.1305).","About 90% of bacteremic cases are due to C. jejuni and C. fetus in roughly equal numbers, despite C. jejuni being far more common overall (Harrison 22e p.1305).","The two major postinfectious sequelae are reactive arthritis (up to 2.5% of cases, no HLA-B27 association, averaging about 3.2 joints, knees most often) and Guillain-Barre syndrome (Harrison 22e p.1305).","Sialylated lipopolysaccharide on certain C. jejuni strains drives molecular mimicry against axonal cell-surface molecules; Campylobacter is estimated to trigger 20-40% of all Guillain-Barre cases despite occurring in only ~1 per 1000-2000 infections (Harrison 22e p.1305).","Diagnosis rests on culture on Campylobacter-selective fastidious media (the gold standard), with species-specific real-time PCR being more sensitive; MALDI-TOF identifies species, and Gram/Wright fecal smears or dark-field microscopy may reveal vibrioid forms with characteristic darting motility (Harrison 22e p.1305-1306).","Infection with C. jejuni in low- and middle-income countries, even when asymptomatic, is associated with childhood stunting (short stature) and a distinctive microbiome signature (Harrison 22e p.1304)."],"pathophysiology":"Campylobacters survive in the gastrointestinal mucous layer of food animals and pets and reach humans mainly via undercooked/contaminated food or animal contact. In C. jejuni enteritis the jejunum, ileum, and colon are injured; biopsy shows a nonspecific acute inflammatory reaction with neutrophils, monocytes, and eosinophils in the lamina propria plus epithelial damage, mucus loss, glandular degeneration, and crypt abscesses. Strain motility and adherence favor disease, whereas classic entero- and cytotoxins (including cytolethal distending toxin) seem not to drive tissue injury; tissue invasion and occasional bacteremia indicate clinically significant invasion. C. fetus pathogenesis is better defined: nearly all clinical isolates bear a proteinaceous S-layer capsule-like structure conferring resistance to complement and opsonization, allowing bacteremia and seeding of extraintestinal sites, while antigenic switching of S-layer proteins contributes to chronicity and recurrence in compromised hosts. Protective immunity is incompletely understood but appears antibody-dependent and may be short-lived without continuous exposure (Harrison 22e p.1304-1305).","additionalDifferentials":["Salmonella gastroenteritis","Shigellosis","Yersinia enterocolitica enteritis","Enterohemorrhagic Escherichia coli infection","Typhoid fever (when fever is the sole early manifestation)","Acute appendicitis (versus C. jejuni pseudoappendicitis)","Ulcerative colitis","Crohn's disease"],"infectionMimics":["Salmonella enteritis","Shigella dysentery","Yersinia enterocolitica infection","Enterohemorrhagic E. coli colitis","Early typhoid fever"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Ulcerative colitis","Crohn's disease","Acute appendicitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool culture on Campylobacter-specific selective media (diagnostic gold standard)","Species-specific real-time PCR (more sensitive than culture)","Culture-independent diagnostic tests / antigen detection (may detect nonviable organisms and give false positives)","MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry for species identification","Gram or Wright stain of fecal smear for vibrioid morphology; phase-contrast or dark-field microscopy for darting motility","Fecal leukocyte/erythrocyte examination","Peripheral leukocyte count (reflects inflammatory severity)","Blood cultures (note: low metabolic activity can make Campylobacter bacteremia hard to detect)"],"redFlags":["High fever","Grossly bloody or severe diarrhea","Symptoms persisting beyond one week or worsening","Signs of sepsis, meningitis, or endovascular infection (suggesting C. fetus)","Septic abortion or infection during pregnancy (often leads to fetal death)","Septic thrombophlebitis (suggests C. fetus)","Immunocompromise, diabetes, liver disease, or extremes of age (risk of severe/recurrent extraintestinal disease)","Neurologic symptoms suggesting Guillain-Barre syndrome or its Miller Fisher variant following infection"],"prognosis":"Almost all patients recover fully from Campylobacter enteritis, spontaneously or with therapy; the rare deaths are linked largely to volume depletion. Symptoms persist beyond one week in 10-20% of patients seeking care, and clinical relapses occur in 5-10% of untreated patients. Some patients develop reactive arthritis or Guillain-Barre syndrome. Systemic C. fetus infection is far more often fatal than infection with related species, with overall reported bacteremia mortality around 12%; outcome depends on how promptly appropriate therapy begins. Otherwise healthy hosts usually survive C. fetus infection without sequelae, whereas compromised hosts often suffer recurrent and/or life-threatening disease (Harrison 22e p.1306).","pitfalls":["Labeling inflammatory bowel disease (ulcerative colitis or Crohn's) before excluding Campylobacter and related infections, since presentations and biopsy findings can overlap and Campylobacter enteritis is far more common in young adults (Harrison 22e p.1306).","Assuming a negative stool culture excludes Campylobacter, as these fastidious organisms are missed without selective media or techniques (Harrison 22e p.1306).","Over-interpreting culture-independent test positivity, which may reflect nonviable bacteria and can be falsely positive, unlike culture which usually implies active or recent infection (Harrison 22e p.1306).","Treating commensal oral organisms (C. sputorum, C. concisus) as pathogens, since they rarely have pathogenic significance (Harrison 22e p.1306).","Missing C. fetus bacteremia because of its low metabolic activity in standard blood culture media (Harrison 22e p.1306).","Overlooking that not every patient with enteritis benefits from antimicrobials, and that antimotility agents can prolong symptoms and have been associated with toxic megacolon and death (Harrison 22e p.1306)."],"severityClassification":"Three patterns of extraintestinal infection are described: (1) transient bacteremia in a normal host with enteritis, which is benign and needs no specific treatment; (2) sustained bacteremia or focal infection in a normal host, arising from enteritis and responding well to antimicrobials; and (3) sustained bacteremia or focal infection in a compromised host, in which enteritis may not be apparent and prompt, possibly prolonged, therapy is required (Harrison 22e p.1305).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1303","Harrison 22e p.1304","Harrison 22e p.1305","Harrison 22e p.1306"],"crossLinks":[]},"infections_due_to_mixed_anaerobic":{"name":"Infections Due to Mixed Anaerobic Organisms","system":"Infectious Diseases (multisystem)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Anaerobic bacterial infections","Mixed aerobic-anaerobic infection","Polymicrobial anaerobic infection","Bacteroides fragilis infection","Non-clostridial anaerobic infection"],"chapter":182,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1371-1379","clinicalPearls":["Most clinically relevant anaerobes (Bacteroides fragilis, Prevotella melaninogenica, Fusobacterium nucleatum) are relatively aerotolerant rather than strict obligate anaerobes; obligate anaerobes are killed by oxygen at or above roughly 0.5%, whereas facultative anaerobes grow with or without oxygen (Harrison 22e p.1371).","Anaerobes dominate the normal mucocutaneous microbiota, accounting for about 99.0-99.9% of cultivable organisms, with concentrations ranging from ~10^3/mL in the nose to ~10^12/mL in gingival crevices and colon; the lower GI tract is the major reservoir (anaerobe-to-aerobe ratio ~1000:1) (Harrison 22e p.1371-1372).","Infections are typically polymicrobial, commonly yielding three to six species, and occasionally more than 10 species from a single suppurative site; the etiologic organisms mirror the neighboring resident microbiota (Harrison 22e p.1372-1373).","Bacteroides fragilis, though only ~0.5-1% of the colonic microbiota, is the anaerobe most often isolated from intraabdominal infections and bacteremia; its zwitterionic capsular polysaccharide A (PSA) is the principal virulence factor driving abscess formation via a TLR2-dependent cytokine/chemokine response (IL-8, IL-17, TNF-alpha) (Harrison 22e p.1373).","Anaerobic gram-negative endotoxin (lipid A) is roughly 100-1000 times less biologically potent than aerobic gram-negative endotoxin, which helps explain why DIC, purpura, and septic shock are less frequent in anaerobic gram-negative bacteremia; Fusobacterium LPS is an exception and contributes to the severity of Lemierre's syndrome (Harrison 22e p.1373).","A putrid/foul-smelling discharge is considered suggestive of anaerobic infection but is a late finding present in only about 30-50% of cases, so its absence does not exclude anaerobes (Harrison 22e p.1374).","Sterile pus paradox: an abscess that yields no growth on routine culture but shows teeming organisms on Gram stain strongly suggests anaerobes that failed to grow because of inadequate transport/culture technique (Harrison 22e p.1373-1378).","Anaerobes are recovered in up to ~30% of brain abscesses, ~20% of epidural abscesses, and ~10% of subdural empyemas, but rarely cause meningitis; brain abscesses from contiguous (otogenic, odontogenic, sinus) spread are the most likely to involve anaerobes (Harrison 22e p.1375).","Bacterial aspiration pneumonia evolves indolently over days to weeks, distinguishing it from chemical pneumonitis (e.g., Mendelson syndrome from acid aspiration), which develops within 4-6 hours and can produce a bilateral chest x-ray 'whiteout' within 8-24 hours (Harrison 22e p.1375).","Empyema fluid characteristics (mean WBC ~55,000 cells/mL, LDH >1000 IU/L, pH <7.2, loculations, thick pleural peel) contrast sharply with a simple parapneumonic effusion (mean WBC ~5000 cells/mL, LDH >200 IU/L, pH ~7.4) (Harrison 22e p.1376).","Aspiration pneumonia/lung abscess localizes to dependent segments: basilar lower-lobe segments if aspiration occurred upright, and posterior upper-lobe (usually right) or superior lower-lobe segments if supine (Harrison 22e p.1375-1376).","Clostridium septicum bacteremia, often arising via neutropenic enterocolitis (typhlitis), is strongly associated with underlying malignancy, particularly colon cancer (Harrison 22e p.1376)."],"pathophysiology":"Anaerobic infection requires an anaerobic microenvironment with a lowered oxidation-reduction potential, which arises directly from tissue ischemia, trauma, surgery, or a perforated viscus, or is established and maintained by co-infecting facultative organisms that consume local oxygen. Disease typically follows breakdown of a mucosal barrier (intestinal perforation, ischemia, surgery) or direct inoculation (bite wounds, trauma), seeding normally sterile tissue with commensal organisms. The resulting infections are usually polymicrobial, with facultative and obligate anaerobes acting synergistically: facultative organisms create and sustain the reduced-oxygen environment, short-chain fatty acids and succinic acid impair phagocyte clearance, certain organisms supply growth factors to others (e.g., oral diphtheroids producing vitamin K needed by P. melaninogenica), and tissue damage promotes spread. Virulence factors include adhesins/fimbriae (P. gingivalis, B. fragilis pili, F. nucleatum as a bridging coaggregator), exoproteins such as P. gingivalis collagenase, and capsular polysaccharides. The B. fragilis capsular polysaccharide complex, particularly the zwitterionic PSA, is the most studied factor; its charged repeating-unit motif drives abscess formation by stimulating peritoneal macrophages (via TLR2) to release IL-8, IL-17, and TNF-alpha, recruiting PMNs that adhere to ICAM-1-upregulated mesothelial cells to form the abscess nidus, with T-cell IL-17/IFN-gamma also required (Harrison 22e p.1372-1373).","additionalDifferentials":["Aerobic abscess (e.g., Staphylococcus aureus), which can also produce deep abscesses and lung abscess without an anaerobic component","Chemical pneumonitis / Mendelson syndrome (acid aspiration) versus bacterial aspiration pneumonia","Foreign-body aspiration with obstruction and atelectasis versus bacterial aspiration pneumonia","Simple parapneumonic effusion (e.g., pneumococcal) versus anaerobic empyema","Necrotizing pneumonia, bronchopleural fistula with purulent effusion, and pneumatocele in the lung-abscess differential","Group A streptococcal necrotizing fasciitis versus mixed aerobic-anaerobic (polymicrobial) necrotizing fasciitis","Malignancy or granulomatous disease mimicked by cervicofacial actinomycosis ('lumpy jaw')"],"infectionMimics":["Staphylococcus aureus abscess or lung abscess (no anaerobic component)","Group A streptococcal necrotizing fasciitis (more rapid, 1-3 day progression)","Pneumococcal pneumonia with parapneumonic effusion"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chemical pneumonitis from gastric acid aspiration (Mendelson syndrome)","Foreign-body airway obstruction","Malignancy or granulomatous disease mimicked by chronic actinomycotic 'lumpy jaw'"],"additionalInvestigations":["Gram stain of pus/sputum showing a mixed bacterial population, especially when routine culture is negative or grows only streptococci or a single aerobe such as E. coli","Anaerobic culture from normally sterile sites (blood, pleural/peritoneal fluid, CSF, sterile-site aspirates or biopsies); samples from mouth, nose, vagina, or feces are unacceptable due to commensal contamination","Air-free syringe collection capped and placed in anaerobic transport media with rapid transport; liquid/tissue specimens preferred over swabs","Pleural fluid analysis (WBC count, LDH, pH) to distinguish empyema from a simple parapneumonic effusion","Imaging (chest x-ray/CT) showing dependent-segment consolidation or cavitary abscess; cross-sectional imaging for intraabdominal/pelvic abscess and for typhlitis bowel-wall thickening (>4 mm)","Referral to a reference laboratory for susceptibility testing in serious, refractory, or prolonged-therapy infections (brain abscess, meningitis, joint infection, osteomyelitis, prosthetic joint infection, endocarditis)"],"redFlags":["Ludwig's angina from molar dental infection: submandibular/sublingual/submental swelling with trismus and posterior-superior tongue displacement causing swallowing impairment and airway obstruction (tracheotomy may be lifesaving)","Noma (cancrum oris): gangrenous orofacial destruction in malnourished young children with untreated mortality of 70-90%","Lemierre's syndrome: oropharyngeal infection with septic internal jugular vein thrombophlebitis and septic emboli, usually due to Fusobacterium necrophorum","Necrotizing fasciitis / synergistic necrotizing cellulitis / Fournier gangrene: subcutaneous gas, foul discharge, cutaneous anesthesia preceding skin necrosis, fever and systemic toxicity","Neutropenic enterocolitis (typhlitis) with bowel-wall thickening, fever, and bacteremia; Clostridium septicum bacteremia signaling possible occult colon malignancy","B. fragilis-group bacteremia indicating underlying colonic pathology (neoplasia, diverticulitis) and carrying high untreated mortality"],"prognosis":"Manifestations of severe sepsis and DIC are unusual in purely anaerobic infection, and septic shock is less common with B. fragilis than with aerobic gram-negative bacillary bacteremia. Nonetheless, isolation of a B. fragilis-group organism from blood indicates serious underlying infection with an untreated mortality rate of about 60%; case-fatality rises with advancing age (reported >66% in patients over 60), with isolation of multiple species, and with failure to surgically remove the focus. Reported relative mortality has been suggested to vary by species (B. thetaiotaomicron > P. distasonis > B. fragilis). Anaerobic endocarditis, though rare, carries significant mortality (~21-43%). Noma is highly lethal without treatment (70-90%). Recovery from empyema may take several months, particularly without surgical intervention (Harrison 22e p.1374, 1377-1378).","pitfalls":["Submitting specimens from heavily colonized sites (mouth, nose, vagina, feces) or expectorated sputum for anaerobic culture, which yields uninterpretable results from commensal contamination","Dismissing an infection as 'sterile' when routine culture is negative despite a Gram stain teeming with organisms, missing inadequate anaerobic transport/culture","Prior antibiotic therapy reducing the cultivability of anaerobes and masking the diagnosis","Using agents with no useful anaerobic activity (aminoglycosides, monobactams, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) and attributing nonresponse to other causes; failure to respond to such agents itself suggests an anaerobic etiology","Treating gas in tissue or a putrid odor as pathognomonic for anaerobes; gas can be produced by aerobes and putrid odor appears in only 30-50% of cases","Overlooking that right-lower-quadrant pain and tenderness are absent in nearly half of typhlitis cases (and may be absent entirely in patients on glucocorticoids)","Relying on antibiotics alone without surgical debridement of devitalized tissue and drainage of large abscesses"],"severityClassification":"No single formal grading scheme; severity is judged by anatomic site, extent, and systemic involvement. Localized/indolent processes (chronic sinusitis, periodontal disease, indolent anaerobic cellulitis) contrast with severe, life-threatening forms (Ludwig's angina with airway compromise, noma, Lemierre's syndrome, necrotizing fasciitis/Fournier gangrene, brain abscess, B. fragilis-group bacteremia, endocarditis). Antibiotic selection is guided empirically by infection location, severity, and local resistance patterns; reference-laboratory susceptibility testing is reserved for serious, refractory, or prolonged-therapy infections (Harrison 22e p.1374-1379).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1371","Harrison 22e p.1372","Harrison 22e p.1373","Harrison 22e p.1374","Harrison 22e p.1375","Harrison 22e p.1376","Harrison 22e p.1377","Harrison 22e p.1378","Harrison 22e p.1379"],"crossLinks":[]},"infections_due_to_pseudomonas_burkholderia":{"name":"Infections Due to Pseudomonas, Burkholderia, and Stenotrophomonas Species","system":"Multisystem / Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection","Burkholderia cepacia complex infection","Melioidosis","Glanders","Stenotrophomonas maltophilia infection","Pseudomallei infection","Ecthyma gangrenosum (Pseudomonas)"],"chapter":170,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1307","clinicalPearls":["The pseudomonads are a heterogeneous group of non-lactose-fermenting gram-negative bacteria now split across three medically important genera: Pseudomonas, Burkholderia, and Stenotrophomonas; most cause disease opportunistically, the exceptions being the primary pathogens B. pseudomallei and B. mallei.","P. aeruginosa rarely causes infection without a breach in host defenses; disease typically requires compromised immunity, mucosal trauma, physiologic derangement, or antibiotic-mediated suppression of normal flora, so most infections cluster in intensive care units.","On primary culture P. aeruginosa is identified by a yellow to dark-green or bluish pigment, a shiny gun-metal colony sheen, a characteristic fruity odor, failure to ferment lactose on MacConkey agar, and a positive oxidase reaction; cystic fibrosis isolates may appear mucoid from overproduction of alginate.","Ecthyma gangrenosum is the distinctive skin lesion of P. aeruginosa bacteremia, occurring almost exclusively in markedly neutropenic patients and those with AIDS; lesions begin pink, darken to purple, then become black and necrotic, and histologically are due to vascular invasion teeming with bacteria.","P. aeruginosa keratitis is a medical emergency because it can progress over roughly 48 hours to involve the entire cornea with opacification and even perforation; it is strongly associated with contact lenses, especially the extended-wear type.","Malignant (necrotizing) otitis externa most often occurs in elderly diabetics and can extend to cranial nerve palsies (most commonly cranial nerve VII) or cavernous sinus thrombosis; the ESR is invariably elevated, often 100 mm/h or more.","Hot-tub folliculitis, toe-web infection, and the green-nail syndrome (pyocyanin diffusing into the nail bed of a Pseudomonas paronychia) are characteristic community skin manifestations; whirlpools, spas, and pools are common sources, and folliculitis is usually self-limited.","Vertebral osteomyelitis and sternoclavicular septic arthritis are uncommon but characteristic complications in people who inject drugs; Pseudomonas osteomyelitis of the foot typically follows a puncture wound through a sneaker, mainly in children, often with negative blood cultures.","Melioidosis (B. pseudomallei) is geographically restricted chiefly to Southeast Asia and northern Australia (with cases in India and China); the organism is a facultative intracellular pathogen acquired from soil and water that can cause latent infection presenting long after exposure.","Melioidosis can mimic tuberculosis with chronic cough, fever, hemoptysis, night sweats, and cavitary upper-lobe disease, and may also present as skin ulceration with lymphangitis and regional lymphadenopathy or as high-mortality septicemia.","Burkholderia cepacia complex can cause the rapidly fatal cepacia syndrome (respiratory distress plus septicemia) in cystic fibrosis; B. cenocepacia in particular is a barrier to lung transplantation, with relatively high one-year mortality.","Stenotrophomonas maltophilia is an opportunist whose emergence requires major disruption of host flora, classically broad-spectrum cephalosporin or carbapenem therapy; distinguishing colonization from true infection in ventilated, non-neutropenic patients is a recurring clinical dilemma."],"pathophysiology":"P. aeruginosa possesses an unusually large arsenal of putative virulence factors, yet few are individually proven to drive human disease, and there is great redundancy. Adhesins (pili, flagellin/flagellar cap binding mucins, LPS outer core binding CFTR to aid internalization, and alginate in mucoid strains) mediate colonization, with adhesion strongest to injured cell surfaces. A type III secretion system injects toxins (ExoS or ExoU, ExoT, ExoY) into host cells, allowing evasion of phagocytes; strains secreting ExoU are associated with higher mortality. A type II secretion system releases diffusible toxins including exotoxin A, multiple proteases (which degrade host cytokines and chemokines), and phospholipases. Rhamnolipids, pyocyanin, and hydrocyanic acid further injure tissue and can cause neutrophil death. Inflammatory responses to lipid A and flagellin via Toll-like receptors (principally TLR4 and TLR5) are required for defense but, when florid, contribute to sepsis and septic shock. In chronic infection (cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis, diffuse panbronchiolitis) defective mucociliary clearance and mucus stasis select for P. aeruginosa, which adapts over time by losing pili and flagella, losing the O side chain of LPS (becoming complement-sensitive), and overproducing mucoid exopolysaccharide, dampening the host response and enabling decades of persistence with attenuated virulence factor production. B. pseudomallei survives within neutrophils and macrophages, aided by a polysaccharide capsule and a type III secretion system, and provokes a florid inflammatory response. B. cepacia complex organisms bind lung mucus and possess potent LPS that drives lung inflammation. S. maltophilia virulence is limited, with disease largely a host inflammatory response to LPS and flagellin; its hallmark is broad intrinsic antibiotic resistance via efflux pumps and the L1 and L2 beta-lactamases.","additionalDifferentials":["Other gram-negative bacterial pneumonia and sepsis (clinically often indistinguishable from P. aeruginosa)","Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia (can also produce ecthyma-gangrenosum-like lesions)","Tuberculosis (chronic cavitary melioidosis mimic)","Pyogenic meningitis of other bacterial etiology (CSF profile of Pseudomonas meningitis is nonspecific)","Polymicrobial osteomyelitis from trauma or decubitus ulcers"],"infectionMimics":["Aspergillosis and mucormycosis (can produce ecthyma-gangrenosum-like lesions)","Staphylococcus aureus and Aspergillus species causing malignant/necrotizing otitis externa","Tuberculosis mimicked by chronic pulmonary melioidosis","Other gram-negative enterocolitis mimicked by Pseudomonas enterocolitis / Shanghai fever"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Fluid overload mistaken for pneumonia on CT in ventilated patients (contributes to overdiagnosis of HAP/VAP)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Sputum Gram stain showing predominant polymorphonuclear leukocytes plus culture positive for P. aeruginosa supports acute pneumonia rather than colonization","Multiplex PCR platforms on respiratory and blood samples identify P. aeruginosa earlier than classical culture","Technetium-99 bone scan as the gold standard for malignant (necrotizing) otitis externa","MRI and bone scan for puncture-wound foot osteomyelitis; needle aspiration usually yields the diagnosis","Deep bone biopsy before treating presumed P. aeruginosa osteomyelitis in trauma or decubitus settings to confirm its causative role","Blood cultures plus echocardiographic/clinical signs for endocarditis (blood cultures often negative in vertebral osteomyelitis unless endocarditis coexists)","Mandatory laboratory susceptibility testing before using newer beta-lactam/beta-lactamase-inhibitor agents or cefiderocol against MDR/XDR strains"],"redFlags":["Ecthyma gangrenosum in a neutropenic or AIDS patient suggesting P. aeruginosa bacteremia","Rapidly progressive corneal opacification/perforation indicating sight-threatening Pseudomonas keratitis (medical emergency)","Severe lancinating ear pain with granulation tissue in a diabetic, plus cranial nerve palsy, signaling malignant otitis externa","Hypothermia or shock in P. aeruginosa bacteremia (marker of severe illness)","Cepacia syndrome: rapidly fatal respiratory distress with septicemia in cystic fibrosis","Hemoptysis, night sweats, and cavitary disease in a patient from an endemic region suggesting melioidosis with systemic spread"],"prognosis":"P. aeruginosa carries high attributable mortality and is among the most feared pathogens in neutropenic patients. Historical crude bacteremia mortality exceeded 50%, with more recent attributable mortality of about 28 to 44% depending on adequacy and timing of treatment and underlying disease severity. Pneumonia mortality has been reported at 40 to 80%, though the fraction attributable to underlying disease is uncertain, and VAP outcomes are poor. Chronic airway infection (e.g., cystic fibrosis) is rarely eradicated and is the leading contributor to respiratory failure and death in CF. Pseudomonas endovascular infection in people who inject drugs is more indolent than S. aureus endocarditis and is more often curable when right-sided. Melioidosis septicemia carries high mortality, with rates as high as 44% reported in Thailand. B. cenocepacia infection has relatively high one-year mortality after lung transplantation. S. maltophilia pneumonia/bacteremia can cause septic shock, with a severe high-mortality form described in neutropenic patients.","pitfalls":["Overdiagnosing HAP/VAP: positive sputum, endotracheal aspirate, or PCR results may reflect tracheobronchial colonization, endotracheal-tube biofilm, or simple tracheobronchitis rather than true pneumonia; clinical judgment is essential.","In trauma- or decubitus-associated osteomyelitis, infection is often polymicrobial and the causative role of P. aeruginosa can be overstated without a deep bone biopsy.","Treating S. maltophilia or P. aeruginosa pulmonary cultures as infection when they may represent colonization, especially in non-neutropenic patients; unnecessary therapy promotes resistance.","Assuming community sputum cultures prove community-acquired P. aeruginosa pneumonia in patients prone to airway colonization by multiple bacterial strains.","Empirically relying on first-line antipseudomonal agents in settings where susceptibility is below 80%, or on the newer agents/cefiderocol without susceptibility testing given unpredictable resistance mechanisms.","Failing to remove indwelling foreign bodies (urinary catheters, stents, stones, central lines) — relapse of UTI or bacteremia may be due to the retained device or ongoing obstruction rather than antibiotic resistance.","Not recognizing that melioidosis can present long after travel to an endemic area due to latent intracellular infection."],"severityClassification":"No single formal severity score is presented. Resistance is graded as multidrug-resistant (MDR) and extensively drug-resistant (XDR) P. aeruginosa, with extremely high MDR rates reported from eastern/southern Europe, Latin America, India, and China, especially in ICUs. Carbapenemase production is highlighted as the most threatening resistance mechanism. The B. cepacia cepacia syndrome denotes a rapidly fatal severe phenotype. Melioidosis spans a clinical spectrum from asymptomatic infection through localized abscesses and pneumonia to high-mortality disseminated septicemic disease.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1307","Harrison 22e p.1308","Harrison 22e p.1309","Harrison 22e p.1310","Harrison 22e p.1311","Harrison 22e p.1312","Harrison 22e p.1313","Harrison 22e p.1314"],"crossLinks":[]},"infections_due_to_the_hacek":{"name":"Infections Due to the HACEK Group and Miscellaneous Gram-Negative Bacteria","system":"Infectious Diseases (cardiovascular/systemic)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HACEK endocarditis","HACEK group infections","Fastidious gram-negative bacterial infections","Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella infections"],"chapter":163,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1265","clinicalPearls":["HACEK is an acronym for a group of fastidious, slow-growing gram-negative bacteria that require a carbon-dioxide-enriched atmosphere and do not grow on routine enteric media such as MacConkey agar: Haemophilus species, Aggregatibacter (formerly Actinobacillus) species, Cardiobacterium species, Eikenella corrodens, and Kingella kingae.","These organisms normally colonize the oral cavity; invasive disease characteristically follows a recent dental procedure or nasopharyngeal infection in a patient with underlying valvular heart disease or prosthetic valves.","A positive HACEK blood culture is strongly predictive of underlying infective endocarditis, with an overall positive predictive value around 60%, though this varies sharply by organism (e.g., 62-100% with Aggregatibacter actinomycetemcomitans bacteremia versus only 0-6% with Eikenella bacteremia).","HACEK organisms cause roughly 0.8-6% of infective endocarditis cases, most often due to Aggregatibacter species, Haemophilus species, and Cardiobacterium hominis; the aortic and mitral valves are most commonly affected.","Compared with non-HACEK endocarditis, HACEK endocarditis occurs in younger patients and is more frequently associated with embolic, vascular, and immunologic manifestations; systemic embolization is common (major emboli in roughly 28-71% of cases) and vegetations are seen on echocardiography in up to 85% of patients.","Kingella kingae behaves differently from the other HACEK organisms: more than half of K. kingae infections are bone and joint infections, oropharyngeal colonization peaks in the first 3 years of life and is higher in daycare-attending children, and it can be transmitted child-to-child and cause outbreaks; K. kingae bacteremia can present with a petechial rash resembling Neisseria meningitidis sepsis.","Capnocytophaga canimorsus and C. cynodegmi are endogenous to the dog and cat mouth; infection often follows a dog or cat bite (or exposure without a bite), and asplenia, glucocorticoid use, and alcohol abuse predispose to severe sepsis with shock and DIC, classically with a petechial rash progressing to purpura and gangrene.","Pasteurella multocida colonizes the respiratory and GI tracts of domestic animals (oropharyngeal colonization 50-90% in cats, 50-70% in dogs) and is transmitted by bites, scratches, or licking; most human infections are skin and soft tissue, and almost two-thirds are caused by cats.","Aeromonas can produce ecthyma gangrenosum (hemorrhagic vesicles with a rim of erythema and central necrosis/ulceration) resembling lesions of Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection, and is associated with water exposure, floods, and other hydrologic disasters (along with Shewanella and Chromobacterium).","Diagnostic recovery of HACEK organisms is improved by alerting the microbiology laboratory; most cultures turn positive within the first week with modern systems (e.g., BACTEC), and prolonged incubation has not been shown to increase recovery of clinically significant isolates.","Molecular methods aid diagnosis: 16S rRNA gene amplification (PCR) on blood or cardiac valve tissue and MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry on colonies increase the accuracy and speed of HACEK identification; real-time PCR and inoculation of synovial fluid into aerobic blood culture bottles enhance recovery of K. kingae in culture-negative cases.","Chromobacterium violaceum causes rare but often fatal soft tissue infections and metastatic abscesses, occurring especially in children with defective neutrophil function such as chronic granulomatous disease."],"pathophysiology":"HACEK organisms are commensals of the human oral cavity. Disruption of the oral mucosa (dental procedures, nasopharyngeal infection, periodontal disease) allows transient bacteremia; in patients with damaged native valves or prosthetic material, the slow-growing organisms seed and form valvular vegetations, producing a typically subacute, indolent endocarditis. Their fastidious, CO2-dependent slow growth accounts for delayed culture positivity and difficulty in susceptibility testing (including underdetection of beta-lactamase). The remaining fastidious and miscellaneous gram-negative organisms in this chapter act largely as opportunists: animal-mouth flora (Pasteurella multocida, Capnocytophaga canimorsus) inoculated through bites/scratches/licking, environmental and waterborne organisms (Aeromonas, Shewanella, Chromobacterium violaceum, Achromobacter xylosoxidans) introduced through wounds or contaminated fluids, and nosocomial nonfermenters (Elizabethkingia/Chryseobacterium, Rhizobium radiobacter, Alcaligenes faecalis) associated with contaminated fluids, indwelling devices, and immunocompromised hosts.","additionalDifferentials":["Viridans group streptococcal endocarditis (also subacute, culture-positive, common in dental-source disease)","Enterococcal endocarditis","Culture-negative endocarditis (Bartonella, Coxiella burnetii, Tropheryma whipplei) when blood cultures are sterile","Staphylococcus aureus endocarditis when the course is more acute","Septic arthritis/osteomyelitis from Staphylococcus aureus in children (where K. kingae may have surpassed S. aureus as a leading cause)","Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection when ecthyma gangrenosum-like skin lesions are present (Aeromonas)"],"infectionMimics":["Neisseria meningitidis sepsis (K. kingae bacteremia can produce a similar petechial rash)","Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection (Aeromonas can cause indistinguishable ecthyma gangrenosum)","Other gram-negative bacteremic pneumonias (Achromobacter xylosoxidans bacteremia, similar high mortality)","Other causes of culture-negative or slow-growing endocarditis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Noninfective embolic disease and vasculitis (HACEK endocarditis frequently presents with embolic, vascular, and immunologic phenomena such as cerebrovascular accidents, mycotic aneurysms, and immune-complex glomerulonephritis that may mimic primary vasculitic or thromboembolic disorders)","Hemolytic-uremic syndrome (rare HUS following bloody diarrhea has been linked to Aeromonas, but HUS itself is the differential)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cultures with notification of the laboratory that a HACEK organism is suspected; modern automated systems (e.g., BACTEC) usually yield growth within the first week","16S rRNA gene amplification (PCR) of blood or cardiac valve specimens for culture-negative or fastidious cases","MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry performed directly on colonies for rapid, accurate species identification","Echocardiography to detect valvular vegetations (seen in up to 85% of HACEK endocarditis)","Real-time PCR of blood or joint fluid for Kingella kingae; inoculation of synovial fluid into aerobic blood culture bottles to improve recovery","In vitro antimicrobial susceptibility testing, including Etest, given slow growth and possible undetected beta-lactamase; susceptibility testing is essential for the miscellaneous multidrug-resistant gram-negative organisms"],"redFlags":["Systemic embolization (cerebral and other major emboli are common in HACEK endocarditis)","Congestive heart failure complicating endocarditis (reported in roughly 19-50% of Haemophilus HACEK cases)","Mycotic aneurysm formation","Petechial/purpuric rash with sepsis after a dog or cat bite in an asplenic, alcohol-abusing, or glucocorticoid-treated patient (Capnocytophaga canimorsus, may progress to shock, DIC, and gangrene)","Petechial rash with bacteremia mimicking meningococcemia (Kingella kingae)","Necrotizing fasciitis or myonecrosis after water/wound exposure (Aeromonas; very high mortality with myonecrosis)","Sepsis with metastatic abscesses in a child with defective neutrophil function (Chromobacterium violaceum)"],"prognosis":"Overall prognosis in both native-valve and prosthetic-valve HACEK endocarditis is excellent and significantly better than endocarditis caused by non-HACEK pathogens; one case-control study found significantly lower 1-year mortality than viridans group streptococcal endocarditis. Mortality associated with HACEK endocarditis in older series ranged from about 28 to 71% depending on the cohort. By contrast, several miscellaneous organisms carry high mortality: Pasteurella multocida invasive disease up to ~30% despite therapy, Achromobacter xylosoxidans bacteremia up to ~67%, Aeromonas sepsis ~25% in immunocompromised adults and >90% with myonecrosis, Chryseobacterium indologenes up to ~50%, and Elizabethkingia anophelis bloodstream infection ~18%.","pitfalls":["Because of slow growth, antimicrobial susceptibility testing may be difficult and beta-lactamase production may go undetected; resistance is most common in Haemophilus and Aggregatibacter species.","Prolonged blood-culture incubation has not been shown to improve recovery of clinically significant HACEK isolates, so relying on extended incubation alone may delay diagnosis—molecular methods may be needed.","Failing to alert the microbiology laboratory that a fastidious HACEK organism is suspected can compromise recovery and identification.","Eikenella corrodens is resistant to clindamycin, metronidazole, and aminoglycosides, so empirical regimens chosen for typical gram-negatives may fail.","Many miscellaneous gram-negative organisms (Achromobacter, Elizabethkingia/Chryseobacterium, Chromobacterium, Aeromonas) are frequently multidrug-resistant—Elizabethkingia/Chryseobacterium are typically resistant to carbapenems while Aeromonas can produce carbapenemases—so therapy must be guided by susceptibility testing rather than assumptions.","Capnocytophaga canimorsus infection may be missed when there is exposure without an obvious scratch or bite, and severe disease can occur in patients without an apparent wound."],"severityClassification":"No formal grading system; clinical severity is stratified by site and host factors. HACEK endocarditis runs a typically subacute course (more aggressive with Kingella kingae) with severity marked by embolic, vascular, and immunologic complications, heart failure, and mycotic aneurysm. For the miscellaneous organisms, severity escalates with immunocompromise, asplenia, cirrhosis, malignancy, diabetes, neutrophil dysfunction, and extremes of age, and with deep/invasive presentations such as bacteremia, necrotizing fasciitis, myonecrosis, and septic shock with DIC.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1265","Harrison 22e p.1266","Harrison 22e p.1267","Harrison 22e p.1268"],"crossLinks":[]},"infections_of_the_skin_muscles":{"name":"Infections of the Skin, Muscles, and Soft Tissues","system":"Skin / Soft Tissue / Musculoskeletal (Infectious Diseases)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Skin and soft tissue infections","SSTI","Soft tissue infections"],"chapter":134,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1049","clinicalPearls":["An anatomic approach is the key to diagnosis: the level and physical appearance of the lesion (epidermis, dermis, subcutaneous fat, fascia, or muscle) together with location, time course, travel/animal-bite history, age, comorbidities, and lifestyle narrow the differential (Harrison 22e p.1049-1052).","Because the avascular epidermis is protected mainly by the stratum corneum, any breach (burns, bites, abrasions, foreign bodies, ulcers, surgery, primary dermatologic disease) lets bacteria reach deeper structures (Harrison 22e p.1049).","Edema with purple bullae, ecchymosis, and cutaneous anesthesia signals loss of vascular integrity and mandates exploration of deep structures for necrotizing fasciitis or myonecrosis (Harrison 22e p.1049).","Unexplained fever plus disproportionate soft-tissue pain and tenderness, even without overt cutaneous inflammation, should raise high suspicion for a deep necrotizing infection and prompt early diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1049).","Vesicular lesions reflect viral proliferation within the epidermis (e.g., varicella with crops of 'dewdrop' vesicles, dermatomal herpes zoster preceded by pain, HSV around lips or genitals, coxsackievirus A16 hand-foot-mouth); rickettsialpox produces a crusted black eschar after mite-bite inoculation of Rickettsia akari (Harrison 22e p.1049-1050).","Crusted golden-brown lesions suggest impetigo: impetigo contagiosa from S. pyogenes (linked to poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis but NOT rheumatic fever) and bullous impetigo from S. aureus; MRSA impetigo epidemics have been reported (Harrison 22e p.1051).","A focused exposure history points to the organism in cellulitis: cat/dog bites (Pasteurella multocida, also S. intermedius, Capnocytophaga), freshwater lacerations (Aeromonas hydrophila), Gulf of Mexico/Atlantic saltwater or raw seafood in cirrhosis (Vibrio vulnificus), fish/swine handling (Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae), and aquariums/swimming pools (Mycobacterium marinum) (Harrison 22e p.1050-1052).","Cultures are frequently unrevealing in cellulitis: even needle aspiration of the leading edge or punch biopsy is positive in only about 20% of cases, suggesting low bacterial numbers and a toxin/inflammatory-mediator effect (Harrison 22e p.1052).","Erysipelas (S. pyogenes) shows abrupt fiery-red swelling with sharply demarcated indurated margins (classically along the nasolabial fold), rapid spread, and intense pain; desquamation follows at 5-10 days (Harrison 22e p.1052).","Imaging (plain films, CT, MRI) helps localize abscess or detect gas in tissue but may show only nonspecific soft-tissue swelling; notably, gas is usually absent in necrotizing fasciitis and myonecrosis caused by group A Streptococcus or MRSA, so a negative gas finding does not exclude fulminant infection (Harrison 22e p.1053).","Hospital admissions for skin and soft tissue infections rose ~27% from 2000-2004, largely due to emergence of the USA300 MRSA clone (Harrison 22e p.1049).","Pyomyositis (usually S. aureus, common in the tropics) typically has no portal of entry and begins at the exact site of blunt trauma or muscle strain; PVL-producing MRSA pyomyositis has occurred in U.S. children (Harrison 22e p.1053)."],"pathophysiology":"Infection localizes according to skin anatomy. The epidermis is avascular and relies on the stratum corneum as a mechanical barrier; disruption permits bacterial entry, and hair follicles act as portals for normal flora (Staphylococcus) or exogenous organisms (Pseudomonas in hot-tub folliculitis). The rich subpapillary capillary plexus mediates inflammatory signs and allows metastatic seeding (Osler nodes, Janeway lesions, palpable purpura in endocarditis; cutaneous lesions of disseminated fungal, gonococcal, Salmonella, Pseudomonas/ecthyma gangrenosum, meningococcal, and staphylococcal infection) and access to the bloodstream. Bacteria such as S. pyogenes spread laterally via lymphatics, producing rapidly extending erysipelas, and subsequent lymphatic obstruction causes flaccid epidermal edema. Excess cytokines or bacterial toxins amplify these responses, causing leukostasis, venous occlusion, and edema; loss of vascular integrity produces purple bullae and cutaneous anesthesia indicative of deep necrotizing infection. In necrotizing fasciitis, infection extends along fascial planes and through venous channels and lymphatics, with dermal-papillary vessel thrombosis and progression to friable, bluish/black skin and systemic toxicity. Group A streptococcal and clostridial muscle infection can produce acute rhabdomyolysis with markedly elevated creatine phosphokinase. Toxin biology drives several syndromes: exfoliatin (phage group II S. aureus) cleaves the stratum corneum in staphylococcal scalded-skin syndrome, while PVL-producing MRSA can cause necrotizing fasciitis and pyomyositis, and TSST-1/enterotoxins underlie shock in toxic shock syndrome (Harrison 22e p.1049-1053).","additionalDifferentials":["Vesicular: varicella, herpes zoster, HSV-1/HSV-2, hand-foot-mouth disease (coxsackievirus A16), orf, molluscum contagiosum, rickettsialpox, blistering distal dactylitis, smallpox/mpox","Bullous: staphylococcal scalded-skin syndrome, necrotizing fasciitis, gas gangrene, halophilic Vibrio (V. vulnificus)","Crusted: impetigo contagiosa/bullous impetigo, ringworm (dermatophytes), sporotrichosis, blastomycosis, disseminated coccidioidomycosis, cutaneous TB, M. chelonae nodules in HIV","Papulonodular: M. marinum, cat-scratch disease/bacillary angiomatosis (Bartonella henselae), cutaneous larva migrans, dracunculiasis, onchocerciasis, verruga peruana (B. bacilliformis), cysticercosis, lepromatous leprosy, secondary/tertiary syphilis, HPV warts","Ulcers/eschars: cutaneous anthrax, ulceroglandular tularemia, bubonic plague, Buruli ulcer (M. ulcerans), leprosy, cutaneous TB, chancroid, primary syphilis"],"infectionMimics":["Sebaceous cysts blocked at the follicular duct can resemble or become secondarily infected like staphylococcal abscesses","Hidradenitis suppurativa (sweat-gland inflammation, especially axillary) can mimic hair-follicle infection","Crusted primary infections with Blastomyces dermatitidis or Sporothrix schenckii can initially resemble ringworm"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Toxic epidermal necrolysis (drug-induced, adult, higher mortality) versus staphylococcal scalded-skin syndrome; distinguished by frozen-section cleavage plane (stratum germinativum in TEN vs stratum corneum in SSSS)","Pyoderma gangrenosum on the anterior shins, distinguished from infectious ulcers by biopsy histology","Decubitus (pressure) ulcers from pressure-induced vascular insufficiency, which may become secondarily infected","Early necrotizing fasciitis misdiagnosed as thrombophlebitis or a simple muscle strain/bruise"],"additionalInvestigations":["Gram stain and culture of drainage, open wound, or obvious portal of entry give a definitive cellulitis diagnosis when present","Needle aspiration of the leading edge or punch biopsy (positive in only ~20% of cellulitis and ~80% negative in deep infections)","Punch biopsy with frozen section to distinguish SSSS from TEN and to evaluate necrotizing fasciitis","KOH staining of skin scrapings for dermatophyte (ringworm) infection","Soft-tissue plain radiography, CT, and MRI to assess depth, localize abscess, and detect tissue gas","Serum creatine phosphokinase, which may be markedly elevated in concomitant myositis/myonecrosis","Open surgical inspection with debridement as the best means of determining extent/severity and obtaining material for Gram stain and culture","Biopsy and culture of crusted lesions in patients from endemic areas (e.g., for Coccidioides immitis)"],"redFlags":["Disproportionate pain and tenderness with unexplained fever and minimal skin findings","Edema with purple/violaceous bullae, ecchymosis, skin sloughing, or cutaneous anesthesia","Rapidly progressing lesion with brawny edema, dark-red or bluish/maroon/black skin discoloration","Systemic toxicity, shock, multiorgan failure, or renal impairment preceding shock (streptococcal necrotizing fasciitis/TSS)","Scrotal/perineal swelling extending to abdominal wall or legs (Fournier gangrene)","Postpartum or post-abortion woman with little/no fever, no purulent discharge, refractory hypotension, marked edema/effusions, hemoconcentration, and a very high WBC count (Clostridium sordellii infection - often rapidly fatal)","Halophilic Vibrio infection that is as fulminant as necrotizing fasciitis with seawater/raw-seafood exposure, especially in cirrhosis"],"prognosis":"Outcome ranges from benign and self-limited (e.g., hot-tub folliculitis, though rare bacteremia and shock have occurred) to rapidly fatal. Necrotizing fasciitis with no defined portal of entry carries high mortality, and survivors often require repeated surgery including amputation. Clostridium sordellii infection (postpartum, post-abortion, or black-tar-heroin injection) is often fatal with rapid death. Mpox case-fatality (1-10%) is lower than smallpox, and TEN carries higher mortality than SSSS. Delay in surgical debridement significantly increases mortality in fulminant infections (Harrison 22e p.1050-1053).","pitfalls":["Relying on absence of gas to exclude necrotizing fasciitis/myonecrosis - gas is usually absent with group A Streptococcus or MRSA","Sending home patients with severe pain and fever after misdiagnosing early necrotizing fasciitis as thrombophlebitis and giving only analgesia","Trusting negative imaging or biopsy (negative in ~80% of deep infections) to rule out fulminant infection","Using empirical vancomycin against Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae, which is unusually vancomycin-resistant among gram-positive organisms","Confusing SSSS with TEN without frozen-section evaluation of the cleavage plane","Letting hyperbaric oxygen delay or substitute for surgical debridement when debridement is indicated"],"severityClassification":"Clinically stratified by depth and aggressiveness rather than a single formal score: (1) superficial epidermal/follicular infections (vesicular, crusted, folliculitis); (2) dermal/lymphatic spread (erysipelas, cellulitis - subdivided into purulent cellulitis [MSSA/MRSA, focal abscess source] versus nonpurulent cellulitis [S. pyogenes, rapidly spreading, lymphangitis/fever]); and (3) deep, life-threatening necrotizing infections of fascia and muscle (necrotizing fasciitis, gas gangrene/myonecrosis, pyomyositis), signaled by systemic inflammatory response, shock, and multiorgan failure (Harrison 22e p.1052-1053).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1049","Harrison 22e p.1050","Harrison 22e p.1051","Harrison 22e p.1052","Harrison 22e p.1053","Harrison 22e p.1054"],"crossLinks":[]},"infectious_arthritis":{"name":"Infectious Arthritis","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["septic arthritis","bacterial arthritis","pyogenic arthritis","joint infection"],"chapter":135,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1055","clinicalPearls":["Staphylococcus aureus, streptococci, and Neisseria gonorrhoeae are the most common causes of infectious arthritis, though mycobacteria, spirochetes, fungi, and viruses can also seed joints.","Acute bacterial infection can rapidly destroy articular cartilage, so every inflamed joint warrants prompt evaluation; cartilage degradation can begin within about 48 hours.","Roughly 90% of acute septic arthritis presents in a single joint, most often the knee, then the hip, and less often shoulder, wrist, or elbow.","In IV drug users, infections of the spine, sacroiliac joints, and sternoclavicular joints are more common than those of the appendicular skeleton.","Acute bacterial infection typically causes mono- or oligoarthritis; subacute/chronic monoarthritis suggests mycobacterial or fungal disease, and acute polyarthritis suggests viral, immunologic (endocarditis, rheumatic fever, DGI), or underlying rheumatoid disease.","Synovial fluid Gram stain is positive in roughly 30-50% of cases overall; organisms are seen on smear in nearly three-quarters of S. aureus and streptococcal infections but only 30-50% of gram-negative and other bacterial infections.","Synovial fluid cultures are positive in over 90% of nongonococcal bacterial arthritis cases, but in gonococcal arthritis synovial cultures are frequently negative and blood cultures positive in fewer than 45%.","Disseminated gonococcal infection most commonly presents as an arthritis-dermatitis syndrome with fever, chills, hemorrhagic pustular rash, tenosynovitis, and migratory arthritis; women are 2-3 times more likely than men to develop DGI, with peak risk during menses and pregnancy.","Lyme disease causes arthritis in up to 60% of untreated persons; serologic IgG to B. burgdorferi is positive in over 90% of Lyme arthritis, and NAAT detects Borrelia DNA in synovial fluid in about 85%.","Tuberculous arthritis is usually a chronic granulomatous monoarthritis of large weight-bearing joints (hips, knees, ankles); synovial fluid acid-fast staining is positive in under one-third of cases but synovial tissue culture is positive in about 90%.","Parvovirus B19 causes polyarthritis or polyarthralgia in about 60% of infected women and 10% of children; rubella, mumps, hepatitis B, and arboviruses (Zika, chikungunya, Ross River) are other notable viral causes.","Prosthetic joint infection complicates roughly 0.5-2% of total joint replacements and is more common after knee than hip arthroplasty; indolent infection with coagulase-negative staphylococci, Cutibacterium, enterococci, or diphtheroids may persist for months to years without constitutional symptoms."],"pathophysiology":"Bacteria reach the joint hematogenously, from a contiguous bone or soft-tissue focus, or by direct inoculation during surgery, injection, bite, or trauma. In hematogenous infection, organisms escape from synovial capillaries, which lack a limiting basement membrane, and within hours provoke neutrophilic infiltration of the synovium. Neutrophils and bacteria enter the joint space and bacteria adhere to articular cartilage. Cartilage degradation begins within about 48 hours owing to increased intraarticular pressure, release of proteases and cytokines from chondrocytes and synovial macrophages, and invasion of cartilage by bacteria and inflammatory cells. Synovial proliferation forms a pannus over the cartilage, and thrombosis of inflamed synovial vessels develops; abscesses may extend into synovium, cartilage, and subchondral bone. S. aureus surface adhesins promote cartilage adherence and endotoxins drive chondrocyte-mediated cartilage breakdown. Gonococcal arthritis-dermatitis and viral arthritides are believed to be largely immune-mediated, reflecting reaction to circulating organisms and immune-complex deposition rather than direct joint invasion.","additionalDifferentials":["Crystal-induced arthritis (gout, pseudogout) - can clinically mimic and may coexist with septic arthritis","Cellulitis","Bursitis","Acute osteomyelitis","Hemarthrosis or fracture","Monoarticular flare of rheumatoid arthritis","Reactive/postinfectious arthritis","Acute rheumatic fever","Osteoarthritis"],"infectionMimics":["Gonococcal disseminated infection presenting as arthritis-dermatitis (versus true septic monoarthritis)","Meningococcemia with dermatitis-arthritis or purulent monoarthritis","Poncet's disease (reactive polyarthritis of disseminated tuberculosis, with no organisms in the joint)","Viral arthritides (parvovirus B19, rubella, hepatitis B, arboviruses)","Postinfectious/reactive arthritis following enteric or chlamydial infection"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Crystal-induced arthritis (gout, pseudogout)","Rheumatoid arthritis flare","Reactive arthritis / serum sickness","Hemarthrosis, fracture, or foreign body","Ischemic necrosis and osteoarthritis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Arthrocentesis with synovial fluid cell count, differential, Gram stain, and culture (obtain before antibiotics)","Synovial fluid examination for crystals to exclude/identify coexistent gout or pseudogout","Blood cultures (positive in up to 50-70% of S. aureus infections)","Inoculation of synovial fluid into blood-culture bottles to improve yield for fastidious organisms","Nucleic acid amplification tests (NAAT) and MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry for partially treated or culture-negative cases","Sonication of explanted prosthetic material to disrupt biofilm and improve culture yield","Plain radiographs; ultrasound for hip effusion; CT/MRI for sacroiliac, sternoclavicular, and spinal joint infection","Mucosal cultures (endocervical/urethral, rectal, oropharyngeal) and urine NAAT when gonococcal arthritis is suspected","Synovial tissue biopsy and culture for suspected mycobacterial or fungal arthritis"],"redFlags":["Acutely inflamed joint - treat as a medical emergency given rapid cartilage destruction","Narrowing of joint space and bony erosions on radiographs indicate advanced infection and poor prognosis","Septic arthritis of the hip, especially in young children, threatens viability of the femoral head and is best managed surgically","Polyarticular involvement or absent fever in rheumatoid, renal, hepatic, or immunosuppressed patients can mask infection","Failure of repeated needle aspiration to clear effusion, lower synovial WBC, and sterilize cultures signals need for arthroscopic or open drainage"],"prognosis":"Prompt systemic antibiotics with timely joint drainage can prevent cartilage destruction, postinfectious degenerative arthritis, joint instability, and deformity, so outcome depends heavily on early recognition and adequate decompression. Radiographic joint-space narrowing and bony erosions indicate advanced disease and a poorer outcome. Gonococcal and Lyme arthritis generally respond well to therapy, and most reactive arthritis resolves within about 6 months, though recurrent disease is more common after chlamydial urethritis. Untreated Lyme arthritis leaves about 10% with chronic erosive synovitis, and certain viral arthropathies (e.g., parvovirus B19) occasionally become chronic.","pitfalls":["Serum procalcitonin is only about 50% sensitive and must not be used to rule out infectious arthritis.","Elevated synovial protein and lactate dehydrogenase with depressed glucose are nonspecific and not required for diagnosis.","Failing to test synovial fluid for crystals can miss coexisting infection and crystal disease, which can occur together.","Relying on synovial fluid culture in suspected gonococcal arthritis is misleading, since cultures are often negative; mucosal-site cultures and NAAT are needed.","Coagulase-negative staphylococci, Cutibacterium, or diphtheroids in a prosthetic joint may represent indolent true infection rather than contamination, so semiquantitative culture and careful aspiration technique are important.","Cellulitis, bursitis, and osteomyelitis can mimic septic arthritis; preserved passive range of motion and less-than-circumferential swelling help distinguish them.","Inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP) are typically elevated but nonspecific."],"severityClassification":"No single formal grading system; synovial fluid cell count guides likelihood and severity. Normal synovial fluid contains fewer than 180 cells/uL (predominantly mononuclear). Acute bacterial infection averages about 100,000 cells/uL (range roughly 25,000-250,000/uL) with over 90% neutrophils. Crystal-induced, rheumatoid, and other noninfectious inflammatory arthritides usually show under 30,000-50,000 cells/uL. Mycobacterial and fungal infections typically show 10,000-30,000 cells/uL with 50-70% neutrophils. Gonococcal DGI synovial fluid usually contains only 10,000-20,000 leukocytes/uL, whereas true gonococcal septic arthritis contains over 50,000/uL.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1055","Harrison 22e p.1056","Harrison 22e p.1057","Harrison 22e p.1058","Harrison 22e p.1059","Harrison 22e p.1060"],"crossLinks":[]},"infectious_complications_of_bites":{"name":"Infectious Complications of Bites","system":"Skin and Soft Tissue / Infectious Disease","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bite wound infection","animal bite infection","human bite infection","dog bite","cat bite","clenched-fist injury","fight bite","rat-bite fever","seal finger"],"chapter":146,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1142-1146","clinicalPearls":["Bite-wound flora generally mirrors the oropharyngeal flora of the biting animal, though organisms from soil, the victim's and animal's skin, and animal feces may also contribute (p.1142).","Dogs account for about 80% of animal bites; roughly 15-20% of dog bites become infected, whereas more than half of cat bites and scratches become infected (p.1142-1143).","Cat bites carry a higher infection risk than dog bites because narrow sharp canine teeth inoculate deeply, predisposing to septic arthritis and osteomyelitis, especially with punctures over or near a joint of the hand (p.1143).","Dog-bite infection typically appears 8-24 h after injury with pain and cellulitis plus purulent, sometimes foul-smelling discharge; the bite-to-symptom interval is notably shorter for cat bites (p.1143).","Pasteurella multocida, a small gram-negative coccobacillus from feline oral flora, causes the majority of cat-bite infections and tends to advance rapidly, sometimes within hours (p.1143).","Capnocytophaga canimorsus following dog bites (or licking of open wounds) can cause fulminant sepsis, DIC, and renal failure, particularly in asplenic, immunosuppressed, or hepatically impaired hosts; this thin gram-negative rod is hard to grow on solid media and may need up to 14 days to grow on blood culture (p.1143).","Human bites become infected more often than other animal bites (~10-15%); clenched-fist ('fight bite') injuries are especially serious because deep hand structures including bone, joint, and tendon are inoculated (p.1144).","Eikenella corrodens is a fastidious organism particularly associated with clenched-fist injuries; the microbiology laboratory should be alerted when it is suspected (p.1144).","Rat-bite fever (Streptobacillus moniliformis in the US; Spirillum minor 'sodoku' mainly in Asia) characteristically manifests after the initial wound has healed; streptobacillary disease causes fever, migratory arthralgias, and a maculopapular rash involving the palms and soles (p.1143).","Aquatic exposures matter: alligator or piranha bites may harbor Aeromonas hydrophila, while shark, moray eel, and barracuda bites are associated with marine Vibrio species (p.1143).","Old World monkey (Macaca) bites can transmit B virus (Macacine herpesvirus 1), a cause of serious CNS infection; nonhuman primate bites otherwise resemble human bites (p.1143).","Seal, walrus, and polar bear bites can cause 'seal finger,' a chronic suppurative infection attributed to Mycoplasma species such as M. phocacerebrale (p.1143)."],"pathophysiology":"Bite injuries inoculate the victim's tissues with the biting animal's oral flora plus environmental and cutaneous organisms, producing a typically polymicrobial mix of aerobes and anaerobes. Deep puncture wounds (cat canines, clenched-fist injuries) drive organisms into joints, tendons, and bone, predisposing to septic arthritis and osteomyelitis. Local spread produces cellulitis with purulent discharge, while seeding can lead to systemic disease (bacteremia, meningitis, brain abscess, endocarditis). Impaired lymphatic drainage (e.g., post-mastectomy limb) or immunocompromise (glucocorticoids, SLE, leukemia, cirrhosis, splenectomy) facilitates dissemination, and Capnocytophaga can incite DIC and renal failure in such hosts (p.1142-1144).","additionalDifferentials":["Rat-bite fever versus Rocky Mountain spotted fever, Lyme disease, leptospirosis, and secondary syphilis (p.1143)","Localized bite-wound cellulitis versus tissue damage from snake envenomation, which can mimic infection (p.1145)","Cat-scratch disease (Bartonella henselae) as a late consequence of cat bites/scratches (p.1143)"],"infectionMimics":["Inflammatory tissue necrosis from venomous snake envenomation, which can be difficult to distinguish from secondary infection (p.1145)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Traumatic soft-tissue injury, crush injury, and laceration without infection","Local envenomation reaction"],"additionalInvestigations":["Gram's stain and aerobic culture of all infected wounds; anaerobic cultures when abscess, devitalized tissue, or foul-smelling exudate is present (p.1144)","Notify the microbiology laboratory when fastidious organisms such as Eikenella corrodens are suspected in human bites (p.1144)","White blood cell count, and blood cultures if systemic infection is suspected (p.1144)","Radiographs of penetrating wounds to assess fracture or retained foreign body such as a tooth fragment (p.1144)","Hand-surgery consultation for hand injuries to evaluate tendon, nerve, and muscular involvement (p.1144)","For suspected Capnocytophaga sepsis, prolonged blood-culture incubation (up to 14 days); organisms may be seen within polymorphonuclear leukocytes on Wright-stained peripheral smears (p.1143)"],"redFlags":["Asplenia, hepatic dysfunction, or immunosuppression after a dog bite raising concern for fulminant Capnocytophaga canimorsus sepsis with DIC and renal failure (p.1143)","Clenched-fist ('fight bite') injury over the metacarpophalangeal joints with possible joint, tendon, or bone involvement (p.1144)","Systemic features: fever, lymphangitis, lymphadenopathy, or signs of bacteremia, meningitis, or endocarditis (p.1143)","Rapidly advancing inflammation within hours suggesting Pasteurella infection (p.1143)","Suspicious human-bite wounds prompting evaluation for domestic or child abuse (p.1144)","Bites posing rabies risk, requiring contact with public-health authorities (p.1144)"],"prognosis":"Most localized bite-wound infections resolve with appropriate wound care and treatment, but a significant minority become severe or life-threatening. Septic arthritis and osteomyelitis prolong the course. Capnocytophaga sepsis can be fulminant in susceptible hosts. Streptobacillary rat-bite fever was frequently fatal in the preantibiotic era and may be complicated by endocarditis, myocarditis, meningitis, pneumonia, and multi-organ abscesses (p.1142-1145).","pitfalls":["Underestimating infection risk in cat bites, whose deep punctures seed joints and bone despite small surface wounds (p.1143)","Missing occult joint/tendon involvement in clenched-fist injuries because patients often present only after frank infection develops (p.1144)","Failing to consider rat-bite fever when illness appears after the original wound has already healed (p.1143)","Inadequate anaerobic culture when devitalized tissue or foul-smelling exudate is present (p.1144)","Not alerting the lab to fastidious organisms (e.g., Eikenella, Capnocytophaga) that require special or prolonged culture conditions (p.1143-1144)","Primary closure of high-risk wounds (e.g., cat puncture wounds, wounds >12 h old on a limb or >24 h old on the face), which raises infection risk (p.1144)"],"severityClassification":"Human-bite wound infection risk stratifies by depth: approximately 2% for superficial wounds versus over 25% for deep wounds such as clenched-fist injuries. Human bites are categorized as occlusional injuries (from actual biting) or clenched-fist ('fight bite') injuries (fist striking teeth), the latter being more common and more serious owing to inoculation of deep hand structures (p.1144).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1142","Harrison 22e p.1143","Harrison 22e p.1144","Harrison 22e p.1145","Harrison 22e p.1146"],"crossLinks":[]},"infectious_mononucleosis":{"name":"Infectious Mononucleosis (EBV)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["EBV infection","glandular fever"],"chapter":199,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1508-1514","clinicalPearls":["Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) is the cause of heterophile-positive infectious mononucleosis (IM), classically marked by the triad of fever, sore throat (pharyngitis), and lymphadenopathy, together with atypical lymphocytosis (Harrison 22e p.1508).","EBV is spread mainly by contact with oral secretions (saliva); by adulthood >90% of individuals are seropositive. IM is typically a disease of young adults, while in young children primary infection is usually asymptomatic or a mild pharyngitis (Harrison 22e p.1508-1509).","The incubation period in young adults is about 4-6 weeks, often preceded by a 1-2 week prodrome of fatigue, malaise, and myalgia before fever, sore throat, and lymphadenopathy appear (Harrison 22e p.1509).","Lymphadenopathy (most often posterior cervical, tender, symmetric, not fixed) and pharyngitis are most prominent in the first 2 weeks, whereas splenomegaly is more prominent during the second and third weeks (Harrison 22e p.1509).","Lymphadenopathy and fever are the most common signs (each in >90%), with pharyngitis/tonsillitis in ~82% and splenomegaly in ~51%; tonsillar exudate can resemble streptococcal pharyngitis (Harrison 22e p.1509).","Characteristic laboratory features: leukocytosis peaking at the second-to-third week with >10% atypical lymphocytes (enlarged cells with abundant cytoplasm, predominantly CD8+ T cells), often with mild neutropenia and thrombocytopenia (Harrison 22e p.1509).","Abnormal liver function (mildly elevated aminotransferases and alkaline phosphatase) occurs in >90% of cases, with hyperbilirubinemia in about 40% (Harrison 22e p.1509).","The heterophile (and the somewhat more sensitive Monospot) test is the standard diagnostic test in children and adults; heterophile antibodies do not interact with EBV proteins. Tests are positive in ~40% in week 1 and 80-90% by week 3, so repeat testing may be needed if performed early (Harrison 22e p.1511).","Heterophile antibodies are often absent in children under 5 years, the elderly, and patients with atypical presentations; in these cases EBV-specific serology should be used (Harrison 22e p.1511).","EBV-specific serology pattern: IgM antibody to viral capsid antigen (VCA) is most useful for acute IM (present only during the first 2-3 months); IgG anti-VCA persists for life and indicates past exposure; antibodies to EBNA appear late (3-6 weeks after symptom onset) and persist for life (Harrison 22e p.1511).","Most cases of IM are self-limited; most patients have symptoms for 2-4 weeks, but nearly 10% have fatigue persisting for 6 months or more (Harrison 22e p.1509).","EBV is associated with several malignancies, including Burkitt's lymphoma (~90% of African cases, ~15% of US cases), anaplastic nasopharyngeal carcinoma (uniformly EBV-associated in southern China), gastric carcinoma (the most common EBV-associated malignancy worldwide), Hodgkin's lymphoma (especially mixed-cellularity type), and various T-/NK-cell and CNS lymphomas; EBV-driven lymphoproliferative disease occurs in immunodeficient and transplant patients (Harrison 22e p.1509-1511).","Large epidemiologic data show a strong association of EBV with multiple sclerosis; one study of over a million military personnel found a 32-fold higher MS risk after primary EBV infection, though the causal role still requires further research (Harrison 22e p.1508, 1511)."],"pathophysiology":"EBV, a herpesvirus, is transmitted in saliva and infects oropharyngeal and salivary epithelium plus B lymphocytes (including tonsillar-crypt lymphocytes directly), then disseminates via the bloodstream. Proliferation of EBV-infected B cells alongside reactive T cells enlarges lymphoid tissue. During acute IM up to 1 in 100 peripheral B cells is infected (falling to 1-50 per million after recovery), and there is an inverted CD4+/CD8+ ratio with large clonal CD8+ T-cell expansions (up to 40% directed against EBV antigens). The EBV receptor on B cells is CD21 (the C3d complement receptor); HLA class II is a co-receptor for B-cell entry, while epithelial entry uses ephrin A2. Memory B cells (not epithelial cells) are the lifelong reservoir. Cellular immunity (NK cells, cytotoxic T cells recognizing EBNAs and LMPs) is more important than humoral immunity in control; if T-cell immunity is impaired, infected B cells may proliferate, and in EBV-associated tumors LMP-1 mimics TNF-receptor-family signaling (e.g., CD40) to drive growth (Harrison 22e p.1508-1509).","additionalDifferentials":["CMV infection (the most common cause of heterophile-negative mononucleosis; older age, longer fever, exudative pharyngitis uncommon) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Acute HIV infection (diffuse rash, oral/genital ulcers, aseptic meningitis) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Toxoplasmosis (less splenomegaly; exposure to cats or raw meat) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","HHV-6 infection (older age at presentation) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Streptococcal pharyngitis (no splenomegaly, less fatigue) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Viral hepatitis (higher aminotransferase levels) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Rubella (maculopapular rash, no splenomegaly) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Lymphoma (fixed, nontender lymph nodes) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Drug reaction, most often to phenytoin, carbamazepine, sulfonamides, or minocycline (can occur at any age) (Harrison 22e p.1511)"],"infectionMimics":["CMV mononucleosis (heterophile-negative) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Acute (primary) HIV infection (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Toxoplasmosis (Harrison 22e p.1511)","HHV-6 infection (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Streptococcal pharyngitis (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Viral hepatitis (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Rubella (Harrison 22e p.1511)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lymphoma (fixed, nontender nodes; atypical lymphocytes) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Drug-induced mononucleosis-like reaction (phenytoin, carbamazepine, sulfonamides, minocycline) (Harrison 22e p.1511)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Heterophile antibody test (titer >=40 diagnostic with compatible symptoms and atypical lymphocytes), or the more sensitive commercial Monospot test (~75% sensitive, ~90% specific vs EBV-specific serology) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","EBV-specific serology: IgM and IgG anti-VCA, anti-EBNA, and (less useful for routine diagnosis) anti-early antigen (EA-D, EA-R) (Harrison 22e p.1511)","Peripheral blood examination for atypical lymphocytes and CBC (leukocytosis, possible neutropenia/thrombocytopenia) (Harrison 22e p.1509)","Liver function tests (aminotransferases, alkaline phosphatase, bilirubin) (Harrison 22e p.1509)","EBV DNA detection by PCR in blood or CSF (useful when serology is equivocal, in CNS lymphoma in AIDS, and for monitoring lymphoproliferative disease); throat or blood culture is NOT helpful because EBV persists for life in the oropharynx and B cells (Harrison 22e p.1511)"],"redFlags":["Splenic rupture (occurs in <0.5% of cases, more common in males; may present as abdominal pain, referred shoulder pain, or hemodynamic compromise) - a leading cause of the rare deaths from IM (Harrison 22e p.1510)","Upper-airway obstruction from tonsillar/adenoidal hypertrophy or epiglottic/pharyngeal/uvular edema (Harrison 22e p.1510)","CNS complications (meningitis, encephalitis, cerebellar ataxia, cranial nerve palsies especially CN VII, Guillain-Barre syndrome, transverse myelitis) - usually within the first 2 weeks (Harrison 22e p.1510)","Autoimmune hemolytic anemia (Coombs-positive, cold agglutinins to red-cell i antigen, ~2% of cases) (Harrison 22e p.1510)","Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, severe cytopenias (red-cell aplasia, severe granulocytopenia, thrombocytopenia, pancytopenia) (Harrison 22e p.1510)","Fulminant hepatitis, myocarditis/pericarditis, pneumonia with pleural effusion (Harrison 22e p.1510)","Fatal IM in patients with X-linked lymphoproliferative disease or other primary immunodeficiencies - and rarely in patients with no obvious preexisting immune defect (Harrison 22e p.1510)"],"prognosis":"Most cases of IM are self-limited and resolve over 2-4 weeks; deaths are very rare and are most often due to CNS complications, splenic rupture, upper-airway obstruction, or bacterial superinfection. Nearly 10% of patients have fatigue persisting for 6 months or more. Disease severity correlates with blood CD8+ T-cell and EBV DNA levels. Most neurologic complications resolve without sequelae. The risk of EBV-positive Hodgkin's lymphoma is significantly increased in young adults for several years after EBV-seropositive IM (Harrison 22e p.1509-1511).","pitfalls":["Ampicillin/penicillin rash pitfall: about 95% of patients with infectious mononucleosis given ampicillin develop a rash; classically a macular rash followed penicillin-derivative use, although more recent studies suggest EBV-associated rashes may occur at similar frequency with or without penicillin exposure, and these rashes are not predictive of future penicillin reactions (Harrison 22e p.1509, 1513).","Heterophile/Monospot tests are often falsely negative early in illness (positive in only ~40% during week 1) and are unreliable in children under 5, the elderly, and atypical presentations - repeat testing or EBV-specific serology may be required (Harrison 22e p.1511).","False-positive Monospot results are more common in connective tissue disease, lymphoma, viral hepatitis, and malaria (Harrison 22e p.1511).","Culture of EBV from throat washings or blood does NOT diagnose acute infection because the virus persists lifelong in the oropharynx and B cells (Harrison 22e p.1511).","Glucocorticoids are NOT indicated for uncomplicated IM and may predispose to bacterial superinfection (Harrison 22e p.1512).","Acyclovir has had no significant clinical impact on IM in controlled trials (Harrison 22e p.1512).","Persistent EBV infection is NOT a cause of chronic fatigue syndrome; antibody titers in such patients are not significantly different from healthy seropositive adults and EBV DNA is not elevated (Harrison 22e p.1510-1511)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1508-1514"],"crossLinks":[]},"infertility_and_contraception":{"name":"Infertility and Contraception","system":"Reproductive / Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Subfertility","Female infertility","Male infertility","Contraception","Birth control","Family planning"],"chapter":408,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3149","clinicalPearls":["Infertility is defined as failure to conceive after 12 months of unprotected intercourse; global prevalence is roughly 17.5%, affecting an estimated 48-72 million couples (Harrison 22e p.3149).","Primary infertility describes couples who have never conceived, whereas secondary infertility follows at least one prior pregnancy; about 85% of couples conceive within 12 months and 95% within 24 months (Harrison 22e p.3149).","Female-only factors account for roughly 30-40% of cases, male-only factors 40-50%, combined factors 20-30%, and unexplained infertility up to about 30% - so a complete workup of BOTH partners is recommended (Harrison 22e p.3149-3150).","Fecundability falls steeply with maternal age: compared with women aged 30-31, it is reduced about 14% at 34-35, 53% at 40-41, and 59% at 42-44 years, largely from oocyte meiotic chromosomal errors; a comparable decline is NOT seen in men under 50 (Harrison 22e p.3149).","Evaluation is generally started after 12 months of trying, but after only 6 months if the female partner is over 35 and immediately if she is over 40 (Harrison 22e p.3150).","The fertile window is about 5-6 days before ovulation; intercourse every 1-2 days during this interval maximizes the chance of conception. A basal body temperature rise confirms ovulation has already occurred and therefore cannot be used prospectively to time intercourse (Harrison 22e p.3151).","Long-acting reversible contraceptives (LARC; IUDs and subdermal implants) are the most effective reversible methods, rivaling sterilization, with high continuation and satisfaction rates, making them a good choice even for adolescents and nulliparous women (Harrison 22e p.3153-3154).","Combined estrogen-progestin contraceptives reduce the risk of endometrial cancer (about 50%) and ovarian cancer (about 40%) and help regulate cycles and reduce menorrhagia, dysmenorrhea and hyperandrogenism (Harrison 22e p.3153).","Unintended pregnancies arise mainly from non-use or inconsistent use rather than method failure; methods requiring daily or coitus-related use fail more often than LARC and permanent methods (Harrison 22e p.3152-3153).","Emergency contraception should be offered to any woman who requests it up to 5 days after unprotected intercourse and should NOT be delayed for a pregnancy test or exam; the copper IUD is the most effective option and ulipristal acetate the most effective oral agent (Harrison 22e p.3154).","Lactation can provide effective contraception during the first 6 postpartum months only if breastfeeding is exclusive and menses have not resumed, with failure rates as low as 0.5-1.5 per 100 women-years (Harrison 22e p.3154)."],"pathophysiology":"Conception requires ovulation of a competent oocyte, patent fallopian tubes, a receptive uterus, and adequate sperm delivery and function; disruption at any step causes infertility. Female causes include tubal damage (often from pelvic inflammatory disease, endometriosis, prior surgery, or salpingitis isthmica nodosum), uterine factors (fibroids distorting the cavity, congenital mullerian anomalies, intrauterine scarring), ovulatory dysfunction (PCOS, diminished ovarian reserve, premature ovarian insufficiency), and endocrine disturbance (hypothyroidism, hyperprolactinemia). Age-related decline reflects accumulating meiotic chromosomal errors in the oocyte after 35. Male causes include anatomic/obstructive problems (vasectomy, infection, congenital absence of the vas), endocrine causes (hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, hypothyroidism, hyperprolactinemia, morbid obesity, certain drugs and exogenous testosterone suppressing spermatogenesis), sexual dysfunction, and primary testicular/genetic defects (Klinefelter syndrome, Y-chromosome microdeletions). Hormonal contraception works through negative feedback: continuous estrogen suppresses FSH and follicular development while continuous progestin suppresses the LH surge and ovulation, thickens cervical mucus, thins/alters the endometrium, and impairs tubal motility; progestin-only and levonorgestrel-IUD effects rely mainly on cervical mucus thickening and endometrial thinning. Copper IUDs act as a spermicide that also impairs fertilization and implantation (Harrison 22e p.3149-3154).","additionalDifferentials":["Polycystic ovary syndrome (most common cause of anovulatory infertility)","Diminished ovarian reserve / premature ovarian insufficiency","Hypothalamic amenorrhea (low body weight, excessive exercise)","Hyperprolactinemia and thyroid dysfunction causing anovulation","Endometriosis (present in ~25-50% of infertile women)","Tubal factor disease and hydrosalpinx","Uterine fibroids/polyps/intrauterine adhesions distorting the cavity","Male obstructive vs nonobstructive azoospermia","Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (e.g., Kallmann syndrome)"],"infectionMimics":["Tubal factor infertility secondary to sexually transmitted infection-related salpingitis (the infection causes structural damage; chronic infertility itself is non-infective)","Mumps orchitis as a historical cause of testicular damage and male infertility"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Endometriosis presenting with pelvic pain and dysmenorrhea overlapping other gynecologic disease","Fibroid-related symptoms mimicking other uterine pathology","Anovulation from systemic endocrine disease (thyroid, prolactin)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal and abdominal pelvic ultrasound for uterine/adnexal pathology and antral follicle count (ovarian reserve)","Ovulation assessment: urinary LH detection kits or serum progesterone ~7 days after ovulation","Hysterosalpingogram (HSG) or saline infusion sonogram to assess tubal patency and intrauterine pathology","Ovarian reserve testing: day 2-3 serum FSH and estradiol plus anti-mullerian hormone (AMH) and antral follicle counts","Endocrine tests in irregular menses: serum TSH, prolactin, total and free testosterone","Semen analysis after 2-7 days abstinence (count, motility, morphology, volume, pH); WHO oligoasthenozoospermia thresholds: sperm count <15 million/mL, motility <40%, normal morphology <4%","Genetic evaluation (karyotype and Y-chromosome microdeletion) for azoospermia or severe oligospermia (<5 million/mL); CFTR testing in congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens","Preconception genetic carrier screening based on ethnicity/family history"],"redFlags":["Female partner over 40 years - evaluate immediately rather than waiting","Azoospermia or severe oligospermia (<5 million/mL) - requires genetic and endocrine evaluation","Smoker aged 35 or older using >=15 cigarettes/day - USMEC category 4 contraindication to estrogen-containing contraception","History of venous thromboembolism, known thrombogenic mutations, stroke, or ischemic heart disease - avoid estrogen-containing contraceptives","Migraine with aura - contraindication to combined hormonal contraception","Current breast cancer - contraindication to hormonal contraception","Recurrent pregnancy loss - consider sperm DNA fragmentation assay"],"prognosis":"About 85% of couples not using contraception conceive within one year and 95% within two years, but success of fertility treatment depends strongly on the female partner's age, ovarian reserve, smoking, BMI, and race; advanced maternal age (>35) raises aneuploidy risk and advanced paternal age (>40) is linked to adverse offspring outcomes. IVF carries an overall low complication risk, with multiple pregnancy being the main hazard and ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome reduced by careful monitoring and GnRH (rather than hCG) triggering. Infertility causes substantial psychological stress, but the condition and its treatment do not appear to produce long-term psychological sequelae (Harrison 22e p.3149-3152).","pitfalls":["Treating only one partner - both must be evaluated because male, female, and combined factors are common.","Using basal body temperature to time intercourse prospectively; the temperature rise only confirms ovulation has already happened.","Over-relying on HSG positive findings - its positive predictive value for tubal occlusion is low even though its negative predictive value is high.","Performing routine sperm-antibody testing, scrotal ultrasound, diagnostic laparoscopy, postcoital test, endometrial biopsy, thrombophilia, immunologic testing, or karyotype as part of the initial infertility workup - these are NOT indicated routinely.","Attempting tubal repair/reconstruction with hydrosalpinx or prior tubal infection - low success and increased ectopic risk; salpingectomy of hydrosalpinges actually improves subsequent IVF outcomes.","Routine screening for familial thrombotic disorders before prescribing hormonal contraception is not recommended.","Withholding or delaying emergency contraception to obtain a pregnancy test or exam, or withholding it from overweight/obese women.","Assuming individual abnormal semen parameters predict infertility - none alone is predictive; risk rises with multiple abnormalities."],"severityClassification":"Contraceptive choice is guided by the U.S. Medical Eligibility Criteria (USMEC, adapted from WHO): Category 4 = unacceptable health risk (e.g., smoker >=35 years with >=15 cigarettes/day, acute DVT or prior VTE at high recurrence risk, stroke or thrombogenic mutations, complicated valvular disease, current breast cancer, migraine with aura, uncontrolled hypertension >=160/100); Category 3 = theoretical/proven risks outweigh benefits (e.g., smoker >=35 with <15 cigarettes/day, controlled or moderate hypertension, certain enzyme-inducing anticonvulsants, rifampin/rifabutin, some HIV regimens, bariatric malabsorptive surgery); Category 2 = benefits generally outweigh risks (e.g., obesity alone). Method effectiveness ranges from highly effective sterilization and LARC (>99%) to less reliable barrier and fertility-awareness methods (Harrison 22e p.3152-3153).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3149","Harrison 22e p.3150","Harrison 22e p.3151","Harrison 22e p.3152","Harrison 22e p.3153","Harrison 22e p.3154"],"crossLinks":[]},"inflammatory_bowel_disease":{"name":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Crohn disease","ulcerative colitis","IBD"],"chapter":337,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2551-2563","clinicalPearls":["IBD is a chronic, idiopathic inflammatory disease of the GI tract; ulcerative colitis (UC) and Crohn's disease (CD) are its two major types (Harrison 22e p.2551).","UC is a mucosal disease that almost always involves the rectum and extends proximally in continuity, without intervening areas of uninvolved (skip) mucosa; ~40-50% have disease limited to rectum/rectosigmoid and ~20% have pancolitis (Harrison 22e p.2555).","CD can affect any part of the GI tract from mouth to anus, is segmental with skip areas, and characteristically spares the rectum (unlike UC); the terminal ileum is involved in ~90% of those with small-intestinal disease (Harrison 22e p.2556).","CD is a transmural process producing fistulas, strictures, abscesses and an endoscopic/radiographic 'cobblestone' appearance, whereas UC inflammation is limited to mucosa and superficial submucosa except in fulminant disease (Harrison 22e p.2555-2556).","Noncaseating granulomas are a characteristic histologic feature of CD but are found less often on mucosal biopsy than on surgical resection specimens; UC shows crypt distortion, basal plasmacytosis, cryptitis and crypt abscesses (Harrison 22e p.2556).","Major UC symptoms are diarrhoea, rectal bleeding, tenesmus, mucus passage and crampy abdominal pain; severe pain is not prominent in UC, whereas CD ileocolitis classically causes right-lower-quadrant pain and diarrhoea, sometimes mimicking acute appendicitis (Harrison 22e p.2556-2557).","Perianal disease (perirectal fistulas, fissures, abscesses, anal stenosis, skin tags) occurs in about one-third of CD patients and points away from UC; extensive perianal lesions in apparent UC should suggest CD (Harrison 22e p.2556, p.2557).","Faecal calprotectin and faecal lactoferrin are neutrophil-derived markers that correlate with histologic inflammation, help distinguish IBD from irritable bowel syndrome, predict relapse and detect pouchitis; calprotectin is more sensitive for ileocolonic/colonic than isolated ileal inflammation (Harrison 22e p.2557, p.2558).","Distinguishing UC from CD is impossible initially in up to 15% of cases (indeterminate colitis); ~5% of resection specimens cannot be classified due to overlapping features (Harrison 22e p.2559).","Up to one-third of IBD patients have at least one extraintestinal manifestation; some parallel bowel activity (erythema nodosum, peripheral arthritis, episcleritis) while others run an independent course (pyoderma gangrenosum, ankylosing spondylitis, sacroiliitis, uveitis, PSC) (Harrison 22e p.2561-2563).","Smoking has opposite associations in the two diseases: it may be protective in UC (OR 0.58) but is a risk factor for CD (OR 1.76); prior appendectomy with confirmed appendicitis is protective against UC but not CD (Harrison 22e p.2551-2552).","Patients with long-standing extensive colitis (UC or Crohn's colitis) have an increased risk of colonic dysplasia and carcinoma; surveillance colonoscopy with multiple biopsies is the standard of care (Harrison 22e p.2612-2613)."],"pathophysiology":"IBD is regarded as an inappropriate, dysregulated mucosal immune response to the endogenous commensal microbiota (with or without an autoimmune component) in a genetically susceptible host, shaped by environmental factors. Homeostasis among the commensal microbiota, intestinal epithelial cells and mucosal immune cells is disrupted, producing chronic inflammation. More than ~240 genetic loci (e.g. NOD2, ATG16L1, IL23R, IL12B, IL10, XBP1) contribute incrementally and affect pathways of innate immunity, autophagy/unfolded-protein response, adaptive immunity and inflammation/healing; ~two-thirds are shared between CD and UC. Dysbiosis (e.g. expansion of adherent/invasive E. coli, loss of Faecalibacterium prausnitzii) and defective oral (mucosal) tolerance lead to unrestrained CD4+ T-cell (TH1/TH2/TH17) and innate immune activation, with a self-perpetuating cytokine cascade (IL-1, IL-6, IL-12, IL-23, TNF) driving fibrogenesis, leukocyte recruitment and tissue damage. TH1-driven transmural granulomatous inflammation resembles CD; TH2-type superficial mucosal inflammation resembles UC. Roughly 5-10% of patients have familial disease and only ~20% of disease risk is currently explained by known genetics (Harrison 22e p.2551-2555).","additionalDifferentials":["Indeterminate colitis (overlapping UC/CD features in up to 15% of cases)","Diverticulitis / diverticular-associated colitis (mimics Crohn's colitis)","Ischemic colitis (segmental like CD or diffuse like UC)","Radiation colitis/enteritis","Collagenous and lymphocytic (microscopic) colitis","Diversion colitis","Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome","Immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI)-related colitis","NSAID-associated colitis","Celiac disease (especially in lymphocytic colitis, which should prompt exclusion)","Eosinophilic gastroenteritis, Behcet's syndrome, graft-versus-host disease, neutropenic colitis"],"infectionMimics":["Campylobacter colitis (can mimic severe UC and trigger UC relapse)","Salmonella, Shigella enterocolitis","Yersinia enterocolitica (terminal ileal ulceration mimicking Crohn's ileitis)","Clostridioides difficile colitis","Toxigenic / enteroinvasive / enteroadherent E. coli","Gonorrhoea, Chlamydia trachomatis and syphilis proctitis","Intestinal tuberculosis and Mycobacterium avium (ileocecal involvement)","Cytomegalovirus and herpes simplex colitis/proctitis (may occur even in immunocompetent hosts)","Entamoeba histolytica (amebiasis) - must be excluded before treating presumed IBD","Other parasites: Isospora belli, Trichuris trichiura, hookworm, Strongyloides","Fungal: histoplasmosis, Candida, Aspergillus (mainly in immunocompromised)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Diverticulitis","Ischemic colitis","Radiation colitis/enteritis","Collagenous / lymphocytic (microscopic) colitis","Diversion colitis","Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome","NSAID-induced colitis","Immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI)-related colitis","Drug/chemical colitis (phosphosoda, gold, mycophenolate mofetil, cocaine, oral contraceptives)","Neoplasia (lymphoma, carcinoma of colon/ileum, carcinoid, metastatic disease, familial polyposis)","Appendicitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Acute-phase reactants (CRP, ESR), platelet count, haemoglobin and serum albumin to gauge activity","Faecal calprotectin and faecal lactoferrin (sensitive markers of intestinal inflammation; distinguish IBD from IBS, predict relapse, detect pouchitis and postoperative recurrence)","Stool studies: bacterial culture, C. difficile toxin, ova and parasites, viral studies to exclude infective colitis","Sigmoidoscopy (to assess activity before treatment) and colonoscopy with multiple biopsies, including terminal ileum","Histology of rectal/colonic biopsy specimens","Wireless capsule endoscopy for small-bowel CD (avoid if stricture suspected; consider patency capsule first)","CT enterography or MR enterography (MR preferred in young patients/serial imaging to avoid radiation)","Pelvic MRI for perianal/ischiorectal fistulas and abscesses","Small-bowel ultrasound and intestinal ultrasound for assessing activity and mucosal healing","MRCP (or ERCP) when primary sclerosing cholangitis is suspected","Serologic markers ASCA and pANCA (limited clinical utility; less helpful than clinical factors)"],"redFlags":["Toxic megacolon (transverse or right colon diameter >6 cm with loss of haustration in a severe attack) - may be precipitated by electrolyte abnormalities and narcotics; risk of perforation","Perforation - most dangerous local complication; peritoneal signs may be masked by glucocorticoids; mortality of perforation complicating toxic megacolon is ~15%","Massive GI haemorrhage","Bowel obstruction from fibrostenotic stricture (CD)","Intraabdominal or pelvic abscess and fistula formation (CD); high-spiking fever suggests abscess","Stricture impassable to the colonoscope - presume malignant until proven otherwise","New or worsening dysplasia/carcinoma on surveillance in long-standing colitis"],"prognosis":"IBD is a lifelong chronic disease with a relapsing-remitting course; only ~15% of UC patients present initially with severe disease. Massive haemorrhage occurs in ~1% of UC patients and toxic megacolon is rare (about 50% of acute dilations resolve with conservative management, the remainder requiring urgent colectomy). In CD the inflammatory process tends to evolve toward a fibrostenotic obstructing pattern or a penetrating fistulous pattern; intestinal obstruction occurs in ~40%, intraabdominal/pelvic abscesses in 10-30%, and free perforation in 1-2%. Long-standing extensive colitis carries an increased colorectal cancer risk (UC meta-analysis figures of ~2% at 10 years, 8% at 20 years, 18% at 30 years, though rates have fallen with better inflammation control and surveillance; overall colorectal cancer rates remain ~1.5-2 times the general population). CD and UC cancer risks are probably equivalent for similar extent and duration. Associated PSC carries a 7-10% risk of cholangiocarcinoma (Harrison 22e p.2557-2563, p.2612-2613).","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude infective colitis (especially C. difficile, Campylobacter, Salmonella, Shigella, E. coli, CMV) and amebiasis before diagnosing or escalating treatment of IBD - infection can both mimic IBD and trigger relapse (Harrison 22e p.2559-2561).","In UC, biopsies of normal-appearing mucosa are usually abnormal, so multiple biopsies should be taken from apparently uninvolved proximal and distal mucosa; effective medical therapy can also create pseudo-skip areas (Harrison 22e p.2555).","Peritoneal signs of perforation may be absent or subtle in patients on glucocorticoids (Harrison 22e p.2557).","A colonic stricture impassable with the colonoscope should be presumed malignant until proven otherwise (Harrison 22e p.2557).","Extensive perianal lesions in apparent UC should raise suspicion for Crohn's disease (Harrison 22e p.2557).","Wireless capsule endoscopy must be avoided when a small-bowel stricture is suspected because of the risk of capsule retention (Harrison 22e p.2558).","Serologic tests (ASCA, pANCA) have variable predictive value and unclear clinical usefulness for distinguishing or predicting IBD (Harrison 22e p.2559).","Pyoderma gangrenosum and ankylosing spondylitis run a course independent of bowel disease and do not reliably improve with colectomy (Harrison 22e p.2561-2562)."],"severityClassification":"Ulcerative colitis is graded by the Montreal classification of extent (E1 proctitis; E2 left-sided/distal UC, distal to splenic flexure; E3 extensive/pancolitis, proximal to splenic flexure) and severity (S0 clinical remission; S1 mild: <=4 stools/day with or without blood, no systemic illness, normal ESR; S2 moderate: >=4 stools/day with minimal systemic toxicity; S3 severe: >=6 bloody stools/day, pulse >=90/min, temperature >=37.5C, haemoglobin <10.5 g/100 mL, ESR >=30 mm/h). Crohn's disease is classified by the Vienna and Montreal systems by age at diagnosis (A1/A2/A3), location (L1 ileal, L2 colonic, L3 ileocolonic, L4 upper/foregut) and behaviour (B1 nonstricturing-nonpenetrating, B2 stricturing, B3 penetrating), with a 'p' modifier for perianal disease (Harrison 22e p.2557-2558).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2551-2563"],"crossLinks":["ibd_flare","colorectal_cancer"]},"inflammatory_myopathies":{"name":"Inflammatory Myopathies","system":"Rheumatology / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["polymyositis","dermatomyositis","inclusion body myositis"],"chapter":377,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2909","clinicalPearls":["Major idiopathic inflammatory myopathies (IMs) are dermatomyositis (DM), polymyositis (PM), immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy (IMNM), antisynthetase syndrome (ASyS), and inclusion body myositis (IBM); idiopathic PM without an overlap syndrome is quite rare, while DM, ASyS, IBM, and IMNM occur in roughly similar frequencies (Harrison 22e p.2909).","Most IMs (DM, PM, IMNM, ASyS) present with symmetric proximal-greater-than-distal weakness; DM additionally has a characteristic rash and PM/IMNM/ASyS do not (DM rash, ASyS rash sometimes) (Harrison 22e p.2909-2910).","Serum creatine kinase (CK) is the most sensitive laboratory marker of muscle destruction; a markedly elevated CK (e.g. >2000 U/L) is almost always due to a myopathy, and CK is always elevated in uncontrolled PM and markedly elevated (usually >10x normal) in IMNM (Harrison 22e p.2910-2912).","DM rash includes the heliotrope rash (violaceous eyelid discoloration with periorbital edema), Gottron sign and Gottron papules over extensor joint surfaces, V-sign (anterior neck/chest), shawl sign (posterior neck/shoulders), nail-bed telangiectasias, and subcutaneous calcium deposits; a heliotrope rash with Gottron papules and dilated nailfold capillaries is diagnostic for DM (Harrison 22e p.2910-2911).","Myositis-specific antibodies (MSAs) help define subtypes: DM (anti-Mi-2, anti-TIF1-gamma, anti-MDA5, anti-NXP-2, anti-SAE); IMNM (anti-HMGCR, anti-SRP); ASyS (antisynthetase antibodies, most commonly anti-Jo-1); IBM (anti-cN-1A) (Harrison 22e p.2909-2914).","EMG/nerve conduction studies help localize the lesion to muscle but are nonspecific for cause; muscle MRI shows edema, atrophy, or fatty replacement and can show subtype-specific patterns (e.g. IBM spares the rectus femoris while involving vastus medialis/lateralis) (Harrison 22e p.2910-2914).","A muscle biopsy is often required to distinguish myopathies when there is no characteristic DM rash or MSA, and should be performed in every case of suspected PM to exclude IBM and other causes; biopsy a clinically affected but not too-weak muscle (e.g. MRC grade 4/5) to avoid sampling only end-stage muscle (Harrison 22e p.2910-2911).","Patients with severe muscle pain, subjective weakness, and fatigue but normal strength/function on exam are not likely to have an IM; consider polymyalgia rheumatica in older patients with raised ESR/CRP but normal CK and EMG, and fibromyalgia when the lab workup is normal (Harrison 22e p.2911).","Overlap syndrome describes an IM associated with another connective tissue disease (scleroderma, MCTD, Sjogren syndrome, SLE, rheumatoid arthritis); overlap syndromes are usually responsive to immunotherapy, except Sjogren syndrome with coexisting IBM (Harrison 22e p.2912).","DM, PM, ASyS, and IMNM are typically responsive to immunotherapy with high-dose glucocorticoids as first-line; IBM does NOT typically respond to any known immunotherapy and is managed with physical/occupational and swallowing therapy (Harrison 22e p.2915-2916)."],"pathophysiology":"The inflammatory myopathies are immune-mediated muscle diseases with distinct mechanisms by subtype. DM microvasculopathy and skin/muscle damage are increasingly attributed to type I interferon (IFN)-mediated toxicity, most likely IFN-beta, rather than the older model of antibody/complement-mediated capillary destruction with watershed ischemia; immunoglobulin deposition on endothelium is largely absent and complement deposition may be secondary, and there is increasing evidence MSAs are directly pathogenic (e.g. anti-Mi-2 entering myonuclei to inhibit the CHD4/NuRD complex). PM is heterogeneous with poorly understood pathogenesis; its infiltrate is predominantly CD8+ T cells and macrophages in endomysial, perimysial, and perivascular regions. IMNM shows multifocal necrotic and regenerating fibers with few inflammatory cells; it does not appear primarily complement-driven (complement inhibition failed in anti-HMGCR and anti-SRP disease), anti-HMGCR antibodies may bind sarcolemmal receptor causing acetyl-CoA and lipid accumulation, and anti-SRP antibodies appear directly causal. ASyS resembles DM histologically but with more perifascicular necrosis and HLA-DR (MHC-II) expression suggesting gamma-IFN rather than type I IFN drive. IBM combines adaptive immune attack (highly differentiated CD8+CD57+ KLRG1+ T cells invading non-necrotic MHC-I/II-expressing fibers, plus a relatively specific autoantibody) with degenerative features including rimmed vacuoles, beta-sheet amyloid inclusions, p62/LC3 aggregates and ER stress/autophagy, mitochondrial abnormality (ragged-red and COX-negative fibers), and extrusion of TDP-43 from myonuclei; whether damage is primarily immune or a secondary neurodegenerative process is unclear.","additionalDifferentials":["Inclusion body myositis (IBM) - must exclude in every suspected PM case","Polymyalgia rheumatica (older patient, raised ESR/CRP, normal CK and EMG)","Fibromyalgia (normal laboratory workup)","Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy (can be mimicked by anti-HMGCR myopathy or anti-SRP/seronegative IMNM)","Muscular dystrophies and metabolic myopathies (may show inflammatory infiltrate on biopsy)","Sporadic late-onset nemaline myopathy (SLONM)","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis / neurogenic process (atrophy with fasciculations)","Myasthenia gravis / neuromuscular junction defect (fatigable weakness)","Spinal cord disorder or polyneuropathy (concomitant sensory symptoms)"],"infectionMimics":["Infection-associated myositis (listed by Harrison among other inflammatory myopathies caused by infection)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Toxic myopathies, including the more common toxic statin-associated myopathy which (unlike anti-HMGCR IMNM) improves on statin discontinuation","Checkpoint inhibitor-triggered myositis","Eosinophilic myositis","Granulomatous myositis (and sarcoidosis, associated with IBM)","Hereditary myopathies (muscular dystrophies, metabolic myopathies) with secondary inflammation","Pathogenic biallelic HMGCR gene variants causing proximal weakness, myalgia, high CK and dystrophic changes"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase (most sensitive marker of muscle destruction); serum aldolase if CK normal (raised in ~10% of normal-CK DM)","Myositis-specific and myositis-associated autoantibody panel (anti-Mi-2, anti-TIF1-gamma, anti-MDA5, anti-NXP-2, anti-SAE; anti-HMGCR, anti-SRP; antisynthetase incl. anti-Jo-1; anti-cN-1A)","Antinuclear antibody (can be positive but nonspecific)","Electromyography and nerve conduction studies (localize to muscle; guide biopsy site)","Skeletal muscle MRI (STIR) for edema/inflammation, atrophy, fatty replacement, fasciitis and subtype-specific patterns","Muscle biopsy with experienced histopathology lab (perifascicular atrophy and MxA staining in DM; endomysial CD8/MHC-I in PM; necrotizing pattern in IMNM; perimysial fragmentation/alkaline phosphatase in ASyS; rimmed vacuoles, p62, COX-negative fibers and 15-21 nm tubulofilaments in IBM)","Skin biopsy in DM (cell-poor interface dermatitis)","Pulmonary function tests and spiral chest CT (honeycomb pattern) for interstitial lung disease","Flow cytometry for large granular lymphocytes and CD4/CD8 ratio in IBM","Malignancy workup (especially adult DM, anti-TIF1-gamma, anti-NXP-2, anti-SAE, anti-HMGCR myopathy)"],"redFlags":["Dermatomyositis rash (heliotrope rash, Gottron sign/papules) - adult-onset DM carries ~15% malignancy risk within the first 2-3 years; enhanced cancer vigilance especially with anti-TIF1-gamma (89% specificity, 70% sensitivity for cancer-associated DM)","Anti-MDA5 antibodies - palmar rash, severe ischemic skin ulceration, and rapidly progressive interstitial lung disease (also frequent in amyopathic DM)","Dyspnea from ILD, bronchopneumonia, alveolitis, or ventilatory muscle weakness","Dysphagia and dysarthria (aspiration risk; common in IBM and seen in DM/IMNM)","Myocarditis / cardiac disease (poor prognostic feature)","Markedly elevated CK (e.g. >2000 U/L) - almost always indicates a myopathy"],"prognosis":"In the absence of malignancy, DM prognosis is generally favorable with 5-year survival of 70-93%; poor prognostic features (DM and PM) are increased age, associated ILD, cardiac disease, and late or previously inadequate treatment. Most PM patients improve with immunotherapy but usually require lifelong treatment. IMNM varies by subtype: anti-HMGCR myopathy is often responsive to IVIG monotherapy, whereas anti-SRP and seronegative IMNM are much harder to treat and need aggressive immunotherapy; increased cancer incidence may exist in anti-HMGCR myopathy. ASyS patients mostly respond but disease can be difficult to treat (especially with ILD, where early rituximab is warranted) with no apparent increased malignancy risk. IBM is slowly progressive and not typically responsive to immunotherapy; most patients require a scooter or wheelchair within ~10-15 years of symptom onset, with no known increased malignancy risk.","pitfalls":["IBM is frequently misdiagnosed and is distinguished from PM by distal/asymmetric weakness with predilection for finger/wrist flexors and quadriceps in patients >50, normal or only mildly elevated CK, and being essentially steroid-refractory and unresponsive to any known immunotherapy - so a normal CK should alert clinicians to the possibility of IBM","Many older series of 'PM' likely actually included patients with IBM, IMNM, or muscular dystrophies with inflammation, biasing reported treatment response and cancer-risk data","Perifascicular atrophy (the classic DM biopsy finding) is present in perhaps only 50% of DM patients; MxA immunostaining is diagnostically more sensitive and highly specific","Rimmed vacuoles may be absent in up to 20-30% of IBM biopsies; mitochondrial abnormality (ragged-red/COX-negative fibers) and p62 inclusions help distinguish IBM from PM in such cases","Anti-HMGCR myopathy does NOT improve when statins are stopped (unlike common toxic statin myopathy) and can occur in statin-naive children/young adults, mimicking limb-girdle muscular dystrophy","IBM EMG can show large-amplitude long-duration motor units that may be misinterpreted as neurogenic but actually reflect chronicity of the myopathy","Muscle biopsy should sample a clinically affected but not too-weak muscle (e.g. MRC 4/5); biopsying an end-stage muscle yields non-diagnostic tissue"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal numeric severity grading scale for the inflammatory myopathies. Disease is characterized instead by clinical subtype (DM, PM, IMNM, ASyS, IBM), pattern and degree of weakness (graded clinically, e.g. MRC strength), serum CK elevation, presence of organ involvement (ILD, myocarditis, dysphagia), associated malignancy, and responsiveness to immunotherapy. Treatment intensity (first-line high-dose glucocorticoids; early second-line agents for severe weakness, organ involvement such as myocarditis/ILD, steroid-complication risk, or IMNM) reflects severity stratification in practice.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2909"],"crossLinks":["ild"]},"influenza":{"name":"Influenza","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["flu"],"chapter":206,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1544","clinicalPearls":["Influenza presents with an abrupt febrile illness: fever, myalgias, fatigue, sore throat, headache, and cough (Harrison 22e p.1576).","Influenza A viruses are the most virulent for humans and cause most severe disease, though type B viruses (Victoria lineage) also cause substantial morbidity; type A is subdivided by hemagglutinin (H) and neuraminidase (N) surface protein subtypes (Harrison 22e p.1576).","Two type A subtypes (H1N1 and H3N2) and one type B lineage (Victoria) cause annual seasonal epidemics, which occur in winter in temperate regions (Harrison 22e p.1576, p.1579).","Influenza pneumonia has an unusually high rate of complication by bacterial superinfection, with staphylococcal and streptococcal pneumonia occurring in as many as 10% of cases in some series (Harrison 22e p.1576).","Antiviral treatment of influenza is effective only when given early in the course of illness; baloxavir marboxil is approved for acute uncomplicated flu when started within 2 days of onset (Harrison 22e p.1537, p.1580).","Antibiotics do NOT improve the outcome of uncomplicated viral respiratory infection in otherwise healthy people, but influenza can be complicated by secondary bacterial infection that does warrant antibacterials (Harrison 22e p.1537).","Annual vaccine reformulation is required because antigenic drift (point mutations in HA and NA) causes antigenic divergence; an occasional antigenic mismatch can render a vaccine component of negligible efficacy (Harrison 22e p.1538)."],"pathophysiology":"Influenza is a single-stranded, segmented, negative-sense RNA genome virus of the family Orthomyxoviridae, with four (sero)types: A, B, C, and D. Influenza A and C infect multiple species; B infects humans almost exclusively; D primarily infects cattle and is not known to infect humans. Influenza A is subdivided into 18 hemagglutinin (H) and 11 neuraminidase (N) surface-protein subtypes. Two distinct mechanisms drive antigenic variation: antigenic drift (accumulation of point mutations in the HA and NA molecules) produces the gradual divergence underlying annual epidemics and requires new vaccines each year; antigenic shift, enabled by the segmented genome allowing reassortment between two viruses co-infecting one host, can produce a major novel virus and a pandemic. Type B viruses mutate less frequently than type A, probably reflecting their near-exclusive human host range. Many highly pathogenic strains circulate in aquatic birds; pigs, which carry both avian and human receptors, can serve as a mixing vessel for reassortment that makes zoonotic viruses more fit for human replication (Harrison 22e p.1576).","additionalDifferentials":["COVID-19 (SARS-CoV-2) respiratory illness","Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) infection","Human metapneumovirus infection","Parainfluenza virus infection","Rhinovirus / common cold","Adenovirus infection","Acute bronchitis"],"infectionMimics":["SARS-CoV-2 (COVID-19)","Respiratory syncytial virus","Human metapneumovirus","Parainfluenza virus","Adenovirus","Rhinovirus","Community-acquired bacterial pneumonia (e.g., S. pneumoniae)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute exacerbation of COPD","Acute exacerbation of chronic bronchitis","Decompensated cardiovascular disease presenting with fever and dyspnea"],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR molecular testing of respiratory secretions (most sensitive and specific; multiplex respiratory panels available)","Rapid antigen-based diagnostic test for influenza viral protein in nasopharyngeal secretions","Viral isolation by cell culture in a reference laboratory (gold standard but insensitive/slow)","Direct or indirect fluorescent antibody detection of virus-infected cells in nasal secretions","Chest radiograph for all patients with suspected pneumonia"],"redFlags":["Secondary bacterial pneumonia complicating influenza, classically caused by S. aureus (including MRSA, with risk of necrotizing pneumonia) and S. pneumoniae/S. pyogenes","Severe or fatal primary viral pneumonia, particularly in elderly patients, nursing-home residents, immunocompromised persons, and very young children","Hypoxia or respiratory failure requiring supplemental oxygen or mechanical ventilation","Avian/zoonotic strains (e.g., H5N1, H7N9) with high mortality and pandemic potential, especially with relevant exposure history"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated influenza in otherwise healthy adults is generally self-limited, and antiviral therapy started early shortens illness. Severe and even fatal pneumonia occurs particularly in elderly patients, nursing-home residents, immunocompromised persons, and very young children. Bacterial superinfection complicates as many as 10% of influenza pneumonia cases in some series. Pandemics caused by novel viruses occur on average every 20-30 years (Harrison 22e p.1576, p.1538).","pitfalls":["RT-PCR is extremely sensitive: viral genome can persist in nasal secretions for weeks after an infection resolves, so a positive test may reflect a recently resolved rather than a currently acute infection, and laboratory contamination can produce false positives (Harrison 22e p.1537).","Rapid diagnostic tests have the highest positive predictive value when applied to high-risk, appropriately seasonal samples; indiscriminate testing lowers predictive value (Harrison 22e p.1537).","Adamantanes (amantadine, rimantadine) should not be relied upon: widespread resistance has occurred in many currently circulating influenza A viruses (Harrison 22e p.1580).","Antiviral therapy started late is of limited benefit, as it is effective mainly when given early in the course (Harrison 22e p.1537)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.1544","Harrison 22e p.1576","Harrison 22e p.1537","Harrison 22e p.1538","Harrison 22e p.1580"],"crossLinks":["CAP"]},"inguinal_hernia":{"name":"Inguinal hernia","system":"GI / Abdominal wall / Surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["groin hernia","indirect inguinal hernia","direct inguinal hernia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as a groin swelling that appears or enlarges on standing, coughing, or straining and often reduces on lying flat","Indirect hernias travel down the inguinal canal along the spermatic cord and lie lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, while direct hernias push forward through a weakness in the posterior canal wall medial to those vessels","A hernia arising below and lateral to the pubic tubercle suggests a femoral hernia, whereas one above and medial to it suggests an inguinal hernia; this landmark helps distinguish the two","Reducibility, cough impulse, and whether the swelling can be controlled at the deep ring are key clinical assessments","Loss of cough impulse with a tense, tender, irreducible lump raises concern for obstruction or strangulation","Indirect hernias are the commonest groin hernia in both sexes and across all ages, reflecting a persistent processus vaginalis","Elective repair, most commonly tension-free mesh repair (open Lichtenstein or laparoscopic), is the definitive treatment; watchful waiting may be reasonable for small, minimally symptomatic hernias in selected patients","Chronic cough, constipation, bladder outflow obstruction, and heavy lifting raise intra-abdominal pressure and should be sought as contributing factors"],"pathophysiology":"An inguinal hernia is protrusion of abdominal contents through the inguinal canal. Indirect hernias pass through the deep (internal) inguinal ring into the canal, often through a patent processus vaginalis, and may descend into the scrotum. Direct hernias bulge forward through an attenuated posterior wall of the canal (Hesselbach's triangle) as a result of acquired weakness of the transversalis fascia. Raised intra-abdominal pressure and weakened abdominal wall connective tissue promote herniation. As a hernia enlarges the neck may narrow relative to the sac, predisposing to incarceration (irreducibility) and strangulation, in which the blood supply to trapped bowel is compromised, leading to ischaemia and, if untreated, perforation.","additionalDifferentials":["Femoral hernia","Inguinal lymphadenopathy","Saphena varix","Hydrocele or encysted hydrocele of the cord","Lipoma of the cord or groin","Undescended or ectopic testis","Femoral artery aneurysm or pseudoaneurysm","Psoas abscess"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination in standing and supine positions with cough impulse assessment","Ultrasound of the groin when the diagnosis is uncertain or the swelling is not clinically obvious","CT or MRI in complex, recurrent, or occult hernias","Herniography in selected occult cases (rarely used now)","Baseline bloods and lactate if strangulation or obstruction is suspected"],"redFlags":["Tense, tender, irreducible groin lump with loss of cough impulse indicating incarceration or strangulation (surgical emergency)","Features of bowel obstruction (colicky pain, distension, vomiting, absolute constipation) with an irreducible hernia","Overlying skin erythema, systemic sepsis, or rising lactate suggesting strangulated ischaemic bowel","Sudden severe pain in a previously reducible hernia"],"prognosis":"Elective repair has a good outcome with low recurrence after tension-free mesh techniques. Emergency presentation with strangulation carries substantially higher morbidity and mortality, particularly if bowel resection is required in an elderly or comorbid patient.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling a femoral hernia as inguinal and underestimating its higher strangulation risk","Attributing a groin lump to a hernia without excluding lymphadenopathy, saphena varix, or vascular causes","Forcible reduction of a strangulated hernia, which can reduce non-viable bowel into the abdomen (reduction en masse)","Failing to identify and address contributing causes of raised intra-abdominal pressure","Delaying surgery in an irreducible, tender hernia and missing evolving strangulation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"inherited_defects_of_membrane_transport":{"name":"Inherited Defects of Membrane Transport","system":"Renal/Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Amino acid transport defects","Cystinuria","Lysinuric protein intolerance","Citrullinemia type 2","Citrin deficiency","Hartnup disease","Cystinosis","SLC transporter disorders"],"chapter":432,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3373","clinicalPearls":["These disorders arise from defects in solute-carrier (SLC) family transporters; tissue distribution of the affected transporter and presence or absence of redundant transporters determine which organs (notably brain and sensory organs) are involved.","Cystinuria is an autosomal recessive defect of apical brush-border transport in the proximal renal tubule and small intestine, impairing reabsorption of the dibasic amino acids cystine, lysine, arginine, and ornithine; only poorly soluble cystine causes clinical disease through stone formation.","Cystine stones account for roughly 1-2% of all urinary calculi and about 4-5% of stones in children; suspect cystinuria when typical hexagonal crystals appear in acidified, concentrated, chilled urine or with a positive nitroprusside test.","Two cystinuria variants exist: type A (SLC3A1, a membrane glycoprotein) and type B (SLC7A9, the b0,+ transporter); the SLC3A1 glycoprotein aids correct processing of the b0,+ transporter, explaining why two different genes cause similar disease.","Lysinuric protein intolerance is a basolateral (not luminal) transport defect (SLC7A7, y+LAT transporter) affecting lysine, arginine, and ornithine but sparing cystine, with secondary impairment of the urea cycle and characteristic episodic hyperammonemia after protein loads or infection.","Citrullinemia type 2 (citrin deficiency, SLC25A13) reduces cytoplasmic aspartate available for the urea cycle and impairs the malate-aspartate NADH shuttle; affected individuals often spontaneously prefer protein/fat-rich foods and avoid carbohydrates, since carbohydrate-driven cytoplasmic NADH worsens the defect.","Hartnup disease is an autosomal recessive defect of the neutral/aromatic amino acid transporter B0AT1 (SLC6A19), which requires collectrin in kidney and ACE2 in intestine for surface expression; symptoms reflect tryptophan and consequent niacin deficiency.","In Hartnup disease only a minority of carriers of the transport defect become symptomatic, indicating that clinical expression depends on additional environmental and nutritional factors.","Cystinosis (CTNS gene; lysosomal cystine/proton transporter cystinosin) causes intralysosomal cystine accumulation and crystal formation; cystine crystals in the cornea on slit-lamp examination are an important diagnostic clue.","Cystinosis has three forms graded by residual transporter function: classic nephropathic (renal Fanconi syndrome with rickets in infancy, progressing to renal failure), juvenile nephropathic (proteinuria leading slowly to kidney failure), and ocular nonnephropathic (photophobia from corneal crystals as the only manifestation).","Citrin deficiency can present in infancy as neonatal intrahepatic cholestasis with failure to thrive and dyslipidemia, or in adulthood (typically ages 20-50) with recurrent hyperammonemic neuropsychiatric episodes resembling hepatic encephalopathy.","Most inherited amino acid transport defects are autosomal recessive; exceptions include certain neuronal/synaptic-vesicle glutamate transporter disorders (e.g., SLC1A2, SLC1A3, SLC17A8) that are autosomal dominant."],"pathophysiology":"Membrane transporters of the solute-carrier (SLC) superfamily move amino acids, peptides, sugars, ions, vitamins, water, and other molecules across plasma membranes and organelle membranes. Inherited loss of a transporter produces disease whose organ pattern depends on the transporter's tissue distribution and whether redundant transporters exist. In cystinuria, defective apical proximal-tubule and intestinal transport of dibasic amino acids leads to massive cystine excretion; because cystine is poorly soluble, it precipitates and forms stones. In lysinuric protein intolerance, a basolateral transport defect traps arginine inside cells and secondarily impairs the urea cycle, with proposed nitric-oxide-mediated immune dysfunction. In citrin deficiency, the mitochondrial aspartate-glutamate carrier defect limits cytoplasmic aspartate for argininosuccinate synthesis and impairs the malate-aspartate NADH shuttle, so a cytoplasmic NADH excess (worsened by carbohydrate) drives metabolic decompensation and hyperammonemia. In Hartnup disease, loss of the neutral amino acid transporter B0AT1 causes renal and intestinal malabsorption of tryptophan, producing functional niacin deficiency and pellagra-like features. In cystinosis, a defective lysosomal cystine/proton transporter causes intralysosomal cystine accumulation and crystal deposition throughout tissues, with the kidney (Fanconi syndrome, progressive renal failure) and cornea most prominently affected.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of urolithiasis (calcium, uric acid, struvite stones) versus cystine stones","Classic urea cycle defects versus the hyperammonemia of lysinuric protein intolerance and citrin deficiency","Dietary niacin deficiency versus Hartnup disease as a cause of pellagra","Nonalcoholic steatohepatitis versus the fatty liver seen in citrullinemia type 2","Other inherited causes of renal Fanconi syndrome versus nephropathic cystinosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Dietary niacin deficiency producing pellagra (mimics Hartnup disease but lacks neutral aminoaciduria)","Nonalcoholic steatohepatitis on liver histology (mimics citrin deficiency)","Other (non-cystine) urinary tract calculi"],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative urinary amino acid analysis (selective overexcretion of cystine, lysine, arginine, ornithine in cystinuria; distinguishes heterozygotes from homozygotes)","Urinary nitroprusside test and microscopy for hexagonal cystine crystals in acidified, concentrated, chilled urine","Plasma and urine amino acids (low plasma lysine/arginine/ornithine with increased urinary excretion and orotic acid in lysinuric protein intolerance)","Genetic sequencing: SLC3A1/SLC7A9 (cystinuria), SLC7A7 (lysinuric protein intolerance), SLC25A13 (citrin deficiency, on hyperammonemia panels), SLC6A19 or CTLRN/collectrin (Hartnup disease), CTNS including deletion analysis (cystinosis)","During acute citrin deficiency attacks: ammonia, citrulline, arginine, glutamine, and red-cell galactose-1-phosphate","Slit-lamp corneal examination and white-blood-cell cystine content for cystinosis"],"redFlags":["Episodic hyperammonemia with altered mental status, seizures, or coma in lysinuric protein intolerance or citrin deficiency (risk of cerebral edema and death)","Recurrent urolithiasis progressing to obstructive uropathy and renal insufficiency in cystinuria","Infantile renal Fanconi syndrome with rickets in nephropathic cystinosis (progression to renal failure by about age 10 without treatment)","Pellagra-like rash with neurologic relapse in Hartnup disease provoked by fever, sunlight, stress, or sulfonamides","Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis, severe osteoporosis, or pancreatitis as adult presentations of lysinuric protein intolerance"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is disorder-specific. Cystinuria can cause recurrent stones and occasional progression to renal failure requiring transplantation. Citrin deficiency, untreated, leads to death from cerebral edema within a few years in most symptomatic patients, but liver transplantation halts progression and normalizes biochemistry. In nephropathic cystinosis, life span was under 10 years before cystine-depleting therapy and transplantation; with current management affected individuals can survive into their late forties with satisfactory quality of life, though many still progress to end-stage renal disease and develop late multisystem complications.","pitfalls":["Attributing pellagra solely to dietary niacin deficiency and missing Hartnup disease; neutral aminoaciduria distinguishes the two and does not occur in dietary deficiency.","Expecting elevated glutamine as in classic urea cycle defects in citrin deficiency, where glutamine is often low or normal during attacks.","Failing to recognize that cystinuria heterozygotes (especially type B) can have increased cystine excretion and may form stones, so quantitative measurement is needed.","Overlooking that only a minority of individuals with the Hartnup transport defect become symptomatic, so genotype does not equal disease.","Missing the carbohydrate-driven decompensation in citrin deficiency, where high sugar intake (and triggers such as acetaminophen, surgery, or alcohol) precipitates attacks."],"severityClassification":"Cystinosis is graded by residual transporter function into classic nephropathic (most severe, infantile Fanconi syndrome and early renal failure), juvenile nephropathic (proteinuria with slower decline to renal failure), and ocular nonnephropathic (photophobia from corneal crystals only). Cystinuria homozygotes excrete 2400-7200 micromol (600-1800 mg) cystine daily versus the maximum urinary solubility of roughly 1200 micromol/L (300 mg/L) at physiologic pH 4.5-7.0.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3373","Harrison 22e p.3374","Harrison 22e p.3375"],"crossLinks":[]},"inherited_disorders_of_amino_acid":{"name":"Inherited Disorders of Amino Acid Metabolism in Adults","system":"Metabolic/Endocrine (inborn errors of metabolism)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Aminoacidopathies","Organic acidemias","Phenylketonuria (PKU)","Homocystinuria","Alkaptonuria","Urea cycle defects","Maple syrup urine disease"],"chapter":431,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3367","clinicalPearls":["Disorders of amino acid metabolism are individually rare (roughly 1 in 10,000 to 1 in 200,000) but collectively affect around 1 in 4000 newborns, and nearly all are inherited as autosomal recessive traits.","Conditions are generally named for the compound that accumulates to highest concentration: aminoacidopathies show excess of the parent amino acid, whereas organic acidemias accumulate downstream catabolic products.","Some defects (e.g., sarcosinemia, cystathioninuria, hyperlysinemia) are clinically benign, while at the opposite extreme complete ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency is lethal in the untreated neonate.","Classic phenylketonuria results from deficient phenylalanine hydroxylase activity; untreated infants are normal at birth but develop microcephaly, progressive intellectual disability, seizures, hypopigmentation, eczema, and a characteristic mousy odor from phenylacetate.","Classic homocystinuria (cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency) typically presents at 3-5 years with dislocated optic lenses, intellectual disability in about half, marfanoid habitus, and osteoporosis, with total plasma homocysteine usually above 100 micromol/L.","Alkaptonuria (homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase deficiency) may be unrecognized until midlife; urine darkens on standing or with alkali, and ochronotic pigment deposits cause degenerative arthritis, scleral and ear pigmentation, and later aortic stenosis.","Ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency is X-linked and the most common urea cycle defect; heterozygous females can become symptomatic when mutant hepatocytes predominate due to random X-inactivation.","Plasma citrulline helps localize urea cycle defects: it is low or undetectable in proximal defects (NAGS, CPS-1, OTC) and markedly elevated in citrullinemia type 1 (argininosuccinate synthase deficiency).","Urine orotic acid is increased only in ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency among the proximal urea cycle defects, distinguishing it from CPS-1 and N-acetylglutamate synthase deficiency.","Diagnosis relies on plasma amino acid analysis, urine organic acid analysis (GC/MS), and plasma acylcarnitine profile (tandem MS), confirmed by enzyme assay or DNA testing.","Some metabolic disorders remain asymptomatic until adulthood, declaring themselves only when fasting or severe stress demands full activity of the affected pathway.","In maternal phenylketonuria, uncontrolled maternal phenylalanine levels before and during pregnancy increase fetal risk of congenital defects, microcephaly, intellectual disability, and growth retardation regardless of fetal genotype."],"pathophysiology":"Each amino acid has a unique degradative pathway that recycles its nitrogen and carbon for synthesis of other amino acids, carbohydrates, and lipids. An enzymatic block causes accumulation of compounds upstream of the lesion; what accumulates depends on the site of the block, reversibility of proximal reactions, and availability of alternative metabolic runoff pathways. In phenylketonuria, accumulated phenylalanine competitively inhibits transport of other amino acids needed for protein and neurotransmitter synthesis, reduces myelin synthesis while increasing its degradation, impairs norepinephrine and serotonin formation, and inhibits tyrosinase causing hypopigmentation. In homocystinuria, defective cystathionine beta-synthase or impaired remethylation of homocysteine to methionine raises homocysteine, which produces endothelial dysfunction and acts as an atherogenic and thrombophilic agent. In urea cycle defects, ammonia generated from protein nitrogen cannot be cleared, so ammonia and glutamine accumulate causing direct neuronal toxicity and brain edema. In alkaptonuria, deficient homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase leads to urinary excretion of homogentisic acid and deposition of its oxidized pigment in connective tissue (ochronosis).","additionalDifferentials":["Phenylketonuria versus the cofactor (tetrahydrobiopterin-related) hyperphenylalaninemias such as DHPR, PTPS, and GTP cyclohydrolase 1 deficiencies","Classic cystathionine beta-synthase homocystinuria versus remethylation defects (methionine synthase / cblE, cblG) and combined methylmalonic acidemia-homocystinuria (cblC, D, F, J)","Urea cycle defects distinguished among each other by plasma citrulline, arginine, and urine orotic acid patterns","Ochronotic arthritis of alkaptonuria versus rheumatoid arthritis (small joints typically spared in ochronosis)"],"infectionMimics":["Nonspecific hepatitis can be misdiagnosed in urea cycle defects presenting with hepatic dysfunction"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Gastrointestinal disorders, food allergies, and behavioral problems are common misdiagnoses in milder urea cycle defects with protein aversion and recurrent vomiting","Acquired hyperammonemia from liver disease of any cause, organic acidemias, or fatty acid oxidation defects can mimic urea cycle defects","Marfan syndrome can mimic the marfanoid habitus and lens dislocation of classic homocystinuria"],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative plasma amino acid analysis (ion-exchange or LC/tandem MS)","Urine organic acid analysis by GC/MS","Plasma acylcarnitine profile by tandem mass spectrometry","Plasma ammonia and plasma glutamine measurement in suspected urea cycle defects","Urine orotic acid to differentiate OTC from CPS-1 and NAGS deficiency","Total plasma homocysteine and methionine for the homocystinurias","Urine organic acid analysis to identify homogentisic acid in alkaptonuria","Enzyme assay on cells/tissues or confirmatory DNA testing","Newborn screening (blood phenylalanine, methionine) with confirmatory metabolic testing for positive screens"],"redFlags":["Neonate with refusal to feed and lethargy progressing to coma (classic urea cycle defect presentation at 1-4 days of life)","Hyperammonemia precipitated by systemic corticosteroids or chemotherapy, which can be fatal in previously asymptomatic individuals of any age","Postpartum behavioral change, stupor, or coma in a female (possible decompensated OTC deficiency from fasting and stress)","Life-threatening coronary, renal, or cerebral arterial thrombosis in a young patient with homocystinuria","Aortic stenosis developing in alkaptonuria, especially after age 60, from pigment deposition in heart valves"],"prognosis":"Clinical manifestations of many of these conditions can be prevented or mitigated when diagnosis is early and appropriate management (e.g., dietary protein/amino acid restriction or vitamin supplementation) is started promptly; for phenylketonuria, treatment must begin before 2 weeks of age to prevent intellectual disability, and children detected and treated at birth show none of the classic abnormalities. Untreated classic disorders carry severe outcomes, including lethality of complete neonatal OTC deficiency and early vascular morbidity/mortality in homocystinuria. Alkaptonuria progresses slowly to disabling ochronotic arthritis and later cardiac valve disease.","pitfalls":["Milder variants of homocystinuria may be missed by newborn methionine screening.","Heterozygous females with X-linked OTC deficiency can be overlooked because symptoms depend on the proportion of mutant hepatocytes from random X-inactivation.","Adult-onset metabolic decompensation can be triggered by involuntary fasting, severe stress, corticosteroids, or chemotherapy and may be mistaken for primary GI, allergic, behavioral, or hepatic disease.","Hyperhomocysteinemia has many acquired causes (B6/folate/B12 deficiency, aging, smoking, renal failure, hypothyroidism, leukemias, and certain drugs such as methotrexate, nitrous oxide, and isoniazid) that must be distinguished from inherited homocystinuria.","An abnormal newborn screen in an infant may actually reflect a maternal metabolic disorder (e.g., glutaric acidemia type 1, methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase deficiency, primary carnitine deficiency, fatty acid oxidation defect)."],"severityClassification":"Severity is largely biochemical and enzyme-activity dependent: complete enzyme deficiencies (e.g., classic urea cycle defects) present catastrophically in the neonatal period, whereas partial/residual-activity variants may remain asymptomatic until adulthood and declare during fasting or stress. For phenylketonuria, dietary restriction is generally instituted when blood phenylalanine exceeds 360 micromol/L, with a target maintenance range of 120-360 micromol/L for life.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3367","Harrison 22e p.3368","Harrison 22e p.3369","Harrison 22e p.3370","Harrison 22e p.3371","Harrison 22e p.3372"],"crossLinks":[]},"insomnia_disorder":{"name":"Insomnia disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chronic insomnia","primary insomnia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The disorder is dissatisfaction with sleep quantity or quality, with difficulty initiating sleep, maintaining sleep, or early morning waking, causing significant daytime distress or impairment","For a chronic diagnosis the difficulty is present several nights per week over an extended period, typically at least three months, despite adequate opportunity to sleep","Daytime consequences such as fatigue, poor concentration, irritability, and impaired performance are integral to the diagnosis, not merely the nocturnal complaint","A thorough evaluation should exclude other sleep disorders such as obstructive sleep apnoea and restless legs, and identify medical, psychiatric, and substance-related contributors","Cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia is the recommended first-line treatment and is more durable than hypnotic medication","Sleep hygiene alone is usually insufficient, but stimulus control, sleep restriction, and cognitive techniques are the effective components of behavioural treatment","Hypnotic medications may help in the short term but carry risks of tolerance, dependence, daytime sedation, and rebound insomnia, so long-term use is discouraged","Insomnia is bidirectionally linked with depression and anxiety, and treating it can improve mood and reduce relapse risk"],"pathophysiology":"Insomnia disorder is conceptualised through a model of hyperarousal, in which heightened cognitive, emotional, and physiological arousal impairs the normal sleep process. Predisposing traits, precipitating stressors, and perpetuating behaviours and beliefs (such as excessive time in bed and worry about sleep) interact, with dysregulation of arousal and sleep-wake regulatory systems maintaining the condition over time.","additionalDifferentials":["Obstructive sleep apnoea","Restless legs syndrome and periodic limb movement disorder","Circadian rhythm sleep-wake disorders","Depressive and anxiety disorders with sleep disturbance","Substance or medication effects, including caffeine, alcohol, and stimulants","Medical conditions causing nocturnal symptoms such as pain or nocturia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed sleep history and sleep diary over one to two weeks","Screening questionnaires for sleep apnoea and other sleep disorders","Assessment for comorbid depression, anxiety, and substance use","Review of medications and stimulant intake","Polysomnography reserved for suspected apnoea, periodic limb movements, or atypical features"],"redFlags":["Excessive daytime sleepiness with witnessed apnoeas suggesting untreated sleep apnoea","Suicidal ideation associated with comorbid depression","Escalating hypnotic or alcohol use to induce sleep","Severe sleep deprivation impairing safety, such as while driving"],"prognosis":"Insomnia disorder often becomes chronic and relapsing, particularly when perpetuating behaviours and beliefs are established, but cognitive behavioural therapy produces durable improvement in most patients. Prognosis is better with early behavioural treatment and management of comorbidities, and worse with long-term hypnotic reliance and untreated psychiatric or medical contributors.","pitfalls":["Reaching for hypnotics as first-line rather than offering cognitive behavioural therapy","Relying on sleep hygiene advice alone, which is rarely sufficient","Missing obstructive sleep apnoea or restless legs as the true cause","Overlooking caffeine, alcohol, and stimulant contributions","Failing to recognise and treat comorbid depression or anxiety"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"intestinal_nematode_infections":{"name":"Intestinal Nematode (Roundworm) Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["soil-transmitted helminths","Ascaris","hookworm","Strongyloides"],"chapter":239,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1814","clinicalPearls":["More than one billion people worldwide are infected with one or more intestinal nematode species; they are most common where sanitation and fecal-waste disposal are inadequate, especially resource-limited tropical and subtropical regions, but are seen with increasing frequency among immigrants and refugees to developed countries (p.1814).","The major intestinal roundworms are Ascaris lumbricoides, the hookworms (Necator americanus, Ancylostoma duodenale, A. ceylanicum), Strongyloides stercoralis, Trichuris trichiura (whipworm), and Enterobius vermicularis (pinworm) (p.1815).","Ascaris, Trichuris, and the hookworms require a soil phase for development, whereas S. stercoralis and E. vermicularis can be transmitted directly from person to person (p.1814).","Because most helminths cannot fully complete their life cycle within the same human host, heavy adult-worm burdens require repeated exposure; clinical disease (versus asymptomatic/subclinical infection) generally develops only with repeated exposure and is typically related to infection intensity (p.1814).","Eosinophilia and elevated serum IgE are features of many helminth infections, the latter particularly when the life cycle involves tissue migration (Ascaris, Strongyloides, hookworms) (p.1814).","Ascaris larval migration through the lungs (~9-12 days after egg ingestion) can cause an irritating nonproductive cough, substernal discomfort, eosinophilia, and transient rounded pulmonary infiltrates - eosinophilic pneumonitis (Loffler's syndrome) (p.1815).","Heavy Ascaris infection can cause small-bowel obstruction by a bolus of entangled worms (more common in children with narrow lumens), sometimes complicated by perforation, intussusception, or volvulus; an adult worm migrating into the biliary tree can cause biliary colic, cholecystitis, cholangitis, pancreatitis, or rarely intrahepatic abscess (p.1815).","In highly endemic areas, intestinal and biliary ascariasis can rival acute appendicitis as a cause of surgical acute abdomen (p.1815).","The major consequence of chronic hookworm infection is iron deficiency; symptoms are minimal if iron stores are adequate, but undernourished individuals develop progressive iron-deficiency anemia and hypoproteinemia (fatigue, weakness, dyspnea). Hookworm disease requires the combination of heavy worm burden, prolonged duration, and inadequate iron intake (p.1816).","Hookworm larvae may cause a pruritic maculopapular rash (ground itch) at the skin penetration site; hypochromic microcytic anemia, sometimes with eosinophilia or hypoalbuminemia, is characteristic of hookworm disease (p.1816).","S. stercoralis is distinguished by its ability - unique among helminths except Capillaria - to complete its life cycle and replicate within a human host (autoinfection), so infection can persist for decades without further exogenous exposure (p.1816).","Larva currens, a rapidly advancing (up to ~10 cm/h) pruritic serpiginous skin eruption, is pathognomonic of strongyloidiasis; recurrent urticaria, often on the buttocks, is the most common cutaneous manifestation (p.1816).","Impaired cell-mediated immunity - especially high-dose glucocorticoid therapy, and less commonly other immunosuppressants or HTLV-1 infection - can trigger Strongyloides hyperinfection with massive larval numbers, leading to colitis/enteritis/malabsorption and disseminated disease (CNS, skin, peritoneum, liver, kidneys) (p.1817).","Disseminated strongyloidiasis is often complicated by gram-negative bacteremia, pneumonia, or meningitis from enteric flora translocating through disrupted mucosal barriers; eosinophilia is often ABSENT in severely infected patients (p.1817).","Even asymptomatic strongyloidiasis must be treated because of the potential for later hyperinfection and fatal dissemination; Strongyloides serology should be performed before starting glucocorticoids or solid-organ transplantation in patients with prior potential exposure, especially with eosinophilia (p.1817).","Most Trichuris (whipworm) infections are asymptomatic, but heavy infections may cause anemia and bloody/mucoid diarrhea resembling inflammatory bowel disease; massive infection in children can cause rectal prolapse from protracted tenesmus (p.1818).","Enterobius (pinworm) is more common in temperate climates than the tropics; perianal pruritus, worse at night when gravid females emerge, is the cardinal symptom, and eosinophilia is uncommon (p.1818)."],"pathophysiology":"Intestinal nematodes are nonsegmented roundworms ranging from ~1 mm to many centimeters. Ascaris and the hookworms have life cycles involving migration through the heart and lungs before maturing in the intestine (larvae penetrate alveoli, ascend the bronchial tree, are swallowed, and reach the small bowel). Ascaris adults live in the small-intestinal lumen and females are extraordinarily fecund (>200,000 eggs/day); eggs are highly environmentally resistant and become infective after maturation in warm, moist soil. Hookworm adults attach to the small-bowel mucosa using buccal teeth (Ancylostoma) or cutting plates (Necator), invade submucosa, and ingest blood and villous tissue, producing iron-deficiency anemia; filariform larvae penetrate skin. S. stercoralis filariform larvae penetrate skin or mucous membranes, migrate via blood to the lungs and then the small intestine, where parthenogenetic adult females embed in the mucosa; rhabditiform larvae can transform directly into filariform larvae that re-penetrate the colonic wall or perianal skin to drive ongoing autoinfection - which can amplify into hyperinfection and dissemination under immunosuppression. Trichuris adults embed their thin anterior portions in colonic/cecal mucosa with no pulmonary larval passage. Enterobius adults dwell in the cecum; gravid females migrate nocturnally to the perianal skin to deposit eggs, which become infective within hours and spread by hand-to-mouth (autoinfection from scratching) and retroinfection. (p.1814-1818)","additionalDifferentials":["Strongyloides midepigastric pain resembles peptic ulcer disease, except it is aggravated by food ingestion (p.1816)","Heavy trichuriasis with bloody/mucoid diarrhea may resemble inflammatory bowel disease (p.1818)","Intestinal/biliary ascariasis may present as a surgical acute abdomen mimicking acute appendicitis in endemic areas (p.1815)"],"infectionMimics":["Strongyloides hyperinfection-associated gram-negative sepsis, pneumonia, or meningitis can dominate or be mistaken for primary bacterial infection (p.1817)","Hookworm rhabditiform larvae in non-fresh stool must be differentiated from Strongyloides larvae (p.1816)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inflammatory bowel disease (heavy trichuriasis) (p.1818)","Peptic ulcer disease (strongyloidiasis epigastric pain) (p.1816)","Acute appendicitis / surgical acute abdomen (intestinal or biliary ascariasis) (p.1815)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool microscopy for characteristic ova and parasites: Ascaris eggs (~65 x 45 um), hookworm eggs (~40 x 60 um, species indistinguishable by light microscopy), Trichuris lemon-shaped eggs (~50 x 20 um) (p.1815-1818)","Strongyloides: rhabditiform larvae (~250 um, short buccal cavity) on stool microscopy; single stool detects only ~one-third of uncomplicated cases, so serial examinations and agar plate method improve sensitivity; duodenojejunal aspirate/biopsy; ELISA serum antibody serology (sensitive for uncomplicated infection); in dissemination, filariform larvae in stool, sputum, bronchoalveolar lavage, or surgical drainage fluid (p.1817)","Enterobius: eggs are NOT found in stool - diagnose with morning cellulose acetate tape applied to the perianal region (eggs oval, ~55 x 25 um, flattened on one side) (p.1818)","Ascaris lung phase: larvae detectable in sputum or gastric aspirates before eggs appear in stool; chest imaging showing transient rounded infiltrates (Loffler's syndrome) (p.1815)","PCR of stool worm DNA (increasing use, mostly research/some clinical) for species-level diagnosis (p.1815-1818)","Abdominal imaging: plain film for masses of worms in obstruction; ultrasound and ERCP for pancreaticobiliary Ascaris (ERCP can also extract worms) (p.1815)","Hookworm disease bloodwork: hypochromic microcytic anemia, sometimes eosinophilia or hypoalbuminemia (p.1816)"],"redFlags":["Disseminated strongyloidiasis - particularly in patients with unsuspected infection who are taking glucocorticoids - has a HIGH mortality rate (p.1817)","Strongyloides hyperinfection with gram-negative sepsis, pneumonia, or meningitis from enteric flora translocation (p.1817)","Eosinophilia is often ABSENT in severe/disseminated strongyloidiasis, removing a reassuring marker (p.1817)","Complete intestinal obstruction with perforation, intussusception, or volvulus from a heavy Ascaris worm bolus (p.1815)","Biliary Ascaris causing cholangitis, pancreatitis, or intrahepatic abscess (p.1815)"],"prognosis":"Intestinal nematode infections are not usually fatal but contribute to malnutrition, impaired physical and cognitive development, and diminished work capacity, particularly in children with marginal nutritional status. Significant protective immunity does not appear to develop in humans. The major exception is strongyloidiasis: autoinfection allows decades-long persistence, and in immunocompromised hosts hyperinfection and dissemination can be fatal (high mortality, especially with unrecognized infection on glucocorticoids). (p.1814, p.1816-1817)","pitfalls":["Failing to screen for and treat asymptomatic strongyloidiasis before glucocorticoids or solid-organ transplantation - asymptomatic infection must still be treated because of the risk of later hyperinfection and fatal dissemination (p.1817)","Relying on eosinophilia to exclude strongyloidiasis - it is often absent in severe/disseminated disease (p.1817)","Using routine stool ova-and-parasite microscopy to diagnose pinworm - Enterobius eggs are not passed in feces; perianal cellulose acetate tape is required (p.1818)","Single stool examination for Strongyloides detects only about one-third of uncomplicated cases (p.1817)","Misidentifying hookworm rhabditiform larvae (in non-fresh stool) as Strongyloides (p.1816)"],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal severity classification for intestinal nematode infections; severity correlates with infection intensity (worm burden) and host factors. Disease ranges from asymptomatic/subclinical light infection to clinical disease with heavy burden (e.g., Ascaris bowel/biliary obstruction; hookworm iron-deficiency anemia/hypoproteinemia; Trichuris dysentery and rectal prolapse). For Strongyloides, the spectrum is uncomplicated strongyloidiasis vs. hyperinfection (amplified autoinfection, larvae confined largely to the normal migration route) vs. disseminated strongyloidiasis (larvae in CNS, skin, peritoneum, liver, kidneys). (p.1814-1817)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1814"],"crossLinks":["iron_deficiency_anemia","bowel_obstruction"]},"intraabdominal_infections_and_abscesses":{"name":"Intraabdominal Infections and Abscesses","system":"Gastrointestinal/Abdominal","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Peritonitis","Primary (spontaneous) bacterial peritonitis","SBP","Secondary peritonitis","Intraperitoneal abscess","Pyogenic liver abscess","Splenic abscess","Perinephric abscess","Renal abscess","Psoas abscess"],"chapter":137,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1069-1076","clinicalPearls":["Intraperitoneal infection follows disruption of a normal anatomic barrier (ruptured appendix, diverticulum, or ulcer; ischemic, tumor-related, or inflammatory bowel wall weakening; or leakage from adjacent pancreatitis or pelvic inflammatory disease), and evolves in two sequential stages: peritonitis first, then abscess formation if the patient survives untreated.","Primary (spontaneous) bacterial peritonitis (PBP) in adults occurs chiefly with cirrhotic ascites, develops in up to about 10% of cirrhotic patients, and is thought to arise from hematogenous spread when impaired hepatic filtration and altered portal flow allow organisms to seed and multiply in ascitic fluid.","A peritoneal-fluid count of more than 250 polymorphonuclear leukocytes/uL is diagnostic of PBP; this threshold does not apply to secondary peritonitis. Normal peritoneal fluid contains under 300 WBC/uL (mainly mononuclear) and protein under 30 g/L.","PBP is typically caused by a single organism (often enteric gram-negative bacilli such as E. coli, but increasingly gram-positive cocci and ESBL-producing Enterobacteriaceae); recovery of multiple organisms including anaerobes should prompt reconsideration of the diagnosis and a search for a secondary source.","Fever is the most common manifestation of PBP (up to 80%), but abdominal pain and peritoneal signs may be absent; nonlocalizing features such as malaise, encephalopathy, jaundice, or unexplained acute kidney injury in a cirrhotic with ascites should also trigger diagnostic paracentesis.","Yield of peritoneal-fluid culture is improved by inoculating about 10 mL directly into a blood-culture bottle, and simultaneous blood cultures should be drawn because bacteremia frequently accompanies PBP; samples are best obtained before antibiotics.","Secondary peritonitis results from spillage from an intraabdominal viscus and yields a mixed flora dominated by facultative gram-negative bacilli and anaerobes, especially with a colonic source; early death is driven by gram-negative bacillary sepsis and circulating endotoxin.","The bacterial burden of a perforation depends on site: a ruptured gastric ulcer (if not achlorhydric) causes mainly low-pH chemical irritation with a low bacterial load, whereas leaked colonic contents (pH 7-8, ~10^11 anaerobes/g feces) cause intense infection from heavy bacterial load.","Bacteroides fragilis, though only about 0.5% of normal colonic flora, is the anaerobe most frequently isolated from intraabdominal infections and abscesses and the most common anaerobic bloodstream isolate; its zwitterionic capsular polysaccharide (polysaccharide A) drives abscess localization via a CD4+ T-cell-dependent host response.","Of all intraabdominal abscesses, about 74% are intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal (non-visceral); most intraperitoneal abscesses arise from fecal spillage (e.g., an inflamed appendix), and diverticular abscesses are the least likely to rupture.","The liver is the organ most subject to abscess; biliary tract disease is now the most common source (formerly ruptured appendicitis), and pylephlebitis (suppurative portal vein thrombosis) is another route of seeding. Half of liver-abscess patients lack hepatomegaly, right-upper-quadrant tenderness, or jaundice, so liver abscess can present as fever of unknown origin, especially in the elderly.","Hypermucoviscous (hypervirulent) Klebsiella pneumoniae causes a distinctive community-acquired syndrome of liver abscess with bacteremia and metastatic infection of the eye(s) and lung(s), originally described in Southeast Asia and now emerging in North America and elsewhere.","Splenic abscess is rare but frequently fatal if untreated and often diagnosed late (at autopsy in about 37% of one series); hematogenous spread predominates, with bacterial endocarditis the most common associated infection and streptococci then S. aureus the usual isolates.","More than 75% of perinephric and renal abscesses now arise from ascending urinary tract infection rather than hematogenous seeding; concomitant obstructing nephrolithiasis is the most important risk factor (renal stones in 20-60% of perinephric abscess cases)."],"pathophysiology":"Intraperitoneal infection begins when a normal anatomic barrier is breached and organisms normally contained within the bowel or another organ enter the sterile peritoneal cavity. Inflammation triggers an early influx of polymorphonuclear leukocytes followed by a prolonged phase of mononuclear cell migration, with the leukocyte phenotype shaped by resident-cell chemokine synthesis. In primary (spontaneous) bacterial peritonitis, a diseased liver with altered portal circulation impairs the normal filtration function, allowing hematogenous organisms to seed ascites; cirrhosis-related defects (low peritoneal complement, diminished PMN opsonic and phagocytic capacity, gut dysbiosis with overgrowth of Enterobacteriaceae, reduced Paneth cell defensins, decreased motility, and portal-hypertensive enteropathy) promote bacterial translocation. Secondary peritonitis follows direct viscus spillage; an early containment phase produces a fibrin- and PMN-rich exudate, and severity reflects whether spillage is chemically irritant (acidic gastric contents) or heavily bacterial (colonic contents). If overt gram-negative sepsis does not prove fatal, the host walls off persisting organisms and PMNs in a fibrous capsule to form an abscess; B. fragilis is uniquely able to induce abscesses, with its zwitterionic capsular polysaccharide A stimulating peritoneal macrophages (TNF-alpha, ICAM-1) and CD4+ T lymphocytes that secrete leukoattractant cytokines, while the alternative complement pathway and fibrinogen also participate.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary versus secondary peritonitis (single organism without source versus mixed flora with an identifiable viscus source)","Pancreatitis versus its infectious complications (infected pseudocyst, pancreatic abscess, infected pancreatic necrosis) - fever, leukocytosis, and abdominal pain alone do not distinguish them","Diverticular abscess versus simple diverticulitis","Tuboovarian abscess and other female genital tract infection extending into the pelvis","Perinephric/renal abscess versus uncomplicated pyelonephritis when fever persists beyond 4-5 days of therapy","Hemoperitoneum (especially in trauma) mimicking infective peritonitis"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculous peritonitis (cross-reference Chap. 183)","Amebic liver abscess (cross-reference Chap. 230) - positive amebic serology in over 95% of cases","Candidal liver or renal abscess following fungemia, often unmasked when PMNs recover after neutropenia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute pancreatitis without infected necrosis (sterile inflammation producing fever and leukocytosis)","Chemical peritonitis from a ruptured gastric ulcer in the absence of significant bacterial load","Malignant ascites or metastatic intraabdominal malignancy","Congestive heart failure, systemic lupus erythematosus, and lymphedema as noncirrhotic settings reported with PBP"],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnostic paracentesis of any cirrhotic with ascites and fever, with PMN count (>250/uL diagnostic of PBP) and culture of fluid in blood-culture bottles plus simultaneous blood cultures","Contrast-enhanced abdominal CT to exclude or identify a primary intraabdominal source (highest yield for abscess localization)","Chest and abdominal radiography to exclude free air signaling perforation (pneumoperitoneum) and to suggest liver abscess via raised right hemidiaphragm, basilar infiltrate, or pleural effusion","Ultrasonography (particularly useful for right upper quadrant, kidneys, and pelvis) and MRI for abscess characterization","Indium-labeled WBC scan or gallium scan to localize occult collections (indium preferred near bowel); PET scanning for resolution and availability","Serum alkaline phosphatase (elevated in about 70% of liver abscess - the single most reliable laboratory finding), bilirubin (elevated in ~50%), AST (~48%); leukocytosis, normochromic normocytic anemia (~50%), hypoalbuminemia (~33%)","Amebic serology and PCR to exclude amebic liver abscess","In CAPD-associated peritonitis, dialysate cell count with differential (cloudy fluid with >100 WBC/uL, >50% neutrophils, or >50% PMN with shortened dwell time) and concentrated dialysate culture in blood-culture bottles","Needle aspiration under CT guidance for culture in suspected infected pancreatic necrosis"],"redFlags":["Free air under the diaphragm on upright chest film indicating bowel perforation with peritonitis requiring urgent intervention","Hemodynamic instability in suspected secondary peritonitis warranting surgery without delay for imaging","Persistent fever after 4-5 days of treatment for pyelonephritis suggesting perinephric or renal abscess","Polymicrobial growth in PBP-suspected peritoneal fluid signaling missed secondary peritonitis source","Liver abscess presenting only as fever of unknown origin, especially in the elderly","Hypervirulent K. pneumoniae liver abscess with metastatic eye and lung involvement","Splenic abscess with underlying endocarditis or hemoglobinopathy given high untreated fatality"],"prognosis":"PBP carries substantial morbidity and mortality, reflecting its concentration among patients with advanced liver disease; reported in-hospital mortality was about 17.6% in a 2018 US cohort, and another series found 23% mortality at 30 days and 37% at 90 days. PBP recurs frequently, with up to 70% of patients experiencing recurrence within 1 year. Patients with PBP usually respond to appropriate antibiotics within 72 h, and delayed therapy increases mortality. In secondary peritonitis, early death is attributable to gram-negative bacillary sepsis and endotoxemia, and the urgency and success of source control - rather than the choice of empiric antibiotics - drive survival odds. Pyogenic liver abscess mortality averaged about 15% despite treatment. Splenic and perinephric abscesses are frequently fatal when diagnosis is delayed, so early diagnosis is the most important determinant of outcome.","pitfalls":["Failing to perform diagnostic paracentesis in a febrile cirrhotic with ascites, as PBP is often subtle and abdominal pain/peritoneal signs may be absent","Misclassifying secondary peritonitis as PBP - the >250 PMN/uL threshold and single-organism rule apply only to PBP; mixed flora or anaerobes indicate a secondary source","Relying on cultures from chronic drain sites, which are not reliable for defining infection etiology","Attributing fever, leukocytosis, and pain in pancreatitis to the inflammation itself and overlooking an infected pseudocyst, pancreatic abscess, or infected necrosis","Most clinical treatment failures in abscesses result from failure to achieve source control (drainage), not antibiotic selection","Injecting barium when perforation is suspected (contraindicated), even though barium enema may occasionally reveal a diverticular abscess","Missing splenic or perinephric abscess because of nonspecific presentation and frequently delayed diagnosis","Overlooking polymicrobial urine culture, known renal stones, or sterile pyuria as clues to perinephric/renal abscess"],"severityClassification":"No single formal severity score is given; the text frames disease along the peritonitis-to-abscess continuum and stratifies risk by setting. PBP is graded operationally by ascitic-fluid PMN count (>250/uL diagnostic) and by markers of advanced liver disease (serum creatinine, BUN, total bilirubin, Child-Turcotte-Pugh score) that identify highest-risk and worst-prognosis patients. Secondary and abscess-related infection severity is stratified clinically as community-acquired mild-to-moderate disease versus critically ill or health care-associated infection, which guides anticipated flora and management intensity.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1069-1076"],"crossLinks":[]},"intrauterine_growth_restriction":{"name":"Intrauterine Growth Restriction","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IUGR","fetal growth restriction","FGR","small for gestational age with pathology"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Intrauterine growth restriction describes a fetus that fails to achieve its genetically determined growth potential, and it should be distinguished from constitutionally small but healthy fetuses","It is broadly divided into placental (uteroplacental insufficiency), maternal, and fetal causes, with placental insufficiency the commonest mechanism in developed settings","Symmetrical restriction, in which head and body are proportionally small, often reflects early insults such as chromosomal abnormality or congenital infection, whereas asymmetrical restriction with relative head sparing suggests later placental insufficiency","Accurate dating early in pregnancy is essential, as growth restriction cannot be assessed reliably without a correct gestational age","Surveillance combines serial ultrasound biometry, amniotic fluid assessment, and umbilical artery Doppler to detect deterioration","A progression of Doppler abnormalities, from raised umbilical artery resistance to absent or reversed end-diastolic flow and abnormal ductus venosus, reflects worsening fetal compromise","Management balances the risks of prematurity against those of continued exposure to a hostile intrauterine environment, and antenatal corticosteroids are given when early delivery is anticipated","Growth-restricted fetuses have higher risks of stillbirth, intrapartum compromise, and neonatal complications, and later cardiovascular and metabolic disease"],"pathophysiology":"The commonest pathway is uteroplacental insufficiency, in which inadequate trophoblastic remodelling of the spiral arteries produces a poorly perfused placenta with reduced capacity to deliver oxygen and nutrients. The fetus adapts by redistributing cardiac output to preserve the brain, heart, and adrenals at the expense of the abdominal viscera and peripheral tissues, producing asymmetrical growth and oligohydramnios from reduced renal perfusion. Progressive hypoxaemia and acidaemia manifest as deteriorating Doppler indices and, ultimately, cardiovascular decompensation. Other cases stem from fetal chromosomal or structural abnormalities, congenital infection, or maternal disease reducing substrate supply.","additionalDifferentials":["Constitutionally small for gestational age but healthy fetus","Incorrect gestational dating","Chromosomal abnormality or genetic syndrome","Congenital infection such as cytomegalovirus or toxoplasmosis","Multiple pregnancy with unequal placental sharing","Maternal disease such as chronic hypertension, renal disease, or thrombophilia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial ultrasound biometry with estimated fetal weight and growth trajectory","Umbilical artery Doppler, with middle cerebral artery and ductus venosus Doppler as disease advances","Amniotic fluid volume assessment","Detailed anatomy scan to identify structural anomalies","Screening for congenital infection and consideration of karyotyping when appropriate","Maternal assessment for hypertension, pre-eclampsia, and other predisposing conditions"],"redFlags":["Absent or reversed umbilical artery end-diastolic flow indicating severe placental compromise","Abnormal ductus venosus flow or a non-reassuring cardiotocograph signalling impending fetal decompensation","Reduced or absent fetal movements","Static or falling growth on serial scans with oligohydramnios","Coexisting severe pre-eclampsia threatening both mother and fetus"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the underlying cause, the severity and gestation at onset, and the timeliness of surveillance and delivery. Early-onset severe placental disease carries the greatest risk of stillbirth and neonatal morbidity, whereas late-onset restriction is often milder but still increases perinatal risk. Survivors have higher rates of neurodevelopmental impairment and of adult cardiovascular and metabolic disease, underscoring the importance of careful monitoring and appropriately timed birth.","pitfalls":["Confusing a constitutionally small healthy fetus with pathological growth restriction, or vice versa","Relying on a single scan rather than serial measurements to judge growth","Neglecting accurate early dating, which undermines all subsequent assessment","Delivering too early for prematurity risk or too late with ongoing fetal hypoxia","Overlooking congenital infection or aneuploidy in early symmetrical restriction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"intussusception":{"name":"Intussusception","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["intestinal invagination","bowel telescoping","ileocolic intussusception"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common cause of intestinal obstruction in infants and toddlers, typically between 6 months and 3 years, in which one segment of bowel telescopes into the adjacent distal segment.","The classic triad is intermittent colicky abdominal pain (with drawing up of the legs), vomiting, and red currant jelly stool, but all three are present in only a minority.","Between painful episodes the infant may appear well or lethargic; lethargy can be a prominent and misleading feature.","A sausage-shaped mass may be palpable, often in the right upper quadrant, with an empty right lower quadrant.","Most cases are ileocolic and idiopathic, sometimes following a viral illness with hypertrophied Peyer patches acting as a lead point.","Ultrasound is the diagnostic investigation of choice, showing a target or doughnut sign on cross-section.","In a stable child without peritonitis or perforation, non-operative reduction with an air or contrast enema is both diagnostic and therapeutic.","Surgery is required when enema reduction fails, or when there is perforation, peritonitis, shock, or a pathological lead point."],"pathophysiology":"A proximal segment of bowel (the intussusceptum) invaginates into the lumen of the adjacent distal segment (the intussuscipiens), dragging its mesentery with it. This compromises venous and lymphatic drainage, producing bowel wall oedema and engorgement; the mixture of sloughed mucosa, blood, and mucus produces the characteristic red currant jelly stool. Progressive swelling obstructs the lumen and, if unrelieved, arterial compromise leads to ischaemia, necrosis, and perforation. In infants a lead point is usually hypertrophied lymphoid tissue, whereas older children more often have a pathological lead point such as a Meckel diverticulum or polyp.","additionalDifferentials":["Gastroenteritis","Incarcerated hernia","Malrotation with volvulus","Appendicitis","Henoch-Schonlein purpura with bowel involvement","Constipation or other causes of colicky pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Abdominal ultrasound (target/doughnut sign) as first-line imaging","Abdominal radiograph to look for obstruction or free air","Air or contrast enema (diagnostic and therapeutic in stable children)","Full blood count and electrolytes to assess dehydration","Assessment for an underlying lead point in older or atypical cases"],"redFlags":["Signs of peritonitis or a rigid, tender abdomen","Free air on radiograph indicating perforation","Shock, marked lethargy, or haemodynamic instability","Bilious vomiting and abdominal distension indicating established obstruction","Fever with toxic appearance suggesting bowel ischaemia or sepsis"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and reduction the outlook is excellent; delay increases the risk of ischaemia, resection, and complications, and a proportion recur after non-operative reduction.","pitfalls":["Waiting for the full classic triad, which is often incomplete, and delaying diagnosis","Attributing marked lethargy to another cause and missing intussusception","Attempting enema reduction in a child with peritonitis, perforation, or shock","Failing to consider a pathological lead point in older children or recurrent cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"iron_deficiency_anemia":{"name":"Iron Deficiency Anemia","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IDA"],"chapter":102,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.764","clinicalPearls":["Classic blood picture is a microcytic, hypochromic anemia (MCV <80 fL in established disease), but microcytosis can be ABSENT in early iron deficiency or masked by liver disease or concomitant folate/B12 deficiency (Harrison 22e p.765, p.450).","Low serum ferritin is the mainstay of absolute iron deficiency, reflecting depleted stores; ferritin <30 µg/L has high sensitivity and specificity (92% and 98%) and can be used as a single diagnostic test (Harrison 22e p.765).","Transferrin saturation (serum iron/TIBC) measures iron available for erythropoiesis; erythropoiesis becomes iron-restricted at values <15-20% (Harrison 22e p.765).","Anemia develops only in the LATE stage because the erythron is relatively spared compared with heart and skeletal muscle, which become iron-depleted earlier; slow progression plus compensation means anemia is often undetected, especially in the young, until hemoglobin falls below ~8 g/dL (Harrison 22e p.765).","Clinical clues include pica (compulsive ingestion of nonnutritive substances such as ice, soil, or clay), nail fragility and koilonychia, hair loss, cheilosis, atrophic glossitis, and dysphagia from esophageal webs (Plummer-Vinson syndrome) (Harrison 22e p.765; Koilonychia association noted p.~).","Even symptomatic isolated iron deficiency WITHOUT anemia matters: suboptimal myocyte, cardiomyocyte, and neuron function can cause fatigue, irritability, poor concentration, restless legs syndrome, and reduced exercise capacity (Harrison 22e p.765).","Severe iron-deficiency anemia may be accompanied by thrombocytosis, due to prevalent megakaryocyte commitment of the common erythroid-platelet progenitor when iron is insufficient (Harrison 22e p.766).","Serum hepcidin is low/undetectable in absolute iron deficiency (maximizing dietary iron absorption) but elevated in inflammation and in IRIDA (TMPRSS6 mutations) (Harrison 22e p.766).","Inadequate therapeutic response (no reticulocytosis after 1 week, or <1 g/dL hemoglobin rise after 2 weeks of iron therapy) should prompt a search for the cause: noncompliance, ongoing blood loss, or malabsorption (Harrison 22e p.766)."],"pathophysiology":"Iron supply becomes inadequate for needs through excessive demand, insufficient intake, defective absorption, or blood loss; multiple causes frequently co-occur, especially in the elderly and in multimorbidity (Harrison 22e p.764). In ISOLATED iron deficiency, stores are depleted while hemoglobin is still normal; when iron remains sufficient for erythropoiesis, red cells are normochromic/normocytic with decreased ferritin (<15-30 µg/L). As transferrin saturation falls <15-20%, iron supply to erythropoiesis is insufficient and cells become hypochromic and microcytic, the classic feature of iron-deficiency anemia (Harrison 22e p.764). Iron deficiency is ABSOLUTE when stores are exhausted and FUNCTIONAL when erythropoiesis is iron-restricted but stores are adequate or even increased. Low hepcidin in iron deficiency maximizes dietary absorption; TMPRSS6 mutations cause hereditary iron-refractory IDA (IRIDA) with reduced absorption and oral-iron refractoriness. In inflammation, high hepcidin sequesters iron into stores, producing functional iron deficiency with low serum iron and transferrin saturation despite elevated ferritin; absolute and functional deficiency often coexist (IBD, CKD, aging). Beyond anemia, heart, muscle, and brain are affected; immune response may be blunted as lymphocytes require iron during the metabolic burst (Harrison 22e p.764).","additionalDifferentials":["Anemia of inflammation (anemia of chronic disease) - the second most common anemia worldwide, due to functional iron deficiency; a diagnosis of exclusion usually occurring with an inflammatory disorder such as cancer or infection (Harrison 22e p.765, p.450).","Thalassemia trait - microcytosis with normal-to-high ferritin and serum iron; red cells are more homogeneous in volume with a low/normal RDW (Harrison 22e p.450, p.767).","Mixed forms (combined absolute and functional iron deficiency) (Harrison 22e p.767).","Other nutritional anemias - vitamin B12 and folate deficiency (megaloblastic, macrocytic); may coexist with and mask iron deficiency in malabsorption (Harrison 22e p.765).","Sideroblastic anemia (rare) as a cause of microcytic hypochromic red cells (Harrison 22e p.~)."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Anemia of inflammation / chronic disease (functional iron deficiency from high hepcidin: low serum iron and transferrin saturation with NORMAL-to-HIGH ferritin) (Harrison 22e p.764, p.767).","Thalassemia trait (microcytic but with normal-to-high ferritin and iron, and a low/normal RDW) (Harrison 22e p.450, p.767)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ferritin (mainstay; <30 µg/L diagnostic, sensitivity 92% / specificity 98%); note ferritin is an acute-phase reactant and may rise severalfold in inflammation (Harrison 22e p.765, p.450).","Transferrin saturation (serum iron/TIBC); iron-restricted erythropoiesis at <15-20% (Harrison 22e p.765).","Serum iron (reduced, but diurnal fluctuation prevents use as a single test) (Harrison 22e p.765).","MCV and RDW from the CBC (MCV <80 fL when established; RDW often elevated reflecting anisocytosis) (Harrison 22e p.765).","Soluble transferrin receptor (sTfR) - increased in iron deficiency; sTfR/log ferritin ratio >2 favors iron deficiency (Harrison 22e p.766, p.767).","Reticulocyte hemoglobin content (CHr, reduced) and percentage of hypochromic red cells (%HYPO, increased >6%), reflecting iron availability days to months before testing (Harrison 22e p.766, p.767).","Serum hepcidin (low/undetectable in absolute deficiency; not routinely measured, reference values not standardized) (Harrison 22e p.766, p.767).","Absence of stainable iron in bone marrow remains the gold standard, although it is rarely needed and unacceptably invasive (Harrison 22e p.765).","Endoscopy and/or radiographic imaging to identify the cause - essential in males and postmenopausal females to rule out malignancy or other severe bleeding sources (Harrison 22e p.765, p.~)."],"redFlags":["Unexplained iron-deficiency anemia in any adult (with the possible exception of a premenopausal, multiparous woman) MANDATES thorough endoscopic and/or radiographic visualization of the entire large bowel - right (ascending) colon cancer causes insidious blood loss and often presents as IDA (Harrison 22e p.~, colorectal cancer chapter).","Imaging and endoscopy to trace the cause are essential in males and postmenopausal females to rule out malignancy or other severe causes of bleeding (Harrison 22e p.765).","Hemoglobin falling below ~8 g/dL or otherwise severe anemia (Harrison 22e p.765).","Myelophthisic blood smear (nucleated red cells, teardrop cells, immature white cells) suggesting marrow infiltration mandates marrow examination - a competing cause of anemia to recognize (Harrison 22e p.450)."],"prognosis":"Iron deficiency and IDA are highly treatable with iron replacement. Oral iron usually requires at least ~3 months of therapy to restore hemoglobin and replenish stores and prevent early recurrence; inadequate response (no reticulocytosis at 1 week or <1 g/dL hemoglobin rise at 2 weeks) should prompt a search for noncompliance, ongoing blood loss, or malabsorption. Prognosis ultimately depends on the underlying cause (e.g., occult GI malignancy) rather than the anemia itself (Harrison 22e p.766).","pitfalls":["Classic microcytosis/hypochromia can be ABSENT in early iron deficiency, in elderly patients, or when masked by concomitant folate/B12 deficiency or liver disease - do not exclude IDA on a normal MCV alone (Harrison 22e p.765, p.450).","Ferritin is an acute-phase reactant; normal or high ferritin during inflammation can mask coexisting iron deficiency (Harrison 22e p.450).","Serum iron alone is unreliable as a single test due to diurnal variation (Harrison 22e p.765).","Treating IDA without investigating the cause can miss occult GI malignancy; isolated symptomatic iron deficiency without anemia should not be underestimated (Harrison 22e p.764, p.765).","Biomarkers other than ferritin and transferrin saturation are limited by poor standardization and accessibility (Harrison 22e p.766)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison describes a continuum rather than a formal grading scale: (1) isolated/absolute iron deficiency with depleted stores (ferritin <15-30 µg/L) but NORMAL hemoglobin and normochromic-normocytic cells; (2) iron-restricted erythropoiesis once transferrin saturation falls <15-20%, with hypochromic microcytic cells; (3) overt iron-deficiency anemia. Severe anemia is generally taken as hemoglobin <=8 g/dL (the threshold at which anemia commonly becomes detectable and at which rapid IV correction is considered) (Harrison 22e p.764-766).","references":["Harrison 22e p.764","Harrison 22e p.765","Harrison 22e p.766","Harrison 22e p.767","Harrison 22e p.450","Camaschella C: Iron deficiency. Blood 133:30, 2019"],"crossLinks":["colorectal_cancer"]},"iron_poisoning":{"name":"Iron poisoning","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["iron overdose","iron toxicity","ferrous sulfate poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Toxicity relates to the amount of elemental iron ingested, not the total salt weight, so the specific preparation must be identified","The classic course has recognised stages: early GI toxicity, a deceptive latent phase of apparent improvement, then shock and metabolic acidosis, hepatic failure, and finally late gastric scarring and outlet obstruction","Early severe vomiting, diarrhoea, and haematemesis reflect direct corrosive injury to the gut mucosa","Do not be misled by the quiescent second phase; serious systemic toxicity can still evolve after the patient seems to settle","Radiopaque tablets may be visible on an abdominal radiograph and can confirm ingestion and guide decontamination","Whole-bowel irrigation is the preferred decontamination method for significant ingestions, as activated charcoal does not bind iron","Deferoxamine is the specific chelating antidote, indicated for systemic toxicity, shock, significant acidosis, or high serum iron levels","A high anion-gap metabolic acidosis, hypotension, and progressive hepatic injury mark severe poisoning requiring urgent chelation and intensive support"],"pathophysiology":"In overdose, free iron overwhelms the body's binding capacity (transferrin) and circulates unbound. It is directly corrosive to the gastrointestinal mucosa, causing haemorrhagic gastroenteritis and fluid loss. Absorbed free iron is a potent catalyst of free-radical formation and disrupts mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation, causing cellular injury most prominently in the liver, along with a high anion-gap metabolic (lactic) acidosis. Iron-induced vasodilatation, increased capillary permeability, and myocardial depression produce shock. Healing of the corrosive gastric injury can later cause strictures and gastric outlet obstruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Other corrosive or caustic ingestions","Acute gastroenteritis of infective cause","Other causes of high anion-gap metabolic acidosis","Paracetamol or other hepatotoxic ingestion","Sepsis with shock"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum iron level, ideally timed to peak absorption","Abdominal radiograph for radiopaque tablets","Arterial blood gas with anion gap and lactate","Liver function tests and coagulation profile to track hepatic injury","Full blood count, electrolytes, renal function, and glucose"],"redFlags":["Haematemesis or melaena indicating corrosive GI injury","Hypotension, shock, or lethargy signalling systemic toxicity","High anion-gap metabolic acidosis with elevated lactate","Evidence of hepatic injury or coagulopathy","Persistent or worsening symptoms after the apparent latent phase"],"prognosis":"Small ingestions causing only GI upset usually resolve, but severe poisoning with shock, acidosis, and hepatic failure carries significant mortality; early recognition, decontamination, and deferoxamine chelation greatly improve survival, though late gastric strictures may still occur.","pitfalls":["Calculating dose from tablet weight rather than elemental iron content","Discharging a patient during the deceptive latent phase before systemic toxicity manifests","Giving activated charcoal, which does not adsorb iron","Delaying deferoxamine in a patient with shock, acidosis, or high iron levels"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"ischemic_colitis":{"name":"Ischaemic colitis","system":"GI / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ischemic colitis","colonic ischaemia","colonic ischemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AASLD & ACG","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest form of intestinal ischaemia, usually affecting older adults and typically arising from a transient, non-occlusive fall in colonic perfusion rather than a fixed arterial blockage","Classic presentation is sudden mild crampy left-sided abdominal pain followed within a day by the passage of bloody or maroon stool, with only modest blood loss","The watershed zones (splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction) are most vulnerable, while the rectum is usually spared because of its dual blood supply","CT often shows segmental bowel-wall thickening, and colonoscopy is the key diagnostic test showing segmental oedema, haemorrhage, and mucosal ulceration with an abrupt transition to normal mucosa","Most cases are non-gangrenous and settle with supportive care: bowel rest, intravenous fluids, and correction of any precipitating hypoperfusion","Right-sided (isolated right colon) involvement carries a worse prognosis and should raise concern for coexisting acute mesenteric ischaemia","Distinguish from infective colitis and inflammatory bowel disease; the segmental distribution and rapid resolution favour an ischaemic cause","Recognised triggers include hypotension, cardiac or vascular surgery, dehydration, hypercoagulable states, and certain drugs such as vasoconstrictors"],"pathophysiology":"Ischaemic colitis results from a temporary reduction in blood flow to a colonic segment, most often a low-flow (non-occlusive) event superimposed on the marginal circulation of watershed areas. Reduced perfusion injures the mucosa first, producing oedema, submucosal haemorrhage, and superficial ulceration. In most patients reperfusion allows healing, but persistent or severe ischaemia can progress to transmural (gangrenous) necrosis with perforation or, later, to fibrotic stricture. It is usually a disease of small vessels and systemic hypoperfusion rather than a large-artery occlusion, which distinguishes it from acute mesenteric ischaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute mesenteric ischaemia (small bowel)","Infective (bacterial) colitis","Inflammatory bowel disease, especially Crohn colitis","Diverticular haemorrhage or diverticulitis","Colorectal malignancy","Pseudomembranous (Clostridioides difficile) colitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT of the abdomen and pelvis to assess segmental wall thickening and exclude perforation","Colonoscopy or flexible sigmoidoscopy showing segmental mucosal injury with an abrupt transition zone","Serum lactate and inflammatory markers to gauge severity and detect possible gangrene","Stool studies to exclude infective causes when the picture is atypical","Cardiac and vascular assessment to identify a precipitating low-flow state"],"redFlags":["Peritonism, fever, marked leucocytosis, or rising lactate suggesting gangrenous colitis and impending perforation","Isolated right-sided colonic involvement, which may signal concurrent acute mesenteric ischaemia","Persistent bleeding, haemodynamic instability, or failure to improve on supportive care","Development of a colonic stricture with obstruction during recovery"],"prognosis":"Non-gangrenous ischaemic colitis usually resolves within a few days with conservative management and has a good outcome. Gangrenous disease requiring surgery carries substantial mortality, and right-sided involvement predicts a worse course. A minority develop chronic segmental colitis or a stricture.","pitfalls":["Assuming rectal sparing excludes colonic ischaemia, or mistaking segmental ischaemic changes for inflammatory bowel disease","Delaying surgical review when signs of transmural necrosis are present","Overlooking a precipitating hypoperfusion event that will otherwise recur","Failing to recognise that isolated right colon disease may reflect small-bowel mesenteric ischaemia needing urgent vascular evaluation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AASLD/ACG guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"ischemic_heart_disease":{"name":"Ischemic Heart Disease","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IHD","Chronic Coronary Disease","Chronic Coronary Syndrome","Coronary Artery Disease","Stable Angina Pectoris"],"chapter":284,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2090","clinicalPearls":["IHD reflects an imbalance between myocardial oxygen supply and demand; the dominant cause is atherosclerotic narrowing of one or more epicardial coronary arteries reducing regional myocardial perfusion (Harrison 22e p.2090).","Anginal discomfort is classically described as heaviness, pressure, squeezing, smothering or choking rather than frank pain, is crescendo-decrescendo, typically lasts 2-5 minutes, and may be signaled by a clenched fist over the sternum (Levine's sign) (Harrison 22e p.2092).","Anginal pain may radiate to either shoulder, both arms (especially the ulnar forearm/hand), back, interscapular region, neck, jaw, teeth, or epigastrium; it is rarely localized below the umbilicus or above the mandible (Harrison 22e p.2092).","Radiation to the trapezius muscles is NOT typical of myocardial ischemia and points more toward pericarditis; this is a useful discriminating feature (Harrison 22e p.2092).","Anginal equivalents (dyspnea, nausea, fatigue, faintness) and atypical presentations are more common in women, the elderly, and patients with diabetes mellitus, in whom angina may not relate strictly to provoking factors (Harrison 22e p.2093).","A resting 12-lead ECG may be entirely normal in typical angina; ST-T and LVH changes are suggestive but nonspecific, whereas dynamic ST-T changes appearing with an anginal episode and resolving afterward are more specific (Harrison 22e p.2094).","A stenosis must reduce epicardial luminal diameter by about 50% to limit flow augmentation on demand, and by roughly 80% before resting flow falls; beyond that, small further reductions cut flow dramatically (Harrison 22e p.2091).","Reversible ischemic injury occurs within roughly 20 minutes of total occlusion (without collaterals); occlusion beyond 20 minutes leads to myocardial necrosis (Harrison 22e p.2091).","The Canadian Cardiovascular Society class grades angina severity and the New York Heart Association class grades functional limitation (Harrison 22e p.2093).","An ischemic stress-ECG response is flat or downsloping ST depression >0.1 mV lasting >0.08 s; upsloping or junctional ST changes are not diagnostic, and a test failing to reach 85% of age/sex-predicted maximal heart rate is nondiagnostic (Harrison 22e p.2094-2095).","Chest wall tenderness, pain reproduced by palpation, or pain localizable with a single fingertip makes a myocardial ischemic origin unlikely (Harrison 22e p.2093-2094).","Most patients who die suddenly from IHD do so from ischemia-induced ventricular tachyarrhythmias (Harrison 22e p.2092)."],"pathophysiology":"Myocardial oxygen demand (MVO2) is governed by heart rate, contractility, and wall tension, while supply depends on oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood and coronary blood flow (predominantly diastolic). Coronary resistance resides mainly in prearteriolar (R2) and arteriolar/capillary (R3) microvascular beds, which normally dilate to match demand (metabolic regulation and autoregulation). Atherosclerosis of epicardial arteries, driven by endothelial dysfunction from high LDL, smoking, hypertension, and diabetes, produces subintimal plaques; plaque rupture or erosion exposes contents to blood, triggering platelet activation/aggregation and the coagulation cascade with fibrin deposition and thrombus formation. As proximal stenoses worsen, distal resistance vessels dilate maximally and flow becomes pressure-dependent, so increased demand (exertion, emotion, tachycardia) precipitates ischemia. Ischemia causes regional contractile dysfunction (hypokinesis to dyskinesis), impaired relaxation, a metabolic shift to anaerobic lactate production with falling intracellular pH and high-energy phosphate depletion, membrane dysfunction with potassium leak and cytosolic calcium rise, characteristic ECG repolarization changes, and electrical instability. The subendocardium is most vulnerable to underperfusion (Harrison 22e p.2090-2092).","additionalDifferentials":["Aortic stenosis with severe LVH causing subendocardial ischemia and angina without coronary atherosclerosis (Harrison 22e p.2091)","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (Harrison 22e p.2093)","Aortic regurgitation and pulmonary hypertension as causes of angina-like chest discomfort (Harrison 22e p.2093)","Microvascular angina / abnormal cardiac nociception in patients with ischemic-type chest pain but no flow-limiting epicardial obstruction, more common in women (Harrison 22e p.2093)","Vasospastic (variant) angina due to coronary spasm (Harrison 22e p.2091, 2093)","Pericarditis (suggested by discomfort radiating to the trapezius) (Harrison 22e p.2092)","Esophageal/gastrointestinal disease and musculoskeletal chest wall pain (Harrison 22e p.2093-2094)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Aortic stenosis (subendocardial ischemia)","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Pericarditis","Esophageal disease","Chest wall / musculoskeletal pain reproducible on palpation"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting lipid panel (total, LDL, HDL cholesterol, triglycerides, lipoprotein(a)) (Harrison 22e p.2094)","Glucose / hemoglobin A1c, creatinine, hematocrit; urinalysis including microalbuminuria; thyroid function if indicated (Harrison 22e p.2094)","High-sensitivity C-reactive protein (1-3 mg/L is an independent risk marker, most useful for reclassifying intermediate-risk patients) (Harrison 22e p.2094)","Resting 12-lead ECG (Harrison 22e p.2094)","Chest x-ray for cardiac enlargement, ventricular aneurysm, or heart failure signs (Harrison 22e p.2094)","Exercise treadmill ECG stress testing (sensitivity about 75%) (Harrison 22e p.2094-2095)","Stress myocardial perfusion imaging (thallium-201 or 99m-technetium sestamibi SPECT) or PET (N-13 ammonia, rubidium-82) with exercise or pharmacologic (adenosine, dobutamine) stress (Harrison 22e p.2095)","Stress echocardiography and cardiac MR stress testing (Harrison 22e p.2095-2096)","Coronary artery calcium scoring (Agatston score) by EBCT/MDCT as adjunctive, not primary screening (Harrison 22e p.2095-2097)","Coronary CT angiography for intermediate pretest probability and plaque assessment (Harrison 22e p.2097)","Invasive coronary arteriography (with FFR/iwFR) per ISCHEMIA-informed indications (Harrison 22e p.2097-2098)"],"redFlags":["Angina occurring with less exertion than before, at rest, or awakening the patient from sleep (features of an unstable syndrome) (Harrison 22e p.2093)","Angina accompanied by symptoms or signs of heart failure, pulmonary edema, transient third heart sound, or mitral regurgitation (Harrison 22e p.2092, 2097)","Inability to exercise beyond 6 minutes / stage II of the Bruce protocol (Harrison 22e p.2097)","Ischemia at low workload, >=0.2 mV ST depression, or ST depression persisting >5 minutes after exercise (Harrison 22e p.2097)","A fall in systolic blood pressure >10 mmHg during exercise or exercise-induced ventricular tachyarrhythmia (Harrison 22e p.2096-2097)","Large or multiple perfusion defects, increased lung tracer uptake, or a fall in LV ejection fraction during stress imaging (Harrison 22e p.2097)","Reduced resting LV ejection fraction (<0.40) (Harrison 22e p.2097)","Sudden cardiac death may be the unheralded presenting manifestation of IHD (Harrison 22e p.2092)"],"prognosis":"Principal prognostic determinants are age, left ventricular function, the location and severity of coronary stenoses, and the severity/activity of ischemia. Recent-onset or unstable angina, early post-MI angina, angina poorly responsive to therapy, and angina with heart failure all carry increased risk. Elevated LV end-diastolic pressure/volume and reduced ejection fraction signal poor prognosis. Left main (>50% diameter) or proximal LAD obstruction carries greater risk than right or circumflex lesions because more myocardium is jeopardized. With any obstructive disease, mortality rises sharply when LV function is impaired; the larger the established necrosis, the poorer the prognosis. Conversely, patients with chest discomfort but normal LV function and normal coronary arteries have an excellent prognosis, as do those completing stage III of the Bruce protocol with a normal perfusion scan or negative stress echo. Greater number/severity of risk factors (advanced age >75, hypertension, dyslipidemia, diabetes, morbid obesity, peripheral/cerebrovascular disease, prior MI), elevated CRP, extensive coronary calcification, and increased carotid intimal thickening all worsen prognosis (Harrison 22e p.2097).","pitfalls":["Relying on coronary artery calcium as the primary screening test or sole basis for therapeutic decisions; it is adjunctive only (Harrison 22e p.2092).","Interpreting a stress ECG without accounting for pretest probability; false positives rise in low-probability patients (e.g., asymptomatic men <40, premenopausal women without risk factors) and in those on digitalis/antiarrhythmics, with conduction disturbances, LVH, resting ST-T abnormalities, or abnormal potassium (Harrison 22e p.2095).","Assuming a negative stress ECG excludes CAD; sensitivity is only about 75%, though it makes three-vessel or left main disease unlikely (Harrison 22e p.2095).","Circumflex-territory disease may yield a false-negative stress test because the posterolateral wall is poorly represented on the surface 12-lead ECG (Harrison 22e p.2095).","Coronary arteriography images only the lumen and can miss severe non-luminal (remodeled, outwardly bulging) atherosclerosis (Harrison 22e p.2097).","Missing atypical/anginal-equivalent presentations in women, the elderly, and patients with diabetes (Harrison 22e p.2093).","Overlooking that about 25% of acute MI survivors never come to medical attention yet share the same adverse prognosis (Harrison 22e p.2092-2093)."],"severityClassification":"Angina severity is graded by the Canadian Cardiovascular Society (CCS) functional classification (Class I: angina only with strenuous/rapid/prolonged exertion; Class II: slight limitation of ordinary activity; Class III: marked limitation with ordinary activity such as walking 1-2 blocks; Class IV: inability to perform any physical activity without discomfort, angina may be present at rest). Functional impact is described by the New York Heart Association (NYHA) classification (Classes I-IV) (Harrison 22e p.2093).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2090","Harrison 22e p.2091","Harrison 22e p.2092","Harrison 22e p.2093","Harrison 22e p.2094","Harrison 22e p.2095","Harrison 22e p.2096","Harrison 22e p.2097"],"crossLinks":[]},"japanese_encephalitis":{"name":"Japanese encephalitis","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JE","Japanese B encephalitis","JEV infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by a mosquito-borne flavivirus transmitted mainly by Culex species, with pigs acting as amplifying hosts and wading birds as the natural reservoir","The great majority of infections are silent; only a small fraction progress to clinical encephalitis, but that subset carries high mortality and disability","In India it is a leading cause of acute encephalitis syndrome, occurring in outbreaks in rice-growing and pig-rearing regions and disproportionately affecting children","Classic presentation is fever, headache and altered sensorium progressing over days, often with seizures and a Parkinsonian movement disorder (masked facies, tremor, rigidity)","There is no specific antiviral therapy; care is supportive, centred on seizure control, airway protection and management of raised intracranial pressure","A safe and effective vaccine exists and is incorporated into immunisation programmes in endemic Indian districts, making prevention the cornerstone of control","MRI often shows characteristic bilateral thalamic lesions, sometimes with haemorrhage, which supports the diagnosis","Confirmation relies on detecting JEV-specific IgM in cerebrospinal fluid or serum by capture ELISA"],"pathophysiology":"Japanese encephalitis virus is a single-stranded RNA flavivirus maintained in an enzootic cycle between Culex mosquitoes and amplifying vertebrate hosts, chiefly pigs and water birds. Humans are incidental dead-end hosts. Following a mosquito bite the virus replicates locally and in regional lymph nodes, producing a viraemia that may seed the central nervous system across the blood-brain barrier. Neuronal infection and the accompanying inflammatory response cause neuronal death particularly in the thalamus, basal ganglia, brainstem and cerebral cortex, accounting for the movement disorders and depressed consciousness seen clinically.","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex encephalitis","Other arboviral encephalitides (e.g. West Nile, dengue encephalopathy)","Bacterial and tuberculous meningitis","Cerebral malaria","Enteroviral encephalitis","Scrub typhus with CNS involvement"],"infectionMimics":["Autoimmune (anti-NMDA receptor) encephalitis","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis","Metabolic or toxic encephalopathy","Non-convulsive status epilepticus"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["JEV-specific IgM capture ELISA in CSF or serum for confirmation","CSF analysis showing lymphocytic pleocytosis with mildly raised protein and normal glucose","MRI brain looking for bilateral thalamic and basal ganglia signal change","EEG to assess for seizure activity and encephalopathic patterns","Investigations to exclude treatable mimics such as HSV PCR and malaria films"],"redFlags":["Rapidly declining consciousness or coma indicating severe encephalitis and need for critical care","Status epilepticus or repeated seizures","Signs of raised intracranial pressure or brainstem involvement (abnormal breathing, pupillary changes)","Progressive respiratory compromise requiring airway protection and ventilation"],"prognosis":"Symptomatic encephalitis carries substantial mortality, and a large proportion of survivors are left with lasting neurological or psychiatric sequelae including motor deficits, movement disorders, cognitive impairment and epilepsy. Outcome is worse at the extremes of age and with delayed presentation.","pitfalls":["Attributing acute encephalitis syndrome to JE without confirmatory serology and missing treatable causes such as herpes encephalitis","Overlooking the diagnosis outside the classic transmission season or in adults in newly endemic areas","Failing to protect the airway and control seizures early in a deteriorating patient","Neglecting vaccination and vector-control opportunities in endemic districts"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_dermatomyositis":{"name":"Juvenile Dermatomyositis","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JDM","juvenile dermatomyositis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common inflammatory myopathy of childhood, characterised by proximal muscle weakness together with distinctive skin changes.","Look for the heliotrope rash (violaceous discolouration of the upper eyelids) and Gottron papules over the knuckles, elbows, and knees.","Weakness is typically symmetrical and proximal, so children struggle to climb stairs, rise from the floor, or lift their arms overhead.","Nailfold capillary changes (dilated, dropout, or tortuous loops) are a useful bedside clue and correlate with disease activity.","Calcinosis of skin and soft tissues is a late complication, more likely when treatment is delayed or inadequate.","Unlike adult dermatomyositis, an underlying malignancy is rarely associated in children.","A small vessel vasculopathy can affect the gastrointestinal tract, potentially causing ulceration, bleeding, or perforation.","Early diagnosis and adequate immunosuppression improve outcomes and reduce the risk of chronic disability and calcinosis."],"pathophysiology":"Juvenile dermatomyositis is an immune-mediated systemic vasculopathy affecting small vessels of skeletal muscle, skin, and other organs. Complement-mediated injury to the capillary endothelium leads to capillary dropout, ischaemia, and perifascicular muscle fibre atrophy. Interferon-driven inflammation and immune complex deposition contribute to ongoing muscle and skin damage, producing the characteristic weakness and rash.","additionalDifferentials":["Muscular dystrophy","Systemic lupus erythematosus / overlap connective tissue disease","Viral or post-viral myositis","Polymyositis","Hypothyroid or metabolic myopathy","Spinal muscular atrophy or other neuromuscular disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum muscle enzymes (creatine kinase, aldolase, LDH, transaminases)","MRI of muscle to identify inflammation and guide biopsy site","Nailfold capillaroscopy","Myositis-specific and myositis-associated autoantibodies","Muscle biopsy when the diagnosis is uncertain","Electromyography in selected cases"],"redFlags":["Severe dysphagia or nasal regurgitation indicating pharyngeal muscle involvement with aspiration risk","Respiratory muscle weakness or interstitial lung disease causing hypoventilation","Gastrointestinal vasculopathy with abdominal pain, bleeding, or perforation","Rapidly progressive weakness or inability to protect the airway","Extensive skin ulceration reflecting severe vasculopathy"],"prognosis":"With prompt, adequate immunosuppression many children achieve remission and regain function; delayed or insufficient treatment increases the risk of chronic disease, calcinosis, and long-term disability. A monocyclic course carries the best prognosis.","pitfalls":["Attributing subtle proximal weakness to clumsiness or normal fatigue and delaying diagnosis","Assuming normal muscle enzymes exclude the disease, as they can be near-normal in some children","Under-treating early disease and thereby increasing the risk of calcinosis","Overlooking dysphagia and aspiration risk when pharyngeal muscles are involved"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_idiopathic_arthritis":{"name":"Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JIA","juvenile rheumatoid arthritis","juvenile chronic arthritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common chronic rheumatic disease of childhood, defined by arthritis beginning before 16 years of age and persisting for at least 6 weeks, with other causes excluded.","It is a heterogeneous group of subtypes, including oligoarticular, polyarticular, systemic, enthesitis-related, and psoriatic forms, each with distinct features and prognosis.","Arthritis presents with joint swelling, morning stiffness or gelling, and limited movement, often with less pain than expected; young children may present with limp or refusal to use a limb.","Oligoarticular JIA is the most common subtype, typically affecting large joints in young girls and carrying a significant risk of chronic anterior uveitis.","Uveitis is frequently asymptomatic, so regular ophthalmological slit-lamp screening is essential to prevent visual loss, particularly in young ANA-positive children.","Systemic JIA features prominent extra-articular disease with quotidian high fever, an evanescent salmon-coloured rash, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, and serositis.","The diagnosis is clinical and one of exclusion; laboratory tests support rather than confirm it, and inflammatory markers may be normal in oligoarticular disease.","A serious complication of systemic JIA is macrophage activation syndrome, a form of haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis that can be rapidly fatal.","Management principles combine anti-inflammatory therapy, disease-modifying agents, and increasingly biologic therapy, alongside physiotherapy to preserve joint function and growth."],"pathophysiology":"JIA results from immune dysregulation leading to chronic inflammation of the synovium. In most subtypes, an aberrant adaptive immune response with T-cell activation and autoantibody production drives synovial proliferation (pannus), which can erode cartilage and bone and disturb the growth of nearby physes. Systemic JIA is instead an autoinflammatory condition driven predominantly by the innate immune system and proinflammatory cytokines such as interleukin-1 and interleukin-6, accounting for its prominent systemic features and its predisposition to macrophage activation syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Septic arthritis and osteomyelitis","Reactive and post-infectious arthritis (including acute rheumatic fever)","Leukaemia and other malignancy presenting with bone or joint pain","Systemic lupus erythematosus and other connective tissue diseases","Inflammatory bowel disease-associated arthritis","Non-inflammatory causes such as trauma, hypermobility, and benign limb pains"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count, ESR, and C-reactive protein","Antinuclear antibody (informs uveitis risk) and rheumatoid factor","HLA-B27 in suspected enthesitis-related arthritis","Slit-lamp ophthalmological examination for uveitis screening","Joint imaging (ultrasound or MRI) to assess synovitis and effusion","Ferritin, triglycerides, and fibrinogen if macrophage activation syndrome is suspected"],"redFlags":["Features of macrophage activation syndrome (persistent fever, falling cell counts, very high ferritin, hepatic dysfunction)","Findings suggesting malignancy such as severe night pain, cytopenias, or systemic decline","A hot, acutely swollen joint with fever suggesting septic arthritis","Visual symptoms or complications of uveitis","Systemic features such as high spiking fever with serositis"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies by subtype; many children with oligoarticular disease do well, while polyarticular and systemic forms may follow a more persistent course, and modern therapy has substantially improved remission rates and reduced long-term joint damage. Uveitis and macrophage activation syndrome remain important causes of morbidity.","pitfalls":["Missing asymptomatic uveitis by omitting scheduled slit-lamp screening","Overlooking leukaemia in a child with bone pain and abnormal counts before starting steroids","Failing to recognise early macrophage activation syndrome in systemic JIA","Attributing a single hot joint to JIA and missing septic arthritis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"juvenile_myoclonic_epilepsy":{"name":"Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy","system":"Neurological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["JME","Janz syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common idiopathic (genetic) generalised epilepsy syndrome, usually beginning in adolescence.","The defining seizure type is myoclonic jerks, typically of the arms and shoulders, occurring soon after waking.","Most patients also have generalised tonic-clonic seizures, and a subset have absence seizures.","Early morning clumsiness, such as dropping objects like a toothbrush or cup, is a classic historical clue that is often overlooked.","Seizures are strongly provoked by sleep deprivation, alcohol, and sometimes photic stimulation.","The interictal EEG characteristically shows generalised polyspike-and-wave discharges, and a photoparoxysmal response may be seen.","Development and intelligence are typically normal, and neuroimaging is usually unremarkable.","Seizures are generally well controlled with appropriate broad-spectrum treatment, but the condition usually requires long-term therapy as relapse on withdrawal is common; certain narrow-spectrum antiseizure medicines can worsen it."],"pathophysiology":"JME is a genetically determined generalised epilepsy involving abnormal thalamocortical network excitability and synchronisation rather than a focal structural lesion. Inherited susceptibility, likely polygenic in most cases, produces a lowered threshold for generalised epileptiform discharges. The characteristic myoclonus and generalised seizures arise from these hyperexcitable cortico-thalamic circuits, and the strong link with the sleep-wake transition reflects state-dependent changes in cortical excitability.","additionalDifferentials":["Other idiopathic generalised epilepsies (childhood or juvenile absence epilepsy)","Progressive myoclonic epilepsies","Focal epilepsy with secondary generalisation","Benign physiological (hypnagogic) myoclonus","Non-epileptic movements or tics","Psychogenic non-epileptic events"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["EEG, ideally including a recording after sleep deprivation and on awakening","Photic stimulation during EEG to elicit a photoparoxysmal response","MRI brain, mainly to exclude structural or progressive causes in atypical cases","Detailed history focusing on early-morning jerks and provoking factors","Metabolic or genetic testing when a progressive myoclonic epilepsy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive cognitive decline or neurological deterioration suggesting a progressive myoclonic epilepsy","Focal neurological signs or focal features on EEG or imaging","Seizures worsening after starting treatment (possible inappropriate narrow-spectrum drug)","Frequent generalised tonic-clonic seizures or status epilepticus","Recurrent seizures driven by unrecognised sleep deprivation or non-adherence"],"prognosis":"Prognosis for seizure control is favourable with suitable therapy and lifestyle measures, and cognition is preserved, but JME is generally a lifelong tendency; most patients relapse if medication is stopped, so long-term or indefinite treatment is often needed.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis by not asking specifically about early-morning myoclonic jerks","Misclassifying generalised seizures as focal and selecting a drug that aggravates myoclonus and absences","Underestimating the impact of sleep deprivation, alcohol, and poor adherence on seizure control","Attempting early withdrawal of treatment and precipitating relapse","Overlooking coexisting absence seizures"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"kala_azar":{"name":"Kala-azar (visceral leishmaniasis)","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["visceral leishmaniasis","VL","black fever","Leishmania donovani infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A systemic protozoal infection caused by Leishmania donovani, transmitted by the bite of female phlebotomine sandflies","In India it is concentrated in Bihar and adjoining eastern states and is the target of a national elimination programme, with humans the main reservoir for the Indian form","Classic presentation is prolonged fever with progressive splenomegaly (often massive), hepatomegaly, weight loss, and darkening of the skin that gives the disease its name","Laboratory hallmarks are pancytopenia and a marked polyclonal hypergammaglobulinaemia","Untreated visceral leishmaniasis is almost uniformly fatal, and secondary bacterial infections and bleeding are common causes of death","Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis can appear after apparent cure and acts as a human reservoir, so its recognition is important for elimination efforts","Diagnosis combines serology (e.g. rK39 rapid test) with demonstration of amastigotes in splenic, bone marrow or other aspirates","Effective antileishmanial therapy exists, with liposomal amphotericin B favoured in India; HIV co-infection worsens response and relapse risk"],"pathophysiology":"Sandfly bites inoculate Leishmania promastigotes, which are taken up by macrophages and transform into amastigotes that multiply within the mononuclear phagocyte system. The parasite disseminates to the spleen, liver and bone marrow, driving reticuloendothelial hyperplasia that produces organomegaly and, through hypersplenism and marrow infiltration, the characteristic pancytopenia. A vigorous but non-protective humoral response causes hypergammaglobulinaemia, while impaired cell-mediated immunity permits unchecked parasite proliferation and progressive, potentially fatal systemic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Malaria","Enteric (typhoid) fever","Miliary or disseminated tuberculosis","Haematological malignancy (leukaemia, lymphoma)","Chronic myeloproliferative disorders causing massive splenomegaly","Infective endocarditis or other causes of prolonged fever with organomegaly"],"infectionMimics":["Lymphoma or leukaemia with organomegaly and cytopenias","Tropical splenomegaly syndrome","Disseminated histoplasmosis","Chronic malaria (hyperreactive malarial splenomegaly)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["rK39 rapid serological test for L. donovani","Demonstration of amastigotes in bone marrow, splenic or lymph node aspirates","Full blood count showing pancytopenia and protein electrophoresis showing hypergammaglobulinaemia","HIV testing given the impact of co-infection on treatment and relapse","PCR-based parasite detection where available"],"redFlags":["Prolonged fever with massive splenomegaly and pancytopenia requiring urgent diagnosis and treatment","Severe anaemia, bleeding or intercurrent bacterial infection from marrow suppression, which can be fatal","Failure to respond to therapy or relapse, especially with HIV co-infection","Emergence of post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis, signalling a transmission reservoir"],"prognosis":"With prompt, effective antileishmanial therapy most patients recover fully, and liposomal amphotericin B has markedly improved outcomes in India. Untreated disease is nearly always fatal, and prognosis is poorer in the very young, malnourished, and those with HIV co-infection or advanced disease.","pitfalls":["Attributing prolonged fever with splenomegaly to malaria or typhoid and missing visceral leishmaniasis in endemic areas","Overlooking HIV co-infection, which alters treatment response and relapse risk","Failing to recognise post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis as a source of ongoing transmission","Delaying treatment while awaiting invasive confirmation when serology and clinical picture are diagnostic"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"kawasaki_shock":{"name":"Kawasaki Disease Shock Syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["KDSS","Kawasaki shock syndrome","Kawasaki disease with shock"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A severe presentation of Kawasaki disease in which children develop haemodynamic instability and hypotension requiring fluid resuscitation or inotropic support.","It should be considered in a child with features of Kawasaki disease (prolonged fever, mucocutaneous changes, conjunctival injection, extremity changes, rash, cervical lymphadenopathy) who also appears shocked.","Compared with classic Kawasaki disease, shock syndrome is more often associated with incomplete presentations, greater systemic inflammation, and a higher risk of coronary artery involvement.","Laboratory markers tend to show more intense inflammation, lower platelet counts, and evidence of consumptive or capillary-leak processes.","These children are more likely to be initially resistant to intravenous immunoglobulin and may need additional immunomodulatory therapy.","The overlap with toxic shock syndrome and septic shock frequently leads to diagnostic delay, so a high index of suspicion is essential.","Prompt echocardiography is important because of the elevated risk of coronary artery aneurysms and myocardial dysfunction.","Timely treatment of the underlying vasculitis, in addition to circulatory support, is key to reducing coronary complications."],"pathophysiology":"Kawasaki disease is a self-limited medium-vessel vasculitis of childhood driven by intense systemic immune activation. In the shock variant, an exaggerated inflammatory response with high circulating cytokine levels produces vasodilation, myocardial dysfunction, and increased vascular permeability with capillary leak. The combination of impaired cardiac contractility and fluid shifts out of the intravascular space results in hypotension and organ hypoperfusion, superimposed on the vasculitic damage that threatens the coronary arteries.","additionalDifferentials":["Toxic shock syndrome (staphylococcal or streptococcal)","Septic shock","Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (MIS-C)","Systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis with macrophage activation","Severe adenoviral or other viral infection","Drug reaction with systemic involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography to assess coronary arteries and ventricular function","Inflammatory markers (CRP, ESR)","Full blood count with attention to platelets and anaemia","Serum albumin, sodium, and liver function tests","Cardiac biomarkers (troponin, natriuretic peptides)","Blood cultures and infection screen to exclude sepsis and toxic shock","Coagulation studies"],"redFlags":["Hypotension or shock requiring fluids or inotropes (EMERGENCY)","Signs of myocardial dysfunction or heart failure","Evidence of coronary artery aneurysm on echocardiography","Resistance to first-line immunoglobulin therapy","Rapidly rising inflammatory markers with falling platelets","Multi-organ hypoperfusion or deteriorating conscious level"],"prognosis":"With early recognition and treatment most children recover, but the shock variant carries a higher rate of coronary artery abnormalities, myocardial dysfunction, and immunoglobulin resistance than classic disease, so outcomes depend on prompt immunomodulation and cardiac follow-up.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling the presentation as septic or toxic shock and delaying Kawasaki-directed treatment","Missing incomplete or atypical Kawasaki features in a shocked child","Aggressive fluid loading without recognising myocardial dysfunction","Assuming a single dose of immunoglobulin has controlled the disease without monitoring for resistance","Neglecting early and repeat echocardiography for coronary surveillance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"keloid":{"name":"Keloid","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["keloid scar","keloidal scar"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A keloid is firm, rubbery, and shiny, and crucially extends beyond the boundaries of the original wound, distinguishing it from a hypertrophic scar that stays within the wound margins","Lesions often itch or feel tender and may continue to enlarge over months to years rather than regressing","Predilection sites include the earlobes, presternal chest, shoulders, upper back, and jawline","Risk is higher in people with darker skin phototypes, in younger patients, and where there is a family history","Even minor triggers such as ear piercing, acne, vaccination, or surgery can precipitate lesions in predisposed individuals","Prevention matters: minimise tension on wounds, avoid unnecessary elective procedures in prone sites, and counsel high-risk patients before piercings or cosmetic surgery","Treatment is often multimodal and includes intralesional anti-inflammatory injection, pressure or silicone therapy, and carefully planned surgery combined with adjuvant measures to reduce recurrence","Surgical excision alone carries a high recurrence rate and can produce a larger keloid, so it should be paired with adjuvant therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Keloids arise from dysregulated wound healing in which the normal resolution of the proliferative phase fails and fibroblasts remain persistently activated. Overexpression of profibrotic mediators, notably transforming growth factor-beta, drives excessive and disorganised deposition of thick, hyalinised collagen bundles that extend into surrounding normal dermis. Reduced apoptosis of fibroblasts, altered mechanotransduction from wound tension, and a genetic predisposition all contribute. Unlike hypertrophic scars, keloids do not remodel and regress over time, reflecting a sustained imbalance between collagen synthesis and breakdown.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypertrophic scar (raised but confined to the wound edges and tends to regress)","Dermatofibroma","Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans","Foreign-body granuloma","Cutaneous manifestation of sarcoidosis in a scar","Nodular presentation of acne keloidalis nuchae on the occipital scalp"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis is clinical based on history and morphology","Skin biopsy only when an alternative or malignant process is suspected, recognising that biopsy itself may stimulate keloid growth","Photographic documentation to monitor size and response to treatment"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth, ulceration, bleeding, or fixation suggesting a fibrosarcomatous tumour rather than a keloid","Recurrent or aggressive lesions in a scar raising the possibility of dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans","Functional impairment or contracture over a joint or the ear requiring specialist input"],"prognosis":"Keloids are chronic and prone to recurrence; some stabilise with combined therapy, but complete and permanent resolution is difficult, and lesions frequently recur after excision unless adjuvant treatment is used. They do not spontaneously involute in the way hypertrophic scars often do.","pitfalls":["Excising a keloid without adjuvant therapy, which commonly produces a larger recurrence","Failing to distinguish a keloid from a self-limiting hypertrophic scar and over-treating","Performing elective cosmetic procedures or piercings in high-risk patients without counselling","Overlooking the symptom burden of itch and pain and treating only for appearance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"keratoconus":{"name":"Keratoconus","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["conical cornea","KC"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically presents in adolescence or early adulthood with progressive blurring and distortion of vision from irregular astigmatism","Frequent changes of spectacle prescription that fail to give satisfactory vision should raise suspicion of a developing ectasia","Often bilateral but asymmetric, and associated with eye rubbing, atopy, and connective tissue disorders such as Down syndrome","Clinical signs include a cone-shaped protrusion of the cornea, thinning at the apex, Vogt striae, Fleischer ring, and a scissoring reflex on retinoscopy","Munson sign is an angular bulging of the lower lid margin seen on downgaze in advanced disease","Corneal topography and tomography are key to early diagnosis and to monitoring progression","Management is staged: spectacles or soft lenses early, rigid gas-permeable or specialty contact lenses for irregular astigmatism, corneal collagen cross-linking to arrest progression, and keratoplasty for advanced scarring or intolerance to lenses","Acute corneal hydrops causes sudden pain, redness, and reduced vision from a break in Descemet membrane allowing aqueous into the stroma"],"pathophysiology":"Keratoconus is a progressive, non-inflammatory ectasia in which the cornea thins and steepens into a conical shape, producing irregular astigmatism and myopia. The stromal thinning reflects disorganisation of collagen lamellae and altered enzyme activity with increased degradation of the corneal matrix. Mechanical stress from chronic eye rubbing and an association with atopy and certain systemic syndromes are recognised contributors. As the cone advances, the apex scars and Descemet membrane may rupture, precipitating acute hydrops with corneal oedema.","additionalDifferentials":["Pellucid marginal degeneration","Keratoglobus","Post-refractive surgery ectasia","Irregular astigmatism from corneal scarring","Contact lens-induced corneal warpage"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Corneal topography and tomography to map curvature and detect early ectasia","Pachymetry to quantify corneal thinning","Retinoscopy to detect the scissoring reflex","Serial imaging to assess progression and guide cross-linking decisions","Assessment for associated atopy or connective tissue disorders when indicated"],"redFlags":["Sudden pain, marked visual loss, and corneal oedema suggesting acute hydrops","Rapidly progressive steepening in young patients requiring urgent cross-linking assessment","Corneal scarring or lens intolerance indicating need for keratoplasty referral"],"prognosis":"Progression is most rapid in younger patients and often stabilises with age; timely cross-linking can halt deterioration, and most patients achieve good functional vision with lenses, though a minority need corneal transplantation.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly changing spectacle prescriptions instead of recognising an underlying ectasia","Overlooking eye rubbing as a modifiable driver of progression","Delaying cross-linking in demonstrable progression, allowing avoidable visual loss","Mistaking acute hydrops for infective keratitis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"kernicterus":{"name":"Kernicterus","system":"Neonatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bilirubin encephalopathy","chronic bilirubin encephalopathy","acute bilirubin encephalopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Kernicterus refers to the neurological injury caused by deposition of unconjugated bilirubin in the brain, classically in the basal ganglia and brainstem nuclei.","It is a preventable complication of severe neonatal unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia and represents the chronic, permanent form of bilirubin neurotoxicity.","Acute bilirubin encephalopathy evolves through phases: early lethargy, hypotonia, and poor feeding, followed by hypertonia with backward arching (retrocollis and opisthotonus), high-pitched cry, and fever, and later apnoea, seizures, and coma.","The classic chronic tetrad includes choreoathetoid cerebral palsy, high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss (or auditory neuropathy), impaired upward gaze, and dental enamel dysplasia.","Only unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin that is not bound to albumin can cross the blood-brain barrier, so free bilirubin and factors lowering albumin binding increase risk.","Prematurity, haemolysis, sepsis, acidosis, hypoalbuminaemia, and certain drugs that displace bilirubin heighten vulnerability at any given bilirubin level.","Prevention rests on identifying and monitoring at-risk newborns, timely phototherapy, and exchange transfusion when bilirubin approaches neurotoxic thresholds.","Once established, chronic bilirubin encephalopathy is largely irreversible, underscoring the importance of early recognition and treatment of hyperbilirubinaemia.","Structured predischarge risk assessment and follow-up of jaundiced newborns reduce the incidence of this now-rare but devastating condition."],"pathophysiology":"Unconjugated bilirubin is normally tightly bound to albumin and does not enter the brain. When the bilirubin load overwhelms albumin binding capacity, or binding is reduced by acidosis, hypoalbuminaemia, prematurity, or competing substances, free unconjugated bilirubin crosses the blood-brain barrier. It is neurotoxic, being preferentially deposited in and damaging the basal ganglia (notably the globus pallidus and subthalamic nuclei), the brainstem auditory and oculomotor nuclei, hippocampus, and cerebellum. Bilirubin impairs mitochondrial function and neuronal signalling, causing acute dysfunction and, if sustained, permanent neuronal loss and gliosis with the characteristic clinical sequelae.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal sepsis or meningitis","Hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy","Inborn errors of metabolism causing encephalopathy","Neonatal seizures of other cause","Hypoglycaemic or electrolyte-related encephalopathy","Other causes of neonatal hypertonia or abnormal movements"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial total and unconjugated bilirubin, interpreted against age-in-hours nomograms","Albumin level and bilirubin-to-albumin ratio to gauge binding reserve","Haemolysis workup (blood group, direct antiglobulin test, blood film, reticulocytes)","Auditory brainstem response testing for auditory neuropathy","MRI showing characteristic basal ganglia signal changes in established disease","Evaluation for contributing factors such as sepsis, acidosis, and prematurity"],"redFlags":["Signs of acute bilirubin encephalopathy: lethargy or hypertonia, retrocollis/opisthotonus, high-pitched cry, poor feeding","Bilirubin approaching or exceeding exchange transfusion thresholds for the infant's age and risk","Apnoea, seizures, or coma in a severely jaundiced newborn","Rapidly rising bilirubin, especially with haemolysis, sepsis, or acidosis","Jaundice in a preterm or unwell infant where binding reserve is reduced"],"prognosis":"Acute bilirubin encephalopathy may be partly reversible if bilirubin is lowered promptly, but progression to chronic kernicterus produces permanent, often severe disability including movement disorder, hearing loss, and gaze abnormalities. Because the condition is largely preventable, prognosis hinges on timely detection and treatment of severe hyperbilirubinaemia.","pitfalls":["Interpreting bilirubin values without reference to the infant's age in hours and risk factors","Underestimating neurotoxic risk in preterm, acidotic, hypoalbuminaemic, or septic infants at lower bilirubin levels","Delaying phototherapy or exchange transfusion while bilirubin rises","Missing early, subtle neurological signs of acute encephalopathy","Discharging a jaundiced newborn without adequate follow-up and risk assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"lambert_eaton":{"name":"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LEMS","Lambert-Eaton syndrome","myasthenic syndrome of Lambert-Eaton"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A presynaptic disorder of neuromuscular transmission caused by antibodies against voltage-gated calcium channels that reduce acetylcholine release","Presents with proximal limb weakness, especially the legs, together with autonomic features such as dry mouth, constipation, and erectile dysfunction","Strength may transiently improve with sustained or repeated effort (post-exercise facilitation), and depressed reflexes can augment after exercise","Ocular and bulbar symptoms are usually milder than in myasthenia gravis, though some overlap occurs","About half of cases are paraneoplastic, most often linked to small-cell lung cancer, and may precede the tumour diagnosis","Repetitive nerve stimulation shows a low baseline compound muscle action potential with decrement at low frequency and marked increment at high frequency or after exercise","Treatment addresses any underlying tumour and enhances acetylcholine release; immunotherapy is used for symptom control","A validated screening score helps stratify the likelihood of underlying small-cell lung cancer and guides surveillance"],"pathophysiology":"In LEMS, IgG autoantibodies target P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels on the presynaptic motor nerve terminal. Reduced calcium influx during depolarisation diminishes the quantal release of acetylcholine, so the endplate potential often fails to reach threshold at rest, causing weakness. Repeated activity allows calcium to accumulate in the terminal, temporarily augmenting release and improving strength, which explains facilitation. The same antibodies act on autonomic ganglia and terminals, producing cholinergic autonomic failure. In paraneoplastic cases the tumour expresses calcium channels that trigger the cross-reactive immune response.","additionalDifferentials":["Myasthenia gravis","Inflammatory or metabolic myopathy","Motor neuron disease","Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy","Botulism (also a presynaptic disorder)","Guillain-Barre syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum antibodies against P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels","Repetitive nerve stimulation at low and high frequency and post-exercise testing","CT of the chest and, if negative, FDG-PET to search for small-cell lung cancer","Interval tumour surveillance imaging when initial screening is negative but risk is high","SOX1 antibodies as a marker supporting an underlying small-cell lung cancer","Single-fibre electromyography in equivocal neuromuscular transmission cases"],"redFlags":["Any new diagnosis should trigger a directed search for occult malignancy, especially small-cell lung cancer","Weight loss, haemoptysis, or a smoking history increasing suspicion of underlying tumour","Progressive bulbar or respiratory weakness requiring monitoring of airway and ventilation","Severe autonomic failure causing symptomatic hypotension or gastrointestinal stasis"],"prognosis":"Symptoms often improve with treatment of any underlying cancer and with therapies that enhance acetylcholine release; the overall outlook is dominated by the associated malignancy when present, whereas non-paraneoplastic disease tends to follow a more indolent, manageable course.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing LEMS as myasthenia gravis and missing the search for lung cancer","Stopping tumour surveillance too early after an initially negative chest scan","Overlooking autonomic symptoms that are an important diagnostic clue","Failing to appreciate post-exercise facilitation on examination and electrophysiology","Attributing proximal weakness to a primary myopathy without neuromuscular junction testing"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"langerhans_cell_histiocytosis":{"name":"Langerhans Cell Histiocytosis","system":"Haematology/Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LCH","histiocytosis X","eosinophilic granuloma","Hand-Schuller-Christian disease","Letterer-Siwe disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A clonal proliferative disorder of Langerhans-type dendritic cells that can range from a single isolated lesion to widespread multisystem disease.","Bone is the most commonly involved site, often presenting as a painful lytic lesion, classically in the skull but also in other flat and long bones.","Skin involvement can mimic seborrhoeic dermatitis or a persistent scaly, petechial rash, particularly in infants.","Diabetes insipidus from pituitary or hypothalamic involvement is a characteristic endocrine manifestation and may be the presenting feature.","Risk organ involvement (liver, spleen, and bone marrow) identifies higher-risk multisystem disease with greater mortality.","Diagnosis rests on biopsy showing characteristic cells expressing CD1a and CD207 (langerin); Birbeck granules may be seen on electron microscopy.","Many cases harbour activating mutations in the MAPK pathway, most commonly BRAF V600E, which has therapeutic implications.","Treatment ranges from observation or local therapy for single-system disease to systemic chemotherapy for multisystem or risk-organ disease."],"pathophysiology":"Langerhans cell histiocytosis is now understood as a myeloid neoplasm driven by activating mutations of the MAPK/ERK signalling pathway, most often BRAF V600E, in dendritic cell precursors. Constitutive pathway activation causes accumulation of pathological CD1a-positive, langerin-positive histiocytes that, together with eosinophils and other inflammatory cells, form granulomatous infiltrates. These lesions destroy local tissue (such as lytic bone lesions) and, when disseminated, impair function of organs including the liver, spleen, marrow, and pituitary.","additionalDifferentials":["Osteomyelitis or Ewing sarcoma (for bone lesions)","Leukaemia or lymphoma","Neuroblastoma with bony metastases","Seborrhoeic or atopic dermatitis (for skin lesions)","Other histiocytic disorders (e.g. haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, juvenile xanthogranuloma)","Chronic infection (e.g. tuberculosis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Biopsy with immunohistochemistry for CD1a and CD207 (langerin)","BRAF V600E and broader MAPK pathway mutation testing","Skeletal imaging survey (radiographs and/or PET) to map bone lesions","Assessment of risk organs: liver and spleen imaging, full blood count, liver function","Endocrine evaluation and MRI of the pituitary/hypothalamus if diabetes insipidus is suspected","Baseline evaluation for pulmonary and CNS involvement when indicated"],"redFlags":["Risk-organ (liver, spleen, marrow) involvement indicating high-risk multisystem disease","Cytopenias suggesting marrow infiltration","Polyuria and polydipsia indicating diabetes insipidus from CNS/pituitary disease","Rapidly progressive multisystem disease in an infant","Neurodegenerative CNS involvement causing progressive neurological decline"],"prognosis":"Single-system disease, especially isolated bone lesions, often has an excellent prognosis and may even regress; multisystem disease with risk-organ involvement carries higher mortality. Survivors are at risk of long-term sequelae such as diabetes insipidus, other endocrinopathies, orthopaedic problems, and neurodegeneration.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the infantile skin rash for simple seborrhoeic dermatitis and delaying diagnosis","Overlooking diabetes insipidus as an early or isolated manifestation","Assuming a solitary lytic bone lesion is infection or a benign cyst without biopsy","Failing to assess for risk-organ involvement, which changes treatment intensity and prognosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"laryngitis":{"name":"Laryngitis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute laryngitis","chronic laryngitis","laryngeal inflammation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Inflammation of the larynx presenting chiefly with hoarseness or voice change, often with throat discomfort and a dry cough","Acute laryngitis is usually viral and self-limiting, frequently accompanying an upper respiratory tract infection","Chronic laryngitis develops over weeks and is linked to vocal abuse, smoking, reflux, chronic sinus infection and occupational irritants","Voice rest and hydration are central to recovery in acute cases, as continued voice use aggravates the inflamed cords","Reflux laryngitis (laryngopharyngeal reflux) can cause posterior laryngeal inflammation, throat clearing and a globus sensation","Management principles emphasise treating the underlying cause, vocal hygiene, and addressing irritants such as smoking and reflux","In young children, inflammatory laryngeal narrowing can cause croup or, in acute epiglottitis, life-threatening airway obstruction","Hoarseness persisting beyond a few weeks, particularly in a smoker, requires laryngeal visualisation to exclude malignancy"],"pathophysiology":"Laryngitis is inflammation of the laryngeal mucosa, especially the vocal folds. In acute laryngitis, viral (or sometimes bacterial) infection produces mucosal hyperaemia and oedema that increase the mass and stiffness of the vocal folds, disturbing their vibratory pattern and causing hoarseness. In chronic laryngitis, sustained irritation from voice overuse, tobacco smoke, gastric reflux or persistent infection leads to ongoing mucosal changes such as oedema, thickening and keratosis. In children, the narrow subglottis means that inflammatory swelling can significantly compromise the airway.","additionalDifferentials":["Vocal cord nodules or polyps","Laryngeal carcinoma","Vocal cord palsy","Laryngopharyngeal reflux","Croup or acute epiglottitis in children","Functional or muscle tension dysphonia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Laryngoscopy (indirect, flexible or videostroboscopy) to inspect the vocal folds","Assessment for reflux and its contribution to symptoms","Voice assessment where vocal misuse is suspected","Biopsy of suspicious lesions to exclude malignancy","Imaging where deep infection, mass or airway compromise is a concern"],"redFlags":["Stridor or respiratory difficulty, especially in a child, suggesting airway compromise or epiglottitis","Hoarseness persisting beyond a few weeks in a smoker raising concern for laryngeal cancer","Associated dysphagia, weight loss, haemoptysis or a neck mass","Rapidly worsening symptoms with drooling and toxaemia"],"prognosis":"Acute laryngitis usually resolves within days to a couple of weeks with conservative measures, whereas chronic laryngitis requires removal of the underlying irritant for improvement; persistent hoarseness always warrants exclusion of a serious cause.","pitfalls":["Attributing persistent hoarseness to simple laryngitis and delaying laryngoscopy in a smoker","Overlooking laryngopharyngeal reflux as a driver of chronic symptoms","Underestimating airway risk in a child with laryngeal inflammation","Failing to address vocal misuse and continued irritant exposure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"laryngomalacia":{"name":"Laryngomalacia","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["floppy larynx","congenital laryngeal stridor","soft larynx"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Laryngomalacia is the most common cause of chronic stridor in infants and results from collapse of soft, immature supraglottic structures during inspiration.","The typical presentation is inspiratory stridor that begins in the first weeks of life, worsens with feeding, crying, agitation, or lying supine, and improves when the infant is calm or prone.","In most infants the condition is mild and self-limiting, with symptoms peaking in the early months and resolving as the airway matures, usually within the first one to two years.","Flexible laryngoscopy in the awake infant is the diagnostic gold standard, demonstrating the characteristic dynamic supraglottic collapse.","Gastro-oesophageal reflux commonly coexists and can aggravate symptoms, so it is often addressed as part of management.","Feeding difficulty, choking or coughing with feeds, and poor weight gain indicate more significant disease and warrant closer evaluation.","Severe disease with significant obstruction, apnoea, or failure to thrive may require surgical intervention such as supraglottoplasty.","Persistent or atypical stridor should prompt evaluation for additional or synchronous airway lesions."],"pathophysiology":"In laryngomalacia the supraglottic tissues, including the epiglottis and aryepiglottic folds, are excessively soft and poorly supported, and there is often associated neuromuscular immaturity affecting airway tone and coordination. During inspiration the negative airway pressure draws these floppy structures inward, causing dynamic collapse over the laryngeal inlet and producing turbulent airflow and inspiratory stridor. The obstruction is worse when airflow demand or negative pressure increases, such as with crying, feeding, or agitation, and lessens with maturation as the tissues become firmer and neuromuscular control improves. Coexisting reflux can worsen supraglottic oedema and symptoms.","additionalDifferentials":["Vocal cord paralysis","Subglottic stenosis","Vascular ring or other extrinsic airway compression","Laryngeal web, cyst, or haemangioma","Tracheomalacia","Acquired causes of stridor such as croup or foreign body"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Flexible awake laryngoscopy","Assessment of feeding and growth","Pulse oximetry or sleep study if obstruction or apnoea is suspected","Bronchoscopy or airway endoscopy to exclude synchronous lesions in severe or atypical cases","Evaluation for gastro-oesophageal reflux when contributory"],"redFlags":["Cyanosis, apnoea, or significant desaturation","Marked work of breathing or worsening obstruction","Feeding difficulty with choking, aspiration, or failure to thrive","Stridor that is severe, progressive, or atypical for laryngomalacia","Life-threatening obstructive episodes"],"prognosis":"The outlook is excellent for the majority, in whom mild laryngomalacia resolves spontaneously as the airway matures; a minority with severe disease require surgery, which is generally effective.","pitfalls":["Assuming all infant stridor is laryngomalacia and missing another airway lesion","Underestimating severity by not assessing feeding, growth, and oxygenation","Overlooking a synchronous second airway abnormality","Failing to recognise and manage contributing gastro-oesophageal reflux"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"latent_autoimmune_diabetes":{"name":"Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LADA","latent autoimmune diabetes of adults","type 1.5 diabetes","slowly progressive autoimmune diabetes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A slowly progressive form of autoimmune diabetes presenting in adulthood, often mistaken for type 2 diabetes at diagnosis","Distinguishing features include adult onset, the presence of islet autoantibodies (typically anti-GAD), and a lack of the marked obesity and insulin resistance often seen in type 2 diabetes","Unlike classic type 1 diabetes, beta-cell destruction is gradual, so patients may not need insulin for months to years after diagnosis","It sits on a spectrum between type 1 and type 2 diabetes and is sometimes called type 1.5 diabetes","Suspect it when a leaner adult with apparent type 2 diabetes shows early failure of oral agents or unexpectedly rapid progression to insulin dependence","Anti-GAD antibodies are the most commonly positive marker and support the diagnosis","There may be an association with other organ-specific autoimmune conditions such as thyroid disease","Recognition matters because these patients progress to insulin requirement and benefit from monitoring beta-cell reserve rather than assuming a stable type 2 course"],"pathophysiology":"Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults is an autoimmune diabetes in which immune-mediated destruction of pancreatic beta cells proceeds more slowly than in classic childhood type 1 diabetes. Circulating islet autoantibodies, most often against glutamic acid decarboxylase, reflect this ongoing autoimmune process. Because residual beta-cell function persists at diagnosis, patients initially maintain some endogenous insulin secretion and may be controlled without insulin, but progressive loss of beta-cell mass leads to eventual insulin dependence. The disorder shares genetic and immunological features with type 1 diabetes while its adult onset and initial non-insulin-requiring course cause frequent misclassification as type 2 diabetes.","additionalDifferentials":["Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Classic type 1 diabetes mellitus","Maturity-onset diabetes of the young","Ketosis-prone diabetes","Secondary diabetes from pancreatic disease","Drug- or steroid-induced hyperglycaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Islet autoantibody testing, particularly anti-GAD antibodies","C-peptide to gauge residual beta-cell function and its decline over time","Standard glycaemic assessment including HbA1c","Screening for associated autoimmune disease such as thyroid dysfunction","Lipid profile and assessment of features of insulin resistance to help differentiate from type 2 diabetes","Periodic reassessment of beta-cell reserve to anticipate insulin requirement"],"redFlags":["Development of ketosis or ketoacidosis indicating significant insulin deficiency","Rapid deterioration of glycaemic control on oral agents","Unexplained weight loss suggesting advancing insulin deficiency","Coexisting autoimmune disorders such as adrenal or thyroid disease"],"prognosis":"Patients generally progress to insulin dependence over months to years as beta-cell function declines; with timely recognition and appropriate transition to insulin, glycaemic control and complication risk can be managed similarly to other forms of diabetes, and monitoring for associated autoimmune conditions is advisable.","pitfalls":["Assuming all adult-onset diabetes is type 2 and missing the autoimmune nature","Delaying insulin when oral agents are clearly failing","Not testing autoantibodies in a leaner adult with atypical type 2 diabetes","Overlooking associated autoimmune endocrine disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"latex_allergy":{"name":"Latex allergy","system":"Immunology / Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["natural rubber latex allergy","latex hypersensitivity","NRL allergy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Middleton's Allergy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["IgE-mediated (type I) reactions to natural rubber latex proteins range from contact urticaria to rhinoconjunctivitis, asthma, and anaphylaxis, and are distinct from irritant or allergic contact dermatitis from rubber-processing chemicals","Healthcare workers, patients with spina bifida or urogenital anomalies, and those with repeated surgical or catheter exposure are at highest risk owing to frequent mucosal and cutaneous contact","Powdered gloves historically amplified sensitisation because latex allergen adsorbed to cornstarch became airborne, so a shift to powder-free low-protein or non-latex gloves has markedly reduced new cases","The latex-fruit syndrome reflects cross-reactivity between latex proteins and plant allergens, so patients may also react to banana, avocado, kiwi, and chestnut","Diagnosis combines a compatible history with skin testing and serum latex-specific IgE; a validated skin test extract may not be uniformly available, making the history and in-vitro testing important","Reactions can occur through skin, mucosal, inhalational, or parenteral routes, and intraoperative anaphylaxis may be the presenting event when latex is contacted internally","Cornerstones of care are strict avoidance, a latex-safe environment for procedures, medical-alert identification, and a self-injectable adrenaline device for those with systemic reactions","Type IV allergic contact dermatitis from rubber accelerators (e.g. thiurams, carbamates) presents as delayed eczema and is confirmed by patch testing, not by IgE tests"],"pathophysiology":"Type I latex allergy results from IgE sensitisation to water-soluble proteins in natural rubber harvested from Hevea brasiliensis. Repeated cutaneous, mucosal, or airborne exposure drives production of latex-specific IgE bound to mast cells and basophils; re-exposure cross-links this IgE and triggers mediator release causing urticaria, bronchospasm, and anaphylaxis. Airborne allergen bound to glove powder facilitates respiratory sensitisation. Structural homology between latex proteins (e.g. hevein-related and profilin allergens) and plant proteins accounts for cross-reactivity in the latex-fruit syndrome. A separate delayed, T-cell-mediated contact dermatitis is caused not by latex protein but by rubber-manufacturing chemicals.","additionalDifferentials":["Irritant contact dermatitis from gloves or hand hygiene","Type IV allergic contact dermatitis to rubber accelerator chemicals","Anaphylaxis from perioperative drugs, antiseptics (chlorhexidine), or antibiotics","Food-related latex-fruit cross-reactive allergy","Idiopathic urticaria or angioedema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum latex-specific IgE testing","Skin prick testing with a standardised latex extract where available","Patch testing for suspected delayed contact dermatitis to rubber chemicals","Serum tryptase during a suspected acute systemic reaction","Supervised glove-use or challenge testing in specialist settings when the diagnosis is uncertain"],"redFlags":["Intraoperative or procedural anaphylaxis with hypotension or bronchospasm requiring immediate intramuscular adrenaline and removal of all latex sources","Progressive airway or respiratory symptoms after mucosal or inhalational latex exposure","Systemic reaction in a high-risk patient (spina bifida, multiple surgeries) not previously identified as latex-allergic","Recurrent reactions despite avoidance suggesting unrecognised hidden latex exposure"],"prognosis":"With reliable avoidance and a latex-safe healthcare environment, most patients avoid further reactions and occupational sensitisation often improves after exposure ceases. Unrecognised sensitisation, however, can lead to severe or fatal perioperative anaphylaxis.","pitfalls":["Confusing delayed contact dermatitis from rubber chemicals with true IgE-mediated latex allergy","Failing to arrange a latex-safe environment for surgery or dental and radiological procedures","Overlooking cross-reactive foods in the latex-fruit syndrome","Not counselling the patient to carry medical-alert identification and an adrenaline device"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"legionellosis":{"name":"Legionellosis (Legionnaires' Disease)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Legionnaires disease","Pontiac fever"],"chapter":164,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1269-1276","clinicalPearls":["Legionella species cause two distinct illnesses collectively termed legionellosis: Legionella pneumonia (Legionnaires' disease) and Pontiac fever; most clinical disease is driven by L. pneumophila, predominantly serotype 1 (Harrison 22e p.1269).","Almost all cases trace to waterborne exposure; aerosolizing or aspiration sources include cooling towers, fountains, water misters, HVAC systems, hot tubs/spas, ice machines, and showerheads/sinks in large premise-plumbing systems (Harrison 22e p.1270-1271).","Legionella pneumonia presents as an 'atypical pneumonia' with nonspecific onset of fever, myalgias, headache, breathlessness, and dry or productive cough; accompanying neurologic or gastrointestinal symptoms (anorexia, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, confusion) raise the likelihood of legionellosis (Harrison 22e p.1272).","A characteristic laboratory cluster supports the diagnosis: hyponatremia and/or renal dysfunction are frequent, with elevated liver enzymes, and leukocytosis or leukopenia plus thrombocytopenia (Harrison 22e p.1272).","Per CDC, a single confirmed Legionella case in a patient who did not leave a health care facility/campus for the 10 days before onset should trigger an outbreak investigation, because nosocomial cases may signal water-system contamination (Harrison 22e p.1270-1273).","Pontiac fever is a milder, self-limited influenza-like illness defined by the ABSENCE of pneumonia, with fever, headache, myalgias, chills, vertigo, nausea/vomiting and diarrhea; antibiotics are not required (Harrison 22e p.1273)."],"pathophysiology":"Legionellae are aerobic gram-negative bacteria ubiquitous in aquatic environments, damp soil, and compost. Their natural hosts are amoebae and other free-living protozoa, within which they replicate intracellularly; humans are accidental hosts. They have a biphasic life cycle (a nutrient-rich replicative phase and a transmissive phase under resource scarcity) and persist in biofilms within natural and engineered water systems, including building premise plumbing where temperature and nutrients support protozoal hosts. After inhalation or aspiration of small aerosol particles, the organisms are phagocytized by alveolar macrophages and monocytes; pathogenic species evade intracellular defenses (forming Legionella-containing vacuoles, downregulating cytokine receptors, inhibiting host protein synthesis, and avoiding lysosomal degradation) and replicate. Innate pattern-recognition receptors (Toll-like and NOD-like receptors) together with adaptive CD4 and CD8 T-cell responses drive interferon-gamma, TNF, and neutrophil recruitment, which can clear the pathogen but may also produce immunopathology. L. pneumophila is more cytopathogenic than most non-pneumophila species, which may underlie its association with severe disease (Harrison 22e p.1269).","additionalDifferentials":["Other atypical-pneumonia pathogens, notably Mycoplasma pneumoniae and Chlamydia pneumoniae (Harrison 22e p.1272)"],"infectionMimics":["In immunocompromised patients, Legionella can present as pulmonary nodules with or without cavitation that mimic fungal infection, or as lung abscesses (Harrison 22e p.1273)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Lower-respiratory culture (sputum, BAL fluid, lung tissue, or extrapulmonary sites) on cysteine-supplemented buffered charcoal yeast extract (BCYE) agar is the gold standard and is essential for epidemiologic typing; growth is slow (typically 3-5 days, longer for non-pneumophila species) (Harrison 22e p.1273-1274)","Polymerase chain reaction / nucleic acid amplification (including LAMP and multiplex respiratory panels) on lower-tract specimens is more sensitive than culture and remains useful during antibiotic therapy, but does not provide serotype information and most assays target only L. pneumophila (Harrison 22e p.1274-1275)","Bronchoscopy when urinary antigen is negative and the patient cannot produce adequate sputum, or in severe disease requiring ICU care (Harrison 22e p.1273)","Serology (paired acute/convalescent titers; fourfold rise to L. pneumophila serogroup 1 confirms) has limited acute sensitivity but aids outbreak investigation (Harrison 22e p.1274)","Direct fluorescent antibody testing of sputum has low and variable sensitivity (~20-70%) and is not recommended for low-risk screening due to false positives (Harrison 22e p.1274)"],"redFlags":["Severe pneumonia with acute respiratory failure or a sepsis-like presentation warrants testing for Legionella (Harrison 22e p.1272)","Pneumonia developing during hospitalization, after recent travel, or during a known or possible outbreak should prompt Legionella testing (Harrison 22e p.1272)","Immunosuppressed patients may lack typical features such as fever and may show radiographic progression during early therapy (Harrison 22e p.1272-1273)"],"prognosis":"Legionella pneumonia carries significant morbidity and mortality, with most patients hospitalized and many requiring ICU admission. The reported case-fatality rate is about 10%, rising to roughly 25% in health care-associated L. pneumophila pneumonia and to approximately 30-50% in immunocompromised hosts. Delayed antibiotic treatment is associated with about a threefold higher mortality. More than a quarter of survivors of severe disease have lasting complications, including recurrent hospitalizations, acute renal failure, respiratory sequelae, and recurrent pneumonias. By contrast, Pontiac fever is self-limited, usually resolving within 3-5 days, with hospitalization, complications, and death extremely rare (Harrison 22e p.1275).","pitfalls":["Urinary antigen testing detects ONLY L. pneumophila serotype 1; it gives false-negative results in most cases caused by non-serotype 1 L. pneumophila and non-pneumophila species, so a negative result does not exclude legionellosis (sensitivity for serotype 1 is ~70%, though specificity is high) (Harrison 22e p.1274).","Urinary antigen can be falsely negative very early in disease yet remain positive for months after infection (especially in immunosuppressed patients), and it cannot be used in anuric patients (Harrison 22e p.1274).","Because community-acquired pneumonia guidelines recommend empirical coverage active against Legionella, specific testing is often skipped and cultures are frequently collected only after antibiotics, reducing yield (Harrison 22e p.1273-1274).","Diagnosis on clinical grounds alone is unreliable; clinical-prediction tools (e.g., Winthrop-University Hospital Criteria, Legionella Score) are more useful for their negative than their positive predictive value (Harrison 22e p.1272).","Routine testing is less common in children, so pediatric cases may be underreported; immunosuppressed patients may present without fever or pulmonary exam findings (Harrison 22e p.1271-1272)."],"severityClassification":"Treatment is stratified by severity: Pontiac fever requires no antibiotics (supportive care only), whereas Legionella pneumonia is managed as mild versus moderate/severe disease. All immunosuppressed patients are considered to have moderate or severe disease and should receive IV therapy where possible; all patients needing inpatient care should receive IV therapy until improving before switching to an oral agent. CDC distinguishes health care-associated cases as presumptive (Legionella pneumonia after >=10 days continuous stay in a health care facility during the 14 days before symptom onset) versus possible (some health care exposure in that window but insufficient to meet presumptive criteria) (Harrison 22e p.1272-1275).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1269-1276"],"crossLinks":[]},"leishmaniasis":{"name":"Leishmaniasis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["kala-azar","visceral leishmaniasis"],"chapter":233,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1781-1788","clinicalPearls":["Caused by obligate intracellular protozoa of the genus Leishmania (~20 species), transmitted by phlebotomine sandflies (Phlebotomus in the Old World, Lutzomyia in the New World); syndromes fall into three categories: visceral (VL), cutaneous (CL), and mucosal (ML) (p.1781).","Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar, Hindi for 'black fever') is caused by the L. donovani complex (L. donovani, L. infantum); classic presentation is abrupt moderate-to-high fever with rigors, progressive massive splenomegaly, moderate hepatomegaly, weight loss/wasting, and skin hyperpigmentation (p.1782-1783).","Lymphadenopathy is common in most VL-endemic regions but is rare on the Indian subcontinent (p.1783).","VL causes pancytopenia from bone marrow dysfunction and reticuloendothelial infiltration: leukopenia and anemia appear early, followed by thrombocytopenia; there is a marked polyclonal increase in serum immunoglobulins (hypergammaglobulinemia) (p.1782-1783).","Fever with neutropenia or pancytopenia in a patient from an endemic region strongly suggests VL; hypergammaglobulinemia in long-standing illness strengthens the diagnosis (p.1784).","Demonstration of amastigotes in tissue aspirate smears is the gold standard for VL: splenic smear sensitivity is >95%, bone marrow 60-85%, lymph node aspirate ~50% (p.1783).","The rK39 rapid immunochromatographic antibody test is used worldwide for VL field diagnosis (sensitivity ~98%, specificity ~90% in immunocompetent individuals; both lower in East Africa) (p.1783).","Cutaneous leishmaniasis: a sandfly bite is followed days-to-weeks later by a papule that becomes a nodule and ulcerates over weeks-to-months, with raised, indurated margins and a usually painless base; most lesions heal spontaneously over 2-15 months, leaving a scar (p.1786).","Mucosal leishmaniasis (espundia) is typically caused by L. (Viannia) braziliensis and is almost entirely confined to the Americas; it presents with nasal stuffiness and bleeding followed by destruction of nasal cartilage, septal perforation, and collapse of the nasal bridge (p.1787-1788).","Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis (PKDL) develops in 2-50% of patients during or after cure of VL on the Indian subcontinent and in East Africa, presenting as hypopigmented macules, papules, nodules, or diffuse skin infiltration (p.1785).","Visceral leishmaniasis is a recognized cause of secondary hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH); Leishmania is the most common parasite associated with HLH (p.1369-1370)."],"pathophysiology":"Sandflies inoculate flagellate promastigotes into the skin; neutrophils first take up the organisms, then macrophages and dendritic cells engulf them, and parasites multiply as nonflagellate amastigotes within macrophages, rupturing cells and invading new macrophages. Acquired resistance depends on a T helper 1 (TH1) response driven by IL-12, IFN-gamma, and TNF-alpha; most infected individuals control infection and never develop symptomatic disease. In active VL there is markedly elevated IL-10, which conditions macrophages for parasite survival, downregulates TNF-alpha and nitric oxide, and suppresses antigen presentation, so patients lack a positive leishmanin (Montenegro) skin test. In VL the reticuloendothelial organs (spleen, liver, lymph nodes, tonsils, intestinal submucosa) are heavily parasitized, and bone marrow dysfunction produces pancytopenia. Diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis reflects an absent cell-mediated/DTH response with high IL-10, TGF-beta, and IL-4, allowing unchecked parasite multiplication (p.1781-1786).","additionalDifferentials":["Malaria (VL is easily mistaken for it)","Typhoid (enteric) fever","Tuberculosis","Brucellosis","Schistosomiasis","Histoplasmosis","Splenomegaly from portal hypertension","Chronic myeloid leukemia","Tropical splenomegaly syndrome (hyperreactive malarial splenomegaly)","For CL: cutaneous tuberculosis, fungal infections, leprosy, sarcoidosis, and malignant ulcers"],"infectionMimics":["Malaria","Typhoid fever","Tuberculosis","Brucellosis","Schistosomiasis","Histoplasmosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Splenomegaly from portal hypertension","Chronic myeloid leukemia","Tropical splenomegaly syndrome","Malignant ulcers (for cutaneous disease)","Sarcoidosis (for cutaneous disease)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Splenic aspirate smear (highest sensitivity >95%, but invasive and potentially dangerous)","Bone marrow aspirate smear (sensitivity 60-85%)","Lymph node aspirate smear (sensitivity ~50%)","Culture of tissue aspirates (increases sensitivity)","rK39 rapid immunochromatographic antibody test (fingerprick blood/serum, result in ~15 min)","ELISA and indirect immunofluorescent antibody test (IFAT)","Qualitative and quantitative (real-time) PCR for leishmanial nucleic acid (allows species identification)","Loop-mediated isothermal amplification (LAMP) of nucleic acids (point-of-care for VL and PKDL)","Slit skin smears, lesion aspirates/biopsies for CL; tissue biopsy plus PCR for ML"],"redFlags":["Massive (hugely enlarged) splenomegaly with fever and pancytopenia in a patient from or returning from an endemic region","Severe anemia, which may cause congestive heart failure","Bleeding (epistaxis, retinal hemorrhages, gastrointestinal bleeding) from thrombocytopenia","Hypoalbuminemia with pedal edema and ascites in advanced illness","HIV co-infection (untreated VL is fatal in 100% of HIV-co-infected patients)","Secondary infections (pneumonia, tuberculosis, dysentery, gastroenteritis)","In ML: destruction of nasal cartilage/septal perforation; aspiration pneumonia may be fatal"],"prognosis":"Untreated visceral leishmaniasis is fatal in most patients, including 100% of those with HIV co-infection. With effective treatment, recovery is quick: defervescence, regression of splenomegaly, weight gain, and hematologic recovery occur within a week, and continued improvement over 12 months suggests cure; a small percentage relapse (usually within 6-12 months) but respond to retreatment. Cutaneous lesions heal spontaneously in most cases (2-15 months), leaving a scar, but may cause permanent scarring and social stigma. Diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis does not heal spontaneously and is difficult to treat. Mucosal leishmaniasis does not heal spontaneously despite strong TH1/DTH immunity; the more extensive the disease, the worse the prognosis (p.1782-1788).","pitfalls":["VL is easily mistaken for malaria and other tropical febrile illnesses; a careful travel history is essential in nonendemic countries.","Antibody detection tests (rK39, ELISA, IFAT) remain positive for years after cure, so they cannot be used to assess cure or detect relapse, and positive serology does not help diagnose PKDL.","In HIV/VL co-infection serodiagnostic tests may be negative in up to 50% of patients, and parasites may be found in unusual sites (e.g., bronchoalveolar lavage fluid, buffy coat).","Splenic aspiration, though most sensitive, is invasive and may be dangerous in untrained hands.","In PKDL, parasites are scanty and skin smear sensitivity is low, especially in hypopigmented macules (parasites are more readily found in nodular lesions).","Patients with active VL and IL-10-mediated suppression do not have a positive leishmanin (Montenegro) skin test.","In ML the strongly positive DTH response fails to distinguish past from present infection."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal grading system; leishmaniasis is classified clinically by syndrome: visceral leishmaniasis (VL/kala-azar), cutaneous leishmaniasis (CL, with Old World and New World forms, plus leishmaniasis recidivans and diffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis [DCL]), and mucosal leishmaniasis (ML). Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis (PKDL) is a sequel of VL.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1781-1788"],"crossLinks":[]},"leprosy":{"name":"Leprosy (Hansen's Disease)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Hansen's disease"],"chapter":184,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1404","clinicalPearls":["Leprosy is a chronic infectious disease caused by Mycobacterium leprae, with manifestations largely confined to skin, peripheral nerves, eyes, and the upper respiratory tract; M. leprae has a predilection for peripheral nerves (p.1404).","Diagnosis rests on three cardinal signs, of which at least one establishes the diagnosis: (1) a hypopigmented or erythematous skin lesion with definite loss/impairment of sensation, (2) involvement of peripheral nerves shown by definite thickening with sensory impairment, and (3) a positive result for acid-fast bacilli on slit-skin smear or biopsy, or a positive biopsy PCR (p.1409-1410).","The differing immune responses to M. leprae produce a clinical spectrum; immunologic resistance is strong at the tuberculoid end and weakest at the lepromatous end (p.1404, p.1406).","Skin surfaces in tuberculoid lesions are dry, hairless, and anaesthetic owing to destruction of underlying cutaneous nerves, plus autonomic damage causing loss of sweating (p.1407).","Slit-skin smear bacteriologic index runs from negative (tuberculoid) up to 4+ to 6+ with globi (lepromatous), and falls an average of ~1 log unit per year on multidrug therapy (p.1404, p.1407-1408).","Leprosy is the principal treatable cause of peripheral neuropathy worldwide in some settings; nerve enlargement is often palpable (e.g., greater auricular, ulnar, median, radial, lateral popliteal, posterior tibial, sural, superficial peroneal) (p.1409-1410).","Treatment principle: WHO-recommended multidrug therapy (a combination of rifampin, dapsone, and clofazimine) is the standard of care and cures the disease; paucibacillary disease uses two drugs and multibacillary disease uses three, with the cure rate ~99% (no doses given here) (p.1410, p.1412).","M. leprae has never been grown in artificial media; it is propagated only in animal models (mouse footpad, armadillo), which underlies the difficulty of culture-based diagnosis (p.1404)."],"pathophysiology":"Leprosy is caused by Mycobacterium leprae, an obligate intracellular, acid-fast, rod-shaped bacterium with a preferred growth temperature of 27-30 degrees C, explaining its predilection for cooler surface tissues (skin, peripheral nerves, testes, upper airways) and relative sparing of inner viscera. After entry (assumed mainly via the respiratory tract, sometimes through damaged skin), the pathogenic process usually begins in peripheral nerves once bacilli are engulfed by Schwann cells. The histopathologic outcome - and thus the type of leprosy - depends on the host cell-mediated immune (CMI) response. The Ridley-Jopling spectrum (TT, BT, BB, BL, LL, with indeterminate IL as an early form) reflects this: at the tuberculoid pole, strong CMI produces epithelioid/giant-cell granulomas that destroy nerves (causing anaesthesia and muscle weakness) but contain few bacilli; toward the lepromatous pole, weak CMI permits unrestricted multiplication of bacilli within macrophages (lepra cells) as clumps/globi, with widespread dissemination and high bacterial load. Tissue damage is largely due to delayed hypersensitivity. M. leprae carries the species-specific phenolic glycolipid PGL-1, used in serodiagnosis. M. lepromatosis is a related species associated with diffuse leprosy of Lucio and Latapi.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypopigmented macules: pityriasis alba, vitiligo, progressive macular hypomelanosis, pityriasis versicolor, pityriasis rosea, postinflammatory hypopigmentation, sarcoidosis, post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis, morphea (p.1410)","Plaque/nodular lesions: granuloma annulare, cutaneous sarcoidosis, cutaneous leishmaniasis, lupus miliaris disseminatus faciei, nodular histiocytosis, lupus erythematosus, cutaneous T-cell lymphomas (especially mycosis fungoides), secondary syphilis (p.1410)","ENL (type 2 reaction) lesions: erythema nodosum of other etiologies, nodular vasculitis, cutaneous polyarteritis nodosa (p.1410)","Mononeuropathy: diabetes, amyloidosis, myxedema (p.1410)","Acute-onset polyneuropathy: Guillain-Barre syndrome, toxic polyneuropathy (p.1410)"],"infectionMimics":["Cutaneous leishmaniasis (p.1410)","Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis (p.1410)","Secondary syphilis (p.1410)","Pityriasis versicolor (p.1410)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Vitiligo (p.1410)","Sarcoidosis / cutaneous sarcoidosis (p.1410)","Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma, especially mycosis fungoides (p.1410)","Lupus erythematosus (p.1410)","Granuloma annulare (p.1410)","Diabetic, amyloid, or myxedema-related mononeuropathy (p.1410)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-skin smear from standard sites (right earlobe, forehead, chin, and left buttock/upper thigh), Ziehl-Neelsen stained, graded by bacteriologic index (0-6) and microbiologic index (p.1410)","Skin biopsy to confirm diagnosis, classify disease, support diagnosis of reactions, and assess cure; immunochemical staining for M. leprae antigens aids detection of early lesions (p.1410)","PCR / gene amplification (conventional, RT-PCR, multiplex targeting RLEP, 16S rRNA, SodA, antigen genes) - useful in bacteriologic-index-negative cases and for subclinical infection in contacts (p.1410)","PGL-1 antibody testing (ELISA, ML flow lateral-flow assay, UPC-LFA) - more often positive in multibacillary than paucibacillary disease (p.1410)","Lepromin (Mitsuda) skin test - measures cellular immunity; negative in LL/BL leprosy (p.1410)","Nerve function assessment: Semmes-Weinstein monofilament (or WHO-recommended ballpoint pen) touch testing, voluntary muscle testing, nerve conduction studies, and ultrasound of peripheral nerves (p.1410-1411)"],"redFlags":["Type 1 (reversal) reaction: delayed-hypersensitivity reaction with sudden upgrading of CMI; acute swelling and redness of existing lesions, painful/tender nerves with neuritis; can cause silent neuritis and irreversible sensory/motor nerve damage - a medical urgency to prevent disability (p.1407, p.1408-1409)","Type 2 reaction / erythema nodosum leprosum (ENL): immune-complex-mediated syndrome with crops of painful red nodules, fever, malaise, and possible neuritis, iridocyclitis, orchitis, nephritis/renal failure, and (late) amyloidosis (p.1408-1409)","Neuritis with nerve function impairment, including 'silent neuritis' (progressive sensory/motor loss without pain or tenderness) - requires prompt treatment to avoid permanent disability and blindness (p.1409, p.1410-1411)","Lucio's phenomenon in diffuse leprosy of Lucio and Latapi: vasculitis and thrombosis causing hemorrhagic skin infarction and necrotic ulcers (p.1409)","Eye involvement (corneal anaesthesia, iridocyclitis, lagophthalmos, corneal ulceration) threatening sight (p.1408)"],"prognosis":"Leprosy can be cured effectively: the cure rate with multidrug therapy is ~99% and treatment-failure rates are very low when therapy is completed. Relapse rates are well below 1% except in patients with a very high baseline bacillary load (bacteriologic index >=4), and relapses usually occur within 5 years of finishing therapy; antimicrobial resistance is rare, so relapsed multibacillary patients can be re-treated with the same regimen. The major determinant of long-term outcome is nerve damage and resulting disability, which drives stigma and social exclusion. Highest risk of (additional) nerve damage is in multibacillary patients with nerve impairment already present at diagnosis (up to ~65%); intermediate risk (~16%) in multibacillary patients without impairment or paucibacillary patients with impairment at diagnosis; and lowest (~3%) in paucibacillary patients without impairment. Leprosy reactions and new nerve damage can occur even after completing therapy, up to 3 years afterward. Cure (cessation of bacterial activity) must be distinguished from sequelae (nerve impairment, reactions, disabilities such as claw hand or neuropathic foot) that may persist and require chronic care.","pitfalls":["Leprosy is frequently diagnosed late - in non-endemic countries it is often misdiagnosed simply because it is not considered - increasing the risk of irreversible nerve damage and disability (p.1404, p.1410)","On the face, sensory impairment may be difficult to demonstrate because of generous bilateral sensory innervation, which can compensate for damage to certain nerve branches and obscure the indeterminate stage (p.1407, p.1409)","'Silent neuritis' / 'quiet nerve paralysis': nerve function can be lost progressively without pain, paresthesia, tenderness, or other signs of reaction - regular nerve function assessment is needed during and after therapy (p.1409, p.1411)","Distinguishing relapse from a type 1 reaction in paucibacillary disease can be difficult; a therapeutic glucocorticoid trial helps - improvement within 4 weeks favors T1R, lack of response favors relapse (p.1412)","Patients often equate cure with absence of symptoms, but in leprosy bacteriologic cure does not eliminate established nerve-impairment sequelae - this must be explained at completion of therapy (p.1412-1413)","Multidrug therapy for clinical leprosy has apparently not interrupted transmission, and subclinical/undiagnosed multibacillary cases are likely a main source of ongoing spread (p.1405)"],"severityClassification":"Ridley-Jopling classification (1962) defines five overlapping categories along the immunologic spectrum: tuberculoid (TT), borderline tuberculoid (BT), mid-borderline (BB), borderline lepromatous (BL), and lepromatous (LL), with indeterminate leprosy (IL) as an early form; immunologic resistance is strongest at TT and weakest at LL, with TT and LL relatively stable and BT/BB/BL unstable. For operational/treatment purposes the WHO simplified classification is used: paucibacillary disease = negative slit-skin smears at all sites (or, on clinical criteria, one to five skin lesions and no/only one involved nerve), and multibacillary disease = positive smear at any site (or six or more lesions and/or more than one involved nerve). The WHO disability grading system scores eyes, hands, and feet (grades 0-2), summed into the Eye-Hand-Foot (EHF) score as an overall impairment indicator (p.1405-1406).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1404"],"crossLinks":[]},"less_common_lymphoid_and_myeloid":{"name":"Less Common Lymphoid and Myeloid Malignancies","system":"Hematologic / Oncologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Rare lymphoid malignancies","Rare myeloid malignancies","Unusual hematologic neoplasms"],"chapter":115,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.871","clinicalPearls":["This chapter covers the uncommon hematologic neoplasms, each individually accounting for under 1% of hematologic malignancies; the rare lymphoid tumors are all mature B-cell, T-cell, or NK-cell neoplasms (Harrison 22e p.871).","Splenic marginal zone lymphoma cells express surface immunoglobulin and CD20 but are negative for CD5, CD10, CD43, and CD103; absence of CD5 separates it from CLL and absence of CD103 separates it from hairy cell leukemia, and circulating cells are villous lymphocytes (Harrison 22e p.872).","Hairy cell leukemia is a frequent cause of a 'dry tap' on marrow aspiration because of increased reticulin fibers; it shows profound monocytopenia (predisposing to atypical mycobacterial infection), strong CD22/CD25/CD103 expression, tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase positivity, and the activating BRAF V600E mutation in most cases (Harrison 22e p.872-873).","Mediastinal (thymic) large B-cell lymphoma typically remains confined to the mediastinum, affects women more than men (ratio about 1:2-3) at a median age of 35-40 years, is CD20 positive and CD45 positive (unlike classical Hodgkin's), and may lack surface immunoglobulin and HLA class I/II expression (Harrison 22e p.873).","Primary effusion lymphoma presents with effusions usually without a tumor mass, is caused by HHV-8/KSHV (often EBV co-infected), occurs mainly in immunodeficiency such as AIDS, and follows a rapidly fatal course (Harrison 22e p.873).","T-cell prolymphocytic leukemia is aggressive with very high WBC counts often exceeding 100,000/uL, hepatosplenomegaly, and adenopathy; chromosome 14 inversion between q11 and q32 occurs in about 80% of patients (Harrison 22e p.874).","T-cell large granular lymphocytic leukemia is dominated by severe neutropenia and is frequently associated with systemic autoimmune disease such as rheumatoid arthritis; pure red cell aplasia occurs in 15-20% and the course is generally indolent (Harrison 22e p.874).","Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type, is an EBV-related angiocentric/angiodestructive CD56-positive lesion prevalent in Asia, Mexico, and Central/South America; historic names include lethal midline granuloma and polymorphic reticulosis (Harrison 22e p.874).","Enteropathy-type T-cell lymphoma is a rare complication of longstanding celiac disease, usually arising in jejunum or ileum, with most patients carrying the celiac-associated HLA DQA1*0501 or DQB1*0201 genotype (Harrison 22e p.874).","Chronic neutrophilic leukemia is a clonal proliferation of mature neutrophils molecularly defined by activating CSF3R mutations (most commonly the membrane-proximal T618I, found in about 90% of cases); diagnosis requires excluding reactive neutrophilia and other myeloid neoplasms (Harrison 22e p.875-876).","Systemic mastocytosis diagnosis rests on a major criterion (multifocal aggregates of >=15 mast cells) or three minor criteria including >25% atypical mast cells, aberrant mast cell expression of CD25/CD2/CD30, KIT D816V or other activating KIT mutation, and serum tryptase >20 ng/mL (Harrison 22e p.881).","Transient myeloproliferative disorder occurs in about 10% of infants with Down syndrome, carries somatic GATA1 mutations, and either regresses spontaneously (about 75%) or progresses to acute megakaryoblastic leukemia (about 25%) (Harrison 22e p.879)."],"pathophysiology":"These entities are individually rare clonal neoplasms of the lymphoid or myeloid lineages. Rare lymphoid tumors are mature B-cell, T-cell, or NK-cell neoplasms; several B-cell variants of diffuse large B-cell lymphoma have distinctive biology, such as intravascular large B-cell lymphoma (confined to vessel lumens, postulated to reflect defective adhesion molecule and homing function with absent beta-1 integrin and ICAM-1), primary effusion lymphoma (HHV-8/KSHV-driven, frequently EBV co-infected), and lymphomatoid granulomatosis (an angiodestructive EBV-infected monoclonal B-cell proliferation outnumbered by reactive T cells). Many T/NK entities are EBV-associated and angiocentric. On the myeloid side, distinct molecular drivers define the entities: activating CSF3R mutations in chronic neutrophilic leukemia, GM-CSF hypersensitivity from dysregulated RAS/MAPK signaling (mutually exclusive RAS, PTPN11, NF1 mutations) in juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia, somatic GATA1 mutations in transient myeloproliferative disorder of Down syndrome, and constitutively active tyrosine kinase gene fusions (involving PDGFRA such as FIP1L1-PDGFRA, PDGFRB, FGFR1, ABL1, JAK2, FLT3) in eosinophilic myeloid/lymphoid neoplasms. Mastocytosis reflects neoplastic mast cell proliferation typically driven by KIT D816V. Dendritic/histiocytic neoplasms arise from antigen-presenting or antigen-processing cell lineages; Langerhans cell histiocytosis cells are S100+/CD1a+ with Birbeck granules and about 55% carry BRAF V600E.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (distinguished from splenic marginal zone lymphoma by CD5 positivity)","Mantle cell lymphoma (overlaps with B-cell prolymphocytic leukemia via CD5 and t(11;14))","Nodular sclerosing Hodgkin's disease (versus mediastinal large B-cell lymphoma; the latter is CD45 positive, lacks lacunar Reed-Sternberg variants)","Anaplastic large T-cell lymphoma with t(2;5) (versus primary cutaneous CD30+ T-cell lymphoma, which lacks t(2;5))","Chronic myeloid leukemia, BCR::ABL1 positive (versus atypical CML and chronic neutrophilic leukemia)"],"infectionMimics":["Reactive neutrophilia from infection or inflammation (mimics chronic neutrophilic leukemia)","Reactive monocytosis from tuberculosis, fungal infection, subacute bacterial endocarditis, viral or protozoal infection (mimics chronic myelomonocytic leukemia)","Hypereosinophilia from tissue-invasive helminths and other parasitic infection (mimics primary/clonal eosinophilic disorders)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Plasma cell neoplasms and metastatic solid tumors producing secondary neutrophilia (mimic chronic neutrophilic leukemia)","Connective tissue disease, sarcoidosis, lipid storage disorders, postsplenectomy state, and marrow regeneration after chemotherapy (mimic CMML monocytosis)","Allergic/vasculitic disease and drug reactions (mimic primary hypereosinophilia)","Hereditary alpha-tryptasemia (raises serum tryptase, confounding mastocytosis criteria)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood smear and bone marrow morphology with immunophenotyping/flow cytometry for lineage markers","Cytogenetics and molecular testing: CSF3R (CNL), KIT D816V (mastocytosis), GATA1 (transient myeloproliferative disorder), SETBP1, RAS-pathway mutations (JMML)","FISH or RT-PCR screening for PDGFRA and PDGFRB rearrangements in hypereosinophilia","Serum tryptase (mast cell disease; elevation also prompts FIP1L1-PDGFRA testing) and soluble CD25 as a hairy cell leukemia activity marker","T-cell receptor gene rearrangement studies and T-cell immunophenotyping in hypereosinophilia to detect lymphocytic-variant disease","Echocardiogram and serum troponin to screen for cardiac involvement in hypereosinophilic syndrome (apical thrombus, endocardial thickening, valve abnormality)","Electron microscopy/immunostaining for Birbeck granules, CD1a, S100, and langerin in Langerhans cell histiocytosis"],"redFlags":["Aggressive NK cell leukemia presenting with fever, pancytopenia, hepatosplenomegaly, hemophagocytosis, coagulopathy, or multiorgan failure","Intravascular large B-cell lymphoma with small-vessel occlusion, neurologic symptoms, and thrombus formation, often diagnosed only late or at autopsy","Hemophagocytic syndrome complicating extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma or subcutaneous panniculitis-like T-cell lymphoma (can drive a fulminant downhill course)","Cardiac involvement in hypereosinophilic syndrome (cardiomyopathy, ventricular apical thrombus, elevated troponin)","Mast cell organopathy C findings: cytopenias, hepatomegaly with portal hypertension/ascites, splenomegaly with hypersplenism, GI infiltration with malabsorption and weight loss, large osteolytic lesions with pathologic fractures","True mast cell leukemia, regarded as an oncologic emergency requiring expedited diagnosis","Bowel perforation from tumor response in treated enteropathy-type T-cell lymphoma"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary widely by entity. Indolent disorders include splenic marginal zone lymphoma (median survival over 10 years), T-cell large granular lymphocytic leukemia, and primary cutaneous CD30+ T-cell lymphoma (5-year survival over 90%). Aggressive entities carry poor outcomes: T-cell prolymphocytic leukemia (median survival about 12 months), enteropathy-type T-cell lymphoma (median 7-11 months), hepatosplenic T-cell lymphoma (median about 2 years), primary effusion lymphoma (death typically within 6 months), and intravascular large B-cell lymphoma. Mediastinal large B-cell lymphoma is potentially curable with good multi-year survival. In extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type, survival tracks with the number of risk factors (B symptoms, advanced stage, elevated LDH, regional node involvement), with 5-year survival ranging from 81% (zero risk factors) down to 7% (three to four factors). Chronic neutrophilic leukemia has a median survival of about 2 years, atypical CML around 10-25 months, and CMML survival ranges by Mayo Molecular Model risk from about 16 to 97 months. Histiocytic sarcoma has a poor prognosis with median survival measured in months to about 2 years.","pitfalls":["Misclassifying nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin's lymphoma as classical Hodgkin's; the L&H 'popcorn' cell is a clonal B cell expressing CD45/CD79a/CD20/CD19/BCL2 and lacking CD30/CD15, behaving more like indolent B-cell NHL","Confusing B-cell prolymphocytic leukemia with leukemic mantle cell lymphoma, since both can express CD5 and t(11;14) and no reliable distinguishing criteria have emerged","Overlooking that a dry tap on marrow aspiration may signal hairy cell leukemia, myelofibrosis, metastatic carcinoma, CML, or acute leukemia rather than a technical failure","Mistaking lymphomatoid granulomatosis for extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma, nasal type, since both are angiodestructive and EBV-related","Attributing the presence of t(2;5) in a cutaneous CD30+ lesion to primary cutaneous disease; its presence should prompt evaluation for systemic anaplastic T-cell lymphoma","Failing to exclude reactive causes of neutrophilia, monocytosis, or eosinophilia before diagnosing CNL, CMML, or a primary eosinophilic disorder","Diagnosing systemic mastocytosis without accounting for hereditary alpha-tryptasemia when interpreting elevated serum tryptase"],"severityClassification":"Several entities have formal staging/risk schemes. Myeloid neoplasms are classified by the ICC and WHO systems using blood counts, smear, marrow morphology, and cytogenetic/molecular tests. CMML is graded as CMML-1 (<10% marrow and <5% blood blasts) and CMML-2 (10-19% marrow or 5-19% blood blasts), with the Mayo Molecular Model assigning low to high risk by truncating ASXL1 mutation, absolute monocytes >10 x10^9/L, hemoglobin <10 g/dL, platelets <100 x10^9/L, and circulating immature myeloid cells. Systemic mastocytosis is subclassified into indolent, smoldering (requires two or more B findings), aggressive, SM with associated myeloid/hematologic neoplasm, and mast cell leukemia (>=20% mast cells in marrow aspirate); advanced SM is defined by an associated neoplasm or organopathy (C findings). Hypereosinophilia is defined as absolute eosinophil count >=1500 x10^9/L, with hypereosinophilic syndrome requiring eosinophil-mediated organ damage. Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma nasal type uses a four-factor risk model (B symptoms, advanced stage, elevated LDH, regional node involvement).","references":["Harrison 22e p.871","Harrison 22e p.872","Harrison 22e p.873","Harrison 22e p.874","Harrison 22e p.875","Harrison 22e p.876","Harrison 22e p.877","Harrison 22e p.878","Harrison 22e p.879","Harrison 22e p.880","Harrison 22e p.881","Harrison 22e p.882"],"crossLinks":[]},"lichen_planus":{"name":"Lichen planus","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LP","lichen planus (cutaneous)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Remember the classic six P's: pruritic, purple (violaceous), polygonal, planar (flat-topped) papules and plaques","Lesions favour the flexor wrists, forearms, and ankles, and fine white lacy lines on the surface (Wickham striae) are a helpful diagnostic clue","New lesions can appear along lines of trauma or scratching (Koebner phenomenon)","Oral lichen planus commonly presents as a white reticular lacy pattern on the buccal mucosa; erosive oral or genital disease is painful and warrants monitoring","Nail involvement can cause ridging and, if severe, scarring with pterygium formation, while scalp disease (lichen planopilaris) causes scarring alopecia","Consider a lichenoid drug eruption when a similar rash follows introduction of a new medication, and screen for hepatitis C where locally relevant","Treatment centres on topical corticosteroids for cutaneous and localised mucosal disease, with phototherapy or systemic immunomodulators for widespread or resistant cases","Long-standing erosive oral or genital lichen planus carries a small risk of malignant transformation and should be followed up"],"pathophysiology":"Lichen planus is a T-cell-mediated inflammatory disorder in which cytotoxic CD8 T lymphocytes target basal keratinocytes, apparently in response to an altered self-antigen. The resulting band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction produces the characteristic histological features of basal cell degeneration, a saw-tooth epidermal profile, hypergranulosis, and apoptotic keratinocytes (Civatte bodies). Pigment released from damaged basal cells accounts for the post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation often seen, particularly in darker skin. Triggers can include certain drugs (lichenoid reactions) and, in some populations, hepatitis C infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Lichenoid drug eruption","Psoriasis","Discoid lupus erythematosus (for scalp and mucosal disease)","Secondary syphilis","Leukoplakia or candidiasis for oral white lesions","Chronic graft-versus-host disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin biopsy showing a lichenoid interface dermatitis in uncertain cases","Direct immunofluorescence to help distinguish from lupus or immunobullous disease when needed","Hepatitis C testing where epidemiologically appropriate","Review of medication history to identify a lichenoid drug trigger","Biopsy of persistent erosive or indurated oral or genital lesions to exclude dysplasia or malignancy"],"redFlags":["Erosive oral or genital disease with non-healing or indurated areas suggesting malignant change","Scarring alopecia or nail destruction indicating a need for prompt treatment to limit permanent damage","Rapidly extensive eruption suggesting a drug cause requiring medication review","Severe painful mucosal disease impairing eating or causing significant morbidity"],"prognosis":"Cutaneous lichen planus is often self-limiting, frequently resolving within one to two years but leaving post-inflammatory pigmentation; mucosal, nail, and scalp forms tend to be chronic, and erosive mucosal disease requires long-term surveillance for malignant transformation.","pitfalls":["Overlooking a lichenoid drug reaction and continuing the causative medication","Failing to examine the mouth, genitalia, nails, and scalp for additional sites of involvement","Not monitoring erosive oral or genital disease for malignant change","Attributing scarring alopecia to non-scarring causes and delaying treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"limbic_encephalitis":{"name":"Limbic encephalitis","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["autoimmune limbic encephalitis","paraneoplastic limbic encephalitis","LE"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An inflammatory syndrome of the medial temporal lobes presenting subacutely with short-term memory loss, confusion, psychiatric change, and seizures","It may be paraneoplastic (driven by an underlying cancer) or a primary autoimmune process, and identifying the antibody helps predict the association with tumour","Antibodies to intracellular antigens (e.g. Hu, Ma2) carry a strong cancer association and respond less well to immunotherapy, whereas surface-antigen antibodies (e.g. LGI1, NMDA receptor, GABA-B) often respond better","LGI1 antibody encephalitis characteristically causes faciobrachial dystonic seizures and hyponatraemia","NMDA-receptor encephalitis often affects younger patients with psychiatric onset, movement disorders, autonomic instability, and reduced consciousness, and is associated with ovarian teratoma","Brain MRI typically shows increased T2/FLAIR signal in the medial temporal lobes, and CSF may show a lymphocytic pleocytosis or oligoclonal bands","A thorough search for an occult malignancy is essential and may need repeating over time if initial screening is negative","Early immunotherapy and treatment of any underlying tumour improve outcome, so prompt recognition matters"],"pathophysiology":"Limbic encephalitis results from an immune attack, often antibody-mediated, directed at neuronal antigens concentrated in the limbic system, particularly the hippocampi and amygdalae. In paraneoplastic disease, a systemic tumour expresses neuronal antigens that trigger a cross-reactive immune response; antibodies against intracellular antigens mark a T-cell-mediated, less reversible process, whereas antibodies against neuronal surface proteins (such as NMDA receptor, LGI1, CASPR2, and GABA-B receptor) are considered directly pathogenic and more treatment-responsive. Inflammation of limbic structures produces the memory, behavioural, and seizure manifestations.","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex and other viral encephalitis","Status epilepticus and other seizure disorders","Rapidly progressive dementia including prion disease","Wernicke encephalopathy and metabolic encephalopathies","CNS lymphoma or other neoplastic infiltration","Primary psychiatric illness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum and cerebrospinal fluid neuronal antibody panels (surface and intracellular antigens)","Brain MRI focused on the medial temporal lobes","Lumbar puncture for cell count, protein, oligoclonal bands, and to exclude infection","EEG for seizures and encephalopathic patterns","Whole-body CT and FDG-PET to search for an underlying malignancy, with repeat surveillance if negative","Age- and antibody-directed tumour screening (e.g. pelvic imaging for ovarian teratoma in NMDA-receptor disease)"],"redFlags":["Subacute encephalopathy warrants urgent workup for autoimmune and paraneoplastic causes alongside exclusion of infective encephalitis","Positive onconeural or surface antibodies mandate a directed and, if negative, repeated search for occult cancer","Refractory seizures or status epilepticus requiring urgent management","Autonomic instability or reduced consciousness (notably in NMDA-receptor encephalitis) needing intensive monitoring"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the antibody and cause: surface-antigen syndromes and prompt immunotherapy often allow substantial recovery, whereas classic paraneoplastic encephalitis with intracellular-antigen antibodies tends to respond poorly and is dominated by the underlying malignancy.","pitfalls":["Failing to start empirical aciclovir while herpes simplex encephalitis remains possible","Not searching thoroughly, or repeatedly, for an occult tumour after a negative initial screen","Missing faciobrachial dystonic seizures as a treatable clue to LGI1 antibody disease","Attributing early psychiatric presentations solely to a primary psychiatric disorder","Delaying immunotherapy while awaiting antibody results in a clinically compatible case"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"listeriosis":{"name":"Listeriosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Listeria monocytogenes infection"],"chapter":156,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1227-1230","clinicalPearls":["Listeriosis is acquired almost always via foodborne transmission; implicated foods are refrigerated ready-to-eat items (deli/delicatessen meats, hot dogs), soft cheeses made with raw/unpasteurised milk, unpasteurised dairy, raw produce (packaged salad, sprouts, enoki mushrooms, cantaloupe, caramel apples, frozen vegetables) and pate/meat spreads (p.1227-1228).","Major risk groups for invasive disease are pregnant women and their neonates, older adults (risk rises with each decade over age 59), and immunocompromised persons (impaired cellular immunity from haematologic malignancy, transplant, HIV, glucocorticoids/immunosuppressants, or TNF-alpha inhibitors) (p.1227-1228).","Bacteraemia without a focus is the most common manifestation of invasive listeriosis; presents with fever, chills, myalgias and arthralgias sometimes preceded by nausea/diarrhoea (p.1228).","Neurolisteriosis is the second most common invasive manifestation; in a French cohort 84% of neurolisteriosis cases presented as meningoencephalitis (meningitis plus altered mental status, seizures, or focal findings) (p.1228).","Compared with other bacterial meningitides, CSF pleocytosis and neutrophil predominance are typically LESS pronounced; ~60-75% have CSF WBC <1000/uL and only ~30-40% have low CSF glucose, so normal-ish CSF does not exclude Listeria (p.1228).","Empiric bacterial-meningitis cover (ceftriaxone + vancomycin) does NOT cover Listeria; add ampicillin for Listeria coverage in patients >50 years old or with comorbid/immunocompromising disease (Chap.143, p.140372 / p.1122).","Rhombencephalitis (brainstem/cerebellar encephalitis) disproportionately affects otherwise healthy older adults, classically biphasic (fever and headache, then asymmetric cranial nerve palsies, ataxia, tremor, hemiparesis or hemisensory deficits); nearly half develop respiratory failure (p.1228).","MRI is superior to CT for diagnosis of neurolisteriosis, including rhombencephalitis (p.1228).","Diagnosis is by isolation from a normally sterile site (blood, CSF, amniotic fluid, placental or fetal tissue); blood cultures are positive only about half the time in pregnant women, and CSF multiplex PCR panels and MALDI-TOF aid rapid identification (p.1229).","Pregnant women usually have only mild flulike illness and rarely develop neurolisteriosis or death, yet transmit infection transplacentally to the fetus with high fetal morbidity; prepartum antibiotics started >=1 day before delivery improve the chance of a healthy infant (p.1228-1229).","In invasive disease virtually all patients are hospitalised and 18% die; consider listeriosis acquired in hospital and investigate hospital-provided food when a hospitalised (especially immunocompromised) patient develops new symptoms (p.1227-1228).","TMP-SMX given as Pneumocystis jirovecii prophylaxis (e.g. in HIV or transplant recipients) also helps prevent listeriosis (p.1229)."],"pathophysiology":"L. monocytogenes is a ubiquitous, nonsporulating, facultatively anaerobic gram-positive rod (showing small zones of beta-haemolysis and characteristic tumbling motility) that lives in soil and decaying vegetation and can grow at refrigerator temperatures as low as 4 C and tolerate low-pH/high-salt environments. Infection follows ingestion of contaminated food; the infectious dose is likely very low in the severely immunocompromised, and raised gastric pH (e.g. from proton-pump inhibitors) probably promotes gut survival. After transcytosis across the intestinal epithelium the organism spreads via mesenteric lymph nodes and bloodstream to its target organs (liver and spleen) and can cross the blood-brain barrier and the placenta. Virulence factors include internalins (InlA, InlB) for invasion of nonphagocytic cells, listeriolysin O and phospholipases for escape from the phagocytic vacuole, and ActA for actin-based cell-to-cell spread that evades antibodies and complement. Iron promotes growth, explaining the association of listeriosis with iron-overload states such as haemochromatosis. Host defence is predominantly cell-mediated (interferon-gamma, TNF-alpha, and CD8+ T cells), accounting for the predilection for those with impaired cellular immunity. (p.1227)","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Other causes of community-acquired bacterial meningitis (S. pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, H. influenzae, group B streptococcus) (p.1228, Chap.143)","Fungal or tuberculous meningitis (low CSF glucose with mononuclear pleocytosis can mimic Listeria) (p.1228, Chap.143)","Brain abscess or other space-occupying CNS infection (Listeria abscess) (p.1228)","Other bacteraemic/septic illnesses presenting with fever without a focus (p.1228)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Primary or metastatic CNS malignancy (Listeria brain abscess may be misdiagnosed as tumour) (p.1228)","Neoplasia / haematologic malignancy presenting with fever (also a risk factor and prognostic comorbidity) (p.1228)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cultures (note positive only ~half the time in pregnancy) (p.1229)","CSF culture and Gram stain (may show gram-positive rods, but often no organisms or misleading gram-positive cocci/diplococci/diphtheroids) (p.1228-1229)","Culture of normally sterile sites: amniotic fluid, placental tissue, fetal tissue (p.1229)","Multiplex PCR panel on CSF (includes L. monocytogenes as a target) (p.1229)","MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry for rapid isolate identification (p.1229)","MRI brain (superior to CT for neurolisteriosis and rhombencephalitis) (p.1228)","Selective enrichment medium (PALCAM Listeria Selective Agar or Oxford Agar) for non-sterile specimens / outbreak work-up (p.1229)","Whole-genome sequencing for outbreak investigation (p.1229)"],"redFlags":["Signs of meningitis with altered mental status, seizures or focal neurologic deficits (meningoencephalitis) (p.1228)","Brainstem/cerebellar signs - asymmetric cranial nerve palsies, ataxia, tremor, hemiparesis, hemisensory deficits (rhombencephalitis), with respiratory failure in nearly half (p.1228)","Fever or signs of fetal distress in a pregnant woman (meconium-stained amniotic fluid, abnormal fetal heart rate) (p.1229)","Multiorgan failure and monocytopenia (<200 cells/uL) - markers associated with death (p.1228)"],"prognosis":"Invasive listeriosis carries substantial mortality: 18% of reported cases die and virtually all are hospitalised. In a large French (MONALISA) cohort the 3-month mortality was 46% for bacteraemia and 30% for neurolisteriosis; death was associated with multiorgan failure, neoplasia, worsening of preexisting comorbidities, monocytopenia (<200 cells/uL), older age and female sex, and (for neurolisteriosis) a positive blood culture and adjunctive dexamethasone use. Nearly half of neurolisteriosis survivors have long-term neurologic impairment. In pregnancy-associated disease, fetal/neonatal outcomes are poor: in a cohort of 107 pregnancies 24% ended in fetal loss, 45% in premature birth and only ~5% had a normal delivery and postpartum course; overall 3% of 189 live-born infants died. (p.1228-1229)","pitfalls":["Cephalosporins are NOT effective against L. monocytogenes; standard empiric meningitis cover (ceftriaxone +/- vancomycin) misses Listeria, so ampicillin must be added empirically in patients >50 years or with comorbid/immunocompromising disease (p.1229, Chap.143 p.140372).","Febrile gastroenteritis form is rarely diagnosed because Listeria is not detected by routine stool cultures or rapid stool tests, and stool culture is NOT indicated in evaluating invasive listeriosis (p.1227, p.1229).","Gram-variable appearance as cocci, diplococci or diphtheroids can obscure the diagnosis; Listeria must be distinguished from other gram-positive rods, especially diphtheroids (p.1227, p.1229).","Relatively bland CSF (WBC often <1000/uL, frequently no organisms on Gram stain, only ~30-40% with low glucose) can falsely reassure - do not exclude Listeria meningitis on CSF profile alone (p.1228).","Brain abscess due to Listeria may be misdiagnosed as primary or metastatic malignancy; CSF can be normal when abscess occurs without concurrent meningitis (p.1228).","Adjunctive dexamethasone is associated with reduced survival in neurolisteriosis and should be avoided; it is also not recommended for established Listeria meningitis (p.1229, Chap.143).","Treatment failures have been reported with meropenem despite in vitro susceptibility, and TMP-SMX resistance occurs - perform susceptibility testing if TMP-SMX is considered (p.1229).","Testing/treatment is NOT indicated for an asymptomatic person who ate a recalled product, even if they have risk factors (p.1229)."],"severityClassification":"No formal severity score. Harrison frames the clinical spectrum from non-invasive self-limited febrile gastroenteritis in healthy persons to invasive listeriosis (bacteraemia without a focus, neurolisteriosis [meningoencephalitis, cerebritis, focal abscess, rhombencephalitis], focal infections, and pregnancy-associated/neonatal disease [granulomatosis infantiseptica, early-onset sepsis, late-onset meningitis]). Severity is judged clinically by syndrome, with prognostic markers including multiorgan failure and monocytopenia. (p.1227-1229)","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Chapter 156: Listeria monocytogenes Infections, pp.1227-1230","Charlier C et al. Clinical features and prognostic factors of listeriosis: The MONALISA National Prospective Cohort Study. Lancet Infect Dis 17:510, 2017","Charlier C et al. Neonatal listeriosis presentation and outcome: A prospective study of 189 cases. Clin Infect Dis 74:8, 2022","Koopmans MM et al. Human listeriosis. Clin Microbiol Rev 36:e0006019, 2023","Thomas J et al. Outbreak of listeriosis in South Africa associated with processed meat. N Engl J Med 382:632, 2020"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS","ENCEPHALITIS"]},"lithium_toxicity":{"name":"Lithium toxicity","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lithium poisoning","lithium overdose","lithium intoxication"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Lithium has a narrow therapeutic window, and toxicity is usually neurological - tremor, hyperreflexia, ataxia, dysarthria, agitation, confusion, and in severe cases seizures and coma","Distinguish acute, acute-on-chronic, and chronic toxicity, as chronic toxicity produces serious neurological features at lower serum levels and is more likely to cause lasting harm","Serum levels correlate poorly with severity in chronic toxicity because lithium distributes slowly into tissues including the central nervous system","Common precipitants of chronic toxicity include dehydration, renal impairment, and interacting drugs such as thiazide diuretics, ACE inhibitors or ARBs, and NSAIDs","There is no antidote; management centres on generous isotonic fluid resuscitation to restore volume and promote renal elimination","Activated charcoal does not bind lithium, but whole-bowel irrigation may help after ingestion of sustained-release preparations","Haemodialysis is the definitive means of removal and is indicated for severe neurological features, renal failure, or very high levels, with rebound rises possible afterwards requiring repeat treatment","Chronic toxicity can leave a permanent cerebellar and neurological deficit (the syndrome of irreversible lithium-effectuated neurotoxicity)"],"pathophysiology":"Lithium is a monovalent cation handled by the kidney similarly to sodium, so states that reduce renal perfusion or increase sodium reabsorption (volume depletion, diuretics, renal impairment) reduce its clearance and raise levels. It moves slowly across cell membranes, accumulating intracellularly and particularly in the central nervous system, which explains the delay between serum levels and clinical effect and the persistence of neurotoxicity. Intracellularly it interferes with neurotransmission and second-messenger systems. Lithium also impairs renal concentrating ability, causing nephrogenic diabetes insipidus that promotes dehydration and a vicious cycle of rising concentration.","additionalDifferentials":["Serotonin syndrome or neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Other causes of encephalopathy (metabolic, infective)","Cerebellar stroke or other structural lesion","Sepsis with delirium","Other drug intoxications causing tremor and confusion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial serum lithium levels to track trend rather than a single value","Serum electrolytes, urea, and creatinine to assess renal function","ECG for T-wave changes, QT effects, and arrhythmia","Thyroid function, as chronic use affects the thyroid","Assessment for precipitants such as dehydration and interacting medications"],"redFlags":["Seizures, marked confusion, or reduced consciousness","Severe neurological signs at only modestly raised levels, suggesting chronic toxicity","Acute kidney injury impairing lithium clearance","Cardiac arrhythmia or significant ECG changes","Persistent or rebound rises in level after initial treatment"],"prognosis":"Acute overdose in a lithium-naive patient often does well with fluids and, if needed, dialysis, whereas chronic toxicity carries a higher risk of prolonged or permanent neurological (especially cerebellar) impairment; outcome depends on severity, renal function, and timeliness of dialysis.","pitfalls":["Relying on a single serum level and underestimating chronic toxicity with a modest number","Giving activated charcoal, which does not adsorb lithium","Overlooking interacting drugs and dehydration that precipitated the toxicity","Delaying haemodialysis in severe or chronic toxicity, risking permanent neurological damage"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"liver_transplantation":{"name":"Liver Transplantation","system":"Gastrointestinal / Hepatobiliary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Orthotopic liver transplantation","OLT","Hepatic allograft transplantation","Living-donor liver transplantation"],"chapter":356,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2718","clinicalPearls":["Orthotopic transplantation (native liver removed, donor organ placed in the same anatomic site) is the preferred and technically most advanced approach; 1-year survival has risen from roughly 30% in the 1970s to over 90% today, attributed more to better patient selection and timing than to technical or immunologic advances alone.","Optimal timing matters: the disease should be advanced enough that medical options are exhausted, yet the procedure performed early enough to allow success. Better pretransplant clinical status predicts higher posttransplant survival.","Allocation of deceased-donor organs is driven by the MELD score (bilirubin, albumin, sodium, creatinine, INR, plus a female adjustment), a continuous scale from 6 to 40 in which higher values indicate greater illness severity; it replaced the older Child-Turcotte-Pugh-based scheme and reduced waiting-list mortality.","Status 1 (highest priority) is reserved for acute liver failure and for early posttransplant catastrophes such as primary graft nonfunction and hepatic artery thrombosis, superseding the MELD score.","Liver recipients with MELD scores below 15 have higher posttransplant mortality than comparable patients who remain on the waiting list, so transplant at very low MELD offers limited benefit.","HLA tissue typing is NOT required for liver transplantation, and preformed cytotoxic HLA antibodies do not preclude the procedure; ABO compatibility and organ size are the key matching considerations.","Tacrolimus has supplanted cyclosporine as primary immunosuppression; it is 10-100 times more potent, but nephrotoxicity and neurotoxicity (tremor, seizures, hallucinations, psychosis, coma) are its principal adverse effects. Cyclosporine and tacrolimus should not be combined because of overlapping nephrotoxicity.","Both calcineurin inhibitors are metabolized by cytochrome P450 IIIA: enzyme inducers (phenytoin, phenobarbital, carbamazepine, rifampin) lower drug levels, while inhibitors (azoles, macrolides, verapamil, diltiazem, ritonavir) raise them.","Sirolimus is avoided early posttransplant because it impairs wound healing and is associated with increased hepatic artery thrombosis in the first 30 days; its antiproliferative effect makes it attractive in recipients with prior or current malignancy such as HCC.","Infection timing follows a predictable pattern: conventional postoperative infections (pneumonia, wound, urinary, line, intraabdominal) dominate the first month, after which opportunistic pathogens (CMV, herpesviruses, Aspergillus, Candida, Cryptococcus, mycobacteria, Pneumocystis, Toxoplasma, Nocardia, Legionella, Listeria) predominate as immunosuppression takes its toll.","Long-term HBIg prophylaxis transformed transplantation for chronic hepatitis B; without prophylaxis hepatitis B almost always recurs, but indefinite/long-term HBIg (often combined with high-barrier oral antivirals such as entecavir or tenofovir) reduces reinfection and mortality, yielding outcomes indistinguishable from non-HBV indications.","Direct-acting antiviral (DAA) agents against HCV have markedly improved both pre- and posttransplant outcomes, allowing pretransplant clearance of viremia (sometimes delaying or obviating transplant) and high sustained virologic response rates in allograft HCV infection."],"pathophysiology":"End-stage liver disease produces portal hypertension, coagulopathy (prolonged prothrombin time, thrombocytopenia), and a hyperdynamic high-cardiac-output circulatory state; transplantation replaces the failing organ and rapidly reverses the hyperdynamic state. The graft is vulnerable to immunologic and ischemic injury. Acute cellular rejection arises 1-2 weeks postoperatively from a T-cell-mediated response, with histology showing a mixed portal infiltrate, bile duct injury, and endothelialitis. Chronic rejection produces progressive cholestasis, vascular lesions (intimal fibrosis, subintimal foam cells, fibrinoid necrosis), and ductopenia (vanishing bile duct syndrome). Calcineurin inhibitors act on the calcium-dependent T-cell signal transduction pathway to block lymphokine (IL-2/3/4, TNF-alpha) gene activation. Ischemic injury during harvesting underlies primary graft nonfunction; preservation in University of Wisconsin solution (rich in lactobionate and raffinose) extends tolerable cold-ischemic time. ABO-mismatched grafts (e.g., group O liver into a group A or B recipient) can cause transient passenger-lymphocyte autoimmune hemolytic anemia that resolves once recipient marrow repopulates the graft.","additionalDifferentials":["Posttransplant graft dysfunction: rejection vs biliary obstruction vs primary graft nonfunction vs vascular compromise (portal vein or hepatic artery thrombosis) vs drug hepatotoxicity vs recurrent primary disease vs viral hepatitis/CMV — these are clinically and biochemically difficult to distinguish, often requiring biliary imaging and percutaneous liver biopsy.","Recurrent primary biliary cholangitis vs chronic rejection (histologically nearly indistinguishable; a florid inflammatory bile duct lesion favors recurrent PBC but is not specific).","Recurrent autoimmune hepatitis or sclerosing cholangitis vs rejection or posttransplant bile duct injury."],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MELD/MELD-Na/MELD-3.0 score calculation (bilirubin, albumin, sodium, creatinine, INR) for allocation and prognosis; PELD for children under 12.","Serum bilirubin and aminotransferases — most reliable, though nonspecific, indicators of allograft rejection.","Percutaneous liver biopsy and radiographic visualization of the biliary tree to differentiate causes of graft dysfunction.","Donor screening: hemodynamic stability, oxygenation, absence of infection/abdominal trauma/hepatic dysfunction; serologic testing for HBV, HCV, HIV (infected organs now used selectively with matching and informed consent).","Recipient cardiopulmonary evaluation (especially age >70) to exclude ischemic cardiac disease; assessment of pulmonary artery pressure and intrapulmonary shunting.","anti-HBs titer monitoring (threshold ~100 mIU/mL) to guide HBIg dosing in hepatitis B recipients."],"redFlags":["Acute liver failure with impending cerebral edema — a transplant emergency requiring rapid donor identification before life-threatening complications set in.","Fever, right upper quadrant pain, and falling bile pigment/volume with rising bilirubin and aminotransferases — suggest acute allograft rejection.","Fibrosing cholestatic hepatitis (marked hyperbilirubinemia and prolonged prothrombin time out of proportion to modest aminotransferase rises) in recurrent hepatitis B — rapidly progressive liver failure.","Postoperative intraperitoneal bleeding raising intraabdominal pressure and reducing renal blood flow — reversible with laparotomy to evacuate clot and ligate the bleeding site.","New opportunistic infection beyond the first postoperative month in an immunosuppressed host demands prompt recognition and treatment.","Severe portopulmonary hypertension (mean pulmonary artery pressure >35 mmHg) or severe hypoxemia from right-to-left intrapulmonary shunts (PO2 <50 mmHg) — relative contraindications signaling high operative risk."],"prognosis":"One-year survival is now about 85-90%, and 5-year survival exceeds 60%. Outcome correlates strongly with pretransplant status: patients still working or only partially disabled achieve roughly >85% 1-year survival; those needing continuous in-hospital care about 70%; those requiring ICU life support about 50%. High pretransplant disease severity (MELD >25) and high-risk features (malignancy, fulminant hepatitis, age >65, concurrent renal failure, respirator dependence, portal vein thrombosis, prior portacaval shunt or multiple right-upper-quadrant operations) reduce survival to about 60% at 1 year and 35% at 5 years. Survival after retransplantation for primary graft nonfunction is about 50%. Failures within the first 3 months are mainly technical, infectious, or hemorrhagic; later failures stem from infection, rejection, or recurrent disease (malignancy, viral hepatitis, return to alcohol use). Renal failure (18% at 5 years, 25% at 10 years) and cardiovascular disease are leading causes of late mortality.","pitfalls":["Treating acute rejection with pulse glucocorticoids in HCV-infected recipients risks triggering severe recurrent allograft hepatitis C (though DAAs now mitigate this concern).","Cyclosporine and tacrolimus must not be co-administered because of overlapping nephrotoxicity and because tacrolimus reduces cyclosporine clearance.","Prior extensive biliary/hepatobiliary surgery complicates transplantation and is a relative contraindication, so biliary diversion has been largely abandoned in sclerosing cholangitis candidates.","Cholangiocarcinoma recurs almost invariably; only highly selected stage I/II patients after neoadjuvant chemoradiation should be considered.","Eradicating HCV before transplant can lower the MELD score and paradoxically reduce allocation priority in patients who still need a graft for ongoing decompensation.","Advanced age alone is no longer an absolute contraindication, but in patients over 70 a thorough cardiac and comorbidity workup is mandatory.","Relying on short-term (2 months or less) HBIg, preoperative vaccination, or interferon alone is ineffective for preventing hepatitis B recurrence; long-term prophylaxis is required."],"severityClassification":"Allocation and prognosis use the Model for End-Stage Liver Disease (MELD) score, a continuous scale from 6 to 40 (values above 40 counted as 40) derived from serum bilirubin, albumin, sodium, creatinine, and INR with a female adjustment; higher scores indicate greater illness severity. Variants include MELD-Na (2016) and MELD-3.0 (2023). PELD is used for candidates under 12. Status 1 (acute liver failure, primary graft nonfunction, hepatic artery thrombosis) supersedes MELD. The older Child-Turcotte-Pugh score (encephalopathy, ascites, bilirubin, albumin, prothrombin time) has been replaced for allocation. MELD exception points are granted (e.g., for HCC) via the National Liver Review Board.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2718","Harrison 22e p.2719","Harrison 22e p.2720","Harrison 22e p.2721","Harrison 22e p.2722","Harrison 22e p.2723","Harrison 22e p.2724","Harrison 22e p.2725"],"crossLinks":[]},"local_anaesthetic_systemic_toxicity":{"name":"Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LAST","local anaesthetic toxicity","systemic local anaesthetic poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Occurs when plasma levels of local anaesthetic rise excessively, usually from inadvertent intravascular injection or absorption from a large or repeated dose","Classic progression moves from early neurological signs (perioral numbness, tinnitus, metallic taste, agitation, dysarthria) to seizures, then to cardiovascular collapse, but atypical or delayed presentations are common","Cardiac toxicity can appear with little warning, especially with more lipophilic and potent agents such as bupivacaine, and may manifest as conduction block, arrhythmia, or refractory arrest","Immediate priorities are stopping the injection, calling for help, securing the airway with oxygenation, and controlling seizures while avoiding large doses of propofol in the unstable patient","Lipid emulsion therapy (intravenous lipid rescue) is the specific antidote and should be started early alongside high-quality resuscitation","During arrest, resuscitation is often prolonged; cardiopulmonary bypass or extracorporeal support may be needed and efforts should not be abandoned prematurely","Prevention relies on aspiration before injection, incremental dosing, use of a test dose, ultrasound guidance, and staying within weight-based maximum limits","Standard-dose adrenaline may be less effective and vasopressin is generally avoided; arrhythmias are managed while lipid rescue takes effect"],"pathophysiology":"Local anaesthetics block voltage-gated sodium channels. When systemic concentrations become excessive, they also disrupt cardiac and central nervous system membrane excitability and interfere with cardiac ion channels, mitochondrial energy production, and myocardial fatty acid metabolism. The central nervous system is affected first because inhibitory pathways are suppressed before excitatory ones, producing an excitatory phase followed by global depression. Cardiac effects include impaired conduction, reduced contractility, and malignant arrhythmias. More potent, lipid-soluble agents bind cardiac tissue avidly, making toxicity harder to reverse. Lipid emulsion is thought to act as a circulating sink that sequesters drug away from tissue and to support myocardial energetics.","additionalDifferentials":["Vasovagal syncope or simple faint during a block","High or total spinal anaesthesia","Primary cardiac arrhythmia or ischaemic event","Anaphylaxis to an injected agent or additive","Hyperventilation or panic reaction","Adrenaline effect from an epinephrine-containing solution"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous ECG and cardiovascular monitoring for arrhythmia and conduction abnormality","Pulse oximetry and capnography to confirm oxygenation and ventilation","Arterial blood gas to assess acidosis and guide resuscitation","Serial electrolytes and glucose during prolonged resuscitation","Plasma local anaesthetic level where available (rarely used acutely)"],"redFlags":["Seizure activity or rapidly declining consciousness after a regional block","Cardiovascular collapse, malignant arrhythmia, or cardiac arrest following local anaesthetic administration","Refractory hypotension or wide-complex rhythms unresponsive to standard measures","Progressive symptoms after a large-volume or repeated injection suggesting rising systemic levels"],"prognosis":"With early recognition, prompt lipid emulsion therapy, and sustained high-quality resuscitation, many patients recover fully; delayed treatment or severe bupivacaine-related arrest carries a higher risk of death or hypoxic injury.","pitfalls":["Attributing early neurological symptoms to anxiety and continuing injection","Delaying lipid emulsion until after prolonged, unsuccessful resuscitation","Abandoning resuscitation too early despite the potential for good recovery","Exceeding weight-based maximum doses or omitting aspiration and incremental dosing","Using large sedative doses that worsen cardiovascular instability"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"long_qt_syndrome":{"name":"Long QT syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LQTS","congenital long QT syndrome","acquired long QT","prolonged QT interval"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["LQTS is characterised by prolongation of the rate-corrected QT interval on ECG and a predisposition to the polymorphic ventricular tachycardia torsades de pointes","It may be congenital, from ion-channel gene mutations, or acquired, most often from QT-prolonging drugs and electrolyte disturbances such as hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, and hypocalcaemia","Presentations range from an incidental ECG finding to palpitations, syncope, seizures, or sudden cardiac death, frequently triggered by exertion, emotion, or auditory stimuli depending on the genotype","The QT interval must be corrected for heart rate, and serial or exercise ECGs may be needed because the interval can be borderline at rest","A careful family history of syncope, seizures, or premature sudden death supports a congenital cause","Management of acquired LQTS centres on withdrawing offending drugs and correcting electrolytes; congenital LQTS is often managed with beta-blockade and activity modification","An implantable cardioverter-defibrillator is considered for survivors of cardiac arrest or those with high-risk features despite therapy","Torsades is treated acutely with magnesium, correction of precipitants, and measures to raise the heart rate, with defibrillation if it degenerates to a sustained unstable rhythm"],"pathophysiology":"The QT interval reflects the duration of ventricular repolarisation. In congenital LQTS, mutations affecting cardiac potassium, sodium, or calcium channels delay repolarisation by reducing outward current or increasing inward current, prolonging the action potential. This heterogeneous prolongation favours early afterdepolarisations that can trigger torsades de pointes. Acquired LQTS most often results from drugs that block the rapid delayed rectifier potassium current or from electrolyte disturbances that impair repolarisation. Bradycardia and pauses further lengthen the QT and can precipitate pause-dependent torsades.","additionalDifferentials":["Acquired QT prolongation from drugs or electrolyte disturbance","Other inherited arrhythmia syndromes such as Brugada syndrome and catecholaminergic polymorphic VT","Vasovagal or other non-cardiac syncope","Epilepsy","Short QT syndrome (opposite repolarisation abnormality)","Structural causes of ventricular arrhythmia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG with accurate rate-corrected QT measurement, repeated as needed","Electrolyte panel including potassium, magnesium, and calcium","Review of all medications for QT-prolonging agents","Exercise or ambulatory ECG to unmask latent QT prolongation and assess dynamics","Genetic testing and family screening when a congenital syndrome is suspected","Echocardiography to exclude structural heart disease"],"redFlags":["MEDICAL EMERGENCY: torsades de pointes, which may cause syncope and can degenerate into ventricular fibrillation and cardiac arrest","Syncope or seizures in a patient with a prolonged QT, indicating a high risk of malignant arrhythmia","Family history of sudden cardiac death at a young age","Marked QT prolongation compounded by multiple QT-prolonging drugs or profound electrolyte disturbance"],"prognosis":"With recognition, avoidance of QT-prolonging triggers, and appropriate therapy including beta-blockade or a defibrillator, prognosis is generally favourable; risk of sudden death rises with greater QT prolongation, prior syncope or arrhythmia, and specific high-risk genotypes.","pitfalls":["Failing to correct the QT for heart rate or missing intermittent prolongation on a single resting ECG","Overlooking cumulative QT effects when several QT-prolonging drugs or electrolyte disturbances coexist","Attributing syncope or seizures to a neurological cause without measuring the QT","Neglecting family screening once a congenital syndrome is diagnosed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"ludwig_angina":{"name":"Ludwig angina","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Ludwig's angina","submandibular space infection","bilateral submandibular cellulitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Ludwig angina is a rapidly spreading cellulitis of the floor of the mouth involving the submandibular, sublingual and submental spaces bilaterally","The great majority arise from dental infection, especially of the lower molar teeth whose roots relate to the mylohyoid line","It produces a tense, brawny, tender swelling of the upper neck and floor of mouth that does not usually fluctuate, since it is a cellulitis rather than a discrete abscess","The tongue is pushed upward and backward, causing drooling, difficulty swallowing, muffled speech and a serious threat to the airway","Infection is typically polymicrobial with mixed aerobic and anaerobic mouth organisms","The overriding priority is airway management, as swelling can rapidly cause life-threatening obstruction","Treatment combines securing the airway, high-dose intravenous antibiotics and surgical decompression or drainage together with removal of the dental source","Contrast CT delineates the extent of spread and any collection but must never delay airway assessment in a deteriorating patient"],"pathophysiology":"Ludwig angina begins most often as an odontogenic infection of the mandibular molars. Because the roots of the second and third molars lie below the attachment of the mylohyoid muscle, infection spreads into the submandibular space and then communicates with the sublingual and submental spaces, involving all three bilaterally. The result is a rapidly advancing cellulitis of the floor of the mouth with dense inflammatory oedema rather than a localised abscess. Swelling of the sublingual tissues elevates and displaces the tongue posteriorly, while induration of the submandibular region narrows the oropharyngeal airway. Untreated, infection can extend along fascial planes to the parapharyngeal and retropharyngeal spaces and into the mediastinum, and airway obstruction or sepsis can be fatal.","additionalDifferentials":["Peritonsillar or parapharyngeal abscess","Submandibular sialadenitis or salivary gland abscess","Deep neck space abscess from other sources","Angioedema of the floor of mouth and neck","Cervical lymphadenitis with cellulitis","Sublingual haematoma (for example after anticoagulation)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Early airway assessment and continuous monitoring, taking priority over imaging","Contrast-enhanced CT of the neck to define spread and detect any collection or mediastinal extension","Full blood count, inflammatory markers and blood cultures","Culture and sensitivity of any pus obtained at drainage","Orthopantomogram or dental assessment to identify the causative tooth","Blood glucose and screening for diabetes or immunosuppression"],"redFlags":["Any sign of airway compromise (stridor, drooling, inability to swallow secretions, respiratory distress) constituting an airway emergency","Rapidly progressive neck swelling and inability to protrude the tongue","Features of spreading sepsis, high fever and haemodynamic instability","Chest pain, dyspnoea or widened mediastinum suggesting descending mediastinitis","Trismus limiting mouth opening and airway access"],"prognosis":"With early airway control, prompt intravenous antibiotics and timely surgical drainage the outlook is good, but delay in securing the airway or in treatment can lead to fatal airway obstruction, descending mediastinitis or sepsis.","pitfalls":["Underestimating the speed of airway deterioration and delaying definitive airway management","Waiting for fluctuation before intervening, when the process is a cellulitis without a drainable abscess early on","Sending an unstable patient for prolonged imaging instead of prioritising the airway","Overlooking and failing to treat the underlying dental source","Missing spread to the mediastinum in a patient who is not improving"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"lumbar_disc_prolapse":{"name":"Lumbar disc prolapse","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["prolapsed intervertebral disc","herniated lumbar disc","slipped disc","lumbar disc herniation","sciatica from disc prolapse"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with low back pain followed by radicular leg pain (sciatica) that typically radiates below the knee in a dermatomal pattern and is worsened by coughing, sneezing, or sitting","Most prolapses occur at L4/5 and L5/S1, affecting the L5 and S1 nerve roots respectively; the level is inferred from the pattern of pain, weakness, sensory loss, and reflex change","A positive straight-leg-raise (or its variants) reproduces radicular pain and supports nerve-root tension from a disc lesion","The great majority settle with conservative management: reassurance, maintained activity, analgesia, and physiotherapy, as most herniations resorb over weeks to months","Cauda equina syndrome from a large central prolapse is a surgical emergency and must be actively excluded in every patient","Imaging (MRI) is reserved for persistent or severe symptoms, progressive neurological deficit, or when surgery is being considered, since asymptomatic disc bulges are common","Surgery (discectomy) is considered for intractable radicular pain unresponsive to conservative care, significant or progressive motor weakness, or cauda equina compression","Isolated back pain without leg symptoms is usually not due to a surgically treatable disc prolapse"],"pathophysiology":"The intervertebral disc comprises a gelatinous nucleus pulposus contained by the fibrous annulus fibrosus. With age and repetitive loading the annulus weakens and fissures, allowing nuclear material to bulge or herniate, usually posterolaterally where the annulus is thinnest and the posterior longitudinal ligament offers least support. The herniated fragment compresses and inflames the traversing or exiting nerve root, producing radicular pain, and inflammatory mediators from the nucleus contribute to root irritation. A large central herniation can compress the cauda equina. Over time herniated material often shrinks through dehydration and resorption, correlating with symptom resolution.","additionalDifferentials":["Lumbar spinal stenosis with neurogenic claudication","Cauda equina syndrome from other causes","Spinal infection (discitis, epidural abscess) or vertebral osteomyelitis","Spinal or metastatic tumour","Peripheral neuropathy or diabetic amyotrophy","Hip osteoarthritis or greater trochanteric pain referring to the leg","Piriformis syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the lumbar spine as the imaging modality of choice when indicated","Detailed neurological examination for the affected root (motor, sensory, reflexes)","Straight-leg-raise and femoral stretch tests to localise root tension","Urgent MRI if cauda equina syndrome is suspected","Inflammatory markers and further imaging if infection or malignancy is a concern"],"redFlags":["SURGICAL EMERGENCY: cauda equina syndrome (saddle anaesthesia, bladder or bowel dysfunction, bilateral leg symptoms) requires urgent MRI and decompression","Progressive or severe motor weakness (e.g. foot drop) indicating significant root compression","Features suggesting infection or malignancy (fever, weight loss, night pain, history of cancer)","New neurological deficit with structural back pain in the presence of trauma or osteoporosis"],"prognosis":"The natural history is favourable, with most patients improving substantially within weeks to a few months as the herniation resorbs; surgery gives faster relief of radicular pain in selected cases but does not clearly improve very long-term outcome, and recurrence or residual back pain can occur.","pitfalls":["Failing to screen for and act on cauda equina syndrome","Ordering MRI too early and over-treating incidental disc bulges that do not explain the symptoms","Attributing pure axial back pain to a disc prolapse amenable to discectomy","Prolonged bed rest, which worsens outcomes compared with maintained activity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"lupus_nephritis":{"name":"Lupus nephritis","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["LN","SLE nephritis","systemic lupus erythematosus nephritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Lupus nephritis is renal involvement in systemic lupus erythematosus and is a major determinant of morbidity and long-term outcome in the disease","It can present across a spectrum from asymptomatic urinary abnormalities to nephrotic syndrome or rapidly progressive nephritic disease","Kidney biopsy is the cornerstone of assessment, classifying disease into histological classes that directly guide the intensity of treatment","The proliferative classes (particularly diffuse involvement) carry the greatest risk of progression and generally require intensive immunosuppression","Low complement levels and rising anti-double-stranded DNA antibodies often accompany active renal disease and can herald flares","Screening urinalysis and quantification of proteinuria at intervals is essential in all lupus patients because early nephritis is frequently silent","Treatment is divided into an induction phase to control active inflammation and a maintenance phase to prevent relapse, alongside general measures such as blood pressure control and reduction of proteinuria","Antiphospholipid antibodies can cause a thrombotic microangiopathy that contributes to renal injury independent of immune-complex nephritis"],"pathophysiology":"Lupus nephritis results from deposition of immune complexes and autoantibodies within the glomerulus, driving complement activation and inflammatory injury. Circulating and in-situ immune complexes, including those containing anti-DNA antibodies, localise in the mesangium, subendothelial, and subepithelial regions, and the site of deposition largely determines the clinical and histological pattern. Subendothelial and mesangial deposits provoke inflammatory, proliferative lesions with a nephritic picture, whereas predominantly subepithelial (membranous) deposits produce heavy proteinuria with less inflammation. Complement consumption, cytokine release, and recruitment of inflammatory cells damage the glomerular capillaries, and in severe forms crescents develop. Chronic activity leads to glomerulosclerosis, tubulointerstitial scarring, and progressive loss of function. Concurrent antiphospholipid antibodies may add a thrombotic microangiopathic component.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary membranous nephropathy","Other immune-complex glomerulonephritides","ANCA-associated or anti-GBM crescentic disease","Thrombotic microangiopathy from antiphospholipid syndrome","Drug-induced lupus-like nephritis","Mixed connective tissue disease with renal involvement"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis with microscopy and quantification of proteinuria","Serum complement (C3 and C4) and anti-double-stranded DNA antibody titres","Full autoimmune panel including antinuclear and antiphospholipid antibodies","Kidney biopsy for histological class, activity, and chronicity indices","Serial renal function and blood pressure monitoring","Assessment for infection and other organ involvement before immunosuppression"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising creatinine with an active nephritic sediment suggesting proliferative or crescentic disease","Nephrotic-range proteinuria with hypoalbuminaemia and oedema","Features of thrombotic microangiopathy such as haemolysis and thrombocytopenia","Signs of severe extrarenal lupus or infection complicating immunosuppression"],"prognosis":"Outcome has improved substantially with modern immunosuppression, but proliferative lupus nephritis still carries a meaningful risk of progression to end-stage kidney disease, especially with delayed treatment, high chronicity on biopsy, persistent proteinuria, or frequent relapses. Adherence, blood pressure control, and reduction of proteinuria strongly influence long-term renal survival.","pitfalls":["Relying on serology alone without biopsy to determine the class and intensity of treatment","Missing silent early nephritis by not performing regular urinalysis in lupus patients","Overlooking antiphospholipid-related thrombotic microangiopathy as a cause of worsening function","Stopping maintenance therapy prematurely and precipitating a relapse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"lyme_disease":{"name":"Lyme Disease (Lyme Borreliosis)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Lyme borreliosis"],"chapter":191,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1448","clinicalPearls":["Caused by spirochetes of the Borrelia burgdorferi sensu lato group (also called Borreliella spp.), transmitted by Ixodes ricinus complex ticks; B. burgdorferi sensu stricto is the predominant cause in the United States, while B. afzelii, B. garinii, and B. bavariensis also occur in Europe and Asia. (p.1448-1449)","Staged illness: stage 1 (early localized) presents with erythema migrans (EM); stage 2 (early disseminated) may follow with secondary skin lesions, neurologic involvement (meningitis, cranial neuritis, radiculoneuritis), carditis with atrioventricular block, and migratory musculoskeletal pain; stage 3 (late/persistent) typically manifests as intermittent arthritis and, rarely, chronic encephalopathy, polyneuropathy, or acrodermatitis. (p.1448-1449)","EM begins after an incubation period of roughly 3-32 days as a red macule or papule at the bite site that expands into a large annular lesion, often with a bright red outer border and partial central clearing; common sites include the thigh, groin, and axilla, and the lesion is warm but usually not painful. (p.1451)","Approximately 20% of patients do NOT develop the characteristic EM skin lesion, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis. (p.1451)","Cardiac involvement occurs in about 8% of patients within weeks of onset; the most common abnormality is fluctuating atrioventricular block (first-degree, Wenckebach, or complete heart block), and most cardiac involvement lasts only a few weeks. (p.1451)","About 15% of untreated patients develop frank neurologic abnormalities after weeks to months; the typical U.S. pattern is fluctuating meningitis with facial palsy (cranial neuritis, often the facial nerve, sometimes bilateral) and peripheral radiculoneuropathy. CSF in U.S. neuroborreliosis shows lymphocytic pleocytosis (~100 cells/uL) with elevated protein and normal or slightly low glucose. (p.1451)","In Europe and Asia the characteristic first neurologic sign is radicular pain followed by CSF pleocytosis (meningopolyneuritis or Bannwarth's syndrome), often without meningeal or encephalitic signs. (p.1451)","Lyme arthritis (stage 3) develops in about 60% of untreated U.S. patients months after onset; the typical pattern is intermittent oligoarticular attacks in large joints, especially the knees. Joint fluid white cell counts range widely (500-110,000/uL, average ~25,000/uL), mostly neutrophils, and rheumatoid factor and ANA tests are usually negative. (p.1451)","Most patients experience early symptoms during summer, reflecting transmission by nymphal Ixodes ticks; the tick must usually remain attached for at least about 24 hours for transmission of B. burgdorferi. (p.1449-1450)","Lyme disease is the most common vector-borne infection in the United States and Europe; in the U.S. more than 30,000 cases are reported annually but true cases are estimated at about 476,000 per year. (p.1450)","Posttreatment Lyme disease syndrome (PTLDS) affects roughly 10% of treated patients (reported figures vary widely) with persistent subjective pain, neurocognitive symptoms, or fatigue; there is NO evidence these symptoms reflect active infection, and randomized trials show repeated antibiotic courses do not help and are not recommended. (p.1451-1453)","Reinfection can occur after EM treated with antibiotics because the immune response after early infection is not protective; however, patients who develop an expanded immune response over months (e.g., those with Lyme arthritis) have protective immunity for years and rarely become reinfected. (p.1454)","Maternal-fetal transmission of B. burgdorferi appears to occur rarely if at all; standard therapy is recommended for pregnant women. (p.1453)","No Lyme disease vaccine is currently commercially available; the previously marketed vaccine was discontinued by its manufacturer. (p.1454)"],"pathophysiology":"B. burgdorferi is a fastidious microaerophilic spirochete with an unusually small genome and more than 100 predicted lipoproteins, mostly plasmid-encoded and displayed on the outer membrane to interact with host and tick. It expresses outer-surface protein A (OspA) in the tick midgut and upregulates OspC during migration to the salivary gland, where OspC binds the tick salivary protein Salp15 (required for mammalian infection). After injection into skin the organism downregulates OspC and upregulates the antigenically variable VlsE lipoprotein, then may migrate outward to produce EM and spread hematogenously or via lymphatics to skin, nervous system, heart, and joints. Its only known virulence factors are surface proteins that attach to host proteins, integrins, glycosaminoglycans, or glycoproteins (e.g., plasminogen binding aids tissue spread; complement regulators such as Factor H protect against lysis; decorin-binding proteins bind collagen-associated decorin, possibly explaining alignment with collagen fibrils). The host mounts innate and adaptive responses producing proinflammatory cytokines (IL-6, TNF-alpha, IL-1beta, IFN-gamma) and opsonizing antibodies; immune mechanisms eventually control disseminated infection even without antibiotics, though spirochetes may persist in localized niches for years. Postinfectious (antibiotic-refractory) Lyme arthritis is not due to persistent infection (culture and PCR of synovium are uniformly negative after antibiotics) but to a dysregulated, excessive proinflammatory response with very high IFN-gamma, host genetic factors (HLA-DRB1*0401, TLR1 1805GG polymorphism), and autoimmune responses to vascular and extracellular matrix autoantigens.","additionalDifferentials":["Uninfected tick bite (red papule that expands little) versus EM","Cellulitis (e.g., streptococcal) or allergic reaction to tick saliva when a lesion expands rapidly","Erythema multiforme in patients with secondary annular lesions (but mucosal blistering and palm/sole involvement are NOT features of B. burgdorferi infection)","Southern tick-associated rash illness (STARI) from Amblyomma americanum bites, an EM-like lesion whose cause is not yet identified","Peripheral spondyloarthropathy or pauciarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis versus oligoarticular Lyme arthritis","Bell's palsy (herpes simplex virus type 1) and Ramsay Hunt syndrome (varicella-zoster virus) versus Lyme facial palsy","Chronic fatigue syndrome and fibromyalgia versus late-stage Lyme disease or PTLDS"],"infectionMimics":["Babesiosis (Babesia microti), co-transmitted by I. scapularis, causing an influenza-like illness with thrombocytopenia and hemolytic anemia","Human granulocytotropic anaplasmosis (Anaplasma phagocytophilum), which may cause leukopenia/thrombocytopenia and can elicit a false-positive IgM response to B. burgdorferi","Borrelia miyamotoi relapsing fever, co-transmitted by I. scapularis","Borrelia mayonii and Ehrlichia muris-like agent infections in the upper midwestern United States","Powassan virus (deer tick virus) infection, which may be fatal","Ehrlichia chaffeensis transmitted by Amblyomma americanum in the same regions as STARI"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Fibromyalgia","Chronic fatigue syndrome","Allergic reaction to tick saliva","Peripheral spondyloarthropathy / juvenile idiopathic arthritis (for the arthritis presentation)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Two-tier serology: conventional approach with ELISA followed by Western blot for equivocal/positive results, or a modified two-test approach using two enzyme immunoassays (e.g., whole-sonicate ELISA followed by VlsE C6 peptide IgG ELISA) that dispenses with the Western blot","IgM and IgG responses during early infection, ideally in paired acute- and convalescent-phase sera (~20-30% positive in acute phase, ~70-80% during convalescence)","After 4-8 weeks, the IgG response by ELISA and Western blot has very high sensitivity and specificity (~99%); a single IgG test is then usually sufficient","Western blot band criteria: IgM positive if 2 of 3 bands (23, 39, 41 kDa); IgG positive if 5 of 10 bands (18, 23, 28, 30, 39, 41, 45, 58, 66, 93 kDa)","PCR of joint fluid for B. burgdorferi DNA (its main PCR application; superior to culture later in disease, though DNA may persist after killing so it does NOT confirm active infection or therapy adequacy)","Culture of B. burgdorferi in Barbour-Stoenner-Kelly (BSK) medium, used mainly in research and positive chiefly early (EM biopsy, occasionally plasma or CSF)","CSF analysis showing lymphocytic pleocytosis with elevated protein in neuroborreliosis","Peripheral blood smear and blood PCR to evaluate for tick-borne co-infections; standard blood counts may give clues (cytopenias)"],"redFlags":["High-degree (third-degree) atrioventricular block or PR interval >0.3 s in carditis, warranting IV therapy and cardiac monitoring","Severe objective neurologic abnormalities (e.g., meningitis, encephalitis, severe neuroborreliosis)","Optic nerve involvement in children from inflammation or raised intracranial pressure, which may lead to blindness","Borrelial encephalomyelitis/leukoencephalitis (rare, mainly B. garinii in Europe) with spastic paraparesis and bladder dysfunction","Rare fatal pancarditis or cardiomegaly","Suspected tick-borne co-infection (e.g., babesiosis, anaplasmosis, Powassan virus), which can cause severe or fatal illness, especially in the very young or elderly"],"prognosis":"Response to treatment is best when given early in the disease; later treatment remains effective but the convalescent period may be longer. Eventually most patients recover with minimal or no residual deficits. Even without antibiotics, innate and adaptive immunity usually control disseminated infection within weeks to months, though untreated spirochetes may persist in localized niches for years. A small percentage develop persistent postinfectious arthritis or, rarely, chronic encephalopathy or polyneuropathy, and about 10% have PTLDS, which usually improves over months but may last years.","pitfalls":["Assuming a negative serologic test excludes acute Lyme disease - serology may be negative during the first several weeks (early seronegativity), so diagnosis of early Lyme disease is clinical","Missing the diagnosis because of atypical EM morphology or because ~20% of patients never develop EM","Relying on a positive IgM result alone in illness lasting more than 2 months - such an isolated IgM is likely false-positive and should not support the diagnosis","Misusing or misinterpreting IgM Western blots, a recognized cause of incorrect Lyme diagnoses in patients with other illnesses","Using serology as a screening test in patients with nonspecific pain or fatigue (low pretest probability), where a false-positive result is more likely than a true-positive","Interpreting persisting seropositivity (including VlsE C6 and IgM responses, which can persist for years after therapy) as evidence of active, recent, or recurrent infection","Treating persistent subjective symptoms as active infection - 'chronic Lyme disease' equated with chronic B. burgdorferi infection is a misnomer, and repeated/prolonged antibiotic courses lack benefit and carry documented harm","Using PCR positivity in joint fluid to judge active infection or adequacy of therapy, since DNA may persist for weeks after spirochetal killing"],"severityClassification":"Staged clinical classification per Harrison: Stage 1 - early localized infection (erythema migrans at the bite site). Stage 2 - early disseminated infection (secondary annular skin lesions, neurologic involvement such as meningitis/cranial neuritis/radiculoneuritis/peripheral neuritis, carditis with atrioventricular nodal block, and migratory musculoskeletal pain). Stage 3 - late/persistent infection (intermittent or persistent arthritis, and rarely chronic encephalopathy, polyneuropathy, or acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans). For diagnostic/testing purposes, the American College of Physicians algorithm stratifies by pretest probability: high (EM - treat empirically without serology), intermediate (e.g., oligoarticular arthritis - test and treat if positive), and low (nonspecific symptoms - neither test nor treat).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1448"],"crossLinks":[]},"lymphedema":{"name":"Lymphoedema","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lymphedema","lymphatic oedema","primary lymphoedema","secondary lymphoedema"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from impaired lymphatic drainage causing accumulation of protein-rich interstitial fluid and progressive limb swelling","Primary forms arise from congenital or inherited lymphatic maldevelopment, while secondary forms follow damage from surgery, radiotherapy, malignancy, infection, or trauma","Worldwide, filarial infection is the leading cause of secondary lymphoedema, whereas in high-income settings cancer treatment predominates","Early swelling is soft and pitting but becomes non-pitting and fibrotic over time, with skin thickening and a positive Stemmer sign at the base of the toes","The distribution characteristically includes the dorsum of the foot and toes, helping distinguish it from other causes of limb swelling","Diagnosis is largely clinical; lymphoscintigraphy can confirm lymphatic dysfunction when the cause is uncertain","Cornerstone management is complete decongestive therapy with skin care, manual lymphatic drainage, compression, and exercise","Meticulous skin hygiene reduces recurrent cellulitis, which itself further damages lymphatics and worsens the oedema"],"pathophysiology":"Lymphoedema develops when lymphatic transport capacity is exceeded by the lymphatic load, leading to accumulation of protein-rich fluid in the interstitium. In primary disease the lymphatic vessels or nodes are congenitally hypoplastic, aplastic, or dysfunctional. In secondary disease, previously normal lymphatics are obstructed or destroyed by surgery, radiotherapy, tumour infiltration, recurrent infection (notably filariasis), or trauma. Stagnant protein draws further fluid osmotically and incites chronic inflammation, fibrosis, adipose deposition, and skin changes, creating a self-reinforcing cycle. Impaired local immune surveillance predisposes to recurrent cellulitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic venous insufficiency and venous oedema","Deep vein thrombosis","Systemic causes of oedema (cardiac, renal, hepatic, hypoalbuminaemia)","Lipoedema","Dependent or immobility-related oedema","Myxoedema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment including Stemmer sign and limb circumference or volume measurement","Lymphoscintigraphy to confirm lymphatic dysfunction when the diagnosis is unclear","Duplex ultrasound to exclude venous disease or thrombosis","Cross-sectional imaging to identify malignancy or nodal obstruction in secondary cases","Evaluation for filariasis in endemic regions"],"redFlags":["Features of cellulitis (spreading erythema, warmth, fever) requiring prompt treatment","Rapidly progressive or unilateral swelling raising concern for underlying malignancy","New or changing cutaneous lesions suggesting lymphangiosarcoma in longstanding lymphoedema","Sudden painful swelling that could indicate concurrent deep vein thrombosis"],"prognosis":"Lymphoedema is generally a chronic, progressive condition without cure, but sustained decongestive therapy and compression can control swelling, preserve function, and reduce complications. Poor adherence, recurrent infection, and untreated underlying causes worsen the outlook, and rarely longstanding disease can undergo malignant change.","pitfalls":["Confusing lymphoedema with venous oedema or lipoedema and applying inappropriate treatment","Applying compression without first excluding significant arterial disease","Overlooking an underlying malignancy in new unilateral limb swelling","Neglecting skin care, allowing recurrent cellulitis that accelerates progression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"lysosomal_storage_diseases":{"name":"Lysosomal Storage Diseases","system":"Metabolic / Inherited (Endocrinology and Metabolism)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Lysosomal system diseases","LSDs","Tay-Sachs disease","Fabry disease","Gaucher disease","Niemann-Pick disease","Mucopolysaccharidoses","Pompe disease","Lysosomal acid lipase deficiency","LALD","Krabbe disease","CLN2 Batten disease","Alpha-mannosidosis"],"chapter":429,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3351-3358","clinicalPearls":["Lysosomes are subcellular organelles holding specific hydrolases that process or degrade proteins, nucleic acids, carbohydrates and lipids; there are more than 50 distinct lysosomal storage diseases (now also called lysosomal system diseases), grouped by the predominant accumulated material.","Consider an LSD in the differential when a patient has unexplained neurologic, renal or muscular degeneration, or unexplained hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, cardiomyopathy, or skeletal dysplasias/deformities; physical findings are disease-specific and diagnosis is confirmed by enzyme assay or genetic testing.","Nearly all LSDs are autosomal recessive, with notable exceptions: Hunter disease (MPS II), Danon disease and Fabry disease are X-linked, and two autosomal dominant conditions exist (Parry-type CLN from DNAJC5 mutations, and frontotemporal dementia/CLN11 from GRN/progranulin mutations).","Tay-Sachs disease (total hexosaminidase A deficiency) is carried by roughly 1 in 30 Ashkenazi Jews; the infantile form features macrocephaly, loss of motor skills, an exaggerated startle reaction and a macular cherry-red spot, with carrier screening recommended in this population.","Fabry disease (X-linked, alpha-galactosidase A deficiency) presents with angiokeratomas, hypohidrosis, corneal and lenticular opacities, debilitating episodic burning pain of the hands and feet (acroparesthesia), and progressive renal, cardiac and cerebrovascular disease; angiokeratomas are punctate, dark red to blue-black, do not blanch and cluster in the 'bathing suit area' between umbilicus and knees.","Gaucher disease (acid beta-glucosidase deficiency, GBA1) is classified by CNS involvement: type 1 is nonneuronopathic with hepatosplenomegaly and bone disease, type 2 is a severe progressive CNS disease causing death by about 2 years, and type 3 has variable CNS and visceral disease; early CNS signs in type 3 may be limited to supranuclear lateral gaze palsy.","GBA1 mutations, whether heterozygous or homozygous, substantially raise the lifetime risk of Parkinson disease.","Niemann-Pick types A and B reflect acid sphingomyelinase deficiency: type A has early onset with rapid CNS deterioration, while type B is later/variable with hepatosplenomegaly, cirrhosis, and lethal progressive pulmonary disease (foam cells in alveoli, lymphatics and pulmonary arteries); Niemann-Pick C is a separate disorder from NPC1/NPC2 defects in cholesterol/sphingolipid transport.","Pompe disease (acid alpha-glucosidase/acid maltase deficiency, GAA) is the only LSD causing primary glycogen storage; the classic infantile form has hypotonia, cardiomyopathy and hepatosplenomegaly with near-total enzyme deficiency, whereas late-onset forms resemble a limb-girdle myopathy and may not have cardiomyopathy.","Lysosomal acid lipase deficiency (LIPA mutations) spans infantile Wolman syndrome (failure to thrive, vomiting, hepatosplenomegaly; median death about 3.7 months) and childhood/adult-onset cholesterol ester storage disease (elevated liver enzymes, microvesicular fatty liver, cryptogenic cirrhosis); neither variant causes primary CNS disease and statins do not halt the underlying tissue pathology.","CLN2 (neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis type 2, TPP1/tripeptidyl peptidase 1 deficiency) usually begins at age 2-4 years with seizures, ataxia, myoclonus and vision loss, progressing to severe disability and death around age 12; it is the only NCL with a specific approved treatment.","Alpha-mannosidosis (MAN2B1 biallelic loss of function) accumulates mannose-rich oligosaccharides and causes intellectual disability, incapacitating skeletal changes, immunodeficiency, ataxia, hearing loss, neurodegeneration and psychosis."],"pathophysiology":"Lysosomal biogenesis requires ongoing synthesis of lysosomal hydrolases, membrane proteins and new membranes; lysosomes form by fusion of trans-Golgi network vesicles with late endosomes, and progressive acidification creates a pH gradient that drives pH-dependent dissociation of receptors from ligands and activates the hydrolases. Lysosomes are part of the lysosome/autophagy/mitophagy system regulated by mTORC1-mediated phosphorylation of transcription factors TFEB/TFE3, which balances anabolic and catabolic pathways; this regulation is disrupted to varying degrees in tissues affected by specific LSDs. Defects at any biosynthetic step can impair enzyme function: oligosaccharide remodeling in the Golgi (including the mannose-6-phosphate lysosomal targeting ligand), membrane sorting signals, phosphorylation, sulfation, additional proteolysis and heteromer assembly are all critical, and abnormalities in these shared processes can produce multiple simultaneous enzyme/protein deficiencies. The final common pathway is accumulation of specific macromolecules within tissues and cells that normally carry a high flux of those substrates; most deficiencies arise from point mutations or rearrangements at a single hydrolase/subunit locus, though some mutations affecting targeting, active sites, macromolecular assembly or trafficking impair several hydrolases at once. Substrate buildup distorts lysosomes and may exert pharmacologic effects, notably activation of innate immune responses. Some substrates are made within the tissue of pathology, while others are delivered exogenously (LDL receptor-mediated uptake in Fabry and LALD; phagocytosis in Gaucher type 1). The threshold hypothesis holds that disease emerges below a certain level of residual enzyme activity, and that activity can be challenged by changes in substrate flux from genetic background, cell turnover, recycling or metabolic demand, so a given residual level may suffice in some tissues but not others.","additionalDifferentials":["Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy (mimicked by late-onset Pompe disease)","Idiopathic hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (about 1-3% have Fabry disease)","Idiopathic stroke at 35-50 years (about 2-5% carry GLA mutations)","Idiopathic renal failure in males (about 5% have GLA mutations)","Cryptogenic cirrhosis / nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (childhood/adult-onset LALD)","Familial hyperlipidemia (LALD)","Parkinson disease (associated with GBA1 carriage)","Acute appendicitis or renal colic (Fabry abdominal pain can mimic these)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (Fabry cardiac variant)","Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy (late-onset Pompe)","Cryptogenic/idiopathic cirrhosis (childhood/adult-onset LALD)","Other neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses (significant clinical overlap with CLN2)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Enzyme assay in nucleated cells, serum/plasma, or leukocytes specific to the suspected disorder","Molecular/genetic testing: whole GBA1 sequencing is the standard for Gaucher (enzyme testing has poor sensitivity for heterozygote detection)","Slit-lamp examination for corneal and lenticular lesions to support a Fabry diagnosis","Decreased acid beta-glucosidase activity (0-20% of normal) in nucleated cells establishes Gaucher disease; the enzyme is normally absent from bodily fluids","Markedly reduced acid sphingomyelinase activity (1-10% of normal) in nucleated cells confirms Niemann-Pick A/B","Confirmation of specific enzyme deficiency in Morquio (GALNS vs acid beta-galactosidase) before therapy","Gene sequencing to confirm CLN2 given overlap among the neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses","Newborn screening (now includes MPS I and, in many states, Pompe disease)","Bone imaging / nuclear scan in Gaucher bone crises (localized absent uptake of pyrophosphate agents indicates acute bone infarction)"],"redFlags":["Macular cherry-red spot with macrocephaly and exaggerated startle in an infant (infantile Tay-Sachs)","Splenic infarction in Gaucher disease that can present as an acute abdomen","Gaucher 'bone crisis' with excruciating localized pain, sometimes erythema, fever and leukocytosis, representing acute bone infarction","Pulmonary hypertension or alveolar Gaucher cell accumulation (uncommon but life-threatening, more common in splenectomized females)","Vertebral collapse and avascular necrosis (femoral head) with potential spinal cord involvement in Gaucher disease","Infantile-onset LALD presenting as a medical emergency with severe failure to thrive, vomiting and hepatosplenomegaly","Fetal hydrops in severe MPS VII (Sly), which can cause stillbirth or perinatal demise","Progressive pulmonary disease with dyspnea and hypoxemia in Niemann-Pick type B","Respiratory insufficiency requiring ventilation in late-onset Pompe disease"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies enormously across and within disorders, each spanning a spectrum from severe to attenuated forms. Infantile Tay-Sachs is fatal in infancy; juvenile-onset Tay-Sachs causes death by 10-15 years. In Fabry disease, death results from cardiovascular, renal or cerebrovascular disease; renal insufficiency, cardiac fibrosis and stroke are irreversible, so early therapy offers the best chance to slow life-threatening complications, and heart disease is the most common life-threatening manifestation in females while renal then cardiovascular disease predominates in males. Gaucher type 2 leads to death by about 2 years, while type 1 and type 3 are highly variable. Niemann-Pick type B can progress to death in adolescence or early adulthood from hepatic or pulmonary disease. Infantile Pompe is rapidly progressive with death typically in the first year without treatment. Infantile-onset LALD is fatal by about 1 year (median 3.7 months) untreated, and untreated childhood/adult-onset LALD progresses to early cirrhosis (mean about age 13) and early-adulthood atherosclerosis. Early infantile Krabbe presents around 4 months and progresses rapidly to death at a mean of about 18 months. CLN2 progresses to severe disability with most children wheelchair-bound in late childhood, death at a mean of about 12 years, and survival beyond adolescence rare.","pitfalls":["Treating the disease nosology as fixed discrete entities is misleading: each LSD is heuristic and clinically expresses a continuous spectrum from severe to attenuated forms.","Angiokeratomas of Fabry disease can be easily overlooked and also occur in several other rare LSDs, so they are not specific.","Fabry abdominal pain may be mistaken for appendicitis or renal colic, delaying diagnosis.","Enzyme testing has poor sensitivity for Gaucher heterozygote detection; molecular GBA1 sequencing is required.","In Niemann-Pick, hepatic or bone marrow transplantation has produced more complications than expected (recurrent hepatic disease from ASM-deficient myeloid repopulation after liver transplant, or failure to clear hepatocyte sphingomyelin after BMT/HSCT).","Enzyme replacement therapy does not cross the blood-brain barrier and does not directly treat CNS disease (e.g., MPS I, MPS II); HSCT has not succeeded against CNS disease in MPS II.","Statins lower hypercholesterolemia in LALD but do not alter the underlying progressive tissue (e.g., liver) pathology.","Late cardiac and respiratory complications of MPS I (obstructive breathing, cardiomyopathy, valve disease) can emerge even after successful HSCT, so lifelong follow-up is required.","Morquio variants A and B require confirmation of the specific enzyme deficiency because approved enzyme therapy (elosulfase alfa) applies only to MPS IVA."],"severityClassification":"Within individual diseases, severity is typically stratified by age of onset and CNS involvement. Examples: Tay-Sachs is divided into infantile, juvenile-onset and adult-onset forms. MPS I is graded as Hurler (MPS I H, severe with neurodegeneration), Hurler-Scheie (MPS I H/S, intermediate, severe somatic disease without major neurologic decline), and Scheie (MPS I S, later-onset without neurologic involvement). Gaucher disease is classed as type 1 (nonneuronopathic), type 2 (acute severe CNS), and type 3 (variable subacute/chronic CNS). Niemann-Pick distinguishes type A (early-onset, progressive CNS), type B (later, variable, with an A/B variant having slowly progressive CNS involvement), and the separate type C. LALD ranges from infantile-onset (Wolman) to childhood/adult-onset (cholesterol ester storage disease). Pompe and Krabbe are divided into early/infantile and later-onset variants.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3351","Harrison 22e p.3355","Harrison 22e p.3356","Harrison 22e p.3357","Harrison 22e p.3358"],"crossLinks":[]},"macrophage_activation_syndrome":{"name":"Macrophage Activation Syndrome","system":"Haematological / Immunological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MAS","secondary haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis","secondary HLH"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A life-threatening hyperinflammatory state, considered a form of secondary haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, characterised by uncontrolled activation of macrophages and T cells.","It most commonly complicates rheumatic conditions, especially systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis, but can be triggered by infection or malignancy.","Suspect it when a child with an underlying inflammatory disease develops persistent high fever, worsening general condition, and hepatosplenomegaly.","A characteristic and counterintuitive clue is falling blood counts (cytopenias) and a paradoxically falling or normalising ESR despite active inflammation.","Markedly elevated ferritin is a hallmark laboratory feature and useful for both suspicion and monitoring.","Other typical findings include high triglycerides, low fibrinogen, deranged liver function, and coagulopathy, sometimes with overt bleeding.","Bone marrow examination may show haemophagocytosis, but a normal marrow does not exclude the diagnosis and treatment should not be delayed for it.","This is a medical emergency requiring rapid immunosuppression to interrupt the cytokine storm before multi-organ failure develops."],"pathophysiology":"MAS results from defective downregulation of the immune response, with impaired cytotoxic function of natural killer cells and cytotoxic T lymphocytes. This allows persistent activation and proliferation of T cells and well-differentiated macrophages (histiocytes), which phagocytose blood cells and release a flood of pro-inflammatory cytokines. This cytokine storm produces sustained fever, cytopenias from marrow phagocytosis and suppression, hepatic dysfunction, coagulopathy resembling disseminated intravascular coagulation, and progressive multi-organ damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary (familial) haemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis","Sepsis with disseminated intravascular coagulation","Flare of the underlying rheumatic disease","Systemic infection (EBV and other viruses, intracellular pathogens)","Malignancy, particularly lymphoma or leukaemia","Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (MIS-C)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ferritin (very high levels are characteristic and trended for response)","Full blood count to detect evolving cytopenias","Fibrinogen, D-dimer, and full coagulation profile","Fasting triglycerides","Liver function tests and lactate dehydrogenase","Soluble interleukin-2 receptor (soluble CD25) and natural killer cell function where available","Bone marrow aspirate to look for haemophagocytosis when the diagnosis is uncertain"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive multi-organ dysfunction from cytokine storm (EMERGENCY)","Persistent high fever with deteriorating clinical state","Falling cell counts across multiple lineages","Falling fibrinogen with rising ferritin and clinical bleeding","Neurological involvement: seizures, altered consciousness","Progression toward disseminated intravascular coagulation or shock"],"prognosis":"MAS carries significant mortality if unrecognised, but early aggressive immunosuppression can be life-saving and many children recover; outcome depends on prompt diagnosis, control of the trigger, and reversal of organ dysfunction before it becomes irreversible.","pitfalls":["Being falsely reassured by a falling or normal ESR, which can accompany the low fibrinogen of MAS","Interpreting the picture solely as a disease flare or sepsis and delaying specific treatment","Waiting for bone marrow confirmation of haemophagocytosis before starting therapy","Underappreciating how quickly the condition can progress to coagulopathy and organ failure","Not monitoring ferritin and blood counts closely to track response"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"major_depressive_disorder":{"name":"Major depressive disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MDD","major depression","clinical depression","unipolar depression"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Diagnosis requires a sustained period (at least around two weeks) of either persistently low mood or loss of interest/pleasure, together with several additional symptoms causing significant distress or functional impairment","Associated features commonly include disturbed sleep, appetite or weight change, fatigue, poor concentration, psychomotor slowing or agitation, feelings of worthlessness or excessive guilt, and recurrent thoughts of death","Always screen actively for suicidal ideation, intent, and plan, and ask about prior attempts and access to means","Screen for a past manic or hypomanic episode before starting an antidepressant, because an unrecognised bipolar diathesis can be destabilised","Exclude medical and substance-related contributors such as hypothyroidism, anaemia, and medication effects before attributing symptoms solely to a primary mood disorder","Management principles combine evidence-based psychotherapy (such as cognitive behavioural therapy or interpersonal therapy) with antidepressant pharmacotherapy, typically first-line SSRIs, chosen by side-effect profile and comorbidity","Antidepressant benefit is often delayed by several weeks, adherence should be reinforced, and treatment is generally continued well beyond symptom remission to reduce relapse","Severe, psychotic, treatment-resistant, or high-suicide-risk presentations may warrant combination pharmacotherapy, augmentation, or electroconvulsive therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Major depressive disorder is understood as a multifactorial condition arising from interactions among genetic vulnerability, dysregulation of monoamine neurotransmission (serotonin, noradrenaline, and dopamine), and disturbance of hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis stress responses. Contemporary models also emphasise impaired neuroplasticity and neurotrophic signalling, altered function in limbic and prefrontal circuits, and the role of chronic stress and adverse life events acting on a predisposed individual.","additionalDifferentials":["Bipolar disorder in a depressive phase","Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia)","Adjustment disorder with depressed mood","Grief and bereavement reactions","Hypothyroidism and other endocrine or metabolic disorders","Substance-induced or medication-induced mood disorder","Depression in the context of a neurocognitive disorder (pseudodementia)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured suicide risk assessment","Standardised symptom rating scales to gauge severity and track response","Thyroid function testing","Full blood count and metabolic panel to exclude anaemia and other medical contributors","Vitamin B12 and folate where clinically indicated","Substance use screening"],"redFlags":["Active suicidal ideation with plan, intent, or access to means","Psychotic features such as mood-congruent delusions or hallucinations","Profound self-neglect, refusal of food or fluids, or catatonia","Rapid deterioration or emerging manic symptoms after starting an antidepressant"],"prognosis":"Major depressive disorder is frequently recurrent; many patients respond to treatment but relapse risk rises with each subsequent episode and with residual symptoms. Prognosis is generally better with early treatment, good adherence, and psychosocial support, and worse with comorbid substance use, chronic medical illness, and untreated recurrent episodes.","pitfalls":["Starting an antidepressant without screening for a prior manic or hypomanic episode","Underestimating suicide risk or failing to reassess it during treatment","Stopping antidepressants too early once mood improves, increasing relapse risk","Attributing symptoms to depression while missing an underlying medical cause such as hypothyroidism","Judging non-response before an adequate dose and duration of treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"malignant_hyperthermia":{"name":"Malignant hyperthermia","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MH","malignant hyperpyrexia","anaesthetic-induced hypermetabolic crisis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A pharmacogenetic hypermetabolic crisis triggered by volatile anaesthetics and depolarising neuromuscular blockers in susceptible individuals","The earliest and most sensitive sign is an unexplained rise in end-tidal carbon dioxide despite increased ventilation, often with tachycardia and mixed acidosis","Masseter or generalised muscle rigidity, rapidly rising temperature, and dark urine from myoglobin are later features; hyperthermia is frequently a late rather than early sign","Immediate management is to stop triggering agents, call for help and the emergency supply of dantrolene, hyperventilate with high-flow oxygen, and switch to a non-triggering technique","Dantrolene is the specific antidote and should be given without delay and repeated until the hypermetabolic state resolves","Supportive care includes active cooling, treating hyperkalaemia and arrhythmias, correcting acidosis, and maintaining urine output to protect against myoglobinuric renal injury","Susceptibility is largely inherited (commonly RYR1 gene variants) and is linked to certain myopathies; a family or personal history should prompt a trigger-free anaesthetic","Confirmatory testing uses the in-vitro contracture (caffeine-halothane) test with genetic testing, and survivors should be counselled and referred to an MH unit"],"pathophysiology":"Malignant hyperthermia results from abnormal regulation of skeletal muscle calcium, most often due to mutations affecting the ryanodine receptor (RYR1) that controls calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. On exposure to volatile agents or succinylcholine, uncontrolled calcium release produces sustained muscle contraction and a massive increase in aerobic and anaerobic metabolism. This drives rapid carbon dioxide and heat generation, oxygen consumption, lactic acidosis, ATP depletion, and eventually muscle breakdown with hyperkalaemia, myoglobinaemia, and rhabdomyolysis. Dantrolene reduces calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum and interrupts the cycle.","additionalDifferentials":["Inadequate anaesthesia or analgesia with sympathetic response","Sepsis or systemic inflammatory response","Thyroid storm or phaeochromocytoma crisis","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome or serotonin syndrome","Equipment-related rebreathing or exhausted carbon dioxide absorbent","Environmental or iatrogenic hyperthermia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous capnography and core temperature monitoring to detect the crisis early","Arterial blood gas showing combined respiratory and metabolic acidosis","Serial potassium, creatine kinase, and myoglobin to track rhabdomyolysis","Coagulation studies to detect disseminated intravascular coagulation","Later in-vitro contracture testing and RYR1 genetic analysis for confirmation"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising end-tidal carbon dioxide with tachycardia and rigidity indicating an evolving MH crisis","Severe hyperthermia with mixed acidosis and hyperkalaemia threatening arrhythmia and arrest","Cardiac arrhythmia or arrest during or after a triggering anaesthetic","Dark urine and rising creatine kinase signalling rhabdomyolysis and renal risk"],"prognosis":"Mortality has fallen dramatically with early recognition and prompt dantrolene, but delayed treatment can lead to disseminated intravascular coagulation, renal failure, and death; survivors and relatives require testing and lifelong trigger avoidance.","pitfalls":["Attributing rising carbon dioxide or tachycardia to light anaesthesia and delaying diagnosis","Failing to have dantrolene immediately available or delaying its administration","Confusing an isolated masseter spasm with a full crisis or, conversely, dismissing it entirely","Neglecting active cooling and management of hyperkalaemia and myoglobinuria","Not counselling the patient and family or arranging specialist referral after recovery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"malnutrition_and_nutritional_assessment":{"name":"Malnutrition and Nutritional Assessment","system":"Gastrointestinal / Nutritional","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Protein-calorie malnutrition","Protein-energy malnutrition","Undernutrition"],"chapter":345,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2615","clinicalPearls":["Malnutrition affects roughly 30-50% of hospitalized patients, with the exact prevalence varying by clinical setting, underlying diagnosis, and the diagnostic criteria applied (Harrison 22e p.2615).","The 2019 GLIM (Global Leadership Initiative on Malnutrition) consensus requires at least one phenotypic criterion (nonvolitional weight loss, low BMI, or reduced muscle mass) PLUS at least one etiologic criterion (reduced food intake/assimilation, or disease burden/inflammation) to diagnose malnutrition (Harrison 22e p.2616).","Severity grading in GLIM is determined by the phenotypic criterion: moderate (stage 1) malnutrition is 5-10% weight loss within 6 months (or 10-20% beyond 6 months), and severe (stage 2) is >10% within 6 months (or >20% beyond) (Harrison 22e p.2616).","Patients at any BMI can be malnourished; overweight or obese individuals may have sarcopenic obesity from muscle loss, and conversely being underweight does not by itself equal malnutrition (Harrison 22e p.2617).","Handgrip strength is among the more clinically useful functional measures because it is easy to perform and helps identify which patients are most likely to benefit from individualized nutritional support (Harrison 22e p.2617).","Patients with apparently adequate oral intake may still be at risk of malnutrition when chronic gastrointestinal disease compromises nutrient assimilation or absorption (Harrison 22e p.2617).","A 5-10% loss of body weight over 6 months is clinically relevant, while >10% loss over the same period is considered severe (Harrison 22e p.2617).","Albumin and prealbumin are negative acute-phase reactants and fall with injury, disease, or inflammation; they have poor sensitivity and specificity for malnutrition, though they retain value for predicting clinical outcomes (Harrison 22e pp.2617-2618).","C-reactive protein is a positive acute-phase reactant useful for confirming inflammation, with the chapter noting supportive thresholds of mild 3.0-9.9 mg/L, moderate 10-50 mg/L, and severe >50 mg/L (Harrison 22e p.2616).","Calf circumference is a practical, valid estimate of skeletal muscle mass that requires less training than mid-upper arm circumference, with ethnicity-specific reference ranges that should be adjusted for BMI extremes (Harrison 22e p.2618).","When inflammation is severe, nutrition care tends to be supportive only; at low-to-moderate inflammation, nutrition care may be therapeutic and improve clinical outcomes (Harrison 22e pp.2615-2616).","Calf circumference, BMI, and other GLIM parameters make malnutrition diagnosis feasible even in the outpatient/ambulatory setting where dietitian access may be limited (Harrison 22e p.2617)."],"pathophysiology":"Malnutrition arises from an imbalance between nutrient intake/assimilation and metabolic demand, and acute or chronic inflammation is now recognized as central to its pathophysiology. Inflammatory mediators raise resting energy expenditure, promote anabolic resistance and muscle catabolism, and cause anorexia that further reduces intake. Severe acute inflammation (e.g., critical illness, sepsis, burns, trauma, closed head injury) may be superimposed on a mild-to-moderate chronic inflammatory state (e.g., malignancy, COPD, congestive heart failure, chronic renal disease, chronic GI disease). The resulting deficits impair wound healing, immune function, and organ function. Because albumin and prealbumin behave as negative acute-phase reactants, the inflammatory milieu also distorts these traditional nutrition biomarkers (Harrison 22e pp.2615-2617).","additionalDifferentials":["Sarcopenia (progressive generalized loss of skeletal muscle mass and strength; screened with SARC-F)","Sarcopenic obesity","Cachexia of chronic disease (malignancy, organ failure)","Frailty syndrome","Dehydration / fluid shifts mimicking weight change","Micronutrient deficiency states"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Edema or fluid overload masking true weight loss (dry weight estimation may be needed)","Hypoalbuminemia from systemic inflammation, hepatic or renal insufficiency, protein-losing enteropathy, or corticosteroid use rather than nutrition itself","Low BUN/creatinine from renal or hepatic disease falsely suggesting reduced muscle mass","Hypocholesterolemia related to acute illness rather than malnutrition per se"],"additionalInvestigations":["Validated risk-screening tools: Malnutrition Screening Tool, Malnutrition Universal Screening Tool (MUST), Nutritional Risk Screening 2002, Mini Nutritional Assessment, Subjective Global Assessment","Anthropometrics: serial body weight (without shoes/heavy garments), BMI, calf circumference, mid-upper arm circumference; height estimated by arm span or knee-height caliper when needed","Body composition: bioelectrical impedance analysis (BIA, portable), dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry (DXA), air displacement plethysmography, and opportunistic CT/MRI muscle measurement","Laboratory: complete blood count with differential (screen for iron, B12, folate, vitamin C deficiencies), C-reactive protein for inflammation, specific micronutrient levels","Indirect calorimetry for resting energy expenditure; 24-h urine urea nitrogen and nitrogen balance to gauge catabolism and protein adequacy (normal renal/hepatic function required)","Functional tests: handgrip strength, Sit-to-Stand test, 6-Meter Walk Test, Clinical Frailty Scale, SARC-F","Nutrition-focused physical examination for edema, subcutaneous fat and muscle loss; diet history including oral supplements, enteral and parenteral intake"],"redFlags":["Nonvolitional weight loss >10% within 6 months (or >20% beyond 6 months) indicating severe malnutrition","BMI <18.5 kg/m2 in adults <70 years (or <20 if >=70 years) indicating severe malnutrition by GLIM","Severe acute inflammatory state with negative nitrogen balance and elevated resting energy expenditure","Low handgrip strength predicting poorer clinical outcomes","Hypocholesterolemia (<120 mg/dL) developing during hospitalization, linked to worse outcomes","Reduced food intake/assimilation (<=50% of energy requirement for >1 week, any reduction >2 weeks, or chronic GI condition impairing absorption)"],"prognosis":"Malnutrition is associated with poor wound healing, compromised immunity, and impaired organ function, leading to longer hospital stays, more readmissions, higher mortality, and increased health care costs. Severe inflammation may limit nutritional interventions to a supportive rather than therapeutic role, and the associated malnutrition can diminish the effectiveness of medical therapies. Robust evidence indicates that a multipronged approach to malnutrition intervention reduces mortality and holds promise for improving quality of life, so timely diagnosis and treatment are important (Harrison 22e pp.2615, 2618).","pitfalls":["Relying on albumin or prealbumin to diagnose malnutrition or judge response to nutrition therapy; they reflect inflammation, hepatic/renal status, hydration, and other factors and have poor specificity (Harrison 22e pp.2617-2618).","Assuming a normal or high BMI excludes malnutrition; sarcopenic obesity and muscle loss can occur at any BMI (Harrison 22e p.2617).","Overlooking edema, which can mask true weight loss unless dry weight is estimated (Harrison 22e p.2617).","Using BUN/creatinine, total lymphocyte count, cholesterol, creatinine-height index, cytokines, ECG, recall-antigen skin testing, or urine 3-methylhistidine as malnutrition indicators; the chapter lists these as NOT recommended for diagnosis (Harrison 22e pp.2618-2619).","Comparing malnutrition prevalence across studies without accounting for differences between diagnostic tools, which yield incomparable rates (Harrison 22e p.2616).","Failing to recognize prolonged pre-admission decreases in intake when an acute event is superimposed on chronic disease (Harrison 22e p.2617)."],"severityClassification":"GLIM severity grading by phenotypic criteria. Stage 1 (moderate): weight loss 5-10% within 6 months or 10-20% beyond 6 months; BMI <20 (<70 years) or <22 (>=70 years); mild-to-moderate muscle mass deficit. Stage 2 (severe): weight loss >10% within 6 months or >20% beyond 6 months; BMI <18.5 (<70 years) or <20 (>=70 years); severe muscle mass deficit. WHO/NIH BMI categories: <18.5 underweight, 18.5-24.9 desirable, 25.0-29.9 overweight, >=30 obese (Harrison 22e pp.2616-2617).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2615","Harrison 22e p.2616","Harrison 22e p.2617","Harrison 22e p.2618","Harrison 22e p.2619"],"crossLinks":[]},"mandibular_fracture":{"name":"Mandibular fracture","system":"Plastic / Reconstructive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["fractured mandible","broken jaw","lower jaw fracture","condylar fracture"],"chapter":null,"source":"Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery / standard references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The mandible commonly fractures at more than one site because of its arch shape, so a fracture at one point should prompt a search for a second, often contralateral, fracture such as the condyle","Malocclusion, a change in the patient's usual bite, is a sensitive sign of mandibular fracture and must be specifically asked about and examined","Other clinical signs include pain on jaw movement, trismus, a step in the dental arch, sublingual haematoma, and numbness of the lower lip from inferior alveolar nerve injury","Panoramic radiography and CT are used to define fracture site, displacement, and the relationship to tooth roots and the inferior alveolar nerve","Fractures involving tooth-bearing bone communicate with the mouth and are effectively open, requiring antibiotics and timely management to reduce infection risk","Restoring the pre-injury occlusion is the central goal, achieved with maxillomandibular fixation and/or open reduction with internal fixation depending on the pattern","Many condylar fractures, particularly in children and minimally displaced cases, can be managed conservatively with a short period of fixation and early mobilisation to avoid ankylosis","Bilateral anterior (parasymphyseal) fractures can allow the tongue and anterior segment to fall backward and threaten the airway"],"pathophysiology":"The mandible is a mobile, U-shaped bone whose weaker regions - the condylar neck, angle (often at an unerupted third molar), and parasymphysis - are prone to fracture. Because it forms a ring-like structure articulating at both temporomandibular joints, force at one point is frequently transmitted to produce a second fracture elsewhere. Displacement is influenced by the pull of the muscles of mastication, which can distract or override fracture segments depending on their orientation (favourable versus unfavourable patterns). Fractures through tooth-bearing segments breach the oral mucosa, creating a contaminated, effectively open injury, and disruption of the inferior alveolar nerve within the canal causes lower lip and chin numbness.","additionalDifferentials":["Temporomandibular joint dislocation without fracture","Dentoalveolar fracture or isolated dental trauma","Zygomatic or midface fracture causing trismus","Mandibular contusion or muscle injury","Pathological fracture through a cyst or tumour","Temporomandibular joint dysfunction unrelated to trauma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Panoramic radiograph (orthopantomogram) as an initial survey of the mandible","CT for detailed assessment of condyle, displacement, and comminution","Careful occlusal and dental examination to detect malocclusion and tooth injury","Inferior alveolar nerve sensory testing of the lower lip and chin","Assessment for a second fracture given the ring anatomy of the mandible"],"redFlags":["Airway compromise from bilateral parasymphyseal fractures with posterior displacement of the tongue (emergency)","Displaced open fracture through tooth-bearing bone with infection risk","Signs of associated cervical spine or head injury in high-energy trauma","Progressive infection or abscess from an untreated open mandibular fracture"],"prognosis":"Most mandibular fractures heal well with restored occlusion and function when reduction and fixation are appropriate, and many condylar fractures do well with conservative management. Poorer outcomes - malunion, malocclusion, nonunion, infection, temporomandibular joint stiffness or ankylosis, and persistent nerve numbness - are associated with delayed treatment, inadequate reduction, open contaminated fractures, or prolonged immobilisation.","pitfalls":["Missing a second (often condylar) fracture after identifying the first","Failing to recognise malocclusion as a key diagnostic sign","Underestimating infection risk in fractures communicating with the oral cavity","Overlooking airway risk in bilateral anterior fractures","Prolonged rigid immobilisation of condylar fractures leading to stiffness or ankylosis","Neglecting to document inferior alveolar nerve deficit before intervention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"maple_syrup_urine_disease":{"name":"Maple Syrup Urine Disease","system":"Genetic-metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MSUD","branched-chain ketoaciduria","BCKD deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive disorder caused by deficiency of the branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase complex, impairing breakdown of the branched-chain amino acids leucine, isoleucine, and valine.","The classic severe form presents in the first week of life with poor feeding, vomiting, lethargy, and worsening encephalopathy after protein feeding begins.","Neurological signs may include fluctuating tone, dystonic posturing, a boxing or bicycling movement pattern, and progression to seizures and coma.","The urine, sweat, or cerumen may carry a characteristic sweet, maple-syrup or burnt-sugar odour.","Leucine and its ketoacid accumulation is the principal driver of neurotoxicity and cerebral oedema.","Newborn screening detects elevated branched-chain amino acids; diagnosis is confirmed by the plasma amino acid pattern (notably raised leucine and the presence of alloisoleucine) and molecular testing.","Acute management centres on halting protein intake, promoting anabolism with adequate energy, and rapidly lowering branched-chain amino acid levels, with dialysis or haemofiltration in severe crises.","Long-term treatment is a carefully monitored diet restricting branched-chain amino acids while providing enough protein and energy for growth.","Intercurrent illness, fasting, or catabolic stress can precipitate metabolic crises even in previously stable, treated children."],"pathophysiology":"A defect in the branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase complex blocks the oxidative decarboxylation of the ketoacids derived from leucine, isoleucine, and valine. As a result these branched-chain amino acids and their corresponding ketoacids accumulate in blood, urine, and tissues. Leucine and its ketoacid are particularly neurotoxic, disturbing neurotransmitter balance, cerebral amino acid transport, and cellular osmolarity, which produces cerebral oedema and encephalopathy. During catabolic states, endogenous protein breakdown releases further branched-chain amino acids, precipitating acute, potentially life-threatening metabolic decompensation.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal sepsis","Hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy","Urea cycle disorders","Organic acidemias","Nonketotic hyperglycinemia","Other causes of neonatal encephalopathy and seizures"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Newborn screening result and confirmatory plasma amino acid profile (elevated leucine, presence of alloisoleucine)","Urine organic acids and detection of branched-chain ketoacids","Molecular genetic testing of the BCKD complex genes","Blood gas, glucose, ammonia, and ketone assessment during crises","Neuroimaging if cerebral oedema is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive encephalopathy with lethargy, abnormal movements, and coma in a neonate","Signs of cerebral oedema and raised intracranial pressure","Metabolic crisis precipitated by intercurrent illness or fasting","Marked elevation of leucine requiring emergency clearance","Seizures or apnoea in a decompensating infant"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on how quickly the diagnosis is made and metabolic control is achieved; early treatment and avoidance of crises support near-normal development, whereas repeated or severe decompensations and prolonged high leucine levels risk permanent neurological damage. Lifelong dietary management and prompt sick-day protocols are essential.","pitfalls":["Mistaking early presentation for neonatal sepsis and delaying metabolic evaluation","Not recognising the characteristic sweet odour as a diagnostic clue","Continuing protein feeds during an acute crisis instead of stopping them and driving anabolism","Underestimating how rapidly catabolic stress can trigger dangerous decompensation","Failing to establish sick-day plans and long-term dietary monitoring"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"mastocytosis":{"name":"Mastocytosis","system":"Immunology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mast cell disease","systemic mastocytosis"],"chapter":366,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2819-2821","clinicalPearls":["Mastocytosis is defined by accumulation of clonally expanded mast cells in tissues (skin, bone marrow, liver, spleen, gut); mast cell expansion is most readily identified in skin and/or bone marrow (p.2819).","More than 90% of patients carry a somatic gain-of-function KIT D816V mutation in the stem cell factor receptor gene (p.2819).","Classification recognizes cutaneous mastocytosis (with variants), systemic mastocytosis (SM, five variants), and mast cell sarcoma; cutaneous mastocytosis is the most common diagnosis in children and is usually diagnosed within the first year of life (p.2819-2820).","Systemic mastocytosis denotes involvement of an extracutaneous site, most often bone marrow; indolent systemic mastocytosis (ISM) accounts for >70% of adult patients and is associated with a normal life expectancy (p.2820).","Cutaneous lesions of maculopapular cutaneous mastocytosis (MPCM, also called urticaria pigmentosa) are reddish-brown macules, papules, or plaques that urticate and become erythematous on rubbing or trauma (Darier's sign) (p.2820).","Clinical manifestations of SM reflect release of bioactive mast cell mediators plus tissue infiltration: intermittent urticaria, flushing, tachycardia and vascular collapse, gastric acid hypersecretion, crampy lower abdominal pain, and diarrhea (p.2820).","Anaphylaxis in mastocytosis is most commonly triggered by Hymenoptera stings and NSAIDs; symptom flares may also follow alcohol, temperature change, stress, and mast cell-interactive opioids (p.2820).","A basal serum tryptase level is a useful noninvasive test to consider before bone marrow biopsy; total serum tryptase >20 ng/mL is a minor diagnostic criterion for SM (p.2821).","Diagnosis of SM requires tissue confirmation (lesional/bone marrow biopsy): one major plus one minor, or three minor criteria. Major = multifocal dense mast cell aggregates (>15 mast cells per aggregate) in extracutaneous tissue; minor = spindled/atypical morphology, aberrant CD25/CD2/CD30 phenotype, activating KIT mutation, or tryptase >20 ng/mL (p.2821).","Patients with recurrent mast cell activation (especially anaphylaxis with hypotensive syncope) who carry KIT D816V but do not meet full SM criteria are termed monoclonal mast cell activation syndrome (p.2821)."],"pathophysiology":"Mastocytosis is a clonal mast cell disorder driven by somatic gain-of-function mutations in the KIT (stem cell factor receptor) gene, with KIT D816V present in >90% of patients. The mutation produces ligand-independent KIT activation and clonal mast cell proliferation/accumulation in skin, bone marrow, liver, spleen, and gut. KIT mutations occur across all forms of SM and in lesional skin of some children with cutaneous disease; additional mutations (TET2, SRSF2, ASXL1, RUNX1) seen in other hematologic neoplasms can appear in advanced SM variants. Disease manifestations arise from release of bioactive mast cell mediators acting locally and distally, tissue infiltration by mast cells, and the tissue response to that infiltrate; mast cell-mediated fibrosis may affect liver, spleen, and bone marrow but not gastrointestinal tissue or skin (p.2819-2820).","additionalDifferentials":["Carcinoid syndrome (exclude with 24-h urine 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid)","Pheochromocytoma (exclude with 24-h urine/plasma metanephrines)","Hereditary alpha-tryptasemia (autosomal dominant extra copies of the alpha-tryptase gene at TPSAB1; elevated baseline serum tryptase with multisystem mast cell activation symptoms)","Recurrent IgE-mediated or idiopathic anaphylaxis (usually presents with urticaria, angioedema and/or bronchospasm, which are not typical manifestations of anaphylaxis in SM)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Carcinoid syndrome (flushing, diarrhea)","Pheochromocytoma (flushing, tachycardia, vascular collapse)","Hereditary alpha-tryptasemia","Idiopathic or IgE-mediated anaphylaxis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Basal (baseline) serum tryptase as a noninvasive screen before bone marrow biopsy","Bone marrow biopsy with mast cell morphology, immunophenotype (CD25, CD2, CD30), and KIT D816V mutation testing for systemic disease","Skin biopsy to confirm diffuse cutaneous and bullous mastocytosis subvariants","24-h urine 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (carcinoid) and metanephrines (pheochromocytoma) to exclude mimics","Bone densitometry, bone scan, or skeletal survey (osteoporosis is increased and may cause pathologic fractures)","CT scan or endoscopy and neuropsychiatric evaluation as directed by presentation"],"redFlags":["Anaphylaxis with rapid, life-threatening vascular collapse (most often after Hymenoptera stings or NSAIDs)","Hypotensive syncope from mast cell activation","C findings of aggressive disease: cytopenias, hepatomegaly with ascites and impaired liver function, splenomegaly with hypersplenism, malabsorption with hypoalbuminemia and weight loss, or osteolytic skeletal lesions with pathologic fractures","Circulating atypical mast cells suggesting mast cell leukemia"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is variant-dependent. Life expectancy is normal for cutaneous mastocytosis and most ISM; progression from ISM to a more advanced form is uncommon (~5% lifetime risk). In SM-AHNMD, outcome is determined by the associated non-mast cell hematologic disorder. SSM carries an intermediate prognosis. Aggressive SM and mast cell leukemia have a poor prognosis, and mast cell leukemia is invariably fatal at present. In infants and children with cutaneous disease, visceral involvement is usually absent and spontaneous resolution before adolescence is common; progression from cutaneous mastocytosis to ISM occurs in ~10% of children, especially with high mast cell burden or hematologic abnormalities (p.2820).","pitfalls":["Atopy is not increased in mastocytosis compared with the general population (p.2819).","Imatinib is not effective in most cases because the KIT D816V mutation confers resistance (p.2821).","Osteoporosis alone without osteolytic lesions does not satisfy the C-finding skeletal criterion for aggressive SM (p.2821).","SM cannot be confirmed on clinical and laboratory grounds alone; tissue diagnosis is required (p.2821).","Anaphylaxis in SM typically lacks the urticaria, angioedema, and bronchospasm seen in IgE-mediated or idiopathic anaphylaxis (p.2821).","Baseline serum tryptase elevation must be interpreted with caution where hereditary alpha-tryptasemia (extra TPSAB1 copies) coexists (p.2821)."],"severityClassification":"Consensus classification: Cutaneous mastocytosis (CM) - maculopapular cutaneous mastocytosis (MPCM/urticaria pigmentosa), solitary mastocytoma of skin, diffuse cutaneous mastocytosis. Systemic mastocytosis (SM) - indolent SM (ISM, >70% of adults, normal life expectancy), smoldering SM (SSM; >=2 B findings, no C findings: high mast cell burden with marrow infiltration >30%, basal tryptase >200 ng/mL, and/or KIT D816V VAF >10%, dysmyelopoiesis, organomegaly), SM with associated clonal hematologic non-mast cell lineage disease (SM-AHNMD), aggressive SM (ASM; >=1 C finding - cytopenias, hepatic dysfunction with ascites, hypersplenism, malabsorption with weight loss, osteolytic fractures), mast cell leukemia (MCL, rarest, invariably fatal). Mast cell sarcoma (MCS) is a rare invasive solid mast cell tumor (Tables 366-1, 366-2; p.2820).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2819-2821"],"crossLinks":["anaphylaxis"]},"mastoiditis":{"name":"Acute mastoiditis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute coalescent mastoiditis","mastoid abscess","suppurative mastoiditis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Usually arises as a complication of acute otitis media when infection spreads into the air cell system of the mastoid and pus fails to drain","Suspect it when a child with a middle ear infection develops worsening pain, fever and swelling behind the ear despite treatment","The classic sign is a boggy, tender postauricular swelling that pushes the pinna forward and downward, often obliterating the retroauricular groove","Otoscopy commonly shows sagging of the posterosuperior canal wall and a bulging or discharging drum","Progressive breakdown of the bony walls between air cells (coalescence) distinguishes surgical disease from simple mastoid mucosal inflammation seen in every acute otitis media","Contrast CT of the temporal bone defines the extent of coalescence, subperiosteal abscess and any early intracranial extension","Management combines aggressive parenteral antibiotics with drainage; cortical mastoidectomy is indicated when there is a subperiosteal abscess, no response to medical therapy, or a suspected complication","Pus should be sent for culture, and common organisms include Streptococcus pneumoniae, Streptococcus pyogenes, Staphylococcus and Pseudomonas in chronic cases"],"pathophysiology":"The mastoid air cells are continuous with the middle ear, so mucosal inflammation accompanies most episodes of acute otitis media. Mastoiditis develops when purulent material is trapped because the aditus ad antrum becomes blocked by oedema, allowing pressure and infection to build within the air cells. Persistent suppuration decalcifies and destroys the thin bony septa between cells, which fuse into an abscess cavity (coalescent mastoiditis). Pus may then track outward through the cortex to form a subperiosteal abscess, along fascial planes into the neck (Bezold abscess), or inward toward the dura, sigmoid sinus and posterior/middle cranial fossae, producing serious intracranial complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Severe acute otitis media without true mastoid coalescence","Postauricular lymphadenitis or infected sebaceous cyst","Suppurating postauricular lymph node from scalp infection","Furunculosis of the external canal with periauricular swelling","Histiocytosis or malignancy involving the temporal bone","Otitis externa with surrounding cellulitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the temporal bone to assess air cell coalescence and abscess formation","MRI with venography when intracranial extension or sinus thrombosis is suspected","Otoscopy for sagging canal wall, drum changes and discharge","Culture and sensitivity of ear or abscess pus to guide antibiotics","Full blood count and inflammatory markers to gauge severity and monitor response","Audiometry once the acute phase settles to document hearing"],"redFlags":["Headache, high fever, neck stiffness or altered consciousness suggesting meningitis or brain abscess (intracranial spread is an emergency)","Swelling and tenderness over the sigmoid sinus region with swinging fever pointing to lateral sinus thrombophlebitis","Neck swelling and torticollis suggesting a Bezold abscess tracking into the neck","Facial nerve weakness or vertigo indicating deeper temporal bone involvement","Rapid clinical deterioration or failure to respond to intravenous antibiotics"],"prognosis":"With early recognition, appropriate antibiotics and timely surgical drainage the outlook is good and hearing is usually preserved, but delayed treatment allows intracranial and intratemporal complications that carry significant morbidity and can be life-threatening.","pitfalls":["Attributing postauricular swelling to simple lymphadenopathy and delaying imaging","Assuming any mastoid opacity on imaging equals surgical mastoiditis without correlating clinical signs and bony coalescence","Under-treating with oral antibiotics when parenteral therapy and drainage are required","Missing evolving intracranial complications by not reassessing the non-improving patient","Overlooking that the pinna is pushed forward, mistaking the picture for external ear pathology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"measles":{"name":"Measles (Rubeola)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["rubeola"],"chapter":211,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1638","clinicalPearls":["Caused by measles virus, a paramyxovirus; transmitted primarily by respiratory droplets and, less commonly, by small-particle aerosols that can remain suspended in air, making it highly transmissible (Harrison 22e p.1639).","Incubation is ~10 days to fever onset and ~14 days to rash onset; this period may be shorter in infants and longer (up to 3 weeks) in adults (Harrison 22e p.1640).","Classic prodrome is fever and malaise beginning ~10 days after exposure, followed by the three Cs: cough, coryza, and conjunctivitis, increasing in severity over ~4 days (Harrison 22e p.1640).","Koplik's spots (bluish-white ~1 mm dots surrounded by erythema on the buccal mucosa opposite the lower molars) are pathognomonic, appear ~2 days before the rash, and fade with rash onset (Harrison 22e p.1641).","The exanthem is an erythematous maculopapular rash beginning behind the ears and on the neck/hairline, spreading cephalocaudally to face, trunk, arms, and then legs/feet by the end of the second day; it fades in the same order and may desquamate, especially in undernourished children (Harrison 22e p.1641).","Care is largely supportive; vitamin A is recommended for children with measles and can markedly reduce morbidity and mortality (Harrison 22e p.1641).","Two doses of live attenuated measles vaccine (commonly as MMR or MMRV) are highly protective; ~85% develop protective antibody after one dose at 9 months and ~95% at 12 months (Harrison 22e p.1642).","Post-exposure human immunoglobulin within 72 h usually prevents infection and, given up to 6 days after exposure, can still prevent or modify disease in susceptible high-risk contacts (Harrison 22e p.1642).","Endemic transmission was eliminated in the United States in 2000, but outbreaks recur from imported cases amplified in under- and unvaccinated communities; vaccine hesitancy and pandemic-related disruption of immunization programs have driven a global resurgence (Harrison 22e p.1638, p.1369)."],"pathophysiology":"Measles virus is deposited in the respiratory tract, oropharynx, or conjunctivae, then proliferates locally in dendritic cells and lymphocytes and spreads to draining lymph nodes. It enters the bloodstream by budding from infected lymphocytes, producing a viremia that disseminates infection throughout the body (respiratory epithelium, lymphatic tissue, spleen, lung, thymus, liver, skin). Signs and symptoms appear 8-12 days after infection as virus replicates in target organs and the host immune response develops; the characteristic rash is itself a consequence of the cellular immune response. TH1 (interferon-gamma) responses are essential for viral clearance and later TH2 (interleukin-4) responses drive protective antibody. Paradoxically, measles induces a state of immunosuppression lasting weeks to months that depresses responses to unrelated antigens, reduces the magnitude and diversity of pre-existing antibody (\"immune amnesia\"), and increases susceptibility to secondary bacterial and viral infections, accounting for much measles-related morbidity and death.","additionalDifferentials":["Rubella (German measles) - milder, no cough, with posterior auricular/suboccipital lymphadenopathy","Kawasaki disease","Infectious mononucleosis (atypical lymphocytosis, in contrast to the leukopenia common in measles)","Roseola (exanthem subitum) - rash appears after fever subsides","Scarlet fever","Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Enterovirus or adenovirus infection","Drug sensitivity/eruption"],"infectionMimics":["Rubella","Infectious mononucleosis (Epstein-Barr virus)","Roseola/exanthem subitum (HHV-6/HHV-7)","Scarlet fever","Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Enterovirus (echovirus, coxsackievirus) infection","Adenovirus infection","Early meningococcemia or rickettsial infection (morbilliform before petechiae/purpura)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Kawasaki disease","Drug eruption / drug sensitivity","Acute graft-versus-host disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["Detection of measles virus-specific IgM in a single serum or oral fluid specimen is diagnostic of acute infection","Fourfold or greater rise in measles virus-specific IgG between acute- and convalescent-phase sera","Note timing pitfall: IgM may not be detectable until 4-5 days after rash onset (false-negatives if drawn too early) and usually falls to undetectable within 4-8 weeks","Reverse-transcription PCR for measles virus RNA (sensitive/specific; can genotype and distinguish wild-type from vaccine strain)","Virus isolation in cell culture from respiratory secretions, nasopharyngeal/conjunctival swabs, blood, or urine","Direct detection of giant cells in respiratory secretions, urine, or biopsy tissue","Neutralization tests (highly correlated with protective immunity but expensive and laborious)"],"redFlags":["Pneumonia - secondary bacterial bronchopneumonia or measles giant cell pneumonitis (the latter in immunocompromised persons); recurrence or persistence of fever after the rash suggests secondary bacterial infection","Acute laryngotracheobronchitis (croup) with risk of airway obstruction, particularly in young children","Post-measles encephalomyelitis (~1 in 1000 cases) within 2 weeks of rash onset with fever, seizures, and neurologic abnormalities - thought to be autoimmune","Measles inclusion body encephalitis (MIBE) - rare, fatal, in those with defective cellular immunity, months after infection","Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE) - slowly progressive, fatal, 5-15 years after infection (mostly in those infected at <2 years)","Diarrhea worsening undernutrition","Absent or delayed rash in immunocompromised or severely undernourished children with impaired cellular immunity - associated with a high case-fatality rate"],"prognosis":"Most persons recover and develop long-term (generally lifelong) protective immunity. Case-fatality varies with age at infection, nutritional and immunologic status, vaccine coverage, and access to care. In most developed countries the case-fatality rate is 0.01-0.1%, but in endemic areas of sub-Saharan Africa it may be 5-10% or higher, and in refugee camps and internally displaced populations rates have reached 20-30%. Previously vaccinated persons who become infected have less severe disease and significantly lower mortality. Measles still causes more than 100,000 deaths per year worldwide.","pitfalls":["Patients are contagious before recognizable disease appears, which undermines isolation measures; nosocomial transmission in waiting rooms, hospitals, and physicians' offices is well recognized.","Clinical diagnosis is difficult during the prodrome, when the rash is attenuated by passively acquired antibody or prior immunization, when the rash is absent/delayed in immunocompromised or severely undernourished children, and in low-incidence regions.","Measles virus-specific IgM may be falsely negative if serum is obtained too early (within the first few days of rash onset).","Persons with impaired cellular immunity may not develop the characteristic rash, may develop giant cell pneumonitis, and have a high case-fatality rate.","There is no specific antiviral therapy; ribavirin benefit has not been conclusively demonstrated in trials.","The 1998 report hypothesizing an MMR-autism link was a misinterpreted temporal association, has been refuted by numerous studies, and was retracted - there is no causal link."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal grading scale. Severity is driven by host and population factors: severe or fatal disease occurs in those with impaired/deficient T-lymphocyte (cellular) immunity and in malnourished/undernourished children (e.g., vitamin A deficiency), among whom the rash may be absent and giant cell pneumonitis and high case-fatality occur; pregnant women, immunocompromised persons, and infants <1 year are at risk of severe measles and are prioritized for immunoglobulin prophylaxis.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1638","Harrison 22e p.1639","Harrison 22e p.1640","Harrison 22e p.1641","Harrison 22e p.1642","Harrison 22e p.1117 (SSPE)","Harrison 22e p.1369 (travel/outbreaks)"],"crossLinks":["ENCEPHALITIS"]},"meconium_aspiration_syndrome":{"name":"Meconium Aspiration Syndrome","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MAS","meconium aspiration"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Respiratory distress in a newborn born through meconium-stained amniotic fluid, when meconium is aspirated into the airways around the time of birth.","It occurs mainly in term and post-term infants and is associated with fetal distress and intrauterine hypoxia.","Affected infants may be meconium-stained, have distress from birth with tachypnoea and recession, and show a barrel-shaped, hyperinflated chest.","The chest radiograph typically shows patchy or coarse infiltrates with areas of hyperinflation and atelectasis, and air leaks are common.","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn frequently complicates the syndrome and contributes to severe hypoxaemia.","Routine intrapartum suctioning of the airway and routine tracheal suction of vigorous meconium-stained infants are no longer recommended.","Non-vigorous infants born through meconium may need resuscitation, and management is largely supportive with oxygen and respiratory support.","Severe cases may require mechanical ventilation, surfactant, inhaled nitric oxide for pulmonary hypertension, and occasionally extracorporeal membrane oxygenation.","Air leaks such as pneumothorax and pneumomediastinum are important and potentially sudden complications."],"pathophysiology":"Intrauterine stress and hypoxia can provoke passage of meconium into the amniotic fluid and gasping that draws it into the airways. Aspirated meconium causes mechanical airway obstruction with a ball-valve effect producing air trapping and air leaks, a chemical pneumonitis with inflammation, and inactivation of surfactant. These changes cause ventilation-perfusion mismatch and hypoxaemia, and, combined with hypoxia-driven pulmonary vasoconstriction, promote persistent pulmonary hypertension with right-to-left shunting.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal pneumonia or sepsis","Transient tachypnoea of the newborn","Respiratory distress syndrome (in less mature infants)","Persistent pulmonary hypertension from other causes","Air-leak syndromes","Congenital heart disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph for patchy infiltrates, hyperinflation, and air leaks","Pulse oximetry (pre- and post-ductal) and blood gas analysis","Echocardiography to assess for pulmonary hypertension","Sepsis screen when infection cannot be excluded","Continuous cardiorespiratory monitoring"],"redFlags":["Severe or refractory hypoxaemia despite respiratory support","Large pre- to post-ductal saturation gradient suggesting pulmonary hypertension","Sudden deterioration indicating pneumothorax or other air leak","Signs of hypoxic-ischaemic injury to other organs","Escalating ventilatory requirements or shock"],"prognosis":"Most infants with mild to moderate disease recover with supportive care, but severe disease complicated by persistent pulmonary hypertension or air leaks carries significant morbidity and mortality; survivors may have transient respiratory sequelae.","pitfalls":["Performing routine intrapartum or tracheal suctioning that is no longer recommended","Underestimating the risk of pneumothorax and missing a sudden air leak","Failing to recognise coexisting persistent pulmonary hypertension","Overlooking associated hypoxic-ischaemic injury to other organs","Delaying escalation of respiratory support in rapidly deteriorating infants"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"megaloblastic_anemias":{"name":"Megaloblastic Anemias","system":"Hematologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Vitamin B12 deficiency anemia","Cobalamin deficiency anemia","Folate deficiency anemia","Pernicious anemia"],"chapter":104,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.780","clinicalPearls":["Megaloblastic anemias share a defect in DNA synthesis affecting rapidly dividing marrow cells, producing characteristic morphology with a hypercellular marrow and ineffective erythropoiesis (Harrison 22e p.780).","The usual cause is deficiency of cobalamin (vitamin B12) or folate, but the picture can also arise from genetic or acquired abnormalities of their metabolism or from DNA-synthesis defects unrelated to either vitamin (Harrison 22e p.780).","Body cobalamin stores (about 2-3 mg) are large enough to last 3-4 years if intake stops, whereas folate stores (about 10 mg total, mostly hepatic) suffice for only 3-4 months, so severe folate deficiency can develop rapidly (Harrison 22e p.780, p.781).","Cobalamin is made only by microorganisms; the sole human source is animal-derived food, so strict vegans are at risk of dietary deficiency (Harrison 22e p.781, p.786).","Pernicious anemia, the dominant cause of severe cobalamin deficiency in Western countries, is gastric atrophy with severe lack of intrinsic factor; median onset is age 70-80, it clusters with other organ-specific autoimmune diseases (thyroid disease, vitiligo, type 1 diabetes, Addison disease), and gastric output of acid, pepsin, and intrinsic factor is severely reduced with raised serum gastrin and low pepsinogen I (Harrison 22e p.785, p.786).","Cobalamin deficiency can cause neurologic disease (subacute combined degeneration) with demyelination of posterior and lateral spinal columns, peripheral neuropathy, optic atrophy, and cerebral changes including dementia and psychosis; patients (more often male) present with paresthesias, weakness, or gait difficulty, often with positive Romberg and Lhermitte signs (Harrison 22e p.783).","Peripheral blood shows oval macrocytes with marked aniso-poikilocytosis, an MCV usually >100 fL (unless coexisting microcytosis), and hypersegmented neutrophils (more than five nuclear lobes) (Harrison 22e p.784).","In a nonanemic patient, a few macrocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils may be the only clue to underlying deficiency, and serum cobalamin may be borderline; supporting tests include MMA, IF/parietal-cell antibodies, and serum gastrin (Harrison 22e p.783, p.784).","Periconceptional folic acid reduces neural tube defects (anencephaly, meningomyelocele, encephalocele, spina bifida) by roughly 80%; the lower the maternal folate, the greater the fetal risk (Harrison 22e p.783).","Older patients with iron deficiency should also be assessed for cobalamin deficiency and vice versa, since coexistence is common (15-20% over age 60) and iron deficiency may blunt the macrocytosis (Harrison 22e p.789).","Removal of 1.2 m or more of terminal ileum, total gastrectomy, fish tapeworm (Diphyllobothrium latum) infestation, and intestinal stagnant-loop syndrome are recognized malabsorptive causes of cobalamin deficiency (Harrison 22e p.786, p.787)."],"pathophysiology":"The common defect is impaired DNA synthesis in rapidly dividing cells. In folate or cobalamin deficiency, conversion of deoxyuridine monophosphate (dUMP) to deoxythymidine monophosphate (dTMP) fails because the folate coenzyme 5,10-methylene-tetrahydrofolate, needed for this step, becomes scarce. The shortage of dTTP precursor slows DNA replication, leaving single-stranded breaks; an alternative theory invokes misincorporation of uracil into DNA. Cobalamin is a cofactor for two reactions: methylmalonyl-CoA mutase and the methionine-synthase-mediated methylation of homocysteine to methionine, which also requires 5-methyl-THF. In cobalamin deficiency, 5-methyl-THF accumulates in plasma while intracellular THF (the substrate for folate polyglutamate formation) falls — the methylfolate trap (THF starvation) — explaining high serum folate but low cell folate, and why the anemia of cobalamin deficiency can respond to large folic acid doses. Death of nucleated red cells in the marrow (ineffective erythropoiesis) produces unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, raised LDH, raised urine urobilinogen, and reduced haptoglobins (Harrison 22e p.782, p.783, p.785).","additionalDifferentials":["Megaloblastic anemia from antifolate/antimetabolite drugs (methotrexate, pyrimethamine, trimethoprim; hydroxyurea, cytarabine, mercaptopurine, thioguanine, AZT) (Harrison 22e p.780, p.790)","Orotic aciduria (responds to uridine) (Harrison 22e p.780, p.790)","Thiamine-responsive megaloblastic anemia (SLC19A2 transporter defect; may have diabetes, deafness, ringed sideroblasts) (Harrison 22e p.780, p.790)","Megaloblastic changes in acute myeloid leukemia and myelodysplasia (refractory to folate/cobalamin) (Harrison 22e p.780, p.790)","Macrocytosis without true megaloblastic change: alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism, pregnancy, myelodysplasia, myeloma/paraproteinemia, aplastic anemia, smoking (Harrison 22e p.790)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Myelodysplasia (megaloblastoid marrow may mimic megaloblastic anemia and, in severe cases, accumulation of blasts may mimic acute myeloid leukemia) (Harrison 22e p.785)","Autoimmune hemolytic anemia (a weakly positive direct antiglobulin test due to complement from ineffective erythropoiesis can lead to a false diagnosis) (Harrison 22e p.785)","Other causes of macrocytosis: alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism, pregnancy (Harrison 22e p.790)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum cobalamin (ELISA or competitive-binding luminescence assay); note IF antibodies can cause falsely normal results in up to 50% of pernicious anemia cases (Harrison 22e p.788)","Serum methylmalonic acid (raised in cobalamin deficiency sufficient for anemia/neuropathy; fluctuates in renal failure) (Harrison 22e p.788, p.789)","Serum homocysteine (raised in early cobalamin and folate deficiency but nonspecific) (Harrison 22e p.789)","Serum folate and red cell folate (red cell folate reflects body stores; false-normal after transfusion or with high reticulocytes) (Harrison 22e p.789)","Serum gastrin (raised in pernicious anemia) and pepsinogen I (low) (Harrison 22e p.785, p.789)","Intrinsic factor and parietal cell antibodies; gastric endoscopy/biopsy (Harrison 22e p.786, p.789)","Bone marrow examination in selected severely anemic patients (Harrison 22e p.784)","Transglutaminase antibodies to exclude gluten-induced enteropathy in folate deficiency (Harrison 22e p.789)"],"redFlags":["Neurologic signs of subacute combined degeneration: loss of proprioception/vibration, positive Romberg and Lhermitte signs, ataxic/spastic gait, hyperreflexia (Harrison 22e p.783)","Cerebral involvement: dementia, depression, psychosis, cognitive impairment, visual impairment from optic atrophy (Harrison 22e p.783)","Nonanemic patient with neurologic/psychiatric features and low or borderline serum cobalamin (deficiency must be actively confirmed) (Harrison 22e p.783)","Severe thrombocytopenia (rarely <40 x 10^9/L) with bruising (Harrison 22e p.783, p.784)","Marked marrow blast accumulation in severe cases mimicking acute leukemia (Harrison 22e p.785)"],"prognosis":"With regular treatment of pernicious anemia, life expectancy is normal in women; men historically had slightly reduced life expectancy due to a higher incidence of gastric carcinoma, with current data unavailable. Neurologic recovery depends on duration before treatment, and a therapeutic trial of at least 3 months may be needed to judge improvement in neuropsychiatric cases. Untreated severe deficiency causes progressive anemia, neurologic deterioration, and infertility (Harrison 22e p.783, p.786).","pitfalls":["Intrinsic-factor antibodies can produce falsely normal serum cobalamin in up to 50% of pernicious anemia patients tested by certain assays — a normal level does not exclude pernicious anemia when clinical suspicion is strong (Harrison 22e p.788).","Giving large doses of folic acid before excluding/correcting cobalamin deficiency can allow cobalamin neuropathy to progress despite hematologic response (Harrison 22e p.789, p.790).","Serum folate falls with recent poor diet but rises in severe cobalamin deficiency (methylfolate trap) and in stagnant-loop syndrome, so it can mislead (Harrison 22e p.789).","Mildly elevated MMA and/or homocysteine occur in up to 30% of healthy volunteers and in 15% of elderly with normal cobalamin, so cutoffs and clinical significance are uncertain (Harrison 22e p.789).","Coexisting iron deficiency may mask macrocytosis; the term 'megaloblastoid' is best avoided as it refers to myelodysplastic change refractory to vitamin therapy (Harrison 22e p.785, p.789).","A weakly positive direct antiglobulin test from ineffective erythropoiesis can be misread as autoimmune hemolytic anemia (Harrison 22e p.785)."],"severityClassification":"Severity is gauged by the degree of anemia and parallels the morphologic changes; marrow involvement is described as severe, intermediate, mild, or early. In megaloblastic anemia due to cobalamin deficiency the serum cobalamin is usually <74 pmol/L (100 ng/L), with 74-148 pmol/L (100-200 ng/L) regarded as borderline; the more severe the deficiency, the lower the level, and very low levels occur with spinal cord involvement even without anemia (Harrison 22e p.785, p.788).","references":["Harrison 22e p.780","Harrison 22e p.781","Harrison 22e p.782","Harrison 22e p.783","Harrison 22e p.784","Harrison 22e p.785","Harrison 22e p.786","Harrison 22e p.787","Harrison 22e p.788","Harrison 22e p.789","Harrison 22e p.790"],"crossLinks":[]},"melasma":{"name":"Melasma","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chloasma","mask of pregnancy","melasma faciei"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as symmetrical, poorly defined brown to grey-brown patches on sun-exposed facial skin, typically the cheeks, forehead, upper lip, and chin","It is far more common in women and in people with darker skin phototypes, and often follows pregnancy or hormonal exposure","Recognised patterns are centrofacial, malar, and mandibular; a similar eruption can also occur on the forearms","Wood's lamp examination can help gauge the depth of pigment, with epidermal pigment enhanced and dermal pigment less so, which influences treatment expectations","Ultraviolet and visible light, including screen and heat exposure, are major drivers, so broad-spectrum photoprotection with a tinted (iron-oxide-containing) product is the cornerstone of care","Combination topical lightening agents and gentle chemical peels can help, but aggressive procedures risk post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation, especially in darker skin","Review and, where feasible, stop contributory hormonal exposures such as combined oral contraception","Set realistic expectations: recurrence is common and lifelong sun protection and maintenance are usually needed"],"pathophysiology":"Melasma reflects an acquired disorder of localised hyperpigmentation in which functionally hyperactive melanocytes deposit excess melanin in the epidermis, dermis, or both. Ultraviolet and visible light, hormonal influences (pregnancy, oestrogen), and a genetic predisposition upregulate melanogenic signalling, while an altered dermal environment with solar elastosis, increased vascularity, and a disrupted basement membrane allows pigment to drop into the dermis. This basement-membrane damage helps explain the chronic, relapsing nature and the resistance of dermal pigment to topical therapy.","additionalDifferentials":["Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation following acne or dermatitis","Solar lentigines and other photo-induced pigmentation","Drug-induced pigmentation","Ochronosis (which can be exogenous, from overuse of hydroquinone)","Riehl's melanosis / pigmented contact dermatitis","Naevus of Ota or acquired dermal melanocytosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Primarily a clinical diagnosis supported by dermoscopy","Wood's lamp examination to help assess pigment depth","Skin biopsy only in atypical or treatment-resistant cases","Review of hormonal and drug history as part of assessment"],"redFlags":["Asymmetric, irregular, or evolving pigmented lesions raising concern for melanoma rather than melasma","Rapidly darkening or nodular change within a pigmented area","Paradoxical darkening or blue-black discolouration suggesting exogenous ochronosis from prolonged skin-lightening use"],"prognosis":"Melasma is typically chronic and relapsing; pregnancy-related disease may fade after delivery, but many patients experience persistence or recurrence, and long-term photoprotection with maintenance therapy is usually required to sustain improvement.","pitfalls":["Neglecting visible-light protection and relying only on conventional UV sunscreen","Provoking post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation with overly aggressive peels or lasers in darker skin","Causing exogenous ochronosis through prolonged, unsupervised use of skin-lightening agents","Promising a permanent cure rather than emphasising control and maintenance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"menieres_disease":{"name":"Meniere's disease","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Meniere disease","endolymphatic hydrops","Meniere's syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by a triad of episodic vertigo, fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus, often with a sense of aural fullness","Vertigo attacks typically last from minutes to hours, distinguishing them from the seconds-long spells of positional vertigo","Hearing loss initially affects lower frequencies and fluctuates, but tends to become permanent and progressive over time","Usually begins unilaterally, though the second ear can become involved in a proportion of patients","The underlying disorder is endolymphatic hydrops, an increase in endolymph volume within the membranous labyrinth","Management principles include dietary salt restriction, diuretics and vestibular sedatives for acute attacks, with lifestyle measures to reduce triggers","Escalating options for intractable disease range from intratympanic therapy to surgical procedures such as endolymphatic sac decompression or labyrinthine ablation","Sudden drop attacks (Tumarkin crises) can cause falls without warning and pose an injury risk"],"pathophysiology":"Meniere's disease is attributed to endolymphatic hydrops, an abnormal accumulation of endolymph that distends the membranous labyrinth. Impaired production, circulation or absorption of endolymph, potentially involving the endolymphatic sac, raises pressure within the scala media. Episodic rupture or dysfunction of the membranes allows mixing of endolymph and perilymph, disturbing the ionic environment of the sensory hair cells and producing acute vertigo, fluctuating hearing loss and tinnitus. Repeated insults cause progressive damage to cochlear and vestibular structures, leading to permanent hearing loss and vestibular hypofunction.","additionalDifferentials":["Vestibular migraine","Vestibular neuritis or labyrinthitis","Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","Acoustic neuroma (vestibular schwannoma)","Autoimmune inner ear disease","Perilymph fistula"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pure tone audiometry to document fluctuating low-frequency sensorineural loss","Tests of vestibular function such as caloric testing and video head impulse testing","Electrocochleography and other specialised tests where available","MRI of the internal auditory meatus to exclude a vestibular schwannoma","Blood tests to exclude alternative causes when clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Progressive unilateral hearing loss with tinnitus raising concern for vestibular schwannoma","Neurological signs suggesting a central cause","Sudden severe sensorineural hearing loss requiring urgent assessment","Recurrent drop attacks causing injury"],"prognosis":"The course is variable and unpredictable, with clustered attacks and remissions; vertigo often diminishes over years as vestibular function declines, but hearing loss tends to be progressive and permanent.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing Meniere's without excluding a retrocochlear lesion such as acoustic neuroma","Confusing it with vestibular migraine, which can present similarly","Overusing vestibular sedatives beyond acute attacks and impairing compensation","Underestimating the psychosocial and safety impact of unpredictable attacks"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"meningioma":{"name":"Meningioma","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["meningeal tumour","dural-based tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Meningiomas are the most common primary intracranial tumours and are usually slow-growing and benign, arising from the meningeal covering of the brain and spinal cord","Many are asymptomatic and discovered incidentally on imaging performed for other reasons","When symptomatic, features depend on location and result from gradual compression, producing focal deficits, seizures, or symptoms of raised intracranial pressure","They are more common in women and their growth can be influenced by hormonal factors; some carry hormone receptors","On imaging they are typically well-defined, extra-axial, dural-based, avidly and homogeneously enhancing masses, often with a dural tail and sometimes adjacent bony change","Small asymptomatic meningiomas are frequently managed by surveillance imaging, reserving intervention for growth or symptoms","Symptomatic or enlarging tumours are usually treated by surgical resection, with radiotherapy or radiosurgery for residual, recurrent, higher-grade, or surgically inaccessible lesions","Although most are low grade, a minority are atypical or malignant with a greater tendency to recur, so histological grading guides follow-up"],"pathophysiology":"Meningiomas arise from meningothelial (arachnoid cap) cells of the meninges and are therefore extra-axial, growing outside the brain parenchyma and compressing it from the surface. They are typically slow-growing and attached to the dura, and their growth can distort adjacent brain, cranial nerves, and vessels, provoke surrounding oedema, and erode or hyperostose overlying bone. Symptoms arise from mass effect at the specific location rather than infiltration. Most are histologically low grade, but atypical and anaplastic variants show more aggressive behaviour and higher recurrence rates. Prior cranial irradiation and certain genetic conditions increase risk.","additionalDifferentials":["Dural metastasis","Solitary fibrous tumour / haemangiopericytoma","Schwannoma (at skull base sites)","Dural-based lymphoma or granulomatous disease","Extra-axial abscess or empyema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI brain or spine with contrast to characterise the extra-axial dural-based mass and dural tail","CT to assess associated hyperostosis, calcification, or bony erosion","Serial imaging to monitor growth of incidental or conservatively managed lesions","Histopathology and grading after biopsy or resection","Vascular imaging when preoperative embolisation of a highly vascular tumour is considered"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth or atypical imaging features suggesting a higher-grade tumour","Progressive neurological deficit, new seizures, or cranial nerve compromise","Signs of raised intracranial pressure from a large lesion with oedema and mass effect","Acute deterioration from peritumoural oedema or, rarely, haemorrhage"],"prognosis":"Most meningiomas are benign and, when completely resected, have an excellent prognosis with low recurrence; incompletely removed, atypical, or malignant tumours recur more often and may need adjuvant radiotherapy and closer surveillance.","pitfalls":["Assuming every dural-based enhancing mass is a benign meningioma without considering metastasis or other lesions","Over-treating small, stable, asymptomatic incidental tumours","Underestimating the recurrence risk of atypical or malignant subtypes","Overlooking peritumoural oedema as a treatable cause of symptoms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"meningococcal_disease":{"name":"Meningococcal Disease","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["meningococcemia","Neisseria meningitidis infection"],"chapter":160,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1244-1253","clinicalPearls":["Neisseria meningitidis most commonly causes asymptomatic nasopharyngeal colonization; invasive disease is rare and usually presents as either bacterial meningitis or meningococcal septicemia (p.1244).","A nonblanching petechial or purpuric rash develops in over 80% of cases, but it is often absent early in the illness and may begin as a blanching maculopapular or urticarial eruption indistinguishable from common viral rashes before becoming petechial/purpuric over hours; some patients, including those with overwhelming sepsis, have no rash at all (p.1248).","In the most severe cases large purpuric lesions develop (purpura fulminans) with signs of peripheral ischemia (p.1248).","Roughly 30-50% present with a meningitis syndrome alone, up to 40% of meningitis patients also have features of septicemia, and meningococcal septicemia alone accounts for up to 20% of cases (p.1248).","In fulminant disease death may occur within hours of the first symptoms; early septicemic symptoms are nonspecific and influenza-like (fever, headache, myalgia, vomiting, abdominal pain) (p.1248).","In children, hypotension is a late sign; they more commonly present with compensated shock (tachycardia, poor peripheral perfusion, normal blood pressure) (p.1248).","Terminal complement component (C5-9), properdin or factor D deficiency, and treatment with complement inhibitors such as eculizumab, increase the risk of meningococcal disease (up to ~600-fold) and may cause recurrent attacks (p.1247).","Chronic meningococcemia is rarely recognized and presents as recurrent episodes of petechial rash with fever, joint pain/arthritis, and splenomegaly, with bacteremia that characteristically clears without treatment then recurs (p.1248-1249).","Tobacco smoking (and passive smoke exposure) is an important risk factor that damages the nasopharyngeal epithelium and increases both carriage and invasive disease (p.1247).","Immunization is regarded as the only rational approach to prevention at a population level; chemoprophylaxis of close contacts aims to eradicate carriage of the invasive strain (p.1250-1253)."],"pathophysiology":"N. meningitidis colonizes the human nasopharynx, then in a susceptible individual may invade the mucosa into the bloodstream, usually within a few days of acquisition. The polysaccharide capsule is a key virulence factor providing resistance to phagocytosis; acapsular strains rarely cause invasive disease. During growth meningococci shed outer-membrane blebs containing lipopolysaccharide (LPS, endotoxin), which binds CD14/TLR4 to trigger an inflammatory cascade (TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6, IL-8, IL-10, PAI-1 and others). Disease severity relates to both endotoxin levels and the magnitude of the inflammatory response. Endothelial injury is central to meningococcemia, causing increased vascular permeability (capillary leak with hypovolemia, tissue and pulmonary edema), altered vascular tone, loss of thromboresistance, intravascular coagulation, and myocardial dysfunction. Procoagulant activation with tissue factor upregulation, downregulation of anticoagulant pathways (loss of thrombomodulin/protein C receptors; reduced antithrombin III, protein C, protein S, tissue factor pathway inhibitor) and impaired thrombolysis from high PAI-1 produce purpura fulminans and skin/limb infarction. Shock results from hypovolemia plus myocardial depression (hypoxia, metabolic derangements such as hypocalcemia, cytokines). Bacteria reaching the meninges provoke a local cytokine-driven inflammatory response that can cause cerebral edema and raised intracranial pressure (p.1247-1248).","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of bacterial meningitis (presentation of meningococcal meningitis is indistinguishable unless an associated petechial/purpuric rash or gram-negative diplococci are present) (p.1248-1249)","Differential of chronic meningococcemia: bacterial endocarditis, acute rheumatic fever, Henoch-Schonlein purpura, infectious mononucleosis, disseminated gonococcal infection, immune-mediated vasculitis (p.1248-1249)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral infections (enteroviruses, influenza and other respiratory viruses, measles, Epstein-Barr virus, cytomegalovirus, parvovirus) - most patients presenting with a petechial/purpuric rash have a viral infection (p.1248)","Pneumococcal, streptococcal, staphylococcal, or other gram-negative bacterial sepsis (p.1248)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Protein C or S deficiency (including post-varicella protein S deficiency) (p.1248)","Platelet disorders (e.g., idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura, drug effects, bone marrow infiltration) (p.1248)","Henoch-Schonlein purpura, connective tissue disorders, and trauma (including nonaccidental injury in children) (p.1248)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood culture for confirmation and public health investigation (positive in up to ~75% of cases); avoid media containing sodium polyanethol sulfonate, which may inhibit meningococcal growth, and avoid delay in transport (p.1249)","Real-time PCR of whole blood increases diagnostic yield by more than 40% and may remain positive for several days after antibiotics (p.1249)","Lumbar puncture for CSF in suspected meningitis (unless contraindicated); CSF Gram stain is up to ~80% sensitive, CSF culture sensitivity ~90%, plus PCR where available; CSF latex agglutination antigen testing is insensitive and should be replaced by molecular diagnosis (p.1249)","Inflammatory markers (WBC count, C-reactive protein, procalcitonin, ESR) may support the diagnosis but can be normal or low in rapidly progressive disease and do not exclude it (p.1249)","Severe septicemia laboratory findings: hypoglycemia, acidosis, hypokalemia, hypocalcemia, hypomagnesemia, hypophosphatemia, anemia, coagulopathy (p.1249)","Measure total hemolytic complement activity to assess for complement deficiency in patients with recurrent disease, particularly non-B capsular groups (p.1247)"],"redFlags":["A nonblanching (petechial or purpuric) rash in a febrile patient should be treated as a medical emergency suggestive of meningococcal disease (p.1248)","Purpura fulminans / large purpuric lesions with peripheral ischemia (p.1248)","Signs of shock: tachycardia, poor peripheral perfusion, tachypnea, oliguria, cold mottled extremities, limb pain (hypotension is a late sign in children) (p.1248)","Reduced level of consciousness, agitation, or confusion (decreased cerebral perfusion or raised intracranial pressure) (p.1248)","Features of raised intracranial pressure: reduced consciousness, relative bradycardia and hypertension, focal neurologic signs, abnormal posturing, unequal/dilated/poorly reactive pupils (p.1248)","Rapid progression - death may occur within hours of first symptoms in fulminant cases (p.1248)"],"prognosis":"About 10% of patients with meningococcal disease die despite antimicrobial therapy and intensive care. Fulminant meningococcemia is more likely to cause death or ischemic skin loss than meningitis alone. Factors associated with poorer prognosis include shock; extremes of age (infancy and old age) and adolescence; coma; purpura fulminans; disseminated intravascular coagulation; thrombocytopenia; leukopenia; absence of meningitis; metabolic acidosis; low antithrombin and protein S/C levels; high PAI-1; and a low ESR or C-reactive protein. The Glasgow Meningococcal Septicaemia Prognostic Score (GMSPS) performs well for severity assessment, though scoring systems do not direct specific interventions; the management priority remains recognition and urgent treatment of compromised airway, breathing, or circulation. Most patients improve rapidly with appropriate antibiotics and supportive care. Complications include scarring after purpuric skin necrosis (~10%, sometimes requiring skin grafting), amputation in 1-2% of survivors after peripheral ischemia or compartment syndrome, hearing loss (~5%), neurologic complications (~7%), chronic pain, and psychological/psychosocial sequelae (p.1249-1250).","pitfalls":["A normal or low WBC count and inflammatory markers do not exclude meningococcal disease and may occur in rapidly progressive disease (p.1249)","The rash may be absent early or absent altogether (including in overwhelming sepsis), and may initially appear blanching/viral before becoming petechial or purpuric (p.1248)","Most patients with a petechial/purpuric rash and fever do NOT have meningococcal disease - fewer than 10% of children (and in some settings under 1%) with this presentation are found to have it, so most have a viral cause (p.1248)","Lumbar puncture should generally be avoided in meningococcal septicemia because positioning may critically compromise circulation in hypovolemic shock; it is also contraindicated with raised intracranial pressure, uncorrected shock, coagulopathy, thrombocytopenia, respiratory insufficiency, local infection, or ongoing convulsions (p.1249)","A normal CT brain scan does not exclude raised intracranial pressure in meningococcal meningitis (p.1249)","CSF latex agglutination antigen testing and urinary antigen testing are insensitive; throat-swab identification has limited diagnostic value because N. meningitidis is normal nasopharyngeal flora (p.1249)","Postmeningococcal reactive (immune-complex) disease arising ~4-10 days after onset (rash, arthritis, iritis, pericarditis/polyserositis with fever) can be mistaken for relapse and lead to unnecessarily prolonged antibiotics; it resolves spontaneously (p.1249)","Aggressive fluid resuscitation with unbuffered electrolyte solutions was found to increase mortality in febrile African children (p.1249-1250)"],"severityClassification":"Clinical spectrum spans asymptomatic nasopharyngeal carriage, occult bacteremia, localized/focal infection (pneumonia, arthritis, osteomyelitis, pericarditis, conjunctivitis), meningitis, and meningococcal septicemia, with chronic meningococcemia as a relapsing form. The Glasgow Meningococcal Septicaemia Prognostic Score (GMSPS) is used for severity assessment. Fulminant meningococcemia with purpura fulminans and Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome (overwhelming meningococcal sepsis) represents the most severe end of the spectrum (p.1244, p.1248-1249).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1244","Harrison 22e p.1247","Harrison 22e p.1248","Harrison 22e p.1249","Harrison 22e p.1250","Harrison 22e p.1253"],"crossLinks":["MENINGITIS","SEPSIS","SEPTIC_SHOCK"]},"meniscal_injury":{"name":"Meniscal injury","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["meniscal tear","torn meniscus","cartilage tear of the knee","bucket-handle tear"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["In younger patients tears usually follow a twisting injury on a loaded, flexed knee; in older patients they are often degenerative and may occur with minimal trauma","Typical symptoms are joint-line pain, an effusion developing over a day or so, and mechanical features such as clicking, catching, or locking","A knee locked in flexion that cannot be fully extended suggests a displaced bucket-handle tear and warrants prompt treatment","Joint-line tenderness and provocative rotation tests (e.g. McMurray's) support the diagnosis, and MRI is the investigation of choice","The peripheral (outer) third of the meniscus is vascular and can heal, favouring repair, whereas the avascular inner portion has poor healing potential","Repair is preferred where feasible, especially in younger patients with peripheral tears, to preserve meniscal function; partial meniscectomy is reserved for irreparable tears","Meniscus preservation is important because meniscectomy increases contact stress and the long-term risk of osteoarthritis","Degenerative tears in older knees with early arthritis often respond as well to conservative management and rehabilitation as to arthroscopic surgery"],"pathophysiology":"The menisci are fibrocartilaginous, wedge-shaped structures that deepen the tibial articular surface, distribute load, absorb shock, and contribute to joint stability and lubrication. A twisting force on a weight-bearing flexed knee shears the meniscus between the femoral and tibial condyles, producing longitudinal, radial, horizontal, or complex tears; a longitudinal tear whose free fragment displaces into the notch forms a bucket-handle lesion that can block extension. With age the meniscal tissue degenerates and tears more readily under low loads. Only the peripheral third has a blood supply capable of healing, so the location of a tear largely determines whether repair or excision is appropriate. Loss of meniscal tissue raises articular contact pressures and predisposes to degenerative change.","additionalDifferentials":["Anterior cruciate ligament tear","Collateral ligament injury","Osteochondral injury or loose body","Patellofemoral pain or patellar instability","Osteoarthritis of the knee","Plica syndrome","Osteochondritis dissecans"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the knee to characterise the tear and assess associated ligament and cartilage injury","Clinical joint-line palpation and provocative tests (e.g. McMurray's, Apley's grind)","Plain radiographs to assess for degenerative change or loose bodies and to exclude fracture","Diagnostic arthroscopy in selected cases where imaging is equivocal and intervention is planned"],"redFlags":["A truly locked knee unable to fully extend, suggesting a displaced bucket-handle tear needing prompt surgery","Acute tense haemarthrosis suggesting a significant associated injury such as an ACL rupture","Hot, swollen, systemically unwell knee raising concern for septic arthritis","Signs of associated ligamentous instability or fracture"],"prognosis":"Peripheral tears repaired successfully can restore durable meniscal function, while small stable tears may settle with conservative care; extensive meniscectomy relieves mechanical symptoms but increases the long-term likelihood of osteoarthritis, and degenerative tears in already arthritic knees have a more limited response to surgery.","pitfalls":["Performing meniscectomy on a degenerative tear in an arthritic knee that would do as well with rehabilitation","Removing repairable peripheral tissue rather than attempting repair, accelerating arthritis","Missing a locked knee from a displaced bucket-handle tear","Overlooking a concurrent ACL injury that leaves the meniscal repair at risk of re-tear"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"menopause_and_postmenopausal_hormone_thera":{"name":"Menopause and Postmenopausal Hormone Therapy","system":"Endocrine / Reproductive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Menopause","Perimenopause","Postmenopausal hormone therapy","Climacteric","Menopausal transition"],"chapter":407,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3142","clinicalPearls":["Menopause is the permanent cessation of menstruation from loss of ovarian follicular function and is diagnosed retrospectively after 12 months of amenorrhea; the average age in U.S. women is about 51 years.","Perimenopause is the transition preceding menopause when fertility wanes and cycles become irregular, typically beginning 2-8 years before the final menses (mean duration about 4 years); smoking advances the transition by roughly 2 years.","Perimenopause is marked by 'irregularly irregular' hormone levels rather than the consistently high FSH and low estradiol of established menopause; intermenstrual intervals often shorten (by about 3 days) due to an accelerated follicular phase.","FSH rises before LH during the transition, attributed to loss of inhibin and estrogen feedback; after menopause estradiol falls markedly while estrone is relatively preserved through peripheral aromatization of adrenal and ovarian androgens.","Vasomotor symptoms (hot flashes, night sweats), irregular bleeding, and vaginal dryness are strongly attributable to the transition; sleep disturbance has moderate supporting evidence, while mood swings, memory/concentration changes, incontinence, and sexual dysfunction are not clearly caused by ovarian aging alone.","Nearly 60% of women in one U.S. study reported hot flashes in the 2 years before their final menses; symptom intensity, frequency, duration, and quality-of-life impact are highly variable.","Estrogen therapy is the most effective treatment for vasomotor and genitourinary symptoms; randomized trial data suggest hormone therapy reduces menopausal symptoms by roughly 65-90%.","The 'timing hypothesis' holds that estrogen may slow early atherosclerosis but adversely affect advanced lesions, so women initiating hormone therapy early after menopause may differ in cardiovascular outcome from those starting many years later.","Primary prevention of cardiovascular disease should NOT be considered an expected benefit of hormone therapy; an increased stroke risk and a small early increase in coronary risk should be weighed.","A 1-year absence of spontaneous menses reliably indicates ovulation has ceased, but natural menstrual pattern cannot be assessed while a woman is taking oral contraceptives.","Estrogen reduces bone turnover and resorption; postmenopausal estrogen (with or without a progestogen) increases spine bone density by about 4-6% and hip density by about 2-3%, with WHI showing roughly a 33% reduction in hip fractures.","Unopposed estrogen markedly raises endometrial cancer risk; adding a progestogen opposes this effect and can even reduce endometrial cancer risk, which is why estrogen-only therapy is reserved for women without a uterus."],"pathophysiology":"Ovarian mass and fertility decline sharply after age 35 and more steeply during perimenopause as the pool of primary follicles, depleting since before birth, runs down. Altered folliculogenesis and reduced inhibin secretion cause FSH to rise; the propensity for anovulatory cycles produces a relatively hyperestrogenic, hypoprogestagenic milieu that may underlie the increased perimenopausal incidence of endometrial hyperplasia/carcinoma, uterine polyps, and leiomyomata. With transition into menopause, estradiol falls markedly while estrone is comparatively preserved through peripheral aromatization of adrenal and ovarian androgens; FSH rises more than LH owing to loss of inhibin plus estrogen feedback. Exogenous estrogen lowers LDL and raises HDL cholesterol by about 10-15% and favorably affects lipoprotein(a), LDL oxidation, and endothelial function, but it also raises triglycerides, promotes coagulation (factor VII, prothrombin fragments, fibrinopeptide A), and elevates C-reactive protein, providing a mechanistic basis for both potential benefits and thrombotic/inflammatory harms.","additionalDifferentials":["Pregnancy as a cause of amenorrhea in perimenopausal women (unintentional pregnancy rate in the forties rivals that of adolescents)","Premature ovarian insufficiency / primary ovarian insufficiency when ovarian failure occurs well before the usual age","Thyroid dysfunction or other endocrine causes of menstrual irregularity and vasomotor-like symptoms","Uterine structural lesions (leiomyomata, endometrial polyps, hyperplasia or carcinoma) presenting as abnormal/heavy bleeding","Other age-related conditions causing midlife mood, sleep, cognitive, or sexual complaints not attributable to ovarian senescence"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Thyroid disease producing vasomotor-like flushing and menstrual change","Anxiety or panic disorder mimicking hot flashes","Endometrial hyperplasia or carcinoma presenting as abnormal bleeding rather than physiologic transition","Medication effects or other causes of secondary amenorrhea"],"additionalInvestigations":["Menstrual cycle history as the principal STRAW+10 criterion for staging reproductive aging, with biomarkers as supportive criteria","FSH measurement: random level >25 IU/L is characteristic of the late menopausal transition; day-3 levels <20, 20 to <30, and >=30 IU/L indicate good, fair, and poor pregnancy likelihood respectively","Estradiol levels, recognizing that FSH and estradiol are imperfect diagnostic indicators in still-menstruating perimenopausal women due to marked intraindividual variability","Anti-mullerian hormone and inhibin B as additional markers of reproductive aging","Transvaginal ultrasound (with saline enhancement) to detect leiomyomata or polyps when bleeding is abnormal","Endometrial aspiration/biopsy to identify hyperplastic or premalignant changes when menorrhagia or abnormal bleeding occurs","Bone mineral density assessment and FRAX 10-year fracture risk estimation to guide osteoporosis decisions; 25-hydroxyvitamin D level >=50 nmol/L considered sufficient for bone maintenance"],"redFlags":["Unexplained or undiagnosed vaginal bleeding requires evaluation to exclude endometrial hyperplasia or carcinoma before attributing it to the transition","An acute coronary event or stroke while on hormone therapy mandates immediate discontinuation of therapy","New or worsening vasomotor symptoms accompanied by features suggesting thromboembolism, given the increased VTE risk with oral estrogen","Heavy menstrual bleeding (menorrhagia) in perimenopause warrants evaluation for uterine disorders rather than assumption of benign cause","Symptoms or signs of estrogen-dependent malignancy in a woman considering or using hormone therapy"],"prognosis":"Most peri- and postmenopausal symptoms are self-limited though variable in duration and intensity. In the WHI, many risks and benefits of hormone therapy dissipated within 5-7 years after stopping, though some persisted: with estrogen-progestin an elevated breast cancer risk continued over long follow-up and a reduced hip fracture risk and reduced endometrial cancer risk persisted; with estrogen alone the breast cancer risk reduction became significant on extended follow-up. Estrogen-progestin carried an overall unfavorable global benefit-risk profile that did not vary by 10-year age group, whereas estrogen-only therapy had a roughly neutral profile overall with a more favorable balance in younger women (relative risks for the global index of about 0.84, 0.99, and 1.17 in ages 50-59, 60-69, and 70-79). Over extended follow-up estrogen alone was associated with reduced all-cause mortality in women aged 50-59 (notably those with bilateral oophorectomy), while estrogen-progestin showed elevated breast cancer risk and a suggestion of increased breast cancer mortality.","pitfalls":["Observational studies overstated cardiovascular benefit because women choosing hormone therapy tend to be healthier and more health-conscious (healthy-user confounding); randomized trials did not confirm the benefit.","Relying on a single FSH or estradiol value to diagnose perimenopause in a menstruating woman is unreliable given extreme intraindividual variability.","Prescribing unopposed estrogen to a woman with an intact uterus markedly raises endometrial cancer and atypical hyperplasia risk; a progestogen is needed for endometrial protection.","Attributing midlife mood, cognitive, urinary, or sexual complaints to menopause when evidence does not support ovarian aging as the principal cause.","Counseling should emphasize absolute rather than relative risk measures, since absolute risks rise with age even when relative risks are similar.","Apparent excess breast cancer detection in early hormone therapy users may partly reflect more frequent screening mammography rather than a true biologic effect.","Assuming the cardiovascular and other effects of one formulation/route apply to all, since transdermal estrogen may carry lower thrombotic and gallbladder risk than oral estrogen."],"severityClassification":"The Stages of Reproductive Aging Workshop +10 (STRAW+10) system stages reproductive aging using menstrual cycle characteristics as principal criteria (e.g., persistent >=7-day differences in consecutive cycle length in the early transition; an interval of amenorrhea of >=60 days in the late transition) with endocrine markers (FSH, AMH, inhibin B, antral follicle count) as supportive criteria, spanning reproductive, menopausal transition (perimenopause), and postmenopause stages around the final menstrual period.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3142","Harrison 22e p.3143","Harrison 22e p.3144","Harrison 22e p.3145","Harrison 22e p.3146","Harrison 22e p.3147","Harrison 22e p.3148"],"crossLinks":[]},"menstrual_disorders_and_pelvic_pain":{"name":"Menstrual Disorders and Pelvic Pain","system":"Reproductive / Gynecologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Amenorrhea","Abnormal uterine bleeding","Dysfunctional uterine bleeding","Oligomenorrhea","Dysmenorrhea","Pelvic pain"],"chapter":405,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3132","clinicalPearls":["Amenorrhea is the absence of menses: primary when bleeding has never occurred without hormonal treatment, secondary when established cycles stop for 3-6 months; primary amenorrhea is rare (<1% of women), whereas roughly 3-5% of women have at least 3 months of secondary amenorrhea in a given year.","Pregnancy is the single most common cause of amenorrhea and must be excluded in every case with a beta-hCG, regardless of the history offered.","A normal cycle recurs every 21-35 days, lasts 4-7 days, and involves up to about 80 mL of blood loss; the term abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB) has replaced dysfunctional uterine bleeding and is categorized by the PALM-COEIN scheme (structural: polyp, adenomyosis, leiomyoma, malignancy/hyperplasia; non-structural: coagulopathy, ovulatory dysfunction, endometrial, iatrogenic, not yet classified).","Evaluation of ovulatory disorders rests on three initial tests: a pregnancy test, an FSH level (to localize the problem to the ovary versus a central cause), and assessment for hyperandrogenism.","FSH is a better marker of ovarian failure than LH because it reflects loss of negative feedback from both estradiol and the inhibins and is less variable; anti-mullerian hormone (AMH) is also low in primary ovarian insufficiency.","Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea is a diagnosis of exclusion, typically driven by a mismatch between energy expenditure and intake and/or significant stress, with suppressed GnRH, low gonadotropins, and normal prolactin; eating disorders, excessive exercise, and chronic disease must be specifically ruled out.","Isolated hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (IHH) is three times more common in men; about half of affected women have anosmia (Kallmann syndrome), and roughly half have an identifiable genetic cause.","Primary ovarian insufficiency (POI) - the preferred term over premature menopause/ovarian failure - denotes ovarian failure before age 40, accounts for about 10% of secondary amenorrhea, and can wax and wane so serial measurements may be needed.","Roughly up to one-third of women between menarche and menopause experience abnormal uterine bleeding at some point.","Up to 60% of pelvic pain is attributable to gastrointestinal causes (appendicitis, cholecystitis, infection, obstruction, diverticulitis, inflammatory bowel disease); urinary and musculoskeletal disorders are also common, so pelvic pain is frequently not gynecologic in origin.","Beta-hCG normally roughly doubles every 48 h in the early first trimester, and 99% of viable intrauterine pregnancies show at least a 53% rise over 2 days; the discriminatory zone (about 1500-3000 IU/mL) is the level above which a normal intrauterine pregnancy should be visible on ultrasound.","Dysmenorrhea begins with menstrual flow and eases over 12-72 h; primary dysmenorrhea is largely prostaglandin-mediated, while secondary dysmenorrhea reflects underlying pelvic pathology such as endometriosis or adenomyosis."],"pathophysiology":"Menstrual function depends on coordinated signaling among four compartments - hypothalamus, pituitary, ovary, and uterus/outflow tract - linked by feedback loops involving GnRH, the gonadotropins FSH and LH, and the ovarian products estradiol, progesterone, and the inhibins (inhibin A and B). Disorders divide into outflow-tract/uterine problems and ovulatory disorders. Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism arises when anatomic, genetic, or functional disturbances impair hypothalamic GnRH output or pituitary responsiveness, producing low estrogen with normal or low LH/FSH; in functional hypothalamic amenorrhea, energy deficit and stress raise CRH, suppress GnRH, and lower TRH input, with leptin and hypothalamic kisspeptin/neurokinin B/dynorphin signaling transducing metabolic and stress cues to the GnRH pulse generator. Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism (ovarian insufficiency) removes negative feedback, raising FSH and LH. In PCOS, abnormal GnRH pulsatility elevates LH and drives ovarian androgen output, and insulin resistance in muscle and fat further augments insulin-stimulated ovarian androgen production; frequent anovulation yields irregular menses and unopposed estrogen effect on the endometrium. Pelvic pain shares mechanisms with abdominal pain - parietal peritoneal inflammation, hollow-viscus obstruction, vascular compromise (e.g., ovarian torsion), and abdominal-wall pain - and may be referred from extrapelvic sources. Primary dysmenorrhea is mediated chiefly by prostaglandin pathways causing intense uterine contractions, reduced blood flow, and peripheral nerve hypersensitivity, with PG-independent mediators such as platelet-activating factor also contributing. Endometriosis reflects ectopic endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterus that respond to hormonal stimulation.","additionalDifferentials":["Pregnancy (must be excluded first in amenorrhea and in any reproductive-age woman with pelvic pain or bleeding)","Outflow-tract obstruction: transverse vaginal septum, imperforate hymen","Mullerian agenesis (Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser syndrome; WNT4 mutations)","Androgen insensitivity syndrome (about 10% of primary amenorrhea; 46,XY karyotype, female external genitalia, absent pubic/axillary hair, male-range testosterone)","5-alpha reductase type 2 deficiency","Asherman's syndrome (intrauterine adhesions, usually post-curettage; genital tuberculosis in endemic areas)","Hypothalamic/pituitary tumors and infiltrative disease, hyperprolactinemia, Sheehan's syndrome, lymphocytic hypophysitis, prior cranial irradiation","Isolated hypogonadotropic hypogonadism / Kallmann syndrome","Primary ovarian insufficiency (idiopathic, Turner syndrome, autoimmune polyglandular failure, fragile X premutation, post-chemo/radiotherapy)","Polycystic ovary syndrome","Rarer causes of hypergonadotropic hypogonadism: FSH/LH receptor mutations, aromatase deficiency, 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency","Pelvic pain mimics outside the reproductive tract: appendicitis, diverticulitis, inflammatory bowel disease, urinary tract disorders, musculoskeletal/neuromuscular and psychosomatic causes"],"infectionMimics":["Pelvic inflammatory disease (upper genital tract infection, often Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoeae)","Tubo-ovarian abscess","Endometritis (post-instrumentation, postpartum, or STI-associated)","Tuberculous salpingitis / genital tuberculosis (also a cause of Asherman's syndrome in endemic regions)","Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome (perihepatitis complicating PID, presenting with right upper quadrant pain)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Ectopic pregnancy","Threatened abortion","Ruptured or hemorrhagic ovarian cyst","Ovarian (or rarely tubal) torsion","Mittelschmerz (ovulation pain)","Endometriosis and endometrioma","Uterine fibroids (including acutely degenerating leiomyoma) and adenomyosis","Pelvic adhesions","Pelvic congestion syndrome","Vulvodynia","Pelvic malignancy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Beta-hCG to exclude pregnancy in every amenorrhea and acute pelvic pain evaluation","FSH (and LH) to localize ovulatory failure to ovary versus central cause; serial/repeat sampling in suspected POI","Prolactin in all patients with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","Estradiol, testosterone and androgen assessment for hyperandrogenism; AMH (low in POI, may be elevated in PCOS)","Karyotype in primary amenorrhea with suspected chromosomal cause and in all POI patients","POI work-up: anti-cortical and 21-hydroxylase antibodies (specific but not sensitive for future adrenal insufficiency), thyroid function and thyroid peroxidase antibodies, FMR1 premutation screening, and bone mineral density (ovarian biopsy is NOT indicated)","Pelvic ultrasound (transvaginal) as first-line imaging for amenorrhea work-up, pelvic pain, ovarian cysts, torsion (with Doppler for ovarian blood flow), endometrioma, and fibroids","MRI of the pelvis for better delineation of reproductive-tract anomalies before surgery and for higher sensitivity/specificity in endometriosis","Cranial MRI when history is atypical, with headache, other hypothalamic dysfunction, or hyperprolactinemia","Wet-mount of vaginal discharge plus nucleic acid amplification testing for C. trachomatis and N. gonorrhoeae in suspected PID","CA-125 may be elevated in endometriosis but has low negative predictive value; diagnostic laparoscopy is the gold standard for endometriosis when empiric treatment fails"],"redFlags":["Shock or peritoneal signs in a woman with pelvic pain - identify life-threatening conditions first as they may need emergent surgery","Reproductive-age woman with abdominal pain or vaginal bleeding - maintain high suspicion for ectopic pregnancy regardless of contraceptive use; tubal rupture is a life-threatening emergency","Severe pain with fever and peritoneal signs in PID suggests tubo-ovarian abscess or peritonitis","Right upper quadrant pain in PID suggests perihepatitis (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome)","Acute unilateral intermittent pain raising concern for ovarian torsion (confirm by absent Doppler flow)","Atypical amenorrhea history with headache, signs of hypothalamic dysfunction, short stature, diabetes insipidus, or galactorrhea - evaluate for a neuroanatomic lesion","Heavy or prolonged bleeding warrants prompt attention, but infrequent or absent bleeding is often under-reported and should be actively elicited"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on cause. Many causes of functional hypothalamic amenorrhea are reversible with restoration of energy balance, though bone density may not fully recover with hormone replacement alone. Amenorrhea of any cause is usually associated with chronically low estrogen, carrying risk to bone and cardiovascular health if untreated. PID untreated risks infertility and ectopic pregnancy, and subclinical PID remains a major worldwide cause of these complications. PCOS carries elevated long-term risk of metabolic syndrome, type 2 diabetes, dyslipidemia, hypertension, cardiovascular disease, obstructive sleep apnea, steatotic liver disease, and a two- to sixfold increase in endometrial hyperplasia/cancer from chronic anovulation; symptoms often improve with age, with most women regaining regular menses and normal androgens by the fourth decade. POI is associated with additional health problems and infertility, though oocyte donation has high success. Ectopic pregnancy with tubal rupture can be fatal.","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude pregnancy before pursuing an amenorrhea or pelvic-pain work-up.","Overlooking infrequent or absent menses because the patient does not volunteer it - a focused menstrual history is needed at every encounter.","Attributing pelvic pain to the reproductive tract when up to 60% of cases are gastrointestinal and urinary/musculoskeletal causes are common.","Waiting for positive microbiology before treating suspected PID - a presumptive clinical diagnosis is sufficient to begin treatment, and negative tests do not exclude it.","Confusing androgen insensitivity syndrome with mullerian agenesis - absent pubic/axillary hair and a male-range testosterone distinguish AIS.","Diagnosing PCOS without excluding mimics (hypothyroidism, hyperprolactinemia, adrenal hyperandrogenism); it is a diagnosis of exclusion, and in adolescents one should wait at least 3 years after menarche and not rely on ultrasound/AMH criteria.","Performing ovarian biopsy in POI - it is not indicated; routine genetic testing beyond FMR1 is also not currently recommended.","Assuming endometriosis severity at laparoscopy correlates with pain - it does not always; a normal CA-125 does not exclude endometriosis.","Delaying surgery for outflow-tract obstruction - early correction reduces the risk of endometriosis from retrograde menstruation."],"severityClassification":"Abnormal uterine bleeding is categorized etiologically by the PALM-COEIN system (structural causes: Polyp, Adenomyosis, Leiomyoma, Malignancy/hyperplasia; non-structural: Coagulopathy, Ovulatory dysfunction, Endometrial, Iatrogenic, Not yet classified). PCOS is diagnosed by the updated Rotterdam criteria (2023 international guidelines): two of three among irregular menses (fewer than 8 per year), clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism, and polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound (>=20 antral follicles or ovarian volume >=10 cm3 in one ovary) or elevated AMH, yielding hyperandrogenic and non-hyperandrogenic phenotypes. Pelvic pain is classified as acute versus chronic and cyclic versus noncyclic to guide evaluation. Endometriosis is staged by laparoscopy.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3132","Harrison 22e p.3133","Harrison 22e p.3134","Harrison 22e p.3135","Harrison 22e p.3136","Harrison 22e p.3137"],"crossLinks":[]},"mesenteric_vascular_insufficiency":{"name":"Mesenteric Vascular Insufficiency","system":"Gastrointestinal / Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Intestinal ischemia","Mesenteric ischemia","Chronic mesenteric ischemia (CMI)","Acute mesenteric ischemia (AMI)","Intestinal angina","Nonocclusive mesenteric ischemia (NOMI)","Mesenteric venous thrombosis","Ischemic colitis"],"chapter":340,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2586","clinicalPearls":["Intestinal ischemia arises when splanchnic perfusion cannot meet the metabolic demands of the gut; it is broadly divided into chronic mesenteric ischemia (CMI) and acute mesenteric ischemia (AMI), with the latter carrying a high mortality (~50-80%) and depending critically on early diagnosis and prompt intervention (p.2586).","AMI is mechanistically categorized into three patterns: arterio-occlusive (embolic or thrombotic), nonocclusive (vasospastic/hypoperfusion), and mesenteric venous thrombosis (p.2587).","Roughly half of AMI cases are embolic, with emboli typically lodging in the mid-to-distal superior mesenteric artery (SMA) just beyond the origin of the middle colic artery; about 25-30% are acute-on-chronic thrombosis on preexisting mesenteric atherosclerosis (p.2586-2587).","The hallmark of acute arterial mesenteric ischemia is severe, abrupt, nonremitting abdominal pain that is strikingly out of proportion to physical examination findings, present in roughly 95% of cases (p.2588).","Pain is disproportionate to exam because ischemia begins in the mucosal layer and advances outward toward the serosa, so peritoneal signs and cardiovascular collapse are late developments (p.2588).","CMI classically presents with insidious, recurrent dull crampy postprandial epigastric pain ('intestinal angina'), fear of eating, and consequent weight loss, often over 6-12 months, in a malnourished patient with other signs of atherosclerosis (p.2587).","CT angiography with 1-mm or thinner cuts is the gold-standard diagnostic test for both acute and chronic mesenteric arterial occlusive disease; a venous-phase study aids detection of mesenteric venous thrombosis (p.2587-2588).","Fasting mesenteric duplex ultrasound is a useful screening tool: a high SMA peak flow velocity carries about an 80% positive predictive value, and a negative duplex study virtually excludes mesenteric ischemia (p.2587).","In suspected AMI the decision to operate is frequently driven by a high index of suspicion from history and exam despite normal laboratory values, and surgical consultation should not be delayed (p.2588).","Mesenteric venous thrombosis (<10% of cases) tends to be associated with hypercoagulable states; documented inherited thrombophilia warrants long-term anticoagulation (p.2587, p.2588-2589).","Severity of ischemic colonic mucosal injury is graded by colonoscopy from mild (minimal erythema) to moderate (pale ulcerations extending into muscularis) to severe (black/green discoloration indicating full-thickness necrosis) (p.2589)."],"pathophysiology":"The intestine is perfused by the celiac artery, superior mesenteric artery (SMA), and inferior mesenteric artery (IMA), with extensive collateralization through the mesenteric arcades; small-bowel collaterals converge at the duodenum and pancreatic bed, while colonic collaterals meet at the splenic flexure (Griffiths' point) and the descending/sigmoid colon (Sudeck's point), the watershed zones most prone to colonic ischemia. The splanchnic bed can receive up to ~30% of cardiac output, and protective mechanisms include abundant collaterals, autoregulation of flow, and the capacity to increase oxygen extraction. Occlusive ischemia results from embolic or thrombotic interruption of a major mesenteric artery (emboli, originating from the heart in >75% of cases, preferentially lodge in the SMA distal to the middle colic origin); development of chronic intestinal angina generally requires significant stenosis of two of the three major vessels, with SMA involvement most concerning. CMI reflects failure to mount the normal postprandial hyperemic increase in intestinal blood flow, analogous to cardiac angina, due to atherosclerotic narrowing of the SMA and/or celiac origins. Nonocclusive ischemia represents disproportionate mesenteric arteriolar vasospasm in response to severe physiologic stress such as shock; if untreated, early mucosal stress ulceration progresses to full-thickness injury. Mesenteric venous thrombosis arises from inherited or acquired hypercoagulable states (p.2587).","additionalDifferentials":["Gastrointestinal malignancy (must be excluded in CMI before intervention)","Peptic ulcer disease and other causes of epigastric/postprandial pain","Biliary colic / biliary tract disease","Acute pancreatitis","Inflammatory bowel disease (ischemic colitis is commonly confused with IBD)","Acute intestinal obstruction (especially strangulated obstruction with vascular insufficiency)"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious colitis","Infectious gastroenteritis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inflammatory bowel disease","Diverticular disease / diverticulitis","Colonic neoplasm","Peptic ulcer disease","Biliary tract disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["CT angiography with thin (1-mm or finer) cuts, including venous phase for venous thrombosis (gold standard)","Fasting mesenteric duplex ultrasound (screening; SMA peak velocity)","Esophagogastroduodenoscopy and colonoscopy (to exclude malignancy in CMI and assess colonic mucosa in ischemic colitis)","Abdominal CT and abdominal ultrasound","Electrocardiogram and echocardiogram (to identify embolic source such as atrial fibrillation; should not delay surgery)","Laboratory markers: leukocytosis, metabolic acidosis, lactic acidosis (support advanced ischemia but do not reliably distinguish reversible ischemia from necrosis)","Hypercoagulability workup in mesenteric venous thrombosis"],"redFlags":["Severe abdominal pain out of proportion to physical findings","Blood per rectum / bloody stools","Peritonitis and cardiovascular collapse (late findings indicating advanced ischemia)","Leukocytosis with metabolic/lactic acidosis suggesting advanced ischemia or necrosis","Black or green colonic mucosal discoloration on colonoscopy indicating full-thickness necrosis","Atrial fibrillation, recent myocardial infarction, or infective endocarditis in a patient with acute abdominal pain (embolic source)"],"prognosis":"Acute mesenteric ischemia carries a mortality rate exceeding 50% and is among the most challenging diagnoses; the timeliness of diagnosis and treatment is the single most important determinant of survival. Chronic mesenteric ischemia managed with revascularization has favorable long-term outcomes, with endovascular stenting associated with about an 80% long-term success rate. Outcomes hinge on early recognition before progression to full-thickness bowel necrosis and peritonitis (p.2587-2588).","pitfalls":["Anchoring on a benign cause and underestimating severity because abdominal pain is markedly out of proportion to a relatively unremarkable exam (p.2588).","Waiting for abnormal laboratory values before acting; lactate, leukocytosis, and acidosis may be normal early or fail to distinguish reversible ischemia from necrosis (p.2588-2589).","Performing mesenteric duplex without fasting, since bowel gas obscures flow and the postprandial vasodilation response (p.2587).","Mistaking ischemic colitis for inflammatory bowel disease (p.2589).","Overlooking nonocclusive ischemia in obtunded, critically ill patients in whom physical findings are obscured by the underlying illness (p.2589).","Failing to recognize that the proximal jejunum may be spared in SMA embolism while the rest of the small bowel to the transverse colon becomes ischemic, leading to incomplete bowel assessment (p.2589)."],"severityClassification":"Ischemic colonic mucosal injury is graded by colonoscopy: mild = minimal mucosal erythema; moderate = pale mucosal ulcerations with extension into the muscular layer; severe = severe ulcerations with black or green mucosal discoloration consistent with full-thickness bowel-wall necrosis. Mechanically, mesenteric ischemia is classified as chronic (CMI) versus acute (AMI), and AMI is subclassified as arterio-occlusive (embolic or thrombotic), nonocclusive (vasospastic/hypoperfusion), or mesenteric venous thrombosis (p.2587, p.2589).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2586","Harrison 22e p.2587","Harrison 22e p.2588","Harrison 22e p.2589"],"crossLinks":[]},"mesenteric_venous_thrombosis":{"name":"Mesenteric venous thrombosis","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MVT","mesenteric vein thrombosis","superior mesenteric vein thrombosis","splanchnic venous thrombosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A less common cause of mesenteric ischaemia, most often involving the superior mesenteric vein and its tributaries","Presentation is frequently subacute with vague, poorly localised abdominal pain evolving over days, in contrast to the abrupt onset of arterial occlusion","The classic teaching is pain out of proportion to abdominal findings, at least early before infarction and peritonitis develop","A hypercoagulable state, whether inherited thrombophilia, malignancy, or acquired prothrombotic conditions, underlies many cases and should be sought","Local factors such as portal hypertension, intra-abdominal inflammation or infection, recent surgery, and pancreatitis also predispose","Contrast-enhanced CT is the investigation of choice, showing thrombus in the mesenteric or portal veins, bowel wall oedema, and signs of compromised perfusion","Prompt systemic anticoagulation is the mainstay when there is no infarction, and it improves outcomes and limits propagation","Bowel infarction, peritonitis, or clinical deterioration mandates surgical exploration with resection of non-viable bowel"],"pathophysiology":"Mesenteric venous thrombosis occurs when thrombus obstructs venous outflow from the bowel, most commonly in the superior mesenteric vein. Impaired venous drainage raises intestinal capillary and interstitial pressure, producing bowel wall oedema, arterial inflow compromise, and progressive congestion. If flow is not restored, transmural ischaemia and infarction ensue, with translocation of bacteria, peritonitis, and sepsis. It is usually driven by hypercoagulable states (primary thrombophilia, malignancy) or local factors such as portal hypertension, abdominal sepsis or inflammation, and recent surgery. The extent of collateral drainage and the speed of thrombus propagation determine whether ischaemia remains reversible.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute arterial mesenteric ischaemia (embolic or thrombotic)","Non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia","Bowel obstruction","Perforated viscus","Acute pancreatitis","Other causes of acute abdomen (e.g. appendicitis, diverticulitis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen with venous phase to identify venous thrombus and bowel compromise","Duplex ultrasound of the portal and mesenteric veins in selected cases","Laboratory markers of ischaemia including lactate and acid-base status","Thrombophilia screen and evaluation for underlying malignancy","Assessment for portal hypertension and intra-abdominal inflammatory sources"],"redFlags":["Peritonism, rising lactate, or haemodynamic instability indicating bowel infarction and the need for emergency surgery","Pain out of proportion to examination raising early suspicion before overt signs appear","Gastrointestinal bleeding or clinical deterioration during treatment","Extension into the portal vein or evidence of extensive bowel involvement"],"prognosis":"When diagnosed before infarction and treated promptly with anticoagulation, mesenteric venous thrombosis has a better prognosis than acute arterial mesenteric ischaemia. Delayed diagnosis with established bowel necrosis carries high morbidity and mortality, and recurrence risk depends on the underlying prothrombotic cause and duration of anticoagulation.","pitfalls":["Attributing subacute nonspecific abdominal pain to benign causes and delaying imaging","Failing to obtain venous-phase CT and missing the venous thrombus","Delaying anticoagulation in the absence of infarction","Not investigating for an underlying thrombophilia or malignancy after the acute event"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"metabolic_alkalosis":{"name":"Metabolic alkalosis","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["metabolic alkalaemia","chloride-responsive alkalosis","chloride-resistant alkalosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Metabolic alkalosis is a primary rise in serum bicarbonate with a compensatory increase in carbon dioxide from reduced ventilation","Understanding it requires separating the process that generates the alkalosis from the factors that allow the kidney to maintain it","Urine chloride is the single most useful test for classification, dividing cases into chloride-responsive (low urine chloride) and chloride-resistant (high urine chloride) forms","Chloride-responsive alkalosis, from vomiting, nasogastric suction, or diuretics, is accompanied by volume and chloride depletion and corrects with saline and potassium repletion","Chloride-resistant alkalosis reflects mineralocorticoid excess or severe potassium depletion and does not respond to saline, so the underlying disorder must be treated","Hypokalaemia both contributes to and is worsened by alkalosis, and the two often need correcting together","Maintenance of the alkalosis depends on factors such as volume contraction, chloride depletion, potassium depletion, and mineralocorticoid activity that impair renal bicarbonate excretion","Severe alkalaemia can cause neuromuscular irritability, arrhythmias, and reduced tissue oxygen delivery, and may need more aggressive correction"],"pathophysiology":"Metabolic alkalosis arises when there is a net gain of bicarbonate or a loss of hydrogen ions, but its persistence depends on the kidney's inability to excrete the excess bicarbonate. Normally the kidney can excrete a large bicarbonate load rapidly, so an alkalosis is only sustained when a maintenance factor is present. Generation occurs through loss of acid from the upper gastrointestinal tract or the kidney, or from a gain of alkali. Maintenance results from conditions that increase renal bicarbonate reabsorption, including reduced effective circulating volume, chloride depletion, potassium depletion, and excess mineralocorticoid activity, all of which enhance sodium and bicarbonate reabsorption and hydrogen secretion. In chloride-responsive states, volume and chloride depletion drive the maintenance and are corrected by saline; in chloride-resistant states, ongoing mineralocorticoid excess or profound potassium loss sustains the alkalosis despite adequate volume.","additionalDifferentials":["Vomiting or nasogastric suction","Diuretic therapy","Primary hyperaldosteronism and other mineralocorticoid excess states","Cushing syndrome","Bartter and Gitelman syndromes","Milk-alkali syndrome or excessive alkali administration","Severe potassium or magnesium depletion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial or venous blood gas with serum bicarbonate to confirm the primary alkalosis and assess compensation","Serum electrolytes including potassium, chloride, and magnesium","Urine chloride to classify chloride-responsive versus chloride-resistant disease","Assessment of volume status and blood pressure","Plasma renin and aldosterone when a mineralocorticoid cause is suspected","Review of medications and history for vomiting, diuretic, or laxative use"],"redFlags":["Severe alkalaemia causing tetany, seizures, or cardiac arrhythmia","Profound hypokalaemia with muscle weakness or arrhythmia risk","Marked volume depletion with haemodynamic compromise","Hypertension with hypokalaemic alkalosis suggesting mineralocorticoid excess"],"prognosis":"Most cases resolve readily once the generating cause is removed and the maintenance factors (volume, chloride, and potassium depletion) are corrected. Chloride-resistant forms require treatment of the underlying disorder and may persist otherwise. Severe alkalaemia is dangerous but usually reversible with appropriate management, and overall outcome is governed by the underlying condition.","pitfalls":["Giving saline to a chloride-resistant alkalosis that will not respond","Failing to measure urine chloride and thereby misclassifying the disorder","Neglecting concurrent potassium and magnesium deficits that perpetuate the alkalosis","Overlooking surreptitious vomiting or diuretic use as the underlying cause"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"methanol_poisoning":{"name":"Methanol poisoning","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["methyl alcohol poisoning","wood alcohol poisoning","methanol toxicity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Methanol itself is relatively inert; toxicity comes from hepatic metabolism to formaldehyde and then formic acid, which produces severe high anion-gap metabolic acidosis and target-organ injury","There is a characteristic latent period of hours (longer if ethanol is co-ingested) before symptoms appear, so early presentation can look deceptively mild","Visual symptoms are a hallmark - blurred vision, photophobia, a snowfield or blizzard-like whiteout, and potentially permanent blindness from optic nerve injury","Look for a wide anion-gap metabolic acidosis together with a raised osmolal gap; the osmolal gap falls as methanol is metabolised while the anion gap rises","Fomepizole is the preferred antidote as it inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase and blocks formation of toxic metabolites; ethanol is an alternative competitive substrate where fomepizole is unavailable","Folinic acid (or folic acid) is given to accelerate metabolism of formic acid to carbon dioxide and water","Haemodialysis removes methanol and formate and corrects acidosis, and is indicated in severe acidosis, visual impairment, renal failure, or high methanol levels","Consider methanol in any unexplained high anion-gap acidosis with visual complaints, particularly after consumption of illicit or adulterated spirits"],"pathophysiology":"Methanol is oxidised by alcohol dehydrogenase to formaldehyde, which is rapidly converted by aldehyde dehydrogenase to formic acid. Formic acid is the principal toxin: it inhibits mitochondrial cytochrome oxidase, causing cellular hypoxia and lactate accumulation, and produces a profound high anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Formate is directly toxic to the retina and optic nerve, causing oedema and demyelination that can lead to irreversible blindness, and can injure the basal ganglia (putaminal necrosis). Because the parent alcohol elevates serum osmolality, an early osmolal gap precedes the later anion-gap acidosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Ethylene glycol poisoning","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Lactic acidosis from other causes","Salicylate poisoning","Uraemia and other high anion-gap acidoses"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum methanol and formate levels where available","Arterial blood gas and calculated anion gap","Serum osmolality with calculated osmolal gap","Serum electrolytes, urea, creatinine, and glucose","Fundoscopy and formal visual assessment for optic involvement"],"redFlags":["Visual disturbance or reduced acuity signalling optic nerve toxicity and impending blindness","Severe high anion-gap metabolic acidosis","Reduced consciousness, seizures, or coma","Rising anion gap with a narrowing osmolal gap indicating ongoing conversion to toxic metabolites","Consumption of illicit or home-distilled alcohol in a cluster of patients"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the interval to treatment and the depth of acidosis at presentation; early antidote and dialysis can be curative, but delayed treatment risks permanent blindness, neurological deficits, and death.","pitfalls":["Being falsely reassured by the asymptomatic latent period, especially with co-ingested ethanol","Waiting for a confirmatory methanol level before starting antidote in a strongly suggestive case","Overlooking the diagnosis when the osmolal gap has already normalised late in the course","Failing to arrange haemodialysis for severe acidosis or visual symptoms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"miliaria":{"name":"Miliaria","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["heat rash","prickly heat","sweat rash","miliaria rubra","miliaria crystallina"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Miliaria arises from blockage of eccrine sweat ducts, so it favours hot, humid conditions, febrile illness, occlusion, and overdressing, and is common in neonates and in the newly bedridden","The subtype reflects the depth of duct obstruction: crystallina shows tiny clear, easily ruptured superficial vesicles; rubra produces itchy or prickly erythematous papulovesicles; and the deeper profunda presents as firm, skin-coloured papules","Typical sites are areas of occlusion and friction such as the trunk, flexures, and skin under clothing or dressings","In infants it commonly affects the neck, groin, and axillae and is easily provoked by warm environments and heavy wrapping","The cornerstone of management is cooling and reducing sweating: a cooler environment, loose breathable clothing, and avoiding occlusion, which allow the ducts to reopen","It is generally self-limiting once the provoking heat and occlusion are removed, and specific drug treatment is rarely needed","Widespread deep miliaria can impair sweating and predispose to heat intolerance and, in extreme heat, heat exhaustion","Secondary bacterial infection (miliaria pustulosa) can complicate the picture if lesions are scratched or persist"],"pathophysiology":"Miliaria results from obstruction of the eccrine sweat ducts, which causes sweat to leak into the surrounding skin rather than reach the surface. Keratin plugging of the duct, often aggravated by an overgrowth of skin flora producing occlusive material, blocks flow at varying levels. The level of the block determines the clinical form: obstruction within the stratum corneum causes the superficial vesicles of crystallina, blockage within the epidermis produces the inflammatory papulovesicles of rubra, and obstruction at the dermo-epidermal junction gives the deeper papules of profunda. The extravasated sweat provokes local inflammation and the sensation of prickling, while widespread duct blockage can compromise thermoregulation.","additionalDifferentials":["Folliculitis","Acute eczema or contact dermatitis","Candidal intertrigo in flexures","Viral exanthem or drug eruption","Neonatal erythema toxicum or transient neonatal pustular melanosis","Grover's disease (transient acantholytic dermatosis) in adults"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis is clinical, based on morphology and a history of heat, occlusion, or fever","Skin swab if secondary bacterial infection (pustular miliaria) is suspected","Investigations are seldom required, and biopsy is reserved for atypical or persistent cases","Assessment of the environment and clothing or dressing practices, especially in infants and bedridden patients"],"redFlags":["Extensive deep (profunda) involvement impairing sweating with symptoms of heat intolerance or heat exhaustion","Signs of secondary bacterial infection such as pustules, increasing pain, or spreading erythema","Systemic upset or fever suggesting an alternative or complicating diagnosis","Failure to settle after removing heat and occlusion, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Miliaria is benign and typically resolves quickly once overheating and occlusion are addressed; recurrence is common with repeated heat exposure, and only extensive deep disease or secondary infection causes significant problems.","pitfalls":["Treating with occlusive creams or heavy dressings that worsen duct blockage","Overwrapping or overdressing infants and perpetuating the rash","Mistaking pustular miliaria with secondary infection for a primary bacterial folliculitis or vice versa","Overlooking impaired thermoregulation in extensive deep disease during hot conditions"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"minimal_change_nephrotic_child":{"name":"Minimal Change Nephrotic Syndrome (Child)","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["minimal change disease","MCNS","idiopathic nephrotic syndrome of childhood","steroid-sensitive nephrotic syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Minimal change disease accounts for the large majority of idiopathic nephrotic syndrome in children, most commonly presenting between about 1 and 6 years of age.","The defining features are heavy proteinuria, hypoalbuminaemia, oedema, and hyperlipidaemia.","Oedema is typically dependent and periorbital and can progress to generalised swelling with ascites and pleural effusions.","Most children are steroid-responsive, and a favourable response to corticosteroids is both diagnostic and therapeutic in the typical age group.","Renal biopsy is generally reserved for atypical features such as age under 1 or over 10 years, persistent hypertension, gross haematuria, low complement, impaired renal function, or steroid resistance.","Children are at increased risk of infection, particularly spontaneous bacterial peritonitis from encapsulated organisms, because of urinary immunoglobulin losses.","A hypercoagulable state develops from urinary loss of anticoagulant proteins, increasing the risk of venous and arterial thrombosis.","Relapses are common, and some children become frequent relapsers or steroid-dependent, requiring steroid-sparing agents."],"pathophysiology":"A poorly understood immune dysregulation, involving circulating permeability factors and T-cell mediated signalling, injures the glomerular podocytes. On light microscopy the glomeruli appear normal, but electron microscopy reveals diffuse effacement of podocyte foot processes. Loss of the size- and charge-selective filtration barrier permits massive albumin leakage into the urine, producing hypoalbuminaemia, reduced oncotic pressure, oedema, and compensatory hepatic lipoprotein synthesis causing hyperlipidaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","Membranous nephropathy","Post-infectious or membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","Lupus nephritis","Congenital nephrotic syndrome (in infants)","Secondary nephrotic syndrome (e.g. Henoch-Schonlein, infection-related)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis and quantification of proteinuria (protein:creatinine ratio or 24-hour protein)","Serum albumin, lipid profile, and renal function","Serum complement (C3, C4)","Autoimmune serology when secondary causes are suspected","Assessment for infection and, in selected cases, screening for thrombosis","Renal biopsy in atypical presentations or steroid resistance"],"redFlags":["Signs of spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (fever, abdominal pain, tenderness)","Features of venous or arterial thrombosis, including limb swelling, respiratory compromise, or neurological signs","Hypovolaemic shock during aggressive diuresis","Severe respiratory distress from large pleural effusions or ascites","Steroid resistance or deteriorating renal function suggesting a more aggressive lesion"],"prognosis":"Children with steroid-sensitive minimal change disease generally have an excellent long-term renal prognosis, though many experience relapses over several years before remitting; steroid-resistant disease, often reflecting focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, carries a higher risk of progression to chronic kidney disease.","pitfalls":["Missing spontaneous bacterial peritonitis because ascitic signs can be subtle in an oedematous child","Overlooking thrombosis risk during periods of heavy proteinuria and diuresis","Assuming all childhood nephrotic syndrome is minimal change and delaying biopsy in atypical cases","Over-diuresing and precipitating hypovolaemia in a child with low intravascular volume"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"mitral_regurgitation":{"name":"Mitral Regurgitation","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MR"],"chapter":275,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2052","clinicalPearls":["Distinguish primary (degenerative) MR, where the leaflets and/or chordae are themselves abnormal, from secondary (functional) MR, where leaflets and chordae are usually normal and regurgitation is driven by LV/LA remodeling, annular dilation, papillary muscle displacement, dyssynchrony, or leaflet tethering; assessment, treatment, and prognosis differ significantly between the two (p.2052).","The characteristic murmur of chronic severe MR is a holosystolic, plateau-configuration systolic murmur of at least grade III/VI, most prominent at the apex and radiating to the axilla (p.2053-2054).","Murmur radiation reflects jet direction: with posterior leaflet prolapse/flail the eccentric anteriorly-directed jet strikes the LA wall near the aortic root and the murmur radiates to the base (can mimic aortic stenosis), whereas anterior leaflet prolapse/flail directs the jet and murmur toward the axilla (p.2054).","Ruptured chordae tendineae may give the systolic murmur a cooing or 'seagull' quality; a flail leaflet may produce a musical murmur (p.2054).","The murmur of chronic MR not due to MVP is intensified by isometric handgrip and reduced during the strain phase of Valsalva (decreased LV preload) (p.2054).","In acute severe MR the murmur is early in timing and decrescendo, ending well before S2, reflecting the rapidly rising LA pressure and diminishing LV-LA gradient — in contrast to the holosystolic plateau murmur of chronic MR (p.2053-2054).","Because EF rises in severe MR when LV function is normal, even a modest fall in EF (below 60%) reflects significant contractile dysfunction (p.2052-2053).","Atrial fibrillation is almost invariably present once the LA dilates significantly in chronic severe MR; LA enlargement and AF support the diagnosis on ECG (p.2053).","'MR begets MR': LA enlargement tensions the posterior leaflet pulling it away from the orifice, and LV dilation increases regurgitation, enlarging LA and LV further in a vicious cycle (p.2052).","With post-MI papillary muscle rupture the posteromedial papillary muscle is involved far more often than the anterolateral muscle because of its single blood supply (p.2052)."],"pathophysiology":"MR can arise from disease of any of the five components of the mitral apparatus: leaflets, annulus, chordae tendineae, papillary muscles, and subjacent myocardium (p.2052). MR reduces resistance to LV emptying (lowers afterload); the LV decompresses into the LA during ejection, so initial compensation is more complete LV emptying. Over time LV volume rises as regurgitation worsens and contractile function deteriorates, and forward cardiac output falls; LV compliance is often increased so LV diastolic pressure does not rise until late (p.2052-2053). The regurgitant volume varies directly with LV systolic pressure and the size of the regurgitant orifice (influenced by LV and annular dilation and leaflet morphology). LA compliance governs the clinical picture: in acute severe MR the regurgitant volume enters a normal-sized, noncompliant LA so LA and pulmonary venous pressures rise sharply with a prominent v wave and pulmonary edema is common; in chronic severe MR the LA enlarges and becomes more compliant, buffering pressure so pulmonary congestion is initially less prominent and fatigue from low forward output predominates (p.2053). Chronic secondary MR results from annular dilation, ventricular remodeling, papillary muscle displacement, and leaflet tethering (e.g., after MI, in dilated cardiomyopathy once LV end-diastolic dimension reaches ~6 cm, or atrial functional MR from chronic AF with annular dilation) (p.2052).","additionalDifferentials":["Aortic stenosis (basally-radiating MR murmur from posterior leaflet prolapse/flail can be confused with AS) (p.2054)","Mitral stenosis (a brief early-diastolic LA-LV gradient with rapid filling sound and mid-diastolic rumble in pure severe MR can masquerade as MS) (p.2053-2054)","Ventricular septal defect and other causes of a holosystolic murmur"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) with Doppler to define mechanism and severity (jet width/area, regurgitant volume, regurgitant fraction, effective regurgitant orifice area, LV volumes/EF, LA/LV size, chordal integrity, annular calcification) (p.2053)","Transesophageal echocardiography (TEE) for greater anatomic detail (p.2053-2054)","Estimation of pulmonary artery systolic pressure from the tricuspid regurgitation jet velocity (p.2053)","Exercise (stress) echocardiography to assess exercise capacity and dynamic change in MR severity, PA pressures, and biventricular function when clinical and resting findings disagree (p.2053)","Cardiac magnetic resonance (CMR) for quantitative regurgitant measures when TTE is discordant or insufficient (noted as not widely available) (p.2052-2053)","ECG (LA enlargement, possible RA enlargement with pulmonary hypertension, AF, eccentric LVH) (p.2053)","Chest X-ray (LA/LV enlargement, pulmonary venous congestion, Kerley B lines, mitral/annular calcification; asymmetric pulmonary edema possible in acute MR) (p.2053-2054)","Left and right heart catheterization with contrast ventriculography and coronary angiography when noninvasive findings are discrepant or to identify coronary disease before surgery (p.2053, p.2056)"],"redFlags":["Acute severe MR is a surgical emergency: papillary muscle rupture post-MI or leaflet destruction/perforation in infective endocarditis can cause sudden pulmonary edema and hemodynamic collapse, requiring urgent stabilization and emergent surgery (p.2052, p.2055-2056)","Acute pulmonary edema, hypotension with narrow pulse pressure, and prominent signs of pulmonary congestion with a non-displaced apex point to acute severe MR (p.2053)","Onset of palpitations or atrial fibrillation in chronic severe MR (p.2053)","Late-onset right heart failure with hepatic congestion, ankle edema, distended neck veins, ascites, and secondary tricuspid regurgitation, indicating pulmonary vascular disease and pulmonary hypertension (p.2053)","Falling LVEF (to 60% or below) or rising LV end-systolic dimension (40 mm or more), even when asymptomatic, signals contractile dysfunction warranting surgical referral (p.2053, p.2056)"],"prognosis":"Chronic mild-to-moderate isolated MR is well tolerated and usually asymptomatic. Chronic severe MR tends to be progressive ('MR begets MR') and, when secondary, severity correlates inversely with prognosis, with adverse outcome tied to the degree of LV remodeling and myocardial dysfunction (p.2052-2053). With surgery, repair carries substantially lower risk than replacement; a 2023 STS database analysis reported mean perioperative mortality of about 1.16% (under 0.5% in patients younger than 65) for repair of primary MR, durable repair is feasible in up to 95% of degenerative cases at high-volume centers, and reoperation runs about 1% per year over the first 10 years. In ischemic MR with severely impaired LV function (EF below 30%), surgical risk is higher, recovery of LV performance is incomplete, and long-term survival is reduced (p.2055-2056).","pitfalls":["In secondary MR the severity can be underappreciated by echocardiographic/Doppler techniques, and lesser degrees of regurgitation may carry relatively greater prognostic weight (p.2053).","An S3 in MR may reflect rapid LV filling, but in ischemic or dilated cardiomyopathy an S3 may instead signify ventricular dysfunction (p.2054).","Because EF is artifactually elevated in severe MR with normal myocardium, a 'low-normal' EF may already represent meaningful contractile dysfunction (p.2052-2053).","Routine surgical valve repair for significant secondary MR due to dilated cardiomyopathy has NOT been shown to improve long-term survival versus optimal guideline-directed medical therapy, especially in the era of quadruple HFrEF therapy (p.2056).","Direct oral anticoagulants should NOT be used if moderate or severe rheumatic mitral stenosis coexists, and are NOT approved for mechanical prosthetic valves (p.2055).","A presystolic murmur is not ordinarily heard with isolated MR (p.2054)."],"severityClassification":"Chronic severe MR (Doppler/CMR quantitative criteria) is defined by a regurgitant volume of at least 60 mL/beat, regurgitant fraction of at least 50%, and effective regurgitant orifice area of at least 0.40 cm2 (p.2053). ACC/AHA disease staging (Stages B, C1, C2, D) is applied: Stage C is asymptomatic severe MR (C1 normal LV systolic function with LVEF above 60% and ESD below 40 mm; C2 LV systolic dysfunction with LVEF at or below 60% or ESD at or above 40 mm) and Stage D is symptomatic severe MR (p.2054-2055).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2052"],"crossLinks":["IE","heart_failure","atrial_fib"]},"mitral_stenosis":{"name":"Mitral Stenosis","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MS"],"chapter":274,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2048","clinicalPearls":["Rheumatic fever is the leading cause of mitral stenosis; pure or predominant MS occurs in roughly 40% of patients with rheumatic heart disease and a history of rheumatic fever (p.2048).","Although declining in temperate, middle- to high-income climates, MS remains a major problem in low-income countries, especially sub-Saharan Africa, India, Southeast Asia, and Oceania (p.2048).","Auscultation classically reveals an accentuated first heart sound (S1), an opening snap (OS) of the mitral valve heard best in expiration at or just medial to the apex, followed by a low-pitched rumbling diastolic murmur heard best at the apex in the left lateral recumbent position (p.2049).","The A2-to-opening-snap interval varies inversely with severity of MS: a shorter interval indicates more severe stenosis (p.2049).","In patients in sinus rhythm, the diastolic murmur often reappears or becomes louder during atrial systole (presystolic accentuation) (p.2049).","Development of persistent atrial fibrillation often marks a turning point in the patient's course and is associated with acceleration of symptom progression (p.2048).","Echocardiography (TTE with Doppler) provides mitral orifice area, transvalvular gradients, leaflet calcification/restriction, and suitability for percutaneous mitral balloon commissurotomy; TEE is especially indicated to exclude LA thrombus before intervention (p.2049).","Mitral valve area 1-1.5 cm2 denotes severe MS; an LA pressure of about 25 mmHg is needed to maintain normal cardiac output when the orifice falls below 1.5 cm2 (p.2048-2049)."],"pathophysiology":"In normal adults the mitral valve orifice area is 4-6 cm2. Significant obstruction occurs when the orifice is reduced to less than about 2 cm2, so blood can only flow from the left atrium to the left ventricle if propelled by an abnormally elevated left atrioventricular pressure gradient, the hemodynamic hallmark of MS. When the orifice is below 1.5 cm2 (severe MS), an LA pressure of about 25 mmHg is required to maintain normal cardiac output. Elevated pulmonary venous and PA wedge pressures reduce pulmonary compliance and cause exertional dyspnea. Because tachycardia shortens diastole proportionately more than systole, an increased heart rate (including rapid AF) augments the transvalvular gradient and further elevates LA pressure. Chronic LA pressure elevation leads to LA enlargement, predisposing to AF and to thrombus formation (particularly in the LA appendage) with risk of systemic embolization. Pulmonary hypertension develops from passive backward transmission of elevated LA pressure, reactive pulmonary arteriolar constriction (the so-called second stenosis), interstitial edema of small pulmonary vessels, and, at end stage, obliterative pulmonary vascular changes; severe pulmonary hypertension produces RV enlargement, secondary tricuspid and pulmonic regurgitation, and right-sided heart failure (p.2048-2049).","additionalDifferentials":["Mitral regurgitation (may have an apical diastolic flow murmur, but it begins slightly later, S1 is soft or absent, and there is no opening snap; LV enlargement is common) (p.2049)","Severe aortic regurgitation with an Austin Flint murmur (apical mid-diastolic murmur not intensified in presystole and softened by amyl nitrite/arterial vasodilators) (p.2049)","Tricuspid stenosis (rare without MS; its diastolic murmur increases with inspiration and the jugular venous y descent is delayed) (p.2049-2050)","Atrial septal defect (RV enlargement and increased pulmonary vascularity, but no LA enlargement or Kerley B lines, with fixed splitting of S2) (p.2050)","Left atrial myxoma (obstructs LA emptying causing a positional diastolic murmur; often with systemic features such as weight loss, fever, anemia, emboli, elevated IgG/IL-6) (p.2050)"],"infectionMimics":["Infective endocarditis with large vegetations is itself a recognized (uncommon) cause of left ventricular inflow obstruction mimicking MS (p.2048)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Congenital mitral valve stenosis (parachute valve, cor triatriatum) (p.2048)","Severe mitral annular calcification with extension onto the leaflets (p.2048)","Systemic lupus erythematosus and rheumatoid arthritis involving the valve (p.2048)","Left atrial myxoma (p.2048)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography with color flow and spectral Doppler for mitral inflow velocities, peak/mean gradients, mitral orifice area, leaflet calcification and restriction, and suitability for percutaneous mitral balloon commissurotomy (p.2049)","Transesophageal echocardiography when TTE is inadequate, and specifically to exclude left atrial thrombus before commissurotomy (p.2049)","Exercise (stress) echocardiography to assess mean mitral diastolic gradient, PA pressures, and RV function when clinical and resting hemodynamic findings are discrepant (p.2049)","ECG showing left atrial enlargement (P-wave changes), and right axis deviation/RV hypertrophy when severe pulmonary hypertension supervenes (p.2049)","Chest X-ray showing straightening of the left cardiac border, prominent main PAs, upper-lobe pulmonary venous dilation, an enlarged LA, and Kerley B lines when mean LA pressure exceeds about 20 mmHg (p.2049)","Left and right heart catheterization when clinical and noninvasive findings are discrepant; CT coronary angiography to screen for coronary disease before valve surgery (p.2050)"],"redFlags":["Atrial fibrillation, which accelerates symptom progression and markedly increases thromboembolic risk (p.2048)","Systemic embolization (incidence 10-20%), more frequent with AF, age over 65, and reduced cardiac output; may be the presenting feature even in mild MS (p.2049)","Hemoptysis from rupture of pulmonary-bronchial venous connections due to pulmonary venous hypertension (p.2048)","Pulmonary hypertension and right-sided heart failure (hepatomegaly, ankle edema, ascites, pleural effusion) (p.2048-2049)","Recurrent pulmonary emboli, sometimes with infarction, an important late cause of morbidity and mortality (p.2048)","Decompensation precipitated by tachycardia, fever, severe anemia, paroxysmal AF, thyrotoxicosis, exertion, or pregnancy (p.2048)","In pregnancy, increased blood volume, cardiac output, and tachycardia can raise the transmitral gradient and cause pulmonary edema or tachyarrhythmias, with the immediate postpartum period a time of particular concern due to rapid volume shifts (p.3900)"],"prognosis":"In temperate climates the latent period between the initial attack of rheumatic carditis and symptomatic MS is generally about two decades, with disability typically beginning in the fourth decade. Historical studies before surgical valvotomy showed that once a patient became seriously symptomatic, the disease progressed inexorably to death within 2-5 years. Onset of persistent AF generally marks acceleration of symptom progression (p.2048).","pitfalls":["When cardiac output is markedly reduced, the typical auscultatory findings including the diastolic rumble may be undetectable (silent MS) and may reappear only as compensation is restored (p.2049)","Systemic embolization may be the presenting feature in otherwise asymptomatic patients with only mild MS (p.2049)","The Graham Steell murmur of pulmonic regurgitation can be indistinguishable from aortic regurgitation, though it may increase with inspiration and is accompanied by a loud, often palpable P2 (p.2049)","A history of rheumatic carditis is increasingly rare and often cannot be elicited (p.2048)"],"severityClassification":"Severity is graded by mitral valve area. The normal orifice is 4-6 cm2; significant obstruction begins below about 2 cm2. A valve area below 1.5 cm2 is termed severe MS (with an LA pressure of about 25 mmHg required to maintain normal cardiac output), and an area below 1 cm2 is very severe, at which cardiac output is subnormal at rest. Echocardiographic measurement of mitral orifice area and transvalvular gradient is the basis for grading; commissurotomy is generally indicated for symptomatic patients with valve area below about 1 cm2/m2 body surface area or below 1.5 cm2 in normal-sized adults (p.2048-2051).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2048"],"crossLinks":["atrial_fib","acute_rheumatic_fever","ischemic_stroke","aortic_stenosis"]},"mitral_valve_prolapse":{"name":"Mitral Valve Prolapse","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MVP","Systolic click-murmur syndrome","Barlow's syndrome","Floppy-valve syndrome","Billowing mitral leaflet syndrome"],"chapter":276,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2057-2059","clinicalPearls":["MVP is a clinically variable syndrome arising from diverse pathologic mechanisms affecting the mitral valve apparatus, commonly excessive or redundant leaflet tissue associated with myxomatous degeneration and increased glycosaminoglycan concentrations (p.2057).","It is the most common abnormality leading to primary mitral regurgitation and to mitral valve repair surgery, and in North America is now the most common cause of isolated severe MR requiring surgery (p.2057-2058).","MVP is more common in women than men and typically presents between ages 15 and 30 with a generally benign course; in older patients (>50 years, often men) MR tends to be more severe because of chordal rupture (p.2058).","The posterior leaflet is usually more affected than the anterior, the annulus is often dilated, and elongated, redundant, or ruptured chordae tendineae frequently cause or contribute to regurgitation (p.2058).","The hallmark auscultatory finding is a mid- or late nonejection systolic click occurring 0.14 s or more after S1, often followed by a high-pitched mid-late systolic crescendo-decrescendo murmur best heard at the apex, sometimes described as whooping or honking (p.2058).","Dynamic auscultation aids diagnosis: standing, the strain phase of Valsalva, and any maneuver decreasing LV volume make the click and murmur occur earlier, whereas squatting and isometric exercise (increasing LV volume) delay the click-murmur complex and may make it disappear (p.2058).","Murmur radiation depends on the leaflet involved: posterior leaflet prolapse directs the jet anteriorly with radiation toward the base, while anterior leaflet involvement directs the jet posteriorly with radiation to the axilla and back (p.2058).","A useful echocardiographic definition is systolic displacement of the belly of the mitral leaflets by at least 2 mm into the left atrium above the plane of the mitral annulus in the parasternal long-axis view (p.2058).","Chest pain in MVP is often substernal, prolonged, and unrelated to exertion, and only rarely resembles angina pectoris (p.2058).","Most patients are asymptomatic and remain so for life; the disease may progress slowly over years to decades or worsen abruptly from chordal rupture or endocarditis (p.2058).","MVP can occur as a rare sequel to acute rheumatic fever, in ischemic heart disease, in various cardiomyopathies, and in about 20% of patients with ostium secundum atrial septal defect (p.2058)."],"pathophysiology":"MVP results from myxomatous degeneration of the mitral valve apparatus with excessive or redundant leaflet tissue and markedly increased glycosaminoglycan content. In many cases the cause is unknown, but some forms are genetically determined; reduced type III collagen production has been implicated, and electron microscopy shows fragmentation of collagen fibrils. Genome-wide association work has identified multiple genetic loci (candidate genes include LMCD1, SPTBN1, LTBP2, TGFB2, NMB, and ALPK3), and associations with LMNA, FLNC, and FLNA that are often expressed as cardiomyopathies. The posterior leaflet is typically more affected, the annulus is often dilated, and elongated, redundant, or ruptured chordae contribute to regurgitation. Prolapse may impose excessive stress on the papillary muscles, producing localized ischemia, infarction, and replacement fibrosis that can serve as a nidus for ventricular arrhythmias (visible as late gadolinium enhancement on cardiac MRI even without coronary disease). Chordal rupture along with progressive annular dilation and calcification worsen regurgitation, placing further stress on the diseased apparatus in a self-perpetuating cycle (p.2057-2058).","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of primary mitral regurgitation","Rheumatic mitral valve disease (chordae thickened but not fused in MVP, unlike rheumatic disease)","Innocent or functional systolic murmurs","Angina pectoris (when atypical chest pain is present)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["ECG (often normal; may show biphasic or inverted T waves in leads II, III, aVF and supraventricular or ventricular premature beats)","Transthoracic echocardiography to identify abnormal leaflet position and prolapse and define the >=2 mm displacement criterion","Color flow and continuous-wave Doppler to assess associated MR and estimate severity","Three-dimensional echocardiography and cardiac MRI for more precise LV volumes and detection of papillary muscle fibrosis/late gadolinium enhancement","Transesophageal echocardiography when detailed anatomy is needed and for intraoperative or transcatheter procedural guidance","Exercise testing (often with rest and immediate poststress TTE) to assess functional capacity, LV/RV function, dynamic MR, and pulmonary pressures","Left ventriculography during catheterization (rarely necessary)"],"redFlags":["Severe MR with depressed LV systolic function (highest risk for sudden death)","Flail leaflet, which carries an excess risk of sudden death and usually accompanies severe MR","High-grade ventricular ectopy with inferior-apical T-wave abnormalities, high-density premature ventricular complexes at rest, mitral annular disjunction, and papillary muscle fibrosis on cardiac MRI","Syncope or light-headedness from arrhythmias","Transient ischemic attacks from emboli due to endothelial disruption on the valve","Features suggesting infective endocarditis in patients with MR and/or leaflet thickening"],"prognosis":"The clinical course is most often benign, and the majority of patients remain asymptomatic for their entire lives. In many the condition progresses gradually over years to decades, while in others it worsens rapidly from chordal rupture or endocarditis. Sudden death is a very rare complication, occurring most often with severe MR and depressed LV systolic function but possible even with normal LV size and function; a small high-risk subset has high-grade ventricular ectopy with characteristic imaging features, and patients with a flail leaflet carry an excess sudden-death risk (p.2058).","pitfalls":["Relying on a static exam can miss MVP; the click and murmur are dynamic and shift with maneuvers that alter LV volume (Valsalva/standing exaggerate, squatting/isometrics diminish them).","Presentations vary: some patients have a mid-systolic click without a murmur, others a murmur without a click, and still others both at different times.","The eccentric MR jet typical of MVP makes effective regurgitant orifice area and regurgitant volume difficult to assess with standard techniques.","Atypical, prolonged, non-exertional chest pain may be misattributed to coronary disease and is often difficult to evaluate.","Sudden death and significant arrhythmias can occur even in patients with normal LV size and function, so apparently low-risk patients are not entirely without risk."],"severityClassification":"Severity is anchored on the associated mitral regurgitation, ranging from a benign isolated systolic click with mild posterior-leaflet prolapse to severe MR from chordal rupture and leaflet flail. Echocardiographic prolapse is defined as >=2 mm systolic displacement of the leaflet belly into the left atrium above the annular plane (parasternal long-axis), with Doppler used to grade MR severity (p.2058).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2057-2059"],"crossLinks":[]},"mixed_connective_tissue":{"name":"Mixed connective tissue disease","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MCTD","Sharp syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An overlap syndrome combining features of systemic lupus erythematosus, systemic sclerosis, and polymyositis, defined serologically by high-titre anti-U1 RNP antibodies","Raynaud phenomenon and swollen 'puffy' hands or fingers are common early manifestations","Typical features include arthritis or arthralgia, inflammatory myopathy, oesophageal dysmotility, and sclerodermatous skin changes","Pulmonary arterial hypertension is a leading cause of death and must be actively sought even when patients are relatively well","Interstitial lung disease is frequent and warrants baseline and periodic monitoring","By definition antibodies to U1 RNP are present, usually in high titre, while antibodies more specific for lupus or scleroderma may be absent","Renal disease tends to be less severe than in lupus, but membranous nephropathy and other lesions can occur","Management is directed at the dominant organ involvement, borrowing treatment principles from lupus, scleroderma, and myositis"],"pathophysiology":"Mixed connective tissue disease is a systemic autoimmune disorder unified by the presence of high-titre autoantibodies against the U1 small nuclear ribonucleoprotein (U1 RNP). B-cell autoreactivity, T-cell involvement, and immune complex formation contribute to widespread inflammation and vasculopathy. Intimal proliferation and medial hypertrophy of small pulmonary arteries underlie the characteristic proliferative pulmonary vasculopathy and pulmonary arterial hypertension. Overlapping mechanisms account for the mixed features of lupus, systemic sclerosis, and inflammatory myopathy, and some patients evolve toward one of these defined diseases over time.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Systemic sclerosis","Polymyositis or dermatomyositis","Undifferentiated connective tissue disease","Rheumatoid arthritis with overlap features","Primary Raynaud phenomenon"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anti-U1 RNP antibodies and a full connective tissue antibody panel (ANA, anti-dsDNA, anti-Sm, scleroderma and myositis antibodies)","Echocardiography with estimation of pulmonary pressures, escalating to right heart catheterisation when pulmonary hypertension is suspected","Pulmonary function tests with diffusing capacity and high-resolution CT chest for interstitial lung disease","Creatine kinase and inflammatory markers to assess myositis and disease activity","Renal function, urinalysis, and nailfold capillaroscopy"],"redFlags":["Exertional dyspnoea, syncope, or right heart strain suggesting pulmonary arterial hypertension","Progressive breathlessness or hypoxia from interstitial lung disease","Severe myositis with respiratory or bulbar weakness","Scleroderma renal crisis with accelerated hypertension and renal impairment","Digital ischaemia or ulceration from severe Raynaud phenomenon"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally better than for lupus or diffuse systemic sclerosis, but pulmonary arterial hypertension and progressive interstitial lung disease are the principal determinants of mortality. Some patients maintain a mild course while others evolve into a defined connective tissue disease.","pitfalls":["Failing to screen for pulmonary arterial hypertension until symptoms are advanced","Assuming benign course and neglecting periodic cardiopulmonary surveillance","Overlooking evolution into lupus or systemic sclerosis over time","Attributing puffy hands and Raynaud phenomenon to a benign cause","Treating with a single-disease framework rather than targeting dominant organ involvement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"mody":{"name":"Maturity-onset diabetes of the young","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MODY","monogenic diabetes","familial young-onset diabetes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of monogenic, autosomal dominant forms of diabetes caused by single-gene defects impairing beta-cell function rather than autoimmunity or insulin resistance","Suspect it in young, non-obese patients with diabetes across several generations and a strong family history consistent with dominant inheritance","Patients are typically negative for islet autoantibodies and retain measurable endogenous insulin (detectable C-peptide) beyond the honeymoon period","It is frequently misclassified as type 1 or type 2 diabetes, so a high index of suspicion and genetic testing are key to correct diagnosis","The specific gene determines the phenotype and treatment: some forms are mild and stable, others progressive","One common subtype often causes mild, stable, non-progressive fasting hyperglycaemia that may need no pharmacological treatment","Another common subtype is characteristically sensitive to sulfonylureas, so accurate genetic diagnosis can allow a change away from insulin","Correct diagnosis has implications for prognosis, treatment choice, family screening, and, in some subtypes, pregnancy management"],"pathophysiology":"Maturity-onset diabetes of the young comprises several monogenic disorders in which a mutation in a single gene critical to pancreatic beta-cell development or function impairs insulin secretion. Inheritance is autosomal dominant, so an affected parent transmits the condition to roughly half of offspring, producing diabetes across successive generations at a young age. Unlike type 1 diabetes, there is no autoimmune beta-cell destruction, and unlike type 2 diabetes, obesity and insulin resistance are not central. Different causative genes give distinct clinical pictures, ranging from mild stable hyperglycaemia due to an altered glucose-sensing threshold, to progressive insulin secretory failure with risk of the usual diabetes complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Type 1 diabetes mellitus","Type 2 diabetes mellitus in the young","Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults","Neonatal diabetes and other monogenic beta-cell disorders","Secondary diabetes from pancreatic disease","Gestational diabetes when first detected in pregnancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Islet autoantibody panel to help exclude autoimmune diabetes","C-peptide to demonstrate preserved endogenous insulin secretion","Detailed multigenerational family history of diabetes","Genetic testing of the relevant MODY genes to confirm the subtype","Assessment for extra-pancreatic features associated with certain subtypes such as renal cysts","Cascade genetic screening of at-risk family members"],"redFlags":["Presentation with ketoacidosis, which is atypical and suggests type 1 diabetes instead","Rapidly progressive hyperglycaemia inconsistent with a mild stable subtype","Associated renal or genitourinary abnormalities suggesting a specific syndromic subtype","Diagnostic uncertainty leading to inappropriate lifelong insulin in a sulfonylurea-responsive form"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies by subtype: the mild glucose-sensing form is typically stable with low complication risk and may need no treatment, whereas transcription-factor subtypes are progressive and can develop classic microvascular and macrovascular complications if hyperglycaemia is uncontrolled; accurate genetic diagnosis enables tailored, often simpler, management.","pitfalls":["Automatically labelling young-onset diabetes as type 1 and committing to lifelong insulin without considering monogenic causes","Overlooking the family history that suggests dominant inheritance","Failing to check autoantibodies and C-peptide before classification","Not offering genetic testing that could change treatment and enable family screening"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"molluscum_contagiosum":{"name":"Molluscum Contagiosum","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["molluscum","MC","water warts"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characteristic lesions are small, firm, dome-shaped papules with a central dimple (umbilication), often pearly or skin-coloured","Spread occurs by direct skin contact, autoinoculation from scratching, and shared items such as towels; in adults, involvement of the genital area can indicate sexual transmission","It is common and typically self-limiting in immunocompetent children, so reassurance and expectant management are often appropriate","A surrounding patch of eczema (molluscum dermatitis) or an inflamed, tender lesion (the beginning-of-the-end sign) often heralds resolution rather than bacterial infection","Extensive, giant, or facial lesions in an adult should prompt consideration of underlying immunosuppression, including HIV","Physical treatments (such as cryotherapy or curettage) and topical agents can be offered when lesions are troublesome, but must be weighed against discomfort and scarring, especially in young children","Advise measures to limit spread: avoid sharing towels, discourage scratching and shaving over lesions, and cover lesions where practical","Reassure that most cases clear spontaneously over months, though this can take a year or more"],"pathophysiology":"Molluscum contagiosum is caused by a poxvirus (molluscum contagiosum virus) that replicates within epidermal keratinocytes. The virus induces localised proliferation of the epidermis and produces large intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies (Henderson-Paterson bodies) packed with viral particles, which form the soft core expressed from the umbilicated centre. The virus encodes proteins that dampen the local host immune response, allowing lesions to persist; eventual immune recognition produces the inflammatory reaction that precedes clearance. Transmission is by direct contact, fomites, and autoinoculation, and impaired cell-mediated immunity permits widespread or atypical disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Common or flat warts","Milia","Closed comedones or early acne papules","Basal cell carcinoma (for a solitary lesion in an adult)","Disseminated cutaneous cryptococcosis or other opportunistic infections in the immunocompromised","Ectopic sebaceous glands or adnexal tumours"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Usually a clinical diagnosis supported by dermoscopy showing central white-yellow structures with surrounding vessels","Consideration of HIV or other immunodeficiency testing when disease is extensive, facial, or atypical in an adult","Screening for other sexually transmitted infections when genital lesions suggest sexual transmission","Biopsy or expression of the core for microscopy in diagnostically uncertain cases"],"redFlags":["Numerous, giant, or facial lesions in an adult suggesting significant immunosuppression such as advanced HIV","A solitary firm nodule in an adult that could represent basal cell carcinoma or an opportunistic infection mimic","Marked periocular involvement risking conjunctivitis or keratitis"],"prognosis":"In immunocompetent individuals the condition is benign and self-limiting, usually resolving spontaneously over several months to a couple of years without scarring; recovery is slower and lesions more extensive when cell-mediated immunity is impaired.","pitfalls":["Mistaking the inflamed, resolving lesion for bacterial infection and treating with unnecessary antibiotics","Subjecting young children to painful, scarring procedures for a self-limiting condition","Missing underlying immunosuppression when an adult presents with widespread or atypical lesions","Overlooking sexual transmission and associated infections in adults with genital involvement"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"monkeypox":{"name":"Mpox (Monkeypox) & Poxvirus Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mpox","monkeypox","molluscum contagiosum"],"chapter":201,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1517","clinicalPearls":["Classic mpox/smallpox prodrome: fever first, followed days later by rash; other prodromal features include malaise, sore throat, and headache (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Lymphadenopathy is a key historical feature distinguishing mpox (present) from smallpox (absent) in endemic countries (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Classic systemic orthopoxvirus rash evolves through macular, papular, vesicular, and pustular phases (the pustular stage with central umbilication), each stage lasting 2-3 days, with lesions in the same anatomic location typically at the same stage of development (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Classic distribution is diffuse, well-circumscribed, and centrifugal: lesions more prominent on face and extremities (including palms and soles) than on the trunk; lesions are painful as they emerge and become pruritic in later stages (Harrison 22e p.1519).","In the 2022 clade IIb outbreak, transmission was primarily through close, intimate (e.g., sexual) contact and predominantly affected gay, bisexual, and other men who have sex with men (MSM) (Harrison 22e p.1518).","Clade IIb mpox often departed from the classic picture: rash was frequently the first or only sign (prodrome absent or concurrent), lesions were often localized to the anogenital region, smaller, fewer (often <10), and could be at different stages at the same anatomic site (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Up to 50% of those diagnosed during the 2022 outbreak in the U.S. context were also living with HIV (Harrison 22e p.1518).","Molluscum contagiosum (MCV) has a classic pearly appearance that may umbilicate as it matures, with little surrounding inflammation and painless lesions that persist for months but gradually regress over 6-12 months (Harrison 22e p.1519).","The infectious period for orthopoxvirus infections lasts from symptom onset until all lesions have crusted, the crusts have separated, and fresh healthy skin has formed underneath; lesion scabs contain viable virus (Harrison 22e p.1518).","Two distinct MPXV clades exist: Clade I (formerly Congo Basin, Central Africa) causes a higher proportion of severe disease than Clade II (formerly West African); Clade II subdivides into IIa (endemic West Africa) and IIb (spread worldwide in 2022) (Harrison 22e p.1517)."],"pathophysiology":"Poxviruses are large double-stranded DNA viruses that replicate entirely in the cytoplasm. Orthopoxvirus infection (variola, MPXV) is thought to involve systemic spread from the inoculation site to local lymph nodes, seeding of lymphoreticular tissue, and then symptomatic (febrile) viremia with viral skin tropism; disease severity reflects how well innate immune and interferon responses control early infection. Immunocompetent hosts develop lesions (often at the exposure site) about 1 week after exposure, progressing through staged phases over 7-14 days with scabbing and resolution by 14-21 days after rash onset. In severe immunocompromise (e.g., advanced HIV with CD4 <200 cells/mm3, organ transplantation), MPXV spread/growth goes unchecked, producing wide dissemination with large confluent lesions persisting for months and additional organ involvement. Most other poxvirus infections (e.g., parapoxviruses, MCV) involve only local viral growth at the inoculation site; yatapoxvirus (tanapox) pathogenesis is poorly understood (Harrison 22e p.1518).","additionalDifferentials":["Varicella (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Herpes simplex virus infection (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Syphilis (anogenital mpox lesions may resemble STIs) (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Gonorrhea (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Chlamydia (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Papillomavirus infection (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Yaws (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Cutaneous anthrax (particularly with parapoxvirus infections) (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Smallpox (variola) - distinguished from mpox classically by absence of lymphadenopathy (Harrison 22e p.1519)"],"infectionMimics":["Varicella (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Herpes simplex virus (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Syphilis and other STIs (gonorrhea, chlamydia) in anogenital presentations (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Yaws (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Papillomavirus infection (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Cutaneous anthrax (with parapoxvirus infections) (Harrison 22e p.1519)","Malaria (tanapox initially presents with very high fever and may be mistaken for malaria in endemic areas) (Harrison 22e p.1519)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nucleic acid (molecular) testing is the most common diagnostic tool: PCR assays for non-variola orthopoxvirus (NVO), orthopoxvirus generic, generic mpox, and mpox clade II; sequencing for full isolate characterization (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Multiplex assays combining NVO- and MPXV-specific targets, and clade-specific testing (U.S. Government and Public Health Laboratories), are available (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Viral culture: orthopoxviruses grow well in standard clinical tissue culture; parapoxviruses are difficult to isolate (primary cells best); MCV cannot be cultured (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Electron microscopy: characteristic large brick-shaped particles on negative stain for orthopoxvirus, yatapoxvirus, or MCV; parapoxviruses show an ovoid structure with crisscross spicules (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Histopathology of biopsy: MCV shows classic Henderson-Patterson bodies (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Serology can demonstrate orthopoxvirus reactivity but most assays cannot distinguish between orthopoxvirus species due to broad antigenic similarity (Harrison 22e p.1519)."],"redFlags":["Severe immunocompromise (advanced HIV with CD4 <200 cells/mm3, especially <50; organ transplantation) predisposes to severe, disseminated, prolonged mpox with numerous large coalescing or necrotic lesions and multi-organ involvement (GI tract, liver, lungs, brain) (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Ocular involvement: eye pain, redness, vision changes/loss, periorbital swelling, blepharitis, conjunctivitis, keratitis - can result in vision loss (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Neurologic complications: encephalitis, myelitis - severe headache, neck pain, altered mentation, focal deficits (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Myopericarditis: dyspnea or palpitations with elevated cardiac biomarkers and ECG changes (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Mucosal complications affecting alimentation, urination, or defecation due to painful or obstructive lesions; may lead to strictures, edema, and severe lymphadenopathy (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Most deaths have occurred predominantly among people with advanced HIV and CD4 <50 cells/mm3 (Harrison 22e p.1519)."],"prognosis":"In immunocompetent hosts most poxvirus infections are self-limited, resolving in weeks (or months for molluscum contagiosum). Exceptions are the generalized orthopoxvirus infections caused by MPXV and variola, which carry higher case-fatality. In unvaccinated individuals smallpox mortality is up to 30%; MPXV mortality is approximately 1.4-10% (clade I), ~1% (clade IIa), and <1% (clade IIb). Immunocompromised hosts may have more severe orthopoxvirus and parapoxvirus disease (severe mpox, progressive vaccinia, eczema vaccinatum, severe borealpox) with higher mortality, or atypical presentations (e.g., giant orf). In advanced HIV, effective antiretroviral therapy is essential to favorable outcomes. MCV can be diffuse in the immunocompromised, and immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) has been associated with recrudescence of MCV. Poxvirus reinfections are seldom reported (Harrison 22e p.1521).","pitfalls":["Do not assume a classic systemic presentation: during the 2022 clade IIb outbreak rash was often the first/only sign with prodrome absent, lesions localized to the anogenital area, smaller, fewer (<10), and could be at different stages at the same site - contrary to the classic rule that lesions in the same location are at the same stage (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Anogenital mpox lesions can be mistaken for common STIs (gonorrhea, chlamydia, syphilis); up to 10% of mpox patients may be coinfected with an STI, so a positive STI test does not exclude mpox (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Characteristic lesions plus exposure history narrow the differential, but laboratory testing is required to confirm the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1519).","Distinguish mpox from varicella (VZV), herpes simplex, and syphilis - overlapping vesiculopustular/anogenital presentations are a recognized diagnostic trap (Harrison 22e p.1519).","MCV cannot be cultured, so reliance on culture for diagnosis of molluscum is futile; biopsy (Henderson-Patterson bodies) or clinical/EM findings are needed (Harrison 22e p.1519)."],"severityClassification":"Mpox ranges from typical self-limited disease (immunocompetent, including HIV with CD4 >200 cells/mm3, generally without increased severity) to severe mpox. Severe manifestations include ocular infection, neurologic complications (encephalitis, myelitis), myopericarditis, obstructive mucosal disease, and uncontrolled viral replication with disseminated/necrotic lesions and multi-organ involvement, occurring chiefly in severely immunocompromised patients (advanced HIV, organ transplantation). Clade I causes a higher proportion of severe disease than clade II (Harrison 22e p.1519).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1517","Harrison 22e p.1518","Harrison 22e p.1519","Harrison 22e p.1521"],"crossLinks":["varicella_zoster","syphilis","hiv_aids"]},"mononeuritis_multiplex":{"name":"Mononeuritis multiplex","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mononeuropathy multiplex","multiple mononeuropathy","multifocal neuropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the sequential or simultaneous involvement of two or more named peripheral nerves in different limbs, producing an asymmetric, patchy deficit","The stepwise, painful onset of individual nerve territories is an important clue that separates it from length-dependent symmetrical neuropathy","Vasculitis is a leading cause, so systemic features such as weight loss, rash, arthralgia, and renal involvement should be sought","Diabetes mellitus is a common metabolic cause and may combine several mechanisms","As lesions accumulate they can become confluent and mimic a symmetrical polyneuropathy, obscuring the underlying pattern","Nerve conduction studies show a multifocal, often axonal process with asymmetry between nerves","Nerve biopsy, typically of the sural nerve, may be needed to confirm vasculitis and guide immunosuppression","Because vasculitic neuropathy reflects ischaemic nerve infarction, prompt recognition and treatment can prevent irreversible axonal loss"],"pathophysiology":"Mononeuritis multiplex arises from multifocal damage to individual peripheral nerves, most classically through occlusion or inflammation of the vasa nervorum (nerve ischaemia) in systemic or non-systemic vasculitis. Ischaemic infarction of nerve fascicles produces predominantly axonal loss in the territories supplied by affected vessels, explaining the patchy, asymmetric deficits. Other mechanisms include direct infiltration, compression at multiple sites, immune-mediated demyelination, and metabolic injury as in diabetes. The random distribution reflects the scattered nature of the underlying microvascular or infiltrative lesions.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic vasculitides (polyarteritis nodosa, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis)","Diabetic neuropathy and diabetic amyotrophy","Multiple compressive mononeuropathies (including hereditary neuropathy with pressure palsies)","Sarcoidosis and neoplastic infiltration","Infective causes such as leprosy, HIV, and Lyme disease","Cryoglobulinaemia and connective tissue disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nerve conduction studies and electromyography to map the multifocal, asymmetric pattern","Inflammatory and autoimmune screen (ESR, CRP, ANCA, ANA, complement, cryoglobulins)","Testing for diabetes, HIV, hepatitis B and C, and other infective causes","Nerve and muscle biopsy to confirm vasculitis when systemic markers are inconclusive","Assessment for systemic organ involvement including renal function and urinalysis","Imaging or further workup for sarcoidosis or malignancy when suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive multifocal deficits with systemic features suggesting active vasculitis requiring urgent immunosuppression","Evidence of multi-organ involvement such as renal impairment, pulmonary haemorrhage, or gut ischaemia","Severe constitutional symptoms indicating an aggressive systemic process","New motor deficits threatening limb function that demand expedited diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on the underlying cause and the speed of treatment; vasculitic cases can recover substantially if immunosuppression is started before extensive axonal infarction, but delayed treatment or severe systemic disease leaves persistent deficits.","pitfalls":["Waiting for a symmetrical pattern to emerge and missing the early asymmetric vasculitic phase","Attributing the picture to diabetes alone without excluding treatable vasculitis","Delaying nerve biopsy and immunosuppression when vasculitis is clinically likely","Overlooking systemic organ involvement that alters urgency and prognosis","Failing to consider infective causes such as leprosy in appropriate settings"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"movement_disorders":{"name":"Movement Disorders (Tremor, Chorea, Dystonia)","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["essential tremor","Huntington disease","dystonia","chorea"],"chapter":447,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3508","clinicalPearls":["Movement disorders are classified as hypokinetic (poverty of movement, e.g. parkinsonism with rest tremor, cogwheel rigidity, bradykinesia) versus hyperkinetic (excess movement, e.g. chorea, dystonia, tremor); the rest tremor and cogwheel rigidity of Parkinson's disease are extrapyramidal signs distinct from the action tremor of essential tremor (Harrison 22e p.3508; cf. p.197 ch.31).","Essential tremor is an action/postural tremor; a key pitfall is mislabeling it as Parkinson's disease, but Parkinson tremor is characteristically a rest tremor accompanied by cogwheel rigidity and bradykinesia, whereas essential tremor lacks these and worsens with sustained posture or movement (Harrison 22e p.3508).","Huntington's disease is an autosomal dominant degenerative disorder producing the triad of chorea, psychiatric/behavioral disturbance (mood, anxiety, psychosis), and progressive dementia; the unpredictable choreic movements give the gait a characteristic dancing quality (Harrison 22e p.3508; gait described p.~3535 ch.32; HD as autosomal-dominant dementia p.198 ch.31).","Dystonia is defined as sustained muscle contractions producing repetitive twisting movements and abnormal posture; it often has a genetic basis, but new asymmetric dystonia—particularly involving gait—in a younger adult may be an early sign of Parkinson's disease (Harrison 22e p.3508; cf. gait chapter p.~3535).","Sydenham's chorea (a manifestation of acute rheumatic fever after group A streptococcal infection) is the prototypical post-infectious chorea: it occurs mainly in females after a prolonged latent period, affects especially the head (darting tongue movements) and upper limbs, may be generalized or hemichoreic, and is frequently accompanied by emotional lability or obsessive-compulsive traits; >50% of patients presenting with chorea have carditis, so echocardiography is warranted (Harrison 22e p.3508; ch. on rheumatic fever p.~2992).","Tardive dyskinesia is a drug-induced hyperkinetic disorder seen with chronic exposure to antipsychotics and other agents that block the D2 dopamine receptor, and is a common cause of stereotypic movement and gait disturbances (Harrison 22e p.3508)."],"pathophysiology":"Movement disorders arise predominantly from dysfunction of the basal ganglia (striatum = caudate plus putamen) and their cortical and thalamic connections, which modulate motor output rather than directly generating it; disruption of these circuits yields either hypokinetic syndromes (parkinsonism) or hyperkinetic syndromes (chorea, dystonia, tremor). Huntington's disease is caused by an expanded CAG trinucleotide repeat (autosomal dominant) with degeneration of the striatum. Wilson's disease results from impaired copper excretion with copper accumulation in liver and brain. Tardive dyskinesia reflects chronic blockade of striatal D2 dopamine receptors (Harrison 22e p.3508; basal ganglia/striatum anatomy p.~199-200 ch.31).","additionalDifferentials":["Parkinson's disease (rest tremor, cogwheel rigidity, bradykinesia) — see parkinsons_disease","Progressive supranuclear palsy (axial rigidity, vertical gaze palsy, early falls)","Multiple-system atrophy and corticobasal syndrome (asymmetric akinesia, rigidity, dystonia, myoclonus, alien limb)","Orthostatic tremor (high-frequency low-amplitude leg tremor, shakiness on standing relieved by sitting/walking)","Drug-induced/tardive dyskinesia from D2-blocking agents","Sydenham's chorea / post-streptococcal autoimmune chorea"],"infectionMimics":["Sydenham's chorea following group A streptococcal infection (acute rheumatic fever)","Other post-infectious or autoimmune (paraneoplastic) chorea and movement disorders"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Wilson's disease (copper accumulation; young patient with chorea, dystonia, parkinsonism or tremor)","Drug-induced movements from dopamine-receptor blockers (tardive dyskinesia, acute dystonia)","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder","Hyperthyroidism (fine tremor; chorea rare)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for the CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion when Huntington's disease is suspected","Serum ceruloplasmin (low), 24-h urinary copper (elevated), slit-lamp exam for Kayser-Fleischer rings, and liver copper to evaluate for Wilson's disease in any young patient with unexplained chorea, dystonia, tremor or parkinsonism","Echocardiography when chorea suggests acute rheumatic fever (>50% have carditis); anti-streptolysin O and anti-DNase B titers for preceding streptococcal infection","MRI brain to assess for basal ganglia and other structural/degenerative changes"],"redFlags":["New chorea, dystonia, tremor or parkinsonism in an adolescent or young adult — exclude Wilson's disease (treatable; look for Kayser-Fleischer rings, low ceruloplasmin, high urinary copper) before manifestations become irreversible","Chorea with autosomal dominant family history plus psychiatric change and cognitive decline — consider Huntington's disease","Acute chorea (especially in a young female) — evaluate for acute rheumatic fever and carditis with echocardiography","Abnormal movements in a patient on long-term antipsychotics/D2 blockers — recognize tardive dyskinesia and acute dystonic reactions"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is disorder-specific. Essential tremor is typically slowly progressive and non-degenerative. Huntington's disease is relentlessly progressive and degenerative. Wilson's disease can improve substantially with prompt diagnosis and copper-chelating treatment before end-stage manifestations become irreversible. Sydenham's chorea is usually self-limited, with choreiform movements resolving within about 6 weeks, occasionally up to 6 months, though associated obsessive-compulsive or emotional symptoms may persist longer (Harrison 22e p.3508; Wilson p.~2713 ch.355; chorea p.~2992).","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing essential (action) tremor as Parkinson's disease, or vice versa — Parkinson tremor is a rest tremor with cogwheel rigidity and bradykinesia","Failing to test for Wilson's disease in a young patient with new movement disorder; Wilson's may present with chronic hepatitis long before neurologic signs or Kayser-Fleischer rings appear","Overlooking carditis in a patient presenting with chorea (Sydenham's), since chorea may be the sole or delayed manifestation of rheumatic fever","Not recognizing drug-induced/tardive movements from D2-blocking medications"],"severityClassification":"No single unified severity scale spans all movement disorders; assessment is disorder-specific and functional. Chorea, for example, ranges from movements evident only on careful examination in mild cases to inability to perform activities of daily living in severe cases (Harrison 22e p.3508).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3508"],"crossLinks":["parkinsons_disease"]},"mucormycosis":{"name":"Mucormycosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["zygomycosis","rhinocerebral mucormycosis"],"chapter":224,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1717","clinicalPearls":["Mucormycosis is a group of life-threatening infections caused by fungi of the order Mucorales (subphylum Mucoromycotina); it is highly invasive and relentlessly progressive, with morbidity/mortality higher than many other infections (Harrison 22e p.1717).","Rhizopus oryzae and R. delemar (family Mucoraceae) are by far the most common causes in the Western Hemisphere; Apophysomyces elegans is a notable cause in India and Mucor irregularis in China (Harrison 22e p.1718).","Five well-defined clinical syndromes: rhino-orbital-cerebral, pulmonary, cutaneous, gastrointestinal, and disseminated; specific host defects predispose to specific syndromes (diabetes/DKA to rhino-orbital-cerebral; leukemia/HSCT to pulmonary) (Harrison 22e p.1718).","Rhino-orbital-cerebral disease is the most common form worldwide; early symptoms are nonspecific (eye/facial pain, facial numbness) progressing to conjunctival suffusion, blurry vision, proptosis, and chemosis (Harrison 22e p.1718-1719).","Biopsy with histopathology is the most sensitive and specific modality: wide (>=6-30 microm), thick-walled, ribbon-like, ASEPTATE hyphae branching at RIGHT angles, in contrast to thinner, septate, acute-angle-branching Aspergillus/Fusarium/Scedosporium (Harrison 22e p.1719).","Mucorales are best visualized with periodic acid-Schiff or H&E; methenamine silver may NOT stain them optimally (Harrison 22e p.1719).","Tissue for culture should be cut into sections and placed in the center of culture media, NOT homogenized, because homogenization kills the long filamentous hyphae and lowers culture yield; the lab should be alerted when mucormycosis is suspected (Harrison 22e p.1720).","It is important to distinguish mucormycosis from aspergillosis because treatments differ: polyenes for Mucorales versus expanded-spectrum triazoles for most septate molds; voriconazole is the first-line treatment for aspergillosis but EXACERBATES mucormycosis in animal models (Harrison 22e p.1719-1720)."],"pathophysiology":"Mucorales are ubiquitous environmental fungi to which humans are constantly exposed; they cause invasive, angioinvasive infection primarily in hosts with uncontrolled diabetes, phagocytic defects (neutropenia or high-dose corticosteroids), and/or elevated free iron, which supports fungal growth in serum and tissue. Iron-overloaded end-stage renal failure patients treated with the iron chelator deferoxamine had high risk of rapidly fatal disseminated disease, because deferoxamine acts as a fungal xenosiderophore that delivers iron directly to the Mucorales. In diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), acidosis dissociates iron from sequestering proteins (enhancing fungal survival/virulence) and the ketoacid beta-hydroxybutyrate upregulates host and fungal receptors that increase fungal adherence and tissue penetration; however the majority of diabetic patients with mucormycosis are NOT acidotic on presentation. Even without acidosis, hyperglycemia raises risk via hyperglycation of iron-sequestering proteins, upregulation of the host receptor GRP78 (which binds Mucorales and enables tissue penetration), poorly characterized phagocytic defects, and enhanced expression of the Mucorales-specific invasin CotH (which binds GRP78). Blood vessel thrombosis and tissue necrosis impair antifungal penetration to the site of infection. (Harrison 22e p.1717-1718)","additionalDifferentials":["Aspergillosis","Scedosporiosis (Scedosporium species)","Fusariosis (Fusarium species)","Dematiaceous (brown-pigmented) mold infections","Entomophthoromycosis (Basidiobolus and Conidiobolus)","Bacterial orbital cellulitis / cavernous sinus thrombosis (Staphylococcus aureus, streptococci, gram-negative species)","Rhinoscleroma (Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis)","Tolosa-Hunt syndrome"],"infectionMimics":["Aspergillosis (septate mold, may co-infect with Mucorales)","Scedosporiosis","Fusariosis","Dematiaceous mold infection","Entomophthoromycosis (Basidiobolus, Conidiobolus)","Bacterial orbital cellulitis / cavernous sinus thrombosis (S. aureus, streptococci, gram-negatives)","Rhinoscleroma (Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Tolosa-Hunt syndrome (painful ophthalmoplegia, ptosis, headache, cavernous sinus inflammation; lacks progression)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Biopsy with histopathologic examination (most sensitive/specific; PAS or H&E staining)","Culture from a sterile site (needle aspirate, tissue biopsy, pleural fluid) for definitive species identification; tissue sectioned NOT homogenized, incubated at both room temperature and 37C","CT of the chest (best method to define extent of pulmonary disease; may detect infection before chest x-ray)","MRI of head/sinuses (more sensitive ~80% than CT for orbital and CNS extension)","Endoscopy and/or surgical exploration with biopsy of suspected areas; blind biopsy of normal-appearing sinus tissue when suspicion is high","Mucorales-specific PCR (useful but no FDA-approved assay)"],"redFlags":["Rhino-orbital-cerebral involvement with a black necrotic eschar and/or painful necrotic ulceration of the hard palate (late finding indicating extensive, well-established angioinvasive infection) is a surgical emergency requiring urgent debridement.","Bilateral proptosis, chemosis, vision loss, and ophthalmoplegia (contralateral eye involvement) suggest cavernous sinus thrombosis and are ominous.","Hyperglycemia or DKA with sinusitis and possible orbital extension, even without a known diabetes history, should raise high suspicion (presentation may be the first recognition of diabetes, often unmasked by recent glucocorticoids).","Necrotizing fasciitis from cutaneous mucormycosis (mortality approaching 80%) and necrotic skin lesions from hematogenous dissemination.","Disseminated disease (most lethal form), seen with antifungal prophylaxis breakthrough and in highly immunocompromised patients."],"prognosis":"Overall mortality remains high though it has declined with earlier initiation of effective antifungal therapy; outcome is often driven by progression of the underlying predisposing condition. Necrotizing fasciitis from cutaneous disease carries mortality approaching 80%, gastrointestinal disease progressing to perforation has extremely high mortality, and widely disseminated disease exceeds 90% mortality. In contrast, isolated nonnecrotizing cutaneous mucormycosis treated with prompt aggressive surgical debridement has a favorable prognosis and low mortality. Rate of progression is highly variable (days to a year or more), not invariably rapid. (Harrison 22e p.1717-1720)","pitfalls":["Misperception that mucormycosis is always rapidly progressive; rate of progression is in fact highly variable (days to a year or more).","Cultures are positive in fewer than half of cases, and up to half of cases are diagnosed only at autopsy; a negative culture does NOT exclude the diagnosis.","Standard tissue homogenization for culture kills the filamentous Mucorales and lowers yield; tissue must be sectioned and not homogenized, and the lab alerted.","Imaging underestimates disease: sinus CT may look like ordinary bacterial sinusitis, and bony erosion into the orbit is rarely seen on CT even when clinically present.","Treating with voriconazole (first-line for aspergillosis) can exacerbate mucormycosis; distinguish the two molds because preferred treatments differ, and consider dual infection in highly compromised patients.","Delaying treatment of the underlying malignancy until mucormycosis is 'cured' is a common error; antifungals provide only a holding action until near-normal immunity is restored, and untreated underlying disease is the typical primary driver of death.","Iron administration and liberal blood transfusion (free iron from hemolysis) should be avoided as iron exacerbates infection.","Therapy should not be delayed for diagnostic confirmation: when suspicion is reasonable, empirical lipid polyene therapy should be started early because earlier polyene therapy improves survival."],"severityClassification":"Harrison classifies mucormycosis by clinical syndrome rather than a numeric severity score: rhino-orbital-cerebral, pulmonary, cutaneous (ranging from isolated nonnecrotizing cutaneous disease with favorable prognosis to necrotizing fasciitis), gastrointestinal, and disseminated/miscellaneous disease, with mortality rising steeply from localized cutaneous disease to disseminated disease (>90%). (Harrison 22e p.1718-1720)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1717"],"crossLinks":[]},"multiple_and_mixed_valvular_heart":{"name":"Multiple and Mixed Valvular Heart Disease","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Multivalvular heart disease","Mixed valve disease","Combined valvular heart disease"],"chapter":279,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2065","clinicalPearls":["Multiple acquired and congenital lesions can produce stenosis and/or regurgitation across one or more valves; rheumatic disease classically affects mitral, aortic, and tricuspid valves alone or in combination.","In multivalvular disease, derangements from the more proximal valve lesion can mask the full hemodynamic expression of a more distal lesion.","Reduced cardiac output from mitral valve disease lowers transaortic flow and can lead to underestimation of the severity of a coexisting aortic lesion.","Functional tricuspid regurgitation accompanying significant mitral valve disease is one of the most common forms of multivalve disease; the tricuspid leaflets are morphologically normal and TR arises from right ventricular and annular dilation.","Onset of atrial fibrillation can be especially deleterious in rheumatic mitral plus aortic disease: loss of atrial systole can critically reduce cardiac output and raise left atrial and LV diastolic pressures.","When AR or MR is the dominant lesion in mixed aortic or mitral disease, the LV is dilated; when AS or MS predominates, LV chamber size is normal or small.","Secondary (functional) MR complicating severe AS reflects altered LV geometry and systolic leaflet tethering with normal leaflets and chordae; it may improve after relief of afterload with valve replacement.","Persistent significant secondary MR after aortic valve replacement is associated with impaired functional outcomes and reduced survival.","Severe mitral annular calcification can cause both regurgitation (reduced systolic annular shortening) and mild-to-moderate stenosis (calcium extending onto the leaflets restricting opening).","An early ejection click, typical of bicuspid aortic valve in young adults, is often absent in congenital mixed AS and AR.","Patients with multiple or mixed valve disease may become symptomatic earlier than those with isolated single-valve lesions, sometimes even with only moderate individual lesions.","Palpitations suggesting AF can flag clinically unsuspected mitral valve disease as an important component of the presentation."],"pathophysiology":"In multivalvular heart disease the hemodynamic effects of a proximal valve lesion can mask those of a more distal lesion. With combined rheumatic mitral and aortic disease, the reduced cardiac output imposed by mitral disease lessens the apparent severity of the aortic lesion, while AF arising during mitral stenosis can abruptly worsen previously silent aortic disease. Reactive pulmonary vascular disease acts as a secondary obstructive lesion in series, and as cardiac output falls with progressive tricuspid disease, associated mitral or aortic disease may be underestimated. Functional TR follows right ventricular and annular dilation with morphologically normal leaflets, producing large systolic c-v waves in the RA pressure pulse whose height depends on RA compliance and regurgitant volume; advanced chronic severe TR ventricularizes the RA waveform. In MS with concomitant AR, enhanced LV filling raises diastolic pressure depending on chamber compliance. Secondary MR in severe AS arises from LV remodeling and leaflet tethering under high systolic pressures. In mixed AS/AR, augmented transvalvular systolic velocities inflate the Doppler gradient and murmur intensity relative to true orifice size, and the Gorlin formula is inaccurate. In mixed mitral disease, the elevated mitral E-wave velocity in severe MR may not reflect any contribution of associated MS to LA hypertension. Noncompliant, nondilated, concentrically hypertrophied ventricles (as in significant AS, radiation heart disease, cardiac amyloid, or obesity/diabetes cardiomyopathy) tolerate acute volume loads poorly, so sudden AR causes rapid rise in LV diastolic pressure and severe heart failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Patent ductus arteriosus (continuous murmur mimicking combined AS and AR systolic-diastolic murmurs)","Ruptured sinus of Valsalva aneurysm (continuous murmur, often after acute chest pain)","Isolated single-valve lesion with falsely overestimated or underestimated severity due to a masked second lesion","Pulmonary hypertension or right-sided valve disease underlying ECG signs of right heart abnormality in a left-sided valve lesion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Patent ductus arteriosus","Ruptured sinus of Valsalva aneurysm"],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography as the principal modality, assessing valve morphology and function, calcification, chamber size, wall thickness, biventricular function, estimated PA systolic pressure, and great-vessel dimensions","Transesophageal echocardiography for more accurate valve (especially mitral) assessment and when infective endocarditis is suspected","Exercise testing with or without echocardiography when symptoms exceed resting TTE findings","Cardiac MR for additional anatomic and physiologic data when echocardiography is suboptimal (less suited to valve morphology)","Cardiac CT for intracardiac structures in complicated IE and for planning transcatheter valve implantation; coronary CT angiography as a noninvasive coronary assessment","Right and left heart catheterization to characterize individual lesion contributions, measure PA pressures, and calculate pulmonary vascular resistance, strongly recommended when clinical and noninvasive findings are discrepant in a symptomatic patient","ECG for ventricular hypertrophy/atrial enlargement and right-sided signs; chest x-ray for chamber enlargement, valve/annular calcification, pulmonary vascular changes (Kerley B lines, enlarged azygos vein indicating RA hypertension)"],"redFlags":["Abrupt development of acute aortic regurgitation (e.g., from IE or paravalvular leak after AVR/TAVI) in a noncompliant, nondilated, hypertrophied LV, causing rapid rise in diastolic pressure and severe heart failure","New atrial fibrillation in moderate mitral disease precipitating heart failure when severe aortic disease coexists","Significant paravalvular regurgitation after transcatheter AVR (risk factor for short- to intermediate-term death)","Symptoms of right heart failure (abdominal fullness/bloating, edema, ascites) signaling advanced disease","ECG or chest x-ray signs of pulmonary hypertension or RA hypertension in a patient with left-sided valve disease"],"prognosis":"Natural history is usually driven by the dominant valve lesion; for moderate mixed AS/AR in which stenosis dominates, the course tends to parallel isolated severe AS. Patients with multiple or mixed disease may develop limiting symptoms or physiologic impairment even with only moderate lesions. Persistent significant secondary MR after AVR carries worse functional outcomes and survival, and significant paravalvular regurgitation after transcatheter AVR is a notable risk factor for short- to intermediate-term death.","pitfalls":["The Gorlin formula is inaccurate in mixed aortic valve disease because augmented transvalvular flow from AR inflates the gradient relative to true orifice size.","Doppler-derived LV-aortic gradients and systolic murmur intensity overestimate true valve orifice severity in mixed AS/AR with preserved LV function.","A high mitral E-wave velocity in severe MR may be misread as indicating significant MS contribution to LA hypertension.","Low cardiac output from a proximal lesion (or AF) can mask the true severity of a more distal aortic or tricuspid lesion, leading to underestimation.","A mid-systolic outflow murmur is common in significant AR even without valve sclerosis/stenosis, so other signs of AS must be sought rather than assuming combined disease.","Combined aortic and mitral valve replacement carries significantly higher perioperative mortality than single-valve surgery, so the decision must be made carefully."],"severityClassification":"No discrete severity grading scheme for combined disease; severity is determined by an integrated clinical and noninvasive assessment that identifies the dominant lesion, whose grading and management recommendations are then applied, with attention to lesion severity, ventricular remodeling, functional capacity, and PA pressures.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2065","Harrison 22e p.2066","Harrison 22e p.2067"],"crossLinks":[]},"multiple_endocrine_neoplasia":{"name":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN)","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MEN1","MEN2","MEN syndromes"],"chapter":400,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3079","clinicalPearls":["MEN1 is characterized primarily by the triad of parathyroid, pancreatic islet, and pituitary tumors (the '3 Ps'); it is an autosomal dominant syndrome (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.392/ch.388).","MEN2 predisposes to medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC), pheochromocytoma, and hyperparathyroidism (Harrison 22e p.3079).","There are three familial forms of MTC: MEN 2A, MEN 2B, and familial MTC without other features of MEN; MTC is more aggressive in MEN 2B than in MEN 2A, and familial MTC is more aggressive than sporadic MTC (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397).","MEN1 is caused by inactivating germline mutations of the MEN1 (menin) tumor-suppressor gene on chromosome 11q13; about half of affected patients develop prolactinomas, while acromegaly and Cushing's disease are less common (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.392).","MEN2 is caused by activating point mutations of the RET protooncogene (a receptor tyrosine kinase) in a single allele, in contrast to the tumor-suppressor loss-of-function mechanism of MEN1 (Harrison 22e p.3079).","Early genetic screening for RET mutations identifies individuals at risk for MEN2 who may benefit from prophylactic thyroidectomy and biochemical screening for pheochromocytoma and hyperparathyroidism (Harrison 22e p.3079).","All patients with MTC should be tested for RET mutations, because genetic counseling and cascade testing of family members can then be offered to those who test positive (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397)."],"pathophysiology":"MEN1 follows the two-hit tumor-suppressor paradigm: the affected individual inherits a mutant copy of the MEN1 gene (menin, chromosome 11q13), and tumorigenesis follows a somatic 'second hit' (deletion or point mutation) that inactivates the remaining normal allele. In contrast, MEN2 is caused by activating point mutations in a single allele of the RET protooncogene, encoding a receptor tyrosine kinase; constitutive RET activity leads to thyroid C-cell hyperplasia in childhood that precedes the development of medullary thyroid carcinoma (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.388/ch.397).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Other familial pituitary tumor / endocrine neoplasia syndromes: MEN 4 (CDKN1B mutation; hyperparathyroidism and pituitary adenomas), Carney complex (PRKAR1A; spotty skin pigmentation, myxomas, endocrine tumors), McCune-Albright syndrome, and familial isolated pituitary adenomas (AIP) (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.392).","Other inherited predispositions to pheochromocytoma that overlap with MEN2: von Hippel-Lindau disease, neurofibromatosis, and hereditary paraganglioma syndromes (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.247)."],"additionalInvestigations":["Germline RET mutation testing in all patients with medullary thyroid carcinoma, with cascade screening of relatives (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397).","MEN1 (menin) gene testing in patients with the parathyroid/pancreatic-islet/pituitary tumor pattern or relevant family history (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.392/ch.376).","Biochemical screening for pheochromocytoma and hyperparathyroidism in RET-mutation carriers identified as at risk for MEN2 (Harrison 22e p.3079)."],"redFlags":["Exclude pheochromocytoma BEFORE any surgery: prior to surgery, pheochromocytoma should be excluded in all patients with a RET mutation (failure to do so risks an intraoperative hypertensive crisis) (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397).","MTC in the context of MEN 2B carries a more aggressive course than MEN 2A and warrants early/prophylactic intervention (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397)."],"prognosis":"Prognosis is driven by the component tumors and genotype. MTC is more aggressive in MEN 2B than in MEN 2A, and familial MTC is more aggressive than sporadic MTC; early genetic identification permits prophylactic thyroidectomy before malignant transformation, improving outcome (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397).","pitfalls":["Proceeding to surgery (e.g., thyroidectomy or parathyroid surgery) without first excluding an unsuspected pheochromocytoma in a RET-mutation carrier (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397).","Failing to perform RET testing in all MTC patients and thereby missing cascade screening opportunities for at-risk family members (Harrison 22e p.3079, ch.397)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.3079"],"crossLinks":["pheo","thyroid_cancer"]},"multiple_system_atrophy":{"name":"Multiple system atrophy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MSA","MSA-P","MSA-C","Shy-Drager syndrome","olivopontocerebellar atrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A sporadic alpha-synucleinopathy combining autonomic failure with either parkinsonism (MSA-P) or cerebellar ataxia (MSA-C)","Prominent, early autonomic dysfunction such as orthostatic hypotension, urinary retention or incontinence, and erectile dysfunction is a defining feature","The parkinsonism is usually symmetric, rapidly progressive, and responds poorly or only briefly to dopaminergic therapy","MSA-C presents with gait and limb ataxia, dysarthria, and eye movement abnormalities","Pyramidal signs, marked dysphonia or dysarthria, and inspiratory stridor may develop and stridor carries prognostic and airway significance","Early falls, antecollis (marked neck flexion), and cold, dusky hands can be supportive clinical clues","MRI may show putaminal, pontine (hot cross bun sign), or cerebellar atrophy","Distinguishing MSA from Parkinson disease rests on early severe autonomic failure, poor levodopa response, and cerebellar or pyramidal signs"],"pathophysiology":"MSA is characterised by abnormal aggregation of alpha-synuclein in oligodendrocytes as glial cytoplasmic inclusions, with associated neuronal loss and gliosis. Degeneration involves the striatonigral system (producing parkinsonism), the olivopontocerebellar system (producing ataxia), and central autonomic pathways including brainstem and intermediolateral spinal cord neurons (producing autonomic failure). The distribution of pathology determines whether the parkinsonian or cerebellar phenotype predominates, although most patients have overlapping features and prominent dysautonomia.","additionalDifferentials":["Parkinson disease","Progressive supranuclear palsy","Corticobasal degeneration","Pure autonomic failure","Sporadic and hereditary cerebellar ataxias","Dementia with Lewy bodies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain for putaminal, pontine, and cerebellar atrophy and the hot cross bun sign","Autonomic function testing including orthostatic blood pressure measurement","Assessment of bladder function and post-void residual volume","Trial of dopaminergic therapy to document the limited response","Sleep evaluation for stridor and REM sleep behaviour disorder","Exclusion of secondary causes of ataxia or parkinsonism"],"redFlags":["Inspiratory stridor or sleep-related airway obstruction, which may cause sudden deterioration","Severe symptomatic orthostatic hypotension causing syncope and falls","Rapid progression with loss of mobility over a short period","Significant dysphagia with aspiration risk"],"prognosis":"MSA progresses faster than Parkinson disease, with loss of independent walking usually within a few years and median survival of roughly a decade; stridor, aspiration, and autonomic complications drive mortality.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing Parkinson disease and missing early prominent autonomic failure","Overtreating with dopaminergic drugs despite poor benefit and worsening hypotension","Failing to recognise stridor as a potentially life-threatening sign","Overlooking urinary retention and its complications","Attributing early falls and ataxia to ageing rather than the disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"mumps":{"name":"Mumps","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["epidemic parotitis"],"chapter":213,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1646-1649","clinicalPearls":["Acute, self-limited systemic viral illness typically characterized by parotitis or other salivary gland swelling; suspect in any patient with parotitis or mumps complications regardless of age, vaccination status, or travel history (p.1646).","Parotitis is generally bilateral but may not be synchronous on both sides; unilateral involvement occurs in about one-third of cases, with tenderness and obliteration of the space between the earlobe and angle of the mandible (p.1647).","Parotitis is often preceded by a several-day prodrome of low-grade fever, malaise, myalgia, headache, and anorexia; it typically lasts ~5 days (range 3-7) with most cases resolving within 10 days (p.1647).","Infection is asymptomatic in ~20% of unvaccinated patients; it can also present as nonspecific respiratory symptoms (p.1647).","Fully vaccinated persons can still contract mumps, but are at lower risk for mumps and for mumps complications (p.1647).","Buccal swabs are the best specimens for virus detection; the parotid gland should be massaged for 30 s before collection, and specimens collected as close to parotitis onset as possible (maximal shedding within 5 days of symptom onset) (p.1649).","Complications can occur in the absence of parotitis and are more common in adults than children and in males than females (largely due to orchitis) (p.1648)."],"pathophysiology":"Caused by mumps virus, a single-stranded negative-sense enveloped RNA paramyxovirus (Rubulavirus genus, Paramyxoviridae); there is only one serotype (p.1646). Humans are the only known natural reservoir; transmission is via direct contact with respiratory droplets or saliva of an infected person. Incubation averages 16-18 days (range 12-25). A person is most infectious from 2 days before until 5 days after onset of parotitis (virus detected in saliva as early as 7 days before to as late as 9 days after onset) (p.1647). Primary replication likely occurs in the nasal/upper respiratory mucosal epithelium, with spread to regional lymph nodes and infection of mononuclear cells facilitating viremia lasting ~3-5 days. Viremia produces acute inflammatory reactions most commonly in the salivary glands (parotitis) and testes (orchitis), with dissemination also to kidneys, CNS, pancreas, heart, ovaries, mammary glands, cochlear perilymph, and (in pregnancy) the fetus. Mumps encephalitis in many cases appears to be a para- or postinfectious demyelinating process rather than direct viral cytotoxicity, though rare primary encephalitis with viral isolation from brain tissue does occur (p.1647-1648).","additionalDifferentials":["Influenza A H3N2 (can increase parotitis cases)","Parainfluenza virus types 1-3","Epstein-Barr virus","Human herpesviruses 6A and 6B","Herpes simplex viruses types 1 and 2","Coxsackievirus A","Adenovirus","Parvovirus B19","Echovirus","Lymphocytic choriomeningitis virus","HIV","Sarcoidosis","Sjogren's syndrome","Mikulicz's syndrome","Parinaud's oculoglandular syndrome","Uremia","Diabetes mellitus","Laundry starch ingestion / malnutrition / cirrhosis and some drug treatments","Salivary duct obstruction, cysts, or tumors (cause of unilateral parotitis)","Testicular torsion (mimics mumps orchitis)","Bacterial prostatic/urinary tract infection, chlamydia, gonorrhea, and viral orchitis (coxsackievirus, varicella, echovirus, CMV)"],"infectionMimics":["Suppurative (bacterial) parotitis","Parainfluenza virus types 1-3","Epstein-Barr virus","Human herpesviruses 6A/6B","Herpes simplex viruses 1 and 2","Coxsackievirus A","Adenovirus","Parvovirus B19","Echovirus","Lymphocytic choriomeningitis virus","HIV","Influenza A H3N2","Enteroviral aseptic meningitis (clinically indistinguishable from mumps meningitis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sarcoidosis","Sjogren's syndrome","Mikulicz's syndrome","Parinaud's oculoglandular syndrome","Uremia","Diabetes mellitus","Malnutrition / laundry starch ingestion / cirrhosis","Drug-induced parotid swelling","Salivary ductal obstruction, cysts, or tumors","Testicular torsion (mimicking orchitis)"],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR (preferred virologic confirmation) on blood, saliva, urine, or CSF; buccal swab is the best specimen","Viral culture","Mumps-specific IgM serology (note: often undetectable, transient, or delayed in vaccinated patients, and can give false positives from cross-reactions)","Paired acute- and convalescent-phase IgG for a fourfold rise (IgG testing generally not recommended, as titers may already be elevated in vaccinated/previously infected patients)","Serum amylase and lipase (both elevated suggests pancreatitis in addition to parotitis)"],"redFlags":["Orchitis (most common complication; up to 30% of unvaccinated and 6% of vaccinated postpubertal males) with possible testicular atrophy and temporary subfertility","Aseptic meningitis (up to 10% of unvaccinated, <=1% of vaccinated patients)","Encephalitis (<=1% of patients; high fever, altered consciousness, seizures, focal neurologic signs; mortality ~1.5%)","Sensorineural hearing loss / deafness (usually sudden, unilateral, transient; bilateral and permanent loss are rare)","Oophoritis (~7% unvaccinated, <=1% vaccinated postpubertal women)","Pancreatitis (~4% unvaccinated, <1% vaccinated)","Supraglottic edema (rare, life-threatening) from obstructed lymphatic drainage","Other CNS complications: cerebellar ataxia, facial palsy, transverse myelitis, hydrocephalus, Guillain-Barre syndrome, flaccid paralysis","Myocarditis and endocardial fibroelastosis (rare but potentially severe)"],"prognosis":"Generally a benign, self-resolving illness; death due to mumps is exceedingly rare (p.1648). Aseptic meningitis is self-limited without significant risk of death or long-term sequelae. Encephalitis carries a mortality of ~1.5%, may leave permanent sequelae, and has poorer outcomes in adults than children. Orchitis can cause testicular atrophy in ~30-50% of affected testicles and temporary sterility/subfertility, but no studies have assessed the risk of permanent infertility. Mumps-related hearing loss is usually transient; bilateral permanent loss is rare. Mumps in pregnancy is generally benign and no more severe than in non-pregnant women, and evidence for an association with increased spontaneous abortion or fetal death is inconclusive (p.1648).","pitfalls":["A negative virologic or serologic result does NOT rule out mumps in a person with clinical signs, especially in vaccinated patients (p.1649).","Failure to detect mumps IgM is very common in vaccinated patients (IgM response often undetectable, transient, or delayed); IgM can also yield false positives from cross-reactions (p.1649).","Presence of parotitis, orchitis, or oophoritis supports a mumps diagnosis but their absence does NOT exclude it; complications can occur without parotitis (p.1648).","A positive IgG titer indicates only prior exposure (vaccination or natural infection) and does NOT predict protection; IgG titers should NOT be used to infer immunity in close contacts since they may instead reflect acute infection (p.1649).","Elevated serum amylase can arise from parotitis OR pancreatitis; lipase is needed to distinguish (p.1648).","Mumps reinfection and recurrent infection can occur and may be more common than previously thought (p.1648).","MMR vaccine has NOT been shown to prevent illness or alter severity in persons already infected and is NOT recommended as postexposure prophylaxis; mumps immune globulin is also NOT recommended for postexposure prophylaxis or treatment (p.1649)."],"severityClassification":"No formal severity classification in the source. Spectrum ranges from asymptomatic infection (~20% of unvaccinated) through classic self-limited parotitis to complicated disease (orchitis, oophoritis, pancreatitis, hearing loss, aseptic meningitis, encephalitis); subclinical CNS involvement (CSF pleocytosis) occurs in up to 55% of patients.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1646-1649"],"crossLinks":[]},"muscular_dystrophies_and_other_muscle":{"name":"Muscular Dystrophies and Other Muscle Diseases","system":"Neuromuscular / Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Myopathies","Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD)","Becker muscular dystrophy (BMD)","Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy (LGMD)","Emery-Dreifuss muscular dystrophy (EDMD)","Myotonic dystrophy (DM1/DM2)","Facioscapulohumeral dystrophy (FSHD)","Oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy (OPMD)","Mitochondrial myopathy","Periodic paralysis / channelopathy","Pompe disease (acid maltase deficiency)","McArdle disease"],"chapter":460,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3634","clinicalPearls":["Most myopathies cause persistent, symmetric, proximal (limb-girdle) weakness with preserved reflexes and intact sensation; associated sensory loss instead points toward a peripheral neuropathy or CNS (myelopathy) process rather than primary muscle disease.","The pattern of muscle involvement is the single most useful clue for narrowing the differential: facial weakness with scapular winging suggests FSHD, facial/distal weakness with grip myotonia is virtually diagnostic of myotonic dystrophy type 1, ptosis/ophthalmoparesis suggests oculopharyngeal dystrophy or mitochondrial myopathy, and asymmetric flexor-forearm plus quadriceps weakness suggests inclusion body myositis.","Creatine kinase (CK) is the most sensitive marker of muscle damage; the MM isoenzyme predominates in skeletal muscle while CK-MB marks cardiac muscle. CK can be elevated in healthy people after strenuous activity, trauma, prolonged cramp, or seizure.","Elevated AST, ALT, aldolase, and LDH can originate from muscle and be misattributed to liver disease; a normal gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT) helps confirm a muscle (rather than hepatic) source because GGT is not present in muscle.","The Gower sign (using the arms to climb up the legs to rise from the floor) and a waddling or hyperlordotic toe-walking gait reflect proximal hip, thigh, and trunk weakness.","Calf enlargement in many limb-girdle dystrophies represents true muscle hypertrophy that stays strong late in the disease, so the term 'pseudohypertrophy' is best avoided; by contrast, early medial gastrocnemius atrophy characterizes Miyoshi myopathy (dysferlin/anoctamin-5).","Cardiac and ventilatory muscle involvement are the leading causes of mortality across the dystrophies, so cardiology follow-up, ECG monitoring, and surveillance for respiratory insufficiency are central to care.","Genetic testing is the gold standard for hereditary myopathies, but next-generation panels can miss large deletions/duplications and intronic mutations and frequently return variants of uncertain significance; muscle imaging (MRI/ultrasound) can support a specific diagnosis by showing characteristic involvement patterns.","Myotonic dystrophy type 1 is multisystem: hatchet facies, frontal balding, distal weakness, cataracts, cardiac conduction block, insulin resistance, gonadal atrophy, hypersomnia, and cognitive impairment; congenital DM1 occurs almost exclusively in infants of affected mothers.","Episodic weakness beginning after age 25 is almost never due to a periodic paralysis, with thyrotoxic periodic paralysis being the notable exception (favoring men of Asian descent despite higher thyrotoxicosis rates in women).","In McArdle disease, a slow warm-up or brief rest produces a 'second-wind' phenomenon as the muscle shifts to fatty acid utilization; the forearm exercise test shows a blunted venous lactate rise with a normal ammonia rise.","Ragged red fibers and cytochrome-oxidase (COX)-negative fibers on muscle biopsy, often with elevated serum lactate, FGF-21, and GDF-15, point toward a mitochondrial myopathy."],"pathophysiology":"Myopathies reflect structural or functional impairment intrinsic to muscle, distinguishing them from anterior horn cell, peripheral nerve, and neuromuscular junction disorders. In the dystrophies, the molecular defect lies in proteins linking the sarcolemma to the cytoskeleton and extracellular matrix: dystrophin (DMD/BMD) connects the actin cytoskeleton through the dystroglycan complex to the matrix, and mutations in sarcoglycans, dysferlin, calpain-3, anoctamin-5, glycosylation enzymes of alpha-dystroglycan, and sarcomeric/Z-disk and nuclear-envelope proteins underlie the limb-girdle, distal, and Emery-Dreifuss phenotypes. Nuclear-membrane protein defects (emerin, lamin A/C, nesprin-1/2, LUMA) destabilize the inner nuclear envelope and cause EDMD with prominent contractures and arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy. Myotonic dystrophies arise from non-coding repeat expansions (CTG in DMPK for DM1, CCTG in CNBP for DM2) acting through a toxic RNA gain of function: mutant RNA forms nuclear inclusions that sequester splicing regulators, causing widespread aberrant splicing and the multisystem phenotype, with anticipation as repeat length grows across generations. FSHD results from hypomethylation at 4q35 (contracted D4Z4 repeats in FSHD1; SMCHD1/DNMT3B/LRIF1 mutations in FSHD2) that permits toxic expression of the DUX4 transcription factor. Energy-metabolism disorders impair glycolysis (e.g., myophosphorylase/McArdle), glycogen handling (acid maltase/Pompe), or fatty-acid transport into mitochondria via carnitine palmitoyltransferase, producing either exertional cramping with rhabdomyolysis/myoglobinuria or progressive weakness. Mitochondrial myopathies stem from maternally inherited mtDNA point mutations or deletions, or nuclear-gene defects of mtDNA maintenance/replication, impairing oxidative phosphorylation. Channelopathies of the sarcolemma (calcium CACNA1S, sodium SCN4A, potassium Kir2.1, chloride CLCN1) disturb membrane excitability to produce periodic paralysis and/or myotonia. Endocrine and toxic myopathies act through hormonal or pharmacologic injury, often producing fiber atrophy rather than destruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Anterior horn cell disease (amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, spinal muscular atrophy)","Peripheral neuropathy / radiculopathy","Myelopathy and other CNS lesions causing weakness","Neuromuscular junction disorders (myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton, congenital myasthenia, botulism)","Inflammatory myopathies (polymyositis, dermatomyositis, immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy, inclusion body myositis)","Fibromyalgia and polymyalgia rheumatica (pain without true weakness; normal CK/EMG/biopsy)","Stiff-person syndrome and Isaacs' (neuromyotonia) syndrome"],"infectionMimics":["Pyomyositis and other bacterial muscle infections causing focal muscle enlargement","Parasitic muscle infection (e.g., causing muscle enlargement)","SLONM occurring in the setting of HIV infection","Viral or bacterial infection-triggered episodes of rhabdomyolysis/myoglobinuria"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy mimicking limb-girdle dystrophy clinically and histopathologically (screen for anti-HMGCR and anti-SRP antibodies)","Sarcoid granulomatous infiltration or amyloid deposition producing muscle enlargement","Focal myositis or fasciitis","Hepatic disease wrongly inferred from muscle-derived transaminase/aldolase/LDH elevations","Thyrotoxic hyperreflexia with fasciculations mimicking amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Diabetic muscle infarction presenting as acute painful indurated thigh/calf swelling"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum CK (MM vs CK-MB isoenzymes); AST, ALT, aldolase, LDH with GGT to localize muscle vs liver origin","Needle EMG (short-duration small-amplitude polyphasic MUAPs firing early; myotonic discharges; irritability in active necrosis), nerve conduction studies, repetitive nerve stimulation, and single-fiber EMG","Skeletal muscle MRI/ultrasound to define involvement pattern and guide biopsy site","Targeted/next-generation genetic testing (gold standard for hereditary myopathies), with awareness of missed large deletions/intronic mutations and variants of uncertain significance","Muscle biopsy with histochemistry, immunohistochemistry (dystrophin, sarcoglycans, dysferlin, emerin, alpha-dystroglycan) and electron microscopy","Forearm exercise test (lactate and ammonia) for glycolytic defects; dried blood spot enzyme assay screening for Pompe disease","Serum lactate, FGF-21, and GDF-15 for mitochondrial disease; serum/urine potassium and thyroid studies for periodic paralyses; ECG for conduction disease across dystrophies","Endocrine workup: thyroid function tests, parathyroid hormone, vitamin D and calcium; autoantibody panels (including anti-HMGCR, anti-SRP, anti-GAD for stiff-person syndrome)"],"redFlags":["Cardiac conduction abnormalities or arrhythmias (atrial fibrillation, atrioventricular/complete heart block, atrial standstill) in EDMD and myotonic dystrophy carrying risk of sudden death","Dilated cardiomyopathy or heart failure in DMD/BMD, LGMD, and myofibrillar myopathies","Ventilatory/diaphragmatic muscle weakness causing respiratory insufficiency (may be the presenting feature in adult Pompe disease and LGMD)","Myoglobinuria with CK rising to over 100 times normal, signaling rhabdomyolysis","Life-threatening hypokalemia-related arrhythmias during hypokalemic periodic paralysis attacks; potentially serious long-QT and ventricular arrhythmias in Andersen-Tawil syndrome","Dysphagia with aspiration risk in oculopharyngeal dystrophy/OPDM and myotonic dystrophy","Complete heart block and cardiac arrest in Kearns-Sayre syndrome; stroke-like episodes in MELAS"],"prognosis":"The dystrophies generally follow a slowly progressive course, though severity varies between and within subtypes. Life expectancy is reduced in DMD and BMD, with most BMD patients surviving into the fourth or fifth decade; death is often attributable to associated cardiomyopathy. FSHD usually spares the heart, but about 20% of patients progress to pelvic-girdle involvement and possible wheelchair dependence. In Kearns-Sayre syndrome, death from heart block occurs in roughly 20% of patients, underscoring the value of cardiac surveillance and prophylactic pacing for conduction defects. MELAS in full expression can lead to dementia, a bedridden state, and a fatal outcome. Most endocrine myopathies respond to treatment of the underlying disorder. For many hereditary myopathies no specific disease-modifying therapy exists, and care centers on preserving function and managing cardiac and respiratory complications.","pitfalls":["Attributing isolated fatigue to primary muscle disease: fatigue without abnormal clinical or laboratory findings almost never indicates a myopathy, and asthenia must be distinguished from true pathologic fatigability.","Misreading muscle-derived transaminase/aldolase/LDH elevations as liver disease; checking GGT clarifies the source.","Diagnosing myositis in FSHD when biopsy shows an inflammatory infiltrate, leading to incorrect immunosuppressive treatment.","Overlooking immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy (a treatable autoimmune cause) in apparent LGMD without an identified pathogenic mutation; antibody screening is warranted.","Calling calf enlargement 'pseudohypertrophy' when it is true hypertrophy in many LGMDs.","Assuming hyperkalemia in hyperkalemic periodic paralysis; patients are often normokalemic during attacks, and the disorder is defined by precipitation with potassium loading.","Misattributing exertional cramps: cramps follow short intense bursts in glycolytic defects, whereas CPT2-deficiency pain appears only after prolonged exercise once muscle breakdown has begun.","Preferring muscle biopsy over imaging in suspected diabetic muscle infarction, where hemorrhage into the biopsy site can occur.","Relying solely on next-generation sequencing panels, which can miss large deletions/duplications and intronic mutations and may yield variants of uncertain significance."],"severityClassification":"No single unifying grading system; severity is assessed disease-specifically. Functional milestones (Gower sign, gait pattern, ambulatory status, wheelchair dependence) and manual muscle testing (e.g., biopsy is best taken from a muscle around grade 4/5) gauge weakness. Mortality risk is stratified by cardiac (conduction disease, cardiomyopathy) and respiratory (ventilatory insufficiency) involvement. In rhabdomyolysis, CK over 100 times normal accompanies myoglobinuria, and for statin myotoxicity, marked CK elevation (more than 3 to 5 times baseline) with severe myalgia, weakness, or myoglobinuria is an indication to stop the drug.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3634","Harrison 22e p.3635","Harrison 22e p.3637","Harrison 22e p.3638","Harrison 22e p.3640","Harrison 22e p.3641","Harrison 22e p.3642","Harrison 22e p.3643","Harrison 22e p.3644","Harrison 22e p.3645","Harrison 22e p.3646","Harrison 22e p.3647","Harrison 22e p.3648"],"crossLinks":[]},"myalgic_encephalomyelitis_chronic_fatigue":{"name":"Myalgic Encephalomyelitis/ Chronic Fatigue Syndrome","system":"Neurologic / Multisystem","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ME/CFS","Chronic Fatigue Syndrome","Myalgic Encephalomyelitis","CFS","ME"],"chapter":461,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3649","clinicalPearls":["ME/CFS is a chronic, complex, multisystem illness whose functional impact on daily functioning has been compared to that of multiple sclerosis, rheumatoid arthritis, and congestive heart failure.","The defining feature is persistent, unexplained fatigue causing substantial functional impairment, together with postexertional malaise (PEM) - worsening of symptoms after physical, mental, or emotional exertion that would not have been a problem before the illness.","PEM relapses last longer than a day and can persist for weeks; the relationship between a relapse and the preceding activity is often not apparent to the patient, so PEM may go unrecognized.","Common accompanying symptoms include pain, cognitive dysfunction (patient-described 'brain fog'), unrefreshing sleep, headache, sore throat, tender lymph nodes, muscle and joint aches, feverishness, and orthostatic intolerance.","There is no diagnostic test; diagnosis rests on a characteristic profile of patient-reported symptoms, requiring evaluation by an experienced clinician.","The 2015 Institute of Medicine criteria require substantial activity reduction with profound new-onset fatigue not relieved by rest, plus PEM, plus unrefreshing sleep, plus either cognitive impairment or orthostatic intolerance.","Routine laboratory tests are typically within normal limits in ME/CFS; their value lies in identifying alternative or contributing illnesses rather than confirming the diagnosis.","Roughly 10% of patients remain ill for 6 months or more after a variety of infections (e.g., Epstein-Barr virus, Ross River virus, Q fever, Ebola, SARS-CoV-1, Giardia); recovery is otherwise the rule.","Recognition of ME/CFS as a diagnosable component of Long COVID has increased clinical awareness, but patients still encounter stigma and misunderstanding from clinicians.","An estimated 80% or more of people who meet ME/CFS criteria have never been diagnosed by a health care provider.","Patients are reported to be more medication-sensitive than the general population, so any drug should begin at lower-than-usual doses and be increased slowly.","Patients may appear well during an office visit and then relapse afterward from the exertion of attending the consultation."],"pathophysiology":"ME/CFS is understood as a complex disorder involving alterations across multiple interrelated homeostatic systems rather than a single mechanism. A wide range of infectious agents (viral and nonviral) can trigger a postinfectious fatiguing illness resembling ME/CFS, and noninfectious stressors - toxins, physical trauma, adverse life events, and cumulative allostatic load ('wear and tear') - are also associated. Twin and family studies suggest contributions from shared environment and genetic factors. Evidence for immunologic dysfunction is inconsistent and not established in most patients; reported findings include modest antinuclear antibody elevations, reduced immunoglobulin subclasses, impaired mitogen-driven lymphocyte proliferation, decreased natural killer cell activity, altered cytokine production, and altered T-cell metabolism. A hypothesis that excess cytokines such as interleukin-1 or interferon-alpha drive the fatigue and flulike symptoms lacks compelling supporting data. Other inconsistent and nondiagnostic findings include nonspecific regional brain MRI changes, autonomic nervous system dysfunction, hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis abnormalities, altered metabolism, and gut microbiome dysbiosis. Unifying multisystem models integrate infection, immune, central and autonomic nervous system, metabolic, genetic, lifestyle, and stress factors.","additionalDifferentials":["Broad differential of fatigue (medical and psychiatric causes evaluated per chronic fatigue workup)","Thyroid dysfunction (e.g., hypothyroidism)","Anemia / iron deficiency or iron overload","Celiac disease","Depression and anxiety disorders","Postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome (POTS) and other dysautonomia","Idiopathic chronic fatigue (unexplained chronic fatigue not meeting full ME/CFS criteria)"],"infectionMimics":["Postinfectious fatiguing illness following Epstein-Barr virus","Q fever (Coxiella burnetii) postinfectious fatigue","Ross River virus infection","Post-acute sequelae of SARS-CoV-2 (Long COVID) with prolonged fatiguing illness","Giardia-associated postinfectious fatigue"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Fibromyalgia","Hypothyroidism and other endocrine disorders","Depression and other psychiatric conditions presenting with fatigue","Anemia","Celiac disease","Sjogren's syndrome"],"additionalInvestigations":["Complete blood count","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein","Electrolytes, fasting glucose, calcium, phosphate","Renal function tests (blood urea nitrogen, glomerular filtration rate)","Liver function panel (bilirubin, ALT, AST, alkaline phosphatase, gamma-glutamyl transferase, total protein, albumin/globulin ratio)","Thyroid function tests (TSH, free thyroxine)","Iron studies to assess both deficiency and overload (serum iron, transferrin saturation, ferritin)","Celiac disease screening tests","Urinalysis","Lean/standing (orthostatic) test for POTS","Mental health assessment screening for depression and anxiety"],"redFlags":["Continued decline in function (should prompt evaluation for another illness rather than being attributed to ME/CFS)","New, worsening, or unusual symptoms warranting further workup for a new illness","Serious depression and increased suicide risk associated with social isolation and loss of hope","Severe disease leaving the patient essentially homebound"],"prognosis":"Severity ranges from mild or moderate (retaining varying degrees of pre-illness function) to severe (essentially homebound). Most patients experience some improvement and stabilize, but a return to the prior level of function is unusual. A continued functional decline is atypical and should trigger evaluation for other illnesses. Because of social isolation and loss of hope, serious depression and an increased risk of suicide are reported, warranting active screening and referral.","pitfalls":["Trivializing the illness or dismissing patients, contributing to stigma and underdiagnosis (most who meet criteria are never diagnosed).","Failing to recognize PEM because patients do not connect relapses to preceding exertion, and may appear well in clinic only to crash afterward.","Attributing a continued functional decline to ME/CFS rather than investigating an alternative diagnosis.","Standard-dose medication initiation in patients who are unusually drug-sensitive, increasing toxicity risk.","Encouraging overexertion on 'good days' that leads to the 'push and crash' cycle and negates functional gains.","Relying on laboratory tests for diagnosis - results are usually normal and serve only to exclude other conditions."],"severityClassification":"Severity is described clinically as mild/moderate (patients retain varying degrees of pre-illness function) versus severe (patients essentially homebound). Diagnostically, the 2015 IOM criteria require substantial activity reduction lasting more than 6 months with profound new-onset fatigue not relieved by rest, plus postexertional malaise, plus unrefreshing sleep, plus either cognitive impairment or orthostatic intolerance, with symptoms present at least half the time and of at least moderate intensity.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3649","Harrison 22e p.3650","Harrison 22e p.3651"],"crossLinks":[]},"myasthenia_gravis":{"name":"Myasthenia Gravis","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MG","Lambert-Eaton syndrome"],"chapter":459,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3624-3633","clinicalPearls":["Cardinal features are fatigable, fluctuating skeletal muscle weakness that worsens with repeated use or late in the day and improves with rest or sleep (Harrison 22e p.3625-3626).","Ocular and eyelid muscles are commonly involved early; diplopia and ptosis are frequent initial symptoms, and facial weakness can produce a 'snarling' expression on attempted smiling (Harrison 22e p.3626).","Bulbar involvement causes nasal/dysarthric 'mushy' speech, dysphagia with nasal regurgitation or aspiration, and chewing weakness; neck extensor weakness can cause head drop (Harrison 22e p.3626).","If weakness stays confined to ocular muscles for 3 years, later generalization is unlikely (ocular MG), though rare patients generalize beyond 5 years (Harrison 22e p.3626).","Bulbar, neck, and ventilatory weakness can be especially prominent in MuSK antibody-positive MG (Harrison 22e p.3626).","Ice-pack test: applying ice over a ptotic eye for 2 min produces a lid rise of 2 mm or more (positive) when ptosis is due to an NMJ defect; it is a quick, very sensitive bedside test (Harrison 22e p.3626-3627).","Approximately 10-15% of MG patients have a thymoma, so chest CT or MRI to evaluate the thymus is performed at diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.3628)."],"pathophysiology":"MG is an autoimmune NMJ disorder in which the number of available postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors (AChRs) is reduced and the postsynaptic folds are flattened ('simplified'), decreasing the efficiency of neuromuscular transmission so that normally released ACh produces small end-plate potentials that may fail to trigger muscle action potentials. Anti-AChR antibodies (IgG, T-cell dependent) act by three mechanisms: accelerated AChR turnover via cross-linking and endocytosis, complement-mediated damage to the postsynaptic membrane, and blockade of the ACh-binding site. Anti-MuSK antibodies (IgG4, do not activate complement) impair MuSK-LRP4 interaction and AChR clustering; anti-LRP4 antibodies (IgG1) cause complement-mediated destruction. The normal decline in ACh release on repeated activity (presynaptic rundown) combined with reduced transmission efficiency produces myasthenic fatigue and the decremental response to repetitive nerve stimulation. The thymus is abnormal in ~75% of AChR antibody-positive patients (~65% hyperplastic, ~10% thymoma) (Harrison 22e p.3624-3626).","additionalDifferentials":["Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS)","Congenital myasthenic syndromes (nonautoimmune, >30 genes; double-seronegative MG mimic)","Drug-induced myasthenia (e.g., penicillamine)","Immune checkpoint inhibitor-related autoimmune MG (and ICI-related myositis/myocarditis mimic)","Botulism","Hyperthyroidism (Graves' disease)","Intracranial mass compressing extraocular nerves (e.g., sphenoid ridge meningioma)","Oculopharyngeal dystrophy","Progressive external ophthalmoplegia / mitochondrial myopathy (Kearns-Sayre syndrome)"],"infectionMimics":["Botulism (Clostridium botulinum toxin cleaves proteins needed for ACh release, causing myasthenia-like bulbar weakness without sensory signs; reflexes present early)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (presynaptic, P/Q-type calcium channel antibodies)","Congenital myasthenic syndromes","Drug-induced myasthenia","Immune checkpoint inhibitor-related myositis","Hyperthyroidism (Graves' disease)","Progressive external ophthalmoplegia / mitochondrial myopathy","Intracranial mass lesion causing diplopia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Anti-AChR antibody radioimmunoassay (positive in ~85% of generalized MG, ~50% of ocular MG; a positive result is virtually diagnostic but a negative result does not exclude MG)","Anti-MuSK antibodies (present in up to ~40% of AChR antibody-negative generalized MG)","Anti-LRP4 antibodies (low specificity; only tested when phenotype/electrodiagnostics fit MG but AChR and MuSK antibodies are absent)","Repetitive nerve stimulation (decrement >10% at 2-3 Hz is highly probable for MG)","Single-fiber EMG (blocking and jitter with normal fiber density; sensitive and confirmatory but not specific)","Ice-pack test for ptosis","Chest CT or MRI to evaluate the thymus for thymoma","Thyroid function tests in all patients with suspected MG","CT or MRI of head/orbits to exclude intracranial lesions in ocular or cranial presentations","Pulmonary function tests / forced vital capacity to assess respiratory impairment"],"redFlags":["Myasthenic crisis: ventilatory weakness requiring intubation or noninvasive ventilation to avoid intubation, managed in intensive care (treat respiratory infection, attend to fluids); requires rescue therapy with IVIg or plasmapheresis (Harrison 22e p.3626, p.3628-3629).","Dysphagia with nasal regurgitation or aspiration of liquids/food (bulbar involvement) (Harrison 22e p.3626).","Enlargement of the thymus in a patient older than 40 years is highly suspicious for thymoma (Harrison 22e p.3628).","Infection or tapering of MG therapy can precipitate myasthenic exacerbation or crisis (Harrison 22e p.3626).","ICI-related MG with concurrent myocarditis carries a high fatality rate in fulminant disease (Harrison 22e p.3627)."],"prognosis":"Prognosis has improved strikingly with modern treatment, and nearly all myasthenic patients can be returned to full productive lives with proper therapy. The course is variable, with exacerbations and remissions, particularly in the first 1-3 years; in ~85% of patients weakness becomes generalized. A cure remains elusive (Harrison 22e p.3625-3628).","pitfalls":["A negative anti-AChR antibody test does not exclude MG, and the antibody level does not correlate well with disease severity (Harrison 22e p.3627).","Distinguish LEMS from MG: LEMS is presynaptic (P/Q-type calcium channel antibodies), preferentially affects proximal lower-limb muscles, typically spares prominent ocular/bulbar onset, and shows depressed/absent reflexes plus autonomic features (dry mouth, orthostasis, impotence); unlike MG, strength and CMAP amplitude increase (incremental response) after brief exercise or high-rate (20-50 Hz) stimulation. In older adults LEMS is associated with malignancy, most commonly small-cell lung cancer (Harrison 22e p.3628).","Anti-striated muscle (titin) antibodies are not pathogenic and do not confirm MG or a thymoma (Harrison 22e p.3627).","Aminoglycoside, quinolone, and macrolide antibiotics, IV magnesium, procainamide, and penicillamine can exacerbate myasthenic weakness; botulinum toxin injections should be avoided in MG patients (Harrison 22e p.3627).","Anti-MuSK MG generally responds less to anticholinesterase agents (and may worsen) and does not respond to complement inhibition because MuSK antibodies are IgG4 and do not activate complement (Harrison 22e p.3626, p.3628-3629).","Diagnosis must be confirmed definitively before treatment because other treatable conditions mimic MG and treatment carries surgical/drug risks (Harrison 22e p.3626)."],"severityClassification":"Weakness is classified as ocular versus generalized (the latter affecting facial, bulbar, axial, or limb muscles in ~85% of patients). Worsening requiring intubation or noninvasive ventilation defines myasthenic crisis; all other worsening is termed exacerbation. Standardized measures referenced include the Myasthenia Gravis Activities of Daily Living (MGADL) and Quantitative MG (QMG) scores (Harrison 22e p.3626, p.3631).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3624-3633"],"crossLinks":["myasthenic_crisis","gbs"]},"mycoplasma_infection":{"name":"Mycoplasma Infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mycoplasma pneumoniae","atypical pneumonia","walking pneumonia"],"chapter":193,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1464-1466","clinicalPearls":["M. pneumoniae pneumonia is also called Eaton agent pneumonia, primary atypical pneumonia, and 'walking' pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Onset of pneumonia is usually gradual over several days; the most common presenting symptom is cough, which is typically nonproductive, though headache, malaise, chills, and fever are noted in the majority of patients (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Pneumonia develops in only 3-13% of infected individuals; upper respiratory illness is far more common, with an estimated incidence up to 20 times that of pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Wheezes or rales are detected in about 80% of patients with M. pneumoniae pneumonia, but in many patients pneumonia can be diagnosed only by chest radiography (Harrison 22e p.1465).","The most common radiographic pattern is peribronchial pneumonia with thickened bronchial markings, streaks of interstitial infiltration, and subsegmental atelectasis; segmental or lobar consolidation is not uncommon (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Clinical presentation does not reliably distinguish M. pneumoniae pneumonia from other causes of community-acquired pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Many patients with extrapulmonary M. pneumoniae disease do not have respiratory disease (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Hematologic manifestations include hemolytic anemia, aplastic anemia, cold agglutinins, disseminated intravascular coagulation, and hypercoagulopathy; when anemia occurs it generally develops in the second or third week of illness (Harrison 22e p.1466).","Erythema multiforme major (Stevens-Johnson syndrome) is the most clinically significant skin eruption associated with M. pneumoniae and appears to occur more commonly with M. pneumoniae than with other infectious agents; in M. pneumoniae-induced rash and mucositis (MIRM)/reactive infectious mucocutaneous eruption (RIME) there may be minimal cutaneous involvement with prominent mucosal disease (Harrison 22e p.1466, p.591).","M. pneumoniae has been implicated as a likely etiologic agent in 5-7% of cases of encephalitis (Harrison 22e p.1465).","Diagnosis is most sensitively and rapidly made by combining PCR of respiratory tract secretions with serologic testing of paired acute- and convalescent-phase samples (Harrison 22e p.1466).","M. pneumoniae is the most frequently detected 'atypical' organism in adult community-acquired pneumonia; older pooled data gave a prevalence of about 22.7%, and more recent (2002-2015) analyses gave about 7.2% (Harrison 22e p.1465)."],"pathophysiology":"Mycoplasmas are prokaryotes of the class Mollicutes, the smallest organisms capable of independent replication; they lack a cell wall and are bound only by a cell membrane, which explains the inactivity of beta-lactam antibiotics (penicillins and cephalosporins) against them. M. pneumoniae is generally an extracellular pathogen that attaches to ciliated respiratory epithelial cells via a complex terminal organelle using interactive adhesins and accessory proteins. Tissue injury is thought to be mediated by production of hydrogen peroxide and an ADP-ribosylating and vacuolating cytotoxin similar to pertussis toxin. Lacking a cell wall, mycoplasmas lack cell-wall-derived innate immune stimulators (lipopolysaccharide, lipoteichoic acid, peptidoglycan), but membrane lipoproteins act through Toll-like receptors (primarily TLR2). Innate immunity provides most lung defense, cellular immunity may be immunopathogenic, and humoral immunity protects against dissemination; patients with humoral immunodeficiency are prone to disseminated disease (arthritis, meningitis, osteomyelitis). Extrapulmonary manifestations may arise from disseminated infection, postinfectious autoimmune phenomena (e.g., Guillain-Barre syndrome), or possibly an ADP-ribosylating toxin (Harrison 22e p.1464-1466).","additionalDifferentials":["Chlamydia (Chlamydophila) pneumoniae community-acquired pneumonia","Legionella species pneumonia","Streptococcus pneumoniae and other bacterial community-acquired pneumonia","Viral respiratory tract infection / pneumonia"],"infectionMimics":["Chlamydophila pneumoniae pneumonia","Legionella pneumonia","Viral atypical pneumonia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Asthma / reactive airway disease (wheezing overlap)","Drug-induced or herpes-associated erythema multiforme major (overlapping with Mycoplasma-associated SJS/MIRM)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Respiratory PCR for M. pneumoniae (sensitivity 65-90%, specificity 90-100%) - allows rapid early diagnosis","Paired acute- and convalescent-phase serology (IgM and IgG) by enzyme-linked immunoassay; a single acute sample is inadequate because antibodies may not develop until ~2 weeks","Respiratory culture on special media (sensitivity <=60%, specificity 100%) - not recommended for routine diagnosis as it may take weeks","Chest radiography","Cerebrospinal fluid PCR for extrapulmonary (CNS) infection","Macrolide-resistance testing by culture and/or PCR when resistance is suspected"],"redFlags":["Neurologic manifestations: meningoencephalitis, encephalitis, Guillain-Barre syndrome, aseptic meningitis, transverse myelitis, acute disseminated encephalomyelitis, cerebrovascular thromboembolic events, acute psychosis, cerebellar ataxia, cranial neuropathy","Severe mucocutaneous disease: erythema multiforme major / Stevens-Johnson syndrome / MIRM","Hemolytic anemia, disseminated intravascular coagulation, hypercoagulopathy","Disseminated infection (septic arthritis, meningitis, osteomyelitis), especially in hypogammaglobulinemic / humorally immunodeficient patients","Community-acquired pneumonia failing to respond to a penicillin or cephalosporin"],"prognosis":"M. pneumoniae pneumonia is generally self-limited, with symptoms usually resolving within 2-3 weeks of onset even without treatment; appropriate antimicrobial therapy significantly shortens the duration of clinical illness. Infection uncommonly results in critical illness and only rarely in death. Long-term recurrent wheezing or reactive airway disease may follow resolution of acute pneumonia, and a possible link to asthma is under investigation. Immunity following severe infection is more protective and longer-lasting than after mild infection, and genuine second attacks of pneumonia are reported infrequently (Harrison 22e p.1465).","pitfalls":["Because mycoplasmas lack a cell wall, beta-lactam antibiotics (penicillins and cephalosporins) are ineffective; consider M. pneumoniae particularly when community-acquired pneumonia fails to respond to these agents (Harrison 22e p.1464-1465).","M. pneumoniae lacks a cell wall and is therefore not visible on Gram stain (Harrison 22e p.1466).","Measurement of cold agglutinin titers is no longer recommended for diagnosis because the findings are nonspecific and specific M. pneumoniae assays are now available (Harrison 22e p.1466).","A single acute-phase serum sample is not adequate for diagnosis, and IgM antibody can persist for up to 1 year, so its presence may indicate recent rather than acute infection - paired samples are needed (Harrison 22e p.1466).","Little evidence supports the commonly held belief that M. pneumoniae is an important cause of otitis media, with or without bullous myringitis (Harrison 22e p.1465).","A test of cure for eradication is not suggested, because appropriate antibiotics do not appear to shorten the duration of detection by culture or PCR (Harrison 22e p.1466).","Ciprofloxacin and ofloxacin are not recommended for M. pneumoniae owing to high MICs and poor experimental performance, in contrast to respiratory fluoroquinolones (Harrison 22e p.1466)."],"severityClassification":"No formal severity classification is given for Mycoplasma infection; M. pneumoniae illness is generally graded clinically from mild self-limited upper respiratory illness through pneumonia to severe/refractory or critical disease, with macrolide resistance and extrapulmonary involvement marking more severe courses (Harrison 22e p.1464-1466).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1464-1466","Harrison 22e p.591"],"crossLinks":[]},"myelodysplastic_syndrome":{"name":"Myelodysplastic syndrome","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MDS","myelodysplasia","myelodysplastic syndromes","preleukaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of clonal stem cell disorders defined by ineffective haematopoiesis, producing peripheral cytopenias despite a marrow that is usually normocellular or hypercellular","Typically affects older adults, often presenting with symptomatic anaemia; neutropenia and thrombocytopenia may also occur, sometimes with recurrent infection or bleeding","The paradox of low blood counts with a busy marrow reflects intramedullary apoptosis of dysplastic precursors","Diagnosis rests on morphological dysplasia in one or more lineages, blast percentage, and cytogenetic or molecular abnormalities; ring sideroblasts and specific mutations (e.g. SF3B1) carry prognostic meaning","Risk stratification with scoring systems that weight blast count, cytogenetics, and depth of cytopenias guides both prognosis and treatment intensity","Lower-risk disease is managed supportively with transfusions, erythropoiesis-stimulating agents, and iron chelation when transfusion burden is high; an isolated 5q deletion responds well to lenalidomide","Higher-risk disease is treated with hypomethylating agents, and allogeneic stem cell transplant offers the only potential cure for suitable patients","Progression to acute myeloid leukaemia is a defining risk, greatest in high-risk categories with excess blasts and adverse cytogenetics"],"pathophysiology":"MDS arises from an acquired mutation in a haematopoietic stem cell that gains a clonal growth advantage. Accumulating mutations in genes governing RNA splicing, epigenetic regulation, transcription, and signalling produce disordered maturation. Precursors are produced abundantly but undergo excessive apoptosis within the marrow, so mature cells are released ineffectively, giving cytopenias in the face of a cellular marrow. Over time the clone may acquire further mutations and expand, with rising blast counts marking transformation towards acute myeloid leukaemia. Prior chemotherapy or radiotherapy can cause therapy-related MDS, which typically carries adverse cytogenetics.","additionalDifferentials":["Aplastic anaemia (especially hypoplastic MDS)","Vitamin B12 or folate deficiency causing megaloblastic dysplasia","Acute myeloid leukaemia","Myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative overlap syndromes (e.g. CMML)","Reactive dysplasia from alcohol, copper deficiency, or drugs","Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count, reticulocytes, and careful blood film review for dysplastic changes","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine with blast count and assessment of dysplasia and ring sideroblasts","Cytogenetic analysis and targeted molecular/next-generation sequencing panels","Serum erythropoietin level to predict response to erythropoiesis-stimulating agents","B12, folate, and copper levels to exclude reversible mimics","Flow cytometry and PNH clone testing when the picture overlaps with marrow failure"],"redFlags":["Rising blast percentage or new adverse cytogenetics signalling transformation to acute leukaemia","Severe neutropenia with fever requiring urgent treatment for sepsis","Profound thrombocytopenia with clinically significant bleeding","Rapidly worsening pancytopenia demanding urgent haematology review and transplant assessment"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary widely by risk category: lower-risk patients may live many years with supportive care, whereas high-risk disease carries short survival and a substantial chance of progression to acute myeloid leukaemia. Allogeneic transplant is the only curative option but is limited by age and comorbidity.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing MDS without excluding reversible causes of dysplasia such as B12 or folate deficiency, copper deficiency, alcohol, or recent chemotherapy","Failing to obtain cytogenetics and molecular testing, which are central to risk stratification","Undertreating iron overload in chronically transfused patients","Overlooking an isolated 5q deletion that has a favourable, targeted treatment","Missing the transition to acute leukaemia by not repeating marrow assessment when counts deteriorate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"myoclonus":{"name":"Myoclonus","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["myoclonic jerks","myoclonic movements"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined as sudden, brief, shock-like involuntary movements, which may be positive (muscle contraction) or negative (a lapse of tone, as in asterixis)","Classified clinically by distribution (focal, segmental, multifocal, or generalised) and by trigger, such as action or stimulus-sensitive myoclonus","Aetiologically grouped into physiological, essential, epileptic, and symptomatic (secondary) forms, the last being the most common in clinical practice","A crucial step is to identify secondary causes, particularly metabolic and toxic encephalopathies, since these are often reversible","Asterixis (negative myoclonus) is a classic sign of hepatic, renal, and other metabolic encephalopathies","Neurophysiological localisation into cortical, subcortical, spinal, or peripheral origin helps guide investigation and treatment","Post-hypoxic action myoclonus (Lance-Adams syndrome) can follow cardiac arrest and cerebral hypoxia","Treatment principles combine correcting any underlying cause with symptomatic antimyoclonic agents chosen according to the presumed generator"],"pathophysiology":"Myoclonus results from abnormal, excessive neuronal discharge that can arise at several levels of the nervous system. Cortical myoclonus reflects hyperexcitability of the sensorimotor cortex and often coexists with epilepsy; subcortical and brainstem forms arise from deeper generators; and spinal and peripheral forms originate in the cord or nerve. Loss of normal inhibitory control, frequently involving impaired GABAergic and serotonergic mechanisms, allows brief synchronous muscle discharges. Underlying processes include metabolic derangement, drugs and toxins, neurodegeneration, hypoxia, and epilepsy.","additionalDifferentials":["Tremor","Tics","Chorea","Seizures and epileptic phenomena","Dystonic jerks and startle responses","Fasciculations and functional jerks"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Metabolic screen including renal, hepatic, glucose, electrolyte, and calcium studies to identify reversible causes","Review of medications and toxins that can provoke myoclonus","EEG, including back-averaging with EMG, to identify a cortical origin and epileptic activity","MRI of the brain and, where relevant, the spinal cord to seek structural or degenerative causes","Targeted testing for infective, autoimmune, paraneoplastic, or metabolic-genetic disorders when indicated"],"redFlags":["Myoclonus with declining consciousness suggesting a serious metabolic, toxic, or hypoxic encephalopathy","Rapidly progressive myoclonus with cognitive decline raising concern for prion disease or an autoimmune encephalitis","Myoclonus following cardiac arrest, which may indicate severe hypoxic brain injury","Myoclonus as part of a status myoclonicus or in the context of status epilepticus requiring urgent treatment"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is dictated by the underlying cause. Myoclonus due to reversible metabolic or drug causes typically resolves once the trigger is corrected, whereas myoclonus from neurodegenerative, prion, or severe hypoxic injury carries a poor outlook. Symptomatic treatment can meaningfully reduce disability in many chronic cases even when the cause cannot be cured.","pitfalls":["Failing to search for and correct a reversible metabolic or drug-induced cause","Misclassifying myoclonus as tremor, tics, or seizures and mismanaging it","Overlooking a serious progressive cause such as prion disease or autoimmune encephalitis","Not localising the myoclonus neurophysiologically, leading to poorly targeted therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"nafld":{"name":"MASLD / NAFLD (Fatty Liver Disease)","system":"Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NAFLD","NASH","MASLD","MASH","fatty liver"],"chapter":354,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2700","clinicalPearls":["Steatotic liver disease (SLD) is the most common cause of chronic liver disease in the US and worldwide; metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) and steatohepatitis (MASH) now replace the older terms NAFLD and NASH, with ~98% population overlap (Harrison 22e p.2700-2701).","Diagnosis of MASLD requires hepatic steatosis plus little/no alcohol intake (<20 g/d women, <30 g/d men) and at least one cardiometabolic risk factor (overweight/obesity, hypertension, dyslipidemia, insulin resistance/T2DM) (Harrison 22e p.2700, 2703).","Most patients are asymptomatic; MASLD is typically detected incidentally via raised ALT or steatosis on imaging done for other reasons (Harrison 22e p.2702-2703).","Disease spans a dynamic, potentially reversible spectrum: simple steatosis (MASL) -> steatohepatitis (MASH) -> fibrosis -> cirrhosis/HCC; chronic steatohepatitis is the primary driver of fibrosis, and fibrosis severity is the chief predictor of liver-related morbidity/mortality (Harrison 22e p.2700-2701, 2704).","FIB-4 (age, AST, ALT, platelets) is the recommended first-line noninvasive score with excellent negative predictive value: FIB-4 <1.3 = low risk (manage in primary care), >=2.67 = moderate/high risk warranting a secondary test (elastography or ELF) (Harrison 22e p.2704-2705).","T2DM is the strongest risk factor for MASLD acquisition, fibrosis progression, and HCC; the MASLD-T2DM relationship is bidirectional, so patients with MASLD should be screened for T2DM (Harrison 22e p.2703).","Foundational treatment is lifestyle modification with weight loss: ~5% weight loss improves steatosis, while >10% improves MASH and can reverse fibrosis; cardiovascular risk reduction and treatment of metabolic comorbidities apply to all patients (Harrison 22e p.2706).","Statins are safe across the MASLD spectrum and indicated for dyslipidemia given high cardiovascular risk; reluctance to use them is not supported by evidence of liver harm (Harrison 22e p.2707)."],"pathophysiology":"MASLD arises from imbalance between energy intake and expenditure: excess caloric intake, reduced physical activity, and limited peripheral adipose expansion drive visceral adiposity, insulin resistance, and metabolic dysfunction. Hepatocyte triglyceride accumulates from increased free fatty acid delivery (dietary uptake, adipose lipolysis, de novo lipogenesis), impaired fatty acid disposal, or impaired triglyceride export; triglyceride storage itself is relatively safe, so steatosis per se does not damage the liver but is a biomarker of metabolic dysfunction. When adaptive mechanisms are overwhelmed, lipotoxicity from dysfunctional lipid handling injures and kills hepatocytes, producing steatohepatitis (MASH) with inflammation and ballooned hepatocytes. Repeated/protracted MASH triggers regenerative responses that become maladaptive, promoting progressive fibrosis (replacement of functional parenchyma by scar) and primary liver cancer; cirrhosis represents futile repair. Heritable factors (e.g., PNPLA3, TM6SF2, MBOAT7 variants), epigenetic influences, gut microbiota alterations, and environmental factors (high-fat/high-sugar diets) modify susceptibility (Harrison 22e p.2701-2702).","additionalDifferentials":["Alcohol-associated liver disease (ALD) and the intermediate MetALD category (metabolic dysfunction with social alcohol use, 20-50 g/d women, 30-60 g/d men)","Hepatitis C, particularly genotype 3","Autoimmune hepatitis","Wilson disease","Hemochromatosis / iron overload","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Inborn errors of metabolism (abetalipoproteinemia, cholesterol ester storage disease, galactosemia, glycogen storage disease, hereditary fructose intolerance, homocystinuria, systemic carnitine deficiency, tyrosinemia)","Weber-Christian syndrome"],"infectionMimics":["Chronic hepatitis C (genotype 3 causes hepatic steatosis)","Chronic hepatitis B"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Alcohol-associated liver disease (histologically can be indistinguishable; alcohol intake must be excluded)","Autoimmune hepatitis (note ~20% of steatotic liver disease patients have a positive ANA)","Medication/drug-associated steatosis or steatohepatitis","Wilson disease, hemochromatosis, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum aminotransferases (ALT/AST) - true normal ALT is ~29-33 U/L (men) and ~19-25 U/L (women); normal liver enzymes do NOT exclude MASLD","FIB-4 score (age, AST, ALT, platelet count) - first-line noninvasive fibrosis risk score","Vibration-controlled transient elastography / liver stiffness measurement (VCTE <8 kPa low, >12 kPa high; LSM >12 kPa associated with cirrhosis)","Enhanced Liver Fibrosis (ELF) blood test (ELF >=9.8 increased risk; >11.8 suspect cirrhosis)","Magnetic resonance elastography (MRE) and cT1 for refined risk stratification","Abdominal ultrasound / cross-sectional imaging for steatosis","Workup to exclude other causes: viral hepatitis serologies, autoimmune markers, iron/copper studies, alpha-1 antitrypsin","Screening for T2DM; assessment of metabolic syndrome components","Liver biopsy - historical gold standard for staging, reserved for indeterminate/discordant NITs, diagnostic uncertainty, or suspected concurrent chronic hepatitis (limited by sampling and observer variability)"],"redFlags":["FIB-4 >=2.67 (moderate/high risk for advanced fibrosis - needs secondary testing/specialist referral)","Fibrosis stage >=F2 = 'at-risk MASH' with increased risk of further progression and adverse liver outcomes","Persistently elevated AST and ALT","Stigmata of advanced disease: spider angiomata, palmar erythema, splenomegaly, thrombocytopenia, clubbing, ascites, encephalopathy","Features of decompensation/end-stage disease: jaundice, ascites, variceal hemorrhage","Clinical/radiologic suspicion of cirrhosis or ELF >11.8"],"prognosis":"Variable and stage-dependent. The 10-year probability of liver-related morbidity/mortality is negligible with steatosis alone, so this group is managed conservatively. Fibrosis severity is the primary predictor of outcome: average fibrosis progression is roughly one stage every 14 years in steatosis versus one stage every 7 years in MASH; once at stage >=F2 ('at-risk MASH') the risk of further progression and bad outcomes rises. Once MASLD-related cirrhosis develops, progression to decompensation runs 3-20% per year and annual primary liver cancer incidence can reach 1-2% per year, prompting biannual HCC surveillance and variceal screening. Cirrhosis can regress, and MASLD outcomes after liver transplantation are generally good in well-selected patients, though comorbid diabetes, obesity, and CVD limit candidacy and disease may recur post-transplant. MASLD is also a premalignant condition increasing risk of primary and extrahepatic malignancy even in noncirrhotic livers (Harrison 22e p.2701, 2704, 2708).","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude significant alcohol intake and other chronic liver diseases (viral, autoimmune, iron/copper overload, alpha-1 antitrypsin) before attributing steatosis to MASLD.","Assuming normal liver enzymes exclude MASLD - they do not; normal ALT 'reference ranges' are inflated by including people with undiagnosed liver disease.","Relying on liver tests for staging - aminotransferases, alkaline phosphatase, bilirubin, albumin, and PT may all be completely normal even with significant disease.","MASLD can occur in lean individuals (BMI <25, or <23 in Asians), especially with truncal/visceral adiposity, so a normal BMI does not exclude it.","FIB-4 has reduced accuracy in patients >65 and <35 years and is a less robust predictor in T2DM, so a secondary noninvasive test is needed in these groups.","Liver biopsy is limited by sampling error, inter/intraobserver variability (worse with cores <2 cm), and gives only a static snapshot of disease activity."],"severityClassification":"Histopathologic/fibrosis staging spectrum: simple steatosis (MASL) -> steatohepatitis (MASH) -> fibrosis -> cirrhosis. Fibrosis stages F0 (none), F1 (minimal), F2 (significant), F3 (advanced/bridging), F4 (cirrhosis, further stratified into compensated and decompensated). 'At-risk MASH' = MASH with fibrosis stage >=F2. Noninvasive risk stratification: FIB-4 <1.3 low risk, >=1.3 intermediate, >=2.67 moderate/high; secondary tests VCTE <8 kPa low / 8-12 intermediate / >12 high, ELF <7.7 low / 7.7-9.8 intermediate / >9.8 high (Harrison 22e p.2704-2705).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2700","Harrison 22e p.2701","Harrison 22e p.2702","Harrison 22e p.2703","Harrison 22e p.2704","Harrison 22e p.2705","Harrison 22e p.2706","Harrison 22e p.2707","Harrison 22e p.2708"],"crossLinks":["obesity","diabetes_mellitus","decomp_cirrhosis","alcohol_liver_disease"]},"narcolepsy":{"name":"Narcolepsy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["narcolepsy type 1","narcolepsy type 2","hypocretin deficiency syndrome","Gelineau syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Cardinal feature is excessive daytime sleepiness with irresistible sleep attacks, often accompanied by a classic tetrad that also includes cataplexy, sleep paralysis, and hypnagogic hallucinations","Cataplexy, sudden transient loss of muscle tone triggered by emotion such as laughter, is highly specific and defines narcolepsy type 1","The disorder reflects intrusion of REM-sleep phenomena into wakefulness, explaining cataplexy, dream-like hallucinations, and sleep paralysis","Narcolepsy type 1 is associated with low or undetectable cerebrospinal fluid hypocretin (orexin) and a strong link to the HLA-DQB1*06:02 allele","Diagnosis is supported by overnight polysomnography followed by a multiple sleep latency test showing short sleep latency and multiple sleep-onset REM periods","Management combines scheduled naps and sleep hygiene with wake-promoting agents for sleepiness and specific therapy for cataplexy","Onset is typically in adolescence or young adulthood and the condition is frequently under-recognised for years","Secondary narcolepsy can follow hypothalamic lesions, so consider imaging when there are atypical features or focal signs"],"pathophysiology":"Narcolepsy type 1 results from selective loss of hypocretin (orexin)-producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus, most likely through an autoimmune process in genetically susceptible individuals carrying HLA-DQB1*06:02. Hypocretin normally stabilises wakefulness and suppresses inappropriate REM sleep; its deficiency destabilises the sleep-wake switch, producing daytime sleepiness and the intrusion of REM features such as cataplexy into wakefulness. Type 2 has preserved hypocretin levels and a less well-defined mechanism.","additionalDifferentials":["Obstructive sleep apnoea and other causes of fragmented sleep","Insufficient sleep syndrome","Idiopathic hypersomnia","Circadian rhythm sleep disorders","Syncope or seizures mistaken for cataplexy","Depression and medication-related sedation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Overnight polysomnography followed by a multiple sleep latency test","HLA typing for DQB1*06:02 as a supportive marker","Cerebrospinal fluid hypocretin (orexin) measurement in selected cases","MRI of the brain when secondary narcolepsy from a hypothalamic lesion is suspected","Sleep diaries and actigraphy to exclude insufficient sleep and circadian disorders"],"redFlags":["New hypersomnolence with focal neurological signs or endocrine disturbance suggesting a hypothalamic mass","Severe sleep attacks endangering driving or operating machinery","Status cataplecticus with prolonged, near-continuous cataplexy","Abrupt withdrawal of anticataplectic therapy precipitating rebound cataplexy"],"prognosis":"Narcolepsy is a lifelong disorder, but symptoms can usually be controlled well enough with treatment and behavioural measures to allow good function. It carries important safety implications for driving and work, and untreated disease significantly impairs quality of life, mood, and daytime performance.","pitfalls":["Attributing chronic sleepiness to lifestyle or depression and delaying diagnosis for years","Missing cataplexy because patients do not volunteer it or it is mistaken for syncope or seizures","Failing to screen for and address driving and occupational safety risks","Overlooking a secondary hypothalamic cause in atypical or late-onset presentations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"nasal_fracture":{"name":"Nasal fracture","system":"Plastic / Reconstructive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["fractured nose","broken nose","nasal bone fracture"],"chapter":null,"source":"Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery / standard references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The nasal bones are the most commonly fractured facial bones, and the diagnosis is largely clinical, based on deformity, swelling, epistaxis, tenderness, and mobility or crepitus of the nasal bridge","Plain radiographs add little to management, so imaging is reserved for suspicion of associated injuries such as naso-orbito-ethmoid or midface fractures","The nose should be examined internally with a speculum to exclude a septal haematoma, which is the key finding not to miss","A septal haematoma requires prompt drainage because untreated it can cause septal cartilage necrosis, perforation, and a saddle-nose deformity","It is important to establish the pre-injury shape of the nose, often with a photograph, to judge post-traumatic deformity accurately","Reduction, when needed, is best performed either within the first few hours before swelling develops or after several days once swelling subsides but before the fracture sets, generally within about two weeks","Persistent deformity or nasal obstruction after healing may need later formal septorhinoplasty rather than early manipulation","Clear rhinorrhoea after nasal trauma should raise concern for a CSF leak from an associated skull base or cribriform injury"],"pathophysiology":"The prominent, relatively thin nasal bones and the cartilaginous septum are readily displaced by frontal or lateral trauma. Lateral forces tend to displace the nasal bones to one side, while frontal impact can flatten and splay the bones and buckle the septum. Bleeding into the subperichondrial space of the septum forms a septal haematoma, which strips the perichondrium from the cartilage and deprives it of its blood supply; the resulting ischaemia and any superimposed infection can destroy the cartilage, leading to septal perforation and loss of dorsal support (saddle deformity). Higher-energy injuries may extend into the naso-orbito-ethmoid region or the skull base.","additionalDifferentials":["Naso-orbito-ethmoid fracture","Septal haematoma or septal fracture without nasal bone fracture","Nasal soft tissue contusion without fracture","Associated midface (Le Fort) or orbital fracture","Pre-existing nasal deformity mistaken for acute injury","Frontal sinus or skull base fracture with CSF leak"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination for external deformity, mobility, and airflow","Internal nasal examination with a speculum to exclude septal haematoma","Pre-injury photographs to assess the original nasal shape","CT when naso-orbito-ethmoid, midface, or skull base injury is suspected","Assessment for CSF rhinorrhoea if clear nasal discharge follows the injury"],"redFlags":["Septal haematoma requiring urgent drainage to prevent cartilage necrosis","Clear rhinorrhoea suggesting a CSF leak from skull base or cribriform injury","Telecanthus, medial canthal disruption, or features of a naso-orbito-ethmoid fracture","Uncontrolled epistaxis or signs of a more extensive midface fracture"],"prognosis":"Most isolated nasal fractures heal well, and timely closed reduction gives good cosmetic and functional results in many patients. A proportion are left with residual deformity or nasal obstruction that may require later septorhinoplasty. The main preventable adverse outcome is the saddle-nose deformity and septal perforation that follow an undrained septal haematoma.","pitfalls":["Failing to examine the septum and missing a septal haematoma","Relying on plain radiographs, which rarely change management","Attempting reduction during peak swelling, giving an inaccurate result","Delaying reduction beyond the window in which the fracture can be manipulated","Overlooking an associated naso-orbito-ethmoid or skull base injury","Not documenting the pre-injury appearance, leading to unrealistic expectations"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"nasal_polyps":{"name":"Nasal polyps","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nasal polyposis","sinonasal polyps","ethmoidal polyps","antrochoanal polyp"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Benign oedematous outgrowths of inflamed sinonasal mucosa presenting with nasal obstruction, reduced smell and rhinorrhoea","Bilateral multiple ethmoidal polyps are typically associated with chronic rhinosinusitis, allergy and asthma","A single antrochoanal polyp usually arises from the maxillary sinus, is more common in younger patients, and extends toward the posterior nasal space","Polyps appear as pale, smooth, mobile, insensitive glistening masses on anterior rhinoscopy or endoscopy","The association of nasal polyps, asthma and aspirin sensitivity is a recognised clinical triad","Management principles combine intranasal and, where needed, short courses of systemic corticosteroids with treatment of underlying allergy and infection","Endoscopic sinus surgery is considered when medical therapy fails or obstruction is severe, though recurrence is common","Unilateral polyps, bleeding, or atypical appearance warrant biopsy and imaging to exclude malignancy or other lesions"],"pathophysiology":"Nasal polyps are pedunculated masses of oedematous, chronically inflamed sinonasal mucosa, most often stemming from persistent inflammation in the ethmoid region. Ongoing mucosal inflammation, frequently eosinophil-rich, causes fluid accumulation within the lamina propria, and gravity together with impaired lymphatic drainage encourages the mucosa to prolapse into the nasal cavity as polyps. Contributing factors include chronic rhinosinusitis, allergy, aspirin sensitivity and, in children, cystic fibrosis. The antrochoanal polyp arises from maxillary sinus mucosa, passes through its ostium and enlarges into the choana and nasopharynx.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypertrophied inferior turbinate mistaken for a polyp","Inverted papilloma","Sinonasal malignancy","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma (in adolescent males)","Encephalocoele or meningoencephalocoele in children","Chronic rhinosinusitis without polyps"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nasal endoscopy to characterise and localise the polyps","CT of the paranasal sinuses to define extent and plan surgery","Allergy testing where an allergic component is suspected","Histopathology of excised or atypical tissue to exclude neoplasm","Investigation for cystic fibrosis in children with polyps"],"redFlags":["Unilateral polyp with bleeding or an atypical firm appearance suggesting malignancy or angiofibroma","Orbital signs such as proptosis, diplopia or visual disturbance indicating spread","A polypoidal mass in a child that could be an encephalocoele, warranting caution before biopsy","Rapid growth, facial pain or cranial nerve involvement"],"prognosis":"Nasal polyps are benign but tend to be chronic and recurrent, particularly in association with asthma and aspirin sensitivity; symptoms are usually controllable with combined medical and surgical management, though repeated treatment is often needed.","pitfalls":["Assuming all nasal masses are benign polyps and missing a tumour or angiofibroma","Biopsying a suspected polyp in a child without first excluding an encephalocoele","Relying on surgery alone without ongoing medical control of underlying inflammation","Overlooking associated asthma and aspirin sensitivity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"nasopharyngeal_carcinoma":{"name":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NPC","nasopharyngeal cancer","carcinoma of the nasopharynx"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Nasopharyngeal carcinoma arises from the epithelium of the nasopharynx, most often in the fossa of Rosenmuller, and shows a strong association with Epstein-Barr virus and with certain geographic and dietary factors","A very common presentation is a painless neck lump from cervical nodal metastasis, which may be the first and only sign","Unilateral serous otitis media with hearing loss in an adult should raise strong suspicion, as the tumour blocks the Eustachian tube opening","Nasal symptoms include blockage and blood-stained nasal or postnasal discharge","Skull base and cavernous sinus invasion can cause cranial nerve palsies, producing diplopia, facial numbness or other neurological deficits","Because the tumour hides in the nasopharynx, diagnosis relies on endoscopic examination and biopsy, supported by imaging and Epstein-Barr virus serology or DNA levels","MRI best defines local and skull base extension while CT and PET assess bony involvement and distant spread","Treatment is primarily radiotherapy, often with concurrent chemotherapy, because the tumour is radiosensitive and surgery has a limited primary role"],"pathophysiology":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma develops in the epithelial lining of the nasopharynx, typically in the recess of the fossa of Rosenmuller near the Eustachian tube opening. Malignant transformation is closely linked to latent Epstein-Barr virus infection, together with genetic susceptibility and environmental exposures such as diets rich in preserved salted foods and nitrosamines. The tumour tends to spread submucosally and infiltrate early, obstructing the Eustachian tube to cause middle ear effusion, and eroding through the skull base foramina to involve cranial nerves and the cavernous sinus. Its rich lymphatic drainage leads to early and often bilateral cervical lymph node metastasis, and distant spread to bone, lung and liver can occur.","additionalDifferentials":["Reactive or tuberculous cervical lymphadenopathy","Lymphoma of the nasopharynx or neck","Adenoid hypertrophy (in younger patients)","Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma","Other nasopharyngeal tumours such as minor salivary gland tumours","Chronic adenoiditis or Thornwaldt cyst","Metastasis from another primary site"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nasopharyngeal endoscopy with biopsy of any suspicious lesion","MRI of the nasopharynx and skull base to assess local and perineural extension","CT to evaluate bony erosion and nodal disease","PET-CT for staging and detection of distant metastases","Epstein-Barr virus serology and plasma EBV DNA levels","Fine needle aspiration of a neck node when the primary is not yet identified","Audiometry and tympanometry to document middle ear effusion"],"redFlags":["Unilateral serous otitis media in an adult, which mandates nasopharyngeal examination to exclude this tumour","A persistent painless neck mass, especially in the upper deep cervical chain","Blood-stained nasal or postnasal discharge","Cranial nerve palsies, diplopia or facial numbness suggesting skull base invasion","Trismus, persistent headache or unexplained weight loss"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on stage at presentation; early disease treated with radiotherapy has a favourable prognosis, whereas advanced disease with skull base invasion or distant metastasis carries a poorer outlook, and the frequently late presentation makes vigilance for early signs important.","pitfalls":["Treating adult unilateral middle ear effusion as simple Eustachian tube dysfunction without examining the nasopharynx","Attributing a neck node to benign causes and delaying evaluation of the primary","Taking an inadequate or superficial biopsy of a submucosal tumour","Underestimating skull base and perineural spread by relying on CT alone rather than MRI","Overlooking cranial nerve findings as unrelated"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"necrotizing_enterocolitis":{"name":"Necrotizing Enterocolitis","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NEC","necrotising enterocolitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common acquired gastrointestinal emergency of the newborn, occurring mainly in preterm, low-birth-weight infants and usually within the first weeks after birth.","Presentation ranges from feeding intolerance, abdominal distension, and bloody stools to systemic signs such as lethargy, temperature instability, apnoea, and shock.","The pathognomonic radiographic sign is pneumatosis intestinalis (intramural gas); portal venous gas and free intraperitoneal air are more ominous findings.","Prematurity is the dominant risk factor; enteral feeding, particularly with formula, and intestinal dysbiosis are contributory, while human milk is protective.","Initial management is medical: stop enteral feeds, decompress the bowel with a nasogastric tube, give intravenous fluids and broad-spectrum antibiotics, and provide supportive care.","Serial abdominal radiographs and clinical review guide progression; free air on imaging indicates perforation and the need for surgery.","Surgical options include peritoneal drainage or laparotomy with resection of necrotic bowel, sometimes with a stoma.","The Bell staging system grades severity from suspected to advanced disease with perforation and is used to guide management.","Extensive resection can leave short bowel syndrome with long-term dependence on parenteral nutrition."],"pathophysiology":"Necrotizing enterocolitis reflects a multifactorial injury to the immature intestine. An excessive inflammatory response of the immature gut mucosa, altered microbial colonisation, ischaemia or impaired mucosal perfusion, and enteral substrate combine to cause mucosal injury, bacterial translocation, and gas production within the bowel wall. Progression leads to full-thickness necrosis, perforation, peritonitis, and systemic inflammatory response with sepsis and shock.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal sepsis with ileus","Spontaneous intestinal perforation","Malrotation with midgut volvulus","Hirschsprung enterocolitis","Infectious gastroenteritis","Milk protein intolerance / allergic colitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Supine and lateral decubitus or cross-table lateral abdominal radiographs for pneumatosis, portal gas, or free air","Full blood count (looking for neutropenia, thrombocytopenia) and inflammatory markers","Blood culture and blood gas with lactate","Serum electrolytes and glucose, coagulation studies","Abdominal ultrasound to assess bowel wall perfusion and free fluid"],"redFlags":["Free intraperitoneal air indicating perforation","Portal venous gas","Persistent metabolic acidosis or rising lactate","Thrombocytopenia, neutropenia, or evidence of disseminated intravascular coagulation","Abdominal wall erythema, fixed mass, or clinical deterioration despite medical therapy"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on gestational age and severity; mild disease often resolves with medical management, but advanced disease requiring surgery carries substantial mortality and long-term morbidity including strictures, short bowel syndrome, and adverse neurodevelopmental outcomes.","pitfalls":["Attributing early nonspecific signs to feeding intolerance and delaying evaluation","Advancing enteral feeds too rapidly in vulnerable preterm infants","Missing a subtle pneumoperitoneum on a single supine film","Underestimating rapid clinical deterioration and delaying surgical consultation","Overlooking strictures that can develop weeks after apparent recovery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_abstinence_syndrome":{"name":"Neonatal Abstinence Syndrome","system":"Neonatal / Neurological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NAS","neonatal opioid withdrawal syndrome","NOWS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A constellation of withdrawal signs in a newborn following intrauterine exposure to substances, most commonly opioids, that the infant is no longer receiving after birth.","Signs typically involve central nervous system irritability, autonomic disturbance, and gastrointestinal dysfunction.","Common features include tremors, high-pitched crying, poor sleep, irritability, hypertonia, sweating, sneezing, nasal stuffiness, feeding difficulty, vomiting, and loose stools.","The timing of onset depends on the substance and its half-life; short-acting opioids produce earlier withdrawal, while longer-acting agents may delay onset for days.","Standardised scoring tools are used to assess severity and guide whether pharmacological treatment is needed.","First-line care emphasises supportive, non-pharmacological measures such as a low-stimulation environment, swaddling, gentle handling, and rooming-in with the mother.","The Eat, Sleep, Console approach focuses on the infant's function rather than symptom counts alone.","When supportive care is insufficient, opioid-based pharmacological treatment is used with gradual weaning; polysubstance exposure and breastfeeding considerations should also be addressed."],"pathophysiology":"During pregnancy, drugs that cross the placenta reach the fetus and lead to physiological dependence, particularly with opioids acting on central opioid receptors. After delivery the sudden loss of transplacental drug supply removes this stimulation, producing a hyperexcitable state affecting the central and autonomic nervous systems and the gastrointestinal tract. The resulting neurotransmitter imbalance manifests as the characteristic irritability, autonomic instability, and feeding disturbance; the pattern and timing vary with the specific agent, dose, and its pharmacokinetics.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal sepsis or infection","Hypoglycaemia","Hypocalcaemia or other electrolyte disturbance","Hyperthyroidism (neonatal thyrotoxicosis)","Hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy","Central nervous system haemorrhage or injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial standardised withdrawal scoring (e.g. modified Finnegan) or function-based assessment","Maternal history of substance use and prescribed medications","Toxicology testing of infant urine, meconium, or other matrices where appropriate","Blood glucose monitoring","Electrolytes and calcium if clinically indicated","Sepsis screen when infection cannot be excluded"],"redFlags":["Seizures attributable to withdrawal","Severe feeding intolerance with dehydration or poor weight gain","Excessive weight loss or failure to thrive","Marked autonomic instability or persistent high scores despite supportive care","Signs suggesting an alternative serious diagnosis such as sepsis or hypoglycaemia","Difficulty consoling with escalating pharmacological requirements"],"prognosis":"Acute withdrawal is usually self-limited and manageable, and most infants recover well with appropriate supportive and, when needed, pharmacological care; longer-term neurodevelopmental outcomes are influenced more by the broader social and environmental context, so coordinated family support and follow-up are important.","pitfalls":["Relying on medication before optimising non-pharmacological, function-focused care","Excessive handling and stimulation that worsen the infant's symptoms","Overlooking polysubstance exposure, which alters the withdrawal course","Missing concurrent problems such as sepsis or hypoglycaemia","Inconsistent scoring between assessors leading to inappropriate treatment decisions","Neglecting maternal support, breastfeeding counselling, and social care coordination"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_hypoglycemia":{"name":"Neonatal Hypoglycaemia","system":"Endocrine/Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["low blood sugar in newborn","hypoglycaemia of the newborn","neonatal low glucose"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Low blood glucose is common in the transitional period after birth as the newborn adapts from continuous placental glucose supply to intermittent feeding.","Infants at highest risk include those of diabetic mothers, large- or small-for-gestational-age babies, preterm infants, and any newborn who is stressed, cold, or septic.","Signs are often subtle and non-specific: jitteriness, poor feeding, lethargy, hypotonia, temperature instability, apnoea, or seizures; many affected infants appear asymptomatic.","At-risk and symptomatic infants should be screened with bedside glucose testing, with low values confirmed on a laboratory sample given the imprecision of point-of-care meters.","Early and frequent feeding is the first-line measure; infants who cannot maintain adequate glucose or who are symptomatic require intravenous dextrose.","Symptomatic hypoglycaemia with a documented low glucose that resolves when glucose is normalised (Whipple triad) warrants investigation and treatment.","Persistent or recurrent hypoglycaemia beyond the first days of life suggests an underlying disorder such as hyperinsulinism, endocrine deficiency, or an inborn error of metabolism.","The clinical concern is that prolonged or severe low glucose can injure the developing brain, so prevention and prompt correction are priorities."],"pathophysiology":"After the cord is cut, the newborn loses its steady maternal glucose supply and must mobilise hepatic glycogen, activate gluconeogenesis, and use alternative fuels such as ketones and lactate, processes driven by a surge in counter-regulatory hormones and a fall in insulin. When glycogen stores are limited (prematurity, growth restriction), glucose demand is high (stress, sepsis, hypothermia), or insulin secretion is excessive (infant of a diabetic mother, hyperinsulinism), this adaptation fails and blood glucose falls. Because the neonatal brain depends heavily on glucose, inadequate supply combined with immature ketogenesis in some infants places the brain at risk.","additionalDifferentials":["Sepsis","Neonatal seizures from other causes","Hypocalcaemia or other electrolyte disturbance","Congenital hyperinsulinism","Panhypopituitarism or cortisol deficiency","Inborn errors of metabolism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Confirmatory laboratory plasma glucose","Critical sample during hypoglycaemia (insulin, cortisol, growth hormone, ketones, free fatty acids) when persistent","Serum electrolytes and calcium","Sepsis screen if clinically indicated","Metabolic screen (ammonia, lactate, acylcarnitine profile) in recurrent or refractory cases"],"redFlags":["Seizures, apnoea, or coma","Persistent hypoglycaemia despite feeding and initial glucose infusion","High glucose infusion requirement suggesting hyperinsulinism","Recurrent hypoglycaemia beyond the transitional period","Associated hypotonia, poor feeding, or encephalopathy"],"prognosis":"Brief, promptly treated transitional hypoglycaemia in an otherwise well infant usually has an excellent outcome, whereas severe, prolonged, symptomatic, or recurrent hypoglycaemia carries a risk of long-term neurodevelopmental impairment.","pitfalls":["Treating an isolated low bedside meter reading without confirming on a laboratory sample or clinical context","Assuming all at-risk infants are symptomatic, or dismissing subtle signs as normal newborn behaviour","Failing to investigate hypoglycaemia that persists or recurs beyond the first days of life","Overlooking hyperinsulinism when a high rate of glucose infusion is needed to keep glucose normal"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_jaundice":{"name":"Neonatal jaundice","system":"Neonatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neonatal hyperbilirubinaemia","jaundice of the newborn","physiological jaundice","icterus neonatorum"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Visible yellow discolouration of skin and sclerae from raised serum bilirubin, extremely common in newborns and usually due to unconjugated (indirect) hyperbilirubinaemia.","Physiological jaundice appears after the first 24 hours, peaks around days 3-5 in term infants, and resolves without harm; it reflects normal neonatal bilirubin handling.","Jaundice in the first 24 hours of life is always pathological and demands urgent evaluation, commonly for haemolysis.","Key pathological causes include blood-group incompatibility (Rh, ABO), G6PD deficiency, sepsis, and other haemolytic conditions.","Conjugated (direct) hyperbilirubinaemia is never physiological and requires investigation for cholestasis, notably biliary atresia, which needs early surgery.","Breastfeeding-associated (early, from inadequate intake) and breast-milk (later, prolonged) jaundice are common benign patterns once other causes are excluded.","Management depends on bilirubin level plotted against age in hours and risk factors; phototherapy is first-line and exchange transfusion is reserved for dangerously high or rapidly rising levels.","The feared complication is bilirubin-induced neurological dysfunction (acute encephalopathy progressing to kernicterus), which is preventable with timely treatment."],"pathophysiology":"Newborns have increased bilirubin production from a high red cell mass with shorter red cell lifespan, combined with immature hepatic conjugation (low UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity) and enhanced enterohepatic recirculation of bilirubin. These factors produce transient physiological unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia. Pathological jaundice arises when production is excessive (haemolysis, extravasated blood), conjugation or uptake is impaired, or excretion is obstructed (cholestasis). Unbound unconjugated bilirubin is lipid-soluble and can cross the blood-brain barrier, depositing in the basal ganglia and brainstem nuclei to cause neurotoxicity.","additionalDifferentials":["Rh or ABO haemolytic disease","G6PD deficiency and other red cell enzyme/membrane defects","Sepsis or congenital infection","Biliary atresia and other causes of neonatal cholestasis","Hypothyroidism","Breastfeeding and breast-milk jaundice","Cephalohaematoma or extensive bruising"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Total and conjugated (direct) serum bilirubin, interpreted against age in hours","Transcutaneous bilirubinometry for screening","Blood group and direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test on infant and mother","Full blood count, reticulocyte count, and blood film","G6PD assay where clinically indicated","Thyroid function and septic screen when suggested by the picture","Assessment for cholestasis (e.g. pale stools, dark urine) when direct bilirubin is raised"],"redFlags":["Jaundice within the first 24 hours of life","Rapidly rising bilirubin or a level near the exchange-transfusion threshold","Signs of acute bilirubin encephalopathy: lethargy, poor feeding, hypotonia progressing to hypertonia, high-pitched cry, arching","Conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia, pale stools, and dark urine suggesting biliary atresia","Prolonged jaundice beyond two weeks (term) or three weeks (preterm) requiring evaluation"],"prognosis":"Most neonatal jaundice is benign and resolves without sequelae; with appropriate monitoring and treatment, kernicterus is largely preventable, but untreated severe hyperbilirubinaemia can cause permanent neurological injury.","pitfalls":["Dismissing jaundice in the first 24 hours as physiological","Failing to fractionate bilirubin and missing cholestasis / biliary atresia","Assessing jaundice by clinical appearance alone rather than measured bilirubin against age-specific thresholds","Overlooking dehydration from inadequate feeding as a contributor in early breastfeeding jaundice","Not investigating jaundice that persists beyond the expected period"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_rds":{"name":"Neonatal Respiratory Distress Syndrome","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hyaline membrane disease","RDS","surfactant deficiency disease","respiratory distress syndrome of the newborn"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A disease of surfactant deficiency affecting mainly preterm infants; incidence rises steeply as gestational age falls, being greatest below about 28 weeks.","Distress typically begins at or soon after birth with tachypnoea, grunting, nasal flaring, intercostal and subcostal recession, and cyanosis that worsen over the first hours to days.","Chest radiograph classically shows a diffuse fine reticulogranular (ground-glass) pattern with air bronchograms and low lung volumes.","Antenatal maternal corticosteroids given before anticipated preterm delivery accelerate lung maturation and markedly reduce incidence and severity.","Exogenous surfactant replacement, delivered into the airway, is a cornerstone of treatment and improves oxygenation and survival.","Early nasal continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) helps maintain functional residual capacity and can reduce the need for intubation and mechanical ventilation.","Maternal diabetes, male sex, second-born twins, caesarean delivery without labour, and perinatal asphyxia increase risk, whereas chronic intrauterine stress and prolonged rupture of membranes may reduce it.","Untreated disease tends to worsen for about 2-3 days before recovery begins, often heralded by a spontaneous diuresis."],"pathophysiology":"Immature type II pneumocytes produce insufficient pulmonary surfactant, so alveolar surface tension is high. This causes progressive diffuse atelectasis, reduced lung compliance, and ventilation-perfusion mismatch. Hypoxaemia and hypercapnia with acidosis follow, and pulmonary vascular resistance may remain elevated with right-to-left shunting. Epithelial injury and protein-rich exudate form the hyaline membranes that give the disease its historical name, further impairing gas exchange.","additionalDifferentials":["Transient tachypnoea of the newborn","Neonatal pneumonia or early-onset sepsis (especially group B streptococcus)","Meconium aspiration syndrome","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn","Congenital heart disease","Air-leak syndromes (pneumothorax)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph to assess the reticulogranular pattern and lung volumes","Arterial or capillary blood gas to quantify hypoxaemia, hypercapnia, and acidosis","Pulse oximetry and continuous cardiorespiratory monitoring","Blood culture and inflammatory markers when sepsis cannot be excluded","Blood glucose and electrolyte monitoring","Echocardiography if congenital heart disease or pulmonary hypertension is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly escalating oxygen or ventilatory requirement","Sudden deterioration suggesting pneumothorax or other air leak","Persistent hypoxaemia despite surfactant and respiratory support (possible pulmonary hypertension)","Apnoea, bradycardia, or signs of impending respiratory failure","Hypotension and poor perfusion suggesting shock or sepsis"],"prognosis":"With antenatal steroids, surfactant, and modern respiratory support, survival is high, particularly in more mature preterm infants; outcome worsens with decreasing gestational age and with complications such as air leak, intraventricular haemorrhage, and progression to bronchopulmonary dysplasia.","pitfalls":["Assuming RDS in a term or near-term infant without considering sepsis, aspiration, or cardiac causes","Over-oxygenation, which increases the risk of retinopathy of prematurity and oxidative lung injury","Delaying surfactant or CPAP in an infant who is deteriorating","Failing to recognise a tension pneumothorax as the cause of abrupt worsening","Not administering antenatal corticosteroids when preterm birth is anticipated"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_seizures":{"name":"Neonatal Seizures","system":"Neurological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["seizures in the newborn","neonatal convulsions","newborn fits"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Seizures in the newborn period are usually a symptom of an acute underlying brain insult rather than a chronic epilepsy, so the priority is to find and treat the cause.","Presentations are often subtle: repetitive eye deviation or blinking, lip-smacking or chewing, cycling or boxing limb movements, and apnoea, in addition to clonic or tonic activity.","Hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy is the most common cause; other important causes include stroke, intracranial haemorrhage, infection, metabolic disturbances, and inborn errors of metabolism.","Because clinical signs are unreliable and electroclinical dissociation is common, EEG or amplitude-integrated EEG monitoring is central to accurate diagnosis.","Immediate workup should exclude readily treatable causes such as hypoglycaemia and electrolyte abnormalities, correcting these before escalating anticonvulsant therapy.","Non-epileptic movements like jitteriness can mimic seizures but are stimulus-sensitive, suppressible by gentle restraint, and lack autonomic or ocular features.","Neuroimaging and, where appropriate, lumbar puncture and metabolic or genetic testing help establish aetiology and guide prognosis.","Pyridoxine-dependent and other vitamin-responsive epilepsies should be considered when seizures are refractory to standard agents."],"pathophysiology":"The immature neonatal brain is intrinsically more excitable because excitatory glutamatergic circuits mature earlier than inhibitory GABAergic systems, and GABA can be depolarising early in development. An acute insult, most often hypoxia-ischaemia, disrupts energy metabolism and ion gradients, leading to neuronal depolarisation and synchronised, excessive electrical discharges. Metabolic derangements, structural lesions, infection, or genetic and metabolic disorders lower the seizure threshold further. Frequent dissociation between electrical seizures and visible clinical signs reflects this immaturity and underlies the need for electrographic monitoring.","additionalDifferentials":["Jitteriness or benign neonatal sleep myoclonus","Apnoea of other cause","Hypoglycaemia or electrolyte disturbance","Meningitis or encephalitis","Intracranial haemorrhage or perinatal stroke","Inborn errors of metabolism and vitamin-responsive epilepsies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["EEG or amplitude-integrated EEG monitoring","Blood glucose, electrolytes, calcium, and magnesium","Cranial imaging (ultrasound and MRI)","Lumbar puncture and sepsis evaluation when infection is possible","Metabolic and genetic testing in unexplained or refractory cases"],"redFlags":["Status epilepticus or very frequent recurrent seizures","Seizures refractory to first-line therapy","Encephalopathy with depressed consciousness and abnormal tone","Signs of meningitis or sepsis","Focal neurological deficits suggesting stroke or a structural lesion"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends largely on the underlying cause and the severity of any brain injury; seizures due to transient metabolic disturbances often resolve well, whereas those from severe hypoxic-ischaemic injury, major structural lesions, or certain genetic-metabolic disorders carry a higher risk of epilepsy and neurodevelopmental impairment.","pitfalls":["Relying on clinical observation alone and missing electrographic-only seizures","Mistaking jitteriness or benign myoclonus for seizures and over-treating","Escalating anticonvulsants before correcting hypoglycaemia or electrolyte abnormalities","Failing to consider vitamin-responsive epilepsies in seizures resistant to standard drugs"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"neonatal_sepsis":{"name":"Neonatal sepsis","system":"Neonatology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sepsis neonatorum","newborn sepsis","early-onset neonatal sepsis","late-onset neonatal sepsis","EOS","LOS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A systemic bacterial (occasionally fungal or viral) infection in the first 28 days of life, conventionally split into early-onset (within roughly the first 72 hours, often maternally acquired) and late-onset (after 72 hours, frequently nosocomial or community-acquired).","Signs are notoriously nonspecific: temperature instability (fever or hypothermia), poor feeding, lethargy or irritability, respiratory distress, apnoea, tachycardia, and abdominal distension.","Group B Streptococcus and Escherichia coli dominate early-onset disease; coagulase-negative staphylococci, Staphylococcus aureus, and Gram-negative organisms are common in late-onset disease, especially in preterm infants with indwelling lines.","Maternal risk factors include prolonged rupture of membranes, chorioamnionitis, maternal fever, and GBS colonisation without adequate intrapartum prophylaxis.","Blood culture is the diagnostic gold standard; a lumbar puncture should be considered because meningitis frequently coexists and may be missed.","Empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics must be started promptly after cultures because deterioration can be rapid; the regimen is later narrowed to culture results.","Supportive care (thermoregulation, respiratory and haemodynamic support, glucose management) is as important as antimicrobials.","A low threshold for treatment is appropriate given nonspecific signs, but antibiotics are stopped early when cultures remain negative and the infant is well."],"pathophysiology":"Neonates are relatively immunocompromised, with immature innate and adaptive defences, low immunoglobulin levels, and impaired neutrophil function. Organisms reach the infant by ascending or intrapartum transmission from the maternal genital tract in early-onset disease, or from the postnatal environment and invasive devices in late-onset disease. Once bloodstream invasion occurs, bacterial products trigger a systemic inflammatory response with cytokine release, endothelial injury, capillary leak, and impaired perfusion that can progress to septic shock, disseminated intravascular coagulation, and multi-organ dysfunction. The immature blood-brain barrier and limited containment of infection predispose to meningitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Transient tachypnoea of the newborn","Respiratory distress syndrome","Congenital heart disease with poor perfusion","Inborn errors of metabolism","Neonatal encephalopathy / birth asphyxia","Congenital viral infection (e.g. herpes simplex, cytomegalovirus)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood culture before starting antibiotics","Complete blood count with differential and immature-to-total neutrophil ratio","C-reactive protein and/or procalcitonin, ideally serially","Lumbar puncture with CSF cell count, protein, glucose and culture","Urine culture (more relevant in late-onset disease)","Chest radiograph if respiratory signs are present","Blood glucose, electrolytes and blood gas to assess metabolic status"],"redFlags":["Septic shock: poor perfusion, hypotension, prolonged capillary refill","Apnoea or worsening respiratory failure requiring support","Bulging fontanelle, seizures, or other signs suggesting meningitis","Petechiae, purpura, or bleeding suggesting disseminated intravascular coagulation","Persistent hypoglycaemia or profound metabolic acidosis"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on gestational age, organism, and speed of treatment; prompt therapy usually leads to recovery, but meningitis, shock, and prematurity carry higher mortality and risk of neurodevelopmental sequelae.","pitfalls":["Attributing nonspecific signs to prematurity or feeding issues and delaying evaluation","Omitting a lumbar puncture and missing coexisting meningitis","Relying on a single normal inflammatory marker to exclude sepsis early in the illness","Failing to remove or consider infected central venous catheters in late-onset disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"neoplasms_of_the_lung":{"name":"Neoplasms of the Lung","system":"Respiratory / Thoracic Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Lung cancer","Bronchogenic carcinoma","Non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC)","Small-cell lung cancer (SCLC)","Pulmonary carcinoma"],"chapter":83,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.614","clinicalPearls":["Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer death worldwide and in the United States; over half of patients present with locally advanced or metastatic disease at diagnosis.","Tobacco smoking accounts for roughly two-thirds of lung cancer deaths globally, but about 65% of new U.S. diagnoses are now in former smokers or never-smokers; one in five women and one in twelve men diagnosed have never smoked.","The WHO divides epithelial lung cancers into four major types: small-cell carcinoma (SCLC) and three non-small-cell types (adenocarcinoma, squamous cell, large-cell). Together these account for about 90% of epithelial lung cancers; adenocarcinoma is now the most common subtype.","Separating SCLC from NSCLC and, within NSCLC, squamous from nonsquamous histology is essential because natural history and management differ markedly; a generic diagnosis of 'non-small-cell carcinoma, not otherwise specified' is no longer acceptable.","A chronic cough with or without hemoptysis in a current or former smoker with COPD aged 40 or older warrants thorough evaluation for lung cancer even with a normal chest x-ray; a persistent pneumonia unresponsive to repeated antibiotics also requires workup.","Tissue sampling is required to confirm diagnosis in all suspected cases; a core biopsy is preferred over fine-needle aspiration to provide adequate material for molecular and PD-L1 testing.","Central, endobronchial lesions (squamous cell, small-cell, carcinoid) are more readily reached by bronchoscopy, whereas peripheral lesions (adenocarcinoma, large-cell) are more amenable to transthoracic biopsy.","Driver oncogenes (EGFR, ALK, ROS1, RET, NTRK, BRAF, MET, HER2, KRAS) are enriched in nonsquamous NSCLC and define distinct molecular cohorts; nearly 90% of SCLCs carry TP53 and RB1 mutations.","Paraneoplastic syndromes are common, especially with SCLC, and may be the presenting feature or first sign of recurrence; they can mimic metastatic disease and lead to inappropriately palliative rather than curative management.","Hypercalcemia from ectopic PTH-related peptide is the most common life-threatening metabolic complication of malignancy and occurs primarily with squamous cell carcinoma.","On FDG PET, a standardized uptake value (SUV) above 2.5 is highly suspicious for malignancy, but PET must never be used alone to diagnose cancer, nodal involvement, or metastasis because of false positives (infection, granulomatous disease such as tuberculosis) and false negatives (lesions under 8 mm, slow-growing tumors, diabetes).","Adenocarcinoma in situ and minimally invasive adenocarcinoma (solitary lesions 3 cm or less with pure or predominant lepidic growth and 5 mm or less invasion) achieve near 100% 5-year disease-free survival after complete resection."],"pathophysiology":"Inhaled environmental carcinogens, principally those in tobacco smoke, drive transformation of bronchoepithelial cells to a malignant phenotype. Susceptibility is modulated by polymorphisms in carcinogen-metabolizing genes (e.g., CYP1A1) and by genetic loci affecting nicotinic acetylcholine receptors and telomerase (5p15, 6p21, 15q25). Tumorigenesis reflects accumulation of gain-of-function mutations in oncogenes and loss-of-function mutations in tumor-suppressor genes, with the sequence differing by histologic type. Adenocarcinomas frequently harbor targetable driver alterations: activating receptor tyrosine kinase mutations (EGFR, HER2/ERBB2, MET), intracellular signaling mutations (KRAS, BRAF), and chromosomal rearrangements activating ALK, ROS1, RET, and NTRK. Squamous tumors may show FGFR1 amplification, DDR2 mutations, PIK3CA mutations/PTEN loss, and BRAF/MET alterations. Inactivated tumor suppressors include TP53, RB1, RASSF1A, CDKN2A/B, LKB1 (STK11), and FHIT; allelic loss on chromosome 3p occurs very early, even in histologically normal smoke-damaged epithelium. Clinical manifestations arise from central/endobronchial growth (cough, hemoptysis, wheeze, postobstructive pneumonia), peripheral growth (pleural/chest-wall pain, dyspnea, cavitation), regional intrathoracic spread (recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy with hoarseness, phrenic nerve palsy, Horner syndrome, Pancoast syndrome, superior vena cava syndrome, pericardial/lymphatic involvement), distant metastasis, or paraneoplastic mechanisms.","additionalDifferentials":["Persistent or recurrent pneumonia unresponsive to antibiotics","Metastatic carcinoma to the lung from an extrapulmonary primary","Malignant pleural mesothelioma (CK5/6, calretinin, WT-1 positive, distinguishing it from adenocarcinoma)","Pulmonary carcinoid tumor (neuroendocrine, often central/endobronchial)","Benign solitary pulmonary nodule (granuloma, hamartoma)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis and other granulomatous disease (false-positive FDG-PET uptake; can mimic malignant nodules)","Postobstructive pneumonia from an underlying endobronchial tumor","Lung abscess from tumor cavitation","Parapneumonic pleural effusion mimicking malignant effusion"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sarcoidosis and other granulomatous disorders","Benign pulmonary nodules (hamartoma, hamartochondroma)","Pulmonary metastases from non-lung primaries","Mesothelioma","Paraneoplastic neurologic syndromes mimicking primary neurologic disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest CT and FDG PET, preferably integrated PET-CT, for anatomic staging and detection of mediastinal nodal and extrathoracic metastases","Tissue biopsy: fiberoptic bronchoscopy with bronchial/transbronchial biopsy, image-guided percutaneous core biopsy, or EBUS-guided biopsy; core biopsy preferred for adequate molecular material","Mediastinal nodal assessment by EBUS- or EUS-guided biopsy, mediastinoscopy, mediastinotomy, thoracoscopy, or thoracotomy","Next-generation sequencing (multigene panel) for driver oncogenes and PD-L1 immunohistochemistry reported as tumor proportion score (TPS)","Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) 'liquid biopsy' when tissue is limited or inaccessible","MRI brain for suspected brain metastases (most effective method) and for superior sulcus tumors to assess brachial plexus involvement","Immunohistochemistry: TTF-1 and napsin-A for adenocarcinoma (TTF-1 positive in over 70%, napsin-A in over 90% of primary lung adenocarcinomas), p40/p63 for squamous; neuroendocrine markers (CD56/NCAM, synaptophysin, chromogranin, INSM1) for SCLC","Thoracentesis with cell block for suspected malignant pleural effusion (repeat if first sample negative)","Spinal CT/MRI for suspected cord compression; brain/spine MRI plus lumbar puncture for suspected leptomeningeal disease","Physiologic evaluation: spirometry (FEV1), DLCO, and cardiopulmonary exercise testing (VO2max) before surgical resection"],"redFlags":["Hemoptysis in a smoker over 40 with chronic cough","Hoarseness (recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement) or superior vena cava syndrome","Pancoast/superior sulcus presentation: shoulder pain radiating in ulnar distribution with destruction of first/second ribs, often with Horner syndrome","Signs of cord compression: pain, weakness, paralysis, urinary retention","Weight loss exceeding 10% of body weight (poor prognostic sign)","Hypercalcemia with nausea, vomiting, constipation, polyuria, altered mental status","Symptomatic brain metastases (headache, nausea/vomiting, seizures, focal deficits) or pathologic fractures/bone pain"],"prognosis":"Outcome is strongly stage-dependent. For NSCLC by the eighth-edition TNM system, 5-year overall survival ranges from roughly 90% for the earliest (stage IA1) disease down to about 10% for stage IVB; SEER data report a 5-year survival of about 63% for localized NSCLC. Lepidic-predominant adenocarcinoma has a favorable prognosis, acinar and papillary intermediate, and solid-predominant and micropapillary patterns poor. SCLC is more aggressive: 60-70% present with extensive-stage disease, and even in limited-stage SCLC median survival is roughly 12-20 months historically. Weight loss over 10% of body weight and thrombotic complications of malignancy are poor prognostic signs. Extrathoracic metastases are found at autopsy in over 50% of squamous, about 80% of adenocarcinoma/large-cell, and over 95% of SCLC cases.","pitfalls":["A normal chest x-ray does not exclude lung cancer in a high-risk symptomatic smoker.","Negative TTF-1 does not exclude a pulmonary primary; TTF-1 is also positive in neuroendocrine and thyroid tumors.","PET-CT cannot establish a tissue diagnosis: false positives occur with tuberculosis/granulomatous and infectious disease and false negatives with sub-8 mm, slow-growing, or carcinoid/well-differentiated tumors.","Paraneoplastic syndromes may be mistaken for metastatic disease, risking inappropriate palliative rather than curative treatment.","A single negative thoracentesis does not exclude a malignant effusion; repeat sampling is essential.","Diagnosing on small biopsy/cytology specimens may blur squamous-versus-nonsquamous distinction; repeat biopsy is advised in advanced disease for adequate molecular and PD-L1 testing.","Low-dose CT screening carries a high false-positive rate (false-positive rate about 96% among positive screens in NLST), with attendant risks of overdiagnosis, unnecessary procedures, and radiation exposure."],"severityClassification":"NSCLC is staged with the eighth-edition (2018) TNM system: T1 (3 cm or less; subdivided T1a 1 cm or less, T1b over 1 to 2 cm, T1c over 2 to 3 cm), T2 (over 3 to 5 cm or visceral pleura/main bronchus involvement/atelectasis; T2a over 3 to 4 cm, T2b over 4 to 5 cm), T3 (over 5 to 7 cm or invasion of chest wall/pericardium/phrenic nerve or satellite nodule in same lobe), T4 (over 7 cm or invasion of mediastinum, heart, great vessels, trachea, recurrent laryngeal nerve, esophagus, vertebra, carina, or separate nodule in a different ipsilateral lobe). Nodes: N0 none, N1 ipsilateral peribronchial/hilar/intrapulmonary, N2 ipsilateral mediastinal/subcarinal, N3 contralateral mediastinal/hilar or scalene/supraclavicular. Metastasis: M1a contralateral nodule or pleural/pericardial nodule/malignant effusion, M1b single extrathoracic metastasis, M1c multiple extrathoracic metastases. SCLC is commonly classified by the two-stage Veterans Administration system: limited-stage (confined to ipsilateral hemithorax within a tolerable radiation port) versus extensive-stage (overt metastatic disease). Physiologic operability thresholds: FEV1 over 2 L or over 80% predicted suggests tolerance of pneumonectomy; FEV1 over 1.5 L suggests adequate reserve for lobectomy; VO2max under 15 mL/kg/min predicts higher postoperative risk; contraindications to thoracic surgery include FEV1 under 1 L, resting PCO2 over 45 mmHg, DLCO under 40%, severe pulmonary hypertension, and recent myocardial infarction.","references":["Harrison 22e p.614","Harrison 22e p.615","Harrison 22e p.616","Harrison 22e p.617","Harrison 22e p.618","Harrison 22e p.619","Harrison 22e p.620","Harrison 22e p.621","Harrison 22e p.622","Harrison 22e p.623"],"crossLinks":[]},"nephrolithiasis":{"name":"Nephrolithiasis","system":"Renal/Genitourinary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Kidney stone disease","Renal calculi","Urolithiasis","Renal stones"],"chapter":330,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2448","clinicalPearls":["Calcium oxalate stones are by far the most common type (~75%), followed by calcium phosphate (~15%), uric acid (~8%), struvite (~1%), and cystine (<1%); many stones are mixtures and contain protein matrix (Harrison 22e p.2448).","Identifying the stone type is clinically important because it informs prognosis and the choice of preventive strategy; patients should be encouraged to retrieve and submit passed stones for composition analysis (Harrison 22e p.2451-2452).","The two classic acute presentations are renal colic and painless gross hematuria; symptom onset reflects a stone moving into the ureter and gives no clue as to when the stone actually formed, since stone growth is typically clinically silent over weeks to years (Harrison 22e p.2450).","Pain radiation depends on stone location: an upper ureteral stone may radiate anteriorly, while a lower ureteral stone may radiate to the ipsilateral testicle (men) or labium (women); a ureterovesical-junction stone can cause urinary urgency and frequency (Harrison 22e p.2450).","Absence of hematuria does not exclude a stone, particularly when urine flow is completely obstructed (Harrison 22e p.2450).","Noncontrast helical CT is the gold-standard imaging test: it detects stones as small as 1 mm and visualizes uric acid stones that are traditionally considered radiolucent on plain films (Harrison 22e p.2450).","Urine pH governs solubility of several stone types: uric acid stones form at pH consistently <=5.5, calcium phosphate stones are favored at pH >=6.5, and cystine is more soluble at higher pH; calcium oxalate stone risk is not influenced by urine pH (Harrison 22e p.2450).","Urine citrate is the most clinically important natural inhibitor of calcium-containing stones; metabolic acidosis (including from high animal-flesh intake) lowers citrate excretion by enhancing its tubular reabsorption (Harrison 22e p.2448, 2449).","Dietary calcium restriction is counterproductive: higher dietary calcium intake lowers stone risk by binding intestinal oxalate and reducing urinary oxalate, whereas low calcium intake increases stone risk and may lower bone density (Harrison 22e p.2449).","Two 24-h urine collections on the patient's usual diet are the cornerstone of preventive evaluation, measuring volume, calcium, oxalate, citrate, uric acid, sodium, potassium, phosphorus, magnesium, pH, and creatinine; two collections are needed because of substantial day-to-day variability (Harrison 22e p.2451-2452).","Urinary risk factors (calcium, oxalate, citrate) are continuous variables with no valid normal/abnormal cutoff; dichotomizing them is misleading and arbitrary thresholds should be avoided (Harrison 22e p.2449, 2452).","Pure uric acid stones can be recognized by their low Hounsfield units on CT, helping distinguish stone composition non-invasively (Harrison 22e p.2452)."],"pathophysiology":"Stones form when urine becomes supersaturated with lithogenic solutes, i.e., when the concentration product exceeds the solubility product. Most people's urine is supersaturated for one or more crystal types, but crystallization inhibitors (notably urine citrate) prevent continuous stone formation. Recent biopsy data have shifted the paradigm for stone initiation: calcium phosphate deposits in the renal interstitium, hypothesized to begin at the thin limb of the loop of Henle, then extends to and erodes through the papillary epithelium, forming Randall's plaque on which most calcium oxalate stones grow. Tubular plugs of calcium phosphate may initiate calcium phosphate stones. Thus stone formation can begin years before a stone is clinically detectable. Higher urine calcium (from greater GI absorption and/or bone turnover) and higher urine oxalate (largely dietary, with higher dietary calcium reducing GI oxalate absorption) raise calcium oxalate/phosphate risk, while low urine citrate removes a key inhibitor (Harrison 22e p.2448-2450).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute cholecystitis (right ureteropelvic-junction stone)","Acute appendicitis (stone at right pelvic brim)","Acute diverticulitis (stone at left pelvic brim)","Bacterial cystitis (ureterovesical-junction stone causing urgency/frequency)","Acute pyelonephritis (obstructing stone with proximal infection)","Musculoskeletal pain","Herpes zoster","Duodenal ulcer","Abdominal aortic aneurysm","Gynecologic conditions","Ureteral stricture","Ureteral obstruction by blood clot or sloughed papilla","Extraluminal ureteral compression (typically gradual onset, not true colic)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute pyelonephritis (an obstructing stone with proximal infection can present this way)","Bacterial cystitis (ureterovesical stone causes urgency/frequency with red and white cells in urine but a negative urine culture)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute cholecystitis","Acute appendicitis","Acute diverticulitis","Abdominal aortic aneurysm","Duodenal ulcer","Herpes zoster","Gynecologic conditions","Musculoskeletal pain"],"additionalInvestigations":["Noncontrast helical CT (gold standard; detects stones as small as 1 mm and uric acid stones)","Renal ultrasound (avoids radiation, shows hydronephrosis, but insensitive for ureteral stones)","Plain abdominal radiograph (KUB) (may miss ureteral/renal stones and gives no obstruction information)","Urinalysis with sediment examination for red/white cells and crystals (calcium oxalate dihydrate = bipyramidal; cystine = hexagonal)","Urine culture if infection is suspected","Serum electrolytes (for hypokalemia/RTA), creatinine, calcium, phosphorus, uric acid","PTH and 25-hydroxy vitamin D when serum/urine calcium is elevated or high/low-normal","At least two 24-h urine collections on usual diet (volume, calcium, oxalate, citrate, uric acid, sodium, potassium, phosphorus, magnesium, pH, creatinine; supersaturation when available)","Stone composition analysis of any passed or retrieved stone"],"redFlags":["UTI in the setting of an obstructing stone is a urologic emergency ('pus under pressure') requiring urgent restoration of drainage via ureteral stent or percutaneous nephrostomy","Complete urinary obstruction by a stone (hematuria may be absent)","Stone measuring >=6 mm or anatomic abnormality (low probability of spontaneous passage)","Intractable pain unresponsive to analgesia","Staghorn or multiple bilateral stones (suggest cystinuria or struvite/infection stones)","Infection (struvite) stones, which if untreated can lead to end-stage renal disease"],"prognosis":"Although renal colic is reported as among the worst pain patients experience, nephrolithiasis is rarely fatal. More than half of first-time stone formers have a recurrence within 10 years, but recurrence of most or all stone types can be prevented with careful evaluation and targeted, often lifelong, intervention. Untreated infection (struvite) stones can have devastating consequences and progress to end-stage renal disease, and repeated obstruction and instrumentation (e.g., in cystinuria) can reduce GFR. Primary hyperoxaluria can cause intraparenchymal calcium oxalate deposition leading to renal failure (Harrison 22e p.2448, 2450, 2451).","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal urinalysis (no hematuria) rules out a stone, which is false especially with complete obstruction","Recommending low dietary calcium, which actually increases stone risk and harms bone density","Using arbitrary normal/abnormal thresholds for urine calcium, oxalate, or citrate when risk is continuous","Basing the preventive regimen on a single 24-h urine collection despite large day-to-day variability","Mistaking a preexisting residual stone for a new stone when baseline imaging was suboptimal, leading to unnecessary changes in therapy","Misattributing flank/abdominal pain to a stone and missing cholecystitis, appendicitis, diverticulitis, AAA, or an occlusive vascular event","Failing to recognize that symptom onset does not indicate when the stone formed"],"severityClassification":"No formal grading; clinical risk stratification rests on likelihood of spontaneous passage (stone size and location, e.g., >=6 mm or anatomic abnormality favoring intervention) and on presence of emergency features such as obstruction with superimposed infection.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2448","Harrison 22e p.2449","Harrison 22e p.2450","Harrison 22e p.2451","Harrison 22e p.2452"],"crossLinks":[]},"nephrotic_syndrome_paediatric":{"name":"Nephrotic syndrome (paediatric)","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["childhood nephrotic syndrome","idiopathic nephrotic syndrome","minimal change disease","paediatric nephrotic syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by the tetrad of heavy proteinuria, hypoalbuminaemia, oedema, and hyperlipidaemia.","In children it is most often idiopathic, and minimal change disease is the predominant histology, especially in preschool children.","Oedema is the usual presenting feature, typically starting periorbitally and becoming generalised with dependent swelling and sometimes ascites.","Most childhood cases are steroid-responsive, so an empirical corticosteroid trial is standard first-line therapy without initial biopsy in the typical age group.","Renal biopsy is reserved for atypical features: older or very young age, gross haematuria, hypertension, impaired renal function, or steroid resistance.","Children are at increased risk of infection (notably pneumococcal peritonitis) from urinary immunoglobulin loss, and of thromboembolism from a hypercoagulable state.","Supportive care includes sensible salt restriction, careful management of oedema, and attention to nutrition and infection prevention including vaccination.","Relapses are common; the pattern of response guides classification into steroid-sensitive, frequently relapsing, steroid-dependent, or steroid-resistant disease."],"pathophysiology":"Injury to the glomerular filtration barrier, particularly the podocytes and their slit diaphragms, increases permeability to plasma proteins, producing heavy albuminuria. In minimal change disease, light microscopy is near-normal and electron microscopy shows diffuse podocyte foot process effacement, implicating a circulating permeability factor and immune dysregulation. Urinary protein loss lowers serum albumin, reducing plasma oncotic pressure and, together with secondary sodium retention, causing oedema. The liver increases lipoprotein synthesis in response to low oncotic pressure, producing hyperlipidaemia. Loss of immunoglobulins and complement factors predisposes to infection, and loss of anticoagulant proteins with raised procoagulants predisposes to thrombosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute glomerulonephritis (nephritic syndrome)","Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","Membranous nephropathy (uncommon in children)","Henoch-Schonlein purpura nephritis","Systemic lupus erythematosus nephritis","Congenital nephrotic syndrome in infants","Oedema from cardiac, hepatic, or nutritional causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis and quantification of proteinuria (urine protein-to-creatinine ratio or timed collection)","Serum albumin, total protein, and lipid profile","Renal function and electrolytes","Full blood count","Complement levels and autoimmune serology when nephritic features are present","Renal biopsy in atypical or steroid-resistant cases"],"redFlags":["Fever with abdominal pain suggesting spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","Signs of thromboembolism (limb swelling, respiratory distress, neurological signs)","Hypertension, gross haematuria, or reduced renal function suggesting a nephritic process","Severe hypovolaemia from rapid fluid shifts, or tense ascites and respiratory compromise","Steroid resistance prompting reassessment and biopsy"],"prognosis":"Children with steroid-sensitive minimal change disease generally have a good long-term renal outcome despite frequent relapses; steroid resistance, often from focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, carries a higher risk of progression to chronic kidney disease.","pitfalls":["Attributing oedema to another cause and missing proteinuria on urinalysis","Overlooking infection or thrombosis, the major acute complications","Missing atypical features that warrant biopsy rather than empirical steroids","Aggressive diuresis precipitating hypovolaemia and thrombosis in an intravascularly depleted child"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"nervous_system_disorders_in_critical":{"name":"Nervous System Disorders in Critical Care","system":"Nervous System","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Neurologic critical care","Neurocritical care disorders","ICU neurologic complications"],"chapter":318,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2342-2352","clinicalPearls":["Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) equals mean arterial pressure (MAP) minus intracranial pressure (ICP); the general targets in neurocritical care are ICP below 20 mmHg and CPP at or above 60 mmHg (Harrison 22e p.2345).","Cerebral autoregulation normally keeps cerebral blood flow constant across a wide MAP range, but in severe brain injury it can be lost, making flow directly pressure-dependent so that hypotension causes ischemia and hypertension causes hyperperfusion (Harrison 22e p.2343-2344).","Cerebral blood flow rises with hypercapnia/acidosis and falls with hypocapnia/alkalosis, which is the basis for short-term hyperventilation to lower ICP; the effect is short-lived (hours) due to interstitial buffering, and abrupt discontinuation can cause rebound ICP elevation (Harrison 22e p.2344, p.2345).","In altered consciousness, the first task is to separate a diffuse/metabolic process from a focal/structural one; prominent focal signs such as pupillary asymmetry, hemiparesis, gaze palsy, or visual field deficit suggest a structural lesion and mandate urgent neuroimaging (Harrison 22e p.2344).","After cardiac arrest, poor-prognosis indicators include absent pupillary light reflex or extensor/absent motor response to pain at days 5-7, bilateral absence of the N20 somatosensory evoked potential component, a burst-suppression or nonreactive EEG, and a markedly elevated neuron-specific enolase (NSE), but sedation, metabolic derangements, and hypothermia confound these signs (Harrison 22e p.2346).","Osmotic demyelination syndrome (formerly central pontine myelinolysis) is typically precipitated by rapid correction of hyponatremia or hyperosmolar states, presenting days later with quadriplegia and pseudobulbar palsy; corrective sodium changes should be limited to roughly 8 mmol/L per 24 h and 15 mmol/L per 48 h (Harrison 22e p.2348-2349).","Wernicke's disease is a thiamine-deficiency emergency; only about one-third of patients show the classic triad of ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and global confusion, and IV glucose can precipitate or worsen it, so thiamine must precede glucose in at-risk patients (Harrison 22e p.2349).","Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES) reflects hyperperfusion/vasogenic edema favoring posterior (occipital) regions; the rate of blood pressure rise rather than the absolute value is the key risk factor, and the syndrome is not always reversible or strictly posterior (Harrison 22e p.2349-2350).","Sepsis-associated encephalopathy is a diffuse brain dysfunction without focal findings, occurs in the majority of patients with sepsis and multiorgan failure, and is not simply explained by metabolic derangements; cytokines such as TNF and interleukins are implicated (Harrison 22e p.2348).","Critical illness polyneuropathy is the most common PNS complication of prolonged critical illness and sepsis; it is a distal axonal sensorimotor neuropathy and should be suspected when a patient fails to wean from the ventilator despite resolving sepsis (Harrison 22e p.2351).","Persistent neuromuscular junction weakness in the ICU is almost always drug-related, most often from nondepolarizing neuromuscular blocking agents (pancuronium, vecuronium, rocuronium, cisatracurium) and potentiated by aminoglycosides; recovery is full once the drug is cleared (Harrison 22e p.2351-2352).","In PNS weakness, intubation should be considered when maximal inspiratory force falls below -25 cmH2O or vital capacity is under 1 L, and the decision should be clinical rather than waiting for oxygen desaturation or CO2 retention (Harrison 22e p.2351)."],"pathophysiology":"Life-threatening neurologic illness in the critically ill arises from primary lesions anywhere along the neuraxis or as a consequence of systemic disorders (hepatic failure, multiorgan failure, cardiac arrest). The unifying goal is preventing secondary brain injury from ischemia, hemorrhage, edema, herniation, and elevated ICP. Brain edema is vasogenic (fluid crossing an incompetent blood-brain barrier) and/or cytotoxic (cellular swelling and death); clinically significant edema usually combines both and can raise ICP and cause tissue shifts. Inadequate oxygen/glucose delivery triggers the ischemic cascade: excitatory amino acid (glutamate) release, calcium and sodium influx, protease/lipase activation, lipid peroxidation, free-radical membrane injury, cytotoxic edema, and necrotic cell death; an alternative apoptotic pathway occurs without edema and is often not seen on imaging. Penumbra denotes still-salvageable ischemic tissue. Secondary insults (hypotension, hypoxia, fever, seizures, hyperglycemia) worsen the cascade. The skull tolerates little added volume, so once intracranial compliance is exhausted, small volume increases sharply raise ICP, lower CPP, and provoke ischemia; ischemia-driven autoregulatory vasodilation can paradoxically further raise blood volume and ICP, creating a vicious cycle.","additionalDifferentials":["Diffuse/metabolic encephalopathy: organ failure (hepatic, renal), drug overdose, sedative/analgesic effect, electrolyte disturbance (hyponatremia, hypoglycemia), hypoxia-ischemia, hypercarbia","Focal/structural lesions: ischemic and hemorrhagic stroke, traumatic brain injury with intracranial hematoma, tumor, abscess, subdural/epidural hematoma","Basilar artery thrombosis causing brainstem ischemia (may mimic generalized seizures with extensor posturing)","Nonconvulsive status epilepticus presenting as unexplained decreased consciousness","Wernicke's disease in any disoriented at-risk patient","Sepsis-associated encephalopathy"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial meningitis presenting as global encephalopathy","Encephalitis (including herpes simplex virus in immunosuppressed transplant patients)","CNS opportunistic infection in transplant patients (Nocardia, Toxoplasma, aspergillosis, CMV, HHV-6, varicella, JC virus PML)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sedative/analgesic or polypharmacy-induced delirium misattributed to depression","Postcardiac bypass microembolic encephalopathy","Calcineurin inhibitor (cyclosporine, tacrolimus) neurotoxicity / PRES, possible even at normal drug levels","Checkpoint inhibitor immune encephalitis and CAR-T neurotoxicity","Posthypoxic myoclonus / myoclonic status epilepticus after cardiac arrest","Korsakoff amnestic state evolving from Wernicke's disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["Noncontrast CT as the initial study for hemorrhage, hydrocephalus, and tissue shifts; hyperdense basilar artery sign suggests thrombus","MRI including diffusion-weighted imaging for acute ischemic stroke and laminar cortical injury after hypoxia-ischemia","CT/MR angiography or venography for arterial occlusion or cerebral venous thrombosis","EEG to detect nonconvulsive status epilepticus and characterize metabolic slowing or burst-suppression/myoclonic status","Lumbar puncture to exclude infection/inflammation (after neuroimaging in coma); elevated opening pressure may indicate venous sinus thrombosis","ICP monitoring (ventriculostomy preferred as it allows CSF drainage; parenchymal device for small ventricles or coagulopathy)","Serum electrolytes (especially sodium, calcium), glucose, renal and hepatic function, CBC, coagulation studies; toxicology screen in unexplained encephalopathy","Somatosensory evoked potentials (N20) and neuron-specific enolase for post-arrest prognostication","Carboxyhemoglobin measurement for suspected carbon monoxide poisoning","Bubble study (agitated saline echocardiography) to evaluate right-to-left shunt after embolic infarction in transplant patients"],"redFlags":["Coma of sudden onset with extensor posturing and cranial nerve abnormalities suggesting basilar artery thrombosis (emergency imaging required)","Coma with unilateral pupillary change as a late sign of elevated ICP requiring immediate intervention","Decreased consciousness with prominent focal neurologic signs indicating a possible structural lesion","Systolic blood pressure below 90 mmHg even briefly, or oxygen saturation below 90%, as secondary insults worsening outcome","Maximal inspiratory force below -25 cmH2O or vital capacity below 1 L, or severe bulbar/palatal weakness, signaling impending respiratory failure","Persistently dilated nonreactive pupils and absent brainstem reflexes after hypoxic-ischemic injury","Failure to wean from mechanical ventilation despite resolving systemic illness (suggests critical illness neuropathy/myopathy or prolonged neuromuscular blockade)"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on the underlying disorder and on prevention of secondary injury. After hypoxia-ischemia, recovery may be full if circulation is restored within 3-5 minutes, while longer insults often cause permanent damage; the brain tolerates pure hypoxia better than combined hypoxia-ischemia. Reliable post-arrest prognostication requires sufficient observation time, and hypothermia/sedation delay when predictors become valid; some patients awaken after a week or longer, so premature withdrawal risks a self-fulfilling prophecy. Sepsis-associated encephalopathy carries roughly 50% mortality when severe enough to cause coma, though this mainly reflects underlying illness severity; the encephalopathy usually improves substantially with successful treatment, but long-term cognitive dysfunction is increasingly recognized, especially in older survivors. Critical illness polyneuropathy and thick-filament critical illness myopathy generally recover spontaneously if the patient survives, though recovery may take weeks to months and some residual weakness can persist. Prolonged neuromuscular blockade causes no permanent damage and resolves once the agent clears.","pitfalls":["Performing lumbar puncture before neuroimaging in a comatose or profoundly encephalopathic patient","Delaying empiric antibiotics for suspected bacterial meningitis while awaiting diagnostic studies","Attributing the hypoactive confusional state of microembolic encephalopathy after cardiac surgery to depression or sedative delirium","Giving IV glucose before thiamine in malnourished/alcoholic patients, precipitating or worsening Wernicke's disease","Correcting hyponatremia too rapidly and triggering osmotic demyelination syndrome","Assuming normal serum calcineurin inhibitor levels exclude drug-induced PRES or neurotoxicity","Prolonged or unmonitored use of nondepolarizing neuromuscular blockers leading to persistent blockade or thick-filament myopathy (use lowest effective dose with peripheral nerve stimulator monitoring)","Using glucocorticoids for ICP in head trauma or ischemic/hemorrhagic stroke, where they are not indicated (reserved for vasogenic edema from tumor or abscess)","Sustained or aggressive hyperventilation, which can worsen cerebral ischemia and cause rebound ICP on discontinuation"],"severityClassification":"Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score of 8 or less defines severe traumatic brain injury and is a key criterion for ICP monitoring (Harrison 22e p.2345). Operational neurocritical care targets include ICP below 20 mmHg and CPP at or above 60 mmHg, with stepwise escalation of therapy for sustained ICP above 20-25 mmHg; CAR-T cytokine release syndrome and neurotoxicity severity track with degree of fever, CRP, and ferritin elevation.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2342-2352"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuroblastoma":{"name":"Neuroblastoma","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neuroblastic tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common extracranial solid tumour of childhood and the most common malignancy of infancy, arising from primitive sympathetic neural crest cells.","It can occur anywhere along the sympathetic chain; the adrenal medulla and paraspinal abdomen are the most frequent primary sites.","An abdominal primary often presents as a firm, irregular mass that crosses the midline, distinguishing it from Wilms tumour.","Presentation is highly variable and may include systemic upset, bone pain, proptosis with periorbital bruising ('raccoon eyes') from orbital metastases, or a paraspinal mass causing cord compression.","Distinctive paraneoplastic features include opsoclonus-myoclonus (dancing eyes, dancing feet) and secretory diarrhoea from vasoactive intestinal peptide.","Most tumours secrete catecholamines, so urinary homovanillic acid (HVA) and vanillylmandelic acid (VMA) are useful markers.","Behaviour spans a remarkable spectrum, from spontaneous regression in infants (including stage MS/4S disease) to aggressive, widely metastatic disease.","MYCN amplification is a key adverse biological marker that upstages risk and intensifies therapy.","Risk stratification integrating age, stage, histology, and genetics guides treatment ranging from observation or surgery to intensive multimodal therapy."],"pathophysiology":"Neuroblastoma develops from neural crest-derived precursors of the sympathetic nervous system, giving rise to a spectrum from undifferentiated neuroblastoma to more mature ganglioneuroblastoma and benign ganglioneuroma. Tumour behaviour is strongly governed by biology: MYCN amplification, segmental chromosomal changes, and DNA ploidy correlate with aggressiveness, while some infant tumours undergo differentiation or apoptosis and regress spontaneously.","additionalDifferentials":["Wilms tumour","Rhabdomyosarcoma","Lymphoma or leukaemic infiltration","Phaeochromocytoma","Ewing sarcoma / other small round blue cell tumours","Hepatoblastoma or other abdominal masses"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinary catecholamine metabolites (HVA and VMA)","Cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI) of the primary site","MIBG scintigraphy for functional imaging and metastasis detection","Bone marrow aspirate and trephine biopsy for marrow involvement","Tumour biopsy for histology and biology including MYCN status","Full blood count, renal and liver function, lactate dehydrogenase and ferritin"],"redFlags":["Signs of spinal cord compression from a paraspinal (dumbbell) tumour","Rapidly progressive or widely metastatic disease","Periorbital bruising and proptosis suggesting orbital metastases","Severe secretory diarrhoea from a VIP-secreting tumour","Systemic deterioration with bone pain and marrow failure"],"prognosis":"Outcome is extremely variable: low-risk and infant disease (including 4S/MS) often has excellent survival and may regress, whereas high-risk disease with MYCN amplification or widespread metastases has a much poorer prognosis despite intensive therapy.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with Wilms tumour when it presents as an abdominal mass","Missing spinal cord compression from a paraspinal component","Overlooking opsoclonus-myoclonus as a paraneoplastic clue to occult tumour","Failing to obtain MYCN status, which changes management","Assuming all disease is aggressive and over-treating low-risk or regressing tumours"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"neurocysticercosis":{"name":"Neurocysticercosis","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NCC","CNS cysticercosis","cerebral cysticercosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Central nervous system infection by larval Taenia solium and one of the leading causes of acquired epilepsy in India and other endemic regions","Seizures are the most frequent presentation, typically from a single or few degenerating parenchymal cysts","Clinical features depend on cyst number, location and stage, ranging from asymptomatic calcified lesions to disabling neurological disease","Extraparenchymal disease (intraventricular, subarachnoid or racemose forms) can obstruct cerebrospinal fluid flow and cause hydrocephalus, and behaves more aggressively","MRI and CT are central to diagnosis; the finding of a cyst with a visible scolex is highly characteristic, and calcified lesions indicate old, dead cysts","Antiparasitic therapy is combined with corticosteroids to blunt the inflammatory reaction to dying cysts, with antiseizure medication for those presenting with seizures","Treatment must be individualised: viable parenchymal cysts, calcified lesions and intraventricular cysts are managed differently, and some forms need neurosurgical intervention","Fundoscopy or ophthalmic assessment is advised before antiparasitic treatment to exclude ocular cysts"],"pathophysiology":"Ingested Taenia solium eggs release oncospheres that reach the brain and develop into cysticerci. Viable cysts in the parenchyma may remain quiescent, but immune-mediated degeneration releases antigens that provoke perilesional inflammation and oedema, generating seizures and focal deficits. As cysts die they may calcify, and these calcified foci can remain epileptogenic. In the ventricles and subarachnoid space, cysts and the associated inflammation can block cerebrospinal fluid pathways, producing raised intracranial pressure and hydrocephalus, while subarachnoid (racemose) disease may cause arachnoiditis and vasculitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Tuberculoma","Cerebral toxoplasmosis","Pyogenic or fungal brain abscess","Primary or metastatic brain tumour","Other parasitic CNS lesions (e.g. hydatid cyst)"],"infectionMimics":["CNS tuberculosis","Toxoplasma encephalitis in immunocompromised patients","Primary CNS neoplasm","Vasculitic or demyelinating lesions"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI brain to characterise cyst stage, detect a scolex and assess perilesional oedema","CT to identify calcified lesions","Serological antibody testing (e.g. EITB) as supportive evidence","Ophthalmological examination to exclude ocular cysts before therapy","CSF studies and assessment for hydrocephalus in suspected extraparenchymal disease"],"redFlags":["New-onset or breakthrough seizures, including status epilepticus, requiring urgent management","Signs of raised intracranial pressure or hydrocephalus from intraventricular disease, a neurosurgical emergency","Rapid neurological deterioration precipitated by inflammation when antiparasitic therapy is started","Focal deficits, altered consciousness or signs of stroke from subarachnoid vasculitis"],"prognosis":"Solitary parenchymal cysts often resolve with good seizure control and a favourable outcome, though some patients develop chronic epilepsy from residual calcified lesions. Extraparenchymal, subarachnoid and heavy-burden disease carries a worse prognosis with risk of hydrocephalus, recurrent complications and higher mortality.","pitfalls":["Giving antiparasitic drugs without corticosteroid cover, risking severe inflammatory worsening and seizures","Treating without first excluding ocular or intraventricular cysts, where drug-induced inflammation is dangerous","Assuming all ring-enhancing brain lesions are neurocysticercosis and missing tuberculoma or tumour","Overlooking hydrocephalus as a cause of deterioration in extraparenchymal disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"neurogenic_bladder":{"name":"Neurogenic bladder","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["neuropathic bladder","neurogenic lower urinary tract dysfunction","NLUTD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Neurogenic bladder describes bladder dysfunction due to disease or injury of the nervous system, with the pattern depending on the level of the neurological lesion","Suprapontine and suprasacral (upper motor neuron) lesions typically cause a spastic, overactive detrusor, often with detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia, whereas sacral or peripheral (lower motor neuron) lesions cause a flaccid, areflexic, poorly emptying bladder","Common causes include spinal cord injury, multiple sclerosis, spina bifida, diabetes mellitus, Parkinson's disease, stroke, and cauda equina lesions","The paramount goal of management is protection of the upper urinary tracts, because high storage pressures and detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia can cause hydronephrosis and renal failure","Video-urodynamics is central to characterising bladder behaviour, storage pressures, and sphincter coordination to guide safe management","Clean intermittent catheterisation is a cornerstone for bladders that do not empty adequately, reducing residual urine and infection risk while preserving the upper tracts","Management principles combine measures to lower storage pressure and improve emptying, such as antimuscarinics, intradetrusor botulinum toxin, catheterisation regimens, and, in selected cases, augmentation or diversion surgery","Patients are at risk of recurrent urinary infection, stones, and, in high spinal cord injuries, autonomic dysreflexia triggered by bladder distension"],"pathophysiology":"Normal micturition depends on coordinated control between the pontine micturition centre, spinal pathways, and sacral (S2-S4) reflexes governing the detrusor and urethral sphincter. Neurological lesions disrupt this coordination in patterns determined by lesion level. Upper motor neuron lesions above the sacral cord release the sacral reflex from higher inhibition, producing detrusor overactivity and often detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia, in which the sphincter contracts against a contracting bladder, generating dangerously high pressures. Lower motor neuron or peripheral lesions abolish the reflex arc, producing an acontractile, flaccid bladder with retention and overflow. Sustained high intravesical pressure and incomplete emptying threaten the upper tracts and predispose to infection and stones.","additionalDifferentials":["Detrusor overactivity from non-neurogenic causes (idiopathic overactive bladder)","Bladder outlet obstruction (BPH, stricture)","Diabetic cystopathy","Cauda equina syndrome","Multiple sclerosis or other demyelinating disease","Spinal cord injury or compression","Pelvic surgery-related denervation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Video-urodynamic studies to assess detrusor pressure, compliance, and sphincter coordination","Renal function tests and upper tract imaging (ultrasound) to monitor for hydronephrosis","Post-void residual measurement","Urinalysis and culture for infection surveillance","Neurological evaluation and imaging directed at the underlying condition","Periodic surveillance for bladder stones and, where relevant, malignancy in long-term catheterised patients"],"redFlags":["High detrusor storage pressures or detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia threatening the upper tracts and renal function","New or progressive hydronephrosis and rising creatinine","Autonomic dysreflexia in high spinal cord injury, a potentially life-threatening emergency triggered by bladder overdistension","Urosepsis in an obstructed or poorly draining neurogenic bladder","Acute cauda equina syndrome presenting with retention and neurological deficit"],"prognosis":"With structured management focused on low-pressure storage, adequate emptying, and regular upper tract surveillance, many patients maintain renal function and continence; neglected high-pressure bladders carry a significant risk of progressive renal impairment and recurrent complications.","pitfalls":["Focusing on continence while neglecting upper tract protection and storage pressures","Failing to recognise and treat detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia","Not anticipating autonomic dysreflexia during bladder distension or instrumentation in high spinal injuries","Inadequate surveillance for renal deterioration, stones, and infection","Treating overactivity with antimuscarinics without ensuring adequate emptying"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuroimaging_in_neurologic_disorders":{"name":"Neuroimaging in Neurologic Disorders","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Neuroimaging","CT and MRI in neurology","Brain and spine imaging"],"chapter":434,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3383","clinicalPearls":["MRI is generally more sensitive than CT for lesions of the central and peripheral nervous system, whereas CT is faster and the pragmatic first choice for uncooperative patients and for suspected acute stroke, hemorrhage, or acute intracranial/spinal trauma (Harrison 22e p.3383).","Diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI) is the single most sensitive technique for detecting acute cerebral or spinal cord ischemia, with restricted diffusion (high DWI signal) appearing in infarction of less than about 7 days; it also lights up encephalitis, abscess, and Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (Harrison 22e p.3383, 3389).","CT is superior to MRI for fine osseous detail and is preferred for the initial workup of conductive hearing loss and skull/spine bone lesions, while MRI better detects bone-marrow infiltrative processes that CT may miss (Harrison 22e p.3383).","On unenhanced CT, denser structures such as bone or acute blood appear bright (high attenuation), whereas water, air, and soft tissue appear dark; this is why acute hemorrhage and bone are well seen on noncontrast CT (Harrison 22e p.3383-3384).","Iodinated contrast highlights vascular structures and blood-brain barrier defects; in the normal CNS only vessels and structures lacking a blood-brain barrier (pituitary, choroid plexus, dura) enhance (Harrison 22e p.3384).","Susceptibility-weighted imaging (SWI) is highly sensitive to blood, calcium, and air and is the sequence of choice for microhemorrhages seen in amyloid angiopathy, hypertension, hemorrhagic metastases, traumatic brain injury, and thrombotic states (Harrison 22e p.3387-3388).","FLAIR suppresses the bright CSF signal of T2 imaging, improving detection of edematous/water-containing lesions adjacent to CSF spaces such as periventricular plaques and cortical lesions (Harrison 22e p.3387).","Gadolinium-based contrast does not cause contrast-induced nephropathy, but linear (group 1/3) agents can cause nephrogenic systemic fibrosis in patients with severe renal impairment; macrocyclic (group 2) agents are far safer and preferred in at-risk patients (Harrison 22e p.3388-3389).","Perfusion imaging that shows a region of prolonged mean transit time with preserved cerebral blood volume identifies tissue at risk (ischemic penumbra) that may be salvageable, distinct from infarcted core where blood volume and flow are reduced (Harrison 22e p.3390).","CTA and gadolinium-enhanced MRA have largely replaced catheter angiography, which is now reserved for cases needing fine small-vessel detail or planned interventional therapy (Harrison 22e p.3383).","Vessel wall MR imaging is a high-resolution technique that interrogates the arterial wall itself and helps distinguish atherosclerosis, primary CNS vasculitis, and reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome (Harrison 22e p.3390).","Amyloid PET can be positive in up to about 25% of cognitively normal older adults, so a positive scan does not by itself diagnose Alzheimer disease; tau PET appears more specific (Harrison 22e p.3392)."],"pathophysiology":"Neuroimaging techniques exploit differing physical tissue properties. CT reconstructs a cross-sectional map of x-ray attenuation: dense tissue (bone, acute clot) attenuates heavily and appears bright, while water and air appear dark. MRI maps the distribution and relaxation behavior of (mainly hydrogen) protons placed in a strong static magnetic field and excited by radiofrequency pulses; T1 relaxation is the time for ~63% of protons to return toward equilibrium and T2 the time for ~63% to dephase. Manipulating pulse timing (TR and TE) yields T1-weighted images (fat and subacute hemorrhage bright; CSF/edema dark) or T2-weighted images (CSF/edema/demyelination/infarct bright). Gray matter contains roughly 10-15% more water than white matter, providing intrinsic contrast. Pathology such as infarction, abscess, and dense tumor restricts microscopic water motion, producing high DWI signal. Iodinated and gadolinium contrast agents reveal blood-brain barrier breakdown by tumor, infarct, or infection. Flow phenomena underlie MR angiography, and regional changes in oxy/deoxyhemoglobin underlie the BOLD signal of functional MRI (Harrison 22e p.3383-3392).","additionalDifferentials":["Ring-enhancing lesion: cerebral abscess versus high-grade glioma or metastasis (restricted central diffusion favors abscess)","Restricted diffusion in medial temporal lobe: herpes simplex encephalitis versus acute infarction versus other limbic inflammatory processes","Multiple low-signal SWI foci: cerebral amyloid angiopathy versus chronic hypertensive microbleeds versus hemorrhagic metastases versus diffuse axonal injury","Focal arterial narrowing/wall enhancement: atherosclerosis versus primary CNS vasculitis versus reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Noncontrast CT for acute hemorrhage, trauma, and acute mental-status change","MRI with diffusion-weighted imaging for suspected ischemia, encephalitis, or abscess","CT angiography or gadolinium-enhanced/time-of-flight MR angiography for vascular assessment","Vessel wall MR imaging to characterize arteriopathies and vasculitis","Perfusion CT or perfusion MRI/ASL to assess core versus penumbra and tumor vascularity","CT myelography for failed-back syndrome, CSF fistula localization, and meningeal/arachnoid cysts","FDG-PET and amyloid/tau PET ligands for oncologic staging and neurodegenerative evaluation","Estimated GFR before contrast in at-risk patients"],"redFlags":["Acute change in mental status, new focal deficit, or head/spine trauma warranting urgent noncontrast CT","Suspected subarachnoid hemorrhage (CT then CTA, with lumbar puncture if CT negative)","Severe renal impairment (eGFR <30 mL/min per 1.73 m2) or dialysis raising concern for nephrogenic systemic fibrosis with linear gadolinium agents","Prior severe contrast (anaphylactoid) reaction, with eightfold higher recurrence risk for gadolinium","Ferromagnetic implants (some aneurysm clips, non-conditional pacemakers/defibrillators) or ocular metallic foreign body before MRI","Suspected spinal block or elevated intracranial pressure before myelography/lumbar puncture"],"prognosis":"As a diagnostic modality, neuroimaging itself is generally safe. Contrast-associated acute kidney injury from iodinated contrast is rare, usually transient with creatinine returning toward baseline within 1-2 weeks, and most strongly associated with preexisting severe renal insufficiency; gadolinium does not cause it. Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis from linear gadolinium agents in severe renal failure is rare (reported incidence ~0.19-4% in GFR <30) but can be severely disabling with multiorgan fibrosis. Severe allergic reactions are uncommon (~0.04% with nonionic iodinated agents; gadolinium severe reactions exceedingly rare). Radiation-induced malignancy from diagnostic CT is felt to be minimal but is dose-dependent (Harrison 22e p.3384-3389).","pitfalls":["Reliance on shellfish or iodine allergy to predict contrast reactions is unreliable; the strongest predictor is a prior contrast reaction (about fivefold risk), plus atopy/asthma and other drug/food allergies (Harrison 22e p.3385).","Premedication regimens shorter than 4-5 hours have no demonstrated efficacy and do not guarantee prevention of contrast reactions (Harrison 22e p.3387).","Bicarbonate and acetylcysteine have not shown protection over normal saline against contrast-associated AKI in recent meta-analyses (Harrison 22e p.3385).","MRA has lower spatial resolution than catheter angiography, is insensitive to slow flow, and may not distinguish near-complete from complete occlusion or detect small-vessel vasculitis/distal vasospasm (Harrison 22e p.3390).","Brain gadolinium deposition (dentate nuclei, globus pallidus) occurs even with normal renal/hepatic function, is dose-dependent and greater with linear agents, but to date has no proven neurotoxic histologic correlate (Harrison 22e p.3387-3388).","The MR magnet is always on; ferromagnetic missile injuries and torqued implants can be fatal, so rigorous screening of patients and staff is mandatory (Harrison 22e p.3389).","Patient motion degrades MRI; uncooperative patients should be sedated or imaged with CT, and children under ~8 years often require monitored anesthesia (Harrison 22e p.3389).","Iodinated contrast should be avoided before planned radioactive iodine thyroid therapy because it reduces radioisotope uptake (Harrison 22e p.3387)."],"severityClassification":"Contrast-associated acute kidney injury is defined per KDIGO criteria within 48 h of contrast: absolute serum creatinine rise >=0.3 mg/dL (>26.4 micromol/L), a >=50% (>=1.5-fold) increase over baseline, or urine output <=0.5 mL/kg per h for at least 6 h. Renal-risk stratification for contrast uses eGFR, with <30 mL/min per 1.73 m2 carrying the greatest evidence for risk (normal >=90 mL/min per 1.73 m2) (Harrison 22e p.3384-3386).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3383","Harrison 22e p.3384","Harrison 22e p.3385","Harrison 22e p.3386","Harrison 22e p.3387","Harrison 22e p.3388","Harrison 22e p.3389","Harrison 22e p.3390","Harrison 22e p.3391","Harrison 22e p.3392","Harrison 22e p.3393"],"crossLinks":[]},"neuromyelitis_optica":{"name":"Neuromyelitis Optica","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NMO","NMOSD","Neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder","Devic's disease","Devic disease"],"chapter":456,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3590","clinicalPearls":["NMO is an aggressive antibody-mediated inflammatory CNS disorder defined by recurrent attacks of optic neuritis and myelitis; the broader term NMOSD captures partial forms and cases with additional CNS involvement (Harrison p.3590).","Strongly female-predominant (women:men roughly 9:1), with a mean onset around age 40 but possible at any age (Harrison p.3590).","Optic neuritis in NMO can be bilateral and cause severe vision loss, a pattern uncommon in MS; myelitis is often severe, transverse, and longitudinally extensive spanning three or more contiguous vertebral segments (Harrison p.3590).","Unlike MS, NMO rarely follows a progressive course; accumulated disability results from attack-related injury rather than progression (Harrison p.3590).","Brain MRI is not invariably normal: lesions may involve the area postrema (intractable hiccups or vomiting), hypothalamus (sleep disorder or endocrinopathy), or cerebral hemispheres; large hemispheric lesions can be asymptomatic, show a 'cloud-like' appearance, be non-destructive, and resolve completely (Harrison p.3590).","Spinal cord lesions are focal enhancing areas of swelling and tissue destruction over three or more segments and are centered on the gray matter on axial imaging (Harrison p.3590).","CSF typically shows greater pleocytosis than MS, sometimes with neutrophils and eosinophils in acute cases, while oligoclonal bands are uncommon (present in fewer than 20% of patients) (Harrison p.3590-3591).","A highly specific serum autoantibody against the water channel aquaporin-4 (AQP4) is present in about 90% of patients and is diagnostic when paired with a typical clinical presentation (Harrison p.3590).","In a subset of AQP4-negative cases, autoantibodies against myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein (MOG) are found (Harrison p.3591).","Up to 40% of NMO patients have a coexisting systemic autoimmune disorder (e.g., SLE, Sjögren's syndrome, p-ANCA vasculitis, myasthenia gravis, Hashimoto's thyroiditis, mixed connective tissue disease) - a feature distinguishing it from MS (Harrison p.3591).","Symptomatic onset may follow acute infection with varicella-zoster virus, Epstein-Barr virus, HIV, or tuberculosis; rare cases are paraneoplastic (breast, lung, or other cancers) (Harrison p.3592).","Asian and African ancestry populations are disproportionately affected, with the highest reported prevalence in Martinique; among white populations MS is far more common than NMO (Harrison p.3591)."],"pathophysiology":"NMO is an autoimmune astrocytopathy driven by autoantibodies against the water channel aquaporin-4 (AQP4), which is concentrated on astrocyte foot processes apposed to endothelium and at paranodal regions near nodes of Ranvier. Pathology shows loss of AQP4 staining with antibody and complement deposition, astrocyte cytolysis, thickened vessel walls, demyelination, and inflammation. AQP4 antibodies are likely pathogenic, since passive transfer reproduces disease features in animals; both complement fixation and antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity contribute to astrocyte injury, and TH17 proinflammatory T cells recognizing an immunodominant AQP4 epitope may also participate. A thymic B-cell population that constitutively expresses AQP4 normally interacts with AQP4-specific T cells to maintain tolerance, and impairment of this interaction may contribute. During acute myelitis attacks, CSF levels of IL-6 and glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP) are markedly elevated, reflecting active inflammation and astrocyte injury (Harrison p.3590-3591).","additionalDifferentials":["Multiple sclerosis (especially opticospinal MS subtype in Asian/African ancestry, which can itself be AQP4-seropositive and represent NMOSD)","MOG antibody-associated disease (AQP4-negative cases)","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis","Idiopathic or postinfectious transverse myelitis","Neurosarcoidosis","Systemic autoimmune myelopathy (SLE, Sjögren's syndrome, Behcet's disease)","Spinal cord infarction (longitudinally extensive anterior cord syndrome)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral myelitis (e.g., herpes simplex virus type 2 causing recurrent myelitis)","Tuberculous CNS involvement","HIV-associated myelopathy"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Multiple sclerosis","MOG antibody-associated disease","Neurosarcoidosis","Systemic lupus erythematosus / Sjögren's-associated myelopathy","Paraneoplastic optic neuropathy/myelopathy","Spinal cord infarction"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum AQP4-IgG testing using a cell-based assay (strongly recommended detection method)","MOG-IgG testing in AQP4-seronegative patients","Spinal cord MRI to demonstrate longitudinally extensive (>=3 segment) gray matter-centered lesions","Brain MRI to detect area postrema, periependymal brainstem, diencephalic, or cloud-like hemispheric lesions","Optic nerve MRI (T2/gadolinium-enhancing lesion over >half optic nerve length or chiasm)","Lumbar puncture for CSF pleocytosis, cell differential, and oligoclonal bands","Screening for coexisting systemic autoimmune disease"],"redFlags":["Bilateral simultaneous optic neuritis with severe vision loss","Longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis spanning three or more vertebral segments","Intractable hiccups, nausea, or vomiting (area postrema syndrome)","Cervical myelitis threatening respiratory failure","Acute brainstem or diencephalic syndrome (e.g., symptomatic narcolepsy, hypothalamic dysfunction)","Recurrent attacks, since more than half of untreated AQP4-seropositive patients relapse within 1 year"],"prognosis":"NMO is typically a relapsing disease; a monophasic course occurs in fewer than 10% of patients and is somewhat more likely in AQP4-antibody-negative individuals. Untreated disease is severely disabling: historically about one-third of patients developed respiratory failure from cervical myelitis, and by 8 years roughly 60% were blind and more than half had permanent limb paralysis. AQP4-seropositive patients carry a high relapse risk (more than half relapse within 1 year if untreated). Long-term outcomes have substantially improved with the advent of attack and relapse therapies, with reported 5-year survival rising from about 68-75% around 1999 to 91-98% by 2017 (Harrison p.3590-3591).","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal brain MRI excludes NMO - characteristic area postrema, periependymal brainstem, diencephalic, and cloud-like hemispheric lesions can be present (Harrison p.3590).","Conflating NMO with MS: bilateral severe optic neuritis, longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis, marked CSF pleocytosis, and frequent absence of oligoclonal bands favor NMO (Harrison p.3590).","Relying on oligoclonal bands, which are present in fewer than 20% of NMO patients (Harrison p.3590-3591).","Overlooking that NMO frequently coexists with systemic autoimmune disease, unlike MS (Harrison p.3591).","Failing to recognize AQP4-negative disease, where MOG antibodies may be present and where seronegative NMOSD requires both optic neuritis and longitudinally extensive myelitis to meet criteria (Harrison p.3591-3592)."],"severityClassification":"No formal severity grading; diagnosis follows the 2015 International Consensus Diagnostic Criteria for NMOSD, which stratify by AQP4-IgG status. AQP4-IgG-positive disease requires at least one core clinical characteristic, a positive AQP4-IgG test, and exclusion of alternatives. AQP4-IgG-negative or unknown-status disease requires at least two core characteristics (one of which must be optic neuritis, acute myelitis with longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis, or area postrema syndrome), dissemination in space, fulfillment of additional MRI requirements, a negative or unavailable AQP4-IgG test, and exclusion of alternatives. Core clinical characteristics: optic neuritis, acute myelitis, area postrema syndrome, acute brainstem syndrome, symptomatic narcolepsy/acute diencephalic syndrome with typical MRI lesions, and symptomatic cerebral syndrome with typical brain lesions (Harrison p.3591, Table 456-1).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3590","Harrison 22e p.3591","Harrison 22e p.3592","Harrison 22e p.3565"],"crossLinks":[]},"nicotine_addiction":{"name":"Nicotine Addiction","system":"Neurologic / Substance Use","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Tobacco dependence","Tobacco use disorder","Nicotine dependence","Cigarette addiction"],"chapter":465,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3681","clinicalPearls":["Addiction is established when nicotine in any form (combusted or heated tobacco, oral pouches, or vaped nicotine/nicotine salt) is used with sufficient intensity for a sufficient duration; addicted users self-titrate the frequency and intensity of use to achieve psychoactive effects and avoid withdrawal.","The diagnosis is clinical and hinges on loss of control over the next use of a nicotine product, evidenced by daily use, high number of episodes per day, use shortly after waking, craving during abstinence, and prior failed quit attempts.","The clinically important metric is the cumulative frequency of any nicotine dosing across all products used, not the specific product source, because it is dosing frequency that creates and sustains addiction.","Nicotine itself causes relatively little disease (notably pregnancy complications); the majority of disease burden comes from repetitive exposure to the carcinogens and toxicants carried with nicotine in various products, which addiction drives by compelling multiple daily exposures.","Withdrawal symptoms intensify with longer abstinence as blood nicotine falls and detaches from receptors, and these symptoms can persist for roughly 4-6 weeks.","Acidic aerosols (cigarettes and nicotine salt vaping devices) reduce airway irritation, permit deep inhalation, and produce rapid arterial nicotine rises reaching the brain, making them more addictive than products with slower delivery.","Cancer risk rises linearly with the number of cigarettes per day and logarithmically with duration of smoking; relative risk of lung cancer in current smokers ranges roughly 13-28 across age groups.","Cigarette smoking accounts for about 80% of COPD, about 90% of peripheral vascular disease in nondiabetics, about 50% of aortic aneurysms, about 24% of coronary artery disease, and about 11% of strokes.","Smoking induces the cytochrome P450 system, which can lower serum levels of drugs such as warfarin; conversely, levels may rise during hospitalization when a patient stops smoking, an important consideration for narrow-therapeutic-index agents.","About 40% of cigarette smokers die prematurely from smoking unless they quit; the population still smoking has shifted toward socially disadvantaged groups, and prevalence has declined more slowly in those over 40 who are most likely to develop disease.","'Lower tar/nicotine' filtered cigarettes do not reduce real exposure because smokers compensate by altering puffing behavior or number smoked; ventilation-hole designs lower only machine-measured yields, not delivered toxicants.","Several genes are associated with nicotine addiction (some reducing nicotine clearance, others increasing dependence likelihood or depression risk), but the large declines in smoking since the mid-1950s indicate non-genetic factors dominate population trends."],"pathophysiology":"Nicotine reaching the brain reversibly binds nicotinic acetylcholine receptors that are highly active in networks mediating mood, reward, joy, and excitement. With sustained high exposure, nicotinic receptors are upregulated on brain cells, so that for most people daily use is required to maintain the addicted state. As blood nicotine falls between doses, nicotine detaches from receptors and addicted individuals no longer feel 'normal,' producing a compulsive drive for the next dose. The strength and speed of addiction depend on dosing frequency and on the arterial nicotine concentration reaching the brain, which varies by product. Unprotonated (freebase) nicotine, favored at alkaline pH, is absorbed readily across mucosa but is airway-irritating at high concentration; addictive products use mildly acidic aerosols in the mouth to limit irritation and permit deep inhalation, after which alveolar pH shifts toward blood pH (~7.4), rapidly liberating absorbable unprotonated nicotine and driving fast arterial rises to the brain.","additionalDifferentials":["Other substance use disorders (alcohol use disorder, stimulant use)","Caffeine use/dependence","Primary mood or anxiety disorders presenting with cravings or restlessness","Cannabis use via vaping devices (refillable pods may aerosolize marijuana oils)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Withdrawal-related irritability and restlessness mimicking primary anxiety or depressive disorders","Chronic cough from smoking-related mucous hyperplasia mimicking other causes of chronic productive cough"],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured history of total daily frequency of all nicotine products used (cigarettes, cigars, roll-your-own, vaping, oral pouches/snuff)","Duration of regular use, prior quit experiences, and current interest in quitting","Markers of addiction intensity: episodes per day, spacing through the day, and use within 30 minutes of waking","Biomarkers of smoke-toxicant exposure (markedly lower in exclusive e-cigarette users than in cigarette smokers)"],"redFlags":["Continued smoking during chemotherapy or radiation, associated with poorer outcomes and reduced survival","Smoking in pregnancy: premature rupture of membranes, abruptio placentae, placenta previa, preterm delivery, small-for-gestational-age, increased perinatal mortality and sudden infant death syndrome","Dual use of e-cigarettes and combusted cigarettes, which negates toxicant-reduction benefits","High-frequency use of noncombusted inhaled nicotine products in patients who never used combusted tobacco","Multiplicative cardiac risk when smoking is combined with hypertension or elevated lipids","Higher vascular graft occlusion rates in smokers after bypass surgery"],"prognosis":"Cessation produces substantial reversible benefit: risk of a second coronary event falls within 6-12 months, and first myocardial infarction and coronary death rates decline within 2-4 years; after about 15 years of abstinence cardiovascular risk approaches that of never-smokers. Cancer risk declines relative to continued smoking after about 4 years, but a roughly three- to fourfold excess lung cancer risk persists even 20 years after quitting. Small-airway changes in young smokers reverse after 1-2 years of abstinence; in established airflow obstruction cessation mainly slows further decline rather than restoring lung function. Quitting is typically cyclical with multiple attempts: about 70-80% of smokers want to quit and over half try in a given year, but only about 6% remain abstinent at 6 months and about 3% at 2 years; systemwide tracking and assistance can raise 12-month abstinence to as high as 25%.","pitfalls":["Assuming cigars or pipe tobacco are lower-risk: most cigars are now made like cigarettes and most pipe tobacco is used for roll-your-own, so risks are similar; clinicians should ask about and not recommend them.","Treating 'low tar/nicotine' cigarettes as safer; compensatory smoking negates any benefit and design changes have raised adenocarcinoma rates.","Focusing only on cigarettes and missing cumulative nicotine intake from vaping, oral pouches, and other products when assessing addiction intensity.","Overlooking smoking-induced cytochrome P450 changes when adjusting drug doses around hospitalization or discharge.","Dismissing patients not currently interested in quitting; many still attempt cessation in the subsequent year and benefit from a clear personalized message.","Equating exclusive e-cigarette use (lower toxicant exposure) with cessation of addiction, since persistent nicotine dependence and relapse risk remain."],"severityClassification":"No formal grading is presented; addiction intensity is gauged clinically by cumulative daily dosing frequency across all products, spacing of use through the day, use within 30 minutes of waking, craving during abstinence, and history of failed quit attempts.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3681","Harrison 22e p.3682","Harrison 22e p.3683","Harrison 22e p.3684","Harrison 22e p.3685"],"crossLinks":[]},"nipah_virus":{"name":"Nipah virus infection","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NiV","Nipah encephalitis","Nipah virus disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A highly lethal zoonotic paramyxovirus (henipavirus) whose natural reservoir is fruit bats (Pteropus species)","In India transmission has been linked to consumption of date-palm sap contaminated by bat secretions and to close contact with infected patients, with documented person-to-person and nosocomial spread","Presents as rapidly progressive encephalitis with fever, headache, altered mental status and often prominent brainstem signs; respiratory involvement may also occur","Case fatality is very high, and survivors may suffer residual neurological deficits or relapsing/late-onset encephalitis","No licensed antiviral or vaccine is established; management is supportive with rigorous infection control","Strict barrier precautions, patient isolation and contact tracing are essential because human-to-human transmission drives outbreaks","It is classified as a priority pathogen of pandemic potential requiring immediate public-health notification","Diagnosis uses RT-PCR on respiratory secretions, CSF, urine or blood, and serology (IgM/IgG ELISA), performed in high-containment laboratories"],"pathophysiology":"Nipah virus is an enveloped RNA henipavirus that infects endothelium and neurons. After spillover from fruit bats (directly, via contaminated food, or from intermediate hosts such as pigs), the virus causes a systemic vasculitis with endothelial infection, syncytium formation, thrombosis and microinfarction, particularly in the central nervous system. This vasculitic and direct neuronal injury produces the severe encephalitis, while pulmonary endothelial and epithelial infection can cause acute respiratory distress. The capacity of the virus to persist may underlie relapsing or delayed-onset encephalitis in some survivors.","additionalDifferentials":["Japanese encephalitis and other viral encephalitides","Herpes simplex encephalitis","Cerebral malaria","Bacterial or tuberculous meningoencephalitis","Scrub typhus with CNS involvement","Leptospirosis with meningoencephalitis"],"infectionMimics":["Autoimmune encephalitis","Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis","Severe sepsis with encephalopathy"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR of throat/nasal swabs, CSF, urine and blood in a biosafe reference laboratory","Nipah-specific IgM and IgG serology by ELISA","MRI brain, which may show disseminated small cortical and subcortical lesions","CSF examination when safe, typically showing lymphocytic pleocytosis","Chest imaging if respiratory involvement is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly deteriorating consciousness or brainstem dysfunction signalling life-threatening encephalitis","Any respiratory distress raising concern for airborne/droplet transmissibility and severe disease","Cluster of cases or contact with a confirmed case, mandating immediate isolation and outbreak response","Autonomic instability or seizures requiring critical-care support"],"prognosis":"Nipah virus disease is one of the most lethal human encephalitides, with case fatality frequently exceeding half of symptomatic cases. Survivors may be left with persistent neurological impairment, and a minority develop relapsing or late-onset encephalitis months to years later.","pitfalls":["Failing to institute isolation and barrier precautions early, allowing nosocomial and family transmission","Delaying notification to public-health authorities during a suspected cluster","Attributing the illness to more common encephalitides and not sending appropriate high-containment testing","Underestimating the risk to healthcare workers during aerosol-generating procedures"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"nocardiosis":{"name":"Nocardiosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Nocardia infection","Actinomycetoma (Nocardia)"],"chapter":179,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1358-1362","clinicalPearls":["Nocardiosis is usually an opportunistic infection occurring primarily in hosts with impaired defenses, especially defects in cell-mediated immunity and specific phagocyte function; typical risk factors include solid organ or hematopoietic stem cell transplantation, systemic corticosteroids or Cushing syndrome, other immunosuppressive therapy, and HIV infection (p.1358-1359).","Pulmonary disease is the usual portal after inhalation of fragmented mycelia; onset is typically subacute over days to weeks (more acute in the severely immunocompromised), with prominent cough producing thick non-malodorous purulent sputum, fever, anorexia, and malaise (p.1359).","Typical chest imaging shows infiltrates, nodules, and masses in one or more lobes, and nodules frequently cavitate; pleural effusion or empyema occurs in about one-third of cases, occasionally without lung involvement (p.1359).","Dissemination occurs in up to one-third of patients with pneumonia, most often to the CNS and to skin/subcutaneous tissues; virtually any organ can be involved (p.1359-1360).","CNS involvement occurs in up to two-thirds of disseminated cases; brain imaging classically shows one or more ring-enhancing, multilobulated lesions, and multiple abscesses are seen in two-thirds of patients (p.1360).","Diagnosis starts with examining sputum or pus for branching, beaded, Gram-positive filaments (~1 um wide, up to 50 um long) that fragment into apparent cocci/coccobacilli; most nocardiae are modified (weak) acid-fast (e.g., modified Kinyoun, modified Ziehl-Neelsen, Fite-Faraco) (p.1360-1361).","Primary cutaneous nocardiosis follows transcutaneous inoculation (often thorn/gardening injury) and takes three forms: cellulitis, lymphocutaneous (sporotrichoid) syndrome, or actinomycetoma; N. brasiliensis is the usual cause of skin-limited and lymphocutaneous disease and of actinomycetoma (p.1358, p.1360).","Nocardiosis tends to remit and relapse; without definitive treatment, pulmonary disease characteristically follows a course of remissions and exacerbations over weeks to months, and prolonged therapy (months) is required (p.1359, p.1362-Table 179-1)."],"pathophysiology":"Nocardia species are saprophytic, filamentous, aerobic Gram-positive bacteria ubiquitous in soil, water, and decaying organic matter; their cell walls contain mycolic acids and trehalose (Mycobacteriales order), and they are weakly (modified) acid-fast and catalase positive. Pulmonary and systemic disease follow inhalation of fragmented bacterial mycelia, while primary cutaneous and ocular disease follow direct inoculation. Most immunocompetent people clear inhaled nocardiae without disease; invasive infection is favored by impaired host defenses. Innate and adaptive responses, with neutrophils and macrophages central to antinocardial defense (underscored by the frequency of nocardiosis in chronic granulomatous disease), normally control the organism. Nocardiae have evolved mechanisms to survive within phagocytes, including neutralization of oxidants and prevention of phagosome-lysosome fusion and phagosome acidification; neutrophils limit but do not efficiently kill them. The characteristic pathology is an abscess with neutrophilic infiltration and prominent necrosis, with little fibrosis/encapsulation, whereas actinomycetoma shows granulomatous inflammation with sinus tract formation. (p.1358-1359)","additionalDifferentials":["Lymphocutaneous (sporotrichoid) presentation overlaps with Sporothrix schenckii and Mycobacterium marinum infection (p.1360).","Mycetoma differential: actinomycetoma (filamentous bacteria, e.g., Nocardia), eumycetoma (fungal), and botryomycosis (cocci/bacilli, often Staphylococcus aureus) are difficult to separate clinically and require granule microscopy, culture, or biopsy (p.1361).","Ocular nocardiosis (keratitis, scleritis, endophthalmitis) cannot be distinguished from other ocular infections by signs and symptoms alone (p.1360)."],"infectionMimics":["Sporothrix schenckii (sporotrichosis) for lymphocutaneous disease","Mycobacterium marinum for lymphocutaneous disease","Eumycetoma (fungal mycetoma) for actinomycetoma","Botryomycosis (often Staphylococcus aureus) for actinomycetoma","Other causes of pulmonary nodules/cavitation and ring-enhancing brain lesions in immunocompromised hosts (e.g., fungi, mycobacteria, toxoplasmosis as discussed in brain abscess context)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pulmonary or cerebral malignancy/metastases presenting as nodules, masses, or ring-enhancing lesions"],"additionalInvestigations":["Sputum or pus microscopy for branching beaded Gram-positive filaments plus modified (weak-acid) acid-fast staining; organisms may also take up silver stains (p.1360-1361).","Bronchoalveolar lavage fluid or lung biopsy when sputum smears are negative, as they frequently are in pulmonary nocardiosis (p.1361).","Selective media (colistin-nalidixic acid agar, modified Thayer-Martin agar, or buffered charcoal-yeast extract agar) for mixed-flora specimens; avoid mycobacterial decontamination procedures, which can kill nocardiae; alert the laboratory to incubate cultures for prolonged periods (up to 2-4 weeks) (p.1361).","Species/complex identification by MALDI-TOF or genomic sequencing (16S rRNA, secA1, hsp65, or gyrB), with antimicrobial susceptibility testing (ideally CLSI broth dilution); susceptibility testing requires experience because nocardiae grow slowly and clump (p.1361).","Granule examination in mycetoma: wash suspect particles in saline, examine microscopically, and culture; culture is the most reliable way to identify the causative organism (p.1361).","Contrast-enhanced CNS imaging (MRI preferred, with IV gadolinium more sensitive than CT) in all patients with non-cutaneous/non-ocular nocardiosis to detect occult brain abscess (p.1360-1362).","Testing for underlying immunodeficiency in patients lacking typical risk factors, including HIV testing; test for chronic granulomatous disease in children and adults with nocardiosis who have no major risk factor (p.1359, p.1362)."],"redFlags":["CNS dissemination: brain abscess may be clinically silent, so absence of neurologic symptoms does not exclude it - one-third of patients with CNS involvement have no CNS symptoms and one-fourth have no apparent pulmonary disease at presentation (p.1360).","Disseminated nocardiosis carries a higher mortality than localized pulmonary disease, especially with CNS involvement (p.1362).","Nocardiosis in a patient without a major risk factor should prompt evaluation for an undiagnosed immunologic defect, including HIV and chronic granulomatous disease (p.1359, p.1362)."],"prognosis":"With appropriate treatment, mortality for localized pulmonary nocardiosis is less than 10%. Disseminated disease carries a higher mortality, especially with CNS involvement. Primary cutaneous and ocular disease often respond to therapy. Mortality from actinomycetoma is exceedingly low, but patients often suffer substantial morbidity, disfigurement, disability, and/or stigmatization. Prolonged therapy is required and disease tends to relapse, so secondary prophylaxis is considered by some experts for patients who remain at high risk after completing antimicrobial therapy. (p.1362)","pitfalls":["Failing to image the brain: because brain abscess may be asymptomatic, all patients with pulmonary or systemic nocardiosis should have contrast-enhanced CNS imaging (MRI preferred) regardless of neurologic symptoms (p.1360, p.1362).","Missing the diagnosis on smear: sputum smears are frequently negative in pulmonary nocardiosis, and the modified (weak) acid-fast technique must be used or organisms may be overlooked; standard mycobacterial decontamination can kill nocardiae (p.1360-1361).","Cultures lost to short incubation: nocardiae grow slowly (colonies up to 2 weeks, characteristic appearance up to 4 weeks), so the laboratory must be informed to incubate cultures for prolonged periods (p.1361).","Overcalling colonization or contamination: Nocardia can colonize the respiratory tract in bronchiectasis/structural lung disease, and isolation from a respiratory specimen does not always indicate infection; blood culture isolates may be contaminants in low-risk patients without clinical disease (p.1361).","Adjunctive topical corticosteroids should NOT be given to patients with nocardial keratitis (p.1362)."],"severityClassification":"Disease is categorized clinically and by host status to guide therapy duration: pulmonary/systemic disease in hosts with intact defenses versus deficient defenses, CNS disease, primary cutaneous disease (cellulitis, lymphocutaneous), osteomyelitis/arthritis/laryngitis/sinusitis, actinomycetoma, and keratitis - longer courses for immunocompromised and CNS disease, with indefinite therapy considered in some AIDS patients with CD4 below 200/uL or chronic granulomatous disease (p.1362). [Durations noted as classification context only; not dosing guidance.]","references":["Harrison 22e p.1358-1362"],"crossLinks":["BRAIN_ABSCESS","LUNG_ABSCESS"]},"nocturnal_enuresis":{"name":"Nocturnal Enuresis","system":"Genitourinary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bedwetting","nighttime wetting","sleep enuresis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Nocturnal enuresis is involuntary voiding during sleep in a child old enough to be expected to have night-time bladder control, generally around five years and older.","It is classified as primary when the child has never achieved a sustained period of night-time dryness and secondary when wetting returns after at least six months of dryness.","Monosymptomatic enuresis involves night-time wetting alone, whereas non-monosymptomatic enuresis is accompanied by daytime lower urinary tract symptoms such as urgency, frequency, or daytime wetting.","Common contributing factors include a family history, delayed maturation of arousal and bladder control, reduced overnight bladder capacity, and relative overnight excess of urine production.","Most primary monosymptomatic enuresis is not caused by serious disease, and evaluation is mainly history, examination, and urinalysis to exclude infection and glycosuria.","Secondary enuresis or associated daytime symptoms warrant closer evaluation for causes such as urinary tract infection, constipation, diabetes, or psychosocial stress.","First-line management emphasises reassurance, education, and behavioural measures, with the enuresis alarm being the most effective long-term intervention for motivated families.","Constipation frequently coexists and, when treated, wetting often improves, so bowel habits should always be assessed."],"pathophysiology":"Night-time continence depends on the balance between overnight urine production, bladder storage capacity, and the ability to either suppress bladder contractions during sleep or wake in response to a full bladder. Enuresis arises when this balance fails: some children produce a relatively large volume of urine at night, some have a smaller functional bladder capacity or overactive bladder, and many have a high arousal threshold so a full or contracting bladder does not wake them. These factors, strongly influenced by genetic predisposition and developmental maturation, often coexist. Constipation can reduce functional bladder capacity and provoke bladder overactivity, and secondary enuresis may reflect an acquired medical or psychosocial trigger.","additionalDifferentials":["Urinary tract infection","Constipation with secondary bladder dysfunction","Diabetes mellitus or diabetes insipidus","Overactive bladder or other daytime voiding dysfunction","Obstructive sleep apnoea","Psychosocial stressors (particularly in secondary enuresis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis to screen for infection and glucosuria","Assessment of bowel habits for constipation","Bladder diary of daytime symptoms and voiding pattern","Blood glucose if polyuria or other features suggest diabetes","Renal or bladder imaging only when structural or neurological pathology is suspected"],"redFlags":["New-onset (secondary) enuresis with weight loss, polyuria, or polydipsia","Daytime incontinence, straining, or a poor urinary stream","Recurrent urinary tract infections","Neurological signs or spinal abnormalities","Significant behavioural change or evidence of psychosocial distress or abuse"],"prognosis":"Primary monosymptomatic enuresis has a high rate of spontaneous resolution with age, and treatment can accelerate dryness; the outlook is generally excellent, though relapses can occur and cases with daytime symptoms may need more prolonged management.","pitfalls":["Treating the child punitively or blaming the child for an involuntary problem","Overlooking coexisting constipation as a contributor","Failing to distinguish monosymptomatic from non-monosymptomatic enuresis before choosing therapy","Missing an underlying cause of secondary enuresis such as infection or diabetes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"non_hodgkin_lymphoma":{"name":"Non-Hodgkin's Lymphoma","system":"Oncology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NHL"],"chapter":113,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.857","clinicalPearls":["NHLs are cancers of mature B, T, and NK cells; ~90% are of B-cell origin. They are distinguished from Hodgkin's lymphoma by the absence of the Reed-Sternberg cell and differ from HL in biology, natural history, and the more variable prognosis (Harrison 22e p.857).","Clinically, NHL is grouped by behavior: indolent/slow-growing (e.g., follicular lymphoma, the most common indolent NHL), aggressive (DLBCL, the most common subtype overall, ~one-third of cases), and highly aggressive/most rapidly growing (Burkitt's lymphoma, doubling time <24 h). Mantle cell lymphoma is intermediate-grade (Harrison 22e p.857, 862).","The duration and pace of symptoms help distinguish aggressive from indolent disease, as does the presence or absence of 'B' symptoms — fevers, drenching night sweats, and unexplained weight loss (>10% of body weight over the 6 months before diagnosis) (Harrison 22e p.860-861).","Most common presentation of follicular lymphoma is new, painless lymphadenopathy at multiple sites; most patients lack elevated LDH or B symptoms. Asymptomatic indolent disease should be observed, as treating asymptomatic patients does not improve survival (Harrison 22e p.863-864).","Serum LDH and serum beta-2-microglobulin are important independent prognostic factors in NHL and should be measured at diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.860).","Aggressive lymphomas (DLBCL, Burkitt's) are potentially curable with combination chemotherapy, whereas most indolent lymphomas are very responsive to therapy but not ultimately curable in the vast majority of cases except in localized early-stage disease (Harrison 22e p.862-863)."],"pathophysiology":"NHLs arise from mature B, T, or NK lymphocytes, with ~90% being B-cell neoplasms; transformation typically requires several genetic events. Characteristic chromosomal translocations create fusion or deregulated oncogenes: t(14;18) with BCL2 overexpression in follicular lymphoma (>85% of cases, necessary but not sufficient for transformation); MYC rearrangement at 8q24 in >95% of Burkitt's lymphoma (most often t(8;14) fusing MYC to IgH); and t(11;18) (API2-MLT) in MALT lymphoma. Gene-expression profiling subdivides DLBCL into germinal-center B-cell (GCB)-like and activated-B-cell (ABC)-like subtypes with differing prognoses. Several NHLs are linked to chronic antigenic stimulation or infection-driven immune dysregulation, and immunodeficiency states strongly predispose to (often EBV-associated) lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.857-863).","additionalDifferentials":["Hodgkin's lymphoma (distinguished by Reed-Sternberg cells)","Reactive follicular hyperplasia (major differential for follicular lymphoma on biopsy)","Lymphoid leukemias (CLL/SLL, ALL), plasma cell disorders (multiple myeloma)","Other causes of lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, or B-symptom-like constitutional illness"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious mononucleosis / EBV infection and other viral lymphadenopathies","Reactive lymphadenopathy from systemic infection","Multicentric Castleman's disease (HHV-8), which causes diffuse lymphadenopathy with fever, malaise, and weight loss mimicking lymphoma B symptoms"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Autoimmune disease with lymphadenopathy (e.g., Sjögren's syndrome, SLE, rheumatoid arthritis)","Reactive follicular hyperplasia","Sarcoidosis and other causes of constitutional symptoms with adenopathy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Adequate tissue biopsy reviewed by an expert hematopathologist (follicular lymphoma and Burkitt's lymphoma can be diagnosed on morphology; immunophenotyping required) (Harrison 22e p.860, 863)","Complete blood count, routine chemistries, liver function tests, uric acid, calcium","Serum protein electrophoresis (to detect monoclonal paraproteins)","Serum LDH and serum beta-2-microglobulin (prognostic) (Harrison 22e p.860)","CT scan of chest, abdomen, and pelvis (essential for staging, especially indolent lymphomas) (Harrison 22e p.861)","FDG-PET (preferred for aggressive lymphomas such as DLBCL and Burkitt's, and to assess suspected transformation of indolent disease) (Harrison 22e p.861)","Bone marrow biopsy","Lumbar puncture in lymphoblastic, Burkitt's, and DLBCL with positive marrow biopsy or CNS-risk presentations (Harrison 22e p.861)","Screening for HIV and hepatitis B and C at diagnosis (risk factors and risk of HBV reactivation with therapy) (Harrison 22e p.861)"],"redFlags":["Burkitt's lymphoma is highly aggressive (doubling time <24 h) — treatment must be initiated quickly and the tumor carries a high risk of tumor lysis syndrome; TLS is most often associated with treatment of Burkitt's lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.862, 1014)","Rapid growth of lymph nodes with new B symptoms in known indolent (follicular) lymphoma suggests histologic transformation to DLBCL (~3% per year) (Harrison 22e p.864)","Disease involving paranasal sinuses, testes, breast, kidneys, adrenal glands, or epidural space — and highly aggressive histologies like Burkitt's — carries increased risk of CNS dissemination (Harrison 22e p.861)","Concerning neurologic signs/symptoms warrant lumbar puncture for CNS involvement (Harrison 22e p.860)","'Double-hit' high-grade B-cell lymphoma (concurrent MYC and BCL2 rearrangements) has an extremely poor prognosis (median OS ~12-18 months) (Harrison 22e p.862-863)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is highly variable and driven more by histology and clinical parameters than by anatomic stage. Overall 5-year survival for NHL is ~74%. The International Prognostic Index (IPI; age >=60, elevated LDH, ECOG performance status >=2, Ann Arbor stage III/IV, and >1 extranodal site) is the best predictor for aggressive NHL: 5-year overall survival ~73%, 51%, 43%, and 26% for 0-1, 2, 3, and 4-5 risk factors. With R-CHOP, ~65-70% of DLBCL patients are cured. The FLIPI predicts follicular lymphoma outcome; most FL patients live 15-20 years but the disease is generally not curable (except localized early-stage), and Burkitt's lymphoma is curable in ~80-90% when treated promptly and correctly (Harrison 22e p.857, 861-862).","pitfalls":["Diagnosis requires an adequate tissue sample interpreted by an expert hematopathologist; an inadequate or unrepresentative specimen risks misclassification (e.g., missing coexisting DLBCL within follicular lymphoma or confusing lymphoma with reactive follicular hyperplasia) (Harrison 22e p.860, 863).","The Ann Arbor anatomic staging system is less useful in NHL than in Hodgkin's lymphoma because NHL disseminates widely rather than in an ordered stepwise fashion; histology and the IPI matter more than stage (Harrison 22e p.861).","Treating asymptomatic indolent (follicular) lymphoma does not improve survival — appropriate patients should be observed rather than treated immediately (Harrison 22e p.864).","Failing to anticipate and prevent tumor lysis syndrome before treating rapidly proliferating Burkitt's lymphoma can be life-threatening (Harrison 22e p.862).","A residual mass on CT after therapy may represent treated fibrosis rather than active disease; PET can help distinguish, but end-of-treatment PET should be delayed (preferably 6-8 weeks after chemotherapy) to avoid false positives (Harrison 22e p.861)."],"severityClassification":"WHO (WHO-HAEM5) classification of lymphoid malignancies categorizes NHL by cell of origin (mature B-cell vs mature T/NK-cell neoplasms) and entity. Anatomic extent is described by the Ann Arbor staging system (Stage I-IV, with suffix A/B for absence/presence of B symptoms). Prognostic stratification uses the International Prognostic Index (IPI) for aggressive NHL and the FLIPI for follicular lymphoma; follicular lymphoma is also graded I-III by centroblasts per high-power field (Harrison 22e p.857-862).","references":["Harrison 22e p.857"],"crossLinks":["hodgkin_lymphoma","infectious_mononucleosis"]},"nontuberculous_mycobacterial_infection":{"name":"Nontuberculous Mycobacterial Infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NTM","MAC","Mycobacterium avium complex"],"chapter":185,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1414-1418","clinicalPearls":["NTM (also termed atypical, environmental, or mycobacteria other than tuberculosis) are ubiquitous in soil and water and refer to mycobacteria other than M. tuberculosis and its close relatives and M. leprae; over 199 species are recognized (p.1414).","Most NTM cause human disease only when host defense is impaired (e.g., bronchiectasis) or breached by inoculation (liposuction, trauma, cardiac surgery); human-to-human transmission is rare and occurs almost exclusively in cystic fibrosis (p.1414).","Pulmonary disease is by far the most common form in North America and is highly associated with pulmonary epithelial defects but NOT with systemic immunodeficiency; disseminated disease instead denotes significant immune dysfunction such as advanced HIV (p.1414, 1416).","Pulmonary MAC most commonly presents in the 6th-7th decade in women with months to years of nagging intermittent cough and fatigue; pulmonary NTM in a tall, thin woman with chest-wall abnormalities is termed 'Lady Windermere syndrome' (p.1416).","Pulmonary MAC affects older nonsmoking white women more than men (onset ~60 years); such patients tend to be taller and thinner with high rates of scoliosis, mitral valve prolapse, and pectus anomalies (p.1416).","Male smokers with upper-lobe cavitary disease tend to carry the same single MAC strain indefinitely, whereas nonsmoking women with nodular bronchiectasis carry several MAC strains simultaneously that change over the disease course (p.1416).","M. kansasii can produce a clinical syndrome strongly resembling tuberculosis with hemoptysis, chest pain, and cavitary lung disease (p.1416).","Disseminated MAC (or M. kansasii) in advanced HIV is now uncommon in North America owing to effective prophylaxis and improved HIV treatment; when common, the portal of entry was the bowel with spread to bone marrow and bloodstream (p.1415).","Disseminated NTM is often indolent and progressive over weeks to months with malaise, fever, weight loss, organomegaly, lymphadenopathy, and anemia; blood cultures may be negative even though involved organs carry heavy organism burdens, sometimes with grossly impaired granulomatous response (p.1415).","Isolated cervical lymphadenopathy is the most common form of NTM infection in young children in North America (usually MAC); the swelling is firm and relatively painless with few systemic signs and is often diagnosed inadvertently at biopsy for suspected malignancy (p.1416).","Children with isolated cervical or intrathoracic NTM infection do NOT commonly have underlying immune defects and usually do NOT go on to develop other opportunistic infections (p.1416).","Cutaneous NTM usually requires a break in the skin; rapidly growing NTM (M. abscessus, M. fortuitum, M. chelonae) cause outbreaks via contaminated surgical instruments/injections, with painful erythematous draining subcutaneous nodules typically WITHOUT fever or systemic symptoms (p.1416).","M. marinum (fish tanks, pools, fish handling) causes 'fish-tank granuloma' and can spread up the arm in a sporotrichoid pattern; M. ulcerans causes painless Buruli ulcer via the toxin mycolactone (p.1416).","'Hot-tub lung' is a pulmonary hypersensitivity reaction to NTM (most often MAC) growing in underchlorinated water, distinct from true pulmonary or disseminated infection (p.1415).","For susceptibility testing, only MAC testing for clarithromycin and M. kansasii testing for rifampin is indicated; few data support other in vitro susceptibility tests, and MAC isolates not previously exposed to macrolides are almost always susceptible (p.1417)."],"pathophysiology":"NTM are ubiquitous environmental organisms (soil and water) with specific niches (e.g., M. simiae in aquifers, M. fortuitum in pedicure baths, M. immunogenum in metalworking fluids). Because exposure is essentially universal yet disease is rare, normal host defenses are strong and significant disease implies a specific susceptibility. Mycobacteria are phagocytosed by macrophages, which release IL-12; IL-12 activates T and NK cells (via STAT4) to secrete IFN-gamma, which activates macrophages/neutrophils (via STAT1) and upregulates TNF-alpha, forming a positive IFN-gamma/IL-12 feedback loop critical for mycobacterial control. Disseminated NTM correlates with declining CD4+ T cells (advanced HIV, idiopathic CD4 lymphocytopenia), TNF-alpha inhibitors (which impair granuloma formation), Mendelian mutations in the IFN-gamma/IL-12 pathway (IFNG, IFNGR1, IFNGR2, STAT1, GATA2, ISG15, IRF8, IL-12A, IL-12RB1, IL-12RB2, CYBB, IKBKG/NEMO and others), or neutralizing anti-IFN-gamma autoantibodies. Only ~70% of non-HIV disseminated cases have a genetic diagnosis. In contrast, the best-recognized substrate for pulmonary NTM is bronchiectasis (cystic fibrosis, primary ciliary dyskinesia, STAT3 hyper-IgE/Job's syndrome, idiopathic), but the mechanism by which bronchiectasis predisposes to locally destructive yet non-systemic disease is unknown (p.1414-1415).","additionalDifferentials":["Pulmonary tuberculosis (M. kansasii closely mimics TB; PPD/IGRA cross-reactivity)","Malignancy (painless cervical adenopathy in children; isolated intrathoracic NTM mistaken for cancer)","Bronchiectasis exacerbation / underlying structural lung disease","Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (predisposes to NTM and Nocardia)","Sporotrichosis (sporotrichoid spread mimicked by M. marinum)"],"infectionMimics":["M. tuberculosis (clinical, radiographic, and AFB-smear overlap)","Nocardia (gram-positive beaded filamentous rods; alveolar proteinosis predisposes to both)","Sporothrix schenckii (sporotrichoid ascending nodules akin to M. marinum)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Malignancy presenting as painless cervical or intrathoracic adenopathy","Hot-tub lung (hypersensitivity pneumonitis-type reaction to NTM rather than invasive infection)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Acid-fast or fluorochrome smears of sputum or body fluids (organisms may appear as beaded gram-positive rods at high burden, but this is unreliable)","Mycobacterial culture using separate processing and dedicated blood culture media; differentiate rapidly growing (<7 days) from slowly growing (>=7 days) species; some labs hold cultures 6 weeks","Liquid culture media for faster isolation; lower incubation temperature (30C) for M. marinum, M. haemophilum, M. ulcerans; special additives (iron/blood for M. haemophilum, mycobactin J for M. genavense)","Species-level identification by biochemical tests, nucleic acid probes/sequencing, HPLC or mass spectrometry; molecular detection for noncultivable species (e.g., M. tilburgii)","Chest CT (tree-in-bud bronchiolar pattern, lingular and right-middle-lobe involvement, bronchiectasis, cavitation)","IGRA (note M. marinum, M. kansasii, M. szulgai carry ESAT6/CFP10 and may cause false-positive IGRA; IGRA is indeterminate with anti-IFN-gamma autoantibodies)","Genetic evaluation of the IFN-gamma/IL-12 pathway and screening for anti-IFN-gamma autoantibodies in disseminated disease without iatrogenic cause; GATA2 evaluation when myelodysplasia coexists"],"redFlags":["Disseminated involvement of two or more organs without an iatrogenic cause (prompts IFN-gamma/IL-12 pathway genetic investigation)","Disseminated NTM with bacterial/viral infections plus conical/peg/missing teeth or abnormal hair pattern in males (suggests NEMO/IKBKG NF-kB defect, possibly with immunoglobulin defects)","Mycobacterial disease with myelodysplasia (investigate GATA2 deficiency)","Disseminated MAC and rapidly growing NTM with other opportunistic infections (e.g., Talaromyces), especially in East Asian women (anti-IFN-gamma autoantibodies)","Infected indwelling IV catheters/lines (M. abscessus, M. fortuitum from contaminated water) requiring catheter removal"],"prognosis":"NTM cause chronic infections evolving slowly over weeks to years, so emergent therapy before diagnosis and speciation is rarely needed. Treatment is complex, often poorly tolerated, and potentially toxic; inadequate single-drug therapy is almost always associated with emergence of resistance and relapse. Pulmonary MAC therapy is prolonged, generally continued for 12 months after culture conversion (typically at least 18 months total). The typical lag between symptom onset and diagnosis is about 5 years in older women (p.1416-1418).","pitfalls":["Not all NTM isolations, especially from the lung, reflect disease or require treatment; distinguishing colonization/contamination from true infection requires an appropriate clinical and radiographic setting (nodules, bronchiectasis, cavities) plus repeated isolation per American Thoracic Society criteria (p.1417).","The single most critical and sensitive step in diagnosis is simply to suspect NTM and request the correct, separately processed mycobacterial smears, cultures, and dedicated blood culture media; many cases go undiagnosed because the appropriate test was never ordered (p.1416-1417).","M. gordonae is frequently recovered from respiratory samples but is almost never a pathogen (p.1417).","PPD does not differentiate NTM from M. tuberculosis well; intermediate (~10 mm) reactions overlap, BCG cross-reacts, but large reactions (>15 mm) most commonly signify TB (p.1416-1417).","Slow-growing NTM typically do NOT proliferate in routine blood culture media, so suspicion and correct bottles are essential; biopsy isolation is strong evidence but laboratory contamination does occur (confirm with organisms on stained sections) (p.1417)."],"severityClassification":"American Thoracic Society criteria for pulmonary NTM diagnosis: growth of NTM from two of three sputum samples (regardless of smear), OR a positive bronchoalveolar lavage sample (regardless of smear), OR a lung parenchymal biopsy with granulomatous inflammation or mycobacteria on section plus NTM on culture, in an appropriate clinical and radiographic setting. Isolation of NTM from blood or organ biopsy in a compatible clinical setting is considered diagnostic. NTM are categorized as rapidly growing (<7 days) versus slowly growing (>=7 days) (p.1417).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1414-1418"],"crossLinks":["PULMONARY_TB","BRONCHIECTASIS_EXACERBATION"]},"normal_pressure_hydrocephalus":{"name":"Normal pressure hydrocephalus","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NPH","idiopathic normal pressure hydrocephalus","Hakim-Adams syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically presents with the triad of gait disturbance, cognitive impairment, and urinary incontinence, though not all three are always present","Gait difficulty is usually the earliest and most prominent feature and is often described as magnetic, broad-based and shuffling, sometimes termed a gait apraxia","It is one of the few potentially reversible causes of dementia, so recognition matters because shunting can help selected patients","Imaging shows ventricular enlargement out of proportion to cortical atrophy, and disproportionate widening of the Sylvian fissures relative to compressed high-convexity sulci is characteristic","Cerebrospinal fluid opening pressure is within the normal range, distinguishing it from obstructive high-pressure hydrocephalus","A high-volume lumbar puncture (tap test) or extended lumbar drainage that transiently improves gait helps predict a favourable response to shunting","Gait tends to respond best to shunting, cognition variably, and long-standing incontinence least reliably","The condition predominantly affects older adults and is frequently misattributed to ageing or Parkinson disease"],"pathophysiology":"Normal pressure hydrocephalus is a communicating hydrocephalus in which impaired cerebrospinal fluid absorption leads to ventricular enlargement despite normal mean intracranial pressure. Intermittent pressure waves and altered periventricular compliance are thought to distort the corona radiata fibres, particularly those serving the legs and bladder, producing the characteristic clinical triad. It may be idiopathic or secondary to prior subarachnoid haemorrhage, meningitis, or head injury that scars the arachnoid granulations.","additionalDifferentials":["Alzheimer disease and other neurodegenerative dementias","Parkinson disease and vascular parkinsonism","Cerebral small vessel disease with vascular cognitive impairment","Obstructive hydrocephalus from a mass lesion","Cervical or lumbar spondylotic myelopathy affecting gait"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain to assess ventricular size, callosal angle, and the DESH pattern of disproportionate CSF distribution","Large-volume lumbar puncture with pre- and post-tap gait assessment","Extended external lumbar drainage in equivocal cases to predict shunt responsiveness","Neuropsychological testing to characterise the cognitive profile","Assessment for comorbid neurodegenerative or vascular disease that may limit surgical benefit"],"redFlags":["The full gait, cognitive and continence triad in an older adult should prompt evaluation for a treatable, potentially reversible cause rather than being dismissed as ageing","Rapidly progressive deterioration or focal deficits suggesting an obstructing mass or alternative diagnosis","Headache, vomiting or reduced consciousness indicating raised pressure and possible acute obstruction","Shunt malfunction or infection after surgery presenting with fever or acute neurological decline"],"prognosis":"Carefully selected patients, especially those with predominant early gait impairment and a positive tap or drainage test, often gain meaningful and sometimes durable benefit from ventriculoperitoneal shunting. Prognosis is worse when significant coexisting neurodegenerative or vascular disease is present or when symptoms are long-standing.","pitfalls":["Attributing the syndrome to normal ageing or a primary dementia and missing a reversible cause","Selecting patients for shunting on imaging alone without supportive CSF drainage testing","Underestimating the surgical risks of shunting, including subdural collections, infection, and over- or under-drainage","Expecting equal improvement in all three symptoms when gait typically responds best and incontinence least"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"nstemi":{"name":"Non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["NSTEMI","non-ST-segment elevation MI","NSTE-ACS","subendocardial infarction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["NSTEMI is an acute coronary syndrome defined by myocardial necrosis, shown by a rise and/or fall in cardiac troponin, without persistent ST-segment elevation","The ECG typically shows ST depression, transient ST elevation, or T-wave inversion, but may be normal early in the course","It is distinguished from unstable angina solely by the presence of detectable troponin release indicating cell death","It must be separated from STEMI, which requires immediate reperfusion, whereas NSTEMI is managed with an early risk-guided invasive strategy","Serial high-sensitivity troponin with a defined delta over time increases diagnostic accuracy and speeds rule-in or rule-out","Core therapy includes dual antiplatelet therapy, anticoagulation, and anti-ischaemic and secondary-prevention agents, with timing of angiography guided by risk","Higher-risk patients (ongoing ischaemia, haemodynamic or electrical instability, high risk scores) benefit from earlier invasive management","A rise in troponin is not specific to type 1 plaque-rupture infarction and can reflect supply-demand mismatch (type 2) or non-ischaemic myocardial injury"],"pathophysiology":"NSTEMI most commonly arises from rupture or erosion of a coronary plaque with partially occlusive thrombus, or from severe fixed stenosis with superimposed thrombosis, producing prolonged ischaemia sufficient to cause myocyte necrosis. Because flow is reduced rather than completely and persistently interrupted, injury is often subendocardial and the ECG lacks persistent ST elevation. Distal embolisation of platelet-fibrin material and dynamic vasoconstriction contribute. Type 2 infarction reflects an imbalance between oxygen supply and demand (for example from tachyarrhythmia, anaemia, hypotension, or hypertensive crisis) without acute plaque rupture.","additionalDifferentials":["Unstable angina","STEMI","Type 2 myocardial infarction from supply-demand mismatch","Myocarditis","Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy","Pulmonary embolism","Aortic dissection","Non-ischaemic causes of troponin elevation such as sepsis or renal failure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial high-sensitivity cardiac troponin with interval delta measurement","Serial 12-lead ECGs to detect dynamic or evolving changes","Continuous rhythm monitoring for arrhythmia and ischaemia","Echocardiography for regional wall-motion abnormalities and ventricular function","Risk stratification scores to determine timing of invasive angiography","Coronary angiography as part of an early invasive strategy in higher-risk patients"],"redFlags":["MEDICAL EMERGENCY: NSTEMI is an acute coronary syndrome with established myocardial necrosis requiring urgent admission, monitoring, and antithrombotic therapy","Refractory or recurrent ischaemic pain despite optimal medical therapy","Haemodynamic instability, cardiogenic shock, or acute heart failure","Sustained ventricular arrhythmia or high-grade conduction block","New mechanical complications such as acute mitral regurgitation"],"prognosis":"Outcomes depend on infarct size, coronary anatomy, left ventricular function, and comorbidities; timely risk-based invasive management and secondary prevention improve survival, though NSTEMI carries meaningful medium- and long-term mortality, sometimes exceeding STEMI over the longer term because of older age and comorbidity burden.","pitfalls":["Interpreting any raised troponin as type 1 infarction without considering type 2 injury or non-ischaemic causes","Mislabelling an NSTEMI as STEMI or vice versa and applying the wrong reperfusion pathway","Relying on a single early troponin or ECG to exclude infarction","Delaying dual antiplatelet and anticoagulant therapy while awaiting angiography in eligible patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"nutritional_rickets":{"name":"Nutritional Rickets","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["rickets","vitamin D deficiency rickets","calcipenic rickets"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A disorder of defective mineralisation of growing bone, most commonly due to deficiency of vitamin D and/or dietary calcium.","Risk factors include exclusive breastfeeding without vitamin D supplementation, darker skin pigmentation, limited sun exposure, and prematurity.","Skeletal signs include widening of the wrists and ankles, a rachitic rosary at the costochondral junctions, frontal bossing, delayed fontanelle closure, and bowing of the legs once weight-bearing begins.","Non-skeletal features can include delayed motor milestones, hypotonia, growth faltering, and increased susceptibility to fractures.","Severe hypocalcaemia may present with tetany, carpopedal spasm, or seizures, especially in infants.","Biochemistry classically shows low or low-normal calcium and phosphate, elevated alkaline phosphatase, raised parathyroid hormone, and low 25-hydroxyvitamin D.","Radiographs of the wrist or knee reveal metaphyseal cupping, fraying, and widening of the growth plate.","Treatment replaces vitamin D and ensures adequate dietary calcium, with biochemical and radiological healing confirming response.","Prevention through routine vitamin D supplementation in infancy is highly effective, and failure to respond should prompt evaluation for inherited or renal forms."],"pathophysiology":"Vitamin D deficiency reduces intestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate, lowering their availability for bone mineralisation. The falling calcium stimulates parathyroid hormone secretion (secondary hyperparathyroidism), which mobilises calcium from bone and promotes renal phosphate wasting. The combination of low calcium-phosphate product and PTH-driven bone resorption leaves the expanding cartilage of the growth plate and newly formed osteoid unmineralised, producing the soft, deformable bones and characteristic metaphyseal changes of rickets.","additionalDifferentials":["Hypophosphataemic (vitamin D-resistant) rickets","Vitamin D-dependent rickets (types 1 and 2)","Renal osteodystrophy from chronic kidney disease","Hypophosphatasia","Osteogenesis imperfecta","Skeletal dysplasias and physiologic bowing"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium, phosphate, and alkaline phosphatase","25-hydroxyvitamin D level","Parathyroid hormone","Renal function and, where indicated, urine calcium and phosphate","Wrist or knee radiographs for metaphyseal changes","1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D in atypical or treatment-resistant cases"],"redFlags":["Hypocalcaemic seizures, tetany, or laryngospasm","Cardiomyopathy associated with severe hypocalcaemia or vitamin D deficiency","Pathological fractures","Failure to respond to appropriate vitamin D and calcium therapy","Progressive deformity or signs suggesting an inherited or renal cause"],"prognosis":"With adequate treatment, biochemical recovery occurs within weeks and radiographic healing over months; most deformities remodel in young children, though severe or late-treated cases may leave residual bowing.","pitfalls":["Attributing bowed legs solely to physiologic causes and missing biochemical rickets","Overlooking dietary calcium deficiency as a cause even when vitamin D is adequate","Failing to investigate inherited or renal rickets when there is no response to treatment","Not providing routine vitamin D supplementation to exclusively breastfed infants"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"obesity":{"name":"Obesity","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["overweight"],"chapter":413,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3181","clinicalPearls":["Obesity is defined as a state of excess adipose tissue mass that adversely affects health; because direct measurement of fat mass is impractical in routine clinical practice, the body mass index (BMI = weight/height² in kg/m²) is used as a proxy (Harrison 22e p.3181).","WHO BMI classes: underweight <18.5; normal 18.5–24.9; overweight 25–29.9; obesity ≥30 kg/m². AACE/ACE management staging further subdivides obesity into class I (30.0–34.9), class II (35.0–39.9), and class III / extreme obesity (≥40) with correspondingly higher disease risk (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187).","BMI-based cutoffs were derived largely from white Western populations; adverse outcomes occur at lower BMI in other ethnic groups, so lower thresholds and ethnic-specific waist-circumference cutpoints (e.g., South Asians/Chinese, Japanese) are recommended (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187).","Fat distribution matters independent of overall BMI: a waist-to-hip ratio >0.9 in women and >1.0 in men, and excess abdominal (visceral) adiposity, are independently associated with type 2 diabetes, dyslipidemia, metabolic syndrome, and cardiovascular disease (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187).","Energy balance is under homeostatic control by the hypothalamus, which integrates the adipose-derived hormone leptin (rises with fat stores, falls fast with caloric restriction) with gut signals — GLP-1, CCK, GIP, PYY, oxyntomodulin, ghrelin — plus pancreatic insulin and amylin (Harrison 22e p.3181–3182).","Insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia is one of the earliest and most prominent biochemical abnormalities of obesity, is worse with central adiposity, and underlies/precedes many adverse cardiometabolic consequences (Harrison 22e p.3186).","Two leading (not mutually exclusive) hypotheses link obesity to insulin resistance: adipose-tissue inflammation (macrophage accumulation around dying adipocytes), and ectopic lipid deposition once safe adipose storage capacity is exceeded — with stronger human data supporting the ectopic-fat / limited-storage mechanism (Harrison 22e p.3186).","Obesity care begins with sensitive, non-stigmatizing language and an obesity-focused history covering diet, physical activity, sleep, stress, contributing factors, complications, and the patient's readiness/motivation to change (Harrison 22e p.3187–3188)."],"pathophysiology":"Adipose tissue evolved to store excess calories as triglyceride in unilocular lipid droplets and release free fatty acids and glycerol when needed. In an obesogenic environment (abundant energy-dense palatable food, larger portions, reduced physical activity), chronic energy intake exceeding expenditure — even by as little as ~7 kcal/day sustained over years — produces a cumulative excess of adipose triglyceride. Genetic factors strongly determine individual susceptibility (twin studies show adiposity tracks with biological, not adoptive, family), acting largely through the brain's control of food intake. Body weight is homeostatically defended by the hypothalamus, which integrates the adipose hormone leptin (whose fall below an individual's habitual level is a powerful drive to eat) with gut hormones (GLP-1, CCK, GIP, PYY, oxyntomodulin, ghrelin) and pancreatic insulin/amylin. Within the arcuate nucleus, leptin stimulates POMC neurons (whose products α-/β-MSH act via MC4R in the paraventricular nucleus to reduce intake and raise energy expenditure) and inhibits orexigenic AGRP/NPY/GABA neurons. Leptin defends strongly against fat loss but responds weakly to fat excess (\"leptin resistance\"), explaining why rising leptin does not prevent obesity. As obesity develops, the earliest abnormality is insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia, worse with central fat; this arises from adipose-tissue inflammation and/or ectopic lipid deposition in liver and muscle once safe adipose storage capacity is exceeded (the human data favouring the ectopic-fat mechanism, as illustrated by lipodystrophy). Insulin resistance/hyperinsulinemia then predisposes to the metabolic, endocrine, and cardiovascular complications of obesity (Harrison 22e p.3181–3186).","additionalDifferentials":["Hypothyroidism — may cause weight gain but is rarely the sole cause of severe obesity; check TSH (and free thyroxine for rare secondary hypothyroidism) in any patient presenting with obesity (Harrison 22e p.3185, 3188)","Cushing's syndrome / endogenous hypercortisolism — suggested by spontaneous bruising, livid striae, myopathy and marked centripetal fat distribution; confirmed via 24-h urinary free cortisol or dexamethasone suppression (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Hypothalamic obesity — from tumors (e.g., craniopharyngioma), inflammatory masses, or severe head injury disrupting hypothalamic energy-balance centres, often with anterior/posterior pituitary dysfunction and short history of hyperphagia (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Insulinoma — weight gain driven by frequent eating to avoid hypoglycemia (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Polycystic ovarian syndrome — secondary cause/association meriting evaluation (Harrison 22e p.3188)","Monogenic / syndromic obesity — leptin or leptin-receptor deficiency, POMC, PCSK1, MC4R, SIM1, BDNF/TrkB, SH2B1 mutations; syndromes such as Prader-Willi, Bardet-Biedl, Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy/pseudohypoparathyroidism, Cohen's, Carpenter's, Alström's, Tubby (Harrison 22e p.3183–3185)","Drug-induced weight gain — insulin, sulfonylureas, thiazolidinediones, glucocorticoids, antipsychotics (clozapine, olanzapine, risperidone), lithium, antidepressants (tricyclics, MAOIs, paroxetine, mirtazapine), antiepileptics (valproate, gabapentin, carbamazepine) (Harrison 22e p.3188)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Very muscular body habitus — BMI may overestimate body fat (Harrison 22e p.3181)","Peripheral edema from NSAIDs or calcium channel blockers — increases weight without increasing body fat (Harrison 22e p.3188)"],"additionalInvestigations":["BMI calculation plus waist circumference (surrogate for visceral fat; measure horizontally above the iliac crest, particularly when BMI ≤35) and/or waist-to-hip ratio (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187)","Fasting lipid profile (total, LDL, HDL cholesterol and triglycerides), chemistry panel, glycated haemoglobin, and blood pressure in all patients (Harrison 22e p.3187)","TSH ± free thyroxine to evaluate for hypothyroidism (Harrison 22e p.3185, 3188)","24-h urinary free cortisol or dexamethasone suppression test when Cushing's syndrome is suspected (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Genetic testing in severe early-onset obesity (up to ~20% of children with severe obesity have chromosomal abnormalities or highly penetrant mutations) (Harrison 22e p.3183)"],"redFlags":["Short history of hyperphagia and rapid weight gain with signs of anterior/posterior pituitary dysfunction — suggests hypothalamic damage from tumor, inflammation, or head injury (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Spontaneous bruising, livid striae, proximal myopathy, marked centripetal fat — suggests Cushing's syndrome rather than common obesity (Harrison 22e p.3185)","Severe early-onset obesity in childhood with intense hyperphagia/food-seeking — prompts screening for monogenic/syndromic causes (Harrison 22e p.3183–3185)","Severe hypertriglyceridemia secondary to insulin resistance — risk of pancreatitis (Harrison 22e p.3186)"],"prognosis":"Obesity is a serious, highly heterogeneous chronic disease that increases morbidity and mortality across many organ systems. The number and severity of complications generally rise with increasing BMI, and excess abdominal/visceral fat confers added cardiometabolic risk. As BMI rises by 5 kg/m², cancer mortality increases by ~10%; in some countries obesity has overtaken smoking as the leading risk factor for cancer. People with obesity have increased morbidity and mortality from atherothrombotic vascular disease (coronary artery disease, stroke) even without diabetes, and are at increased risk of heart failure (especially diastolic dysfunction) and atrial fibrillation. Modest, sustained weight loss (an initial goal of ~8–10% over 6 months) improves obesity-related complications and quality of life; some complications, including type 2 diabetes (e.g., the DiRECT trial showed remission in nearly half of participants with intensive low-energy dietary intervention), can substantially improve or remit with weight loss (Harrison 22e p.3186–3187, 3190).","pitfalls":["Treating BMI alone: BMI only reflects body size, not body composition, fat distribution, health, or quality of life — not all patients with a high BMI need treatment, and BMI overestimates fat in very muscular individuals (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187, 3190).","Applying white-Western-derived BMI cutoffs uniformly: adverse outcomes occur at lower BMI in other ethnic groups, and ethnic-specific (e.g., Asia-Pacific) thresholds should be used (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187).","Overlooking that severe hypothyroidism is rarely the sole cause of severe obesity, yet thyroid function should still always be checked (Harrison 22e p.3185).","Mistaking the lower GH response to provocative testing in common obesity for a structural hypothalamic-pituitary lesion (Harrison 22e p.3186).","Assuming a specific macronutrient ratio is key: weight loss depends primarily on total caloric reduction and adherence, not on the proportion of carbohydrate, fat, or protein (Harrison 22e p.3190).","Using stigmatizing language ('obesity', 'morbid obesity', 'fatness') rather than neutral terms ('weight', 'excess weight', 'unhealthy weight'), which leaves patients feeling judged (Harrison 22e p.3188).","Initiating lifestyle change before assessing readiness, which leads to frustration and may hamper future efforts (Harrison 22e p.3188)."],"severityClassification":"BMI-based (kg/m²): underweight <18.5; healthy/normal weight 18.5–24.9; overweight 25.0–29.9; obesity class I 30.0–34.9 (high disease risk); obesity class II 35.0–39.9 (very high risk); extreme/class III obesity ≥40 (extremely high risk). Risk is refined by excess abdominal fat (ethnic-specific waist-circumference cutpoints; waist-to-hip ratio >0.9 women / >1.0 men). Disease-staging systems augment BMI: the AACE/ACE staging (stage 0 = no complications; stage 1 = mild-moderate complications; stage 2 = at least one severe complication) and the Edmonton Obesity Staging System (EOSS, grades 0–4 across medical, functional, and psychological domains, independent of BMI) (Harrison 22e p.3181, 3187–3188).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3181","Harrison 22e p.3187"],"crossLinks":["diabetes_mellitus","sleep_apnea"]},"obsessive_compulsive_disorder":{"name":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OCD","obsessive compulsive disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by obsessions (intrusive, unwanted, distressing thoughts, images, or urges) and/or compulsions (repetitive behaviours or mental acts performed to reduce anxiety or prevent a feared outcome)","Symptoms are time-consuming or cause marked distress and functional impairment; patients often recognise them as excessive, though insight varies","Common themes include contamination with washing, doubt with checking, symmetry with ordering, and taboo intrusive thoughts","First-line treatment is cognitive behavioural therapy with exposure and response prevention, which is highly effective","Pharmacotherapy principles favour SSRIs, often needing higher doses and longer trials than in depression before judging response","Clomipramine is an effective alternative, and augmentation strategies are considered in resistant cases","Distinguish from obsessive-compulsive personality disorder, which involves ego-syntonic perfectionism and rigidity rather than distressing intrusive symptoms","Assess for comorbid depression, tic disorders, and hoarding, and ask about suicidal ideation"],"pathophysiology":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder is associated with dysfunction of cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical circuits, particularly hyperactivity involving the orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate, and basal ganglia, together with serotonergic and glutamatergic dysregulation. Genetic factors contribute, and in some children symptom onset has been linked to post-streptococcal autoimmune processes.","additionalDifferentials":["Generalized anxiety disorder","Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder","Body dysmorphic disorder","Hoarding disorder","Tic disorders and Tourette syndrome","Illness anxiety disorder","Psychotic disorder when insight is absent"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Standardised OCD severity rating scales to quantify symptoms and monitor treatment","Screening for comorbid depression, anxiety, and tic disorders","Assessment of insight and degree of functional impairment","Consideration of post-streptococcal aetiology in abrupt paediatric onset"],"redFlags":["Suicidal ideation, particularly with comorbid depression","Severe functional incapacity from time-consuming rituals","Complete loss of insight suggesting a psychotic process","Self-harm from compulsions such as excessive washing causing skin breakdown"],"prognosis":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder is typically chronic with waxing and waning severity, but many patients achieve substantial improvement with exposure and response prevention and adequate pharmacotherapy. Early onset, poor insight, and comorbidity are associated with a more persistent course.","pitfalls":["Confusing OCD with obsessive-compulsive personality disorder","Judging SSRI non-response before an adequately high dose and prolonged trial","Providing reassurance that inadvertently reinforces compulsive checking","Overlooking comorbid depression and suicide risk","Failing to offer or refer for exposure and response prevention, the most effective psychotherapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"obstetric_cholestasis":{"name":"Obstetric Cholestasis","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy","ICP","cholestasis of pregnancy","pruritus gravidarum with cholestasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Obstetric cholestasis is a pregnancy-specific liver disorder characterised by pruritus and raised bile acids, typically presenting in the second half of pregnancy","The hallmark symptom is generalised itching, often severe and worse at night, classically involving the palms and soles, in the absence of a primary skin rash","Diagnosis rests on elevated serum bile acids with or without raised transaminases once other causes of itch and abnormal liver function are excluded","The main concern is fetal rather than maternal, as high bile acid concentrations are associated with an increased risk of stillbirth, preterm birth, and meconium-stained liquor","The risk of stillbirth rises with the degree of bile acid elevation, so severity is graded on peak bile acid levels and guides timing of delivery","Ursodeoxycholic acid is commonly used to relieve maternal itching, though its effect on fetal outcome is limited, and vitamin K may be considered if there is prolonged clotting from fat malabsorption","Symptoms and biochemistry resolve after delivery, but the condition recurs in a high proportion of future pregnancies and with oestrogen-containing contraception","Persistent abnormal liver function after delivery should prompt investigation for underlying hepatobiliary disease"],"pathophysiology":"Obstetric cholestasis reflects impaired hepatocellular secretion and enterohepatic circulation of bile acids in genetically susceptible women, in whom the high oestrogen and progesterone levels of pregnancy reduce the function of bile acid transport proteins. This leads to accumulation of bile acids in the maternal circulation, causing pruritus, and their transfer across the placenta is thought to underlie the fetal risks, with high concentrations having arrhythmogenic and vasoconstrictive effects that may contribute to sudden fetal death. Cholestasis can also impair absorption of fat-soluble vitamins including vitamin K.","additionalDifferentials":["Physiological pruritus of pregnancy without cholestasis","Pregnancy-specific skin conditions such as polymorphic eruption or pemphigoid gestationis","Pre-eclampsia or HELLP syndrome with liver involvement","Acute fatty liver of pregnancy","Viral hepatitis or other intrinsic liver disease","Gallstone disease or biliary obstruction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum bile acid measurement, ideally with serial monitoring of peak levels","Liver function tests including transaminases and bilirubin","Coagulation studies where cholestasis is prolonged or severe","Viral hepatitis screen and liver autoantibodies to exclude other liver disease","Liver ultrasound to exclude biliary obstruction","Fetal surveillance, recognising that routine monitoring poorly predicts sudden stillbirth"],"redFlags":["Markedly elevated bile acids indicating higher stillbirth risk and need for planned delivery","Coexisting features of pre-eclampsia or HELLP suggesting an alternative or additional diagnosis","Jaundice, right upper quadrant pain, or systemic upset suggesting significant hepatic pathology","Reduced fetal movements or other signs of fetal compromise","Prolonged clotting from vitamin K deficiency increasing bleeding risk"],"prognosis":"For the mother the condition is usually self-limiting and resolves promptly after birth, though it recurs frequently in later pregnancies and with oestrogen exposure. The principal concern is the fetus, and careful monitoring of bile acid levels with timely delivery reduces perinatal risk; failure of liver function to normalise postpartum should prompt further hepatological assessment.","pitfalls":["Dismissing severe pruritus in pregnancy without checking bile acids and liver function","Failing to exclude pre-eclampsia, HELLP, and acute fatty liver in a woman with abnormal liver tests","Over-reliance on cardiotocography to predict a sudden stillbirth it cannot reliably foresee","Not grading severity by bile acid level to guide timing of delivery","Overlooking persistent postpartum liver dysfunction that signals underlying disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"obstructive_sleep_apnoea":{"name":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OSA","OSAS","obstructive sleep apnoea-hypopnoea syndrome","sleep-disordered breathing"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by repetitive collapse of the upper airway during sleep, producing apnoeas and hypopnoeas with recurrent oxygen desaturations and arousals","Typical features include loud habitual snoring, witnessed breathing pauses, choking or gasping at night, and unrefreshing sleep","Excessive daytime sleepiness, morning headache, poor concentration, and mood disturbance are common daytime consequences","Obesity, increased neck circumference, male sex, retrognathia, and enlarged tonsils are important predisposing factors","Polysomnography is the diagnostic standard, with severity graded by the apnoea-hypopnoea index; home sleep testing can be used in selected patients","Continuous positive airway pressure is the mainstay of therapy for moderate-to-severe disease, splinting the airway open during sleep","Weight reduction, avoidance of alcohol and sedatives, positional therapy, and treatment of nasal obstruction are useful adjuncts","Untreated OSA is linked to hypertension, arrhythmias, ischaemic heart disease, stroke, insulin resistance, and increased motor-vehicle accident risk"],"pathophysiology":"During sleep, reduced tone of the pharyngeal dilator muscles allows the anatomically narrowed or collapsible upper airway to close, producing partial (hypopnoea) or complete (apnoea) obstruction despite continued respiratory effort. The resulting hypoxaemia, hypercapnia, and rising respiratory effort trigger brief cortical arousals that restore airway patency but fragment sleep. Recurrent cycles of desaturation-reoxygenation and arousal drive sympathetic activation, surges in blood pressure, oxidative stress, and systemic inflammation, which over time contribute to cardiovascular and metabolic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Central sleep apnoea","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Narcolepsy and other primary hypersomnias","Insufficient sleep or poor sleep hygiene","Hypothyroidism-related somnolence","Depression-related fatigue"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["In-laboratory polysomnography to confirm diagnosis and grade severity","Home sleep apnoea testing in appropriately selected patients without significant comorbidity","Overnight or nocturnal oximetry as a screening adjunct","Validated questionnaires and sleepiness scales to assess pretest probability and symptom burden","Assessment for associated conditions including hypertension, thyroid dysfunction, and metabolic disease"],"redFlags":["Severe daytime sleepiness in occupational drivers or machine operators posing safety risk","Signs of daytime hypoventilation or right heart failure suggesting overlap with hypoventilation syndromes","Resistant hypertension or nocturnal arrhythmias","Rapidly progressive symptoms or marked nocturnal desaturation"],"prognosis":"With consistent adherence to positive airway pressure and weight management, symptoms and quality of life usually improve markedly and cardiovascular risk is reduced; poorly controlled or untreated disease carries substantial long-term cardiovascular, metabolic, and accident-related morbidity.","pitfalls":["Attributing daytime sleepiness solely to lifestyle and missing significant OSA","Overlooking OSA as a driver of resistant hypertension or atrial fibrillation","Assuming all patients are obese; OSA can occur with normal body habitus due to craniofacial anatomy","Failing to address poor CPAP adherence, which undermines treatment benefit"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"occupational_lung_disease":{"name":"Occupational and Environmental Lung Disease","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pneumoconiosis","silicosis","asbestosis"],"chapter":300,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2234","clinicalPearls":["A detailed occupational and environmental exposure history is the single most important step; include hobbies, home characteristics, secondhand smoke, and proximity to traffic/industry, plus short- and long-term exposures in the distant past (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Inorganic/organic dust exposure characteristically causes interstitial lung disease with a restrictive pattern and reduced diffusing capacity, whereas dusts/chemicals causing occupational asthma or COPD produce airway obstruction (Harrison 22e p.2234).","A drop in FEV1 measured before vs after a work shift can detect an acute occupational bronchoconstrictive response (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Radiographic pattern hints at etiology: small rounded opacities suggest silicosis or coal worker's pneumoconiosis, while small linear/irregular opacities suggest asbestosis (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Asbestos exposure causes a spectrum of disease: pleural plaques and benign effusions (marking exposure only), asbestosis (interstitial fibrosis), bronchogenic lung cancer, and pleural/peritoneal mesothelioma (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Pleural plaques indicate past asbestos exposure but, by themselves, imply only exposure and not pulmonary impairment (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Smoking and asbestos exposure interact more than additively, producing lung cancer risk greater than the sum of each factor; mesothelioma risk is NOT associated with smoking (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Silicosis raises the risk of mycobacterial (including M. tuberculosis), atypical mycobacterial, and fungal infection because silica impairs alveolar macrophage function; treatment of latent TB is given for a longer duration in these patients (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Eggshell calcification of hilar nodes (seen in up to ~20% of cases) is a characteristic radiographic clue to silicosis (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Acute (accelerated) silicosis from very heavy exposure (e.g., sandblasting in confined spaces, denim sandblasting, artificial-stone countertop fabrication) can resemble pulmonary alveolar proteinosis with a 'crazy paving' HRCT pattern and may progress despite stopping exposure (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Chronic beryllium disease mimics sarcoidosis (noncaseating granulomas); the distinguishing feature is a beryllium-specific cell-mediated (delayed hypersensitivity) immune response demonstrated by the beryllium lymphocyte proliferation test (BeLPT) (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Caplan syndrome is the combination of pneumoconiotic nodules and seropositive rheumatoid arthritis, first described in coal miners and later in silicosis (Harrison 22e p.2234).","Documented mesothelioma in a patient with occupational/environmental asbestos exposure may be compensable, as >80% of mesotheliomas are associated with asbestos exposure (Harrison 22e p.2234)."],"pathophysiology":"Disease depends on the chemical/physical properties of the inhaled agent, which determine dose and site of deposition: water-soluble gases (ammonia, SO2) are absorbed in proximal airways causing irritation/bronchoconstriction, while less-soluble gases (NO2, phosgene) reach bronchioles/alveoli causing chemical pneumonitis. Particles >10-15 micrometres deposit in the nose/throat; particles <10 micrometres reach below the larynx, with coarse-mode (~2.5-10 micrometres, crustal elements like silica) depositing high in the tracheobronchial tree, fine-mode (<2.5 micrometres, from combustion/high-temperature processes) dominating surface area for toxin delivery to lower airways, and ultrafine (<0.1 micrometres, nanoparticles) potentially entering the circulation. Asbestos fibers induce fibrosis through oxidative injury from reactive oxygen species generated by surface transition metals and by phagocytosing cells. Silica causes alveolar macrophage dysfunction (predisposing to infection) and has immunoadjuvant properties (predisposing to autoimmune connective tissue disease). Beryllium drives a CD4+ T-cell-mediated granulomatous inflammation, with susceptibility strongly linked to HLA-DP alleles bearing glutamic acid at position 69 of the beta chain. (Harrison 22e p.2234)","additionalDifferentials":["Asbestosis vs other causes of diffuse interstitial fibrosis (IPF/UIP) (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Chronic beryllium disease vs sarcoidosis (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Silicosis (complicated/PMF) vs coal worker's pneumoconiosis and progressive massive fibrosis (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Acute silicosis vs pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Acute farmer's lung (hypersensitivity pneumonitis) vs influenza or community-acquired pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Occupational asthma / RADS vs nonoccupational asthma and COPD (Harrison 22e p.2234)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute farmer's lung (hypersensitivity pneumonitis from thermophilic actinomycete spores) presenting 4-8 h after exposure with fever, chills, malaise, cough, and dyspnea can mimic influenza or pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Acute silicosis with profuse miliary infiltration may radiographically resemble miliary infectious processes (Harrison 22e p.2234)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chronic beryllium disease closely mimics sarcoidosis (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Aluminum and titanium dioxide exposure can produce a sarcoid-like reaction in lung tissue (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Asbestosis mimics idiopathic and other diffuse interstitial fibroses (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Hard-metal (tungsten carbide/cobalt) exposure can cause giant cell interstitial pneumonitis (Harrison 22e p.2234)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Pre- and post-shift spirometry (change in FEV1) to detect acute bronchoconstriction (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Chest radiograph graded by the ILO International Classification of Radiographs of Pneumoconioses (Harrison 22e p.2234)","CT and high-resolution CT (HRCT) - more sensitive than radiograph for lung opacities, pleural thickening, and interstitial change (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Pulmonary function testing (restrictive pattern with reduced lung volumes and diffusing capacity in fibrosing disease) (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Skin prick testing or specific IgE for immediate hypersensitivity (e.g., flour antigens in bakers) (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Specific IgG precipitating antibody titers for hypersensitivity pneumonitis agents (e.g., pigeon antigen in bird handlers) (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Beryllium lymphocyte proliferation test (BeLPT) for beryllium sensitization (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Fiberoptic bronchoscopy with transbronchial lung biopsy (e.g., to diagnose chronic beryllium disease); occasionally video-assisted thoracoscopic surgical biopsy for definitive diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2234)"],"redFlags":["Mesothelioma may follow relatively short asbestos exposures (<=1-2 years) with latency up to ~40 years - a thorough exposure history is essential (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Lung cancer after asbestos exposure has a minimum latency of ~15-19 years from first exposure (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Progressive massive fibrosis (PMF), occurring in complicated silicosis and CWP, causes severe lung function deficits and is associated with premature mortality; PMF is rising at an alarming rate in the Appalachian coal belt (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Active tuberculosis risk is increased in silicosis (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Acute silicosis can be severe and progressive even after exposure ceases (Harrison 22e p.2234)"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies by exposure and disease. Pleural plaques alone do not imply impairment. Simple silicosis and simple CWP are often present without significant functional impairment, but nodular fibrosis can progress even after exposure ceases, coalescing into complicated disease and PMF, which carries severe functional deficits and premature mortality. Acute silicosis may be severe and progressive despite stopping exposure. No specific therapy exists for asbestosis (supportive care as for any diffuse interstitial fibrosis); newly diagnosed cases usually reflect exposures from many years earlier. Asbestos exposure carries excess lung cancer risk (latency ~15-19 years) and mesothelioma risk (latency up to ~40 years). (Harrison 22e p.2234)","pitfalls":["Failing to take an occupational/environmental exposure history is the cardinal pitfall - the etiologic link to occupation is missed unless specifically sought, e.g., asking about beryllium exposure in a patient labeled as having sarcoidosis (Harrison 22e p.2234)","The ILO radiographic classification, though useful for screening populations, is problematic for the individual worker: rounded-opacity disease may look extensive on film with minimal functional impairment, whereas linear/irregular opacities (asbestosis) may lead to underestimation of impairment until late (Harrison 22e p.2234)","In early chronic beryllium disease both chest imaging and pulmonary function tests may be normal (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Attributing dust-related chronic bronchitis/COPD solely to occupation without accounting for cigarette smoking, whose effect is generally additive (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Overlooking that talc and vermiculite may be contaminated with asbestos, conferring fibrosis, lung cancer, and mesothelioma risk (Harrison 22e p.2234)","Combat-related embedded metal fragments (shrapnel) in veterans may contain beryllium and serve as an unrecognized exposure source (Harrison 22e p.2234)"],"severityClassification":"Silicosis and coal worker's pneumoconiosis are categorized as simple (small rounded opacities, usually without functional impairment) versus complicated (nodules >=1 cm) which can progress to progressive massive fibrosis (PMF, conglomerate masses >1 cm with restrictive and obstructive impairment). Acute (accelerated) silicosis is distinguished from chronic silicosis by very heavy short-duration exposure and an alveolar-proteinosis-like presentation. Occupational asthma is divided into immunologic (high- vs low-molecular-weight stimuli, with a latency period) and irritative (reactive airway dysfunction syndrome, RADS) forms. (Harrison 22e p.2234)","references":["Harrison 22e p.2234"],"crossLinks":["ild","PULMONARY_TB","lung_cancer"]},"odontogenic_sinusitis":{"name":"Odontogenic sinusitis","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dental sinusitis","maxillary sinusitis of dental origin","odontogenic maxillary sinusitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Odontogenic sinusitis is maxillary sinus inflammation arising from a dental source and accounts for a significant minority of maxillary sinus infections","It should be suspected in unilateral maxillary sinusitis, particularly when symptoms are refractory to standard rhinological treatment","A foul smell or taste and unilateral purulent discharge are characteristic clues pointing to a dental origin","Common causes include periapical infection of upper posterior teeth, periodontal disease, oroantral communication after extraction, and displaced roots or implants","The close relationship between the roots of maxillary premolars and molars and the sinus floor explains the spread of infection","Cone-beam CT is valuable for identifying the culprit tooth and the extent of sinus involvement","Effective treatment must address the dental cause, since sinus-directed therapy alone often fails","Management may combine dental treatment or extraction, closure of any oroantral communication, and sinus care in collaboration with ENT"],"pathophysiology":"Odontogenic sinusitis develops when the integrity of the barrier between the maxillary posterior teeth and the sinus floor is breached, allowing bacteria and inflammatory products to enter the sinus. This may follow periapical or periodontal infection extending through thin or dehiscent bone, an oroantral communication created during extraction, or displacement of roots, foreign material, or implants into the sinus. The resulting mixed, often anaerobic, infection disrupts mucociliary clearance and can obstruct the ostiomeatal complex, perpetuating sinus inflammation. Because the driver is dental, the process persists until the source is eliminated.","additionalDifferentials":["Rhinogenic (non-dental) bacterial or viral sinusitis","Allergic rhinosinusitis","Fungal sinusitis","Sinonasal tumour","Oroantral fistula without active sinusitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cone-beam or conventional CT to localise the dental source and sinus disease","Thorough dental examination with pulp testing of upper posterior teeth","Assessment for oroantral communication after recent extraction","Nasal endoscopy and ENT assessment where indicated","Culture of purulent discharge in resistant or severe cases"],"redFlags":["Spread beyond the sinus with orbital swelling, visual changes, or signs of intracranial extension","Features suggesting invasive fungal sinusitis in immunocompromised or diabetic patients","Persistent unilateral disease or a mass raising suspicion of malignancy","Systemic sepsis from an odontogenic infection extending into deep spaces"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is good when the dental cause is identified and treated, with resolution of sinus symptoms; failure to address the source leads to persistent or recurrent sinusitis and occasional serious complications.","pitfalls":["Treating recurrent unilateral maxillary sinusitis medically while missing the dental cause","Overlooking an oroantral communication after upper molar extraction","Failing to test the vitality of upper posterior teeth adjacent to the sinus","Neglecting displaced roots or implants within the sinus as a persistent nidus"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"oncologic_emergencies":{"name":"Oncologic Emergencies","system":"Oncology / Emergency","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tumor lysis syndrome","superior vena cava syndrome"],"chapter":80,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.585-595","clinicalPearls":["Emergencies in cancer fall into three groups: pressure/obstruction from a space-occupying lesion (structural-obstructive), metabolic or hormonal problems (paraneoplastic), and treatment-related complications (Harrison 22e p.585).","Superior vena cava syndrome (SVCS) reflects SVC obstruction with severe reduction of venous return from head, neck and upper extremities; lung cancer (especially small-cell and squamous) accounts for ~85% of malignant cases, and in young adults lymphoma is a leading cause (Harrison 22e p.585).","Benign causes of SVCS are increasing with intravascular device use (central catheters, pacemaker/defibrillator leads) and now account for at least 40% of cases (Harrison 22e p.585).","SVCS typically presents with neck and facial swelling (especially periorbital), dyspnea, cough, dilated neck veins, collateral chest-wall veins, cyanosis and facial plethora that worsens when supine; bending forward or lying down aggravates symptoms (Harrison 22e p.585).","The diagnosis of SVCS is clinical; the most significant chest radiograph finding is widening of the superior mediastinum (most often right-sided), and CT provides the most reliable mediastinal anatomy (Harrison 22e p.586).","Malignant spinal cord compression (MSCC) is compression of cord and/or cauda equina by an extradural tumor mass; spinal cord compression occurs in 5-10% of cancer patients and is the first manifestation of malignancy in ~10% (Harrison 22e p.588).","Localized back pain and tenderness is the most common initial symptom of spinal cord compression, often present for days to months before neurologic findings; it characteristically worsens when supine, unlike disk-disease pain (Harrison 22e p.588-589).","The thoracic spine is the most common site of cord compression (70%), followed by lumbosacral (20%) and cervical (10%); lung, breast and prostate cancers are the most frequent offenders (Harrison 22e p.588).","Treatment of spinal cord compression is more often successful in patients still ambulatory and with sphincter control at initiation; patients whose exam suggests compression should receive dexamethasone immediately and undergo MRI (Harrison 22e p.589).","Full-length spinal MRI is the imaging procedure of choice for cord compression and is as good as or better than myelography plus post-myelogram CT (Harrison 22e p.589).","About 25% of cancer patients die with intracranial metastases; lung and breast cancer and melanoma metastasize to brain most often, and brain metastases present features of an expanding intracranial lesion: headache, nausea, vomiting, behavioral change, seizures and focal progressive deficits (Harrison 22e p.589-590).","Dexamethasone is the best initial treatment for all symptomatic patients with brain metastases and raised intracranial pressure (Harrison 22e p.590).","Hypercalcemia is the most common paraneoplastic syndrome, and SIADH is the most common cause of hyponatremia among cancer patients (both detailed in Chaps. 98 and 422/393) (Harrison 22e p.592).","Tumor lysis syndrome (TLS) is characterized by hyperuricemia, hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia and hypocalcemia from destruction of large numbers of rapidly proliferating cells; acidosis may develop and acute renal failure occurs frequently (Harrison 22e p.593).","TLS is most often associated with treatment of Burkitt's lymphoma, ALL, AML and other rapidly proliferating lymphomas, and usually occurs during or shortly (1-5 days) after chemotherapy; risk correlates with tumor burden and high LDH (>1500 U/L) (Harrison 22e p.593).","Recognition of risk and prevention are the most important steps in TLS management; the standard preventive approach is allopurinol plus aggressive hydration (Harrison 22e p.593).","Febrile neutropenia is the clinical presentation of fever with <1500 granulocytes/uL; it is a key treatment-related emergency in cancer patients (Harrison 22e p.575)."],"pathophysiology":"Oncologic emergencies arise by three mechanisms (Harrison 22e p.585). Structural-obstructive emergencies result from a space-occupying lesion: in SVCS, malignant tumor or thrombosis obstructs the superior vena cava, reducing venous return from head, neck and upper limbs; in malignant spinal cord compression, an expanding extradural tumor (usually metastasis to vertebral body or pedicle, or paravertebral extension through the intervertebral foramen) mechanically injures axons and myelin and compromises blood flow, causing ischemia/infarction; raised intracranial pressure results from brain metastases and surrounding edema, sometimes with hemorrhage or CSF obstruction causing hydrocephalus and herniation (Harrison 22e p.585-590). Metabolic/hormonal (paraneoplastic) emergencies include hypercalcemia, SIADH-related hyponatremia, lactic acidosis and hypoglycemia (Harrison 22e p.592-593). Treatment-related TLS occurs when cytotoxic killing of a large rapidly proliferating tumor releases intracellular contents: nucleic acid turnover raises uric acid which precipitates in the acidic renal tubules; released phosphate causes hyperphosphatemia with reciprocal hypocalcemia and calcium-phosphate nephrocalcinosis; and massive cell destruction releases potassium causing hyperkalemia, which in renal failure can cause fatal ventricular arrhythmias (Harrison 22e p.593).","additionalDifferentials":["SVCS in a young man with a mediastinal mass: lymphoma versus primary mediastinal germ cell tumor (Harrison 22e p.585)","Cauda equina syndrome: low back pain, saddle sensory loss, rectal/bladder dysfunction, sexual impotence and absent reflexes from compression of nerve roots forming the cauda equina (Harrison 22e p.589)","Abrupt stroke-like onset of headache, nausea, vomiting and focal deficit in brain metastasis usually indicates hemorrhage into the metastasis (melanoma, germ cell and renal cell tumors bleed readily) (Harrison 22e p.590)"],"infectionMimics":["Pyogenic epidural abscess or vertebral tuberculosis can mimic malignant spinal cord compression (Harrison 22e p.589)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Spinal cord compression mimics: osteoporotic vertebral collapse, disk disease, radiation myelopathy, neoplastic leptomeningitis, benign tumors, epidural hematoma and spinal lipomatosis (Harrison 22e p.589)","About 20% of vertebral collapse (especially in older patients and postmenopausal women) is due to osteoporosis rather than cancer (Harrison 22e p.589)","Benign causes of SVCS: aortic aneurysm, thyromegaly, thrombosis, and fibrosing mediastinitis from prior irradiation, histoplasmosis or Behcet's syndrome (Harrison 22e p.585)","Pseudohyperkalemia from high leukocyte/platelet counts (cell lysis after blood draw) can falsely elevate potassium in TLS; no ECG abnormalities are present (Harrison 22e p.593)","Pseudohypoglycemia from glucose consumption in the test tube with hyperleukocytosis, myeloproliferative disease or CSF treatment (Harrison 22e p.593)"],"additionalInvestigations":["SVCS: chest radiograph (superior mediastinal widening, usually right-sided), CT thorax (most reliable mediastinal view; 'hot quadrate' hepatic hotspot sign suggests SVC obstruction), MRI (100% sensitivity/specificity but supine positioning may be difficult in dyspneic patients) (Harrison 22e p.586)","Spinal cord compression: neurologic exam and plain spine films first (earliest finding is the 'winking owl' sign of pedicle erosion; plain films are insensitive), then full-length spinal MRI (procedure of choice); myelography reserved for poor or unobtainable MRI (Harrison 22e p.589)","Brain metastases/raised ICP: gadolinium-enhanced MRI is superior to CT and more sensitive for meningeal involvement and small posterior-fossa lesions (Harrison 22e p.590)","TLS pretreatment evaluation in at-risk patients: complete blood count, serum chemistry (electrolytes, creatinine, calcium, phosphate, uric acid) and urinalysis; follow plasma rather than serum potassium when pseudohyperkalemia is suspected; sonography/CT of kidneys and retroperitoneum if baseline renal function is abnormal (Harrison 22e p.593)","A urinary uric acid to urinary creatinine ratio >1 supports acute hyperuricemic nephropathy; <1 suggests renal failure of other cause (Harrison 22e p.593)"],"redFlags":["SVCS with cerebral and/or laryngeal edema, proptosis, glossal/laryngeal edema, obtundation or postural hypotension carries poorer prognosis and requires urgent evaluation and early stenting (Harrison 22e p.585-586)","Tracheal obstruction from a superior mediastinal mass is the one potentially life-threatening complication of SVCS and demands emergent therapy; cardiac arrest or respiratory failure can occur, particularly with sedatives or general anesthesia (Harrison 22e p.585-586)","Cancer patient with back pain plus a crescendo pain pattern, Lhermitte's sign, or pain aggravated by cough/Valsalva/recumbency should be evaluated for cord compression as quickly as possible (Harrison 22e p.589)","Loss of bowel or bladder control, autonomic dysfunction (decreased anal tone, distended bladder, absent anal wink/bulbocavernosus reflex) signals significant cord involvement and is an unfavorable prognostic feature (Harrison 22e p.588)","Hyperkalemia in TLS with renal failure can rapidly become life-threatening, causing ventricular arrhythmias and sudden death (Harrison 22e p.593)","Acute adrenal insufficiency (adrenal crisis) is potentially lethal and may be masked by nonspecific symptoms attributed to cancer or therapy (Harrison 22e p.593)","Febrile neutropenia (fever with <1500 granulocytes/uL) is a treatment-related emergency requiring prompt management (Harrison 22e p.575)"],"prognosis":"In SVCS, mortality relates to the underlying cause rather than to caval obstruction itself, and clinical improvement occurs in most patients (sometimes via collateral circulation); SVCS recurs in 10-30% (Harrison 22e p.586). For spinal cord compression, tumor histology is an important determinant of recovery and survival, and rapid onset/progression of signs and symptoms are poor prognostic features; treatment is more often successful in ambulatory patients who retain sphincter control (Harrison 22e p.589). Brain metastases frequently cause major symptoms, disability and early death (Harrison 22e p.590). Lactic acidosis in cancer carries a poor prognosis regardless of treatment, and cancer patients with malignant cells on pericardial cytology have very poor survival (Harrison 22e p.587, 592). Despite aggressive prophylaxis, TLS with oliguric/anuric renal failure may still occur and often requires renal replacement therapy (Harrison 22e p.594).","pitfalls":["A normal chest radiograph is still compatible with the diagnosis of SVCS if other characteristic findings are present (Harrison 22e p.586)","A normal appearance of plain spine films does not exclude cancer; plain films are insensitive for cord compression (Harrison 22e p.589)","Vertebral collapse is not a reliable indicator of tumor since ~20% of collapses are osteoporotic (Harrison 22e p.589)","After SVC stenting, the prompt increase in venous return may precipitate heart failure and pulmonary edema (Harrison 22e p.586)","Urinary alkalinization with sodium bicarbonate is no longer recommended in TLS: although it increases uric acid solubility, a high pH decreases solubility of xanthine, hypoxanthine and calcium phosphate, potentially increasing intratubular crystallization (Harrison 22e p.593)","Rasburicase is contraindicated in glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, and it causes ex vivo degradation of uric acid in the test tube giving spuriously low levels unless samples are cooled immediately (Harrison 22e p.594)","Pseudohyperkalemia in TLS shows no ECG abnormalities; plasma rather than serum potassium should be followed (Harrison 22e p.593)","Arterial blood gas oxygen tension can be spuriously low in hyperleukocytosis due to oxygen consumption by white cells; pulse oximetry is the most accurate measure of oxygenation (Harrison 22e p.591)"],"severityClassification":"Spinal cord compression management uses the NOMS framework (Memorial Sloan Kettering): Neurologic assessment (degree of epidural compression, myelopathy, functional radiculopathy), Oncologic assessment (tumor radiosensitivity), Mechanical assessment (spinal stability), and Systemic factors (extent of disease and comorbidities determining tolerance of therapy) (Harrison 22e p.589-590). TLS risk is stratified by tumor burden, renal function, hyperuricemia and high LDH (>1500 U/L); a high-risk management algorithm escalates from hydration and allopurinol to recombinant urate oxidase/rasburicase and hemodialysis based on serum uric acid, creatinine, potassium, phosphate and symptomatic hypocalcemia thresholds (Harrison 22e p.593-594).","references":["Harrison 22e p.585-595","Azizi AH et al: Superior vena cava syndrome. JACC Cardiovasc Interv 13:2896, 2020","Durani U, Hogan WJ: Emergencies in haematology: Tumour lysis syndrome. Br J Haematol 188:494, 2020","Lawton AJ et al: Assessment and management of patients with metastatic spinal cord compression: A multidisciplinary review. J Clin Oncol 37:61, 2019"],"crossLinks":["svc_obstruction","cord_compression","hypercalcemia","FEBRILE_NEUTROPENIA","hyponatremia"]},"opioid_related_disorders":{"name":"Opioid-Related Disorders","system":"Neurologic/Psychiatric (substance use)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Opioid use disorder","Opioid addiction","Opioid dependence","Opioid intoxication","Opioid withdrawal","Opioid overdose"],"chapter":467,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3688","clinicalPearls":["DSM-5 (2022) replaced the older terms dependence and addiction with opioid use disorder, opioid intoxication, and opioid withdrawal; the disorder is defined as repeated use over a 12-month period causing problems in two or more domains (tolerance, withdrawal, using larger amounts than intended, craving, and continued use despite adverse consequences) (Harrison 22e p.3688).","The mu receptor is the receptor most clinically relevant to opioid effects; mu actions include analgesia, euphoric reinforcement, cough and appetite suppression, decreased respiration, decreased GI motility, sedation, and hormonal changes, while kappa activation tends to produce dysphoria and psychotic symptoms (Harrison 22e p.3689).","An opioid 'high' occurs only when the rate of rise in dopamine is fast, so rapid routes such as smoking and intravenous injection produce a 'rush' whereas oral and transdermal routes give analgesia/sedation without the euphoric high (Harrison 22e p.3689).","Opioid overdose is recognized clinically by shallow, slow respirations, pupillary miosis (mydriasis appears only after significant brain anoxia), bradycardia, hypothermia, and stupor or coma; immediate management is based on clinical criteria rather than waiting for toxicology (Harrison 22e p.3690).","Fentanyl overdose may require roughly twice the reversal-agent dose needed for other opioids, and reversal antagonists are short-acting relative to most opioids, so re-sedation can occur after an initial response (Harrison 22e pp.3688, 3690).","Adding xylazine (a nonopioid veterinary sedative) to illicit fentanyl markedly worsens respiratory suppression and is associated with tissue necrosis and increased risk of limb amputation in people who inject (Harrison 22e p.3690).","Opioids generally do not cause seizures except in unusual situations: polydrug use with meperidine, high-dose tramadol, or the newborn (Harrison 22e p.3690).","Withdrawal risk has a strong genetic component (up to ~50% related to functional polymorphisms), including a mu-opioid receptor variant conferring threefold higher affinity for opioids and beta-endorphin; epigenetic methylation may compensate by reducing mu-receptor numbers (Harrison 22e p.3689).","Methadone can prolong the QT interval (rates reported as high as ~16% above non-methadone injecting patients) and has caused sudden death; QT monitoring is relevant during overdose and maintenance (Harrison 22e pp.3690, 3691).","Heroin users are frequently polydrug users (alcohol, sedatives, cannabinoids, stimulants); none substitute for opioids but they have additive effects, so concurrent alcohol or sedative withdrawal (which can be more dangerous) must be excluded before managing opioid withdrawal (Harrison 22e p.3690).","Buprenorphine's partial agonism means escalating doses shift toward antagonist activity, so primary buprenorphine overdose is nearly impossible, but high doses can precipitate withdrawal in patients on full agonists such as morphine or methadone (Harrison 22e pp.3690-3691).","Most opioid-using individuals meet more than three diagnostic criteria, so reducing the DSM threshold from three to two problem areas did not change measured disease rates (Harrison 22e p.3688)."],"pathophysiology":"Opioid receptors (mu, kappa, delta, nociceptin/orphanin) are G protein-linked and coupled to the cyclic AMP (cAMP) second-messenger system and to G protein-coupled inwardly rectifying potassium channels (GIRKs); receptor binding increases potassium permeability, hyperpolarizes neurons, and inhibits action potentials, producing analgesia, sedation, and reinforcement. Reinforcing euphoria is mediated through the mesolimbic dopaminergic pathway from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens, where opioids indirectly raise dopamine by inhibiting GABAergic interneurons; the subjective high requires a fast rate of dopamine change. Tolerance and withdrawal arise from adaptive upregulation of the cAMP-PKA-CREB cascade in locus coeruleus noradrenergic neurons after chronic mu-receptor inhibition: sustained inhibition triggers compensatory increases in cyclic AMP enzyme activity, so abrupt opioid cessation produces unopposed noradrenergic overactivity and withdrawal symptoms; this model underlies the use of alpha2-agonists to suppress that activation. PKA-driven phosphorylation of CREB also induces genes such as tyrosine hydroxylase and BDNF, the latter implicated in long-term neuroplastic changes (Harrison 22e pp.3689-3690).","additionalDifferentials":["Concurrent sedative-hypnotic (benzodiazepine) overdose or withdrawal, which may coexist and be more dangerous to manage","Alcohol intoxication or withdrawal in a polydrug user","Stimulant co-intoxication (cocaine/methamphetamine), including combined 'speedball' use","Other causes of coma with respiratory depression (CNS structural lesion, metabolic encephalopathy) before miosis-based diagnosis is confirmed"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Toxic megacolon from severe opioid-induced constipation mimicking primary abdominal catastrophe","MPTP-induced parkinsonism from contaminated illicitly synthesized meperidine, mimicking idiopathic Parkinson disease","Orthostatic hypotension from opioid-related histamine release mimicking other causes of hypotension"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood or urine toxicology to confirm a suspected opioid diagnosis (management must not wait for results)","ECG for QT-interval monitoring, particularly with methadone or in overdose","Arterial/clinical assessment of respiratory status and gag reflex (aspiration pneumonia risk in overdose)","Liver function tests when considering opioid-antagonist relapse-prevention therapy (most providers avoid it if LFTs exceed about three times normal)","Screening for blood-borne infection (hepatitis B, hepatitis C, HIV) in people who inject"],"redFlags":["Shallow/slow respirations with pinpoint miosis, bradycardia, hypothermia, and stupor or coma indicating life-threatening overdose","Mydriasis in a suspected overdose suggesting significant brain anoxia has supervened","Limited or absent response to standard reversal dosing, prompting suspicion of fentanyl, high antagonist requirement, or concurrent benzodiazepine overdose","Loss of gag reflex with aspiration pneumonia risk","Skin necrosis or limb compromise suggesting xylazine-adulterated fentanyl use","Septic complications of injection use such as meningitis, osteomyelitis, or organ abscesses"],"prognosis":"Opioid use disorder is a chronic, relapsing condition; most patients tend to relapse during or after detoxification, and stopping use is far easier than preventing relapse. Long-term relapse prevention requires combined pharmacologic and psychosocial approaches. Medication-free inpatient/outpatient programs have poor 1- to 5-year outcomes compared with pharmacotherapy, except for residential programs lasting roughly 6-18 months. Untreated potentially lethal overdose progresses to respiratory and cardiovascular collapse and death. Globally the World Drug Report attributes the greatest burden of drug-related morbidity and mortality to opioid misuse; in 2022 over 76,000 US overdose deaths involved opioids, with rates accelerating due to high-potency fentanyl derivatives and stimulant co-use (Harrison 22e pp.3688, 3691-3692).","pitfalls":["Administering so much antagonist that opioid withdrawal is precipitated, rather than targeting reversal of respiratory depression","Assuming a single antagonist dose is sufficient, since antagonists last only a few hours while most opioids persist longer, risking re-sedation (close monitoring/continuous antagonism often needed)","Overlooking concurrent benzodiazepine or alcohol/sedative involvement when reversal has only limited effect","Misjudging dose in naive or infrequent users because actual ingested potency is hard to estimate, especially with fentanyl derivatives","Forgetting that buprenorphine and methadone themselves can be misused and that buprenorphine can precipitate withdrawal in fentanyl- or high-dose-methadone-dependent patients"],"severityClassification":"Withdrawal time course (heroin): symptoms begin ~8-10 h after the last dose, with early lacrimation, rhinorrhea, yawning, and sweating; later stages bring restless sleep, weakness, chills, piloerection ('cold turkey'), nausea/vomiting, muscle aches, involuntary movements, hyperpnea, hyperthermia, and hypertension. Acute withdrawal lasts ~7-10 days, followed by a protracted abstinence phase of ~26-30 weeks (hypotension, bradycardia, hypothermia, mydriasis, blunted respiratory CO2 responsiveness). Tolerance/withdrawal can develop within ~6-8 weeks of chronic daily use. Plasma half-lives vary widely: ~2.5-3 h for morphine, >22 h for methadone, minutes for fentanyl-related opioids, and longest for buprenorphine/metabolites (Harrison 22e pp.3689-3690).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3688","Harrison 22e p.3689","Harrison 22e p.3690","Harrison 22e p.3691","Harrison 22e p.3692"],"crossLinks":[]},"opioid_use_disorder":{"name":"Opioid use disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OUD","opioid dependence","opioid addiction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The disorder is a problematic pattern of opioid use, whether of prescribed analgesics or illicit opioids such as heroin, leading to clinically significant impairment or distress with impaired control, craving, and continued use despite harm","Intoxication features include pinpoint pupils, drowsiness, slurred speech, and, dangerously, respiratory depression, which is the principal cause of death in overdose","Withdrawal is intensely unpleasant but not usually life-threatening, with features such as dilated pupils, sweating, gastrointestinal upset, muscle aches, yawning, and piloerection","Opioid agonist therapy with long-acting agents is the evidence-based cornerstone of treatment and substantially reduces mortality and illicit use","Antagonist treatment is an option for motivated, detoxified patients, while overdose reversal with an opioid antagonist is a life-saving emergency intervention","Take-home overdose-reversal medication and harm-reduction measures should be offered to patients and, where appropriate, their families","Injecting use carries risks of blood-borne virus transmission, endocarditis, and skin and soft-tissue infection, so screening and preventive care are essential","Comorbid mental illness, chronic pain, and social instability are common and should be addressed within an integrated treatment plan"],"pathophysiology":"Opioid use disorder results from repeated stimulation of mu-opioid receptors, producing potent dopaminergic reward signalling and rapid neuroadaptation in reward, stress, and executive-control circuits. Tolerance and physical dependence develop through receptor and second-messenger adaptations, and withdrawal reflects rebound noradrenergic overactivity, particularly from the locus coeruleus, while genetic, psychiatric, and environmental factors modulate vulnerability and relapse.","additionalDifferentials":["Physiological dependence on appropriately prescribed opioid analgesia without a use disorder","Other sedative or substance intoxication","Non-opioid causes of altered consciousness such as hypoglycaemia or head injury","Sepsis or other acute medical illness in an injecting drug user","Comorbid depression or anxiety driving misuse"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine toxicology confirming opioid and other substance use","Assessment of withdrawal severity with a standardised scale","Screening for blood-borne viruses and injection-related infection","Liver function and general medical assessment","Screening for comorbid psychiatric disorders and suicide risk","Review of prescription monitoring data where available"],"redFlags":["Respiratory depression or reduced consciousness indicating overdose requiring immediate reversal","Features of endocarditis, sepsis, or deep soft-tissue infection in injecting users","High overdose risk following loss of tolerance, such as after detoxification or release from custody","Suicidal ideation or intent"],"prognosis":"Opioid use disorder is a chronic, relapsing condition with substantial mortality, largely from overdose, but agonist maintenance treatment markedly improves survival, retention in care, and function. Prognosis is better with sustained maintenance treatment, psychosocial support, and social stability, and worse with continued injecting, polysubstance use, and disengagement from care.","pitfalls":["Abrupt discontinuation of opioids or detoxification without maintenance planning, which raises overdose risk","Failing to offer take-home overdose-reversal medication","Undertreating pain or comorbid mental illness, perpetuating illicit use","Overlooking the sharply increased overdose risk after periods of abstinence","Stigmatising attitudes that deter engagement with treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"optic_neuritis":{"name":"Optic neuritis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ON","retrobulbar neuritis","papillitis","optic nerve inflammation","demyelinating optic neuritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically presents in a young adult (more often female) with subacute unilateral visual loss developing over hours to days, accompanied by pain worse on eye movement","A relative afferent pupillary defect is present, and colour vision (especially red desaturation) is impaired out of proportion to acuity","In retrobulbar neuritis the optic disc looks normal at onset ('the patient sees nothing and the doctor sees nothing'), whereas papillitis shows a swollen disc","It is commonly the presenting or an associated feature of multiple sclerosis, so demyelination should be considered","MRI of the brain and orbits with contrast assesses the optic nerve and detects demyelinating white-matter lesions that predict multiple sclerosis risk","Vision usually recovers spontaneously over weeks; intravenous corticosteroids speed recovery but do not change final visual outcome","Atypical features (bilateral, no pain, poor recovery, severe disc swelling) should prompt consideration of neuromyelitis optica, MOG-associated disease, or an infective/compressive cause"],"pathophysiology":"Optic neuritis is inflammation and demyelination of the optic nerve, most commonly immune-mediated and associated with multiple sclerosis. An inflammatory, often T-cell-driven process attacks the myelin sheath of optic nerve axons, disrupting conduction and causing loss of visual acuity, colour vision, and contrast. Because conduction rather than the axon itself is initially affected, remyelination allows substantial recovery, though repeated or severe attacks cause axonal loss and permanent deficits with optic atrophy. Antibody-mediated variants (aquaporin-4 in neuromyelitis optica, MOG antibody disease) and infective or granulomatous causes produce similar inflammation through different mechanisms.","additionalDifferentials":["Anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy (arteritic and non-arteritic)","Neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder","MOG-antibody-associated optic neuritis","Compressive optic neuropathy from tumour or aneurysm","Toxic, nutritional, or hereditary (Leber) optic neuropathy","Central retinal artery occlusion and other causes of acute visual loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assessment of visual acuity, colour vision, and relative afferent pupillary defect","Visual field testing (commonly shows a central scotoma)","Contrast MRI of the brain and orbits to assess the nerve and demyelinating lesions","Aquaporin-4 and MOG antibody testing in atypical or severe cases","OCT of the retinal nerve fibre layer to monitor atrophy; bloods/lumbar puncture where an infective or inflammatory cause is suspected"],"redFlags":["Bilateral simultaneous, severe, or poorly recovering visual loss suggesting neuromyelitis optica or a non-demyelinating cause","Absence of pain or a progressive course pointing to a compressive or ischaemic optic neuropathy","Systemic features suggesting infection, sarcoidosis, or vasculitis","Older patient with headache and jaw claudication — exclude giant cell arteritis as a cause of ischaemic optic neuropathy"],"prognosis":"Most patients with typical demyelinating optic neuritis recover good vision over several weeks regardless of steroid use, though some residual colour and contrast deficits and a risk of future multiple sclerosis remain.","pitfalls":["Attributing every acute optic neuropathy to demyelination and missing arteritic ischaemic optic neuropathy in older patients","Assuming steroids improve the final visual outcome rather than just the speed of recovery","Overlooking neuromyelitis optica or MOG disease in atypical or severe cases that need different management","Failing to counsel on and investigate the associated risk of multiple sclerosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"oral_candidiasis":{"name":"Oral candidiasis","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["oral thrush","oral candidosis","candidal stomatitis","moniliasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Oral candidiasis is usually an opportunistic infection by Candida species, most often Candida albicans, and typically signals a local or systemic predisposing factor","The pseudomembranous form (thrush) shows white curd-like plaques that wipe off to reveal an erythematous, sometimes bleeding, base","Erythematous (atrophic) candidiasis presents as a red, sore mucosa or a depapillated tongue, and is common under dentures or after antibiotics","Angular cheilitis at the mouth corners frequently coexists and reflects candidal and bacterial colonisation with local moisture","Denture stomatitis appears as erythema confined to the denture-bearing palatal mucosa and relates to poor denture hygiene and continuous wear","Common predisposing factors include inhaled or systemic corticosteroids, broad-spectrum antibiotics, xerostomia, diabetes, and immunosuppression","Management combines antifungal therapy with correction of the underlying cause, including denture hygiene and control of dry mouth","Chronic hyperplastic candidiasis presents as an adherent white patch that will not rub off and carries a small malignant potential warranting biopsy"],"pathophysiology":"Candida species are common oral commensals kept in check by intact mucosal defences, salivary factors, and the resident microbiota. Disruption of this balance allows overgrowth and conversion to an invasive hyphal form that adheres to and penetrates the epithelium. Local factors such as denture coverage, dry mouth, and topical steroids, together with systemic factors such as antibiotics, immunosuppression, diabetes, and nutritional deficiency, create the conditions for opportunistic infection. The clinical pattern (pseudomembranous, erythematous, or hyperplastic) reflects the host state and chronicity rather than different organisms.","additionalDifferentials":["Oral leukoplakia and other keratotic white lesions","Lichen planus and lichenoid reactions","Geographic tongue for the atrophic form","Chemical or thermal mucosal burns","Mucosal manifestations of anaemia or nutritional deficiency"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Oral swab, smear, or oral rinse for microscopy and culture","Biopsy for adherent white patches to assess hyperplastic candidiasis and dysplasia","Blood glucose and screening for diabetes","Haematinics (iron, folate, B12) where atrophic or recurrent disease","Evaluation for immunodeficiency, including HIV, in unexplained or severe cases"],"redFlags":["Unexplained or severe oral candidiasis in a previously healthy adult raising suspicion of undiagnosed HIV or other immunodeficiency","Chronic hyperplastic candidiasis with dysplasia and potential for malignant transformation","Extension into the oesophagus with dysphagia suggesting significant immunosuppression"],"prognosis":"Most acute candidiasis responds well to antifungal treatment when predisposing factors are corrected, but recurrence is common if the underlying cause persists, and chronic hyperplastic lesions require monitoring for dysplasia.","pitfalls":["Treating recurrent candidiasis without seeking and correcting the underlying predisposing factor","Assuming an adherent white patch is thrush when it is hyperplastic candidiasis or leukoplakia needing biopsy","Overlooking denture hygiene and continuous wear as the driver of denture stomatitis","Missing an immunocompromising condition in unexplained or florid infection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"oral_leukoplakia":{"name":"Oral leukoplakia","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["leukoplakia","oral white patch","potentially malignant white lesion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Leukoplakia is a clinical diagnosis of exclusion: a white patch that cannot be wiped off and cannot be attributed to any other definable lesion","It is regarded as an oral potentially malignant disorder, meaning it carries an increased risk of progression to squamous cell carcinoma","Tobacco use, including smokeless forms, areca nut, and alcohol are the principal risk factors","Homogeneous, uniformly white, flat lesions carry lower risk than non-homogeneous, nodular, or speckled (erythroleukoplakia) lesions","Speckled lesions with a red component and lesions on the floor of mouth or ventrolateral tongue have a higher malignant potential","Proliferative verrucous leukoplakia is a distinct, multifocal, treatment-resistant form with high transformation risk","Biopsy is essential to assess for and grade epithelial dysplasia, which is the key predictor of malignant change","Management combines removal of risk factors, treatment or surveillance of the lesion, and long-term monitoring for recurrence or transformation"],"pathophysiology":"Leukoplakia reflects an alteration in the oral epithelium in which increased keratin production and epithelial thickening scatter light to produce a white appearance that does not rub off. Chronic exposure to carcinogens such as tobacco and areca nut drives genetic and epigenetic changes in basal keratinocytes, which may manifest histologically as hyperkeratosis with or without epithelial dysplasia. Dysplastic change represents disordered maturation and cellular atypia that can accumulate over time, and lesions with higher grades of dysplasia carry a greater likelihood of progressing to invasive squamous cell carcinoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Frictional or traumatic keratosis","Chronic hyperplastic candidiasis","Oral lichen planus","Leukoedema","White sponge naevus","Established oral squamous cell carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Incisional or excisional biopsy for histological grading of dysplasia","Careful documentation of site, size, and clinical pattern","Removal of local irritants and reassessment to exclude frictional keratosis","Fungal investigation where hyperplastic candidiasis is suspected","Adjunctive imaging or referral when carcinoma is suspected"],"redFlags":["Non-homogeneous, speckled, or verrucous lesions and any red component, indicating higher malignant potential","Ulceration, induration, or a developing mass suggesting transformation to carcinoma","High-risk sites such as the floor of mouth and ventrolateral tongue","Presence of moderate to severe epithelial dysplasia on biopsy"],"prognosis":"Many lesions remain stable or regress after risk factors are removed, but a proportion undergo malignant transformation, and the risk rises with non-homogeneous appearance, high-risk sites, and higher grades of dysplasia, making long-term surveillance important.","pitfalls":["Labelling a white patch as benign without biopsy or follow-up","Assuming absence of symptoms means absence of risk","Sampling only a homogeneous area and missing dysplasia in a red or nodular component","Failing to address tobacco, areca nut, and alcohol use that perpetuate the lesion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"oral_squamous_cell_carcinoma":{"name":"Oral squamous cell carcinoma","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["oral cancer","oral SCC","mouth cancer","squamous cell carcinoma of the oral cavity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Oral squamous cell carcinoma is the most common malignancy of the oral cavity and often presents as a non-healing ulcer, lump, or persistent red or white patch","Classic worrying features are an indurated ulcer with rolled margins, fixation to underlying tissue, and failure to heal beyond two to three weeks","Tobacco in all forms, areca nut, and alcohol are the dominant risk factors and act synergistically","The lateral and ventral tongue and the floor of mouth are high-risk sites, though any oral surface can be affected","Early lesions may be painless, so any persistent oral abnormality warrants a high index of suspicion and referral","Cervical lymphadenopathy, especially firm or fixed nodes, may indicate regional metastatic spread","Definitive diagnosis requires biopsy, and staging guides treatment involving surgery, radiotherapy, and chemotherapy as appropriate","Prognosis depends heavily on stage at diagnosis, underscoring the value of early detection and opportunistic screening"],"pathophysiology":"Oral squamous cell carcinoma develops through the accumulation of genetic and epigenetic alterations in oral keratinocytes, typically driven by chronic exposure to carcinogens such as tobacco, areca nut, and alcohol. Many tumours arise within or adjacent to potentially malignant disorders such as leukoplakia or erythroplakia, progressing through increasing grades of epithelial dysplasia to carcinoma in situ and then invasive disease when malignant cells breach the basement membrane. The invasive tumour infiltrates local tissue, can destroy bone, and spreads via lymphatics to cervical nodes, with distant metastasis in advanced disease. Field change means the whole mucosa is at risk of further primaries.","additionalDifferentials":["Traumatic ulcer","Recurrent aphthous ulceration","Oral leukoplakia or erythroplakia without invasion","Chronic infective ulcers such as tuberculosis or syphilis","Erosive lichen planus","Salivary gland or other oral malignancy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Incisional biopsy of the lesion for histological diagnosis and grading","Examination and imaging of the neck for lymph node involvement","Cross-sectional imaging (CT, MRI) to assess local extent and staging","Assessment for distant metastasis and synchronous primaries","Multidisciplinary evaluation for treatment planning"],"redFlags":["Any oral ulcer or lesion persisting beyond two to three weeks requires urgent specialist referral to exclude cancer","Induration, fixation, rolled margins, or spontaneous bleeding of a lesion","Unexplained firm or fixed cervical lymphadenopathy","Persistent unexplained pain, numbness, loosening of teeth, or difficulty swallowing"],"prognosis":"Outcome is strongly stage-dependent, with early localised disease carrying a favourable prognosis, whereas advanced tumours with nodal involvement have substantially poorer survival and higher morbidity from treatment.","pitfalls":["Attributing a persistent ulcer to trauma or denture irritation and delaying biopsy","Reassurance because a lesion is painless, since early cancers are often symptomless","Failing to examine and palpate the neck for nodal disease","Overlooking the risk of second primaries in a field-changed mucosa"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"orbital_cellulitis":{"name":"Orbital cellulitis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["postseptal cellulitis","post-septal cellulitis","orbital infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A sight- and life-threatening infection of the orbital tissues behind the orbital septum, most often spreading from adjacent (especially ethmoid) sinusitis","Key features distinguishing it from preseptal cellulitis are painful or restricted eye movements (ophthalmoplegia), proptosis, and pain on eye movement","Systemic signs of fever and malaise accompany a red, swollen, tender lid; a relative afferent pupillary defect signals optic nerve compromise","Reduced visual acuity, colour vision loss, or an afferent pupillary defect indicate optic nerve threat and demand emergency intervention","Commonest in children and frequently caused by Staphylococcus, Streptococcus, and Haemophilus species, sometimes anaerobes","Urgent contrast CT of the orbit and sinuses identifies subperiosteal or orbital abscess and sinus involvement","Requires admission and prompt intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotics, with surgical drainage of abscesses or sinuses when needed"],"pathophysiology":"Orbital cellulitis is infection of the soft tissues posterior to the orbital septum. Infection most commonly extends directly from the paranasal sinuses (particularly the ethmoid, separated from the orbit only by the thin lamina papyracea), but can also arise from trauma, dacryocystitis, dental infection, or haematogenous spread. Inflammation and oedema within the confined bony orbit raise pressure, displace the globe forward (proptosis), impair extraocular muscle function, and can compress or ischaemically injure the optic nerve. Spread posteriorly risks cavernous sinus thrombosis, meningitis, and intracranial abscess.","additionalDifferentials":["Preseptal (periorbital) cellulitis","Idiopathic orbital inflammation (orbital pseudotumour)","Thyroid eye disease","Cavernous sinus thrombosis","Orbital tumour or rhabdomyosarcoma in children","Allergic or traumatic periorbital oedema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Contrast-enhanced CT of the orbit, sinuses, and brain to detect abscess and sinus disease","Assessment of visual acuity, colour vision, pupil reactions, and eye movements","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers","Swabs/cultures of any discharge or drained material","Ophthalmology and ENT review; consider MRI if intracranial extension is suspected"],"redFlags":["Emergency — threatens vision (optic nerve compression) and life (cavernous sinus thrombosis, meningitis, intracranial abscess)","Reduced acuity, impaired colour vision, or a relative afferent pupillary defect indicate imminent optic nerve damage","Restricted or painful eye movements and proptosis distinguishing it from benign preseptal cellulitis","Bilateral involvement, drowsiness, or cranial nerve palsies suggesting cavernous sinus or intracranial spread"],"prognosis":"With prompt intravenous antibiotics and timely drainage the outlook is good, but delay can lead to permanent visual loss, intracranial complications, and death.","pitfalls":["Mistaking sight-threatening orbital cellulitis for benign preseptal cellulitis and failing to assess eye movements and optic nerve function","Delaying CT imaging and abscess drainage","Treating as an outpatient rather than admitting for intravenous therapy","Overlooking the underlying sinusitis that needs concurrent treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteoarthritis":{"name":"Osteoarthritis","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OA","degenerative joint disease"],"chapter":383,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2949","clinicalPearls":["OA is the most common type of arthritis and a leading cause of disability in the elderly; prevalence rises strikingly with age, being uncommon before 40 and highly prevalent after 60 (Harrison 22e p.2949).","Joint involvement is characteristic: hips, knees, first MTP joint, cervical/lumbosacral spine, and in the hands the DIP, PIP, and thumb base; the wrist, elbow, and ankle are usually spared (Harrison 22e p.2949).","Joint pain is primarily activity-related early on, coming on during or just after use and resolving with rest; as disease progresses pain becomes continuous and may be bothersome at night (Harrison 22e p.2952).","Morning stiffness, when present, is usually brief (<30 min), in contrast to the prolonged stiffness (hours) of inflammatory arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2952).","Hand OA produces bony enlargement of the DIP joints (Heberden's nodes) and PIP joints (Bouchard's nodes) (Harrison 22e p.2949).","On hip examination, OA can be detected by loss of internal rotation on passive movement (Harrison 22e p.2953).","Mechanical symptoms (buckling, catching, locking) are common in knee OA and need further evaluation for internal derangement only when they follow an acute knee injury (Harrison 22e p.2952).","Osteophytes are an important radiographic hallmark of OA, along with joint-space narrowing reflecting cartilage loss (Harrison 22e pp.2952-2953)."],"pathophysiology":"OA is joint failure in which all joint structures undergo pathologic change. The pathologic hallmark is focal, initially nonuniform loss of hyaline articular cartilage, accompanied by thickening and sclerosis of the subchondral bony plate, marginal osteophyte outgrowth, stretching of the articular capsule, variable synovitis, and weakness of muscles bridging the joint (meniscal degeneration occurs in knees). Disease begins with joint injury when protective mechanisms (joint capsule/ligaments, bridging muscle, sensory afferents, underlying bone, and synovial-fluid lubrication via hyaluronic acid and lubricin) fail. Cartilage shows depletion of aggrecan and loss/unfurling of type 2 collagen, losing compressive stiffness; chondrocytes shift toward catabolism, producing matrix-degrading enzymes (MMPs, ADAMTS-5). Local inflammation (including mechanoinflammation), with cytokines, alarmins/DAMPs, and growth factors (BMP-2, TGF-beta drive osteophytes; VEGF drives vascular invasion), accelerates progression. Risk reflects the balance between joint vulnerability (age, female sex, genetics such as GDF5, prior damage, malalignment, bridging-muscle weakness, high bone density) and joint loading (obesity, occupational/repetitive use, major injury) (Harrison 22e pp.2949-2952).","additionalDifferentials":["Inflammatory arthritis (suggested by prolonged morning stiffness and involvement of many joints)","Bursitis (anserine bursitis medial/distal to the knee; trochanteric bursitis lateral to the hip)","Iliotibial band syndrome","Internal derangement of the knee (anterior cruciate ligament or meniscal tear), especially after acute injury"],"infectionMimics":["Septic arthritis (consider when synovial fluid white count is elevated/inflammatory)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Gout and pseudogout (crystal arthritis; suggested by synovial fluid white count >1000/uL with identifiable crystals)","Rheumatoid arthritis and other inflammatory arthritides","Fibromyalgia (noninflammatory pain amplification)"],"additionalInvestigations":["No blood tests are routinely indicated unless symptoms or signs suggest inflammatory arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2953).","Synovial fluid examination is often more helpful diagnostically than x-ray; a white count >1000/uL suggests inflammatory arthritis, gout, or pseudogout, the latter two confirmed by crystals (Harrison 22e p.2953).","Radiographs are not routinely indicated; order only when pain and findings are atypical of OA or when pain persists after effective treatment. Imaging findings correlate poorly with pain, and x-rays may be normal in early disease (insensitive to cartilage loss) (Harrison 22e p.2953).","MRI is not indicated in the diagnostic workup; meniscal tears and cartilage/bone lesions are common both in OA and in older persons without joint pain, and MRI findings rarely warrant a change in therapy (Harrison 22e p.2953)."],"redFlags":["Prominent nocturnal pain in the absence of end-stage OA merits a distinct workup (Harrison 22e p.2953).","Prolonged morning stiffness with many joints affected suggests inflammatory arthritis rather than OA (Harrison 22e p.2952).","Mechanical symptoms (buckling, catching, locking) developing after an acute knee injury warrant evaluation for internal derangement (Harrison 22e p.2952)."],"prognosis":"OA is a chronic, generally progressive disorder of the elderly whose high prevalence and impact on physical function make it a leading cause of disability. Severely malaligned knees may be destined to progress regardless of other risk factors, whereas weight loss greater than 10% of body weight reduces cartilage loss and, in most affected persons, alleviates pain (Harrison 22e pp.2949-2952).","pitfalls":["Distinguish OA from rheumatoid (and other inflammatory) arthritis, crystal arthritis (gout/pseudogout), and septic arthritis; prolonged morning stiffness, systemic features, and an inflammatory synovial fluid white count point away from OA.","Do not rely on radiographs to gauge symptom severity: x-ray changes correlate poorly with pain, and films may be normal in early disease.","Many persons with x-ray or MRI evidence of OA have no joint symptoms, so structural findings alone do not establish symptomatic OA.","Back pain and neck pain are not tied to x-ray findings of spinal OA and are evaluated separately."],"severityClassification":"Harrison's chapter 383 does not present a single formal grading scale; OA is characterized clinically along a spectrum from early, episodic activity-related pain with brief morning stiffness to advanced/end-stage disease with continuous and nocturnal pain. The chapter references the WOMAC index as a patient-reported outcome measure of pain, stiffness, and function (Harrison 22e p.2952).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2949"],"crossLinks":["rheumatoid","crystal_arthritis"]},"osteomyelitis":{"name":"Osteomyelitis","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bone infection","vertebral osteomyelitis","spondylodiskitis","disk-space infection","septic diskitis","spinal osteomyelitis","long-bone osteomyelitis","periprosthetic joint infection","Brodie's abscess"],"chapter":136,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1061","clinicalPearls":["Three mechanisms underlie osteomyelitis: hematogenous spread, contiguous spread after surgery or trauma, and secondary infection due to vascular insufficiency or neuropathy; in adults hematogenous disease most often involves the vertebral column, whereas in children long bones are seeded (Harrison 22e p.1061).","The clinically decisive distinction is not a fixed duration but the presence or absence of bone necrosis (sequestra): acute osteomyelitis without necrosis can generally be cured with antimicrobials alone, while chronic osteomyelitis with sequestra requires combined surgical debridement (Harrison 22e p.1062).","Back pain is the leading symptom of vertebral osteomyelitis (>85%), and the location maps to the level of infection (lumbar ~60%, thoracic ~30%, cervical ~10%); only about half of patients are febrile, partly because of analgesic use (Harrison 22e p.1062).","ESR and CRP are markedly more useful than the leukocyte count for screening: CRP is elevated in essentially all and ESR in ~98% of vertebral osteomyelitis cases, whereas leukocytosis and neutrophilia have sensitivities of only ~65% and ~40% (Harrison 22e p.1062).","In patients without sepsis syndrome, antibiotics should be withheld until a pathogen is documented (by blood culture, bone biopsy, or aspirated pus), because pretreatment markedly lowers culture yield and an established microbiologic diagnosis guides therapy (Harrison 22e p.1062-1063).","MRI is the diagnostic gold standard for vertebral osteomyelitis and should be obtained urgently when neurologic impairment is present to detect epidural abscess or exclude disk herniation; 18F-FDG PET is the alternative when MRI is contraindicated or implants/multifocal disease are suspected (Harrison 22e p.1062).","A primary focus is found in only about half of hematogenous cases; common sources are the urinary tract, skin/soft tissue, intravascular catheters, and the endocardium, and endocarditis is identified in roughly 10% overall (and in ~50% of viridans streptococcal cases) (Harrison 22e p.1061-1062).","Implanted foreign material creates a zone of local immunodeficiency and a host-protein-coated surface that promotes staphylococcal adherence and biofilm formation, so device-related bone and joint infection generally cannot be cured by antimicrobials alone (Harrison 22e p.1062, 1066).","Brodie's abscess is a subacute form occurring mainly in young males, presenting with pain (~98%) and swelling (~53%) in the tibia or femur, with a median diagnostic delay of about 3 months; localized unexplained tibial/femoral pain in a young patient warrants imaging (Harrison 22e p.1064).","Subacute or chronic vertebral disease is typically caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis or Brucella in endemic regions, and by low-virulence organisms (coagulase-negative staphylococci, Cutibacterium acnes) in implant-associated infection; tuberculous spondylitis preferentially involves the thoracic spine (about two-thirds of cases) (Harrison 22e p.1062).","Synovial fluid analysis is central to diagnosing periprosthetic joint infection: leukocyte thresholds of ~1700/uL (knee) and ~4200/uL (hip) are about 90% sensitive and specific, and alpha-defensin is highly specific but limited by cost and sensitivity, so it should confirm rather than screen (Harrison 22e p.1066).","Chronic osteomyelitis can reactivate decades later; recurrence after a symptom-free interval exceeding 70 years has been described, most often in elderly patients with S. aureus disease acquired in the preantibiotic era (Harrison 22e p.1063-1064)."],"pathophysiology":"Osteomyelitis is infection of bone reached by microorganisms via one of three routes. In hematogenous disease, circulating organisms seed the metaphyses of long bones in children and the vertebral column in adults; because the adult intervertebral disk is avascular, organisms enter through the segmental arteries supplying the adjacent endplates and then spread into the disk, producing the characteristic involvement of two contiguous vertebrae and the intervening disk. Alternative routes include retrograde seeding through the prevertebral venous plexus and direct inoculation during spinal surgery, epidural procedures, or trauma. Contiguous-focus disease follows bone trauma or surgery, with wound infection after sternotomy, open-fracture repair, or prosthetic-joint placement. Vascular-insufficiency/neuropathic disease, most often in poorly controlled diabetes, arises from progressively deep foot infection driven by ischemia of skin, soft tissue, and bone combined with motor, sensory, and autonomic neuropathy. Foreign material is highly vulnerable: host proteins such as fibronectin coat the device, favoring staphylococcal adherence and biofilm formation that resists phagocytosis and antibiotics. Failure of timely or adequate therapy leads to bone necrosis (sequestra) and progression to chronic osteomyelitis with sinus tracts and soft-tissue destruction (Harrison 22e p.1061-1066).","additionalDifferentials":["Pyelonephritis (febrile back pain mimic)","Pancreatitis","Viral syndromes","Erosive osteochondrosis (most common MRI-based alternative diagnosis to vertebral osteomyelitis)","Bone metastases","Osteoporotic vertebral fracture"],"infectionMimics":["Spinal epidural abscess from another source","Septic arthritis adjacent to bone","Tuberculous spondylitis (Pott's disease) versus pyogenic osteomyelitis","Brucellar spondylitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Seronegative spondyloarthritis (ankylosing spondylitis, psoriatic, reactive, enteropathic arthritis), including Andersson erosive diskovertebral lesions","Spinal stenosis","Gouty spondylodiskitis","Septic/aseptic bone necrosis","Osteoporotic fracture and bone metastases on imaging"],"additionalInvestigations":["CRP and ESR as sensitive screening/monitoring markers (CRP elevated in ~100%, ESR in ~98% of vertebral cases)","Blood cultures before any antibiotics (positivity 30-78%, heavily reduced by pretreatment)","CT-guided or open bone biopsy when blood cultures are negative, with aerobic, anaerobic, and fungal culture plus histopathology","Mycobacterial and brucellar testing in subacute/chronic, granulomatous, or epidemiologically suggestive cases","Nonculture molecular methods (eubacterial/multiplex PCR, metagenomics) when cultures are negative despite suggestive histology, to detect unusual organisms (e.g., Tropheryma whipplei, Helicobacter)","MRI as gold standard; CT to guide biopsy and assess sequestra; plain radiography useful mainly in subacute/chronic disease","18F-FDG PET when MRI contraindicated or multifocal/implant disease suspected (sensitive but not specific; useful to exclude PJI)","SPECT/CT with labeled leukocytes or antigranulocyte antibodies for osteomyelitis of >1 year duration; three-phase bone scan limited within ~1 year of surgery due to ongoing bone remodeling","Synovial fluid leukocyte count and alpha-defensin, multiple intraoperative tissue cultures/histology, and sonication of explanted hardware for biofilm organisms in suspected PJI"],"redFlags":["New or progressive neurologic deficit (radiculopathy, weakness, sensory loss, bowel/bladder dysfunction, paralysis) suggesting spinal epidural abscess, present in about one-third of vertebral cases","Cervical or thoracic spinal involvement and S. aureus etiology, identified risk factors for severe neurologic deficit","Persistent or rising CRP, persistent pain, or sepsis syndrome indicating undrained abscess or treatment failure","Persistent pain after CRP normalization, suggesting mechanical complication such as severe osteonecrosis or spinal instability requiring orthopedic evaluation","Sinus tract, wound-healing impairment, or implant loosening signaling deep device-related infection"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on early diagnosis, presence of bone necrosis, and whether an implant is involved. Acute hematogenous vertebral and long-bone osteomyelitis without sequestra or hardware can usually be cured with antimicrobials alone; a randomized trial in pyogenic vertebral osteomyelitis reported ~91% cure at one year with no difference between 6- and 12-week regimens. Failure of adequate treatment, open fractures, and unaddressed implant infection drive progression to chronic osteomyelitis with sinus tracts and soft-tissue damage. In fracture-related infection, implant-retention success falls with chronicity (about 86-100% in early, 82-89% in delayed, and ~67% in late infection). In periprosthetic joint infection, implant retention offers >80% infection-free survival only when the infection is acute, the implant is stable, the pathogen is susceptible to a biofilm-active agent, and the soft tissues are intact; in patients with severe comorbidity, lifelong suppressive therapy may be the chosen goal rather than cure (Harrison 22e p.1063-1067).","pitfalls":["Starting empiric antibiotics before obtaining cultures in a non-septic patient, which markedly lowers culture yield and obscures the microbiologic diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1062-1063).","Relying on plain radiographs or the leukocyte count in acute disease, both of which have low sensitivity; normal radiographs do not exclude acute osteomyelitis (Harrison 22e p.1062).","Using follow-up MRI to judge treatment response in uncomplicated cases, since radiologic improvement correlates poorly with clinical healing; imaging follow-up is reserved for pyogenic complications (Harrison 22e p.1063).","Misinterpreting a three-phase bone scan or 18F-FDG PET within ~1 year of surgery, when ongoing bone remodeling produces nonspecific uptake (Harrison 22e p.1064, 1066).","Assuming antimicrobials alone will cure device-associated osteomyelitis or PJI; without appropriate surgery such therapy is generally only suppressive (Harrison 22e p.1061, 1067).","Dismissing coagulase-negative staphylococci or Cutibacterium acnes as contaminants, when in the setting of an implant or prolonged bacteremia they may be the true low-virulence pathogens (Harrison 22e p.1062).","Failing to consider tuberculous or brucellar disease in patients from or traveling to endemic regions, where subacute/chronic vertebral osteomyelitis predominates (Harrison 22e p.1063-1065)."],"severityClassification":"No single accepted comprehensive scheme exists. Osteomyelitis is categorized by pathogenesis (hematogenous, contiguous, vascular insufficiency/neuropathic), by course (acute versus subacute/chronic, with the presence or absence of sequestra being the decisive feature), and by location (long bone, vertebral, periarticular). The Cierny-Mader staging system classifies long-bone disease by anatomic extent, host physiologic class (systemic and local compromise of immunity, metabolism, and vascularity), and radiographic findings, and is used mainly to guide surgical management. Implant-associated disease is further classified by timing relative to surgery (e.g., early/acute, delayed, late), and periprosthetic joint infection is grouped for therapeutic decisions as acute hematogenous, early postinterventional, or chronic (Harrison 22e p.1061, 1066).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1061","Harrison 22e p.1062","Harrison 22e p.1063","Harrison 22e p.1064","Harrison 22e p.1066","Harrison 22e p.1067"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteoporosis":{"name":"Osteoporosis","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic Bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["low bone density"],"chapter":423,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3291","clinicalPearls":["WHO operationally defines osteoporosis as a bone density T-score of -2.5 or below (>=2.5 SD below the young-adult mean for same sex/race), measured at lumbar spine, total hip, or femoral neck; osteopenia (low bone density) is a T-score of -1.0 to -2.5 and is a risk factor for developing osteoporosis (Harrison 22e p.3291).","A fragility fracture (hip or vertebral) by definition establishes osteoporosis and warrants treatment, regardless of the T-score; patients presenting with hip or spine fractures by definition have osteoporosis (Harrison 22e p.3291, p.3298).","Osteoporosis is most common among postmenopausal women, but ~30% of fragility fractures occur in men; secondary osteoporosis arises from other underlying diseases (Harrison 22e p.3291).",">50% of fractures, including hip fractures, among postmenopausal women occur in individuals with osteopenia rather than densitometric osteoporosis, because that population is much larger - hence the shift toward absolute fracture-risk assessment (Harrison 22e p.3291).","FRAX and the Garvan Fracture Risk Calculator estimate 10-year probability of hip or major osteoporotic fracture by combining age, sex, and clinical risk factors with or without BMD, and inform country-specific intervention thresholds distinct from diagnostic thresholds (Harrison 22e p.3291, p.3298).","Glucocorticoids are the most common cause of medication-induced osteoporosis (Harrison 22e p.3296).","Any fracture (excluding fingers, toes, face, skull) in a woman aged >50 or a man aged >60 should trigger investigation for osteoporosis (Harrison 22e p.3293)."],"pathophysiology":"Osteoporosis is reduced bone strength leading to skeletal fragility and fractures; BMD alone underestimates fragility because microarchitectural deterioration and component misalignment also contribute. A low peak bone mass (genetic, hormonal, nutritional) may underlie disease, with superimposed age-related and extrinsic changes in bone remodeling. In adults remodeling (not modeling) predominates and serves to repair microdamage and supply calcium. Estrogen deficiency drives bone loss by (1) activating new remodeling sites and (2) creating an imbalance favoring resorption over formation: loss of estrogen increases RANKL and reduces osteoprotegerin, increasing osteoclast formation/recruitment, while shortening osteoblast life span and prolonging osteoclast longevity/activity. Trabecular bone (80% of surface area) is preferentially affected, so vertebral fractures are the earliest skeletal consequence. Estrogen also regulates remodeling in men. Heritability of BMD is ~50-80%; LRP5 activating mutations cause high bone mass and inactivating mutations cause osteoporosis-pseudoglioma syndrome (Harrison 22e p.3293-3295).","additionalDifferentials":["Osteomalacia (severe vitamin D deficiency in adults)","Multiple myeloma masquerading as generalized osteoporosis","Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS)","Primary hyperparathyroidism","Thyrotoxicosis / excessive thyroxine","Cushing's syndrome","Malabsorption / celiac disease","Mastocytosis","Osteogenesis imperfecta"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Myeloma (presents with bone pain and characteristic punched-out lesions; can masquerade as generalized osteoporosis)","Osteomalacia from vitamin D deficiency","Metabolic bone disease such as CKD-mineral bone disorder","Osteoarthritis/spinal spondylosis (bone spurs falsely raise spine BMD on DXA)"],"additionalInvestigations":["DXA of lumbar spine and hip (standard for BMD; T-scores vs young adults, Z-scores vs age/sex-matched reference)","Vertebral imaging per age/BMD/risk-factor indications (e.g. women >=70, men >=80; or with low-trauma fracture, height loss >=4 cm, glucocorticoid use)","Complete blood count; serum and 24-h urine calcium; renal and liver function tests","Serum PTH (and PTHrP) when hypercalcemia is present to distinguish hyperparathyroidism from malignancy","Serum 25(OH)D in those with fragility fracture or osteoporotic-range BMD","TSH to evaluate for hyperthyroidism","Serum/urine protein electrophoresis and serum free light chains to exclude myeloma; bone marrow biopsy if equivocal","Transglutaminase IgA antibodies for celiac disease","24-h urinary free cortisol or dexamethasone-suppressed cortisol when Cushing's suspected","24-h urine histamine or serum tryptase when mastocytosis suspected","Serum testosterone in men with osteoporosis","Biochemical bone turnover markers (P1NP, osteocalcin, bone-specific ALP for formation; serum/urine N- and C-telopeptide for resorption) mainly to monitor treatment response","Bone biopsy with double tetracycline labeling (now largely replaced; retained for CKD-MBD and research)"],"redFlags":["Vertebral fracture (even asymptomatic) is a major sign of skeletal fragility and markedly increases risk of subsequent fracture, especially within the first 12-24 months (imminent fracture risk)","Significant height loss (>4 cm) suggesting occult vertebral fracture","Hip fracture - high mortality (20-25% die within the year) and major loss of independence","More chronic severe pain after vertebral fracture should raise suspicion of multiple myeloma or other underlying bone condition"],"prognosis":"Lifetime osteoporotic fracture risk for a 50-year-old Caucasian woman is ~50% and ~25% for a man; lifetime hip fracture probability ~14% (women) and ~5% (men), roughly half for African Americans. Hip fractures carry high morbidity and mortality with 20-25% of patients dying within the year (higher among men and African Americans), and ~30% of survivors requiring long-term care, many never regaining prior independence. A first fracture exponentially increases risk of further fracture, especially in the first year; almost 20% of women sustain a second fracture within 2 years (Harrison 22e p.3291-3293).","pitfalls":["Failing to exclude secondary causes of osteoporosis (e.g. myeloma, hyperparathyroidism, thyrotoxicosis, Cushing's, malabsorption/celiac, hypogonadism, mastocytosis) before attributing low bone mass to primary disease, particularly in patients with fractures or unexpectedly low Z-scores.","Excluding a patient from osteoporosis evaluation simply because a fracture was traumatic - patients with osteoporosis are also more likely to sustain traumatic fractures, and any fracture warrants skeletal assessment.","Relying solely on BMD, which can underestimate fragility (microarchitectural deterioration not captured) and is falsely elevated in the spine by osteoarthritic bone spurs/spondylosis; small slim people show falsely low DXA values.","Fewer than 20% of fracture patients are investigated or treated for osteoporosis within 6 months; postfracture care remains fragmented.","Drug holidays are appropriate to consider for oral/IV bisphosphonates but should be avoided for denosumab and anabolic agents because of more rapid offset."],"severityClassification":"WHO densitometric categories: normal (T-score >= -1.0), osteopenia/low bone density (T-score -1.0 to -2.5), osteoporosis (T-score <= -2.5 at lumbar spine, femoral neck, or total hip). Presence of a fragility fracture (notably hip or vertebral) defines established/clinical osteoporosis regardless of T-score. Absolute fracture risk (FRAX/Garvan 10-year hip and major osteoporotic fracture probability) is increasingly used alongside or instead of T-score for treatment decisions (Harrison 22e p.3291, p.3297-3298).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3291"],"crossLinks":[]},"osteosarcoma":{"name":"Osteosarcoma","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["osteogenic sarcoma","primary bone sarcoma","malignant bone tumour"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest primary malignant bone tumour, arising most often in adolescents and young adults around the knee (distal femur and proximal tibia) and proximal humerus","Presents with progressive deep bone pain, often worse at night and not related to activity, sometimes with a palpable mass, swelling, or a pathological fracture","Radiographs may show bone destruction, new bone formation, a 'sunburst' spiculated pattern, and periosteal elevation (Codman's triangle)","Any persistent, unexplained, or night bone pain and swelling in a young person should raise suspicion and prompt urgent imaging rather than reassurance","Diagnosis requires biopsy, which should be carefully planned, ideally at the treating specialist centre, so as not to compromise later limb-salvage surgery","Staging with MRI of the whole bone and CT of the chest is essential, as the lungs are the commonest site of metastasis","Treatment is multidisciplinary, combining chemotherapy with surgical resection (limb salvage where feasible, otherwise amputation), transforming what was once a rapidly fatal disease","A second peak occurs in older adults, often secondary to Paget's disease or previous irradiation"],"pathophysiology":"Osteosarcoma is a malignant tumour of mesenchymal cells that produce osteoid or immature bone. It typically arises in the metaphysis of rapidly growing long bones, explaining its predilection for the knee region in adolescence during the growth spurt. The tumour destroys normal bone while laying down disorganised neoplastic osteoid, lifts and stimulates the periosteum, and breaks through the cortex to form a soft-tissue mass. It spreads haematogenously, with a strong tendency to metastasise to the lungs, and micrometastases are often present at diagnosis even when not radiologically evident. Predisposing factors include prior radiation, Paget's disease of bone, and genetic conditions such as hereditary retinoblastoma and Li-Fraumeni syndrome.","additionalDifferentials":["Ewing's sarcoma","Osteomyelitis (can mimic tumour clinically and radiologically)","Chondrosarcoma","Metastatic bone disease","Benign but aggressive lesions (e.g. giant cell tumour, osteoblastoma)","Aneurysmal bone cyst","Stress fracture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs of the affected region as the initial imaging","MRI of the whole involved bone to define extent, soft-tissue mass, and skip lesions","CT of the chest to detect pulmonary metastases","Biopsy (carefully planned at the treating centre) for histological diagnosis","Bone scan or PET for staging and detection of distant disease, plus baseline bloods including alkaline phosphatase and LDH"],"redFlags":["Persistent or night-time bone pain with swelling in a child, adolescent, or young adult requiring urgent specialist referral","Pathological fracture through a destructive bone lesion","A biopsy performed outside the treating centre risking tumour contamination and jeopardising limb salvage","Rapidly enlarging soft-tissue mass over bone"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends principally on the presence of metastases at diagnosis and the tumour's response to chemotherapy; with modern combined chemotherapy and surgery many patients with localised, chemosensitive disease achieve long-term survival, whereas metastatic or chemoresistant disease carries a substantially worse outlook.","pitfalls":["Dismissing persistent bone pain in a young person as growing pains or a sports injury, delaying diagnosis","Performing an ill-planned biopsy that contaminates tissue planes and precludes limb salvage","Confusing osteosarcoma with osteomyelitis and vice versa without adequate imaging and histology","Failing to stage the chest and missing pulmonary metastases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"otitis_externa":{"name":"Otitis externa","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["external ear infection","swimmer's ear","diffuse otitis externa"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Inflammation of the external auditory canal presenting with ear pain, itching, discharge and a feeling of fullness","Pain on moving the tragus or pinna is a hallmark that helps distinguish it from middle ear disease","Moisture, trauma from cotton buds, and a disrupted acidic canal environment are common predisposing factors","The localised furuncular form is a staphylococcal infection of a hair follicle in the outer cartilaginous canal, while the diffuse form is often associated with Pseudomonas","Otomycosis (fungal otitis externa) typically shows itching with visible fungal debris such as blotting-paper or fluffy growth in the canal","Management principles include meticulous aural toileting, topical antimicrobial and anti-inflammatory drops, analgesia and keeping the ear dry","Malignant (necrotising) otitis externa is a serious osteomyelitis of the skull base, seen mainly in elderly diabetics and the immunocompromised, and demands urgent recognition","Recurrent or treatment-resistant disease should prompt review for underlying dermatological conditions, diabetes or immunosuppression"],"pathophysiology":"Otitis externa is inflammation of the skin lining the external auditory canal. Breakdown of the canal's protective barrier, through excess moisture, mechanical trauma, cerumen removal, or altered pH, allows bacterial or fungal overgrowth. The resulting inflammation causes oedema, discharge and narrowing of the canal, producing pain and conductive hearing loss when the lumen is occluded. In malignant otitis externa, infection (typically Pseudomonas) spreads from the soft tissue into the temporal bone and skull base, causing progressive osteomyelitis and potential cranial nerve involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute otitis media with perforation and discharge","Chronic suppurative otitis media","Furuncle of the canal","Otomycosis","Eczematous or seborrhoeic dermatitis of the canal","Malignancy of the external canal"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Otoscopy or otomicroscopy to inspect the canal and clear debris","Swab of discharge for bacterial and fungal culture in resistant cases","Blood glucose and inflammatory markers when necrotising disease is suspected","CT or MRI of the temporal bone and skull base for suspected malignant otitis externa","Biopsy of granulation tissue where malignancy or necrotising disease is a concern"],"redFlags":["Severe, disproportionate pain with granulation tissue in an elderly diabetic suggesting necrotising otitis externa","Cranial nerve palsies (especially facial nerve) indicating skull base osteomyelitis","Spreading cellulitis, fever or systemic upset","Failure to respond to standard topical therapy"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated otitis externa usually resolves with topical treatment and ear care, but malignant otitis externa is potentially life-threatening and requires prolonged systemic therapy and close monitoring.","pitfalls":["Missing a perforation or middle ear disease as the source of discharge","Overlooking necrotising otitis externa in a diabetic with severe pain","Ineffective treatment because the canal was not adequately cleaned","Failing to identify and treat an underlying fungal or dermatological cause"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"otitis_media_with_effusion":{"name":"Otitis media with effusion","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["OME","glue ear","serous otitis media","secretory otitis media"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by a non-infected fluid collection in the middle ear behind an intact tympanic membrane, without acute signs of infection","A leading cause of acquired conductive hearing loss in children and often presents as inattention, poor school performance or delayed speech","Frequently follows acute otitis media or arises from persistent Eustachian tube dysfunction, often related to adenoid hypertrophy","Otoscopy may reveal a dull, retracted drum, an air-fluid level or bubbles, and reduced mobility","Tympanometry showing a flat (type B) trace and audiometry demonstrating conductive loss support the diagnosis","In adults, unilateral persistent effusion should prompt examination of the postnasal space to exclude a nasopharyngeal tumour","Many cases resolve spontaneously, so an initial period of watchful waiting with reassessment is appropriate","Persistent effusion with significant hearing loss may be managed with ventilation (grommet) tubes, often alongside adenoidectomy"],"pathophysiology":"Otitis media with effusion results from prolonged Eustachian tube dysfunction that impairs middle ear ventilation. Persistent negative pressure encourages transudation of fluid and metaplasia of the middle ear mucosa toward a secretory epithelium, producing a serous or mucoid (glue-like) effusion. The retained fluid dampens ossicular movement and causes a conductive hearing loss. Contributing factors include adenoid hypertrophy, recurrent upper respiratory infections, allergy, cleft palate and, in adults, nasopharyngeal masses obstructing the tube.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute otitis media","Chronic suppurative otitis media","Congenital ossicular or cochlear hearing loss","Tympanosclerosis","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma (in unilateral adult cases)","Cholesteatoma with retraction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pneumatic otoscopy to assess drum mobility","Tympanometry to detect middle ear fluid and negative pressure","Pure tone audiometry to quantify conductive hearing loss","Nasopharyngeal examination or endoscopy in persistent unilateral adult effusion","Assessment of adenoids and allergic or sinonasal contributors"],"redFlags":["Unilateral persistent effusion in an adult raising concern for nasopharyngeal carcinoma","Progressive hearing loss affecting speech and language development in a child","Signs of retraction pocket or cholesteatoma developing","Effusion associated with cranial nerve signs or neck mass"],"prognosis":"The majority of childhood cases resolve spontaneously within weeks to months; persistent disease can impair hearing and language development but generally responds well to ventilation tubes and treatment of underlying causes.","pitfalls":["Overprescribing antibiotics for a fundamentally non-infective effusion","Failing to examine the nasopharynx in an adult with unilateral effusion","Intervening surgically too early before allowing spontaneous resolution","Missing associated speech, language or educational impact in children"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"otosclerosis":{"name":"Otosclerosis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["otospongiosis","stapedial otosclerosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A localised disease of the bony labyrinth in which normal bone is replaced by spongy, vascular new bone that most often fixes the stapes footplate","Typically presents in young to middle-aged adults with slowly progressive bilateral conductive hearing loss and a family history","Often worsens during pregnancy, suggesting a hormonal influence, and is more common in women","Patients frequently hear better in noisy surroundings (paracusis Willisii) and may speak softly","The tympanic membrane is characteristically normal; a pink flush over the promontory (Schwartze sign) reflects active vascular disease","Tuning fork tests show a negative Rinne and audiometry reveals conductive loss, often with the characteristic Carhart notch dip in bone conduction around 2 kHz","Cochlear otosclerosis can add a sensorineural component leading to mixed hearing loss","Management options include a hearing aid, or stapes surgery (stapedectomy/stapedotomy with a prosthesis) to restore ossicular movement; sodium fluoride may be used to stabilise active disease"],"pathophysiology":"Otosclerosis is a primary disorder of endochondral bone of the otic capsule in which foci of normal lamellar bone are resorbed and replaced by irregular, highly cellular and vascular spongy bone that later matures into dense sclerotic bone. The commonest site is the region of the fissula ante fenestram just anterior to the oval window; as the focus enlarges it invades and fixes the stapes footplate, producing progressive conductive hearing loss. Extension of foci into the cochlear endosteum can damage the neural elements and cause sensorineural loss. The condition is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern with incomplete penetrance, and hormonal factors appear to modulate its activity.","additionalDifferentials":["Ossicular fixation or discontinuity from other causes","Congenital stapes fixation","Adhesive otitis media or tympanosclerosis","Otitis media with effusion","Van der Hoeve syndrome (otosclerosis with osteogenesis imperfecta)","Paget disease of the temporal bone","Superior semicircular canal dehiscence mimicking conductive loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pure tone audiometry to document conductive or mixed loss and the Carhart notch","Tuning fork tests to confirm conductive hearing loss","Tympanometry, typically showing normal to reduced compliance, with absent stapedial reflexes","Speech audiometry to assess discrimination before surgery","High-resolution CT of the temporal bone to map active foci and exclude other pathology in selected cases"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive or asymmetrical sensorineural loss prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Vertigo or disequilibrium suggesting labyrinthine or alternative pathology","Sudden deterioration or facial nerve symptoms after stapes surgery requiring urgent review","Unilateral pulsatile tinnitus or other features that raise concern for a vascular or retrocochlear lesion"],"prognosis":"The disease is progressive but stapes surgery closes the conductive gap and restores useful hearing in most suitably selected patients, while hearing aids remain an effective non-surgical option; established cochlear involvement, however, is not reversible.","pitfalls":["Overlooking otosclerosis when the drum looks normal and attributing the loss to wax or effusion","Failing to counsel about the small risk of dead ear and sensorineural loss with stapes surgery","Missing a coexisting sensorineural (cochlear) component that limits surgical benefit","Not recognising the Carhart notch as a bone conduction artefact rather than true cochlear loss"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"ovarian_cancer":{"name":"Ovarian Cancer","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["carcinoma of the ovary","ovarian carcinoma","epithelial ovarian cancer","ovarian malignancy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most cases are epithelial in origin, and high-grade serous carcinoma, now thought largely to arise from the distal fallopian tube, is the commonest and most aggressive subtype","Symptoms are frequently vague and persistent, including abdominal bloating, early satiety, pelvic or abdominal discomfort, and urinary urgency, so a high index of suspicion is needed particularly in women over fifty","Because there is no reliable early symptom or screening test, the majority present with advanced intra-abdominal disease","Inherited mutations in BRCA1 and BRCA2 and Lynch syndrome markedly increase lifetime risk and should prompt genetic assessment","A pelvic mass combined with ascites and a raised CA-125 in a postmenopausal woman is highly suggestive and warrants specialist referral","Management centres on maximal cytoreductive surgery to remove all visible tumour combined with platinum-based chemotherapy","Risk assessment tools that combine ultrasound features, menopausal status, and CA-125 help triage women to gynaecological oncology","Germ cell and sex cord-stromal tumours are important in younger women and can produce hormonal effects or tumour markers such as AFP, hCG, or inhibin"],"pathophysiology":"High-grade serous ovarian cancer is now believed to originate frequently from serous tubal intraepithelial carcinoma in the fimbrial end of the fallopian tube, with early TP53 mutation and genomic instability, often in the context of homologous recombination deficiency from BRCA pathway defects. Malignant cells exfoliate and disseminate across the peritoneal cavity, seeding the omentum, bowel serosa, and diaphragm, producing ascites through increased vascular permeability and lymphatic obstruction. Other histological types arise from ovarian surface epithelium, germ cells, or sex cord-stromal cells with distinct biology.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign ovarian cyst or endometrioma","Functional ovarian cyst","Uterine fibroids or other pelvic mass","Metastatic gastrointestinal cancer to the ovary (Krukenberg tumour)","Tubo-ovarian abscess","Primary peritoneal or fallopian tube carcinoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal and transabdominal pelvic ultrasound to characterise the mass","Serum CA-125 and, in younger women, germ cell markers (AFP, hCG, LDH) and inhibin","CT of the abdomen and pelvis to assess disease extent and operability","Risk-of-malignancy scoring combining ultrasound, menopausal status and tumour markers","Histological confirmation from surgical specimen or image-guided biopsy where surgery is not upfront","Genetic testing for BRCA and mismatch repair mutations"],"redFlags":["Rapidly accumulating ascites with breathlessness or abdominal distension","Bowel obstruction from peritoneal or omental disease","Marked weight loss and cachexia suggesting advanced malignancy","Pleural effusion indicating spread beyond the abdomen","A complex solid-cystic pelvic mass with markedly elevated CA-125 in a postmenopausal woman"],"prognosis":"Overall survival is dominated by stage at presentation and by the completeness of surgical cytoreduction; disease confined to the ovary is potentially curable, whereas widespread peritoneal disease carries a poorer outlook despite platinum sensitivity. Tumour grade, histological subtype, and BRCA/homologous recombination status influence response to chemotherapy and to maintenance agents such as PARP inhibitors, and relapse is common in advanced disease.","pitfalls":["Dismissing persistent bloating and abdominal symptoms in older women as functional bowel disturbance","Assuming a normal CA-125 excludes malignancy, as it can be normal in early or non-serous tumours","Rupturing or morcellating an undiagnosed malignant cyst during surgery","Failing to refer suspicious masses to a gynaecological oncology centre before intervention","Overlooking germ cell tumours in young women by not checking age-appropriate tumour markers"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"ovarian_torsion":{"name":"Ovarian Torsion","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["adnexal torsion","torsion of the ovary","ovarian and tubal torsion","twisted ovarian cyst"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Torsion is a gynaecological emergency in which the ovary, often with the fallopian tube, twists on its pedicle and compromises its blood supply, threatening ovarian viability","It classically presents with sudden severe unilateral pelvic pain, often colicky and associated with nausea and vomiting, sometimes provoked by exertion or a change in position","An enlarged ovary or an ovarian mass, particularly cysts around five centimetres or larger, predisposes to torsion, so it is uncommon in a normal-sized ovary","Doppler ultrasound may show reduced or absent flow, but preserved arterial flow does not exclude torsion because dual blood supply and intermittent twisting can maintain signals","Torsion can occur in pregnancy, especially after ovulation induction with enlarged ovaries, and in premenarchal girls in whom the diagnosis is easily missed","Prompt surgical intervention, usually laparoscopic detorsion with conservation of the ovary, is preferred over oophorectomy even when the ovary looks dusky, as tissue often recovers","The classic error is delay: repeated observation while awaiting imaging or normalisation of pain risks irreversible ischaemic loss of the ovary","Intermittent torsion produces recurrent self-limiting episodes of pain and warrants evaluation before a definitive infarction occurs"],"pathophysiology":"When the ovary or adnexa rotates about the infundibulopelvic and utero-ovarian ligaments, the lower-pressure venous and lymphatic drainage is occluded first, producing congestion and marked ovarian oedema and enlargement. Continued or tightening rotation eventually compromises arterial inflow, leading to ischaemia, haemorrhagic infarction, and necrosis. Because arterial flow may persist longer than venous outflow owing to the thicker arterial wall and dual blood supply, viability can be maintained for a variable period, defining a window in which detorsion can salvage the ovary.","additionalDifferentials":["Ruptured or haemorrhagic ovarian cyst","Ectopic pregnancy","Acute appendicitis","Pelvic inflammatory disease or tubo-ovarian abscess","Ureteric colic","Degenerating or torting pedunculated fibroid"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal or transabdominal pelvic ultrasound with Doppler to assess ovarian size, mass, and blood flow","Urine or serum beta-hCG to exclude pregnancy and ectopic","Full blood count and inflammatory markers to help exclude infective or appendiceal causes","Diagnostic laparoscopy, which is both confirmatory and therapeutic when suspicion is high","Pelvic MRI in selected non-acute or pregnant cases when ultrasound is inconclusive"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe unilateral pelvic pain with an adnexal mass, mandating urgent assessment to preserve the ovary","Persistent pain with peritonism suggesting ischaemia or necrosis","Haemodynamic instability raising concern for haemorrhage or a ruptured ectopic","Signs of sepsis suggesting necrotic or infected adnexa","Recurrent episodic pain indicating intermittent torsion needing prompt surgical evaluation"],"prognosis":"When detorsion is performed promptly the ovary usually recovers function even if it appeared ischaemic at surgery, and future fertility is generally preserved. Delayed diagnosis leads to infarction requiring oophorectomy and loss of that ovary's function; recurrence can occur, and oophoropexy may be considered in selected cases such as recurrent torsion or a normal ovary in a child.","pitfalls":["Excluding torsion because Doppler shows preserved arterial flow","Delaying surgery for repeated imaging or observation while ischaemia progresses","Performing oophorectomy on a dusky ovary that would recover after simple detorsion","Failing to consider torsion in premenarchal girls and pregnant women","Missing intermittent torsion by attributing recurrent pain to functional cysts"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pagets_disease_bone":{"name":"Paget's Disease of Bone","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic Bone","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["osteitis deformans"],"chapter":424,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3311-3314","clinicalPearls":["A localized bone-remodeling disorder affecting widespread, noncontiguous areas of the skeleton; initiated by overactive osteoclastic resorption followed by compensatory osteoblastic new bone formation, producing a disorganized mosaic of woven and lamellar bone (p.3311).","Most patients are asymptomatic; diagnosis is often made incidentally from an elevated alkaline phosphatase (ALP) on routine blood chemistry or an abnormal skeletal radiograph obtained for another indication (p.3311, p.3312-3313).","Serum calcium and phosphate levels are normal in Paget's disease; isolated/raised ALP with normal calcium is the characteristic biochemical picture (p.3314).","Sites most commonly involved are the pelvis, vertebral bodies, skull, femur, and tibia; familial early-onset cases often have numerous active sites (p.3313).","The most common presenting symptom is pain, which may arise from increased bony vascularity, expanding lytic lesions, fractures, bowing, or deformity; long-bone bowing causes gait abnormalities and secondary osteoarthritis of hip or knee (p.3313).","Skull involvement may cause headaches, frontal bossing, increased head size, and narrowing of cranial foramina leading to hearing loss (cochlear nerve damage from temporal bone involvement), cranial nerve palsies, and platybasia with risk of brainstem compression (p.3313).","For most patients, serum total ALP remains the test of choice for both diagnosis and assessing response to therapy; bone resorption markers (N-telopeptide, C-telopeptide) fall more rapidly with treatment than ALP (p.3313).","Radionuclide 99mTc bone scans are less specific but more sensitive than standard radiographs for identifying sites of active skeletal lesions (p.3313)."],"pathophysiology":"The principal abnormality is increased number and activity of osteoclasts, which are large, increased 10- to 100-fold in number, and contain many nuclei (up to 100 vs 3-5 in normal osteoclasts), creating a sevenfold rise in resorptive surfaces. Contributing mechanisms include osteoclast precursor hypersensitivity to 1,25(OH)2D3, hyperresponsiveness to RANK ligand (RANKL), increased RANKL expression by marrow stromal cells, increased IL-6 (raising precursor recruitment), increased c-fos expression, and overexpression of the antiapoptotic oncogene Bcl-2. Osteoblasts are recruited to resorption sites and lay down large amounts of new bone matrix, so turnover is high and bone mass is normal or increased (not reduced) unless there is concomitant calcium/vitamin D deficiency. The process passes through an initial osteolytic phase (prominent resorption and hypervascularization, seen radiographically as an advancing lytic wedge or 'blade of grass' lesion), an active mixed phase replacing lamellar bone with haphazard woven bone (marrow may be replaced by fibrous tissue), and a final sclerotic ('burned-out') phase of hard, dense, less vascular mosaic bone; all three phases may coexist at different sites. Etiology remains unknown but evidence supports both genetic (family history in 15-25%; familial syndromes linked to TNFRSF11B/osteoprotegerin, TNFRSF11A/RANK, profilin 1, valosin-containing protein, and the common SQSTM1/p62 mutation) and viral (paramyxovirus-like inclusions and viral mRNA in osteoclasts) contributions (p.3311-3313).","additionalDifferentials":["Osteosarcoma or other sarcoma arising in long-standing pagetic bone (new pain in a long-standing lesion)","Osteoclast-rich benign giant cell tumor arising adjacent to pagetic bone (responds to glucocorticoids)","Metastatic disease in bone (assessed by MRI; definitive diagnosis of malignancy often requires bone biopsy)","Other causes of an isolated raised alkaline phosphatase"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Familial bone disorders that clinically and radiographically resemble Paget's disease with more severe, earlier-onset presentation: juvenile Paget's disease (familial idiopathic hyperphosphatasia, TNFRSF11B/osteoprotegerin deletion), familial expansile osteolysis, expansile skeletal hyperphosphatasia, and early-onset Paget's disease (TNFRSF11A/RANK mutations)","Inclusion body myopathy with Paget's disease and frontotemporal dementia (IBMPFD; valosin-containing protein mutation)","Sarcoma, giant cell tumor, or metastatic disease producing focal changes in or near pagetic bone"],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain radiographs (typical findings: bone enlargement/expansion, cortical thickening, coarsened trabeculae, lytic and sclerotic changes; skull 'cotton wool'/osteoporosis circumscripta; 'picture frame' or 'ivory' vertebra; pelvic 'brim sign' and protrusio acetabuli; long-bone bowing)","Radionuclide 99mTc bone scan (more sensitive but less specific than radiographs for active lesions)","Serum total alkaline phosphatase (ALP) - first-line for diagnosis and monitoring; bone-specific ALP may be raised when total ALP is normal in single-site disease","Bone formation marker P1NP (reflects disease activity; can be used instead of total ALP)","Bone resorption markers (serum/urine N-telopeptide or C-telopeptide)","Serum calcium and phosphate (normal in Paget's disease)","CT for assessment of possible fracture; MRI to assess possible sarcoma, giant cell tumor, or metastatic disease","Bone biopsy for definitive diagnosis of suspected malignancy","Note: serum osteocalcin is NOT always elevated and is NOT recommended for diagnosis or management"],"redFlags":["New pain in a long-standing pagetic lesion - suggests sarcomatous (most often osteosarcoma) transformation, although neoplasms arising from pagetic bone are rare (<0.5%)","High-output cardiac state and cardiac enlargement from extensive arteriovenous shunting when large portions (15-35%) of the skeleton are involved with high disease activity (e.g., ALP four times above normal); overt high-output heart failure is relatively rare and usually requires concomitant cardiac pathology","Platybasia (softening of the skull base) with risk of brainstem compression; spinal cord compression from bone enlargement or vascular steal syndrome","Fractures, typically in long bones at areas of active or advancing lytic lesions (common at femoral shaft and subtrochanteric regions)","Discovery of hypercalcemia (even with immobilization) should prompt a search for another cause of hypercalcemia"],"prognosis":"Most patients are asymptomatic and have a benign course; for unclear reasons the prevalence and severity are decreasing and the age of diagnosis is increasing. Effective potent antiresorptive (bisphosphonate) therapy can normalize bone turnover and is associated with an apparently decreasing incidence of sarcoma. Serious complications - fractures, deformity, secondary osteoarthritis, neurologic compromise (hearing loss, cranial nerve palsies, cord/brainstem compression), high-output cardiac state, and rare (<0.5%) malignant transformation (usually osteosarcoma) - drive morbidity. Whether early therapy prevents late complications remains to be determined; deformities may take many years to manifest (p.3312-3314).","pitfalls":["Assuming an isolated raised ALP is benign - it can be the only clue to asymptomatic Paget's disease and warrants workup (radiograph plus bone scan).","A symptomatic patient with progression at a single site may have a normal total ALP but an increased bone-specific ALP.","Using serum osteocalcin to diagnose or monitor disease - it is not always elevated and is not recommended.","Attributing hypercalcemia to Paget's disease/immobilization - normal calcium and phosphate are expected, so hypercalcemia should prompt a search for another cause.","Starting bisphosphonate therapy without ensuring adequate calcium and vitamin D intake first - very active bone formation with rapid resorption suppression can precipitate hypocalcemia or mild secondary hyperparathyroidism."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not give a formal staging system. Disease activity is gauged by the extent of skeletal involvement and the degree of bone-turnover marker elevation (notably total ALP, with ALP roughly four times above normal cited as marking high activity and cardiovascular risk when 15-35% of the skeleton is involved). Pathologically the disease evolves through three phases that may coexist: an osteolytic phase, an active mixed (woven-bone) phase, and a final sclerotic 'burned-out' phase (p.3313-3314).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3311-3314"],"crossLinks":[]},"pancreatic_cancer":{"name":"Pancreatic Cancer","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pancreatic adenocarcinoma"],"chapter":88,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.671","clinicalPearls":["The classic presentation of cancer arising in the head of the pancreas is 'painless' jaundice; jaundice is visually detectable once bilirubin exceeds ~2 mg/dL and may be accompanied by pruritus from bile salt skin deposition (p.672).","Pain, when present, is typically mid-epigastric and 'boring' with radiation to the back (from retroperitoneal invasion of the splanchnic nerve plexus) and may be worsened by eating or lying flat (p.672).","A palpable, non-tender gallbladder in the setting of biliary obstruction is Courvoisier's sign (p.672).","Migratory superficial thrombophlebitis and combined deep venous plus arterial thromboses (Trousseau's syndrome) can occur; metastatic disease is frequently associated with a hypercoagulable state requiring anticoagulation (p.672-674).","New-onset diabetes (especially with concomitant weight loss) or unexplained worsening of type 2/type 3c diabetes, or rising HbA1c/hyperglycemia in the preceding 1-2 years, may signal underlying pancreatic cancer (p.671-672).","CA19-9 is best drawn after the bilirubin has normalized because biliary obstruction itself elevates CA19-9; CA125 can help in the 8-15% of Lewis-antigen non-secretors who have undetectable CA19-9 (p.674).","Cigarette smoking is the greatest environmental risk factor, accounting for an estimated 25-30% of cases, with risk persisting at least 10 years after cessation (p.671).","Histologic/cytologic tissue diagnosis is essential and also enables molecular testing (next-generation sequencing for KRAS and other alterations) and microsatellite status determination, which increasingly guide therapy (p.674).","Signs of late-stage disease include a left supraclavicular Virchow's node and a periumbilical Sister Mary Joseph's nodule indicating peritoneal metastases (p.672-673)."],"pathophysiology":"Ductal adenocarcinoma is the most common and most lethal exocrine pancreatic neoplasm, characterized by infiltrating desmoplastic stromal reaction and arising in the head, body, or tail. Most cancers arise from pancreatic intraepithelial neoplasia (PanIN, graded 1-3 in a progression model), with genetic alterations accumulating as PanIN grade increases; some arise from noninvasive cystic precursors (e.g., IPMN). The mutational landscape is dominated by four commonly altered genes: KRAS (mutated in 90-95%, primarily codon 12), p16/CDKN2A (inactivated in >90% of invasive tumors), TP53 (~75%), and DPC4/SMAD4 (~50%); BRCA2 is mutated in 3.5-10%. A 'KRAS wildtype' subset (more common in patients under 55) may harbor actionable BRAF mutations, oncogenic fusions (NRG-1, NTRK1-3), or microsatellite instability (p.672).","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic pancreatitis","Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor","Acinar cell carcinoma","Colloid carcinoma","Medullary carcinoma","Adenosquamous carcinoma","Intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm (IPMN)","Mucinous cystic neoplasm","Metastasis to the pancreas (renal, breast, lung, urothelial carcinoma, melanoma, lymphoma)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Chronic pancreatitis (can be difficult to distinguish from adenocarcinoma and is itself a risk factor)","Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor","Mucinous cystic neoplasm and IPMN","Metastatic disease to the pancreas from other primaries"],"additionalInvestigations":["Dual-phase contrast-enhanced CT using a pancreas-cancer protocol (arterial and portal venous phases) to assess tumor-vessel relationships, resectability, and staging","Abdominal ultrasonography (often first imaging in the jaundiced patient)","MRI","18F-glucose PET to detect occult metastatic disease","Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) with EUS-guided core needle biopsy of pancreatic lesions","Tru-Cut core needle biopsy (preferred over fine-needle aspiration) for histologic diagnosis; percutaneous biopsy for liver/lymph node lesions","Next-generation sequencing for genomic alterations (including KRAS) and microsatellite status","Serum CA19-9 (drawn after bilirubin normalizes), CEA, and CA125","Staging laparoscopy (selectively, before or at definitive surgery)"],"redFlags":["Painless obstructive jaundice (classic for head-of-pancreas tumor)","Palpable non-tender gallbladder with obstruction (Courvoisier's sign)","Unintentional weight loss with loss of muscle mass","New-onset diabetes, especially with concomitant weight loss","Mid-epigastric boring pain radiating to the back","Migratory superficial thrombophlebitis / venous or arterial thrombosis (Trousseau's syndrome)","Left supraclavicular Virchow's node or periumbilical Sister Mary Joseph's nodule (late disease)","Light/clay-colored stools and steatorrhea"],"prognosis":"Pancreatic cancer is the most lethal human cancer, with an overall 5-year survival of about 13% (up from 3% in 1975). SEER estimates 5-year survival of ~44% for localized disease, ~16% for regional disease, and ~3.2% for metastatic disease. Late presentation is common, only about 10-20% of tumors are resectable, and the disease is the third leading cause of cancer mortality in the U.S. and projected to become the second by 2030 (p.671-672).","pitfalls":["Drawing CA19-9 while bilirubin is elevated falsely raises the level, since biliary obstruction itself elevates CA19-9 (p.674).","8-15% of patients are Lewis-antigen non-secretors with undetectable CA19-9, so a normal CA19-9 does not exclude disease; CA125 may help in this group (p.674).","Even high-grade PanIN precursor lesions are not detectable by current imaging, limiting early detection (p.672).","For average-risk individuals with no predisposing factors there is currently no recommended screening (p.671).","Whether diabetes causes or is caused by pancreatic cancer is not clearly established (p.671).","Fine-needle aspiration cytology is acceptable but less preferred than core (Tru-Cut) biopsy, which yields adequate tissue for molecular testing (p.674).","Biliary stenting is not necessarily warranted if upfront surgery is planned, but is required (where medically indicated) before neoadjuvant systemic therapy (p.674)."],"severityClassification":"Staged by the American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) staging system. Clinically also categorized by extent of disease and therapeutic approach: resectable (~10-20%), borderline resectable/locally advanced, and metastatic (p.674).","references":["Harrison 22e p.671","Harrison 22e p.672","Harrison 22e p.673","Harrison 22e p.674"],"crossLinks":["pancreatitis"]},"panic_disorder":{"name":"Panic disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["panic attacks disorder","episodic paroxysmal anxiety"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The core feature is recurrent, unexpected surges of intense fear or discomfort that peak within minutes, accompanied by prominent physical symptoms such as palpitations, chest tightness, breathlessness, sweating, trembling, dizziness, and a sense of impending doom","Diagnosis also requires persistent worry about further attacks or a maladaptive change in behaviour to avoid them, distinguishing the disorder from isolated panic attacks","Many patients present first to emergency or cardiology services convinced they are having a heart attack, so a compassionate explanation of the mind-body link is central to engagement","Exclude medical mimics before settling on the diagnosis, including arrhythmia, hyperthyroidism, hypoglycaemia, phaeochromocytoma, asthma, and caffeine or stimulant effects","Agoraphobic avoidance frequently develops as patients begin to avoid places or situations from which escape might feel difficult during an attack","First-line management combines cognitive behavioural therapy, including interoceptive exposure and breathing retraining, with SSRIs or SNRIs as pharmacological options","Benzodiazepines may give rapid relief but carry dependence and rebound risks, so they are generally reserved for short-term or adjunctive use rather than maintenance","Reassurance, psychoeducation about the self-limiting nature of attacks, and reduction of stimulants can markedly reduce anticipatory anxiety"],"pathophysiology":"Panic disorder is thought to arise from dysregulation of fear-processing circuits, particularly hypersensitivity of the amygdala and brainstem structures involved in the alarm and respiratory response, with contributions from serotonergic, noradrenergic, and GABAergic dysfunction. A heritable predisposition interacts with heightened sensitivity to internal bodily cues (interoceptive conditioning) and catastrophic misinterpretation of benign somatic sensations, which perpetuates the cycle of fear and physiological arousal.","additionalDifferentials":["Cardiac arrhythmia or acute coronary syndrome","Hyperthyroidism and other endocrine disturbances","Phaeochromocytoma","Hypoglycaemia","Substance intoxication or withdrawal, including caffeine, stimulants, and alcohol","Other anxiety disorders such as generalised anxiety disorder or social anxiety disorder","Vestibular or respiratory disorders presenting with dizziness or breathlessness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electrocardiogram to exclude arrhythmia in appropriate presentations","Thyroid function testing","Blood glucose measurement during symptomatic episodes","Substance and caffeine use screening","Standardised anxiety rating scales to gauge severity and monitor response"],"redFlags":["Syncope, sustained chest pain, or objective signs suggesting a primary cardiac event","Emerging suicidal ideation, particularly with comorbid depression","Escalating benzodiazepine or alcohol use to self-manage symptoms","Severe agoraphobic avoidance causing housebound behaviour and functional collapse"],"prognosis":"Panic disorder tends to run a chronic, fluctuating course, but a substantial proportion of patients achieve marked improvement or remission with adequate treatment. Prognosis is better with early intervention and engagement in cognitive behavioural therapy, and worse when agoraphobia, depression, or substance misuse is comorbid or when treatment is delayed.","pitfalls":["Repeated extensive medical investigations that reinforce illness beliefs rather than addressing the anxiety","Relying on long-term benzodiazepines and creating dependence","Missing comorbid depression and its associated suicide risk","Underdosing or prematurely stopping SSRIs before an adequate therapeutic trial","Overlooking caffeine, stimulants, or withdrawal states as drivers of attacks"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"papilloedema":{"name":"Papilloedema","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["papilledema","optic disc swelling from raised intracranial pressure"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The term papilloedema refers specifically to optic disc swelling caused by raised intracranial pressure, and is characteristically bilateral","Central vision is often preserved early, while an enlarged blind spot and transient visual obscurations lasting seconds are typical","Accompanying symptoms of raised intracranial pressure include headache worse on waking or with Valsalva, nausea, vomiting, and pulsatile tinnitus, and a sixth nerve palsy may be a false localising sign","Fundus signs include blurred disc margins, hyperaemia, loss of the physiological cup, obscuration of vessels crossing the disc, and loss of spontaneous venous pulsation, with haemorrhages and cotton wool spots in more advanced cases","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension classically affects young women who are overweight, but a space-occupying lesion or venous sinus thrombosis must be excluded first","It must be distinguished from other causes of a swollen disc, such as optic neuritis, anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy, and pseudopapilloedema from optic disc drusen","Chronic untreated papilloedema leads to progressive optic atrophy and irreversible visual field loss"],"pathophysiology":"Papilloedema is passive swelling of the optic nerve head produced by raised intracranial pressure transmitted along the subarachnoid space surrounding the optic nerve. The elevated pressure impedes axoplasmic flow within the retinal ganglion cell axons at the optic disc, causing them to become distended, along with venous congestion and leakage that produce the characteristic disc appearance. Because the mechanism is mechanical rather than primarily ischaemic or inflammatory, central acuity is often spared until late. Persistently raised pressure eventually causes axonal loss and secondary optic atrophy.","additionalDifferentials":["Optic neuritis (usually unilateral, with pain and reduced acuity)","Anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy","Malignant hypertension with hypertensive retinopathy","Pseudopapilloedema from optic disc drusen or a crowded hypermetropic disc","Central retinal vein occlusion","Infiltrative or inflammatory optic disc swelling"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent neuroimaging (MRI or CT with venography) to exclude a mass lesion or venous sinus thrombosis","Lumbar puncture with opening pressure and cerebrospinal fluid analysis once imaging excludes a mass","Visual field testing to detect an enlarged blind spot and peripheral loss","Blood pressure measurement to exclude malignant hypertension","Optical coherence tomography and fundus photography to grade and monitor disc swelling","Assessment of colour vision and pupils to help distinguish from optic neuropathies"],"redFlags":["Bilateral disc swelling mandating urgent workup for raised intracranial pressure, including neuroimaging to exclude a tumour or venous sinus thrombosis","Progressive visual field loss or declining acuity indicating threatened optic nerve function","New focal neurological signs or a sixth nerve palsy","Very high intracranial or blood pressure requiring emergency management"],"prognosis":"Vision can be preserved if the underlying cause of raised intracranial pressure is treated promptly, but delayed or chronic papilloedema leads to optic atrophy and permanent visual field loss.","pitfalls":["Mistaking pseudopapilloedema from disc drusen for true papilloedema and initiating unnecessary invasive workup","Diagnosing idiopathic intracranial hypertension before excluding a mass lesion or venous thrombosis","Reassuring the patient because central acuity is normal while field loss progresses","Overlooking malignant hypertension as a cause of disc swelling"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"paraneoplastic_syndromes_endocrinologic_he":{"name":"Paraneoplastic Syndromes: Endocrinologic/Hematologic","system":"Multisystem (Oncology / Endocrine / Hematologic)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Ectopic hormone syndromes","Humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy","Tumor-associated SIADH","Ectopic ACTH syndrome","Trousseau's syndrome"],"chapter":98,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.736","clinicalPearls":["Paraneoplastic syndromes are disorders accompanying benign or malignant tumors that are NOT due to direct mass effect, invasion, or metastasis; neuroendocrine tumors such as small-cell lung carcinoma (SCLC) and carcinoids are especially common sources, and these syndromes are more common than generally appreciated (Harrison 22e p.736).","Hormones may be eutopic (from normal tissue of origin) or ectopic (atypical source); ectopic expression is often quantitative rather than absolute and is characterized by defective feedback regulation (e.g., ectopic ACTH) and release of large, unprocessed precursor peptides such as POMC (Harrison 22e p.736).","The three most common paraneoplastic endocrine syndromes are hypercalcemia from PTHrP overproduction, hyponatremia from excess vasopressin (SIADH), and Cushing's syndrome from ectopic ACTH (Harrison 22e p.737).","Humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy (HHM) occurs in up to 20% of cancer patients, is most often caused by PTHrP, and favoring features over primary hyperparathyroidism are known malignancy, recent onset, and very high calcium; PTH is suppressed and an elevated PTHrP is raised in roughly 80% of hypercalcemic cancer patients (Harrison 22e p.737-738).","HHM produces hypercalciuria and hypophosphatemia like hyperparathyroidism, but is typically accompanied by metabolic alkalosis rather than the hyperchloremic acidosis of hyperparathyroidism; lymphomas can cause hypercalcemia via 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D rather than PTHrP (Harrison 22e p.737-738).","Ectopic vasopressin causes SIADH in at least half of SCLC patients; hyponatremia with low serum osmolality occurs alongside an inappropriately normal or high urine osmolality, and intake of free water (oral or IV) can rapidly worsen the hyponatremia (Harrison 22e p.738).","Ectopic ACTH accounts for 10-20% of Cushing's syndrome; SCLC is the leading cause, followed by bronchial and thymic carcinoids; plasma ACTH is usually >22 pmol/L (>100 pg/mL) and urine-free cortisol is typically more than 2-4 times normal (Harrison 22e p.738-739).","Ectopic ACTH Cushing's differs from pituitary disease: less weight gain/centripetal fat (brief exposure plus cachexia), but prominent fluid retention, hypertension, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, glucose intolerance, and hyperpigmentation from MSH within the POMC precursor (Harrison 22e p.739).","Most ectopic ACTH sources fail to suppress with high-dose dexamethasone (8 mg suppresses ~80% of pituitary adenomas but fails in ~90% of ectopic cases), though bronchial and other carcinoids are notable exceptions that may show pituitary-like feedback (Harrison 22e p.739).","Non-islet-cell tumor hypoglycemia is driven by IGF-II precursor; serum IGF-II may not be markedly elevated, but an IGF-II/IGF-I ratio greater than 10:1 is suggestive, and the responsible tumor is usually large (>10 cm) and clinically apparent (Harrison 22e p.739-740).","Oncogenic (tumor-induced) osteomalacia from FGF23 (phosphatonin) presents with low serum phosphorus, renal phosphate wasting, low 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, but NORMAL serum calcium and PTH; it is usually caused by benign mesenchymal tumors and reverses with tumor resection (Harrison 22e p.739-741).","Among paraneoplastic hematologic syndromes (erythrocytosis, granulocytosis, thrombocytosis, eosinophilia), red/white/platelet elevations are usually self-limited and asymptomatic; by far the most consequential hematologic abnormality in cancer patients is hypercoagulability (Harrison 22e p.740).","Migratory or recurrent thrombophlebitis can be the initial manifestation of cancer; peripheral venous thrombosis coexisting with visceral carcinoma, especially pancreatic cancer, is termed Trousseau's syndrome (Harrison 22e p.741)."],"pathophysiology":"Neoplastic cells, particularly those of neuroendocrine origin (SCLC, carcinoids), secrete peptide hormones, cytokines, and growth factors or induce antibody production, altering endocrine, hematologic, and other systems independently of tumor bulk. Most ectopic hormone production reflects cellular dedifferentiation with derepression of developmental gene programs (epigenetic changes, altered microRNA expression); genetic rearrangements bringing a hormone gene under aberrant regulatory control are a rare alternative mechanism. HHM arises chiefly from tumor PTHrP, which binds the PTH receptor to mimic hyperparathyroidism (driven by PTHLH hypomethylation, hedgehog/Gli, TGF-beta, Ras activation, p53 loss); lymphomas may instead convert 25-OH vitamin D to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. SIADH results from ectopic vasopressin (often with co-expressed oxytocin) causing water retention and dilutional hyponatremia. Ectopic ACTH comes from unregulated POMC expression, with release of large biologically inactive fragments plus small amounts of active ACTH; very high cortisol overwhelms renal 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type II, allowing cortisol to act at mineralocorticoid receptors and produce severe hypokalemia. IGF-II precursor (from loss of 11p15 imprinting) causes hypoglycemia by acting on insulin/IGF-I receptors and suppressing GH and insulin. FGF23 forms a ternary complex with klotho and renal FGF receptors to reduce phosphate reabsorption and inhibit 1-alpha-hydroxylation. Hematologic syndromes are mediated by ectopic erythropoietin (erythrocytosis); G-CSF, GM-CSF, IL-6 (granulocytosis); IL-6/thrombopoietin (thrombocytosis); IL-5 (eosinophilia); and poorly defined procoagulants/cytokines (hypercoagulability).","additionalDifferentials":["Primary hyperparathyroidism (vs HHM)","Non-malignant causes of SIADH: CNS trauma, infection, pulmonary disease, medications including many chemotherapeutic agents","Pituitary (Cushing's disease), adrenal adenoma, and iatrogenic/exogenous glucocorticoid causes of Cushing's syndrome","Granulomatous disorders producing 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D","Polycythemia vera and hemoglobinopathies with increased O2 affinity (vs paraneoplastic erythrocytosis)","Myeloproliferative disorders causing primary granulocytosis/thrombocytosis/eosinophilia","Renal, adrenal, or thyroid insufficiency as causes of hyponatremia"],"infectionMimics":["Granulocytosis from infection or tumor necrosis mimicking paraneoplastic granulocytosis","Eosinophilia with pulmonary infiltrates resembling infectious or hypersensitivity pneumonitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hypercalcemia of primary hyperparathyroidism","Exogenous glucocorticoid (iatrogenic) Cushing's syndrome","Drug-induced or volume-depletion hyponatremia","Insulinoma or factitious hypoglycemia (vs IGF-II tumor hypoglycemia)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum calcium with PTH (suppressed in HHM) and PTHrP level; 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D in suspected lymphoma","Serum sodium and osmolality with paired urine osmolality and urine sodium for SIADH","Plasma ACTH, urine-free cortisol, high-dose (8 mg) dexamethasone suppression test, and inferior petrosal sinus sampling with CRH stimulation (>3:1 central:peripheral ACTH ratio suggests pituitary source)","Serum glucose with insulin, IGF-II, and IGF-II/IGF-I ratio (>10:1 suggestive) for tumor hypoglycemia","Serum phosphorus, FGF23, and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D for oncogenic osteomalacia; octreotide scan to localize tumor","Hematocrit, red cell mass, and serum erythropoietin for erythrocytosis","Compression ultrasonography/impedance plethysmography (venography if equivocal) for DVT; ventilation-perfusion scan, chest radiograph, ECG, and ABG for suspected pulmonary embolism","Functional imaging (CT, MRI, PET, octreotide scintigraphy) to localize hormone-producing tumors, particularly when endocrine features precede cancer diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Markedly elevated calcium (>3.5 mmol/L [>14 mg/dL]) with fatigue, altered mentation, polyuria, dehydration, or arrhythmias (short ST/QT, bundle branch block, bradyarrhythmia)","Severe hyponatremia (Na <115 meq/L) or any hyponatremia with confusion, depressed mental status, or seizures","Profound hypokalemia with metabolic alkalosis and hypertension in ectopic ACTH; opportunistic infections (Pneumocystis, mycoses) are a frequent cause of death","Increased venous thromboembolism risk in ectopic ACTH and in cancer generally; migratory/recurrent thrombophlebitis or clot in an unusual site","New atypical clinical or metabolic findings in a known cancer patient should prompt consideration of a paraneoplastic syndrome","Markedly elevated eosinophils (>5000/uL) causing dyspnea, wheezing, and diffuse pulmonary infiltrates"],"prognosis":"Endocrine paraneoplastic syndromes can cause substantial morbidity and may be the presenting clue to an occult malignancy; treatment of the underlying tumor is the mainstay but rarely alters overall survival or cancer progression. Hematologic cell-count syndromes are generally self-limited and asymptomatic, but thrombocytosis can mark advanced-stage disease and poorer prognosis. Hypercoagulability is the most consequential hematologic complication, and oncogenic osteomalacia and several endocrinopathies can reverse with successful tumor resection; checkpoint-inhibitor-associated endocrinopathies are generally irreversible and require lifelong hormone replacement.","pitfalls":["Attributing atypical metabolic or neurologic findings to the malignancy or its treatment and overlooking a paraneoplastic syndrome","Confusing HHM with primary hyperparathyroidism; PTH should be suppressed and acid-base pattern differs (alkalosis in HHM vs hyperchloremic acidosis in hyperparathyroidism)","Rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia, which can precipitate central pontine myelinolysis; correction should be gradual (about 0.5-1 meq/L per hour)","Excluding ectopic ACTH on the basis of high-dose dexamethasone suppression alone, since bronchial/thymic carcinoids can suppress like pituitary adenomas; conversely a suppressed ACTH indicates an ACTH-independent cause","Relying on serum IGF-II levels, which may be normal in tumor hypoglycemia because assays may miss IGF-II precursors (use the IGF-II/IGF-I ratio)","Over-relying on D-dimer in cancer patients, where it is less predictive of DVT and rises with age without thrombosis","Pursuing extensive cancer work-up after a first unprovoked thrombophlebitis or PE in a patient without cancer; targeted evaluation is reserved for refractory, unusual-site, or migratory/recurrent clots"],"severityClassification":"No single formal severity scale; severity is gauged by the magnitude and acuity of the biochemical derangement (e.g., calcium level and rate of rise, sodium level [Na <115 meq/L denotes severe hyponatremia], eosinophil count >5000/uL). For venous thromboembolism risk during chemotherapy, the Khorana risk score (Harrison Table 98-3) stratifies patients using cancer type, therapy, blood counts, and body mass index.","references":["Harrison 22e p.736","Harrison 22e p.737","Harrison 22e p.738","Harrison 22e p.739","Harrison 22e p.740","Harrison 22e p.741","Harrison 22e p.742"],"crossLinks":[]},"paraquat_poisoning":{"name":"Paraquat poisoning","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["paraquat toxicity","herbicide poisoning","gramoxone poisoning"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A highly lethal herbicide with no effective antidote; even small deliberate ingestions of concentrated solution are frequently fatal","It concentrates in and selectively destroys the lungs, causing progressive pulmonary oedema, haemorrhage, and then relentless fibrosis over days to weeks","Early features include painful corrosive burns to the mouth, throat, and oesophagus, vomiting, and abdominal pain","Oxygen supplementation should generally be avoided or minimised, as high oxygen tension accelerates free-radical lung injury; supplemental oxygen is reserved for severe, refractory hypoxaemia","Immediate gastrointestinal decontamination with an adsorbent such as activated charcoal or Fuller's earth is a priority if the patient presents very early","Enhanced elimination (haemoperfusion or haemodialysis) and immunosuppression with corticosteroids and cyclophosphamide have been tried, but none reliably alters the poor outcome","Acute kidney injury and hepatic injury commonly precede the delayed, usually fatal, pulmonary fibrosis","Plasma paraquat concentration plotted against time since ingestion helps estimate prognosis and guide the intensity of intervention"],"pathophysiology":"Paraquat undergoes cyclical reduction and oxidation (redox cycling), generating large amounts of superoxide and other reactive oxygen species that deplete NADPH and cause lipid peroxidation and cell death. It is actively taken up and concentrated by alveolar epithelial (type I and II) cells, making the lung the primary target: an early destructive phase of alveolitis and haemorrhagic oedema is followed by an obliterative pulmonary fibrosis. The kidneys and liver, which also accumulate the compound, sustain acute injury, and high oxygen tensions worsen the oxidative damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Corrosive ingestion from other agents","Other lethal pesticide ingestions (organophosphate, aluminium phosphide)","Acute respiratory distress syndrome from other causes","Acute interstitial pneumonitis","Caustic oesophageal injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plasma or urine paraquat level and the urine dithionite qualitative test","Serial renal and liver function tests","Arterial blood gas and serial chest imaging to track evolving lung injury","Full blood count and electrolytes","Prognostic assessment using plasma concentration versus time since ingestion"],"redFlags":["Ingestion of concentrated solution or any large deliberate ingestion","Early acute kidney injury, which predicts high toxin burden and poor outcome","Progressive hypoxaemia and radiographic lung changes","Extensive oropharyngeal and oesophageal corrosive burns","Rising plasma paraquat level for the time since ingestion indicating a lethal exposure"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally poor and dose-dependent; ingestion of concentrated formulation is usually fatal, with death from multi-organ failure early or from progressive pulmonary fibrosis over subsequent days to weeks, and no treatment has been shown to reliably improve survival.","pitfalls":["Giving liberal supplemental oxygen, which accelerates oxidative lung injury","Delaying adsorbent decontamination in an early presentation","Offering false reassurance during the deceptively stable early period before lung failure","Underestimating lethality and under-treating the psychosocial and palliative needs of the patient"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"parathyroid_calcium_disorders":{"name":"Parathyroid & Calcium Disorders","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hyperparathyroidism","hypoparathyroidism","hypocalcemia"],"chapter":422,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3271","clinicalPearls":["Primary hyperparathyroidism (pHPT) and malignancy are the two most common causes of hypercalcemia; the diagnostic split hinges on the PTH level (Harrison 22e p.3271).","pHPT is almost always a benign neoplastic disorder of the parathyroids (adenoma or hyperplasia) producing PTH that is not appropriately suppressed by the elevated calcium; parathyroid carcinoma is a rare cause (Harrison 22e p.3271).","In hypercalcemia, an elevated or 'inappropriately normal' PTH with elevated calcium and low/low-normal phosphorus is almost always pHPT; because of PTH's phosphaturic action, pHPT hypercalcemia is often accompanied by low/frankly low serum phosphate (Harrison 22e p.3271).","A suppressed PTH in the face of hypercalcemia means the process is independent of the parathyroid glands and is most often malignancy; many solid tumors secrete PTHrP, which mimics PTH at its receptor on bone and kidney (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Hypercalcemia from lymphoma or granulomatous disease (e.g., sarcoidosis, tuberculosis) is driven by extrarenal macrophage CYP27B1 conversion of 25(OH)D to 1,25(OH)2D; in such cases 1,25(OH)2D is increased or inappropriately normal (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Classic chronic hypercalcemia (months to years, fluctuating between high-normal and just above normal) is most often pHPT or FHH, occasionally multiple myeloma; malignancy-associated hypercalcemia usually has a more acute course (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Mild hypercalcemia (10.5-11.9 mg/dL) is largely asymptomatic; symptoms ('stones, bones, groans') include nephrolithiasis, reduced bone density / bone pain or pathologic fractures with long-standing hyperparathyroidism, constipation/nausea/vomiting/anorexia/peptic ulcer/pancreatitis, plus fatigue, proximal weakness, hyporeflexia, and neuropsychiatric changes (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Hypocalcemia is differentiated by whether PTH is low (hypoparathyroidism) or high (secondary hyperparathyroidism); a low or 'inappropriately low' PTH with hypocalcemia establishes hypoparathyroidism, whereas an elevated PTH points to the vitamin D axis or primary small-bowel calcium malabsorption (Harrison 22e p.3271).","In adults, hypoparathyroidism most commonly results from inadvertent damage to all four parathyroid glands during thyroid or parathyroid surgery; autoimmune endocrinopathy accounts for about a quarter of cases (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Neuromuscular irritability of hypocalcemia is detected by Chvostek's sign (perioral twitch on tapping the facial nerve) and the more specific Trousseau's sign (carpal spasm after inflating a BP cuff above systolic for 3 min); paresthesias of fingers, toes, and circumoral region are typical (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Because PTH is the main minute-by-minute defense against hypocalcemia, hypoparathyroidism can produce profound, life-threatening hypocalcemia, whereas vitamin D deficiency causes less severe hypocalcemia since the parathyroids mount a compensatory PTH rise (at the expense of bone) (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Impaired PTH secretion may be secondary to magnesium deficiency (via intracellular effects); hypomagnesemic hypocalcemia will not correct until magnesium is repleted (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Hungry bone syndrome after parathyroidectomy is a recognized cause of post-operative hypocalcemia with high PTH (Harrison 22e p.3271)."],"pathophysiology":"Extracellular calcium is held in a narrow range (~8.5-10.5 mg/dL) by feedback among the parathyroids, kidney, bone, and intestine. A fall in ECF calcium is sensed by the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) on parathyroid chief cells, triggering PTH release; PTH increases bone calcium resorption, distal-tubule reabsorption of filtered calcium, and renal proximal-tubule 1,25(OH)2D production, and 1,25(OH)2D in turn raises intestinal calcium absorption. Hypercalcemia arises from derangements of this loop: PTH-dependent overproduction (pHPT due to adenoma/hyperplasia, rarely carcinoma; tertiary hyperparathyroidism; ectopic PTH; FHH and CaSR-altering states such as lithium), or PTH-independent mechanisms (PTHrP-secreting tumors, lytic skeletal metastases, extrarenal 1,25(OH)2D production in granulomatous disease/lymphoma, vitamin D intoxication, increased bone resorption from thyrotoxicosis/Paget's/immobilization, and calcium overload e.g. milk-alkali). Hypocalcemia results from low PTH (hypoparathyroidism: surgical, autoimmune, infiltrative, genetic, CaSR-activating, or magnesium-related) or high PTH (secondary hyperparathyroidism from vitamin D deficiency, renal failure, intestinal malabsorption, or PTH resistance/pseudohypoparathyroidism). Low calcium increases neuromuscular irritability (Harrison 22e p.3271).","additionalDifferentials":["Primary hyperparathyroidism (PTH-dependent hypercalcemia) - parathyroid adenoma or hyperplasia, rarely carcinoma","Hypercalcemia of malignancy (PTH-suppressed) - PTHrP overproduction or lytic skeletal metastases (breast, myeloma)","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (FHH) - inactivating CASR/GNA11/AP2S1 mutations","Tertiary hyperparathyroidism (long-standing renal insufficiency)","Granulomatous disease- or lymphoma-associated hypercalcemia (extrarenal 1,25(OH)2D)","Vitamin D intoxication","Hypercalcemia of immobilization, thyrotoxicosis, Paget's disease, milk-alkali syndrome","Multiple myeloma","Postsurgical (post-thyroidectomy / post-parathyroidectomy) hypoparathyroidism","Autoimmune hypoparathyroidism (including APS-1)","Vitamin D deficiency / chronic renal insufficiency causing secondary hyperparathyroidism","Pseudohypoparathyroidism (PTH resistance)","Hypomagnesemia (or severe hypermagnesemia) impairing PTH","Autosomal dominant hypocalcemia (activating CaSR/GNA11 mutations)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis - granulomatous extrarenal 1,25(OH)2D production causing hypercalcemia","Leprosy and fungal infections - granulomatous causes of 1,25(OH)2D-mediated hypercalcemia","COVID-19 - reported transient hypoparathyroidism (and even spontaneous resolution of pHPT) via unknown mechanisms"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pseudohypercalcemia (e.g., calcium-binding IgM in Waldenstrom's macroglobulinemia) and pseudohypocalcemia (low albumin from malnutrition, nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis; gadoversetamide MRI contrast) - artifactual values requiring albumin correction before workup","Sarcoidosis and other granulomatous disease - 1,25(OH)2D-mediated hypercalcemia","Lymphoma (B cell, cutaneous T cell/Sezary) - extrarenal 1,25(OH)2D","Thyrotoxicosis, Paget's disease, immobilization - increased bone resorption mimicking parathyroid hypercalcemia","Thiazides, lithium, vitamin A, foscarnet, teriparatide, aromatase inhibitors - drug-induced hypercalcemia","Acute pancreatitis, rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, burns - hypocalcemia from tissue injury / hyperphosphatemia","Respiratory or metabolic alkalosis - hypocalcemia from increased calcium binding to albumin"],"additionalInvestigations":["Total serum calcium with serum albumin to calculate corrected calcium (add 0.8 mg/dL per 1.0 g/dL albumin below 4.0; subtract when albumin is high)","Intact PTH by two-site assay - the key second test that separates PTH-dependent from PTH-independent disorders","Serum phosphorus (low/low-normal in pHPT; elevated in some hypocalcemic states)","Serum magnesium (hypo- or severe hypermagnesemia impairs PTH)","Serum creatinine / renal function","Serum 25(OH)D - useful in both hyper- and hypocalcemia to assess vitamin D stores","Serum 1,25(OH)2D - useful in hypercalcemia (increased/inappropriately normal in granulomatous disease or lymphoma) and suspected vitamin D resistance","PTHrP level when hypercalcemia of malignancy is suspected with suppressed PTH","Serum free light chains / SPEP-UPEP with immunofixation to exclude multiple myeloma","Calcium/creatinine clearance ratio (<0.01 suggests FHH); 24-hour urinary fractional calcium excretion (>0.02 supports pHPT)","Genetic sequencing of CASR, GNA11, AP2S1 for definitive FHH diagnosis when urine studies are inconclusive; genetic testing for syndromic/hereditary pHPT (MEN, CDC73)","ECG (hypercalcemia: bradycardia, AV block, QRS widening, short QTc; hypocalcemia: QTc prolongation)"],"redFlags":["Moderate-to-severe hypercalcemia (>=12-14 mg/dL) with progressive lethargy, stupor, or coma","Hypercalcemia with acute renal failure and volume depletion from polyuria","Hypercalcemic ECG changes: bradycardia, AV block, QRS widening, short QTc","Severe hypocalcemia causing seizures, carpopedal spasm, bronchospasm, or laryngospasm","Hypocalcemic QTc prolongation with risk of ventricular tachyarrhythmia (Torsade) and sudden death","Tetany (positive Trousseau's sign / carpal spasm) signaling impending neuromuscular crisis","New severe hypocalcemia after thyroid or parathyroid neck surgery (damage to all four glands), especially in patients with prior malabsorption","Syndromic pHPT with a jaw tumor (CDC73 mutation) carrying ~30% lifetime risk of parathyroid carcinoma"],"prognosis":"Mild, asymptomatic hypercalcemia (commonly pHPT detected incidentally in settings with routine calcium screening) carries a benign short-term course, whereas advanced pHPT can cause severe skeletal disease (osteitis fibrosa cystica). Hypoparathyroidism, because PTH is the chief minute-by-minute defense against hypocalcemia, can be profound, debilitating, and life-threatening, while vitamin D-deficiency hypocalcemia is typically milder owing to compensatory PTH secretion (at the expense of bone). Severe hypercalcemia (coma, renal failure, arrhythmia) and severe hypocalcemia (seizures, laryngospasm, Torsade) are medical emergencies (Harrison 22e p.3271).","pitfalls":["Failing to correct calcium for albumin before launching a workup - low albumin can mask true hypercalcemia and falsely suggest hypocalcemia (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Mistaking FHH for pHPT: FHH can present with mildly elevated PTH and hypercalcemia, but parathyroid surgery is ineffective; a calcium/creatinine clearance ratio <0.01 and family history of mild asymptomatic hypercalcemia or repeated failed neck surgeries should prompt CASR/GNA11/AP2S1 testing (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Overlooking lithium therapy, which raises the CaSR set point and can cause hyperparathyroidism (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Not repleting magnesium in hypocalcemia: hypomagnesemia impairs PTH secretion and the hypocalcemia will not correct until magnesium is restored (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Chvostek's sign is nonspecific - present in ~10% of normal individuals; Trousseau's sign is the more definitive marker of neuromuscular irritability (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Attributing hypercalcemia of immobilization solely to bed rest - an unrecognized resorptive process (pHPT, Paget's, myeloma) may be the real driver, especially in older patients (Harrison 22e p.3271).","When tetany accompanies alkalosis, a second cause of impaired calcium homeostasis is usually present (Harrison 22e p.3271).","Ectopic PTH secretion, unlike pHPT, does not fluctuate with correction of calcium because it is not under CaSR control (Harrison 22e p.3271)."],"severityClassification":"Hypercalcemia severity (Harrison 22e p.3271): mild 10.5-11.9 mg/dL (largely asymptomatic); moderate >=12-14 mg/dL; severe >=14 mg/dL (progressive lethargy, stupor, coma, prominent GI symptoms, acute renal failure). Hypocalcemia severity ranges from asymptomatic (mild/chronic) to moderate-severe with paresthesias, positive Chvostek's/Trousseau's signs, through severe with seizures, carpopedal spasm, broncho-/laryngospasm, and QTc prolongation with arrhythmia risk.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3271","Harrison 22e ch.57 p.362-366"],"crossLinks":["hypercalcemia","thyroid_cancer"]},"parkinsons_disease":{"name":"Parkinson's Disease","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PD","parkinsonism"],"chapter":446,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3493","clinicalPearls":["PD is the second most common age-related neurodegenerative disease after Alzheimer's; mean age of onset ~60 years, lifetime risk ~3% in men and ~2% in women, though cases can occur in the twenties or younger, particularly with a pathogenic gene mutation (p.3493).","Cardinal motor features are bradykinesia, rest tremor, rigidity, and gait dysfunction with postural instability; additional motor features include freezing of gait, micrographia, masked facies (hypomimia), reduced eye blinking, drooling, soft voice (hypophonia), and dysphagia (p.3493-3494).","Parkinsonism (bradykinesia with rigidity and/or tremor) combined with rest tremor, asymmetry of motor impairment, and a good levodopa response is much more likely to predict the correct pathologic diagnosis; with these U.K. Brain Bank Criteria, PD can be confirmed pathologically in >90% of patients (p.3495).","Levodopa is the gold standard symptomatic therapy; almost all PD patients improve, and failure to respond to an adequate trial of levodopa should cause the diagnosis to be questioned (p.3501).","Dopamine-system imaging (PET/SPECT) typically shows reduced, asymmetric striatal uptake, most pronounced in the posterior putamen with relative sparing of the caudate; it is useful when the diagnosis is uncertain but is not routinely required (p.3495-3496).","Among forms of parkinsonism, PD is the most common (~75% of cases) (p.3495)."],"pathophysiology":"Pathologic hallmarks are degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta (SNc), reduced striatal dopamine, and intraneuronal alpha-synuclein-staining inclusions in cell bodies and axons (Lewy bodies and Lewy neurites; collectively Lewy pathology). Lewy pathology can also affect cholinergic neurons of the nucleus basalis of Meynert, noradrenergic neurons of the locus coeruleus, serotonergic raphe neurons, and the olfactory system, cerebral hemispheres, spinal cord, and peripheral autonomic nervous system, accounting for nonmotor features. Pathology may begin in the peripheral autonomic nervous system, GI tract, olfactory system, or dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus and spread sequentially (Braak staging), giving rise to 'body-first' versus 'brain-first' concepts. In the basal-ganglia network, dopamine denervation leads to increased firing of the subthalamic nucleus (STN) and globus pallidus interna (GPi), excessive inhibition of the thalamus, reduced cortical motor activation, and the emergence of parkinsonian features. Pathogenesis involves an interacting network of oxidative stress, mitochondrial dysfunction, protein aggregation, excitotoxicity, and inflammation; a 'double hit' of genetic susceptibility plus an environmental insult has been proposed (p.3493-3494, p.3498-3500).","additionalDifferentials":["Atypical parkinsonism: multiple-system atrophy (MSA) - cerebellar (MSA-c) and Parkinson (MSA-p) types","Progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP) - parkinsonian and Richardson variants","Corticobasal syndrome (CBS)","Dementia with Lewy bodies / PD with dementia","Secondary parkinsonism: drug-induced, tumor, infection, vascular, normal-pressure hydrocephalus, trauma, liver failure, toxins (carbon monoxide, manganese, MPTP, cyanide, hexane, methanol, carbon disulfide)","Essential tremor (high-frequency 6-10 Hz symmetric postural tremor with prominent kinetic component)","Wilson's disease","Huntington's disease (especially the juvenile Westphal variant)","Dopa-responsive dystonia (DRD)","Spinocerebellar ataxias and neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation (e.g., PANK-associated neurodegeneration)"],"infectionMimics":["Infection-related secondary parkinsonism (basal ganglia damage from CNS infection)","Post-influenza / postencephalitic parkinsonism (historical association with the influenza A epidemic); new-onset PD has been reported following SARS-CoV-2 infection, which can also worsen existing PD features"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Drug-induced parkinsonism - most commonly dopamine-blocking neuroleptics, and also metoclopramide (a neuroleptic agent used for GI problems), tetrabenazine, calcium channel blockers (flunarizine, cinnarizine), amiodarone, and lithium","Vascular parkinsonism (stroke affecting the SNc may closely resemble PD)","Toxin-induced parkinsonism (carbon monoxide or manganese damaging the globus pallidus resemble atypical parkinsonism with poor levodopa response)","Tumor and normal-pressure hydrocephalus","Atypical parkinsonism (MSA, PSP, CBS)","Essential tremor","Dopa-responsive dystonia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Dopamine-system imaging with PET or SPECT (reduced, asymmetric striatal/posterior-putamen uptake); not routinely required","Metabolic basal ganglia/thalamus imaging (18F-deoxyglucose) - may help distinguish atypical parkinsonism (decreased GPi with increased thalamic activity, the reverse of PD)","MRI - to evaluate for vascular/structural causes and atypical features (e.g., midbrain 'hummingbird sign' in PSP, putaminal rim change or pontine 'hot cross bun' sign in MSA); transcranial sonography or special MRI protocols may detect increased SNc iron","Alpha-synuclein seeding amplification assay (SAA) on CSF or skin - high sensitivity and specificity, can distinguish PD from other parkinsonisms (currently mainly research use)","Genetic testing - selective use for strong family history, early onset (<40 years), or particular ethnic backgrounds (GBA1, LRRK2, SNCA, etc.); monogenic forms account for ~5% of cases (~15% including GBA1 variants)","Serum/urinary copper studies to exclude Wilson's disease, which is treatable with copper chelators"],"redFlags":["Early speech and gait impairment with early falls, lack of rest tremor, and lack of motor asymmetry - suggests atypical parkinsonism","Poor or no response to an adequate trial of levodopa - suggests atypical or secondary parkinsonism","Exposure to neuroleptics - suggests drug-induced parkinsonism","Onset before age 40 years - consider a genetic form of PD, Wilson's disease, or dopa-responsive dystonia","Liver disease - consider Wilson's disease or non-Wilsonian hepatolenticular degeneration","Hallucinations and dementia preceding or as the first/early feature - suggests dementia with Lewy bodies","Diplopia, impaired vertical gaze, slow saccades with impaired downgaze - suggests PSP","Prominent orthostatic hypotension or prominent cerebellar signs - suggests MSA"],"prognosis":"PD is progressive. Levodopa prolongs independence and employability, improves quality of life, and increases life span, but chronic treatment with ongoing disease progression is associated in most patients with motor complications - response fluctuations ('on'/'off', wearing-off, on-off phenomenon) and dyskinesias. As the disease advances, nondopaminergic features that respond poorly to levodopa emerge - falling, freezing, autonomic dysfunction, sleep disorders, and dementia - and these (especially falls and dementia) are the primary source of disability and the main reason for hospitalization and nursing home placement in advanced PD. Atypical parkinsonisms generally follow a more aggressive course than classic PD (p.3496-3497, p.3501).","pitfalls":["Diagnosing PD on two of three cardinal features alone carries a ~24% postmortem error rate; rest tremor, motor asymmetry, and a good levodopa response markedly improve diagnostic accuracy (p.3495).","Drug-induced parkinsonism is the most common secondary parkinsonism and is easily missed - remember that metoclopramide, used for GI symptoms, is a neuroleptic, and that calcium channel blockers (flunarizine, cinnarizine), tetrabenazine, amiodarone, and lithium can also be responsible (p.3497).","Wilson's disease must be actively excluded because progression can be prevented with copper chelators (p.3497).","Dopamine-system imaging cannot separate PD from atypical parkinsonism, since striatal dopamine depletion occurs in both (p.3496).","Some genetic forms of PD (e.g., certain LRRK2 cases) may occur without Lewy bodies, and MPTP toxicity causes parkinsonism typically without Lewy bodies (p.3496, p.3498)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not present a single formal severity grading; clinical diagnostic confidence is staged by the MDS Clinical Diagnostic Criteria into 'clinically established PD' and 'clinically probable PD', which combine core motor parkinsonism with supportive criteria, absolute exclusion criteria, and red flags. Disease evolution is commonly framed by the emergence of levodopa-related motor complications and levodopa-unresponsive nondopaminergic features in advanced PD (p.3495-3496, p.3501).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3493"],"crossLinks":[]},"paroxysmal_nocturnal":{"name":"Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PNH","paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acquired clonal stem cell disorder in which red cells lack complement-regulating surface proteins and undergo intravascular haemolysis","The classic clinical triad is complement-mediated haemolytic anaemia, a strong tendency to thrombosis, and variable degrees of bone marrow failure","Haemoglobinuria is often most noticeable in the first morning urine, reflecting overnight complement activation, though continuous low-grade haemolysis is the rule","Thrombosis, frequently at unusual sites such as hepatic (Budd-Chiari), portal, mesenteric, or cerebral veins, is the leading cause of death and is disproportionate to the degree of thrombocytopenia","Free haemoglobin scavenges nitric oxide, producing smooth-muscle-related symptoms such as abdominal pain, dysphagia, fatigue, and erectile dysfunction","PNH is closely linked to aplastic anaemia and myelodysplasia, and clones can emerge from a failing marrow, so screening is warranted in these settings","Diagnosis is made by flow cytometry demonstrating deficiency of GPI-anchored proteins (such as CD55 and CD59), often using the FLAER reagent, rather than older lysis tests","Terminal complement inhibition markedly reduces haemolysis and thrombotic risk; anticoagulation is used for thrombotic events, and allogeneic transplant is reserved for marrow failure or refractory disease, with vaccination against encapsulated organisms before complement blockade"],"pathophysiology":"PNH arises from an acquired somatic mutation in the PIGA gene within a haematopoietic stem cell, impairing synthesis of the glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI) anchor. As a result, the affected clone and its progeny lack GPI-anchored surface proteins, including the complement regulators CD55 and CD59 that normally protect cells from complement attack. Unprotected red cells are lysed by activated complement, producing chronic intravascular haemolysis with haemoglobinaemia and haemoglobinuria. Complement activation and free haemoglobin also promote platelet activation and consume nitric oxide, driving a marked prothrombotic state and smooth-muscle dystonia. The clone typically expands in the context of marrow injury, explaining its association with aplastic anaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Aplastic anaemia","Myelodysplastic syndrome","Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia","Microangiopathic haemolytic anaemias (TTP/HUS)","Other causes of intravascular haemolysis","Inherited haemolytic anaemias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood flow cytometry for GPI-anchored proteins (CD55/CD59) and FLAER to identify and size the PNH clone","Full blood count, reticulocytes, and blood film","Markers of intravascular haemolysis: LDH, haptoglobin, and urinary haemosiderin","Direct antiglobulin test (typically negative) to exclude immune haemolysis","Bone marrow examination with cytogenetics to assess for aplastic anaemia or myelodysplasia","Imaging for suspected venous thrombosis, particularly abdominal veins"],"redFlags":["Acute venous thrombosis, especially Budd-Chiari or other intra-abdominal or cerebral vein thrombosis","Severe haemolytic exacerbation with profound anaemia or acute kidney injury","Progressive pancytopenia indicating evolving marrow failure","Meningococcal or other encapsulated-organism sepsis in patients receiving complement inhibition"],"prognosis":"Historically PNH carried substantial mortality, largely from thrombosis and marrow failure, but terminal complement inhibitors have greatly reduced haemolysis and thrombotic events and improved survival towards that of the general population; outcomes still depend on the degree of underlying marrow failure and access to therapy.","pitfalls":["Not screening for a PNH clone in patients with unexplained intravascular haemolysis, aplastic anaemia, or myelodysplasia","Attributing thrombosis solely to other risk factors and missing the underlying complement-mediated cause","Relying on outdated lysis-based tests instead of flow cytometry for diagnosis","Starting terminal complement inhibition without meningococcal vaccination and infection counselling","Overlooking iron and folate needs or the nitric-oxide-related symptoms that drive morbidity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"paroxysmal_supraventricular_tachycardias":{"name":"Paroxysmal Supraventricular Tachycardias","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PSVT","AV nodal reentry tachycardia","AVNRT","AV reciprocating tachycardia","AVRT","Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome related SVT"],"chapter":256,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1938","clinicalPearls":["PSVT here refers to sustained supraventricular tachycardias that depend on the AV node: AVNRT, junctional tachycardia, AV reciprocating tachycardia (AVRT) using an accessory pathway, and preexcited tachycardias (Harrison 22e p.1938).","AVNRT is the most common form of PSVT, accounting for roughly 60% of cases referred for catheter ablation, typically presenting in the second to fourth decades and more often in women (Harrison 22e p.1939).","AVNRT mechanism is reentry within the AV node and perinodal atrium, made possible by dual conduction pathways; the typical form uses a slowly conducting pathway (right inferior nodal extension) for the antegrade limb and the fast pathway as the exit (Harrison 22e p.1939).","In typical AVNRT atrial and ventricular activation are near-simultaneous, so the P wave falls during, just before, or just after the QRS and is hard to see — often appearing as a pseudo-r' in V1 and pseudo-S waves in leads II, III, and aVF (Harrison 22e p.1939).","Simultaneous atrial and ventricular contraction drives atrial contraction against a closed tricuspid valve, producing cannon A waves and a fluttering neck sensation; elevated venous pressure can release natriuretic peptides causing post-tachycardia diuresis (Harrison 22e p.1939).","Unlike atrial tachycardias, maneuvers or drugs that block the AV node terminate AVNRT and orthodromic AVRT, because the AV node is part of the reentry circuit (Harrison 22e p.1939).","Accessory pathways occur in about 1 in 1500-2000 people; most patients have structurally normal hearts, but APs are associated with Ebstein's anomaly and certain hypertrophic cardiomyopathies including PRKAG2 mutations, Danon's disease, and Fabry's disease (Harrison 22e p.1940).","Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome is defined as ventricular preexcitation during sinus rhythm (short PR <0.12 s, delta wave, widened QRS) plus episodes of PSVT (Harrison 22e p.1940).","Orthodromic AVRT is the most common AP-mediated PSVT: the wavefront goes antegrade down the AV node/His-Purkinje and returns retrograde up the AP, giving a narrow (or aberrant) QRS with the P wave always following the QRS, never simultaneous with it (Harrison 22e p.1941).","Concealed accessory pathways conduct only retrograde (ventricle to atrium), so no preexcitation is seen in sinus rhythm, yet SVT can still occur (Harrison 22e pp.1940-1941).","Antidromic AVRT and bystander AP conduction produce preexcited (wide-complex) tachycardias that can be indistinguishable from monomorphic VT; the presence of preexcitation in sinus rhythm suggests the diagnosis (Harrison 22e pp.1941-1942).","Junctional ectopic tachycardia arises from automaticity within the AV node, is rare in adults, and is more often seen as an incessant tachycardia in children, especially perioperatively after congenital heart surgery (Harrison 22e p.1940)."],"pathophysiology":"PSVTs in this group are AV node-dependent. AVNRT is a reentrant circuit within the compact AV node and perinodal atrium enabled by dual pathways: a slowly conducting pathway (usually the right inferior nodal extension running toward the coronary sinus floor) and a faster pathway near the His bundle, allowing a self-sustaining loop. Accessory pathways are congenital muscular connections that bridge atrium and ventricle across the fibrous AV rings owing to incomplete partitioning during development; they most often lie at the left free wall, followed by posteroseptal, right free wall, and anteroseptal sites. Antegrade conduction over an AP produces ventricular preexcitation (short PR, delta wave, wide QRS), with QRS/delta morphology determined by pathway location. AVRT reentry circulates between the AV node/His-Purkinje system and the AP (orthodromic when antegrade over the node and retrograde over the AP, antidromic when reversed). Junctional ectopic tachycardia is due to abnormal automaticity within the AV node rather than reentry (Harrison 22e pp.1938-1942).","additionalDifferentials":["Atrioventricular nodal reentry tachycardia (AVNRT)","Orthodromic AV reciprocating tachycardia (concealed or manifest AP)","Atypical AVNRT (long R-P, P wave between QRS complexes)","Focal atrial tachycardia","Atrial flutter","Atrial fibrillation","Permanent junctional reciprocating tachycardia (PJRT) from a slowly conducting septal AP","Junctional ectopic / accelerated junctional rhythm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Ventricular tachycardia (especially versus antidromic AVRT or PSVT with aberrancy, wide-complex tachycardia)","Sinus tachycardia","Lateral or inferior wall myocardial infarction Q waves (mimicked by negative delta waves of left-sided or diaphragmatic accessory pathways)","Bundle branch block pattern (mimicked by preexcitation: right-sided AP gives LBBB-like V1, left-sided AP gives RBBB-like V1)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous ECG monitoring during the episode and during vagal/pharmacologic maneuvers (the response can establish the mechanism)","12-lead ECG during tachycardia (P-wave timing and morphology, pseudo-r' in V1, pseudo-S in inferior leads) and during sinus rhythm (delta wave, short PR)","Echocardiography to exclude Ebstein's anomaly, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy associated with APs, or tachycardia-mediated cardiomyopathy","Invasive electrophysiology study to define mechanism and to assess whether an AP can support rapid antegrade conduction (risk stratification)","Ambulatory monitoring or exercise testing to look for abrupt loss of preexcitation suggesting a lower-risk pathway (not completely reliable)"],"redFlags":["Hemodynamic instability with hypotension, loss of consciousness, or respiratory distress","Preexcited atrial fibrillation presenting as a very irregular wide-complex tachycardia","Accessory pathway with shortest preexcited R-R interval <250 ms during AF (about 25% of preexcitation-causing APs), associated with higher risk of ventricular fibrillation and sudden death","Wide-complex tachycardia that cannot be confidently distinguished from VT (manage as VT until proven otherwise)","Rapid SVT in the elderly precipitating angina, pulmonary edema, hypotension, or syncope"],"prognosis":"In patients without structural heart disease, AVNRT is not life-threatening though it may cause significant symptoms. Catheter ablation of the slow AV nodal pathway is curative in over 95% of cases, with a risk of AV block requiring permanent pacing in under 1%. For accessory pathways, ablation efficacy is around 95% (varies by location) with serious complications in under 3% and procedural mortality under 1 in 1000. Risk of cardiac arrest in symptomatic preexcitation patients is roughly 2 per 1000 adults and likely higher in children. Adults with asymptomatic preexcitation have an estimated sudden death risk of about 1 per 1000 patient-years, while children carry a higher risk of around 2 per 1000 patient-years (Harrison 22e pp.1939-1942).","pitfalls":["Administering AV nodal-blocking agents (verapamil, diltiazem, beta blockers, adenosine, amiodarone) during preexcited AF is contraindicated, as slowing the AV node without slowing the AP can accelerate ventricular rate and precipitate ventricular fibrillation (Harrison 22e p.1942).","Gradual loss of AP conduction with rising sympathetic tone on exercise testing does not reliably indicate a low-risk pathway, because it can simply reflect shortening AV nodal conduction time (Harrison 22e p.1942).","Adenosine itself can precipitate atrial fibrillation in up to ~15% of patients, which is hazardous in WPW where AF may cause hemodynamic instability (Harrison 22e p.1942).","Adenosine is contraindicated after cardiac transplantation due to denervation hypersensitivity and may aggravate bronchospasm (Harrison 22e p.1942).","Negative delta waves from left-sided or diaphragmatic/paraseptal APs can mimic Q waves of lateral or inferior myocardial infarction (Harrison 22e p.1940).","Left free wall APs may show minimal or absent preexcitation on the 12-lead ECG because of the distance from the sinus node, so preexcitation can be missed (Harrison 22e p.1940).","PJRT and atypical AVNRT both produce long R-P tachycardias that can be difficult to distinguish from atrial tachycardia without an invasive EP study (Harrison 22e p.1941)."],"severityClassification":"No formal severity grading scale; risk is stratified clinically by hemodynamic stability and, for accessory pathways, by the capacity for rapid antegrade conduction. A shortest preexcited R-R interval <250 ms during atrial fibrillation marks a high-risk pathway predisposing to ventricular fibrillation and sudden death (Harrison 22e p.1942).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1938","Harrison 22e p.1939","Harrison 22e p.1940","Harrison 22e p.1941","Harrison 22e p.1942"],"crossLinks":[]},"parvovirus_infections":{"name":"Parvovirus Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases (Hematologic/Multisystem viral infection)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Parvovirus B19 infection","B19V infection","Erythema infectiosum","Fifth disease","Slapped-cheek disease","Human bocavirus infection"],"chapter":202,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1522","clinicalPearls":["Parvovirus B19 (B19V) has a narrow tropism for erythroid progenitor cells in the bone marrow, which underlies most of its clinical effects on red cell production.","Most B19V infections are asymptomatic or cause only a mild nonspecific illness; the characteristic disease, erythema infectiosum (fifth disease, slapped-cheek disease), occurs mainly in children 3-12 years old.","Transmission is primarily respiratory and occurs before the onset of rash or arthralgia; once the rash appears the child is no longer infectious and can return to school.","In adults the classic slapped-cheek appearance is usually absent; instead they often present with arthralgia, with or without a macular rash.","Arthropathy occurs in roughly 50% of infected adults, is more common in women, tends to be symmetrical and involves small joints of the hands (sometimes ankles, knees, wrists), and can mimic rheumatoid arthritis with detectable rheumatoid factor.","In patients dependent on rapid red cell turnover (hemolytic disorders, hemoglobinopathies, red cell enzymopathies, autoimmune hemolytic anemia), B19V is the predominant cause of transient aplastic crisis (TAC).","Bone marrow in TAC and pure red cell aplasia shows characteristic giant pronormoblasts with absent or scarce erythroid precursors; in a sickle-cell patient, reticulocytopenia with acute worsening anemia is diagnostic without marrow examination.","Chronic B19V infection causing pure red cell aplasia (PRCA)/persistent anemia is a treatable cause of anemia in immunocompromised hosts and is important to recognize because effective immunoglobulin therapy exists.","Individuals genetically lacking the blood group P antigen (globoside) are naturally resistant to B19V infection because that antigen is a required receptor.","Detection of B19V DNA in tissue by PCR alone does not establish causation, since viral DNA can persist in healthy tissues for decades; quantitative PCR and serologic correlation are needed.","Human bocavirus 1 (HBoV1) is a separate human parvovirus causing upper and lower respiratory tract infection in young children (peak ~2 years) and infecting nearly all children before age 5.","An immune-mediated rash can appear in chronically infected immunocompromised patients after immunoglobulin administration, due to antigen-antibody complexes in skin."],"pathophysiology":"B19V is a small nonenveloped single-stranded DNA virus (genus Erythroparvovirus) that replicates in erythroid progenitor cells. Cell entry depends on the unknown primary receptor recognized by VP1u and, at a later step, the blood group P antigen (globoside); people lacking P antigen are naturally resistant. Infection produces high-titer viremia (up to ~10^12-10^14 particles/mL at the peak) and virus-induced cytotoxicity that halts red cell production. In immunocompetent hosts with normal hematopoiesis the arrest of erythropoiesis is transient with only a minimal fall in hemoglobin, resolving as neutralizing antibody is produced. In hosts with increased erythropoiesis (e.g., hemolytic anemia) the same arrest precipitates a transient aplastic crisis; in those who fail to mount neutralizing antibody (immunocompromised hosts or the fetus) lytic infection persists and chronic anemia/PRCA develops. The classic rash and arthropathy of erythema infectiosum are immune-mediated, beginning 2-3 weeks after infection as the IgM response peaks. B19V can also enter nonpermissive cells (B cells, monocytes, endothelial cells) by antibody-dependent enhancement and persist as DNA for life in many tissues (heart, liver, kidney, synovium, brain, bone), generally without disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Other viral exanthems (B19V rash is difficult to distinguish clinically; laboratory confirmation is advised when a definite diagnosis is needed, such as in pregnancy)","Rheumatoid arthritis (B19V polyarthropathy can mimic it, and rheumatoid factor may be present)","Other causes of nonimmune hydrops fetalis","Other causes of transient aplastic crisis or red cell aplasia"],"infectionMimics":["Other respiratory viral infections (for HBoV1, which presents like other viral RTIs with fever, cough, wheezing)","Co-infection with Plasmodium has been suggested to contribute to severe anemia in young children with malaria"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Rheumatoid arthritis (clinical and serologic overlap with B19V polyarthropathy syndrome)","Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura (B19V can be associated with thrombocytopenia)"],"additionalInvestigations":["B19V IgM by indirect enzyme immunoassay - detectable at the time of rash in erythema infectiosum and by the third day of TAC; may persist about 3 months or longer","B19V IgG - detectable by the seventh day of illness and persists for life, marking immunity; seroconversion or a fourfold or greater rise on paired sera taken 2 weeks apart is diagnostic of acute infection","IgG avidity testing and epitope-type-specific (ETS) EIA to distinguish acute from past infection","B19V DNA by PCR in serum (or amniotic fluid), with quantitative PCR preferred because low-level DNA can persist for months to years even in healthy people","Bone marrow examination showing giant pronormoblasts and absent/scant erythroid precursors in TAC or PRCA","In suspected fetal infection: maternal serology in exposed seronegative mothers, with regular monitoring if positive, and PCR of amniotic fluid or tissue"],"redFlags":["Severe to life-threatening anemia with a low reticulocyte count in a patient with a known hemolytic disorder (transient aplastic crisis)","Persistent anemia with reticulocytopenia and extremely high serum B19V DNA titers in an immunocompromised patient (pure red cell aplasia)","Maternal B19V infection before gestational week 20, given the risk of fetal loss and hydrops fetalis","Nonimmune hydrops fetalis with gross edema and severe fetal anemia (B19V causes an estimated 10-20% of such cases)","Hemophagocytic syndrome occasionally precipitated by B19V"],"prognosis":"Most B19V infections are self-limited. Erythema infectiosum and B19V-associated arthropathy generally resolve spontaneously, though joint symptoms may recur for weeks to months. Transient aplastic crisis is, by definition, transient: anemia resolves once cytopathic infection of erythroid progenitors ceases, and lifelong immunity follows. Chronic anemia/PRCA in immunocompromised hosts is treatable and can be cured or ameliorated with immunoglobulin. Risk of fetal loss is highest (excess risk roughly 9%) when maternal infection occurs before gestational week 20 and is very low thereafter; most fetal infections resolve spontaneously, B19V does not appear to be teratogenic, and rare reports of eye damage, CNS abnormalities, and congenital anemia exist.","pitfalls":["Interpreting tissue B19V DNA detected by PCR as proof of active disease - viral DNA persists in healthy tissues for decades, so PCR alone in tissue should not establish a B19V etiology.","Presence of B19V DNA in synovium does not prove a causal role in arthritis, since healthy individuals also harbor viral DNA in synovia.","In TAC and chronic infection, classic immune-mediated symptoms (rash, arthralgia) are often absent, making the diagnosis easy to miss in immunocompromised or hemolytic patients.","Relying on qualitative PCR for HBoV1 in respiratory secretions can misattribute etiology, since HBoV1 DNA may persist for weeks to months after acute infection.","Serology may be negative or only weakly positive in immunodeficient patients with PRCA, so quantitative serum DNA is needed."],"severityClassification":"Clinical spectrum of B19V infection (per Table 202-1): fifth disease in healthy children (IgM and IgG positive, viral load >10^4 IU/mL); polyarthropathy syndrome in healthy adults; transient aplastic crisis in patients with increased erythropoiesis (often >10^12 IU/mL, rapidly decreasing); persistent anemia/pure red cell aplasia in immunodeficient or immunosuppressed patients (often >10^12 IU/mL, typically >10^6 untreated); and hydrops fetalis/congenital anemia in fetuses of mothers infected before 20 weeks.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1522","Harrison 22e p.1523","Harrison 22e p.1524","Harrison 22e p.1525"],"crossLinks":[]},"patent_ductus_arteriosus":{"name":"Patent Ductus Arteriosus","system":"Paediatric cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PDA","persistent ductus arteriosus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Persistence of the fetal ductus arteriosus beyond the normal postnatal closure period allows shunting between the aorta and pulmonary artery; it is especially common in preterm infants.","Because aortic pressure exceeds pulmonary pressure once pulmonary resistance falls, the shunt is left-to-right, increasing pulmonary blood flow and left heart volume load.","The classic sign is a continuous machinery murmur beneath the left clavicle, though in neonates only a systolic component may be audible.","A large shunt produces bounding peripheral pulses and a wide pulse pressure from diastolic runoff into the pulmonary circulation.","In preterm infants a significant ductus can worsen respiratory distress, contribute to heart failure, and is associated with complications such as necrotising enterocolitis and intraventricular haemorrhage.","In some duct-dependent congenital heart lesions the ductus is essential for survival, and a prostaglandin infusion is used to keep it open until definitive intervention.","Closure of an isolated symptomatic ductus may use pharmacological prostaglandin inhibitors in preterm infants or transcatheter/surgical closure in others.","Echocardiography confirms the ductus, estimates shunt size, and assesses left heart dilation and pulmonary pressures.","A large, longstanding, unrepaired ductus risks pulmonary vascular disease and eventual shunt reversal."],"pathophysiology":"The ductus arteriosus normally constricts and closes after birth in response to rising oxygen tension and falling circulating prostaglandins. When it remains patent, the postnatal fall in pulmonary vascular resistance establishes a left-to-right shunt from the aorta to the pulmonary artery throughout the cardiac cycle. This increases pulmonary blood flow and volume-loads the left atrium and left ventricle, while diastolic runoff from the aorta lowers diastolic pressure and can compromise systemic and coronary perfusion. A large chronic shunt raises pulmonary pressures and can lead to pulmonary vascular remodelling and shunt reversal over time.","additionalDifferentials":["Ventricular septal defect","Aortopulmonary window","Venous hum (an innocent continuous murmur)","Coronary or systemic arteriovenous fistula","Truncus arteriosus","Peripheral pulmonary stenosis murmur"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography to confirm the ductus and assess shunt magnitude and left heart size","Chest radiograph for cardiomegaly and increased pulmonary vascular markings in large shunts","Electrocardiogram for left ventricular volume overload","Assessment of pulse character and pulse pressure","Monitoring of respiratory status and feeding in preterm infants"],"redFlags":["Worsening heart failure or respiratory deterioration in a preterm infant with a significant ductus","Abrupt cardiovascular collapse in a neonate with a duct-dependent lesion as the ductus closes","Signs of poor systemic perfusion from diastolic runoff (weak distal perfusion, oliguria, feeding intolerance)","Development of pulmonary hypertension or shunt reversal in a large longstanding ductus","Necrotising enterocolitis or intraventricular haemorrhage associated with a large shunt"],"prognosis":"An isolated ductus closed in a timely fashion has an excellent outlook. In preterm infants outcome depends heavily on the associated prematurity complications. A large duct left untreated risks irreversible pulmonary vascular disease and a poorer prognosis.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise that some critical congenital heart lesions depend on ductal patency, and inadvertently allowing the ductus to close","Misinterpreting a benign venous hum as a pathological continuous murmur","Underestimating the contribution of a significant ductus to a preterm infant's respiratory difficulty","Overlooking the wide pulse pressure and bounding pulses that signal a large shunt","Delaying closure of a haemodynamically significant duct until pulmonary vascular disease develops"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"pathobiology_of_neurologic_diseases":{"name":"Pathobiology of Neurologic Diseases","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Neuroimmunology and neuroinflammation","Mechanisms of neurodegeneration","Prion pathobiology","Neural stem cell biology"],"chapter":435,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3394","clinicalPearls":["Microglia, the brain's resident innate immune and antigen-presenting cells, arise from primitive yolk-sac macrophages that seed the CNS before the blood-brain barrier forms and are sustained largely by self-renewal rather than by replenishment from the circulation; under some conditions peripherally derived macrophages can become microglia-like and replace damaged cells.","Microglia sculpt and prune synapses via classical complement proteins (C1q, C3) and complement receptors; in Alzheimer's disease, excess beta-amyloid drives C1q/C3 tagging of synapses that are then eliminated by CR3-expressing microglia, and knockout of C1q or C3 can rescue some neurodegenerative pathology in models.","Genetic data link innate immunity to neurodegeneration: more than half of genes implicated in Alzheimer's GWAS involve innate immune/microglial processes, and loss-of-function mutations in the microglial receptor TREM2 increase Alzheimer's risk up to roughly fourfold.","Around 10% of frontotemporal degeneration cases are due to heterozygous granulin (GRN) mutations causing progranulin haploinsufficiency; progranulin is a neurotrophic factor essential for lysosomal function and microglial homeostasis.","Activated microglia (via IL-1alpha, TNF, and C1q) can convert astrocytes to a reactive, neurotoxic (A1) phenotype that loses phagocytic capacity and damages neurons and oligodendrocytes through complement, nitric oxide, and free radicals; astrocytes are non-cell-autonomous contributors across MS, AD, PD, and ALS.","The glymphatic system uses periarterial CSF inflow through aquaporin-4-rich astrocyte foot processes to clear interstitial solutes and debris; clearance accelerates during sleep, and in models sleep deprivation increased beta-amyloid plaque accumulation while aquaporin-4 knockout reduced interstitial flow.","Harrison proposes a prion-based reclassification of neurodegenerative disease in which host proteins (PrP, Abeta, tau, alpha-synuclein, SOD1, possibly huntingtin) adopt self-propagating, beta-sheet-rich conformations that template normal precursor protein and spread between synaptically connected neurons.","The prion concept holds that the transmissible agents of kuru, CJD, scrapie, and BSE are devoid of nucleic acid and composed of host-encoded protein; AD is framed as a 'double-prion' disease in which both Abeta and tau prions coexist.","Late onset of inherited neurodegenerations, despite lifelong expression of the mutant protein, is attributed to age-dependent decline in protein quality-control/clearance mechanisms, consistent with the view that these are disorders of the aging nervous system.","Toxic small oligomers, rather than the mature plaques, tangles, and inclusion bodies, are increasingly viewed as the harmful species; visible aggregates may represent a relatively protective sequestration of toxic protein.","Impaired autophagy and lysosomal handling are central to neurodegeneration; GBA (glucocerebrosidase) mutations underlie about 5% of Parkinson's disease and 8-9% of dementia with Lewy bodies, with bidirectional inhibition between alpha-synuclein and glucocerebrosidase.","Induced pluripotent stem cell (iPSC)-derived neurons and 3D cerebral organoids model human neurodevelopmental disease (microcephaly, lissencephaly/Miller-Dieker, fragile X, Rett, Timothy syndrome) and confirmed Zika virus tropism for human neural progenitor (radial glia) cells as the cause of congenital microcephaly."],"pathophysiology":"This foundational chapter frames nervous-system disease around several converging mechanisms rather than a single illness. (1) Neuroimmunology/neuroinflammation: microglia and brain macrophages (yolk-sac-derived innate immune cells) and astrocytes are active, non-passive participants that prune synapses through complement, present antigen, secrete cytokines and trophic factors, and can shift between homeostatic and tissue-damaging states; crosstalk among microglia, astrocytes, and oligodendrocytes produces non-cell-autonomous pathology in MS, AD, PD, and ALS. (2) Myelin biology: oligodendrocytes maintain saltatory conduction and provide metabolic/neuroprotective support to axons, with activity-dependent (adaptive) myelination. (3) Waste clearance: the glymphatic system and meningeal lymphatic-like vessels clear interstitial solutes and aggregated proteins, with sleep-dependent and age-dependent modulation via aquaporin-4. (4) The microbiome can influence brain disease through molecular mimicry, immune modulation, circulating bacterial metabolites, enteric neural signaling, and possible prion-like seeding (e.g., the unproven hypothesis that E. coli Curli templates alpha-synuclein). (5) Protein aggregation and prion biology: misfolded, self-propagating proteins (Abeta, tau, alpha-synuclein, PrP, SOD1, TDP-43, expanded-polyglutamine huntingtin) spread trans-synaptically; toxic oligomers, defective ubiquitin-proteasome and autophagy-lysosome clearance, mitochondrial dynamics (fission/fusion via Drp1, MFN1/2, OPA1; mitophagy via PINK1/parkin), and retromer (VPS35/VPS26/VPS29) dysfunction all contribute to neuronal death. (6) Stem cell biology: iPSCs and cerebral organoids reproduce early human brain-development disorders and enable disease modeling and drug screening.","additionalDifferentials":["Alzheimer's disease and other amyloid/tau dementias","Parkinson's disease, dementia with Lewy bodies, and multiple-system atrophy (alpha-synucleinopathies)","Frontotemporal degeneration (tau, TDP-43, FUS; GRN and C9orf72)","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (SOD1, TDP-43, FUS, C9orf72)","Huntington's disease and other polyglutamine cerebellar degenerations","Multiple sclerosis and autoimmune/neuroinflammatory demyelination","Prion diseases (CJD, kuru, GSS, fatal insomnia)","Neurodevelopmental disorders (primary microcephaly, lissencephaly/Miller-Dieker, fragile X, Rett, Timothy syndrome)"],"infectionMimics":["Slow-virus paradigm historically invoked for kuru, CJD, and scrapie, later supplanted by the nucleic-acid-free prion hypothesis","Congenital Zika virus syndrome producing microcephaly via direct infection of neural progenitor cells","Microbiome/bacterial-protein seeding hypotheses (e.g., E. coli Curli templating alpha-synuclein) blurring infective versus degenerative boundaries"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Protein-aggregation neurodegenerations whose visible plaques/tangles/inclusions may be markers rather than the primary toxic species","Mitochondrial disorders (Friedreich's ataxia; MFN2-related Charcot-Marie-Tooth type 2A; OPA1-related dominant optic atrophy) presenting as neurodegeneration"],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for disease-causing/modifying mutations (APP, MAPT, SNCA, LRRK2, parkin, PINK1, DJ-1, GBA, SOD1, TDP-43, FUS, C9orf72, GRN, PRNP, VPS35, FMR1, MECP2, CACNA1C)","Neuropathologic/immunostaining characterization of aggregates (Abeta plaques, neurofibrillary tau tangles, Lewy bodies, glial cytoplasmic inclusions, ubiquitinated TDP-43 inclusions)","Patient-derived iPSC lines and cerebral organoids for disease modeling and high-throughput compound screening","Single-cell transcriptomic sequencing to resolve microglial and astrocyte heterogeneity"],"redFlags":["Progressive, age-related cognitive or motor decline suggesting an underlying self-propagating proteinopathy","Family history with high penetrance (e.g., GRN mutations highly penetrant for FTD) prompting genetic evaluation","Rapidly progressive dementia raising concern for a transmissible prion disease","Microcephaly with developmental delay and intractable epilepsy in neurodevelopmental disorders"],"prognosis":"As a mechanistic overview the chapter does not give a single prognosis. It frames many neurodegenerative diseases as relentlessly progressive disorders of the aging nervous system driven by self-propagating prion-like proteins, while emphasizing that emerging understanding of complement, microglial, lysosomal/autophagy, mitochondrial, and prion-clearance pathways offers new and rational therapeutic targets (e.g., anti-C1q and anti-TREM2 antibody trials, progranulin-directed and gene/protein-replacement strategies) and new hope for prevention, treatment, and repair.","pitfalls":["Assuming that macroscopic aggregates (plaques, tangles, inclusion bodies) directly cause neuronal dysfunction; toxic soluble oligomers may be the harmful species and sequestration may be protective.","Viewing microglia and astrocytes as merely secondary scavengers; genetic evidence places them as primary drivers of many neurodegenerative conditions.","Treating the resting-versus-activated microglial dichotomy as adequate; single-cell data reveal extensive, functionally heterogeneous states.","Over-extrapolating from rodent models; several human organoid findings (e.g., outer radial glia defects in Miller-Dieker syndrome) were not seen in mice, and some iPSC findings require replication before confidence.","Accepting unproven microbiome-seeding hypotheses (e.g., bacterial Curli triggering Parkinson's) as established; no proven microbiome-modulating therapy yet exists for any brain disease.","Therapeutic targeting of broadly acting systems (e.g., complement) risks off-target toxicity and loss of homeostatic functions such as physiologic synaptic pruning."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.3394","Harrison 22e p.3399","Harrison 22e p.3400","Harrison 22e p.3401","Harrison 22e p.3403"],"crossLinks":[]},"pathogenesis_diagnosis_and_treatment_of":{"name":"Pathogenesis, Diagnosis, and Treatment of Fungal Infections","system":"Multisystem / Infectious Diseases (Mycoses)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Fungal infections (general principles)","Mycoses overview","Principles of medical mycology"],"chapter":217,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1687","clinicalPearls":["Human fungal disease has risen sharply with the AIDS pandemic, broad antibacterial use, cytotoxic chemotherapy, biologic/precision-medicine immunosuppressants, and solid-organ or hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (Harrison 22e p.1687).","Mycoses are categorized clinically as mucocutaneous versus deep-organ disease, and epidemiologically as endemic versus opportunistic; mucocutaneous disease causes morbidity but is rarely fatal, whereas deep-organ disease is often severe with high mortality (Harrison 22e p.1687).","Endemic fungi are environmentally acquired and not part of normal flora, typically causing self-limited disease in immunocompetent hosts but severe illness when immunity is impaired; opportunistic fungi such as Candida and Aspergillus are commensal or ubiquitous and invade when host defenses fail (Harrison 22e p.1687).","Definitive diagnosis requires histopathologic demonstration of the fungus invading tissue together with culture from the specimen; a positive culture from blood or tissue can instead reflect colonization or laboratory contamination depending on organism and site (Harrison 22e p.1690).","PAS and Gomori methenamine silver (GMS) are the principal fungal stains; Candida (unlike most fungi) is visible on Gram-stained tissue, and a positive India ink stain of CSF is diagnostic for cryptococcosis (Harrison 22e p.1690).","The most reliable antigen/serologic tests are Coccidioides antibody, Histoplasma antigen, and cryptococcal polysaccharide antigen; galactomannan (best in BAL fluid) aids aspergillosis diagnosis but has frequent false negatives during antifungal prophylaxis and false positives with other fungi (Harrison 22e p.1691).","The serum beta-D-glucan assay has a high negative predictive value for invasive candidiasis but lacks specificity, and T2 magnetic resonance is FDA-approved for detecting Candida in blood (Harrison 22e p.1691).","Inherited CARD9 deficiency is the only known primary immunodeficiency that produces fungus-specific susceptibility without predisposition to other infections, autoimmunity, allergy, or cancer, causing chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, CNS Candida/Aspergillus/phaeohyphomycete infection, and deep dermatophytosis (Harrison 22e p.1688).","Antifungal host defense is compartmentalized: IL-17-producing lymphoid cells protect mucosal surfaces against Candida invasion, neutrophils control invasive Aspergillus and Candida, and the IL-12/IFN-gamma axis with activated macrophages controls intramacrophagic fungi such as Cryptococcus, Pneumocystis, and endemic dimorphic fungi (Harrison 22e pp.1688-1689).","AIDS predisposes to mucosal but not invasive candidiasis (reflecting IL-17 dependence), whereas vaginal candidiasis arises with antibiotic use rather than AIDS or IL-17-targeted biologics, highlighting the protective role of the vaginal microbiota (Harrison 22e p.1689).","Chronic granulomatous disease, caused by NADPH-oxidase subunit mutations impairing neutrophil superoxide generation, carries roughly a 40% lifetime risk of invasive aspergillosis; Aspergillus nidulans is seen almost exclusively in this condition (Harrison 22e pp.1688-1689).","Early initiation of appropriate antifungal therapy is a key determinant of outcome in candidemia, aspergillosis, and mucormycosis, alongside source control (catheter removal in candidemia, abscess drainage, surgical debridement in mucormycosis) and immune reconstitution (Harrison 22e p.1691)."],"pathophysiology":"Fungi are eukaryotes classified morphologically as yeasts (round/budding single cells), molds (filamentous hyphae at both room temperature and in tissue, e.g., Aspergillus, Mucorales, dermatophytes), and dimorphic fungi (yeast or spherical structures in tissue but filamentous forms in the environment). Candida produces both yeast and pseudohyphal forms in tissue except C. glabrata and C. auris, which form only yeasts; Cryptococcus exists only as yeast. Deep-organ infection by molds and endemic dimorphic fungi follows inhalation, whereas commensal Candida invades from mucocutaneous colonization in the gut or skin. Antifungal immunity centers on the C-type lectin receptor/SYK/CARD9 signaling pathway, which recognizes fungal polysaccharides and drives proinflammatory mediator release, leukocyte recruitment, inflammasome activation, and Th17 differentiation. Protection is site- and pathogen-specific: at mucosa, IL-17A/IL-17F and IL-22 from Th17, Tc17, gamma-delta T, and innate lymphoid cells stimulate epithelial antimicrobial peptide production and barrier repair (STAT3-dependent); against invasive molds and Candida, recruited neutrophils assemble the six-subunit NADPH oxidase to generate superoxide for fungal killing; against intramacrophagic fungi, IL-12-producing macrophages activate STAT4 in T cells to release IFN-gamma, which signals through STAT1 (with TNF-alpha and GM-CSF) to enable intracellular killing. Defects at any node, whether inherited (e.g., IL17F/IL17RA/IL17RC/TRAF3IP2, STAT3, STAT1, CARD9, NADPH oxidase subunits, IL12RB1, IFNGR1/2, IRF8, GATA2) or pharmacologic/acquired (IL-17/IL-23 biologics, BTK inhibitors, glucocorticoids, anti-TNF agents, JAK inhibitors, anti-IFN-gamma or anti-GM-CSF autoantibodies), produce predictable, compartment-specific fungal susceptibility (Harrison 22e pp.1687-1689).","additionalDifferentials":["Distinguishing true invasive fungal infection from colonization or laboratory contamination when fungi are recovered from culture (Harrison 22e p.1690)","Chronic cavitary pulmonary mycosis versus cavitary tuberculosis (Harrison 22e p.1690)","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis versus invasive pulmonary aspergillosis in differing host settings (Harrison 22e p.1688)"],"infectionMimics":["Mycobacterial infection (notably tuberculosis), which shares granulomatous histology and intramacrophagic survival with endemic dimorphic fungi (Harrison 22e p.1689)","Salmonellosis and other intramacrophagic pathogens that exploit the same IL-12/IFN-gamma defects as intramacrophagic fungi (Harrison 22e p.1689)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Neoplasia and other causes of granulomatous or necrotizing tissue reaction (H&E-defined granuloma, angioinvasion, necrosis are nonspecific without organism identification) (Harrison 22e p.1690)","Sarcoidosis and other granulomatous conditions in patients predisposed to cryptococcosis (Harrison 22e p.1688)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Histopathology with PAS and GMS stains, plus H&E to assess granuloma, angioinvasion, and necrosis (Harrison 22e p.1690)","Calcofluor white staining with fluorescence microscopy for fungi in fluid specimens (Harrison 22e p.1690)","Lysis-centrifugation blood culture to increase sensitivity for Candida and less common fungi such as Histoplasma (Harrison 22e p.1690)","MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry for detection and speciation of cultured fungi (Harrison 22e pp.1690-1691)","Antigen and serologic assays: cryptococcal polysaccharide antigen, Histoplasma antigen, Coccidioides antibody, galactomannan (BAL preferred), and serum beta-D-glucan (Harrison 22e p.1691)","T2 magnetic resonance assay for Candida in whole blood (Harrison 22e p.1691)","India ink stain of CSF, diagnostic for cryptococcosis (Harrison 22e p.1690)"],"redFlags":["Profound or prolonged neutropenia, allogeneic HSCT, or high-dose glucocorticoids portend invasive mold disease (aspergillosis, mucormycosis) and invasive candidiasis (Harrison 22e pp.1688-1689)","Diabetic ketoacidosis as a setting for rhinocerebral/sinopulmonary mucormycosis (Harrison 22e p.1688)","Advanced AIDS with low CD4 counts predisposing to intramacrophagic and mucosal mycoses (Harrison 22e p.1689)","Delay in appropriate antifungal therapy in candidemia, aspergillosis, or mucormycosis, which worsens outcome (Harrison 22e p.1691)","Failure to achieve source control (retained central venous catheter in candidemia, undrained abscess, undebrided necrotic sinus tissue in mucormycosis) (Harrison 22e p.1691)","Emerging drug-resistant species: azole/echinocandin-resistant Candida glabrata, Candida auris, and azole-resistant Aspergillus fumigatus (Harrison 22e p.1687)"],"prognosis":"Outcome hinges on host immune status, the fungus and site involved, timeliness of appropriate therapy, source control, and immune reconstitution. Mucocutaneous mycoses cause morbidity but are rarely fatal, whereas deep-organ infections are frequently severe with high mortality. Before amphotericin B, cryptococcal meningitis and other disseminated mycoses were nearly always fatal. A favorable prognosis in opportunistic mycoses depends substantially on restoring host immunity, for example neutrophil recovery, tapering of immunosuppression, or starting combination antiretroviral therapy in AIDS (Harrison 22e pp.1687, 1691).","pitfalls":["Treating a positive fungal culture as proof of infection when it may represent colonization or contamination, depending on organism and anatomic site (Harrison 22e p.1690)","Relying on galactomannan for aspergillosis without accounting for frequent false negatives under antifungal prophylaxis and false positives from other fungi (Harrison 22e p.1691)","Interpreting a beta-D-glucan result without recognizing its lack of specificity despite a high negative predictive value (Harrison 22e p.1691)","Attempting to identify fungi reliably on H&E alone, which defines tissue reaction but is insufficient for speciation (Harrison 22e p.1690)","Overlooking that fluconazole and echinocandins lack activity against molds (and echinocandins against Cryptococcus and endemic dimorphic fungi), and that voriconazole/echinocandins do not cover Mucorales (Harrison 22e pp.1691-1692)","Failing to monitor drug levels where pharmacokinetics are variable (e.g., itraconazole capsules, voriconazole with CYP2C19 polymorphism) (Harrison 22e pp.1691-1692)","Not recognizing that emerging immunomodulatory biologics (IL-17/IL-23 inhibitors, BTK inhibitors, JAK inhibitors, anti-TNF, SYK inhibitors) create predictable, compartment-specific fungal risk (Harrison 22e pp.1688-1689)"],"severityClassification":"Classified by anatomic depth (mucocutaneous infections, which are rarely fatal, versus deep-organ infections, which are severe and often highly fatal) and by epidemiologic category (endemic versus opportunistic). Severity within these groups is further modulated by host immune competence, the implicated fungus, and the involved tissue (Harrison 22e p.1687).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1687","Harrison 22e p.1688","Harrison 22e p.1689","Harrison 22e p.1690","Harrison 22e p.1691","Harrison 22e p.1692"],"crossLinks":[]},"pediatric_status_asthmaticus":{"name":"Pediatric Status Asthmaticus","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["status asthmaticus","acute severe asthma","severe asthma exacerbation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A severe, sustained asthma exacerbation that does not respond adequately to initial bronchodilator therapy and constitutes a medical emergency.","Assessment combines clinical signs (work of breathing, accessory muscle use, air entry, mental state) with objective measures such as oxygen saturation and, where feasible, peak flow.","A quiet chest with poor air entry is ominous, indicating severe airflow limitation rather than improvement.","Rising carbon dioxide on blood gas in a tiring child signals impending respiratory failure; early in an attack hypocapnia is expected, so a normalising or high CO2 is a warning sign.","First-line treatment uses inhaled short-acting beta-agonists, often with inhaled anticholinergic agents, and early systemic corticosteroids, alongside oxygen to maintain saturations.","Escalation options for refractory cases include intravenous magnesium sulfate, continuous nebulised bronchodilator, and intravenous beta-agonist, with careful monitoring.","Agitation, drowsiness, exhaustion, cyanosis, or a silent chest indicate life-threatening disease requiring intensive care and possible ventilatory support.","Mechanical ventilation carries particular risks in asthma (air trapping and barotrauma) and is reserved for refractory respiratory failure using lung-protective, permissive strategies.","Identifying and addressing triggers, ensuring correct inhaler technique, and providing a written action plan help prevent recurrence."],"pathophysiology":"Acute severe asthma results from intense airway inflammation, bronchial smooth muscle constriction, mucosal oedema, and mucus plugging, producing widespread airflow obstruction that is worse on expiration. Air trapping and dynamic hyperinflation increase the work of breathing and impair gas exchange, causing ventilation-perfusion mismatch and hypoxaemia. Early tachypnoea drives off carbon dioxide, but as the child fatigues and obstruction worsens, alveolar ventilation falls, carbon dioxide rises, and respiratory acidosis develops, heralding respiratory failure. Hyperinflation also impairs venous return and can compromise cardiac output.","additionalDifferentials":["Bronchiolitis or viral-induced wheeze in younger children","Foreign body aspiration","Anaphylaxis with bronchospasm","Pneumonia with wheeze","Vocal cord dysfunction","Cardiac failure or a wheeze-mimicking cardiac lesion"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous pulse oximetry and clinical severity scoring","Blood gas in severe or deteriorating cases to assess for rising carbon dioxide","Peak expiratory flow where age and cooperation allow","Chest radiograph if complications such as pneumothorax, consolidation, or foreign body are suspected","Electrolytes when high-dose beta-agonist therapy is used (risk of hypokalaemia)"],"redFlags":["Silent chest with poor air entry","Exhaustion, drowsiness, confusion, or agitation indicating hypoxia and impending failure","Rising or normalising carbon dioxide in a tiring child (impending respiratory failure)","Cyanosis or falling oxygen saturation despite maximal therapy","Poor response to initial bronchodilators or rapid clinical deterioration"],"prognosis":"Most children with severe exacerbations recover fully with prompt, escalating treatment. A minority progress to respiratory failure requiring intensive care and ventilation, which carries higher risk. Poor prognostic factors include delayed treatment, prior life-threatening attacks, and inadequate preventive management; good discharge planning reduces relapse.","pitfalls":["Misreading a quiet chest or a normalising carbon dioxide as improvement rather than deterioration","Delaying systemic corticosteroids or under-treating a severe exacerbation","Over-relying on oxygen saturation alone without assessing work of breathing and mental state","Instituting mechanical ventilation without anticipating air trapping and barotrauma","Discharging without addressing triggers, inhaler technique, and an action plan"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"pelvic_inflammatory_disease":{"name":"Pelvic inflammatory disease","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PID","salpingitis","pelvic infection","endometritis-salpingitis-oophoritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["PID is infection and inflammation of the upper female genital tract, encompassing endometritis, salpingitis, oophoritis, tubo-ovarian abscess, and pelvic peritonitis","It is usually an ascending infection, most commonly caused by Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae, often with anaerobes and other vaginal flora","The clinical picture is variable and often subtle; classic findings are lower abdominal pain, deep dyspareunia, abnormal discharge, and cervical motion, uterine, or adnexal tenderness","Because sequelae are serious and diagnosis is imprecise, a low threshold for empirical treatment in at-risk women is recommended","Antibiotic regimens are broad-spectrum to cover gonococcus, chlamydia, and anaerobes; sexual partners should be evaluated and treated to prevent reinfection","Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome (perihepatitis) causes right upper quadrant pain and can mimic hepatobiliary disease","Intrauterine device users, recent instrumentation, and multiple sexual partners increase risk","Long-term consequences include tubal-factor infertility, ectopic pregnancy, and chronic pelvic pain, and risk rises with each recurrent episode"],"pathophysiology":"PID arises when micro-organisms ascend from the vagina and cervix through the endometrial cavity to the fallopian tubes and adjacent structures. Sexually transmitted pathogens, chiefly Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae, disrupt the cervical barrier and facilitate co-infection with endogenous aerobic and anaerobic vaginal flora. The resulting inflammation damages the tubal epithelium and cilia, leading to scarring, adhesions, and tubal occlusion. Progression can produce pyosalpinx, tubo-ovarian abscess, and pelvic peritonitis; spread to the hepatic capsule causes perihepatitis.","additionalDifferentials":["Ectopic pregnancy","Acute appendicitis","Ovarian cyst accident (rupture, haemorrhage, or torsion)","Endometriosis","Urinary tract infection","Functional or irritable bowel-related pelvic pain"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pregnancy test to exclude ectopic pregnancy","Endocervical or vaginal nucleic acid amplification testing for chlamydia and gonorrhoea","Microscopy and culture of genital tract samples","Inflammatory markers and full blood count","Pelvic ultrasound to detect tubo-ovarian abscess or pelvic collection","Laparoscopy when the diagnosis is uncertain or the patient fails to respond"],"redFlags":["Signs of a tubo-ovarian abscess such as a tender adnexal mass with high fever","Peritonitis or suspicion of a ruptured abscess causing sepsis","Haemodynamic instability or systemic sepsis","Inability to tolerate oral therapy, pregnancy, or failure of outpatient treatment requiring admission","Severe right upper quadrant pain raising concern for perihepatitis or an alternative surgical diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Prompt, adequate antibiotic treatment usually resolves acute infection, but even treated PID carries appreciable risk of long-term tubal damage. Each recurrent episode further increases the likelihood of infertility, ectopic pregnancy, and chronic pelvic pain, so prevention and partner treatment are central to a good outcome.","pitfalls":["Withholding treatment while awaiting definitive confirmation, allowing tubal damage to progress","Missing an ectopic pregnancy by not performing a pregnancy test","Neglecting to test for and treat sexual partners, leading to reinfection","Failing to recognise or drain a tubo-ovarian abscess","Assuming an absence of fever or normal inflammatory markers excludes PID"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"pemphigus_vulgaris":{"name":"Pemphigus vulgaris","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pemphigus","PV"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A potentially life-threatening autoimmune blistering disease that most often begins with painful, non-healing oral erosions before skin involvement appears","Blisters are flaccid and fragile because the split is within the epidermis, so intact bullae are rarely seen and the picture is dominated by raw erosions","Gentle lateral pressure on skin can extend a blister or shear the epidermis (positive Nikolsky sign), reflecting loss of keratinocyte adhesion","Any patient with persistent unexplained mucosal erosions, particularly in the mouth, should raise suspicion for pemphigus","Diagnosis rests on skin biopsy showing suprabasal acantholysis and direct immunofluorescence demonstrating intercellular IgG deposition in a fishnet pattern","Circulating antibodies to desmoglein 3 (with or without desmoglein 1) can be detected serologically and correlate with disease activity","Management relies on systemic immunosuppression, typically corticosteroids with steroid-sparing immunomodulators or B-cell-depleting therapy for extensive disease","Meticulous wound care, pain control, nutritional support, and vigilance for infection are essential given widespread skin barrier loss"],"pathophysiology":"Pemphigus vulgaris is an autoimmune disease in which IgG autoantibodies target desmoglein 3 (and desmoglein 1 in mucocutaneous disease), transmembrane desmosomal cadherins that mediate keratinocyte adhesion. Antibody binding disrupts desmosomal function and triggers intracellular signalling that causes loss of cell-to-cell adhesion (acantholysis). This produces a suprabasal split within the epidermis, giving rise to fragile flaccid blisters and erosions. Because desmoglein 3 is expressed strongly in mucosa, mucosal disease often predominates; involvement of both desmogleins produces combined mucosal and cutaneous lesions. Extensive erosions lead to fluid loss, protein depletion, and susceptibility to secondary infection and sepsis.","additionalDifferentials":["Bullous pemphigoid (tense blisters, subepidermal split, older patients)","Mucous membrane (cicatricial) pemphigoid","Paraneoplastic pemphigus","Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis","Aphthous stomatitis or herpetic gingivostomatitis for oral-only disease","Erosive lichen planus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin or mucosal biopsy for histology showing suprabasal acantholysis","Perilesional biopsy for direct immunofluorescence showing intercellular IgG and C3","Serum indirect immunofluorescence and desmoglein antibody assays to support diagnosis and monitor activity","Screening for underlying malignancy if paraneoplastic pemphigus is suspected","Baseline and monitoring investigations before and during immunosuppressive therapy, including infection screening"],"redFlags":["Extensive skin and mucosal erosion with fluid and protein loss and risk of sepsis","Inability to eat or drink due to painful oropharyngeal involvement","Signs of secondary bacterial infection or systemic instability","Features suggesting paraneoplastic pemphigus, which mandates malignancy evaluation"],"prognosis":"Once frequently fatal, pemphigus vulgaris now has a much better outlook with immunosuppressive and B-cell-depleting therapy; however, it remains a serious chronic disease and much of the residual mortality relates to treatment complications and infection.","pitfalls":["Dismissing chronic oral erosions and delaying diagnosis until skin disease is widespread","Confusing it with bullous pemphigoid and misjudging severity and treatment intensity","Under-appreciating infection risk from extensive barrier loss and immunosuppression","Tapering immunosuppression too rapidly and precipitating relapse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"penetrating_abdominal_trauma":{"name":"Penetrating abdominal trauma","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["penetrating abdominal injury","stab wound abdomen","gunshot wound abdomen"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Manage as a trauma patient using the primary survey, with haemorrhage control as the priority once the airway is secure","A haemodynamically unstable patient or one with peritonitis or evisceration needs immediate surgery rather than further imaging","Gunshot wounds usually breach the peritoneum and injure viscera and generally warrant laparotomy, whereas selected stab wounds may be managed non-operatively with close observation","Do not remove an impaling object in the emergency setting; stabilise it and remove it in a controlled operative environment","The trajectory can be deceptive, so consider injury to the chest, retroperitoneum, and pelvis and inspect the back and flanks for other wounds","Serial abdominal examination detects evolving peritonitis in patients selected for conservative management","Give tetanus prophylaxis and early antibiotics when hollow-viscus injury is likely, and resuscitate with blood products in shock"],"pathophysiology":"A penetrating object breaches the abdominal wall and can lacerate solid organs (liver, spleen), causing haemorrhage, or hollow viscera (bowel, stomach), causing spillage of contents and peritoneal contamination. Vascular injury may produce rapid exsanguination into the peritoneal cavity or retroperitoneum. Low-energy stab wounds injure tissue along a relatively predictable track, whereas high-energy gunshot wounds transfer energy widely, causing cavitation and injury beyond the visible path. The result is a combination of haemorrhagic shock from bleeding and, later, peritonitis and sepsis from contamination.","additionalDifferentials":["Superficial wound that has not breached the peritoneum","Associated thoracic injury from a thoraco-abdominal track","Retroperitoneal vascular or renal injury","Diaphragmatic injury with thoraco-abdominal communication","Concurrent blunt trauma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Focused assessment with sonography for trauma (FAST) to detect free intraperitoneal fluid","CT abdomen and pelvis in the stable patient to define trajectory and organ injury","Local wound exploration in selected stab wounds to assess peritoneal breach","Group and crossmatch with coagulation studies","Erect chest radiograph to detect free air or associated thoracic injury"],"redFlags":["Haemodynamic instability indicating major haemorrhage and the need for immediate laparotomy","Peritonitis, evisceration, or a retained impaling object mandating surgical exploration","Gunshot trajectory crossing the peritoneal cavity","Rising lactate or persistent shock despite resuscitation","Evolving peritoneal signs during observation of a non-operatively managed wound"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the organs injured, blood loss, and time to control of haemorrhage and contamination. Early surgery for unstable or peritonitic patients improves survival, whereas delay allows exsanguination or sepsis with high mortality.","pitfalls":["Removing an impaling object in the emergency department, which can trigger uncontrolled bleeding","Being reassured by a benign initial abdomen when injury can evolve","Underestimating trajectory and missing thoracic, retroperitoneal, or back wounds","Delaying surgery in an unstable patient to obtain imaging","Neglecting antibiotics and tetanus prophylaxis when hollow viscus injury is likely"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"peptic_ulcer_disease_and_related":{"name":"Peptic Ulcer Disease and Related Disorders","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PUD","Duodenal ulcer","Gastric ulcer","Acid peptic disease"],"chapter":335,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2514","clinicalPearls":["A peptic ulcer is a break in the mucosa larger than 5 mm that extends into the submucosa; more than 90% of patients with the classic dyspepsia symptom complex have no ulcer, and many patients with true ulcers are asymptomatic (Harrison 22e p.2514, p.2522).","Burning/gnawing epigastric pain typically occurs 90 minutes to 3 hours after eating in duodenal ulcer and is relieved by food or antacids; pain that wakes the patient between midnight and 3 A.M. is the most discriminating symptom (about two-thirds of DU patients), though it also occurs in roughly one-third with non-ulcer dyspepsia (Harrison 22e p.2522).","In gastric ulcer the pain may instead be precipitated by food, and nausea and weight loss are more common than in duodenal ulcer (Harrison 22e p.2522-2523).","H. pylori and NSAIDs are the two dominant risk factors (US odds ratios about 3.7 and 3.3); idiopathic and non-Hp/non-NSAID ulcers are rising as H. pylori prevalence falls (Harrison 22e p.2518, p.2522).","Gastric ulcers can be malignant and must be biopsied at discovery; duodenal ulcers are almost never malignant (Harrison 22e p.2518).","NSAID toxicity correlates poorly with symptoms: over 80% of patients with serious NSAID-related complications had no preceding dyspepsia, and even 75 mg/day of aspirin can cause serious ulceration, so no NSAID dose is completely safe (Harrison 22e p.2520-2521).","About two-thirds of PUD patients have no abdominal pain, and up to 87% of those with NSAID-induced disease present with a complication without prior symptoms; more than 50% of ulcer hemorrhages occur without warning (Harrison 22e p.2522).","Antral-predominant H. pylori gastritis predisposes to duodenal ulcer, whereas corpus-predominant gastritis predisposes to gastric ulcer, atrophy, and ultimately gastric carcinoma (Harrison 22e p.2519-2520).","Endoscopy is the most sensitive and specific test; it detects an ulcer in fewer than 30% of patients presenting with dyspepsia, allows biopsy to exclude malignancy and detect H. pylori, and barium studies are now rarely used (Harrison 22e p.2523).","A fasting serum gastrin above ten times normal strongly suggests Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, but two-thirds of ZES patients have gastrin levels that overlap with commoner causes; a secretin-stimulated rise of 120 pg/mL or more within 15 minutes is over 90% sensitive and specific (Harrison 22e p.2535-2536).","Suspect gastrinoma with ulcers in unusual locations (second part of duodenum or beyond), refractory or recurrent ulcers, ulcers with complications, or ulcers without H. pylori or NSAID use; about 25% of gastrinomas arise in MEN 1 (Harrison 22e p.2534-2535).","Stress-related mucosal injury (Cushing's ulcer after head injury, Curling's ulcer after burns) histologically lacks inflammation and H. pylori, so 'gastritis' is a misnomer; bleeding typically appears 48-72 h after the insult with mechanical ventilation and coagulopathy as key risk factors (Harrison 22e p.2536)."],"pathophysiology":"Ulcers result from an imbalance between mucosal aggressive factors (gastric acid secreted by parietal-cell H+,K+-ATPase, pepsin from chief cells) and mucosal defense/repair (a preepithelial mucus-bicarbonate-phospholipid layer, epithelial restitution and tight junctions, and a subepithelial microvascular bed), with prostaglandins, nitric oxide, and mucosal blood flow central to protection. H. pylori, a gram-negative microaerophilic urease-producing rod, colonizes gastric mucus, causes chronic active gastritis in nearly all infected individuals, and produces frank ulceration in only 10-15%; virulence factors include urease (generating ammonia), CagA delivered by a type IV secretion system from cag-PAI-positive strains, VacA, and adhesins. Antral-predominant gastritis raises acid and promotes duodenal ulcer, whereas corpus-predominant pangastritis lowers acid and favors gastric ulcer and carcinoma. NSAIDs injure mucosa both systemically (COX-1 inhibition depletes protective prostaglandins) and topically (weak acids that become trapped and ionized intracellularly), with neutrophil adherence to the gastric microcirculation initiating injury; H. pylori and NSAIDs act as independent and synergistic risk factors. In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, autonomous gastrin secretion from a neuroendocrine tumor drives acid hypersecretion, parietal-cell hyperplasia, ulceration, esophagitis, and diarrhea (Harrison 22e p.2514-2521, p.2534).","additionalDifferentials":["Functional (non-ulcer/essential) dyspepsia, including postprandial distress syndrome and epigastric pain syndrome (commonest cause of upper abdominal discomfort)","Gastroesophageal reflux disease","Proximal gastrointestinal tumors / gastric malignancy","Pancreaticobiliary disease (biliary colic, chronic pancreatitis)","Gastroduodenal Crohn's disease","Mesenteric vascular disease","Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (gastrinoma)"],"infectionMimics":["Cytomegalovirus gastritis/ulceration (immunocompromised hosts)","Herpes simplex virus gastritis/ulceration","Helicobacter heilmannii infection","Phlegmonous (bacterial) gastritis","Infectious causes of large gastric folds mimicking Menetrier's disease (CMV, histoplasmosis, syphilis, tuberculosis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Functional dyspepsia","Drug/toxin-induced ulceration (bisphosphonates, clopidogrel, glucocorticoids combined with NSAIDs, potassium chloride, mycophenolate, chemotherapy, immune checkpoint inhibitors, crack cocaine)","Crohn's disease and eosinophilic gastroenteritis","Sarcoidosis and other infiltrative disease","Ischemia and radiation injury","Menetrier's disease and gastric polyposis presenting as large gastric folds","Idiopathic hypersecretory state"],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper endoscopy with biopsy (most sensitive/specific; biopsy gastric ulcers to exclude malignancy)","H. pylori testing: rapid urease biopsy test, histology, culture (allows susceptibility testing)","Noninvasive H. pylori tests: 13C-urea breath test and stool monoclonal antigen test (both >95% sensitivity/specificity); serology cannot distinguish active from prior infection","Fasting serum gastrin (two occasions) when ZES is suspected","Secretin stimulation test (rise >=120 pg/mL at 15 min) and gastric pH/acid analysis for gastrinoma","Serum calcium, parathyroid hormone, prolactin, and pancreatic polypeptide to screen for MEN 1 in gastrinoma patients","Tumor localization imaging and somatostatin-receptor-based studies for gastrinoma","Screening blood/urine for surreptitious aspirin/NSAID use in refractory H. pylori-negative ulcers"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe generalized abdominal pain with a board-like, rigid abdomen suggesting perforation","Tarry stools or coffee-ground emesis indicating gastrointestinal bleeding; tachycardia/orthostasis indicating hypovolemia","Persistent pain no longer relieved by food/antacids and radiating to the back suggesting posterior penetration (pancreas)","Early satiety, vomiting of undigested food, and a succussion splash suggesting gastric outlet obstruction","Weight loss, anorexia, or new dyspepsia at age 60 or older warranting endoscopy before empiric therapy","Ulcers in unusual locations, refractory/recurrent ulcers, or multiple ulcers prompting evaluation for gastrinoma"],"prognosis":"Eradication of H. pylori reduces duodenal ulcer recurrence by more than 80%, and improving sanitation has steadily lowered ulcer incidence, surgery rates, and physician visits over the past decades. Complications still carry meaningful mortality: GI bleeding (the commonest, ~15% of patients) has a 30-day mortality of about 2.5-10% and is more frequent in those over 60; perforation occurs in about 6-7% of patients with 30-day mortality over 20%; gastric outlet obstruction is the least common (1-2%). Approximately 15,000 deaths per year in the US are attributed to complicated PUD. For gastrinoma, overall 5- and 10-year survival is about 62-75% and 47-53%; complete resection or negative laparotomy yields survival over 90%, while hepatic metastasis drops 5-year survival below 20% (Harrison 22e p.2514, p.2516, p.2522-2523, p.2536).","pitfalls":["Treating dyspepsia as PUD: abdominal pain has poor predictive value and most dyspeptic patients have no ulcer, so empiric assumptions can miss alternative diagnoses.","Relying on symptoms to gauge NSAID risk: serious complications usually occur without preceding dyspepsia.","Failing to biopsy a gastric ulcer to exclude malignancy.","Misinterpreting an elevated fasting gastrin: PPIs, hypochlorhydria/achlorhydria, pernicious anemia, H. pylori, renal insufficiency, retained antrum, and G-cell hyperplasia all raise gastrin, and PPIs must be stopped (about 7 days, bridged with an H2 antagonist) before ZES testing to avoid false positives.","Assuming serology proves active H. pylori infection, when it cannot distinguish current from prior infection and is unreliable for follow-up.","Abruptly stopping PPIs in gastrinoma patients, which can precipitate severe acid-related complications.","Calling stress-related mucosal injury 'gastritis' despite the absence of inflammation, or labeling endoscopic erythema as gastritis without histology."],"severityClassification":"PUD severity is framed clinically by the presence and type of complication: uncomplicated ulcer versus bleeding (commonest, ~15%, 30-day mortality ~2.5-10%), perforation/penetration (~6-7%, 30-day mortality >20%; DU penetrates posteriorly into pancreas, GU into left hepatic lobe), and gastric outlet obstruction (1-2%, may be inflammatory/reversible or fixed scar). For gastrinoma, prognosis is stratified by resectability and metastatic burden rather than a formal stage in this chapter (Harrison 22e p.2522-2523, p.2536).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2514","Harrison 22e p.2515","Harrison 22e p.2516","Harrison 22e p.2517","Harrison 22e p.2518","Harrison 22e p.2519","Harrison 22e p.2520","Harrison 22e p.2521","Harrison 22e p.2522","Harrison 22e p.2523","Harrison 22e p.2534","Harrison 22e p.2535","Harrison 22e p.2536","Harrison 22e p.2539"],"crossLinks":[]},"peptic_ulcer_perforation":{"name":"Perforated peptic ulcer","system":"GI / Upper GI / Surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["peptic ulcer perforation","perforated duodenal ulcer","perforated gastric ulcer"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classically presents with sudden, severe, generalised abdominal pain in a patient who may have a history of dyspepsia or NSAID use","Examination shows a rigid, board-like abdomen with generalised tenderness, guarding, and absent bowel sounds from peritonitis","Perforation evolves through phases: initial chemical peritonitis from gastric acid, a deceptive period of apparent improvement, and then bacterial peritonitis with sepsis","An erect chest radiograph often shows free air under the diaphragm, but its absence does not exclude perforation; CT is more sensitive","Key risk factors are Helicobacter pylori infection, NSAID and aspirin use, smoking, and physiological stress","Initial management is aggressive resuscitation, analgesia, nasogastric decompression, intravenous proton pump inhibition, and broad-spectrum antibiotics while preparing for surgery","The standard operation is closure of the perforation, commonly with an omental (Graham) patch, and thorough peritoneal lavage; large or malignant gastric perforations may require resection","Selected patients with a sealed perforation and no generalised peritonitis may be managed non-operatively with close monitoring"],"pathophysiology":"Perforation occurs when a peptic ulcer erodes through the full thickness of the gastric or duodenal wall, allowing gastric and duodenal contents to spill into the peritoneal cavity. Mucosal breakdown is driven by an imbalance between aggressive factors (acid, pepsin, Helicobacter pylori, NSAIDs) and mucosal defence. Escaped acidic contents first cause a sterile chemical peritonitis, followed within hours by bacterial contamination and generalised bacterial peritonitis. The resulting inflammatory cascade produces fluid sequestration, sepsis, and, if untreated, septic shock and multi-organ failure. Duodenal and pyloric ulcers most often perforate anteriorly.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute pancreatitis","Acute cholecystitis or biliary perforation","Perforation elsewhere in the GI tract (colonic, appendiceal)","Ruptured or leaking abdominal aortic aneurysm","Myocardial infarction (inferior)","Mesenteric ischaemia","Ruptured ectopic pregnancy or ovarian pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Erect chest radiograph to look for free subdiaphragmatic gas","CT abdomen and pelvis as the most sensitive test for free air and to localise the perforation","Bloods including full blood count, urea and electrolytes, amylase or lipase, CRP, and lactate","Arterial blood gas and group and save or crossmatch for resuscitation and theatre","Helicobacter pylori testing and, later, endoscopy to confirm ulcer healing and exclude malignancy in gastric ulcers"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe pain with a rigid abdomen and generalised peritonitis (surgical emergency)","Free intraperitoneal gas on imaging","Sepsis, haemodynamic instability, or rising lactate indicating septic shock","Deterioration after a deceptive symptom-free interval signalling established bacterial peritonitis"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on age, comorbidity, size of perforation, and, critically, the delay before surgery; early intervention gives good recovery, whereas delayed presentation with established peritonitis and sepsis carries high morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Excluding perforation on the basis of a normal erect chest radiograph alone","Being reassured by the transient improvement of the deceptive second phase","Delaying resuscitation and surgery in a septic patient","Failing to biopsy or follow up a perforated gastric ulcer to exclude malignancy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"periapical_abscess":{"name":"Periapical abscess","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dentoalveolar abscess","apical abscess","acute periapical abscess","dental abscess"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A periapical abscess is a localised collection of pus at the root apex arising from a necrotic, infected pulp","Classic features are severe, well-localised pain, tenderness to percussion, and a feeling that the tooth is extruded or high on biting","The affected tooth is typically non-vital, so pulp sensibility testing is negative while percussion and palpation are markedly positive","Pus may drain spontaneously through a sinus tract (gum boil), which can relieve pain and produce a chronic, less symptomatic picture","Early radiographs may look normal because bone changes lag behind, whereas chronic lesions show a periapical radiolucency","Primary management is drainage and elimination of the source through root canal treatment or extraction; antibiotics are adjuncts reserved for spreading infection or systemic involvement","Infection can track along fascial planes, so the pattern of swelling depends on tooth position relative to muscle attachments","Recurrent or persistent sinus tracts indicate ongoing infection needing definitive endodontic or surgical treatment"],"pathophysiology":"A periapical abscess develops when pulp necrosis, usually secondary to caries or trauma, allows bacteria and their toxins to egress through the apical foramen into the periapical tissues. The host inflammatory response produces a purulent collection localised to the periradicular bone. Rising pressure causes intense pain and may resorb surrounding bone, seen later as a radiolucency. Pus follows the path of least resistance, perforating cortical bone and tracking into soft tissue, where the relationship of the perforation to muscle attachments determines whether a localised vestibular swelling, a draining sinus, or a spreading fascial-space infection results.","additionalDifferentials":["Periodontal abscess arising from the gingival sulcus","Irreversible pulpitis without abscess formation","Periapical granuloma or radicular cyst","Osteomyelitis of the jaw","Odontogenic infection of adjacent teeth or sinus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pulp sensibility testing (expected non-vital response)","Percussion, palpation, and mobility assessment","Periapical radiography and, when needed, cone-beam CT for extent","Assessment for fascial-space spread and airway compromise in swelling","Culture and systemic markers if severe or spreading infection"],"redFlags":["Rapidly spreading swelling of the floor of mouth or submandibular region raising concern for Ludwig angina and airway obstruction","Trismus, difficulty swallowing, drooling, or voice change indicating deep-space infection","Systemic sepsis, high fever, or periorbital/cavernous sinus spread from upper anterior teeth","Immunocompromised or diabetic patients in whom infection can progress dangerously fast"],"prognosis":"With timely drainage and source control the prognosis is excellent, but delayed treatment risks spread to fascial spaces, osteomyelitis, airway compromise, or life-threatening sepsis.","pitfalls":["Prescribing antibiotics without draining the abscess or removing the source","Being reassured by a normal early radiograph despite clear clinical signs","Missing progression to a deep-space or airway-threatening infection","Confusing a periodontal abscess with a periapical abscess and mistreating the cause"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"periarticular_disorders_of_the_extremities":{"name":"Periarticular Disorders of the Extremities","system":"Musculoskeletal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Bursitis","Rotator cuff tendinitis","Impingement syndrome","Frozen shoulder","Adhesive capsulitis","Tennis elbow","Golfer's elbow","De Quervain's tenosynovitis","Plantar fasciitis","Iliotibial band syndrome","Calcific tendinitis"],"chapter":387,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2973","clinicalPearls":["Periarticular disorders are common soft-tissue musculoskeletal problems affecting people of any age, arising from structures around (rather than within) the joint such as bursae, tendons, fascia, and connective-tissue bands.","Bursitis is inflammation of a synovial-lined sac; adults have over 150 bursae that ease gliding of tendons and muscles over bony prominences, and overuse, trauma, systemic disease (rheumatoid arthritis, gout), or infection can trigger inflammation.","Subacromial (subdeltoid) bursitis is the most common bursitis and frequently coexists with rotator cuff tendinitis, presenting with anterolateral shoulder pain.","Acutely inflamed olecranon or prepatellar bursitis with warmth/redness warrants aspiration with Gram stain, culture, and crystal examination to exclude infection or gout before assuming a mechanical cause.","Rotator cuff tendinitis (impingement syndrome) is the leading cause of a painful shoulder; the supraspinatus tendon is most often affected due to impingement between the humeral head and the anterior acromion/coracoacromial ligament, classically producing a painful abduction arc of 60-120 degrees.","Calcific tendinitis reflects hydroxyapatite (calcium salt) deposition within a tendon, most often the supraspinatus (involved in about 63% of cases), typically after age 40, and may be asymptomatic or cause sudden severe shoulder pain.","De Quervain's tenosynovitis involves the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis at the radial styloid; a positive Finkelstein test (thumb tucked in fist with ulnar wrist deviation) reproduces radial styloid pain.","Adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder) causes painful, globally restricted active and passive shoulder motion, usually without intrinsic joint disease, and is associated with diabetes, thyroid disease, chronic pulmonary disease, and prior myocardial infarction.","Lateral epicondylitis (tennis elbow) arises from repetitive resisted wrist extension/supination; most patients are injured by activities other than tennis such as pulling weeds, carrying briefcases, or using a screwdriver.","Plantar fasciitis classically causes severe heel pain with the first steps in the morning or after rest, easing with initial activity then worsening with prolonged weight-bearing; tenderness localizes to the medial calcaneal attachment of the fascia.","Drug-induced tendinopathy is increasingly recognized, with quinolones, glucocorticoids, aromatase inhibitors, and statins implicated; the Achilles tendon is most often affected and rupture can be the presenting event.","Yergason's test (pain on resisted forearm supination with the elbow at 90 degrees) supports bicipital tendinitis; biceps tendon rupture in older patients may be painless and recognized by a retracted bulge of the long head."],"pathophysiology":"These are disorders of the soft tissues surrounding joints rather than the synovial joint itself. Bursitis is synovial inflammation of a bursa from frictional overuse, trauma, systemic inflammatory/crystal disease, or infection. Tendinopathies (rotator cuff, bicipital, patellar, epicondylar, De Quervain's) result from mechanical impingement and repetitive resisted loading producing microtears, inflammation, and degeneration at tendon insertions; the supraspinatus is uniquely vulnerable to impingement beneath the acromion. Calcific tendinitis is caused by hydroxyapatite deposition within tendon substance through poorly understood phases of calcification that can incite acute inflammation. Adhesive capsulitis features a thickened shoulder capsule with mild chronic inflammatory infiltrate and fibrosis, often after a period of immobility. Plantar fasciitis arises at the fascial origin on the medial calcaneal tuberosity from cumulative loading. Drug-induced tendinopathies have an unknown mechanism but are linked to specific drug classes.","additionalDifferentials":["Glenohumeral osteoarthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis","Gout and crystal arthropathy","Spondyloarthritis","Cervical radiculopathy referring to the shoulder","Acromioclavicular joint pathology","Intraarticular knee pathology (osteoarthritis, meniscal injury) versus periarticular knee pain"],"infectionMimics":["Septic bursitis (e.g., olecranon or prepatellar)","Infectious tenosynovitis","Septic arthritis presenting as periarticular pain and swelling"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Gouty bursitis/tendinitis (urate deposition)","Calcium pyrophosphate or apatite crystal deposition","Rheumatoid or inflammatory arthritis involving periarticular structures","Referred pain from intraarticular degenerative joint disease"],"additionalInvestigations":["History and directed physical examination (often sufficient for diagnosis)","Provocative maneuvers (painful abduction arc, drop-arm test, Finkelstein test, Yergason's test)","Bursal aspiration with Gram stain, culture, and crystal analysis when infection or gout is suspected","Ultrasound (for bursitis, tendinitis, rotator cuff tears, calcific deposits, plantar fascia thickening, and guided injection)","Plain radiographs (may show tendon calcification or incidental heel spurs)","MRI (sensitive for rotator cuff tears, tendon integrity, and plantar fasciitis when diagnosis is unclear)"],"redFlags":["Acutely warm, red, tender bursa suggesting septic bursitis or gout requiring aspiration","Weakness of abduction/external rotation or a positive drop-arm test suggesting a rotator cuff tear","Acute tendon rupture (e.g., Achilles in drug-induced tendinopathy, biceps in active patients)","Globally restricted active and passive shoulder motion suggesting adhesive capsulitis rather than a focal tendinitis","Periarticular pain in the setting of systemic disease (rheumatoid arthritis, diabetes, thyroid disease) prompting evaluation of the underlying condition"],"prognosis":"Most periarticular disorders are self-limited and respond to conservative measures. Adhesive capsulitis usually improves spontaneously over 1-3 years, though many patients retain some residual loss of shoulder motion. More than 80% of plantar fasciitis cases resolve within 12 months. Calcific tendinitis is largely self-limited. Rotator cuff tears vary in outcome depending on tear size, chronicity, pain, functional loss, and coexisting osteoarthritis.","pitfalls":["Anserine bursitis is a frequently overlooked periarticular cause of knee pain.","Assuming inflamed olecranon or prepatellar bursitis is mechanical without excluding infection or gout by aspiration.","Mislabeling globally restricted shoulder motion as simple tendinitis when it is adhesive capsulitis.","Attributing heel pain to a radiographic heel spur, which is of little diagnostic significance in plantar fasciitis.","Overlooking medication as a cause of tendon pain or rupture (drug-induced tendinopathy), and reintroducing the offending agent.","Confusing iliopsoas bursitis pain with pain originating within the hip joint itself."],"severityClassification":"No formal grading system; rotator cuff tears are clinically characterized as acute, subacute, or chronic and as partial- or full-thickness, with management guided by tear severity, symptom duration, pain, functional loss, and coexisting osteoarthritis.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2973","Harrison 22e p.2974","Harrison 22e p.2975"],"crossLinks":[]},"pericardial_disease":{"name":"Pericardial Disease","system":"Cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["constrictive pericarditis","pericardial effusion"],"chapter":281,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2078-2084","clinicalPearls":["Acute pericarditis has four principal diagnostic features: chest pain, a pericardial friction rub, characteristic ECG changes, and pericardial effusion (Harrison 22e p.2078).","Pericarditic chest pain is typically retrosternal/left precordial, often pleuritic, may radiate to the trapezius ridge, and is characteristically intensified by lying supine and relieved by sitting up and leaning forward (Harrison 22e p.2079).","A pericardial friction rub is audible at some point in approximately 85% of patients with acute pericarditis; it may have up to three components per cycle and is best heard at end expiration with the patient upright and leaning forward (Harrison 22e p.2079).","The ECG of acute pericarditis classically evolves through four stages: stage 1 widespread upwardly-concave ST elevation with PR-segment depression, stage 2 normalization of ST, stage 3 T-wave inversion, and stage 4 return to normal (Harrison 22e p.2079).","Beck's triad of cardiac tamponade is hypotension, soft or absent heart sounds, and jugular venous distention with a prominent x descent but an absent y descent (Harrison 22e p.2081).","Pulsus paradoxus (inspiratory fall in systolic pressure >10 mmHg) is an important clue to tamponade, but also occurs in about one-third of constrictive pericarditis cases and in hypovolemic shock, obstructive airway disease, and pulmonary embolism (Harrison 22e p.2081-2082).","In constrictive pericarditis the rigid thickened pericardium limits ventricular filling only after early diastole (unimpeded in early diastole, then abruptly reduced), whereas in tamponade filling is impeded throughout diastole (Harrison 22e p.2083).","Kussmaul's sign (failure of venous pressure to decline, or a rise, during inspiration) is common in chronic constrictive pericarditis but is also seen in tricuspid stenosis, right ventricular infarction, and restrictive cardiomyopathy; it is negative in cor pulmonale (Harrison 22e p.2083-2084).","A pericardial knock, an early third heart sound at the apex from abrupt cessation of ventricular filling, is often conspicuous in constrictive pericarditis (Harrison 22e p.2083).","Constrictive pericarditis produces an M-shaped atrial pressure contour with prominent x and y descents; the y descent (diminished/absent in tamponade) is the most prominent deflection, and the ventricular pressure pulse shows a 'square root' sign in diastole (Harrison 22e p.2083).","In developing nations a high percentage of chronic constrictive pericarditis is tuberculous, but TB is now an uncommon cause in North America and Western Europe; other causes include prior viral/idiopathic pericarditis, cardiac surgery, mediastinal irradiation, and neoplasm (Harrison 22e p.2083)."],"pathophysiology":"The pericardium is a double-layered sac (visceral and parietal layers) separated by 15-50 mL of plasma ultrafiltrate; it restrains sudden cardiac chamber dilation and fixes cardiac position. In acute pericarditis, inflammation produces fibrinous or effusive disease. Cardiac tamponade occurs when fluid accumulates in the pericardial space in quantity sufficient to obstruct ventricular inflow, raising intrapericardial pressure and limiting diastolic filling throughout diastole, reducing stroke volume, cardiac output, and arterial pressure. As little as 200 mL can cause tamponade when fluid develops rapidly, versus >2000 mL when it accumulates slowly and the pericardium stretches. In chronic constrictive pericarditis, healing of fibrinous/serofibrinous pericarditis or resorption of effusion produces granulation tissue that contracts into a rigid, sometimes calcified scar encasing the heart; ventricular filling is unimpeded early in diastole then abruptly halts at the pericardium's elastic limit. End-diastolic pressures in both ventricles and mean atrial, pulmonary venous, and systemic venous pressures equalize within ~5 mmHg, while resting systolic function is typically normal or only mildly impaired (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2081, p.2083).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute myocardial infarction (common confusion with acute pericarditis; in pericarditis ST elevations are upwardly concave, biomarker elevations are modest, and pain may precede or coincide with fever) (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2083)","Restrictive cardiomyopathy (shares restriction of ventricular filling; must be distinguished from constrictive pericarditis because the latter is surgically correctable) (Harrison 22e p.2084)","Right ventricular infarction (may mimic tamponade with hypotension, elevated JVP, slow y descent, occasional paradoxical pulse) (Harrison 22e p.2082)","Cor pulmonale (systemic venous hypertension and paradoxical pulse, but Kussmaul's sign is negative and advanced parenchymal lung disease is apparent) (Harrison 22e p.2084)","Tricuspid stenosis (congestive hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, ascites, venous distention, but characteristic murmur present) (Harrison 22e p.2084)","Hepatic cirrhosis (constrictive pericarditis with hepatomegaly, jaundice, and intractable ascites may be mistaken for cirrhosis; inspection of distended neck veins avoids the error) (Harrison 22e p.2084)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral pericarditis (coxsackievirus A and B, echovirus, influenza, mumps, herpes simplex, varicella-zoster, adenovirus, cytomegalovirus, HIV, post-acute COVID, mpox) (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2082)","Tuberculous pericarditis (effusion, constriction, or effusive-constrictive disease; suggested by high adenosine deaminase or M. tuberculosis DNA by PCR, often requiring pericardial tissue) (Harrison 22e p.2082, p.2084)","Pyogenic/purulent bacterial pericarditis (pneumococcus, Streptococcus, Staphylococcus, Neisseria, Legionella, Chlamydia; from cardiothoracic surgery, lung/pleural extension, esophageal rupture, or valve-ring abscess; fever, chills, septicemia, poor prognosis) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Fungal pericarditis (histoplasmosis, coccidioidomycosis, blastomycosis, aspergillosis, candidiasis) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Parasitic/protozoal and syphilitic pericarditis (amebiasis, toxoplasmosis, echinococcosis, trichinosis) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Uremic and dialysis-associated pericarditis (renal failure; fibrinous, serosanguinous effusions, friction rub common but pain usually mild) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Neoplastic pericarditis (lung and breast carcinoma, melanoma, lymphoma, leukemia; metastatic invasion, primary tumors, mesothelioma) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Postcardiac injury syndrome (postpericardiotomy, posttraumatic, post-MI Dressler's syndrome; hypersensitivity/autoimmune reaction to myocardial antigens 1-4 weeks after injury) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2082)","Collagen vascular / autoimmune disease (SLE, rheumatoid arthritis, scleroderma, ankylosing spondylitis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, IgG4 disease, rheumatic fever, polyarteritis nodosa) (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Drug-induced pericarditis (procainamide, hydralazine, phenytoin, isoniazid, minoxidil, anticoagulants, methysergide) (Harrison 22e p.2078)","Postirradiation (mediastinal radiation) pericarditis, acute or chronic constrictive (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2083)","Aortic dissection with leakage into the pericardial sac (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2081)","Acute myocardial infarction and metabolic causes (myxedema, cholesterol pericarditis) (Harrison 22e p.2078)","Familial Mediterranean fever and other autoinflammatory syndromes (Harrison 22e p.2078)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography: most widely used, sensitive, specific, bedside-capable; localizes and estimates pericardial fluid and shows late-diastolic collapse of the RV free wall and right atrium in tamponade (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2082)","Doppler echocardiography: in tamponade and constriction, inspiratory increase in tricuspid/pulmonic flow velocities with decrease in pulmonary venous/mitral/aortic velocities and a leftward septal shift (respirophasic variation) (Harrison 22e p.2082, p.2084)","CT and MRI: superior for loculated effusions, pericardial thickening, pericardial masses, calcification, and detecting pericardial inflammation; more accurate than echocardiography for confirming or excluding constriction (MRI assesses myocardial involvement) (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2084)","ECG: stage 1-4 evolution in acute pericarditis; low QRS voltage and electrical alternans with large effusion/tamponade; low voltage and diffuse T-wave changes with atrial fibrillation in constriction (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2081, p.2083)","Chest radiograph: enlarged cardiac silhouette with a 'water bottle' configuration in large effusion; pericardial calcification (most common in tuberculous constriction) (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2084)","Pericardiocentesis with fluid analysis: cell counts, cytology for neoplastic cells, cultures, M. tuberculosis PCR and adenosine deaminase (Harrison 22e p.2082)","Pericardial biopsy (e.g., via limited thoracotomy) when fluid analysis is non-diagnostic, especially for suspected tuberculous or neoplastic disease (Harrison 22e p.2082, p.2084)","Laboratory: elevated C-reactive protein and white blood cell count in viral/idiopathic pericarditis (Harrison 22e p.2082)"],"redFlags":["Cardiac tamponade: hypotension, elevated jugular venous pressure, and unexplained sudden enlargement of the cardiac silhouette; may be rapidly fatal if not recognized and treated promptly (Harrison 22e p.2081)","Reduced QRS amplitude with electrical alternans of P, QRS, or T waves raises suspicion of tamponade (Harrison 22e p.2081)","Predictors of poor prognosis warranting hospitalization in acute pericarditis: fever >38C, subacute onset, large pericardial effusion, immunosuppression, or evidence of tamponade (Harrison 22e p.2080)","Purulent (pyogenic) pericarditis carries a poor prognosis and requires immediate drainage and antibiotics (Harrison 22e p.2083)","Without treatment, tuberculous pericardial disease can progress to constriction; constriction should be treated surgically (Harrison 22e p.2084)"],"prognosis":"Viral or idiopathic acute pericarditis usually runs its course in a few days to 4 weeks; recurrent (relapsing) pericarditis occurs in approximately one-fourth of patients, and both tamponade and constriction are possible but infrequent complications. Most patients with acute pericarditis can be managed as outpatients with careful follow-up. Untreated cardiac tamponade may be rapidly fatal. Purulent pericarditis generally has a poor prognosis. Without treatment, tuberculous pericardial disease may progress to constriction. Pericardial resection is the only definitive treatment of constrictive pericarditis, with benefits accruing progressively over months; operative mortality is in the range of 5-10% even at experienced centers, highest in the most severe disease, especially radiation-induced (Harrison 22e p.2080, p.2082, p.2083, p.2084).","pitfalls":["Distinguishing constrictive pericarditis from restrictive cardiomyopathy is critical because constriction is surgically correctable; their hemodynamics overlap (square-root sign and equalized diastolic pressures occur in both) and echocardiography alone cannot definitively establish or exclude constriction, so MRI/CT is needed (Harrison 22e p.2083, p.2084).","Acute pericarditis is commonly mistaken for AMI and vice versa; in pericarditis the ST elevations are upwardly concave, reciprocal depression is limited to aVR/V1, and biomarker rises (from epi-myocarditis) are modest relative to AMI (Harrison 22e p.2079, p.2083).","Pericardial calcification may occur without constriction, and constriction may occur without calcification, so calcification neither confirms nor excludes the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2084).","Acute idiopathic pericarditis is a diagnosis of exclusion with no specific test; all other causes of fibrinous pericarditis must be considered (Harrison 22e p.2082-2083).","Constrictive pericarditis with hepatomegaly, jaundice, and intractable ascites can be misdiagnosed as hepatic cirrhosis unless the distended neck veins with characteristic waveform are recognized (Harrison 22e p.2084).","Pain is often absent in slowly developing tuberculous, postirradiation, neoplastic, and uremic pericarditis, so its absence does not exclude pericardial disease (Harrison 22e p.2079).","Anticoagulants should be avoided in acute pericarditis because they could cause bleeding into the pericardial cavity and tamponade (Harrison 22e p.2080)."],"severityClassification":"Clinical classification: I. Acute pericarditis (<6 weeks) - A. Fibrinous, B. Effusive (serous or sanguineous); II. Subacute pericarditis (6 weeks to 6 months) - A. Effusive-constrictive, B. Constrictive; III. Chronic pericarditis (>6 months) - A. Constrictive, B. Adhesive (nonconstrictive). Etiologic classification: infectious, noninfectious, and presumed autoimmune. Table 281-2 distinguishes tamponade, constrictive pericarditis, restrictive cardiomyopathy, right ventricular myocardial infarction, and effusive-constrictive pericarditis by clinical, ECG, echocardiographic, and CT/MRI features (Harrison 22e p.2078, p.2080).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2078-2084","Harrison 22e Chapter 281, Pericardial Disease"],"crossLinks":[]},"perinatal_hie":{"name":"Perinatal Hypoxic-Ischaemic Encephalopathy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["HIE","hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy","birth asphyxia encephalopathy","neonatal encephalopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Brain injury in the newborn resulting from a perinatal event that reduces cerebral oxygen delivery and blood flow, producing an evolving encephalopathy.","It is a leading cause of neonatal mortality and long-term neurodevelopmental disability, including cerebral palsy.","Severity is graded clinically using the Sarnat stages (mild, moderate, severe) based on level of consciousness, tone, reflexes, autonomic function, and seizures.","Supportive evidence of a hypoxic-ischaemic insult includes fetal distress, low Apgar scores, the need for resuscitation, and a low umbilical cord arterial pH with metabolic acidosis.","Therapeutic hypothermia, started within the first hours of life for eligible term and near-term infants with moderate to severe encephalopathy, reduces death and disability.","Seizures are common and may be subtle; EEG or amplitude-integrated EEG assists detection and monitoring.","The injury frequently involves other organs, causing renal, cardiac, hepatic, and haematological dysfunction that also require support.","MRI in the first week helps define the pattern and extent of injury and informs prognosis.","Important mimics, including infection, metabolic disorders, and stroke, should be considered rather than assuming every encephalopathy is purely hypoxic-ischaemic."],"pathophysiology":"A hypoxic-ischaemic insult around the time of birth causes an initial phase of energy failure with cellular depolarisation, excitotoxic glutamate release, and calcium influx. After partial recovery of cerebral metabolism, a delayed (secondary) phase of energy failure ensues over hours to days, driven by mitochondrial dysfunction, oxidative stress, inflammation, and apoptotic and necrotic cell death. This latent window is the rationale for early therapeutic hypothermia, which slows the destructive secondary cascade.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal sepsis or meningitis","Inborn errors of metabolism","Neonatal (arterial or venous) stroke","Intracranial haemorrhage","Neonatal seizures from other causes (e.g. electrolyte disturbance)","Congenital brain malformation or genetic encephalopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Umbilical cord and early postnatal blood gas for acidosis and base deficit","EEG or amplitude-integrated EEG for seizure detection and background assessment","MRI of the brain (usually within the first week) for injury pattern and prognosis","Assessment of multi-organ function (renal, hepatic, cardiac, coagulation, glucose, electrolytes)","Evaluation to exclude infection and metabolic causes"],"redFlags":["Deep coma, absent brainstem reflexes, or flaccid tone","Refractory or clinically evident status epilepticus","Multi-organ failure","Severely abnormal EEG background or extensive injury on MRI","Persistent need for cardiorespiratory support"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on severity: mild encephalopathy usually recovers well, moderate carries a variable risk of impairment, and severe disease is associated with high mortality and a high rate of cerebral palsy, epilepsy, and cognitive and sensory disability; therapeutic hypothermia improves outcomes in eligible infants.","pitfalls":["Assuming all neonatal encephalopathy is hypoxic-ischaemic and missing infection, metabolic disease, or stroke","Missing subtle seizures without EEG monitoring","Delaying assessment for and initiation of therapeutic hypothermia within the treatment window","Allowing hyperthermia, which worsens injury","Overlooking coexisting multi-organ dysfunction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"perinatal_hiv":{"name":"Perinatal HIV Infection","system":"Immunological","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["mother-to-child HIV transmission","vertical HIV transmission","paediatric HIV"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most childhood HIV infection is acquired from the mother during pregnancy, around delivery, or through breastfeeding, and transmission is largely preventable with effective interventions.","Maternal antiretroviral therapy that suppresses viral load, appropriate mode of delivery, infant antiretroviral prophylaxis, and safe infant feeding practices dramatically reduce vertical transmission.","Infected infants are frequently asymptomatic at birth, so diagnosis relies on virological (nucleic acid) testing rather than antibody tests, because maternal antibodies cross the placenta and persist for months.","Untreated perinatal infection can progress rapidly in infancy, with failure to thrive, recurrent or severe infections, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, and developmental regression.","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia is a serious early opportunistic infection, so prophylaxis is important in at-risk and infected infants.","Early initiation of antiretroviral therapy in infected infants improves survival and outcomes and is recommended as soon as diagnosis is confirmed.","A definitive diagnosis of infection or its exclusion requires a defined schedule of repeat virological testing over the first months of life.","Comprehensive care includes immunisations adapted to immune status, growth and developmental monitoring, and support for the whole family."],"pathophysiology":"HIV crosses to the infant transplacentally, through exposure to maternal blood and genital secretions during delivery, or via breast milk. The virus infects CD4 T lymphocytes and other immune cells, integrating into host DNA and causing progressive depletion and dysfunction of CD4 cells. In infants the immune system is immature and the initial viral burden relative to immune capacity is high, so without treatment immunodeficiency and disease progression can occur faster than in adults, predisposing to opportunistic and recurrent infections and end-organ involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Other congenital infections (e.g., cytomegalovirus, syphilis)","Primary immunodeficiency disorders","Failure to thrive of other cause","Recurrent infections from other immune defects","Tuberculosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["HIV nucleic acid (virological) testing on the recommended schedule","CD4 count and percentage","HIV viral load","Screening for co-infections (tuberculosis, hepatitis B and C, syphilis)","Full blood count and growth and developmental assessment"],"redFlags":["Failure to thrive or growth faltering","Recurrent, severe, or unusual infections","Signs of Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia or other opportunistic infection","Developmental regression or encephalopathy","Persistent hepatosplenomegaly or generalised lymphadenopathy"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis, prompt antiretroviral therapy, and prophylaxis against opportunistic infections, children can achieve viral suppression, immune recovery, and near-normal life expectancy; untreated perinatal infection carries high morbidity and mortality in early childhood.","pitfalls":["Using antibody testing rather than virological testing to diagnose infection in young infants","Interpreting a single negative early test as excluding infection without completing the testing schedule","Delaying antiretroviral therapy or Pneumocystis prophylaxis in an at-risk or infected infant","Overlooking co-infections such as tuberculosis or hepatitis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"periodic_fever_pfapa":{"name":"PFAPA Syndrome (Periodic Fever, Aphthous Stomatitis, Pharyngitis, Adenitis)","system":"Immunological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PFAPA","Marshall syndrome","periodic fever syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common periodic fever syndrome of childhood, typically beginning before the age of five.","The defining feature is highly regular, recurrent episodes of fever occurring at strikingly predictable intervals, often every 3-6 weeks.","Episodes classically combine aphthous mouth ulcers, pharyngitis, and cervical lymphadenopathy, though not every feature is present each time.","A hallmark is that the child is entirely well, thriving, and asymptomatic between episodes with normal growth and development.","It is a clinical diagnosis of exclusion; recurrent streptococcal infection, cyclic neutropenia, and monogenic autoinflammatory disorders should be considered.","Fevers respond dramatically to a single dose of corticosteroid, which can be both therapeutic and supportive of the diagnosis, though steroids may shorten the interval between episodes.","Routine antibiotics are ineffective because the condition is inflammatory rather than infective.","The disorder is self-limiting, usually resolving spontaneously over several years without long-term sequelae; tonsillectomy is an option in selected cases."],"pathophysiology":"PFAPA is regarded as an autoinflammatory disorder of innate immunity rather than an infection or classic autoimmune disease. During attacks there is dysregulated activation of the innate immune system with increased pro-inflammatory cytokine signalling (including interleukin-1 beta pathway activity), producing the stereotyped febrile episodes. Unlike the monogenic periodic fever syndromes, no single consistent genetic cause has been identified, and inflammation resolves completely between episodes.","additionalDifferentials":["Recurrent streptococcal pharyngitis or tonsillitis","Cyclic neutropenia","Familial Mediterranean fever and other monogenic autoinflammatory syndromes","Hyper-IgD syndrome (mevalonate kinase deficiency)","TNF-receptor associated periodic syndrome (TRAPS)","Recurrent viral infections in young children"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Documentation of the fever pattern with a symptom and temperature diary","Full blood count during and between episodes (to exclude cyclic neutropenia)","Inflammatory markers during an attack","Throat swab or streptococcal testing to exclude recurrent bacterial pharyngitis","Genetic testing for monogenic periodic fever syndromes when features are atypical","Immunoglobulin levels where an alternative autoinflammatory diagnosis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Poor growth or failure to thrive, which is not expected in PFAPA","Persistent symptoms or inflammation between episodes","Irregular, unpredictable, or evolving fever patterns suggesting another diagnosis","Neutropenia coinciding with fevers (points to cyclic neutropenia)","Features suggesting a monogenic autoinflammatory disorder such as serositis, rash, or a strong family history"],"prognosis":"Excellent; PFAPA is benign and self-limiting, typically resolving over several years with no lasting effect on growth, development, or long-term health, and it is not associated with amyloidosis or organ damage.","pitfalls":["Repeatedly treating episodes with antibiotics for presumed bacterial pharyngitis","Diagnosing PFAPA in a child who is unwell or failing to thrive between episodes","Overlooking cyclic neutropenia by not checking blood counts during febrile episodes","Assuming steroid responsiveness alone confirms the diagnosis without excluding mimics","Not recognising that corticosteroids may shorten the interval to the next attack"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"periodontitis":{"name":"Periodontitis","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["periodontal disease","chronic periodontitis","gum disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Periodontitis is a biofilm-driven inflammatory disease causing irreversible destruction of the periodontal attachment and alveolar bone","The hallmark distinguishing it from gingivitis is loss of clinical attachment and true periodontal pocketing, not just bleeding gums","It is often painless and slowly progressive, so patients may present late with mobility, drifting teeth, or gingival recession","Probing depths, attachment levels, bleeding on probing, and radiographic bone loss are the core diagnostic measurements","Smoking, poorly controlled diabetes, and genetic susceptibility strongly modify disease severity and treatment response","There is a bidirectional relationship with diabetes and associations with cardiovascular and other systemic conditions","Treatment centres on meticulous plaque control and mechanical debridement to disrupt the subgingival biofilm, with surgery for residual defects","Long-term supportive periodontal maintenance is essential because the disease tends to recur without ongoing care"],"pathophysiology":"Periodontitis arises from a dysbiotic subgingival biofilm dominated by anaerobic and gram-negative species that provoke a chronic host immune-inflammatory response. Bacterial virulence factors and the resulting inflammatory mediators drive activation of osteoclasts and matrix-degrading enzymes, leading to breakdown of the periodontal ligament and resorption of alveolar bone. This produces apical migration of the junctional epithelium and true pocket formation. The extent of tissue destruction reflects the balance between microbial challenge and host response, which is heavily modulated by risk factors such as smoking, diabetes, and genetics, ultimately threatening tooth support.","additionalDifferentials":["Gingivitis without attachment loss","Necrotising periodontal disease","Periodontal abscess","Endodontic-periodontal combined lesions","Occlusal trauma causing mobility","Manifestations of systemic disease affecting the periodontium"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full-mouth periodontal charting of probing depths and attachment loss","Bleeding on probing and plaque indices","Periapical and bitewing radiographs to assess bone loss","Assessment of tooth mobility and furcation involvement","Screening for diabetes and smoking status as risk modifiers"],"redFlags":["Rapid attachment and bone loss in a young patient suggesting aggressive disease","Necrotising periodontitis with pain, ulceration, and tissue destruction, especially in immunocompromised patients","Periodontal manifestations pointing to undiagnosed diabetes or immune deficiency"],"prognosis":"With effective plaque control and professional care, disease progression can usually be halted and teeth retained, but lost attachment and bone do not regenerate spontaneously and advanced cases carry a guarded prognosis for affected teeth.","pitfalls":["Treating bleeding gums as simple gingivitis while missing established attachment loss","Neglecting smoking cessation and glycaemic control that undermine treatment","Relying on antibiotics instead of mechanical biofilm disruption","Failing to arrange ongoing maintenance, allowing recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"perioperative_anaphylaxis":{"name":"Perioperative anaphylaxis","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["anaesthetic anaphylaxis","intraoperative anaphylaxis","perioperative hypersensitivity reaction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A life-threatening acute hypersensitivity reaction during anaesthesia, often presenting abruptly with cardiovascular collapse rather than the skin signs seen in awake patients","Common triggers include neuromuscular blocking agents, antibiotics, latex, and chlorhexidine, with contrast and colloids as additional causes","Cardiovascular features (profound hypotension, tachycardia, and collapse) and respiratory features (bronchospasm, high airway pressures, desaturation) predominate under drapes","Cutaneous signs such as flushing, urticaria, and angioedema may be absent, masked, or noticed late because the patient is covered","Adrenaline (epinephrine) is the first-line treatment, titrated to severity, alongside stopping the likely trigger, giving oxygen, and aggressive intravenous fluid resuscitation","Refractory cases may need escalating vasopressor support, and cardiac arrest is managed with standard advanced life support with prolonged effort as needed","A mast cell tryptase level taken during and after the reaction supports the diagnosis, and the patient must be referred for allergy testing to identify the culprit","Clear documentation and patient warning about the responsible agent are essential to prevent recurrence at future anaesthetics"],"pathophysiology":"Perioperative anaphylaxis is usually an immediate hypersensitivity reaction, often IgE-mediated, in which prior sensitisation leads to cross-linking of IgE on mast cells and basophils upon re-exposure to an antigen. Some reactions are non-IgE-mediated or result from direct mast cell activation. Degranulation releases histamine, tryptase, leukotrienes, and other mediators that cause vasodilation, increased vascular permeability with fluid shift, bronchoconstriction, and myocardial depression. The result is rapid loss of intravascular volume, distributive shock, and airway compromise. Adrenaline counteracts these effects through vasoconstriction, bronchodilation, and stabilisation of mast cells.","additionalDifferentials":["Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity","Primary cardiac event or arrhythmia","High or total spinal anaesthesia","Severe bronchospasm from asthma","Pulmonary embolism or air embolism","Haemorrhagic or septic shock"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial mast cell tryptase levels during the reaction and after recovery","Continuous ECG, blood pressure, and oxygenation monitoring","Arterial blood gas to assess oxygenation and acidosis","Referral for later skin and specific IgE allergy testing to identify the trigger","Review of all agents and materials administered around the time of the reaction"],"redFlags":["Sudden cardiovascular collapse or cardiac arrest during anaesthesia","Severe bronchospasm with high airway pressures and difficulty ventilating","Profound refractory hypotension despite fluids and vasopressors","Rapidly progressive airway swelling or angioedema threatening the airway"],"prognosis":"With prompt recognition and early adrenaline most patients recover, but delayed treatment or severe refractory shock can be fatal or cause hypoxic organ injury; identifying and avoiding the trigger prevents recurrence.","pitfalls":["Delaying adrenaline while awaiting confirmation of the diagnosis","Expecting skin signs and dismissing anaphylaxis when they are absent under drapes","Failing to send timed tryptase samples or to refer for allergy testing","Under-resuscitating the large fluid deficit caused by capillary leak","Not documenting or warning the patient about the causative agent"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"peripheral_arterial_disease":{"name":"Peripheral arterial disease","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PAD","peripheral vascular disease","PVD","lower limb arterial disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["PAD is atherosclerotic narrowing of the arteries supplying the limbs, most often the lower extremities, and is a marker of widespread atherosclerosis with high cardiovascular risk","The classic symptom is intermittent claudication, exertional muscle pain in the calf, thigh, or buttock that is relieved by rest, though many patients are asymptomatic or have atypical leg symptoms","Examination may reveal diminished or absent pulses, arterial bruits, cool skin, hair loss, and delayed capillary refill","The ankle-brachial index is a simple, key bedside test; a reduced value confirms obstruction, while an abnormally high or incompressible value suggests medial calcification, common in diabetes and chronic kidney disease","Critical limb ischaemia, with rest pain, non-healing ulcers, or gangrene, indicates severe disease that threatens the limb and warrants urgent vascular assessment","Acute limb ischaemia is a vascular emergency presenting with the classic features of a suddenly painful, pale, pulseless, cold limb with sensory and motor changes","Management emphasises aggressive cardiovascular risk reduction, structured exercise, and antiplatelet and statin therapy, with revascularisation for lifestyle-limiting or limb-threatening disease","Because PAD reflects systemic atherosclerosis, coexisting coronary and cerebrovascular disease should be actively addressed"],"pathophysiology":"PAD is usually caused by atherosclerosis, in which lipid-laden plaque progressively narrows the arterial lumen and limits blood flow to the limbs. When demand rises with exertion, a flow-limiting stenosis cannot meet the increased oxygen requirement of exercising muscle, producing the ischaemic pain of claudication. As disease advances, resting perfusion becomes inadequate, causing rest pain, impaired wound healing, ulceration, and tissue loss (critical limb ischaemia). Acute limb ischaemia results from sudden occlusion by thrombosis of a diseased segment or by embolism, abruptly interrupting flow and threatening tissue viability within hours.","additionalDifferentials":["Lumbar spinal stenosis causing neurogenic claudication","Deep vein thrombosis and chronic venous insufficiency","Musculoskeletal or arthritic leg pain","Peripheral neuropathy","Chronic compartment syndrome","Nerve root compression or sciatica"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ankle-brachial index measurement, with exercise testing if resting values are equivocal","Duplex ultrasound to localise and grade stenoses","CT or MR angiography, or invasive angiography, to plan revascularisation","Assessment of cardiovascular risk factors including lipids, glucose, and smoking status","Toe-brachial index or other measures when arteries are incompressible","Wound and infection assessment in patients with ulcers or tissue loss"],"redFlags":["MEDICAL EMERGENCY: acute limb ischaemia with a painful, pale, pulseless, cold limb and sensory or motor deficit requiring urgent revascularisation","Critical limb ischaemia with rest pain, non-healing ulceration, or gangrene threatening the limb","Rapidly progressive tissue loss or signs of spreading infection in an ischaemic foot","Sudden onset of severe leg symptoms suggesting embolic occlusion"],"prognosis":"Limb prognosis is often stable in claudication with good risk-factor control, but PAD signals a substantially elevated risk of myocardial infarction, stroke, and cardiovascular death; critical and acute limb ischaemia carry high risks of amputation and mortality without timely intervention.","pitfalls":["Relying on a normal or high ankle-brachial index in patients with incompressible calcified vessels, as in diabetes or renal disease","Attributing exertional leg pain to musculoskeletal or neurogenic causes and missing PAD","Focusing on the limb while neglecting the high coronary and cerebrovascular risk","Failing to recognise acute limb ischaemia as an emergency requiring urgent revascularisation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"peripheral_neuropathy":{"name":"Peripheral Neuropathy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["polyneuropathy","peripheral nerve disease"],"chapter":457,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3594","clinicalPearls":["Three goals in the patient with neuropathy: localize the lesion, identify the cause, and determine treatment; the assessment is structured around seven key questions covering systems involved, distribution of weakness, nature of sensory loss, upper motor neuron signs, temporal evolution, hereditary clues, and associated medical conditions (Harrison 22e p.3595).","Pattern recognition is central: length-dependent symmetric distal sensory loss (Pattern 2) points to diabetes/metabolic, drugs, toxins, alcohol, hereditary causes; symmetric proximal AND distal weakness with sensory loss (Pattern 1) is the hallmark of acquired immune demyelinating polyneuropathy (GBS and CIDP); asymmetric/multifocal deficits (Pattern 3, mononeuropathy multiplex) suggest vasculitis, cryoglobulinemia, amyloid, sarcoid, or infection (Harrison 22e p.3596).","Nerve conduction studies and needle EMG distinguish axonal degeneration (low-amplitude potentials, relatively preserved distal latencies and conduction velocities, fibrillations on EMG) from segmental demyelination (slow conduction velocities, prolonged distal latencies, relatively preserved amplitudes, no fibrillations) (Harrison 22e p.3597).","Nonuniform slowing, conduction block, or temporal dispersion on NCS suggests an ACQUIRED demyelinating neuropathy (GBS or CIDP), whereas uniform slowing suggests a HEREDITARY demyelinating neuropathy (e.g., CMT type 1) (Harrison 22e p.3597).","Pure loss of pain and temperature with preserved vibration/position sense, normal strength and reflexes, and normal NCS indicates a small-fiber neuropathy; the most commonly identified causes are diabetes mellitus or glucose intolerance, with amyloid neuropathy also to be considered (Harrison 22e p.3596).","Dramatic proprioceptive/vibratory loss with normal strength, especially when non-length-dependent (arms more than legs or asymmetric), points to a sensory neuronopathy/ganglionopathy (Pattern 9) rather than a typical length-dependent polyneuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3597).","Diabetes mellitus is the most common cause of peripheral neuropathy in developed countries; distal symmetric sensory/sensorimotor polyneuropathy (DSPN) is the most common form, beginning in the toes and ascending (Harrison 22e p.3604).","Diabetic third nerve palsies are characteristically pupil-sparing (Harrison 22e p.3604)."],"pathophysiology":"Peripheral nerves contain mixed sensory, motor, and autonomic fibers grouped as large myelinated, small myelinated, and small unmyelinated classes; motor axons are usually large myelinated fast-conducting (~50 m/s) fibers, large-diameter sensory fibers carry proprioception and vibration, and small myelinated/unmyelinated fibers carry pain, temperature, and autonomic signals. Disease may target the neuronal cell body (neuronopathy/ganglionopathy), the myelin sheath (myelinopathy/demyelination), or the axon (axonopathy), each producing distinct clinical and electrophysiologic features. Most generalized polyneuropathies are length-dependent axonopathies producing distal (glove-and-stocking) deficits, whereas ganglionopathies produce non-length-dependent loss. Demyelination slows or blocks conduction; axonal loss reduces response amplitudes (Harrison 22e p.3594).","additionalDifferentials":["Inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy - Guillain-Barre syndrome (acute) and CIDP (chronic/relapsing) (Pattern 1) (Harrison 22e p.3595)","Cryptogenic/idiopathic sensory polyneuropathy (CSPN) - no etiology found in about half of patients despite extensive evaluation (Harrison 22e p.3595)","Diabetes mellitus and other metabolic neuropathies (DSPN, autonomic, radiculoplexus/Bruns-Garland, mononeuropathies) (Harrison 22e p.3604)","Hereditary neuropathies - Charcot-Marie-Tooth, HSAN, familial amyloid polyneuropathy, SORD neuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Vasculitic neuropathy presenting as mononeuropathy multiplex (Pattern 3) (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Uremic neuropathy in renal failure (~60% of patients) (Harrison 22e p.3605)","Nutritional/toxic neuropathies (B12, thiamine, alcohol, drugs, chemotherapy, heavy metals) (Harrison 22e p.3611)","Sensory neuronopathy/ganglionopathy - paraneoplastic (anti-Hu), Sjogren's syndrome, CANVAS, cisplatin, vitamin B6 toxicity (Pattern 9) (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Connective tissue/autoimmune disease - rheumatoid arthritis, SLE, scleroderma, sarcoidosis (Harrison 22e p.3605)"],"infectionMimics":["HIV infection - distal symmetric polyneuropathy, AIDP/CIDP, progressive polyradiculopathy, multiple mononeuropathies, sensory ganglionopathy (Harrison 22e p.3606)","Leprosy (Mycobacterium leprae) - most common cause of peripheral neuropathy in Southeast Asia, Africa, and South America; mononeuropathies or symmetric sensorimotor polyneuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3606)","Lyme disease (Borrelia burgdorferi) - facial neuropathy (bilateral in ~half, unlike idiopathic Bell's palsy), polyradiculoneuropathy, multiple mononeuropathies (Harrison 22e p.3606)","Diphtheria (Corynebacterium diphtheriae) - toxin-mediated neuropathy 3-4 weeks after infection (Harrison 22e p.3606)","Other infectious/inflammatory causes producing CSF pleocytosis in suspected GBS/CIDP - HIV, Lyme, sarcoidosis, lymphomatous/leukemic root infiltration (Harrison 22e p.3598)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Motor neuron disease (e.g., ALS) - asymmetric distal weakness without sensory loss, especially with upper motor neuron findings (Pattern 5) (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Multifocal motor neuropathy - treatable mimic of motor neuron disease (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Radiculopathy, plexopathy, and compressive/entrapment mononeuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Neuromuscular junction disorders - myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Myopathy, including isolated neck extensor myopathy (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Combined system degeneration - distal sensory signs plus symmetric upper motor neuron involvement from B12, copper, or vitamin E deficiency, severe hepatic disease, or adrenomyeloneuropathy (Pattern 6) (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Critical illness polyneuropathy versus critical illness myopathy in the ICU patient who cannot be weaned from the ventilator (Harrison 22e p.3606)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Nerve conduction studies and needle EMG to localize (mono- vs polyneuropathy vs radiculopathy/plexopathy) and classify as axonal vs demyelinating (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Autonomic testing (heart rate response to deep breathing, Valsalva, tilt-table, quantitative sudomotor axon reflex) for pure small-fiber or autonomic neuropathy when routine NCS are normal (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Baseline labs: CBC, electrolytes, renal and hepatic function, fasting glucose and HbA1c, thyroid function, B12, folate, ESR, rheumatoid factor, ANA, SPEP with immunofixation, and serum/urine free light chains (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Oral glucose tolerance test in painful sensory neuropathy even when fasting glucose and HbA1c are normal (abnormal in ~one-third) (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Serum free light chains and kappa/lambda ratio (more sensitive than SPEP/immunofixation) and skeletal survey if amyloidosis or monoclonal gammopathy suspected; hematology referral for marrow biopsy (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Vasculitis workup for mononeuropathy multiplex: ANCA, cryoglobulins, hepatitis serology, Lyme Western blot, HIV, occasionally CMV titer (Harrison 22e p.3598)","Lumbar puncture in suspected GBS/CIDP for elevated CSF protein (pleocytosis should be absent in idiopathic cases) (Harrison 22e p.3598)","Antibodies to SS-A/Ro, SS-B/La, and antineuronal nuclear (anti-Hu) antibodies in sensory ataxia/ganglionopathy; nerve biopsy reserved mainly for suspected amyloid or vasculitis and only when NCS are abnormal; skin biopsy for small-fiber neuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3598)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive symmetric proximal and distal weakness with sensory loss (Pattern 1) - acute (GBS) or chronic (CIDP) demyelinating neuropathy; GBS can require respiratory support, so identifying this treatable pattern is critical (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Acute/subacute course (days to weeks) - GBS, vasculitis, diabetic or Lyme radiculopathy require prompt evaluation (Harrison 22e p.3597)","Autonomic dysfunction without diabetes (orthostatic drop without compensatory tachycardia, bowel/bladder/sexual dysfunction) - should raise concern for amyloid polyneuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3595)","Mononeuropathy multiplex pattern - suggests vasculitis and warrants an urgent vasculitis workup (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Severe sensory ataxia from a ganglionopathy - consider paraneoplastic (anti-Hu, often small-cell lung cancer); the neuropathy can precede detection of malignancy and should prompt a cancer search (Harrison 22e p.3598)"],"prognosis":"Most neuropathies are insidious and slowly progressive; course may be monophasic, progressive, or relapsing (e.g., CIDP, porphyria). Despite extensive evaluation, about half of patients have no etiology identified (cryptogenic/idiopathic sensory polyneuropathy). Outcome depends heavily on cause: treatable acquired demyelinating neuropathies (GBS, CIDP) and treatable systemic causes can respond to therapy, whereas axonal degeneration tends to recover slowly and incompletely. Even with adequate cobalamin replacement, treatment of B12-deficiency neuropathy usually does not fully reverse deficits and at least 50% of patients retain some permanent neurologic deficit (Harrison 22e p.3595, p.3612).","pitfalls":["Missing a treatable cause - acquired demyelinating neuropathy (GBS/CIDP) and multifocal motor neuropathy are treatable and should be distinguished from untreatable mimics such as ALS (Harrison 22e p.3596)","Overlooking treatable systemic/metabolic causes such as B12 deficiency, thyroid disease, or a paraprotein/monoclonal gammopathy in an apparently idiopathic polyneuropathy (Harrison 22e p.3597)","B12-deficiency neuropathy can occur with only peripheral findings and without anemia or macrocytosis (lacking in up to 40%); check methylmalonic acid and homocysteine when suspicion is high (Harrison 22e p.3612)","Routine antiganglioside autoantibody panels have no proven added value beyond a complete clinical exam and detailed EDx, and a heavy metal screen is not a useful blind screening test without exposure history or suggestive findings (Harrison 22e p.3598)","Nerve biopsy rarely distinguishes one neuropathy from another beyond clinical exam and NCS; reserve it for suspected amyloid or vasculitis and only when NCS are abnormal (Harrison 22e p.3598)","A hereditary neuropathy can be missed when patients have marked sensory signs but few sensory symptoms plus slowly progressive distal weakness and skeletal clues (high/flat arches, hammer toes, scoliosis); examining family members may be needed (Harrison 22e p.3597)"],"severityClassification":"No formal numeric severity score is used; characterization is by anatomic/electrophysiologic classification rather than a grading scale - by fiber type affected (motor, sensory, autonomic, or combined; large- vs small-fiber), by distribution (mononeuropathy, mononeuropathy multiplex, polyneuropathy, radiculopathy, plexopathy; ten clinical patterns in Table 457-2), by underlying pathology (axonal degeneration vs segmental demyelination on NCS/EMG, Table 457-3), and by temporal course (acute: days to 4 weeks; subacute: 4-8 weeks; chronic: >8 weeks) (Harrison 22e p.3595, p.3597).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3594","Harrison 22e p.3595","Harrison 22e p.3596","Harrison 22e p.3597","Harrison 22e p.3598","Harrison 22e p.3604","Harrison 22e p.3612"],"crossLinks":["gbs","diabetes_mellitus","vasculitis"]},"peritonsillar_abscess":{"name":"Peritonsillar abscess","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["quinsy","peritonsillar cellulitis progressing to abscess"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A collection of pus in the peritonsillar space, usually a complication of acute tonsillitis, most common in young adults","Presents with severe unilateral sore throat, progressively worsening odynophagia, fever and marked malaise","Classic signs include trismus, a muffled hot-potato voice, drooling and foul breath","Examination shows a bulging, congested soft palate that displaces the uvula and tonsil toward the opposite side","Management principles include drainage of pus by needle aspiration or incision, together with systemic antibiotics, analgesia and hydration","Interval tonsillectomy is often advised after resolution, particularly with recurrent abscesses or a background of recurrent tonsillitis","Trismus can make examination and airway assessment difficult, so vigilance for airway compromise is essential","Untreated infection can spread to the parapharyngeal space and beyond, risking deep neck infection and airway obstruction"],"pathophysiology":"Peritonsillar abscess develops when infection extends beyond the tonsillar capsule into the loose peritonsillar space between the tonsil and the superior constrictor muscle, typically as a progression of acute tonsillitis or from obstructed minor salivary (Weber) glands. Cellulitis in this space evolves into a localised collection of pus, most often at the upper pole. The expanding abscess pushes the tonsil and soft palate medially, irritates the pterygoid muscles to cause trismus, and can dissect into adjacent deep neck spaces, threatening the airway and vascular structures.","additionalDifferentials":["Severe acute tonsillitis without abscess","Parapharyngeal or retropharyngeal abscess","Infectious mononucleosis with tonsillar enlargement","Tonsillar or oropharyngeal malignancy","Dental or peritonsillar space infection from another source","Epiglottitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Oropharyngeal examination assessing palatal bulge, uvular deviation and trismus","Needle aspiration to confirm and localise pus","Full blood count and inflammatory markers","Contrast CT of the neck when deep neck space extension is suspected or examination is limited","Airway assessment and monitoring in severe cases"],"redFlags":["Signs of airway compromise such as stridor, respiratory distress, severe trismus or drooling requiring urgent airway management","Rapid spread into parapharyngeal or retropharyngeal spaces indicating deep neck infection","Neck swelling, torticollis or signs of vascular involvement","Systemic sepsis or failure to improve after drainage and antibiotics"],"prognosis":"With timely drainage and antibiotics the outlook is good, but delayed treatment can lead to airway obstruction, deep neck space infection, mediastinitis or vascular complications, which carry significant risk.","pitfalls":["Underestimating airway risk in a patient with severe trismus and drooling","Treating with antibiotics alone when pus requires drainage","Missing extension into deep neck spaces","Failing to arrange follow-up and consider interval tonsillectomy in recurrent cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"persistent_depressive_disorder":{"name":"Persistent depressive disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dysthymia","dysthymic disorder","chronic depression","PDD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The defining feature is chronically depressed mood present more days than not for at least two years in adults, or one year in children and adolescents, with fewer symptom-free intervals","Associated symptoms may include poor appetite or overeating, sleep disturbance, low energy, low self-esteem, poor concentration, and feelings of hopelessness","Because symptoms are long-standing, patients often regard the low mood as part of their personality and may not recognise it as a treatable illness","A major depressive episode can be superimposed on the chronic baseline, a pattern often described as double depression, which increases severity and risk","Suicide risk is present and should be assessed actively despite the seemingly milder day-to-day picture","Management combines evidence-based psychotherapy with antidepressant pharmacotherapy, and chronic forms may need longer treatment to achieve and maintain response","Screen for a history of hypomania or mania and for medical contributors such as hypothyroidism before attributing chronic low mood solely to a primary mood disorder","Functional impairment accumulates over years, so early recognition and sustained treatment can substantially improve quality of life"],"pathophysiology":"Persistent depressive disorder shares much of the neurobiology of major depression, with contributions from genetic vulnerability, monoamine dysregulation, and altered stress-axis and neuroplastic signalling, but is characterised by chronicity. Early adversity, ongoing psychosocial stressors, temperament, and reinforced negative cognitive styles are thought to sustain the persistent low-grade mood disturbance over years.","additionalDifferentials":["Major depressive disorder, episodic type","Bipolar disorder, particularly bipolar II, in a depressive phase","Cyclothymic disorder","Hypothyroidism and other chronic medical illness","Substance-induced or medication-induced mood disorder","Adjustment disorder with prolonged stressor","Personality traits or disorder contributing to chronic dysphoria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured suicide risk assessment","Standardised depression rating scales to track chronic severity","Thyroid function testing","Full blood count and metabolic panel to exclude medical contributors","Screening for hypomanic or manic episodes","Substance use screening"],"redFlags":["Active suicidal ideation with plan, intent, or access to means","Emergence of a superimposed major depressive episode with worsening severity","Emerging manic or hypomanic symptoms after starting an antidepressant","Profound self-neglect or loss of function"],"prognosis":"Persistent depressive disorder is by definition chronic and can be more treatment-resistant than episodic depression, with high rates of relapse and comorbidity. Prognosis improves with sustained combined treatment and psychosocial support, but is worse with early onset, superimposed major depression, and untreated comorbid conditions.","pitfalls":["Assuming chronic low mood is fixed personality rather than a treatable disorder","Underestimating suicide risk because symptoms appear mild day to day","Missing a superimposed major depressive episode (double depression)","Failing to screen for bipolarity before antidepressant treatment","Ending treatment too early given the chronic, relapsing nature of the condition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"perthes_disease":{"name":"Perthes' disease","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Legg-Calve-Perthes disease","idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head in children","coxa plana"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head in children, typically presenting between about 4 and 8 years and more common in boys","Presents with a limp and hip, groin, or referred knee pain, often intermittent, with restricted abduction and internal rotation of the hip","Knee pain in a child can be referred from the hip, so the hip must always be examined in a limping child","Early radiographs may be normal; later changes include increased femoral head density, fragmentation, flattening, and eventual remodelling","The key aims are to relieve symptoms, maintain hip movement, and keep the femoral head 'contained' within the acetabulum so it remodels into a congruent, spherical shape","Younger children with more remaining growth and greater remodelling potential generally have a better prognosis than older children","Treatment ranges from observation, activity restriction, and physiotherapy to containment by bracing or surgery (femoral or pelvic osteotomy) in more severe or older cases","Bilateral or atypical presentations should prompt consideration of alternative diagnoses such as skeletal dysplasia or hypothyroidism"],"pathophysiology":"Perthes' disease results from a temporary interruption of the blood supply to the immature femoral head epiphysis, causing avascular necrosis. The precise trigger is unknown but the epiphysis undergoes a sequence of ischaemic necrosis, followed by fragmentation and resorption of dead bone, then reossification and remodelling as revascularisation occurs. During the biologically 'plastic' necrotic and fragmentation phases the softened femoral head is vulnerable to deformation under load, and can flatten and enlarge (coxa magna) or become incongruent if not well contained within the acetabulum. The ultimate shape of the healed head, and hence long-term function, depends on how well sphericity and containment are preserved through this self-limiting but prolonged process.","additionalDifferentials":["Transient synovitis (irritable hip)","Septic arthritis or osteomyelitis of the hip","Slipped capital femoral epiphysis (in older children)","Developmental dysplasia of the hip","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Skeletal dysplasia or epiphyseal dysplasia (especially if bilateral)","Hypothyroidism and avascular necrosis from other causes (e.g. sickle cell disease, steroids)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anteroposterior and frog-lateral radiographs of both hips to assess and stage the changes","MRI for early detection before radiographic changes appear and to assess the extent of necrosis","Clinical assessment of hip range of movement, particularly abduction and internal rotation","Investigations to exclude infection or inflammatory arthritis when the presentation is acute","Consideration of bilateral or systemic workup (e.g. thyroid function, skeletal survey) if presentation is atypical or bilateral"],"redFlags":["Fever, systemic upset, and an acutely irritable hip suggesting septic arthritis requiring urgent exclusion","Bilateral or atypical disease suggesting skeletal dysplasia or an underlying systemic cause","Marked loss of hip movement and femoral head extrusion indicating a head at risk of permanent deformity","An older child at presentation, associated with a poorer remodelling capacity"],"prognosis":"The disease is self-limiting and many younger children with limited head involvement remodel to a near-normal, congruent hip; however, older children, extensive necrosis, and a poorly contained or deformed femoral head are associated with residual incongruity and an increased risk of premature hip osteoarthritis in adulthood.","pitfalls":["Attributing a child's knee pain to the knee and failing to examine the hip","Assuming a normal early radiograph excludes the disease and missing early presentation on MRI","Not distinguishing Perthes' disease from septic arthritis in the acutely painful hip","Overlooking bilateral involvement that should prompt evaluation for an underlying systemic disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"pertussis":{"name":"Pertussis (Whooping Cough)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["whooping cough","Bordetella pertussis"],"chapter":165,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1276-1280","clinicalPearls":["Acute respiratory tract infection caused by Bordetella pertussis; 'pertussis' means 'violent cough' and the inspiratory whoop at the end of a paroxysm gives the disease its common name (p.1276).","Classic course runs through three stages: catarrhal phase (1-2 weeks of coryza, lacrimation, mild cough, low-grade fever, malaise, indistinguishable from a common cold), then paroxysmal phase (frequent spasmodic bursts of 5-10 coughs within a single expiration), then convalescent phase (p.1278-1279).","Paroxysms may end in an audible whoop produced by rapid inspiration against a closed glottis; post-tussive vomiting is frequent and a mucous plug may be expelled (p.1279).","During a coughing spasm there may be neck-vein distension, bulging eyes, tongue protrusion, and cyanosis; paroxysms can be precipitated by noise, eating, or physical contact and are often worse at night (p.1279).","Between attacks the patient looks normal but shows increasing fatigue; fever is uncommon and, when present, suggests bacterial superinfection (p.1279).","The convalescent phase lasts 1-3 months with gradual resolution; for 6-12 months afterward, intercurrent viral infections may trigger a recrudescence of paroxysmal cough (p.1279).","Incubation period averages 7-10 days (p.1279).","The whoop is variable: uncommon in infants 6 months of age or younger and frequently absent in older children and adults (p.1276).","Lymphocytosis (absolute lymphocyte count >108-109/L) is common in young children and unusual with other infections, but is not a feature in adolescents and adults (p.1279).","Highly communicable: attack rates of 80-100% among unimmunized household contacts; cyclical outbreaks occur every 3-5 years despite widespread immunization (p.1277).","Severe morbidity and high mortality are restricted almost entirely to infants; in the US (2000-2017) infants under 2 months accounted for 84% of pertussis deaths (p.1278)."],"pathophysiology":"Infection begins with attachment of B. pertussis to ciliated epithelial cells of the nasopharynx, mediated by surface adhesins (pertactin, filamentous hemagglutinin) binding integrin-family proteins, probably with pertussis toxin. The organism multiplies locally and produces toxins causing mucosal damage (tracheal cytotoxin, dermonecrotic toxin); host defense is impaired by pertussis toxin and adenylate cyclase-hemolysin toxin. There is local cellular invasion with intracellular persistence, but systemic dissemination does NOT occur; systemic manifestations such as lymphocytosis result from the toxins. The cause of the hallmark paroxysmal cough is not known; a pivotal role for pertussis toxin has been proposed but not confirmed. Neurologic events (seizures, encephalopathy) are thought to result from hypoxia due to coughing paroxysms or apnea rather than from specific bacterial products. Deaths in young infants are frequently associated with very high leukocytosis and pulmonary hypertension (p.1278).","additionalDifferentials":["Bordetella parapertussis (causes a similar but typically milder illness; does not express pertussis toxin gene) (p.1276).","Bordetella holmesii (found in up to 20% of patients with a pertussis-like syndrome using improved PCR) (p.1276).","Mycoplasma pneumoniae (p.1280).","Chlamydia pneumoniae (p.1280).","Adenovirus (p.1280).","Influenza virus and other respiratory viruses (p.1280).","Respiratory syncytial virus and rhinovirus -- isolated from patients with clinical pertussis but probably represent co-infection, particularly in children under 1 year (p.1280)."],"infectionMimics":["Bordetella parapertussis infection (p.1280)","Bordetella holmesii infection (p.1276)","Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection (p.1280)","Chlamydia pneumoniae infection (p.1280)","Adenovirus, influenza, and other respiratory viral infections (p.1280)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["ACE inhibitor-induced cough (p.1280)","Reactive airway disease (p.1280)","Gastroesophageal reflux disease (p.1280)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Nasopharyngeal culture (Bordet-Gengou or Regan-Lowe medium) -- gold standard owing to 100% specificity; cultures become positive by day 5 of incubation (p.1279).","PCR / multitarget real-time PCR -- has replaced culture in most laboratories due to greater sensitivity and faster (hours) results; differentiates B. pertussis, B. parapertussis, and B. holmesii (p.1279).","Nasopharyngeal aspiration is the best specimen; B. pertussis is highly sensitive to drying so specimens must be inoculated without delay or placed in transport medium (p.1279).","Complete blood count for lymphocytosis (informative in young children, not in adolescents/adults) (p.1279).","Serology -- enzyme immunoassays for IgA/IgG to pertussis toxin, filamentous hemagglutinin, pertactin, and fimbriae; increasingly used in adolescents, adults, and those symptomatic >4 weeks; single-specimen anti-pertussis-toxin serology is becoming standardized, especially for outbreaks and surveillance (p.1279)."],"redFlags":["Apnea in infants (occurred in 27.1% of children under 2 years hospitalized with pertussis) (p.1279).","Cyanosis during coughing spasms (p.1279).","Seizures and encephalopathy, thought to result from hypoxia of paroxysms/apnea (p.1278-1279).","Very high leukocytosis and pulmonary hypertension in young infants -- frequently associated with death (p.1278).","Pneumonia (occurs in up to 22% of infants with pertussis) (p.1278-1279).","Infants, especially those under 2-3 months, carry the highest risk of complications and death and should generally be hospitalized (p.1278, 1279)."],"prognosis":"With universal childhood immunization, reported cases fell by >90% and mortality declined even more dramatically. Severe morbidity and high mortality remain almost entirely restricted to infants; WHO estimates ~40 million cases and ~400,000 deaths globally each year, mostly among infants under 3 months. Immunity after natural infection is NOT lifelong; immunity after whole-cell vaccination is short-lived (little protection after 10-12 years) and wanes even faster (within 2-4 years of the fifth or sixth dose) in those immunized with acellular vaccines (p.1277-1278).","pitfalls":["In adolescents and adults the presentation is often atypical -- frequently no paroxysmal cough or whoop -- so pertussis is a commonly missed cause of prolonged cough; B. pertussis may be the agent in 12-30% of adults whose cough does not improve within 2 weeks (p.1278).","Antibiotic therapy aims to eradicate nasopharyngeal carriage; it does NOT substantially alter the clinical course unless given early in the catarrhal phase (p.1279).","Cough suppressants are NOT effective and have no role in management; beta-adrenergic agonists and glucocorticoids have been advocated but are NOT proven effective (p.1280).","Lymphocytosis is helpful in young children but is NOT a reliable feature in adolescents and adults (p.1279).","There is only a small window for culture/PCR diagnosis because much of the period of organism recovery is during the catarrhal phase when pertussis is not yet suspected (p.1279).","Cross-reactivity of antigens such as filamentous hemagglutinin and pertactin among Bordetella species makes diagnosis based on seroconversion of a single antibody type unreliable (p.1279).","Suspect pertussis in any patient with a cough not improving within 14 days, a paroxysmal cough of any duration, cough followed by vomiting (adolescents/adults), or respiratory symptoms after contact with a laboratory-confirmed case (p.1280).","Waning vaccine-derived immunity (faster with acellular vaccines) underlies resurgent outbreaks even in fully immunized older children, adolescents, and adults (p.1278)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd ed., Chapter 165: Pertussis and Other Bordetella Infections (ElSherif & Halperin), pp.1276-1280.","Kilgore PE et al. Pertussis: Microbiology, disease, treatment, and prevention. Clin Microbiol Rev 29:449, 2016 (Table 165-1 source, p.1278).","Craig R et al. Asymptomatic infection and transmission of pertussis in households: A systematic review. Clin Infect Dis 70:152, 2020.","Havers FP et al. Use of Tdap vaccines: Updated ACIP recommendations, United States 2019. MMWR 69:77, 2020."],"crossLinks":[]},"phenylketonuria":{"name":"Phenylketonuria","system":"Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PKU","phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency","hyperphenylalaninaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive inborn error of amino acid metabolism caused most often by deficiency of the enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase.","Untreated, accumulation of phenylalanine causes progressive and irreversible intellectual disability, seizures, and behavioural problems.","Affected infants are typically normal at birth, so detection relies on newborn screening rather than clinical signs.","Classic untreated features can include a musty or mousy body odour, eczema, and reduced pigmentation with fair hair and skin.","Early diagnosis through screening and prompt dietary treatment prevent the neurological damage almost entirely.","Treatment centres on a lifelong low-phenylalanine diet with a special protein substitute, guided by regular monitoring of blood phenylalanine levels.","A subset of patients respond to the cofactor tetrahydrobiopterin (sapropterin), and disorders of biopterin metabolism must be excluded as a separate cause of high phenylalanine.","Maternal PKU with poorly controlled phenylalanine during pregnancy can damage the fetus, causing microcephaly, congenital heart disease, and developmental delay, so strict control is essential before and during pregnancy.","Lifelong follow-up in a metabolic service supports adherence and long-term neurocognitive outcomes."],"pathophysiology":"Deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase (or, less commonly, of its tetrahydrobiopterin cofactor) blocks the conversion of phenylalanine to tyrosine. Phenylalanine therefore accumulates in blood and tissues and is diverted to alternative metabolites such as phenylketones. High phenylalanine levels are neurotoxic to the developing brain, interfering with amino acid transport across the blood-brain barrier, neurotransmitter synthesis, and myelination, producing the intellectual disability and neurological features of untreated disease. Reduced tyrosine availability also limits synthesis of melanin, contributing to hypopigmentation.","additionalDifferentials":["Tetrahydrobiopterin (cofactor) deficiency disorders","Transient hyperphenylalaninaemia of the newborn","Other inborn errors of metabolism presenting with developmental delay","Non-metabolic causes of intellectual disability and seizures","Tyrosinaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Newborn bloodspot screening for elevated phenylalanine","Quantitative plasma amino acids (phenylalanine and tyrosine)","Tetrahydrobiopterin loading test and pterin studies to exclude cofactor defects","Dihydropteridine reductase activity measurement","Genetic analysis of the PAH gene","Serial blood phenylalanine monitoring to guide dietary treatment"],"redFlags":["Markedly elevated phenylalanine levels requiring urgent dietary intervention","Seizures or neurological deterioration in an untreated infant","Poor metabolic control during pregnancy (maternal PKU) risking fetal harm","Failure of phenylalanine to fall despite dietary treatment, suggesting a cofactor defect","Signs of nutritional deficiency from an overly restricted diet"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and consistent dietary control, cognitive development is normal or near-normal; delayed treatment or poor lifelong control leads to irreversible neurocognitive impairment, and maternal control critically affects offspring.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal newborn appearance excludes the diagnosis","Failing to exclude tetrahydrobiopterin deficiency as a treatable alternative cause","Relaxing dietary control in older children and adults, risking cognitive and neuropsychiatric effects","Overlooking the need for strict preconception and pregnancy control in affected mothers"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"pheochromocytoma":{"name":"Pheochromocytoma","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pheochromocytoma and paraganglioma","PPGL","Paraganglioma","Chromaffin cell tumor"],"chapter":399,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3073","clinicalPearls":["Pheochromocytomas and paragangliomas (PPGLs) are catecholamine-producing tumors arising from sympathetic (adrenal medulla, sympathetic trunk) or parasympathetic (carotid body, glomus tympanicum/jugulare/vagale) paraganglia; by WHO usage pheochromocytoma denotes the adrenal tumor and paraganglioma denotes tumors at all other sites.","The classic triad is episodic palpitations, headache, and profuse sweating, present in roughly one-third of patients; the combination of this triad with hypertension makes pheochromocytoma a likely diagnosis, though tumors can remain asymptomatic for years.","Hypertension is the dominant sign and may be paroxysmal (classic) or sustained (also common); paroxysms generally last under one hour.","The classic 'rule of tens' states that about 10% are bilateral, 10% extra-adrenal, and 10% metastatic.","Diagnosis rests on two equally important pillars: biochemical documentation of catecholamine excess and imaging localization of the tumor.","Plasma free metanephrines are the most sensitive biochemical test and are less prone to false-positive elevation from stress including venipuncture; most tumors continuously leak O-methylated metabolites even when catecholamine secretion fluctuates.","Values three times the upper limit of normal make the diagnosis highly likely regardless of assay; because the tumor is rare, borderline elevations are more likely to be false positives, so drug/dietary confounders should be excluded and testing repeated.","On unenhanced CT, an adrenal mass with attenuation below 10 Hounsfield units makes pheochromocytoma unlikely; about 5% of adrenal incidentalomas prove to be pheochromocytomas on endocrine evaluation.","Histology classically shows a 'Zellballen' pattern of neuroendocrine chief cell nests with peripheral sustentacular cells; chief cells stain for chromogranin and synaptophysin, sustentacular cells for S-100, and loss of SDHB staining suggests an underlying germline SDH mutation.","About 25-33% of patients have an inherited syndrome (MEN 2, VHL, NF1, paraganglioma syndromes, TMEM127, MAX); these patients present on average about 15 years younger than those with sporadic tumors.","Universal germline panel testing is the gold standard and identifies a genetic cause in up to about 30% of cases, with higher yield in early onset, extra-adrenal, multiple, or metastatic tumors or a family history.","Pheochromocytoma in pregnancy may produce adrenergic crises, possibly via hCG/LHCG-receptor-mediated stimulation of epinephrine production, and is challenging to manage."],"pathophysiology":"PPGLs are well-vascularized neuroendocrine tumors derived from chromaffin cells of the sympathetic and parasympathetic paraganglial system. They synthesize and store catecholamines (norepinephrine, epinephrine, dopamine), and tumoral release drives the clinical syndrome; the name pheochromocytoma reflects the dark staining produced by chromaffin oxidation of catecholamines. The etiology of sporadic tumors is unknown, but 25-33% carry germline mutations. Susceptibility genes fall into three pathogenetic clusters: cluster 1 (pseudohypoxia: SDHx, FH, VHL, HIF2A), cluster 2 (kinase signaling: RET, NF1, TMEM127, MAX, HRAS, KIF1B-beta, PDH), and cluster 3 (Wnt signaling: CSDE1, MAML3). Most genes act as tumor suppressors with biallelic inactivation, while RET is a protooncogene whose mutations activate receptor tyrosine kinase activity; SDH is a Krebs-cycle/mitochondrial respiratory chain enzyme, and the VHL protein is part of a ubiquitin E3 ligase whose loss upregulates cell-cycle, glucose metabolism, and oxygen-sensing pathways.","additionalDifferentials":["Essential hypertension","Anxiety/panic attacks (may be mistaken for psychiatric illness)","Cocaine or amphetamine use","Mastocytosis or carcinoid syndrome (usually without hypertension)","Intracranial lesions","Clonidine withdrawal","Autonomic epilepsy","Factitious crises (sympathomimetic amine use)","For an asymptomatic adrenal mass: nonfunctioning adrenal adenoma, aldosteronoma, cortisol-producing adenoma (Cushing's syndrome)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Essential hypertension with adrenergic features","Panic disorder / anxiety attacks","Sympathomimetic drug effect (cocaine, amphetamines)","Carcinoid syndrome and systemic mastocytosis","Clonidine or other antihypertensive withdrawal"],"additionalInvestigations":["24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines and catecholamines (high sensitivity for initial screening)","Plasma free metanephrines (most sensitive, least stress-confounded)","Contrast CT or T2-weighted MRI with gadolinium for localization (MRI somewhat better for extra-adrenal disease)","Functional imaging: 68Ga-DOTATATE PET/CT and 18F-DOPA PET/CT (very high sensitivity and specificity, >95%)","123I/131I-MIBG scintigraphy and 18F-FDG PET, useful in hereditary and metastatic disease","Immunohistochemistry (chromogranin, synaptophysin, S-100, SDHB) on resected tissue","Universal germline genetic panel testing","Postoperative documentation of catecholamine normalization and ACTH stimulation test after bilateral cortex-sparing surgery"],"redFlags":["Catecholamine crisis causing heart failure, pulmonary edema, arrhythmias, or intracranial hemorrhage","Paroxysms precipitated by surgery, anesthesia/intubation, positional change, exercise, micturition (bladder paraganglioma), pregnancy, or drugs (tricyclics, opiates, metoclopramide)","Paradoxical hypertensive response to antihypertensive drugs","Beta blockade given before adequate alpha blockade (risk of worsened hypertension)","Hypertensive crisis or adrenergic crisis during pregnancy","Features suggesting a hereditary syndrome (early onset, bilateral/multifocal, extra-adrenal, family history, MTC, retinal/CNS/renal tumors)"],"prognosis":"Complete tumor removal is potentially curative and can prevent lethal hypertensive crises. About 5-10% of PPGLs are metastatic; histologic criteria alone are insufficient to define malignancy, and the WHO now uses 'metastatic' (lymph node or distant spread, commonly to lung, bone, or liver) rather than 'malignant.' The prognosis of metastatic disease is variable with 5-year survival of roughly 30-60%. Hereditary syndromes influence risk: about one-third of PGL4 (SDHB) patients develop metastases, the highest rate among the associated syndromes, and metastatic disease also occurs notably with VHL, NF1, and PGL1.","pitfalls":["Borderline biochemical elevations are frequently false positives given tumor rarity; failing to exclude dietary/drug confounders (sympathomimetics, diuretics, tricyclics, alpha/beta blockers, levodopa withdrawal) and repeat testing leads to overdiagnosis.","Serial catecholamine levels vary widely due to fluctuating secretion, so reliance on a single catecholamine measurement can miss the diagnosis; metanephrines are continuously leaked.","Starting a beta blocker before adequate alpha blockade can precipitate severe hypertension.","FNA of a presumed adrenal mass that is actually a pheochromocytoma can provoke a crisis.","Standard histologic atypia, mitoses, and local invasion do not reliably establish malignancy.","Symptoms can be misattributed to a primary psychiatric/panic disorder.","NF1 germline mutations are essentially never found in sporadic pheochromocytoma, so NF1 testing is unnecessary without other neurofibromatosis features.","Sequencing protocols may miss large single- or multi-exon deletions."],"severityClassification":"Malignancy/recurrence risk is assessed by markers including tumor size >5 cm, the Pheochromocytoma of the Adrenal gland Scaled Score (PASS), the Grading system for Adrenal Pheochromocytoma and Paraganglioma (GAPP), and SDHB status, although these remain controversial. Disease is now categorized by the WHO as localized versus metastatic (regional lymph node or distant metastasis) rather than benign versus malignant.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3073","Harrison 22e p.3074","Harrison 22e p.3075","Harrison 22e p.3076","Harrison 22e p.3077","Harrison 22e p.3078"],"crossLinks":[]},"phimosis":{"name":"Phimosis","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["non-retractile foreskin","preputial stenosis","tight foreskin"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Phimosis is an inability to retract the foreskin over the glans; it is physiological in most infants and young boys and usually resolves as natural preputial separation occurs with age","Pathological (acquired) phimosis follows scarring from recurrent balanitis, forced retraction, or the skin condition balanitis xerotica obliterans (lichen sclerosus), producing a firm white cicatricial ring","It must be distinguished from paraphimosis, in which a retracted foreskin becomes trapped behind the glans and cannot be reduced, causing painful swelling and constituting an emergency","Symptoms may include ballooning of the foreskin on voiding, difficulty with hygiene, recurrent infection, painful erections, and, in severe cases, obstructive voiding","Physiological phimosis is managed conservatively with reassurance, gentle hygiene, and avoidance of forcible retraction, often supplemented by topical corticosteroid application to soften the ring","Balanitis xerotica obliterans is an important pathological cause that typically requires circumcision and histological confirmation, and can involve the meatus and urethra","Circumcision or preputioplasty is indicated for true pathological phimosis, recurrent balanoposthitis, or troublesome symptoms not responding to conservative measures","Persistent phimosis in adults, particularly with lichen sclerosus, is a risk factor for penile carcinoma and warrants careful examination of the glans"],"pathophysiology":"In physiological phimosis the foreskin is non-retractile simply because natural adhesions between the inner prepuce and glans have not yet separated and the preputial opening is developmentally narrow. Pathological phimosis results from fibrosis and scarring of the preputial ring, most often due to chronic or recurrent inflammation (balanoposthitis), repeated trauma from forced retraction, or lichen sclerosus (balanitis xerotica obliterans), an inflammatory dermatosis that causes progressive sclerosis. The scarred, inelastic ring restricts retraction and may obstruct the urinary stream.","additionalDifferentials":["Paraphimosis","Balanitis or balanoposthitis","Balanitis xerotica obliterans (lichen sclerosus)","Preputial adhesions (physiological)","Penile carcinoma or premalignant lesion","Meatal stenosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination of the foreskin, glans, and meatus","Assessment for the cicatricial white ring of lichen sclerosus","Urinalysis if infection or voiding symptoms are present","Histological examination of excised preputial tissue after circumcision, particularly when lichen sclerosus or malignancy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Paraphimosis with a trapped, swollen, painful glans requiring emergency reduction","Severe phimosis causing obstructed voiding or acute urinary retention","Suspicious glans lesion or induration suggesting penile carcinoma","Features of lichen sclerosus with meatal or urethral involvement"],"prognosis":"Physiological phimosis almost always resolves with growth and conservative care; pathological phimosis is effectively cured by circumcision, but lichen sclerosus can recur or involve the meatus and carries a long-term association with penile malignancy.","pitfalls":["Forcibly retracting a young boy's physiological phimosis, which can cause scarring and true phimosis","Mislabelling normal developmental non-retractility as pathological and recommending unnecessary surgery","Missing lichen sclerosus or an underlying penile malignancy in an adult with acquired phimosis","Confusing phimosis with paraphimosis and delaying emergency reduction","Overlooking meatal stenosis in patients with lichen sclerosus after circumcision"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"physical_examination_of_the_cardiovascular":{"name":"Physical Examination of the Cardiovascular System","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Cardiac physical examination","Bedside cardiovascular assessment","Cardiovascular examination"],"chapter":246,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1859","clinicalPearls":["The jugular venous pressure is the single most important bedside measurement for estimating volume status; the internal jugular vein is preferred over the external because the latter is valved and not directly in line with the superior vena cava and right atrium.","Using the sternal angle (angle of Louis) as the reference point systematically underestimates central venous pressure, so it is better used to separate normal from elevated CVP than to quantify it; venous pulsations above the clavicle in the sitting position are clearly abnormal since the clavicle-to-right-atrium distance is at least 10 cm.","The venous waveform is biphasic in sinus rhythm whereas the carotid pulse is monophasic; the venous pulse is not easily obliterated by palpation and changes with posture and inspiration, helping distinguish it from the arterial pulse.","A cannon a wave occurs with atrioventricular dissociation when the right atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve; in a wide-complex tachycardia, cannon a waves identify the rhythm as ventricular in origin. The a wave is absent in atrial fibrillation.","Kussmaul's sign (a rise or lack of fall of the JVP with inspiration) is classically associated with constrictive pericarditis but also occurs with restrictive cardiomyopathy, massive pulmonary embolism, right ventricular infarction, and advanced left ventricular systolic heart failure, and is common after cardiac surgery.","Pulsus parvus et tardus (weak and delayed carotid pulse) suggests severe aortic stenosis, whereas a sharp rapidly collapsing carotid upstroke (Corrigan's or water-hammer pulse) points to chronic severe aortic regurgitation.","Pulsus paradoxus (inspiratory fall in systolic pressure >10 mmHg) is seen in pericardial tamponade but also occurs with massive pulmonary embolism, hemorrhagic shock, severe obstructive lung disease, and tension pneumothorax; it reflects exaggerated interventricular dependence.","Fixed splitting of S2 (a wide A2-P2 interval that does not vary with respiration) is characteristic of a secundum atrial septal defect, while reversed (paradoxical) splitting occurs with left bundle branch block, right ventricular pacing, severe aortic stenosis, hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy, and acute myocardial ischemia.","The mitral valve prolapse click-murmur complex moves away from S1 with maneuvers that increase preload (squatting) and closer to S1 on standing, a dynamic behavior that helps distinguish it from other systolic events.","Lower extremity edema with an elevated JVP indicates volume overload (heart failure or constrictive pericarditis), whereas edema without jugular venous hypertension suggests hypoalbuminemia (nephrotic syndrome, liver failure), venous insufficiency, or dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker use.","Janeway lesions (nontender hemorrhagic macules on palms and soles) and Osler's nodes (tender nodules on finger/toe pads), along with splinter hemorrhages in the mid-nail bed, are peripheral signs of infective endocarditis.","An ankle-brachial index (ankle systolic pressure divided by the higher brachial systolic pressure) is a powerful predictor of long-term cardiovascular mortality; a >2% finger-to-toe oxygen saturation difference on pulse oximetry performs comparably for detecting lower extremity peripheral arterial disease."],"pathophysiology":"The cardiovascular examination derives diagnostic and prognostic information from the mechanical and hemodynamic consequences of cardiac and vascular disease. The jugular venous waveform reflects right atrial pressure dynamics: the a wave is presystolic right atrial contraction, the x descent is atrial relaxation, the c wave is the systolic bulging of the closed tricuspid valve, the v wave is atrial filling during ventricular systole, and the y descent is right atrial emptying after tricuspid opening. Reduced right ventricular compliance accentuates the a wave; tricuspid regurgitation accentuates the v wave with a rapid y descent and progressively 'ventricularizes' the waveform; obstruction to right ventricular inflow (tricuspid stenosis, tamponade) blunts or prolongs the y descent. Arterial pulse contour depends on stroke volume, ejection velocity, vascular compliance, and systemic vascular resistance, so fixed outflow obstruction blunts and delays the upstroke while wide pulse pressure states produce a bounding collapsing pulse. Heart sound intensity reflects valve leaflet excursion and mobility, ventricular contractility, and the PR interval, while the distance between the stethoscope and the cardiac event (obesity, lung disease, effusion) attenuates all sounds. Splitting of S2 depends on the relative timing of aortic and pulmonic valve closure, which is altered by conduction delays, shunts, and outflow obstruction.","additionalDifferentials":["Aortic stenosis (pulsus parvus et tardus, sustained apex, soft A2)","Aortic regurgitation (water-hammer pulse, wide pulse pressure, low diastolic pressure)","Tricuspid regurgitation (prominent v wave, systolic hepatic pulsations)","Constrictive pericarditis (Kussmaul's sign, prominent rapid y descent, pericardial knock)","Pericardial tamponade (pulsus paradoxus, blunted y descent)","Atrial septal defect (fixed split S2)","Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (bisferiens pulse, triple apical cadence)","Mitral valve prolapse (nonejection click and dynamic murmur)","Aortic coarctation / dissection (interarm or arm-leg blood pressure differential, femoral delay)"],"infectionMimics":["Infective endocarditis presenting with peripheral stigmata (Janeway lesions, Osler's nodes, splinter hemorrhages, splenomegaly, clubbing, Roth-type funduscopic findings)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Connective tissue disease syndromes producing thoracic cage and skeletal abnormalities (Marfan, Loeys-Dietz, ankylosing spondylitis, straight back syndrome) that suggest valvular or aortic disease","Hemochromatosis (bronze skin) as a cause of systolic heart failure","Sarcoidosis (lupus pernio, erythema nodosum) as a cause of dilated cardiomyopathy with conduction disease","Hereditary lipid disorders (tendon, eruptive, or palmar xanthomas; lipemia retinalis) signaling atherosclerotic risk","Noncardiac causes of dyspnea such as obstructive lung disease (barrel chest, pursed-lip breathing)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Noninvasive imaging (echocardiography, including hand-held ultrasound) to confirm structural cardiac disease","Screening abdominal ultrasound when an abdominal aortic aneurysm is suspected by palpation","24-hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring to detect white coat and masked hypertension","Ankle-brachial index measurement for peripheral arterial disease and cardiovascular risk","Funduscopic examination of the microvasculature in atherosclerosis, hypertension, diabetes, suspected endocarditis, and acute visual change","Allen's test before radial artery instrumentation","Pulse oximetry comparing finger and toe oxygen saturation for lower extremity peripheral arterial disease"],"redFlags":["Markedly elevated JVP that does not pulsate with a dusky cyanotic head and neck, suggesting superior vena cava syndrome","Pulsus paradoxus with elevated JVP and blunted y descent, suggesting pericardial tamponade","An interarm systolic blood pressure difference >10 mmHg or femoral pulse delay, raising concern for aortic dissection or coarctation","Differential cyanosis of the lower extremities with a large patent ductus and pulmonary hypertension (right-to-left shunting)","New peripheral embolic stigmata with fever pointing toward infective endocarditis","Orthostatic hypotension without compensatory tachycardia, suggesting autonomic insufficiency"],"prognosis":"Bedside findings carry independent prognostic weight. In heart failure, an elevated JVP and a third heart sound predict the pulmonary artery wedge pressure and are associated with higher risk of heart failure hospitalization and death; an estimated right atrial pressure >10 mmHg predicted a wedge pressure >22 mmHg with high positive value in advanced heart failure. The ankle-brachial index is a powerful predictor of long-term cardiovascular mortality, and higher ambulatory systolic pressures independently predict cardiovascular events and all-cause death. Pulsus alternans indicates severe left ventricular systolic dysfunction, and when accompanied by electrocardiographic T-wave alternans the arrhythmic risk appears increased.","pitfalls":["Using an inappropriately small blood pressure cuff overestimates pressure, while an oversized cuff underestimates it; the bladder should be about 80% of arm length and 40% of arm circumference.","Examining the carotid upstrokes simultaneously, or before auscultating for a bruit, and applying heavy pressure can precipitate carotid hypersensitivity and syncope in susceptible elderly patients.","A cervical bruit correlates poorly with the degree of carotid stenosis, and its absence does not exclude significant obstruction.","Palpation alone is insufficiently accurate to diagnose an abdominal aortic aneurysm, and sensitivity falls with increasing body size.","Homans' sign is neither sensitive nor specific for deep venous thrombosis.","Arcus senilis lacks specificity as a marker of coronary heart disease risk.","Physician-measured blood pressures tend to be higher than nurse- or self-recorded values, contributing to white coat hypertension; conversely masked hypertension can be missed when clinic pressures are normal despite target organ damage.","Heart and pulse findings can be misleading in low cardiac output states and in stiffened arteries from aging, hypertension, or peripheral arterial disease."],"severityClassification":"No formal grading scale; specific findings convey severity within individual conditions (e.g., pulsus parvus et tardus and a soft A2 indicate severe aortic stenosis; the A2-opening snap interval in mitral stenosis is inversely proportional to the left atrial-left ventricular gradient; a JVP distance >4.5 cm above the sternal angle at 30 degrees, or any venous pulsation above the clavicle in the sitting position, is abnormal; orthostatic hypotension is a fall in systolic pressure >20 mmHg or diastolic >10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1859","Harrison 22e p.1860","Harrison 22e p.1861","Harrison 22e p.1862","Harrison 22e p.1863","Harrison 22e p.1864","Harrison 22e p.1865"],"crossLinks":[]},"physiologic_and_nonphysiologic_sinus_rhyth":{"name":"Physiologic and Nonphysiologic Sinus Rhythm","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sinus tachycardia","Sinus arrhythmia","Sinus bradycardia","Inappropriate sinus tachycardia","IST","Postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome","POTS"],"chapter":254,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1935","clinicalPearls":["The sinus node sits at the lateral-superior junction of the right atrium and superior vena cava, within the upper portion of the crista terminalis where the smooth posterior wall (from the sinus venosus) meets the trabeculated anterior right atrium.","Normal sinus rhythm runs between roughly 60 and 100 beats/min, with a sinus P wave that is upright in leads II, III, and aVF, negative in aVR, and initially positive/biphasic in V1, reflecting an inferiorly and leftward-directed frontal-plane axis.","Sinus arrhythmia is a common, usually asymptomatic finding defined as a sinus-origin rhythm with P-P interval variation greater than 120 ms; when P-wave morphology also varies, premature atrial contractions rather than true sinus arrhythmia are the likely cause.","Three patterns of sinus arrhythmia are described: respirophasic (vagal tone reflexively falls in inspiration and is restored in expiration), ventriculophasic (seen with heart block or after a PVC, where an interposed ventricular beat shortens the P-P interval), and nonphasic (P-P variation unrelated to cardiac or respiratory cycle).","Sinus tachycardia (>100 beats/min) arises from sympathetic stimulation and/or vagal withdrawal; the earliest activation focus shifts more leftward toward the superior septal crista terminalis, producing taller inferior-lead P waves than in resting sinus rhythm.","Sinus bradycardia is defined as a rate <60 beats/min but can be entirely normal during sleep and in physically fit individuals.","A gradual rate rise with an identifiable trigger (such as exertion) favors sinus tachycardia, whereas abrupt onset and offset favor focal atrial tachycardia originating near the sinus node.","Inappropriate sinus tachycardia (IST) is an uncommon disorder of autonomic dysregulation in which the sinus rate climbs at rest or out of proportion to physiologic demand; it most often affects women in their third or fourth decade.","POTS is defined by symptomatic sinus tachycardia on standing, with a rate increase of at least 30 beats/min or to >120 beats/min within 10 minutes of standing in the absence of hypotension.","POTS can follow a viral illness and may resolve spontaneously over 3-12 months; a POTS-like autonomic picture has been described in prolonged post-COVID-19 (long COVID) syndromes.","Distinguishing IST from POTS is clinically critical because they require fundamentally different management approaches; conflating them risks worsening symptoms.","Asymptomatic sinus arrhythmia is physiologic, is not considered pathogenic, and does not warrant further cardiac evaluation."],"pathophysiology":"Sinus rhythm originates from spontaneous depolarization of pacemaker cells in the sinus node at the right atrium-SVC junction within the crista terminalis. Heart rate is modulated by the autonomic balance between sympathetic input (which accelerates and shifts the activation focus) and parasympathetic (vagal) input (which slows the node). Sinus arrhythmia reflects normal beat-to-beat shifts in autonomic tone, classically respiratory-linked vagal modulation. Sinus tachycardia results from increased sympathetic drive and/or vagal withdrawal raising the rate of spontaneous nodal depolarization; when this is an appropriate response to exercise, stress, or illness it is physiologic. Inappropriate sinus tachycardia is thought to stem from an imbalance between sympathetic and parasympathetic input to the node, altered membrane automaticity of sinus node cells, or both, though the precise mechanism remains poorly defined. POTS reflects orthostatic autonomic dysfunction, sometimes postviral.","additionalDifferentials":["Focal atrial tachycardia arising near the sinus node (abrupt onset/offset favors AT)","Premature atrial contractions causing rhythm irregularity (varying P-wave morphology)","Inappropriate sinus tachycardia versus appropriate physiologic sinus tachycardia","Postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome","Anxiety or panic disorder presenting with sinus tachycardia"],"infectionMimics":["Fever or systemic infection driving appropriate (physiologic) sinus tachycardia","Postviral autonomic dysfunction producing a POTS-like picture, including post-COVID-19 (long COVID)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Anemia","Hyperthyroidism and other thyroid dysfunction","Dehydration / volume depletion","Pulmonary embolism","Pheochromocytoma","Decompensated heart failure or valvular disease","Anxiety / panic attack","Stimulant or drug effect (caffeine, sympathomimetics, cocaine, amphetamines, beta-blocker withdrawal)"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG to confirm sinus P-wave morphology and axis","Ambulatory / outpatient telemetry monitoring to distinguish gradual sinus tachycardia from paroxysmal atrial tachycardia","Thyroid function testing to exclude hyperthyroidism as a reversible cause","Hemoglobin / evaluation for anemia","Orthostatic (supine-to-standing) heart rate and blood pressure assessment when POTS is suspected","Invasive electrophysiology study in selected cases where the ECG distinction remains unclear"],"redFlags":["Sinus tachycardia accompanied by hypotension, impaired consciousness, angina, or heart failure, suggesting a serious underlying cause requiring urgent attention","Unexplained or persistent sinus tachycardia mandating exclusion of pulmonary embolism, myocardial ischemia, and structural/functional cardiac disease before labeling it nonphysiologic","Syncope or disabling palpitations accompanying tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia as a possible marker of underlying systemic illness (e.g., infection, malignancy, endocrine disorder) or drug toxicity"],"prognosis":"Sinus arrhythmia is benign and physiologic, requiring no treatment. Physiologic sinus tachycardia resolves with correction of the underlying condition and often needs no specific therapy. Inappropriate sinus tachycardia tends to be a chronic, often disabling condition that responds poorly to therapy and may be difficult to control long term. POTS frequently improves over time, and postviral POTS may resolve spontaneously within 3-12 months.","pitfalls":["Mislabeling rhythm irregularity with varying P-wave morphology as sinus arrhythmia when it is actually premature atrial contractions.","Diagnosing nonphysiologic sinus tachycardia before excluding reversible drivers such as anemia, thyroid disease, and pulmonary embolism.","Failing to separate inappropriate sinus tachycardia from POTS, since blood-pressure-raising strategies appropriate for POTS are inappropriate for IST, and sinus node modification is ineffective for POTS and may worsen its nontachycardia features.","Pursuing catheter-based sinus node modification for IST, which often yields poor long-term symptom control and can lead to symptomatic sinus bradycardia, sinus arrest, or chronotropic incompetence requiring a permanent pacemaker.","Overtreating an asymptomatic, constitutionally elevated resting or exercise sinus rate with no identifiable underlying cause."],"severityClassification":"Sinus tachycardia is rate-defined as >100 beats/min and sinus bradycardia as <60 beats/min, with normal sinus rhythm spanning 60-100 beats/min. POTS is defined diagnostically by an orthostatic heart-rate rise of >=30 beats/min (or to >120 beats/min) within 10 minutes of standing without hypotension. Sinus arrhythmia is defined by P-P interval variability >120 ms of sinus origin.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1935","Harrison 22e p.1936","Harrison 22e p.1937"],"crossLinks":[]},"pilonidal_sinus":{"name":"Pilonidal sinus","system":"Skin / Soft tissue / Surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pilonidal disease","sacrococcygeal sinus","jeep bottom","pilonidal abscess"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Occurs in the natal cleft over the sacrococcygeal region, presenting with discharge, recurrent swelling, or an acute painful abscess","Characteristic midline pits in the natal cleft, often containing tufts of hair, are the key clinical sign","Predominantly affects young adults, more often men, and is associated with hirsutism, obesity, prolonged sitting, and poor local hygiene","An acute pilonidal abscess is treated by incision and drainage, usually off the midline, to relieve sepsis","Chronic or recurrent disease is managed electively by a range of options from pit-picking and laying open to wide excision with midline or off-midline (flap) closure","Off-midline closure and flap techniques generally give lower recurrence and better healing than midline closure","Recurrence is common, and meticulous hair control and hygiene reduce the risk","Long-standing, non-healing, or atypical sinuses rarely undergo malignant change and should be biopsied if suspicious"],"pathophysiology":"Pilonidal disease is now regarded as an acquired condition in which loose hairs are driven into the skin of the natal cleft, provoking a foreign-body inflammatory reaction and forming midline pits. The pits become epithelialised sinuses that trap further hair and debris, leading to chronic infection, abscess formation, and secondary tracks extending laterally. Friction, sweating, deep natal clefts, hirsutism, and prolonged sitting promote hair insertion and infection. Recurrent cycles of infection and incomplete healing perpetuate the disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Anorectal abscess or fistula-in-ano tracking posteriorly","Hidradenitis suppurativa","Infected sebaceous or epidermoid cyst","Perianal Crohn's disease","Sacrococcygeal osteomyelitis with sinus","Presacral dermoid or teratoma with sinus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical inspection for midline pits and lateral tracks","Examination to distinguish from anal fistula (relationship to the anal canal)","MRI in complex or recurrent disease or when a deeper or connecting track is suspected","Biopsy of chronic non-healing lesions to exclude malignancy"],"redFlags":["Spreading cellulitis or systemic sepsis from an untreated abscess","A chronic non-healing indurated sinus raising concern for malignant change","Extensive or recurrent disease in an immunosuppressed or diabetic patient","Features suggesting an alternative diagnosis such as anal fistula or Crohn's disease"],"prognosis":"Acute abscesses settle with drainage, but pilonidal disease is prone to recurrence. Definitive excisional and flap-based procedures improve healing and lower recurrence, and long-term outcome is strongly influenced by ongoing hair control and hygiene.","pitfalls":["Midline closure of large wounds, which heals poorly and recurs frequently","Mistaking a posteriorly tracking anal fistula for pilonidal disease","Neglecting hair control and hygiene advice, allowing recurrence","Failing to biopsy a chronic non-healing sinus"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"pituitary_apoplexy":{"name":"Pituitary apoplexy","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pituitary tumour apoplexy","haemorrhagic pituitary infarction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pituitary apoplexy is a neuroendocrine emergency caused by sudden haemorrhage or infarction, usually within a pre-existing pituitary adenoma","It presents abruptly with severe headache, visual disturbance, ophthalmoplegia, and often nausea, vomiting, and reduced consciousness","Visual field loss (classically bitemporal from optic chiasm compression) and cranial nerve palsies affecting eye movements arise from expansion into the suprasellar region and cavernous sinus","The most dangerous complication is acute secondary adrenal insufficiency from ACTH deficiency, which can cause life-threatening hypotension and hyponatraemia","Empirical stress-dose corticosteroid cover is a cornerstone of initial management once apoplexy is suspected, before biochemical confirmation delays treatment","MRI is the preferred imaging to demonstrate the pituitary lesion and haemorrhage, as CT may miss subtle changes","Urgent surgical decompression is considered for severe or deteriorating visual loss or reduced consciousness, while selected stable cases may be managed conservatively with close monitoring","Long-term follow-up is needed because many patients develop persistent hormone deficiencies requiring replacement"],"pathophysiology":"Pituitary apoplexy results from acute haemorrhage, infarction, or both within the pituitary gland, most commonly in an existing (often previously undiagnosed) adenoma. Rapid expansion of the gland within the confined sella and suprasellar space compresses adjacent structures: upward pressure on the optic chiasm impairs vision, and lateral extension into the cavernous sinus affects the oculomotor, trochlear, abducens, and trigeminal branches. Sudden loss of functioning pituitary tissue precipitates hypopituitarism, with acute corticotroph failure being the immediate threat to life. Leakage of blood and necrotic material can also cause meningism.","additionalDifferentials":["Subarachnoid haemorrhage","Bacterial meningitis","Migraine or other primary headache","Cavernous sinus thrombosis","Ischaemic or haemorrhagic stroke","Ruptured intracranial aneurysm"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent MRI of the pituitary and sella to confirm haemorrhage or infarction and assess chiasmal compression","CT head when MRI is unavailable or to exclude subarachnoid haemorrhage","Baseline anterior pituitary hormone panel and serum cortisol before or alongside starting steroids","Serum electrolytes to detect hyponatraemia and monitor for diabetes insipidus","Formal visual acuity and visual field assessment"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe headache with visual loss or ophthalmoplegia indicating an endocrine and neurosurgical emergency","Haemodynamic instability and hyponatraemia from acute adrenal insufficiency requiring immediate corticosteroids","Rapidly progressive or severe visual field loss needing urgent surgical decompression","Declining conscious level or signs of raised intracranial pressure"],"prognosis":"With prompt corticosteroid cover and, when indicated, timely decompression, visual and neurological recovery is often good; however, permanent hypopituitarism is common and delayed recognition of adrenal crisis can be fatal.","pitfalls":["Withholding stress-dose steroids while awaiting hormone results in a suspected case","Relying on CT alone and missing the diagnosis when MRI would reveal the lesion","Mistaking the presentation for subarachnoid haemorrhage or meningitis and delaying endocrine treatment","Failing to arrange long-term endocrine follow-up for evolving hormone deficiencies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"pituitary_tumor_syndromes":{"name":"Pituitary Tumor Syndromes","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pituitary adenoma","Prolactinoma","Hyperprolactinemia","Acromegaly","Pituitary gigantism","Cushing's disease","ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma","Nonfunctioning pituitary adenoma","Gonadotropinoma","Sellar mass"],"chapter":392,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2998","clinicalPearls":["Pituitary adenomas are benign monoclonal neoplasms arising from one of the five anterior pituitary cell types; the secreted hormone (PRL, GH, ACTH, TSH, or LH/FSH) determines the clinical syndrome. About one-third are clinically nonfunctioning, most of these originating from gonadotrope or null cells (p.3003-3004).","Hormone output does not reliably track tumor size: small secretory microadenomas can be highly symptomatic, whereas large adenomas producing little hormone may stay clinically silent until mass effects appear (p.3003).","Suprasellar extension classically compresses the optic chiasm to produce bitemporal hemianopia, often worse superiorly, because crossing nasal ganglion-cell fibers in the ventral chiasm are most vulnerable; loss of red color perception can be an early sign (p.2998-2999).","The 'stalk section' phenomenon: any sellar mass that compresses the pituitary stalk and portal vessels causes early hyperprolactinemia (typically 30-100 ug/L) with later loss of other anterior pituitary hormones, even when the mass itself is not a prolactinoma (p.2998-2999).","Hyperprolactinemia is the most common pituitary hypersecretion syndrome; PRL levels above 200 ug/L almost always indicate a prolactin-secreting adenoma, while milder elevations are more often drug-induced, stalk-related, or due to hypothyroidism or renal failure (p.3004-3005).","Microprolactinomas show a marked female predominance (~20:1) and present early with amenorrhea/galactorrhea/infertility, whereas macroprolactinomas have a near 1:1 sex ratio and men tend to present later with larger, invasive tumors and mass effects (p.3005-3006).","Acromegaly is typically indolent and may go undiagnosed for a decade or more; acral and soft-tissue overgrowth (frontal bossing, prognathism, enlarged hands/feet, coarse features) plus visceromegaly are characteristic, and GH excess before epiphyseal closure causes gigantism (p.3008-3009).","Random single GH measurement is not diagnostic for acromegaly because of GH pulsatility; an age- and sex-matched IGF-1 is the screening test, and diagnosis is confirmed by failure of GH to suppress below threshold after an oral glucose load (p.3009).","Cushing's disease (corticotrope adenoma) accounts for ~70% of endogenous Cushing's syndrome, is 5-10 times more common in women, and the tumors are usually small microadenomas, about half undetectable on MRI (p.3010-3011).","Iatrogenic glucocorticoid use remains the most common overall cause of cushingoid features and must be excluded by history before pursuing an endogenous workup (p.3010).","ACTH-secreting adenomas retain partial suppressibility by high-dose glucocorticoids, distinguishing them from ectopic ACTH sources; inferior petrosal sinus sampling with CRH (central:peripheral ratio >2 basal, >3 post-CRH) has >95% sensitivity for localizing a pituitary source (p.3011-3012).","Nonfunctioning/gonadotrope adenomas are the most common adenoma type at presentation and are usually macroadenomas discovered through mass effect or as incidentalomas; mildly elevated PRL from stalk compression must not be mistaken for a true prolactinoma since these tumors do not shrink with dopamine agonists (p.3012-3013).","Most pituitary glands harbor an unsuspected microadenoma at autopsy (up to one-quarter), and imaging detects clinically inapparent lesions (incidentalomas) in at least 10% of people; small nonsecretory intrasellar lesions can be safely followed with serial MRI (p.2999, p.3003)."],"pathophysiology":"Pituitary adenomas are benign, almost always monoclonal neoplasms derived from a single anterior pituitary cell lineage, implying acquisition of somatic mutation(s) conferring a growth advantage. Hormonally active tumors secrete autonomously with reduced responsiveness to physiologic feedback. Several molecular events are implicated: about 35% of GH-secreting tumors carry activating Gs-alpha mutations that lower GTPase activity, raise cyclic AMP, and drive somatotrope proliferation; growth factors (bFGF, EGFR signaling, the latter linked to USP8 mutation in some ACTH tumors), activated oncogenes (RAS, PTTG), loss of suppressors (e.g., MEG3), and loss of negative feedback (as in primary hypothyroidism/hypogonadism) also contribute. Hypothalamic hormones GHRH and CRH can additionally stimulate mitotic activity, so ectopic GHRH/CRH-producing chest or abdominal tumors can cause somatotrope or corticotrope hyperplasia. Familial settings include MEN 1 (MENIN, 11q13), MEN 4 (CDKN1B), Carney complex (PRKAR1A), McCune-Albright (mosaic Gs-alpha), and familial acromegaly (AIP). Clinical effects arise both from hormone hyper-/hyposecretion and from local mass effect: dorsal expansion lifts the optic chiasm, stalk compression disrupts dopaminergic inhibition of lactotropes (hyperprolactinemia) and portal hormone delivery, and lateral extension into the cavernous sinus impairs cranial nerves III, IV, VI and trigeminal branches.","additionalDifferentials":["Craniopharyngioma (suprasellar cystic, often calcified, Rathke's pouch origin; BRAF V600E in papillary type)","Meningioma (enhancing, may show bony hyperostosis/calcification)","Rathke's cleft cyst","Sellar/clival chordoma","Hypothalamic hamartoma or gangliocytoma (may secrete GnRH/GHRH/CRH)","Hypothalamic and optic gliomas","Parasellar germ cell tumors (germinoma, dysgerminoma, teratoma, choriocarcinoma)","Pituitary metastases (esp. breast and lung; favor posterior pituitary)","Lymphoma, leukemia, plasmacytoma involving the sella","Lymphocytic hypophysitis","Langerhans cell histiocytosis / Histiocytosis X","Empty sella","Arachnoid cyst","Macroprolactinemia (biologically inactive aggregated PRL causing falsely high PRL)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Drug-induced hyperprolactinemia (antipsychotics esp. risperidone, metoclopramide, methyldopa, verapamil, estrogens, opiates, H2 blockers)","Primary hypothyroidism causing reflex hyperprolactinemia","Chronic renal failure and cirrhosis (reduced PRL clearance)","Pregnancy, lactation, chest-wall stimulation, stress and sleep as physiologic PRL elevation","Polycystic ovarian syndrome (overlap with FSH/LH-secreting tumor causing ovarian hyperstimulation)","Iatrogenic/exogenous glucocorticoid excess mimicking Cushing's disease","Ectopic ACTH or ectopic CRH secretion mimicking Cushing's disease","Adrenal adenoma/carcinoma or adrenal hyperplasia as ACTH-independent Cushing's syndrome","Ectopic GHRH (bronchial/abdominal carcinoid) or rare ectopic GH causing acromegaly"],"additionalInvestigations":["Sagittal and coronal T1-weighted MRI before and after gadolinium (adenomas are hypodense on T1, brighter on T2); CT reserved for bony erosion or calcification","Formal perimetric visual field testing for any chiasm-impinging mass","Baseline pituitary hormonal screen: prolactin, IGF-1, 24-h urinary free cortisol and/or overnight 1-mg dexamethasone suppression, alpha-subunit/FSH/LH, and thyroid function tests","Acromegaly: IGF-1 (age/sex matched) and oral glucose tolerance test with GH at 0/30/60 min (failure to suppress confirms diagnosis)","Hyperprolactinemia: fasting morning PRL with sample dilution when very high (assay 'hook' artifact) and exclusion of macroprolactin","Cushing's disease: 24-h urinary free cortisol, overnight/low- and high-dose dexamethasone suppression, late-night salivary or midnight serum cortisol, basal ACTH, CRH stimulation","Bilateral inferior petrosal sinus ACTH sampling with CRH to localize an ACTH source when MRI is non-diagnostic","Gonadotropinoma/nonfunctioning: FSH, LH, free alpha-subunit, sex steroids; TRH stimulation may provoke atypical gonadotropin/subunit rise","TSH-secreting adenoma: free T4, free T3, TSH, alpha-subunit (inappropriately normal/high TSH with high free T4/T3)","Immunohistochemistry for hormones and cell-specific transcription factors on resected tissue for histologic confirmation"],"redFlags":["Progressive visual field loss or new bitemporal hemianopia (chiasmal compression)","Cranial nerve III/IV/VI palsy, diplopia, ptosis, or facial numbness indicating cavernous sinus invasion","Severe sudden headache with visual loss or ophthalmoplegia suggesting pituitary apoplexy (intratumoral hemorrhage/infarction)","Signs of raised intracranial pressure: headache, vomiting, papilledema, hydrocephalus","Arginine vasopressin deficiency (polyuria) as a presenting feature, which suggests posterior pituitary involvement or a non-adenomatous lesion such as metastasis or germ cell tumor","Rapid-onset hypercortisolism with hyperpigmentation, severe myopathy, and hypokalemic alkalosis suggesting an ectopic ACTH source","Features of acute adrenal insufficiency from coexisting hypopituitarism"],"prognosis":"Pituitary tumors are overwhelmingly benign and slow growing; true carcinomas with extracranial metastases are exceedingly rare. Outcomes depend on tumor type and size and require lifelong follow-up. Prolactinomas respond well to dopamine agonists with normoprolactinemia and gonadal recovery in roughly 80% of microadenomas and tumor shrinkage in most macroadenomas; about 20% (especially men) are dopamine-resistant, and hyperprolactinemia may resolve spontaneously in up to 30% of hyperprolactinemic patients without a visible adenoma. Surgical PRL normalization reaches ~70% for microprolactinomas but only ~40% for macroadenomas, with substantial long-term recurrence (>50% for macroadenomas). In acromegaly, untreated GH excess roughly triples mortality and shortens survival by about 10 years, driven by cardiovascular, cerebrovascular, and respiratory disease and increased colon cancer risk; transsphenoidal remission is ~70% for microadenomas and under 50% for macroadenomas. Cushing's disease has a microadenoma surgical remission of ~80% (<50% for macroadenomas), with ~5% biochemical recurrence and a postoperative period of ACTH deficiency. Untreated hypercortisolism carries excess mortality, chiefly cardiovascular, plus increased infection and suicide risk.","pitfalls":["Assuming PRL suppression by a dopamine agonist proves a prolactinoma: stalk-compression hyperprolactinemia from a nonfunctioning mass also falls with dopamine agonists even though the mass does not shrink.","Very high prolactin levels (>1000 ug/L) may be reported falsely low due to assay 'hook' artifact unless the sample is diluted, leading to underestimation of macroprolactinoma.","Falsely elevated PRL from biologically inactive macroprolactin can prompt unnecessary evaluation.","Diagnosing acromegaly on a single random GH level is unreliable because of GH pulsatility; IGF-1 and a glucose-suppression test are required.","Attributing every small pituitary lesion to a secretory tumor: incidental microadenomas are very common, and ~50% of ACTH adenomas are MRI-occult, so imaging cannot reliably localize the ACTH source.","Overlap in basal ACTH levels between pituitary and ectopic ACTH sources means ACTH measurement alone cannot distinguish them; dynamic testing or petrosal sampling is needed.","Mistaking iatrogenic glucocorticoid excess for endogenous Cushing's syndrome.","Headache severity correlates poorly with adenoma size; a small intrasellar tumor can cause significant headache while a large tumor may not."],"severityClassification":"Pituitary adenomas are conventionally classified by size into microadenomas (<10 mm) and macroadenomas (>=10 mm, often locally invasive with parasellar extension), and by secretory phenotype into functioning (lactotrope/PRL, somatotrope/GH, corticotrope/ACTH, thyrotrope/TSH, gonadotrope/FSH-LH, or plurihormonal) versus clinically nonfunctioning (frequently gonadotrope or null-cell). Sellar mass severity is further graded by anatomic impact in Table 392-1 (compression of pituitary, optic chiasm, hypothalamus, cavernous sinus, frontal lobe, or brain), as larger/invasive tumors carry higher surgical risk and lower cure rates.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2998","Harrison 22e p.2999","Harrison 22e p.3000","Harrison 22e p.3001","Harrison 22e p.3002","Harrison 22e p.3003","Harrison 22e p.3004","Harrison 22e p.3005","Harrison 22e p.3006","Harrison 22e p.3007","Harrison 22e p.3008","Harrison 22e p.3009","Harrison 22e p.3010","Harrison 22e p.3011","Harrison 22e p.3012","Harrison 22e p.3013"],"crossLinks":[]},"pityriasis_rosea":{"name":"Pityriasis Rosea","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PR","pityriasis rosea of Gibert"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A single larger oval plaque (the herald patch) commonly precedes the widespread eruption by days to a couple of weeks","The secondary rash consists of smaller oval, salmon-coloured plaques with a fine trailing collarette of scale at the inner edge of the border","Lesions align along skin cleavage lines on the trunk, producing the classic Christmas-tree pattern over the back","It is often preceded or accompanied by a mild viral prodrome and is self-limiting, so reassurance is usually the mainstay of management","Symptomatic itch can be eased with emollients, mild topical anti-inflammatory agents, and antihistamines; some evidence supports sunlight or phototherapy for extensive or itchy disease","Because a widespread scaly eruption on the trunk can mimic secondary syphilis, palmar and plantar involvement or risk factors should prompt syphilis serology","Consider a drug-induced pityriasis rosea-like reaction when the history includes a recently started medication or when features are atypical","Counsel pregnant patients appropriately, as some data suggest an association between early-pregnancy disease and adverse outcomes"],"pathophysiology":"Pityriasis rosea is widely regarded as a self-limiting exanthem linked to reactivation of human herpesviruses 6 and 7, supported by the viral prodrome, seasonal clustering, and detection of viral markers in some patients. The eruption reflects a T-cell-mediated inflammatory response in the skin producing spongiotic dermatitis with focal parakeratosis, which accounts for the fine collarette of scale. The alignment of lesions along Langer's cleavage lines gives the distinctive distribution. A clinically indistinguishable pityriasis rosea-like eruption can be triggered by certain drugs and vaccines through a similar hypersensitivity mechanism.","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary syphilis (especially with palm and sole involvement)","Guttate psoriasis","Tinea corporis (the herald patch can be mistaken for ringworm)","Nummular eczema","Drug-induced pityriasis rosea-like eruption","Pityriasis lichenoides"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis is clinical based on the herald patch and characteristic distribution","Syphilis serology when the presentation is atypical or there are risk factors, or when palms and soles are involved","Mycology to exclude tinea when a solitary herald patch is uncertain","Review of medication history to identify a possible drug-induced eruption","Skin biopsy reserved for persistent or diagnostically unclear cases"],"redFlags":["Involvement of the palms and soles or a suggestive sexual history pointing to secondary syphilis","Failure to resolve within the expected few months, prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Atypical morphology or a temporal link to a new drug suggesting a drug-induced reaction","Early-pregnancy disease, given a possible association with adverse pregnancy outcomes"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and self-limiting, typically clearing over several weeks to a few months without scarring; post-inflammatory pigment change may persist for a while, especially in darker skin, and recurrence is uncommon.","pitfalls":["Overlooking secondary syphilis in a widespread scaly truncal eruption","Mistaking the herald patch for tinea and treating with antifungals alone","Failing to link an atypical eruption to a recently started drug","Over-investigating or over-treating a self-limiting condition instead of reassuring the patient"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"placenta_previa":{"name":"Placenta praevia","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["placenta previa","low-lying placenta","praevia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Placenta praevia is placental implantation over or very close to the internal cervical os, classically presenting as painless bright-red vaginal bleeding in the second half of pregnancy","Digital vaginal examination must be avoided when praevia is suspected because it can provoke catastrophic haemorrhage; ultrasound is the diagnostic tool","Many low-lying placentas identified early in pregnancy migrate upward as the lower segment develops, so diagnosis is confirmed on later transvaginal scanning","Risk factors include previous caesarean section, prior praevia, multiparity, advanced maternal age, multiple pregnancy, and assisted conception","Prior caesarean scars raise the risk of associated placenta accreta spectrum, which should be actively sought on imaging","The fetus often lies in an abnormal (transverse or breech) presentation because the placenta occupies the lower segment","Delivery is by caesarean section for significant praevia; timing and setting are planned to allow for major haemorrhage","Management of bleeding episodes includes resuscitation, blood availability, corticosteroids for fetal lung maturity when preterm, and anti-D for rhesus-negative women"],"pathophysiology":"In placenta praevia the placenta implants in the lower uterine segment overlying or adjacent to the internal os. As the lower segment stretches and the cervix effaces in later pregnancy and labour, the inelastic placental attachment is disrupted from the underlying decidua, tearing maternal vessels and causing painless bleeding. Because the lower segment contracts poorly after delivery, the placental bed is prone to continued haemorrhage. Prior uterine scarring predisposes to abnormally adherent or invasive placentation (accreta spectrum).","additionalDifferentials":["Placental abruption","Vasa praevia","Local causes such as cervical ectropion, polyp, or cervical carcinoma","Bloody show of labour","Uterine rupture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transabdominal and confirmatory transvaginal ultrasound to localise the placenta relative to the os","Colour Doppler or MRI to evaluate for placenta accreta spectrum, especially with prior caesarean","Full blood count, group and save or cross-match according to bleeding","Coagulation studies if haemorrhage is significant","Cardiotocography to assess fetal wellbeing during bleeding episodes"],"redFlags":["Heavy or recurrent vaginal bleeding causing maternal haemodynamic instability","Signs of hypovolaemic shock requiring urgent resuscitation and delivery","Imaging features of placenta accreta, increta, or percreta","Non-reassuring fetal heart pattern during a bleed","Bleeding with uterine contractions suggesting onset of labour with praevia"],"prognosis":"With antenatal diagnosis, planned caesarean delivery, and ready access to blood products, maternal and perinatal outcomes are generally good. Uncontrolled haemorrhage, particularly with coexisting accreta spectrum, remains a major cause of maternal morbidity, massive transfusion, and peripartum hysterectomy.","pitfalls":["Performing a digital vaginal examination before excluding praevia","Labelling an early low-lying placenta as definitive praevia without rescanning later","Overlooking placenta accreta spectrum in women with previous caesarean and anterior praevia","Underestimating blood loss and failing to prepare adequate transfusion support","Attributing painless antepartum bleeding to a benign local cause without imaging"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"placental_abruption":{"name":"Placental abruption","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["abruptio placentae","placental abruptio","premature separation of the placenta"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Abruption is premature separation of a normally sited placenta before delivery, classically presenting with painful vaginal bleeding and a tender, firm, or hypertonic uterus","Bleeding may be concealed behind the placenta, so the degree of vaginal blood loss can badly underestimate the true haemorrhage","Risk factors include hypertension and pre-eclampsia, trauma, cocaine or tobacco use, prior abruption, polyhydramnios with rapid decompression, and thrombophilia","Fetal compromise or death can occur rapidly because the separated placenta cannot support gas exchange","It is largely a clinical diagnosis; ultrasound is insensitive and a normal scan does not exclude abruption","Disseminated intravascular coagulation is an important complication, particularly with large abruptions or fetal demise","Management depends on maternal and fetal status: resuscitation, and prompt delivery (often by caesarean) when there is fetal distress or maternal instability","Anti-D should be given to rhesus-negative women and blood products used to correct coagulopathy"],"pathophysiology":"Abruption begins with rupture of maternal decidual vessels, causing bleeding into the decidua basalis that splits the placenta from the uterine wall. The expanding retroplacental clot further separates the placenta, reducing the functional area for maternal-fetal exchange and provoking uterine irritability and contraction. Thromboplastins released from the disrupted placenta and decidua enter the maternal circulation and can trigger consumptive coagulopathy (disseminated intravascular coagulation), while extravasation of blood into the myometrium (Couvelaire uterus) impairs contractility and postpartum haemostasis.","additionalDifferentials":["Placenta praevia","Uterine rupture","Preterm labour","Vasa praevia","Local genital tract causes of bleeding","Acute abdomen from a non-obstetric cause"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous cardiotocography to detect fetal compromise and uterine hypertonicity","Full blood count and serial haemoglobin","Coagulation screen including fibrinogen to identify disseminated intravascular coagulation","Group and cross-match for transfusion","Ultrasound mainly to exclude praevia rather than to confirm abruption","Kleihauer test in rhesus-negative women to quantify fetomaternal haemorrhage"],"redFlags":["Maternal haemodynamic instability or shock, which may be disproportionate to visible blood loss due to concealed haemorrhage","Non-reassuring or absent fetal heart activity","Developing coagulopathy with falling fibrinogen and abnormal clotting","A rigid, exquisitely tender uterus with frequent contractions","Rapid clinical deterioration requiring emergency delivery"],"prognosis":"Small abruptions with a stable mother and reassuring fetus may allow expectant management with good outcomes. Large abruptions carry high risks of fetal death, maternal haemorrhage, coagulopathy, and organ injury, and abruption is a leading cause of perinatal mortality and antepartum stillbirth. Recurrence risk in future pregnancies is increased.","pitfalls":["Being falsely reassured by minimal external bleeding when concealed haemorrhage is severe","Relying on ultrasound to rule out abruption","Underestimating and undertreating evolving coagulopathy","Delaying delivery in the face of fetal distress or maternal instability","Failing to consider abruption after seemingly minor abdominal trauma in pregnancy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"plague":{"name":"Plague","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Yersinia pestis"],"chapter":176,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1341-1347","clinicalPearls":["Plague is a systemic zoonosis caused by Yersinia pestis, predominantly affecting small rodents in rural areas of Africa, Asia, and the Americas and usually transmitted to humans by an arthropod (flea) vector; less often via contact with animal tissues or respiratory droplets (p.1341).","Three principal clinical forms: bubonic, septicemic, and pneumonic. Worldwide, bubonic plague is the predominant reported form; a minority (10-20%) present as primary septicemic or pneumonic disease (p.1343).","Bubonic plague: after a 2-6 day incubation, sudden onset of fever (>38 degC), malaise, myalgia, dizziness, and increasing pain from progressive regional lymphadenitis. The bubo is a tense, tender swelling with a boggy consistency and underlying hard core, usually solitary and erythematous with periganglionic edema, most commonly inguinal but may be crural, axillary, cervical, or submaxillary depending on bite site (p.1343).","Children most commonly present with cervical or axillary buboes; abdominal pain from intraabdominal node involvement can occur without other visible signs (p.1343).","Primary pneumonic plague follows inhalation of infectious droplets from another person or animal with plague pneumonia; short incubation (a few hours to 2-3 days, range 1-7 days) with abrupt fever, headache, myalgia, weakness, nausea, vomiting, dizziness; respiratory signs (cough, dyspnea, chest pain, sputum with hemoptysis) typically appear after 24 h (p.1344).","Primary pneumonic plague is generally the least common but most fatal of the main presentations; treated pneumonic plague has an estimated death rate of about 17% versus 98% for untreated pneumonic plague (p.1343).","Primary septicemic plague (10-25% of infections) presents as gram-negative septicemia (hypotension, shock) WITHOUT preceding lymphadenopathy; occurs in all age groups but risk is elevated in persons over 40 (p.1344).","WBC count is generally raised (10,000-20,000/uL) with neutrophilic leukocytosis and left shift; counts can be very high in children (>100,000/uL), but in some cases the WBC is normal or leukopenia develops. DIC occurs in a significant minority (p.1346).","Appropriate treatment of bubonic plague resolves fever within 2-5 days, but buboes may remain enlarged for more than a week after initial treatment and can become fluctuant (p.1344)."],"pathophysiology":"Y. pestis is a gram-negative bacterium of the order Enterobacterales that recently evolved from the enteric pathogen Y. pseudotuberculosis. It has a two-stage life cycle alternating between arthropod (flea) and a wide range of mammalian hosts. In the flea, the organism multiplies and forms biofilm-embedded aggregates in the midgut; some fleas become 'blocked' at the proventriculus and regurgitate bacteria into the bite site, while early-phase unblocked transmission also occurs. In the mammalian host, dissemination from the inoculation site is initially dependent on plasminogen activator (Pla), which activates plasminogen, degrades complement, and binds laminin, and is essential for high-level virulence and bubo formation. Bacteria are rapidly transported to regional lymph nodes where Y. pestis replicates extracellularly with full expression of antiphagocytic systems (type III secretion machinery and effectors encoded by pYV plus the F1 capsule), producing a bubo (hemorrhagic/necrotic node with thrombosed vessels, replaced architecture, and abundant bacteria). LcrV targets phagocytes and exerts an anti-inflammatory effect, and modified lipopolysaccharide minimizes Toll-like receptor 4 stimulation, prolonging high-grade bacteremia. Spread via efferent lymphatics to the thoracic duct produces high-grade bacteremia and hematogenous seeding of spleen, liver, secondary buboes, lungs (secondary plague pneumonia), and meninges, with uncontrolled septicemia leading to death; the septicemic phase can occur without an obvious prior bubo (p.1342-1343).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute focal lymphadenopathy of other etiologies (for bubonic plague): streptococcal or staphylococcal infection, tularemia, cat-scratch disease, tick typhus, infectious mononucleosis, lymphatic filariasis (p.1343).","Tuberculosis (in differential of fever and hemoptysis in plague-endemic areas, as a small-airborne-particle-transmitted infection) (p.1347).","Tonsillitis with peritonsillar abscess (resembles plague pharyngitis) (p.1344)."],"infectionMimics":["Tularemia (Francisella tularensis) (p.1343)","Cat-scratch disease (Bartonella henselae) (p.1343)","Tick typhus (rickettsial) (p.1343)","Infectious mononucleosis (p.1343)","Lymphatic filariasis (p.1343)","Streptococcal or staphylococcal lymphadenitis (p.1343)","Tuberculosis (fever and hemoptysis differential) (p.1347)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bubo aspirate (bubonic), bronchoalveolar lavage fluid or sputum (pneumonic), and blood (septicemic) are the appropriate specimens; culture of postmortem organ biopsy can also be diagnostic. Transport medium such as Cary-Blair preserves viability (p.1345).","Direct microscopy: Gram-negative coccobacilli with characteristic bipolar 'closed safety pin' appearance on Wayson or Wright-Giemsa stain; bipolar bacilli may be visible on direct blood smear in septicemic plague, indicating very high circulating bacterial numbers and a poor prognosis (p.1345).","Culture on nutrient-rich media (sheep blood agar, brain-heart infusion broth) and selective agar (MacConkey, EMB, or Yersinia-specific CIN agar); optimal growth at <37 degC (25-29 degC). Y. pestis is oxidase-negative, catalase-positive, urease-negative, indole-negative, lactose-negative, and nonmotile (distinguishing it from other Yersinia species) (p.1345).","F1 antigen detection: direct immunofluorescence and rapid immunochromatographic test strip (F1RDT), effective on bubo aspirates and sputum and widely used in endemic countries; a Pla antigen strip can detect F1-negative strains (p.1345).","PCR/RT-PCR requiring positivity for at least two Y. pestis-specific targets (e.g., caf1, pla, and chromosomal genes); FDA-authorized BioFire FilmArray panels include Y. pestis (p.1346).","Serology: rising hemagglutinating antibody titer to F1 antigen, or seroconversion/fourfold rise in paired sera; ELISA for IgG and IgM to F1 antigen (p.1345-1346).","WHO case definitions (suspected/probable/confirmed) guide presumptive diagnosis followed by reference laboratory confirmation (p.1345)."],"redFlags":["Pneumonic plague is transmissible person-to-person via respiratory droplets (productive cough), making it a public-health emergency requiring immediate isolation and notification (p.1344, 1347).","Aerosolized Y. pestis is a recognized potential bioterrorism agent (Tier 1 select agent); an outbreak of primary pneumonic plague in a nonendemic region or urban setting should raise concern for intentional release (p.1342, 1344, 1345).","Rapid progression of primary pneumonic plague from segmental pneumonitis to lobar and bilateral involvement with hemoptysis and ARDS within hours to days (p.1344).","Bipolar bacilli visible on direct blood smear indicate very high circulating bacterial numbers and a poor prognosis (p.1345).","Disseminated intravascular coagulation with shock in septicemic disease (p.1346)."],"prognosis":"Untreated plague carries high mortality, but it is treatable with antimicrobials. Treated pneumonic plague has an estimated death rate of about 17% compared with 98% for untreated pneumonic plague. In the United States, case-fatality fell from 66% in 1900-1941 to 16% in the post-antibiotic era (1942 onward); in 2000-2018, mortality was 8% in patients receiving any antimicrobial and 4% in those receiving currently recommended high-efficacy agents. The global case-fatality rate notified to WHO for 2013-2018 was 17%. Visible bipolar bacilli on direct blood smear signal very high bacteremia and poor prognosis (p.1343, 1345).","pitfalls":["The term 'septicemic plague' can be confusing because most patients with buboes have detectable bacteremia at some stage, with or without systemic sepsis signs (p.1344).","Automated biochemical or mass-spectrometry identification systems can misidentify Y. pestis as Y. pseudotuberculosis or other species (p.1345).","Meningeal plague may be associated with suboptimal antimicrobial therapy (delayed treatment, penicillin use, or low-dose tetracycline) (p.1344).","A single negative rapid antigen test is insufficient to exclude plague; WHO exclusion requires two negative F1RDT tests on specimens collected 24 h apart when confirmatory tests are unavailable (p.1345-1346).","F1RDT specificity for culture-confirmed pneumonic plague was only 71%, so a positive sputum test in an endemic area identifies a probable case requiring treatment but needs additional confirmatory testing (p.1345-1346).","Culture yield can be low after prehospital antimicrobial use (as observed in the 2017 Madagascar outbreak) (p.1345)."],"severityClassification":"WHO case classification of plague: Suspected case (clinical presentation suggestive of plague plus an epidemiologic context of possible exposure within 10 days of onset); Probable case (suspected case plus one of: F1 antigen detected by rapid test or immunofluorescence, a single anti-F1 serology without prior infection/immunization, or direct microscopy showing bipolar-staining gram-negative coccobacilli); Confirmed case (suspected case plus one of: isolation of Y. pestis, seroconversion or fourfold anti-F1 titer change in paired sera, or species-specific PCR positivity); Not a case (suspected case with negative confirmatory testing) (p.1345).","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Chapter 176: Plague and Other Yersinia Infections (Michael B. Prentice), pp.1341-1347","Bertherat E, Jullien S: Revision of the international definition of plague cases. Wkly Epidemiol Rec 24:238, 2021","Nelson CA et al: Antimicrobial treatment and prophylaxis of plague: Recommendations for naturally acquired infections and bioterrorism response. MMWR Recomm Rep 70(No. RR-3):1, 2021","Salam AP et al: Deaths associated with pneumonic plague, 1946-2017"],"crossLinks":[]},"plasma_cell_disorders":{"name":"Plasma Cell Disorders (MGUS, Waldenström)","system":"Oncology / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["MGUS","monoclonal gammopathy","Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia","POEMS"],"chapter":116,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.883","clinicalPearls":["Plasma cell disorders are monoclonal neoplasms of late B-lymphocyte lineage; the group includes multiple myeloma (MM), Waldenström's macroglobulinemia, primary (AL) amyloidosis, and the heavy chain diseases, also termed monoclonal gammopathies, paraproteinemias, plasma cell dyscrasias, or dysproteinemias (Harrison 22e p.883).","MGUS is vastly more common than myeloma, occurring in ~1% of the population aged >50 and up to ~10% of those aged >75; no specific intervention is indicated and annual (or less frequent) follow-up is adequate, with closer monitoring only for higher-risk MGUS (Harrison 22e p.887-890).","About ~1% of MGUS patients per year progress to myeloma, but essentially all cases of myeloma are preceded by MGUS (Harrison 22e p.887).","Higher-risk MGUS features are non-IgG subtype, abnormal kappa/lambda free light chain ratio, and serum M-protein >15 g/L (1.5 g/dL); absence of all three predicts ~5% progression while presence of all three predicts ~60% progression over 20 years (Harrison 22e p.887).","Diagnostic workup for a monoclonal protein uses serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) for quantitation and immunoelectrophoresis/immunofixation for confirmation of a single heavy and/or light chain; serum free light chain assay and urine studies (UPEP) detect light-chain-only and Bence Jones disease (Harrison 22e p.883-884).","An M component must be present at ~5 g/L (0.5 g/dL) to be accurately quantitated by electrophoresis; the serum M-protein level is a reliable measure of tumor burden but is not specific enough to screen asymptomatic patients (Harrison 22e p.883-884).","MGUS diagnostic criteria: serum monoclonal protein (non-IgM) <30 g/L, clonal bone marrow plasma cells <10%, and absence of myeloma-defining events or amyloidosis attributable to the plasma cell disorder (Harrison 22e p.888, Table 116-1).","Waldenström's macroglobulinemia (WM) is a malignancy of IgM-secreting lymphoplasmacytoid cells; unlike myeloma it presents with lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly and its major manifestation is hyperviscosity syndrome (Harrison 22e p.893).","A distinct MYD88 L265P somatic mutation is present in >90% of WM patients and the majority of IgM MGUS, and is used as a diagnostic test to discriminate WM from marginal zone lymphoma, IgM-secreting myeloma, and CLL with plasmacytic differentiation (Harrison 22e p.893).","In WM the IgM may have specificity for myelin-associated glycoprotein (MAG); a peripheral neuropathy may precede the neoplasm and about half of these patients are anti-MAG positive (Harrison 22e p.893).","POEMS syndrome = Polyneuropathy, Organomegaly, Endocrinopathy, M-protein, and Skin changes; patients typically have a severe progressive sensorimotor polyneuropathy with sclerotic bone lesions, and polyneuropathy occurs in only ~1.4% of myelomas with POEMS being a rare subset (Harrison 22e p.893).","AL (primary) amyloidosis is a plasma cell disorder most often caused by clonal bone marrow plasma cells secreting a misfolding monoclonal light chain; it can coexist with multiple myeloma, Waldenström's macroglobulinemia, or non-Hodgkin's lymphoma (Harrison 22e ch.117 p.896-897).","Macroglossia and periorbital ('raccoon-eye') ecchymoses are pathognomonic but uncommon signs of AL amyloidosis, each seen in only ~10% of patients (Harrison 22e ch.117 p.897)."],"pathophysiology":"In normal B-cell maturation, antigen exposure drives differentiation of mature B lymphocytes into antibody-secreting plasma cells; in plasma cell disorders this control is lost, producing a monoclonal expansion that secretes a single (monoclonal) immunoglobulin or fragment (M component). Clinical manifestations arise from neoplastic cell expansion, from the secreted products (intact immunoglobulin, free light/heavy chains, lymphokines), and from the host response. In multiple myeloma, recurrent chromosomal lesions include hyperdiploidy and 14q32 translocations (e.g., t(11;14), t(4;14), t(14;16)), with errors in immunoglobulin switch recombination implicated in early transformation. Waldenström's macroglobulinemia arises from a post-germinal-center IgM-bearing memory B cell, driven by MYD88 L265P (TLR/IL-1R IRAK and NF-kB signaling, plus BTK/HCK signaling) and frequently CXCR4 mutations. In AL amyloidosis, clonal plasma cells produce a monoclonal light chain (lambda predominates) that misfolds into insoluble amyloid fibrils deposited extracellularly in tissues (Harrison 22e p.883-884, 893; ch.117 p.896-897).","additionalDifferentials":["Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS)","Smoldering (asymptomatic) multiple myeloma","Symptomatic multiple myeloma (CRAB / myeloma-defining events)","Solitary plasmacytoma of bone or soft tissue","Nonsecretory myeloma","Plasma cell leukemia (>2000 circulating plasma cells/uL)","Waldenström's macroglobulinemia","IgM myeloma (lytic lesions, CD138+ plasma cells, t(11;14))","POEMS syndrome","AL (primary) amyloidosis","Heavy chain diseases (gamma/Franklin's, alpha/Seligmann's and IPSID, mu)","Marginal zone lymphoma and CLL with plasmacytic differentiation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["M components in other lymphoid neoplasms (CLL, B- or T-cell lymphomas)","M components in nonlymphoid neoplasms (chronic myeloid leukemia, breast cancer, colon cancer)","Nonneoplastic conditions: cirrhosis, sarcoidosis, parasitic diseases, Gaucher's disease, pyoderma gangrenosum","Autoimmune conditions: rheumatoid arthritis, myasthenia gravis, cold agglutinin disease","Post organ-transplant immunosuppression (and rarely allogeneic transplant)","Skin diseases with monoclonal gammopathy: lichen myxedematosus (papular mucinosis), necrobiotic xanthogranuloma (~10% progress to myeloma)","Mixed cryoglobulinemia (IgM/IgA complexed with IgG) seen in rheumatoid arthritis and other autoimmune disease, usually not associated with malignancy"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) for M-protein quantitation","Serum and urine immunofixation electrophoresis (SIFE/UIFE) to confirm monoclonality and identify heavy/light chain type","Urine protein electrophoresis (UPEP) / 24-h urine for Bence Jones (free light chain) protein","Serum free light chain assay with kappa/lambda ratio (essential to assess ratio, not just absolute level, since renal insufficiency nonspecifically raises both)","Bone marrow biopsy/aspirate for clonal plasma cell percentage and CD138+ / kappa or lambda light chain restriction by flow cytometry, immunohistochemistry, or in situ hybridization","MYD88 (L265P) and CXCR4 mutation testing to discriminate WM from MZL, IgM myeloma, and CLL","Skeletal survey, CT, or PET-CT for osteolytic lesions; MRI of spine/pelvis for focal lesions","Serum calcium, creatinine/creatinine clearance, and hemoglobin (to assess CRAB end-organ damage)","VEGF level and evaluation for Castleman's disease when POEMS is suspected","Tissue biopsy with Congo red staining and mass spectrometry-based typing when amyloidosis is suspected (SPEP/UPEP alone are inadequate screens for AL)"],"redFlags":["Hyperviscosity syndrome in Waldenström's macroglobulinemia / IgM gammopathy: headache, visual disturbance, retinal vein segmentation and dilation, epistaxis, altered consciousness, paresis (most IgM is intravascular and responds to plasmapheresis)","Myeloma-defining end-organ damage (CRAB): hyperCalcemia, Renal insufficiency, Anemia, Bone lytic lesions","Spinal cord compression: new lower-extremity neurologic deficit, severe localized back pain, or loss of bowel/bladder control requiring emergency MRI and radiation/decompression","Acute renal failure from light chain cast nephropathy, especially with dehydration","Cardiac involvement in AL amyloidosis (70-80%) is the leading cause of death; low-voltage ECG with pseudo-infarct pattern and thickened ventricles on echo","MGUS with severe polyneuropathy (consider causal IgM/anti-MAG neuropathy)"],"prognosis":"MGUS carries a low malignant potential, with ~1% per year progression to myeloma (risk stratified by IgG vs non-IgG subtype, free light chain ratio, and M-protein level: ~5% at 20 years with no risk features vs ~60% with all three). Smoldering myeloma progresses to symptomatic MM in ~25% at 5 years with one risk feature and ~76% with all three high-risk features. Waldenström's macroglobulinemia is often indolent and may not require therapy; median survival is reported as ~50 months, with older age, male sex, general symptoms, and cytopenias defining a high-risk group. AL amyloidosis is often rapidly progressive and fatal if untreated (occurs after age 40); very low dFLC (<50 mg/L) predicts significantly better outcome (Harrison 22e p.887-888, 893-894; ch.117 p.896-898).","pitfalls":["Do not equate a monoclonal protein with malignancy: distinguish MGUS (clonal marrow plasma cells <10%, M-protein <30 g/L, no myeloma-defining events/amyloidosis) from multiple myeloma, which requires marrow plasmacytosis and myeloma-defining events / CRAB end-organ damage (Harrison 22e p.887-888).","SPEP/UPEP are useful in myeloma but are NOT adequate screening tests for AL amyloidosis, because the clonal light chain is often insufficient to produce an M-spike; use serum/urine immunofixation and free light chain assay (>90% positive) (Harrison 22e ch.117 p.897).","A monoclonal protein alone is not diagnostic of amyloidosis since MGUS is common in older patients; when MGUS coexists with biopsy-proven amyloidosis, AL type must be ruled out and 'smoldering myeloma' patients should be screened for AL (Harrison 22e ch.117 p.897).","WM does not cause bone lesions or hypercalcemia (unlike myeloma); large IgM paraprotein causes little renal excretion so renal disease is uncommon (~20% excrete light chains)—reserve the diagnosis of IgM myeloma for lytic bone lesions with CD138+ plasma cells and t(11;14) (Harrison 22e p.893).","Suspected cryoglobulin: draw blood into a warm syringe and transport in warm water to avoid errors in quantitation (Harrison 22e p.893).","Rituximab can produce an IgM flare in WM—use plasmapheresis first or initially withhold rituximab in patients with high IgM levels (Harrison 22e p.894).","Pseudohyponatremia and a decreased anion gap can occur in myeloma because the cationic M component retains chloride and displaces serum water (Harrison 22e p.887)."],"severityClassification":"MGUS vs smoldering (asymptomatic) myeloma vs symptomatic multiple myeloma per IMWG criteria (Harrison Table 116-1). MGUS: non-IgM serum M-protein <30 g/L, clonal marrow plasma cells <10%, no myeloma-defining events/amyloidosis. Smoldering MM: serum M-protein (IgG/IgA) >=30 g/L or urine M-protein >=500 mg/24h and/or clonal marrow plasma cells 10-60%, without myeloma-defining events. Symptomatic MM: clonal plasma cells (or plasmacytoma) plus a myeloma-defining event—CRAB end-organ damage (hyperCalcemia, Renal insufficiency, Anemia, Bone lesions) or a biomarker of malignancy (clonal marrow plasma cells >=60%, involved:uninvolved serum free light chain ratio >=100, or >1 focal MRI lesion). POEMS requires polyneuropathy + monoclonal plasma cell disorder + (sclerotic bone lesions / Castleman's / elevated VEGF) + an additional system feature.","references":["Harrison 22e p.883","Harrison 22e p.884","Harrison 22e p.887","Harrison 22e p.888","Harrison 22e p.893","Harrison 22e p.894","Harrison 22e ch.117 p.896","Harrison 22e ch.117 p.897"],"crossLinks":[]},"platelet_disorders":{"name":"Platelet Disorders (Thrombocytopenia)","system":"Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thrombocytopenia","ITP","HIT","platelet dysfunction"],"chapter":120,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.921-926","clinicalPearls":["Thrombocytopenia arises from one or more of three mechanisms: decreased bone marrow production, sequestration (usually in an enlarged spleen), and/or increased platelet destruction (Harrison 22e p.921).","First step in evaluating thrombocytopenia is reviewing the peripheral blood smear and ruling out pseudothrombocytopenia, especially when there is no apparent cause (Harrison 22e p.921).","Petechiae are pinpoint, nonblanching hemorrhages that usually signify a decreased platelet number rather than platelet dysfunction; they first appear in areas of increased venous pressure (ankles/feet in ambulatory patients) (Harrison 22e p.921-922).","Wet purpura (blood blisters on the oral mucosa) and retinal hemorrhages are thought to denote an increased risk of life-threatening hemorrhage in the thrombocytopenic patient (Harrison 22e p.922, 924).","Drugs are the most common cause of thrombocytopenia; in a patient with no apparent cause, all drugs (including herbal and OTC preparations) should be suspect and stopped or substituted if possible (Harrison 22e p.922).","Infection is the most common noniatrogenic cause of thrombocytopenia; viral and bacterial infections can affect both platelet production and survival, and immune mechanisms may also contribute (Harrison 22e p.921-922).","In HIT, thrombocytopenia is usually not severe (nadir rarely <20,000/uL) and is not associated with bleeding; instead it markedly increases the risk of thrombosis (Harrison 22e p.923).","Most patients develop HIT 5-14 days after heparin exposure; onset before 5 days suggests prior heparin exposure within ~100 days with circulating anti-heparin/PF4 antibodies (Harrison 22e p.923).","ITP is characterized by mucocutaneous bleeding and a low, often very low, platelet count with an otherwise normal peripheral blood smear and cell counts (Harrison 22e p.924).","In thrombotic microangiopathies (TTP/HUS) the PT and aPTT are characteristically normal, which helps distinguish them from DIC, where a coagulopathy predominates with elevated PT and often aPTT (Harrison 22e p.924-925).","An ADAMTS13 activity level of <10% is diagnostic of TTP (Harrison 22e p.925).","Markedly elevated platelet counts (>1.5 million), usually in a myeloproliferative disorder, carry an increased risk of bleeding rather than thrombosis, due in part to acquired von Willebrand disease (Harrison 22e p.926)."],"pathophysiology":"Hemostasis depends on platelets and the vessel wall. Platelets are activated on adhesion to von Willebrand factor (VWF) and collagen in exposed subendothelium after injury; adhesion (primarily via VWF) generates signals activating the GpIIb/IIIa (alphaIIb-beta3) receptor and aggregation, with release of granule contents and stabilization by a fibrin mesh. Normal platelet count is 150,000-450,000/uL; thrombopoietin (TPO), made in the liver, is the major regulator, and roughly one-third of platelets reside in the spleen. Thrombocytopenia results from decreased production, splenic sequestration, and/or increased destruction. In HIT, antibodies form to a complex of platelet factor 4 (PF4) and heparin/glycosaminoglycans; the anti-heparin/PF4 antibody activates platelets via the FcgammaRIIa receptor and also activates monocytes, endothelium, and coagulation, producing a prothrombotic state. ITP is immune-mediated platelet destruction with possible inhibition of platelet release from megakaryocytes. In TTP, deficiency of or antibodies to the metalloprotease ADAMTS13 leave ultra-large VWF multimers uncleaved, driving pathogenic platelet adhesion/aggregation and microvascular thrombosis. HUS features acute renal failure with microangiopathic hemolytic anemia and thrombocytopenia; atypical HUS arises from genetic complement-regulatory defects or anti-complement-regulator antibodies causing chronic complement activation.","additionalDifferentials":["Pseudothrombocytopenia (in vitro EDTA-induced platelet clumping)","Decreased production (bone marrow disorders, myelodysplasia, chemotherapy-induced marrow suppression)","Splenic sequestration / hypersplenism","Drug-induced immune thrombocytopenia","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT/HITT)","Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)","Hemolytic-uremic syndrome (HUS) including atypical HUS","Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)","Inherited thrombocytopenia (e.g., MYH9-related autosomal dominant macrothrombocytopenia: May-Hegglin anomaly, Sebastian, Epstein, Fechtner syndromes)","Evans syndrome (combined autoimmune hemolytic anemia with ITP)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral and bacterial infections causing thrombocytopenia (most common noniatrogenic cause)","Systemic gram-negative bacterial infection with DIC","COVID-19-associated thrombocytopenia/DIC","Infectious mononucleosis and early HIV (immune-mediated thrombocytopenia)","Late HIV with pancytopenia and dysplastic platelet production","Hepatitis C and HIV as secondary causes of ITP","Escherichia coli O157:H7 (diarrhea-associated HUS)","Helicobacter pylori (uncertain, geographically variable association with ITP)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pseudothrombocytopenia from EDTA-induced platelet clumping (in vitro artifact)","Myelodysplasia presenting as isolated thrombocytopenia","Iron deficiency, inflammation, or malignancy causing reactive thrombocytosis (when evaluating platelet count abnormalities)","Other microangiopathies: bone marrow transplant-associated, drug-induced, pregnancy-related, and vasculitis-associated"],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood smear review (rule out clumping; assess RBC morphology and platelet size)","Repeat platelet count in sodium citrate (blue top) or heparin (green top) tube, or fresh fingerstick smear, if EDTA clumping suspected","Complete blood count with hemoglobin and white blood cell count","Bone marrow examination (isolated thrombocytopenia in patients >60 years or nonresponders to initial therapy)","4Ts scoring for HIT pretest probability (thrombocytopenia, timing, thrombosis/sequelae, other causes)","Anti-heparin/PF4 immunoassay (high sensitivity, low specificity) and serotonin release platelet-activation assay (lower sensitivity, higher specificity) for HIT","Lower extremity duplex Doppler imaging to evaluate for thrombosis in HIT","ADAMTS13 activity and inhibitor/antibody testing (activity <10% diagnostic of TTP)","LDH, unconjugated bilirubin, haptoglobin (evidence of hemolysis in MAHA)","PT and aPTT (normal in TTP/HUS; elevated in DIC)","Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test if anemia present (rule out Evans syndrome)","Secondary ITP workup: HIV, hepatitis C, SLE serology, serum protein electrophoresis, immunoglobulin levels, IgA deficiency / monoclonal gammopathy testing, H. pylori","Complement regulatory gene DNA variant testing for atypical HUS"],"redFlags":["HIT: thrombosis (venous and/or arterial) despite a falling platelet count -- stop all heparin immediately (including LMWH, which cross-reacts) and start a non-heparin anticoagulant","Wet purpura (oral mucosal blood blisters) and retinal hemorrhages herald life-threatening, including CNS, bleeding","VITT: high D-dimer with thrombosis in unusual sites such as cerebral venous sinuses (fatal in ~20%)","New thrombocytopenia with microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (schistocytes) suggesting TTP -- a devastating disease if not promptly diagnosed and treated","Severe thrombocytopenia (<5000/uL) or signs of impending bleeding in ITP requiring urgent inpatient combined-modality therapy","Introducing warfarin alone in HIT/HITT may precipitate thrombosis (e.g., venous gangrene) due to reduced protein C and S"],"prognosis":"ITP: in children usually acute, post-infectious, and self-limited; in adults more chronic, though some remit spontaneously within months. Patients with platelet counts >30,000/uL appear not to have increased mortality from the thrombocytopenia. TTP: introduction of plasma exchange reduced mortality from 85-100% to 10-30%; however 25-45% relapse within 30 days of remission and 12-40% have late relapses, with relapses more frequent in those with severe ADAMTS13 deficiency. HUS: diarrhea-associated HUS has overall mortality <5% (about 40% of children need dialysis), whereas non-diarrhea-associated HUS mortality is higher (~26%). VITT is fatal in about 20%.","pitfalls":["Pseudothrombocytopenia: EDTA-induced platelet agglutination is an in vitro artifact and not true thrombocytopenia; confirm with a smear and a count in citrate/heparin or fresh fingerstick blood.","Anti-heparin/PF4 antibodies are common without clinical disease (e.g., ~50% after cardiopulmonary bypass), so the HIT immunoassay has low specificity; HIT remains a clinical diagnosis.","The 4T score is very useful for excluding HIT but overdiagnoses it in settings (e.g., ICU) where thrombocytopenia and thrombosis from other causes are common.","LMWH cross-reacts with HIT antibodies and must not be used to treat HIT.","Starting warfarin alone in HIT/HITT may precipitate thrombosis and venous gangrene; overlap with a DTI, fondaparinux, or DOAC and start only after thrombocytopenia resolves.","Serologic (antibody) testing in ITP is generally unhelpful due to low sensitivity and specificity.","Plasma infusion/exchange has not been shown to alter the overall course of HUS or atypical HUS (except aHUS with antibodies to factor H).","Petechiae indicate decreased platelet number, not platelet dysfunction -- excessive bruising can occur with disorders of either number or function."],"severityClassification":"No single formal grading scale is given; clinically meaningful thresholds noted: ~5000-10,000/uL platelets needed to maintain microvascular integrity; severe ITP thrombocytopenia <5000/uL (or impending bleeding) warrants inpatient combined-modality therapy; ITP counts >30,000/uL not associated with increased mortality; HIT nadir rarely <20,000/uL; ADAMTS13 activity <10% diagnostic of TTP; platelet counts >1.5 million (myeloproliferative) carry bleeding risk. The 4Ts score is used for HIT pretest probability.","references":["Harrison 22e p.921-926"],"crossLinks":["itp","ttp_hus","dic","coagulation_disorders"]},"pleural_empyema":{"name":"Pleural empyema","system":"Respiratory","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["empyema","empyema thoracis","pyothorax","complicated parapneumonic effusion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Empyema is the presence of pus within the pleural space, most commonly a complication of bacterial pneumonia (parapneumonic effusion)","Presents with persistent or worsening fever, pleuritic chest pain, breathlessness, and failure to improve despite pneumonia treatment","Suspect empyema when a parapneumonic effusion is large, loculated, or associated with ongoing sepsis","Pleural fluid sampling is key; frankly purulent fluid, positive Gram stain or culture, low pH, low glucose, and high LDH indicate a complicated effusion or empyema needing drainage","Contrast CT and ultrasound help characterise loculations, pleural thickening, and the underlying lung and guide drainage","Effective management combines prompt drainage of the infected space with appropriate antimicrobial therapy covering likely organisms","Loculated or organising collections may require intrapleural fibrinolytics or surgical intervention such as decortication","Anaerobes and mixed flora are common, particularly with aspiration, and treatment should reflect the likely microbiology"],"pathophysiology":"Empyema typically evolves through stages from an initial free-flowing exudative parapneumonic effusion, as infection and inflammation increase pleural capillary permeability. Bacterial invasion of the pleural fluid promotes neutrophil influx, fibrin deposition, and metabolic activity that lower fluid pH and glucose and raise LDH. Fibrin strands then organise the collection into loculations (fibrinopurulent stage). If untreated, fibroblast proliferation produces a thick, inelastic pleural peel that traps the lung and restricts expansion (organising stage), impairing recovery.","additionalDifferentials":["Uncomplicated parapneumonic effusion","Malignant pleural effusion","Tuberculous pleural effusion","Haemothorax","Lung abscess","Transudative effusion from heart failure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnostic pleural aspiration with fluid pH, glucose, LDH, protein, Gram stain, and culture","Chest ultrasound to detect loculations and guide sampling and drainage","Contrast-enhanced CT of the chest to define pleural collections and underlying pathology","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers to assess systemic infection","Cytology and mycobacterial studies where malignancy or tuberculosis is possible"],"redFlags":["Signs of severe sepsis or septic shock","Large or rapidly accumulating collection causing respiratory compromise","Failure to improve despite antibiotics and initial drainage, suggesting loculation or trapped lung","Development of a bronchopleural fistula or spreading infection"],"prognosis":"With early recognition, adequate drainage, and appropriate antibiotics, most patients recover well; delayed or inadequate treatment allows loculation and pleural thickening, leading to prolonged illness, the need for surgery, chronic respiratory impairment, and higher mortality, particularly in frail or comorbid patients.","pitfalls":["Managing an infected pleural collection with antibiotics alone without adequate drainage","Delaying pleural fluid sampling in a non-resolving pneumonia","Underestimating loculated collections on plain radiography without ultrasound or CT","Overlooking anaerobic or aspiration-related organisms when choosing antimicrobial cover"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"pneumococcal_disease":{"name":"Pneumococcal Disease","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Streptococcus pneumoniae infection"],"chapter":151,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1187-1195","clinicalPearls":["S. pneumoniae can infect nearly any tissue; the four most common syndromes are otitis media, pneumonia, bacteraemia, and meningitis (Harrison 22e p.1191).","There is no pathognomonic presentation of pneumococcal disease, and a microbiologically confirmed diagnosis is made in only a minority of cases, so empirical therapy covering S. pneumoniae is often indicated (Harrison 22e p.1191).","In the elderly, pneumococcal pneumonia may present non-specifically with confusion or malaise without fever or cough; a high index of suspicion is required because delayed treatment leads to rapid, severe deterioration (Harrison 22e p.1192).","Pneumococcal pneumonia can mimic an acute abdomen (upper abdominal pain from diaphragmatic pleural involvement); clinicians should remember to keep this non-abdominal process in the differential (Harrison 22e p.1192).","Now that H. influenzae type b vaccine is in routine childhood use, S. pneumoniae and Neisseria meningitidis are the most common bacterial causes of meningitis in both adults and children (Harrison 22e p.1193).","Pneumococcal meningitis can be a primary presentation or a complication of skull fracture, otitis media, mastoiditis, or bacteraemia; most cases arise from haematogenous spread rather than direct extension (Harrison 22e p.1191, 1193).","A positive urinary pneumococcal antigen test is useful for etiologic attribution in adults (low nasopharyngeal colonization) but is generally uninformative in children and in high-colonization low-income settings (Harrison 22e p.1192-1193).","Most pneumococcal pneumonia is non-bacteraemic; only ~5-20% of cases are accompanied by bacteraemia, and blood cultures are positive in fewer than 30% of cases (Harrison 22e p.1190, 1192).","Empyema is the most common focal complication of pneumococcal pneumonia (<5% of cases); pleural fluid with frank pus, visible bacteria, or pH <=7.1 indicates empyema and demands complete drainage (Harrison 22e p.1192)."],"pathophysiology":"Pneumococci are encapsulated gram-positive diplococci that colonise the human nasopharynx from an early age; colonisation is common while disease is rare. All pneumococcal infection ultimately stems from nasopharyngeal acquisition. The anti-phagocytic polysaccharide capsule is the key virulence determinant, resisting complement deposition in the absence of type-specific antibody. The organism dynamically modulates capsule expression: it downregulates capsule in the nasopharynx (rough colonies) to evade immune recognition and upregulates it on tissue invasion (smooth colonies). From the nasopharynx, organisms spread haematogenously to distant sites (brain, joints, bone, peritoneum) or locally to mucosal surfaces (otitis media, pneumonia). Intercurrent viral respiratory infection increases susceptibility by upregulating epithelial adhesion factors, permitting adhesion via surface adhesins (PsaA, PspA, CbpA/PspC, pneumolysin, neuraminidases). Disease pathology is driven by host inflammation: cell-wall teichoic acids and peptidoglycan induce IL-1, IL-6, and TNF and activate the alternative complement pathway, and pneumolysin drives proinflammatory cytokine release. Host defence depends on intact respiratory epithelium, the cough reflex and mucociliary escalator, splenic function, complement, neutrophils/macrophages, CRP binding of cell-wall phosphorylcholine, and TLR2/TLR4 recognition, together with acquired anticapsular IgG (Harrison 22e p.1190-1191).","additionalDifferentials":["Viral pneumonia","Mycoplasma pneumonia","Haemophilus influenzae pneumonia","Klebsiella pneumoniae pneumonia","Staphylococcus aureus pneumonia","Legionella pneumonia","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (in HIV-infected/immunocompromised hosts)","Myocardial infarction","Heart failure with atypical pulmonary oedema","Atelectasis","Cholecystitis, appendicitis, perforated peptic ulcer, or subphrenic abscess (when abdominal symptoms predominate)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral lower respiratory tract infection / viral pneumonia","Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection","Legionella infection","Pneumonia due to H. influenzae, K. pneumoniae, or S. aureus","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia in immunocompromised hosts","Other bacterial pyogenic meningitis (e.g., N. meningitidis), clinically indistinguishable from pneumococcal meningitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Myocardial infarction","Heart failure with atypical pulmonary oedema","Pulmonary atelectasis","Lung cancer (can produce round/spherical consolidation mimicking pneumococcal round pneumonia)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiography (lobar/segmental consolidation; >1 lobe in ~30%; infiltrate may be absent early or with dehydration)","Blood cultures (positive in <30% of pneumococcal pneumonia)","Gram's stain and culture of high-quality sputum (mainstay of adult diagnosis; yield depends on specimen quality and prior antibiotics)","Urinary pneumococcal antigen assay (cell-wall polysaccharide; serotype-specific quantitative assays for up to 24 serotypes emerging)","CSF examination for meningitis: turbidity, elevated protein, elevated WBC, reduced glucose, plus Gram's stain, culture, antigen testing, or PCR","Pleural fluid analysis (pus, microscopy, pH) when effusion present","Sterile aspiration/culture (and capsular antigen or PCR) of fluid from the infected site for other invasive syndromes; lung tissue pathology is the gold standard for pneumonia"],"redFlags":["Asplenia or splenic dysfunction (e.g., sickle cell disease) confers risk of overwhelming, fulminant pneumococcal sepsis","Leukopenia in pneumococcal pneumonia (<10% of cases) is a poor prognostic sign associated with fatal outcome","Hypotension and signs of severe pneumonia/sepsis at presentation predict higher case-fatality","Meningitis features: altered consciousness, seizures, stiff neck, signs of raised intracranial pressure (bradycardia, hypertension), cranial nerve palsies","In children: inability to drink, convulsions, lethargy, or severe malnutrition signify very severe disease warranting immediate antibiotics and referral","Pleural effusion with persistent fever/leukocytosis after 4-5 days of appropriate therapy suggests empyema"],"prognosis":"Case-fatality varies with age, comorbidity, disease severity at presentation, and access to care. For pneumococcal pneumonia, CFR ranges from <5% in hospitalised patients aged 18-44 to >12% in those over 65, even with timely management; severity at presentation (rather than the presence of bacteraemia) drives mortality, and early deaths reflect a rapidly progressive cascade that often unfolds irrespective of antibiotic administration. Pneumococcal meningitis carries a mortality of ~20%, and up to 50% of survivors have acute or chronic sequelae (deafness, hydrocephalus, and cognitive impairment in children; diffuse brain swelling, subarachnoid bleeding, hydrocephalus, cerebrovascular complications, and hearing loss in adults) (Harrison 22e p.1190, 1193).","pitfalls":["Treating an apparent acute abdomen while overlooking pneumococcal lower-lobe pneumonia causing referred abdominal pain (Harrison 22e p.1192).","Relying on a normal early chest radiograph to exclude pneumonia; an infiltrate may be absent early or with dehydration and appear after rehydration (Harrison 22e p.1192).","Over-interpreting a positive urinary pneumococcal antigen in children or in high-colonization settings, where it does not reliably attribute etiology (Harrison 22e p.1192-1193).","Diagnosing otitis media from tympanic-membrane redness alone, which is insufficient (Harrison 22e p.1193).","Attributing round/spherical consolidation solely to pneumococcus without considering other causes, especially cancer (Harrison 22e p.1192).","Assuming PPSV23 reliably prevents non-bacteraemic pneumococcal pneumonia, particularly in the elderly and immunodeficient, where effectiveness is reduced and protection wanes after ~5 years (Harrison 22e p.1194)."],"severityClassification":"Pneumococcal syndromes are classified as invasive (a normally sterile site is infected or bacteraemia is present, e.g., bacteraemic pneumonia, meningitis, bacteraemia) versus noninvasive (contiguous spread, e.g., otitis media, sinusitis, bronchitis); whether non-bacteraemic pneumonia counts as invasive is debated. In children, WHO IMCI algorithms stratify illness by danger signs (very severe disease: inability to drink, convulsions, lethargy, severe malnutrition) and by cough with tachypnoea plus lower chest-wall indrawing to guide antibiotics and referral; no equivalent validated algorithm exists for adults (Harrison 22e p.1191).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1187-1195"],"crossLinks":["CAP","MENINGITIS"]},"pneumoconiosis":{"name":"Pneumoconiosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["occupational lung disease","coal workers' pneumoconiosis","CWP","dust lung disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of parenchymal lung diseases caused by chronic inhalation of inorganic mineral dusts, most often occupational (coal, silica, asbestos, beryllium)","A detailed occupational and exposure history, including duration and intensity of dust contact and latency, is essential to diagnosis","Many patients are asymptomatic early, with disease detected on surveillance chest imaging before dyspnoea and cough develop","Chest radiography and CT typically show small rounded or irregular opacities, with characteristic patterns and distributions depending on the causative dust","Coal workers' pneumoconiosis ranges from simple disease with small nodules to progressive massive fibrosis with large conglomerate masses","There is no specific treatment to reverse the fibrosis; management centres on removal from exposure, supportive care, and prevention of complications","Asbestos exposure additionally raises the risk of pleural plaques, mesothelioma, and bronchogenic carcinoma, warranting long-term vigilance","Prevention through dust control, respiratory protection, and workplace exposure limits is the most effective strategy"],"pathophysiology":"Inhaled mineral dust particles that reach the distal airways and alveoli are engulfed by alveolar macrophages. Depending on particle size, surface reactivity, and burden, the macrophages are activated or killed, releasing inflammatory mediators, cytokines, and reactive oxygen species. Sustained exposure drives chronic inflammation and fibroblast activation, leading to collagen deposition, nodule formation, and, in advanced disease, coalescence into large fibrotic masses that distort lung architecture and impair gas exchange. The specific pattern reflects the fibrogenic potential of the particular dust.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and other interstitial lung diseases","Sarcoidosis","Pulmonary tuberculosis (which may also complicate pneumoconiosis)","Metastatic or miliary pulmonary nodular disease","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Pulmonary manifestations of connective tissue disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiography, ideally read against standardised classification of pneumoconiosis opacities","High-resolution CT to characterise nodularity, fibrosis, and pleural disease","Pulmonary function testing to quantify restrictive, obstructive, or mixed impairment and gas transfer","Detailed occupational and environmental exposure documentation","Screening for complicating tuberculosis or, with asbestos, for malignancy where clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging masses or new nodules suggesting progressive massive fibrosis or malignancy","Constitutional symptoms or haemoptysis raising concern for tuberculosis or cancer","Rapid decline in lung function or new hypoxaemia","Pleural effusion or thickening in an asbestos-exposed patient suggesting mesothelioma"],"prognosis":"Simple pneumoconiosis may remain stable and cause little functional impairment, whereas progressive massive fibrosis and asbestos-related disease can lead to progressive respiratory failure; asbestos exposure carries a long-term risk of mesothelioma and lung cancer, and outcomes improve when further exposure is halted early.","pitfalls":["Failing to take an adequate occupational history and therefore missing the diagnosis","Attributing all radiographic change to dust without excluding superimposed tuberculosis or malignancy","Assuming a short exposure excludes disease, given the often long latency","Overlooking the need to remove the worker from ongoing exposure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"pneumocystis_pneumonia":{"name":"Pneumocystis Pneumonia (PCP)","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCP","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia","PJP"],"chapter":227,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1730-1734","clinicalPearls":["Caused by the fungus Pneumocystis jirovecii (formerly P. carinii); the organism cannot be cultured and is host-specific to humans, infecting only via other humans (Harrison 22e p.1730-1731).","An opportunistic pathogen of immunocompromised hosts: HIV infection, organ transplantation, haematologic malignancy, and those on high-dose glucocorticoids or certain immunosuppressive monoclonal antibodies (Harrison 22e p.1730).","Among people with HIV, PCP incidence is inversely related to CD4+ T-cell count: at least 80% of cases occur at counts <200/uL and most actually develop at counts <100/uL (Harrison 22e p.1731-1732).","Classic presentation is acute or subacute pneumonia, often beginning with a vague sense of dyspnoea alone, then progressing to fever, nonproductive cough, and progressive shortness of breath (Harrison 22e p.1731).","Decreased oxygen saturation at rest or with exertion is characteristic; untreated it progresses to severe hypoxaemia (Harrison 22e p.1731).","Serum lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) is often elevated from pulmonary damage (Harrison 22e p.1731).","Classic chest radiograph shows diffuse bilateral interstitial infiltrates that are perihilar and symmetric, which can progress to alveolar filling (Harrison 22e p.1731).","High-resolution chest CT shows diffuse ground-glass opacities in virtually all PCP patients, often before the plain chest radiograph becomes abnormal (Harrison 22e p.1731).","Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) is the most common sampling method; demonstration of organisms in BAL fluid is almost 100% sensitive and specific for PCP (Harrison 22e p.1733).","Since the organism cannot be cultured, diagnosis relies on staining of cyst or trophozoite forms in lung tissue/secretions with methenamine silver, toluidine blue O, Giemsa, or specific immunofluorescent antibody (Harrison 22e p.1733).","An induced sputum sample, when obtained and interpreted by an experienced provider, can be highly sensitive and specific, but sensitivity is widely variable (55-90%); expectorated sputum and throat swabs have very low sensitivity (Harrison 22e p.1733).","Adjunctive glucocorticoids improve survival in moderate-to-severe disease (initial room-air PaO2 <70 mmHg or alveolar-arterial gradient >=35 mmHg) by reducing the inflammation that follows organism die-off once specific therapy begins; they should be started when PCP therapy is initiated, even if the diagnosis is only suspected (Harrison 22e p.1734).","Prophylaxis is the cornerstone of prevention; for HIV, CD4+ counts <200/uL (or CD4 percentage <15) are an indication to start or continue chemoprophylaxis, alongside prior PCP, unexplained prolonged fever, or recent oropharyngeal candidiasis (Harrison 22e p.1734).","PCP now occurs more often from non-HIV-related immunosuppression than from HIV infection in many health care systems (Harrison 22e p.1731)."],"pathophysiology":"Pneumocystis jirovecii is a fungus with unique tropism for the lung. Organisms are inhaled into the alveolar space, presumably after being exhaled by another human; most people are exposed and infected early in life. Disease may arise from primary infection, reinfection, or reactivation of latent infection. Clinically apparent pneumonia occurs only in immunocompromised hosts, where defects in cellular and/or humoral immunity (congenital, HIV-related, or drug-induced) permit proliferation. CD4+ T cells are critical for host defence. The organism proliferates in the lung provoking a mononuclear cell response; alveoli fill with proteinaceous material and alveolar-capillary injury with surfactant abnormalities develops. Stained sections show foamy, vacuolated alveolar exudates of viable and nonviable organisms, with interstitial oedema and fibrosis. Much of the clinical deterioration after starting specific therapy is attributed to enhanced inflammation from dying organisms, the rationale for adjunctive glucocorticoids (Harrison 22e p.1731-1734).","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial pneumonia (including community-acquired pneumonia)","Pulmonary tuberculosis (especially with upper-lobe cavitary pattern seen after aerosolized pentamidine prophylaxis)","Cytomegalovirus and other viral pneumonias in the immunocompromised host","Other invasive fungal pneumonias (e.g., Aspergillus)","Congestive heart failure","Pulmonary embolism","Pulmonary hypertension","Drug toxicity","Neoplastic process involving the lung","Lymphoid interstitial pneumonitis (LIP) and nonspecific interstitial pneumonitis (NIP) in HIV"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial pneumonia","Pulmonary tuberculosis","Cytomegalovirus pneumonia","Invasive aspergillosis / other fungal pneumonia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Congestive heart failure","Pulmonary embolism","Pulmonary hypertension","Drug-induced pulmonary toxicity","Pulmonary neoplasm","Lymphoid interstitial pneumonitis (LIP) / nonspecific interstitial pneumonitis (NIP)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Pulse oximetry / oxygen saturation at rest and with exertion, and arterial blood gases (hypoxaemia with widened alveolar-arterial gradient; used for staging severity and directing treatment)","Serum lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) - often elevated but neither sensitive nor specific","High-resolution chest CT (diffuse ground-glass opacities; a normal chest CT essentially rules out PCP)","Bronchoalveolar lavage with methenamine silver, toluidine blue O, Giemsa, or immunofluorescent staining","Induced sputum examination (experienced-provider dependent)","PCR of respiratory specimens (more useful for ruling out PCP when negative than for confirming it when positive; cannot distinguish colonization from disease and is not standardized)","Serum (1->3)-beta-D-glucan (frequently elevated but neither perfectly sensitive nor highly specific)","Transbronchial or open-lung biopsy when less invasive sampling is non-diagnostic"],"redFlags":["PaO2 <70 mmHg on room air (or alveolar-arterial oxygen gradient >=35 mmHg) marking moderate-to-severe disease that warrants adjunctive glucocorticoids","Profound or progressive hypoxaemia / progression to respiratory failure","Development of pneumothorax (pneumatoceles and pneumothoraces are characteristic, especially in HIV) - associated with higher mortality","Need for mechanical ventilation","Distributive shock due to PCP (rare)","Clinical deterioration after 3-4 days of therapy, or failure to improve by 7-10 days, prompting reevaluation for other or coexisting processes"],"prognosis":"Untreated, PCP is invariably fatal. HIV-associated disease is often indolent, presenting early as mild exercise intolerance or chest tightness without fever or cough and a normal or near-normal chest radiograph, then progressing over days to months to fever, cough, diffuse alveolar infiltrates, and profound hypoxaemia; non-HIV immunosuppressed patients more often have acute disease progressing over days to respiratory failure. Mortality is influenced by age, degree of immunosuppression, preexisting lung disease, need for mechanical ventilation, and development of pneumothorax. With advances in critical care, prognosis for those requiring intubation has improved and depends largely on comorbidities and the underlying disease (Harrison 22e p.1733).","pitfalls":["A normal LDH does not rule out PCP, and an elevated LDH is not specific for PCP (Harrison 22e p.1731).","PCP can occur at CD4+ counts >200/uL in HIV (uncommon, especially at counts well above 200), and in non-HIV immunosuppression a CD4+ count >200/uL does not imply protection (Harrison 22e p.1731).","PCR is so sensitive that it cannot reliably distinguish colonization from true pneumonia; there is no validated PCR threshold, so a positive result does not definitively attribute disease to Pneumocystis (Harrison 22e p.1733).","Serum/BAL (1->3)-beta-D-glucan and serum PCR are not sufficiently sensitive or specific to establish or exclude PCP (Harrison 22e p.1733).","If treated with specific therapy but without corticosteroids, patients of any severity often deteriorate during the first few days from inflammation induced by dying organisms (Harrison 22e p.1733).","Initial chest radiograph may be normal in mild disease, and the classic dense perihilar infiltrate is unusual in AIDS; relying on plain film can delay diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1730-1731).","Extrapulmonary pneumocystosis is rare and nonspecific; when present, the priority is to look for another concurrent infectious or neoplastic process (Harrison 22e p.1733)."],"severityClassification":"Severity is staged largely by oxygenation. Moderate-to-severe disease is defined by an initial room-air PaO2 <70 mmHg or an alveolar-arterial oxygen gradient >=35 mmHg; this threshold identifies patients (with HIV, and probably other immunodeficiencies) who benefit from adjunctive glucocorticoids in addition to specific antimicrobial therapy. Arterial blood-gas measurement both aids diagnosis and directs treatment by staging severity (Harrison 22e p.1731, 1734).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1730-1734 (Ch.227, Pneumocystis Infections)","Harrison 22e p.1730 (Ch.208, HIV/AIDS clinical manifestations - PCP)"],"crossLinks":[]},"poisoning_and_drug_overdose":{"name":"Poisoning and Drug Overdose","system":"Toxicology / Multisystem","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["xenobiotic poisoning","drug overdose","intoxication","toxic ingestion","acute poisoning"],"chapter":470,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3703-3708","clinicalPearls":["Poisoning severity is dose-related, but individual responses to a given dose vary with genetic polymorphisms, enzyme induction or inhibition from co-ingested xenobiotics, and acquired tolerance; the functional reserve of the target organ (influenced by age and preexisting disease) also shapes severity and reversibility.","Diagnosis of an unknown poisoning rests on pattern recognition: first characterize the overall physiologic state as stimulated, depressed, discordant, or normal, then look for a toxidrome and finally for agent-specific clues.","Core temperature must be measured and reassessed frequently even in combative patients, because temperature elevation is the single most reliable predictor of poor outcome in stimulant poisoning (e.g., cocaine) and in withdrawal states (e.g., alcohol, GHB).","Mydriasis is characteristic of all stimulant states but is most marked in anticholinergic poisoning because pupillary reactivity is muscarinic; sympathomimetic poisoning also dilates pupils but some light reactivity persists, whereas the anticholinergic toxidrome adds hot, dry, flushed skin, decreased bowel sounds, and urinary retention.","Rotatory nystagmus points specifically to phencyclidine or ketamine, the only physiologic stimulants that produce this sign.","Miosis is most pronounced in opioid and cholinergic poisoning; cholinergic poisoning is distinguished by accompanying muscarinic and nicotinic features.","A normal exam can be deceptive: 'toxic time-bombs' (slowly absorbed, slowly distributed, requiring metabolic activation, or disrupting metabolism) and the proliferation of once-daily extended-release formulations mean delayed, severe toxicity is increasingly common.","A rapid response to a diagnostic antidote can confirm the cause: improvement within minutes of IV dextrose, naloxone, or flumazenil is essentially diagnostic of hypoglycemia, opioid poisoning, and benzodiazepine intoxication respectively.","Qualitative urine drug-of-abuse screens are screening tests only: false positives and negatives are common (e.g., fluoroquinolones cause false-positive opiate screens), and newer agents such as synthetic cannabinoids (spice), cathinones (bath salts), and kratom are not detected at all.","Symptomatic patients (e.g., apnea from opioids) should be managed on the basis of history, examination, and the observed toxidrome without waiting for laboratory confirmation.","Effects after overdose typically begin sooner, peak later, and last longer than after a therapeutic dose, and the toxicokinetic profile differs from the published therapeutic pharmacokinetics in standard references.","Most patients who are asymptomatic 6 h after ingestion are unlikely to develop later toxicity and can be discharged safely, but longer observation is mandatory after suspected toxic time-bomb ingestions."],"pathophysiology":"Poisoning is the development of dose-related adverse effects from exposure to chemicals, drugs, or other xenobiotics. Effects may be local (skin, eyes, lungs) or systemic depending on the route of exposure and the chemical's physical properties and mechanism of action. Toxidromes reflect the net effect on autonomic and neuromuscular function: stimulated states arise from sympathetic, anticholinergic, or hallucinogen activity or from drug withdrawal; depressed states from functional sympatholytics, cholinergic agents, opioids, and sedative-hypnotic GABAergic agents; discordant states from asphyxiants, CNS syndromes, membrane-active agents, and anion-gap metabolic acidosis inducers. In opioid and sedative-hypnotic poisoning, vital-sign derangement is secondary to depression of central cardiovascular and respiratory centers (or resulting hypoxemia) and is seen only with marked depression of consciousness, whereas direct/peripheral sympatholytics can cause prominent cardiovascular depression without comparable CNS depression. Some agents (acetaminophen, ethylene glycol, methanol) owe their toxicity to metabolites more toxic than the parent compound.","additionalDifferentials":["Structural CNS lesion (focal neurologic findings are uncommon in poisoning and should prompt imaging)","Sepsis or other infection causing altered mental status","Primary psychiatric illness or psychogenic illness (fear of poisoning, mass hysteria)","Metabolic encephalopathy (hepatic, renal, or respiratory failure)","Hypoglycemia from non-toxic causes","Trauma and occult head injury"],"infectionMimics":["Sepsis with shock and multiorgan dysfunction (hypoxemia/shock from severe poisoning can mimic, and secondary aspiration pneumonia and sepsis can complicate poisoning)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Psychogenic illness and anxiety reactions, which can produce mild physiologic stimulation indistinguishable from a toxic cause without observation or ancillary testing","Primary cardiac arrhythmia or conduction disease","Endocrine emergencies (e.g., thyroid storm mimicking a sympathomimetic toxidrome)","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome and serotonin syndrome (CNS syndromes that may overlap with stimulant toxidromes)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Anion-gap calculation (elevated AGMA in advanced methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylate poisoning, and any poisoning causing organ failure, seizures, or shock; abnormally low anion gap suggests bromide, calcium, iodine, lithium, or magnesium)","Serum lactate (typically low relative to anion gap in toxic-alcohol/salicylate poisoning, high and near-equal to the gap in hypoperfusion)","Osmolal gap (>10 mmol/L difference suggests a low-molecular-weight solute such as a toxic alcohol or glycol)","Serum glucose and ketones","Electrolytes including potassium and calcium","ECG (bradycardia/AV block, QRS and QT prolongation from membrane-active agents and hyperkalemia, ventricular tachyarrhythmias)","Quantitative serum levels for acetaminophen, alcohols (including ethylene glycol and methanol), anticonvulsants, barbiturates, digoxin, heavy metals, iron, lithium, salicylate, theophylline, carboxyhemoglobin, and methemoglobin","Qualitative urine drug screen (most useful when toxicity is severe or unexplained, e.g., coma, seizures, cardiovascular instability, acidosis, nonsinus rhythms)","Radiography/abdominal CT (radiopaque densities suggest chloral hydrate, chlorinated hydrocarbons, heavy metals, drug packets, iodinated compounds, potassium salts, enteric-coated tablets, or salicylates; chest x-ray for pulmonary edema, metal fume fever, elemental mercury)","Review of the state Prescription Monitoring Program for controlled-substance history","PT/INR (useful for risk stratification in warfarin/rodenticide poisoning, but unreliable for direct oral anticoagulant overdose)"],"redFlags":["Coma or progressively decreasing level of consciousness","Respiratory depression, apnea, or hypoxemia","Hypotension or cardiovascular collapse","Cardiac conduction abnormalities or arrhythmias (QRS/QT prolongation, ventricular tachyarrhythmias)","Seizures","Hyperthermia or hypothermia (temperature elevation is the most reliable predictor of poor outcome in stimulant/withdrawal states)","Suspected ingestion of a toxic time-bomb or an agent with toxic metabolites (acetaminophen, ethylene glycol, methanol)","Body packing or body stuffing (concealment of illicit drug packets)","Intentional (suicidal) overdose requiring continuous observation and self-injury precautions"],"prognosis":"Overall mortality is low at less than 1% of all poisoning exposures, with most being minor or non-toxic and involving children under 6 years. Mortality is substantially higher (1-2%) among hospitalized patients with intentional (suicidal) overdose or complications from drugs of abuse, who account for most serious poisonings. Acetaminophen is the pharmaceutical most often implicated in fatal poisoning. Carbon monoxide is the overall leading cause of poisoning death, although it is underrepresented in hospital and poison-center statistics because such patients are typically dead when found and referred directly to medical examiners. Drug overdose deaths have risen steadily since 1999 (over 106,600 U.S. deaths in 2021; age-adjusted rate 32.4 per 100,000), driven largely by synthetic opioids such as fentanyl and emerging agents such as xylazine. Once the offending agent is eliminated, maintaining tissue perfusion is critical to complete recovery.","pitfalls":["Relying on the gag reflex as an indicator of the need for intubation; it is not reliable, and a patient may protect the airway while stimulated but not when left alone.","Treating a negative qualitative drug screen as exclusion of poisoning; the agent may be undetectable by the assay or present below the limit of detection, and repeat testing later may turn positive.","Treating a positive qualitative screen as diagnostic or forensic; cross-reacting drugs cause false positives and confirmatory GC/MS may take weeks.","Assuming blood levels correlate with toxicity during absorption/distribution (levels can exceed tissue concentrations) or during resolution (levels fall below tissue concentrations); rebound increases (e.g., lithium) can occur after extracorporeal removal stops.","Discharging too early after ingestion of a toxic time-bomb (slow-absorption, slow-distribution, metabolic-activation, or metabolism-disrupting agents) on the basis of an initially benign presentation.","Failing to consider all medications available in the home (including pet medications) when the history is unclear in a suspected poisoning.","Overlooking intentional self-harm: failing to maintain continuous observation and self-injury precautions in patients who have attempted suicide.","Using class IA, IC, or III antiarrhythmics, or beta blockers driven by sympathetic hyperactivity, for toxin-induced ventricular arrhythmias; sodium bicarbonate is the preferred first agent for membrane-active toxicity."],"severityClassification":"Harrison grades physiologic stimulation and depression on a 1-4 scale. Stimulation: Grade 1 - anxious, irritable, tremulous with normal vital signs; Grade 2 - agitated, possible confusion/hallucinations but still able to converse and follow commands, mildly-to-moderately elevated vital signs; Grade 3 - delirious, unintelligible speech, uncontrollable motor hyperactivity, markedly raised vital signs, possible tachyarrhythmias; Grade 4 - coma, seizures, cardiovascular collapse. Depression: Grade 1 - lethargic/sleeping but arousable, able to converse; Grade 2 - responds to pain not voice, brainstem reflexes intact; Grade 3 - unresponsive to pain, depressed brainstem reflexes, decreased tone/respiration/temperature; Grade 4 - flaccid paralysis, absent brainstem reflexes and respirations, decreased cardiovascular vital signs.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3703-3708"],"crossLinks":[]},"polyarteritis_nodosa":{"name":"Polyarteritis nodosa","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PAN","periarteritis nodosa","medium-vessel vasculitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A necrotising vasculitis of medium-sized muscular arteries that characteristically spares the lungs and is not associated with ANCA","Presents with systemic symptoms (fever, weight loss, malaise) plus organ-specific ischaemic damage","Peripheral nervous system involvement, especially mononeuritis multiplex, is common and an important clue","Skin manifestations include livedo reticularis, tender nodules, ulcers, and digital ischaemia","Renal involvement is typically renovascular ischaemia and hypertension rather than glomerulonephritis, and gut involvement can cause mesenteric ischaemia and abdominal pain","A recognised association with hepatitis B infection exists, so viral serology should be checked","Diagnosis rests on biopsy of affected tissue showing necrotising arteritis, or angiography demonstrating microaneurysms and stenoses in visceral vessels","Treatment uses corticosteroids with additional immunosuppression for severe disease, while hepatitis B-associated cases also require antiviral therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Polyarteritis nodosa is a systemic necrotising vasculitis affecting medium and small muscular arteries. Transmural inflammation with fibrinoid necrosis weakens the vessel wall, producing the microaneurysms characteristic of visceral angiography, while intimal proliferation and thrombosis cause luminal narrowing and downstream tissue infarction. Lesions are segmental and favour branch points. Unlike small-vessel vasculitides it does not typically involve capillaries, glomeruli, or the pulmonary circulation and is ANCA-negative. Immune complex mechanisms are implicated, particularly in hepatitis B-associated disease.","additionalDifferentials":["ANCA-associated vasculitides (granulomatosis with polyangiitis, microscopic polyangiitis)","Cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis","Infective endocarditis with embolic phenomena","Fibromuscular dysplasia and segmental arterial mediolysis","Cholesterol embolisation syndrome","Other causes of mononeuritis multiplex"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Hepatitis B (and hepatitis C) serology","Visceral angiography (renal, mesenteric) for microaneurysms and stenoses","Biopsy of clinically involved tissue (e.g. nerve, muscle, skin) showing necrotising arteritis","Inflammatory markers, renal function, and urinalysis (typically without active urinary sediment)","Nerve conduction studies for mononeuritis multiplex and ANCA testing (expected negative)"],"redFlags":["Acute abdomen from mesenteric ischaemia, bowel infarction, or perforation","Rapidly progressive renal impairment or malignant hypertension from renal artery involvement","Cardiac ischaemia or heart failure from coronary arteritis","New neurological deficit including stroke or severe mononeuritis multiplex","Ruptured visceral microaneurysm causing haemorrhage"],"prognosis":"Untreated disease has a poor outlook, but immunosuppression markedly improves survival. Prognosis is worse with significant gastrointestinal, renal, cardiac, or central nervous system involvement; hepatitis B-associated disease can respond well when the virus is also treated.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with ANCA-associated vasculitis and expecting lung or glomerular disease","Failing to test for hepatitis B, which alters management","Missing mesenteric ischaemia in a patient with vague abdominal pain","Overlooking mononeuritis multiplex as a diagnostic clue","Delaying tissue or angiographic confirmation in a critically ill patient"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"polycystic_kidney_disease":{"name":"Polycystic Kidney Disease","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ADPKD","PKD"],"chapter":327,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2430","clinicalPearls":["Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) is the most common life-threatening monogenic disease, affecting ~12 million people worldwide (estimated prevalence 1:1000 to 1:400), and is a leading cause of kidney failure; it is inherited as an autosomal dominant trait with complete penetrance but variable expressivity (p.2430-2431).","ADPKD is caused by mutations in PKD1 (chromosome 16p13, ~85% of cases, encodes polycystin-1) and PKD2 (chromosome 4q21-q23, ~15% of cases, encodes polycystin-2); PKD2 patients tend to have milder disease and may be underdiagnosed. Polycystins are localized on the primary cilium, and defective ciliary function places ADPKD among the ciliopathies (p.2430-2431).","It is characterized by progressive bilateral formation of epithelial-lined renal cysts; cysts arise in only ~5% of tubules but their enormous growth destroys surrounding tissue. Enlarged kidneys can reach a fourfold increase in length and up to 20 times the normal weight (p.2430-2431).","Back or flank pain is a frequent symptom in ~60% of patients and may result from cyst infection, hemorrhage, or nephrolithiasis; gross hematuria from cyst rupture occurs in ~40% during the disease course, often recurrently (p.2432).","Hypertension is common and typically occurs before any reduction in GFR; it is a risk factor for both cardiovascular and kidney disease progression. Cardiovascular complications are the major cause of mortality in ADPKD (p.2432).","Intracranial (berry) aneurysm occurs four to five times more frequently than in the general population and causes high mortality; about 20-50% of patients may have 'warning headaches' preceding subarachnoid hemorrhage from a ruptured aneurysm (p.2432).","Liver (biliary epithelial) cysts are the most common extrarenal complication; massive polycystic liver disease occurs almost exclusively in women, particularly those with multiple pregnancies. Mitral valve prolapse occurs in up to 30% of patients, and colonic diverticulae and abdominal wall hernias are also increased (p.2432).","Diagnosis is typically made from a positive autosomal dominant family history plus bilateral kidney cysts on ultrasonography; in at-risk subjects aged 15-29 years, two renal cysts (unilateral or bilateral) suffice (sensitivity 96%, specificity 100%), with higher cyst counts required at older ages because simple cysts increase with age (p.2432-2433).","Tolvaptan, a vasopressin V2-receptor (V2R) antagonist, inhibits the cAMP pathway in collecting ducts and reduces cell proliferation by lowering renal cAMP, slowing the decline of renal function; the FDA has indicated it only for patients at risk of rapidly progressing disease (p.2433).","Infection is the second most common cause of death; up to half of patients have one or more renal infections, most commonly an infected cyst or acute pyelonephritis due to gram-negative bacteria, with fever and flank pain (p.2432)."],"pathophysiology":"ADPKD results from mutations in PKD1 and PKD2, encoding polycystin-1 (PC1, a large 11-transmembrane G-protein-coupled-receptor-like protein) and polycystin-2 (PC2, a calcium-permeable six-transmembrane TRP-family cation channel). PC1 and PC2 bind via their C-terminal tails to form a receptor-channel complex (proposed 1:3 PC1:PC2 stoichiometry) located on the primary cilium and at cell membranes and cell-cell junctions of tubular epithelium, functioning as a mechano-/chemosensor regulating calcium and G-protein signaling. Every cell carries a germline mutant allele, but cysts develop in only a small fraction of nephrons, thought to arise from clonal growth of cells sustaining a somatic 'second hit' in the normal allele; a 'third hit' such as renal injury or an additional genetic/epigenetic event may accelerate cyst formation. Loss of ciliary PC1/PC2 function is hypothesized to cause aberrant calcium signaling, increased adenylyl cyclase and decreased phosphodiesterase activity, raising cellular cAMP; increased cAMP promotes protein kinase A activity, driving cyst growth through proliferation and chloride/aquaporin-mediated fluid secretion. Cellular defects include increased proliferation and fluid secretion, decreased differentiation, and abnormal extracellular matrix; affected signaling pathways include Wnt, mTOR, STAT3, cMET, PI3K/Akt, GPCR, EGFR, and CFTR. Rarer ADPKD-associated genes GANAB and DNAJB11 affect PC1 trafficking and cause milder cystic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD, PKHD1) - distal/collecting-duct cysts, oligohydramnios if severe, hypertension, congenital hepatic fibrosis and Caroli disease; absence of renal cysts in either parent >40y on ultrasound helps distinguish ARPKD from ADPKD (p.2430, p.2434)","ADPKD-like disease (GANAB, DNAJB11) - normal-to-smaller kidneys with fewer cysts, variable liver cysts (p.2430-2432)","Autosomal dominant tubulointerstitial kidney disease (ADTKD; UMOD, MUC1, REN, HNF1B, SEC61A1) - small fibrotic kidneys, medullary cysts, gout in adults (p.2430)","Tuberous sclerosis (TSC1/TSC2) - renal cysts (20-30%), angiomyolipomas, increased risk of renal cell carcinoma, facial angiofibromas, CNS hamartomas; TSC2 is adjacent to PKD1 so a contiguous gene deletion can cause both ADPKD and TS (p.2430, p.2434)","Von Hippel-Lindau disease (VHL) - renal cysts, renal cell carcinoma, retinal angiomas, CNS hemangioblastomas, pheochromocytomas (p.2430)","Nephronophthisis and other ciliopathies (e.g., Bardet-Biedl, Meckel-Gruber, Senior-Loken) - cystic kidney phenotypes with syndromic features (p.2430)","Renal cysts and diabetes syndrome (HNF1B) - kidney cysts with aberrant nephrogenesis and diabetes (p.2430)","Acquired simple renal cysts (increase in frequency with age and can confound diagnostic cyst counts) (p.2432-2433)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Renal ultrasonography - first-line for presymptomatic screening and donor evaluation; age-stratified cyst-count criteria for diagnosis (p.2432-2433)","CT and T2-weighted MRI (with/without contrast) - more sensitive than ultrasound, detect smaller cysts (MRI with gadolinium can detect 2-3 mm cysts); useful to distinguish malignancy from a complex cyst (p.2432-2433)","Magnetic resonance angiography for presymptomatic intracranial aneurysm screening in patients with a positive family history of aneurysm (p.2432)","CT scan as first diagnostic test for suspected subarachnoid hemorrhage, with lumbar puncture to confirm (p.2432)","Genetic testing by linkage and mutational analysis for ambiguous cases; PKD1 analysis is difficult and costly owing to gene size and homologous pseudogenes (paired-end next-generation sequencing may improve this) (p.2433)"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe headache or 'warning headache' suggesting subarachnoid hemorrhage from ruptured intracranial (berry) aneurysm (p.2432)","Positive family history of intracranial aneurysm - a risk factor for aneurysm rupture warranting presymptomatic MRA screening (p.2432)","Fever and flank pain indicating infected cyst or acute pyelonephritis (with or without bacteremia); infection is the second most common cause of death (p.2432)","Gross hematuria from cyst rupture (p.2432)","Diffuse arterial dolichoectasia of the cerebral circulation predisposing to arterial dissection and stroke (p.2432)"],"prognosis":"ADPKD progression shows striking inter- and intrafamilial variability; disease can present as early as in utero, but end-stage renal disease typically occurs in late middle age. Risk factors for progression include early diagnosis, hypertension, gross hematuria, multiple pregnancies, and large kidney size; PKD2 disease is generally milder with later onset. More than half of patients eventually require peritoneal dialysis, hemodialysis, or kidney transplantation. Cardiovascular complications are the major cause of mortality, and renal infection is the second most common cause of death (p.2431-2433).","pitfalls":["Proteinuria is usually only a MINOR feature of ADPKD, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis (p.2432).","Renal cell carcinoma is NOT increased in frequency compared with the general population, but when it does occur in ADPKD it is more often bilateral, multicentric, and sarcomatoid; radiologic imaging is often unable to distinguish cyst infection from cyst hemorrhage due to their complexity (p.2432).","Diagnostic cyst-count thresholds rise with age because simple renal cysts increase with age; criteria have lower sensitivity for PKD2 patients owing to late onset (p.2432-2433).","No specific FDA-approved treatment prevents cyst growth or the decline of renal function; more rigorous blood pressure control does not equal greater benefit, and targeting systolic pressure to 110 mmHg in moderate/advanced disease may worsen progression by reducing renal blood flow (p.2433).","Most renal cysts are not connected to glomerular filtration, so cyst infection requires lipid-soluble antibiotics that penetrate cyst walls (e.g., trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, quinolones, chloramphenicol), often for 4-6 weeks (p.2433).","Polycystic liver disease in ADPKD is distinct from isolated autosomal dominant polycystic liver disease (ADPLD; PRKCSH/SEC63), which does NOT progress to renal failure (p.2432)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal stage-based severity classification for ADPKD. Genotype broadly stratifies severity (PKD1 disease ~85% of cases is more severe with earlier onset than PKD2 disease ~15%; GANAB/DNAJB11 mutations cause milder cystic disease). Risk of rapid progression - used to select patients for tolvaptan - is estimated by combining genotypic and imaging (kidney growth rate) information; recognized clinical risk factors for progression include early diagnosis, hypertension, gross hematuria, multiple pregnancies, and large kidney size (p.2431-2433).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2430"],"crossLinks":["ckd","sah","renal_colic","htn_emergency"]},"polycystic_ovary_syndrome":{"name":"Polycystic ovary syndrome","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PCOS","polycystic ovarian syndrome","Stein-Leventhal syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["PCOS is diagnosed using the Rotterdam criteria, requiring at least two of oligo- or anovulation, clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism, and polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound, after excluding mimics","Common presentations include irregular or absent periods, hirsutism and acne, subfertility, and difficulty with weight","Insulin resistance is central to the syndrome and is often present regardless of body weight, though obesity worsens it","It is the leading cause of anovulatory infertility","Unopposed oestrogen from chronic anovulation increases the long-term risk of endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma, so regular endometrial protection is important","Lifestyle modification with weight management is the first-line intervention and can restore ovulation and improve metabolic profile","Management is symptom-directed: cycle regulation and endometrial protection, treatment of hyperandrogenic features, and ovulation induction when fertility is desired","Screening for and managing metabolic complications, including impaired glucose tolerance, type 2 diabetes, and dyslipidaemia, is an essential part of long-term care"],"pathophysiology":"PCOS reflects a complex interplay of hormonal and metabolic disturbances. Insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinaemia stimulates ovarian theca cells to overproduce androgens and reduces hepatic sex hormone-binding globulin, raising free androgen levels. Altered gonadotrophin secretion, with a relatively high luteinising hormone drive, further promotes androgen production and disrupts follicular maturation, so multiple small antral follicles accumulate without a dominant follicle emerging, causing anovulation. Chronic unopposed oestrogen exposure from anovulation places the endometrium at risk of hyperplasia.","additionalDifferentials":["Thyroid dysfunction","Hyperprolactinaemia","Non-classical congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Cushing syndrome","Androgen-secreting ovarian or adrenal tumour","Functional hypothalamic amenorrhoea"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum total and free testosterone and other androgens","Luteinising hormone, follicle-stimulating hormone, and sex hormone-binding globulin","Thyroid function and prolactin to exclude alternative causes","17-hydroxyprogesterone to screen for non-classical congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Transvaginal ultrasound for ovarian morphology and antral follicle count","Oral glucose tolerance test and lipid profile to assess metabolic risk"],"redFlags":["Rapid onset or severe virilisation suggesting an androgen-secreting tumour rather than PCOS","Prolonged amenorrhoea with abnormal bleeding raising concern for endometrial hyperplasia or carcinoma","Markedly elevated androgens beyond the range typical of PCOS","Features of Cushing syndrome","Severe metabolic derangement such as newly diagnosed diabetes"],"prognosis":"PCOS is a lifelong condition whose reproductive symptoms often improve with weight management and targeted therapy, and many women conceive with or without ovulation induction. The main long-term concerns are metabolic, with increased risks of type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and, through chronic anovulation, endometrial cancer; proactive metabolic surveillance improves outcomes.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing PCOS without excluding thyroid disease, hyperprolactinaemia, and other mimics","Relying on ultrasound alone, since polycystic ovarian morphology can occur in healthy women","Overlooking endometrial protection in women with prolonged amenorrhoea","Focusing only on fertility and neglecting long-term metabolic and cardiovascular risk","Attributing severe or rapidly progressive virilisation to PCOS"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"polycythemia_vera_and_other_myeloprolifera":{"name":"Polycythemia Vera and Other Myeloproliferative Neoplasms","system":"Hematologic/Oncologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Myeloproliferative neoplasms","MPN","PV","Primary myelofibrosis","PMF","Agnogenic myeloid metaplasia","Myelofibrosis with myeloid metaplasia","Essential thrombocytosis","Essential thrombocythemia","ET"],"chapter":108,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.818","clinicalPearls":["The classic chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms covered here (PV, PMF, ET) all arise from a hematopoietic stem cell and share constitutive activation of JAK2 through driver mutations in JAK2, MPL, or CALR; their natural histories are usually measured in decades, and leukemic transformation is uncommon in the absence of chemotherapy or radiation (Harrison 22e p.818).","JAK2 V617F is expressed in more than 95% of PV patients and in roughly 50% of PMF and ET patients; most JAK2-negative PV patients carry a JAK2 exon 12 mutation, while a few have LNK or calreticulin mutations (Harrison 22e p.818-819).","In PV the plasma volume is expanded, unlike other causes of true erythrocytosis, so an elevated red cell mass can be masked, especially in women; below a hemoglobin of about 20 g/dL (hematocrit 60%) one cannot reliably separate true erythrocytosis from plasma-volume contraction without red cell mass and plasma volume measurement (Harrison 22e p.819-820).","PV should be suspected in any woman presenting with hepatic vein (Budd-Chiari) thrombosis, since this is the type of thrombosis particularly associated with JAK2 V617F; portal vein thrombosis is more common in male PV patients (Harrison 22e p.819).","Aquagenic pruritus and erythromelalgia are the symptoms that can suggest PV over other causes of erythrocytosis; pruritus may reflect JAK2 V617F-driven mast cell activation, and Helicobacter pylori incidence is increased in PV (Harrison 22e p.819-820).","Only three conditions cause microcytic erythrocytosis: beta-thalassemia trait, hypoxic erythrocytosis, and PV; the red cell distribution width is typically normal in thalassemia trait but may be elevated in hypoxic erythrocytosis or PV when iron deficiency coexists (Harrison 22e p.820).","In a PV patient with massive splenomegaly, a 'normal' hematocrit or hemoglobin should be regarded as indicating an elevated red cell mass until proven otherwise, because the associated plasma volume increase masks the true red cell mass (Harrison 22e p.820).","In PMF the peripheral smear shows a leukoerythroblastic picture with teardrop-shaped red cells, nucleated red cells, myelocytes, and promyelocytes; marrow is usually inaspirable ('dry tap') owing to fibrosis, and bone x-rays may show osteosclerosis (Harrison 22e p.821-822).","PMF marrow fibroblasts are polyclonal and not part of the neoplastic clone; fibrosis is driven by overproduction of transforming growth factor beta, tissue inhibitors of metalloproteinases, and thrombopoietin, while osteosclerosis relates to osteoprotegerin and angiogenesis to vascular endothelial growth factor (Harrison 22e p.821).","In ET the large platelet mass can cause spurious (test-tube) hyperkalemia from platelet potassium release on clotting, with no electrocardiographic correlate, and can make arterial oxygen measurements inaccurate unless blood is collected on ice (Harrison 22e p.823).","Very high platelet counts (above about 1 million/microL) are associated primarily with bleeding from acquired von Willebrand disease due to proteolysis of high-molecular-weight von Willebrand multimers, not with thrombosis; tobacco use is the most important thrombotic risk factor in ET (Harrison 22e p.823-824).","PMF and ET diagnoses are largely diagnoses of exclusion: significant splenomegaly in apparent ET suggests another MPN, and FISH for BCR-ABL is preferred to exclude CML in JAK2-negative thrombocytosis because reverse-transcriptase PCR for BCR-ABL is prone to false positives (Harrison 22e p.823-824)."],"pathophysiology":"These chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms originate in a single hematopoietic stem cell and produce overabundant mature blood elements without significant dysplasia, together with a tendency toward extramedullary hematopoiesis, myelofibrosis, and eventual leukemic transformation. PV, PMF, and ET share constitutive activation of the nonreceptor tyrosine kinase JAK2, the cognate kinase and chaperone for the erythropoietin and thrombopoietin receptors. The JAK2 V617F mutation in the autoinhibitory pseudokinase domain causes ligand-independent kinase activation, explaining erythropoietin hypersensitivity, erythropoietin-independent erythroid colony growth, increased Bcl-XL expression, and apoptosis resistance in PV progenitors. Loss of heterozygosity on chromosome 9p (uniparental disomy) leads to JAK2 V617F homozygosity, common in PV, less so in PMF, and rare in ET. JAK2 V617F alone does not account for the entire phenotype and is probably not the initiating lesion: the same mutation yields different phenotypes in ET versus PMF, familial PV can occur without it, and leukemia may arise in a JAK2-negative progenitor, implying an ancestral precursor. PMF and ET may instead carry MPL (thrombopoietin receptor) or CALR (calreticulin) mutations; mutant CALR binds and activates MPL at the cell surface. In PMF, marrow fibrosis is a reactive, polyclonal fibroblast response driven by cytokines from the clone (TGF-beta, thrombopoietin), with extramedullary hematopoiesis in liver and spleen. Megakaryocyte genome reduplication is endomitotic and thrombopoietin-driven; paradoxically, MPL expression is reduced and plasma thrombopoietin is increased across these MPNs despite the expanded megakaryocyte/platelet mass.","additionalDifferentials":["Relative (spurious/stress) erythrocytosis from plasma volume contraction (Gaisbock's syndrome) due to dehydration, diuretics, ethanol, androgens, or tobacco","Hypoxic erythrocytosis (pulmonary disease, right-to-left shunt, sleep apnea, high altitude, carbon monoxide, high-oxygen-affinity hemoglobin)","Tumor- or renal-disease-associated erythrocytosis (hypernephroma, hepatoma, cerebellar hemangioblastoma, uterine myoma, pheochromocytoma, renal artery stenosis, renal cysts, post-transplant)","Congenital/familial erythrocytosis (erythropoietin receptor, VHL/Chuvash, 2,3-BPG, PHD2/EGLN1, HIF2alpha/EPAS1, LNK mutations) and recombinant erythropoietin or SGLT2-inhibitor effect","Reactive thrombocytosis (iron deficiency, infection, inflammation, malignancy, hemorrhage, surgery, postsplenectomy, B12/folate-deficiency rebound)","Chronic myeloid leukemia (BCR-ABL positive), myelodysplasia including 5q- syndrome and sideroblastic anemia (SF3B1)","Secondary myelofibrosis (myelophthisis) from metastatic carcinoma, lymphoma, multiple myeloma, hairy cell leukemia, systemic mastocytosis, and nonmalignant causes such as HIV, tuberculosis, SLE, hyperparathyroidism, renal osteodystrophy, vitamin D deficiency"],"infectionMimics":["HIV infection (can cause secondary myelofibrosis)","Tuberculosis (can cause secondary myelofibrosis and constitutional symptoms)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Reactive thrombocytosis from iron deficiency, inflammation, or malignancy mimicking ET","Stress/spurious erythrocytosis (Gaisbock's syndrome) mimicking PV","Chronic myeloid leukemia mimicking ET or PMF","Myelodysplastic syndromes (5q- syndrome, sideroblastic anemia) presenting with thrombocytosis","Metastatic carcinoma, lymphoma, or systemic mastocytosis causing myelophthisic marrow fibrosis","Systemic lupus erythematosus, hyperparathyroidism, vitamin D deficiency, and renal osteodystrophy causing nonmalignant myelofibrosis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative next-generation-sequencing assay for JAK2 V617F or JAK2 exon 12 mutations (now supersedes surrogate tests for PV diagnosis)","Red cell mass and plasma volume determination to confirm absolute versus relative erythrocytosis when hemoglobin is below ~20 g/dL","Serum erythropoietin level (elevated level favors a secondary cause; a normal level does not exclude PV)","Red cell count, mean corpuscular volume, and red cell distribution width (microcytic erythrocytosis narrows differential)","Mutation testing for MPL and CALR (exon 9, type 1 deletions and type 2 insertions) in JAK2-negative ET/PMF","FISH for BCR-ABL to exclude CML in JAK2-negative thrombocytosis","Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy (megakaryocyte clustering/hyperplasia, reticulin/collagen fibrosis in PMF; absent stainable iron with hypercellularity favors PV)","Cytogenetic analysis (del 20q, del 13q, trisomy 8/9, partial trisomy 1q; complex karyotype portends poor prognosis in PMF)","Mutational analysis of ASXL1, EZH2, SRSF2, and IDH1/2 for refined PMF prognosis","Ristocetin cofactor activity to detect acquired von Willebrand disease at very high platelet counts","Serum lactate dehydrogenase and alkaline phosphatase (may be elevated in PMF), uric acid"],"redFlags":["Hepatic vein thrombosis (Budd-Chiari) in a young woman, or portal/mesenteric vein thrombosis, as a presenting feature of PV","Neurologic hyperviscosity symptoms: vertigo, tinnitus, headache, visual disturbance, transient ischemic attack","Arterial or venous thrombosis in cerebral, cardiac, or mesenteric vessels","Platelet count above 1 million/microL with bleeding from acquired von Willebrand disease","Sudden splenic enlargement with painful splenic infarction (fever, pleuritic chest pain)","Development of transfusion-dependent anemia, increasing organomegaly, or peripheral blood blasts signaling marrow failure or accelerated/blastic transformation in PMF","Constitutional symptoms (night sweats, fatigue, weight loss) suggesting a myeloproliferative rather than reactive process","Spinal cord compression, pericardial tamponade, or ureteral/intestinal obstruction from exuberant extramedullary hematopoiesis in PMF"],"prognosis":"PV is generally indolent, with a clinical course measured in decades; most patients live long lives without functional impairment when red cell mass is controlled, and the incidence of acute leukemia is very low in those not exposed to chemotherapy or radiation. Myelofibrosis is part of the natural history of PV but is usually reactive and reversible without prognostic significance, although about 15% develop hematopoietic stem cell failure with extramedullary hematopoiesis and transfusion-dependent anemia. PMF has the shortest survival of the three: it follows a course of progressive marrow failure and organomegaly, can evolve to an accelerated phase, and about 10% transform spontaneously to a treatment-refractory acute leukemia. PMF survival is stratified by IPSS, DIPSS, and DIPSS-Plus risk factors (anemia, leukocytosis, peripheral blasts, constitutional symptoms, age over 65, unfavorable karyotype, thrombocytopenia, transfusion dependence); ASXL1, EZH2, SRSF2, and IDH1/2 mutations and complex cytogenetics independently predict early death or leukemic transformation, and triple-negative PMF carries the worst prognosis. ET is more benign than previously thought; survival is comparable to the general population regardless of driver mutation, and leukemic evolution is more often a consequence of therapy than of the disease.","pitfalls":["Treating the platelet count rather than the patient in ET: a high platelet count alone does not establish thrombotic risk, and the cause of any symptom must be confirmed to be platelet-related before therapy","Assuming a high platelet count causes thrombosis when very high counts more often cause hemorrhage via acquired von Willebrand disease; aspirin can promote bleeding in this setting","Interpreting a normal hematocrit/hemoglobin as excluding elevated red cell mass in a PV patient with massive splenomegaly (plasma volume expansion masks it)","Relying on a low-level JAK2 V617F allele burden (VAF 5% or less) to diagnose a blood disease, since not everyone with low burden has one","Assuming a normal serum erythropoietin excludes PV (it does not), or overlooking that an elevated level points to a secondary cause","Diagnosing ET in a JAK2 V617F-positive patient without red cell mass/plasma volume studies, since many such patients actually have PV","Misreading spurious hyperkalemia or inaccurate arterial oxygen values caused by the large platelet mass in ET","Mistaking secondary myelofibrosis/myelophthisis for PMF and missing a treatable underlying disorder","Using leukemogenic agents (alkylating agents, radioactive phosphorus-32) and prolonged hydroxyurea, which is itself mutagenic and antagonizes TP53, in disorders with otherwise long survival"],"severityClassification":"For primary myelofibrosis, prognostic risk is estimated by the IPSS (2009), DIPSS (2010), and DIPSS-Plus (2011) scoring systems. Risk factors include anemia (hemoglobin <10 g/dL), leukocytosis (>25,000/microL), peripheral blood blasts (>=1%), constitutional symptoms, and age over 65; DIPSS-Plus adds unfavorable karyotype, platelet count <100,000/microL, and transfusion dependence. In DIPSS, anemia is weighted as 2 points. Categories run Low, Intermediate-1, Intermediate-2, and High by number/weight of factors. These systems are not accurate for risk assessment of PV or ET patients who have developed secondary myelofibrosis.","references":["Harrison 22e p.818","Harrison 22e p.819","Harrison 22e p.820","Harrison 22e p.821","Harrison 22e p.822","Harrison 22e p.823","Harrison 22e p.824"],"crossLinks":[]},"polymorphic_ventricular_tachycardia_and_ve":{"name":"Polymorphic Ventricular Tachycardia and Ventricular Fibrillation","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Polymorphic VT","Torsades des pointes","Ventricular fibrillation","VF"],"chapter":262,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1968","clinicalPearls":["Sustained polymorphic VT shows a QRS configuration that changes continuously beat to beat, reflecting an ever-changing ventricular activation sequence; unlike monomorphic VT it does not necessarily imply a fixed structural abnormality or single automatic focus (p.1968).","Sustained polymorphic VT commonly degenerates into ventricular fibrillation (p.1969).","Acute myocardial infarction or ischemia is a frequent cause and should be the first consideration; roughly 10% of acute MI patients develop VT that degenerates to VF, with greatest risk in the first hour (p.1969).","Polymorphic VT/VF occurring within the first 48 hours of acute MI carries higher in-hospital mortality, but survivors to discharge are not at increased risk of later arrhythmic sudden death (p.1969).","Torsades des pointes is the polymorphic VT linked to QT prolongation and classically begins in a pause-dependent fashion: a PVC induces a pause, the following sinus beat has a longer QT, and a PVC interrupting that T wave starts the run (p.1969).","Congenital long QT syndrome stems from ion-channel gene mutations; LQTS-1, -2, and -3 account for roughly 80-90% of cases, with LQTS-1/-2 involving potassium channels and LQTS-3 involving the sodium channel (p.1969-1970).","Genotype influences triggers: LQTS-1 events tend to occur during exertion (notably swimming), LQTS-2 with sudden auditory stimuli or emotional upset, and LQTS-3 sudden death tends to occur during sleep (p.1970).","Brugada syndrome causes syncope or cardiac arrest from polymorphic VT in the absence of structural heart disease; arrest may occur during sleep or be provoked by febrile illness, and males are more commonly affected (p.1970).","Catecholaminergic polymorphic VT is a rare familial disorder of abnormal sarcoplasmic calcium handling producing arrhythmias resembling digitalis toxicity, often with bidirectional (alternating QRS morphology) VT, presenting in childhood with exertion- or emotion-induced events (p.1971).","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, the most common genetic cardiovascular disorder (about 1 in 500), is a prominent cause of sudden death before age 35, typically via polymorphic VT/VF (p.1971).","Ventricular fibrillation shows disordered electrical ventricular activation with no identifiable QRS complexes; spiral wave reentry and multiple circulating reentry wavefronts are proposed mechanisms (p.1971).","After surviving acute MI and revascularization, poorly coupled but surviving Purkinje fibers (which tolerate ischemia owing to glycogen stores and oxygenation from the LV cavity) can trigger PVCs and ventricular fibrillation (p.1971)."],"pathophysiology":"Polymorphic VT arises from a continually changing ventricular activation sequence. Proposed mechanisms include reentry with variable paths, spiral wave reentry, and multiple automatic foci, often near fibrotic myocardium and potentiated by damaged Purkinje cells or ventricular hypertrophy. Abnormal transmural dispersion of repolarization can drive it in the setting of channelopathies or antiarrhythmic drugs even without structural disease. In acute MI/ischemia, reentry through the infarct border zone is implicated, and surviving automatic Purkinje cells can initiate it. Torsades des pointes is associated with abnormal QT prolongation and pause-dependent initiation. Congenital LQTS reflects mutations in ion-channel genes governing repolarization (potassium channels in LQTS-1/-2, sodium channel in LQTS-3, and calcium channels in others). Short QT syndrome reflects a gain of function of the IKr potassium channel or reduced inward depolarizing currents. Brugada syndrome reflects dispersion of repolarization across the ventricular wall, with epicardial and M cells more affected than endocardial cells, localized to the right ventricular outflow tract; sodium channel mutations (SCN5A, SCN1B, SCN10A) are found in about 25% of cases. Catecholaminergic polymorphic VT results from cardiac ryanodine receptor (and less often calsequestrin 2) mutations causing abnormal sarcoplasmic calcium handling. Ventricular fibrillation represents disordered activation without organized QRS, frequently as degeneration of sustained polymorphic or monomorphic VT (p.1968-1971).","additionalDifferentials":["Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia (implies a fixed structural substrate or automatic focus, unlike polymorphic VT)","Acquired (drug- or electrolyte-induced) long QT with torsades des pointes","Congenital long QT syndrome (LQTS-1, -2, -3)","Short QT syndrome","Brugada syndrome","Early repolarization syndrome","Catecholaminergic polymorphic VT (bidirectional VT)","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Genetic dilated cardiomyopathies (e.g., Lamin A/C, SCN5A)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Brugada pattern ST elevation must be distinguished from LV hypertrophy, pericarditis, myocardial ischemia/MI, hyperkalemia, hypothermia, right bundle branch block, and arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (p.1970)","Asymptomatic Brugada ECG pattern versus the clinical Brugada syndrome (analogous to Wolff-Parkinson-White pattern versus syndrome)","J-point elevation as a benign normal variant versus early repolarization syndrome associated with arrhythmia risk"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG with measurement of corrected QT (QTc); congenital LQTS typically prolongs QTc to >440 ms in men and >460 ms in women (p.1969)","Short QT syndrome: QTc <360 ms, usually <300 ms (p.1970)","Telemetry/rhythm strip to capture pause-dependent initiation of torsades and characteristic morphologies (p.1969)","Genetic testing/genotyping for family screening and diagnostic reassurance in LQTS (p.1970)","Provocative sodium channel blocker testing (e.g., flecainide, ajmaline, procainamide) to unmask Brugada ST elevation (p.1970)","Assessment of residual LV function/ejection fraction to guide ICD decisions (p.1969)","Reference resource crediblemeds.org to check whether a medication has been reported to prolong the QT interval (p.1969)"],"redFlags":["Near-syncope, syncope, or cardiac arrest as the presenting event (p.1969)","Repeated episodes of polymorphic VT suggesting ongoing myocardial infarction/inadequate reperfusion (p.1969)","QTc exceeding 500 ms, female gender, and prior syncope or cardiac arrest as markers of increased risk in LQTS (p.1970)","Family history of sudden death (relevant in Brugada syndrome, early repolarization syndrome, and genetic cardiomyopathies) (p.1970-1971)","Febrile illness provoking cardiac arrest in Brugada syndrome (p.1970)","HCM features: young age, nonsustained VT, failure of blood pressure to rise with exercise, recent syncope (within 6 months), ventricular wall thickness >3 cm (p.1971)"],"prognosis":"Polymorphic VT/VF within the first 48 hours of acute MI is associated with greater in-hospital mortality, yet patients who survive to discharge are not at increased risk for later arrhythmic sudden death. Long-term risk after infarction is governed largely by residual LV function. In LQTS, higher risk is conferred by QTc >500 ms, female gender, and prior syncope or cardiac arrest. HCM is a prominent cause of sudden death before age 35; surgical myectomy carries a sudden death rate <1% per year, and transcoronary ethanol septal ablation has a reported annual rate of sustained VT or sudden death of roughly 1-5% (p.1969-1971).","pitfalls":["Assuming polymorphic VT implies a fixed structural focus as monomorphic VT does (p.1968)","Overlooking ongoing ischemia/inadequate reperfusion when polymorphic VT/VF recurs after MI (p.1969)","Failing to recognize numerous noncardiac QT-prolonging drugs and drug interactions that raise levels of an offending agent as precipitants of torsades (p.1969)","Re-exposing a patient who had QT-prolongation-induced polymorphic VT to QT-prolonging medications; such patients should be considered susceptible and avoid all future exposure (p.1969)","Misinterpreting benign J-point elevation (a common normal variant) as pathologic early repolarization syndrome (p.1971)","Confusing the asymptomatic Brugada ECG pattern with true Brugada syndrome (p.1970)"],"severityClassification":"No single formal severity grade; risk is stratified by substrate. In post-MI patients, an ICD is indicated for persistent severe LV dysfunction (LV ejection fraction <35%). In LQTS, high-risk features include QTc >500 ms, female gender, and prior syncope/cardiac arrest. In HCM, high-risk markers include young age, nonsustained VT, failure of blood pressure to rise with exercise, recent syncope, and ventricular wall thickness >3 cm (p.1969-1971).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1968","Harrison 22e p.1969","Harrison 22e p.1970","Harrison 22e p.1971","Harrison 22e p.1972"],"crossLinks":[]},"polymyositis":{"name":"Polymyositis","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PM","idiopathic inflammatory myopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with progressive symmetric proximal muscle weakness affecting shoulder and hip girdles, causing difficulty rising from chairs, climbing stairs, and lifting the arms","Weakness rather than pain predominates, and there is no characteristic rash (distinguishing it from dermatomyositis)","Serum creatine kinase is typically markedly elevated, reflecting muscle fibre damage","Myositis-specific antibodies, including the antisynthetase antibodies (e.g. anti-Jo-1), help define clinical subsets","The antisynthetase syndrome combines myositis with interstitial lung disease, arthritis, Raynaud phenomenon, fever, and 'mechanic's hands'","It is a diagnosis of exclusion; inclusion body myositis in older patients and other myopathies must be considered before labelling weakness as polymyositis","Muscle biopsy classically shows endomysial CD8 T-cell infiltration surrounding and invading non-necrotic fibres","Corticosteroids are the mainstay of initial treatment, with steroid-sparing immunosuppressants added to control disease and limit steroid toxicity"],"pathophysiology":"Polymyositis is an idiopathic inflammatory myopathy driven by cell-mediated autoimmunity. Cytotoxic CD8 T lymphocytes recognise antigens expressed on muscle fibres and invade non-necrotic myofibres, causing fibre necrosis and regeneration and consequent weakness. Upregulation of MHC class I on muscle fibres is characteristic. Associations with specific autoantibodies define distinct phenotypes, and there is overlap with other connective tissue diseases and, less strongly than in dermatomyositis, with malignancy.","additionalDifferentials":["Dermatomyositis","Inclusion body myositis","Immune-mediated necrotising myopathy (including statin-associated)","Metabolic, mitochondrial, or muscular dystrophy","Endocrine myopathy (hypothyroidism, Cushing) and drug-induced myopathy","Motor neuron disease and myasthenia gravis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum creatine kinase and other muscle enzymes","Myositis-specific and myositis-associated antibody panel including anti-Jo-1","Electromyography showing a myopathic pattern with irritability","MRI of muscle to identify oedema and guide biopsy","Muscle biopsy for endomysial inflammation; screening for interstitial lung disease with pulmonary function tests and high-resolution CT"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive dyspnoea or hypoxia from interstitial lung disease, especially with antisynthetase antibodies","Bulbar or respiratory muscle weakness causing dysphagia, aspiration, or ventilatory compromise","Cardiac involvement with arrhythmia or heart failure","Features suggesting an underlying occult malignancy","Severe dysphagia risking aspiration pneumonia"],"prognosis":"With treatment many patients regain strength, but relapses are common and prognosis is worse in the presence of interstitial lung disease, cardiac involvement, severe dysphagia, or associated malignancy. Long-term functional outcome depends on early treatment and control of extramuscular disease.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing polymyositis without excluding inclusion body myositis and necrotising myopathy, which respond differently to therapy","Overlooking interstitial lung disease, a major cause of morbidity and mortality","Attributing weakness solely to steroid myopathy during treatment without reassessing disease activity","Failing to screen for dysphagia and aspiration risk","Missing an associated malignancy or overlap connective tissue disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"polytrauma":{"name":"Polytrauma","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["multiple trauma","multisystem trauma","major trauma"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Assess and resuscitate simultaneously using the primary survey sequence: airway with cervical spine protection, breathing, circulation with haemorrhage control, disability, and exposure with environment control","Life threats identified during the primary survey are treated as they are found, before moving to the next step","The most common preventable cause of early death is uncontrolled haemorrhage, so external bleeding is compressed and internal sources are sought promptly","Permissive hypotension and balanced resuscitation with blood products help avoid dilutional coagulopathy in bleeding patients until definitive control is achieved","The lethal triad of hypothermia, acidosis, and coagulopathy worsens outcomes and guides damage-control strategies","A structured secondary survey (head-to-toe examination and history) is performed only once the patient is stabilised, to detect injuries missed initially","Reassess frequently, as trauma physiology is dynamic and deterioration can be rapid","Adjuncts such as focused ultrasound, chest and pelvic imaging, and blood gases are integrated into the primary survey"],"pathophysiology":"Polytrauma involves injury to two or more body regions or systems, at least one of which is life-threatening. Simultaneous tissue damage triggers haemorrhage, hypovolaemic shock, and a systemic inflammatory response. Hypoperfusion causes tissue hypoxia and lactic acidosis, while ongoing bleeding and crystalloid dilution impair clotting. Hypothermia further inhibits enzymatic coagulation, and the resulting lethal triad of hypothermia, acidosis, and coagulopathy establishes a self-reinforcing cycle. Later, immune activation predisposes to systemic inflammatory response syndrome, acute respiratory distress, and multi-organ dysfunction.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated major haemorrhage from a single cavity (chest, abdomen, pelvis, long bones)","Neurogenic shock from spinal cord injury","Cardiac tamponade or tension pneumothorax as the cause of shock","Medical collapse (e.g. cardiac event, seizure) preceding a traumatic mechanism","Toxicological or metabolic contribution to altered consciousness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Focused assessment with sonography for trauma (FAST/eFAST) to detect free fluid and pneumothorax","Chest and pelvic radiographs as primary-survey adjuncts","Whole-body CT (trauma pan-scan) in the stabilised patient to map injuries","Arterial blood gas with lactate and base deficit to gauge shock severity","Group and crossmatch, coagulation studies, and viscoelastic testing to guide transfusion"],"redFlags":["Persistent or recurrent hypotension despite resuscitation, indicating ongoing haemorrhage requiring immediate surgical or interventional control","Rising lactate or worsening base deficit signalling inadequate perfusion","Signs of the lethal triad (hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy) mandating damage-control resuscitation","Deteriorating conscious level suggesting evolving brain injury or shock","Mechanism or examination suggesting occult truncal or pelvic bleeding"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends on injury burden, time to haemorrhage control, and physiological reserve. Early definitive control of bleeding and correction of the lethal triad improve survival, whereas prolonged shock and multi-organ dysfunction carry high mortality.","pitfalls":["Completing the secondary survey before life threats in the primary survey are addressed","Over-resuscitation with crystalloid worsening dilutional coagulopathy and hypothermia","Anchoring on an obvious dramatic injury while missing a silent source of shock","Failing to reassess after each intervention as physiology evolves","Neglecting active warming and haemostatic resuscitation in the bleeding patient"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"porphyria":{"name":"Porphyria","system":"Metabolic / Hematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute intermittent porphyria","porphyria cutanea tarda"],"chapter":428,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3340-3348","clinicalPearls":["The porphyrias are metabolic disorders, each from deficient (or, for XLP, increased) activity of a specific enzyme in the heme biosynthetic pathway; inherited as autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, or X-linked, except porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT) which is usually sporadic (p.3340).","Classified as hepatic or erythropoietic (by primary site of porphyrin/precursor overproduction) and as acute or cutaneous (by clinical manifestation) (p.3340, 3344).","Acute hepatic porphyrias (AIP, HCP, VP) usually present in adult life after puberty with acute attacks of neurovisceral symptoms and elevated urinary porphyrin precursors ALA and/or PBG (p.3344).","Acute intermittent porphyria (AIP) is autosomal dominant from half-normal HMB-synthase (porphobilinogen deaminase) activity; the most common acute hepatic porphyria (~1 in 20,000 Caucasians), especially frequent in Scandinavia and Great Britain (p.3340, 3346).","AIP abdominal pain is the most common symptom: poorly localized, often with cramping, ileus, distention, and decreased bowel sounds (though increased bowel sounds and diarrhea may occur) (p.3346).","AIP attacks also feature nausea, vomiting, constipation, tachycardia, hypertension, mental symptoms, limb/head/neck/chest pain, muscle weakness, sensory loss, dysuria, and urinary retention; tachycardia, hypertension, restlessness, tremor, and sweating reflect sympathetic overactivity (p.3346).","AIP peripheral neuropathy is due to axonal degeneration (not demyelination) and primarily affects motor neurons; proximal muscles (shoulders/arms) are involved first, and it may be focal or involve cranial nerves (p.3346).","Mental symptoms in acute attacks include anxiety, insomnia, depression, disorientation, hallucinations, and paranoia; seizures may be neurologic or due to hyponatremia (p.3346).","Diagnosis of an acute attack rests on substantial elevation of urinary PBG (and ALA); urinary PBG is always increased during acute attacks of AIP, HCP, and VP and is not substantially raised in any other condition, making a spot urine PBG both sensitive and specific (p.3345-3346).","PCT is the most common porphyria overall; hepatic URO-decarboxylase is deficient in all types, and ~80% of patients have no UROD mutation (sporadic type 1) (p.3347).","PCT presents with blistering, photosensitive skin lesions most commonly on the backs of the hands that rupture, crust, and scar, with skin friability, milia, hypertrichosis, and hyperpigmentation; neurologic features are absent (p.3347).","An acute attack may begin with sharp abdominal pain, then agitation/hallucinations/seizures, with back and extremity pain followed by weakness several days later, mimicking Guillain-Barre syndrome; weakness may be asymmetric, proximal or distal, and involve facial/bulbar muscles (p.3602-3603).","Urine may appear brownish during an attack due to high porphyrin metabolite concentration (p.3602-3603)."],"pathophysiology":"Each porphyria results from deficient (or, in XLP, increased) activity of one of the eight enzymes of the heme biosynthetic pathway converting glycine and succinyl-CoA to heme. In acute hepatic porphyrias, half-normal enzyme activity becomes limiting when hepatic heme synthesis is increased: induction of the rate-limiting hepatic gene ALAS1 (regulated in part by PGC-1alpha, which is induced by fasting) leads to accumulation and urinary excretion of the porphyrin precursors ALA and PBG, which underlie the acute neurovisceral attacks. Common precipitating factors include endogenous and exogenous steroids (estrogens/progestins), porphyrinogenic drugs (often P450-metabolized), alcohol, low-calorie/low-carbohydrate diets, infections, and surgery. AIP is almost always latent before puberty, suggesting adult steroid hormone levels drive expression; symptoms are commoner in women, with premenstrual (luteal-phase progesterone) attacks. Hyponatremia results from hypothalamic involvement with inappropriate vasopressin secretion or from electrolyte depletion (vomiting, diarrhea, poor intake, renal sodium loss). In PCT, substantial deficiency of hepatic URO-decarboxylase (to ~20% of normal or less, via an iron- and oxidative-stress-dependent uroporphomethene inhibitor) causes accumulation of porphyrins that, on excitation by long-wave UV light in the skin, produce blistering photosensitivity (p.3340, 3345-3347).","additionalDifferentials":["Intestinal obstruction (acute abdomen) — pain of porphyria is usually difficult to distinguish because severe hyperperistalsis is prominent in both (p.3344-ch.40)","Lead colic / lead intoxication (lead inhibits ALA-dehydratase, raising urinary ALA and coproporphyrin III) (p.3342, ch.40)","Hereditary tyrosinemia type 1 (succinylacetone inhibits ALA-dehydratase, mimicking acute porphyria) (p.3342)","Guillain-Barre syndrome (acute attack with ascending weakness mimics GBS) (p.3603)","Chronic liver disease (can cause nonspecific increases in urinary porphyrins leading to misdiagnosis) (p.3345)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute surgical abdomen / intestinal obstruction (acute hepatic porphyria attack mimics this) (p.3344, ch.40)","Lead poisoning (lead colic and neuropathy mimic acute porphyria) (p.3342)","Guillain-Barre syndrome (ascending motor weakness in an acute attack) (p.3603)","Hereditary tyrosinemia type 1 (biochemical and clinical mimic of acute porphyria) (p.3342)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Spot (single-void) urine for porphobilinogen (PBG) — first-line; always raised in acute attacks of AIP/HCP/VP and specific; 24-h collection unnecessary (p.3345-3346)","Quantitative urinary ALA, PBG, and creatinine on the same spot sample (also detects ADP) (p.3345)","Total urinary porphyrins (interpret cautiously — often nonspecific) (p.3345)","Urinary, fecal, and plasma porphyrins to distinguish AIP from HCP and VP (fecal porphyrins normal/minimal in AIP) (p.3346)","Total plasma porphyrins with fluorometric scanning of diluted plasma at neutral pH (distinguishes VP from PCT; VP emission peak ~626 nm) for cutaneous porphyrias (p.3346, 3348)","Erythrocyte protoporphyrin (more sensitive for EPP/XLP; confirm EPP by predominant free, not zinc, protoporphyrin) (p.3346)","Isocoproporphyrins in feces — diagnostic of hepatic URO-decarboxylase deficiency (PCT) (p.3348)","Erythrocyte URO-decarboxylase activity (~50% in type 2 PCT; markedly deficient, 3-10%, in HEP) (p.3348)","Molecular genetic mutation analysis of the relevant heme-pathway gene (definitive; diagnostic even when precursor levels have normalized, and for latent heterozygotes/relatives) (p.3342, 3346)"],"redFlags":["Acute neurovisceral attack: severe poorly-localized abdominal pain with autonomic instability (tachycardia, hypertension, sweating), motor neuropathy/weakness, and psychiatric disturbance — the AIP acute hepatic porphyria red-flag constellation (p.3346)","Hyponatremia (from inappropriate vasopressin secretion or electrolyte depletion) and seizures during an attack (p.3346)","Markedly raised urinary PBG/ALA in a patient with abdominal pain after puberty (p.3345-3346)","Exposure to a porphyria-precipitating drug, low-calorie diet, alcohol, infection, or surgery preceding the attack (p.3346)","Progression to respiratory and bulbar paralysis; sudden death may result from sympathetic overactivity and cardiac arrhythmia, especially with delayed diagnosis/treatment (p.3346)"],"prognosis":"AIP can be disabling but is rarely fatal; the prognosis of individuals with HMBS mutations is generally favorable, and most heterozygotes remain latent. When an attack resolves, abdominal pain may disappear within hours and paresis begins to improve within days, sometimes continuing to improve over years; recovery from severe motor neuropathy may take months to years and depends on the degree of neuronal damage and early treatment. Long-term complications of AIP include increased risk of hypertension and chronic kidney disease (up to 59% develop CKD), chronic liver function abnormalities with increased risk of hepatocellular carcinoma (warranting hepatic imaging at least every 6 months), and persistent neuropathy, fatigue, chronic pain, nausea, depression, and anxiety. Homozygous dominant AIP usually presents in infancy and most affected children die at an early age. PCT can almost always be brought into complete remission with treatment, but patients characteristically have chronic liver disease, sometimes cirrhosis, and are at risk for hepatocellular carcinoma (p.3346-3348).","pitfalls":["A great mimic of the acute abdomen: porphyric (and lead colic) pain is hard to distinguish from intestinal obstruction because of prominent hyperperistalsis — risking unnecessary surgery (p.3344, ch.40).","Because symptoms are nonspecific and rarely occur before puberty, diagnosis is often delayed; a high index of suspicion is required (p.3340, 3346).","Measuring urinary porphyrins alone for screening should be avoided — minimal nonspecific increases (e.g., in chronic liver disease) cause misdiagnosis; use urinary PBG (p.3345).","Erythrocyte HMB-synthase assay is not a useful first-line test: activity is not decreased in all AIP patients, borderline-low values are not diagnostic, and it is not deficient in other acute porphyrias; some HMBS mutations give normal erythrocyte enzyme so genetic testing is needed (p.3345-3346).","ALA/PBG levels often remain high after symptoms resolve, so diagnosing an acute attack in a biochemically proven AIP patient relies primarily on clinical features (p.3346).","Most antiseizure drugs can exacerbate AIP, making seizure treatment difficult (clonazepam may be safer than phenytoin or barbiturates) (p.3346).","Abdominal tenderness, fever, and leukocytosis are usually absent or mild in AIP because the process is neurologic, not inflammatory — their prominence should prompt reconsideration (p.3346).","In PCT, neurologic features are absent — abdominal/neurovisceral attacks should not be attributed to it (p.3347)."],"severityClassification":"Porphyrias are classified by primary overproduction site (hepatic vs erythropoietic) and by clinical manifestation (acute neurovisceral vs cutaneous). Acute hepatic porphyrias: AIP, HCP, VP (and the rare autosomal recessive ADP) present with acute neurovisceral attacks and raised ALA/PBG; AIP causes neurovisceral disease without photosensitivity, whereas HCP and VP can have both neurovisceral attacks and PCT-like cutaneous photosensitivity. Cutaneous: PCT (and HEP) present with chronic blistering photosensitivity. Erythropoietic porphyrias (CEP, EPP, XLP) present with cutaneous photosensitivity (EPP/XLP non-blistering, immediate and painful). Acute-attack severity ranges from mild (abdominal pain without paresis or hyponatremia, manageable with carbohydrate loading) to severe (paresis, hyponatremia, bulbar/respiratory paralysis) (p.3340, 3344, 3346-3347).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3340-3348","Harrison 22e p.3602-3603"],"crossLinks":["peripheral_neuropathy","hyponatremia"]},"post_operative_nausea_vomiting":{"name":"Postoperative nausea and vomiting","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PONV","post-anaesthetic nausea and vomiting","postoperative emesis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common and distressing complication after anaesthesia that reduces patient satisfaction, delays discharge, and occasionally causes serious complications","Established patient risk factors include female sex, non-smoking status, history of PONV or motion sickness, and younger age, often summarised in simplified risk scores","Anaesthetic and surgical factors include use of volatile agents and nitrous oxide, perioperative opioids, and longer or certain types of surgery","A multimodal approach works best: reducing baseline risk (opioid-sparing analgesia, regional techniques, total intravenous anaesthesia) and combining antiemetics from different classes","Prophylactic agents span several receptor targets, including serotonin antagonists, corticosteroids, dopamine antagonists, and antihistamines, chosen according to risk","For breakthrough symptoms, an antiemetic from a class not already used for prophylaxis is preferred rather than repeating the same drug","Adequate hydration, avoidance of unnecessary opioids, and treatment of pain and hypotension help reduce symptoms","Persistent or severe postoperative vomiting should prompt consideration of surgical, metabolic, or intracranial causes rather than assuming simple PONV"],"pathophysiology":"Nausea and vomiting are coordinated by brainstem centres receiving input from the chemoreceptor trigger zone, vestibular system, higher cortical centres, and vagal afferents from the gastrointestinal tract. Multiple neurotransmitters, including serotonin, dopamine, histamine, acetylcholine, and substance P, mediate these pathways. Anaesthetic agents, opioids, gastric distension, hypotension, and surgical stimulation activate one or more of these inputs. Because several receptor systems contribute, blocking a single pathway is often insufficient, which is why combining antiemetics acting at different receptors is more effective than escalating a single agent.","additionalDifferentials":["Bowel obstruction or ileus","Gastric distension from mask ventilation","Raised intracranial pressure or neurological cause","Metabolic disturbance such as hyponatraemia or hypoglycaemia","Medication or opioid effect","Hypotension or hypovolaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of hydration, pain, and haemodynamics","Review of anaesthetic and analgesic agents administered","Electrolytes and glucose if vomiting is persistent","Abdominal imaging if obstruction or ileus is suspected","Neurological assessment if a central cause is possible"],"redFlags":["Persistent vomiting causing dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, or aspiration risk","Vomiting with headache, focal neurology, or reduced consciousness suggesting an intracranial cause","Bilious or feculent vomiting indicating bowel obstruction","Vomiting with severe abdominal pain suggesting a surgical complication"],"prognosis":"PONV is usually self-limiting and responds to multimodal prophylaxis and treatment, but uncontrolled symptoms can delay recovery, cause dehydration, and rarely lead to aspiration or wound complications.","pitfalls":["Relying on a single antiemetic in high-risk patients instead of combining agents","Repeating the same drug class for breakthrough symptoms","Overlooking modifiable contributors such as opioids, hypotension, and dehydration","Assuming all postoperative vomiting is benign and missing a surgical or neurological cause","Failing to risk-stratify and tailor prophylaxis preoperatively"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"postdural_puncture_headache":{"name":"Postdural puncture headache","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PDPH","post-lumbar puncture headache","low-pressure headache"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A characteristically postural headache that worsens on sitting or standing and improves on lying flat, developing within a few days of dural puncture","Typically frontal or occipital and may be accompanied by neck stiffness, photophobia, nausea, tinnitus, or hearing changes","Risk is higher with larger-gauge and cutting (rather than pencil-point) needles, younger age, female sex, and after accidental dural puncture during epidural placement","Diagnosis is largely clinical, but a headache lacking postural features or with neurological signs should prompt evaluation for other causes","Initial management is conservative with rest as needed, hydration, simple analgesia, and reassurance, as many cases resolve spontaneously","An epidural blood patch is the definitive treatment for severe or persistent headache and is generally highly effective","Caffeine and other pharmacological measures have limited and temporary benefit compared with a blood patch","Rare but serious complications of intracranial hypotension include subdural haematoma and cerebral venous sinus thrombosis, so atypical or worsening features require imaging"],"pathophysiology":"A persistent hole in the dura allows cerebrospinal fluid to leak into the epidural space faster than it is produced, lowering cerebrospinal fluid volume and pressure. In the upright position, reduced buoyant support causes traction on pain-sensitive intracranial structures such as the meninges and bridging veins, and compensatory cerebral vasodilation may contribute, producing the postural headache. Larger dural defects leak more and cause more severe symptoms. An epidural blood patch works by sealing the defect and raising epidural and cerebrospinal fluid pressure.","additionalDifferentials":["Migraine or tension-type headache","Meningitis (infective) or chemical meningism","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis","Subdural haematoma","Pre-eclampsia or eclampsia in the obstetric patient","Pneumocephalus or caffeine-withdrawal headache"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment focusing on the postural nature of the headache","Neurological examination to exclude focal signs","Brain imaging (CT or MRI) when features are atypical, worsening, or accompanied by neurological signs","Investigations for pre-eclampsia in the postpartum patient when relevant","Assessment for infection if fever or meningism is present"],"redFlags":["Loss of the postural component or a headache that becomes constant, suggesting an alternative or serious cause","Focal neurological deficit, seizure, or reduced consciousness suggesting subdural haematoma or venous thrombosis","Fever, neck stiffness, or photophobia raising concern for meningitis","Sudden thunderclap onset or features suggesting subarachnoid haemorrhage"],"prognosis":"Most postdural puncture headaches resolve within days to weeks with conservative care, and a blood patch relieves the majority of severe cases; rare progression to intracranial haemorrhage or venous thrombosis can cause serious morbidity.","pitfalls":["Assuming every post-procedure headache is a postdural puncture headache and missing a dangerous cause","Delaying an effective blood patch in severe, disabling headache","Overlooking evolving neurological signs that indicate subdural haematoma or venous sinus thrombosis","Failing to consider pre-eclampsia in a postpartum woman with headache","Relying on caffeine or bed rest alone for persistent severe symptoms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"postpartum_depression":{"name":"Postpartum depression","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["postnatal depression","peripartum depression","major depressive disorder with peripartum onset","PPD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["This is a major depressive episode with onset during pregnancy or in the weeks following delivery, presenting with persistent low mood, loss of interest, and the associated features of depression","It must be distinguished from the common, transient postpartum blues, which are mild and self-limiting within about two weeks, and from the rare psychiatric emergency of postpartum psychosis","Presentations often include prominent anxiety, guilt about parenting ability, and intrusive thoughts, and mothers may be reluctant to disclose symptoms for fear of judgement","Actively assess suicidal ideation and any thoughts of harming the infant, as these carry serious risk and require urgent action","Routine screening in the perinatal period, for example with a validated postnatal depression scale, improves detection of an often hidden condition","Management combines psychotherapy and, when indicated, antidepressant pharmacotherapy, with treatment choices weighing symptom severity against breastfeeding considerations","Untreated maternal depression can adversely affect mother-infant bonding and child development, so timely treatment benefits both mother and child","Postpartum psychosis, with confusion, delusions, or thoughts of harming the infant, is an emergency requiring immediate assessment and usually admission"],"pathophysiology":"Postpartum depression is thought to result from the interaction of an abrupt drop in reproductive hormones after delivery with a predisposing vulnerability, superimposed on the neurobiology shared with major depression, including monoamine and stress-axis dysregulation. Sleep deprivation, psychosocial stressors, a history of mood disorder, and inadequate support further increase risk in susceptible women.","additionalDifferentials":["Postpartum blues (transient and self-limiting)","Postpartum psychosis","Bipolar disorder presenting or relapsing in the perinatal period","Postpartum thyroiditis and other thyroid dysfunction","Anaemia and other medical causes of fatigue","Anxiety disorders or obsessive-compulsive disorder with perinatal onset"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Validated perinatal depression screening, such as the Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale","Structured suicide risk assessment and assessment of thoughts of harm to the infant","Thyroid function testing","Full blood count to exclude anaemia","Screening for a history of bipolar disorder before starting an antidepressant"],"redFlags":["Suicidal ideation with plan or intent","Thoughts of harming the infant or command-type intrusive urges","Features of postpartum psychosis such as confusion, delusions, or hallucinations","Severe self-neglect or inability to care for the infant"],"prognosis":"Postpartum depression generally responds well to treatment, and most women recover, but there is an increased risk of recurrence in subsequent pregnancies and of future depressive episodes. Prognosis is better with early detection, treatment, and social support, and worse when the condition is unrecognised, when there is a bipolar diathesis, or when comorbidities and psychosocial adversity persist.","pitfalls":["Dismissing significant depression as normal postpartum blues","Failing to ask directly about suicidal thoughts and thoughts of harming the infant","Missing an underlying bipolar disorder before prescribing an antidepressant","Overlooking postpartum thyroiditis as a contributor","Not recognising postpartum psychosis as an emergency requiring urgent care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"postpartum_haemorrhage":{"name":"Postpartum haemorrhage","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["postpartum hemorrhage","PPH","obstetric haemorrhage after delivery"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Postpartum haemorrhage is excessive bleeding after birth; primary PPH occurs within the first 24 hours and secondary PPH from 24 hours up to about 12 weeks postpartum","The causes are usefully remembered as the four Ts: tone (uterine atony), trauma (genital tract lacerations), tissue (retained placenta or products), and thrombin (coagulopathy)","Uterine atony is by far the commonest cause, so uterine massage and uterotonic agents are first-line alongside resuscitation","Active management of the third stage of labour with a prophylactic uterotonic reduces the incidence and severity of PPH","Blood loss is frequently underestimated visually, so vital signs, quantitative assessment, and a high index of suspicion are essential","A structured stepwise escalation moves from uterotonics to mechanical measures (bimanual compression, balloon tamponade) and then to surgical options such as compression sutures, vessel ligation, or hysterectomy","Tranexamic acid given early reduces death from bleeding and should be administered promptly","Early involvement of senior obstetric, anaesthetic, and haematology teams with activation of a massive transfusion protocol improves outcomes"],"pathophysiology":"Normal postpartum haemostasis depends on vigorous myometrial contraction that mechanically compresses the spiral arteries of the placental bed, supported by the clotting cascade. Postpartum haemorrhage results when one or more of these mechanisms fail: the uterus fails to contract (atony, often from overdistension, prolonged or augmented labour, infection, or retained tissue); vessels are torn by genital tract trauma; retained placental tissue or invasive placentation prevents effective contraction; or coagulation is impaired by dilution, consumption, or a pre-existing disorder. Ongoing loss can precipitate hypovolaemic shock and a self-perpetuating coagulopathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Uterine atony","Genital tract trauma including cervical or vaginal lacerations and haematomas","Retained placenta or retained products of conception","Placenta accreta spectrum","Uterine inversion or rupture","Inherited or acquired coagulopathy including disseminated intravascular coagulation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Quantitative measurement of blood loss and continuous vital sign monitoring","Full blood count and serial haemoglobin","Coagulation screen with fibrinogen level","Group and cross-match with early activation of transfusion support","Examination under anaesthesia and ultrasound to identify retained tissue or genital tract trauma"],"redFlags":["Tachycardia, hypotension, and other signs of hypovolaemic shock","Continued brisk bleeding despite uterotonics and uterine massage","Clinical or laboratory evidence of coagulopathy","A high uterine fundus with a boggy uterus indicating atony, or a suddenly inverted uterus","Suspected placenta accreta spectrum after a difficult placental delivery"],"prognosis":"With prompt recognition, resuscitation, and stepwise treatment most women recover fully. Delay in diagnosis or treatment can lead to shock, organ failure, need for hysterectomy with loss of fertility, and death; postpartum haemorrhage remains one of the leading causes of maternal mortality worldwide, particularly in low-resource settings.","pitfalls":["Underestimating blood loss by visual assessment alone","Failing to recognise ongoing bleeding masked by the physiological cardiovascular reserve of pregnancy until late collapse","Not systematically excluding retained tissue and genital tract trauma when atony seems controlled but bleeding persists","Delaying tranexamic acid and blood product replacement","Escalating too slowly to mechanical and surgical measures when medical therapy fails"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"poststreptococcal_glomerulonephritis":{"name":"Post-streptococcal Glomerulonephritis","system":"Renal","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PSGN","acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis","APSGN","acute post-infectious glomerulonephritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A classic cause of acute nephritic syndrome in children, most often between about 5 and 12 years of age.","Follows infection with specific nephritogenic strains of group A streptococcus, typically 1-2 weeks after pharyngitis or 3-6 weeks after skin infection.","Presents with the nephritic picture of haematuria (often cola- or tea-coloured urine), oedema, hypertension, and variable reduction in urine output.","Complement C3 is characteristically low and typically returns to normal within about 6-8 weeks; persistently low C3 beyond this window suggests another diagnosis.","Evidence of preceding streptococcal infection is supported by elevated or rising antistreptococcal antibodies such as ASO or anti-DNase B.","Management is largely supportive, centred on salt and fluid restriction and control of hypertension and fluid overload.","Antibiotics treat any active streptococcal infection but do not alter the course of the established nephritis.","Renal biopsy is usually unnecessary and reserved for atypical features such as persistent low complement, progressive kidney failure, or nephrotic-range proteinuria.","The prognosis in children is excellent, with the large majority recovering complete renal function."],"pathophysiology":"Infection with nephritogenic strains of group A beta-haemolytic streptococcus triggers an immune-complex-mediated glomerular injury. Streptococcal antigens (such as nephritis-associated plasmin receptor and streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin B) deposit in the glomeruli and, together with circulating and in situ immune complexes, activate the complement system, predominantly via the alternative pathway. The resulting inflammation causes endocapillary proliferation and neutrophil infiltration, reducing glomerular filtration and producing sodium and water retention, which manifests as haematuria, oedema, and hypertension.","additionalDifferentials":["IgA nephropathy","Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","Lupus nephritis","Henoch-Schonlein purpura (IgA vasculitis) nephritis","Haemolytic uraemic syndrome","Alport syndrome or thin basement membrane disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum complement C3 (and C4) with follow-up to document normalisation","Antistreptococcal serology (ASO, anti-DNase B) or streptozyme panel","Throat or skin swab and culture if active infection present","Urinalysis with microscopy for red cell casts and dysmorphic red cells","Serum urea, creatinine, and electrolytes to assess renal function","Renal biopsy only in atypical or progressive cases"],"redFlags":["Severe or refractory hypertension, including hypertensive encephalopathy","Rapidly rising creatinine suggesting rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis","Pulmonary oedema or heart failure from fluid overload","Marked oliguria or anuria","Persistently low complement or nephrotic-range proteinuria indicating an alternative diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Most children recover fully; acute complications resolve over days to weeks, complement normalises within about two months, and microscopic haematuria may persist for up to a year with an excellent long-term outlook.","pitfalls":["Attributing every child with haematuria and low C3 to PSGN without follow-up complement testing","Overlooking hypertensive complications by not monitoring blood pressure closely","Failing to consider alternative glomerulonephritides when complement stays low beyond eight weeks","Aggressive fluid administration worsening volume overload in an oliguric child"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"precocious_puberty":{"name":"Precocious Puberty","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sexual precocity","central precocious puberty","peripheral precocious puberty","early puberty"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Precocious puberty is conventionally defined as the onset of secondary sexual characteristics before about 8 years in girls and 9 years in boys.","It is broadly classified as central (gonadotropin-dependent, from early activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis) or peripheral (gonadotropin-independent, from sex steroids produced outside the normal axis).","Central precocious puberty is far more common in girls and is usually idiopathic, whereas in boys an underlying CNS lesion is more likely and must be excluded.","Accelerated linear growth and advanced bone age are typical; paradoxically, premature epiphyseal fusion can compromise final adult height.","A GnRH (or GnRH-agonist) stimulation test helps distinguish central from peripheral causes by assessing the gonadotropin response.","Isolated premature thelarche or premature adrenarche are usually benign normal variants but should be monitored to ensure they are not the start of true precocity.","Peripheral causes include congenital adrenal hyperplasia, gonadal or adrenal tumours, McCune-Albright syndrome, and exogenous sex steroid exposure.","Beyond physical changes, early puberty can carry psychosocial consequences that warrant support for the child and family."],"pathophysiology":"In central precocious puberty the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis reactivates prematurely, with pulsatile GnRH secretion stimulating pituitary gonadotropins and subsequent gonadal sex steroid production, reproducing normal but early puberty. In peripheral precocious puberty sex steroids arise independently of GnRH from the gonads, adrenals, tumours, or exogenous sources, producing secondary sexual characteristics without central axis activation. Sex steroids accelerate growth and skeletal maturation, so advanced bone age can ultimately limit final height.","additionalDifferentials":["Premature thelarche (benign isolated breast development)","Premature adrenarche (early pubic/axillary hair without other puberty)","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Gonadal or adrenal tumour","McCune-Albright syndrome","CNS lesion (hamartoma, tumour, prior irradiation, or injury)","Exogenous sex steroid exposure"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bone age radiograph","Basal and GnRH-stimulated LH and FSH","Sex steroid levels (oestradiol or testosterone)","Adrenal androgens and 17-hydroxyprogesterone when a peripheral cause is suspected","Pelvic ultrasound in girls to assess ovarian and uterine development","MRI of the brain, particularly in boys or where a central lesion is suspected"],"redFlags":["Neurological signs, headaches, or visual disturbance suggesting an intracranial lesion","Rapidly progressive virilisation or a palpable abdominal/gonadal mass suggesting a hormone-secreting tumour","Precocious puberty in a boy, which more often has a serious underlying cause","Very early or rapidly advancing puberty threatening severe height compromise","Cafe-au-lait macules with bony abnormalities suggesting McCune-Albright syndrome"],"prognosis":"Idiopathic central precocious puberty generally has a good outcome, and appropriate treatment can preserve growth potential and address psychosocial concerns; prognosis in secondary forms depends on the underlying cause, such as a CNS lesion or hormone-secreting tumour.","pitfalls":["Assuming precocious puberty is idiopathic in a boy without imaging to exclude a CNS lesion","Confusing benign premature thelarche or adrenarche with true central precocity","Overlooking the impact of advanced bone age on final adult height","Neglecting the psychosocial needs of a child developing ahead of peers"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"preeclampsia":{"name":"Pre-eclampsia","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["preeclampsia","pre-eclampsia","PET","pregnancy-induced hypertension with proteinuria","gestational proteinuric hypertension"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by new-onset hypertension after 20 weeks' gestation combined with proteinuria or evidence of maternal organ dysfunction (renal, hepatic, neurological, haematological) or uteroplacental compromise","Proteinuria is no longer mandatory for diagnosis when hypertension is accompanied by features such as thrombocytopenia, raised transaminases, renal impairment, pulmonary oedema, or cerebral/visual symptoms","Warning symptoms include persistent severe headache, visual disturbance (flashing lights, blurring), right upper quadrant or epigastric pain, and rapid oedema of the face and hands","HELLP syndrome (haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets) is a severe variant that can occur with only modest blood pressure elevation","Definitive treatment is delivery of the placenta; timing balances gestational age against maternal and fetal risk","Magnesium sulfate is used for seizure prophylaxis in severe disease and to treat eclamptic seizures, not as an antihypertensive","Antihypertensive therapy aims to lower dangerously high blood pressure to reduce maternal stroke risk while preserving placental perfusion","Low-dose aspirin from early pregnancy reduces incidence in women at high risk (prior pre-eclampsia, chronic hypertension, renal disease, diabetes, multiple pregnancy, antiphospholipid syndrome)"],"pathophysiology":"Pre-eclampsia originates in abnormal placentation: inadequate remodelling of the maternal spiral arteries by trophoblast leads to a poorly perfused, ischaemic placenta. The ischaemic placenta releases anti-angiogenic factors (such as soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase-1 and soluble endoglin) that antagonise vascular endothelial and placental growth factors, producing widespread maternal endothelial dysfunction. This causes vasospasm, increased vascular permeability, activation of coagulation, and multi-organ hypoperfusion, manifesting as hypertension, proteinuria, hepatic and cerebral involvement, and fetal growth restriction.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic (pre-existing) hypertension","Gestational hypertension without organ involvement","Chronic kidney disease with proteinuria","Acute fatty liver of pregnancy","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura or haemolytic uraemic syndrome","Systemic lupus erythematosus flare"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial blood pressure measurement and urine protein quantification (protein:creatinine ratio or 24-hour collection)","Full blood count for thrombocytopenia and evidence of haemolysis","Liver transaminases and lactate dehydrogenase","Renal function including serum creatinine and urate","Angiogenic markers (sFlt-1/PlGF ratio) where available to aid risk stratification","Fetal assessment with ultrasound growth, amniotic fluid volume, and umbilical artery Doppler"],"redFlags":["Severe hypertension unresponsive to treatment threatening maternal stroke","Neurological signs (severe headache, visual loss, hyperreflexia with clonus) heralding impending eclampsia","Right upper quadrant pain with abnormal liver enzymes suggesting HELLP or hepatic haematoma/rupture","Pulmonary oedema or oliguria indicating organ failure","Fetal growth restriction with abnormal Dopplers or non-reassuring fetal status"],"prognosis":"With timely surveillance, blood pressure control, seizure prophylaxis, and appropriately timed delivery most women recover fully postpartum, though there is an increased lifetime risk of cardiovascular and renal disease. Severe or early-onset disease carries higher maternal and perinatal morbidity, and recurrence in future pregnancies is common.","pitfalls":["Excluding the diagnosis because proteinuria is absent despite organ dysfunction","Underestimating severity in HELLP syndrome where blood pressure may be only mildly raised","Over-aggressive blood pressure lowering compromising placental perfusion","Failing to continue monitoring postpartum, when eclampsia and severe hypertension can still occur","Treating magnesium sulfate as an antihypertensive rather than as seizure prophylaxis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"premature_ventricular_contractions_nonsust":{"name":"Premature Ventricular Contractions, Nonsustained Idioventricular Rhythm","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PVCs","Ventricular premature complexes","Ventricular ectopy","Nonsustained ventricular tachycardia","NSVT","Accelerated idioventricular rhythm","AIVR"],"chapter":260,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1960","clinicalPearls":["Ventricular ectopy spanning PVCs, nonsustained VT (NSVT), and accelerated idioventricular rhythm (AIVR) is very common and is most often asymptomatic, detected incidentally on telemetry or prompted by palpitations.","A PVC is a single ventricular beat occurring earlier than the next expected supraventricular beat; PVCs from one focus share an identical QRS morphology (unifocal), while differing morphologies indicate multifocal origin and raise suspicion for structural or myopathic disease.","Two consecutive ventricular beats form a couplet; three or more consecutive beats above 100/min define VT, and VT terminating spontaneously within 30 seconds is termed nonsustained.","QRS morphology localizes the focus: a dominant S wave in V1 (left bundle branch block-like) points to the right ventricle or septum, whereas a dominant R wave in V1 (right bundle branch block-like) points to the left ventricle; the frontal-plane axis distinguishes superior (inferior-wall) versus cranial origin.","Most ventricular arrhythmias without structural disease have a left bundle branch block-like pattern, while a right bundle branch block configuration or multiple PVC morphologies more often signal structural heart disease.","Idiopathic ectopy commonly arises from the right or left ventricular outflow tracts near the valve annuli, and also from papillary muscles and fascicular tissue; benign idiopathic NSVT is usually monomorphic with rates under 200/min.","Bigeminy (every other beat a PVC) can cause pseudobradycardia because the premature beats may not generate a palpable pulse, so counted rate can read falsely low.","In the peri-infarct period, PVCs and NSVT are common early manifestations of ischemia and can herald ventricular fibrillation.","AIVR is defined as three or more ventricular beats at a rate slower than 100/min, usually due to enhanced automaticity; it is common during acute MI and may emerge during sinus bradycardia or sleep.","Frequent ectopy can itself depress ventricular function (PVC-induced cardiomyopathy), but this rarely occurs unless PVCs account for at least 10-20% of total beats over 24 hours, and only a minority of such patients develop reversible cardiomyopathy.","Smooth, sharp QRS deflections of a PVC suggest a focus in relatively normal myocardium, whereas broad notching and slurring suggest a diseased substrate.","Recently inflammation has been recognized as contributing to PVC genesis in inflammatory and inherited cardiomyopathies."],"pathophysiology":"PVCs arise via enhanced (abnormal) automaticity, triggered activity, or localized reentry. They are frequently sympathetically sensitive and can reflect heightened sympathetic tone, myocardial ischemia, hypoxia, electrolyte disturbances (notably hypokalemia), or underlying structural heart disease. The site of earliest ventricular activation dictates the activation sequence and therefore the QRS morphology, allowing the surface ECG to suggest the anatomic origin and the likelihood of structural disease. In the setting of ischemia or structural disease, PVCs and NSVT can be harbingers of sustained VT or VF. AIVR is typically driven by automaticity, though rare reentrant circuits in diseased myocardium can produce it. PVC-induced ventricular dysfunction is thought to act through a mechanism akin to chronic tachycardia or through ventricular dyssynchrony.","additionalDifferentials":["Idiopathic outflow-tract or fascicular ventricular arrhythmia (benign)","Papillary muscle ectopy associated with mitral valve prolapse and mitral annular disjunction","Sustained monomorphic VT (must be distinguished from NSVT by duration and hemodynamic impact)","Supraventricular tachycardia with aberrant conduction (wide-complex differential)","Genetic arrhythmia syndromes: long QT, Brugada, short QT, catecholaminergic polymorphic VT","Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Electrolyte abnormality (especially hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia) provoking ectopy","Stimulant or adrenergic effect (caffeine, alcohol, sympathomimetics)","Myocardial ischemia presenting as new ventricular ectopy","Drug or toxic effect on conduction (e.g., sodium-channel-blocking agents producing slowed, wide QRS rhythms)"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG in sinus rhythm (benign idiopathic arrhythmia usually shows a completely normal ECG; any abnormality warrants further workup)","Ambulatory/telemetry monitoring to quantify PVC burden and capture NSVT","Transthoracic echocardiography to assess ventricular function, wall-motion abnormalities, and valvular disease","Contrast-enhanced cardiac MRI to detect scar and late gadolinium enhancement suggesting underlying cardiomyopathy","Exercise stress testing for effort-related symptoms or coronary risk","Assessment of electrolytes (potassium, magnesium) during acute illness","Family history evaluation and consideration of genetic syndromes when a family history of sudden death exists"],"redFlags":["Syncope or near-syncope, especially without prodrome, during exertion, or with an abnormal ECG or structural heart disease","Very rapid or polymorphic NSVT, or a 'short-coupled' first beat occurring before the peak of the T wave","Family history of sudden death prompting evaluation for inherited cardiomyopathy or channelopathy","Any abnormality on the 12-lead ECG (Q waves of prior infarction, ventricular hypertrophy, repolarization abnormalities of long QT/Brugada/ARVC)","Right bundle branch block-like or multifocal PVC morphology suggesting structural disease","Depressed ventricular function with frequent/repetitive ectopy"],"prognosis":"PVCs, NSVT, and AIVR are most often benign when they occur in structurally normal hearts with normal ECGs, and idiopathic ectopy generally has excellent long-term arrhythmia-free outcomes (catheter ablation suppresses idiopathic PVCs in roughly 90% of patients). In contrast, the same arrhythmias carry prognostic weight in the presence of structural disease: after MI and in heart failure, frequent (typically >10 PVCs/h), repetitive PVCs, couplets, and NSVT are markers of depressed ventricular function and increased mortality, and they are likewise associated with worse outcomes in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and congenital heart disease with ventricular dysfunction. AIVR is usually well tolerated, though loss of AV synchrony may cause hemodynamic compromise in susceptible patients.","pitfalls":["Bigeminal PVCs can produce pseudobradycardia, leading to an erroneously low measured heart rate.","Asymptomatic PVCs and NSVT discovered in a hospitalized or acutely ill patient generally do not require specific antiarrhythmic treatment beyond addressing the underlying illness and correcting aggravating factors.","Assuming all ventricular ectopy is benign; very rapid, polymorphic, short-coupled, or multifocal patterns and any ECG/structural abnormality demand evaluation for sudden-death risk.","Overlooking reversible PVC-induced cardiomyopathy in patients with very frequent ectopy and otherwise unexplained left ventricular dysfunction.","Attributing a wide-complex rhythm to VT without distinguishing it from supraventricular tachycardia with aberrancy."],"severityClassification":"Risk is stratified less by ectopy count alone than by context: (1) structurally normal heart with normal ECG and monomorphic NSVT under 200/min - benign/low risk; (2) structural heart disease (post-MI, heart failure, HCM, congenital disease) where greater frequency and complexity (couplets, NSVT) mark more severe disease and higher mortality; (3) high-risk features warranting concern - syncope, very rapid/polymorphic/short-coupled NSVT, multifocal or RBBB-like morphology, abnormal ECG, or family history of sudden death. PVC burden of at least 10-20% of total beats over 24 hours is the threshold associated with potential PVC-induced ventricular dysfunction.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1960","Harrison 22e p.1961","Harrison 22e p.1962"],"crossLinks":[]},"presbycusis":{"name":"Presbycusis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["age-related hearing loss","presbyacusis","senile deafness"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presbycusis is the progressive, symmetrical sensorineural hearing loss that accompanies ageing and is the commonest cause of hearing loss in older adults","It characteristically begins with the high frequencies, so patients first struggle with consonant sounds and with speech in noisy or crowded settings","A common complaint is that people can hear that someone is speaking but cannot make out the words, reflecting impaired speech discrimination","Tinnitus and difficulty localising sound frequently accompany the hearing loss","Audiometry typically shows a bilateral, symmetrical, high-frequency down-sloping sensorineural pattern","Recruitment (abnormal loudness growth) can make amplified sound uncomfortable and complicate hearing aid fitting","Contributing and aggravating factors include noise exposure, ototoxic drugs, vascular disease and diabetes, which should be addressed","Management is largely rehabilitative with hearing aids, assistive listening devices and auditory training, and cochlear implantation is considered for severe cases with poor aided benefit"],"pathophysiology":"Presbycusis results from cumulative degenerative changes in the auditory system with age. Several subtypes are described: sensory presbycusis from loss of hair cells in the basal (high-frequency) cochlea; neural presbycusis from loss of spiral ganglion neurons, which particularly impairs speech discrimination; strial or metabolic presbycusis from atrophy of the stria vascularis affecting the endocochlear potential across frequencies; and cochlear conductive (mechanical) presbycusis from stiffening of the basilar membrane. Central auditory pathway changes further degrade processing of speech, especially in noise. Genetic predisposition, lifelong noise exposure, vascular and metabolic disease and ototoxins accelerate these changes.","additionalDifferentials":["Noise-induced hearing loss","Ototoxic drug-induced hearing loss","Cerumen impaction or middle ear disease causing conductive loss","Acoustic neuroma or other retrocochlear lesion (especially if asymmetrical)","Meniere disease","Metabolic or autoimmune inner ear disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pure tone audiometry to confirm the symmetrical high-frequency sensorineural pattern","Speech audiometry to assess discrimination and predict hearing aid benefit","Tympanometry to exclude a middle ear conductive component","Otoscopy to exclude wax impaction and other external or middle ear disease","MRI of the internal auditory meatus if the loss is markedly asymmetrical or there is unilateral tinnitus"],"redFlags":["Marked asymmetry between ears suggesting a retrocochlear or other unilateral lesion","Sudden or rapidly progressive hearing loss requiring urgent evaluation","Unilateral tinnitus, vertigo or facial numbness raising concern for acoustic neuroma","Associated neurological signs that point away from simple age-related loss"],"prognosis":"Presbycusis is irreversible and slowly progressive, but most patients maintain good communication and quality of life with hearing aids and rehabilitation, and cochlear implantation can help those with severe loss and poor aided speech understanding.","pitfalls":["Assuming all hearing loss in the elderly is presbycusis and missing wax, effusion or a tumour","Overlooking asymmetry that warrants imaging","Neglecting the impact of poor speech discrimination and recruitment on hearing aid success","Failing to address modifiable contributors such as ongoing noise exposure and ototoxic medication"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"pressure_ulcer":{"name":"Pressure ulcer","system":"Plastic / Reconstructive","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pressure sore","decubitus ulcer","bedsore","pressure injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery / standard references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pressure ulcers arise over bony prominences (sacrum, ischium, greater trochanter, heel, occiput) where sustained pressure exceeds capillary perfusion pressure","Staging ranges from non-blanchable erythema of intact skin, through partial and full thickness skin loss, to exposure of muscle, tendon, or bone, with unstageable ulcers obscured by slough or eschar","The visible skin defect often underestimates the true extent, as necrosis is frequently more advanced in the deeper tissues nearest the bone","Prevention through repositioning, pressure-redistributing surfaces, skin care, moisture control, and nutrition is more effective than treatment of established ulcers","Management principles include pressure offloading, debridement of nonviable tissue, moist wound care, optimisation of nutrition, and control of spasticity, incontinence, and infection","Deep ulcers with dead space or exposed bone may require surgical debridement and reconstruction with local flaps, and osteomyelitis should be considered when bone is exposed","Flap reconstruction is offered to selected, optimised patients, and recurrence is common if the underlying causes of pressure and shear are not corrected","A multidisciplinary approach addressing mobility, sensation, continence, and social circumstances is essential to prevent recurrence"],"pathophysiology":"Pressure ulcers result from prolonged unrelieved pressure, often combined with shear and friction, that compresses soft tissue between bone and an external surface. When interface pressure exceeds capillary closing pressure, local ischaemia and impaired lymphatic drainage lead to tissue hypoxia, accumulation of metabolic waste, and cell death. Muscle is more susceptible to ischaemia than skin, so damage typically begins in deep tissue near the bony prominence and progresses outward, producing a cone-shaped area of necrosis. Contributing factors include immobility, impaired sensation, malnutrition, moisture, incontinence, vascular disease, and advanced age.","additionalDifferentials":["Moisture-associated skin damage or incontinence dermatitis","Diabetic or neuropathic ulcer","Arterial or venous leg ulcer","Skin malignancy arising in a chronic wound (Marjolin ulcer)","Pyoderma gangrenosum","Deep tissue infection or necrotising fasciitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Wound assessment with staging, measurement, and photographic documentation","Nutritional assessment including albumin and prealbumin","Wound or bone cultures and inflammatory markers when infection is suspected","Imaging (MRI or bone biopsy) to evaluate for underlying osteomyelitis","Assessment of vascular supply and tissue perfusion","Screening for and management of contributing factors such as spasticity and incontinence"],"redFlags":["Spreading cellulitis, systemic sepsis, or signs of necrotising infection arising from the ulcer","Exposed bone with suspected osteomyelitis","Rapid deterioration or deep tissue injury under intact skin","Chronic non-healing ulcer with atypical features suggesting malignant transformation"],"prognosis":"Superficial pressure injuries can heal with conservative care once pressure is relieved, but deep ulcers heal slowly and often require surgery. Even after successful flap reconstruction, recurrence rates are high in patients who remain immobile or insensate, so long-term outcome depends heavily on ongoing prevention and management of underlying risk factors.","pitfalls":["Underestimating the depth of tissue damage from the surface appearance alone","Attempting surgical closure without first correcting pressure, nutrition, spasticity, and incontinence","Failing to debride nonviable tissue and eschar before assessing wound bed","Overlooking underlying osteomyelitis in deep ulcers with exposed bone","Neglecting prevention and repositioning, leading to new or recurrent ulcers","Missing malignant change in a long-standing chronic ulcer"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Grabb & Smith's Plastic Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"preterm_labour":{"name":"Preterm labour","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["preterm labor","premature labour","PTL","spontaneous preterm birth"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Preterm labour is regular uterine contractions with cervical change occurring before 37 completed weeks of gestation","It is the leading cause of neonatal morbidity and mortality, largely through complications of prematurity such as respiratory distress, intraventricular haemorrhage, and necrotising enterocolitis","The strongest predictors are a previous preterm birth and a short cervix on transvaginal ultrasound","Antenatal corticosteroids given to accelerate fetal lung maturation are among the most effective interventions to improve neonatal outcomes","Magnesium sulfate given before anticipated early preterm birth provides fetal neuroprotection, reducing the risk of cerebral palsy","Tocolytic drugs are used mainly to delay delivery briefly to allow steroids to act and to enable transfer to a unit with neonatal intensive care","Fetal fibronectin testing and cervical length measurement help identify women at genuinely high or low risk of imminent delivery","Group B streptococcus status guides intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis, and progesterone supplementation can reduce risk in selected high-risk women"],"pathophysiology":"Preterm labour is a syndrome with multiple pathways converging on premature activation of the mechanisms of labour: uterine contractility, cervical ripening, and decidual-membrane activation. Recognised triggers include intrauterine infection and inflammation, uteroplacental ischaemia or haemorrhage (decidual bleeding), excessive uterine distension (as in multiple pregnancy or polyhydramnios), cervical insufficiency, and maternal or fetal stress activating the corticotrophin-releasing hormone axis. These pathways generate prostaglandins and inflammatory mediators that stimulate contractions and cervical change ahead of term.","additionalDifferentials":["Braxton Hicks (physiological) contractions","Urinary tract infection or pyelonephritis provoking uterine irritability","Placental abruption","Round ligament or musculoskeletal pain","Preterm prelabour rupture of membranes","Other causes of abdominal pain such as appendicitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal ultrasound cervical length measurement","Fetal fibronectin testing to help predict imminent delivery","Cardiotocography and tocography to assess contractions and fetal wellbeing","Speculum examination to assess for ruptured membranes and take genital tract swabs","Screening for infection including urinalysis and culture and group B streptococcus status","Ultrasound for fetal presentation, growth, and amniotic fluid volume"],"redFlags":["Signs of chorioamnionitis such as maternal fever, uterine tenderness, and fetal tachycardia","Preterm prelabour rupture of membranes with cord prolapse risk","Vaginal bleeding suggesting abruption or praevia","Non-reassuring fetal status","Advanced cervical dilatation with imminent delivery at a very early gestation"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on gestational age at delivery; each additional week in utero at early gestations substantially improves survival and reduces disability. Antenatal steroids, magnesium neuroprotection, and delivery in a centre with neonatal intensive care improve results. Women with a prior spontaneous preterm birth remain at increased risk in subsequent pregnancies.","pitfalls":["Mistaking true preterm labour for Braxton Hicks contractions and delaying steroids","Overtreating with tocolytics when there is a contraindication such as chorioamnionitis or significant abruption","Failing to give antenatal corticosteroids and magnesium neuroprotection within the appropriate window","Not arranging timely transfer to a unit with adequate neonatal facilities","Overlooking an underlying infection driving the labour"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"priapism":{"name":"Priapism","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["persistent erection","ischaemic priapism","low-flow priapism","non-ischaemic priapism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Priapism is a prolonged penile erection (typically beyond four hours) unrelated to sexual stimulation, and the critical first step is to distinguish ischaemic (low-flow) from non-ischaemic (high-flow) types","Ischaemic priapism is a painful, rigid erection representing a compartment syndrome of the corpora cavernosa and is a urological emergency because prolonged ischaemia causes fibrosis and permanent erectile dysfunction","Non-ischaemic (high-flow) priapism is usually painless, semi-rigid, and follows perineal or penile trauma with an arteriolar-sinusoidal fistula; it is not an emergency and often resolves or is managed by embolisation","Cavernosal blood gas analysis distinguishes the two: ischaemic blood is dark and hypoxic/acidotic, whereas high-flow blood is bright red with near-arterial values","Common causes of ischaemic priapism include sickle cell disease, haematological malignancy, and drugs, notably intracavernosal vasoactive agents and certain psychotropic and recreational drugs","Emergency management of ischaemic priapism proceeds stepwise from corporal aspiration and irrigation to intracavernosal sympathomimetic injection, and to surgical shunting if these fail","Underlying conditions such as sickle cell disease require concurrent systemic treatment alongside local measures","Stuttering (recurrent) priapism, common in sickle cell disease, warrants preventive strategies to reduce recurrent ischaemic episodes"],"pathophysiology":"Priapism reflects a disturbance of the normal mechanisms that terminate erection. In ischaemic (low-flow) priapism, veno-occlusion persists so blood becomes trapped in the corpora cavernosa, producing stagnant, hypoxic, acidotic blood and a compartment-syndrome-like state; prolonged ischaemia leads to smooth muscle necrosis and corporal fibrosis. Causes include sickle cell disease and other haemoglobinopathies, leukaemic infiltration, and pharmacological agents. Non-ischaemic (high-flow) priapism results from unregulated arterial inflow, typically through a cavernosal artery fistula created by perineal or penile trauma, so oxygenation is maintained and tissue ischaemia does not occur.","additionalDifferentials":["Ischaemic (low-flow) versus non-ischaemic (high-flow) priapism distinction","Stuttering (recurrent) priapism","Penile trauma with fistula","Drug-induced erection (intracavernosal agents, psychotropics, recreational drugs)","Sickle cell disease or other haemoglobinopathy","Pelvic or penile malignant infiltration"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cavernosal blood gas analysis to differentiate ischaemic from non-ischaemic priapism","Penile colour Doppler ultrasound to assess cavernosal blood flow and identify a fistula","Full blood count and film with haemoglobinopathy screen (sickle cell testing)","Toxicology and medication review for causative agents","Selective pudendal arteriography if embolisation of a high-flow fistula is planned"],"redFlags":["Ischaemic priapism is a urological emergency; delay leads to corporal fibrosis and permanent erectile dysfunction","A rigid, painful erection persisting beyond four hours","Underlying sickle cell crisis or haematological malignancy requiring urgent systemic treatment","Failure of aspiration and intracavernosal therapy, mandating urgent surgical shunting"],"prognosis":"Prompt relief of ischaemic priapism improves the chance of preserved erectile function, but episodes lasting many hours frequently result in fibrosis and long-term erectile dysfunction; non-ischaemic priapism has a benign course and often resolves spontaneously or after embolisation.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish ischaemic from non-ischaemic priapism before treatment, leading to inappropriate management","Delaying emergency intervention in ischaemic priapism and risking irreversible fibrosis","Overlooking an underlying cause such as sickle cell disease or a causative medication","Treating high-flow priapism as an emergency with aggressive corporal procedures","Neglecting preventive management of recurrent stuttering priapism"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_and_metastatic_tumors_of":{"name":"Primary and Metastatic Tumors of the Nervous System","system":"Nervous system (neuro-oncology)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Brain tumors","CNS tumors","Intracranial neoplasms","Glioma","Glioblastoma","Meningioma","Brain metastases","Leptomeningeal metastases","Epidural spinal cord compression"],"chapter":95,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.716-726","clinicalPearls":["Brain tumors of any histology tend to present with a mixture of generalized features (headache, nausea/vomiting, cognitive decline, personality change, gait disturbance from raised intracranial pressure or hydrocephalus) and focal features (hemiparesis, aphasia, visual-field defect) (Harrison 22e p.716).","The classic morning-predominant brain tumor headache that eases through the day is actually seen in only a minority of patients; tumor headaches may be holocephalic, ipsilateral to the lesion, or even migraine-like with throbbing pain and scotoma (Harrison 22e p.716).","Seizures occur in roughly 25% of patients with brain metastases or malignant gliomas but are the presenting symptom in up to 90% of low-grade gliomas; all tumor-related seizures have a focal onset whether or not that is clinically apparent (Harrison 22e p.716).","Contrast (gadolinium) MRI is the preferred diagnostic study; CT is reserved for patients who cannot undergo MRI. Neuroimaging is generally the only test needed to diagnose a brain tumor, and laboratory tests are rarely useful (Harrison 22e p.716).","Malignant tumors (primary or metastatic) typically enhance with gadolinium, show central necrosis, and are ringed by white-matter edema, whereas low-grade gliomas usually do NOT enhance and are best seen on FLAIR sequences (Harrison 22e p.716).","Meningiomas are dural-based enhancing extra-axial masses with a characteristic dural tail that compress but do not invade brain; their principal differential is a dural metastasis or dural lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.716, p.722).","Adult diffuse gliomas are now classified molecularly: IDH-mutant astrocytoma (1p/19q intact), IDH-mutant oligodendroglioma (1p/19q co-deleted), and IDH wild-type glioblastoma; these molecular markers carry major prognostic weight (Harrison 22e p.718-719).","An unmethylated MGMT promoter predicts relative temozolomide resistance and a worse glioblastoma prognosis; MGMT promoter hypermethylation (gene silencing) predicts better response (Harrison 22e p.720).","Meningiomas are the most common primary brain tumor (~35%), are more common in women and with prior cranial irradiation and NF2, and many are found incidentally; small asymptomatic ones can simply be monitored with serial MRI (Harrison 22e p.721-722).","Most brain metastases lodge at the gray-white junction in watershed territories; ~85% are supratentorial and ~15% in the posterior fossa, and distribution roughly mirrors regional blood flow (Harrison 22e p.723).","Melanoma has the greatest propensity to seed the brain (found in ~80% at autopsy), but lung and breast carcinomas are the most common sources overall; prostate and breast cancers also favor dural metastases that can mimic meningioma (Harrison 22e p.723).","In epidural spinal metastasis, back pain is the presenting symptom in virtually all patients and may precede neurologic signs by weeks to months; the pain characteristically worsens on lying down, in contrast to arthritic pain which eases with recumbency (Harrison 22e p.725).","Any cancer patient with severe back pain should undergo whole-spine MRI; plain films, bone scan, or CT may show bony disease but only MRI reliably delineates epidural tumor (Harrison 22e p.725)."],"pathophysiology":"Most primary brain tumors have no identified cause; established risk factors are limited to ionizing radiation exposure (meningiomas, gliomas, schwannomas) and immunosuppression (primary CNS lymphoma), while electromagnetic field/cell-phone exposure, head injury, dietary N-nitroso compounds, and occupational factors are not proven. Tumors arise through a multistep accumulation of genetic alterations: loss of tumor-suppressor genes (p53, CDKN2A/B, PTEN) and amplification/overexpression of proto-oncogenes (EGFR, PDGFR), producing uncontrolled growth; many gliomas show marked molecular heterogeneity. The 2016 and 2021 WHO classifications integrate molecular parameters (e.g., IDH mutation, 1p/19q co-deletion, H3 K27/G34 alterations) with histology. Brain metastases spread hematogenously, lodging in terminal arterioles at the gray-white junction; leptomeningeal disease reaches the subarachnoid space via arterial circulation or retrograde venous flow and may also follow resection of a cerebellar metastasis. Epidural cord compression generally results from metastases to the axial skeleton (typically breast and prostate), though lymphoma can invade through an intervertebral foramen without bone destruction (Harrison 22e p.717, p.719, p.723, p.724-725).","additionalDifferentials":["High-grade glioma (glioblastoma, IDH wild-type)","Low-grade IDH-mutant astrocytoma or oligodendroglioma","Meningioma vs dural metastasis vs dural lymphoma","Primary CNS lymphoma vs metastasis vs infection","Vestibular schwannoma vs cerebellopontine-angle meningioma","Brain metastasis vs primary brain tumor vs second malignancy in a cancer patient"],"infectionMimics":["Brain abscess","Toxoplasmosis (ring-enhancing lesions, especially in immunocompromised, mimicking PCNSL or metastases)","Epidural abscess (mimicking epidural tumor)","Subacute/chronic meningitis (leptomeningeal lymphoma can present this way)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Demyelinating lesions","Sarcoidosis","Radiation necrosis in a previously treated patient","Cerebral infarction or hemorrhage","Migraine (when tumor headache has migrainous features)","Depression (apathy/social withdrawal from a frontal tumor)","Epidural hematoma, epidural lipomatosis, or extramedullary hematopoiesis (mimicking epidural tumor)","Arthritic/degenerative back pain (vs epidural metastasis pain)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Gadolinium-enhanced cranial MRI (preferred for all suspected brain tumors); CT only if MRI not possible","FLAIR sequences to detect non-enhancing low-grade glioma and infiltrative disease","Functional MRI for presurgical mapping of eloquent cortex","PET, MR perfusion, and MR spectroscopy to assess metabolic activity and help distinguish tumor progression from treatment-related necrosis","Molecular/integrated histopathologic testing (IDH, 1p/19q, MGMT promoter methylation, H3 status, etc.) for classification and prognosis","Stereotactic biopsy for histologic diagnosis of PCNSL (withhold glucocorticoids until biopsy where possible, as they are cytolytic to lymphoma)","CSF cytology and flow cytometry for leptomeningeal and primary CNS lymphoma; HIV testing and EBV DNA in immunocompromised PCNSL","Whole-spine MRI (or CT myelography if MRI impossible) for suspected epidural cord compression"],"redFlags":["New focal neurologic deficit (hemiparesis, aphasia, visual-field loss) that is subacute and progressive","New-onset focal seizures, especially in an adult without prior epilepsy","Severe back pain in a cancer patient (suggests epidural metastasis) — warrants urgent whole-spine MRI","Leg weakness, sensory level, or sphincter dysfunction suggesting evolving spinal cord compression (a neuro-oncologic emergency)","New limb pain in a patient with breast, lung cancer, or melanoma (consider leptomeningeal spread)","Signs of raised intracranial pressure / hydrocephalus (worsening headache, vomiting, declining consciousness)","Multilevel neuraxis signs (combined cranial neuropathies plus radiculopathies) suggesting leptomeningeal carcinomatosis"],"prognosis":"Outcome is highly histology- and molecular-subtype-dependent. Low-grade IDH-mutant astrocytomas have median survival around 10 years (most eventually transform to higher grade); oligodendrogliomas (1p/19q co-deleted) generally exceed 10 years. Glioblastoma carries a poor prognosis with median survival in the 15-18 month range with combined therapy and ~10% 5-year survival; adverse factors are older age, unmethylated MGMT promoter, poor Karnofsky status, and unresectable tumor. H3 K27-altered diffuse midline glioma has a median survival of roughly 1 year regardless of grade; H3 G34-mutant hemispheric glioma about 18-22 months. Medulloblastoma achieves long-term survival in ~70% but often with neurocognitive cost, varying by molecular subgroup (WNT best; group 3 worst). Completely resected meningiomas and schwannomas can be cured. Brain metastases treated with whole-brain radiotherapy alone yield median survival of only ~4-6 months, though stereotactic radiosurgery achieves local control in 80-90% and occasional cure. Leptomeningeal metastasis is treated palliatively with no curative therapy. In epidural metastasis, patients fare well if treated before a severe deficit develops, but overall survival is often short owing to widespread systemic disease (Harrison 22e p.718-725).","pitfalls":["Assuming a tumor headache must be morning-predominant — most are not, and tumor headaches can mimic migraine.","Misattributing frontal-lobe apathy and withdrawal to primary depression rather than tumor.","Giving glucocorticoids before biopsy in suspected PCNSL — their cytolytic effect on lymphoma cells can yield non-diagnostic tissue.","Relying on a single CSF cytology in suspected leptomeningeal disease — it is positive in only ~50% on first lumbar puncture and still misses ~10% after three samples; imaging is diagnostic in only ~75%.","Forgetting that the smallest brain metastases can produce extensive edema while large ones cause little, and that imaging appearance is nonspecific.","Overlooking that ~10% of systemic cancers can first present with a brain metastasis, sometimes requiring biopsy of the brain lesion when no accessible systemic site exists.","Treating low-grade glioma as benign — most ultimately transform to higher grade.","Delaying spine imaging in a cancer patient with back pain until neurologic deficits appear."],"severityClassification":"Gliomas are graded by the WHO into grades 2-4 (low- vs high-grade) integrating histology with molecular markers (IDH mutation, 1p/19q co-deletion; CDKN2A/B homozygous deletion upgrades an IDH-mutant astrocytoma to grade 4). Meningiomas are WHO grade I (benign), II (atypical), or III (malignant). Brain tumor symptoms are categorized as generalized (raised-ICP related) versus focal/lateralizing. Nervous-system metastatic disease is grouped clinically into intraparenchymal brain metastases, leptomeningeal (carcinomatous meningitis) metastases, and epidural spinal cord compression (Harrison 22e p.716, p.719, p.722).","references":["Harrison 22e p.716","Harrison 22e p.717","Harrison 22e p.718","Harrison 22e p.719","Harrison 22e p.720","Harrison 22e p.721","Harrison 22e p.722","Harrison 22e p.723","Harrison 22e p.724","Harrison 22e p.725","Harrison 22e p.726"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_hyperoxaluria":{"name":"Primary hyperoxaluria","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PH","hereditary oxalosis","primary oxalosis","AGT deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Primary hyperoxaluria is a group of inherited disorders of glyoxylate metabolism that cause marked overproduction of oxalate by the liver","It typically presents with recurrent calcium oxalate kidney stones and nephrocalcinosis, often from childhood, and can progress to renal failure","Type 1, from deficiency of the hepatic enzyme alanine-glyoxylate aminotransferase, is the most common and generally the most severe form","As renal function declines, oxalate can no longer be cleared and deposits throughout the body (systemic oxalosis), affecting bone, heart, retina, and vessels","The diagnosis should be considered in any patient with recurrent oxalate stones, especially with early onset, a family history, or nephrocalcinosis","Elevated urinary oxalate, genetic testing, and sometimes liver biopsy establish the diagnosis, and high fluid intake with crystallisation inhibitors is a supportive measure","A subset of type 1 disease responds biochemically to pyridoxine, and definitive treatment historically involved combined or sequential liver and kidney transplantation, with newer RNA-targeted therapies now available"],"pathophysiology":"Primary hyperoxaluria results from autosomal recessive defects in enzymes of hepatic glyoxylate detoxification. In type 1, deficiency of the peroxisomal enzyme alanine-glyoxylate aminotransferase allows glyoxylate to be oxidised to oxalate; types 2 and 3 involve other enzymes in the same pathway. The liver overproduces oxalate, which the kidneys excrete in high amounts, promoting calcium oxalate crystallisation, stone formation, and nephrocalcinosis. Because oxalate is poorly cleared once renal function fails, it accumulates systemically and is deposited in multiple tissues as oxalosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Enteric (secondary) hyperoxaluria from fat malabsorption","Dietary oxalate overload","Idiopathic recurrent calcium oxalate stone disease","Other hereditary stone-forming disorders such as cystinuria","Distal renal tubular acidosis with nephrocalcinosis","Medullary sponge kidney"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["24-hour urinary oxalate measurement","Plasma oxalate, particularly as renal function declines","Stone analysis confirming calcium oxalate composition","Renal imaging for stones and nephrocalcinosis","Genetic testing for the causative enzyme defect","Liver enzyme assay on biopsy in selected diagnostic uncertainty","Assessment for systemic oxalosis when renal failure is present"],"redFlags":["Progressive decline in renal function toward end-stage disease","Systemic oxalosis with cardiac, skeletal, retinal, or vascular involvement","Recurrent obstructing stones with infection or acute kidney injury","Early-onset stone disease with nephrocalcinosis suggesting a severe genotype"],"prognosis":"The outlook varies with type and genotype; type 1 disease can progress to end-stage renal failure and life-threatening systemic oxalosis, whereas some pyridoxine-responsive and non-type-1 forms are milder, and early diagnosis with modern therapies has improved outcomes considerably.","pitfalls":["Treating recurrent stones symptomatically without recognising an underlying inherited metabolic cause","Confusing primary with enteric hyperoxaluria, which have different mechanisms and management","Overlooking systemic oxalosis as renal function falls","Performing isolated kidney transplantation in type 1 disease without addressing continued hepatic oxalate overproduction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_hyperparathyroidism":{"name":"Primary hyperparathyroidism","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PHPT","primary HPT","parathyroid adenoma-related hypercalcaemia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The leading cause of hypercalcaemia in ambulatory patients, most often detected incidentally on routine biochemistry","The biochemical hallmark is hypercalcaemia with a PTH level that is elevated or inappropriately normal rather than suppressed","Most cases are due to a single parathyroid adenoma; a minority reflect four-gland hyperplasia or, rarely, carcinoma","Classic symptomatic disease is summarised as stones, bones, abdominal groans, and psychiatric moans, but most patients are now asymptomatic","Skeletal effects include reduced bone density, particularly at cortical sites such as the distal radius, with fracture risk","Renal complications include nephrolithiasis, nephrocalcinosis, and impaired renal function; urine calcium helps assess risk","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia is a key mimic and is distinguished by a low urinary calcium excretion","Parathyroidectomy is curative and is indicated for symptomatic disease and for asymptomatic patients meeting defined criteria on calcium, bone density, renal parameters, or age"],"pathophysiology":"Primary hyperparathyroidism results from autonomous overproduction of parathyroid hormone by one or more parathyroid glands, usually a benign adenoma. Excess PTH raises serum calcium by increasing bone resorption, enhancing renal tubular calcium reabsorption, and stimulating renal conversion of vitamin D to its active form, which augments intestinal calcium absorption. PTH also promotes renal phosphate excretion, tending to lower serum phosphate. The loss of normal calcium-mediated negative feedback on the parathyroid glands sustains the hypercalcaemia. Chronic PTH excess drives cortical bone loss and contributes to hypercalciuria and stone formation.","additionalDifferentials":["Malignancy-associated hypercalcaemia (PTH-related peptide or bony metastases)","Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia","Tertiary hyperparathyroidism in chronic kidney disease","Vitamin D or vitamin A intoxication","Granulomatous disease producing excess active vitamin D","Thiazide diuretic or lithium effect on calcium"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Simultaneous serum calcium (corrected or ionised) and intact PTH","Serum phosphate, magnesium, and renal function","25-hydroxyvitamin D level, as deficiency can worsen disease and confound interpretation","24-hour urinary calcium to help distinguish FHH and assess stone risk","Bone mineral density including a cortical site such as the distal one-third radius","Renal imaging for stones or nephrocalcinosis, and parathyroid localisation studies before surgery"],"redFlags":["Severe hypercalcaemia with dehydration, confusion, or cardiac dysrhythmia","Rapidly rising calcium with a palpable neck mass suggesting parathyroid carcinoma","Acute kidney injury or symptomatic nephrolithiasis","Very high PTH with markedly elevated calcium out of keeping with a simple adenoma"],"prognosis":"Successful parathyroidectomy cures the disease, halts bone loss with partial recovery of density, and reduces stone recurrence; asymptomatic patients managed conservatively often remain stable but require monitoring, and parathyroid carcinoma has a worse outlook with a tendency to recur.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia, leading to unnecessary neck surgery","Overlooking concurrent vitamin D deficiency that masks or modifies the biochemical picture","Attributing hypercalcaemia to hyperparathyroidism when PTH is actually suppressed, indicating another cause","Neglecting bone density and renal assessment that determine the need for surgery in asymptomatic patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_immunodeficiency":{"name":"Primary Immunodeficiency","system":"Immunology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PID","common variable immunodeficiency","CVID"],"chapter":362,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2793-2804","clinicalPearls":["The most frequent symptom prompting the diagnosis of a PID is recurrent or unusually severe infection; recurrent allergic or autoimmune manifestations may also be the alerting feature (Harrison 22e p.2793).","PIDs are classified by (1) the arm of the immune system that is defective and (2) the mechanism of the defect; the major categories are antibody/B-cell defects (agammaglobulinaemia, CVID, hyper-IgM, IgA deficiency), T-cell/cellular defects, combined defects (SCID), and innate-immune defects of phagocytes, complement, or innate receptors/signalling (Harrison 22e p.2794).","The site of infection and the causal microorganism point to the affected compartment: respiratory-tract (sinus, bronchial) infection mostly suggests defective antibody responses; invasive bacterial infection can result from complement deficiency, innate-signalling defects, asplenia, or antibody defects; viral, recurrent Candida, and opportunistic infections suggest impaired T-cell immunity; skin infections and deep abscesses primarily reflect innate (phagocyte) defects such as chronic granulomatous disease (Harrison 22e p.2794).","B-lymphocyte (antibody) deficiencies are the most frequent PIDs, accounting for 60-70% of all cases; T-cell deficiencies account for ~20% and innate-immune defects for ~10% (Harrison 22e p.2794, 2796, 2797).","Encapsulated/pyogenic bacteria (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, M. catarrhalis) and recurrent sinopulmonary infection are the hallmark of defective antibody production; untreated recurrent bronchial infection leads to bronchiectasis, cor pulmonale, and death (Harrison 22e p.2801).","Invasive Neisseria infection points to deficiencies of the complement lytic phase (C5-C9) or the alternative pathway (factors D, properdin); classic-pathway and lytic-phase complement deficiencies may also predispose to autoimmune disease, notably SLE (Harrison 22e p.2797).","CVID is recognised predominantly in adults, although clinical manifestations can begin earlier in life; in most cases no molecular cause is identified and the diagnosis is made only after excluding hypomorphic agammaglobulinaemia mutations and more subtle T-cell defects, especially in children (Harrison 22e p.2802).","First-line laboratory work-up is guided by the clinical picture and includes blood cell counts/morphology, serum immunoglobulin levels (IgG, IgA, IgM, IgE), specific antibody responses to vaccines (tetanus toxoid, unconjugated pneumococcal polysaccharide), and lymphocyte subset phenotyping; more specific (genetic) tests then confirm a definitive diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2794-2795).","Even with profound B-cell deficiency, infections rarely occur before 6 months of age because of transplacental maternal IgG; conversely, in SCID clinical consequences typically appear within 3-6 months of birth (Harrison 22e p.2801, 2799).","Immunoglobulin replacement should be guided by the occurrence of infection, not by laboratory IgG/antibody levels alone, to avoid unjustified infusions; it is lifelong therapy (Harrison 22e p.2802).","A precise molecular/genetic diagnosis has become a milestone in the care of PID patients, guiding treatment selection, genetic counselling, and prenatal diagnosis; suspected cases should be referred to experienced centres (Harrison 22e p.2804)."],"pathophysiology":"PIDs are predominantly Mendelian genetic diseases that arise whenever the expression or function of a nonredundant effector or mediator of the immune system is genetically impaired. More than 550 conditions have been described, with deleterious mutations identified in roughly 500 genes; estimated prevalence is 5-10 per 100,000 (probably an underestimate). They are increasingly grouped within the broader category of inborn errors of immunity that also includes autoinflammatory syndromes. Defects can involve any aspect of innate or adaptive immunity, cell differentiation, effector function, or immune regulation. The clinical consequence is multiple levels of vulnerability to infection, ranging from extremely broad (as in SCID, where T-cell development is blocked) to narrowly restricted to a single class of organism (as in Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease, an IL-12-IFN-gamma axis defect). Antibody (B-cell) defects (agammaglobulinaemia from BTK and pre-B-cell-receptor gene defects, hyper-IgM from class-switch-recombination defects such as CD40L/CD40, IgA deficiency, and CVID) impair production or class switching of immunoglobulins, leaving patients unable to opsonise and neutralise pyogenic encapsulated bacteria. Cellular (T-cell) defects impair help for antibody production, macrophage activation, and cytotoxicity, causing susceptibility to viral, fungal (Candida), and opportunistic infections (e.g., Pneumocystis jirovecii, Cryptosporidium). Combined defects (SCID) abolish both arms. Innate defects affect phagocyte number (severe congenital neutropenia), adhesion (LAD), or microbial killing (chronic granulomatous disease, from defective NADPH-oxidase reactive-oxygen-species production), as well as complement components and innate receptor/signalling pathways (TLR adaptors IRAK4/MYD88). Beyond infection, PIDs can drive immunopathology including allergy, lymphoproliferation, autoimmunity, and an increased risk of malignancy (notably lymphoma) (Harrison 22e p.2793-2794, 2796-2802).","additionalDifferentials":["Severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID)","X-linked or autosomal-recessive agammaglobulinaemia","Hyper-IgM (HIGM) syndromes (CD40L/CD40 or AID/UNG defects)","IgA deficiency and selective IgG-subclass deficiency","Selective antibody deficiency to polysaccharide antigens with normal total Ig","Chronic granulomatous disease and other phagocyte killing defects","Leukocyte adhesion deficiency and severe congenital neutropenia","Complement component deficiencies (classic, alternative, lytic-phase)","Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease (IL-12-IFN-gamma axis defects)","Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome, ataxia-telangiectasia, DiGeorge syndrome, hyper-IgE syndrome","Regulatory-pathway disorders: HLH, ALPS, IPEX, APECED"],"infectionMimics":["Recurrent pyogenic sinopulmonary infection from anatomical causes (e.g., cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis of other aetiology) mimicking antibody deficiency","HIV/AIDS and other secondary (acquired) immunodeficiency producing an opportunistic-infection pattern resembling a T-cell PID","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia as a presenting opportunistic infection of T-cell or combined defects","Recurrent or chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis","Disseminated or recurrent nontuberculous mycobacterial infection (suggesting MSMD)","Invasive aspergillosis or other filamentous-mould infection (suggesting CGD)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Secondary hypogammaglobulinaemia from thymoma (profound hypogammaglobulinaemia observed in adults)","Hypogammaglobulinaemia secondary to myelodysplastic syndrome (can present as agammaglobulinaemia)","Protein-losing states (nephrotic syndrome, protein-losing enteropathy) lowering immunoglobulin levels","Drug-induced or malignancy-associated (e.g., CLL, lymphoma) immunosuppression","Cytokine-neutralising autoantibodies (to IFN-gamma, IL-6, IL-7, type I interferon) that can mimic a PID","VEXAS syndrome (somatic UBA1 mutation) presenting with late-onset inflammation, vasculitis, and cytopenias"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cell counts and morphology (neutrophil and lymphocyte counts adjusted for age; eosinophilia; Howell-Jolly bodies suggesting asplenia)","Serum immunoglobulin levels (IgG, IgA, IgM) and IgE","Specific antibody responses to vaccination (tetanus toxoid; unconjugated pneumococcal polysaccharide antigens)","Lymphocyte phenotyping: T-, B-, and NK-cell counts and subsets; switched (CD27+ IgM-IgD-) and non-switched (CD27+ IgM+IgD+) memory B cells","Dihydrorhodamine (DHR) fluorescence or nitroblue tetrazolium (NBT) assay for neutrophil reactive-oxygen-species production (chronic granulomatous disease)","Complement functional assays CH50 and AP50 (classic and alternative pathways)","Chest x-ray for thymic shadow (SCID, DiGeorge) and costochondral junctions (ADA deficiency); bone x-ray of metaphyses (cartilage-hair hypoplasia)","Abdominal ultrasonography for spleen size (asplenia)","Newborn TREC (T-cell receptor excision circle) screening on the Guthrie card for SCID","Targeted gene-panel resequencing and/or whole-exome/genome sequencing for definitive molecular diagnosis"],"redFlags":["Recurrent, unusually severe, or persistent infections, or infection by unusual/opportunistic organisms","A family history of early childhood deaths (suggesting X-linked or recessive inheritance, e.g., SCID)","Lymphocytopenia in an infant (strongly suggestive of SCID in >90% of cases) or absent thymic shadow on chest x-ray","Failure to thrive with protracted diarrhoea, recurrent oral candidiasis, or Pneumocystis pneumonia in early infancy","Complications of live vaccines (e.g., BCG dissemination, rotavirus diarrhoea) signalling a severe T-cell defect","Invasive Neisseria infection (complement lytic-phase or alternative-pathway deficiency) or recurrent encapsulated-organism sepsis with asplenia","Recurrent infection accompanied by autoimmunity, lymphoproliferation, granulomatous disease, or lymphoma","Absence of pus at sites of infection (hallmark of severe congenital neutropenia)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies widely with the molecule and arm of immunity affected. T-cell and combined defects generally carry a poor prognosis and require early diagnosis and intervention; SCID is fatal in infancy without treatment, but allogeneic HSCT offers a high curative potential in patients in otherwise reasonable condition, and gene therapy has succeeded in X-linked (gamma-c) SCID, ADA-SCID, and Artemis deficiency. Functional T-cell defects carry a worse HSCT prognosis than SCID because many patients are already chronically infected at diagnosis. For antibody deficiencies, lifelong immunoglobulin replacement combined with management of chronic lung disease reduces respiratory infections and prevents progression to bronchiectasis and cor pulmonale; in CVID, lymphoproliferation, granulomatous disease, autoimmunity, and lymphoma define long-term prognosis. DNA-repair disorders such as ataxia-telangiectasia are progressive and carry a very high risk of lymphoma, leukaemia, and (in adulthood) carcinoma. Newborn TREC screening, by enabling treatment of uninfected SCID patients, maximises the chance of cure. Early diagnosis with referral to experienced centres is essential to optimal outcome (Harrison 22e p.2794, 2799-2800, 2802, 2804).","pitfalls":["CVID can present in adulthood and is easily missed; it is recognised predominantly in adults although manifestations may begin earlier (Harrison 22e p.2802).","High variability of expression for a given PID (e.g., WAS, XLA, CTLA4 deficiency) can hamper diagnosis; modifier genes and environmental triggers such as EBV (in XLP) modulate the phenotype (Harrison 22e p.2804).","Leaky BTK mutations causing mild hypogammaglobulinaemia with low but detectable B cells should not be confused with CVID (Harrison 22e p.2802).","T-cell lymphopenia in SCID may be masked by maternally-engrafted T cells, so screening for maternal cells with genetic markers should be performed when needed (Harrison 22e p.2799).","Somatic mutations in an affected gene can attenuate the phenotype of T-cell PIDs (described for ADA, X-linked SCID, RAG, NEMO, and most often WAS), while somatic mutations can also create PID-like disease (as in ALPS) (Harrison 22e p.2804).","Cytokine-neutralising autoantibodies (to IFN-gamma, IL-6, IL-7, type I interferon) can mimic a PID (Harrison 22e p.2804).","Caution with immunoglobulin replacement in patients with complete IgA deficiency and residual antibody production: anti-IgA antibodies can trigger anaphylactic shock, so IgA-free IgG preparations should be used (Harrison 22e p.2803).","Ataxia-telangiectasia should be considered in young children with IgA deficiency and recurrent problematic infections, as ataxia and telangiectasia may not appear before age 3-4 years (Harrison 22e p.2799-2800)."],"severityClassification":"PIDs are classified along two axes: (1) the arm of the immune system that is defective and (2) the mechanism of the defect (when known). The two main divisions are deficiencies of the innate immune system (phagocytic cells - impaired production [severe congenital neutropenia], asplenia, impaired adhesion [LAD], impaired killing [CGD]; innate-immunity receptors/signal transduction [TLR-signalling defects, MSMD]; complement deficiencies of the classical/alternative/lectin pathways and the lytic phase) and deficiencies of the adaptive immune system (T lymphocytes - SCID, DiGeorge, combined immunodeficiencies, hyper-IgE, DOCK8, CD40L, Wiskott-Aldrich, ataxia-telangiectasia and DNA-repair defects; B lymphocytes - X-linked/AR agammaglobulinaemia, hyper-IgM, CVID, IgA deficiency), together with regulatory defects (autoinflammatory syndromes, severe colitis, HLH, ALPS, autoimmunity such as IPEX and APECED). The classification is acknowledged to involve some arbitrary decisions because of overlap and incomplete data (Harrison 22e p.2794).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2793-2804"],"crossLinks":["pneumococcal_disease","pneumocystis_pneumonia","candidiasis","nontuberculous_mycobacterial_infection","meningococcal_disease","aspergillosis","hiv_aids"]},"primary_myelofibrosis":{"name":"Primary myelofibrosis","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PMF","myelofibrosis","chronic idiopathic myelofibrosis","agnogenic myeloid metaplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A myeloproliferative neoplasm defined by clonal proliferation with progressive marrow fibrosis and extramedullary haematopoiesis, classically producing marked splenomegaly","Typical features include fatigue and anaemia, constitutional symptoms (weight loss, night sweats, fever), and abdominal discomfort or early satiety from splenic enlargement","The blood film shows a leucoerythroblastic picture with teardrop-shaped red cells (dacrocytes), nucleated red cells, and immature myeloid forms","Attempts to aspirate marrow often yield a dry tap because of fibrosis, so a trephine biopsy demonstrating reticulin or collagen fibrosis is essential","Driver mutations in JAK2, CALR, or MPL are present in most cases and support the diagnosis; additional high-risk mutations refine prognosis","Prognostic scoring incorporates age, symptoms, cytopenias, blast count, and cytogenetic/molecular features to guide management decisions","JAK inhibitors reduce spleen size and constitutional symptoms and can improve quality of life, though they are not curative","Allogeneic stem cell transplant is the only curative therapy and is considered for suitable higher-risk patients; transformation to acute leukaemia is a feared complication"],"pathophysiology":"Primary myelofibrosis originates from a clonal haematopoietic stem cell with dysregulated JAK-STAT signalling, most often driven by JAK2, CALR, or MPL mutations. The abnormal clone, particularly its megakaryocytes, secretes fibrogenic cytokines and growth factors (such as transforming growth factor-beta and platelet-derived growth factor) that stimulate marrow fibroblasts to deposit reticulin and collagen. As the marrow becomes progressively fibrotic, haematopoiesis relocates to the spleen and liver (extramedullary haematopoiesis), causing organomegaly and release of immature cells into the circulation. A chronic inflammatory cytokine state produces constitutional symptoms, and clonal evolution can culminate in acute leukaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Secondary (reactive) myelofibrosis from metastatic cancer or infection","Post-polycythaemia vera or post-essential thrombocythaemia myelofibrosis","Chronic myeloid leukaemia","Myelodysplastic syndrome with fibrosis","Hairy cell leukaemia and other marrow-infiltrating disorders","Autoimmune myelofibrosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film for leucoerythroblastic changes and teardrop cells","Bone marrow trephine biopsy with reticulin/collagen staining to grade fibrosis","Molecular testing for JAK2, CALR, and MPL, plus high-molecular-risk mutation panels","BCR-ABL1 testing to exclude chronic myeloid leukaemia","Cytogenetic analysis for prognostic stratification","Abdominal imaging to document splenomegaly and extramedullary disease"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising blast count or new cytogenetic changes indicating leukaemic transformation","Severe anaemia or thrombocytopenia with bleeding requiring urgent support","Massive symptomatic splenomegaly with risk of splenic infarction or rupture","Portal hypertension or splanchnic vein thrombosis from extramedullary haematopoiesis and hypercoagulability"],"prognosis":"Survival in primary myelofibrosis is highly variable, ranging from many years in low-risk disease to under two years in high-risk categories; adverse cytogenetics, high-risk mutations, transfusion dependence, and excess blasts predict poorer outcomes, and only allogeneic transplant offers cure.","pitfalls":["Assuming a leucoerythroblastic film always indicates PMF without excluding marrow infiltration by malignancy or infection","Relying on a marrow aspirate alone when fibrosis produces a dry tap and a biopsy is needed","Failing to exclude chronic myeloid leukaemia with BCR-ABL1 testing","Overlooking prognostic mutations that would prompt earlier transplant referral","Not recognising that a JAK inhibitor controls symptoms and spleen size but does not prevent progression"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"primary_open_angle_glaucoma":{"name":"Primary open-angle glaucoma","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["POAG","open-angle glaucoma","chronic simple glaucoma","chronic open-angle glaucoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically an insidious, painless, bilateral (often asymmetric) chronic optic neuropathy detected on screening rather than by symptoms","Peripheral visual field is lost first, with central acuity preserved until late, so patients are frequently unaware until damage is advanced","The drainage angle is open on gonioscopy, distinguishing it from angle-closure disease","Hallmark disc changes include increased cup-to-disc ratio, focal neuroretinal rim thinning or notching, and disc haemorrhages","Risk rises with elevated intraocular pressure, older age, family history, African ancestry, high myopia, and thin central corneas","Normal-tension glaucoma shows characteristic optic nerve and field damage despite pressures in the statistically normal range","Management aims to lower intraocular pressure with topical medication first, escalating to laser trabeculoplasty or surgical drainage (trabeculectomy) to slow progression"],"pathophysiology":"Primary open-angle glaucoma is a progressive optic neuropathy in which aqueous outflow through an anatomically open trabecular meshwork is impaired, raising resistance to drainage. The resulting intraocular pressure, sometimes within the normal range, contributes to mechanical and vascular stress at the optic nerve head, causing loss of retinal ganglion cell axons. This produces characteristic cupping of the optic disc and a corresponding pattern of arcuate and peripheral visual field loss that advances slowly over years.","additionalDifferentials":["Normal-tension glaucoma","Ocular hypertension without nerve damage","Secondary open-angle glaucoma (pigmentary, pseudoexfoliation, steroid-induced)","Non-glaucomatous optic neuropathy","Physiological large optic disc cup"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Applanation tonometry to measure intraocular pressure","Gonioscopy to confirm the angle is open","Dilated optic disc examination for cupping and rim changes","Automated perimetry (visual field testing) to detect and monitor field loss","Optical coherence tomography of the retinal nerve fibre layer and ganglion cell complex","Central corneal thickness (pachymetry), which influences pressure interpretation and risk"],"redFlags":["Progressive visual field loss or disc change despite apparently controlled pressure","Disc (splinter) haemorrhage, a marker of ongoing progression","Very high presenting pressure or advanced cupping threatening fixation","Rapid deterioration suggesting a secondary or aggressive glaucoma"],"prognosis":"With early detection and sustained pressure control most patients retain useful vision for life, but untreated or poorly controlled disease progresses to irreversible visual field loss and blindness.","pitfalls":["Relying on a single pressure reading, as pressure fluctuates and can be normal in normal-tension glaucoma","Attributing all disc cupping to glaucoma without correlating fields and nerve fibre layer","Poor adherence to topical therapy going unrecognised as the cause of progression","Overlooking secondary causes such as pseudoexfoliation or long-term steroid use"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"prion_diseases":{"name":"Prion Diseases (CJD)","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease","CJD","spongiform encephalopathy"],"chapter":449,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3525","clinicalPearls":["CJD is a rapidly progressive disorder associated with dementia, focal cortical signs, rigidity, and myoclonus, causing death less than 1 year after first symptoms appear (p.3525-3526).","CJD is the classic cause of a rapidly progressive dementia (RPD: progression from symptom onset to dementia within a year or less), especially when associated with myoclonus, though more often RPD is due to AD, another neurodegenerative disorder, or autoimmune encephalitis (p.237).","On examination, CJD is suggested by diffuse rigidity, an akinetic-mute state, and prominent, often startle-sensitive myoclonus (p.239).","MRI diffusion-weighted imaging reveals restricted diffusion within the cortical ribbon and/or basal ganglia in most patients; basal ganglia or thalamus hyperintensity may also be seen on diffusion/FLAIR MRI (p.240, p.3526).","EEG is not routinely used but can suggest CJD by showing repetitive bursts of diffuse high-amplitude sharp waves (periodic complexes) (p.241).","Prion diseases such as CJD are rare (prevalence ~1 per million); the rapidity of progression distinguishes CJD from AD, though AD can occasionally present as a rapidly progressive dementia (p.3525-3526).","Prion disease is one cause of subacute progressive ataxia, especially when an infectious etiology such as transmission from contaminated human growth hormone is responsible (p.3590)."],"pathophysiology":"Prion diseases are caused by prions, host-encoded proteins (the prion protein) that adopt an alternative, abnormal conformation (misfolded PrPSc) which undergoes self-propagation. PrPSc imposes its conformation on the normal precursor protein, which in turn becomes self-templating, producing faithful copies; the misfolded protein is enriched for beta-sheet and assembles into amyloid fibrils. The amyloid plaques in kuru and CJD are filled with PrPSc prions assembled into fibrils. CJD is one of the major degenerative dementias characterized by abnormal aggregation of a specific protein (misfolded prion protein, PrPSc). Familial CJD is genetically linked to mutations in the PrP gene (p.237, p.3400-3401).","additionalDifferentials":["Alzheimer's disease (can occasionally present as rapidly progressive dementia)","Corticobasal degeneration (CBD) - rapid degenerative dementia with prominent movement abnormalities, more likely mistaken for CJD","Dementia with Lewy bodies (DLB) - rapid degenerative dementia with prominent movement abnormalities, more likely mistaken for CJD","Other rapidly progressive dementias"],"infectionMimics":["Viral encephalitides","Bacterial encephalitides","Other CNS infections causing rapidly progressive dementia"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hashimoto's encephalopathy","CNS vasculitis","CNS lymphoma","Paraneoplastic/autoimmune syndromes (autoimmune encephalitis)","Prolonged focal or generalized seizures (can rarely produce a similar imaging appearance)"],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI diffusion-weighted imaging - restricted diffusion in cortical ribbon and/or basal ganglia (cortical ribboning); basal ganglia or thalamus hyperintensity on diffusion/FLAIR","EEG - periodic complexes (repetitive bursts of diffuse high-amplitude sharp waves)","Lumbar puncture/CSF - to exclude CNS infection or inflammation among rapidly progressive dementia mimics","MRI/neuroimaging to exclude treatable structural causes (neoplasm, subdural hematoma, NPH)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive dementia (onset to dementia within a year or less)","Dementia accompanied by myoclonus (often startle-sensitive)","Diffuse rigidity and akinetic-mute state","Subacute progressive ataxia","Cortical ribboning / basal ganglia or thalamic hyperintensity on diffusion/FLAIR MRI","Periodic complexes on EEG"],"prognosis":"Uniformly fatal. CJD causes death less than 1 year after the first symptoms appear (p.3525-3526).","pitfalls":["Must exclude treatable causes of rapidly progressive dementia (e.g., autoimmune/paraneoplastic encephalitis, Hashimoto's encephalopathy, CNS vasculitis, CNS infection, lymphoma) before attributing a rapidly progressive dementia to CJD.","More rapid degenerative dementias with prominent movement abnormalities (CBD, DLB) are more likely to be mistaken for CJD.","Rarely, prolonged focal or generalized seizures can produce an MRI appearance similar to CJD."],"severityClassification":"Etiologic classification of prion diseases (all caused by PrPSc): sporadic (e.g., classic CJD), familial/genetic (linked to PrP gene mutations - includes familial CJD, Gerstmann-Straussler-Scheinker disease [GSS], and fatal insomnia), iatrogenic (transmission e.g. from contaminated human growth hormone), and acquired/transmissible forms (kuru). PrPSc prions also cause animal spongiform encephalopathies including scrapie, bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE), and chronic wasting disease (p.3400-3401, p.3590).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3525","Harrison 22e p.3526","Harrison 22e p.237","Harrison 22e p.239","Harrison 22e p.240","Harrison 22e p.241","Harrison 22e p.3400","Harrison 22e p.3401","Harrison 22e p.3590"],"crossLinks":["alzheimers_disease","movement_disorders"]},"progressive_supranuclear_palsy":{"name":"Progressive supranuclear palsy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PSP","Steele-Richardson-Olszewski syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An atypical parkinsonian syndrome caused by tau protein accumulation, with early postural instability and unexplained backward falls","The hallmark is a vertical supranuclear gaze palsy, especially impaired downgaze, which may be preceded by slowing of vertical saccades","Axial rigidity predominates over limb rigidity and patients often adopt an extended, erect posture unlike the flexed posture of Parkinson disease","Parkinsonism in PSP is typically symmetric, has little tremor, and responds poorly and only transiently to dopaminergic therapy","A characteristic surprised or astonished facial expression results from frontalis overactivity and reduced blinking","Early frontal-type cognitive change, pseudobulbar affect, and dysarthria or dysphagia are common","MRI may show midbrain atrophy producing the hummingbird or penguin sign on midsagittal images","Poor levodopa response, early falls, and early gaze problems help separate it from Parkinson disease"],"pathophysiology":"PSP is a four-repeat tauopathy in which abnormal tau aggregates as neurofibrillary tangles, tufted astrocytes, and coiled bodies in the basal ganglia, subthalamic nucleus, substantia nigra, midbrain tegmentum, and brainstem nuclei controlling eye movements. Degeneration of the midbrain and its ocular motor circuitry produces the supranuclear gaze palsy, while nigral and striatal involvement causes the poorly levodopa-responsive parkinsonism. Damage to brainstem postural and gait pathways underlies the early instability and falls, and frontal-subcortical involvement drives the cognitive and behavioural features.","additionalDifferentials":["Parkinson disease","Multiple system atrophy","Corticobasal degeneration","Dementia with Lewy bodies","Vascular parkinsonism","Normal pressure hydrocephalus and other causes of gait instability"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the brain to assess midbrain atrophy and exclude structural or vascular mimics","Detailed clinical assessment of vertical saccades and gaze range","Trial of dopaminergic therapy to document the typically poor and transient response","Neuropsychological assessment of frontal-executive function","Swallowing evaluation given aspiration risk","Exclusion of reversible causes such as normal pressure hydrocephalus where indicated"],"redFlags":["Recurrent unexplained falls, especially backward, causing injury and needing safety measures","Progressive dysphagia with aspiration risk requiring swallowing assessment","Rapid cognitive or behavioural decline affecting safety and capacity","Features suggesting an alternative treatable diagnosis such as hydrocephalus"],"prognosis":"PSP is relentlessly progressive with no disease-modifying therapy; mobility, communication, and swallowing decline over several years, and death is commonly related to aspiration and its complications.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing early PSP as Parkinson disease before the gaze palsy is evident","Attributing frequent falls to general frailty rather than the disease","Persisting with high-dose dopaminergic therapy despite a clearly poor response","Overlooking dysphagia and aspiration risk until a pneumonia occurs","Missing the surprised facial appearance and slowed vertical saccades as early clues"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"prolactinoma":{"name":"Prolactinoma","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["prolactin-secreting pituitary adenoma","lactotroph adenoma","hyperprolactinaemia (tumoural)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The commonest hormone-secreting pituitary tumour; presentation differs by sex, with menstrual disturbance and galactorrhoea in women and reduced libido or erectile dysfunction in men","Because men lack an early hormonal warning sign, they more often present late with large tumours causing mass effect","Hyperprolactinaemia suppresses gonadotrophin secretion, producing hypogonadism, infertility, and reduced bone density over time","Before diagnosing a prolactinoma, exclude other causes of raised prolactin such as pregnancy, hypothyroidism, renal failure, and dopamine-blocking drugs","Stalk compression by any large sellar mass can modestly raise prolactin; very high levels are more specific for a true prolactinoma","In large tumours with only mildly elevated prolactin, laboratory dilution may be needed to exclude the hook effect artefact","Unlike most pituitary tumours, prolactinomas are usually managed medically with dopamine agonists rather than surgery as first line","Dopamine agonist therapy typically lowers prolactin, restores gonadal function, and shrinks tumour volume"],"pathophysiology":"A prolactinoma is a benign lactotroph adenoma of the anterior pituitary that autonomously secretes prolactin. Prolactin is normally under tonic inhibitory control by hypothalamic dopamine, which is why dopamine agonists are effective and dopamine-blocking drugs raise prolactin. Excess prolactin inhibits pulsatile gonadotrophin-releasing hormone secretion, reducing LH and FSH and causing hypogonadism, galactorrhoea, and infertility. Larger tumours (macroadenomas) may compress the optic chiasm and adjacent pituitary tissue, producing visual field defects and hypopituitarism. Non-tumoural hyperprolactinaemia from stalk disconnection, drugs, or systemic disease must be distinguished from a true adenoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Physiological hyperprolactinaemia of pregnancy or lactation","Drug-induced hyperprolactinaemia (antipsychotics, antiemetics, some antidepressants)","Primary hypothyroidism raising prolactin via TRH","Chronic kidney disease reducing prolactin clearance","Stalk-effect hyperprolactinaemia from a non-lactotroph sellar mass","Macroprolactinaemia (biologically inactive complexed prolactin)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting serum prolactin, repeated if borderline, with sample dilution when a hook effect is suspected","Pregnancy test, thyroid function, and renal function to exclude secondary causes","Review of medications that raise prolactin","Pituitary MRI to characterise adenoma size and suprasellar extension","Assessment of macroprolactin when clinical picture and level are discordant","Visual fields and anterior pituitary hormone panel for macroadenomas"],"redFlags":["Bitemporal visual field loss from chiasmal compression by a macroadenoma","Sudden severe headache with visual or cranial nerve signs suggesting apoplexy","Symptoms and signs of coexisting hypopituitarism, including adrenal insufficiency","Very large invasive tumours threatening vision or requiring urgent decompression"],"prognosis":"Most microprolactinomas respond well to dopamine agonists with normalisation of prolactin, restoration of fertility, and often no significant growth; macroadenomas also usually shrink with medical therapy, and in selected patients treatment can eventually be tapered, though relapse of hyperprolactinaemia is possible.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing a prolactinoma without excluding drugs, hypothyroidism, renal failure, and pregnancy","Missing the hook effect and underestimating prolactin in a large tumour, leading to inappropriate surgery","Overlooking macroprolactin as a cause of asymptomatic elevation","Failing to counsel on bone health and fertility implications of chronic hypogonadism"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"prostate_cancer":{"name":"Prostate Disease (BPH & Cancer)","system":"Oncology / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["prostate cancer","BPH","benign prostatic hyperplasia"],"chapter":92,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.695-704","clinicalPearls":["Both benign and malignant prostate changes rise sharply with age: autopsies of men in the eighth decade show hyperplastic changes in >90% and malignant changes in >70%, so management is risk-adapted and weighed against competing causes of morbidity and mortality (p.695).","BPH arises in the periurethral transition zone surrounding the urethra, whereas most cancers arise in the peripheral zone; peripheral-zone cancers may or may not be palpable on digital rectal exam (DRE) (p.696).","BPH causes lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS) split into obstructive symptoms (hesitancy, straining, weak stream, terminal dribbling, prolonged voiding, incomplete emptying) and irritative symptoms (frequency, urgency, urge incontinence, small voided volumes); LUTS reflect both prostatic enlargement and age-related detrusor dysfunction, not mass effect alone (p.703).","PSA (kallikrein KLK3) is prostate specific, NOT prostate cancer specific: it can rise from prostatitis, BPH, or cancer; a lower free PSA fraction is more likely associated with cancer at biopsy (p.696-697).","After radical prostatectomy PSA should become undetectable within about 6 weeks (no prostate = no detectable PSA); persistence of measurable PSA after gland removal is typical of recurrent cancer (p.696-697).","Only 20-25% of patients with an abnormal DRE have prostate cancer; carcinoma is characteristically hard, nodular, irregular, asymmetric, but induration can also be BPH or calculi (p.696).",">95% of prostate cancers are adenocarcinomas; the remainder are squamous, transitional cell, rarely carcinosarcoma or small-cell histologies (p.697).","Most men diagnosed with prostate cancer will NOT die of it; the incidence/mortality ratio is extremely high relative to other malignancies, which drives the active-surveillance vs treatment balance (p.695).","Multiparametric prostate MRI (mpMRI) graded by PI-RADS is now obtained in most cases BEFORE biopsy: PI-RADS 1-2 lesions can safely avoid biopsy; PI-RADS 3-5 lesions get targeted biopsy, reducing detection of indolent Gleason 6 (grade group 1) cancers without missing clinically relevant ones (p.697)."],"pathophysiology":"The prostate (pelvic, adjacent to rectum, bladder, neurovascular bundles and urinary sphincter) is composed of tubuloalveolar glands in fibromuscular stroma. PSA is produced by epithelial cells; both epithelial and stromal cells express androgen receptors (AR) and depend on androgens for growth. Testosterone is converted by prostatic 5alpha-reductase to the more potent dihydrotestosterone. BPH is non-malignant proliferation of the periurethral transition zone (begins at puberty, accelerates after age 55) producing bladder-outlet obstruction; symptoms also reflect age-related detrusor dysfunction. Malignancy usually arises in the peripheral zone; the cancer continuum runs from preneoplastic/localized lesion to metastatic, symptomatic, lethal disease over years to decades. Prostate cancer is AR-signaling dependent, so androgen deprivation is the mainstay of metastatic therapy; castration-resistant prostate cancer (CRPC) progresses despite castrate testosterone (<=50 ng/mL) because most CRPCs continue to express and signal through the AR, and androgen-resistant cells are present from diagnosis (cure is not achieved by androgen depletion alone). (p.695-696, 702-703)","additionalDifferentials":["Benign prostatic hyperplasia (transition-zone enlargement, bladder-outlet obstruction)","Acute/chronic bacterial prostatitis (can elevate PSA)","Overactive bladder (frequently coexists with BPH-related LUTS)","Age-related detrusor dysfunction / primary bladder dysfunction (detected on pressure-flow urodynamics)","Urethral stricture or bladder-neck contracture as alternative cause of obstructive voiding","Prostatic calculi (cause of palpable induration on DRE)"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial prostatitis (raises PSA and can mimic malignancy biochemically; treat symptomatic bacterial prostatitis with antibiotics before biopsy, but routine antibiotics in an asymptomatic patient with elevated PSA are discouraged)","Recurrent urinary tract infection as a complication of bladder-outlet obstruction"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["BPH-related induration on DRE mimicking carcinoma","Prostatic calculi causing nodularity","Degenerative spine disease, spinal stenosis, or osteoporotic vertebral collapse mimicking metastatic bone pain in advanced disease (require accurate diagnosis before attributing pain to metastases)","Direct invasion of the prostate by colon cancer or bladder transitional cell carcinoma (true metastases TO the prostate are rare)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Digital rectal exam (assesses size, symmetry, consistency, nodules; NOT a validated standalone screening test due to frequent false positives and false negatives)","Serum PSA (total); free/total PSA ratio; common biopsy-prompting cut point total PSA >=4 ng/mL (no PSA level carries zero cancer risk)","Second-line stratifying tests after elevated PSA: 4K Score, Prostate Health Index (PHI = free PSA + total PSA + [-2]proPSA), urine ExoDx exosome test, Select-MDx mRNA test","Multiparametric prostate MRI (mpMRI) with PI-RADS scoring before biopsy","Image-guided needle biopsy (TRUS-, MRI-, or fusion-guided; extended 12-core pattern; MRI-guided superior to ultrasound-guided)","Gleason grading and the five-tier grade group system (grade group 1 = Gleason <=6 favorable, up to grade group 5 = Gleason 9-10)","TNM staging (T1c PSA-detected, T2 palpable organ-confined, T3-T4 extraprostatic); risk stratification into low/intermediate(favorable vs unfavorable)/high/very-high risk","PSMA PET scan (FDA approved for intermediate/high-risk disease; more sensitive/specific than conventional imaging but cannot detect low-volume microscopic disease)","For BPH: AUA Symptom Index (AUASI) / International Prostate Symptom Score (IPSS), uroflowmetry, bladder ultrasound for postvoid residual, pressure-flow urodynamics, cystoscopy if hematuria, upper-tract imaging if hematuria/calculi/prior tract problems"],"redFlags":["Osteoblastic bone metastases (prostate cancer characteristically produces bone-forming metastases) presenting as back/skeletal pain; bone is the dominant site of metastatic and castration-resistant progression","Spinal cord compression / cord compromise or base-of-skull symptoms from metastatic disease require EMERGENCY evaluation because loss of neurologic function may be permanent if not treated quickly","GnRH agonists are relatively contraindicated in patients with significant urinary obstruction, cancer-related pain, or spinal cord compromise because the initial testosterone surge can cause a disease flare (AR antagonists are added to blunt flare; GnRH antagonists and abiraterone do not cause a surge)","BPH danger signs mandating evaluation/treatment regardless of gland size: inability to urinate (acute urinary retention), gross hematuria, recurrent infection, or bladder stones","Rising nadir PSA >4 ng/mL at 6-8 months after starting androgen deprivation predicts markedly inferior survival","Marrow infiltration (myelophthisis), coagulopathy, or thromboembolism as manifestations of widespread metastatic disease"],"prognosis":"Most men with prostate cancer do not die from it; >70-80% of newly diagnosed cancers are now organ-confined. Nonmetastatic disease is stratified low/intermediate/high (and very-high) risk; grade group system accurately predicts prognosis with grade group 1 (Gleason <=6) being favorable. A single PSA at age 60 correlates strongly (AUC 0.90) with lifetime risk of death from prostate cancer, and ~90% of prostate-cancer deaths occur in the top PSA quartile (>2 ng/mL), though only a minority of those men develop lethal disease. Androgen deprivation produces an initial response and dormant period but is not curative; PSA normalizes in 60-70% and measurable disease regresses in >50%, with survival inversely proportional to disease extent at treatment start and to the 6-8 month nadir PSA. Castration-resistant prostate cancer is lethal for most patients; multiple agents (abiraterone, enzalutamide, docetaxel, cabazitaxel, radium-223, Lu-177 PSMA-617, PARP inhibitors for BRCA/DNA-repair mutations, checkpoint inhibitors for MSI-high/dMMR) prolong survival. (p.695, 697, 701-703)","pitfalls":["PSA screening is controversial: it generates both false positives and false negatives and can lead to diagnosis and treatment of indolent cancers that pose little risk; the decision (USPSTF: individual shared decision for men 55-69, recommended AGAINST screening at age >=70; AUA: shared decision, earlier for Black ancestry/germline mutations/strong family history) must balance benefit against overtreatment harm.","PSA is prostate specific, not cancer specific - do not equate an elevated PSA with cancer; BPH and prostatitis also raise it.","There is NO PSA level below which prostate cancer risk is zero (Prostate Cancer Prevention Trial); a 'normal' PSA does not exclude cancer.","DRE alone is inaccurate for staging (capsular invasion, seminal-vesicle and nodal involvement) and is not a validated screening test by itself.","Patients misread Gleason 6 as 'mid-scale' and assume aggressive disease; the grade group system was created because Gleason 6 (grade group 1) is actually the lowest, favorable-risk category.","PSMA PET, despite high sensitivity, cannot detect low-volume microscopic nodal disease - a negative scan does not exclude micrometastasis.","A negative biopsy in a patient with elevated PSA does NOT rule out a future cancer diagnosis - continue follow-up.","No agent is currently approved for prostate cancer prevention (5-alpha-reductase inhibitors reduced mainly low-risk indolent cancers; vitamins/supplements showed no benefit).","No rationale to screen asymptomatic men with short life expectancy; antiandrogen monotherapy gives inferior cancer-specific outcomes versus castration."],"severityClassification":"Gleason grade group system: grade group 1 (Gleason <=6), 2 (3+4=7), 3 (4+3=7), 4 (4+4=8), 5 (Gleason 9-10). TNM staging: T1c clinically inapparent PSA-detected; T2 palpable, confined within prostate; T3 extends through capsule/seminal vesicles (T3b); T4 fixed or invading adjacent structures. Nonmetastatic risk groups: low (organ-confined, PSA <10, grade group 1); intermediate (organ-confined, PSA 10-20, or grade group 2 favorable / 3 unfavorable, with >50% positive cores = unfavorable); high (extraprostatic, PSA >20, or grade group 4-5); very-high (seminal vesicle T3b or adjacent-organ T4 invasion or multiple high-grade biopsy samples). Metastatic states: noncastrate metastatic (testosterone >50 ng/dL) vs castration-resistant (CRPC, testosterone <=50 ng/mL). (p.697-698, 701-703)","references":["Harrison 22e p.695-704"],"crossLinks":["cord_compression"]},"protein_losing_enteropathy":{"name":"Protein-Losing Enteropathy","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PLE","intestinal protein loss","gut protein loss"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["This is a syndrome, not a single disease, in which excessive loss of plasma proteins into the gastrointestinal tract leads to hypoproteinaemia and oedema.","It should be suspected in a child with hypoalbuminaemia and oedema once liver disease, kidney protein loss, and inadequate intake have been excluded.","Underlying mechanisms fall into two broad groups: lymphatic obstruction or elevated lymphatic pressure, and mucosal disease with increased permeability or erosion.","Lymphatic causes include intestinal lymphangiectasia and elevated central venous pressure after cardiac surgery (notably the Fontan circulation), while mucosal causes include inflammatory, allergic, infectious, and infiltrative enteropathies.","Because losses are non-selective, other serum proteins such as immunoglobulins and clotting factors may also fall, sometimes causing lymphopenia and predisposing to infection.","A stool alpha-1-antitrypsin measurement is a useful non-invasive marker of enteric protein loss because it is not degraded in the gut.","Management is directed at the underlying cause together with nutritional support, and in lymphatic causes a low-fat diet enriched with medium-chain triglycerides can reduce lymphatic protein loss.","Peripheral oedema, ascites, or effusions in a child with unexplained low albumin should prompt evaluation of the gut as a source."],"pathophysiology":"Plasma proteins normally enter the gut lumen only in small amounts. In protein-losing enteropathy this loss is markedly increased by one of two general processes. In lymphatic disorders, obstructed or high-pressure intestinal lymphatics (from congenital lymphangiectasia or raised systemic venous pressure, as after Fontan surgery) leak protein-rich lymph into the lumen. In mucosal disorders, inflammation, ulceration, or increased epithelial permeability from infectious, allergic, inflammatory, or infiltrative disease allows serum proteins to escape across the damaged barrier. Because the loss is non-selective, albumin, immunoglobulins, and other proteins are depleted, producing hypoalbuminaemia, oedema, and sometimes immune compromise.","additionalDifferentials":["Nephrotic syndrome","Chronic liver disease with impaired synthesis","Malnutrition or inadequate protein intake","Malabsorption syndromes","Congestive heart failure","Sepsis or capillary leak states"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum albumin and total protein","Stool alpha-1-antitrypsin (clearance)","Serum immunoglobulins and lymphocyte count","Urinalysis to exclude renal protein loss","Endoscopy with biopsy when mucosal disease is suspected","Imaging or echocardiography to assess lymphatics and cardiac status"],"redFlags":["Severe hypoalbuminaemia with anasarca, ascites, or effusions","Signs of immunodeficiency or recurrent infection from immunoglobulin loss","Evidence of underlying serious cardiac or lymphatic disease","Growth failure","Features of an underlying inflammatory or malignant infiltrative process"],"prognosis":"Outlook depends heavily on the underlying cause; some mucosal causes resolve when the primary disease is treated, whereas lymphangiectasia and Fontan-associated protein loss can be chronic and difficult to manage.","pitfalls":["Attributing hypoalbuminaemia and oedema to renal or hepatic causes without considering the gut","Overlooking accompanying immunoglobulin loss and infection risk","Failing to identify and treat the specific underlying disorder","Neglecting nutritional support and the role of a low-fat, medium-chain triglyceride diet in lymphatic loss"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"protozoal_intestinal_infections_and_tricho":{"name":"Protozoal Intestinal Infections and Trichomoniasis","system":"Gastrointestinal / Genitourinary (infectious)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Giardiasis","Cryptosporidiosis","Cystoisosporiasis","Cyclosporiasis","Microsporidiosis","Balantidiasis","Blastocystosis","Dientamoebiasis","Sarcocystosis","Trichomonas vaginalis infection","Intestinal protozoa"],"chapter":236,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1804","clinicalPearls":["Giardia (G. duodenalis, assemblages A and B in humans) is a non-invasive parasite confined to the proximal small bowel that does not spread hematogenously; an inoculum of as few as ~10 cysts can establish infection, and cysts resist routine chlorination but are killed by boiling or filtration.","Giardiasis classically presents with upper-intestinal/small-bowel features (nausea, vomiting, bloating, belching, sulfurous flatus, abdominal pain) and non-bloody diarrhea; fever, blood, or mucus in stool point away from giardiasis toward colitis or a concomitant illness.","Cryptosporidium (C. hominis, C. parvum) is self-limited (about 1-2 weeks) in immunocompetent hosts but causes chronic, profuse watery diarrhea with stool volumes ranging from 1 to 25 L/day in AIDS patients with CD4 counts below 100/uL.","Cystoisospora belli is distinctive among enteric protozoa in that it can produce peripheral eosinophilia in immunocompetent hosts; in AIDS it mimics cryptosporidiosis with chronic profuse watery diarrhea.","Cyclospora cayetanensis is linked to foodborne outbreaks from imported produce (basil, sweet peas, raspberries) and waterborne spread, and characteristically causes prolonged diarrhea that may persist beyond one month; absence of fecal blood and leukocytes indicates the small-bowel mucosa is not destroyed.","Conventional ova-and-parasite (O+P) stool examination does NOT reliably detect Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, or microsporidia; specific tests (modified acid-fast stain, immunoassays, direct fluorescent antibody, or NAATs) must be requested explicitly.","Microsporidia are obligate intracellular spore-forming organisms that are small and gram-positive; they are most often opportunistic in AIDS, where Enterocytozoon bieneusi and Encephalitozoon intestinalis cause chronic diarrhea and wasting and have been found in 10-40% of patients with chronic diarrhea.","Encephalitozoon species can disseminate beyond the gut to cause keratoconjunctivitis, sinusitis, bronchiolitis/respiratory disease, cholangitis, nephritis, and cystitis, so microsporidiosis is not solely an intestinal disease.","Balantidium coli is a large ciliated protozoan producing a large-bowel disease resembling amebiasis (mucosal invasion, focal necrosis, ulceration); it is zoonotic from pigs and, unlike amebiasis, only rarely disseminates hematogenously to other organs.","Blastocystis hominis has uncertain pathogenicity; finding it in stool warrants a full search for other bacterial, viral, or protozoal causes of diarrhea before attributing symptoms to it.","Dientamoeba fragilis is unique among intestinal protozoa in having only a trophozoite stage and no cyst stage; immediate fixation of stool improves detection of the labile trophozoites and NAATs outperform microscopy.","Trichomonas vaginalis is one of the most prevalent protozoal parasites in the United States (an estimated 2-3 million infections per year); it is essentially always transmitted venereally, and most infected men are asymptomatic, serving as a reservoir for reinfection."],"pathophysiology":"Most of these protozoa are acquired by fecal-oral ingestion of environmentally resistant cysts or oocysts (Trichomonas is the exception, being venereally transmitted). Giardia trophozoites excyst in the small intestine, adhere to but do not invade the proximal small-bowel epithelium, and may cause enterocyte apoptosis, epithelial barrier dysfunction, and malabsorption/secretion; the precise mechanism of diarrhea is unknown, and consequent loss of brush-border enzyme activity produces lactose intolerance and, in some chronic cases, malabsorption with villous flattening resembling tropical sprue or celiac disease. Cryptosporidium sporozoites infect intestinal epithelial cells within an intracellular vacuole, completing asexual and sexual cycles to generate immediately infectious oocysts; the means of secretory diarrhea are uncertain, infection can be patchy in the small bowel, and biliary tract involvement may cause papillary stenosis, sclerosing cholangitis, or cholecystitis, especially in AIDS. Cystoisospora and Cyclospora similarly invade small-bowel epithelial cells (Cyclospora oocysts must mature in the environment before becoming infectious). Microsporidia inject sporoplasm into host cells via an extruded polar tubule and replicate by merogony and sporogony, releasing spores in stool, urine, respiratory secretions, or other fluids. Hypogammaglobulinemia predisposes to prolonged, severe, treatment-refractory giardiasis, indicating humoral immunity is important for control.","additionalDifferentials":["Tropical sprue (chronic giardiasis with villous flattening can resemble it)","Gluten-sensitive enteropathy / celiac disease (mimicked by chronic malabsorptive giardiasis)","Cystic fibrosis with superimposed enteric infection","Bacterial enteric infection (more acute, disabling course than giardiasis)","Lactose intolerance (may follow giardiasis from brush-border enzyme loss)","Amebiasis (large-bowel disease mimicked by balantidiasis)"],"infectionMimics":["Amebiasis (Entamoeba histolytica) versus balantidiasis in the colon","Other infectious vaginitis (bacterial vaginosis, vulvovaginal candidiasis) versus trichomonal vaginitis - clinical features do not reliably distinguish them","Cryptosporidiosis, cystoisosporiasis, cyclosporiasis, and microsporidiosis can clinically overlap as causes of chronic watery diarrhea in AIDS","Other bacterial, viral, or protozoal diarrhea when Blastocystis hominis is detected"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Celiac disease / gluten-sensitive enteropathy","Tropical sprue","Inflammatory bowel disease (for invasive colonic balantidiasis)","Functional/chronic diarrhea of uncertain cause"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool ova-and-parasite examination (detects Giardia and Balantidium but is insufficient for Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, microsporidia)","Fecal modified acid-fast staining (Cryptosporidium, Cystoisospora, Cyclospora oocysts)","Fecal antigen immunoassays (Giardia, Cryptosporidium)","Direct immunofluorescent antibody (Giardia, Cryptosporidium; and for Trichomonas, more sensitive at 70-90% than wet mount)","Nucleic acid amplification tests (NAATs) - highly sensitive across the protozoa and useful for microsporidial speciation","Modified trichrome or fluorescent stains of stool/urine/tissue for microsporidia; electron microscopy often required in tissue","Repeated stool sampling, duodenal fluid aspiration, and small-bowel biopsy when cyst/oocyst excretion is intermittent or low-level","Wet-mount microscopy of vaginal or prostatic secretions for motile trichomonads (sensitivity only ~50-60%); NAATs on urine or vaginal/endocervical swabs are first-line"],"redFlags":["CD4+ T-cell count below 100/uL with watery diarrhea (risk of severe, chronic, profuse cryptosporidiosis)","Very high stool output (up to 25 L/day) with marked fluid and electrolyte depletion in immunocompromised cryptosporidiosis","Significant weight loss, wasting, dehydration, or growth retardation in children with chronic giardiasis or cryptosporidiosis","Right-upper-quadrant or mid-epigastric pain suggesting biliary tract involvement (cryptosporidiosis or microsporidiosis: papillary stenosis, sclerosing cholangitis, cholecystitis)","Hypogammaglobulinemia presenting with prolonged, treatment-refractory giardiasis","Fever, blood, or mucus in stool during presumed giardiasis - suggests an alternative diagnosis or coinfection","Eosinophilia with enteric symptoms (suggests cystoisosporiasis among the enteric protozoa, or sarcocystosis-associated myositis)","Disseminated microsporidiosis (fever, sinusitis, cholangitis, bronchiolitis, keratoconjunctivitis, encephalitis) in AIDS"],"prognosis":"Outcomes depend heavily on immune status. Giardiasis ranges from asymptomatic carriage to fulminant diarrhea and malabsorption; most patients are cured, though some with chronic infection have delayed symptom resolution attributed to slow brush-border enzyme regeneration, and hypogammaglobulinemic or AIDS patients may have refractory disease. Cryptosporidiosis is self-limited (1-2 weeks) in immunocompetent hosts but can be chronic and severe in AIDS, where improved immune status from antiretroviral therapy can lead to resolution. Cystoisosporiasis and cyclosporiasis can be self-limited but tend to relapse and become chronic in HIV-infected patients. Microsporidiosis can be self-limited in the immunocompetent but contributes to chronic diarrhea and wasting in AIDS, and definitive therapy is not well established. Balantidiasis ranges from asymptomatic to fulminant dysentery; sarcocystosis and most Blastocystis or Dientamoeba infections are mild or self-limited.","pitfalls":["Relying on routine stool O+P examination, which misses Cryptosporidium, Cyclospora, and microsporidia - specific stains or NAATs must be ordered","Stopping at a single stool exam: cyst/oocyst excretion is variable and intermittent, so repeated sampling or duodenal aspiration/biopsy may be needed","Attributing symptoms to Blastocystis hominis without excluding other pathogens, given its uncertain pathogenicity","Missing Dientamoeba fragilis because of delayed fixation of stool, which degrades its labile trophozoites (it has no cyst stage)","Assuming routine water chlorination eliminates Giardia and Cryptosporidium - their cysts/oocysts resist standard chlorination; boiling or submicron filtration is needed","Diagnosing trichomonal vaginitis by wet mount alone, given its modest (~50-60%) sensitivity","Treating trichomoniasis without concurrently treating sexual partners, leading to apparent treatment failure that is actually reinfection (true high-level metronidazole resistance also occurs)"],"severityClassification":"No formal staging; severity is gauged clinically by host immune status (notably CD4 count below 100/uL marking risk of severe cryptosporidiosis), stool volume and degree of fluid/electrolyte loss (up to 25 L/day in severe cryptosporidiosis), presence of malabsorption/weight loss/wasting, dehydration, growth retardation in children, and biliary or disseminated extraintestinal involvement.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1804","Harrison 22e p.1805","Harrison 22e p.1806","Harrison 22e p.1807","Harrison 22e p.1808"],"crossLinks":[]},"psoriasis":{"name":"Psoriasis","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["psoriasis vulgaris","plaque psoriasis","chronic plaque psoriasis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic lesions are well-demarcated, salmon-pink plaques with adherent silvery scale, favouring extensor surfaces (elbows, knees), scalp, and the lumbosacral region","Gentle removal of scale may reveal pinpoint bleeding points (Auspitz sign), and new plaques can appear at sites of trauma (Koebner phenomenon)","Nail changes such as pitting, onycholysis, and oil-drop discolouration are common and support the diagnosis","Up to a third of patients develop psoriatic arthritis, so ask about joint pain, stiffness, and dactylitis at every review","Recognise clinical variants: guttate (often post-streptococcal in younger patients), inverse/flexural, pustular, and erythrodermic forms","Treatment is stepped: topical agents (corticosteroids, vitamin D analogues) for limited disease, phototherapy for widespread involvement, and systemic or biologic immunomodulators for severe or refractory disease","Screen for and address associated cardiometabolic risk, obesity, and depression, as psoriasis is a systemic inflammatory condition","Avoid abrupt withdrawal of systemic corticosteroids, which can precipitate a pustular or erythrodermic flare"],"pathophysiology":"Psoriasis is a chronic immune-mediated disorder driven by dysregulated activation of the innate and adaptive immune systems, particularly the interleukin-23/T-helper-17 axis and tumour necrosis factor signalling. Activated T cells and dendritic cells release cytokines that drive keratinocyte hyperproliferation and abnormal differentiation, dramatically shortening epidermal turnover time and producing thickened, scaly plaques. Dilated dermal vessels and a neutrophilic and lymphocytic infiltrate account for the erythema and, in pustular variants, sterile collections of neutrophils. A strong polygenic predisposition (including PSORS1/HLA-C associations) interacts with triggers such as infection, trauma, drugs, and stress.","additionalDifferentials":["Seborrhoeic dermatitis (especially on the scalp and face)","Chronic eczema or lichen simplex chronicus","Tinea corporis or tinea capitis","Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (mycosis fungoides)","Pityriasis rubra pilaris","Secondary syphilis in atypical guttate-like eruptions"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Largely a clinical diagnosis; skin biopsy reserved for atypical presentations","Throat swab or streptococcal serology when guttate psoriasis is suspected","Joint assessment and imaging when psoriatic arthritis is suspected","Baseline and monitoring bloods (including liver function and infection screening) before systemic or biologic therapy","Metabolic screening for cardiovascular risk factors, lipids, and glucose"],"redFlags":["Erythroderma with widespread redness, scaling, and impaired thermoregulation requiring urgent care","Generalised pustular psoriasis with fever and systemic upset","Rapidly progressive joint destruction or dactylitis suggesting aggressive psoriatic arthritis","Signs of secondary infection or sepsis in extensive skin breakdown"],"prognosis":"Psoriasis is a lifelong relapsing-remitting condition; most patients achieve good control with appropriate therapy, but disease severity fluctuates and associated arthritis and cardiometabolic disease influence long-term outcomes.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling scalp or flexural psoriasis as seborrhoeic dermatitis or fungal infection","Overlooking psoriatic arthritis until irreversible joint damage has occurred","Using potent topical steroids in flexures or on the face without appropriate caution","Precipitating pustular or erythrodermic flares by withdrawing systemic corticosteroids"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"psoriatic_arthritis":{"name":"Psoriatic arthritis","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PsA","arthritis psoriatica","psoriatic arthropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An inflammatory arthritis associated with psoriasis; skin disease usually precedes joint involvement but arthritis can appear first or without evident skin lesions","Presents in several patterns including asymmetric oligoarthritis, symmetric polyarthritis resembling rheumatoid arthritis, distal interphalangeal predominant disease, spondylitis, and the destructive arthritis mutilans","Dactylitis ('sausage digit') and enthesitis are hallmark features that help distinguish it from rheumatoid arthritis","Nail changes such as pitting and onycholysis correlate with distal interphalangeal joint involvement","Usually seronegative for rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies","Radiographs may show combined erosion and new bone formation, with the classic 'pencil-in-cup' deformity in advanced disease","Associated comorbidities include uveitis, inflammatory bowel disease, and increased cardiovascular and metabolic risk","Treatment is guided by domains involved (peripheral joints, axial disease, enthesitis, dactylitis, skin, nails), using NSAIDs, conventional DMARDs, and biologic or targeted synthetic agents (TNF, IL-17, IL-23, JAK inhibitors)"],"pathophysiology":"Psoriatic arthritis is a seronegative spondyloarthropathy resulting from immune-mediated inflammation in genetically predisposed individuals. Dysregulation of the TNF and IL-23/IL-17 axes drives inflammation at synovium, entheses, and skin. Unlike rheumatoid arthritis, both bone erosion and pathological new bone formation occur, producing the mixed destructive and proliferative changes seen radiographically. Enthesitis is considered a primary lesion, and there are associations with HLA-B27 (particularly axial disease) and other HLA alleles.","additionalDifferentials":["Rheumatoid arthritis","Ankylosing spondylitis and other spondyloarthritides","Reactive arthritis","Gout and other crystal arthropathies","Osteoarthritis, particularly of distal interphalangeal joints","Undifferentiated inflammatory arthritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Examination of skin and nails for psoriasis, pitting, and onycholysis","Rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies to help distinguish from rheumatoid arthritis","Radiographs of hands, feet, and axial skeleton for erosions, new bone, and pencil-in-cup change","Ultrasound or MRI to detect enthesitis, synovitis, and dactylitis","Inflammatory markers and metabolic/cardiovascular risk screening"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive, deforming or mutilating arthritis with joint destruction (arthritis mutilans)","Acute painful red eye indicating uveitis requiring ophthalmology review","Features suggesting axial disease with neurological compromise","Symptoms of undiagnosed inflammatory bowel disease","Extensive or erythrodermic/pustular skin disease indicating severe systemic involvement"],"prognosis":"Course ranges from mild intermittent arthritis to progressive erosive disease with joint destruction and disability. Early recognition and treat-to-target therapy improve outcomes; poor prognostic markers include polyarticular involvement, elevated inflammatory markers, early erosions, and dactylitis.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis when arthritis precedes or occurs without obvious skin psoriasis","Mislabelling it as rheumatoid arthritis and overlooking dactylitis, enthesitis, and nail disease","Failing to assess and treat all involved domains rather than joints alone","Neglecting cardiovascular, metabolic, and uveitis comorbidities","Assuming seronegativity excludes an inflammatory arthritis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"psychiatric_disorders":{"name":"Psychiatric Disorders","system":"Psychiatric / Behavioral (Neurologic Disorders)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Mental disorders","Anxiety disorders","Mood disorders","Depression","Bipolar disorder","Schizophrenia","Somatic symptom disorder","Eating disorders","Personality disorders"],"chapter":463,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3657","clinicalPearls":["Mental or substance use disorders affect roughly 30% of the U.S. population, yet only about one-third of those affected are currently in treatment; four of the ten leading causes of global disability are psychiatric in origin, and depression is the single largest contributor to disability worldwide.","Brief validated office instruments (PRIME-MD / Patient Health Questionnaire, SDDS-PC, the four-item PC-PTSD screen) take only minutes and map patient responses onto formal DSM criteria, helping primary care clinicians make the initial diagnosis.","Panic attacks reach peak intensity within about 10 minutes and usually resolve within an hour; up to ~75% of panic disorder patients eventually meet criteria for major depression, and panic is a frequent occult cause of chest pain with normal coronary angiograms and of unexplained syncope.","Agoraphobia and housebound behavior are often concealed out of embarrassment, so the syndrome is missed unless the clinician asks directly; about one-third of patients presenting with anxiety have an underlying medical etiology.","Major depression requires depressed mood or anhedonia plus a total of at least 5 of 9 symptoms for a minimum of 2 weeks; key distinguishing features from grief are pervasive worthlessness/self-loathing and persistent (rather than wave-like) dysphoria.","Suicide risk must be probed by direct, empathic questioning: about 4-5% of depressed patients die by suicide, and most have seen a physician within the month before death; coexisting anxiety, panic, or agitation raises near-term risk.","Recognizing a bipolar diathesis in a patient presenting with depression is essential because antidepressant monotherapy can worsen symptoms and accelerate cycling; clues include childhood onset, antidepressant treatment failure, atypical features (hypersomnia, weight gain), and marked irritability/impulsivity.","Anorexia nervosa carries the highest mortality of any psychiatric disorder (~5.1 per 1000 untreated); characteristic lab and exam findings include leukopenia with relative lymphocytosis, raised BUN, metabolic alkalosis and hypokalemia with purging, amenorrhea, lanugo, hypotension, hypothermia, and sinus bradycardia.","Bulimia nervosa usually occurs at normal or slightly above-normal weight, and physical exam is often unremarkable except for dental enamel erosion and parotid gland enlargement; medical danger stems from purging-related fluid/electrolyte disturbance and cardiac conduction abnormalities.","Schizophrenia requires at least two characteristic symptoms (positive and/or negative) over a 1-month period with continuous signs for at least 6 months; about 10% of patients die by suicide, and negative symptoms predominate in roughly one-third and predict poor drug response.","Personality change can be the first manifestation of serious neurologic, endocrine, or other medical disease (e.g., a frontal-lobe tumor presenting with altered motivation while the neurologic exam stays normal), so a personality disorder is a diagnosis that requires exclusion of such causes.","In somatic symptom disorder the defining feature is the patient's disproportionate interpretation and preoccupation with symptoms, not the absence of a medical explanation; patients with genuine medical disease can still qualify, and repeated tests and exploratory surgery are counterproductive."],"pathophysiology":"Psychiatric disorders are conceptualized as CNS disorders arising from interacting genetic, developmental, and environmental factors, with substantial heritability and considerable overlap of genetic risk variants across diagnoses (pleiotropy). Anxiety and panic involve noradrenergic discharge from the locus coeruleus and serotonergic dorsal-raphe pathways, with lactate, yohimbine, CCK-4, and CO2 capable of provoking attacks; generalized anxiety implicates the GABA-A receptor/chloride-channel complex and serotonergic modulation. PTSD is associated with excessive locus coeruleus norepinephrine release facilitating fear-memory encoding, insular threat-detection overactivity, and blunted pituitary-adrenal responses. OCD involves orbitofrontal cortex-caudate-globus pallidus circuitry. Major depression shows elevated cortisol and CRH, dexamethasone non-suppression, blunted TSH response to TRH, shortened REM latency with increased REM density and reduced slow-wave sleep, altered proinflammatory cytokines and neurotrophins, oxidative stress, telomere shortening, and possible gut-microbiome changes; delayed therapeutic action of antidepressants implicates adaptive second-messenger and neurotrophic changes. Bipolar disorder shows very high monozygotic concordance (approaching 80%) with risk genes (CACNA1C, ANK3, NCAN, ODZ4, TRANK1) shared with schizophrenia and autism, plus circadian and calcium dysregulation. Schizophrenia involves dopamine D2/D3 signaling in the ventral striatum, many small-effect GWAS loci and rare copy-number variants, and pathways of immunity, inflammation, and cell signaling.","additionalDifferentials":["Major depression vs bipolar depression vs depression secondary to general medical illness or substance use","Major depressive episode vs normal bereavement/grief (grief features emptiness and wave-like pangs with preserved self-esteem)","Generalized anxiety disorder vs panic disorder (they segregate independently in families)","Schizophrenia vs schizophreniform disorder (shorter duration), schizoaffective disorder, delusional disorder, and brief psychotic disorder","Somatic symptom disorder vs illness anxiety disorder vs conversion (functional neurologic symptom) disorder vs factitious disorder vs malingering","Anorexia nervosa vs avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder (the latter lacks fear of weight gain and body-image distortion)"],"infectionMimics":["HIV-associated depression and CNS disease (lifetime depression prevalence in HIV-positive individuals ~22-45%)","Chronic hepatitis C-associated depression, which may worsen with interferon-alpha therapy","AIDS as a cause of secondary mania"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Pheochromocytoma, thyrotoxicosis, and hypoglycemia presenting as anxiety/panic","Hyperthyroidism, Huntington's disease, Wilson's disease, frontotemporal dementia, and cerebrovascular accidents presenting as secondary mania","Hypothyroidism (including subclinical) and hyperthyroidism presenting with depressed mood and cognitive/memory impairment","Frontal-lobe tumor presenting as personality change with a normal neurologic exam","Depressive pseudodementia in the elderly mimicking neurodegenerative dementia","Cardiovascular conditions (paroxysmal atrial tachycardia, mitral valve prolapse) detected on ECG/echocardiography in patients labeled with panic","Drug- or medication-induced psychosis (parkinsonian agents, clonidine, quinacrine, procaine derivatives) mimicking schizophrenia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Targeted history, physical exam, and focused labs to exclude medical mimics of anxiety (e.g., thyroid studies, glucose, catecholamine workup as indicated)","ECG and echocardiography when a cardiovascular cause of panic-like symptoms is suspected","Thyroid function testing in mood disorders and in rapid-cycling bipolar disorder (lithium has antithyroid effects)","TCA plasma levels to guide dosing and interpret apparent treatment resistance or toxicity","Lithium blood levels (target ~0.8-1.2 meq/L acutely) given the narrow therapeutic-to-toxic window; valproate level 50-125 ug/mL; carbamazepine 4-12 ug/mL","In anorexia nervosa: CBC (leukopenia/relative lymphocytosis), electrolytes (hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis with purging), BUN, and bone-density assessment for osteoporosis"],"redFlags":["Suicidal or homicidal ideation, a specific plan, or prior attempts - warrants immediate referral to a mental health specialist","Psychotic symptoms, mania, or severe depression/anxiety failing first-line treatment - indications for psychiatric consultation or transfer of care","Coexisting anxiety, panic, or agitation in a depressed patient, which raises near-term suicide risk","Critical weight loss, hypotension, hypothermia, bradycardia, or electrolyte/cardiac conduction abnormalities in eating disorders","Refeeding syndrome risk when restoring weight too rapidly in anorexia nervosa (target gain ~0.5-1 lb/week)","Serotonin syndrome (myoclonus, agitation, abdominal cramping, hyperpyrexia, hypertension) - a contraindication to combining MAOIs with SSRIs","Hypertensive crisis from tyramine-containing foods or sympathomimetics in patients taking MAOIs","New-onset psychosis requiring exclusion of a drug/medication cause before diagnosing schizophrenia"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary widely by disorder. Panic disorder responds to treatment in 75-85% of patients, though complete remission is somewhat lower. Most depressed patients (60-70%) respond to an adequately dosed antidepressant over 6-8 weeks, and about three-quarters improve by ~2 months; the best predictor of recurrence is the number of prior episodes (50-60% of first-episode patients have at least one or two recurrences). Bipolar disorder is recurrent and requires maintenance therapy; lithium responses are 70-80% in acute mania and a sustained level >=0.8 meq/L reduces suicide risk. About 4-5% of depressed and ~10% of schizophrenic patients die by suicide. Anorexia nervosa has the highest mortality of any psychiatric condition (~5.1/1000 untreated), though most patients achieve remission within 5 years; binge-eating disorder has the most favorable prognosis among eating disorders. Schizophrenia often has a poor outcome with insidious onset, but positive symptoms tend to attenuate with age and permanent remission occasionally occurs.","pitfalls":["Basing psychiatric referral on the mere absence of a physical explanation rather than on positive signs/symptoms of a mental disorder","Failing to ask directly about agoraphobia, OCD behaviors, or suicidal ideation, all of which patients commonly conceal","Subjecting somatic symptom disorder patients to repeated tests and exploratory surgery, or confronting them in a way that causes humiliation and treatment abandonment","Treating an unrecognized bipolar depression with antidepressant monotherapy, risking induction of mania or cycle acceleration","Prescribing benzodiazepines beyond 4-6 weeks (tolerance, abuse, dependence) and not anticipating withdrawal, which can include seizures","Giving benzodiazepines or SSRIs in the immediate aftermath of trauma, and using debriefing, which does not prevent PTSD and may worsen symptoms","Using TCAs in patients with serious cardiovascular risk or bundle branch block, and providing large supplies to suicidal patients (TCA overdose can be lethal, desipramine highest risk)","Overlooking lithium's narrow therapeutic index and its antithyroid/renal effects during maintenance monitoring"],"severityClassification":"Diagnoses are framed by DSM-5-TR (with ICD-10-CM used more widely worldwide); alternative research frameworks include NIMH RDoC and the dimensional HiTOP taxonomy, none yet adequately validated. Severity and subtyping markers within disorders include: bipolar I (full manic episode >=1 week) vs bipolar II (hypomania >=4 days plus recurrent depression) vs cyclothymia (>=2 years of subthreshold fluctuations); rapid cycling defined as >=4 mood episodes per year; persistent depressive (dysthymic) disorder as chronic symptoms >=2 years; PTSD specifiers (with dissociative symptoms, with delayed expression >=6 months); anorexia nervosa restricting type vs binge-eating/purging type; and Munchausen's syndrome as the most severe form of factitious disorder.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3657","Harrison 22e p.3658","Harrison 22e p.3661","Harrison 22e p.3664","Harrison 22e p.3667","Harrison 22e p.3670","Harrison 22e p.3671","Harrison 22e p.3672"],"crossLinks":[]},"pterygium":{"name":"Pterygium","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["surfer's eye","pterygia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A pterygium is a wing-shaped fibrovascular growth of conjunctival tissue that encroaches onto the cornea, most commonly on the nasal side","Strongly associated with chronic exposure to ultraviolet light, wind, and dust, and therefore more common in people living in sunny, outdoor environments near the equator","It is distinguished from a pinguecula, which is a similar degenerative conjunctival lesion that does not cross onto the cornea","Symptoms range from a cosmetic concern and irritation or redness to visual disturbance when it induces astigmatism or approaches the visual axis","A leading edge (Stocker's line of iron deposition) and a fibrovascular head advancing over the cornea are characteristic","Management is conservative with lubricants and UV protection for mild lesions; surgical excision is indicated for visual axis threat, marked astigmatism, discomfort, or cosmesis","Recurrence after excision is common, and adjuncts such as conjunctival autografting reduce this risk compared with bare-scleral excision","Atypical or rapidly growing lesions should be evaluated to exclude ocular surface squamous neoplasia"],"pathophysiology":"A pterygium is a degenerative and proliferative condition of the bulbar conjunctiva in which chronic ultraviolet and environmental exposure causes elastotic degeneration of subepithelial collagen along with fibrovascular proliferation. The abnormal tissue invades the superficial cornea, disrupting Bowman's layer and the corneal architecture at its advancing head. Progressive growth can flatten the cornea and induce astigmatism, and encroachment onto the visual axis reduces vision.","additionalDifferentials":["Pinguecula","Pseudopterygium (from prior injury or inflammation)","Ocular surface squamous neoplasia (conjunctival intraepithelial neoplasia)","Conjunctival naevus or other pigmented lesion","Limbal dermoid"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination to document size, vascularity, and encroachment onto the cornea","Refraction and corneal topography to assess induced astigmatism","Serial photography to monitor progression","Excision biopsy with histology when ocular surface neoplasia is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapid growth, marked thickening, or leukoplakic change suggesting ocular surface squamous neoplasia","Encroachment onto or across the visual axis threatening vision","Significant induced astigmatism degrading acuity","Restricted ocular motility from a large or recurrent lesion with scarring"],"prognosis":"Most pterygia are benign and slowly progressive with a good prognosis; surgery is effective for symptomatic or vision-threatening lesions, though recurrence is a recognised problem, particularly with bare-scleral techniques and in younger patients.","pitfalls":["Assuming all fleshy conjunctival lesions are benign pterygia and missing ocular surface neoplasia","Operating too early on quiescent lesions given the risk of recurrence","Using bare-scleral excision alone, which has a high recurrence rate compared with grafting","Neglecting UV protection advice, allowing progression or recurrence"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"ptosis":{"name":"Ptosis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["blepharoptosis","drooping eyelid"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Ptosis is abnormal drooping of the upper eyelid; severity is gauged by the margin-reflex distance and the effect on the visual axis","Assessing levator function is central to classification and surgical planning","Aponeurotic (involutional) ptosis is the commonest, showing good levator function, a high skin crease, and often thinning of the eyelid","A ptosis with a small pupil and ipsilateral anhidrosis suggests Horner syndrome; a ptosis with a dilated pupil and an eye deviated down and out suggests a third nerve palsy","Variable, fatigable ptosis worsening through the day points to myasthenia gravis and warrants systemic evaluation","Congenital ptosis usually reflects levator dystrophy with poor function and risks amblyopia if the visual axis is occluded in a child","The rare Marcus Gunn jaw-winking phenomenon links lid elevation to jaw movement"],"pathophysiology":"Ptosis results from impaired elevation of the upper lid, which is normally raised by the levator palpebrae superioris (oculomotor nerve) and Muller muscle (sympathetic). Aponeurotic ptosis is caused by dehiscence or stretching of the levator aponeurosis with age. Neurogenic ptosis follows interruption of the nerve supply, as in third nerve palsy or sympathetic denervation (Horner syndrome). Myogenic ptosis reflects muscle disease such as myasthenia gravis, chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia, or myotonic dystrophy. Mechanical ptosis arises from lid mass or scarring, and congenital ptosis from maldevelopment of the levator muscle.","additionalDifferentials":["Aponeurotic (involutional) ptosis","Third nerve palsy","Horner syndrome","Myasthenia gravis and other myogenic causes","Mechanical ptosis from lid mass or oedema","Pseudoptosis from dermatochalasis, brow ptosis, or contralateral lid retraction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Measurement of margin-reflex distance, palpebral aperture, levator function, and skin crease position","Pupil examination to identify Horner syndrome or third nerve palsy","Assessment for fatigability and ice-pack or targeted testing when myasthenia is suspected","Neuroimaging when a compressive or neurogenic cause is suspected","Systemic and antibody testing for suspected myasthenia gravis"],"redFlags":["Painful ptosis with a dilated pupil and ophthalmoplegia suggesting a compressive third nerve palsy (possible aneurysm) requiring urgent imaging","Ptosis with anisocoria and anhidrosis (Horner syndrome), which may indicate serious underlying pathology such as an apical lung lesion or carotid dissection","Fatigable or variable ptosis with diplopia or bulbar symptoms suggesting myasthenia gravis","Ptosis occluding the visual axis in a child, threatening amblyopia"],"prognosis":"Aponeurotic and congenital ptosis generally respond well to surgery; the outlook for neurogenic and myogenic ptosis depends on the underlying cause, and childhood ptosis carries a risk of amblyopia if not managed promptly.","pitfalls":["Treating ptosis as purely cosmetic and missing a serious neurological cause such as third nerve palsy or Horner syndrome","Failing to test for fatigability and overlooking myasthenia gravis","Not recognising amblyopia risk in congenital ptosis","Mistaking pseudoptosis from brow droop or dermatochalasis for true ptosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"ptsd":{"name":"Post-traumatic stress disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PTSD","post traumatic stress disorder","posttraumatic stress disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Develops after exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence, whether directly experienced, witnessed, or learned about affecting a close other","Core symptom clusters are intrusion (flashbacks, nightmares, distressing memories), avoidance of trauma reminders, negative alterations in mood and cognition, and heightened arousal and reactivity","Symptoms persist beyond about one month and cause significant distress or impairment; an acute stress reaction is the label used within the first month","Hyperarousal features include hypervigilance, exaggerated startle, irritability, sleep disturbance, and concentration problems","Trauma-focused psychotherapies (such as trauma-focused CBT and eye movement desensitisation and reprocessing) are first-line treatments","Pharmacotherapy principles favour SSRIs or SNRIs; prazosin may help trauma-related nightmares, and benzodiazepines are generally avoided","Screen for common comorbidities including depression, substance use, and suicidal ideation, and for traumatic brain injury in relevant populations","Dissociative symptoms and delayed onset (months to years after the event) are recognised presentations"],"pathophysiology":"Post-traumatic stress disorder reflects dysregulated fear processing, with exaggerated amygdala responses, impaired prefrontal inhibition, and hippocampal changes affecting contextual memory, alongside altered hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis and noradrenergic stress responses. These changes underpin persistent conditioned fear, impaired extinction of trauma memories, and chronic hyperarousal.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute stress disorder","Adjustment disorder","Major depressive disorder","Panic disorder and other anxiety disorders","Obsessive-compulsive disorder","Substance use disorder","Psychosis when flashbacks are mistaken for hallucinations","Traumatic brain injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured trauma history and PTSD symptom rating scales","Suicide risk assessment","Screening for comorbid depression and substance use","Assessment for traumatic brain injury where relevant","Evaluation of sleep disturbance and nightmares"],"redFlags":["Suicidal ideation or self-harm","Severe dissociative episodes with risk to safety","Escalating substance or alcohol use to cope with symptoms","Aggression or risk to others during flashbacks or heightened arousal"],"prognosis":"Many people recover within the first year, especially with early trauma-focused treatment, but a substantial minority develop a chronic course. Prognosis is worse with severe or prolonged trauma, lack of social support, and comorbid depression or substance use.","pitfalls":["Using benzodiazepines, which do not treat core symptoms and may impair recovery","Missing comorbid depression, substance use, and suicide risk","Overlooking delayed-onset presentations by not taking a trauma history","Mislabelling normal early stress reactions as PTSD before the required duration","Failing to refer for evidence-based trauma-focused psychotherapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_fibrosis":{"name":"Pulmonary fibrosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IPF","idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","interstitial lung disease","fibrosing ILD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with progressive exertional breathlessness and a persistent dry cough, often over months to years","Fine bibasal end-inspiratory crackles (often described as Velcro-like) and finger clubbing are characteristic examination findings","High-resolution CT showing a usual interstitial pneumonia pattern, with subpleural and basal reticulation, traction bronchiectasis, and honeycombing, is central to diagnosis","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is a diagnosis of exclusion requiring careful evaluation for connective tissue disease, drug toxicity, and environmental or occupational exposures","Pulmonary function tests typically show a restrictive pattern with reduced lung volumes and impaired gas transfer (low DLCO)","Multidisciplinary discussion combining clinical, radiological, and where needed histological data improves diagnostic accuracy","Antifibrotic therapy can slow the decline in lung function in IPF; supportive measures include oxygen, pulmonary rehabilitation, and vaccination","Acute exacerbations cause rapid deterioration and carry high mortality; lung transplantation should be considered in suitable candidates"],"pathophysiology":"Repeated microinjury to the alveolar epithelium in a susceptible (often ageing, genetically predisposed) individual triggers aberrant wound-healing rather than normal repair. Activated fibroblasts and myofibroblasts accumulate in fibroblastic foci and deposit excess extracellular matrix, progressively distorting and stiffening the lung architecture. The result is loss of functional alveolar units, honeycomb cystic change, impaired gas exchange, and reduced compliance, producing a restrictive physiology with worsening hypoxaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Connective tissue disease-associated interstitial lung disease","Chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Pneumoconioses such as asbestosis or silicosis","Drug-induced or radiation-induced lung injury","Sarcoidosis with fibrosis","Chronic left heart failure with interstitial oedema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-resolution CT of the chest to assess for a UIP pattern and distribution of fibrosis","Full pulmonary function testing including spirometry, lung volumes, and diffusing capacity","Serological screen for connective tissue disease and, where indicated, hypersensitivity precipitins","Six-minute walk test with oximetry to assess functional impairment and desaturation","Surgical or transbronchial lung biopsy in selected cases where imaging is non-diagnostic"],"redFlags":["Rapid worsening of dyspnoea with new diffuse ground-glass change suggesting an acute exacerbation","Resting hypoxaemia or significant desaturation on exertion","Signs of pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure","Features suggesting an underlying treatable systemic disease that alter management"],"prognosis":"Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is generally progressive with a poor prognosis and median survival of only a few years from diagnosis, though the course is variable; antifibrotic drugs slow decline and transplantation can prolong survival in eligible patients, while acute exacerbations markedly worsen outcome.","pitfalls":["Labelling fibrosis as idiopathic without excluding occupational, drug, and connective tissue causes","Using corticosteroids or immunosuppression indiscriminately in IPF, where they may be harmful","Missing coexisting emphysema, which can mask spirometric restriction","Delaying transplant referral until the patient is too deconditioned to be a candidate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulmonary_hypertension":{"name":"Pulmonary Hypertension","system":"Cardiology / Pulmonary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["PH","PAH","pulmonary arterial hypertension"],"chapter":294,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2185","clinicalPearls":["PH is diagnosed when mean pulmonary artery pressure (mPAP) is >20 mmHg by right heart catheterisation; this threshold was lowered from the older >=25 mmHg cut-off to emphasise earlier disease detection (p.2185, 2191).","The most common causes of PH are left heart disease or primary lung disease; pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) is an uncommon but distinct subtype with an obliterative arteriopathy (p.2185).","Most patients present with dyspnoea and/or fatigue; oedema, chest pain, presyncope and syncope are less common and signal more advanced disease (p.2188).","The current WSPH classification (last revised 2018, Sixth World Symposium) defines five groups: 1 = PAH; 2 = PH due to left heart disease; 3 = PH due to chronic lung disease/hypoxia or sleep-disordered breathing; 4 = chronic thromboembolic PH (CTEPH); 5 = miscellaneous/multifactorial (p.2191).","Echocardiography with agitated-saline (bubble) study is the most important initial screening test; estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure >35 mmHg, a notched continuous-wave Doppler waveform, or a hypertrophied/dilated right ventricle supports the diagnosis (p.2188).","Hemodynamic subtypes by RHC: precapillary PH = PAWP/LVEDP <=15 mmHg with PVR >2.0 Wood units; isolated postcapillary PH = PAWP >15 mmHg with PVR <=2.0 WU; combined pre-/postcapillary PH = mPAP >20 mmHg with PAWP >15 mmHg and PVR >2.0 WU (p.2191).","In PAH, an isolated reduction in diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide (DLCO) on pulmonary function testing is a classic finding (p.2189).","BNP and NT-proBNP correlate with right ventricular dysfunction, hemodynamic severity and functional status in PAH, and NT-proBNP can serve as a biomarker of treatment response once a baseline is established (p.2191).","Vasoreactivity testing (with short-acting agents such as inhaled nitric oxide or inhaled epoprostenol) is reserved for idiopathic or hereditary PAH; a positive response is a fall in mPAP by >=10 mmHg to an absolute level <=40 mmHg without a fall in cardiac output, seen in <5% of patients but carrying a particularly favourable prognosis (p.2191).","CTEPH is curable in many patients by surgical pulmonary endarterectomy but is widely underdiagnosed; ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) scanning remains the primary screening test and should be considered in any PH of unclear cause (p.2190-2191)."],"pathophysiology":"PAH arises from apoptosis resistance, cell proliferation, dysregulated metabolism and increased oxidant stress in pulmonary vascular cells, pericytes and adventitial fibroblasts, producing hypertrophic, fibrotic and plexogenic remodelling of distal (small) pulmonary arterioles with extracellular-matrix expansion, reduced vascular compliance and in-situ thrombosis. This raises pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) and mean pulmonary artery pressure (mPAP). To preserve cardiac output against the elevated right-ventricular afterload, RV work increases; sustained overload depletes myocardial energy and causes right ventricular-pulmonary arterial uncoupling, and in end-stage disease cardiac output and mPAP both fall. The most common germline driver of hereditary PAH is a BMPR2 variant (a TGF-beta superfamily receptor) with variable penetrance (as low as ~20%); other drivers include TBX4 and SOX17, and EIF2AK4 for pulmonary veno-occlusive disease. In group 2 PH the hallmark is elevated left atrial pressure causing pulmonary venous hypertension, venule sclerosis and secondary arteriolar remodelling. (p.2185-2192)","additionalDifferentials":["Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF)","Left ventricular systolic dysfunction","Mitral or aortic valvular disease","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) / emphysema","Interstitial / restrictive lung disease","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH)","Connective tissue disease, especially systemic sclerosis-associated PAH","Portopulmonary hypertension","HIV-associated PAH","Congenital heart disease with intracardiac shunt / Eisenmenger's syndrome","Hepatopulmonary syndrome (distinct: causes intrapulmonary shunting, not pulmonary vascular remodelling)"],"infectionMimics":["HIV infection (HIV-PAH is an important cause of mortality in HIV-infected patients)","Schistosomiasis (affects >230 million worldwide, ~5% develop PAH; among the most common causes of PAH globally)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Asthma (overlapping nonspecific dyspnoea)","Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction","Physical deconditioning","Obstructive sleep apnoea / obesity-hypoventilation syndrome","Sarcoidosis with pulmonary vascular involvement"],"additionalInvestigations":["Right heart catheterisation (RHC) - gold standard for diagnosis and severity assessment; required mPAP >20 mmHg","Transthoracic echocardiography with agitated-saline (bubble) study - most important initial screening test","Electrocardiography - may show right ventricular hypertrophy or strain in advanced disease","Chest roentgenography - enlargement of central pulmonary arteries and obliteration of the retrosternal space","Pulmonary function testing including DLCO (isolated low DLCO classic for PAH)","High-resolution CT of the chest (vascular pruning, distinguishing ILD/emphysema/overlap; signs of venous congestion)","Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) scan - primary screen for CTEPH","CT pulmonary angiography and digital subtraction pulmonary angiography (definitive for CTEPH; shows webbing, stricture, tapering)","6-minute walk distance (6-MWD) for functional capacity and prognosis","Cardiopulmonary exercise testing (CPET); peak VO2 <15 mL/kg/min is prognostic but not diagnostic","Nocturnal oximetry screening in all patients (nocturnal desaturation common even without sleep-disordered breathing)","BNP / NT-proBNP","Serology: HIV test (when indicated), antinuclear antibodies, rheumatoid factor, anti-Scl-70; liver function and hepatitis serology; methamphetamine screening where relevant","Vasoreactivity testing (idiopathic/hereditary PAH only)"],"redFlags":["Syncope or presyncope (signals advanced disease)","Signs of right ventricular failure: elevated jugular venous pressure, lower-extremity oedema, ascites","Rapid progression of symptoms with severely reduced exercise capacity","Decompensated right heart failure (the major cause of mortality if untreated)","Significant decline in exercise tolerance from baseline even when CPET appears reassuring (e.g. in athletes - may indicate early PAH or subacute PE)"],"prognosis":"If left untreated, PH carries a high mortality rate, largely owing to decompensated right heart failure. Idiopathic PAH is progressive and leads to right heart failure and early mortality; in left-sided heart failure or HFpEF even mildly elevated mPAP carries adverse outcome. Among IPAH, prevalence favours women ~3:1 but hemodynamics are more severe and prognosis less favourable in men. Hereditary BMPR2 PAH presents younger with more severe hemodynamics and greater clinical risk (conversion ~2.3%/year in carriers). Vasoreactive responders (<5%) have a particularly favourable prognosis. Scleroderma-associated PAH and HIV-PAH carry especially poor prognoses; portopulmonary hypertension is a marker of adverse post-liver-transplant outcome (up to 100% mortality reported with mPAP >=50 mmHg in one study). Peak VO2 <15 mL/kg/min is prognostic. (p.2185, 2191-2192)","pitfalls":["Right heart catheterisation - not echocardiography - is the diagnostic gold standard; echocardiography only screens, and an echo in which tricuspid regurgitation is simply not detected does NOT exclude PH (PH is found in one-third of such patients).","PH symptoms are nonspecific, insidious and overlap with asthma, HFpEF and deconditioning, so the diagnosis is frequently missed without a reasonable index of suspicion.","Do not assume that PH in patients with comorbid cardiopulmonary disease (e.g. ILD, mitral valve disease) is merely an extension of the underlying condition rather than a specific entity.","Patients with left heart disease aggressively diuresed before RHC may falsely appear to have an isolated precapillary (PAH-like) profile - diuresis can mask the true hemodynamic classification.","A low peak VO2 occurs across the heart-lung disease spectrum and is NOT diagnostic of PH per se.","Chronic thromboembolic disease (thrombotic remodelling with reduced exercise capacity but without PH) requires consideration in patients with prior PE and dyspnoea even when echocardiography is reassuring."],"severityClassification":"World Symposium on Pulmonary Hypertension (WSPH) clinical classification (revised 2018) into 5 groups: Group 1 - pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH: idiopathic, heritable, drug/toxin-associated, or associated with connective tissue disease, HIV, portal hypertension, congenital heart disease, schistosomiasis; plus PVOD/PCH and persistent PH of the newborn); Group 2 - PH associated with left heart disease (HFpEF, HFrEF, valvular, congenital/acquired postcapillary); Group 3 - PH associated with lung disease and/or hypoxia (obstructive, restrictive, mixed, hypoventilation, high-altitude hypoxia, developmental); Group 4 - PH associated with pulmonary artery obstruction (CTEPH and other obstructions); Group 5 - PH with unclear/multifactorial mechanisms (haematological, systemic, metabolic, chronic renal failure +/- haemodialysis, pulmonary tumour thrombotic microangiopathy, fibrosing mediastinitis). Hemodynamic categorisation by RHC: precapillary, isolated postcapillary, and combined pre-/postcapillary PH. (Table 294-1, p.2191)","references":["Harrison 22e p.2185"],"crossLinks":["heart_failure","pe","ild","copd_exac_ni","systemic_sclerosis"]},"pulmonic_valve_disease":{"name":"Pulmonic Valve Disease","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Pulmonary valve disease","Pulmonic stenosis","Pulmonic regurgitation","PS","PR"],"chapter":278,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2063","clinicalPearls":["Pulmonic stenosis is overwhelmingly a congenital lesion; the valve is typically domed in isolated cases, and the pulmonic valve is only very rarely affected by the rheumatic process.","Dysplastic pulmonic valves occur as a feature of Noonan syndrome (mapped to chromosome 12), with PTPN1 gene mutations identified in roughly half of Noonan cases.","The pulmonic ejection sound (click) is the single right-sided acoustic event that DECREASES in intensity with inspiration, because elevated RV end-diastolic pressure pre-opens the valve.","As pulmonic stenosis worsens, the ejection sound migrates toward S1 and eventually becomes inaudible, the systolic murmur peaks later and may extend past A2, and P2 becomes reduced or absent.","The systolic murmur of pulmonic stenosis is mid-systolic and crescendo-decrescendo, best heard in the left second interspace, and it increases in intensity during inspiration.","The Graham Steell murmur of pulmonic regurgitation is a high-pitched, decrescendo diastolic murmur along the left sternal border that can be mistaken for aortic regurgitation; it tends to grow louder with inspiration.","In survivors of childhood tetralogy of Fallot or PS/pulmonary atresia repair, an RV-PA conduit lacking a valve can be freely regurgitant, producing a misleadingly low-pitched, short diastolic murmur despite significant PR and RV volume overload.","Right ventricular dysfunction from afterload mismatch appears earlier and at lower peak systolic pressures in pulmonic stenosis than left ventricular failure does in aortic stenosis, because the RV tolerates pressure overload poorly.","Cardiac output is maintained until late in pulmonic stenosis, so many patients with mild or even moderate disease are asymptomatic and are detected only by an incidental murmur or click.","A reduction in RV ejection fraction may be an early sign of hemodynamic compromise in pulmonic regurgitation, before overt symptoms develop.","Trace or mild pulmonic regurgitation of no hemodynamic significance is frequently seen on transthoracic echocardiography even when the valve is structurally normal.","Carcinoid characteristically produces mixed pulmonic valve disease, combining both regurgitation and stenosis."],"pathophysiology":"Pulmonic stenosis is defined hemodynamically by a systolic pressure gradient between the right ventricle and the main pulmonary artery. Sustained obstruction to RV outflow drives RV hypertrophy and prolongs systolic ejection; because the RV adapts poorly to pressure overload, dysfunction from afterload mismatch develops earlier and at lower pressures than analogous left-sided changes in aortic stenosis. The right atrial a wave rises in proportion to the higher pressures needed to fill a stiff, hypertrophied RV, and a prominent RA v wave reflects functional tricuspid regurgitation from RV and annular dilation; cardiac output is preserved until late. Pulmonic stenosis rarely progresses when mild but can worsen with age in moderate disease owing to valve thickening and calcification. In pulmonic regurgitation, severe disease causes RV chamber enlargement and eccentric hypertrophy, creating a state of increased preload and afterload. The diastolic pressure gradient from pulmonary artery to RV that drives the regurgitant flow falls progressively through diastole, explaining the decrescendo murmur, and the murmur shortens as RV diastolic pressure rises. Forward output is preserved early but fails to augment normally with exercise and declines over time; advanced stages show marked RV and RA enlargement with elevated jugular venous pressure. PR arises from primary valve pathology, annular enlargement (e.g., long-standing severe pulmonary hypertension dilating the valve ring), or their combination, and may follow surgical relief of RV outflow obstruction or balloon valvotomy.","additionalDifferentials":["Aortic regurgitation (the Graham Steell murmur of PR can mimic the AR murmur)","Other causes of a midsystolic murmur such as aortic stenosis, hypertrophic obstructive physiology, or an atrial septal defect with enhanced pulmonic flow","Pulmonary hypertension as the dominant clinical problem rather than the valve lesion itself"],"infectionMimics":["Infective endocarditis of the pulmonic valve (an etiology of both PS from bulky vegetations and PR)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Carcinoid heart disease causing mixed pulmonic valve lesions","Obstructing tumors producing RV outflow obstruction that mimics valvular PS","Idiopathic dilation of the pulmonary artery / annulus causing functional PR","Marfan syndrome-related annular enlargement"],"additionalInvestigations":["ECG: right axis deviation, RV hypertrophy, and right atrial enlargement in severe PS; RVH/RA enlargement may appear in PR depending on etiology and severity","Chest x-ray: poststenotic dilation of the main pulmonary artery on the frontal view, retrosternal airspace filling from RV enlargement on the lateral view, and apex lifted off the left hemidiaphragm in some patients","Transthoracic echocardiography (Doppler): definitive diagnosis in most cases, with jet velocity, gradient, RV function, pulmonary artery pressures (which should be low in PS), and associated lesions; RV systolic pressure estimated from the tricuspid regurgitant jet","Transesophageal echocardiography: better delineation of the RV outflow tract and infundibular hypertrophy when needed","Cardiac MR: greater anatomic detail and precise RV volume/function assessment, especially in repaired congenital heart disease","Cardiac catheterization: not routinely required; if performed in PS, pressures are taken just below and above the valve with attention to a possible dynamic gradient"],"redFlags":["Exertional dyspnea or early-onset fatigue indicating severe pulmonic stenosis","Anginal chest pain from RV oxygen supply-demand mismatch, or syncope, with very severe obstruction","Destabilizing triggers (atrial fibrillation, fever, infection, anemia, pregnancy) precipitating decompensation in severe PS","Signs of right heart failure (hepatomegaly, ascites, peripheral edema) from RV systolic dysfunction","Abdominal fullness/bloating and lower-extremity swelling in progressive severe PR with RV dysfunction"],"prognosis":"Mild pulmonic stenosis rarely progresses, whereas moderate disease may worsen with age due to valve thickening and calcification. Because cardiac output is maintained until late, many patients remain asymptomatic for years; severe disease eventually brings RV dysfunction and, uncommonly and only very late, overt right heart failure. In pulmonic regurgitation, forward output is preserved early but does not rise normally with exertion and declines over time, with a falling RV ejection fraction serving as an early marker of compromise and advanced disease producing marked RV/RA enlargement and elevated jugular venous pressure.","pitfalls":["The Graham Steell murmur of PR is easily confused with aortic regurgitation along the left sternal border.","A valveless RV-PA conduit after congenital repair can give a low-pitched, short diastolic murmur that understates the true severity of PR and RV volume overload.","Doppler peak instantaneous gradient correlates only weakly with the catheterization peak-to-peak gradient; the catheter peak-to-peak value tends to track better with the Doppler MEAN gradient.","Trace or mild PR seen on echocardiography is usually of no clinical or hemodynamic consequence and should not be over-interpreted.","Relying on the systolic ejection sound can mislead in severe PS, where the click moves toward S1 and may become inaudible."],"severityClassification":"Pulmonic stenosis severity (normal systolic function and cardiac output): severe = peak systolic gradient >64 mmHg (mean gradient >35 mmHg, Doppler jet velocity >4 m/s); moderate = peak gradient 36-64 mmHg (jet velocity 3-4 m/s); mild = jet velocity <3 m/s (peak gradient <36 mmHg).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2063","Harrison 22e p.2064"],"crossLinks":[]},"pulpitis":{"name":"Pulpitis","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["dental pulp inflammation","toothache","reversible pulpitis","irreversible pulpitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Pulpitis is inflammation of the dental pulp, most often a consequence of deep caries, cracks, or trauma allowing bacterial ingress","Reversible pulpitis produces sharp, short-lived pain to stimuli such as cold that settles quickly once the stimulus is removed","Irreversible pulpitis causes spontaneous, lingering, often throbbing pain that may worsen when lying down and can be poorly localised","Heat that intensifies pain and cold that relieves it can indicate an irreversibly inflamed or partly necrotic pulp","Pulp sensibility testing (cold, electric) helps distinguish vital from non-vital teeth and reversible from irreversible states","Reversible pulpitis is managed by removing the cause and placing a protective restoration, whereas irreversible pulpitis requires root canal treatment or extraction","As inflammation progresses to necrosis, pain may transiently ease before periapical infection develops","Referred pain patterns mean patients frequently misidentify which tooth or arch is affected"],"pathophysiology":"Pulpitis begins when bacterial products, most commonly from advancing caries, reach the pulp through dentinal tubules or direct exposure, triggering an inflammatory response within the rigid, low-compliance confines of the pulp chamber. Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability raise intrapulpal pressure, and because the pulp cannot expand, this compresses vessels and nerves, amplifying pain. In reversible disease the inflammation is limited and the pulp can recover once the irritant is removed. With continued insult the inflammation becomes irreversible, progressing to ischaemia, localised then total pulp necrosis, and eventual extension of infection through the apical foramen into the periapical tissues.","additionalDifferentials":["Dentine hypersensitivity","Cracked tooth syndrome","Periapical periodontitis or abscess","Maxillary sinusitis referring pain to upper teeth","Atypical odontalgia or trigeminal neuralgia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thermal and electric pulp sensibility testing","Percussion and palpation to assess periapical involvement","Periapical radiography to evaluate caries depth and apical changes","Bite test and transillumination for suspected cracks","Selective anaesthesia to localise the source of poorly localised pain"],"redFlags":["Facial swelling, fever, or trismus signalling spread of odontogenic infection","Rapidly worsening pain with systemic upset suggesting progression to abscess or cellulitis","Airway-threatening swelling from infection tracking into deep neck spaces"],"prognosis":"Reversible pulpitis usually resolves fully after the irritant is treated, while irreversible pulpitis will not recover and needs endodontic treatment or extraction; treated teeth generally have a good outcome when infection is eliminated.","pitfalls":["Assuming pain localisation is accurate when pulpal pain is often diffuse and referred","Treating irreversible pulpitis with a simple restoration and expecting resolution","Missing a maxillary sinus origin for upper posterior tooth pain","Interpreting a sudden reduction in pain as recovery rather than progression to necrosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"pyloric_stenosis":{"name":"Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","system":"Gastroenterology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","IHPS","pyloric stenosis","congenital pyloric stenosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Hypertrophy of the pyloric muscle causing gastric outlet obstruction, typically presenting between 3 and 6 weeks of age and more common in firstborn males.","The hallmark is progressive, forceful non-bilious (projectile) vomiting shortly after feeds, with the infant remaining hungry and eager to feed again.","The classic metabolic picture is a hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis from loss of gastric acid and chloride.","A palpable olive-shaped mass in the epigastrium/right upper quadrant and visible gastric peristalsis are classic but not always present.","Ultrasound is the diagnostic investigation of choice, demonstrating a thickened and elongated pyloric muscle.","The vomiting is characteristically non-bilious, because the obstruction is proximal to the ampulla of Vater; bilious vomiting should prompt consideration of other diagnoses.","Initial management is medical resuscitation: correcting dehydration and the electrolyte and acid-base disturbance before any operation.","Definitive treatment is surgical pyloromyotomy, which is not an emergency and should follow adequate fluid and electrolyte correction."],"pathophysiology":"Progressive hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the circular smooth muscle of the pylorus narrows and elongates the pyloric channel, obstructing gastric emptying. The exact cause is unknown but involves genetic and environmental factors. Persistent vomiting of gastric contents leads to loss of hydrogen and chloride ions, producing a hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis; ongoing losses and renal compensation drive potassium loss and, later, paradoxical aciduria. Fluid losses cause dehydration. The obstruction is proximal to the biliary drainage, so vomitus is non-bilious.","additionalDifferentials":["Gastro-oesophageal reflux","Overfeeding","Malrotation with volvulus (bilious vomiting)","Gastroenteritis","Cow's milk protein intolerance","Metabolic disorders or raised intracranial pressure causing vomiting","Adrenal insufficiency (salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Abdominal ultrasound to measure pyloric muscle thickness and channel length","Serum electrolytes, chloride, and bicarbonate","Blood gas to characterise the alkalosis","Assessment of hydration status and weight","Blood glucose"],"redFlags":["Signs of significant dehydration or shock","Severe electrolyte or acid-base derangement","Bilious vomiting, which points away from pyloric stenosis toward a surgical emergency such as volvulus","Lethargy or reduced responsiveness from dehydration and electrolyte imbalance"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent after appropriate resuscitation and pyloromyotomy, with prompt recovery and no long-term gastrointestinal sequelae.","pitfalls":["Rushing to surgery before correcting fluid and electrolyte abnormalities","Mistaking the illness for simple reflux or overfeeding and delaying diagnosis","Overlooking that vomiting is characteristically non-bilious, and failing to reconsider the diagnosis if it is bilious","Underestimating the degree of dehydration and metabolic alkalosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"pyomyositis":{"name":"Pyomyositis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tropical pyomyositis","bacterial myositis","primary muscle abscess","suppurative myositis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / IDSA","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A primary bacterial infection of skeletal muscle that forms an intramuscular abscess, most often affecting large muscles such as the thigh, buttock, or trunk","Staphylococcus aureus, including community-acquired MRSA, is the leading cause","Classically described in tropical regions but increasingly recognised in temperate climates, especially in diabetics and immunocompromised or HIV-positive patients","Evolves through an early invasive phase of vague muscle ache and low-grade fever, a suppurative phase with abscess and localised signs, and a late septic phase","Early disease is easily missed because overlying skin often looks normal and superficial fluctuance is absent","MRI is the most sensitive imaging modality for detecting muscle inflammation and abscess formation","Management principles combine antistaphylococcal antibiotics with drainage of any abscess, either percutaneously or surgically","Blood cultures are frequently positive and multiple muscle sites can be involved, so a thorough search is warranted"],"pathophysiology":"Pyomyositis is a suppurative infection arising within skeletal muscle, thought to require transient bacteraemia together with muscle that has been rendered susceptible by trauma, strenuous exercise, or underlying compromise. Skeletal muscle is normally resistant to infection, but injury allows haematogenously seeded bacteria to establish a focus that progresses from diffuse inflammation to a walled-off intramuscular abscess. Without drainage the infection can extend, cause systemic sepsis, and, if untreated, seed distant sites.","additionalDifferentials":["Deep vein thrombosis of the affected limb","Muscle haematoma or strain","Cellulitis or deep soft-tissue infection","Septic arthritis or osteomyelitis of an adjacent bone","Necrotising fasciitis in the septic phase"],"infectionMimics":["Muscle contusion or exertional injury","Deep vein thrombosis presenting with limb swelling and pain","Bone tumour or soft-tissue sarcoma","Inflammatory myopathy"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI to localise muscle inflammation and abscess; ultrasound or CT as alternatives","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers","Aspiration or drainage of collected pus for Gram stain and culture","Creatine kinase, which is often only mildly elevated despite muscle involvement","Screening for diabetes, HIV, or other immunosuppression"],"redFlags":["Signs of septic shock in the late phase requiring urgent resuscitation","Rapidly spreading pain and toxicity raising concern for necrotising fasciitis needing surgical exploration","Extension into adjacent joint or bone suggesting septic arthritis or osteomyelitis","Large or multiple abscesses requiring prompt drainage"],"prognosis":"With timely antibiotics and adequate drainage the outlook is generally good; delayed diagnosis, extensive or multifocal disease, and progression to septic shock worsen outcomes, and immunocompromised patients are at higher risk of complications.","pitfalls":["Dismissing early muscle pain with normal-looking skin as a simple strain","Relying on creatine kinase, which may be near normal, to exclude the diagnosis","Treating with antibiotics alone when an abscess needs drainage","Failing to look for additional muscle foci or an underlying immunocompromising condition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","IDSA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"rabies":{"name":"Rabies","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["rabies encephalitis"],"chapter":214,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1650","clinicalPearls":["Rabies is a rapidly progressive acute CNS infection by rabies virus (a lyssavirus, family Rhabdoviridae), normally transmitted via the bite of an infected animal; it has encephalitic and paralytic forms that progress to death (p.1650).","Worldwide, ~99% of human rabies is transmitted from dogs in countries with endemic canine rabies; endemic canine rabies causes an estimated 59,000 human deaths annually, with most deaths in Asia and Africa and rural populations and children disproportionately affected (p.1650-1651).","Incubation period is usually 20-90 days but in rare cases may be as short as a few days or longer than 1 year (p.1651).","Earliest specific neurologic symptoms are paresthesias, pain, or pruritus near the exposure site, occurring in 50-80% of patients and strongly suggesting rabies; the wound has usually healed by this point (p.1652).","Two acute neurologic forms occur: encephalitic (furious) in ~80% and paralytic (dumb) in ~20% (p.1652).","Encephalitic rabies presents as atypical encephalitis with relative preservation of consciousness; episodes of hyperexcitability alternate with periods of complete lucidity that shorten as disease progresses (p.1652).","Early brainstem involvement produces the classic hydrophobia (involuntary painful contraction of diaphragm and accessory respiratory, laryngeal, and pharyngeal muscles on swallowing liquids) and aerophobia (same response to a draft of air) (p.1652).","Autonomic dysfunction is common and may cause hypersalivation, gooseflesh, cardiac arrhythmia, and priapism; hypersalivation plus pharyngeal dysfunction produces the classic 'foaming at the mouth' (p.1652).","Paralytic rabies features early prominent flaccid weakness often beginning in the bitten extremity, progressing to quadriparesis and facial weakness, with sphincter involvement common and sensory involvement usually mild (p.1652).","The Negri body (eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusion in brain neurons, e.g. cerebellar Purkinje cells and hippocampal pyramidal neurons) is the most characteristic pathologic finding, but is NOT seen in all cases (p.1651-1652).","Diagnosis should be considered in patients with acute atypical encephalitis or acute flaccid paralysis, including those in whom Guillain-Barre syndrome is suspected; absence of an animal-bite history is common in North America due to unrecognized bat exposures (p.1653).","Postexposure prophylaxis must be initiated after a recognized exposure and before any symptoms or signs develop; the emphasis for prevention is on PEP (p.1652)."],"pathophysiology":"Rabies virus, a single-strand negative-sense RNA lyssavirus (family Rhabdoviridae), is usually inoculated by an animal bite. During most of the incubation period the virus remains at or near the inoculation site; in muscle it binds nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at neuromuscular junctions. It spreads centripetally along peripheral nerves toward the spinal cord or brainstem by retrograde fast axonal transport (up to ~250 mm/d, with ~12-h delays at each synapse). Once in the CNS it disseminates rapidly along neuroanatomic connections; neurons are prominently infected while astrocyte infection is unusual. After CNS infection is established there is centrifugal spread along sensory and autonomic nerves to salivary glands, heart, adrenal glands, and skin; viral replication in salivary gland acinar cells leads to secretion in saliva (the vehicle of animal transmission). There is no well-documented evidence for hematogenous spread. CNS inflammatory changes are mild (mononuclear infiltrate, perivascular cuffing, microglial Babes nodules) with little neuronal death; clinical disease is attributed to neuronal dysfunction rather than death, with oxidative stress from mitochondrial dysfunction implicated. (p.1650-1652)","additionalDifferentials":["Herpes simplex encephalitis","Arboviral encephalitis (e.g., West Nile)","Anti-NMDA receptor and other autoimmune encephalitides","Postinfectious (immune-mediated) encephalomyelitis (following influenza, measles, mumps, or neural-tissue rabies vaccine)","Guillain-Barre syndrome (mimicked by paralytic rabies)","Rabies hysteria / somatic symptom disorder (psychological response to fear of rabies)","Misdiagnosis as a primary psychiatric disorder"],"infectionMimics":["Herpes simplex encephalitis","Arboviral (e.g., West Nile) encephalitis","Postinfectious encephalomyelitis after influenza, measles, or mumps"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis (especially young females; behavioral changes, autonomic instability, hypoventilation, seizures)","Other antibody-mediated autoimmune encephalitis","Guillain-Barre syndrome","Rabies hysteria (somatic symptom disorder) with shorter course and eventual recovery","Psychiatric disorder"],"additionalInvestigations":["Rabies virus-specific serum neutralizing antibodies (diagnostic in a previously unimmunized patient; may not develop until late and some patients die without them)","Rabies virus-specific neutralizing antibodies in CSF (suggest rabies encephalitis regardless of immunization status)","RT-PCR for rabies virus RNA (highly sensitive/specific; saliva, skin biopsy, CSF [less sensitive], brain tissue; genetic sequencing can identify the variant/source)","Direct fluorescent antibody (DFA) testing for rabies virus antigen (skin biopsy and brain tissue; in skin, antigen detected in cutaneous nerves at the base of hair follicles)","Skin biopsy from hairy skin at the nape of the neck","CSF examination (typically mild mononuclear pleocytosis with mildly elevated protein)","MRI brain (variable, nonspecific brainstem/gray-matter signal abnormalities); CT usually normal; EEG nonspecific"],"redFlags":["Near-100% fatal once symptomatic, so post-exposure prophylaxis is everything - do NOT delay PEP after a recognized exposure.","Paresthesias, pain, or pruritus at a healed animal-bite site preceding neurologic illness strongly suggest rabies.","Hydrophobia or aerophobia with preserved consciousness (atypical encephalitis) is essentially diagnostic of encephalitic rabies.","Absence of a recalled animal bite does NOT exclude rabies - bat exposures are frequently unrecognized; consider PEP when a small child or sleeping adult shared a space with a bat and an unrecognized bite cannot be reliably excluded.","Negative antemortem rabies-specific laboratory tests never exclude the diagnosis; repeat testing may be needed.","Acute flaccid paralysis resembling Guillain-Barre syndrome may be paralytic rabies - fever, bladder dysfunction, normal sensory exam, and CSF pleocytosis favor rabies."],"prognosis":"Rabies is an almost uniformly fatal disease but is nearly always preventable after recognized exposures with appropriate postexposure therapy during the early incubation period. Most patients die within several days of symptom onset despite aggressive critical-care support. There is no established treatment and no antiviral has demonstrated efficacy; aggressive supportive care has yielded at least 33 documented survivors (many recently reported from India), but recent treatment failures (>60) have far outnumbered successes. All but 2 of the 33 documented survivors had received one or more doses of rabies vaccine before disease onset, and the unvaccinated survivors had neutralizing antibodies in CSF at presentation. A palliative approach may be appropriate for patients who are not candidates for aggressive management. (p.1654)","pitfalls":["Rabies is often not considered until late in the clinical course in North America even with a typical presentation.","Relying on a history of animal bite to make the diagnosis - many cases (especially bat-associated) have no recalled exposure.","Assuming absence of hydrophobia excludes rabies - lack of hydrophobia is not unusual.","Misdiagnosing paralytic rabies as Guillain-Barre syndrome.","Treating negative antemortem rabies-specific tests as exclusionary.","Severe CSF pleocytosis (>1000 white cells/uL) is unusual for rabies and should prompt a search for an alternative diagnosis.","Delaying local wound care while awaiting the 10-day animal observation period - wound care should not be delayed.","Administering passive immunization (RIG) more than 7 days after the first vaccine dose, when endogenous antibody production has begun and passive immunization may be counterproductive; rabies vaccine and RIG must never be given at the same site.","Minor deviations from current PEP recommendations can lead to prophylaxis failure."],"severityClassification":"Clinical stages: incubation period (20-90 days, asymptomatic); prodrome (2-10 days: fever, malaise, anorexia, nausea, vomiting; paresthesias/pain/pruritus at the wound site); acute neurologic disease - encephalitic form (~80%, 2-7 days: anxiety, agitation, hyperactivity, bizarre behavior, hallucinations, autonomic dysfunction, hydrophobia) or paralytic form (~20%, 2-10 days: flaccid limb paralysis progressing to quadriparesis with facial paralysis); then coma and death (0-14 days; recovery rare). (p.1653)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1650"],"crossLinks":["ENCEPHALITIS","gbs"]},"radiculopathy":{"name":"Radiculopathy","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nerve root compression","pinched nerve","cervical radiculopathy","lumbosacral radiculopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from dysfunction of one or more spinal nerve roots, producing pain, sensory change, and weakness in a dermatomal and myotomal distribution","Pain characteristically radiates along the affected root and is often aggravated by manoeuvres that raise intraspinal pressure such as coughing or straining","The commonest cause is a degenerative disc or spondylotic osteophyte compressing the root within or near the intervertebral foramen","Localising signs follow the root: for example L5 causes foot-drop with weak dorsiflexion, while S1 reduces the ankle reflex and plantar flexion","Provocative tests such as straight-leg raise (lumbar) or Spurling manoeuvre (cervical) support a root cause","Most cases are self-limiting and improve with conservative management, so imaging is guided by red flags and by persistence or progression of deficits","MRI is the preferred imaging test to correlate structural compression with the clinical level","Electromyography helps confirm the affected root and distinguish radiculopathy from plexopathy or peripheral neuropathy"],"pathophysiology":"Radiculopathy reflects mechanical compression, inflammation, or ischaemia of a nerve root, most often where a herniated disc or degenerative spondylotic change narrows the intervertebral foramen or lateral recess. Compression and local inflammatory mediators disrupt axonal conduction and cause both ectopic pain signals and conduction failure, producing radicular pain, dermatomal sensory loss, and myotomal weakness. Chronic compression may lead to axonal loss with denervation. Non-degenerative causes include infection, malignant infiltration, and inflammatory or infiltrative processes affecting the roots.","additionalDifferentials":["Peripheral entrapment neuropathy or mononeuropathy","Brachial or lumbosacral plexopathy","Referred pain from shoulder, hip, or joint disease","Spinal cord compression or myelopathy","Peripheral polyneuropathy","Infective or neoplastic root involvement (e.g. epidural abscess, metastasis, meningeal disease)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the relevant spinal region to identify compressive pathology","CT or CT myelography when MRI is contraindicated","Electromyography and nerve conduction studies to localise the root and exclude mimics","Inflammatory markers and imaging for infection or malignancy when red flags are present","Plain radiographs for alignment, instability, or degenerative change in selected cases"],"redFlags":["New bladder or bowel dysfunction, saddle anaesthesia, or bilateral leg symptoms suggesting cauda equina syndrome (surgical emergency)","Progressive or severe motor weakness rather than isolated pain","Fever, systemic illness, or immunosuppression raising concern for spinal infection","History of cancer, unexplained weight loss, or night pain suggesting malignant infiltration"],"prognosis":"Most degenerative radiculopathies resolve or substantially improve with conservative treatment over weeks to a few months; persistent severe compression, marked motor deficit, or a serious underlying cause predicts a poorer outcome and may require surgery.","pitfalls":["Missing cauda equina syndrome by not asking about sphincter and saddle symptoms","Over-reliance on incidental imaging findings that do not match the clinical level","Attributing radicular pain to a benign disc when infection or malignancy is the true cause","Delaying surgical referral in progressive motor weakness","Confusing peripheral entrapment or plexopathy with root disease without electrophysiology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"rapidly_progressive_gn":{"name":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RPGN","crescentic glomerulonephritis","crescentic GN"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["RPGN is a nephrological emergency defined by a rapid loss of renal function over days to weeks, accompanied by a nephritic urine sediment with dysmorphic red cells and red cell casts","The histological hallmark is crescent formation in a large proportion of glomeruli, reflecting severe glomerular injury and rupture of capillary walls","It is classified into three immunopathological groups: anti-glomerular basement membrane disease, immune-complex-mediated disease, and pauci-immune (usually ANCA-associated) disease","Coexisting pulmonary haemorrhage with glomerulonephritis (pulmonary-renal syndrome) suggests anti-GBM disease or ANCA-associated vasculitis and demands urgent evaluation","Early kidney biopsy is central to diagnosis and to gauging the balance of active (treatable) crescents versus fibrous (irreversible) scarring","Serological testing for ANCA, anti-GBM antibodies, complement levels, and other markers helps categorise the disease and guide treatment","Outcome depends critically on how quickly immunosuppression is started, since delay allows crescents to organise into irreversible fibrosis","Management principles centre on prompt aggressive immunosuppression, with plasma exchange added in anti-GBM disease and in selected severe or dialysis-dependent vasculitis cases"],"pathophysiology":"Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis describes a clinical syndrome of accelerating renal failure caused by severe inflammatory glomerular injury, characterised pathologically by extensive crescents. Crescents form when damage to the glomerular capillary wall allows plasma proteins, fibrin, and inflammatory cells to enter Bowman space, where proliferating parietal epithelial cells and macrophages compress the glomerular tuft. Three mechanisms underlie the syndrome: linear deposition of antibodies against the glomerular basement membrane (anti-GBM disease), deposition of immune complexes as seen in lupus, post-infectious, and IgA-related disease, and a pauci-immune process typically associated with ANCA-mediated small-vessel vasculitis. In all forms, unchecked inflammation rapidly destroys glomeruli and, without timely treatment, progresses to fibrous scarring and end-stage kidney disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute tubular necrosis","Acute interstitial nephritis","Thrombotic microangiopathy","Atheroembolic renal disease","Severe acute post-infectious glomerulonephritis","Accelerated hypertension with renal injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine microscopy for dysmorphic red cells and red cell casts","Kidney biopsy for crescent quantification and immunofluorescence pattern","ANCA testing (including antigen-specific assays) and anti-GBM antibody titres","Complement levels and other autoimmune serologies including antinuclear and anti-dsDNA antibodies","Chest imaging when pulmonary haemorrhage is suspected","Quantification of proteinuria and serial renal function monitoring"],"redFlags":["Pulmonary haemorrhage with haemoptysis and hypoxaemia indicating a pulmonary-renal syndrome","Rapidly rising creatinine or oliguria threatening imminent dialysis dependence","Severe hypertension or fluid overload","Systemic vasculitic features such as mononeuritis, purpura, or ENT involvement"],"prognosis":"Untreated RPGN progresses to end-stage kidney disease within weeks. With early immunosuppression many patients recover useful renal function, but prognosis worsens sharply with delayed treatment, a high proportion of fibrous crescents, dialysis dependence at presentation, and anti-GBM disease with advanced injury. Relapse is common in ANCA-associated disease and requires ongoing surveillance.","pitfalls":["Delaying biopsy and treatment while awaiting serology, allowing crescents to become irreversible","Missing coexisting pulmonary haemorrhage that changes urgency and management","Assuming a negative ANCA excludes vasculitis, since a minority are seronegative","Starting heavy immunosuppression in a kidney already dominated by fibrous scarring with little chance of recovery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"raynaud_phenomenon":{"name":"Raynaud phenomenon","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Raynaud's phenomenon","Raynaud syndrome","primary Raynaud","secondary Raynaud"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Episodic, reversible digital vasospasm provoked by cold or emotional stress, classically producing a triphasic colour change of pallor, then cyanosis, then reactive hyperaemia","Primary Raynaud is common, typically begins in young women, is symmetric, and occurs without an underlying disease or tissue damage","Secondary Raynaud arises from an underlying condition, most importantly connective tissue disease, and carries a greater risk of digital ischaemia and ulceration","Features favouring a secondary cause include older age at onset, asymmetry, digital ulcers or pitting scars, and abnormal nailfold capillaries","A positive antinuclear antibody and abnormal nailfold capillaroscopy suggest an evolving connective tissue disease, often systemic sclerosis","General measures emphasise cold avoidance, keeping the whole body warm, and smoking cessation","Pharmacological therapy, when needed, uses vasodilators (calcium channel blockers first-line, with other agents for refractory or ischaemic disease)","Critical digital ischaemia is an emergency requiring urgent assessment and consideration of secondary causes"],"pathophysiology":"Raynaud phenomenon results from exaggerated vasoconstriction of digital arteries, precapillary arterioles, and cutaneous arteriovenous shunts in response to cold or stress. In primary disease this reflects a functional imbalance of vascular tone with heightened sympathetic and local vascular reactivity and no structural vessel damage. In secondary disease, structural vasculopathy, endothelial dysfunction, and abnormal balance of vasoconstrictors (such as endothelin) and vasodilators (such as nitric oxide), often in the setting of systemic sclerosis or other connective tissue disease, produce fixed narrowing that predisposes to persistent ischaemia, ulceration, and tissue loss.","additionalDifferentials":["Systemic sclerosis and other connective tissue diseases (secondary Raynaud)","Thoracic outlet syndrome and other causes of arterial compression","Atherosclerotic or embolic peripheral arterial disease","Vibration-induced (hand-arm) vascular injury and occupational causes","Drug- or toxin-induced vasospasm","Acrocyanosis, chilblains, and cryoglobulinaemia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Nailfold capillaroscopy to distinguish primary from secondary disease","Antinuclear antibody and, if positive, connective tissue disease-specific antibodies","Inflammatory markers and assessment for features of systemic autoimmune disease","Vascular assessment for fixed arterial obstruction when ischaemia is asymmetric or severe","Targeted investigations for occupational, drug, or haematological causes when suspected"],"redFlags":["Digital ulceration, gangrene, or critical ischaemia threatening tissue loss","Asymmetric or unilateral symptoms suggesting fixed arterial obstruction or embolism","New Raynaud phenomenon in an older adult indicating a likely secondary cause","Accompanying features of connective tissue disease such as skin tightening, dysphagia, or breathlessness","Abnormal nailfold capillaries with positive autoantibodies signalling evolving systemic sclerosis"],"prognosis":"Primary Raynaud phenomenon is benign, usually stable or improving over time, and rarely causes tissue damage. Secondary Raynaud has a more variable outlook determined by the underlying disease and carries a real risk of digital ulceration and loss, particularly in systemic sclerosis.","pitfalls":["Labelling all Raynaud as benign primary disease without screening for secondary causes","Missing evolving systemic sclerosis by not performing nailfold capillaroscopy and autoantibody testing","Underestimating critical digital ischaemia as ordinary vasospasm","Overlooking drug, occupational, or vascular contributors","Neglecting non-pharmacological measures such as whole-body warming and smoking cessation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"relapsing_fever":{"name":"Relapsing Fever","system":"Infectious Disease (Spirochetal / Vector-borne)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Louse-borne relapsing fever","LBRF","Soft tick relapsing fever","STRF","Hard tick relapsing fever","HTRF","Borrelia recurrentis infection","Borrelia miyamotoi disease"],"chapter":190,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1444","clinicalPearls":["Relapsing fever is caused by several Borrelia spirochete species and occurs in three epidemiologic forms: louse-borne (LBRF, B. recurrentis), soft tick-borne (STRF, multiple Ornithodoros-transmitted species), and hard tick-borne (HTRF, mainly B. miyamotoi and B. lonestari) (p.1444).","The hallmark is two or more febrile episodes separated by afebrile intervals of well-being; the relapsing course reflects antigenic variation of the spirochete's surface lipoproteins, with each variant cleared as specific antibody develops (p.1444-1446).","In LBRF the first fever is typically unremitting for 3-6 days and is usually followed by a single milder relapse, whereas STRF produces multiple febrile periods of 1-3 days each; interfebrile intervals span roughly 4-14 days (p.1446).","A 'crisis' may terminate the first fever episode (rigors, a further rise in temperature, increased pulse and blood pressure for about 15-30 min), followed by drenching sweats, falling temperature, and hypotension lasting several hours (p.1446).","Soft ticks (Ornithodoros) feed briefly, often under 30 minutes and usually while the victim sleeps, then leave undetected, so the exposure may go unrecognized (p.1445).","Transovarial transmission in soft ticks means infection risk can persist in a dwelling for years, and a case may be diagnosed long after the last reported local case (p.1445-1447).","LBRF is acquired not from the louse bite itself but by rubbing crushed louse hemolymph into the bite site or auto-inoculating it into conjunctivae or a wound; humans are the sole reservoir of B. recurrentis (p.1445).","Jaundice, epistaxis, and subconjunctival hemorrhages are common in LBRF but typically absent in STRF and HTRF; splenomegaly or splenic tenderness is common in both LBRF and STRF (p.1446).","Localizing neurologic signs are more frequent in STRF than in LBRF/HTRF; B. turicatae is notably neurotropic, and unilateral or bilateral Bell's palsy (cranial neuritis) characteristically appears in the second or third febrile episode rather than the first (p.1446).","B. miyamotoi disease (HTRF) is less distinctive, often presenting as acute fever with chills, headache, and myalgia 1-2 weeks after a tick bite, and has been mistaken for undifferentiated sepsis; meningoencephalitis occurs particularly in patients with impaired humoral immunity (e.g., after rituximab) (p.1446).","Diagnosis of LBRF/STRF rests on direct microscopic detection of spirochetes in blood (Wright, Giemsa, or Giemsa-Wright stain), best collected at or just before the fever peak; spirochete density in B. miyamotoi/B. lonestari may be too low for smear alone (p.1447).","Outbreaks of LBRF (often alongside typhus) cluster in settings of famine, war, refugee migration, homelessness, and incarceration, and can appear in migrant camps far from the country of origin (p.1445)."],"pathophysiology":"Borrelia are slender, double-membrane spirochetes that are extracellular pathogens with a limited biosynthetic genome about one-quarter the size of E. coli; their flagella lie between the two membranes. Tick-borne spirochetes enter in tick saliva at the onset of feeding, and from a small inoculum STRF organisms proliferate in blood (doubling roughly every 6 h) to 10^6-10^7/mL or more, while HTRF organisms grow more slowly to lower densities. Although they lack potent exotoxins and lack lipopolysaccharide endotoxin, abundant Borrelia lipoproteins activate Toll-like receptors, driving an endotoxemia-like proinflammatory response with elevated TNF-alpha, IL-6, and IL-8. Spirochete binding to erythrocytes causes red cell aggregation, splenic/hepatic sequestration, hepatosplenomegaly, and anemia; bleeding reflects thrombocytopenia, impaired hepatic clotting-factor production, and small-vessel blockage by aggregates of spirochetes, erythrocytes, and platelets. Serotype-specific IgM appears after a few days and lyses circulating bacteria by bactericidal action or opsonization; release of bacterial products from dying organisms drives a spontaneous 'crisis,' the antibiotic-triggered counterpart of which is the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction. Antigenic variation of surface lipoproteins produces successive bacteremic relapses, each ended by variant-specific antibody. Some species are neurotropic and enter the relatively immune-sheltered brain, and spirochetes can cross the maternal-fetal barrier to cause placental damage, intrauterine growth retardation, and congenital infection (p.1444-1447).","additionalDifferentials":["Rocky Mountain spotted fever and other rickettsioses","Ehrlichiosis","Anaplasmosis","Tick-borne viral infection (including a viral encephalitis transmitted by Ixodes)","Babesiosis","Lyme disease (especially when STRF presents with Bell's palsy in co-endemic areas)","Malaria","Typhoid fever","Epidemic (louse-borne) typhus","Dengue","Leptospirosis","Trench fever (Bartonella quintana, also louse-transmitted)","Influenza (LBRF cough plus fever and myalgia can mimic it)","Undifferentiated sepsis (notably with B. miyamotoi disease)"],"infectionMimics":["Malaria (periodic fever, co-endemic, can coexist as co-infection)","Typhoid fever","Epidemic louse-borne typhus (Rickettsia prowazekii) and other rickettsioses","Leptospirosis","Dengue","Lyme disease (cross-reactive serology and shared Bell's palsy)","Anaplasmosis, ehrlichiosis, and babesiosis","Influenza"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Wright, Giemsa, or Giemsa-Wright-stained thin blood smear examined over several oil-immersion fields (sensitive when spirochete density is >= 10^5/mL), ideally drawn at or just before the fever peak","Thick blood smear treated with 0.5% acetic acid before staining, or a wet mount of citrated blood examined by phase-contrast/dark-field microscopy for motile spirochetes","PCR / nucleic acid amplification testing of blood or CSF (more sensitive than thin smear, especially between febrile episodes; preferred for B. miyamotoi and B. lonestari)","Culture of blood or CSF in Barbour-Stoenner-Kelly broth medium (slow, rarely offered)","GlpQ-based serologic assay (specific for relapsing-fever Borrelia and negative in Lyme disease species, but often negative when smear/PCR are positive)","CSF analysis in any case with signs of meningitis/meningoencephalitis (mononuclear pleocytosis and mildly-to-moderately elevated protein support CNS involvement)","Complete blood count (normocytic anemia; variable leukocyte count; thrombocytopenia, sometimes <50,000/uL)","C-reactive protein and procalcitonin (elevated)","Liver enzymes and coagulation studies (elevated aminotransferases; PT/PTT may be moderately prolonged)","ECG and chest radiography where myocarditis is suspected (prolonged QTc, cardiomegaly, pulmonary edema)"],"redFlags":["Signs of meningitis or meningoencephalitis (warrants CSF analysis and consideration of CNS-directed therapy)","Cranial neuritis, especially uni- or bilateral Bell's palsy, or visual impairment from iridocyclitis/panophthalmitis (which may be permanent)","Myocarditis: gallop rhythm, prolonged QTc, cardiomegaly, and pulmonary edema","Acute respiratory distress syndrome","Diffuse bleeding, jaundice, and poor liver function","Stupor or coma on admission","Pregnancy (high risk of abortion, stillbirth, or perinatal death)","Concurrent malaria, typhus, or typhoid","Abrupt clinical worsening within 1-3 h of starting antibiotics (Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction with hypotension and falling platelets), which can be fatal without monitoring"],"prognosis":"Untreated mortality is approximately 10-70% for LBRF and 4-10% for STRF, depending heavily on coexisting conditions (e.g., malnutrition, other infection) and available medical support. With prompt antibiotic treatment, mortality falls to about 2-5% for LBRF and under 2% for STRF; no deaths from HTRF have been reported. Poor-prognosis features include concurrent malaria, typhus, or typhoid; pregnancy; stupor or coma at admission; diffuse bleeding; poor liver function; myocarditis; and bronchopneumonia. The Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction in LBRF carries roughly a 5% mortality without adequate monitoring and resuscitation. LBRF or STRF in pregnancy frequently causes abortion, stillbirth, or perinatal death, though congenital malformations have not been reported. Documented posttreatment sequelae and prolonged disability have not been established for any form, and partial immunity to reinfection appears to develop in residents of perennially high-risk areas (p.1448).","pitfalls":["Whole-cell serologic assays (including ELISA and the C6 peptide ELISA for Lyme disease) can be falsely positive in relapsing fever due to antigenic cross-reactivity among spirochetes (p.1447).","Serology cannot distinguish among Borrelia species as the cause, and a positive IgG on a single specimen may reflect past infection rather than the current illness (p.1447).","Specific serologic assays (e.g., GlpQ-based) are commonly negative at the very time a blood smear or PCR would be positive (p.1447).","Spirochete density in B. miyamotoi or B. lonestari infection may be too low for diagnosis by blood smear alone, so PCR is preferred (p.1447).","Smears drawn during afebrile intervals may miss spirochetemia; sampling at/just before the fever peak (or using NAAT) improves yield (p.1447).","For B. miyamotoi HTRF, prompt removal of an embedded tick may not prevent infection because transmission occurs soon after feeding begins (p.1447).","STRF presenting with Bell's palsy can be misdiagnosed as Lyme disease in co-endemic regions (p.1447).","Co-infections (malaria, typhus, typhoid with relapsing fever; B. miyamotoi with Lyme disease) may obscure the diagnosis (p.1447).","A Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction can be provoked even by antibiotics with suboptimal antiborrelial activity (e.g., ciprofloxacin) given for an unsuspected case (p.1448)."],"severityClassification":"No formal numeric severity score is given. Clinically, severity is stratified by relapsing-fever type and complications: LBRF carries higher untreated mortality (about 10-70%) than STRF (about 4-10%), while HTRF/B. miyamotoi disease is mildest with no reported deaths. CNS involvement (confirmed or suspected meningitis/meningoencephalitis, supported by CSF mononuclear pleocytosis and elevated protein) marks severe disease warranting parenteral therapy. Features defining a severe/poor-prognosis course include concurrent malaria, typhus, or typhoid; pregnancy; stupor or coma at admission; diffuse bleeding; poor liver function; myocarditis; and bronchopneumonia (p.1446-1448).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1444","Harrison 22e p.1445","Harrison 22e p.1446","Harrison 22e p.1447","Harrison 22e p.1448"],"crossLinks":[]},"relapsing_polychondritis":{"name":"Relapsing Polychondritis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Rheumatologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RP"],"chapter":378,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2917","clinicalPearls":["Rare systemic disease defined by recurrent inflammation of cartilaginous structures, with the ears, nose, respiratory tract, and joints as hallmark sites; the eyes, inner ear, nervous system, skin, and cardiovascular system may also be involved (p.2917).","Ear involvement is the most common manifestation, affecting roughly 80-90% of patients, and typically spares the non-cartilaginous lobule; pain may occur with minimal visible swelling or erythema (p.2918).","Triggers for ear pain often include minor trauma (lying on the affected side, wearing glasses) or temperature change; 'cauliflower ear' from cartilage damage occurs only in a small subset after recurrent episodes (p.2918).","Nasal disease often presents as a pressure sensation at the bridge of the nose; a saddle-nose deformity may develop but is not universal, and unlike GPA the nasal perforations in RP are usually anterior rather than septal (p.2918-2919).","Airway involvement such as subglottic stenosis may be misdiagnosed as adult-onset asthma; severe narrowing can cause stridor and is a medical emergency, and chronic damage can lead to tracheomalacia, bronchomalacia, or tracheal calcification (p.2919).","Costochondral joint inflammation can produce severe bilateral chest pain that is reproducible on palpation and not movement-related, sometimes mimicking acute coronary syndrome and prompting emergency evaluation (p.2919-2920).","Recurrent chondritis of the ears and nose in older male patients with severe systemic inflammation and progressive bone marrow failure should prompt evaluation for VEXAS syndrome (somatic UBA1 mutation) (p.2917, 2920).","Episcleritis is the most frequent ocular manifestation; scleritis is rare but warrants urgent ophthalmologic evaluation given the risk of vision loss, scleromalacia, or globe rupture (p.2920).","Because disease activity is intermittent and patients may appear non-inflamed during a clinic visit, a longitudinal history and review of patient-provided photographs are essential to diagnosis (p.2918).","MAGIC syndrome (mouth and genital ulcers with inflamed cartilage) overlaps with Behcet's syndrome features such as pathergy and GI involvement and may include large-vessel vasculitis of the aorta (p.2920).","Some patients carry concurrent rheumatologic diagnoses such as Sjogren's syndrome or systemic lupus erythematosus (p.2917).","Neurologic attribution to RP is a diagnosis of exclusion; infection and malignancy must be ruled out first, though encephalitis and meningitis can occur in RP (p.2920)."],"pathophysiology":"The exact mechanisms remain unclear despite study of genetic, environmental, and immunologic factors. Limited animal-model data suggest autoimmunity against cartilage components may contribute: immunizing certain animals against type II collagen, type IX collagen, or matrilin-1 can reproduce aspects of the human phenotype. However, autoantibodies (including anti-type II collagen) are neither sensitive nor specific enough for diagnosis, and observational data showing B-cell-depleting therapy is not particularly effective argue against a dominant antibody-mediated mechanism. Proinflammatory cytokines and chemokines linked to both innate and adaptive immunity have been associated with RP but are inconsistent. Histologically, acute cartilage shows a mixed myeloid and lymphoid inflammatory infiltrate at the cartilage interface; over time there is lacunar breakdown, loss of chondrocytes, and replacement by fibrosis (p.2918).","additionalDifferentials":["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA) - distinguished by ANCA positivity and glomerulonephritis, which are not features of RP (p.2920)","Autoinflammatory diseases (p.2920)","VEXAS syndrome - especially in older males with cytopenia, macrocytosis, and lymphopenia (p.2920)","Rheumatoid arthritis - the small-joint pattern can mimic RA but RP arthritis is nonerosive and nondeforming (p.2919)","Behcet's syndrome - overlap via MAGIC syndrome (p.2920)","Adult-onset asthma - airway chondritis is frequently misattributed to asthma (p.2919)"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious diseases of the external ear mimicking ear chondritis (p.2920)","Infectious diseases mimicking nasal chondritis (p.2920)","Infectious airway disease mimicking airway chondritis (p.2920)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Traumatic otohematoma and red ear syndrome (ear) (p.2920)","Cutaneous diseases of the external ear (p.2920)","Angiocentric centrofacial lymphoma (nasal chondritis) (p.2920)","Traumatic airway stenosis and congenital airway disorders (p.2920)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Audiometry to assess for conductive and sensorineural hearing loss in all suspected patients (p.2920)","Dynamic expiratory-phase chest CT to evaluate tracheobronchomalacia (p.2920)","Direct laryngoscopy to visualize upper-airway cartilage damage; bronchoscopy of the lower airway can exacerbate disease and must be used with caution (p.2920-2921)","Echocardiography to assess valvular heart disease (p.2921)","FDG-PET to detect inflammation in large arteries and larger airways (p.2921)","Vestibular testing (finger-to-nose, Romberg, nystagmus) when dizziness is present (p.2920)","Genetic testing for UBA1 and bone marrow assessment for VEXAS in older males with cytopenias (p.2920)","Cartilage biopsy reserved for excluding mimics (infection, malignancy) since findings are nonspecific (p.2918, 2921)"],"redFlags":["Stridor from severe airway/subglottic narrowing - a medical emergency requiring urgent attention to avoid mortality (p.2919)","Features of scleritis - urgent ophthalmology referral due to risk of vision loss, scleromalacia, or globe rupture (p.2920)","Cytopenia with macrocytosis and lymphopenia in older males - suggests VEXAS and possible progressive bone marrow failure (p.2917, 2920)","Severe costochondral chest pain that can mimic acute coronary syndrome (p.2919-2920)"],"prognosis":"Outcomes depend heavily on organ involvement. Airway disease can progress to tracheomalacia, bronchomalacia, or tracheal calcification, and severe subglottic narrowing carries mortality risk. Scleritis can lead to vision loss or globe rupture. VEXAS-associated disease involves progressive bone marrow failure and is potentially life-threatening, so prompt recognition is essential. Acute-phase reactants (ESR, CRP) are not reliably elevated, so they cannot be used to gauge severity (p.2917-2920).","pitfalls":["Airway involvement is commonly misdiagnosed as adult-onset asthma, delaying treatment and risking chronic large-airway damage (p.2919)","Normal ESR and CRP do not exclude active RP, as acute-phase reactants are not reliably elevated (p.2918)","There is no diagnostic blood or tissue biomarker; relying on a single test is a pitfall - diagnosis rests on clinical pattern recognition over time (p.2918, 2920)","Cartilage biopsy yields nonspecific findings and can cause morbidity; it should not be expected to confirm RP (p.2918, 2921)","Patients may appear non-inflamed at a single visit because disease activity is intermittent (p.2918)","Published diagnostic criteria were derived from small cohorts, are largely expert-opinion-based, and have not been formally validated (p.2920)","Assuming a saddle nose implies GPA - in RP the deformity usually lacks septal perforation (perforations are anterior) (p.2918-2919)"],"severityClassification":"No formally validated severity grading exists. Several diagnostic schemes have been proposed: McAdam's criteria require 3 of 6 features (bilateral auricular chondritis, nonerosive seronegative inflammatory polyarthritis, nasal chondritis, ocular inflammation, respiratory-tract chondritis, vestibular/cochlear dysfunction); the Damiani-Levine modification accepts 3 clinical features, or 1 feature plus histologic chondritis, or 2 features plus clinical response to treatment; Michet's criteria use 3 major criteria (ear, nose, or respiratory-tract inflammation) and 4 minor criteria (ocular inflammation, hearing loss, vestibular dysfunction, seronegative inflammatory arthritis), requiring 2 major or 1 major plus 2 minor. All are limited by lack of precise organ-involvement definitions (p.2920).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2917","Harrison 22e p.2918","Harrison 22e p.2919","Harrison 22e p.2920","Harrison 22e p.2921"],"crossLinks":[]},"renal_artery_stenosis":{"name":"Renal artery stenosis","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RAS","renovascular disease","renovascular hypertension","ischaemic nephropathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Renal artery stenosis is a narrowing of one or both renal arteries that can cause secondary hypertension and, when bilateral or affecting a single kidney, progressive ischaemic nephropathy","Atherosclerosis accounts for the great majority of cases, typically in older patients with widespread vascular disease, while fibromuscular dysplasia is the main cause in younger women","Clues suggesting a renovascular cause include hypertension of abrupt onset or that is severe or resistant, an abdominal bruit, and unexplained kidney asymmetry","A significant rise in creatinine after starting a renin-angiotensin inhibitor, especially with bilateral disease, is a classic pointer to renovascular disease","Recurrent flash pulmonary oedema without a clear cardiac cause can be a manifestation of severe bilateral stenosis","Non-invasive imaging such as duplex ultrasound, CT angiography, or MR angiography is used for diagnosis, with catheter angiography reserved for when intervention is planned","Medical therapy with renin-angiotensin blockade, other antihypertensives, statins, and antiplatelet treatment is the foundation of management for atherosclerotic disease","Revascularisation is reserved for selected patients such as those with fibromuscular dysplasia, rapidly declining function, refractory hypertension, or recurrent pulmonary oedema, since routine stenting has not shown benefit over medical therapy in most atherosclerotic cases"],"pathophysiology":"Renal artery stenosis reduces perfusion pressure to the affected kidney, which the kidney interprets as volume depletion and responds to by activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. The resulting angiotensin-mediated vasoconstriction and aldosterone-driven sodium retention raise systemic blood pressure. When only one kidney is affected, the contralateral kidney can excrete the retained sodium, so hypertension is predominantly renin-driven. With bilateral disease or a single functioning kidney, sodium and volume retention dominate and drive the hypertension. Chronic hypoperfusion also causes ischaemic injury with tubular atrophy, interstitial fibrosis, and glomerulosclerosis, producing ischaemic nephropathy. Renin-angiotensin blockade can precipitate a fall in glomerular filtration because efferent arteriolar tone, needed to maintain filtration pressure in the underperfused kidney, is removed.","additionalDifferentials":["Essential (primary) hypertension","Primary hyperaldosteronism","Phaeochromocytoma","Other secondary causes of hypertension","Hypertensive nephrosclerosis","Atheroembolic renal disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Duplex ultrasound of the renal arteries with velocity measurements","CT or MR angiography to define the anatomy of the stenosis","Assessment of renal function and kidney size asymmetry","Monitoring of creatinine after introduction of renin-angiotensin blockade","Catheter angiography when revascularisation is being considered","Screening for other secondary causes of hypertension when appropriate"],"redFlags":["Rapidly deteriorating renal function, particularly after starting renin-angiotensin blockade","Recurrent flash pulmonary oedema without a cardiac explanation","Severe or resistant hypertension unresponsive to multiple agents","Acute kidney injury suggesting critical bilateral stenosis or renal artery occlusion"],"prognosis":"Fibromuscular dysplasia generally responds well to angioplasty with a good chance of improving or curing hypertension. Atherosclerotic disease reflects generalised vascular disease and carries substantial cardiovascular risk; for most such patients optimal medical therapy gives outcomes comparable to intervention, and prognosis is driven largely by comorbid cardiovascular disease and the degree of established ischaemic nephropathy.","pitfalls":["Reflexively stenting atherosclerotic lesions when evidence favours medical therapy for most patients","Stopping renin-angiotensin blockade for a modest, stable creatinine rise that is actually acceptable","Missing fibromuscular dysplasia as a curable cause of hypertension in a young woman","Failing to recognise bilateral disease as the cause of recurrent flash pulmonary oedema"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"renal_cell_carcinoma":{"name":"Renal Cell Carcinoma","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RCC","kidney cancer"],"chapter":90,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.687-690","clinicalPearls":["RCC accounts for 90-95% of malignant neoplasms arising from the kidney; the remaining 5-10% are transitional (urothelial) cell carcinomas of the renal pelvis (covered separately in Chap. 91) (p.687-688).","The classic triad of hematuria, flank pain, and a palpable abdominal mass is now uncommon; most tumors are detected incidentally on cross-sectional imaging (CT/MRI) performed for other reasons (p.688-689).","Other presenting features include fever, weight loss, anemia, and a varicocele (p.688).","Once called the \"internist's tumor\" because it was frequently first detected via a paraneoplastic syndrome before modern imaging was common (p.688-689).","Clear cell is the predominant histology (~70% of cases) and is found in >80% of patients who develop metastases; it arises from epithelial cells of the proximal tubules (p.688).","Any solid renal mass should be considered malignant until proven otherwise; a definitive diagnosis is required (p.689).","Most common sites of distant metastasis are the lungs, lymph nodes, liver, bone, and brain; the clinical course can be unpredictable and protracted (p.689).","RCC is associated with anecdotal reports of spontaneous regression (p.687).","Increasing detection of incidental low-stage tumors has improved 5-year survival and increased use of nephron-sparing surgery (partial nephrectomy) (p.689)."],"pathophysiology":"RCC is a heterogeneous group of tumors. Most cases are sporadic, but familial forms exist. Clear cell RCC (the predominant subtype) is characterized by loss of chromosome 3p as the earliest event, causing loss of heterozygosity for 3p genes including VHL, PBRM1, BAP1, and SETD2. VHL encodes a tumor-suppressor protein that regulates transcription of VEGF and other effectors via ubiquitination of hypoxia-inducible factors (HIF); VHL inactivation upregulates VEGF signaling, promoting tumor angiogenesis and growth and rendering clear cell RCC susceptible to antiangiogenic therapy. Clear cell RCC also arises in von Hippel-Lindau syndrome (autosomal dominant, VHL gene on chromosome 3p), which carries a ~70% lifetime risk of clear cell RCC. Papillary subtypes are linked to activating MET mutations or gain of chromosome 7; chromophobe tumors originate from the distal nephron and typically show aneuploidy. Early loss of chromosome 9p confers high risk of early metastatic dissemination and correlates with poor cancer-specific survival (p.687-688).","additionalDifferentials":["Simple/complex renal cysts (p.689)","Benign neoplasms: adenoma, angiomyolipoma, oncocytoma (p.689)","Inflammatory lesions: pyelonephritis or renal abscess (p.689)","Transitional (urothelial) cell carcinoma of the renal pelvis (p.689)","Renal sarcoma (p.689)","Renal lymphoma (p.689)","Wilms' tumor (p.689)","Metastases from cancers originating in other organs (p.689)"],"infectionMimics":["Pyelonephritis (inflammatory renal lesion in the differential of a renal mass) (p.689)","Renal abscess (inflammatory renal lesion in the differential of a renal mass) (p.689)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Renal cysts (p.689)","Angiomyolipoma (p.689)","Oncocytoma (p.689)","Renal adenoma (p.689)"],"additionalInvestigations":["CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis - part of standard evaluation of a suspected renal tumor (p.689)","Chest radiograph; CT of the chest if metastatic disease is suspected on chest x-ray (p.689)","Urine analysis (p.689)","MRI - useful for evaluating inferior vena cava when tumor thrombus/invasion is suspected, or when IV contrast for CT is precluded by impaired renal function (p.689)","Needle biopsy to confirm histology in small tumors managed with radiographic surveillance before delayed surgery (p.689)"],"redFlags":["Gross hematuria (p.688)","Flank or abdominal pain (p.688)","Palpable abdominal/flank mass (p.688)","Constitutional symptoms: fever, weight loss, anemia (p.688)","New varicocele (p.688)","Paraneoplastic features: erythrocytosis, hypercalcemia, nonmetastatic hepatic dysfunction (Stauffer's syndrome) (p.688)"],"prognosis":"Staging uses the AJCC TNM system. Stage I tumors are <=7 cm and confined to the kidney; stage II are >7 cm and confined to the kidney; stage III extend through the renal capsule but remain within Gerota's fascia, grossly infiltrate the renal vein, or involve regional nodes (N1); stage IV invades adjacent organs or involves nonregional nodes or distant metastases. About 65% present at stage I-II, 15-20% at stage III, and 15-20% at stage IV. Overall 5-year survival is currently ~77% but varies greatly by stage. For metastatic disease, the IMDC risk model uses six factors associated with worse survival (poor performance status, low hemoglobin, high serum calcium, high neutrophils, high platelets, and <1-year interval from diagnosis to systemic treatment). Zero risk factors confer significantly longer median survival (>=5 years) than one to two factors (~4 years) or three to six factors (~3 years) with first-line checkpoint-inhibitor-containing regimens. Projected overall survival for newly diagnosed metastatic disease has tripled over the past 15-20 years (p.688-690).","pitfalls":["Relying on the classic triad (hematuria, flank pain, palpable mass) for diagnosis - it is now uncommon, and most tumors present incidentally (p.688-689).","Assuming chemotherapy will help - metastatic RCC is refractory to cytotoxic chemotherapy; treatment relies on molecularly targeted agents and checkpoint immunotherapy (p.689).","Treating a solid renal mass as benign without proof - any solid renal mass should be considered malignant until proven otherwise (p.689).","Despite many candidate biomarkers investigated for predictive value in metastatic RCC, none have been validated for clinical use to date (p.690).","Renal vein or inferior vena cava invasion does not preclude surgical resection if no metastases are present (p.689)."],"severityClassification":"AJCC TNM staging (stages I-IV; Fig. 90-1): Stage I <=7 cm confined to kidney; Stage II >7 cm confined to kidney; Stage III extends through renal capsule within Gerota's fascia, infiltrates renal vein, or N1 nodal involvement; Stage IV invades adjacent organs or has nonregional nodal or distant metastases. For metastatic disease, the IMDC (International Metastatic RCC Database Consortium) risk model stratifies prognosis using six clinical/laboratory factors (p.688-689).","references":["Harrison 22e p.687-690"],"crossLinks":["hypercalcemia"]},"renal_tubular_acidosis":{"name":"Renal tubular acidosis","system":"Renal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RTA","distal RTA","type 1 RTA","proximal RTA","type 2 RTA","type 4 RTA","hyperkalaemic RTA"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / KDIGO","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["RTA produces a normal anion gap (hyperchloraemic) metabolic acidosis despite relatively preserved glomerular filtration, distinguishing it from the high anion gap acidosis of advanced renal failure","Type 1 (distal) RTA reflects failure of the distal nephron to secrete hydrogen ions, so urine pH stays inappropriately high (typically above 5.5) in the face of systemic acidosis, and is usually accompanied by hypokalaemia","Type 2 (proximal) RTA arises from impaired proximal reabsorption of bicarbonate; once serum bicarbonate falls below the lowered threshold, distal acidification can still generate acidic urine","Type 4 RTA is a hyperkalaemic form driven by aldosterone deficiency or resistance, most commonly seen in diabetic kidney disease, and impairs both acid and potassium excretion","Nephrocalcinosis and calcium-containing stones are characteristic of distal RTA because of hypercalciuria, low urinary citrate, and alkaline urine","The urine anion gap is a useful bedside tool: a positive (or inappropriately non-negative) value in the presence of acidosis points to impaired renal ammonium excretion rather than gastrointestinal bicarbonate loss","Proximal RTA frequently coexists with generalised proximal tubular dysfunction (Fanconi syndrome), so glycosuria, phosphaturia, and aminoaciduria should be sought","Management principles centre on alkali replacement; distal RTA usually needs modest alkali plus potassium, while proximal RTA requires larger alkali loads and often potassium supplementation because bicarbonate therapy worsens kaliuresis"],"pathophysiology":"The renal tubular acidoses are disorders in which the kidney fails to appropriately acidify the urine or reclaim filtered bicarbonate despite normal or near-normal glomerular filtration, producing a hyperchloraemic (normal anion gap) metabolic acidosis. In distal (type 1) RTA the collecting duct cannot secrete hydrogen ions effectively, so net acid excretion falls and the urine cannot be maximally acidified. In proximal (type 2) RTA the proximal tubule cannot reabsorb the filtered bicarbonate load, leading to bicarbonate wasting until serum levels reach a new lower steady state. Type 4 RTA results from deficient or ineffective aldosterone action, reducing distal sodium reabsorption and potassium and hydrogen secretion, which produces hyperkalaemia and a mild acidosis. Potassium behaves differently across subtypes: distal and proximal forms tend toward hypokalaemia, whereas the aldosterone-related form causes hyperkalaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Diarrhoea and other gastrointestinal bicarbonate losses","Early or advanced chronic kidney disease with reduced ammoniagenesis","Ureteral diversion into bowel","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor or other drug effect","Dilutional acidosis from rapid saline infusion","Addison disease or hypoaldosteronism from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum electrolytes with calculation of the plasma anion gap to confirm a normal-gap acidosis","Simultaneous urine pH and urine electrolytes to calculate the urine anion gap and assess ammonium excretion","Serum potassium to help classify the subtype (low in types 1 and 2, high in type 4)","Urinalysis for glycosuria, proteinuria, and evidence of proximal tubular dysfunction","Renal imaging for nephrocalcinosis or stones, especially in distal RTA","Plasma renin and aldosterone when a hyperkalaemic (type 4) picture is suspected"],"redFlags":["Severe hypokalaemia causing muscle weakness, paralysis, or arrhythmia in distal or proximal RTA","Dangerous hyperkalaemia in type 4 RTA, particularly with concurrent potassium-retaining drugs","Growth failure, rickets, or osteomalacia in children and adults from chronic acidosis and phosphate loss","Recurrent stones or progressive nephrocalcinosis threatening renal function"],"prognosis":"With appropriate alkali and electrolyte correction most patients do well, and complications such as bone disease, stones, and growth impairment can be prevented or limited. Outcome depends heavily on the underlying cause; type 4 RTA linked to diabetic nephropathy tends to progress with the primary kidney disease, while inherited distal RTA requires lifelong therapy.","pitfalls":["Attributing a normal anion gap acidosis to diarrhoea without checking the urine anion gap or urine ammonium","Giving bicarbonate in proximal RTA without adequate potassium, precipitating worsening hypokalaemia","Overlooking type 4 RTA as a cause of unexplained hyperkalaemia in a diabetic patient","Failing to search for Fanconi syndrome features when proximal RTA is diagnosed","Assuming a high urine pH always indicates distal RTA without excluding urinary infection with urea-splitting organisms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","KDIGO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"renovascular_disease":{"name":"Renovascular Disease","system":"Renal/Genitourinary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Renal artery stenosis","Renovascular hypertension","Atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis","ARAS","Ischemic nephropathy"],"chapter":289,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2150","clinicalPearls":["Renovascular disease is a specifically treatable 'secondary' cause of hypertension because restoring renal perfusion pressure can reverse the pressor pathways it activates (Harrison 22e p.2150).","Roughly 90% of renovascular hypertension is due to atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis (ARAS), which is typically unilateral with a discrete lesion near the renal artery origin in older patients with a broader atherosclerotic risk profile (Harrison 22e p.2139, 2150).","Fibromuscular dysplasia (FMD) accounts for a minority of cases and characteristically affects younger patients aged ~15 to 50, predominantly women; lesions are more diffuse with an irregular 'sawtooth/string-of-beads' appearance located in the distal renal artery (Harrison 22e p.2139, 2150).","FMD should be suspected with abrupt-onset hypertension (especially in young women) or resistant/recently worsening hypertension; ARAS should be suspected in older patients with other ASCVD evidence or otherwise unexplained decline in kidney function (Harrison 22e p.2139).","Critical stenosis (usually >70 to 80% luminal obstruction) reduces perfusion pressure, activating the renin-angiotensin system, reducing sodium excretion, and engaging sympathetic pathways (Harrison 22e p.2150).","Early renovascular hypertension is angiotensin-dependent and marked by widely varying pressures, loss of circadian BP rhythm, and accelerated target organ injury including left ventricular hypertrophy and renal fibrosis (Harrison 22e p.2150).","An abdominal renal artery bruit, or carotid/femoral bruits, may be audible and supports the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2139, 2150).","When large-vessel disease primarily threatens kidney function the entity is termed ischemic nephropathy; gradually reduced flow can leave function reduced but stable for years, whereas advanced disease produces cortical hypoperfusion and overt tissue hypoxia (Harrison 22e p.2150).","ARAS commonly coexists with preexisting small-vessel disease from hypertension, aging, and diabetes; nearly 85% of patients considered for revascularization already have stage 3 to 5 CKD (eGFR <60) (Harrison 22e p.2150).","The presence of ARAS independently predicts cardiovascular morbidity and mortality regardless of whether revascularization is performed (Harrison 22e p.2150).","Atheroembolic renal disease most often follows arterial procedures (e.g., coronary angiography), with cholesterol crystals lodging in downstream microvessels; manifestations typically appear 1 to 14 days after the inciting event (Harrison 22e p.2152).","Thromboembolic renal disease can cause flank pain, fever, leukocytosis, nausea and vomiting, and a transient extreme rise in LDH if infarction occurs; bilateral involvement causes precipitous functional decline and reduced urine output (Harrison 22e p.2153)."],"pathophysiology":"The renal cortex receives high arteriolar flow in excess of its metabolic needs for filtration, while the postglomerular medullary circulation has lower flow yet high oxygen consumption, leaving the deeper medulla near the margin of hypoxemia. Large-vessel occlusive disease (atherosclerosis, fibromuscular dysplasia, dissection, extrinsic compression, aortic stent graft) reduces perfusion pressure to the kidney. Once stenosis is hemodynamically significant (typically >70 to 80% lumen occlusion), the drop in perfusion pressure activates the renin-angiotensin system, reduces sodium excretion, and stimulates sympathetic adrenergic pathways, producing angiotensin-dependent systemic hypertension. Sustained pressure and ischemia drive endothelial injury (reflected by increased urinary albumin excretion), microvascular proliferation in early atherosclerosis and later microvascular rarefaction in chronic ischemia, with consequent tubulointerstitial fibrosis and loss of function. In atheroembolic disease, cholesterol crystals from fractured plaque occlude microvessels leaving a characteristic 'cleft' on biopsy (Harrison 22e p.2150-2152).","additionalDifferentials":["Essential (primary) hypertension","Renal parenchymal disease","Primary aldosteronism","Pheochromocytoma","Hypertensive nephrosclerosis / arteriolonephrosclerosis","Atheroembolic renal disease","Thromboembolic renal disease / renal infarction"],"infectionMimics":["Renal infarction from septic emboli (e.g., subacute bacterial endocarditis vegetations)","Thrombotic renal occlusion precipitated by systemic infection such as COVID-19"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Renal parenchymal disease causing secondary hypertension","Primary aldosteronism","Hypertensive nephrosclerosis","Cholesterol atheroembolism mimicking vasculitis (livedo reticularis, eosinophilia, elevated ESR, hypocomplementemia)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Duplex (Doppler) ultrasonography: peak systolic renal artery velocity >200 cm/s suggests >60% occlusion (some trials use >300 cm/s to reduce false positives); renal resistive index informs kidney viability but is operator-dependent","Contrast-enhanced CT angiography: excellent vascular images and assessment of accessory/distal vessels; stents do not cause artifacts; small contrast toxicity risk","Magnetic resonance angiography: non-nephrotoxic but gadolinium is avoided when eGFR <30 due to nephrogenic systemic fibrosis risk; cannot visualize stented vessels","Captopril renography (99mTc-MAG3): strong negative predictive value when entirely normal; limited utility with advanced atherosclerosis or creatinine >2.0 mg/dL","Intraarterial (digital subtraction) angiography: gold standard for large-vessel disease, often performed at time of planned intervention","Plasma renin activity is subject to timing, drugs, and sodium intake and does not reliably predict response to revascularization; renal venous renin sampling is now rarely used due to false results","Kidney biopsy: confirms atheroembolic disease (cholesterol-crystal cleft in microvessels)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive or resistant hypertension failing optimal medical therapy","Progressive decline in GFR during BP treatment, or decline associated with ACE inhibitor/ARB therapy","Recurrent flash pulmonary edema / congestive heart failure not explained by LV dysfunction","Abrupt-onset hypertension in a young woman (suggests FMD) or worsening kidney function in an older patient with ASCVD (suggests ARAS)","New worsening hypertension and deteriorating renal function days after an arterial/angiographic procedure (atheroembolism), possibly reaching a malignant phase","Acute flank pain with fever and markedly elevated LDH suggesting renal infarction; anuria with bilateral acute occlusion"],"prognosis":"FMD patients are usually younger with otherwise normal vessels and long life expectancy and often respond well to angioplasty; FMD seldom threatens kidney function. Untreated ARAS progresses in nearly 50% of cases over 5 years, sometimes to total occlusion, though intensive BP and statin therapy slow progression. After endovascular stenting renal function recovers in only ~25%, is unchanged in ~50%, and deteriorates in the remainder. ARAS independently predicts cardiovascular events and death. Atheroembolic renal disease carries an ominous prognosis with 1-year mortality exceeding 38%; untreated malignant hypertension historically had 1-year mortality over 90% (and >50% at 6 to 12 months with target organ injury), but current 5-year survival exceeds 50% with effective antihypertensive therapy (Harrison 22e p.2150-2154).","pitfalls":["Plasma renin activity is transient and influenced by drugs/sodium/timing, so it does not reliably predict response to revascularization; over-reliance on it is misleading (Harrison 22e p.2151).","Multiple randomized trials show no compelling benefit of revascularization over optimal medical therapy for moderate ARAS regarding BP or renal function over 3 to 5 years; revascularization should not be assumed beneficial just because flow is restored (Harrison 22e p.2139, 2150).","Revascularization rarely normalizes BP alone because most patients have preexisting hypertension and need continued drug therapy (Harrison 22e p.2150).","Atheroembolic complications of revascularization can be catastrophic and accelerate the very hypertension and renal failure the procedure intended to prevent (Harrison 22e p.2151).","Atheroembolic renal disease is frequently underdiagnosed and is often a diagnosis of exclusion; systemic features (fever, abdominal pain, weight loss) are present in fewer than half of patients (Harrison 22e p.2152).","Hypertensive nephrosclerosis is often assigned without specific etiologic proof; nephrosclerosis changes also occur with aging, dyslipidemia, and glucose intolerance with only modest association with BP, and antihypertensive therapy does not alter its course (Harrison 22e p.2154)."],"severityClassification":"No formal staging is given; severity is gauged by degree of luminal stenosis (hemodynamically critical at >70 to 80% occlusion), CKD stage (most revascularization candidates are stage 3 to 5 with eGFR <60), and clinical syndrome (stable medically managed disease versus rapidly progressive/resistant disease). Table 289-2 outlines clinical factors favoring revascularization plus medical therapy (progressive GFR decline, medical failure of BP control, GFR decline with ACEi/ARB, recurrent unexplained heart failure) versus medical therapy with surveillance (controlled BP with stable function, stable non-progressing stenosis, advanced age/limited life expectancy, high atheroembolic risk, concomitant parenchymal disease with proteinuria) (Harrison 22e p.2152).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2150","Harrison 22e p.2151","Harrison 22e p.2152","Harrison 22e p.2153","Harrison 22e p.2154","Harrison 22e p.2139"],"crossLinks":[]},"respiratory_acidosis":{"name":"Respiratory acidosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["respiratory acidosis","hypercapnic acidosis","CO2 retention","ventilatory failure"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Respiratory acidosis results from alveolar hypoventilation, causing carbon dioxide retention and a fall in arterial pH","Arterial blood gases show a raised PaCO2 with low pH; bicarbonate rises as compensation develops","Acute and chronic forms are distinguished by the degree of renal (metabolic) compensation, with bicarbonate rising modestly in acute and substantially in chronic disease","Clinical features range from headache, drowsiness, and confusion to asterixis and, in severe hypercapnia, coma (CO2 narcosis)","Causes include central respiratory depression (drugs, CNS lesions), neuromuscular weakness, airway obstruction, and severe parenchymal or chest wall disease","Management targets the underlying cause and restoration of adequate alveolar ventilation, sometimes with non-invasive or invasive ventilation","In chronic CO2 retainers, excessive supplemental oxygen can blunt respiratory drive and worsen hypercapnia, so oxygen is titrated carefully","Correcting hypercapnia too rapidly risks post-hypercapnic metabolic alkalosis if the compensatory bicarbonate is not accounted for"],"pathophysiology":"Carbon dioxide is generated by metabolism and normally excreted by alveolar ventilation, keeping PaCO2 constant. When ventilation is inadequate relative to CO2 production, PaCO2 rises; through the carbonic acid equilibrium this increases hydrogen ion concentration and lowers pH. The body compensates in two phases: acutely, tissue and blood buffers slightly raise bicarbonate, while over days the kidneys enhance hydrogen ion excretion and bicarbonate reabsorption, producing a larger rise in bicarbonate that returns pH toward normal in chronic respiratory acidosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Metabolic alkalosis with compensatory hypoventilation","Mixed acid-base disorders","Acute versus chronic respiratory acidosis differentiation","Causes of acute hypercapnic respiratory failure (opioid overdose, acute severe asthma, COPD exacerbation)","Neuromuscular disorders such as Guillain-Barre syndrome or myasthenic crisis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Arterial blood gas analysis to confirm hypercapnia and assess pH and bicarbonate","Assessment of the expected compensation to identify mixed disorders","Chest imaging and pulmonary function or bedside respiratory assessment to find the cause","Investigations for central, neuromuscular, or airway causes as clinically directed","Serum electrolytes to evaluate accompanying metabolic derangements"],"redFlags":["Depressed conscious level or CO2 narcosis indicating severe hypercapnia","Rapidly rising PaCO2 with falling pH suggesting impending ventilatory failure","Signs of exhaustion in a patient with increased work of breathing","Severe acidaemia causing haemodynamic instability"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the underlying cause and its reversibility; acute respiratory acidosis from a rapidly correctable insult often resolves fully, whereas chronic hypercapnia reflects advanced or progressive disease, and acute-on-chronic decompensation can be life-threatening without prompt ventilatory support.","pitfalls":["Giving high-flow oxygen indiscriminately to a chronic CO2 retainer and precipitating worsening hypercapnia","Failing to recognise acute-on-chronic decompensation by misreading compensation","Missing a coexisting metabolic disorder in a mixed acid-base picture","Delaying ventilatory support in a tiring patient with rising PaCO2"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"restless_legs_syndrome":{"name":"Restless legs syndrome","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RLS","Willis-Ekbom disease","restless leg syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by an irresistible urge to move the legs, usually with uncomfortable sensations, that is partly or wholly relieved by movement","Symptoms worsen at rest and in the evening or night, disrupting sleep onset and contributing to daytime fatigue","Most patients also have periodic limb movements of sleep, which can be documented on polysomnography","Iron deficiency is a key and treatable contributor, so serum ferritin and transferrin saturation should be checked even when the haemoglobin is normal","Common secondary associations include pregnancy, chronic kidney disease and uraemia, and peripheral neuropathy","Certain drugs, including many antidepressants, antihistamines, and dopamine antagonists, can precipitate or aggravate symptoms","Treatment principles include iron repletion when stores are low, alpha-2-delta ligands, and dopaminergic agents, with attention to non-pharmacological measures","Long-term dopamine agonist use can lead to augmentation, a worsening and earlier onset of symptoms that requires careful management"],"pathophysiology":"Restless legs syndrome is a sensorimotor disorder linked to dysfunction of central dopaminergic transmission and to abnormal brain iron handling, since iron is a cofactor for dopamine synthesis. Reduced iron availability within the central nervous system, even with normal peripheral stores, is thought to impair dopaminergic signalling in circuits including the spinal cord and diencephalon. There is a strong genetic contribution, and it may be primary or secondary to iron deficiency, uraemia, pregnancy, or neuropathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Peripheral neuropathy and radiculopathy","Nocturnal leg cramps","Akathisia, particularly drug-induced","Peripheral vascular disease and venous insufficiency","Positional discomfort and habitual foot tapping","Periodic limb movement disorder without the sensory urge"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ferritin and transferrin saturation to assess iron status","Renal function tests to identify uraemia","Blood glucose and evaluation for peripheral neuropathy where clinically suspected","Review of medications that may provoke symptoms","Polysomnography in selected cases, particularly to document periodic limb movements or exclude other sleep disorders"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive or asymmetric sensory or motor deficits suggesting a neuropathy or radiculopathy rather than primary RLS","Augmentation on dopaminergic therapy with earlier, more intense, and spreading symptoms","Symptoms secondary to unrecognised chronic kidney disease or severe iron deficiency","Severe sleep deprivation with significant mood disturbance or impaired daytime function"],"prognosis":"Primary restless legs syndrome is usually a chronic condition that can fluctuate over time, often worsening with age. Secondary forms may resolve when the underlying cause, such as iron deficiency or pregnancy, is corrected. Most patients achieve good symptom control with appropriate treatment, though augmentation can complicate long-term dopaminergic therapy.","pitfalls":["Failing to check and correct iron status, missing a readily treatable driver of symptoms","Overlooking culprit medications that provoke or worsen the syndrome","Relying on long-term dopamine agonists and precipitating augmentation","Confusing the condition with akathisia, cramps, or neuropathy and treating the wrong problem"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"restrictive_cardiomyopathy":{"name":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RCM","restrictive heart disease","infiltrative cardiomyopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Restrictive cardiomyopathy is characterised by stiff, non-compliant ventricles causing impaired diastolic filling with preserved or near-preserved systolic function and normal or reduced ventricular cavity size early on","The dominant physiology is diastolic dysfunction with raised filling pressures, so patients present with signs of both right- and left-sided heart failure, prominent among them systemic venous congestion","Causes include infiltrative diseases such as amyloidosis, storage disorders, endomyocardial fibrosis, and post-radiation or idiopathic fibrosis; amyloidosis is a leading cause to actively exclude","A key clinical challenge is distinguishing restrictive cardiomyopathy from constrictive pericarditis, because the latter is potentially curable with surgery","Atrial enlargement is characteristic because the atria bear the burden of filling stiff ventricles, predisposing to atrial fibrillation","Multimodality imaging including echocardiography and cardiac MRI, sometimes with biopsy or specific tests for amyloid, is used to establish the cause","Management is largely directed at the underlying cause and at cautious control of congestion, since these patients are sensitive to changes in filling","Because the ventricles depend on adequate preload, excessive diuresis or loss of atrial contribution can precipitate a marked fall in output"],"pathophysiology":"In restrictive cardiomyopathy the ventricular myocardium or endocardium becomes abnormally stiff, either from infiltration by abnormal material (as in amyloidosis or storage disease), fibrosis (as in endomyocardial fibrosis or after radiation), or idiopathic processes. The stiff ventricle resists filling, so filling pressures rise sharply for only small increases in volume and most filling occurs rapidly in early diastole. This raises atrial and venous pressures, dilates the atria, and produces pulmonary and systemic congestion while systolic ejection is initially preserved. As disease advances, systolic function and conduction may also be affected, and the physiology can closely mimic constrictive pericarditis.","additionalDifferentials":["Constrictive pericarditis","Cardiac amyloidosis and other infiltrative diseases","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy in a non-dilated phase","Hypertensive heart disease with severe diastolic dysfunction","Right heart failure from other causes","Pericardial effusion with tamponade physiology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography demonstrating restrictive filling with biatrial enlargement and preserved cavity size","Cardiac MRI for tissue characterisation and to help identify infiltration","Specific evaluation for amyloidosis, including relevant imaging, laboratory testing, and sometimes biopsy","Invasive haemodynamic study when distinguishing restriction from constriction is difficult","12-lead ECG, which may show low voltages or conduction disease in infiltrative disease","Endomyocardial biopsy in selected cases to establish the underlying cause"],"redFlags":["Progressive heart failure refractory to therapy indicating advanced disease","Haemodynamic deterioration after aggressive diuresis due to preload dependence","Conduction disease or arrhythmia, particularly with infiltrative causes such as amyloidosis, raising the risk of syncope or sudden death","Features suggesting cardiac amyloidosis, which carries specific prognostic and treatment implications"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is largely determined by the underlying cause; idiopathic and infiltrative forms, especially advanced cardiac amyloidosis, often carry a poor outlook, whereas identifying a treatable cause or distinguishing curable constrictive pericarditis can markedly change the trajectory.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish restrictive cardiomyopathy from potentially curable constrictive pericarditis","Missing cardiac amyloidosis as an underlying cause","Over-diuresing a preload-dependent ventricle and precipitating a fall in output","Attributing raised filling pressures to systolic failure when systolic function is preserved"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"retinal_detachment":{"name":"Retinal detachment","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RD","detached retina","rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","RRD","tractional retinal detachment","exudative retinal detachment"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic warning symptoms are sudden floaters and flashing lights (photopsia) followed by a curtain or shadow spreading across the field of vision","Central vision is preserved until the macula detaches ('macula-on' versus 'macula-off'), which is the key prognostic and timing factor","The commonest type is rhegmatogenous, caused by a retinal tear that lets fluid pass under the retina; tractional and exudative types have different mechanisms","Risk factors include high myopia, previous cataract surgery, ocular trauma, lattice degeneration, and a detachment in the fellow eye","Fundoscopy shows an elevated, mobile, often corrugated grey retina; a relative afferent pupillary defect may be present in large detachments","Treatment is surgical (laser/cryo for tears, pneumatic retinopexy, scleral buckle, or vitrectomy) and is prioritised urgently when the macula is still attached","A posterior vitreous detachment with a fresh tear can be treated with prophylactic retinopexy to prevent progression"],"pathophysiology":"In rhegmatogenous retinal detachment, a full-thickness retinal break (usually from vitreous traction as the vitreous separates with age) allows liquefied vitreous to track through the tear into the potential space between the neurosensory retina and the retinal pigment epithelium, lifting the photoreceptor layer off its blood supply. Tractional detachment occurs when contracting fibrovascular membranes (as in proliferative diabetic retinopathy) pull the retina forward, and exudative detachment when fluid accumulates beneath an intact retina from inflammatory, vascular, or neoplastic processes. Separated photoreceptors become ischaemic and, if not reattached, degenerate.","additionalDifferentials":["Posterior vitreous detachment without tear","Vitreous haemorrhage","Retinoschisis","Choroidal detachment or mass","Central serous chorioretinopathy","Migraine with visual aura"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated indirect ophthalmoscopy with scleral indentation to locate breaks","Slit-lamp examination and assessment for pigment cells in the anterior vitreous (tobacco dust)","B-scan ultrasonography when the view is obscured by haemorrhage or cataract","Optical coherence tomography to determine macular status","Assessment of visual acuity and for a relative afferent pupillary defect"],"redFlags":["Sudden new floaters and flashes with a visual field defect — sight-threatening, refer urgently","A 'macula-on' detachment is a surgical emergency because prompt repair preserves central vision","Rapidly enlarging shadow or curtain indicating extension toward the macula","New symptoms in the fellow eye of a patient with previous detachment"],"prognosis":"Anatomical reattachment is achieved in most cases with modern surgery, but final visual outcome depends heavily on whether the macula was detached and for how long before repair.","pitfalls":["Dismissing floaters and flashes without a dilated peripheral retinal examination","Delaying referral in a macula-on detachment and allowing the macula to detach","Missing a small peripheral break because the retina was not examined with indentation","Attributing symptoms to migraine or vitreous floaters without excluding a tear"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"retinitis_pigmentosa":{"name":"Retinitis pigmentosa","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RP","rod-cone dystrophy","hereditary retinal dystrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A group of inherited retinal dystrophies in which rod photoreceptors degenerate first, so patients present with night blindness (nyctalopia) and progressive peripheral field loss","Field constriction advances to tunnel vision, with central acuity typically preserved until late unless the macula becomes involved","The classic fundus triad is bone-spicule pigmentation in the mid-periphery, waxy pallor of the optic disc, and attenuated retinal arterioles","It may be isolated or part of a syndrome, notably Usher syndrome (with sensorineural deafness) and Bardet-Biedl syndrome, so associated systemic features should be sought","Inheritance can be autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, or X-linked, which influences prognosis and genetic counselling","Common complications include posterior subcapsular cataract and cystoid macular oedema, both of which are treatable and can improve residual vision","Management focuses on genetic counselling, low-vision support, monitoring for treatable complications, and emerging gene-based therapies for specific mutations"],"pathophysiology":"Retinitis pigmentosa comprises inherited dystrophies caused by mutations in numerous genes essential to photoreceptor and retinal pigment epithelium function. Progressive apoptosis of rod photoreceptors, followed later by cones, leads to loss of peripheral and night vision that spreads centrally over time. Degenerating photoreceptors and migration of retinal pigment epithelial cells into the retina produce the characteristic bone-spicule pigmentation, while secondary changes cause arteriolar narrowing and optic disc pallor. The underlying genetic defect determines the pattern of inheritance and the rate of progression.","additionalDifferentials":["Cone-rod dystrophy","Choroideremia","Gyrate atrophy","Leber congenital amaurosis","Pigmentary retinopathy secondary to drug toxicity (e.g. phenothiazines), infection, or inflammation","Congenital stationary night blindness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Electroretinography to demonstrate reduced or extinguished rod and later cone responses","Visual field testing to document peripheral constriction","Optical coherence tomography to assess the macula and detect cystoid macular oedema","Fundus autofluorescence imaging to map areas of degeneration","Genetic testing to identify the causative mutation and guide counselling and therapy","Systemic evaluation, including audiology, when a syndromic form is suspected"],"redFlags":["Sudden or accelerated central visual loss suggesting macular oedema or another superimposed problem","Associated hearing loss indicating Usher syndrome requiring multidisciplinary care","Systemic features pointing to a syndromic association needing broader evaluation","New symptoms inconsistent with the expected slow progression, prompting search for another cause"],"prognosis":"Retinitis pigmentosa is generally slowly progressive, and although many patients retain useful central vision into adulthood, most eventually experience severe peripheral field loss; prognosis varies with genotype and inheritance pattern.","pitfalls":["Overlooking treatable complications such as cystoid macular oedema and cataract that can improve vision","Failing to identify a syndromic association such as Usher or Bardet-Biedl syndrome","Not offering genetic counselling and testing to patients and at-risk relatives","Mistaking a pigmentary retinopathy from drug toxicity or prior inflammation for hereditary disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"retinoblastoma":{"name":"Retinoblastoma","system":"Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RB","retinal blastoma"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common primary intraocular malignancy of childhood, usually diagnosed before 5 years of age.","Leukocoria (a white pupillary reflex, sometimes first noticed in photographs) is the most common presenting sign and warrants urgent ophthalmology referral.","Strabismus is the second most common presentation and should never be dismissed without excluding an intraocular cause.","Disease may be unilateral or bilateral; bilateral and multifocal tumours are almost always heritable.","Heritable retinoblastoma arises from germline mutation of the RB1 tumour suppressor gene and carries a lifelong risk of second malignancies, especially osteosarcoma.","Diagnosis is made clinically by an experienced ophthalmologist under examination, supported by imaging; biopsy is avoided because of the risk of tumour seeding.","Treatment aims to save life first, then preserve the eye and vision, using approaches such as chemotherapy, focal therapies, and enucleation for advanced disease.","Children with heritable disease and affected families need genetic counselling and long-term surveillance for new tumours and secondary cancers."],"pathophysiology":"Retinoblastoma results from biallelic inactivation of the RB1 tumour suppressor gene in developing retinal cells. In heritable disease one mutated allele is inherited or arises in the germline, and a second somatic hit inactivates the remaining allele, producing early, often bilateral, multifocal tumours (consistent with the two-hit hypothesis). Loss of functional RB protein removes control of the cell cycle at the G1-S checkpoint, driving unregulated proliferation of retinal precursor cells and tumour formation.","additionalDifferentials":["Coats disease","Persistent fetal vasculature (persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous)","Toxocariasis (ocular larva migrans)","Retinopathy of prematurity","Congenital cataract","Endophthalmitis or other causes of leukocoria"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed dilated fundus examination under anaesthesia by an ocular oncology team","Ocular ultrasound and MRI of the orbits and brain","RB1 genetic testing and genetic counselling","Examination of the fellow eye and screening of at-risk relatives","Evaluation for metastatic or trilateral (pineal) disease when clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Leukocoria in any child requires urgent same-pathway ophthalmology referral to exclude retinoblastoma","New-onset strabismus without an obvious benign cause","Rapidly progressive proptosis or an inflamed, painful eye suggesting advanced or extraocular spread","Bilateral disease or a family history indicating heritable retinoblastoma","Signs of intracranial (trilateral) disease such as neurological deterioration"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and modern therapy, survival in high-resource settings exceeds 95 percent for intraocular disease; extraocular or metastatic spread markedly worsens outcome. Children with heritable disease remain at elevated lifelong risk of second malignancies.","pitfalls":["Dismissing leukocoria or a subtle white reflex noticed in photographs as artefact","Attributing strabismus solely to a benign squint without a dilated fundus examination","Delaying urgent referral, which can be the difference between saving and losing an eye or life","Overlooking the need for genetic testing and family screening in apparently unilateral disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"retinopathy_of_prematurity":{"name":"Retinopathy of prematurity","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ROP","retrolental fibroplasia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A proliferative retinal vascular disorder affecting premature, low-birthweight infants, and a leading preventable cause of childhood blindness","The principal risk factors are low gestational age, low birthweight, and prolonged or unregulated supplemental oxygen exposure","Screening of at-risk preterm infants according to gestational age and birthweight thresholds is essential because early disease is asymptomatic","Severity is described by zone (I to III, indicating how posterior the disease is), stage (1 to 5, from a demarcation line to total retinal detachment), and the presence of 'plus' disease (dilated, tortuous posterior vessels)","'Plus' disease and posterior (zone I) involvement are the key markers of aggressive disease needing treatment","Treatment of threshold or type 1 disease is with laser ablation of the avascular peripheral retina, with intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy used in selected cases","Advanced untreated disease progresses to tractional retinal detachment (stages 4 and 5) with severe visual loss","Survivors remain at increased lifelong risk of myopia, strabismus, amblyopia, and late retinal detachment, requiring ongoing follow-up"],"pathophysiology":"Retinopathy of prematurity results from disordered development of the retinal vasculature in the incompletely vascularised preterm retina. In an initial phase, the relatively hyperoxic extrauterine environment (worsened by supplemental oxygen) suppresses vascular growth factors and halts normal vessel growth, leaving peripheral avascular retina. As the metabolically active retina matures, the resulting hypoxia drives excessive vascular endothelial growth factor production and pathological neovascularisation at the junction of vascular and avascular retina. These new vessels can bleed and form fibrovascular tissue, exerting traction that leads to retinal detachment.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial exudative vitreoretinopathy","Persistent fetal vasculature (persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous)","Incontinentia pigmenti with retinal involvement","Coats disease","Congenital retinal fold or other developmental vitreoretinopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured screening dilated indirect ophthalmoscopy of at-risk preterm infants at defined intervals","Documentation of zone, stage, and presence of plus disease at each examination","Wide-field retinal imaging where available to aid documentation and telemedicine review","Longer-term assessment of refraction, ocular alignment, and amblyopia in survivors"],"redFlags":["Zone I disease or the presence of plus disease indicating aggressive, treatment-requiring retinopathy","Rapidly progressive posterior (aggressive) ROP that can advance to detachment quickly","Any signs of tractional retinal detachment (stage 4 or 5)","Missed or delayed screening in eligible infants, risking preventable blindness"],"prognosis":"Mild ROP frequently regresses spontaneously with a good visual outcome, but severe disease can progress to retinal detachment and blindness if untreated. Timely screening and treatment markedly reduce the risk of severe visual loss, though treated eyes and preterm survivors remain prone to refractive error, strabismus, and amblyopia.","pitfalls":["Failing to screen eligible preterm infants or examining them too late to intervene","Underestimating aggressive posterior disease that lacks the classic ridge appearance","Poor regulation of supplemental oxygen increasing the risk and severity of disease","Discharging treated infants without long-term follow-up for refractive and detachment risks"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"reye_syndrome":{"name":"Reye Syndrome","system":"Neurological","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Reye's syndrome","acute encephalopathy with fatty liver"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare but potentially fatal disorder marked by acute non-inflammatory encephalopathy occurring alongside fatty change in the liver and other organs.","Classically follows a viral illness, especially influenza or varicella, during the recovery phase.","Salicylate (aspirin) exposure during a preceding viral infection is a well-recognised association, and the sharp decline in incidence tracks with public advice to avoid aspirin in children.","Typical evolution is protracted vomiting followed by progressive neurological deterioration: irritability or lethargy advancing to confusion, delirium, seizures, and coma.","Liver dysfunction is present but jaundice is characteristically absent or minimal, which can be a useful discriminating clue.","Laboratory findings include raised transaminases, hyperammonaemia, prolonged prothrombin time, and often hypoglycaemia in younger children.","In any child presenting with this picture, an underlying inborn error of metabolism (particularly a fatty acid oxidation or urea cycle defect) must be excluded, as these mimic Reye syndrome.","Management is supportive with a focus on controlling raised intracranial pressure, correcting metabolic derangements, and protecting cerebral perfusion.","Prognosis correlates strongly with the depth of coma and the degree of hyperammonaemia at presentation."],"pathophysiology":"The core lesion is acute, generalised mitochondrial injury. Diffuse mitochondrial dysfunction in the liver impairs the urea cycle and fatty acid oxidation, producing microvesicular fatty infiltration of hepatocytes, hyperammonaemia, and disturbed intermediary metabolism. In the brain this mitochondrial failure, combined with hyperammonaemia, drives cytotoxic cerebral oedema and raised intracranial pressure, which underlie the encephalopathy. Salicylate metabolites are thought to potentiate mitochondrial injury in susceptible children following a viral trigger.","additionalDifferentials":["Inborn errors of metabolism (fatty acid oxidation defects, urea cycle disorders, organic acidaemias)","Toxic ingestion or drug-induced hepatotoxicity","Acute encephalitis or meningitis","Fulminant viral hepatitis","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Haemorrhagic shock and encephalopathy syndrome"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ammonia","Liver transaminases, bilirubin, and coagulation profile","Blood glucose monitoring","Metabolic screen: plasma amino acids, urine organic acids, acylcarnitine profile","Neuroimaging to assess cerebral oedema and exclude other causes","Lumbar puncture only when safe and to exclude CNS infection","Liver biopsy in atypical or diagnostically uncertain cases"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive encephalopathy or declining conscious level (EMERGENCY)","Signs of raised intracranial pressure: abnormal posturing, pupillary changes, bradycardia with hypertension","Marked or rising hyperammonaemia","Seizures or status epilepticus","Coagulopathy with bleeding","Progression to coma requiring airway protection and intensive care"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on how advanced the encephalopathy is at presentation; children who remain in earlier stages often recover well, whereas deep coma and severe hyperammonaemia carry high risk of death or lasting neurological deficit. Early recognition and aggressive management of intracranial pressure improve survival.","pitfalls":["Attributing the illness to Reye syndrome without excluding an inborn error of metabolism, which is now a more common cause of this presentation","Being reassured by absent jaundice despite significant hepatic derangement","Delaying management of raised intracranial pressure while awaiting confirmatory tests","Overlooking hypoglycaemia, especially in infants and young children","Failing to elicit a history of aspirin or salicylate-containing product use"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"rh_isoimmunization":{"name":"Rhesus Isoimmunisation","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Rh isoimmunization","rhesus alloimmunisation","Rh(D) sensitisation","haemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rhesus isoimmunisation occurs when a Rh(D)-negative mother is exposed to Rh(D)-positive fetal red cells and develops anti-D antibodies that can cross the placenta in subsequent pregnancies","Sensitising events include delivery, miscarriage, termination, ectopic pregnancy, antepartum bleeding, abdominal trauma, and invasive procedures such as amniocentesis","Because sensitisation usually produces no maternal symptoms, prevention and surveillance depend on routine antenatal blood grouping and antibody screening","Prophylactic anti-D immunoglobulin given after sensitising events and routinely in the third trimester has dramatically reduced the incidence of the disease","Once a woman is sensitised, anti-D immunoglobulin is no longer protective, and management focuses on monitoring the fetus for anaemia","Fetal anaemia is assessed non-invasively by Doppler measurement of the middle cerebral artery peak systolic velocity, which rises as anaemia develops","Severe fetal anaemia can be treated by intrauterine transfusion, and timing of delivery is balanced against the severity of disease","Other red cell antibodies such as anti-Kell and anti-c can also cause severe haemolytic disease, so screening is not limited to the D antigen"],"pathophysiology":"When Rh(D)-positive fetal red cells enter the circulation of a Rh(D)-negative mother, her immune system mounts an antibody response, initially IgM and then IgG anti-D. In a later pregnancy with a Rh(D)-positive fetus, maternal IgG crosses the placenta, binds fetal red cells, and leads to their destruction in the fetal reticuloendothelial system. The resulting fetal anaemia drives extramedullary haematopoiesis and, when severe, high-output cardiac failure with generalised oedema known as hydrops fetalis, alongside accumulation of bilirubin that threatens the neonate after birth.","additionalDifferentials":["Haemolytic disease from non-D red cell antibodies such as anti-Kell or anti-c","ABO incompatibility causing neonatal haemolysis","Fetal anaemia from parvovirus B19 infection","Fetomaternal haemorrhage from other causes","Alpha thalassaemia causing hydrops","Non-immune hydrops fetalis from cardiac or structural causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Maternal blood group and antibody screen at booking and repeated during pregnancy","Quantification and titre of anti-D and other clinically significant antibodies","Paternal and, where available, fetal Rh(D) genotyping including cell-free fetal DNA testing","Serial middle cerebral artery Doppler peak systolic velocity to detect fetal anaemia","Ultrasound assessment for signs of hydrops","Kleihauer or flow cytometry to estimate the volume of fetomaternal haemorrhage"],"redFlags":["Rising antibody titres or Doppler velocities indicating progressive fetal anaemia and risk of fetal compromise","Ultrasound evidence of hydrops fetalis signalling severe disease","A significant sensitising event without timely anti-D prophylaxis","Reduced fetal movements or non-reassuring fetal assessment in a sensitised pregnancy","Previous pregnancy affected by severe haemolytic disease, predicting earlier and more severe recurrence"],"prognosis":"With routine antenatal screening, effective anti-D prophylaxis, non-invasive monitoring, and intrauterine transfusion, most affected pregnancies now achieve good outcomes. Prognosis worsens with early-onset severe anaemia and hydrops, and sensitised pregnancies tend to be affected earlier and more severely in successive gestations, making specialist surveillance essential.","pitfalls":["Omitting anti-D prophylaxis after a sensitising event or assuming it helps an already sensitised woman","Focusing only on anti-D and overlooking other antibodies such as anti-Kell","Relying on antibody titres alone without fetal Doppler surveillance in established sensitisation","Underestimating small but sensitising fetomaternal haemorrhages","Failing to quantify large fetomaternal haemorrhage so that the anti-D dose is inadequate"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"rheumatic_heart_disease":{"name":"Rheumatic heart disease","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["RHD","chronic rheumatic valvular disease","post-rheumatic valve disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Rheumatic heart disease is the chronic valvular sequel of acute rheumatic fever, an immune-mediated complication of group A streptococcal infection, and remains a major cause of valve disease in many parts of the world","The mitral valve is most often affected, and mitral stenosis is the most characteristic lesion, though mixed mitral and aortic valve involvement is common","Patients may be asymptomatic for years before developing exertional breathlessness, fatigue, palpitations from atrial fibrillation, or features of heart failure","Although the valve damage itself is non-infective, these valves are prone to infective endocarditis, which must be considered when there is fever or clinical deterioration","Mitral stenosis predisposes to atrial fibrillation and left atrial thrombus, and to systemic embolism including stroke","Echocardiography is central to assessing valve morphology, severity, and haemodynamic consequences","Prevention relies on prompt treatment of streptococcal pharyngitis and long-term secondary antibiotic prophylaxis to prevent recurrent rheumatic fever","Advanced disease may require balloon valvuloplasty for suitable mitral stenosis, or surgical repair or valve replacement"],"pathophysiology":"Acute rheumatic fever follows group A streptococcal pharyngitis in susceptible individuals, in whom molecular mimicry between streptococcal antigens and host tissue drives a cross-reactive immune response affecting the heart, joints, skin, and nervous system. Recurrent or severe carditis produces chronic inflammation and scarring of the valves, with leaflet thickening, commissural fusion, and chordal shortening. Over years this yields stenosis, regurgitation, or mixed dysfunction, most commonly of the mitral valve. The resulting pressure and volume loading leads to atrial enlargement, atrial fibrillation, pulmonary hypertension, and eventually heart failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Degenerative (calcific) valve disease","Congenital valve abnormalities such as bicuspid aortic valve","Infective endocarditis affecting native valves","Non-rheumatic mitral regurgitation from prolapse or ischaemia","Other causes of mitral stenosis such as severe annular calcification","Cardiomyopathy presenting with functional valve regurgitation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic and, when needed, transoesophageal echocardiography to define valve morphology and severity","12-lead ECG to detect atrial fibrillation and chamber enlargement","Chest radiography for chamber size, pulmonary congestion, and valve calcification","Evidence of prior streptococcal infection and inflammatory markers in suspected acute disease","Assessment of pulmonary pressures and right heart consequences","Blood cultures and endocarditis workup when infection is suspected"],"redFlags":["Fever or systemic features suggesting superimposed infective endocarditis on a damaged valve","New atrial fibrillation with embolic risk, or acute systemic embolism such as stroke","Acute pulmonary oedema or rapidly progressive heart failure","Haemoptysis or severe pulmonary hypertension in advanced mitral stenosis"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the valves involved, severity, and access to secondary prophylaxis and intervention; secondary prophylaxis and timely valve intervention allow many patients to do well, but advanced disease with pulmonary hypertension, atrial fibrillation, and heart failure carries significant morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Overlooking superimposed infective endocarditis when a patient with known rheumatic valve disease develops fever","Failing to maintain long-term secondary antibiotic prophylaxis to prevent recurrent rheumatic fever","Underestimating stroke risk from atrial fibrillation and left atrial thrombus in mitral stenosis","Delaying valve intervention until irreversible pulmonary hypertension or ventricular dysfunction has developed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"rickettsial_diseases":{"name":"Rickettsial Diseases","system":"Infectious / Multisystem (vasculotropic)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Rickettsioses","Spotted fever and typhus group infections","Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Mediterranean spotted fever (boutonneuse fever)","African tick-bite fever","Rickettsialpox","Epidemic (louse-borne) typhus","Endemic (murine) typhus","Scrub typhus","Ehrlichiosis (HME)","Anaplasmosis (HGA)","Q fever"],"chapter":192,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1454","clinicalPearls":["Rickettsiae are small, obligately intracellular, gram-negative coccobacilli/short bacilli, most spread by a tick, mite, flea, or louse; except for louse-borne typhus, humans are incidental hosts. The agents span seven genera (Rickettsia, Orientia, Ehrlichia, Anaplasma, Neorickettsia, Candidatus Neoehrlichia, and Coxiella).","During the first roughly 5 days the acute presentations are largely indistinguishable: fever, headache, and myalgia, with or without nausea, vomiting, and cough; later differentiating features such as rash, eschar, pneumonitis, or meningoencephalitis emerge. Heightened clinical suspicion based on exposure history should prompt empiric antibiotics rather than waiting for confirmation (p.1454).","A characteristic laboratory triad supports the diagnosis across many of these infections: thrombocytopenia, a normal or low white-cell count, mildly to moderately elevated hepatic aminotransferases, and hyponatremia (p.1454).","Coxiella burnetii, R. prowazekii, and R. typhi can survive long periods outside the host and are highly infectious by inhalation (a single Coxiella organism can cause pneumonia), making several agents bioterrorism concerns (p.1454).","In Rocky Mountain spotted fever the rash is progressive and unreliable: present in only about 14% on day 1 and roughly 49% by day 3, appearing on day 6 or later in ~20% and never appearing in about 10% of cases; classic palm/sole involvement tends to come late and may not occur at all (p.1456-1457).","Only about 3% of RMSF patients show the classic triad of fever, rash, and tick exposure in the first 3 days, and only ~60% recall a tick bite; serology is typically negative at presentation, so treatment must not await a positive result (p.1457).","An eschar (tache noire) at the bite site is a useful clue in several spotted fevers (notably African tick-bite fever ~90%, rickettsialpox, Mediterranean spotted fever ~50%) and in scrub typhus, but is absent in the typhus group (epidemic and murine typhus have no eschar) (p.1455).","Ehrlichioses/anaplasmoses (Anaplasmataceae) target hematopoietic cells: E. chaffeensis (HME) and E. muris infect mononuclear phagocytes, while E. ewingii and A. phagocytophilum (HGA) infect neutrophils; intracytoplasmic morulae may be seen on blood smear but only in a minority (about 20% or less in HME, 20-75% in HGA) (p.1460-1462).","Of the Rickettsiaceae, in descending case-fatality order the life-threatening species are R. rickettsii (RMSF), R. prowazekii (louse-borne typhus), Orientia tsutsugamushi (scrub typhus), R. conorii (MSF), and R. typhi (murine typhus); many spotted-fever species (e.g., R. parkeri, R. africae, R. akari, R. felis) have never been documented to cause a fatal illness (p.1456).","Of the rickettsial agents, only C. burnetii is documented to cause chronic illness, and only R. prowazekii causes a recrudescent illness (Brill-Zinsser disease) when latent infection reactivates years after the acute episode (p.1454, p.1459).","Q fever is an inhalational zoonosis from sheep, goats, and cattle (organism shed heavily in parturition material); the infectious dose is extremely low (probably 1-10 organisms) and ~60% of infections are asymptomatic seroconversions. Acute disease presents geographically as either pneumonia or hepatitis (p.1462).","Scrub typhus shows remarkable antigenic diversity with rapid loss of cross-protective immunity, so reinfection can occur within weeks to months; the classic eschar/rash/lymphadenopathy triad is often absent, with under half of travelers developing an eschar and under 40% a rash (p.1460)."],"pathophysiology":"The spotted fever and typhus group organisms (Rickettsia and Orientia) infect vascular endothelial cells and disseminate lymphohematogenously, producing widespread small-vessel infection. The fundamental injury is increased vascular permeability rather than occlusive thrombosis or ischemic necrosis; resulting edema, hypovolemia, and tissue ischemia drive the clinical picture. Platelet consumption causes thrombocytopenia in roughly a third to half of RMSF cases, but overt DIC with hypofibrinogenemia is rare; thrombin generation and fibrinolysis appear to be homeostatic responses to nonocclusive hemostatic plugs at sites of endothelial injury. Hyponatremia reflects appropriate ADH secretion in response to hypovolemia. Pathogenic rickettsiae have small, reductively evolved genomes lacking biosynthetic genes for intracellularly available molecules. Ehrlichia and Anaplasma instead parasitize vacuoles of hematopoietic cells; vasculitis and granulomas are not features of HME or HGA. Coxiella burnetii is genetically distinct from the Rickettsiaceae, survives harsh environments, escapes macrophage killing by inhibiting the final step of phagosome maturation, and adapts to the acidic phagolysosome.","additionalDifferentials":["Influenza and other viral febrile illnesses","Enteroviral infection and infectious mononucleosis","Viral hepatitis","Leptospirosis","Typhoid (enteric) fever","Gram-negative or gram-positive bacterial sepsis / toxic shock syndrome","Meningococcemia and disseminated gonococcal infection","Measles (rubeola), rubella, secondary syphilis","Drug hypersensitivity reaction","Immune thrombocytopenic purpura and thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Kawasaki disease","Immune-complex vasculitis","Bacterial or viral meningoencephalitis (when CNS involvement predominates)","Acute abdomen / enterocolitis (when GI symptoms predominate, has prompted laparotomy)","Culture-negative endocarditis (chronic Q fever)"],"infectionMimics":["Other rickettsioses within the group (RMSF vs HME, HGA, murine typhus, sylvatic flying-squirrel typhus, rickettsialpox can mimic one another)","Epidemic typhus is frequently misdiagnosed as typhoid fever in tropical settings","HGA can mimic atypical or severe Lyme disease and patients may develop Lyme antibodies without true co-infection","Leptospirosis, dengue, and other tropical febrile illnesses","Q fever pneumonia mimics community-acquired pneumonia of other etiologies; Q fever hepatitis mimics other granulomatous hepatitides"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Immune thrombocytopenic purpura / thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Kawasaki disease","Drug hypersensitivity / drug rash","Immune-complex (leukocytoclastic) vasculitis","Acute abdomen prompting exploratory laparotomy when abdominal pain and tenderness dominate"],"additionalInvestigations":["Indirect immunofluorescence (IFA) serology on paired acute and convalescent sera is the mainstay; for RMSF a titer of >=64 is not detectable until ~7-10 days after onset (IgG sensitivity 89-100%, specificity 99-100%), so it is usually negative at presentation","Immunohistologic (immunohistochemical) examination of a 3-mm punch biopsy of a rash lesion for R. rickettsii is ~70% sensitive and 100% specific - the most useful test during acute RMSF","PCR on skin/eschar biopsy or swab (higher yield than blood); PCR of peripheral blood is generally insufficiently sensitive for RMSF and typhus because organisms are concentrated in endothelium, not circulation","Peripheral-blood smear for intracytoplasmic morulae (ehrlichiosis/anaplasmosis); cell-culture isolation is feasible but rarely done due to technical difficulty and biohazard","For Q fever, IFA is the serologic gold standard distinguishing acute (phase II IgG seroconversion, fourfold rise) from chronic disease (high phase I IgG, e.g. titer >512 or >=1:800/1:1024); ELISA and complement fixation are alternatives; PCR on blood/serum can be positive in the first 1-2 weeks","For chronic Q fever, imaging including transesophageal echocardiography (higher vegetation detection than transthoracic) and FDG-PET/CT to detect valvular, intravascular, osteomyelitic, or lymphadenitic foci","Supportive labs: CBC (thrombocytopenia, leukopenia/neutropenia), hepatic aminotransferases, serum sodium, acute-phase proteins (CRP, procalcitonin, ferritin, fibrinogen), creatine kinase in suspected myositis; CSF analysis when meningoencephalitis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Petechial or hemorrhagic rash, especially with palm/sole involvement, in a febrile patient with possible tick exposure (advanced RMSF)","Progressive worsening over days after an initial viral-seeming presentation - mandates re-evaluation, as RMSF mimics viral illness early","Altered mental status, confusion, lethargy, stupor, ataxia, seizures, or coma (meningoencephalitis)","Noncardiogenic pulmonary edema, ARDS, or need for mechanical ventilation","Hypotension, sepsis/septic-shock-like or toxic-shock-like presentation with multiorgan failure","Fulminant RMSF (death within 5 days), seen most often in Black male patients with G6PD deficiency","Acute kidney injury / oliguria, jaundice, gastrointestinal bleeding (hematemesis/melena)","Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis or a DIC-like picture in ehrlichiosis/anaplasmosis","Immunocompromise (diabetes, cancer, transplant, asplenia, hepatitis C, HIV, immunosuppressive drugs) markedly raises risk of severe/fatal ehrlichiosis and anaplasmosis","Culture-negative endocarditis, infected aneurysm, or infected vascular prosthesis suggesting chronic Q fever"],"prognosis":"Outcome depends heavily on early recognition and prompt empiric therapy. RMSF mortality was 20-25% before antibiotics and is roughly 3-5% now, principally driven by diagnostic/treatment delay; confirmed-case fatality is higher (~9-10% in regions where R. rickettsii is the sole spotted-fever species), is highest in children under 10 and adults over 70, and approaches 50% in parts of Mexico and Brazil for unknown reasons. Untreated fatal RMSF typically dies 8-15 days after onset. Survivors usually recover fully, but severe disease can leave permanent neurologic deficits or gangrene requiring amputation. Mediterranean spotted fever has a severe form (~50% mortality) in patients with diabetes, alcoholism, or heart failure. Epidemic (louse-borne) typhus is fatal in 7-40% of untreated cases. Untreated classic scrub typhus has ~6% case-fatality. Acute Q fever case-fatality is ~1-2%; chronic Q fever carries high mortality (~25% in proven Dutch-outbreak cases, highest at 33% when both endocarditis and vascular infection are present). Q fever fatigue syndrome follows up to 20% of acute cases. Many SFG species (e.g., R. parkeri, R. africae, R. akari, R. felis) have never caused documented fatal illness, and African tick-bite fever is typically mild.","pitfalls":["Waiting for serologic confirmation before treating - serology is usually negative at acute presentation; delay worsens outcome and empiric doxycycline should not be withheld","Excluding RMSF because there is no rash or no recalled tick bite (~10% never rash; only ~60% recall a bite) or because palms/soles are not involved","Interpreting a single acute-phase positive titer as diagnostic in endemic areas - cross-reactive preexisting antibody (e.g., from the highly prevalent R. amblyommatis or in HGA-endemic regions) can mislead; the recent apparent RMSF surge largely reflects cross-reactive single-titer enzyme immunoassays rather than true R. rickettsii disease","Relying on blood PCR for RMSF or typhus - insufficiently sensitive because organisms reside in endothelium, not blood; skin/eschar biopsy is preferable","Assuming negative HME serology because E. chaffeensis tests can cross-react when the true agent is another Ehrlichia; separate specific tests are needed for HME vs HGA","Overlooking chronic Q fever as a cause of culture-negative endocarditis or unexplained infected aneurysm/vascular prosthesis - median latency from primary infection is ~13 months and can exceed 9 years","Mislabeling epidemic typhus as typhoid fever in tropical/resource-limited settings","Attributing prolonged post-Q-fever fatigue to active infection and prescribing antibiotics - a randomized trial showed long-term doxycycline did not reduce fatigue versus placebo"],"severityClassification":"No single unified rickettsial severity score; severity is judged by organ-system involvement and progression. Across the group, escalating clinical lines after nonspecific fever are: (1) macular/maculopapular rash; (2) eschar at the bite site; (3) vesicular rash; (4) pneumonitis with radiographic opacities/rales; (5) meningoencephalitis; and (6) progressive hypotension and multiorgan failure (sepsis/toxic-shock-like). For Q fever, the Dutch consensus group classifies chronic disease as proven, probable, or possible based on PCR positivity in blood/tissue or phase I IgG IFA >=1:800/1:1024 combined with endocarditis (modified Duke criteria), proven large-vessel/prosthetic infection on imaging, or compatible clinical and histologic findings.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1454","Harrison 22e p.1455","Harrison 22e p.1456","Harrison 22e p.1457","Harrison 22e p.1458","Harrison 22e p.1459","Harrison 22e p.1460","Harrison 22e p.1461","Harrison 22e p.1462","Harrison 22e p.1463"],"crossLinks":[]},"rosacea":{"name":"Rosacea","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acne rosacea","rhinophyma (severe phymatous form)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic facial condition centred on the convexities of the central face (cheeks, nose, chin, forehead), typically in fair-skinned middle-aged adults","Cardinal features are persistent central facial erythema, flushing, telangiectasia, and inflammatory papules and pustules","The absence of comedones is a key point distinguishing rosacea from acne vulgaris","Recognise clinical subtypes: erythematotelangiectatic (flushing and redness), papulopustular, phymatous (skin thickening such as rhinophyma), and ocular","Ocular rosacea (grittiness, dryness, blepharitis, conjunctival injection) is common, frequently underdiagnosed, and can threaten vision if untreated","Trigger avoidance is central: sun exposure, heat, hot drinks, spicy food, alcohol, and emotional stress commonly provoke flares","Treatment is tailored to subtype, using topical anti-inflammatory agents, oral tetracycline-class antibacterials for their anti-inflammatory effect in papulopustular disease, and laser or light therapy for telangiectasia and erythema","Daily photoprotection and gentle skin care are important adjuncts for all patients"],"pathophysiology":"Rosacea is a chronic inflammatory disorder of uncertain cause with several contributing mechanisms. Dysregulation of the innate immune system with abnormal cathelicidin antimicrobial peptide processing promotes inflammation and vascular changes. Neurovascular dysregulation drives flushing and persistent erythema, while an increased density of Demodex mites and altered cutaneous microbiome may stimulate the inflammatory response. Ultraviolet radiation, chronic inflammation, and vascular and lymphatic changes contribute to telangiectasia and, over time, the fibrous and sebaceous overgrowth of phymatous disease. Genetic predisposition and environmental triggers modulate expression.","additionalDifferentials":["Acne vulgaris (presence of comedones)","Perioral dermatitis","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Systemic lupus erythematosus (malar rash)","Flushing from carcinoid syndrome or menopause","Contact or photodermatitis of the face"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on morphology and distribution","Ophthalmological assessment when ocular involvement is suspected","Skin biopsy only in atypical cases to exclude lupus or other diagnoses","Investigation for systemic causes of flushing when the history is atypical","Consideration of Demodex assessment in treatment-resistant papulopustular disease"],"redFlags":["Ocular rosacea with pain, photophobia, or visual disturbance suggesting keratitis","Rapidly progressive facial swelling or induration requiring reassessment","Features suggesting lupus or systemic disease rather than rosacea","Disfiguring phymatous change causing functional or psychological impact"],"prognosis":"Rosacea is a chronic relapsing condition that is controllable but not curable; symptoms often persist for years with fluctuating severity, and phymatous changes, once established, tend to be permanent without procedural treatment.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing rosacea as acne and prescribing inappropriate comedolytic-only regimens","Overlooking ocular rosacea and risking corneal complications","Applying potent topical corticosteroids, which can trigger or worsen a rosacea-like eruption","Neglecting trigger identification and photoprotection, leading to persistent flares"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"rotator_cuff_tear":{"name":"Rotator cuff tear","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["rotator cuff rupture","supraspinatus tear","cuff tear","rotator cuff injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with shoulder pain, weakness on elevation and abduction, and difficulty with overhead activities; supraspinatus is the most commonly torn tendon","Tears may be degenerative (attritional, in older patients) or traumatic (e.g. after a fall or dislocation, especially in those over 40)","A painful mid-range arc of abduction and positive cuff tests (e.g. weakness of resisted abduction or external rotation, drop-arm sign) support the diagnosis","Ultrasound and MRI are the key investigations, distinguishing partial from full-thickness tears and assessing tendon retraction and muscle atrophy","Many degenerative partial or small full-thickness tears are managed conservatively with physiotherapy, activity modification, and analgesia","Acute traumatic tears in younger, active patients and large tears with functional loss are generally considered for surgical repair, ideally before significant retraction and fatty muscle degeneration","Chronic massive, irreparable tears may lead to cuff-tear arthropathy, sometimes treated with reverse shoulder arthroplasty","New weakness after shoulder dislocation in an older patient should prompt assessment for an acute cuff tear, not just presumed axillary nerve injury"],"pathophysiology":"The rotator cuff (supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis) stabilises the humeral head in the glenoid and enables elevation and rotation. Tendon integrity is compromised by age-related degeneration, hypovascularity near the supraspinatus insertion, and mechanical impingement beneath the coracoacromial arch, making tears increasingly common with age. Repetitive overhead loading and acute trauma superimpose on this degeneration. Once a tear occurs, the tendon tends to retract and the corresponding muscle undergoes atrophy and fatty infiltration over time, reducing repairability and allowing superior migration of the humeral head, which can eventually produce cuff-tear arthropathy.","additionalDifferentials":["Subacromial impingement or bursitis without tear","Frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis)","Calcific tendinitis","Glenohumeral or acromioclavicular osteoarthritis","Cervical radiculopathy","Axillary or suprascapular nerve injury","Biceps tendon pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound of the shoulder to assess cuff integrity dynamically","MRI to characterise tear size, retraction, and muscle quality (fatty atrophy)","Plain radiographs to assess acromial morphology, humeral head position, and arthropathy","Clinical cuff-specific strength tests (e.g. empty-can, external rotation, lift-off, drop-arm)","MR arthrography where partial or labral lesions are suspected"],"redFlags":["Acute loss of active shoulder movement after trauma or dislocation suggesting an acute repairable tear","Weakness that could reflect nerve injury (axillary or suprascapular) requiring differentiation from cuff rupture","Signs of infection in a hot, swollen, systemically unwell shoulder","Progressive functional loss and pseudoparalysis indicating a large or massive tear"],"prognosis":"Many small and degenerative tears remain functionally acceptable with rehabilitation, but larger or acute traumatic tears repaired promptly generally do better than chronic retracted tears with fatty muscle degeneration; untreated massive tears can progress to cuff-tear arthropathy with persistent weakness and pain.","pitfalls":["Assuming all post-dislocation weakness in older patients is axillary nerve palsy and missing an acute cuff tear","Delaying repair of an acute traumatic tear until retraction and fatty atrophy make it irreparable","Relying on radiographs alone, which do not show the tendon","Confusing frozen shoulder with a cuff tear by not distinguishing passive from active range restriction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"rotavirus_gastroenteritis":{"name":"Rotavirus Gastroenteritis","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["rotavirus diarrhoea","rotavirus infection","viral gastroenteritis (rotavirus)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A leading cause of severe dehydrating diarrhoea in infants and young children, especially between about 3 and 24 months of age.","Typically presents with abrupt onset of vomiting followed by profuse watery, non-bloody stools, often with fever.","Spread is faecal-oral; the virus is highly contagious and stable in the environment, so outbreaks in daycare and hospital settings are common.","Assessment of hydration status guides management far more than laboratory testing, which is rarely needed for diagnosis.","Oral rehydration therapy is the cornerstone of treatment for mild to moderate dehydration; intravenous fluids are reserved for severe dehydration or failed oral therapy.","Early refeeding with an age-appropriate diet is encouraged once rehydration is achieved; prolonged fasting and dilute feeds are discouraged.","Antibiotics and antimotility agents have no role and may be harmful; routine antiemetics are generally avoided in young children.","Live oral rotavirus vaccines given in infancy substantially reduce severe disease and hospitalisation.","Antigen immunoassay or PCR on stool can confirm the pathogen when confirmation is required for epidemiologic or infection-control purposes."],"pathophysiology":"Rotavirus infects the mature enterocytes at the tips of the small intestinal villi, causing villous blunting and loss of absorptive surface. This produces malabsorption of nutrients and water with an osmotic diarrhoea, while the viral nonstructural protein NSP4 acts as an enterotoxin that triggers secretory diarrhoea and disrupts calcium homeostasis. The combination of impaired absorption, secretion, and reduced disaccharidase (including lactase) activity leads to voluminous watery stool and rapid fluid and electrolyte losses.","additionalDifferentials":["Norovirus and other viral gastroenteritis","Bacterial enteritis (Salmonella, Shigella, Campylobacter, enterotoxigenic E. coli)","Parasitic infection (Giardia, Cryptosporidium)","Antibiotic-associated diarrhoea","Systemic infection presenting with diarrhoea (e.g. urinary tract infection, sepsis)","Surgical abdomen (intussusception) when vomiting and lethargy predominate"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool rotavirus antigen enzyme immunoassay or PCR when confirmation is needed","Serum electrolytes, urea, and creatinine in moderate to severe dehydration","Capillary or venous blood gas to assess acid-base status in severe illness","Blood glucose in a lethargic or severely dehydrated child","Stool culture if bacterial infection is suspected (blood, mucus, high fever)"],"redFlags":["Signs of severe dehydration or hypovolaemic shock","Persistent vomiting preventing oral rehydration","Altered consciousness, lethargy, or seizures","Bloody stools or high fever suggesting alternative or complicating diagnosis","Abdominal distension or severe pain raising concern for intussusception"],"prognosis":"Most children recover fully within about a week with appropriate rehydration; mortality is low where fluid therapy is accessible but remains significant in resource-limited settings due to severe dehydration.","pitfalls":["Underestimating fluid deficit and failing to reassess hydration during treatment","Using dilute or lactose-heavy feeds and prolonged fasting instead of prompt refeeding","Prescribing antibiotics or antimotility drugs that offer no benefit","Missing intussusception in a child with vomiting, lethargy, and abdominal signs"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"rubella_german_measles":{"name":"Rubella (German Measles)","system":"Infectious Diseases (viral exanthem / congenital infection)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["German measles","three-day measles","Rubivirus infection","Congenital rubella syndrome","CRS"],"chapter":212,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1643","clinicalPearls":["Caused by rubella virus, an enveloped single-stranded positive-sense RNA virus of the Matonaviridae family (genus Rubivirus); only a single antigenic type exists and humans are the only known reservoir (Harrison 22e p.1643).","Acquired rubella typically presents as a mild generalized maculopapular rash lasting up to about 3 days, but as many as roughly half of infections are subclinical or occur without a rash (Harrison 22e p.1643).","Incubation period averages 14 days (range about 12-23 days); in older children and adults a 1- to 5-day prodrome of low-grade fever, malaise, and upper respiratory symptoms may precede the rash, whereas in young children rash is often the first sign (Harrison 22e p.1643).","Occipital and postauricular lymphadenopathy is characteristic and may appear during the second week after exposure (Harrison 22e p.1643).","Arthralgia and arthritis are common complications in infected adults, particularly women; thrombocytopenia and encephalitis are less common (Harrison 22e p.1643).","Persons with acquired rubella may shed virus from about 7 days before rash onset to roughly 5-7 days afterward, and both clinical and subclinical infections are considered contagious (Harrison 22e p.1643).","The most serious consequence is congenital rubella syndrome (CRS), which results from maternal infection during pregnancy, especially the first trimester; the classic triad is cataracts, hearing impairment, and heart defects (Harrison 22e p.1644).","Hearing impairment is the single most common defect of CRS, and the classic complete triad is seen in only about 10% of CRS infants (Harrison 22e p.1644).","Maternal infection within the first 10 weeks of gestation carries roughly a 90% risk of delivering an infant with CRS; risk declines later in pregnancy and fetal defects are rare after about 16 weeks, though sensorineural hearing deficit can still occur with infection up to about 20 weeks (Harrison 22e p.1644-1645).","Infants with CRS can shed large quantities of virus (notably from throat secretions and urine) for up to 1 year, so only rubella-immune individuals should care for them (Harrison 22e p.1643).","Reinfection with rubella virus, although uncommon, is possible and may produce detectable IgM antibodies (Harrison 22e p.1644).","Rubella is no longer endemic in the United States (endemic transmission interrupted by 2001), but importations and sporadic cases continue, so vigilance is warranted in adults migrating from or returning from areas without rubella control (Harrison 22e p.1643)."],"pathophysiology":"Rubella virus spreads person to person via respiratory droplets. After primary implantation and replication in the nasopharynx it spreads to regional lymph nodes, followed by viremia. In pregnant women the viremia frequently infects the placenta; placental replication can then seed fetal organs. In the infected fetus nearly all organs may be involved, and the hallmark is a chronic infection that persists throughout intrauterine development and for up to 1 year after birth. Tissue effects of rubella virus range from no obvious impact to cell destruction. The pathology of postnatal (acquired) rubella is limited because the disease is mild, whereas the pathology of CRS is well defined even though its pathogenesis remains poorly delineated (Harrison 22e p.1643).","additionalDifferentials":["Measles (rubeola)","Scarlet fever","Roseola (exanthem subitum)","Toxoplasmosis","Fifth disease (erythema infectiosum / parvovirus B19)","Zika virus infection","Other febrile illnesses with suboccipital and postauricular lymphadenopathy"],"infectionMimics":["Measles (rubeola)","Roseola","Fifth disease (parvovirus B19)","Toxoplasmosis","Zika virus infection","For CRS: other intrauterine (TORCH) infections such as cytomegalovirus and toxoplasmosis producing nonspecific neonatal findings"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Scarlet fever (toxin-mediated streptococcal exanthem clinically similar to rubella)","Other drug or hypersensitivity maculopapular eruptions"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serology: rubella IgM in an acute-phase serum specimen, or a fourfold rise in IgG titer between acute- and convalescent-phase sera; IgM-capture ELISA is regarded as most accurate (Harrison 22e p.1644).","Timing of paired sera: acute specimen within 7-10 days of illness onset, convalescent specimen about 14-21 days later; repeat IgM testing if an early specimen (before day 5 after rash) is negative (Harrison 22e p.1644).","IgG avidity testing to distinguish primary infection (low-avidity antibodies, recent infection) from reinfection (mature high-avidity IgG indicating infection at least ~2 months earlier); especially useful in pregnant women to assess CRS risk (Harrison 22e p.1644).","RT-PCR for viral RNA detection (preferred from nasopharyngeal swab; throat swab or urine acceptable), collected as soon as possible after onset, ideally 1-3 days and no later than 7 days; virus isolation by culture also possible (Harrison 22e p.1644).","CRS confirmation: serum IgM (normally present up to ~6 months, sometimes up to 1 year; may be absent at birth and require retesting at 1 month), and persistence of rubella IgG beyond the expected decline of passively transferred maternal antibody (titer not declining at the expected twofold-per-month rate); RT-PCR/culture of nasopharyngeal, throat, or urine specimens, most likely positive within the first 6 months (Harrison 22e p.1644).","Routine prenatal screening for rubella IgG; a positive IgG indicates immunity (Harrison 22e p.1644-1645)."],"redFlags":["Maternal rubella infection during the first trimester (highest risk of CRS, up to ~90% within the first 10 weeks of gestation) (Harrison 22e p.1644).","A susceptible pregnant woman with exposure to or symptoms of a rubella-like illness, requiring IgM testing and low-avidity IgG confirmation (Harrison 22e p.1645).","A neonate with cataracts, hearing impairment, congenital heart defects, or 'blueberry muffin' purpura suggestive of CRS (Harrison 22e p.1644).","Encephalitis or significant thrombocytopenia complicating acquired rubella (Harrison 22e p.1643).","An index case of CRS in a nosocomial or congregate setting, given prolonged viral shedding and outbreak potential (Harrison 22e p.1643)."],"prognosis":"Acquired rubella is generally a mild, self-limited illness; the rash usually resolves within about 3 days, and serious complications such as encephalitis and thrombocytopenia are uncommon. The major prognostic concern is congenital infection: maternal infection in early pregnancy may lead to miscarriage, fetal death, premature delivery, or live birth with severe and often permanent defects (eyes, ears, heart, and CNS). Permanent CRS manifestations include deafness, congenital heart defects, cataracts and other eye defects, microcephaly, and neurodevelopmental sequelae, whereas manifestations such as hepatosplenomegaly, thrombocytopenic purpura, and adenopathy are transient. Immunity following infection or a single dose of rubella-containing vaccine (seroconversion in >=95%) is considered long-term and probably lifelong (Harrison 22e p.1644-1645).","pitfalls":["Relying on clinical diagnosis is unreliable because rubella mimics many rash illnesses, has varied presentations, and is frequently subclinical or mild; laboratory confirmation is the only reliable way to confirm acute disease (Harrison 22e p.1644).","A negative rubella IgM drawn before day 5 after rash onset does not exclude infection and should be repeated (Harrison 22e p.1644).","Rubella IgM testing is not recommended in pregnant women without a history of illness or contact with a rubella-like illness because of the risk of false-positive results (Harrison 22e p.1645).","An infant with suspected CRS who tests IgM-negative shortly after birth should be retested at 1 month, since IgM may not yet be detectable (Harrison 22e p.1644).","Worldwide rubella case counts are likely underestimated because disease is often mild, care may not be sought, cases may go unrecognized or unreported, and some countries detect cases only through measles surveillance (Harrison 22e p.1643).","Live attenuated rubella-containing vaccine should not be given to women known to be pregnant, and pregnancy should be avoided for 28 days after vaccination; however, inadvertent vaccination during pregnancy is not ordinarily a reason to consider termination, as no CRS occurred among ~3000 such pregnancies followed (Harrison 22e p.1645)."],"severityClassification":"No formal severity grading system is described. Clinically, infection is categorized by host and timing: subclinical/mild acquired rubella; symptomatic acquired rubella (rash with possible arthralgia/arthritis, rarely thrombocytopenia or encephalitis); congenital rubella virus infection without apparent signs; and congenital rubella syndrome (CRS) with transient versus permanent manifestations, where fetal risk is greatest with maternal infection in the first 10 weeks of gestation (~90%) and declines markedly after ~16 weeks (Harrison 22e p.1644-1645).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1643","Harrison 22e p.1644","Harrison 22e p.1645","Harrison 22e p.1646"],"crossLinks":[]},"salmonellosis":{"name":"Nontyphoidal Salmonellosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nontyphoidal Salmonella","NTS","nontyphoidal Salmonella gastroenteritis","invasive NTS disease"],"chapter":171,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1314, 1319-1321","clinicalPearls":["NTS infection most often produces a gastroenteritis that is clinically indistinguishable from that caused by other enteric pathogens, with nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea beginning 6-48 h after ingestion of contaminated food or water; stools are usually loose, nonbloody, and of moderate volume, though large-volume watery stools, bloody stools, or frank dysentery can occur (p.1320).","NTS gastroenteritis is usually self-limited: diarrhea resolves within 3-7 days and fever within 72 h; stool cultures may remain positive for 4-5 weeks after infection (p.1320).","Globally S. Typhimurium and S. Enteritidis are the most common serotypes causing human disease; in the United States NTS is the second most common cause of foodborne outbreaks after norovirus (p.1319).","Unlike S. Typhi and S. Paratyphi (human-only reservoir), NTS is acquired from multiple animal and plant reservoirs - especially eggs, poultry, undercooked ground meat, and dairy, plus produce contaminated with animal waste and contact with animals (reptiles, amphibians, chicks, etc.) (p.1319).","Up to 8% of patients with NTS gastroenteritis develop bacteremia, and of those 5-10% develop localized (metastatic) infections; bacteremia and metastatic infection are most common with S. Choleraesuis and S. Dublin and among infants, the elderly, and the immunocompromised, especially those with HIV (p.1320).","Invasive NTS disease (predominantly S. Typhimurium ST313 and S. Enteritidis ST11) is a leading cause of bacteremia in children and HIV-infected adults in sub-Saharan Africa and Southeast Asia; it typically presents as a nonspecific febrile illness resembling enteric fever and uncommonly causes diarrhea (p.1319-1320).","Diagnosis rests on isolating the organism from freshly passed stool or from blood or another normally sterile fluid; culture-independent molecular tests are increasingly used but positive specimens should still undergo primary isolation, and all NTS isolates should be referred to public health for serotyping (p.1320-1321).","Reactive arthritis can follow NTS gastroenteritis, occurring most frequently in persons carrying the HLA-B27 antigen (p.1321).","Localized NTS infections include intraabdominal (hepatic/splenic abscess, cholecystitis), CNS (meningitis - chiefly in infants 1-4 months and HIV-infected adults), pulmonary (lobar pneumonia), urinary/genital, and bone/joint disease; Salmonella osteomyelitis classically affects the femur, tibia, humerus, or lumbar vertebrae and is strongly linked to sickle cell disease and hemoglobinopathies (p.1320-1321)."],"pathophysiology":"All Salmonella infections begin with ingestion of organisms, usually in contaminated food or water; the infectious dose ranges from ~200 to 10^6 CFU, and the ingested dose helps determine incubation period and severity. Conditions reducing gastric acidity (age <1 year, acid-suppression therapy, achlorhydria) or compromising intestinal integrity (inflammatory bowel disease, cytotoxic chemotherapy, prior GI surgery, antibiotic-induced microbiome alteration) increase susceptibility. In contrast to enteric fever (mononuclear infiltration of small-bowel mucosa), NTS gastroenteritis is characterized by massive polymorphonuclear leukocyte infiltration into both large- and small-bowel mucosa. This response depends largely on induction of interleukin 8, a strong neutrophil chemoattractant secreted by intestinal cells in response to NTS colonization and translocation of bacterial proteins and LPS into host cytoplasm with subsequent inflammasome activation; neutrophil degranulation and release of toxic substances damages the mucosa, producing inflammatory diarrhea. NTS persistence is aided by its ability to use tetrathionate (generated from thiosulfate via inflammatory reactive oxygen species) for metabolism in a microaerophilic, inflamed gut, allowing it to outcompete endogenous flora. Unlike typhoidal serotypes, NTS effectively colonizes the intestinal tract rather than evolving as a primarily systemic pathogen.","additionalDifferentials":["Other bacterial enteric pathogens (Campylobacter, Shigella, enteroinvasive/enterotoxigenic E. coli)","Norovirus and other viral gastroenteritis","Intestinal parasites","Pseudoappendicitis (NTS can mimic acute appendicitis)","Inflammatory bowel disease (NTS rarely produces an IBD-like illness)","Enteric (typhoid) fever - especially with invasive NTS presenting as nonspecific febrile illness"],"infectionMimics":["Enteric (typhoid/paratyphoid) fever from S. Typhi/S. Paratyphi presenting as undifferentiated febrile illness","Other causes of community-acquired bloodstream infection in endemic regions (malaria co-infection common)","Other bacterial gastroenteritides (Campylobacter, Shigella, E. coli)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute appendicitis (pseudoappendicitis)","Inflammatory bowel disease","Postinfectious irritable bowel syndrome / functional dyspepsia (recognized sequelae)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool culture (freshly passed) with primary isolation even after culture-independent molecular detection","Blood cultures - obtain whenever fever is prolonged or recurrent; high-grade bacteremia defined as >50% of three or more blood-culture sets positive suggests endovascular infection","Culture-independent molecular diagnostic panels for enteric pathogens","Serotyping of all NTS isolates via public health laboratories","Echocardiography to evaluate suspected endocarditis","CT imaging to localize deep/focal infection","Indium-labeled white-cell scanning to identify localized infection","Culture of joint fluid aspirate, bone biopsy, abscess drainage, or CSF when localized infection is suspected","Antimicrobial susceptibility testing with attention to ciprofloxacin MIC (Etest or alternative method) given the lowered susceptibility breakpoint and decreased-ciprofloxacin-susceptibility strains not reliably detected by nalidixic acid screening or standard disk diffusion"],"redFlags":["Invasive disease / bacteremia and endovascular seeding in the elderly, immunocompromised (including HIV), infants/neonates, and patients with sickle cell disease or other hemoglobinopathies","High-grade or persistent bacteremia, particularly with preexisting valvular heart disease, atherosclerotic vascular disease, prosthetic vascular graft, or aortic aneurysm - suspect NTS endovascular infection","Prolonged fever with back, chest, or abdominal pain after an episode of gastroenteritis in an elderly patient - suspect arteritis/mycotic aneurysm","NTS meningitis in infants 1-4 months of age or HIV-infected adults - severe sequelae and death in up to 60% of cases","Persons >50 years with known or suspected atherosclerosis (increased risk of invasive infection and endovascular seeding)"],"prognosis":"NTS gastroenteritis is usually self-limited, with diarrhea resolving in 3-7 days and fever within 72 h; chronic carriage develops in <1% of cases. Outcomes worsen markedly with invasive disease: endocarditis and arteritis are rare (<1%) but carry serious, potentially fatal complications (valve perforation, endomyocardial abscess, infected mural thrombus, pericarditis, mycotic aneurysm, aneurysm rupture, aortoenteric fistula, vertebral osteomyelitis), and NTS meningitis is fatal in up to 60% of cases. In 2017 there were ~535,000 invasive NTS cases and ~77,500 deaths worldwide, ~80% in sub-Saharan Africa. Acute NTS gastroenteritis was associated with a threefold increased risk of dyspepsia and irritable bowel syndrome at 1 year in a Spanish study.","pitfalls":["Antibiotics should NOT be used routinely to treat uncomplicated NTS gastroenteritis - the illness is usually self-limited and antibiotics do not significantly shorten fever or diarrhea, while they have been associated with increased relapse rates, prolonged fecal/gastrointestinal carriage, and adverse drug reactions.","Only ~1% of clinical isolates ferment lactose, so a high index of suspicion is needed to detect rare lactose-fermenting strains.","Decreased-ciprofloxacin-susceptibility NTS strains may not be reliably detected by nalidixic acid susceptibility testing or standard ciprofloxacin disk diffusion, risking missed resistance and treatment failure.","Neonates, the elderly, and immunosuppressed patients with NTS gastroenteritis are especially prone to dehydration and invasive infection and may need hospitalization and antibiotic therapy - they are exceptions to the no-routine-antibiotics rule."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal graded severity score for NTS. Disease is described along a clinical spectrum: (1) uncomplicated self-limited gastroenteritis; (2) bacteremia (low-grade defined as <50% of blood cultures positive vs high-grade >50% of three or more sets positive, the latter raising concern for endovascular infection); and (3) localized/metastatic infection (intraabdominal, CNS, pulmonary, urinary/genital, bone/joint/soft tissue) and endovascular infection (endocarditis, arteritis). Host-risk stratification for invasive disease includes neonates (up to ~3 months), persons >50 years with atherosclerosis, and those with immunosuppression, cardiac valvular/endovascular abnormalities, or significant joint disease.","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, Chapter 171: Salmonellosis (Pegues DA, Miller SI), pp.1314, 1319-1321","Marchello CS et al: Complications and mortality of non-typhoidal salmonella invasive disease: A global systematic review and meta-analysis. Lancet Infect Dis 22:692, 2022","Medalla F et al: Increased Incidence of Antimicrobial-Resistant Nontyphoidal Salmonella Infections, United States, 2004-2016. Emerg Infect Dis 27:1662, 2021","Onwuezobe IA et al: Antimicrobials for treating symptomatic non-typhoidal Salmonella infection. Cochrane Database Syst Rev 11:CD001167, 2012","Pulford CV et al: Stepwise evolution of Salmonella Typhimurium ST313 causing bloodstream infection in Africa. Nat Microbiol 6:327, 2021"],"crossLinks":["GASTROENTERITIS","ENTERIC_FEVER","DYSENTERY"]},"sarcoidosis_lung":{"name":"Pulmonary sarcoidosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sarcoidosis","pulmonary sarcoidosis","sarcoid","Boeck's sarcoid"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A multisystem granulomatous disorder of unknown cause in which the lungs and intrathoracic lymph nodes are most often involved","Presentations range from asymptomatic bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy found incidentally to cough, dyspnoea, and chest discomfort","Loefgren syndrome (bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, erythema nodosum, arthralgia, and fever) is an acute presentation with a favourable prognosis","Chest imaging is often staged by the presence of hilar adenopathy and parenchymal infiltration, with fibrosis in advanced disease","Diagnosis rests on compatible clinical and radiological features plus biopsy showing non-caseating granulomas, after exclusion of infection and other granulomatous disease","Serum ACE may be raised but is neither sensitive nor specific and should not be relied upon for diagnosis or monitoring","Hypercalcaemia and hypercalciuria can occur due to dysregulated vitamin D activation within granulomas","Many patients remit spontaneously; corticosteroids are reserved for significant or progressive organ involvement, with immunosuppressants as steroid-sparing options"],"pathophysiology":"Sarcoidosis is thought to arise when an unknown antigen triggers an exaggerated cell-mediated immune response in a genetically susceptible host. Antigen-presenting cells activate CD4 T-helper lymphocytes that secrete Th1 cytokines, driving accumulation of macrophages and their organisation into compact non-caseating epithelioid granulomas. In the lung these granulomas cluster along lymphatics and around bronchovascular structures. Granulomas may resolve or, with persistent inflammation, be replaced by fibrosis, causing irreversible architectural distortion and functional impairment.","additionalDifferentials":["Tuberculosis and other mycobacterial disease","Lymphoma and metastatic malignancy","Fungal infections such as histoplasmosis","Berylliosis, which is clinically and histologically similar","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Other interstitial lung diseases"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiography and CT to assess lymphadenopathy, parenchymal involvement, and fibrosis","Biopsy (often via endobronchial ultrasound-guided nodal sampling) to demonstrate non-caseating granulomas","Pulmonary function testing including diffusing capacity to gauge functional impairment","Serum and urinary calcium to detect dysregulated calcium metabolism","Assessment for extrapulmonary involvement including cardiac, ocular, hepatic, and neurological evaluation"],"redFlags":["Symptoms or findings suggesting cardiac sarcoidosis such as arrhythmia, heart block, or heart failure","Neurosarcoidosis with cranial nerve or central nervous system involvement","Significant hypercalcaemia with renal impairment","Progressive pulmonary fibrosis or pulmonary hypertension"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is generally favourable, with a substantial proportion of patients undergoing spontaneous remission, especially in acute presentations such as Loefgren syndrome; however, chronic progressive disease with pulmonary fibrosis, cardiac involvement, or neurosarcoidosis carries greater morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing sarcoidosis without excluding tuberculosis and other infective granulomatous disease","Relying on serum ACE levels for diagnosis or disease monitoring","Missing clinically silent but dangerous cardiac or neurological involvement","Treating asymptomatic or mildly affected patients who may remit without therapy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"scabies":{"name":"Scabies","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sarcoptic itch","seven-year itch","Sarcoptes scabiei infestation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Suspect scabies in any patient with severe, generalised itch that is characteristically worse at night","The pathognomonic lesion is the burrow, a fine serpiginous track most often found in finger webs, wrists, and other warm intertriginous sites","Typical distribution spares the head in adults but involves the face, scalp, palms, and soles in infants","Itch results from a delayed hypersensitivity reaction, so symptoms may take weeks to appear after first infestation but recur within days on re-infestation","It spreads by prolonged skin-to-skin contact, so all close contacts and household members should be treated simultaneously even if asymptomatic","Treatment is with a topical scabicide applied to the whole body per protocol, with laundering of clothing and bedding; oral therapy is useful for outbreaks or when topical treatment is impractical","Crusted (Norwegian) scabies presents with hyperkeratotic crusted plaques, carries an enormous mite burden, is highly contagious, and occurs in immunosuppressed or debilitated patients","Warn patients that itch can persist for weeks after successful eradication (post-scabetic itch) and does not necessarily indicate treatment failure"],"pathophysiology":"Scabies is caused by infestation with the mite Sarcoptes scabiei var. hominis. The fertilised female mite burrows into the stratum corneum, laying eggs and depositing faeces as she tunnels. The clinical eruption and intense pruritus are largely due to a delayed type IV hypersensitivity response to the mite, its eggs, and its excreta, which explains the delay in symptoms after primary infestation. In classic scabies the mite burden is low (typically a small number of adult mites), whereas in crusted scabies impaired immune control permits proliferation of thousands to millions of mites, producing thick hyperkeratotic crusts and extreme infectivity.","additionalDifferentials":["Atopic dermatitis or other eczema","Papular urticaria and insect bite reactions","Dermatitis herpetiformis","Contact dermatitis","Pruritus from systemic causes (renal, hepatic, haematological)","Bullous pemphigoid in older patients with intense itch"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Skin scraping of a burrow examined microscopically for mites, eggs, or faecal pellets","Dermoscopy to visualise the mite at the end of a burrow (delta-wing sign)","Clinical diagnosis supported by history of contacts with similar itch","Skin biopsy occasionally in crusted or atypical disease","Assessment for immunosuppression when crusted scabies is present"],"redFlags":["Crusted (Norwegian) scabies with widespread hyperkeratosis and high transmission risk","Secondary bacterial infection with impetiginisation, and risk of post-streptococcal complications","Institutional or household outbreaks requiring public health and infection-control measures","Immunosuppression predisposing to severe or atypical infestation"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is excellent with correct treatment of the patient and all contacts; failures usually reflect incomplete application, missed contacts, or re-infestation rather than true drug resistance.","pitfalls":["Treating the patient alone and not treating asymptomatic close contacts, leading to reinfestation","Incorrect application of the scabicide, missing sites such as under nails, genitalia, or the feet","Interpreting persistent post-scabetic itch as treatment failure and repeatedly re-treating","Missing crusted scabies in an immunosuppressed or institutionalised patient and allowing an outbreak"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"schistosomiasis":{"name":"Schistosomiasis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bilharzia","trematode infection"],"chapter":241,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1825","clinicalPearls":["Infection is contracted through contact with freshwater bodies harboring infected intermediate-host snails; cercariae penetrate intact human skin within a few minutes (p.1826).","A travel/migration history of freshwater exposure (bathing, washing, fishing, rice-field work, swimming) in an endemic area is central to the diagnosis, especially in travelers (p.1827, p.1829).","Most disease manifestations are due to immunologic granulomatous reactions to eggs retained in host tissues, not to the adult worms; about half of eggs are trapped in tissue (p.1827).","Cercarial dermatitis ('swimmer's itch') is a maculopapular rash from cercarial skin penetration; a severe form follows exposure to avian schistosomes (e.g., lakes in Europe/US) that die in the skin (p.1828).","Acute schistosomiasis (Katayama fever) occurs 2 weeks to 3 months after exposure, usually in first-time-infected travelers, with fever, myalgia, malaise, headache, nonproductive cough, and abdominal symptoms (p.1828).","Eosinophilia is a common finding and is often associated with schistosomiasis; transient pulmonary infiltrates may accompany Katayama fever (p.1828, p.1829).","Intestinal schistosomiasis (S. mansoni, S. japonicum): worms in mesenteric veins; eggs trapped in intestinal tissue cause granulomatous inflammation, microulceration, intermittent abdominal pain, and sometimes bloody diarrhea (p.1829).","Hepatosplenic schistosomiasis (S. mansoni, S. japonicum): late disease produces periportal (Symmers clay-pipestem) fibrosis with portal vein occlusion, portal hypertension, splenomegaly, esophageal varices, and hematemesis; hepatic function usually remains normal (p.1827, p.1829).","Urogenital schistosomiasis (S. haematobium): hallmark of the active stage is painless terminal hematuria, with dysuria and suprapubic discomfort; eggs in bladder mucosa cause inflammation, fibrosis, 'sandy patches', and possible ureteric obstruction with hydronephrosis (p.1829).","S. haematobium is an IARC group 1 carcinogen; chronic infection is associated with squamous cell carcinoma of the urinary bladder (p.1829).","Detection of schistosome eggs in stool or urine indicates active infection and is the standard diagnostic method; S. haematobium eggs (terminal spine) are excreted in urine, other species (S. mansoni lateral spine, S. japonicum small lateral knob) in feces (p.1826, p.1829).","Neuroschistosomiasis is one of the most severe forms: S. mansoni/S. haematobium can cause transverse myelitis (an acute complication in returning travelers), while S. japonicum causes cerebral granulomas with seizures and focal neurologic deficits (p.1829)."],"pathophysiology":"Free-swimming cercariae released from infected freshwater snails penetrate intact skin within minutes, transform into schistosomula, enter veins/lymphatics, traverse the lungs and heart, and mature in the portal vein into paired adult male and female worms that migrate to the mesenteric (S. mansoni, S. japonicum) or pelvic/vesical (S. haematobium) venous plexus. The prepatent period is 5-7 weeks (up to 12 for S. haematobium). About half of the eggs produced are excreted in feces or urine; the remainder become trapped in tissue. Most morbidity results from egg-induced, TH2-driven granulomatous immune responses (lymphocytes, eosinophils, alternatively activated macrophages; IL-4/5/13). Egg antigen excess early in infection forms soluble immune complexes causing a serum sickness-like illness (Katayama fever). Hepatic egg deposition leads to periportal (Symmers) fibrosis, portal-branch occlusion, and portal hypertension; S. haematobium egg deposition in the bladder/ureters causes inflammation, fibrosis, obstructive uropathy, and predisposes to squamous bladder carcinoma (p.1826-1829).","additionalDifferentials":["Malaria (acute febrile illness in returning traveler/migrant) (p.1829)","Salmonellosis (p.1829)","Acute viral hepatitis (p.1829)","Trichinosis (fever with eosinophilia) (p.1829)","Strongyloidiasis (fever with eosinophilia) (p.1829)","Visceral larva migrans / toxocariasis (fever with eosinophilia) (p.1829)","Other causes of portal hypertension and hepatosplenomegaly, including cirrhosis (p.1829)"],"infectionMimics":["Opisthorchis and Clonorchis (liver fluke) infections causing fever and eosinophilia (p.1829)","Invasive ankylostomiasis (hookworm) (p.1829)","Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia (p.1829)","Strongyloidiasis (p.1829)","Malaria (p.1829)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cirrhosis from other causes presenting with portal hypertension, splenomegaly, and variceal bleeding (p.1829)","Bladder squamous cell carcinoma from non-schistosomal causes (chronic urogenital disease) (p.1829)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Microscopic detection of eggs in stool (e.g., ~50 mg) or urine (filtration of ~10 mL) with quantitation (eggs per gram or per 10 mL); concentration methods (e.g., formol-ether) for low-burden travelers (p.1829)","Full blood count showing eosinophilia (p.1829)","Rectal biopsy for eggs (S. mansoni and S. haematobium); occasionally Pap smears and semen samples (S. haematobium) (p.1829)","PCR-based detection of Schistosoma DNA in stool or urine (more sensitive than parasitology); CSF Schistosoma DNA for neuroschistosomiasis (p.1829)","Serology for anti-schistosome antibodies (CDC: FAST-ELISA with S. mansoni microsomal antigen plus confirmatory species-specific immunoblot) - useful in travelers, less so in endemic populations (p.1829-1830)","Circulating anodic/cathodic antigen (CAA/CCA) detection in serum or urine by ELISA or lateral flow as a marker of active infection; point-of-care urine CCA assay used in community screening (p.1830)","Ultrasonography to demonstrate periportal fibrosis, portal vein dilation, and obstructive uropathy/hydronephrosis (p.1829)","Cystoscopy showing 'sandy patches' of calcified eggs in chronic urogenital disease; endoscopy or ultrasound for esophageal varices (p.1829)"],"redFlags":["Hematemesis or melena from esophageal varices in hepatosplenic disease - the most severe complication; death may result from massive blood loss (p.1829)","Acute transverse myelitis or other cerebral/spinal cord manifestations - neuroschistosomiasis, even from light infections (p.1829)","Severe Katayama fever with weight loss, dyspnea, diarrhea, and hepatomegaly; rarely fatal (p.1829)","Painless terminal hematuria in a traveler/migrant from an endemic area (urogenital S. haematobium) (p.1829)","Obstructive uropathy progressing to uremia from ureteral fibrosis (p.1829)"],"prognosis":"Many patients recover spontaneously from acute schistosomiasis after 2-10 weeks, though some follow a more severe course, and the acute syndrome can rarely be fatal. In hepatosplenic disease, hepatic function usually remains normal even with marked periportal fibrosis and portal hypertension; however, severe periportal (Symmers) fibrosis is often irreversible, and hematemesis from variceal bleeding may be fatal. Early inflammatory hepatosplenomegaly in children generally decreases over time as immunity develops. Ureteral fibrosis can cause irreversible obstructive uropathy progressing to uremia. The adult worm lifespan averages 3-5 years but can reach 30 years (p.1826-1829).","pitfalls":["Patients with periportal fibrosis (late hepatosplenic disease) may excrete very few or no eggs in feces, so a negative stool exam does not exclude advanced disease (p.1829).","In the chronic urogenital stage, urinary egg excretion may be sparse or absent despite ongoing urogenital tract pathology (p.1829).","Egg excretion may be very low in people from nonendemic areas (travelers), requiring larger samples and concentration methods (p.1829).","Katayama fever is rare in people chronically exposed in S. mansoni- or S. haematobium-endemic areas, so its absence does not exclude infection in residents (p.1829).","CAA/CCA antigen detection is not currently suitable for diagnosing travelers, who have low worm burdens (p.1830).","Serology is less useful in people from endemic areas with ongoing transmission (p.1829-1830)."],"severityClassification":"Disease is staged clinically by duration and intensity of infection into three phases - acute (cercarial dermatitis and Katayama fever), active/established, and chronic (e.g., late hepatosplenic periportal fibrosis with portal hypertension, or chronic urogenital fibrosis with sandy patches and obstructive uropathy). Severity of early hepatosplenomegaly is closely associated with infection intensity (p.1828-1829).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1825"],"crossLinks":[]},"schizoaffective_disorder":{"name":"Schizoaffective disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["schizoaffective psychosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The concept describes an illness in which a major mood episode (depressive or manic) coexists with psychotic symptoms that also meet criteria characteristic of schizophrenia over the same continuous period","The distinguishing requirement is that delusions or hallucinations must be present for a substantial period in the absence of a prominent mood episode, separating it from mood disorder with psychotic features","Two subtypes are recognised according to whether the mood disturbance is predominantly bipolar or depressive in nature","Diagnosis is longitudinal and often provisional, requiring observation over time, because the relative burden of mood and psychotic symptoms determines the label","Management typically combines an antipsychotic with mood stabilisation or antidepressant treatment guided by the predominant mood pattern","Suicide risk is significant and should be assessed repeatedly, particularly during depressive phases and following resolution of psychosis","Exclude substance-induced psychosis and general medical causes, and confirm that psychotic symptoms are not confined to mood episodes","Psychosocial support, adherence promotion, and long-term follow-up are important because the course is chronic and relapsing"],"pathophysiology":"Schizoaffective disorder is conceptualised as lying on a spectrum between schizophrenia and the mood disorders, sharing neurobiological features with both, including dopaminergic dysregulation implicated in psychosis and monoaminergic and stress-axis disturbances implicated in mood pathology. Genetic vulnerability overlapping with both schizophrenia and bipolar disorder, neurodevelopmental factors, and altered corticolimbic circuitry are thought to contribute.","additionalDifferentials":["Schizophrenia","Bipolar disorder with psychotic features","Major depressive disorder with psychotic features","Substance-induced psychotic disorder","Psychosis due to a general medical condition","Delusional disorder","Schizophreniform disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Longitudinal assessment documenting the temporal relationship of mood and psychotic symptoms","Structured suicide risk assessment","Urine drug screen and substance use history","Baseline metabolic monitoring before and during antipsychotic treatment","Neuroimaging and medical workup where an organic cause is suspected","Collateral history to clarify longitudinal course"],"redFlags":["Active suicidal or homicidal ideation with plan or intent","Command hallucinations directing harm to self or others","Acute manic escalation with dangerous behaviour or impaired judgement","Catatonia, severe self-neglect, or refusal of food and fluids"],"prognosis":"The prognosis of schizoaffective disorder is generally intermediate between that of schizophrenia and the mood disorders, tending to be better than schizophrenia but worse than a pure mood disorder. Outcome is more favourable with predominant mood symptoms, good treatment adherence, and strong psychosocial support, and less favourable with prominent persistent psychosis and poor functioning between episodes.","pitfalls":["Labelling mood disorder with psychotic features as schizoaffective without evidence of psychosis outside mood episodes","Underestimating suicide risk during and after psychotic phases","Neglecting metabolic monitoring during antipsychotic treatment","Missing substance-induced or medical causes of combined mood and psychotic symptoms","Making a firm diagnosis prematurely without longitudinal observation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"schizophrenia":{"name":"Schizophrenia","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["schizophrenic disorder","chronic psychosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chronic psychotic disorder defined by a combination of positive symptoms (delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech and behaviour) and negative symptoms (blunted affect, avolition, social withdrawal), with significant functional decline","Diagnosis requires continuous disturbance over an extended period (on the order of six months) with an active phase of characteristic symptoms, after excluding mood and substance-related causes","Onset is typically in late adolescence or early adulthood, often somewhat earlier and with poorer premorbid function in men","Cognitive impairment (attention, working memory, executive function) is a core feature and a major driver of disability","Antipsychotic medication is the cornerstone of treatment, described here as a principle; clozapine is reserved for treatment-resistant illness and requires haematological monitoring","Comprehensive care combines pharmacotherapy with psychosocial interventions, family education, supported employment, and relapse prevention","Suicide risk is elevated, particularly early in the illness and in those with insight into their deterioration","Duration of untreated psychosis affects outcome, so early recognition and intervention are important"],"pathophysiology":"Schizophrenia is a neurodevelopmental disorder with strong genetic contribution in which aberrant dopaminergic signalling (mesolimbic overactivity linked to positive symptoms and mesocortical hypofunction linked to negative and cognitive symptoms) interacts with glutamatergic and other neurotransmitter abnormalities. Structural and functional brain changes, synaptic pruning abnormalities, and gene-environment interactions such as obstetric complications and cannabis exposure contribute to the emergence of illness.","additionalDifferentials":["Schizoaffective disorder","Bipolar disorder or major depression with psychotic features","Brief psychotic disorder and schizophreniform disorder","Delusional disorder","Substance-induced psychosis","Psychosis secondary to a medical or neurological condition","Autoimmune or paraneoplastic encephalitis presenting with psychosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Substance use screening including cannabis and stimulants","Neuroimaging and, where indicated, investigation for organic or autoimmune causes in first-episode psychosis","Baseline metabolic parameters (weight, glucose, lipids) and ECG before and during antipsychotic treatment","Full blood count monitoring during clozapine treatment","Cognitive assessment to characterise functional impairment"],"redFlags":["Command hallucinations or suicidal ideation with risk to self","Delusional beliefs that place others at risk","First-episode psychosis with atypical features suggesting an organic cause","Signs of neuroleptic malignant syndrome such as rigidity, hyperthermia, and autonomic instability","Agranulocytosis during clozapine therapy"],"prognosis":"Schizophrenia usually follows a chronic course with episodic exacerbations and variable residual impairment. Better outcomes are associated with acute onset, good premorbid functioning, prominent mood symptoms, and early treatment, whereas insidious onset, prominent negative symptoms, and long untreated psychosis predict poorer functional recovery.","pitfalls":["Attributing first-episode psychosis to a primary psychiatric disorder without excluding organic and substance causes","Neglecting metabolic monitoring in patients on antipsychotics","Delaying a clozapine trial in genuinely treatment-resistant illness","Underestimating suicide risk, especially early in the course","Focusing only on positive symptoms while ignoring disabling negative and cognitive symptoms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"scleritis":{"name":"Scleritis","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["anterior scleritis","posterior scleritis","necrotising scleritis","scleromalacia perforans"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with severe, deep, boring ocular pain that is often worse at night, may radiate to the face or jaw, and characteristically disturbs sleep","The redness has a bluish or violaceous hue from involvement of the deep scleral vessels, unlike the bright red of episcleritis","Scleral vessels do not blanch with topical phenylephrine, which helps distinguish scleritis from episcleritis","Classified into anterior (diffuse, nodular, or necrotising) and posterior forms; necrotising scleritis is the most destructive and sight-threatening","Scleromalacia perforans is a painless necrotising form seen in long-standing rheumatoid arthritis","Roughly half of cases are associated with systemic disease, especially rheumatoid arthritis and ANCA-associated vasculitis such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Posterior scleritis can mimic other conditions with proptosis, pain on eye movement, and disc swelling, and is confirmed on ultrasound (T-sign)","Management is with systemic anti-inflammatory therapy: oral NSAIDs for milder disease and systemic corticosteroids or immunosuppression for necrotising or refractory cases"],"pathophysiology":"Scleritis is inflammation of the sclera, frequently immune-mediated and often a manifestation of systemic autoimmune or vasculitic disease. Immune-complex deposition and T-cell-mediated inflammation cause oedema and cellular infiltration of the sclera and episclera; in necrotising disease, occlusive vasculitis of the deep episcleral vessels leads to scleral ischaemia, necrosis, and thinning, with risk of perforation. Chronic or severe inflammation can extend to adjacent structures causing keratitis, uveitis, glaucoma, and, in posterior disease, exudative retinal detachment and optic nerve involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Episcleritis","Anterior uveitis","Conjunctivitis","Orbital cellulitis or orbital inflammatory disease (posterior scleritis)","Infective (e.g. herpetic or pseudomonal) scleritis","Choroidal tumour or exudative retinal detachment (posterior scleritis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Slit-lamp examination with assessment of deep vessel involvement and response to topical phenylephrine","B-scan ultrasonography for posterior scleritis (scleral thickening and T-sign)","Inflammatory and autoimmune workup: full blood count, ESR/CRP, ANA, ANCA, rheumatoid factor","Investigations for infective causes (including syphilis and tuberculosis) when indicated","Imaging or systemic referral when an underlying vasculitis is suspected"],"redFlags":["Necrotising scleritis with scleral thinning or threatened perforation","Severe pain with reduced vision indicating posterior segment or optic nerve involvement","Features of systemic vasculitis (e.g. granulomatosis with polyangiitis) requiring urgent systemic treatment","Rapid progression despite therapy suggesting infective scleritis or aggressive vasculitis"],"prognosis":"Diffuse and nodular anterior scleritis usually respond to anti-inflammatory treatment with good visual outcomes, but necrotising scleritis is associated with significant ocular morbidity and, when linked to systemic vasculitis, increased mortality if the underlying disease is untreated.","pitfalls":["Mistaking scleritis for the benign, self-limiting episcleritis and undertreating it","Failing to search for and treat an underlying systemic vasculitis or connective-tissue disease","Missing posterior scleritis, which lacks obvious external redness and mimics orbital disease","Using topical steroids alone when systemic immunosuppression is required for necrotising disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"scoliosis":{"name":"Scoliosis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["spinal curvature","adolescent idiopathic scoliosis","lateral curvature of the spine"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A three-dimensional deformity combining lateral curvature and vertebral rotation, most commonly adolescent idiopathic scoliosis in otherwise healthy children, predominantly girls","It is often painless and detected as asymmetry of the shoulders, waist, or scapulae; a rib hump on the forward-bending (Adam's) test reflects the rotational component","Curve magnitude is quantified by the Cobb angle on a standing radiograph, and skeletal maturity (e.g. Risser sign) helps predict the risk of progression","Significant back pain, a rapidly progressive curve, an atypical (e.g. left thoracic) curve, or neurological signs should prompt a search for an underlying cause and MRI","Scoliosis may be idiopathic, congenital (vertebral anomalies), or neuromuscular (e.g. cerebral palsy, muscular dystrophy), and management differs accordingly","Small curves are observed, moderate curves in a growing child may be braced to limit progression, and large or progressive curves are considered for surgical correction and fusion","The likelihood of progression is greatest during the adolescent growth spurt, so monitoring is intensified during rapid growth","Severe thoracic curves can restrict pulmonary function, an important consideration in early-onset and neuromuscular disease"],"pathophysiology":"Scoliosis is a structural lateral deviation of the spine accompanied by rotation of the vertebrae, which produces the characteristic rib or paravertebral prominence. In adolescent idiopathic scoliosis the cause is unknown but multifactorial, with genetic, growth-related, and neuromuscular balance factors implicated, and the deformity tends to worsen during periods of rapid skeletal growth. Congenital scoliosis results from failures of vertebral formation or segmentation present from birth, while neuromuscular scoliosis arises from asymmetrical muscle forces acting on the growing spine in conditions such as cerebral palsy. Progressive rotation and wedging of vertebrae make the curve increasingly rigid, and large thoracic curves can distort the chest and compromise lung capacity.","additionalDifferentials":["Postural (non-structural) scoliosis that corrects on forward bending","Leg-length discrepancy causing compensatory curvature","Congenital vertebral anomalies","Neuromuscular scoliosis (cerebral palsy, muscular dystrophy, spinal muscular atrophy)","Syndromic scoliosis (e.g. neurofibromatosis, Marfan syndrome)","Intraspinal pathology (syrinx, tethered cord, tumour) causing an atypical or painful curve"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Standing posteroanterior and lateral spinal radiographs to measure the Cobb angle and assess balance","Assessment of skeletal maturity (e.g. Risser sign, hand/wrist bone age)","Forward-bending (Adam's) test with a scoliometer to gauge rotation","MRI of the whole spine for atypical, painful, rapidly progressive, or early-onset curves, or when there are neurological signs","Pulmonary function testing for large thoracic or neuromuscular curves"],"redFlags":["Neurological signs or symptoms suggesting an intraspinal cause requiring MRI","Significant or persistent back pain, unusual for idiopathic scoliosis","Rapid curve progression or an atypical curve pattern (e.g. left thoracic)","Respiratory compromise from a severe thoracic curve"],"prognosis":"Most mild adolescent idiopathic curves remain stable and require only monitoring, and bracing can prevent progression of moderate curves during growth; large or progressive curves may need surgical correction, and untreated severe curves can cause deformity, back pain, and in extreme thoracic cases cardiopulmonary compromise, while neuromuscular and congenital curves tend to be more progressive.","pitfalls":["Assuming all scoliosis is benign idiopathic disease and missing an underlying intraspinal or neuromuscular cause","Overlooking a painful or atypical curve that warrants MRI","Failing to monitor closely during the adolescent growth spurt when progression is most likely","Confusing a compensatory postural curve from leg-length discrepancy with structural scoliosis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"scorpion_sting":{"name":"Scorpion sting","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scorpion envenomation","scorpion bite","scorpionism"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Venom is a mixture of neurotoxins that activate sodium channels and drive a massive autonomic (catecholamine) surge, producing a mixed sympathetic and parasympathetic toxidrome","Local effects are often minor (intense pain, tingling, minimal swelling), so systemic severity can be underestimated from the sting site alone","Early cholinergic features (vomiting, profuse sweating, salivation, priapism) are typically followed by an adrenergic phase with hypertension, tachycardia, and agitation","The feared complication is myocardial dysfunction with acute pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock, particularly in children","Prazosin, an alpha-blocker, is a key pharmacological measure to counter the catecholamine storm and offload the heart in significant envenomation","Species-specific scorpion antivenom may be indicated for systemic envenomation where available, ideally given early","Supportive care includes analgesia, close cardiorespiratory monitoring, and management of pulmonary oedema; dobutamine may help the failing heart","Children and those presenting late with signs of myocarditis or shock carry the greatest risk and need intensive monitoring"],"pathophysiology":"Scorpion venom contains peptide neurotoxins that delay inactivation of voltage-gated sodium channels and block potassium channels at nerve terminals, causing repetitive depolarisation and unregulated release of neurotransmitters. This produces an initial cholinergic outpouring followed by an overwhelming sympathetic (catecholamine) surge. The resulting effects include hypertension, tachyarrhythmias, increased systemic vascular resistance, and direct catecholamine-mediated myocardial injury. Myocarditis, transient left ventricular dysfunction, and increased capillary permeability lead to acute pulmonary oedema, which is the principal cause of death, especially in children.","additionalDifferentials":["Other venomous envenomation (snakebite, spider bite)","Acute myocarditis from other causes","Sympathomimetic drug toxicity","Acute anaphylaxis","Acute coronary syndrome with pulmonary oedema"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous ECG monitoring for arrhythmia and ischaemic changes","Echocardiography to assess left ventricular function in systemic envenomation","Cardiac biomarkers (troponin) where myocardial injury is suspected","Chest imaging for pulmonary oedema","Blood glucose, electrolytes, and arterial blood gas in severe cases"],"redFlags":["Acute pulmonary oedema, respiratory distress, or frothy sputum indicating myocardial involvement","Signs of cardiogenic shock or cool clammy peripheries","Priapism, profuse sweating, and repeated vomiting signalling significant systemic envenomation","Young children with any systemic feature, who deteriorate rapidly","New arrhythmia or ECG changes suggesting myocarditis"],"prognosis":"Most stings cause only local pain and resolve without sequelae, but systemic envenomation with myocardial dysfunction and pulmonary oedema can be fatal, particularly in children; early alpha-blockade, antivenom where indicated, and intensive supportive care markedly improve outcomes.","pitfalls":["Judging severity by the local reaction and missing evolving systemic toxicity","Delaying prazosin or antivenom while awaiting overt pulmonary oedema","Aggressive fluid loading in a patient with myocardial dysfunction, worsening pulmonary oedema","Under-monitoring children, who can deteriorate abruptly"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"seborrheic_dermatitis":{"name":"Seborrheic dermatitis","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["seborrhoeic dermatitis","seborrhoeic eczema","dandruff (mild scalp form)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as ill-defined erythematous patches with greasy yellowish scale in sebum-rich areas: scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, ears, and central chest","Mild scalp involvement manifests simply as dandruff, while more severe disease produces inflamed, scaly, sometimes itchy plaques","It has a characteristic bimodal age pattern, occurring in infants (including cradle cap) and again in adults","The condition is linked to the commensal yeast Malassezia, so antifungal agents are a mainstay of treatment","Management combines antifungal shampoos or creams with short courses of low-potency topical corticosteroids or calcineurin inhibitors for inflammation, and recognises that it is a relapsing condition needing maintenance","Unusually severe, extensive, or treatment-resistant seborrhoeic dermatitis should prompt consideration of underlying HIV infection or Parkinson's disease","Distinguish it from psoriasis (sharper, thicker, silvery plaques) and from atopic or contact dermatitis based on distribution and morphology"],"pathophysiology":"Seborrhoeic dermatitis is a chronic inflammatory disorder occurring in sebaceous-rich skin, driven by an abnormal host inflammatory response to Malassezia yeasts that are part of the normal skin flora. The yeasts metabolise sebum lipids, releasing free fatty acids and other products that irritate the skin and provoke inflammation in susceptible individuals. Sebaceous gland activity, an altered skin barrier, individual immune reactivity, and neurological or immunosuppressive comorbidities all influence expression. This explains its predilection for sebum-rich sites, its association with hormonal activity in infancy and adulthood, and its increased severity in HIV infection and Parkinson's disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Psoriasis (particularly scalp and flexural)","Atopic dermatitis","Contact dermatitis","Tinea capitis or tinea faciei","Rosacea (facial involvement)","Subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus in atypical facial cases"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on distribution and greasy scale","Skin scrapings for mycology if dermatophyte infection is a consideration","HIV testing when disease is severe, sudden, or refractory","Skin biopsy in atypical or diagnostically uncertain presentations","Assessment for neurological comorbidity such as Parkinson's disease when relevant"],"redFlags":["Severe, extensive, or abrupt-onset disease prompting evaluation for immunosuppression such as HIV","Erythroderma with widespread involvement","Failure to respond to appropriate first-line therapy suggesting an alternative diagnosis","Secondary bacterial infection of excoriated or fissured plaques"],"prognosis":"Seborrhoeic dermatitis is a benign but chronic relapsing condition; infantile disease usually resolves within months, whereas adult disease tends to persist with periodic flares and is controlled rather than cured.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with psoriasis or dermatophyte infection and choosing the wrong therapy","Relying on topical corticosteroids alone and neglecting antifungal treatment of the yeast component","Overusing potent steroids on the face, risking atrophy and rosacea-like changes","Failing to investigate unusually severe or refractory disease for underlying HIV or neurological disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"secondary_peritonitis":{"name":"Secondary peritonitis","system":"GI / Peritoneum / Surgery","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute secondary peritonitis","surgical peritonitis","generalised peritonitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from bacterial contamination of the peritoneal cavity following perforation, ischaemia, or inflammation of an abdominal viscus, unlike primary (spontaneous) peritonitis","Presents with severe, constant abdominal pain worsened by movement, with tenderness, guarding, rigidity, and rebound, and the patient characteristically lies still","Common sources include perforated peptic ulcer, perforated appendix, perforated diverticular disease, and bowel ischaemia or anastomotic leak","Systemic features of sepsis develop rapidly: fever, tachycardia, hypotension, oliguria, and, if untreated, septic shock and multi-organ failure","The management triad is aggressive resuscitation, appropriate broad-spectrum antibiotics, and prompt source control of the underlying cause","Source control, achieved surgically or by radiological drainage, is the definitive step; antibiotics and resuscitation support but do not replace it","Faeculent peritonitis from colonic perforation carries a particularly heavy bacterial and endotoxin load and a high mortality","Peritoneal contamination is often graded (localised, diffuse, purulent, faeculent) and guides the extent of surgery and lavage"],"pathophysiology":"Secondary peritonitis is inflammation of the peritoneum caused by spillage of gastrointestinal contents and micro-organisms into the peritoneal cavity following a breach of a hollow viscus or transmural necrosis. Gut flora, typically a mix of coliforms and anaerobes, provoke an intense inflammatory response with fibrinopurulent exudate, large third-space fluid losses, and paralytic ileus. Absorption of bacteria and endotoxin across the vast peritoneal surface drives systemic inflammatory response, sepsis, and septic shock. The peritoneum may localise infection into abscesses, but diffuse contamination overwhelms these defences and threatens life.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary (spontaneous bacterial) peritonitis","Acute pancreatitis","Mesenteric ischaemia","Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm","Intra-abdominal abscess without generalised peritonitis","Medical causes of acute abdomen (diabetic ketoacidosis, inferior myocardial infarction)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bloods including full blood count, urea and electrolytes, CRP, amylase or lipase, blood cultures, and lactate","Arterial blood gas to assess acid-base status and organ perfusion","Erect chest radiograph for free subdiaphragmatic gas","CT abdomen and pelvis to identify the source and any collections","Peritoneal fluid sampling and culture when the diagnosis or source is unclear"],"redFlags":["Generalised peritonitis with sepsis, hypotension, or rising lactate indicating septic shock (surgical emergency)","Faeculent contamination from colonic perforation with heavy sepsis burden","Progressive organ dysfunction (oliguria, confusion, respiratory compromise)","Failure to improve after resuscitation, indicating inadequate source control"],"prognosis":"Outcome hinges on early recognition, prompt resuscitation, and timely source control; delay, faeculent contamination, advanced age, and comorbidity markedly increase mortality, which remains high in established diffuse peritonitis with septic shock.","pitfalls":["Delaying source control while awaiting response to antibiotics and fluids","Under-resuscitating a patient with large third-space and septic losses","Attributing peritonism to a medical cause and missing a surgical source","Inadequate peritoneal lavage or failing to address ongoing contamination at surgery"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"sedation_complications":{"name":"Sedation complications","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["procedural sedation complications","conscious sedation complications","sedation-related adverse events"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sedation exists on a continuum from minimal sedation to general anaesthesia, and patients can drift into a deeper level than intended, so providers must be able to rescue from the next deeper level","The most common serious complications are respiratory: airway obstruction, hypoventilation, apnoea, and hypoxaemia, which can progress rapidly if unrecognised","Cardiovascular effects include hypotension and bradycardia, and combining sedatives with opioids markedly increases the risk of respiratory depression","Continuous monitoring of oxygenation with pulse oximetry and of ventilation with capnography, plus regular assessment of conscious level, is essential throughout and during recovery","Appropriate patient selection and pre-procedure assessment (airway, comorbidities, aspiration risk, and fasting status) reduce complications","Resuscitation equipment, oxygen, suction, and reversal agents must be immediately available, and staff must be trained to manage airway and cardiovascular emergencies","Reversal agents (such as an opioid antagonist for opioids and a benzodiazepine antagonist for benzodiazepines) can be used, but their duration may be shorter than the sedative, risking re-sedation","Elderly, frail, obese, and sleep-apnoea patients, and those at the extremes of age, are especially vulnerable and warrant conservative dosing and heightened vigilance"],"pathophysiology":"Sedative and analgesic agents depress the central nervous system in a dose-dependent way, reducing conscious level, blunting protective airway reflexes, and depressing respiratory drive and upper airway muscle tone. Loss of pharyngeal tone causes obstruction, while reduced central drive causes hypoventilation and apnoea, together producing hypoxaemia and hypercapnia. Many agents also cause vasodilation and myocardial depression, leading to hypotension, and some blunt compensatory reflexes. Because effects are additive, combinations of sedatives and opioids produce disproportionate respiratory and cardiovascular depression. Depressed reflexes also raise the risk of aspiration in patients with a full stomach.","additionalDifferentials":["Airway obstruction from other causes","Aspiration of gastric contents","Anaphylaxis to a sedative agent","Underlying cardiac or respiratory disease causing deterioration","Hypoglycaemia or metabolic disturbance altering consciousness","Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity when combined with local blocks"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous pulse oximetry and capnography during sedation and recovery","Regular assessment of sedation depth and responsiveness","Blood pressure and ECG monitoring appropriate to patient risk","Arterial or capillary blood gas if significant hypoventilation is suspected","Glucose check if altered consciousness is unexplained"],"redFlags":["Apnoea, severe hypoventilation, or airway obstruction with falling oxygen saturation","Unintended progression to deep sedation or general anaesthesia without rescue capability","Significant hypotension or bradycardia","Aspiration during sedation in an at-risk patient"],"prognosis":"With appropriate patient selection, monitoring, and prompt rescue, procedural sedation is generally safe; unrecognised respiratory depression can cause hypoxic injury or death, and most serious events are linked to inadequate monitoring or rescue.","pitfalls":["Providing sedation without the skills or equipment to rescue a deeper-than-intended level","Relying on oximetry alone and missing early hypoventilation that capnography would detect","Underestimating the additive respiratory depression of sedative-opioid combinations","Assuming a reversal agent fully solves the problem despite its shorter duration and risk of re-sedation","Inadequate pre-procedure assessment of airway, comorbidity, and aspiration risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"seizures_and_epilepsy":{"name":"Seizures and Epilepsy","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Epilepsy","Seizure disorder","Convulsive disorder"],"chapter":436,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3408","clinicalPearls":["A seizure is a transient event caused by abnormal excessive or hypersynchronous neuronal activity; epilepsy is the distinct condition of a chronic underlying tendency to recurrent unprovoked seizures, so a single or provoked seizure does not by itself constitute epilepsy (though a single seizure with high-risk clinical/EEG features may suffice).","Roughly 5-10% of people experience at least one seizure in life, with peaks in early childhood and late adulthood; epilepsy prevalence is approximately 5-30 per 1000.","The 2017 ILAE scheme classifies seizures by onset as focal, generalized, or unknown; focal seizures are described by awareness (intact vs impaired) and motor vs nonmotor onset, replacing the older 'simple/complex partial' and 'secondary generalization' terms.","Focal motor seizures may show a Jacksonian march (spread along the motor strip), and a postictal focal weakness (Todd's paralysis) lasting minutes to hours can mimic a stroke.","Auras are themselves focal seizures with intact awareness; temporal-lobe auras include rising epigastric sensations, foul smells/tastes, fear, deja vu, and micropsia/macropsia.","Typical absence seizures produce brief lapses of consciousness without postural loss or postictal confusion, with a 3-Hz generalized spike-and-wave EEG provoked by hyperventilation; they are frequently misdiagnosed as attention-deficit disorder or daydreaming.","Generalized tonic-clonic seizures begin with a tonic phase (ictal cry, apnea/cyanosis, tongue biting, sympathetic surge) evolving to a clonic phase, followed by postictal flaccidity, confusion, headache, and myalgia.","Mesial temporal lobe epilepsy with hippocampal sclerosis is the most common cause of focal seizures with impaired awareness and is characteristically medically refractory but responds well to surgery.","Etiology is strongly age-dependent: neonates - perinatal hypoxia-ischemia, infection, and metabolic derangements; young children - febrile seizures; adolescents/young adults - trauma, genetic syndromes, drugs, autoantibodies; older adults - cerebrovascular disease (up to ~50% of new epilepsy over 65), tumor, and degenerative disease.","Distinguishing seizure from syncope: aura, cyanosis, prolonged motor activity (30-60 s), and many minutes to hours of postictal confusion favor seizure, whereas brief loss of consciousness with rapid recovery, pallor, and provocation by pain/standing favor syncope; headache and incontinence are unreliable discriminators.","A transient serum prolactin rise in the first 30 minutes after most generalized and some focal seizures can support a true seizure over a psychogenic event, but is unreliable without a baseline and because some drugs elevate prolactin.","Penetrating head injury carries up to a 45% risk of subsequent epilepsy via epileptogenesis, illustrating how an acquired structural lesion can permanently lower seizure threshold."],"pathophysiology":"Seizures arise from a shift in the normal balance between neuronal excitation and inhibition in the CNS. Focal seizure activity begins in a discrete cortical region and can invade surrounding cortex; the hallmark is the electrographic spike produced by near-simultaneous bursting of many excitatory neurons (hypersynchronization). At the cellular level, a prolonged paroxysmal depolarization shift driven by calcium influx opens voltage-dependent sodium channels and generates repetitive action potentials, followed by a GABA- or potassium-channel-mediated hyperpolarizing afterpotential. Spread is normally limited by intact hyperpolarization and feedforward surround inhibition; recruitment of neighboring neurons occurs through rising extracellular potassium, presynaptic calcium accumulation enhancing transmitter release, NMDA-receptor activation, and ephaptic effects, allowing propagation via local and commissural (corpus callosum) pathways. Epilepsy results from a dynamic interplay of endogenous (often genetic, maturational) factors, epileptogenic factors (e.g., severe head trauma, stroke, infection, neurodegeneration, developmental malformations), and precipitating factors (sleep deprivation, stress, hormonal change, toxins, photic stimulation). Many gene mutations affecting ion channels (e.g., SCN1A, KCNQ2), neurotransmitter receptors, and metabolic transporters (e.g., SLC2A1/GLUT-1) underlie specific syndromes.","additionalDifferentials":["Syncope (vasovagal, cardiac arrhythmia, valvular disease, orthostatic hypotension)","Psychogenic nonepileptic seizures / conversion disorder","Transient ischemic attack (especially basilar artery TIA)","Migraine (confusional or basilar)","Metabolic disturbances - hypoglycemia, hypoxia, hyponatremia, hypocalcemia","Sleep disorders - narcolepsy/cataplexy, benign sleep myoclonus","Movement disorders - tics, nonepileptic myoclonus, paroxysmal choreoathetosis","Panic attack / hyperventilation","In children: breath-holding spells, night terrors, sleepwalking, benign paroxysmal vertigo, apnea"],"infectionMimics":["CNS infection (meningitis/encephalitis) - both a cause of acute symptomatic seizures and a mimic via confusion/altered behavior; lumbar puncture is mandatory in HIV-infected patients presenting with a seizure even when asymptomatic"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Convulsive syncope (brief tonic-clonic-like movements during a faint, especially when kept upright)","Psychogenic nonepileptic seizures (side-to-side head movement, ictal eye closure, asynchronous limb shaking, pelvic thrusting, waxing-waning prolonged events)","Transient ischemic attack","Migraine aura","Movement disorders and parasomnias"],"additionalInvestigations":["EEG, including activation procedures (hyperventilation, photic stimulation, sleep, sleep deprivation); a normal routine interictal EEG does not exclude epilepsy and is normal in 50-80% of known epilepsy patients on first recording","Prolonged video-EEG telemetry for poorly characterized or refractory events and to distinguish psychogenic from epileptic seizures","MRI (superior to CT) for structural lesions including hippocampal sclerosis and neuronal migration abnormalities; emergent CT when MRI unavailable or mass/infection suspected","Routine bloods - electrolytes, glucose, calcium, magnesium, hepatic and renal function; toxicology screen of blood and urine","Lumbar puncture when meningitis/encephalitis suspected, and mandatory in HIV-infected patients","Serum and CSF autoantibody testing in fulminant epilepsy with psychiatric or cognitive features","Genetic testing in suspected genetic epilepsy syndromes (diagnostic and may guide therapy choice and predict drug toxicity, e.g., HLA-B*1502 and severe cutaneous reactions)","Functional imaging (PET, SPECT) and advanced localization (high-density EEG/ESI, magnetoencephalography/MSI) in medically refractory focal epilepsy"],"redFlags":["Generalized convulsive status epilepticus - continuous or repetitive seizures (practically, GCSE lasting beyond ~5 min); a medical emergency that can cause cardiorespiratory failure, hyperthermia, metabolic derangement, and irreversible CNS injury","After 30-45 minutes of uninterrupted seizures the clinical signs may become subtle while damaging electrographic seizures continue, including in patients paralyzed by neuromuscular blockade","Fulminant new-onset seizures with psychiatric symptoms and cognitive change suggesting autoimmune (e.g., anti-potassium-channel or anti-glutamate-receptor) epilepsy","New seizure in an older adult suggesting cerebrovascular disease, tumor, or degenerative disease","First seizure with focal features, persistent neurologic deficit, fever, or suspected CNS infection prompting urgent imaging and possible lumbar puncture","Atonic 'drop attacks' with high risk of head injury"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies widely by syndrome and etiology. Typical absence epilepsy and juvenile myoclonic epilepsy generally respond well to medication (though JME remission is uncommon), whereas mesial temporal lobe epilepsy, Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, and epilepsies tied to structural lesions or developmental delay are often refractory. About half of patients do not respond to the first medication, and roughly 30% have ongoing seizures despite optimal drug therapy. Favorable predictors for successfully discontinuing therapy include 1-5 years of complete control, a single seizure type (generalized carrying a better prognosis than focal), normal neurologic examination and intelligence, no family history of epilepsy, and a normal EEG; a normal EEG generally implies a better prognosis. People with epilepsy have a death rate roughly 2-3 times that of matched controls, mostly attributable to the underlying cause but also to accidents, status epilepticus, and SUDEP.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish a focal seizure that secondarily generalizes from a primary generalized tonic-clonic seizure because bystanders emphasize the convulsive phase and overlook subtle focal onset/aura - a distinction that materially changes evaluation and treatment.","Misdiagnosing absence epilepsy as attention-deficit disorder or simple daydreaming.","Concluding bizarre paroxysmal stereotyped behavior is non-epileptic; focal (especially frontal-lobe) seizures can produce highly unusual behaviors.","Assuming a normal interictal EEG excludes epilepsy, or that epileptiform discharges by themselves prove epilepsy.","Over-relying on incontinence or headache to separate seizure from syncope, as these are unreliable.","Assuming psychogenic nonepileptic seizures exclude true epilepsy - the two frequently coexist.","Misreading subtle late-stage status epilepticus as resolution when damaging electrographic seizures persist."],"severityClassification":"Seizures are classified by ILAE 2017 onset: focal (with intact or impaired awareness, motor or nonmotor), generalized (motor such as tonic-clonic, atonic, myoclonic; or nonmotor/absence), or unknown onset. Severity at the syndromic extreme is status epilepticus: continuous or repetitive seizures with impaired interictal consciousness, subdivided into generalized convulsive status epilepticus (GCSE) and nonconvulsive status epilepticus (NCSE). Although traditionally defined as 15-30 minutes of activity, GCSE is practically treated as an emergency when convulsions last beyond about 5 minutes. Febrile seizures are graded as simple (single, brief, symmetric) versus complex (repeated, lasting over 15 minutes, or with focal features).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3408","Harrison 22e p.3409","Harrison 22e p.3410","Harrison 22e p.3411","Harrison 22e p.3413","Harrison 22e p.3414","Harrison 22e p.3416","Harrison 22e p.3417","Harrison 22e p.3418","Harrison 22e p.3419","Harrison 22e p.3425","Harrison 22e p.3427"],"crossLinks":[]},"selective_iga_deficiency":{"name":"Selective IgA deficiency","system":"Immunology / Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["IgA deficiency","selective immunoglobulin A deficiency","sIgAD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Middleton's Allergy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Selective IgA deficiency is the most common primary immunodeficiency and is defined by a very low or absent serum IgA with normal IgG and IgM and otherwise intact immunity","The majority of affected individuals are asymptomatic and are detected incidentally, so a low IgA alone does not equate to clinical disease","When symptomatic, patients tend to have recurrent sinopulmonary and gastrointestinal infections, reflecting the loss of secretory IgA at mucosal surfaces","There is a recognised association with atopy and with autoimmune conditions such as coeliac disease, so unexplained IgA deficiency warrants screening for these","Coeliac serology based on IgA antibodies can be falsely negative in IgA deficiency, so IgG-based testing or histology should be used instead","A minority have severe anaphylactic reactions to blood products because of anti-IgA antibodies, so washed or IgA-deficient blood components may be required","There is no specific replacement therapy because IgA cannot be effectively replaced; management centres on prompt treatment of infections and, where needed, prophylactic antibiotics","A small proportion, particularly those with associated IgG subclass deficiency or poor vaccine responses, can progress over time toward common variable immunodeficiency and need monitoring"],"pathophysiology":"Selective IgA deficiency results from a failure of terminal differentiation of IgA-committed B cells into IgA-secreting plasma cells, so that little or no IgA reaches serum and mucosal secretions. The loss of secretory IgA impairs mucosal immune exclusion of pathogens at the respiratory and gastrointestinal surfaces, though compensatory secretory IgM often limits the clinical impact. The precise molecular basis is usually unknown; it shares genetic susceptibility loci (including MHC haplotypes and TACI variants) with CVID, and the two conditions can occur within the same families and occasionally evolve into one another. Immune dysregulation underlies the associated atopic and autoimmune disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Common variable immunodeficiency (may share features and can evolve from IgA deficiency)","Transient low IgA in early childhood before immune maturation","Drug-induced IgA suppression (e.g. certain anticonvulsants)","IgG subclass or specific antibody deficiency presenting with recurrent infection","Coeliac disease and other associated autoimmune disorders","Secondary immunodeficiency from protein loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum IgA, IgG, and IgM to confirm isolated IgA deficiency","IgG subclass levels and vaccine-specific antibody responses to assess for broader deficiency","Coeliac evaluation using IgG-based serology or duodenal biopsy given unreliable IgA-based tests","Screening for associated autoimmune disease when clinically indicated","Testing for anti-IgA antibodies before transfusion in patients with prior reactions","Periodic reassessment of immunoglobulins to detect evolution toward CVID"],"redFlags":["Anaphylactic transfusion reaction from anti-IgA antibodies, requiring IgA-deficient or washed blood products","Progressive fall in IgG or poor vaccine responses signalling evolution to CVID","Recurrent severe or invasive infection prompting reassessment of the full antibody profile","Unexplained malabsorption or systemic autoimmune features suggesting an associated disorder"],"prognosis":"Most individuals with selective IgA deficiency remain well and have a normal life expectancy. Symptomatic patients generally do well with treatment of infections, but a subset with additional immune defects have more infections and may progress to CVID, and rare transfusion anaphylaxis is a serious hazard.","pitfalls":["Interpreting an incidental low IgA as clinically significant disease in an asymptomatic person","Missing coeliac disease because of falsely negative IgA-based serology","Transfusing standard blood products in a patient with anti-IgA antibodies","Failing to reassess immunoglobulins over time and thus missing progression to CVID"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"septic_bursitis":{"name":"Septic bursitis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["infective bursitis","infected bursa","septic olecranon bursitis","septic prepatellar bursitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / IDSA","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most often affects the superficial olecranon and prepatellar bursae, which are prone to trauma and abrasion","Infection usually follows direct inoculation through breached skin rather than haematogenous spread","Staphylococcus aureus is the predominant organism, with beta-haemolytic streptococci next most common","Distinguishing septic from non-septic (crystal, traumatic, or inflammatory) bursitis is the central clinical challenge and may require bursal fluid aspiration","Overlying warmth, erythema, tenderness, and fever favour infection but can be mimicked by gout or trauma","Unlike septic arthritis, passive joint movement is typically well preserved because the bursa lies outside the joint","Management principles combine aspiration for diagnosis, antistaphylococcal antibiotics, and drainage of persistent or loculated collections","Recurrent or refractory cases may need surgical bursectomy, and predisposing occupational trauma should be addressed"],"pathophysiology":"A bursa is a synovial-lined sac that reduces friction over bony prominences. Superficial bursae such as the olecranon and prepatellar sit just beneath the skin and are readily seeded by direct trauma or overlying cellulitis. Introduced bacteria, usually skin flora, provoke an inflammatory exudate within the bursal cavity, producing pain, swelling, warmth, and sometimes abscess formation. Because the bursa is anatomically separate from the joint, infection tends to remain localised unless it extends into surrounding tissue.","additionalDifferentials":["Crystal-induced (gout or pseudogout) bursitis","Traumatic or haemorrhagic bursitis","Rheumatoid or other inflammatory bursitis","Cellulitis overlying the bursa","Septic arthritis of the adjacent joint"],"infectionMimics":["Acute gouty bursitis or tophaceous deposits","Repetitive-strain (occupational) non-infective bursitis","Rheumatoid nodule with overlying inflammation","Post-traumatic haematoma of the bursa"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Aspiration of bursal fluid for Gram stain, culture, cell count, and crystal analysis","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers when systemic signs are present","Comparison of bursal fluid findings with adjacent joint fluid if joint infection is a concern","Ultrasound to confirm a bursal collection and guide aspiration","Assessment for underlying immunosuppression or predisposing trauma"],"redFlags":["Spread into the adjacent joint or deep tissues suggesting septic arthritis or deeper infection","Systemic sepsis or rapidly advancing surrounding cellulitis requiring urgent treatment","Failure to improve after aspiration and antibiotics indicating a loculated or abscessed collection needing drainage","Immunocompromised host in whom infection may progress atypically or rapidly"],"prognosis":"Superficial septic bursitis generally resolves well with aspiration and appropriate antibiotics; recurrence or chronicity is more likely with repeated trauma or delayed treatment, and a minority require surgical bursectomy.","pitfalls":["Assuming an inflamed bursa is gouty or traumatic and missing infection","Confusing septic bursitis with septic arthritis, which requires more aggressive intervention","Injecting corticosteroid into a bursa without excluding infection","Stopping treatment prematurely and precipitating relapse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","IDSA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"serum_sickness":{"name":"Serum sickness","system":"Immunology / Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["serum sickness reaction","type III hypersensitivity reaction","serum-sickness-like reaction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Middleton's Allergy","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Serum sickness is the classic immune-complex (type III) hypersensitivity reaction, presenting with fever, rash, arthralgia or arthritis, and lymphadenopathy","Symptoms characteristically begin about one to three weeks after first exposure to the offending agent, with a faster onset on re-exposure because the patient is already sensitised","Historically it followed heterologous (animal) antiserum, and today it is most often triggered by antitoxins, antivenoms, therapeutic antibodies, and certain drugs","The rash is often urticarial or a serpiginous purplish eruption at the border of the palms and soles, a helpful clinical clue","During the active phase complement is consumed, so serum C3 and C4 fall and can be used to support the diagnosis and track resolution","A serum-sickness-like reaction, most often attributed to antibiotics such as cefaclor, resembles serum sickness clinically but lacks the true immune-complex and hypocomplementaemic features and is generally benign","The condition is usually self-limiting once the causative agent is withdrawn, with recovery over days to a couple of weeks","Management principles are stopping and avoiding the trigger, symptomatic relief with antihistamines and anti-inflammatory agents, and corticosteroids for severe or systemic disease"],"pathophysiology":"Serum sickness is mediated by circulating antigen-antibody (immune) complexes. After exposure to a foreign protein or drug-protein conjugate, the host mounts an antibody response; as antibody rises while antigen is still present, soluble immune complexes form in antigen-relative excess. These complexes deposit in small vessels of the skin, joints, kidneys, and elsewhere, where they activate complement and recruit neutrophils, producing local inflammation and tissue injury (a type III hypersensitivity reaction). Complement activation consumes C3 and C4, accounting for the transient hypocomplementaemia. Once the antigen is cleared and complexes are removed, the process resolves. Serum-sickness-like reactions produce a similar clinical picture through incompletely understood mechanisms without demonstrable immune-complex disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Serum-sickness-like reaction to antibiotics (e.g. cefaclor)","Acute viral infection (e.g. hepatitis B, parvovirus) with rash and arthralgia","Systemic lupus erythematosus and other connective-tissue disease","Urticarial vasculitis and other small-vessel vasculitides","Acute drug reaction such as DRESS or an urticarial drug eruption","Reactive or infectious arthritis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum C3 and C4, which fall during the active immune-complex phase","Full blood count and inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP)","Urinalysis to detect renal involvement","Assessment for circulating immune complexes or cryoglobulins where available","Serologies to exclude viral and autoimmune mimics","Skin biopsy in atypical cases to evaluate for vasculitis"],"redFlags":["Significant renal involvement with proteinuria, haematuria, or rising creatinine","Concurrent anaphylaxis or angioedema at the time of re-exposure to the antigen","Neurologic involvement or severe systemic illness suggesting extensive immune-complex disease","Features pointing to a severe drug reaction such as DRESS rather than simple serum sickness"],"prognosis":"Serum sickness is generally self-limiting and resolves without sequelae once the causative agent is removed, with symptoms settling over days to a couple of weeks. Recovery may be hastened by corticosteroids in severe cases; significant organ involvement is uncommon but warrants closer follow-up.","pitfalls":["Failing to take a careful exposure history for antisera, antivenoms, or therapeutic antibodies given weeks earlier","Confusing serum sickness with an acute IgE-mediated allergic reaction and mismanaging the tempo of illness","Overlooking renal involvement by not checking urinalysis and renal function","Re-administering the same heterologous protein or drug without recognising the sensitisation risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Middleton's Allergy: Principles and Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"severe_acute_malnutrition":{"name":"Severe acute malnutrition","system":"Nutrition","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SAM","severe malnutrition","marasmus","kwashiorkor","protein-energy malnutrition"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined in young children by severe wasting (weight-for-height well below the reference median, or a very low mid-upper arm circumference) and/or bilateral pitting oedema of nutritional origin.","Marasmus is severe wasting from overall energy deficit; kwashiorkor is characterised by oedema, and mixed pictures occur.","Kwashiorkor features include oedema, sparse depigmented hair, skin changes (flaky-paint dermatosis), an enlarged fatty liver, and apathy.","Children with SAM are highly vulnerable to hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, and overwhelming infection, which is often present without classic signs.","Refeeding must be cautious and staged to avoid refeeding syndrome; overly rapid feeding and excess fluid or sodium can precipitate cardiac failure and death.","Initial stabilisation prioritises treating and preventing hypoglycaemia and hypothermia, correcting dehydration carefully, and giving broad micronutrient support.","Rehydration is preferably oral or nasogastric with a low-sodium, higher-potassium solution, because intravenous fluids readily cause fluid overload.","Management follows a stabilisation phase with cautious feeding, followed by a rehabilitation phase with catch-up growth once appetite and stability return."],"pathophysiology":"Prolonged inadequate intake of energy, protein, and micronutrients forces the body into a state of reductive adaptation: metabolic rate falls, organ function is downregulated, and body composition shifts with loss of fat and muscle. Sodium-potassium pump activity is impaired, causing intracellular sodium retention and total-body potassium and magnesium depletion despite oedema. Hepatic and cardiac function, immune competence, and thermoregulation are all compromised. In kwashiorkor, hypoalbuminaemia and increased capillary permeability contribute to oedema. These adaptations make the child dangerously intolerant of the metabolic demands of aggressive feeding and fluid loads.","additionalDifferentials":["Oedema from nephrotic syndrome or cardiac/hepatic failure","Chronic infection (including tuberculosis and HIV)","Malabsorption syndromes (e.g. coeliac disease, cystic fibrosis)","Chronic diarrhoeal disease","Underlying malignancy or chronic illness causing wasting"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Weight, height/length, and mid-upper arm circumference with oedema assessment","Blood glucose (to detect hypoglycaemia)","Electrolytes including potassium, magnesium, and phosphate","Haemoglobin and assessment for anaemia","Screening for infection (including tuberculosis, HIV where relevant) and malaria in endemic areas","Assessment of hydration status, which is difficult to judge clinically in SAM"],"redFlags":["Hypoglycaemia and hypothermia","Signs of shock or severe dehydration (difficult to assess and easy to overtreat)","Developing signs of heart failure during rehydration or refeeding (refeeding syndrome)","Severe anaemia requiring careful transfusion","Lethargy, hypothermia, or hypoglycaemia as subtle markers of serious infection"],"prognosis":"With careful phased management and treatment of complications, most children recover, but case fatality is high when complications such as hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, sepsis, or refeeding syndrome are missed or when rehydration is too aggressive.","pitfalls":["Feeding too much too quickly and precipitating refeeding syndrome and heart failure","Using standard intravenous rehydration and causing fluid overload","Relying on usual signs of infection, which are often absent","Missing hypoglycaemia and hypothermia because signs are subtle","Treating oedema as fluid overload rather than recognising nutritional kwashiorkor"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"sexually_transmitted_infections_overview_a":{"name":"Sexually Transmitted Infections: Overview and Clinical Approach","system":"Genitourinary / Reproductive","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["STI overview","STD overview","Sexually transmitted diseases","Venereal disease"],"chapter":141,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1096","clinicalPearls":["STIs are syndrome-driven in practice: most patients are managed initially by presenting symptoms, signs, and risk factors rather than by a confirmed microbial diagnosis, with common syndromes including male urethritis, epididymitis, mucopurulent cervicitis, vulvovaginitis, genital/perianal ulcers, PID, and proctitis (Harrison 22e p.1100).","More than 40 microorganisms are now recognized as predominantly sexually transmitted or frequently sexually transmissible, spanning bacteria, viruses, protozoa, fungi, and ectoparasites (Harrison 22e p.1096).","The initial rate of spread of any STI in a population is the product of three factors: the rate of sexual exposure of susceptible to infectious persons, the per-exposure transmission efficiency, and the duration of infectivity (Harrison 22e p.1096).","Genital HPV is described as the most common sexually transmitted pathogen in the United States, and HPV and hepatitis B virus remain major causes of cervical and hepatocellular carcinoma respectively in the developing world (Harrison 22e p.1096-1097).","Reported syphilis in the United States in 2022 (207,255 cases, all stages and congenital) was the highest since 1950, with congenital syphilis rate of 102.5 per 100,000 live births and 282 congenital syphilis-related stillbirths/infant deaths (Harrison 22e p.1097).","All four major curable STIs (gonorrhea, chlamydial infection, syphilis, trichomoniasis) plus bacterial vaginosis have been associated with increased risk of HIV transmission or acquisition (Harrison 22e p.1097).","Acute epididymitis in sexually active men under 35 is most often due to C. trachomatis (less often N. gonorrhoeae) and usually accompanies overt or subclinical urethritis, whereas in older men or after urinary instrumentation it is usually caused by urinary pathogens (Harrison 22e p.1101).","Unsolicited reporting of abnormal vaginal discharge (increased amount or abnormal odor) most often denotes bacterial vaginosis or trichomoniasis, not cervical gonorrhea or chlamydia, which usually do not increase discharge volume or odor (Harrison 22e p.1101).","Sexually transmitted HSV causes most genital ulcer disease worldwide; HSV-2 produces more frequent genital recurrences and is more infectious to partners than HSV-1, so typing has prognostic value (Harrison 22e p.1097, 1108).","STI control combines reducing exposure (education, behavior change, post-exposure prophylaxis), shortening infectivity (early diagnosis and curative or suppressive treatment), and reducing per-exposure transmission (condoms, vaccines, male medical circumcision) (Harrison 22e p.1096).","Despite the strong epidemiologic HSV-HIV link, randomized trials of antiviral HSV suppression showed no protective effect against HIV acquisition or transmission (Harrison 22e p.1097)."],"pathophysiology":"STIs are acquired through sexual exposure to a susceptible person from an infectious one, with spread amplified within highly interconnected sexual networks marked by high partner-change rates or concurrent partners (e.g., transactional sex, some MSM, drug-using populations), while infections such as chlamydia, genital HPV, and genital herpes propagate efficiently even in relatively low-risk populations. Many of these organisms colonize or invade genital, anal, oral, or systemic epithelium; ascending spread from cervix or vagina to endometrium and fallopian tubes underlies PID, with potential extension to pelvic peritonitis, perihepatitis, perisplenitis, or abscess. Immunopathology contributes to tissue damage in chlamydial salpingitis, where repeated exposure produces the greatest inflammation. Hormonal and anatomic factors modulate susceptibility (e.g., cervical ectopy may increase susceptibility to gonococcus, chlamydia, and HIV; oral contraceptives favor ectopy persistence; menstruation appears to facilitate ascending gonococcal/chlamydial infection).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute epididymitis must be distinguished from testicular torsion (a surgical emergency with sudden pain, testicular elevation, and absent Doppler flow), testicular tumor, and trauma (Harrison 22e p.1101)","Dysuric women: STI-associated urethral syndrome (sterile pyuria, <10^2 conventional uropathogens/mL) versus bacterial cystitis (single uropathogen >=10^2/mL) versus acute pyelonephritis (costovertebral tenderness/fever) (Harrison 22e p.1100-1101)","Persistent genital ulcer not resolving with syndrome-based therapy: consider donovanosis, carcinoma, and other nonvenereal dermatoses on biopsy (Harrison 22e p.1108)"],"infectionMimics":["Pelvic inflammatory disease that is not due to specific STIs (secondary to appendicitis, regional ileitis, diverticulitis, hematogenous tuberculosis or staphylococcal bacteremia, or tropical disease such as schistosomiasis) (Harrison 22e p.1104)","Chronic granulomatous epididymal disease such as tuberculosis mimicking sexually acquired epididymitis when symptoms persist after therapy (Harrison 22e p.1101)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cervical ectopy (columnar epithelium on the ectocervix), often mislabeled 'cervical erosion,' confused with infectious endocervicitis but showing clear/cloudy mucus and intact epithelium on colposcopy (Harrison 22e p.1104)","Testicular tumor or trauma presenting as scrotal pain/swelling mimicking epididymitis (Harrison 22e p.1101)","Genital carcinoma or nonvenereal dermatoses presenting as a persistent genital ulcer (Harrison 22e p.1108)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Risk assessment using a structured sexual-history questionnaire (e.g., the eleven-question STD/HIV risk assessment) to guide screening and presumptive treatment decisions (Harrison 22e p.1098)","Urethral Gram's stain to confirm urethritis and detect gram-negative diplococci, recognizing that up to ~30% of gonococcal urethral infections can yield a negative Gram's stain (Harrison 22e p.1099)","Nucleic acid amplification testing (NAAT) for N. gonorrhoeae, C. trachomatis, M. genitalium, and T. vaginalis on appropriate genital/urine specimens (Harrison 22e p.1099-1100)","Genital ulcer workup: dark-field examination, direct fluorescent antibody, or PCR for T. pallidum; RPR/VDRL/EIA serology for syphilis; culture, FA, ELISA, or PCR for HSV; HSV-2 type-specific serology; PCR or culture for H. ducreyi in chancroid-endemic settings (Harrison 22e p.1108-1109)","Biopsy of a genital ulcer persisting beyond the expected natural history (herpes 2-3 weeks; chancroid or syphilis up to 6 weeks) to exclude donovanosis and malignancy (Harrison 22e p.1108)","Aspiration of fluctuant inguinal nodes for culture/PCR (H. ducreyi) plus Gram's stain to exclude other pyogenic bacteria (Harrison 22e p.1108)"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe testicular pain with testicular elevation and absent Doppler flow indicating testicular torsion, a surgical emergency (Harrison 22e p.1101)","Costovertebral angle pain/tenderness or fever in a dysuric woman suggesting acute pyelonephritis (Harrison 22e p.1100)","Genital ulcer in a patient with HIV risk (e.g., MSM, known HIV infection): treat presumptively for syphilis rather than awaiting seroconversion given high syphilis rates (Harrison 22e p.1108)","Suspected PID with peritoneal extension (pelvic or generalized peritonitis, perihepatitis, perisplenitis, or pelvic abscess) (Harrison 22e p.1104)","Congenital syphilis exposure in pregnancy, given the sharp recent rise including syphilis-related stillbirths and infant deaths (Harrison 22e p.1097)"],"prognosis":"Many STIs are curable when diagnosed and treated early, and prompt treatment shortens infectivity and reduces onward transmission. Outcomes worsen with delayed care: untreated chlamydial and gonococcal infection can ascend to cause PID with risk of tubal damage and infertility, ectopic pregnancy, and chronic pelvic pain, and immunopathology contributes to chlamydial tubal injury. HPV and HBV carry long-term oncogenic risk (cervical and other anogenital cancers; hepatocellular carcinoma). HSV-2 causes lifelong recurrent infection with asymptomatic shedding and ongoing transmission risk. In men, urethritis with neither chlamydiae nor gonococci detected carries a higher risk of recurrent nongonococcal urethritis, and most persistent urethritis is attributable to M. genitalium. Congenital syphilis can result in stillbirth or infant death.","pitfalls":["Treating genital symptoms without performing the microbiologic tests needed to establish a cause; self-diagnosis of vulvovaginal candidiasis is often incorrect (Harrison 22e p.1101)","Relying on urethral Gram's stain alone to exclude gonorrhea, since up to ~30% of gonococcal urethral infections are smear-negative (Harrison 22e p.1099)","Misreading cervical ectopy as infectious endocervicitis and unnecessarily cauterizing it (Harrison 22e p.1104)","Assuming a positive HSV-2 serology proves current lesions are herpetic, when nearly one-fifth of the U.S. population becomes HSV-2 seropositive in early adulthood and type-specific assays are not 100% specific (Harrison 22e p.1108)","Failing to evaluate and treat sexual partners with contact in the relevant window (e.g., within the prior 60 days for urethritis), allowing reinfection (Harrison 22e p.1099)","Withholding presumptive syphilis treatment in a high-risk patient with a genital ulcer while awaiting seroconversion (Harrison 22e p.1108)","Attributing male urethritis recurrence to treatment failure without considering reexposure to an untreated partner or M. genitalium (often resistant to doxycycline and azithromycin) (Harrison 22e p.1100, 1099)"],"severityClassification":"No single staging system applies to STIs as a group; severity is assessed by syndrome and complications. Localized lower-tract syndromes (uncomplicated urethritis, cervicitis, vulvovaginitis, genital ulcer) are distinguished from complicated or upper-tract/disseminated disease (PID with peritonitis, perihepatitis, perisplenitis, or pelvic abscess; disseminated gonococcal infection; epididymo-orchitis), and from long-term oncologic or reproductive sequelae (cervical/anogenital neoplasia, infertility). Genital ulcer evaluation also uses lesion natural-history timelines (herpes 2-3 weeks; chancroid and syphilis up to ~6 weeks) to flag atypical persistence (Harrison 22e p.1104, 1108).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1096","Harrison 22e p.1097","Harrison 22e p.1098","Harrison 22e p.1099","Harrison 22e p.1100","Harrison 22e p.1101","Harrison 22e p.1104","Harrison 22e p.1108","Harrison 22e p.1109"],"crossLinks":[]},"shigellosis":{"name":"Shigellosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["bacillary dysentery"],"chapter":172,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1322-1325","clinicalPearls":["Classic dysentery is a syndrome of fever, intestinal cramps, and frequent passage of small, bloody, mucopurulent stools; tenesmus is characteristic (p.1322).","Shigella has a very low infectious dose; experimental infection of volunteers occurs with as few as ~100 CFU, accounting for high attack rates in day-care centers (33-73%) and high secondary attack rates among family members (26-33%) (p.1322).","Spread is primarily fecal-oral via hand carriage; the organism survives gastric passage owing to acid resistance, and can also be transmitted sexually (p.1322-1323).","Shigellosis typically evolves through four phases: incubation (usually 1-4 days, up to 8), watery diarrhea, dysentery, and a postinfectious phase (p.1323).","Dysentery reflects invasion of the colonic mucosa, producing acute colitis mainly involving the distal colon and rectum, with uninterrupted small-volume bloody mucopurulent stools, tenesmus, and cramps (p.1323).","Of the four species, S. flexneri predominates in least-developed areas while S. sonnei is more common in industrialized/emerging economies; S. dysenteriae type 1 (Shiga-toxin producer) drives the most severe epidemic disease (p.1322).","With effective antibiotics, clinical improvement occurs within ~48 h, with fewer complications, shorter symptoms, and clearance of Shigella from stool; in the US every culture-confirmed case is recommended for treatment because of transmissibility (p.1324).","Reactive arthritis (arthritis, ocular inflammation, urethritis) can develop weeks to months after infection, almost exclusively after S. flexneri and in HLA-B27-positive patients (about 3% of S. flexneri infections) (p.1323-1324)."],"pathophysiology":"Pathogenicity is governed by a ~214-kb virulence plasmid encoding a type III secretion system that injects effectors into host cells. After acid-resistant passage through the stomach, bacteria invade colonic epithelium either directly at crypt openings or after crossing M cells over mucosal lymphoid follicles; they induce apoptosis of resident macrophages, drive cytoskeletal uptake, lyse the containing vacuole to reach the cytosol, then spread cell-to-cell using actin-based motility. A massive cytokine-driven influx of polymorphonuclear leukocytes destabilizes the epithelial barrier and produces the acute colitis of shigellosis. Early watery diarrhea is attributed to combined enterotoxin (ShET-1) action and mucosal inflammation. In S. dysenteriae type 1, Shiga toxin (an A1-B5 toxin binding globotriaosylceramide and inhibiting protein synthesis via 28S rRNA N-glycosidase activity) is translocated into the circulation and, by acting on renal target cells, can cause hemolytic-uremic syndrome (p.1322-1323).","additionalDifferentials":["Salmonella infection","Campylobacter jejuni infection","Clostridium difficile colitis","Yersinia enterocolitica infection","Entamoeba histolytica (amebic dysentery)","Inflammatory bowel disease (first flare of Crohn's disease or ulcerative colitis), particularly in industrialized countries","Enteroinvasive E. coli (can cause a shigellosis-like dysentery)"],"infectionMimics":["Salmonellosis","Campylobacter jejuni enteritis","Clostridium difficile colitis","Yersinia enterocolitica enterocolitis","Amebic dysentery (Entamoeba histolytica)","Enteroinvasive E. coli dysentery","Enterohemorrhagic E. coli (EHEC) infection (predominant cause of HUS in industrialized countries)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Inflammatory bowel disease - Crohn's disease","Inflammatory bowel disease - ulcerative colitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Stool culture is the gold standard; sample the bloody/mucopurulent portion directly, or use rectal swabs (highest yield in the acute phase).","Because Shigella is fragile and dies during transport, use a holding medium (buffered glycerol saline or Cary-Blair) with prompt inoculation onto isolation media.","Use multiple media to raise yield: nonselective (bromocresol-purple lactose), low-selectivity (MacConkey, eosin-methylene blue), and high-selectivity (Hektoen, Salmonella-Shigella, or xylose-lysine-deoxycholate agar).","Microscopy of stool smears: bacterial enteroinvasive infection (including shigellosis) shows high PMN counts per field, whereas E. histolytica shows erythrophagocytic trophozoites with very few PMNs.","Identify shigellae as non-lactose-fermenting colonies that are glucose-positive (usually no gas), lactose-negative, H2S-negative, and nonmotile; confirm serogroup by slide agglutination.","Blood cultures are positive in fewer than 5% of cases but should be obtained when there is a clinical picture of severe sepsis.","Abdominal x-ray when toxic megacolon is suspected (transverse colon dilation, thumbprinting, loss of haustra, pseudopolyps; pneumoperitoneum if perforated).","In HUS: full blood count and smear (schizocytes), platelets, LDH, free hemoglobin, reticulocytes, creatinine; Coombs test (HUS is Coombs-negative).","PCR for Shigella virulence genes or mass spectrometry are available but not yet standardized for global use."],"redFlags":["Nonbloody diarrhea, moderate-to-severe dehydration, bacteremia, absence of fever, abdominal tenderness, and rectal prolapse are risk factors for death in clinically severe cases.","Altered consciousness (seizures, delirium, coma), especially in children under 5, carries a poor prognosis.","Abdominal distention and tenderness suggesting toxic megacolon, with or without peritonitis.","Pallor, asthenia, irritability, oliguria, increasing edema, or mucosal bleeding heralding hemolytic-uremic syndrome (most often after several days of diarrhea).","Fever persisting beyond 48-72 h in toxic megacolon raises the possibility of local perforation or abscess.","Severe, unusual manifestations such as meningitis in the absence of serious malnutrition may indicate an inborn innate-immune defect (e.g., IRAK-4 deficiency) and warrant genetic investigation."],"prognosis":"Most episodes are self-limited and resolve without treatment within about 1 week; with appropriate therapy recovery occurs within a few days to a week, usually without sequelae. Acute life-threatening complications occur most often in children under 5 (especially the malnourished) and the elderly. In HUS, acute renal failure occurs in 55-70% of cases but renal function recovers in most (up to ~70%). S. dysenteriae type 1 epidemics historically caused high mortality. Shigellosis is a major contributor to stunted growth among children in endemic regions because of the exudative enteropathy and anorexia it causes.","pitfalls":["Avoid antimotility agents in bloody diarrhea: they have been implicated in prolonged fever in shigellosis, are suspected of increasing the risk of toxic megacolon, and were implicated in HUS in children with EHEC infection.","Because shigellosis often manifests only as watery diarrhea, systematic attempts to isolate Shigella are necessary - a watery presentation does not exclude the diagnosis.","Shigella is fragile and commonly disappears during transport with changes in temperature and pH; failure to use a holding medium and prompt inoculation leads to false-negative cultures.","Negative initial blood cultures do not exclude shigellosis (positive in fewer than 5% of cases).","Predisposing factors for toxic megacolon - hypokalemia and use of opioids, anticholinergics, loperamide, psyllium seeds, and antidepressants - should be sought and addressed.","In HUS, oral rehydration solutions and potassium-rich nutrition must be discontinued (water restriction is required), in contrast to routine rehydration management.","Bacteriologic proof in mild adult cases often arrives only after symptoms have resolved, so empiric decisions cannot await culture in fragile patients."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal grading scale. Severity is described as depending on infecting serotype and, more importantly, on host age, immunologic and nutritional status. The clinical course is staged in four phases (incubation, watery diarrhea, dysentery, postinfectious). Severe/complicated disease is recognized by markers of poor outcome (nonbloody diarrhea, moderate-to-severe dehydration, bacteremia, absence of fever, abdominal tenderness, rectal prolapse, altered consciousness) and by major complications: intestinal (toxic megacolon, perforation, rectal prolapse), metabolic (hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, dehydration), and HUS (in S. dysenteriae type 1).","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, Chapter 172: Shigellosis (Sansonetti PJ, Bergounioux J), pp.1322-1325.","Kotloff KL et al. The incidence, aetiology, and adverse clinical consequences of less severe diarrhoeal episodes among infants and children residing in low-income and middle-income countries (GEMS follow-on). Lancet Glob Health 7:E568, 2019.","WHO. Guidelines for the control of shigellosis, including epidemics due to Shigella dysenteriae type 1.","Niyogi SK. Shigellosis. J Microbiol 43:133, 2005."],"crossLinks":[]},"short_bowel_syndrome":{"name":"Short bowel syndrome","system":"GI / Hepatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SBS","short gut syndrome","intestinal failure"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / AASLD & ACG","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A malabsorptive state resulting from loss of functional small-intestinal length, usually after extensive surgical resection for conditions such as mesenteric ischaemia, Crohn disease, trauma, or radiation injury","Clinical severity depends on the length and site of remaining bowel, whether the ileocaecal valve and colon are preserved, and the health of the residual intestine","The main manifestations are diarrhoea, steatorrhoea, dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, weight loss, and specific nutrient deficiencies","Loss of the terminal ileum impairs absorption of vitamin B12 and bile salts, and bile-salt spillage into the colon can worsen diarrhoea and predispose to gallstones","An intact colon is nutritionally valuable because it salvages energy by fermenting malabsorbed carbohydrate into short-chain fatty acids","Over months to years the residual bowel undergoes adaptation, increasing absorptive capacity, so nutritional needs may lessen with time","Management is staged: fluid and electrolyte correction, parenteral nutrition when needed, gradual introduction of enteral feeding to drive adaptation, and dietary modification","Long-term parenteral nutrition carries important complications including catheter-related bloodstream infection, venous thrombosis, and intestinal-failure-associated liver disease","Enterohormonal therapy (such as GLP-2 analogues) and, in selected refractory cases, intestinal transplantation are options for those dependent on parenteral support"],"pathophysiology":"Short bowel syndrome arises when the absorptive surface of the small intestine is reduced below the level needed to maintain nutrition and hydration, most often after massive resection. The functional consequences depend on which segment is lost: jejunal loss reduces overall absorptive surface, while ileal loss additionally impairs vitamin B12 and bile-salt reabsorption, disrupting the enterohepatic circulation and causing bile-salt diarrhoea and fat malabsorption. Loss of the ileocaecal valve accelerates transit and predisposes to small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth. Rapid transit and reduced surface lead to fluid, electrolyte, and nutrient losses. Over time the remnant intestine adapts through mucosal hyperplasia and slowed transit, partially restoring absorptive capacity.","additionalDifferentials":["Chronic pancreatic exocrine insufficiency","Coeliac disease and other mucosal malabsorptive disorders","Small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth","Inflammatory bowel disease with active malabsorption","Chronic infective or inflammatory diarrhoea","Enteric fistula causing functional bowel loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Assessment of remaining bowel anatomy and length from operative records and imaging","Nutritional and electrolyte panel including magnesium, calcium, and trace elements","Fat-soluble vitamin, vitamin B12, and iron status","Monitoring of fluid balance and stool or stoma output","Liver function monitoring in patients on parenteral nutrition and evaluation for bacterial overgrowth"],"redFlags":["Severe dehydration with acute kidney injury or dangerous electrolyte disturbances","Catheter-related bloodstream infection or sepsis in patients on parenteral nutrition","Progressive intestinal-failure-associated liver disease or cholestasis","Central venous thrombosis threatening long-term access for parenteral nutrition"],"prognosis":"Outlook depends on residual bowel length and anatomy and on adaptation. Many patients eventually achieve enteral autonomy, while those with very short remnants or absent colon may need lifelong parenteral nutrition, whose complications (liver disease, line sepsis, thrombosis) drive much of the long-term morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Underestimating fluid and electrolyte losses, particularly magnesium, in high-output patients","Withholding enteral nutrition entirely and thereby delaying adaptation","Overlooking vitamin B12 and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency after ileal resection","Missing small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth or catheter sepsis as causes of deterioration"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","AASLD/ACG guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"short_stature":{"name":"Short Stature","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["growth failure","poor linear growth","faltering height","paediatric short stature"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Short stature is generally defined as height more than about 2 standard deviations below the mean for age and sex, but the growth velocity and trajectory matter more than a single measurement.","The two most common causes are normal variants: familial (genetic) short stature and constitutional delay of growth and puberty.","Serial, accurately measured heights plotted on a growth chart are the single most valuable assessment, since a falling growth velocity is a key warning sign.","Bone age radiography helps distinguish constitutional delay (delayed bone age, preserved height potential) from familial short stature (bone age near chronological age).","A low or crossing-down growth velocity with poor weight gain suggests systemic disease or malnutrition, whereas short but proportionate growth with normal velocity favours a normal variant.","Endocrine causes such as growth hormone deficiency and hypothyroidism typically produce a child who is relatively overweight for height, in contrast to malnutrition or chronic illness.","Disproportionate short stature points towards a skeletal dysplasia and warrants assessment of body segment ratios.","Estimating mid-parental target height helps interpret whether a child's growth is appropriate for their genetic background."],"pathophysiology":"Linear growth depends on an intact growth plate responding to growth hormone, insulin-like growth factor 1, thyroid hormone, and sex steroids, supported by adequate nutrition and freedom from chronic illness. Short stature arises when any of these are disturbed: genetic determination of a lower height potential, temporary delay in the tempo of maturation, endocrine deficiency, chronic systemic disease, undernutrition, or intrinsic bone/cartilage disorders. The pattern of growth velocity, body proportions, and bone age reflects which mechanism predominates.","additionalDifferentials":["Familial (genetic) short stature","Constitutional delay of growth and puberty","Growth hormone deficiency","Hypothyroidism","Chronic systemic disease (e.g. coeliac disease, inflammatory bowel disease, renal or cardiac disease)","Turner syndrome and other genetic/syndromic causes","Skeletal dysplasia","Undernutrition or psychosocial deprivation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Accurate serial height and weight with growth velocity calculation","Bone age radiograph","Screening bloods (full blood count, renal and liver function, inflammatory markers, coeliac serology)","Thyroid function and, where indicated, IGF-1 and growth hormone provocation testing","Karyotype or genetic testing in girls (to exclude Turner syndrome) and where a syndrome is suspected","Skeletal survey if disproportion suggests a dysplasia"],"redFlags":["A crossing-down growth velocity or fall across major height centiles","Short stature with neurological signs, headaches, or visual disturbance suggesting an intracranial lesion","Disproportionate short stature suggesting a skeletal dysplasia","Poor growth with weight loss or gastrointestinal symptoms suggesting chronic disease","Dysmorphic features or other findings suggesting a genetic syndrome"],"prognosis":"Normal variants such as familial short stature and constitutional delay have an excellent prognosis with normal health, and constitutional delay usually reaches a normal adult height; outcomes for pathological causes depend on early identification and treatment of the underlying condition.","pitfalls":["Relying on a single height measurement rather than growth velocity over time","Labelling a child as a normal variant without excluding treatable disease when velocity is poor","Missing an intracranial cause of growth hormone deficiency","Failing to consider Turner syndrome in a short girl"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"sialadenitis":{"name":"Acute sialadenitis","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["salivary gland infection","acute suppurative parotitis","sialoadenitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sialadenitis is inflammation of a salivary gland, most commonly affecting the parotid or submandibular gland","Acute bacterial infection typically develops in dehydrated, debilitated or postoperative patients with reduced salivary flow, classically ascending parotitis","It presents with a painful, tender, swollen gland, and gentle massage may express pus from the duct opening in the mouth","Staphylococcus aureus is the usual organism in acute suppurative parotitis, whereas viral parotitis (mumps) is a key non-bacterial cause","Submandibular sialadenitis is often related to a stone (sialolith) obstructing the duct, causing pain and swelling that worsen at mealtimes","General measures such as good hydration, oral hygiene, warm compresses, gland massage and sialogogues promote salivary flow and recovery, alongside appropriate antibiotics for bacterial disease","An enlarging, fluctuant, non-resolving gland may indicate abscess formation needing drainage","Recurrent or chronic sialadenitis warrants imaging to look for stones, strictures or an underlying mass or systemic disease"],"pathophysiology":"Salivary glands are normally protected from ascending infection by continuous salivary flow, which flushes the ducts, and by antibacterial constituents of saliva. When flow is reduced, by dehydration, poor oral intake, debility, medications that dry the mouth, or mechanical obstruction from a duct stone or stricture, oral bacteria ascend the duct and infect the gland, producing acute suppurative sialadenitis with parenchymal inflammation and pus. Stasis of saliva also predisposes to stone formation, and calculi in turn obstruct outflow, causing painful mealtime swelling and recurrent infection. Repeated episodes lead to chronic inflammation, ductal changes and glandular fibrosis. Viral sialadenitis such as mumps causes a non-suppurative inflammatory swelling.","additionalDifferentials":["Mumps and other viral parotitis","Salivary duct calculus without active infection","Salivary gland tumour (benign or malignant)","Sjogren syndrome and other autoimmune sialadenitis","Sarcoidosis or IgG4-related disease of the salivary glands","Reactive cervical lymphadenopathy near the gland","Dental or masticator space infection"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Ultrasound of the gland to assess for stones, abscess or a mass","Culture of pus expressed from the duct or obtained at drainage","Plain radiography or CT to detect calculi","Sialography or MR sialography for ductal anatomy in recurrent disease","Blood tests including inflammatory markers and, where relevant, mumps serology and autoimmune screening","Fine needle aspiration or biopsy if a neoplasm is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly spreading swelling, systemic sepsis or airway compromise from a deep neck or floor of mouth extension (an emergency)","A persistent hard gland mass, facial nerve weakness or skin fixation suggesting malignancy","Fluctuant swelling indicating an abscess requiring drainage","Failure to resolve with appropriate treatment, prompting imaging and reassessment","Bilateral gland involvement with systemic features suggesting a systemic or autoimmune cause"],"prognosis":"Acute bacterial sialadenitis usually resolves with rehydration, salivary stimulation and antibiotics, and outcomes are good when the cause is treated; recurrent obstruction from stones or strictures and chronic sialadenitis may require repeated treatment or gland surgery.","pitfalls":["Overlooking dehydration and reduced salivary flow as the precipitant, especially postoperatively","Missing an obstructing calculus in recurrent mealtime swelling","Assuming all salivary swellings are infective and overlooking a tumour","Delaying drainage of an established abscess","Neglecting facial nerve assessment when a hard parotid mass is present"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"sick_sinus_syndrome":{"name":"Sick sinus syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SSS","sinus node dysfunction","sinoatrial node disease","tachy-brady syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Sick sinus syndrome is a spectrum of sinoatrial node dysfunction producing inappropriate bradycardia, sinus pauses or arrest, sinoatrial exit block, and chronotropic incompetence","A common variant is the tachy-brady syndrome, in which paroxysms of atrial tachyarrhythmia (often atrial fibrillation) alternate with symptomatic bradycardia or pauses, especially on termination of the tachyarrhythmia","Symptoms are often intermittent and non-specific, including fatigue, dizziness, presyncope, syncope, and exertional breathlessness from failure of the rate to rise","Because symptoms are paroxysmal, prolonged or ambulatory monitoring is frequently required to correlate them with rhythm disturbances","It is predominantly a disease of older adults from degenerative fibrosis of the sinus node, but drugs, ischaemia, and infiltrative disease can contribute","Reversible contributors such as rate-slowing drugs, hypothyroidism, and electrolyte disturbance should be excluded before attributing symptoms to intrinsic node disease","Permanent pacing is the main treatment for symptomatic bradycardia, and the tachy-brady form often needs both pacing and rhythm or rate control of the tachyarrhythmia","Anticoagulation should be considered when atrial fibrillation coexists, based on thromboembolic risk"],"pathophysiology":"Sick sinus syndrome results from failure of normal impulse generation in the sinoatrial node or failure of conduction from the node to the surrounding atrium. The most common cause is idiopathic degenerative fibrosis of the node and perinodal tissue with ageing. Ischaemia, infiltrative and inflammatory diseases, prior cardiac surgery, and autonomic influences also contribute, and many drugs suppress node function. In the tachy-brady syndrome, atrial remodelling supports tachyarrhythmias, while the diseased node produces prolonged pauses when the tachyarrhythmia stops because of overdrive suppression of an already impaired pacemaker.","additionalDifferentials":["Physiological sinus bradycardia (for example in athletes)","Drug-induced bradycardia from rate-slowing agents","AV block and other conduction disturbances","Hypothyroidism and other metabolic causes of bradycardia","Neurocardiogenic (vasovagal) syncope","Carotid sinus hypersensitivity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG and prolonged or ambulatory monitoring to correlate symptoms with rhythm","Implantable loop recorder when symptoms are infrequent","Review of rate-slowing medications and thyroid function testing","Electrolyte assessment and evaluation for ischaemia when relevant","Exercise testing to assess chronotropic competence","Echocardiography to evaluate structural heart disease"],"redFlags":["Syncope or recurrent presyncope from prolonged sinus pauses or arrest","Very long pauses, particularly following termination of a tachyarrhythmia in tachy-brady syndrome","Haemodynamic compromise from severe bradycardia","Coexisting atrial fibrillation raising thromboembolic and stroke risk"],"prognosis":"Sick sinus syndrome is rarely immediately life-threatening but causes considerable morbidity from recurrent symptoms; pacing effectively relieves bradycardic symptoms, and overall outcome is largely determined by associated atrial fibrillation and thromboembolic risk and by underlying cardiac disease.","pitfalls":["Attributing symptoms to intrinsic node disease without excluding reversible drug or metabolic causes","Failing to capture the arrhythmia because monitoring was too brief for paroxysmal symptoms","Overlooking the need for anticoagulation when atrial fibrillation coexists","Treating bradycardia with pacing while ignoring the tachyarrhythmia component of tachy-brady syndrome"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"sickle_cell_disease":{"name":"Sickle Cell Disease","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sickle cell anemia","hemoglobinopathy"],"chapter":103,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.769-776","clinicalPearls":["Sickle cell disease is a clinical/haematologic phenotype caused by an assortment of genotypes; sickle cell anaemia (HbSS, homozygosity for the HbS Glu6Val mutation) is the most common, followed by HbSC disease (compound heterozygosity for HbS and HbC) and the HbS-beta thalassaemias (p.772).","The chief presenting symptom is pain: a hand-foot syndrome (dactylitis) in young children, and typical acute painful episodes in older children and adults (p.772).","Acute painful episodes are the most common acute events; pain is typically unprovoked, severe, in extremities or torso, often symmetrical and stereotypical for each patient, and lasts days to weeks (p.773).","In HbSC disease and HbS-beta+ thalassaemia, acute vaso-occlusive episodes occur at about half the rate of sickle cell anaemia and complications develop later; rarely such patients are asymptomatic (p.772).","Complications fall into two related groups: those driven by sickle vaso-occlusion (pain, acute chest syndrome, osteonecrosis, splenic infarction) and those driven by intravascular haemolysis (stroke, pulmonary/systemic vasculopathy, nephropathy, leg ulcers, gallstones, priapism); vaso-occlusive complications respond best to HbF induction (p.772-773).","Carriers of sickle cell trait (HbAS) outnumber patients with disease ~25 to 1; trait is usually benign with normal life expectancy, but carries a small risk of sudden death from heat-related exertional rhabdomyolysis and complications such as haematuria, papillary necrosis, hyposthenuria, splenic infarction at altitude and increased VTE risk (p.772, 775).","Autosplenectomy from repeated childhood splenic infarction is usual in sickle cell anaemia; a palpable spleen after age 5 suggests a coexisting genotype (e.g. thalassaemia or HbC) (p.769-770 reference and Table 103-2).","Hydroxyurea (acting mainly by inducing HbF) is standard of care for all patients with sickle cell anaemia and HbS-beta0 thalassaemia regardless of symptoms; in adults it roughly halves pain and acute chest syndrome and reduced mortality by 49% over 17.5 years (p.775).","Cure is possible with HLA-identical related-donor allogeneic stem cell transplant (event-free survival >95%, though only ~15% of patients have a fully matched donor) and with two approved ex vivo autologous gene therapies (lentiviral betaT87Q HbA gene; CRISPR/Cas9 disruption of the BCL11A enhancer) (p.775-776)."],"pathophysiology":"HbS behaves like HbA except that it polymerises when deoxygenated: contacts between beta6 valine residues of deoxy-HbS and beta/alpha-globin residues form hemoglobin fascicles that injure the sickle erythrocyte, after a delay between initiation of polymerisation and accumulation of damaging polymer. Polymerisation depends on roughly the 30th power of hemoglobin concentration, so small changes in concentration or cell dehydration have large effects. Membrane vesiculation/microparticle release, activation of dehydrating channels (Gardos, K/Cl cotransport, Psickle) which raise mean corpuscular HbS concentration and reduce deformability, translocation of phosphatidylserine to the outer membrane leaflet, and oxidation produce irreversibly sickled cells (ISCs) that remain deformed regardless of oxygenation. Sickle erythrocytes adhere to endothelium and other blood cells, occluding small and large vessels (vaso-occlusion), and survive <20 days (normal ~120), haemolysing intra- and extravascularly. Intravascular haemolysis depletes haptoglobin and hemopexin and liberates heme, arginase and other DAMPs which scavenge nitric oxide, activate platelets and endothelium, reduce antioxidant activity, cause vasoconstriction and drive inflammation (p.772-774).","additionalDifferentials":[],"infectionMimics":["Acute chest syndrome (pneumonia-like illness with chest pain, cough, fever, hypoxia and a pulmonary infiltrate) closely resembles and may coexist with bacterial pneumonia; antibiotics are almost always used even though a causative bacterium is often not cultured (p.775).","Multiorgan failure accompanying severe acute chest syndrome is often confused with, and can coexist with, sepsis (p.774, Table 103-3)."],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["High-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) and hemoglobin electrophoresis to quantify hemoglobin fractions (HbS, HbF, HbA, HbA2, HbC) and distinguish trait, sickle cell anaemia and HbSC disease (Table 103-2, Fig. 103-4, p.772).","Peripheral blood film: irreversibly sickled cells, target cells and nucleated red cells in HbSS/HbS-beta0 thalassaemia; HbC crystals, taco-folded cells and contracted microspherocytes in HbSC disease (Fig. 103-4, p.772-774).","DNA-based diagnosis for genetic counselling and family planning; antenatal diagnosis by chorionic villus sampling or cell-free DNA (p.772, 776).","Transcranial Doppler screening in children (ages 2-16) to identify stroke risk (Table 103-3, p.776).","Echocardiographic measurement of tricuspid regurgitant jet velocity (TRV) annually to screen for pulmonary hypertension.","Screening for microalbuminuria from age 10 for nephropathy, and annual fluorescein angiography from age 10 for retinopathy."],"redFlags":["Acute chest syndrome - chest pain, cough, fever, hypoxia and a pulmonary infiltrate; can be devastating with multilobar involvement, severe hypoxia, ARDS, multiorgan failure and rapid death (often from embolised necrotic marrow) (p.775).","Splenic sequestration (acute anemic episode) - rapid drop in hemoglobin; recurrent episodes are an indication for splenectomy (Table 103-3, p.774).","Aplastic crisis from parvovirus B19 infection causing acute worsening of anaemia with reticulocytopenia (Table 103-3, p.774; reticulocytopenia in acute worsening noted elsewhere).","Stroke and silent infarction - occur in 10-15% of cases, from early childhood into adulthood, with neurocognitive abnormalities even without apparent stroke; haemorrhagic stroke in adults (Table 103-3, p.773).","Functional/anatomic asplenia (autosplenectomy) places patients at high risk of overwhelming infection with encapsulated organisms, notably overwhelming pneumococcal disease (p.776, 769/Table; pneumococcal chapter p.999).","Sudden unexpected death shortly after admission for acute pain (possible arrhythmia or pulmonary embolism); thrombocytopenia, leukocytosis >20,000 and rapidly developing acute anaemia portend severe acute chest syndrome and multiorgan failure (p.773, 775).","Multiorgan failure - can accompany severe acute chest syndrome, affecting CNS, liver, muscle, lung and kidney (Table 103-3, p.774)."],"prognosis":"In the United States childhood death is now rare and the median age of death in sickle cell anaemia is in the fifth or sixth decade; by contrast in high-burden regions such as Nigeria most affected newborns die before age 5. Hydroxyurea reduced mortality by 49% over 17.5 years of follow-up. Genotype modifies severity: HbSC disease and HbS-beta+ thalassaemia have about half the complication rate of HbSS, and coexistent alpha thalassaemia (present in ~30% of HbSS) is associated with fewer strokes and leg ulcers and less pulmonary vascular and renal disease. Allogeneic transplantation and gene therapy offer potential cure (p.772, 775-776).","pitfalls":["No diagnostic test can confirm or refute an acute pain episode; physical examination is often unhelpful and the presence of ISCs and the reticulocyte count are of no diagnostic value (p.773).","Supplemental oxygen is unnecessary unless hypoxia is present, and NSAIDs add little once intravenous opioids are given; overhydration and excessive opioids can compound dyspnoea and hypoxia in acute chest syndrome (p.773, 775).","Opioids should not be dosed on an 'as-needed' schedule - patient-controlled or fixed scheduled dosing with rescue doses is preferred, with monitoring to avoid excessive sedation; many patients are opioid-tolerant and need higher doses (p.773).","Transfusions are both overutilised and underutilised; they should usually be avoided in acute pain episodes and for stable chronic anaemia (p.776).","A palpable spleen after age 5 in sickle cell disease suggests a coexisting genotype rather than simple HbSS (p.769-770)."],"severityClassification":"Severity is largely defined by genotype: sickle cell trait (HbAS, usually benign); sickle cell anaemia (HbSS, vaso-occlusion- and haemolysis-related complications, Hb ~7-10 g/dL); HbS-beta0 thalassaemia (complication rate similar to HbSS); HbS-beta+ thalassaemia (~half the rate of HbSS depending on percent HbA); HbSC disease (near-asymptomatic to as severe as HbSS, ~half the complication rate, increased retinopathy risk); HbSE (resembles HbS-beta+ thalassaemia, often Asian/Indian ancestry); and HbSS-alpha thalassaemia (phenocopies HbS-beta0 thalassaemia with fewer strokes/leg ulcers and less pulmonary/renal disease). HbF level (influenced by HbS haplotype) modifies severity (p.772).","references":["Harrison 22e p.769-776"],"crossLinks":["pneumococcal_disease"]},"sigmoid_volvulus":{"name":"Sigmoid volvulus","system":"GI / Colorectal / Surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["volvulus of the sigmoid colon","sigmoid colon volvulus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with the features of large bowel obstruction: gross abdominal distension, colicky pain, absolute constipation, and often relatively little vomiting early on","Most common in elderly, institutionalised, or neuropsychiatric patients and those with chronic constipation and a long redundant sigmoid loop on a narrow mesenteric base","The abdominal radiograph classically shows a hugely dilated loop arising from the pelvis, described as a coffee-bean or inverted-U sign pointing toward the right upper quadrant","In the absence of peritonitis or bowel compromise, initial treatment is endoscopic decompression (flexible sigmoidoscopy or rigid scope) with insertion of a flatus tube","Successful decompression relieves the acute episode but recurrence is common, so elective sigmoid resection is often advised in fit patients","Signs of peritonitis, failed decompression, or suspicion of gangrenous or perforated bowel mandate emergency laparotomy","At emergency operation for compromised bowel, resection (commonly a Hartmann's procedure) is performed rather than simple untwisting","A CT scan confirms the diagnosis, shows the whirl sign of the twisted mesentery, and assesses for ischaemia or perforation"],"pathophysiology":"Sigmoid volvulus occurs when a redundant, elongated sigmoid loop twists around its narrow mesenteric pedicle, producing a closed-loop large bowel obstruction. Chronic constipation and a lengthy, mobile sigmoid on a narrow mesenteric base predispose to torsion. The twist obstructs the lumen at both ends, and progressive distension raises intraluminal and intramural pressure. If the mesenteric vessels are compromised by the torsion, the loop becomes ischaemic, progressing to gangrene and perforation with faeculent peritonitis and sepsis. Massive gaseous distension of the closed loop is characteristic.","additionalDifferentials":["Caecal volvulus","Obstructing colorectal carcinoma","Pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie's syndrome)","Diverticular stricture with obstruction","Faecal impaction with megacolon","Adhesional or hernia-related large bowel obstruction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Plain abdominal radiograph for the characteristic dilated sigmoid loop","CT abdomen and pelvis to confirm the diagnosis, show the whirl sign, and assess for ischaemia or perforation","Bloods including full blood count, electrolytes, and lactate to gauge ischaemia and fitness for intervention","Endoscopic assessment of mucosal viability during attempted decompression"],"redFlags":["Peritonitis, systemic sepsis, or shock suggesting ischaemic or perforated bowel (surgical emergency)","Bloodstained or necrotic mucosa or dark fluid at decompression indicating gangrene","Failure of endoscopic decompression","Rising lactate, worsening distension, or free gas on imaging"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated cases usually respond to endoscopic decompression but recur frequently unless definitive surgery is performed. Emergency surgery for gangrenous or perforated bowel in typically frail, elderly patients carries substantial morbidity and mortality.","pitfalls":["Persisting with endoscopic decompression when peritonitis or ischaemia is present","Mistaking the presentation for simple constipation and delaying imaging","Failing to offer elective resection to fit patients after decompression, allowing recurrence","Overlooking an obstructing carcinoma as an alternative cause of distal large bowel obstruction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"silicosis":{"name":"Silicosis","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["silicosis","silica pneumoconiosis","quartz dust lung disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / GOLD & ATS guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Silicosis is a fibrotic lung disease caused by inhaling crystalline silica dust, seen in mining, quarrying, sandblasting, stone-cutting, and engineered-stone work","Chronic silicosis develops after prolonged exposure and may be asymptomatic initially, later causing exertional dyspnoea and cough","Accelerated and acute (silicoproteinosis) forms follow intense exposure and progress far more rapidly","Chest imaging classically shows small upper-zone nodular opacities, sometimes with eggshell calcification of hilar lymph nodes","Simple nodular disease can coalesce into progressive massive fibrosis, producing large conglomerate masses and significant impairment","Silica exposure substantially increases susceptibility to tuberculosis (silicotuberculosis), which should be actively sought and treated","There is no treatment that reverses fibrosis; management focuses on stopping exposure, supportive care, and screening for and treating complications","Silica is also associated with increased risks of lung cancer, chronic airflow obstruction, and certain autoimmune diseases"],"pathophysiology":"Inhaled crystalline silica particles reaching the alveoli are taken up by macrophages. The reactive silica surface is toxic to these macrophages, triggering activation of inflammasome pathways, cell death, and release of inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species. This provokes recruitment of further inflammatory cells and stimulation of fibroblasts, which lay down collagen to form the characteristic silicotic nodules. Continued or intense exposure sustains the cycle, with nodules enlarging and coalescing into progressive massive fibrosis, while impaired macrophage function increases vulnerability to mycobacterial infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Other pneumoconioses such as coal workers' pneumoconiosis","Pulmonary tuberculosis (which may coexist)","Sarcoidosis","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and other interstitial lung diseases","Metastatic or miliary nodular lung disease","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiography with attention to upper-zone nodularity and eggshell nodal calcification","High-resolution CT to characterise nodules and detect progressive massive fibrosis","Pulmonary function testing to quantify restrictive, obstructive, or mixed impairment","Screening for tuberculosis given the heightened risk","Detailed occupational exposure history including engineered-stone and sandblasting work"],"redFlags":["New constitutional symptoms, haemoptysis, or cavitation suggesting tuberculosis or malignancy","Rapidly progressive breathlessness indicating accelerated or acute silicosis","Enlarging conglomerate masses consistent with progressive massive fibrosis","Development of respiratory failure or cor pulmonale"],"prognosis":"Chronic simple silicosis may remain stable with limited impairment, but progressive massive fibrosis, accelerated, and acute forms carry a poor prognosis with progressive respiratory failure; the lifelong increased risk of tuberculosis and lung cancer means continued surveillance is important, and outcomes improve when exposure ceases early.","pitfalls":["Failing to elicit an occupational silica exposure history, especially newer engineered-stone work","Overlooking coexisting tuberculosis in a patient with silicosis","Attributing all nodular change to silicosis without considering malignancy","Not removing the worker from ongoing dust exposure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","GOLD/ATS guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"sj_gren_s_disease":{"name":"Sjögren’s Disease","system":"Immune-Mediated / Rheumatologic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sjögren’s syndrome","Sicca syndrome","Autoimmune epithelitis","Keratoconjunctivitis sicca with xerostomia"],"chapter":373,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2876","clinicalPearls":["Prototype autoimmune disease defined by lymphocytic infiltration of exocrine glands causing xerostomia and dry eyes (keratoconjunctivitis sicca), together with marked B-cell hyperactivity.","Strongly female-predominant (female-to-male ratio roughly 10–20:1) and chiefly affects middle-aged women, but can present at any age including childhood; prevalence is approximately 0.5–1%.","Earlier age at onset tends to mark a more aggressive phenotype with more systemic manifestations and more serum autoantibodies.","Can occur alone or in association with other systemic rheumatic diseases, most commonly rheumatoid arthritis, limited scleroderma, and systemic lupus erythematosus; 5–20% of patients with other autoimmune diseases can have sicca features.","Principal oral symptom is dryness (xerostomia) with difficulty swallowing dry food, burning mouth, increased dental caries; examination may show dry erythematous sticky mucosa, atrophic tongue papillae, and a deeply fissured tongue.","Intermittent or persistent enlargement of the parotid or other major salivary glands occurs in about two-thirds of patients.","Ocular involvement classically produces a sandy or gritty sensation under the lids, with burning, stranded secretions at the inner canthi, reduced tearing, redness, and photosensitivity from corneal and conjunctival epithelial destruction (keratoconjunctivitis sicca).","Extraglandular (systemic) manifestations occur in about one-third of patients and are categorized as nonspecific, periepithelial, immune complex–mediated, and lymphoma.","Carries the highest risk of lymphoma among all autoimmune diseases; most are extranodal, low-grade, marginal zone (MALT) B-cell lymphomas, often found incidentally on labial minor salivary gland biopsy.","Most patients (about two-thirds) have an elevated ESR, hypergammaglobulinemia, antinuclear antibodies, rheumatoid factor, and anti-Ro52/Ro60 and anti-La autoantibodies.","The disease course is usually slow and benign in the majority of patients; antecedent major stressful life events have been reported prior to onset.","Recently described as triggerable by PD-1/PD-L1 checkpoint inhibitor therapy."],"pathophysiology":"Activated T lymphocytes are the major infiltrating cells in affected exocrine (chiefly salivary and lacrimal) glands, with B-cell populations prevailing in heavily infiltrated labial minor salivary gland tissue; follicular, myeloid, and plasmacytoid dendritic cells and macrophages are also present, the latter (with inflammasome activation) being linked to lymphoma risk. Ductal and acinar epithelial cells play an active pathogenic role: they aberrantly express costimulatory molecules and surface Ro/La autoantigens, secrete proinflammatory cytokines and lymphocyte-attracting chemokines that support ectopic germinal center formation, express innate-immunity Toll-like receptors (TLR3, 7, 9), and display immunoregulatory molecules (ICAM, CD40). Epithelial cells produce B cell–activating factor (BAFF), induced by type I and II interferons, and circulating BAFF is elevated (especially with hypergammaglobulinemia and autoantibodies), exerting an antiapoptotic effect on B lymphocytes; epithelial cells themselves show increased apoptosis. Disease initiation and perpetuation reflect interplay of endogenous triggers (intracellular stress, inappropriate expression of endogenous nucleic acids) and exogenous triggers (viruses, hormonal factors, stressful life events) on a genetically hyperactive immune background. Established genetic loci include HLA-DQA1*0501, interferon/BAFF axis variants (IRF5, STAT4, BAFF), B-cell function genes (EBF1, BLK), and combined deficiencies of the classical complement pathway.","additionalDifferentials":["Sarcoidosis (granulomatous salivary gland infiltration, no Ro/La antibodies)","IgG4-related disease","Amyloidosis","Graft-versus-host disease","Diabetes mellitus and other metabolic causes of dry mouth/parotid enlargement","Drug-induced dryness (psychotherapeutic, parasympatholytic, antihypertensive agents)","Prior head and neck radiation","Autoimmune thyroid disease","Marginal zone (MALT) B-cell lymphoma complicating the disease"],"infectionMimics":["HIV infection with diffuse infiltrative lymphocytosis / sicca syndrome (young males, CD8+ glandular infiltrates, lacks anti-Ro/La, positive HIV serology)","Hepatitis C virus infection","Viral parotitis (mumps, influenza, Epstein-Barr virus, coxsackievirus A, cytomegalovirus)","Tuberculosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sarcoidosis","IgG4-related disease","Amyloidosis","Autoimmune thyroid disease","Diabetes mellitus, hyperlipoproteinemia (types IV and V), chronic pancreatitis, hepatic cirrhosis","Acromegaly and gonadal hypofunction","Eosinophilic sialodochitis","Drug-induced or radiation-induced gland dysfunction","Stevens-Johnson syndrome, pemphigoid, chronic conjunctivitis/blepharitis, hypovitaminosis A (dry eye)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Schirmer’s I test for tear flow (≤5 mm/5 min on at least one eye supports diagnosis)","Tear breakup time or tear lysozyme content for tear composition","Slit-lamp examination after lissamine green or Rose Bengal staining; ocular staining score ≥5 or van Bijsterveld score ≥4","Sialometry / unstimulated whole saliva flow rate (≤0.1 mL/min supportive)","Salivary gland ultrasound (emerging diagnostic and prognostic tool); MRI and magnetic resonance sialography","Labial minor salivary gland biopsy with focus score ≥1 (foci per 4 mm2)","Serum autoantibodies: anti-Ro52/Ro60, anti-La, rheumatoid factor, ANA; anticentromere (limited scleroderma–like), antimitochondrial (autoimmune cholangitis), anti-21-hydroxylase (blunted adrenal response), anti-citrullinated peptide (arthritis), anticalponin-3 (peripheral neuropathy)","Complement (low C4), serum protein electrophoresis, cryoglobulins, serum IgG4, thyroid autoantibodies","Testing to exclude chronic viral infection (HCV, HIV) and chest x-ray to exclude sarcoidosis","FDG-PET/CT to help exclude lymphoma when suspected"],"redFlags":["Parotid or major salivary gland enlargement, especially persistent or with a lymph node mass >7 cm — concern for lymphoma","Palpable purpura, urticarial lesions, skin ulcerations, or mononeuritis multiplex (immune complex–mediated vasculitis)","Low C4 complement, monoclonal gammopathy (paraproteinemia), and cryoglobulins — lymphoma risk markers","Heavy focal lymphocytic infiltrates with germinal center formation on salivary biopsy — lymphoma risk","B symptoms (constitutional) with lymphadenopathy","Severe tongue atrophy and leukopenia in the appropriate context","Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis with mixed type II/III cryoglobulinemia","Myelitis with anti-aquaporin-4 antibodies (rare CNS involvement)"],"prognosis":"The disease usually evolves slowly and follows a benign course in most patients. The dominant adverse outcome is malignant transformation: Sjögren’s disease carries the highest lymphoma risk of any autoimmune disease, with clinical (parotid enlargement, severe tongue atrophy, palpable purpura), hematologic/serologic (leukopenia, autoantibodies, low C4, monoclonal gammopathy, cryoglobulins), and histopathologic (germinal center formation, heavy focal lymphocytic infiltrates) risk factors. Survival is reduced in patients with B symptoms, lymph node mass >7 cm, and high or intermediate histologic grade. Patients also have an increased risk of cardiovascular disease, paralleling rheumatoid arthritis and SLE.","pitfalls":["Failing to take a careful medication history — diuretics, antihypertensives, anticholinergics, and antidepressants can themselves cause dryness and mimic or worsen sicca.","Overlooking HIV- or HCV-associated sicca syndrome, which can closely resemble Sjögren’s but lacks anti-Ro/La and has distinct demographics and glandular infiltrate phenotype.","Missing renal tubular dysfunction: interstitial nephritis may be asymptomatic, presenting only as hyposthenuria or renal tubular acidosis, and untreated acidosis can progress to nephrocalcinosis.","Attributing all salivary gland enlargement to benign disease and missing an incidental MALT lymphoma on labial biopsy.","Not excluding sarcoidosis, amyloidosis, IgG4-related disease, graft-versus-host disease, and prior head/neck radiation before diagnosing Sjögren’s disease.","Assuming arthritis is erosive — arthritis in primary Sjögren’s disease is characteristically nonerosive (may lead to Jaccoud’s arthropathy)."],"severityClassification":"Diagnosis/classification per the 2016 ACR-EULAR criteria: suspect in patients with dry eyes and/or dry mouth (or systemic features such as major salivary gland enlargement, fatigue, Raynaud’s, small-joint arthralgia/arthritis, palpable purpura/urticaria, renal tubular acidosis/membranoproliferative GN, ILD/small airway disease, autoimmune cholangitis, peripheral neuropathy, or MS-like lesions), supported by objective findings: unstimulated whole saliva flow ≤0.1 mL/min, Schirmer’s test ≤5 mm/5 min, ocular staining score ≥5 (or van Bijsterveld ≥4), serum anti-Ro antibodies, and minor salivary gland biopsy focus score ≥1. Exclusions include prior head/neck radiation, active HCV, AIDS, sarcoidosis, amyloidosis, graft-versus-host disease, and IgG4-related disease. Extraglandular disease severity is gauged by the categories of involvement (nonspecific, periepithelial, immune complex–mediated, and lymphoma).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2876","Harrison 22e p.2877","Harrison 22e p.2878","Harrison 22e p.2879"],"crossLinks":[]},"sjogren_syndrome":{"name":"Sjögren syndrome","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SS","sicca syndrome","Sjogren's syndrome","autoimmune epithelitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Characterised by dry eyes (keratoconjunctivitis sicca) and dry mouth (xerostomia) from immune-mediated destruction of lacrimal and salivary glands","Predominantly affects middle-aged women and may be primary or secondary to another connective tissue disease such as rheumatoid arthritis or lupus","Systemic features include fatigue, arthralgia, Raynaud phenomenon, parotid gland swelling, and cutaneous vasculitis","Anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB antibodies are characteristic, along with positive antinuclear antibody and rheumatoid factor","Objective sicca can be confirmed with Schirmer test, ocular surface staining, and unstimulated salivary flow; labial minor salivary gland biopsy shows focal lymphocytic sialadenitis","There is a markedly increased lifetime risk of B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma, particularly MALT lymphoma","Anti-Ro antibodies in pregnancy carry a risk of neonatal lupus and congenital heart block","Management centres on symptomatic relief of dryness (tear and saliva substitutes, secretagogues) with immunosuppression reserved for significant systemic involvement"],"pathophysiology":"Sjögren syndrome is a chronic autoimmune exocrinopathy in which lymphocytic (mainly CD4 T-cell and B-cell) infiltration of the exocrine glands destroys secretory epithelium, sometimes termed autoimmune epithelitis. B-cell hyperactivity produces autoantibodies (anti-Ro/SSA, anti-La/SSB) and hypergammaglobulinaemia, and sustained clonal B-cell proliferation underlies the elevated lymphoma risk. Type I interferon signalling and genetic HLA associations contribute to disease susceptibility. Extraglandular disease reflects immune complex deposition and lymphocytic infiltration of organs including skin, joints, lungs, kidneys, and nervous system.","additionalDifferentials":["Age-related or medication-induced sicca (anticholinergics)","Sarcoidosis and IgG4-related disease affecting salivary glands","Chronic viral infection (hepatitis C, HIV)","Radiation-induced glandular damage","Other connective tissue diseases with secondary sicca","Amyloidosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB antibodies, ANA, and rheumatoid factor","Schirmer test and ocular surface staining for aqueous tear deficiency","Unstimulated whole salivary flow and salivary gland ultrasound or imaging","Labial minor salivary gland biopsy showing focal lymphocytic sialadenitis","Serum immunoglobulins and protein electrophoresis; monitoring for lymphoma if persistent gland enlargement or new lymphadenopathy"],"redFlags":["Persistent unilateral parotid enlargement, lymphadenopathy, or new cytopenias suggesting lymphoma transformation","Signs of systemic vasculitis, mononeuritis multiplex, or interstitial lung disease","Renal involvement such as renal tubular acidosis or interstitial nephritis","Pregnancy with anti-Ro antibodies given the risk of fetal congenital heart block","Severe corneal complications from untreated dry eye"],"prognosis":"Most patients have a good prognosis with a slowly progressive course dominated by sicca symptoms, but systemic involvement and, importantly, lymphoma development can worsen outcomes. Lifelong monitoring for lymphoproliferative disease is warranted.","pitfalls":["Dismissing sicca symptoms as trivial and missing systemic or lymphomatous complications","Failing to consider mimics such as IgG4-related disease, sarcoidosis, or viral infection","Not counselling and monitoring anti-Ro-positive women in pregnancy for fetal heart block","Overlooking increased dental caries and ocular surface damage from chronic dryness","Attributing new persistent gland swelling to disease flare rather than investigating for lymphoma"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"skin_cancer":{"name":"Skin Cancer","system":"Dermatology / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["melanoma","basal cell carcinoma","squamous cell carcinoma"],"chapter":81,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.598-609","clinicalPearls":["Apply the ABCDE rule to pigmented lesions to detect melanoma early: Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variegation, Diameter >6 mm (pencil-eraser size), and Evolving (any change in size, shape, color, elevation, or new symptoms such as bleeding, itching, or crusting) (p.601).","The 'ugly duckling' sign: any nevus that looks atypical and different from the rest of an individual's nevi should be considered suspicious (p.601).","Melanoma is an aggressive malignancy of melanocytes and accounts for over half of all deaths from skin cancer, even though it is far less common than nonmelanoma skin cancer (p.598).","Basal cell carcinoma is among the most common cancers; its natural history is a slowly enlarging, locally invasive neoplasm with low metastatic potential (~0.1% in immunocompetent patients), though the risk of recurrence or a new primary NMSC is about 40% over 5 years (p.607).","Nodular BCC classically presents as a pearly nodule with tortuous telangiectatic vessels, rolled borders, and a central crust; pigmented BCC may contain melanin and be confused with melanoma (p.606-607).","Squamous cell carcinoma arises from keratinizing epidermal cells on sun-exposed skin and can metastasize (0.3-5.2%), most often to regional lymph nodes; lower-lip and ear tumors metastasize in 13% and 11% respectively (p.606-607).","Actinic keratoses and actinic cheilitis are premalignant forms of SCC; SCC in situ (Bowen's disease) presents as a scaling erythematous plaque, and treating premalignant/in-situ lesions reduces subsequent invasive disease (p.607).","NMSCs (SCC ~80%, BCC ~18% of cases) are the most common cancers in the US, with an estimated >5.3 million cases annually (p.606).","Breslow thickness (greatest tumor thickness measured from the top of the granular layer or ulcer base to the bottom of the tumor) is the best predictor of melanoma recurrence and, with ulceration, defines the T stage (p.601-602)."],"pathophysiology":"Melanoma is a malignancy of melanocytes, neural-crest-derived pigment-producing cells; most melanomas arise in skin but can occur in mucosa, eye, meninges, and upper esophagus. UV radiation is the dominant environmental driver, and cutaneous melanomas carry one of the highest somatic mutation rates of any cancer (>10 mutations/Mb), the majority bearing a UV signature. Key driver mutations affect the MAP-kinase and PI3K/AKT proliferative pathways: BRAF (commonly V600E) in 40-50% of cutaneous melanomas, NRAS in ~20%, and loss-of-function NF1 in 10-15%; germline CDKN2A (p16/ARF, RB and p53 pathways), CDK4, MITF, BAP1, POT1, TERT, and moderate-risk MC1R variants underlie inherited susceptibility. Nonmelanoma skin cancers are also UV-driven: SCC is associated with p53 and N-RAS damage, whereas BCC is driven by hedgehog (Hh) pathway activation, usually loss of function of the tumor-suppressor PTCH1 (which normally inhibits SMO). Merkel cell carcinoma is linked to the oncogenic Merkel cell polyomavirus, present in ~80% of tumors (p.598-607).","additionalDifferentials":["Benign nevomelanocytic nevus (vs melanoma)","Dysplastic/atypical nevus","Seborrheic keratosis","Lentigo","Nummular eczema and psoriasis (may mimic superficial BCC)","Wart or callus (may mimic SCC when inflammation is minimal)","Keratoacanthoma (rapidly growing low-grade SCC variant)","Merkel cell carcinoma","Cutaneous B- or T-cell lymphoma","Extramammary Paget's disease"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Superficial BCC mimicking nummular eczema or psoriasis","SCC mistaken for a wart or callus when inflammatory response is minimal","Pigmented (nodular) BCC mimicking melanoma","Desmoplastic melanoma mimicking nodular basal cell carcinoma","Benign nevus mimicking melanoma"],"additionalInvestigations":["Excisional biopsy with 1-3 mm narrow margins (preferred) for any changing/suspicious pigmented lesion; incisional (shave, saucerization, or punch) biopsy of the most nodular/darkest area when excision is impractical - incisional biopsy does not facilitate melanoma spread","Histopathology report should include Breslow thickness, mitotic rate, ulceration, lymphatic/vascular/neural invasion, regression, microsatellitosis, and margin status","Dermoscopy (low-level magnification with polarized light or water interface) and total-body photography for surveillance in high-risk patients","Genetic expression profiles (GEP, e.g. 31-gene), FISH with multiple probes, or comparative genomic hybridization to distinguish difficult melanomas from benign nevi and estimate prognosis","Complete blood count, complete metabolic panel, and serum LDH (LDH is part of AJCC stage IV criteria)","Sentinel lymph node biopsy for pathologic staging","CT, PET, or combined CT/PET (and MRI) when symptoms, exam, or abnormal labs suggest metastatic disease","Genetic testing for individuals with >=3 primary melanomas or with families having >=1 invasive melanoma plus >=2 cases of melanoma and/or pancreatic cancer, ocular melanoma, mesothelioma, or renal cell carcinoma on one side"],"redFlags":["Any pigmented lesion that has changed (size, shape, color, elevation) or that meets ABCDE criteria is a candidate for biopsy","An 'ugly duckling' nevus that differs from a patient's other nevi","New bleeding, itching, or crusting in a pigmented lesion","Thick and/or ulcerated melanoma (key adverse T-stage features)","Microsatellite lesions, in-transit metastases, nodal involvement, elevated LDH, or distant metastases to brain/liver/bone","SCC on the lower lip or ear, recurrent SCC (~30% metastatic risk), or SCC in scars/chronic ulcers, genital/mucosal surfaces, or in immunosuppressed patients (aggressive behavior)","Rapidly expanding lesion in an older, immunosuppressed, UV-exposed patient (MCC: AEIOU - Asymptomatic, Expanding rapidly, Immunosuppression, Older than 50, UV-exposed site)"],"prognosis":"Melanoma prognosis is dominated by Breslow thickness and ulceration (T stage); favorable primary sites are the forearm and leg, unfavorable sites the scalp, hands, feet, and mucous membranes. 10-year melanoma-specific survival ranges from >99% for stage 0 (in situ) and ~98% for stage IA down to ~24-30% for stage IIID. Women with stage I/II disease and younger patients do better; older men have worse outcomes (delayed diagnosis, thicker tumors, more head/neck, acral and BRAF wild-type tumors). High mitotic rate and lymphatic/vascular invasion are additional adverse factors. Overall melanoma mortality has been falling, attributed to immunotherapy and targeted therapy. BCC rarely metastasizes (~0.1% in immunocompetent patients) but recurs frequently. Cutaneous SCC metastasizes in 0.3-5.2% of cases overall, higher for lip/ear/recurrent/immunosuppressed disease. Merkel cell carcinoma is aggressive: ~90% cure with local disease but ~52% survival with nodal and ~10% with distant disease (p.602-609).","pitfalls":["Morpheaform, infiltrative, and micronodular BCCs have indistinct borders and subtle presentation, so treatment is often delayed and tumors are more extensive than they appear clinically","SCC can be mistaken for a benign wart or callus when the inflammatory response is minimal","Pigmented BCC may be confused with melanoma; desmoplastic melanoma can mimic nodular BCC","Melanoma frequently develops on non-sun-exposed skin (acral, mucosal); in dark-skinned, Hispanic, and East Asian populations melanoma is often non-UV-related, diagnosed at higher stage, and has worse outcomes","The 31-gene GEP can estimate distant-relapse probability but has not supplanted prognostic estimates from surgical staging, and genomic pathway data are not yet incorporated into AJCC staging","Standard labs and imaging rarely detect occult metastatic disease in early melanoma and should be directed by symptoms/exam findings"],"severityClassification":"Melanoma is staged by the AJCC TNM system (T stage built primarily on Breslow thickness and ulceration; pathologic stage incorporates sentinel lymph node biopsy results), from stage 0 (in situ, Tis) through stage IV (distant metastasis); 10-year melanoma-specific survival is depicted by stage in Table 81-3. Merkel cell carcinoma has its own TNM staging incorporating tumor size (<2 cm vs >2 cm), nodal status, and distant metastases (p.602-609).","references":["Harrison 22e p.598-609"],"crossLinks":[]},"sleep_apnea":{"name":"Sleep Apnea","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["obstructive sleep apnea","OSA","central sleep apnea"],"chapter":308,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2277-2282","clinicalPearls":["OSA diagnosis requires either symptoms of nocturnal breathing disturbance (snoring, snorting, gasping, breathing pauses) impairing quality of life or unexplained daytime sleepiness/fatigue, PLUS at least 5 obstructive apnoeas/hypopnoeas per hour of sleep (apnoea-hypopnoea index, AHI) on a sleep study; OSA may also be diagnosed without symptoms if AHI is at least 15 episodes/h (p.2277-2278).","Each apnoea/hypopnoea is a reduction in breathing for at least 10 s, commonly causing a fall in oxygen saturation of at least 3% or a cortical arousal (p.2278).","The major risk factors for OSA are obesity, male sex, and older age; roughly 40-60% of cases are attributable to excess weight, but the absence of obesity does NOT exclude the diagnosis (p.2278).","Snoring is the most common symptom, but its absence does NOT exclude OSA because pharyngeal collapse can occur without tissue vibration; the most common daytime symptom is excessive sleepiness, though women preferentially report fatigue rather than sleepiness (p.2279).","Dyspnoea is unusual in OSA, and its absence generally distinguishes OSA from paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, nocturnal asthma, and acid reflux with laryngospasm (p.2279).","Overnight in-laboratory polysomnography (PSG) is the gold standard for diagnosis; a negative in-lab PSG usually rules out OSA, but false-negatives can occur with night-to-night variation, insufficient REM sleep, or less supine sleep than typical (p.2279).","Home sleep tests recording only respiratory and cardiac channels are a cost-effective option; when prior probability of OSA is high, a negative home study should be followed by PSG (p.2279).","CPAP is the standard medical therapy with the highest level of evidence; it acts as a mechanical splint to hold the airway open during sleep, and auto-titrating (APAP) home devices have eliminated the need for titration studies in many patients (p.2281).","Although CPAP reduces 24-h ambulatory blood pressure, the overall effect is modest (averaging 2-4 mmHg), with larger improvements in patients with high AHI, daytime sleepiness, or resistant hypertension (p.2280-2281).","Large randomized trials have FAILED to show that CPAP reduces cardiovascular/stroke events or prolongs survival, possibly because sleepy patients (who may have the greatest risk) were excluded and because of limited treatment adherence (p.2281).","Central sleep apnoea (CSA) is less common than OSA and may occur alone or, more often, combined with obstructive events as mixed apnoeas; it is often driven by increased sensitivity to PCO2 causing unstable breathing, and a prolonged circulation delay (as in congestive heart failure) produces the crescendo-decrescendo Cheyne-Stokes pattern (p.2282).","Treatment of CSA has NOT been shown to improve long-term health outcomes, so specific treatment is generally indicated only for symptomatic CSA; unlike OSA, treatment is difficult and depends on the underlying cause (p.2282)."],"pathophysiology":"In OSA the pharyngeal airway lacks fixed bony/cartilaginous support, so patency depends on pharyngeal dilator muscles. Inspiration generates increasingly negative intraluminal pressure (a suctioning force); at sleep onset neuromuscular output to these muscles declines, and in a collapsible airway this causes transient pharyngeal collapse (apnoea) or near-collapse (hypopnoea), typically terminated by an arousal that restores muscle activity. Collapse sites include the soft palate (most common), tongue base, lateral pharyngeal walls, and epiglottis. OSA is often worst in REM sleep (lowest skeletal-muscle output) and supine (gravity). A small pharyngeal lumen (soft-tissue enlargement from fat, lymphoid tissue, or genetic variation; craniofacial factors such as mandibular retroposition/micrognathia), low lung volume (reduced caudal pharyngeal traction, pronounced in obesity), and high nasal resistance all predispose to collapse. Pharyngeal muscle activation is linked to ventilatory drive: an over-sensitive ventilatory control system, a low arousal threshold (preempting CO2-mediated muscle compensation), or a high arousal threshold (prolonging apnoeas) all contribute. In OSA-related disease, sleep fragmentation, cortical arousals, and intermittent hypoxaemia/hypercapnia drive sympathetic overactivity, blood-pressure surges, a prothrombotic/proinflammatory state, insulin resistance, and large negative intrathoracic pressure swings that alter cardiac preload/afterload and promote cardiac/electrical remodelling. CSA is usually caused by increased PCO2 sensitivity producing hyperventilation alternating with apnoea, plus prolonged circulatory delay (heart failure) giving Cheyne-Stokes breathing.","additionalDifferentials":["Narcolepsy (multiple sleep latency / maintenance of wakefulness testing helps distinguish from OSA)","Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea","Nocturnal asthma","Acid reflux with laryngospasm","Insomnia (common in the general population, may coexist with OSA)","Obesity-hypoventilation / hypoventilation syndromes","Periodic limb movement disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea","Nocturnal asthma","Acid reflux with laryngospasm","Narcolepsy","Cheyne-Stokes / central sleep apnoea of heart failure"],"additionalInvestigations":["Overnight in-laboratory polysomnography (PSG) - gold standard","Home sleep test (respiratory and cardiac channels) for cost-effective diagnosis","Apnoea-hypopnoea index (AHI) and respiratory disturbance index (RDI) quantification","Overnight oximetry profile (mean, nadir, time at low saturation)","Multiple sleep latency test / maintenance of wakefulness test (quantify sleepiness, distinguish narcolepsy)","Upper-airway imaging (cephalometry, MRI, CT) and fibreoptic endoscopy to identify anatomic risk factors (not routine; useful for surgical planning)","Cardiac evaluation for systolic/diastolic dysfunction or structural abnormality","Overnight blood-pressure monitoring (may show nondipping pattern)","Arterial blood gas (usually normal awake; waking hypoxaemia/hypercarbia suggests cardiopulmonary disease or hypoventilation)","Drug-induced sleep endoscopy (for hypoglossal-nerve stimulation candidacy)"],"redFlags":["Waking hypoxaemia or hypercarbia, suggesting coexisting cardiopulmonary disease or a hypoventilation syndrome","Evidence of cor pulmonale, suggesting a comorbid cardiopulmonary condition (OSA alone is NOT thought to cause right-heart failure)","Resistant or uncontrolled hypertension","Excessive daytime sleepiness with occupational accident or motor-vehicle-crash risk (drowsy driving)","Coexisting heart failure (raises risk of both OSA and CSA)","Severe nocturnal hypoxaemia with elevated haemoglobin"],"prognosis":"OSA is the most common medical cause of daytime sleepiness and reduces quality of life. It is strongly associated with cardiac, cerebrovascular and metabolic disease (coronary artery disease, heart failure with and without reduced EF, atrial and ventricular arrhythmias, atherosclerosis, stroke, diabetes), premature death, and increased risk of certain cancers. Untreated OSA carries a roughly twofold risk of occupational accidents and a two- to threefold increase in motor-vehicle crashes. CPAP improves alertness, mood, quality of life, blood pressure and insulin sensitivity, but randomized trials have NOT shown that CPAP reduces cardiovascular/stroke events or prolongs survival. CSA carries increased risk for heart failure and atrial fibrillation, and its treatment has not been shown to improve long-term outcomes; adaptive servoventilation showed no cardiovascular or mortality benefit and increased mortality in patients with LVEF below 45%.","pitfalls":["Assuming absence of snoring excludes OSA - pharyngeal collapse can occur without tissue vibration.","Assuming a normal weight excludes OSA - absence of obesity does NOT exclude the diagnosis.","Underrecognising OSA in women, who have a lower arousal threshold, shorter and more REM-predominant apnoeas, and tend to report fatigue/insomnia rather than classic sleepiness.","Relying on symptoms/signs to gauge severity - they do not accurately predict the severity of sleep-related breathing disturbance; objective measurement is required.","Treating a single negative home sleep study as definitive when prior probability is high - follow with PSG.","Attributing right-heart failure/cor pulmonale to OSA alone, which is not thought to cause it - look for a comorbid cardiopulmonary condition.","Using adaptive servoventilation in patients with CSA and LVEF below 45% (FDA warning following increased mortality).","Sedative-hypnotics have inconsistent effects and should be avoided in most patients with moderate-to-severe OSA; opioids worsen CSA in a dose-dependent way."],"severityClassification":"OSA severity by apnoea-hypopnoea index (AHI = apnoeas plus hypopnoeas per hour of sleep): mild OSAHS = AHI 5-14 events/h; moderate OSAHS = AHI 15-29 events/h; severe OSAHS = AHI at least 30 events/h. The respiratory disturbance index (RDI) additionally counts respiratory effort-related arousals (RERAs). Each AHI level can be further qualified by the degree of sleepiness and associated hypoxaemia (Table 308-2, p.2280).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2277-2282"],"crossLinks":["obesity","heart_failure","pulmonary_hypertension","atrial_fib"]},"slipped_capital_femoral_epiphysis":{"name":"Slipped capital femoral epiphysis","system":"Musculoskeletal / Orthopaedics","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCFE","SUFE","slipped upper femoral epiphysis","femoral head epiphysis slip"],"chapter":null,"source":"Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A displacement of the proximal femoral epiphysis through the growth plate, typically in overweight adolescents around the time of the pubertal growth spurt","Presents with hip, groin, thigh, or referred knee pain and a limp; knee or thigh pain in an adolescent must always prompt examination of the hip","A characteristic sign is obligatory external rotation of the hip when it is passively flexed, with limited internal rotation","Radiographs should include a frog-lateral view, as subtle slips are best seen laterally; a line along the superior femoral neck (Klein's line) normally intersects the epiphysis","Slips are classified by symptom duration (acute, chronic, acute-on-chronic) and by whether the child can weight-bear (stable versus unstable), which strongly influences the risk of avascular necrosis","It is an orthopaedic urgency: once suspected the child should stop weight-bearing and be referred promptly, as further slipping worsens the outlook","Treatment is surgical stabilisation, usually in-situ pinning to prevent progression, and the contralateral hip may be pinned if at high risk","Consider an underlying endocrine or metabolic disorder (e.g. hypothyroidism, renal disease) in atypical, young, or bilateral cases"],"pathophysiology":"Slipped capital femoral epiphysis occurs when shear forces exceed the strength of the proximal femoral physis, allowing the epiphysis to displace, typically posteriorly and inferiorly, relative to the femoral neck (so the neck effectively rotates anteriorly and externally on the head). The physis is mechanically weakest during the adolescent growth spurt, and obesity increases the mechanical load, while hormonal factors influence physeal strength, explaining associations with endocrine disorders. In unstable slips the epiphyseal blood supply, which runs along the femoral neck, is at high risk of disruption, predisposing to avascular necrosis. Residual deformity from a healed slip alters femoral head-neck morphology and can cause femoroacetabular impingement and early osteoarthritis.","additionalDifferentials":["Perthes' disease","Transient synovitis of the hip","Septic arthritis or osteomyelitis","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Femoral neck stress fracture or trauma","Muscle strain or apophyseal avulsion","Referred pain from other hip or spinal pathology"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Anteroposterior and frog-lateral pelvic radiographs of both hips (the lateral view is key for subtle slips)","Assessment of Klein's line and the physeal appearance","MRI or ultrasound in equivocal cases or to assess a pre-slip","Endocrine and metabolic workup (e.g. thyroid function, renal function) in young, atypical, or bilateral presentations","Careful hip examination for loss of internal rotation and obligatory external rotation on flexion"],"redFlags":["SURGICAL URGENCY: suspected SCFE requires immediate non-weight-bearing and prompt referral to prevent further slip","Unstable slip (inability to weight-bear) carrying a high risk of avascular necrosis","Adolescent knee or thigh pain that could be referred from an undiagnosed hip slip","Bilateral or very young presentation suggesting an underlying endocrine or metabolic disorder"],"prognosis":"Stable slips treated promptly with in-situ fixation generally do well, whereas unstable slips carry a substantial risk of avascular necrosis and chondrolysis; even after successful fixation, residual femoral head-neck deformity can cause impingement and predispose to premature osteoarthritis, so long-term follow-up is important.","pitfalls":["Attributing knee or thigh pain to the knee and failing to image the hip, leading to missed or late diagnosis","Allowing continued weight-bearing on a suspected slip, risking further displacement and avascular necrosis","Missing a subtle slip by not obtaining a frog-lateral radiograph","Overlooking an underlying endocrine cause in atypical or bilateral cases"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Apley & Solomon's System of Orthopaedics and Fractures"],"crossLinks":[]},"social_anxiety_disorder":{"name":"Social anxiety disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["social phobia","SAD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The defining feature is marked, persistent fear of one or more social or performance situations in which the person feels exposed to possible scrutiny, with fear of acting in a way that will be humiliating or negatively judged","Feared situations are avoided or endured with intense distress, and the anxiety is out of proportion to any real threat and typically lasts many months","A performance-only subtype exists, confined to situations such as public speaking, and often responds well to targeted strategies","Onset is usually in childhood or adolescence, and the disorder is frequently underrecognised because sufferers are quiet and avoidant rather than disruptive","Comorbidity with depression, other anxiety disorders, and alcohol misuse is common, and alcohol is often used as maladaptive self-medication","First-line treatment is cognitive behavioural therapy with graded exposure and cognitive restructuring, with SSRIs or SNRIs as effective pharmacological options","For circumscribed performance anxiety, beta-adrenergic blockade can dampen peripheral symptoms such as tremor and palpitations, used situationally rather than daily","Distinguish from normal shyness by the degree of distress, avoidance, and functional impairment it causes"],"pathophysiology":"Social anxiety disorder reflects an interaction between heritable temperamental traits, notably behavioural inhibition in early childhood, and altered function of fear and threat-appraisal circuits involving the amygdala and prefrontal cortex. Dysregulation of serotonergic and dopaminergic systems, heightened sensitivity to social evaluation, and learned avoidance that prevents disconfirmation of feared outcomes together maintain the condition.","additionalDifferentials":["Normal shyness or introversion","Panic disorder with or without agoraphobia","Generalised anxiety disorder","Avoidant personality disorder","Autism spectrum disorder with social difficulties","Major depressive disorder with social withdrawal","Body dysmorphic disorder driving social avoidance"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Structured clinical interview focusing on feared and avoided situations","Standardised social anxiety rating scales to gauge severity and track response","Screening for comorbid depression and alcohol or substance use","Developmental history to distinguish from autism spectrum and personality disorders"],"redFlags":["Emerging suicidal ideation, particularly with comorbid depression","Escalating alcohol or sedative use to cope with social situations","Progressive social isolation with collapse of education, employment, or relationships"],"prognosis":"Social anxiety disorder is often chronic and, if untreated, can persist for many years with substantial functional impairment. Prognosis improves with cognitive behavioural therapy and pharmacotherapy, and is better with earlier onset of treatment and fewer comorbidities; the performance-only subtype generally carries a more favourable outlook.","pitfalls":["Dismissing the presentation as mere shyness and missing significant impairment","Overlooking comorbid alcohol misuse used as self-medication","Failing to include exposure-based work, which is central to durable improvement","Prescribing daily benzodiazepines and risking dependence","Stopping SSRIs prematurely before an adequate response has developed"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"soft_tissue_sarcoma":{"name":"Soft Tissue & Bone Sarcoma","system":"Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["sarcoma","osteosarcoma","GIST","Ewing sarcoma"],"chapter":96,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.727","clinicalPearls":["Sarcomas are rare (<1% of all malignancies) mesenchymal neoplasms arising in bone and soft tissues, usually of mesodermal origin and biologically distinct from the more common epithelial cancers (p.727).","The most common presentation of a soft tissue sarcoma is an asymptomatic mass; mechanical symptoms from pressure, traction, or entrapment of nerves or muscles may also occur (p.728).","Approximately 60% of soft tissue sarcomas arise in the extremities (lower limbs involved three times as often as upper), 30% in the trunk (retroperitoneum = 40% of trunk lesions), and 10% in the head and neck (p.727).","The pulmonary parenchyma is the most common site of metastasis for soft tissue sarcomas; lung radiograph plus chest CT is important for detecting lung metastases (p.728).","Histologic grade and primary tumor size are the most important prognostic factors; prognosis is related to AJCC stage and cure is common in the absence of metastatic disease (p.728).","About one-third of soft tissue sarcomas carry a characteristic translocation with diagnostic/prognostic relevance (e.g., t(X;18)(p11;q11) involving SYT in ~90% of synovial sarcomas) (p.728).","Most malignant GISTs carry activating c-kit mutations causing ligand-independent KIT receptor tyrosine kinase activation; ~5-10% have PDGFRA mutations, and KIT/PDGFRA wild-type GISTs may show SDH B/C/D mutations (p.728).","Osteosarcoma accounts for almost 45% of bone sarcomas, is a spindle-cell neoplasm producing osteoid/bone, with predilection for metaphyses of long bones (distal femur, proximal tibia, proximal humerus); ~60% occur in the second decade (p.729-730).","Classic osteosarcoma radiographic findings: a destructive moth-eaten lesion, spiculated periosteal reaction (sunburst appearance), and a cuff of periosteal new bone at the soft-tissue mass margin (Codman's triangle) (p.730).","The most important predictive factor for long-term survival in osteosarcoma is response to chemotherapy; long-term survival in extremity osteosarcoma ranges from 60-80% (p.730).","Ewing's sarcoma (~10-15% of bone sarcomas) peaks in the second decade, shows an 'onion peel' periosteal reaction with a soft-tissue mass, expresses p30/32 (MIC-2 product), and carries the t(11;22) translocation fusing fli-1 (chr 11) and ews (chr 22) (p.730).","Chondrosarcoma (~20-25% of bone sarcomas) is a tumor of adulthood/old age (peak 4th-6th decades) with predilection for flat bones (shoulder, pelvic girdles); it has an indolent natural history and is mostly chemoresistant, with surgical resection the mainstay (p.730).","Prostate, breast, and lung primaries account for 80% of all bone metastases; metastatic bone tumors are more common than primary bone tumors (p.730).","Bone is a common metastatic site for prostate, breast, lung, kidney, bladder, and thyroid carcinomas and for lymphomas and sarcomas; in descending frequency the sites are vertebrae, proximal femur, pelvis, ribs, sternum, proximal humerus, and skull (p.730).","When a patient with back pain develops neurologic signs or symptoms, emergency evaluation for spinal cord compression is indicated (p.730)."],"pathophysiology":"Sarcomas are mesenchymal neoplasms (mostly mesodermal, occasionally neuroectodermal) classified into ~20 groups by their pattern of differentiation toward normal tissue (e.g., rhabdomyosarcoma shows skeletal-muscle cross-striations, leiomyosarcoma shows smooth-muscle spindle fascicles, liposarcoma contains adipocytes). Malignant transformation of benign soft tissue tumors is extremely rare, except that malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumors (neurofibrosarcoma, malignant schwannoma) can arise from neurofibromas in neurofibromatosis. Etiologic contributors include radiation (tumor arises in the irradiated field, risk rising with time), scar tissue (prior surgery, burn, fracture, foreign body), chemical carcinogens (polycyclic hydrocarbons, asbestos, dioxin), HHV-8 (Kaposi's sarcoma - the only sarcoma virus-associated), and immunodeficiency/immunosuppression. Genetic syndromes include Li-Fraumeni (germline p53), neurofibromatosis 1 (NF1 on chr 17, encoding neurofibromin which inhibits ras), and germline RB1 mutation (chr 13q14, predisposing to osteosarcoma and radiation-unrelated soft tissue sarcomas). About one-third of soft tissue sarcomas carry characteristic translocations; most malignant GISTs have activating c-kit (KIT receptor tyrosine kinase) mutations, fewer have PDGFRA mutations. Bone metastases occur via hematogenous spread (or local invasion); tumors produce osteolytic lesions (via PTH-related peptide, vitamin D-like steroids, prostaglandins, IL-1, TNF inducing osteoclasts) and/or osteoblastic lesions (cytokines activating osteoblasts), with most lesions showing a mix.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign soft tissue tumor / well-differentiated liposarcoma (atypical lipomatous tumor) - lacks metastatic potential","Benign bone lesions with malignant potential: enchondroma and osteochondroma (to chondrosarcoma); fibrous dysplasia, bone infarcts, Paget's disease (to UPS or osteosarcoma)","Lymphoma, embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma, and small-cell carcinoma (can be confused with the small round blue cells of Ewing's sarcoma)","Osteoporosis vs metastatic spine disease in older patients (cortical bone preserved in osteoporosis; cortical destruction with metastases)","Plasma cell tumors (the most common malignant tumor of bone)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Osteoporotic vertebral disease mimicking spinal bone metastasis in older patients","Benign enchondroma / osteochondroma mimicking low-grade chondrosarcoma (difficult to distinguish by x-ray or histology; new pain, inflammation, or progressive size increase suggests malignancy)","Well-encapsulated benign-appearing lesion produced by the compressed pseudocapsule of a soft tissue sarcoma"],"additionalInvestigations":["Core-needle (cutting needle) or small-incision biopsy of all new, persistent, or growing masses, placed so it can be encompassed in the definitive resection","Plain radiographs and MRI for primary tumors of extremities or head/neck (MRI best for intramedullary and soft-tissue extension)","CT for tumors of the chest, abdomen, or retroperitoneum (CT best for bone destruction and calcification pattern in osteosarcoma)","Chest radiograph and chest CT to detect lung metastases","Bone scan or FDG-PET to detect skeletal metastases (almost all osteosarcomas are hypervascular and PET-avid)","Radionuclide bone scanning (sensitive to new bone formation) for osteoblastic lesions; plain radiography for osteolytic lesions (may not be apparent until >1 cm)","Serum alkaline phosphatase (elevated with prominent osteoblastic activity) and monitoring for hypercalcemia (osteolytic) or hypocalcemia (extensive osteoblastic)"],"redFlags":["An enlarging, deep, or large soft tissue mass requires biopsy - all new, persistent, or growing masses should be biopsied rather than ignored","Back pain with new neurologic signs or symptoms mandates emergency evaluation for spinal cord compression","New onset of pain, signs of inflammation, and progressive size increase in a cartilaginous lesion suggest malignant (chondrosarcoma) transformation","Bone pain that develops gradually over weeks, is localized, and is more severe at night suggests bone metastasis","Pathologic fracture, nerve root or cord compression, or myelophthisis from a destructive bony lesion","Hypercalcemia symptoms in the setting of bony destruction"],"prognosis":"For soft tissue sarcoma, histologic grade and size are the most important prognostic factors and prognosis tracks AJCC stage; cure is common without metastatic disease, and a small number with metastases can still be cured. Metastatic soft tissue sarcomas are largely incurable, but up to 20% achieving complete response become long-term survivors. Historically most stage IV patients died within 12 months, but with multiple treatment lines, median survival in second-line and beyond is now 13-18 months and some live with stable/slowly progressive disease for years. Extremity osteosarcoma long-term survival is 60-80%, with response to chemotherapy the key predictor. Ewing's sarcoma overall cure rates approach 60-70% for localized tumors. For bone metastases, overall survival is related to the prognosis of the underlying tumor; some (lymphoma, Hodgkin's lymphoma) are curable while most are treated palliatively.","pitfalls":["Soft tissue sarcomas grow along fascial planes and compress surrounding tissue into a pseudocapsule, giving a deceptive well-encapsulated appearance; 'shelling out' (marginal excision / enucleation) results in a 50-90% probability of local recurrence - wide excision with a negative margin incorporating the biopsy site is standard.","A biopsy of an enlarging/deep/large mass must be planned so the tract can be encompassed in the definitive resection and does not compromise a limb-sparing operation; an inappropriately placed incision compromises future limb salvage.","Low-grade chondrosarcoma is difficult to distinguish from benign lesions on x-ray or histology, so diagnosis is influenced by clinical history and examination.","Osteosarcoma is radioresistant - radiation therapy has no role in routine management.","Forgetting non-pulmonary metastatic patterns: GISTs metastasize to liver, myxoid liposarcomas seek fatty tissue (retroperitoneum, mediastinum, subcutaneous), clear cell sarcomas may go to bone, and alveolar soft part sarcoma to CNS.","In older patients (especially women), metastatic spine disease must be distinguished from osteoporosis - cortical bone is preserved in osteoporosis but destroyed by metastatic cancer."],"severityClassification":"Soft tissue sarcomas use the AJCC (8th edition) TNM/grade system: T1 (<=5 cm), T2 (>5-10 cm), T3 (>10-15 cm), T4 (>15 cm); N0/N1; M0/M1; with grade G1-G3 driving stage grouping (e.g., Stage IA = T1 N0 M0 G1; Stage IV = any T N1 M0, or any T any N M1). Bone sarcomas use the Musculoskeletal Tumor Society system (stage I low grade, II high grade, III nodal/distant metastases; A = intracompartmental, B = extracompartmental) and a TNM system: T1 (<=8 cm), T2 (>8 cm), T3 (discontinuous tumors in the primary bone); M1a (lung) vs M1b (other sites); grade G1-G4 (Ewing's always G4).","references":["Harrison 22e p.727","Harrison 22e p.728","Harrison 22e p.729","Harrison 22e p.730"],"crossLinks":["lung_cancer"]},"somatic_symptom_disorder":{"name":"Somatic symptom disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SSD","somatization","somatisation disorder","somatoform disorder"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by one or more distressing physical symptoms accompanied by excessive thoughts, feelings, or behaviours related to those symptoms","The diagnosis rests on the disproportionate and persistent nature of the response, not on whether the symptoms are medically explained","Features include disproportionate and persistent worry about the seriousness of symptoms, high health-related anxiety, and excessive time and energy devoted to symptoms or health concerns","Symptoms are typically persistent (often more than six months) even if individual complaints vary over time","Distinguish from illness anxiety disorder (preoccupation with having illness with few or no somatic symptoms) and from conversion disorder (neurological symptoms incompatible with recognised disease)","Management principles emphasise a consistent single coordinating clinician, regular scheduled visits, limiting unnecessary tests and referrals, and validating distress while gently shifting focus toward functioning","Cognitive behavioural therapy is the best-supported psychotherapy; treat comorbid depression and anxiety, where SSRIs or SNRIs may help","A genuine coexisting medical illness does not exclude the diagnosis, so maintain appropriate but restrained medical vigilance"],"pathophysiology":"Somatic symptom disorder is understood through a biopsychosocial model in which heightened attention to and amplification of bodily sensations, cognitive misinterpretation of benign symptoms as dangerous, and autonomic and stress responses interact. Predisposing factors include early adverse experiences, trauma, comorbid anxiety and depression, and learned illness behaviour, with reinforcement from repeated medical attention perpetuating the pattern.","additionalDifferentials":["Illness anxiety disorder","Conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder)","Factitious disorder and malingering","Major depressive disorder and anxiety disorders with somatic features","Panic disorder","Undiagnosed genuine medical illness","Body dysmorphic disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Focused, proportionate medical evaluation guided by objective findings rather than repeated broad testing","Review of prior investigations to avoid duplication","Screening for comorbid depression and anxiety","Assessment of functional impairment and healthcare utilisation patterns"],"redFlags":["New objective clinical signs suggesting an emergent genuine medical condition","Suicidal ideation associated with comorbid depression","Escalating analgesic or sedative use and dependence","Iatrogenic harm from repeated invasive investigations or procedures"],"prognosis":"Somatic symptom disorder is often chronic and fluctuating, but consistent, coordinated care can reduce distress, unnecessary investigations, and healthcare costs while improving function. Prognosis is worse with prolonged illness, multiple comorbidities, and ongoing reinforcement through repeated medical intervention.","pitfalls":["Ordering repeated investigations and referrals that reinforce illness behaviour and risk iatrogenic harm","Dismissing symptoms as not real, which damages the therapeutic alliance","Assuming the diagnosis excludes genuine disease and missing a new medical problem","Failing to identify and treat comorbid depression and anxiety","Lack of a single coordinating clinician leading to fragmented, duplicated care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"specific_phobia":{"name":"Specific phobia","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["simple phobia","isolated phobia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The hallmark is marked, disproportionate fear or anxiety about a specific object or situation that almost always provokes an immediate response and is actively avoided or endured with intense distress","Common types include animals, the natural environment such as heights or storms, blood-injection-injury, situational triggers such as flying or enclosed spaces, and other stimuli","The blood-injection-injury type is distinctive because it can provoke a vasovagal response with fainting, unlike most phobias where arousal is heightened","Diagnosis generally requires that the fear is persistent, typically lasting six months or more, and causes clinically significant distress or impairment","Exposure-based cognitive behavioural therapy, especially graded in vivo exposure, is the treatment of choice and is often highly effective, sometimes in a single prolonged session","Pharmacotherapy plays only a limited, adjunctive role, and long-term medication is usually unnecessary for isolated phobias","For blood-injection-injury phobia, applied tension techniques help counter the drop in blood pressure and prevent fainting during exposure","Assess how much the phobia genuinely restricts the person's life, since avoidance of rare triggers may need no treatment while others cause major impairment"],"pathophysiology":"Specific phobia is understood through models of classical fear conditioning and its persistence, in which a stimulus becomes associated with danger and avoidance prevents extinction of the learned fear. Heritable predisposition, heightened amygdala-driven threat responses, evolutionary preparedness for certain stimuli, and vicarious or informational learning all contribute, while the blood-injection-injury type involves a biphasic autonomic response predisposing to vasovagal syncope.","additionalDifferentials":["Panic disorder with situational attacks","Agoraphobia","Social anxiety disorder when avoidance centres on scrutiny","Post-traumatic stress disorder with trauma-related avoidance","Obsessive-compulsive disorder with contamination-related avoidance","Separation anxiety disorder in younger patients"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Focused clinical interview to characterise the feared stimulus and avoidance","Assessment of functional impairment attributable to the phobia","Screening for comorbid anxiety, mood, and substance use disorders","In blood-injection-injury type, evaluation of any history of syncope"],"redFlags":["Phobic avoidance that prevents essential medical care, such as avoidance of injections or blood tests in a patient needing treatment","Syncope with injury risk in blood-injection-injury phobia","Comorbid depression with emerging suicidal ideation"],"prognosis":"Specific phobias arising in childhood may remit spontaneously, whereas those persisting into adulthood tend to be chronic if untreated. The prognosis with exposure-based therapy is generally excellent, with many patients achieving lasting improvement, though untreated phobias affecting essential activities can cause enduring impairment.","pitfalls":["Avoiding exposure work in favour of reassurance, which maintains the fear","Overlooking a phobia that blocks necessary medical procedures","Relying on as-needed sedatives that undermine exposure learning","Mislabelling situational panic or agoraphobia as an isolated phobia","Failing to teach applied tension in blood-injection-injury phobia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"spina_bifida":{"name":"Spina bifida","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["myelomeningocele","meningocele","spina bifida occulta","neural tube defect"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Spina bifida is a congenital neural tube defect ranging from the hidden spina bifida occulta to the severe open myelomeningocele","Myelomeningocele exposes neural tissue and typically causes lower limb weakness, sensory loss, and bladder and bowel dysfunction whose level reflects the anatomical lesion","It is frequently associated with a Chiari II malformation and hydrocephalus, which often needs cerebrospinal fluid diversion","Spina bifida occulta is usually asymptomatic and found incidentally, but cutaneous markers such as a tuft of hair, dimple, or lipoma over the spine can signal an underlying tethered cord","Periconceptional folic acid supplementation substantially reduces the risk of neural tube defects and is a key public health measure","Antenatal diagnosis is supported by maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein and detailed ultrasound, allowing planning of delivery and early surgery","Early surgical closure of an open defect protects exposed neural tissue and reduces infection risk, and fetal repair is offered in selected cases","Lifelong multidisciplinary care addresses mobility, urological, orthopaedic, and neurosurgical needs including shunt surveillance and tethered cord"],"pathophysiology":"Spina bifida results from failure of the neural tube to close completely during early embryonic development, leaving a midline defect in the vertebral arches and, in open forms, in the overlying tissues. In myelomeningocele the meninges and dysplastic spinal cord protrude through the defect, so neural function below the lesion is impaired. Disordered cerebrospinal fluid dynamics and the commonly associated Chiari II malformation predispose to hydrocephalus. In occult forms the cord may be abnormally low and tethered by a thickened filum or lipoma, and growth-related traction can produce progressive neurological, urological, or orthopaedic deterioration.","additionalDifferentials":["Sacrococcygeal teratoma or other congenital masses","Tethered cord syndrome from other causes","Spinal dysraphism variants (diastematomyelia, dermal sinus)","Caudal regression syndrome","Simple sacral dimple without dysraphism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Antenatal ultrasound and maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein for prenatal detection","MRI of the spine and brain to define the lesion, cord tethering, and associated Chiari II malformation","Cranial imaging and head circumference monitoring to detect hydrocephalus","Urodynamic and renal tract assessment for neurogenic bladder","Orthopaedic evaluation of the spine, hips, and lower limbs"],"redFlags":["Open defect with exposed neural tissue at risk of infection needing urgent protection and closure","Signs of associated hydrocephalus or Chiari II brainstem dysfunction in an infant","New or progressive neurological, urological, or orthopaedic decline suggesting tethered cord or shunt failure","Fever or CSF leak indicating possible meningitis or ventriculitis"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies widely with the level and severity of the lesion; occulta is usually benign, whereas myelomeningocele typically causes lasting motor, sensory, and sphincter deficits, though multidisciplinary care and timely surgery improve function and reduce complications.","pitfalls":["Dismissing cutaneous spinal markers that may indicate occult dysraphism and a tethered cord","Failing to monitor for evolving hydrocephalus or shunt malfunction","Overlooking neurogenic bladder and the associated risk to the upper urinary tract","Attributing new deficits in a growing child to the original lesion rather than a tethered cord"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinal_anaesthesia_complications":{"name":"Spinal anaesthesia complications","system":"Anaesthesia / Perioperative","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["subarachnoid block complications","intrathecal anaesthesia complications","high spinal","total spinal"],"chapter":null,"source":"Miller's Anesthesia / standard perioperative references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Hypotension and bradycardia are the most common early effects, caused by sympathetic blockade, and are managed with fluids, vasopressors, and positioning","Excessively high block can spread to thoracic and cervical levels, producing profound hypotension, difficulty breathing, and eventually a total spinal with apnoea and cardiovascular collapse requiring airway support and resuscitation","Postdural puncture headache is a frequent delayed complication, typically postural and related to needle size and design","Urinary retention is common and reflects blockade of sacral autonomic fibres, requiring monitoring of bladder volume","Serious neurological complications are rare but include epidural or spinal haematoma, abscess, arachnoiditis, cauda equina syndrome, and transient neurological symptoms","Neuraxial haematoma risk is increased by abnormal coagulation or anticoagulant therapy, and adherence to timing guidelines around anticoagulation is critical","New or progressive back pain, leg weakness, sensory change, or bladder or bowel dysfunction after neuraxial block demands urgent imaging and neurosurgical assessment","Meticulous asepsis reduces the risk of infective complications, and this reference entry addresses the non-infective mechanical and physiological complications"],"pathophysiology":"Injection of local anaesthetic into the subarachnoid space blocks nerve roots and the spinal cord, producing sensory, motor, and autonomic blockade. Sympathetic blockade causes vasodilation and, with higher blocks, loss of cardiac accelerator fibre activity, leading to hypotension and bradycardia. Excessive cephalad spread can impair respiratory muscles and brainstem perfusion. Dural puncture allows cerebrospinal fluid leakage, lowering intracranial pressure and producing a postural headache. Mechanical trauma, mass lesions such as haematoma or abscess, or neurotoxicity can compress or injure neural tissue, causing ischaemia and potentially permanent deficit if not decompressed promptly.","additionalDifferentials":["Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity from inadvertent vascular uptake","Vasovagal syncope","Haemorrhagic or septic shock unrelated to the block","Pre-existing neurological disease","Anaphylaxis to an injected agent","Positioning-related peripheral nerve injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Continuous blood pressure, heart rate, and oxygenation monitoring","Assessment of block height and progression","Urgent MRI of the spine if a compressive lesion (haematoma or abscess) is suspected","Coagulation studies when neuraxial bleeding is a concern","Bladder scan to detect urinary retention"],"redFlags":["Rapidly ascending block with respiratory distress, hypotension, or loss of consciousness indicating a high or total spinal","Cardiovascular collapse or severe bradycardia requiring resuscitation","New progressive motor weakness, sensory loss, or bladder/bowel dysfunction suggesting cord or cauda equina compression","Severe or worsening back pain with neurological signs after neuraxial block"],"prognosis":"Common physiological effects such as hypotension resolve with treatment and most postdural puncture headaches settle, but a missed compressive haematoma or abscess can cause permanent paralysis, so outcome depends on rapid recognition and decompression.","pitfalls":["Failing to anticipate and promptly treat sympathetic-blockade hypotension and bradycardia","Not recognising an ascending high spinal early enough to support ventilation","Overlooking neuraxial haematoma risk in anticoagulated patients","Attributing new neurological deficits to residual block and delaying urgent imaging","Missing urinary retention in the postoperative period"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Miller's Anesthesia"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinal_epidural_abscess":{"name":"Spinal epidural abscess","system":"Neurology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SEA","epidural abscess","spinal extradural abscess"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A neurosurgical and infective emergency in which a collection of pus in the epidural space can rapidly compress the spinal cord or cauda equina","The classic triad of back pain, fever, and neurological deficit is often incomplete, so a high index of suspicion is essential","Progression from localised back pain to root pain, then weakness and sphincter dysfunction, then paralysis can occur over hours to days","Staphylococcus aureus is the most frequent organism, often reaching the spine by haematogenous spread or by direct extension","Risk factors include diabetes, injection drug use, immunosuppression, indwelling catheters, recent spinal procedures, and bacteraemia","Urgent whole-spine MRI with contrast is the investigation of choice and may reveal additional non-contiguous collections","Management usually combines prompt surgical drainage with prolonged targeted antimicrobial therapy, though selected cases are managed medically","Neurological outcome is largely determined by the deficit at the time of intervention, so delay is the main preventable cause of permanent paralysis"],"pathophysiology":"Bacteria reach the spinal epidural space by haematogenous seeding (from skin, urinary, respiratory, or endovascular sources), by contiguous spread from vertebral osteomyelitis or discitis, or by direct inoculation during procedures. The abscess damages the cord through two mechanisms: direct mechanical compression and, importantly, vascular compromise causing ischaemic or venous infarction of the cord, which can produce deficits out of proportion to the degree of compression. The thoracic and lumbar regions are most often involved, and the collection may track over several vertebral levels.","additionalDifferentials":["Vertebral osteomyelitis or discitis without abscess","Metastatic epidural spinal cord compression","Transverse myelitis or other inflammatory myelopathy","Herniated disc with cord or root compression","Spinal subdural or intramedullary abscess","Spinal epidural haematoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent contrast-enhanced MRI of the whole spine","Blood cultures before starting antimicrobials whenever feasible","Inflammatory markers (CRP, ESR) and full blood count","Culture of aspirated or surgically obtained pus for organism identification and sensitivities","Echocardiography and a search for a primary source when endocarditis or bacteraemia is suspected","HbA1c and screening for immunosuppression or injection drug use"],"redFlags":["Any new neurological deficit with back pain and fever mandates emergency imaging and specialist referral","Rapidly progressive weakness, sensory level, or new sphincter dysfunction indicating impending or actual cord compression","Sepsis or haemodynamic instability from the underlying infection","Known immunosuppression, injection drug use, or recent spinal instrumentation with new spinal pain"],"prognosis":"Outcome hinges on the speed of diagnosis and decompression; patients treated before significant weakness often recover well, whereas established paralysis or delayed intervention frequently leaves permanent deficits, and severe sepsis carries appreciable mortality.","pitfalls":["Waiting for the complete triad of pain, fever, and deficit before imaging, thereby losing critical time","Imaging only the symptomatic level and missing skip lesions elsewhere in the spine","Starting antibiotics before blood cultures in a stable patient and losing microbiological data","Attributing back pain to a benign musculoskeletal cause in a high-risk patient","Underestimating how quickly a stable neurological picture can deteriorate to irreversible paralysis"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinal_muscular_atrophy":{"name":"Spinal Muscular Atrophy","system":"Genetic-metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SMA","SMN-related spinal muscular atrophy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autosomal recessive disorder of anterior horn (lower motor neuron) degeneration, most commonly caused by homozygous loss of function of the SMN1 gene.","It produces progressive, symmetrical muscle weakness and wasting that is typically more marked proximally and in the legs more than the arms.","Disease severity is graded into types (from severe infantile-onset to milder later-onset forms) and correlates broadly with the number of copies of the modifying SMN2 gene.","Severe infantile SMA (type 1) presents with profound hypotonia, poor head control, a weak cry, feeding and swallowing difficulty, and paradoxical (belly) breathing with a bell-shaped chest.","Tongue fasciculations and absent deep tendon reflexes are characteristic, while cognition and sensation are preserved.","Respiratory muscle and bulbar weakness drive the major morbidity and mortality, particularly recurrent aspiration and respiratory failure in the most severe forms.","Diagnosis is confirmed by genetic testing showing biallelic SMN1 loss, with SMN2 copy number providing prognostic information.","Disease-modifying therapies that increase SMN protein have substantially changed the outlook, and newborn screening enables early, presymptomatic treatment.","Multidisciplinary supportive care addresses respiratory support, nutrition, orthopaedic complications such as scoliosis and contractures, and physiotherapy."],"pathophysiology":"Loss of functional SMN protein, resulting from homozygous deletion or mutation of SMN1, leads to degeneration and loss of the motor neurons of the anterior horn of the spinal cord and brainstem motor nuclei. The nearly identical SMN2 gene produces only a small amount of full-length functional protein, so a higher SMN2 copy number partially compensates and mitigates severity. Denervation of skeletal muscle causes progressive weakness and atrophy, with the respiratory and bulbar musculature critically affected in severe forms, resulting in hypoventilation, impaired airway clearance, aspiration, and respiratory failure.","additionalDifferentials":["Congenital myopathies","Congenital muscular dystrophies","Neonatal or congenital myasthenic syndromes","Hypoxic-ischaemic or metabolic encephalopathy causing hypotonia","Prader-Willi syndrome and other central causes of hypotonia","Pompe disease and other metabolic myopathies"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Genetic testing for SMN1 deletion/mutation with SMN2 copy number","Newborn screening result where available","Assessment of respiratory function and swallowing safety","Creatine kinase (usually normal or only mildly elevated, helping distinguish from dystrophies)","Electromyography and nerve conduction studies if genetic testing is inconclusive","Nutritional and growth assessment"],"redFlags":["Respiratory failure or hypoventilation from progressive respiratory muscle weakness","Bulbar weakness with unsafe swallowing and recurrent aspiration","Rapidly progressive weakness and loss of motor milestones in an infant","Weak cough and impaired airway clearance during intercurrent respiratory infection","Signs of impending ventilatory decompensation such as paradoxical breathing and rising work of breathing"],"prognosis":"Historically severe infantile-onset disease carried a very poor prognosis with early respiratory failure, while later-onset forms had longer survival with variable disability. Early diagnosis through newborn screening and initiation of SMN-directed disease-modifying therapy have markedly improved motor outcomes and survival, especially when started presymptomatically.","pitfalls":["Attributing infantile hypotonia to a central cause and missing the lower motor neuron pattern","Overlooking tongue fasciculations and preserved cognition as clues to anterior horn disease","Underestimating respiratory and bulbar risk, especially during intercurrent illness","Delaying genetic confirmation and thereby missing the window for early therapy","Failing to organise multidisciplinary respiratory, nutritional, and orthopaedic support"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"spinal_stenosis":{"name":"Spinal stenosis","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lumbar spinal stenosis","cervical spinal stenosis","spinal canal stenosis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Lumbar spinal stenosis classically causes neurogenic claudication, with leg pain, heaviness, or numbness on standing and walking that eases with sitting or leaning forward","The forward-flexed posture that relieves symptoms explains why patients often report easier walking uphill or when leaning on a trolley","Cervical stenosis can produce cervical myelopathy, with clumsy hands, gait imbalance, and upper motor neuron signs in the legs","It is usually a degenerative condition of older adults driven by disc bulging, facet hypertrophy, and ligamentum flavum thickening narrowing the canal or neural foramina","Distinguishing neurogenic from vascular claudication rests on the posture-dependent pattern, preserved peripheral pulses, and provocation by standing rather than a fixed walking distance","Initial management is usually conservative with physiotherapy, activity modification, and analgesia; decompressive surgery is considered for disabling or progressive symptoms","Progressive myelopathy in cervical stenosis or any sphincter disturbance warrants prompt specialist assessment","Imaging findings of stenosis are common with age and must be correlated with symptoms rather than treated in isolation"],"pathophysiology":"Spinal stenosis is narrowing of the spinal canal, lateral recesses, or neural foramina that compresses the cord, cauda equina, or nerve roots. It is most often degenerative, resulting from intervertebral disc bulging, osteophyte formation, facet joint hypertrophy, and buckling or thickening of the ligamentum flavum, sometimes compounded by spondylolisthesis or congenitally narrow canals. In the lumbar spine, compression of the cauda equina and roots produces neurogenic claudication, aggravated by extension which further narrows the canal and relieved by flexion. In the cervical spine, cord compression can cause myelopathy through direct pressure and ischaemia.","additionalDifferentials":["Peripheral vascular (vascular) claudication","Lumbar disc herniation with radiculopathy","Peripheral neuropathy","Hip or knee osteoarthritis","Other causes of myelopathy in cervical disease (e.g. tumour, demyelination)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the relevant spinal region to characterise the level and severity of compression","CT or CT myelography when MRI is contraindicated or bony detail is needed","Plain radiographs including flexion-extension views to assess instability or spondylolisthesis","Nerve conduction studies or EMG when peripheral neuropathy is a competing diagnosis","Ankle-brachial index or vascular studies to exclude arterial claudication"],"redFlags":["New or progressive cauda equina symptoms such as saddle anaesthesia, urinary retention, or bowel dysfunction requiring urgent evaluation","Rapidly progressive cervical myelopathy with worsening gait or hand function","Bilateral neurological deficit or significant motor weakness","Features suggesting infection or malignancy, such as fever, night pain, or weight loss"],"prognosis":"Symptoms often follow a fluctuating or slowly progressive course; many patients are managed conservatively for years, and decompression can relieve claudication and stabilise myelopathy, though established cord damage in cervical disease may not fully reverse.","pitfalls":["Treating incidental imaging stenosis that does not match the clinical picture","Confusing neurogenic with vascular claudication and missing peripheral arterial disease","Overlooking early cervical myelopathy, mistaking gait and hand changes for ageing","Failing to recognise cauda equina features as an emergency"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"spondyloarthritis":{"name":"Spondyloarthritis","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ankylosing spondylitis","axial spondyloarthritis","reactive arthritis","psoriatic arthritis"],"chapter":374,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2879-2892","clinicalPearls":["Spondyloarthritis (SpA) is a family of immune-mediated inflammatory arthritides sharing clinical, genetic, and pathologic features; classified by ASAS (2009) into axial (axSpA) and peripheral (pSpA). Axial skeletal involvement is hinted at in the name but is NOT mandatory (Harrison 22e p.2879).","axSpA is subdivided into radiographic (r-axSpA, = ankylosing spondylitis [AS]) and nonradiographic (nr-axSpA), the difference based on presence/absence of definitive sacroiliitis on plain radiographs; the two share the same clinical features and disease burden (Harrison 22e p.2880, 2883).","pSpA encompasses psoriatic arthritis (PsA), reactive arthritis (ReA), IBD-associated arthritis, and undifferentiated pSpA; SAPHO, acne-associated, and hidradenitis suppurativa-associated arthritis are sometimes included (Harrison 22e p.2880).","HLA-B27 is the strongest genetic risk factor: present in 85-90% of r-axSpA and 50-90% of nr-axSpA, versus ~5% of the healthy Caucasian population (Harrison 22e p.2881).","Inflammatory back pain (IBP): insidious onset, age <45, chronic (>3 months), improves with exercise/activity but NOT with rest, worse in second half of night with improvement after getting up, and prolonged (>30 min) morning stiffness; this distinguishes it from mechanical back pain (Harrison 22e p.2883).","Characteristic SpA pattern is asymmetric mono-/oligoarticular (<=4 joints) inflammatory arthritis of large lower-limb joints (usually knee) with enthesitis, tendonitis, and dactylitis (sausage digits), contrasting with the symmetric polyarticular MCP/PIP involvement of rheumatoid arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2879).","Hips are the most common nonspinal ('root') joints affected in axSpA; peripheral arthritis occurs in ~30-40% of axSpA patients (Harrison 22e p.2883).","Uveitis is the most common extramusculoskeletal manifestation (~40% of axSpA), typically acute, anterior, unilateral, and episodic; other extra-articular features include psoriasis (~10%) and IBD (frank IBD ~10%, subclinical gut inflammation up to 50%) (Harrison 22e p.2883-2884).","Enthesitis (inflammation at tendon/ligament insertions) is common in axSpA, presenting as heel pain (plantar fasciitis, Achilles insertion), chest wall pain, epicondylitis, or pelvic rim pain; dactylitis is relatively rare in axSpA but common in PsA (Harrison 22e p.2883).","Persistent axial inflammation leads to bony fusion of sacroiliac and apophyseal joints and syndesmophyte formation; complete ankylosis of the spine is termed 'bamboo spine,' seen in only a very small percentage of late-stage AS patients (Harrison 22e p.2880, 2884).","Typical SpA syndesmophytes are vertically oriented, distinguishing them from the horizontal osteophytes of spinal osteoarthritis; PsA syndesmophytes are often chunky and nonmarginal (Harrison 22e p.2884, 2889).","Reactive arthritis follows infectious gastroenteritis (Salmonella, Shigella, Yersinia, Campylobacter) or genitourinary Chlamydia; classic features include urethritis, conjunctivitis, mucosal ulcers, and keratoderma blennorrhagica (Harrison 22e p.2880).","IBD-associated arthritis: r-axSpA develops in up to 10% of IBD patients and pSpA in 10-30%; conversely axSpA patients have a 5-10% risk of developing IBD. HLA-B27 is NOT a risk factor for IBD itself (Harrison 22e p.2891-2892)."],"pathophysiology":"SpA is a prototypic chronic immune-mediated inflammatory disease that couples inflammation to structural damage, with the unique feature that inflammation-induced bone loss coexists with pathologic new bone formation at specific skeletal sites (sacroiliac joints, anterior spinal ligaments, peripheral entheses). HLA-B27, an MHC class I allele, is the strongest genetic risk factor; proposed mechanisms include the arthritogenic peptide theory (HLA-B27 presenting microbial peptides to CD8+ T cells with molecular mimicry), HLA-B27 homodimerization engaging innate receptors (KIR3DL2) to drive IL-17, and unfolded protein response/autophagy promoting IL-23-driven type 17 immunity. Disease requires additional factors beyond HLA-B27 (only ~5% of HLA-B27-positive people, and 20% of HLA-B27-positive first-degree relatives, develop axSpA); key instigators are loss of intestinal barrier integrity with microbial dysbiosis (germ-free HLA-B27 transgenic rats fail to develop disease) and aberrant response to mechanical stress. The IL-23/IL-17 axis and TNF-alpha are central, with overlapping yet domain-distinct cytokine signatures across gut, joint, entheses, and skin (Harrison 22e p.2880-2882).","additionalDifferentials":["Mechanical (nonspecific) back pain - the etiology in 95-96% of chronic back pain; axSpA accounts for only 4-5% (Harrison 22e p.2885)","Fibromyalgia / central sensitization (especially mistaken for SpA in women, delaying diagnosis) (Harrison 22e p.2885)","Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis (DISH) - noninflammatory, flowing osteophytes over >=4 contiguous vertebrae, normal sacroiliac joints, in obese diabetic males >50; can be mistaken for bamboo spine (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Osteitis condensans ilii (OCI) - asymptomatic triangular iliac-side sclerosis in multiparous women; lack of erosions distinguishes from axSpA (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Osteoarthritis of the spine - horizontal osteophytes vs vertical syndesmophytes (Harrison 22e p.2884)","Rheumatoid arthritis - symmetric polyarticular MCP/PIP disease, RF/nodules present (Harrison 22e p.2879)","Hypothyroidism and hypovitaminosis D in young patients with generalized body pain (Harrison 22e p.2886)"],"infectionMimics":["Septic sacroiliitis from tuberculosis, brucellosis, fungi, or other infectious agents - can cause inflammatory back pain with sclerotic sacroiliac changes (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Osteomyelitis (consider with red-flag features) (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Infectious enterocolitis-like acute gut inflammation seen histologically in SpA terminal ileum (Harrison 22e p.2891)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Postpartum sacral insufficiency fracture (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Osteoporotic vertebral fracture (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Metastatic disease / malignancy, including acute lymphoblastic leukemia presenting with inflammatory back pain and sacroiliac sclerosis (Harrison 22e p.2886)","MRI osteitis-like changes from mechanical stress in athletes, postpartum women, and normal individuals >40 (risk of overdiagnosis) (Harrison 22e p.2885)","Calcium pyrophosphate deposition disease (CPPD) and gout (may coexist with/mimic PsA) (Harrison 22e p.2889)","SAPHO and hidradenitis suppurativa (may mimic palmoplantar psoriasis/PsA) (Harrison 22e p.2889)"],"additionalInvestigations":["HLA-B27 typing - supportive but neither necessary nor sufficient for diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2884)","ESR and CRP - elevated in only 30-40% of active axSpA; neither sensitive nor specific, but useful for monitoring (Harrison 22e p.2884, 2888)","Plain AP or Ferguson pelvic radiograph for sacroiliitis (multiple oblique views add little and increase gonadal radiation) (Harrison 22e p.2884)","MRI of sacroiliac joints/spine - detects active osteitis/bone marrow edema (STIR) and structural lesions (sclerosis, fat metaplasia, erosions, backfill, ankylosis) early; not indicated for routine monitoring (Harrison 22e p.2885, 2888)","Low-dose CT of sacroiliac joints/spine - emerging alternative to radiography for diagnosis and progression (Harrison 22e p.2885)","Synovial fluid analysis in monoarthritis - WBC >2000/mL with negative crystals/Gram stain/culture suggests inflammatory arthritis; rules out septic and crystal arthritis (Harrison 22e p.2888)","Fecal calprotectin (elevated with microscopic gut inflammation in SpA) (Harrison 22e p.2891)","Disease activity indices: BASDAI, ASDAS; function: BASFI; metrology: BASMI (Harrison 22e p.2886)"],"redFlags":["Fever, weight loss, advanced age, or past malignancy in a back-pain patient - prompt evaluation for osteomyelitis, osteoporotic fracture, or metastatic disease (Harrison 22e p.2886)","Cauda equina syndrome (urinary hesitancy and/or saddle anesthesia) - late axSpA complication (Harrison 22e p.2884)","Cardiac conduction abnormalities (heart block) at any stage; aortic valve insufficiency as a late complication (Harrison 22e p.2884)","AA (renal) amyloidosis causing nephrotic syndrome after prolonged uncontrolled inflammation; IgA nephropathy with microscopic hematuria (Harrison 22e p.2884)","Spinal fractures in ankylosed (bamboo) spine - consider if back pain persists/relapses despite therapy (Harrison 22e p.2888)","Atlantoaxial subluxation (rare, in PsA) with potentially serious neurologic consequences (Harrison 22e p.2889)","Acute anterior uveitis (eye pain, redness, photophobia, miosis) requiring urgent ophthalmologic care (Harrison 22e p.2884)"],"prognosis":"Course is variable: ~50% of patients progress from nonradiographic to radiographic stage over 20 years, but <10% progress to significant spinal involvement with bamboo spine. Risk factors for osteoproliferation (worse structural outcome) include male sex, persistent inflammation (MRI sacroiliac/spine activity, high CRP), existing syndesmophytes, HLA-B27 positivity, and smoking. With increased awareness and earlier treatment, structural damage has steadily decreased over recent decades, with a shift toward higher prevalence of nr-axSpA over r-axSpA. axSpA significantly impairs well-being, function, productivity, and quality of life. Reactive arthritis is often self-limiting but may become chronic, with HLA-B27 linked to chronicity (Harrison 22e p.2880, 2884, 2891).","pitfalls":["PITFALL: confusing inflammatory back pain with common mechanical back pain - mechanical causes must be considered first since axSpA is the etiology in only 4-5% of chronic back pain; conversely IBP improves with exercise and worsens with rest, the reverse of mechanical pain (Harrison 22e p.2883, 2885).","The term 'inflammatory back pain' is itself a partial misnomer - only ~15% of patients with IBP have demonstrable axial inflammation (Harrison 22e p.2883).","Diagnostic delay of 5-14 years is common, worse in women (back pain/enthesitis mistaken for fibromyalgia) and in nonwhite populations with lower HLA-B27 prevalence (Harrison 22e p.2884).","Interreader reliability for distinguishing r-axSpA from nr-axSpA on radiographs is poor (kappa 0.35-0.45), even between trained musculoskeletal radiologists (Harrison 22e p.2883).","MRI overdiagnosis: osteitis-type and fat-metaplasia changes occur from mechanical stress in athletes, postpartum women, degenerative arthritis, and normal individuals >40 (Harrison 22e p.2885).","Normal/negative inflammatory markers do NOT exclude axSpA - ESR/CRP elevated in only 30-40% of active disease (Harrison 22e p.2884).","Plain spine radiographs should generally be avoided in chronic back pain; multiple oblique sacroiliac views add little but increase gonadal radiation (Harrison 22e p.2884).","Avoid glucocorticoid injection around lower-extremity weight-bearing entheses due to risk of tendon rupture; in IBD-associated arthritis anti-IL17 therapy is contraindicated and NSAIDs may trigger IBD flares (Harrison 22e p.2890-2892)."],"severityClassification":"axSpA is classified as radiographic (r-axSpA / ankylosing spondylitis) vs nonradiographic (nr-axSpA) using definitive sacroiliitis on plain radiographs per the modified New York criteria: definitive = grade 2 bilateral OR grade 3-4 unilateral or bilateral (r-axSpA); lower-grade sacroiliitis = nr-axSpA. ASAS criteria further split SpA into axial (axSpA) and peripheral (pSpA). Diagnosis of axSpA in chronic back pain follows the schema of Figure 374-2 (definite sacroiliitis on x-ray -> AS; otherwise count SpA features >=4 or compelling clinical picture plus MRI sacroiliitis/HLA-B27 -> nr-axSpA). Disease activity graded with BASDAI/ASDAS; PsA classified with CASPAR criteria, activity by DAPSA/MDA (Harrison 22e p.2883-2884, 2889).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2879-2892","Harrison 22e ch.374 (Deodhar & Elewaut)"],"crossLinks":[]},"spondylolisthesis":{"name":"Spondylolisthesis","system":"Neurosurgery / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["isthmic spondylolisthesis","degenerative spondylolisthesis","vertebral slip"],"chapter":null,"source":"Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Spondylolisthesis is forward (or occasionally backward) slippage of one vertebra on the one below, most often at the lumbosacral junction","Isthmic slips relate to a defect in the pars interarticularis (spondylolysis), often from repetitive extension stress in young athletes, while degenerative slips occur in older adults from facet and disc degeneration","Many slips are asymptomatic; when symptomatic, patients report low back pain, hamstring tightness, and sometimes radicular leg pain or neurogenic claudication","Severity is commonly graded by the degree of slip relative to the vertebral body width, which helps guide monitoring and management","Standing lateral and flexion-extension radiographs are useful to demonstrate the slip and any dynamic instability","Most cases are managed conservatively with activity modification, physiotherapy focusing on core stabilisation, and analgesia","Surgery, typically decompression with or without fusion, is considered for persistent disabling symptoms, neurological deficit, or progressive high-grade slips","High-grade slips in the skeletally immature warrant closer surveillance because of the potential for progression"],"pathophysiology":"Spondylolisthesis is displacement of one vertebra relative to the adjacent vertebra, usually anterior translation of the upper on the lower body. In isthmic disease a stress-related defect or fracture of the pars interarticularis (spondylolysis) allows the anterior elements to slip forward. In degenerative disease, age-related disc narrowing and facet joint arthropathy reduce the segment's stability and permit gradual slippage, typically without a pars defect. The malalignment can narrow the central canal and neural foramina, tethering or compressing nerve roots and, in higher grades, causing back pain and radicular or claudicant symptoms.","additionalDifferentials":["Mechanical low back pain","Lumbar disc herniation","Lumbar spinal stenosis without slip","Vertebral fracture or pathological lesion","Sacroiliac joint dysfunction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Standing lateral radiographs to demonstrate and grade the slip","Flexion-extension views to assess dynamic instability","MRI to evaluate neural compression, disc, and root involvement","CT or oblique views to characterise a pars defect when suspected","Bone imaging (e.g. SPECT or MRI) in young athletes for a suspected active pars stress injury"],"redFlags":["New or progressive neurological deficit or radiculopathy","Cauda equina symptoms such as saddle anaesthesia or sphincter disturbance","High-grade or progressive slip, particularly in a young or skeletally immature patient","Severe unremitting pain or features suggesting infection or malignancy"],"prognosis":"Most low-grade, degenerative, and isthmic slips remain stable and respond well to conservative care; higher-grade slips and those with neurological compromise are more likely to need surgery, which generally improves symptoms though progression can occur in growing patients.","pitfalls":["Relying on supine imaging, which can underestimate a slip that is only apparent when standing","Missing dynamic instability by omitting flexion-extension views","Overlooking a pars stress injury in a young athlete with extension-related back pain","Attributing all symptoms to the slip without excluding coexisting stenosis or disc pathology"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Youmans & Winn Neurological Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"squamous_cell_carcinoma_skin":{"name":"Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["cutaneous SCC","SCC of skin","squamous cell cancer of skin"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically presents as an enlarging, firm, hyperkeratotic or crusted nodule or plaque on sun-exposed skin, often tender and prone to ulceration or bleeding","It frequently arises within a field of sun damage, from a pre-existing actinic keratosis, or from Bowen's disease (in-situ SCC)","Unlike basal cell carcinoma, cutaneous SCC carries a genuine, if modest, risk of regional and distant metastasis, so early diagnosis matters","High-risk features include large or thick tumours, poorly differentiated histology, perineural invasion, and sites such as the ear, lip, and non-sun-exposed areas","It arises with particularly high frequency and aggressiveness in immunosuppressed patients, especially organ transplant recipients, and in areas of chronic wounds, scars, or prior radiotherapy","Examine and, where indicated, image the draining lymph nodes as part of staging","Surgical excision with adequate margins is the mainstay, with margin-controlled surgery, radiotherapy, or systemic therapy for high-risk or advanced disease decided in a multidisciplinary setting","Emphasise sun protection, treatment of field damage, and regular surveillance, and consider reducing immunosuppression in transplant patients where feasible"],"pathophysiology":"Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma is a malignant proliferation of epidermal keratinocytes that have acquired full-thickness atypia and then breached the basement membrane to invade the dermis. Cumulative ultraviolet radiation is the dominant driver, producing signature mutations including in TP53 and other tumour-suppressor and oncogene pathways, with additional contributions from immunosuppression, human papillomavirus in some tumours, chronic inflammation or scarring, and carcinogen exposure. Progression typically follows a continuum from dysplastic keratinocytes (actinic keratosis) through in-situ disease (Bowen's) to invasive carcinoma. Invasive nests of atypical keratinocytes with variable keratinisation can track along nerves and invade lymphatics, giving the potential for metastasis that distinguishes it from basal cell carcinoma.","additionalDifferentials":["Basal cell carcinoma","Keratoacanthoma (rapidly growing crateriform lesion on the SCC spectrum)","Actinic keratosis or Bowen's disease (pre-invasive lesions)","Amelanotic melanoma","Viral wart or hypertrophic actinic damage","Cutaneous metastasis or adnexal tumour"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnostic skin biopsy to confirm invasion and assess grade, depth, and perineural involvement","Clinical examination and, when indicated, ultrasound or cross-sectional imaging of regional lymph nodes","Staging imaging for large, high-risk, or node-positive tumours","Multidisciplinary review for high-risk and advanced disease","Assessment of immunosuppression status, particularly in transplant recipients"],"redFlags":["A rapidly enlarging, ulcerating, or bleeding lesion, especially at high-risk sites such as the lip or ear","Palpable regional lymphadenopathy or new neurological symptoms suggesting perineural or nodal spread","A tumour arising in a chronic wound, scar, or previously irradiated skin (potential for aggressive behaviour)","Multiple or aggressive tumours in an immunosuppressed or transplant patient"],"prognosis":"Most low-risk cutaneous SCCs are cured by complete excision, but prognosis worsens with high-risk features such as large size, deep invasion, poor differentiation, perineural involvement, high-risk sites, and immunosuppression, which raise the likelihood of recurrence and metastasis; early detection and adequate treatment are key to good outcomes.","pitfalls":["Underestimating the metastatic potential and treating cutaneous SCC as casually as basal cell carcinoma","Failing to biopsy and assess an indurated or growing lesion within a field of actinic damage","Omitting lymph node examination and staging in high-risk tumours","Not recognising the heightened risk and aggressive course in immunosuppressed and transplant patients"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"st_segment_elevation_myocardial_infarction":{"name":"ST-Segment Elevation Myocardial Infarction","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["STEMI","Acute ST-elevation MI","Q-wave myocardial infarction","Transmural myocardial infarction"],"chapter":286,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2113","clinicalPearls":["STEMI typically arises when coronary flow drops abruptly after a thrombus forms over an atherosclerotic plaque that has eroded or ruptured; slowly progressive high-grade stenoses less often cause STEMI because collaterals develop over time (Harrison 22e p.2113).","Plaques prone to disruption tend to have a large lipid core and a thin fibrous cap, yet fewer than 5% of such thin-capped fibroatheromas actually become the nidus for an acute MI during long-term follow-up (Harrison 22e p.2113).","Deep, visceral chest pain described as heavy, squeezing, or crushing, usually central and/or epigastric and lasting longer than angina, is the most common presentation; it may radiate to the arms, back, lower jaw, or neck but not below the umbilicus (Harrison 22e p.2114).","Pain is not universal: painless STEMI is more frequent in patients with diabetes mellitus and increases with age, and the event may instead present as sudden breathlessness, syncope, confusion, profound weakness, arrhythmia, peripheral embolism, or an unexplained fall in blood pressure (Harrison 22e p.2114).","Anterior infarction may produce sympathetic overactivity (tachycardia/hypertension), whereas inferior infarction may produce parasympathetic overactivity (bradycardia/hypotension) (Harrison 22e p.2114).","Substernal chest pain lasting more than 30 minutes combined with diaphoresis strongly suggests STEMI (Harrison 22e p.2114).","The 12-lead ECG is the pivotal triage tool: total epicardial coronary occlusion produces ST-segment elevation, and most patients presenting with ST elevation ultimately evolve Q waves (Harrison 22e p.2115).","Cardiac troponin T and I are the preferred biomarkers when measured with high-sensitivity assays and may rise to roughly 20-50 times the upper reference limit in classic MI, remaining elevated for at least 7-10 days after STEMI (Harrison 22e pp.2115-2116).","Because urgent reperfusion decisions rest on clinical and ECG findings, high-sensitivity troponin is of less immediate value in confirmed STEMI than in the broader chest-pain workup (Harrison 22e p.2116).","Wall-motion abnormalities on two-dimensional echocardiography are almost universal in STEMI and aid triage, but acute infarction cannot be distinguished from old scar or severe acute ischemia by echo alone (Harrison 22e p.2116).","The two major classes of complications determining prognosis in the primary-PCI era are electrical (arrhythmias) and mechanical (pump failure); most out-of-hospital STEMI deaths result from sudden ventricular fibrillation (Harrison 22e p.2117).","Temperature can rise up to 38 degrees C during the first week, and a nonspecific leukocytosis (often 12,000-15,000/microL) appears within hours and persists 3-7 days, with the ESR rising more slowly and peaking in the first week (Harrison 22e pp.2114, 2116)."],"pathophysiology":"STEMI usually results from abrupt thrombotic occlusion of a coronary artery already affected by atherosclerosis. Vascular injury promoted by smoking, hypertension, lipid accumulation, and inflammation leads to disruption of an atherosclerotic plaque by erosion or rupture, exposing its contents to blood under conditions favoring thrombogenesis. A platelet monolayer forms first; agonists such as collagen, ADP, epinephrine, and serotonin activate platelets, releasing thromboxane A2 (a local vasoconstrictor) and converting the glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptor to a high-affinity state that binds fibrinogen and cross-links platelets. Exposure of tissue factor activates the coagulation cascade (factors VII and X), generating thrombin, which converts fibrinogen to fibrin; an autoamplifying thrombin reaction culminates in an occlusive thrombus of platelet aggregates and fibrin. The extent of necrosis depends on the territory supplied, completeness and duration of occlusion, collateral supply, myocardial oxygen demand, endogenous thrombolysis, and adequacy of reperfusion. Microvascular dysfunction can produce no-reflow. Healing proceeds through acute (first hours-7 days), healing (7-28 days), and healed (29+ days) stages, with polymorphonuclear leukocytes then proinflammatory and reparative monocytes driving clearance, angiogenesis, extracellular matrix production, scar formation, and ventricular remodeling (Harrison 22e pp.2113-2116).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute pericarditis (radiation of discomfort to the trapezius favors pericarditis and is not seen in STEMI)","Pulmonary embolism","Acute aortic dissection","Costochondritis","Gastrointestinal disorders (the xiphoid/epigastric location can mimic indigestion)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute pericarditis","Pulmonary embolism","Acute aortic dissection","Costochondritis","Esophageal and other gastrointestinal causes of chest pain","Type 2 MI from oxygen supply-demand imbalance unrelated to acute atherothrombosis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial 12-lead ECG to detect ST-segment elevation and evolution of Q waves","Cardiac troponin T or I (high-sensitivity assays preferred); a rise and/or fall with at least one value above the 99th percentile upper reference limit defines myocardial injury/infarction","CK and CK-MB historically used; a CK-MB mass to CK activity relative index of 2.5 or greater suggests but is not diagnostic of myocardial origin, and CK-MB's rapid decline can help detect early reinfarction","Two-dimensional echocardiography for regional wall-motion abnormalities, LV function, RV infarction, ventricular aneurysm, pericardial effusion, LV thrombus, and Doppler detection of VSD and mitral regurgitation","Radionuclide myocardial perfusion imaging (e.g., 99mTc-sestamibi) shows a 'cold spot' but cannot distinguish acute infarct from chronic scar"],"redFlags":["Sudden development of ventricular fibrillation, the leading cause of out-of-hospital STEMI death, with most such deaths occurring within the first 24 hours and over half within the first hour","Hypotension or unexplained drop in arterial pressure","Sudden loss of consciousness, confusional state, or profound weakness as an atypical STEMI presentation","New apical systolic murmur suggesting mitral valve apparatus dysfunction, a serious mechanical complication","Inferior infarction with elevated jugular venous pressure, clear lungs, and hypotension suggesting right ventricular infarction","Pericardial friction rub in transmural STEMI","Signs of pump failure / cardiogenic shock and mechanical complications such as ventricular septal defect"],"prognosis":"About half of AMI-related deaths occur before the patient reaches the hospital. In-hospital mortality after admission for AMI has fallen from roughly 10% to about 5%, and the 1-year mortality after AMI is approximately 15%. Mortality is roughly fourfold higher in patients older than 75 years than in younger patients. Up to one-third of STEMI patients achieve spontaneous reperfusion of the infarct-related artery within 24 hours with improved healing. Outcome hinges largely on electrical (arrhythmic) and mechanical (pump failure) complications (Harrison 22e pp.2113, 2117).","pitfalls":["Equating absence of pain with absence of STEMI: painless infarction is common in diabetics and older patients and may present only as dyspnea, syncope, or hypotension (Harrison 22e p.2114).","Misattributing xiphoid/epigastric pain to indigestion, compounded by the patient's own denial of a heart attack (Harrison 22e p.2114).","Older terms Q-wave/non-Q-wave and transmural/nontransmural MI correlate poorly with pathology and have been replaced by STEMI and NSTEMI; Q-wave development depends more on infarct volume than on transmurality (Harrison 22e p.2115).","Peak biomarker concentration correlates only weakly with infarct size, and early reperfusion causes earlier biomarker peaking from rapid washout, so peak levels should not be used to gauge infarct size (Harrison 22e p.2116).","Echocardiographic wall-motion abnormalities cannot distinguish acute STEMI from old scar or severe acute ischemia (Harrison 22e p.2116).","Radionuclide perfusion 'cold spots' cannot distinguish acute infarct from chronic scar, so they are not specific for acute MI (Harrison 22e p.2117)."],"severityClassification":"Patients with prolonged ischemic discomfort are first classified as an acute coronary syndrome and triaged by the 12-lead ECG into ST-segment elevation versus no ST-segment elevation; biomarkers then separate unstable angina from NSTEMI. The Fourth Universal Definition of Myocardial Infarction classifies MI into five types: type 1 (atherothrombotic), type 2 (oxygen supply-demand imbalance unrelated to acute atherothrombosis), type 3 (cardiac death with presumed new ischemic ECG changes before troponin became available), type 4 (PCI-related, including 4a, 4b stent thrombosis, 4c restenosis), and type 5 (CABG-related). Procedure-related MI within 48 h is defined by troponin elevation greater than 5-fold (type 4a) or greater than 10-fold (type 5) the 99th percentile URL with additional ischemic criteria (Harrison 22e pp.2114, 2117-2118).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2113","Harrison 22e p.2114","Harrison 22e p.2115","Harrison 22e p.2116","Harrison 22e p.2117","Harrison 22e p.2118"],"crossLinks":[]},"stable_angina":{"name":"Stable angina pectoris","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chronic stable angina","exertional angina","stable ischaemic heart disease","SIHD"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic presentation is retrosternal chest discomfort or pressure precipitated by exertion or emotion and relieved within minutes by rest or short-acting nitrates","Discomfort commonly radiates to the left arm, neck, or jaw, and anginal equivalents such as exertional breathlessness or fatigue are frequent in older adults, women, and people with diabetes","Symptoms are reproducible and stable over weeks to months; a change in the threshold, frequency, or duration signals progression toward an acute coronary syndrome","The resting ECG is often normal between episodes, so a normal tracing does not exclude significant coronary disease","Functional imaging or stress testing establishes inducible ischaemia, while coronary CT angiography is increasingly used to assess anatomy in patients at low to intermediate risk","Management combines aggressive risk-factor and lifestyle modification, antianginal therapy to control symptoms, and antiplatelet and statin therapy to reduce cardiovascular events","Revascularisation is considered for symptoms refractory to medical therapy or for high-risk anatomy such as significant left main or proximal multivessel disease","Grading anginal severity (for example by the Canadian Cardiovascular Society classes) helps track response to treatment and guide escalation"],"pathophysiology":"Stable angina results from a mismatch between myocardial oxygen demand and supply, most often caused by fixed atherosclerotic narrowing of one or more epicardial coronary arteries. When demand rises with exertion, a flow-limiting stenosis cannot augment supply sufficiently, producing transient subendocardial ischaemia and the sensation of angina. Endothelial dysfunction, coronary microvascular disease, and vasospasm can contribute or cause ischaemia even without high-grade obstruction. Ischaemia is reversible and resolves once demand falls, distinguishing it from the sustained supply-demand failure of infarction.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute coronary syndrome (unstable angina, NSTEMI, STEMI)","Gastro-oesophageal reflux and oesophageal spasm","Musculoskeletal or costochondral chest pain","Pericarditis","Aortic dissection","Pulmonary embolism","Anxiety or panic disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Resting 12-lead ECG and comparison with prior tracings","Exercise or pharmacological stress testing with ECG or imaging to demonstrate inducible ischaemia","Coronary CT angiography to define anatomy in low to intermediate risk patients","Invasive coronary angiography when high-risk features or refractory symptoms are present","Assessment for anaemia, thyroid dysfunction, and metabolic risk factors including lipids and glucose","Resting echocardiography to assess left ventricular function and exclude structural disease"],"redFlags":["Angina occurring at rest, at low workload, or with escalating frequency or duration, suggesting an evolving acute coronary syndrome","Chest pain with haemodynamic instability, syncope, or new heart failure","Tearing or interscapular pain suggesting aortic dissection","Prolonged pain unrelieved by rest or nitrates that may indicate infarction"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the extent and location of coronary disease, left ventricular function, and control of risk factors; many patients do well for years on guideline-directed medical therapy, but left main or extensive multivessel disease and impaired ventricular function carry higher event rates.","pitfalls":["Assuming a normal resting ECG excludes significant coronary disease","Overlooking atypical or equivalent presentations in women, older adults, and people with diabetes","Attributing new rest or crescendo symptoms to stable angina rather than recognising an acute coronary syndrome","Focusing on symptom relief while neglecting event-reducing secondary prevention"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"staphylococcal_infections":{"name":"Staphylococcal Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Staph aureus infection","MRSA"],"chapter":152,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1195; cross-referenced material on pp.389, 402, 967, 974-975, 581, 993-995","clinicalPearls":["Staphylococcus aureus causes a wide spectrum of disease, ranging from localized skin and soft-tissue infection to invasive bloodstream, endocardial, and bone infection and to toxin-mediated syndromes; methicillin-resistant S. aureus (MRSA) is now common in both community and health-care settings (p.1195, p.389).","Furunculosis (boils) is caused by S. aureus and has risen in prominence with community-acquired MRSA (CA-MRSA); patients frequently mistake furuncles for spider or insect bites, and family members or close contacts may also be affected (p.389).","Whenever possible, send lesional material from staphylococcal skin infection for culture; incision and drainage may be adequate therapy for a small solitary furuncle without surrounding cellulitis or systemic symptoms (p.389).","Impetigo is caused most often by S. aureus (and sometimes group A streptococci) and forms a characteristic honey-colored crust; bullous impetigo, with tense clear bullae, is produced by S. aureus exfoliative toxin (phage type II) (p.389).","In staphylococcal scalded-skin syndrome (SSSS) the site of staphylococcal infection is usually extracutaneous (e.g., conjunctivitis, rhinorrhea, otitis media, pharyngitis) and the skin lesions themselves are sterile, whereas in bullous impetigo the skin lesions are the actual site of infection (p.402).","Toxic shock syndrome (TSS) is defined by clinical criteria — fever, diffuse macular (sunburn-type) erythroderma, hypotension, and multiorgan involvement — with three criteria involving mucocutaneous sites (diffuse skin erythema, desquamation of palms and soles 1-2 weeks after onset, and mucous-membrane hyperemia of the vagina, oropharynx, or conjunctivae) (p.402, p.993).","In staphylococcal TSS, the vagina or a postoperative wound is typically colonized rather than overtly infected, and mucosal areas appear hyperemic but not infected; this contrasts with streptococcal TSS, which is more often linked to overt skin/soft-tissue infection and bacteremia (p.993).","Acute bacterial endocarditis from S. aureus follows a far more aggressive course than subacute endocarditis, can attack previously normal native valves, and native-valve S. aureus (including MRSA) endocarditis is increasing in frequency (p.994).","Coagulase-negative staphylococci are skin commensals; a single positive blood-culture bottle for skin flora (especially one taking >24 h to turn positive) should raise suspicion of contamination or catheter colonization rather than true bloodstream infection, and cultures should be repeated from both central and peripheral sites (p.581)."],"pathophysiology":"S. aureus expresses cell wall-anchored adhesins called MSCRAMMs (microbial surface components recognizing adhesive matrix molecules, e.g., SdrC) that mediate binding to host extracellular-matrix proteins and underpin adherence and colonization (p.967). Like other extracellular pathogens it can form biofilms, which contribute to device- and prosthesis-associated infection (p.967). Disease is driven partly by potent secreted toxins. Superantigens — including toxic shock syndrome toxin-1 (TSST-1) and the staphylococcal enterotoxins — bind invariant regions of MHC class II molecules and the T-cell receptor, nonspecifically activating vast numbers of T cells and triggering a cytokine storm responsible for fever, rash, and the shock of TSS (p.974, p.402). S. aureus also produces an erythrotoxin causing scarlatiniform rash and exfoliative toxin (\"exfoliatin\") that cleaves the superficial epidermis to produce bullous impetigo and SSSS (p.402, p.389). It elaborates several pore-forming toxins — leukocidins, alpha- and gamma-hemolysins, and phenol-soluble modulins — that lyse host cells, protect the organism from phagocytosis, and impair development of protective adaptive immunity (p.975). Methicillin resistance results from acquisition of the mobile mecA gene carried on the SCCmec cassette, which has spread widely through the S. aureus population (p.1195).","additionalDifferentials":["Group A streptococcal infection (overlaps with staphylococci in impetigo, cellulitis, necrotizing fasciitis, and toxic shock syndrome) (p.389, p.993)","Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome (more often with overt soft-tissue infection/necrotizing fasciitis and bacteremia, less prominent exanthem) (p.993)","Toxic epidermal necrolysis and severe drug eruptions (in the differential of widespread skin blistering versus SSSS) (p.389, p.402)","Kawasaki disease and scarlet fever (other causes of scarlatiniform rash with palm/sole desquamation) (p.402)"],"infectionMimics":["Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome","Scarlet fever (group A streptococcal erythrogenic toxin)","Clostridium sordellii toxic shock (childbirth-associated or black-tar heroin injection; fever typically absent)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Toxic epidermal necrolysis / severe drug eruption (mimics SSSS)","Kawasaki disease (polymorphous rash, conjunctival injection, desquamation; clues are cervical lymphadenopathy, cheilitis, thrombocytosis)","SARS-CoV-2-associated multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children"],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cultures, repeated from both central and peripheral sites, to confirm bacteremia and distinguish true infection from skin-flora contamination/catheter colonization (differential time-to-positivity can localize a catheter source) (p.581)","Frozen section of the blister roof or exfoliative cytology of blister contents to distinguish SSSS from toxic epidermal necrolysis rapidly (p.402)","Culture of lesional material from skin/soft-tissue infection to guide therapy and detect MRSA (p.389)","mecA gene detection as the genotypic basis of methicillin resistance, enabling rapid genotypic MRSA identification (p.1195)","Double-disk diffusion (D-zone) test to detect inducible clindamycin resistance when an isolate is erythromycin-resistant but clindamycin-susceptible (p.383)","Echocardiography and a search for metastatic foci in S. aureus bacteremia to evaluate for endocarditis (p.994)"],"redFlags":["S. aureus bacteremia warrants an active search for infective endocarditis and metastatic/seeded foci, because any primary focus can give rise to bloodstream infection with secondary seeding (p.994).","In TSS, rapid hypotension developing within hours of symptom onset, and early renal failure preceding hypotension, are warning features (early renal failure helps distinguish TSS from other septic shock syndromes) (p.993).","Acute S. aureus endocarditis: a changing murmur, congestive heart failure, rapid valvular destruction (especially aortic) with pulmonary edema and hypotension, myocardial abscess with conduction block/arrhythmia, and large friable vegetations causing arterial emboli, metastatic infection, or stroke (p.994).","Widespread skin blistering or radial perioral crusting with diffuse tenderness suggests SSSS and demands prompt evaluation (p.402).","S. aureus among organisms that can present as intractable hypotension/septic shock with a primary soft-tissue focus (p.993)."],"prognosis":"Outcome varies sharply by syndrome. For TSS, reported mortality is about 5% for menstruation-associated and 10-15% for nonmenstrual disease (compared with 30-70% for streptococcal TSS) (p.993). Acute bacterial endocarditis, including native-valve S. aureus/MRSA disease, carries mortality of roughly 10-40%, and older patients with S. aureus endocarditis often present with nonspecific symptoms that delay diagnosis and worsen prognosis (p.994). Rapid recognition and intervention are crucial across invasive staphylococcal syndromes.","pitfalls":["Attributing a single positive blood culture for coagulase-negative staphylococci to true bacteremia when it may represent skin-flora contamination or catheter colonization — repeat paired cultures and assess time-to-positivity (p.581).","Assuming a primary skin focus is present in staphylococcal TSS; colonization (vaginal or wound) without overt infection is typical, so the portal may be subtle (p.993).","Missing the diagnosis of S. aureus endocarditis in older patients, whose nonspecific presentation delays recognition (p.994).","Confusing SSSS with toxic epidermal necrolysis or severe drug eruption; SSSS occurs mainly in infants/toddlers, is more superficial, spares the oral mucosa, and is toxin- rather than drug-mediated (p.402).","Inducible clindamycin resistance can cause treatment failure in apparently clindamycin-susceptible isolates unless the double-disk (D-zone) test is performed (p.383)."],"severityClassification":"No single graded severity scale is presented; staphylococcal disease is categorized by clinical syndrome and invasiveness — superficial skin/soft-tissue infection (impetigo, furunculosis), toxin-mediated syndromes (TSS, SSSS, scarlatiniform eruption), and invasive disease (bacteremia, infective endocarditis, osteomyelitis, septic intracranial/epidural infection). TSS is defined by formal clinical criteria (fever, diffuse erythroderma, hypotension, multiorgan involvement with mucocutaneous features) (p.402, p.993).","references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e, Ch.152 Staphylococcal Infections, p.1195","Harrison's 22e, Ch.60 Eczema, Psoriasis, Cutaneous Infections..., p.389 (impetigo, bullous impetigo, SSSS, furunculosis)","Harrison's 22e, Ch.61 (cutaneous manifestations: SSSS vs TEN, TSS, scarlatiniform eruption), p.402","Harrison's 22e, Ch.125 Molecular Mechanisms of Microbial Pathogenesis, pp.967, 974-975 (MSCRAMMs, biofilms, superantigens, pore-forming toxins)","Harrison's 22e, Ch.127 Approach to the Acutely Ill Infected Febrile Patient, pp.993-995 (TSS, acute bacterial endocarditis)","Harrison's 22e, Ch.79 Infections in Patients with Cancer, p.581 (coagulase-negative staphylococci, CLABSI vs contamination)"],"crossLinks":[]},"strabismus":{"name":"Strabismus","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["squint","ocular misalignment","esotropia","exotropia","heterotropia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Strabismus is a misalignment of the visual axes and is described by direction: esotropia (inward), exotropia (outward), hypertropia (upward), and hypotropia (downward)","It is classified as concomitant (the deviation is similar in all directions of gaze, typical of childhood squints) or incomitant (varying with gaze, suggesting a nerve palsy or restriction)","In children the main concern is amblyopia in the deviating eye, whereas new-onset strabismus in adults usually causes diplopia","The cover test (cover-uncover and alternate cover) is the key clinical method for detecting and characterising a manifest or latent deviation","Accommodative esotropia is linked to uncorrected hypermetropia and often improves substantially with the appropriate glasses","A new incomitant squint, especially with a cranial nerve palsy pattern, warrants investigation for a neurological cause","Management principles include correcting refractive error, treating any amblyopia (e.g. occlusion), and surgical realignment of the extraocular muscles when indicated","Leukocoria or a sudden squint in a child requires urgent examination to exclude retinoblastoma or other serious pathology"],"pathophysiology":"Strabismus arises from a failure of the normal binocular alignment maintained by coordinated extraocular muscle action and central fusional control. Causes include refractive error (notably hypermetropia driving accommodative esotropia), sensory deprivation from poor vision in one eye, abnormalities of the neuromuscular pathways (cranial nerve palsies), mechanical restriction of muscles, and disorders of central fusion. In children, misalignment during the critical period of visual development leads to suppression of the deviating eye's image and can result in amblyopia and loss of binocular single vision.","additionalDifferentials":["Pseudostrabismus from prominent epicanthic folds or facial asymmetry","Cranial nerve palsy (third, fourth, or sixth nerve)","Restrictive myopathy (e.g. thyroid eye disease, orbital fracture)","Myasthenia gravis","Internuclear ophthalmoplegia and other supranuclear disorders","Retinoblastoma or other cause of sensory deprivation in a child"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Cover-uncover and alternate cover tests to detect and measure the deviation","Assessment of visual acuity in each eye to detect amblyopia","Cycloplegic refraction to identify a correctable refractive cause","Ocular motility examination and assessment of binocular function/stereopsis","Neuroimaging when an incomitant or paralytic squint suggests a neurological lesion"],"redFlags":["New-onset incomitant squint with features of a cranial nerve palsy suggesting intracranial pathology","Leukocoria or a squint accompanying poor vision in a child, raising concern for retinoblastoma","Sudden diplopia with headache or other neurological signs","Painful or restricted eye movements suggesting orbital or inflammatory disease"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are generally favourable when strabismus and any associated amblyopia are detected and treated early in childhood, with good prospects for alignment and, in some cases, binocular vision; late detection reduces the chance of restoring binocularity, and adult acquired strabismus depends on the underlying cause.","pitfalls":["Dismissing a genuine squint as pseudostrabismus and delaying amblyopia treatment","Failing to perform cycloplegic refraction and missing correctable accommodative esotropia","Not investigating a new incomitant or paralytic squint for a neurological cause","Overlooking a serious underlying cause such as retinoblastoma in a child presenting with a squint"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"streptococcal_infections":{"name":"Streptococcal Infections","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["group A strep","GAS","Streptococcus pyogenes","group B strep","GBS","Streptococcus agalactiae"],"chapter":153,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1206-1214","clinicalPearls":["Group A Streptococcus (GAS; S. pyogenes) is the only common cause of sore throat that should be treated with antibiotics; GAS accounts for 20-40% of exudative pharyngitis in children and is rare under age 3 (p.1208).","Streptococcal pharyngitis cannot be reliably diagnosed on clinical grounds alone given overlap with many viral and bacterial causes; throat culture remains the diagnostic gold standard, with rapid antigen tests (specificity >95%) a useful adjunct (p.1208).","Antibiotic treatment of GAS pharyngitis is given primarily to prevent suppurative complications and acute rheumatic fever; symptoms resolve in 3-5 days and are shortened little by treatment (p.1208).","Prevention of ARF depends on eradicating the organism from the pharynx (not just symptom resolution) and requires a 10-day penicillin course; a single dose or short course is inadequate (p.1208).","Penicillin treatment of streptococcal pharyngitis reduces the likelihood of ARF but NOT of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (p.1209).","Worldwide, GAS infections and their post-infectious sequelae (chiefly ARF and rheumatic heart disease) cause an estimated 500,000 deaths/year; incidence of GAS disease and RHD is roughly tenfold higher in resource-limited countries (p.1206-1207).","Scarlet fever is streptococcal infection (usually pharyngitis) plus a rash from pyrogenic exotoxins: blanching sandpaper-textured papular rash on trunk and extremities sparing palms and soles, with circumoral pallor, strawberry tongue, and Pastia's lines in skin folds, followed by desquamation of palms and soles (p.1209).","Impetigo is most often a superficial GAS infection (occasionally other streptococci or S. aureus) of young children; honey/amber crusted lesions on face and legs; lesions are generally NOT painful and fever is NOT a feature — if fever is present, suspect deeper infection or another diagnosis (p.1209-1210).","Erysipelas is a streptococcal cellulitis of the superficial dermis: bright red, warm, shiny, edematous plaque with a sharply demarcated raised border and peau d'orange texture, typically on the face (malar) or lower extremities, with abrupt onset, fever and chills; classic cases are almost always due to beta-hemolytic streptococci (usually GAS) (p.1210).","Streptococcal cellulitis favors sites of disrupted lymphatic drainage (prior cellulitis, post-mastectomy/axillary dissection arm, prior DVT or lymphedema leg, saphenous vein harvest site); treating tinea pedis can stop recurrent leg cellulitis after vein harvest (p.1210-1211).","GAS is among the few pathogens producing wound infection and surrounding cellulitis within the first 24 h after surgery, with thin exudate and rapid spread (p.1211).","Necrotizing fasciitis is a surgical emergency: GAS is implicated in ~60% of cases; early surgical exploration and extensive debridement are diagnostic, therapeutic, and life-saving, with antibiotics as a critical adjunct (p.1211).","Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome (TSS) features fever, hypotension, renal impairment, and respiratory distress; in contrast to staphylococcal TSS, most patients with streptococcal TSS are bacteremic, rash usually does NOT develop, and the most common associated infection is a soft tissue infection (necrotizing fasciitis, myositis, or cellulitis) (p.1211-1212).","Streptococcal TSS carries a mortality rate of >=30%, mostly from shock and respiratory failure; because of its rapidly progressive lethal course, early recognition is essential (p.1212).","Group B Streptococcus (GBS; S. agalactiae) is a leading cause of neonatal sepsis and meningitis and of peripartum fever; early-onset disease (first week, median 20 h) is acquired from the colonized maternal genital tract, while late-onset disease (1 week-3 months) most often presents as meningitis, frequently capsular type III (p.1212-1213).","GBS infection in adults increasingly affects the elderly and those with chronic illness (e.g., diabetes, malignancy), presenting as cellulitis/soft-tissue infection including diabetic ulcers, UTI, pneumonia, endocarditis, and septic arthritis (p.1213-1214)."],"pathophysiology":"GAS (S. pyogenes) is the single species of Lancefield group A, a beta-hemolytic, chain-forming, gram-positive facultative anaerobe. M protein (encoded by the emm gene, >200 emm types) is the major surface protein and chief virulence factor: it resists phagocytic killing partly by binding plasma fibrinogen and interfering with complement deposition, and type-specific anti-M antibody confers protection only against the same M type. A hyaluronic acid capsule (a weak immunogen owing to structural identity with mammalian connective tissue) protects against phagocytosis and aids pharyngeal colonization via CD44 binding; capsule is not essential for virulence. GAS elaborates streptolysins S and O, streptokinase, DNases, SpyCEP (cleaves IL-8 to impair neutrophil recruitment), and pyrogenic exotoxins (formerly erythrogenic toxins) that cause the scarlet fever rash and act as superantigens triggering T-cell cytokine release, implicated in severe invasive disease including necrotizing fasciitis and streptococcal TSS. The post-infectious sequelae ARF (uniquely associated with S. pyogenes) and PSGN are thought to result from immune responses to streptococcal infection. GBS is encapsulated by 1 of 10 polysaccharides; the capsule is a key virulence factor and type-specific anticapsular antibody is protective.","additionalDifferentials":["Scarlet fever differential: measles and other viral exanthems, Kawasaki disease, toxic shock syndrome, and systemic allergic/drug eruptions (p.1209)","Pharyngitis differential includes group C/G streptococci, mixed anaerobes (Vincent's angina), Arcanobacterium haemolyticum, Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Corynebacterium diphtheriae, EBV/CMV/HIV, and other viruses (Table 153-2, p.1208)","Impetigo differential: bullous impetigo due to S. aureus, and herpetic lesions (orolabial HSV, chickenpox, or zoster), distinguished by grouped discrete vesicles and positive Tzanck/PCR (p.1210)"],"infectionMimics":["Staphylococcus aureus cellulitis/soft tissue infection mimicking streptococcal cellulitis (suspect S. aureus when cellulitis develops around a wound or ulcer) (p.1210-1211)","Bullous impetigo (S. aureus) mimicking streptococcal impetigo (p.1210)","Staphylococcal toxic shock syndrome (shares features with streptococcal TSS, but staph TSS patients are usually not bacteremic) (p.1211-1212)","Herpes simplex virus or varicella-zoster lesions mimicking impetigo (p.1210)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Kawasaki disease (mimics scarlet fever) (p.1209)","Systemic allergic reactions / drug eruptions (mimic scarlet fever) (p.1209)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Throat culture (gold standard; vigorous rubbing of both tonsillar pillars) and rapid antigen test (latex agglutination/EIA, specificity >95%, relative sensitivity 70-90%); confirm negative rapid test with culture in higher-risk individuals (p.1208)","Antibody tests to streptolysin O (ASO) and DNase B for serodiagnosis of preceding streptococcal infection in suspected ARF or PSGN (p.1207)","emm-typing by PCR/sequencing of the variable region of the emm gene (CDC database) for strain typing (p.1207)","In streptococcal TSS, laboratory findings: marked left shift with immature granulocytes, hypocalcemia, hypoalbuminemia, and thrombocytopenia (worsening on day 2-3) (p.1211)","Blood cultures (most streptococcal TSS patients are bacteremic) (p.1211-1212)","Early surgical exploration of suspected necrotizing fasciitis for diagnosis (reveals fascial-plane necrosis and inflammatory fluid without muscle involvement) (p.1211)","Cultures of vesicular fluid in difficult impetigo cases; culture to rule out MRSA if empirical treatment fails (p.1210)"],"redFlags":["Necrotizing fasciitis is a surgical emergency: severe pain out of proportion to physical findings, with only minimal early skin erythema; progression over hours to dusky/mottled erythema and edema; tenderness evolving into anesthesia from infarction of cutaneous nerves — demands early surgical exploration and debridement (p.1211)","Streptococcal TSS: rapidly progressive, lethal (mortality >=30%) with hypotension and multiorgan failure; early recognition essential (p.1211-1212)","Fever in a patient with impetigo suggests extension to deeper tissues or an alternative diagnosis (p.1210)","GAS bacteremia without an identified source raises the possibility of endocarditis, occult abscess, or osteomyelitis (p.1212)","Unusually severe or prolonged pharyngitis, or localized pain with high fever and toxic appearance, suggests a peritonsillar or parapharyngeal abscess (p.1209)"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated GAS pharyngitis is self-limited, with symptoms resolving in 3-5 days. Suppurative complications (peritonsillar/retropharyngeal abscess, cervical lymphadenitis, sinusitis, otitis, meningitis, bacteremia, endocarditis, pneumonia) are now uncommon with antibiotics; nonsuppurative sequelae are ARF and PSGN. Necrotizing fasciitis and streptococcal TSS are rapidly progressive and life-threatening; streptococcal TSS has a mortality rate of >=30%. Globally GAS sequelae account for ~500,000 deaths/year. Neonatal GBS incidence has fallen from ~2-3 to ~0.6 per 1000 live births with prenatal screening. Recurrent invasive GBS in adults occurs in ~4% of cases.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing streptococcal pharyngitis on clinical grounds alone is unreliable; clinicians over-prescribe antibiotics for sore throat (p.1208)","Rapid antigen tests are less sensitive than culture (relative sensitivity 70-90%), so a negative rapid test should be confirmed by throat culture in higher-risk patients (p.1208)","Improper swab technique lowers rapid-test sensitivity; both tonsillar pillars (and any exudate/ulceration) must be vigorously sampled (p.1208)","In necrotizing fasciitis, early physical findings may be unimpressive despite severe pain; relying on skin appearance delays diagnosis (p.1211)","Macrolide resistance is common in many countries and rising in invasive US GAS isolates (>30% since 2021); avoid macrolides where resistance exceeds 5-10% unless susceptibility is known (p.1208-1209)","Clindamycin resistance occurs in >=30% of invasive GAS isolates; susceptibility should not be assumed without in vitro testing before relying on clindamycin (p.1212)","Aggressive eradication of asymptomatic GAS pharyngeal carriage is usually not justified (carriers have lower risk of ARF and of transmission than symptomatic patients); exceptions arise when a carrier is a source of transmission to others (p.1209)","Endocarditis due to nonenterococcal group D streptococci (S. gallolyticus/S. bovis group) is associated with occult GI neoplasm (often colon carcinoma/polyp; abnormalities in >60%) and should prompt GI evaluation (p.1214)"],"severityClassification":"Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome case definition (CDC, 1993; updated 2010; Table 153-4): I. Isolation of GAS from (A) a normally sterile site or (B) a nonsterile site; II. Clinical signs of severity = (A) hypotension AND (B) >=2 of: renal impairment, coagulopathy, liver function impairment, ARDS, generalized erythematous macular rash that may desquamate, or soft tissue necrosis (including necrotizing fasciitis or myositis) or gangrene. A DEFINITE case meets IA + IIA + IIB; a PROBABLE case meets IB + IIA + IIB if no other etiology is identified. Pharyngitis pretest risk is stratified clinically by the Centor criteria (fever history, absence of cough, tender anterior cervical adenopathy, tonsillar exudate/swelling).","references":["Wessels MR. Streptococcal Infections. In: Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd ed, Ch. 153, pp. 1206-1214.","Carapetis JR et al. The global burden of group A streptococcal diseases. Lancet Infect Dis 5:685, 2005.","Streptococcal Toxic Shock Syndrome (STSS) 2010 Case Definition, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention."],"crossLinks":[]},"strongyloidiasis":{"name":"Strongyloidiasis","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Strongyloides stercoralis infection","threadworm infection","strongyloides hyperinfection syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A soil-transmitted intestinal nematode acquired when larvae in contaminated soil penetrate skin, endemic in warm, humid parts of India","Uniquely among common helminths it can complete its life cycle within the host (autoinfection), so infection may persist silently for decades","Chronic infection is often mild, with intermittent abdominal symptoms, urticaria, and a characteristic migratory serpiginous rash (larva currens); eosinophilia is a common clue","The feared complication is hyperinfection/disseminated disease, precipitated by immunosuppression, especially corticosteroids and HTLV-1 co-infection","Screening and treating latent infection before starting immunosuppression is a key preventive principle","Disseminated strongyloidiasis often carries Gram-negative bacteraemia or meningitis as larvae transport gut bacteria","A single stool examination is insensitive; serology and repeated or concentrated stool studies improve detection","Antiparasitic therapy with ivermectin is the treatment principle, with prolonged/repeated courses needed in disseminated disease"],"pathophysiology":"Filariform larvae penetrate the skin, migrate through the circulation to the lungs, ascend the airways and are swallowed, maturing into adult worms in the small intestine. Females produce eggs that hatch into rhabditiform larvae; some transform into infective filariform larvae within the gut or perianal skin and reinvade the host, sustaining autoinfection. When host cellular immunity is impaired, this autoinfective cycle accelerates uncontrollably, causing massive larval migration through multiple organs (hyperinfection and dissemination) and carrying enteric bacteria that seed the bloodstream, meninges and other sites.","additionalDifferentials":["Other soil-transmitted helminth infections (hookworm, ascariasis)","Peptic ulcer disease or functional dyspepsia","Inflammatory bowel disease","Chronic urticaria of other cause","Hypereosinophilic syndromes"],"infectionMimics":["Drug-induced eosinophilia","Allergic/atopic disease with urticaria","Other causes of Gram-negative sepsis in the immunosuppressed","Eosinophilic gastroenteritis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serology for Strongyloides antibodies, which is more sensitive than single stool testing","Repeated stool microscopy with concentration or agar-plate culture to detect larvae","Full blood count looking for eosinophilia (which may be absent in dissemination)","Examination of sputum, duodenal aspirate or other fluids for larvae in suspected hyperinfection","Screening for HTLV-1 and assessment of immune status in severe disease"],"redFlags":["Hyperinfection or dissemination in an immunosuppressed patient, a life-threatening emergency","Gram-negative bacteraemia or meningitis complicating heavy larval migration","Planned corticosteroid or other immunosuppression in someone from an endemic area without prior screening","Rapid respiratory or gastrointestinal deterioration with detectable larvae"],"prognosis":"Chronic uncomplicated infection responds well to antiparasitic therapy and has an excellent outlook, though eradication should be confirmed given the autoinfection risk. Hyperinfection and disseminated disease, by contrast, carry very high mortality despite treatment, particularly when diagnosis is delayed.","pitfalls":["Starting corticosteroids or immunosuppression without screening at-risk patients, precipitating fatal hyperinfection","Relying on a single negative stool sample to exclude infection","Assuming eosinophilia will always be present, as it is often absent in dissemination","Failing to give a sufficiently prolonged antiparasitic course in disseminated disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"subacute_combined_degeneration":{"name":"Subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SCD","SACD","subacute combined degeneration","B12 myelopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Caused by vitamin B12 (cobalamin) deficiency, which preferentially damages the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts of the spinal cord","Typically presents with symmetrical distal paraesthesiae, loss of vibration and joint-position sense, and a progressive spastic-ataxic gait","Combines upper motor neuron signs (spasticity, extensor plantars) with impaired proprioception and sometimes absent ankle reflexes, giving a mixed picture","Neuropsychiatric features such as cognitive slowing, irritability, or depression may accompany or precede the myelopathy","Serum B12 can be borderline or normal, so raised methylmalonic acid and homocysteine are more sensitive markers of tissue deficiency","Common causes include pernicious anaemia, malabsorption, strict vegan diet, gastric surgery, and prolonged nitrous oxide exposure","Early replacement of B12 can halt and partially reverse deficits; delayed treatment risks permanent disability","Macrocytic anaemia may be absent, so neurological disease can occur without haematological changes"],"pathophysiology":"Vitamin B12 is a cofactor for methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. Deficiency impairs myelin synthesis and maintenance, leading to vacuolar demyelination and axonal degeneration that begins in the dorsal columns and later involves the lateral (corticospinal) columns, hence the term 'combined'. Nitrous oxide irreversibly oxidises the cobalt core of cobalamin and can precipitate the disorder in those with marginal stores. The pathology accounts for the combination of sensory ataxia from posterior column loss and spasticity from corticospinal tract involvement.","additionalDifferentials":["Copper deficiency myelopathy (clinically near-identical picture)","Multiple sclerosis and other demyelinating myelopathy","Compressive cervical myelopathy or spondylotic myelopathy","Tabes dorsalis and other causes of sensory ataxia","Hereditary spastic paraplegia","HTLV-1 associated myelopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum vitamin B12 with methylmalonic acid and homocysteine when B12 is borderline","Full blood count and blood film for macrocytosis and hypersegmented neutrophils","Intrinsic factor and parietal cell antibodies to identify pernicious anaemia","MRI of the spine showing high T2 signal in the dorsal columns (inverted V or dot sign)","Serum copper and caeruloplasmin to exclude copper deficiency mimicking the syndrome","Nerve conduction studies if a coexisting peripheral neuropathy is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive myelopathy causing gait failure or sphincter disturbance","Recent or repeated nitrous oxide use with acute neurological decline","Severe anaemia or pancytopenia requiring urgent evaluation","Coexisting cognitive decline suggesting advanced or long-standing deficiency"],"prognosis":"Recovery depends on how early replacement begins; symptoms present for less than a few months often improve substantially, whereas long-standing deficits may only stabilise, leaving residual sensory ataxia or spasticity.","pitfalls":["Relying on a normal serum B12 and missing functional deficiency demonstrable by methylmalonic acid","Assuming anaemia must be present, thereby overlooking purely neurological presentations","Failing to ask about recreational or repeated medical nitrous oxide exposure","Overlooking copper deficiency, which produces an indistinguishable myelopathy","Attributing spastic-ataxic gait solely to spondylosis and delaying B12 assessment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"subacute_thyroiditis":{"name":"Subacute thyroiditis","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["de Quervain thyroiditis","subacute granulomatous thyroiditis","giant cell thyroiditis","painful subacute thyroiditis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Typically follows a viral upper respiratory illness by a few weeks and presents with a painful, tender thyroid and referred pain to the jaw or ears","Classically evolves through phases: an early thyrotoxic phase from follicular disruption, followed by transient hypothyroidism, and usually a return to euthyroidism","In the thyrotoxic phase radioiodine uptake is characteristically low, which distinguishes it from Graves' disease where uptake is high","The erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein are markedly elevated, reflecting the inflammatory process","Thyrotoxic symptoms arise from release of preformed hormone rather than increased synthesis, so antithyroid drugs are ineffective","Management is largely symptomatic, with anti-inflammatory agents for milder disease and corticosteroids reserved for severe pain, alongside beta-blockade for adrenergic symptoms","A minority of patients are left with permanent hypothyroidism, so thyroid function should be monitored during recovery"],"pathophysiology":"Subacute thyroiditis is thought to result from a viral infection or post-viral inflammatory response affecting the thyroid, producing granulomatous inflammation with giant cells and destruction of thyroid follicles. Disruption of follicles releases stored thyroid hormone into the circulation, causing an initial destructive thyrotoxicosis with suppressed TSH and low radioiodine uptake. As follicular stores are depleted and the gland heals, patients pass through a hypothyroid phase before glandular recovery restores normal hormone production in most cases.","additionalDifferentials":["Graves' disease","Painless (silent) thyroiditis and postpartum thyroiditis","Acute suppurative thyroiditis","Haemorrhage into a thyroid cyst or nodule","Toxic multinodular goitre or toxic adenoma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thyroid function tests to characterise the phase of disease","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein","Radioactive iodine uptake scan showing low uptake in the thyrotoxic phase","Thyroid ultrasound with reduced vascularity distinguishing it from Graves' disease","TSH-receptor antibody testing when Graves' disease is a serious consideration"],"redFlags":["Severe fever, fluctuance, or a rapidly enlarging tender gland suggesting suppurative thyroiditis needing drainage and antimicrobials","Marked thyrotoxic symptoms with cardiac compromise","Persistent hypothyroid symptoms indicating the need for hormone replacement","Compressive symptoms or an atypical mass raising concern for malignancy"],"prognosis":"The illness is usually self-limiting over weeks to months with full recovery of thyroid function in most patients, though pain can be prolonged, recurrences occur occasionally, and a small proportion develop permanent hypothyroidism.","pitfalls":["Mistaking destructive thyrotoxicosis for Graves' disease and prescribing ineffective antithyroid drugs","Failing to check radioiodine uptake or ultrasound vascularity to separate the two","Overlooking the later hypothyroid phase and not monitoring thyroid function through recovery","Attributing a painful gland to malignancy without recognising the inflammatory picture"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"subconjunctival_hemorrhage":{"name":"Subconjunctival haemorrhage","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["subconjunctival hemorrhage","hyposphagma","SCH"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as a painless, flat, bright red patch of blood beneath the conjunctiva with a sharply defined border and normal vision","Usually benign and self-limiting, resolving over one to two weeks as the blood is reabsorbed and changes colour","Common precipitants include Valsalva-type events such as coughing, sneezing, straining, or vomiting, minor trauma, and rubbing","Recurrent or bilateral haemorrhages should prompt review of hypertension, anticoagulant or antiplatelet use, and bleeding disorders","The absence of pain, discharge, and visual disturbance distinguishes it from more serious causes of a red eye","A haemorrhage without a visible posterior margin after trauma may indicate a source from behind the orbit, raising concern for orbital fracture or ruptured globe","Reassurance is the mainstay; lubricants may ease mild irritation"],"pathophysiology":"A subconjunctival haemorrhage is bleeding from small conjunctival or episcleral vessels into the potential space between the conjunctiva and the underlying sclera. Because blood is confined to this superficial layer, it appears as a well-demarcated red sheet without affecting the cornea or intraocular structures. Rises in venous pressure (Valsalva), fragile vessels, trauma, or impaired haemostasis predispose to rupture. The blood is gradually cleared by resorption, passing through the usual colour changes of a resolving bruise.","additionalDifferentials":["Conjunctivitis (bacterial, viral, haemorrhagic)","Ruptured globe with subconjunctival bleeding after trauma","Conjunctival or Kaposi lesion mimicking haemorrhage","Anterior uveitis or scleritis (painful red eye)","Bleeding from orbital or retrobulbar haemorrhage"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood pressure measurement, especially in recurrent cases","Review of anticoagulant and antiplatelet medication","Coagulation studies and platelet count when bleeding is recurrent or spontaneous","Slit-lamp examination to exclude other causes of red eye","Assessment for globe rupture or orbital injury when trauma is involved"],"redFlags":["History of significant trauma with a haemorrhage lacking a posterior border, suggesting globe rupture or orbital fracture","Associated pain, reduced vision, or a relative afferent pupillary defect","Recurrent or spontaneous bilateral haemorrhages indicating a systemic bleeding disorder or uncontrolled hypertension","Proptosis or restricted eye movements suggesting orbital pathology"],"prognosis":"Isolated spontaneous subconjunctival haemorrhage resolves completely without treatment and carries no visual risk; prognosis depends on any underlying systemic or traumatic cause.","pitfalls":["Attributing a traumatic haemorrhage to a benign cause and missing an occult globe rupture","Failing to check blood pressure and clotting in recurrent cases","Overlooking anticoagulant overtreatment as a contributor","Providing unnecessary antibiotics for a non-infective condition"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"substance_use_disorder":{"name":"Substance use disorder","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SUD","addiction","substance dependence","substance abuse"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The disorder is a cluster of cognitive, behavioural, and physiological features indicating continued use of a substance despite significant harm, with severity graded from mild to severe by the number of features present","Core domains include impaired control over use, social impairment, risky use in hazardous situations, and pharmacological features such as tolerance and withdrawal","Tolerance and withdrawal alone in a patient taking a prescribed medication as directed do not by themselves indicate a use disorder","Craving, unsuccessful attempts to cut down, and continued use despite recognised harm are central to the behavioural picture","Screening should be routine and non-judgemental, using validated tools, and should cover alcohol, tobacco, prescription medicines, and illicit substances","Management integrates psychosocial interventions such as motivational interviewing and cognitive behavioural therapy with substance-specific pharmacotherapy and relapse-prevention strategies","Comorbid psychiatric illness is common, and integrated treatment of both the substance disorder and the mental disorder improves outcomes","Withdrawal from some substances, notably alcohol and sedatives, can be life-threatening and may require medically supervised detoxification"],"pathophysiology":"Substance use disorders arise from repeated activation of the mesolimbic dopaminergic reward pathway, producing neuroadaptations in reward, motivation, memory, and executive control circuits that drive compulsive use and craving. Genetic vulnerability, early exposure, psychiatric comorbidity, and environmental and social stressors interact with these neuroadaptations, while allostatic changes in stress and reward systems underlie tolerance, withdrawal, and relapse liability.","additionalDifferentials":["Primary mood or anxiety disorder driving or mimicking substance-related symptoms","Psychotic disorder versus substance-induced psychosis","Delirium or other cognitive disorder from intoxication or withdrawal","Other behavioural addictions","Non-adherence or physiological dependence on prescribed medication without a use disorder","Underlying medical illness producing similar symptoms"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Validated screening instruments and a detailed substance use history","Urine or serum toxicology as appropriate","Liver function tests and other markers of organ damage","Screening for blood-borne viruses in injecting drug use","Assessment of withdrawal severity using standardised scales","Screening for comorbid psychiatric disorders and suicide risk"],"redFlags":["Signs of severe or complicated withdrawal such as seizures, autonomic instability, or delirium tremens","Overdose or respiratory depression requiring urgent intervention","Suicidal ideation or intent, which is elevated in substance use disorders","Injecting practices with risk of sepsis, endocarditis, or blood-borne infection"],"prognosis":"Substance use disorders are chronic, relapsing conditions, but many people achieve sustained remission, particularly with sustained treatment engagement and social support. Prognosis is worse with early onset, severe dependence, polysubstance use, untreated psychiatric comorbidity, and limited social stability, and better with strong recovery support and continuity of care.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling appropriate physiological dependence on prescribed medication as a use disorder","Underestimating the danger of alcohol or sedative withdrawal","Treating the substance disorder while ignoring comorbid psychiatric illness","A judgemental stance that undermines engagement and honest disclosure","Overlooking elevated suicide and overdose risk"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"sudden_infant_death_syndrome":{"name":"Sudden Infant Death Syndrome","system":"Respiratory / General","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SIDS","cot death","crib death"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined as the sudden and unexpected death of an infant under one year that remains unexplained after a thorough death scene investigation, review of history, and complete postmortem examination.","It is a diagnosis of exclusion and forms part of the broader category of sudden unexpected infant death (SUID).","Incidence peaks between about 2 and 4 months of age, and most deaths occur during sleep.","Prone and side sleeping positions are major modifiable risk factors; supine sleep is strongly protective.","Additional risks include soft bedding, bed-sharing, overheating, maternal smoking during and after pregnancy, prematurity, and low birth weight.","Protective measures include breastfeeding, immunisation, pacifier use at sleep, and room-sharing without bed-sharing.","The prevailing model proposes a vulnerable infant meeting an exogenous stressor during a critical developmental period (the triple-risk hypothesis).","Safe-sleep counselling for every family is a core preventive intervention, and any death requires a compassionate, thorough investigation to exclude other causes."],"pathophysiology":"SIDS is thought to arise when an underlying vulnerability, an environmental stressor, and a critical developmental window coincide (the triple-risk model). The vulnerability is believed to involve abnormalities in brainstem regions that regulate arousal, cardiorespiratory control, and responses to hypoxia and hypercarbia, including serotonergic network dysfunction. When such an infant encounters a stressor, for example a prone position, rebreathing of expired air, or overheating during sleep, an impaired protective arousal and autonomic response may fail to correct hypoxia, leading to death.","additionalDifferentials":["Accidental asphyxia or unsafe sleep-related suffocation","Undiagnosed infection or sepsis","Inborn errors of metabolism (e.g. fatty acid oxidation defects)","Cardiac channelopathy or arrhythmia (e.g. long QT syndrome)","Non-accidental injury or inflicted harm","Occult congenital or structural abnormality"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full postmortem examination (autopsy)","Detailed death scene investigation and review of circumstances","Comprehensive clinical and family history review","Metabolic screening including postmortem samples where indicated","Genetic testing for cardiac channelopathies in selected cases","Microbiology and toxicology as part of the exclusion process"],"redFlags":["Modifiable risk factors that mandate active safe-sleep counselling for prevention","Non-supine sleep position, soft bedding, or bed-sharing","Exposure to tobacco smoke before or after birth","History or scene findings suggesting suffocation or non-accidental injury","Recurrent unexplained infant deaths in a family, warranting careful evaluation","Prematurity or low birth weight increasing baseline risk"],"prognosis":"SIDS is by definition fatal; the clinical focus is prevention through consistent safe-sleep practices, which have substantially reduced incidence, and supportive bereavement care for affected families.","pitfalls":["Assigning the label SIDS without a complete investigation that excludes other causes","Failing to deliver safe-sleep advice at every relevant contact","Overlooking an unsafe sleep environment as a contributing factor","Not considering metabolic or cardiac causes in apparently unexplained deaths","Neglecting sensitive support and follow-up for the bereaved family"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"sudden_sensorineural_hearing_loss":{"name":"Sudden sensorineural hearing loss","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SSNHL","sudden deafness","idiopathic sudden sensorineural hearing loss"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined as a rapid-onset sensorineural hearing loss developing over hours to a few days, most often unilateral","It is an otological emergency where prompt evaluation and treatment offer the best chance of hearing recovery","Patients often report a blocked ear, tinnitus and sometimes vertigo, and may notice the loss on waking","Tuning fork tests help confirm a sensorineural pattern: Weber lateralises to the better-hearing ear and Rinne remains positive","The majority of cases are idiopathic, but identifiable causes include infection, vascular events, trauma, ototoxic drugs and, importantly, vestibular schwannoma","Management principles centre on early corticosteroid therapy, given systemically or intratympanically, after excluding a conductive cause","MRI of the internal auditory meatus is important to exclude a retrocochlear lesion such as acoustic neuroma","Delay in diagnosis and treatment reduces the likelihood of hearing recovery, so urgent referral is essential"],"pathophysiology":"Sudden sensorineural hearing loss reflects acute dysfunction of the cochlea or auditory nerve. Although often idiopathic, proposed mechanisms include viral infection or inflammation of the labyrinth, microvascular compromise of the cochlear blood supply, rupture of intracochlear membranes, and autoimmune injury. These processes damage the delicate hair cells and neural elements or disturb the cochlear ionic and metabolic environment, producing abrupt hearing loss. A minority result from identifiable structural causes such as a vestibular schwannoma compressing the cochlear nerve, which is why imaging is required.","additionalDifferentials":["Impacted cerumen or other conductive causes (must be excluded)","Vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma)","Meniere's disease","Perilymph fistula","Ototoxicity from drugs","Central causes such as stroke affecting auditory pathways"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Otoscopy and tuning fork tests to distinguish sensorineural from conductive loss","Pure tone audiometry to confirm and quantify the sensorineural loss","MRI of the internal auditory meatus and posterior fossa to exclude retrocochlear pathology","Blood tests for infective, inflammatory or autoimmune causes as indicated","Vestibular assessment when vertigo accompanies the hearing loss"],"redFlags":["Sudden unilateral hearing loss itself, which is an emergency requiring urgent assessment and treatment","Accompanying neurological signs or severe vertigo suggesting a central or serious cause","Progressive or fluctuating loss with tinnitus indicating possible vestibular schwannoma","Bilateral sudden loss suggesting a systemic, autoimmune or ototoxic process"],"prognosis":"Outcome is variable; a substantial proportion recover partially or fully, particularly with early treatment, but severe loss, delayed presentation, vertigo and poor initial audiometry are associated with worse recovery.","pitfalls":["Dismissing sudden hearing loss as wax or a benign blocked ear and delaying treatment","Failing to distinguish sensorineural from conductive loss with tuning fork tests","Not arranging MRI to exclude a vestibular schwannoma","Missing the narrow window in which corticosteroid therapy is most effective"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"superficial_fungal_infections":{"name":"Superficial Fungal Infections","system":"Skin and Soft Tissue","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Dermatophytosis","Ringworm","Tinea","Pityriasis versicolor","Tinea versicolor","Onychomycosis","Mucocutaneous candidiasis"],"chapter":225,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1722","clinicalPearls":["Superficial fungal infections are caused by molds and yeasts that colonize the outer skin layers, hair follicles, and nails without invading deeper tissue; they are the most common human fungal infections but rarely cause serious disease.","Dermatophytes (Trichophyton, Microsporum, Epidermophyton) are not part of normal skin flora and confine themselves to keratinized structures; symptoms arise from inflammatory reaction to fungal antigens rather than tissue invasion, and an estimated billion people are affected worldwide.","The annular, ring-shaped cutaneous lesion ('ringworm') results from centrifugal outward growth of the organism in the stratum corneum; the term tinea (Latin for worm) is preferred and is paired with the affected body site.","Tinea capitis predominantly affects children aged 3 to 7 years, producing well-demarcated scaly patches with hair shafts broken off near the skin surface and possible alopecia.","Tinea pedis typically begins in the toe web spaces with peeling, maceration, and pruritus, and is a recognized portal of entry for streptococci and staphylococci, predisposing to lower-extremity cellulitis through interdigital fissures.","Onychomycosis affects toenails more often than fingernails, is most common in those with tinea pedis, and shows thickened, discolored, crumbling nails with onycholysis; older age, vascular disease, diabetes, and nail trauma are risk factors.","Malassezia yeasts (notably M. furfur, M. pachydermatis) are lipophilic commensals of sebum-rich areas (back, chest, scalp, face) and cause tinea/pityriasis versicolor, folliculitis, and seborrheic dermatitis; cutaneous IL-17 signaling normally controls them.","Tinea versicolor appears as flat round scaly hypo- or hyperpigmented patches on the neck, chest, or upper arms, usually asymptomatic, and can be confused with vitiligo (which is non-scaly).","KOH (potassium hydroxide) preparation of lesion-edge scrapings demonstrating hyphae is the key bedside diagnostic test; Malassezia shows a characteristic mix of budding bottle-shaped yeasts and short septate hyphae.","Mucocutaneous candidiasis is most often caused by Candida albicans; oral thrush yields white mucosal plaques that scrape off, and antibiotic use, diabetes, and pregnancy are major risk factors for vulvovaginal candidiasis.","Candida auris is an emerging multidrug-resistant species that persistently colonizes human skin, hair follicles, healthcare environments, and devices, grows avidly in human sweat, and drives nosocomial outbreaks.","Seborrheic dermatitis (a Malassezia-associated condition) can be especially severe in patients with AIDS, and Malassezia fungemia, though rare, occurs almost exclusively in premature neonates receiving parenteral lipid nutrition."],"pathophysiology":"Superficial fungi inhabit only the keratinized superficial layers (stratum corneum, hair follicles, nail plate) and do not invade beneath the stratum corneum. Dermatophytes live within keratin and produce disease through host inflammatory responses to fungal antigens rather than by direct tissue invasion; nail involvement begins via the lateral or superficial nail plate then spreads, and hair-shaft invasion may be intra- or peri-shaft. Host defense is mediated by IL-17 responses, which restrict dermatophyte growth and control cutaneous Malassezia; infections are more frequent in males, and progesterone can inhibit dermatophyte growth. Malassezia organisms are lipophilic commensals of sebaceous areas that elicit little inflammation but, when IL-17 responses are excessive, can exacerbate atopic inflammation. Candida species are mucosal/cutaneous commensals; control depends on IL-17-producing lymphoid cells (TH17, gamma-delta T cells, innate lymphoid cells) at barrier sites, and defects in IL-17 signaling predispose to oral and chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis.","additionalDifferentials":["Contact dermatitis","Eczema","Psoriasis (can mimic both tinea corporis and onychomycosis)","Erythrasma","Intertriginous candidiasis","Vitiligo (for tinea versicolor; differentiated by absence of scale)","Bacterial folliculitis","Seborrheic dermatitis / dandruff","Leukoplakia (for oral thrush)"],"infectionMimics":["Intertriginous candidiasis (vs tinea cruris)","Bacterial folliculitis (vs Malassezia folliculitis)","Oral leukoplakia from other infections vs Candida thrush"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Psoriasis","Eczema","Contact dermatitis","Vitiligo","Seborrheic dermatitis","Mucosal leukoplakia"],"additionalInvestigations":["KOH (potassium hydroxide) microscopy of scrapings from the lesion edge to detect hyphae","Fungal culture, indicated when an outbreak is suspected or when there is no response to therapy","KOH preparation showing budding bottle-shaped yeasts with short septate hyphae for Malassezia","Culture of Malassezia requires sterile olive oil added to the medium for suspected disseminated infection","Skin biopsy or nail clippings with culture and microscopy for skin and nail candidiasis","Biopsy of oral/esophageal tissue to distinguish invasive thrush (yeasts, pseudohyphae, and hyphae invading epithelium) from mere colonization (superficial yeast forms only) and from mimics such as leukoplakia","Culture and microscopy of vaginal secretions (swab) for vulvovaginal candidiasis"],"redFlags":["Tinea pedis with interdigital fissuring predisposing to lower-extremity cellulitis from streptococcal/staphylococcal entry","Severe seborrheic dermatitis as a clue to AIDS / advanced immunosuppression","Malassezia fungemia/disseminated infection in premature neonates on parenteral lipid nutrition","Candida auris colonization as a nosocomial multidrug-resistant outbreak risk","Recurrent or extensive mucocutaneous candidiasis suggesting inborn errors of immunity (e.g., STAT1 gain-of-function, IL-17 signaling defects) or AIDS","Moderate-to-severe oral thrush or esophageal involvement requiring systemic evaluation"],"prognosis":"Superficial fungal infections rarely cause serious disease. Malassezia skin infections are benign and self-limited, though recurrences are common. Dermatophyte infections generally respond well to therapy, but onychomycosis is slow to clear and prone to relapse. The outcome of rare systemic Malassezia infection depends on the host's underlying condition, though most affected neonates do well.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing onychomycosis clinically without confirmation, since psoriasis can mimic nail fungal disease (usually with associated skin lesions)","Mistaking tinea versicolor for vitiligo; the absence of scale distinguishes vitiligo","Interpreting superficial yeast forms on oral/esophageal biopsy as invasive candidiasis when they actually represent colonization only","Overlooking tinea pedis as a portal for bacterial cellulitis","Not pursuing culture when a patient fails to respond to empiric therapy or when an outbreak is suspected"],"severityClassification":"Clinically stratified by extent and site: localized cutaneous tinea versus extensive tinea and onychomycosis; for mucocutaneous candidiasis, mild oral thrush is distinguished from moderate/severe or esophageal disease that requires systemic management.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1722","Harrison 22e p.1723","Harrison 22e p.1724"],"crossLinks":[]},"superficial_thrombophlebitis":{"name":"Superficial thrombophlebitis","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["superficial venous thrombosis","SVT","superficial vein thrombophlebitis","phlebitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as a tender, warm, erythematous, palpable cord along the course of a superficial vein, most often in the lower limb","Commonly arises in varicose veins, but can also follow intravenous cannulation or occur in otherwise normal veins","It is frequently a benign, self-limiting process, yet it should not be dismissed because it can coexist with or extend into deep vein thrombosis","Duplex ultrasound is used to confirm the diagnosis, define clot extent, and assess proximity to the saphenofemoral or saphenopopliteal junctions and the deep system","Thrombus close to a deep junction or of substantial length carries a higher risk of propagation and venous thromboembolism, influencing whether anticoagulation is used","Management ranges from symptomatic measures for limited disease to a course of anticoagulation for extensive or junction-approaching thrombus","Migratory or recurrent superficial thrombophlebitis without varicosities should prompt consideration of an underlying thrombophilia or occult malignancy","Suppurative (septic) thrombophlebitis is a distinct infective complication requiring urgent treatment and is not the typical bland form"],"pathophysiology":"Superficial thrombophlebitis is thrombosis with accompanying inflammation of a superficial vein. The elements of Virchow's triad apply: venous stasis (notably in varicose veins), endothelial injury (for example from cannulation), and hypercoagulability. Thrombus formation within the superficial vein triggers a local inflammatory response producing the characteristic tender palpable cord. Because superficial and deep systems communicate through perforators and saphenous junctions, thrombus can propagate into the deep veins, linking the condition to venous thromboembolism.","additionalDifferentials":["Cellulitis","Deep vein thrombosis","Lymphangitis","Ruptured Baker cyst","Erythema nodosum","Contact dermatitis or insect bite reaction"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Venous duplex ultrasound to confirm diagnosis, measure clot length, and assess junctional and deep involvement","Assessment for deep vein thrombosis when clinical suspicion exists","Thrombophilia evaluation in recurrent or unprovoked cases without varicosities","Consideration of malignancy screening in migratory or recurrent disease","Review of intravenous access sites when catheter-related"],"redFlags":["Thrombus extending toward or reaching the saphenofemoral or saphenopopliteal junction, with risk of deep extension and pulmonary embolism","Symptoms or signs of concurrent deep vein thrombosis","Fever, purulence, or systemic upset suggesting septic (suppurative) thrombophlebitis","Migratory or recurrent phlebitis raising concern for occult malignancy or thrombophilia"],"prognosis":"Localised superficial thrombophlebitis in varicose veins is usually self-limiting with a good outcome, whereas extensive disease or thrombus near a deep junction carries a meaningful risk of progression to deep vein thrombosis or pulmonary embolism. Recurrence is common when underlying venous disease or a prothrombotic state persists.","pitfalls":["Assuming all superficial thrombophlebitis is benign and failing to assess deep or junctional extension","Mistaking it for cellulitis and treating with antibiotics alone","Overlooking an underlying malignancy or thrombophilia in migratory or recurrent cases","Not arranging duplex assessment when clot proximity to the deep system would change management"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"supraventricular_tachycardia":{"name":"Supraventricular tachycardia","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SVT","paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia","PSVT","AVNRT","AVRT"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["SVT is a broad term for regular, usually narrow-complex tachycardias originating at or above the atrioventricular node, most commonly AV nodal reentrant tachycardia and AV reentrant tachycardia using an accessory pathway","Typical presentation is abrupt-onset, regular palpitations with sudden termination, often with lightheadedness, chest discomfort, or breathlessness","The QRS is usually narrow unless there is aberrant conduction or a pre-existing bundle branch block, when it can mimic ventricular tachycardia","Vagal manoeuvres are the first-line acute intervention to interrupt reentry through the AV node","When vagal manoeuvres fail in a stable patient, an AV nodal blocking agent given under monitoring is the usual next step","Synchronised cardioversion is indicated when the patient is haemodynamically unstable","Catheter ablation offers a high cure rate for recurrent AVNRT and AVRT and is a definitive long-term option","A regular narrow-complex tachycardia should be distinguished from atrial flutter and atrial fibrillation, which have different management"],"pathophysiology":"Most paroxysmal SVT is caused by reentry. In AV nodal reentrant tachycardia a functional circuit forms within or near the AV node using fast and slow pathways. In AV reentrant tachycardia the circuit incorporates an accessory pathway connecting atrium and ventricle, as in Wolff-Parkinson-White. Atrial tachycardias instead arise from an ectopic atrial focus with enhanced automaticity or microreentry. In reentrant mechanisms a premature beat finds one limb refractory and conducts down the other, then returns retrogradely, establishing a self-sustaining loop that the AV node often participates in, which is why AV nodal blockade frequently terminates the arrhythmia.","additionalDifferentials":["Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter","Ventricular tachycardia (especially with aberrant conduction)","Sinus tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Pre-excited tachycardia in Wolff-Parkinson-White"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG during tachycardia and in sinus rhythm to characterise the mechanism and detect pre-excitation","Continuous rhythm monitoring or ambulatory ECG for paroxysmal episodes","Electrolyte and thyroid function assessment to identify reversible triggers","Echocardiography to exclude structural heart disease","Electrophysiology study to define the circuit when ablation is considered"],"redFlags":["Haemodynamic instability with hypotension, ischaemic chest pain, pulmonary oedema, or reduced consciousness, requiring urgent synchronised cardioversion","Wide-complex tachycardia that cannot be confidently distinguished from ventricular tachycardia, which should be treated as VT until proven otherwise","Pre-excited tachycardia where AV nodal blocking drugs may be hazardous","Recurrent syncope during episodes"],"prognosis":"Most forms of SVT are not life-threatening and carry an excellent prognosis, with catheter ablation curing the majority of reentrant cases; prognosis worsens when SVT complicates structural heart disease or occurs as pre-excited tachycardia with a risk of rapid ventricular rates.","pitfalls":["Giving AV nodal blocking drugs in a pre-excited tachycardia, which can accelerate ventricular response","Misdiagnosing a wide-complex tachycardia as SVT with aberrancy when it is actually ventricular tachycardia","Delaying cardioversion in an unstable patient while attempting pharmacological control","Overlooking reversible precipitants such as stimulants, thyroid disease, or electrolyte disturbance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"sustained_ventricular_tachycardia":{"name":"Sustained Ventricular Tachycardia","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Sustained monomorphic VT","Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia","Idiopathic VT","Belhassen's VT","Verapamil-sensitive VT","Bundle branch reentry VT","Outflow tract VT"],"chapter":261,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1963","clinicalPearls":["Sustained monomorphic VT is a wide-QRS ventricular arrhythmia lasting at least 30 seconds, or one requiring intervention (antitachycardia pacing or cardioversion) for termination; each QRS resembles the others, pointing to a single focal origin or repetitive exit from a fixed reentry circuit.","In structural heart disease the usual substrate is patchy replacement fibrosis from prior infarction, fibrosis, inflammation, or previous cardiac surgery, which sets up anatomic or functional reentry; less often VT arises from reentry or automaticity in diseased Purkinje conduction tissue.","Clinical tolerance depends on rate, baseline ventricular function, and autonomic response: rapid VT may cause hypotension and syncope (especially with poor LV function), while patients with normal function may have only palpitations despite fast rates.","Hemodynamic stability during a wide-complex tachycardia does NOT distinguish VT from supraventricular tachycardia with aberrancy; tolerance of the rhythm should never be used to exclude VT.","Ventriculoatrial (AV) dissociation is a reliable marker of VT, provided the atrial rate is slower than the ventricular rate; however a P wave following every QRS does not exclude VT because 1:1 ventriculo-atrial conduction can occur.","A monophasic R or Rs complex in lead aVR, or concordance (all monophasic R or all S waves) across V1 to V6, is relatively specific for VT.","In a patient with known structural heart disease, a wide-QRS tachycardia is most likely VT regardless of QRS morphology; depressed LV function or evidence of structural disease makes scar-related reentry the most likely mechanism of sustained monomorphic VT.","Scar is suggested by pathologic Q waves on ECG, segmental wall-motion abnormalities on echo or nuclear imaging, and areas of delayed gadolinium enhancement on cardiac MRI.","Idiopathic VT occurs in structurally normal hearts (normal sinus ECG, normal function and no scar on imaging), is provoked by sympathetic stimulation or acute sympathetic withdrawal (e.g. immediately post-exercise), and is generally benign with low risk of sudden death.","Outflow tract VT most often arises in the right ventricular outflow tract, producing a left bundle branch block pattern in V1 with an inferiorly directed axis (tall R waves in II, III, aVF); a prominent R wave in V1 or V2 suggests a left ventricular outflow tract or aortic-root origin.","LV fascicular VT (Belhassen's / verapamil-sensitive VT) is the second most common idiopathic form: it shows a right bundle branch block-like configuration negative in the inferior leads and is due to a small reentry circuit near the septal ramifications of the left Purkinje system; despite being reentrant its course is typically benign.","Repeated incessant bursts of nonsustained monomorphic VT (repetitive monomorphic VT) can produce a tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy with depressed ventricular function that recovers once the arrhythmia is suppressed."],"pathophysiology":"Sustained monomorphic VT most commonly results from reentry around a fixed substrate. In structural heart disease this substrate is an area of patchy replacement fibrosis (from infarction, inflammation, fibrosis, or surgical scar) creating anatomic or functional reentrant circuits; the uniform QRS reflects a single focal origin or repetitive exit from a stable circuit. In coronary artery disease the typical setting is a remote large myocardial infarction with a remodeled ventricle and depressed LV function, where the infarct scar provides a durable reentry substrate that is not generally dependent on ongoing acute ischemia. In nonischemic dilated cardiomyopathy, scar-related reentry from progressive replacement fibrosis underlies VT, with scars often intramural or subepicardial and frequently adjacent to a valve annulus; sarcoidosis, Chagas' disease, and Lamin A/C mutation cardiomyopathy are particularly associated. Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy involves fibrofatty replacement chiefly of right ventricular myocardium producing a left bundle branch block-like VT. Bundle branch reentry VT (about 5% of monomorphic VT with structural disease) uses a macroreentrant circuit through the Purkinje system, usually retrograde up the left bundle and anterograde down the right bundle, generating a left bundle branch block configuration. Idiopathic VT in structurally normal hearts arises either from focal triggered automaticity (outflow tract foci near the pulmonic or aortic annuli) or from small reentry circuits in the Purkinje system (fascicular VT). Rapid or scar-associated monomorphic VT may degenerate into ventricular fibrillation.","additionalDifferentials":["Supraventricular tachycardia with left or right bundle branch block aberrancy","Supraventricular tachycardia conducted to the ventricles over an accessory pathway (pre-excited tachycardia)","Rapid ventricular pacing (appropriate or inappropriate) in a patient with a pacemaker or defibrillator","Telemetry noise and movement artifact simulating VT"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["SVT with aberrant conduction (bundle branch block)","Antidromic AV reentrant tachycardia / pre-excited SVT over an accessory pathway","Pacemaker-mediated rapid ventricular pacing"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG during tachycardia analyzing AV dissociation, aVR morphology, and V1-V6 concordance to differentiate VT from SVT with aberrancy","Comparison of tachycardia QRS morphology with the baseline sinus-rhythm QRS (identical morphology in a known bundle branch block favors SVT but is not definitive)","Echocardiography to assess ventricular function and detect segmental wall-motion abnormalities indicating scar","Cardiac MRI for delayed gadolinium enhancement to identify and localize fibrosis (intramural/subepicardial in nonischemic cardiomyopathy)","Nuclear imaging for wall-motion abnormalities","Cardiac biomarkers (troponin, CK-MB) to evaluate myocardial injury, recognizing that acute MI rarely causes sustained monomorphic VT and modest elevations usually reflect demand ischemia during the arrhythmia rather than an acute coronary event","Electrophysiologic study when noninvasive criteria leave the diagnosis uncertain"],"redFlags":["Hypotension, impaired consciousness, or pulmonary edema accompanying the tachycardia (hemodynamic instability)","Rapid VT in a patient with significant ventricular dysfunction (high risk of syncope and degeneration to VF)","VT presenting as resuscitated out-of-hospital cardiac arrest where VF was the initial recorded rhythm","Sustained monomorphic VT in the setting of structural heart disease (high recurrence and sudden-death risk)","Frequent recurrent or incessant VT episodes","Syncope or cardiac arrest as the presenting event in inherited cardiomyopathies (ARVC, repaired tetralogy of Fallot)"],"prognosis":"Prognosis hinges on the presence and type of underlying heart disease. Scar-related monomorphic VT in structural heart disease carries a high recurrence risk; with ischemic cardiomyopathy and a prior large MI, up to 70% of patients have arrhythmia recurrence within 2 years, and even after device-based secondary prevention the depressed-ejection-fraction population remains at risk for heart failure, recurrent ischemic events, and recurrent VT, with a 5-year mortality exceeding 30%. Device shocks and antitachycardia pacing are themselves adverse events linked to higher rates of heart failure, mortality, and psychological stress. By contrast, idiopathic VT in a structurally normal heart is generally benign, sudden death is rare, and a defibrillator is not recommended; LV fascicular (Belhassen's) VT likewise follows a benign course despite being reentrant. In ARVC, clinical heart failure is rare until late stages and survival to advanced age can be expected provided the VT is controlled. In repaired tetralogy of Fallot the risk of sudden cardiac death may reach roughly 1% per year in adulthood by the fourth or fifth decade.","pitfalls":["Using hemodynamic stability to rule out VT - tolerance of the rhythm does not exclude VT.","Assuming a P wave after every QRS excludes VT, when 1:1 ventriculo-atrial conduction can mimic this.","Relying on a single QRS-morphology criterion; all such criteria have limitations and are unreliable in severe heart disease, and bundle branch reentry VT can closely resemble the native sinus QRS.","Interpreting troponin/CK-MB elevations during VT as proof of an acute coronary event - these usually reflect demand ischemia from hypotension and fixed coronary disease; acute MI is rarely the cause of sustained monomorphic VT.","Mistaking telemetry noise or movement artifact for VT, leading to unnecessary tests and interventions.","Attributing a typical outflow-tract QRS morphology automatically to benign idiopathic VT, since cardiomyopathies (notably ARVC) can produce PVCs/VT from the same region and must be excluded.","Failing to recognize repetitive monomorphic (incessant nonsustained) VT as a reversible cause of tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy."],"severityClassification":"Harrison classifies sustained ventricular arrhythmias by substrate/etiology rather than a graded severity scale: (1) idiopathic VT without structural heart disease - outflow tract (RV outflow: LBBB pattern in V1 with inferior axis and late precordial transition; LV outflow: inferior axis with early precordial transition and prominent R in V2-V3), LV fascicular VT (RBBB pattern in V1 with left axis deviation), and papillary muscle VT; (2) ischemic cardiomyopathy (monomorphic VT common after large prior MI); (3) nonischemic cardiomyopathy (fibrotic scar VT, e.g. sarcoidosis, Chagas', Lamin A/C); (4) arrhythmogenic RV cardiomyopathy (RV-origin LBBB-pattern VT); (5) repaired tetralogy of Fallot (RV-origin VT); (6) hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; and (7) genetic arrhythmia syndromes (long QT, Brugada, catecholaminergic polymorphic VT, short QT/early repolarization). Practically, the most important severity distinction is between benign idiopathic VT in a structurally normal heart and scar-related VT in structural heart disease, the latter conferring substantial sudden-death risk.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1963","Harrison 22e p.1964","Harrison 22e p.1965","Harrison 22e p.1966","Harrison 22e p.1967","Harrison 22e p.1968"],"crossLinks":[]},"syphilis":{"name":"Syphilis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lues","Treponema pallidum infection"],"chapter":187,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1428-1436","clinicalPearls":["Chronic systemic infection by Treponema pallidum subsp. pallidum, usually sexually transmitted, characterized by episodes of active disease separated by asymptomatic latent periods (Harrison 22e p.1428).","Staging is the organizing framework: a primary chancre at the site of inoculation, then generalized secondary mucocutaneous disease, followed by latency, and in a minority of untreated patients tertiary disease (gumma, cardiovascular, late neurosyphilis) (Harrison 22e p.1428, p.1430).","Primary chancre is typically a single painless papule that erodes into an indurated ulcer with a firm cartilaginous edge; it is accompanied by regional (usually inguinal) lymphadenopathy that is firm, nonsuppurative, and painless, and heals spontaneously in about 4-6 weeks (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Secondary syphilis classically produces a widespread macular-to-papular, nonpruritic rash that frequently involves the palms and soles, plus generalized nontender lymphadenopathy (noted in ~85% of patients) (Harrison 22e p.1430-1431).","Condylomata lata (broad, moist, highly infectious lesions in intertriginous areas, ~10% of secondary cases) and mucous patches (painless silver-gray erosions of oral/genital mucosa) are characteristic secondary lesions (Harrison 22e p.1431).","Latent syphilis is defined by reactive serology with a normal CSF and no clinical manifestations; early latent is within the first year, late latent is >=1 year or of unknown duration (Harrison 22e p.1431).","Two classes of serologic tests: lipoidal (nontreponemal) RPR/VDRL, which are quantitative and reflect disease activity, and treponemal tests (FTA-ABS, TPPA, EIA/CIA); a reactive lipoidal screen must be confirmed by a treponemal test (Harrison 22e p.1433).","RPR is preferred for rapid serologic diagnosis; the VDRL remains the standard for CSF examination and is superior to RPR for that purpose (Harrison 22e p.1433-1434).","After treatment of early syphilis a fourfold (two-dilution) fall in VDRL/RPR titer (e.g., 1:32 to 1:8) signals adequate response; treponemal tests typically remain reactive and are not useful for monitoring therapy (Harrison 22e p.1433, p.1435).","Penicillin is the drug of choice for all stages as a principle; its efficacy against T. pallidum remains undiminished after decades of use (Harrison 22e p.1434).","Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction (fever, chills, myalgia, headache, tachycardia) may follow the start of therapy, thought to be a response to lipoproteins released by dying treponemes; it occurs in ~50% of primary and ~90% of secondary cases, defervesces within 12-24 h, is self-limited, and does not require steroids (Harrison 22e p.1435).","Earliest manifestations of early congenital syphilis include rhinitis ('snuffles'), mucocutaneous lesions, periostitis/bone changes, and hepatosplenomegaly; classic late stigmata include Hutchinson's teeth, mulberry molars, saddle nose, and saber shins (Harrison 22e p.1432-1433)."],"pathophysiology":"Treponema pallidum subsp. pallidum is a thin spiral organism with a lipid-rich outer membrane and periplasmic endoflagella that drive motility; it has severely limited metabolic capacity and depends on host amino acids, carbohydrates, and lipids. Humans are the only known natural host. After penetrating intact mucous membranes or skin abrasions, treponemes reach the lymphatics and blood within hours, producing systemic dissemination and metastatic foci well before the primary lesion appears (median incubation ~21 days, rarely >6 weeks). Tissue damage reflects the host immune response rather than a direct treponemal toxin: lesions show perivascular infiltration by CD4+ and CD8+ T cells, plasma cells, and macrophages with endarteritis obliterans (obliteration of small vessels). Antigenic variation of the TprK outer-membrane protein allows immune evasion and persistence, explaining the paradoxical appearance of secondary lesions after primary lesions clear. Tertiary lesions (gumma, aortitis of the vasa vasorum of the ascending aorta, parenchymal neurosyphilis) arise from a slowly progressive inflammatory process that begins early in infection (Harrison 22e p.1428-1430, p.1432).","additionalDifferentials":["Primary chancre vs herpes simplex virus genital ulceration (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Primary chancre vs chancroid (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Primary chancre vs donovanosis (granuloma inguinale) (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Primary chancre vs traumatic genital injury (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Neonatal congenital syphilis vs rubella, cytomegalovirus, herpes simplex, toxoplasmosis, and erythroblastosis fetalis (Harrison 22e p.1432)"],"infectionMimics":["Genital herpes simplex virus infection (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Chancroid (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Donovanosis (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Other treponemal infections (yaws, pinta, bejel) that also give reactive treponemal serology (Harrison 22e p.1433)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Traumatic genital injury mimicking a primary chancre (Harrison 22e p.1430)","Rectosigmoid mass of secondary syphilis mistaken for malignancy (Harrison 22e p.1431)","Ocular syphilis (uveitis/iritis) mistaken for non-infectious uveitis when it fails to respond to topical steroids (Harrison 22e p.1431)"],"additionalInvestigations":["RPR or VDRL lipoidal (nontreponemal) serology for screening and quantitation, confirmed by a treponemal test (FTA-ABS or TPPA) (Harrison 22e p.1433)","Treponemal EIA/CIA automated assays as screening tests in large laboratories, with reflex TPPA confirmation (Harrison 22e p.1433-1434)","Dark-field microscopy or direct fluorescent antibody of moist lesions to demonstrate T. pallidum (rarely available outside research settings) (Harrison 22e p.1433)","PCR of lesion swabs, tissue, blood, CSF, ocular fluid, urine, or oropharyngeal/rectal swabs (not commercially available; in-house validated assays) (Harrison 22e p.1433)","CSF examination (mononuclear pleocytosis >5/uL, protein >45 mg/dL, CSF VDRL) for suspected neurosyphilis; CSF VDRL is highly specific but insensitive (Harrison 22e p.1432, p.1434)","HIV testing in all patients with newly diagnosed syphilis (Harrison 22e p.1434)"],"redFlags":["Neurosyphilis at any stage: meningeal syphilis (headache, neck stiffness, cranial nerve involvement, seizures, mental status change), meningovascular syphilis (stroke syndrome in a relatively young adult), or late parenchymatous disease (general paresis, tabes dorsalis) (Harrison 22e p.1432)","Ocular syphilis (uveitis, iritis, optic neuritis, pupillary abnormalities)—permanent blindness may result without prompt diagnosis and treatment (Harrison 22e p.1431)","Otic syphilis (sensorineural hearing loss, vertigo, tinnitus)—deafness may result if untreated (Harrison 22e p.1431)","Cardiovascular syphilis: aortitis with aortic regurgitation, saccular aneurysm of the ascending aorta, or coronary ostial stenosis (Harrison 22e p.1432)","Maternal syphilis in pregnancy: high risk of fetal loss, stillbirth, prematurity, neonatal death, and congenital syphilis (Harrison 22e p.1432)","Serum RPR titer >=1:32 marks higher risk of neurosyphilis (Harrison 22e p.1432, p.1434)"],"prognosis":"Untreated primary and secondary lesions resolve spontaneously, but infection persists. In the preantibiotic era about one-third of untreated patients with latent infection progressed to tertiary disease; the Tuskegee Study showed untreated syphilis raised the death rate ~17%, largely from cardiovascular disease. With early treatment, prognosis is excellent and tertiary syphilis is now nearly eliminated in Western countries; the clinical response to therapy for benign tertiary (gumma) disease is usually impressive. Congenital syphilis prognosis is poor when fulminant disease is clinically apparent at birth, but adequate maternal treatment before the 16th week of pregnancy should prevent fetal damage. Response to therapy is monitored by a fourfold decline in VDRL/RPR titer (Harrison 22e p.1430, p.1432, p.1435).","pitfalls":["Prozone phenomenon: a high-titer specimen may give a nonreactive or only weakly reactive lipoidal test with undiluted serum that becomes reactive only on dilution—a falsely negative screen if dilution is not performed (Harrison 22e p.1433).","Primary syphilis is often missed in women and in MSM because chancres on the cervix, vaginal wall, anal canal, rectum, or mouth go unseen; atypical or trivial dark-field-negative genital lesions should still prompt consideration of syphilis (Harrison 22e p.1430).","Both lipoidal and treponemal tests may be nonreactive in early primary syphilis; an early primary ulcer can present with nonreactive serology, so negative serology does not exclude it (Harrison 22e p.1431-1433).","Biological false-positive lipoidal tests occur (usually titer <=1:8, e.g., in autoimmune disease or injection drug use); a nonreactive treponemal test excludes syphilis in this setting (Harrison 22e p.1433-1434).","Treponemal tests usually remain reactive for life after treatment and cannot distinguish active from past infection or gauge response to therapy (Harrison 22e p.1433, p.1435).","CSF VDRL is highly specific but insensitive—it may be nonreactive even in symptomatic neurosyphilis—and RPR must not be substituted for VDRL on CSF (Harrison 22e p.1434).","Benzathine penicillin does not achieve treponemicidal levels in CSF even at high doses, so it is inadequate for neurosyphilis and viable T. pallidum has been recovered from CSF after such treatment (Harrison 22e p.1434).","Syphilis is a 'great imitator': its protean mucocutaneous and systemic features overlap with many other diseases, so it must be considered broadly (Harrison 22e p.1430-1431)."],"severityClassification":"Disease is described by clinical stage rather than a graded severity score: primary syphilis (chancre with regional lymphadenopathy); secondary syphilis (generalized mucocutaneous rash including palms and soles, condylomata lata, mucous patches, constitutional symptoms); latent syphilis (reactive serology, normal CSF, no clinical signs; early latent <1 year vs late latent >=1 year or unknown duration); tertiary/late syphilis (gumma, cardiovascular syphilis, late neurosyphilis). Neurosyphilis spans a continuum: early (asymptomatic meningitis, meningeal and meningovascular syphilis) and late parenchymatous disease (general paresis, tabes dorsalis). Congenital syphilis is divided into early (within first 2 years) and late (after 2 years) (Harrison 22e p.1428, p.1431-1433).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1428-1436"],"crossLinks":[]},"syringomyelia":{"name":"Syringomyelia","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["syrinx","spinal cord cavitation","hydromyelia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A fluid-filled cavity (syrinx) within the spinal cord that classically produces a dissociated sensory loss, with impaired pain and temperature but preserved light touch and proprioception","The dissociated sensory loss often has a cape-like distribution over the shoulders and arms as the central cord and crossing spinothalamic fibres are affected first","Painless burns or injuries and neuropathic joint degeneration (Charcot joints) can result from the loss of protective pain sensation","Progressive lower motor neuron signs in the hands and arms occur as the syrinx expands into the anterior horns","Frequently associated with a Chiari I malformation at the craniocervical junction, and also with prior trauma, tumour, or arachnoiditis","MRI of the whole spine and craniocervical junction is the definitive investigation and defines the syrinx and any underlying cause","Management targets the underlying cause, for example posterior fossa decompression for Chiari-associated syrinx, rather than the cavity alone","Extension of the cavity into the brainstem (syringobulbia) can cause lower cranial nerve and bulbar dysfunction"],"pathophysiology":"Syringomyelia is characterised by a longitudinal cavity within the spinal cord that disrupts neural tissue from the centre outward. Altered cerebrospinal fluid dynamics, often related to obstruction at the craniocervical junction as in Chiari I malformation, are thought to drive fluid accumulation and cavity expansion. The central location preferentially damages the decussating spinothalamic fibres, producing dissociated sensory loss, and progressive enlargement affects anterior horn cells, long tracts, and autonomic pathways.","additionalDifferentials":["Intramedullary spinal cord tumour","Multiple sclerosis and other myelopathies","Cervical spondylotic myelopathy","Motor neuron disease when lower motor neuron hand wasting predominates","Peripheral neuropathy","Leprosy and other causes of dissociated sensory loss"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the entire spine and craniocervical junction to define the syrinx and identify Chiari malformation or other cause","MRI of the brain to assess the posterior fossa and hindbrain","Contrast-enhanced imaging when an underlying tumour is suspected","CSF flow studies in selected cases to assess craniocervical junction dynamics","Neurophysiological studies where the differential includes motor neuron disease or neuropathy"],"redFlags":["Rapid neurological deterioration or new bulbar symptoms from syringobulbia requiring urgent neurosurgical assessment","Progressive weakness, sensory loss, or sphincter disturbance indicating cord compromise","Associated Chiari malformation with brainstem compression signs","An underlying spinal cord tumour presenting as a secondary syrinx"],"prognosis":"Syringomyelia often progresses slowly over years, with a stepwise or gradual accumulation of deficits. Treating the underlying cause, particularly decompressing a Chiari malformation, can stabilise or improve symptoms, but established neurological deficits may not fully reverse and some patients continue to deteriorate despite surgery.","pitfalls":["Failing to recognise dissociated sensory loss and its localising value","Overlooking an associated Chiari malformation or underlying tumour as the treatable cause","Attributing hand wasting solely to motor neuron disease without imaging the cord","Not counselling patients about protecting insensate areas from burns and injury"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"systemic_lupus_erythematosus":{"name":"Systemic Lupus Erythematosus","system":"Immune-Mediated / Rheumatologic (multisystem)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["SLE","Lupus","Lupus erythematosus, systemic"],"chapter":368,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2827","clinicalPearls":["SLE is an autoimmune disease driven by overactive innate and adaptive immunity, with tissue injury mediated by autoantibodies and immune complexes; cutaneous, musculoskeletal, and renal systems are most often affected, followed by pulmonary, hematologic, cardiovascular, serosal, and CNS involvement (p.2827).","Roughly 90% of patients are women, most of childbearing age; in the US the highest prevalence is in Black women, then Hispanic, White, and Asian/Pacific Islander women, and non-White patients tend to have more severe disease (p.2827).","Autoantibodies can be detectable years before clinical diagnosis, so seropositivity helps attribute nonspecific constitutional symptoms to SLE (p.2827, p.2830).","ANA at a titer of at least 1:80 is the best screening test and is present in nearly all patients; repeatedly negative immunofluorescence ANA makes the diagnosis unlikely, though about 5% of patients are ANA-negative (p.2832).","Anti-dsDNA (present in ~40-60%, specificity ~75-99%) and anti-Sm (present in ~30%, highly specific) are the only ANA subtypes in the EULAR/ACR criteria; rising anti-dsDNA with falling C3/C4 signals active disease, especially impending nephritis (p.2832, p.2830).","Lupus arthritis is a nonerosive polyarthritis of wrists, MCP/PIP joints, and knees; periarticular ligament damage can cause reversible Jaccoud-like deformities, and overlap with rheumatoid arthritis is termed 'rhupus' (p.2829).","The classic malar 'butterfly' rash spares the nasolabial folds (distinguishing it from rosacea); subacute cutaneous lupus is photosensitive and linked to anti-Ro/SSA, while discoid lesions are the most common chronic cutaneous form (p.2829).","Nephritis occurs in about 35% of patients, may be asymptomatic, and warrants screening every 3 months with a urine protein-to-creatinine ratio; biopsy is generally prompted by UPCr >0.5 or declining renal function (p.2830, p.2833).","Antiphospholipid antibodies are present in ~50% of patients and markedly raise risk of arterial/venous thrombosis, thrombocytopenia, recurrent fetal loss, Libman-Sacks endocarditis, and stroke (p.2830, p.2832).","Anti-Ro/SSA antibodies can cross the placenta and cause neonatal lupus (transient rash/lab abnormalities or, less commonly, congenital heart block); high-titer Ro warrants fetal echocardiography screening (p.2836).","Avascular (osteo)necrosis should be suspected when pain is out of proportion in a single hip, shoulder, or knee, particularly with prior corticosteroid use (p.2829).","Some patients are categorized as 'type 1 lupus' (classic immune-driven features such as nephritis, arthritis, vasculitis, generally steroid-responsive) versus 'type 2 lupus' (fatigue, diffuse pain, cognitive dysfunction/'brain fog', less responsive to immunosuppression) (p.2827-2828)."],"pathophysiology":"SLE arises from multiple immunoregulatory defects in genetically predisposed individuals interacting with environmental triggers. There is a strong genetic component (24% vs 2% concordance in monozygotic vs dizygotic twins; >150 GWAS susceptibility loci), with the MHC the most common association and nearly half of loci tied to type 1 interferon production or signaling (e.g., TLR7, IRF5/7/8, STAT4); complement-cascade defects (C1q, C1r, C1s, C2, C4) impair clearance of apoptotic debris and immune complexes and underlie severe monogenic disease. Environmental contributors include UV light, crystalline silica, smoking, sex hormones, Epstein-Barr virus, and gut-microbiome-related increased intestinal permeability. Innate defects cause excess cellular debris (high neutrophil turnover and NETosis) that overwhelms macrophage/dendritic clearance, triggering nucleic-acid sensors and TLRs in plasmacytoid dendritic cells to produce type 1 interferon, which sensitizes the adaptive immune system. Loss of B- and T-cell tolerance (including expansion of age-associated B cells, T follicular/peripheral helper cell help, and defective regulatory T cells) drives production of pathogenic autoantibodies; autoantibody-nucleic acid immune complexes deposit in tissue, activate monocytes, and produce inflammation, repair, and eventually fibrosis after recurrent flares.","additionalDifferentials":["Drug-induced lupus (antihistone-antibody positive; rarely renal or CNS; resolves after stopping the offending drug)","Rheumatoid arthritis (and rhupus overlap)","Mixed connective tissue disease / anti-U1-RNP-associated disease","Sjogren's syndrome","Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma)","Inflammatory myositis (dermatomyositis/polymyositis)","Antiphospholipid syndrome (primary)","Rosacea (mimics malar rash)","Other ANA-associated autoimmune conditions: autoimmune hepatitis, Hashimoto's thyroiditis, primary biliary cholangitis"],"infectionMimics":["Infectious encephalitis (must be excluded when SLE psychosis/confusional state is considered)","Pulmonary infection (acute lupus pneumonitis can appear indistinguishable from infection on imaging)","Viral infection-related cytopenias (cytopenias in SLE may be due to infection rather than disease activity)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Glucocorticoid-induced psychosis (mimics neuropsychiatric lupus psychosis)","Migraine or tension headache (overlaps with neuropsychiatric lupus headache)","Rosacea (mimics the malar/butterfly rash)","Drug-induced lupus (clinically and serologically overlaps with SLE)","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura / atypical hemolytic-uremic syndrome (microangiopathy mimic)"],"additionalInvestigations":["ANA by immunofluorescence with titer (screening; positive threshold 1:80 or higher)","Anti-dsDNA and anti-Sm antibodies (specific subtypes; anti-dsDNA also tracks disease activity)","Anti-Ro/SSA antibodies (useful in ANA-negative suspected SLE; neonatal-lupus risk)","Antihistone antibodies (drug-induced lupus, positive in ~75% of DIL)","Antiphospholipid panel: anticardiolipin, anti-beta-2-glycoprotein-1 (IgG/IgM/IgA), and lupus anticoagulant via dilute Russell viper venom time","Complement levels C3 and C4 (fall with active disease / immune-complex consumption)","Complete blood count for cytopenias; peripheral smear for schistocytes and ADAMTS13 activity plus LDH if microangiopathy suspected","Renal assessment: eGFR, serum creatinine, random or 24-h urine protein-to-creatinine ratio, screened quarterly","Renal biopsy (ISN/RPS classification) when UPCr exceeds 0.5 or renal function declines","Emerging urinary biomarkers when available (urinary IL-16, CD163, type 1 IFN, TGF-beta)","Creatine kinase and aldolase with muscle biopsy if inflammatory myopathy suspected","Baseline and yearly retinal examination when on hydroxychloroquine (maculopathy surveillance)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly declining renal function or rising proteinuria suggesting class III/IV proliferative lupus nephritis (~20% of class IV cases die or reach ESRD within 10 years)","Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage or acute pneumonitis (life-threatening pulmonary involvement)","Severe neuropsychiatric disease: psychosis, seizures, transverse myelitis, stroke","Severe thrombocytopenia (<20,000-50,000/uL) with bleeding risk, or life-threatening hemolytic anemia","Atypical hemolytic-uremic syndrome / thrombotic microangiopathy (hemolysis, thrombocytopenia, schistocytes, low ADAMTS13, high LDH)","Mesenteric vasculitis (risk of intestinal perforation, bleeding, ischemia)","Antiphospholipid syndrome with arterial/venous thrombosis or catastrophic APS","Pulmonary arterial hypertension or prior arterial thrombosis in pregnancy (highest maternal mortality risk)","New chest pain in a young patient given accelerated atherosclerosis and premature coronary disease"],"prognosis":"Prognosis and survival vary widely by geographic region, race/ethnicity, and access to care and medications. Most patients have active disease despite therapy or recurrent flares; about 15% have relatively mild disease. Renal disease is a major determinant of outcome: roughly 20% of US patients with class IV lupus nephritis die or develop ESRD within 10 years, and non-White patients more often progress to ESRD. Accelerated atherosclerosis becomes a leading cause of death after about 5 years of disease, with elevated myocardial infarction and stroke rates driven by both traditional risk factors and lupus-specific factors (antiphospholipid antibodies, high disease activity, low C3, high glucocorticoid dose, elevated homocysteine/leptin). Glucocorticoids contribute to irreversible, dose-dependent organ damage (cataracts, osteoporotic fractures, avascular necrosis, diabetes) even at low doses; class V nephritis carries a better renal prognosis than class III/IV. Sustained low disease activity (LLDAS) for 2 or more years is associated with significantly less damage, and meta-analyses suggest an increased overall risk of cancer and cancer-related death.","pitfalls":["Treating ANA positivity as diagnostic: ANA occurs at low titer in healthy people and in many other autoimmune diseases; diagnosis ultimately rests on clinical judgment, and classification criteria were designed for trial enrollment, not diagnosis.","Excluding SLE because ANA is negative: about 5% of patients are ANA-negative, where anti-Ro/SSA can support the diagnosis.","Missing asymptomatic lupus nephritis or cytopenias by not screening quarterly, since renal and hematologic disease can be present without symptoms.","Attributing acute pulmonary infiltrates to lupus pneumonitis when infection looks identical on imaging.","Ascribing psychosis or confusion to neuropsychiatric lupus without ruling out glucocorticoid-induced psychosis and infectious encephalitis.","Overlooking avascular necrosis as a cause of disproportionate single-joint pain in patients on corticosteroids.","Confusing drug-induced lupus (antihistone-positive, usually sparing kidney/brain, resolving after withdrawal) with idiopathic SLE.","Using estrogen-containing contraceptives or HRT in patients with antiphospholipid antibodies or active disease, increasing thromboembolism risk.","Using direct oral anticoagulants instead of warfarin for concomitant APS, or continuing teratogenic agents (mycophenolate, methotrexate, warfarin) in pregnancy."],"severityClassification":"Lupus nephritis is graded by the International Society of Nephrology/Renal Pathology Society (ISN/RPS) classes: I (mesangial deposits without hypercellularity), II (mesangial proliferative), III (focal proliferative glomerulonephritis), IV (diffuse proliferative glomerulonephritis), and V (membranous), with III/IV +/- V combinations carrying the highest risk; class V (~20% of nephritis) more often presents with nephrotic syndrome but has a better prognosis than III/IV. Disease activity is quantified with the SLEDAI (and SLEDAI-2K), where low disease activity is a score of 0-4 and remission a score of 0 (with stable hydroxychloroquine/immunosuppressive use and prednisone equivalent 5 mg/day or less). Overall disease severity is also stratified clinically as mild (constitutional symptoms, mild arthritis, rash <=9% BSA, platelets 50-100, SLEDAI <=6), moderate (rheumatoid-like arthritis, rash 9-18% BSA, platelets 20-50, serositis, SLEDAI 7-12), and severe (major organ-threatening disease such as cerebritis, myelitis, pneumonitis, mesenteric vasculitis; platelets <20; TTP-like or hemophagocytic syndrome; rash >18% BSA; SLEDAI >12). Treat-to-target endpoints include LLDAS and DORIS remission, and trials use SRI, BICLA, and CLASI instruments.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2827","Harrison 22e p.2828","Harrison 22e p.2829","Harrison 22e p.2830","Harrison 22e p.2831","Harrison 22e p.2832","Harrison 22e p.2833","Harrison 22e p.2835","Harrison 22e p.2836"],"crossLinks":[]},"systemic_sclerosis":{"name":"Systemic Sclerosis (Scleroderma)","system":"Rheumatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["scleroderma"],"chapter":372,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2859-2872","clinicalPearls":["Two major subsets: limited cutaneous SSc (lcSSc) with skin confined to fingers, distal limbs and face (trunk spared) and an indolent course; and diffuse cutaneous SSc (dcSSc) with rapidly progressive skin involvement of fingers, extremities, face and trunk (p.2859, 2866).","In lcSSc, Raynaud's phenomenon typically precedes sclerodactyly and other manifestations, sometimes by years; the CREST constellation (calcinosis cutis, Raynaud's, esophageal dysmotility, sclerodactyly, telangiectasia) is a distinct lcSSc subset (p.2859).","Bilateral symmetrical skin thickening starting in the fingers and advancing distal-to-proximal is the hallmark distinguishing SSc from other connective tissue diseases (p.2867).","SSc-specific autoantibodies are mutually exclusive and aid risk stratification: anti-centromere is associated with lcSSc and isolated PAH (renal crisis rare); anti-topoisomerase I (Scl-70) with dcSSc, extensive skin, early ILD and cardiac involvement; anti-RNA polymerase III with rapidly progressive skin, renal crisis and contemporaneous cancer (p.2864, 2871).","Antinuclear antibodies (ANA) are detectable in virtually all SSc patients, even in early/preclinical stages (p.2864, 2871).","Nailfold capillaroscopy distinguishes primary Raynaud's disease (regular, evenly spaced parallel hairpin loops) from secondary Raynaud's/SSc (giant capillaries, dilated and distorted loops, microhemorrhages, capillary dropout) and helps establish early SSc diagnosis (p.2867, 2871).","ILD and pulmonary vascular disease together account for the majority of SSc-related deaths; survival is particularly poor when both coexist (p.2868).","Scleroderma renal crisis presents with accelerated hypertension plus acute kidney injury, occurs in <15% of patients almost always within 4 years of onset, and is associated with diffuse/progressive skin involvement and anti-RNA polymerase III (up to 50%); anti-centromere appears protective (p.2870-2871).","Palpable tendon friction rubs are associated with increased risk of renal and cardiac complications and reduced survival, and may be a harbinger of impending renal crisis (p.2871).","SSc patients should be screened for PAH at initial evaluation and annually thereafter, and screened for ILD with regular pulmonary monitoring for several years after diagnosis (p.2868, 2872).","SSc sine scleroderma: characteristic Raynaud's plus typical clinical/serologic features of SSc in the absence of detectable skin thickening (~10% of patients) (p.2859, 2866, 2872)."],"pathophysiology":"SSc is a chronic, often progressive disease of unknown etiology defined pathologically by widespread microangiopathy (fibroproliferative vasculopathy with capillary loss and obliteration) combined with multiorgan fibrosis, on a background of autoimmunity. An initial vascular insult in a genetically predisposed individual (modest HLA class II and immune-regulatory gene associations) triggers inflammation, generation of SSc-specific autoantibodies, and sustained activation of fibroblasts/myofibroblasts driven by mediators such as TGF-beta, IL-6, CTGF, PDGF and endothelin. Maladaptive repair leads to replacement of normal tissue with rigid, avascular, acellular connective tissue affecting skin, lungs, heart, GI tract, kidneys and other organs (p.2859-2866).","additionalDifferentials":["Localized scleroderma (morphea, linear scleroderma, coup de sabre) - generally not complicated by Raynaud's or internal organ involvement (p.2860, 2871)","Mixed connective tissue disease and SSc overlap syndromes (with polymyositis, Sjogren's, SLE, polyarthritis, autoimmune liver disease) (p.2860, 2866)","Eosinophilic fasciitis (Shulman's disease) (p.2860)","Scleromyxedema (papular mucinosis) and scleredema (Buschke / diabetic) (p.2860)","Chronic graft-versus-host disease (p.2860)","Primary Raynaud's disease - benign, must be differentiated from early/limited SSc (p.2867, 2871)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis (associated with gadolinium exposure) (p.2860)","Vinyl chloride-induced disease and other chemically/drug-associated scleroderma-like conditions (p.2860)","Eosinophilia-myalgia syndrome (L-tryptophan contaminant) (p.2860)","Stiff skin syndrome and pachydermoperiostosis (p.2860)","Paraneoplastic scleroderma-like syndrome (p.2860)","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura / thrombotic microangiopathy - scleroderma renal crisis is sometimes initially misdiagnosed as TTP (p.2871)"],"additionalInvestigations":["ANA and SSc-specific autoantibodies (anti-centromere, anti-topoisomerase I/Scl-70, anti-RNA polymerase III, U3-RNP/fibrillarin, Th/To, PM/Scl) for diagnosis and risk stratification (p.2864, 2871)","Nailfold capillaroscopy (low-power stereomicroscope or videocapillaroscopy under immersion oil) (p.2867)","High-resolution chest CT (HRCT) for ILD detection and prognostic assessment of fibrosis extent (p.2868)","Pulmonary function testing including FVC and DLCO for ILD monitoring (a DLCO reduction out of proportion to lung volumes suggests pulmonary vascular disease) (p.2868)","Doppler echocardiography for PAH screening; right heart (cardiac) catheterization to confirm PAH and exclude venoocclusive/postcapillary causes (p.2868)","NT-proBNP correlates with presence/severity of PAH and survival (not specific) (p.2868-2869, 2871)","Cardiac MRI / tissue Doppler / SPECT for occult myocardial involvement (p.2871)","Blood film for microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, platelet count, urinalysis and creatinine in suspected renal crisis (p.2871)"],"redFlags":["Scleroderma renal crisis: accelerated/new hypertension (generally >150/90 mmHg) with progressive oliguric renal failure - a medical emergency (note ~10% present normotensive, with poorer outcome) (p.2870-2871)","Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia with fragmented red cells and thrombocytopenia (hallmark of renal crisis) (p.2871)","New unexplained anemia, thrombocytopenia, pericardial effusion or new tendon friction rubs as harbingers of impending renal crisis (p.2871)","Pulmonary arterial hypertension: exertional dyspnea, near-syncope, loud P2, right heart failure - 3-year survival of untreated PAH <50% (p.2868)","Rapidly progressive ILD with declining FVC (can fall ~30%/year), most rapid in the first 3-5 years (p.2868)","Critical digital ischemia with fingertip ulceration/necrosis and acro-osteolysis (p.2867)"],"prognosis":"Highly variable; dcSSc carries a more rapidly progressive course and worse prognosis than lcSSc. SSc confers a fivefold to eightfold higher age/sex-adjusted mortality, with median survival ~11 years; 5- and 10-year survival are ~70%/55% in dcSSc and ~90%/75% in lcSSc. Major causes of death are PAH, pulmonary fibrosis, GI involvement and cardiac disease. Scleroderma renal crisis carries ~30% 3-year mortality (markedly improved by ACE inhibitors). Poor-prognosis markers include male sex, African ancestry, older age at onset, extensive truncal skin involvement, palpable tendon friction rubs, significant/progressive visceral involvement, and laboratory findings of elevated ESR, anemia, proteinuria and anti-topoisomerase I antibodies (p.2873).","pitfalls":["Early dcSSc may present with only upper-extremity edema and puffy fingers and Raynaud's may be absent, leading to misdiagnosis as arthritis, SLE, myositis or undifferentiated connective tissue disease (p.2872)","Scleroderma renal crisis can be misdiagnosed as TTP or other thrombotic microangiopathy; renal biopsy and von Willebrand factor-cleaving protease activity may help (p.2871)","Glucocorticoids (especially prednisone >=10 mg/d) are associated with precipitating scleroderma renal crisis and should be used only when essential and at low dose in high-risk patients (p.2870)","PFTs and conventional echocardiography are relatively insensitive for early ILD and early cardiac involvement, respectively (p.2868, 2871)","Echocardiography can over- or underestimate pulmonary artery pressures, so PAH must be confirmed by cardiac catheterization (p.2868)","SSc sine scleroderma can be missed because diagnostic skin induration is absent despite organ involvement (p.2872)"],"severityClassification":"ACR/EULAR classification criteria are >90% specific and sensitive but are intended for research rather than clinical practice; weighted items include skin thickening proximal to MCP joints (score 9), sclerodactyly/puffy fingers, fingertip lesions, telangiectasia, abnormal nailfold capillaries, ILD/PAH, Raynaud's phenomenon, and SSc-specific autoantibodies (anti-centromere, Scl-70, RNA polymerase III). Clinically, patients are stratified into limited cutaneous and diffuse cutaneous subsets by extent of skin involvement (p.2860).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2859-2873"],"crossLinks":["ild"]},"tabes_dorsalis":{"name":"Tabes dorsalis","system":"Neurology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tabetic neurosyphilis","syphilitic myelopathy","progressive locomotor ataxia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A late (tertiary) form of neurosyphilis caused by Treponema pallidum, developing years to decades after untreated infection","Results from degeneration of the dorsal columns and dorsal roots, producing a progressive sensory ataxia with a broad-based, stamping gait","Lightning pains, lancinating paroxysms in the limbs or trunk, are a characteristic feature","Loss of proprioception and vibration causes a positive Romberg sign, and deep tendon reflexes are often absent","The Argyll Robertson pupil accommodates but does not react to light and is a classic associated sign","Impaired pain and position sense can lead to painless Charcot joints and trophic foot ulcers","Visceral crises, such as sudden severe abdominal pain, and bladder dysfunction may occur","Diagnosis rests on positive serum and cerebrospinal fluid treponemal testing, and penicillin-based therapy halts progression but may not reverse established deficits"],"pathophysiology":"Chronic infection with Treponema pallidum leads, in the tertiary phase, to inflammatory degeneration of the posterior (dorsal) columns and the dorsal nerve roots and ganglia. Loss of large sensory fibres carrying proprioception and vibration produces sensory ataxia, areflexia, and the characteristic gait, while dorsal root involvement generates the lancinating lightning pains. The associated pupillary and autonomic findings reflect more widespread involvement of the nervous system. The condition is now rare where syphilis is treated early but persists in undertreated populations and those with HIV co-infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Subacute combined degeneration from vitamin B12 deficiency","Sensory ganglionopathy (paraneoplastic or toxic)","Diabetic pseudotabes","Hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathies","Other causes of dorsal column dysfunction and sensory ataxia","Compressive posterior column myelopathy"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum treponemal and non-treponemal serology (e.g. TPPA and RPR/VDRL)","Cerebrospinal fluid examination including CSF-VDRL, cell count, and protein","HIV testing given frequent co-infection and altered natural history","MRI of the spine to demonstrate posterior column change and exclude other myelopathy","Nerve conduction studies to characterise the sensory involvement","Ophthalmological assessment of pupillary abnormalities"],"redFlags":["New or evolving neurological deficits in a patient with untreated or inadequately treated syphilis","Concurrent HIV infection, which accelerates and complicates neurosyphilis","Visceral crises or autonomic dysfunction requiring exclusion of other acute pathology","Neuropathic joint destruction or non-healing trophic ulcers indicating advanced sensory loss"],"prognosis":"Antibiotic treatment arrests further progression and treats active infection, but long-established dorsal column and root damage often persists, so residual ataxia, pain, and pupillary abnormalities are common; earlier treatment yields better outcomes.","pitfalls":["Overlooking syphilis as a cause of sensory ataxia in the modern era","Confusing the picture with B12 deficiency and not sending treponemal serology","Failing to test for and address concurrent HIV infection","Misattributing lightning pains to a primary painful neuropathy","Expecting antibiotics to reverse fixed neurological deficits and neglecting supportive care"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"takayasu_arteritis":{"name":"Takayasu arteritis","system":"Rheumatology / MSK","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TAK","pulseless disease","aortic arch syndrome","large-vessel vasculitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ACR-EULAR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A large-vessel vasculitis affecting the aorta and its major branches, characteristically in young women under 40, often of Asian descent","An early systemic phase of malaise, fever, and arthralgia may precede a later occlusive phase with vascular symptoms","Classic signs include diminished or absent peripheral pulses, blood pressure asymmetry between limbs, vascular bruits, and limb claudication","Renovascular involvement is an important cause of secondary hypertension in young patients","Inflammatory markers are often raised but can be normal despite ongoing vascular damage","Cross-sectional vascular imaging (MR or CT angiography, PET) demonstrates wall thickening, stenoses, occlusions, and aneurysms","Blood pressure should be measured in the arm with the least affected artery, as readings can be falsely low with subclavian stenosis","Corticosteroids are first-line, with steroid-sparing immunosuppressants or biologics for relapsing disease, and revascularisation reserved for critical lesions once inflammation is controlled"],"pathophysiology":"Takayasu arteritis is a chronic granulomatous large-vessel vasculitis of unknown cause. Cell-mediated immunity drives infiltration of the arterial wall by T cells, macrophages, and giant cells, beginning in the adventitia and vasa vasorum and extending through the media. Chronic inflammation produces intimal proliferation, medial destruction, and fibrosis, leading to segmental stenosis, occlusion, aneurysm formation, and thrombosis of the aorta and its principal branches. The resulting reduced or absent blood flow causes the ischaemic and 'pulseless' clinical manifestations.","additionalDifferentials":["Giant cell arteritis (large-vessel involvement in older patients)","Fibromuscular dysplasia","Atherosclerotic occlusive disease","IgG4-related aortitis and other aortitides","Behçet disease with vascular involvement","Infective aortitis (e.g. syphilis, tuberculosis)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bilateral limb blood pressures and pulse examination with auscultation for bruits","MR or CT angiography of the aorta and branches for wall thickening, stenosis, and aneurysm","FDG-PET to assess vascular wall inflammatory activity","Inflammatory markers (ESR and CRP) recognising they may be normal","Assessment for renovascular hypertension and end-organ ischaemia"],"redFlags":["Cerebrovascular symptoms, visual loss, or syncope from carotid or vertebral artery involvement","Severe or refractory hypertension from renal artery stenosis with risk of end-organ damage","Critical limb or mesenteric ischaemia","Aortic aneurysm with risk of dissection or rupture, or significant aortic regurgitation","Myocardial ischaemia from coronary ostial involvement"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and immunosuppression many patients do well, but relapses are common and progressive vascular damage can cause hypertension, ischaemic complications, and aneurysms. Long-term morbidity relates to arterial stenoses, aortic regurgitation, and cardiovascular events.","pitfalls":["Missing the diagnosis in a young woman with nonspecific systemic symptoms before vascular signs appear","Relying on a single-arm blood pressure that is falsely low due to subclavian stenosis","Assuming normal inflammatory markers exclude active disease","Attempting revascularisation during active inflammation with high restenosis risk","Overlooking renovascular hypertension as a treatable cause of raised blood pressure"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ACR/EULAR guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"tension_pneumothorax":{"name":"Tension pneumothorax","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["tension PTX"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["This is a clinical diagnosis to be treated immediately, without waiting for imaging, because delay is fatal","Suspect it in a shocked, breathless patient with reduced breath sounds and hyperresonance on one side, especially after trauma or positive-pressure ventilation","Distended neck veins, tracheal deviation away from the affected side, and hypotension are supportive but often late or absent signs","Immediate treatment is emergency decompression of the affected side, followed by definitive chest drain insertion","In a mechanically ventilated patient, sudden hypotension with high airway pressures should prompt consideration of tension pneumothorax","It arises during the breathing step of the primary survey and takes priority over other breathing problems","Reassess after decompression to confirm improvement and to guard against recurrence or a drain that has stopped working"],"pathophysiology":"A one-way valve mechanism allows air to enter the pleural space during inspiration but prevents its escape, so intrapleural pressure rises progressively. The affected lung collapses, and mounting pressure pushes the mediastinum towards the opposite side, compressing the other lung and, critically, kinking the great veins to impair venous return to the heart. The combination of impaired gas exchange and obstructed venous return produces profound hypoxia and obstructive shock. Positive-pressure ventilation can accelerate air accumulation and the development of tension physiology.","additionalDifferentials":["Simple or open pneumothorax without tension physiology","Massive haemothorax","Cardiac tamponade","Massive pulmonary embolism causing obstructive shock","Right main bronchus intubation causing unilateral reduced breath sounds"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination is the basis for immediate treatment; do not delay for imaging","Bedside thoracic ultrasound can rapidly confirm absent lung sliding when immediately available","Chest radiograph only after decompression or when the diagnosis is genuinely uncertain and the patient is stable","Continuous monitoring of oxygenation and haemodynamics","Post-procedure imaging to confirm lung re-expansion and drain position"],"redFlags":["Shock with unilateral absent breath sounds requiring immediate needle or finger decompression before any imaging","Sudden hypotension and rising airway pressures in a ventilated patient","Distended neck veins with tracheal deviation and respiratory distress","Rapid clinical deterioration in a patient with known chest injury or pneumothorax","Recurrence of tension physiology after apparent treatment, indicating a blocked or displaced drain"],"prognosis":"With immediate recognition and decompression the outlook is excellent, but even a short delay can be rapidly fatal because of progressive obstructive shock and hypoxia.","pitfalls":["Waiting for a chest radiograph to confirm the diagnosis before treating","Relying on late signs such as tracheal deviation, which may never appear","Failing to consider it in a deteriorating ventilated patient","Assuming decompression is definitive and not proceeding to a chest drain","Not reassessing for recurrence after initial treatment"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"testicular_cancer":{"name":"Testicular Cancer","system":"Genitourinary / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Testicular germ cell tumor","Testicular GCT","Germ cell tumor of the testis"],"chapter":93,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.704","clinicalPearls":["Germ cell tumors (GCTs) account for about 95% of all testicular neoplasms; non-GCTs of the testis are much less common, and roughly 5% of GCTs arise in extragonadal sites such as the mediastinum, retroperitoneum, and pineal gland.","Testicular cancer is regarded as a model curable neoplasm: about 95% of newly diagnosed patients are cured, and treatment is dictated by pathology (seminoma vs nonseminoma) and stage.","It is the most common malignancy in adolescent and young adult males (age 15-39), most often diagnosed in Caucasians and much less common in African Americans; incidence in those over 50 is rising.","Strongest risk factors are a prior history of GCT, cryptorchidism, and germ cell neoplasia in situ; a prior GCT carries a 1-2% risk of a contralateral tumor (usually metachronous), and cryptorchidism raises risk roughly four- to sixfold.","Orchidopexy before puberty reduces but does not eliminate the cryptorchidism-associated risk, and the contralateral normally descended testis also remains at risk.","A solid mass on scrotal ultrasound (typically hypoechoic, sometimes multifocal) must be considered malignant until proven otherwise; transscrotal aspiration or biopsy must never be performed because it risks seeding the scrotum or inguinal nodes.","Tumor marker biology is key: seminomas never secrete AFP, so a histologic seminoma with elevated AFP is managed as a nonseminomatous GCT; hCG can be elevated in both seminoma and nonseminoma, and choriocarcinoma may produce markedly high hCG.","Serum tumor markers (AFP, hCG, LDH) should be measured before and after orchiectomy; AFP is elevated in roughly 60-70% of nonseminomatous GCTs, while most seminomas and up to a third of nonseminomas have no marker elevation.","Lymphatic spread follows a predictable pattern: left-sided tumors drain first to left paraaortic nodes below the left renal vessels, right-sided tumors to aortocaval nodes below the right renal vessels, then to retrocrural, mediastinal, and supraclavicular nodes; the lung is the most common hematogenous site.","Genetically, most GCTs show excess copies of isochromosome 12p (i[12p]); the strongest heritable-risk locus identified is KITLG (KIT ligand) on chromosome 12, and overall heritability is nearly 50%.","Teratoma is chemotherapy-resistant and must be managed surgically; the other nonseminomatous subtypes (embryonal carcinoma, yolk sac tumor, choriocarcinoma) and seminomas are chemosensitive.","Sperm banking should be offered before chemotherapy: about 30% of newly diagnosed patients already have severe oligospermia or azoospermia, and treatment-related sterility is common (though ~80% recover spermatogenesis over years)."],"pathophysiology":"GCTs originate from the primordial germ cell, and most malignant GCTs arise from germ cell neoplasia in situ; the precise molecular steps driving progression from in situ neoplasia to invasive tumor are not fully defined. Genetic analysis shows excess copy number of isochromosome 12p in most cases, and genome-wide studies implicate several loci, most strongly KITLG on chromosome 12. Tumors are classified as seminoma (must be 100% seminoma; any mixed tumor is treated as nonseminomatous) or nonseminoma. Seminomas may contain syncytiotrophoblastic cells that secrete hCG but never secrete AFP. Nonseminomatous subtypes include embryonal carcinoma (the most undifferentiated, able to differentiate into the others, and may secrete AFP and/or hCG), yolk sac tumor (often AFP-secreting), choriocarcinoma (aggressive, often very high hCG), and teratoma (mature, immature, or malignant somatic tissue derived from two or more germ layers).","additionalDifferentials":["Epididymitis","Orchitis","Testicular atrophy","Hereditary persistence of AFP or hepatic disease (false AFP elevation)","Non-germ-cell testicular tumors"],"infectionMimics":["Epididymitis (a trial of antibacterials may be considered, but failure to respond should prompt scrotal ultrasound)","Orchitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Testicular atrophy","Hereditary persistence of AFP (mildly elevated baseline AFP)","Hepatic disease causing false AFP elevation","False hCG elevation from hypogonadism, marijuana use, or assay interference"],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal ultrasound of both testicles (GCT typically hypoechoic, possibly multifocal)","Serum AFP, hCG, and LDH before and after orchiectomy","CT scan of abdomen and pelvis for staging","Chest x-ray, with chest CT if retroperitoneal disease or lung nodules are present","Pathologic review of the entire orchiectomy specimen by an experienced pathologist","Serum microRNA miR-371a-3p (a promising investigational biomarker undergoing validation)"],"redFlags":["Painless testicular mass (pathognomonic of GCT) or testicular swelling, firmness, or discomfort","Back or flank pain suggesting retroperitoneal metastases","Cough, dyspnea, or hemoptysis indicating lung metastases","Gynecomastia in the setting of elevated hCG","Suspected epididymitis/orchitis that fails to respond to antibiotics","Rapidly progressive disease with life-threatening symptoms (e.g., hemoptysis) warranting emergent treatment even without tissue diagnosis"],"prognosis":"Outcomes are excellent overall, with about 95% of newly diagnosed patients cured. Nearly all stage I patients (seminoma and nonseminoma) achieve cure. In metastatic (stage III) disease classified by the International Germ Cell Consensus Classification, most patients have good-risk disease with >90% cure; intermediate- and poor-risk groups have approximately 80% and 50% five-year survival, respectively. About 20-30% of patients with metastatic GCT treated with cisplatin-based chemotherapy do not achieve durable control, with most relapses occurring within 2 years of completing treatment.","pitfalls":["Performing transscrotal biopsy or aspiration of a testicular mass, which can seed the scrotum or inguinal nodes and should never be done.","Misclassifying a histologic seminoma with elevated AFP as a true seminoma, when it must be approached as a nonseminomatous GCT.","Over-relying on tumor markers for diagnosis, since most seminomas and up to a third of nonseminomas have no marker elevation.","Diagnostic delay (not uncommon) leading to more advanced stage at presentation, including mislabeling a GCT as epididymitis/orchitis.","Assuming residual postchemotherapy masses are benign: imaging cannot distinguish necrosis/fibrosis from teratoma or viable tumor, and enlarging cystic retroperitoneal masses with normalized markers may represent growing teratoma syndrome.","Forgetting that LDH is a nonspecific marker whose main role is risk classification in metastatic disease, not diagnosis."],"severityClassification":"Three main AJCC TNM-based stages: Stage I limited to the testis; Stage II involving retroperitoneal lymph nodes (subgrouped IIA/IIB/IIC by node size: <=2 cm, >2-5 cm, >5 cm); Stage III with lymph node involvement beyond the retroperitoneum and/or distant metastases. Stage III is further stratified into good-, intermediate-, and poor-risk groups by the International Germ Cell Consensus Classification using histology, primary site, presence of nonpulmonary visceral metastases, and postorchiectomy marker levels (e.g., for NSGCT good-risk: AFP <1000 ng/mL, hCG <5000 mIU/mL, LDH <1.5x ULN; poor-risk: AFP >10,000 ng/mL, hCG >50,000 mIU/mL, LDH >10x ULN, mediastinal primary, or nonpulmonary visceral metastases). Marker half-lives: AFP 5-7 days, hCG 24-36 hours.","references":["Harrison 22e p.704","Harrison 22e p.705","Harrison 22e p.706","Harrison 22e p.707","Harrison 22e p.708"],"crossLinks":[]},"testicular_torsion":{"name":"Testicular torsion","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["torsion of the testis","spermatic cord torsion","testis torsion"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Classic presentation is sudden, severe unilateral scrotal pain of rapid onset, often with nausea and vomiting, most commonly in adolescents and young men but also seen in neonates","Examination typically shows a swollen, exquisitely tender, high-riding testis that may lie transversely (horizontal lie) with an absent cremasteric reflex","It is a time-critical surgical emergency: testicular salvage is high if detorsion occurs within about six hours and falls sharply thereafter","The bell-clapper anomaly, in which the tunica vaginalis invests the testis abnormally, predisposes to intravaginal torsion and is often bilateral","Diagnosis is fundamentally clinical; imaging should never delay surgery when torsion is strongly suspected","Colour Doppler ultrasound showing absent or reduced intratesticular blood flow supports the diagnosis, but a normal study does not reliably exclude it","Definitive treatment is emergency scrotal exploration with detorsion and orchidopexy of the affected testis, plus fixation of the contralateral testis because the predisposing anomaly is usually bilateral","Manual detorsion may be attempted as a temporising measure but does not replace surgical exploration and fixation"],"pathophysiology":"Testicular torsion is twisting of the spermatic cord that obstructs venous drainage first and then arterial inflow, producing ischaemia and, if unrelieved, haemorrhagic infarction of the testis. The intravaginal form, most common in adolescents, is facilitated by the bell-clapper deformity where the tunica vaginalis surrounds the testis and epididymis completely, allowing the testis to rotate freely within it. Extravaginal torsion occurs in neonates when the entire tunica and its contents twist above the level of attachment. The degree of twisting and the duration of ischaemia determine the likelihood of viability.","additionalDifferentials":["Epididymo-orchitis","Torsion of a testicular or epididymal appendage (appendix testis)","Incarcerated or strangulated inguinal hernia","Idiopathic scrotal oedema","Testicular trauma or haematoma","Testicular tumour with haemorrhage","Henoch-Schonlein purpura involving the scrotum"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent surgical exploration when clinical suspicion is high (imaging must not delay this)","Colour Doppler ultrasound to assess intratesticular blood flow when the diagnosis is uncertain and readily available","Urinalysis, which is typically normal and helps distinguish from infection","Assessment of the contralateral testis given the frequently bilateral anatomical predisposition"],"redFlags":["Testicular torsion is a urological emergency; salvage depends on detorsion within roughly six hours, so exploration must not be delayed for investigations","Sudden severe scrotal pain with a high-riding testis and absent cremasteric reflex until proven otherwise","Neonatal torsion, which may present with a firm, discoloured, painless scrotal swelling","Progressive testicular ischaemia leading to non-viable testis and need for orchidectomy"],"prognosis":"Testicular viability depends critically on time to detorsion, with excellent salvage within a few hours and progressively poorer outcomes beyond six to twelve hours; a non-viable testis requires orchidectomy, and infarction may later affect fertility.","pitfalls":["Attributing acute scrotal pain to epididymo-orchitis and delaying surgical exploration","Waiting for or relying on ultrasound when clinical suspicion is high","Assuming a normal Doppler study excludes torsion, especially in intermittent or partial torsion","Failing to fix the contralateral testis, leaving it at risk of subsequent torsion","Dismissing intermittent testicular pain that may represent recurrent torsion-detorsion"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"tetanus":{"name":"Tetanus","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lockjaw"],"chapter":157,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1230-1233","clinicalPearls":["Trismus (lockjaw) is typically the earliest symptom, progressing to neck and trunk rigidity, dysphagia, and pharyngeal/laryngeal spasm; risus sardonicus and opisthotonos reflect the characteristic sustained muscle rigidity.","Generalized tetanus is the most common form, characterized by muscle rigidity with generalized spasms; localized tetanus affects only isolated muscle groups but can progress to the generalized form.","Cephalic tetanus is a localized form involving the cranial nerves, which can cause pharyngeal or laryngeal spasm leading to aspiration, airway obstruction, or respiratory failure.","Neonatal tetanus classically presents with loss of the ability to suck and cry (a newborn normal in the first 2 days who deteriorates between days 3 and 28), poor feeding, rigidity, and generalized spasms.","Laryngeal spasm causing apnea or respiratory failure is a life-threatening event and, without immediate respiratory support, is the most common cause of death.","Autonomic nervous system disturbance characteristically emerges in the second week of severe disease, with fluctuating heart rate and blood pressure, sweating, gastrointestinal stasis, and increased tracheal secretions; cardiovascular events then become the major threat.","Disease severity is graded with the Ablett classification (Grades I-IV), where Grade IV is Grade III plus autonomic dysfunction.","Abdominal rigidity in tetanus is characteristically continuous, distinguishing it from the rigidity of peritonitis or an acute abdomen.","Roughly 20-30% of cases have no identifiable entry wound; superficial limb abrasions are the most common portal of entry in adults, while deeper injuries (open fractures, drug injection) are linked to more severe disease.","Natural infection does not confer protective immunity because tetanus toxin is poorly immunogenic, so survivors still require a full primary immunization course."],"pathophysiology":"Tetanus is caused by tetanus toxin (tetanospasmin) produced by Clostridium tetani, an anaerobic, gram-positive, spore-forming rod whose resilient environmental spores enter through wounds, abrasions, or the umbilical stump and germinate in anaerobic tissue. Only toxin-producing organisms cause disease. The toxin is a 150-kDa protein cleaved into a heavy (100-kDa) and light (50-kDa) chain joined by a disulfide bond; the heavy-chain carboxy terminal binds presynaptic motor nerve terminals, leading to internalization and retrograde intra-axonal transport to motor nuclei of cranial nerves and spinal cord ventral horns. The toxin then crosses the synapse into GABA-ergic presynaptic inhibitory interneurons, where the light chain (a zinc-dependent endopeptidase) cleaves VAMP2 (synaptobrevin), blocking inhibitory neurotransmitter release. Loss of inhibition produces unregulated motor activity (muscle rigidity and spasms), and similar disinhibition in the autonomic system produces autonomic instability; elevated circulating catecholamines in severe disease drive cardiovascular complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Strychnine poisoning","Dystonic reactions to antidopaminergic (antidopaminergic drug) agents","Stiff person syndrome","Somatic symptom disorder","Hypocalcemia (in neonatal tetanus)","Meningoencephalitis (in neonatal tetanus)","Oropharyngeal, peritonsillar, or submaxillary infection / lymphadenitis and cranial nerve disease (mimics of cephalic tetanus / trismus)","Intracranial hemorrhage","Peritonitis or acute abdominal emergency (versus continuous abdominal rigidity of tetanus)"],"infectionMimics":["Oropharyngeal infection","Peritonsillar infection","Submaxillary lymphadenitis","Meningoencephalitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Strychnine poisoning","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Dystonic reaction to antidopaminergic drugs","Stiff person syndrome","Somatic symptom disorder","Hypocalcemia","Intracranial hemorrhage"],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis is clinical; confirmatory laboratory testing is limited.","Wound culture of C. tetani cannot confirm the diagnosis (a positive culture is non-confirmatory).","Serum antitetanus IgG measured before antitoxin/immunoglobulin administration: levels >0.1 IU/mL (standard ELISA) are deemed protective and argue against tetanus.","Serum tetanus toxin bioassay may help if antibody is below the protective threshold, but a negative result does not exclude tetanus and the assay is not generally performed.","PCR and recombinase polymerase amplification to detect the tetanus neurotoxin gene are promising but emerging techniques."],"redFlags":["Laryngeal spasm with apnea or respiratory failure - life-threatening and the most common cause of death.","Autonomic nervous system disturbance (labile heart rate and blood pressure, sweating, GI stasis, increased tracheal secretions), typically in the second week, marking high cardiovascular mortality risk.","Short incubation period (wound to first symptom) and short period of onset (first symptom to first generalized/pharyngeal/laryngeal spasm) - rapid progression predicts worse outcome.","Dysphagia with laryngeal hyperactivity, which makes airway management difficult and raises aspiration risk."],"prognosis":"Tetanus has a high survival rate when complications are controlled and interventions are prompt, though recovery is prolonged, typically taking about 4-6 weeks. Rapid onset predicts more severe disease: the shorter the incubation period (wound to first symptom) and the period of onset (first symptom to first generalized or pharyngeal/laryngeal spasm), the worse the prognosis. The Tetanus Severity Score (TSS) outperforms the Dakar and Philips scores for predicting severity and outcome (sensitivity ~66%, specificity ~91%; a cutoff score of >=8 predicts death). Surviving adults, especially the elderly, may have reduced long-term functional outcomes, while affected children and neonates have a higher incidence of neurologic sequelae (neonates may face increased risk of learning disabilities, behavioral problems, cerebral palsy, and deafness).","pitfalls":["Diagnosis is clinical - waiting for laboratory confirmation is misguided; a positive wound culture does not confirm and a protective antitetanus IgG (>0.1 IU/mL) argues against the diagnosis.","Treatment should not be delayed once tetanus is clinically diagnosed.","Assuming natural infection confers immunity: tetanus toxin is poorly immunogenic, so survivors still require a full primary immunization course.","Overlooking absence of a wound: about 20-30% of cases have no identifiable entry site, so a negative wound history does not exclude tetanus.","Failure to identify and debride the entry wound (to remove anaerobic foci) can lead to recurrent or prolonged disease.","Long-term ICU care with deep sedation predisposes to hospital-acquired infections (ventilator-associated pneumonia, bloodstream and urinary tract infection, sepsis), venous thromboembolism, and other complications - prognosis depends on controlling these."],"severityClassification":"Ablett classification of tetanus severity: Grade I (mild) - mild trismus, general spasticity, no respiratory compromise, no spasms, no dysphagia; Grade II (moderate) - moderate trismus and rigidity, short spasms, mild dysphagia, moderate respiratory involvement, respiratory rate >30/min; Grade III (severe) - severe trismus, generalized rigidity, prolonged spasms, severe dysphagia, apneic spells, pulse >120/min, respiratory rate >40/min; Grade IV (very severe) - Grade III features plus autonomic dysfunction. Prognostic scoring is provided by the Tetanus Severity Score (TSS), which is superior to the Dakar and Philips scores.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1230-1233","Harrison 22e p.1233"],"crossLinks":[]},"tetralogy_of_fallot":{"name":"Tetralogy of Fallot","system":"Paediatric cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TOF","ToF"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common cyanotic congenital heart lesion beyond infancy, comprising four features: a large ventricular septal defect, an overriding aorta, right ventricular outflow tract obstruction, and right ventricular hypertrophy.","The degree of cyanosis reflects the severity of right ventricular outflow obstruction; some infants are minimally cyanotic (so-called pink Fallot) while others present with marked desaturation.","A harsh systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border arises from the outflow obstruction, not from the septal defect itself; the murmur softens during a hypercyanotic spell as flow across the outflow tract falls.","Hypercyanotic (tet) spells are episodes of sudden, worsening cyanosis with agitation and deep breathing, often on waking or after feeding or crying, and can be life-threatening.","Calming manoeuvres for a spell aim to increase systemic vascular resistance and outflow through the lungs: comforting the child, bringing the knees to the chest, and giving oxygen, with pharmacological support as needed.","The chest radiograph classically shows a boot-shaped heart with a concave main pulmonary artery segment and normal or reduced pulmonary vascularity.","Echocardiography confirms the anatomy and defines the outflow tract, pulmonary arteries, and coronary artery course before surgery.","Older, unrepaired children may adopt squatting to relieve exertional cyanosis by transiently raising systemic resistance and pulmonary blood flow.","Complete surgical repair, involving closure of the septal defect and relief of outflow obstruction, is the definitive treatment and is usually performed in infancy."],"pathophysiology":"Anterior and cephalad deviation of the infundibular septum during development produces right ventricular outflow tract obstruction, a malaligned ventricular septal defect, and an aorta that overrides the defect. Obstruction to pulmonary outflow, combined with the large communication between the ventricles, allows deoxygenated right ventricular blood to shunt right-to-left into the aorta, producing systemic desaturation. The volume of pulmonary blood flow and the resulting cyanosis depend dynamically on the balance between the fixed and dynamic outflow obstruction and systemic vascular resistance; a fall in systemic resistance or a spasm of the infundibulum increases right-to-left shunting and precipitates a hypercyanotic spell.","additionalDifferentials":["Transposition of the great arteries","Tricuspid atresia","Pulmonary atresia with ventricular septal defect","Truncus arteriosus","Severe isolated pulmonary stenosis","Double-outlet right ventricle"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography to define the outflow tract, septal defect, aortic override, and pulmonary artery anatomy","Chest radiograph looking for a boot-shaped heart and pulmonary vascularity","Electrocardiogram showing right axis deviation and right ventricular hypertrophy","Pulse oximetry and, in spells, assessment of acidosis","Cardiac catheterisation or CT/MR angiography when coronary or pulmonary artery anatomy is unclear","Genetic evaluation for 22q11.2 deletion when associated features are present"],"redFlags":["Hypercyanotic (tet) spell with deepening cyanosis, agitation, and a disappearing murmur","Loss of consciousness, seizures, or limpness during a spell","Duct-dependent presentation with severe outflow obstruction and cardiovascular collapse as the ductus closes","Profound or worsening hypoxaemia unresponsive to calming measures","Metabolic acidosis indicating inadequate pulmonary blood flow"],"prognosis":"With timely complete surgical repair the long-term outlook is generally good, though survivors need lifelong cardiology follow-up for pulmonary regurgitation, residual outflow obstruction, arrhythmia, and possible need for pulmonary valve replacement. Untreated disease carries high morbidity and mortality from progressive cyanosis, spells, and complications.","pitfalls":["Interpreting a softening or absent murmur reassuringly when it actually signals a severe hypercyanotic spell","Aggressively sedating or agitating an infant in a spell rather than using calming, resistance-raising manoeuvres","Overlooking a duct-dependent lesion in a neonate whose cyanosis worsens as the ductus arteriosus closes","Attributing exertional symptoms or squatting in an older child to behaviour rather than underlying cyanotic heart disease","Failing to screen for associated 22q11.2 deletion and other anomalies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"the_bradyarrhythmias_disorders_of_the":{"name":"The Bradyarrhythmias: Disorders of the Atrioventricular Node","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["AV block","Atrioventricular block","AV conduction block","Heart block","Bradyarrhythmia due to AV node disease"],"chapter":252,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1924","clinicalPearls":["The AV node is specialized for slow conduction, creating a delay between atrial and ventricular activation and acting as the electrical gatekeeper to the ventricle; its intrinsic backup pacemaker (escape) rate is roughly 20-60 beats/min, normally overridden by the faster sinus node (60-100 beats/min).","First-degree AV block means every atrial impulse still conducts but with an abnormally prolonged PR interval (>200 ms); the term block is a misnomer since conduction is delayed, not interrupted.","Mobitz I (Wenckebach) second-degree block shows progressive PR lengthening before a dropped beat with grouped beating and progressive RR shortening; it is typically within the AV node, hemodynamically stable, and usually does not warrant pacing when asymptomatic.","Mobitz II second-degree block shows a fixed PR interval before a dropped beat, is usually infranodal with a wider QRS, carries a risk of sudden death from an unreliable escape rhythm, and warrants permanent pacing even without symptoms.","In 2:1 AV block the ECG cannot reliably distinguish type I from type II; a PR <160 ms with a wide QRS before the conducted beat favors infranodal (type II) block.","QRS width of the escape rhythm localizes the block: a narrow QRS escape implies block in the AV node or proximal His with a faster, more stable junctional rhythm, whereas a wide QRS escape (without preexisting bundle branch block) implies distal His or bundle branch block and a slower, less reliable rhythm.","Vagal maneuvers and carotid sinus massage slow AV nodal conduction but may paradoxically improve infranodal conduction, while atropine, isoproterenol, and exercise improve AV nodal conduction but may worsen infranodal conduction - useful for localizing the level of block.","On the His bundle electrogram, the AH interval (AV nodal conduction) is normally <130 ms and the HV interval (His-Purkinje conduction) is normally <=55 ms; a markedly prolonged HV interval predicts progression to higher-grade block.","Functional causes of AV block (autonomic, metabolic/endocrine, drug-related) tend to be reversible, whereas most structural causes producing fibrosis are permanent.","Congenital complete heart block with a narrow QRS typically increases heart rate with exercise, whereas acquired complete heart block (especially wide QRS) does not respond to exercise.","Acquired persistent AV conduction failure is rare in healthy adults (estimated incidence about 1 per 5000 per year in the U.S.), but becomes much more common with ischemia, aging/fibrosis, or infiltrative disease.","Idiopathic progressive fibrosis/sclerosis of the conduction system is the most common acquired cause, accounting for roughly 50% of AV block; it often begins in the fourth decade and is accelerated by atherosclerosis, hypertension, and diabetes."],"pathophysiology":"AV nodal myocytes sit at a relatively high resting membrane potential (~-60 mV), exhibit slow phase-0 upstrokes mediated by L-type calcium current (with a relative lack of fast sodium current), and show spontaneous phase-4 depolarization, conferring backup automaticity. Weak electrical coupling from sparse gap junctions (predominantly connexin-40) plus increased extracellular volume slows conduction and protects the ventricle. The AV junction comprises a transitional (atrionodal) cell zone showing decremental conduction, the compact node, and the penetrating His bundle; conduction continues through the His, right and left bundle branches, and the fast-conducting Purkinje network (1-3 m/s). Bradycardia and block arise when conduction across this axis is compromised by fibrosis/sclerosis, calcification (Lev, Lenegre disease), ischemia/infarction, infiltrative or inflammatory disease, drugs, heightened vagal tone, or congenital/genetic abnormalities; consequences range from fatigue and syncope to asystolic death if no reliable escape emerges. Functional (autonomic/metabolic/drug) causes are often reversible while structural fibrosis is typically permanent.","additionalDifferentials":["Sinus node dysfunction / sinus bradycardia (sick sinus syndrome) as a competing cause of symptomatic bradycardia","Drug effect (beta blockers, verapamil, diltiazem, digoxin) producing reversible AV conduction slowing","Heightened vagal tone (sleep, well-conditioned athletes, carotid sinus hypersensitivity, vasovagal/cough/micturition syncope)","Acute myocardial ischemia/infarction-related conduction block","Infiltrative cardiomyopathy (sarcoidosis, amyloidosis, hemochromatosis)","Congenital complete heart block (e.g., maternal lupus)"],"infectionMimics":["Lyme carditis (Borrelia burgdorferi) - a common infectious cause of AV block, typically AV-nodal with narrow junctional escape >40 beats/min, usually improving within about a week of antibiotic therapy","Infective endocarditis with perivalvular abscess - AV block here should prompt urgent surgical consultation","Viral myocarditis","Chagas disease","Toxoplasmosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Senile degenerative fibrosis/calcification of the conduction system (Lev disease)","Lenegre disease (sclerodegenerative distal bundle branch disease in younger patients)","Aortic (and less commonly mitral) valve annular calcification encroaching on the conduction system","Cardiac sarcoidosis and amyloidosis","Autoimmune/rheumatologic disease (SLE, rheumatoid arthritis, mixed connective tissue disease, scleroderma)","Neuromuscular disease (myotonic dystrophy, Kearns-Sayre syndrome, muscular dystrophies)","Iatrogenic injury (valve/cardiac surgery, TAVR, septal myectomy/alcohol septal ablation, catheter ablation)","Hereditary progressive familial heart block (e.g., SCN5A mutations) and lamin A/C cardiomyopathy"],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG to classify the degree and pattern of block and assess QRS width/escape rhythm","Transthoracic echocardiography to assess for structural/valvular disease, ejection fraction, and wall motion","Advanced imaging (cardiac MRI, CT, FDG-PET, technetium pyrophosphate scanning) for infiltrative disease, especially when block develops at age <=60 years","Ambulatory/continuous ECG monitoring to correlate symptoms with bradyarrhythmia","Provocative testing - vagal maneuvers, carotid sinus massage, exercise, atropine, isoproterenol - to localize the level of block","Invasive electrophysiologic study with His bundle electrogram (AH and HV interval measurement) in syncope with suspected high-grade or infranodal block","Evaluation for ischemia when clinical suspicion is present"],"redFlags":["Wide-complex escape rhythm at a slow rate (e.g., ~30 beats/min) implying infranodal block and an unreliable escape","Mobitz II second-degree or complete (third-degree) heart block","Syncope or presyncope attributable to bradycardia","Markedly prolonged HV interval (>100 ms carries an annual incidence of complete AV block approaching 10%)","High-grade AV block complicating anterior MI (distal block, wide unstable escape, high mortality)","New AV block with infective endocarditis suggesting perivalvular abscess","Alternating bundle branch block or bifascicular/trifascicular block with intermittent third-degree block"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends heavily on the level of block. AV-nodal (proximal) block - including Mobitz I and most inferior-MI-related block - tends to have a faster, stable narrow-complex escape and is often reversible with a favorable outcome. Infranodal (distal His-Purkinje) block, Mobitz II, and high-grade block in anterior MI carry unstable wide-complex escape rhythms, higher mortality, and a risk of sudden asystolic death, so pacing is indicated even when asymptomatic. Functional/reversible causes generally resolve; structural fibrotic disease is usually permanent and progressive.","pitfalls":["Mislabeling 2:1 AV block as type I when it may be infranodal type II - the surface ECG cannot reliably distinguish them","Assuming all AV block is benign vagal phenomenon and missing serious infranodal disease that warrants pacing despite absent symptoms","Overlooking reversible/treatable causes (offending drugs, electrolyte derangement, ischemia, hypothyroidism, sleep apnea, Lyme carditis) before committing to permanent therapy","Failing to pursue advanced imaging for infiltrative disease (e.g., sarcoidosis) when AV block presents at a younger age (<=60 years)","Misinterpreting Mobitz I at very short paced cycle lengths as pathologic - it can be a normal physiologic response; it is abnormal at cycle lengths >500 ms (<120 beats/min) without high vagal tone","Not recognizing that a new perivalvular abscess in endocarditis can present as AV block requiring urgent surgery"],"severityClassification":"Electrocardiographic grading: First-degree AV block - all atrial impulses conduct but PR is prolonged (>200 ms), delay usually within the AV node. Second-degree AV block - intermittent failure of AV conduction; Type I/Mobitz I/Wenckebach (progressive PR prolongation until a dropped beat, usually AV-nodal) and Type II/Mobitz II (fixed PR before a dropped beat, usually infranodal, often with wide QRS). Third-degree (complete) AV block - complete AV dissociation with a ventricular (escape) rate slower than the atrial rate.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1924","Harrison 22e p.1925","Harrison 22e p.1926","Harrison 22e p.1927","Harrison 22e p.1928","Harrison 22e p.1929","Harrison 22e p.1930"],"crossLinks":[]},"the_hyperbilirubinemias":{"name":"The Hyperbilirubinemias","system":"Hepatobiliary / Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Gilbert syndrome","Crigler-Najjar syndrome","Dubin-Johnson syndrome","Rotor syndrome","unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia","conjugated hyperbilirubinemia","inherited hyperbilirubinemias"],"chapter":349,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2637","clinicalPearls":["Bilirubin is the terminal product of heme catabolism, with roughly 70-90% derived from breakdown of hemoglobin in senescent red cells; hepatic handling involves four linked steps: carrier-mediated uptake, intracellular binding to glutathione-S-transferases (ligandins), conjugation by UGT1A1, and canalicular excretion (Harrison 22e p.2637-2638).","Conjugation by UGT1A1 adds one or two glucuronic acid moieties, disrupting internal hydrogen bonding so the resulting glucuronides become water-soluble; conjugation is obligatory for biliary excretion (Harrison 22e p.2638).","Unconjugated bilirubin is too tightly albumin-bound to be filtered and does not appear in urine, whereas conjugated (water-soluble) bilirubin is readily filtered, so bilirubinuria indicates a conjugated (direct) hyperbilirubinemia; the kidney acts as an overflow valve so renal failure can amplify jaundice in conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (Harrison 22e p.2638).","Isolated hemolysis in an otherwise healthy person produces a purely unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia with direct fraction <=15% of total; because marrow erythropoiesis can rise only about eightfold, hemolysis alone rarely sustains a bilirubin above ~68 micromol/L (4 mg/dL), so higher values imply concomitant hepatic dysfunction (Harrison 22e p.2638-2639).","Gilbert syndrome is a common, benign cause of mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (usually <51 micromol/L, <3 mg/dL) with normal liver tests and histology; levels rise with fasting, stress, fatigue, alcohol, and intercurrent illness and fall with caloric intake or enzyme inducers (Harrison 22e p.2640).","Crigler-Najjar type I shows extreme lifelong unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (~340-765 micromol/L, 20-45 mg/dL) with essentially absent UGT1A1 activity, no response to phenobarbital, and high kernicterus risk; type II has lower levels, detectable enzyme, pigmented bile, and a >25% bilirubin fall with phenobarbital (Harrison 22e p.2639-2640).","Dubin-Johnson syndrome is a benign, autosomal recessive predominantly conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (typically 34-85 micromol/L) caused by mutation of the canalicular transporter MRP2 (ABCC2); the liver is grossly black from lysosomal pigment and urinary coproporphyrin is >=80% isomer I despite a normal total (Harrison 22e p.2640-2641).","Rotor syndrome resembles Dubin-Johnson clinically (conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, bilirubinuria) but the liver is normal in color, the gallbladder is visualized on oral cholecystography, and total urinary coproporphyrin is markedly increased with isomer I usually <70%; it results from combined deficiency of OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 (Harrison 22e p.2641-2642).","Most neonates develop mild physiologic unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia between days 2 and 5 (peak typically <85-170 micromol/L, 5-10 mg/dL) due to low UGT1A1 and enterohepatic recycling, resolving within about 2 weeks (Harrison 22e p.2639).","A rapidly rising neonatal unconjugated bilirubin, or absolute levels above ~340 micromol/L (20 mg/dL), risks bilirubin encephalopathy (kernicterus) as pigment crosses an immature blood-brain barrier and deposits in the basal ganglia (Harrison 22e p.2639).","Acquired liver disease (acute hepatitis, common bile duct stone) generally produces a mixed conjugated and unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, and serum levels or fractions cannot reliably distinguish intrahepatic from extrahepatic obstruction (Harrison 22e p.2640).","Gilbert syndrome impairs glucuronidation of the irinotecan (CPT-11) active metabolite SN-38, predisposing to severe diarrhea and myelosuppression; the HIV protease inhibitors indinavir and atazanavir inhibit UGT1A1 and worsen hyperbilirubinemia, most markedly in preexisting Gilbert syndrome (Harrison 22e p.2640)."],"pathophysiology":"Bilirubin, the end product of heme degradation (chiefly from senescent erythrocyte hemoglobin), circulates tightly bound to albumin and is processed by hepatocytes in four steps: carrier-mediated uptake, intracellular binding to glutathione-S-transferases, conjugation with glucuronic acid by UGT1A1 (the physiologically critical isoform encoded by exon A1 plus shared exons 2-5 of the UGT1 gene complex on chromosome 2), and ATP-dependent canalicular excretion of mono- and diglucuronides via MRP2. A portion of glucuronides is also exported into the portal circulation by MRP3 and recaptured by sinusoidal OATP1B1/OATP1B3. Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia arises from overproduction (hemolysis, ineffective erythropoiesis, resorption of large hematomas/infarcts), decreased hepatic uptake (drugs such as rifampin, novobiocin; contributory in Gilbert syndrome), or impaired conjugation (neonatal immaturity, breast-milk jaundice, drug inhibition of UGT1A1, and hereditary UGT1A1 defects of Crigler-Najjar I/II and Gilbert syndrome). Gilbert syndrome most often reflects a homozygous extra TA insertion (A[TA]7TAA rather than A[TA]6TAA) in the UGT1A1 promoter reducing enzyme expression; Crigler-Najjar reflects coding/structural UGT1 mutations causing absent (type I) or markedly reduced (type II) activity. Predominantly conjugated familial hyperbilirubinemias result from defective canalicular excretion: Dubin-Johnson from MRP2 (ABCC2) mutation and Rotor from combined OATP1B1/OATP1B3 deficiency impairing reuptake of refluxed conjugated bilirubin.","additionalDifferentials":["Hemolytic anemias (hereditary spherocytosis, G6PD deficiency, beta-thalassemia) causing unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia","Ineffective erythropoiesis (thalassemia major, megaloblastic anemia, congenital dyserythropoietic anemias, lead poisoning)","Drug-induced unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (rifampin, novobiocin, atazanavir, indinavir, chloramphenicol, gentamicin)","Physiologic, breast-milk, and Lucey-Driscoll neonatal jaundice","Acquired hepatocellular or cholestatic liver disease (acute viral hepatitis, common bile duct stone) with mixed hyperbilirubinemia","Benign recurrent intrahepatic cholestasis (BRIC) and progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis (PFIC types 1-3)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute viral hepatitis, which the first cholestatic episodes of benign recurrent intrahepatic cholestasis may mimic"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Extrahepatic biliary obstruction (e.g., choledocholithiasis) presenting with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia","Intrahepatic cholestatic injury","Resorbing large hematoma or massive tissue infarction causing transient unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fractionation of serum bilirubin into conjugated (direct) and unconjugated (indirect) components","Urinalysis for bilirubinuria (present in conjugated, absent in unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia)","Standard liver biochemistries (aminotransferases, alkaline phosphatase, albumin, prothrombin time) and hemolysis workup (reticulocytes, haptoglobin)","Urinary coproporphyrin isomer analysis to distinguish Dubin-Johnson (>=80% isomer I, normal total) from Rotor (markedly increased total, isomer I usually <70%)","Phenobarbital challenge to separate Crigler-Najjar type II (>25% fall) from type I (no response)","UGT1A1 genotyping (e.g., A[TA]7TAA promoter for Gilbert syndrome); oral cholecystography (gallbladder non-visualized in Dubin-Johnson, visualized in Rotor)"],"redFlags":["Rapidly rising neonatal unconjugated bilirubin or absolute level above ~340 micromol/L (20 mg/dL) signaling risk of kernicterus","Sustained unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia above ~68 micromol/L (4 mg/dL) attributed to hemolysis, implying additional hepatic dysfunction","Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with bilirubinuria, prompting evaluation for hepatocellular or obstructive disease","Intercurrent febrile illness in Crigler-Najjar that can precipitate late-onset bilirubin encephalopathy","Prolonged, debilitating cholestatic attacks in BRIC and progression of PFIC to end-stage liver disease"],"prognosis":"Gilbert, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes are benign with normal life expectancy and no progression to chronic liver disease. Crigler-Najjar type I carries a grave prognosis: before phototherapy most patients died of kernicterus in infancy or early childhood, and even survivors developed bilirubin-induced neurologic dysfunction, with early liver transplantation the best means to prevent brain injury and death. Crigler-Najjar type II is far less often complicated by kernicterus and is generally compatible with normal life, though intercurrent illness, fasting, or low albumin can precipitate dangerous bilirubin elevations. BRIC is benign and does not cause cirrhosis, whereas PFIC progresses to end-stage liver disease in childhood.","pitfalls":["Assuming hemolysis alone explains a markedly elevated bilirubin; sustained levels above ~68 micromol/L (4 mg/dL) imply coexisting hepatic dysfunction","Using serum bilirubin levels or conjugated/unconjugated proportions to differentiate intrahepatic from extrahepatic causes of jaundice, which is not reliable","Overdiagnosing Gilbert syndrome from a single value, since levels fluctuate widely and at least 25% of patients show transiently normal bilirubin on follow-up","Male predominance in Gilbert syndrome may be artifactual, reflecting use of male-derived reference ranges","Mistaking the first episodes of benign recurrent intrahepatic cholestasis for acute viral hepatitis","Prescribing drugs cleared by glucuronidation (notably irinotecan) without caution in Gilbert syndrome given risk of severe toxicity"],"severityClassification":"Inherited unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia forms a severity continuum by residual UGT1A1 activity: Crigler-Najjar type I (activity absent; bilirubin ~340-765 micromol/L, 20-45 mg/dL; no phenobarbital response; usual kernicterus), Crigler-Najjar type II (reduced activity, typically <=10% of normal; bilirubin ~100-430 micromol/L, 6-25 mg/dL; >25% phenobarbital response; rare kernicterus), and Gilbert syndrome (activity ~10-35% of normal; bilirubin usually <=70 micromol/L, 4 mg/dL absent fasting/hemolysis; normal histology). Familial conjugated hyperbilirubinemias of canalicular function (Dubin-Johnson, Rotor) are benign, distinguished from the progressive cholestatic disorders BRIC (episodic, non-progressive) and PFIC (progressive to end-stage liver disease).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2637","Harrison 22e p.2638","Harrison 22e p.2639","Harrison 22e p.2640","Harrison 22e p.2641","Harrison 22e p.2642"],"crossLinks":[]},"the_metabolic_syndrome":{"name":"The Metabolic Syndrome","system":"Endocrine/Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Syndrome X","Insulin resistance syndrome","MetS"],"chapter":420,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3252","clinicalPearls":["Metabolic syndrome is a clustering of metabolic abnormalities (central obesity, hypertriglyceridemia, low HDL cholesterol, hyperglycemia, and hypertension) that together raise the risk of cardiovascular disease and diabetes.","Diagnosis under NCEP:ATPIII (2001) requires three or more of: waist circumference >102 cm in men or >88 cm in women; triglycerides >=150 mg/dL; HDL <40 mg/dL (men) or <50 mg/dL (women); BP >=130/85 mmHg; fasting glucose >=100 mg/dL (or specific medication for, or prior diagnosis of, the relevant condition).","The Harmonizing definition uses ethnicity-specific waist circumference cutoffs (e.g., for South Asian and Chinese men >=90 cm, Europid men >=94 cm, Japanese men >=85 cm), reflecting that risk at a given waist circumference differs between populations.","Visceral (intra-abdominal) adipose tissue is more strongly linked to insulin resistance, diabetes, and CVD than subcutaneous fat, but waist circumference alone cannot reliably distinguish the two depots; DEXA, CT, or MRI is needed for that distinction.","Normal-weight individuals can still be insulin-resistant and meet criteria for metabolic syndrome, a phenotype that is particularly evident in populations from India, Southeast Asia, and Central America.","The syndrome is typically asymptomatic; physical examination often shows only elevated waist circumference and blood pressure, which should trigger a search for the other biochemical components.","Lipoatrophy and acanthosis nigricans, when present, signal severe insulin resistance, though they are far less common than the standard components.","Hypertriglyceridemia is an excellent marker of the insulin-resistant state; fasting triglycerides above roughly 180 mg/dL (~2.0 mM) are typically accompanied by a predominance of small, dense, more atherogenic LDL particles.","Most patients (~75%) with type 2 diabetes or impaired glucose tolerance have metabolic syndrome, and its presence in these groups marks a higher CVD burden than in those without the syndrome.","Conditions strongly associated with insulin resistance and the syndrome include metabolic-associated fatty liver disease (MAFLD/MASH), polycystic ovary syndrome, and obstructive sleep apnea, which should be screened for during evaluation.","Obstructive sleep apnea can predict metabolic syndrome even without excess adiposity, and insulin resistance tends to be more severe in apnea patients than in weight-matched controls.","Hyperuricemia reflects insulin-resistance-related defects in renal tubular handling of uric acid and may itself contribute to hypertension via effects on endothelial nitric oxide synthase and activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system."],"pathophysiology":"Insulin resistance is the most widely accepted unifying mechanism. Early insulin resistance manifests as postprandial then fasting hyperinsulinemia, eventually progressing to hyperglycemia as compensatory beta-cell secretion fails. An overabundance of circulating free fatty acids (FFAs), released from expanded adipose tissue and from lipoprotein-lipase-mediated lipolysis, drives the process: FFAs impair insulin-mediated glucose uptake in muscle, promote intramuscular and cardiac triglyceride accumulation, and increase hepatic glucose and triglyceride production with VLDL secretion. Insulin normally suppresses lipolysis (its most sensitive action), so resistance amplifies FFA release in a self-perpetuating loop. Increased VLDL/apoB and elevated apoC-III inhibit lipoprotein lipase, raising triglyceride-rich remnants; HDL becomes small, dense, and cleared faster, lowering HDL cholesterol. Loss of insulin-mediated vasodilation (nitric oxide) with preserved renal sodium reabsorption and sympathetic activation, plus adipose angiotensinogen expression, contributes to hypertension. Contributing/associated mechanisms include leptin resistance, reduced anti-inflammatory adiponectin, increased proinflammatory cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, IL-18, TNF-alpha, resistin) from adipose-derived macrophages, a prothrombotic state (fibrinogen, PAI-1), oxidative stress with mitochondrial dysfunction, and alterations in the gut microbiome (increased Firmicutes/Bacteroidetes ratio).","additionalDifferentials":["Type 2 diabetes mellitus / impaired glucose tolerance","Genetic lipodystrophies (e.g., Berardinelli-Seip congenital, Dunnigan familial partial lipodystrophy)","Acquired lipodystrophy (e.g., HIV-related and antiretroviral-associated)","Polycystic ovary syndrome","Cushing's syndrome / other endocrine causes of central obesity and hypertension","Primary dyslipidemias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lipodystrophy syndromes (severe insulin resistance with many overlapping components)","Cushing's syndrome (central adiposity, hypertension, hyperglycemia)","Polycystic ovary syndrome with insulin resistance"],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting lipid panel (triglycerides, HDL cholesterol) and fasting glucose are required for diagnosis","Waist circumference measurement and blood pressure","Optional insulin-resistance biomarkers individualized to the patient: apoB, hsCRP, fibrinogen, uric acid, urinary albumin/creatinine ratio, liver function tests","Sleep study if symptoms of obstructive sleep apnea are present","If PCOS suspected (clinical features and anovulation): testosterone, luteinizing hormone, follicle-stimulating hormone","MAFLD assessment via FIB-4 fibrosis score or elastography","DEXA, CT, or MRI if discrimination of visceral versus subcutaneous fat is needed"],"redFlags":["Acanthosis nigricans or lipoatrophy indicating severe insulin resistance","Established type 2 diabetes or impaired glucose tolerance (markedly elevated diabetes and CVD risk)","Coexisting metabolic syndrome and elevated hsCRP (associated with increased all-cause mortality)","Features suggesting MASH/advanced liver fibrosis (precursor of cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma)","Symptoms of obstructive sleep apnea"],"prognosis":"Metabolic syndrome roughly doubles the risk of cardiovascular death and confers about a threefold higher risk of acute myocardial infarction or stroke; the risk for type 2 diabetes is increased three- to fivefold (fasting glucose explaining most of this). In the REGARDS cohort, metabolic syndrome carried a relative risk of about 1.34 for all-cause mortality, with the relationship to hsCRP and mortality stronger in white than in black participants. Prevalence among patients with coronary heart disease may reach up to 60% in those over 75. Risk and prevalence are modifiable: cardiac rehabilitation and lifestyle change (improved nutrition, physical activity, weight reduction) can reduce the prevalence of the syndrome.","pitfalls":["Waist circumference does not reliably separate subcutaneous from visceral fat, so it can misestimate true metabolic risk without imaging.","A single ethnicity-blind waist cutoff under- or over-estimates risk across populations; ethnicity-specific thresholds are needed.","Normal body weight does not exclude the syndrome, so insulin resistance can be missed in lean individuals.","Whether metabolic syndrome adds CVD or diabetes risk beyond the simple sum of its individual components remains debated, so it should not be over-interpreted as an independent diagnosis.","Fasting insulin levels or HOMA capture only part of insulin resistance, so they inadequately quantify the contribution of resistance to hypertension.","Obstructive sleep apnea and PCOS are commonly overlooked associated conditions if not actively screened for."],"severityClassification":"There is no graded severity scale; the syndrome is defined categorically by meeting three or more of the five diagnostic components (NCEP:ATPIII 2001 or the ethnicity-adjusted Harmonizing definition, Table 420-1). Risk rises with the number of components present and with associated markers such as elevated hsCRP.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3252","Harrison 22e p.3253","Harrison 22e p.3254","Harrison 22e p.3255","Harrison 22e p.3256"],"crossLinks":[]},"the_vasculitis_syndromes":{"name":"The Vasculitis Syndromes","system":"Multisystem / Rheumatologic (Immune-Mediated Vascular)","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Vasculitis","Systemic vasculitis","ANCA-associated vasculitis","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Microscopic polyangiitis","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Churg-Strauss)","Polyarteritis nodosa","Giant cell (temporal) arteritis","Takayasu arteritis","IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein)","Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis","Polymyalgia rheumatica"],"chapter":375,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2893","clinicalPearls":["Vasculitis is inflammation of and damage to blood vessel walls that typically narrows the lumen and produces downstream tissue ischemia; it may be the sole disease, may be secondary to another disease, and may be single-organ or multisystem.","Suspect vasculitis in any unexplained systemic illness; clinical clues include palpable purpura, pulmonary infiltrates with microscopic hematuria, chronic sinusitis, mononeuritis multiplex, unexplained ischemic events, and glomerulonephritis with multisystem disease.","Definitive diagnosis usually rests on biopsy of clinically involved tissue; 'blind' biopsy of organs without subjective or objective involvement has very low yield and should be avoided. Catheter-directed arteriography is used when PAN, Takayasu, or primary CNS vasculitis is suspected.","ANCA subtypes: cANCA targets proteinase-3 (>90% positive in active granulomatosis with polyangiitis); pANCA most relevantly targets myeloperoxidase (variably positive in microscopic polyangiitis, EGPA, and isolated necrotizing crescentic glomerulonephritis). Only anti-MPO and anti-PR3 are convincingly vasculitis-associated.","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis classically combines granulomatous vasculitis of the upper and lower respiratory tracts with glomerulonephritis; upper airway involvement occurs in ~95% and renal disease (~77% over course) dominates prognosis. Renal lesion is pauci-immune (no immune-complex deposition).","Microscopic polyangiitis resembles GPA but lacks granulomatous inflammation; it favors small vessels (capillaries, venules, arterioles), with glomerulonephritis in at least 79% and alveolar hemorrhage in ~12%. Upper airway disease and lung nodules are atypical and, if present, point instead to GPA. ANCA (usually anti-MPO) in ~75%.","EGPA (Churg-Strauss) is defined by asthma, blood and tissue eosinophilia, and vasculitis; eosinophilia exceeds 1000 cells/uL in >80% and ANCA (usually anti-MPO) is present in ~48%. Cardiac involvement is the leading cause of death, so echocardiography is advised at diagnosis.","Polyarteritis nodosa is a necrotizing vasculitis of small- and medium-sized muscular arteries that spares pulmonary arteries and does NOT cause glomerulonephritis, granuloma, or significant eosinophilia; renal involvement is arteritis (microaneurysms, hypertension), not glomerular. ANCA are rarely found; screen all patients for hepatitis B and C.","Giant cell arteritis occurs almost exclusively after age 50, presents with fever, anemia, high ESR/CRP, and headache; ischemic optic neuropathy with sudden blindness is the dreaded complication, and patients carry an ~18-fold increased risk of thoracic aortic aneurysm. Treatment should not be delayed for biopsy.","Polymyalgia rheumatica (aching/stiffness of neck, shoulder and hip girdles with raised ESR/CRP) coexists with GCA in 40-50% of GCA patients; creatine kinase is normal and RF/anti-CCP should be negative. ~10-20% with isolated PMR later develop GCA.","Takayasu arteritis is a large-vessel panarteritis with strong predilection for the aortic arch and its branches, classically in adolescent girls and young women; suspect it in a young woman with diminished or absent pulses, an interarm blood-pressure discrepancy, or arterial bruits.","IgA vasculitis (Henoch-Schonlein) is a small-vessel, immune-complex (IgA) vasculitis most common in children 4-7 years, with palpable purpura over buttocks/lower limbs, arthralgias, colicky GI symptoms (intussusception risk), and usually mild self-limited glomerulonephritis; serum complement is normal and IgA is elevated in about half.","Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis is most often associated with hepatitis C (estimated in ~5% of chronic HCV); rheumatoid factor is almost always present, hypocomplementemia occurs in ~90%, and membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis accounts for ~80% of renal lesions.","Temporal artery biopsy yield improves with a 3-5 cm segment and serial sectioning because involvement is segmental; biopsy may still show arteritis after about 14 days of glucocorticoid therapy, so starting treatment promptly does not preclude later diagnosis."],"pathophysiology":"Most vasculitic syndromes are thought to be at least partly immune-mediated responses to antigenic stimuli, with the precise mechanism varying by disease. Three principal hypothesized mechanisms are recognized. (1) Pathogenic immune-complex formation/deposition (e.g., IgA vasculitis, lupus vasculitis, serum-sickness/cutaneous syndromes, HCV-associated cryoglobulinemic vasculitis, HBV-associated vasculitis): antigen-antibody complexes formed in antigen excess deposit in vessel walls whose permeability has been raised by vasoactive amines, activate complement (notably chemotactic C5a), recruit neutrophils that phagocytose complexes and release intracytoplasmic enzymes damaging the wall, with later mononuclear infiltration and luminal compromise. (2) ANCA production (GPA, microscopic polyangiitis, EGPA): when neutrophils/monocytes are primed by TNF-alpha or IL-1 or alternative-complement activation, PR3 and MPO translocate to the cell membrane where ANCA can engage them, triggering degranulation, reactive oxygen species, endothelial injury, and release of IL-1/IL-8; however, some patients have active GPA without ANCA and titers correlate poorly with activity, so a primary pathogenic role remains debated. (3) Pathogenic T-lymphocyte responses and granuloma formation (giant cell arteritis, Takayasu arteritis, GPA, EGPA): cytokine-activated endothelium expresses HLA class II and secretes IL-1, enabling CD4+ T-cell interactions; IL-1 and TNF-alpha upregulate ELAM-1 and VCAM-1 to enhance leukocyte adhesion. In GCA the disease is antigen-driven, with clonally restricted T cells suggesting an arterial-wall antigen, and both TH1 (IFN-gamma) and TH17 (IL-17) CD4 lineages contribute. The size, type, and location of affected vessels, host genetic predisposition, environmental exposures, reticuloendothelial clearance of complexes, hemodynamic factors, and endothelial integrity all influence the ultimate phenotype.","additionalDifferentials":["Other primary vasculitides when the full classic syndrome is incomplete","Anti-glomerular basement membrane (Goodpasture's) disease","Relapsing polychondritis","IgG4-related disease (aortitis/periaortitis mimicking Takayasu)","Connective tissue disease-associated (lupus, rheumatoid, sarcoid) vasculitis","Seronegative rheumatoid arthritis or other inflammatory arthropathy (vs polymyalgia rheumatica)","Lymphomatoid granulomatosis and extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma (vs GPA midline destructive disease)"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial endocarditis","Disseminated gonococcal infection","Pulmonary histoplasmosis","Coccidioidomycosis","Syphilis","Lyme disease","Rocky Mountain spotted fever","Whipple's disease","Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis and rhinoscleroma (midline destructive mimics of GPA)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Antiphospholipid syndrome","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura / thrombotic microangiopathies","Atrial myxoma","Lymphoma and carcinomatosis","Drug toxicity (cocaine, levamisole-adulterated cocaine, amphetamines, ergot alkaloids, methysergide, arsenic)","Sarcoidosis","Atheroembolic disease","Amyloidosis","Migraine","Fibromuscular dysplasia","Heritable connective tissue disorders","Segmental arterial mediolysis","Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome"],"additionalInvestigations":["Tissue biopsy of clinically involved organ (highest yield: lung for GPA; symptomatic skin nodules, painful testes, or nerve/muscle for PAN)","ANCA testing (anti-PR3 / cANCA and anti-MPO / pANCA) with awareness of variable predictive value by presentation","Catheter-directed (dye) arteriography for suspected PAN; MR or CT arteriography of aorta and branches for Takayasu and large-vessel GCA","Temporal artery biopsy (3-5 cm with serial sections) and/or temporal +/- axillary artery ultrasound 'halo' sign for GCA","ESR and/or CRP, complete blood count (anemia, leukocytosis, thrombocytosis, eosinophilia in EGPA)","Urinalysis for hematuria, proteinuria, and red-cell casts; serum creatinine","Hepatitis B and C serologies/RNA (PAN, cryoglobulinemic vasculitis)","Serum cryoglobulins, rheumatoid factor, and complement levels for cryoglobulinemic vasculitis","Skin biopsy with immunofluorescence (IgA and C3 deposition in IgA vasculitis; leukocytoclastic vasculitis in cutaneous vasculitis)","Echocardiography at diagnosis in EGPA given cardiac mortality risk","Consider DADA2 (adenosine deaminase type 2 deficiency) evaluation in early-onset PAN-like vasculitis"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis or rising creatinine (GPA, microscopic polyangiitis, cryoglobulinemic vasculitis)","Hemoptysis / diffuse alveolar hemorrhage (microscopic polyangiitis, GPA)","New or worsening headache, jaw or tongue claudication, scalp tenderness, or any visual symptom in a patient over 50 (impending GCA-related blindness)","Subglottic stenosis or progressive airway obstruction in GPA","Mononeuritis multiplex","Abdominal pain with GI bleeding suggesting bowel infarction/perforation (PAN, EGPA, IgA vasculitis with intussusception)","Cardiac involvement (myocarditis/coronary vasculitis) in EGPA or PAN","Interarm blood-pressure discrepancy, absent pulses, or aortic aneurysm/dissection in large-vessel disease","Concurrent anti-GBM antibody positivity in ANCA-associated vasculitis"],"prognosis":"Outcome varies markedly by syndrome and by treatment. Untreated granulomatosis with polyangiitis was once uniformly fatal within months; modern cyclophosphamide- or rituximab-based regimens with glucocorticoids achieve improvement in >90% and remission in ~75%, with 5-year survival >80%, though 50-70% of remissions are followed by relapse and many patients accrue irreversible damage (renal insufficiency, hearing loss, subglottic stenosis, saddle-nose deformity). Treated microscopic polyangiitis has ~74% 5-year survival with relapse in at least 34%, mortality from alveolar hemorrhage or GI/cardiac/renal disease. Untreated EGPA has a ~25% 5-year survival; with treatment about 72% survive at 78 months, and myocardial involvement causes ~39% of deaths. Untreated PAN carries a 10-20% 5-year survival (death from bowel infarction/perforation and cardiovascular causes); combination immunosuppression markedly improves survival, with relapse in ~10-20%. GCA seldom causes acute death but carries late risk of aortic aneurysm rupture/dissection; PMR generally responds well. Takayasu is usually chronic and relapsing with widely variable reported survival. IgA vasculitis prognosis is excellent (mortality exceedingly rare; 1-5% of children reach end-stage renal disease, possibly higher in adults; recurrences in 10-40%). In cryoglobulinemic vasculitis, glomerulonephritis is a poor prognostic sign (~15% progress to end-stage renal disease, 40% of whom later die of cardiovascular disease, infection, or liver failure).","pitfalls":["Do NOT treat a rise in ANCA titer alone as evidence of relapse; only ~43% of patients relapsed within a year of an ANCA rise, and many remain ANCA-positive for years in remission.","ANCA predictive value falls sharply when renal disease is absent (~30-60% with sinus/lung-only disease vs ~90% with active glomerulonephritis), so biopsy is needed to exclude infection or malignancy that can cause false-positive ANCA.","Upper airway lesions in GPA should NEVER be irradiated; midline destructive disease mimics include NK/T-cell lymphoma and cocaine/levamisole injury (anti-neutrophil-elastase ANCA, granulocytopenia point to levamisole).","Eosinophilia is rare in PAN; when markedly elevated it should redirect toward EGPA.","In PAN, MR and CT arteriography lack the resolution to visualize the small/medium aneurysms, so catheter-directed dye arteriography is required.","When tocilizumab or other IL-6 blockade is used in GCA, ESR and CRP normalize and can no longer be used to gauge disease activity or detect infection.","TMP-SMX should never be used alone to treat active GPA involving organs beyond the sinonasal tissue.","Failure to exclude infection (especially endocarditis) before starting empirical immunosuppression in a rapidly deteriorating patient can be catastrophic.","Glucocorticoids do not reliably benefit the skin or renal disease of IgA vasculitis and do not shorten its course or reduce recurrence."],"severityClassification":"For ANCA-associated vasculitis (GPA/microscopic polyangiitis), Harrison frames management by severe vs nonsevere disease. Severe disease involves life- or organ-threatening manifestations and is judged by both extent and the specific organ affected: pulmonary disease may be nonsevere (nodules without respiratory compromise) or severe (alveolar hemorrhage), whereas active glomerulonephritis is ALWAYS considered severe even when renal function is normal. Treatment is conceptually divided into a remission-induction phase followed by a maintenance phase, with regimen choice stratified by this severity assessment along with relapse history and comorbidities.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2893","Harrison 22e p.2894","Harrison 22e p.2895","Harrison 22e p.2896","Harrison 22e p.2897","Harrison 22e p.2898","Harrison 22e p.2899","Harrison 22e p.2900","Harrison 22e p.2901","Harrison 22e p.2902","Harrison 22e p.2903","Harrison 22e p.2904","Harrison 22e p.2905","Harrison 22e p.2906"],"crossLinks":[]},"thermal_burns":{"name":"Thermal burns","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["burn injury","burns","thermal injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Approach the burned patient as a trauma patient first, following the primary survey, because associated injuries and airway compromise can be immediately lethal","Assess the airway early: facial burns, singed nasal hair, soot in the mouth, hoarseness, and stridor warn of inhalational injury and the need for early intubation before oedema closes the airway","Estimate burn size using validated methods (rule of nines or a palm-based estimate) counting only partial- and full-thickness burns","Burn depth guides prognosis: superficial burns blanch and are painful, whereas full-thickness burns are leathery, insensate, and non-blanching","Large burns require fluid resuscitation titrated to urine output using a body-surface-area and weight-based formula as a starting estimate","Circumferential full-thickness burns of a limb or the chest can cause a compartment or ventilatory crisis requiring escharotomy","Suspect carbon monoxide or cyanide toxicity in enclosed-space fires, since pulse oximetry can read falsely normal","Keep the patient warm and provide adequate analgesia, as burns cause rapid heat and fluid loss and severe pain"],"pathophysiology":"Heat denatures proteins and causes coagulative necrosis of skin, with a central zone of coagulation surrounded by zones of stasis and hyperaemia that can progress to necrosis if perfusion falls. Loss of the skin barrier and a systemic inflammatory response increase capillary permeability, causing large fluid shifts, intravascular volume depletion, and burn shock in extensive injuries. Inhalational injury combines direct thermal damage to the upper airway, chemical injury to the lower airways from combustion products, and systemic asphyxiants such as carbon monoxide and cyanide. Later, the denuded surface predisposes to infection and hypermetabolism.","additionalDifferentials":["Chemical or electrical burns with different management priorities","Scald injury versus non-accidental injury patterns","Toxic epidermal necrolysis or Stevens-Johnson syndrome mimicking a burn","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","Cold injury (frostbite) in the differential of tissue necrosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Carboxyhaemoglobin level and arterial blood gas where inhalation is suspected","Serial urine output monitoring to titrate fluid resuscitation","Baseline bloods including electrolytes, renal function, and haematocrit","Bronchoscopy to assess and confirm inhalational airway injury in selected cases","Serial examination of circumferential burns for compartment compromise"],"redFlags":["Signs of inhalational injury (hoarseness, stridor, soot, facial burns) demanding early airway control before oedema progresses","Extensive burns with evolving hypovolaemic shock requiring aggressive titrated resuscitation","Circumferential full-thickness burns threatening limb perfusion or ventilation and needing escharotomy","Suspected carbon monoxide or cyanide poisoning from an enclosed-space fire","Rapidly rising resuscitation fluid requirements or falling urine output indicating under-resuscitation"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on burn size and depth, age, inhalational injury, and comorbidity. Small partial-thickness burns heal well, whereas large or full-thickness burns and inhalational injury carry higher mortality and long-term morbidity, including scarring and functional loss.","pitfalls":["Delaying airway assessment until oedema makes intubation difficult or impossible","Under- or over-estimating burn area, leading to incorrect fluid resuscitation","Relying on pulse oximetry, which can appear normal in carbon monoxide poisoning","Missing associated trauma by focusing only on the visible burn","Allowing hypothermia during exposure and fluid administration"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"thoracic_aortic_aneurysm":{"name":"Thoracic aortic aneurysm","system":"Vascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TAA","thoracoabdominal aortic aneurysm","TAAA","ascending aortic aneurysm","descending thoracic aortic aneurysm"],"chapter":null,"source":"Rutherford's Vascular Surgery / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most thoracic aortic aneurysms are asymptomatic and discovered incidentally on chest imaging performed for other reasons","Symptoms, when present, reflect local compression and may include chest or back pain, hoarseness, dysphagia, or stridor","Ascending and arch aneurysms are often linked to degeneration of the aortic media, while descending aneurysms are more commonly atherosclerotic","Connective tissue disorders such as Marfan and Loeys-Dietz syndromes, bicuspid aortic valve, and a family history predispose to earlier and more aggressive disease","CT angiography and MR angiography define aneurysm extent, diameter, and involvement of aortic branches for surveillance and operative planning","Repair thresholds are based on maximal diameter, growth rate, symptoms, and underlying aetiology, with lower thresholds for connective tissue disease","Blood pressure control and cardiovascular risk-factor management slow expansion and reduce dissection risk during surveillance","Open surgical and endovascular (thoracic endovascular aortic repair) approaches are selected according to aneurysm location, anatomy, and patient fitness"],"pathophysiology":"Thoracic aortic aneurysm is a permanent localised dilatation of the thoracic aorta resulting from weakening of the aortic wall. Degeneration of medial elastin and smooth muscle, driven by genetic connective tissue disorders, bicuspid valve-associated aortopathy, atherosclerosis, or ageing, reduces wall integrity. According to the law of Laplace, increasing diameter raises wall tension, promoting further expansion in a self-perpetuating cycle. As the wall weakens it becomes vulnerable to acute dissection, in which an intimal tear allows blood to track within the media, and ultimately to rupture.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute aortic dissection","Aortic intramural haematoma or penetrating atherosclerotic ulcer","Mediastinal mass or lymphadenopathy","Aortic tortuosity or pseudoaneurysm","Acute coronary syndrome (for chest pain presentations)","Pulmonary embolism"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["CT angiography of the thoracic aorta for diameter, extent, and branch involvement","MR angiography as a radiation-sparing alternative for surveillance","Transthoracic and transoesophageal echocardiography for the aortic root and valve","Genetic evaluation and family screening when a heritable aortopathy is suspected","Assessment of cardiac and respiratory fitness prior to intervention"],"redFlags":["Sudden severe chest or back pain suggesting acute dissection or impending or actual rupture, a surgical emergency","Haemodynamic instability or syncope indicating rupture or tamponade","New hoarseness, stridor, or dysphagia from rapid enlargement","Rapid interval growth or diameter exceeding operative thresholds"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on aneurysm size, growth rate, and aetiology; small aneurysms often remain stable under surveillance, whereas large or rapidly expanding lesions carry a high risk of dissection or rupture. Elective repair markedly improves survival compared with the very high mortality of rupture, though operative risk varies with aneurysm extent and comorbidity.","pitfalls":["Dismissing incidental thoracic aortic dilatation without arranging appropriate surveillance","Applying general size thresholds to patients with connective tissue disease who warrant earlier repair","Attributing acute chest and back pain solely to cardiac causes and missing dissection","Neglecting family screening in heritable aortopathies"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Rutherford's Vascular Surgery","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"thrombotic_renovascular_disorders":{"name":"Thrombotic Renovascular Disorders","system":"Renal/Genitourinary","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Thrombotic microangiopathy of the kidney","Renal TMA","Renal microvascular thrombotic disorders"],"chapter":329,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2444","clinicalPearls":["Thrombotic microangiopathy (TMA) is a pathologic lesion of endothelial injury in terminal arterioles and capillaries, with platelet and hyaline thrombi causing partial or complete occlusion; it is usually but not always accompanied by microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (MAHA) featuring thrombocytopenia and schistocytes.","Renal histology of TMA shows swollen endocapillary cells (endotheliosis), fibrin thrombi, platelet plugs, arterial intimal fibrosis, and a membranoproliferative glomerular pattern; fibrin thrombi may extend into the arteriolar pole producing glomerular collapse and at times cortical necrosis, and recovered kidneys may develop secondary focal segmental glomerulosclerosis.","The classic TTP pentad (MAHA, thrombocytopenia, neurologic symptoms, fever, renal failure) is rare: fewer than 5% of immune-mediated TTP cases show the full pentad, so do not wait for all five to act.","TTP is defined as MAHA associated with ADAMTS13 activity below 5-10%; ultra-large von Willebrand factor multimers accumulate, form clots, and shear erythrocytes, but loss of ADAMTS13 alone may need an additional trigger (infection, surgery, pancreatitis, pregnancy) to provoke clinical disease.","Atypical (complement-mediated) HUS reflects alternative complement pathway dysregulation; affected patients often have low C3 with normal C4, and factor H deficiency is the most common defect, with more than 70 factor H gene mutations described.","Shiga toxin-producing E. coli (STEC) HUS, classically D+ (diarrhea-associated) HUS, mostly affects children under 5 but can affect adults; bloody diarrhea precedes MAHA within one week in over 80% of cases, fever is typically absent, and neurologic involvement is common, especially in adults.","Scleroderma renal crisis presents with acute kidney injury, abrupt-onset hypertension, and a normal urine sediment (though none is strictly required); it occurs in about 12% of diffuse but only ~2% of limited systemic sclerosis, and its renal histology of 'onion-skinning' arcuate artery intimal/medial proliferation is indistinguishable from malignant hypertension.","Antiphospholipid syndrome produces a predisposition to arterial and venous thrombosis and recurrent fetal loss; 'triple positivity' (lupus anticoagulant, anti-beta-2 glycoprotein I, and anticardiolipin antibodies) carries the highest thrombosis risk, and APLS is the leading cause of stroke in patients under 45.","HELLP syndrome (hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets) is a microvascular pregnancy complication; the defining organ is the liver (subcapsular hematoma with potential rupture), renal failure occurs in about half of patients, and proteinuria is present in 86-100%.","Renal vein thrombosis can present with flank pain, tenderness, hematuria, rapid renal decline, and proteinuria, or be silent and discovered during a pulmonary embolism workup; the left renal vein is more commonly involved and two-thirds of cases are bilateral.","Sickle cell nephropathy arises from occlusion of the vasa recta in the renal medulla, where low oxygen tension and high osmolarity favor hemoglobin S polymerization and sickling; sequelae include hyposthenuria, hematuria, and papillary necrosis (which can also occur in sickle trait).","Drug-induced TMA occurs via two mechanisms: dose-dependent toxic/endothelial damage (chemotherapeutics such as proteasome inhibitors, mitomycin C, gemcitabine; immunosuppressives such as calcineurin inhibitors, interferon, sirolimus) or non-dose-dependent drug-induced autoantibodies (e.g., quinine, which can react even after a single re-exposure)."],"pathophysiology":"Thrombotic renovascular disorders share endothelial injury of the renal microvasculature (terminal arterioles and capillaries) leading to platelet/hyaline thrombi and microangiopathic hemolysis. In TTP, deficient ADAMTS13 activity (below ~5-10%, from congenital mutation in cTTP/Upshaw-Schulman syndrome or acquired autoantibody in iTTP) allows ultra-large von Willebrand factor multimers to accumulate, forming platelet-rich thrombi that shear red cells; an inflammatory trigger is often required to manifest disease. In atypical HUS, dysregulation of the alternative complement pathway (commonly factor H deficiency, or defects in factor I, factor B, CD46/membrane cofactor protein, C3, CFHR1/3/5, thrombomodulin, or autoantibody to factor H in DEAP HUS) causes complement-mediated endothelial damage. STEC HUS results from Shiga toxins (Stx1/Stx2, verotoxins) binding the glycolipid receptor Gb3 on renal microvascular cells, inducing inflammatory cytokines (IL-8, MCP-1, SDF-1) and chemokine receptors (CXCR4/CXCR7) with platelet aggregation; Streptococcus pneumoniae neuraminidase can expose the cryptic Thomsen-Friedenreich antigen so preformed IgM triggers MAHA. Scleroderma renal crisis features arcuate artery intimal/medial proliferation with onion-skinning, fibrinoid necrosis, and thrombosis. Antiphospholipid syndrome causes intralobular fibrous intimal hyperplasia and arterial/arteriolar occlusion. Renal vein thrombosis results from endothelial damage, venous stasis, or hypercoagulability (notably nephrotic syndrome, especially membranous nephropathy).","additionalDifferentials":["Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (congenital cTTP and immune iTTP)","Hemolytic-uremic syndrome (STEC/D+ HUS, pneumococcal neuraminidase-associated, atypical complement-mediated/aHUS, DEAP HUS)","Malignant hypertension","Scleroderma renal crisis","Antiphospholipid syndrome (including catastrophic APLS)","Preeclampsia/HELLP syndrome","Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation-associated TMA","Cancer-associated TMA","Drug-induced TMA","POEMS syndrome","Sickle cell nephropathy","Renal vein thrombosis","COVID-19 (SARS-CoV-2) microvascular disease"],"infectionMimics":["Shiga toxin-producing E. coli (EHEC/STEC) HUS","Streptococcus pneumoniae neuraminidase-associated HUS","HIV-related thrombotic microangiopathy (advanced AIDS, low CD4 count)","COVID-19/SARS-CoV-2 microvascular thrombosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Malignant hypertension (histologically indistinguishable from scleroderma renal crisis)","Preeclampsia/HELLP syndrome","Cancer-associated TMA (tumor microvascular obstruction)","Drug-induced TMA","Acute tubular necrosis (contributes to renal failure in HELLP)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral smear for schistocytes and microangiopathic hemolytic anemia","ADAMTS13 activity assay (markedly reduced, below ~5-10%, in TTP; reduced only 30-60% in HELLP)","Platelet count and serial monitoring","LDH (elevated in hemolysis/TMA), haptoglobin (decreased), bilirubin","Direct and indirect Coombs test (negative in microangiopathic hemolysis)","Complement studies including C3 and C4 (low C3 with normal C4 suggests alternative pathway activation in aHUS); complement-regulatory gene mutation testing","Stool studies for Shiga toxin / E. coli O157:H7 in suspected STEC HUS","Antiphospholipid antibody panel: lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, anti-beta-2 glycoprotein I antibodies","Antinuclear antibody pattern and anti-RNA polymerase I/III, anti-U3-RNP for scleroderma renal crisis","Doppler ultrasonography, CT angiography (nearly 100% sensitive), or MR angiography for renal vein thrombosis","Renal biopsy when diagnosis is uncertain (required for HIV-related TMA; recommended in atypical APLS or scleroderma renal involvement without hypertension)","Urinalysis and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (screening in sickle cell nephropathy)","LDH-to-AST ratio (about 13:1 in HELLP/preeclampsia vs about 29:1 without preeclampsia), antithrombin III (decreased in HELLP not TTP), D-dimer (elevated in HELLP not TTP)"],"redFlags":["Neurologic deterioration (seizures, encephalopathy, cerebral infarction) in HUS/TTP, especially in adults","Untreated immune-mediated TTP carries a mortality exceeding 90%, and 20% die within the first month even with modern therapy","Catastrophic antiphospholipid syndrome: rapid onset, multiorgan failure, and severe thrombotic events","Scleroderma renal crisis with cardiac involvement (myocarditis, pericarditis, arrhythmias) indicating an especially poor prognosis, or with pulmonary edema","HELLP syndrome with subcapsular hepatic hematoma/spontaneous liver rupture, cerebral infarction/hemorrhage/edema, or placental abruption","HSCT-associated TMA, which carries a 75% mortality within 3 months","Bilateral renal vein thrombosis with rapid decline in renal function or life-threatening complications"],"prognosis":"Prognosis varies by subtype. Untreated immune-mediated TTP has a mortality exceeding 90%, and even with modern therapy about 20% die within the first month from microvascular thrombosis. HSCT-associated TMA is highly lethal, with roughly 75% mortality within 3 months. Scleroderma renal crisis historically had over 90% mortality at 1 month, reduced to about 30% at 3 years with renin-angiotensin system blockade; nearly two-thirds of patients may require dialysis, with renal recovery in about half (median ~1 year). HELLP syndrome carries a mortality of about 7.4-34% and usually resolves spontaneously after delivery. POEMS syndrome rarely progresses to end-stage renal disease. Renal injury in aHUS can be severe and progress to end-stage renal disease, with recurrence risk after transplant depending on the complement-regulatory protein involved. Chronic kidney disease develops in roughly 25-33% of sickle cell nephropathy patients, and nephrotic-range proteinuria predicts progression to renal failure.","pitfalls":["Waiting for the full TTP pentad before acting is a mistake, since fewer than 5% of immune-mediated TTP cases display all five features.","Assuming TMA always shows MAHA can mislead: TMA is usually but not always accompanied by microangiopathic hemolysis, and in antiphospholipid syndrome renal TMA is common while signs of MAHA and platelet consumption are usually absent.","Scleroderma renal crisis is histologically indistinguishable from malignant hypertension and the two can coexist, so renal biopsy is recommended for atypical renal involvement, especially when hypertension is absent.","ADAMTS13 reduction in HELLP (30-60%) is milder than in TTP (below ~10%), so interpreting modest reductions as diagnostic of TTP can mislead during pregnancy when aHUS and TTP can also be triggered.","Thrombocytopenia in HIV-related TMA may prohibit renal biopsy, yet biopsy is required for diagnosis because other HIV-associated renal diseases mimic it; ADAMTS13 activity is not reduced in HIV-related TMA.","In drug-induced autoantibody TMA (e.g., quinine), disease can recur after even a single re-exposure in a previously sensitized patient, so it is not strictly dose-dependent.","Distinguishing HSCT-associated TMA is difficult because thrombocytopenia, anemia, and renal insufficiency are common after transplantation for other reasons."],"severityClassification":"No single unified severity scale; classification is by etiologic subtype and laboratory criteria. TTP is defined as MAHA with ADAMTS13 activity below 5-10% and subdivided into congenital (cTTP/Upshaw-Schulman) and immune (iTTP) forms. HUS variants include STEC/D+ HUS, pneumococcal neuraminidase-associated HUS, atypical complement-mediated HUS (aHUS), and DEAP HUS. HSCT-associated TMA has formal diagnostic criteria (Harrison Table 329-1): the International Working Group requires schistocytes (over 4% in one set, or RBC fragmentation with at least 2 schistocytes per high-power field), de novo/progressive thrombocytopenia, rising LDH, falling hemoglobin or increased transfusion need, negative Coombs, falling haptoglobin, and concurrent renal/neurologic dysfunction without other explanation. Antiphospholipid syndrome severity is stratified by antibody profile, with 'triple positivity' conferring the highest thrombosis risk and a catastrophic variant defined by rapid multiorgan failure.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2444","Harrison 22e p.2445","Harrison 22e p.2446","Harrison 22e p.2447","Harrison 22e p.2448"],"crossLinks":[]},"thyroid_cancer":{"name":"Thyroid Nodular Disease and Cancer","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thyroid nodule","thyroid carcinoma","papillary thyroid cancer","medullary thyroid cancer"],"chapter":397,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3040-3050","clinicalPearls":["Thyroid nodules are common: ~3-7% of adults by palpation, but up to 50% have nodules on ultrasound, with the majority <1 cm. The vast majority of nodules are benign (Harrison 22e p.3040, p.3044, p.3048).","Initial workup of a nodule begins with a TSH level. If TSH is suppressed, a radionuclide scan is done; a hyperfunctioning ('hot') nodule is almost never malignant, so FNA is unnecessary. Otherwise proceed to thyroid ultrasound (Harrison 22e p.3048).","Thyroid ultrasound with a validated risk-stratification system (e.g., ACR TI-RADS, TR1-TR5) is used to decide which nodules need fine-needle aspiration (FNA), combining sonographic pattern with nodule size; more suspicious patterns warrant FNA at lower size cutoffs (Harrison 22e p.3042, p.3048).","FNA, ideally ultrasound-guided, is the diagnostic procedure of choice for nodules. Cytology is reported with the six-tier Bethesda System (version 3): benign ~4% malignancy risk, AUS ~22%, follicular neoplasm ~30%, suspicious for malignancy ~74%, malignant ~97% (Harrison 22e p.3044, p.3049).","Papillary thyroid cancer (PTC) is the most common type (80-85% of well-differentiated cancers); classic and follicular variants are usually indolent, spread mainly via lymphatics, and have an excellent prognosis (>95% present at stage I or II) (Harrison 22e p.3046).","Follicular thyroid cancer (FTC) accounts for ~5% of US thyroid cancers and cannot be distinguished from benign follicular lesions by FNA cytology alone; diagnosis requires histologic evidence of capsular and/or vascular invasion (Harrison 22e p.3046-3047, p.3048).","Medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC) arises from calcitonin-producing C cells, accounts for ~5% of thyroid cancers, and can be sporadic or familial (MEN 2A, MEN 2B, familial MTC); elevated serum calcitonin is a marker of residual/recurrent disease (Harrison 22e p.3048).","All patients with MTC should be tested for RET mutations so that genetic counseling and family testing can be offered; pheochromocytoma must be excluded before surgery in any patient with a RET mutation (Harrison 22e p.3048).","Anaplastic thyroid cancer (ATC) is poorly differentiated and aggressive with a bleak prognosis; most patients die within 6 months of diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.3040, p.3048).","Serum thyroglobulin (Tg) is a sensitive marker of residual/recurrent disease for PTC, FTC, and oncocytic carcinoma after total thyroidectomy and radioablation, while calcitonin serves this role for MTC (Harrison 22e p.3045, p.3047).","Molecular diagnostic panels (next-generation sequencing, microRNA, gene expression) are increasingly used on FNA samples with indeterminate cytology (AUS, follicular neoplasm) to distinguish benign from malignant lesions and reduce unnecessary diagnostic surgery; current validated tests have negative predictive values >95% (Harrison 22e p.3046, p.3050)."],"pathophysiology":"Thyroid nodular disease reflects disordered growth of thyroid cells that may be hyperplastic or neoplastic. Thyroid neoplasms can arise from follicular cells (papillary, follicular, oncocytic, poorly differentiated, anaplastic carcinomas), calcitonin-producing C cells (medullary carcinoma), lymphocytes (lymphoma), or stromal/vascular elements. Cancers are monoclonal, arising from mutations conferring a growth advantage or resistance to cell death. Activation of the RET-RAS-BRAF (MAPK) signaling pathway is seen in up to 70% of PTCs; BRAF V600E mutations occur in up to 60% of PTC, RET rearrangements in ~15% (more frequent after Chernobyl fallout), and RAS mutations in ~15%. FTCs harbor BRAF/RAS mutations plus PAX8-PPARgamma rearrangements (~70% have a mutation/rearrangement). Anaplastic cancers accumulate additional TERT promoter, BRAF, CTNNB1 (beta-catenin), and TP53 mutations driving dedifferentiation and genetic instability. MEN 2-associated MTC carries inherited activating RET point mutations and is preceded by C-cell hyperplasia. External and fallout radiation predispose to chromosomal breaks and increase risk of benign and malignant nodules, especially in children. Most differentiated cancers retain TSH receptor expression and respond to TSH, the basis for both LT4 suppression and radioiodine uptake.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign hyperplastic (colloid) nodule","Follicular adenoma","Oncocytic (Hurthle cell) adenoma","Thyroid cyst (colloid cyst)","Multinodular goiter (nodule within an MNG)","Toxic adenoma / hyperfunctioning ('hot') nodule","Noninvasive follicular thyroid neoplasm with papillary-like nuclear features (NIFTP) - no longer considered malignant","Thyroglossal duct cyst / developmental abnormality","Thyroid lymphoma","Metastasis to the thyroid from another primary"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hashimoto's thyroiditis (background for thyroid lymphoma; may produce a goitrous, nodular-feeling gland)","Subclinical thyrotoxicosis from autonomous nodule (mimics need for cancer workup but reflects functioning, benign nodule)","Hoarseness from gastroesophageal reflux rather than laryngeal nerve invasion by tumor"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum TSH (first test in nodule evaluation)","Radionuclide (I-123) scan if TSH suppressed, to identify hyperfunctioning 'hot' nodule","Thyroid and cervical lymph node ultrasound with risk-stratification (ACR TI-RADS / ATA / EU-TIRADS)","Ultrasound-guided fine-needle aspiration (FNA) with Bethesda System cytology reporting","Molecular testing of indeterminate FNA samples (next-generation sequencing, microRNA, gene-expression panels)","Serum thyroglobulin (Tg) for surveillance of differentiated cancer after thyroidectomy/ablation","Serum calcitonin (and RET mutation testing) for medullary thyroid carcinoma","Whole-body radioiodine scan (WBS) for iodine-avid metastases or elevated Tg with negative imaging","Neck/chest cross-sectional imaging (CT/MRI) and PET-CT (18FDG or Ga-68 Dotatate avidity raises malignancy suspicion)"],"redFlags":["Nodule fixed to adjacent structures (firm/hard, immobile)","Vocal cord paralysis or hoarse voice (suggests recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement)","Lateral cervical lymphadenopathy ipsilateral to the nodule","Rapidly enlarging neck mass","Prior head/neck irradiation before age 18 (e.g., mantle radiation for Hodgkin's, cranial radiation for childhood leukemia)","Childhood/adolescent exposure to ionizing radiation from fallout","Age <20 or >65 years","Male gender","Family history of PTC in two or more first-degree relatives, MEN 2, or syndromes (Cowden's, familial adenomatous polyposis, Carney complex, PTEN hamartoma)","18FDG or Ga-68 Dotatate PET avidity of the nodule"],"prognosis":"Prognosis spans a wide range by histologic type. Differentiated cancers (PTC and FTC) are often curable with good prognosis in early-stage disease; the 20-year disease-specific mortality for low-risk thyroid cancer is ~1%, and most PTC present at stage I/II with survival similar to expected. Mortality rises markedly in stage IV disease, especially with distant metastases (stage IVB, ~1% of patients). FTC, particularly angioinvasive, carries less favorable outcomes than PTC partly due to more stage IV presentation; poor features include distant metastases, age >55, tumor >4 cm, and marked vascular invasion. MTC is more aggressive in MEN 2B than MEN 2A, and familial MTC more aggressive than sporadic. Anaplastic thyroid cancer is aggressive with a bleak prognosis, most patients dying within 6 months. Older age (>65) and male gender are associated with worse outcomes overall.","pitfalls":["Thyroid cancer is being overdiagnosed: rising incidence is driven by small T1 papillary tumors while disease-specific mortality has not changed in 40 years; routine FNA is not recommended for nodules <1 cm unless metastatic cervical nodes are present.","A hyperfunctioning ('hot') nodule on radionuclide scan is almost never malignant; FNA is unnecessary and should not be performed for these.","FNA cytology cannot reliably distinguish benign follicular adenoma from follicular carcinoma because nuclear features do not differ; the distinction requires histologic evidence of capsular/vascular invasion.","LT4 (TSH-suppression) therapy does NOT shrink nodules in iodine-replete (iodine-sufficient) populations and should not be used for that purpose; if it has not reduced nodule size after 6-12 months it should be stopped.","Spongiform nodules (TR1) have <3% cancer risk and warrant observation rather than FNA.","Iodine-containing contrast agents should be avoided in nodular goiter due to the risk of the Jod-Basedow effect (iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis from autonomous nodules).","NIFTP can only be diagnosed on surgical pathology, so it is still included within Bethesda malignancy risk estimates.","Hoarseness more commonly reflects benign causes (e.g., reflux) than malignancy, but new vocal cord paralysis is a genuine red flag.","In MTC, surgery should not proceed until pheochromocytoma has been excluded in any patient with a RET mutation.","For thyroid lymphoma, initial surgical resection should be avoided because it may spread otherwise localized disease."],"severityClassification":"Histologic classification (WHO/Table 397-1): follicular cell-derived (benign follicular/oncocytic adenoma; low-risk neoplasms including NIFTP; malignant - papillary, follicular, oncocytic, differentiated/poorly differentiated high-grade, anaplastic), C-cell-derived medullary carcinoma, mixed medullary-follicular, lymphoma, and others. Staging uses the AJCC/UICC 8th-edition TNM system (tumor size and extrathyroidal invasion, regional nodal metastases, distant metastases) for differentiated and anaplastic cancers. Sonographic risk stratification uses ACR TI-RADS levels TR1 (benign) to TR5 (highly suspicious). FNA cytology uses the six-tier Bethesda System v3 (I nondiagnostic ~13%, II benign ~4%, III AUS ~22%, IV follicular neoplasm ~30%, V suspicious for malignancy ~74%, VI malignant ~97% malignancy risk). MTC risk in MEN 2 is further stratified by specific RET mutation.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3040-3050"],"crossLinks":["multiple_endocrine_neoplasia","hyperthyroidism"]},"thyroid_eye_disease":{"name":"Thyroid eye disease","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thyroid-associated orbitopathy","Graves ophthalmopathy","thyroid orbitopathy","TED","TAO"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An autoimmune orbitopathy most often associated with Graves disease, though patients may be hyperthyroid, euthyroid, or hypothyroid at presentation","Cardinal features include eyelid retraction, lid lag on downgaze, proptosis, restrictive strabismus with diplopia, and ocular surface exposure","It follows an active inflammatory phase lasting months to a couple of years, followed by a quiescent fibrotic phase; treatment strategy depends on which phase the patient is in","Smoking strongly worsens the disease and its response to treatment, so cessation is a key intervention","Disease activity and severity are assessed separately, for example using a clinical activity score and grading of soft tissue, proptosis, motility, corneal, and optic nerve findings","Active moderate-to-severe disease is managed with immunosuppression and orbital measures, while stable disease may require rehabilitative surgery in the sequence of orbital decompression, then squint, then eyelid surgery","The most feared complication is dysthyroid optic neuropathy from apical crowding, which threatens sight and needs urgent treatment"],"pathophysiology":"Thyroid eye disease is an autoimmune inflammatory disorder of the orbit linked to Graves disease. Autoantibodies against the thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor, expressed on orbital fibroblasts, drive activation and differentiation of these cells. The resulting inflammation causes accumulation of glycosaminoglycans, oedema, and enlargement of the extraocular muscles and orbital fat. The expanded orbital contents within a fixed bony cavity produce proptosis, restrictive myopathy, and, when the crowded orbital apex compresses the optic nerve, dysthyroid optic neuropathy. Over time inflammation gives way to fibrosis, leaving fixed muscle restriction and lid changes.","additionalDifferentials":["Orbital cellulitis","Orbital inflammatory syndrome (orbital pseudotumour)","Orbital tumour or lymphoma","Carotid-cavernous fistula","IgG4-related orbital disease","Myasthenia gravis (as a cause of diplopia and lid signs, and a recognised association)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Thyroid function tests and thyroid autoantibodies including TSH-receptor antibodies","Assessment of clinical activity score and disease severity grading","Exophthalmometry to quantify proptosis","Orbital CT or MRI to assess muscle enlargement and apical crowding","Visual acuity, colour vision, visual fields, and pupillary assessment to detect optic neuropathy","Ocular motility and diplopia assessment"],"redFlags":["Dysthyroid optic neuropathy with reduced acuity, impaired colour vision, a relative afferent pupillary defect, or disc swelling requiring urgent treatment","Corneal exposure or ulceration from severe proptosis and lagophthalmos","Rapidly progressive proptosis or globe subluxation","Marked orbital congestion with raised intraocular pressure"],"prognosis":"Many patients have mild, self-limiting disease, but moderate-to-severe cases can leave lasting proptosis, diplopia, and disfigurement; optic neuropathy risks permanent visual loss if not treated promptly, and smoking cessation improves the overall outlook.","pitfalls":["Assuming euthyroid patients cannot have thyroid eye disease and delaying diagnosis","Missing early optic neuropathy because central acuity can be preserved while colour vision and fields deteriorate","Performing rehabilitative surgery during the active inflammatory phase","Neglecting smoking cessation and ocular surface protection"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"thyroid_gland_physiology_and_testing":{"name":"Thyroid Gland Physiology and Testing","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Thyroid axis physiology","Thyroid function testing","HPT axis"],"chapter":394,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3020","clinicalPearls":["TSH is the single most useful physiologic marker of thyroid hormone action; because of its long plasma half-life (~50 min) a single measurement reliably reflects circulating levels (p.3021).","A normal TSH essentially excludes a primary thyroid abnormality, so the logical first step in evaluation is to determine whether TSH is suppressed, normal, or elevated (p.3026).","Primary hyperthyroidism produces a low/suppressed TSH and primary hypothyroidism produces an elevated TSH because thyroid hormone exerts dominant negative feedback on TSH gene expression (p.3021, p.3026).","TSH is a glycoprotein with an alpha subunit shared with LH, FSH, and hCG and a unique beta subunit; the structurally similar hCG weakly stimulates the TSH-R, lowering TSH in the first trimester of pregnancy (p.3021, p.3024).","T4 is secreted in roughly 15-fold excess over T3 and acts largely as a prohormone, being converted peripherally to the more potent T3 by deiodinases (p.3023-3024).","Nuclear thyroid hormone receptors silence target genes when unliganded by binding co-repressors, so hormone deficiency causes both loss of stimulation and active gene repression, giving deficiency a more profound effect than receptor gene deletion (p.3025).","About 5-15% of euthyroid women and up to 2% of euthyroid men carry thyroid (anti-TPO) antibodies and are at increased risk of future thyroid dysfunction; nearly all autoimmune hypothyroid patients and up to 80% of Graves' patients have TPO antibodies (p.3026).","Functioning (hot) thyroid nodules are almost never malignant and do not require fine-needle aspiration, whereas non-functioning (cold) nodules carry a ~5-10% malignancy risk (p.3026-3027).","Serum thyroglobulin is elevated in all forms of thyrotoxicosis except factitious thyrotoxicosis (exogenous hormone), and its principal clinical use is surveillance after thyroidectomy/radioablation for thyroid cancer (p.3026).","After total thyroidectomy and radioablation for thyroid cancer, thyroglobulin should be <0.2 ng/mL in the absence of anti-Tg antibodies; a measurable level suggests incomplete ablation or recurrence (p.3026)."],"pathophysiology":"The hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis is a classic endocrine feedback loop. Hypothalamic TRH stimulates pituitary thyrotrope production of TSH, which binds the TSH-R (a Gs-coupled seven-transmembrane GPCR) on the basolateral surface of thyroid follicular cells, activating adenylyl cyclase/cAMP and phospholipase C to drive gland growth and hormone synthesis. Iodide is actively trapped by the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS), transported apically via pendrin, and oxidized/organified onto tyrosyl residues of thyroglobulin by thyroid peroxidase using DUOX-generated hydrogen peroxide; iodotyrosines are coupled to form T4 and T3, which are released after lysosomal proteolysis of reabsorbed thyroglobulin. Circulating hormone is largely protein-bound (TBG, transthyretin, albumin) with only the small unbound fraction biologically available; deiodinases (types I, II, III) regulate peripheral conversion of T4 to active T3 or inactivating reverse T3. T3 acts mainly through nuclear receptors TRalpha and TRbeta, which heterodimerize with RXR on thyroid response elements, exchanging co-repressors for co-activators upon ligand binding. Thyroid hormones feed back, predominantly via TRbeta2 in hypothalamus and pituitary, to suppress TRH and TSH, establishing the axis set point.","additionalDifferentials":["Euthyroid hyperthyroxinemia (familial dysalbuminemic hyperthyroxinemia, TBG excess, transthyretin variants) with elevated total but normal free T4/T3","Resistance to thyroid hormone (TRbeta mutation) with high free T4/T3 and inappropriately normal/elevated TSH","TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma causing inappropriate TSH with high thyroid hormones","Sick euthyroid (nonthyroidal illness) syndrome altering TSH and T4-to-T3 conversion","MCT8 transporter mutation (X-linked) with low T4, high T3, high TSH and psychomotor retardation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["TBG excess from estrogen (pregnancy, oral contraceptives, tamoxifen) raising total T4/T3 while free hormone stays normal","TBG deficiency (X-linked) lowering total T4/T3 with normal free levels and euthyroid state","Biotin supplement interference falsely lowering TSH and falsely raising T4/T3 in biotinylated assays","Drugs displacing hormone from binding proteins (salicylates, salsalate) or impairing T4-to-T3 conversion (propranolol, amiodarone, glucocorticoids, propylthiouracil, oral contrast agents)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Sensitive third-generation TSH immunoradiometric assay (detects to <=0.01 mIU/L) as the screening test","Free (unbound) T4 by immunoassay or equilibrium dialysis; free T3 when TSH is suppressed but free T4 is normal (to detect T3 toxicosis)","Anti-TPO (and anti-thyroglobulin) antibodies for autoimmune thyroid disease","TRAb / thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin assay for Graves' disease and to predict fetal/neonatal thyrotoxicosis","Serum thyroglobulin for thyroiditis and post-thyroidectomy cancer surveillance","Radioiodine (123I/131I) uptake and thyroid scan, or 99mTc pertechnetate imaging, to define etiology of thyrotoxicosis","Thyroid ultrasound (10-12 MHz) with risk-stratification systems (TI-RADS) and ultrasound-guided fine-needle aspiration for nodules"],"redFlags":["Retrosternal goiter causing venous distention and dyspnea on raising the arms (Pemberton's sign)","Suspicious sonographic nodule features (hypoechoic solid nodule, irregular margins, punctate echogenic microcalcifications) with >90% cancer risk","Cervical or supraclavicular lymphadenopathy on thyroid examination suggesting malignancy","High maternal TRAb/TSI (>3x upper limit of normal) in the third trimester predicting fetal/neonatal thyrotoxicosis","Untreated maternal/neonatal hypothyroidism in iodine-deficient regions risking cretinism (intellectual disability, growth retardation)"],"prognosis":"Most physiologic and binding-protein variants (TBG abnormalities, familial dysalbuminemic hyperthyroxinemia, classic TRbeta resistance) are benign and require no treatment once recognized, the main goal being avoidance of inappropriate therapy. Early thyroid hormone replacement in congenital hypothyroidism prevents severe developmental abnormality, whereas untreated iodine deficiency in early life causes irreversible cretinism and even mild deficiency can subtly reduce IQ.","pitfalls":["Treating low total T4 in TBG deficiency: attempts to normalize total T4 cause thyrotoxicosis and are futile due to rapid clearance.","Relying on TSH alone in suspected hypothalamic-pituitary (secondary) hypothyroidism, where TSH is inappropriately low-to-normal for a low T4; free T4 must also be measured.","Failing to stop biotin supplements >=2 days before testing, leading to falsely low TSH and falsely high T4/T3.","Missing T3 toxicosis (2-5% of cases) if only free T4 is checked when TSH is suppressed.","Misinterpreting transiently low TSH (0.01-0.1 mIU/L) of early pregnancy, recent hyperthyroidism treatment, or glucocorticoid/dopamine therapy as true thyrotoxicosis.","Performing fine-needle aspiration on a hot/functioning nodule, which is almost never malignant; scintigraphy should precede biopsy when TSH is subnormal.","Confusing thyroid hormone resistance or a TSH-secreting adenoma with simple primary hyperthyroidism because of elevated hormones with non-suppressed TSH."],"severityClassification":"Not applicable as a discrete disease; thyroid status is categorized biochemically by TSH and free hormone levels into euthyroid, subclinical hypo-/hyperthyroidism (abnormal TSH with normal free hormones), and overt hypo-/hyperthyroidism (abnormal TSH with abnormal free hormones), with trimester-specific reference ranges applied in pregnancy.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3020","Harrison 22e p.3021","Harrison 22e p.3022","Harrison 22e p.3023","Harrison 22e p.3024","Harrison 22e p.3025","Harrison 22e p.3026","Harrison 22e p.3027"],"crossLinks":[]},"thyroid_nodule":{"name":"Thyroid nodule","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["thyroid lump","solitary thyroid nodule","thyroid mass","nodular goitre"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Thyroid nodules are common and usually benign, but the central task is to identify the minority that are malignant","Initial evaluation begins with a serum TSH; a suppressed value points towards an autonomously functioning (usually benign) nodule and warrants a radionuclide scan","Ultrasound characterises nodules and stratifies malignancy risk, with features such as marked hypoechogenicity, microcalcifications, irregular margins, and a taller-than-wide shape raising concern","Fine-needle aspiration is the key diagnostic test, with results reported using standardised cytology categories that guide management","Hot (functioning) nodules on scanning rarely harbour malignancy, so aspiration is generally directed at non-functioning nodules","History of childhood neck irradiation, family history of thyroid cancer, rapid growth, hoarseness, or fixed hard nodules increase suspicion of cancer","Molecular testing of indeterminate cytology samples can refine the risk of malignancy and help avoid unnecessary surgery"],"pathophysiology":"Thyroid nodules arise from focal proliferation of follicular cells and may be colloid nodules, adenomas, cysts, areas of thyroiditis, or carcinomas. Autonomously functioning nodules develop constitutive activation of thyroid hormone production, often through activating mutations of the TSH-receptor or G-protein signalling pathway, suppressing TSH and appearing 'hot' on scanning. Malignant nodules result from oncogenic alterations (for example in the MAPK pathway) driving unregulated growth, and their behaviour ranges from indolent differentiated cancers to aggressive anaplastic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign colloid or hyperplastic nodule","Follicular adenoma","Differentiated thyroid carcinoma (papillary or follicular)","Medullary or anaplastic thyroid carcinoma","Thyroid cyst or focal thyroiditis","Parathyroid or other neck mass mimicking a thyroid nodule"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum TSH as the first-line test","Thyroid ultrasound to characterise the nodule and assess cervical lymph nodes","Radionuclide thyroid scan when TSH is suppressed","Ultrasound-guided fine-needle aspiration cytology","Molecular marker testing for indeterminate cytology","Serum calcitonin when medullary carcinoma is suspected"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging, hard, or fixed nodule","New hoarseness or vocal cord palsy suggesting recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement","Cervical lymphadenopathy","History of head and neck irradiation or family history of thyroid cancer or MEN syndromes","Compressive symptoms such as dysphagia or stridor"],"prognosis":"Most nodules are benign and can be followed conservatively, and even most malignant nodules are differentiated cancers with an excellent prognosis after treatment, though anaplastic and some medullary cancers carry a poor outlook.","pitfalls":["Proceeding to biopsy before checking TSH and missing an autonomous nodule that does not need aspiration","Relying on nodule size alone rather than sonographic risk features to decide on aspiration","Falsely reassuring the patient after a non-diagnostic aspirate without repeating sampling","Overlooking calcitonin measurement when clinical features suggest medullary carcinoma"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"tinea_versicolor":{"name":"Tinea Versicolor","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pityriasis versicolor","PV","Malassezia infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as multiple well-defined oval macules and patches on the upper trunk, shoulders, and neck, which coalesce and carry a fine, powdery scale revealed by gently stretching or scratching the skin","Lesions vary in colour, appearing hypopigmented on tanned or darker skin and pink-brown on paler skin, which explains the name versicolor","It is caused by an overgrowth of commensal Malassezia yeast rather than a true dermatophyte, so despite the name it is not classic ringworm","Warmth, humidity, sweating, oily skin, and immunosuppression favour the shift from yeast to the pathogenic filamentous form","Microscopy of scale shows the typical spaghetti-and-meatballs pattern of short hyphae and spores, confirming the diagnosis","Treatment relies on topical antifungal or keratolytic agents, with systemic antifungals reserved for extensive or recurrent disease; recurrence is common and prophylactic regimens may be needed","Warn patients that pigmentary changes can persist for weeks to months after successful treatment, as this does not indicate treatment failure","A Wood's lamp may show a coppery-orange or pale fluorescence, aiding assessment"],"pathophysiology":"Tinea versicolor results from overgrowth of lipophilic Malassezia yeasts that are normal residents of sebum-rich skin. Under predisposing conditions such as heat, humidity, occlusion, and increased sebum, the yeast converts to its hyphal, pathogenic form and proliferates within the stratum corneum. The organism produces dicarboxylic acids, notably azelaic acid, that inhibit melanocyte tyrosinase and cause the hypopigmented patches, while an associated low-grade inflammatory and hyperkeratotic response produces the scale and, in some, hyperpigmentation. Because the yeast is part of the normal flora, the condition is more a disturbance of host-commensal balance than a transmissible infection, and it readily recurs.","additionalDifferentials":["Vitiligo (complete depigmentation without scale)","Pityriasis alba","Post-inflammatory hypo- or hyperpigmentation","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Tinea corporis due to a true dermatophyte","Erythrasma or, when hypopigmented patches are extensive, early indeterminate leprosy in endemic areas"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Usually clinical, confirmed by microscopy of skin scrapings showing hyphae and spores","Wood's lamp examination for characteristic fluorescence","Culture is generally unnecessary because the organism is a normal commensal","Consideration of underlying immunosuppression or endocrine factors in extensive or recalcitrant disease"],"redFlags":["Widespread or unusually treatment-resistant disease prompting evaluation for immunosuppression","Persistent hypopigmented patches that lack scale and could represent another disorder such as vitiligo or, in endemic settings, leprosy","Signs of deeper Malassezia infection such as folliculitis with pustules in an immunocompromised patient"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and responds well to antifungal therapy, but recurrence is frequent because the causative yeast is part of the normal skin flora and predisposing conditions persist; residual pigmentary change resolves gradually over weeks to months once treated.","pitfalls":["Interpreting residual hypopigmentation after treatment as treatment failure","Confusing hypopigmented patches with vitiligo and omitting to examine for scale","Failing to counsel about the high recurrence rate and the role of maintenance therapy","Overlooking predisposing factors such as heat, humidity, or immunosuppression that drive relapse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"tinnitus":{"name":"Tinnitus","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ringing in the ears","subjective tinnitus","objective tinnitus"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Tinnitus is the perception of sound in the absence of an external source and is a symptom rather than a disease in itself","It is usefully divided into subjective tinnitus, heard only by the patient, and the much rarer objective tinnitus, which can also be detected by the examiner","Most subjective tinnitus accompanies sensorineural hearing loss from noise exposure, presbycusis, ototoxic drugs or Meniere disease","Pulsatile tinnitus that is synchronous with the heartbeat suggests a vascular cause such as a glomus tumour, an arteriovenous malformation or a dural sinus abnormality and needs vascular imaging","Rhythmic clicking that is not pulse-synchronous may be due to palatal or middle ear muscle myoclonus or a patulous Eustachian tube","Unilateral tinnitus with asymmetrical hearing loss should prompt evaluation for a retrocochlear lesion such as an acoustic neuroma","Aggravating factors such as ototoxic and other implicated medications, caffeine, stress and poor sleep should be reviewed","Because there is often no curative treatment, management centres on treating any underlying cause, hearing aids or sound therapy for masking, and cognitive and habituation-based approaches such as tinnitus retraining therapy"],"pathophysiology":"Subjective tinnitus is thought to arise from aberrant neural activity generated when the cochlea or auditory nerve is damaged; loss of input leads to maladaptive changes and increased spontaneous firing and reorganisation in the central auditory pathways, so the brain perceives sound that is not present. Emotional and attentional networks amplify and sustain the perception, which explains why distress and the loudness of tinnitus are only loosely related. Objective tinnitus, in contrast, has a genuine internal acoustic source: turbulent or pulsatile blood flow in vascular lesions, or repetitive contraction of palatal, tensor tympani or stapedius muscles, generates a real sound transmitted to the ear.","additionalDifferentials":["Noise-induced or age-related sensorineural hearing loss","Meniere disease","Ototoxicity from drugs","Otosclerosis or middle ear effusion","Acoustic neuroma or other retrocochlear lesion","Vascular causes (glomus tumour, arteriovenous malformation, carotid or dural sinus disease)","Palatal or middle ear myoclonus and patulous Eustachian tube"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Otoscopy and pure tone audiometry to characterise associated hearing loss","Tympanometry and acoustic reflex testing where middle ear or myoclonic causes are suspected","Tinnitus pitch and loudness matching for assessment and rehabilitation","MRI of the internal auditory meatus for unilateral or asymmetrical tinnitus","CT/MR angiography or venography for pulsatile tinnitus","Blood tests for anaemia, thyroid dysfunction or vascular risk factors where clinically indicated"],"redFlags":["Unilateral tinnitus with asymmetrical hearing loss suggesting a retrocochlear tumour","Pulsatile tinnitus requiring vascular imaging to exclude a treatable vascular lesion","Associated neurological signs, facial numbness or weakness","Sudden hearing loss accompanying the tinnitus, which is an emergency","Tinnitus with severe distress, depression or suicidal ideation needing urgent mental health support"],"prognosis":"Many patients habituate over time and cope well with reassurance, sound enrichment and psychological approaches, and tinnitus linked to a treatable cause may improve when that cause is addressed; however, some individuals have persistent, distressing tinnitus that requires ongoing support.","pitfalls":["Missing an acoustic neuroma behind unilateral tinnitus","Failing to image pulsatile tinnitus for a vascular source","Dismissing the symptom without exploring its psychological impact and sleep disturbance","Overlooking ototoxic medication or reversible contributors","Promising a cure rather than framing management around control and habituation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"tmj_disorder":{"name":"Temporomandibular joint disorder","system":"Oral / Dental / Maxillofacial","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TMD","temporomandibular disorder","TMJ dysfunction","myofascial pain dysfunction"],"chapter":null,"source":"Standard oral medicine & OMFS references","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Temporomandibular disorders are a group of musculoskeletal conditions affecting the jaw joints, the muscles of mastication, or both","Typical features are jaw pain, joint sounds such as clicking or crepitus, limited or deviated mouth opening, and pain on chewing","Pain is often localised to the preauricular region and may radiate to the temple, ear, or face, and can mimic ear or dental pathology","Most cases are muscular (myofascial) rather than intra-articular, and parafunctional habits like clenching and grinding are common contributors","Disc displacement with reduction typically produces reciprocal clicking, whereas displacement without reduction causes locking and restricted opening","It is largely a clinical diagnosis; imaging is reserved for atypical, persistent, or red-flag presentations","Initial management is conservative, including reassurance, jaw rest, soft diet, physiotherapy, occlusal splints, and stress and habit control","Psychological factors, stress, and chronic pain behaviours strongly influence symptoms and treatment response"],"pathophysiology":"Temporomandibular disorders arise from a multifactorial interplay of biomechanical overload, parafunctional habits, trauma, and psychosocial stress acting on the masticatory system. Sustained muscle hyperactivity from clenching or grinding produces myofascial pain and fatigue, while abnormal loading and internal derangement of the joint can disturb the normal disc-condyle relationship, causing clicking, locking, or degenerative change. Central sensitisation and psychological factors can amplify and perpetuate pain. The result is a spectrum ranging from purely muscular pain to intra-articular disc displacement and degenerative joint disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Odontogenic pain from pulpal or periapical disease","Otitis or other ear pathology","Trigeminal neuralgia or other neuropathic facial pain","Giant cell (temporal) arteritis in older patients","Salivary gland disease","Referred pain from cervical spine or headache disorders"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical assessment of jaw movement, joint sounds, and muscle tenderness","Evaluation of occlusion and parafunctional habits","MRI for suspected internal derangement when symptoms persist","CT or cone-beam imaging for bony or degenerative joint changes","Inflammatory markers if temporal arteritis is a concern in older patients"],"redFlags":["New onset facial pain with headache, scalp tenderness, or visual symptoms in an older patient suggesting giant cell arteritis","Progressive limitation of opening, a hard end-feel, or a mass suggesting joint ankylosis or neoplasia","Unilateral worsening symptoms with cranial nerve signs pointing to a sinister cause","Recent significant trauma raising the possibility of condylar fracture"],"prognosis":"Most patients improve with conservative, reversible measures and the condition often follows a self-limiting or fluctuating course, though a minority develop chronic pain or progressive degenerative joint change.","pitfalls":["Proceeding to irreversible occlusal or surgical treatment before trying conservative care","Missing giant cell arteritis in an older patient with jaw and temporal symptoms","Attributing all facial pain to the joint and overlooking dental or neuropathic causes","Neglecting parafunctional habits and psychosocial factors that drive symptoms"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Burket's Oral Medicine","Standard OMFS references"],"crossLinks":[]},"tourette_syndrome":{"name":"Tourette syndrome","system":"Psychiatry / Mental health","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Tourette's disorder","Gilles de la Tourette syndrome","TS"],"chapter":null,"source":"Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry; DSM-5-TR","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The diagnosis requires both multiple motor tics and one or more vocal (phonic) tics, present at some time during the illness, with onset before age 18 and persistence for more than a year","Tics are sudden, rapid, recurrent, nonrhythmic movements or vocalisations that wax and wane in severity and often shift in type over time","Many patients experience premonitory urges, an uncomfortable sensation relieved by performing the tic, and can suppress tics temporarily at the cost of rising inner tension","Coprolalia, the involuntary utterance of obscenities, is popularly associated with the condition but actually occurs in only a minority of patients","Comorbidity is the rule rather than the exception, particularly attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder and obsessive-compulsive disorder, which often cause more impairment than the tics","Behavioural treatment, especially comprehensive behavioural intervention for tics including habit reversal training, is first-line; medication is reserved for troublesome tics","Pharmacological options for severe tics include agents acting on dopaminergic transmission and certain alpha-adrenergic agents, chosen by symptom burden and comorbidity","Psychoeducation, school liaison, and treating comorbid conditions usually improve function more than suppressing the tics themselves"],"pathophysiology":"Tourette syndrome is a neurodevelopmental disorder involving dysfunction of cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical circuits, with abnormalities in dopaminergic neurotransmission within the basal ganglia thought to underlie the generation of tics. It is highly heritable with a complex polygenic basis, and environmental and possibly immune-mediated factors may modulate expression, while overlapping circuitry accounts for the frequent association with obsessive-compulsive and attentional symptoms.","additionalDifferentials":["Provisional (transient) tic disorder or persistent motor or vocal tic disorder","Stereotypic movement disorder","Myoclonus, dystonia, or chorea from a neurological cause","Obsessive-compulsive disorder with compulsions resembling tics","Medication-induced or substance-induced movement disorder","Functional (psychogenic) movement disorder"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Detailed clinical history and observation of tics over time","Screening for comorbid ADHD, obsessive-compulsive disorder, anxiety, and mood disorders","Standardised tic severity rating scales to monitor course","Neurological examination to exclude other movement disorders","Further neurological workup only if atypical features suggest a secondary cause"],"redFlags":["Self-injurious tics causing physical harm","Abrupt, explosive onset or neurological signs suggesting a secondary or acquired cause","Severe comorbid depression with suicidal ideation","Marked functional deterioration from comorbid ADHD or obsessive-compulsive symptoms"],"prognosis":"Tics typically emerge in childhood, peak in severity in early adolescence, and then decline in many patients, with a substantial proportion experiencing marked improvement or near-resolution by adulthood. Prognosis for overall function depends heavily on comorbid conditions, which often persist and drive impairment, so their recognition and treatment strongly influence outcome.","pitfalls":["Focusing on tic suppression while neglecting more impairing comorbid ADHD or obsessive-compulsive disorder","Assuming coprolalia is required for diagnosis when it is uncommon","Overmedicating mild tics that do not warrant pharmacotherapy","Misinterpreting suppressible tics as voluntary or attention-seeking","Missing an atypical presentation that points to a secondary movement disorder"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry","DSM-5-TR"],"crossLinks":[]},"toxic_and_drug_induced_hepatitis":{"name":"Toxic and Drug-Induced Hepatitis","system":"Hepatobiliary / Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Drug-induced liver injury","DILI","Hepatotoxicity","Toxic hepatitis"],"chapter":351,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2665-2672","clinicalPearls":["Drug-induced liver injury (DILI) is the single most common cause of acute liver failure, and hepatotoxicity signals are the leading reason candidate compounds fail to gain approval during drug development; a meticulous history covering prescription, over-the-counter, herbal, dietary, occupational and household chemical exposures is essential (Harrison 22e p.2665).","Two broad categories are recognized: direct (intrinsic) toxic injury, which is predictable, dose-dependent and has a short latency (often hours), and idiosyncratic injury, which is infrequent (roughly 1 in 1,000 to 1 in 100,000 exposures), less clearly dose-related and typically appears 5-90 days after starting the drug (Harrison 22e p.2665-2666).","The R value, the ratio of ALT to alkaline phosphatase (each as a multiple of the upper limit of normal), classifies the injury pattern: R greater than 5 indicates hepatocellular injury, R less than 2 indicates cholestatic injury, and R of 2-5 indicates a mixed pattern (Harrison 22e p.2667).","Hy's Law, named for Hyman Zimmerman, holds that drug-induced jaundice during a trial predicts serious liver injury, with roughly a 10-to-1 ratio of jaundiced cases to cases of acute liver failure; such cases often portend failure of drug approval (Harrison 22e p.2667).","Acetaminophen is the prototypic direct hepatotoxin and the most prevalent cause of acute liver failure in the Western world; it produces dose-related centrilobular necrosis, and aminotransferases can exceed 10,000 IU/L, far above levels seen in viral hepatitis, often with a relatively low bilirubin in this hyperacute injury (Harrison 22e p.2668).","A characteristic biochemical clue to acetaminophen toxicity is the combination of extremely high aminotransferases with low bilirubin; clinical suspicion should prompt empiric N-acetylcysteine even before history is confirmed, since denial or altered mentation can obscure the diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.2668).","Isoniazid causes transient mild aminotransferase elevation in about 10% of recipients early in therapy (an 'adaptive' response that usually resolves despite continued use); clinically significant hepatocellular injury can appear over a latency of up to 6 months and rises sharply with age, being highest in those over 50 and lowest in those under 20 (Harrison 22e p.2670).","Amoxicillin-clavulanate is the most commonly implicated single agent in DILI in the United States and Europe, producing a fairly specific mixed or predominantly cholestatic injury that may begin after the drug has already been stopped owing to a long latency (Harrison 22e p.2667, 2670-2671).","Complementary and alternative medicines, including herbal and dietary weight-loss and body-building products, now account for more than 20% of DILI cases in the United States; implicated agents include green tea extract (Camellia sinensis), kava, germander, chaparral, comfrey (pyrrolizidine alkaloids), Hydroxycut and OxyElite Pro (Harrison 22e p.2671).","Certain drugs produce an autoimmune-hepatitis-like phenotype with positive antinuclear antibodies, classically nitrofurantoin, minocycline, hydralazine and alpha-methyldopa (Harrison 22e p.2667, 2670-2671).","Immune checkpoint inhibitors (anti-CTLA-4 ipilimumab; anti-PD-1 pembrolizumab and nivolumab) cause an immune-mediated hepatitis within weeks, often ANA-positive, mediated predominantly by CD8+ lymphocytes and responsive to glucocorticoids; concurrent immune injury to thyroid, muscle and colon may occur (Harrison 22e p.2671-2672).","Withdrawal of the suspected agent at the first sign of an adverse reaction, or when aminotransferases reach five times the upper limit of normal, is critical, because continued use despite symptoms and signs is associated with lethal outcomes (Harrison 22e p.2668)."],"pathophysiology":"Hepatotoxicity arises from direct hepatocyte injury (free radicals or reactive metabolites causing membrane lipid peroxidation) or from drug/metabolite activation of innate or adaptive immune pathways, apoptotic signaling, and disruption of bile excretory transport. Interference with canalicular pumps allows injurious endogenous bile acids to accumulate, producing cholestasis; other mechanisms inhibit protein synthesis or impair mitochondrial fatty-acid oxidation, leading to lactic acidosis and microvesicular steatosis. Most drugs are metabolized via phase I cytochrome P450 (oxidation) followed by phase II conjugation (glucuronidation, sulfation) or glutathione inactivation; most toxicity reflects formation of a reactive phase I metabolite, with glutathione depletion removing a protective pathway. For acetaminophen, phase II conjugation yields harmless metabolites, but a fraction is converted by CYP2E1 to the toxic intermediate NAPQI, normally detoxified by glutathione; when glutathione is overwhelmed or depleted, NAPQI binds covalently to hepatocyte macromolecules forming protein adducts that drive necrosis. Susceptibility to idiosyncratic injury is influenced by HLA haplotypes (e.g., amoxicillin-clavulanate, flucloxacillin), polymorphisms in metabolizing pathways and protective cytokines, and activation of nuclear receptors such as CAR and PXR. Factors potentiating acetaminophen injury include enzyme induction (alcohol via CYP2E1, phenobarbital, isoniazid), fasting/starvation and other states that lower hepatic glutathione (Harrison 22e p.2665-2666, 2668-2670).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute viral hepatitis (A, B, C) - idiosyncratic hepatocellular DILI can be clinically and histologically indistinguishable","Autoimmune hepatitis (especially with nitrofurantoin, minocycline, hydralazine, methyldopa)","Alcoholic hepatitis (amiodarone produces a pseudoalcoholic picture)","Ischemic hepatitis / shock liver","Biliary obstruction and primary sclerosing cholangitis (DILI can mimic sclerosing cholangitis after floxuridine or long-term ketamine)","Cholestasis of pregnancy (shares genetic canalicular transporter defects with estrogen-induced cholestasis)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute viral hepatitis A, B, and C"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Autoimmune hepatitis","Alcoholic / nonalcoholic steatohepatitis","Ischemic hepatopathy","Mechanical biliary obstruction"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial ALT, AST, alkaline phosphatase and bilirubin to define pattern and compute the R value (ALT/ALP as multiples of upper limit of normal)","Serum acetaminophen level timed 4-8 h after ingestion, interpreted against the treatment nomogram (levels above 300 mcg/mL at 4 h predict severe injury; below 150 mcg/mL injury unlikely)","Acetaminophen-protein (acetaminophen-Cys) adduct measurement by HPLC as an investigational diagnostic marker","Arterial blood lactate in acute liver failure (above 3.5 mmol/L suggests likely need for transplantation)","Autoantibodies (ANA, anti-smooth muscle, anti-LKM2, anti-mitochondrial) when an autoimmune-pattern or checkpoint-inhibitor injury is suspected","Liver biopsy when the pattern is uncertain (may show centrilobular necrosis, microvesicular steatosis, granulomas, phospholipid-laden lamellar bodies in amiodarone, or vanishing bile ducts)","Exclusion of viral, autoimmune and biliary causes; structured causality assessment (DILIN expert opinion, RUCAM, or newer RECAM)"],"redFlags":["Jaundice arising during drug exposure (Hy's Law) predicts serious injury and possible liver failure","Coagulopathy and hepatic encephalopathy - markers of acute liver failure, which develops in about 10% of DILI cases","Aminotransferases above 10,000 IU/L with a low bilirubin - hyperacute acetaminophen pattern","Continued ingestion despite nausea, fatigue or jaundice (common scenario in fatal isoniazid and acetaminophen toxicity)","Acetaminophen ingestion of 25 g or more, or high timed plasma levels on the nomogram","Arterial lactate above 3.5 mmol/L in acetaminophen acute liver failure","Concurrent renal failure or myocardial injury in acetaminophen overdose"],"prognosis":"Most DILI is self-limited and resolves after withdrawal of the offending agent. Acute liver failure develops in about 10% of DILI cases; once that threshold (coagulopathy plus encephalopathy) is reached, spontaneous recovery occurs in fewer than 30%, and over 40% of those patients undergo liver transplantation. Survivors of acetaminophen overdose rarely have lasting liver injury, though the associated acute kidney injury (seen in nearly 75% of severe cases) is almost always self-limited. Anabolic steroid cholestasis typically resolves over weeks to months with prolonged jaundice. Some agents cause chronic injury: methotrexate, tamoxifen and amiodarone can lead to cirrhosis, and several drugs cause chronic autoimmune-like hepatitis (Harrison 22e p.2668-2670).","pitfalls":["DILI is nearly always a diagnosis of exclusion; causality is hard to prove and many disorders produce an identical clinicopathologic picture","Mild transient aminotransferase elevations that resolve during continued therapy ('adaptation,' seen with isoniazid, valproate, phenytoin and statins) can be mistaken for significant toxicity","Long latency drugs (notably amoxicillin-clavulanate and nitrofurantoin) may cause injury that manifests only after the drug has been stopped","Idiosyncratic reactions occurring in under 1 in 10,000 recipients are missed by clinical trials and only detected through postmarketing surveillance (e.g., troglitazone)","In chronic alcohol users, glutathione depletion and CYP2E1 induction can make the toxic acetaminophen dose as low as 2 g","Activated charcoal or cholestyramine are ineffective if given more than 30 minutes after acetaminophen ingestion","Routine liver test monitoring during statin therapy is not recommended, and asymptomatic isolated aminotransferase elevations need not prompt discontinuation","Herbal/dietary supplements may contain undeclared pharmaceuticals (e.g., diclofenac in antirheumatic 'herbs'), so the history must specifically probe alternative-medicine use"],"severityClassification":"Injury pattern is graded by the R value (ALT/ALP as multiples of ULN): hepatocellular (R > 5), cholestatic (R < 2), or mixed (R 2-5). Severity is judged clinically by progression to acute liver failure (coagulopathy plus encephalopathy). Causality likelihood is assessed by structured tools including the DILIN expert-opinion scale (definite, highly likely, probable, possible, unlikely), RUCAM, and the newer RECAM. Hy's Law identifies high-risk hepatocellular injury when jaundice accompanies hepatocellular enzyme elevation (Harrison 22e p.2667-2668).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2665","Harrison 22e p.2666","Harrison 22e p.2667","Harrison 22e p.2668","Harrison 22e p.2669","Harrison 22e p.2670","Harrison 22e p.2671","Harrison 22e p.2672"],"crossLinks":[]},"toxic_shock_streptococcal":{"name":"Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["STSS","streptococcal TSS","group A streptococcal toxic shock","invasive GAS toxic shock"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / IDSA","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Driven by invasive group A Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes) whose exotoxins act as superantigens, provoking massive cytokine release and rapid-onset shock","Frequently arises from a soft-tissue focus such as necrotising fasciitis or cellulitis, though a portal of entry is not always evident","Pain out of proportion to visible skin findings is an early warning sign and should prompt urgent surgical assessment","Hypotension progresses quickly to multi-organ dysfunction, with renal impairment often preceding the drop in blood pressure","A generalised erythematous rash may be subtle or absent compared with staphylococcal toxic shock; desquamation can occur late","Management combines aggressive fluid resuscitation, prompt source control, and antibiotic principles pairing a beta-lactam with a protein-synthesis inhibitor such as clindamycin to switch off toxin production","Intravenous immunoglobulin is considered as an adjunct to neutralise circulating superantigens in severe disease","Mortality is high, so early recognition, critical-care support, and surgical debridement of any necrotising focus are decisive"],"pathophysiology":"Invasive strains of Streptococcus pyogenes release pyrogenic exotoxins and other superantigens that bind directly to MHC class II molecules and T-cell receptors outside the normal antigen groove. This triggers polyclonal T-cell activation and an overwhelming release of pro-inflammatory cytokines (a cytokine storm), producing capillary leak, vasodilatation, hypotension, and multi-organ failure. Bacteraemia and deep tissue invasion, often within necrotising soft-tissue infection, amplify the toxin load and accelerate the shock state.","additionalDifferentials":["Staphylococcal toxic shock syndrome","Septic shock from other Gram-positive or Gram-negative organisms","Meningococcaemia","Necrotising fasciitis without established shock","Severe drug reaction with systemic features"],"infectionMimics":["Anaphylaxis with distributive shock","Heat stroke with multi-organ dysfunction","Adrenal crisis","Severe acute pancreatitis with systemic inflammatory response"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood cultures and cultures from any surgical or wound specimen","Serial lactate, renal and liver function, coagulation profile and creatine kinase to track organ involvement","Imaging (CT or MRI) of a suspected deep soft-tissue focus, without delaying surgery when necrotising infection is likely","Surgical exploration for tissue diagnosis when necrotising fasciitis is suspected","Assessment for disseminated intravascular coagulation"],"redFlags":["Pain disproportionate to examination findings suggesting underlying necrotising infection requiring urgent debridement","Rapidly progressing hypotension and organ failure indicating established septic shock","Skin crepitus, bullae, dusky discolouration or rapidly advancing erythema pointing to deep tissue necrosis","Falling urine output and rising creatinine signalling early multi-organ dysfunction"],"prognosis":"Even with intensive treatment, mortality remains substantial and is highest when there is an associated necrotising soft-tissue infection or delayed source control; survival depends on early recognition, rapid surgery, and organ support.","pitfalls":["Attributing severe limb pain to a minor injury and missing early necrotising infection","Relying on antibiotics alone without urgent surgical source control","Omitting a protein-synthesis inhibitor such as clindamycin, forgoing its anti-toxin effect","Underestimating fluid requirements and delaying critical-care escalation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","IDSA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"toxoplasmosis":{"name":"Toxoplasmosis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Toxoplasma gondii infection"],"chapter":235,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1797","clinicalPearls":["Caused by the obligate intracellular parasite Toxoplasma gondii; up to a third of the world's population is latently infected (Harrison 22e p.1797).","Two transmissible stages reach humans: tissue cysts containing bradyzoites in undercooked meat (e.g., lamb, pork, venison, ground beef), and sporulated oocysts shed in cat feces that contaminate soil, food, or water; sporulated oocysts are environmentally hardy and highly infectious (Harrison 22e p.1797).","In the immunocompetent host, acute infection is usually asymptomatic and self-limited, going unrecognized in 80-90% of acquired infections (Harrison 22e p.1799).","The most common manifestation of symptomatic acute toxoplasmosis in immunocompetent patients is cervical lymphadenopathy; nodes are usually nontender and discrete, with associated headache, malaise, fatigue, and low-grade fever (temperature usually <40 degrees C) (Harrison 22e p.1799).","The classic severe presentation is reactivation encephalitis in immunocompromised hosts (especially HIV-positive individuals or transplant recipients) in whom latent infection has reactivated (Harrison 22e p.1797).","In AIDS, >95% of Toxoplasma encephalitis cases are due to recrudescent infection, typically developing when the CD4+ T-cell count falls below 100/microL; encephalitis is the predominant manifestation while meningeal involvement is uncommon (Harrison 22e p.1800).","On MRI/CT, Toxoplasma encephalitis classically produces multiple ring-enhancing lesions, most often in the basal ganglia and corticomedullary junction; a single lesion increases the likelihood of primary CNS lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.1801-1802).","T. gondii is the most common cause of posterior uveitis in immunocompetent individuals and is estimated to cause about 35% of all chorioretinitis cases in the US and Europe (Harrison 22e p.1799, p.1801).","Congenital toxoplasmosis results from transplacental passage during primary maternal infection; infants may be asymptomatic at birth but later manifest chorioretinitis, strabismus, epilepsy, hydrocephalus, microcephaly, intellectual disability, and psychomotor retardation (Harrison 22e p.1797, p.1799).","Antibody titers are NOT useful for establishing reactivation or following infection activity in immunocompromised patients; however, an absence of positive serology suggests an alternative diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1800).","Diagnosis rests on serology (combined IgG and IgM, with circulating IgA favoring acute infection) and PCR; PCR of body fluids is specific but its sensitivity may be low (Harrison 22e p.1801).","A therapeutic trial of anti-Toxoplasma medications is frequently used in AIDS patients with CNS mass lesions: >50% improve by day 3 and >90% by day 7, whereas worsening by day 7 (or lymphoma) prompts brain biopsy (Harrison 22e p.1802)."],"pathophysiology":"T. gondii is an intracellular coccidian. Ingested tissue cysts (bradyzoites) or oocysts (sporozoites) release organisms that invade gut epithelium and transform into rapidly dividing tachyzoites, which can infect and replicate in all nucleated mammalian cells via a parasitophorous vacuole, causing cell death and focal necrosis with an acute mononuclear inflammatory response. Parasites disseminate as free tachyzoites or within phagocytic 'Trojan horse' cells through blood and lymphatics to lymphatic tissue, skeletal muscle, myocardium, retina, placenta, and CNS. In the immunocompetent host, humoral and cell-mediated immunity (parasiticidal antibody, macrophage activation, IFN-gamma, IL-12-driven TH1 response, CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes) controls infection; tissue cysts containing bradyzoites then form 7-10 days after systemic infection and persist principally in the CNS and muscle, completing the asexual cycle and establishing lifelong chronic infection. Subclinical cycles of cyst rupture and reformation are the probable source of recrudescent infection. Immunocompromised or fetal hosts lack the immune factors (notably CD8+ T cells; reactivating T cells fail to secrete IFN-gamma and IL-2) needed to contain tachyzoite spread, allowing progressive focal destruction (necrotizing encephalitis, pneumonia, myocarditis). The sexual stage occurs only in the cat (definitive host), producing oocysts shed in feces (Harrison 22e p.1797-1800).","additionalDifferentials":["Mononucleosis-like syndrome from acute toxoplasmic lymphadenopathy: EBV infection, CMV infection, HIV infection, Bartonella (cat-scratch disease), and lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.1800-1801).","CNS mass lesions in an AIDS patient: primary CNS lymphoma, metastatic tumor, brain abscess, progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (JC virus), fungal infection, and mycobacterial infection (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Chorioretinitis in an immunocompetent individual: tuberculosis, syphilis, and histoplasmosis (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Chorioretinitis in an AIDS patient: CMV, syphilis, HSV, VZV, and fungal infection (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Cerebral toxoplasmosis must also be differentiated from herpes simplex encephalitis, cryptococcal meningitis, and HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder (HAND) (Harrison 22e p.1800).","Congenital infection: CMV, HSV, rubella, syphilis, and listeriosis (Harrison 22e p.1801)."],"infectionMimics":["Primary CNS lymphoma (EBV-associated) presenting as ring-enhancing brain lesion(s) in AIDS","Cytomegalovirus infection","Epstein-Barr virus infection (infectious mononucleosis)","Bartonella henselae (cat-scratch disease)","Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (JC virus)","Cryptococcal meningitis","Herpes simplex encephalitis","Tuberculous, fungal, or bacterial brain abscess","Pneumocystis pneumonia (Toxoplasma pneumonia can be confused with it)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Primary CNS lymphoma","Metastatic brain tumor / other CNS malignancy","Lymphoma (as a cause of lymphadenopathy)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serology: combined IgG and IgM antibody to Toxoplasma; circulating IgA favors acute infection; Sabin-Feldman dye test, indirect fluorescent antibody test, and ELISA measure IgG; double-sandwich IgM-ELISA or IgM-ISAGA preferred for IgM (Harrison 22e p.1801).","IgG avidity testing to help time infection, particularly useful in pregnancy (Harrison 22e p.1801).","PCR amplification of T. gondii DNA from blood, CSF, ocular fluid, bronchoalveolar lavage, or amniotic fluid; PCR is the mainstay for detecting organisms but a negative PCR does not rule out toxoplasmosis (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Double-dose contrast CT and contrast MRI of the brain (standard imaging for Toxoplasma encephalitis); MRI is more sensitive for monitoring response (Harrison 22e p.1802).","CSF analysis in suspected CNS disease (modest pleocytosis and protein elevation without glucose change; CSF PCR specific but not sensitive) (Harrison 22e p.1800-1801).","Lymph node biopsy / histology showing follicular hyperplasia and clusters of epithelioid macrophages; demonstration of tachyzoites establishes acute infection (Harrison 22e p.1799, p.1801).","Dilated ophthalmologic examination for chorioretinitis; ocular fluid PCR, Western blot, or Goldmann-Witmer coefficient for ocular disease (Harrison 22e p.1801-1802).","In HIV-infected persons, screening for IgG antibody to Toxoplasma at the time of HIV diagnosis to identify latent infection (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Weekly blood PCR screening after allogeneic HSCT in patients seropositive before transplant; pre-transplant donor and recipient serologies (Harrison 22e p.1800).","Next-generation sequencing (metagenomics) of blood or body fluids, especially in immunocompromised individuals (Harrison 22e p.1801)."],"redFlags":["Focal neurologic deficits, seizures, altered mental status, or ring-enhancing CNS mass lesion(s) in an HIV/AIDS patient (especially CD4+ <100/microL) - reactivation Toxoplasma encephalitis, which is rapidly fatal if untreated (Harrison 22e p.1800).","A solitary ring-enhancing CNS lesion - increases the likelihood of primary CNS lymphoma over toxoplasmosis (Harrison 22e p.1802).","Failure to improve clinically by day 7 of empiric anti-Toxoplasma therapy - prompts brain biopsy and consideration of lymphoma or alternative diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1802).","Fulminant sepsis, fever of unknown origin, or pneumonia in an immunocompetent person with travel to or residence in South/Central America - virulent atypical genotypes (Harrison 22e p.1797-1799).","Toxoplasma pneumonia / disseminated disease in immunocompromised hosts may progress to acute respiratory failure with hemoptysis, metabolic acidosis, hypotension, and disseminated intravascular coagulation (Harrison 22e p.1800).","Acute maternal infection in pregnancy (risk of transplacental transmission; severity highest with first-trimester infection) (Harrison 22e p.1798)."],"prognosis":"In immunocompetent individuals, acute infection is usually asymptomatic and self-limited, resolving within several weeks though lymphadenopathy may persist for months; chronic infection persists lifelong with little consequence. Ocular disease can be self-limited but episodic chorioretinitis flares progressively destroy retinal tissue and may lead to glaucoma. In immunocompromised hosts, disease may be rapidly fatal if untreated, mandating prompt diagnosis and therapy; with treatment of Toxoplasma encephalitis, >50% improve by day 3 and >90% by day 7, but relapses are common and maintenance/secondary prophylaxis is needed while CD4+ counts remain low. For congenital toxoplasmosis, factors associated with severe disability include delayed diagnosis/treatment, neonatal hypoxia and hypoglycemia, profound visual impairment, uncorrected hydrocephalus, and raised intracranial pressure; with appropriate treatment upward of 70% of children have normal developmental, neurologic, and ophthalmologic findings at follow-up. Infected infants normal at birth may still have higher rates of learning disabilities and chronic neurologic sequelae (Harrison 22e p.1797-1801).","pitfalls":["Acute toxoplasmosis is asymptomatic and self-limited in 80-90% of immunocompetent adults and children, so the diagnosis is easily missed (in one epidemic, toxoplasmosis was correctly diagnosed in only 3 of 25 patients who sought care) (Harrison 22e p.1799).","Antibody titers do not establish reactivation or track infection activity in immunocompromised patients; do not rely on serial titers to gauge active disease (Harrison 22e p.1800).","A negative PCR does not rule out toxoplasmosis - PCR is specific but may be of low sensitivity depending on the body fluid (Harrison 22e p.1801).","Isolation/demonstration of tissue cysts (bradyzoites) from biopsied tissue indicates only the presence of cysts and should NOT be misinterpreted as evidence of acute toxoplasmosis; only demonstration of tachyzoites (e.g., in lymph nodes) establishes acute infection (Harrison 22e p.1801).","IgM can persist for >1 year, so a positive IgM with positive IgG should not necessarily be interpreted as acute disease; a negative IgM with positive IgG indicates distant infection (Harrison 22e p.1801).","MRI/CT findings are not pathognomonic - 40% of CNS lymphomas are multifocal and 50% are ring-enhancing, and the false-negative rate for both MRI and CT is ~10% (Harrison 22e p.1802).","In AIDS patients with TE, IgM is usually undetectable and titers may be borderline/low (or transplant patients on B-cell-targeted immunosuppression may become seronegative); fewer than 3% have no demonstrable antibody at diagnosis (Harrison 22e p.1800, p.1801).","Up to 25% of congenitally infected newborns may be seronegative with normal routine physical examinations, so eye, brain, CSF, and radiologic assessment are needed (Harrison 22e p.1802).","Toxoplasma pneumonia can be confused with Pneumocystis pneumonia, and organisms may also be found in healthy tissue, complicating interpretation (Harrison 22e p.1800).","If a patient is seronegative for T. gondii, the likelihood that a CNS mass lesion is due to toxoplasmosis is <10%, favoring earlier biopsy (Harrison 22e p.1802)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal severity grading system for toxoplasmosis. Clinical stratification is by host immune status and syndrome: (1) immunocompetent acute infection - usually asymptomatic/self-limited (most commonly cervical lymphadenopathy); (2) ocular toxoplasmosis (chorioretinitis); (3) reactivation disease in the immunocompromised host - most importantly Toxoplasma encephalitis in AIDS (typically CD4+ <100/microL), which is rapidly fatal if untreated, plus pneumonitis, myocarditis, and disseminated disease; and (4) congenital toxoplasmosis, with severity inversely related to gestational age at maternal infection (earliest infection = lowest transmission but most severe fetal disease) (Harrison 22e p.1797-1800).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1797"],"crossLinks":["BRAIN_ABSCESS","ENCEPHALITIS"]},"tracheoesophageal_fistula":{"name":"Tracheoesophageal Fistula","system":"Neonatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TEF","TOF/TEF","esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula","EA/TEF"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An abnormal congenital communication between the trachea and oesophagus, most often occurring together with oesophageal atresia; the commonest anatomical type is proximal oesophageal atresia with a distal tracheoesophageal fistula.","Typical newborn features include excessive drooling and frothy secretions, choking, coughing, and cyanosis with feeds, and inability to pass a nasogastric tube into the stomach.","A characteristic radiographic clue is a feeding tube coiling in the upper oesophageal pouch; gas in the stomach and bowel indicates a distal fistula, while a gasless abdomen suggests atresia without a distal fistula.","Aspiration of saliva and, via the fistula, gastric contents into the airway causes respiratory compromise and pneumonitis.","An isolated H-type fistula without atresia may present later with recurrent coughing during feeds, cyanotic spells, and recurrent respiratory infections.","Initial management protects the airway and lungs: keep the infant nil by mouth, apply continuous suction to the upper pouch, and position to reduce aspiration and reflux through the fistula.","It is frequently part of the VACTERL association, so evaluation for vertebral, anorectal, cardiac, renal, and limb anomalies is important.","Definitive treatment is surgical division of the fistula and repair of the oesophageal atresia, timed according to the infant's stability and anatomy.","Long-term issues can include anastomotic stricture, gastro-oesophageal reflux, tracheomalacia, and dysphagia requiring ongoing follow-up."],"pathophysiology":"Abnormal separation of the primitive foregut into trachea and oesophagus during embryogenesis leaves an abnormal connection between the two and, commonly, a discontinuous oesophagus (atresia). In the common configuration, the upper oesophagus ends in a blind pouch while the lower oesophagus connects to the trachea. Swallowed secretions collect in and overflow from the upper pouch into the airway, and the distal fistula allows gastric contents to reflux into the trachea and air to distend the stomach. These mechanisms produce aspiration, chemical pneumonitis, and, with a large distal fistula, gaseous abdominal distension that further impairs ventilation.","additionalDifferentials":["Isolated oesophageal atresia without fistula","Laryngeal cleft","Choanal atresia","Severe gastro-oesophageal reflux with aspiration","Vascular ring causing feeding difficulty","Swallowing dysfunction of neurological origin"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Attempted passage of a nasogastric tube with radiograph showing tube coiling in the upper pouch","Chest and abdominal radiograph to assess the pouch and bowel gas pattern","Echocardiography to identify cardiac anomalies and aortic arch side before surgery","Renal ultrasound and spinal and limb assessment for VACTERL anomalies","Bronchoscopy or contrast study to define an H-type fistula when suspected"],"redFlags":["Choking, cyanosis, and respiratory distress with attempted feeding","Aspiration pneumonitis from overflow of secretions or reflux through the fistula","Progressive gastric and bowel distension impairing ventilation via a large distal fistula","Recurrent respiratory infections and cyanotic spells suggesting a missed H-type fistula","Associated critical cardiac or other major anomalies within the VACTERL spectrum"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and surgical repair the outlook is generally favourable, and prognosis is influenced strongly by prematurity, birth weight, and associated anomalies, particularly cardiac lesions. Many children require management of later complications such as strictures, reflux, and tracheomalacia but achieve good long-term function.","pitfalls":["Feeding a newborn with excessive drooling before excluding oesophageal atresia, precipitating aspiration","Missing a delayed-presenting H-type fistula in a child with recurrent feeding-related respiratory symptoms","Failing to search for associated VACTERL anomalies, especially cardiac lesions, before surgery","Overlooking that a gasless abdomen indicates atresia without a distal fistula","Underestimating long-term reflux, stricture, and tracheomalacia complications requiring follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"transfusion_reactions":{"name":"Transfusion Reactions","system":"Hematology / Transfusion","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hemolytic transfusion reaction","TRALI","TACO"],"chapter":118,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.901","clinicalPearls":["Acute immune-mediated haemolytic reactions occur when recipient preformed antibodies lyse transfused donor RBCs, during or up to 24 h after transfusion; anti-A/anti-B antibodies cause most of the most severe (potentially fatal) reactions, though alloantibodies against Rh, Kell, and Duffy antigens can also cause severe haemolysis (Harrison 22e p.906).","The most dramatic acute haemolytic reactions are usually caused by a failure in product or patient identification, erroneous blood grouping, or unidentified recipient anti-RBC alloimmunisation - i.e. a clerical/identification error (Harrison 22e p.906).","Acute haemolytic reactions may present with hypotension, tachypnoea, tachycardia, fever (rise of 1-2 degrees C), chills, chest and back/flank pain, haemoglobinuria, and haemoglobinemia; in the most severe cases DIC, acute renal failure, shock, and death may occur (Harrison 22e p.906).","RED FLAG: when an adverse reaction is suspected the transfusion must be STOPPED, the recipient's clinical status assessed, and supportive care initiated; a thorough clerical check is crucial to prevent a possible second misidentified transfusion (Harrison 22e p.908).","Management of acute immune haemolytic reaction is mainly supportive: prompt interruption of transfusion, biological workup, vigorous isotonic saline hydration with diuretics to maintain urine output; may require forced alkaline diuresis, electrolyte correction, and pressor support, and in DIC with bleeding, platelet/plasma/cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen (Harrison 22e p.908).","Diagnosis of transfusion-associated haemolysis relies on persistent/worsening anaemia, depleted haptoglobin, haemoglobinemia and haemoglobinuria, and elevated LDH and unconjugated bilirubin; the direct antiglobulin test (DAT/direct Coombs) is most often positive, as is the indirect antiglobulin test (IAT) (Harrison 22e p.908).","Delayed haemolytic reactions present with icterus and persisting or worsening anaemia and result from an anamnestic response in previously sensitised patients who had a negative alloantibody screen at transfusion (low antibody levels); the alloantibody becomes detectable 1-2 weeks after transfusion (Harrison 22e p.906).","FNHTR (febrile non-haemolytic transfusion reaction) is the most frequent reaction to cellular components (up to 300 per 100,000), characterised by chills, rigors and a rise of at least 1 degree C, caused by proinflammatory cytokines in the component or recipient antibodies against donor antigens; it is a diagnosis of exclusion once infection and haemolysis are ruled out (Harrison 22e p.911).","Leukocyte reduction (especially prestorage) can prevent FNHTR; premedication with antipyretics has generally proven ineffective and may mask relevant clinical symptoms (Harrison 22e p.911).","Most allergic transfusion reactions are mild (rash, pruritus, urticaria, localised oedema) and relate to plasma proteins; rarely they are severe/anaphylactic with bronchospasm, respiratory distress, hypotension, nausea, vomiting and shock - anaphylaxis can occur after only a few millilitres of component (Harrison 22e p.911).","TRALI is characterised by occurrence or worsening of hypoxia and NONcardiogenic pulmonary oedema with bilateral interstitial infiltrates on chest x-ray during or within 6 h of transfusion (delayed cases up to 72 h); it is among the most common causes of transfusion-related fatalities and treatment is supportive only (Harrison 22e p.911).","TRALI usually results from donor plasma containing high-titre anti-HLA class II antibodies binding recipient cognate antigen (anti-HLA class I and anti-HNA antibodies may also be involved); a cytokine/chemokine-mediated mechanism without HLA interaction can also occur (Harrison 22e p.911).","TACO (transfusion-associated circulatory overload) is common and underrecognised and is now the main cause of transfusion-related death since TRALI risk was mitigated; it presents with dyspnoea, hypoxia, bilateral and predominantly ALVEOLAR infiltrates on chest x-ray, frequent systolic hypertension, and elevated BNP (fever may also occur) (Harrison 22e p.912).","TRALI vs TACO distinction: TRALI gives noncardiogenic oedema with predominantly interstitial infiltrates within 6 h, whereas TACO is volume overload with predominantly alveolar infiltrates (within 12 h), systolic hypertension and elevated BNP; TACO risk factors include older age, renal failure, preexisting fluid overload, cardiac dysfunction, large transfusion volume and excessive transfusion rate (Harrison 22e p.911-912).","Transfusion-related GVHD is extremely rare but fatal in over 90% of cases; mediated by engrafted donor T lymphocytes in a recipient unable to reject them, it presents 5-10 days post-transfusion with cytopenia, fever, characteristic skin rash, diarrhoea and liver function abnormalities, and is prevented by irradiation (minimum 25 Gy) of cellular components in at-risk patients (Harrison 22e p.911).","Transfusion-transmitted bacterial infection remains a significant concern, notably with platelet concentrates stored at room temperature; some gram-negatives such as Yersinia grow at 4 degrees C and can contaminate RBC units - recipients may develop abrupt fever and chills deteriorating to septic shock, DIC and death, and after culture sampling broad-spectrum antibiotics should be started promptly (Harrison 22e p.913).","Iron overload: each RBC concentrate contains 200-250 mg of iron; in frequently transfused recipients untreated accumulation impairs endocrine, hepatic and cardiac function (death may occur from cardiac failure or arrhythmia), assessed by serum ferritin, MRI and liver biopsy, and prevented/treated by monitoring and iron chelation (Harrison 22e p.912).","Acute hypotensive transfusion reactions are an abrupt drop in systolic blood pressure of more than 30 mmHg early after starting transfusion that resolves once transfusion is stopped; they are more likely in patients taking ACE inhibitors (impaired bradykinin metabolism) and the same product should NOT be restarted (Harrison 22e p.912)."],"pathophysiology":"Adverse reactions to blood components arise via immune or nonimmune mechanisms. Immune-mediated acute haemolysis occurs when recipient preformed antibodies (anti-A/anti-B IgM/IgG, or alloantibodies to Rh, Kell, Duffy) lyse transfused donor RBCs, with complement activation; ABO incompatibility classically results from clerical/identification error. Delayed haemolysis reflects an anamnestic alloantibody response (neoformed allogeneic IgG, C3b-mediated opsonisation and ADCC-mediated phagocytosis) detectable 1-2 weeks later. FNHTR is driven by proinflammatory cytokines accumulated in the component or recipient antibodies against donor leukocyte antigens. Allergic/anaphylactic reactions relate to plasma proteins and soluble mediators triggering histamine/serotonin release (IgA-deficient recipients may react to IgA). TRALI results from donor anti-HLA (class II, also class I) or anti-HNA antibodies binding recipient antigen, or cytokine/chemokine priming, causing neutrophil aggregation in pulmonary vasculature and release of inflammatory mediators producing noncardiogenic pulmonary oedema. TACO is hydrostatic circulatory overload from excessive volume/rate relative to cardiac/renal reserve. Transfusion-associated GVHD is mediated by engrafted donor T lymphocytes attacking host HLA antigens. (Harrison 22e p.906-912)","additionalDifferentials":["Febrile non-haemolytic transfusion reaction (diagnosis of exclusion once infection and haemolysis excluded)","Acute immune-mediated haemolytic transfusion reaction (ABO/clerical error)","Delayed haemolytic transfusion reaction (anamnestic alloantibody response)","Hyperhaemolytic transfusion reaction in haemoglobinopathy/sickle cell disease (bystander haemolysis)","Allergic / anaphylactic transfusion reaction","TRALI (transfusion-related acute lung injury)","TACO (transfusion-associated circulatory overload)","Transfusion-transmitted bacterial infection / septic transfusion reaction","Transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease","Posttransfusion purpura","Acute hypotensive transfusion reaction (bradykinin-related, ACE inhibitor)"],"infectionMimics":["Transfusion-transmitted bacterial contamination/sepsis presenting with abrupt fever, chills, septic shock and DIC can mimic an acute haemolytic reaction (fever over 2 degrees C is a warning sign favouring bacterial contamination/haemolysis)","Septic shock from a contaminated component can mimic anaphylactic or haemolytic shock"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["TRALI may be difficult to distinguish from other causes of hypoxia, particularly circulatory overload (TACO)","G6PD/enzymatic RBC defects and underlying haemolytic conditions exacerbated by transfusion can mimic immune-mediated haemolytic transfusion reaction","Hyperhaemolysis in sickle cell disease can mimic and be exacerbated by transfusion"],"additionalInvestigations":["Direct antiglobulin test (DAT / direct Coombs) detecting immunoglobulin and possibly complement (C3d) on recipient RBCs","Indirect antiglobulin test (IAT / indirect Coombs) detecting anti-RBC alloantibody in serum, with antibody elution to identify the culprit alloantibody","Plasma haptoglobin (depleted), LDH and unconjugated bilirubin (elevated), haemoglobinemia and haemoglobinuria","Repeat ABO determination and thorough clerical check; alloantibody screen and crossmatch","Bacterial culture of the component and recipient before starting broad-spectrum antibiotics when bacterial contamination suspected","Chest x-ray (bilateral interstitial infiltrates in TRALI; predominantly alveolar infiltrates in TACO) and brain natriuretic peptide (elevated in TACO)","Serum ferritin, MRI and liver biopsy to assess iron overload in frequently transfused patients","Test for IgA deficiency in patients with anaphylactic or repeated allergic reactions"],"redFlags":["STOP the transfusion immediately when any adverse reaction is suspected","Fever, flank/back pain and haemoglobinuria suggesting acute haemolytic (ABO-incompatible) reaction - perform an urgent clerical/identification check to prevent a second misidentified transfusion","Hypotension (systolic drop of at least 30 mmHg) - haemolytic, anaphylactic or septic shock","Dyspnoea within 6 h (TRALI) or within 12 h (TACO) of transfusion","Temperature rise over 2 degrees C - favours transfusion-transmitted bacterial infection or haemolysis","Rash over two-thirds of body within 5 minutes with dyspnoea and shock - anaphylaxis","Development of DIC, acute renal failure or shock during/after transfusion"],"prognosis":"Most adverse reactions are non-life-threatening, though serious reactions can present with mild symptoms. An average of ~37 transfusion-associated fatalities (possible-to-definite imputability) were reported yearly to the FDA between 2017-2021 among ~15 million transfused components, with leading causes of death being TACO (32%), TRALI (21%), haemolysis (21%) and microbial contamination (13%). Acute haemolysis is often self-limiting but can be fatal (DIC, acute renal failure, shock). Transfusion-associated GVHD is fatal in over 90% of cases and highly resistant to treatment. (Harrison 22e p.906-911)","pitfalls":["Premedication with antipyretics for FNHTR is generally ineffective and may mask relevant clinical warning symptoms","FNHTR must not be diagnosed until infection and haemolysis are excluded","Failing to perform a clerical/identification check risks a second misidentified (incompatible) transfusion","TRALI and TACO are easily confused; both cause hypoxia and bilateral infiltrates - TACO is now the leading cause of transfusion death and is underrecognised","In acute hypotensive reactions the same blood product should not be restarted; consider switching ACE inhibitor for patients needing further transfusion","Delayed haemolytic reactions occur despite a negative pretransfusion alloantibody screen (low antibody levels) - icterus and worsening anaemia 1-2 weeks later","Iron overload is frequently underreported in chronically transfused patients","Further RBC transfusion in hyperhaemolytic reaction (sickle cell disease) typically exacerbates ongoing haemolysis"],"severityClassification":"Warning-sign based recognition of transfusion adverse reactions (Harrison Table 118-5): fever of at least 38 degrees C (rise of 1-2 degrees C within 4 h favours FNHTR or TRALI; rise over 2 degrees C favours bacterial infection/haemolysis); hypotension (systolic drop of at least 30 mmHg) suggesting haemolytic, anaphylactic or septic shock; dyspnoea (TRALI within 6 h, TACO within 12 h, severe allergy within 4 h); haemoglobinuria (intravascular haemolysis); rash (minor allergy if under two-thirds body, severe if over two-thirds, anaphylaxis if with dyspnoea/shock within 5 min); icterus (delayed haemolysis); rash/diarrhoea/fever 2 days-6 weeks (GVHD); gum bleeding/purpura 5-12 days (posttransfusion purpura). Reactions are also categorised as immune-mediated vs nonimmunologic, and by timing (acute vs delayed). (Harrison 22e p.908)","references":["Harrison 22e p.901","Harrison 22e p.906","Harrison 22e p.908","Harrison 22e p.911","Harrison 22e p.912","Harrison 22e p.913","Delaney M et al: Transfusion reactions: Prevention, diagnosis, and treatment","Panch SR et al: Hemolytic transfusion reactions. N Engl J Med"],"crossLinks":["anaphylaxis","hemolytic_anemia","ards"]},"transient_tachypnea_of_newborn":{"name":"Transient Tachypnea of the Newborn","system":"Respiratory","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TTN","transient tachypnoea of the newborn","wet lung","retained fetal lung fluid"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A common, benign, self-limited cause of respiratory distress in term and late-preterm newborns caused by delayed clearance of fetal lung fluid.","It typically presents soon after birth with tachypnoea, mild grunting, nasal flaring, and mild recession, usually without severe distress.","Delivery by caesarean section (especially without labour), maternal diabetes, and rapid or precipitous delivery are recognised risk factors.","The chest radiograph classically shows prominent perihilar vascular markings, fluid in the interlobar fissures, hyperinflation, and sometimes small pleural effusions.","It is largely a diagnosis of exclusion, and sepsis and pneumonia should be considered, particularly if distress is marked or prolonged.","Management is supportive with oxygen as required and attention to feeding; the condition usually resolves within the first day or two of life.","Oxygen requirement is generally modest, and the need for high oxygen or escalating support should prompt reconsideration of the diagnosis.","Distress persisting well beyond 2-3 days argues against transient tachypnoea and warrants further evaluation."],"pathophysiology":"During fetal life the lungs are filled with fluid that is normally cleared around birth by labour-associated catecholamine and hormonal surges that switch alveolar epithelial cells from fluid secretion to sodium-driven absorption, aided by mechanical squeeze during vaginal delivery. When this clearance is delayed or incomplete, residual fluid remains in the interstitium and air spaces, reducing lung compliance and causing tachypnoea and mild hypoxaemia until the fluid is reabsorbed.","additionalDifferentials":["Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome","Early-onset neonatal pneumonia or sepsis","Meconium aspiration syndrome","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn","Congenital heart disease","Air-leak syndromes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Chest radiograph showing wet-lung features","Pulse oximetry and monitoring of oxygen requirement","Blood gas if distress is significant","Sepsis screen and consideration of antibiotics when infection cannot be excluded","Blood glucose monitoring"],"redFlags":["High or rising oxygen requirement","Distress persisting beyond the first few days of life","Marked recession, grunting, or signs of respiratory failure","Features suggesting sepsis or pneumonia","Differential pre- and post-ductal saturations suggesting pulmonary hypertension or cardiac disease"],"prognosis":"The condition is benign and self-limited, resolving within hours to a few days with supportive care and no long-term respiratory sequelae in the great majority of infants.","pitfalls":["Anchoring on transient tachypnoea and missing early sepsis or pneumonia","Overlooking congenital heart disease presenting with tachypnoea","Failing to reconsider the diagnosis when oxygen needs are high or distress is prolonged","Unnecessarily delaying feeding or investigation in a stable infant","Attributing every caesarean-born tachypnoeic infant to TTN without appropriate observation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"transposition_of_great_arteries":{"name":"Transposition of the Great Arteries","system":"Paediatric cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TGA","d-TGA","transposition of great vessels"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["In d-transposition the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle, so the systemic and pulmonary circulations run in parallel rather than in series.","It is a leading cause of cyanosis presenting in the newborn period, often in an otherwise well-grown, term infant.","Survival depends on mixing between the two parallel circuits through a patent foramen ovale, atrial or ventricular septal defect, or the ductus arteriosus.","Cyanosis is typically profound and, unlike many respiratory causes, does not improve substantially with supplemental oxygen.","A prostaglandin infusion is started to maintain ductal patency and improve intercirculatory mixing while the infant is stabilised.","Balloon atrial septostomy may be performed to enlarge the atrial communication and improve mixing when cyanosis is severe.","Chest radiograph may show an egg-on-a-string cardiac silhouette with a narrow mediastinum, though this is not always present.","Echocardiography confirms the discordant great-artery connections, defines associated defects, and maps the coronary arteries before surgery.","The arterial switch operation, restoring normal ventriculoarterial connections, is the definitive repair and is performed in the neonatal period."],"pathophysiology":"Abnormal division of the truncus arteriosus results in the aorta connecting to the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery to the left ventricle. As a consequence, deoxygenated systemic venous blood is pumped back to the body and oxygenated pulmonary venous blood is returned to the lungs, forming two separate parallel circuits. Life is only possible if oxygenated and deoxygenated blood mix through a communication at the atrial, ventricular, or ductal level. Inadequate mixing produces severe systemic hypoxaemia and progressive metabolic acidosis, which worsens as the ductus arteriosus and foramen ovale close after birth.","additionalDifferentials":["Tetralogy of Fallot","Total anomalous pulmonary venous return","Tricuspid atresia","Truncus arteriosus","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn","Severe respiratory disease causing cyanosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography to confirm ventriculoarterial discordance, mixing sites, and coronary anatomy","Hyperoxia test showing little rise in oxygenation with supplemental oxygen (suggesting a cardiac cause)","Chest radiograph looking for an egg-on-a-string silhouette","Blood gas to assess hypoxaemia and acidosis","Pulse oximetry, including pre- and post-ductal comparison"],"redFlags":["Severe cyanosis in a newborn that fails to improve with supplemental oxygen","Rapid deterioration and acidosis as the ductus arteriosus and foramen ovale close, restricting mixing","Duct-dependent collapse before prostaglandin is established","Worsening metabolic acidosis indicating inadequate systemic oxygen delivery","Inadequate intercirculatory mixing requiring urgent septostomy"],"prognosis":"With prompt stabilisation and an arterial switch operation in the neonatal period, most children do well with good long-term function, though follow-up is required for coronary, valve, and great-artery complications. Without intervention the condition is rapidly fatal.","pitfalls":["Attributing severe neonatal cyanosis to lung disease and delaying cardiac evaluation because oxygen does not help","Failing to start prostaglandin promptly, allowing the ductus to close and mixing to fail","Overlooking the need to enhance mixing (septostomy) in a critically hypoxaemic infant","Assuming a normal chest radiograph excludes the diagnosis","Not mapping coronary anatomy before surgical planning"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"transverse_myelitis":{"name":"Transverse myelitis","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["acute transverse myelitis","ATM","transverse myelopathy"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["An acute or subacute inflammatory myelopathy producing bilateral motor, sensory, and autonomic dysfunction below the level of the lesion","A clearly demarcated sensory level, with bladder and bowel involvement, is a key clue to a spinal cord process rather than a peripheral one","Rapidly evolving cord compression must be excluded urgently with imaging, because it may be surgically treatable and demands emergency intervention","Longitudinally extensive lesions spanning three or more vertebral segments should raise suspicion of neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder and prompt aquaporin-4 antibody testing","It may be idiopathic/post-infectious or the presenting event of multiple sclerosis, NMOSD, MOG-associated disease, or a systemic autoimmune disorder","MRI of the cord with contrast and cerebrospinal fluid analysis are central to diagnosis and to identifying the underlying cause","High-dose corticosteroids are the usual first-line treatment, with plasma exchange considered for severe or steroid-refractory cases","Identifying whether the episode is monophasic or part of a relapsing disorder guides long-term immunotherapy"],"pathophysiology":"Transverse myelitis is focal inflammation of the spinal cord causing demyelination and axonal injury across one or more segments, disrupting ascending sensory and descending motor tracts as well as autonomic pathways. It may follow infection or vaccination as a para- or post-infectious immune response, arise from autoimmune demyelinating disease such as multiple sclerosis, NMOSD, or MOG antibody disease, or occur with systemic autoimmune conditions. Breakdown of the blood-cord barrier and inflammatory cell infiltration produce cord swelling and dysfunction.","additionalDifferentials":["Compressive myelopathy from disc, tumour, abscess, or haematoma","Spinal cord infarction (anterior spinal artery syndrome)","Multiple sclerosis and NMOSD","Infective myelitis (e.g. viral)","Nutritional or metabolic myelopathy such as B12 deficiency","Guillain-Barre syndrome when presenting with ascending weakness"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urgent MRI of the spine with and without contrast to exclude compression and define the inflammatory lesion","MRI of the brain to assess for demyelinating lesions suggesting multiple sclerosis","Cerebrospinal fluid analysis including cell count, protein, oligoclonal bands, and infective studies","Serum aquaporin-4 and MOG antibodies","Autoimmune and infective serology, and vitamin B12, as guided by the clinical picture"],"redFlags":["Rapidly progressive weakness with a sensory level and sphincter disturbance requiring emergency MRI to exclude compressive cord lesions amenable to surgery","Ascending deficit approaching the cervical cord threatening respiratory function","New bladder or bowel dysfunction and saddle sensory loss indicating significant cord involvement","Fever and back pain with neurological deficit suggesting epidural abscess as a surgical emergency"],"prognosis":"Outcome is variable and depends on cause, severity, and rapidity of onset. Some patients recover substantially, while others are left with significant residual deficits. Recurrent disease and poorer recovery are more typical of NMOSD and longitudinally extensive lesions, underscoring the importance of accurate aetiological diagnosis and timely immunotherapy.","pitfalls":["Delaying MRI and missing a compressive lesion that requires emergency decompression","Failing to test for aquaporin-4 and MOG antibodies and misclassifying the disorder","Attributing the presentation to a peripheral cause and overlooking the sensory level and cord signs","Not investigating for an infective cause before or alongside immunosuppression when indicated"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"traumatic_brain_injury":{"name":"Traumatic brain injury","system":"Emergency / Trauma / Critical care","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TBI","head injury","traumatic head injury"],"chapter":null,"source":"ATLS / Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine (critical care)","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Severity is graded with the Glasgow Coma Scale, and a trend in the score matters more than any single reading","The overriding aim is to prevent secondary brain injury by avoiding hypoxia and hypotension, which independently worsen outcome","Maintain adequate oxygenation and cerebral perfusion pressure while controlling intracranial pressure","Unequal or fixed dilated pupils, deteriorating consciousness, and a lateralising motor deficit suggest an expanding mass lesion and impending herniation","Cushing's response (hypertension with bradycardia) is a late and ominous sign of dangerously raised intracranial pressure","Early CT imaging identifies surgically evacuable lesions such as extradural and subdural haematomas","Head-injured patients frequently have concomitant injuries, and hypotension should prompt a search for extracranial bleeding rather than being attributed to the brain injury alone","Basic measures to lower intracranial pressure include head-of-bed elevation, avoiding venous obstruction, and preventing fever, seizures, and hypercarbia"],"pathophysiology":"The primary injury is the mechanical damage sustained at impact, which cannot be reversed. Secondary injury develops over hours to days from hypoxia, hypotension, oedema, haematoma expansion, seizures, and disturbed autoregulation. Because the skull is a fixed compartment, expanding blood or oedema raises intracranial pressure; once compensatory mechanisms are exhausted, small volume increases cause steep pressure rises. Rising intracranial pressure reduces cerebral perfusion pressure and drives ischaemia, and pressure gradients can force brain tissue across dural folds or the foramen magnum, producing herniation and brainstem compression.","additionalDifferentials":["Ischaemic or haemorrhagic stroke presenting with a fall or collapse","Hypoglycaemia or other metabolic causes of reduced consciousness","Intoxication or drug overdose","Post-ictal state after seizure","Non-traumatic intracranial haemorrhage (e.g. aneurysmal subarachnoid haemorrhage)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Non-contrast CT head to detect haemorrhage, contusion, oedema, and skull fracture","Cervical spine imaging given the frequent association of head and neck injury","Blood glucose and coagulation studies, including reversal assessment for anticoagulated patients","Invasive intracranial pressure monitoring in selected severe cases","Repeat imaging if neurological status deteriorates or bleeding may progress"],"redFlags":["Falling GCS or a drop of two or more points indicating deterioration","Unequal, dilated, or unreactive pupil suggesting uncal herniation from a mass lesion needing urgent decompression","Cushing's response (hypertension with bradycardia) signalling critically raised intracranial pressure","New focal neurological deficit or seizures","Hypoxia or hypotension, which must be corrected immediately to limit secondary injury"],"prognosis":"Outcome ranges from full recovery in mild injury to death or severe disability in severe injury. Prevention of secondary insults, timely evacuation of surgical lesions, and control of intracranial pressure improve results, whereas sustained raised pressure and low cerebral perfusion predict poor recovery.","pitfalls":["Attributing hypotension to the brain injury and overlooking extracranial haemorrhage","Allowing hypoxia, hypercarbia, or hypotension that aggravate secondary injury","Missing an expanding haematoma by not repeating imaging after clinical deterioration","Over-sedating so that neurological reassessment becomes impossible","Failing to reverse anticoagulation promptly in a bleeding head injury"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["ATLS (Advanced Trauma Life Support)","Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"trichinellosis_and_other_tissue_nematode":{"name":"Trichinellosis and Other Tissue Nematode Infections","system":"Infectious Disease (Parasitology / Tissue Helminths)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Trichinosis","Trichinella infection","Tissue nematode infections","Visceral larva migrans","Ocular larva migrans","Toxocariasis","Cutaneous larva migrans","Creeping eruption","Cerebral angiostrongyliasis","Rat lungworm disease","Gnathostomiasis"],"chapter":238,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1810","clinicalPearls":["Trichinellosis follows ingestion of meat (classically pork, but increasingly wild game such as bear and walrus) containing encysted Trichinella larvae; most infections are mild or asymptomatic, but heavy burdens can be life-threatening (Harrison 22e p.1810).","Disease severity tracks larval burden: light infections (<10 larvae/g muscle) tend to be asymptomatic, whereas heavy infections (>50 larvae/g muscle) can be life-threatening (Harrison 22e p.1811).","Clinical course is biphasic: an early enteric phase (diarrhea, abdominal pain, constipation, nausea) in the first weeks, followed in the second week by larval-migration symptoms (fever, periorbital/facial edema, eosinophilia) and then a myositis phase as larvae encyst (Harrison 22e p.1811).","Periorbital and facial edema with subconjunctival, retinal, and nail-bed (splinter) hemorrhages are characteristic of the migratory phase of trichinellosis (Harrison 22e p.1811).","Most deaths in trichinellosis result from myocarditis (with tachyarrhythmias or heart failure) and, less commonly, encephalitis or pneumonitis (Harrison 22e p.1811).","Muscle involvement preferentially affects the extraocular muscles, biceps, and the jaw, neck, lower back, and diaphragm muscles, with symptoms peaking around 3 weeks (Harrison 22e p.1811).","Visceral larva migrans (most often Toxocara canis) chiefly affects preschool children who ingest soil contaminated with embryonated eggs, producing fever, hepatosplenomegaly, wheezing, and extraordinary eosinophilia that can approach 90% (Harrison 22e p.1812).","Ocular larva migrans typically occurs in older children/young adults without pica and usually lacks eosinophilia or visceral signs; the retinal granuloma can mimic retinoblastoma and lead to unnecessary enucleation if misdiagnosed (Harrison 22e p.1812).","Cutaneous larva migrans ('creeping eruption'), usually from the dog/cat hookworm Ancylostoma braziliense, produces an intensely pruritic serpiginous track that advances several centimeters per day; the larvae cannot mature in humans and self-resolve over weeks to a couple of months (Harrison 22e p.1812).","Angiostrongylus cantonensis (rat lungworm) is the most common cause of human eosinophilic meningitis; CSF shows elevated opening pressure, pleocytosis (150-2000 cells/uL) with eosinophilic pleocytosis >20%, elevated protein, and normal glucose (Harrison 22e p.1813).","Gnathostomiasis is acquired from raw fish/poultry (e.g., som fak, sashimi) and causes migratory, recurrent pruritic cutaneous swellings; CNS invasion produces eosinophilic myeloencephalitis with agonizing radicular pain followed by paraplegia, and cerebral involvement is often fatal (Harrison 22e p.1813).","Stool examination is worthless in toxocariasis because the larvae never develop into egg-producing adults in the human host (Harrison 22e p.1812)."],"pathophysiology":"These are zoonotic tissue-dwelling nematode infections arising from incidental human exposure; symptoms derive largely from invasive larval stages that (except for Trichinella) do not mature in humans. In trichinellosis, ingested encysted larvae are freed by gastric acid and proteases, invade small-bowel mucosa, mature to adults, and after about one week females release newborn larvae that migrate via the circulation to striated muscle, where most species encyst by radically transforming muscle-cell architecture into a 'nurse cell'; host immune responses help expel adult intestinal worms but have little effect on muscle larvae. Migrating larvae provoke a marked local and systemic hypersensitivity reaction with eosinophilia. In visceral larva migrans, ingested Toxocara eggs hatch, penetrate the gut, and disseminate to liver, lungs, CNS, and other tissues, eliciting intense eosinophilic granulomatous inflammation. In cutaneous larva migrans, animal hookworm larvae penetrate skin and burrow along the dermal-epidermal junction. In angiostrongyliasis, ingested infective larvae migrate to the brain and die there, inciting eosinophilic inflammation, hemorrhage, necrosis, and granuloma formation. In gnathostomiasis, aberrant migration of a single larva into cutaneous, visceral, neural, or ocular tissue causes local inflammation, including ascending migration along nerve tracts producing myeloencephalitis (Harrison 22e p.1810-1813).","additionalDifferentials":["Polymyositis / dermatomyositis (myalgia, weakness, elevated muscle enzymes) - note T. pseudospiralis can mimic a prolonged polymyositis-like illness","Periorbital edema from other causes (allergic, nephrotic, angioedema)","Acute myocarditis of other etiology","Eosinophilic meningitis from other parasites (see infectionMimics)","Retinoblastoma (mimicked by ocular toxocariasis retinal granuloma)","Other hypereosinophilic syndromes"],"infectionMimics":["Other causes of eosinophilic meningitis in endemic areas: gnathostomiasis, paragonimiasis, schistosomiasis, neurocysticercosis, and coccidioidomycosis","Other eosinophilia-associated helminthiases (e.g., strongyloidiasis, filariasis)","Pleurodynia (coxsackievirus B) and bacterial myositis as causes of myalgia overlapping with trichinellosis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Idiopathic inflammatory myopathies (polymyositis, dermatomyositis)","Retinoblastoma (versus ocular larva migrans)","Allergic/angioedematous periorbital swelling","Idiopathic hypereosinophilic syndrome"],"additionalInvestigations":["Peripheral blood eosinophilia (develops in >90% of symptomatic trichinellosis, may peak at >50% at 2-4 weeks)","Serum muscle enzymes including creatine phosphokinase (elevated in most symptomatic trichinellosis)","Trichinella parasite-specific serology (titer rise usually only after the third week of infection)","Muscle biopsy (>=1 g, near tendon insertions in symptomatic muscle) with fresh tissue compressed between glass slides and examined microscopically, since routine histology may miss larvae","ELISA for toxocaral antibodies (may be negative in ocular larva migrans); leukocytosis and hypergammaglobulinemia in visceral larva migrans; chest x-ray for transient pulmonary infiltrates","CSF examination for angiostrongyliasis (opening pressure, cell count, eosinophilic pleocytosis, protein, glucose); A. cantonensis PCR on CSF/tissue (available via CDC and Hawaii Dept of Public Health)"],"redFlags":["Myocarditis with tachyarrhythmias or heart failure (leading cause of death in trichinellosis)","Encephalitis or pneumonitis in trichinellosis","Dysphagia, dyspnea, or diaphragmatic muscle involvement","Severe neurologic, pneumonic, or myocardial involvement in visceral larva migrans (rare deaths)","Ascending radicular pain and paresthesias followed by paraplegia in gnathostomiasis (myeloencephalitis)","Cerebral involvement with focal hemorrhages in gnathostomiasis (often fatal)","Permanent neurologic sequelae from heavy CNS angiostrongyliasis"],"prognosis":"Most lightly infected trichinellosis patients recover uneventfully, with symptoms peaking near 3 weeks and subsiding gradually over a prolonged convalescence; heavy infections can be life-threatening, with death usually due to myocarditis. Most visceral larva migrans infections are self-limited (rare deaths from severe organ involvement). Cutaneous larva migrans self-resolves over weeks to a couple of months as the larvae die. Cerebral angiostrongyliasis is usually self-limited with complete recovery, though heavy infections can leave permanent neurologic sequelae or cause death. Gnathostomiasis cutaneous swellings can recur intermittently over years; CNS gnathostomiasis is a serious complication and cerebral involvement is often fatal (Harrison 22e p.1811-1813).","pitfalls":["Most mild trichinellosis cases remain undiagnosed; a high index of suspicion and a careful dietary/exposure history are needed.","Trichinella serology is typically negative until after the third week, so a presumptive clinical diagnosis (fever, eosinophilia, periorbital edema, myalgia after a suspect meal) may be required early.","Stool examination is useless in toxocariasis because larvae do not mature into egg-producing adults in humans.","Ocular toxocariasis can be mistaken for retinoblastoma, risking unnecessary enucleation; it often lacks eosinophilia and visceral signs.","In angiostrongyliasis, A. cantonensis larvae are only rarely seen in CSF and serologic tests are unstandardized and may be negative at symptom onset.","Arctic T. nativa larvae in walrus or bear meat are relatively freeze-resistant and may remain viable despite freezing, so usual freezing prevention may fail.","Gnathostomiasis may present with ocular or CNS involvement without antecedent cutaneous swellings."],"severityClassification":"Trichinellosis severity correlates with muscle larval burden: light/asymptomatic infection <10 larvae per gram of muscle; heavy, potentially life-threatening infection >50 larvae per gram of muscle. Clinically graded as mild, moderate, and severe (severe = significant myositis/myocarditis) per Harrison's tissue nematode framework (Harrison 22e p.1811-1812).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1810","Harrison 22e p.1811","Harrison 22e p.1812","Harrison 22e p.1813"],"crossLinks":[]},"tricuspid_valve_disease":{"name":"Tricuspid Valve Disease","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Tricuspid stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation","TS","TR"],"chapter":277,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2059","clinicalPearls":["Tricuspid stenosis is far less common than mitral stenosis in North America and Western Europe, is usually rheumatic in origin, occurs more often in women, and essentially never appears as an isolated lesion (it is typically combined with mitral stenosis) (Harrison 22e p.2059).","Hemodynamically significant tricuspid stenosis is seen in roughly 5-10% of patients with severe mitral stenosis, and rheumatic tricuspid stenosis is commonly accompanied by some tricuspid regurgitation; nonrheumatic causes of stenosis are rare (Harrison 22e p.2059).","More than 85% of tricuspid regurgitation encountered clinically is secondary (functional), arising from tricuspid annular dilation and leaflet tethering in the setting of RV remodeling from pressure/volume overload, MI, or trauma rather than from intrinsic valve disease (Harrison 22e p.2060).","New-onset atrial fibrillation, especially in older patients, can produce 'atrial functional' tricuspid regurgitation through atrial and annular remodeling (Harrison 22e p.2060-2061).","Patients with severe tricuspid stenosis characteristically complain of relatively little dyspnea given the marked hepatomegaly, ascites, and edema present, because the right-sided obstruction limits pulmonary congestion (Harrison 22e p.2059).","Tricuspid regurgitation is more often detected by inspecting the neck veins (prominent c-v waves with rapid y descent) than by cardiac auscultation (Harrison 22e p.2061).","Carvallo's sign - inspiratory accentuation of the holosystolic murmur at the lower left sternal border - helps distinguish the murmur of tricuspid regurgitation from that of mitral regurgitation; it diminishes on expiration or during the Valsalva strain phase (Harrison 22e p.2061).","The murmur of tricuspid stenosis resembles that of mitral stenosis but is best heard at the lower left sternal border/xiphoid, is most prominent in presystole during sinus rhythm, and increases with inspiration while decreasing during expiration and Valsalva strain (Harrison 22e p.2060).","In tricuspid stenosis with sinus rhythm, the right atrial a wave can be enormous (approaching RV systolic pressure) and the y descent is prolonged; presystolic pulsations of an enlarged liver may also be palpable (Harrison 22e p.2059).","On ECG, absence of right ventricular hypertrophy in a patient with right-sided heart failure who is presumed to have mitral stenosis should raise suspicion for coexisting tricuspid valve disease; right atrial enlargement gives tall peaked P waves in lead II and prominent upright P waves in V1 (Harrison 22e p.2060).","A distinct category of tricuspid regurgitation is caused by cardiac implantable electronic device (CIED) leads crossing the valve, producing leaflet entrapment or perforation; right ventricular apical pacing can additionally cause dyssynchronous contraction and functional TR (Harrison 22e p.2060-2061).","Coexisting tricuspid stenosis can mask the hemodynamic and clinical features of associated mitral stenosis by lowering cardiac output and keeping left atrial, pulmonary artery, and RV systolic pressures only normal or mildly elevated (Harrison 22e p.2059)."],"pathophysiology":"Tricuspid stenosis is defined by a diastolic pressure gradient between the right atrium and right ventricle that increases with inspiration (greater transvalvular flow) and falls with expiration. A mean diastolic gradient of about 4 mmHg is usually enough to raise mean right atrial pressure to levels causing systemic venous congestion - hepatomegaly, ascites, and edema. Resting cardiac output is typically depressed and fails to rise with exercise. In tricuspid regurgitation, the incompetent valve allows backward flow from RV to RA, with the regurgitant volume governed by RV systolic driving pressure and the size of the regurgitant orifice; severity is modulated by pulmonary artery systolic pressure, annular dimensions, respiratory changes in RV preload, and RA compliance. Significant TR enlarges the right atrium, raises RA and jugular venous pressures with prominent c-v waves, can 'ventricularize' the RA waveform, and leads to RV volume overload, dilation, and eventual systolic dysfunction - accelerated by any added pressure load from pulmonary hypertension or prior myocardial fibrosis (Harrison 22e p.2059-2061).","additionalDifferentials":["Mitral stenosis (frequently coexists and dominates the picture)","Mitral regurgitation (murmur can be confused with TR without attention to respiratory variation)","Constrictive pericarditis / restrictive cardiomyopathy as a cause of right-sided venous congestion","Other causes of right heart failure with hepatic congestion and ascites"],"infectionMimics":["Infective endocarditis of the tricuspid valve causing primary regurgitation"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Carcinoid heart disease causing tricuspid leaflet retraction and regurgitation","Ebstein's malformation and other congenital tricuspid deformities","Myxomatous tricuspid valve prolapse","Radiation-induced valve disease","Endomyocardial fibrosis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Transthoracic echocardiography (Doppler) - assesses leaflet thickening/doming, transvalvular gradient, RA and IVC size, associated TR severity, mitral and LV/RV function, and PA pressure","Continuous-wave Doppler of the TR jet velocity to estimate PA systolic pressure (may be blunted when TR is very severe)","ECG for right atrial enlargement, RVH, prior inferior/RV MI pattern, atrial fibrillation, or preexcitation in Ebstein's anomaly","Chest x-ray - RA and superior vena cava prominence with azygos engorgement in combined TS/MS; RA/RV enlargement in TR","Real-time three-dimensional echocardiography and cardiac MR for difficult assessment of TR severity, PA pressures, and RV size/function","Exercise testing to assess functional capacity in asymptomatic severe TR","Cardiac catheterization - not routinely needed for TS assessment"],"redFlags":["Severe hepatic congestion progressing to cirrhosis, jaundice, serious malnutrition, anasarca, and ascites in severe tricuspid valve disease","Persistent right-sided failure after an adequate mitral commissurotomy, suggesting unrecognized tricuspid stenosis","Progressive RV dilation and declining systolic function in severe TR","Marked reduction in cardiac output with prominent c-v wave and rapid y descent and loss of the x descent in severe TR"],"prognosis":"Mild-to-moderate tricuspid regurgitation is generally well tolerated when there is no other hemodynamic disturbance. Because TR commonly coexists with left-sided valve lesions, LV dysfunction, or pulmonary hypertension, those conditions often dominate the clinical course. As isolated severe TR progresses, reduced forward cardiac output causes fatigue and exertional dyspnea, and declining RV function leads to cervical pulsations, abdominal fullness, anorexia, muscle wasting, weight gain, and painful lower-extremity swelling. Severe TR is associated with markedly reduced cardiac output and elevated mean RA and RV end-diastolic pressures (Harrison 22e p.2061-2062).","pitfalls":["Tricuspid stenosis is easily overlooked because it almost always occurs alongside obvious mitral disease and its diastolic murmur resembles that of mitral stenosis - the diagnosis must be deliberately considered (Harrison 22e p.2059-2060).","The TR murmur may be mistaken for mitral regurgitation unless its inspiratory accentuation and the degree of RV enlargement are appreciated (Harrison 22e p.2061).","Accurate echocardiographic quantification of TR severity, PA pressure, and RV size/function is genuinely difficult in many patients (Harrison 22e p.2062).","Continuous-wave Doppler estimation of PA systolic pressure can be falsely low when TR is very severe because the jet velocity is blunted by rapidly rising RA pressure (Harrison 22e p.2062)."],"severityClassification":"Severe tricuspid stenosis is defined echocardiographically by a tricuspid valve area of 1 cm2 or less, or a pressure half-time of 190 ms or more; a mean diastolic gradient around 4 mmHg is hemodynamically significant. For tricuspid regurgitation, severity is staged (progressive Stage B, asymptomatic severe Stage C, symptomatic severe Stage D); annular dilation is defined as >40 mm (>21 mm/m2) on transthoracic echocardiography or >70 mm on direct intraoperative measurement, and severe TR is accompanied by hepatic vein systolic flow reversal (Harrison 22e p.2060-2062).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2059","Harrison 22e p.2060","Harrison 22e p.2061","Harrison 22e p.2062"],"crossLinks":[]},"tricyclic_antidepressant_overdose":{"name":"Tricyclic antidepressant overdose","system":"Toxicology / Environmental","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["TCA overdose","tricyclic poisoning","amitriptyline overdose","tricyclic antidepressant toxicity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO & national toxicology guidance","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Toxicity combines an anticholinergic toxidrome (dry flushed skin, dilated pupils, urinary retention, agitation, delirium) with the far more dangerous cardiovascular and neurological effects","Sodium-channel blockade widens the QRS complex; a broad QRS is the key marker of cardiotoxicity and predicts arrhythmia and seizures","A prominent terminal R wave in lead aVR is a supportive ECG sign of sodium-channel blockade","Life-threatening features include ventricular arrhythmias, hypotension from myocardial depression and vasodilatation, seizures, and rapid deterioration in conscious level","Intravenous sodium bicarbonate is the cornerstone treatment for a widening QRS, ventricular arrhythmia, or hypotension, working by sodium loading and alkalinisation","Deterioration can be abrupt; a patient who looks well early can rapidly progress to coma, seizures, and cardiac arrest, so early cardiac monitoring is essential","Class Ia and Ic antiarrhythmics are contraindicated as they aggravate sodium-channel blockade; refractory arrhythmia may be treated with additional bicarbonate and supportive measures","Intravenous lipid emulsion may be considered as a rescue therapy in refractory cardiovascular collapse"],"pathophysiology":"Tricyclic antidepressants exert several toxic actions. Blockade of fast cardiac sodium channels slows myocardial depolarisation, widening the QRS and predisposing to ventricular arrhythmia and myocardial depression; this effect is worsened by acidosis. Anticholinergic (antimuscarinic) activity produces the classic peripheral and central anticholinergic features. Antagonism of peripheral alpha-adrenergic receptors causes vasodilatation and hypotension, and central effects plus GABA and other receptor actions lower the seizure threshold. Seizures worsen acidosis, which in turn intensifies sodium-channel blockade in a dangerous positive-feedback loop.","additionalDifferentials":["Other sodium-channel-blocking drug toxicity (some antiarrhythmics, carbamazepine)","Anticholinergic poisoning from other agents","Sympathomimetic toxicity","Other causes of wide-complex tachycardia","Hyperkalaemia causing QRS widening"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serial 12-lead ECG focusing on QRS width and the aVR terminal R wave","Continuous cardiac monitoring","Arterial blood gas and serum electrolytes including potassium","Capillary glucose and paracetamol/salicylate co-ingestant screen","Creatine kinase if prolonged seizures or immobility raise concern for rhabdomyolysis"],"redFlags":["QRS widening on the ECG indicating significant cardiotoxicity","Ventricular arrhythmia or haemodynamic instability","Seizures or rapidly declining conscious level","Refractory hypotension unresponsive to fluids","Rapid clinical deterioration in a patient who initially appeared stable"],"prognosis":"Most deaths occur early from arrhythmia, refractory hypotension, or seizures, often before or soon after arrival; patients who reach hospital and receive prompt bicarbonate and supportive care and survive the first day generally recover well.","pitfalls":["Being reassured by an initially well-appearing patient and delaying continuous cardiac monitoring","Using class Ia or Ic antiarrhythmics, which worsen sodium-channel blockade","Failing to give sodium bicarbonate promptly for a widening QRS or arrhythmia","Allowing acidosis from seizures or hypoventilation to persist and aggravate cardiotoxicity"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO/national toxicology guidance"],"crossLinks":[]},"trigeminal_neuralgia_bell_s_palsy":{"name":"Trigeminal Neuralgia, Bell’s Palsy, and Other Cranial Nerve Disorders","system":"Nervous system","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Tic douloureux","Idiopathic facial paralysis","Cranial neuropathy","Glossopharyngeal neuralgia","Cavernous sinus syndrome","Tolosa-Hunt syndrome"],"chapter":452,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3551","clinicalPearls":["Trigeminal neuralgia produces brief (seconds to a minute or two) paroxysms of excruciating, superficial, stabbing pain usually in the lips, gums, cheek, or chin (V2/V3); ophthalmic-division (V1) involvement is rare. Attacks may be triggered by light tactile stimuli at facial, lip, or tongue trigger zones (washing, brushing teeth, a draft of air) and by speaking, chewing, or smiling.","An essential diagnostic feature of classic trigeminal neuralgia is the ABSENCE of demonstrable objective sensory loss on examination; demonstrable trigeminal sensory or motor deficit instead points to a structural trigeminal neuropathy or secondary cause.","Trigeminal neuralgia is relatively common (incidence ~4–8 per 100,000/year), affects mainly middle-aged and elderly persons, and roughly 60% of cases occur in women; bouts persist for weeks to months and tend to remit then recur.","When trigeminal neuralgia occurs in a young adult or is bilateral, suspect multiple sclerosis (demyelinating plaque near the trigeminal root entry zone in the pons), especially if subtle facial sensory loss is present.","Bell’s palsy is the most common form of facial paralysis (annual incidence ~25 per 100,000; lifetime risk ~1 in 60); onset is abrupt with maximal weakness by 48 h, often preceded by retroauricular pain for a day or two, and may include loss of taste and hyperacusis.","A peripheral (lower motor neuron) facial palsy weakens the whole hemiface including the forehead and eyelid closure, whereas a supranuclear (central) lesion relatively spares the forehead and orbicularis oculi because upper-face muscles receive bilateral corticobulbar innervation.","Localizing the seventh-nerve lesion: loss of taste over the anterior two-thirds of the tongue points to a middle-ear-segment lesion; hyperacusis indicates involvement of the nerve to stapedius; accompanying deafness, tinnitus, or vertigo suggests an internal auditory meatus lesion affecting the eighth nerve.","Glossopharyngeal neuralgia resembles trigeminal neuralgia but is much less common; pain is paroxysmal and intense, arises in the tonsillar fossa/throat, may radiate to the ear, and is provoked by swallowing or coughing; rarely it triggers bradycardia/asystole, hypotension, and syncope.","Anatomic clustering of cranial nerves yields recognizable syndromes: jugular foramen (IX, X, XI), cavernous sinus (III, IV, V1, VI), pontocerebellar angle (V, VI, VII, VIII), and retroparotid space (IX, X, XI, XII plus Horner’s).","Cavernous sinus syndrome is frequently life-threatening: orbital/facial pain, orbital swelling, chemosis, fever, ophthalmoplegia (III, IV, VI), and V1 (±V2) trigeminal involvement—cavernous sinus thrombosis from facial/orbital/sinus infection (including mucormycosis in diabetics) is the most common cause and demands early diagnosis.","Tolosa-Hunt syndrome (idiopathic granulomatous cavernous-sinus/orbital inflammation) causes painful ophthalmoparesis with V1/V2 numbness and shows a dramatic response to glucocorticoids, but its picture can be mimicked by sarcoid, vasculitis, IgG4-related disease, lymphoma, and fungal infection—so confirm before attributing to it.","An eleventh-nerve (spinal accessory) palsy causes little overt neck weakness because other muscles assist head turning; detection relies on palpating absent sternocleidomastoid contraction during head turning and on noting a lower shoulder, scapular winging, and inability to abduct the arm beyond 90 degrees."],"pathophysiology":"The cranial nerves are 12 paired nerves with mixed motor, sensory, and autonomic functions linked by their close brainstem relationships; nine connect directly to brainstem nuclei, while CN I and II are effectively brain fiber tracts and CN XI arises largely from upper cervical cord. In trigeminal neuralgia, symptoms arise from ectopic action-potential generation in pain-sensitive afferents of the fifth-nerve root just proximal to its entry at the lateral pons; demyelination of large myelinated (non-nociceptive) fibers renders them hyperexcitable and electrically coupled to adjacent small unmyelinated/poorly myelinated pain fibers, so tactile input can ignite paroxysms of pain. Compression of the root by a blood vessel—most often the superior cerebellar artery (or a tortuous vein)—is implicated in most cases; age-related brain sagging and increased vascular tortuosity may explain its predilection for later life. The trigeminal nerve carries oligodendrocyte-derived (central-type) myelin for up to ~7 mm beyond the brainstem, which may underlie trigeminal neuralgia in MS. In Bell’s palsy there is inflammation of the facial nerve with mononuclear cell infiltration suggesting an infectious/immune mechanism; reactivation of herpes simplex virus type 1 in the geniculate ganglion is thought to cause most cases, with varicella-zoster reactivation implicated in up to one-third (Ramsay Hunt syndrome). The vagus and other lower cranial nerves can be injured along their medullary, meningeal, foraminal, or extracranial courses; the recurrent laryngeal nerve (especially the left) is most often damaged by intrathoracic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Migraine and cluster headache (deep, steady pain rather than superficial stabbing; cluster-tic overlap exists)","Temporal (giant cell) arteritis (superficial facial pain with myalgias/systemic symptoms and elevated ESR/CRP)","Short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache (SUNCT/SUNA) and paroxysmal hemicrania","Dental, jaw, or sinus disease as a source of face pain","Trigeminal neuropathy from tumor (meningioma, schwannoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, metastasis), Sjögren’s/connective-tissue disease, sarcoidosis, leprosy","Supranuclear facial palsy from stroke/structural hemispheric lesion (forehead-sparing, often with limb weakness or aphasia)","Lyme disease facial palsy (unilateral or bilateral) in endemic areas","Guillain-Barré syndrome (often bilateral facial palsy; Fisher variant with ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, areflexia)","Myasthenia gravis for pure motor cranial weakness without atrophy","Polymyositis/dermatomyositis and myotonic dystrophy mimicking vagal dysphagia/dysphonia","Progressive bulbar palsy (motor neuron disease) for multiple lower cranial motor involvement"],"infectionMimics":["Ramsay Hunt syndrome (geniculate herpes zoster) mimicking Bell’s palsy, with auricular/pharyngeal vesicles and often eighth-nerve involvement","Lyme disease (Borrelia burgdorferi) causing uni- or bilateral facial palsy","HIV seroconversion-associated facial palsy","Herpes zoster involving the trigeminal ganglion (acute or postherpetic) or lower cranial nerves","Leprosy involving the facial or trigeminal nerve","Chronic/carcinomatous meningitis, tuberculosis, otitis media/mastoiditis spread to the semilunar ganglion","Diphtheria affecting the pharyngeal branches of the vagus (nasal voice, nasal regurgitation)","Mucormycosis and other invasive fungal infections at the cavernous sinus/orbital apex (especially in diabetics)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Sarcoidosis (often bilateral facial palsy; cause of multiple cranial neuropathy)","Tolosa-Hunt syndrome and other idiopathic granulomatous orbital/cavernous inflammation","IgG4-related disease, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, and other vasculitides","Lymphoma and metastatic/locally infiltrating tumors (nasopharyngeal carcinoma, neurofibroma, meningioma, chordoma, cholesteatoma)","Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome (recurrent facial palsy, recurrent facial/labial edema, fissured tongue)","Diabetes mellitus, amyloidosis, and connective-tissue/collagen-vascular disease (e.g., Sjögren’s, SLE, scleroderma, MCTD)","Skull-base and posterior-fossa structural lesions (acoustic neuroma, platybasia, basilar invagination, Paget’s disease, Chiari malformation)","Carotid-cavernous fistula or carotid aneurysm","Temporal bone fracture or blunt head injury","Iatrogenic/traumatic injury (neck surgery, jugular vein cannulation, carotid endarterectomy/dissection, post-intubation Tapia syndrome)"],"additionalInvestigations":["ESR and/or CRP when temporal arteritis is suspected as a cause of facial pain or in atypical facial palsy","Neuroimaging (MRI/MR angiography) to exclude secondary causes of trigeminal neuralgia and to map the relationship of the fifth-nerve root to adjacent vessels for surgical planning","MRI of the facial nerve, which in idiopathic Bell’s palsy often shows smooth linear enhancement and swelling of the geniculate ganglion and facial nerve (genu, tympanic, mastoid segments); similar enhancement can occur in Lyme disease, sarcoidosis, and perineural malignant spread","Electromyography for prognosis in Bell’s palsy (denervation evident after 10 days indicates axonal degeneration and a longer, possibly incomplete recovery)","In atypical/uncertain facial palsy: testing for diabetes mellitus, Lyme serology, HIV serology, ACE level with chest imaging for sarcoidosis, and lumbar puncture if Guillain-Barré is suspected","Lumbar puncture/CSF examination (mild CSF lymphocytosis may occur in Bell’s palsy; useful for meningeal/inflammatory causes)","Laryngoscopy and chest/mediastinal imaging for vocal cord palsy to evaluate intrathoracic causes of recurrent laryngeal nerve injury","Biopsy when granulomatous/infiltrative cavernous sinus or orbital apex disease (e.g., Tolosa-Hunt mimics, IgG4-related disease) is suspected"],"redFlags":["Objective trigeminal sensory loss, jaw weakness, or jaw deviation accompanying facial pain (suggests structural trigeminal neuropathy rather than classic neuralgia)","Bilateral trigeminal neuralgia or onset in a young adult (consider multiple sclerosis)","Isolated numbness over the chin (mental neuropathy), which can be the sole manifestation of systemic malignancy","Forehead-sparing facial weakness with limb weakness or aphasia (central/supranuclear lesion such as stroke)","Facial palsy with vesicles in the ear canal/palate, severe pain, or eighth-nerve signs (Ramsay Hunt syndrome)","Slowly progressive or insidious facial weakness (suggests tumor invading the temporal bone rather than Bell’s palsy)","Orbital/facial pain with orbital swelling, chemosis, fever, and ophthalmoplegia (cavernous sinus thrombosis—life-threatening)","Glossopharyngeal neuralgia with bradycardia, asystole, hypotension, or syncope","Successive involvement of multiple cranial nerves, especially with bone erosion or foraminal enlargement (infiltrating tumor, granulomatous or neoplastic meningitis)","Corneal anesthesia (loss of V1 corneal sensation) increasing risk of ulceration (neurokeratitis)"],"prognosis":"Trigeminal neuralgia characteristically remits spontaneously after bouts lasting weeks to months, and remissions can be long-lasting, though the disorder ultimately recurs in most patients. In Bell’s palsy, about 80% of patients recover within a few weeks to months; incomplete paralysis during the first week is the most favorable prognostic sign, whereas EMG evidence of denervation after 10 days predicts axonal degeneration with delayed (typically ~3 months) and possibly incomplete recovery, and recurrences occur in roughly 7% of cases. Idiopathic trigeminal neuropathy usually recovers gradually. Most patients with isolated spinal accessory neuropathy recover. Post-intubation Tapia syndrome (vagal plus hypoglossal palsy) usually resolves within a few months.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing classic trigeminal neuralgia despite objective trigeminal sensory loss—such findings indicate trigeminal neuropathy from a structural/secondary cause (aneurysm, neurofibroma, acoustic schwannoma, meningioma).","Overlooking multiple sclerosis as the cause of trigeminal neuralgia in a young adult or when symptoms are bilateral.","Mistaking a central (supranuclear) forehead-sparing facial weakness for peripheral Bell’s palsy and missing a stroke or hemispheric lesion.","Failing to examine the eighth and other cranial nerves in a patient with facial palsy—the eighth nerve runs close to the facial nerve at the pontomedullary junction and in the temporal bone.","Attributing an insidious, progressive facial weakness to Bell’s palsy when it actually reflects a temporal bone or pontine tumor.","Missing Ramsay Hunt syndrome by not inspecting the external auditory canal and palate for zoster vesicles.","Misdiagnosing vagal/laryngeal or pharyngeal muscle disease (polymyositis, dermatomyositis, myotonic dystrophy) as a primary vagus nerve lesion in dysphagia/dysphonia.","Searching intracranially for an isolated vocal cord palsy when intrathoracic disease (aortic aneurysm, enlarged left atrium, mediastinal/bronchial tumor) is the far more common cause.","Delaying recognition and treatment of cavernous sinus thrombosis, an early-diagnosis emergency."],"severityClassification":"No formal grading scale is presented in this chapter. In Bell’s palsy, clinical severity is gauged by completeness of paralysis, with incomplete paralysis in the first week being the most favorable prognostic indicator and electromyographic denervation after 10 days indicating axonal degeneration and a worse, more prolonged recovery. The diagnosis of Bell’s palsy is supported clinically by a typical presentation, absence of other risk factors/symptoms, no zoster lesions in the ear canal, and a normal neurologic examination apart from the facial nerve.","references":["Harrison 22e p.3551","Harrison 22e p.3552","Harrison 22e p.3553","Harrison 22e p.3554","Harrison 22e p.3555","Harrison 22e p.3556","Harrison 22e p.3557","Harrison 22e p.3558","Harrison 22e p.3559"],"crossLinks":[]},"tubulointerstitial_disease":{"name":"Tubulointerstitial Kidney Disease","system":"Nephrology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["interstitial nephritis","acute interstitial nephritis","renal tubular acidosis"],"chapter":328,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2437-2444","clinicalPearls":["Tubulointerstitial diseases primarily affect the tubules and interstitium with relative sparing of the glomeruli and renal vessels, and are divided into acute and chronic tubulointerstitial nephritis (TIN) (Harrison 22e p.2437).","Classic allergic acute interstitial nephritis (AIN) presents with fever, rash, peripheral eosinophilia, and oliguric acute kidney injury occurring after 7-10 days of a beta-lactam (e.g., methicillin) - but this classic tetrad is the exception rather than the rule; patients are more often found incidentally to have a rising creatinine (Harrison 22e p.2438).","Acute TIN most often presents as acute kidney injury; today AIN is far more often an allergic reaction to a drug than a postinfectious phenomenon (Harrison 22e p.2437).","Urinalysis in allergic AIN shows pyuria with white blood cell casts and hematuria; white blood cell (leukocyte) casts are a characteristic finding of interstitial nephritis (Harrison 22e p.2438, p.2456).","The mainstay of treatment for drug-induced AIN is discontinuation of the offending agent, which often leads to reversal of kidney injury; glucocorticoids may accelerate recovery but are NOT required in most cases and are reserved for severe injury (Harrison 22e p.2438).","Chronic TIN is more indolent and manifests with disorders of tubular function: polyuria from impaired concentrating ability (nephrogenic diabetes insipidus), Fanconi's syndrome from defective proximal reabsorption (glycosuria, phosphaturia, aminoaciduria, hypokalemia, and type II/proximal RTA from bicarbonaturia), or non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with hyperkalemia (type IV RTA) from impaired ammoniagenesis (Harrison 22e p.2437).","Proteinuria in chronic TIN is usually modest (rarely >2 g/d) from decreased tubular reabsorption of filtered proteins, but nephrotic-range albuminuria may occur when secondary focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS) develops (Harrison 22e p.2437).","Tubulointerstitial nephritis with uveitis (TINU) accounts for <5% of AIN, affects females 3x more than males, with median onset age 15 years; hallmark is painful anterior uveitis plus lymphocyte-predominant interstitial nephritis, with sterile pyuria, mild proteinuria, features of Fanconi's syndrome, and elevated ESR (Harrison 22e p.2439).","Sterile pyuria with elevated creatinine and features of Fanconi's syndrome should raise suspicion for TINU; ocular symptoms precede or accompany kidney disease in only one-third of cases (Harrison 22e p.2439).","Papillary necrosis is a key feature of analgesic nephropathy; a sloughed necrotic papilla can cause gross hematuria and ureteric colic from ureteral obstruction (Harrison 22e p.2442).","Renal ultrasound in reflux nephropathy characteristically shows asymmetric small kidneys with irregular outlines, thinned cortices, and regions of compensatory hypertrophy (Harrison 22e p.2441)."],"pathophysiology":"A group of disorders that primarily affect the renal tubules and interstitium with relative sparing of the glomeruli and vessels, divided into acute and chronic tubulointerstitial nephritis (TIN) (Harrison 22e p.2437). Acute TIN causes AKI either via aggressive inflammatory infiltrates producing tissue edema, tubular cell injury, and compromised tubular flow, or via frank tubular obstruction by casts, cellular debris, or crystals; flank pain may arise from renal capsule distention (Harrison 22e p.2437). Acute interstitial nephritis (AIN) is most commonly an allergic, immune-mediated drug reaction (antibiotics such as beta-lactams/sulfonamides/quinolones/vancomycin/rifampin, NSAIDs/COX-2 inhibitors, proton pump inhibitors, diuretics, anticonvulsants, allopurinol, immune checkpoint inhibitors), but may also be immune-mediated within autoimmune syndromes (Sjogren's, SLE, TINU, granulomatous/sarcoid, IgG4-related disease) or infection-associated (Harrison 22e p.2437-2438). Chronic TIN produces interstitial fibrosis with patchy mononuclear cell infiltration, widespread tubular atrophy, luminal dilation, and thickened tubular basement membranes, manifesting as tubular dysfunction (nephrogenic DI, Fanconi's syndrome, RTA) and progressive azotemia (Harrison 22e p.2437). Because the chronic histopathology is nonspecific, biopsy rarely yields a specific diagnosis, so diagnosis relies on history, drug/toxin exposure, associated symptoms, and imaging (Harrison 22e p.2437).","additionalDifferentials":["Acute interstitial nephritis (drug-induced/allergic) (Harrison 22e p.2437)","Tubulointerstitial nephritis with uveitis (TINU) (Harrison 22e p.2439)","Sjogren's syndrome with tubulointerstitial nephritis, distal RTA, and nephrogenic DI (Harrison 22e p.2438)","SLE with tubulointerstitial predominance and type IV RTA (Harrison 22e p.2439)","Granulomatous interstitial nephritis / sarcoidosis (Harrison 22e p.2439)","IgG4-related systemic disease (Harrison 22e p.2439)","Immune checkpoint inhibitor-associated AIN (Harrison 22e p.2440)","Light chain cast nephropathy / myeloma kidney (Harrison 22e p.2440)","Crystal deposition / obstructive tubulopathies (acute urate, acute phosphate, oxalate, drug crystals) (Harrison 22e p.2440)","Reflux nephropathy (vesicoureteral reflux) (Harrison 22e p.2441)","Sickle cell nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2442)","Analgesic nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2442)","Aristolochic acid nephropathy (Chinese herbal / Balkan endemic) (Harrison 22e p.2442)","Lithium-associated nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2442)","Calcineurin inhibitor nephrotoxicity (Harrison 22e p.2443)","Heavy metal (lead, cadmium) nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2443)","Hypercalcemic and hypokalemic nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2443)"],"infectionMimics":["Acute bacterial pyelonephritis - infection-associated AIN should be distinguished from it; pyelonephritis does not generally cause AKI unless bilateral or causing septic shock (Harrison 22e p.2440)","Reflux nephropathy was previously mislabeled 'chronic pyelonephritis' because it was thought to result from recurrent childhood UTIs (Harrison 22e p.2441)","Infection-associated AIN, currently most often polyomavirus BK reactivation in kidney transplant recipients (Harrison 22e p.2440)","Granulomatous interstitial nephritis - tuberculosis must be ruled out before treatment, as it is a rare cause (Harrison 22e p.2439)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Acute tubular necrosis and other causes of unexplained AKI (biopsy-proven AIN accounts for no more than ~15% of unexplained AKI, likely an underestimate) (Harrison 22e p.2437)","Primary glomerulonephritis causing secondary tubulointerstitial damage; in advanced disease the initial cause is often difficult to discern on biopsy (Harrison 22e p.2442)","Behcet's disease and sarcoidosis as alternative causes of uveitis when considering TINU (Harrison 22e p.2439)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis - active sediment with leukocytes and cellular casts in acute TIN; pyuria with white blood cell casts and hematuria in allergic AIN; bland sediment in aristolochic acid nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2437-2438, p.2442)","Peripheral blood eosinophil count - supports AIN but present in only a minority of patients (Harrison 22e p.2438)","Urinary eosinophils - NOT recommended; neither sensitive nor specific for AIN (Harrison 22e p.2438)","Serum creatinine and BUN to assess azotemia (Harrison 22e p.2437)","Anti-Ro (SS-A) and anti-La (SS-B) antibodies when Sjogren's syndrome suspected (Harrison 22e p.2438)","Serum and urine protein electrophoresis and immunofixation, plus serum/urine free light chains, when light chain cast nephropathy suspected (Harrison 22e p.2440)","Renal ultrasonography - 'medical renal disease' changes (increased echogenicity, loss of corticomedullary differentiation); asymmetric scarred kidneys in reflux nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2437, p.2441)","Kidney biopsy - not usually required for AIN diagnosis but shows interstitial/tubular leukocyte infiltration including eosinophils; required (with other criteria) for aristolochic acid nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2438, p.2442)"],"redFlags":["Classic allergic AIN tetrad: fever, rash, peripheral eosinophilia, and oliguric AKI after a drug (Harrison 22e p.2438)","Particularly severe and rapid-onset AIN upon reintroduction of rifampin after a drug-free period (Harrison 22e p.2438)","Painful anterior uveitis (often bilateral, with blurred vision and photophobia) with sterile pyuria and AKI - suggests TINU (Harrison 22e p.2439)","Gross hematuria with ureteric colic from a sloughed necrotic papilla in analgesic or sickle cell nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2442)","Triad of saturnine gout, hypertension, and impaired kidney function suggesting lead nephropathy (Harrison 22e p.2443)","Anemia disproportionately severe relative to kidney dysfunction in aristolochic acid nephropathy (with very high risk of upper urinary tract urothelial cancer) (Harrison 22e p.2442)"],"prognosis":"In allergic/drug-induced AIN, discontinuation of the offending agent often reverses the kidney injury, though recovery may be incomplete depending on duration of exposure and degree of tubular atrophy and interstitial fibrosis (Harrison 22e p.2438). Granulomatous, idiopathic, TINU, and Sjogren's-related interstitial nephritis generally respond to glucocorticoids, especially when treated early before significant fibrosis, but may relapse and require maintenance immunosuppression (Harrison 22e p.2438-2439). Chronic TIN damage that has already occurred is impossible to reverse, but further deterioration may be slowed by treating glomerular hypertension (Harrison 22e p.2440). Reflux nephropathy affecting both kidneys often progresses inexorably to ESRD over years even without ongoing infection or reflux (Harrison 22e p.2441). Lithium-associated CKD is often irreversible and slowly progresses to ESRD even after lithium is stopped (Harrison 22e p.2443). AKI from acute hypercalcemia may be fully reversible, whereas chronic hypercalcemic kidney dysfunction may not improve even after correcting the calcium disorder (Harrison 22e p.2443).","pitfalls":["The classic AIN tetrad (fever, rash, eosinophilia, oliguric AKI) is the exception rather than the rule; relying on it misses most cases (Harrison 22e p.2438).","NSAID-induced AIN often lacks fever, rash, and eosinophilia but presents with AKI and heavy proteinuria (Harrison 22e p.2438).","Urinary eosinophil testing is neither sensitive nor specific for AIN and is not recommended (Harrison 22e p.2438).","Absence of peripheral eosinophilia does not exclude AIN, as it is present in only a minority of patients (Harrison 22e p.2438).","A negative urine dipstick for protein with elevated total spot urine protein is highly suggestive of Bence-Jones protein (light chains), which dipsticks do not detect - missing myeloma kidney (Harrison 22e p.2440).","Reflux nephropathy may go unnoticed until adulthood and can be mistaken for 'chronic pyelonephritis'; a single affected kidney may be silent except for hypertension (Harrison 22e p.2441).","Because chronic TIN histopathology is nonspecific, biopsy rarely gives a specific diagnosis; over-reliance on biopsy without history/exposure analysis is a pitfall (Harrison 22e p.2437)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison classifies tubulointerstitial diseases into Acute Tubulointerstitial Disorders - acute interstitial nephritis (therapeutic agents/drugs, infection, autoimmune including TINU, Sjogren's, SLE, granulomatous, IgG4-related) and acute obstructive disorders (light chain cast nephropathy, acute phosphate nephropathy, acute urate nephropathy) - versus Chronic Tubulointerstitial Disorders (reflux nephropathy, sickle cell disease, chronic toxin/drug exposure such as analgesics, lithium, heavy metals, aristolochic acid, calcineurin inhibitors), metabolic disturbances (hypercalcemia/nephrocalcinosis, hyperuricemia, hypokalemia, hyperoxaluria, cystinosis), cystic/hereditary disorders, and miscellaneous causes (aging, chronic glomerulonephritis, obstruction, ischemia, radiation) (Harrison 22e p.2437).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2437","Harrison 22e p.2438","Harrison 22e p.2439","Harrison 22e p.2440","Harrison 22e p.2441","Harrison 22e p.2442","Harrison 22e p.2443","Harrison 22e p.2444"],"crossLinks":["aki","ckd"]},"tularemia":{"name":"Tularemia","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Francisella tularensis infection"],"chapter":175,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1335-1340","clinicalPearls":["Zoonosis caused by Francisella tularensis, a small facultatively intracellular aerobic gram-negative coccobacillus; humans are infected via arthropod bites (ticks, tabanid/deer flies, mosquitoes), inhalation of infectious aerosols, handling infected animals, or ingestion of contaminated water/food (Harrison 22e p.1335-1336).","Take a deliberate exposure history: wild animal contact (rodents, rabbits, hares), inhalation of dust contaminated with their urine or feces, recent tick/fly/mosquito bites, brush-cutting or grass-trimming, handling wood or hay, and walking or hunting in wildlife habitats; cluster cases sharing the same exposure are a clue (Harrison 22e p.1337).","The most common initial manifestation is an influenza-like illness with high fever (acute onset, myalgia, headache, constitutional symptoms); fever may be persistent or intermittent. Incubation is usually 3-5 days but may range from 1 to 21 days (Harrison 22e p.1337).","Inflamed, swollen, tender regional lymph nodes are typical; the initial infection route determines which lymphoid tissue is engaged (skin or pharyngeal entry gives regional nodes; inhalation gives mediastinal/hilar nodes) (Harrison 22e p.1337).","Ulceroglandular form (most common, ~47% of US cases): skin inoculation by a blood-feeding arthropod or direct contact with an infected animal/contaminated material produces a primary ulcer plus tender regional lymphadenopathy; the primary ulcer is often a small skin break with limited inflammation that requires careful examination to detect (Harrison 22e p.1337-1338).","Glandular form (~18% of US cases): regional lymphadenopathy without a detectable primary skin ulcer (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Oculoglandular form (~2% of US cases): conjunctivitis with pronounced local inflammation plus regional (e.g., preauricular) lymphadenopathy, acquired by touching the eye with contaminated fingers or by contaminated aerosol (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Oropharyngeal form (~2% of US cases): acute pharyngitis with pronounced local inflammation following ingestion of contaminated water or food, with cervical/tonsillar lymphadenopathy (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Respiratory/pneumonic form (~17% of US cases): acquired by inhaling contaminated aerosol; more often bacteremic and more likely to progress to severe disease requiring inpatient care, particularly in patients aged 65 and over and those with comorbidities. Onset of a dry cough may occur more than 7 days after the influenza-like illness; engaged lymphoid tissue is in the mediastinum or lung hila (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Typhoidal form (~14% of US cases): an older term for a typhoid-like syndrome with prolonged high fever, fatigue, headache, and nausea; Harrison recommends avoiding the term because most historic typhoidal cases were in fact respiratory/pneumonic tularemia acquired by inhalation (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Routine blood biochemistry is generally unhelpful; findings are nonspecific and may or may not show elevated liver enzymes or hematuria. In a Swedish type B outpatient series, white-cell counts stayed within normal limits in 70% of patients and CRP was only moderately elevated (Harrison 22e p.1338).","When tularemia is clinically suspected, communicate the suspicion to the microbiology laboratory in advance so that cultures are handled safely, because F. tularensis causes laboratory-acquired infection and culture requires BSL-3 containment; it is also a US HHS Tier 1 Select Agent (Harrison 22e p.1335, 1339).","For ulcer sampling, swab the edge of the wound (remove any scab first) and rub firmly so the specimen is slightly blood-tinged to maximize yield (Harrison 22e p.1339)."],"pathophysiology":"Natural transmission is usually through the skin or by inhalation of aerosols, but ingestion of contaminated food/water can also infect. An extremely low dose of about 10-50 organisms inoculated through skin microtrauma or inhaled suffices to cause disease, whereas roughly 10^6-10^8 organisms are required by the oral route. Tissue-resident macrophages (alveolar macrophages in lung, Langerhans cells in skin) transport the organism via blood and lymphatics to lymph nodes, spleen, and bone marrow; in skin, neutrophils phagocytose F. tularensis early. After uptake, the bacterium escapes the phagosome into the cytosol, replicates, exits the cell, and disseminates. F. tularensis is a 'stealth pathogen' that manipulates host-cell signaling, delaying the immune response and permitting rapid systemic spread. Characteristic tissue reactions are granuloma formation and necrotic foci surrounded by macrophages, lymphocytes, and giant cells in lymph nodes, lung, spleen, kidney, and liver; pulmonary autopsy findings show necrotizing pneumonia with fibrin, cellular debris, and neutrophils. Effective protective immunity requires both B and T cells, and antigen-specific recall T-cell responses can persist 25-30 years after infection or vaccination (Harrison 22e p.1337, 1339).","additionalDifferentials":["Lung cancer (pulmonary nodules or dense consolidation can mimic malignancy, prompting CT/PET and biopsy before tularemia is suspected) (Harrison 22e p.1337, 1339)","Head and neck cancer (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Tuberculosis (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Unspecified viral infection / 'a severe virus' (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Common skin and soft tissue infection (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Community-acquired pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Undifferentiated fever or sepsis (Harrison 22e p.1337)"],"infectionMimics":["Tuberculosis (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Community-acquired pneumonia (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Common skin and soft tissue infection (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Unspecified viral infection (Harrison 22e p.1337)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Lung cancer (Harrison 22e p.1337, 1339)","Head and neck cancer (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Lymphoma (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Suspected tumor with lymphoid tissue engagement (Harrison 22e p.1337)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Laboratory confirmation by recovery of an F. tularensis isolate, detection of F. tularensis-specific antigens or nucleic acids, or detection of specific antibodies by serology (Harrison 22e p.1338-1339)","Direct detection by PCR/nucleic acid testing of clinical specimens is rapid and reliable; for ulcer specimens it is comparable to culture in sensitivity and specificity (Harrison 22e p.1339)","Appropriate specimens for direct detection or culture: ulcer specimens (sampled from the wound edge), lower respiratory tract specimens (sputum, tracheal washing, bronchoalveolar lavage), lymph node biopsies or aspirates, and blood cultures (Harrison 22e p.1339)","Blood culture: F. tularensis is slow-growing and a 10-day incubation is recommended for optimal sensitivity; MALDI-TOF identification from blood cultures is an increasingly common, sometimes unexpected, finding (Harrison 22e p.1339)","Serology (ELISA or microagglutination) is the mainstay in many laboratories and is especially useful when tularemia is suspected at a later stage; antibodies are generally detectable 10-20 days post-infection (Harrison 22e p.1339)","Chest imaging in respiratory tularemia: single or multifocal consolidation, pleural effusion, and hilar/mediastinal lymphadenopathy; CT may show rounded subpleural consolidations and necrotic lymph nodes; pathology resolution on imaging can be slow and take months (Harrison 22e p.1339)"],"redFlags":["Respiratory/pneumonic form, which is more often bacteremic and may progress to severe disease requiring inpatient care, particularly in patients aged 65 and over and those with comorbidities (Harrison 22e p.1338)","Multiorgan involvement or hemodynamic instability, indicating need for inpatient care (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Host factors increasing risk of severe infection: geriatric frailty, immunosuppression, severe lung and/or heart impairment, and other significant comorbidities (Harrison 22e p.1337)","Lymph node abscess formation, which occurs in about one-third of patients and is associated with antibiotic treatment delay of more than 2-3 weeks after disease onset (Harrison 22e p.1340)","Severe complications reported: adult respiratory distress syndrome, myocarditis, pericarditis, endocarditis, meningitis, osteomyelitis, hepatitis, and renal failure (Harrison 22e p.1340)"],"prognosis":"Before antibiotics, US case-fatality rates were typically 5-15% and could reach as high as 60% in respiratory/pneumonic tularemia. Recent US surveillance (2006-2021) showed 27 of 903 patients (3.0%) with available outcome data died; treatment with at least one high-efficacy antimicrobial class was independently associated with greatly increased odds of survival. Type A is generally more lethal than type B, though there is heterogeneity among type A genetic varieties. Overall type B fatality is estimated at less than 1% in Sweden, where severe respiratory type B tularemia carried about 1.5% fatality. Recovery (and resolution of imaging abnormalities) may be slow, and some patients report postinfectious persisting fatigue and disability of uncertain cause (Harrison 22e p.1339-1340).","pitfalls":["Clinical recognition is challenging, especially if the patient does not spontaneously report fever; tularemia may not be suspected until after extensive and costly investigations, and even in high-endemic areas patients are often initially mistreated for other conditions. A high index of suspicion is critical (Harrison 22e p.1337).","If care is sought several weeks after symptom onset, the acute fever onset may not be reported spontaneously and must be actively elicited (Harrison 22e p.1337).","The primary ulcer can be a small, minimally inflamed skin break that is easily missed or may be undetectable, in which case the disease is classified as glandular rather than ulceroglandular (Harrison 22e p.1338).","Failure to alert the microbiology laboratory to suspected tularemia risks laboratory-acquired infection, as F. tularensis is highly virulent and requires BSL-3 culture conditions (Harrison 22e p.1335, 1339).","Acute-phase serology can be misleading because F. tularensis antibodies persist (single titers can remain above the diagnostic cutoff for at least 1 year), so reliable acute diagnosis should rely on a significant rise between paired sera taken 2-4 weeks apart (Harrison 22e p.1339).","Culture specimens taken from an established lymph node abscess are typically F. tularensis negative (Harrison 22e p.1340).","Tularemia patients do not require isolation because there is no human-to-human transmission (Harrison 22e p.1340)."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal severity score. Clinically, management level is decided by physical examination of the anatomic site and extent of inflammation (pneumonitis, lymph node inflammation, skin ulcers/pustules, pharyngitis), the rate of progression, and host risk factors: localized symptoms are generally suitable for outpatient care, whereas multiorgan involvement or hemodynamic instability suggests inpatient care. The recognized clinical forms (ulceroglandular, glandular, oropharyngeal, oculoglandular, respiratory/pneumonic, typhoidal) are classified by F. tularensis infection route rather than by severity (Harrison 22e p.1337-1338).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1335-1340"],"crossLinks":[]},"tumors_of_the_liver_and":{"name":"Tumors of the Liver and Biliary Tree","system":"Hepatobiliary / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Hepatocellular carcinoma","HCC","Cholangiocarcinoma","Gallbladder cancer","Liver cancer","Hepatic tumors","Biliary tract cancer"],"chapter":87,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.659-670","clinicalPearls":["Liver cancer is the sixth most common cancer worldwide and the third leading cause of cancer-related death; hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) makes up about 85% of primary liver cancers, intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma about 10%, with rarer entities including fibrolamellar HCC, mixed HCC-iCCA, epithelioid hemangioendothelioma, and pediatric hepatoblastoma (Harrison 22e p.659).","Cirrhosis of any cause is the dominant risk factor for HCC; about one-third of cirrhotic patients develop HCC over their lifetime, with an annual risk of roughly 3-8% in HBV- or HCV-related cirrhosis and a lower 1-2% per year in cirrhosis from alcohol, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH), alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency, autoimmune hepatitis, Wilson's disease, and cholestatic disorders (Harrison 22e p.659-660).","HBV accounts for roughly 50% of HCC cases globally (about 60% in Asia and Africa, about 20% in the West) and can cause tumors via insertional mutagenesis; importantly, 25-30% of MASH-associated HCCs arise in the absence of cirrhosis (Harrison 22e p.660).","HCC has a strong male preponderance (male-to-female ratio about 2.5:1) and incidence peaks at 65-70 years, though it presents earlier (40-50 years) in populations with vertical HBV transmission (Harrison 22e p.659).","The radiologic hallmark of HCC on four-phase CT or dynamic contrast-enhanced MRI is arterial-phase contrast uptake with washout in the portal venous or delayed phase, a pattern with about 95-100% specificity that can make biopsy unnecessary in cirrhotic patients (Harrison 22e p.661).","The Barcelona Clinic Liver Cancer (BCLC) system is the most accepted staging scheme; the TNM staging system is NOT used in HCC because it does not capture liver function and performance status, the key prognostic variables (Harrison 22e p.662-664).","Cholangiocarcinoma is the second most common primary liver cancer and is classified by location into intrahepatic (iCCA, ~20-30%), perihilar (pCCA, ~50-60%), and distal (dCCA, ~20-30%); perihilar and distal forms together are termed extrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma (Harrison 22e p.667).","FGFR2 fusions and IDH1/2 mutations occur mostly in iCCA, whereas ERBB2/3 amplifications and SMAD4 aberrations characterize extrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma; up to about 40% of iCCAs carry a targetable mutation (Harrison 22e p.667).","Acute painless jaundice occurs in about 90% of perihilar cholangiocarcinoma patients, while roughly 10% present with cholangitis; distal cholangiocarcinoma can be hard to distinguish from early pancreatic cancer (Harrison 22e p.668-669).","Gallbladder cancer is the most common biliary tract malignancy worldwide, has a female-to-male ratio of about 3:1, and cholelithiasis is the major risk factor though fewer than 1% of patients with gallstones develop it; gallbladder polyps 10 mm or larger carry transformation risk (Harrison 22e p.669-670).","Fibrolamellar HCC typically affects children and young adults (10-30 years) without underlying liver disease, carries the PRKACA-DNAJB1 fusion oncogene in 80-100% of cases, and has a better prognosis than conventional HCC (Harrison 22e p.670).","Hepatic (hepatocellular) adenomas occur predominantly in women aged 35-40 (female-to-male ratio ~10:1), are linked to oral contraceptive use in women and anabolic androgenic steroid use in male bodybuilders, and carry risk of hemorrhage and malignant transformation, particularly when larger than 5 cm (Harrison 22e p.670)."],"pathophysiology":"HCC develops through a multistep process beginning with precancerous low-grade dysplastic nodules that progress to high-grade dysplastic nodules and then early HCC; adult hepatocytes are the principal cell of origin, either transforming directly or de-differentiating into precursor cells, with progenitor cells contributing in a subset. It is a prototypical inflammation-associated cancer in which oxidative stress and the immune microenvironment of chronically damaged liver drive mutation. Tumors accumulate roughly 40-60 somatic alterations, of which 4-8 are driver genes; the leading drivers are TERT promoter mutations (~56%), TP53 (~27%), and CTNNB1 (~26%), all currently non-actionable. Aflatoxin B1 produces a characteristic TP53 R249S hotspot mutation. Only about 20-25% of HCCs carry an actionable mutation. Two molecular subclasses exist: a proliferative class (poor outcome, HBV-related, AFP overexpression, Ras/mTOR/IGF activation) and a nonproliferative class (often CTNNB1-mutated, better outcome). Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma arises from adult cholangiocytes, transdifferentiated hepatocytes, or progenitor-cholangiocyte precursors, whereas extrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma arises from biliary epithelium and peribiliary glands. Hepatic adenomas are clonal proliferations from single-gene driver mutations (e.g., CTNNB1, HNF1A inactivation, inflammatory or Hedgehog pathway activation), and focal nodular hyperplasia is a polyclonal hepatocellular proliferation secondary to an arterial malformation (Harrison 22e p.660-670).","additionalDifferentials":["Low-grade and high-grade dysplastic nodules versus early HCC in a cirrhotic liver","Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma versus metastatic adenocarcinoma (colorectal, breast, lung) versus mixed HCC-iCCA","Distal cholangiocarcinoma versus early pancreatic cancer","Benign liver lesions: hemangioma, focal nodular hyperplasia, hepatocellular adenoma","Mixed HCC-iCCA and cholangiolocellular carcinoma","IgG4-associated cholangiopathy mimicking cholangiocarcinoma stricture"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Hepatic hemangioma","Focal nodular hyperplasia","Hepatocellular adenoma","Dysplastic regenerative nodules in cirrhosis","Metastatic adenocarcinoma to the liver"],"additionalInvestigations":["Six-monthly abdominal ultrasound with serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) for HCC surveillance in high-risk cirrhotic patients (sensitivity ~65%, specificity >90%)","Four-phase contrast-enhanced CT or dynamic/gadoxetic-enhanced MRI for noninvasive HCC diagnosis using the arterial uptake/washout hallmark","LI-RADS categorization of liver nodules (LI-RADS-5 ~95% probability HCC; LI-RADS-4 ~60-70%; LI-RADS-1 0%)","Liver biopsy with hepatopathologist review when noncirrhotic, atypical imaging, or LI-RADS-4; supportive stains GPC3, glutamine synthetase, HSP70, clathrin heavy chain (2 of 4 positive is highly specific for HCC); a negative biopsy does NOT exclude HCC","CA 19-9 (prognostic at cutoffs ~100-129 U/mL for cholangiocarcinoma but poor for early diagnosis, sensitivity/specificity ~60%)","MRI/MRCP and chest-abdomen CT and PET for cholangiocarcinoma staging; PET helps detect occult lymph node metastasis","Endoscopic retrograde cholangiography with brushing cytology plus FISH for polysomy in perihilar/distal cholangiocarcinoma (FISH raises cytology sensitivity from ~20% to ~40%); serum IgG4 to exclude IgG4 cholangiopathy","Child-Pugh score (bilirubin, albumin, prothrombin time, ascites, encephalopathy) and ECOG performance status for prognostic assessment"],"redFlags":["Symptomatic HCC (malaise, weight loss, anorexia, abdominal discomfort, or signs of liver dysfunction) indicates advanced disease with median survival under 1 year","Macrovascular (portal, hepatic, or branch vein) invasion, lymph node involvement, or extrahepatic spread denotes advanced BCLC C disease","Child-Pugh class C / severe liver dysfunction (BCLC D)","Rising or markedly elevated AFP (>=20 ng/mL prompting imaging; >=400 ng/dL highly suspicious but not diagnostic)","Painless jaundice or cholangitis suggesting biliary tract malignancy","Hepatic adenoma larger than 5 cm (hemorrhage and malignant transformation risk); active adenoma bleeding"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on stage, tumor burden, liver function, and performance status. About 40% of HCC patients are diagnosed at BCLC stage 0 or A and are eligible for potentially curative resection, transplantation, or ablation, with median overall survival beyond 5 years (up to ~10 years after transplantation). Optimal surgical resection candidates have 5-year survival of 60-70% (35-55% in suboptimal candidates); RFA achieves ~60% 5-year survival; liver transplantation within Milan criteria yields 70-80% 5-year survival with ~10-15% recurrence at 5 years. Intermediate-stage (BCLC B) untreated natural history is about 16 months, improved to ~25-30 months with TACE. Symptomatic/advanced disease has median survival under 1 year; end-stage (BCLC D) survival is about 3-6 months. Cholangiocarcinoma overall carries a 5-year survival of about 10%; resectable iCCA single tumors have median survival ~40-50 months. Gallbladder cancer 5-year survival ranges 60-90% in resected localized disease but only ~10-12 months median in advanced disease. Fibrolamellar HCC has better prognosis (median ~70 months after resection). Hepatoblastoma overall 5-year survival is ~70% (50-90% by PRETEXT stage); notably, low AFP (<100 ng/mL) indicates poor prognosis in hepatoblastoma, opposite to HCC.","pitfalls":["A negative liver biopsy does NOT exclude HCC; sensitivity falls below 50% for tumors 1-2 cm, and a second biopsy is advised if findings are inconclusive or the lesion grows/changes enhancement","Nodules under 1 cm are unlikely to be HCC and are difficult to diagnose; short-interval ultrasound follow-up (every 3-4 months) is recommended rather than immediate biopsy","PET and contrast-enhanced ultrasound perform poorly for early HCC diagnosis and are not recommended as primary diagnostic tools","An AFP of >=400 ng/dL is highly suspicious but NOT diagnostic of HCC by guidelines","TNM staging should NOT be applied to HCC because it omits liver function and performance status","About 25-30% of MASH-related HCCs occur without cirrhosis, so absence of cirrhosis does not exclude HCC in this group","Surveillance is generally NOT recommended in advanced (Child-Pugh C) cirrhosis since curative therapy is precluded, except for liver transplant waiting-list patients","Liver biopsy carries a ~2-3% risk of complications including tumor seeding and bleeding"],"severityClassification":"Hepatocellular carcinoma is staged by the Barcelona Clinic Liver Cancer (BCLC) system into five stages: very early (BCLC 0, single nodule <=2 cm, Child-Pugh A, ECOG 0), early (BCLC A, single or up to three nodules <=3 cm, Child-Pugh A-B, ECOG 0), intermediate (BCLC B, multinodular, Child-Pugh A-B, ECOG 0), advanced (BCLC C, portal/macrovascular invasion, N1 or M1, or ECOG 1-2), and terminal (BCLC D, Child-Pugh C or ECOG >2). Liver function is graded by the Child-Pugh score (classes A-C) and performance by ECOG. Milan criteria (single tumor <=5 cm, or up to three nodules each <=3 cm, no macrovascular invasion or extrahepatic spread) define transplant eligibility. Cholangiocarcinoma is classified anatomically into intrahepatic, perihilar, and distal subtypes; hepatoblastoma uses the PRETEXT (Pretreatment Extent of Tumor) classification (Harrison 22e p.661-669).","references":["Harrison 22e p.659","Harrison 22e p.660","Harrison 22e p.661","Harrison 22e p.662","Harrison 22e p.663","Harrison 22e p.664","Harrison 22e p.667","Harrison 22e p.668","Harrison 22e p.669","Harrison 22e p.670"],"crossLinks":[]},"turner_syndrome":{"name":"Turner Syndrome","system":"Genetic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["45,X","monosomy X","Ullrich-Turner syndrome","gonadal dysgenesis (45,X)"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A chromosomal disorder affecting females caused by complete or partial absence of one X chromosome, most commonly a 45,X karyotype, with mosaic forms also occurring.","The two most consistent features are short stature and gonadal dysgenesis leading to ovarian failure.","Newborns may show puffiness of the hands and feet (lymphoedema) and redundant nuchal skin; a fetal cystic hygroma may be detected antenatally.","Characteristic features can include a webbed neck, low posterior hairline, widely spaced nipples on a broad chest, and a wide carrying angle at the elbows.","Congenital cardiovascular anomalies, particularly bicuspid aortic valve and coarctation of the aorta, carry a risk of aortic dilatation and dissection and require ongoing surveillance.","Renal anomalies such as a horseshoe kidney are common, as are recurrent otitis media and hearing loss.","Most girls have normal intelligence but may have specific difficulties with visuospatial and mathematical tasks.","Growth hormone therapy is used to improve final height, and oestrogen replacement is used to induce puberty and maintain bone and uterine health.","Diagnosis is confirmed by karyotype, and lifelong multidisciplinary follow-up addresses cardiac, endocrine, and reproductive needs."],"pathophysiology":"Loss of all or part of the second sex chromosome causes haploinsufficiency of genes that normally escape X-inactivation and are needed in two copies. Deficiency of the SHOX gene contributes to short stature and skeletal features, while accelerated loss of ovarian follicles leads to gonadal dysgenesis and oestrogen deficiency. Abnormal fetal lymphatic development produces the lymphoedema, nuchal changes, and associated features such as the webbed neck, and disrupted development contributes to the cardiovascular and renal malformations.","additionalDifferentials":["Noonan syndrome (overlapping webbed neck and short stature, normal karyotype)","Other causes of short stature (growth hormone deficiency, constitutional delay)","Other causes of primary ovarian insufficiency or delayed puberty","Mixed gonadal dysgenesis","Skeletal dysplasias"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Karyotype to confirm monosomy X or mosaicism (with additional cells if mosaicism suspected)","Echocardiography and cardiac MRI to assess aortic valve, arch, and aortic dimensions","Renal ultrasound for structural anomalies","Gonadotrophins (FSH, LH) and thyroid function testing","Audiological assessment","Bone age and growth monitoring; screening for coeliac disease"],"redFlags":["Aortic dilatation or symptoms suggesting aortic dissection","Severe coarctation or critical cardiac lesions in infancy","Progressive hearing loss","Features suggesting Y-chromosome material, which raises gonadoblastoma risk","Signs of untreated hypothyroidism or coeliac disease"],"prognosis":"With appropriate hormone therapy and surveillance most girls achieve improved height, undergo induced puberty, and have a good quality of life; long-term outlook is influenced chiefly by cardiovascular complications, and fertility is usually impaired though assisted reproduction may be possible.","pitfalls":["Not considering the diagnosis in a girl with unexplained short stature or delayed puberty","Missing mosaicism when only a limited number of cells are analysed","Overlooking the need for lifelong aortic surveillance","Failing to screen for associated hypothyroidism, coeliac disease, and hearing loss"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"tympanic_membrane_perforation":{"name":"Tympanic membrane perforation","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["perforated eardrum","ruptured tympanic membrane","TM perforation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Perforations arise most often from infection (acute or chronic otitis media) but also from trauma such as a slap to the ear, blast injury, barotrauma or instrumentation","Central perforations of the pars tensa are generally safe, whereas attic or marginal (posterosuperior) perforations are unsafe because they associate with cholesteatoma","The degree of conductive hearing loss reflects both the size and site of the perforation, being greater with larger and posterior defects","A discharging perforation suggests active middle ear disease and warrants aural toilet and treatment of infection","Many traumatic and small perforations heal spontaneously if the ear is kept dry and free of infection","Water precautions are important because contamination can precipitate recurrent infection and discharge","Persistent dry perforations affecting hearing or causing recurrent infection can be repaired by myringoplasty or tympanoplasty","Examination should assess the size, site and margins of the defect and the state of the middle ear mucosa and ossicles"],"pathophysiology":"The tympanic membrane can be breached when middle ear pressure rises from suppurative infection until necrosis of the drum produces a perforation, or when external forces (direct trauma, sudden pressure change, or a foreign body) tear it mechanically. Loss of an intact vibrating surface and of the round window sound-shielding effect reduces the efficiency of sound transfer to the cochlea, producing conductive hearing loss proportional to the area lost and its position. Small tears usually re-epithelialise and heal, but persistent perforation, ongoing infection, or ingrowth of squamous epithelium through a marginal or attic defect can prevent closure and predispose to cholesteatoma and chronic disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Retracted but intact tympanic membrane simulating a defect","Healed thin (dimeric) segment mimicking a perforation","Cholesteatoma with a marginal or attic defect","Grommet (ventilation tube) in situ","Otitis externa with debris obscuring the drum","Congenital ossicular anomaly causing conductive loss with an intact drum"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Otoscopy and, ideally, microscopy to define size, site and margins of the perforation","Pure tone audiometry and tuning fork tests to quantify conductive loss","Tympanometry, which shows a large ear canal volume with a perforation","Culture of any discharge in active infection","CT of the temporal bone if cholesteatoma or ossicular pathology is suspected"],"redFlags":["Marginal or attic perforation with foul discharge or crusting suggesting cholesteatoma","Vertigo, sensorineural loss or facial weakness indicating inner ear or facial nerve involvement","Perforation following head trauma with cerebrospinal fluid otorrhoea or associated fracture","Persistent bloody discharge or granulations raising concern for neoplasm"],"prognosis":"Small, uncomplicated and traumatic perforations often heal spontaneously with good recovery of hearing, and surgical repair of dry persistent defects has a high success rate; outcomes are worse when there is ongoing infection, ossicular damage or an underlying cholesteatoma.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish a dangerous marginal or attic perforation from a safe central one","Allowing water to enter the ear and precipitate recurrent infection","Assuming all perforations need surgery when many heal with conservative care","Overlooking ossicular discontinuity as an additional cause of hearing loss"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"type1_diabetes_child":{"name":"Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus (Child)","system":"Endocrine","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["T1DM","type 1 diabetes","insulin-dependent diabetes","juvenile diabetes","paediatric type 1 diabetes"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common form of diabetes in children, caused by autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells leading to absolute insulin deficiency.","Classic presenting features are polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss, and fatigue, often over a few weeks; new secondary enuresis in a previously dry child is a useful clue.","A substantial proportion of children present in diabetic ketoacidosis, which can be the first manifestation of the disease.","Lifelong insulin replacement is essential; unlike type 2 diabetes, it cannot be managed by lifestyle measures or oral agents alone.","A 'honeymoon' period of partial remission with reduced insulin needs may follow initial stabilisation but is temporary.","Management is multidisciplinary, combining insulin, carbohydrate awareness, glucose monitoring, and structured education for the child and family.","Careful attention to preventing and recognising hypoglycaemia is central to safe day-to-day care.","Long-term care includes screening for associated autoimmune conditions (such as thyroid disease and coeliac disease) and for microvascular complications over time."],"pathophysiology":"Type 1 diabetes results from T-cell mediated autoimmune destruction of insulin-producing pancreatic beta cells in genetically predisposed children, often triggered by environmental factors. Progressive beta-cell loss produces absolute insulin deficiency, so glucose cannot enter cells and hyperglycaemia develops. Without insulin the body shifts to lipolysis and ketogenesis; unrestrained ketone production causes metabolic acidosis, while osmotic diuresis from glycosuria leads to dehydration and electrolyte loss, culminating in diabetic ketoacidosis if untreated.","additionalDifferentials":["Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Monogenic diabetes (MODY) and neonatal diabetes","Secondary diabetes (e.g. cystic fibrosis-related, steroid-induced)","Diabetes insipidus (for polyuria and polydipsia)","Transient stress hyperglycaemia during acute illness","Urinary tract infection (for enuresis or dysuria)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Blood glucose and HbA1c","Blood and/or urine ketones","Venous blood gas and electrolytes to assess for ketoacidosis","Pancreatic autoantibodies (e.g. GAD, IA-2, ZnT8, islet cell)","Thyroid function and coeliac screening at diagnosis and during follow-up","C-peptide where the diabetes type is uncertain"],"redFlags":["New-onset type 1 diabetes presenting in diabetic ketoacidosis (vomiting, abdominal pain, deep sighing breathing, dehydration, drowsiness) is a medical emergency","Reduced consciousness or focal neurological signs suggesting cerebral oedema during DKA management","Severe hypoglycaemia with seizures or loss of consciousness","Signs of significant dehydration or shock","Persistent vomiting in a known diabetic child, which may signal evolving ketoacidosis"],"prognosis":"With modern insulin regimens, glucose monitoring, and multidisciplinary support most children live full lives; long-term outcome depends heavily on sustained glycaemic control, which reduces the risk of microvascular and macrovascular complications. Acute risks include ketoacidosis and severe hypoglycaemia.","pitfalls":["Mistaking early symptoms for a urinary tract infection or behavioural enuresis and delaying diagnosis","Failing to check ketones and acid-base status in a hyperglycaemic, unwell child","Correcting fluids and glucose too aggressively in DKA and precipitating cerebral oedema","Assuming the honeymoon phase means resolution and reducing vigilance","Overlooking associated autoimmune conditions during follow-up"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"umbilical_hernia":{"name":"Umbilical hernia","system":"GI / Abdominal wall / Surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["paraumbilical hernia","supraumbilical hernia","congenital umbilical hernia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["True umbilical hernias in infants protrude through the umbilical ring and most close spontaneously within the first few years of life","In adults the defect is usually paraumbilical, occurring through the linea alba just above or below the umbilicus, and does not resolve without surgery","Adult paraumbilical hernias are more frequent in women, and predisposing factors include obesity, multiparity, ascites, and chronically raised intra-abdominal pressure","The neck is often narrow relative to the sac, so incarceration and strangulation are relatively common, particularly with a fibrous fatty omental content","Contents may include omentum, small bowel, or transverse colon, and long-standing hernias can become multiloculated","Elective repair (suture repair for small defects, mesh for larger ones) is advised for symptomatic or enlarging adult hernias to avoid emergency complications","In children, repair is usually deferred and reserved for defects that persist beyond a few years, are large, or become complicated","Skin changes over a large tense hernia, such as thinning or ulceration, warrant urgent assessment"],"pathophysiology":"Umbilical and paraumbilical hernias arise from a defect at or adjacent to the umbilical cicatrix. In infants the umbilical ring fails to close fully after cord separation. In adults, weakening and separation of the linea alba fibres around the umbilicus, combined with sustained raised intra-abdominal pressure from obesity, pregnancy, or ascites, allows protrusion of preperitoneal fat, omentum, or bowel through a paraumbilical defect. The characteristically narrow, fibrous neck restricts reduction and readily compromises the vascular supply of trapped contents, predisposing to incarceration and strangulation.","additionalDifferentials":["Epigastric hernia","Incisional hernia near the umbilicus","Divarication of the recti","Umbilical granuloma or omphalitis in infants","Metastatic umbilical nodule (Sister Mary Joseph nodule)","Lipoma or sebaceous cyst of the umbilicus"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical examination for reducibility, cough impulse, and defect size","Ultrasound to confirm the defect and characterise contents when uncertain","CT in large, recurrent, or complex hernias and to assess in patients with ascites","Assessment for underlying causes of raised intra-abdominal pressure such as ascites"],"redFlags":["Irreducible, tense, tender umbilical swelling indicating incarceration or strangulation (surgical emergency)","Features of bowel obstruction with an irreducible hernia","Overlying skin ischaemia, ulceration, or impending rupture over a large hernia","Systemic sepsis or peritonism suggesting strangulated bowel"],"prognosis":"Most infantile umbilical hernias close spontaneously. Adult paraumbilical hernias do not resolve and carry a meaningful risk of incarceration and strangulation; elective mesh repair gives good results, whereas emergency surgery, especially in obese or comorbid patients, is associated with higher complication rates.","pitfalls":["Assuming an adult paraumbilical hernia will behave like a self-resolving infantile hernia","Underestimating strangulation risk because of the narrow neck","Overlooking uncontrolled ascites, which increases recurrence and complicates repair","Missing a Sister Mary Joseph nodule by attributing all umbilical lumps to hernia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"uncommon_disseminated_fungal_infections":{"name":"Uncommon Disseminated Fungal Infections","system":"Multisystem (Infectious Diseases / Mycoses)","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Paracoccidioidomycosis","Talaromycosis","Penicilliosis","Emergomycosis","Disseminated emmonsiosis","Adiaspiromycosis","Phaeohyphomycosis","Fusariosis","Scedosporiosis","Lomentosporiosis","Trichosporonosis"],"chapter":226,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1725","clinicalPearls":["This group spans endemic dimorphic fungi (paracoccidioidomycosis, talaromycosis, emergomycosis, adiaspiromycosis), hyaline and dematiaceous molds (fusariosis, scedosporiosis/lomentosporiosis, phaeohyphomycoses), and the yeast-like Trichosporon; the more common invasive mycoses (histoplasmosis, coccidioidomycosis, blastomycosis, sporotrichosis, candidiasis, cryptococcosis, aspergillosis, mucormycosis) are covered separately (Harrison 22e p.1725).","Dimorphic fungi exist as conidia-producing molds in the environment (the infectious form) and convert to yeast (or spherules/adiaspores) at temperatures above about 35 degrees C in tissue; infection generally begins with inhalation of conidia (Harrison 22e p.1725).","Paracoccidioidomycosis (Paracoccidioides brasiliensis complex, P. lutzii) is acquired by inhalation in humid Central/South American forests, with highest incidence in Brazil; chronic disease shows a striking male predominance (reported male-to-female ratios from 14:1 up to ~70:1) (Harrison 22e p.1726).","The acute/juvenile form of paracoccidioidomycosis is uncommon, occurs typically in those under 30, primarily disseminates through the reticuloendothelial system, and often shows peripheral eosinophilia; the chronic/adult form accounts for ~90% of cases (Harrison 22e p.1726).","On tissue, Paracoccidioides yeast classically appears as a central yeast ringed by narrow-necked daughter buds (the 'mariner's wheel'); some commercial serologic tests may fail to detect P. lutzii (Harrison 22e p.1726).","Talaromycosis (Talaromyces marneffei, formerly Penicillium marneffei) is endemic in Vietnam, Thailand, and other Southeast Asian countries, is epidemiologically linked to bamboo rats, and occurs mostly in advanced AIDS; its presentation mimics disseminated histoplasmosis with umbilicated molluscum-like skin papules (Harrison 22e p.1727).","In culture T. marneffei produces a distinctive diffusible red pigment, and tissue/blood smears show yeast-like organisms reproducing by bipolar fission (two ellipses with a central septum) (Harrison 22e p.1727).","Emergomycosis (Emergomyces, several formerly Emmonsia) is an emerging dimorphic-fungus infection of patients with impaired cellular immunity; cutaneous involvement (disseminated ulcers, papules, nodules) accompanies pulmonary disease in most patients, while CNS spread is rare (Harrison 22e p.1727).","Fusariosis is angioinvasive and mimics aspergillosis, but is distinguished by very common, often painful, nodular or necrotic skin lesions appearing around the onset of fever and by frequently positive blood cultures (~50%); banana-shaped macroconidia are characteristic on culture (Harrison 22e p.1728).","Phaeohyphomycoses are caused by melanin-producing (dematiaceous/brown-black) molds; melanin is a virulence factor, certain species (Cladophialophora bantiana, Rhinocladiella mackenziei) are neurotropic, and golden-brown hyphae are highlighted by the Fontana-Masson melanin stain (Harrison 22e p.1728).","Trichosporonosis (most often Trichosporon asahii) resembles invasive candidiasis with fungemia, but the presence of arthroconidia helps distinguish it from candidemia, and the serum cryptococcal antigen latex agglutination test can be falsely positive because T. asahii shares polysaccharide antigens with Cryptococcus neoformans (Harrison 22e p.1729).","Scedosporiosis (Scedosporium apiospermum complex) and lomentosporiosis (Lomentospora prolificans) mimic aspergillosis radiographically and histologically; L. prolificans can grow in blood cultures whereas S. apiospermum usually does not (Harrison 22e p.1729)."],"pathophysiology":"Most of these fungi enter through inhalation of environmental conidia, with a smaller number introduced by traumatic skin or corneal inoculation or by injection of contaminated material. Dimorphic fungi (Paracoccidioides, Talaromyces, Emergomyces, Emmonsia crescens) convert from mold to invasive yeast/adiaspore form under thermal pressure in the lungs and then disseminate hematogenously to the reticuloendothelial system and other organs; granulomatous inflammation is typical. Disease frequently reflects host immune deficits: inborn errors affecting the IL-12/IFN-gamma axis predispose to severe paracoccidioidomycosis and talaromycosis, and IFN-gamma-primed monocytes/macrophages and neutrophils control Talaromyces. Molds such as Fusarium, Scedosporium, and Lomentospora are angioinvasive, and protection against Fusarium and Trichosporon depends heavily on neutrophils and their oxidative burst, explaining the strong link with profound prolonged neutropenia and with chronic granulomatous disease (defective NADPH oxidase). Dematiaceous molds use melanin as a virulence factor; defense against disseminated phaeohyphomycosis involves Dectin-1 (CLEC7A) and the adaptor CARD9, and deficiencies in this pathway predispose to disseminated and brain infection even in otherwise immunocompetent hosts. In adiaspiromycosis, inhaled E. crescens conidia enlarge into nonreplicating thick-walled adiaspores (up to ~500 micrometers) that drive a granulomatous inflammatory response.","additionalDifferentials":["Disseminated histoplasmosis (talaromycosis and emergomycosis mimic it clinically and the Histoplasma antigen test cross-reacts with Emergomyces)","Invasive aspergillosis (fusariosis, scedosporiosis, and lomentosporiosis mimic it clinically, radiographically, and histologically)","Invasive candidiasis / candidemia (resembled by trichosporonosis)","Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis and squamous cell carcinoma (mimic the mucocutaneous lesions of chronic paracoccidioidomycosis)","Molluscum contagiosum (umbilicated skin papules of talaromycosis)"],"infectionMimics":["Disseminated histoplasmosis","Invasive aspergillosis","Invasive candidiasis","Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis","Molluscum contagiosum","Cryptococcosis (cross-reacting cryptococcal antigen in trichosporonosis)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Squamous cell carcinoma (mucocutaneous lesions of paracoccidioidomycosis; rarely arises in chronic chromoblastomycosis)","Necrotic/embolic skin lesions of other causes (mimicked by painful necrotic lesions of fusariosis)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Culture from blood and/or tissue is central; identification to mold phase is often required because yeast/hyphal tissue forms can be misidentified (e.g., Emergomyces resembling Histoplasma; Fusarium/Scedosporium resembling Aspergillus)","Histopathology with special stains, including Fontana-Masson for melanized (dematiaceous) molds","Blood cultures (positive in up to ~50% of disseminated fusariosis and can grow Lomentospora prolificans; S. apiospermum usually negative)","Serology for Paracoccidioides (with the caveat that some assays miss P. lutzii)","PCR-based molecular identification (including 28S rDNA and rRNA ITS) through fungal reference laboratories, useful in culture-negative or difficult cases","Awareness of cross-reactivity: Histoplasma antigen test cross-reacts with Emergomyces; cryptococcal antigen can be positive in Trichosporon asahii infection","Species-level identification and susceptibility testing in a reference laboratory to guide management of resistant molds"],"redFlags":["Persistent fever in a profoundly neutropenic patient despite broad-spectrum antibiotics (suggests angioinvasive mold such as Fusarium or Scedosporium/Lomentospora)","New painful nodular or necrotic skin lesions in a neutropenic or leukemic patient, especially appearing with the onset of fever (fusariosis)","Positive fungal blood culture in an immunocompromised host (disseminated fusariosis or Lomentospora)","Disseminated cutaneous ulcers/papules/nodules with pulmonary disease in an HIV/AIDS or transplant patient (emergomycosis, talaromycosis)","Indolent brain abscess with golden-brown pigmented hyphae, or intraventricular CNS spread (neurotropic dematiaceous molds such as Cladophialophora bantiana)","CNS involvement in any of these disseminated infections, which carries notably high mortality"],"prognosis":"Outcomes vary widely by organism and host immune status. Paracoccidioidomycosis generally responds well, though pulmonary fibrosis can progress in chronic disease. Untreated disseminated talaromycosis is usually fatal, but treated mortality is about 10%. Disseminated fusariosis carries high mortality (historically up to ~85%, now around 50% with better therapy) and approaches 100% if neutropenia persists; survival hinges on neutrophil recovery. Invasive S. apiospermum infection has roughly 50% mortality, whereas L. prolificans remains about 85-100% lethal. Disseminated trichosporonosis mortality has been as high as 70% and remains very high with persistent neutropenia. Disseminated and visceral phaeohyphomycoses, particularly with CNS involvement, have high mortality.","pitfalls":["Misidentifying tissue forms: Emergomyces can be mistaken for Histoplasma, and Fusarium/Scedosporium/Lomentospora are difficult to distinguish from Aspergillus on histology, so culture identification is essential","Cross-reacting antigen tests can mislead: Histoplasma antigen cross-reacts with Emergomyces, and cryptococcal antigen may be positive in Trichosporon asahii infection","Assuming AmB activity is universal: many of these molds (Scedosporium, Lomentospora) are intrinsically resistant to amphotericin B, and Fusarium MICs are relatively high","Treating a respiratory mold isolate as disease without evidence of tissue invasion, since Scedosporium/Lomentospora may merely colonize the airway (e.g., in bronchiectasis)","In vitro susceptibility of dematiaceous molds may not reflect response because muriform/tissue forms can behave differently from hyphal forms","Overlooking Fusarium onychomycosis as a portal for later disseminated disease in patients heading into neutropenia or transplantation","Missing the diagnosis in 'immunocompetent' patients with disseminated/brain phaeohyphomycosis who may carry CLEC7A/CARD9 pathway defects"],"severityClassification":"No single formal staging applies across this heterogeneous group. Practically, infections are stratified by extent and host status: localized cutaneous/keratitis/onychomycosis or pulmonary-limited disease versus disseminated/angioinvasive infection; presence of CNS involvement marks the most severe end (associated with the highest mortality). For dimorphic fungi (e.g., paracoccidioidomycosis, talaromycosis, emergomycosis), disease is broadly described as mild/moderate versus severe, and as acute (juvenile) versus chronic (adult) forms for paracoccidioidomycosis.","references":["Harrison 22e p.1725","Harrison 22e p.1726","Harrison 22e p.1727","Harrison 22e p.1728","Harrison 22e p.1729","Harrison 22e p.1730"],"crossLinks":[]},"unstable_angina":{"name":"Unstable angina","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["UA","crescendo angina","acute coronary syndrome without ST elevation","rest angina"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined by angina that is new in onset, occurs at rest or with minimal exertion, or is more frequent, severe, or prolonged than a patient's previous stable pattern","It sits on the acute coronary syndrome spectrum and is distinguished from NSTEMI by the absence of a rise in cardiac troponin","The ECG may show transient ST depression or T-wave changes, but can also be normal; serial tracings are important","High-sensitivity troponin measured serially is required to separate unstable angina from NSTEMI, since myocardial necrosis defines the latter","Early risk stratification using validated scores guides the timing of an invasive versus a conservative strategy","Initial management centres on antiplatelet and anticoagulant therapy, anti-ischaemic measures, and admission for monitoring","Because it reflects an unstable coronary plaque, it can progress rapidly to infarction and warrants urgent evaluation rather than outpatient workup"],"pathophysiology":"Unstable angina is usually precipitated by rupture or erosion of an atherosclerotic plaque with superimposed non-occlusive or intermittent thrombus formation, causing an acute reduction in coronary blood flow. Dynamic vasoconstriction and platelet aggregation further limit supply. Unlike infarction, the ischaemia is severe but does not persist long enough or completely enough to produce detectable myocardial necrosis, so cardiac biomarkers remain normal. The same unstable plaque can progress to complete occlusion, linking unstable angina pathophysiologically to NSTEMI and STEMI.","additionalDifferentials":["NSTEMI and STEMI","Aortic dissection","Acute pericarditis or myopericarditis","Pulmonary embolism","Oesophageal spasm or reflux","Musculoskeletal chest pain","Coronary vasospasm (Prinzmetal angina)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Immediate and serial 12-lead ECGs to detect dynamic ischaemic changes","Serial high-sensitivity cardiac troponin to distinguish from NSTEMI","Continuous cardiac monitoring for arrhythmia and recurrent ischaemia","Risk stratification with validated scoring tools to guide invasive strategy timing","Coronary angiography when an early invasive approach is indicated","Echocardiography to assess wall motion and ventricular function"],"redFlags":["MEDICAL EMERGENCY: presentation is an acute coronary syndrome requiring urgent assessment and monitoring because of the risk of progression to myocardial infarction","Ongoing or recurrent rest pain despite initial therapy indicating refractory ischaemia","Haemodynamic instability, new heart failure, or malignant arrhythmia","Dynamic ST-segment shifts or a rising troponin signalling evolving infarction"],"prognosis":"With prompt recognition, antithrombotic therapy, and appropriate risk-based revascularisation most patients stabilise, but delayed treatment or high-risk features carry a substantial early risk of infarction, arrhythmia, and death.","pitfalls":["Discharging a patient with rest or crescendo chest pain because a single troponin or ECG is normal","Failing to obtain serial ECGs and biomarkers to capture dynamic change","Underestimating risk in atypical presentations, particularly in women, older adults, and those with diabetes","Delaying antithrombotic therapy or admission while awaiting definitive imaging"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"upper_gastrointestinal_tract_cancers":{"name":"Upper Gastrointestinal Tract Cancers","system":"Gastrointestinal / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Esophageal cancer","Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma","Esophageal adenocarcinoma","Gastroesophageal junction (GEJ) cancer","Gastric cancer","Stomach cancer","Gastric adenocarcinoma","Gastric lymphoma","MALT lymphoma","Gastrointestinal stromal tumor (GIST)","Small-bowel neoplasm"],"chapter":85,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.641","clinicalPearls":["Two biologically distinct esophageal cancers arise in the same organ: squamous cell carcinoma (SCC), favoring the upper and mid esophagus, and adenocarcinoma, almost always in the lower esophagus and at the gastroesophageal junction (GEJ); globally SCC predominates while adenocarcinoma now accounts for over 75% of incident U.S./Western European cases.","Because dysphagia and odynophagia are typically not noticed until the lumen is substantially obstructed, most esophageal cancers are locally advanced or metastatic at diagnosis; any patient with these symptoms warrants upper endoscopy with biopsy.","Helicobacter pylori is a major chronic-inflammation driver of gastric cancer, but cancer develops in only a small subset of the roughly half of humans infected; eradication has been shown in prospective trials to lower subsequent gastric cancer risk, supporting treatment of asymptomatic H. pylori in those with a first-degree relative with gastric cancer or a prior early-stage gastric cancer.","Physical findings suggesting advanced/metastatic gastric cancer include left supraclavicular adenopathy (Virchow's node), a periumbilical nodule (Sister Mary Joseph nodule), a palpable rectal-shelf mass (Blumer's shelf), ascites, and an ovarian mass (Krukenberg tumor); more commonly the exam is unrevealing.","Diffuse (signet-cell) gastric cancer may not form a discrete mass or ulcer, instead producing thickened gastric rugae and tracking beneath an intact mucosal surface, so initial superficial biopsies can be falsely negative and EUS-guided deeper biopsy may be required.","Germline CDH1 (e-cadherin) mutation markedly increases risk of the diffuse/signet-cell gastric cancer subtype and also raises lobular breast cancer risk; prophylactic gastrectomy is an option in CDH1 carriers.","Molecular profiling is therapeutically decisive: roughly 20-25% of esophageal/GEJ and about 20% of gastric adenocarcinomas overexpress or amplify HER2/ERBB2, and MSI-high/mismatch-repair-deficient and PD-L1-high (high combined positive score) tumors identify candidates for immune-modulating therapy; NGS plus PD-L1 IHC should be routine in metastatic disease.","Gastric lymphomas (about 3% of gastric malignancies) are extranodal non-Hodgkin lymphomas; the GI tract is the most common extranodal NHL site and the stomach is the most common GI site, with MALT (~40%) and diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (~55%) being the dominant subtypes.","MALT lymphoma of the stomach is strongly H. pylori-associated, and the stomach accounts for about 35% of all MALT lymphomas; the t(11;18) translocation marks tumors less likely to respond to H. pylori eradication.","GISTs arise from the interstitial cells of Cajal and are driven by activating cKIT (~majority) or PDGFRA mutations; the stomach is the most frequent primary site (~60%), followed by small bowel (~25%); spindle-cell histology predominates (~70%) and KIT expression is found in ~95%.","Small-bowel neoplasms account for only about 3% of GI tumors; in the U.S., neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumors (40-45%) slightly outnumber adenocarcinomas (30-40%), and the duodenum is the most common site (~50%).","EBV is associated with a distinct gastric cancer subtype (identified in roughly 8-10% of gastric cancer patients) and is described as a driver in some esophageal/GEJ adenocarcinomas; current EBV positivity does not by itself change therapeutic options."],"pathophysiology":"Esophageal SCC is linked to mucosal injury from alcohol and tobacco (synergistic), ingestion of very hot beverages, areca/betel-nut chewing, chronic achalasia, prior radiation, lye-induced strictures, and Plummer-Vinson (Patterson-Kelly) syndrome; genomically it resembles SCCs of other sites (e.g., head and neck) more than esophageal adenocarcinoma, and HPV is not implicated. Esophageal/GEJ adenocarcinoma arises in the setting of chronic gastroesophageal reflux producing inflammation and Barrett's metaplasia (glandular gastric-type mucosa extending into the esophagus); progression through dysplasia accumulates TP53 mutations and aneuploidy, with annual risk of progression of Barrett's to cancer about 0.4-0.5%. Gastric adenocarcinoma is predominantly an inflammation-driven malignancy: H. pylori (and the broader gastric microbiome) and ingestion of nitrate-rich smoked/salted/dried foods generate carcinogenic nitrites; the intestinal Lauren subtype associates with H. pylori and atrophic gastritis, while the diffuse subtype (~20%) associates with germline or somatic CDH1 loss. TCGA defines four molecular subtypes (EBV-positive, microsatellite-unstable/hypermutated, genomically stable [diffuse], and chromosomally unstable). The proximal esophagus-GEJ-gastric cardia adenocarcinomas share a similar genomic profile suggesting common driving factors. Gastric MALT lymphoma is driven by chronic antigenic stimulation from H. pylori, with the t(11;18) API1-MALT1 fusion activating NF-kB signaling. GISTs originate from Cajal cells via gain-of-function KIT or PDGFRA mutations.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign peptic ulcer disease","Reflux esophagitis / Barrett's esophagus without malignancy","Achalasia and other benign esophageal motility/stricture disorders","Gastric adenocarcinoma vs primary gastric lymphoma (treatment differs markedly, so adequate tissue is essential)","Gastric neuroendocrine tumor / neuroendocrine carcinoma","Gastrointestinal stromal tumor (submucosal smooth bulging mass)","Metastatic disease to stomach or esophagus from another primary","Small-bowel adenocarcinoma vs neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor, lymphoma, or GIST"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Benign gastric or duodenal ulcer","Reflux esophagitis / Barrett's esophagus","Achalasia or benign esophageal stricture","Submucosal benign tumors (lipoma, leiomyoma, neurofibroma, lymphoid nodular hyperplasia)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Upper endoscopy with biopsy to establish histology (deep or repeated biopsies / EUS-guided sampling for suspected diffuse gastric cancer or lymphoma that tracks submucosally)","Contrast-enhanced CT of chest, abdomen, and pelvis to assess metastatic disease","FDG-PET/CT (more sensitive than CT for metastases, but may be non-informative in diffuse-type gastric cancer or non-FDG-avid tumors)","Endoscopic ultrasonography (EUS) to determine depth of tumor penetration (T stage) and regional nodal involvement when no metastatic disease is seen","Staging laparoscopy with peritoneal washing cytology for GEJ/gastric tumors with advanced T stage to detect peritoneal involvement","Bronchoscopy for mid/upper esophageal tumors to exclude tracheobronchial invasion (its presence precludes curative surgery)","Molecular/NGS profiling, PD-L1 IHC, HER2/ERBB2 (IHC/FISH/NGS), and MSI/mismatch-repair-protein testing on metastatic tumors to guide systemic therapy","Plasma circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) when biopsy tissue is insufficient, and as an investigational measure of minimal residual disease","H. pylori status assessment in all gastric lymphoma patients (histology, and if negative, stool antigen or urea breath test); HBV testing before lymphoma therapy","Gallium-68 or copper-64 DOTATATE scan for neuroendocrine tumors; CT/MR enterography and device-assisted enteroscopy or capsule endoscopy for small-bowel neoplasms","Germline genetic testing in small-bowel adenocarcinoma and in patients suspected of hereditary syndromes (CDH1, Lynch, FAP, Peutz-Jeghers, BRCA)"],"redFlags":["New or progressive dysphagia or odynophagia, especially with weight loss","Hematemesis or melena, or unexplained iron-deficiency anemia from occult GI bleeding","Anorexia, early satiety, and unexplained weight loss","Left supraclavicular (Virchow's) node, Sister Mary Joseph periumbilical nodule, Blumer's shelf, ascites, or Krukenberg ovarian mass indicating advanced disease","Dysphagia, odynophagia, or upper abdominal discomfort in a patient under 50, given the rising incidence of early-onset upper GI cancers (symptoms must not be dismissed as benign)","Small-bowel obstruction, perforation, or intussusception as a presentation of small-bowel neoplasm"],"prognosis":"Esophageal cancer prognosis remains poor overall but is slowly improving; by clinical (pretreatment) AJCC staging, 5-year survival ranges from about 75-82% for stage 0-I down to roughly 5-18% for metastatic (stage IVB) disease, and most U.S. patients present with locally advanced or metastatic disease. For gastric cancer, surgery alone yields about 25% overall 5-year survival, with stage-dependent rates falling from about 90% (stage 0) to roughly 3% for stage IV; multimodality therapy (perioperative chemotherapy plus surgery) improves 5-year survival by about 10-15% over surgery alone (e.g., MAGIC trial 36% vs 23%). Even with modern systemic and immune-modulating therapy, overall survival for stage IV gastric cancer is still only about 12-15 months. Gastric MSI-high tumors had the best outcomes and genomically stable (diffuse)/mesenchymal subtypes the worst in genomic series. Lynch-syndrome-associated small-bowel cancers probably have a better prognosis. For nonmetastatic gastric GIST, resected tumors 2 cm or smaller with a mitotic index of 5% or less carry near-zero progression risk, whereas tumors 10 cm or larger with mitotic index above 5% are moderate-to-high risk.","pitfalls":["Falsely reassuring superficial endoscopic biopsies in diffuse-type gastric cancer or gastric lymphoma, which track below the mucosa; adequate deep tissue or EUS-guided sampling is required.","Relying on FDG-PET to exclude disease in diffuse-type gastric cancer, which is often not FDG-avid (PET may be non-informative).","Using tumor markers (CEA, CA19-9) for diagnosis; they are nonspecific and elevated in many cancers, and are better for monitoring response/progression.","Attributing alarm symptoms in patients under 50 to benign illness and delaying diagnostic workup, given rising early-onset upper GI cancer incidence.","Missing tracheobronchial invasion in mid/upper esophageal tumors, which precludes curative surgery, by omitting bronchoscopy.","Treating gastric lymphoma like adenocarcinoma; surgical resection has no role in primary gastric lymphoma and MALT may respond to H. pylori eradication alone, so accurate subtyping is critical.","Assuming a t(11;18)-positive MALT lymphoma will respond to H. pylori eradication; this subgroup is less likely to respond.","Forgetting HBV testing before initiating lymphoma therapy (risk of reactivation)."],"severityClassification":"Esophageal and gastric cancers are staged with the AJCC TNM system (eighth edition; ninth edition expected 2024), with separate clinical (cTNM, pretreatment), pathologic (pTNM, for patients undergoing initial surgery), and post-neoadjuvant (ypTNM) classifications. The esophageal T stage reflects depth of penetration through the wall (which over most of its course lacks serosa); regional nodal disease is still potentially curable while metastatic disease is generally treated palliatively. Gastric staging similarly progresses from Tis (mucosa-confined, stage 0) through wall penetration and increasing nodal involvement to distant metastasis (stage IV). Gastric lymphomas are NOT staged by TNM; the Lugano system (an Ann Arbor modification) is used: stage I confined to the gastric wall, stage II regional nodal or local-structure involvement, stage III nodes on both sides of the diaphragm, stage IV more extensive nodal or distant/extranodal (including marrow) disease. GIST recurrence risk is stratified by primary tumor size, mitotic index, and GI location. Neuroendocrine tumors are graded 1-3 by Ki-67 positivity, distinct from fully malignant small-/large-cell neuroendocrine carcinomas.","references":["Harrison 22e p.641","Harrison 22e p.642","Harrison 22e p.643","Harrison 22e p.644","Harrison 22e p.645","Harrison 22e p.646","Harrison 22e p.647","Harrison 22e p.648","Harrison 22e p.649","Harrison 22e p.650","Harrison 22e p.651","Harrison 22e p.652"],"crossLinks":[]},"urethral_stricture":{"name":"Urethral stricture","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["urethral stenosis","urethral narrowing","stricture urethra"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A urethral stricture is a fibrotic narrowing of the urethral lumen, largely a condition of the male anterior urethra, presenting with obstructive voiding symptoms such as a weak, splayed, or spraying stream, straining, and incomplete emptying","Common causes include prior instrumentation or catheterisation, straddle and pelvic trauma, infection (historically gonococcal urethritis), and lichen sclerosus affecting the meatus and distal urethra","Symptoms overlap with prostatic obstruction, but a young man or a history of trauma, catheterisation, or urethritis should raise suspicion of stricture","Uroflowmetry classically shows a prolonged, low, plateau-shaped flow curve, and retrograde urethrography with voiding studies defines the site, length, and number of strictures","Complications include recurrent urinary infection, bladder stones, diverticula, and, in longstanding severe disease, back-pressure effects on the upper tracts and acute retention","Management principles depend on stricture length and location: dilatation or internal urethrotomy for short strictures, and urethroplasty for longer, recurrent, or complex disease, which offers the most durable cure","Repeated dilatation or urethrotomy for recurrent strictures tends to fail and may worsen fibrosis, so early referral for reconstructive urethroplasty is preferred in appropriate cases","Meatal or distal strictures should prompt evaluation for lichen sclerosus"],"pathophysiology":"A urethral stricture is the result of scarring within the corpus spongiosum (spongiofibrosis) and urethral epithelium following injury or inflammation. Trauma, infection, instrumentation, or inflammatory disease such as lichen sclerosus provokes healing by fibrosis, which contracts and narrows the lumen. The narrowed segment increases outflow resistance, causing high-pressure voiding, turbulent flow, and incomplete emptying. Persistent obstruction leads to proximal urethral dilatation, bladder wall changes, stasis with infection and stone formation, and in severe cases upper tract dilatation and retention.","additionalDifferentials":["Benign prostatic hyperplasia / bladder outlet obstruction","Bladder neck stenosis or contracture","Prostate cancer","Meatal stenosis","Neurogenic bladder","Urethral or periurethral tumour","Detrusor underactivity"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Uroflowmetry demonstrating a low, plateau-shaped flow pattern","Retrograde urethrography with voiding cystourethrography to localise and measure the stricture","Flexible urethroscopy to inspect the stricture directly","Urinalysis and culture for associated infection","Post-void residual and renal tract ultrasound to assess retention and upper tracts","Assessment for lichen sclerosus in distal or meatal strictures"],"redFlags":["Acute urinary retention from a tight stricture, a urological emergency requiring urgent drainage","Obstructive uropathy with upper tract dilatation and renal impairment","Recurrent urinary infection, periurethral abscess, or urethral fistula","Failure of a difficult catheterisation, which should prompt suprapubic drainage rather than forceful attempts","Suspicion of urethral malignancy in an atypical or non-healing stricture"],"prognosis":"Short strictures may respond to dilatation or urethrotomy but recurrence is common; urethroplasty provides high long-term success for suitable strictures, whereas complex, recurrent, or lichen sclerosus-associated disease can require staged or repeated reconstruction.","pitfalls":["Attributing obstructive symptoms to the prostate in a younger man and missing a stricture","Repeated dilatation or urethrotomy for recurrent strictures, worsening scarring and delaying definitive repair","Forcing a urethral catheter through a stricture, risking false passage and injury","Overlooking lichen sclerosus in distal strictures","Failing to assess the upper tracts and renal function in longstanding obstruction"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"urethritis":{"name":"Urethritis","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["urethral inflammation","gonococcal urethritis","non-gonococcal urethritis","NGU"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Urethritis is inflammation of the urethra presenting with urethral discharge, dysuria, and urethral itch or discomfort, most often sexually transmitted","It is broadly divided into gonococcal urethritis (Neisseria gonorrhoeae) and non-gonococcal urethritis, of which Chlamydia trachomatis is the leading cause, along with Mycoplasma genitalium and others","Gonococcal urethritis classically produces a profuse purulent discharge, whereas non-gonococcal urethritis tends to cause a scantier, more mucoid discharge, though overlap is common and co-infection frequent","Diagnosis rests on demonstrating urethral inflammation (urethral smear with leucocytes or a positive leucocyte esterase on first-void urine) together with nucleic acid amplification testing for the causative organisms","Management principles include empirical antibiotic therapy covering both gonococcal and chlamydial infection, guided by local resistance patterns, plus abstinence until treatment is complete","Partner notification, treatment of sexual contacts, and a full sexually transmitted infection screen (including HIV and syphilis) are essential components of care","Persistent or recurrent urethritis after treatment may reflect reinfection, non-adherence, or organisms such as Mycoplasma genitalium with antimicrobial resistance","Reactive arthritis (urethritis, conjunctivitis, arthritis) may follow chlamydial urethritis, particularly in HLA-B27-positive individuals"],"pathophysiology":"Urethritis is inflammation of the urethral mucosa, usually caused by sexually transmitted organisms ascending the urethra. Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis are the principal pathogens, with Mycoplasma genitalium, Trichomonas vaginalis, and other organisms also implicated. Infection provokes a neutrophilic inflammatory response in the urethral epithelium, producing discharge and dysuria. Non-infective urethritis can follow trauma or instrumentation. Untreated infection may ascend or spread, causing epididymo-orchitis, prostatitis, disseminated gonococcal infection, or, historically, inflammatory urethral stricture, and in women pelvic inflammatory disease.","additionalDifferentials":["Cystitis or lower urinary tract infection","Prostatitis","Epididymo-orchitis","Balanitis","Reactive arthritis-associated urethritis","Non-infective (traumatic or chemical) urethritis","Foreign body or instrumentation-related irritation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["First-void urine or urethral swab nucleic acid amplification testing for gonorrhoea and chlamydia","Urethral Gram-stain smear for leucocytes and Gram-negative intracellular diplococci","Culture and antimicrobial sensitivities for Neisseria gonorrhoeae given resistance concerns","Testing for Mycoplasma genitalium and Trichomonas where indicated","Full sexually transmitted infection screen including HIV and syphilis serology","Urinalysis to help exclude urinary tract infection"],"redFlags":["Features of disseminated gonococcal infection (fever, skin lesions, arthritis)","Ascending infection causing epididymo-orchitis or prostatitis","Persistent or recurrent urethritis suggesting resistant organism or reinfection","Systemic sexually transmitted co-infection such as HIV requiring identification","Reactive arthritis with ocular or joint involvement"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated urethritis usually resolves rapidly with appropriate antibiotics and partner treatment; outcomes are worsened by reinfection, non-adherence, resistant organisms, and complications such as epididymo-orchitis or reactive arthritis.","pitfalls":["Treating for chlamydia alone and missing co-existing gonococcal infection (or vice versa)","Neglecting partner notification and treatment, leading to reinfection","Failing to perform a full STI screen including HIV and syphilis","Overlooking Mycoplasma genitalium in persistent non-gonococcal urethritis","Not counselling on abstinence until treatment and partner therapy are complete"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"urinary_incontinence":{"name":"Urinary incontinence","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["UI","involuntary urine leakage","loss of bladder control"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Urinary incontinence is any involuntary leakage of urine, and classifying the type guides management: stress, urgency, mixed, overflow, and functional (continuous) incontinence","Stress incontinence is leakage with raised intra-abdominal pressure (coughing, laughing, exertion) due to sphincter or pelvic floor weakness, common after childbirth and in men after prostatectomy","Urgency incontinence is leakage accompanied by a sudden compelling desire to void, usually from detrusor overactivity, and is part of the overactive bladder syndrome","Overflow incontinence is dribbling from a chronically distended, poorly emptying bladder, often due to outlet obstruction or an underactive detrusor, and must not be missed","A focused history, examination (including pelvic and neurological), a bladder (frequency-volume) diary, and post-void residual measurement usually clarify the type","First-line management of stress incontinence is pelvic floor muscle training, while urgency incontinence is treated with bladder training and antimuscarinic or beta-3 agonist therapy","Reversible contributors summarised by the DIAPPERS concept (delirium, infection, atrophic changes, pharmaceuticals, psychological, excess output, restricted mobility, stool impaction) should be sought and corrected","Surgical options such as mid-urethral slings or colposuspension are considered for stress incontinence, and intravesical botulinum toxin or neuromodulation for refractory urgency incontinence"],"pathophysiology":"Continence requires coordinated bladder storage at low pressure with a competent, closed outlet, governed by autonomic and somatic innervation. Stress incontinence arises from a weakened pelvic floor and urethral sphincter (from childbirth, ageing, or prostatic surgery) so that raised abdominal pressure overcomes closure. Urgency incontinence results from involuntary detrusor contractions (detrusor overactivity), which may be idiopathic or neurogenic. Overflow incontinence occurs when a chronically overdistended bladder leaks around an obstruction or fails to contract effectively, as in outlet obstruction or an underactive/neurogenic detrusor. Mixed incontinence combines these mechanisms.","additionalDifferentials":["Stress urinary incontinence","Urgency incontinence / overactive bladder","Mixed incontinence","Overflow incontinence from obstruction or detrusor underactivity","Neurogenic bladder","Urinary tract infection","Vesicovaginal or other urinary fistula (continuous leakage)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Urinalysis to exclude infection and haematuria","Bladder (frequency-volume) diary","Post-void residual volume measurement","Cough stress test and pelvic examination (assessing prolapse and pelvic floor)","Urodynamic studies in complex, mixed, or pre-surgical cases","Cystoscopy or imaging when fistula, malignancy, or structural abnormality is suspected"],"redFlags":["Associated haematuria requiring exclusion of urinary tract malignancy","New incontinence with neurological signs suggesting cord compression or cauda equina syndrome","Overflow incontinence from unrecognised chronic retention with risk of renal impairment","Continuous leakage suggesting a urinary fistula","Recurrent urinary infection or significant post-void residual"],"prognosis":"Many patients improve substantially with conservative measures and correction of reversible factors; stress and urgency incontinence respond well to targeted therapy, and surgery offers durable benefit for refractory stress incontinence, though outcomes vary with cause and comorbidity.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling overflow incontinence as urgency and prescribing antimuscarinics that worsen retention","Failing to measure post-void residual before treating","Overlooking reversible causes such as infection, medications, or stool impaction","Missing haematuria or a neurological cause behind new incontinence","Recommending surgery without adequate assessment of the incontinence type"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"urinary_tract_infections_cystitis_prostati":{"name":"Urinary Tract Infections: Cystitis, Prostatitis, and Pyelonephritis","system":"Genitourinary","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["UTI","Acute cystitis","Acute bacterial prostatitis (ABP)","Chronic bacterial prostatitis (CBP)","Pyelonephritis","Catheter-associated UTI (CAUTI)"],"chapter":140,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1086","clinicalPearls":["UTI denotes symptomatic infection somewhere along the urinary tract; bacteria in urine WITHOUT related symptoms is asymptomatic bacteriuria (ASB), not UTI, and generally should NOT be treated (Harrison 22e p.1086).","Treatment of ASB is discouraged except in two settings: pregnancy (untreated ASB is linked to pyelonephritis, preterm delivery, and low birth weight) and before urologic procedures causing mucosal injury such as lithotripsy or transurethral prostate resection (Harrison 22e p.1086).","Cystitis presents with dysuria, frequency, and urgency, often with suprapubic discomfort, new nocturia, hesitancy, or gross hematuria; fever, rigors, and unilateral flank/back pain are INCONSISTENT with uncomplicated cystitis and point to infection beyond the bladder (Harrison 22e p.1088).","Fever is a key feature distinguishing cystitis from pyelonephritis; mild pyelonephritis may show only low-grade fever with or without costovertebral angle pain, while severe disease brings high fever, rigors, nausea, vomiting, and flank pain (Harrison 22e p.1089).","E. coli is the predominant uropathogen across UTI syndromes, accounting for roughly 75-90% of acute cystitis isolates; Staphylococcus saprophyticus causes about 5-15% (more in younger women) (Harrison 22e p.1087).","Isolation of S. aureus or Salmonella from urine should raise suspicion of concomitant bacteremia, since hematogenous seeding (<2% of UTIs) typically involves these more virulent organisms (Harrison 22e p.1088).","Acute bacterial prostatitis (ABP) is a typically severe illness with dysuria, frequency, and prostatic/pelvic/perineal pain, sometimes with fever, rigors, or bladder outlet obstruction; chronic bacterial prostatitis (CBP) presents insidiously as recurrent cystitis episodes, often without systemic signs (Harrison 22e p.1088).","Chronic pelvic pain syndrome (formerly chronic prostatitis) is far more common than bacterial prostatitis, is NOT associated with bacteriuria, and does NOT improve with antibiotics; obtaining urine cultures in male UTI helps distinguish these entities (Harrison 22e p.1088).","Diagnostic stewardship - not culturing urine in patients lacking UTI signs/symptoms (except pregnancy or pre-urologic-procedure screening) - is the key intervention to reduce misdiagnosis and overtreatment of ASB (Harrison 22e p.1088).","In catheterized patients bacteriuria and pyuria are essentially inevitable, so an abnormal urinalysis alone cannot diagnose CAUTI; localizing or systemic symptoms must be present and other causes excluded (Harrison 22e p.1090).","Recurrent UTI is not automatically 'complicated' - individual episodes can be uncomplicated; early recurrence (within 2 weeks) is usually relapse rather than reinfection and should prompt a search for a sequestered focus (Harrison 22e pp.1086-1087)."],"pathophysiology":"The urinary tract behaves as a continuous column of urine from kidney to urethra. Most UTIs arise by ascending infection: periurethral and vaginal-introital colonization by intestinal flora (usually E. coli) is the critical first step, followed by ascent up the urethra to the bladder and, in renal infection, up the ureter to the kidney. Hematogenous seeding accounts for less than 2% of UTIs and usually involves virulent organisms (e.g., S. aureus, Salmonella). Outcome depends on the interplay of host, pathogen, and environmental factors. The flushing action of normal voiding clears bacteria; urinary stasis, obstruction, foreign bodies (stones, catheters), and biofilm formation favor infection. Uropathogenic E. coli express virulence factors: type 1 pili bind mannose on bladder uroepithelium to initiate cystitis, while P fimbriae bind the P blood-group antigen on renal epithelium and are important in pyelonephritis and bloodstream invasion; toxins, iron-acquisition systems, biofilms, and capsules also contribute. Host susceptibility has a hereditary component (familial/maternal history; non-secretor blood-group phenotypes increasing E. coli adherence; Toll-like receptor and IL-8 receptor gene variants). Disruption of vaginal lactobacilli (spermicide/nonoxynol-9, menopausal atrophy) promotes E. coli colonization (Harrison 22e pp.1087-1088).","additionalDifferentials":["Asymptomatic bacteriuria (positive culture without localizing symptoms)","Sexually transmitted infections - urethritis/cervicitis from Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Mycoplasma genitalium","Vaginitis (Candida albicans, Trichomonas vaginalis)","Herpetic urethritis","Interstitial cystitis","Chronic pelvic pain syndrome (in men)","Noninfectious vulvar/vaginal irritation"],"infectionMimics":["Cervicitis due to Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Vaginitis (Trichomonas vaginalis, Candida albicans)","Herpetic urethritis","Urethritis from Mycoplasma genitalium"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Interstitial cystitis / bladder pain syndrome","Chronic pelvic pain syndrome (noninfectious prostatic pain without bacteriuria)","Noninfectious vaginal or vulvar irritation","Delirium from non-urinary causes mislabeled as UTI in older adults with incidental ASB"],"additionalInvestigations":["Urine dipstick for nitrite and leukocyte esterase (best used to help rule out UTI; only Enterobacterales convert nitrate to nitrite, so non-Enterobacterales and dilute frequently-voided urine can be falsely negative)","Microscopic urinalysis for pyuria and hematuria","Urine culture with susceptibilities - warranted in pregnancy, suspected resistant organisms, recurrent or complicated UTI, and all male UTI; ASB screening in pregnancy must use culture because dipstick is not sufficiently sensitive","Blood cultures when fever or systemic signs suggest infection beyond the bladder","Imaging (CT or ultrasound) in men with febrile UTI (even first episode) to evaluate for obstruction or anatomic abnormality; suspect intraparenchymal abscess with persistent fever/bacteremia despite therapy","Urologic consultation for unclear diagnosis or recurrent UTI in men"],"redFlags":["Fever, rigors, or unilateral flank/back pain in a patient with urinary symptoms (suggests pyelonephritis, prostatitis, or bloodstream involvement)","Severe pyelonephritis with high fever, rigors, nausea, vomiting, and flank pain","Emphysematous pyelonephritis (gas in renal/perinephric tissue), occurring almost exclusively in poorly controlled diabetes","Acute papillary necrosis causing obstructive uropathy; bilateral papillary necrosis may present with rapidly rising serum creatinine","Continued fever and/or bacteremia despite antibiotics (suggests intraparenchymal renal abscess)","Signs of sepsis in an older patient with altered mentation","S. aureus or Salmonella in urine (consider concomitant bacteremia and metastatic prostatic focus)","Bladder outlet obstruction with acute bacterial prostatitis"],"prognosis":"UTI is common and patients typically respond favorably and rapidly to appropriately chosen modern antimicrobials. Uncomplicated cystitis in low-risk women carries a low risk of serious complications and can often be managed remotely. About 20-30% of women with one UTI develop recurrences (average ~2.6 reinfections/year; rate 0.3-7.6 per patient-year), with likelihood of recurrence decreasing as time since the last infection lengthens. Up to 80% of women have at least one UTI in their lifetime; lifetime prevalence in U.S. men is about 14%. Untreated ASB in pregnancy carries risk of pyelonephritis, preterm delivery, and low birth weight. Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis causes suppurative destruction of renal tissue and its mass effect can mimic renal malignancy (Harrison 22e pp.1086-1089).","pitfalls":["Diagnosing UTI from pyuria/bacteriuria alone without requiring symptoms - this conflates ASB with true UTI; degree of pyuria in an asymptomatic patient should not drive the diagnosis (very high pyuria is common with urinary stasis, ESRD, or obstructed catheters).","Treating ASB outside pregnancy or pre-urologic-procedure settings - it does not reduce future UTIs and may even increase infection risk (e.g., in renal transplant recipients).","Premature diagnostic closure: attributing an older patient's isolated altered mentation to 'UTI' without excluding sepsis and other time-sensitive causes of delirium; a reported history of recurrent UTIs may actually reflect persistent ASB.","Relying on a negative dipstick to rule out UTI - false negatives occur with non-Enterobacterales organisms, dilute frequently-voided urine, and it is insufficiently sensitive to exclude bacteriuria in pregnancy.","Mistaking an STI (especially C. trachomatis) for UTI, or treating dysuria due to vaginitis/cervicitis as UTI; vaginal discharge should prompt STI evaluation.","Assuming all male UTI is 'complicated' or that complicated-UTI guidelines apply to prostatitis; not all men with UTI have urinary tract abnormalities, especially those 45 years or younger.","Misinterpreting a mixed-flora culture as infection - usually contamination except in long-term catheterization, chronic retention, or urinary-enteric/genital fistula."],"severityClassification":"Clinical syndrome-based: asymptomatic bacteriuria (ASB) vs symptomatic UTI. Uncomplicated UTI = infection confined to the bladder (cystitis) in a woman or man without a urinary catheter. Complicated UTI = infection extending beyond the bladder (prostatitis, pyelonephritis, bacteremia) or in the setting of catheterization (CAUTI). Pyelonephritis is graded clinically from mild (low-grade fever +/- back/CVA pain) to severe (high fever, rigors, nausea, vomiting, flank pain). Severe/special forms: emphysematous pyelonephritis and xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis. Lab/diagnostic thresholds: pyuria conventionally >10 WBCs/HPF (poor specificity ~36%; a cutoff nearer 250 WBCs/HPF correlates better with symptoms in older women); for women with cystitis symptoms a colony count >10^2 bacteria/mL is more sensitive (95%) than the classic 10^5/mL; CAUTI bacteriuria threshold is >=10^3 CFU/mL (Harrison 22e pp.1086-1092).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1086","Harrison 22e p.1087","Harrison 22e p.1088","Harrison 22e p.1089","Harrison 22e p.1090","Harrison 22e p.1091","Harrison 22e p.1092"],"crossLinks":[]},"urinary_tract_obstruction":{"name":"Urinary Tract Obstruction","system":"Nephrology / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["obstructive uropathy","hydronephrosis"],"chapter":331,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2453","clinicalPearls":["Urinary tract obstruction (UTO) is a common and potentially reversible cause of both acute and chronic kidney disease (obstructive nephropathy); early recognition and prompt relief can prevent or reverse devastating effects on kidney structure and function (p.2453).","Any patient with otherwise unexplained renal failure, or with a history of nephrolithiasis, hematuria, diabetes mellitus, prostatic enlargement, pelvic surgery, trauma, or tumor should be evaluated for UTO (p.2455-2456).","When suspected, a bladder catheter should be inserted and abdominal ultrasonography performed to assess renal/bladder size and pyelocalyceal contour; bladder volume can be readily assessed at the bedside by ultrasound (p.2454-2455).","Flank pain is the most common presenting symptom and is due to distention of the collecting system or renal capsule; its severity is influenced more by the rate at which distention develops than by the degree of distention (p.2454).","Complete bilateral obstruction should be suspected when acute renal failure is accompanied by anuria (p.2455).","Wide fluctuations in urine output in an azotemic patient should always raise the possibility of intermittent or partial UTO (p.2455).","Azotemia with hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis (acquired distal renal tubular acidosis pattern) should prompt consideration of UTO (p.2455).","Flank pain that occurs only with micturition is pathognomonic of vesicoureteral reflux (p.2455).","Lower obstruction above the bladder causes unilateral hydroureter and hydronephrosis, whereas lesions at or below the bladder cause bilateral involvement (p.2454).","Posterior urethral valves are the most common cause of bilateral hydronephrosis in boys; in adults UTO is usually due to acquired defects, with pelvic tumors, calculi, and urethral stricture predominating (p.2454).","As many as 50% of men aged >40 years may have lower urinary tract symptoms associated with benign prostatic hypertrophy, but these symptoms may occur without bladder outlet obstruction (p.2454).","Noncontrast CT is the imaging test of choice for acute flank pain and improves visualization of the urinary tract in renal impairment; retrograde/antegrade urography carry no risk of contrast-induced AKI in patients with renal insufficiency (p.2455-2456)."],"pathophysiology":"Obstruction to urine flow produces stasis and a rise in hydrostatic pressure proximal to the blockade; this pressure is transmitted to the glomerular urinary space, decreasing or stopping filtration. Acutely, bilateral obstruction increases renal blood flow with rising ureteral/tubule pressures (mediated by vasodilator prostaglandins and nitric oxide), then renal blood flow and GFR fall with chronicity as vasoconstrictor prostaglandins and renin-angiotensin production increase. Tubular dysfunction develops: loss of medullary hypertonicity and concentrating ability (AVP-insensitive polyuria, a form of acquired nephrogenic diabetes insipidus from dysregulation of aquaporin-2 water channels), natriuresis from downregulation of proximal salt reabsorption and transporters (Na+/K+-ATPase, NKCC2, ENaC), and partial bilateral UTO causing acquired distal RTA with hyperkalemia and renal salt wasting (disrupted H+-ATPase trafficking in alpha-intercalated cells, reduced ENaC-driven Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion, impaired ammonium excretion). The renal interstitium becomes edematous with mononuclear infiltrate early; later interstitial fibrosis and atrophy of papillae and medulla precede cortical involvement. Increased angiotensin II drives inflammation and fibroblast accumulation via profibrotic cytokines, progressing to chronic kidney damage and permanent loss of renal mass (renal atrophy). Obstruction can be mechanical (intrinsic or extrinsic) at any level from the renal tubules to the urethral meatus, or functional from abnormal pontine/sacral micturition control (e.g., neurogenic bladder). Genetic/fetal causes (CAKUT, LUTO) reflect reduced nephron number, as there is no nephrogenesis after birth (p.2453-2456).","additionalDifferentials":["Benign prostatic hyperplasia (bladder outlet obstruction)","Carcinoma of prostate, bladder, cervix, colon, or rectum","Retroperitoneal fibrosis","Lymphoma (particularly follicular) with retroperitoneal involvement","Calculi (ureteric, bladder, urethral)","Urethral stricture","Ureteropelvic junction (UPJ) narrowing/obstruction","Vesicoureteral reflux","Posterior urethral valves (boys)","Neurogenic bladder / functional obstruction (diabetic neuropathy, spinal cord disease, cauda equina syndrome)","Pregnant uterus / uterine leiomyomata","Accidental surgical ligation or injury of the ureter during pelvic or colonic surgery","Aortic aneurysm (extrinsic ureteric compression)","Drug-related urinary retention (anticholinergics, alpha-adrenergic agonists, opiates)"],"infectionMimics":["Pyelonephritis (urinary stasis from obstruction encourages organism growth; UTO must always be considered with UTIs)","Generalized sepsis from an infected obstructed system"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Prerenal azotemia (acute partial bilateral obstruction may mimic it with high BUN-to-creatinine ratio, concentrated urine, and sodium retention)","Renal cystic disease (congenital UPJ obstruction may be mistaken for it; extrarenal pelvis is a normal variant causing false-positive hydronephrosis)","Other causes of polyuria/nocturia and concentrating defects","ADPKD and other ciliopathies (cystic compression of normal nephrons leading to fibrosis)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Bladder catheter insertion and postvoid residual measurement","Bedside bladder ultrasound to assess bladder volume","Abdominal ultrasonography (~90% sensitive and specific for hydronephrosis)","Noncontrast multidetector CT (test of choice for acute flank pain; visualizes retroperitoneum and intrinsic/extrinsic sites of obstruction; characterizes stones by Hounsfield units)","Duplex Doppler ultrasonography (increased resistive index)","Retrograde or antegrade urography (no contrast-induced AKI risk in renal insufficiency)","Voiding cystourethrography (vesicoureteral reflux, bladder neck and urethral obstruction; postvoid residual urine)","Cystourethroscopy and urodynamic studies (cystometry, pressure-flow analysis)","Radionuclide (renal) scan for differential renal function and to predict reversibility after prolonged decompression","Abdominal scout film/KUB (insensitive; may show nephrocalcinosis or radiopaque stone)","Furosemide-augmented imaging to distinguish functional from anatomic obstruction"],"redFlags":["UTO complicated by infection (infected obstructed system) requires immediate relief of obstruction to prevent generalized sepsis and progressive renal damage; sepsis necessitates prompt urologic intervention with drainage (nephrostomy, ureterostomy, or catheterization)","Acute renal failure with anuria suggesting complete bilateral obstruction","Overflow incontinence with sudden-onset fecal incontinence, severe lower back pain, and saddle anesthesia requires emergency evaluation for cauda equina syndrome","Complete obstruction with infection can lead to total destruction of the kidney within days","Fetal LUTO with maternal oligohydramnios or anhydramnios (risk of pulmonary hypoplasia and infant death if untreated)"],"prognosis":"With relief of obstruction, return of renal function depends largely on whether irreversible renal damage has occurred. If obstruction is not relieved, course depends on whether it is complete or incomplete, bilateral or unilateral, and whether infection coexists. Complete obstruction with infection can destroy the kidney within days. Partial return of GFR may follow relief of complete obstruction of 1-2 weeks' duration, but after 8 weeks of obstruction recovery is unlikely. In the absence of definitive evidence of irreversibility, every effort should be made to decompress; a renal radionuclide scan after a prolonged period of decompression may predict reversibility. Chronic fibrotic progression to a failing kidney has no demonstrated reversal of scarring to date (p.2453, 2456-2457).","pitfalls":["UTO is a reversible cause of AKI that must be actively excluded in any unexplained AKI (insert bladder catheter and perform bladder scan/ultrasound) - the urine sediment is often normal even with marked azotemia and extensive structural damage.","Hydronephrosis may be ABSENT on ultrasound when obstruction is <48 h in duration, or associated with volume contraction, staghorn calculi, retroperitoneal fibrosis, or infiltrative renal disease - a negative ultrasound does not exclude obstruction when suspicion remains high.","False-positive hydronephrosis can occur with diuresis, renal cysts, or a normal extrarenal pelvis.","Insidious obstruction (e.g., chronic UPJ narrowing) may cause little or no pain yet result in total destruction of the affected kidney; unilateral ureteric injury during surgery may remain undetected.","After relief, anticipate postobstructive diuresis (see below) - iatrogenic over-replacement of fluids can sustain the diuresis, while under-recognition can lead to profound dehydration and vascular collapse.","NSAIDs for renal colic must account for potential renal harm, and opiates in patients with decreased renal function may be dangerous and used with caution.","Alpha-adrenergic blockers for BPH carry a risk of intraoperative floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery."],"severityClassification":"No formal severity grade; characterized along clinically meaningful axes per Tables 331-1 and 331-2: by level (upper tract/ureter vs bladder outlet vs urethra), laterality (unilateral hydroureter/hydronephrosis vs bilateral involvement at/below the bladder), completeness (complete vs partial/incomplete), acuity (acute vs chronic vs release/postobstructive phase), and presence of superimposed infection. Postobstructive diuresis follows relief of bilateral, but not unilateral, complete obstruction.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2453","Harrison 22e p.2454","Harrison 22e p.2455","Harrison 22e p.2456","Harrison 22e p.2457"],"crossLinks":["aki","renal_colic","ckd"]},"urticaria_angioedema":{"name":"Urticaria, Angioedema & Allergic Rhinitis","system":"Immunology / Allergy","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hives","urticaria","hereditary angioedema","allergic rhinitis"],"chapter":363,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2804-2811","clinicalPearls":["Urticaria (hives) are well-circumscribed wheals (dilation of superficial dermal vessels) that are intensely pruritic, often migrate, individually last <24 h, and resolve without bruising or scarring; angioedema arises from deeper dermis/subcutaneous tissue with more pain than pruritus and minimal erythema, taking hours to days to resolve (p.2807).","Urticaria and angioedema reflect similar pathophysiology at different skin levels and often co-occur, with roughly 40% of patients reporting both; together they affect >20% of the population at some point in life (p.2807).","Acute urticaria/angioedema are episodes lasting <6 weeks (usually food, environmental, or drug allergen, or viral infection); attacks persisting >6 weeks are termed chronic and are often spontaneous/idiopathic with no identifiable cause. More than two-thirds of new-onset cases are ultimately diagnosed as acute (p.2807).","Inducible (physical) urticarias are identified by the precipitating stimulus: dermatographism (linear wheal after brisk stroke, affects 2-5%), cholinergic urticaria (small 1-2 mm pruritic wheals with large surrounding erythema triggered by a rise in core body temperature such as fever, hot bath, or exercise), cold, solar, pressure, vibratory, heat, aquagenic, and contact urticaria; most inducible-urticaria lesions individually last <2 h (p.2807-2808).","Hereditary angioedema (HAE) is a fully penetrant autosomal dominant disorder from SERPING1 mutation causing C1 inhibitor (C1INH) deficiency (type 1, ~85%) or dysfunctional protein (type 2); presentation in childhood/adolescence and a family history of angioedema are distinguishing features (p.2808).","HAE diagnosis is suggested by family history, early age of onset, lack of pruritus and urticarial lesions, recurrent gastrointestinal colic, episodes of laryngeal edema, and failure to respond to H1 antihistamines; laboratory confirmation shows low C1INH antigen (type 1) or nonfunctional protein (type 2), with chronically depleted C4 and C2 that fall further during attacks (p.2808-2809).","Cold urticaria can be confirmed by ice-cube placement on the volar forearm provoking a wheal within minutes; cryoglobulins or cold agglutinins are present in up to 5% of these patients, and immersion in cold water (swimming) can progress to vascular collapse (p.2807-2808).","Up to 45% of chronic urticaria has an autoimmune basis, including autoantibodies to IgE or to the alpha chain of FcepsilonRI, and may be associated with antithyroid antibodies (antimicrosomal peroxidase/thyroglobulin) in both Hashimoto's thyroiditis and a euthyroid state (p.2808).","Allergic rhinitis is characterized by sneezing, rhinorrhea, nasal obstruction, lacrimation, and itching of the conjunctiva/nasal mucosa/oropharynx; the nasal mucosa is classically pale and boggy. It requires a temporal relationship to allergen exposure plus documented IgE sensitization (p.2808-2809).","Allergic rhinitis usually occurs in atopic individuals and overlaps strongly with other atopic disease: up to 50% of allergic rhinitis patients have asthma, while 70-80% of asthmatics and 80% of those with chronic bilateral sinusitis have allergic rhinitis (p.2809).","Seasonal allergic rhinitis is elicited by wind-pollinated tree, grass, and weed pollens (in temperate North America: trees Mar-May, grasses Jun-early Jul, weeds mid-Aug-early Oct); perennial rhinitis is driven by year-round allergens such as dust mites (Dermatophagoides), animal dander, cockroach proteins, and mold spores (p.2809).","Pollen-associated (oral allergy) food syndrome causes oropharyngeal pruritus and/or mild swelling after ingesting plant foods cross-reactive with the offending tree, grass, or weed pollen (p.2809)."],"pathophysiology":"Atopy is a tendency to manifest asthma, rhinitis, urticaria, food allergy, and atopic dermatitis in association with allergen-specific IgE, though urticaria and anaphylaxis can also occur in non-atopic individuals. Mast cells are the key effector cells: IgE binds the high-affinity receptor FcepsilonRI (sensitization), and antigen-driven receptor aggregation triggers Lyn/Syk/Fyn tyrosine-kinase signaling, calcium influx, and degranulation. This releases preformed granule mediators (histamine, tryptase, chymase, carboxypeptidase A, proteoglycans), newly generated lipid mediators (PGD2 via cyclooxygenase; LTB4 and cysteinyl leukotrienes LTC4/D4/E4 via 5-lipoxygenase/FLAP; PAF), and cytokines/chemokines (TNF-alpha, IL-4, IL-5, IL-13, GM-CSF). Mast cells can also be activated IgE-independently via C5a/C3a anaphylatoxin receptors, TLRs, alarmin (TSLP, IL-33) receptors, and MRGPRX2 (a target of small-molecule drugs such as ciprofloxacin and rocuronium and of neuropeptides). Histamine and lipid mediators increase venular permeability producing dermal edema (urticaria) or deeper subcutaneous/dermal edema (angioedema). Cold urticaria provides the best evidence for IgE/mast-cell involvement, with documented mast cell degranulation and elevated lesional histamine. In contrast, bradykinin-mediated angioedema (HAE from C1INH deficiency, or ACE inhibitor-induced) results from excess bradykinin: C1INH normally inhibits activated factor XII, kallikrein, and C1r/C1s, and its deficiency leads to bradykinin accumulation with falling C4/C2. Allergic rhinitis arises from sensitized intraepithelial and perivenular nasal mast cells that, on allergen exposure, release histamine, PGD2, and leukotrienes, producing submucosal edema and eosinophilic infiltration.","additionalDifferentials":["Acute vs chronic urticaria/angioedema (cutoff 6 weeks duration)","Inducible (physical) urticarias: dermatographism, cholinergic, cold, solar, pressure, vibratory, heat, aquagenic, contact","Urticarial vasculitis (immune-complex disease; small-vessel leukocytoclastic vasculitis)","Mastocytosis (cutaneous or systemic; urticaria pigmentosa)","Hereditary angioedema (C1INH type 1/2; type 3 with normal C1INH e.g. factor XII mutation)","Acquired C1INH deficiency (lymphoproliferative or autoimmune disease)","ACE inhibitor-induced (bradykinin-mediated) angioedema","CIAS1/NLRP3-associated periodic fever syndromes (familial cold autoinflammatory syndrome, Muckle-Wells syndrome)","Schnitzler's syndrome, Gleich's syndrome, hypereosinophilic syndrome","Serum sickness and hypocomplementemic urticarial vasculitis","Allergic rhinitis: nonallergic rhinitis with eosinophilia (NARES), vasomotor/perennial nonallergic rhinitis, rhinitis medicamentosa, gustatory rhinitis, hormonal (pregnancy) rhinitis, drug-induced rhinitis, hypothyroidism, anatomic/structural nasal defects, nasal foreign body"],"infectionMimics":["Viral infection-associated acute urticaria (a common trigger of new-onset acute urticaria)","Bacterial or parasitic infection-associated urticaria (travel history should prompt parasite evaluation)","Hepatitis B or C and SARS-CoV-2 infection associated with urticaria/urticarial vasculitis","Upper respiratory tract infection mimicking rhinitis; secondary bacterial sinusitis/otitis from mucosal swelling"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Urticarial vasculitis (lesions >24 h, painful/burning rather than pruritic, residual petechiae/bruising)","Mastocytosis presenting with flushing and hyperpigmented papules that urticate on stroking (Darier's sign)","Thyroid disease (hypo-/hyperthyroidism) and autoimmune thyroiditis","Serum sickness / serum sickness-like reactions","Vasomotor (nonallergic) rhinitis and NARES mimicking allergic rhinitis"],"additionalInvestigations":["History-directed skin testing (puncture/prick) or serum allergen-specific IgE for suspected allergen triggers","Provocation/threshold testing for inducible urticaria (e.g. ice-cube test for cold urticaria; stroking for dermatographism)","Limited labs in unrevealing chronic urticaria: CBC with eosinophil assessment, ESR or CRP (most chronic urticaria has no lab abnormality)","Skin biopsy for lesions lasting >36 h, scarring, or painful/non-pruritic - to assess for urticarial vasculitis (cellular infiltrate, nuclear debris, fibrinoid venular necrosis)","For isolated angioedema: basic chemistries with liver function tests and complement levels","C1INH antigen level and functional (catalytic inhibition) assay, with C4 and C2 levels, for suspected HAE; C1q and C1 function additionally reduced in acquired C1INH deficiency","Evaluation for paraproteinemia/lymphoproliferative disease where presentation suggests acquired C1INH deficiency","Autologous serum skin test and basophil activation markers (CD63/CD203) for autoimmune chronic urticaria; antithyroid antibodies","Nasal secretion eosinophils and serum/skin specific IgE for allergic rhinitis (skin test more sensitive; serum specific IgE less sensitive but highly specific)"],"redFlags":["Angioedema of the upper respiratory tract / larynx may be life-threatening due to transient laryngeal obstruction - airway emergency","Gastrointestinal angioedema with abdominal colic can mimic an acute abdomen and lead to unnecessary surgical intervention","Cold urticaria with cold-water immersion may progress to vascular collapse","Progression of urticaria/angioedema to anaphylaxis with vascular collapse (e.g. exercise-induced anaphylaxis, food/drug triggers)","Urticarial lesions lasting >24-36 h with bruising/petechiae, scarring, or burning/pain rather than pruritus suggest urticarial vasculitis and warrant biopsy"],"prognosis":"More than two-thirds of new-onset urticaria cases are ultimately acute and self-limited. Chronic spontaneous urticaria is most common in the third to fifth decades and the vast majority shows no laboratory abnormality. Dermatographism generally has a duration of <5 years. Allergic rhinitis prevalence in North America is 10-30% (peak >30% in the fifth decade). Allergen immunotherapy (SCIT/SLIT) for allergic rhinitis provides durable symptom benefit and can reduce asthma symptoms; guidelines recommend 3-5 years of SCIT with discontinuation after minimal symptoms over two consecutive allergen seasons.","pitfalls":["Failing to distinguish histaminergic from bradykinin-mediated angioedema: bradykinin-mediated angioedema (hereditary angioedema from C1INH deficiency, and ACE inhibitor-induced) is conspicuously WITHOUT concomitant urticaria or pruritus, frequently involves the GI tract, lasts >24 h, and characteristically does NOT respond to H1 antihistamines (nor to adrenaline) - treat with C1INH replacement, bradykinin B2-receptor antagonist (icatibant), or kallikrein inhibitor rather than antihistamines.","Mistaking urticarial vasculitis for simple urticaria: individual lesions persist >24 h, may leave bruising/petechiae and scarring, and cause burning rather than pruritus - biopsy shows leukocytoclastic vasculitis and may signal underlying SLE, Sjogren's, or complement deficiency.","Overlooking laryngeal angioedema as an airway emergency, or mistaking GI angioedema for a surgical abdomen.","Topical glucocorticoids are of no value in urticaria, and systemic glucocorticoids are generally avoided in idiopathic, allergen-induced, or inducible urticaria due to long-term toxicity.","Prolonged use of topical alpha-adrenergic nasal decongestants (7-14 days) causes rebound rhinitis medicamentosa.","Concomitant beta-adrenergic blocker therapy increases the risk of severe/refractory anaphylaxis during allergen immunotherapy; immunotherapy is contraindicated in significant cardiovascular disease or unstable asthma."],"severityClassification":"Urticaria/angioedema classified by duration: acute = episodes lasting <6 weeks; chronic = attacks persisting >6 weeks (Table 363-1 further classifies by precipitating factor into acute vs chronic categories, including drug, food, infection, physical/inducible, autoimmune, vasculitic, mastocytosis, and hereditary forms). Hereditary angioedema typed as: type 1 (low C1INH antigen, ~85%), type 2 (dysfunctional C1INH protein), and type 3 (normal C1INH/complement, often identifiable gene mutation e.g. factor XII). Allergic rhinitis classified as seasonal vs perennial, and managed by severity (mild vs moderate-to-severe).","references":["Harrison 22e p.2804-2811"],"crossLinks":["anaphylaxis","angioedema_acei","mastocytosis"]},"uterine_fibroids":{"name":"Uterine fibroids","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["leiomyoma","myoma","fibromyoma","uterine leiomyomata"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Fibroids are benign monoclonal tumours of uterine smooth muscle and are the most common pelvic tumour in women","Many are asymptomatic; when symptomatic, they commonly cause heavy or prolonged menstrual bleeding, pelvic pressure, bulk-related urinary or bowel symptoms, and sometimes subfertility","Location matters more than size: submucosal fibroids are most associated with abnormal bleeding and reproductive problems, while subserosal fibroids tend to cause pressure symptoms","They are oestrogen and progesterone dependent, so they typically grow during the reproductive years and regress after the menopause","Treatment is guided by symptoms, fibroid characteristics, and the woman's fertility wishes, ranging from expectant management to medical therapy, uterine artery embolisation, myomectomy, or hysterectomy","Medical options aim to reduce bleeding and shrink fibroids, including hormonal treatments and agents that suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis","Rapid growth, particularly after the menopause, should raise suspicion of the rare leiomyosarcoma","In pregnancy fibroids may undergo red (carneous) degeneration causing acute pain, and can be associated with malpresentation and obstructed labour"],"pathophysiology":"Fibroids arise from a single transformed myometrial smooth muscle cell that proliferates under the influence of ovarian sex steroids to form a well-circumscribed, pseudo-encapsulated mass of smooth muscle and extracellular matrix. Oestrogen and progesterone, along with local growth factors, drive their growth, which is why they enlarge in the reproductive years and shrink after menopause. Heavy bleeding results from an increased and distorted endometrial surface, altered local vasculature and prostaglandins, and impaired uterine contraction. As fibroids outgrow their blood supply they can undergo hyaline, cystic, calcific, or red degeneration.","additionalDifferentials":["Adenomyosis","Endometrial polyp","Ovarian mass","Uterine leiomyosarcoma","Pregnancy","Endometrial hyperplasia or carcinoma as a cause of abnormal bleeding"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Transvaginal and transabdominal ultrasound to characterise number, size, and location","Saline infusion sonohysterography or hysteroscopy to define submucosal fibroids and the cavity","MRI for detailed mapping before myomectomy or embolisation and to help distinguish adenomyosis","Full blood count to detect iron-deficiency anaemia from heavy bleeding","Endometrial sampling when there is abnormal bleeding with risk factors for endometrial pathology"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging uterine mass or postmenopausal growth suggesting leiomyosarcoma","Severe anaemia from heavy menstrual bleeding","Acute severe pain from fibroid degeneration or torsion of a pedunculated fibroid","Hydronephrosis from ureteric compression by a large mass","Acute urinary retention from a large or cervical fibroid"],"prognosis":"Fibroids are benign and most women have a good outcome; malignant transformation is very rare. Symptoms often stabilise or regress after the menopause. Recurrence after uterine-conserving treatments such as myomectomy is possible, whereas hysterectomy is definitive but ends fertility.","pitfalls":["Assuming all abnormal bleeding in a woman with fibroids is due to the fibroids and missing coexisting endometrial pathology","Overlooking submucosal fibroids as a cause of infertility or recurrent bleeding when only transabdominal imaging is used","Attributing a rapidly growing mass to a benign fibroid without considering sarcoma","Neglecting to treat iron-deficiency anaemia","Failing to consider fibroid degeneration as a cause of acute pain in pregnancy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"uterine_prolapse":{"name":"Uterine Prolapse","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["pelvic organ prolapse","uterovaginal prolapse","descent of the uterus","genital prolapse"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Uterine prolapse is descent of the uterus and cervix into or beyond the vagina due to weakening of the pelvic floor supports, and it often coexists with prolapse of the vaginal walls, bladder, and rectum","Typical symptoms include a sensation of vaginal bulge or something coming down, pelvic heaviness or dragging, and symptoms that worsen through the day and with standing or straining","Associated urinary, bowel, and sexual dysfunction are common and should be assessed directly, as women may not volunteer them","Major risk factors include vaginal childbirth, increasing age and menopause, chronically raised intra-abdominal pressure from obesity, chronic cough or constipation, and connective tissue laxity","Severity is graded by how far the leading part of the prolapse descends relative to the hymen, using a standardised staging system","Conservative management with pelvic floor muscle training and vaginal pessaries is appropriate for many women, particularly if surgery is undesirable or unsafe","Surgical options range from uterine-preserving procedures to hysterectomy with vault support, and the choice depends on symptoms, fertility wishes, and fitness","Vaginal oestrogen can improve atrophic tissue and support pessary use in postmenopausal women"],"pathophysiology":"The uterus and vagina are held in position by the levator ani muscles and the endopelvic fascia and ligaments, including the uterosacral and cardinal ligaments. Damage to these supports, most often from stretching and denervation during vaginal delivery, together with age-related and oestrogen-deficiency-related weakening of connective tissue and muscle, allows the pelvic organs to descend. Sustained increases in intra-abdominal pressure accelerate this process. As support fails, the uterus and cervix prolapse downward, frequently dragging the vaginal walls and adjacent bladder and rectum with them.","additionalDifferentials":["Cystocele or rectocele without significant uterine descent","Vaginal vault prolapse after hysterectomy","Enterocele","Large vaginal or cervical cyst or fibroid","Chronic uterine inversion","Urethral diverticulum or other vaginal mass"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Pelvic examination in different positions and with straining to define the compartments and stage","Assessment of pelvic floor muscle strength","Urinary assessment including post-void residual and urodynamics where incontinence complicates management","Consideration of imaging for large or atypical masses","Evaluation and treatment of contributing factors such as constipation, chronic cough, and atrophy"],"redFlags":["Ulceration, bleeding, or infection of exposed prolapsed tissue","Urinary retention or obstructed voiding from advanced prolapse","Hydronephrosis or renal impairment from ureteric kinking in severe procidentia","New abnormal bleeding requiring exclusion of malignancy","Complete procidentia that cannot be reduced, needing prompt specialist assessment"],"prognosis":"Uterine prolapse is a benign but often progressive quality-of-life condition; conservative measures relieve symptoms in many women, and surgery provides good results though prolapse can recur in other compartments over time. Prognosis is favourable overall, and attention to modifiable risk factors such as weight, constipation, and chronic cough helps limit progression and recurrence.","pitfalls":["Failing to ask about associated urinary, bowel, and sexual symptoms","Overlooking coexisting prolapse in other vaginal compartments","Missing occult stress incontinence unmasked after prolapse correction","Neglecting to exclude malignancy in a woman with abnormal bleeding","Not treating vaginal atrophy or constipation that undermines pessary use and surgical outcomes"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"varicella_zoster":{"name":"Varicella-Zoster Virus Infection","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["chickenpox","shingles","VZV","herpes zoster"],"chapter":198,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"1504-1508","clinicalPearls":["VZV causes two distinct clinical syndromes: primary infection (varicella/chickenpox), usually a benign childhood exanthematous vesicular illness, and reactivation of latent virus (herpes zoster/shingles), a dermatomal vesicular rash usually associated with severe pain (p.1504).","In chickenpox, skin lesions appear in successive crops over a 2-4 day period and are present in various stages of evolution simultaneously (maculopapules, vesicles, and scabs) on a given body region - the classic small vesicle on an erythematous base ('dewdrop on a rose petal') (p.1505).","Chickenpox is highly contagious (attack rate >=90% in seronegatives); patients are infectious ~48 h before the rash appears, throughout vesicle formation, and until all vesicles are crusted. Incubation is usually 14-17 days (range 10-21) (p.1505).","Herpes zoster results from reactivation of latent VZV in dorsal root ganglia; most patients have no recent exposure to others with VZV. Incidence is highest in the sixth decade of life and beyond; recurrent zoster is exceedingly rare except in immunocompromised hosts, especially those with AIDS (p.1505).","Zoster pain (dermatomal) may precede visible lesions by 48-72 h; in the prodromal stage before the rash appears, the diagnosis can be exceedingly difficult (p.1505-1506).","Dermatomes T3 to L3 are most frequently involved in herpes zoster (p.1505).","PCR detection of VZV DNA in vesicular fluid is the diagnostic method of choice; specimens for PCR include lesions, blood, and saliva. The Tzanck smear gives a rapid impression but has low sensitivity (~60%) (p.1507).","Patients with herpes zoster can transmit infection to seronegative individuals, resulting in chickenpox (not zoster) in the contact (p.1506)."],"pathophysiology":"VZV is a member of the Herpesviridae with double-stranded DNA (~125,000 bp), a lipid envelope, and an icosahedral nucleocapsid (~180-200 nm). Primary transmission is by the respiratory route, with localized replication (presumably nasopharynx) seeding the lymphatic/reticuloendothelial system and producing viremia, which manifests as diffuse, scattered skin lesions. Vesicles involve the corium/dermis with ballooning degeneration, multinucleated giant cells, and eosinophilic intranuclear inclusions; localized skin blood-vessel involvement can cause necrosis and epidermal hemorrhage. During chickenpox the virus infects dorsal root ganglia and remains latent (in sensory dorsal, cranial, and enteric ganglia) until an unknown trigger causes reactivation, producing herpes zoster; active zoster ganglia show hemorrhage, edema, and lymphocytic infiltration. Pulmonary involvement shows interstitial pneumonitis with giant cells, intranuclear inclusions, and hemorrhage; CNS infection produces perivascular cuffing similar to other viral encephalitides, while focal hemorrhagic necrosis (characteristic of HSV encephalitis) develops infrequently in VZV infection (p.1504).","additionalDifferentials":["For chickenpox: disseminated HSV infection in patients with atopic dermatitis; disseminated vesiculopapular lesions of coxsackievirus or echovirus infection; atypical measles (these rashes are more often morbilliform/hemorrhagic than vesicular) (p.1506)","Rickettsialpox - distinguished by the 'herald spot' (eschar) at the mite-bite site and a more pronounced headache; serology helps differentiate it from varicella (p.1506)","Mpox - consider in travelers from endemic areas and in HIV-infected individuals (p.1506)","Smallpox - bioterrorism concern; smallpox lesions are larger than chickenpox lesions and all at the same stage of evolution at any given time (p.1506)","For herpes zoster: both HSV and coxsackievirus infections can cause dermatomal vesicular lesions (p.1506)"],"infectionMimics":["Disseminated HSV infection (notably in atopic dermatitis) (p.1506)","Coxsackievirus infection (p.1506)","Echovirus infection (p.1506)","Atypical measles (p.1506)","Rickettsialpox (p.1506)","Mpox (p.1506)","Smallpox (p.1506)","HSV and coxsackievirus causing dermatomal vesicular lesions (mimicking zoster) (p.1506)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["PCR for VZV DNA in vesicular fluid - diagnostic method of choice; also performed on lesions, blood, and saliva (p.1507)","Tzanck smear (scraping of lesion base for multinucleated giant cells) - rapid but low sensitivity (~60%) (p.1507)","Viral isolation in susceptible tissue-culture cell lines (unequivocal confirmation) (p.1507)","Direct immunofluorescent staining of cells from the lesion base or immunoperoxidase detection of viral antigens (p.1507)","Serology: seroconversion or fourfold rise in antibody titer between acute- and convalescent-phase sera; FAMA (fluorescent antibody to membrane antigen) test and ELISA are the most sensitive serologic tools (p.1507)"],"redFlags":["Zoster ophthalmicus - involvement of the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve; a debilitating condition that can result in blindness in the absence of antiviral therapy. Lesions on the tip of the nose (Hutchinson sign) signal likely ocular involvement; refer immediately to an ophthalmologist (p.1505-1507)","Ramsay Hunt syndrome - geniculate ganglion (sensory branch of facial nerve) involvement causing pain and vesicles in the external auditory canal, loss of taste in the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, and ipsilateral facial palsy (p.1506)","Disseminated/progressive herpes zoster in immunocompromised hosts - patients with Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma are at greatest risk; cutaneous dissemination occurs in ~40% untreated, raising the risk of pneumonitis, meningoencephalitis, and hepatitis by 5-10% (p.1506)","Varicella pneumonia - the most serious complication of chickenpox; more common in adults (up to 20%) than children and particularly severe in pregnant women; typically requires ventilatory support (p.1505, 1507)","Visceral dissemination in immunocompromised patients - occurs in 30-50% of cases and is fatal 15% of the time without antiviral therapy (p.1505)","Perinatal varicella - mortality as high as 30% when maternal disease begins within 5 days before to 48 h after delivery (p.1505)","CNS involvement: encephalitis (0.1-0.2% of children with chickenpox), aseptic meningitis, transverse myelitis, Guillain-Barre syndrome; in zoster, symptomatic meningoencephalitis and rare granulomatous angiitis with contralateral hemiplegia (p.1505-1506)"],"prognosis":"In the immunocompetent host chickenpox is usually benign and self-limited (total duration ~7-10 days; skin may take 2-4 weeks to fully normalize). Childhood acute cerebellar ataxia is generally benign and does not require hospitalization. Postherpetic neuralgia is the most debilitating complication of zoster: uncommon in the young but affects at least 50% of patients over age 50, who report dermatomal pain for months after cutaneous healing. Even in immunocompromised patients, disseminated zoster is rarely fatal. Hematopoietic stem cell transplant recipients are at particularly high risk, with a mortality rate of ~10% for posttransplantation VZV infection. The Shingrix subunit vaccine showed 97.2% efficacy in adults >=50 years, although vaccine immunity wanes over time (p.1505-1507).","pitfalls":["Aspirin should never be given to children with chickenpox because of its association with Reye syndrome; aspirin is no longer used for this reason (p.1505, 1507)","Zoster sine herpetica: dermatomal pain with serologic evidence of zoster can occur in the absence of any skin lesions - shingles without a rash has been reported, making diagnosis difficult (p.1506)","In the prodromal stage of herpes zoster (before lesions appear), diagnosis may be possible only after lesions emerge or by retrospective serology (p.1506)","Glucocorticoids for zoster should not be used without concomitant antiviral therapy, and steroid use is appropriate only for relatively healthy elderly patients with moderate/severe pain; patients with osteoporosis, diabetes mellitus, glycosuria, or hypertension may not be appropriate candidates (p.1507)","VZIG is NOT indicated for a newborn if the mother has zoster (as opposed to varicella); it is indicated when maternal varicella onsets within 5 days before to 48 h after delivery (p.1507-1508)"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Whitley RJ. Varicella-Zoster Virus Infections. In: Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd ed, Chapter 198, pp. 1504-1508.","Arvin A. Aging, immunity, and the varicella-zoster virus. N Engl J Med 352:2266, 2005.","Cohen JI. A new vaccine to prevent herpes zoster. N Engl J Med 372:2149, 2015.","Gershon AA et al. Varicella zoster virus infection. Nat Rev Dis Primers 1:15016, 2015.","Gnann JW, Whitley RJ. Herpes zoster. N Engl J Med 347:340, 2002.","Lai H et al. Efficacy of an adjuvanted herpes zoster subunit vaccine in older adults. N Engl J Med 372:2087, 2015."],"crossLinks":[]},"varicocele":{"name":"Varicocele","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["varicocoele","scrotal varicocele","pampiniform plexus varicosity"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A varicocele is an abnormal dilatation of the pampiniform venous plexus, classically described as feeling like a bag of worms on scrotal palpation","The great majority occur on the left side, attributed to the left testicular vein draining at a right angle into the left renal vein and having a longer course","It is more prominent on standing and with Valsalva and typically decompresses on lying down; a varicocele that does not empty when supine should raise concern for a retroperitoneal or renal mass","A sudden new right-sided or non-reducing varicocele in an older patient warrants imaging to exclude a renal tumour obstructing venous drainage","Clinical grading ranges from subclinical (detectable only on imaging) to visible through the scrotal skin, with intermediate grades palpable with or without Valsalva","Varicoceles are a common and potentially reversible factor in male subfertility, associated with impaired sperm count, motility, and morphology, and sometimes with testicular growth arrest in adolescents","Most are asymptomatic or cause a dull dragging ache; treatment (surgical varicocelectomy or radiological embolisation) is considered for pain, infertility with abnormal semen analysis, or testicular growth failure in adolescents","Semen analysis and assessment of testicular volume guide management decisions in the fertility and adolescent contexts"],"pathophysiology":"A varicocele results from incompetent or absent valves in the internal spermatic (testicular) veins, leading to retrograde flow and dilatation of the pampiniform plexus. The predominance on the left relates to the anatomy of left testicular venous drainage into the left renal vein at a right angle with a longer, higher-pressure course. The resulting venous stasis is thought to impair testicular function through elevated scrotal temperature, venous hypertension, hypoxia, and reflux of adrenal or renal metabolites, contributing to defective spermatogenesis and, in some cases, testicular atrophy.","additionalDifferentials":["Spermatocele or epididymal cyst","Hydrocele","Inguinal hernia (indirect)","Testicular tumour","Epididymo-orchitis","Retroperitoneal or renal mass obstructing venous drainage (secondary varicocele)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Scrotal examination in both standing and supine positions with Valsalva","Scrotal colour Doppler ultrasound to confirm venous reflux and grade the varicocele","Measurement of testicular volume to detect growth arrest, especially in adolescents","Semen analysis in men presenting with subfertility","Abdominal/renal imaging when a right-sided, isolated, or non-reducing varicocele suggests a retroperitoneal cause"],"redFlags":["New-onset right-sided or non-decompressing varicocele suggesting a renal or retroperitoneal tumour","Progressive ipsilateral testicular atrophy or growth arrest in an adolescent","Acute painful scrotal swelling raising alternative diagnoses that require exclusion","Significant deterioration in semen parameters in a man seeking fertility"],"prognosis":"Many varicoceles are asymptomatic and require no intervention; when treated for pain, subfertility, or adolescent growth arrest, repair often improves semen parameters and testicular growth, though not all men achieve improved fertility.","pitfalls":["Failing to examine the patient standing and with Valsalva, missing a lower-grade varicocele","Overlooking a secondary varicocele from a renal tumour in an isolated right-sided or non-reducible case","Recommending repair for asymptomatic subclinical varicoceles without a clear indication","Neglecting testicular volume assessment in adolescents","Attributing subfertility solely to the varicocele without full evaluation of both partners"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"varicose_veins":{"name":"Varicose veins","system":"Vascular / Surgery","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["lower limb varicosities","varicosities","superficial venous insufficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery / Sabiston Textbook of Surgery","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Present as dilated, tortuous, superficial leg veins, often with aching, heaviness, itching, or swelling that worsens with prolonged standing and eases on elevation","Most arise from primary valvular incompetence in the great or small saphenous systems, allowing reflux and venous hypertension","Assessment includes standing inspection and duplex ultrasound to map the sites of reflux and incompetent junctions, which has largely replaced the older tourniquet tests","Chronic venous hypertension produces skin changes: haemosiderin pigmentation, venous eczema, lipodermatosclerosis, atrophie blanche, and ultimately venous ulceration around the gaiter area","Conservative measures include graduated compression stockings, weight loss, exercise, and leg elevation","Interventional options include endovenous thermal ablation (laser or radiofrequency), foam sclerotherapy, and conventional surgery (ligation and stripping)","Secondary varicose veins may follow deep vein thrombosis or pelvic outflow obstruction, so a history of DVT and signs of deep venous disease should be sought","Sudden bleeding from a ruptured varicosity is controlled by elevation and direct compression, not by a tourniquet"],"pathophysiology":"Varicose veins develop when incompetent valves in the superficial venous system, or at the saphenofemoral or saphenopopliteal junctions, permit reflux of blood back down the leg. The resulting venous hypertension dilates and elongates the superficial veins, making them tortuous. Sustained ambulatory venous hypertension is transmitted to the skin microcirculation, causing capillary leakage, fibrin deposition, inflammation, and progressive skin changes that culminate in venous ulceration. Contributing factors include heredity, prolonged standing, pregnancy, obesity, and previous deep vein thrombosis, the last causing secondary varicosities through deep venous obstruction or valvular damage.","additionalDifferentials":["Deep venous insufficiency or post-thrombotic syndrome","Chronic lymphoedema","Klippel-Trenaunay and other congenital venous malformations","Superficial thrombophlebitis","Arterial disease contributing to leg ulceration","Cellulitis of the lower leg"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Duplex ultrasound to map superficial and deep reflux and identify incompetent junctions and perforators","Ankle-brachial pressure index before compression therapy to exclude significant arterial disease","Assessment for prior deep vein thrombosis and deep venous patency","Photographs or clinical grading (e.g. CEAP classification) to document severity"],"redFlags":["Torrential haemorrhage from a ruptured surface varicosity requiring elevation and direct pressure","Non-healing venous ulcer, especially with atypical features suggesting malignant change (Marjolin's ulcer)","Signs of deep vein thrombosis or coexisting significant arterial disease before compression","Rapidly progressive skin changes or superficial thrombophlebitis extending toward the saphenofemoral junction"],"prognosis":"Symptoms and skin changes usually improve with compression and, where appropriate, ablation or surgery, and venous ulcers can heal with effective compression, but recurrence of varicosities and ulcers is common, particularly with deep venous disease or poor compliance.","pitfalls":["Applying compression without first excluding significant arterial disease","Overlooking underlying deep venous disease or previous DVT causing secondary varicosities","Attempting to control variceal bleeding with a tourniquet rather than elevation and compression","Missing malignant change in a chronic non-healing venous ulcer"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Bailey & Love's Short Practice of Surgery","Sabiston Textbook of Surgery"],"crossLinks":[]},"vascular_dementia":{"name":"Vascular Dementia","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vascular cognitive impairment","multi-infarct dementia"],"chapter":444,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3489","clinicalPearls":["Traditionally defined as dementia due primarily to one or more symptomatic strokes; ranked the second most frequent cause of dementia after Alzheimer's disease, and especially common where vascular risk factors are undertreated (p.3489).","The concept has been broadened to vascular contributions to cognitive impairment and dementia (VCID), reflecting that cerebrovascular pathology is highly prevalent in the elderly and contributes to cognitive impairment, whether in isolation or—more commonly—combined with neurodegenerative processes (p.3489).","Clinical evaluation focuses on identifying vascular risk factors (hypertension, diabetes mellitus, dyslipidemia, tobacco use, atrial fibrillation, coronary artery disease, peripheral vascular disease), prior stroke/TIA, and family history of early stroke or vascular disease (p.3491).","Although stepwise progression and deficits such as loss of executive function are particularly suggestive, most individuals with VCID follow the more typical pattern of gradual progression of impaired episodic memory (p.3491).","Patients often show a mixture of executive and visuospatial deficits with prominent psychomotor slowing, and may present with psychiatric symptoms such as depression, anxiety, delusions, disinhibition, or apathy (p.196).","Gait disturbance is common in vascular dementia (as well as PD/DLB and NPH) (p.195).","Strategic single infarcts in locations such as thalamus, medial temporal cortex, anterior corpus callosum, or dominant-side angular gyrus can sufficiently impair episodic memory and function to meet memory-based dementia criteria (p.3489).","At least some cognitive impairment is present in approximately half of stroke survivors and increases with longer follow-up; even patients with good cognitive recovery show accelerated poststroke cognitive decline (p.3489).","One late-onset community autopsy cohort attributed 25% of dementia risk to cerebrovascular disease (vs 39% Alzheimer's, 17% LATE, 12% Lewy body), and most late-onset dementia involves multiple coexisting pathologies (p.195)."],"pathophysiology":"Vascular dementia results from the cumulative impact of cerebrovascular disease on age-related cognitive decline. Symptomatic strokes (ischemic or hemorrhagic) produce irreversible injury whose cognitive effect depends on size and location. Cerebral small-vessel disease—principally arteriolosclerosis (thickening of arterioles from plasma-protein infiltration; risk factors age, hypertension, diabetes; centered on deep penetrating territories such as thalamus, basal ganglia, brainstem) and cerebral amyloid angiopathy (beta-amyloid deposition in walls of small cortical arteries/arterioles/capillaries; main risk factor advancing age; favoring lobar cortex and subcortical white matter)—is often clinically asymptomatic and produces lacunar infarcts, microinfarcts, cerebral microbleeds, and white matter hyperintensities. These small distributed injuries disconnect nodes within distributed brain networks. Mixed pathology is the rule: cerebrovascular lesions frequently co-occur with neurodegenerative disease (most commonly Alzheimer's), producing greater impairment than either mechanism alone, possibly via loss of blood-brain barrier integrity and impaired perivascular clearance of beta-amyloid (p.3489-3491).","additionalDifferentials":["Alzheimer's disease (most common dementia; mixed AD-vascular pathology very common)","Dementia with Lewy bodies / PDD spectrum","Frontotemporal dementia","Limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy (LATE)","Normal pressure hydrocephalus","Delirium"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Alzheimer's disease (frequently coexists as mixed dementia—a key pitfall)","Normal pressure hydrocephalus (shares gait disturbance)","Delirium (prominent attention/executive deficits)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Brain MRI is the mainstay for detection and subtyping of cerebrovascular disease; should include FLAIR, diffusion-weighted, and T2*-weighted sequences to detect large and small chronic infarcts, acute microinfarcts, microbleeds, and white matter hyperintensities (p.3491)","Diffusion tensor MRI (e.g., peak width of skeletonized mean diffusivity, PSMD) detects white matter disconnection (largely research use) (p.3490)","Vessel imaging (CT or MR angiography) is not required for initial cognitive evaluation but may help determine the cause of identified macroscopic infarcts (p.3491)","Screening for atrial fibrillation in individuals with asymptomatic infarcts; prolonged cardiac monitoring for embolic-appearing infarcts (p.3491)","Genetic testing for hereditary VCID (CADASIL, hereditary cerebral amyloid angiopathy) when young onset, positive family history, or suggestive imaging—otherwise unnecessary (p.3491)"],"redFlags":["Young onset, positive family history, or suggestive neuroimaging—consider hereditary forms such as CADASIL or hereditary cerebral amyloid angiopathy (p.3491)","Lobar intracerebral hemorrhage or convexity subarachnoid blood, suggesting cerebral amyloid angiopathy (p.3490)","Rapid progression with motor rigidity and myoclonus suggests CJD rather than vascular dementia (p.195)"],"prognosis":"Stroke survivors show accelerated poststroke cognitive decline even after good initial recovery; in one longitudinal study trajectory shifted from a prestroke cognitive gain to poststroke loss. Multiple strokes and larger infarcted volumes raise the likelihood of poststroke cognitive dysfunction. Epidemiologic data show declining age-adjusted dementia incidence paralleling improved hypertension control and stroke prevention, and the SPRINT-MIND trial showed intensive systolic blood pressure lowering reduced rates of mild cognitive impairment and combined MCI/probable dementia, underscoring VCID as a target for prevention (p.3489).","pitfalls":["Mixed dementia is the rule rather than the exception—cerebrovascular disease commonly co-occurs with Alzheimer's and other neurodegenerative pathologies, so attributing cognitive impairment to a single etiology is often incorrect (p.3489).","Most individuals with VCID do not show classic stepwise decline but instead a gradual progression of impaired episodic memory resembling Alzheimer's (p.3491).","White matter hyperintensities are an almost ubiquitous, nonspecific feature of aging (gliosis, demyelination, or increased water content) and should not be over-interpreted (p.3490).","Many chronic lacunar infarcts and microinfarcts cannot be readily identified on routine imaging, so absence of visible lesions does not exclude small-vessel disease (p.3490)."],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison 22e p.3489","Harrison 22e p.3490","Harrison 22e p.3491","Harrison 22e p.195","Harrison 22e p.196"],"crossLinks":["alzheimers_disease","ischemic_stroke"]},"ventilator_associated_pneumonia":{"name":"Ventilator-associated pneumonia","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VAP","ventilator associated pneumonia","ventilator-associated lower respiratory infection"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / IDSA","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Defined as pneumonia developing more than 48 hours after endotracheal intubation and mechanical ventilation","Suspected when a ventilated patient develops new or progressive infiltrates with fever, leukocytosis, purulent secretions, and worsening oxygenation","Common pathogens include Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Staphylococcus aureus (including MRSA), and other Gram-negative bacilli, with resistant organisms more likely in late-onset disease","Early-onset VAP tends to involve more antibiotic-susceptible organisms than late-onset VAP","Lower respiratory tract sampling for culture before starting antibiotics helps target and later de-escalate therapy","Management principles are prompt empiric antibiotics guided by local resistance patterns and patient risk factors, followed by de-escalation once cultures return","Risk factors for resistant organisms include recent antibiotic exposure, prolonged hospitalisation, and prior colonisation","Prevention bundles (head-of-bed elevation, sedation and ventilator weaning protocols, oral care, and subglottic secretion drainage) reduce incidence"],"pathophysiology":"The endotracheal tube bypasses upper airway defences and impairs the cough reflex and mucociliary clearance, while its surface accumulates a biofilm of microorganisms. Micro-aspiration of contaminated oropharyngeal and gastric secretions pooling above the cuff delivers pathogens to the lower airways. In critically ill patients the oropharynx becomes colonised with hospital flora, and impaired host defences allow these organisms to establish infection in the lung parenchyma, producing an inflammatory pneumonic process with impaired gas exchange.","additionalDifferentials":["Tracheobronchitis without parenchymal pneumonia","Acute respiratory distress syndrome","Pulmonary oedema from cardiac failure or fluid overload","Atelectasis","Pulmonary embolism or infarction"],"infectionMimics":["ARDS with new infiltrates but no infection","Aspiration pneumonitis without established infection","Alveolar haemorrhage","Drug-induced or chemical pneumonitis"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Endotracheal aspirate or bronchoscopic sampling for Gram stain and quantitative or semiquantitative culture","Chest radiography or CT to assess infiltrates and complications","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers","Arterial blood gases and oxygenation indices to gauge severity","Pleural fluid sampling if a significant effusion or empyema is suspected"],"redFlags":["Progressive hypoxaemia and haemodynamic instability indicating severe pneumonia and possible septic shock requiring urgent escalation","Failure to improve on empiric therapy suggesting resistant organisms, complication, or wrong diagnosis","Development of empyema, lung abscess, or necrotising pneumonia","Multidrug-resistant pathogen risk warranting broader empiric cover"],"prognosis":"VAP prolongs mechanical ventilation and ICU stay and is associated with substantial attributable mortality, which is higher with resistant pathogens such as Pseudomonas, inappropriate initial antibiotics, and severe underlying illness; timely appropriate therapy improves outcomes.","pitfalls":["Starting or continuing antibiotics for colonisation or non-infective infiltrates without adequate evidence of pneumonia","Choosing empiric therapy that ignores local resistance patterns and patient risk factors","Failing to de-escalate once culture results are available, promoting resistance","Overlooking preventable contributors and neglecting VAP prevention bundles"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","IDSA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"ventricular_septal_defect":{"name":"Ventricular Septal Defect","system":"Paediatric cardiology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VSD","ventricular septal defects"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common congenital heart malformation, an opening in the interventricular septum that permits shunting between the ventricles; most are membranous or muscular in location.","The direction and size of the shunt depend on the defect size and the relative resistances of the pulmonary and systemic circulations, so many defects become clinically apparent only as pulmonary resistance falls in the first weeks of life.","Small defects produce a loud, often harsh holosystolic murmur at the lower left sternal border but little haemodynamic burden, and many close spontaneously.","Large defects cause a significant left-to-right shunt with excessive pulmonary blood flow, leading to heart failure signs such as tachypnoea, poor feeding, sweating, and failure to thrive in infancy.","A large shunt may generate a mid-diastolic rumble at the apex from increased flow across the mitral valve.","Untreated large defects risk progressive pulmonary vascular disease that can eventually reverse the shunt (Eisenmenger physiology), a late and largely irreversible complication.","Echocardiography defines the defect location and size, estimates shunt magnitude, and assesses chamber enlargement and pulmonary pressures.","Management of a haemodynamically significant defect focuses on treating heart failure and supporting growth, with surgical or catheter closure for defects that do not shrink or that cause failure or pulmonary hypertension.","Many small muscular and membranous defects close on their own during early childhood."],"pathophysiology":"A communication in the ventricular septum allows blood to shunt according to the pressure and resistance gradient between the ventricles. After birth, as pulmonary vascular resistance declines, left-to-right shunting increases, raising pulmonary blood flow and volume-loading the left atrium and left ventricle. A large, sustained shunt increases pulmonary artery pressure and flow, and over time medial hypertrophy and intimal changes in the pulmonary vasculature raise resistance irreversibly; when pulmonary resistance exceeds systemic, the shunt reverses and produces cyanosis (Eisenmenger syndrome).","additionalDifferentials":["Atrioventricular septal defect","Patent ductus arteriosus","Atrial septal defect","Tetralogy of Fallot with a dominant VSD murmur","Innocent (functional) murmur","Aortic or pulmonary stenosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Echocardiography to localise the defect and quantify shunt and chamber size","Chest radiograph for cardiomegaly and pulmonary plethora in large shunts","Electrocardiogram for left or biventricular hypertrophy","Assessment of growth and feeding as markers of shunt significance","Cardiac catheterisation to measure pulmonary vascular resistance when pulmonary hypertension is suspected"],"redFlags":["Signs of congestive heart failure in infancy: tachypnoea, feeding difficulty, sweating, and failure to thrive","Development of pulmonary hypertension or a shrinking left-to-right shunt suggesting rising pulmonary resistance","New cyanosis or a diminishing murmur indicating shunt reversal (Eisenmenger physiology)","Poor weight gain despite medical therapy","Signs of infective endocarditis in a child with a known defect"],"prognosis":"Small defects have an excellent outlook and often close spontaneously. Large defects, if repaired before irreversible pulmonary vascular disease develops, generally have a good outcome; delayed closure risks fixed pulmonary hypertension and a markedly worse prognosis.","pitfalls":["Assuming a loud murmur means a large, dangerous defect when small defects are often the loudest","Missing a large defect in the newborn period before pulmonary resistance falls and the murmur becomes apparent","Overlooking progression to pulmonary vascular disease by delaying closure of a significant shunt","Attributing poor growth to feeding problems rather than an underlying significant shunt","Failing to recognise shunt reversal when cyanosis appears in a previously acyanotic child"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"ventricular_tachycardia":{"name":"Ventricular tachycardia","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VT","monomorphic ventricular tachycardia","polymorphic ventricular tachycardia","wide-complex tachycardia"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["VT is a wide-complex tachycardia arising below the bundle of His; any wide-complex tachycardia should be assumed to be VT until proven otherwise, especially in patients with structural heart disease or prior infarction","It is classified as non-sustained or sustained, and as monomorphic or polymorphic, distinctions that guide both diagnosis and management","Monomorphic VT usually reflects a fixed reentry substrate such as a myocardial scar, while polymorphic VT often indicates acute ischaemia or, in the setting of a long QT, torsades de pointes","ECG clues favouring VT over SVT with aberrancy include AV dissociation, capture and fusion beats, and very broad or concordant QRS morphology","Haemodynamically unstable sustained VT requires immediate synchronised cardioversion, and pulseless VT is treated as cardiac arrest with defibrillation","Stable sustained monomorphic VT may be treated pharmacologically under monitoring, but readiness to cardiovert must be maintained","Identifying and correcting reversible triggers such as ischaemia, electrolyte disturbance, and QT-prolonging drugs is essential","An implantable cardioverter-defibrillator is central to secondary prevention in patients with sustained VT and structural heart disease, and catheter ablation reduces recurrent episodes"],"pathophysiology":"Most sustained monomorphic VT is due to reentry around regions of myocardial scar, typically after prior infarction, where slow conduction through surviving fibres embedded in fibrosis creates a stable circuit. Polymorphic VT commonly arises from acute ischaemia producing heterogeneous repolarisation, or from delayed repolarisation in long QT syndromes, generating triggered activity that degenerates into torsades de pointes. Abnormal automaticity and triggered activity from afterdepolarisations also contribute, particularly with electrolyte disturbance, drug effects, or catecholamine excess. Sustained rapid ventricular rates impair filling and cardiac output and can degenerate into ventricular fibrillation.","additionalDifferentials":["Supraventricular tachycardia with aberrant conduction","Pre-excited tachycardia (antidromic AVRT or atrial fibrillation with an accessory pathway)","Ventricular fibrillation","Torsades de pointes in long QT syndrome","Paced rhythm with rapid tracking","Artefact mimicking wide-complex tachycardia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG during tachycardia and in sinus rhythm to assess morphology and baseline substrate","Electrolytes including potassium and magnesium, and review of QT-prolonging medications","Echocardiography and often cardiac MRI to define structural substrate and scar","Assessment for coronary ischaemia including angiography when indicated","Electrophysiology study to characterise the circuit and guide ablation","Ambulatory monitoring to capture non-sustained episodes"],"redFlags":["MEDICAL EMERGENCY: pulseless VT or degeneration into ventricular fibrillation is cardiac arrest requiring immediate defibrillation and resuscitation","Haemodynamically unstable sustained VT with hypotension, ischaemia, or reduced consciousness requiring urgent synchronised cardioversion","Polymorphic VT with a prolonged QT (torsades) requiring correction of the QT and specific measures","Incessant or recurrent VT (electrical storm) requiring intensive management"],"prognosis":"Prognosis is strongly tied to the underlying substrate and left ventricular function; VT in structurally normal hearts is often benign, whereas sustained VT with impaired ventricular function or scar carries a high risk of sudden death that is mitigated by defibrillator therapy and substrate-targeted treatment.","pitfalls":["Treating a wide-complex tachycardia as SVT with aberrancy when it is VT, potentially with hazardous AV nodal blocking drugs","Delaying cardioversion in an unstable patient in favour of drug therapy","Missing a reversible cause such as ischaemia, hyperkalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, or QT-prolonging drugs","Overlooking the need for defibrillator evaluation in secondary prevention after sustained VT with structural disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"vertebral_osteomyelitis":{"name":"Vertebral osteomyelitis","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["spinal osteomyelitis","spondylodiscitis","discitis-osteomyelitis","pyogenic spondylitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / IDSA","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Usually arises from haematogenous seeding of the vertebral body, with the lumbar spine most commonly affected","New or worsening back pain, often unremitting and worse at night, with or without fever should prompt consideration, especially with a recent bacteraemia","Staphylococcus aureus is the most frequent pathogen; consider Gram-negatives with urinary sources and Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Pott's disease) in endemic or chronic presentations","Risk factors include diabetes, injecting drug use, indwelling vascular catheters, recent bacteraemia, endocarditis, and immunosuppression","MRI with contrast is the imaging investigation of choice for early and accurate diagnosis and for detecting epidural extension","Obtaining a microbiological diagnosis, by blood cultures or image-guided biopsy, before starting antibiotics is important unless the patient is septic","Management principles are prolonged targeted antibiotic therapy guided by culture, with surgery reserved for neurological compromise, instability, or failure of medical treatment","Screen for infective endocarditis and other seeding foci when S. aureus bacteraemia is present"],"pathophysiology":"Pyogenic vertebral osteomyelitis typically begins when bacteria lodge in the vascular end-plate region of a vertebral body via haematogenous spread from a distant focus. Infection then spreads across the relatively avascular intervertebral disc to involve the adjacent vertebra, producing the characteristic pattern of spondylodiscitis. Progressive infection can form paravertebral or epidural abscesses, erode bone leading to vertebral collapse and deformity, and compress neural structures. Less commonly infection reaches the spine by contiguous spread or direct inoculation from surgery or trauma.","additionalDifferentials":["Mechanical or degenerative back pain","Vertebral compression fracture","Metastatic or primary bone tumour","Tuberculous spondylitis (Pott's disease)","Modic degenerative end-plate changes"],"infectionMimics":["Malignant infiltration of the vertebral body","Osteoporotic compression fracture","Erosive degenerative disc disease","Inflammatory spondyloarthropathy"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["MRI of the spine with contrast as the primary imaging test","Blood cultures and inflammatory markers (ESR and CRP) which are useful for diagnosis and monitoring","Image-guided or open biopsy for microbiological and, where relevant, histological diagnosis","Echocardiography to exclude endocarditis when S. aureus or other typical organisms are isolated","Investigations for tuberculosis or brucellosis when the clinical picture is subacute or endemic"],"redFlags":["New neurological deficit, radicular weakness, or bladder or bowel dysfunction suggesting epidural abscess with cord or cauda equina compression requiring emergency imaging and surgery","Signs of spinal instability or progressive vertebral collapse","Sepsis or endocarditis complicating the infection","Rapidly progressive, unremitting, or nocturnal back pain in an at-risk patient"],"prognosis":"With early diagnosis and appropriate prolonged antibiotics most patients recover, but delayed treatment or epidural abscess with cord compression can leave permanent neurological deficits; relapse is more likely with inadequate therapy duration or unaddressed source.","pitfalls":["Starting antibiotics before securing a microbiological diagnosis in a stable patient, obscuring the causative organism","Attributing persistent back pain to mechanical causes and delaying MRI","Missing an associated epidural abscess or endocarditis","Treating for too short a duration and precipitating relapse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","IDSA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"vesicoureteric_reflux":{"name":"Vesicoureteric reflux","system":"Genitourinary / Urology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VUR","vesicoureteral reflux","vesico-ureteric reflux"],"chapter":null,"source":"Smith & Tanagho's General Urology / Campbell-Walsh Urology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Vesicoureteric reflux is the retrograde flow of urine from the bladder up the ureter toward the kidney, predisposing to recurrent pyelonephritis and renal scarring, mainly in children","Primary VUR results from a congenitally short or abnormal submucosal ureteric tunnel with an incompetent vesicoureteric junction, whereas secondary VUR arises from raised bladder pressure due to obstruction or a neurogenic or dysfunctional bladder","It commonly presents with febrile urinary tract infections in infancy or childhood, or is detected during investigation of antenatal hydronephrosis","Severity is graded I to V by the voiding cystourethrogram appearance, from reflux confined to a non-dilated ureter up to gross dilatation and tortuosity with a severely dilated collecting system","The principal concern is reflux nephropathy: renal scarring from recurrent infection combined with reflux, which can lead to hypertension, proteinuria, and chronic kidney disease","Voiding cystourethrography is the standard test to confirm and grade reflux, while DMSA scintigraphy detects renal scarring and pyelonephritis","Management principles emphasise prevention and prompt treatment of infection, attention to bladder and bowel dysfunction, and continuous antibiotic prophylaxis in selected children, since many low-grade cases resolve spontaneously with growth","Surgical or endoscopic correction (ureteric reimplantation or subureteric injection) is reserved for high-grade, persistent, or breakthrough-infection cases"],"pathophysiology":"Vesicoureteric reflux occurs when the valve-like mechanism of the vesicoureteric junction fails, allowing urine to reflux from the bladder into the ureter and renal pelvis. Normally an adequate length of submucosal ureter within the bladder wall is compressed during bladder filling and voiding, preventing reflux; in primary VUR this tunnel is congenitally short or laterally placed. Secondary VUR develops when high intravesical pressures from bladder outlet obstruction or neurogenic/dysfunctional voiding overcome the junction. Reflux of infected urine and elevated pressures transmitted to the kidney cause pyelonephritis and scarring (reflux nephropathy), which underlies later hypertension and renal impairment.","additionalDifferentials":["Recurrent urinary tract infection without reflux","Obstructive uropathy (posterior urethral valves, PUJ obstruction)","Neurogenic or dysfunctional bladder (bladder-bowel dysfunction)","Antenatal hydronephrosis of other cause","Duplex collecting system with ectopic ureter or ureterocele","Primary megaureter"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Voiding (micturating) cystourethrography to confirm and grade reflux","DMSA renal scintigraphy to detect scarring and acute pyelonephritis","Renal and bladder ultrasound to assess for dilatation and anatomy","Urinalysis and culture during febrile illness","Assessment of renal function and blood pressure","Evaluation of bladder and bowel function (uroflowmetry, voiding diary) in older children"],"redFlags":["Febrile urinary tract infection with an obstructed or refluxing system risking urosepsis, a urological emergency requiring prompt drainage and antibiotics","Progressive renal scarring or reduced differential renal function","New or worsening hypertension or proteinuria indicating reflux nephropathy","High-grade reflux with breakthrough infections despite prophylaxis","Bilateral high-grade reflux with impaired renal function"],"prognosis":"Many children with low-grade primary reflux resolve spontaneously as they grow and do well with infection prevention; high-grade reflux, recurrent pyelonephritis, and established scarring carry a greater risk of hypertension and chronic kidney disease, making surveillance important.","pitfalls":["Failing to investigate recurrent febrile UTIs in a child for underlying reflux","Overlooking bladder and bowel dysfunction, which perpetuates reflux and infection","Missing secondary reflux from obstruction such as posterior urethral valves","Neglecting long-term monitoring of blood pressure and renal function in children with scarring","Delaying treatment of a febrile infection in an obstructed, refluxing system"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Smith & Tanagho's General Urology","Campbell-Walsh Urology"],"crossLinks":[]},"vestibular_neuritis":{"name":"Vestibular neuritis","system":"Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vestibular neuronitis","acute peripheral vestibulopathy","acute vestibular neuritis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as acute, prolonged, spontaneous vertigo lasting days, with nausea, vomiting, and gait imbalance but without hearing loss","When hearing is also affected the term labyrinthitis is used, implying inflammation of the whole labyrinth rather than the vestibular nerve alone","The spontaneous nystagmus is horizontal-torsional, beats away from the affected ear, and is suppressed by visual fixation, consistent with a peripheral lesion","The head impulse test shows a corrective saccade toward the affected side, indicating a peripheral vestibular deficit","The HINTS examination (head impulse, nystagmus direction, test of skew) is more sensitive than early MRI for distinguishing peripheral neuritis from a posterior circulation stroke","Central features such as direction-changing nystagmus, skew deviation, or a normal head impulse test in an acutely vertiginous patient should raise concern for stroke","Management centres on short-term symptom relief and early vestibular rehabilitation to promote central compensation, avoiding prolonged vestibular suppressants","A thought to be post-viral or inflammatory process, it usually follows a monophasic course with gradual recovery"],"pathophysiology":"Vestibular neuritis is attributed to inflammation of the vestibular nerve, possibly from reactivation of latent herpes simplex virus in the vestibular ganglion, causing acute unilateral loss of peripheral vestibular input. The superior vestibular division is most often affected. The sudden asymmetry between the two vestibular systems generates a strong sense of rotational vertigo and spontaneous nystagmus. Over subsequent days to weeks, central adaptive mechanisms recalibrate the vestibular imbalance (central compensation), which underlies clinical recovery even if peripheral function does not fully return.","additionalDifferentials":["Posterior circulation ischaemic stroke (cerebellar or brainstem)","Labyrinthitis (with hearing loss)","Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","Meniere disease","Vestibular migraine","Multiple sclerosis affecting brainstem vestibular pathways"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Bedside HINTS examination in acute continuous vertigo to separate peripheral from central causes","MRI with attention to the posterior fossa when central features or vascular risk are present","Audiometry to detect associated hearing loss suggesting labyrinthitis or other cause","Caloric testing or video head impulse testing to quantify vestibular function","Assessment of vascular risk factors when stroke is a consideration"],"redFlags":["Central signs (direction-changing nystagmus, skew deviation, normal head impulse test) suggesting stroke rather than neuritis","Acute vertigo with other focal neurological deficits such as dysarthria, limb ataxia, or weakness","New severe headache or neck pain raising concern for vertebral artery dissection","Sudden hearing loss accompanying vertigo, which may indicate a vascular or other serious cause"],"prognosis":"Most patients recover over days to weeks as central compensation develops, though some have persistent unsteadiness or motion sensitivity; early vestibular rehabilitation improves recovery and reduces chronic dizziness.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing a cerebellar stroke as vestibular neuritis by relying on imaging that can be falsely reassuring early","Prescribing prolonged vestibular suppressants, which impair central compensation","Assuming any acute vertigo is peripheral without performing the HINTS examination","Overlooking associated hearing loss that changes the differential","Delaying vestibular rehabilitation and prolonging disability"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Bradley's Neurology in Clinical Practice"],"crossLinks":[]},"vipoma":{"name":"VIPoma","system":"Endocrine / Metabolic","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vasoactive intestinal peptide-secreting tumour","Verner-Morrison syndrome","WDHA syndrome","pancreatic cholera"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / Endocrine Society","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A rare pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour secreting vasoactive intestinal peptide, producing the syndrome of watery diarrhoea, hypokalaemia, and achlorhydria","The diarrhoea is large-volume and secretory, meaning it persists during fasting, unlike osmotic diarrhoea","Profound potassium loss in stool causes hypokalaemia with weakness, and dehydration can be severe","VIP also tends to reduce gastric acid secretion (achlorhydria or hypochlorhydria) and may cause flushing and hypercalcaemia","Diagnosis relies on demonstrating a markedly elevated fasting plasma VIP level in the setting of secretory diarrhoea","Most tumours are located in the pancreas and a substantial proportion are malignant with metastases at diagnosis, so cross-sectional and functional imaging are needed for localisation and staging","Consider an association with multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 in appropriate clinical contexts","Initial management prioritises aggressive rehydration and correction of electrolyte disturbance, with somatostatin analogues to control secretion and surgery for resectable disease"],"pathophysiology":"A VIPoma is a functioning neuroendocrine tumour, usually arising in the pancreatic islets, that secretes excessive vasoactive intestinal peptide. VIP stimulates intestinal secretion of water and electrolytes while inhibiting absorption, producing a high-volume secretory diarrhoea that continues despite fasting. The consequent losses of potassium and bicarbonate in stool lead to hypokalaemia and metabolic acidosis, and VIP's inhibition of gastric acid secretion causes achlorhydria. VIP can also promote vasodilation and flushing and, through its effects on bone and calcium handling, contribute to hypercalcaemia. A significant proportion of these tumours are malignant and may have metastasised, most often to the liver, by the time of diagnosis.","additionalDifferentials":["Other secretory diarrhoeas such as gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome)","Carcinoid syndrome","Chronic infective or inflammatory diarrhoea","Laxative abuse","Medullary thyroid carcinoma causing diarrhoea","Systemic mastocytosis"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Fasting plasma VIP concentration","Serum electrolytes, bicarbonate, calcium, and glucose to characterise the metabolic disturbance","Stool studies to confirm a secretory pattern and exclude other causes","Cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI) of the pancreas and liver for localisation and staging","Somatostatin-receptor functional imaging for occult or metastatic disease","Screening for other endocrine tumours where MEN1 is suspected"],"redFlags":["Severe dehydration and haemodynamic instability from massive fluid loss","Life-threatening hypokalaemia with cardiac dysrhythmia","Metastatic disease, most commonly hepatic, at presentation","Refractory diarrhoea unresponsive to initial supportive measures"],"prognosis":"Symptom control with rehydration and somatostatin analogues is usually achievable, and complete surgical resection of localised disease can be curative; however, many tumours are malignant with metastases at diagnosis, and prognosis then depends on tumour burden and response to systemic therapy.","pitfalls":["Attributing chronic watery diarrhoea to common causes and delaying measurement of VIP","Failing to recognise the secretory nature of the diarrhoea because it persists with fasting","Under-resuscitating profound fluid and potassium losses","Overlooking a possible MEN1 association and associated tumours"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","Endocrine Society guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"viral_exanthems":{"name":"Viral Exanthems of Childhood","system":"Dermatological","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["viral rashes in children","childhood exanthems","exanthematous viral illness"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Viral exanthems are widespread skin eruptions accompanying systemic viral infections and are among the most common causes of rash with fever in children.","The classic childhood exanthems include measles, rubella, erythema infectiosum (parvovirus B19), roseola (human herpesvirus 6/7), and varicella, each with fairly characteristic patterns.","Diagnosis is often clinical, based on the morphology and distribution of the rash, its timing relative to fever, and associated features such as enanthems and prodromal symptoms.","Measles is distinguished by high fever with cough, coryza, and conjunctivitis, Koplik spots on the buccal mucosa, and a rash spreading from the head downward.","Roseola typically causes several days of high fever in a well-appearing infant, followed by a rash that appears as the fever resolves.","Erythema infectiosum produces the characteristic slapped-cheek facial appearance followed by a lacy, reticular rash on the limbs.","Most viral exanthems are self-limiting and managed supportively, but measles carries a notable risk of complications and remains vaccine-preventable.","Recognising non-viral mimics, especially meningococcaemia, scarlet fever, Kawasaki disease, and drug reactions, is essential because some require urgent specific treatment."],"pathophysiology":"In many viral exanthems the rash reflects the systemic phase of infection when virus disseminates through the bloodstream and, together with the host immune response, involves the skin. Some eruptions result from direct viral infection of skin cells (as in varicella, where the virus produces vesicular lesions), while others are largely immune-mediated, appearing as the immune response develops and sometimes coinciding with defervescence, as in roseola. The specific morphology, distribution, and timing are determined by the particular virus, its tropism, and the character of the immune reaction, which is why individual exanthems have recognisable patterns.","additionalDifferentials":["Meningococcaemia or other bacterial sepsis with petechiae or purpura","Scarlet fever","Kawasaki disease","Drug eruption or hypersensitivity reaction","Enteroviral exanthems","Other congenital or systemic infections with rash"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis based on rash pattern and associated features in most cases","Serology or PCR for specific viruses when confirmation is needed (e.g., measles, parvovirus)","Full blood count and inflammatory markers if a bacterial or inflammatory cause is considered","Public health notification and testing for notifiable infections such as measles","Investigations to exclude serious mimics when the child is unwell or the rash is atypical"],"redFlags":["Petechial or purpuric, non-blanching rash","Toxic or septic appearance","Signs suggesting Kawasaki disease (prolonged fever with mucocutaneous features)","Mucosal involvement, blistering, or skin sloughing suggesting a severe drug reaction","Features of measles complications such as pneumonia or encephalitis"],"prognosis":"The great majority of viral exanthems are benign and self-limiting with full recovery, though certain infections, notably measles and varicella in vulnerable hosts, and parvovirus in specific settings, can cause significant complications.","pitfalls":["Mislabelling a serious non-blanching or purpuric rash as a benign viral exanthem","Failing to consider Kawasaki disease in a child with prolonged fever and rash","Overlooking measles and its public health and complication implications","Attributing a drug hypersensitivity eruption to a viral cause"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"viral_gastroenteritis":{"name":"Viral Gastroenteritis","system":"Gastrointestinal","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Acute viral gastroenteritis","Norovirus gastroenteritis","Norwalk virus infection","Rotavirus gastroenteritis","Stomach flu"],"chapter":209,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1627","clinicalPearls":["Most viral gastroenteritis is caused by RNA viruses, but DNA enteric adenoviruses (types 40 and 41) are also recognized causes; the principal pathogens are rotavirus, norovirus, sapovirus, astrovirus, and enteric adenovirus.","Illness presents with acute onset of vomiting and/or diarrhea, often with fever, nausea, abdominal cramps, anorexia, and malaise; the diarrhea is characteristically watery and nonbloody, without mucus or fecal leukocytes.","Norovirus has become the leading cause of medically attended gastroenteritis in young children in the United States and other developed countries following the decline in severe rotavirus disease after routine rotavirus vaccination, and it affects all age groups.","Norovirus is responsible for roughly half of all reported gastroenteritis outbreaks in the United States and is the major cause of gastroenteritis epidemics worldwide; it also causes traveler's diarrhea and outbreaks among deployed military personnel.","The infectious dose for most viral agents is very low (about 10-100 viral particles), in contrast to the higher inoculum typically required for many bacterial pathogens; norovirus can spread by fecal-oral route, aerosolization of vomitus, contaminated fomites, and person-to-person contact.","Nearly all children worldwide are infected with rotavirus by 3-5 years of age; first symptomatic infection usually occurs after 3 months of age, disease incidence peaks at 4-23 months, and severity decreases with each repeat infection.","Protection against rotavirus correlates with virus-specific secretory IgA in the intestine (and to some degree serum); because intestinal IgA is short-lived, full protection is temporary, but successive reinfections build progressively greater immunity.","Susceptibility to norovirus is influenced by host genetics: histo-blood group antigen (HBGA) phenotype and secretor status affect attachment, and functional antibodies that block norovirus binding to HBGAs correlate with protective immunity.","Rotavirus antigenemia and viremia are common in acutely infected children, although serum antigen and RNA levels are far lower than stool levels; rotavirus is shed in very large quantities in stool (10^7-10^12 per gram).","In temperate climates viral gastroenteritis shows winter seasonality, whereas in tropical/year-round settings seasonal peaks are less pronounced; before vaccination, US rotavirus season began in the Southwest in autumn/early winter and migrated to peak in the Northeast in late winter/spring.","Fever is common with both rotavirus and norovirus but uncommon with the other viral agents; up to one-third of rotavirus patients may have a temperature above 39 degrees C.","Death is a rare outcome and usually results from severe dehydration in vulnerable hosts such as debilitated elderly patients and young children; acute gastroenteritis is a leading cause of childhood death in developing countries (roughly 0.5 million deaths annually) and a major cause of pediatric hospitalization (6-8%) in industrialized nations."],"pathophysiology":"Enteric viruses injure the small-intestinal mucosa to produce predominantly watery diarrhea. With human caliciviruses (Norwalk/norovirus), carbohydrates resembling human histo-blood group antigens on the gastroduodenal epithelium of secretor-phenotype individuals appear to serve as attachment ligands; infection causes reversible lesions of the upper jejunum with broadening and blunting of villi, microvillar shortening, epithelial vacuolization, crypt hyperplasia, and lamina propria infiltration by neutrophils and lymphocytes. These changes persist for at least 4 days after symptom resolution and produce malabsorption of carbohydrates and fats with reduced brush-border enzymes; adenylate cyclase activity is unchanged. No histopathologic change occurs in stomach or colon, but delayed gastric motor function is thought to drive the characteristic nausea and vomiting. Rotavirus infects and destroys mature enterocytes of the proximal small-intestinal villous epithelium; loss of absorptive cells plus proliferation of secretory crypt cells yields secretory diarrhea, while reduced brush-border enzymes leave unmetabolized disaccharides that cause osmotic diarrhea. The nonstructural rotavirus protein NSP4 acts as an enterotoxin altering epithelial permeability and function, and rotavirus may also stimulate fluid secretion via activation of the enteric nervous system.","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial gastroenteritis (e.g., Salmonella, Shigella, Campylobacter, Vibrio cholerae, enterotoxigenic E. coli) - distinguished by inflammatory/bloody diarrhea, fecal leukocytes, and epidemiologic features","Preformed bacterial toxin food poisoning (Staphylococcus aureus, Bacillus cereus) - very short incubation of hours","Clostridioides difficile colitis","Protozoal/parasitic diarrhea (e.g., Giardia, Cryptosporidium)"],"infectionMimics":["Bacterial enteritis (Salmonella, Shigella, Campylobacter jejuni, Vibrio, E. coli)","Staphylococcus aureus and Bacillus cereus preformed-toxin food poisoning","Clostridioides difficile colitis","Gastrointestinal symptoms of systemic respiratory viral infections (SARS-CoV, influenza A/H5N1, pandemic influenza A/H1N1)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Osmotic or secretory diarrhea from other causes","Carbohydrate malabsorption states","Other causes of acute vomiting without true gastroenteritis"],"additionalInvestigations":["Commercial enzyme immunoassays (EIA) for rotavirus and enteric adenovirus in stool","RT-PCR / multiplex molecular panels for norovirus and other enteric pathogens (increasingly used clinically)","Electron microscopy and polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (PAGE) for viral RNA - largely research/public-health tools","Fecal examination for leukocytes and blood to help separate viral (typically negative) from inflammatory bacterial causes","Stool culture on special media when bacterial pathogens are suspected"],"redFlags":["Signs of severe dehydration (especially in young children and debilitated elderly)","Inability to tolerate oral intake due to frequent vomiting","Bloody diarrhea or fecal leukocytes - should prompt evaluation for a bacterial/inflammatory cause rather than viral gastroenteritis","Protracted diarrhea with prolonged viral excretion or systemic dissemination in severely immunocompromised hosts (e.g., post-bone-marrow transplant rotavirus disease)"],"prognosis":"Viral gastroenteritis is generally self-limited. Human calicivirus illness has a sudden onset after a mean incubation of about 24 hours (range 12-72 h) and typically lasts 12-60 h; norovirus and sapovirus illness usually resolves in 1-3 days. Rotavirus illness begins abruptly after a 1-3 day incubation, with vomiting often preceding diarrhea, and gastrointestinal symptoms generally resolve in 3-7 days. Among healthy young adults the illness is rarely fatal but causes substantial morbidity and lost work time. The main serious complication is dehydration; death is uncommon and concentrated in the very young, the elderly, and those with debilitating conditions. Severely immunodeficient children may develop protracted rotavirus diarrhea with prolonged shedding and, rarely, systemic dissemination.","pitfalls":["Distinguishing viral from bacterial gastroenteritis on clinical and epidemiologic grounds alone is often unreliable; laboratory testing may be needed, and viral gastroenteritis is frequently a diagnosis of exclusion in practice.","Antibiotics and antimotility agents are inappropriate for viral gastroenteritis and are contraindicated.","Commercial stool EIA kits for norovirus have limited sensitivity and are most useful in outbreak investigation (many specimens tested) rather than for diagnosing individual patients.","Higher preexisting antibody to Norwalk virus does not guarantee protection - early studies paradoxically found higher antibody levels associated with greater susceptibility, later explained by host HBGA genetics rather than simple antibody titer.","Rotavirus has been associated with diverse conditions (SIDS, necrotizing enterocolitis, intussusception, Kawasaki disease, type 1 diabetes) and with respiratory/neurologic features, but no causal relationship has been confirmed for any of these.","Asymptomatically infected and pre-symptomatic persons can shed norovirus, and shedding can persist for weeks after recovery (prolonged in immunocompromised hosts), so a single negative or positive result must be interpreted with care."],"severityClassification":"Harrison does not provide a formal grading scale; clinical severity is described per agent and ranges from subclinical/mild infection to severe gastroenteritis with life-threatening dehydration. Relative clinical severity by virus: group A rotavirus is the most severe (predominantly children under 5), norovirus is moderate (all ages), and sapovirus, astrovirus, and enteric adenovirus are generally milder (mainly children under 5).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1627","Harrison 22e p.1628","Harrison 22e p.1629","Harrison 22e p.1630","Harrison 22e p.1631","Harrison 22e p.1632"],"crossLinks":[]},"viral_hemorrhagic_fever":{"name":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Ebola","Marburg","VHF"],"chapter":216,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1679","clinicalPearls":["VHF is an acute viral syndrome of severe disease and multisystem organ dysfunction sharing a common disruption of vascular function, stability, and integrity; overt hemorrhage is often NOT the most prominent or severe feature, so absence of bleeding does not exclude VHF (p.1674).","Travel to an endemic or active-outbreak area within the incubation period is a major diagnostic clue; rural exposures more often suggest VHF except for urban-host/vector agents (Seoul virus, dengue, yellow fever) (p.1675).","For filovirid (Ebola/Marburg) disease, the incubation period is 2-25 days (most commonly 7-10 days), beginning with a nonspecific influenza-like illness (sudden fever/chills, headache, myalgia, pharyngitis, arthralgia, maculopapular rash) before a second phase (~5-7 days on) with GI, respiratory, cardiovascular, and CNS involvement (p.1685).","Common filovirid hemorrhagic signs are subconjunctival injection, petechial rash, gingival bleeding, and bleeding at injection sites; frank epistaxis, hematemesis, hematuria, and melena occur but are LESS common, and severe blood loss is rare (except during/after childbirth) (pp.1684-1685).","Ebola/Marburg present with very similar clinical phenotypes that cannot be distinguished at the bedside and, for practical purposes, may be managed as the same disease (p.1685).","Early recognition is crucial: prompt isolation and hospitalization are indicated even in the absence of clinical signs, with rapid triage and ongoing vital-sign monitoring (p.1675).","Filovirids are highly infectious but not exceptionally contagious; human-to-human spread occurs by direct contact with an infected person or their body fluids/tissues, with NO evidence of aerosol or respiratory droplet transmission in natural outbreak settings (p.1679).","Persistence of filovirids in immune-privileged sites (most commonly semen of male survivors; rarely CNS, eye, placenta) can cause recrudescent disease and rare reignition of outbreaks; sexual abstinence or condom use is advised for male survivors until semen is shown to be filovirid-RNA free (pp.1686-1687)."],"pathophysiology":"Across VHFs, direct or indirect microvascular damage increases vascular permeability and (when platelet function is reduced) causes disruption and local hemorrhage, most evident in skin and mucous membranes; hypotension and shock reflect generalized circulatory dysfunction rather than life-threatening blood loss. Pathogenesis is incompletely understood and varies by virus, with direct vascular/parenchymal damage in some and soluble mediators dominating in others. For filovirids specifically, virions enter via skin lesions, mucosal surfaces, or parenteral inoculation; GP1,2 spikes engage cell-surface molecules and the intracellular receptor NPC1. First targets are macrophages, monocytes, and dendritic cells, and viral proteins (VP35, VP40, VP24) suppress innate immunity (e.g., type I interferon pathways), enabling high viral titers and widespread tissue dissemination. Hallmarks include profound immune dysregulation, massive lymphoid depletion with severe lymphopenia, coagulopathy (thrombocytopenia, elevated tissue factor, consumption of clotting factors, raised D-dimers, falling protein C; possible DIC, though controversial), hepatocyte necrosis with synthetic dysfunction contributing to coagulopathy, and endothelial dysfunction with increased vascular permeability leading to fluid redistribution, interstitial edema, and hypovolemic or septic shock (pp.1674-1675, 1683-1685).","additionalDifferentials":["Falciparum malaria (a most important mimic and a treatable, potentially fatal alternative)","Typhoid fever","Other treatable travel-associated febrile illnesses: shigellosis, leptospirosis, relapsing fever, rickettsial diseases","Other VHFs/high-consequence viral infections, in particular Lassa fever and yellow fever","Dengue and other viral hemorrhagic fevers in the bleeding/febrile traveler"],"infectionMimics":["Falciparum malaria","Typhoid fever","Enterohemorrhagic E. coli enteritis","Gram-negative septicemia (including shigellosis)","Meningococcal septicemia","Rickettsial infections","Fulminant viral hepatitis","Leptospirosis","Measles","Other high-consequence viral infections (Lassa fever, yellow fever)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Venomous snakebite","Warfarin (anticoagulant) intoxication","Acquired or inherited coagulopathies in the bleeding patient"],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR targeting one or more filovirid genes is the diagnostic assay of choice; in-field PCR platforms (e.g., Cepheid GeneXpert) are deployed during outbreaks","Acute-phase blood/serum is the preferred specimen as it usually contains high virion titers","Antigen-capture ELISA / immunochromatographic rapid diagnostic tests (currently limited; EBOV antigen RDT sensitivity restricts use largely to postmortem samples)","IgM- and IgG-capture ELISAs (including multiplex) for antibodies in later disease and in survivors","Virus isolation/plaque assays and electron microscopy require maximum-containment laboratories; in-field genome sequencing supports outbreak epidemiology","Supportive labs typically show leukopenia (then leukocytosis), thrombocytopenia, raised aminotransferases (AST > ALT) and GGT, raised creatinine/urea with proteinuria, electrolyte derangement, hypoglycemia, hypoalbuminemia, prolonged PT/PTT, elevated CPK, and markedly elevated CRP in severe illness","Hemoconcentration from vascular leakage is usually evident (most marked in HFRS and severe dengue)"],"redFlags":["Strict barrier nursing and infection-control isolation with appropriate PPE are mandatory for suspected VHF (except dengue, orthohantavirus, RVFV, or yellow fever); isolate and hospitalize even before clinical signs appear","Immediately notify infectious disease specialists and public health authorities if filovirid disease is suspected on epidemiologic or clinical grounds","Progressive multiorgan dysfunction: shock, acute kidney injury, CNS injury (encephalopathy, coma, seizures), respiratory failure, and severe hemorrhage signal critical illness","Exposure history raising suspicion: caves/mines, direct contact with bats, nonhuman primates, or bushmeat, contact with severely ill local residents, or patient-to-patient/healthcare-worker clusters in rural hospitals","Hemodynamic instability/hypotension and capillary leak with effusions and edema","Pregnancy and labor are associated with severe complications and poor outcomes (clotting-factor consumption, fetal loss, severe peripartum hemorrhage)"],"prognosis":"FVD is among the most severe acute human viral diseases and generally carries a poor prognosis, though with far more heterogeneity than historically assumed (the long-cited 70-90% case-fatality rate for EVD has required revision). Outcome likely depends on the specific filovirid, host factors (age, immune status, coinfections, host genetics), exposure route and dose, viral load, presence and severity of organ dysfunction, and critically the availability of filovirid-specific countermeasures and supportive/critical care. Even with effective EBOV-specific therapeutics, outcomes in severe EVD with high viral loads and complex organ dysfunction remain suboptimal. For other VHFs prognosis varies widely by agent (e.g., Argentinian/Bolivian hemorrhagic fevers can have case-fatality rates exceeding 50% in individual outbreaks) (pp.1675, 1685-1687).","pitfalls":["Filovirid/VHF disease cannot be diagnosed on clinical presentation alone; numerous more common febrile illnesses (especially malaria and typhoid) closely mimic it and are far more likely in non-outbreak settings, so do not anchor on VHF without epidemiologic context and laboratory confirmation.","Absence of overt hemorrhage does not exclude VHF, since hemorrhagic manifestations are often neither the most prominent nor the most severe feature.","Treat DIC only with clear laboratory evidence and feasible monitoring; there is no proven benefit otherwise.","Fluid management is a balancing act: VHF patients are commonly hypovolemic from insensible and GI losses, yet may also have decreased cardiac output, capillary leak, and oliguria requiring judicious fluids and inotropic/vasopressor support.","Do not assume respiratory/aerosol spread for filovirids in natural settings and do not reuse medical equipment (gloves, syringes) used for an infected patient."],"severityClassification":"No formal numeric severity score is provided; clinically, the illness progresses from an incubation period through a nonspecific febrile prodrome (early phase) to a peak phase, with severity marked by progressive multiorgan dysfunction (shock, acute kidney injury, respiratory failure, CNS injury, severe hemorrhage). Filovirid disease course is depicted across incubation, early, peak, and recovery phases. Severity and case-fatality vary by causative virus and the availability of specific countermeasures and supportive care (pp.1675, 1683-1686).","references":["Harrison 22e p.1679"],"crossLinks":["DENGUE","MALARIA"]},"viral_warts":{"name":"Viral Warts","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["verruca","verruca vulgaris","common wart","plantar wart","verruca plantaris","HPV wart"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Common warts are firm, hyperkeratotic papules with a rough surface, often studded with tiny black dots representing thrombosed capillaries","Paring the surface of a plantar wart reveals these capillary dots and interrupts the skin lines, helping to distinguish it from a corn or callus","Morphological variants include flat (plane) warts, filiform warts around the face, and mosaic plantar warts","Transmission is by direct contact and via contaminated moist surfaces such as swimming-pool floors, with autoinoculation and the Koebner phenomenon spreading lesions","Most warts in healthy people resolve spontaneously as immunity develops, so a period of watchful waiting is reasonable","First-line treatments such as topical keratolytics and cryotherapy work by physical destruction and stimulating an immune response, and typically require repeated, persistent application","Widespread, recalcitrant, or unusual warts should prompt consideration of immunosuppression","Genital warts require assessment for sexually transmitted infection, and their prevention links to HPV vaccination"],"pathophysiology":"Viral warts result from infection of keratinocytes by human papillomavirus, which enters through microabrasions and drives epidermal proliferation and hyperkeratosis. Different HPV genotypes show tropism for particular sites and clinical morphologies. The virus establishes infection in the basal layer and completes its life cycle as keratinocytes differentiate towards the surface, producing the koilocytic changes seen histologically. Clearance depends on an effective cell-mediated immune response, which explains both spontaneous resolution over time and the persistence or proliferation of warts in immunocompromised patients. Certain high-risk genital genotypes carry oncogenic potential.","additionalDifferentials":["Corn or callus (skin lines preserved, no capillary dots)","Molluscum contagiosum","Seborrhoeic keratosis","Actinic keratosis or squamous cell carcinoma in a persistent lesion on sun-damaged skin","Acral or amelanotic melanoma masquerading as a plantar wart","Knuckle pads or other keratotic papules"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Diagnosis is usually clinical, aided by paring the lesion and by dermoscopy","Skin biopsy for atypical, persistent, or rapidly changing lesions to exclude malignancy","Assessment for immunosuppression when warts are extensive or recalcitrant","Sexual health screening for anogenital warts"],"redFlags":["A verrucous or ulcerated lesion that is enlarging, bleeding, or failing to respond, raising concern for squamous cell carcinoma","A pigmented or irregular acral lesion that could be melanoma rather than a wart","Numerous or atypical warts suggesting underlying immunosuppression, including epidermodysplasia verruciformis","Anogenital warts in a child prompting safeguarding consideration"],"prognosis":"The outlook is generally excellent, with most warts in immunocompetent people clearing spontaneously within one to two years; some are stubborn and require repeated treatment, and clearance is slower and relapse more likely where immunity is impaired.","pitfalls":["Misdiagnosing an amelanotic melanoma or squamous cell carcinoma as a resistant wart and delaying biopsy","Confusing a plantar wart with a simple corn or callus","Persisting with destructive treatments that cause more scarring and pain than the wart itself, particularly in children","Failing to consider immunosuppression in widespread or treatment-resistant disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"vitamin_a_deficiency":{"name":"Vitamin A Deficiency","system":"Nutritional","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hypovitaminosis A","xerophthalmia","retinol deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A major cause of preventable childhood blindness and increased infection-related mortality in low- and middle-income countries.","The earliest and most sensitive symptom is night blindness, reflecting impaired rod photoreceptor function.","Progressive ocular signs (xerophthalmia) include conjunctival dryness, Bitot spots, corneal xerosis, and ultimately corneal ulceration and keratomalacia that can rapidly destroy the eye.","Deficiency also impairs epithelial integrity and immune function, increasing severity of infections such as measles and diarrhoeal disease.","It commonly coexists with protein-energy malnutrition and other micronutrient deficiencies.","Measles can precipitate acute deficiency and corneal damage, so supplementation is recommended during measles infection in at-risk children.","Diagnosis is largely clinical, supported by a low serum retinol level and dietary history.","High-dose vitamin A supplementation reverses early ocular signs and reduces mortality, and periodic supplementation programmes are a key public health measure.","Excessive vitamin A intake is toxic, so dosing schedules must be followed carefully."],"pathophysiology":"Vitamin A (retinol) is essential for the visual cycle, where its aldehyde form retinal combines with opsin to form rhodopsin in rod cells; deficiency reduces rhodopsin regeneration and causes night blindness. Retinoic acid also regulates differentiation and maintenance of epithelial tissues and mucosal barriers, so deficiency leads to squamous metaplasia and keratinisation of conjunctival and corneal epithelium (xerophthalmia) and of other epithelia. Impaired epithelial barriers and immune regulation increase susceptibility to and severity of infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Other causes of night blindness (retinitis pigmentosa)","Fat malabsorption syndromes (cystic fibrosis, cholestatic liver disease, coeliac disease)","Protein-energy malnutrition with multiple deficiencies","Infective keratoconjunctivitis","Zinc deficiency","Dry eye from other causes"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum retinol level","Clinical ocular examination for Bitot spots, xerosis, and corneal changes","Assessment of nutritional status and dietary intake","Investigation for fat malabsorption where suspected","Screening for coexisting micronutrient deficiencies"],"redFlags":["Corneal ulceration or keratomalacia threatening irreversible blindness","Rapidly progressing ocular signs, particularly during measles","Severe concurrent infection in a malnourished child","Signs of advanced protein-energy malnutrition","Bilateral corneal involvement"],"prognosis":"Early ocular changes are fully reversible with prompt supplementation, but corneal ulceration and keratomalacia can cause permanent blindness; adequate vitamin A status also lowers infection-related mortality.","pitfalls":["Overlooking night blindness as an early, treatable warning sign","Failing to supplement vitamin A during measles in at-risk children","Missing underlying fat malabsorption in a child with deficiency and adequate intake","Causing toxicity by exceeding recommended supplementation doses"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"vitamin_and_trace_mineral_deficiency":{"name":"Vitamin and Trace Mineral Deficiency and Excess","system":"Nutritional / Gastrointestinal","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Micronutrient deficiency","Hidden hunger","Vitamin deficiency","Trace mineral deficiency","Hypovitaminosis","Vitamin toxicity"],"chapter":344,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.2605","clinicalPearls":["Overt single-nutrient deficiencies are uncommon in Western settings, but subclinical 'hidden hunger' is frequent in the elderly and the socioeconomically deprived; multiple deficiencies cluster in the chronically ill, those with alcohol use disorder, the impoverished, and post-bariatric-surgery patients.","Body stores differ enormously: vitamin B12 and vitamin A reserves are large (deficiency may take a year or more to appear), whereas folate and thiamine can be depleted within weeks of an inadequate diet.","Thiamine should be replenished before or during refeeding of malnourished or alcoholic patients, because giving carbohydrate (especially IV glucose) without thiamine can precipitate acute deficiency with lactic acidosis and Wernicke encephalopathy.","Wernicke encephalopathy is the classic triad of ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus, cerebellar ataxia, and confusion; addition of memory loss and confabulation defines Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, which is underdiagnosed despite a typical history.","Pellagra (niacin deficiency) is remembered as the 'four Ds' - dermatitis (notably the photosensitive Casal's necklace), diarrhea, dementia, and death; it arises with corn-based diets, alcoholism, Hartnup disease, carcinoid syndrome, and isoniazid use.","Pyridoxine (B6) should be co-administered with isoniazid to prevent neuropathy; paradoxically, chronic high-dose B6 (above ~100 mg/d) itself causes a reversible severe sensory neuropathy, so long-term supplementation should be discouraged.","Vitamin A deficiency is the leading cause of preventable childhood blindness worldwide (xerophthalmia, Bitot's spots, keratomalacia) and impairs immune defenses, increasing mortality from diarrhea, measles, malaria, and respiratory infection.","Fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies (A, D, E, K) characteristically accompany fat-malabsorptive states such as celiac disease, cholestatic liver disease, cystic fibrosis, short-bowel syndrome, and bariatric surgery.","Zinc deficiency presents with growth retardation/dwarfism, hypogonadism, hypogeusia, alopecia, immune dysfunction, and a periorificial/acral rash; the inherited form is acrodermatitis enteropathica, and penicillamine or chronic zinc itself (via copper depletion) can be culprits.","Copper deficiency causes an anemia refractory to iron plus neutropenia and can be precipitated by chronic high-dose zinc; inherited disorders include Menkes kinky-hair syndrome (ATP7A) and aceruloplasminemia.","Selenium deficiency causes Keshan cardiomyopathy in low-soil regions; combined iodine and selenium deficiency can aggravate cretinism, while selenium excess causes selenosis (hair/nail loss, garlic breath, neuropathy).","Biotin and choline deficiencies occur mainly with inborn errors or parenteral nutrition; high-dose biotin supplements can spuriously skew streptavidin-biotin immunoassays (e.g., falsely high free T4, falsely low TSH or troponin)."],"pathophysiology":"Vitamins are organic micronutrients that the body cannot synthesize in adequate amounts and that act largely as coenzymes or prosthetic groups (e.g., thiamine pyrophosphate in alpha-ketoacid decarboxylation; FAD/FMN flavoenzymes; NAD/NADP in redox reactions; pyridoxal phosphate in amino-acid metabolism). Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) require intact fat absorption and act in vision, gene transcription, calcium homeostasis, antioxidant defense, and gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors. Trace minerals function within metalloenzymes (zinc in numerous metalloenzymes and transcription factors; copper in ceruloplasmin/ferroxidase, cytochrome c oxidase, superoxide dismutase; selenium in glutathione peroxidase and deiodinases). Deficiency arises from inadequate intake, malabsorption, increased losses (hemodialysis, diuretics, increased urinary excretion), drug interference (isoniazid-B6, warfarin-vitamin K, metformin-B12/thiamine, penicillamine-zinc), or genetic transport/enzyme defects. Conversely, supplements, fortification, and occupational exposures can cause clinically important toxicities; in the inflammatory/acute-phase state, micronutrients redistribute and standard laboratory levels become unreliable.","additionalDifferentials":["Protein-energy malnutrition","Malabsorption syndromes (celiac disease, Crohn's disease, short-bowel syndrome)","Chronic alcohol use disorder","Post-bariatric-surgery nutrient deficiency","Inborn errors of metabolism affecting micronutrient handling (Hartnup disease, biotinidase deficiency, abetalipoproteinemia, Menkes/Wilson disease)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Peripheral neuropathy from diabetes or heavy-metal toxicity (mimics thiamine, B6, B12, or vitamin E deficiency)","Dermatitis/seborrhea of other cause (mimics niacin, riboflavin, biotin, or zinc deficiency)","Megaloblastic anemia from drugs or marrow disease (mimics folate/B12 deficiency)","Pseudotumor cerebri or hypercalcemia of other etiology (mimics chronic vitamin A or D toxicity)","Coagulopathy of hepatic disease (mimics vitamin K deficiency - distinguished by failure of prothrombin time to correct with vitamin K)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Erythrocyte transketolase activity with thiamine pyrophosphate stimulation (>25% / activity coefficient >1.25 abnormal); serum/blood thiamine by HPLC","Erythrocyte glutathione reductase activity for riboflavin; urinary 2-pyridone/2-methylnicotinamide for niacin","Plasma pyridoxal 5'-phosphate (deficiency <20 nmol/L) for vitamin B6; plasma/leukocyte ascorbate for vitamin C","Serum retinol (normal 1.05-3.50 micromol/L; deficient <0.70 micromol/L [20 microg/dL]) interpreted alongside C-reactive protein and alpha1-acid glycoprotein","Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D (>20 ng/mL adequate for bone health); plasma alpha-tocopherol (deficient <5 microg/mL) for vitamin E","Prothrombin time and PIVKA-II (undercarboxylated prothrombin) plus serum phylloquinone (<0.15 microg/L) for vitamin K","Serum zinc (<12 micromol/L / <70 microg/dL), serum copper (<65 microg/dL) with ceruloplasmin (<20 mg/dL), interpreted with albumin and acute-phase status"],"redFlags":["Confusion, ophthalmoplegia, or ataxia in an alcoholic or malnourished patient before/during refeeding - possible Wernicke encephalopathy requiring immediate thiamine","High-output cardiac failure with edema (wet beriberi) or Keshan-type cardiomyopathy (selenium)","Night blindness, corneal xerosis, or keratomalacia signaling sight-threatening vitamin A deficiency","Bleeding/hemorrhage with prolonged prothrombin time, especially neonatal intracranial or GI bleeding (vitamin K deficiency)","Increased intracranial pressure, bulging fontanels, hypercalcemia, or papilledema suggesting vitamin A or D intoxication","Iron-refractory anemia with neutropenia (copper deficiency, often from chronic high-dose zinc)"],"prognosis":"Most deficiency syndromes respond well and reversibly to timely repletion: thiamine produces cardiovascular and ophthalmoplegic improvement within ~24 hours and scurvy improves within days, although Wernicke-Korsakoff psychosis may be permanent and vitamin E or B12 neurologic deficits may only partially recover if advanced. Vitamin A deficiency carries substantial mortality - keratomalacia blinds an estimated quarter-million children yearly with case-fatality of 4-25%, and supplementation in deficient populations reduces childhood mortality by roughly 23-34%. Untreated severe thiamine, niacin (death is the fourth 'D'), or selenium (Keshan) deficiency can be fatal, as can acute vitamin A or D intoxication.","pitfalls":["Interpreting micronutrient blood levels during inflammation/acute illness without accounting for acute-phase redistribution (e.g., copper rises with ceruloplasmin, zinc and retinol fall), leading to over- or under-diagnosis","Refeeding a malnourished or alcoholic patient with carbohydrate before giving thiamine, precipitating acute deficiency and lactic acidosis","Attributing a prolonged prothrombin time solely to vitamin K deficiency in liver disease - if it fails to correct with vitamin K, the cause is hepatocellular","Overlooking drug-induced deficiencies (isoniazid-B6, warfarin/orlistat-vitamin K, metformin-B12, chronic zinc-copper, penicillamine-zinc) and chronic high-dose B6 causing neuropathy","Missing biotin supplement interference with streptavidin-biotin immunoassays (spurious thyroid, troponin, or hCG results)","Assuming carotenemia (yellow palms/soles, spared sclerae) indicates jaundice; and giving high-dose beta-carotene to smokers, which raises lung cancer risk"],"severityClassification":"Deficiency severity is graded from subclinical/biochemical (laboratory abnormality without symptoms - 'hidden hunger') through overt clinical deficiency syndromes (beriberi, pellagra, scurvy, xerophthalmia, rickets) to life-threatening complications (Wernicke-Korsakoff, keratomalacia/blindness, hemorrhage, cardiomyopathy). Thiamine deficiency is classically dichotomized into wet (cardiovascular) and dry (neuropathic) beriberi with considerable overlap, and vitamin A xerophthalmia is staged from night blindness and conjunctival xerosis/Bitot's spots to corneal ulceration and keratomalacia.","references":["Harrison 22e p.2605","Harrison 22e p.2606","Harrison 22e p.2607","Harrison 22e p.2608","Harrison 22e p.2609","Harrison 22e p.2610","Harrison 22e p.2611","Harrison 22e p.2612","Harrison 22e p.2613","Harrison 22e p.2614","Harrison 22e p.2615"],"crossLinks":[]},"vitiligo":{"name":"Vitiligo","system":"Skin / Dermatology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["leucoderma","acquired depigmentation"],"chapter":null,"source":"Fitzpatrick's Dermatology / Rook's Textbook of Dermatology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents as well-demarcated, chalk-white macules and patches with complete loss of pigment, typically symmetrical in the common non-segmental form","Favoured sites include periorificial areas (around the eyes and mouth), the hands, and other acral and extensor surfaces","Wood's lamp examination accentuates the depigmented patches and helps detect early or subtle lesions, especially in lighter skin","New patches may appear at sites of trauma or friction (Koebner phenomenon)","As an autoimmune condition, it is associated with other autoimmune diseases, particularly thyroid disease, so consider screening for these","Management options include topical corticosteroids and calcineurin inhibitors, phototherapy for more extensive disease, and rigorous photoprotection of depigmented skin","The psychosocial impact can be substantial, particularly in darker skin and in visible areas, and should be addressed directly","Set realistic expectations: repigmentation is slow and variable, and hair-bearing and facial areas respond better than acral sites"],"pathophysiology":"Vitiligo is an acquired disorder characterised by immune-mediated destruction of epidermal melanocytes, leading to loss of pigment. Current understanding emphasises an interplay of genetic susceptibility, intrinsic melanocyte vulnerability with oxidative stress, and an autoimmune response in which CD8 cytotoxic T cells and interferon-gamma signalling target and destroy melanocytes. The absence of functioning melanocytes leaves affected skin devoid of melanin, producing the characteristic depigmented macules. Non-segmental vitiligo tends to be symmetrical and progressive with autoimmune associations, whereas segmental vitiligo is localised to a dermatomal-like distribution and often stabilises after an early active phase.","additionalDifferentials":["Pityriasis versicolor (hypopigmented type)","Post-inflammatory hypopigmentation","Pityriasis alba","Tuberculoid leprosy (hypopigmented anaesthetic patch)","Naevus depigmentosus or naevus anaemicus","Chemical or occupational leucoderma"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Clinical diagnosis aided by Wood's lamp examination","Thyroid function tests and thyroid autoantibodies given autoimmune associations","Screening for other autoimmune disease when clinically indicated","Skin biopsy only in atypical cases to confirm melanocyte loss","Assessment of disease activity and extent to guide and monitor therapy"],"redFlags":["Rapidly spreading depigmentation indicating active, unstable disease","A solitary hypopigmented patch with reduced sensation, which should prompt exclusion of leprosy where endemic","Symptoms suggesting associated autoimmune disease, such as thyroid dysfunction","Significant psychological distress warranting mental health support"],"prognosis":"Vitiligo follows an unpredictable course; spontaneous repigmentation is uncommon and treatment results are variable, with facial and truncal lesions generally more responsive than acral and hair-bearing sites. It is not physically dangerous but can be psychologically distressing and often chronic.","pitfalls":["Confusing it with pityriasis versicolor or post-inflammatory hypopigmentation","Failing to protect depigmented skin from sunburn and photodamage","Overlooking associated autoimmune thyroid disease","Underestimating the psychological burden and promising unrealistic repigmentation"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Fitzpatrick's Dermatology","Rook's Textbook of Dermatology"],"crossLinks":[]},"vitreous_hemorrhage":{"name":"Vitreous haemorrhage","system":"Eye / Ophthalmology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vitreous hemorrhage","bleeding into the vitreous","VH"],"chapter":null,"source":"Parsons' Diseases of the Eye / Kanski Clinical Ophthalmology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Presents with the sudden painless onset of floaters, cobwebs, a reddish haze, or, with dense bleeding, profound loss of vision","Proliferative diabetic retinopathy and retinal tears (with or without detachment) are among the commonest causes, alongside retinal vein occlusion and trauma","A key priority is to identify a retinal tear or detachment, as these are urgently treatable causes","When the fundus cannot be seen because of dense blood, B-scan ultrasound is essential to look for a retinal tear or detachment","The loss of the red reflex and inability to visualise retinal detail on ophthalmoscopy indicate significant haemorrhage","Initial management often involves upright rest to allow blood to settle inferiorly and improve the view, with treatment of the underlying cause once identified","Persistent non-clearing haemorrhage or an associated retinal detachment is managed surgically with vitrectomy","In children, non-accidental injury must be considered, and spontaneous haemorrhage warrants a search for an underlying vascular or neovascular cause"],"pathophysiology":"Vitreous haemorrhage is extravasation of blood into or around the vitreous cavity. It occurs when abnormal new vessels (as in proliferative diabetic retinopathy or ischaemic vein occlusion) bleed, when normal retinal vessels are torn during posterior vitreous detachment or a retinal tear, or when trauma ruptures vessels. Blood disperses through the vitreous gel, scattering light and obscuring vision. Clearance is slow because the avascular vitreous reabsorbs blood inefficiently, and long-standing blood can lead to fibrosis and tractional complications.","additionalDifferentials":["Proliferative diabetic retinopathy","Retinal tear or rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","Posterior vitreous detachment","Retinal vein occlusion with neovascularisation","Ocular trauma","Age-related macular degeneration with subretinal bleed, or other neovascular disease","Terson syndrome (associated with intracranial haemorrhage)"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Dilated fundus examination of both eyes when the view allows","B-scan ultrasonography when the retina cannot be visualised, to exclude a tear or detachment","Assessment of the fellow eye for clues to the underlying cause (e.g. diabetic changes)","Optical coherence tomography and fluorescein angiography once the media clear","Systemic evaluation for diabetes, hypertension, and bleeding tendency as appropriate"],"redFlags":["An underlying retinal tear or detachment requiring urgent treatment to preserve vision","Sudden profound visual loss with an obscured fundus needing prompt ultrasound assessment","Neovascularisation from proliferative diabetic retinopathy or ischaemic vein occlusion requiring timely treatment","Vitreous haemorrhage in a child raising concern for non-accidental injury","Bilateral haemorrhage or associated neurological symptoms suggesting Terson syndrome"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the cause; haemorrhage from a posterior vitreous detachment often clears well, whereas bleeding from proliferative retinopathy or an associated retinal detachment carries a higher risk of recurrence and permanent visual loss without treatment.","pitfalls":["Failing to perform B-scan ultrasound and missing an underlying retinal detachment behind dense blood","Attributing the bleed to an obvious cause without examining for a retinal tear","Overlooking non-accidental injury in a child with vitreous haemorrhage","Delaying treatment of proliferative retinopathy while awaiting spontaneous clearance"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Parsons' Diseases of the Eye","Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology"],"crossLinks":[]},"vocal_cord_nodules":{"name":"Vocal cord nodules","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vocal fold nodules","singer's nodules","screamer's nodules"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Vocal cord nodules are small, usually bilateral swellings on the vocal folds caused by chronic voice misuse or overuse","They typically sit at the junction of the anterior third and posterior two-thirds of the fold, the point of maximal contact during phonation","They are common in professional voice users, teachers, singers and habitually loud children","The main symptom is progressive hoarseness, often with vocal fatigue and a breathy quality, worse with use","On laryngoscopy early lesions look soft and reddish while long-standing nodules become firm, white and fibrotic","The mainstay of treatment is voice rest and speech therapy to correct the underlying vocal behaviour, which resolves most early nodules","Surgical excision (microlaryngoscopy) is reserved for mature fibrotic nodules that fail conservative treatment, and must be followed by voice therapy to prevent recurrence","Any hoarseness persisting beyond a few weeks, particularly in adults with risk factors, needs laryngeal examination to exclude malignancy"],"pathophysiology":"Vocal cord nodules form in response to repeated mechanical trauma from vocal abuse. Forceful or excessive vocal fold collision, especially at the midpoint of the membranous cord where impact stress is greatest, causes localised oedema and microhaemorrhage in the superficial lamina propria. With continued strain this reactive change organises into fibrosis and thickening of the overlying epithelium, producing symmetrical nodules on the medial edges of both folds. These masses prevent complete glottic closure and add mass to the vibrating edge, disrupting the mucosal wave and generating hoarseness and a breathy voice. Because both folds strike each other, lesions are characteristically bilateral and roughly symmetrical.","additionalDifferentials":["Vocal cord polyp (usually unilateral)","Reinke oedema","Vocal cord cyst","Contact granuloma of the vocal process","Laryngeal papilloma","Early laryngeal carcinoma or leukoplakia","Functional (muscle tension) dysphonia"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Laryngoscopy (flexible or rigid) to visualise the folds","Videostroboscopy to assess the mucosal wave and glottic closure","Voice assessment and acoustic analysis by a speech and language therapist","Biopsy of any atypical or suspicious lesion to exclude malignancy","Assessment for contributing factors such as reflux, allergy and vocal habits"],"redFlags":["Persistent hoarseness beyond a few weeks in an adult, especially with smoking or alcohol use, requiring exclusion of malignancy","A unilateral, irregular or ulcerated lesion suggesting neoplasia rather than a nodule","Associated stridor, airway compromise or difficulty swallowing","Neck mass or referred otalgia accompanying the voice change","Unexplained weight loss or haemoptysis"],"prognosis":"Early, soft nodules usually resolve completely with voice rest and speech therapy, and the outlook is excellent when vocal behaviour is corrected; mature fibrotic nodules may need surgery and, without behavioural change, nodules commonly recur.","pitfalls":["Proceeding to surgery before an adequate trial of voice therapy","Mistaking a unilateral polyp, cyst or malignancy for benign nodules","Failing to arrange voice therapy after surgery, leading to recurrence","Ignoring persistent adult hoarseness that warrants cancer exclusion","Overlooking contributing factors such as reflux and ongoing vocal abuse"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"vocal_cord_palsy":{"name":"Vocal cord palsy","system":"ENT / Otorhinolaryngology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vocal fold paralysis","recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy","laryngeal nerve paralysis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Results from disruption of the vagus or recurrent laryngeal nerve supply to the intrinsic laryngeal muscles, and may be unilateral or bilateral","Unilateral palsy typically causes a breathy, weak voice and sometimes aspiration, as the healthy cord may compensate over time","Bilateral palsy is more dangerous: the cords may sit near the midline, threatening the airway and causing stridor","The left recurrent laryngeal nerve has a longer intrathoracic course, making it more vulnerable to chest pathology such as mediastinal or lung disease","Common causes include surgical injury (notably thyroid surgery), malignancy along the nerve's course, and neurological or idiopathic causes","A thorough search for the cause is essential, examining the neck, thyroid, chest and skull base along the nerve pathway","Management principles include voice therapy, treating the underlying cause, and procedures such as injection or medialisation to improve glottic closure","In bilateral palsy with airway compromise, securing the airway takes priority, sometimes requiring tracheostomy"],"pathophysiology":"Vocal cord palsy arises from interruption anywhere along the motor pathway to the larynx, from the brainstem nucleus through the vagus and recurrent laryngeal nerves to the intrinsic laryngeal muscles. Loss of innervation impairs abduction and adduction of the affected fold, so it rests in a position determined by the residual muscle tone and any compensatory changes. The recurrent laryngeal nerve's long course, especially on the left where it loops under the aortic arch, exposes it to compression or invasion by neck and chest disease and to iatrogenic injury during surgery. The functional consequence depends on the resting position of the cords and whether one or both are affected.","additionalDifferentials":["Cricoarytenoid joint fixation (e.g. from arthritis or trauma)","Laryngeal carcinoma restricting cord movement","Functional or psychogenic dysphonia","Vocal fold nodules, polyps or scarring","Myasthenia gravis affecting laryngeal muscles","Post-intubation laryngeal injury"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Laryngoscopy to confirm reduced or absent cord movement and assess position","CT or MRI along the course of the vagus and recurrent laryngeal nerves from skull base to chest","Chest imaging to exclude thoracic causes","Thyroid and neck evaluation, including relevant surgical history","Neurological assessment and laryngeal electromyography in selected cases"],"redFlags":["Bilateral palsy with stridor or respiratory distress requiring urgent airway management","Progressive symptoms with a neck mass, weight loss or haemoptysis suggesting malignancy","Aspiration with recurrent chest infections from poor glottic closure","Associated cranial nerve deficits pointing to a skull base or brainstem lesion"],"prognosis":"Prognosis depends on the cause and whether the palsy is unilateral or bilateral; some idiopathic or postviral unilateral palsies recover, others compensate with voice therapy, while bilateral palsy often needs airway intervention and malignant causes carry the outlook of the underlying disease.","pitfalls":["Labelling a palsy idiopathic without imaging the entire nerve pathway to exclude a tumour","Underestimating airway risk in bilateral palsy","Missing silent aspiration in patients with poor glottic closure","Confusing cricoarytenoid joint fixation or cancer with a neurogenic palsy"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Dhingra, Diseases of Ear, Nose and Throat"],"crossLinks":[]},"von_willebrand_disease":{"name":"Von Willebrand disease","system":"Haematology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["VWD","von Willebrand disease","vWD","von Willebrand factor deficiency"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common inherited bleeding disorder, caused by a quantitative or qualitative defect in von Willebrand factor, which mediates platelet adhesion and carries and stabilises factor VIII","The bleeding pattern is that of primary haemostatic failure: mucocutaneous bleeding with easy bruising, epistaxis, menorrhagia, and prolonged bleeding after dental or surgical procedures","Type 1 is a partial quantitative deficiency (most common and usually mild), type 2 comprises qualitative variants, and type 3 is a severe near-complete deficiency with the heaviest bleeding","Because von Willebrand factor protects factor VIII, low levels can produce a modestly prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time alongside normal platelet count","Diagnosis relies on measuring von Willebrand factor antigen, its functional (ristocetin cofactor or equivalent) activity, and factor VIII, with multimer analysis to subtype","Factor levels fluctuate with stress, pregnancy, oestrogens, and inflammation, so borderline results may need repeat testing","Desmopressin releases stored von Willebrand factor and is useful in type 1 and many type 2 cases but is ineffective or inappropriate in type 3 and certain subtypes","When desmopressin is unsuitable, von Willebrand factor-containing concentrates and antifibrinolytic agents are the mainstays for bleeding or procedures; an acquired form can occur with other disorders"],"pathophysiology":"Von Willebrand factor is a large multimeric glycoprotein synthesised by endothelial cells and megakaryocytes. It bridges platelets to exposed subendothelial collagen at sites of vascular injury (particularly under high shear) and serves as the carrier that protects circulating factor VIII from degradation. In VWD, reduced quantity (types 1 and 3) or impaired function (type 2 variants) of this factor impairs platelet adhesion and aggregation, and the loss of factor VIII protection lowers its level, adding a secondary coagulation defect. The result is defective primary haemostasis with a mucocutaneous bleeding phenotype. Acquired von Willebrand syndrome can arise from adsorption onto abnormal cells, high shear (e.g. aortic stenosis), or autoantibodies.","additionalDifferentials":["Haemophilia A (factor VIII deficiency)","Platelet function disorders (e.g. Glanzmann thrombasthenia, storage pool disease)","Immune thrombocytopenia and other causes of thrombocytopenia","Acquired von Willebrand syndrome","Vascular/connective tissue causes of mucocutaneous bleeding","Vitamin K deficiency or liver disease affecting coagulation"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Full blood count and blood film to assess platelet number and morphology","Coagulation screen including activated partial thromboplastin time","Von Willebrand factor antigen level and functional (ristocetin cofactor/collagen-binding) activity","Factor VIII coagulant activity","Von Willebrand factor multimer analysis and ristocetin-induced platelet aggregation to define subtype","Repeat testing when results are borderline, accounting for stress, pregnancy, and hormonal influences"],"redFlags":["Severe or life-threatening haemorrhage, including gastrointestinal or intracranial bleeding, particularly in type 3 disease","Uncontrolled menorrhagia causing haemodynamic compromise or severe anaemia","Major unplanned surgery or trauma in a patient with a significant factor deficiency","New or worsening bleeding suggesting acquired von Willebrand syndrome linked to another disease"],"prognosis":"Most patients, especially those with type 1 disease, have a mild course and a normal life expectancy with appropriate peri-procedural planning; type 3 and severe type 2 disease carry a higher lifelong bleeding burden but are manageable with modern replacement and adjunctive therapy.","pitfalls":["Excluding VWD on the basis of a normal activated partial thromboplastin time or normal platelet count","Missing the diagnosis because factor levels are transiently normalised by stress, pregnancy, or oestrogen use","Giving desmopressin in type 2B (which can worsen thrombocytopenia) or in type 3 (where it is ineffective)","Overlooking acquired von Willebrand syndrome in older patients or those with lymphoproliferative, myeloproliferative, or cardiac conditions","Failing to plan replacement or antifibrinolytic cover before invasive procedures"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"vulvovaginal_candidiasis":{"name":"Vulvovaginal Candidiasis","system":"Obstetrics / Gynaecology","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["vaginal thrush","candidal vaginitis","yeast infection","monilial vaginitis"],"chapter":null,"source":"Williams Obstetrics / Novak's Gynecology","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["Most infections are caused by Candida albicans, a commensal yeast that becomes symptomatic when the vaginal environment or host defences change","Typical features are intense vulval itching, soreness, and a thick, white, non-malodorous discharge often likened to cottage cheese, with vulval erythema and sometimes fissuring","The vaginal pH usually remains normal and acidic, which helps distinguish it from bacterial vaginosis and trichomoniasis","Predisposing factors include recent antibiotic use, pregnancy, diabetes, immunosuppression, and high oestrogen states","Diagnosis is clinical, supported by microscopy showing budding yeasts and pseudohyphae, and culture is useful in recurrent or treatment-resistant cases to identify non-albicans species","Uncomplicated disease responds well to topical or oral antifungal therapy, whereas recurrent disease often needs a longer induction and maintenance approach","Recurrent or resistant infection should prompt evaluation for diabetes, immunosuppression, and non-albicans species such as Candida glabrata, which are less responsive to standard azoles","It is not classed as a sexually transmitted infection, and routine treatment of asymptomatic male partners is not indicated"],"pathophysiology":"Candida species colonise the vagina asymptomatically in many women, kept in check by the resident lactobacillus-dominant flora and local immunity. Disturbance of this balance, for example by antibiotics, or increased glycogen availability from high oestrogen states and hyperglycaemia, allows the yeast to proliferate and transform into an invasive hyphal form. Adherence to and penetration of the vaginal epithelium triggers a brisk inflammatory response, producing the pruritus, erythema, and discharge characteristic of symptomatic infection.","additionalDifferentials":["Bacterial vaginosis","Trichomonas vaginalis infection","Contact or irritant vulvar dermatitis","Lichen sclerosus or lichen planus","Genital herpes","Physiological discharge"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Vaginal pH measurement, typically normal in candidiasis","Saline and potassium hydroxide wet-mount microscopy for yeasts and pseudohyphae","Fungal culture in recurrent, severe, or treatment-resistant cases to speciate","Blood glucose or HbA1c testing where recurrent infection suggests diabetes","Consideration of HIV or immunosuppression assessment in refractory disease"],"redFlags":["Recurrent or refractory infection suggesting undiagnosed diabetes or immunosuppression","Severe vulval swelling, fissuring, or ulceration prompting reconsideration of the diagnosis","Systemic candidiasis in significantly immunocompromised patients","Failure to respond to standard azole therapy suggesting a non-albicans species","Coexisting symptoms pointing to another sexually transmitted infection"],"prognosis":"Uncomplicated vulvovaginal candidiasis responds rapidly to antifungal treatment with an excellent outlook. A minority of women develop recurrent disease that is troublesome and requires prolonged maintenance therapy, and outcomes in these cases improve when underlying predisposing factors and non-albicans species are identified and addressed.","pitfalls":["Diagnosing thrush on symptoms alone and missing bacterial vaginosis or dermatoses","Repeatedly treating recurrent infection without investigating for diabetes or immunosuppression","Assuming all cases are Candida albicans and overlooking azole-resistant species","Unnecessarily treating asymptomatic partners","Attributing chronic vulval symptoms to candidiasis when a dermatological condition is responsible"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Williams Obstetrics","Novak's Gynecology"],"crossLinks":[]},"whipple_disease":{"name":"Whipple Disease","system":"Infectious Diseases","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["Tropheryma whipplei infection"],"chapter":181,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.1367-1370","clinicalPearls":["Whipple disease is a rare chronic infection caused by Tropheryma whipplei; because it is uncommon, mimics many other conditions, and needs nonroutine tests, years typically pass between symptom onset and diagnosis (p.1367).","\"Classic\" Whipple disease presents as some combination of migratory arthralgias/seronegative arthritis, weight loss, chronic diarrhea, abdominal pain, and fever, with the duodenum and/or jejunum involved over years (p.1367, p.1368).","Migratory oligo- or polyarthralgia (knees, wrists, ankles, MCP joints) typically precedes gastrointestinal symptoms by an average of 6-8 years; joint disease is present in >80% of patients (p.1368, p.2546).","CNS disease (detected by CSF PCR for T. whipplei) develops in roughly 50% of patients and many are asymptomatic; manifestations include cognitive decline progressing to dementia, personality/mood change, and hypothalamic involvement, and portend a poor prognosis (p.1368).","Oculomasticatory and oculofacial myorhythmia are highly suggestive of Whipple disease; supranuclear gaze palsy (usually vertical) is another neuro-ophthalmologic clue (p.1368).","Diagnosis rests on small-bowel (postbulbar duodenal) biopsy showing lamina propria infiltration by foamy macrophages with diastase-resistant PAS-positive inclusions, combined with PCR for T. whipplei; PCR has substantially improved sensitivity and specificity (p.1368, p.1369).","It is prudent to collect CSF even without CNS symptoms, because asymptomatic CNS disease is common and the CNS is the most common site of relapse, informing treatment duration (p.1369).","Treatment requires prolonged therapy; clinical response is often rapid, but eradication and durable cure are challenged by relapse and host predisposition to reinfection, which can occur years to decades later (p.1369, p.1370).","WD should be considered in any chronic multisystemic process, especially the combination of rheumatologic and intestinal disease with weight loss, with or without neurologic and cardiac involvement (p.1368, p.1369)."],"pathophysiology":"T. whipplei is a weakly staining gram-positive, non-acid-fast bacillus with a small (<1-megabase) genome and incomplete biosynthetic pathways, consistent with an intracellular/nutritionally dependent pathogen; it is one of the slowest-growing human pathogens (doubling time ~18 days). Exposure is far more common than disease, and chronic infection (WD) is a rare outcome (<0.01% of those exposed). Establishing chronic infection involves both an impaired host inflammatory response and pathogen-driven immune modulation: HLA alleles DRB1*13 and DQB1*06 (favoring humoral over cell-mediated responses) raise risk, an impaired TH1 response with increased anti-inflammatory cytokines and regulatory T-cell activity, M2 macrophage polarization with impaired phagosome-lysosome fusion, and blunted T. whipplei-specific T-cell development occur. The organism has tropism for myeloid cells, which it invades and within which it avoids killing; affected tissue is infiltrated by foamy macrophages containing PAS-staining inclusions (ingested bacteria). In the small intestine, villus atrophy, lymphangiectasia, crypt hyperplasia, and surface epithelial apoptosis lead to malabsorptive diarrhea. (p.1367, p.1368)","additionalDifferentials":["Seronegative inflammatory arthritis / rheumatoid arthritis (initial arthralgic phase is often confused with rheumatologic disorders; RF and ANA usually negative) (p.1368)","Lymphoma (mesenteric/retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy on CT commonly raises this concern) (p.1368)","Sarcoidosis or granulomatous vasculitis (noncaseating granulomas from lymphatic/hepatic involvement; ocular disease may also be misdiagnosed as sarcoid) (p.1368)","Behcet's disease (in patients with ocular/uveitic disease) (p.1368)"],"infectionMimics":["Mycobacterial infection (PAS positivity is nonspecific and also occurs with mycobacteria and other microorganisms) (p.1369)","Giardia lamblia co-infection, occasionally identified, can cause diagnostic misdirection (p.1368)","Other causes of culture-negative endocarditis (T. whipplei causes 2.6-6.3% of culture-negative endocarditis) (p.1368)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Crohn disease (intestinal phase confusion) (p.1368)","Celiac disease (intestinal phase confusion; small-bowel biopsy differential) (p.1368, p.2546)","Other rheumatologic disorders during the early arthralgic stage (p.1368)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Postbulbar (more distal) duodenal biopsies with PAS staining for diastase-resistant inclusions in lamina propria macrophages plus PCR for T. whipplei (tissue yield exceeds body-fluid yield) (p.1369)","PCR of duodenal biopsy: a cycle threshold of <=30 can help confirm the diagnosis even when PAS staining is negative (p.1369)","Immunohistochemistry (greater specificity and sensitivity than PAS, usable on archived fixed tissue) and fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) as complementary tools (p.1369)","Electron microscopy to identify the trilaminar cell wall of T. whipplei (p.1369)","CSF examination and PCR for T. whipplei (collect even when asymptomatic); CSF may be normal or show lymphocyte-predominant leukocytosis and elevated protein, with low glucose reported (p.1369)","Brain MRI (lesions usually T2/FLAIR hyperintense, may enhance with gadolinium; may be normal) (p.1368)","Echocardiography for suspected endocarditis (vegetations seen in 50-75%) (p.1368)","FDG-PET may show uptake at affected sites and can guide tissue sampling (p.1368, p.1369)","Biopsy of normal-appearing skin may detect T. whipplei as a minimally invasive diagnostic route in classic WD (p.1369)","Video-capsule endoscopy to identify more distal small-bowel lesions when initial duodenal biopsy is nondiagnostic (p.1368)","Urine PCR as a potential noninvasive test and therapeutic-monitoring tool (positive in ~75% of cases pretreatment in one small study); plasma cell-free DNA next-generation sequencing for endocarditis (p.1369, p.1370)"],"redFlags":["CNS involvement (cognitive decline/dementia, personality change, hypothalamic dysfunction such as polyuria/polydipsia or sleep-cycle disorder, supranuclear gaze palsy, oculomasticatory myorhythmia) portends a poor prognosis with significant mortality risk and neurologic sequelae (p.1368)","Endocarditis (often afebrile, Duke criteria rarely met) may be complicated by congestive heart failure (~40%), embolic events, arrhythmias, or mycotic aneurysm (p.1368)","Progressive weight loss with chronic diarrhea and malabsorption (p.1367, p.1368)","Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS) after treatment, with recurrent fever, arthritis, skin lesions, pleuritis, uveitis, and periorbital inflammation; some cases have been fatal (p.1369)"],"prognosis":"Untreated Whipple disease is often fatal, and delayed diagnosis can lead to irreparable organ damage, particularly in the CNS. With appropriate treatment, clinical response is often rapid and at times remarkable, and timely recognition may yield cure with medical management alone; however, eradication requires prolonged therapy and a durable response is challenged by relapse (especially CNS relapse) and host predisposition to reinfection occurring years to decades later. CNS disease carries a significant mortality risk and may leave neurologic sequelae. (p.1367, p.1368, p.1369, p.1370)","pitfalls":["Serology is of little value: patients with active infection mount a poor IgM/IgG response, and a positive result most likely reflects prior exposure and clearance (p.1369)","A normal macroscopic duodenal appearance is common, and a negative test does not exclude WD; pretest probability must be considered (p.1369)","Histology/PAS alone is not optimally sensitive and is nonspecific (also positive with mycobacteria and other organisms); anti-TNF-alpha therapy may decrease PAS sensitivity (p.1369)","Saliva and fecal PCR are inappropriate as sole diagnostic tools because of low positive predictive value (more often reflecting colonization than disease); a positive result needs confirmation from end-organ tissue or body fluid (p.1370)","Treating presumed inflammatory arthritis or uveitis with glucocorticoids or anti-TNF-alpha agents can accelerate disease progression and unmask/precipitate disease; consider screening for WD before immunosuppression (p.1368)","Oral tetracycline or TMP-SMX monotherapy has unacceptable relapse rates, particularly for CNS disease; sequence data indicate TMP is not active against T. whipplei (no dihydrofolate reductase) (p.1369, p.1370)","Detection of T. whipplei sequence in BALF of asymptomatic individuals does not establish disease; caution is needed before diagnosing isolated pneumonia on sequence alone (p.1368)","Culture is not practical for diagnosis as it takes months to turn positive (p.1370)"],"severityClassification":"Harrison frames the disease by clinical syndrome rather than a graded severity score: asymptomatic colonization/carriage (PCR-detectable T. whipplei in stool, saliva, or duodenal tissue without symptoms); acute infection (e.g., gastroenteritis in children, febrile bacteremia, acute pneumonia); chronic infection comprising \"classic\" Whipple disease (intestinal involvement with multisystem disease) and \"isolated\" infection (organ disease such as endocarditis, neurologic, ocular, or pulmonary disease without intestinal symptoms); and reinfection/relapsing disease, including treatment-associated IRIS. (p.1367, p.1368, p.1369)","references":["Harrison 22e p.1367-1370","Harrison 22e p.2546"],"crossLinks":[]},"wilms_tumour":{"name":"Wilms Tumour","system":"Renal / Oncology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["nephroblastoma","Wilms tumor"],"chapter":null,"source":"Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["The most common primary renal malignancy of childhood, typically presenting in preschool children around 2-5 years of age.","The usual presentation is an asymptomatic, smooth abdominal mass discovered by a parent or on routine examination, sometimes with abdominal pain, haematuria, or hypertension.","The mass characteristically does not cross the midline, in contrast to neuroblastoma.","Associated with syndromes and anomalies such as WAGR, Beckwith-Wiedemann, and Denys-Drash, and with hemihypertrophy and aniridia; these children warrant surveillance.","Examination should be gentle, avoiding vigorous palpation of the mass because of the risk of tumour rupture.","Staging and histology (favourable versus anaplastic) guide a multimodal approach combining surgery, chemotherapy, and sometimes radiotherapy.","Bilateral disease occurs in a minority and requires nephron-sparing strategies to preserve renal function.","The lungs are the most common site of metastatic spread, so chest imaging is part of staging.","Overall survival is high, making Wilms tumour one of the success stories of paediatric oncology."],"pathophysiology":"Wilms tumour arises from embryonal nephrogenic (metanephric) blastema, often showing a classic triphasic pattern of blastemal, epithelial, and stromal elements. It is linked to abnormalities of genes involved in kidney development, including WT1, and to epigenetic changes at chromosome 11p; persistent nephrogenic rests are precursor lesions. Anaplastic histology confers a worse prognosis and greater treatment resistance.","additionalDifferentials":["Neuroblastoma","Mesoblastic nephroma (in infants)","Renal cell carcinoma (older children)","Clear cell sarcoma or rhabdoid tumour of the kidney","Multicystic dysplastic kidney or hydronephrosis","Hepatoblastoma or other abdominal masses"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["Abdominal ultrasound as the initial imaging test","Cross-sectional imaging (CT or MRI) of the abdomen to define the mass and the contralateral kidney","Chest imaging to assess pulmonary metastases","Full blood count, renal and liver function, urinalysis","Blood pressure measurement","Assessment for inferior vena cava or renal vein tumour thrombus"],"redFlags":["Rapidly enlarging abdominal mass","Gross haematuria or significant hypertension","Features of tumour rupture (acute abdominal pain, distension, haemodynamic instability)","Respiratory symptoms suggesting pulmonary metastases","Signs of vascular (caval) extension"],"prognosis":"With risk-adapted multimodal therapy, most children with favourable-histology disease are cured; prognosis is worse with anaplastic histology, advanced stage, or unfavourable biological features.","pitfalls":["Aggressive palpation of the abdominal mass, risking rupture","Confusing it with neuroblastoma, which typically crosses the midline and has different markers","Missing hypertension as an associated finding","Failing to screen children with predisposing syndromes","Overlooking the contralateral kidney for bilateral disease"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"],"crossLinks":[]},"wilsons_disease":{"name":"Wilson's Disease","system":"Hepatology / Neurology","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["hepatolenticular degeneration"],"chapter":427,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","pages":"Harrison 22e p.3338","clinicalPearls":["Autosomal recessive disorder of copper transport caused by loss-of-function variants in ATP7B (a P-type copper-transporting ATPase), which normally mediates copper removal from the liver via biliary excretion and prevents brain copper accumulation (p.3338).","Typically affects adolescents and young adults; presenting features include nonspecific liver disease, neurologic abnormalities, psychiatric illness, hemolytic anemia, renal tubular Fanconi syndrome, and skeletal abnormalities (p.3338).","Age influences presentation: nearly all individuals presenting with liver disease are <30 years, whereas neurologic or psychiatric presentations may range from the first to the fifth decade; regardless of presentation, some degree of liver disease is invariably present (p.3338).","Neurologic presentation reflects basal ganglia (caudate, putamen) involvement: dysarthria, dystonia (including facial grimacing/risus sardonicus), rigidity, tremor (classically 'wing-beating'), choreiform movements, abnormal gait, and uncoordinated handwriting; WD can properly be classified as a movement disorder (p.3338, p.3518).","Kayser-Fleischer ring (golden to greenish-brown band in the peripheral cornea, from copper deposition in Descemet's membrane) is pathognomonic; present in ~95% of patients with neurologic signs but only about two-thirds of those with hepatic presentations (p.3339).","Supportive biochemistry: low serum copper, low serum ceruloplasmin, increased 24-h urinary copper excretion (>100 ug/24 h), elevated transaminases, aminoaciduria, and hemolytic anemia (p.3339).","Hepatic copper >200 ug per gram dry weight (normal 20-50) on percutaneous needle liver biopsy is a gold-standard biochemical diagnostic finding (p.3339).","Diagnosis relies on a combination of clinical, biochemical, and molecular features; the Leipzig scoring system (2001), endorsed by EASL, weights and collates these features as a valuable diagnostic guide (p.3339).","One of the best-characterized and most effectively managed inborn errors of metabolism; prompt diagnosis in the presymptomatic or early symptomatic phase plus lifelong treatment are needed to prevent premature mortality (p.3338)."],"pathophysiology":"Autosomal recessive disease caused by loss-of-function variants in ATP7B, a highly conserved P-type copper-transporting ATPase expressed primarily in liver and kidney. ATP7B normally mediates copper removal from the liver via biliary excretion and prevents brain copper accumulation; its loss leads to pathologic copper accumulation, primarily impacting the liver and brain. Neurologic disease reflects copper toxicity in the basal ganglia (caudate, putamen, striatum) with neurodegeneration and astrogliosis. Copper also deposits in the cornea (Kayser-Fleischer ring) and lens (sunflower cataract), and renal copper toxicity produces tubular (Fanconi) dysfunction. Massive sudden release of hepatic copper into the circulation directly damages red cell membranes, causing hemolytic anemia (p.3338-3339).","additionalDifferentials":["Viral hepatitis and cirrhosis (often the initial diagnostic considerations in patients who actually have WD) (p.3338)","Autoimmune hepatitis / chronic hepatitis (WD in adolescence may present with chronic-hepatitis features long before neurologic signs or Kayser-Fleischer rings appear) (p.3026-area AIH text, ch.355/427)","Parkinson disease and other movement disorders (WD may be mistakenly diagnosed as these) (p.3338, p.3518)","Substance abuse (a competing consideration in late-teen/early-twenties patients presenting with psychiatric features) (p.3339)"],"infectionMimics":["Viral hepatitis (a frequent initial misdiagnosis in patients with the hepatic presentation of WD) (p.3338)"],"nonInfectiousMimics":["Cirrhosis of other cause (p.3338)","Autoimmune/chronic hepatitis (p.3338, ch.355/427)","Parkinson disease and other movement disorders (p.3338, p.3518)","Primary psychiatric illness or substance abuse in adolescents/young adults (p.3339)"],"additionalInvestigations":["Serum ceruloplasmin (initial screening test; characteristically low) and serum copper (low) (p.3339)","24-h urinary copper excretion (elevated, >100 ug/24 h; use acid-washed copper-free containers); penicillamine challenge with serial urine copper as a variation (p.3339)","Percutaneous needle liver biopsy for quantitative hepatic copper (gold standard; >200 ug/g dry weight characteristic) by ICP-MS or atomic absorption spectrometry; histochemical copper staining is less reliable (p.3339)","Slit-lamp or optical coherence tomography ophthalmologic examination for Kayser-Fleischer rings (required to detect early rings) (p.3339)","Radiolabeled 64Cu incorporation into ceruloplasmin (highly specific; WD patients incorporate very little) (p.3339)","ATP7B molecular/genetic testing (>650 pathogenic variants catalogued; common missense variants H1069Q, M645R, R778L) (p.3339)","T2-weighted brain MRI in neurologic disease may show generalized atrophy and signal hypointensity in caudate head, putamen, globus pallidus, substantia nigra, and red nucleus (~50%), though imaging correlates poorly with clinical features (p.3518)"],"redFlags":["Hemolytic anemia from sudden, massive release of hepatic copper into the circulation directly toxic to red cell membranes can be sudden and catastrophic (p.3339).","Any teenager or young adult with new-onset psychiatric signs should have WD formally excluded (p.3339).","WD should always be considered in the differential of a movement disorder, particularly when arising in the first decades of life (p.3518)."],"prognosis":"In the absence of treatment, the course is progressive, leading to severe neurologic dysfunction and early/premature death in most patients, although a small proportion experience a relatively benign course. WD is one of the most effectively managed inborn errors of metabolism: prompt diagnosis in the presymptomatic or early symptomatic phase and lifelong treatment prevent premature mortality. Liver transplantation, generally needed only when delayed diagnosis or poor compliance has caused irreversible hepatic damage, can correct both hepatic failure and crippling neurologic impairment (p.3338, p.3340, p.3518).","pitfalls":["WD is treatable and progression is preventable, so it is critical to test for it in any young patient with liver disease and/or a movement disorder or new psychiatric illness; missed diagnoses are tragic (p.3338, p.3518).","Normal blood copper, ceruloplasmin, or urinary copper levels do NOT exclude the diagnosis (p.3518).","WD in adolescence may present with chronic-hepatitis features long before neurologic manifestations or Kayser-Fleischer rings appear (p.427/355).","Kayser-Fleischer rings are absent in about one-third of patients with hepatic presentations, so their absence does not rule out WD (p.3339).","In patients with neurologic presentations, penicillamine treatment can induce paradoxical worsening of neurologic status (p.3340)."],"severityClassification":"No single graded severity scale is given in the source; diagnosis is formalized using a combination of clinical, biochemical, and molecular features, collated by the Leipzig scoring system (2001, endorsed by EASL). Disease is broadly staged as presymptomatic/early symptomatic versus advanced; advanced or medically unresponsive hepatic disease is the indication for liver transplantation (p.3339-3340).","references":["Harrison 22e p.3338","Harrison 22e p.3339","Harrison 22e p.3340","Harrison 22e p.3518"],"crossLinks":["movement_disorders","hemolytic_anemia"]},"wolff_parkinson_white":{"name":"Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome","system":"Cardiovascular","class":"non_infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["WPW","WPW syndrome","ventricular pre-excitation","accessory pathway syndrome"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / ESC & ACC-AHA guidelines","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["WPW describes ventricular pre-excitation from an accessory pathway together with symptomatic tachyarrhythmia; the resting ECG classically shows a short PR interval, a delta wave, and a widened QRS","The most common arrhythmia is orthodromic AV reentrant tachycardia, a regular narrow-complex tachycardia using the AV node antegrade and the accessory pathway retrograde","The dangerous arrhythmia is atrial fibrillation conducted rapidly down the accessory pathway, producing an irregular, broad, bizarre, and very fast ventricular response that can degenerate into ventricular fibrillation","In pre-excited atrial fibrillation, AV nodal blocking drugs are contraindicated because they can accelerate conduction over the accessory pathway","An unstable patient with a pre-excited tachyarrhythmia should undergo urgent synchronised cardioversion","The term WPW pattern refers to asymptomatic pre-excitation on ECG, whereas WPW syndrome requires accompanying arrhythmia","Catheter ablation of the accessory pathway is highly effective and is often curative, and is preferred in symptomatic patients","Risk stratification assesses how rapidly the pathway can conduct, since a short refractory period predicts higher risk during atrial fibrillation"],"pathophysiology":"In WPW an accessory atrioventricular pathway bypasses the normal AV nodal delay, allowing early activation of ventricular myocardium and producing the delta wave and short PR interval. This creates the substrate for reentry: in orthodromic AVRT the impulse travels down the AV node and back up the accessory pathway, while in the less common antidromic form the direction is reversed, giving a wide-complex tachycardia. The gravest scenario occurs when atrial fibrillation develops and the accessory pathway, lacking the rate-limiting properties of the AV node, conducts rapid atrial impulses to the ventricles, risking degeneration to ventricular fibrillation and sudden death.","additionalDifferentials":["AV nodal reentrant tachycardia without an accessory pathway","Atrial fibrillation or flutter with aberrant conduction","Ventricular tachycardia","Other causes of a short PR interval","Bundle branch block mimicking a broad QRS"],"infectionMimics":[],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["12-lead ECG to identify pre-excitation and characterise arrhythmia","Ambulatory monitoring to capture paroxysmal tachyarrhythmias","Exercise testing to assess for abrupt loss of pre-excitation as a marker of a longer pathway refractory period","Electrophysiology study to localise the pathway and measure its conduction properties","Echocardiography to assess for associated structural disease such as Ebstein anomaly"],"redFlags":["MEDICAL EMERGENCY: pre-excited atrial fibrillation with a rapid, irregular, broad-complex response that can degenerate into ventricular fibrillation","Haemodynamic instability during any pre-excited tachyarrhythmia requiring urgent synchronised cardioversion","Syncope or aborted sudden cardiac death indicating a high-risk pathway","Use of AV nodal blocking agents in pre-excited atrial fibrillation, which can precipitate deterioration"],"prognosis":"Most patients have an excellent prognosis, and catheter ablation is curative in the great majority; the principal risk is sudden death from rapidly conducted atrial fibrillation in the minority with a short-refractory-period pathway, which risk stratification and ablation substantially reduce.","pitfalls":["Administering AV nodal blocking drugs in pre-excited atrial fibrillation","Assuming an asymptomatic WPW pattern requires no assessment when risk stratification may be warranted","Mistaking pre-excited atrial fibrillation for ventricular tachycardia or ordinary atrial fibrillation","Delaying cardioversion in an unstable pre-excited tachyarrhythmia"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","ESC/ACC-AHA guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"yellow_fever":{"name":"Yellow fever","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["YF","yellow fever virus infection","yellow jack"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mosquito-borne flaviviral haemorrhagic fever endemic to parts of sub-Saharan Africa and tropical South America, transmitted by Aedes and forest mosquitoes","India has no endemic yellow fever, but requires proof of vaccination from travellers arriving from endemic areas because the competent Aedes vector is present, posing an introduction risk","Illness is often biphasic: an initial febrile phase with headache and myalgia, sometimes followed after a brief remission by a toxic phase with jaundice, haemorrhage and organ failure","The toxic phase is characterised by hepatic and renal injury, coagulopathy and bleeding, and carries high mortality","A relative bradycardia despite high fever (Faget sign) is a described clinical clue","There is no specific antiviral treatment; care is supportive with management of bleeding, liver and kidney failure","A single dose of the live attenuated vaccine confers durable, likely lifelong immunity and is the cornerstone of prevention and international travel requirements","Diagnosis uses RT-PCR in early illness and serology (IgM), with care to distinguish from other flaviviruses and viral haemorrhagic fevers"],"pathophysiology":"Yellow fever virus, an RNA flavivirus, is inoculated by an infected mosquito and spreads via lymphatics and blood to target organs, with marked hepatotropism. Infection and death of hepatocytes (with characteristic midzonal necrosis and Councilman bodies) impair synthesis of clotting factors and cause jaundice, while direct renal injury produces acute kidney injury. The combination of coagulopathy, thrombocytopenia and vascular injury drives the haemorrhagic manifestations, and a dysregulated inflammatory response contributes to shock and multi-organ failure in the toxic phase.","additionalDifferentials":["Other viral haemorrhagic fevers (e.g. Ebola, Lassa, Marburg)","Severe malaria","Leptospirosis (Weil disease)","Viral hepatitis with fulminant course","Severe dengue","Rickettsial infection"],"infectionMimics":["Fulminant viral hepatitis of other cause","Sepsis with disseminated intravascular coagulation","Other flaviviral illnesses","Drug-induced or toxic hepatic failure"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR for yellow fever virus in early illness","Yellow fever IgM serology with confirmatory testing given flavivirus cross-reactivity","Liver function tests and coagulation profile to assess hepatic injury and coagulopathy","Renal function and urinalysis to detect kidney involvement","Concurrent testing to exclude malaria, leptospirosis and other haemorrhagic fevers"],"redFlags":["Entry into the toxic phase with jaundice, bleeding and organ dysfunction, a life-threatening emergency","Haemorrhagic manifestations with coagulopathy and shock","Acute kidney injury or fulminant hepatic failure","A febrile returning traveller from an endemic region, requiring isolation, notification and exclusion of other viral haemorrhagic fevers"],"prognosis":"Many infections are mild or self-limiting and recover fully. Once the toxic phase develops, mortality is high, with death typically from hepatic and renal failure, bleeding and shock; survivors of the toxic phase generally recover without chronic sequelae.","pitfalls":["Failing to elicit a travel history and consider yellow fever in a febrile traveller from an endemic area","Mistaking the early phase for uncomplicated dengue or influenza and missing progression to the toxic phase","Overlooking vaccination requirements and prevention counselling for travellers","Not instituting appropriate isolation and public-health notification pending exclusion of viral haemorrhagic fever"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]},"zika_virus":{"name":"Zika virus infection","system":"Infectious / Tropical","class":"infective","referenceOnly":true,"aliases":["ZIKV","Zika fever","Zika virus disease"],"chapter":null,"source":"Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine / WHO / India national programmes","pages":null,"clinicalPearls":["A mosquito-borne flavivirus spread chiefly by Aedes aegypti and Aedes albopictus, the same vectors as dengue and chikungunya","Most infections are asymptomatic or mild; when symptomatic, features include low-grade fever, maculopapular rash, arthralgia and notably non-purulent conjunctivitis","The major clinical concern is congenital infection: maternal Zika in pregnancy can cause microcephaly and a broader congenital Zika syndrome","It is also associated with Guillain-Barre syndrome as a post-infectious complication","Beyond mosquito bites, sexual and vertical transmission are recognised, which influences counselling and prevention","India has reported local outbreaks, so it should be considered alongside dengue and chikungunya in febrile patients with rash","Management is supportive; there is no specific antiviral therapy or licensed vaccine","Diagnosis uses RT-PCR in blood and urine during acute viraemia, with serology complicated by cross-reactivity among flaviviruses"],"pathophysiology":"Zika virus is an RNA flavivirus that, after inoculation by an Aedes mosquito, replicates in dendritic cells and spreads via lymphatics and blood. It has particular tropism for neural progenitor cells, which underlies its ability to disrupt fetal brain development when it crosses the placenta, producing microcephaly and other structural anomalies. Immune-mediated mechanisms, including molecular mimicry, are implicated in the associated Guillain-Barre syndrome. The virus can persist in immune-privileged sites such as semen, enabling sexual transmission.","additionalDifferentials":["Dengue fever","Chikungunya","Measles and rubella","Parvovirus B19 infection","Leptospirosis","Other flaviviral febrile illnesses"],"infectionMimics":["Drug reaction with rash","Acute rheumatological illness with arthralgia and rash","Enteroviral exanthem"],"nonInfectiousMimics":[],"additionalInvestigations":["RT-PCR of serum and urine during the acute viraemic phase","Zika IgM serology with confirmatory neutralisation testing given flavivirus cross-reactivity","Fetal ultrasound surveillance in infected pregnant women","Concurrent testing for dengue and chikungunya in co-endemic settings","Nerve conduction studies if Guillain-Barre syndrome is suspected"],"redFlags":["Infection or exposure during pregnancy, given risk of severe congenital malformation","Progressive ascending weakness suggesting Guillain-Barre syndrome and possible respiratory failure","Features overlapping with dengue warning signs that require exclusion of severe dengue","Neurological deterioration warranting urgent evaluation"],"prognosis":"In non-pregnant adults the illness is usually mild and self-limiting with full recovery. The serious burden lies in adverse pregnancy and neonatal outcomes from congenital infection and in Guillain-Barre syndrome, which can cause prolonged disability or, rarely, death.","pitfalls":["Assuming a dengue-like febrile rash illness cannot be Zika in co-endemic regions","Failing to counsel about sexual transmission and pregnancy risk after exposure","Over-relying on serology alone given cross-reactivity with other flaviviruses","Missing evolving Guillain-Barre syndrome in a recently infected patient"],"severityClassification":null,"references":["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22e","WHO guidelines"],"crossLinks":[]}}};
